(11323,44,93,'heading',3,'10.4 Methods of Preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11324,44,94,'heading',4,'10.4.1 From Alcohols',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11325,44,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Alkyl halides are best prepared from alcohols, which are easily accessible. The hydroxyl group of an alcohol is replaced by halogen on reaction with concentrated halogen acids, phosphorus halides or thionyl chloride. Thionyl chloride is preferred because the other two products are escapable gases. Hence the reaction gives pure alkyl halides. Phosphorus tribromide and triiodide are usually generated in situ (produced in the reaction mixture) by the reaction of red phosphorus with bromine and iodine respectively. The preparation of alkyl chloride is carried out either by passing dry hydrogen chloride gas through a solution of alcohol or by heating a solution of alcohol in concentrated aqueous acid.','[{"s": "Alkyl halides are best prepared from alcohols, which are easily accessible. The hydroxyl group of an alcohol is replaced by halogen on reaction with concentrated halogen acids, phosphorus halides or thionyl chloride. Thionyl chloride is preferred because the other two products are escapable gases. Hence the reaction gives pure alkyl halides. Phosphorus tribromide and triiodide are usually generated in situ (produced in the reaction mixture) by the reaction of red phosphorus with bromine and iodine respectively. The preparation of alkyl chloride is carried out either by passing dry hydrogen chloride gas through a solution of alcohol or by heating a solution of alcohol in concentrated aqueous acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11326,44,96,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image70.png'),
(11327,44,97,'paragraph',NULL,'The reactions of primary and secondary alcohols with HX require the presence of a catalyst, ZnCl 2 . With tertiary alcohols, the reaction is conducted by simply shaking the alcohol with concentrated HCl at room temperature. Constant boiling with HBr (48%) is used for preparing alkyl bromide. Good yields of R—I may be obtained by heating alcohols with sodium or potassium iodide in 95% orthophosphoric acid. The order of reactivity of alcohols with a given haloacid is 3°>2°>1°. The above methods are not applicable for the preparation of aryl halides because the carbon-oxygen bond in phenols has a partial double bond character and is difficult to break being stronger than a single bond (Unit 11, Class XI).','[{"s": "The reactions of primary and secondary alcohols with HX require the presence of a catalyst, ZnCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". With tertiary alcohols, the reaction is conducted by simply shaking the alcohol with concentrated HCl at room temperature. Constant boiling with HBr (48%) is used for preparing alkyl bromide. Good yields of R—I may be obtained by heating alcohols with sodium or potassium iodide in 95% orthophosphoric acid. The order of reactivity of alcohols with a given haloacid is 3°>2°>1°. The above methods are not applicable for the preparation of aryl halides because the carbon-oxygen bond in phenols has a partial double bond character and is difficult to break being stronger than a single bond (Unit 11, Class XI).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11328,44,98,'heading',4,'10.4.2 From Hydrocarbons',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11329,44,99,'paragraph',NULL,'(a)By free radical halogenation','[{"s": "(a)By free radical halogenation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12643,44,1413,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Butane < 1-chlorobutane < 1-bromobutane < 1-iodobutane','[{"s": "(a) Butane < 1-chlorobutane < 1-bromobutane < 1-iodobutane", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11330,44,100,'paragraph',NULL,'Free radical chlorination or bromination of alkanes gives a complex mixture of isomeric mono- and polyhaloalkanes, which is difficult to separate as pure compounds. Consequently, the yield of any single compound is low (Unit 13, Class XI).','[{"s": "Free radical chlorination or bromination of alkanes gives a complex mixture of isomeric mono- and polyhaloalkanes, which is difficult to separate as pure compounds. Consequently, the yield of any single compound is low (Unit 13, Class XI).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11331,44,101,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image87.png'),
(11332,44,102,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.3','[{"s": "Example 10.3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11333,44,103,'paragraph',NULL,'Identify all the possible monochloro structural isomers expected to be formed on free radical monochlorination of (CH 3 ) 2 CHCH 2 CH 3 .','[{"s": "Identify all the possible monochloro structural isomers expected to be formed on free radical monochlorination of (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CHCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11334,44,104,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11335,44,105,'paragraph',NULL,'In the given molecule, there are four different types of hydrogen atoms. Replacement of these hydrogen atoms will give the following','[{"s": "In the given molecule, there are four different types of hydrogen atoms. Replacement of these hydrogen atoms will give the following", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11336,44,106,'paragraph',NULL,'(CH 3 ) 2 CHCH 2 CH 2 Cl (CH 3 ) 2 CHCH(Cl)CH 3','[{"s": "(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CHCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CHCH(Cl)CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11337,44,107,'paragraph',NULL,'(CH 3 ) 2 C(Cl)CH 2 CH 3 CH 3 CH(CH 2 Cl)CH 2 CH 3','[{"s": "(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C(Cl)CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl)CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11338,44,108,'paragraph',NULL,'(II) From alkenes','[{"s": "(II) From alkenes", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11339,44,109,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Addition of hydrogen halides: An alkene is converted to corresponding alkyl halide by reaction with hydrogen chloride, hydrogen bromide or hydrogen iodide.','[{"s": "(i) Addition of hydrogen halides:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "An alkene is converted to corresponding alkyl halide by reaction with hydrogen chloride, hydrogen bromide or hydrogen iodide.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["241158"]',NULL),
(11340,44,110,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image35.png'),
(11341,44,111,'paragraph',NULL,'Propene yields two products, however only one predominates as per Markovnikov’s rule. (Unit 13, Class XI)','[{"s": "Propene yields two products, however only one predominates as per Markovnikov’s rule. (Unit 13, Class XI)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11342,44,112,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image78.png'),
(11343,44,113,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Addition of halogens: In the laboratory, addition of bromine in CCl 4 to an alkene resulting in discharge of reddish brown colour of bromine constitutes an important method for the detection of double bond in a molecule. The addition results in the synthesis of vic-dibromides, which are colourless (Unit 13, Class XI).','[{"s": "(ii) Addition of halogens:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the laboratory, addition of bromine in CCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to an alkene resulting in discharge of reddish brown colour of bromine constitutes an important method for the detection of double bond in a molecule.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The addition results in the synthesis of vic-dibromides, which are colourless (Unit 13, Class XI).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["241121"]',NULL),
(11344,44,114,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image108.png'),
(11345,44,115,'heading',4,'10.4.3 Halogen Exchange',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11346,44,116,'paragraph',NULL,'Alkyl iodides are often prepared by the reaction of alkyl chlorides/ bromides with NaI in dry acetone. This reaction is known as Finkelstein reaction.','[{"s": "Alkyl iodides are often prepared by the reaction of alkyl chlorides/ bromides with NaI in dry acetone. This reaction is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Finkelstein", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11347,44,117,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image18.png'),
(11348,44,118,'paragraph',NULL,'NaCl or NaBr thus formed is precipitated in dry acetone. It facilitates the forward reaction according to Le Chatelier’s Principle.','[{"s": "NaCl or NaBr thus formed is precipitated in dry acetone. It facilitates the forward reaction according to Le Chatelier’s Principle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11349,44,119,'paragraph',NULL,'The synthesis of alkyl fluorides is best accomplished by heating an alkyl chloride/bromide in the presence of a metallic fluoride such as AgF, Hg 2 F 2 , CoF 2 or SbF 3 . The reaction is termed as Swarts reaction.','[{"s": "The synthesis of alkyl fluorides is best accomplished by heating an alkyl chloride/bromide in the presence of a metallic fluoride such as AgF, Hg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", CoF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or SbF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The reaction is termed as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Swarts", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11350,44,120,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image61.png'),
(11351,44,121,'heading',3,'10.5 Preparation of Haloarenes',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11352,44,122,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) From hydrocarbons by electrophilic substitution','[{"s": "(i) From hydrocarbons by electrophilic substitution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12644,44,1414,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) 1-iodobutane < 1-bromobutane < 1-chlorobutane < Butane','[{"s": "(b) 1-iodobutane < 1-bromobutane < 1-chlorobutane < Butane", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11353,44,123,'paragraph',NULL,'Aryl chlorides and bromides can be easily prepared by electrophilic substitution of arenes with chlorine and bromine respectively in the presence of Lewis acid catalysts like iron or iron(III) chloride.','[{"s": "Aryl chlorides and bromides can be easily prepared by electrophilic substitution of arenes with chlorine and bromine respectively in the presence of Lewis acid catalysts like iron or iron(III) chloride.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11354,44,124,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image110.png'),
(11355,44,125,'paragraph',NULL,'The ortho and para isomers can be easily separated due to large difference in their melting points. Reactions with iodine are reversible in nature and require the presence of an oxidising agent (HNO 3 , HIO 4 ) to oxidise the HI formed during iodination. Fluoro compounds are not prepared by this method due to high reactivity of fluorine.','[{"s": "The ortho and para isomers can be easily separated due to large difference in their melting points. Reactions with iodine are reversible in nature and require the presence of an oxidising agent (HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", HIO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") to oxidise the HI formed during iodination. Fluoro compounds are not prepared by this method due to high reactivity of fluorine.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11356,44,126,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) From amines by Sandmeyer’s reaction','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "From amines by Sandmeyer’s reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11357,44,127,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image48.png'),
(11358,44,128,'paragraph',NULL,'Replacement of the diazonium group by iodine does not require the presence of cuprous halide and is done simply by shaking the diazonium salt with potassium iodide.','[{"s": "Replacement of the diazonium group by iodine does not require the presence of cuprous halide and is done simply by shaking the diazonium salt with potassium iodide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
