(11408,44,178,'image',NULL,'Img06',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image75.png'),
(11409,44,179,'paragraph',NULL,'Groups like cyanides and nitrites possess two nucleophilic centres and are called ambident nucleophiles . Actually cyanide group is a hybrid of two contributing structures and therefore can act as a nucleophile in two different ways [VC≡N ↔ :C=NV], i.e., linking through carbon atom resulting in alkyl cyanides and through nitrogen atom leading to isocyanides. Similarly nitrite ion also represents an ambident nucleophile with two different points of linkage [–O— Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image20.png =O]. The linkage through oxygen results in alkyl nitrites while through nitrogen atom, it leads to nitroalkanes.','[{"s": "Groups like cyanides and nitrites possess two nucleophilic centres and are called ambident nucleophiles", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Actually cyanide group is a hybrid of two contributing structures and therefore can act as a nucleophile in two different ways [VC≡N ↔ :C=NV], i.e., linking through carbon atom resulting in alkyl cyanides and through nitrogen atom leading to isocyanides. Similarly nitrite ion also represents an ambident nucleophile with two different points of linkage [–O—", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image20.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "=O]. The linkage through oxygen results in alkyl nitrites while through nitrogen atom, it leads to nitroalkanes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11410,44,180,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.5','[{"s": "Example 10.5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11411,44,181,'paragraph',NULL,'Haloalkanes react with KCN to form alkyl cyanides as main product while AgCN forms isocyanides as the chief product. Explain.','[{"s": "Haloalkanes react with KCN to form alkyl cyanides as main product while AgCN forms isocyanides as the chief product. Explain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11412,44,182,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11413,44,183,'paragraph',NULL,'KCN is predominantly ionic and provides cyanide ions in solution. Although both carbon and nitrogen atoms are in a position to donate electron pairs, the attack takes place mainly through carbon atom and not through nitrogen atom since C—C bond is more stable than C—N bond. However, AgCN is mainly covalent in nature and nitrogen is free to donate electron pair forming isocyanide as the main product.','[{"s": "KCN is predominantly ionic and provides cyanide ions in solution. Although both carbon and nitrogen atoms are in a position to donate electron pairs, the attack takes place mainly through carbon atom and not through nitrogen atom since C—C bond is more stable than C—N bond. However, AgCN is mainly covalent in nature and nitrogen is free to donate electron pair forming isocyanide as the main product.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11414,44,184,'paragraph',NULL,'Mechanism: This reaction has been found to proceed by two different mechanims which are described below:','[{"s": "Mechanism:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This reaction has been found to proceed by two different mechanims which are described below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11415,44,185,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Substitution nucleophilic bimolecular (S N 2)','[{"s": "(a) Substitution nucleophilic bimolecular (S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11416,44,186,'paragraph',NULL,'The reaction between CH 3 Cl and hydroxide ion to yield methanol and chloride ion follows a second order kinetics, i.e., the rate depends upon the concentration of both the reactants.','[{"s": "The reaction between CH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Cl and hydroxide ion to yield methanol and chloride ion follows a second order kinetics, i.e., the rate depends upon the concentration of both the reactants.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["241124"]',NULL),
