(11433,44,203,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Substitution nucleophilic unimolecular (S N 1)','[{"s": "(b) Substitution nucleophilic unimolecular (S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11434,44,204,'paragraph',NULL,'S N 1 reactions are generally carried out in polar protic solvents (like water, alcohol, acetic acid, etc.). The reaction between tert-butyl bromide and hydroxide ion yields tert-butyl alcohol and follows the first order kinetics, i.e., the rate of reaction depends upon the concentration of only one reactant, which is tert- butyl bromide.','[{"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 reactions are generally carried out in polar protic solvents (like water, alcohol, acetic acid, etc.). The reaction between tert-butyl bromide and hydroxide ion yields tert-butyl alcohol and follows the first order kinetics, i.e., the rate of reaction depends upon the concentration of only one reactant, which is tert- butyl bromide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11435,44,205,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image22.png'),
(12528,44,1298,'paragraph',NULL,'With the aid of given structures it is clearly seen that 2-bromobutane where','[{"s": "With the aid of given structures it is clearly seen that 2-bromobutane where", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12623,44,1393,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 26 Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image8.png','[{"s": "Q. 26", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image8.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11436,44,206,'paragraph',NULL,'It occurs in two steps. In step I, the polarised C—Br bond undergoes slow cleavage to produce a carbocation and a bromide ion. The carbocation thus formed is then attacked by nucleophile in step II to complete the substitution reaction.','[{"s": "It occurs in two steps. In step I, the polarised C—Br bond undergoes slow cleavage to produce a carbocation and a bromide ion. The carbocation thus formed is then attacked by nucleophile in step II to complete the substitution reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11437,44,207,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image86.png'),
(11438,44,208,'paragraph',NULL,'Step I is the slowest and reversible. It involves the C–Br bond breaking for which the energy is obtained through solvation of halide ion with the proton of protic solvent. Since the rate of reaction depends upon the slowest step, the rate of reaction depends only on the concentration of alkyl halide and not on the concentration of hydroxide ion. Further, greater the stability of carbocation, greater will be its ease of formation from alkyl halide and faster will be the rate of reaction. In case of alkyl halides, 3° alkyl halides undergo S N 1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of 3° carbocations. We can sum up the order of reactivity of alkyl halides towards S N 1 and S N 2 reactions as follows:','[{"s": "Step I is the slowest and reversible. It involves the C–Br bond breaking for which the energy is obtained through solvation of halide ion with the proton of protic solvent. Since the rate of reaction depends upon the slowest step, the rate of reaction depends only on the concentration of alkyl halide and not on the concentration of hydroxide ion. Further, greater the stability of carbocation, greater will be its ease of formation from alkyl halide and faster will be the rate of reaction. In case of alkyl halides, 3° alkyl halides undergo S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of 3° carbocations. We can sum up the order of reactivity of alkyl halides towards S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 reactions as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11439,44,209,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image88.png'),
(11440,44,210,'paragraph',NULL,'For the same reasons, allylic and benzylic halides show high reactivity towards the S N 1 reaction. The carbocation thus formed gets stabilised through resonance (Unit 12, Class XI) as shown below:','[{"s": "For the same reasons, allylic and benzylic halides show high reactivity towards the S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 reaction. The carbocation thus formed gets stabilised through resonance (Unit 12, Class XI) as shown below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
