(11455,44,225,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) CH 3 CH 2 CH 2 CH 2 Br < (CH 3 ) 2 CHCH 2 Br < CH 3 CH 2 CH( Br )CH 3 < (CH 3 ) 3 C Br (S N 1)','[{"s": "(i) CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CHCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11456,44,226,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 3 CH 2 CH 2 CH 2 Br > (CH 3 ) 2 CHCH 2 Br > CH 3 CH 2 CH( Br )CH 3 > (CH 3 ) 3 C Br (S N 2)','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CHCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11457,44,227,'paragraph',NULL,'Of the two primary bromides, the carbocation intermediate derived from (CH 3 ) 2 CHCH 2 Br is more stable than derived from CH 3 CH 2 CH 2 CH 2 Br because of greater electron donating inductive effect of (CH 3 ) 2 CH- group. Therefore, (CH 3 ) 2 CHCH 2 Br is more reactive than CH 3 CH 2 CH 2 CH 2 Br in S N 1 reactions. CH 3 CH 2 CH(Br)CH 3 is a secondary bromide and (CH 3 ) 3 CBr is a tertiary bromide. Hence the above order is followed in S N 1. The reactivity in S N 2 reactions follows the reverse order as the steric hinderance around the electrophilic carbon increases in that order.','[{"s": "Of the two primary bromides, the carbocation intermediate derived from (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CHCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br is more stable than derived from CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br because of greater electron donating inductive effect of (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH- group. Therefore, (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CHCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br is more reactive than CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br in S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 reactions. CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(Br)CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a secondary bromide and (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CBr is a tertiary bromide. Hence the above order is followed in S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1. The reactivity in S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 reactions follows the reverse order as the steric hinderance around the electrophilic carbon increases in that order.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11458,44,228,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) C 6 H 5 C(CH 3 )(C 6 H 5 ) Br > C 6 H 5 CH(C 6 H 5 ) Br > C 6 H 5 CH(CH 3 ) Br > C 6 H 5 CH 2 Br (S N 1)','[{"s": "(ii) C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11459,44,229,'paragraph',NULL,'C 6 H 5 C(CH 3 )(C 6 H 5 ) Br < C 6 H 5 CH(C 6 H 5 ) Br < C 6 H 5 CH(CH 3 ) Br < C 6 H 5 CH 2 Br (S N 2)','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11460,44,230,'paragraph',NULL,'Of the two secondary bromides, the carbocation intermediate obtained from C 6 H 5 CH(C 6 H 5 ) Br is more stable than obtained from C 6 H 5 CH(CH 3 ) Br because it is stabilised by two phenyl groups due to resonance. Therefore, the former bromide is more reactive than the latter in S N 1 reactions. A phenyl group is bulkier than a methyl group. Therefore, C 6 H 5 CH(C 6 H 5 ) Br is less reactive than C 6 H 5 CH(CH 3 ) Br in S N 2 reactions.','[{"s": "Of the two secondary bromides, the carbocation intermediate obtained from C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is more stable than obtained from C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "because it is stabilised by two phenyl groups due to resonance. Therefore, the former bromide is more reactive than the latter in S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 reactions. A phenyl group is bulkier than a methyl group. Therefore, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is less reactive than C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 reactions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11461,44,231,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Stereochemical aspects of nucleophilic substitution reactions','[{"s": "(c) Stereochemical aspects of nucleophilic substitution reactions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11462,44,232,'paragraph',NULL,'In order to understand the stereochemical aspects of substitution reactions, we need to learn some basic stereochemical principles and notations ( optical activity, chirality, retention, inversion, racemisation, etc.).','[{"s": "In order to understand the stereochemical aspects of substitution reactions, we need to learn some basic stereochemical principles and notations (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "optical activity, chirality, retention, inversion, racemisation,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "etc.).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11463,44,233,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Optical activity: Plane of plane polarised light produced by passing ordinary light through Nicol prism is rotated when it is passed through the solutions of certain compounds. Such compounds are called optically active compounds. The angle by which the plane polarised light is rotated is measured by an instrument called polarimeter. If the compound rotates the plane of plane polarised light to the right, i.e., clockwise direction, it is called dextrorotatory (Greek for right rotating) or the d-form and is indicated by placing a positive (+) sign before the degree of rotation. If the light is rotated towards left (anticlockwise direction), the compound is said to be laevo-rotatory or the l-form and a negative (–) sign is placed before the degree of rotation. Such (+) and (–) isomers of a compound are called optical isomers and the phenomenon is termed as optical isomerism.','[{"s": "(i) Optical activity: Plane of plane polarised light produced by passing ordinary light through Nicol prism is rotated when it is passed through the solutions of certain compounds. Such compounds are called optically active compounds. The angle by which the plane polarised light is rotated is measured by an instrument called polarimeter. If the compound rotates the plane of plane polarised light to the right, i.e., clockwise direction, it is called dextrorotatory (Greek for right rotating) or the d-form and is indicated by placing a positive (+) sign before the degree of rotation. If the light is rotated towards left (anticlockwise direction), the compound is said to be laevo-rotatory or the l-form and a negative (–) sign is placed before the degree of rotation. Such (+) and (–) isomers of a compound are called optical isomers and the phenomenon is termed as optical isomerism.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11464,44,234,'paragraph',NULL,'William Nicol (1768-1851) developed the first prism that produced plane polarised light.','[{"s": "William Nicol (1768-1851) developed the first prism that produced plane polarised light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11465,44,235,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Molecular asymmetry, chirality and enantiomers: The observation of Louis Pasteur (1848) that crystals of certain compounds exist in the form of mirror images laid the foundation of modern stereochemistry. He demonstrated that aqueous solutions of both types of crystals showed optical rotation, equal in magnitude (for solution of equal concentration) but opposite in direction. He believed that this difference in optical activity was associated with the three dimensional arrangements of atoms in the molecules ( configurations ) of two types of crystals. Dutch scientist, J. Van’t Hoff and French scientist, C. Le Bel in the same year (1874), independently argued that the spatial arrangement of four groups (valencies) around a central carbon is tetrahedral and if all the substituents attached to that carbon are different, the mirror image of the molecule is not superimposed (overlapped) on the molecule; such a carbon is called asymmetric carbon or stereocentre . The resulting molecule would lack symmetry and is referred to as asymmetric molecule. The asymmetry of the molecule along with non superimposability of mirror images is responsible for the optical activity in such organic compounds.','[{"s": "(ii) Molecular asymmetry, chirality and enantiomers: The observation of Louis Pasteur (1848) that crystals of certain compounds exist in the form of mirror images laid the foundation of modern stereochemistry. He demonstrated that aqueous solutions of both types of crystals showed optical rotation, equal in magnitude (for solution of equal concentration) but opposite in direction. He believed that this difference in optical activity was associated with the three dimensional arrangements of atoms in the molecules (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configurations", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of two types of crystals. Dutch scientist, J. Van’t Hoff and French scientist, C. Le Bel in the same year (1874), independently argued that the spatial arrangement of four groups (valencies) around a central carbon is tetrahedral and if all the substituents attached to that carbon are different, the mirror image of the molecule is not superimposed (overlapped) on the molecule; such a carbon is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "asymmetric carbon", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "stereocentre", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The resulting molecule would lack symmetry and is referred to as asymmetric molecule. The asymmetry of the molecule along with non superimposability of mirror images is responsible for the optical activity in such organic compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11466,44,236,'paragraph',NULL,'Jacobus Hendricus Van’t Hoff (1852-1911) received the first Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1901 for his work on solutions.','[{"s": "Jacobus Hendricus Van’t Hoff (1852-1911) received the first Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1901 for his work on solutions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11467,44,237,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image13.png'),
(11468,44,238,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 10.4: Some common examples of chiral and achiral objects','[{"s": "Fig 10.4: Some common examples of chiral and achiral objects", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11469,44,239,'paragraph',NULL,'The symmetry and asymmetry are also observed in many day to day objects: a sphere, a cube, a cone, are all identical to their mirror images and can be superimposed. However, many objects are non superimposable on their mirror images. For example, your left and right hand look similar but if you put your left hand on your right hand by moving them in the same plane, they do not coincide. The objects which are non-superimposable on their mirror image (like a pair of hands) are said to be chiral and this property is known as chirality . Chiral molecules are optically active, while the objects, which are, superimposable on their mirror images are called achiral. These molecules are optically inactive.','[{"s": "The symmetry and asymmetry are also observed in many day to day objects: a sphere, a cube, a cone, are all identical to their mirror images and can be superimposed. However, many objects are non superimposable on their mirror images. For example, your left and right hand look similar but if you put your left hand on your right hand by moving them in the same plane, they do not coincide.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The objects which are non-superimposable on their mirror image (like a pair of hands) are said to be", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "chiral", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and this property is known as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "chirality", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chiral molecules are optically active, while the objects, which are, superimposable on their mirror images are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "achiral.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These molecules are optically inactive.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["241159"]',NULL),
(12530,44,1300,'paragraph',NULL,'shows chirality. While as other molecules do not fulfill this condition of asymmetry hence achiral.','[{"s": "shows chirality. While as other molecules do not fulfill this condition of asymmetry hence achiral.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12531,44,1301,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 23 Reaction of','[{"s": "Q. 23 Reaction of", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12532,44,1302,'paragraph',NULL,'C','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12533,44,1303,'paragraph',NULL,'6','[{"s": "6", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12534,44,1304,'paragraph',NULL,'H','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11470,44,240,'paragraph',NULL,'The above test of molecular chirality can be applied to organic molecules by constructing models and its mirror images or by drawing three dimensional structures and attempting to superimpose them in our minds. There are other aids, however, that can assist us in recognising chiral molecules. One such aid is the presence of a single asymmetric carbon atom. Let us consider two simple molecules propan-2-ol (Fig.10.5) and butan-2-ol (Fig.10.6) and their mirror images.','[{"s": "The above test of molecular chirality can be applied to organic molecules by constructing models and its mirror images or by drawing three dimensional structures and attempting to superimpose them in our minds. There are other aids, however, that can assist us in recognising chiral molecules. One such aid is the presence of a single asymmetric carbon atom. Let us consider two simple molecules propan-2-ol (Fig.10.5) and butan-2-ol (Fig.10.6) and their mirror images.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11471,44,241,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image37.png'),
(11472,44,242,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 10.5: B is mirror image of A; B is rotated by 180o and C is obtained; C is superimposable on A.','[{"s": "Fig 10.5: B is mirror image of A; B is rotated by 180o and C is obtained; C is superimposable on A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11473,44,243,'paragraph',NULL,'As you can see very clearly, propan-2-ol (A) does not contain an asymmetric carbon, as all the four groups attached to the tetrahedral carbon are not different. We rotate the mirror image (B) of the molecule by 180° (structure C) and try to overlap the structure (C) with the structure (A), these structures completely overlap. Thus propan-2-ol is an achiral molecule.','[{"s": "As you can see very clearly, propan-2-ol (A) does not contain an asymmetric carbon, as all the four groups attached to the tetrahedral carbon are not different. We rotate the mirror image (B) of the molecule by 180° (structure C) and try to overlap the structure (C) with the structure (A), these structures completely overlap. Thus propan-2-ol is an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "achiral", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
