(12535,44,1305,'paragraph',NULL,'5','[{"s": "5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12536,44,1306,'paragraph',NULL,'C','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11488,44,258,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Retention: Retention of configuration is the preservation of the spatial arrangement of bonds to an asymmetric centre during a chemical reaction or transformation.','[{"s": "(iii) Retention: Retention of configuration is the preservation of the spatial arrangement of bonds to an asymmetric centre during a chemical reaction or transformation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11489,44,259,'paragraph',NULL,'In general, if during a reaction, no bond to the stereocentre is broken, the product will have the same general configuration of groups around the stereocentre as that of reactant. Such a reaction is said to proceed with retention of the configuration. Consider as an example, the reaction that takes place when (–)-2-methylbutan-1-ol is heated with concentrated hydrochloric acid.','[{"s": "In general, if during a reaction, no bond to the stereocentre is broken, the product will have the same general configuration of groups around the stereocentre as that of reactant. Such a reaction is said to proceed with retention of the configuration. Consider as an example, the reaction that takes place when (–)-2-methylbutan-1-ol is heated with concentrated hydrochloric acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11490,44,260,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image11.png'),
(11491,44,261,'paragraph',NULL,'It is important to note that configuration at a symmetric centre in the reactant and product is same but the sign of optical rotation has changed in the product. This is so because two different compounds with same configuration at asymmetric centre may have different optical rotation. One may be dextrorotatory (plus sign of optical rotation) while other may be laevorotatory (negative sign of optical rotation).','[{"s": "It is important to note that configuration at a symmetric centre in the reactant and product is same but the sign of optical rotation has changed in the product. This is so because two different compounds with same configuration at asymmetric centre may have different optical rotation. One may be dextrorotatory (plus sign of optical rotation) while other may be laevorotatory (negative sign of optical rotation).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11492,44,262,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Inversion, retention and racemisation: There are three outcomes for a reaction at an asymmetric carbon atom, when a bond directly linked to an asymmetric carbon atom is broken. Consider the replacement of a group X by Y in the following reaction;','[{"s": "(iv) Inversion, retention and racemisation: There are three outcomes for a reaction at an asymmetric carbon atom, when a bond directly linked to an asymmetric carbon atom is broken. Consider the replacement of a group X by Y in the following reaction;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11493,44,263,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image82.png'),
(11494,44,264,'paragraph',NULL,'If (A) is the only compound obtained, the process is called retention of configuration. Note that configuration has been rotated in A.','[{"s": "If (A) is the only compound obtained, the process is called retention of configuration. Note that configuration has been rotated in A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11495,44,265,'paragraph',NULL,'If (B) is the only compound obtained, the process is called inversion of configuration. Configuration has been inverted in B.','[{"s": "If (B) is the only compound obtained, the process is called inversion of configuration. Configuration has been inverted in B.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11496,44,266,'paragraph',NULL,'If a 50:50 mixture of A and B is obtained then the process is called racemisation and the product is optically inactive, as one isomer will rotate the plane polarised light in the direction opposite to another.','[{"s": "If a 50:50 mixture of A and B is obtained then the process is called racemisation and the product is optically inactive, as one isomer will rotate the plane polarised light in the direction opposite to another.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11497,44,267,'paragraph',NULL,'Now let us have a fresh look at S N 1 and S N 2 mechanisms by taking examples of optically active alkyl halides.','[{"s": "Now let us have a fresh look at S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 mechanisms by taking examples of optically active alkyl halides.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11498,44,268,'paragraph',NULL,'In case of optically active alkyl halides, the product formed as a result of S N','[{"s": "In case of optically active alkyl halides, the product formed as a result of S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11499,44,269,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image55.png'),
(11500,44,270,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, S N 2 reactions of optically active halides are accompanied by inversion of configuration.','[{"s": "Thus, S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 reactions of optically active halides are accompanied by inversion of configuration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11501,44,271,'paragraph',NULL,'In case of optically active alkyl halides, S N 1 reactions are accompanied by racemisation. Can you think of the reason why it happens? Actually the carbocation formed in the slow step being sp 2 hybridised is planar (achiral). The attack of the nucleophile may be accomplished from either side of the plane of carbocation resulting in a mixture of products, one having the same configuration (the –OH attaching on the same position as halide ion) and the other having opposite configuration (the –OH attaching on the side opposite to halide ion). This may be illustrated by hydrolysis of optically active 2-bromobutane, which results in the formation of (±)-butan-2-ol.','[{"s": "In case of optically active alkyl halides, S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 reactions are accompanied by racemisation. Can you think of the reason why it happens? Actually the carbocation formed in the slow step being sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridised is planar (achiral). The attack of the nucleophile may be accomplished from either side of the plane of carbocation resulting in a mixture of products, one having the same configuration (the –OH attaching on the same position as halide ion) and the other having opposite configuration (the –OH attaching on the side opposite to halide ion). This may be illustrated by hydrolysis of optically active 2-bromobutane, which results in the formation of (±)-butan-2-ol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11502,44,272,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image107.png'),
(11503,44,273,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image105.png'),
(11504,44,274,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Elimination reactions','[{"s": "2. Elimination reactions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12537,44,1307,'paragraph',NULL,'H','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12538,44,1308,'paragraph',NULL,'2','[{"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12539,44,1309,'paragraph',NULL,'B','[{"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(12540,44,1310,'paragraph',NULL,'r','[{"s": "r", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11505,44,275,'paragraph',NULL,'When a haloalkane with β-hydrogen atom is heated with alcoholic solution of potassium hydroxide, there is elimination of hydrogen atom from β-carbon and a halogen atom from the α-carbon atom. 3','[{"s": "When a haloalkane with β-hydrogen atom is heated with alcoholic solution of potassium hydroxide, there is elimination of hydrogen atom from β-carbon and a halogen atom from the α-carbon atom.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["241121", "241158", "241162"]',NULL),
(11506,44,276,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image83.png'),
(11507,44,277,'paragraph',NULL,'As a result, an alkene is formed as a product. Since β-hydrogen atom is involved in elimination, it is often called β -elimination .','[{"s": "As a result, an alkene is formed as a product. Since β-hydrogen atom is involved in elimination, it is often called β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-elimination", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11508,44,278,'paragraph',NULL,'Location of α and β carbon in a molecule','[{"s": "Location of α and β carbon in a molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11509,44,279,'paragraph',NULL,'Carbon on which halogen atom is directly attached is called α-carbon and the carbon atom adjacent to this carbon is called β-carbon.','[{"s": "Carbon on which halogen atom is directly attached is called α-carbon and the carbon atom adjacent to this carbon is called β-carbon.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11510,44,280,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image71.png'),
(11511,44,281,'paragraph',NULL,'If there is possibility of formation of more than one alkene due to the availability of more than one β-hydrogen atoms, usually one alkene is formed as the major product. These form part of a pattern first observed by Russian chemist, Alexander Zaitsev (also pronounced as Saytzeff) who in 1875 formulated a rule which can be summarised as “in dehydrohalogenation reactions, the preferred product is that alkene which has the greater number of alkyl groups attached to the doubly bonded carbon atoms.” Thus, 2-bromopentane gives pent-2-ene as the major product.','[{"s": "If there is possibility of formation of more than one alkene due to the availability of more than one β-hydrogen atoms, usually one alkene is formed as the major product.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These form part of a pattern first observed by Russian chemist, Alexander Zaitsev (also pronounced as Saytzeff) who in 1875 formulated a rule which can be summarised as “in dehydrohalogenation reactions, the preferred product is that alkene which has the greater number of alkyl groups attached to the doubly bonded carbon atoms.” Thus, 2-bromopentane gives pent-2-ene as the major product.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["241150"]',NULL),
(11512,44,282,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image95.png'),
(11513,44,283,'paragraph',NULL,'Elimination versus substitution','[{"s": "Elimination versus substitution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11514,44,284,'paragraph',NULL,'A chemical reaction is the result of competition; it is a race that is won by the fastest runner. A collection of molecules tend to do, by and large, what is easiest for them. An alkyl halide with β-hydrogen atoms when reacted with a base or a nucleophile has two competing routes: substitution (S N 1 and S N 2) and elimination. Which route will be taken up depends upon the nature of alkyl halide, strength and size of base/nucleophile and reaction conditions. Thus, a bulkier nucleophile will prefer to act as a base and abstracts a proton rather than approach a tetravalent carbon atom (steric reasons) and vice versa. Similarly, a primary alkyl halide will prefer a S N 2 reaction, a secondary halide- S N 2 or elimination depending upon the strength of base/nucleophile and a tertiary halide- S N 1 or elimination depending upon the stability of carbocation or the more substituted alkene.','[{"s": "A chemical reaction is the result of competition; it is a race that is won by the fastest runner. A collection of molecules tend to do, by and large, what is easiest for them. An alkyl halide with β-hydrogen atoms when reacted with a base or a nucleophile has two competing routes: substitution (S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2) and elimination. Which route will be taken up depends upon the nature of alkyl halide, strength and size of base/nucleophile and reaction conditions. Thus, a bulkier nucleophile will prefer to act as a base and abstracts a proton rather than approach a tetravalent carbon atom (steric reasons) and vice versa. Similarly, a primary alkyl halide will prefer a S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 reaction, a secondary halide- S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 or elimination depending upon the strength of base/nucleophile and a tertiary halide- S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 or elimination depending upon the stability of carbocation or the more substituted alkene.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11515,44,285,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image10.png'),
(11516,44,286,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Reaction with metals','[{"s": "3. Reaction with metals", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11517,44,287,'paragraph',NULL,'Most organic chlorides, bromides and iodides react with certain metals to give compounds containing carbon-metal bonds. Such compounds are known as organo-metallic compounds . An important class of organo-metallic compounds discovered by Victor Grignard in 1900 is alkyl magnesium halide, RMgX, referred as Grignard Reagents . These reagents are obtained by the reaction of haloalkanes with magnesium metal in dry ether.','[{"s": "Most organic chlorides, bromides and iodides react with certain metals to give compounds containing carbon-metal bonds. Such compounds are known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "organo-metallic compounds", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". An important class of organo-metallic compounds discovered by Victor Grignard in 1900 is alkyl magnesium halide, RMgX, referred as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Grignard Reagents", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". These reagents are obtained by the reaction of haloalkanes with magnesium metal in dry ether.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11518,44,288,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image84.png'),
(11519,44,289,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image9.png'),
