(11597,44,367,'paragraph',NULL,'DDT, the first chlorinated organic insecticides, was originally prepared in 1873, but it was not until 1939 that Paul Muller of Geigy Pharmaceuticals in Switzerland discovered the effectiveness of DDT as an insecticide. Paul Muller was awarded the Nobel Prize in Medicine and Physiology in 1948 for this discovery. The use of DDT increased enormously on a worldwide basis after World War II, primarily because of its effectiveness against the mosquito that spreads malaria and lice that carry typhus. However, problems related to extensive use of DDT began to appear in the late 1940s. Many species of insects developed resistance to DDT, and it was also discovered to have a high toxicity towards fish. The chemical stability of DDT and its fat solubility compounded the problem. DDT is not metabolised very rapidly by animals; instead, it is deposited and stored in the fatty tissues. If ingestion continues at a steady rate, DDT builds up within the animal over time. The use of DDT was banned in the United States in 1973, although it is still in use in some other parts of the world.','[{"s": "DDT, the first chlorinated organic insecticides, was originally prepared in 1873, but it was not until 1939 that Paul Muller of Geigy Pharmaceuticals in Switzerland discovered the effectiveness of DDT as an insecticide. Paul Muller was awarded the Nobel Prize in Medicine and Physiology in 1948 for this discovery. The use of DDT increased enormously on a worldwide basis after World War II, primarily because of its effectiveness against the mosquito that spreads malaria and lice that carry typhus. However, problems related to extensive use of DDT began to appear in the late 1940s. Many species of insects developed resistance to DDT, and it was also discovered to have a high toxicity towards fish. The chemical stability of DDT and its fat solubility compounded the problem. DDT is not metabolised very rapidly by animals; instead, it is deposited and stored in the fatty tissues. If ingestion continues at a steady rate, DDT builds up within the animal over time. The use of DDT was banned in the United States in 1973, although it is still in use in some other parts of the world.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11598,44,368,'image',NULL,'80',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Haloalkanes_and_Haloarenes-image97.png'),
(11599,44,369,'heading',4,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11600,44,370,'paragraph',NULL,'Alkyl/ Aryl halides may be classified as mono, di, or polyhalogen (tri-, tetra-, etc.) compounds depending on whether they contain one, two or more halogen atoms in their structures. Since halogen atoms are more electronegative than carbon, the carbon-halogen bond of alkyl halide is polarised; the carbon atom bears a partial positive charge, and the halogen atom bears a partial negative charge.','[{"s": "Alkyl/ Aryl halides", "t": "text"}, {"s": "may be classified as mono, di, or polyhalogen (tri-, tetra-, etc.) compounds depending on whether they contain one, two or more halogen atoms in their structures. Since halogen atoms are more electronegative than carbon, the carbon-halogen bond of alkyl halide is polarised; the carbon atom bears a partial positive charge, and the halogen atom bears a partial negative charge.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11601,44,371,'paragraph',NULL,'Alkyl halides are prepared by the free radical halogenation of alkanes, addition of halogen acids to alkenes, replacement of –OH group of alcohols with halogens using phosphorus halides, thionyl chloride or halogen acids. Aryl halides are prepared by electrophilic substitution to arenes. Fluorides and iodides are best prepared by halogen exchange method.','[{"s": "Alkyl halides are prepared by the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "free radical halogenation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of alkanes, addition of halogen acids to alkenes, replacement of –OH group of alcohols with halogens using phosphorus halides, thionyl chloride or halogen acids. Aryl halides are prepared by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrophilic substitution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to arenes. Fluorides and iodides are best prepared by halogen exchange method.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11602,44,372,'paragraph',NULL,'The boiling points of organohalogen compounds are comparatively higher than the corresponding hydrocarbons because of strong dipole-dipole and van der Waals forces of attraction. These are slightly soluble in water but completely soluble in organic solvents.','[{"s": "The boiling points of organohalogen compounds are comparatively higher than the corresponding hydrocarbons because of strong dipole-dipole and van der Waals forces of attraction. These are slightly soluble in water but completely soluble in organic solvents.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11603,44,373,'paragraph',NULL,'The polarity of carbon-halogen bond of alkyl halides is responsible for their nucleophilic substitution, elimination and their reaction with metal atoms to form organometallic compounds . Nucleophilic substitution reactions are categorised into S N 1 and S N 2 on the basis of their kinetic properties. Chirality has a profound role in understanding the reaction mechanisms of S N 1 and S N 2 reactions. S N 2 reactions of chiral alkyl halides are characterised by the inversion of configuration while S N 1 reactions are characterised by racemisation.','[{"s": "The polarity of carbon-halogen bond of alkyl halides is responsible for their", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nucleophilic substitution, elimination", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and their reaction with metal atoms to form", "t": "text"}, {"s": "organometallic compounds", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Nucleophilic substitution reactions are categorised into", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on the basis of their kinetic properties.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chirality", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has a profound role in understanding the reaction mechanisms of S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 reactions. S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 reactions of chiral alkyl halides are characterised by the inversion of configuration while S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 reactions are characterised by racemisation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(11604,44,374,'paragraph',NULL,'A number of polyhalogen compounds e.g., dichloromethane , chloroform , iodoform , carbon tetrachloride , freon and DDT have many industrial applications. However, some of these compounds cannot be easily decomposed and even cause depletion of ozone layer and are proving environmental hazards .','[{"s": "A number of polyhalogen compounds e.g.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dichloromethane", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "chloroform", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "iodoform", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "carbon tetrachloride", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "freon", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "DDT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "have many industrial applications. However, some of these compounds cannot be easily decomposed and even cause depletion of ozone layer and are proving", "t": "text"}, {"s": "environmental hazards", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
