(13001,45,1,'heading',2,'Hydrocarbons',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13002,45,2,'paragraph',NULL,'Objectives','[{"s": "Objectives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13003,45,3,'paragraph',NULL,'After studying this unit, you will be able to','[{"s": "After studying this unit, you will be able to", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13004,45,4,'paragraph',NULL,'• name hydrocarbons according to IUPAC system of nomenclature;','[{"s": "• name hydrocarbons according to IUPAC system of nomenclature;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13005,45,5,'paragraph',NULL,'• recognise and write structures of isomers of alkanes, alkenes, alkynes and aromatic hydrocarbons;','[{"s": "• recognise and write structures of isomers of alkanes, alkenes, alkynes and aromatic hydrocarbons;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13006,45,6,'paragraph',NULL,'• learn about various methods of preparation of hydrocarbons;','[{"s": "• learn about various methods of preparation of hydrocarbons;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13007,45,7,'paragraph',NULL,'• distinguish between alkanes, alkenes, alkynes and aromatic hydrocarbons on the basis of physical and chemical properties;','[{"s": "• distinguish between alkanes, alkenes, alkynes and aromatic hydrocarbons on the basis of physical and chemical properties;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13008,45,8,'paragraph',NULL,'• draw and differentiate between various conformations of ethane;','[{"s": "• draw and differentiate between various conformations of ethane;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13009,45,9,'paragraph',NULL,'• appreciate the role of hydrocarbons as sources of energy and for other industrial applications;','[{"s": "• appreciate the role of hydrocarbons as sources of energy and for other industrial applications;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13010,45,10,'paragraph',NULL,'• predict the formation of the addition products of unsymmetrical alkenes and alkynes on the basis of electronic mechanism;','[{"s": "• predict the formation of the addition products of unsymmetrical alkenes and alkynes on the basis of electronic mechanism;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13011,45,11,'paragraph',NULL,'• comprehend the structure of benzene, explain aromaticity and understand mechanism of electrophilic substitution reactions of benzene;','[{"s": "• comprehend the structure of benzene, explain aromaticity and understand mechanism of electrophilic substitution reactions of benzene;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13012,45,12,'paragraph',NULL,'• predict the directive influence of substituents in monosubstituted benzene ring;','[{"s": "• predict the directive influence of substituents in monosubstituted benzene ring;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13013,45,13,'paragraph',NULL,'• learn about carcinogenicity and toxicity.','[{"s": "• learn about carcinogenicity and toxicity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13014,45,14,'paragraph',NULL,'“ Hydrocarbons are the important sources of energy. ”','[{"s": "“", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hydrocarbons are the important sources of energy.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "”", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13015,45,15,'heading',3,'13.1 CLASSIFICATION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13016,45,16,'heading',3,'13.2 ALKANES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13017,45,17,'paragraph',NULL,'As already mentioned, alkanes are saturated open chain hydrocarbons containing carbon - carbon single bonds. Methane (CH 4 ) is the first member of this family. Methane is a gas found in coal mines and marshy places. If you replace one hydrogen atom of methane by carbon and join the required number of hydrogens to satisfy the tetravalence of the other carbon atom, what do you get? You get C 2 H 6 . This hydrocarbon with molecular formula C 2 H 6 is known as ethane. Thus you can consider C 2 H 6 as derived from CH4 by replacing one hydrogen atom by -CH 3 group. Go on constructing alkanes by doing this theoretical exercise i.e., replacing hydrogen atom by –CH 3 group. The next molecules will be C 3 H 8 , C 4 H 10 …','[{"s": "As already mentioned, alkanes are saturated open chain hydrocarbons containing carbon - carbon single bonds. Methane (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is the first member of this family. Methane is a gas found in coal mines and marshy places. If you replace one hydrogen atom of methane by carbon and join the required number of hydrogens to satisfy the tetravalence of the other carbon atom, what do you get? You get C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This hydrocarbon with molecular formula C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is known as ethane. Thus you can consider C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as derived from CH4 by replacing one hydrogen atom by -CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group. Go on constructing alkanes by doing this theoretical exercise i.e., replacing hydrogen atom by –CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group. The next molecules will be C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "…", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13018,45,18,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image35.png'),
(13019,45,19,'paragraph',NULL,'These hydrocarbons are inert under normal conditions as they do not react with acids, bases and other reagents. Hence, they were earlier known as paraffins (latin : parum, little; affinis, affinity). Can you think of the general formula for alkane family or homologous series ? If we examine the formula of different alkanes we find that the general formula for alkanes is C n H 2n+2 . It represents any particular homologue when n is given appropriate value. Can you recall the structure of methane? According to VSEPR theory (Unit 4), methane has a tetrahedral structure (Fig. 13.1), in which carbon atom lies at the centre and the four hydrogen atoms lie at the four corners of a regular tetrahedron. All H-C-H bond angles are of 109.5°.','[{"s": "These hydrocarbons are inert under normal conditions as they do not react with acids, bases and other reagents. Hence, they were earlier known as paraffins (latin : parum, little; affinis, affinity). Can you think of the general formula for alkane family or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "homologous series", "t": "text"}, {"s": "? If we examine the formula of different alkanes we find that the general formula for alkanes is C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2n+2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It represents any particular homologue when n is given appropriate value. Can you recall the structure of methane? According to VSEPR theory (Unit 4), methane has a tetrahedral structure (Fig. 13.1), in which carbon atom lies at the centre and the four hydrogen atoms lie at the four corners of a regular tetrahedron. All H-C-H bond angles are of 109.5°.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13020,45,20,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image90.png'),
(13021,45,21,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.1 Structure of methane','[{"s": "Fig. 13.1 Structure of methane", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13022,45,22,'paragraph',NULL,'In alkanes, tetrahedra are joined together in which C-C and C-H bond lengths are 154 pm and 112 pm respectively (Unit 12). You have already read that C–C and C–H σ bonds are formed by head-on overlapping of sp3 hybrid orbitals of carbon and 1s orbitals of hydrogen atoms.','[{"s": "In alkanes, tetrahedra are joined together in which C-C and C-H bond lengths are 154 pm and 112 pm respectively (Unit 12). You have already read that C–C and C–H σ bonds are formed by head-on overlapping of sp3 hybrid orbitals of carbon and 1s orbitals of hydrogen atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13023,45,23,'heading',4,'13.2.1 Nomenclature and Isomerism',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13024,45,24,'paragraph',NULL,'You have already read about nomenclature of different classes of organic compounds in Unit 12. N omenclature and isomerism in alkanes can further be understood with the help of a few more examples. Common names are given in parenthesis. First three alkanes – methane, ethane and propane have only one structure but higher alkanes can have more than one structure. Let us write structures for C 4 H 10 . Four carbon atoms of C 4 H 10 can be joined either in a continuous chain or with a branched chain in the following two ways :','[{"s": "You have already read about nomenclature of different classes of organic compounds in Unit 12. N", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "omenclature and isomerism in alkanes can further be understood with the help of a few more examples. Common names are given in parenthesis. First three alkanes – methane, ethane and propane have only one structure but higher alkanes can have more than one structure. Let us write structures for C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Four carbon atoms of C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be joined either in a continuous chain or with a branched chain in the following two ways :", "t": "text"}]','marked','["131501"]',NULL),
