(13058,45,58,'paragraph',NULL,'Based upon the number of carbon atoms attached to a carbon atom, the carbon atom is termed as primary (1°), secondary (2°), tertiary (3°) or quaternary (4°). Carbon atom attached to no other carbon atom as in methane or to only one carbon atom as in ethane is called primary carbon atom. Terminal carbon atoms are always primary. Carbon atom attached to two carbon atoms is known as secondary. Tertiary carbon is attached to three carbon atoms and neo or quaternary carbon is attached to four carbon atoms. Can you identify 1°, 2°, 3° and 4° carbon atoms in structures I to V ? If you go on constructing structures for higher alkanes, you will be getting still larger number of isomers. C 6 H 14 has got five isomers and C 7 H 16 has nine. As many as 75 isomers are possible for C 10 H 22 .','[{"s": "Based upon the number of carbon atoms attached to a carbon atom, the carbon atom is termed as primary (1°), secondary (2°), tertiary (3°) or quaternary (4°). Carbon atom attached to no other carbon atom as in methane or to only one carbon atom as in ethane is called primary carbon atom. Terminal carbon atoms are always primary. Carbon atom attached to two carbon atoms is known as secondary. Tertiary carbon is attached to three carbon atoms and neo or quaternary carbon is attached to four carbon atoms. Can you identify 1°, 2°, 3° and 4° carbon atoms in structures I to V ? If you go on constructing structures for higher alkanes, you will be getting still larger number of isomers. C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has got five isomers and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has nine. As many as 75 isomers are possible for C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "22", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13059,45,59,'paragraph',NULL,'In structures II, IV and V, you observed that –CH 3 group is attached to carbon atom numbered as 2. You will come across groups like –CH 3 , –C 2 H 5 , –C 3 H 7 etc. attached to carbon atoms in alkanes or other classes of compounds. These groups or substituents are known as alkyl groups as they are derived from alkanes by removal of one hydrogen atom. General formula for alkyl groups is C n H 2n+1 (Unit 12).','[{"s": "In structures II, IV and V, you observed that –CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group is attached to carbon atom numbered as 2. You will come across groups like –CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", –C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", –C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "etc. attached to carbon atoms in alkanes or other classes of compounds. These groups or substituents are known as alkyl groups as they are derived from alkanes by removal of one hydrogen atom. General formula for alkyl groups is C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2n+1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Unit 12).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
