(13173,45,173,'image',NULL,'table6',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image88.jpg'),
(13174,45,174,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image205.png'),
(13175,45,175,'paragraph',NULL,'Chloroethane (13.14)','[{"s": "Chloroethane (13.14)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13176,45,176,'paragraph',NULL,'It is found that the rate of reaction of alkanes with halogens is F 2 > Cl 2 > Br 2 > I 2 . Rate of replacement of hydrogens of alkanes is : 3° > 2° > 1°. Fluorination is too violent to be controlled. Iodination is very slow and a reversible reaction. It can be carried out in the presence of oxidizing agents like HIO 3 or HNO 3 .','[{"s": "It is found that the rate of reaction of alkanes with halogens is F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Rate of replacement of hydrogens of alkanes is : 3° > 2° > 1°. Fluorination is too violent to be controlled. Iodination is very slow and a reversible reaction. It can be carried out in the presence of oxidizing agents like HIO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13177,45,177,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image116.png (13.15)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image116.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.15)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13178,45,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image43.png (13.16)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image43.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13179,45,179,'paragraph',NULL,'Halogenation is supposed to proceed via free radical chain mechanism involving three steps namely initiation, propagation and termination as given below:','[{"s": "Halogenation is supposed to proceed via free radical chain mechanism involving three steps namely initiation, propagation and termination as given below:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["131502"]',NULL),
(13180,45,180,'heading',4,'Mechanism',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13181,45,181,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Initiation : The reaction is initiated by homolysis of chlorine molecule in the presence of light or heat. The Cl–Cl bond is weaker than the C–C and C–H bond and hence, is easiest to break.','[{"s": "(i) Initiation :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The reaction is initiated by homolysis of chlorine molecule in the presence of light or heat. The Cl–Cl bond is weaker than the C–C and C–H bond and hence, is easiest to break.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13182,45,182,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image183.png'),
(13183,45,183,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Propagation : Chlorine free radical attacks the methane molecule and takes the reaction in the forward direction by breaking the C-H bond to generate methyl free radical with the formation of H-Cl.','[{"s": "(ii) Propagation :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chlorine free radical attacks the methane molecule and takes the reaction in the forward direction by breaking the C-H bond to generate methyl free radical with the formation of H-Cl.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13184,45,184,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image49.png'),
(13185,45,185,'paragraph',NULL,'The methyl radical thus obtained attacks the second molecule of chlorine to form CH 3 – Cl with the liberation of another chlorine free radical by homolysis of chlorine molecule.','[{"s": "The methyl radical thus obtained attacks the second molecule of chlorine to form CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– Cl with the liberation of another chlorine free radical by homolysis of chlorine molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13186,45,186,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image32.png'),
(13187,45,187,'paragraph',NULL,'The chlorine and methyl free radicals generated above repeat steps (a) and (b) respectively and thereby setup a chain of reactions. T he propagation steps (a) and (b) are those which directly give principal products, but many other propagation steps are possible and may occur. Two such steps given below explain how more highly haloginated products are formed.','[{"s": "The chlorine and methyl free radicals generated above repeat steps (a) and (b) respectively and thereby setup a chain of reactions.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "he propagation steps (a) and (b) are those which directly give principal products, but many other propagation steps are possible and may occur. Two such steps given below explain how more highly haloginated products are formed.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["130879"]',NULL),
(13188,45,188,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image69.png'),
(13189,45,189,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Termination: The reaction stops after some time due to consumption of reactants and / or due to the following side reactions :','[{"s": "(iii) Termination:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The reaction stops after some time due to consumption of reactants and / or due to the following side reactions :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13190,45,190,'paragraph',NULL,'The possible chain terminating steps are :','[{"s": "The possible chain terminating steps are :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13191,45,191,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Hydrocarbons-image19.png','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hydrocarbons-image19.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13192,45,192,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Hydrocarbons-image180.png','[{"s": "(b)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hydrocarbons-image180.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13193,45,193,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Hydrocarbons-image61.png','[{"s": "(c)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hydrocarbons-image61.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13194,45,194,'paragraph',NULL,'Though in (c), CH3 – Cl, the one of the products is formed but free radicals are consumed and the chain is terminated . The above mechanism helps us to understand the reason for the formation of ethane as a byproduct during chlorination of methane.','[{"s": "Though in (c), CH3 – Cl, the one of the products is formed but free radicals are consumed and the chain is terminated", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The above mechanism helps us to understand the reason for the formation of ethane as a byproduct during chlorination of methane.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["130826"]',NULL),
(13195,45,195,'heading',4,'2. Combustion',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13196,45,196,'paragraph',NULL,'Alkanes on heating in the presence of air or dioxygen are completely oxidized to carbon dioxide and water with the evolution of large amount of heat.','[{"s": "Alkanes on heating in the presence of air or dioxygen are completely oxidized to carbon dioxide and water with the evolution of large amount of heat.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13197,45,197,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image83.png (13.17)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image83.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13198,45,198,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image145.png'),
(13199,45,199,'paragraph',NULL,'(13.18)','[{"s": "(13.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13200,45,200,'paragraph',NULL,'The general combustion equation for any alkane is :','[{"s": "The general combustion equation for any alkane is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13201,45,201,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image198.png (13.19)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image198.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.19)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13202,45,202,'paragraph',NULL,'Due to the evolution of large amount of heat during combustion, alkanes are used as fuels.','[{"s": "Due to the evolution of large amount of heat during combustion, alkanes are used as fuels.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13203,45,203,'paragraph',NULL,'During incomplete combustion of alkanes with insufficient amount of air or dioxygen, carbon black is formed which is used in the manufacture of ink, printer ink, black pigments and as filters.','[{"s": "During incomplete combustion of alkanes with insufficient amount of air or dioxygen, carbon black is formed which is used in the manufacture of ink, printer ink, black pigments and as filters.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13204,45,204,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image25.png'),
(13205,45,205,'paragraph',NULL,'(13.20)','[{"s": "(13.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13206,45,206,'heading',4,'3. Controlled oxidation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13207,45,207,'paragraph',NULL,'Alkanes on heating with a regulated supply of dioxygen or air at high pressure and in the presence of suitable catalysts give a variety of oxidation products.','[{"s": "Alkanes on heating with a regulated supply of dioxygen or air at high pressure and in the presence of suitable catalysts give a variety of oxidation products.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13208,45,208,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image179.png'),
(13209,45,209,'paragraph',NULL,'(13.21)','[{"s": "(13.21)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13210,45,210,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image184.png'),
(13211,45,211,'paragraph',NULL,'(13.22)','[{"s": "(13.22)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13212,45,212,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image9.png'),
(13213,45,213,'paragraph',NULL,'(13.23)','[{"s": "(13.23)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13214,45,214,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Ordinarily alkanes resist oxidation but alkanes having tertiary H atom can be oxidized to corresponding alcohols by potassium permanganate.','[{"s": "(iv) Ordinarily alkanes resist oxidation but alkanes having tertiary H atom can be oxidized to corresponding alcohols by potassium permanganate.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13215,45,215,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image122.png'),
(13216,45,216,'paragraph',NULL,'(13.24)','[{"s": "(13.24)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13217,45,217,'heading',4,'4. Isomerisation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13218,45,218,'paragraph',NULL,'n-Alkanes on heating in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride and hydrogen chloride gas isomerise to branched chain alkanes. Major products are given below. Some minor products are also possible which you can think over. Minor products are generally not reported in organic reactions.','[{"s": "n-Alkanes on heating in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride and hydrogen chloride gas isomerise to branched chain alkanes. Major products are given below. Some minor products are also possible which you can think over. Minor products are generally not reported in organic reactions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13219,45,219,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image12.png'),
(13220,45,220,'paragraph',NULL,'(13.25)','[{"s": "(13.25)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13221,45,221,'heading',4,'5. Aromatization',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13222,45,222,'paragraph',NULL,'n-Alkanes having six or more carbon atoms on heating to 773K at 10-20 atmospheric pressure in the presence of oxides of vanadium, molybdenum or chromium supported over alumina get dehydrogenated and cyclised to benzene and its homologues. This reaction is known as aromatization or reforming.','[{"s": "n-Alkanes having six or more carbon atoms on heating to 773K at 10-20 atmospheric pressure in the presence of oxides of vanadium, molybdenum or chromium supported over alumina get dehydrogenated and cyclised to benzene and its homologues. This reaction is known as aromatization or reforming.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13223,45,223,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image30.png'),
(13224,45,224,'paragraph',NULL,'(13.26)','[{"s": "(13.26)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13225,45,225,'paragraph',NULL,'Toluene (C7H8) is methyl derivative of benzene. Which alkane do you suggest for preparation of toluene ?','[{"s": "Toluene (C7H8) is methyl derivative of benzene. Which alkane do you suggest for preparation of toluene ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13226,45,226,'heading',4,'6. Reaction with steam',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13227,45,227,'paragraph',NULL,'Methane reacts with steam at 1273 K in the presence of nickel catalyst to form carbon monoxide and dihydrogen. This method is used for industrial preparation of dihydrogen gas','[{"s": "Methane reacts with steam at 1273 K in the presence of nickel catalyst to form carbon monoxide and dihydrogen. This method is used for industrial preparation of dihydrogen gas", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13228,45,228,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image56.png (13.27)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image56.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.27)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13229,45,229,'paragraph',NULL,'7. Pyrolysis','[{"s": "7. Pyrolysis", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13230,45,230,'paragraph',NULL,'Higher alkanes on heating to higher temperature decompose into lower alkanes, alkenes etc . Such a decomposition reaction into smaller fragments by the application of heat is called pyrolysis or cracking.','[{"s": "Higher alkanes on heating to higher temperature decompose into lower alkanes, alkenes etc", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Such a decomposition reaction into smaller fragments by the application of heat is called pyrolysis or cracking.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["130807"]',NULL),
(13231,45,231,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image108.png'),
(13232,45,232,'paragraph',NULL,'(13.28)','[{"s": "(13.28)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13233,45,233,'paragraph',NULL,'Pyrolysis of alkanes is believed to be a free radical reaction. Preparation of oil gas or petrol gas from kerosene oil or petrol involves the principle of pyrolysis. For example, dodecane, a constituent of kerosene oil on heating to 973K in the presence of platinum, palladium or nickel gives a mixture of heptane and pentene.','[{"s": "Pyrolysis of alkanes is believed to be a free radical reaction. Preparation of oil gas or petrol gas from kerosene oil or petrol involves the principle of pyrolysis. For example, dodecane, a constituent of kerosene oil on heating to 973K in the presence of platinum, palladium or nickel gives a mixture of heptane and pentene.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13234,45,234,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image141.png'),
(13235,45,235,'paragraph',NULL,'(13.29)','[{"s": "(13.29)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13236,45,236,'heading',4,'13.2.4 Conformations',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13237,45,237,'paragraph',NULL,'Alkanes contain carbon-carbon sigma (σ) bonds. Electron distribution of the sigma molecular orbital is symmetrical around the internuclear axis of the C–C bond which is not disturbed due to rotation about its axis. This permits free rotation about C–C single bond. This rotation results into different spatial arrangements of atoms in space which can change into one another. Such spatial arrangements of atoms which can be converted into one another by rotation around a C-C single bond are called conformations or conformers or rotamers . Alkanes can thus have infinite number of conformations by rotation around C-C single bonds. However, it may be remembered that rotation around a C-C single bond is not completely free. It is hindered by a small energy barrier of 1-20 kJ mol–1 due to weak repulsive interaction between the adjacent bonds. Such a type of repulsive interaction is called torsional strain .','[{"s": "Alkanes contain carbon-carbon sigma (σ) bonds. Electron distribution of the sigma molecular orbital is symmetrical around the internuclear axis of the C–C bond which is not disturbed due to rotation about its axis. This permits free rotation about C–C single bond. This rotation results into different spatial arrangements of atoms in space which can change into one another. Such spatial arrangements of atoms which can be converted into one another by rotation around a C-C single bond are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "conformations", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "conformers", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rotamers", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Alkanes can thus have infinite number of conformations by rotation around C-C single bonds. However, it may be remembered that rotation around a C-C single bond is not completely free. It is hindered by a small energy barrier of 1-20 kJ mol–1 due to weak repulsive interaction between the adjacent bonds. Such a type of repulsive interaction is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "torsional strain", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14993,46,342,'paragraph',NULL,'Reagents attack the reactive site of the substrate. The reactive site may be electron deficient portion of the molecule (a positive reactive site) e.g., an atom with incomplete electron shell or the positive end of the dipole in the molecule. If the attacking species is electron rich, it attacks these sites. If attacking species is electron deficient, the reactive site for it is that part of the substrate molecule which can supply electrons, e.g., Pi electrons in a double bond.','[{"s": "Reagents attack the reactive site of the substrate. The reactive site may be electron deficient portion of the molecule (a positive reactive site) e.g., an atom with incomplete electron shell or the positive end of the dipole in the molecule. If the attacking species is electron rich, it attacks these sites. If attacking species is electron deficient, the reactive site for it is that part of the substrate molecule which can supply electrons, e.g., Pi electrons in a double bond.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13238,45,238,'paragraph',NULL,'Conformations of ethane : Ethane molecule (C 2 H 6 ) contains a carbon – carbon single bond with each carbon atom attached to three hydrogen atoms. Considering the ball and stick model of ethane, keep one carbon atom stationary and rotate the other carbon atom around the C-C axis. This rotation results into infinite number of spatial arrangements of hydrogen atoms attached to one carbon atom with respect to the hydrogen atoms attached to the other carbon atom. These are called conformational isomers (conformers). Thus there are infinite number of conformations of ethane. However, there are two extreme cases. One such conformation in which hydrogen atoms attached to two carbons are as closed together as possible is called eclipsed conformation and the other in which hydrogens are as far apart as possible is known as the staggered conformation. Any other intermediate conformation is called a skew conformation.It may be remembered that in all the conformations, the bond angles and the bond lengths remain the same. Eclipsed and the staggered conformations can be represented by Sawhorse and Newman projections.','[{"s": "Conformations of ethane :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ethane molecule (C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") contains a carbon – carbon single bond with each carbon atom attached to three hydrogen atoms. Considering the ball and stick model of ethane, keep one carbon atom stationary and rotate the other carbon atom around the C-C axis. This rotation results into infinite number of spatial arrangements of hydrogen atoms attached to one carbon atom with respect to the hydrogen atoms attached to the other carbon atom. These are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "conformational isomers", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(conformers).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus there are infinite number of conformations of ethane.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "However, there are two extreme cases. One such conformation in which hydrogen atoms attached to two carbons are as closed together as possible is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eclipsed", "t": "text"}, {"s": "conformation and the other in which hydrogens are as far apart as possible is known as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "staggered", "t": "text"}, {"s": "conformation. Any other intermediate conformation is called a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "skew", "t": "text"}, {"s": "conformation.It may be remembered that in all the conformations, the bond angles and the bond lengths remain the same. Eclipsed and the staggered conformations can be represented by Sawhorse and Newman projections.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["130805"]',NULL),
(13239,45,239,'heading',4,'1. Sawhorse projections',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13240,45,240,'paragraph',NULL,'In this projection, the molecule is viewed along the molecular axis. It is then projected on paper by drawing the central C–C bond as a somewhat longer straight line. Upper end of the line is slightly tilted towards right or left hand side. The front carbon is shown at the lower end of the line, whereas the rear carbon is shown at the upper end. Each carbon has three lines attached to it corresponding to three hydrogen atoms. The lines are inclined at an angle of 120° to each other. Sawhorse projections of eclipsed and staggered conformations of ethane are depicted in Fig. 13.2.','[{"s": "In this projection, the molecule is viewed along the molecular axis. It is then projected on paper by drawing the central C–C bond as a somewhat longer straight line. Upper end of the line is slightly tilted towards right or left hand side. The front carbon is shown at the lower end of the line, whereas the rear carbon is shown at the upper end. Each carbon has three lines attached to it corresponding to three hydrogen atoms. The lines are inclined at an angle of 120° to each other. Sawhorse projections of eclipsed and staggered conformations of ethane are depicted in Fig. 13.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13241,45,241,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image161.png'),
(13242,45,242,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.2 Sawhorse projections of ethane','[{"s": "Fig. 13.2 Sawhorse projections of ethane", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13243,45,243,'heading',4,'2. Newman projections',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13244,45,244,'paragraph',NULL,'In this projection, the molecule is viewed at the C–C bond head on. The carbon atom nearer to the eye is represented by a point. Three hydrogen atoms attached to the front carbon atom are shown by three lines drawn at an angle of 120° to each other. The rear carbon atom (the carbon atom away from the eye) is represented by a circle and the three hydrogen atoms are shown attached to it by the shorter lines drawn at an angle of 120° to each other. The Newman’s projections are depicted in Fig. 13.3.','[{"s": "In this projection, the molecule is viewed at the C–C bond head on.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The carbon atom nearer to the eye is represented by a point.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Three hydrogen atoms attached to the front carbon atom are shown by three lines drawn at an angle of 120° to each other. The rear carbon atom (the carbon atom away from the eye) is represented by a circle and the three hydrogen atoms are shown attached to it by the shorter lines drawn at an angle of 120° to each other. The Newman’s projections are depicted in Fig. 13.3.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["130809"]',NULL),
(13245,45,245,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image178.png'),
(13246,45,246,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.3 Newman’s projections of ethane','[{"s": "Fig. 13.3 Newman’s projections of ethane", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13247,45,247,'paragraph',NULL,'Relative stability of conformations: As mentioned earlier, in staggered form of ethane, the electron clouds of carbon-hydrogen bonds are as far apart as possible. Thus, there are minimum repulsive forces, minimum energy and maximum stability of the molecule. On the other hand, when the staggered form changes into the eclipsed form, the electron clouds of the carbon – hydrogen bonds come closer to each other resulting in increase in electron cloud repulsions. To check the increased repulsive forces, molecule will have to possess more energy and thus has lesser stability. As already mentioned, the repulsive interaction between the electron clouds, which affects stability of a conformation, is called torsional strain . Magnitude of torsional strain depends upon the angle of rotation about C–C bond. This angle is also called dihedral angle or torsional angle . Of all the conformations of ethane, the staggered form has the least torsional strain and the eclipsed form, the maximum torsional strain. Therefore, staggered conformation is more stable than the eclipsed conformation. Hence, molecule largely remains in staggered conformation or we can say that it is preferred conformation. Thus it may be inferred that rotation around C–C bond in ethane is not completely free. The energy difference between the two extreme forms is of the order of 12.5 kJ mol–1, which is very small. Even at ordinary temperatures, the ethane molecule gains thermal or kinetic energy sufficient enough to overcome this energy barrier of 12.5 kJ mol–1 through intermolecular collisions. Thus, it can be said that rotation about carbon-carbon single bond in ethane is almost free for all practical purposes. It has not been possible to separate and isolate different conformational isomers of ethane.','[{"s": "Relative stability of conformations:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "As mentioned earlier, in staggered form of ethane, the electron clouds of carbon-hydrogen bonds are as far apart as possible. Thus, there are minimum repulsive forces, minimum energy and maximum stability of the molecule. On the other hand, when the staggered form changes into the eclipsed form, the electron clouds of the carbon – hydrogen bonds come closer to each other resulting in increase in electron cloud repulsions. To check the increased repulsive forces, molecule will have to possess more energy and thus has lesser stability. As already mentioned, the repulsive interaction between the electron clouds, which affects stability of a conformation, is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "torsional strain", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Magnitude of torsional strain depends upon the angle of rotation about C–C bond. This angle is also called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dihedral angle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "torsional angle", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Of all the conformations of ethane, the staggered form has the least torsional strain and the eclipsed form, the maximum torsional strain. Therefore, staggered conformation is more stable than the eclipsed conformation. Hence, molecule largely remains in staggered conformation or we can say that it is preferred conformation. Thus it may be inferred that rotation around C–C bond in ethane is not completely free. The energy difference between the two extreme forms is of the order of 12.5 kJ mol–1, which is very small. Even at ordinary temperatures, the ethane molecule gains thermal or kinetic energy sufficient enough to overcome this energy barrier of 12.5 kJ mol–1 through intermolecular collisions. Thus, it can be said that rotation about carbon-carbon single bond in ethane is almost free for all practical purposes. It has not been possible to separate and isolate different conformational isomers of ethane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13248,45,248,'heading',3,'13.3 ALKENES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13249,45,249,'paragraph',NULL,'Alkenes are unsaturated hydrocarbons containing at least one double bond. What should be the general formula of alkenes? If there is one double bond between two carbon atoms in alkenes, they must possess two hydrogen atoms less than alkanes. Hence, general formula for alkenes is C n H 2n . Alkenes are also known as olefins (oil forming) since the first member, ethylene or ethene (C2H4) was found to form an oily liquid on reaction with chlorine.','[{"s": "Alkenes are unsaturated hydrocarbons containing at least one double bond. What should be the general formula of alkenes? If there is one double bond between two carbon atoms in alkenes, they must possess two hydrogen atoms less than alkanes. Hence, general formula for alkenes is C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Alkenes are also known as olefins (oil forming) since the first member, ethylene or ethene (C2H4) was found to form an oily liquid on reaction with chlorine.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13250,45,250,'heading',4,'13.3.1 Structure of Double Bond',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
