(13485,45,485,'paragraph',NULL,'1,3 Dimethylbenzene (m-Xylene) 1,4-Dimethylbenzene ( p-Xylene)','[{"s": "1,3 Dimethylbenzene (m-Xylene) 1,4-Dimethylbenzene ( p-Xylene)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13486,45,486,'heading',4,'13.5.2 Structure of Benzene',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13487,45,487,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image151.png'),
(13488,45,488,'paragraph',NULL,'FRIEDRICH AUGUST','[{"s": "FRIEDRICH AUGUST", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13489,45,489,'paragraph',NULL,'KEKULÉ','[{"s": "KEKULÉ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13490,45,490,'paragraph',NULL,'(7th September 1829–13th July 1896)','[{"s": "(7th September 1829–13th July 1896)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13491,45,491,'paragraph',NULL,'Friedrich August Kekulé,a German chemist was born in 1829 at Darmsdt in Germany. He became Professor in 1856 and Fellow of Royal Society in 1875. He made major contribution to structural organic chemistry by proposing in 1858 that carbon atoms can join to one another to form chains and later in 1865,he found an answer to the challenging problem of benzene structure by suggesting that these chains can close to form rings. He gave the dynamic structural formula to benzene which forms the basis for its modern electronic structure. He described the discovery of benzene structure later as:','[{"s": "Friedrich August Kekulé,a German chemist was born in 1829 at Darmsdt in Germany. He became Professor in 1856 and Fellow of Royal Society in 1875. He made major contribution to structural organic chemistry by proposing in 1858 that carbon atoms can join to one another to form chains and later in 1865,he found an answer to the challenging problem of benzene structure by suggesting that these chains can close to form rings. He gave the dynamic structural formula to benzene which forms the basis for its modern electronic structure. He described the discovery of benzene structure later as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13492,45,492,'paragraph',NULL,'“I was sitting writing at my textbook,but the work did not progress; my thoughts were elsewhere. I turned my chair to the fire, and dozed. Again the atoms were gambolling before my eyes. This time the smaller groups kept modestly in the background. My mental eye, rendered more acute by repeated visions of this kind, could now distinguish larger structures of manifold conformations; long rows,sometimes more closely fitted together; all twisting and turning in snake like motion. But look! What was that? One of the snakes had seized hold of it’s own tail, and the form whirled mockingly before my eyes. As if by a flash of lightning I woke;.... I spent the rest of the night working out the consequences of the hypothesis. Let us learn to dream, gentlemen, and then perhaps we shall learn the truth but let us beware of making our dreams public before they have been approved by the waking mind.”( 1890).','[{"s": "“I was sitting writing at my textbook,but the work did not progress; my thoughts were elsewhere. I turned my chair to the fire, and dozed. Again the atoms were gambolling before my eyes. This time the smaller groups kept modestly in the background. My mental eye, rendered more acute by repeated visions of this kind, could now distinguish larger structures of manifold conformations; long rows,sometimes more closely fitted together; all twisting and turning in snake like motion. But look! What was that? One of the snakes had seized hold of it’s own tail, and the form whirled mockingly before my eyes. As if by a flash of lightning I woke;.... I spent the rest of the night working out the consequences of the hypothesis. Let us learn to dream, gentlemen, and then perhaps we shall learn the truth but let us beware of making our dreams public before they have been approved by the waking mind.”( 1890).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13493,45,493,'paragraph',NULL,'One hundred years later, on the occasion of Kekulé’s centenary celebrations a group of compounds having polybenzenoid structures have been named as Kekulenes.','[{"s": "One hundred years later, on the occasion of Kekulé’s centenary celebrations a group of compounds having polybenzenoid structures have been named as Kekulenes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13494,45,494,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image79.png'),
(20501,54,139,'paragraph',NULL,'q p = H 2 – H 1 = ∆H','[{"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∆H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13495,45,495,'paragraph',NULL,'The Kekulé structure indicates the possibility of two isomeric 1, 2-dibromobenzenes. In one of the isomers, the bromine atoms are attached to the doubly bonded carbon atoms whereas in the other, they are attached to the singly bonded carbons.','[{"s": "The Kekulé structure indicates the possibility of two isomeric 1, 2-dibromobenzenes. In one of the isomers, the bromine atoms are attached to the doubly bonded carbon atoms whereas in the other, they are attached to the singly bonded carbons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13496,45,496,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image33.png'),
(13497,45,497,'paragraph',NULL,'However, benzene was found to form only one ortho disubstituted product. This problem was overcome by Kekulé by suggesting the concept of oscillating nature of double bonds in benzene as given below.','[{"s": "However, benzene was found to form only one ortho disubstituted product. This problem was overcome by Kekulé by suggesting the concept of oscillating nature of double bonds in benzene as given below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13498,45,498,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image148.png'),
(13499,45,499,'paragraph',NULL,'Even with this modification, Kekulé structure of benzene fails to explain unusual stability and preference to substitution reactions than addition reactions, which could later on be explained by resonance.','[{"s": "Even with this modification, Kekulé structure of benzene fails to explain unusual stability and preference to substitution reactions than addition reactions, which could later on be explained by resonance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13500,45,500,'paragraph',NULL,'Resonance and stability of benzene','[{"s": "Resonance and stability of benzene", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13501,45,501,'paragraph',NULL,'According to Valence Bond Theory, the concept of oscillating double bonds in benzene is now explained by resonance. Benzene is a hybrid of various resonating structures. The two structures, A and B given by Kekulé are the main contributing structures. The hybrid structure is represented by inserting a circle or a dotted circle in the hexagon as shown in (C). The circle represents the six electrons which are delocalised between the six carbon atoms of the benzene ring.','[{"s": "According to Valence Bond Theory, the concept of oscillating double bonds in benzene is now explained by resonance. Benzene is a hybrid of various resonating structures. The two structures, A and B given by Kekulé are the main contributing structures. The hybrid structure is represented by inserting a circle or a dotted circle in the hexagon as shown in (C). The circle represents the six electrons which are delocalised between the six carbon atoms of the benzene ring.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13502,45,502,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image96.png'),
(13503,45,503,'paragraph',NULL,'(A) (B) (C)','[{"s": "(A) (B) (C)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13504,45,504,'paragraph',NULL,'The orbital overlapping gives us better picture about the structure of benzene. All the six carbon atoms in benzene are sp2 hybridized. Two sp2 hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom overlap with sp2 hybrid orbitals of adjacent carbon atoms to form six C—C sigma bonds which are in the hexagonal plane. The remaining sp2 hybrid orbital of each carbon atom overlaps with s orbital of a hydrogen atom to form six C—H sigma bonds. Each carbon atom is now left with one unhybridised p orbital perpendicular to the plane of the ring as shown below:','[{"s": "The orbital overlapping gives us better picture about the structure of benzene. All the six carbon atoms in benzene are sp2 hybridized. Two sp2 hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom overlap with sp2 hybrid orbitals of adjacent carbon atoms to form six C—C sigma bonds which are in the hexagonal plane. The remaining sp2 hybrid orbital of each carbon atom overlaps with s orbital of a hydrogen atom to form six C—H sigma bonds. Each carbon atom is now left with one unhybridised p orbital perpendicular to the plane of the ring as shown below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13505,45,505,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image47.png'),
(13506,45,506,'paragraph',NULL,'The unhybridised p orbital of carbon atoms are close enough to form a π bond by lateral overlap. There are two equal possibilities of forming three π bonds by overlap of p orbitals of C 1 –C 2 , C 3 – C 4 , C 5 – C 6 or C 2 – C 3 , C 4 – C 5 , C 6 – C 1 respectively as shown in the following figures.','[{"s": "The unhybridised p orbital of carbon atoms are close enough to form a π bond by lateral overlap. There are two equal possibilities of forming three π bonds by overlap of p orbitals of C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively as shown in the following figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13507,45,507,'paragraph',NULL,'Structures shown in Fig. 13.7(a) and (b) correspond to two Kekulé’s structure with localised π bonds. The internuclear distance between all the carbon atoms in the ring has been determined by the X-ray diffraction to be the same; there is equal probability for the p orbital of each carbon atom to overlap with the p orbitals of adjacent carbon atoms [Fig. 13.7 (c)]. This can be represented in the form of two doughtnuts (rings) of electron clouds [Fig. 13.7 (d)], one above and one below the plane of the hexagonal ring as shown below:','[{"s": "Structures shown in Fig. 13.7(a) and (b) correspond to two Kekulé’s structure with localised π bonds. The internuclear distance between all the carbon atoms in the ring has been determined by the X-ray diffraction to be the same; there is equal probability for the p orbital of each carbon atom to overlap with the p orbitals of adjacent carbon atoms [Fig. 13.7 (c)]. This can be represented in the form of two doughtnuts (rings) of electron clouds [Fig. 13.7 (d)], one above and one below the plane of the hexagonal ring as shown below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13508,45,508,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image74.png'),
(13509,45,509,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.7 (a)','[{"s": "Fig. 13.7 (a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13510,45,510,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image94.png'),
(13511,45,511,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.7 (b)','[{"s": "Fig. 13.7 (b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13512,45,512,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image194.png'),
(13513,45,513,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.7 (c) Fig. 13.7 (d)','[{"s": "Fig. 13.7 (c) Fig. 13.7 (d)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13514,45,514,'paragraph',NULL,'(electron cloud)','[{"s": "(electron cloud)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13515,45,515,'paragraph',NULL,'X-Ray diffraction data reveals that benzene is a planar molecule. Had any one of the above structures of benzene (A or B) been correct, two types of C—C bond lengths were expected. However, X-ray data indicates that all the six C—C bond lengths are of the same order (139 pm) which is intermediate between C— C single bond (154 pm) and C—C double bond (133 pm). Thus the absence of pure double bond in benzene accounts for the reluctance of benzene to show addition reactions under normal conditions, thus explaining the unusual behaviour of benzene.','[{"s": "X-Ray diffraction data reveals that benzene is a planar molecule. Had any one of the above structures of benzene (A or B) been correct, two types of C—C bond lengths were expected. However, X-ray data indicates that all the six C—C bond lengths are of the same order (139 pm) which is intermediate between C— C single bond (154 pm) and C—C double bond (133 pm). Thus the absence of pure double bond in benzene accounts for the reluctance of benzene to show addition reactions under normal conditions, thus explaining the unusual behaviour of benzene.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13516,45,516,'heading',4,'13.5.3 Aromaticity',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13517,45,517,'paragraph',NULL,'Benzene was considered as parent ‘aromatic’ compound. Now, the name is applied to all the ring systems whether or not having benzene ring, possessing following characteristics.','[{"s": "Benzene was considered as parent ‘aromatic’ compound. Now, the name is applied to all the ring systems whether or not having benzene ring, possessing following characteristics.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13518,45,518,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Planarity','[{"s": "(i) Planarity", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13519,45,519,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Complete delocalisation of the π electrons in the ring','[{"s": "(ii) Complete delocalisation of the π electrons in the ring", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13520,45,520,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Presence of (4n + 2) π electrons in the ring where n is an integer (n = 0, 1, 2, . . .).','[{"s": "(iii) Presence of (4n + 2) π electrons in the ring where n is an integer (n = 0, 1, 2, . . .).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13521,45,521,'paragraph',NULL,'This is often referred to as Hückel Rule .','[{"s": "This is often referred to as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hückel Rule", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13522,45,522,'paragraph',NULL,'Some examples of aromatic compounds are given below:','[{"s": "Some examples of aromatic compounds are given below:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["130894"]',NULL),
(13523,45,523,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image50.png'),
(13524,45,524,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image71.png'),
(13525,45,525,'heading',4,'13.5.4 Preparation of Benzene',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13526,45,526,'paragraph',NULL,'Benzene is commercially isolated from coal tar. However, it may be prepared in the laboratory by the following methods.','[{"s": "Benzene is commercially isolated from coal tar. However, it may be prepared in the laboratory by the following methods.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13527,45,527,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Cyclic polymerisation of ethyne: (Section 13.4.4)','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cyclic polymerisation of ethyne:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Section 13.4.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13528,45,528,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Decarboxylation of aromatic acids: Sodium salt of benzoic acid on heating with sodalime gives benzene.','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Decarboxylation of aromatic acids:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Sodium salt of benzoic acid on heating with sodalime gives benzene.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13529,45,529,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image98.png (13.70)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image98.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.70)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13530,45,530,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Reduction of phenol: Phenol is reduced to benzene by passing its vapours over heated zinc dust','[{"s": "(iii) Reduction of phenol: Phenol is reduced to benzene by passing its vapours over heated zinc dust", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13531,45,531,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image165.png (13.71)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image165.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.71)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13532,45,532,'heading',4,'13.5.5 Properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13533,45,533,'heading',4,'Physical properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13534,45,534,'paragraph',NULL,'Aromatic hydrocarbons are non- polar molecules and are usually colourless liquids or solids with a characteristic aroma. You are also familiar with naphthalene balls which are used in toilets and for preservation of clothes because of unique smell of the compound and the moth repellent property. Aromatic hydrocarbons are immiscible with water but are readily miscible with organic solvents. They burn with sooty flame.','[{"s": "Aromatic hydrocarbons are non- polar molecules and are usually colourless liquids or solids with a characteristic aroma. You are also familiar with naphthalene balls which are used in toilets and for preservation of clothes because of unique smell of the compound and the moth repellent property. Aromatic hydrocarbons are immiscible with water but are readily miscible with organic solvents. They burn with sooty flame.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13535,45,535,'heading',4,'Chemical properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13536,45,536,'paragraph',NULL,'Arenes are characterised by electrophilic substitution reactions. However, under special conditions they can also undergo addition and oxidation reactions.','[{"s": "Arenes are characterised by electrophilic substitution reactions.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "However, under special conditions they can also undergo addition and oxidation reactions.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["130885"]',NULL),
(13537,45,537,'heading',4,'Electrophilic substitution reactions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13538,45,538,'paragraph',NULL,'The common electrophilic substitution reactions of arenes are nitration, halogenation, sulphonation, Friedel Craft’s alkylation and acylation reactions in which attacking reagent is an electrophile (E+) 5','[{"s": "The common electrophilic substitution reactions of arenes are nitration, halogenation, sulphonation, Friedel Craft’s alkylation and acylation reactions in which attacking reagent is an electrophile (E+)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "5", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["130874", "130875", "130878", "131524", "131525"]',NULL),
(13539,45,539,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Nitration: A nitro group is introduced into benzene ring when benzene is heated with a mixture of concentrated nitric acid and concentrated sulphuric acid (nitrating mixture).','[{"s": "(i) Nitration:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A nitro group is introduced into benzene ring when benzene is heated with a mixture of concentrated nitric acid and concentrated sulphuric acid (nitrating mixture).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13540,45,540,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image169.png (13.72)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image169.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.72)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13541,45,541,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitrobenzene','[{"s": "Nitrobenzene", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13542,45,542,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Halogenation: Arenes react with halogens in the presence of a Lewis acid like anhydrous FeCl 3 , FeBr 3 or AlCl 3 to yield haloarenes. 2','[{"s": "(ii) Halogenation:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Arenes react with halogens in the presence of a Lewis acid like anhydrous FeCl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", FeBr", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "or AlCl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "to yield haloarenes.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["130873", "131526"]',NULL),
(13543,45,543,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image104.png'),
(13544,45,544,'paragraph',NULL,'Chlorobenzene','[{"s": "Chlorobenzene", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13545,45,545,'paragraph',NULL,'(13.73)','[{"s": "(13.73)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13546,45,546,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Sulphonation: The replacement of a hydrogen atom by a sulphonic acid group in a ring is called sulphonation. It is carried out by heating benzene with fuming sulphuric acid (oleum).','[{"s": "(iii) Sulphonation:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The replacement of a hydrogen atom by a sulphonic acid group in a ring is called sulphonation. It is carried out by heating benzene with fuming sulphuric acid (oleum).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13547,45,547,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image72.png (13.74)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image72.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.74)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13548,45,548,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Friedel-Crafts alkylation reaction: When benzene is treated with an alkyl halide in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, alkylbenene is formed.','[{"s": "(iv) Friedel-Crafts alkylation reaction:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When benzene is treated with an alkyl halide in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, alkylbenene is formed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13549,45,549,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image85.png (13.75)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image85.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.75)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13550,45,550,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image164.png (13.76)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image164.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.76)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13551,45,551,'paragraph',NULL,'Why do we get isopropyl benzene on treating benzene with 1-chloropropane instead of n-propyl benzene?','[{"s": "Why do we get isopropyl benzene on treating benzene with 1-chloropropane instead of n-propyl benzene?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13552,45,552,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction: The reaction of benzene with an acyl halide or acid anhydride in the presence of Lewis acids (AlCl 3 ) yields acyl benzene.','[{"s": "(v) Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The reaction of benzene with an acyl halide or acid anhydride in the presence of Lewis acids (AlCl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") yields acyl benzene.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["130882"]',NULL),
(13553,45,553,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image199.png (13.77)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image199.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.77)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13554,45,554,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image187.png (13.78)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image187.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.78)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13555,45,555,'paragraph',NULL,'If excess of electrophilic reagent is used, further substitution reaction may take place in which other hydrogen atoms of benzene ring may also be successively replaced by the electrophile. For example, benzene on treatment with excess of chlorine in the presence of anhydrous AlCl3 can be chlorinated to hexachlorobenzene (C 6 Cl 6 )','[{"s": "If excess of electrophilic reagent is used, further substitution reaction may take place in which other hydrogen atoms of benzene ring may also be successively replaced by the electrophile. For example, benzene on treatment with excess of chlorine in the presence of anhydrous AlCl3 can be chlorinated to hexachlorobenzene (C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13556,45,556,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image154.png (13.79)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image154.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.79)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13557,45,557,'heading',4,'Mechanism of electrophilic substitution reactions:',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13558,45,558,'paragraph',NULL,'According to experimental evidences, SE (S = substitution; E = electrophilic) reactions are supposed to proceed via the following three steps:','[{"s": "According to experimental evidences, SE (S = substitution; E = electrophilic) reactions are supposed to proceed via the following three steps:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13559,45,559,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Generation of the eletrophile','[{"s": "(a) Generation of the eletrophile", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13560,45,560,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Formation of carbocation intermediate','[{"s": "(b) Formation of carbocation intermediate", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13561,45,561,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Removal of proton from the carbocation intermediate','[{"s": "(c) Removal of proton from the carbocation intermediate", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13562,45,562,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Generation of electrophile E⊕: During chlorination, alkylation and acylation of benzene, anhydrous AlCl 3 , being a Lewis acid helps in generation of the elctrophile Cl⊕, R⊕, RC⊕O (acylium ion) respectively by combining with the attacking reagent.','[{"s": "(a) Generation of electrophile E⊕:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "During chlorination, alkylation and acylation of benzene, anhydrous AlCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", being a Lewis acid helps in generation of the elctrophile Cl⊕, R⊕, RC⊕O (acylium ion) respectively by combining with the attacking reagent.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13563,45,563,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image6.png'),
(13564,45,564,'paragraph',NULL,'In the case of nitration, the electrophile, nitronium ion, Hydrocarbons-image54.png is produced by transfer of a proton (from sulphuric acid) to nitric acid in the following manner:','[{"s": "In the case of nitration, the electrophile, nitronium ion,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hydrocarbons-image54.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is produced by transfer of a proton (from sulphuric acid) to nitric acid in the following manner:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
