(13587,45,587,'paragraph',NULL,'Combustion: When heated in air, benzene burns with sooty flame producing CO2 and H2O','[{"s": "Combustion:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When heated in air, benzene burns with sooty flame producing CO2 and H2O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13588,45,588,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons-image36.png (13.82)','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons-image36.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.82)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13589,45,589,'paragraph',NULL,'General combustion reaction for any hydrocarbon may be given by the following chemical equation:','[{"s": "General combustion reaction for any hydrocarbon may be given by the following chemical equation:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13590,45,590,'paragraph',NULL,'CxHy + (x + Hydrocarbons-image177.png ) O2 → x CO2 + Hydrocarbons-image185.png H2O (13.83)','[{"s": "CxHy + (x +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hydrocarbons-image177.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ") O2 → x CO2 +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hydrocarbons-image185.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "H2O (13.83)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13591,45,591,'heading',4,'13.5.6 Directive influence of a functional group in monosubstituted benzene',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13592,45,592,'paragraph',NULL,'When monosubstituted benzene is subjected to further substitution, three possible disubstituted products are not formed in equal amounts. Two types of behaviour are observed. Either ortho and para products or meta product is predominantly formed. It has also been observed that this behaviour depends on the nature of the substituent already present in the benzene ring and not on the nature of the entering group. This is known as directive influence of substituents. Reasons for ortho/para or meta directive nature of groups are discussed below:','[{"s": "When monosubstituted benzene is subjected to further substitution, three possible disubstituted products are not formed in equal amounts. Two types of behaviour are observed. Either ortho and para products or meta product is predominantly formed. It has also been observed that this behaviour depends on the nature of the substituent already present in the benzene ring and not on the nature of the entering group. This is known as directive influence of substituents. Reasons for ortho/para or meta directive nature of groups are discussed below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13593,45,593,'paragraph',NULL,'Ortho and para directing groups: The groups which direct the incoming group to ortho and para positions are called ortho and para directing groups.','[{"s": "Ortho and para directing groups:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The groups which direct the incoming group to ortho and para positions are called ortho and para directing groups.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]','marked','["130877"]',NULL),
(13594,45,594,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image86.png'),
(13595,45,595,'paragraph',NULL,'It is clear from the above resonating structures that the electron density is more on o – and p – positions. Hence, the substitution takes place mainly at these positions. However, it may be noted that –I effect of – OH group also operates due to which the electron density on ortho and para positions of the benzene ring is slightly reduced. But the overall electron density increases at these positions of the ring due to resonance. Therefore, –OH group activates the benzene ring for the attack by an electrophile. Other examples of activating groups are –NH 2 , –NHR, –NHCOCH 3 , –OCH 3 , –CH 3 , –C 2 H 5 , etc.','[{"s": "It is clear from the above resonating structures that the electron density is more on o – and p – positions. Hence, the substitution takes place mainly at these positions. However, it may be noted that –I effect of – OH group also operates due to which the electron density on ortho and para positions of the benzene ring is slightly reduced. But the overall electron density increases at these positions of the ring due to resonance. Therefore, –OH group activates the benzene ring for the attack by an electrophile. Other examples of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "activating", "t": "text"}, {"s": "groups are –NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", –NHR, –NHCOCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", –OCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", –CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", –C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13596,45,596,'paragraph',NULL,'In the case of aryl halides, halogens are moderately deactivating . Because of their strong – I effect, overall electron density on benzene ring decreases. It makes further substitution difficult. However, due to resonance the electron density on o– and p– positions is greater than that at the m-position. Hence, they are also o– and p– directing groups. Resonance structures of chlorobenzene are given below:','[{"s": "In the case of aryl halides, halogens are moderately", "t": "text"}, {"s": "deactivating", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Because of their strong – I effect, overall electron density on benzene ring decreases. It makes further substitution difficult. However, due to resonance the electron density on o– and p– positions is greater than that at the m-position. Hence, they are also o– and p– directing groups. Resonance structures of chlorobenzene are given below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13597,45,597,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image84.png'),
(13598,45,598,'paragraph',NULL,'Meta directing group: The groups which direct the incoming group to meta position are called meta directing groups. Some examples of meta directing groups are –NO 2 , –CN, –CHO, –COR, –COOH, –COOR, –SO 3 H, etc.','[{"s": "Meta directing group:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The groups which direct the incoming group to meta position are called meta directing groups. Some examples of meta directing groups are –NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", –CN, –CHO, –COR, –COOH, –COOR, –SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H, etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13599,45,599,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us take the example of nitro group. Nitro group reduces the electron density in the benzene ring due to its strong–I effect. Nitrobenzene is a resonance hybrid of the following structures.','[{"s": "Let us take the example of nitro group. Nitro group reduces the electron density in the benzene ring due to its strong–I effect. Nitrobenzene is a resonance hybrid of the following structures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13600,45,600,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image22.png'),
(13601,45,601,'paragraph',NULL,'In this case, the overall electron density on benzene ring decreases making further substitution difficult, therefore these groups are also called ‘deactivating groups’. The electron density on o– and p– position is comparatively less than that at meta position. Hence, the electrophile attacks on comparatively electron rich meta position resulting in meta substitution.','[{"s": "In this case, the overall electron density on benzene ring decreases making further substitution difficult, therefore these groups are also called ‘deactivating groups’. The electron density on o– and p– position is comparatively less than that at meta position. Hence, the electrophile attacks on comparatively electron rich meta position resulting in meta substitution.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13602,45,602,'heading',3,'13.6 CARCINOGENICITY AND TOXICITY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13603,45,603,'paragraph',NULL,'Benzene and polynuclear hydrocarbons containing more than two benzene rings fused together are toxic and said to possess cancer producing (carcinogenic) property. Such polynuclear hydrocarbons are formed on incomplete combustion of organic materials like tobacco, coal and petroleum. They enter into human body and undergo various biochemical reactions and finally damage DNA and cause cancer. Some of the carcinogenic hydrocarbons are given below (see box).','[{"s": "Benzene and polynuclear hydrocarbons containing more than two benzene rings fused together are toxic and said to possess cancer producing (carcinogenic) property. Such polynuclear hydrocarbons are formed on incomplete combustion of organic materials like tobacco, coal and petroleum. They enter into human body and undergo various biochemical reactions and finally damage DNA and cause cancer. Some of the carcinogenic hydrocarbons are given below (see box).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13604,45,604,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Hydrocarbons-image46.png'),
(13605,45,605,'paragraph',NULL,'SUMMARY','[{"s": "SUMMARY", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13606,45,606,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrocarbons are the compounds of carbon and hydrogen only. Hydrocarbons are mainly obtained from coal and petroleum, which are the major sources of energy. Petrochemicals are the prominent starting materials used for the manufacture of a large number of commercially important products. LPG (liquefied petroleum gas) and CNG (compressed natural gas), the main sources of energy for domestic fuels and the automobile industry, are obtained from petroleum. Hydrocarbons are classified as open chain saturated (alkanes) and unsaturated (alkenes and alkynes), cyclic (alicyclic) and aromatic, according to their structure.','[{"s": "Hydrocarbons are the compounds of carbon and hydrogen only. Hydrocarbons are mainly obtained from coal and petroleum, which are the major sources of energy. Petrochemicals are the prominent starting materials used for the manufacture of a large number of commercially important products. LPG (liquefied petroleum gas) and CNG (compressed natural gas), the main sources of energy for domestic fuels and the automobile industry, are obtained from petroleum. Hydrocarbons are classified as open chain saturated (alkanes) and unsaturated (alkenes and alkynes), cyclic (alicyclic) and aromatic, according to their structure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13607,45,607,'paragraph',NULL,'The important reactions of alkanes are free radical substitution, combustion, oxidation and aromatization. Alkenes and alkynes undergo addition reactions, which are mainly electrophilic additions. Aromatic hydrocarbons, despite having unsaturation, undergo mainly electrophilic substitution reactions. These undergo addition reactions only under special conditions.','[{"s": "The important reactions of alkanes are free radical substitution, combustion, oxidation and aromatization. Alkenes and alkynes undergo addition reactions, which are mainly electrophilic additions. Aromatic hydrocarbons, despite having unsaturation, undergo mainly electrophilic substitution reactions. These undergo addition reactions only under special conditions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(13608,45,608,'paragraph',NULL,'Benzene and benzenoid compounds show aromatic character. Aromaticity, the property of being aromatic is possessed by compounds having specific electronic structure characterised by Hückel (4n+2)π electron rule. The nature of groups or substituents attached to benzene ring is responsible for activation or deactivation of the benzene ring towards further electrophilic substitution and also for orientation of the incoming group. Some of the polynuclear hydrocarbons having fused benzene ring system have carcinogenic property.','[{"s": "Benzene and benzenoid compounds show aromatic character. Aromaticity, the property of being aromatic is possessed by compounds having specific electronic structure characterised by Hückel (4n+2)π electron rule. The nature of groups or substituents attached to benzene ring is responsible for activation or deactivation of the benzene ring towards further electrophilic substitution and also for orientation of the incoming group. Some of the polynuclear hydrocarbons having fused benzene ring system have carcinogenic property.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
