(14885,46,234,'paragraph',NULL,'• The functional group present is an alcohol (OH). Hence the suffix is ‘-ol’.','[{"s": "• The functional group present is an alcohol (OH). Hence the suffix is ‘-ol’.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14886,46,235,'paragraph',NULL,'• The longest chain containing -OH has eight carbon atoms. Hence the corresponding saturated hydrocarbon is octane.','[{"s": "• The longest chain containing -OH has eight carbon atoms. Hence the corresponding saturated hydrocarbon is octane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14887,46,236,'paragraph',NULL,'• The -OH is on carbon atom 3. In addition, a methyl group is attached at 6th carbon.','[{"s": "• The -OH is on carbon atom 3. In addition, a methyl group is attached at 6th carbon.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14888,46,237,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence, the systematic name of this compound is 6-Methyloctan-3-ol.','[{"s": "Hence, the systematic name of this compound is 6-Methyloctan-3-ol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14889,46,238,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image117.png'),
(14890,46,239,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14891,46,240,'paragraph',NULL,'The functional group present is ketone (>C=O), hence suffix ‘-one’. Presence of two keto groups is indicated by ‘di’, hence suffix becomes ‘dione’. The two keto groups are at carbons 2 and 4. The longest chain contains 6 carbon atoms, hence, parent hydrocarbon is hexane. Thus, the systematic name is Hexane-2,4-dione.','[{"s": "The functional group present is ketone (>C=O), hence suffix ‘-one’. Presence of two keto groups is indicated by ‘di’, hence suffix becomes ‘dione’. The two keto groups are at carbons 2 and 4. The longest chain contains 6 carbon atoms, hence, parent hydrocarbon is hexane. Thus, the systematic name is Hexane-2,4-dione.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14892,46,241,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image40.png'),
(14893,46,242,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14894,46,243,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image29.png'),
(14895,46,244,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14896,46,245,'paragraph',NULL,'The two C=C functional groups are present at carbon atoms 1 and 3, while the C≡C functional group is present at carbon 5. T hese groups are indicated by suffixes ‘diene’ and ‘yne’ respectively. The longest chain containing the functional groups has 6 carbon atoms; hence the parent hydrocarbon is hexane. The name of compound, therefore, is Hexa-1,3-dien-5-yne.','[{"s": "The two C=C functional groups are present at carbon atoms 1 and 3, while the C≡C functional group is present at carbon 5.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hese groups are indicated by suffixes ‘diene’ and ‘yne’ respectively. The longest chain containing the functional groups has 6 carbon atoms; hence the parent hydrocarbon is hexane. The name of compound, therefore, is Hexa-1,3-dien-5-yne.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["121318"]',NULL),
(14897,46,246,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 12.9','[{"s": "Problem 12.9", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14898,46,247,'paragraph',NULL,'Derive the structure of (i) 2-Chlorohexane, (ii) Pent-4-en-2-ol, (iii) 3- Nitrocyclohexene, (iv) Cyclohex-2-en-1-ol, (v) 6-Hydroxy-heptanal.','[{"s": "Derive the structure of (i) 2-Chlorohexane, (ii) Pent-4-en-2-ol, (iii) 3- Nitrocyclohexene, (iv) Cyclohex-2-en-1-ol, (v) 6-Hydroxy-heptanal.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["121319"]',NULL),
(14899,46,248,'paragraph',NULL,'\\Solution','[{"s": "\\\\Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14900,46,249,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) 3 CH 2 CH 2 CH 2 CH(Cl)CH 3 .','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(Cl)CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14901,46,250,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) ‘pent’ indicates that parent hydrocarbon contains 5 carbon atoms in the chain. ‘en’ and ‘ol’ correspond to the functional groups C=C and -OH at carbon atoms 4 and 2 respectively. Thus, the structure is','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "‘pent’ indicates that parent hydrocarbon contains 5 carbon atoms in the chain. ‘en’ and ‘ol’ correspond to the functional groups C=C and -OH at carbon atoms 4 and 2 respectively. Thus, the structure is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14902,46,251,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 2 =CHCH 2 CH (OH)CH 3 .','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=CHCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH (OH)CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14903,46,252,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Six membered ring containing a carbon-carbon double bond is implied by cyclohexene, which is numbered as shown in (I). The prefix 3-nitro means that a nitro group is present on C-3. Thus, complete structural formula of the compound is (II). Double bond is suffixed functional group whereas NO 2 is prefixed functional group therefore double bond gets preference over –NO2 group:','[{"s": "(iii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Six membered ring containing a carbon-carbon double bond is implied by cyclohexene, which is numbered as shown in (I). The prefix 3-nitro means that a nitro group is present on C-3. Thus, complete structural formula of the compound is (II). Double bond is suffixed functional group whereas NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is prefixed functional group therefore double bond gets preference over –NO2 group:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14904,46,253,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image60.png'),
(14905,46,254,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv)','[{"s": "(iv)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14906,46,255,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image187.png'),
(14907,46,256,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) 3 CH(OH)CH 2 CH 2 CH 2 CH 2 CHO. Carbon atom of –CHO group is included while numbering the carbon chain.','[{"s": "(v)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH(OH)CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CHO. Carbon atom of –CHO group is included while numbering the carbon chain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14908,46,257,'heading',4,'12.5.4 Nomenclature of Substituted Benzene Compounds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14909,46,258,'paragraph',NULL,'For IUPAC nomenclature of substituted benzene compounds, the substituent is placed as prefix to the word benzene as shown in the following examples. However, common names (written in bracket below) of many substituted benzene compounds are also universally used.','[{"s": "For IUPAC nomenclature of substituted benzene compounds, the substituent is placed as prefix to the word benzene as shown in the following examples. However, common names (written in bracket below) of many substituted benzene compounds are also universally used.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14910,46,259,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image77.png'),
(14911,46,260,'paragraph',NULL,'Methylbenzene Methoxybenzene Aminobenzene','[{"s": "Methylbenzene Methoxybenzene Aminobenzene", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14912,46,261,'paragraph',NULL,'(Toluene) (Anisole) (Aniline)','[{"s": "(Toluene) (Anisole) (Aniline)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14913,46,262,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image163.png'),
(14914,46,263,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitrobenzene Bromobenzene','[{"s": "Nitrobenzene Bromobenzene", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14915,46,264,'paragraph',NULL,'If benzene ring is disubstituted, the position of substituents is defined by numbering the carbon atoms of the ring such that the substituents are located at the lowest numbers possible.For example, the compound(b) is named as 1,3-dibromobenzene and not as 1,5-dibromobenzene.','[{"s": "If benzene ring is disubstituted, the position of substituents is defined by numbering the carbon atoms of the ring such that the substituents are located at the lowest numbers possible.For example, the compound(b) is named as 1,3-dibromobenzene and not as 1,5-dibromobenzene.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14916,46,265,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image12.png'),
(14917,46,266,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) 1,2-Dibromo-benzene (b) 1,3-Dibromo-benzene (c) 1,4-Dibromo-benzene','[{"s": "(a) 1,2-Dibromo-benzene (b) 1,3-Dibromo-benzene (c) 1,4-Dibromo-benzene", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14918,46,267,'paragraph',NULL,'In the trivial system of nomenclature the terms ortho (o), meta (m) and para (p) are used as prefixes to indicate the relative positions 1,2;1,3 and 1,4 respectively. Thus,1,3-dibromobenzene (b) is named as m-dibromobenzene (meta is abbreviated as m-) and the other isomers of dibromobenzene 1,2-(a) and 1,4-(c), are named as ortho (or just o-) and para (or just p-)-dibromobenzene, respectively.','[{"s": "In the trivial system of nomenclature the terms ortho (o), meta (m) and para (p) are used as prefixes to indicate the relative positions 1,2;1,3 and 1,4 respectively. Thus,1,3-dibromobenzene (b) is named as m-dibromobenzene (meta is abbreviated as m-) and the other isomers of dibromobenzene 1,2-(a) and 1,4-(c), are named as ortho (or just o-) and para (or just p-)-dibromobenzene, respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14919,46,268,'paragraph',NULL,'For tri - or higher substituted benzene derivatives, these prefixes cannot be used and the compounds are named by identifying substituent positions on the ring by following the lowest locant rule. In some cases, common name of benzene derivatives is taken as the base compound.','[{"s": "For tri - or higher substituted benzene derivatives, these prefixes cannot be used and the compounds are named by identifying substituent positions on the ring by following the lowest locant rule. In some cases, common name of benzene derivatives is taken as the base compound.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24133,61,145,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Its magnitude is given by the change in the magnitude of potential per unit displacement normal to the equipotential surface at the point.','[{"s": "(ii) Its magnitude is given by the change in the magnitude of potential per unit displacement normal to the equipotential surface at the point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14920,46,269,'paragraph',NULL,'Substituent of the base compound is assigned number1 and then the direction of numbering is chosen such that the next substituent gets the lowest number. The substituents appear in the name in alphabetical order. Some examples are given below.','[{"s": "Substituent of the base compound is assigned number1 and then the direction of numbering is chosen such that the next substituent gets the lowest number. The substituents appear in the name in alphabetical order. Some examples are given below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
