(24134,61,146,'heading',3,'2.6 EQUIPOTENTIAL SURFACES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(14995,46,344,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the electrophiles receive electron pair from the substrate when the two undergo bonding interaction . A curved-arrow notation is used to show the movement of an electron pair from the nucleophile to the electrophile. Some examples of nucleophiles are the negatively charged ions with lone pair of electrons such as hydroxide (HO – ), cyanide (NC – ) ions and carbanions (R3C: – Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image102.png etc., can also act as nucleophiles due to the presence of lone pair of electrons. Examples of electrophiles include carbocations ( Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image89.png ) and neutral molecules having functional groups like carbonyl group (>C=O) or alkyl halides (R3C-X, where X is a halogen atom). The carbon atom in carbocations has sextet configuration; hence, it is electron deficient and can receive a pair of electrons from the nucleophiles. In neutral molecules such as alkyl halides, due to the polarity of the C-X bond a partial positive charge is generated on the carbon atom and hence the carbon atom becomes an electrophilic centre at which a nucleophile can attack.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "Thus, the electrophiles receive electron pair from the substrate when the two undergo bonding interaction", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A curved-arrow notation is used to show the movement of an electron pair from the nucleophile to the electrophile. Some examples of nucleophiles are the negatively charged ions with lone pair of electrons such as hydroxide (HO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), cyanide (NC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") ions and carbanions (R3C:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image102.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "etc., can also act as nucleophiles due to the presence of lone pair of electrons. Examples of electrophiles include carbocations (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image89.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ") and neutral molecules having functional groups like carbonyl group (>C=O) or alkyl halides (R3C-X, where X is a halogen atom). The carbon atom in carbocations has sextet configuration; hence, it is electron deficient and can receive a pair of electrons from the nucleophiles. In neutral molecules such as alkyl halides, due to the polarity of the C-X bond a partial positive charge is generated on the carbon atom and hence the carbon atom becomes an electrophilic centre at which a nucleophile can attack.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["121367"]',NULL),
