(15011,46,360,'paragraph',NULL,'Among CH 3 HC*=O, H 3 3 C*–I, the starred carbon atoms are electrophilic centers as they will have partial positive charge due to polarity of the bond.','[{"s": "Among CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "HC*=O, H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C*–I, the starred carbon atoms are electrophilic centers as they will have partial positive charge due to polarity of the bond.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15012,46,361,'heading',4,'12.7.3 Electron Movement in Organic Reactions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15013,46,362,'paragraph',NULL,'The movement of electrons in organic reactions can be shown by curved-arrow notation. It shows how changes in bonding occur due to electronic redistribution during the reaction. To show the change in position of a pair of electrons, curved arrow starts from the point from where an electron pair is shifted and it ends at a location to which the pair of electron may move.','[{"s": "The movement of electrons in organic reactions can be shown by curved-arrow notation. It shows how changes in bonding occur due to electronic redistribution during the reaction. To show the change in position of a pair of electrons, curved arrow starts from the point from where an electron pair is shifted and it ends at a location to which the pair of electron may move.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15014,46,363,'paragraph',NULL,'Presentation of shifting of electron pair is given below :','[{"s": "Presentation of shifting of electron pair is given below :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15015,46,364,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image121.png from π bond to adjacent bond position','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image121.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "from π bond to adjacent bond position", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15016,46,365,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image145.png from π bond to adjacent atom','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image145.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "from π bond to adjacent atom", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15017,46,366,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image176.png from atom to adjacent bond position','[{"s": "(iii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image176.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "from atom to adjacent bond position", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15018,46,367,'paragraph',NULL,'Movement of single electron is indicated by a single barbed ‘fish hooks’ (i.e. half headed curved arrow). For example, in transfer of hydroxide ion giving ethanol and in the dissociation of chloromethane, the movement of electron using curved arrows can be depicted as follows:','[{"s": "Movement of single electron is indicated by a single barbed ‘fish hooks’ (i.e. half headed curved arrow). For example, in transfer of hydroxide ion giving ethanol and in the dissociation of chloromethane, the movement of electron using curved arrows can be depicted as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15019,46,368,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image26.png'),
(15020,46,369,'heading',4,'12.7.4 Electron Displacement Effects in Covalent Bonds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15021,46,370,'paragraph',NULL,'The electron displacement in an organic molecule may take place either in the ground state under the influence of an atom or a substituent group or in the presence of an appropriate attacking reagent. The electron displacements due to the influence of an atom or a substituent group present in the molecule cause permanent polarlisation of the bond. Inductive effect and resonance effects are examples of this type of electron displacements. Temporary electron displacement effects are seen in a molecule when a reagent approaches to attack it. This type of electron displacement is called electromeric effect or polarisability effect. In the following sections we will learn about these types of electronic displacements.','[{"s": "The electron displacement in an organic molecule may take place either in the ground state under the influence of an atom or a substituent group or in the presence of an appropriate attacking reagent. The electron displacements due to the influence of an atom or a substituent group present in the molecule cause permanent polarlisation of the bond. Inductive effect and resonance effects are examples of this type of electron displacements. Temporary electron displacement effects are seen in a molecule when a reagent approaches to attack it. This type of electron displacement is called electromeric effect or polarisability effect. In the following sections we will learn about these types of electronic displacements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15022,46,371,'heading',4,'12.7.5 Inductive Effect',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15023,46,372,'paragraph',NULL,'When a covalent bond is formed between atoms of different electronegativity, the electron density is more towards the more electronegative atom of the bond. Such a shift of electron density results in a polar covalent bond. Bond polarity leads to various electronic effects in organic compounds.','[{"s": "When a covalent bond is formed between atoms of different electronegativity, the electron density is more towards the more electronegative atom of the bond. Such a shift of electron density results in a polar covalent bond. Bond polarity leads to various electronic effects in organic compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15024,46,373,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us consider cholorethane (CH 3 CH 2 Cl) in which the C–Cl bond is a polar covalent bond. It is polarised in such a way that the carbon-1 gains some positive charge (δ+) and the chlorine some negative charge (δ–). The fractional electronic charges on the two atoms in a polar covalent bond are denoted by symbol δ (delta) and the shift of electron density is shown by an arrow that points from δ+ to δ– end of the polar bond.','[{"s": "Let us consider cholorethane (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl) in which the C–Cl bond is a polar covalent bond. It is polarised in such a way that the carbon-1 gains some positive charge (δ+) and the chlorine some negative charge (δ–). The fractional electronic charges on the two atoms in a polar covalent bond are denoted by symbol δ (delta) and the shift of electron density is shown by an arrow that points from δ+ to δ– end of the polar bond.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15025,46,374,'image',NULL,'image14',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image16.jpg'),
(15026,46,375,'paragraph',NULL,'In turn carbon-1, which has developed partial positive charge (δ+) draws some electron density towards it from the adjacent C-C bond. Consequently, some positive charge (δδ+) develops on carbon-2 also, where δδ+ symbolises relatively smaller positive charge as compared to that on carbon – 1. In other words, the polar C – Cl bond induces polarity in the adjacent bonds. Such polarisation of σ-bond caused by the polarisation of adjacent σ-bond is referred to as the inductive effect . This effect is passed on to the subsequent bonds also but the effect decreases rapidly as the number of intervening bonds increases and becomes vanishingly small after three bonds. The inductive effect is related to the ability of substituent(s) to either withdraw or donate electron density to the attached carbon atom. 2 Based on this ability, the substitutents can be classified as electron-withdrawing or electron donating groups relative to hydrogen. Halogens and many other groups such as nitro (- NO 2 ), cyano (- CN), carboxy (- COOH), ester (COOR), aryloxy (-OAr, e.g. – OC 6 H 5 ), etc. are electron-withdrawing groups. On the other hand, the alkyl groups like methyl (–CH 3 2 –CH 3 ) are usually considered as electron donating groups.','[{"s": "In turn carbon-1, which has developed partial positive charge (δ+) draws some electron density towards it from the adjacent C-C bond. Consequently, some positive charge (δδ+) develops on carbon-2 also, where δδ+ symbolises relatively smaller positive charge as compared to that on carbon – 1. In other words, the polar C – Cl bond induces polarity in the adjacent bonds. Such polarisation of σ-bond caused by the polarisation of adjacent σ-bond is referred to as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "inductive effect", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This effect is passed on to the subsequent bonds also but the effect decreases rapidly as the number of intervening bonds increases and becomes vanishingly small after three bonds.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The inductive effect is related to the ability of substituent(s) to either withdraw or donate electron density to the attached carbon atom.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Based on this ability, the substitutents can be classified as electron-withdrawing or electron donating groups relative to hydrogen. Halogens and many other groups such as nitro (- NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), cyano (- CN), carboxy (- COOH), ester (COOR), aryloxy (-OAr, e.g. – OC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), etc. are electron-withdrawing groups. On the other hand, the alkyl groups like methyl (–CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") are usually considered as electron donating groups.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["121368", "121377"]',NULL),
