(15041,46,390,'paragraph',NULL,'Benzene','[{"s": "Benzene", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15042,46,391,'paragraph',NULL,'benzene. Its cyclic structure containing alternating C–C single and C=C double bonds shown is inadequate for explaining its characteristic properties.','[{"s": "benzene. Its cyclic structure containing alternating C–C single and C=C double bonds shown is inadequate for explaining its characteristic properties.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15043,46,392,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image48.png'),
(15044,46,393,'paragraph',NULL,'As per the above representation, benzene should exhibit two different bond lengths, due to C–C single and C=C double bonds. However, as determined experimentally benzene has a uniform C–C bond distances of 139 pm, a value intermediate between the C–C single(154 pm) and C=C double (134 pm) bonds. Thus, the structure of benzene cannot be represented adequately by the above structure. Further, benzene can be represented equally well by the energetically identical structures I and II.','[{"s": "As per the above representation, benzene should exhibit two different bond lengths, due to C–C single and C=C double bonds. However, as determined experimentally benzene has a uniform C–C bond distances of 139 pm, a value intermediate between the C–C single(154 pm) and C=C double (134 pm) bonds. Thus, the structure of benzene cannot be represented adequately by the above structure. Further, benzene can be represented equally well by the energetically identical structures I and II.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15045,46,394,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, according to the resonance theory (Unit 4) the actual structure of benzene cannot be adequately represented by any of these structures, rather it is a hybrid of the two structures (I and II) called resonance structures. The resonance structures (canonical structures or contributing structures) are hypothetical and individually do not represent any real molecule . They contribute to the actual structure in proportion to their stability.','[{"s": "Therefore, according to the resonance theory (Unit 4) the actual structure of benzene cannot be adequately represented by any of these structures, rather it is a hybrid of the two structures (I and II) called resonance structures.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The resonance structures (canonical structures or contributing structures) are hypothetical and individually do not represent any real molecule", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". They contribute to the actual structure in proportion to their stability.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15046,46,395,'paragraph',NULL,'Another example of resonance is provided by nitromethane (CH 3 NO 2 ) which can be represented by two Lewis structures, (I and II). There are two types of N-O bonds in these structures.','[{"s": "Another example of resonance is provided by nitromethane (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") which can be represented by two Lewis structures, (I and II). There are two types of N-O bonds in these structures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15047,46,396,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image69.png'),
(15048,46,397,'paragraph',NULL,'However, it is known that the two N–O bonds of nitromethane are of the same length (intermediate between a N–O single bond and a N=O double bond). The actual structure of nitromethane is therefore a resonance hybrid of the two canonical forms I and II.','[{"s": "However, it is known that the two N–O bonds of nitromethane are of the same length (intermediate between a N–O single bond and a N=O double bond). The actual structure of nitromethane is therefore a resonance hybrid of the two canonical forms I and II.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15049,46,398,'paragraph',NULL,'The energy of actual structure of the molecule (the resonance hybrid) is lower than that of any of the canonical structures. The difference in energy between the actual structure and the lowest energy resonance structure is called the resonance stabilization energy or simply the resonance energy . The more the number of important contributing structures, the more is the resonance energy. Resonance is particularly important when the contributing structures are equivalent in energy.','[{"s": "The energy of actual structure of the molecule (the resonance hybrid) is lower than that of any of the canonical structures. The difference in energy between the actual structure and the lowest energy resonance structure is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "resonance stabilization energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or simply the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "resonance energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The more the number of important contributing structures, the more is the resonance energy. Resonance is particularly important when the contributing structures are equivalent in energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15050,46,399,'paragraph',NULL,'The following rules are applied while writing resonance structures:','[{"s": "The following rules are applied while writing resonance structures:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20503,54,141,'paragraph',NULL,'For finite changes at constant pressure, we can write equation 6.7 as','[{"s": "For finite changes at constant pressure, we can write equation 6.7 as", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60954"]',NULL),
(20504,54,142,'paragraph',NULL,'∆H = ∆U + ∆pV','[{"s": "∆H = ∆U + ∆pV", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60954"]',NULL),
(20505,54,143,'paragraph',NULL,'Since p is constant, we can write','[{"s": "Since p is constant, we can write", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60954", "60955"]',NULL),
(15051,46,400,'paragraph',NULL,'The resonance structures have (i) the same positions of nuclei and (ii) the same number of unpaired electrons. Among the resonance structures, the one which has more number of covalent bonds, all the atoms with octet of electrons (except hydrogen which has a duplet), less separation of opposite charges, (a negative charge if any on more electronegative atom, a positive charge if any on more electropositive atom) and more dispersal of charge, is more stable than others.','[{"s": "The resonance structures have (i) the same positions of nuclei and (ii) the same number of unpaired electrons. Among the resonance structures, the one which has more number of covalent bonds, all the atoms with octet of electrons (except hydrogen which has a duplet), less separation of opposite charges, (a negative charge if any on more electronegative atom, a positive charge if any on more electropositive atom) and more dispersal of charge, is more stable than others.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15052,46,401,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 12.16','[{"s": "Problem 12.16", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15053,46,402,'paragraph',NULL,'Write resonance structures of CH 3 COO– and show the movement of electrons by curved arrows.','[{"s": "Write resonance structures of CH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "COO– and show the movement of electrons by curved arrows.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["121369"]',NULL),
(15054,46,403,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15055,46,404,'paragraph',NULL,'First, write the structure and put unshared pairs of valence electrons on appropriate atoms. Then draw the arrows one at a time moving the electrons to get the other structures.','[{"s": "First, write the structure and put unshared pairs of valence electrons on appropriate atoms. Then draw the arrows one at a time moving the electrons to get the other structures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15056,46,405,'paragraph',NULL,'Stability: I > II > III','[{"s": "Stability: I > II > III", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
