(15062,46,411,'paragraph',NULL,'[I: Most stable, more number of covalent bonds, each carbon and oxygen atom has an octet and no separation of opposite charge II: negative charge on more electronegative atom and positive charge on more electropositive atom; III: does not contribute as oxygen has positive charge and carbon has negative charge, hence least stable].','[{"s": "[I: Most stable, more number of covalent bonds, each carbon and oxygen atom has an octet and no separation of opposite charge II: negative charge on more electronegative atom and positive charge on more electropositive atom; III: does not contribute as oxygen has positive charge and carbon has negative charge, hence least stable].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15063,46,412,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image82.png'),
(15064,46,413,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 12.18','[{"s": "Problem 12.18", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15065,46,414,'paragraph',NULL,'Explain why the following two structures, I and II cannot be the major contributors to the real structure of CH 3 COOCH 3 .','[{"s": "Explain why the following two structures, I and II cannot be the major contributors to the real structure of CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "COOCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15066,46,415,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15067,46,416,'paragraph',NULL,'The two structures are less important contributors as they involve charge separation. Additionally, structure I contains a carbon atom with an incomplete octet.','[{"s": "The two structures are less important contributors as they involve charge separation. Additionally, structure I contains a carbon atom with an incomplete octet.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15068,46,417,'heading',4,'12.7.7 Resonance Effect',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15069,46,418,'paragraph',NULL,'The resonance effect is defined as ‘the polarity produced in the molecule by the interaction of two π-bonds or between a π-bond and lone pair of electrons present on an adjacent atom’. The effect is transmitted through the chain. There are two types of resonance or mesomeric effect designated as R or M effect.','[{"s": "The resonance effect is defined as ‘the polarity produced in the molecule by the interaction of two π-bonds or between a π-bond and lone pair of electrons present on an adjacent atom’. The effect is transmitted through the chain. There are two types of resonance or mesomeric effect designated as R or M effect.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15070,46,419,'heading',4,'(i) Positive Resonance Effect (+R effect)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15071,46,420,'paragraph',NULL,'In this effect, the transfer of electrons is away from an atom or substituent group attached to the conjugated system. This electron displacement makes certain positions in the molecule of high electron densities. This effect in aniline is shown as :','[{"s": "In this effect, the transfer of electrons is away from an atom or substituent group attached to the conjugated system. This electron displacement makes certain positions in the molecule of high electron densities. This effect in aniline is shown as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15072,46,421,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image88.png'),
(15073,46,422,'heading',4,'(ii) Negative Resonance Effect (- R effect)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15074,46,423,'paragraph',NULL,'This effect is observed when the transfer of electrons is towards the atom or substituent group attached to the conjugated system. For example in nitrobenzene this electron displacement can be depicted as :','[{"s": "This effect is observed when the transfer of electrons is towards the atom or substituent group attached to the conjugated system. For example in nitrobenzene this electron displacement can be depicted as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15075,46,424,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image41.png'),
(15076,46,425,'paragraph',NULL,'The atoms or substituent groups, which represent +R or –R electron displacement effects are as follows :','[{"s": "The atoms or substituent groups, which represent +R or –R electron displacement effects are as follows :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15077,46,426,'paragraph',NULL,'+R effect: – halogen, –OH, –OR, –OCOR, –NH 2 , –NHR, –NR 2 , –NHCOR,','[{"s": "+R effect: – halogen, –OH, –OR, –OCOR, –NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", –NHR, –NR", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", –NHCOR,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15078,46,427,'paragraph',NULL,'– R effect: – COOH, –CHO, >C=O, – CN, –NO 2','[{"s": "– R effect: – COOH, –CHO, >C=O, – CN, –NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15079,46,428,'paragraph',NULL,'The presence of alternate single and double bonds in an open chain or cyclic system is termed as a conjugated system. These systems often show abnormal behaviour. The examples are 1,3- butadiene, aniline and nitrobenzene etc. In such systems, the π-electrons are delocalised and the system develops polarity.','[{"s": "The presence of alternate single and double bonds in an open chain or cyclic system is termed as a conjugated system. These systems often show abnormal behaviour. The examples are 1,3- butadiene, aniline and nitrobenzene etc. In such systems, the π-electrons are delocalised and the system develops polarity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15080,46,429,'heading',4,'12.7.8 Electromeric Effect (E effect)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15081,46,430,'paragraph',NULL,'It is a temporary effect. The organic compounds having a multiple bond Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image46.png ). There are two distinct types of electromeric effect.','[{"s": "It is a temporary effect. The organic compounds having a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "multiple bond", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image46.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "). There are two distinct types of electromeric effect.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15082,46,431,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Positive Eelctromeric Effect (+E effect) In this effect the π−electrons of the multiple bond are transferred to that atom to which the reagent gets attached. For example :','[{"s": "(i) Positive Eelctromeric Effect (+E effect) In this effect the π−electrons of the multiple bond are transferred to that atom to which the reagent gets attached. For example :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15083,46,432,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image30.png'),
(15084,46,433,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Negative Electromeric Effect (–E effect) In this effect the π - electrons of the multiple bond are transferred to that atom to which the attacking reagent does not get attached. For example:','[{"s": "(ii) Negative Electromeric Effect (–E effect) In this effect the π - electrons of the multiple bond are transferred to that atom to which the attacking reagent does not get attached. For example:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15085,46,434,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image182.png'),
(15086,46,435,'paragraph',NULL,'When inductive and electromeric effects operate in opposite directions, the electromeric effect predominates.','[{"s": "When inductive and electromeric effects operate in opposite directions, the electromeric effect predominates.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15087,46,436,'heading',4,'12.7.9 Hyperconjugation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15088,46,437,'paragraph',NULL,'Hyperconjugation is a general stabilising interaction. It involves delocalisation of σ electrons of C—H bond of an alkyl group directly attached to an atom of unsaturated system or to an atom with an unshared p orbital. The σ electrons of C—H bond of the alkyl group enter into partial conjugation with the attached unsaturated system or with the unshared p orbital. Hyperconjugation is a permanent effect.','[{"s": "Hyperconjugation is a general stabilising interaction.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It involves delocalisation of σ electrons of C—H bond of an alkyl group directly attached to an atom of unsaturated system or to an atom with an unshared p orbital. The σ electrons of C—H bond of the alkyl group enter into partial conjugation with the attached unsaturated system or with the unshared p orbital. Hyperconjugation is a permanent effect.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["121333"]',NULL),
(15089,46,438,'paragraph',NULL,'To understand hyperconjugation effect, let us take an example of Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image75.png (ethyl cation) in which the positively charged carbon atom has an empty p orbital. One of the C-H bonds of the methyl group can align in the plane of this empty p orbital and the electrons constituting the C-H bond in plane with this p orbital can then be delocalised into the empty p orbital as depicted in Fig. 12.4 (a).','[{"s": "To understand hyperconjugation effect, let us take an example of", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image75.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(ethyl cation) in which the positively charged carbon atom has an empty p orbital. One of the C-H bonds of the methyl group can align in the plane of this empty p orbital and the electrons constituting the C-H bond in plane with this p orbital can then be delocalised into the empty p orbital as depicted in Fig. 12.4 (a).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["121328"]',NULL),
(15090,46,439,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image140.png'),
(15091,46,440,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 12.4(a) Orbital diagram showing hyperconjugation in ethyl cation','[{"s": "Fig. 12.4(a) Orbital diagram showing hyperconjugation in ethyl cation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15092,46,441,'paragraph',NULL,'This type of overlap stabilises the carbocation because electron density from the adjacent σ bond helps in dispersing the positive charge.','[{"s": "This type of overlap stabilises the carbocation because electron density from the adjacent σ bond helps in dispersing the positive charge.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15093,46,442,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image6.png'),
(15094,46,443,'paragraph',NULL,'In general, greater the number of alkyl groups attached to a positively charged carbon atom, the greater is the hyperconjugation interaction and stabilisation of the cation. Thus, we have the following relative stability of carbocations :','[{"s": "In general, greater the number of alkyl groups attached to a positively charged carbon atom, the greater is the hyperconjugation interaction and stabilisation of the cation.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, we have the following relative stability of carbocations :", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["121371"]',NULL),
(15095,46,444,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image171.png'),
(15096,46,445,'paragraph',NULL,'Hyperconjugation is also possible in alkenes and alkylarenes. 2','[{"s": "Hyperconjugation is also possible in alkenes and alkylarenes.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["121329", "121338"]',NULL),
(15097,46,446,'paragraph',NULL,'Delocalisation of electrons by hyperconjugation in the case of alkene can be depicted as in Fig. 12.4(b).','[{"s": "Delocalisation of electrons by hyperconjugation in the case of alkene can be depicted as in Fig. 12.4(b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15098,46,447,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image142.png'),
(15099,46,448,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 12.4(b) Orbital diagram showing hyperconjugation in propene','[{"s": "Fig. 12.4(b) Orbital diagram showing hyperconjugation in propene", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15100,46,449,'paragraph',NULL,'There are various ways of looking at the hyperconjugative effect. One of the way is to regard C—H bond as possessing partial ionic character due to resonance.','[{"s": "There are various ways of looking at the hyperconjugative effect. One of the way is to regard C—H bond as possessing partial ionic character due to resonance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15101,46,450,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image19.png'),
(15102,46,451,'paragraph',NULL,'The hyperconjugation may also be regarded as no bond resonance.','[{"s": "The hyperconjugation may also be regarded as no bond resonance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15103,46,452,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 12.19','[{"s": "Problem 12.19", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15104,46,453,'paragraph',NULL,'Explain why (CH 3 ) 3 C+ is more stable than CH 3+ CH 2 and + CH 3 CH3C+H2 and C+H3 is the least stable cation.','[{"s": "Explain why (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C+ is more stable than", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH3C+H2 and C+H3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the least stable cation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15105,46,454,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15106,46,455,'paragraph',NULL,'Hyperconjugation interaction in (CH 3 ) 3 C+ is greater than in','[{"s": "Hyperconjugation interaction in (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C+ is greater than in", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15107,46,456,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 3+ CH 2 CH3C+H2 as the (CH 3 ) 3 C+ has nine C-H bonds. In + CH 3 , vacant p orbital is perpendicular to the plane in which C-H bonds lie; hence cannot overlap with it. Thus, + CH 3 lacks hyperconjugative stability.','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH3C+H2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as the (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C+ has nine C-H bonds. In", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", vacant p orbital is perpendicular to the plane in which C-H bonds lie; hence cannot overlap with it. Thus,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lacks hyperconjugative stability.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15108,46,457,'heading',4,'12.7.10 Types of Organic Reactions and Mechanisms',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15109,46,458,'paragraph',NULL,'Organic reactions can be classified into the following categories:','[{"s": "Organic reactions can be classified into the following categories:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15110,46,459,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Substitution reactions','[{"s": "(i) Substitution reactions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15111,46,460,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Addition reactions','[{"s": "(ii) Addition reactions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15112,46,461,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Elimination reactions','[{"s": "(iii) Elimination reactions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15113,46,462,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Rearrangement reactions','[{"s": "(iv) Rearrangement reactions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15114,46,463,'paragraph',NULL,'You will be studying these reactions in Unit 13 and later in class XII.','[{"s": "You will be studying these reactions in Unit 13 and later in class XII.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15115,46,464,'heading',3,'12.8 METHODS OF PURIFICATION OF ORGANIC COMPOUNDS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15116,46,465,'paragraph',NULL,'Once an organic compound is extracted from a natural source or synthesised in the laboratory, it is essential to purify it. Various methods used for the purification of organic compounds are based on the nature of the compound and the impurity present in it.','[{"s": "Once an organic compound is extracted from a natural source or synthesised in the laboratory, it is essential to purify it. Various methods used for the purification of organic compounds are based on the nature of the compound and the impurity present in it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15117,46,466,'paragraph',NULL,'The common techniques used for purification are as follows :','[{"s": "The common techniques used for purification are as follows :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15118,46,467,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Sublimation','[{"s": "(i) Sublimation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15119,46,468,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Crystallisation','[{"s": "(ii) Crystallisation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15120,46,469,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Distillation','[{"s": "(iii) Distillation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15121,46,470,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Differential extraction and','[{"s": "(iv) Differential extraction and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15122,46,471,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) Chromatography','[{"s": "(v) Chromatography", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15123,46,472,'paragraph',NULL,'Finally, the purity of a compound is ascertained by determining its melting or boiling point. Most of the pure compounds have sharp melting points and boiling points. New methods of checking the purity of an organic compound are based on different types of chromatographic and spectroscopic techniques.','[{"s": "Finally, the purity of a compound is ascertained by determining its melting or boiling point. Most of the pure compounds have sharp melting points and boiling points. New methods of checking the purity of an organic compound are based on different types of chromatographic and spectroscopic techniques.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15124,46,473,'paragraph',NULL,'12.8.1 Sublimation','[{"s": "12.8.1 Sublimation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15125,46,474,'paragraph',NULL,'You have learnt earlier that on heating, some solid substances change from solid to vapour state without passing through liquid state . The purification technique based on the above principle is known as sublimation and is used to separate sublimable compounds from non-sublimable impurities.','[{"s": "You have learnt earlier that on heating, some solid substances change from solid to vapour state without passing through liquid state", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The purification technique based on the above principle is known as sublimation and is used to separate sublimable compounds from non-sublimable impurities.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["121341"]',NULL),
(15126,46,475,'heading',4,'12.8.2 Crystallisation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15127,46,476,'paragraph',NULL,'This is one of the most commonly used techniques for the purification of solid organic compounds. It is based on the difference in the solubilities of the compound and the impurities in a suitable solvent. The impure compound is dissolved in a solvent in which it is sparingly soluble at room temperature but appreciably soluble at higher temperature. The solution is concentrated to get a nearly saturated solution. On cooling the solution, pure compound crystallises out and is removed by filtration. The filtrate (mother liquor) contains impurities and small quantity of the compound. If the compound is highly soluble in one solvent and very little soluble in another solvent, crystallisation can be satisfactorily carried out in a mixture of these solvents. Impurities, which impart colour to the solution are removed by adsorbing over activated charcoal. Repeated crystallisation becomes necessary for the purification of compounds containing impurities of comparable solubilities.','[{"s": "This is one of the most commonly used techniques for the purification of solid organic compounds. It is based on the difference in the solubilities of the compound and the impurities in a suitable solvent. The impure compound is dissolved in a solvent in which it is sparingly soluble at room temperature but appreciably soluble at higher temperature. The solution is concentrated to get a nearly saturated solution. On cooling the solution, pure compound crystallises out and is removed by filtration. The filtrate (mother liquor) contains impurities and small quantity of the compound. If the compound is highly soluble in one solvent and very little soluble in another solvent, crystallisation can be satisfactorily carried out in a mixture of these solvents. Impurities, which impart colour to the solution are removed by adsorbing over activated charcoal. Repeated crystallisation becomes necessary for the purification of compounds containing impurities of comparable solubilities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15128,46,477,'heading',4,'12.8.3 Distillation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15129,46,478,'paragraph',NULL,'This important method is used to separate (i) volatile liquids from nonvolatile impurities and (ii) the liquids having sufficient difference in their boiling points. Liquids having different boiling points vaporise at different temperatures. The vapours are cooled and the liquids so formed are collected separately. Chloroform (b.p 334 K) and aniline (b.p. 457 K) are easily separated by the technique of distillation (Fig 12.5). The liquid mixture is taken in a round bottom flask and heated carefully. On boiling, the vapours of lower boiling component are formed first. The vapours are condensed by using a condenser and the liquid is collected in a receiver. The vapours of higher boiling component form later and the liquid can be collected separately.','[{"s": "This important method is used to separate (i) volatile liquids from nonvolatile impurities and (ii) the liquids having sufficient difference in their boiling points. Liquids having different boiling points vaporise at different temperatures. The vapours are cooled and the liquids so formed are collected separately. Chloroform (b.p 334 K) and aniline (b.p. 457 K) are easily separated by the technique of distillation (Fig 12.5). The liquid mixture is taken in a round bottom flask and heated carefully. On boiling, the vapours of lower boiling component are formed first. The vapours are condensed by using a condenser and the liquid is collected in a receiver. The vapours of higher boiling component form later and the liquid can be collected separately.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15130,46,479,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image5.png'),
(15131,46,480,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.5 Simple distillation. The vapours of a substance formed are condensed and the liquid is collected in conical flask.','[{"s": "Fig.12.5 Simple distillation. The vapours of a substance formed are condensed and the liquid is collected in conical flask.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15132,46,481,'paragraph',NULL,'Fractional Distillation: If the difference in boiling points of two liquids is not much, simple distillation cannot be used to separate them. The vapours of such liquids are formed within the same temperature range and are condensed simultaneously. The technique of fractional distillation is used in such cases. In this technique, vapours of a liquid mixture are passed through a fractionating column before condensation. The fractionating column is fitted over the mouth of the round bottom flask (Fig.12.6, page 358).','[{"s": "Fractional Distillation:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If the difference in boiling points of two liquids is not much, simple distillation cannot be used to separate them. The vapours of such liquids are formed within the same temperature range and are condensed simultaneously. The technique of fractional distillation is used in such cases. In this technique, vapours of a liquid mixture are passed through a fractionating column before condensation. The fractionating column is fitted over the mouth of the round bottom flask (Fig.12.6, page 358).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15133,46,482,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image179.png'),
(15134,46,483,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.6 Fractional distillation. The vapours of lower boiling fraction reach the top of the column first followed by vapours of higher boiling fractions.','[{"s": "Fig.12.6 Fractional distillation. The vapours of lower boiling fraction reach the top of the column first followed by vapours of higher boiling fractions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16455,48,214,'paragraph',NULL,'In CO molecule, there are one sigma and two π bonds between carbon and oxygen, :C ≡ O: . Because of the presence of a lone pair on carbon, CO molecule acts as a donor and reacts with certain metals when heated to form metal carbonyls. The highly poisonous nature of CO arises because of its ability to form a complex with haemoglobin, which is about 300 times more stable than the oxygen-haemoglobin complex. This prevents haemoglobin in the red blood corpuscles from carrying oxygen round the body and ultimately resulting in death.','[{"s": "In CO molecule, there are one sigma and two π bonds between carbon and oxygen, :C ≡ O: . Because of the presence of a lone pair on carbon, CO molecule acts as a donor and reacts with certain metals when heated to form metal carbonyls. The highly poisonous nature of CO arises because of its ability to form a complex with haemoglobin, which is about 300 times more stable than the oxygen-haemoglobin complex. This prevents haemoglobin in the red blood corpuscles from carrying oxygen round the body and ultimately resulting in death.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16456,48,215,'heading',4,'11.8.2 Carbon Dioxide',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20506,54,144,'paragraph',NULL,'∆H = ∆U + p∆V (6.8)','[{"s": "∆H = ∆U + p∆V (6.8)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60954"]',NULL),
(15135,46,484,'paragraph',NULL,'Vapours of the liquid with higher boiling point condense before the vapours of the liquid with lower boiling point. The vapours rising up in the fractionating column become richer in more volatile component. By the time the vapours reach to the top of the fractionating column, these are rich in the more volatile component. Fractionating columns are available in various sizes and designs as shown in Fig.12.7. A fractionating column provides many surfaces for heat exchange between the ascending vapours and the descending condensed liquid. Some of the condensing liquid in the fractionating column obtains heat from the ascending vapours and revaporises. The vapours thus become richer in low boiling component. The vapours of low boiling component ascend to the top of the column. On reaching the top, the vapours become pure in low boiling component and pass through the condenser and the pure liquid is collected in a receiver. After a series of successive distillations, the remaining liquid in the distillation flask gets enriched in high boiling component. Each successive condensation and vaporisation unit in the fractionating column is called a theoretical plate. Commercially, columns with hundreds of plates are available.','[{"s": "Vapours of the liquid with higher boiling point condense before the vapours of the liquid with lower boiling point. The vapours rising up in the fractionating column become richer in more volatile component. By the time the vapours reach to the top of the fractionating column, these are rich in the more volatile component. Fractionating columns are available in various sizes and designs as shown in Fig.12.7. A fractionating column provides many surfaces for heat exchange between the ascending vapours and the descending condensed liquid. Some of the condensing liquid in the fractionating column obtains heat from the ascending vapours and revaporises. The vapours thus become richer in low boiling component. The vapours of low boiling component ascend to the top of the column. On reaching the top, the vapours become pure in low boiling component and pass through the condenser and the pure liquid is collected in a receiver. After a series of successive distillations, the remaining liquid in the distillation flask gets enriched in high boiling component. Each successive condensation and vaporisation unit in the fractionating column is called a theoretical plate. Commercially, columns with hundreds of plates are available.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15136,46,485,'paragraph',NULL,'One of the technological applications of fractional distillation is to separate different fractions of crude oil in petroleum industry .','[{"s": "One of the technological applications of fractional distillation is to separate different fractions of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "crude oil in petroleum industry", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15137,46,486,'paragraph',NULL,'Distillation under reduced pressure: This method is used to purify liquids having very high boiling points and those, which decompose at or below their boiling points. Such liquids are made to boil at a temperature lower than their normal boiling points by reducing the pressure on their surface. A liquid boils at a temperature at which its vapour pressure is equal to the external pressure. The pressure is reduced with the help of a water pump or vacuum pump (Fig.12.8). Glycerol can be separated from spent-lye in soap industry by using this technique.','[{"s": "Distillation under reduced pressure:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This method is used to purify liquids having very high boiling points and those, which decompose at or below their boiling points. Such liquids are made to boil at a temperature lower than their normal boiling points by reducing the pressure on their surface. A liquid boils at a temperature at which its vapour pressure is equal to the external pressure. The pressure is reduced with the help of a water pump or vacuum pump (Fig.12.8). Glycerol can be separated from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "spent-lye in soap industry", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by using this technique.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15138,46,487,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image146.png'),
(15139,46,488,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.7 Different types of fractionating columns.','[{"s": "Fig.12.7 Different types of fractionating columns.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15140,46,489,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.8 Distillation under reduced pressure. A liquid boils at a temperature below its vapour pressure by reducing the pressure.','[{"s": "Fig.12.8 Distillation under reduced pressure. A liquid boils at a temperature below its vapour pressure by reducing the pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15141,46,490,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image183.png'),
(15142,46,491,'paragraph',NULL,'Steam Distillation: This technique is applied to separate substances which are steam volatile and are immiscible with water. In steam distillation, steam from a steam generator is passed through a heated flask containing the liquid to be distilled. The mixture of steam and the volatile organic compound is condensed and collected. 3 The compound is later separated from water using a separating funnel. In steam distillation, the liquid boils when the sum of vapour pressures due to the organic liquid (p 1 ) and that due to water (p 2 ) becomes equal to the atmospheric pressure (p), i.e. p =p1+ p 2 . Since p1 is lower than p, the organic liquid vaporises at lower temperature than its boiling point.','[{"s": "Steam Distillation:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This technique is applied to separate substances which are steam volatile and are immiscible with water. In steam distillation, steam from a steam generator is passed through a heated flask containing the liquid to be distilled.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The mixture of steam and the volatile organic compound is condensed and collected.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The compound is later separated from water using a separating funnel. In steam distillation, the liquid boils when the sum of vapour pressures due to the organic liquid (p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and that due to water (p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") becomes equal to the atmospheric pressure (p), i.e. p =p1+ p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since p1 is lower than p, the organic liquid vaporises at lower temperature than its boiling point.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["121340", "121342", "121372"]',NULL),
(15143,46,492,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, if one of the substances in the mixture is water and the other, a water insoluble substance, then the mixture will boil close to but below, 373K. A mixture of water and the substance is obtained which can be separated by using a separating funnel. Aniline is separated by this technique from aniline – water mixture (Fig.12.9, Page 360).','[{"s": "Thus, if one of the substances in the mixture is water and the other, a water insoluble substance, then the mixture will boil close to but below, 373K. A mixture of water and the substance is obtained which can be separated by using a separating funnel. Aniline is separated by this technique from aniline – water mixture (Fig.12.9, Page 360).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15144,46,493,'heading',4,'12.8.4 Differential Extraction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16457,48,216,'paragraph',NULL,'It is prepared by complete combustion of carbon and carbon containing fuels in excess of air.','[{"s": "It is prepared by complete combustion of carbon and carbon containing fuels in excess of air.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16458,48,217,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image25.png'),
(16459,48,218,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image28.png'),
(15145,46,494,'paragraph',NULL,'When an organic compound is present in an aqueous medium, it is separated by shaking it with an organic solvent in which it is more soluble than in water. The organic solvent and the aqueous solution should be immiscible with each other so that they form two distinct layers which can be separated by separatory funnel. The organic solvent is later removed by distillation or by evaporation to get back the compound. Differential extraction is carried out in a separatory funnel as shown in Fig. 12.10 (Page 360). If the organic compound is less soluble in the organic solvent, a very large quantity of solvent would be required to extract even a very small quantity of the compound. The technique of continuous extraction is employed in such cases. In this technique same solvent is repeatedly used for extraction of the compound.','[{"s": "When an organic compound is present in an aqueous medium, it is separated by shaking it with an organic solvent in which it is more soluble than in water. The organic solvent and the aqueous solution should be immiscible with each other so that they form two distinct layers which can be separated by separatory funnel. The organic solvent is later removed by distillation or by evaporation to get back the compound. Differential extraction is carried out in a separatory funnel as shown in Fig. 12.10 (Page 360). If the organic compound is less soluble in the organic solvent, a very large quantity of solvent would be required to extract even a very small quantity of the compound. The technique of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "continuous extraction", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is employed in such cases. In this technique same solvent is repeatedly used for extraction of the compound.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15146,46,495,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image129.png'),
(15147,46,496,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.9 Steam distillation. Steam volatile component volatilizes, the vapours condense in the condenser and the liquid collects in conical flask.','[{"s": "Fig.12.9 Steam distillation. Steam volatile component volatilizes, the vapours condense in the condenser and the liquid collects in conical flask.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15148,46,497,'heading',4,'12.8.5 Chromatography',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15149,46,498,'paragraph',NULL,'Chromatography is an important technique extensively used to separate mixtures into their components, purify compounds and also to test the purity of compounds. The name chromatography is based on the Greek word chroma, for colour since the method was first used for the separation of coloured substances found in plants. In this technique, the mixture of substances is applied onto a stationary phase, which may be a solid or a liquid. A pure solvent, a mixture of solvents, or a gas is allowed to move slowly over the stationary phase. The components of the mixture get gradually separated from one another. The moving phase is called the mobile phase.','[{"s": "Chromatography is an important technique extensively used to separate mixtures into their components, purify compounds and also to test the purity of compounds. The name chromatography is based on the Greek word chroma, for colour since the method was first used for the separation of coloured substances found in plants. In this technique, the mixture of substances is applied onto a stationary phase, which may be a solid or a liquid. A pure solvent, a mixture of solvents, or a gas is allowed to move slowly over the stationary phase. The components of the mixture get gradually separated from one another. The moving phase is called the mobile phase.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15150,46,499,'paragraph',NULL,'Based on the principle involved, chromatography is classified into different categories. Two of these are:','[{"s": "Based on the principle involved, chromatography is classified into different categories. Two of these are:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15151,46,500,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image113.png'),
(15152,46,501,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.10 Differential extraction. Extraction of compound takes place based on difference in solubility','[{"s": "Fig.12.10 Differential extraction. Extraction of compound takes place based on difference in solubility", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15153,46,502,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Adsorption chromatography, and','[{"s": "(a) Adsorption chromatography, and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15154,46,503,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Partition chromatography.','[{"s": "(b) Partition chromatography.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15155,46,504,'paragraph',NULL,'a) Adsorption Chromatography: Adsor-ption chromatography is based on the fact that different compounds are adsorbed on an adsorbent to different degrees. Commonly used adsorbents are silica gel and alumina. When a mobile phase is allowed to move over a stationary phase (adsorbent), the components of the mixture move by varying distances over the stationary phase. Following are two main types of chromatographic techniques based on the principle of differential adsorption.','[{"s": "a) Adsorption Chromatography:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Adsor-ption chromatography is based on the fact that different compounds are adsorbed on an adsorbent to different degrees. Commonly used adsorbents are silica gel and alumina. When a mobile phase is allowed to move over a stationary phase (adsorbent), the components of the mixture move by varying distances over the stationary phase. Following are two main types of chromatographic techniques based on the principle of differential adsorption.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15156,46,505,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Column chromatography, and','[{"s": "(a) Column chromatography, and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15157,46,506,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Thin layer chromatography.','[{"s": "(b) Thin layer chromatography.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15158,46,507,'paragraph',NULL,'Column Chromatography: Column chromatography involves separation of a mixture over a column of adsorbent (stationary phase) packed in a glass tube. The column is fitted with a stopcock at its lower end (Fig. 12.11). The mixture adsorbed on adsorbent is placed on the top of the adsorbent column packed in a glass tube. An appropriate eluant which is a liquid or a mixture of liquids is allowed to flow down the column slowly. Depending upon the degree to which the compounds are adsorbed, complete separation takes place. The most readily adsorbed substances are retained near the top and others come down to various distances in the column (Fig.12.11).','[{"s": "Column Chromatography:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Column chromatography involves separation of a mixture over a column of adsorbent (stationary phase) packed in a glass tube. The column is fitted with a stopcock at its lower end (Fig. 12.11). The mixture adsorbed on adsorbent is placed on the top of the adsorbent column packed in a glass tube. An appropriate eluant which is a liquid or a mixture of liquids is allowed to flow down the column slowly. Depending upon the degree to which the compounds are adsorbed, complete separation takes place. The most readily adsorbed substances are retained near the top and others come down to various distances in the column (Fig.12.11).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15159,46,508,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image43.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(15160,46,509,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.11 Column chromatography. Different stages of separation of components of a mixture.','[{"s": "Fig.12.11 Column chromatography. Different stages of separation of components of a mixture.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15161,46,510,'paragraph',NULL,'Thin Layer Chromatography: Thin layer chromatography (TLC) is another type of adsorption chromatography, which involves separation of substances of a mixture over a thin layer of an adsorbent coated on glass plate. A thin layer (about 0.2mm thick) of an adsorbent (silica gel or alumina) is spread over a glass plate of suitable size. The plate is known as thin layer chromatography plate or chromaplate. The solution of the mixture to be separated is applied as a small spot about 2 cm above one end of the TLC plate. The glass plate is then placed in a closed jar containing the eluant (Fig. 12.12a). As the eluant rises up the plate, the components of the mixture move up along with the eluant to different distances depending on their degree of adsorption and separation takes place. The relative adsorption of each component of the mixture is expressed in terms of its retardation factor i.e. Rf value (Fig.12.12 b).','[{"s": "Thin Layer Chromatography:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thin layer chromatography (TLC) is another type of adsorption chromatography, which involves separation of substances of a mixture over a thin layer of an adsorbent coated on glass plate. A thin layer (about 0.2mm thick) of an adsorbent (silica gel or alumina) is spread over a glass plate of suitable size. The plate is known as thin layer chromatography plate or chromaplate. The solution of the mixture to be separated is applied as a small spot about 2 cm above one end of the TLC plate. The glass plate is then placed in a closed jar containing the eluant (Fig. 12.12a). As the eluant rises up the plate, the components of the mixture move up along with the eluant to different distances depending on their degree of adsorption and separation takes place. The relative adsorption of each component of the mixture is expressed in terms of its retardation factor i.e. Rf value (Fig.12.12 b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15162,46,511,'image',NULL,'image15',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image160.jpg'),
(15163,46,512,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image143.png'),
(15164,46,513,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.12 (a) Thin layer chromatography. Chromatogram being developed.','[{"s": "Fig.12.12 (a) Thin layer chromatography. Chromatogram being developed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15165,46,514,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image35.png'),
(15166,46,515,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.12 (b) Developed chromatogram.','[{"s": "Fig.12.12 (b) Developed chromatogram.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15167,46,516,'paragraph',NULL,'The spots of coloured compounds are visible on TLC plate due to their original colour. The spots of colourless compounds, which are invisible to the eye but fluoresce in ultraviolet light, can be detected by putting the plate under ultraviolet light. Another detection technique is to place the plate in a covered jar containing a few crystals of iodine. Spots of compounds, which adsorb iodine, will show up as brown spots. Sometimes an appropriate reagent may also be sprayed on the plate. For example, amino acids may be detected by spraying the plate with ninhydrin solution (Fig.12.12b).','[{"s": "The spots of coloured compounds are visible on TLC plate due to their original colour. The spots of colourless compounds, which are invisible to the eye but fluoresce in ultraviolet light, can be detected by putting the plate under ultraviolet light. Another detection technique is to place the plate in a covered jar containing a few crystals of iodine. Spots of compounds, which adsorb iodine, will show up as brown spots. Sometimes an appropriate reagent may also be sprayed on the plate. For example, amino acids may be detected by spraying the plate with ninhydrin solution (Fig.12.12b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15168,46,517,'paragraph',NULL,'Partition Chromatography:','[{"s": "Partition Chromatography:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15169,46,518,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image8.png'),
(15170,46,519,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image23.png'),
(15171,46,520,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.13 Paper chromatography. Chromatography paper in two different shapes.','[{"s": "Fig.12.13 Paper chromatography. Chromatography paper in two different shapes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15172,46,521,'paragraph',NULL,'A strip of chromatography paper spotted at the base with the solution of the mixture is suspended in a suitable solvent or a mixture of solvents (Fig. 12.13). This solvent acts as the mobile phase. The solvent rises up the paper by capillary action and flows over the spot. The paper selectively retains different components according to their differing partition in the two phases. The paper strip so developed is known as a chromatogram. The spots of the separated coloured compounds are visible at different heights from the position of initial spot on the chromatogram. The spots of the separated colourless compounds may be observed either under ultraviolet light or by the use of an appropriate spray reagent as discussed under thin layer chromatography.','[{"s": "A strip of chromatography paper spotted at the base with the solution of the mixture is suspended in a suitable solvent or a mixture of solvents (Fig. 12.13). This solvent acts as the mobile phase. The solvent rises up the paper by capillary action and flows over the spot.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The paper selectively retains different components according to their differing partition in the two phases.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The paper strip so developed is known as a chromatogram. The spots of the separated coloured compounds are visible at different heights from the position of initial spot on the chromatogram. The spots of the separated colourless compounds may be observed either under ultraviolet light or by the use of an appropriate spray reagent as discussed under thin layer chromatography.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["121339"]',NULL),
(15173,46,522,'heading',3,'12.9 QUALITATIVE ANALYSIS OF ORGANIC COMPOUNDS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15174,46,523,'paragraph',NULL,'The elements present in organic compounds are carbon and hydrogen. In addition to these, they may also contain oxygen, nitrogen, sulphur, halogens and phosphorus.','[{"s": "The elements present in organic compounds are carbon and hydrogen. In addition to these, they may also contain oxygen, nitrogen, sulphur, halogens and phosphorus.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15175,46,524,'heading',4,'12.9.1 Detection of Carbon and Hydrogen',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16460,48,219,'paragraph',NULL,'In the laboratory it is conveniently prepared by the action of dilute HCl on calcium carbonate.','[{"s": "In the laboratory it is conveniently prepared by the action of dilute HCl on calcium carbonate.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15176,46,525,'paragraph',NULL,'Carbon and hydrogen are detected by heating the compound with copper(II) oxide. Carbon present in the compound is oxidised to carbon dioxide (tested with lime-water, which develops turbidity) and hydrogen to water (tested with anhydrous copper sulphate, which turns blue).','[{"s": "Carbon and hydrogen are detected by heating the compound with copper(II) oxide. Carbon present in the compound is oxidised to carbon dioxide (tested with lime-water, which develops turbidity) and hydrogen to water (tested with anhydrous copper sulphate, which turns blue).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15177,46,526,'paragraph',NULL,'C + 2CuO Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image13.png 2Cu + CO2','[{"s": "C + 2CuO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "2Cu + CO2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15178,46,527,'paragraph',NULL,'2H + CuO Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image13.png Cu + H2O','[{"s": "2H + CuO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Cu + H2O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15179,46,528,'paragraph',NULL,'CO2 + Ca(OH)2 → CaCO3↓ + H2O','[{"s": "CO2 + Ca(OH)2 → CaCO3↓ + H2O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15180,46,529,'paragraph',NULL,'5H2O + CuSO4 → CuSO4.5H2O','[{"s": "5H2O + CuSO4 → CuSO4.5H2O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15181,46,530,'paragraph',NULL,'White Blue','[{"s": "White Blue", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15182,46,531,'heading',4,'12.9.2 Detection of Other Elements',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15183,46,532,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitrogen, sulphur, halogens and phosphorus present in an organic compound are detected by “Lassaigne’s test”. The elements present in the compound are converted from covalent form into the ionic form by fusing the compound with sodium metal. Following reactions take place:','[{"s": "Nitrogen, sulphur, halogens and phosphorus present in an organic compound are detected by “Lassaigne’s test”.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The elements present in the compound are converted from covalent form into the ionic form by fusing the compound with sodium metal. Following reactions take place:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["121345"]',NULL),
(15184,46,533,'paragraph',NULL,'Na + C + N Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image13.png NaCN','[{"s": "Na + C + N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "NaCN", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15185,46,534,'paragraph',NULL,'2Na + S Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image13.png Na 2 S','[{"s": "2Na + S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15186,46,535,'paragraph',NULL,'Na + X Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image13.png Na X','[{"s": "Na + X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Na X", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15187,46,536,'paragraph',NULL,'(X = Cl, Br or I)','[{"s": "(X = Cl, Br or I)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15188,46,537,'paragraph',NULL,'C, N, S and X come from organic compound.','[{"s": "C, N, S and X come from organic compound.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15189,46,538,'paragraph',NULL,'Cyanide, sulphide and halide of sodium so formed on sodium fusion are extracted from the fused mass by boiling it with distilled water. This extract is known as sodium fusion extract.','[{"s": "Cyanide, sulphide and halide of sodium so formed on sodium fusion are extracted from the fused mass by boiling it with distilled water. This extract is known as sodium fusion extract.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15190,46,539,'heading',4,'(A) Test for Nitrogen',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15191,46,540,'paragraph',NULL,'The sodium fusion extract is boiled with iron(II) sulphate and then acidified with concentrated sulphuric acid. The formation of Prussian blue colour confirms the presence of nitrogen.','[{"s": "The sodium fusion extract is boiled with iron(II) sulphate and then acidified with concentrated sulphuric acid.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The formation of Prussian blue colour confirms the presence of nitrogen.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]','marked','["121344"]',NULL),
(15192,46,541,'paragraph',NULL,'6CN– + Fe 2+ → [Fe(CN) 6 ] 4–','[{"s": "6CN– + Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ [Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4–", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15193,46,542,'paragraph',NULL,'3[Fe(CN) 6 ] 4– + 4Fe 3+ Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image110.png Fe 4 [Fe(CN) 6 ] 3 .xH 2 O','[{"s": "3[Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 4Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image110.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".xH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15194,46,543,'paragraph',NULL,'Prussian blue','[{"s": "Prussian blue", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15195,46,544,'heading',4,'(B) Test for Sulphur',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15196,46,545,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The sodium fusion extract is acidified with acetic acid and lead acetate is added to it. A black precipitate of lead sulphide indicates the presence of sulphur.','[{"s": "(a) The sodium fusion extract is acidified with acetic acid and lead acetate is added to it. A black precipitate of lead sulphide indicates the presence of sulphur.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15197,46,546,'paragraph',NULL,'S 2– + Pb 2+ → PbS','[{"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ Pb", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ PbS", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15198,46,547,'paragraph',NULL,'Black','[{"s": "Black", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15199,46,548,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) On treating sodium fusion extract with sodium nitroprusside, appearance of a violet colour further indicates the presence of sulphur.','[{"s": "(b) On treating sodium fusion extract with sodium nitroprusside, appearance of a violet colour further indicates the presence of sulphur.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15200,46,549,'paragraph',NULL,'S 2– + [Fe(CN) 5 NO] 2– → [Fe(CN) 5 NOS] 4–','[{"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ [Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NO]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ [Fe(CN)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NOS]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4–", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15201,46,550,'paragraph',NULL,'Violet','[{"s": "Violet", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15202,46,551,'paragraph',NULL,'In case, nitrogen and sulphur both are present in an organic compound, sodium thiocyanate is formed. It gives blood red colour and no Prussian blue since there are no free cyanide ions.','[{"s": "In case, nitrogen and sulphur both are present in an organic compound, sodium thiocyanate is formed. It gives", "t": "text"}, {"s": "blood red", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "colour and no Prussian blue since there are no free cyanide ions.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["121373"]',NULL),
(15203,46,552,'paragraph',NULL,'Na + C + N + S → NaSCN','[{"s": "Na + C + N + S → NaSCN", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15204,46,553,'paragraph',NULL,'Fe 3+ +SCN– → [Fe(SCN)] 2+','[{"s": "Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+SCN– → [Fe(SCN)]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15205,46,554,'paragraph',NULL,'Blood red','[{"s": "Blood red", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15206,46,555,'paragraph',NULL,'If sodium fusion is carried out with excess of sodium, the thiocyanate decomposes to yield cyanide and sulphide. These ions give their usual tests.','[{"s": "If sodium fusion is carried out with excess of sodium, the thiocyanate decomposes to yield cyanide and sulphide. These ions give their usual tests.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15207,46,556,'paragraph',NULL,'NaSCN + 2Na → NaCN+Na 2 S','[{"s": "NaSCN + 2Na → NaCN+Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15208,46,557,'heading',4,'(C) Test for Halogens',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15209,46,558,'paragraph',NULL,'The sodium fusion extract is acidified with nitric acid and then treated with silver nitrate. A white precipitate, soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of chlorine, a yellowish precipitate, sparingly soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of bromine and a yellow precipitate, insoluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of iodine.','[{"s": "The sodium fusion extract is acidified with nitric acid and then treated with silver nitrate. A white precipitate, soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of chlorine, a yellowish precipitate, sparingly soluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of bromine and a yellow precipitate, insoluble in ammonium hydroxide shows the presence of iodine.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15210,46,559,'paragraph',NULL,'X – + Ag + → AgX','[{"s": "X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ Ag", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ AgX", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15211,46,560,'paragraph',NULL,'X represents a halogen – Cl, Br or I.','[{"s": "X represents a halogen – Cl, Br or I.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15212,46,561,'paragraph',NULL,'If nitrogen or sulphur is also present in the compound, the sodium fusion extract is first boiled with concentrated nitric acid to decompose cyanide or sulphide of sodium formed during Lassaigne’s test. These ions would otherwise interfere with silver nitrate test for halogens.','[{"s": "If nitrogen or sulphur is also present in the compound, the sodium fusion extract is first boiled with concentrated nitric acid to decompose cyanide or sulphide of sodium formed during Lassaigne’s test. These ions would otherwise interfere with silver nitrate test for halogens.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15213,46,562,'heading',4,'(D) Test for Phosphorus',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15214,46,563,'paragraph',NULL,'The compound is heated with an oxidising agent (sodium peroxide). The phosphorus present in the compound is oxidised to phosphate. The solution is boiled with nitric acid and then treated with ammonium molybdate. A yellow colouration or precipitate indicates the presence of phosphorus.','[{"s": "The compound is heated with an oxidising agent (sodium peroxide). The phosphorus present in the compound is oxidised to phosphate. The solution is boiled with nitric acid and then treated with ammonium molybdate. A yellow colouration or precipitate indicates the presence of phosphorus.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15215,46,564,'paragraph',NULL,'Na 3 PO 4 + 3HNO 3 → H 3 PO 4 +3NaNO 3','[{"s": "Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 3HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+3NaNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15216,46,565,'paragraph',NULL,'H 3 PO 4 + 12(NH 4 ) 2 MoO 4 + 21HNO 3 →','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 12(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MoO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 21HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15217,46,566,'paragraph',NULL,'Ammonium molybdate','[{"s": "Ammonium molybdate", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15218,46,567,'paragraph',NULL,'(NH 4 ) 3 PO 4 .12MoO 3 + 21NH 4 NO 3 + 12H 2 O','[{"s": "(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".12MoO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 21NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 12H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15219,46,568,'paragraph',NULL,'Ammonium phosphomolybdate','[{"s": "Ammonium phosphomolybdate", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15220,46,569,'heading',3,'12.10 QUANTITATIVE ANALYSIS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15221,46,570,'paragraph',NULL,'Quantitative analysis of compounds is very important in organic chemistry. It helps chemists in the determination of mass per cent of elements present in a compound. You have learnt in Unit-1 that mass per cent of elements is required for the determination of emperical and molecular formula.','[{"s": "Quantitative analysis of compounds is very important in organic chemistry. It helps chemists in the determination of mass per cent of elements present in a compound. You have learnt in Unit-1 that mass per cent of elements is required for the determination of emperical and molecular formula.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15222,46,571,'paragraph',NULL,'The percentage composition of elements present in an organic compound is determined by the following methods:','[{"s": "The percentage composition of elements present in an organic compound is determined by the following methods:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15223,46,572,'paragraph',NULL,'12.10.1 Carbon and Hydrogen','[{"s": "12.10.1 Carbon and Hydrogen", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15224,46,573,'paragraph',NULL,'Both carbon and hydrogen are estimated in one experiment. A known mass of an organic compound is burnt in the presence of excess of oxygen and copper(II) oxide. Carbon and hydrogen in the compound are oxidised to carbon dioxide and water respectively.','[{"s": "Both carbon and hydrogen are estimated in one experiment. A known mass of an organic compound is burnt in the presence of excess of oxygen and copper(II) oxide. Carbon and hydrogen in the compound are oxidised to carbon dioxide and water respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15225,46,574,'paragraph',NULL,'CxHy + (x + y/4) O2 → x CO2 + (y/2) H2O','[{"s": "CxHy + (x + y/4) O2 → x CO2 + (y/2) H2O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15226,46,575,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image42.png'),
(15227,46,576,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.14 Estimation of carbon and hydrogen. Water and carbon dioxide formed on oxidation of substance are absorbed in anhydrous calcium chloride and potassium hydroxide solutions respectively contained in U tubes.','[{"s": "Fig.12.14 Estimation of carbon and hydrogen. Water and carbon dioxide formed on oxidation of substance are absorbed in anhydrous calcium chloride and potassium hydroxide solutions respectively contained in U tubes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15228,46,577,'paragraph',NULL,'Let the mass of organic compound be m g, mass of water and carbon dioxide produced be m1 and m2 g respectively;','[{"s": "Let the mass of organic compound be m g, mass of water and carbon dioxide produced be m1 and m2 g respectively;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15229,46,578,'paragraph',NULL,'Percentage of carbon= 12×m 2 ×100/44×m','[{"s": "Percentage of carbon=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12×m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×100/44×m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15230,46,579,'paragraph',NULL,'Percentage of hydrogen = 2×m 1 ×100/18×m','[{"s": "Percentage of hydrogen =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2×m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×100/18×m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15231,46,580,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 12.20','[{"s": "Problem 12.20", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15232,46,581,'paragraph',NULL,'On complete combustion, 0.246 g of an organic compound gave 0.198g of carbon dioxide and 0.1014g of water. Determine the percentage composition of carbon and hydrogen in the compound.','[{"s": "On complete combustion, 0.246 g of an organic compound gave 0.198g of carbon dioxide and 0.1014g of water. Determine the percentage composition of carbon and hydrogen in the compound.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15233,46,582,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15234,46,583,'paragraph',NULL,'Percentage of carbon= 12×0.198×100/44×0.246','[{"s": "Percentage of carbon=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12×0.198×100/44×0.246", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15235,46,584,'paragraph',NULL,'= 21.95%','[{"s": "= 21.95%", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15236,46,585,'paragraph',NULL,'Percentage of hydrogen = 2×0.1014×100/18×0.246 = 4.58%','[{"s": "Percentage of hydrogen =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2×0.1014×100/18×0.246", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.58%", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15237,46,586,'heading',4,'12.10.2 Nitrogen',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15238,46,587,'paragraph',NULL,'There are two methods for estimation of nitrogen: (i) Dumas method and (ii) Kjeldahl’s method. 2','[{"s": "There are two methods for estimation of nitrogen: (i) Dumas method and (ii) Kjeldahl’s method.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["121349", "121351"]',NULL),
(15239,46,588,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Dumas method: The nitrogen containing organic compound, when heated with copper oxide in an atmosphere of carbon dioxide, yields free nitrogen in addition to carbon dioxide and water.','[{"s": "(i) Dumas method:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The nitrogen containing organic compound, when heated with copper oxide in an atmosphere of carbon dioxide, yields free nitrogen in addition to carbon dioxide and water.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15240,46,589,'paragraph',NULL,'C x H y N z + (2x + y/2) CuO → x CO 2 + y/2 H 2 O + z/2 N 2 + (2x + y/2) Cu','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ (2x + y/2) CuO → x CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ y/2 H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O + z/2 N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ (2x + y/2) Cu", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15241,46,590,'paragraph',NULL,'Traces of nitrogen oxides formed, if any, are reduced to nitrogen by passing the gaseous mixture over a heated copper gauze. The mixture of gases so produced is collected over an aqueous solution of potassium hydroxide which absorbs carbon dioxide. Nitrogen is collected in the upper part of the graduated tube (Fig.12.15).','[{"s": "Traces of nitrogen oxides formed, if any, are reduced to nitrogen by passing the gaseous mixture over a heated copper gauze. The mixture of gases so produced is collected over an aqueous solution of potassium hydroxide which absorbs carbon dioxide. Nitrogen is collected in the upper part of the graduated tube (Fig.12.15).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15242,46,591,'paragraph',NULL,'Let the mass of organic compound = m g','[{"s": "Let the mass of organic compound = m g", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15243,46,592,'paragraph',NULL,'Volume of nitrogen collected = V1 mL','[{"s": "Volume of nitrogen collected = V1 mL", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15244,46,593,'paragraph',NULL,'Room temperature = T1K','[{"s": "Room temperature = T1K", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15245,46,594,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Chemistry_Some_Basic_Principles_and_Techniques-image93.png'),
(15246,46,595,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.12.15 Dumas method. The organic compound yields nitrogen gas on heating it with copper(II) oxide in the presence of CO2 gas. The mixture of gases is collected over potassium hydroxide solution in which CO2 is absorbed and volume of nitrogen gas is determined.','[{"s": "Fig.12.15 Dumas method. The organic compound yields nitrogen gas on heating it with copper(II) oxide in the presence of CO2 gas. The mixture of gases is collected over potassium hydroxide solution in which CO2 is absorbed and volume of nitrogen gas is determined.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15247,46,596,'paragraph',NULL,'Volume of nitrogen at STP= p 1 V 1 ×273/760×T 1','[{"s": "Volume", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of nitrogen at STP=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×273/760×T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15248,46,597,'paragraph',NULL,'(Let it be V mL)','[{"s": "(Let it be V mL)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15249,46,598,'paragraph',NULL,'Where p1 and V1 are the pressure and volume of nitrogen, p1 is different from the atmospheric pressure at which nitrogen gas is collected. The value of p1 is obtained by the relation;','[{"s": "Where p1 and V1 are the pressure and volume of nitrogen, p1 is different from the atmospheric pressure at which nitrogen gas is collected. The value of p1 is obtained by the relation;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15250,46,599,'paragraph',NULL,'p1= Atmospheric pressure – Aqueous tension','[{"s": "p1= Atmospheric pressure – Aqueous tension", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15251,46,600,'paragraph',NULL,'22400 mL N2 at STP weighs 28 g.','[{"s": "22400 mL N2 at STP weighs 28 g.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15252,46,601,'paragraph',NULL,'V mL N 2 at STP weighs= 28×V/22400 g','[{"s": "V mL N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at STP weighs=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "28×V/22400", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15253,46,602,'paragraph',NULL,'Percentage of nitrogen = 28×V×100/22400×m','[{"s": "Percentage of nitrogen =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "28×V×100/22400×m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15254,46,603,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 12.21','[{"s": "Problem 12.21", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15255,46,604,'paragraph',NULL,'In Dumas’ method for estimation of nitrogen, 0.3g of an organic compound gave 50mL of nitrogen collected at 300K temperature and 715mm pressure. Calculate the percentage composition of nitrogen in the compound. (Aqueous tension at 300K=15 mm)','[{"s": "In Dumas’ method for estimation of nitrogen, 0.3g of an organic compound gave 50mL of nitrogen collected at 300K temperature and 715mm pressure. Calculate the percentage composition of nitrogen in the compound. (Aqueous tension at 300K=15 mm)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15256,46,605,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15257,46,606,'paragraph',NULL,'Volume of nitrogen collected at 300K and 715mm pressure is 50 mL','[{"s": "Volume of nitrogen collected at 300K and 715mm pressure is 50 mL", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15258,46,607,'paragraph',NULL,'Actual pressure = 715-15 =700 mm','[{"s": "Actual pressure = 715-15 =700 mm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15259,46,608,'paragraph',NULL,'Volume of nitrogen at STP= 273×700×50/300×760','[{"s": "Volume of nitrogen at STP=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "273×700×50/300×760", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15260,46,609,'paragraph',NULL,'=41.9 mL','[{"s": "=41.9 mL", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
