(15734,47,33,'paragraph',NULL,'13.2 (i) Write structures of different isomeric amines corresponding to the molecular formula, C 4 H 11 N.','[{"s": "13.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(i) Write structures of different isomeric amines corresponding to the molecular formula, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15735,47,34,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Write IUPAC names of all the isomers.','[{"s": "(ii) Write IUPAC names of all the isomers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30244,73,302,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image39.png'),
(15736,47,35,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) What type of isomerism is exhibited by different pairs of amines?','[{"s": "(iii) What type of isomerism is exhibited by different pairs of amines?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15737,47,36,'heading',3,'13.4 Preparation of Amines',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15738,47,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Amines are prepared by the following methods:','[{"s": "Amines are prepared by the following methods:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15739,47,38,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Reduction of nitro compounds','[{"s": "1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Reduction of nitro compounds", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["270802"]',NULL),
(15740,47,39,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitro compounds are reduced to amines by passing hydrogen gas in the presence of finely divided nickel, palladium or platinum and also by reduction with metals in acidic medium. Nitroalkanes can also be similarly reduced to the corresponding alkanamines.','[{"s": "Nitro compounds are reduced to amines by passing hydrogen gas in the presence of finely divided nickel, palladium or platinum and also by reduction with metals in acidic medium. Nitroalkanes can also be similarly reduced to the corresponding alkanamines.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15741,47,40,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image67.png'),
(15742,47,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Reduction with iron scrap and hydrochloric acid is preferred because FeCl 2 formed gets hydrolysed to release hydrochloric acid during the reaction. Thus, only a small amount of hydrochloric acid is required to initiate the reaction.','[{"s": "Reduction with iron scrap and hydrochloric acid is preferred because FeCl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "formed gets hydrolysed to release hydrochloric acid during the reaction.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus, only a small amount of hydrochloric acid is required to initiate the reaction.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["270965"]',NULL),
(15743,47,42,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Ammonolysis of alkyl halides','[{"s": "2. Ammonolysis of alkyl halides", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15744,47,43,'paragraph',NULL,'You have read (Unit 10, Class XII) that the carbon - halogen bond in alkyl or benzyl halides can be easily cleaved by a nucleophile. Hence, an alkyl or benzyl halide on reaction with an ethanolic solution of ammonia undergoes nucleophilic substitution reaction in which the halogen atom is replaced by an amino (–NH 2 ) group. This process of cleavage of the C–X bond by ammonia molecule is known as ammonolysis . The reaction is carried out in a sealed tube at 373 K. The primary amine thus obtained behaves as a nucleophile and can further react with alkyl halide to form secondary and tertiary amines, and finally quaternary ammonium salt.','[{"s": "You have read (Unit 10, Class XII) that the carbon - halogen bond in alkyl or benzyl halides can be easily cleaved by a nucleophile. Hence, an alkyl or benzyl halide on reaction with an ethanolic solution of ammonia undergoes nucleophilic substitution reaction in which the halogen atom is replaced by an amino (–NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") group. This process of cleavage of the C–X bond by ammonia molecule is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ammonolysis", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The reaction is carried out in a sealed tube at 373 K. The primary amine thus obtained behaves as a nucleophile and can further react with alkyl halide to form secondary and tertiary amines, and finally quaternary ammonium salt.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15745,47,44,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image1.png'),
(15746,47,45,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image84.png'),
(15747,47,46,'paragraph',NULL,'The free amine can be obtained from the ammonium salt by treatment with a strong base:','[{"s": "The free amine can be obtained from the ammonium salt by treatment with a strong base:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15748,47,47,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image90.png'),
(15749,47,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Ammonolysis has the disadvantage of yielding a mixture of primary, secondary and tertiary amines and also a quaternary ammonium salt. However, primary amine is obtained as a major product by taking large excess of ammonia.','[{"s": "Ammonolysis has the disadvantage of yielding a mixture of primary, secondary and tertiary amines and also a quaternary ammonium salt. However, primary amine is obtained as a major product by taking large excess of ammonia.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15750,47,49,'paragraph',NULL,'The order of reactivity of halides with amines is RI > RBr >RCl.','[{"s": "The order of reactivity of halides with amines is RI > RBr >RCl.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15751,47,50,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.1','[{"s": "Example 13.1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
