(15788,47,87,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Benzene into aniline (ii) Benzene into N, N-dimethylaniline','[{"s": "(i) Benzene into aniline (ii) Benzene into N, N-dimethylaniline", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15789,47,88,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Cl–(CH 2 ) 4 –Cl into hexan-1,6-diamine?','[{"s": "(iii) Cl–(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–Cl into hexan-1,6-diamine?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15790,47,89,'heading',3,'13.5 Physical Properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15791,47,90,'paragraph',NULL,'The lower aliphatic amines are gases with fishy odour. Primary amines with three or more carbon atoms are liquid and still higher ones are solid. Aniline and other arylamines are usually colourless but get coloured on storage due to atmospheric oxidation.','[{"s": "The lower aliphatic amines are gases with fishy odour. Primary amines with three or more carbon atoms are liquid and still higher ones are solid. Aniline and other arylamines are usually colourless but get coloured on storage due to atmospheric oxidation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15792,47,91,'paragraph',NULL,'Lower aliphatic amines are soluble in water because they can form hydrogen bonds with water molecules. However, solubility decreases with increase in molar mass of amines due to increase in size of the hydrophobic alkyl part. Higher amines are essentially insoluble in water. Considering the electronegativity of nitrogen of amine and oxygen of alcohol as 3.0 and 3.5 respectively, you can predict the pattern of solubility of amines and alcohols in water. Out of butan-1-ol and butan-1-amine, which will be more soluble in water and why? Amines are soluble in organic solvents like alcohol, ether and benzene. You may remember that alcohols are more polar than amines and form stronger intermolecular hydrogen bonds than amines.','[{"s": "Lower aliphatic amines are soluble in water because they can form hydrogen bonds with water molecules. However, solubility decreases with increase in molar mass of amines due to increase in size of the hydrophobic alkyl part. Higher amines are essentially insoluble in water. Considering the electronegativity of nitrogen of amine and oxygen of alcohol as 3.0 and 3.5 respectively, you can predict the pattern of solubility of amines and alcohols in water. Out of butan-1-ol and butan-1-amine, which will be more soluble in water and why? Amines are soluble in organic solvents like alcohol, ether and benzene. You may remember that alcohols are more polar than amines and form stronger intermolecular hydrogen bonds than amines.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15793,47,92,'paragraph',NULL,'Primary and secondary amines are engaged in intermolecular association due to hydrogen bonding between nitrogen of one and hydrogen of another molecule. This intermolecular association is more in primary amines than in secondary amines as there are two hydrogen atoms available for hydrogen bond formation in it. Tertiary amines do not have intermolecular association due to the absence of hydrogen atom available for hydrogen bond formation. Therefore, the order of boiling points of isomeric amines is as follows:','[{"s": "Primary and secondary amines are engaged in intermolecular association due to hydrogen bonding between nitrogen of one and hydrogen of another molecule. This intermolecular association is more in primary amines than in secondary amines as there are two hydrogen atoms available for hydrogen bond formation in it. Tertiary amines do not have intermolecular association due to the absence of hydrogen atom available for hydrogen bond formation. Therefore, the order of boiling points of isomeric amines is as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15794,47,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Primary > Secondary > Tertiary','[{"s": "Primary > Secondary > Tertiary", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15795,47,94,'paragraph',NULL,'Intermolecular hydrogen bonding in primary amines is shown in Fig. 13.2.','[{"s": "Intermolecular hydrogen bonding in primary amines is shown in Fig. 13.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15796,47,95,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image5.png'),
(15797,47,96,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.2 Intermolecular hydrogen bonding in primary amines','[{"s": "Fig. 13.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Intermolecular hydrogen bonding in primary amines", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15798,47,97,'paragraph',NULL,'Boiling points of amines, alcohols and alkanes of almost the same molar mass are shown in Table 13.2.','[{"s": "Boiling points of amines, alcohols and alkanes of almost the same molar mass are shown in Table 13.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15799,47,98,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 13.2: Comparison of Boiling Points of Amines, Alcohols and Alkanes of Similar Molecular Masses','[{"s": "Table 13.2: Comparison of Boiling Points of Amines, Alcohols and Alkanes of Similar Molecular Masses", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15800,47,99,'image',NULL,'Img02',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image60.png'),
(15801,47,100,'heading',3,'13.6 Chemical Reactions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15802,47,101,'paragraph',NULL,'Difference in electronegativity between nitrogen and hydrogen atoms and the presence of unshared pair of electrons over the nitrogen atom makes amines reactive. The number of hydrogen atoms attached to nitrogen atom also decides the course of reaction of amines; that is why primary (–NH 2 ), secondary Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image26.png and tertiary amines Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image91.png differ in many reactions. Moreover, amines behave as nucleophiles due to the presence of unshared electron pair. Some of the reactions of amines are described below:','[{"s": "Difference in electronegativity between nitrogen and hydrogen atoms and the presence of unshared pair of electrons over the nitrogen atom makes amines reactive. The number of hydrogen atoms attached to nitrogen atom also decides the course of reaction of amines; that is why primary (–NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), secondary", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image26.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and tertiary amines", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image91.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "differ in many reactions. Moreover, amines behave as nucleophiles due to the presence of unshared electron pair. Some of the reactions of amines are described below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15803,47,102,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Basic character of amines','[{"s": "1. Basic character of amines", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15804,47,103,'paragraph',NULL,'Amines, being basic in nature, react with acids to form salts.','[{"s": "Amines, being basic in nature, react with acids to form salts.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
