(15856,47,155,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Reaction with nitrous acid','[{"s": "5. Reaction with nitrous acid", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15857,47,156,'paragraph',NULL,'Three classes of amines react differently with nitrous acid which is prepared in situ from a mineral acid and sodium nitrite.','[{"s": "Three classes of amines react differently with nitrous acid which is prepared", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in situ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from a mineral acid and sodium nitrite.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15858,47,157,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Primary aliphatic amines react with nitrous acid to form aliphatic diazonium salts which being unstable, liberate nitrogen gas quantitatively and alcohols . Quantitative evolution of nitrogen is used in estimation of amino acids and proteins.','[{"s": "(a) Primary aliphatic amines react with nitrous acid to form aliphatic diazonium salts which being unstable, liberate nitrogen gas quantitatively and alcohols", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Quantitative evolution of nitrogen is used in estimation of amino acids and proteins.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["270825"]',NULL),
(15859,47,158,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image78.png'),
(15860,47,159,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Aromatic amines react with nitrous acid at low temperatures (273-278 K) to form diazonium salts, a very important class of compounds used for synthesis of a variety of aromatic compounds discussed in Section 13.7.','[{"s": "(b) Aromatic amines react with nitrous acid at low temperatures (273-278 K) to form diazonium salts, a very important class of compounds used for synthesis of a variety of aromatic compounds discussed in Section 13.7.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15861,47,160,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image38.png'),
(15862,47,161,'paragraph',NULL,'Secondary and tertiary amines react with nitrous acid in a different manner.','[{"s": "Secondary and tertiary amines react with nitrous acid in a different manner.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15863,47,162,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Reaction with arylsulphonyl chloride','[{"s": "6. Reaction with arylsulphonyl chloride", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15864,47,163,'paragraph',NULL,'Benzenesulphonyl chloride (C 6 H 5 SO 2 Cl), which is also known as Hinsberg’s reagent, reacts with primary and secondary amines to form sulphonamides. 2','[{"s": "Benzenesulphonyl chloride (C", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Cl), which is also known as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Hinsberg’s reagent,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "reacts with primary and secondary amines to form sulphonamides.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["270808", "270821"]',NULL),
(15865,47,164,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The reaction of benzenesulphonyl chloride with primary amine yields N-ethylbenzenesulphonyl amide.','[{"s": "(a) The reaction of benzenesulphonyl chloride with primary amine yields N-ethylbenzenesulphonyl amide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15866,47,165,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image71.png'),
(15867,47,166,'paragraph',NULL,'The hydrogen attached to nitrogen in sulphonamide is strongly acidic due to the presence of strong electron withdrawing sulphonyl group. Hence, it is soluble in alkali.','[{"s": "The hydrogen attached to nitrogen in sulphonamide is strongly acidic due to the presence of strong electron withdrawing sulphonyl group. Hence, it is soluble in alkali.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15868,47,167,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) In the reaction with secondary amine, N,N-diethyl-benzenesulphonamide is formed.','[{"s": "(b) In the reaction with secondary amine, N,N-diethyl-benzenesulphonamide is formed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15869,47,168,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image65.png'),
(15870,47,169,'paragraph',NULL,'Since N, N-diethylbenzene sulphonamide does not contain any hydrogen atom attached to nitrogen atom, it is not acidic and hence insoluble in alkali.','[{"s": "Since N, N-diethylbenzene sulphonamide does not contain any hydrogen atom attached to nitrogen atom, it is not acidic and hence insoluble in alkali.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15871,47,170,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Tertiary amines do not react with benzenesulphonyl chloride. This property of amines reacting with benzenesulphonyl chloride in a different manner is used for the distinction of primary, secondary and tertiary amines and also for the separation of a mixture of amines. However, these days benzenesulphonyl chloride is replaced by p -toluenesulphonyl chloride.','[{"s": "(c) Tertiary amines do not react with benzenesulphonyl chloride. This property of amines reacting with benzenesulphonyl chloride in a different manner is used for the distinction of primary, secondary and tertiary amines and also for the separation of a mixture of amines. However, these days benzenesulphonyl chloride is replaced by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-toluenesulphonyl chloride.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15872,47,171,'paragraph',NULL,'7. Electrophilic substitution','[{"s": "7. Electrophilic substitution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16468,48,227,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image32.png'),
(20508,54,146,'paragraph',NULL,'Remember ∆H = q p , heat absorbed by the system at constant pressure.','[{"s": "Remember ∆H = q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", heat absorbed by the system at constant pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20509,54,147,'paragraph',NULL,'∆H is negative for exothermic reactions which evolve heat during the reaction and ∆H is positive for endothermic reactions which absorb heat from the surroundings.','[{"s": "∆H is negative for exothermic reactions which evolve heat during the reaction and ∆H is positive for endothermic reactions which absorb heat from the surroundings.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15873,47,172,'paragraph',NULL,'You have read earlier that aniline is a resonance hybrid of five structures. Where do you find the maximum electron density in these structures? Ortho- and para- positions to the –NH 2 group become centres of high electron density. Thus –NH 2 group is ortho and para directing and a powerful activating group.','[{"s": "You have read earlier that aniline is a resonance hybrid of five structures. Where do you find the maximum electron density in these structures?", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ortho-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "para-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "positions to the –NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group become centres of high electron density. Thus –NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ortho", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "para", "t": "text"}, {"s": "directing and a powerful activating group.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15874,47,173,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Bromination : Aniline reacts with bromine water at room temperature to give a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline.','[{"s": "(a) Bromination", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ": Aniline reacts with bromine water at room temperature to give a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["270937"]',NULL),
(15875,47,174,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image58.png'),
(15876,47,175,'paragraph',NULL,'The main problem encountered during electrophilic substitution reactions of aromatic amines is that of their very high reactivity. Substitution tends to occur at ortho- and para- positions. If we have to prepare monosubstituted aniline derivative, how can the activating effect of –NH 2 group be controlled ? This can be done by protecting the -NH 2 group by acetylation with acetic anhydride, then carrying out the desired substitution followed by hydrolysis of the substituted amide to the substituted amine.','[{"s": "The main problem encountered during electrophilic substitution reactions of aromatic amines is that of their very high reactivity. Substitution tends to occur at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ortho-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "para-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "positions. If we have to prepare monosubstituted aniline derivative, how can the activating effect of –NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group be controlled ?", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This can be done by protecting the -NH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "group by acetylation with acetic anhydride, then carrying out the desired substitution followed by hydrolysis of the substituted amide to the substituted amine.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["270965"]',NULL),
(15877,47,176,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image43.png'),
(15878,47,177,'paragraph',NULL,'The lone pair of electrons on nitrogen of acetanilide interacts with oxygen atom due to resonance as shown below:','[{"s": "The lone pair of electrons on nitrogen of acetanilide interacts with oxygen atom due to resonance as shown below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15879,47,178,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image76.png'),
(15880,47,179,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence, the lone pair of electrons on nitrogen is less available for donation to benzene ring by resonance. Therefore, activating effect of –NHCOCH 3 group is less than that of amino group.','[{"s": "Hence, the lone pair of electrons on nitrogen is less available for donation to benzene ring by resonance. Therefore, activating effect of –NHCOCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group is less than that of amino group.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15881,47,180,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Nitration : Direct nitration of aniline yields tarry oxidation products in addition to the nitro derivatives. Moreover, in the strongly acidic medium, aniline is protonated to form the anilinium ion which is meta directing. That is why besides the ortho and para derivatives, significant amount of meta derivative is also formed.','[{"s": "(b) Nitration", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ": Direct nitration of aniline yields tarry oxidation products in addition to the nitro derivatives.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Moreover, in the strongly acidic medium, aniline is protonated to form the anilinium ion which is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "meta", "t": "text"}, {"s": "directing. That is why besides the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ortho", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "para", "t": "text"}, {"s": "derivatives, significant amount of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "meta", "t": "text"}, {"s": "derivative is also formed.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["270809"]',NULL),
(15882,47,181,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image64.png'),
(15883,47,182,'paragraph',NULL,'However, by protecting the –NH 2 group by acetylation reaction with acetic anhydride, the nitration reaction can be controlled and the p -nitro derivative can be obtained as the major product.','[{"s": "However, by protecting the –NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group by acetylation reaction with acetic anhydride, the nitration reaction can be controlled and the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-nitro derivative can be obtained as the major product.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15884,47,183,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image42.png'),
(15885,47,184,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Sulphonation: Aniline reacts with concentrated sulphuric acid to form anilinium hydrogensulphate which on heating with sulphuric acid at 453-473K produces p-aminobenzene sulphonic acid, commonly known as sulphanilic acid, as the major product.','[{"s": "(c) Sulphonation: Aniline reacts with concentrated sulphuric acid to form anilinium hydrogensulphate which on heating with sulphuric acid at 453-473K produces p-aminobenzene sulphonic acid, commonly known as sulphanilic acid, as the major product.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15886,47,185,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image31.png'),
(15887,47,186,'paragraph',NULL,'Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction (alkylation and acetylation) due to salt formation with aluminium chloride, the Lewis acid, which is used as a catalyst. Due to this, nitrogen of aniline acquires positive charge and hence acts as a strong deactivating group for further reaction.','[{"s": "Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction (alkylation and acetylation) due to salt formation with aluminium chloride, the Lewis acid, which is used as a catalyst. Due to this, nitrogen of aniline acquires positive charge and hence acts as a strong deactivating group for further reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15888,47,187,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15889,47,188,'paragraph',NULL,'13.4 Arrange the following in increasing order of their basic strength:','[{"s": "13.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Arrange the following in increasing order of their basic strength:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15890,47,189,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) C 2 H 5 NH 2 , C 6 H 5 NH 2 , NH 3 , C 6 H 5 CH 2 NH 2 and (C 2 H 5 )2NH','[{"s": "(i) C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and (C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")2NH", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15891,47,190,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) C 2 H 5 NH 2 , (C 2 H 5 )2NH, (C 2 H 5 )3N, C 6 H 5 NH 2','[{"s": "(ii) C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", (C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")2NH, (C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")3N, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15892,47,191,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) CH 3 NH 2 , (CH 3 )2NH, (CH 3 )3N, C 6 H 5 NH 2 , C 6 H 5 CH 2 NH 2 .','[{"s": "(iii) CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")2NH, (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")3N, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15893,47,192,'paragraph',NULL,'13.5 Complete the following acid-base reactions and name the products:','[{"s": "13.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Complete the following acid-base reactions and name the products:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15894,47,193,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) CH 3 CH 2 CH 2 NH 2 + HCl → (ii) (C 2 H 5 )3N + HCl →','[{"s": "(i) CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ HCl → (ii) (C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")3N + HCl →", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15895,47,194,'paragraph',NULL,'13.6 Write reactions of the final alkylation product of aniline with excess of methyl iodide in the presence of sodium carbonate solution.','[{"s": "13.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Write reactions of the final alkylation product of aniline with excess of methyl iodide in the presence of sodium carbonate solution.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15896,47,195,'paragraph',NULL,'13.7 Write chemical reaction of aniline with benzoyl chloride and write the name of the product obtained.','[{"s": "13.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Write chemical reaction of aniline with benzoyl chloride and write the name of the product obtained.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15897,47,196,'paragraph',NULL,'13.8 Write structures of different isomers corresponding to the molecular formula, C 3 H 9 N. Write IUPAC names of the isomers which will liberate nitrogen gas on treatment with nitrous acid.','[{"s": "13.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Write structures of different isomers corresponding to the molecular formula, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N. Write IUPAC names of the isomers which will liberate nitrogen gas on treatment with nitrous acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15898,47,197,'paragraph',NULL,'II. Diazonium Salts','[{"s": "II. Diazonium Salts", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15899,47,198,'paragraph',NULL,'The diazonium salts have the general formula Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image30.png where R stands for an aryl group and Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image11.png ion may be Cl – Br, – Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image75.png , Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image74.png , etc. They are named by suffixing diazonium to the name of the parent hydrocarbon from which they are formed, followed by the name of anion such as chloride, hydrogensulphate, etc. The Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image48.png group is called diazonium group. For example, Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image35.png is named as benzenediazonium chloride and C 6 H 5 N 2 + HSO 4 – is known as benzenediazonium hydrogensulphate.','[{"s": "The diazonium salts have the general formula", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image30.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "where R stands for an aryl group and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image11.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "ion may be Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image75.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image74.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", etc. They are named by suffixing diazonium to the name of the parent hydrocarbon from which they are formed, followed by the name of anion such as chloride, hydrogensulphate, etc. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image48.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "group is called diazonium group. For example,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image35.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is named as benzenediazonium chloride and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "HSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is known as benzenediazonium hydrogensulphate.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15900,47,199,'paragraph',NULL,'Primary aliphatic amines form highly unstable alkyldiazonium salts (refer to Section 13.6). Primary aromatic amines form arenediazonium salts which are stable for a short time in solution at low temperatures (273-278 K). The stability of arenediazonium ion is explained on the basis of resonance.','[{"s": "Primary aliphatic amines form highly unstable alkyldiazonium salts (refer to Section 13.6).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Primary aromatic amines form arenediazonium salts which are stable for a short time in solution at low temperatures (273-278 K). The stability of arenediazonium ion is explained on the basis of resonance.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["270939"]',NULL),
(15901,47,200,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image24.png'),
(15902,47,201,'heading',3,'13.7 Method of Preparation of Diazoniun Salts',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15903,47,202,'paragraph',NULL,'Benzenediazonium chloride is prepared by the reaction of aniline with nitrous acid at 273-278K. Nitrous acid is produced in the reaction mixture by the reaction of sodium nitrite with hydrochloric acid. The conversion of primary aromatic amines into diazonium salts is known as diazotisation . Due to its instability, the diazonium salt is not generally stored and is used immediately after its preparation.','[{"s": "Benzenediazonium chloride is prepared by the reaction of aniline with nitrous acid at 273-278K. Nitrous acid is produced in the reaction mixture by the reaction of sodium nitrite with hydrochloric acid. The conversion of primary aromatic amines into diazonium salts is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "diazotisation", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Due to its instability, the diazonium salt is not generally stored and is used immediately after its preparation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15904,47,203,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image96.png'),
(15905,47,204,'heading',3,'13.8 Physical Properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15906,47,205,'paragraph',NULL,'Benzenediazonium chloride is a colourless crystalline solid. It is readily soluble in water and is stable in cold but reacts with water when warmed. It decomposes easily in the dry state. Benzenediazonium fluoroborate is water insoluble and stable at room temperature.','[{"s": "Benzenediazonium chloride is a colourless crystalline solid. It is readily soluble in water and is stable in cold but reacts with water when warmed. It decomposes easily in the dry state. Benzenediazonium fluoroborate is water insoluble and stable at room temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15907,47,206,'heading',3,'13.9 Chemical Reactions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15908,47,207,'paragraph',NULL,'The reactions of diazonium salts can be broadly divided into two categories, namely (A) reactions involving displacement of nitrogen and (B) reactions involving retention of diazo group.','[{"s": "The reactions of diazonium salts can be broadly divided into two categories, namely (A) reactions involving displacement of nitrogen and (B) reactions involving retention of diazo group.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15909,47,208,'paragraph',NULL,'A. Reactions involving displacement of nitrogen','[{"s": "A. Reactions involving displacement of nitrogen", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15910,47,209,'paragraph',NULL,'Diazonium group being a very good leaving group, is substituted by other groups such as Cl – , Br – , I – , CN – and OH – which displace nitrogen from the aromatic ring. The nitrogen formed escapes from the reaction mixture as a gas.','[{"s": "Diazonium group being a very good leaving group, is substituted by other groups such as Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which displace nitrogen from the aromatic ring. The nitrogen formed escapes from the reaction mixture as a gas.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15911,47,210,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Replacement by halide or cyanide ion : The Cl – , Br – and CN – nucleophiles can easily be introduced in the benzene ring in the presence of Cu(I) ion. This reaction is called Sandmeyer reaction .','[{"s": "1. Replacement by halide or cyanide ion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": The Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and CN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nucleophiles can easily be introduced in the benzene ring in the presence of Cu(I) ion.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This reaction is called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Sandmeyer reaction", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["270959"]',NULL),
(15912,47,211,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image79.png'),
(15913,47,212,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternatively, chlorine or bromine can also be introduced in the benzene ring by treating the diazonium salt solution with corresponding halogen acid in the presence of copper powder. This is referred as Gatterman reaction .','[{"s": "Alternatively, chlorine or bromine can also be introduced in the benzene ring by treating the diazonium salt solution with corresponding halogen acid in the presence of copper powder.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This is referred as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gatterman reaction", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["270938"]',NULL),
(15914,47,213,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image29.png'),
(15915,47,214,'paragraph',NULL,'The yield in Sandmeyer reaction is found to be better than Gattermann reaction.','[{"s": "The yield in Sandmeyer reaction is found to be better than Gattermann reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15916,47,215,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Replacement by iodide ion : Iodine is not easily introduced into the benzene ring directly, but, when the diazonium salt solution is treated with potassium iodide, iodobenzene is formed.','[{"s": "2. Replacement by iodide ion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Iodine is not easily introduced into the benzene ring directly, but, when the diazonium salt solution is treated with potassium iodide, iodobenzene is formed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15917,47,216,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image89.png'),
(15918,47,217,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Replacement by fluoride ion : When arenediazonium chloride is treated with fluoroboric acid, arene diazonium fluoroborate is precipitated which on heating decomposes to yield aryl fluoride.','[{"s": "3. Replacement by fluoride ion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": When arenediazonium chloride is treated with fluoroboric acid, arene diazonium fluoroborate is precipitated which on heating decomposes to yield aryl fluoride.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15919,47,218,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image19.png'),
(15920,47,219,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Replacement by H : Certain mild reducing agents like hypophosphorous acid (phosphinic acid) or ethanol reduce diazonium salts to arenes and themselves get oxidised to phosphorous acid and ethanal, respectively. 2','[{"s": "4.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Replacement by H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ": Certain mild reducing agents like hypophosphorous acid (phosphinic acid) or ethanol reduce diazonium salts to arenes and themselves get oxidised to phosphorous acid and ethanal, respectively.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["270960", "270961"]',NULL),
(15921,47,220,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image20.png'),
(15922,47,221,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Replacement by hydroxyl group : If the temperature of the diazonium salt solution is allowed to rise upto 283 K, the salt gets hydrolysed to phenol.','[{"s": "5. Replacement by hydroxyl group", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": If the temperature of the diazonium salt solution is allowed to rise upto 283 K, the salt gets hydrolysed to phenol.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15923,47,222,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image9.png'),
(15924,47,223,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Replacement by –NO 2 group : When diazonium fluoroborate is heated with aqueous sodium nitrite solution in the presence of copper, the diazonium group is replaced by –NO 2 group.','[{"s": "6. Replacement by –NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": When diazonium fluoroborate is heated with aqueous sodium nitrite solution in the presence of copper, the diazonium group is replaced by –NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "group.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15925,47,224,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image81.png'),
(15926,47,225,'paragraph',NULL,'B. Reactions involving retention of diazo group coupling reactions','[{"s": "B.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Reactions involving retention of diazo group coupling reactions", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["270969"]',NULL),
(15927,47,226,'paragraph',NULL,'The azo products obtained have an extended conjugate system having both the aromatic rings joined through the –N=N– bond. These compounds are often coloured and are used as dyes. Benzene diazonium chloride reacts with phenol in which the phenol molecule at its para position is coupled with the diazonium salt to form p -hydroxyazobenzene. This type of reaction is known as coupling reaction. 3 Similarly the reaction of diazonium salt with aniline yields p -aminoazobenzene. This is an example of electrophilic substitution reaction.','[{"s": "The azo products obtained have an extended conjugate system having both the aromatic rings joined through the –N=N– bond.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "These compounds are often coloured and are used as dyes. Benzene diazonium chloride reacts with phenol in which the phenol molecule at its para position is coupled with the diazonium salt to form", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-hydroxyazobenzene.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This type of reaction is known as coupling reaction.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Similarly the reaction of diazonium salt with aniline yields", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-aminoazobenzene. This is an example of electrophilic substitution reaction.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["270816", "270939", "270964", "270968"]',NULL),
(15928,47,227,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image87.png'),
(15929,47,228,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image33.png'),
(15930,47,229,'heading',3,'13.10 Importance of Diazonium Salts in Synthesis of Aromatic Compounds',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15931,47,230,'paragraph',NULL,'From the above reactions, it is clear that the diazonium salts are very good intermediates for the introduction of –F, –Cl, –Br, –I, –CN, –OH, –NO 2 groups into the aromatic ring.','[{"s": "From the above reactions, it is clear that the diazonium salts are very good intermediates for the introduction of –F, –Cl, –Br, –I, –CN, –OH, –NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "groups into the aromatic ring.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15932,47,231,'paragraph',NULL,'Aryl fluorides and iodides cannot be prepared by direct halogenation. The cyano group cannot be introduced by nucleophilic substitution of chlorine in chlorobenzene but cyanobenzene can be easily obtained from diazonium salt.','[{"s": "Aryl fluorides and iodides cannot be prepared by direct halogenation. The cyano group cannot be introduced by nucleophilic substitution of chlorine in chlorobenzene but cyanobenzene can be easily obtained from diazonium salt.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15933,47,232,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the replacement of diazo group by other groups is helpful in preparing those substituted aromatic compounds which cannot be prepared by direct substitution in benzene or substituted benzene.','[{"s": "Thus, the replacement of diazo group by other groups is helpful in preparing those substituted aromatic compounds which cannot be prepared by direct substitution in benzene or substituted benzene.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15934,47,233,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.5','[{"s": "Example 13.5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15935,47,234,'paragraph',NULL,'How will you convert 4-nitrotoluene to 2-bromobenzoic acid','[{"s": "How will you convert 4-nitrotoluene to 2-bromobenzoic acid", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15936,47,235,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15937,47,236,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Organic_Compounds_Containing_Nitrogen-image37.png'),
(15938,47,237,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Question','[{"s": "Intext Question", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15939,47,238,'paragraph',NULL,'13.9 Convert','[{"s": "13.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Convert", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15940,47,239,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) 3-Methylaniline into 3-nitrotoluene.','[{"s": "(i) 3-Methylaniline into 3-nitrotoluene.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15941,47,240,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Aniline into 1,3,5 - tribromobenzene.','[{"s": "(ii) Aniline into 1,3,5 - tribromobenzene.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15942,47,241,'heading',5,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16469,48,228,'paragraph',NULL,'By this process plants make food for themselves as well as for animals and human beings. Unlike CO, it is not poisonous. But the increase in combustion of fossil fuels and decomposition of limestone for cement manufacture in recent years seem to increase the CO 2 content of the atmosphere. This may lead to increase in green house effect and thus, raise the temperature of the atmosphere which might have serious consequences.','[{"s": "By this process plants make food for themselves as well as for animals and human beings. Unlike CO, it is not poisonous. But the increase in combustion of fossil fuels and decomposition of limestone for cement manufacture in recent years seem to increase the CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "content of the atmosphere. This may lead to increase in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "green house effect", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and thus, raise the temperature of the atmosphere which might have serious consequences.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(20510,54,148,'paragraph',NULL,'At constant volume (∆V = 0), ∆U = q V , therefore equation 6.8 becomes','[{"s": "At constant volume (∆V = 0), ∆U = q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", therefore equation 6.8 becomes", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20511,54,149,'paragraph',NULL,'∆H = ∆U = q V','[{"s": "∆H = ∆U = q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15943,47,242,'paragraph',NULL,'Amines can be considered as derivatives of ammonia obtained by replacement of hydrogen atoms with alkyl or aryl groups. Replacement of one hydrogen atom of ammonia gives rise to structure of the type R-NH 2 , known as primary amine. Secondary amines are characterised by the structure R 2 NH or R-NHR′ and tertiary amines by R 3 N, RNR′R′′ or R 2 NR′. Secondary and tertiary amines are known as simple amines if the alkyl or aryl groups are the same and mixed amines if the groups are different. Like ammonia, all the three types of amines have one unshared electron pair on nitrogen atom due to which they behave as Lewis bases.','[{"s": "Amines can be considered as derivatives of ammonia obtained by replacement of hydrogen atoms with alkyl or aryl groups. Replacement of one hydrogen atom of ammonia gives rise to structure of the type R-NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", known as primary amine. Secondary amines are characterised by the structure R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH or R-NHR′ and tertiary amines by R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N, RNR′R′′ or R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NR′. Secondary and tertiary amines are known as simple amines if the alkyl or aryl groups are the same and mixed amines if the groups are different. Like ammonia, all the three types of amines have one unshared electron pair on nitrogen atom due to which they behave as Lewis bases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15944,47,243,'paragraph',NULL,'Amines are usually formed from nitro compounds, halides, amides, imides, etc. They exhibit hydrogen bonding which influence their physical properties. In alkylamines, a combination of electron releasing, steric and H-bonding factors influence the stability of the substituted ammonium cations in protic polar solvents and thus affect the basic nature of amines. Alkyl amines are found to be stronger bases than ammonia. In aromatic amines, electron releasing and withdrawing groups, respectively increase and decrease their basic character. 2 Aniline is a weaker base than ammonia. Reactions of amines are governed by availability of the unshared pair of electrons on nitrogen. Influence of the number of hydrogen atoms at nitrogen atom on the type of reactions and nature of products is responsible for identification and distinction between primary, secondary and tertiary amines. p-Toluenesulphonyl chloride is used for the identification of primary, secondary and tertiary amines. Presence of amino group in aromatic ring enhances reactivity of the aromatic amines. Reactivity of aromatic amines can be controlled by acylation process, i.e., by treating with acetyl chloride or acetic anhydride. Tertiary amines like trimethylamine are used as insect attractants.','[{"s": "Amines are usually formed from nitro compounds, halides, amides, imides, etc. They exhibit hydrogen bonding which influence their physical properties. In alkylamines, a combination of electron releasing, steric and H-bonding factors influence the stability of the substituted ammonium cations in protic polar solvents and thus affect the basic nature of amines. Alkyl amines are found to be stronger bases than ammonia.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In aromatic amines, electron releasing and withdrawing groups, respectively increase and decrease their basic character.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Aniline is a weaker base than ammonia. Reactions of amines are governed by availability of the unshared pair of electrons on nitrogen. Influence of the number of hydrogen atoms at nitrogen atom on the type of reactions and nature of products is responsible for identification and distinction between primary, secondary and tertiary amines. p-Toluenesulphonyl chloride is used for the identification of primary, secondary and tertiary amines. Presence of amino group in aromatic ring enhances reactivity of the aromatic amines. Reactivity of aromatic amines can be controlled by acylation process, i.e., by treating with acetyl chloride or acetic anhydride. Tertiary amines like trimethylamine are used as insect attractants.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["270810", "270811"]',NULL),
(15945,47,244,'paragraph',NULL,'Aryldiazonium salts, usually obtained from arylamines, undergo replacement of the diazonium group with a variety of nucleophiles to provide advantageous methods for producing aryl halides, cyanides, phenols and arenes by reductive removal of the diazo group. Coupling reaction of aryldiazonium salts with phenols or arylamines give rise to the formation of azo dyes.','[{"s": "Aryldiazonium salts, usually obtained from arylamines, undergo replacement of the diazonium group with a variety of nucleophiles to provide advantageous methods for producing aryl halides, cyanides, phenols and arenes by reductive removal of the diazo group. Coupling reaction of aryldiazonium salts with phenols or arylamines give rise to the formation of azo dyes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15946,47,245,'paragraph',NULL,'Exercises','[{"s": "Exercises", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15947,47,246,'paragraph',NULL,'13.1 Write IUPAC names of the following compounds and classify them into primary, secondary and tertiary amines.','[{"s": "13.1 Write IUPAC names of the following compounds and classify them into primary, secondary and tertiary amines.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15948,47,247,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) (CH 3 ) 2 CHNH 2 (ii) CH 3 (CH 2 ) 2 NH 2 (iii) CH 3 NHCH(CH 3 ) 2','[{"s": "(i) (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CHNH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(ii) CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(iii) CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NHCH(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15949,47,248,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) (CH 3 ) 3 CNH 2 (v) C 6 H 5 NHCH 3 (vi) (CH 3 CH 2 ) 2 NCH 3','[{"s": "(iv) (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CNH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(v) C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NHCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(vi) (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NCH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15950,47,249,'paragraph',NULL,'(vii) m–BrC 6 H 4 NH 2','[{"s": "(vii) m–BrC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15951,47,250,'paragraph',NULL,'13.2Give one chemical test to distinguish between the following pairs of compounds.','[{"s": "13.2Give one chemical test to distinguish between the following pairs of compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15952,47,251,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Methylamine and dimethylamine (ii) Secondary and tertiary amines','[{"s": "(i) Methylamine and dimethylamine (ii) Secondary and tertiary amines", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15953,47,252,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Ethylamine and aniline (iv) Aniline and benzylamine','[{"s": "(iii) Ethylamine and aniline (iv) Aniline and benzylamine", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15954,47,253,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) Aniline and N-methylaniline.','[{"s": "(v) Aniline and N-methylaniline.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15955,47,254,'paragraph',NULL,'13.3Account for the following:','[{"s": "13.3Account for the following:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15956,47,255,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) pKb of aniline is more than that of methylamine.','[{"s": "(i) pKb of aniline is more than that of methylamine.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15957,47,256,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Ethylamine is soluble in water whereas aniline is not.','[{"s": "(ii) Ethylamine is soluble in water whereas aniline is not.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15958,47,257,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Methylamine in water reacts with ferric chloride to precipitate hydrated ferric oxide.','[{"s": "(iii) Methylamine in water reacts with ferric chloride to precipitate hydrated ferric oxide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15959,47,258,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Although amino group is o– and p– directing in aromatic electrophilic substitution reactions, aniline on nitration gives a substantial amount of m-nitroaniline.','[{"s": "(iv) Although amino group is o– and p– directing in aromatic electrophilic substitution reactions, aniline on nitration gives a substantial amount of m-nitroaniline.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15960,47,259,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction.','[{"s": "(v) Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(15961,47,260,'paragraph',NULL,'(vi) Diazonium salts of aromatic amines are more stable than those of aliphatic amines. 2','[{"s": "(vi) Diazonium salts of aromatic amines are more stable than those of aliphatic amines.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["270815", "270950"]',NULL),
(15962,47,261,'paragraph',NULL,'(vii) Gabriel phthalimide synthesis is preferred for synthesising primary amines.','[{"s": "(vii) Gabriel phthalimide synthesis is preferred for synthesising primary amines.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
