(16305,48,64,'paragraph',NULL,'White fumes appear around the bottle of anhydrous aluminium chloride. Give reason.','[{"s": "White fumes appear around the bottle of anhydrous aluminium chloride. Give reason.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16306,48,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16307,48,66,'paragraph',NULL,'Anhydrous aluminium chloride is partially hydrolysed with atmospheric moisture to liberate HCl gas. Moist HCl appears white in colour.','[{"s": "Anhydrous aluminium chloride is partially hydrolysed with atmospheric moisture to liberate HCl gas. Moist HCl appears white in colour.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16308,48,67,'heading',3,'11.2 IMPORTANT TRENDS AND ANOMALOUS PROPERTIES OF BORON',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16309,48,68,'paragraph',NULL,'Certain important trends can be observed in the chemical behaviour of group 13 elements. The tri-chlorides, bromides and iodides of all these elements being covalent in nature are hydrolysed in water. Species like tetrahedral [M(OH) 4 ] – and octahedral [M(H 2 O) 6 ] 3+ , except in boron, exist in aqueous medium.','[{"s": "Certain important trends can be observed in the chemical behaviour of group 13 elements. The tri-chlorides, bromides and iodides of all these elements being covalent in nature are hydrolysed in water. Species like tetrahedral [M(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and octahedral [M(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", except in boron, exist in aqueous medium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16310,48,69,'paragraph',NULL,'The monomeric trihalides, being electron deficient, are strong Lewis acids. Boron trifluoride easily reacts with Lewis bases such as NH3 to complete octet around boron.','[{"s": "The monomeric trihalides, being electron deficient, are strong Lewis acids.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Boron trifluoride easily reacts with Lewis bases such as NH3 to complete octet around boron.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["113920"]',NULL),
(16311,48,70,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image10.png'),
(16312,48,71,'paragraph',NULL,'It is due to the absence of d orbitals that the maximum covalence of B is 4. Since the d orbitals are available with Al and other elements, the maximum covalence can be expected beyond 4. Most of the other metal halides (e.g., AlCl 3 ) are dimerised through halogen bridging (e.g., Al 2 Cl 6 ). The metal species completes its octet by accepting electrons from halogen in these halogen bridged molecules.','[{"s": "It is due to the absence of d orbitals that the maximum covalence of B is 4. Since the d orbitals are available with Al and other elements, the maximum covalence can be expected beyond 4. Most of the other metal halides (e.g., AlCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") are dimerised through halogen bridging (e.g., Al", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). The metal species completes its octet by accepting electrons from halogen in these halogen bridged molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16313,48,72,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 11.3','[{"s": "Problem 11.3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16314,48,73,'paragraph',NULL,'Boron is unable to form BF63– ion. Explain.','[{"s": "Boron is unable to form BF63– ion. Explain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16315,48,74,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16316,48,75,'paragraph',NULL,'Due to non-availability of d orbitals, boron is unable to expand its octet. Therefore, the maximum covalence of boron cannot exceed 4.','[{"s": "Due to non-availability of d orbitals, boron is unable to expand its octet. Therefore, the maximum covalence of boron cannot exceed 4.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16317,48,76,'heading',3,'11.3 SOME IMPORTANT COMPOUNDS OF BORON',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16318,48,77,'paragraph',NULL,'Some useful compounds of boron are borax, orthoboric acid and diborane. We will briefly study their chemistry.','[{"s": "Some useful compounds of boron are borax, orthoboric acid and diborane. We will briefly study their chemistry.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16319,48,78,'heading',4,'11.3.1 Borax',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16320,48,79,'paragraph',NULL,'It is the most important compound of boron. It is a white crystalline solid of formula Na 2 B 4 O 7 ⋅10H 2 O. In fact it contains the tetranuclear units P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image18.png and correct formula; therefore, is Na 2 [B 4 O 5 (OH)4].8H 2 O. Borax dissolves in water to give an alkaline solution.','[{"s": "It is the most important compound of boron. It is a white crystalline solid of formula Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⋅10H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O. In fact it contains the tetranuclear units", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image18.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and correct formula; therefore, is Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(OH)4].8H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O. Borax dissolves in water to give an alkaline solution.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16321,48,80,'paragraph',NULL,'Na 2 B 4 O 7 + 7H 2 O → 2NaOH + 4H 3 BO 3','[{"s": "Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 7H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O → 2NaOH + 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16322,48,81,'paragraph',NULL,'Orthoboric acid','[{"s": "Orthoboric acid", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16323,48,82,'paragraph',NULL,'On heating, borax first loses water molecules and swells up. On further heating it turns into a transparent liquid, which solidifies into glass like material known as borax bead.','[{"s": "On heating, borax first loses water molecules and swells up.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "On further heating it turns into a transparent liquid, which solidifies into glass like material known as borax bead.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["113921"]',NULL),
(24135,61,147,'paragraph',NULL,'An equipotential surface is a surface with a constant value of potential at all points on the surface. For a single charge q, the potential is given by Eq. (2.8):','[{"s": "An equipotential surface is a surface with a constant value of potential at all points on the surface. For a single charge q, the potential is given by Eq. (2.8):", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16324,48,83,'paragraph',NULL,'Na2B4O7.10H2O P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image14.png Na2B4O7 P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image21.png 2NaBO2 + B 2 O 3','[{"s": "Na2B4O7.10H2O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Na2B4O7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image21.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "2NaBO2 + B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16325,48,84,'paragraph',NULL,'Sodium metaborate Boric anhydride','[{"s": "Sodium metaborate Boric anhydride", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16326,48,85,'paragraph',NULL,'The metaborates of many transition metals have characteristic colours and, therefore, borax bead test can be used to identify them in the laboratory . For example, when borax is heated in a Bunsen burner flame with CoO on a loop of platinum wire, a blue coloured Co(BO 2 ) 2 bead is formed.','[{"s": "The metaborates of many transition metals have characteristic colours and, therefore, borax bead test can be used to identify them in the laboratory", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For example, when borax is heated in a Bunsen burner flame with CoO on a loop of platinum wire, a blue coloured Co(BO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bead is formed.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["113922"]',NULL),
(16327,48,86,'heading',4,'11.3.2 Orthoboric acid',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16328,48,87,'paragraph',NULL,'Orthoboric acid, H 3 BO 3 is a white crystalline solid, with soapy touch. It is sparingly soluble in water but highly soluble in hot water. It can be prepared by acidifying an aqueous solution of borax.','[{"s": "Orthoboric acid, H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a white crystalline solid, with soapy touch. It is sparingly soluble in water but highly soluble in hot water. It can be prepared by acidifying an aqueous solution of borax.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16329,48,88,'paragraph',NULL,'Na 2 B 4 O 7 + 2HCl + 5H 2 O → 2NaCl + 4B(OH) 3','[{"s": "Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2HCl + 5H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O → 2NaCl + 4B(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16330,48,89,'paragraph',NULL,'It is also formed by the hydrolysis (reaction with water or dilute acid) of most boron compounds (halides, hydrides, etc.). It has a layer structure in which planar BO3 units are joined by hydrogen bonds as shown in Fig. 11.1.','[{"s": "It is also formed by the hydrolysis (reaction with water or dilute acid) of most boron compounds (halides, hydrides, etc.). It has a layer structure in which planar BO3 units are joined by hydrogen bonds as shown in Fig. 11.1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16331,48,90,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image4.png'),
(16332,48,91,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11. 1 Structure of boric acid; the dotted lines represent hydrogen bonds','[{"s": "Fig. 11. 1 Structure of boric acid; the dotted lines represent hydrogen bonds", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16333,48,92,'paragraph',NULL,'Boric acid is a weak monobasic acid. It is not a protonic acid but acts as a Lewis acid by accepting electrons from a hydroxyl ion:','[{"s": "Boric acid is a weak monobasic acid. It is not a protonic acid but acts as a Lewis acid by accepting electrons from a hydroxyl ion:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16334,48,93,'paragraph',NULL,'B(OH) 3 + 2HOH → [B(OH) 4 ] – + H3O +','[{"s": "B(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2HOH → [B(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ H3O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16335,48,94,'paragraph',NULL,'On heating, orthoboric acid above 370K forms metaboric acid, HBO2 which on further heating yields boric oxide, B2O3.','[{"s": "On heating, orthoboric acid above 370K forms metaboric acid, HBO2 which on further heating yields boric oxide, B2O3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16336,48,95,'paragraph',NULL,'H 3 BO 3 P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image31.png HBO 2 P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image31.png B 2 O 3','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image31.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "HBO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image31.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(16337,48,96,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 11.4','[{"s": "Problem 11.4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16338,48,97,'paragraph',NULL,'Why is boric acid considered as a weak acid?','[{"s": "Why is boric acid considered as a weak acid?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16339,48,98,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16340,48,99,'paragraph',NULL,'Because it is not able to release H+ ions on its own. It receives OH – ions from water molecule to complete its octet and in turn releases H + ions.','[{"s": "Because it is not able to release H+ ions on its own. It receives OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions from water molecule to complete its octet and in turn releases H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16341,48,100,'heading',4,'11.3.3 Diborane, B2H6',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16342,48,101,'paragraph',NULL,'The simplest boron hydride known, is diborane. It is prepared by treating boron trifluoride with LiAlH 4 in diethyl ether.','[{"s": "The simplest boron hydride known, is diborane. It is prepared by treating boron trifluoride with LiAlH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in diethyl ether.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16343,48,102,'paragraph',NULL,'4BF 3 + 3 LiAlH 4 → 2B 2 H 6 + 3LiF + 3AlF 3','[{"s": "4BF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 3 LiAlH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 2B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 3LiF + 3AlF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16344,48,103,'paragraph',NULL,'A convenient laboratory method for the preparation of diborane involves the oxidation of sodium borohydride with iodine.','[{"s": "A convenient laboratory method for the preparation of diborane involves the oxidation of sodium borohydride with iodine.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24136,61,148,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image20.png'),
(16345,48,104,'paragraph',NULL,'2NaBH 4 + I 2 → B 2 H 6 + 2NaI + H 2','[{"s": "2NaBH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2NaI + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16346,48,105,'paragraph',NULL,'Diborane is produced on an industrial scale by the reaction of BF 3 with sodium hydride.','[{"s": "Diborane is produced on an industrial scale by the reaction of BF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with sodium hydride.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16347,48,106,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image35.png'),
(16348,48,107,'paragraph',NULL,'Diborane is a colourless, highly toxic gas with a b.p. of 180 K. Diborane catches fire spontaneously upon exposure to air. It burns in oxygen releasing an enormous amount of energy.','[{"s": "Diborane is a colourless, highly toxic gas with a b.p. of 180 K. Diborane catches fire spontaneously upon exposure to air. It burns in oxygen releasing an enormous amount of energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16349,48,108,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image17.png'),
(16350,48,109,'paragraph',NULL,'Most of the higher boranes are also spontaneously flammable in air. Boranes are readily hydrolysed by water to give boric acid.','[{"s": "Most of the higher boranes are also spontaneously flammable in air. Boranes are readily hydrolysed by water to give boric acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16351,48,110,'paragraph',NULL,'B 2 H 6 (g) + 6H 2 O(l) → 2B(OH) 3 (aq) + 6H 2 (g)','[{"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 6H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) → 2B(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 6H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16352,48,111,'paragraph',NULL,'Diborane undergoes cleavage reactions with Lewis bases(L) to give borane adducts, BH3⋅L','[{"s": "Diborane undergoes cleavage reactions with Lewis bases(L) to give borane adducts, BH3⋅L", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16353,48,112,'paragraph',NULL,'B 2 H 6 + 2 NMe 3 → 2BH 3 ⋅NMe 3','[{"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2 NMe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 2BH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⋅NMe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16354,48,113,'paragraph',NULL,'B 2 H 6 + 2 CO → 2BH 3 ⋅CO','[{"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2 CO → 2BH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⋅CO", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16355,48,114,'paragraph',NULL,'Reaction of ammonia with diborane gives initially B 2 H6.2NH 3 which is formulated as [BH 2 (NH 3 ) 2 ] + [BH 4 ] – ; further heating gives borazine, B 3 N 3 H 6 known as “inorganic benzene” in view of its ring structure with alternate BH and NH groups.','[{"s": "Reaction of ammonia with diborane gives initially B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H6.2NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which is formulated as [BH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[BH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; further heating gives borazine, B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "known as “inorganic benzene” in view of its ring structure with alternate BH and NH groups.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16356,48,115,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image27.png'),
(16357,48,116,'paragraph',NULL,'The structure of diborane is shown in Fig.11.2(a). The four terminal hydrogen atoms and the two boron atoms lie in one plane . Above and below this plane, there are two bridging hydrogen atoms. The four terminal B-H bonds are regular two centre-two electron bonds while the two bridge (B-H-B) bonds are different and can be described in terms of three centre–two electron bonds shown in Fig.11.2 (b).','[{"s": "The structure of diborane is shown in Fig.11.2(a). The four terminal hydrogen atoms and the two boron atoms lie in one plane", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Above and below this plane, there are two bridging hydrogen atoms.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The four terminal B-H bonds are regular two centre-two electron bonds while the two bridge (B-H-B) bonds are different and can be described in terms of three centre–two electron bonds shown in Fig.11.2 (b).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["113923"]',NULL),
(16358,48,117,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image34.png'),
(16359,48,118,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.11.2(a) The structure of diborane, B 2 H 6','[{"s": "Fig.11.2(a) The structure of diborane, B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16360,48,119,'paragraph',NULL,'Boron also forms a series of hydridoborates; the most important one is the tetrahedral [BH 4 ] – ion. Tetrahydridoborates of several metals are known. Lithium and sodium tetra-hydridoborates, also known as borohydrides , are prepared by the reaction of metal hydrides with B2H6 in diethyl ether.','[{"s": "Boron also forms a series of hydridoborates; the most important one is the tetrahedral [BH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion. Tetrahydridoborates of several metals are known. Lithium and sodium tetra-hydridoborates, also known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "borohydrides", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", are prepared by the reaction of metal hydrides with B2H6 in diethyl ether.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16361,48,120,'paragraph',NULL,'2MH + B 2 H 6 → 2 M + [BH 4 ] – (M = Li or Na)','[{"s": "2MH + B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 2 M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[BH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(M = Li or Na)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16362,48,121,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image26.png'),
(16363,48,122,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.11.2(b) Bonding in diborane. Each B atom uses sp3 hybrids for bonding. Out','[{"s": "Fig.11.2(b) Bonding in diborane. Each B atom uses sp3 hybrids for bonding. Out", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16364,48,123,'paragraph',NULL,'of the four sp3 hybrids on each B atom, one is without an electron shown in broken lines. The terminal B-H bonds are normal 2-centre-2-electron bonds but the two bridge bonds are 3-centre-2-electron bonds. The 3-centre-2-electron bridge bonds are also referred to as banana bonds.','[{"s": "of the four sp3 hybrids on each B atom, one is without an electron shown in broken lines. The terminal B-H bonds are normal 2-centre-2-electron bonds but the two bridge bonds are 3-centre-2-electron bonds. The 3-centre-2-electron bridge bonds are also referred to as banana bonds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16365,48,124,'paragraph',NULL,'Both LiBH 4 and NaBH 4 are used as reducing agents in organic synthesis. They are useful starting materials for preparing other metal borohydrides.','[{"s": "Both LiBH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and NaBH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are used as reducing agents in organic synthesis. They are useful starting materials for preparing other metal borohydrides.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16366,48,125,'heading',3,'11.4 USES OF BORON AND ALUMINIUM AND THEIR COMPOUNDS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16367,48,126,'paragraph',NULL,'Boron being extremely hard refractory solid of high melting point, low density and very low electrical conductivity, finds many applications. Boron fibres are used in making bullet-proof vest and light composite material for aircraft. The boron-10 (10B) isotope has high ability to absorb neutrons and, therefore, metal borides are used in nuclear industry as protective shields and control rods. The main industrial application of borax and boric acid is in the manufacture of heat resistant glasses (e.g., Pyrex), glass-wool and fibreglass. Borax is also used as a flux for soldering metals, for heat, scratch and stain resistant glazed coating to earthenwares and as constituent of medicinal soaps. An aqueous solution of orthoboric acid is generally used as a mild antiseptic.','[{"s": "Boron being extremely hard refractory solid of high melting point, low density and very low electrical conductivity, finds many applications. Boron fibres are used in making bullet-proof vest and light composite material for aircraft. The boron-10 (10B) isotope has high ability to absorb neutrons and, therefore, metal borides are used in nuclear industry as protective shields and control rods. The main industrial application of borax and boric acid is in the manufacture of heat resistant glasses (e.g., Pyrex), glass-wool and fibreglass. Borax is also used as a flux for soldering metals, for heat, scratch and stain resistant glazed coating to earthenwares and as constituent of medicinal soaps. An aqueous solution of orthoboric acid is generally used as a mild antiseptic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16368,48,127,'paragraph',NULL,'Aluminium is a bright silvery-white metal, with high tensile strength. It has a high electrical and thermal conductivity. On a weight-to-weight basis, the electrical conductivity of aluminium is twice that of copper. Aluminium is used extensively in industry and everyday life. It forms alloys with Cu, Mn, Mg, Si and Zn. Aluminium and its alloys can be given shapes of pipe, tubes, rods, wires, plates or foils and, therefore, find uses in packing, utensil making, construction, aeroplane and transportation industry. The use of aluminium and its compounds for domestic purposes is now reduced considerably because of their toxic nature.','[{"s": "Aluminium is a bright silvery-white metal, with high tensile strength. It has a high electrical and thermal conductivity. On a weight-to-weight basis, the electrical conductivity of aluminium is twice that of copper. Aluminium is used extensively in industry and everyday life. It forms alloys with Cu, Mn, Mg, Si and Zn. Aluminium and its alloys can be given shapes of pipe, tubes, rods, wires, plates or foils and, therefore, find uses in packing, utensil making, construction, aeroplane and transportation industry. The use of aluminium and its compounds for domestic purposes is now reduced considerably because of their toxic nature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16369,48,128,'heading',3,'11.5 GROUP 14 ELEMENTS: THE CARBON FAMILY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16370,48,129,'paragraph',NULL,'Carbon, silicon, germanium, tin lead and flerovium are the members of group 14. Carbon is the seventeenth most abundant element by mass in the earth’s crust. It is widely distributed in nature in free as well as in the combined state. In elemental state it is available as coal, graphite and diamond; however, in combined state it is present as metal carbonates, hydrocarbons and carbon dioxide gas (0.03%) in air. One can emphatically say that carbon is the most versatile element in the world. Its combination with other elements such as dihydrogen, dioxygen, chlorine and sulphur provides an astonishing array of materials ranging from living tissues to drugs and plastics. Organic chemistry is devoted to carbon containing compounds. It is an essential constituent of all living organisms. Naturally occurring carbon contains two stable isotopes:12C and 13C. In addition to these, third isotope, 14C is also present. It is a radioactive isotope with half-life 5770 years and used for radiocarbon dating. Silicon is the second (27.7 % by mass) most abundant element on the earth’s crust and is present in nature in the form of silica and silicates. Silicon is a very important component of ceramics, glass and cement. Germanium exists only in traces. Tin occurs mainly as cassiterite, SnO 2 and lead as galena, PbS. Flerovium is synthetically prepared radioactive element','[{"s": "Carbon, silicon, germanium, tin lead and flerovium are the members of group 14. Carbon is the seventeenth most abundant element by mass in the earth’s crust. It is widely distributed in nature in free as well as in the combined state. In elemental state it is available as coal, graphite and diamond; however, in combined state it is present as metal carbonates, hydrocarbons and carbon dioxide gas (0.03%) in air. One can emphatically say that carbon is the most versatile element in the world. Its combination with other elements such as dihydrogen, dioxygen, chlorine and sulphur provides an astonishing array of materials ranging from living tissues to drugs and plastics. Organic chemistry is devoted to carbon containing compounds. It is an essential constituent of all living organisms. Naturally occurring carbon contains two stable isotopes:12C and 13C. In addition to these, third isotope, 14C is also present. It is a radioactive isotope with half-life 5770 years and used for radiocarbon dating. Silicon is the second (27.7 % by mass) most abundant element on the earth’s crust and is present in nature in the form of silica and silicates. Silicon is a very important component of ceramics, glass and cement. Germanium exists only in traces. Tin occurs mainly as cassiterite, SnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and lead as galena, PbS. Flerovium is synthetically prepared radioactive element", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16371,48,130,'paragraph',NULL,'Ultrapure form of germanium and silicon are used to make transistors and semiconductor devices.','[{"s": "Ultrapure form of germanium and silicon are used to make transistors and semiconductor devices.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16372,48,131,'paragraph',NULL,'Symbol of Flerovium is Fl. It has atomic number 114, atomic mass 289 gmol -1 and electronic configuration [Rn] 5f 14 6d 10 7s 2 7p 2 . It has been prepared only in small amount. Its half life is short and its chemistry has not been established yet. The important atomic and physical properties along with their electronic configuration of the elements of group 14 leaving flerovium are given in Table 11.3. Some of the atomic, physical and chemical properties are discussed below:','[{"s": "Symbol of Flerovium is Fl. It has atomic number 114, atomic mass 289 gmol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and electronic configuration [Rn] 5f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It has been prepared only in small amount. Its half life is short and its chemistry has not been established yet. The important atomic and physical properties along with their electronic configuration of the elements of group 14 leaving flerovium are given in Table 11.3. Some of the atomic, physical and chemical properties are discussed below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16373,48,132,'heading',4,'11.5.1 Electronic Configuration',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26426,65,488,'paragraph',NULL,'BH = horizontal component of the magnetic field =μ 0 /4π sin θm/r 3','[{"s": "BH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= horizontal component of the magnetic field", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4π sin θm/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16374,48,133,'paragraph',NULL,'The valence shell electronic configuration of these elements is ns 2 np 2 . The inner core of the electronic configuration of elements in this group also differs.','[{"s": "The valence shell electronic configuration of these elements is ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "np", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The inner core of the electronic configuration of elements in this group also differs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16375,48,134,'heading',4,'11.5.2 Covalent Radius',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16376,48,135,'paragraph',NULL,'There is a considerable increase in covalent radius from C to Si, thereafter from Si to Pb a small increase in radius is observed. This is due to the presence of completely filled d and f orbitals in heavier members.','[{"s": "There is a considerable increase in covalent radius from C to Si, thereafter from Si to Pb a small increase in radius is observed. This is due to the presence of completely filled d and f orbitals in heavier members.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16377,48,136,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 11.3 Atomic and Physical Properties of Group 14 Elements','[{"s": "Table 11.3 Atomic and Physical Properties of Group 14 Elements", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16378,48,137,'image',NULL,'table2',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image23.jpg'),
(16379,48,138,'paragraph',NULL,'a for M IV oxidation state; b 6–coordination; c Pauling scale; d 293 K; e for diamond; for graphite, density is 2.22; f β-form (stable at room temperature)','[{"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "IV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "oxidation state;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6–coordination;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Pauling scale;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "293 K;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for diamond; for graphite, density is 2.22;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "β-form (stable at room temperature)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16380,48,139,'heading',4,'11.5.3 Ionization Enthalpy',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16381,48,140,'paragraph',NULL,'The first ionization enthalpy of group 14 members is higher than the corresponding members of group 13. The influence of inner core electrons is visible here also. In general the ionisation enthalpy decreases down the group. Small decrease in ∆ i H from Si to Ge to Sn and slight increase in ∆ i H from Sn to Pb is the consequence of poor shielding effect of intervening d and f orbitals and increase in size of the atom.','[{"s": "The first ionization enthalpy of group 14 members is higher than the corresponding members of group 13. The influence of inner core electrons is visible here also. In general the ionisation enthalpy decreases down the group. Small decrease in ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H from Si to Ge to Sn and slight increase in ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H from Sn to Pb is the consequence of poor shielding effect of intervening d and f orbitals and increase in size of the atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16382,48,141,'heading',4,'11.5.4 Electronegativity',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16383,48,142,'paragraph',NULL,'Due to small size, the elements of this group are slightly more electronegative than group 13 elements. The electronegativity values for elements from Si to Pb are almost the same.','[{"s": "Due to small size, the elements of this group are slightly more electronegative than group 13 elements. The electronegativity values for elements from Si to Pb are almost the same.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16384,48,143,'heading',4,'11.5.5 Physical Properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16385,48,144,'paragraph',NULL,'All members of group14 are solids. Carbon and silicon are non-metals, germanium is a metalloid, whereas tin and lead are soft metals with low melting points. Melting points and boiling points of group 14 elements are much higher than those of corresponding elements of group 13.','[{"s": "All members of group14 are solids. Carbon and silicon are non-metals, germanium is a metalloid, whereas tin and lead are soft metals with low melting points. Melting points and boiling points of group 14 elements are much higher than those of corresponding elements of group 13.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16386,48,145,'heading',4,'11.5.6 Chemical Properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16387,48,146,'heading',4,'Oxidation states and trends in chemical reactivity',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16388,48,147,'paragraph',NULL,'The group 14 elements have four electrons in outermost shell. The common oxidation states exhibited by these elements are +4 and +2. Carbon also exhibits negative oxidation states. Since the sum of the first four ionization enthalpies is very high, compounds in +4 oxidation state are generally covalent in nature. In heavier members the tendency to show +2 oxidation state increases in the sequence Ge<Snns 2 electrons of valence shell to participate in bonding. The relative stabilities of these two oxidation states vary down the group. Carbon and silicon mostly show +4 oxidation state. Germanium forms stable compounds in +4 state and only few compounds in +2 state. Tin forms compounds in both oxidation states (Sn in +2 state is a reducing agent). Lead compounds in +2 state are stable and in +4 state are strong oxidising agents. In tetravalent state the number of electrons around the central atom in a molecule (e.g., carbon in CCl 4 ) is eight. Being electron precise molecules, they are normally not expected to act as electron acceptor or electron donor species. Although carbon cannot exceed its covalence more than 4, other elements of the group can do so. It is because of the presence of d orbital in them. Due to this, their halides undergo hydrolysis and have tendency to form complexes by accepting electron pairs from donor species. For example, the species like, SiF 6 2– , [GeCl 6 ] 2– , [Sn(OH) 6 ] 2– exist where the hybridisation of the central atom is sp 3 d 2 .','[{"s": "The group 14 elements have four electrons in outermost shell. The common oxidation states exhibited by these elements are +4 and +2. Carbon also exhibits negative oxidation states. Since the sum of the first four ionization enthalpies is very high, compounds in +4 oxidation state are generally covalent in nature. In heavier members the tendency to show +2 oxidation state increases in the sequence Ge<Snns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons of valence shell to participate in bonding.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The relative stabilities of these two oxidation states vary down the group.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Carbon and silicon mostly show +4 oxidation state. Germanium forms stable compounds in +4 state and only few compounds in +2 state. Tin forms compounds in both oxidation states (Sn in +2 state is a reducing agent). Lead compounds in +2 state are stable and in +4 state are strong oxidising agents. In tetravalent state the number of electrons around the central atom in a molecule (e.g., carbon in CCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is eight. Being electron precise molecules, they are normally not expected to act as electron acceptor or electron donor species. Although carbon cannot exceed its covalence more than 4, other elements of the group can do so. It is because of the presence of d orbital in them. Due to this, their halides undergo hydrolysis and have tendency to form complexes by accepting electron pairs from donor species. For example, the species like, SiF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", [GeCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", [Sn(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "exist where the hybridisation of the central atom is sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["113924"]',NULL),
(16389,48,148,'heading',4,'(i) Reactivity towards oxygen',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16390,48,149,'paragraph',NULL,'All members when heated in oxygen form oxides. There are mainly two types of oxides, i.e., monoxide and dioxide of formula MO and MO 2 respectively. SiO only exists at high temperature. Oxides in higher oxidation states of elements are generally more acidic than those in lower oxidation states. The dioxides — CO 2 , SiO 2 and GeO 2 are acidic, whereas SnO 2 and PbO 2 are amphoteric in nature. Among monoxides, CO is neutral, GeO is distinctly acidic whereas SnO and PbO are amphoteric.','[{"s": "All members when heated in oxygen form oxides. There are mainly two types of oxides, i.e., monoxide and dioxide of formula MO and MO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively. SiO only exists at high temperature. Oxides in higher oxidation states of elements are generally more acidic than those in lower oxidation states. The dioxides — CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", SiO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and GeO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are acidic, whereas SnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and PbO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are amphoteric in nature. Among monoxides, CO is neutral, GeO is distinctly acidic whereas SnO and PbO are amphoteric.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16391,48,150,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 11.5','[{"s": "Problem 11.5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16392,48,151,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16393,48,152,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) carbon (ii) lead','[{"s": "(i) carbon (ii) lead", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16394,48,153,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) silicon and germanium','[{"s": "(iii) silicon and germanium", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16395,48,154,'heading',4,'(ii) Reactivity towards water',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16396,48,155,'paragraph',NULL,'Carbon, silicon and germanium are not affected by water. Tin decomposes steam to form dioxide and dihydrogen gas.','[{"s": "Carbon, silicon and germanium are not affected by water. Tin decomposes steam to form dioxide and dihydrogen gas.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16397,48,156,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image19.png'),
(16398,48,157,'paragraph',NULL,'Lead is unaffected by water, probably because of a protective oxide film formation.','[{"s": "Lead is unaffected by water, probably because of a protective oxide film formation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16399,48,158,'heading',4,'(iii) Reactivity towards halogen',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16400,48,159,'paragraph',NULL,'These elements can form halides of formula MX 2 and MX 4 (where X = F, Cl, Br, I). Except carbon, all other members react directly with halogen under suitable condition to make halides. Most of the MX 4 are covalent in nature. The central metal atom in these halides undergoes sp3 hybridisation and the molecule is tetrahedral in shape . Exceptions are SnF 4 and PbF 4 , which are ionic in nature . PbI 4 does not exist because Pb—I bond initially formed during the reaction does not release enough energy to unpair 6s 2 electrons and excite one of them to higher orbital to have four unpaired electrons around lead atom. Heavier members Ge to Pb are able to make halides of formula MX 2 . Stability of dihalides increases down the group . Considering the thermal and chemical stability, GeX 4 is more stable than GeX 2 , whereas PbX 2 is more than PbX 4 . Except CCl 4 , other tetrachlorides are easily hydrolysed by water because the central atom can accommodate the lone pair of electrons from oxygen atom of water molecule in d orbital.','[{"s": "These elements can form halides of formula MX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and MX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(where X = F, Cl, Br, I). Except carbon, all other members react directly with halogen under suitable condition to make halides. Most of the MX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are covalent in nature. The central metal atom in these halides undergoes sp3 hybridisation and the molecule is tetrahedral in shape", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Exceptions are SnF", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and PbF", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", which are ionic in nature", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PbI", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "does not exist because Pb—I bond initially formed during the reaction does not release enough energy to unpair 6s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electrons and excite one of them to higher orbital to have four unpaired electrons around lead atom. Heavier members Ge to Pb are able to make halides of formula MX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Stability of dihalides increases down the group", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Considering the thermal and chemical stability, GeX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is more stable than GeX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", whereas PbX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is more than PbX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Except CCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", other tetrachlorides are easily hydrolysed by water because the central atom can accommodate the lone pair of electrons from oxygen atom of water molecule in d orbital.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["113906", "113907"]',NULL),
(16401,48,160,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image36.png'),
(16402,48,161,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydrolysis can be understood by taking the example of SiCl 4 . It undergoes hydrolysis by initially accepting lone pair of electrons from water molecule in d orbitals of Si, finally leading to the formation of Si(OH) 4 as shown below :','[{"s": "Hydrolysis can be understood by taking the example of SiCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It undergoes hydrolysis by initially accepting lone pair of electrons from water molecule in d orbitals of Si, finally leading to the formation of Si(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as shown below :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16403,48,162,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 11. 6','[{"s": "Problem 11. 6", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16404,48,163,'paragraph',NULL,'[SiF 6 ] 2– is known whereas [SiCl 6 ] 2– not. Give possible reasons.','[{"s": "[SiF", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "]", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is known whereas [SiCl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "]", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "not.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Give possible reasons.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["113907"]',NULL),
(16405,48,164,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16406,48,165,'paragraph',NULL,'The main reasons are :','[{"s": "The main reasons are :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16407,48,166,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) six large chloride ions cannot be accommodated around Si 4+ due to limitation of its size.','[{"s": "(i) six large chloride ions cannot be accommodated around Si", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "due to limitation of its size.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16408,48,167,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) interaction between lone pair of chloride ion and Si 4+ is not very strong.','[{"s": "(ii) interaction between lone pair of chloride ion and Si", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is not very strong.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16409,48,168,'heading',3,'11.6 IMPORTANT TRENDS AND ANOMALOUS BEHAVIOUR OF CARBON',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16410,48,169,'paragraph',NULL,'Like first member of other groups, carbon also differs from rest of the members of its group. It is due to its smaller size, higher electronegativity, higher ionisation enthalpy and unavailability of d orbitals.','[{"s": "Like first member of other groups, carbon also differs from rest of the members of its group. It is due to its smaller size, higher electronegativity, higher ionisation enthalpy and unavailability of d orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16411,48,170,'paragraph',NULL,'In carbon, only s and p orbitals are available for bonding and, therefore, it can accommodate only four pairs of electrons around it. This would limit the maximum covalence to four whereas other members can expand their covalence due to the presence of d orbitals.','[{"s": "In carbon, only s and p orbitals are available for bonding and, therefore, it can accommodate only four pairs of electrons around it. This would limit the maximum covalence to four whereas other members can expand their covalence due to the presence of d orbitals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16412,48,171,'paragraph',NULL,'Carbon also has unique ability to form pπ– pπ multiple bonds with itself and with other atoms of small size and high electronegativity. Few examples of multiple bonding are: C=C, C ≡ C, C = O, C = S, and C ≡ N. Heavier elements do not form pπ– pπ bonds because their atomic orbitals are too large and diffuse to have effective overlapping.','[{"s": "Carbon also has unique ability to form pπ– pπ multiple bonds with itself and with other atoms of small size and high electronegativity. Few examples of multiple bonding are: C=C, C ≡ C, C = O, C = S, and C ≡ N. Heavier elements do not form pπ– pπ bonds because their atomic orbitals are too large and diffuse to have effective overlapping.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16413,48,172,'paragraph',NULL,'Carbon atoms have the tendency to link with one another through covalent bonds to form chains and rings. This property is called catenation . This is because C—C bonds are very strong. Down the group the size increases and electronegativity decreases, and, thereby, tendency to show catenation decreases. This can be clearly seen from bond enthalpies values. The order of catenation is C > > Si > Ge ≈ Sn. Lead does not show catenation.','[{"s": "Carbon atoms have the tendency to link with one another through covalent bonds to form chains and rings. This property is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "catenation", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This is because C—C bonds are very strong. Down the group the size increases and electronegativity decreases, and, thereby, tendency to show catenation decreases. This can be clearly seen from bond enthalpies values. The order of catenation is C > > Si > Ge ≈ Sn. Lead does not show catenation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16414,48,173,'image',NULL,'table3',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image8.jpg'),
(16415,48,174,'paragraph',NULL,'Due to property of catenation and pπ– pπ bond formation, carbon is able to show allotropic forms.','[{"s": "Due to property of catenation and pπ– pπ bond formation, carbon is able to show allotropic forms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16416,48,175,'heading',3,'11.7 ALLOTROPES OF CARBON',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16417,48,176,'paragraph',NULL,'Carbon exhibits many allotropic forms; both crystalline as well as amorphous. Diamond and graphite are two well-known crystalline forms of carbon. In 1985, third form of carbon known as fullerenes was discovered by H.W.Kroto, E.Smalley and R.F.Curl. For this discovery they were awarded the Nobel Prize in 1996.','[{"s": "Carbon exhibits many allotropic forms; both crystalline as well as amorphous. Diamond and graphite are two well-known crystalline forms of carbon. In 1985, third form of carbon known as fullerenes was discovered by H.W.Kroto, E.Smalley and R.F.Curl. For this discovery they were awarded the Nobel Prize in 1996.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16418,48,177,'heading',4,'11.7.1 Diamond',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16419,48,178,'paragraph',NULL,'It has a crystalline lattice . In diamond each carbon atom undergoes sp3 hybridisation and linked to four other carbon atoms by using hybridised orbitals in tetrahedral fashion. The C–C bond length is 154 pm. The structure extends in space and produces a rigid three-dimensional network of carbon atoms. In this structure (Fig. 11.3) directional covalent bonds are present throughout the lattice.','[{"s": "It has a crystalline lattice", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In diamond each carbon atom undergoes sp3 hybridisation and linked to four other carbon atoms by using hybridised orbitals in tetrahedral fashion.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The C–C bond length is 154 pm. The structure extends in space and produces a rigid three-dimensional network of carbon atoms. In this structure (Fig. 11.3) directional covalent bonds are present throughout the lattice.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["113925"]',NULL),
(16420,48,179,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image16.png'),
(16421,48,180,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.3 The structure of diamond','[{"s": "Fig. 11.3 The structure of diamond", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16422,48,181,'paragraph',NULL,'It is very difficult to break extended covalent bonding and, therefore, diamond is a hardest substance on the earth. It is used as an abrasive for sharpening hard tools, in making dyes and in the manufacture of tungsten filaments for electric light bulbs.','[{"s": "It is very difficult to break extended covalent bonding and, therefore, diamond is a hardest substance on the earth. It is used as an abrasive for sharpening hard tools, in making dyes and in the manufacture of tungsten filaments for electric light bulbs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16423,48,182,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 11.7','[{"s": "Problem 11.7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16424,48,183,'paragraph',NULL,'Diamond is covalent, yet it has high melting point. Why ?','[{"s": "Diamond is covalent, yet it has high melting point. Why ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16425,48,184,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16426,48,185,'paragraph',NULL,'Diamond has a three-dimensional network involving strong C—C bonds, which are very difficult to break and, in turn has high melting point.','[{"s": "Diamond has a three-dimensional network involving strong C—C bonds, which are very difficult to break and, in turn has high melting point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16427,48,186,'heading',4,'11.7.2 Graphite',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16428,48,187,'paragraph',NULL,'Graphite has layered structure (Fig.11.4). Layers are held by van der Waals forces and distance between two layers is 340 pm. Each layer is composed of planar hexagonal rings of carbon atoms. C—C bond length within the layer is 141.5 pm. Each carbon atom in hexagonal ring undergoes sp2 hybridisation and makes three sigma bonds with three neighbouring carbon atoms. Fourth electron forms a π bond. The electrons are delocalised over the whole sheet. Electrons are mobile and, therefore, graphite conducts electricity along the sheet. Graphite cleaves easily between the layers and, therefore, it is very soft and slippery. For this reason graphite is used as a dry lubricant in machines running at high temperature, where oil cannot be used as a lubricant.','[{"s": "Graphite has layered structure (Fig.11.4).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Layers are held by van der Waals forces and distance between two layers is 340 pm. Each layer is composed of planar hexagonal rings of carbon atoms. C—C bond length within the layer is 141.5 pm.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Each carbon atom in hexagonal ring undergoes sp2 hybridisation and makes three sigma bonds with three neighbouring carbon atoms.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Fourth electron forms a π bond. The electrons are delocalised over the whole sheet. Electrons are mobile and, therefore, graphite conducts electricity along the sheet. Graphite cleaves easily between the layers and, therefore, it is very soft and slippery.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For this reason graphite is used as a dry lubricant in machines running at high temperature, where oil cannot be used as a lubricant.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["113905", "113925", "113926"]',NULL),
(16429,48,188,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image12.png'),
(16430,48,189,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 11.4 The structure of graphite','[{"s": "Fig 11.4 The structure of graphite", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16431,48,190,'heading',4,'11.7.3 Fullerenes',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16432,48,191,'paragraph',NULL,'Fullerenes are made by the heating of graphite in an electric arc in the presence of inert gases such as helium or argon. The sooty material formed by condensation of vapourised Cn small molecules consists of mainly C60 with smaller quantity of C70 and traces of fullerenes consisting of even number of carbon atoms up to 350 or above. Fullerenes are the only pure form of carbon because they have smooth structure without having ‘dangling’ bonds. Fullerenes are cage like molecules . C60 molecule has a shape like soccer ball and called Buckminsterfullerene (Fig. 11.5).','[{"s": "Fullerenes are made by the heating of graphite in an electric arc in the presence of inert gases such as helium or argon. The sooty material formed by condensation of vapourised Cn small molecules consists of mainly C60 with smaller quantity of C70 and traces of fullerenes consisting of even number of carbon atoms up to 350 or above. Fullerenes are the only pure form of carbon because they have smooth structure without having ‘dangling’ bonds.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Fullerenes are cage like molecules", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "C60 molecule has a shape like soccer ball and called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Buckminsterfullerene", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Fig. 11.5).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["113926"]',NULL),
(16433,48,192,'paragraph',NULL,'It contains twenty six- membered rings and twelve five-membered rings. A six membered ring is fused with six or five membered rings but a five membered ring can only fuse with six membered rings. All the carbon atoms are equal and they undergo sp2 hybridisation. Each carbon atom forms three sigma bonds with other three carbon atoms. The remaining electron at each carbon is delocalised in molecular orbitals, which in turn give aromatic character to molecule. This ball shaped molecule has 60 vertices and each one is occupied by one carbon atom and it also contains both single and double bonds with C–C distances of 143.5 pm and 138.3 pm respectively. Spherical fullerenes are also called bucky ballsin short.','[{"s": "It contains twenty six- membered rings and twelve five-membered rings. A six membered ring is fused with six or five membered rings but a five membered ring can only fuse with six membered rings. All the carbon atoms are equal and they undergo sp2 hybridisation. Each carbon atom forms three sigma bonds with other three carbon atoms. The remaining electron at each carbon is delocalised in molecular orbitals, which in turn give aromatic character to molecule. This ball shaped molecule has 60 vertices and each one is occupied by one carbon atom and it also contains both single and double bonds with C–C distances of 143.5 pm and 138.3 pm respectively. Spherical fullerenes are also called bucky ballsin short.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16434,48,193,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image3.png'),
(16435,48,194,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.11.5 The structure of C60, Buckminster-fullerene : Note that molecule has the shape of a soccer ball (football).','[{"s": "Fig.11.5 The structure of C60, Buckminster-fullerene : Note that molecule has the shape of a soccer ball (football).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16436,48,195,'paragraph',NULL,'It is very important to know that graphite is thermodynamically most stable allotrope of carbon and, therefore, ∆ f H of graphite is taken as zero. ∆ f H values of diamond and fullerene, C60 are 1.90 and 38.1 kJ mol –1 , respectively.','[{"s": "It is very important to know that graphite is thermodynamically most stable allotrope of carbon and, therefore, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H of graphite is taken as zero. ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H values of diamond and fullerene, C60 are 1.90 and 38.1 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16437,48,196,'paragraph',NULL,'Other forms of elemental carbon like carbon black, coke, and charcoal are all impure forms of graphite or fullerenes. Carbon black is obtained by burning hydrocarbons in a limited supply of air. Charcoal and coke are obtained by heating wood or coal respectively at high temperatures in the absence of air.','[{"s": "Other forms of elemental carbon like carbon black, coke, and charcoal are all impure forms of graphite or fullerenes. Carbon black is obtained by burning hydrocarbons in a limited supply of air. Charcoal and coke are obtained by heating wood or coal respectively at high temperatures in the absence of air.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16438,48,197,'heading',4,'11.7.4 Uses of Carbon',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16439,48,198,'paragraph',NULL,'Graphite fibres embedded in plastic material form high strength, lightweight composites. The composites are used in products such as tennis rackets, fishing rods, aircrafts and canoes. Being good conductor, graphite is used for electrodes in batteries and industrial electrolysis. Crucibles made from graphite are inert to dilute acids and alkalies. Being highly porous, activated charcoal is used in adsorbing poisonous gases; also used in water filters to remove organic contaminators and in airconditioning system to control odour. Carbon black is used as black pigment in black ink and as filler in automobile tyres. Coke is used as a fuel and largely as a reducing agent in metallurgy. Diamond is a precious stone and used in jewellery. It is measured in carats (1 carat = 200 mg).','[{"s": "Graphite fibres embedded in plastic material form high strength, lightweight composites. The composites are used in products such as tennis rackets, fishing rods, aircrafts and canoes. Being good conductor, graphite is used for electrodes in batteries and industrial electrolysis. Crucibles made from graphite are inert to dilute acids and alkalies. Being highly porous, activated charcoal is used in adsorbing poisonous gases; also used in water filters to remove organic contaminators and in airconditioning system to control odour. Carbon black is used as black pigment in black ink and as filler in automobile tyres. Coke is used as a fuel and largely as a reducing agent in metallurgy. Diamond is a precious stone and used in jewellery. It is measured in carats (1 carat = 200 mg).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16440,48,199,'heading',3,'11.8 SOME IMPORTANT COMPOUNDS OF CARBON AND SILICON',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16441,48,200,'heading',4,'Oxides of Carbon',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16442,48,201,'paragraph',NULL,'Two important oxides of carbon are carbon monoxide, CO and carbon dioxide, CO 2 .','[{"s": "Two important oxides of carbon are carbon monoxide, CO and carbon dioxide, CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16443,48,202,'heading',4,'11.8.1 Carbon Monoxide',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16444,48,203,'paragraph',NULL,'Direct oxidation of C in limited supply of oxygen or air yields carbon monoxide.','[{"s": "Direct oxidation of C in limited supply of oxygen or air yields carbon monoxide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16445,48,204,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image13.png'),
(16446,48,205,'paragraph',NULL,'On small scale pure CO is prepared by dehydration of formic acid with concentrated H 2 SO 4 at 373 K','[{"s": "On small scale pure CO is prepared by dehydration of formic acid with concentrated H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at 373 K", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16447,48,206,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image38.png'),
(16448,48,207,'paragraph',NULL,'On commercial scale it is prepared by the passage of steam over hot coke. The mixture of CO and H2 thus produced is known as water gas or synthesis gas .','[{"s": "On commercial scale it is prepared by the passage of steam over hot coke. The mixture of CO and H2 thus produced is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "water gas", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "synthesis gas", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16449,48,208,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image9.png'),
(16450,48,209,'paragraph',NULL,'When air is used instead of steam, a mixture of CO and N 2 is produced, which is called producer gas .','[{"s": "When air is used instead of steam, a mixture of CO and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is produced, which is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "producer gas", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16470,48,229,'paragraph',NULL,'Carbon dioxide can be obtained as a solid in the form of dry ice by allowing the liquified CO 2 to expand rapidly. Dry ice is used as a refrigerant for ice-cream and frozen food. Gaseous CO 2 is extensively used to carbonate soft drinks. Being heavy and non-supporter of combustion it is used as fire extinguisher. A substantial amount of CO 2 is used to manufacture urea.','[{"s": "Carbon dioxide can be obtained as a solid in the form of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dry ice", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by allowing the liquified CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to expand rapidly. Dry ice is used as a refrigerant for ice-cream and frozen food. Gaseous CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is extensively used to carbonate soft drinks. Being heavy and non-supporter of combustion it is used as fire extinguisher. A substantial amount of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is used to manufacture urea.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16471,48,230,'paragraph',NULL,'In CO 2 molecule carbon atom undergoes sp hybridisation. Two sp hybridised orbitals of carbon atom overlap with two p orbitals of oxygen atoms to make two sigma bonds while other two electrons of carbon atom are involved in pπ– pπ bonding with oxygen atom. This results in its linear shape [with both C–O bonds of equal length (115 pm)] with no dipole moment. The resonance structures are shown below:','[{"s": "In CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule carbon atom undergoes sp hybridisation. Two sp hybridised orbitals of carbon atom overlap with two p orbitals of oxygen atoms to make two sigma bonds while other two electrons of carbon atom are involved in pπ– pπ bonding with oxygen atom. This results in its linear shape [with both C–O bonds of equal length (115 pm)] with no dipole moment. The resonance structures are shown below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16472,48,231,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image40.png'),
(16473,48,232,'paragraph',NULL,'Resonance structures of carbon dioxide','[{"s": "Resonance structures of carbon dioxide", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16474,48,233,'heading',4,'11.8.3 Silicon Dioxide, SiO2',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16475,48,234,'paragraph',NULL,'95% of the earth’s crust is made up of silica and silicates. Silicon dioxide, commonly known as silica, occurs in several crystallographic forms. Quartz, cristobalite and tridymite are some of the crystalline forms of silica, and they are interconvertable at suitable temperature. Silicon dioxide is a covalent, three-dimensional network solid in which each silicon atom is covalently bonded in a tetrahedral manner to four oxygen atoms. Each oxygen atom in turn covalently bonded to another silicon atoms as shown in diagram (Fig 11.6 ). Each corner is shared with another tetrahedron. The entire crystal may be considered as giant molecule in which eight membered rings are formed with alternate silicon and oxygen atoms.','[{"s": "95% of the earth’s crust is made up of silica and silicates. Silicon dioxide, commonly known as silica, occurs in several crystallographic forms. Quartz, cristobalite and tridymite are some of the crystalline forms of silica, and they are interconvertable at suitable temperature. Silicon dioxide is a covalent, three-dimensional network solid in which each silicon atom is covalently bonded in a tetrahedral manner to four oxygen atoms. Each oxygen atom in turn covalently bonded to another silicon atoms as shown in diagram (Fig 11.6 ). Each corner is shared with another tetrahedron. The entire crystal may be considered as giant molecule in which eight membered rings are formed with alternate silicon and oxygen atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16476,48,235,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image29.png'),
(16477,48,236,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.6 Three dimensional structure of SiO2','[{"s": "Fig. 11.6 Three dimensional structure of SiO2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16478,48,237,'paragraph',NULL,'Silica in its normal form is almost non-reactive because of very high Si—O bond enthalpy. It resists the attack by halogens, dihydrogen and most of the acids and metals even at elevated temperatures. However, it is attacked by HF and NaOH.','[{"s": "Silica in its normal form is almost non-reactive because of very high Si—O bond enthalpy. It resists the attack by halogens, dihydrogen and most of the acids and metals even at elevated temperatures. However, it is attacked by HF and NaOH.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16479,48,238,'paragraph',NULL,'SiO 2 + 2NaOH → Na 2 SiO 3 + H 2 O','[{"s": "SiO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2NaOH → Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SiO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16480,48,239,'paragraph',NULL,'SiO 2 + 4HF → SiF 4 + 2H 2 O','[{"s": "SiO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 4HF → SiF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16481,48,240,'paragraph',NULL,'Quartz is extensively used as a piezoelectric material; it has made possible to develop extremely accurate clocks, modern radio and television broadcasting and mobile radio communications. Silica gel is used as a drying agent and as a support for chromatographic materials and catalysts. Kieselghur, an amorphous form of silica is used in filtration plants.','[{"s": "Quartz is extensively used as a piezoelectric material; it has made possible to develop extremely accurate clocks, modern radio and television broadcasting and mobile radio communications. Silica gel is used as a drying agent and as a support for chromatographic materials and catalysts. Kieselghur, an amorphous form of silica is used in filtration plants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16482,48,241,'heading',4,'11.8.4 Silicones',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16483,48,242,'paragraph',NULL,'They are a group of organosilicon polymers, which have (R 2 SiO) as a repeating unit . The starting materials for the manufacture of silicones are alkyl or aryl substituted silicon chlorides, R n SiCl (4–n) , where R is alkyl or aryl group. When methyl chloride reacts with silicon in the presence of copper as a catalyst at a temperature 573K various types of methyl substituted chlorosilane of formula MeSiCl 3 , Me 2 SiCl 2 , Me 3 SiCl with small amount of Me 4 Si are formed. Hydrolysis of dimethyl-dichlorosilane, (CH 3 ) 2 SiCl 2 followed by condensation polymerisation yields straight chain polymers.','[{"s": "They are a group of organosilicon polymers, which have (R", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "SiO) as a repeating unit", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The starting materials for the manufacture of silicones are alkyl or aryl substituted silicon chlorides, R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SiCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4–n)", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where R is alkyl or aryl group. When methyl chloride reacts with silicon in the presence of copper as a catalyst at a temperature 573K various types of methyl substituted chlorosilane of formula MeSiCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Me", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SiCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Me", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SiCl with small amount of Me", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Si are formed. Hydrolysis of dimethyl-dichlorosilane, (CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SiCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "followed by condensation polymerisation yields straight chain polymers.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["113927"]',NULL),
(16484,48,243,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image2.png'),
(16485,48,244,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image1.png'),
(16486,48,245,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image11.png'),
(16487,48,246,'paragraph',NULL,'The chain length of the polymer can be controlled by adding (CH 3 ) 3 SiCl which blocks the ends as shown below :','[{"s": "The chain length of the polymer can be controlled by adding (CH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ")", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "SiCl which blocks the ends as shown below :", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["113504"]',NULL),
(16488,48,247,'paragraph',NULL,'Silicones being surrounded by non-polar alkyl groups are water repelling in nature. They have in general high thermal stability, high dielectric strength and resistance to oxidation and chemicals. They have wide applications. They are used as sealant, greases, electrical insulators and for water proofing of fabrics. Being biocompatible they are also used in surgical and cosmetic plants.','[{"s": "Silicones being surrounded by non-polar alkyl groups are water repelling in nature. They have in general high thermal stability, high dielectric strength and resistance to oxidation and chemicals. They have wide applications. They are used as sealant, greases, electrical insulators and for water proofing of fabrics. Being biocompatible they are also used in surgical and cosmetic plants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16489,48,248,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem: 11.8','[{"s": "Problem: 11.8", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16490,48,249,'paragraph',NULL,'What are silicones ?','[{"s": "What are silicones ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16491,48,250,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16492,48,251,'paragraph',NULL,'Simple silicones consist of P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image15.png chains in which alkyl or phenyl groups occupy the remaining bonding positions on each silicon. They are hydrophobic (water repellant) in nature.','[{"s": "Simple silicones consist of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image15.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "chains in which alkyl or phenyl groups occupy the remaining bonding positions on each silicon. They are hydrophobic (water repellant) in nature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16493,48,252,'heading',4,'11.8.5 Silicates',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16494,48,253,'paragraph',NULL,'A large number of silicates minerals exist in nature. Some of the examples are feldspar, zeolites, mica and asbestos. n silicates either the discrete unit is present or a number of such units are joined toge The basic structural unit of silicates is SiO 4 4– (Fig.11.7) in which silicon atom is bonded to four oxygen atoms in tetrahedron fashion. 2 I ther via corners by sharing 1,2,3 or 4 oxygen atoms per silicate units. When silicate units are linked together, they form chain, ring, sheet or three-dimensional structures . Negative charge on silicate structure is neutralised by positively charged metal ions. If all the four corners are shared with other tetrahedral units, a three-dimensional network is formed.','[{"s": "A large number of silicates minerals exist in nature.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Some of the examples are feldspar, zeolites, mica and asbestos.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n silicates either the discrete unit is present or a number of such units are joined toge", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The basic structural unit of silicates is SiO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Fig.11.7) in which silicon atom is bonded to four oxygen atoms in tetrahedron fashion.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ther via corners by sharing 1,2,3 or 4 oxygen atoms per silicate units.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When silicate units are linked together, they form chain, ring, sheet or three-dimensional structures", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Negative charge on silicate structure is neutralised by positively charged metal ions. If all the four corners are shared with other tetrahedral units, a three-dimensional network is formed.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["113913", "113928", "113929"]',NULL),
(16495,48,254,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_13_14-image24.png'),
(16496,48,255,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) (b)','[{"s": "(a) (b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16497,48,256,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.7 (a) Tetrahedral structure of SiO44–anion; (b) Representation of SiO44–unit','[{"s": "Fig. 11.7 (a) Tetrahedral structure of SiO44–anion; (b) Representation of SiO44–unit", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16498,48,257,'paragraph',NULL,'Two important man-made silicates are glass and cement.','[{"s": "Two important man-made silicates are glass and cement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16499,48,258,'heading',4,'11.8.6 Zeolites',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16500,48,259,'paragraph',NULL,'If aluminium atoms replace a few silicon atoms in three-dimensional network of silicon dioxide, overall structure known as aluminosilicate acquires a negative charge. Cations such as Na + , K + or Ca 2+ balance the negative charge. Examples are feldspar and zeolites. Zeolites are widely used as a catalyst in petrochemical industries for cracking of hydrocarbons and isomerisation, e.g., ZSM-5 (A type of zeolite) used to convert alcohols directly into gasoline. Hydrated zeolites are used as ion exchangers in softening of “hard” water.','[{"s": "If aluminium atoms replace a few silicon atoms in three-dimensional network of silicon dioxide, overall structure known as aluminosilicate acquires a negative charge.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Cations such as Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or Ca", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "balance the negative charge. Examples are feldspar and zeolites.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Zeolites are widely used as a catalyst in petrochemical industries for cracking of hydrocarbons and isomerisation, e.g., ZSM-5 (A type of zeolite) used to convert alcohols directly into gasoline.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Hydrated zeolites are used as ion exchangers in softening of “hard” water.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["113928", "113930"]',NULL),
(16501,48,260,'paragraph',NULL,'SUMMARY','[{"s": "SUMMARY", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24137,61,149,'paragraph',NULL,'This shows that V is a constant if r is constant. Thus, equipotential surfaces of a single point charge are concentric spherical surfaces centred at the charge.','[{"s": "This shows that V is a constant if r is constant. Thus, equipotential surfaces of a single point charge are concentric spherical surfaces centred at the charge.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26427,65,489,'paragraph',NULL,'θ = 90° lattitude as measured from the magnetic equator.','[{"s": "θ = 90°", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lattitude as measured from the magnetic equator.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16502,48,261,'paragraph',NULL,'p-Block of the periodic table is unique in terms of having all types of elements – metals , nonmetals and metalloids . There are six groups of p-block elements in the periodic table numbering from 13 to 18. Their valence shell electronic configuration is ns 2 np 1–6 (except for He). Differences in the inner core of their electronic configuration greatly influence their physical and chemical properties. As a consequence of this, a lot of variation in properties among these elements is observed. In addition to the group oxidation state, these elements show other oxidation states differing from the total number of valence electrons by unit of two. While the group oxidation state is the most stable for the lighter elements of the group, lower oxidation states become progressively more stable for the heavier elements. The combined effect of size and availability of d orbitals considerably influences the ability of these elements to form π-bonds. While the lighter elements form pπ–pπ bonds, the heavier ones form dπ–pπ or dπ–dπ bonds. Absence of d orbital in second period elements limits their maximum covalence to 4 while heavier ones can exceed this limit.','[{"s": "p-Block", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the periodic table is unique in terms of having all types of elements –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "metals", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nonmetals", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "metalloids", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". There are six groups of p-block elements in the periodic table numbering from 13 to 18. Their valence shell electronic configuration is ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "np", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1–6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(except for He). Differences in the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "inner core", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of their electronic configuration greatly influence their physical and chemical properties. As a consequence of this, a lot of variation in properties among these elements is observed. In addition to the group oxidation state, these elements show other oxidation states differing from the total number of valence electrons by unit of two. While the group oxidation state is the most stable for the lighter elements of the group, lower oxidation states become progressively more stable for the heavier elements. The combined effect of size and availability of d orbitals considerably influences the ability of these elements to form π-bonds. While the lighter elements form", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pπ–pπ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bonds, the heavier ones form", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dπ–pπ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dπ–dπ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bonds. Absence of d orbital in second period elements limits their maximum covalence to 4 while heavier ones can exceed this limit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16503,48,262,'paragraph',NULL,'Boron is a typical non-metal and the other members are metals. The availability of 3 valence electrons (2s 2 2p 1 ) for covalent bond formation using four orbitals (2s, 2p x , 2p y and 2p z ) leads to the so-called electron deficiency in boron compounds. This deficiency makes them good electron acceptors and thus boron compounds behave as Lewis acids . Boron forms covalent molecular compounds with dihydrogen as boranes, the simplest of which is diborane , B 2 H 6 . Diborane contains two bridging hydrogen atoms between two boron atoms; these bridge bonds are considered to be three-center two-electron bonds . The important compounds of boron with dioxygen are boric acid and borax . Boric acid, B(OH) 3 is a weak monobasic acid; it acts as a Lewis acid by accepting electrons from hydroxyl ion. Borax is a white crystalline solid of formula Na 2 [B 4 O 5 (OH) 4 ]·8H 2 O. The borax bead test gives characteristic colors of transition metals.','[{"s": "Boron is a typical non-metal and the other members are metals. The availability of 3 valence electrons (2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") for covalent bond formation using four orbitals (2s, 2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") leads to the so-called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electron deficiency", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in boron compounds. This deficiency makes them good electron acceptors and thus boron compounds behave as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Lewis acids", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Boron forms covalent molecular compounds with dihydrogen as boranes, the simplest of which is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "diborane", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Diborane contains two bridging hydrogen atoms between two boron atoms; these bridge bonds are considered to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "three-center two-electron bonds", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The important compounds of boron with dioxygen are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "boric acid", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "borax", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Boric acid, B(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a weak monobasic acid; it acts as a Lewis acid by accepting electrons from hydroxyl ion. Borax is a white crystalline solid of formula Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]·8H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O. The borax bead test gives characteristic colors of transition metals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16504,48,263,'paragraph',NULL,'Aluminum exhibits +3 oxidation state. With heavier elements +1 oxidation state gets progressively stabilized on going down the group. This is a consequence of the so called inert pair effect .','[{"s": "Aluminum exhibits +3 oxidation state. With heavier elements +1 oxidation state gets progressively stabilized on going down the group.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is a consequence of the so called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "inert pair effect", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["113902"]',NULL),
(16505,48,264,'paragraph',NULL,'Carbon is a typical non-metal forming covalent bonds employing all its four valence electrons (2s 2 2p 2 ). It shows the property of catenation , the ability to form chains or rings, not only with C–C single bonds but also with multiple bonds (C=C or C≡C). The tendency to catenation decreases as C>>Si>Ge ~ Sn > Pb. Carbon provides one of the best examples of allotropy. Three important allotropes of carbon are diamond , graphite and fullerenes . The members of the carbon family mainly exhibit +4 and +2 oxidation states; compounds in +4 oxidation states are generally covalent in nature. The tendency to show +2 oxidation state increases among heavier elements. Lead in +2 state is stable whereas in +4 oxidation state it is a strong oxidizing agent. Carbon also exhibits negative oxidation states. It forms two important oxides: CO and CO 2 . Carbon monoxide is neutral whereas CO 2 is acidic in nature. Carbon monoxide having lone pairs of electrons on C forms metal carbonyls. It is deadly poisonous due to higher stability of its hemoglobin complex as compared to that of oxyhaemoglobin complex. Carbon dioxide as such is not toxic. However, increased content of CO 2 in the atmosphere due to combustion of fossil fuels and decomposition of limestone is feared to cause an increase in ‘greenhouse effect’. This, in turn, raises the temperature of the atmosphere and causes serious complications. Silica, silicates and silicones are important classes of compounds and find applications in industry and technology.','[{"s": "Carbon is a typical non-metal forming covalent bonds employing all its four valence electrons (2s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). It shows the property of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "catenation", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the ability to form chains or rings, not only with C–C single bonds but also with multiple bonds (C=C or C≡C). The tendency to catenation decreases as C>>Si>Ge", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Sn > Pb. Carbon provides one of the best examples of allotropy. Three important allotropes of carbon are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "diamond", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "graphite", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "fullerenes", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The members of the carbon family mainly exhibit +4 and +2 oxidation states; compounds in +4 oxidation states are generally covalent in nature. The tendency to show +2 oxidation state increases among heavier elements. Lead in +2 state is stable whereas in +4 oxidation state it is a strong oxidizing agent. Carbon also exhibits negative oxidation states. It forms two important oxides: CO and CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Carbon monoxide is neutral whereas CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is acidic in nature. Carbon monoxide having lone pairs of electrons on C forms metal carbonyls. It is deadly poisonous due to higher stability of its hemoglobin complex as compared to that of oxyhaemoglobin complex. Carbon dioxide as such is not toxic. However, increased content of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the atmosphere due to combustion of fossil fuels and decomposition of limestone is feared to cause an increase in ‘greenhouse effect’. This, in turn, raises the temperature of the atmosphere and causes serious complications. Silica, silicates and silicones are important classes of compounds and find applications in industry and technology.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
