(16904,48,663,'paragraph',NULL,'49. A non-metallic element of group 13, used in making bullet proof vests is extremely hard solid of black colour. It can exist in many allotropic forms and has unusually high melting point. Its trifluoride acts as Lewis acid towards ammonia.','[{"s": "49. A non-metallic element of group 13, used in making bullet proof vests is extremely hard solid of black colour. It can exist in many allotropic forms and has unusually high melting point. Its trifluoride acts as Lewis acid towards ammonia.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16905,48,664,'paragraph',NULL,'The element exhibits maximum covalency of four. Identify the element and write the reaction of its trifluoride with ammonia. Explain why does the trifluoride act as a Lewis acid?','[{"s": "The element exhibits maximum covalency of four. Identify the element and write the reaction of its trifluoride with ammonia. Explain why does the trifluoride act as a Lewis acid?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16906,48,665,'paragraph',NULL,'50. A tetravalent element forms monoxide and dioxide with oxygen. When air is passed over heated element (1273 K), produces gas is obtained. Monoxide of the element is a powerful reducing agent and reduces ferric oxide to iron.(i) B to Tl and (ii) C to Pb.','[{"s": "50. A tetravalent element forms monoxide and dioxide with oxygen. When air is passed over heated element (1273 K), produces gas is obtained. Monoxide of the element is a powerful reducing agent and reduces ferric oxide to iron.(i) B to Tl and (ii) C to Pb.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16907,49,0,'paragraph',NULL,'Diversity in chemistry is the hallmark of p–block elements manifested in their ability to react with the elements of s–, d– and f–blocks as well as with their own.','[{"s": "Diversity in chemistry is the hallmark of p–block elements manifested in their ability to react with the elements of s–, d– and f–blocks as well as with their own.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16908,49,1,'paragraph',NULL,'Objectives','[{"s": "Objectives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16909,49,2,'paragraph',NULL,'After studying this Unit, you will be able to','[{"s": "After studying this Unit, you will be able to", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16910,49,3,'paragraph',NULL,'In Class XI, you have learnt that the p -block elements are placed in groups 13 to 18 of the periodic table. Their valence shell electronic configuration is ns 2 np 1–6 (except He which has 1 s 2 configuration). The properties of p -block elements like that of others are greatly influenced by atomic sizes, ionisation enthalpy, electron gain enthalpy and electronegativity. The absence of d -orbitals in second period and presence of d or d and f orbitals in heavier elements (starting from third period onwards) have significant effects on the properties of elements. In addition, the presence of all the three types of elements; metals, metalloids and non-metals bring diversification in chemistry of these elements.','[{"s": "In Class XI, you have learnt that the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block elements are placed in groups 13 to 18 of the periodic table. Their valence shell electronic configuration is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "np", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1–6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(except He which has 1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration). The properties of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block elements like that of others are greatly influenced by atomic sizes, ionisation enthalpy, electron gain enthalpy and electronegativity. The absence of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-orbitals in second period and presence of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals in heavier elements (starting from third period onwards) have significant effects on the properties of elements. In addition, the presence of all the three types of elements; metals, metalloids and non-metals bring diversification in chemistry of these elements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16911,49,4,'paragraph',NULL,'Having learnt the chemistry of elements of Groups 13 and 14 of the p -block of periodic table in Class XI, you will learn the chemistry of the elements of subsequent groups in this Unit.','[{"s": "Having learnt the chemistry of elements of Groups 13 and 14 of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-block of periodic table in Class XI, you will learn the chemistry of the elements of subsequent groups in this Unit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16912,49,5,'heading',3,'7.1 GROUP 15 ELEMENTS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16913,49,6,'paragraph',NULL,'Group 15 includes nitrogen, phosphorus, arsenic, antimony, bismuth and moscovium. As we go down the group, there is a shift from non-metallic to metallic through metalloidic character. Nitrogen and phosphorus are non-metals, arsenic and antimony metalloids, bismuth and moscovium are typical metals.','[{"s": "Group 15 includes nitrogen, phosphorus, arsenic, antimony, bismuth and moscovium. As we go down the group, there is a shift from non-metallic to metallic through metalloidic character. Nitrogen and phosphorus are non-metals, arsenic and antimony metalloids, bismuth and moscovium are typical metals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16914,49,7,'heading',4,'7.1.1 OCCURRENCE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16915,49,8,'paragraph',NULL,'Molecular nitrogen comprises 78% by volume of the atmosphere. In the earth’s crust, it occurs as sodium nitrate, NaNO 3 (called Chile saltpetre) and potassium nitrate (Indian saltpetre). It is found in the form of proteins in plants and animals. Phosphorus occurs in minerals of the apatite family, Ca 9 (PO 4 ) 6 . CaX 2 (X = F, Cl or OH) (e.g., fluorapatite Ca 9 (PO 4 ) 6 . CaF 2 ) which are the main components of phosphate rocks. Phosphorus is an essential constituent of animal and plant matter. It is present in bones as well as in living cells. Phosphoproteins are present in milk and eggs. Arsenic, antimony and bismuth are found mainly as sulphide minerals. Moscovium is a synthetic radioactive element. Its symbol is Mc, atomic number 115, atomic mass 289 and electronic configuration [Rn] 5 f 14 6 d 10 7 s 2 7 p 3 . Due to very short half life and availability in very little amount, its chemistry is yet to be established.','[{"s": "Molecular nitrogen comprises 78% by volume of the atmosphere. In the earth’s crust, it occurs as sodium nitrate, NaNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(called Chile saltpetre) and potassium nitrate (Indian saltpetre). It is found in the form of proteins in plants and animals. Phosphorus occurs in minerals of the apatite family, Ca", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". CaX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(X = F, Cl or OH) (e.g., fluorapatite Ca", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". CaF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") which are the main components of phosphate rocks. Phosphorus is an essential constituent of animal and plant matter. It is present in bones as well as in living cells. Phosphoproteins are present in milk and eggs. Arsenic, antimony and bismuth are found mainly as sulphide minerals. Moscovium is a synthetic radioactive element. Its symbol is Mc, atomic number 115, atomic mass 289 and electronic configuration [Rn] 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Due to very short half life and availability in very little amount, its chemistry is yet to be established.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
