(16919,49,12,'paragraph',NULL,'Trends of some of the atomic, physical and chemical properties of the group are discussed below.','[{"s": "Trends of some of the atomic, physical and chemical properties of the group are discussed below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16920,49,13,'heading',4,'7.1.2 ELECTRONIC CONFIGURATION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16921,49,14,'paragraph',NULL,'The valence shell electronic configuration of these elements is ns 2 np 3 . The s orbital in these elements is completely filled and p orbitals are half-filled, making their electronic configuration extra stable.','[{"s": "The valence shell electronic configuration of these elements is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "np", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbital in these elements is completely filled and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals are half-filled, making their electronic configuration extra stable.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16922,49,15,'heading',4,'7.1.3 ATOMIC AND IONIC RADII',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16923,49,16,'paragraph',NULL,'Covalent and ionic (in a particular state) radii increase in size down the group. There is a considerable increase in covalent radius from N to P. However, from As to Bi only a small increase in covalent radius is observed. This is due to the presence of completely filled d and/or f orbitals in heavier members.','[{"s": "Covalent and ionic (in a particular state) radii increase in size down the group. There is a considerable increase in covalent radius from N to P. However, from As to Bi only a small increase in covalent radius is observed. This is due to the presence of completely filled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and/or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals in heavier members.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16924,49,17,'heading',4,'7.1.4 IONISATION ENTHALPY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16925,49,18,'paragraph',NULL,'Ionisation enthalpy decreases down the group due to gradual increase in atomic size. Because of the extra stable half-filled p orbitals electronic configuration and smaller size, the ionisation enthalpy of the group 15 elements is much greater than that of group 14 elements in the corresponding periods. The order of successive ionisation enthalpies, as expected is ∆iH 1 < ∆iH 2 < ∆iH 3 (Table 7.1).','[{"s": "Ionisation enthalpy decreases down the group due to gradual increase in atomic size. Because of the extra stable half-filled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals electronic configuration and smaller size, the ionisation enthalpy of the group 15 elements is much greater than that of group 14 elements in the corresponding periods. The order of successive ionisation enthalpies, as expected is ∆iH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< ∆iH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< ∆iH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Table 7.1).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16926,49,19,'heading',4,'7.1.5 ELECTRONEGATIVITY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16927,49,20,'paragraph',NULL,'The electronegativity value, in general, decreases down the group with increasing atomic size. However, amongst the heavier elements, the difference is not that much pronounced.','[{"s": "The electronegativity value, in general, decreases down the group with increasing atomic size. However, amongst the heavier elements, the difference is not that much pronounced.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16928,49,21,'heading',4,'7.1.6 PHYSICAL PROPERTIES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21602,55,572,'paragraph',NULL,'7.13 A coil of inductance 0.50 H and resistance 100 Ω is connected to a 240 V, 50 Hz ac supply.','[{"s": "7.13 A coil of inductance 0.50 H and resistance 100 Ω is connected to a 240 V, 50 Hz ac supply.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000045277", "2000112687"]',NULL),
(16929,49,22,'paragraph',NULL,'All the elements of this group are polyatomic. Dinitrogen is a diatomic gas while all others are solids. Metallic character increases down the group. Nitrogen and phosphorus are non-metals, arsenic and antimony metalloids and bismuth is a metal. This is due to decrease in ionisation enthalpy and increase in atomic size. The boiling points, in general, increase from top to bottom in the group but the melting point increases upto arsenic and then decreases upto bismuth. Except nitrogen, all the elements show allotropy.','[{"s": "All the elements of this group are polyatomic. Dinitrogen is a diatomic gas while all others are solids. Metallic character increases down the group. Nitrogen and phosphorus are non-metals, arsenic and antimony metalloids and bismuth is a metal. This is due to decrease in ionisation enthalpy and increase in atomic size. The boiling points, in general, increase from top to bottom in the group but the melting point increases upto arsenic and then decreases upto bismuth. Except nitrogen, all the elements show allotropy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16930,49,23,'heading',4,'7.1.7 CHEMICAL PROPERTIES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16931,49,24,'paragraph',NULL,'Oxidation states and trends in chemical reactivity','[{"s": "Oxidation states and trends in chemical reactivity", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16932,49,25,'paragraph',NULL,'The common oxidation states of these elements are –3, +3 and +5. The tendency to exhibit –3 oxidation state decreases down the group due to increase in size and metallic character. In fact last member of the group, bismuth hardly forms any compound in –3 oxidation state. The stability of +5 oxidation state decreases down the group. The only well characterised Bi (V) compound is BiF 5 . The stability of +5 oxidation state decreases and that of +3 state increases (due to inert pair effect) down the group. Besides +5 oxidation state, nitrogen exhibits + 1, + 2, + 4 oxidation states also when it reacts with oxygen. However, it does not form compounds in +5 oxidation state with halogens as nitrogen does not have d -orbitals to accommodate electrons from other elements to form bonds. Phosphorus also shows +1 and +4 oxidation states in some oxoacids.','[{"s": "The common oxidation states of these elements are –3, +3 and +5. The tendency to exhibit –3 oxidation state decreases down the group due to increase in size and metallic character. In fact last member of the group, bismuth hardly forms any compound in –3 oxidation state. The stability of +5 oxidation state decreases down the group. The only well characterised Bi (V) compound is BiF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The stability of +5 oxidation state decreases and that of +3 state increases (due to inert pair effect) down the group. Besides +5 oxidation state, nitrogen exhibits + 1, + 2, + 4 oxidation states also when it reacts with oxygen. However, it does not form compounds in +5 oxidation state with halogens as nitrogen does not have", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-orbitals to accommodate electrons from other elements to form bonds. Phosphorus also shows +1 and +4 oxidation states in some oxoacids.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16933,49,26,'paragraph',NULL,'In the case of nitrogen, all oxidation states from +1 to +4 tend to disproportionate in acid solution. For example,','[{"s": "In the case of nitrogen, all oxidation states from +1 to +4 tend to disproportionate in acid solution. For example,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16934,49,27,'paragraph',NULL,'3HNO 2 → HNO 3 + H 2 O + 2NO','[{"s": "3HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O + 2NO", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16935,49,28,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, in case of phosphorus nearly all intermediate oxidation states disproportionate into +5 and –3 both in alkali and acid. However +3 oxidation state in case of arsenic, antimony and bismuth becomes increasingly stable with respect to disproportionation.','[{"s": "Similarly, in case of phosphorus nearly all intermediate oxidation states disproportionate into +5 and –3 both in alkali and acid. However +3 oxidation state in case of arsenic, antimony and bismuth becomes increasingly stable with respect to disproportionation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16936,49,29,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitrogen is restricted to a maximum covalency of 4 since only four (one s and three p) orbitals are available for bonding. The heavier elements have vacant d orbitals in the outermost shell which can be used for bonding (covalency) and hence, expand their covalence as in PF – 6 .','[{"s": "Nitrogen is restricted to a maximum covalency of 4 since only four (one s and three p) orbitals are available for bonding. The heavier elements have vacant d orbitals in the outermost shell which can be used for bonding (covalency) and hence, expand their covalence as in PF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16937,49,30,'paragraph',NULL,'Anomalous properties of nitrogen','[{"s": "Anomalous properties of nitrogen", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16938,49,31,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitrogen differs from the rest of the members of this group due to its small size, high electronegativity, high ionisation enthalpy and non-availability of d orbitals. Nitrogen has unique ability to form p π -p π multiple bonds with itself and with other elements having small size and high electronegativity (e.g., C, O). Heavier elements of this group do not form pπ-pπ bonds as their atomic orbitals are so large and diffuse that they cannot have effective overlapping. Thus, nitrogen exists as a diatomic molecule with a triple bond (one s and two p) between the two atoms. Consequently, its bond enthalpy (941.4 kJ mol –1 ) is very high. On the contrary, phosphorus, arsenic and antimony form single bonds as P–P, As–As and Sb–Sb while bismuth forms metallic bonds in elemental state. However, the single N–N bond is weaker than the single P–P bond because of high interelectronic repulsion of the non-bonding electrons, owing to the small bond length. As a result the catenation tendency is weaker in nitrogen. Another factor which affects the chemistry of nitrogen is the absence of d orbitals in its valence shell. Besides restricting its covalency to four, nitrogen cannot form d π –p π bond as the heavier elements can e.g., R 3 P = O or R 3 P = CH 2 (R = alkyl group). Phosphorus and arsenic can form d π –d π bond also with transition metals when their compounds like P(C 2 H 5 ) 3 and As(C 6 H 5 ) 3 act as ligands.','[{"s": "Nitrogen differs from the rest of the members of this group due to its small size, high electronegativity, high ionisation enthalpy and non-availability of d orbitals. Nitrogen has unique ability to form", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "multiple", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bonds with itself and with other elements having small size and high electronegativity (e.g., C, O). Heavier elements of this group do not form pπ-pπ bonds as their atomic orbitals are so large and diffuse that they cannot have effective overlapping. Thus, nitrogen exists as a diatomic molecule with a triple bond (one s and two p) between the two atoms. Consequently, its bond enthalpy (941.4 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is very high. On the contrary, phosphorus, arsenic and antimony form single bonds as P–P, As–As and Sb–Sb while bismuth forms metallic bonds in elemental state. However, the single N–N bond is weaker than the single P–P bond because of high interelectronic repulsion of the non-bonding electrons, owing to the small bond length. As a result the catenation tendency is weaker in nitrogen. Another factor which affects the chemistry of nitrogen is the absence of d orbitals in its valence shell. Besides restricting its covalency to four, nitrogen cannot form", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bond", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as the heavier elements can e.g., R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P = O or R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P = CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(R = alkyl group). Phosphorus and arsenic can form", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bond", "t": "text"}, {"s": "also with transition metals when their compounds like P(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and As(C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "act as ligands.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17872,50,227,'paragraph',NULL,'In section (8.2.1) we have already discussed that the metals – zinc (Zn), copper (Cu) and silver (Ag) through tendency to lose electrons show their reducing activity in the order Zn> Cu>Ag. Like metals, activity series also exists for the halogens. The power of these elements as oxidising agents decreases as we move down from fluorine to iodine in group 17 of the periodic table. This implies that fluorine is so reactive that it can replace chloride, bromide and iodide ions in solution. In fact, fluorine is so reactive that it attacks water and displaces the oxygen of water :','[{"s": "In section (8.2.1) we have already discussed that the metals – zinc (Zn), copper (Cu) and silver (Ag) through tendency to lose electrons show their reducing activity in the order Zn> Cu>Ag. Like metals, activity series also exists for the halogens. The power of these elements as oxidising agents decreases as we move down from fluorine to iodine in group 17 of the periodic table. This implies that fluorine is so reactive that it can replace chloride, bromide and iodide ions in solution. In fact, fluorine is so reactive that it attacks water and displaces the oxygen of water :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16939,49,32,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Reactivity towards hydrogen: All the elements of Group 15 form hydrides of the type EH 3 where E = N, P, As, Sb or Bi. Some of the properties of these hydrides are shown in Table 7.2. The hydrides show regular gradation in their properties. The stability of hydrides decreases from NH 3 to BiH 3 which can be observed from their bond dissociation enthalpy. Consequently, the reducing character of the hydrides increases. Ammonia is only a mild reducing agent while BiH 3 is the strongest reducing agent amongst all the hydrides. Basicity also decreases in the order NH 3 > PH 3 > AsH 3 > SbH 3 > BiH 3 . Due to high electronegativity and small size of nitrogen, NH 3 exhibits hydrogen bonding in solid as well as liquid state. Because of this, it has higher melting and boiling points than that of PH 3 .','[{"s": "(i) Reactivity towards hydrogen: All the elements of Group 15 form hydrides of the type EH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where E = N, P, As, Sb or Bi. Some of the properties of these hydrides are shown in Table 7.2. The hydrides show regular gradation in their properties. The stability of hydrides decreases from NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to BiH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which can be observed from their bond dissociation enthalpy. Consequently, the reducing character of the hydrides increases. Ammonia is only a mild reducing agent while BiH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the strongest reducing agent amongst all the hydrides. Basicity also decreases in the order NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> PH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> AsH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> SbH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> BiH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Due to high electronegativity and small size of nitrogen, NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "exhibits hydrogen bonding in solid as well as liquid state. Because of this, it has higher melting and boiling points than that of PH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16940,49,33,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 7.2: Properties of Hydrides of Group 15 Elements','[{"s": "Table 7.2:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Properties of Hydrides of Group 15 Elements", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["214287"]',NULL),
(16941,49,34,'image',NULL,'7b',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image41.png'),
(16942,49,35,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Reactivity towards oxygen: All these elements form two types of oxides: E 2 O 3 and E 2 O 5 . The oxide in the higher oxidation state of the element is more acidic than that of lower oxidation state. Their acidic character decreases down the group. The oxides of the type E 2 O 3 of nitrogen and phosphorus are purely acidic, that of arsenic and antimony amphoteric and those of bismuth predominantly basic.','[{"s": "(ii) Reactivity towards oxygen: All these elements form two types of oxides: E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The oxide in the higher oxidation state of the element is more acidic than that of lower oxidation state. Their acidic character decreases down the group. The oxides of the type E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of nitrogen and phosphorus are purely acidic, that of arsenic and antimony amphoteric and those of bismuth predominantly basic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16943,49,36,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Reactivity towards halogens: These elements react to form two series of halides: EX 3 and EX 5 . Nitrogen does not form pentahalide due to non-availability of the d orbitals in its valence shell. Pentahalides are more covalent than trihalides. This is due to the fact that in pentahalides +5 oxidation state exists while in the case of trihalides +3 oxidation state exists. Since elements in +5 oxidation state will have more polarising power than in +3 oxidation state, the covalent character of bonds is more in pentahalides. All the trihalides of these elements except those of nitrogen are stable. In case of nitrogen, only NF 3 is known to be stable. Trihalides except BiF 3 are predominantly covalent in nature.','[{"s": "(iii) Reactivity towards halogens: These elements react to form two series of halides: EX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and EX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Nitrogen does not form pentahalide due to non-availability of the d orbitals in its valence shell. Pentahalides are more covalent than trihalides. This is due to the fact that in pentahalides +5 oxidation state exists while in the case of trihalides +3 oxidation state exists. Since elements in +5 oxidation state will have more polarising power than in +3 oxidation state, the covalent character of bonds is more in pentahalides. All the trihalides of these elements except those of nitrogen are stable. In case of nitrogen, only NF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is known to be stable. Trihalides except BiF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are predominantly covalent in nature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16944,49,37,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Reactivity towards metals: All these elements react with metals to form their binary compounds exhibiting –3 oxidation state, such as, Ca 3 N 2 (calcium nitride) Ca 3 P 2 (calcium phosphide), Na 3 As (sodium arsenide), Zn 3 Sb 2 (zinc antimonide) and Mg 3 Bi 2 (magnesium bismuthide).','[{"s": "(iv) Reactivity towards metals: All these elements react with metals to form their binary compounds exhibiting –3 oxidation state, such as, Ca", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(calcium nitride) Ca", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(calcium phosphide), Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "As (sodium arsenide), Zn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Sb", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(zinc antimonide) and Mg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Bi", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(magnesium bismuthide).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16945,49,38,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.1','[{"s": "Example 7.1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16946,49,39,'paragraph',NULL,'Though nitrogen exhibits +5 oxidation state, it does not form pentahalide. Give reason.','[{"s": "Though nitrogen exhibits +5 oxidation state, it does not form pentahalide. Give reason.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16947,49,40,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16948,49,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitrogen with n = 2, has s and p orbitals only. It does not have d orbitals to expand its covalence beyond four. That is why it does not form pentahalide.','[{"s": "Nitrogen with n = 2, has s and p orbitals only. It does not have d orbitals to expand its covalence beyond four. That is why it does not form pentahalide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16949,49,42,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.2','[{"s": "Example 7.2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(16950,49,43,'paragraph',NULL,'PH 3 has lower boiling point than NH 3 . Why?','[{"s": "PH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has lower boiling point than NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16951,49,44,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17873,50,228,'paragraph',NULL,'+1 –2 0 +1 –1 0','[{"s": "+1 –2 0 +1 –1 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16952,49,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Unlike NH 3 , PH 3 molecules are not associated through hydrogen bonding in liquid state. That is why the boiling point of PH 3 is lower than NH 3 .','[{"s": "Unlike NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", PH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules are not associated through hydrogen bonding in liquid state. That is why the boiling point of PH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is lower than NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16953,49,46,'heading',3,'7.2 DINITROGEN',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16954,49,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Preparation','[{"s": "Preparation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16955,49,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Dinitrogen is produced commercially by the liquefaction and fractional distillation of air. Liquid dinitrogen (b.p. 77.2 K) distils out first leaving behind liquid oxygen (b.p. 90 K).','[{"s": "Dinitrogen is produced commercially by the liquefaction and fractional distillation of air. Liquid dinitrogen (b.p. 77.2 K) distils out first leaving behind liquid oxygen (b.p. 90 K).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16956,49,49,'paragraph',NULL,'In the laboratory, dinitrogen is prepared by treating an aqueous solution of ammonium chloride with sodium nitrite.','[{"s": "In the laboratory, dinitrogen is prepared by treating an aqueous solution of ammonium chloride with sodium nitrite.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16957,49,50,'paragraph',NULL,'NH 4 CI(aq) + NaNO 2 (aq) P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image19.png N 2 (g) + 2H 2 O(l) + NaCl (aq)','[{"s": "NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CI(aq) + NaNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image19.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) + NaCl (aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16958,49,51,'paragraph',NULL,'Small amounts of NO and HNO 3 are also formed in this reaction; these impurities can be removed by passing the gas through aqueous sulphuric acid containing potassium dichromate. It can also be obtained by the thermal decomposition of ammonium dichromate.','[{"s": "Small amounts of NO and HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are also formed in this reaction; these impurities can be removed by passing the gas through aqueous sulphuric acid containing potassium dichromate.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It can also be obtained by the thermal decomposition of ammonium dichromate.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["214210"]',NULL),
(16959,49,52,'paragraph',NULL,'(NH 4 ) 2 Cr 2 O 7 P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image38.png N 2 + 4H 2 O + Cr 2 O 3','[{"s": "(NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image38.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O + Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16960,49,53,'paragraph',NULL,'Very pure nitrogen can be obtained by the thermal decomposition of sodium or barium azide.','[{"s": "Very pure nitrogen can be obtained by the thermal decomposition of sodium or barium azide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16961,49,54,'paragraph',NULL,'Ba(N 3 ) 2 P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image19.png Ba + 3N 2','[{"s": "Ba(N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image19.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Ba + 3N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16962,49,55,'paragraph',NULL,'Properties','[{"s": "Properties", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16963,49,56,'paragraph',NULL,'Dinitrogen is a colourless, odourless, tasteless and non-toxic gas. Nitrogen atom has two stable isotopes: 14 N and 15 N. It has a very low solubility in water (23.2 cm 3 per litre of water at 273 K and 1 bar pressure) and low freezing and boiling points (Table 7.1).','[{"s": "Dinitrogen is a colourless, odourless, tasteless and non-toxic gas. Nitrogen atom has two stable isotopes:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N. It has a very low solubility in water (23.2 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "per litre of water at 273 K and 1 bar pressure) and low freezing and boiling points (Table 7.1).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16964,49,57,'paragraph',NULL,'Dinitrogen is rather inert at room temperature because of the high bond enthalpy of N≡N bond. Reactivity, however, increases rapidly with rise in temperature. At higher temperatures, it directly combines with some metals to form predominantly ionic nitrides and with non-metals, covalent nitrides. A few typical reactions are:','[{"s": "Dinitrogen is rather inert at room temperature because of the high bond enthalpy of N≡N bond. Reactivity, however, increases rapidly with rise in temperature. At higher temperatures, it directly combines with some metals to form predominantly ionic nitrides and with non-metals, covalent nitrides. A few typical reactions are:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16965,49,58,'paragraph',NULL,'6Li + N 2 P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image38.png 2Li 3 N','[{"s": "6Li + N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image38.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "2Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16966,49,59,'paragraph',NULL,'3Mg + N 2 P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image38.png Mg 3 N 2','[{"s": "3Mg + N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image38.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Mg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(16967,49,60,'paragraph',NULL,'It combines with hydrogen at about 773 K in the presence of a catalyst (Haber’s Process) to form ammonia:','[{"s": "It combines with hydrogen at about 773 K in the presence of a catalyst (Haber’s Process) to form ammonia:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
