(17023,49,116,'paragraph',NULL,'Why does NO 2 dimerise ?','[{"s": "Why does NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dimerise ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17024,49,117,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17025,49,118,'paragraph',NULL,'NO 2 contains odd number of valence electrons. It behaves as a typical odd molecule. On dimerisation, it is converted to stable N 2 O 4 molecule with even number of electrons.','[{"s": "NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "contains odd number of valence electrons. It behaves as a typical odd molecule. On dimerisation, it is converted to stable N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule with even number of electrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17026,49,119,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Question','[{"s": "Intext Question", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17027,49,120,'paragraph',NULL,'7.6 What is the covalence of nitrogen in N 2 O 5 ?','[{"s": "7.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What is the covalence of nitrogen in N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17028,49,121,'heading',3,'7.5 NITRIC ACID',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17029,49,122,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitrogen forms oxoacids such as H 2 N 2 O 2 (hyponitrous acid), HNO 2 (nitrous acid) and HNO 3 (nitric acid). Amongst them HNO 3 is the most important.','[{"s": "Nitrogen forms oxoacids such as H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(hyponitrous acid), HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(nitrous acid) and HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(nitric acid). Amongst them HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the most important.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17030,49,123,'paragraph',NULL,'Preparation','[{"s": "Preparation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17031,49,124,'paragraph',NULL,'In the laboratory, nitric acid is prepared by heating KNO 3 or NaNO 3 and concentrated H 2 SO 4 in a glass retort.','[{"s": "In the laboratory, nitric acid is prepared by heating KNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or NaNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and concentrated H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in a glass retort.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17032,49,125,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image119.png'),
(17033,49,126,'paragraph',NULL,'On a large scale it is prepared mainly by Ostwald’s process.','[{"s": "On a large scale it is prepared mainly by Ostwald’s process.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["214303"]',NULL),
(17034,49,127,'paragraph',NULL,'This method is based upon catalytic oxidation of NH 3 by atmospheric oxygen.','[{"s": "This method is based upon catalytic oxidation of NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by atmospheric oxygen.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17035,49,128,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image29.png'),
(17036,49,129,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitric oxide thus formed combines with oxygen giving NO 2 .','[{"s": "Nitric oxide thus formed combines with oxygen giving NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17037,49,130,'image',NULL,'7eq4',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image82.png'),
(17038,49,131,'paragraph',NULL,'Nitrogen dioxide so formed, dissolves in water to give HNO 3 .','[{"s": "Nitrogen dioxide so formed, dissolves in water to give HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17039,49,132,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image123.png'),
(17040,49,133,'paragraph',NULL,'NO thus formed is recycled and the aqueous HNO 3 can be concentrated by distillation upto ~ 68% by mass. Further concentration to 98% can be achieved by dehydration with concentrated H 2 SO 4 .','[{"s": "NO thus formed is recycled and the aqueous HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be concentrated by distillation upto ~ 68% by mass. Further concentration to 98% can be achieved by dehydration with concentrated H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17041,49,134,'paragraph',NULL,'Properties','[{"s": "Properties", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17042,49,135,'paragraph',NULL,'It is a colourless liquid (f.p. 231.4 K and b.p. 355.6 K). Laboratory grade nitric acid contains ~ 68% of the HNO 3 by mass and has a specific gravity of 1.504.','[{"s": "It is a colourless liquid (f.p. 231.4 K and b.p. 355.6 K). Laboratory grade nitric acid contains ~ 68% of the HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by mass and has a specific gravity of 1.504.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17043,49,136,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image120.png'),
(17044,49,137,'paragraph',NULL,'In the gaseous state, HNO 3 exists as a planar molecule with the structure as shown.','[{"s": "In the gaseous state, HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "exists as a planar molecule with the structure as shown.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17045,49,138,'paragraph',NULL,'In aqueous solution, nitric acid behaves as a strong acid giving hydronium and nitrate ions.','[{"s": "In aqueous solution, nitric acid behaves as a strong acid giving hydronium and nitrate ions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17046,49,139,'paragraph',NULL,'HNO 3 (aq) + H 2 O(l) → H 3 O + (aq) + NO 3 – (aq)','[{"s": "HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) → H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17047,49,140,'paragraph',NULL,'Concentrated nitric acid is a strong oxidising agent and attacks most metals except noble metals such as gold and platinum. The products of oxidation depend upon the concentration of the acid, temperature and the nature of the material undergoing oxidation.','[{"s": "Concentrated nitric acid is a strong oxidising agent and attacks most metals except noble metals such as gold and platinum.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The products of oxidation depend upon the concentration of the acid, temperature and the nature of the material undergoing oxidation.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["214202"]',NULL),
(17048,49,141,'paragraph',NULL,'3Cu + 8 HNO 3 (dilute) → 3Cu(NO 3 ) 2 + 2NO + 4H 2 O','[{"s": "3Cu + 8 HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(dilute) → 3Cu(NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2NO + 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17049,49,142,'paragraph',NULL,'Cu + 4HNO 3 (conc.) → Cu(NO 3 ) 2 + 2NO 2 + 2H 2 O','[{"s": "Cu + 4HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(conc.) → Cu(NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17050,49,143,'paragraph',NULL,'Zinc reacts with dilute nitric acid to give N 2 O and with concentrated acid to give NO 2 .','[{"s": "Zinc reacts with dilute nitric acid to give N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O and with concentrated acid to give NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17051,49,144,'paragraph',NULL,'4Zn + 10HNO 3 (dilute) → 4 Zn (NO 3 ) 2 + 5H 2 O + N 2 O','[{"s": "4Zn + 10HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(dilute) → 4 Zn (NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 5H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O + N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17052,49,145,'paragraph',NULL,'Zn + 4HNO 3 (conc.) → Zn (NO 3 ) 2 + 2H 2 O + 2NO 2','[{"s": "Zn + 4HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(conc.) → Zn (NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O + 2NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17053,49,146,'paragraph',NULL,'Some metals (e.g., Cr, Al) do not dissolve in concentrated nitric acid because of the formation of a passive film of oxide on the surface.','[{"s": "Some metals (e.g., Cr, Al) do not dissolve in concentrated nitric acid because of the formation of a passive film of oxide on the surface.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17054,49,147,'paragraph',NULL,'Concentrated nitric acid also oxidises non–metals and their compounds. Iodine is oxidised to iodic acid, carbon to carbon dioxide, sulphur to H 2 SO 4 , and phosphorus to phosphoric acid.','[{"s": "Concentrated nitric acid also oxidises non–metals and their compounds. Iodine is oxidised to iodic acid, carbon to carbon dioxide, sulphur to H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and phosphorus to phosphoric acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17055,49,148,'paragraph',NULL,'I 2 + 10HNO 3 → 2HIO 3 + 10NO 2 + 4H 2 O','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 10HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 2HIO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 10NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17056,49,149,'paragraph',NULL,'C + 4HNO 3 → CO 2 + 2H 2 O + 4NO 2','[{"s": "C + 4HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O + 4NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17057,49,150,'paragraph',NULL,'S 8 + 48HNO 3 → 8H 2 SO 4 + 48NO 2 + 16H 2 O','[{"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 48HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 8H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 48NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 16H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17058,49,151,'paragraph',NULL,'P 4 + 20HNO 3 → 4H 3 PO 4 + 20NO 2 + 4H 2 O','[{"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 20HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 20NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17059,49,152,'paragraph',NULL,'Brown Ring Test: The familiar brown ring test for nitrates depends on the ability of Fe 2+ to reduce nitrates to nitric oxide, which reacts with Fe 2+ to form a brown coloured complex. The test is usually carried out by adding dilute ferrous sulphate solution to an aqueous solution containing nitrate ion, and then carefully adding concentrated sulphuric acid along the sides of the test tube. A brown ring at the interface between the solution and sulphuric acid layers indicates the presence of nitrate ion in solution.','[{"s": "Brown Ring Test: The familiar brown ring test for nitrates depends on the ability of Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to reduce nitrates to nitric oxide, which reacts with Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to form a brown coloured complex. The test is usually carried out by adding dilute ferrous sulphate solution to an aqueous solution containing nitrate ion, and then carefully adding concentrated sulphuric acid along the sides of the test tube. A brown ring at the interface between the solution and sulphuric acid layers indicates the presence of nitrate ion in solution.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17060,49,153,'paragraph',NULL,'NO 3 - + 3Fe 2+ + 4H + → NO + 3Fe 3+ + 2H 2 O','[{"s": "NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 3Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ NO + 3Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17061,49,154,'image',NULL,'7eq5',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image116.png'),
(17874,50,229,'paragraph',NULL,'2H 2 O (l) + 2F 2 (g) → 4HF(aq) + O 2 (g) (8.40)','[{"s": "2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l) + 2F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4HF(aq) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) (8.40)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17062,49,155,'paragraph',NULL,'Uses : The major use of nitric acid is in the manufacture of ammonium nitrate for fertilisers and other nitrates for use in explosives and pyrotechnics. It is also used for the preparation of nitroglycerin, trinitrotoluene and other organic nitro compounds. Other major uses are in the pickling of stainless steel, etching of metals and as an oxidiser in rocket fuels.','[{"s": "Uses", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": The major use of nitric acid is in the manufacture of ammonium nitrate for fertilisers and other nitrates for use in explosives and pyrotechnics. It is also used for the preparation of nitroglycerin, trinitrotoluene and other organic nitro compounds. Other major uses are in the pickling of stainless steel, etching of metals and as an oxidiser in rocket fuels.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17063,49,156,'heading',3,'7.6 PHOSPHORUS — ALLOTROPIC FORMS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17064,49,157,'paragraph',NULL,'Phosphorus is found in many allotropic forms, the important ones being white, red and black.','[{"s": "Phosphorus is found in many allotropic forms, the important ones being white, red and black.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17065,49,158,'paragraph',NULL,'White phosphorus is a translucent white waxy solid. It is poisonous, insoluble in water but soluble in carbon disulphide and glows in dark (chemiluminescence). It dissolves in boiling NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere giving PH 3 .','[{"s": "White phosphorus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a translucent white waxy solid. It is poisonous, insoluble in water but soluble in carbon disulphide and glows in dark (chemiluminescence). It dissolves in boiling NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere giving PH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17066,49,159,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image1.png'),
(17067,49,160,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image11.png'),
(17068,49,161,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.2 White phosphorus','[{"s": "Fig. 7.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "White phosphorus", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17069,49,162,'paragraph',NULL,'White phosphorus is less stable and therefore, more reactive than the other solid phases under normal conditions because of angular strain in the P 4 molecule where the angles are only 60°. It readily catches fire in air to give dense white fumes of P 4 O 10 .','[{"s": "White phosphorus is less stable and therefore, more reactive than the other solid phases under normal conditions because of angular strain in the P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule where the angles are only 60°. It readily catches fire in air to give dense white fumes of P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17070,49,163,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image27.png'),
(17071,49,164,'paragraph',NULL,'It consists of discrete tetrahedral P 4 molecule as shown in Fig. 7.2.','[{"s": "It consists of discrete tetrahedral P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule as shown in Fig. 7.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17072,49,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Red phosphorus is obtained by heating white phosphorus at 573K in an inert atmosphere for several days. When red phosphorus is heated under high pressure, a series of phases of black phosphorus is formed. Red phosphorus possesses iron grey lustre. It is odourless, non-poisonous and insoluble in water as well as in carbon disulphide. Chemically, red phosphorus is much less reactive than white phosphorus. It does not glow in the dark.','[{"s": "Red phosphorus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is obtained by heating white phosphorus at 573K in an inert atmosphere for several days. When red phosphorus is heated under high pressure, a series of phases of black phosphorus is formed. Red phosphorus possesses iron grey lustre. It is odourless, non-poisonous and insoluble in water as well as in carbon disulphide. Chemically, red phosphorus is much less reactive than white phosphorus. It does not glow in the dark.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17073,49,166,'paragraph',NULL,'It is polymeric, consisting of chains of P 4 tetrahedra linked together in the manner as shown in Fig. 7.3.','[{"s": "It is polymeric, consisting of chains of P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tetrahedra linked together in the manner as shown in Fig. 7.3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17074,49,167,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image107.png'),
(17075,49,168,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.7.3: Red phosphorus','[{"s": "Fig.7.3:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Red phosphorus", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17076,49,169,'paragraph',NULL,'Black phosphorus has two forms α-black phosphorus and β-black phosphorus. α-Black phosphorus is formed when red phosphorus is heated in a sealed tube at 803K. It can be sublimed in air and has opaque monoclinic or rhombohedral crystals. It does not oxidise in air. β-Black phosphorus is prepared by heating white phosphorus at 473 K under high pressure. It does not burn in air upto 673 K.','[{"s": "Black phosphorus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has two forms α-black phosphorus and β-black phosphorus. α-Black phosphorus is formed when red phosphorus is heated in a sealed tube at 803K. It can be sublimed in air and has opaque monoclinic or rhombohedral crystals. It does not oxidise in air. β-Black phosphorus is prepared by heating white phosphorus at 473 K under high pressure. It does not burn in air upto 673 K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17077,49,170,'heading',3,'7.7 PHOSPHINE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17078,49,171,'paragraph',NULL,'Preparation','[{"s": "Preparation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17079,49,172,'paragraph',NULL,'Phosphine is prepared by the reaction of calcium phosphide with water or dilute HCl.','[{"s": "Phosphine is prepared by the reaction of calcium phosphide with water or dilute HCl.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17080,49,173,'paragraph',NULL,'Ca 3 P 2 + 6H 2 O → 3Ca(OH) 2 + 2PH 3','[{"s": "Ca", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 6H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O → 3Ca(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2PH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17081,49,174,'paragraph',NULL,'Ca 3 P 2 + 6HCl → 3CaCl 2 + 2PH 3','[{"s": "Ca", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 6HCl → 3CaCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2PH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17082,49,175,'paragraph',NULL,'In the laboratory, it is prepared by heating white phosphorus with concentrated NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere of CO 2 .','[{"s": "In the laboratory, it is prepared by heating white phosphorus with concentrated NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17083,49,176,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image24.png'),
(17084,49,177,'paragraph',NULL,'When pure, it is non inflammable but becomes inflammable owing to the presence of P 2 H 4 or P 4 vapours. To purify it from the impurities, it is absorbed in HI to form phosphonium iodide (PH 4 I) which on treating with KOH gives off phosphine.','[{"s": "When pure, it is non inflammable but becomes inflammable owing to the presence of P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "vapours. To purify it from the impurities, it is absorbed in HI to form phosphonium iodide (PH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I) which on treating with KOH gives off phosphine.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17085,49,178,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image92.png'),
(17086,49,179,'paragraph',NULL,'Properties','[{"s": "Properties", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17087,49,180,'paragraph',NULL,'It is a colourless gas with rotten fish smell and is highly poisonous. It explodes in contact with traces of oxidising agents like HNO 3 , Cl 2 and Br 2 vapours.','[{"s": "It is a colourless gas with rotten fish smell and is highly poisonous. It explodes in contact with traces of oxidising agents like HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "vapours.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17088,49,181,'paragraph',NULL,'It is slightly soluble in water. The solution of PH 3 in water decomposes in presence of light giving red phosphorus and H 2 . When absorbed in copper sulphate or mercuric chloride solution, the corresponding phosphides are obtained.','[{"s": "It is slightly soluble in water. The solution of PH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in water decomposes in presence of light giving red phosphorus and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". When absorbed in copper sulphate or mercuric chloride solution, the corresponding phosphides are obtained.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17089,49,182,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image97.png'),
(17090,49,183,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image83.png'),
(17091,49,184,'paragraph',NULL,'Phosphine is weakly basic and like ammonia, gives phosphonium compounds with acids e.g.,','[{"s": "Phosphine is weakly basic and like ammonia, gives phosphonium compounds with acids e.g.,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17092,49,185,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image32.png'),
(17093,49,186,'paragraph',NULL,'Uses : The spontaneous combustion of phosphine is technically used in Holme’s signals. Containers containing calcium carbide and calcium phosphide are pierced and thrown in the sea when the gases evolved burn and serve as a signal. It is also used in smoke screens .','[{"s": "Uses", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ": The spontaneous combustion of phosphine is technically used in Holme’s signals.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Containers containing calcium carbide and calcium phosphide are pierced and thrown in the sea when the gases evolved burn and serve as a signal. It is also used in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "smoke screens", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["214203"]',NULL),
