(17148,49,241,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17149,49,242,'paragraph',NULL,'PCl 5 has a trigonal bipyramidal structure and the three equatorial P-Cl bonds are equivalent, while the two axial bonds are different and longer than equatorial bonds.','[{"s": "PCl", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "has a trigonal bipyramidal structure and the three equatorial P-Cl bonds are equivalent, while the two axial bonds are different and longer than equatorial bonds.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["214204"]',NULL),
(17150,49,243,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17151,49,244,'paragraph',NULL,'7.9 What happens when PCl 5 is heated?','[{"s": "7.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What happens when PCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is heated?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17152,49,245,'paragraph',NULL,'7.10 Write a balanced equation for the reaction of PCl 5 with water.','[{"s": "7.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Write a balanced equation for the reaction of PCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with water.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17153,49,246,'heading',3,'7.9 OXOACIDS OF PHOSPHORUS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17154,49,247,'paragraph',NULL,'Phosphorus forms a number of oxoacids. The important oxoacids of phosphorus with their formulas, methods of preparation and the presence of some characteristic bonds in their structures are given in Table 7.5.','[{"s": "Phosphorus forms a number of oxoacids. The important oxoacids of phosphorus with their formulas, methods of preparation and the presence of some characteristic bonds in their structures are given in Table 7.5.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17156,49,249,'image',NULL,'7e',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image13.png'),
(17157,49,250,'paragraph',NULL,'The compositions of the oxoacids are interrelated in terms of loss or gain of H 2 O molecule or O-atom. The structures of some important oxoacids are given next.','[{"s": "The compositions of the oxoacids are interrelated in terms of loss or gain of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O molecule or O-atom. The structures of some important oxoacids are given next.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17158,49,251,'paragraph',NULL,'In oxoacids phosphorus is tetrahedrally surrounded by other atoms. All these acids contain at least one P=O bond and one P–OH bond. The oxoacids in which phosphorus has lower oxidation state (less than +5) contain, in addition to P=O and P–OH bonds, either P–P (e.g., in H 4 P 2 O 6 ) or P–H (e.g., in H 3 PO 2 ) bon but not both. These acids in +3 oxidation state of phosphorus tend to disproportionate to higher and lower oxidation states. For example, orthophophorous acid (or phosphorous acid) on heating disproportionates to give orthophosphoric acid (or phosphoric acid) and phosphine.','[{"s": "In oxoacids phosphorus is tetrahedrally surrounded by other atoms. All these acids contain at least one P=O bond and one P–OH bond.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The oxoacids in which phosphorus has lower oxidation state (less than +5) contain, in addition to P=O and P–OH bonds, either P–P (e.g., in H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "P", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "O", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") or P–H (e.g., in H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") bon but not both.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "These acids in +3 oxidation state of phosphorus tend to disproportionate to higher and lower oxidation states. For example, orthophophorous acid (or phosphorous acid) on heating disproportionates to give orthophosphoric acid (or phosphoric acid) and phosphine.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["214293"]',NULL),
(17159,49,252,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image36.png'),
(17160,49,253,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image46.png'),
(17161,49,254,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image34.png'),
(17162,49,255,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image96.png'),
(17163,49,256,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image30.png'),
(17164,49,257,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image102.png'),
(17165,49,258,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.4 Structures of some important oxoacids of phosphorus','[{"s": "Fig. 7.4 Structures of some important oxoacids of phosphorus", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17166,49,259,'paragraph',NULL,'The acids which contain P–H bond have strong reducing properties. Thus, hypophosphorous acid is a good reducing agent as it contains two P–H bonds and reduces, for example, AgNO 3 to metallic silver.','[{"s": "The acids which contain P–H bond have strong reducing properties. Thus, hypophosphorous acid is a good reducing agent as it contains two P–H bonds and reduces, for example, AgNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to metallic silver.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17167,49,260,'paragraph',NULL,'4 AgNO 3 + 2H 2 O + H 3 PO 2 → 4Ag + 4HNO 3 + H 3 PO 4','[{"s": "4 AgNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 4Ag + 4HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17168,49,261,'paragraph',NULL,'These P–H bonds are not ionisable to give H + and do not play any role in basicity. Only those H atoms which are attached with oxygen in P–OH form are ionisable and cause the basicity. Thus, H 3 PO 3 and H 3 PO 4 are dibasic and tribasic, respectively as the structure of H 3 PO 3 has two P–OH bonds and H 3 PO 4 three.','[{"s": "These P–H bonds are not ionisable to give H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and do not play any role in basicity. Only those H atoms which are attached with oxygen in P–OH form are ionisable and cause the basicity. Thus, H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are dibasic and tribasic, respectively as the structure of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has two P–OH bonds and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "three.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17169,49,262,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.9','[{"s": "Example 7.9", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17170,49,263,'paragraph',NULL,'How do you account for the reducing behaviour of H 3 PO 2 on the basis of its structure? 2','[{"s": "How do you account for the reducing behaviour of H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "on the basis of its structure? 2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["214205", "214207"]',NULL),
(17171,49,264,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17172,49,265,'paragraph',NULL,'In H 3 PO 2 , two H atoms are bonded directly to P atom which imparts reducing character to the acid.','[{"s": "In H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", two H atoms are bonded directly to P atom which imparts reducing character to the acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17173,49,266,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17174,49,267,'paragraph',NULL,'7.11 What is the basicity of H 3 PO 4 ?','[{"s": "7.11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What is the basicity of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17175,49,268,'paragraph',NULL,'7.12 What happens when H 3 PO 3 is heated?','[{"s": "7.12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What happens when H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is heated?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17176,49,269,'heading',3,'7.10 GROUP 16 ELEMENTS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17177,49,270,'paragraph',NULL,'Oxygen, sulphur, selenium, tellurium, polonium and livermorium constitute Group 16 of the periodic table. This is sometimes known as group of chalcogens. The name is derived from the Greek word for brass and points to the association of sulphur and its congeners with copper. Most copper minerals contain either oxygen or sulphur and frequently the other members of the group.','[{"s": "Oxygen, sulphur, selenium, tellurium, polonium and livermorium constitute Group 16 of the periodic table. This is sometimes known as group of chalcogens. The name is derived from the Greek word for brass and points to the association of sulphur and its congeners with copper. Most copper minerals contain either oxygen or sulphur and frequently the other members of the group.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17178,49,271,'heading',4,'7.10.1 OCCURRENCE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17179,49,272,'paragraph',NULL,'Oxygen is the most abundant of all the elements on earth. Oxygen forms about 46.6% by mass of earth’s crust. Dry air contains 20.946% oxygen by volume.','[{"s": "Oxygen is the most abundant of all the elements on earth. Oxygen forms about 46.6% by mass of earth’s crust. Dry air contains 20.946% oxygen by volume.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17180,49,273,'paragraph',NULL,'However, the abundance of sulphur in the earth’s crust is only 0.03-0.1%. Combined sulphur exists primarily as sulphates such as gypsum CaSO 4 .2H 2 O, epsom salt MgSO 4 .7H 2 O, baryte BaSO 4 and sulphides such as galena PbS, zinc blende ZnS, copper pyrites CuFeS 2 . Traces of sulphur occur as hydrogen sulphide in volcanoes. Organic materials such as eggs, proteins, garlic, onion, mustard, hair and wool contain sulphur.','[{"s": "However, the abundance of sulphur in the earth’s crust is only 0.03-0.1%. Combined sulphur exists primarily as sulphates such as gypsum CaSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, epsom salt MgSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".7H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, baryte BaSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and sulphides such as galena PbS, zinc blende ZnS, copper pyrites CuFeS", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Traces of sulphur occur as hydrogen sulphide in volcanoes. Organic materials such as eggs, proteins, garlic, onion, mustard, hair and wool contain sulphur.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17181,49,274,'paragraph',NULL,'Selenium and tellurium are also found as metal selenides and tellurides in sulphide ores. Polonium occurs in nature as a decay product of thorium and uranium minerals. Livermorium is a synthetic radioactive element. Its symbol is Lv, atomic number 116, atomic mass 292 and electronic configuration [Rn] 5f 14 6d 10 7s 2 7p 4 . It has been produced only in a very small amount and has very short half-life (only a small fraction of one second). This limits the study of properlies of Lv.','[{"s": "Selenium and tellurium are also found as metal selenides and tellurides in sulphide ores. Polonium occurs in nature as a decay product of thorium and uranium minerals. Livermorium is a synthetic radioactive element. Its symbol is Lv, atomic number 116, atomic mass 292 and electronic configuration [Rn] 5f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It has been produced only in a very small amount and has very short half-life (only a small fraction of one second). This limits the study of properlies of Lv.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17182,49,275,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, except for livermorium, important atomic and physical properties of other elements of Group16 along with their electronic configurations are given in Table 7.6. Some of the atomic, physical and chemical properties and their trends are discussed below.','[{"s": "Here, except for livermorium, important atomic and physical properties of other elements of Group16 along with their electronic configurations are given in Table 7.6. Some of the atomic, physical and chemical properties and their trends are discussed below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17183,49,276,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 7.6: Some Physical Properties of Group 16 Elements','[{"s": "Table 7.6: Some Physical Properties of Group 16 Elements", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17184,49,277,'image',NULL,'7f',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image112.png'),
(17185,49,278,'heading',4,'7.10.2 ELECTRONIC CONFIGURATION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17186,49,279,'paragraph',NULL,'The elements of Group16 have six electrons in the outermost shell and have ns 2 np 4 general electronic configuration.','[{"s": "The elements of Group16 have six electrons in the outermost shell and have ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "np", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "general electronic configuration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17187,49,280,'heading',4,'7.10.3 ATOMIC AND IONIC RADII',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17188,49,281,'paragraph',NULL,'Due to increase in the number of shells, atomic and ionic radii increase from top to bottom in the group. The size of oxygen atom is, however, exceptionally small.','[{"s": "Due to increase in the number of shells, atomic and ionic radii increase from top to bottom in the group. The size of oxygen atom is, however, exceptionally small.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17189,49,282,'heading',4,'7.10.4 IONISATION ENTHALPY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17190,49,283,'paragraph',NULL,'Ionisation enthalpy decreases down the group. It is due to increase in size. However, the elements of this group have lower ionisation enthalpy values compared to those of Group15 in the corresponding periods. This is due to the fact that Group 15 elements have extra stable half-filled p orbitals electronic configurations.','[{"s": "Ionisation enthalpy decreases down the group. It is due to increase in size. However, the elements of this group have lower ionisation enthalpy values compared to those of Group15 in the corresponding periods. This is due to the fact that Group 15 elements have extra stable half-filled p orbitals electronic configurations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17191,49,284,'heading',4,'7.10.5 ELECTRON GAIN ENTHALPY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17192,49,285,'paragraph',NULL,'Because of the compact nature of oxygen atom, it has less negative electron gain enthalpy than sulphur. However, from sulphur onwards the value again becomes less negative upto polonium.','[{"s": "Because of the compact nature of oxygen atom, it has less negative electron gain enthalpy than sulphur. However, from sulphur onwards the value again becomes less negative upto polonium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17193,49,286,'heading',4,'7.10.6 ELECTRONEGATIVITY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17194,49,287,'paragraph',NULL,'Next to fluorine, oxygen has the highest electronegativity value amongst the elements. Within the group, electronegativity decreases with an increase in atomic number. This implies that the metallic character increases from oxygen to polonium.','[{"s": "Next to fluorine, oxygen has the highest electronegativity value amongst the elements. Within the group, electronegativity decreases with an increase in atomic number. This implies that the metallic character increases from oxygen to polonium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17195,49,288,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.10','[{"s": "Example 7.10", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17196,49,289,'paragraph',NULL,'Elements of Group 16 generally show lower value of first ionisation enthalpy comparedto the corresponding periods of group 15. Why?','[{"s": "Elements of Group 16 generally show lower value of first ionisation enthalpy comparedto the corresponding periods of group 15. Why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17197,49,290,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17198,49,291,'paragraph',NULL,'Due to extra stable half-filled p orbitals electronic configurations of Group 15 elements, larger amount of energy is required to remove electrons compared to Group 16 elements.','[{"s": "Due to extra stable half-filled p orbitals electronic configurations of Group 15 elements, larger amount of energy is required to remove electrons compared to Group 16 elements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17199,49,292,'heading',4,'7.10.7 PHYSICAL PROPERTIES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17200,49,293,'paragraph',NULL,'Some of the physical properties of Group 16 elements are given in Table 7.6. Oxygen and sulphur are non-metals, selenium and tellurium metalloids, whereas polonium is a metal. Polonium is radioactive and is short lived (Half-life 13.8 days). All these elements exhibit allotropy. The melting and boiling points increase with an increase in atomic number down the group. The large difference between the melting and boiling points of oxygen and sulphur may be explained on the basis of their atomicity; oxygen exists as diatomic molecule (O 2 ) whereas sulphur exists as polyatomic molecule (S 8 ).','[{"s": "Some of the physical properties of Group 16 elements are given in Table 7.6. Oxygen and sulphur are non-metals, selenium and tellurium metalloids, whereas polonium is a metal. Polonium is radioactive and is short lived (Half-life 13.8 days). All these elements exhibit allotropy. The melting and boiling points increase with an increase in atomic number down the group. The large difference between the melting and boiling points of oxygen and sulphur may be explained on the basis of their atomicity; oxygen exists as diatomic molecule (O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") whereas sulphur exists as polyatomic molecule (S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17201,49,294,'heading',4,'7.10.8 CHEMICAL PROPERTIES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17202,49,295,'paragraph',NULL,'Oxidation states and trends in chemical reactivity','[{"s": "Oxidation states and trends in chemical reactivity", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17203,49,296,'paragraph',NULL,'The elements of Group 16 exhibit a number of oxidation states (Table 7.6). The stability of -2 oxidation state decreases down the group. Polonium hardly shows –2 oxidation state. Since electronegativity of oxygen is very high, it shows only negative oxidation state as –2 except in the case of OF 2 where its oxidation state is + 2. Other elements of the group exhibit + 2, + 4, + 6 oxidation states but + 4 and + 6 are more common. Sulphur, selenium and tellurium usually show + 4 oxidation state in their compounds with oxygen and + 6 with fluorine. The stability of + 6 oxidation state decreases down the group and stability of + 4 oxidation state increases (inert pair effect). Bonding in +4 and +6 oxidation states is primarily covalent.','[{"s": "The elements of Group 16 exhibit a number of oxidation states (Table 7.6). The stability of -2 oxidation state decreases down the group. Polonium hardly shows –2 oxidation state. Since electronegativity of oxygen is very high, it shows only negative oxidation state as –2 except in the case of OF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where its oxidation state is + 2. Other elements of the group exhibit + 2, + 4, + 6 oxidation states but + 4 and + 6 are more common. Sulphur, selenium and tellurium usually show + 4 oxidation state in their compounds with oxygen and + 6 with fluorine. The stability of + 6 oxidation state decreases down the group and stability of + 4 oxidation state increases (inert pair effect). Bonding in +4 and +6 oxidation states is primarily covalent.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17204,49,297,'paragraph',NULL,'Anomalous behaviour of oxygen','[{"s": "Anomalous behaviour of oxygen", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17875,50,230,'paragraph',NULL,'It is for this reason that the displacement reactions of chlorine, bromine and iodine using fluorine are not generally carried out in aqueous solution. On the other hand, chlorine can displace bromide and iodide ions in an aqueous solution as shown below:','[{"s": "It is for this reason that the displacement reactions of chlorine, bromine and iodine using fluorine are not generally carried out in aqueous solution. On the other hand, chlorine can displace bromide and iodide ions in an aqueous solution as shown below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17876,50,231,'paragraph',NULL,'0 +1 –1 +1 –1 0','[{"s": "0 +1 –1 +1 –1 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17205,49,298,'paragraph',NULL,'The anomalous behaviour of oxygen, like other members of p-block present in second period is due to its small size and high electronegativity. One typical example of effects of small size and high electronegativity is the presence of strong hydrogen bonding in H 2 O which is not found in H 2 S.','[{"s": "The anomalous behaviour of oxygen, like other members of p-block present in second period is due to its small size and high electronegativity. One typical example of effects of small size and high electronegativity is the presence of strong hydrogen bonding in H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O which is not found in H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17206,49,299,'paragraph',NULL,'The absence of d orbitals in oxygen limits its covalency to four and in practice, rarely exceeds two. On the other hand, in case of other elements of the group, the valence shells can be expanded and covalence exceeds four.','[{"s": "The absence of d orbitals in oxygen limits its covalency to four and in practice, rarely exceeds two. On the other hand, in case of other elements of the group, the valence shells can be expanded and covalence exceeds four.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17207,49,300,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Reactivity with hydrogen: All the elements of Group 16 form hydrides of the type H 2 E (E = O, S, Se, Te, Po). Some properties of hydrides are given in Table 7.7. Their acidic character increases from H 2 O to H 2 Te. The increase in acidic character can be explained in terms of decrease in bond enthalpy for the dissociation of H–E bond down the group. Owing to the decrease in enthalpy for the dissociation of H–E bond down the group, the thermal stability of hydrides also decreases from H 2 O to H 2 Po. All the hydrides except water possess reducing property and this character increases from H 2 S to H 2 Te.','[{"s": "(i) Reactivity with hydrogen: All the elements of Group 16 form hydrides of the type H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E (E = O, S, Se, Te, Po). Some properties of hydrides are given in Table 7.7.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Their acidic character increases from H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O to H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Te. The increase in acidic character can be explained in terms of decrease in bond enthalpy for the dissociation of H–E bond down the group.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Owing to the decrease in enthalpy for the dissociation of H–E bond down the group, the thermal stability of hydrides also decreases from H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O to H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Po. All the hydrides except water possess reducing property and this character increases from H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S to H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Te.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17208,49,301,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 7.7: Properties of Hydrides of Group 16 Elements 3','[{"s": "Table 7.7: Properties of Hydrides of Group 16 Elements", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]','marked','["214209", "214284", "214286"]',NULL),
(17209,49,302,'image',NULL,'7g',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image89.png'),
(17210,49,303,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Reactivity with oxygen: All these elements form oxides of the EO 2 and EO 3 types where E = S, Se, Te or Po. Ozone (O 3 ) and sulphur dioxide (SO 2 ) are gases while selenium dioxide (SeO 2 ) is solid. Reducing property of dioxide decreases from SO 2 to TeO 2 ; SO 2 is reducing while TeO 2 is an oxidising agent. Besides EO 2 type, sulphur, selenium and tellurium also form EO 3 type oxides (SO 3 , SeO 3 , TeO 3 ). Both types of oxides are acidic in nature.','[{"s": "(ii) Reactivity with oxygen: All these elements form oxides of the EO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and EO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "types where E = S, Se, Te or Po. Ozone (O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and sulphur dioxide (SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") are gases while selenium dioxide (SeO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is solid. Reducing property of dioxide decreases from SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to TeO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is reducing while TeO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is an oxidising agent. Besides EO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "type, sulphur, selenium and tellurium also form EO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "type oxides (SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", SeO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", TeO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Both types of oxides are acidic in nature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17211,49,304,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Reactivity towards the halogens: Elements of Group 16 form a large number of halides of the type, EX 6 , EX 4 and EX 2 where E is an element of the group and X is a halogen. The stability of the halides decreases in the order F – > Cl – > Br – > I – . Amongst hexahalides, hexafluorides are the only stable halides. All hexafluorides are gaseous in nature. They have octahedral structure. Sulphur hexafluoride, SF 6 is exceptionally stable for steric reasons.','[{"s": "(iii) Reactivity towards the halogens: Elements of Group 16 form a large number of halides of the type, EX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", EX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and EX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where E is an element of the group and X is a halogen. The stability of the halides decreases in the order F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Amongst hexahalides, hexafluorides are the only stable halides. All hexafluorides are gaseous in nature. They have octahedral structure. Sulphur hexafluoride, SF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is exceptionally stable for steric reasons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17212,49,305,'paragraph',NULL,'Amongst tetrafluorides, SF 4 is a gas, SeF 4 a liquid and TeF 4 a solid. These fluorides have sp 3 d hybridisation and thus, have trigonal bipyramidal structures in which one of the equatorial positions is occupied by a lone pair of electrons. This geometry is also regarded as see-saw geometry.','[{"s": "Amongst tetrafluorides, SF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a gas, SeF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a liquid and TeF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a solid. These fluorides have sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d hybridisation and thus, have trigonal bipyramidal structures in which one of the equatorial positions is occupied by a lone pair of electrons. This geometry is also regarded as see-saw geometry.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17213,49,306,'paragraph',NULL,'All elements except oxygen form dichlorides and dibromides. These dihalides are formed by sp 3 hybridisation and thus, have tetrahedral structure. The well known monohalides are dimeric in nature. Examples are S 2 F 2 , S 2 Cl 2 , S 2 Br 2 , Se 2 Cl 2 and Se 2 Br 2 . These dimeric halides undergo disproportionation as given below:','[{"s": "All elements except oxygen form dichlorides and dibromides. These dihalides are formed by sp", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hybridisation and thus, have tetrahedral structure. The well known monohalides are dimeric in nature. Examples are S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Se", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Se", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". These dimeric halides undergo disproportionation as given below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17214,49,307,'paragraph',NULL,'2Se 2 Cl 2 → SeCl 4 + 3Se','[{"s": "2Se", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ SeCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 3Se", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
