(17225,49,318,'paragraph',NULL,'Dioxygen can be obtained in the laboratory by the following ways:','[{"s": "Dioxygen can be obtained in the laboratory by the following ways:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17226,49,319,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) By heating oxygen containing salts such as chlorates, nitrates and permanganates.','[{"s": "(i) By heating oxygen containing salts such as chlorates, nitrates and permanganates.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17227,49,320,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image104.png'),
(17228,49,321,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) By the thermal decomposition of the oxides of metals low in the electrochemical series and higher oxides of some metals.','[{"s": "(ii) By the thermal decomposition of the oxides of metals low in the electrochemical series and higher oxides of some metals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17229,49,322,'paragraph',NULL,'2Ag 2 O(s) → 4Ag(s) + O 2 (g); 2Pb 3 O 4 (s) → 6PbO(s) + O 2 (g)','[{"s": "2Ag", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(s) → 4Ag(s) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g); 2Pb", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) → 6PbO(s) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17230,49,323,'paragraph',NULL,'2HgO(s) → 2Hg(l) + O 2 (g) ; 2PbO 2 (s) → 2PbO(s) + O 2 (g)','[{"s": "2HgO(s) → 2Hg(l) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) ; 2PbO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) → 2PbO(s) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17231,49,324,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Hydrogen peroxide is readily decomposed into water and dioxygen by catalysts such as finely divided metals and manganese dioxide.','[{"s": "(iii) Hydrogen peroxide is readily decomposed into water and dioxygen by catalysts such as finely divided metals and manganese dioxide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17232,49,325,'paragraph',NULL,'2H 2 O 2 (aq) → 2H 2 O(1) + O 2 (g)','[{"s": "2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) → 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(1) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17233,49,326,'paragraph',NULL,'On large scale it can be prepared from water or air. Electrolysis of water leads to the release of hydrogen at the cathode and oxygen at the anode.','[{"s": "On large scale it can be prepared from water or air. Electrolysis of water leads to the release of hydrogen at the cathode and oxygen at the anode.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17234,49,327,'paragraph',NULL,'Industrially, dioxygen is obtained from air by first removing carbon dioxide and water vapour and then, the remaining gases are liquefied and fractionally distilled to give dinitrogen and dioxygen.','[{"s": "Industrially, dioxygen is obtained from air by first removing carbon dioxide and water vapour and then, the remaining gases are liquefied and fractionally distilled to give dinitrogen and dioxygen.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17235,49,328,'paragraph',NULL,'Properties','[{"s": "Properties", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17877,50,232,'paragraph',NULL,'Cl 2 (g) + 2KBr (aq) → 2 KCl (aq) + Br 2 (l) (8.41)','[{"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 2KBr (aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 KCl (aq) + Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(l) (8.41)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17236,49,329,'paragraph',NULL,'Dioxygen is a colourless and odourless gas. Its solubility in water is to the extent of 3.08 cm 3 in 100 cm 3 water at 293 K which is just sufficient for the vital support of marine and aquatic life. It liquefies at 90 K and freezes at 55 K. Oxygen atom has three stable isotopes: 16 O, 17 O and 18 O. Molecular oxygen, O 2 is unique in being paramagnetic inspite of having even number of electrons (see Class XI Chemistry Book, Unit 4).','[{"s": "Dioxygen is a colourless and odourless gas. Its solubility in water is to the extent of 3.08 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in 100 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "water at 293 K which is just sufficient for the vital support of marine and aquatic life. It liquefies at 90 K and freezes at 55 K. Oxygen atom has three stable isotopes:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Molecular oxygen, O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is unique in being paramagnetic inspite of having even number of electrons (see Class XI Chemistry Book, Unit 4).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17237,49,330,'paragraph',NULL,'Dioxygen directly reacts with nearly all metals and non-metals except some metals ( e.g., Au, Pt) and some noble gases. Its combination with other elements is often strongly exothermic which helps in sustaining the reaction. However, to initiate the reaction, some external heating is required as bond dissociation enthalpy of oxgyen-oxygen double bond is high (493.4 kJ mol –1 ).','[{"s": "Dioxygen directly reacts with nearly all metals and non-metals except some metals ( e.g., Au, Pt) and some noble gases. Its combination with other elements is often strongly exothermic which helps in sustaining the reaction. However, to initiate the reaction, some external heating is required as bond dissociation enthalpy of oxgyen-oxygen double bond is high (493.4 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17238,49,331,'paragraph',NULL,'Some of the reactions of dioxygen with metals, non-metals and other compounds are given below:','[{"s": "Some of the reactions of dioxygen with metals, non-metals and other compounds are given below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
