(17255,49,348,'heading',3,'7.12 SIMPLE OXIDES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17256,49,349,'paragraph',NULL,'A binary compound of oxygen with another element is called oxide. As already stated, oxygen reacts with most of the elements of the periodic table to form oxides. In many cases one element forms two or more oxides. The oxides vary widely in their nature and properties.','[{"s": "A binary compound of oxygen with another element is called oxide. As already stated, oxygen reacts with most of the elements of the periodic table to form oxides. In many cases one element forms two or more oxides. The oxides vary widely in their nature and properties.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17878,50,233,'paragraph',NULL,'0 +1–1 +1 –1 0','[{"s": "0 +1–1 +1 –1 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17879,50,234,'paragraph',NULL,'Cl 2 (g) + 2KI (aq) → 2 KCl (aq) + I 2 (s) (8.42)','[{"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 2KI (aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 KCl (aq) + I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) (8.42)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17880,50,235,'paragraph',NULL,'As Br 2 and I 2 are coloured and dissolve in CCl 4 , can easily be identified from the colour of the solution. The above reactions can be written in ionic form as:','[{"s": "As Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are coloured and dissolve in CCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", can easily be identified from the colour of the solution. The above reactions can be written in ionic form as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17257,49,350,'paragraph',NULL,'Oxides can be simple (e.g., MgO, Al 2 O 3 ) or mixed (Pb 3 O 4 , Fe 3 O 4 ). Simple oxides can be classified on the basis of their acidic, basic or amphoteric character. An oxide that combines with water to give an acid is termed acidic oxide (e.g., SO 2 , Cl 2 O 7 , CO 2 , N 2 O 5 ). For example, SO 2 combines with water to give H 2 SO 3 , an acid.','[{"s": "Oxides can be simple (e.g., MgO, Al", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") or mixed (Pb", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Simple oxides can be classified on the basis of their acidic, basic or amphoteric character. An oxide that combines with water to give an acid is termed acidic oxide (e.g., SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). For example, SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "combines with water to give H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", an acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17258,49,351,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image62.png'),
(17259,49,352,'paragraph',NULL,'As a general rule, only non-metal oxides are acidic but oxides of some metals in high oxidation state also have acidic character (e.g., Mn 2 O 7 , CrO 3 , V 2 O 5 ). The oxides which give a base with water are known as basic oxides (e.g., Na 2 O, CaO, BaO). For example, CaO combines with water to give Ca(OH) 2 , a base.','[{"s": "As a general rule, only non-metal oxides are acidic but oxides of some metals in high oxidation state also have acidic character (e.g., Mn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", CrO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). The oxides which give a base with water are known as basic oxides (e.g., Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, CaO, BaO). For example, CaO combines with water to give Ca(OH)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", a base.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17260,49,353,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image114.png'),
(17261,49,354,'paragraph',NULL,'In general, metallic oxides are basic.','[{"s": "In general, metallic oxides are basic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17262,49,355,'paragraph',NULL,'Some metallic oxides exhibit a dual behaviour. They show characteristics of both acidic as well as basic oxides. Such oxides are known as amphoteric oxides. They react with acids as well as alkalies. For example, Al 2 O 3 reacts with acids as well as alkalies.','[{"s": "Some metallic oxides exhibit a dual behaviour. They show characteristics of both acidic as well as basic oxides. Such oxides are known as amphoteric oxides. They react with acids as well as alkalies. For example, Al", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reacts with acids as well as alkalies.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17263,49,356,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image14.png'),
(17264,49,357,'paragraph',NULL,'There are some oxides which are neither acidic nor basic. Such oxides are known as neutral oxides. Examples of neutral oxides are CO, NO and N 2 O.','[{"s": "There are some oxides which are neither acidic nor basic. Such oxides are known as neutral oxides. Examples of neutral oxides are CO, NO and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17265,49,358,'heading',3,'7.13 OZONE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17266,49,359,'paragraph',NULL,'Ozone is an allotropic form of oxygen. It is too reactive to remain for long in the atmosphere at sea level. At a height of about 20 kilometres, it is formed from atmospheric oxygen in the presence of sunlight. This ozone layer protects the earth’s surface from an excessive concentration of ultraviolet (UV) radiations.','[{"s": "Ozone is an allotropic form of oxygen. It is too reactive to remain for long in the atmosphere at sea level. At a height of about 20 kilometres, it is formed from atmospheric oxygen in the presence of sunlight. This ozone layer protects the earth’s surface from an excessive concentration of ultraviolet (UV) radiations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17267,49,360,'paragraph',NULL,'Preparation','[{"s": "Preparation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17268,49,361,'paragraph',NULL,'When a slow dry stream of oxygen is passed through a silent electrical discharge, conversion of oxygen to ozone (10%) occurs. The product is known as ozonised oxygen.','[{"s": "When a slow dry stream of oxygen is passed through a silent electrical discharge, conversion of oxygen to ozone (10%) occurs. The product is known as ozonised oxygen.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17269,49,362,'paragraph',NULL,'3O 2 → 2O 3 ∆H Ɵ (298 K) = +142 kJ mol –1','[{"s": "3O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 2O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ɵ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(298 K) = +142 kJ mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17270,49,363,'paragraph',NULL,'Since the formation of ozone from oxygen is an endothermic process, it is necessary to use a silent electrical discharge in its preparation to prevent its decomposition.','[{"s": "Since the formation of ozone from oxygen is an endothermic process, it is necessary to use a silent electrical discharge in its preparation to prevent its decomposition.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17271,49,364,'paragraph',NULL,'If concentrations of ozone greater than 10 per cent are required, a battery of ozonisers can be used, and pure ozone (b.p. 101.1K) can be condensed in a vessel surrounded by liquid oxygen.','[{"s": "If concentrations of ozone greater than 10 per cent are required, a battery of ozonisers can be used, and pure ozone (b.p. 101.1K) can be condensed in a vessel surrounded by liquid oxygen.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17272,49,365,'paragraph',NULL,'Properties','[{"s": "Properties", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17881,50,236,'paragraph',NULL,'0 –1 –1 0','[{"s": "0 –1 –1 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28997,71,130,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 13.3 Exponential decay of a radioactive species. After a lapse of T 1/2 , population of the given species drops by a factor of 2.','[{"s": "Figure 13.3 Exponential decay of a radioactive species. After a lapse of T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", population of the given species drops by a factor of 2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17273,49,366,'paragraph',NULL,'Pure ozone is a pale blue gas, dark blue liquid and violet-black solid. Ozone has a characteristic smell and in small concentrations it is harmless. However, if the concentration rises above about 100 parts per million, breathing becomes uncomfortable resulting in headache and nausea.','[{"s": "Pure ozone is a pale blue gas, dark blue liquid and violet-black solid. Ozone has a characteristic smell and in small concentrations it is harmless. However, if the concentration rises above about 100 parts per million, breathing becomes uncomfortable resulting in headache and nausea.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17274,49,367,'paragraph',NULL,'Ozone is thermodynamically unstable with respect to oxygen since its decomposition into oxygen results in the liberation of heat (∆H is negative) and an increase in entropy (∆S is positive). These two effects reinforce each other, resulting in large negative Gibbs energy change (∆G) for its conversion into oxygen. It is not really surprising, therefore, high concentrations of ozone can be dangerously explosive.','[{"s": "Ozone is thermodynamically unstable with respect to oxygen since its decomposition into oxygen results in the liberation of heat (∆H is negative) and an increase in entropy (∆S is positive).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "These two effects reinforce each other, resulting in large negative Gibbs energy change (∆G) for its conversion into oxygen. It is not really surprising, therefore, high concentrations of ozone can be dangerously explosive.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["214295"]',NULL),
(17275,49,368,'paragraph',NULL,'Due to the ease with which it liberates atoms of nascent oxygen (O 3 → O 2 + O), it acts as a powerful oxidising agent. For example, it oxidises lead sulphide to lead sulphate and iodide ions to iodine.','[{"s": "Due to the ease with which it liberates atoms of nascent oxygen (O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ O), it acts as a powerful oxidising agent. For example, it oxidises lead sulphide to lead sulphate and iodide ions to iodine.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17276,49,369,'paragraph',NULL,'PbS(s) + 4O 3 (g) → PbSO 4 (s) + 4O 2 (g)','[{"s": "PbS(s) + 4O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → PbSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 4O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17277,49,370,'paragraph',NULL,'2I – (aq) + H 2 O(l) + O 3 (g) → 2OH – (aq) + I 2 (s) + O 2 (g)','[{"s": "2I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → 2OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17278,49,371,'paragraph',NULL,'When ozone reacts with an excess of potassium iodide solution buffered with a borate buffer (pH 9.2), iodine is liberated which can be titrated against a standard solution of sodium thiosulphate. This is a quantitative method for estimating O 3 gas.','[{"s": "When ozone reacts with an excess of potassium iodide solution buffered with a borate buffer (pH 9.2), iodine is liberated which can be titrated against a standard solution of sodium thiosulphate. This is a quantitative method for estimating O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gas.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17279,49,372,'paragraph',NULL,'Experiments have shown that nitrogen oxides (particularly nitrogen monoxide) combine very rapidly with ozone and there is, thus, the possibility that nitrogen oxides emitted from the exhaust systems of supersonic jet aeroplanes might be slowly depleting the concentration of the ozone layer in the upper atmosphere.','[{"s": "Experiments have shown that nitrogen oxides (particularly nitrogen monoxide) combine very rapidly with ozone and there is, thus, the possibility that nitrogen oxides emitted from the exhaust systems of supersonic jet aeroplanes might be slowly depleting the concentration of the ozone layer in the upper atmosphere.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17280,49,373,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image5.png'),
(17281,49,374,'paragraph',NULL,'Another threat to this ozone layer is probably posed by the use of freons which are used in aerosol sprays and as refrigerants.','[{"s": "Another threat to this ozone layer is probably posed by the use of freons which are used in aerosol sprays and as refrigerants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17282,49,375,'paragraph',NULL,'The two oxygen-oxygen bond lengths in the ozone molecule are identical (128 pm) and the molecule is angular as expected with a bond angle of about 117 o . It is a resonance hybrid of two main forms:','[{"s": "The two oxygen-oxygen bond lengths in the ozone molecule are identical (128 pm) and the molecule is angular as expected with a bond angle of about 117", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is a resonance hybrid of two main forms:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17283,49,376,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image106.png'),
(17284,49,377,'paragraph',NULL,'Uses : It is used as a germicide, disinfectant and for sterilising water. It is also used for bleaching oils, ivory, flour, starch, etc. It acts as an oxidising agent in the manufacture of potassium permanganate.','[{"s": "Uses", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": It is used as a germicide, disinfectant and for sterilising water. It is also used for bleaching oils, ivory, flour, starch, etc. It acts as an oxidising agent in the manufacture of potassium permanganate.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17285,49,378,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17286,49,379,'paragraph',NULL,'7.18 Why does O 3 act as a powerful oxidising agent?','[{"s": "7.18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Why does O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "act as a powerful oxidising agent?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17287,49,380,'paragraph',NULL,'7.19 How is O 3 estimated quantitatively?','[{"s": "7.19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "How is O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "estimated quantitatively?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17288,49,381,'heading',3,'7.14 SULPHUR — ALLOTROPIC FORMS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17289,49,382,'paragraph',NULL,'Sulphur forms numerous allotropes of which the yellow rhombic (α-sulphur) and monoclinic (β -sulphur) forms are the most important. The stable form at room temperature is rhombic sulphur, which transforms to monoclinic sulphur when heated above 369 K.','[{"s": "Sulphur forms numerous allotropes of which the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "yellow rhombic", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(α-sulphur) and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "monoclinic", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(β -sulphur) forms are the most important. The stable form at room temperature is rhombic sulphur, which transforms to monoclinic sulphur when heated above 369 K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17290,49,383,'paragraph',NULL,'Rhombic sulphur (α-sulphur)','[{"s": "Rhombic sulphur (α-sulphur)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17291,49,384,'paragraph',NULL,'This allotrope is yellow in colour, m.p. 385.8 K and specific gravity 2.06. Rhombic sulphur crystals are formed on evaporating the solution of roll sulphur in CS 2 . It is insoluble in water but dissolves to some extent in benzene, alcohol and ether. It is readily soluble in CS 2 .','[{"s": "This allotrope is yellow in colour, m.p. 385.8 K and specific gravity 2.06. Rhombic sulphur crystals are formed on evaporating the solution of roll sulphur in CS", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is insoluble in water but dissolves to some extent in benzene, alcohol and ether. It is readily soluble in CS", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17292,49,385,'paragraph',NULL,'Monoclinic sulphur (β-sulphur)','[{"s": "Monoclinic sulphur (β-sulphur)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17293,49,386,'paragraph',NULL,'Its m.p. is 393 K and specific gravity 1.98. It is soluble in CS 2 . This form of sulphur is prepared by melting rhombic sulphur in a dish and cooling, till crust is formed. Two holes are made in the crust and the remaining liquid poured out. On removing the crust, colourless needle shaped crystals of β-sulphur are formed. It is stable above 369 K and transforms into α-sulphur below it. Conversely, α-sulphur is stable below 369 K and transforms into β-sulphur above this. At 369 K both the forms are stable. This temperature is called transition temperature.','[{"s": "Its m.p. is 393 K and specific gravity 1.98. It is soluble in CS", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This form of sulphur is prepared by melting rhombic sulphur in a dish and cooling, till crust is formed. Two holes are made in the crust and the remaining liquid poured out. On removing the crust, colourless needle shaped crystals of β-sulphur are formed. It is stable above 369 K and transforms into α-sulphur below it. Conversely, α-sulphur is stable below 369 K and transforms into β-sulphur above this. At 369 K both the forms are stable. This temperature is called transition temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17294,49,387,'paragraph',NULL,'Both rhombic and monoclinic sulphur have S 8 molecules. These S 8 molecules are packed to give different crystal structures. The S 8 ring in both the forms is puckered and has a crown shape. The molecular dimensions are given in Fig. 7.5(a).','[{"s": "Both rhombic and monoclinic sulphur have S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules. These S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules are packed to give different crystal structures. The S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ring in both the forms is puckered and has a crown shape. The molecular dimensions are given in Fig. 7.5(a).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17295,49,388,'paragraph',NULL,'P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image17.png (a) (b) P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image85.png','[{"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image17.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(a) (b)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image85.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17296,49,389,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.5: The structures of (a) S 8 ring in rhombic sulphur and (b) S 6 form','[{"s": "Fig. 7.5: The structures of (a) S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ring in rhombic sulphur and (b) S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "form", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17297,49,390,'paragraph',NULL,'Several other modifications of sulphur containing 6-20 sulphur atoms per ring have been synthesised in the last two decades. In cyclo-S 6 , the ring adopts the chair form and the molecular dimensions are as shown in Fig. 7.5 (b). At elevated temperatures (~1000 K), S 2 is the dominant species and is paramagnetic like O 2 .','[{"s": "Several other modifications of sulphur containing 6-20 sulphur atoms per ring have been synthesised in the last two decades. In cyclo-S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the ring adopts the chair form and the molecular dimensions are as shown in Fig. 7.5 (b). At elevated temperatures (~1000 K), S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the dominant species and is paramagnetic like O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17298,49,391,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.12','[{"s": "Example 7.12", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17299,49,392,'paragraph',NULL,'Which form of sulphur shows paramagnetic behaviour?','[{"s": "Which form of sulphur shows paramagnetic behaviour?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17300,49,393,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17301,49,394,'paragraph',NULL,'In vapour state sulphur partly exists as S 2 molecule which has two unpaired electrons in the antibonding π* orbitals like O 2 and, hence, exhibits paramagnetism.','[{"s": "In vapour state sulphur partly exists as S", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "molecule which has two unpaired electrons in the antibonding π* orbitals like O", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and, hence, exhibits paramagnetism.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["214294"]',NULL),
(17302,49,395,'heading',3,'7.15 SULPHUR DIOXIDE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17303,49,396,'paragraph',NULL,'Preparation','[{"s": "Preparation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17304,49,397,'paragraph',NULL,'Sulphur dioxide is formed together with a little (6-8%) sulphur trioxide when sulphur is burnt in air or oxygen:','[{"s": "Sulphur dioxide is formed together with a little (6-8%) sulphur trioxide when sulphur is burnt in air or oxygen:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17305,49,398,'paragraph',NULL,'S(s) + O 2 (g) → SO 2 (g)','[{"s": "S(s) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17306,49,399,'paragraph',NULL,'In the laboratory it is readily generated by treating a sulphite with dilute sulphuric acid.','[{"s": "In the laboratory it is readily generated by treating a sulphite with dilute sulphuric acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17307,49,400,'paragraph',NULL,'SO 3 2- (aq) + 2H + (aq) → H 2 O(l) + SO 2 (g)','[{"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) → H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) + SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17308,49,401,'paragraph',NULL,'Industrially, it is produced as a by-product of the roasting of sulphide ores.','[{"s": "Industrially, it is produced as a by-product of the roasting of sulphide ores.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17309,49,402,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image80.png'),
(17310,49,403,'paragraph',NULL,'The gas after drying is liquefied under pressure and stored in steel cylinders.','[{"s": "The gas after drying is liquefied under pressure and stored in steel cylinders.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17311,49,404,'paragraph',NULL,'Properties','[{"s": "Properties", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17312,49,405,'paragraph',NULL,'Sulphur dioxide is a colourless gas with pungent smell and is highly soluble in water. It liquefies at room temperature under a pressure of two atmospheres and boils at 263 K.','[{"s": "Sulphur dioxide is a colourless gas with pungent smell and is highly soluble in water. It liquefies at room temperature under a pressure of two atmospheres and boils at 263 K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17313,49,406,'paragraph',NULL,'Sulphur dioxide, when passed through water, forms a solution of sulphurous acid.','[{"s": "Sulphur dioxide, when passed through water, forms a solution of sulphurous acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17314,49,407,'image',NULL,'7eq6',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image58.png'),
(17315,49,408,'paragraph',NULL,'It reacts readily with sodium hydroxide solution, forming sodium sulphite, which then reacts with more sulphur dioxide to form sodium hydrogen sulphite.','[{"s": "It reacts readily with sodium hydroxide solution, forming sodium sulphite, which then reacts with more sulphur dioxide to form sodium hydrogen sulphite.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17316,49,409,'paragraph',NULL,'2NaOH + SO 2 → Na 2 SO 3 + H 2 O','[{"s": "2NaOH + SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17317,49,410,'paragraph',NULL,'Na 2 SO 3 + H 2 O + SO 2 → 2NaHSO 3','[{"s": "Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O + SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 2NaHSO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17318,49,411,'paragraph',NULL,'In its reaction with water and alkalies, the behaviour of sulphur dioxide is very similar to that of carbon dioxide.','[{"s": "In its reaction with water and alkalies, the behaviour of sulphur dioxide is very similar to that of carbon dioxide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17319,49,412,'paragraph',NULL,'Sulphur dioxide reacts with chlorine in the presence of charcoal (which acts as a catalyst) to give sulphuryl chloride, SO 2 Cl 2 . It is oxidised to sulphur trioxide by oxygen in the presence of vanadium(V) oxide catalyst.','[{"s": "Sulphur dioxide reacts with chlorine in the presence of charcoal (which acts as a catalyst) to give sulphuryl chloride, SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is oxidised to sulphur trioxide by oxygen in the presence of vanadium(V) oxide catalyst.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17320,49,413,'paragraph',NULL,'SO 2 (g) + Cl 2 (g) → SO 2 Cl 2 (l)','[{"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(l)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17321,49,414,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image8.png'),
(17322,49,415,'paragraph',NULL,'When moist, sulphur dioxide behaves as a reducing agent. For example, it converts iron(III) ions to iron(II) ions and decolourises acidified potassium permanganate(VII) solution; the latter reaction is a convenient test for the gas.','[{"s": "When moist, sulphur dioxide behaves as a reducing agent. For example, it converts iron(III) ions to iron(II) ions and decolourises acidified potassium permanganate(VII) solution; the latter reaction is a convenient test for the gas.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17323,49,416,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image124.png'),
(17324,49,417,'paragraph',NULL,'The molecule of SO 2 is angular. It is a resonance hybrid of the two canonical forms:','[{"s": "The molecule of SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is angular. It is a resonance hybrid of the two canonical forms:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17325,49,418,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/P_Block_Elements_Group_15_to_18-image9.png'),
(17326,49,419,'paragraph',NULL,'Uses : Sulphur dioxide is used (i) in refining petroleum and sugar (ii) in bleaching wool and silk and (iii) as an anti-chlor, disinfectant and preservative. Sulphuric acid, sodium hydrogen sulphite and calcium hydrogen sulphite (industrial chemicals) are manufactured from sulphur dioxide. Liquid SO 2 is used as a solvent to dissolve a number of organic and inorganic chemicals.','[{"s": "Uses", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Sulphur dioxide is used (i) in refining petroleum and sugar (ii) in bleaching wool and silk and (iii) as an anti-chlor, disinfectant and preservative. Sulphuric acid, sodium hydrogen sulphite and calcium hydrogen sulphite (industrial chemicals) are manufactured from sulphur dioxide. Liquid SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is used as a solvent to dissolve a number of organic and inorganic chemicals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17327,49,420,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17328,49,421,'paragraph',NULL,'7.20 What happens when sulphur dioxide is passed through an aqueous solution of Fe(III) salt?','[{"s": "7.20", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What happens when sulphur dioxide is passed through an aqueous solution of Fe(III) salt?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17329,49,422,'paragraph',NULL,'7.21 Comment on the nature of two S–O bonds formed in SO 2 molecule. Are the two S–O bonds in this molecule equal ?','[{"s": "7.21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Comment on the nature of two S–O bonds formed in SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule. Are the two S–O bonds in this molecule equal ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17330,49,423,'paragraph',NULL,'7.22 How is the presence of SO 2 detected ?','[{"s": "7.22", "t": "text"}, {"s": "How is the presence of SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "detected ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17331,49,424,'heading',3,'7.16 OXOACIDS OF SULPHUR',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17882,50,237,'paragraph',NULL,'Cl 2 (g) + 2Br – (aq) → 2Cl – (aq) + Br 2 (l) (8.41a)','[{"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 2Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(l) (8.41a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17332,49,425,'paragraph',NULL,'Sulphur forms a number of oxoacids such as H 2 SO 3 , H 2 S 2 O 3 , H 2 S 2 O 4 , H 2 S 2 O 5 , H 2 S x O 6 (x = 2 to 5), H 2 SO 4 , H 2 S 2 O 7 , H 2 SO 5 , H 2 S 2 O 8 . Some of these acids are unstable and cannot be isolated. They are known in aqueous solution or in the form of their salts. Structures of some important oxoacids are shown in Fig. 7.6.','[{"s": "Sulphur forms a number of oxoacids such as H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(x = 2 to 5), H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Some of these acids are unstable and cannot be isolated. They are known in aqueous solution or in the form of their salts. Structures of some important oxoacids are shown in Fig. 7.6.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
