(17634,49,727,'paragraph',NULL,'7.33 Balance the following equation: XeF 6 + H 2 O → XeO 2 F 2 + HF','[{"s": "7.33", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Balance the following equation: XeF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O → XeO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ HF", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17635,49,728,'paragraph',NULL,'7.34 Why has it been difficult to study the chemistry of radon?','[{"s": "7.34", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Why has it been difficult to study the chemistry of radon?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17636,49,729,'heading',3,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17637,49,730,'paragraph',NULL,'Groups 13 to 18 of the periodic table consist of p-block elements with their valence shell electronic configuration ns 2 np 1–6 . Groups 13 and 14 were dealt with in Class XI. In this Unit remaining groups of the p-block have been discussed.','[{"s": "Groups 13 to 18 of the periodic table consist of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p-block elements", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with their valence shell electronic configuration ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "np", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1–6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Groups 13 and 14 were dealt with in Class XI. In this Unit remaining groups of the p-block have been discussed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17638,49,731,'paragraph',NULL,'Group 15 consists of five elements namely, N, P, As, Sb and Bi which have general electronic configuration ns 2 np 3 . Nitrogen differs from other elements of this group due to small size, formation of p π –p π multiple bonds with itself and with highly electronegative atom like O or C and non-availability of d orbitals to expand its valence shell. Elements of group 15 show gradation in properties. They react with oxygen, hydrogen and halogens. They exhibit two important oxidation states, + 3 and + 5 but +3 oxidation is favoured by heavier elements due to ‘inert pair effect’.','[{"s": "Group 15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "consists of five elements namely, N, P, As, Sb and Bi which have general electronic configuration ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "np", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Nitrogen differs from other elements of this group due to small size, formation of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "multiple bonds", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with itself and with highly electronegative atom like O or C and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "non-availability of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbitals", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to expand its valence shell. Elements of group 15 show gradation in properties. They react with oxygen, hydrogen and halogens. They exhibit two important oxidation states, + 3 and + 5 but +3 oxidation is favoured by heavier elements due to ‘inert pair effect’.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17639,49,732,'paragraph',NULL,'Dinitrogen can be prepared in laboratory as well as on industrial scale. It forms oxides in various oxidation states as N 2 O, NO, N 2 O 3 , NO 2 , N 2 O 4 and N 2 O 5 . These oxides have resonating structures and have multiple bonds. Ammonia can be prepared on large scale by Haber’s process . HNO 3 is an important industrial chemical. It is a strong monobasic acid and is a powerful oxidising agent. Metals and non-metals react with HNO 3 under different conditions to give NO or NO 2 .','[{"s": "Dinitrogen can be prepared in laboratory as well as on industrial scale. It forms oxides in various oxidation states as N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, NO, N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". These oxides have", "t": "text"}, {"s": "resonating structures", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and have multiple bonds.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ammonia can be prepared on large scale by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Haber’s process", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is an important industrial chemical. It is a strong monobasic acid and is a powerful oxidising agent. Metals and non-metals react with HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "under different conditions to give NO or NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17640,49,733,'paragraph',NULL,'Phosphorus exists as P 4 in elemental form. It exists in several allotropic forms . It forms hydride, PH 3 which is a highly poisonous gas. It forms two types of halides as PX 3 and PX 5 . PCl 3 is prepared by the reaction of white phosphorus with dry chlorine while PCl 5 is prepared by the reaction of phosphorus with SO 2 Cl 2 . Phosphorus forms a number of oxoacids. Depending upon the number of P–OH groups, their basicity varies. The oxoacids which have P–H bonds are good reducing agents.','[{"s": "Phosphorus exists as P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in elemental form. It exists in several", "t": "text"}, {"s": "allotropic forms", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It forms hydride, PH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which is a highly poisonous gas. It forms two types of halides as PX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and PX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". PCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is prepared by the reaction of white phosphorus with dry chlorine while PCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is prepared by the reaction of phosphorus with SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Phosphorus forms a number of oxoacids. Depending upon the number of P–OH groups, their basicity varies. The oxoacids which have P–H bonds are good reducing agents.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17888,50,243,'paragraph',NULL,'The halogen displacement reactions have a direct industrial application. The recovery of halogens from their halides requires an oxidation process, which is represented by:','[{"s": "The halogen displacement reactions have a direct industrial application. The recovery of halogens from their halides requires an oxidation process, which is represented by:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17889,50,244,'paragraph',NULL,'2X – → X 2 + 2e – (8.44)','[{"s": "2X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(8.44)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17641,49,734,'paragraph',NULL,'The Group 16 elements have general electronic configuration ns 2 np 4 . They show maximum oxidation state, +6. Gradation in physical and chemical properties is observed in the group 16 elements. In laboratory, dioxygen is prepared by heating KClO 3 in presence of MnO 2 . It forms a number of oxides with metals. Allotropic form of oxygen is O 3 which is a highly oxidising agent. Sulphur forms a number of allotropes. Of these, α– and β– forms of sulphur are the most important. Sulphur combines with oxygen to give oxides such as SO 2 and SO 3 . SO 2 is prepared by the direct union of sulphur with oxygen. SO 2 is used in the manufacture of H 2 SO 4 . Sulphur forms a number of oxoacids. Amongst them, the most important is H 2 SO 4 . It is prepared by contact process . It is a dehydrating and oxidising agent. It is used in the manufacture of several compounds.','[{"s": "The Group 16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "elements have general electronic configuration ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "np", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". They show maximum oxidation state, +6. Gradation in physical and chemical properties is observed in the group 16 elements. In laboratory, dioxygen is prepared by heating KClO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in presence of MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It forms a number of oxides with metals. Allotropic form of oxygen is O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which is a highly oxidising agent. Sulphur forms a number of allotropes. Of these, α– and β– forms of sulphur are the most important. Sulphur combines with oxygen to give oxides such as SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is prepared by the direct union of sulphur with oxygen. SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is used in the manufacture of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Sulphur forms a number of oxoacids. Amongst them, the most important is H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is prepared by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "contact process", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is a dehydrating and oxidising agent. It is used in the manufacture of several compounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17642,49,735,'paragraph',NULL,'Group 17 of the periodic table consists of the following elements F, Cl, Br, I and At.These elements are extremely reactive and as such they are found in the combined state only. The common oxidation state of these elements is –1. However, highest oxidation state can be +7. They show regular gradation in physical and chemical properties. They form oxides, hydrogen halides, interhalogen compounds and oxoacids. Chlorine is conveniently obtained by the reaction of HCl with KMnO 4 . HCl is prepared by heating NaCl with concentrated H 2 SO 4 . Halogens combine with one another to form interhalogen compounds of the type XX 1 n (n = 1, 3, 5, 7) where X 1 is lighter than X. A number of oxoacids of halogens are known. In the structures of these oxoacids, halogen is the central atom which is bonded in each case with one OH bond as X–OH. In some cases X = 0 bonds are also found.','[{"s": "Group 17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the periodic table consists of the following elements F, Cl, Br, I and At.These elements are extremely reactive and as such they are found in the combined state only. The common oxidation state of these elements is –1. However, highest oxidation state can be +7. They show regular gradation in physical and chemical properties. They form oxides, hydrogen halides, interhalogen compounds and oxoacids. Chlorine is conveniently obtained by the reaction of HCl with KMnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". HCl is prepared by heating NaCl with concentrated H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Halogens combine with one another to form", "t": "text"}, {"s": "interhalogen compounds", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the type XX", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(n = 1, 3, 5, 7) where X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is lighter than X. A number of oxoacids of halogens are known. In the structures of these oxoacids, halogen is the central atom which is bonded in each case with one OH bond as X–OH. In some cases X = 0 bonds are also found.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17643,49,736,'paragraph',NULL,'Group 18 of the periodic table consists of noble gases . They have ns 2 np 6 valence shell electronic configuration except He which has 1s 2 . All the gases except Rn occur in atmosphere. Rn is obtained as the decay product of 226 Ra.','[{"s": "Group 18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the periodic table consists of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "noble gases", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". They have ns", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "np", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "valence shell electronic configuration except He which has 1s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". All the gases except Rn occur in atmosphere. Rn is obtained as the decay product of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "226", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ra.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17644,49,737,'paragraph',NULL,'Due to complete octet of outermost shell, they have less tendency to form compounds. The best characterised compounds are those of xenon with fluorine and oxygen only under certain conditions. These gases have several uses. Argon is used to provide inert atmosphere, helium is used in filling balloons for meteorological observations, neon is used in discharge tubes and fluorescent bulbs.','[{"s": "Due to complete octet of outermost shell, they have less tendency to form compounds. The best characterised compounds are those of xenon with fluorine and oxygen only under certain conditions. These gases have several uses. Argon is used to provide inert atmosphere, helium is used in filling balloons for meteorological observations, neon is used in discharge tubes and fluorescent bulbs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17645,50,0,'paragraph',NULL,'Where there is oxidation, there is always reduction – Chemistry is essentially a study of redox systems . ”','[{"s": "Where there is oxidation, there is always reduction – Chemistry is essentially a study of redox systems", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "”", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17646,50,1,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemistry deals with varieties of matter and change of one kind of matter into the other. Transformation of matter from one kind into another occurs through the various types of reactions. One important category of such reactions is Redox Reactions. A number of phenomena, both physical as well as biological, are concerned with redox reactions. These reactions find extensive use in pharmaceutical, biological, industrial, metallurgical and agricultural areas. The importance of these reactions is apparent from the fact that burning of different types of fuels for obtaining energy for domestic, transport and other commercial purposes, electrochemical processes for extraction of highly reactive metals and non-metals, manufacturing of chemical compounds like caustic soda, operation of dry and wet batteries and corrosion of metals fall within the purview of redox processes. Of late, environmental issues like Hydrogen Economy (use of liquid hydrogen as fuel) and development of ‘Ozone Hole’ have started figuring under redox phenomenon.','[{"s": "Chemistry deals with varieties of matter and change of one kind of matter into the other. Transformation of matter from one kind into another occurs through the various types of reactions. One important category of such reactions is Redox Reactions. A number of phenomena, both physical as well as biological, are concerned with redox reactions. These reactions find extensive use in pharmaceutical, biological, industrial, metallurgical and agricultural areas. The importance of these reactions is apparent from the fact that burning of different types of fuels for obtaining energy for domestic, transport and other commercial purposes, electrochemical processes for extraction of highly reactive metals and non-metals, manufacturing of chemical compounds like caustic soda, operation of dry and wet batteries and corrosion of metals fall within the purview of redox processes. Of late, environmental issues like Hydrogen Economy (use of liquid hydrogen as fuel) and development of ‘Ozone Hole’ have started figuring under redox phenomenon.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17647,50,2,'heading',3,'8.1 CLASSICAL IDEA OF REDOX REACTIONS – OXIDATION AND REDUCTION REACTIONS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17648,50,3,'paragraph',NULL,'2 Mg (s) + O 2 (g) → 2 MgO (s) (8.1)','[{"s": "2 Mg (s) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 MgO (s) (8.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17649,50,4,'paragraph',NULL,'S (s) + O 2 (g) → SO 2 (g) (8.2)','[{"s": "S (s) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) (8.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29000,71,133,'paragraph',NULL,'R = λ N 0 e −λt','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−λt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17650,50,5,'paragraph',NULL,'In reactions (8.1) and (8.2), the elements magnesium and sulphur are oxidised on account of addition of oxygen to them. Similarly, methane is oxidised owing to the addition of oxygen to it.','[{"s": "In reactions (8.1) and (8.2), the elements magnesium and sulphur are oxidised on account of addition of oxygen to them. Similarly, methane is oxidised owing to the addition of oxygen to it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17651,50,6,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 4 (g) + 2O 2 (g) → CO 2 (g) + 2H 2 O (l) (8.3)','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 2O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l) (8.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17652,50,7,'paragraph',NULL,'A careful examination of reaction (8.3) in which hydrogen has been replaced by oxygen prompted chemists to reinterpret oxidation in terms of removal of hydrogen from it and, therefore, the scope of term oxidation was broadened to include the removal of hydrogen from a substance. The following illustration is another reaction where removal of hydrogen can also be cited as an oxidation reaction.','[{"s": "A careful examination of reaction (8.3) in which hydrogen has been replaced by oxygen prompted chemists to reinterpret oxidation in terms of removal of hydrogen from it and, therefore, the scope of term oxidation was broadened to include the removal of hydrogen from a substance. The following illustration is another reaction where removal of hydrogen can also be cited as an oxidation reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17653,50,8,'paragraph',NULL,'2 H 2 S(g) + O 2 (g) → 2 S (s) + 2 H 2 O (l) (8.4)','[{"s": "2 H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S(g) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 S (s) + 2 H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l) (8.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17654,50,9,'paragraph',NULL,'As knowledge of chemists grew, it was natural to extend the term oxidation for reactions similar to (8.1 to 8.4), which do not involve oxygen but other electronegative elements. The oxidation of magnesium with fluorine, chlorine and sulphur etc. occurs according to the following reactions :','[{"s": "As knowledge of chemists grew, it was natural to extend the term oxidation for reactions similar to (8.1 to 8.4), which do not involve oxygen but other electronegative elements. The oxidation of magnesium with fluorine, chlorine and sulphur etc. occurs according to the following reactions :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17655,50,10,'paragraph',NULL,'Mg (s) + F 2 (g) → MgF 2 (s) (8.5)','[{"s": "Mg (s) + F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MgF", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) (8.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17656,50,11,'paragraph',NULL,'Mg (s) + Cl 2 (g) → MgCl 2 (s) (8.6)','[{"s": "Mg (s) + Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MgCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) (8.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
