(17706,50,61,'paragraph',NULL,'This splitting of the reaction under examination into two half reactions automatically reveals that here sodium is oxidised and hydrogen is reduced, therefore, the complete reaction is a redox change.','[{"s": "This splitting of the reaction under examination into two half reactions automatically reveals that here sodium is oxidised and hydrogen is reduced, therefore, the complete reaction is a redox change.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17707,50,62,'paragraph',NULL,'8.2.1 Competitive Electron Transfer Reactions','[{"s": "8.2.1 Competitive Electron Transfer Reactions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17708,50,63,'paragraph',NULL,'Place a strip of metallic zinc in an aqueous solution of copper nitrate as shown in Fig. 8.1, for about one hour. You may notice that the strip becomes coated with reddish metallic copper and the blue colour of the solution disappears. Formation of Zn 2+ ions among the products can easily be judged when the blue colour of the solution due to Cu 2+ has disappeared. If hydrogen sulphide gas is passed through the colourless solution containing Zn 2+ ions, appearance of white zinc sulphide, ZnS can be seen on making the solution alkaline with ammonia.','[{"s": "Place a strip of metallic zinc in an aqueous solution of copper nitrate as shown in Fig. 8.1, for about one hour. You may notice that the strip becomes coated with reddish metallic copper and the blue colour of the solution disappears. Formation of Zn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions among the products can easily be judged when the blue colour of the solution due to Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has disappeared. If hydrogen sulphide gas is passed through the colourless solution containing Zn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions, appearance of white zinc sulphide, ZnS can be seen on making the solution alkaline with ammonia.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17709,50,64,'paragraph',NULL,'The reaction between metallic zinc and the aqueous solution of copper nitrate is :','[{"s": "The reaction between metallic zinc and the aqueous solution of copper nitrate is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17710,50,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Zn(s) + Cu 2+ (aq) → Zn 2+ (aq) + Cu(s) (8.15)','[{"s": "Zn(s) + Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Zn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + Cu(s) (8.15)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17711,50,66,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Redox_Reaction-image6.png'),
(17712,50,67,'paragraph',NULL,'In reaction (8.15), zinc has lost electrons to form Zn 2+ and, therefore, zinc is oxidised. Evidently, now if zinc is oxidised, releasing electrons, something must be reduced, accepting the electrons lost by zinc. Copper ion is reduced by gaining electrons from the zinc.','[{"s": "In reaction (8.15), zinc has lost electrons to form Zn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and, therefore, zinc is oxidised. Evidently, now if zinc is oxidised, releasing electrons, something must be reduced, accepting the electrons lost by zinc. Copper ion is reduced by gaining electrons from the zinc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17713,50,68,'paragraph',NULL,'Reaction (8.15) may be rewritten as :','[{"s": "Reaction (8.15) may be rewritten as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17714,50,69,'paragraph',NULL,'At this stage we may investigate the state of equilibrium for the reaction represented by equation (8.15). For this purpose, let us place a strip of metallic copper in a zinc sulphate solution. No visible reaction is noticed and attempt to detect the presence of Cu 2+ ions by passing H 2 S gas through the solution to produce the black colour of cupric sulphide, CuS, does not succeed. Cupric sulphide has such a low solubility that this is an extremely sensitive test; yet the amount of Cu 2+ formed cannot be detected. We thus conclude that the state of equilibrium for the reaction (8.15) greatly favours the products over the reactants.','[{"s": "At this stage we may investigate the state of equilibrium for the reaction represented by equation (8.15). For this purpose, let us place a strip of metallic copper in a zinc sulphate solution. No visible reaction is noticed and attempt to detect the presence of Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions by passing H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S gas through the solution to produce the black colour of cupric sulphide, CuS, does not succeed. Cupric sulphide has such a low solubility that this is an extremely sensitive test; yet the amount of Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "formed cannot be detected. We thus conclude that the state of equilibrium for the reaction (8.15) greatly favours the products over the reactants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17715,50,70,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Redox_Reaction-image10.png'),
