(17870,50,225,'paragraph',NULL,'Fe(s) + 2HCl(aq) → FeCl 2 (aq) + H 2 (g) (8.39)','[{"s": "Fe(s) + 2HCl(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "FeCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) (8.39)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17871,50,226,'paragraph',NULL,'Reactions (8.37 to 8.39) are used to prepare dihydrogen gas in the laboratory. Here, the reactivity of metals is reflected in the rate of hydrogen gas evolution, which is the slowest for the least active metal Fe, and the fastest for the most reactive metal, Mg. Very less active metals, which may occur in the native state such as silver (Ag), and gold (Au) do not react even with hydrochloric acid.','[{"s": "Reactions (8.37 to 8.39) are used to prepare dihydrogen gas in the laboratory. Here, the reactivity of metals is reflected in the rate of hydrogen gas evolution, which is the slowest for the least active metal Fe, and the fastest for the most reactive metal, Mg. Very less active metals, which may occur in the native state such as silver (Ag), and gold (Au) do not react even with hydrochloric acid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17890,50,245,'paragraph',NULL,'here X denotes a halogen element. Whereas chemical means are available to oxidise Cl – , Br – and I – , as fluorine is the strongest oxidising agent; there is no way to convert F – ions to F 2 by chemical means. The only way to achieve F 2 from F – is to oxidise electrolytically, the details of which you will study at a later stage.','[{"s": "here X denotes a halogen element. Whereas chemical means are available to oxidise Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", as fluorine is the strongest oxidising agent; there is no way to convert F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions to F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by chemical means. The only way to achieve F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is to oxidise electrolytically, the details of which you will study at a later stage.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17891,50,246,'heading',3,'4. DISPROPORTIONATION REACTIONS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17892,50,247,'paragraph',NULL,'Disproportionation reactions are a special type of redox reactions. In a disproportionation reaction an element in one oxidation state is simultaneously oxidised and reduced. One of the reacting substances in a disproportionation reaction always contains an element that can exist in at least three oxidation states. The element in the form of reacting substance is in the intermediate oxidation state; and both higher and lower oxidation states of that element are formed in the reaction. The decomposition of hydrogen peroxide is a familiar example of the reaction, where oxygen experiences disproportionation.','[{"s": "Disproportionation reactions are a special type of redox reactions. In a disproportionation reaction an element in one oxidation state is simultaneously oxidised and reduced. One of the reacting substances in a disproportionation reaction always contains an element that can exist in at least three oxidation states. The element in the form of reacting substance is in the intermediate oxidation state; and both higher and lower oxidation states of that element are formed in the reaction. The decomposition of hydrogen peroxide is a familiar example of the reaction, where oxygen experiences disproportionation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17893,50,248,'paragraph',NULL,'+1 –1 +1 –2 0','[{"s": "+1 –1 +1 –2 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17894,50,249,'paragraph',NULL,'2H 2 O 2 (aq) → 2H 2 O(l) + O 2 (g) (8.45)','[{"s": "2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) (8.45)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17895,50,250,'paragraph',NULL,'Here the oxygen of peroxide, which is present in –1 state, is converted to zero oxidation state in O 2 and decreases to –2 oxidation state in H 2 O.','[{"s": "Here the oxygen of peroxide, which is present in –1 state, is converted to zero oxidation state in O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and decreases to –2 oxidation state in H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17896,50,251,'paragraph',NULL,'Phosphorous, sulphur and chlorine undergo disproportionation in the alkaline medium as shown below :','[{"s": "Phosphorous, sulphur and chlorine undergo disproportionation in the alkaline medium as shown below :", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["220835"]',NULL),
