(17953,50,308,'paragraph',NULL,'+4 –1 +2 0','[{"s": "+4 –1 +2 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17954,50,309,'paragraph',NULL,'PbO 2 + 4HCl → PbCl 2 + Cl 2 +2H 2 O (redox reaction)','[{"s": "PbO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 4HCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PbCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (redox reaction)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17955,50,310,'paragraph',NULL,'Since HNO 3 itself is an oxidising agent therefore, it is unlikely that the reaction may occur between PbO 2 and HNO 3 . However, the acid-base reaction occurs between PbO and HNO 3 as:','[{"s": "Since HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "itself is an oxidising agent therefore, it is unlikely that the reaction may occur between PbO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". However, the acid-base reaction occurs between PbO and HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17956,50,311,'paragraph',NULL,'2PbO + 4HNO 3 → 2Pb(NO 3 ) 2 + 2H 2 O','[{"s": "2PbO + 4HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2Pb(NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17957,50,312,'paragraph',NULL,'It is the passive nature of PbO 2 against HNO 3 that makes the reaction different from the one that follows with HCl.','[{"s": "It is the passive nature of PbO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "against HNO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "that makes the reaction different from the one that follows with HCl.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17958,50,313,'paragraph',NULL,'8.3.2 Balancing of Redox Reactions','[{"s": "8.3.2 Balancing of Redox Reactions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17959,50,314,'paragraph',NULL,'Two methods are used to balance chemical equations for redox processes. One of these methods is based on the change in the oxidation number of reducing agent and the oxidising agent and the other method is based on splitting the redox reaction into two half reactions — one involving oxidation and the other involving reduction. Both these methods are in use and the choice of their use rests with the individual using them.','[{"s": "Two methods are used to balance chemical equations for redox processes. One of these methods is based on the change in the oxidation number of reducing agent and the oxidising agent and the other method is based on splitting the redox reaction into two half reactions — one involving oxidation and the other involving reduction. Both these methods are in use and the choice of their use rests with the individual using them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17960,50,315,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Oxidation Number Method: In writing equations for oxidation-reduction reactions, just as for other reactions, the compositions and formulas must be known for the substances that react and for the products that are formed. The oxidation number method is now best illustrated in the following steps:','[{"s": "(a) Oxidation Number Method:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In writing equations for oxidation-reduction reactions, just as for other reactions, the compositions and formulas must be known for the substances that react and for the products that are formed. The oxidation number method is now best illustrated in the following steps:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17961,50,316,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 1: Write the correct formula for each reactant and product.','[{"s": "Step 1: Write the correct formula for each reactant and product.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17962,50,317,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 2: Identify atoms which undergo change in oxidation number in the reaction by assigning the oxidation number to all elements in the reaction.','[{"s": "Step 2: Identify atoms which undergo change in oxidation number in the reaction by assigning the oxidation number to all elements in the reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17963,50,318,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3: Calculate the increase or decrease in the oxidation number per atom and for the entire molecule/ion in which it occurs. If these are not equal then multiply by suitable number so that these become equal. (If you realise that two substances are reduced and nothing is oxidised or vice-versa, something is wrong. Either the formulas of reactants or products are wrong or the oxidation numbers have not been assigned properly).','[{"s": "Step 3: Calculate the increase or decrease in the oxidation number per atom and for the entire molecule/ion in which it occurs. If these are not equal then multiply by suitable number so that these become equal. (If you realise that two substances are reduced and nothing is oxidised or vice-versa, something is wrong. Either the formulas of reactants or products are wrong or the oxidation numbers have not been assigned properly).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17964,50,319,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 4: Ascertain the involvement of ions if the reaction is taking place in water, add H + or OH – ions to the expression on the appropriate side so that the total ionic charges of reactants and products are equal. If the reaction is carried out in acidic solution, use H+ ions in the equation; if in basic solution, use OH – ions.','[{"s": "Step 4: Ascertain the involvement of ions if the reaction is taking place in water, add H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions to the expression on the appropriate side so that the total ionic charges of reactants and products are equal. If the reaction is carried out in acidic solution, use H+ ions in the equation; if in basic solution, use OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17965,50,320,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 5: Make the numbers of hydrogen atoms in the expression on the two sides equal by adding water (H 2 O) molecules to the reactants or products. Now, also check the number of oxygen atoms. If there are the same number of oxygen atoms in the reactants and products, the equation then represents the balanced redox reaction.','[{"s": "Step 5: Make the numbers of hydrogen atoms in the expression on the two sides equal by adding water (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O) molecules to the reactants or products. Now, also check the number of oxygen atoms. If there are the same number of oxygen atoms in the reactants and products, the equation then represents the balanced redox reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17966,50,321,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us now explain the steps involved in the method with the help of a few problems given below:','[{"s": "Let us now explain the steps involved in the method with the help of a few problems given below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17967,50,322,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 8.8','[{"s": "Problem 8.8", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17968,50,323,'paragraph',NULL,'Write the net ionic equation for the reaction of potassium dichromate(VI), K 2 Cr 2 O 7 with sodium sulphite, Na 2 SO 3 , in an acid solution to give chromium(III) ion and the sulphate ion.','[{"s": "Write the net ionic equation for the reaction of potassium dichromate(VI), K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with sodium sulphite, Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", in an acid solution to give chromium(III) ion and the sulphate ion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17969,50,324,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17970,50,325,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 1: The skeletal ionic equation is:','[{"s": "Step 1: The skeletal ionic equation is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17971,50,326,'paragraph',NULL,'Cr 2 O 7 2– (aq) + SO 3 2– (aq) → Cr 3+ (aq) + SO 4 2– (aq)','[{"s": "Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) → Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17972,50,327,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 2: Assign oxidation numbers for Cr and S','[{"s": "Step 2:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Assign oxidation numbers for Cr and S", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17973,50,328,'paragraph',NULL,'+6 –2 +4 –2 +3 +6 –2','[{"s": "+6 –2 +4 –2 +3 +6 –2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27775,68,482,'paragraph',NULL,'v =ω R = 0.44 s -1 × 12 cm = 5.3 cm s -1','[{"s": "v =ω R = 0.44 s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× 12 cm = 5.3 cm s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17974,50,329,'paragraph',NULL,'Cr 2 O 7 2– (aq) + SO 3 2– (aq) → Cr(aq)+SO 4 2– (aq)','[{"s": "Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) → Cr(aq)+SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17975,50,330,'paragraph',NULL,'This indicates that the dichromate ion is the oxidant and the sulphite ion is the reductant.','[{"s": "This indicates that the dichromate ion is the oxidant and the sulphite ion is the reductant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17976,50,331,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3: Calculate the increase and decrease of oxidation number, and make them equal: from step-2 we can notice that there is change in oxidation state of chromium and sulphur. Oxidation state of chromium changes form +6 to +3. There is decrease of +3 in oxidation state of chromium on right hand side of the equation. Oxidation state of sulphur changes from +4 to +6. There is an increase of +2 in the oxidation state of sulphur on right hand side. To make the increase and decrease of oxidation state equal, place numeral 2 before cromium ion on right hand side and numeral 3 before sulphate ion on right hand side and balance the chromium and sulphur atoms on both the sides of the equation. Thus we get','[{"s": "Step 3: Calculate the increase and decrease of oxidation number, and make them equal: from step-2 we can notice that there is change in oxidation state of chromium and sulphur. Oxidation state of chromium changes form +6 to +3. There is decrease of +3 in oxidation state of chromium on right hand side of the equation. Oxidation state of sulphur changes from +4 to +6. There is an increase of +2 in the oxidation state of sulphur on right hand side. To make the increase and decrease of oxidation state equal, place numeral 2 before cromium ion on right hand side and numeral 3 before sulphate ion on right hand side and balance the chromium and sulphur atoms on both the sides of the equation. Thus we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17977,50,332,'paragraph',NULL,'+6 –2 +4 –2 +3 +6 –2','[{"s": "+6 –2 +4 –2 +3 +6 –2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17978,50,333,'paragraph',NULL,'Cr 2 O 7 2– (aq) + 3SO 3 2– (aq) → 2Cr 3+ (aq) + 3SO 4 2– (aq)','[{"s": "Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 3SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) → 2Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 3SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17979,50,334,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 4: As the reaction occurs in the acidic medium, and further the ionic charges are not equal on both the sides, add 8H+ on the left to make ionic charges equal','[{"s": "Step 4: As the reaction occurs in the acidic medium, and further the ionic charges are not equal on both the sides, add 8H+ on the left to make ionic charges equal", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17980,50,335,'paragraph',NULL,'Cr 2 O 7 2– (aq) + 3SO 3 2– (aq)+ 8H + → 2Cr 3+ (aq) + 3SO 4 2– (aq)','[{"s": "Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 3SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)+ 8H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 2Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 3SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17981,50,336,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 5: Finally, count the hydrogen atoms, and add appropriate number of water molecules (i.e., 4H2O) on the right to achieve balanced redox change . 2','[{"s": "Step 5: Finally, count the hydrogen atoms, and add appropriate number of water molecules (i.e., 4H2O) on the right to achieve balanced redox change", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]','marked','["220805", "220838"]',NULL),
(17982,50,337,'paragraph',NULL,'Cr 2 O 7 2– (aq) + 3SO 3 2– (aq)+ 8H + (aq) → 2Cr 3+ (aq) + 3SO 4 2– (aq) +4H 2 O (l)','[{"s": "Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 3SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)+ 8H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) → 2Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 3SO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) +4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
