(17991,50,346,'paragraph',NULL,'this indicates that permanganate ion is the oxidant and bromide ion is the reductant.','[{"s": "this indicates that permanganate ion is the oxidant and bromide ion is the reductant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17992,50,347,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3: Calculate the increase and decrease of oxidation number, and make the increase equal to the decrease.','[{"s": "Step 3: Calculate the increase and decrease of oxidation number, and make the increase equal to the decrease.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17993,50,348,'paragraph',NULL,'+7 –1 +4 +5','[{"s": "+7 –1 +4 +5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17994,50,349,'paragraph',NULL,'2MnO 4 – (aq) + Br – (aq) → 2MnO 2 (s) + BrO 3 – (aq)','[{"s": "2MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + BrO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17995,50,350,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 4: As the reaction occurs in the basic medium, and the ionic charges are not equal on both sides, add 2 OH– ions on the right to make ionic charges equal.','[{"s": "Step 4: As the reaction occurs in the basic medium, and the ionic charges are not equal on both sides, add 2 OH– ions on the right to make ionic charges equal.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17996,50,351,'paragraph',NULL,'2MnO 4 – (aq) + Br – (aq) → 2MnO 2 (s) + BrO 3 – (aq) + 2OH – (aq)','[{"s": "2MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + BrO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 2OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17997,50,352,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 5: Finally, count the hydrogen atoms and add appropriate number of water molecules (i.e. one H 2 O molecule) on the left side to achieve balanced redox change.','[{"s": "Step 5: Finally, count the hydrogen atoms and add appropriate number of water molecules (i.e. one H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O molecule) on the left side to achieve balanced redox change.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17998,50,353,'paragraph',NULL,'2MnO 4 – (aq) + Br – (aq) + H 2 O(l) → 2MnO 2 (s) + BrO 3 – (aq) + 2OH – (aq)','[{"s": "2MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + BrO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 2OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(17999,50,354,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Half Reaction Method: In this method, the two half equations are balanced separately and then added together to give balanced equation.','[{"s": "(b) Half Reaction Method:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In this method, the two half equations are balanced separately and then added together to give balanced equation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18000,50,355,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose we are to balance the equation showing the oxidation of Fe 2+ ions to Fe 3+ ions by dichromate ions (Cr 2 O 7 ) 2– in acidic medium, wherein, Cr 2 O 7 2– ions are reduced to Cr3+ ions. The following steps are involved in this task.','[{"s": "Suppose we are to balance the equation showing the oxidation of Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions to Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions by dichromate ions (Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in acidic medium, wherein, Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions are reduced to Cr3+ ions. The following steps are involved in this task.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18001,50,356,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 1: Produce unbalanced equation for the reaction in ionic form :','[{"s": "Step 1: Produce unbalanced equation for the reaction in ionic form :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18002,50,357,'paragraph',NULL,'Fe 2+ (aq) + Cr 2 O 7 2– (aq) → Fe 3+ (aq) + Cr 3+ (aq) (8.50)','[{"s": "Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) (8.50)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18003,50,358,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 2: Separate the equation into half-reactions:','[{"s": "Step 2: Separate the equation into half-reactions:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18004,50,359,'paragraph',NULL,'+2 +3','[{"s": "+2 +3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18005,50,360,'paragraph',NULL,'Oxidation half : Fe 2+ (aq) → Fe 3+ (aq) (8.51)','[{"s": "Oxidation half : Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) (8.51)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18006,50,361,'paragraph',NULL,'+6 –2 +3','[{"s": "+6 –2 +3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18007,50,362,'paragraph',NULL,'Reduction half : Cr 2 O 7 2– (aq) → Cr 3+ (aq) (8.52)','[{"s": "Reduction half : Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) (8.52)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18008,50,363,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3: Balance the atoms other than O and H in each half reaction individually. Here the oxidation half reaction is already balanced with respect to Fe atoms. For the reduction half reaction, we multiply the Cr 3+ by 2 to balance Cr atoms.','[{"s": "Step 3: Balance the atoms other than O and H in each half reaction individually. Here the oxidation half reaction is already balanced with respect to Fe atoms. For the reduction half reaction, we multiply the Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by 2 to balance Cr atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18009,50,364,'paragraph',NULL,'Cr 2 O 7 2– (aq) → 2Cr 3+ (aq) (8.53)','[{"s": "Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) (8.53)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18010,50,365,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 4: For reactions occurring in acidic medium, add H 2 O to balance O atoms and H + to balance H atoms.','[{"s": "Step 4: For reactions occurring in acidic medium, add H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O to balance O atoms and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to balance H atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18011,50,366,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, we get :','[{"s": "Thus, we get :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18012,50,367,'paragraph',NULL,'Cr 2 O 7 2– (aq) + 14H + (aq) → 2Cr 3+ (aq) + 7H 2 O (l) (8.54)','[{"s": "Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 14H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 7H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l) (8.54)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18013,50,368,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 5: Add electrons to one side of the half reaction to balance the charges. If need be, make the number of electrons equal in the two half reactions by multiplying one or both half reactions by appropriate number.','[{"s": "Step 5: Add electrons to one side of the half reaction to balance the charges. If need be, make the number of electrons equal in the two half reactions by multiplying one or both half reactions by appropriate number.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18014,50,369,'paragraph',NULL,'The oxidation half reaction is thus rewritten to balance the charge:','[{"s": "The oxidation half reaction is thus rewritten to balance the charge:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18015,50,370,'paragraph',NULL,'Fe 2+ (aq) → Fe 3+ (aq) + e – (8.55)','[{"s": "Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(8.55)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18016,50,371,'paragraph',NULL,'Now in the reduction half reaction there are net twelve positive charges on the left hand side and only six positive charges on the right hand side. Therefore, we add six electrons on the left side.','[{"s": "Now in the reduction half reaction there are net twelve positive charges on the left hand side and only six positive charges on the right hand side.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Therefore, we add six electrons on the left side.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["220839"]',NULL),
(18017,50,372,'paragraph',NULL,'Cr 2 O 7 2– (aq) + 14H + (aq) + 6e – → 2Cr 3+ (aq) + 7H 2 O (l) (8.56)','[{"s": "Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 14H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 6e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 7H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l) (8.56)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18018,50,373,'paragraph',NULL,'To equalise the number of electrons in both the half reactions, we multiply the oxidation half reaction by 6 and write as :','[{"s": "To equalise the number of electrons in both the half reactions, we multiply the oxidation half reaction by 6 and write as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18019,50,374,'paragraph',NULL,'6Fe 2+ (aq) → 6Fe 3+ (aq) + 6e – (8.57)','[{"s": "6Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 6e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(8.57)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18020,50,375,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 6: We add the two half reactions to achieve the overall reaction and cancel the electrons on each side. This gives the net ionic equation as :','[{"s": "Step 6: We add the two half reactions to achieve the overall reaction and cancel the electrons on each side. This gives the net ionic equation as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18021,50,376,'paragraph',NULL,'6Fe 2+ (aq) + Cr 2 O 7 2– (aq) + 14H + (aq) → 6 Fe 3+ (aq) + 2Cr 3+ (aq) + 7H 2 O(l) (8.58)','[{"s": "6Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 14H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6 Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 2Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 7H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) (8.58)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18022,50,377,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 7: Verify that the equation contains the same type and number of atoms and the same charges on both sides of the equation. This last check reveals that the equation is fully balanced with respect to number of atoms and the charges.','[{"s": "Step 7: Verify that the equation contains the same type and number of atoms and the same charges on both sides of the equation. This last check reveals that the equation is fully balanced with respect to number of atoms and the charges.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18023,50,378,'paragraph',NULL,'For the reaction in a basic medium, first balance the atoms as is done in acidic medium. Then for each H + ion, add an equal number of OH – ions to both sides of the equation. Where H + and OH – appear on the same side of the equation, combine these to give H 2 O.','[{"s": "For the reaction in a basic medium, first balance the atoms as is done in acidic medium. Then for each H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion, add an equal number of OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions to both sides of the equation. Where H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "appear on the same side of the equation, combine these to give H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18024,50,379,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 8.10','[{"s": "Problem 8.10", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18025,50,380,'paragraph',NULL,'Permanganate(VII) ion, MnO 4 – in basic solution oxidises iodide ion, I – to produce molecular iodine (I 2 ) and manganese (IV) oxide (MnO 2 ) . Write a balanced ionic equation to represent this redox reaction.','[{"s": "Permanganate(VII) ion, MnO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "in basic solution oxidises iodide ion, I", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "to produce molecular iodine (I", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") and manganese (IV) oxide (MnO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ")", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Write a balanced ionic equation to represent this redox reaction.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["220845"]',NULL),
(18026,50,381,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18027,50,382,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 1: First we write the skeletal ionic equation, which is','[{"s": "Step 1: First we write the skeletal ionic equation, which is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18028,50,383,'paragraph',NULL,'MnO 4 – (aq) + I – (aq) → MnO 2 (s) + I 2 (s)','[{"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18029,50,384,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 2: The two half-reactions are:','[{"s": "Step 2: The two half-reactions are:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18030,50,385,'paragraph',NULL,'–1 0','[{"s": "–1 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18031,50,386,'paragraph',NULL,'Oxidation half : I – (aq) → I 2 (s)','[{"s": "Oxidation half : I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18032,50,387,'paragraph',NULL,'+7 +4','[{"s": "+7 +4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18033,50,388,'paragraph',NULL,'Reduction half: MnO 4 – (aq) → MnO 2 (s)','[{"s": "Reduction half: MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18034,50,389,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3: To balance the I atoms in the oxidation half reaction, we rewrite it as:','[{"s": "Step 3: To balance the I atoms in the oxidation half reaction, we rewrite it as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18035,50,390,'paragraph',NULL,'2I – (aq) → I 2 (s)','[{"s": "2I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18036,50,391,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 4: To balance the O atoms in the reduction half reaction, we add two water molecules on the right:','[{"s": "Step 4: To balance the O atoms in the reduction half reaction, we add two water molecules on the right:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18037,50,392,'paragraph',NULL,'MnO 4 – (aq) → MnO 2 (s) + 2 H 2 O (l)','[{"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 2 H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18038,50,393,'paragraph',NULL,'To balance the H atoms, we add four H+ ions on the left:','[{"s": "To balance the H atoms, we add four H+ ions on the left:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18039,50,394,'paragraph',NULL,'MnO 4 – (aq) + 4 H + (aq) → MnO 2 (s) + 2H 2 O (l)','[{"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 4 H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18040,50,395,'paragraph',NULL,'As the reaction takes place in a basic solution, therefore, for four H + ions, we add four OH – ions to both sides of the equation:','[{"s": "As the reaction takes place in a basic solution, therefore, for four H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions, we add four OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions to both sides of the equation:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18041,50,396,'paragraph',NULL,'MnO 4 – (aq) + 4H + (aq) + 4OH – (aq) → MnO 2 (s) + 2 H 2 O(l) + 4OH – (aq)','[{"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 4OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 2 H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) + 4OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18042,50,397,'paragraph',NULL,'Replacing the H + and OH – ions with water, the resultant equation is:','[{"s": "Replacing the H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions with water, the resultant equation is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18043,50,398,'paragraph',NULL,'MnO 4 – (aq) + 2H 2 O (l) → MnO 2 (s) + 4 OH – (aq)','[{"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 4 OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18044,50,399,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 5: In this step we balance the charges of the two half-reactions in the manner depicted as:','[{"s": "Step 5: In this step we balance the charges of the two half-reactions in the manner depicted as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18045,50,400,'paragraph',NULL,'2I – (aq) → I 2 (s) + 2e –','[{"s": "2I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 2e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18046,50,401,'paragraph',NULL,'MnO 4 – (aq) + 2H 2 O(l) + 3e – → MnO 2 (s) + 4OH – (aq)','[{"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) + 3e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 4OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18047,50,402,'paragraph',NULL,'Now to equalise the number of electrons, we multiply the oxidation half-reaction by 3 and the reduction half-reaction by 2.','[{"s": "Now to equalise the number of electrons, we multiply the oxidation half-reaction by 3 and the reduction half-reaction by 2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18048,50,403,'paragraph',NULL,'6I – (aq) → 3I 2 (s) + 6e –','[{"s": "6I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 6e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18049,50,404,'paragraph',NULL,'2 MnO 4 – (aq) + 4H 2 O (l) +6e – → 2MnO 2 (s) + 8OH – (aq)','[{"s": "2 MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l) +6e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 8OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18050,50,405,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 6: Add two half-reactions to obtain the net reactions after cancelling electrons on both sides.','[{"s": "Step 6: Add two half-reactions to obtain the net reactions after cancelling electrons on both sides.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18051,50,406,'paragraph',NULL,'6I – (aq) + 2MnO 4 – (aq) + 4H 2 O(l) → 3I 2 (s) + 2MnO 2 (s) + 8OH – (aq)','[{"s": "6I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 2MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 2MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 8OH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18052,50,407,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 7: A final verification shows that the equation is balanced in respect of the number of atoms and charges on both sides.','[{"s": "Step 7: A final verification shows that the equation is balanced in respect of the number of atoms and charges on both sides.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18053,50,408,'paragraph',NULL,'8.3.3 Redox Reactions as the Basis for Titrations','[{"s": "8.3.3 Redox Reactions as the Basis for Titrations", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18054,50,409,'paragraph',NULL,'In acid-base systems we come across with a titration method for finding out the strength of one solution against the other using a pH sensitive indicator. Similarly, in redox systems, the titration method can be adopted to determine the strength of a reductant/oxidant using a redox sensitive indicator. The usage of indicators in redox titration is illustrated below:','[{"s": "In acid-base systems we come across with a titration method for finding out the strength of one solution against the other using a pH sensitive indicator. Similarly, in redox systems, the titration method can be adopted to determine the strength of a reductant/oxidant using a redox sensitive indicator. The usage of indicators in redox titration is illustrated below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18055,50,410,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) In one situation, the reagent itself is intensely coloured, e.g., permanganate ion, MnO 4 – . Here MnO 4 – acts as the self indicator. The visible end point in this case is achieved after the last of the reductant (Fe 2+ or C 2 O 4 2– ) is oxidised and the first lasting tinge of pink colour appears at MnO 4 – concentration as low as 10 –6 mol dm –3 (10 –6 mol L –1 ). This ensures a minimal ‘overshoot’ in colour beyond the equivalence point, the point where the reductant and the oxidant are equal in terms of their mole stoichiometry.','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In one situation, the reagent itself is intensely coloured, e.g., permanganate ion, MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Here MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "acts as the self indicator. The visible end point in this case is achieved after the last of the reductant (Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is oxidised and the first lasting tinge of pink colour appears at MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "concentration as low as 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mol dm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mol L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). This ensures a minimal ‘overshoot’ in colour beyond the equivalence point, the point where the reductant and the oxidant are equal in terms of their mole stoichiometry.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18056,50,411,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) If there is no dramatic auto-colour change (as with MnO 4 – titration), there are indicators which are oxidised immediately after the last bit of the reactant is consumed, producing a dramatic colour change. The best example is afforded by Cr 2 O 7 2– , which is not a self-indicator, but oxidises the indicator substance diphenylamine just after the equivalence point to produce an intense blue colour, thus signalling the end point.','[{"s": "(ii) If there is no dramatic auto-colour change (as with MnO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "titration), there are indicators which are oxidised immediately after the last bit of the reactant is consumed, producing a dramatic colour change. The best example is afforded by Cr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which is not a self-indicator, but oxidises the indicator substance diphenylamine just after the equivalence point to produce an intense blue colour, thus signalling the end point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18057,50,412,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) There is yet another method which is interesting and quite common. Its use is restricted to those reagents which are able to oxidise I– ions, say, for example, Cu(II):','[{"s": "(iii) There is yet another method which is interesting and quite common. Its use is restricted to those reagents which are able to oxidise I– ions, say, for example, Cu(II):", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18058,50,413,'paragraph',NULL,'2Cu 2+ (aq) + 4I – (aq) → Cu 2 I 2 (s) + I 2 (aq) (8.59)','[{"s": "2Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 4I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) (8.59)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18059,50,414,'paragraph',NULL,'This method relies on the facts that iodine itself gives an intense blue colour with starch and has a very specific reaction with thiosulphate ions (S2O32–), which too is a redox reaction:','[{"s": "This method relies on the facts that iodine itself gives an intense blue colour with starch and has a very specific reaction with thiosulphate ions (S2O32–), which too is a redox reaction:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["220820"]',NULL),
(18060,50,415,'paragraph',NULL,'I 2 (aq) + 2 S 2 O 3 2– (aq) → 2I – (aq) + S 4 O 6 2– (aq) (8.60)','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + 2 S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) + S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(aq) (8.60)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18061,50,416,'paragraph',NULL,'I 2 , though insoluble in water, remains in solution containing KI as KI 3 .','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", though insoluble in water, remains in solution containing KI as KI", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18062,50,417,'paragraph',NULL,'On addition of starch after the liberation of iodine from the reaction of Cu 2+ ions on iodide ions, an intense blue colour appears. This colour disappears as soon as the iodine is consumed by the thiosulphate ions. Thus, the end-point can easily be tracked and the rest is the stoichiometric calculation only.','[{"s": "On addition of starch after the liberation of iodine from the reaction of Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ions on iodide ions, an intense blue colour appears. This colour disappears as soon as the iodine is consumed by the thiosulphate ions. Thus, the end-point can easily be tracked and the rest is the stoichiometric calculation only.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18063,50,418,'paragraph',NULL,'8.3.4 Limitations of Concept of Oxidation Number','[{"s": "8.3.4 Limitations of Concept of Oxidation Number", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18064,50,419,'paragraph',NULL,'As you have observed in the above discussion, the concept of redox processes has been evolving with time. This process of evolution is continuing. In fact, in recent past the oxidation process is visualised as a decrease in electron density and reduction process as an increase in electron density around the atom(s) involved in the reaction.','[{"s": "As you have observed in the above discussion, the concept of redox processes has been evolving with time. This process of evolution is continuing. In fact, in recent past the oxidation process is visualised as a decrease in electron density and reduction process as an increase in electron density around the atom(s) involved in the reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18065,50,420,'heading',3,'8.4 REDOX REACTIONS AND ELECTRODE PROCESSES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18066,50,421,'paragraph',NULL,'The experiment corresponding to reaction (8.15), can also be observed if zinc rod is dipped in copper sulphate solution. The redox reaction takes place and during the reaction, zinc is oxidised to zinc ions and copper ions are reduced to metallic copper due to direct transfer of electrons from zinc to copper ion. During this reaction heat is also evolved. Now we modify the experiment in such a manner that for the same redox reaction transfer of electrons takes place indirectly. This necessitates the separation of zinc metal from copper sulphate solution. We take copper sulphate solution in a beaker and put a copper strip or rod in it. We also take zinc sulphate solution in another beaker and put a zinc rod or strip in it. Now reaction takes place in either of the beakers and at the interface of the metal and its salt solution in each beaker both the reduced and oxidized forms of the same species are present. These represent the species in the reduction and oxidation half reactions . A redox couple is defined as having together the oxidised and reduced forms of a substance taking part in an oxidation or reduction half reaction.','[{"s": "The experiment corresponding to reaction (8.15), can also be observed if zinc rod is dipped in copper sulphate solution. The redox reaction takes place and during the reaction, zinc is oxidised to zinc ions and copper ions are reduced to metallic copper due to direct transfer of electrons from zinc to copper ion. During this reaction heat is also evolved. Now we modify the experiment in such a manner that for the same redox reaction transfer of electrons takes place indirectly. This necessitates the separation of zinc metal from copper sulphate solution. We take copper sulphate solution in a beaker and put a copper strip or rod in it. We also take zinc sulphate solution in another beaker and put a zinc rod or strip in it. Now reaction takes place in either of the beakers and at the interface of the metal and its salt solution in each beaker both the reduced and oxidized forms of the same species are present.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These represent the species in the reduction and oxidation half reactions", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A redox couple is defined as having together the oxidised and reduced forms of a substance taking part in an oxidation or reduction half reaction.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["220839"]',NULL),
(18067,50,422,'paragraph',NULL,'This is represented by separating the oxidised form from the reduced form by a vertical line or a slash representing an interface (e.g. solid/solution). For example in this experiment the two','[{"s": "This is represented by separating the oxidised form from the reduced form by a vertical line or a slash representing an interface (e.g. solid/solution). For example in this experiment the two", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18068,50,423,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Redox_Reaction-image9.png'),
(18069,50,424,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 8.3 The set-up for Daniell cell. Electrons produced at the anode due to oxidation of Zn travel through the external circuit to the cathode where these reduce the copper ions. The circuit is completed inside the cell by the migration of ions through the salt bridge. It may be noted that the direction of current is opposite to the direction of electron flow .','[{"s": "Fig. 8.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The set-up for Daniell cell. Electrons produced at the anode due to oxidation of Zn travel through the external circuit to the cathode where these reduce the copper ions. The circuit is completed inside the cell by the migration of ions through the salt bridge. It may be noted that the direction of current is opposite to the direction of electron flow", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18070,50,425,'paragraph',NULL,'redox couples are represented as Zn 2+ /Zn and Cu 2+ /Cu. In both cases, oxidised form is put before the reduced form. Now we put the beaker containing copper sulphate solution and the beaker containing zinc sulphate solution side by side (Fig. 8.3) . We connect solutions in two beakers by a salt bridge (a U-tube containing a solution of potassium chloride or ammonium nitrate usually solidified by boiling with agar agar and later cooling to a jelly like substance). This provides an electric contact between the two solutions without allowing them to mix with each other. The zinc and copper rods are connected by a metallic wire with a provision for an ammeter and a switch. The set-up as shown in Fig.8.3 is known as Daniell cell. When the switch is in the off position, no reaction takes place in either of the beakers and no current flows through the metallic wire. As soon as the switch is in the on position, we make the following observations:','[{"s": "redox couples are represented as Zn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/Zn and Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/Cu. In both cases, oxidised form is put before the reduced form.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Now we put the beaker containing copper sulphate solution and the beaker containing zinc sulphate solution side by side (Fig. 8.3)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". We connect solutions in two beakers by a salt bridge (a U-tube containing a solution of potassium chloride or ammonium nitrate usually solidified by boiling with agar agar and later cooling to a jelly like substance). This provides an electric contact between the two solutions without allowing them to mix with each other. The zinc and copper rods are connected by a metallic wire with a provision for an ammeter and a switch. The set-up as shown in Fig.8.3 is known as Daniell cell. When the switch is in the off position, no reaction takes place in either of the beakers and no current flows through the metallic wire. As soon as the switch is in the on position, we make the following observations:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["220840"]',NULL),
(18071,50,426,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The transfer of electrons now does not take place directly from Zn to Cu 2+ but through the metallic wire connecting the two rods as is apparent from the arrow which indicates the flow of current.','[{"s": "1. The transfer of electrons now does not take place directly from Zn to Cu", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "but through the metallic wire connecting the two rods as is apparent from the arrow which indicates the flow of current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18072,50,427,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The electricity from solution in one beaker to solution in the other beaker flows by the migration of ions through the salt bridge. We know that the flow of current is possible only if there is a potential difference between the copper and zinc rods known as electrodes here.','[{"s": "2. The electricity from solution in one beaker to solution in the other beaker flows by the migration of ions through the salt bridge. We know that the flow of current is possible only if there is a potential difference between the copper and zinc rods known as electrodes here.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18073,50,428,'paragraph',NULL,'The potential associated with each electrode is known as electrode potential. If the concentration of each species taking part in the electrode reaction is unity (if any gas appears in the electrode reaction, it is confined to 1 atmospheric pressure) and further the reaction is carried out at 298K, then the potential of each electrode is said to be the Standard Electrode Potential. By convention, the standard electrode potential (E e )of hydrogen electrode is 0.00 volts. The electrode potential value for each electrode process is a measure of the relative tendency of the active species in the process to remain in the oxidised/reduced form. A negative E means that the redox couple is a stronger reducing agent than the H + /H 2 couple. A positive E means that the redox couple is a weaker reducing agent than the H + /H 2 couple. The standard electrode potentials are very important and we can get a lot of other usefulinformation from them. The values of standard electrode potentials for some selected electrode processes (reduction reactions) are given in Table 8.1. You will learn more about electrode reactions and cells in Class XII.','[{"s": "The potential associated with each electrode is known as electrode potential.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If the concentration of each species taking part in the electrode reaction is unity (if any gas appears in the electrode reaction, it is confined to 1 atmospheric pressure) and further the reaction is carried out at 298K, then the potential of each electrode is said to be the Standard Electrode Potential.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "By convention, the standard electrode potential (E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")of hydrogen electrode is 0.00 volts. The electrode potential value for each electrode process is a measure of the relative tendency of the active species in the process to remain in the oxidised/reduced form. A negative E means that the redox couple is a stronger reducing agent than the H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "couple. A positive E means that the redox couple is a weaker reducing agent than the H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "couple. The standard electrode potentials are very important and we can get a lot of other usefulinformation from them. The values of standard electrode potentials for some selected electrode processes (reduction reactions) are given in Table 8.1. You will learn more about electrode reactions and cells in Class XII.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["220829"]',NULL),
(18074,50,429,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 8.1 The Standard Electrode Potentials at 298 K3','[{"s": "Table 8.1 The Standard Electrode Potentials at 298 K3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["220840", "220841", "220846"]',NULL),
