(18213,51,92,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 2.2: Experimental results for the solubility of HCl gas in cyclohexane at 293 K. The slope of the line is the Henry’s Law constant, K H .','[{"s": "Fig. 2.2: Experimental results for the solubility of HCl gas in cyclohexane at 293 K. The slope of the line is the Henry’s Law constant, K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18214,51,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Different gases have different K H values at the same temperature (Table 2.2). This suggests that K H is a function of the nature of the gas.','[{"s": "Different gases have different K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values at the same temperature (Table 2.2). This suggests that K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a function of the nature of the gas.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18215,51,94,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 2.2: Values of Henry’s Law Constant for Some Selected Gases in Water','[{"s": "Table 2.2: Values of Henry’s Law Constant for Some Selected Gases in Water", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18216,51,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Solutions-image47.png It is obvious from equation (2.11) that higher the value of K H at a given pressure, the lower is the solubility of the gas in the liquid. It can be seen from Table 2.2 that K H values for both N 2 and O 2 increase with increase of temperature indicating that the solubility of gases increases with decrease of temperature. It is due to this reason that aquatic species are more comfortable in cold waters rather than in warm waters.','[{"s": "Solutions-image47.png", "t": "img", "alt": "2d"}, {"s": "It is obvious from equation (2.11) that higher the value of K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at a given pressure, the lower is the solubility of the gas in the liquid. It can be seen from Table 2.2 that K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values for both N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "increase with increase of temperature indicating that the solubility of gases increases with decrease of temperature. It is due to this reason that aquatic species are more comfortable in cold waters rather than in warm waters.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18217,51,96,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.4','[{"s": "Example 2.4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18218,51,97,'paragraph',NULL,'If N 2 gas is bubbled through water at 293 K, how many millimoles of N 2 gas would dissolve in 1 litre of water? Assume that N 2 exerts a partial pressure of 0.987 bar. Given that Henry’s law constant for N 2 at 293 K is 76.48 kbar.','[{"s": "If N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gas is bubbled through water at 293 K, how many millimoles of N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gas would dissolve in 1 litre of water? Assume that N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "exerts a partial pressure of 0.987 bar. Given that Henry’s law constant for N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at 293 K is 76.48 kbar.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18219,51,98,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18220,51,99,'paragraph',NULL,'The solubility of gas is related to the mole fraction in aqueous solution. The mole fraction of the gas in the solution is calculated by applying Henry’s law. Thus:','[{"s": "The solubility of gas is related to the mole fraction in aqueous solution. The mole fraction of the gas in the solution is calculated by applying Henry’s law. Thus:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18221,51,100,'paragraph',NULL,'x (Nitrogen) Solutions-image26.png = Solutions-image109.png = Solutions-image111.png = 1.29 × 10 –5','[{"s": "x (Nitrogen)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image26.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image109.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image111.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 1.29 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18222,51,101,'paragraph',NULL,'As 1 litre of water contains 55.5 mol of it, therefore if n represents number of moles of N 2 in solution,','[{"s": "As 1 litre of water contains 55.5 mol of it, therefore if n represents number of moles of N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in solution,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18223,51,102,'paragraph',NULL,'x (Nitrogen) = Solutions-image19.png = Solutions-image62.png = 1.29 × 10 –5','[{"s": "x (Nitrogen) =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image19.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image62.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 1.29 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18224,51,103,'paragraph',NULL,'(n in denominator is neglected as it is < < 55.5)','[{"s": "(n in denominator is neglected as it is < < 55.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18225,51,104,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus n = 1.29 × 10 –5 × 55.5 mol = 7.16 × 10 –4 mol','[{"s": "Thus n = 1.29 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× 55.5 mol = 7.16 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18226,51,105,'paragraph',NULL,'= Solutions-image82.png = 0.716 mmol','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image82.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 0.716 mmol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18227,51,106,'paragraph',NULL,'Henry’s law finds several applications in industry and explains some biological phenomena. Notable among these are:','[{"s": "Henry’s law finds several applications in industry and explains some biological phenomena. Notable among these are:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18228,51,107,'paragraph',NULL,'Effect of Temperature','[{"s": "Effect of Temperature", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18229,51,108,'paragraph',NULL,'Solubility of gases in liquids decreases with rise in temperature. When dissolved, the gas molecules are present in liquid phase and the process of dissolution can be considered similar to condensation and heat is evolved in this process. We have learnt in the last Section that dissolution process involves dynamic equilibrium and thus must follow Le Chatelier’s Principle . As dissolution is an exothermic process, the solubility should decrease with increase of temperature.','[{"s": "Solubility of gases in liquids decreases with rise in temperature. When dissolved, the gas molecules are present in liquid phase and the process of dissolution can be considered similar to condensation and heat is evolved in this process. We have learnt in the last Section that dissolution process involves dynamic equilibrium and thus must follow", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Le Chatelier’s Principle", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". As dissolution is an exothermic process, the solubility should decrease with increase of temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18230,51,109,'paragraph',NULL,'Intext Questions','[{"s": "Intext Questions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18231,51,110,'paragraph',NULL,'2.6 H 2 S, a toxic gas with rotten egg like smell, is used for the qualitative analysis. If the solubility of H 2 S in water at STP is 0.195 m, calculate Henry’s law constant.','[{"s": "2.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S, a toxic gas with rotten egg like smell, is used for the qualitative analysis. If the solubility of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S in water at STP is 0.195 m, calculate Henry’s law constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18232,51,111,'paragraph',NULL,'2.7 Henry’s law constant for CO 2 in water is 1.67x10 8 Pa at 298 K. Calculate the quantity of CO 2 in 500 mL of soda water when packed under 2.5 atm CO 2 pressure at 298 K.','[{"s": "2.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Henry’s law constant for CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in water is 1.67x10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Pa at 298 K. Calculate the quantity of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in 500 mL of soda water when packed under 2.5 atm CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pressure at 298 K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18233,51,112,'heading',3,'2.4 Vapour Pressure of Liquid Solutions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18234,51,113,'paragraph',NULL,'Liquid solutions are formed when solvent is a liquid. The solute can be a gas, a liquid or a solid. Solutions of gases in liquids have already been discussed in Section 2.3.2. In this Section, we shall discuss the solutions of liquids and solids in a liquid. Such solutions may contain one or more volatile components. Generally, the liquid solvent is volatile. The solute may or may not be volatile. We shall discuss the properties of only binary solutions, that is, the solutions containing two components, namely, the solutions of (i) liquids in liquids and (ii) solids in liquids.','[{"s": "Liquid solutions are formed when solvent is a liquid. The solute can be a gas, a liquid or a solid. Solutions of gases in liquids have already been discussed in Section 2.3.2. In this Section, we shall discuss the solutions of liquids and solids in a liquid. Such solutions may contain one or more volatile components. Generally, the liquid solvent is volatile. The solute may or may not be volatile. We shall discuss the properties of only binary solutions, that is, the solutions containing two components, namely, the solutions of (i) liquids in liquids and (ii) solids in liquids.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18235,51,114,'heading',4,'2.4.1 Vapour Pressure of Liquid-Liquid Solutions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18236,51,115,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us consider a binary solution of two volatile liquids and denote the two components as 1 and 2. When taken in a closed vessel, both the components would evaporate and eventually an equilibrium would be established between vapour phase and the liquid phase. Let the total vapour pressure at this stage be p total and p 1 and p 2 be the partial vapour pressures of the two components 1 and 2 respectively. These partial pressures are related to the mole fractions x 1 and x 2 of the two components 1 and 2 respectively.','[{"s": "Let us consider a binary solution of two volatile liquids and denote the two components as 1 and 2. When taken in a closed vessel, both the components would evaporate and eventually an equilibrium would be established between vapour phase and the liquid phase. Let the total vapour pressure at this stage be p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "total", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the partial vapour pressures of the two components 1 and 2 respectively. These partial pressures are related to the mole fractions x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the two components 1 and 2 respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18237,51,116,'paragraph',NULL,'The French chemist, Francois Marte Raoult (1886) gave the quantitative relationship between them . The relationship is known as the Raoult’s law which states that for a solution of volatile liquids, the partial vapour pressure of each component of the solution is directly proportional to its mole fraction present in solution .','[{"s": "The French chemist, Francois Marte Raoult (1886) gave the quantitative relationship between them", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The relationship is known as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Raoult’s law", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which states that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for a solution of volatile liquids, the partial vapour pressure of each component of the solution is directly proportional to its mole fraction present in solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18238,51,117,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, for component 1','[{"s": "Thus, for component 1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18239,51,118,'paragraph',NULL,'p 1 ∝ x 1','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∝ x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18240,51,119,'paragraph',NULL,'and p 1 = Solutions-image6.png x 1 (2.12)','[{"s": "and p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image6.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18241,51,120,'paragraph',NULL,'where Solutions-image6.png is the vapour pressure of pure component 1 at the same temperature.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image6.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is the vapour pressure of pure component 1 at the same temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18242,51,121,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, for component 2','[{"s": "Similarly, for component 2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18243,51,122,'paragraph',NULL,'p 2 = p 2 0 x 2 (2.13)','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.13)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18244,51,123,'paragraph',NULL,'where p 2 0 represents the vapour pressure of the pure component','[{"s": "where", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "0", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "represents the vapour pressure of the pure component", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["160913"]',NULL),
(18245,51,124,'paragraph',NULL,'According to Dalton’s law of partial pressures , the total pressure ( Solutions-image10.png ) over the solution phase in the container will be the sum of the partial pressures of the components of the solution and is given as:','[{"s": "According to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Dalton’s law of partial pressures", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the total pressure (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image10.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ") over the solution phase in the container will be the sum of the partial pressures of the components of the solution and is given as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18246,51,125,'paragraph',NULL,'p total = p 1 + p 2 (2.14)','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "total", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.14)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18247,51,126,'paragraph',NULL,'Substituting the values of p 1 and p 2 , we get','[{"s": "Substituting the values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18248,51,127,'paragraph',NULL,'p total = x 1 p 1 0 + x 2 p 2 0','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "total", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18249,51,128,'paragraph',NULL,'= (1 – x 2 ) p 1 0 + x 2 p 2 0 (2.15)','[{"s": "= (1 – x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.15)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18250,51,129,'paragraph',NULL,'= p 1 0 + (p 2 0 – p 1 0 ) x 2 (2.16)','[{"s": "= p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ (p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18251,51,130,'paragraph',NULL,'Following conclusions can be drawn from equation (2.16).','[{"s": "Following conclusions can be drawn from equation (2.16).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18252,51,131,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Total vapour pressure over the solution can be related to the mole fraction of any one component.','[{"s": "(i) Total vapour pressure over the solution can be related to the mole fraction of any one component.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18253,51,132,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Total vapour pressure over the solution varies linearly with the mole fraction of component 2.','[{"s": "(ii) Total vapour pressure over the solution varies linearly with the mole fraction of component 2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18254,51,133,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Depending on the vapour pressures of the pure components 1 and 2, total vapour pressure over the solution decreases or increases with the increase of the mole fraction of component 1.','[{"s": "(iii) Depending on the vapour pressures of the pure components 1 and 2, total vapour pressure over the solution decreases or increases with the increase of the mole fraction of component 1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18255,51,134,'paragraph',NULL,'A plot of p 1 or p 2 versus the mole fractions x 1 and x 2 for a solution gives a linear plot as shown in Fig. 2.3. These lines (I and II) pass through the points for which x 1 and x 2 are equal to unity. Similarly the plot (line III) of p total versus x 2 is also linear (Fig. 2.3). The minimum value of p total is p 1 0 and the maximum value is p 2 0 , assuming that component 1 is less volatile than component 2, i.e., p 1 0 < p 2 0 .','[{"s": "A plot of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "versus the mole fractions", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for a solution gives a linear plot as shown in Fig. 2.3. These lines (I and II) pass through the points for which", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are equal to unity. Similarly the plot (line III) of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "total", "t": "text"}, {"s": "versus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is also linear (Fig. 2.3). The minimum value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "total", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the maximum value is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", assuming that component 1 is less volatile than component 2, i.e.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18256,51,135,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Solutions-image84.png'),
(18257,51,136,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 2.3: The plot of vapour pressure and mole fraction of an ideal solution at constant temperature. The dashed lines I and II represent the partial pressure of the components. (It can be seen from the plot that p 1 and p 2 are directly proportional to x 1 and x 2 , respectively). The total vapour pressure is given by line marked III in the figure.','[{"s": "Fig. 2.3: The plot of vapour pressure and mole fraction of an ideal solution at constant temperature. The dashed lines I and II represent the partial pressure of the components. (It can be seen from the plot that p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are directly proportional to x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively). The total vapour pressure is given by line marked III in the figure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18258,51,137,'paragraph',NULL,'The composition of vapour phase in equilibrium with the solution is determined by the partial pressures of the components. If y 1 and y 2 are the mole fractions of the components 1 and 2 respectively in the vapour phase then, using Dalton’s law of partial pressures:','[{"s": "The composition of vapour phase in equilibrium with the solution is determined by the partial pressures of the components. If y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the mole fractions of the components 1 and 2 respectively in the vapour phase then, using Dalton’s law of partial pressures:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18259,51,138,'paragraph',NULL,'p 1 = y 1 p total (2.17)','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "total", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
