(18343,51,222,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, M 2 = 170 g mol –1','[{"s": "Therefore, M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 170 g mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18344,51,223,'heading',4,'2.6.2 Elevation of Boiling Point',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18345,51,224,'paragraph',NULL,'We have learnt in Unit 5, Class XI, that the vapour pressure of a liquid increases with increase of temperature. It boils at the temperature at which its vapour pressure is equal to the atmospheric pressure. For example, water boils at 373.15 K (100° C) because at this temperature the vapour pressure of water is 1.013 bar (1 atmosphere). We have also learnt in the last section that vapour pressure of the solvent decreases in the presence of non-volatile solute. Fig. 2.7 depicts the variation of vapour pressure of the pure solvent and solution as a function of temperature. For example, the vapour pressure of an aqueous solution of sucrose is less than 1.013 bar at 373.15 K. In order to make this solution boil, its vapour pressure must be increased to 1.013 bar by raising the temperature above the boiling temperature of the pure solvent (water). Thus, the boiling point of a solution is always higher than that of the boiling point of the pure solvent in which the solution is prepared as shown in Fig. 2.7. Similar to lowering of vapour pressure, the elevation of boiling point also depends on the number of solute molecules rather than their nature. A solution of 1 mol of sucrose in 1000 g of water boils at 373.52 K at one atmospheric pressure.','[{"s": "We have learnt in Unit 5, Class XI, that the vapour pressure of a liquid increases with increase of temperature. It boils at the temperature at which its vapour pressure is equal to the atmospheric pressure. For example, water boils at 373.15 K (100° C) because at this temperature the vapour pressure of water is 1.013 bar (1 atmosphere). We have also learnt in the last section that vapour pressure of the solvent decreases in the presence of non-volatile solute. Fig. 2.7 depicts the variation of vapour pressure of the pure solvent and solution as a function of temperature. For example, the vapour pressure of an aqueous solution of sucrose is less than 1.013 bar at 373.15 K. In order to make this solution boil, its vapour pressure must be increased to 1.013 bar by raising the temperature above the boiling temperature of the pure solvent (water). Thus, the boiling point of a solution is always higher than that of the boiling point of the pure solvent in which the solution is prepared as shown in Fig. 2.7. Similar to lowering of vapour pressure, the elevation of boiling point also depends on the number of solute molecules rather than their nature. A solution of 1 mol of sucrose in 1000 g of water boils at 373.52 K at one atmospheric pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18346,51,225,'image',NULL,'2e',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Solutions-image90.png'),
(18347,51,226,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 2.7: The vapour pressure curve for solution lies below the curve for pure water. The diagram shows that ∆T b denotes the elevation of boiling point of a solvent in solution.','[{"s": "Fig. 2.7:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The vapour pressure curve for solution lies below the curve for pure water. The diagram shows that ∆T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "denotes the elevation of boiling point of a solvent in solution.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18348,51,227,'paragraph',NULL,'Let Solutions-image27.png be the boiling point of pure solvent and Solutions-image33.png be the boiling point of solution. The increase in the boiling point Solutions-image81.png is known as elevation of boiling point .','[{"s": "Let", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image27.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "be the boiling point of pure solvent and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image33.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "be the boiling point of solution. The increase in the boiling point", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image81.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "elevation of boiling point", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18349,51,228,'paragraph',NULL,'Experiments have shown that for dilute solutions the elevation of boiling point (∆T b ) is directly proportional to the molal concentration of the solute in a solution. Thus','[{"s": "Experiments have shown that for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dilute solutions", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the elevation of boiling point (∆T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is directly proportional to the molal concentration of the solute in a solution. Thus", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18350,51,229,'paragraph',NULL,'∆T b ∝ m (2.29)','[{"s": "∆T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∝ m (2.29)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18351,51,230,'paragraph',NULL,'or ∆T b = K b m (2.30)','[{"s": "or ∆T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m (2.30)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24139,61,151,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image22.png'),
(18352,51,231,'paragraph',NULL,'Here m (molality) is the number of moles of solute dissolved in 1 kg of solvent and the constant of proportionality, K b is called Boiling Point Elevation Constant or Molal Elevation Constant (Ebullioscopic Constant) . The unit of K b is K kg mol -1 . Values of K b for some common solvents are given in Table 2.3. If w 2 gram of solute of molar mass M 2 is dissolved in w 1 gram of solvent, then molality, m of the solution is given by the expression:','[{"s": "Here m (molality) is the number of moles of solute dissolved in 1 kg of solvent and the constant of proportionality, K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Boiling Point Elevation Constant or Molal Elevation Constant (Ebullioscopic Constant)", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The unit of K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is K kg mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Values of K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for some common solvents are given in Table 2.3. If w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gram of solute of molar mass M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is dissolved in w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gram of solvent, then molality, m of the solution is given by the expression:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18353,51,232,'paragraph',NULL,'m = Solutions-image102.png = Solutions-image28.png (2.31)','[{"s": "m =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image102.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image28.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.31)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18354,51,233,'paragraph',NULL,'Substituting the value of molality in equation (2.30) we get','[{"s": "Substituting the value of molality in equation (2.30) we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18355,51,234,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ T b = Solutions-image104.png (2.32)','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image104.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.32)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18356,51,235,'paragraph',NULL,'M 2 = Solutions-image88.png (2.33)','[{"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Solutions-image88.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.33)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18357,51,236,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, in order to determine M 2 , molar mass of the solute, known mass of solute in a known mass of the solvent is taken and ∆ T b is determined experimentally for a known solvent whose K b value is known.','[{"s": "Thus, in order to determine", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", molar mass of the solute, known mass of solute in a known mass of the solvent is taken and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is determined experimentally for a known solvent whose", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "value is known.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18358,51,237,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.7','[{"s": "Example 2.7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18359,51,238,'paragraph',NULL,'18 g of glucose, C 6 H 12 O 6 , is dissolved in 1 kg of water in a saucepan. At what temperature will water boil at 1.013 bar? K b for water is 0.52 K kg mol -1 .','[{"s": "18 g of glucose, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", is dissolved in 1 kg of water in a saucepan. At what temperature will water boil at 1.013 bar? K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for water is 0.52 K kg mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18360,51,239,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18361,51,240,'paragraph',NULL,'Moles of glucose = 18 g/ 180 g mol –1 = 0.1 mol','[{"s": "Moles of glucose = 18 g/ 180 g mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.1 mol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18362,51,241,'paragraph',NULL,'Number of kilograms of solvent = 1 kg','[{"s": "Number of kilograms of solvent = 1 kg", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18363,51,242,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus molality of glucose solution = 0.1 mol kg -1','[{"s": "Thus molality of glucose solution = 0.1 mol kg", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["160972"]',NULL),
(18364,51,243,'paragraph',NULL,'For water, change in boiling point','[{"s": "For water, change in boiling point", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18365,51,244,'paragraph',NULL,'∆T b = K b × m = 0.52 K kg mol –1 × 0.1 mol kg –1 = 0.052 K','[{"s": "∆T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× m = 0.52 K kg mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× 0.1 mol kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.052 K", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18366,51,245,'paragraph',NULL,'Since water boils at 373.15 K at 1.013 bar pressure, therefore, the boiling point of solution will be 373.15 + 0.052 = 373.202 K.','[{"s": "Since water boils at 373.15 K at 1.013 bar pressure, therefore, the boiling point of solution will be 373.15 + 0.052 = 373.202 K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18367,51,246,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.8','[{"s": "Example 2.8", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18368,51,247,'paragraph',NULL,'The boiling point of benzene is 353.23 K. When 1.80 g of a non-volatile solute was dissolved in 90 g of benzene, the boiling point is raised to 354.11 K. Calculate the molar mass of the solute. K b for benzene is 2.53 K kg mol –1','[{"s": "The boiling point of benzene is 353.23 K. When 1.80 g of a non-volatile solute was dissolved in 90 g of benzene, the boiling point is raised to 354.11 K. Calculate the molar mass of the solute. K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for benzene is 2.53 K kg mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(18369,51,248,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
