(19004,51,883,'paragraph',NULL,'56. Using Raoult’s law explain how the total vapour pressure over the solution is related to mole fraction of components in the following solutions.','[{"s": "56. Using Raoult’s law explain how the total vapour pressure over the solution is related to mole fraction of components in the following solutions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19005,51,884,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) CHCl 3 (l) and CH 2 Cl 2 (l)','[{"s": "(a) CHCl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(l)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(l)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19006,51,885,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) NaCl (s) and H 2 O (l)','[{"s": "(b) NaCl (s) and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(l)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19007,51,886,'paragraph',NULL,'57. Explain the terms ideal and non-ideal solutions in the light of forces of interactions operating between molecules in liquid solutions.','[{"s": "57. Explain the terms ideal and non-ideal solutions in the light of forces of interactions operating between molecules in liquid solutions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19008,51,887,'paragraph',NULL,'58. Why is it not possible to obtain pure ethanol by fractional distillation? What general name is given to binary mixtures which show deviation from Raoult’s law and whose components cannot be separated by fractional distillation. How many types of such mixtures are there?','[{"s": "58. Why is it not possible to obtain pure ethanol by fractional distillation? What general name is given to binary mixtures which show deviation from Raoult’s law and whose components cannot be separated by fractional distillation. How many types of such mixtures are there?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19009,51,888,'paragraph',NULL,'59. When kept in water, raisin swells in size. Name and explain the phenomenon involved with the help of a diagram. Give three applications of the phenomenon.','[{"s": "59. When kept in water, raisin swells in size. Name and explain the phenomenon involved with the help of a diagram. Give three applications of the phenomenon.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19010,51,889,'paragraph',NULL,'60. Discuss biological and industrial applications of osmosis.','[{"s": "60. Discuss biological and industrial applications of osmosis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27781,68,488,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Vector quantities are quantities with magnitude and direction both. Examples are displacement, velocity and acceleration. They obey special rules of vector algebra.','[{"s": "2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Vector quantities", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are quantities with magnitude and direction both. Examples are displacement, velocity and acceleration. They obey special rules of vector algebra.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19011,51,890,'paragraph',NULL,'61. How can you remove the hard calcium carbonate layer of the egg without damaging its semipermeable membrane? Can this egg be inserted into a bottle with a narrow neck without distorting its shape? Explain the process involved.','[{"s": "61. How can you remove the hard calcium carbonate layer of the egg without damaging its semipermeable membrane? Can this egg be inserted into a bottle with a narrow neck without distorting its shape? Explain the process involved.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19012,51,891,'paragraph',NULL,'62. Why is the mass determined by measuring a colligative property in case of some solutes abnormal? Discuss it with the help of van’t Hoff factor.','[{"s": "62. Why is the mass determined by measuring a colligative property in case of some solutes abnormal? Discuss it with the help of van’t Hoff factor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19013,52,0,'paragraph',NULL,'"Chemistry is the science of molecules and their transformations. It is the science not so much of the one hundred elements but of the infinite variety of molecules that may be built from them."','[{"s": "\\"Chemistry is the science of molecules and their transformations. It is the science not so much of the one hundred elements but of the infinite variety of molecules that may be built from them.\\"", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19014,52,1,'paragraph',NULL,'Roald Hoffmann','[{"s": "Roald Hoffmann", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19015,52,2,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemistry deals with the composition, structure and properties of matter. These aspects can be best described and understood in terms of basic constituents of matter: atoms and molecules. That is why chemistry is called the science of atoms and molecules. Can we see, weigh and perceive these entities? Is it possible to count the number of atoms and molecules in a given mass of matter and have a quantitative relationship between the mass and number of these particles (atoms and molecules)? We will like to answer some of these questions in this Unit. We would further describe how physical properties of matter can be quantitatively described using numerical values with suitable units.','[{"s": "Chemistry deals with the composition, structure and properties of matter. These aspects can be best described and understood in terms of basic constituents of matter: atoms and molecules. That is why chemistry is called the science of atoms and molecules. Can we see, weigh and perceive these entities? Is it possible to count the number of atoms and molecules in a given mass of matter and have a quantitative relationship between the mass and number of these particles (atoms and molecules)? We will like to answer some of these questions in this Unit. We would further describe how physical properties of matter can be quantitatively described using numerical values with suitable units.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19016,52,3,'heading',3,'1.1 IMPORTANCE OF CHEMISTRY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19017,52,4,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemistry plays a central role in science and is often intertwined with other branches of science.','[{"s": "Chemistry plays a central role in science and is often intertwined with other branches of science.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19018,52,5,'paragraph',NULL,'Principles of chemistry are applicable in diverse areas, such as weather patterns, functioning of brain and operation of a computer, production in chemical industries, manufacturing fertilizers, alkalis, acids, salts, dyes, polymers, drugs, soaps, detergents, metals, alloys, etc., including new material.','[{"s": "Principles of chemistry are applicable in diverse areas, such as weather patterns, functioning of brain and operation of a computer, production in chemical industries, manufacturing fertilizers, alkalis, acids, salts, dyes, polymers, drugs, soaps, detergents, metals, alloys, etc., including new material.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19019,52,6,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemistry contributes in a big way to the national economy. It also plays an important role in meeting human needs for food, healthcare products and other material aimed at improving the quality of life. This is exemplified by the large-scale production of a variety of fertilizers, improved variety of pesticides and insecticides. Chemistry provides methods for the isolation of life-saving drugs from natural sources and makes possible synthesis of such drugs. Some of these drugs are cisplatin and taxol , which are effective in cancer therapy. The drug AZT (Azidothymidine) is used for helping AIDS patients.','[{"s": "Chemistry contributes in a big way to the national economy. It also plays an important role in meeting human needs for food, healthcare products and other material aimed at improving the quality of life. This is exemplified by the large-scale production of a variety of fertilizers, improved variety of pesticides and insecticides. Chemistry provides methods for the isolation of life-saving drugs from natural sources and makes possible synthesis of such drugs. Some of these drugs are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cisplatin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "taxol", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which are effective in cancer therapy. The drug AZT (Azidothymidine) is used for helping AIDS patients.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19020,52,7,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemistry contributes to a large extent in the development and growth of a nation. With a better understanding of chemical principles it has now become possible to design and synthesize new material having specific magnetic, electric and optical properties. This has lead to the production of superconducting ceramics, conducting polymers, optical fibers, etc. Chemistry has helped in establishing industries which manufacture utility goods, like acids, alkalies, dyes, polymers metals, etc. These industries contribute in a big way to the economy of a nation and generate employment.','[{"s": "Chemistry contributes to a large extent in the development and growth of a nation. With a better understanding of chemical principles it has now become possible to design and synthesize new material having specific magnetic, electric and optical properties. This has lead to the production of superconducting ceramics, conducting polymers, optical fibers, etc. Chemistry has helped in establishing industries which manufacture utility goods, like acids, alkalies, dyes, polymers metals, etc. These industries contribute in a big way to the economy of a nation and generate employment.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19021,52,8,'paragraph',NULL,'In recent years, chemistry has helped in dealing with some of the pressing aspects of environmental degradation with a fair degree of success. Safer alternatives to environmentally hazardous refrigerants, like CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons), responsible for ozone depletion in the stratosphere, have been successfully synthesized. However, many big environmental problems continue to be matters of grave concern to the chemists. One such problem is the management of the Green House gases, like methane, carbon dioxide, etc. Understanding of biochemical processes, use of enzymes for large-scale production of chemicals and synthesis of new exotic material are some of the intellectual challenges for the future generation of chemists. A developing country, like India, needs talented and creative chemists for accepting such challenges. To be a good chemist and to accept such challanges, one needs to understand the basic concepts of chemistry, which begin with the concept of matter. Let us start with the nature of matter.','[{"s": "In recent years, chemistry has helped in dealing with some of the pressing aspects of environmental degradation with a fair degree of success. Safer alternatives to environmentally hazardous refrigerants, like CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons), responsible for ozone depletion in the stratosphere, have been successfully synthesized. However, many big environmental problems continue to be matters of grave concern to the chemists. One such problem is the management of the Green House gases, like methane, carbon dioxide, etc. Understanding of biochemical processes, use of enzymes for large-scale production of chemicals and synthesis of new exotic material are some of the intellectual challenges for the future generation of chemists. A developing country, like India, needs talented and creative chemists for accepting such challenges. To be a good chemist and to accept such challanges, one needs to understand the basic concepts of chemistry, which begin with the concept of matter. Let us start with the nature of matter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19022,52,9,'heading',3,'1.2 Nature of Matter',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19023,52,10,'paragraph',NULL,'You are already familiar with the term matter from your earlier classes. Anything which has mass and occupies space is called matter . Everything around us, for example, book, pen, pencil, water, air, all living beings, etc., are composed of matter. You know that they have mass and they occupy space. Let us recall the characteristics of the states of matter, which you learnt in your previous classes.','[{"s": "You are already familiar with the term", "t": "text"}, {"s": "matter", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from your earlier classes. Anything which has mass and occupies space is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "matter", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Everything around us, for example, book, pen, pencil, water, air, all living beings, etc., are composed of matter. You know that they have mass and they occupy space. Let us recall the characteristics of the states of matter, which you learnt in your previous classes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19024,52,11,'heading',4,'1.2.1 States of Matter',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19025,52,12,'paragraph',NULL,'You are aware that matter can exist in three physical states viz. solid , liquid and gas . The constituent particles of matter in these three states can be represented as shown in Fig. 1.1.','[{"s": "You are aware that matter can exist in three physical states viz.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "solid", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "liquid", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gas", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The constituent particles of matter in these three states can be represented as shown in Fig. 1.1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19026,52,13,'paragraph',NULL,'Particles are held very close to each other in solids in an orderly fashion and there is not much freedom of movement. In liquids , the particles are close to each other but they can move around. However, in gases , the particles are far apart as compared to those present in solid or liquid states and their movement is easy and fast. Because of such arrangement of particles, different states of matter exhibit the following characteristics:','[{"s": "Particles are held very close to each other in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "solids", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in an orderly fashion and there is not much freedom of movement. In", "t": "text"}, {"s": "liquids", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the particles are close to each other but they can move around. However, in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gases", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the particles are far apart as compared to those present in solid or liquid states and their movement is easy and fast. Because of such arrangement of particles, different states of matter exhibit the following characteristics:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19027,52,14,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image30.png'),
(19028,52,15,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.1 Arrangement of particles in solid, liquid and gaseous state','[{"s": "Fig. 1.1 Arrangement of particles in solid, liquid and gaseous state", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19029,52,16,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Solids have definite volume and definite shape.','[{"s": "(i) Solids have definite volume and definite shape.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19030,52,17,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Liquids have definite volume but do not have definite shape. They take the shape of the container in which they are placed.','[{"s": "(ii) Liquids have definite volume but do not have definite shape. They take the shape of the container in which they are placed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19031,52,18,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Gases have neither definite volume nor definite shape. They completely occupy the space in the container in which they are placed.','[{"s": "(iii) Gases have neither definite volume nor definite shape. They completely occupy the space in the container in which they are placed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19032,52,19,'paragraph',NULL,'These three states of matter are interconvertible by changing the conditions of temperature and pressure.','[{"s": "These three states of matter are interconvertible by changing the conditions of temperature and pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19033,52,20,'paragraph',NULL,'Solid Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image19.png liquid Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image19.png Gas','[{"s": "Solid", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image19.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "liquid", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image19.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Gas", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19034,52,21,'paragraph',NULL,'On heating, a solid usually changes to a liquid, and the liquid on further heating changes to gas (or vapour). In the reverse process, a gas on cooling liquifies to the liquid and the liquid on further cooling freezes to the solid.','[{"s": "On heating, a solid usually changes to a liquid, and the liquid on further heating changes to gas (or vapour). In the reverse process, a gas on cooling liquifies to the liquid and the liquid on further cooling freezes to the solid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19035,52,22,'heading',4,'1.2.2. Classification of Matter',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19036,52,23,'paragraph',NULL,'In class IX (Chapter 2), you have learnt that at the macroscopic or bulk level, matter can be classified as mixture or pure substance . These can be further sub-divided as shown in Fig. 1.2.','[{"s": "In class IX (Chapter 2), you have learnt that at the macroscopic or bulk level, matter can be classified as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mixture", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pure substance", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". These can be further sub-divided as shown in Fig. 1.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19037,52,24,'paragraph',NULL,'When all constituent particles of a substance are same in chemical nature, it is said to be a pure substance. A mixture contains many types of particles.','[{"s": "When all constituent particles of a substance are same in chemical nature, it is said to be a pure substance. A mixture contains many types of particles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22976,58,232,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Photoelectric effect is the phenomenon of emission of electrons by metals when illuminated by light of suitable frequency. Certain metals respond to ultraviolet light while others are sensitive even to the visible light.','[{"s": "2. Photoelectric effect is the phenomenon of emission of electrons by metals when illuminated by light of suitable frequency. Certain metals respond to ultraviolet light while others are sensitive even to the visible light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22977,58,233,'paragraph',NULL,'Photoelectric effect involves conversion of light energy into electrical energy. It follows the law of conservation of energy. The photoelectric emission is an instantaneous process and possesses certain special features.','[{"s": "Photoelectric effect involves conversion of light energy into electrical energy. It follows the law of conservation of energy. The photoelectric emission is an instantaneous process and possesses certain special features.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19038,52,25,'paragraph',NULL,'A mixture contains particles of two or more pure substances which may be present in it in any ratio. Hence, their composition is variable. Pure sustances forming mixture are called its components. Many of the substances present around you are mixtures . For example, sugar solution in water, air, tea, etc., are all mixtures. A mixture may be homogeneous or heterogeneous . In a homogeneous mixture , the components completely mix with each other. This means particles of components of the mixture are uniformly distributed throughout the bulk of the mixture and its composition is uniform throughout. Sugar solution and air are the examples of homogeneous mixtures. In contrast to this, in a heterogeneous mixture , the composition is not uniform throughout and sometimes different components are visible. For example, mixtures of salt and sugar, grains and pulses along with some dirt (often stone pieces), are heterogeneous mixtures. You can think of many more examples of mixtures which you come across in the daily life. It is worthwhile to mention here that the components of a mixture can be separated by using physical methods, such as simple hand-picking, filtration, crystallisation, distillation, etc.','[{"s": "A mixture", "t": "text"}, {"s": "contains particles of two or more pure substances which may be present in it in any ratio. Hence, their composition is variable. Pure sustances forming mixture are called its components. Many of the substances present around you are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mixtures", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For example, sugar solution in water, air, tea, etc., are all mixtures. A mixture may be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "homogeneous", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "heterogeneous", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "homogeneous mixture", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the components completely mix with each other. This means particles of components of the mixture are uniformly distributed throughout the bulk of the mixture and its composition is uniform throughout. Sugar solution and air are the examples of homogeneous mixtures. In contrast to this, in a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "heterogeneous mixture", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the composition is not uniform throughout and sometimes different components are visible. For example, mixtures of salt and sugar, grains and pulses along with some dirt (often stone pieces), are heterogeneous mixtures. You can think of many more examples of mixtures which you come across in the daily life. It is worthwhile to mention here that the components of a mixture can be separated by using physical methods, such as simple hand-picking, filtration, crystallisation, distillation, etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19039,52,26,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image55.png'),
(19040,52,27,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.2 Classification of matter','[{"s": "Fig. 1.2 Classification of matter", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19041,52,28,'paragraph',NULL,'Pure substances have characteristics different from mixtures. Constituent particles of pure substances have fixed composition. Copper, silver, gold, water and glucose are some examples of pure substances. Glucose contains carbon, hydrogen and oxygen in a fixed ratio and its particles are of same composition. Hence, like all other pure substances, glucose has a fixed composition. Also, its constituents—carbon, hydrogen and oxygen—cannot be separated by simple physical methods.','[{"s": "Pure substances", "t": "text"}, {"s": "have characteristics different from mixtures. Constituent particles of pure substances have fixed composition. Copper, silver, gold, water and glucose are some examples of pure substances. Glucose contains carbon, hydrogen and oxygen in a fixed ratio and its particles are of same composition. Hence, like all other pure substances, glucose has a fixed composition. Also, its constituents—carbon, hydrogen and oxygen—cannot be separated by simple physical methods.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19042,52,29,'paragraph',NULL,'Pure substances can further be classified into elements and compounds . Particles of an element consist of only one type of atoms. These particles may exist as atoms or molecules . You may be familiar with atoms and molecules from the previous classes; however, you will be studying about them in detail in Unit 2. Sodium, copper, silver, hydrogen, oxygen, etc., are some examples of elements. Their all atoms are of one type. However, the atoms of different elements are different in nature. Some elements, such as sodium or copper, contain atoms as their constituent particles, whereas, in some others, the constituent particles are molecules which are formed by two or more atoms. For example, hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen gases consist of molecules, in which two atoms combine to give their respective molecules. This is illustrated in Fig. 1.3.','[{"s": "Pure substances can further be classified into", "t": "text"}, {"s": "elements", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "compounds", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Particles of an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "element", "t": "text"}, {"s": "consist of only one type of atoms. These particles may exist as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atoms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". You may be familiar with atoms and molecules from the previous classes; however, you will be studying about them in detail in Unit 2. Sodium, copper, silver, hydrogen, oxygen, etc., are some examples of elements. Their all atoms are of one type. However, the atoms of different elements are different in nature. Some elements, such as sodium or copper, contain atoms as their constituent particles, whereas, in some others, the constituent particles are molecules which are formed by two or more atoms. For example, hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen gases consist of molecules, in which two atoms combine to give their respective molecules. This is illustrated in Fig. 1.3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19043,52,30,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image24.png'),
(19044,52,31,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.3 A representation of atoms and molecules','[{"s": "Fig. 1.3 A representation of atoms and molecules", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19045,52,32,'paragraph',NULL,'When two or more atoms of different elements combine together in a definite ratio, the molecule of a compound is obtained. Moreover, the constituents of a compound cannot be separated into simpler substances by physical methods. They can be separated by chemical methods. Examples of some compounds are water, ammonia, carbon dioxide, sugar, etc. The molecules of water and carbon dioxide are represented in Fig. 1.4.','[{"s": "When two or more atoms of different elements combine together in a definite ratio, the molecule of a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "compound", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is obtained. Moreover, the constituents of a compound cannot be separated into simpler substances by physical methods. They can be separated by chemical methods. Examples of some compounds are water, ammonia, carbon dioxide, sugar, etc. The molecules of water and carbon dioxide are represented in Fig. 1.4.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19046,52,33,'image',NULL,'1a111',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image58.png'),
(19047,52,34,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.4 A depiction of molecules of water and carbon dioxide','[{"s": "Fig. 1.4 A depiction of molecules of water and carbon dioxide", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19048,52,35,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that a water molecule comprises two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Similarly, a molecule of carbon dioxide contains two oxygen atoms combined with one carbon atom. Thus, the atoms of different elements are present in a compound in a fixed and definite ratio and this ratio is characteristic of a particular compound. Also, the properties of a compound are different from those of its constituent elements. For example, hydrogen and oxygen are gases, whereas, the compound formed by their combination i.e., water is a liquid. It is interesting to note that hydrogen burns with a pop sound and oxygen is a supporter of combustion, but water is used as a fire extinguisher.','[{"s": "Note that a water molecule comprises two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Similarly, a molecule of carbon dioxide contains two oxygen atoms combined with one carbon atom. Thus, the atoms of different elements are present in a compound in a fixed and definite ratio and this ratio is characteristic of a particular compound. Also, the properties of a compound are different from those of its constituent elements. For example, hydrogen and oxygen are gases, whereas, the compound formed by their combination i.e., water is a liquid. It is interesting to note that hydrogen burns with a pop sound and oxygen is a supporter of combustion, but water is used as a fire extinguisher.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19049,52,36,'paragraph',NULL,'Moreover, the constituents of a compound cannot be separated into simpler substances by physical methods. They can be separated by chemical methods.','[{"s": "Moreover, the constituents of a compound cannot be separated into simpler substances by physical methods. They can be separated by chemical methods.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19050,52,37,'heading',3,'1.3 Properties of Matter and their Measurement',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19051,52,38,'heading',4,'1.3.1 Physical and chemical properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19052,52,39,'paragraph',NULL,'Every substance has unique or characteristic properties. These properties can be classified into two categories — physical properties , such as colour, odour, melting point, boiling point, density, etc., and chemical properties, like composition, combustibility, ractivity with acids and bases, etc.','[{"s": "Every substance has unique or characteristic properties. These properties can be classified into two categories —", "t": "text"}, {"s": "physical properties", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "such as colour, odour, melting point, boiling point, density, etc., and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "chemical properties,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "like composition, combustibility, ractivity with acids and bases, etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19053,52,40,'paragraph',NULL,'Physical properties can be measured or observed without changing the identity or the composition of the substance. The measurement or observation of chemical properties requires a chemical change to occur. Measurement of physical properties does not require occurance of a chemical change. The examples of chemical properties are characteristic reactions of different substances; these include acidity or basicity, combustibility, etc. Chemists describe, interpret and predict the behaviour of substances on the basis of knowledge of their physical and chemical properties, which are determined by careful measurement and experimentation. In the following section, we will learn about the measurement of physical properties.','[{"s": "Physical properties", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be measured or observed without changing the identity or the composition of the substance. The measurement or observation of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "chemical properties", "t": "text"}, {"s": "requires a chemical change to occur. Measurement of physical properties does not require occurance of a chemical change. The examples of chemical properties are characteristic reactions of different substances; these include acidity or basicity, combustibility, etc. Chemists describe, interpret and predict the behaviour of substances on the basis of knowledge of their physical and chemical properties, which are determined by careful measurement and experimentation. In the following section, we will learn about the measurement of physical properties.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19054,52,41,'heading',4,'1.3.2 Measurement of physical properties',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19055,52,42,'paragraph',NULL,'Quantitative measurement of properties is reaquired for scientific investigation. Many properties of matter, such as length, area, volume, etc., are quantitative in nature. Any quantitative observation or measurement is represented by a number followed by units in which it is measured. For example, length of a room can be represented as 6 m; here, 6 is the number and m denotes metre, the unit in which the length is measured.','[{"s": "Quantitative measurement of properties is reaquired for scientific investigation. Many properties of matter, such as length, area, volume, etc., are quantitative in nature. Any quantitative observation or measurement is represented by a number followed by units in which it is measured. For example, length of a room can be represented as 6 m; here, 6 is the number and m denotes metre, the unit in which the length is measured.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19056,52,43,'paragraph',NULL,'Earlier, two different systems of measurement, i.e., the English System and the Metric System were being used in different parts of the world. The metric system, which originated in France in late eighteenth century, was more convenient as it was based on the decimal system. Late, need of a common standard system was felt by the scientific community. Such a system was established in 1960 and is discussed in detail below.','[{"s": "Earlier, two different systems of measurement, i.e., the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "English System", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Metric System", "t": "text"}, {"s": "were being used in different parts of the world. The metric system, which originated in France in late eighteenth century, was more convenient as it was based on the decimal system. Late, need of a common standard system was felt by the scientific community. Such a system was established in 1960 and is discussed in detail below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19057,52,44,'paragraph',NULL,'Maintaining the National','[{"s": "Maintaining the National", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19058,52,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Standards of Measurement','[{"s": "Standards of Measurement", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19059,52,46,'heading',4,'1.3.3 The International System of Units (SI)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19060,52,47,'paragraph',NULL,'The International System of Units (in French Le Systeme International d’Unités — abbreviated as SI) was established by the11th General Conference on Weights and Measures (CGPM from ConferenceGenerale des Poids et Measures). The CGPM is an inter-governmental treaty organisation created by a diplomatic treaty known as Metre Convention, which was signed in Paris in 1875.','[{"s": "The International System of Units (in French Le Systeme International d’Unités — abbreviated as SI) was established by the11th General Conference on Weights and Measures (CGPM from ConferenceGenerale des Poids et Measures). The CGPM is an inter-governmental treaty organisation created by a diplomatic treaty known as Metre Convention, which was signed in Paris in 1875.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19061,52,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 1.1 Base Physical Quantities and their Units','[{"s": "Table 1.1 Base Physical Quantities and their Units", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19062,52,49,'image',NULL,'1a2',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image65.png'),
(19063,52,50,'paragraph',NULL,'The SI system has seven base units and they are listed in Table 1.1. These units pertain to the seven fundamental scientific quantities. The other physical quantities, such as speed, volume, density, etc., can be derived from these quantities.','[{"s": "The SI system has seven", "t": "text"}, {"s": "base units and they are listed in Table 1.1. These units pertain to the seven fundamental scientific quantities. The other physical quantities, such as speed, volume, density, etc., can be derived from these quantities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19064,52,51,'paragraph',NULL,'The definitions of the SI base units are given in Table 1.2.','[{"s": "The definitions of the SI base units are given in Table 1.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19065,52,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us now quickly go through some of the quantities which you will be often using in this book.','[{"s": "Let us now quickly go through some of the quantities which you will be often using in this book.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19066,52,53,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 1.2 Definitions of SI Base Units','[{"s": "Table 1.2 Definitions of SI Base Units", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19067,52,54,'image',NULL,'1q33',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image16.png'),
(19068,52,55,'paragraph',NULL,'*Triple point of water is 0.01 °C or 279.16K (32.01 ° F)','[{"s": "*Triple point of water is 0.01 °C or 279.16K (32.01 ° F)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19069,52,56,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 1.3 Prefixes used in the SI System','[{"s": "Table 1.3 Prefixes used in the SI System", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19070,52,57,'image',NULL,'1a44',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image7.png'),
(19071,52,58,'paragraph',NULL,'The SI system allows the use of prefixes to indicate the multiples or submultiples of a unit. These prefixes are listed in Table 1.3.','[{"s": "The SI system allows the use of prefixes to indicate the multiples or submultiples of a unit. These prefixes are listed in Table 1.3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19072,52,59,'heading',4,'1.3.4 Mass and Weight',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19073,52,60,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass of a substance is the amount of matter present in it, while weight is the force exerted by gravity on an object. The mass of a substance is constant, whereas, its weight may vary from one place to another due to change in gravity. You should be careful in using these terms.','[{"s": "Mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of a substance is the amount of matter present in it, while", "t": "text"}, {"s": "weight", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the force exerted by gravity on an object. The mass of a substance is constant, whereas, its weight may vary from one place to another due to change in gravity. You should be careful in using these terms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19074,52,61,'paragraph',NULL,'The mass of a substance can be determined accurately in the laboratory by using an analytical balance (Fig. 1.5).','[{"s": "The mass of a substance can be determined accurately in the laboratory by using an analytical balance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Fig. 1.5).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19075,52,62,'paragraph',NULL,'The SI unit of mass as given in Table 1.1 is kilogram. However, its fraction named as gram (1 kg = 1000 g), is used in laboratories due to the smaller amounts of chemicals used in chemical reactions.','[{"s": "The SI unit of mass as given in Table 1.1 is kilogram. However, its fraction named as gram (1 kg = 1000 g), is used in laboratories due to the smaller amounts of chemicals used in chemical reactions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19076,52,63,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image33.png'),
(19077,52,64,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.5 Analytical balance','[{"s": "Fig. 1.5 Analytical balance", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19078,52,65,'heading',4,'1.3.5 Volume',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19079,52,66,'paragraph',NULL,'Volume is the amont of space occupied by a substance. It has the units of (length) 3 . So in SI system, volume has units of m 3 . But again, in chemistry laboratories, smaller volumes are used. Hence, volume is often denoted in cm 3 or dm 3 units.','[{"s": "Volume is the amont of space occupied by a substance. It has the units of (length)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". So in SI system, volume has units of m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". But again, in chemistry laboratories, smaller volumes are used. Hence, volume is often denoted in cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or dm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "units.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19080,52,67,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.6 helps to visualise these relations.','[{"s": "Fig. 1.6 helps to visualise these relations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19081,52,68,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image44.png'),
(19082,52,69,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.6 Different units used to express volume','[{"s": "Fig. 1.6 Different units used to express volume", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19083,52,70,'paragraph',NULL,'A common unit, litre (L) which is not an SI unit, is used for measurement of volume of liquids.','[{"s": "A common unit, litre (L) which is not an SI unit, is used for measurement of volume of liquids.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19084,52,71,'paragraph',NULL,'1 L = 1000 mL , 1000 cm 3 = 1 dm 3','[{"s": "1 L = 1000 mL , 1000 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1 dm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19085,52,72,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image34.png'),
(19086,52,73,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.7 Some volume measuring devices','[{"s": "Fig. 1.7 Some volume measuring devices", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19087,52,74,'paragraph',NULL,'In the laboratory, the volume of liquids or solutions can be measured by graduated cylinder, burette, pipette, etc. A volumetric flask is used to prepare a known volume of a solution. These measuring devices are shown in Fig. 1.7.','[{"s": "In the laboratory, the volume of liquids or solutions can be measured by graduated cylinder, burette, pipette, etc.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A volumetric flask is used to prepare a known volume of a solution. These measuring devices are shown in Fig. 1.7.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19088,52,75,'heading',4,'1.3.6 Density',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19089,52,76,'paragraph',NULL,'The two properties — mass and volume discussed above are related as follows:','[{"s": "The two properties — mass and volume discussed above are related as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19090,52,77,'image',NULL,'1q22336',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image13.png'),
(19091,52,78,'paragraph',NULL,'Density of a substance is its amount of mass per unit volume. So, SI units of density can be obtained as follows:','[{"s": "Density of a substance is its amount of mass per unit volume. So, SI units of density can be obtained as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19092,52,79,'image',NULL,'1e4377',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image23.png'),
(19093,52,80,'paragraph',NULL,'This unit is quite large and a chemist often expresses density in g cm –3 , where mass is expressed in gram and volume is expressed in cm 3 . Density of a substance tells us about how closely its particles are packed. If density is more, it means particles are more closely packed.','[{"s": "This unit is quite large and a chemist often expresses density in g cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where mass is expressed in gram and volume is expressed in cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Density of a substance tells us about how closely its particles are packed. If density is more, it means particles are more closely packed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19094,52,81,'heading',4,'1.3.7 Temperature',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19095,52,82,'paragraph',NULL,'There are three common scales to measure temperature — °C (degree celsius), °F (degree fahrenheit) and K (kelvin). Here, K is the SI unit. The thermometers based on these scales are shown in Fig. 1.8. Generally, the thermometer with celsius scale are calibrated from 0° to 100°, where these two temperatures are the freezing point and the boiling point of water, respectively. The fahrenheit scale is represented between 32° to 212°.','[{"s": "There are three common scales to measure temperature — °C (degree celsius), °F (degree fahrenheit) and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K (kelvin). Here, K is the SI unit.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The thermometers based on these scales are shown in Fig. 1.8. Generally, the thermometer with celsius scale are calibrated from 0° to 100°, where these two temperatures are the freezing point and the boiling point of water, respectively. The fahrenheit scale is represented between 32° to 212°.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19096,52,83,'paragraph',NULL,'The temperatures on two scales are related to each other by the following relationship:','[{"s": "The temperatures on two scales are related to each other by the following relationship:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19097,52,84,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image38.png'),
(19098,52,85,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image64.png'),
(19099,52,86,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.8 Thermometers using different temperature scales','[{"s": "Fig. 1.8 Thermometers using different temperature scales", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19100,52,87,'paragraph',NULL,'The kelvin scale is related to celsius scale as follows:','[{"s": "The kelvin scale is related to celsius scale as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19101,52,88,'paragraph',NULL,'K = °C + 273.15','[{"s": "K = °C + 273.15", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19102,52,89,'paragraph',NULL,'It is interesting to note that temperature below 0 °C (i.e., negative values) are possible in Celsius scale but in Kelvin scale, negative temperature is not possible.','[{"s": "It is interesting to note that temperature below 0 °C (i.e., negative values) are possible in Celsius scale but in Kelvin scale, negative temperature is not possible.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19103,52,90,'paragraph',NULL,'Reference Standard','[{"s": "Reference Standard", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19104,52,91,'paragraph',NULL,'After defining a unit of measurement such as the kilogram or the meter, scientists agreed on reference standards that make it possible to calibrate all measuring devices. For getting reliable measurements, all devices such as meter sticks and analytical balances have been calibrated by their manufacturers to give correct readings. However, each of these devices is stanhttp://uidstart-11216-uidenddardised or calibrated against some reference. The mass standard is the kilogram since 1889. It has been defined as the mass of platinum-iridium (Pt-Ir) cylinder that is stored in an airtight jar at International Bureau of Weights and Measures in Sevres, France. Pt-Ir was chosen for this standard because it is highly resistant to chemical attack and its mass will not change for an extremely long time.','[{"s": "After defining a unit of measurement such as the kilogram or the meter, scientists agreed on reference standards that make it possible to calibrate all measuring devices. For getting reliable measurements, all devices such as meter sticks and analytical balances have been calibrated by their manufacturers to give correct readings. However, each of these devices is stanhttp://uidstart-11216-uidenddardised or calibrated against some reference. The mass standard is the kilogram since 1889. It has been defined as the mass of platinum-iridium (Pt-Ir) cylinder that is stored in an airtight jar at International Bureau of Weights and Measures in Sevres, France. Pt-Ir was chosen for this standard because it is highly resistant to chemical attack and its mass will not change for an extremely long time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19105,52,92,'paragraph',NULL,'Scientists are in search of a new standard for mass. This is being attempted through accurate determination of Avogadro constant. Work on this new standard focuses on ways to measure accurately the number of atoms in a well-defined mass of sample. One such method, which uses X-rays to determine the atomic density of a crystal of ultrapure silicon, has an accuracy of about 1 part in 10 6 but has not yet been adopted to serve as a standard. There are other methods but none of them are presently adequate to replace the Pt-Ir cylinder . No doubt, changes are expected within this decade.','[{"s": "Scientists are in search of a new standard for mass. This is being attempted through accurate determination of Avogadro constant. Work on this new standard focuses on ways to measure accurately the number of atoms in a well-defined mass of sample. One such method, which uses X-rays to determine the atomic density of a crystal of ultrapure silicon, has an accuracy of about 1 part in 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "but has not yet been adopted to serve as a standard. There are other methods but none of them are presently adequate to replace the Pt-Ir cylinder", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". No doubt, changes are expected within this decade.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19106,52,93,'paragraph',NULL,'The metre was originally defined as the length between two marks on a Pt-Ir bar kept at a temperature of 0°C (273.15 K). In 1960 the length of the metre was defined as 1.65076373 × 10 6 times the wavelength of light emitted by a krypton laser. Although this was a cumbersome number, it preserved the length of the metre at its agreed value. The metre was redefined in 1983 by CGPM as the length of path travelled by light in vacuum during a time interval of 1/299 792 458 of a second. Similar to the length and the mass, there are reference standards for other physical quantities.','[{"s": "The metre was originally defined as the length between two marks on a Pt-Ir bar kept at a temperature of 0°C (273.15 K).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In 1960 the length of the metre was defined as 1.65076373 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "times the wavelength of light emitted by a krypton laser. Although this was a cumbersome number, it preserved the length of the metre at its agreed value.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The metre was redefined in 1983 by CGPM as the length of path travelled by light in vacuum during a time interval of 1/299 792 458 of a second. Similar to the length and the mass, there are reference standards for other physical quantities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19107,52,94,'heading',3,'1.4 Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19108,52,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Many a time in the study of chemistry, one has to deal with experimental data as well as theoretical calculations. There are meaningful ways to handle the numbers conveniently and present the data realistically with certainty to the extent possible. These ideas are discussed below in detail.','[{"s": "Many a time in the study of chemistry, one has to deal with experimental data as well as theoretical calculations. There are meaningful ways to handle the numbers conveniently and present the data realistically with certainty to the extent possible. These ideas are discussed below in detail.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19109,52,96,'heading',4,'1.4.1 Scientific Notation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19110,52,97,'paragraph',NULL,'As chemistry is the study of atoms and molecules, which have extremely low masses and are present in extremely large numbers, a chemist has to deal with numbers as large as 602, 200,000,000,000,000,000,000 for the molecules of 2 g of hydrogen gas or as small as 0.00000000000000000000000166 g mass of a H atom. Similarly, other constants such as Planck’s constant, speed of light, charges on particles, etc., involve numbers of the above magnitude.','[{"s": "As chemistry is the study of atoms and molecules, which have extremely low masses and are present in extremely large numbers, a chemist has to deal with numbers as large as 602, 200,000,000,000,000,000,000 for the molecules of 2 g of hydrogen gas or as small as 0.00000000000000000000000166 g mass of a H atom. Similarly, other constants such as Planck’s constant, speed of light, charges on particles, etc., involve numbers of the above magnitude.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19111,52,98,'paragraph',NULL,'It may look funny for a moment to write or count numbers involving so many zeros but it offers a real challenge to do simple mathematical operations of addition, subtraction, multiplication or division with such numbers. You can write any two numbers of the above type and try any one of the operations you like to accept as a challenge, and then, you will really appreciate the difficulty in handling such numbers.','[{"s": "It may look funny for a moment to write or count numbers involving so many zeros but it offers a real challenge to do simple mathematical operations of addition, subtraction, multiplication or division with such numbers. You can write any two numbers of the above type and try any one of the operations you like to accept as a challenge, and then, you will really appreciate the difficulty in handling such numbers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19112,52,99,'paragraph',NULL,'This problem is solved by using scientific notation for such numbers, i.e., exponential notation in which any number can be represented in the form N × 10 n , where n is an exponent having positive or negative values and N is a number (called digit term) which varies between 1.000... and 9.999....','[{"s": "This problem is solved by using scientific notation for such numbers, i.e., exponential notation in which any number can be represented in the form N × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where n is an exponent having positive or negative values and N is a number (called digit term) which varies between 1.000... and 9.999....", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19113,52,100,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, we can write 232.508 as 2.32508 ×10 2 in scientific notation. Note that while writing it, the decimal had to be moved to the left by two places and same is the exponent (2) of 10 in the scientific notation.','[{"s": "Thus, we can write 232.508 as 2.32508 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in scientific notation. Note that while writing it, the decimal had to be moved to the left by two places and same is the exponent (2) of 10 in the scientific notation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19114,52,101,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, 0.00016 can be written as 1.6 × 10 –4 . Here, the decimal has to be moved four places to the right and (–4) is the exponent in the scientific notation.','[{"s": "Similarly, 0.00016 can be written as 1.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Here, the decimal has to be moved four places to the right and (–4) is the exponent in the scientific notation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19115,52,102,'paragraph',NULL,'While performing mathematical operations on numbers expressed in scientific notations, the following points are to be kept in mind.','[{"s": "While performing mathematical operations on numbers expressed in scientific notations, the following points are to be kept in mind.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19116,52,103,'paragraph',NULL,'Multiplication and Division','[{"s": "Multiplication and Division", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19117,52,104,'paragraph',NULL,'These two operations follow the same rules which are there for exponential numbers, i.e.','[{"s": "These two operations follow the same rules which are there for exponential numbers, i.e.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19118,52,105,'image',NULL,'11e32222',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image20.png'),
(19119,52,106,'paragraph',NULL,'Addition and Subtraction','[{"s": "Addition and Subtraction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19120,52,107,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, for adding 6.65 × 10 4 and 8.95 × 10 3 , exponent is made same for both the numbers. Thus, we get (6.65 × 10 4 ) + (0.895 × 10 4 )','[{"s": "Thus, for adding 6.65 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 8.95 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", exponent is made same for both the numbers. Thus, we get (6.65 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") + (0.895 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(19121,52,108,'paragraph',NULL,'Then, these numbers can be added as follows (6.65 + 0.895) × 10 4 = 7.545 × 10 4','[{"s": "Then, these numbers can be added as follows (6.65 + 0.895) × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 7.545 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19122,52,109,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, the subtraction of two numbers can be done as shown below:','[{"s": "Similarly, the subtraction of two numbers can be done as shown below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19123,52,110,'paragraph',NULL,'(2.5 × 10 –2 ) – (4.8 × 10 –3 )','[{"s": "(2.5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") – (4.8 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19124,52,111,'paragraph',NULL,'= (2.5 × 10 –2 ) – (0.48 × 10 –2 )','[{"s": "= (2.5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") – (0.48 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19125,52,112,'paragraph',NULL,'= (2.5 – 0.48) × 10 –2 = 2.02 × 10 –2','[{"s": "= (2.5 – 0.48) × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.02 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19126,52,113,'heading',4,'1.4.2 Significant Figures',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22978,58,234,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Photoelectric current depends on (i) the intensity of incident light, (ii) the potential difference applied between the two electrodes, and (iii) the nature of the emitter material.','[{"s": "3. Photoelectric current depends on (i) the intensity of incident light, (ii) the potential difference applied between the two electrodes, and (iii) the nature of the emitter material.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19127,52,114,'paragraph',NULL,'Every experimental measurement has some amount of uncertainty associated with it because of limitation of measuring instrument and the skill of the person making the measurement. For example, mass of an object is obtained using a platform balance and it comes out to be 9.4g. On measuring the mass of this object on an analytical balance, the mass obtained is 9.4213g. The mass obtained by an analytical balance is slightly higher than the mass obtained by using a platform balance. Therefore, digit 4 placed after decimal in the measurement by platform balance is uncertain.','[{"s": "Every experimental measurement has some amount of uncertainty associated with it because of limitation of measuring instrument and the skill of the person making the measurement.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For example, mass of an object is obtained using a platform balance and it comes out to be 9.4g. On measuring the mass of this object on an analytical balance, the mass obtained is 9.4213g. The mass obtained by an analytical balance is slightly higher than the mass obtained by using a platform balance. Therefore, digit 4 placed after decimal in the measurement by platform balance is uncertain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19128,52,115,'paragraph',NULL,'The uncertainty in the experimental or the calculated values is indicated by mentioning the number of significant figures. Significant figures are meaningful digits which are known with certainty plus one which is estimated or uncertain. The uncertainty is indicated by writing the certain digits and the last uncertain digit. Thus, if we write a result as 11.2 mL, we say the 11 is certain and 2 is uncertain and the uncertainty would be +1 in the last digit. Unless otherwise stated, an uncertainty of +1 in the last digit is always understood.','[{"s": "The uncertainty in the experimental or the calculated values is indicated by mentioning the number of significant figures.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Significant figures", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are meaningful digits which are known with certainty plus one which is estimated or uncertain.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The uncertainty is indicated by writing the certain digits and the last uncertain digit. Thus, if we write a result as 11.2 mL, we say the 11 is certain and 2 is uncertain and the uncertainty would be +1 in the last digit. Unless otherwise stated, an uncertainty of +1 in the last digit is always understood.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19129,52,116,'paragraph',NULL,'There are certain rules for determining the number of significant figures. These are stated below:','[{"s": "There are certain rules for determining the number of significant figures. These are stated below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19130,52,117,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) All non-zero digits are significant. For example in 285 cm, there are three significant figures and in 0.25 mL, there are two significant figures.','[{"s": "(1) All non-zero digits are significant. For example in 285 cm, there are three significant figures and in 0.25 mL, there are two significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19131,52,118,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) Zeros preceding to first non-zero digit are not significant. Such zero indicates the position of decimal point. Thus, 0.03 has one significant figure and 0.0052 has two significant figures.','[{"s": "(2) Zeros preceding to first non-zero digit are not significant. Such zero indicates the position of decimal point. Thus, 0.03 has one significant figure and 0.0052 has two significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19132,52,119,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) Zeros between two non-zero digits are significant. Thus, 2.005 has four significant figures.','[{"s": "(3) Zeros between two non-zero digits are significant. Thus, 2.005 has four significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19133,52,120,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) Zeros at the end or right of a number are significant, provided they are on the right side of the decimal point. For example, 0.200 g has three significant figures. But, if otherwise, the terminal zeros are not significant if there is no decimal point. For example, 100 has only one significant figure, but 100. has three significant figures and 100.0 has four significant figures. Such numbers are better represented in scientific notation. We can express the number 100 as 1×10 2 for one significant figure, 1.0×10 2 for two significant figures and 1.00×10 2 for three significant figures.','[{"s": "(4) Zeros at the end or right of a number are significant, provided they are on the right side of the decimal point. For example, 0.200 g has three significant figures. But, if otherwise, the terminal zeros are not significant if there is no decimal point. For example, 100 has only one significant figure, but 100. has three significant figures and 100.0 has four significant figures. Such numbers are better represented in scientific notation. We can express the number 100 as 1×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for one significant figure, 1.0×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for two significant figures and 1.00×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for three significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19134,52,121,'paragraph',NULL,'(5) Counting the numbers of object, for example, 2 balls or 20 eggs, have infinite significant figures as these are exact numbers and can be represented by writing infinite number of zeros after placing a decimal i.e., 2 = 2.000000 or 20 = 20.000000.','[{"s": "(5) Counting the numbers of object, for example, 2 balls or 20 eggs, have infinite significant figures as these are exact numbers and can be represented by writing infinite number of zeros after placing a decimal i.e., 2 = 2.000000 or 20 = 20.000000.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19135,52,122,'paragraph',NULL,'In numbers written in scientific notation, all digits are significant e.g., 4.01×10 2 has three significant figures, and 8.256 × 10 –3 has four significant figures.','[{"s": "In numbers written in scientific notation, all digits are significant e.g., 4.01×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has three significant figures, and 8.256 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has four significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19136,52,123,'paragraph',NULL,'However, one would always like the results to be precise and accurate. Precision and accuracy are often referred to while we talk about the measurement.','[{"s": "However, one would always like the results to be precise and accurate. Precision and accuracy are often referred to while we talk about the measurement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22979,58,235,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The stopping potential (V o 0 = (1/2) m v 2 max = K max .','[{"s": "4. The stopping potential (V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (1/2) m v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "max", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "max", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19137,52,124,'paragraph',NULL,'Precision refers to the closeness of various measurements for the same quantity. However, accuracy is the agreement of a particular value to the true value of the result. For example, if the true value for a result is 2.00 g and student ‘A’ takes two measurements and reports the results as 1.95 g and 1.93 g. These values are precise as they are close to each other but are not accurate. Another student ‘B’ repeats the experiment and obtains 1.94 g and 2.05 g as the results for two measurements. These observations are neither precise nor accurate. When the third student ‘C’ repeats these measurements and reports 2.01 g and 1.99 g as the result, these values are both precise and accurate. This can be more clearly understood from the data given in Table 1.4.','[{"s": "Precision", "t": "text"}, {"s": "refers to the closeness of various measurements for the same quantity. However,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "accuracy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the agreement of a particular value to the true value of the result. For example, if the true value for a result is 2.00 g and student ‘A’ takes two measurements and reports the results as 1.95 g and 1.93 g. These values are precise as they are close to each other but are not accurate. Another student ‘B’ repeats the experiment and obtains 1.94 g and 2.05 g as the results for two measurements. These observations are neither precise nor accurate. When the third student ‘C’ repeats these measurements and reports 2.01 g and 1.99 g as the result, these values are both precise and accurate. This can be more clearly understood from the data given in Table 1.4.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19138,52,125,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 1.4 Data to Illustrate Precision and Accuracy','[{"s": "Table 1.4 Data to Illustrate Precision and Accuracy", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19139,52,126,'image',NULL,'ww322111',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image68.png'),
(19140,52,127,'paragraph',NULL,'Addition and Subtraction of Significant Figures','[{"s": "Addition and Subtraction of Significant Figures", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19141,52,128,'paragraph',NULL,'The result cannot have more digits to the right of the decimal point than either of the original numbers.','[{"s": "The result cannot have more digits to the right of the decimal point than either of the original numbers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19142,52,129,'paragraph',NULL,'12.11','[{"s": "12.11", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19143,52,130,'paragraph',NULL,'18.0','[{"s": "18.0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19144,52,131,'paragraph',NULL,'1.012','[{"s": "1.012", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19145,52,132,'paragraph',NULL,'31.122','[{"s": "31.122", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19146,52,133,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, 18.0 has only one digit after the decimal point and the result should be reported only up to one digit after the decimal point, which is 31.1.','[{"s": "Here, 18.0 has only one digit after the decimal point and the result should be reported only up to one digit after the decimal point, which is 31.1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19147,52,134,'paragraph',NULL,'Multiplication and Division of Significant Figures','[{"s": "Multiplication and Division of Significant Figures", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19148,52,135,'paragraph',NULL,'In these operations, the result must be reported with no more significant figures as in the measurement with the few significant figures.','[{"s": "In these operations, the result must be reported with no more significant figures as in the measurement with the few significant figures.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["11152"]',NULL),
(19149,52,136,'paragraph',NULL,'2.5×1.25 = 3.125','[{"s": "2.5×1.25 = 3.125", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19150,52,137,'paragraph',NULL,'Since 2.5 has two significant figures, the result should not have more than two significant figures, thus, it is 3.1.','[{"s": "Since 2.5 has two significant figures, the result should not have more than two significant figures, thus, it is 3.1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19151,52,138,'paragraph',NULL,'While limiting the result to the required number of significant figures as done in the above mathematical operation, one has to keep in mind the following points for rounding off the numbers','[{"s": "While limiting the result to the required number of significant figures as done in the above mathematical operation, one has to keep in mind the following points for rounding off the numbers", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19152,52,139,'paragraph',NULL,'1. If the rightmost digit to be removed is more than 5, the preceding number is increased by one. For example, 1.386. If we have to remove 6, we have to round it to 1.39.','[{"s": "1. If the rightmost digit to be removed is more than 5, the preceding number is increased by one. For example, 1.386. If we have to remove 6, we have to round it to 1.39.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19153,52,140,'paragraph',NULL,'2. If the rightmost digit to be removed is less than 5, the preceding number is not changed. For example, 4.334 if 4 is to be removed, then the result is rounded upto 4.33.','[{"s": "2. If the rightmost digit to be removed is less than 5, the preceding number is not changed. For example, 4.334 if 4 is to be removed, then the result is rounded upto 4.33.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19154,52,141,'paragraph',NULL,'3. If the rightmost digit to be removed is 5, then the preceding number is not changed if it is an even number but it is increased by one if it is an odd number. For example, if 6.35 is to be rounded by removing 5, we have to increase 3 to 4 giving 6.4 as the result. However, if 6.25 is to be rounded off it is rounded off to 6.2.','[{"s": "3. If the rightmost digit to be removed is 5, then the preceding number is not changed if it is an even number but it is increased by one if it is an odd number. For example, if 6.35 is to be rounded by removing 5, we have to increase 3 to 4 giving 6.4 as the result. However, if 6.25 is to be rounded off it is rounded off to 6.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19155,52,142,'heading',4,'1.4.3 Dimensional Analysis',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19156,52,143,'paragraph',NULL,'Often while calculating, there is a need to convert units from one system to the other. The method used to accomplish this is called factor label method or unit factor method or dimensional analysis . This is illustrated below.','[{"s": "Often while calculating, there is a need to convert units from one system to the other. The method used to accomplish this is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "factor label method", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "unit factor method", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dimensional analysis", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This is illustrated below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19157,52,144,'paragraph',NULL,'Example','[{"s": "Example", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19158,52,145,'paragraph',NULL,'A piece of metal is 3 inch (represented by in) long. What is its length in cm?','[{"s": "A piece of metal is 3 inch (represented by in) long. What is its length in cm?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19159,52,146,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19160,52,147,'paragraph',NULL,'We know that 1 in = 2.54 cm','[{"s": "We know that 1 in = 2.54 cm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19161,52,148,'paragraph',NULL,'From this equivalence, we can write','[{"s": "From this equivalence, we can write", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19162,52,149,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image50.png'),
(19163,52,150,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image53.png equals 1 and Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image2.png also equals 1. Both of these are called unit factors . If some number is multiplied by these unit factors (i.e., 1), it will not be affected otherwise.','[{"s": "Thus,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image53.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "equals 1 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image2.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "also equals 1. Both of these are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "unit factors", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If some number is multiplied by these unit factors (i.e., 1), it will not be affected otherwise.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19164,52,151,'paragraph',NULL,'Say, the 3 in given above is multiplied by the unit factor. So,','[{"s": "Say, the 3 in given above is multiplied by the unit factor. So,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19165,52,152,'paragraph',NULL,'3 in = 3 in × Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image2.png = 3 × 2.54 cm = 7.62 cm','[{"s": "3 in = 3 in ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image2.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 3 × 2.54 cm = 7.62 cm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19166,52,153,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, the unit factor by which multiplication is to be done is that unit factor ( Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image2.png in the above case) which gives the desired units i.e., the numerator should have that part which is required in the desired result.','[{"s": "Now, the unit factor by which multiplication is to be done is that unit factor (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image2.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "in the above case) which gives the desired units i.e., the numerator should have that part which is required in the desired result.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19167,52,154,'paragraph',NULL,'It should also be noted in the above example that units can be handled just like other numerical part. It can be cancelled, divided, multiplied, squared, etc. Let us study one more example.','[{"s": "It should also be noted in the above example that units can be handled just like other numerical part. It can be cancelled, divided, multiplied, squared, etc. Let us study one more example.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19168,52,155,'paragraph',NULL,'Example','[{"s": "Example", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19169,52,156,'paragraph',NULL,'A jug contains 2L of milk. Calculate the volume of the milk in m 3 .','[{"s": "A jug contains 2L of milk. Calculate the volume of the milk in m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19170,52,157,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19171,52,158,'paragraph',NULL,'Since 1 L = 1000 cm 3','[{"s": "Since 1 L = 1000 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19172,52,159,'paragraph',NULL,'and 1m = 100 cm, which gives Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image66.png','[{"s": "and 1m = 100 cm, which gives", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image66.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19173,52,160,'paragraph',NULL,'To get m 3 from the above unit factors, the first unit factor is taken and it is cubed.','[{"s": "To get m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from the above unit factors, the first unit factor is taken and it is cubed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
