(19190,52,177,'heading',3,'1.5 Laws of Chemical Combinations',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19191,52,178,'paragraph',NULL,'The combination of elements to form compounds is governed by the following five basic laws.','[{"s": "The combination of elements to form compounds is governed by the following five basic laws.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19192,52,179,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image1.png'),
(19193,52,180,'paragraph',NULL,'Antoine Lavoisier','[{"s": "Antoine Lavoisier", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19194,52,181,'paragraph',NULL,'(1743–1794)','[{"s": "(1743–1794)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19195,52,182,'heading',4,'1.5.1 Law of Conservation of Mass',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19196,52,183,'paragraph',NULL,'It states that matter can neither be created nor destroyed.','[{"s": "It states that matter can neither be created nor destroyed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19197,52,184,'paragraph',NULL,'This law was put forth by Antoine Lavoisier in 1789. He performed careful experimental studies for combustion reactions and reached to the conclusion that in all physical and chemical changes, there is no net change in mass duting the process. Hence, he reached to the conclusion that matter can neither be created nor destroyed. This is called ‘Law of Conservation of Mass’. This law formed the basis for several later developments in chemistry. Infact, this was the result of exact measurement of masses of reactants and products, and carefully planned experiments performed by Lavoisier.','[{"s": "This law was put forth by Antoine Lavoisier in 1789. He performed careful experimental studies for combustion reactions and reached to the conclusion that in all physical and chemical changes, there is no net change in mass duting the process. Hence, he reached to the conclusion that matter can neither be created nor destroyed. This is called ‘Law of Conservation of Mass’. This law formed the basis for several later developments in chemistry. Infact, this was the result of exact measurement of masses of reactants and products, and carefully planned experiments performed by Lavoisier.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19198,52,185,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image15.png'),
(19199,52,186,'paragraph',NULL,'Joseph Proust','[{"s": "Joseph Proust", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19200,52,187,'paragraph',NULL,'(1754–1826)','[{"s": "(1754–1826)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19201,52,188,'heading',4,'1.5.2 Law of Definite Proportions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19202,52,189,'paragraph',NULL,'This law was given by, a French chemist, Joseph Proust. He stated that a given compound always contains exactly the same proportion of elements by weight.','[{"s": "This law was given by, a French chemist, Joseph Proust. He stated that a given compound always contains exactly the same proportion of elements by weight.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19203,52,190,'paragraph',NULL,'Proust worked with two samples of cupric carbonate — one of which was of natural origin and the other was synthetic. He found that the composition of elements present in it was same for both the samples as shown below:','[{"s": "Proust worked with two samples of cupric carbonate — one of which was of natural origin and the other was synthetic. He found that the composition of elements present in it was same for both the samples as shown below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19204,52,191,'image',NULL,'1dsfs21',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image46.png'),
(19205,52,192,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, he concluded that irrespective of the source, a given compound always contains same elements combined together in the same proportion by mass. The validity of this law has been confirmed by various experiments. It is sometimes also referred to as Law of Definite Composition .','[{"s": "Thus, he concluded that irrespective of the source, a given compound always contains same elements combined together in the same proportion by mass. The validity of this law has been confirmed by various experiments. It is sometimes also referred to as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Law of Definite Composition", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19206,52,193,'heading',4,'1.5.3 Law of Multiple Proportions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19207,52,194,'paragraph',NULL,'This law was proposed by Dalton in 1803. According to this law, if two elements can combine to form more than one compound, the masses of one element that combine with a fixed mass of the other element, are in the ratio of small whole numbers.','[{"s": "This law was proposed by Dalton in 1803. According to this law, if two elements can combine to form more than one compound, the masses of one element that combine with a fixed mass of the other element, are in the ratio of small whole numbers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19208,52,195,'paragraph',NULL,'For example, hydrogen combines with oxygen to form two compounds, namely, water and hydrogen peroxide.','[{"s": "For example, hydrogen combines with oxygen to form two compounds, namely, water and hydrogen peroxide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19209,52,196,'image',NULL,'fhg35d',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image57.png'),
(19210,52,197,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, the masses of oxygen (i.e., 16 g and 32 g), which combine with a fixed mass of hydrogen (2g) bear a simple ratio, i.e., 16:32 or 1: 2.','[{"s": "Here,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the masses of oxygen (i.e., 16 g and 32 g), which combine with a fixed mass of hydrogen (2g) bear a simple ratio, i.e., 16:32 or 1: 2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19211,52,198,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image5.png'),
(19212,52,199,'paragraph',NULL,'Joseph Louis','[{"s": "Joseph Louis", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19213,52,200,'paragraph',NULL,'Gay Lussac','[{"s": "Gay Lussac", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19214,52,201,'heading',4,'1.5.4 Gay Lussac’s Law of Gaseous Volumes',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19215,52,202,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, 100 mL of hydrogen combine with 50 mL of oxygen to give 100 mL of water vapour.','[{"s": "Thus, 100 mL of hydrogen combine with 50 mL of oxygen to give 100 mL of water vapour.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19216,52,203,'image',NULL,'dsfwe23',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image35.png'),
(19217,52,204,'paragraph',NULL,'Gay Lussac’s discovery of integer ratio in volume relationship is actually the law of definite proportions by volume. The law of definite proportions, stated earlier, was with respect to mass. The Gay Lussac’s law was explained properly by the work of Avogadro in 1811.','[{"s": "Gay Lussac’s discovery of integer ratio in volume relationship is actually the law of definite proportions by volume. The law of definite proportions, stated earlier, was with respect to mass. The Gay Lussac’s law was explained properly by the work of Avogadro in 1811.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19218,52,205,'heading',4,'1.5.5 Avogadro’s Law',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22980,58,236,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Below a certain frequency (threshold frequency) ν 0 , characteristic of the metal, no photoelectric emission takes place, no matter how large the intensity may be.','[{"s": "5. Below a certain frequency (threshold frequency) ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", characteristic of the metal, no photoelectric emission takes place, no matter how large the intensity may be.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19219,52,206,'paragraph',NULL,'In 1811, Avogadro proposed that equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure should contain equal number of molecules. Avogadro made a distinction between atoms and molecules which is quite understandable in present times. If we consider again the reaction of hydrogen and oxygen to produce water, we see that two volumes of hydrogen combine with one volume of oxygen to give two volumes of water without leaving any unreacted oxygen.','[{"s": "In 1811, Avogadro proposed that equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure should contain equal number of molecules. Avogadro made a distinction between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atoms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which is quite understandable in present times. If we consider again the reaction of hydrogen and oxygen to produce water, we see that two", "t": "text"}, {"s": "volumes of hydrogen combine with one volume of oxygen to give two volumes of water without leaving any unreacted oxygen.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19220,52,207,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image36.png'),
(19221,52,208,'paragraph',NULL,'Lorenzo Romano Amedeo Carlo Avogadro di Quareqa edi Carreto','[{"s": "Lorenzo Romano Amedeo Carlo Avogadro di Quareqa edi Carreto", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19222,52,209,'paragraph',NULL,'(1776–1856)','[{"s": "(1776–1856)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19223,52,210,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that in the Fig. 1.9 (Page 16) each box contains equal number of molecules. In fact, Avogadro could explain the above result by considering the molecules to be polyatomic. If hydrogen and oxygen were considered as diatomic as recognised now, then the above results are easily understandable. However, Dalton and others believed at that time that atoms of the same kind cannot combine and molecules of oxygen or hydrogen containing two atoms did not exist. Avogadro’s proposal was published in the French Journal de Physique. In spite of being correct, it did not gain much support.','[{"s": "Note that in the Fig. 1.9 (Page 16) each box contains equal number of molecules. In fact, Avogadro could explain the above result by considering the molecules to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "polyatomic.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If hydrogen and oxygen were considered as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "diatomic", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as recognised now, then the above results are easily understandable. However,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Dalton and others believed at that time that atoms of the same kind cannot combine and molecules of oxygen or hydrogen containing two atoms did not exist.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Avogadro’s proposal was published in the French Journal de Physique. In spite of being correct, it did not gain much support.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19224,52,211,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image39.png'),
(19225,52,212,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.9 Two volumes of hydrogen react with one volume of oxygen to give two volumes of water vapour','[{"s": "Fig. 1.9 Two volumes of hydrogen react with one volume of oxygen to give two volumes of water vapour", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19226,52,213,'paragraph',NULL,'After about 50 years, in 1860, the first international conference on chemistry was held in Karlsruhe, Germany, to resolve various ideas. At the meeting, Stanislao Cannizaro presented a sketch of a course of chemical philosophy, which emphasised on the importance of Avogadro’s work .','[{"s": "After about 50 years, in 1860, the first international conference on chemistry was held in Karlsruhe, Germany, to resolve various ideas. At the meeting, Stanislao Cannizaro presented a sketch of a course of chemical philosophy, which emphasised on the importance of Avogadro’s work", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19227,52,214,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image41.png'),
(19228,52,215,'paragraph',NULL,'John Dalton','[{"s": "John Dalton", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19229,52,216,'paragraph',NULL,'(1776–1884)','[{"s": "(1776–1884)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19230,52,217,'heading',3,'1.6 Dalton’s Atomic Theory',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19231,52,218,'paragraph',NULL,'Although the origin of the idea that matter is composed of small indivisible particles called ‘a-tomio’ (meaning, indivisible), dates back to the time of Democritus, a Greek Philosopher (460–370 BC), it again started emerging as a result of several experimental studies which led to the laws mentioned above.','[{"s": "Although the origin of the idea that matter is composed of small indivisible particles called ‘a-tomio’ (meaning, indivisible), dates back to the time of Democritus, a Greek Philosopher (460–370 BC), it again started emerging as a result of several experimental studies which led to the laws mentioned above.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19232,52,219,'paragraph',NULL,'In 1808, Dalton published ‘A New System of Chemical Philosophy’, in which he proposed the following :','[{"s": "In 1808, Dalton published ‘A New System of Chemical Philosophy’, in which he proposed the following :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19233,52,220,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Matter consists of indivisible atoms.','[{"s": "1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Matter consists of indivisible atoms.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["11154"]',NULL),
(19234,52,221,'paragraph',NULL,'2. All atoms of a given element have identical properties, including identical mass. Atoms of different elements differ in mass.','[{"s": "2. All atoms of a given element have identical properties, including identical mass. Atoms of different elements differ in mass.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19235,52,222,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Compounds are formed when atoms of different elements combine in a fixed ratio.','[{"s": "3. Compounds are formed when atoms of different elements combine in a fixed ratio.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19236,52,223,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Chemical reactions involve reorganisation of atoms. These are neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction.','[{"s": "4. Chemical reactions involve reorganisation of atoms. These are neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19237,52,224,'paragraph',NULL,'Dalton’s theory could explain the laws of chemical combination. However, it could not explain the laws of gaseous volumes. It could not provide the reason for combining of atoms, which was answered later by other scientists.','[{"s": "Dalton’s theory could explain the laws of chemical combination. However, it could not explain the laws of gaseous volumes. It could not provide the reason for combining of atoms, which was answered later by other scientists.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19238,52,225,'heading',3,'1.7 Atomic and Molecular Masses',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19239,52,226,'paragraph',NULL,'After having some idea about the terms atoms and molecules, it is appropriate here to understand what do we mean by atomic and molecular masses.','[{"s": "After having some idea about the terms atoms and molecules, it is appropriate here to understand what do we mean by atomic and molecular masses.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19241,52,228,'paragraph',NULL,'The atomic mass or the mass of an atom is actually very-very small because atoms are extremely small. Today, we have sophisticated techniques e.g., mass spectrometry for determining the atomic masses fairly accurately . But in the nineteenth century, scientists could determine the mass of one atom relative to another by experimental means, as has been mentioned earlier. Hydrogen, being the lightest atom was arbitrarily assigned a mass of 1 (without any units) and other elements were assigned masses relative to it. However, the present system of atomic masses is based on carbon-12 as the standard and has been agreed upon in 1961. Here, Carbon-12 is one of the isotopes of carbon and can be represented as 12C. In this system, 12C is assigned a mass of exactly 12 atomic mass unit ( amu ) and masses of all other atoms are given relative to this standard. One atomic mass unit is defined as a mass exactly equal to one-twelfth of the mass of one carbon - 12 atom.','[{"s": "The atomic mass or the mass of an atom is actually very-very small because atoms are extremely small.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Today, we have sophisticated techniques e.g., mass spectrometry for determining the atomic masses fairly accurately", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". But in the nineteenth century, scientists could determine the mass of one atom", "t": "text"}, {"s": "relative", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to another by experimental means, as has been mentioned earlier. Hydrogen, being the lightest atom was arbitrarily assigned a mass of 1 (without any units) and other elements were assigned masses relative to it. However, the present system of atomic masses is based on carbon-12 as the standard and has been agreed upon in 1961. Here, Carbon-12 is one of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "isotopes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of carbon and can be represented as 12C. In this system, 12C is assigned a mass of exactly 12 atomic mass unit (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "amu", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and masses of all other atoms are given relative to this standard. One", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atomic mass unit", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is defined as a mass exactly equal to one-twelfth of the mass of one carbon - 12 atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19242,52,229,'paragraph',NULL,'And 1 amu = 1.66056×10–24 g','[{"s": "And 1 amu = 1.66056×10–24 g", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19243,52,230,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass of an atom of hydrogen = 1.6736×10–24 g','[{"s": "Mass of an atom of hydrogen = 1.6736×10–24 g", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19244,52,231,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, in terms of amu, the mass','[{"s": "Thus, in terms of amu, the mass", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19245,52,232,'paragraph',NULL,'of hydrogen atom = Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image54.png','[{"s": "of hydrogen atom =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image54.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19246,52,233,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1.0078 amu','[{"s": "= 1.0078 amu", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19247,52,234,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1.0080 amu','[{"s": "= 1.0080 amu", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19248,52,235,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, the mass of oxygen - 16 (16O) atom would be 15.995 amu.','[{"s": "Similarly, the mass of oxygen - 16 (16O) atom would be 15.995 amu.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19249,52,236,'paragraph',NULL,'At present, ‘amu’ has been replaced by ‘u’ , which is known as unified mass .','[{"s": "At present,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "‘amu’", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has been replaced by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "‘u’", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "unified mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19250,52,237,'paragraph',NULL,'When we use atomic masses of elements in calculations, we actually use average atomic masses of elements, which are explained below.','[{"s": "When we use atomic masses of elements in calculations, we actually use average atomic masses of elements, which are explained below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19251,52,238,'heading',4,'1.7.2 Average Atomic Mass',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19252,52,239,'paragraph',NULL,'Many naturally occurring elements exist as more than one isotope. When we take into account the existence of these isotopes and their relative abundance (per cent occurrence), the average atomic mass of that element can be computed. For example, carbon has the following three isotopes with relative abundances and masses as shown against each of them.','[{"s": "Many naturally occurring elements exist as more than one isotope. When we take into account the existence of these isotopes and their relative abundance (per cent occurrence), the average atomic mass of that element can be computed. For example, carbon has the following three isotopes with relative abundances and masses as shown against each of them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19253,52,240,'image',NULL,'dsf32dsf',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image56.png'),
(19254,52,241,'paragraph',NULL,'From the above data, the average atomic mass of carbon will come out to be:','[{"s": "From the above data, the average atomic mass of carbon will come out to be:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19255,52,242,'paragraph',NULL,'(0.98892) (12 u) + (0.01108) (13.00335 u) + (2 × 10–12) (14.00317 u) = 12.011 u','[{"s": "(0.98892) (12 u) + (0.01108) (13.00335 u) + (2 × 10–12) (14.00317 u) = 12.011 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19256,52,243,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, average atomic masses for other elements can be calculated. In the periodic table of elements, the atomic masses mentioned for different elements actually represent their average atomic masses.','[{"s": "Similarly, average atomic masses for other elements can be calculated. In the periodic table of elements, the atomic masses mentioned for different elements actually represent their average atomic masses.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19257,52,244,'heading',4,'1.7.3 Molecular Mass',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19258,52,245,'paragraph',NULL,'Molecular mass is the sum of atomic masses of the elements present in a molecule. It is obtained by multiplying the atomic mass of each element by the number of its atoms and adding them together. For example, molecular mass of methane, which contains one carbon atom and four hydrogen atoms, can be obtained as follows:','[{"s": "Molecular mass is the sum of atomic masses of the elements present in a molecule. It is obtained by multiplying the atomic mass of each element by the number of its atoms and adding them together. For example, molecular mass of methane, which contains one carbon atom and four hydrogen atoms, can be obtained as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19259,52,246,'paragraph',NULL,'Molecular mass of methane,','[{"s": "Molecular mass of methane,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19260,52,247,'paragraph',NULL,'(CH 4 ) = (12.011 u) + 4 (1.008 u)','[{"s": "(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = (12.011 u) + 4 (1.008 u)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19261,52,248,'paragraph',NULL,'= 16.043 u','[{"s": "= 16.043 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19262,52,249,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, molecular mass of water (H 2 O)','[{"s": "Similarly, molecular mass of water (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19263,52,250,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2 × atomic mass of hydrogen + 1 × atomic mass of oxygen','[{"s": "= 2 × atomic mass of hydrogen + 1 × atomic mass of oxygen", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19264,52,251,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2 (1.008 u) + 16.00 u','[{"s": "= 2 (1.008 u) + 16.00 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19265,52,252,'paragraph',NULL,'= 18.02 u','[{"s": "= 18.02 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19266,52,253,'heading',3,'1.7.4 Formula Mass',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19267,52,254,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image11.png'),
(19268,52,255,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.10 Packing of Na+ and Cl– ions in sodium chloride','[{"s": "Fig. 1.10 Packing of Na+ and Cl– ions in sodium chloride", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19269,52,256,'paragraph',NULL,'Some substances, such as sodium chloride, do not contain discrete molecules as their constituent units. In such compounds, positive (sodium ion) and negative (chloride ion) entities are arranged in a three-dimensional structure, as shown in Fig. 1.10.','[{"s": "Some substances, such as sodium chloride, do not contain discrete molecules as their constituent units. In such compounds, positive (sodium ion) and negative (chloride ion) entities are arranged in a three-dimensional structure,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as shown in Fig. 1.10.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19270,52,257,'paragraph',NULL,'It may be noted that in sodium chloride, one Na+ ion is surrounded by six Cl– ion and vice-versa.','[{"s": "It may be noted that in sodium chloride, one Na+ ion is surrounded by six Cl– ion and vice-versa.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19271,52,258,'paragraph',NULL,'The formula, such as NaCl, is used to calculate the formula mass instead of molecular mass as in the solid state sodium chloride does not exist as a single entity.','[{"s": "The formula, such as NaCl, is used to calculate the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "formula mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": "instead of molecular mass as in the solid state sodium chloride does not exist as a single entity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19272,52,259,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the formula mass of sodium chloride is atomic mass of sodium + atomic mass of chlorine','[{"s": "Thus, the formula mass of sodium chloride is atomic mass of sodium + atomic mass of chlorine", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19273,52,260,'paragraph',NULL,'= 23.0 u + 35.5 u = 58.5 u','[{"s": "= 23.0 u + 35.5 u = 58.5 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19274,52,261,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 1.1','[{"s": "Problem 1.1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19275,52,262,'paragraph',NULL,'Calculate the molecular mass of glucose (C 6 H 12 O 6 ) molecule.','[{"s": "Calculate the molecular mass of glucose (C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19276,52,263,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19277,52,264,'paragraph',NULL,'Molecular mass of glucose (C 6 H 12 O 6 )','[{"s": "Molecular mass of glucose (C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19278,52,265,'paragraph',NULL,'= 6(12.011 u) + 12(1.008 u) + 6(16.00 u)','[{"s": "= 6(12.011 u) + 12(1.008 u) + 6(16.00 u)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19279,52,266,'paragraph',NULL,'= (72.066 u) + (12.096 u) + (96.00 u)','[{"s": "= (72.066 u) + (12.096 u) + (96.00 u)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19280,52,267,'paragraph',NULL,'= 180.162 u','[{"s": "= 180.162 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19281,52,268,'heading',3,'1.8 Mole concept and Molar Masses',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19282,52,269,'paragraph',NULL,'Atoms and molecules are extremely small in size and their numbers in even a small amount of any substance is really very large. To handle such large numbers, a unit of convenient magnitude is required.','[{"s": "Atoms and molecules are extremely small in size and their numbers in even a small amount of any substance is really very large. To handle such large numbers, a unit of convenient magnitude is required.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19283,52,270,'paragraph',NULL,'Just as we denote one dozen for 12 items, score for 20 items, gross for 144 items, we use the idea of mole to count entities at the microscopic level (i.e., atoms, molecules, particles, electrons, ions, etc).','[{"s": "Just as we denote one dozen for 12 items, score for 20 items, gross for 144 items, we use the idea of mole to count entities at the microscopic level (i.e., atoms, molecules, particles, electrons, ions, etc).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19284,52,271,'paragraph',NULL,'In SI system, mole (symbol, mol) was introduced as seventh base quantity for the amount of a substance.','[{"s": "In SI system,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mole", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(symbol, mol) was introduced as seventh base quantity for the amount of a substance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19285,52,272,'paragraph',NULL,'One mole is the amount of a substance that contains as many particles or entities as there are atoms in exactly 12 g (or 0.012 kg) of the 12 C isotope. It may be emphasised that the mole of a substance always contains the same number of entities, no matter what the substance may be. In order to determine this number precisely, the mass of a carbon–12 atom was determined by a mass spectrometer and found to be equal to 1.992648 × 10 –23 g. Knowing that one mole of carbon weighs 12 g, the number of atoms in it is equal to:','[{"s": "One", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mole is the amount of a substance that contains as many particles or entities as there are atoms in exactly 12 g (or 0.012 kg) of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C isotope.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It may be emphasised that the mole of a substance always contains the same number of entities, no matter what the substance may be.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In order to determine this number precisely, the mass of a carbon–12 atom was determined by a mass spectrometer and found to be equal to 1.992648 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–23", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g. Knowing that one mole of carbon weighs 12 g, the number of atoms in it is equal to:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19286,52,273,'image',NULL,'dfsd32434',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image67.png'),
(19287,52,274,'paragraph',NULL,'This number of entities in 1 mol is so important that it is given a separate name and symbol. It is known as ‘Avogadro constant’ , or Avogadro number denoted by N A in honour of Amedeo Avogadro.2 To appreciate the largeness of this number, let us write it with all zeroes without using any powers of ten.','[{"s": "This number of entities in 1 mol is so important that it is given a separate name and symbol.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is known as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "‘Avogadro", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "constant’", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", or Avogadro number denoted by N", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "in honour of Amedeo Avogadro.2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "To appreciate the largeness of this number, let us write it with all zeroes without using any powers of ten.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["11111", "11155"]',NULL),
(19288,52,275,'paragraph',NULL,'602213670000000000000000','[{"s": "602213670000000000000000", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19289,52,276,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence, so many entities (atoms, molecules or any other particle) constitute one mole of a particular substance.','[{"s": "Hence, so many entities (atoms, molecules or any other particle) constitute one mole of a particular substance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19290,52,277,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image25.png'),
(19291,52,278,'paragraph',NULL,'Having defined the mole, it is easier to know the mass of one mole of a substance or the constituent entities. The mass of one mole of a substance in grams is called its molar mass. The molar mass in grams is numerically equal to atomic/molecular/formula mass in u.','[{"s": "Having defined the mole, it is easier to know the mass of one mole of a substance or the constituent entities.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The mass of one mole of a substance in grams is called its molar mass.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The molar mass in grams is numerically equal to atomic/molecular/formula mass in u.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["11115"]',NULL),
(19292,52,279,'paragraph',NULL,'Molar mass of water = 18.02 g mol -1','[{"s": "Molar mass of water = 18.02 g mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19293,52,280,'paragraph',NULL,'Molar mass of sodium chloride = 58.5 g mol -1','[{"s": "Molar mass of sodium chloride = 58.5 g mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19294,52,281,'heading',3,'1.9 Percentage Composition',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19295,52,282,'paragraph',NULL,'So far, we were dealing with the number of entities present in a given sample. But many a time, information regarding the percentage of a particular element present in a compound is required. Suppose, an unknown or new compound is given to you, the first question you would ask is: what is its formula or what are its constituents and in what ratio are they present in the given compound? For known compounds also, such information provides a check whether the given sample contains the same percentage of elements as present in a pure sample. In other words, one can check the purity of a given sample by analysing this data.','[{"s": "So far, we were dealing with the number of entities present in a given sample. But many a time, information regarding the percentage of a particular element present in a compound is required. Suppose, an unknown or new compound is given to you, the first question you would ask is: what is its formula or what are its constituents and in what ratio are they present in the given compound? For known compounds also, such information provides a check whether the given sample contains the same percentage of elements as present in a pure sample. In other words, one can check the purity of a given sample by analysing this data.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19296,52,283,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image6.png'),
(19297,52,284,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 1.11 One mole of various substances','[{"s": "Fig. 1.11 One mole of various substances", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19298,52,285,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us understand it by taking the example of water (H 2 O). 2 Since water contains hydrogen and oxygen, the percentage composition of both these elements can be calculated as follows:','[{"s": "Let us understand it by taking the example of water (H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "O).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Since water contains hydrogen and oxygen, the percentage composition of both these elements can be calculated as follows:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["11156", "11157"]',NULL),
(19299,52,286,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass % of an element =','[{"s": "Mass % of an element =", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19300,52,287,'image',NULL,'fsdf34234',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image48.png'),
(19301,52,288,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us take one more example. What is the percentage of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen in ethanol?','[{"s": "Let us take one more example. What is the percentage of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen in ethanol?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19302,52,289,'paragraph',NULL,'Molecular formula of ethanol is: C 2 H 5 OH Molar mass of ethanol is:','[{"s": "Molecular formula of ethanol is: C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OH Molar mass of ethanol is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19303,52,290,'paragraph',NULL,'(2×12.01 + 6×1.008 + 16.00) g = 46.068 g','[{"s": "(2×12.01 + 6×1.008 + 16.00) g = 46.068 g", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19304,52,291,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass per cent of carbon = Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image63.png = 52.14%','[{"s": "Mass per cent of carbon =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image63.png", "t": "img", "alt": "ddsd111111"}, {"s": "= 52.14%", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19305,52,292,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass per cent of hydrogen = Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image61.png = 13.13%','[{"s": "Mass per cent of hydrogen =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image61.png", "t": "img", "alt": "dfad222222222"}, {"s": "= 13.13%", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19306,52,293,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass per cent of oxygen = Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image32.png = 34.73%','[{"s": "Mass per cent of oxygen =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image32.png", "t": "img", "alt": "sdf333333333"}, {"s": "= 34.73%", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19307,52,294,'heading',4,'1.9.1 Empirical Formula for Molecular Formula',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19308,52,295,'paragraph',NULL,'An empirical formula represents the simplest whole number ratio of various atoms present in a compound, whereas, the molecular formula shows the exact number of different types of atoms present in a molecule of a compound.','[{"s": "An", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "empirical formula", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "represents the simplest whole number ratio of various atoms present in a compound, whereas, the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "molecular formula", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "shows the exact number of different types of atoms present in a molecule of a compound.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["11158"]',NULL),
(19309,52,296,'paragraph',NULL,'If the mass per cent of various elements present in a compound is known, its empirical formula can be determined. Molecular formula can further be obtained if the molar mass is known. The following example illustrates this sequence.','[{"s": "If the mass per cent of various elements present in a compound is known, its empirical formula can be determined. Molecular formula can further be obtained if the molar mass is known. The following example illustrates this sequence.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19310,52,297,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 1.2','[{"s": "Problem 1.2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19311,52,298,'paragraph',NULL,'A compound contains 4.07% hydrogen, 24.27% carbon and 71.65% chlorine. Its molar mass is 98.96 g. What are its empirical and molecular formulas?','[{"s": "A compound contains 4.07% hydrogen, 24.27% carbon and 71.65% chlorine. Its molar mass is 98.96 g. What are its empirical and molecular formulas?", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["11159"]',NULL),
(19312,52,299,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19313,52,300,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 1. Conversion of mass per cent to grams','[{"s": "Step 1. Conversion of mass per cent to grams", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19314,52,301,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 2. Convert into number moles of each element','[{"s": "Step 2. Convert into number moles of each element", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19315,52,302,'paragraph',NULL,'Divide the masses obtained above by respective atomic masses of various elements. This gives the number of moles of constituent elements in the compound','[{"s": "Divide the masses obtained above by respective atomic masses of various elements. This gives the number of moles of constituent elements in the compound", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19316,52,303,'paragraph',NULL,'Moles of hydrogen = Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image27.png = 4.04','[{"s": "Moles of hydrogen", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image27.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 4.04", "t": "text"}]','marked','["11132"]',NULL),
(19317,52,304,'paragraph',NULL,'Moles of carbon = Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image40.png','[{"s": "Moles of carbon =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image40.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19318,52,305,'paragraph',NULL,'Moles of chlorine = Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image52.png','[{"s": "Moles of chlorine =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image52.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(19319,52,306,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3. Divide each of the mole values obtained above by the smallest number amongst them','[{"s": "Step 3. Divide each of the mole values obtained above by the smallest number amongst them", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19320,52,307,'paragraph',NULL,'Since 2.021 is smallest value, division by it gives a ratio of 2:1:1 for H:C:Cl .','[{"s": "Since 2.021 is smallest value, division by it gives a ratio of 2:1:1 for H:C:Cl .", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19321,52,308,'paragraph',NULL,'In case the ratios are not whole numbers, then they may be converted into whole number by multiplying by the suitable coefficient.','[{"s": "In case the ratios are not whole numbers, then they may be converted into whole number by multiplying by the suitable coefficient.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19322,52,309,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 4. Write down the empirical formula by mentioning the numbers after writing the symbols of respective elements','[{"s": "Step 4.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Write down the empirical formula by mentioning the numbers after writing the symbols of respective elements", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["11160"]',NULL),
(19323,52,310,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 2 Cl is, thus, the empirical formula of the above compound.','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl is, thus, the empirical formula of the above compound.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19324,52,311,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 5. Writing molecular formula','[{"s": "Step 5. Writing molecular formula", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19325,52,312,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Determine empirical formula mass by','[{"s": "(a) Determine empirical formula mass by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19326,52,313,'paragraph',NULL,'adding the atomic masses of various atoms present in the empirical formula.','[{"s": "adding the atomic masses of various atoms present in the empirical formula.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19327,52,314,'paragraph',NULL,'For CH 2 Cl, empirical formula mass is','[{"s": "For CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl, empirical formula mass is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19328,52,315,'paragraph',NULL,'12.01 + (2 × 1.008) + 35.453','[{"s": "12.01 + (2 × 1.008) + 35.453", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19329,52,316,'paragraph',NULL,'= 49.48 g','[{"s": "= 49.48 g", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19330,52,317,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Divide Molar mass by empirical formula mass','[{"s": "(b) Divide Molar mass by empirical formula mass", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19331,52,318,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Some_Basic_Concept_of_Chemistry-image51.png'),
(19332,52,319,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2 = (n)','[{"s": "= 2 = (n)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19333,52,320,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Multiply empirical formula by n obtained above to get the molecular formula','[{"s": "(c) Multiply empirical formula by n obtained above to get the molecular formula", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19334,52,321,'paragraph',NULL,'Empirical formula = CH 2 Cl, n = 2. Hence molecular formula is C 2 H 4 Cl 2 .','[{"s": "Empirical formula = CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl, n = 2. Hence molecular formula is C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19335,52,322,'heading',3,'1.10 Stoichiometry and Stoichiometric Calculations',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19336,52,323,'paragraph',NULL,'The word ‘stoichiometry’ is derived from two Greek words — stoicheion (meaning, element) and metron (meaning, measure) . Stoichiometry, thus, deals with the calculation of masses (sometimes volumes also) of the reactants and the products involved in a chemical reaction. Before understanding how to calculate the amounts of reactants required or the products produced in a chemical reaction, let us study what information is available from the balanced chemical equation of a given reaction. Let us consider the combustion of methane. A balanced equation for this reaction is as given below:','[{"s": "The word ‘stoichiometry’ is derived from two Greek words — stoicheion (meaning, element) and metron (meaning, measure)", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Stoichiometry, thus, deals with the calculation of masses (sometimes volumes also) of the reactants and the products involved in a chemical reaction.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Before understanding how to calculate the amounts of reactants required or the products produced in a chemical reaction, let us study what information is available from the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "balanced", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "chemical equation of a given reaction.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Let us consider the combustion of methane. A balanced equation for this reaction is as given below:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["11138"]',NULL),
(19337,52,324,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 4 (g) + 2O 2 (g) → CO 2 (g) + 2 H 2 O (g)','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 2O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 2 H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19338,52,325,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, methane and dioxygen are called reactants and carbon dioxide and water are called products. Note that all the reactants and the products are gases in the above reaction and this has been indicated by letter (g) in the brackets next to its formula. Similarly, in case of solids and liquids, (s) and (l) are written respectively.','[{"s": "Here, methane and dioxygen are called reactants and carbon dioxide and water are called products. Note that all the reactants and the products are gases in the above reaction and this has been indicated by letter (g) in the brackets next to its formula. Similarly, in case of solids and liquids, (s) and (l) are written respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19339,52,326,'paragraph',NULL,'The coefficients 2 for O 2 and H 2 O are called stoichiometric coefficients. 2 Similarly the coefficient for CH 4 and CO 2 is one in each case. They represent the number of molecules (and moles as well) taking part in the reaction or formed in the reaction.','[{"s": "The coefficients 2 for O", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "O are called stoichiometric coefficients.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Similarly the coefficient for CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is one in each case. They represent the number of molecules (and moles as well) taking part in the reaction or formed in the reaction.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["11161", "11162"]',NULL),
(19340,52,327,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, according to the above chemical reaction,','[{"s": "Thus, according to the above chemical reaction,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19341,52,328,'paragraph',NULL,'• One mole of CH 4 (g) reacts with two moles of O 2 (g) to give one mole of CO 2 (g) and two moles of H 2 O(g)','[{"s": "• One", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mole", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) reacts with two", "t": "text"}, {"s": "moles", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) to give one", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mole", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) and two", "t": "text"}, {"s": "moles", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19342,52,329,'paragraph',NULL,'• One molecule of CH 4 molecules of O 2 (g) to give one molecule of CO 2 (g) and 2 molecules of H 2 O(g)','[{"s": "• One", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "molecules", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) to give one", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) and 2 molecules of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19343,52,330,'paragraph',NULL,'• 22.7 L of CH 4 (g) reacts with 45.4 L of O 2 (g) to give 22.7 L of CO 2 (g) and 45.4 L of H 2 O(g)','[{"s": "• 22.7 L of CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) reacts with 45.4 L of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) to give 22.7 L of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) and 45.4 L of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19344,52,331,'paragraph',NULL,'• 16 g of CH 4 (g) reacts with 2×32 g of O 2 (g) to give 44 g of CO 2 (g) and 2×18 g of H 2 O (g).','[{"s": "• 16 g of CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) reacts with 2×32 g of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) to give 44 g of CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) and 2×18 g of H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (g).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19345,52,332,'paragraph',NULL,'From these relationships, the given data can be interconverted as follows:','[{"s": "From these relationships, the given data can be interconverted as follows:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["11152"]',NULL),
