(19348,52,335,'heading',4,'1.10.1 Limiting Reagent',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19349,52,336,'paragraph',NULL,'Many a time, reactions are carried out with the amounts of reactants that are different than the amounts as required by a balanced chemical reaction. In such situations, one reactant is in more amount than the amount required by balanced chemical reaction. The reactant which is present in the least amount gets consumed after sometime and after that further reaction does not take place whatever be the amount of the other reactant. Hence, the reactant, which gets consumed first, limits the amount of product formed and is, therefore, called the limiting reagent . In performing stoichiometric calculations, this aspect is also to be kept in mind.','[{"s": "Many a time, reactions are carried out with the amounts of reactants that are different than the amounts as required by a balanced chemical reaction. In such situations, one reactant is in more amount than the amount required by balanced chemical reaction. The reactant which is present in the least amount gets consumed after sometime and after that further reaction does not take place whatever be the amount of the other reactant. Hence, the reactant, which gets consumed first, limits the amount of product formed and is, therefore, called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "limiting reagent", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In performing stoichiometric calculations, this aspect is also to be kept in mind.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19350,52,337,'heading',4,'1.10.2 Reactions in Solutions',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19351,52,338,'paragraph',NULL,'A majority of reactions in the laboratories are carried out in solutions. Therefore, it is important to understand as how the amount of substance is expressed when it is present in the solution. The concentration of a solution or the amount of substance present in its given volume can be expressed in any of the following ways.','[{"s": "A majority of reactions in the laboratories are carried out in solutions.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Therefore, it is important to understand as how the amount of substance is expressed when it is present in the solution. The concentration of a solution or the amount of substance present in its given volume can be expressed in any of the following ways.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["11236"]',NULL),
(19352,52,339,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Mass per cent or weight per cent (w/w %)','[{"s": "1. Mass per cent or weight per cent (w/w %)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19353,52,340,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Mole fraction','[{"s": "2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Mole fraction", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["11112"]',NULL),
(19354,52,341,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Molarity','[{"s": "3. Molarity", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19355,52,342,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Molality','[{"s": "4. Molality", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19356,52,343,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us now study each one of them in detail.','[{"s": "Let us now study each one of them in detail.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19357,52,344,'paragraph',NULL,'Balancing a chemical equation 2','[{"s": "Balancing a chemical equation 2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["11136", "11137"]',NULL),
(19358,52,345,'paragraph',NULL,'According to the law of conservation of mass, a balanced chemical equation has the same number of atoms of each element on both sides of the equation. Many chemical equations can be balanced by trial and error. Let us take the reactions of a few metals and non-metals with oxygen to give oxides','[{"s": "According to the law of conservation of mass, a balanced chemical equation has the same number of atoms of each element on both sides of the equation. Many chemical equations can be balanced by trial and error. Let us take the reactions of a few metals and non-metals with oxygen to give oxides", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19359,52,346,'paragraph',NULL,'4 Fe(s) + 3O 2 (g) → 2Fe 2 O 3 (s) (a) balanced equation','[{"s": "4 Fe(s) + 3O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → 2Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) (a) balanced equation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19360,52,347,'paragraph',NULL,'2 Mg(s) + O 2 (g) → 2MgO(s) (b) balanced equation','[{"s": "2 Mg(s) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → 2MgO(s) (b) balanced equation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19361,52,348,'paragraph',NULL,'P 4 (s) + O 2 (g) → P 4 O 10 (s) (c) unbalanced equation','[{"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) (c) unbalanced equation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19362,52,349,'paragraph',NULL,'Equations (a) and (b) are balanced, since there are same number of metal and oxygen atoms on each side of the equations. However equation (c) is not balanced. In this equation, phosphorus atoms are balanced but not the oxygen atoms. To balance it, we must place the coefficient 5 on the left of oxygen on the left side of the equation to balance the oxygen atoms appearing on the right side of the equation.','[{"s": "Equations (a) and (b) are balanced, since there are same number of metal and oxygen atoms on each side of the equations. However equation (c) is not balanced. In this equation, phosphorus atoms are balanced but not the oxygen atoms. To balance it, we must place the coefficient 5 on the left of oxygen on the left side of the equation to balance the oxygen atoms appearing on the right side of the equation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19363,52,350,'paragraph',NULL,'P 4 (s) + 5O 2 (g) → P 4 O 10 (s) balanced equation','[{"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) + 5O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) balanced equation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19364,52,351,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, let us take combustion of propane, C 3 H 8 . This equation can be balanced in steps.','[{"s": "Now, let us take combustion of propane, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This equation can be balanced in steps.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19365,52,352,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 1 Write down the correct formulas of reactants and products. Here, propane and oxygen are reactants, and carbon dioxide and water are products.','[{"s": "Step 1 Write down the correct formulas of reactants and products.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Here, propane and oxygen are reactants, and carbon dioxide and water are products.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["11104"]',NULL),
(19366,52,353,'paragraph',NULL,'C 3 H 8 (g) + O 2 (g) → CO 2 (g) + H 2 O(l) unbalanced equation','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) unbalanced equation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19367,52,354,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 2 Balance the number of C atoms: Since 3 carbon atoms are in the reactant, therefore, three CO 2 molecules are required on the right side.','[{"s": "Step 2 Balance the number of C atoms: Since 3 carbon atoms are in the reactant, therefore, three CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules are required on the right side.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19368,52,355,'paragraph',NULL,'C 3 H 8 (g) + O 2 (g) → 3CO 2 (g) + H 2 O (l)','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → 3CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19369,52,356,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 3 Balance the number of H atoms: on the left there are 8 hydrogen atoms in the reactants however, each molecule of water has two hydrogen atoms, so four molecules of water will be required for eight hydrogen atoms on the right side.','[{"s": "Step 3 Balance the number of H atoms: on the left there are 8 hydrogen atoms in the reactants however, each molecule of water has two hydrogen atoms, so four molecules of water will be required for eight hydrogen atoms on the right side.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19370,52,357,'paragraph',NULL,'C 3 H 8 (g) +O 2 (g) → 3CO 2 (g)+4H 2 O (l)','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) +O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → 3CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)+4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3583,31,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.7 Muscle tissue : (a) Skeletal (striated) muscle tissue (b) Smooth muscle tissue (c) Cardiac muscle tissue','[{"s": "Figure 7.7 Muscle tissue : (a) Skeletal (striated) muscle tissue (b) Smooth muscle tissue (c) Cardiac muscle tissue", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["70758"]',NULL),
(19371,52,358,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 4 Balance the number of O atoms: There are 10 oxygen atoms on the right side (3 × 2 = 6 in CO 2 and 4 × 1= 4 in water). Therefore, five O 2 molecules are needed to supply the required 10 CO 2 and 4 × 1= 4 in water). Therefore, five O 2 molecules are needed to supply the required 10 oxygen atoms.','[{"s": "Step 4 Balance the number of O atoms: There are 10 oxygen atoms on the right side (3 × 2 = 6 in CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 4 × 1= 4 in water). Therefore, five O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules are needed to supply the required 10 CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 4 × 1= 4 in water). Therefore, five O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules are needed to supply the required", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10 oxygen atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19372,52,359,'paragraph',NULL,'C 3 H 8 (g) +5O 2 (g) → 3CO 2 (g) + 4H 2 O (l)','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) +5O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → 3CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 4H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O (l)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19373,52,360,'paragraph',NULL,'Step 5 Verify that the number of atoms of each element is balanced in the final equation. The equation shows three carbon atoms, eight hydrogen atoms, and 10 oxygen atoms on each side.','[{"s": "Step 5 Verify that the number of atoms of each element is balanced in the final equation. The equation shows three carbon atoms, eight hydrogen atoms, and 10 oxygen atoms on each side.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19374,52,361,'paragraph',NULL,'All equations that have correct formulas for all reactants and products can be balanced. Always remember that subscripts in formulas of reactants and products cannot be changed to balance an equation.','[{"s": "All equations that have correct formulas for all reactants and products can be balanced. Always remember that subscripts in formulas of reactants and products cannot be changed to balance an equation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
