(26433,66,3,'paragraph',NULL,'10.2 Pressure','[{"s": "10.2 Pressure", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19913,53,88,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Calculations by Rutherford showed that the volume occupied by the nucleus is negligibly small as compared to the total volume of the atom. The radius of the atom is about 10 –10 m, while that of nucleus is 10 –15 m. One can appreciate this difference in size by realising that if a cricket ball represents a nucleus, then the radius of atom would be about 5 km.','[{"s": "(iii) Calculations by Rutherford showed that the volume occupied by the nucleus is negligibly small as compared to the total volume of the atom. The radius of the atom is about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m, while that of nucleus is 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. One can appreciate this difference in size by realising that if a cricket ball represents a nucleus, then the radius of atom would be about 5 km.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19914,53,89,'paragraph',NULL,'On the basis of above observations and conclusions, Rutherford proposed the nuclear model of atom. According to this model:','[{"s": "On the basis of above observations and conclusions, Rutherford proposed the nuclear model of atom. According to this model:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19915,53,90,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The positive charge and most of the mass of the atom was densely concentrated in extremely small region. This very small portion of the atom was called nucleus by Rutherford.','[{"s": "(i) The positive charge and most of the mass of the atom was densely concentrated in extremely small region. This very small portion of the atom was called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nucleus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by Rutherford.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19916,53,91,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) The nucleus is surrounded by electrons that move around the nucleus with a very high speed in circular paths called orbits . Thus, Rutherford’s model of atom resembles the solar system in which the nucleus plays the role of sun and the electrons that of revolving planets.','[{"s": "(ii) The nucleus is surrounded by electrons that move around the nucleus with a very high speed in circular paths called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbits", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, Rutherford’s model of atom resembles the solar system in which the nucleus plays the role of sun and the electrons that of revolving planets.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19917,53,92,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Electrons and the nucleus are held together by electrostatic forces of attraction.','[{"s": "(iii) Electrons and the nucleus are held together by electrostatic forces of attraction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19918,53,93,'heading',4,'2.2.3 Atomic Number and Mass Number',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19919,53,94,'paragraph',NULL,'The presence of positive charge on the nucleus is due to the protons in the nucleus. As established earlier, the charge on the proton is equal but opposite to that of electron. The number of protons present in the nucleus is equal to atomic number (Z ). For example, the number of protons in the hydrogen nucleus is 1, in sodium atom it is 11, therefore their atomic numbers are 1 and 11 respectively. In order to keep the electrical neutrality, the number of electrons in an atom is equal to the number of protons (atomic number, Z ). For example, number of electrons in hydrogen atom and sodium atom are 1 and 11 respectively.','[{"s": "The presence of positive charge on the nucleus is due to the protons in the nucleus. As established earlier, the charge on the proton is equal but opposite to that of electron. The number of protons present in the nucleus is equal to atomic number (Z ). For example, the number of protons in the hydrogen nucleus is 1, in sodium atom it is 11, therefore their atomic numbers are 1 and 11 respectively. In order to keep the electrical neutrality, the number of electrons in an atom is equal to the number of protons (atomic number, Z ). For example, number of electrons in hydrogen atom and sodium atom are 1 and 11 respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19920,53,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Atomic number ( Z ) = number of protons in the nucleus of an atom = number of electrons in a neutral atom (2.3)','[{"s": "Atomic number (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") = number of protons in the nucleus of an atom = number of electrons in a neutral atom", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(2.3)", "t": "text"}]','marked','["22401"]',NULL),
(19921,53,96,'paragraph',NULL,'While the positive charge of the nucleus is due to protons, the mass of the nucleus, due to protons and neutrons. As discussed earlier protons and neutrons present in the nucleus are collectively known as nucleons . The total number of nucleons is termed as mass number (A) of the atom.','[{"s": "While the positive charge of the nucleus is due to protons, the mass of the nucleus, due to protons and neutrons. As discussed earlier protons and neutrons present in the nucleus are collectively known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nucleons", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The total number of nucleons is termed as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mass number (A)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19922,53,97,'paragraph',NULL,'mass number (A) = number of protons ( Z )','[{"s": "mass number (A) = number of protons (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19923,53,98,'paragraph',NULL,'+ number of neutrons (n) (2.4)','[{"s": "+ number of neutrons (n)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19924,53,99,'heading',4,'2.2.4 Isobars and Isotopes',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19925,53,100,'paragraph',NULL,'The composition of any atom can be represented by using the normal element symbol (X) with super-script on the left hand side as the atomic mass number (A) and subscript (Z) on the left hand side as the atomic number (i.e., A Z X).','[{"s": "The composition of any atom can be represented by using the normal element symbol (X) with super-script on the left hand side as the atomic mass number (A) and subscript (Z) on the left hand side as the atomic number (i.e.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "X).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26434,66,4,'paragraph',NULL,'10.3 Streamline flow','[{"s": "10.3 Streamline flow", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26435,66,5,'paragraph',NULL,'10.4 Bernoulli’s principle','[{"s": "10.4 Bernoulli’s principle", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26436,66,6,'paragraph',NULL,'10.5 Viscosity','[{"s": "10.5 Viscosity", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26437,66,7,'paragraph',NULL,'10.6 Reynolds number','[{"s": "10.6 Reynolds number", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
