(19937,53,112,'paragraph',NULL,'Atomic mass number = number of protons + number of neutrons','[{"s": "Atomic mass number = number of protons + number of neutrons", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19938,53,113,'paragraph',NULL,'= 16 + 16 = 32','[{"s": "= 16 + 16 = 32", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19939,53,114,'paragraph',NULL,'Species is not neutral as the number of protons is not equal to electrons. It is anion (negatively charged) with charge equal to excess electrons = 18 – 16 = 2. Symbol is Structure_of_Atom-image31.png .','[{"s": "Species is not neutral as the number of protons is not equal to electrons. It is anion (negatively charged) with charge equal to excess electrons = 18 – 16 = 2. Symbol is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Structure_of_Atom-image31.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19940,53,115,'paragraph',NULL,'Note : Before using the notation A Z X, find out whether the species is a neutral atom, a cation or an anion. If it is a neutral atom, equation (2.3) is valid, i.e., number of protons = number of electrons = atomic number. If the species is an ion, determine whether the number of protons are larger (cation, positive ion) or smaller (anion, negative ion) than the number of electrons. Number of neutrons is always given by A–Z, whether the species is neutral or ion.','[{"s": "Note :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Before using the notation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "X, find out whether the species is a neutral atom, a cation or an anion. If it is a neutral atom, equation (2.3) is valid, i.e., number of protons = number of electrons = atomic number. If the species is an ion, determine whether the number of protons are larger (cation, positive ion) or smaller (anion, negative ion) than the number of electrons. Number of neutrons is always given by A–Z, whether the species is neutral or ion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19941,53,116,'heading',4,'2.2.5 Drawbacks of Rutherford Model',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26443,66,13,'paragraph',NULL,'Appendix','[{"s": "Appendix", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26444,66,14,'heading',4,'10.1 Introduction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26445,66,15,'paragraph',NULL,'In this chapter, we shall study some common physical properties of liquids and gases. Liquids and gases can flow and are therefore, called fluids. It is this property that distinguishes liquids and gases from solids in a basic way.','[{"s": "In this chapter, we shall study some common physical properties of liquids and gases. Liquids and gases can flow and are therefore, called fluids. It is this property that distinguishes liquids and gases from solids in a basic way.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19942,53,117,'paragraph',NULL,'As you have learnt above, Rutherford nuclear model of an atom is like a small scale solar system with the nucleus playing the role of the massive sun and the electrons being similar to the lighter planets. When classical mechanics* is applied to the solar system, it shows that the planets describe well-defined orbits around the sun. The gravitational force between the planets is given by the expression Structure_of_Atom-image39.png where m 1 and m 2 are the masses, r is the distance of separation of the masses and G is the gravitational constant. The theory can also calculate precisely the planetary orbits and these are in agreement with the experimental measurements.','[{"s": "As you have learnt above, Rutherford nuclear model of an atom is like a small scale solar system with the nucleus playing the role of the massive sun and the electrons being similar to the lighter planets. When classical mechanics* is applied to the solar system, it shows that the planets describe well-defined orbits around the sun. The gravitational force between the planets is given by the expression", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Structure_of_Atom-image39.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-03 09-32-17"}, {"s": "where m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the masses, r is the distance of separation of the masses and G is the gravitational constant. The theory can also calculate precisely the planetary orbits and these are in agreement with the experimental measurements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19943,53,118,'paragraph',NULL,'The similarity between the solar system and nuclear model suggests that electrons should move around the nucleus in well defined orbits. Further, the coulomb force (kq 1 q 2 /r 2 where q 1 and q 2 are the charges, r is the distance of separation of the charges and k is the proportionality constant) between electron and the nucleus is mathematically similar to the gravitational force. However, when a body is moving in an orbit, it undergoes acceleration even if it is moving with a constant speed in an orbit because of changing direction. So an electron in the nuclear model describing planet like orbits is under acceleration. According to the electromagnetic theory of Maxwell, charged particles when accelerated should emit electromagnetic radiation (This feature does not exist for planets since they are uncharged). Therefore, an electron in an orbit will emit radiation, the energy carried by radiation comes from electronic motion. The orbit will thus continue to shrink. Calculations show that it should take an electron only 10 –8','[{"s": "The similarity between the solar system and nuclear model suggests that electrons should move around the nucleus in well defined orbits. Further, the coulomb force (kq", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the charges, r is the distance of separation of the charges and k is the proportionality constant) between electron and the nucleus is mathematically similar to the gravitational force. However, when a body is moving in an orbit, it undergoes acceleration even if it is moving with a constant speed in an orbit because of changing direction. So an electron in the nuclear model describing planet like orbits is under acceleration. According to the electromagnetic theory of Maxwell, charged particles when accelerated should emit electromagnetic radiation (This feature does not exist for planets since they are uncharged). Therefore, an electron in an orbit will emit radiation, the energy carried by radiation comes from electronic motion. The orbit will thus continue to shrink. Calculations show that it should take an electron only 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19944,53,119,'paragraph',NULL,'* Classical mechanics is a theoretical science based on Newton’s laws of motion. It specifies the laws of motion of macroscopic objects.','[{"s": "*", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Classical mechanics is a theoretical science based on Newton’s laws of motion. It specifies the laws of motion of macroscopic objects.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19945,53,120,'paragraph',NULL,'Another serious drawback of the Rutherford model is that it says nothing about distribution of the electrons around the nucleus and the energies of these electrons.','[{"s": "Another serious drawback of the Rutherford model is that it says nothing about distribution of the electrons around the nucleus and the energies of these electrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19946,53,121,'paragraph',NULL,'2.3 Developments Leading to the Bohr’s Model of Atom','[{"s": "2.3 Developments Leading to the Bohr’s Model of Atom", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19947,53,122,'paragraph',NULL,'Historically, results observed from the studies of interactions of radiations with matter have provided immense information regarding the structure of atoms and molecules. Neils Bohr utilised these results to improve upon the model proposed by Rutherford. Two developments played a major role in the formulation of Bohr’s model of atom. These were:','[{"s": "Historically, results observed from the studies of interactions of radiations with matter have provided immense information regarding the structure of atoms and molecules. Neils Bohr utilised these results to improve upon the model proposed by Rutherford. Two developments played a major role in the formulation of Bohr’s model of atom. These were:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19948,53,123,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Dual character of the electromagnetic radiation which means that radiations possess both wave like and particle like properties, and','[{"s": "(i) Dual character of the electromagnetic radiation which means that radiations possess both wave like and particle like properties, and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19949,53,124,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Experimental results regarding atomic spectra.','[{"s": "(ii) Experimental results regarding atomic spectra.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19950,53,125,'paragraph',NULL,'First, we will discuss about the duel nature of electromagnetic radiations. Experimental results regarding atomic spectra will be discussed in Section 2.4.','[{"s": "First, we will discuss about the duel nature of electromagnetic radiations. Experimental results regarding atomic spectra will be discussed in Section 2.4.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19951,53,126,'heading',4,'2.3.1 Wave Nature of Electromagnetic Radiation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26446,66,16,'paragraph',NULL,'Fluids are everywhere around us. Earth has an envelop of air and two-thirds of its surface is covered with water. Water is not only necessary for our existence; every mammalian body constitute mostly of water. All the processes occurring in living beings including plants are mediated by fluids. Thus understanding the behaviour and properties of fluids is important.','[{"s": "Fluids are everywhere around us. Earth has an envelop of air and two-thirds of its surface is covered with water. Water is not only necessary for our existence; every mammalian body constitute mostly of water. All the processes occurring in living beings including plants are mediated by fluids. Thus understanding the behaviour and properties of fluids is important.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27782,68,489,'paragraph',NULL,'3. A vector A multiplied by a real number λ is also a vector, whose magnitude is λ times the magnitude of the vector A and whose direction is the same or opposite depending upon whether λ is positive or negative.','[{"s": "3. A vector A multiplied by a real number λ is also a vector, whose magnitude is λ times the magnitude of the vector A and whose direction is the same or opposite depending upon whether λ is positive or negative.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19952,53,127,'paragraph',NULL,'In the mid-nineteenth century, physicists actively studied absorption and emission of radiation by heated objects. These are called thermal radiations. They tried to find out of what the thermal radiation is made. It is now a well-known fact that thermal radiations consist of electromagnetic waves of various frequencies or wavelengths. It is based on a number of modern concepts, which were unknown in the mid-nineteenth century. First active study of thermal radiation laws occured in the 1850’s and the theory of electromagnetic waves and the emission of such waves by accelerating charged particles was developed in the early 1870’s by James Clerk Maxwell, which was experimentally confirmed later by Heinrich Hertz. Here, we will learn some facts about electromagnetic radiations.','[{"s": "In the mid-nineteenth century, physicists actively studied absorption and emission of radiation by heated objects. These are called thermal radiations. They tried to find out of what the thermal radiation is made. It is now a well-known fact that thermal radiations consist of electromagnetic waves of various frequencies or wavelengths. It is based on a number of modern concepts, which were unknown in the mid-nineteenth century. First active study of thermal radiation laws occured in the 1850’s and the theory of electromagnetic waves and the emission of such waves by accelerating charged particles was developed in the early 1870’s by James Clerk Maxwell, which was experimentally confirmed later by Heinrich Hertz. Here, we will learn some facts about electromagnetic radiations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19953,53,128,'paragraph',NULL,'James Maxwell (1870) was the first to give a comprehensive explanation about the interaction between the charged bodies and the behaviour of electrical and magnetic fields on macroscopic level. He suggested that when electrically charged particle moves under accelaration, alternating electrical and magnetic fields are produced and transmitted. These fields are transmitted in the forms of waves called electromagnetic waves or electromagnetic radiation .','[{"s": "James Maxwell (1870) was the first to give a comprehensive explanation about the interaction between the charged bodies and the behaviour of electrical and magnetic fields on macroscopic level. He suggested that when electrically charged particle moves under accelaration, alternating electrical and magnetic fields are produced and transmitted. These fields are transmitted in the forms of waves called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electromagnetic waves", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electromagnetic radiation", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19954,53,129,'paragraph',NULL,'Light is the form of radiation known from early days and speculation about its nature dates back to remote ancient times. In earlier days (Newton) light was supposed to be made of particles (corpuscules). It was only in the 19th century when wave nature of light was established.','[{"s": "Light is the form of radiation known from early days and speculation about its nature dates back to remote ancient times. In earlier days (Newton) light was supposed to be made of particles (corpuscules). It was only in the 19th century when wave nature of light was established.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19955,53,130,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image42.png'),
(19956,53,131,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.2.6 The electric and magnetic field components of an electromagnetic wave. These components have the same wavelength, frequency, speed and amplitude, but they vibrate in two mutually perpendicular planes.','[{"s": "Fig.2.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The electric and magnetic field components of an electromagnetic wave. These components have the same wavelength, frequency, speed and amplitude, but they vibrate in two mutually perpendicular planes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19957,53,132,'paragraph',NULL,'Maxwell was again the first to reveal that light waves are associated with oscillating electric and magnetic character (Fig. 2.6). Although electromagnetic wave motion is complex in nature, we will consider here only a few simple properties.','[{"s": "Maxwell was again the first to reveal that light waves are associated with oscillating electric and magnetic character (Fig. 2.6). Although electromagnetic wave motion is complex in nature, we will consider here only a few simple properties.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19958,53,133,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The oscillating electric and magnetic fields produced by oscillating charged particles are perpendicular to each other and both are perpendicular to the direction of propagation of the wave. Simplified picture of electromagnetic wave is shown in Fig. 2.6.','[{"s": "(i) The oscillating electric and magnetic fields produced by oscillating charged particles are perpendicular to each other and both are perpendicular to the direction of propagation of the wave. Simplified picture of electromagnetic wave is shown in Fig. 2.6.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19959,53,134,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Unlike sound waves or waves produced in water, electromagnetic waves do not require medium and can move in vacuum.','[{"s": "(ii) Unlike sound waves or waves produced in water, electromagnetic waves do not require medium and can move in vacuum.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19960,53,135,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) It is now well established that there are many types of electromagnetic radiations, which differ from one another in wavelength (or frequency). These constitute what is called electromagnetic spectrum (Fig. 2.7). Different regions of the spectrum are identified by different names. Some examples are: radio frequency region around 10 6 Hz, used for broadcasting; microwave region around 10 10 Hz used for radar; infrared region around 10 13 Hz used for heating; ultraviolet region around 10 16 Hz a component of sun’s radiation. The small portion around 10 15 Hz, is what is ordinarily called visible light . It is only this part which our eyes can see (or detect). Special instruments are required to detect non-visible radiation.','[{"s": "(iii) It is now well established that there are many types of electromagnetic radiations, which differ from one another in wavelength (or frequency). These constitute what is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electromagnetic spectrum", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Fig. 2.7). Different regions of the spectrum are identified by different names. Some examples are: radio frequency region around 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz, used for broadcasting; microwave region around 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz used for radar; infrared region around 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz used for heating; ultraviolet region around 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz a component of sun’s radiation. The small portion around 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz, is what is ordinarily called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "visible light", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is only this part which our eyes can see (or detect). Special instruments are required to detect non-visible radiation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19961,53,136,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Different kinds of units are used to represent electromagnetic radiation.','[{"s": "(iv) Different kinds of units are used to represent electromagnetic radiation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19962,53,137,'paragraph',NULL,'These radiations are characterised by the properties, namely, frequency (ν) and wavelength (λ).','[{"s": "These radiations are characterised by the properties, namely, frequency (ν) and wavelength (λ).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19963,53,138,'paragraph',NULL,'The SI unit for frequency (ν) is hertz (Hz, s –1 ), after Heinrich Hertz. It is defined as the number of waves that pass a given point in one second.','[{"s": "The SI unit for frequency (ν) is hertz (Hz, s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), after Heinrich Hertz. It is defined as the number of waves that pass a given point in one second.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19964,53,139,'paragraph',NULL,'Wavelength should have the units of length and as you know that the SI units of length is meter (m). Since electromagnetic radiation consists of different kinds of waves of much smaller wavelengths, smaller units are used. Fig.2.7 shows various types of electro-magnetic radiations which differ from one another in wavelengths and frequencies.','[{"s": "Wavelength should have the units of length and as you know that the SI units of length is meter (m). Since electromagnetic radiation consists of different kinds of waves of much smaller wavelengths, smaller units are used. Fig.2.7 shows various types of electro-magnetic radiations which differ from one another in wavelengths and frequencies.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19965,53,140,'paragraph',NULL,'In vaccum all types of electromagnetic radiations, regardless of wavelength, travel at the same speed, i.e., 3.0 × 10 8 m s –1 (2.997925 × 10 8 m s –1 , to be precise). This is called speed of light and is given the symbol ‘c‘. The frequency (ν ), wavelength (λ) and velocity of light (c) are related by the equation (2.5).','[{"s": "In vaccum all types of electromagnetic radiations, regardless of wavelength, travel at the same speed, i.e., 3.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.997925 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", to be precise). This is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "speed of light", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and is given the symbol ‘c‘. The frequency (ν ), wavelength (λ) and velocity of light (c) are related by the equation (2.5).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19966,53,141,'paragraph',NULL,'c = ν λ (2.5)','[{"s": "c = ν λ (2.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19967,53,142,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 09-46-11',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image68.png'),
(19968,53,143,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 2.7 (a) The spectrum of electromagnetic radiation. (b) Visible spectrum. The visible region is only a small part of the entire spectrum .','[{"s": "Fig. 2.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) The spectrum of electromagnetic radiation. (b) Visible spectrum. The visible region is only a small part of the entire spectrum", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19969,53,144,'paragraph',NULL,'The other commonly used quantity specially in spectroscopy, is the wavenumber ( Structure_of_Atom-image18.png ). It is defined as the number of wavelengths per unit length. Its units are reciprocal of wavelength unit, i.e., m –1 . However commonly used unit is cm –1 (not SI unit).','[{"s": "The other commonly used quantity specially in spectroscopy, is the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "wavenumber", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Structure_of_Atom-image18.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "). It is defined as the number of wavelengths per unit length. Its units are reciprocal of wavelength unit, i.e., m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". However commonly used unit is cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(not SI unit).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19970,53,145,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 2.3','[{"s": "Problem 2.3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19971,53,146,'paragraph',NULL,'The Vividh Bharati station of All India Radio, Delhi, broadcasts on a frequency of 1,368 kHz (kilo hertz). Calculate the wavelength of the electromagnetic radiation emitted by transmitter. Which part of the electromagnetic spectrum does it belong to?','[{"s": "The Vividh Bharati station of All India Radio, Delhi, broadcasts on a frequency of 1,368 kHz (kilo hertz). Calculate the wavelength of the electromagnetic radiation emitted by transmitter. Which part of the electromagnetic spectrum does it belong to?", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["22471"]',NULL),
(19972,53,147,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(19973,53,148,'paragraph',NULL,'The wavelength, λ, is equal to c/ν, where c is the speed of electromagnetic radiation in vacuum and ν is the frequency. Substituting the given values, we have','[{"s": "The wavelength, λ, is equal to c/ν, where c is the speed of electromagnetic radiation in vacuum and ν is the frequency.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Substituting the given values, we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
