(20003,53,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 2.8(a) Black body','[{"s": "Fig. 2.8(a) Black body", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20512,54,150,'paragraph',NULL,'The difference between ∆H and ∆U is not usually significant for systems consisting of only solids and / or liquids . Solids and liquids do not suffer any significant volume changes upon heating. The difference, however, becomes significant when gases are involved. Let us consider a reaction involving gases. If V A is the total volume of the gaseous reactants, V B is the total volume of the gaseous products, n A is the number of moles of gaseous reactants and n B is the number of moles of gaseous products, all at constant pressure and temperature, then using the ideal gas law, we write,','[{"s": "The difference between ∆H and ∆U is not usually significant for systems consisting of only solids and / or liquids", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". Solids and liquids do not suffer any significant volume changes upon heating. The difference, however, becomes significant when gases are involved. Let us consider a reaction involving gases. If V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the total volume of the gaseous reactants, V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the total volume of the gaseous products, n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the number of moles of gaseous reactants and n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the number of moles of gaseous products, all at constant pressure and temperature, then using the ideal gas law, we write,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["60944"]',NULL),
(20004,53,179,'paragraph',NULL,'But the results of the above experiment could not be explained satisfactorily on the basis of the wave theory of light. Max Planck arrived at a satisfactory relationship by making an assumption that absorption and emmission of radiation arises from oscillator i.e., atoms in the wall of black body. Their frequency of oscillation is changed by interaction with oscilators of electromagnetic radiation. Planck assumed that radiation could be sub-divided into discrete chunks of energy. He suggested that atoms and molecules could emit or absorb energy only in discrete quantities and not in a continuous manner. He gave the name quantum to the smallest quantity of energy that can be emitted or absorbed in the form of electromagnetic radiation. The energy (E ) of a quantum of radiation is proportional to its frequency (ν ) and is expressed by equation (2.6).','[{"s": "But the results of the above experiment could not be explained satisfactorily on the basis of the wave theory of light. Max Planck arrived at a satisfactory relationship by making an assumption that absorption and emmission of radiation arises from oscillator i.e., atoms in the wall of black body. Their frequency of oscillation is changed by interaction with oscilators of electromagnetic radiation. Planck assumed that radiation could be sub-divided into discrete chunks of energy. He suggested that atoms and molecules could emit or absorb energy only in discrete quantities and not in a continuous manner. He gave the name", "t": "text"}, {"s": "quantum", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to the smallest quantity of energy that can be emitted or absorbed in the form of electromagnetic radiation.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The energy (E ) of a quantum of radiation is proportional to its frequency (ν ) and is expressed by equation (2.6).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["22407"]',NULL),
(20005,53,180,'paragraph',NULL,'E = hυ (2.6)','[{"s": "E = hυ (2.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20006,53,181,'paragraph',NULL,'The proportionality constant, ‘h’ is known as Planck’s constant and has the value 6.626×10–34 J s.','[{"s": "The proportionality constant, ‘h’ is known as Planck’s constant and has the value 6.626×10–34 J s.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["22412"]',NULL),
(20007,53,182,'paragraph',NULL,'With this theory, Planck was able to explain the distribution of intensity in the radiation from black body as a function of frequency or wavelength at different temperatures.','[{"s": "With this theory, Planck was able to explain the distribution of intensity in the radiation from black body as a function of frequency or wavelength at different temperatures.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["22410"]',NULL),
(20008,53,183,'paragraph',NULL,'Quantisation has been compared to standing on a staircase. A person can stand on any step of a staircase, but it is not possible for him/her to stand in between the two steps. The energy can take any one of the values from the following set, but cannot take on any values between them.','[{"s": "Quantisation has been compared to standing on a staircase. A person can stand on any step of a staircase, but it is not possible for him/her to stand in between the two steps. The energy can take any one of the values from the following set, but cannot take on any values between them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20009,53,184,'paragraph',NULL,'E = 0, hυ, 2hυ, 3hυ....nhυ.....','[{"s": "E = 0, hυ, 2hυ, 3hυ....nhυ.....", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
