(20083,53,258,'paragraph',NULL,'The threshold frequency ν 0 for a metal is 7.0 ×10 14 s –1 . Calculate the kinetic energy of an electron emitted when radiation of frequency ν =1.0 ×10 15 s –1 hits the metal.','[{"s": "The threshold frequency ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for a metal is 7.0 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Calculate the kinetic energy of an electron emitted when radiation of frequency ν =1.0 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hits the metal.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20084,53,259,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20085,53,260,'paragraph',NULL,'According to Einstein’s equation','[{"s": "According to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Einstein’s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "equation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20086,53,261,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic energy = ½ m e v 2 =h(ν – ν 0 )','[{"s": "Kinetic energy = ½ m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=h(ν – ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20087,53,262,'paragraph',NULL,'= (6.626 ×10 –34 J s) (1.0 × 10 15 s –1 – 7.0 ×10 14 s –1 )','[{"s": "= (6.626 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–34", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J s) (1.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– 7.0 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20088,53,263,'paragraph',NULL,'= (6.626 ×10 –34 J s) (10.0 ×10 14 s –1 – 7.0 ×10 14 s –1 )','[{"s": "= (6.626 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–34", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J s) (10.0 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– 7.0 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20089,53,264,'paragraph',NULL,'= (6.626 ×10 –34 J s) × (3.0 ×10 14 s –1 )','[{"s": "= (6.626 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–34", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J s) × (3.0 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20090,53,265,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1.988 ×10 –19 J','[{"s": "= 1.988 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20091,53,266,'paragraph',NULL,'2.3.3 Evidence for the quantized Electronic Energy Levels: Atomic spectra','[{"s": "2.3.3 Evidence for the quantized Electronic Energy Levels: Atomic spectra", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20515,54,153,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, pV B – pV A = n B RT – n A RT = (n B –n A )RT','[{"s": "Thus, pV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– pV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RT – n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RT = (n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")RT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20092,53,267,'paragraph',NULL,'The speed of light depends upon the nature of the medium through which it passes. As a result, the beam of light is deviated or refracted from its original path as it passes from one medium to another. It is observed that when a ray of white light is passed through a prism, the wave with shorter wavelength bends more than the one with a longer wavelength. Since ordinary white light consists of waves with all the wavelengths in the visible range, a ray of white light is spread out into a series of coloured bands called spectrum . The light of red colour which has longest wavelength is deviated the least while the violet light, which has shortest wavelength is deviated the most. The spectrum of white light, that we can see, ranges from violet at 7.50 × 10 14 Hz to red at 4×10 14 Hz. Such a spectrum is called continuous spectrum. Continuous because violet merges into blue, blue into green and so on. A similar spectrum is produced when a rainbow forms in the sky. Remember that visible light is just a small portion of the electromagnetic radiation (Fig.2.7). When electromagnetic radiation interacts with matter, atoms and molecules may absorb energy and reach to a higher energy state. With higher energy, these are in an unstable state. For returning to their normal (more stable, lower energy states) energy state, the atoms and molecules emit radiations in various regions of the electromagnetic spectrum.','[{"s": "The speed of light depends upon the nature of the medium through which it passes. As a result, the beam of light is deviated or refracted from its original path as it passes from one medium to another. It is observed that when a ray of white light is passed through a prism, the wave with shorter wavelength bends more than the one with a longer wavelength. Since ordinary white light consists of waves with all the wavelengths in the visible range, a ray of white light is spread out into a series of coloured bands called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "spectrum", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The light of red colour which has longest wavelength is deviated the least while the violet light, which has shortest wavelength is deviated the most. The spectrum of white light, that we can see, ranges from violet at 7.50 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz to red at 4×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz. Such a spectrum is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "continuous spectrum.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Continuous because violet merges into blue, blue into green and so on. A similar spectrum is produced when a rainbow forms in the sky. Remember that visible light is just a small portion of the electromagnetic radiation (Fig.2.7). When electromagnetic radiation interacts with matter, atoms and molecules may absorb energy and reach to a higher energy state. With higher energy, these are in an unstable state. For returning to their normal (more stable, lower energy states) energy state, the atoms and molecules emit radiations in various regions of the electromagnetic spectrum.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20093,53,268,'paragraph',NULL,'Emission and Absorption Spectra','[{"s": "Emission and Absorption Spectra", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(20094,53,269,'paragraph',NULL,'The spectrum of radiation emitted by a substance that has absorbed energy is called an emission spectrum . Atoms, molecules or ions that have absorbed radiation are said to be “excited” . To produce an emission spectrum, energy is supplied to a sample by heating it or irradiating it and the wavelength (or frequency) of the radiation emitted, as the sample gives up the absorbed energy, is recorded.','[{"s": "The spectrum of radiation emitted by a substance that has absorbed energy is called an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "emission spectrum", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Atoms, molecules or ions that have absorbed radiation are said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "“excited”", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". To produce an emission spectrum, energy is supplied to a sample by heating it or irradiating it and the wavelength (or frequency) of the radiation emitted, as the sample gives up the absorbed energy, is recorded.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20095,53,270,'paragraph',NULL,'An absorption spectrum is like the photographic negative of an emission spectrum. A continuum of radiation is passed through a sample which absorbs radiation of certain wavelengths. The missing wavelength which corresponds to the radiation absorbed by the matter, leave dark spaces in the bright continuous spectrum.','[{"s": "An absorption spectrum is like the photographic negative of an emission spectrum. A continuum of radiation is passed through a sample which absorbs radiation of certain wavelengths. The missing wavelength which corresponds to the radiation absorbed by the matter, leave dark spaces in the bright continuous spectrum.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20096,53,271,'paragraph',NULL,'The study of emission or absorption spectra is referred to as spectroscopy . The spectrum of the visible light, as discussed above, was continuous as all wavelengths (red to violet) of the visible light are represented in the spectra. The emission spectra of atoms in the gas phase, on the other hand, do not show a continuous spread of wavelength from red to violet, rather they emit light only at specific wavelengths with dark spaces between them. Such spectra are called line spectra or atomic spectra because the emitted radiation is identified by the appearance of bright lines in the spectra (Fig. 2.10 page 45).','[{"s": "The study of emission or absorption spectra is referred to as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "spectroscopy", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The spectrum of the visible light, as discussed above, was continuous as all wavelengths (red to violet) of the visible light are represented in the spectra. The emission spectra of atoms in the gas phase, on the other hand, do not show a continuous spread of wavelength from red to violet, rather they emit light only at specific wavelengths with dark spaces between them. Such spectra are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "line spectra", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atomic spectra", "t": "text"}, {"s": "because the emitted radiation is identified by the appearance of bright lines in the spectra (Fig. 2.10 page 45).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20097,53,272,'paragraph',NULL,'Line emission spectra are of great interest in the study of electronic structure. Each element has a unique line emission spectrum. The characteristic lines in atomic spectra can be used in chemical analysis to identify unknown atoms in the same way as fingerprints are used to identify people. The exact matching of lines of the emission spectrum of the atoms of a known element with the lines from an unknown sample quickly establishes the identity of the latter, German chemist, Robert Bunsen (1811-1899) was one of the first investigators to use line spectra to identify elements.','[{"s": "Line emission spectra are of great interest in the study of electronic structure. Each element has a unique line emission spectrum. The characteristic lines in atomic spectra can be used in chemical analysis to identify unknown atoms in the same way as fingerprints are used to identify people. The exact matching of lines of the emission spectrum of the atoms of a known element with the lines from an unknown sample quickly establishes the identity of the latter, German chemist, Robert Bunsen (1811-1899) was one of the first investigators to use line spectra to identify elements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20098,53,273,'paragraph',NULL,'Elements like rubidium (Rb), caesium (Cs) thallium (Tl), indium (In), gallium (Ga) and scandium (Sc) were discovered when their minerals were analysed by spectroscopic methods. The element helium (He) was discovered in the sun by spectroscopic method.','[{"s": "Elements like rubidium (Rb), caesium (Cs) thallium (Tl), indium (In), gallium (Ga) and scandium (Sc) were discovered when their minerals were analysed by spectroscopic methods. The element helium (He) was discovered in the sun by spectroscopic method.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20099,53,274,'paragraph',NULL,'Line Spectrum of Hydrogen','[{"s": "Line Spectrum of Hydrogen", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20100,53,275,'paragraph',NULL,'When an electric discharge is passed through gaseous hydrogen, the H 2 molecules dissociate and the energetically excited hydrogen atoms produced emit electromagnetic radiation of discrete frequencies. The hydrogen spectrum consists of several series of lines named after their discoverers. Balmer showed in 1885 on the basis of experimental observations that if spectral lines are expressed in terms of wavenumber ( Structure_of_Atom-image23.png ), then the visible lines of the hydrogen spectrum obey the following formula:','[{"s": "When an electric discharge is passed through gaseous hydrogen, the H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules dissociate and the energetically excited hydrogen atoms produced emit electromagnetic radiation of discrete frequencies. The hydrogen spectrum consists of several series of lines named after their discoverers. Balmer showed in 1885 on the basis of experimental observations that if spectral lines are expressed in terms of wavenumber (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Structure_of_Atom-image23.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "), then the visible lines of the hydrogen spectrum obey the following formula:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20101,53,276,'paragraph',NULL,'* The restriction of any property to discrete values is called quantization.','[{"s": "*", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The restriction of any property to discrete values is called quantization.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20102,53,277,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 10-35-42',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image71.png'),
(20103,53,278,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 2.10 (a) Atomic emission. The light emitted by a sample of excited hydrogen atoms (or any other element) can be passed through a prism and separated into certain discrete wavelengths. Thus an emission spectrum, which is a photographic recording of the separated wavelengths is called as line spectrum. Any sample of reasonable size contains an enormous number of atoms. Although a single atom can be in only one excited state at a time, the collection of atoms contains all possible excited states. The light emitted as these atoms fall to lower energy states is responsible for the spectrum. (b) Atomic absorption. When white light is passed through unexcited atomic hydrogen and then through a slit and prism, the transmitted light is lacking in intensity at the same wavelengths as are emitted in (a) The recorded absorption spectrum is also a line spectrum and the photographic negative of the emission spectrum.','[{"s": "Fig. 2.10 (a) Atomic emission.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The light emitted by a sample of excited hydrogen atoms (or any other element) can be passed through a prism and separated into certain discrete wavelengths. Thus an emission spectrum, which is a photographic recording of the separated wavelengths is called as line spectrum. Any sample of reasonable size contains an enormous number of atoms. Although a single atom can be in only one excited state at a time, the collection of atoms contains all possible excited states. The light emitted as these atoms fall to lower energy states is responsible for the spectrum.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(b) Atomic absorption.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When white light is passed through unexcited atomic hydrogen and then through a slit and prism, the transmitted light is lacking in intensity at the same wavelengths as are emitted in (a) The recorded absorption spectrum is also a line spectrum and the photographic negative of the emission spectrum.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20104,53,279,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 10-36-49',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image48.png'),
(20105,53,280,'paragraph',NULL,'where n is an integer equal to or greater than 3 (i.e., n = 3,4,5,....)','[{"s": "where n is an integer equal to or greater than 3 (i.e., n = 3,4,5,....)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20106,53,281,'paragraph',NULL,'The series of lines described by this formula are called the Balmer series . of the electromagnetic spectrum. expression :','[{"s": "The series of lines described by this formula are called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Balmer series", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "of the electromagnetic spectrum.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "expression :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20107,53,282,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 10-37-13',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image67.png'),
(20108,53,283,'paragraph',NULL,'where n 1 =1,2........','[{"s": "where n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=1,2........", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20109,53,284,'paragraph',NULL,'n 2 = n 1 + 1, n 1 + 2......','[{"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 1, n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 2......", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20110,53,285,'paragraph',NULL,'The value 109,677 cm –1 is called the Rydberg constant for hydrogen. The first five series of lines that correspond to n 1 = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are known as Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Bracket and Pfund series, respectively, Table 2.3 shows these series of transitions in the hydrogen spectrum. Fig 2.11 (page, 46) shows the Lyman, Balmer and Paschen series of transitions for hydrogen atom.','[{"s": "The value 109,677 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Rydberg constant", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for hydrogen.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The first five series of lines that correspond to n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are known as Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Bracket and Pfund series, respectively, Table 2.3 shows these series of transitions in the hydrogen spectrum. Fig 2.11 (page, 46) shows the Lyman, Balmer and Paschen series of transitions for hydrogen atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20111,53,286,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 2.3 The Spectral Lines for Atomic Hydrogen','[{"s": "Table 2.3 The Spectral Lines for Atomic Hydrogen", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20112,53,287,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image22.png'),
(20113,53,288,'paragraph',NULL,'Of all the elements, hydrogen atom has the simplest line spectrum. Line spectrum becomes more and more complex for heavier atom. There are, however, certain features which are common to all line spectra, i.e., (i) line spectrum of element is unique and (ii) there is regularity in the line spectrum of each element. The questions which arise are: What are the reasons for these similarities? Is it something to do with the electronic structure of atoms? These are the questions need to be answered. We shall find later that the answers to these questions provide the key in understanding electronic structure of these elements.','[{"s": "Of all the elements, hydrogen atom has the simplest line spectrum. Line spectrum becomes more and more complex for heavier atom. There are, however, certain features which are common to all line spectra, i.e., (i) line spectrum of element is unique and (ii) there is regularity in the line spectrum of each element. The questions which arise are: What are the reasons for these similarities? Is it something to do with the electronic structure of atoms? These are the questions need to be answered. We shall find later that the answers to these questions provide the key in understanding electronic structure of these elements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20114,53,289,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image20.png'),
(20115,53,290,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 2.11 Transitions of the electron in the hydrogen atom (The diagram shows the Lyman, Balmer and Paschen series of transitions)','[{"s": "Fig. 2.11 Transitions of the electron in the hydrogen atom (The diagram shows the Lyman, Balmer and Paschen series of transitions)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20116,53,291,'heading',3,'2.4 Bohr’s Model for Hydrogen Atom',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20117,53,292,'paragraph',NULL,'Neils Bohr (1913) was the first to explain quantitatively the general features of the structure of hydrogen atom and its spectrum. He used Planck’s concept of quantisation of energy. Though the theory is not the modern quantum mechanics, it can still be used to rationalize many points in the atomic structure and spectra. Bohr’s model for hydrogen atom is based on the following postulates:','[{"s": "Neils Bohr (1913) was the first to explain quantitatively the general features of the structure of hydrogen atom and its spectrum. He used Planck’s concept of quantisation of energy. Though the theory is not the modern quantum mechanics, it can still be used to rationalize many points in the atomic structure and spectra.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Bohr’s model for hydrogen atom is based on the following postulates:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20118,53,293,'paragraph',NULL,'i) The electron in the hydrogen atom can move around the nucleus in a circular path of fixed radius and energy. These paths are called orbits, stationary states or allowed energy states. These orbits are arranged concentrically around the nucleus. ii) The energy of an electron in the orbit does not change with time. However, the electron will move from a lower stationary state to a higher stationary state when required amount of energy is absorbed by the electron or energy is emitted when electron moves from higher stationary state to lower stationary state (equation 2.16). The energy change does not take place in a continuous manner.','[{"s": "i) The electron in the hydrogen atom can move around the nucleus in a circular path of fixed radius and energy. These paths are called orbits, stationary states or allowed energy states. These orbits are arranged concentrically around the nucleus.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ii) The energy of an electron in the orbit does not change with time. However, the electron will move from a lower stationary state to a higher stationary state when required amount of energy is absorbed by the electron or energy is emitted when electron moves from higher stationary state to lower stationary state (equation 2.16). The energy change does not take place in a continuous manner.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20119,53,294,'paragraph',NULL,'Angular Momentum','[{"s": "Angular Momentum", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20120,53,295,'paragraph',NULL,'Just as linear momentum is the product of mass (m) and linear velocity (v), angular momentum is the product of moment of inertia (I) and angular velocity (ω). For an electron of mass m e , moving in a circular path of radius r around the nucleus,','[{"s": "Just as linear momentum is the product of mass (m) and linear velocity (v), angular momentum is the product of moment of inertia (I) and angular velocity (ω). For an electron of mass m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", moving in a circular path of radius r around the nucleus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20121,53,296,'paragraph',NULL,'angular momentum = I × ω','[{"s": "angular momentum = I × ω", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20122,53,297,'paragraph',NULL,'Since I = m e r 2 , and ω = v/r where v is the linear velocity,','[{"s": "Since I = m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and ω = v/r where v is the linear velocity,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20123,53,298,'paragraph',NULL,'∴angular momentum = m e r 2 × v/r = m e vr','[{"s": "∴angular momentum = m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× v/r = m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "vr", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20124,53,299,'paragraph',NULL,'iii) The frequency of radiation absorbed or emitted when transition occurs between two stationary states that differ in energy by ∆E, is given by:','[{"s": "iii) The frequency of radiation absorbed or emitted when transition occurs between two stationary states that differ in energy by ∆E, is given by:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20125,53,300,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 10-55-21',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image78.png'),
(20126,53,301,'paragraph',NULL,'Where E 1 and E 2 are the energies of the lower and higher allowed energy states respectively. This expression is commonly known as Bohr’s frequency rule.','[{"s": "Where E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the energies of the lower and higher allowed energy states respectively. This expression is commonly known as Bohr’s frequency rule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20127,53,302,'paragraph',NULL,'iv) The angular momentum of an electron is quantised. In a given stationary state it can be expressed as in equation (2.11)','[{"s": "iv) The angular momentum of an electron is quantised. In a given stationary state it can be expressed as in equation (2.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20128,53,303,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 10-55-54',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image27.png'),
(20129,53,304,'paragraph',NULL,'Where m e is the mass of electron, v is the velocity and r is the radius of the orbit in which electron is moving.','[{"s": "Where m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the mass of electron, v is the velocity and r is the radius of the orbit in which electron is moving.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20130,53,305,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus an electron can move only in those orbits for which its angular momentum is integral multiple of h/2π. That means angular momentum is quantised. Radiation is emitted or obsorbed only when transition of electron takes place from one quantised value of angular momentum to another. Therefore, Maxwell’s electromagnetic theory does not apply here that is why only certain fixed orbits are allowed.','[{"s": "Thus an electron can move only in those orbits for which its angular momentum is integral multiple of h/2π. That means angular momentum is quantised. Radiation is emitted or obsorbed only when transition of electron takes place from one quantised value of angular momentum to another. Therefore, Maxwell’s electromagnetic theory does not apply here that is why only certain fixed orbits are allowed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20131,53,306,'paragraph',NULL,'The details regarding the derivation of energies of the stationary states used by Bohr, are quite complicated and will be discussed in higher classes. However, according to Bohr’s theory for hydrogen atom:','[{"s": "The details regarding the derivation of energies of the stationary states used by Bohr, are quite complicated and will be discussed in higher classes. However,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "according to Bohr’s theory for hydrogen atom:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20132,53,307,'paragraph',NULL,'a) The stationary states for electron are numbered n = 1,2,3.......... These integral numbers (Section 2.6.2) are known as Principal quantum numbers.','[{"s": "a) The stationary states for electron are numbered n = 1,2,3.......... These integral numbers (Section 2.6.2) are known as Principal quantum numbers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20516,54,154,'paragraph',NULL,'or p (V B – V A ) = (n B – n A ) RT','[{"s": "or p (V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = (n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") RT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20517,54,155,'paragraph',NULL,'or p ∆V = ∆n g RT (6.9)','[{"s": "or p ∆V = ∆n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RT (6.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20518,54,156,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, ∆n g refers to the number of moles of gaseous products minus the number of moles of gaseous reactants.','[{"s": "Here, ∆n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "refers to the number of moles of gaseous products minus the number of moles of gaseous reactants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20133,53,308,'paragraph',NULL,'b) The radii of the stationary states are expressed as: r n = n 2 a 0 (2.12) where a 0 = 52.9 pm. Thus the radius of the first stationary state, called the Bohr orbit, is 52.9 pm. Normally the electron in the hydrogen atom is found in this orbit (that is n=1). As n increases the value of r will increase. In other words the electron will be present away from the nucleus.','[{"s": "b) The radii of the stationary states are expressed as: r", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "n", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "= n", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "a", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "0", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(2.12)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "where a", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "0", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "= 52.9 pm.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus the radius of the first stationary state, called the Bohr orbit, is 52.9 pm. Normally the electron in the hydrogen atom is found in this orbit (that is n=1). As n increases the value of r will increase. In other words the electron will be present away from the nucleus.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["22414", "22420", "22422", "22425"]',NULL),
(20134,53,309,'paragraph',NULL,'c) The most important property associated with the electron, is the energy of its stationary state. It is given by the expression.','[{"s": "c) The most important property associated with the electron, is the energy of its stationary state. It is given by the expression.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["22414", "22423", "22478"]',NULL),
(20135,53,310,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 10-57-36',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image52.png'),
(20136,53,311,'paragraph',NULL,'where R H is called Rydberg constant and its value is 2.18×10 –18 1 = –2.18×10 –18 ( Structure_of_Atom-image37.png ) = –2.18×10 –18 J. The energy of the stationary state for n = 2, will','[{"s": "where R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Rydberg constant", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and its value is 2.18×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –2.18×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Structure_of_Atom-image37.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-03 10-58-14"}, {"s": ") = –2.18×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J. The energy of the stationary state for n = 2, will", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20137,53,312,'paragraph',NULL,'be : E 2 = –2.18×10 –18 J ( Structure_of_Atom-image76.png )= –0.545×10 –18 J. Fig. 2.11 depicts the energies of different stationary states or energy levels of hydrogen atom. This representation is called an energy level diagram.','[{"s": "be : E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –2.18×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Structure_of_Atom-image76.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-03 10-58-59"}, {"s": ")= –0.545×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J. Fig. 2.11 depicts the energies of different stationary states or energy levels of hydrogen atom. This representation is called an energy level diagram.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20138,53,313,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image72.png'),
(20139,53,314,'paragraph',NULL,'Niels Bohr','[{"s": "Niels Bohr", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20140,53,315,'paragraph',NULL,'(1885–1962)','[{"s": "(1885–1962)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20141,53,316,'paragraph',NULL,'Niels Bohr, a Danish physicist received his Ph.D. from the University of Copenhagen in 1911. He then spent a year with J.J. Thomson and Ernest Rutherford in England. In 1913, he returned to Copenhagen where he remained for the rest of his life. In 1920 he was named Director of the Institute of theoretical Physics. After first World War, Bohr worked energetically for peaceful uses of atomic energy. He received the first Atoms for Peace award in 1957. Bohr was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1922.','[{"s": "Niels Bohr, a Danish physicist received his Ph.D. from the University of Copenhagen in 1911. He then spent a year with J.J. Thomson and Ernest Rutherford in England. In 1913, he returned to Copenhagen where he remained for the rest of his life. In 1920 he was named Director of the Institute of theoretical Physics. After first World War, Bohr worked energetically for peaceful uses of atomic energy. He received the first Atoms for Peace award in 1957. Bohr was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1922.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20142,53,317,'paragraph',NULL,'When the electron is free from the influence of nucleus, the energy is taken as zero. The electron in this situation is associated with the stationary state of Principal Quantum number = n = ∞ and is called as ionized hydrogen atom. When the electron is attracted by the nucleus and is present in orbit n, the energy is emitted and its energy is lowered. That is the reason for the presence of negative sign in equation (2.13) and depicts its stability relative to the reference state of zero energy and n = ∞.','[{"s": "When the electron is free from the influence of nucleus, the energy is taken as zero. The electron in this situation is associated with the stationary state of Principal Quantum number = n = ∞ and is called as ionized hydrogen atom. When the electron is attracted by the nucleus and is present in orbit n, the energy is emitted and its energy is lowered. That is the reason for the presence of negative sign in equation (2.13) and depicts its stability relative to the reference state of zero energy and n = ∞.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20143,53,318,'paragraph',NULL,'What does the negative electronic energy (En) for hydrogen atom mean?','[{"s": "What does the negative electronic energy (En) for hydrogen atom mean?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20144,53,319,'paragraph',NULL,'The energy of the electron in a hydrogen atom has a negative sign for all possible orbits (eq. 2.13). What does this negative sign convey? This negative sign means that the energy of the electron in the atom is lower than the energy of a free electron at rest. A free electron at rest is an electron that is infinitely far away from the nucleus and is assigned the energy value of zero. Mathematically, this corresponds to setting n equal to infinity in the equation (2.13) so that E∞=0. As the electron gets closer to the nucleus (as n decreases), En becomes larger in absolute value and more and more negative. The most negative energy value is given by n=1 which corresponds to the most stable orbit. We call this the ground state.','[{"s": "The energy of the electron in a hydrogen atom has a negative sign for all possible orbits (eq. 2.13). What does this negative sign convey? This negative sign means that the energy of the electron in the atom is lower than the energy of a free electron at rest. A free electron at rest is an electron that is infinitely far away from the nucleus and is assigned the energy value of zero. Mathematically, this corresponds to setting n equal to infinity in the equation (2.13) so that E∞=0. As the electron gets closer to the nucleus (as n decreases), En becomes larger in absolute value and more and more negative. The most negative energy value is given by n=1 which corresponds to the most stable orbit. We call this the ground state.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20145,53,320,'paragraph',NULL,'d) Bohr’s theory can also be applied to the ions containing only one electron, similar to that present in hydrogen atom. For example, He + Li 2+ , Be 3+ and so on. The energies of the stationary states associated with these kinds of ions (also known as hydrogen like species) are given by the expression.','[{"s": "d) Bohr’s theory can also be applied to the ions containing only one electron, similar to that present in hydrogen atom. For example, He", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Li", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", Be", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and so on. The energies of the stationary states associated with these kinds of ions (also known as hydrogen like species) are given by the expression.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["22416", "22422", "22425", "22434"]',NULL),
(20146,53,321,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 11-03-03',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image60.png'),
(20147,53,322,'paragraph',NULL,'where Z is the atomic number and has values 2,3 for the helium and lithium atoms respectively. From the above equations, it is evident that the value of energy becomes more negative and that of radius becomes smaller with increase of Z . This means that electron will be tightly bound to the nucleus.','[{"s": "where Z is the atomic number and has values 2,3 for the helium and lithium atoms respectively. From the above equations, it is evident that the value of energy becomes more negative and that of radius becomes smaller with increase of Z . This means that electron will be tightly bound to the nucleus.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20148,53,323,'paragraph',NULL,'e) It is also possible to calculate the velocities of electrons moving in these orbits. Although the precise equation is not given here, qualitatively the magnitude of velocity of electron increases with increase of positive charge on the nucleus and decreases with increase of principal quantum number.','[{"s": "e) It is also possible to calculate the velocities of electrons moving in these orbits. Although the precise equation is not given here, qualitatively the magnitude of velocity of electron increases with increase of positive charge on the nucleus and decreases with increase of principal quantum number.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20149,53,324,'paragraph',NULL,'2.4.1 Explanation of Line Spectrum of Hydrogen','[{"s": "2.4.1 Explanation of Line Spectrum of Hydrogen", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20150,53,325,'paragraph',NULL,'Line spectrum observed in case of hydrogen atom, as mentioned in section 2.3.3, can be explained quantitatively using Bohr’s model. According to assumption 2, radiation (energy) is absorbed if the electron moves from the orbit of smaller Principal quantum number to the orbit of higher Principal quantum number, whereas the radiation (energy) is emitted if the electron moves from higher orbit to lower orbit. The energy gap between the two orbits is given by equation (2.16)','[{"s": "Line spectrum observed in case of hydrogen atom, as mentioned in section 2.3.3, can be explained quantitatively using Bohr’s model.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "According to assumption 2, radiation (energy) is absorbed if the electron moves from the orbit of smaller Principal quantum number to the orbit of higher Principal quantum number, whereas the radiation (energy) is emitted if the electron moves from higher orbit to lower orbit.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The energy gap between the two orbits is given by equation (2.16)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["22421"]',NULL),
(20151,53,326,'paragraph',NULL,'∆E = E f – E i (2.16)','[{"s": "∆E = E", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "– E", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "i", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(2.16)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["22421"]',NULL),
(20152,53,327,'paragraph',NULL,'Combining equations (2.13) and (2.16)','[{"s": "Combining equations (2.13) and (2.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20153,53,328,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 13-56-44',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image79.png'),
(20154,53,329,'paragraph',NULL,'The frequency (ν ) associated with the absorption and emission of the photon can be evaluated by using equation (2.18)','[{"s": "The frequency (ν ) associated with the absorption and emission of the photon can be evaluated by using equation (2.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20155,53,330,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 13-57-49',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image62.png'),
(20156,53,331,'paragraph',NULL,'In case of absorption spectrum, n f > n i and the term in the parenthesis is positive and energy is absorbed. On the other hand in case of emission spectrum n i > n f , ∆ E is negative and energy is released.','[{"s": "In case of absorption spectrum, n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the term in the parenthesis is positive and energy is absorbed. On the other hand in case of emission spectrum n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", ∆ E is negative and energy is released.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20157,53,332,'paragraph',NULL,'The expression (2.17) is similar to that used by Rydberg (2.9) derived empirically using the experimental data available at that time. Further, each spectral line, whether in absorption or emission spectrum, can be associated to the particular transition in hydrogen atom. In case of large number of hydrogen atoms, different possible transitions can be observed and thus leading to large number of spectral lines. The brightness or intensity of spectral lines depends upon the number of photons of same What are the frequency and wavelength of a photon emitted during a transition from n = 5 state to the n = 2 state in the hydrogen atom?','[{"s": "The expression (2.17) is similar to that used by Rydberg (2.9) derived empirically using the experimental data available at that time. Further, each spectral line, whether in absorption or emission spectrum, can be associated to the particular transition in hydrogen atom. In case of large number of hydrogen atoms, different possible transitions can be observed and thus leading to large number of spectral lines. The brightness or intensity of spectral lines depends upon the number of photons of same", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What are the frequency and wavelength of a photon emitted during a transition from n = 5 state to the n = 2 state in the hydrogen atom?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20158,53,333,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20159,53,334,'paragraph',NULL,'Since n i = 5 and n f = 2, this transition gives rise to a spectral line in the visible region of the Balmer series. From equation (2.17)','[{"s": "Since n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5 and n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2, this transition gives rise to a spectral line in the visible region of the Balmer series. From equation (2.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
