(20198,53,373,'heading',4,'2.5.2 Heisenberg’s Uncertainty Principle',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20199,53,374,'paragraph',NULL,'Werner Heisenberg a German physicist in 1927, stated uncertainty principle which is the consequence of dual behaviour of matter and radiation. It states that it is impossible to determine simultaneously, the exact position and exact momentum (or velocity) of an electron.','[{"s": "Werner Heisenberg a German physicist in 1927, stated", "t": "text"}, {"s": "uncertainty principle which is the consequence of dual behaviour of matter and radiation.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It states that it is impossible to determine simultaneously, the exact position and exact momentum (or velocity) of an electron.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["22428", "22433", "22461"]',NULL),
(20200,53,375,'paragraph',NULL,'Mathematically, it can be given as in equation (2.23).','[{"s": "Mathematically, it can be given as in equation (2.23).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20201,53,376,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 14-07-54',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image12.png'),
(20202,53,377,'paragraph',NULL,'where ∆x is the uncertainty in position and ∆px (or ∆v x ) is the uncertainty in momentum (or velocity) of the particle. If the position of the electron is known with high degree of accuracy (∆x is small), then the velocity of the electron will be uncertain [∆(v x','[{"s": "where ∆x is the uncertainty in position and ∆px (or ∆v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is the uncertainty in momentum (or velocity) of the particle. If the position of the electron is known with high degree of accuracy (∆x is small), then the velocity of the electron will be uncertain [∆(v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20203,53,378,'paragraph',NULL,'The uncertainty principle can be best understood with the help of an example. Suppose you are asked to measure the thickness of a sheet of paper with an unmarked metrestick. Obviously, the results obtained would be extremely inaccurate and meaningless, In order to obtain any accuracy, you should use an instrument graduated in units smaller than the thickness of a sheet of the paper. Analogously, in order to determine the position of an electron, we must use a meterstick calibrated in units of smaller than the dimensions of electron (keep in mind that an electron is considered as a point charge and is therefore, dimensionless). To observe an electron, we can illuminate it with “light” or electromagnetic radiation. The “light” used must have a wavelength smaller than the dimensions of an electron. The high momentum photons of such light Structure_of_Atom-image14.png would change the energy of electrons by collisions. In this process we, no doubt, would be able to calculate the position of the electron, but we would know very little about the velocity of the electron after the collision.','[{"s": "The uncertainty principle can be best understood with the help of an example. Suppose you are asked to measure the thickness of a sheet of paper with an unmarked metrestick. Obviously, the results obtained would be extremely inaccurate and meaningless, In order to obtain any accuracy, you should use an instrument graduated in units smaller than the thickness of a sheet of the paper. Analogously, in order to determine the position of an electron, we must use a meterstick calibrated in units of smaller than the dimensions of electron (keep in mind that an electron is considered as a point charge and is therefore, dimensionless). To observe an electron, we can illuminate it with “light” or electromagnetic radiation. The “light” used must have a wavelength smaller than the dimensions of an electron. The high momentum photons of such light", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Structure_of_Atom-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-03 14-08-59"}, {"s": "would change the energy of electrons by collisions. In this process we, no doubt, would be able to calculate the position of the electron, but we would know very little about the velocity of the electron after the collision.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20204,53,379,'paragraph',NULL,'Significance of Uncertainty Principle','[{"s": "Significance of Uncertainty Principle", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20521,54,159,'paragraph',NULL,'The equation 6.10 is useful for calculating ∆H from ∆U and vice versa.','[{"s": "The equation 6.10 is useful for calculating ∆H from ∆U and vice versa.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20522,54,160,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 6.5','[{"s": "Problem 6.5", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60911"]',NULL),
(20205,53,380,'paragraph',NULL,'One of the important implications of the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is that it rules out existence of definite paths or trajectories of electrons and other similar particles. The trajectory of an object is determined by its location and velocity at various moments. If we know where a body is at a particular instant and if we also know its velocity and the forces acting on it at that instant, we can tell where the body would be sometime later. We, therefore, conclude that the position of an object and its velocity fix its trajectory. Since for a sub-atomic object such as an electron, it is not possible simultaneously to determine the position and velocity at any given instant to an arbitrary degree of precision, it is not possible to talk of the trajectory of an electron.','[{"s": "One of the important implications of the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "it rules out existence of definite paths or trajectories of electrons and other similar particles.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The trajectory of an object is determined by its location and velocity at various moments. If we know where a body is at a particular instant and if we also know its velocity and the forces acting on it at that instant, we can tell where the body would be sometime later. We, therefore, conclude that the position of an object and its velocity fix its trajectory. Since for a sub-atomic object such as an electron, it is not possible simultaneously to determine the position and velocity at any given instant to an arbitrary degree of precision, it is not possible to talk of the trajectory of an electron.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20206,53,381,'paragraph',NULL,'The effect of Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is significant only for motion of microscopic objects and is negligible for that of macroscopic objects. This can be seen from the following examples.','[{"s": "The effect of Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is significant only for motion of microscopic objects and is negligible for that of macroscopic objects.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This can be seen from the following examples.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20207,53,382,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image43.png'),
(20208,53,383,'paragraph',NULL,'Werner Heisenberg (1901 – 1976) Werner Heisenberg (1901 – 1976) received his Ph.D. in physics from the University of Munich in 1923. He then spent a year working with Max Born at Gottingen and three years with Niels Bohr in Copenhagen. He was professor of physics at the University of Leipzig from 1927 to 1941. During World War II, Heisenberg was in charge of German research on the atomic bomb. After the war he was named director of Max Planck Institute for physics in Gottingen. He was also accomplished mountain climber. Heisenberg was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1932.','[{"s": "Werner Heisenberg (1901 – 1976) Werner Heisenberg (1901 – 1976) received his Ph.D. in physics from the University of Munich in 1923. He then spent a year working with Max Born at Gottingen and three years with Niels Bohr in Copenhagen. He was professor of physics at the University of Leipzig from 1927 to 1941. During World War II, Heisenberg was in charge of German research on the atomic bomb. After the war he was named director of Max Planck Institute for physics in Gottingen. He was also accomplished mountain climber. Heisenberg was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1932.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20209,53,384,'paragraph',NULL,'If uncertainty principle is applied to an object of mass, say about a milligram (10–6 kg), then','[{"s": "If uncertainty principle is applied to an object of mass, say about a milligram (10–6 kg), then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20210,53,385,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 14-09-52',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image65.png'),
(20211,53,386,'paragraph',NULL,'The value of ∆v∆x obtained is extremely small and is insignificant. Therefore, one may say that in dealing with milligram-sized or heavier objects, the associated uncertainties are hardly of any real consequence.','[{"s": "The value of ∆v∆x obtained is extremely small and is insignificant. Therefore, one may say that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in dealing with milligram-sized or heavier objects, the associated uncertainties are hardly of any real consequence.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20212,53,387,'paragraph',NULL,'In the case of a microscopic object like an electron on the other hand. ∆v.∆x obtained is much larger and such uncertainties are of real consequence. For example, for an electron whose mass is 9.11×10 –31 kg., according to Heisenberg uncertainty principle','[{"s": "In the case of a microscopic object like an electron on the other hand. ∆v.∆x obtained is much larger and such uncertainties are of real consequence. For example, for an electron whose mass is 9.11×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg., according to Heisenberg uncertainty principle", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20213,53,388,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-03 14-13-42',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Structure_of_Atom-image54.png'),
(20214,53,389,'paragraph',NULL,'It, therefore, means that if one tries to find the exact location of the electron, say to an uncertainty of only 10 –8 m, then the uncertainty ∆v in velocity would be','[{"s": "It, therefore, means that if one tries to find the exact location of the electron, say to an uncertainty of only 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m, then the uncertainty ∆v in velocity would be", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20215,53,390,'paragraph',NULL,'which is so large that the classical picture of electrons moving in Bohr’s orbits (fixed) cannot hold good. It, therefore, means that the precise statements of the position and momentum of electrons have to be replaced by the statements of probability, that the electron has at a given position and momentum. This is what happens in the quantum mechanical model of atom.','[{"s": "which is so large that the classical picture of electrons moving in Bohr’s orbits (fixed) cannot hold good.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It, therefore, means that the precise statements of the position and momentum of electrons have to be replaced by the statements of probability, that the electron has at a given position and momentum. This is what happens in the quantum mechanical model of atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
