(20528,54,166,'paragraph',NULL,'= 37.904 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "= 37.904 kJ mol–1", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60911"]',NULL),
(20529,54,167,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Extensive and Intensive Properties','[{"s": "(b) Extensive and Intensive Properties", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27783,68,490,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Two vectors A and B may be added graphically using head-to-tail method or parallelogram method .','[{"s": "4. Two vectors A and B may be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "added graphically", "t": "text"}, {"s": "using", "t": "text"}, {"s": "head-to-tail method", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallelogram method", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20270,53,445,'paragraph',NULL,'In 1925, George Uhlenbeck and Samuel Goudsmit proposed the presence of the fourth quantum number known as the electron spin quantum number ( ms ). An electron spins around its own axis, much in a similar way as earth spins around its own axis while revolving around the sun. In other words, an electron has, besides charge and mass, intrinsic spin angular quantum number. Spin angular momentum of the electron — a vector quantity, can have two orientations relative to the chosen axis. These two orientations are distinguished by the spin quantum numbers m s which can take the values of +½ or –½. These are called the two spin states of the electron and are normally represented by two arrows, ↑ (spin up) and ↓ (spin down). Two elec trons that have different m s values (one +½ and the other –½) are said to have opposite spins. An orbital cannot hold more than two electrons and these two electrons should have opposite spins.','[{"s": "In 1925, George Uhlenbeck and Samuel Goudsmit proposed the presence of the fourth quantum number known as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electron spin quantum number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ms", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An electron spins around its own axis, much in a similar way as earth spins around its own axis while revolving around the sun. In other words, an electron has, besides charge and mass, intrinsic spin angular quantum number. Spin angular momentum of the electron — a vector quantity, can have two orientations relative to the chosen axis. These two orientations are distinguished by the spin quantum numbers m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which can take the values of +½ or –½. These are called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "two spin states of the electron", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and are normally", "t": "text"}, {"s": "represented by two arrows,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "↑", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(spin up) and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "↓", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(spin down). Two elec", "t": "text"}, {"s": "trons that have different m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "values (one +½ and the other –½) are said to have opposite spins. An orbital cannot hold more than two electrons and these two electrons should have opposite spins.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20271,53,446,'paragraph',NULL,'To sum up, the four quantum numbers provide the following information :','[{"s": "To sum up, the four quantum numbers provide the following information", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]','marked','["22449", "22450", "22451", "22452"]',NULL),
(20272,53,447,'paragraph',NULL,'i) n defines the shell, determines the size of the orbital and also to a large extent the energy of the orbital.','[{"s": "i) n defines the shell, determines the size of the orbital and also to a large extent the energy of the orbital.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20273,53,448,'paragraph',NULL,'ii) There are n subshells in the n th shell. l identifies the subshell and determines the shape of the orbital (see section 2.6.2). There are (2l+1) orbitals of each type in a subshell , that is, one s orbital (l = 0), three p orbitals (l = 1) and five d orbitals (l = 2) per subshell. To some extent l also determines the energy of the orbital in a multi-electron atom.','[{"s": "ii) There are n subshells in the n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "shell. l identifies the subshell and determines the shape of the orbital (see section 2.6.2).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "There are (2l+1) orbitals of each type in a subshell", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", that is, one s orbital (l = 0), three p orbitals (l = 1) and five d orbitals (l = 2) per subshell. To some extent l also determines the energy of the orbital in a multi-electron atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20274,53,449,'paragraph',NULL,'iii) ml designates the orientation of the orbital. For a given value of l, ml has (2l+1) values, the same as the number of orbitals per subshell. It means that the number of orbitals is equal to the number of ways in which they are oriented.','[{"s": "iii) ml designates the orientation of the orbital. For a given value of l, ml has (2l+1) values, the same as the number of orbitals per subshell. It means that the number of orbitals is equal to the number of ways in which they are oriented.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20275,53,450,'paragraph',NULL,'iv) m s refers to orientation of the spin of the electron.','[{"s": "iv) m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "refers to orientation of the spin of the electron.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20276,53,451,'paragraph',NULL,'Orbit, orbital and its importance','[{"s": "Orbit, orbital and its importance", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20277,53,452,'paragraph',NULL,'Orbit and orbital are not synonymous. An orbit, as proposed by Bohr, is a circular path around the nucleus in which an electron moves. A precise description of this path of the electron is impossible according to Heisenberg uncertainty principle. Bohr orbits, therefore, have no real meaning and their existence can never be demonstrated experimentally. An atomic orbital, on the other hand, is a quantum mechanical concept and refers to the one electron wave function ψ in an atom. It is characterized by three quantum numbers ( n, l and ml ) and its value depends upon the coordinates of the electron. ψ has, by itself, no physical meaning. It is the square of the wave function i.e., |ψ| 2 which has a physical meaning. |ψ| 2 at any point in an atom gives the value of probability density at that point. Probability density (|ψ| 2 ) is the probability per unit volume and the product of |ψ| 2 and a small volume (called a volume element) yields the probability of finding the electron in that volume (the reason for specifying a small volume element is that |ψ| 2 varies from one region to another in space but its value can be assumed to be constant within a small volume element). The total probability of finding the electron in a given volume can then be calculated by the sum of all the products of |ψ| 2 and the corresponding volume elements. It is thus possible to get the probable distribution of an electron in an orbital.','[{"s": "Orbit and orbital are not synonymous. An orbit, as proposed by Bohr, is a circular path around the nucleus in which an electron moves. A precise description of this path of the electron is impossible according to Heisenberg uncertainty principle. Bohr orbits, therefore, have no real meaning and their existence can never be demonstrated experimentally. An atomic orbital, on the other hand, is a quantum mechanical concept and refers to the one electron wave function ψ in an atom. It is characterized by three quantum numbers (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n, l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ml", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and its value depends upon the coordinates of the electron. ψ has, by itself, no physical meaning. It is the square of the wave function i.e., |ψ|", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which has a physical meaning. |ψ|", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at any point in an atom gives the value of probability density at that point. Probability density (|ψ|", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is the probability per unit volume and the product of |ψ|", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and a small volume (called a volume element) yields the probability of finding the electron in that volume (the reason for specifying a small volume element is that |ψ|", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "varies from one region to another in space but its value can be assumed to be constant within a small volume element). The total probability of finding the electron in a given volume can then be calculated by the sum of all the products of |ψ|", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the corresponding volume elements. It is thus possible to get the probable distribution of an electron in an orbital.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
