(27792,68,499,'paragraph',NULL,'7. The subtraction of vector B from A is defined as the sum of A and –B :','[{"s": "7. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "subtraction", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of vector B from A is defined as the sum of A and –B :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27793,68,500,'paragraph',NULL,'A – B = A+ (–B)','[{"s": "A – B = A+ (–B)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20360,53,535,'paragraph',NULL,'Erwin Schrödinger, in 1926, proposed an equation called Schrödinger equation to describe the electron distributions in space and the allowed energy levels in atoms. This equation incorporates de Broglie’s concept of wave-particle duality and is consistent with Heisenberg uncertainty principle. When Schrödinger equation is solved for the electron in a hydrogen atom, the solution gives the possible energy states the electron can occupy [and the corresponding wave function(s) (ψ) (which in fact are the mathematical functions) of the electron associated with each energy state]. These quantized energy states and corresponding wave functions which are characterized by a set of three quantum numbers ( principal quantum number n , azimuthal quantum number l and magnetic quantum number m l) arise as a natural consequence in the solution of the Schrödinger equation. The restrictions on the values of these three quantum numbers also come naturally from this solution. The quantum mechanical model of the hydrogen atom successfully predicts all aspects of the hydrogen atom spectrum including some phenomena that could not be explained by the Bohr model.','[{"s": "Erwin Schrödinger, in 1926, proposed an equation called Schrödinger equation to describe the electron distributions in space and the allowed energy levels in atoms. This equation incorporates de Broglie’s concept of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "wave-particle duality", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and is consistent with Heisenberg uncertainty principle. When Schrödinger equation is solved for the electron in a hydrogen atom, the solution gives the possible energy states the electron can occupy [and the corresponding wave function(s) (ψ) (which in fact are the mathematical functions) of the electron associated with each energy state]. These quantized energy states and corresponding wave functions which are characterized by a set of three quantum numbers (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "principal quantum number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "azimuthal quantum number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "magnetic quantum number m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l) arise as a natural consequence in the solution of the Schrödinger equation. The restrictions on the values of these three quantum numbers also come naturally from this solution. The quantum mechanical model of the hydrogen atom successfully predicts all aspects of the hydrogen atom spectrum including some phenomena that could not be explained by the Bohr model.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20361,53,536,'paragraph',NULL,'According to the quantum mechanical model of the atom, the electron distribution of an atom containing a number of electrons is divided into shells . The shells, in turn, are thought to consist of one or more subshells and subshells are assumed to be composed of one or more orbitals, which the electrons occupy. While for hydrogen and hydrogen like systems (such as He + , Li 2+ etc.) all the orbitals within a given shell have same energy, the energy of the orbitals in a multi-electron atom depends upon the values of n and l : The lower the value of ( n + l ) for an orbital, the lower is its energy. If two orbitals have the same ( n + l ) value, the orbital with lower value of n has the lower energy. In an atom many such orbitals are possible and electrons are filled in those orbitals in order of increasing energy in accordance with Pauli exclusion principle (no two electrons in an atom can have the same set of four quantum numbers) and Hund’s rule of maximum multiplicity (pairing of electrons in the orbitals belonging to the same subshell does not take place until each orbital belonging to that subshell has got one electron each, i.e., is singly occupied). This forms the basis of the electronic structure of atoms.','[{"s": "According to the quantum mechanical model of the atom, the electron distribution of an atom containing a number of electrons is divided into", "t": "text"}, {"s": "shells", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The shells, in turn, are thought to consist of one or more", "t": "text"}, {"s": "subshells", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and subshells are assumed to be composed of one or more orbitals, which the electrons occupy. While for hydrogen and hydrogen like systems (such as He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "etc.) all the orbitals within a given shell have same energy, the energy of the orbitals in a multi-electron atom depends upon the values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": The lower the value of (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n + l", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") for an orbital, the lower is its energy. If two orbitals have the same (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") value, the orbital with lower value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has the lower energy. In an atom many such orbitals are possible and electrons are filled in those orbitals in order of increasing energy in accordance with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Pauli exclusion principle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(no two electrons in an atom can have the same set of four quantum numbers) and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hund’s rule of maximum", "t": "text"}, {"s": "multiplicity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(pairing of electrons in the orbitals belonging to the same subshell does not take place until each orbital belonging to that subshell has got one electron each, i.e., is singly occupied). This forms the basis of the electronic structure of atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20362,54,0,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemical energy stored by molecules can be released as heat during chemical reactions when a fuel like methane, cooking gas or coal burns in air. The chemical energy may also be used to do mechanical work when a fuel burns in an engine or to provide electrical energy through a galvanic cell like dry cell. Thus, various forms of energy are interrelated and under certain conditions, these may be transformed from one form into another. The study of these energy transformations forms the subject matter of thermodynamics. The laws of thermodynamics deal with energy changes of macroscopic systems involving a large number of molecules rather than microscopic systems containing a few molecules. Thermodynamics is not concerned about how and at what rate these energy transformations are carried out, but is based on initial and final states of a system undergoing the change. Laws of thermodynamics apply only when a system is in equilibrium or moves from one equilibrium state to another equilibrium state. Macroscopic properties like pressure and temperature do not change with time for a system in equilibrium state. In this unit, we would like to answer some of the important questions through thermodynamics, like:','[{"s": "Chemical energy stored by molecules can be released as heat during chemical reactions when a fuel like methane, cooking gas or coal burns in air. The chemical energy may also be used to do mechanical work when a fuel burns in an engine or to provide electrical energy through a galvanic cell like dry cell. Thus, various forms of energy are interrelated and under certain conditions, these may be transformed from one form into another. The study of these energy transformations forms the subject matter of thermodynamics. The laws of thermodynamics deal with energy changes of macroscopic systems involving a large number of molecules rather than microscopic systems containing a few molecules. Thermodynamics is not concerned about how and at what rate these energy transformations are carried out, but is based on initial and final states of a system undergoing the change. Laws of thermodynamics apply only when a system is in equilibrium or moves from one equilibrium state to another equilibrium state. Macroscopic properties like pressure and temperature do not change with time for a system in equilibrium state. In this unit, we would like to answer some of the important questions through thermodynamics, like:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20363,54,1,'paragraph',NULL,'How do we determine the energy changes involved in a chemical reaction/process? Will it occur or not?','[{"s": "How do we determine the energy changes involved in a chemical reaction/process? Will it occur or not?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20364,54,2,'paragraph',NULL,'What drives a chemical reaction/process?','[{"s": "What drives a chemical reaction/process?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20365,54,3,'paragraph',NULL,'To what extent do the chemical reactions proceed?','[{"s": "To what extent do the chemical reactions proceed?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20366,54,4,'heading',3,'Thermodynamic terms',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20367,54,5,'paragraph',NULL,'We are interested in chemical reactions and the energy changes accompanying them. For this we need to know certain thermodynamic terms. These are discussed below.','[{"s": "We are interested in chemical reactions and the energy changes accompanying them. For this we need to know certain thermodynamic terms. These are discussed below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20368,54,6,'paragraph',NULL,'The System and the Surroundings','[{"s": "The System and the Surroundings", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20369,54,7,'paragraph',NULL,'A system in thermodynamics refers to that part of universe in which observations are made and remaining universe constitutes the surroundings. The surroundings include everything other than the system. System and the surroundings together constitute the universe .','[{"s": "A system in thermodynamics refers to that part of universe in which observations are made and remaining universe constitutes the surroundings. The surroundings include everything other than the system. System and the surroundings together constitute the universe .", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20370,54,8,'paragraph',NULL,'The universe = The system + The surroundings','[{"s": "The universe = The system + The surroundings", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20371,54,9,'paragraph',NULL,'However, the entire universe other than the system is not affected by the changes taking place in the system. Therefore, for all practical purposes, the surroundings are that portion of the remaining universe which can interact with the system. Usually, the region of space in the neighbourhood of the system constitutes its surroundings.','[{"s": "However, the entire universe other than the system is not affected by the changes taking place in the system. Therefore, for all practical purposes, the surroundings are that portion of the remaining universe which can interact with the system. Usually, the region of space in the neighbourhood of the system constitutes its surroundings.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20372,54,10,'paragraph',NULL,'For example, if we are studying the reaction between two substances A and B kept in a beaker, the beaker containing the reaction mixture is the system and the room where the beaker is kept is the surroundings (Fig. 6.1).','[{"s": "For example, if we are studying the reaction between two substances A and B kept in a beaker, the beaker containing the reaction mixture is the system and the room where the beaker is kept is the surroundings (Fig. 6.1).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20373,54,11,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image37.png'),
(20374,54,12,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.1 System and the surroundings','[{"s": "Fig. 6.1 System and the surroundings", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20375,54,13,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the system may be defined by physical boundaries, like beaker or test tube, or the system may simply be defined by a set of Cartesian coordinates specifying a particular volume in space. It is necessary to think of the system as separated from the surroundings by some sort of wall which may be real or imaginary. The wall that separates the system from the surroundings is called boundary. This is designed to allow us to control and keep track of all movements of matter and energy in or out of the system.','[{"s": "Note that the system may be defined by physical boundaries, like beaker or test tube, or the system may simply be defined by a set of Cartesian coordinates specifying a particular volume in space. It is necessary to think of the system as separated from the surroundings by some sort of wall which may be real or imaginary. The wall that separates the system from the surroundings is called boundary. This is designed to allow us to control and keep track of all movements of matter and energy in or out of the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20376,54,14,'paragraph',NULL,'Types of the System','[{"s": "Types of the System", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20377,54,15,'paragraph',NULL,'We, further classify the systems according to the movements of matter and energy in or out of the system.','[{"s": "We, further classify the systems according to the movements of matter and energy in or out of the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20378,54,16,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Open System','[{"s": "1. Open System", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20379,54,17,'paragraph',NULL,'In an open system, there is exchange of energy and matter between system and surroundings [Fig. 6.2 (a)]. The presence of reactants in an open beaker is an example of an open system*. Here the boundary is an imaginary surface enclosing the beaker and reactants.','[{"s": "In an open system, there is exchange of energy and matter between system and surroundings [Fig. 6.2 (a)]. The presence of reactants in an open beaker is an example of an open system*. Here the boundary is an imaginary surface enclosing the beaker and reactants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20380,54,18,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Closed System','[{"s": "2. Closed System", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20381,54,19,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Isolated System','[{"s": "3. Isolated System", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20382,54,20,'paragraph',NULL,'In an isolated system, there is no exchange of energy or matter between the system and the surroundings [Fig. 6.2 (c)]. The presence of reactants in a thermos flask or any other closed insulated vessel is an example of an isolated system.','[{"s": "In an isolated system, there is no exchange of energy or matter between the system and the surroundings [Fig. 6.2 (c)]. The presence of reactants in a thermos flask or any other closed insulated vessel is an example of an isolated system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20383,54,21,'paragraph',NULL,'6.1.3 The State of the System','[{"s": "6.1.3 The State of the System", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20384,54,22,'paragraph',NULL,'The system must be described in order to make any useful calculations by specifying quantitatively each of the properties such as its pressure (p), volume (V), and temperature (T ) as well as the composition of the system. We need to describe the system by specifying it before and after the change. You would recall from your Physics course that the state of a system in mechanics is completely specified at a given instant of time, by the position and velocity of each mass point of the system. In thermodynamics, a different and much simpler concept of the state of a system is introduced. It does not need detailed knowledge of motion of each particle because, we deal with average measurable properties of the system. We specify the state of the system by state functions or state variables.','[{"s": "The system must be described in order to make any useful calculations by specifying quantitatively each of the properties such as its pressure (p), volume (V), and temperature (T ) as well as the composition of the system. We need to describe the system by specifying it before and after the change. You would recall from your Physics course that the state of a system in mechanics is completely specified at a given instant of time, by the position and velocity of each mass point of the system. In thermodynamics, a different and much simpler concept of the state of a system is introduced. It does not need detailed knowledge of motion of each particle because, we deal with average measurable properties of the system. We specify the state of the system by state functions or state variables.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27794,68,501,'paragraph',NULL,'8. A vector A can be resolved into component along two given vectors a and b lying in the same plane :','[{"s": "8. A vector A can be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "resolved", "t": "text"}, {"s": "into component along two given vectors a and b lying in the same plane :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27795,68,502,'paragraph',NULL,'A = λ a + µ b','[{"s": "A = λ a + µ b", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27796,68,503,'paragraph',NULL,'where λ and µ are real numbers.','[{"s": "where λ and µ are real numbers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27797,68,504,'paragraph',NULL,'9. A unit vector associated with a vector A has magnitude 1 and is along the vector A:','[{"s": "9. A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "unit vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "associated with a vector A has magnitude 1 and is along the vector A:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27798,68,505,'image',NULL,'Eqn54',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image51.png'),
(20385,54,23,'paragraph',NULL,'The state of a thermodynamic system is described by its measurable or macroscopic (bulk) properties. We can describe the state of a gas by quoting its pressure (p), volume (V), temperature (T ), amount (n) etc. Variables like p, V, T are called state variables or state functions because their values depend only on the state of the system and not on how it is reached. In order to completely define the state of a system it is not necessary to define all the properties of the system; as only a certain number of properties can be varied independently. This number depends on the nature of the system. Once these minimum number of macroscopic properties are fixed, others automatically have definite values.','[{"s": "The state of a thermodynamic system is described by its measurable or macroscopic (bulk) properties. We can describe the state of a gas by quoting its pressure (p), volume (V), temperature (T ), amount (n) etc. Variables like p, V, T are called state variables or state functions because their values depend only on the state of the system and not on how it is reached. In order to completely define the state of a system it is not necessary to define all the properties of the system; as only a certain number of properties can be varied independently. This number depends on the nature of the system. Once these minimum number of macroscopic properties are fixed, others automatically have definite values.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20386,54,24,'paragraph',NULL,'The state of the surroundings can never be completely specified; fortunately it is not necessary to do so.','[{"s": "The state of the surroundings can never be completely specified; fortunately it is not necessary to do so.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20387,54,25,'paragraph',NULL,'The Internal Energy as a State Function','[{"s": "The Internal Energy as a State Function", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20388,54,26,'paragraph',NULL,'When we talk about our chemical system losing or gaining energy, we need to introduce a quantity which represents the total energy of the system. It may be chemical, electrical, mechanical or any other type of energy you may think of, the sum of all these is the energy of the system. In thermodynamics, we call it the internal energy, U of the system, which may change, when','[{"s": "When we talk about our chemical system losing or gaining energy, we need to introduce a quantity which represents the total energy of the system. It may be chemical, electrical, mechanical or any other type of energy you may think of, the sum of all these is the energy of the system. In thermodynamics, we call it the internal energy, U of the system, which may change, when", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20389,54,27,'paragraph',NULL,'• heat passes into or out of the system,','[{"s": "• heat passes into or out of the system,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20390,54,28,'paragraph',NULL,'• work is done on or by the system,','[{"s": "• work is done on or by the system,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20391,54,29,'paragraph',NULL,'• matter enters or leaves the system.','[{"s": "• matter enters or leaves the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20392,54,30,'paragraph',NULL,'These systems are classified accordingly as you have already studied in section 6.1.2.','[{"s": "These systems are classified accordingly as you have already studied in section 6.1.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20393,54,31,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Work','[{"s": "(a) Work", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20394,54,32,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us first examine a change in internal energy by doing work. We take a system containing some quantity of water in a thermos flask or in an insulated beaker. This would not allow exchange of heat between the system and surroundings through its boundary and we call this type of system as adiabatic. The manner in which the state of such a system may be changed will be called adiabatic process. Adiabatic process is a process in which there is no transfer of heat between the system and surroundings. Here, the wall separating the system and the surroundings is called the adiabatic wall (Fig 6.3).','[{"s": "Let us first examine a change in internal energy by doing work. We take a system containing some quantity of water in a thermos flask or in an insulated beaker. This would not allow exchange of heat between the system and surroundings through its boundary and we call this type of system as adiabatic. The manner in which the state of such a system may be changed will be called adiabatic process. Adiabatic process is a process in which there is no transfer of heat between the system and surroundings. Here, the wall separating the system and the surroundings is called the adiabatic wall (Fig 6.3).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20395,54,33,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us bring the change in the internal energy of the system by doing some work on it. Let us call the initial state of the system as state A and its temperature as T A . Let the internal energy of the system in state A be called U A . We can change the state of the system in two different ways.','[{"s": "Let us bring the change in the internal energy of the system by doing some work on it. Let us call the initial state of the system as state A and its temperature as T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Let the internal energy of the system in state A be called U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We can change the state of the system in two different ways.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20396,54,34,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image76.png'),
(20397,54,35,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.3 An adiabatic system which does not permit the transfer of heat through its boundary.','[{"s": "Fig. 6.3 An adiabatic system which does not permit the transfer of heat through its boundary.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20398,54,36,'paragraph',NULL,'One way: We do some mechanical work, say 1 kJ, by rotating a set of small paddles and thereby churning water. Let the new state be called B state and its temperature, as T B . It is found that T B > T A and the change in temperature, ∆T = T B –T A . Let the internal energy of the system in state B be U B and the change in internal energy, ∆U =U B – U A .','[{"s": "One way: We do some mechanical work, say 1 kJ, by rotating a set of small paddles and thereby churning water. Let the new state be called B state and its temperature, as T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is found that T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the change in temperature, ∆T = T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Let the internal energy of the system in state B be U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the change in internal energy, ∆U =U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20399,54,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Second way: We now do an equal amount (i.e., 1kJ) electrical work with the help of an immersion rod and note down the temperature change. We find that the change in temperature is same as in the earlier case, say, T B – T A .','[{"s": "Second way: We now do an equal amount (i.e., 1kJ) electrical work with the help of an immersion rod and note down the temperature change. We find that the change in temperature is same as in the earlier case, say, T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20400,54,38,'paragraph',NULL,'In fact, the experiments in the above manner were done by J. P. Joule between 1840–50 and he was able to show that a given amount of work done on the system, no matter how it was done (irrespective of path) produced the same change of state, as measured by the change in the temperature of the system.','[{"s": "In fact, the experiments in the above manner were done by J. P. Joule between 1840–50 and he was able to show that a given amount of work done on the system, no matter how it was done (irrespective of path) produced the same change of state, as measured by the change in the temperature of the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20401,54,39,'paragraph',NULL,'So, it seems appropriate to define a quantity, the internal energy U, whose value is characteristic of the state of a system, whereby the adiabatic work, w ad required to bring about a change of state is equal to the difference between the value of U in one state and that in another state, ∆U i.e.,','[{"s": "So, it seems appropriate to define a quantity, the internal energy U, whose value is characteristic of the state of a system, whereby the adiabatic work, w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ad", "t": "text"}, {"s": "required to bring about a change of state is equal to the difference between the value of U in one state and that in another state, ∆U i.e.,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20402,54,40,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image32.png'),
(20403,54,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, internal energy, U, of the system is a state function.','[{"s": "Therefore, internal energy, U, of the system is a state function.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60960"]',NULL),
(20404,54,42,'paragraph',NULL,'By conventions of IUPAC in chemical thermodynamics. The positive sign expresses that w ad is positive when work is done on the system and the internal energy of system increases. Similarly, if the work is done by the system,w ad will be negative because internal energy of the system decreases.','[{"s": "By conventions of IUPAC in chemical thermodynamics. The positive sign expresses that w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ad", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is positive when work is done on the system and the internal energy of system increases. Similarly, if the work is done by the system,w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ad", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will be negative because internal energy of the system decreases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20405,54,43,'paragraph',NULL,'Can you name some other familiar state functions? Some of other familiar state functions are V, p, and T. For example, if we bring a change in temperature of the system from 25°C to 35°C, the change in temperature is 35°C–25°C = +10°C, whether we go straight up to 35°C or we cool the system for a few degrees, then take the system to the final temperature. Thus, T is a state function and the change in temperature is independent of the route taken. Volume of water in a pond, for example, is a state function, because change in volume of its water is independent of the route by which water is filled in the pond, either by rain or by tubewell or by both.','[{"s": "Can you name some other familiar state functions? Some of other familiar state functions are V, p, and T. For example, if we bring a change in temperature of the system from 25°C to 35°C, the change in temperature is 35°C–25°C = +10°C, whether we go straight up to 35°C or we cool the system for a few degrees, then take the system to the final temperature. Thus, T is a state function and the change in temperature is independent of the route taken. Volume of water in a pond, for example, is a state function, because change in volume of its water is independent of the route by which water is filled in the pond, either by rain or by tubewell or by both.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20406,54,44,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Heat','[{"s": "(b) Heat", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20407,54,45,'paragraph',NULL,'We can also change the internal energy of a system by transfer of heat from the surroundings to the system or vice-versa without expenditure of work. This exchange of energy, which is a result of temperature difference is called heat, q. Let us consider bringing about the same change in temperature (the same initial and final states as before in section 6.1.4 (a) by transfer of heat through thermally conducting walls instead of adiabatic walls (Fig. 6.4).','[{"s": "We can also change the internal energy of a system by transfer of heat from the surroundings to the system or vice-versa without expenditure of work. This exchange of energy, which is a result of temperature difference is called heat, q. Let us consider bringing about the same change in temperature (the same initial and final states as before in section 6.1.4 (a) by transfer of heat through thermally conducting walls instead of adiabatic walls (Fig. 6.4).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20408,54,46,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image23.png'),
(20409,54,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.4 A system which allows heat transfer through its boundary.','[{"s": "Fig. 6.4 A system which allows heat transfer through its boundary.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20410,54,48,'paragraph',NULL,'We take water at temperature, T A in a container having thermally conducting walls, say made up of copper and enclose it in a huge heat reservoir at temperature, T B . The heat absorbed by the system (water), q can be measured in terms of temperature difference , T B – T A . In this case change in internal energy, ∆U= q, when no work is done at constant volume.','[{"s": "We take water at temperature, T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in a container having thermally conducting walls, say made up of copper and enclose it in a huge heat reservoir at temperature, T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The heat absorbed by the system (water), q can be measured in terms of temperature difference , T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In this case change in internal energy, ∆U= q, when no work is done at constant volume.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27799,68,506,'paragraph',NULL,'The unit vectors Motion_in_a_Plane-image57.png are vectors of unit magnitude and point in the direction of the x-, y -, and z- axes, respectively in a right-handed coordinate system.','[{"s": "The unit vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Plane-image57.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screen Shot 2019-06-08 at 6.22.02 PM"}, {"s": "are vectors of unit magnitude and point in the direction of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x-, y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-, and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "axes, respectively in a right-handed coordinate system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20411,54,49,'paragraph',NULL,'By conventions of IUPAC in chemical thermodynamics. The q is positive, when heat is transferred from the surroundings to the system and the internal energy of the system increases and q is negative when heat is transferred from system to the surroundings resulting in decrease of the internal energy of the system.','[{"s": "By conventions of IUPAC in chemical thermodynamics. The q is positive, when heat is transferred from the surroundings to the system and the internal energy of the system increases and q is negative when heat is transferred from system to the surroundings resulting in decrease of the internal energy of the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20412,54,50,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The general case','[{"s": "(c) The general case", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20413,54,51,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us consider the general case in which a change of state is brought about both by doing work and by transfer of heat. We write change in internal energy for this case as:','[{"s": "Let us consider the general case in which a change of state is brought about both by doing work and by transfer of heat. We write change in internal energy for this case as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20414,54,52,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ U = q + w (6.1)','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ w (6.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20415,54,53,'paragraph',NULL,'For a given change in state, q and w can vary depending on how the change is carried out. However, q +w = ∆ U will depend only on initial and final state. It will be independent of the way the change is carried out. If there is no transfer of energy as heat or as work (isolated system) i.e., if w = 0 and q U = 0.','[{"s": "For a given change in state,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and w can vary depending on how the change is carried out. However,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+w = ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will depend only on initial and final state. It will be independent of the way the change is carried out. If there is no transfer of energy as heat or as work (isolated system) i.e., if w = 0 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20416,54,54,'paragraph',NULL,'The equation 6.1 i.e., ∆ U = q + w is mathematical statement of the first law of thermodynamics, which states that3','[{"s": "The equation 6.1 i.e., ∆", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "U", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "=", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "q", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+ w is mathematical statement of the first law of thermodynamics, which states that3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60907", "60915", "60960"]',NULL),
(20417,54,55,'paragraph',NULL,'The energy of an isolated system is constant.','[{"s": "The energy of an isolated system is constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20418,54,56,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image47.png'),
(20419,54,57,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.2 Open, closed and isolated systems.','[{"s": "Fig. 6.2 Open, closed and isolated systems.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20420,54,58,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us consider the general case in which a change of state is brought about both by doing work and by transfer of heat. We write change in internal energy for this case as:','[{"s": "Let us consider the general case in which a change of state is brought about both by doing work and by transfer of heat. We write change in internal energy for this case as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20421,54,59,'paragraph',NULL,'∆U = q + w (6.1)','[{"s": "∆U = q + w (6.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20422,54,60,'paragraph',NULL,'The equation 6.1 i.e., ∆U = q + w is mathematical statement of the first law of thermodynamics, which states that','[{"s": "The equation 6.1 i.e., ∆U = q + w is mathematical statement of the first law of thermodynamics, which states that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20423,54,61,'paragraph',NULL,'The energy of an isolated system is constant.','[{"s": "The energy of an isolated system is constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20424,54,62,'paragraph',NULL,'It is commonly stated as the law of conservation of energy i.e., energy can neither be created nor be destroyed.','[{"s": "It is commonly stated as the law of conservation of energy i.e., energy can neither be created nor be destroyed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20425,54,63,'paragraph',NULL,'Note: There is considerable difference between the character of the thermodynamic property energy and that of a mechanical property such as volume. We can specify an unambiguous (absolute) value for volume of a system in a particular state, but not the absolute value of the internal energy. However, we can measure only the changes in the internal energy, ∆U of the system.','[{"s": "Note: There is considerable difference between the character of the thermodynamic property energy and that of a mechanical property such as volume. We can specify an unambiguous (absolute) value for volume of a system in a particular state, but not the absolute value of the internal energy. However, we can measure only the changes in the internal energy, ∆U of the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20426,54,64,'heading',3,'Applications',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20427,54,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Many chemical reactions involve the generation of gases capable of doing mechanical work or the generation of heat. It is important for us to quantify these changes and relate them to the changes in the internal energy. Let us see how!','[{"s": "Many chemical reactions involve the generation of gases capable of doing mechanical work or the generation of heat. It is important for us to quantify these changes and relate them to the changes in the internal energy. Let us see how!", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20428,54,66,'paragraph',NULL,'6.2.1 Work','[{"s": "6.2.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Work", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60912"]',NULL),
(20429,54,67,'paragraph',NULL,'First of all, let us concentrate on the nature of work a system can do. We will consider only mechanical work i.e., pressure-volume work.','[{"s": "First of all, let us concentrate on the nature of work a system can do. We will consider only mechanical work i.e., pressure-volume work.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60912"]',NULL),
(27800,68,507,'paragraph',NULL,'10. A vector A can be expressed as','[{"s": "10. A vector A can be expressed as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27801,68,508,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-08 at 6.22.07 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image55.png'),
(2922,26,53,'paragraph',NULL,'The pteridophytes are further classified into four classes: Psilopsida (Psilotum); Lycopsida (Selaginella, Lycopodium), Sphenopsida (Equisetum) and Pteropsida (Dryopteris, Pteris, Adiantum).','[{"s": "The pteridophytes are further classified into four classes: Psilopsida (Psilotum); Lycopsida (Selaginella, Lycopodium), Sphenopsida (Equisetum) and Pteropsida (Dryopteris, Pteris, Adiantum).", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(20430,54,68,'paragraph',NULL,'For understanding pressure-volume work, let us consider a cylinder which contains one mole of an ideal gas fitted with a frictionless piston. Total volume of the gas is V i and pressure of the gas inside is p. If external pressure is p ex which is greater than p, piston is moved inward till the pressure inside becomes equal to p ex . Let this change be achieved in a single step and the final volume be V f . During this compression, suppose piston moves a distance, l and is cross-sectional area of the piston is A','[{"s": "For understanding pressure-volume work, let us consider a cylinder which contains one mole of an ideal gas fitted with a frictionless piston. Total volume of the gas is V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "i", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and pressure of the gas inside is p. If external pressure is p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "which is greater than p, piston is moved inward till the pressure inside becomes equal to p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". Let this change be achieved in a single step and the final volume be V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". During this compression, suppose piston moves a distance, l and is cross-sectional area of the piston is A", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60912"]',NULL),
(20431,54,69,'paragraph',NULL,'[Fig. 6.5(a)] .','[{"s": "[Fig. 6.5(a)]", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60912"]',NULL),
(20432,54,70,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image71.png'),
(20433,54,71,'paragraph',NULL,'then, volume change = l × A = ∆V = (V f – V i )','[{"s": "then, volume change = l × A = ∆V = (V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "i", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ")", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60912"]',NULL),
(20434,54,72,'paragraph',NULL,'We also know, Thermodynamics-image24.png','[{"s": "We also know,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image24.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-05 15-54-46"}]','marked','["60912"]',NULL),
(20435,54,73,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, force on the piston = p ex . A','[{"s": "Therefore, force on the piston = p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". A", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60912"]',NULL),
(20436,54,74,'paragraph',NULL,'If w is the work done on the system by movement of the piston then','[{"s": "If w is the work done on the system by movement of the piston then", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60912"]',NULL),
(20437,54,75,'paragraph',NULL,'w = force × distance = p ex . A .l','[{"s": "w = force × distance = p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". A .l", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60912"]',NULL),
(20438,54,76,'paragraph',NULL,'= p ex . (–∆V) = – p ex ∆V = – p ex (V f – V i ) (6.2)','[{"s": "= p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". (–∆V) = – p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "∆V = – p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "i", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") (6.2)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60912"]',NULL),
(20439,54,77,'paragraph',NULL,'The negative sign of this expression is required to obtain conventional sign for w, which will be positive. It indicates that in case of compression work is done on the system. Here (V f – V i ) will be negative and negative multiplied by negative will be positive. Hence the sign obtained for the work will be positive.','[{"s": "The negative sign of this expression is required to obtain conventional sign for w, which will be positive. It indicates that in case of compression work is done on the system. Here (V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "i", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") will be negative and negative multiplied by negative will be positive. Hence the sign obtained for the work will be positive.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60915"]',NULL),
(20440,54,78,'paragraph',NULL,'If the pressure is not constant at every stage of compression, but changes in number of finite steps, work done on the gas will be summed over all the steps and will be equal to Thermodynamics-image15.png [Fig. 6.5 (b)]','[{"s": "If the pressure is not constant at every stage of compression, but changes in number of finite steps, work done on the gas will be summed over all the steps and will be equal to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image15.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[Fig. 6.5 (b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20441,54,79,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image27.png'),
(20442,54,80,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.5 (b) pV-plot when pressure is not constant and changes in finite steps during compression from initial volume, V i to final volume, V f . Work done on the gas is represented by the shaded area .','[{"s": "Fig. 6.5 (b) pV-plot when pressure is not constant and changes in finite steps during compression from initial volume, V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to final volume, V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Work done on the gas is represented by the shaded area", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["60906"]',NULL),
(20443,54,81,'paragraph',NULL,'If the pressure is not constant but changes during the process such that it is always infinitesimally greater than the pressure of the gas, then, at each stage of compression, the volume decreases by an infinitesimal amount, dV. In such a case we can calculate the work done on the gas by the relation','[{"s": "If the pressure is not constant but changes during the process such that it is always infinitesimally greater than the pressure of the gas, then, at each stage of compression, the volume decreases by an infinitesimal amount, dV. In such a case we can calculate the work done on the gas by the relation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20444,54,82,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 15-55-42',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image3.png'),
(20445,54,83,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, p ex at each stage is equal to (p in + dp) in case of compression [Fig. 6.5(c)]. In an expansion process under similar conditions, the external pressure is always less than the pressure of the system i.e., p ex = (p in – dp). In general case we can write, p ex = (p in + dp). Such processes are called reversible processes.','[{"s": "Here, p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at each stage is equal to (p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ dp) in case of compression [Fig. 6.5(c)]. In an expansion process under similar conditions, the external pressure is always less than the pressure of the system i.e., p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– dp). In general case we can write, p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ dp). Such processes are called reversible processes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20446,54,84,'paragraph',NULL,'A process or change is said to be reversible, if a change is brought out in such a way that the process could, at any moment, be reversed by an infinitesimal change. A reversible process proceeds infinitely slowly by a series of equilibrium states such that system and the surroundings are always in near equilibrium with each other. Processes other than reversible processes are known as irreversible processes.','[{"s": "A process or change is said to be reversible, if a change is brought out in such a way that the process could, at any moment, be reversed by an infinitesimal change. A reversible process proceeds infinitely slowly by a series of equilibrium states such that system and the surroundings are always in near equilibrium with each other. Processes other than reversible processes are known as irreversible processes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20447,54,85,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image69.png'),
(20448,54,86,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.5 (c) pV-plot when pressure is not constant and changes in infinite steps (reversible conditions) during compression from initial volume, Vi to final volume, Vf . Work done on the gas is represented by the shaded area.','[{"s": "Fig. 6.5 (c) pV-plot when pressure is not constant and changes in infinite steps (reversible conditions) during compression from initial volume, Vi to final volume, Vf . Work done on the gas is represented by the shaded area.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20449,54,87,'paragraph',NULL,'In chemistry, we face problems that can be solved if we relate the work term to the internal pressure of the system. We can relate work to internal pressure of the system under reversible conditions by writing equation 6.3 as follows:','[{"s": "In chemistry, we face problems that can be solved if we relate the work term to the internal pressure of the system. We can relate work to internal pressure of the system under reversible conditions by writing equation 6.3 as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20450,54,88,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 15-56-47',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image42.png'),
(20451,54,89,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, the pressure of the gas (p in which we can write as p now) can be expressed in terms of its volume through gas equation. For n mol of an ideal gas i.e., pV =nRT','[{"s": "Now, the pressure of the gas (p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which we can write as p now) can be expressed in terms of its volume through gas equation. For n mol of an ideal gas i.e., pV =nRT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20452,54,90,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 15-57-36',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image20.png'),
(20453,54,91,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, at constant temperature (isothermal process),','[{"s": "Therefore, at constant temperature (isothermal process),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20454,54,92,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 15-57-12',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image45.png'),
(20455,54,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Free expansion : Expansion of a gas in vacuum (p ex = 0) is called free expansion. No work is done during free expansion of an ideal gas whether the process is reversible or irreversible (equation 6.2 and 6.3).','[{"s": "Free expansion : Expansion of a gas in vacuum (p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "= 0) is called free expansion. No work is done during free expansion of an ideal gas whether the process is reversible or irreversible (equation 6.2 and 6.3).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60910", "60957"]',NULL),
(20456,54,94,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, we can write equation 6.1 in number of ways depending on the type of processes.','[{"s": "Now, we can write equation 6.1 in number of ways depending on the type of processes.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60910"]',NULL),
(20457,54,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us substitute w = – p ex ∆V (eq. 6.2) in equation 6.1, and we get','[{"s": "Let us substitute w = – p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "∆V (eq. 6.2) in equation 6.1, and we get", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60910"]',NULL),
(20458,54,96,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 15-58-07',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image52.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(20459,54,97,'paragraph',NULL,'If a process is carried out at constant volume (∆V = 0), then','[{"s": "If a process is carried out at constant volume (∆V = 0), then", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60910"]',NULL),
(20460,54,98,'paragraph',NULL,'∆U = q V','[{"s": "∆U = q", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "V", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60910"]',NULL),
(20461,54,99,'paragraph',NULL,'the subscript V in q V denotes that heat is supplied at constant volume.','[{"s": "the subscript V in q", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "denotes that heat is supplied at constant volume.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60910"]',NULL),
(20462,54,100,'paragraph',NULL,'Isothermal and free expansion of an ideal gas','[{"s": "Isothermal and free expansion of an ideal gas", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60930"]',NULL),
(20463,54,101,'paragraph',NULL,'For isothermal (T = constant) expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum ; w = 0 since p ex','[{"s": "For isothermal (T = constant) expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum ; w = 0 since p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]','marked','["60930", "60957"]',NULL),
(20464,54,102,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation 6.1, Thermodynamics-image58.png can be expressed for isothermal irreversible and reversible changes as follows:','[{"s": "Equation 6.1,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image58.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "can be expressed for isothermal irreversible and reversible changes as follows:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60930", "60958"]',NULL),
(20465,54,103,'paragraph',NULL,'1. For isothermal irreversible change','[{"s": "1. For isothermal irreversible change", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60930"]',NULL),
(20466,54,104,'paragraph',NULL,'q = – w = p ex (V f – V i )','[{"s": "q = – w = p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "i", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ")", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60930"]',NULL),
(20467,54,105,'paragraph',NULL,'2. For isothermal reversible change','[{"s": "2. For isothermal reversible change", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60930"]',NULL),
(20468,54,106,'paragraph',NULL,'q = – w = nRT ln Thermodynamics-image14.png','[{"s": "q = – w = nRT ln", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]','marked','["60930"]',NULL),
(20469,54,107,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2.303 nRT log Thermodynamics-image14.png','[{"s": "= 2.303 nRT log", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]','marked','["60930"]',NULL),
(20470,54,108,'paragraph',NULL,'3. For adiabatic change, q = 0,','[{"s": "3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For adiabatic change, q = 0,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60976"]',NULL),
(20471,54,109,'paragraph',NULL,'∆U = w ad','[{"s": "∆U = w", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ad", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60976"]',NULL),
(20472,54,110,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 6.2','[{"s": "Problem 6.2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20473,54,111,'paragraph',NULL,'Two litres of an ideal gas at a pressure of 10 atm expands isothermally at 25 °C into a vacuum until its total volume is 10 litres. How much heat is absorbed and how much work is done in the expansion ?','[{"s": "Two litres of an ideal gas at a pressure of 10 atm expands isothermally at 25 °C into a vacuum until its total volume is 10 litres.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "How much heat is absorbed and how much work is done in the expansion ?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["60907", "60943"]',NULL),
(20474,54,112,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20475,54,113,'paragraph',NULL,'We have q = – w = p ex (10 – 2) = 0(8) = 0','[{"s": "We have q = – w = p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(10 – 2) = 0(8) = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20476,54,114,'paragraph',NULL,'No work is done; no heat is absorbed.','[{"s": "No work is done; no heat is absorbed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20477,54,115,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 6.3','[{"s": "Problem 6.3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20478,54,116,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider the same expansion, but this time against a constant external pressure of 1 atm.','[{"s": "Consider the same expansion, but this time against a constant external pressure of 1 atm.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20479,54,117,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20480,54,118,'paragraph',NULL,'We have q = – w = p ex (8) = 8 litre-atm','[{"s": "We have q = – w = p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(8) = 8 litre-atm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20481,54,119,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 6.4','[{"s": "Problem 6.4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20482,54,120,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider the expansion given in problem 6.2, for 1 mol of an ideal gas conducted reversibly.','[{"s": "Consider the expansion given in problem 6.2, for 1 mol of an ideal gas conducted reversibly.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60977"]',NULL),
(20483,54,121,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20484,54,122,'paragraph',NULL,'We have q = – w = 2.303 nRT Thermodynamics-image57.png','[{"s": "We have q = – w = 2.303 nRT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image57.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20485,54,123,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2.303 × 1 × 0.8206 × 298 × log Thermodynamics-image55.png','[{"s": "= 2.303 × 1 × 0.8206 × 298 × log", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image55.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20486,54,124,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2.303 x 0.8206 x 298 x log 5','[{"s": "= 2.303 x 0.8206 x 298 x log 5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20487,54,125,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2.303 x 0.8206 x 298 x 0.6990','[{"s": "= 2.303 x 0.8206 x 298 x 0.6990", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20488,54,126,'paragraph',NULL,'= 393.66 L atm','[{"s": "= 393.66 L atm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20489,54,127,'paragraph',NULL,'6.2.2 Enthalpy, H','[{"s": "6.2.2 Enthalpy, H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20490,54,128,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) A Useful New State Function','[{"s": "(a) A Useful New State Function", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20491,54,129,'paragraph',NULL,'We know that the heat absorbed at constant volume is equal to change in the internal energy i.e., ∆U = q V . But most of chemical reactions are carried out not at constant volume, but in flasks or test tubes under constant atmospheric pressure. We need to define another state function which may be suitable under these conditions.','[{"s": "We know that the heat absorbed at constant volume is equal to change in the internal energy i.e., ∆U = q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". But most of chemical reactions are carried out not at constant volume, but in flasks or test tubes under constant atmospheric pressure. We need to define another state function which may be suitable under these conditions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20492,54,130,'paragraph',NULL,'p – p∆V at constant pressure, where q p is heat absorbed by the system and –p∆V represent expansion work done by the system.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– p∆V at constant pressure, where q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is heat absorbed by the system and –p∆V represent expansion work done by the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20493,54,131,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us represent the initial state by subscript 1 and final state by 2','[{"s": "Let us represent the initial state by subscript 1 and final state by 2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20494,54,132,'paragraph',NULL,'We can rewrite the above equation as','[{"s": "We can rewrite the above equation as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20495,54,133,'paragraph',NULL,'U 2 –U 1 = q p – p (V 2 – V 1 )','[{"s": "U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– p (V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20496,54,134,'paragraph',NULL,'On rearranging, we get','[{"s": "On rearranging, we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20497,54,135,'paragraph',NULL,'q p = (U 2 + pV 2 ) – (U 1 + pV 1 ) (6.6)','[{"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ pV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") – (U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ pV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (6.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20498,54,136,'paragraph',NULL,'Now we can define another thermodynamic function, the enthalpy H [Greek word enthalpien, to warm or heat content] as :','[{"s": "Now we can define another thermodynamic function, the enthalpy H [Greek word enthalpien, to warm or heat content] as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20499,54,137,'paragraph',NULL,'H = U + pV (6.7)','[{"s": "H = U + pV (6.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20500,54,138,'paragraph',NULL,'so, equation (6.6) becomes','[{"s": "so, equation (6.6) becomes", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20531,54,169,'paragraph',NULL,'Those properties which do not depend on the quantity or size of matter present are known as intensive properties. For example temperature, density, pressure etc. are intensive properties. A molar property, χ m , is the value of an extensive property χ of the system for 1 mol of the substance. If n is the amount of matter, Thermodynamics-image70.png is independent of the amount of matter. Other examples are molar volume, V m and molar heat capacity, C m . Let us understand the distinction between extensive and intensive properties by considering a gas enclosed in a container of volume V and at temperature T [Fig. 6.6(a)]. Let us make a partition such that volume is halved, each part [Fig. 6.6 (b)] now has one half of the original volume, Thermodynamics-image77.png , but the temperature will still remain the same i.e., T. It is clear that volume is an extensive property and temperature is an intensive property.','[{"s": "Those properties which do not depend on the quantity or size of matter present are known as intensive properties. For example temperature, density, pressure etc. are intensive properties. A molar property, χ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", is the value of an extensive property χ of the system for 1 mol of the substance. If n is the amount of matter,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image70.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is independent of the amount of matter. Other examples are molar volume, V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and molar heat capacity, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Let us understand the distinction between extensive and intensive properties by considering a gas enclosed in a container of volume V and at temperature T [Fig. 6.6(a)]. Let us make a partition such that volume is halved, each part [Fig. 6.6 (b)] now has one half of the original volume,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image77.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", but the temperature will still remain the same i.e., T. It is clear that volume is an extensive property and temperature is an intensive property.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20532,54,170,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image78.png'),
(20533,54,171,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.6(a) A gas at volume V and temperature T','[{"s": "Fig. 6.6(a) A gas at volume V and temperature T", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20534,54,172,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image41.png'),
(20535,54,173,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.6 (b) Partition, each part having half the volume of the gas','[{"s": "Fig. 6.6 (b) Partition, each part having half the volume of the gas", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20536,54,174,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Heat Capacity','[{"s": "(c) Heat Capacity", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20537,54,175,'paragraph',NULL,'In this sub-section, let us see how to measure heat transferred to a system. This heat appears as a rise in temperature of the system in case of heat absorbed by the system.','[{"s": "In this sub-section, let us see how to measure heat transferred to a system. This heat appears as a rise in temperature of the system in case of heat absorbed by the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20538,54,176,'paragraph',NULL,'The increase of temperature is proportional to the heat transferred','[{"s": "The increase of temperature is proportional to the heat transferred", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20539,54,177,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 16-07-12',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image25.png'),
(20540,54,178,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitude of the coefficient depends on the size, composition and nature of the system. We can also write it as q = C ∆T','[{"s": "The magnitude of the coefficient depends on the size, composition and nature of the system. We can also write it as q = C ∆T", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20541,54,179,'paragraph',NULL,'The coefficient, C is called the heat capacity.','[{"s": "The coefficient, C is called the heat capacity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20542,54,180,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, we can measure the heat supplied by monitoring the temperature rise, provided we know the heat capacity.','[{"s": "Thus, we can measure the heat supplied by monitoring the temperature rise, provided we know the heat capacity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20543,54,181,'paragraph',NULL,'When C is large, a given amount of heat results in only a small temperature rise. Water has a large heat capacity i.e., a lot of energy is needed to raise its temperature.','[{"s": "When C is large, a given amount of heat results in only a small temperature rise. Water has a large heat capacity i.e., a lot of energy is needed to raise its temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20544,54,182,'paragraph',NULL,'C is directly proportional to amount of substance. The molar heat capacity of a substance, Thermodynamics-image49.png , is the heat capacity for one mole of the substance and is the quantity of heat needed to raise the temperature of one mole by one degree celsius (or one kelvin). Specific heat, also called specific heat capacity is the quantity of heat required to raise the temperature of one unit mass of a substance by one degree celsius (or one kelvin). For finding out the heat, q, required to raise the temperatures of a sample, we multiply the specific heat of the substance, c, by the mass m, and temperatures change, ∆T as','[{"s": "C is directly proportional to amount of substance.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The molar heat capacity of a substance,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image49.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-05 16-07-39"}, {"s": ", is the heat capacity for one mole of the substance and is the quantity of heat needed to raise the temperature of one mole by one degree celsius (or one kelvin). Specific heat, also called specific heat capacity is the quantity of heat required to raise the temperature of one unit mass of a substance by one degree celsius (or one kelvin). For finding out the heat, q, required to raise the temperatures of a sample, we multiply the specific heat of the substance, c, by the mass m, and temperatures change, ∆T as", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60914"]',NULL),
(20545,54,183,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 16-08-04',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image61.png'),
(20546,54,184,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) The Relationship between C P and C V for an Ideal Gas','[{"s": "(d) The Relationship between C", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "P", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "for an Ideal Gas", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60908"]',NULL),
(20547,54,185,'paragraph',NULL,'At constant volume, the heat capacity, C is denoted by C V and at constant pressure, this is denoted by C p . Let us find the relationship between the two.','[{"s": "At constant volume, the heat capacity, C is denoted by C", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and at constant pressure, this is denoted by C", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". Let us find the relationship between the two.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60908"]',NULL),
(20548,54,186,'paragraph',NULL,'We can write equation for heat, q','[{"s": "We can write equation for heat, q", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60908"]',NULL),
(20549,54,187,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 16-08-33',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image5.png'),
(20550,54,188,'heading',3,'Measurement of ∆U and ∆H: Calorimetry',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20551,54,189,'paragraph',NULL,'We can measure energy changes associated with chemical or physical processes by an experimental technique called calorimetry. In calorimetry, the process is carried out in a vessel called calorimeter, which is immersed in a known volume of a liquid. Knowing the heat capacity of the liquid in which calorimeter is immersed and the heat capacity of calorimeter, it is possible to determine the heat evolved in the process by measuring temperature changes. Measurements are made under two different conditions:','[{"s": "We can measure energy changes associated with chemical or physical processes by an experimental technique called calorimetry. In calorimetry, the process is carried out in a vessel called calorimeter, which is immersed in a known volume of a liquid. Knowing the heat capacity of the liquid in which calorimeter is immersed and the heat capacity of calorimeter, it is possible to determine the heat evolved in the process by measuring temperature changes. Measurements are made under two different conditions:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20552,54,190,'paragraph',NULL,'i) at constant volume, qV','[{"s": "i) at constant volume, qV", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20553,54,191,'paragraph',NULL,'ii) at constant pressure, qp','[{"s": "ii) at constant pressure, qp", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20554,54,192,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) ∆U Measurements','[{"s": "(a) ∆U Measurements", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20555,54,193,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 16-08-33',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image5.png'),
(20556,54,194,'paragraph',NULL,'For chemical reactions, heat absorbed at constant volume, is measured in a bomb calorimeter (Fig. 6.7). Here, a steel vessel (the bomb) is immersed in a water bath. The whole device is called calorimeter. The steel vessel is immersed in water bath to ensure that no heat is lost to the surroundings. A combustible substance is burnt in pure dioxygen supplied in the steel bomb. Heat evolved during the reaction is transferred to the water around the bomb and its temperature is monitored. Since the bomb calorimeter is sealed, its volume does not change i.e., the energy changes associated with reactions are measured at constant volume. Under these conditions, no work is done as the reaction is carried out at constant volume in the bomb calorimeter. Even for reactions involving gases, there is no work done as ∆V = 0. Temperature change of the calorimeter produced by the completed reaction is then converted to q V , by using the known heat capacity of the calorimeter with the help of equation 6.11.','[{"s": "For chemical reactions, heat absorbed at constant volume, is measured in a bomb calorimeter (Fig. 6.7). Here, a steel vessel (the bomb) is immersed in a water bath. The whole device is called calorimeter. The steel vessel is immersed in water bath to ensure that no heat is lost to the surroundings. A combustible substance is burnt in pure dioxygen supplied in the steel bomb. Heat evolved during the reaction is transferred to the water around the bomb and its temperature is monitored. Since the bomb calorimeter is sealed, its volume does not change i.e., the energy changes associated with reactions are measured at constant volume. Under these conditions, no work is done as the reaction is carried out at constant volume in the bomb calorimeter. Even for reactions involving gases, there is no work done as ∆V = 0. Temperature change of the calorimeter produced by the completed reaction is then converted to q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", by using the known heat capacity of the calorimeter with the help of equation 6.11.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20557,54,195,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image60.png'),
(20558,54,196,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.7 Bomb calorimeter','[{"s": "Fig. 6.7 Bomb calorimeter", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20559,54,197,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) ∆H Measurements','[{"s": "(b) ∆H Measurements", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20560,54,198,'paragraph',NULL,'Measurement of heat change at constant pressure (generally under atmospheric pressure) can be done in a calorimeter shown in Fig. 6.8. We know that ∆Η = q p (at constant p) and, therefore, heat absorbed or evolved, q p at constant pressure is also called the heat of reaction or enthalpy of reaction, ∆ r H.','[{"s": "Measurement of heat change at constant pressure (generally under atmospheric pressure) can be done in a calorimeter shown in Fig. 6.8. We know that ∆Η = q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(at constant p) and, therefore, heat absorbed or evolved, q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at constant pressure is also called the heat of reaction or enthalpy of reaction, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20561,54,199,'paragraph',NULL,'In an exothermic reaction, heat is evolved, and system loses heat to the surroundings. Therefore, q p will be negative and ∆ r H will also be negative. Similarly in an endothermic reaction, heat is absorbed, q p is positive and ∆ r H will be positive.','[{"s": "In an exothermic reaction, heat is evolved, and system loses heat to the surroundings. Therefore, q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will be negative and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H will also be negative. Similarly in an endothermic reaction, heat is absorbed, q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is positive and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H will be positive.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20562,54,200,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image59.png'),
(20563,54,201,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.8 Calorimeter for measuring heat changes at constant pressure (atmospheric pressure).','[{"s": "Fig. 6.8 Calorimeter for measuring heat changes at constant pressure (atmospheric pressure).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20564,54,202,'paragraph',NULL,'Problem 6.6','[{"s": "Problem 6.6", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20565,54,203,'paragraph',NULL,'1g of graphite is burnt in a bomb calorimeter in excess of oxygen at 298 K and 1 atmospheric pressure according to the equation','[{"s": "1g of graphite is burnt in a bomb calorimeter in excess of oxygen at 298 K and 1 atmospheric pressure according to the equation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20566,54,204,'paragraph',NULL,'C (graphite) + O 2 (g) → CO 2 (g)','[{"s": "C (graphite) + O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20567,54,205,'paragraph',NULL,'During the reaction, temperature rises from 298 K to 299 K. If the heat capacity of the bomb calorimeter is 20.7kJ/K, what is the enthalpy change for the above reaction at 298 K and 1 atm?','[{"s": "During the reaction, temperature rises from 298 K to 299 K. If the heat capacity of the bomb calorimeter is 20.7kJ/K, what is the enthalpy change for the above reaction at 298 K and 1 atm?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20568,54,206,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20569,54,207,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose q is the quantity of heat from the reaction mixture and C V is the heat capacity of the calorimeter, then the quantity of heat absorbed by the calorimeter.','[{"s": "Suppose q is the quantity of heat from the reaction mixture and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the heat capacity of the calorimeter, then the quantity of heat absorbed by the calorimeter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20570,54,208,'paragraph',NULL,'q = C V × ∆T','[{"s": "q = C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× ∆T", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20571,54,209,'paragraph',NULL,'Quantity of heat from the reaction will have the same magnitude but opposite sign because the heat lost by the system (reaction mixture) is equal to the heat gained by the calorimeter.','[{"s": "Quantity of heat from the reaction will have the same magnitude but opposite sign because the heat lost by the system (reaction mixture) is equal to the heat gained by the calorimeter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
