(20587,54,225,'paragraph',NULL,'= [H m (CO 2 ,g) + 2H m (H 2 O, l)]– [H m (CH 4 , g) + 2H m (O 2 , g)]','[{"s": "= [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",g) + 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, l)]– [H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", g) + 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", g)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20588,54,226,'paragraph',NULL,'where H m is the molar enthalpy.','[{"s": "where H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the molar enthalpy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20589,54,227,'paragraph',NULL,'Enthalpy change is a very useful quantity. Knowledge of this quantity is required when one needs to plan the heating or cooling required to maintain an industrial chemical reaction at constant temperature. It is also required to calculate temperature dependence of equilibrium constant.','[{"s": "Enthalpy change is a very useful quantity. Knowledge of this quantity is required when one needs to plan the heating or cooling required to maintain an industrial chemical reaction at constant temperature. It is also required to calculate temperature dependence of equilibrium constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20590,54,228,'paragraph',NULL,'6.2.2 Enthalpy, H (a) A useful new state function We know that the heat absorbed at constant volume is equal to change in the internal energy i.e., ∆U = q V . But most of chemical reactions are carried out not at constant volume, but in flasks or test tubes under constant atmospheric pressure. We need to define another state function which may be suitable under these conditions. We may write equation (6.1) as ∆ = − ∆ U q p V p at constant pressure, where qp is heat absorbed by the system and –p∆V represent expansion work done by the system. Let us represent the initial state by subscript 1 and final state by 2 We can rewrite the above equation as U2 –U1 = qp – p (V2 – V1 ) On rearranging, we get qp = (U2 + pV2 ) – (U1 + pV1 ) (6.6) Now we can define another thermodynamic function, the enthalpy H [Greek word enthalpien, to warm or heat content] as : H = U + pV (6.7) so, equation (6.6) becomes qp= H2 – H1 = ∆H Although q is a path dependent function, H is a state function because it depends on U, p and V, all of which are state functions. Therefore, ∆H is independent of path. Hence, qp is also independent of path. For finite changes at constant pressure, we can write equation 6.7 as ∆H = ∆U + ∆pV Since p is constant, we can write ∆H = ∆U + p∆V','[{"s": "6.2.2 Enthalpy, H (a) A useful new state function We know that the heat absorbed at constant volume is equal to change in the internal energy i.e., ∆U = q V . But most of chemical reactions are carried out not at constant volume, but in flasks or test tubes under constant atmospheric pressure. We need to define another state function which may be suitable under these conditions. We may write equation (6.1) as ∆ = − ∆ U q p V p at constant pressure, where qp is heat absorbed by the system and –p∆V represent expansion work done by the system. Let us represent the initial state by subscript 1 and final state by 2 We can rewrite the above equation as U2 –U1 = qp – p (V2 – V1 ) On rearranging, we get qp = (U2 + pV2 ) – (U1 + pV1 ) (6.6) Now we can define another thermodynamic function, the enthalpy H [Greek word enthalpien, to warm or heat content] as : H = U + pV (6.7) so, equation (6.6) becomes qp= H2 – H1 = ∆H Although q is a path dependent function, H is a state function because it depends on U, p and V, all of which are state functions. Therefore, ∆H is independent of path. Hence, qp is also independent of path. For finite changes at constant pressure, we can write equation 6.7 as ∆H = ∆U + ∆pV Since p is constant, we can write ∆H = ∆U + p∆V", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60919", "60920", "60921", "60922", "60923"]',NULL),
(20591,54,229,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Standard Enthalpy of Reactions','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Standard Enthalpy of Reactions", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60905"]',NULL),
(33776,78,337,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer There are 3 significant figures in the measured mass whereas there are only 2 significant figures in the measured volume. Hence the density should be expressed to only 2 significant figures.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "There are 3 significant figures in the measured mass whereas there are only 2 significant figures in the measured volume. Hence the density should be expressed to only 2 significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33777,78,338,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image4.png'),
(20592,54,230,'paragraph',NULL,'Enthalpy of a reaction depends on the conditions under which a reaction is carried out. It is, therefore, necessary that we must specify some standard conditions. The standard enthalpy of reaction is the enthalpy change for a reaction when all the participating substances are in their standard states.','[{"s": "Enthalpy of a reaction depends on the conditions under which a reaction is carried out. It is, therefore, necessary that we must specify some standard conditions.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The standard enthalpy of reaction is the enthalpy change for a reaction when all the participating substances are in their standard states.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60905"]',NULL),
(20593,54,231,'paragraph',NULL,'The standard state of a substance at a specified temperature is its pure form at 1 bar.','[{"s": "The standard state of a substance at a specified temperature is its pure form at 1 bar.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]','marked','["60905"]',NULL),
(20594,54,232,'paragraph',NULL,'Standard conditions are denoted by adding the superscript 0 to the symbol ∆H, e.g., ∆H ⊖','[{"s": "Standard conditions are denoted by adding the superscript 0 to the symbol ∆H, e.g., ∆H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60905"]',NULL),
(20595,54,233,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Enthalpy Changes during Phase Transformation s','[{"s": "(b) Enthalpy Changes during Phase Transformation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20596,54,234,'paragraph',NULL,'Phase transformations also involve energy changes. Ice, for example, requires heat for melting. Normally this melting takes place at constant pressure (atmospheric pressure) and during phase change, temperature remains constant (at 273 K).','[{"s": "Phase transformations also involve energy changes. Ice, for example, requires heat for melting. Normally this melting takes place at constant pressure (atmospheric pressure) and during phase change, temperature remains constant (at 273 K).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20597,54,235,'paragraph',NULL,'H 2 O(s) → H 2 O(l); ∆ fus H ⊖ = 6.00 kJ moI–1','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(s) → H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l); ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "fus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 6.00 kJ moI–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20598,54,236,'paragraph',NULL,'Here ∆ fus H ⊖ is enthalpy of fusion in standard state. If water freezes, then process is reversed and equal amount of heat is given off to the surroundings.','[{"s": "Here ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "fus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is enthalpy of fusion in standard state. If water freezes, then process is reversed and equal amount of heat is given off to the surroundings.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20599,54,237,'paragraph',NULL,'The enthalpy change that accompanies melting of one mole of a solid substance in standard state is called standard enthalpy of fusion or molar enthalpy of fusion, ∆fusH ⊖ .','[{"s": "The enthalpy change that accompanies melting of one mole of a solid substance in standard state is called standard enthalpy of fusion or molar enthalpy of fusion,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "∆fusH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60928"]',NULL),
(20600,54,238,'paragraph',NULL,'Melting of a solid is endothermic, so all enthalpies of fusion are positive. Water requires heat for evaporation. At constant temperature of its boiling point T b and at constant pressure:','[{"s": "Melting of a solid is endothermic, so all enthalpies of fusion are positive. Water requires heat for evaporation. At constant temperature of its boiling point T", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "b", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and at constant pressure:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60928"]',NULL),
(20601,54,239,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 6.1 Standard Enthalpy Changes of Fusion and Vaporisation','[{"s": "Table 6.1 Standard Enthalpy Changes of Fusion and Vaporisation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
