(20604,54,242,'paragraph',NULL,'H 2 O(l) → H 2 O(g); ∆ vap H ⊖ = + 40.79 kJ moI–1 ∆ vap H ⊖ is the standard enthalpy of vaporisation','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l) → H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(g); ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "vap", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= + 40.79 kJ moI–1 ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "vap", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "standard enthalpy of vaporisation", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60919"]',NULL),
(20605,54,243,'paragraph',NULL,'Amount of heat required to vaporize one mole of a liquid at constant temperature and under standard pressure (1bar) is called its standard enthalpy of vaporization or molar enthalpy of vaporization, ∆vapH ⊖ .','[{"s": "Amount of heat required to vaporize one mole of a liquid at constant temperature and under standard pressure (1bar) is called its standard enthalpy of vaporization or molar enthalpy of vaporization,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "∆vapH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60919"]',NULL),
(20606,54,244,'paragraph',NULL,'Sublimation is direct conversion of a solid into its vapour. Solid CO 2 or ‘dry ice’ sublimes at 195K with ∆ sub H ⊖ =25.2 kJ mol–1; naphthalene sublimes slowly and for this ∆ sub H ⊖ = 73.0 kJ mol–1 .','[{"s": "Sublimation is direct conversion of a solid into its vapour. Solid CO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "or ‘dry ice’ sublimes at 195K with ∆", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "sub", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "=25.2 kJ mol–1; naphthalene sublimes slowly and for this ∆", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "sub", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "= 73.0 kJ mol–1 .", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60919"]',NULL),
(20607,54,245,'paragraph',NULL,'Standard enthalpy of sublimation, ∆ sub H ⊖ is the change in enthalpy when one mole of a solid substance sublimes at a constant temperature and under standard pressure (1bar).','[{"s": "Standard enthalpy of sublimation,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "sub", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is the change in enthalpy when one mole of a solid substance sublimes at a constant temperature and under standard pressure (1bar).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60919"]',NULL),
(20608,54,246,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitude of the enthalpy change depends on the strength of the intermolecular interactions in the substance undergoing the phase transfomations. For example, the strong hydrogen bonds between water molecules hold them tightly in liquid phase. For an organic liquid, such as acetone, the intermolecular dipole-dipole interactions are significantly weaker. Thus, it requires less heat to vaporise 1 mol of acetone than it does to vaporize 1 mol of water. Table 6.1 gives values of standard enthalpy changes of fusion and vaporisation for some substances.','[{"s": "The magnitude of the enthalpy change depends on the strength of the intermolecular interactions in the substance undergoing the phase transfomations. For example, the strong hydrogen bonds between water molecules hold them tightly in liquid phase. For an organic liquid, such as acetone, the intermolecular dipole-dipole interactions are significantly weaker. Thus, it requires less heat to vaporise 1 mol of acetone than it does to vaporize 1 mol of water. Table 6.1 gives values of standard enthalpy changes of fusion and vaporisation for some substances.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
