(20623,54,261,'paragraph',NULL,'It is important to understand that a standard molar enthalpy of formation, ∆ f H ⊖ , is just a special case of ∆ r H ⊖ , where one mole of a compound is formed from its constituent elements, as in the above three equations, where 1 mol of each, water, methane and ethanol is formed. In contrast, the enthalpy change for an exothermic reaction:','[{"s": "It is important to understand that a standard molar enthalpy of formation, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", is just a special case of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where one mole of a compound is formed from its constituent elements, as in the above three equations, where 1 mol of each, water, methane and ethanol is formed. In contrast, the enthalpy change for an exothermic reaction:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
