(20628,54,266,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r H ⊖ = – 178.3kJ mol–1','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 178.3kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20629,54,267,'paragraph',NULL,'Here two moles, instead of one mole of the product is formed from the elements, i.e., .','[{"s": "Here two moles, instead of one mole of the product is formed from the elements, i.e., .", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20630,54,268,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r H ⊖ = 2∆ f H ⊖','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20631,54,269,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, by dividing all coefficients in the balanced equation by 2, expression for enthalpy of formation of HBr (g) is written as','[{"s": "Therefore, by dividing all coefficients in the balanced equation by 2, expression for enthalpy of formation of HBr (g) is written as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20632,54,270,'paragraph',NULL,'½H 2 (g) + ½Br 2 (1) → HBr(g);','[{"s": "½H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + ½Br", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1) → HBr(g);", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20633,54,271,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ f H ⊖ = – 36.4 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 36.4 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20634,54,272,'paragraph',NULL,'By convention, standard enthalpy for formation, ∆ f H ⊖ , of an element in reference state, i.e., its most stable state of aggregation is taken as zero.','[{"s": "By convention, standard enthalpy for formation, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", of an element in reference state, i.e., its most stable state of aggregation is taken as zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20635,54,273,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose, you are a chemical engineer and want to know how much heat is required to decompose calcium carbonate to lime and carbon dioxide, with all the substances in their standard state.','[{"s": "Suppose, you are a chemical engineer and want to know how much heat is required to decompose calcium carbonate to lime and carbon dioxide, with all the substances in their standard state.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20636,54,274,'paragraph',NULL,'CaCO 3 (s) → CaO(s) + CO 2 (g); ∆ r H ⊖ = ?','[{"s": "CaCO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) → CaO(s) + CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g); ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20637,54,275,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, we can make use of standard enthalpy of formation and calculate the enthalpy change for the reaction. The following general equation can be used for the enthalpy change calculation.','[{"s": "Here, we can make use of standard enthalpy of formation and calculate the enthalpy change for the reaction. The following general equation can be used for the enthalpy change calculation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20638,54,276,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r H ⊖ = Thermodynamics-image18.png a i ∆ f H ⊖ (products) – Thermodynamics-image18.png b i ∆ f H ⊖ (reactants) (6.15)','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image18.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(products) –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image18.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(reactants) (6.15)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20639,54,277,'paragraph',NULL,'where a and b represent the coefficients of the products and reactants in the balanced equation. Let us apply the above equation for decomposition of calcium carbonate. Here, coefficients ‘a’ and ‘b’ are 1 each. Therefore,','[{"s": "where a and b represent the coefficients of the products and reactants in the balanced equation. Let us apply the above equation for decomposition of calcium carbonate. Here, coefficients ‘a’ and ‘b’ are 1 each. Therefore,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20640,54,278,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r H ⊖ = ∆ f H ⊖ = [CaO(s)]+ ∆ f H ⊖ [CO 2 (g)]','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [CaO(s)]+ ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20641,54,279,'paragraph',NULL,'– ∆ f H ⊖ = [CaCO 3 (s)]','[{"s": "– ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [CaCO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20642,54,280,'paragraph',NULL,'=1 (–635.1 kJ mol–1) + 1(–393.5 kJ mol–1)','[{"s": "=1 (–635.1 kJ mol–1) + 1(–393.5 kJ mol–1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20643,54,281,'paragraph',NULL,'–1(–1206.9 kJ mol–1)','[{"s": "–1(–1206.9 kJ mol–1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20644,54,282,'paragraph',NULL,'= 178.3 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "= 178.3 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20645,54,283,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the decomposition of CaCO 3 (s) is an endothermic process and you have to heat it for getting the desired products.','[{"s": "Thus, the decomposition of CaCO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s) is an endothermic process and you have to heat it for getting the desired products.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20646,54,284,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Thermochemical Equations','[{"s": "(d) Thermochemical Equations", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20647,54,285,'paragraph',NULL,'A balanced chemical equation together with the value of its ∆ r H is called a thermochemical equation. We specify the physical state (alongwith allotropic state) of the substance in an equation. For example:','[{"s": "A balanced chemical equation together with the value of its ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H is called a thermochemical equation. We specify the physical state (alongwith allotropic state) of the substance in an equation. For example:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20648,54,286,'paragraph',NULL,'C 2 H 5 OH(l) + 3O 2 (g) → 2CO 2 (g) + 3H 2 O(l);','[{"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OH(l) + 3O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → 2CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) + 3H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O(l);", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20649,54,287,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r H ⊖ = – 1367 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 1367 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20650,54,288,'paragraph',NULL,'The above equation describes the combustion of liquid ethanol at constant temperature and pressure. The negative sign of enthalpy change indicates that this is an exothermic reaction.','[{"s": "The above equation describes the combustion of liquid ethanol at constant temperature and pressure. The negative sign of enthalpy change indicates that this is an exothermic reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20651,54,289,'paragraph',NULL,'It would be necessary to remember the following conventions regarding thermo-chemical equations.','[{"s": "It would be necessary to remember the following conventions regarding thermo-chemical equations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20652,54,290,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The coefficients in a balanced thermo-chemical equation refer to the number of moles (never molecules) of reactants and products involved in the reaction.','[{"s": "1. The coefficients in a balanced thermo-chemical equation refer to the number of moles (never molecules) of reactants and products involved in the reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20653,54,291,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The numerical value of ∆ r H ⊖ refers to the number of moles of substances specified by an equation. Standard enthalpy change ∆ r H ⊖ will have units as kJ mol–1.','[{"s": "2. The numerical value of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "refers to the number of moles of substances specified by an equation. Standard enthalpy change ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will have units as kJ mol–1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20654,54,292,'paragraph',NULL,'To illustrate the concept, let us consider the calculation of heat of reaction for the following reaction :','[{"s": "To illustrate the concept, let us consider the calculation of heat of reaction for the following reaction :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
