(20810,54,448,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Enthalpy of Atomization (symbol: ∆ a H ⊖ )','[{"s": "(b) Enthalpy of Atomization (symbol: ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20811,54,449,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemical reactions involve the breaking and making of chemical bonds. Energy is required to break a bond and energy is released when a bond is formed. It is possible to relate heat of reaction to changes in energy associated with breaking and making of chemical bonds. With reference to the enthalpy changes associated with chemical bonds, two different terms are used in thermodynamics.','[{"s": "Chemical reactions involve the breaking and making of chemical bonds. Energy is required to break a bond and energy is released when a bond is formed. It is possible to relate heat of reaction to changes in energy associated with breaking and making of chemical bonds. With reference to the enthalpy changes associated with chemical bonds, two different terms are used in thermodynamics.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20812,54,450,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Bond dissociation enthalpy','[{"s": "(i) Bond dissociation enthalpy", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20813,54,451,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Mean bond enthalpy','[{"s": "(ii) Mean bond enthalpy", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20814,54,452,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us discuss these terms with reference to diatomic and polyatomic molecules.','[{"s": "Let us discuss these terms with reference to diatomic and polyatomic molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20815,54,453,'paragraph',NULL,'Diatomic Molecules: Consider the following process in which the bonds in one mole of dihydrogen gas (H 2 ) are broken:','[{"s": "Diatomic Molecules: Consider the following process in which the bonds in one mole of dihydrogen gas (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") are broken:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20816,54,454,'paragraph',NULL,'H 2 (g) → 2H(g) ; ∆ H–H H ⊖ = 435.0 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → 2H(g) ; ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H–H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 435.0 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20817,54,455,'paragraph',NULL,'The enthalpy change involved in this process is the bond dissociation enthalpy of H–H bond. The bond dissociation enthalpy is the change in enthalpy when one mole of covalent bonds of a gaseous covalent compound is broken to form products in the gas phase.','[{"s": "The enthalpy change involved in this process is the bond dissociation enthalpy of H–H bond. The bond dissociation enthalpy is the change in enthalpy when one mole of covalent bonds of a gaseous covalent compound is broken to form products in the gas phase.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20818,54,456,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that it is the same as the enthalpy of atomization of dihydrogen. This is true for all diatomic molecules. For example:','[{"s": "Note that it is the same as the enthalpy of atomization of dihydrogen. This is true for all diatomic molecules. For example:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20819,54,457,'paragraph',NULL,'Cl 2 (g) → 2Cl(g) ; ∆ Cl–Cl H ⊖ = 242 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → 2Cl(g) ; ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl–Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 242 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20820,54,458,'paragraph',NULL,'O 2 (g) → 2O(g) ; ∆ O=O H ⊖ = 428 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → 2O(g) ; ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O=O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 428 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20821,54,459,'paragraph',NULL,'In the case of polyatomic molecules, bond dissociation enthalpy is different for different bonds within the same molecule.','[{"s": "In the case of polyatomic molecules, bond dissociation enthalpy is different for different bonds within the same molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20822,54,460,'paragraph',NULL,'Polyatomic Molecules: Let us now consider a polyatomic molecule like methane, CH 4 . The overall thermochemical equation for its atomization reaction is given below:','[{"s": "Polyatomic Molecules: Let us now consider a polyatomic molecule like methane, CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The overall thermochemical equation for its atomization reaction is given below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20823,54,461,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 14-26-52',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image11.png'),
(20824,54,462,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ a H ⊖ = 1665 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1665 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20825,54,463,'paragraph',NULL,'In methane, all the four C – H bonds are identical in bond length and energy. However, the energies required to break the individual C – H bonds in each successive step differ :','[{"s": "In methane, all the four C – H bonds are identical in bond length and energy. However, the energies required to break the individual C – H bonds in each successive step differ :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20826,54,464,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 4 (g) → CH 3 (g)+H(g);∆ bond H ⊖ = +427 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)+H(g);∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bond", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= +427 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20827,54,465,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 3 (g) → CH 2 (g)+H(g);∆ bond H ⊖ = +439 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g)+H(g);∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bond", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= +439 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20828,54,466,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 2 (g) → CH(g)+H(g);∆ bond H ⊖ = +452 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → CH(g)+H(g);∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bond", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= +452 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20829,54,467,'paragraph',NULL,'CH(g) → C(g)+H(g);∆ bond H ⊖ = +347 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "CH(g) → C(g)+H(g);∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bond", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= +347 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20830,54,468,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore,','[{"s": "Therefore,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20831,54,469,'paragraph',NULL,'CH 4 (g) → C(g)+4H(g);∆ a H ⊖ = 1665 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) → C(g)+4H(g);∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1665 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20832,54,470,'paragraph',NULL,'In such cases we use mean bond enthalpy of C – H bond.','[{"s": "In such cases we use mean bond enthalpy of C – H bond.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20833,54,471,'paragraph',NULL,'For example in CH 4 , ∆ C–H H ⊖ is calculated as:','[{"s": "For example in CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C–H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is calculated as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20834,54,472,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ C–H H ⊖ = ¼ (∆ a H ⊖ ) = ¼ (1665 kJ mol–1)','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C–H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ¼ (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = ¼ (1665 kJ mol–1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20835,54,473,'paragraph',NULL,'= 416 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "= 416 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20836,54,474,'paragraph',NULL,'We find that mean C–H bond enthalpy in methane is 416 kJ/mol. It has been found that mean C–H bond enthalpies differ slightly from compound to compound, as in CH 3 CH 2 Cl,CH 3 NO 2 , etc, but it does not differ in a great deal*. Using Hess’s law, bond enthalpies can be calculated. Bond enthalpy values of some single and multiple bonds are given in Table 6.3. The reaction enthalpies are very important quantities as these arise from the changes that accompany the breaking of old bonds and formation of the new bonds. We can predict enthalpy of a reaction in gas phase, if we know different bond enthalpies. The standard enthalpy of reaction, ∆ r H ⊖ is related to bond enthalpies of the reactants and products in gas phase reactions as:','[{"s": "We find that mean C–H bond enthalpy in methane is 416 kJ/mol. It has been found that mean C–H bond enthalpies differ slightly from compound to compound, as in CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl,CH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", etc, but it does not differ in a great deal*. Using Hess’s law, bond enthalpies can be calculated. Bond enthalpy values of some single and multiple bonds are given in Table 6.3. The reaction enthalpies are very important quantities as these arise from the changes that accompany the breaking of old bonds and formation of the new bonds. We can predict enthalpy of a reaction in gas phase, if we know different bond enthalpies. The standard enthalpy of reaction, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is related to bond enthalpies of the reactants and products in gas phase reactions as:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20837,54,475,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 14-27-29',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image2.png'),
(20838,54,476,'paragraph',NULL,'This relationship is particularly more useful when the required values of ∆ f H ⊖ are not available. The net enthalpy change of a reaction is the amount of energy required to break all the bonds in the reactant molecules minus the amount of energy required to break all the bonds in the product molecules. Remember that this relationship is approximate and is valid when all substances (reactants and products) in the reaction are in gaseous state.','[{"s": "This relationship is particularly more useful when the required values of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are not available. The net enthalpy change of a reaction is the amount of energy required to break all the bonds in the reactant molecules minus the amount of energy required to break all the bonds in the product molecules. Remember that this relationship is approximate and is valid when all substances (reactants and products) in the reaction are in gaseous state.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20839,54,477,'paragraph',NULL,'The lattice enthalpy of an ionic compound is the enthalpy change which occurs when one mole of an ionic compound dissociates into its ions in gaseous state.','[{"s": "The lattice enthalpy of an ionic compound is the enthalpy change which occurs when one mole of an ionic compound dissociates into its ions in gaseous state.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20840,54,478,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 14-29-15',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image22.png'),
(20841,54,479,'paragraph',NULL,'Since it is impossible to determine lattice enthalpies directly by experiment, we use an indirect method where we construct an enthalpy diagram called a Born-Haber Cycle (Fig. 6.9).','[{"s": "Since it is impossible to determine lattice enthalpies directly by experiment, we use an indirect method where we construct an enthalpy diagram called a Born-Haber Cycle (Fig. 6.9).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20842,54,480,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us now calculate the lattice enthalpy of Na+Cl–(s) by following steps given below :','[{"s": "Let us now calculate the lattice enthalpy of Na+Cl–(s) by following steps given below :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20843,54,481,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Na ( s ) → Na ( g ), sublimation of sodium metal, ∆ sub H ⊖ = 108.4 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "1. Na ( s ) → Na ( g ), sublimation of sodium metal, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sub", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 108.4 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20844,54,482,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Na ( g ) → Na + ( g ) + e −1 ( g ) ,, the ionization of sodium atoms, ionization enthalpy ∆ i H ⊖ = 496 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "2. Na ( g ) → Na", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "( g ) + e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "( g ) ,, the ionization of sodium atoms, ionization enthalpy ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 496 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20845,54,483,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Thermodynamics-image29.png , the dissociation of chlorine, the reaction enthalpy is half the bond dissociation enthalpy.','[{"s": "3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image29.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-05 14-32-49"}, {"s": ", the dissociation of chlorine, the reaction enthalpy is half the bond dissociation enthalpy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20846,54,484,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 14-33-12',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image75.png'),
(20847,54,485,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Thermodynamics-image17.png electron gained by chlorine atoms. The electron gain enthalpy, ∆ eg H 0 = – 348.6 kJ mol–1.','[{"s": "4.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image17.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-05 14-33-25"}, {"s": "electron gained by chlorine atoms. The electron gain enthalpy, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H 0 = – 348.6 kJ mol–1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20848,54,486,'paragraph',NULL,'You have learnt about ionization enthalpy and electron gain enthalpy in Unit 3. In fact, these terms have been taken from thermodynamics. Earlier terms, ionization energy and electron affinity were in practice in place of the above terms (see the box for justification).','[{"s": "You have learnt about ionization enthalpy and electron gain enthalpy in Unit 3. In fact, these terms have been taken from thermodynamics. Earlier terms, ionization energy and electron affinity were in practice in place of the above terms (see the box for justification).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20849,54,487,'paragraph',NULL,'Ionization Energy and Electron Affinity','[{"s": "Ionization Energy and Electron Affinity", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20850,54,488,'paragraph',NULL,'Ionization energy and electron affinity are defined at absolute zero. At any other temperature, heat capacities for the reactants and the products have to be taken into account. Enthalpies of reactions for','[{"s": "Ionization energy and electron affinity are defined at absolute zero. At any other temperature, heat capacities for the reactants and the products have to be taken into account. Enthalpies of reactions for", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20851,54,489,'paragraph',NULL,'M(g) → M+(g) + e– (for ionization)','[{"s": "M(g) → M+(g) + e– (for ionization)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20852,54,490,'paragraph',NULL,'M(g) + e– → M–(g) (for electron gain)','[{"s": "M(g) + e– → M–(g) (for electron gain)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20853,54,491,'paragraph',NULL,'at temperature, T is','[{"s": "at temperature, T is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20854,54,492,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r H ⊖ (T ) = ∆ r H ⊖ (0) + Thermodynamics-image9.png ∆ r C P ⊖ dT','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(T ) = ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image9.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20855,54,493,'paragraph',NULL,'The value of C p for each species in the above reaction is 5/2 R (C V = 3/2R)','[{"s": "The value of C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for each species in the above reaction is 5/2 R (C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3/2R)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20856,54,494,'paragraph',NULL,'So, ∆ r C p ⊖ = + 5/2 R (for ionization)','[{"s": "So, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= + 5/2 R (for ionization)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20857,54,495,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r C p ⊖ = – 5/2 R (for electron gain)','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 5/2 R (for electron gain)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20858,54,496,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore,','[{"s": "Therefore,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20859,54,497,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r H0 (ionization enthalpy)','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H0 (ionization enthalpy)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20860,54,498,'paragraph',NULL,'= E 0 (ionization energy) + 5/2 RT','[{"s": "= E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(ionization energy) + 5/2 RT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20861,54,499,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r H0 (electron gain enthalpy)','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H0 (electron gain enthalpy)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20862,54,500,'paragraph',NULL,'= – A( electron affinity) – 5/2 RT','[{"s": "= – A( electron affinity) – 5/2 RT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20863,54,501,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Thermodynamics-image30.png','[{"s": "5.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image30.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-05 14-37-32"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20864,54,502,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ lattice H ⊖ = 411.2 + 108.4 + 121 + 496 – 348.6','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lattice", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 411.2 + 108.4 + 121 + 496 – 348.6", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20865,54,503,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ lattice H ⊖ = + 788kJ','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lattice", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= + 788kJ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20866,54,504,'paragraph',NULL,'for NaCl(s) d Na+(g) + Cl–(g)','[{"s": "for NaCl(s) d Na+(g) + Cl–(g)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20867,54,505,'paragraph',NULL,'Internal energy is smaller by 2RT ( because ∆n g = 2) and is equal to + 783 kJ mol–1.','[{"s": "Internal energy is smaller by 2RT ( because ∆n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2) and is equal to + 783 kJ mol–1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20868,54,506,'paragraph',NULL,'Now we use the value of lattice enthalpy to calculate enthalpy of solution from the expression:','[{"s": "Now we use the value of lattice enthalpy to calculate enthalpy of solution from the expression:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20869,54,507,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ sol H ⊖ = ∆ lattice H ⊖ + ∆ hyd H ⊖','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lattice", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hyd", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20870,54,508,'paragraph',NULL,'For one mole of NaCl(s),','[{"s": "For one mole of NaCl(s),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20871,54,509,'paragraph',NULL,'lattice enthalpy = + 788 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "lattice enthalpy = + 788 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20872,54,510,'paragraph',NULL,'and ∆ hyd H ⊖ = – 784 kJ mol–1( from the literature)','[{"s": "and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hyd", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 784 kJ mol–1( from the literature)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20873,54,511,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ sol H ⊖ = + 788 kJ mol–1 – 784 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= + 788 kJ mol–1 – 784 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20874,54,512,'paragraph',NULL,'= + 4 kJ mol–1','[{"s": "= + 4 kJ mol–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20875,54,513,'paragraph',NULL,'The dissolution of NaCl(s) is accompanied by very little heat change.','[{"s": "The dissolution of NaCl(s) is accompanied by very little heat change.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20876,54,514,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) Enthalpy of Solution (symbol : ∆ sol H ⊖ )','[{"s": "(e) Enthalpy of Solution (symbol : ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20877,54,515,'paragraph',NULL,'Enthalpy of solution of a substance is the enthalpy change when one mole of it dissolves in a specified amount of solvent. The enthalpy of solution at infinite dilution is the enthalpy change observed on dissolving the substance in an infinite amount of solvent when the interactions between the ions (or solute molecules) are negligible.','[{"s": "Enthalpy of solution of a substance is the enthalpy change when one mole of it dissolves in a specified amount of solvent. The enthalpy of solution at infinite dilution is the enthalpy change observed on dissolving the substance in an infinite amount of solvent when the interactions between the ions (or solute molecules) are negligible.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20878,54,516,'paragraph',NULL,'When an ionic compound dissolves in a solvent, the ions leave their ordered positions on the crystal lattice. These are now more free in solution. But solvation of these ions (hydration in case solvent is water) also occurs at the same time. This is shown diagrammatically, for an ionic compound, AB (s)','[{"s": "When an ionic compound dissolves in a solvent, the ions leave their ordered positions on the crystal lattice. These are now more free in solution. But solvation of these ions (hydration in case solvent is water) also occurs at the same time. This is shown diagrammatically, for an ionic compound, AB (s)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20879,54,517,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image40.png'),
(20880,54,518,'paragraph',NULL,'The enthalpy of solution of AB(s), ∆ sol H ⊖ , in water is, therefore, determined by the selective values of the lattice enthalpy,∆ lattice H ⊖ and enthalpy of hydration of ions, ∆ hyd H ⊖ as','[{"s": "The enthalpy of solution of AB(s), ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", in water is, therefore, determined by the selective values of the lattice enthalpy,∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lattice", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and enthalpy of hydration of ions, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hyd", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20881,54,519,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ sol H ⊖ = ∆ lattice H ⊖ + ∆ hyd H ⊖','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lattice", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hyd", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20882,54,520,'paragraph',NULL,'For most of the ionic compounds, ∆ sol H ⊖ is positive and the dissociation process is endothermic. Therefore the solubility of most salts in water increases with rise of temperature. If the lattice enthalpy is very high, the dissolution of the compound may not take place at all. Why do many fluorides tend to be less soluble than the corresponding chlorides? Estimates of the magnitudes of enthalpy changes may be made by using tables of bond energies (enthalpies) and lattice energies (enthalpies).','[{"s": "For most of the ionic compounds, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is positive and the dissociation process is endothermic. Therefore the solubility of most salts in water increases with rise of temperature. If the lattice enthalpy is very high, the dissolution of the compound may not take place at all. Why do many fluorides tend to be less soluble than the corresponding chlorides? Estimates of the magnitudes of enthalpy changes may be made by using tables of bond energies (enthalpies) and lattice energies (enthalpies).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20883,54,521,'paragraph',NULL,'(f) Enthalpy of Dilution','[{"s": "(f) Enthalpy of Dilution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(20884,54,522,'paragraph',NULL,'It is known that enthalpy of solution is the enthalpy change associated with the addition of a specified amount of solute to the specified amount of solvent at a constant temperature and pressure. This argument can be applied to any solvent with slight modification. Enthalpy change for dissolving one mole of gaseous hydrogen chloride in 10 mol of water can be represented by the following equation. For convenience we will use the symbol aq. for water','[{"s": "It is known that enthalpy of solution is the enthalpy change associated with the addition of a specified amount of solute to the specified amount of solvent at a constant temperature and pressure. This argument can be applied to any solvent with slight modification. Enthalpy change for dissolving one mole of gaseous hydrogen chloride in 10 mol of water can be represented by the following equation. For convenience we will use the symbol aq. for water", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20885,54,523,'paragraph',NULL,'HCl(g) + 10 aq. → HCl.10 aq.','[{"s": "HCl(g) + 10 aq. → HCl.10 aq.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20886,54,524,'paragraph',NULL,'∆H = –69.01 kJ / mol','[{"s": "∆H = –69.01 kJ / mol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20887,54,525,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us consider the following set of enthalpy changes:','[{"s": "Let us consider the following set of enthalpy changes:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20888,54,526,'paragraph',NULL,'(S-1) HCl(g) + 25 aq. → HCl.25 aq. ∆H = –72.03 kJ / mol','[{"s": "(S-1) HCl(g) + 25 aq. → HCl.25 aq. ∆H = –72.03 kJ / mol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20889,54,527,'paragraph',NULL,'(S-2) HCl(g) + 40 aq. → HCl.40 aq. ∆H = –72.79 kJ / mol','[{"s": "(S-2) HCl(g) + 40 aq. → HCl.40 aq. ∆H = –72.79 kJ / mol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20890,54,528,'paragraph',NULL,'(S-3) HCl(g) + ∞ aq. → HCl. ∞ aq. ∆H = –74.85 kJ / mol','[{"s": "(S-3) HCl(g) + ∞ aq. → HCl. ∞ aq. ∆H = –74.85 kJ / mol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20891,54,529,'paragraph',NULL,'The values of ∆H show general dependence of the enthalpy of solution on amount of solvent. As more and more solvent is used, the enthalpy of solution approaches a limiting value, i.e, the value in infinitely dilute solution. For hydrochloric acid this value of ∆H is given above in equation (S-3).','[{"s": "The values of ∆H show general dependence of the enthalpy of solution on amount of solvent. As more and more solvent is used, the enthalpy of solution approaches a limiting value, i.e, the value in infinitely dilute solution. For hydrochloric acid this value of ∆H is given above in equation (S-3).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20892,54,530,'paragraph',NULL,'If we subtract the first equation (equation S-1) from the second equation (equation S-2) in the above set of equations, we obtain–','[{"s": "If we subtract the first equation (equation S-1) from the second equation (equation S-2) in the above set of equations, we obtain–", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20893,54,531,'paragraph',NULL,'HCl.25 aq. + 15 aq. → HCl.40 aq.','[{"s": "HCl.25 aq. + 15 aq. → HCl.40 aq.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20894,54,532,'paragraph',NULL,'∆H = [ –72.79 – (–72.03)] kJ / mol','[{"s": "∆H = [ –72.79 – (–72.03)] kJ / mol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20895,54,533,'paragraph',NULL,'= – 0.76 kJ / mol','[{"s": "= – 0.76 kJ / mol", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20896,54,534,'paragraph',NULL,'This value (–0.76kJ/mol) of ∆H is enthalpy of dilution. It is the heat withdrawn from the surroundings when additional solvent is added to the solution. The enthalpy of dilution of a solution is dependent on the original concentration of the solution and the amount of solvent added.','[{"s": "This value (–0.76kJ/mol) of ∆H is enthalpy of dilution. It is the heat withdrawn from the surroundings when additional solvent is added to the solution. The enthalpy of dilution of a solution is dependent on the original concentration of the solution and the amount of solvent added.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20897,54,535,'heading',3,'spontaneity',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20898,54,536,'paragraph',NULL,'The first law of thermodynamics tells us about the relationship between the heat absorbed and the work performed on or by a system. It puts no restrictions on the direction of heat flow. However, the flow of heat is unidirectional from higher temperature to lower temperature. In fact, all naturally occurring processes whether chemical or physical will tend to proceed spontaneously in one direction only. For example, a gas expanding to fill the available volume, burning carbon in dioxygen giving carbon dioxide.','[{"s": "The first law of thermodynamics tells us about the relationship between the heat absorbed and the work performed on or by a system. It puts no restrictions on the direction of heat flow. However, the flow of heat is unidirectional from higher temperature to lower temperature. In fact, all naturally occurring processes whether chemical or physical will tend to proceed spontaneously in one direction only. For example, a gas expanding to fill the available volume, burning carbon in dioxygen giving carbon dioxide.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20899,54,537,'paragraph',NULL,'But heat will not flow from colder body to warmer body on its own, the gas in a container will not spontaneously contract into one corner or carbon dioxide will not form carbon and dioxygen spontaneously. These and many other spontaneously occurring changes show unidirectional change. We may ask ‘what is the driving force of spontaneously occurring changes ? What determines the direction of a spontaneous change ? In this section, we shall establish some criterion for these processes whether these will take place or not.','[{"s": "But heat will not flow from colder body to warmer body on its own, the gas in a container will not spontaneously contract into one corner or carbon dioxide will not form carbon and dioxygen spontaneously. These and many other spontaneously occurring changes show unidirectional change. We may ask ‘what is the driving force of spontaneously occurring changes ? What determines the direction of a spontaneous change ? In this section, we shall establish some criterion for these processes whether these will take place or not.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20900,54,538,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us first understand what do we mean by spontaneous reaction or change ? You may think by your common observation that spontaneous reaction is one which occurs immediately when contact is made between the reactants. Take the case of combination of hydrogen and oxygen. These gases may be mixed at room temperature and left for many years without observing any perceptible change. Although the reaction is taking place between them, it is at an extremely slow rate. It is still called spontaneous reaction. So spontaneity means ‘having the potential to proceed without the assistance of external agency’. However, it does not tell about the rate of the reaction or process. Another aspect of spontaneous reaction or process, as we see is that these cannot reverse their direction on their own. We may summarise it as follows:','[{"s": "Let us first understand what do we mean by spontaneous reaction or change ? You may think by your common observation that spontaneous reaction is one which occurs immediately when contact is made between the reactants. Take the case of combination of hydrogen and oxygen. These gases may be mixed at room temperature and left for many years without observing any perceptible change. Although the reaction is taking place between them, it is at an extremely slow rate. It is still called spontaneous reaction. So spontaneity means ‘having the potential to proceed without the assistance of external agency’. However, it does not tell about the rate of the reaction or process. Another aspect of spontaneous reaction or process, as we see is that these cannot reverse their direction on their own. We may summarise it as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20901,54,539,'paragraph',NULL,'A spontaneous process is an irreversible process and may only be reversed by some external agency.','[{"s": "A spontaneous process is an irreversible process and may only be reversed by some external agency.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20902,54,540,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Is Decrease in Enthalpy a Criterion for Spontaneity ?','[{"s": "(a) Is Decrease in Enthalpy a Criterion for Spontaneity ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20903,54,541,'paragraph',NULL,'If we examine the phenomenon like flow of water down hill or fall of a stone on to the ground, we find that there is a net decrease in potential energy in the direction of change. By analogy, we may be tempted to state that a chemical reaction is spontaneous in a given direction, because decrease in energy has taken place, as in the case of exothermic reactions. For example:','[{"s": "If we examine the phenomenon like flow of water down hill or fall of a stone on to the ground, we find that there is a net decrease in potential energy in the direction of change. By analogy, we may be tempted to state that a chemical reaction is spontaneous in a given direction, because decrease in energy has taken place, as in the case of exothermic reactions. For example:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20904,54,542,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermodynamics-image62.png N 2 (g) + Thermodynamics-image51.png H 2 (g) = NH 3 (g) ;','[{"s": "Thermodynamics-image62.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image51.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) = NH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(g) ;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
