(20954,54,592,'paragraph',NULL,'One decides the spontaneity of a reaction by considering','[{"s": "One decides the spontaneity of a reaction by considering", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20955,54,593,'paragraph',NULL,'∆�����=-∆��⊝��� ����tan� ��������=--1648×103 � ���-1298 �=5530 ��-1 ���-1','[{"s": "∆�����=-∆��⊝��� ����tan� ��������=--1648×103 � ���-1298 �=5530 ��-1 ���-1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20956,54,594,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, total entropy change for this reaction','[{"s": "Thus, total entropy change for this reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20957,54,595,'paragraph',NULL,'∆rStotal=5530 JK-1mol-1+-549.4 JK-1mol-1 =4980.6 JK-1mol-1','[{"s": "∆rStotal=5530 JK-1mol-1+-549.4 JK-1mol-1 =4980.6 JK-1mol-1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20958,54,596,'paragraph',NULL,'This shows that the above reaction is spontaneous.','[{"s": "This shows that the above reaction is spontaneous.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20959,54,597,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Gibbs Energy and Spontaneity','[{"s": "(c)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gibbs Energy and Spontaneity", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60903"]',NULL),
(20960,54,598,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen that for a system, it is the total entropy change, ∆S total which decides the spontaneity of the process. But most of the chemical reactions fall into the category of either closed systems or open systems. Therefore, for most of the chemical reactions there are changes in both enthalpy and entropy. It is clear from the discussion in previous sections that neither decrease in enthalpy nor increase in entropy alone can determine the direction of spontaneous change for these systems.','[{"s": "We have seen that for a system, it is the total entropy change, ∆S", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "total", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "which decides the spontaneity of the process. But most of the chemical reactions fall into the category of either closed systems or open systems. Therefore, for most of the chemical reactions there are changes in both enthalpy and entropy. It is clear from the discussion in previous sections that neither decrease in enthalpy nor increase in entropy alone can determine the direction of spontaneous change for these systems.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60903"]',NULL),
(20961,54,599,'paragraph',NULL,'For this purpose, we define a new thermodynamic function the Gibbs energy or Gibbs function, G, as','[{"s": "For this purpose, we define a new thermodynamic function the Gibbs energy or Gibbs function, G, as", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60903", "60936"]',NULL),
(20962,54,600,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 11-50-53',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image1.png'),
(20963,54,601,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, Gibbs energy change = enthalpy change – temperature × entropy change, and is referred to as the Gibbs equation, one of the most important equations in chemistry. Here, we have considered both terms together for spontaneity: energy (in terms of ∆H) and entropy (∆S, a measure of disorder) as indicated earlier. Dimensionally if we analyse, we find that ∆G has units of energy because, both ∆H and the T∆S are energy terms, since T∆S = (K) (J/K) = J .','[{"s": "Thus, Gibbs energy change = enthalpy change – temperature × entropy change, and is referred to as the Gibbs equation, one of the most important equations in chemistry. Here, we have considered both terms together for spontaneity: energy (in terms of ∆H) and entropy (∆S, a measure of disorder) as indicated earlier. Dimensionally if we analyse, we find that ∆G has units of energy because, both ∆H and the T∆S are energy terms, since T∆S = (K) (J/K) = J", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60903", "60904", "60918", "60935"]',NULL),
(20964,54,602,'paragraph',NULL,'Now let us consider how ∆G is related to reaction spontaneity.','[{"s": "Now let us consider how ∆G is related to reaction spontaneity.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60903"]',NULL),
(20965,54,603,'paragraph',NULL,'We know,','[{"s": "We know,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60933"]',NULL),
(20966,54,604,'paragraph',NULL,'∆S total = ∆S sys + ∆S surr','[{"s": "∆S", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "total", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "= ∆S", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "sys", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+ ∆S", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "surr", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60933"]',NULL),
(20967,54,605,'paragraph',NULL,'If the system is in thermal equilibrium with the surrounding, then the temperature of the surrounding is same as that of the system. Also, increase in enthalpy of the surrounding is equal to decrease in the enthalpy of the system.','[{"s": "If the system is in thermal equilibrium with the surrounding, then the temperature of the surrounding is same as that of the system. Also, increase in enthalpy of the surrounding is equal to decrease in the enthalpy of the system.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60933"]',NULL),
(20968,54,606,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, entropy change of surroundings,','[{"s": "Therefore, entropy change of surroundings,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60933"]',NULL),
(20969,54,607,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 12-03-09',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image53.png'),
(20970,54,608,'paragraph',NULL,'∆H sys is the enthalpy change of a reaction, T∆S sys is the energy which is not available to do useful work. So ∆G is the net energy available to do useful work and is thus a measure of the ‘free energy’. For this reason, it is also known as the free energy of the reaction.','[{"s": "∆H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sys", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the enthalpy change of a reaction, T∆S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sys", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the energy which is not available to do useful work. So ∆G is the net energy available to do useful work and is thus a measure of the ‘free energy’. For this reason, it is also known as the free energy of the reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20971,54,609,'paragraph',NULL,'∆G gives a criteria for spontaneity at constant pressure and temperature.','[{"s": "∆G gives a criteria for spontaneity at constant pressure and temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20972,54,610,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) If ∆G is negative (< 0), the process is spontaneous.','[{"s": "(i) If ∆G is negative (< 0), the process is spontaneous.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20973,54,611,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) If ∆G is positive (> 0), the process is non spontaneous.','[{"s": "(ii) If ∆G is positive (> 0), the process is non spontaneous.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20974,54,612,'paragraph',NULL,'Note : If a reaction has a positive enthalpy change and positive entropy change, it can be spontaneous when T∆S is large enough to outweigh ∆H. This can happen in two ways; (a) The positive entropy change of the system can be ‘small’ in which case T must be large. (b) The positive entropy change of the system can be ’large’, in which case T may be small. The former is one of the reasons why reactions are often carried out at high temperature. Table 6.4 summarises the effect of temperature on spontaneity of reactions.','[{"s": "Note : If a reaction has a positive enthalpy change and positive entropy change, it can be spontaneous when T∆S is large enough to outweigh ∆H. This can happen in two ways; (a) The positive entropy change of the system can be ‘small’ in which case T must be large. (b) The positive entropy change of the system can be ’large’, in which case T may be small. The former is one of the reasons why reactions are often carried out at high temperature.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Table 6.4 summarises the effect of temperature on spontaneity of reactions.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60934"]',NULL),
(20975,54,613,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Entropy and Second Law of Thermodynamics','[{"s": "(d) Entropy and Second Law of Thermodynamics", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60933"]',NULL),
(20976,54,614,'paragraph',NULL,'We know that for an isolated system the change in energy remains constant. Therefore, increase in entropy in such systems is the natural direction of a spontaneous change. This, in fact is the second law of thermodynamics. Like first law of thermodynamics, second law can also be stated in several ways. The second law of thermodynamics explains why spontaneous exothermic reactions are so common. In exothermic reactions heat released by the reaction increases the disorder of the surroundings and overall entropy change is positive which makes the reaction spontaneous .','[{"s": "We know that for an isolated system the change in energy remains constant. Therefore, increase in entropy in such systems is the natural direction of a spontaneous change. This, in fact is the second law of thermodynamics. Like first law of thermodynamics, second law can also be stated in several ways. The second law of thermodynamics explains why spontaneous exothermic reactions are so common. In exothermic reactions heat released by the reaction increases the disorder of the surroundings and overall entropy change is positive which makes the reaction spontaneous", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["60933"]',NULL),
(20977,54,615,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) Absolute Entropy and Third Law of Thermodynamics','[{"s": "(e) Absolute Entropy and Third Law of Thermodynamics", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["60932"]',NULL),
(20978,54,616,'paragraph',NULL,'Molecules of a substance may move in a straight line in any direction, they may spin like a top and the bonds in the molecules may stretch and compress. These motions of the molecule are called translational, rotational and vibrational motion respectively. When temperature of the system rises, these motions become more vigorous and entropy increases. On the other hand when temperature is lowered, the entropy decreases. The entropy of any pure crystalline substance approaches zero as the temperature approaches absolute zero. This is called third law of thermodynamics. This is so because there is perfect order in a crystal at absolute zero. The statement is confined to pure crystalline solids because theoretical arguments and practical evidences have shown that entropy of solutions and super cooled liquids is not zero at 0 K. The importance of the third law lies in the fact that it permits the calculation of absolute values of entropy of pure substance from thermal data alone. For a pure substance, this can be done by summing Thermodynamics-image65.png increments from 0 K to 298 K . Standard entropies can be used to calculate standard entropy changes by a Hess’s law type of calculation.','[{"s": "Molecules of a substance may move in a straight line in any direction, they may spin like a top and the bonds in the molecules may stretch and compress. These motions of the molecule are called translational, rotational and vibrational motion respectively. When temperature of the system rises, these motions become more vigorous and entropy increases. On the other hand when temperature is lowered, the entropy decreases. The entropy of any pure crystalline substance approaches zero as the temperature approaches absolute zero. This is called third law of thermodynamics. This is so because there is perfect order in a crystal at absolute zero. The statement is confined to pure crystalline solids because theoretical arguments and practical evidences have shown that entropy of solutions and super cooled liquids is not zero at 0 K. The importance of the third law lies in the fact that it permits the calculation of absolute values of entropy of pure substance from thermal data alone. For a pure substance, this can be done by summing", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image65.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "increments from 0 K to 298 K", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Standard entropies can be used to calculate standard entropy changes by a Hess’s law type of calculation.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["60932"]',NULL),
(20979,54,617,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen how a knowledge of the sign and magnitude of the free energy change of a chemical reaction allows:','[{"s": "We have seen how a knowledge of the sign and magnitude of the free energy change of a chemical reaction allows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20980,54,618,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Prediction of the spontaneity of the chemical reaction.','[{"s": "(i) Prediction of the spontaneity of the chemical reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20981,54,619,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Prediction of the useful work that could be extracted from it.','[{"s": "(ii) Prediction of the useful work that could be extracted from it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20982,54,620,'paragraph',NULL,'So far we have considered free energy changes in irreversible reactions. Let us now examine the free energy changes in reversible reactions.','[{"s": "So far we have considered free energy changes in irreversible reactions. Let us now examine the free energy changes in reversible reactions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20983,54,621,'paragraph',NULL,'‘Reversible’ under strict thermodynamic sense is a special way of carrying out a process such that system is at all times in perfect equilibrium with its surroundings. When applied to a chemical reaction, the term ‘reversible’ indicates that a given reaction can proceed in either direction simultaneously, so that a dynamic equilibrium is set up. This means that the reactions in both the directions should proceed with a decrease in free energy, which seems impossible. It is possible only if at equilibrium the free energy of the system is minimum. If it is not, the system would spontaneously change to configuration of lower free energy.','[{"s": "‘Reversible’ under strict thermodynamic sense is a special way of carrying out a process such that system is at all times in perfect equilibrium with its surroundings. When applied to a chemical reaction, the term ‘reversible’ indicates that a given reaction can proceed in either direction simultaneously, so that a dynamic equilibrium is set up. This means that the reactions in both the directions should proceed with a decrease in free energy, which seems impossible. It is possible only if at equilibrium the free energy of the system is minimum. If it is not, the system would spontaneously change to configuration of lower free energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20984,54,622,'paragraph',NULL,'So, the criterion for equilibrium','[{"s": "So, the criterion for equilibrium", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20985,54,623,'paragraph',NULL,'A + B ⇋ C + D ; is','[{"s": "A + B ⇋ C + D ; is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20986,54,624,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r G = 0','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20987,54,625,'paragraph',NULL,'Gibbs energy for a reaction in which all reactants and products are in standard state, ∆ r G ⊖ is related to the equilibrium constant of the reaction as follows:','[{"s": "Gibbs energy for a reaction in which all reactants and products are in standard state, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is related to the equilibrium constant of the reaction as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(20988,54,626,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 10-49-25',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image74.png'),
(20989,54,627,'paragraph',NULL,'For strongly endothermic reactions, the value of ∆ r H ⊖ r H ⊖ is large and negative, and ∆ r G ⊖ is likely to be large and negative too. In such cases, K will be much larger than 1. We may expect strongly exothermic reactions to have a large K, and hence can go to near completion. ∆ r G ⊖ also depends upon ∆ r S ⊖ , if the changes in the entropy of reaction is also taken into account, the value of K or extent of chemical reaction will also be affected, depending upon whether ∆ r S ⊖ is positive or negative.','[{"s": "For strongly endothermic reactions, the value of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is large and negative, and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is likely to be large and negative too. In such cases, K will be much larger than 1. We may expect strongly exothermic reactions to have a large K, and hence can go to near completion. ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "also depends upon ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", if the changes in the entropy of reaction is also taken into account, the value of K or extent of chemical reaction will also be affected, depending upon whether ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "⊖", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is positive or negative.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
