(21015,54,653,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 11-04-41',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image26.png'),
(21016,54,654,'heading',3,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21017,54,655,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermodynamics deals with energy changes in chemical or physical processes and enables us to study these changes quantitatively and to make useful predictions. For these purposes, we divide the universe into the system and the surroundings. Chemical or physical processes lead to evolution or absorption of heat (q), part of which may be converted into work (w). These quantities are related through the first law of thermodynamics via ∆U = q + w. ∆U, change in internal energy, depends on initial and final states only and is a state function, whereas q and w depend on the path and are not the state functions. We follow sign conventions of q and w by giving the positive sign to these quantities when these are added to the system. We can measure the transfer of heat from one system to another which causes the change in temperature. The magnitude of rise in temperature depends on the heat capacity (C) of a substance. Therefore, heat absorbed or evolved is q = C∆T. Work can be measured by w = –p ex ∆V, in case of expansion of gases . Under reversible process, we can put p ex = p for infinitesimal changes in the volume making w rev = – p dV. In this condition, we can use gas equation, pV = nRT.','[{"s": "Thermodynamics deals with energy changes in chemical or physical processes and enables us to study these changes quantitatively and to make useful predictions. For these purposes, we divide the universe into the system and the surroundings.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Chemical or physical processes lead to evolution or absorption of heat (q), part of which may be converted into work (w). These quantities are related through the first law of thermodynamics via ∆U = q + w. ∆U, change in internal energy, depends on initial and final states only and is a state function, whereas q and w depend on the path and are not the state functions. We follow sign conventions of q and w by giving the positive sign to these quantities when these are added to the system. We can measure the transfer of heat from one system to another which causes the change in temperature. The magnitude of rise in temperature depends on the heat capacity (C) of a substance.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Therefore, heat absorbed or evolved is q = C∆T.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Work can be measured by w = –p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "∆V, in case of expansion of gases", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Under reversible process, we can put p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ex", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for infinitesimal changes in the volume making w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rev", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – p dV. In this condition, we can use gas equation, pV = nRT.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["60906", "60907"]',NULL),
(21018,54,656,'paragraph',NULL,'At constant volume, w = 0, then ∆U = q V , heat transfer at constant volume. But in study of chemical reactions, we usually have constant pressure. We define another state function enthalpy. Enthalpy change, ∆H = ∆U + ∆n g RT, can be found directly from the heat changes at constant pressure, ∆H = q p .','[{"s": "At constant volume, w = 0, then ∆U = q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", heat transfer at constant volume. But in study of chemical reactions, we usually have constant pressure. We define another state function enthalpy. Enthalpy change, ∆H = ∆U + ∆n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RT, can be found directly from the heat changes at constant pressure, ∆H = q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21019,54,657,'paragraph',NULL,'There are varieties of enthalpy changes. Changes of phase such as melting, vaporization and sublimation usually occur at constant temperature and can be characterized by enthalpy changes which are always positive. Enthalpy of formation, combustion and other enthalpy changes can be calculated using Hess’s law. Enthalpy change for chemical reactions can be determined by','[{"s": "There are varieties of enthalpy changes. Changes of phase such as melting, vaporization and sublimation usually occur at constant temperature and can be characterized by enthalpy changes which are always positive. Enthalpy of formation, combustion and other enthalpy changes can be calculated using Hess’s law. Enthalpy change for chemical reactions can be determined by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21020,54,658,'image',NULL,'Screenshot from 2019-06-05 10-41-51',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/chemistry/images/Thermodynamics-image67.png'),
(21021,54,659,'paragraph',NULL,'and in gaseous state by','[{"s": "and in gaseous state by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21022,54,660,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r H0 = Σ bond enthalpies of the reactants – Σ bond enthalpies of the products','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H0 = Σ bond enthalpies of the reactants – Σ bond enthalpies of the products", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21023,54,661,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermodynamics-image13.png for a reversible process. Thermodynamics-image13.png is independent of path.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "for a reversible process.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is independent of path.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21024,54,662,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemical reactions are generally carried at constant pressure, so we define another state function Gibbs energy, G, which is related to entropy and enthalpy changes of the system by the equation:','[{"s": "Chemical reactions are generally carried at constant pressure, so we define another state function Gibbs energy, G, which is related to entropy and enthalpy changes of the system by the equation:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21025,54,663,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r G = ∆ r H – T ∆ r S','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G = ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H – T ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21026,54,664,'paragraph',NULL,'For a spontaneous change, ∆G sys < 0 and at equilibrium, ∆G sys = 0.','[{"s": "For a spontaneous change, ∆G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sys", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< 0 and at equilibrium, ∆G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sys", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21027,54,665,'paragraph',NULL,'Standard Gibbs energy change is related to equilibrium constant by','[{"s": "Standard Gibbs energy change is related to equilibrium constant by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21028,54,666,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ r G0 = – RT ln K.','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G0 = – RT ln K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21029,54,667,'paragraph',NULL,'K can be calculated from this equation, if we know ∆ r G0 which can be found from Thermodynamics-image4.png . Temperature is an important factor in the equation. Many reactions which are non-spontaneous at low temperature, are made spontaneous at high temperature for systems having positive entropy of reaction.','[{"s": "K can be calculated from this equation, if we know ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G0 which can be found from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermodynamics-image4.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screenshot from 2019-06-05 10-43-27"}, {"s": ". Temperature is an important factor in the equation. Many reactions which are non-spontaneous at low temperature, are made spontaneous at high temperature for systems having positive entropy of reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21030,55,0,'heading',2,'Chapter Seven',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21031,55,1,'heading',1,'Alternating Current',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21032,55,2,'heading',3,'7.1 INTRODUCTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21034,55,4,'paragraph',NULL,'* The phrases ac voltage and ac current are contradictory and redundant, respectively, since they mean, literally, alternating current voltage and alternating current current. Still, the abbreviation ac to designate an electrical quantity displaying simple harmonic time dependance has become so universally accepted that we follow others in its use. Further, voltage – another phrase commonly used means potential difference between two points.','[{"s": "* The phrases ac voltage and ac current are contradictory and redundant, respectively, since they mean, literally, alternating current voltage and alternating current current. Still, the abbreviation ac to designate an electrical quantity displaying simple harmonic time dependance has become so universally accepted that we follow others in its use. Further, voltage – another phrase commonly used means potential difference between two points.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21035,55,5,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image25.png'),
(21036,55,6,'paragraph',NULL,'Nicola Tesla (1856 – 1943) Serbian-American scientist, inventor and genius. He conceived the idea of the rotating magnetic field, which is the basis of practically all alternating current machinery, and which helped usher in the age of electric power. He also invented among other things the induction motor, the polyphase system of ac power, and the high frequency induction coil (the Tesla coil) used in radio and television sets and other electronic equipment. The SI unit of magnetic field is named in his honour.','[{"s": "Nicola Tesla (1856 – 1943) Serbian-American scientist, inventor and genius. He conceived the idea of the rotating magnetic field, which is the basis of practically all alternating current machinery, and which helped usher in the age of electric power. He also invented among other things the induction motor, the polyphase system of ac power, and the high frequency induction coil (the Tesla coil) used in radio and television sets and other electronic equipment. The SI unit of magnetic field is named in his honour.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21037,55,7,'heading',3,'7.2 AC VOLTAGE APPLIED TO A RESISTOR',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21038,55,8,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.1 shows a resistor connected to a source ε of ac voltage. The symbol for an ac source in a circuit diagram is Alternating_Current-image47.png . We consider a source which produces sinusoidally varying potential difference across its terminals. Let this potential difference, also called ac voltage, be given by','[{"s": "Figure 7.1 shows a resistor connected to a source ε of ac voltage. The symbol for an ac source in a circuit diagram is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alternating_Current-image47.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". We consider a source which produces sinusoidally varying potential difference across its terminals. Let this potential difference, also called ac voltage, be given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21039,55,9,'paragraph',NULL,'V = Vm sin ωt (7.1)','[{"s": "V = Vm sin ωt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21040,55,10,'paragraph',NULL,'where v m is the amplitude of the oscillating potential difference and ω is its angular frequency.','[{"s": "where v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the amplitude of the oscillating potential difference and ω is its angular frequency.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21041,55,11,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image139.png'),
(21042,55,12,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.1 AC voltage applied to a resistor.','[{"s": "Figure 7.1 AC voltage applied to a resistor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21043,55,13,'paragraph',NULL,'To find the value of current through the resistor, we apply Kirchhoff’s loop rule','[{"s": "To find the value of current through the resistor, we apply Kirchhoff’s loop rule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21044,55,14,'paragraph',NULL,'∑ε(t)=0','[{"s": "∑ε(t)=0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21045,55,15,'paragraph',NULL,'(refer to Section 3.13), to the circuit shown in Fig. 7.1 to get','[{"s": "(refer to Section 3.13), to the circuit shown in Fig. 7.1 to get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21046,55,16,'paragraph',NULL,'V m sin ωt = iR','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin ωt = iR", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21047,55,17,'paragraph',NULL,'or','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21048,55,18,'paragraph',NULL,'i = V m R sin ωt','[{"s": "i = V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R sin ωt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21049,55,19,'paragraph',NULL,'Since R is a constant, we can write this equation as','[{"s": "Since R is a constant, we can write this equation as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21050,55,20,'paragraph',NULL,'i = i m sin ωt','[{"s": "i = i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin ωt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21051,55,21,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.2)','[{"s": "(7.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21052,55,22,'paragraph',NULL,'where the current amplitude im is given by','[{"s": "where the current amplitude im is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21053,55,23,'paragraph',NULL,'i m = V m /R','[{"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/R", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21054,55,24,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.3)','[{"s": "(7.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21055,55,25,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image11.png'),
(21056,55,26,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.2 In a pure resistor, the voltage and current are in phase. The minima, zero and maxima occur at the same respective times.','[{"s": "Figure 7.2 In a pure resistor, the voltage and current are in phase. The minima, zero and maxima occur at the same respective times.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21057,55,27,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (7.3) is Ohm’s law, which for resistors, works equally well for both ac and dc voltages. The voltage across a pure resistor and the current through it, given by Eqs. (7.1) and (7.2) are plotted as a function of time in Fig. 7.2. Note, in particular that both v and i reach zero, minimum and maximum values at the same time. Clearly, the voltage and current are in phase with each other.','[{"s": "Equation (7.3) is Ohm’s law, which for resistors, works equally well for both ac and dc voltages. The voltage across a pure resistor and the current through it, given by Eqs. (7.1) and (7.2) are plotted as a function of time in Fig. 7.2. Note, in particular that both v and i reach zero, minimum and maximum values at the same time. Clearly, the voltage and current are in phase with each other.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21058,55,28,'paragraph',NULL,'We see that, like the applied voltage, the current varies sinusoidally and has corresponding positive and negative values during each cycle. Thus, the sum of the instantaneous current values over one complete cycle is zero, and the average current is zero. The fact that the average current is zero, however, does not mean that the average power consumed is zero and that there is no dissipation of electrical energy. As you know, Joule heating is given by i2R and depends on i2 (which is always positive whether i is positive or negative) and not on i. Thus, there is Joule heating and dissipation of electrical energy when an ac current passes through a resistor.','[{"s": "We see that, like the applied voltage, the current varies sinusoidally and has corresponding positive and negative values during each cycle. Thus, the sum of the instantaneous current values over one complete cycle is zero, and the average current is zero. The fact that the average current is zero, however, does not mean that the average power consumed is zero and that there is no dissipation of electrical energy. As you know, Joule heating is given by i2R and depends on i2 (which is always positive whether i is positive or negative) and not on i. Thus, there is Joule heating and dissipation of electrical energy when an ac current passes through a resistor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21059,55,29,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image1.png'),
(21060,55,30,'paragraph',NULL,'The instantaneous power dissipated in the resistor is-','[{"s": "The instantaneous power dissipated in the resistor is-", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21061,55,31,'paragraph',NULL,'P = i 2 R = i m 2 sin 2 ωt R (7.4)','[{"s": "P = i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R = i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ωt R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21063,55,33,'paragraph',NULL,'P¯ = i 2 R = i m 2 sin 2 ωt R [7.5(a)]','[{"s": "P¯ = i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R = i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ωt R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[7.5(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21064,55,34,'paragraph',NULL,'Since, i 2m and R are constants,','[{"s": "Since, i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and R are constants,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21065,55,35,'paragraph',NULL,'P = i m 2 R < sin 2 ωt> [7.5(b)]','[{"s": "P = i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ωt>", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[7.5(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21066,55,36,'paragraph',NULL,'Using the trigonometric identity, sin 2 ωt = 1/2 (1– cos 2ωt), we have < sin 2 ωt > = (1/2) (1– < cos 2ωt >) and since < cos2ωt > = 0**, we have,','[{"s": "Using the trigonometric identity, sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ωt = 1/2 (1– cos 2ωt), we have < sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ωt > = (1/2) (1– < cos 2ωt >) and since < cos2ωt > = 0**, we have,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21067,55,37,'paragraph',NULL,'<sin2 ωt> = 1/2','[{"s": "<sin2 ωt> = 1/2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21068,55,38,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus,','[{"s": "Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21069,55,39,'paragraph',NULL,'p = 1/2i m 2 R [7.5(c)]','[{"s": "p = 1/2i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[7.5(c)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21070,55,40,'paragraph',NULL,'To express ac power in the same form as dc power (P = I 2 R), a special value of current is defined and used. It is called, root mean square (rms) or effective current (Fig. 7.3) and is denoted by I rms or I.','[{"s": "To express ac power in the same form as dc power (P = I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R), a special value of current is defined and used. It is called, root mean square (rms) or effective current (Fig. 7.3) and is denoted by I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or I.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21071,55,41,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image16.png'),
(21072,55,42,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.3 The rms current I is related to the peak current im by I = i m/ √2','[{"s": "Figure 7.3 The rms current I is related to the peak current im by I =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "√2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21073,55,43,'paragraph',NULL,'* The average value of a function F (t) over a period T is given by','[{"s": "*", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The average value of a function F (t) over a period T is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21074,55,44,'paragraph',NULL,'< F(t)> = 1/T ∫ 0 T F(t)dt','[{"s": "<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F(t)> = 1/T ∫", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F(t)dt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21075,55,45,'paragraph',NULL,'** <cos 2ωt> = 1/T∫ 0 T cos 2ωtdt = 1/T[sin 2ωt2ω] 0 T = 1/2ωT[sin 2ωt - 0] = 0','[{"s": "**", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<cos 2ωt> = 1/T∫", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos 2ωtdt = 1/T[sin 2ωt2ω]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1/2ωT[sin 2ωt - 0] = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21077,55,47,'paragraph',NULL,'I= √i 2 = √ 12 i 2m = i m √2 = 0.707 i m (7.6)','[{"s": "I= √i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= √", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "√2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.707 i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21078,55,48,'paragraph',NULL,'In terms of I, the average power, denoted by P is','[{"s": "In terms of I, the average power, denoted by P is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21079,55,49,'paragraph',NULL,'P = P = 12im2R = I2R (7.7)','[{"s": "P =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 12im2R = I2R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21080,55,50,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, we define the rms voltage or effective voltage by','[{"s": "Similarly, we define the rms voltage or effective voltage by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21081,55,51,'paragraph',NULL,'V = v m √2 = 0.707 v m (7.8)','[{"s": "V =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "√2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.707 v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21082,55,52,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eq. (7.3), we have','[{"s": "From Eq. (7.3), we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21083,55,53,'paragraph',NULL,'v m = i m R','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21084,55,54,'paragraph',NULL,'or, v m √2 = i m √2 R','[{"s": "or,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "√2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "√2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21085,55,55,'paragraph',NULL,'or, V = IR (7.9)','[{"s": "or, V = IR (7.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21088,55,58,'paragraph',NULL,'v m =√2V = (1.414)(220 V) = 311 V','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=√2V = (1.414)(220 V) = 311 V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(21089,55,59,'paragraph',NULL,'In fact, the I or rms current is the equivalent dc current that would produce the same average power loss as the alternating current. Equation (7.7) can also be written as','[{"s": "In fact, the I or rms current is the equivalent dc current that would produce the same average power loss as the alternating current. Equation (7.7) can also be written as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21090,55,60,'paragraph',NULL,'P = V 2 / R = I V (since V = I R)','[{"s": "P = V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ R = I V (since V = I R)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21092,55,62,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21093,55,63,'paragraph',NULL,'R = V 2 P = (220 V) 2 100 W = 484 Ω','[{"s": "R =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(220 V)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "100 W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 484 Ω", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21094,55,64,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The peak voltage of the source is','[{"s": "(b) The peak voltage of the source is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21095,55,65,'paragraph',NULL,'V m = √2V = 311 V','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= √2V = 311 V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21096,55,66,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Since, P = I V','[{"s": "(c) Since, P = I V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21097,55,67,'paragraph',NULL,'I = PV = 100 W/220 V = 0.454 A','[{"s": "I =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "100 W/220 V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.454 A", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21098,55,68,'heading',3,'7.3 REPRESENTATION OF AC CURRENT AND VOLTAGE BY ROTATING VECTORS — PHASORS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21099,55,69,'paragraph',NULL,'In the previous section, we learnt that the current through a resistor is in phase with the ac voltage. But this is not so in the case of an inductor, a capacitor or a combination of these circuit elements. In order to show phase relationship between voltage and current in an ac circuit, we use the notion of phasors. The analysis of an ac circuit is facilitated by the use of a phasor diagram. A phasor* is a vector which rotates about the origin with angular speed ω, as shown in Fig. 7.4. The vertical components of phasors V and I represent the sinusoidally varying quantities v and i. The magnitudes of phasors V and I represent the amplitudes or the peak values v m and i m of these oscillating quantities. Figure 7.4(a) shows the voltage and current phasors and their relationship at time t 1 for the case of an ac source connected to a resistor i.e., corresponding to the circuit shown in Fig. 7.1. The projection of voltage and current phasors on vertical axis, i.e., v m sinωt and i m sinωt, respectively represent the value of voltage and current at that instant. As they rotate with frequency ω, curves in Fig. 7.4(b) are generated.','[{"s": "In the previous section, we learnt that the current through a resistor is in phase with the ac voltage. But this is not so in the case of an inductor, a capacitor or a combination of these circuit elements. In order to show phase relationship between voltage and current in an ac circuit, we use the notion of phasors. The analysis of an ac circuit is facilitated by the use of a phasor diagram. A phasor* is a vector which rotates about the origin with angular speed ω, as shown in Fig. 7.4. The vertical components of phasors V and I represent the sinusoidally varying quantities v and i. The magnitudes of phasors V and I represent the amplitudes or the peak values v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of these oscillating quantities. Figure 7.4(a) shows the voltage and current phasors and their relationship at time t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for the case of an ac source connected to a resistor i.e., corresponding to the circuit shown in Fig. 7.1. The projection of voltage and current phasors on vertical axis, i.e., v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sinωt and i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sinωt, respectively represent the value of voltage and current at that instant. As they rotate with frequency ω, curves in Fig. 7.4(b) are generated.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21100,55,70,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image107.png'),
(21101,55,71,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.4 (a) A phasor diagram for the circuit in Fig 7.1. (b) Graph of v and i versus ωt.','[{"s": "Figure 7.4 (a) A phasor diagram for the circuit in Fig 7.1. (b) Graph of v and i versus ωt.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21102,55,72,'paragraph',NULL,'From Fig. 7.4(a) we see that phasors V and I for the case of a resistor are in the same direction. This is so for all times. This means that the phase angle between the voltage and the current is zero.','[{"s": "From Fig. 7.4(a) we see that phasors V and I for the case of a resistor are in the same direction. This is so for all times. This means that the phase angle between the voltage and the current is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21103,55,73,'paragraph',NULL,'* Though voltage and current in ac circuit are represented by phasors – rotating vectors, they are not vectors themselves. They are scalar quantities. It so happens that the amplitudes and phases of harmonically varying scalars combine mathematically in the same way as do the projections of rotating vectors of corresponding magnitudes and directions. The rotating vectors that represent harmonically varying scalar quantities are introduced only to provide us with a simple way of adding these quantities using a rule that we already know.','[{"s": "* Though voltage and current in ac circuit are represented by phasors – rotating vectors, they are not vectors themselves. They are scalar quantities. It so happens that the amplitudes and phases of harmonically varying scalars combine mathematically in the same way as do the projections of rotating vectors of corresponding magnitudes and directions. The rotating vectors that represent harmonically varying scalar quantities are introduced only to provide us with a simple way of adding these quantities using a rule that we already know.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21104,55,74,'heading',3,'7.4 AC VOLTAGE APPLIED TO AN INDUCTOR',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21105,55,75,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.5 shows an ac source connected to an inductor.','[{"s": "Figure 7.5 shows an ac source connected to an inductor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21106,55,76,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image143.png'),
(21107,55,77,'paragraph',NULL,'Usually, inductors have appreciable resistance in their windings, but we shall assume that this inductor has negligible resistance. Thus, the circuit is a purely inductive ac circuit. Let the voltage across the source be v = v m sin ωt. Using the Kirchhoff’s loop rule, ∑ε (t) = 0 , and since there is no resistor in the circuit,','[{"s": "Usually, inductors have appreciable resistance in their windings, but we shall assume that this inductor has negligible resistance. Thus, the circuit is a purely inductive ac circuit. Let the voltage across the source be v = v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin ωt. Using the Kirchhoff’s loop rule, ∑ε (t) = 0 , and since there is no resistor in the circuit,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21108,55,78,'paragraph',NULL,'v - Ldi/dt = 0 (7.10)','[{"s": "v - Ldi/dt = 0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21109,55,79,'paragraph',NULL,'where the second term is the self-induced Faraday emf in the inductor; and L is the self-inductance of','[{"s": "where the second term is the self-induced Faraday emf in the inductor; and L is the self-inductance of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21110,55,80,'paragraph',NULL,'di/dt = v/L = v m /L sin ωt (7.11)','[{"s": "di/dt = v/L = v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/L sin ωt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33781,78,342,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) If the length and breadth of a thin rectangular sheet are measured, using a metre scale as 16.2 cm and, 10.1 cm respectively, there are three significant figures in each measurement. It means that the length l may be written as','[{"s": "(1) If the length and breadth of a thin rectangular sheet are measured, using a metre scale as 16.2 cm and, 10.1 cm respectively, there are three significant figures in each measurement. It means that the length", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "may be written as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33782,78,343,'paragraph',NULL,'l = 16.2 ± 0.1 cm','[{"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 16.2 ± 0.1 cm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21112,55,82,'paragraph',NULL,'∫di/dt dt = v m /L∫sin ωt dt','[{"s": "∫di/dt dt = v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/L∫sin ωt dt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21113,55,83,'paragraph',NULL,'and get,','[{"s": "and get,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21114,55,84,'paragraph',NULL,'i = -v m /ωLcos(ωt) + constant','[{"s": "i = -v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ωLcos(ωt) + constant", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21115,55,85,'paragraph',NULL,'The integration constant has the dimension of current and is time-independent. Since the source has an emf which oscillates symmetrically about zero, the current it sustains also oscillates symmetrically about zero, so that no constant or time-independent component of the current exists. Therefore, the integration constant is zero.','[{"s": "The integration constant has the dimension of current and is time-independent. Since the source has an emf which oscillates symmetrically about zero, the current it sustains also oscillates symmetrically about zero, so that no constant or time-independent component of the current exists. Therefore, the integration constant is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21116,55,86,'paragraph',NULL,'Using','[{"s": "Using", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21117,55,87,'paragraph',NULL,'- cos (ωt) = (sin ωt - π/2) , we have','[{"s": "- cos (ωt) = (sin ωt - π/2)", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21118,55,88,'paragraph',NULL,'i = i m sin (ωt - π/2) (7.12)','[{"s": "i = i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin (ωt - π/2)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21119,55,89,'paragraph',NULL,'where i m = v m /ωL is the amplitude of the current.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ωL", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the amplitude of the current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21120,55,90,'paragraph',NULL,'The quantity ω L is analogous to the resistance and is called inductive reactance, denoted by X L :','[{"s": "The quantity ω L is analogous to the resistance and is called inductive reactance, denoted by X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21121,55,91,'paragraph',NULL,'X L = ω L (7.13)','[{"s": "X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ω L (7.13)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21122,55,92,'paragraph',NULL,'The amplitude of the current is, then','[{"s": "The amplitude of the current is, then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21123,55,93,'paragraph',NULL,'i m = v m /X L (7.14)','[{"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.14)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21124,55,94,'paragraph',NULL,'The dimension of inductive reactance is the same as that of resistance and its SI unit is ohm (Ω). The inductive reactance limits the current in a purely inductive circuit in the same way as the resistance limits the current in a purely resistive circuit. The inductive reactance is directly proportional to the inductance and to the frequency of the current.','[{"s": "The dimension of inductive reactance is the same as that of resistance and its SI unit is ohm (Ω). The inductive reactance limits the current in a purely inductive circuit in the same way as the resistance limits the current in a purely resistive circuit. The inductive reactance is directly proportional to the inductance and to the frequency of the current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21125,55,95,'paragraph',NULL,'A comparison of Eqs. (7.1) and (7.12) for the source voltage and the current in an inductor shows that the current lags the voltage by π/2 or one-quarter (1/4) cycle. Figure 7.6 (a) shows the voltage and the current phasors in the present case at instant t 1 . The current phasor I is π/2 behind the voltage phasor V. When rotated with frequency ω counter-clockwise, they generate the voltage and current given by Eqs. (7.1) and (7.12), respectively and as shown in Fig. 7.6(b).','[{"s": "A comparison of Eqs. (7.1) and (7.12) for the source voltage and the current in an inductor shows that the current lags the voltage by π/2 or one-quarter (1/4) cycle. Figure 7.6 (a) shows the voltage and the current phasors in the present case at instant t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The current phasor I is π/2 behind the voltage phasor V. When rotated with frequency ω counter-clockwise, they generate the voltage and current given by Eqs. (7.1) and (7.12), respectively and as shown in Fig. 7.6(b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21126,55,96,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image108.png'),
(21127,55,97,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.6 (a) A Phasor diagram for the circuit in Fig. 7.5. (b) Graph of v and i versus ωt.','[{"s": "Figure 7.6 (a) A Phasor diagram for the circuit in Fig. 7.5. (b) Graph of v and i versus ωt.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21128,55,98,'paragraph',NULL,'We see that the current reaches its maximum value later than the voltage by one-fourth of a period [T/4=π/2/ω] . You have seen that an inductor has reactance that limits current similar to resistance in a dc circuit. Does it also consume power like a resistance? Let us try to find out.','[{"s": "We see that the current reaches its maximum value later than the voltage by one-fourth of a period", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[T/4=π/2/ω]", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". You have seen that an inductor has reactance that limits current similar to resistance in a dc circuit. Does it also consume power like a resistance? Let us try to find out.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21130,55,100,'paragraph',NULL,'p L = iv = i m sin (ωt - π/2)×v m sin (ωt)','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= iv = i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin (ωt - π/2)×v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin (ωt)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21131,55,101,'paragraph',NULL,'= - i m v m cos (ωt) sin (ωt)','[{"s": "= - i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos (ωt) sin (ωt)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21132,55,102,'paragraph',NULL,'= -i m v m /2 sin (2ωt)','[{"s": "= -i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2 sin (2ωt)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21133,55,103,'paragraph',NULL,'So, the average power over a complete cycle is','[{"s": "So, the average power over a complete cycle is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21134,55,104,'paragraph',NULL,'null','[{"s": "null", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21135,55,105,'paragraph',NULL,'since the average of sin (2ωt) over a complete cycle is zero.','[{"s": "since the average of sin (2ωt) over a complete cycle is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21136,55,106,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the average power supplied to an inductor over one complete cycle is zero.','[{"s": "Thus, the average power supplied to an inductor over one complete cycle is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21137,55,107,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.7 explains it in detail.','[{"s": "Figure 7.7 explains it in detail.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21138,55,108,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.2 A pure inductor of 25.0 mH is connected to a source of 220 V. Find the inductive reactance and rms current in the circuit if the frequency of the source is 50 Hz.','[{"s": "Example 7.2 A pure inductor of 25.0 mH is connected to a source of 220 V. Find the inductive reactance and rms current in the circuit if the frequency of the source is 50 Hz.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21139,55,109,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution The inductive reactance,','[{"s": "Solution The inductive reactance,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21140,55,110,'paragraph',NULL,'XL = 2πνL = 2×3.14×50×25×10-3 W','[{"s": "XL = 2πνL = 2×3.14×50×25×10-3 W", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21141,55,111,'paragraph',NULL,'= 7.85Ω','[{"s": "= 7.85Ω", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21142,55,112,'paragraph',NULL,'The rms current in the circuit is','[{"s": "The rms current in the circuit is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21143,55,113,'paragraph',NULL,'I = VXL = 220 V7.85 Ω = 28 A','[{"s": "I = VXL = 220 V7.85 Ω = 28 A", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21144,55,114,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image113.png'),
(21145,55,115,'paragraph',NULL,'One complete cycle of voltage/current. Note that the current lags the voltage.','[{"s": "One complete cycle of voltage/current. Note that the current lags the voltage.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21146,55,116,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternating_Current-image31.png 0-1 Current i through the coil entering at A increase from zero to a maximum value. Flux lines are set up i.e., the core gets magnetised. With the polarity shown voltage and current are both positive. So their product p is positive. ENERGY IS ABSORBED FROM THE SOURCE.','[{"s": "Alternating_Current-image31.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "0-1 Current i through the coil entering at A increase from zero to a maximum value. Flux lines are set up i.e., the core gets magnetised. With the polarity shown voltage and current are both positive. So their product p is positive. ENERGY IS ABSORBED FROM THE SOURCE.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21147,55,117,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image81.png'),
(21148,55,118,'paragraph',NULL,'1-2 Current in the coil is still positive but is decreasing. The core gets demagnetised and the net flux becomes zero at the end of a half cycle. The voltage v is negative (since di/dt is negative). The product of voltage and current is negative, and ENERGY IS BEING RETURNED TO SOURCE.','[{"s": "1-2 Current in the coil is still positive but is decreasing. The core gets demagnetised and the net flux becomes zero at the end of a half cycle. The voltage v is negative (since di/dt is negative). The product of voltage and current is negative, and ENERGY IS BEING RETURNED TO SOURCE.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21149,55,119,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image38.png'),
(21150,55,120,'paragraph',NULL,'2-3 Current i becomes negative i.e., it enters at B and comes out of A. Since the direction of current has changed, the polarity of the magnet changes. The current and voltage are both negative. So their product p is positive. ENERGY IS ABSORBED.','[{"s": "2-3 Current i becomes negative i.e., it enters at B and comes out of A. Since the direction of current has changed, the polarity of the magnet changes. The current and voltage are both negative. So their product p is positive. ENERGY IS ABSORBED.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21151,55,121,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image123.png'),
(21152,55,122,'paragraph',NULL,'3-4 Current i decreases and reaches its zero value at 4 when core is demagnetised and flux is zero. The voltage is positive but the current is negative. The power is, therefore, negative. ENERGY ABSORBED DURING THE CYCLE 2-3 IS RETURNED TO THE SOURCE.','[{"s": "3-4 Current i decreases and reaches its zero value at 4 when core is demagnetised and flux is zero. The voltage is positive but the current is negative. The power is, therefore, negative. ENERGY ABSORBED DURING THE CYCLE 2-3 IS RETURNED TO THE SOURCE.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21153,55,123,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.7 Magnetisation and demagnetisation of an inductor.','[{"s": "Figure 7.7 Magnetisation and demagnetisation of an inductor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21154,55,124,'heading',3,'7.5 AC VOLTAGE APPLIED TO A CAPACITOR',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21155,55,125,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.8 shows an ac source ε generating ac voltage v = v m sinωt connected to a capacitor only, a purely capacitive ac circuit.','[{"s": "Figure 7.8 shows an ac source ε generating ac voltage v = v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sinωt connected to a capacitor only, a purely capacitive ac circuit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21156,55,126,'paragraph',NULL,'When a capacitor is connected to a voltage source in a dc circuit, current will flow for the short time required to charge the capacitor. As charge accumulates on the capacitor plates, the voltage across them increases, opposing the current. That is, a capacitor in a dc circuit will limit or oppose the current as it charges. When the capacitor is fully charged, the current in the circuit falls to zero.','[{"s": "When a capacitor is connected to a voltage source in a dc circuit, current will flow for the short time required to charge the capacitor. As charge accumulates on the capacitor plates, the voltage across them increases, opposing the current. That is, a capacitor in a dc circuit will limit or oppose the current as it charges. When the capacitor is fully charged, the current in the circuit falls to zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33783,78,344,'paragraph',NULL,'= 16.2 cm ± 0.6 %.','[{"s": "= 16.2 cm ± 0.6 %.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21157,55,127,'paragraph',NULL,'When the capacitor is connected to an ac source, as in Fig. 7.8, it limits or regulates the current, but does not completely prevent the flow of charge. The capacitor is alternately charged and discharged as the current reverses each half cycle. Let q be the charge on the capacitor at any time t. The instantaneous voltage v across the capacitor is','[{"s": "When the capacitor is connected to an ac source, as in Fig. 7.8, it limits or regulates the current, but does not completely prevent the flow of charge. The capacitor is alternately charged and discharged as the current reverses each half cycle. Let q be the charge on the capacitor at any time t. The instantaneous voltage v across the capacitor is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21158,55,128,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternating_Current-image162.png (7.15)','[{"s": "Alternating_Current-image162.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(7.15)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21159,55,129,'paragraph',NULL,'From the Kirchhoff’s loop rule, the voltage across the source and the capacitor are equal,','[{"s": "From the Kirchhoff’s loop rule, the voltage across the source and the capacitor are equal,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
