(21170,55,140,'paragraph',NULL,'Comparing it to i m = v m /R for a purely resistive circuit, we find that (1/ωC) plays the role of resistance. It is called capacitive reactance and is denoted by X c ,','[{"s": "Comparing it to i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/R for a purely resistive circuit, we find that (1/ωC) plays the role of resistance. It is called capacitive reactance and is denoted by X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21171,55,141,'paragraph',NULL,'X c = 1/ωC (7.17)','[{"s": "X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1/ωC (7.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21173,55,143,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternating_Current-image3.png (7.18)','[{"s": "Alternating_Current-image3.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(7.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21174,55,144,'paragraph',NULL,'The dimension of capacitive reactance is the same as that of resistance and its SI unit is ohm (Ω). The capacitive reactance limits the amplitude of the current in a purely capacitive circuit in the same way as the resistance limits the current in a purely resistive circuit. But it is inversely proportional to the frequency and the capacitance.','[{"s": "The dimension of capacitive reactance is the same as that of resistance and its SI unit is ohm (Ω). The capacitive reactance limits the amplitude of the current in a purely capacitive circuit in the same way as the resistance limits the current in a purely resistive circuit. But it is inversely proportional to the frequency and the capacitance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21176,55,146,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.9 (a) A Phasor diagram for the circuit in Fig. 7.8. (b) Graph of v and i versus ωt.','[{"s": "Figure 7.9 (a) A Phasor diagram for the circuit in Fig. 7.8. (b) Graph of v and i versus ωt.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21177,55,147,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.9(a) shows the phasor diagram at an instant t 1 . Here the current phasor I is π/2 ahead of the voltage phasor V as they rotate counterclockwise. Figure 7.9(b) shows the variation of voltage and current with time. We see that the current reaches its maximum value earlier than the voltage by one-fourth of a period.','[{"s": "Figure 7.9(a) shows the phasor diagram at an instant t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Here the current phasor I is π/2 ahead of the voltage phasor V as they rotate counterclockwise. Figure 7.9(b) shows the variation of voltage and current with time. We see that the current reaches its maximum value earlier than the voltage by one-fourth of a period.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21178,55,148,'paragraph',NULL,'The instantaneous power supplied to the capacitor is','[{"s": "The instantaneous power supplied to the capacitor is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
