(21338,55,308,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eqs. [7.36 (b)] and [7.36 (c)], we see that Alternating_Current-image86.png . So, larger the value of Q, the smaller is the value of 2∆ω or the bandwidth and sharper is the resonance. Using Alternating_Current-image6.png , Eq. [7.36(c)] can be equivalently expressed as Q = 1/ω 0 CR.','[{"s": "From Eqs. [7.36 (b)] and [7.36 (c)], we see that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alternating_Current-image86.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". So, larger the value of Q, the smaller is the value of 2∆ω or the bandwidth and sharper is the resonance. Using", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alternating_Current-image6.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", Eq. [7.36(c)] can be equivalently expressed as Q = 1/ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CR.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21339,55,309,'paragraph',NULL,'We see from Fig. 7.15, that if the resonance is less sharp, not only is the maximum current less, the circuit is close to resonance for a larger range ∆ω of frequencies and the tuning of the circuit will not be good. So, less sharp the resonance, less is the selectivity of the circuit or vice versa. From Eq. (7.36), we see that if quality factor is large, i.e., R is low or L is large, the circuit is more selective.','[{"s": "We see from Fig. 7.15, that if the resonance is less sharp, not only is the maximum current less, the circuit is close to resonance for a larger range ∆ω of frequencies and the tuning of the circuit will not be good. So, less sharp the resonance, less is the selectivity of the circuit or vice versa. From Eq. (7.36), we see that if quality factor is large, i.e., R is low or L is large, the circuit is more selective.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21340,55,310,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.6 A resistor of 200 Ω and a capacitor of 15.0 µF are connected in series to a 220 V, 50 Hz ac source. (a) Calculate the current in the circuit; (b) Calculate the voltage (rms) across the resistor and the capacitor. Is the algebraic sum of these voltages more than the source voltage? If yes, resolve the paradox.','[{"s": "Example 7.6 A resistor of 200 Ω and a capacitor of 15.0 µF are connected in series to a 220 V, 50 Hz ac source. (a) Calculate the current in the circuit; (b) Calculate the voltage (rms) across the resistor and the capacitor. Is the algebraic sum of these voltages more than the source voltage? If yes, resolve the paradox.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21341,55,311,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21342,55,312,'paragraph',NULL,'Given','[{"s": "Given", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
