(21370,55,340,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternating_Current-image51.png [7.38(b)]','[{"s": "Alternating_Current-image51.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[7.38(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21371,55,341,'paragraph',NULL,'So, the average power dissipated depends not only on the voltage and current but also on the cosine of the phase angle φ between them. The quantity cosφ is called the power factor. Let us discuss the following cases:','[{"s": "So, the average power dissipated depends not only on the voltage and current but also on the cosine of the phase angle φ between them. The quantity cosφ is called the power factor. Let us discuss the following cases:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21372,55,342,'paragraph',NULL,'Case (i) Resistive circuit: If the circuit contains only pure R, it is called resistive. In that case φ = 0, cos φ = 1. There is maximum power dissipation.','[{"s": "Case (i) Resistive circuit: If the circuit contains only pure R, it is called resistive. In that case φ = 0, cos φ = 1. There is maximum power dissipation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21373,55,343,'paragraph',NULL,'Case (ii) Purely inductive or capacitive circuit: If the circuit contains only an inductor or capacitor, we know that the phase difference between voltage and current is π/2. Therefore, cos φ = 0, and no power is dissipated even though a current is flowing in the circuit. This current is sometimes referred to as wattless current.','[{"s": "Case (ii) Purely inductive or capacitive circuit: If the circuit contains only an inductor or capacitor, we know that the phase difference between voltage and current is π/2. Therefore, cos φ = 0, and no power is dissipated even though a current is flowing in the circuit. This current is sometimes referred to as wattless current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24146,61,158,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image24.png'),
(24147,61,159,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.11 Some equipotential surfaces for (a) a dipole, (b) two identical positive charges.','[{"s": "Figure 2.11 Some equipotential surfaces for (a) a dipole, (b) two identical positive charges.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24148,61,160,'heading',2,'2.7 Potential Energy of a System of Charges',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21374,55,344,'paragraph',NULL,'Case (iii) LCR series circuit: In an LCR series circuit, power dissipated is given by Eq. (7.38) where φ = tan –1 (X c – X L )/ R. So, φ may be non-zero in a RL or RC or RCL circuit. Even in such cases, power is dissipated only in the resistor.','[{"s": "Case (iii) LCR series circuit: In an LCR series circuit, power dissipated is given by Eq. (7.38) where φ = tan", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")/ R. So, φ may be non-zero in a RL or RC or RCL circuit. Even in such cases, power is dissipated only in the resistor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21375,55,345,'paragraph',NULL,'Case (iv) Power dissipated at resonance in LCR circuit: At resonance X c – X L = 0, and φ = 0. Therefore, cosφ = 1 and P = I 2 Z = I 2 R. That is, maximum power is dissipated in a circuit (through R) at resonance.','[{"s": "Case (iv) Power dissipated at resonance in LCR circuit: At resonance X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0, and φ = 0. Therefore, cosφ = 1 and P = I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z = I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R. That is, maximum power is dissipated in a circuit (through R) at resonance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21376,55,346,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.7 (a) For circuits used for transporting electric power, a low power factor implies large power loss in transmission. Explain.','[{"s": "Example 7.7 (a) For circuits used for transporting electric power, a low power factor implies large power loss in transmission. Explain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21377,55,347,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Power factor can often be improved by the use of a capacitor of appropriate capacitance in the circuit. Explain.','[{"s": "(b) Power factor can often be improved by the use of a capacitor of appropriate capacitance in the circuit. Explain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21378,55,348,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution (a) We know that P = I V cosφ where cosφ is the power factor. To supply a given power at a given voltage, if cosφ is small, we have to increase current accordingly. But this will lead to large power loss (I 2 R) in transmission.','[{"s": "Solution (a) We know that P = I V cosφ where cosφ is the power factor. To supply a given power at a given voltage, if cosφ is small, we have to increase current accordingly. But this will lead to large power loss (I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R) in transmission.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21379,55,349,'paragraph',NULL,'(b)Suppose in a circuit, current I lags the voltage by an angle φ. Then power factor cosφ =R/Z.','[{"s": "(b)Suppose in a circuit, current I lags the voltage by an angle φ. Then power factor cosφ =R/Z.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21381,55,351,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image106.png'),
(21382,55,352,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.17','[{"s": "Figure 7.17", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21383,55,353,'paragraph',NULL,'It’s clear from this analysis that if we want to improve power factor, we must completely neutralize the lagging wattless current I q by an equal leading wattless current I′ q .','[{"s": "It’s clear from this analysis that if we want to improve power factor, we must completely neutralize the lagging wattless current I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by an equal leading wattless current I′", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21384,55,354,'paragraph',NULL,'This can be done by connecting a capacitor of appropriate value in parallel so that I q and I′ q cancel each other and P is effectively I p V.','[{"s": "This can be done by connecting a capacitor of appropriate value in parallel so that I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and I′", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cancel each other and P is effectively I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21385,55,355,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.8 A sinusoidal voltage of peak value 283 V and frequency 50 Hz is applied to a series LCR circuit in which R = 3 Ω, L = 25.48 mH, and C = 796 µF. Find (a) the impedance of the circuit; (b) the phase difference between the voltage across the source and the current; (c) the power dissipated in the circuit; and (d) the power factor.','[{"s": "Example 7.8 A sinusoidal voltage of peak value 283 V and frequency 50 Hz is applied to a series LCR circuit in which R = 3 Ω, L = 25.48 mH, and C = 796 µF. Find (a) the impedance of the circuit; (b) the phase difference between the voltage across the source and the current; (c) the power dissipated in the circuit; and (d) the power factor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21386,55,356,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21387,55,357,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) To find the impedance of the circuit, we first calculate X L and X C .','[{"s": "(a) To find the impedance of the circuit, we first calculate X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21388,55,358,'paragraph',NULL,'X L = 2 πνL','[{"s": "X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2 πνL", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21389,55,359,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2 × 3.14 × 50 × 25.48 × 10 –3 Ω = 8 Ω','[{"s": "= 2 × 3.14 × 50 × 25.48 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ω = 8 Ω", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
