(33790,78,351,'paragraph',NULL,'This leads us to quote the final result as','[{"s": "This leads us to quote the final result as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21422,55,392,'paragraph',NULL,'We know that a capacitor and an inductor can store electrical and magnetic energy, respectively. When a capacitor (initially charged) is connected to an inductor, the charge on the capacitor and the current in the circuit exhibit the phenomenon of electrical oscillations similar to oscillations in mechanical systems (Chapter 14, Class XI).','[{"s": "We know that a capacitor and an inductor can store electrical and magnetic energy, respectively. When a capacitor (initially charged) is connected to an inductor, the charge on the capacitor and the current in the circuit exhibit the phenomenon of electrical oscillations similar to oscillations in mechanical systems (Chapter 14, Class XI).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21423,55,393,'paragraph',NULL,'Let a capacitor be charged q m (at t = 0) and connected to an inductor as shown in Fig. 7.18.','[{"s": "Let a capacitor be charged q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(at t = 0) and connected to an inductor as shown in Fig. 7.18.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21424,55,394,'paragraph',NULL,'The moment the circuit is completed, the charge on the capacitor starts decreasing, giving rise to current in the circuit. Let q and i be the charge and current in the circuit at time t. Since di/dt is positive, the induced emf in L will have polarity as shown, i.e., v b < v a . According to Kirchhoff’s loop rule,','[{"s": "The moment the circuit is completed, the charge on the capacitor starts decreasing, giving rise to current in the circuit. Let q and i be the charge and current in the circuit at time t. Since di/dt is positive, the induced emf in L will have polarity as shown, i.e., v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". According to Kirchhoff’s loop rule,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21425,55,395,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternating_Current-image39.png (7.39)','[{"s": "Alternating_Current-image39.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(7.39)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21426,55,396,'paragraph',NULL,'i = – (dq/dt) in the present case (as q decreases, i increases). Therefore, Eq. (7.39) becomes:','[{"s": "i = – (dq/dt) in the present case (as q decreases, i increases). Therefore, Eq. (7.39) becomes:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21427,55,397,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternating_Current-image96.png (7.40)','[{"s": "Alternating_Current-image96.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(7.40)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21428,55,398,'paragraph',NULL,'This equation has the form Alternating_Current-image70.png for a simple harmonic oscillator. The charge on the capacitor, therefore, oscillates with a natural frequency','[{"s": "This equation has the form", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alternating_Current-image70.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "for a simple harmonic oscillator. The charge on the capacitor, therefore, oscillates with a natural frequency", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21430,55,400,'paragraph',NULL,'and varies sinusoidally with time as','[{"s": "and varies sinusoidally with time as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21431,55,401,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternating_Current-image20.png (7.42)','[{"s": "Alternating_Current-image20.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(7.42)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21432,55,402,'paragraph',NULL,'where q m is the maximum value of q and φ is a phase constant. Since q = q m at t = 0, we have cos φ =1 or φ = 0. Therefore, in the present case,','[{"s": "where q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the maximum value of q and φ is a phase constant. Since q = q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at t = 0, we have cos φ =1 or φ = 0. Therefore, in the present case,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
