(21439,55,409,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.18 At the instant shown, the current is increasing; so the polarity of induced emf in the inductor is as shown.','[{"s": "Figure 7.18 At the instant shown, the current is increasing; so the polarity of induced emf in the inductor is as shown.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21440,55,410,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.19(a) shows a capacitor with initial charge qm connected to an ideal inductor. The electrical energy stored in the charged capacitor is Alternating_Current-image44.png . Since, there is no current in the circuit, energy in the inductor is zero. Thus, the total energy of LC circuit is,','[{"s": "Figure 7.19(a) shows a capacitor with initial charge qm connected to an ideal inductor. The electrical energy stored in the charged capacitor is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alternating_Current-image44.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". Since, there is no current in the circuit, energy in the inductor is zero. Thus, the total energy of LC circuit is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21441,55,411,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image79.png'),
(21442,55,412,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image83.png'),
(21443,55,413,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.19 The oscillations in an LC circuit are analogous to the oscillation of a block at the end of a spring. The figure depicts one-half of a cycle.','[{"s": "Figure 7.19 The oscillations in an LC circuit are analogous to the oscillation of a block at the end of a spring. The figure depicts one-half of a cycle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33791,78,352,'paragraph',NULL,'l b = 164 + 3 cm 2','[{"s": "l b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 164", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21429,55,399,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternating_Current-image10.png (7.41)','[{"s": "Alternating_Current-image10.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(7.41)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000045112", "2000112664"]',NULL),
(21444,55,414,'paragraph',NULL,'At t = 0, the switch is closed and the capacitor starts to discharge [Fig. 7.19(b)]. As the current increases, it sets up a magnetic field in the inductor and thereby, some energy gets stored in the inductor in the form of magnetic energy: U B = (1/2) Li 2 . As the current reaches its maximum value im, (at t = T/4) as in Fig. 7.19(c), all the energy is stored in the magnetic field: U B = (1/2) Li 2 m . You can easily check that the maximum electrical energy equals the maximum magnetic energy. The capacitor now has no charge and hence no energy. The current now starts charging the capacitor, as in Fig. 7.19(d). This process continues till the capacitor is fully charged (at t = T/2) [Fig. 7.19(e)]. But it is charged with a polarity opposite to its initial state in Fig. 7.19(a). The whole process just described will now repeat itself till the system reverts to its original state. Thus, the energy in the system oscillates between the capacitor and the inductor.','[{"s": "At t = 0, the switch is closed and the capacitor starts to discharge [Fig. 7.19(b)]. As the current increases, it sets up a magnetic field in the inductor and thereby, some energy gets stored in the inductor in the form of magnetic energy: U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (1/2) Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". As the current reaches its maximum value im, (at t = T/4) as in Fig. 7.19(c), all the energy is stored in the magnetic field:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (1/2) Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". You can easily check that the maximum electrical energy equals the maximum magnetic energy. The capacitor now has no charge and hence no energy. The current now starts charging the capacitor, as in Fig. 7.19(d). This process continues till the capacitor is fully charged (at t = T/2) [Fig. 7.19(e)]. But it is charged with a polarity opposite to its initial state in Fig. 7.19(a). The whole process just described will now repeat itself till the system reverts to its original state. Thus, the energy in the system oscillates between the capacitor and the inductor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21445,55,415,'paragraph',NULL,'The LC oscillation is similar to the mechanical oscillation of a block attached to a spring. The lower part of each figure in Fig. 7.19 depicts the corresponding stage of a mechanical system (a block attached to a spring). As noted earlier, for a block of a mass m oscillating with frequency ω 0 , the equation is','[{"s": "The LC oscillation is similar to the mechanical oscillation of a block attached to a spring. The lower part of each figure in Fig. 7.19 depicts the corresponding stage of a mechanical system (a block attached to a spring). As noted earlier, for a block of a mass m oscillating with frequency ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the equation is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21446,55,416,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image105.png'),
(21447,55,417,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, Alternating_Current-image59.png , and k is the spring constant. So, x corresponds to q. In case of a mechanical system F = ma = m (dv/dt) = m (d 2 x/dt 2 ). For an electrical system, ε = –L (di/dt) = –L (d 2 q/dt 2 ). Comparing these two equations, we see that L is analogous to mass m: L is a measure of resistance to change in current. In case of LC circuit, Alternating_Current-image22.png and for mass on a spring, Alternating_Current-image59.png . So, 1/C is analogous to k. The constant k (=F/x) tells us the (external) force required to produce a unit displacement whereas 1/C (=V/q) tells us the potential difference required to store a unit charge. Table 7.1 gives the analogy between mechanical and electrical quantities.','[{"s": "Here,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alternating_Current-image59.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", and k is the spring constant. So, x corresponds to q. In case of a mechanical system F = ma = m (dv/dt) = m (d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x/dt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). For an electrical system, ε = –L (di/dt) = –L (d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q/dt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Comparing these two equations, we see that L is analogous to mass m: L is a measure of resistance to change in current. In case of LC circuit,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alternating_Current-image22.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and for mass on a spring,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alternating_Current-image59.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". So, 1/C is analogous to k. The constant k (=F/x) tells us the (external) force required to produce a unit displacement whereas 1/C (=V/q) tells us the potential difference required to store a unit charge. Table 7.1 gives the analogy between mechanical and electrical quantities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21448,55,418,'image',NULL,'312',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Alternating_Current-image97.jpg'),
(21449,55,419,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the above discussion of LC oscillations is not realistic for two reasons:','[{"s": "Note that the above discussion of LC oscillations is not realistic for two reasons:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21450,55,420,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Every inductor has some resistance. The effect of this resistance is to introduce a damping effect on the charge and current in the circuit and the oscillations finally die away.','[{"s": "(i) Every inductor has some resistance. The effect of this resistance is to introduce a damping effect on the charge and current in the circuit and the oscillations finally die away.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21451,55,421,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Even if the resistance were zero, the total energy of the system would not remain constant. It is radiated away from the system in the form of electromagnetic waves (discussed in the next chapter). In fact, radio and TV transmitters depend on this radiation.','[{"s": "(ii) Even if the resistance were zero, the total energy of the system would not remain constant. It is radiated away from the system in the form of electromagnetic waves (discussed in the next chapter). In fact, radio and TV transmitters depend on this radiation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21452,55,422,'heading',3,'TWO DIFFERENT PHENOMENA, SAME MATHEMATICAL TREATMENT',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21453,55,423,'paragraph',NULL,'You may like to compare the treatment of a forced damped oscillator discussed in Section 14.10 of Class XI physics textbook, with that of an LCR circuit when an ac voltage is applied in it. We have already remarked that Eq. [14.37(b)] of Class XI Textbook is exactly similar to Eq. (7.28) here, although they use different symbols and parameters. Let us therefore list the equivalence between different quantities in the two situations:','[{"s": "You may like to compare the treatment of a forced damped oscillator discussed in Section 14.10 of Class XI physics textbook, with that of an LCR circuit when an ac voltage is applied in it. We have already remarked that Eq. [14.37(b)] of Class XI Textbook is exactly similar to Eq. (7.28) here, although they use different symbols and parameters. Let us therefore list the equivalence between different quantities in the two situations:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21454,55,424,'paragraph',NULL,'Forced oscillations Driven LCR circuit','[{"s": "Forced oscillations Driven LCR circuit", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
