(21882,56,140,'paragraph',NULL,'The negative sign of the total energy of an electron moving in an orbit means that the electron is bound with the nucleus. Energy will thus be required to remove the electron from the hydrogen atom to a distance infinitely far away from its nucleus (or proton in hydrogen atom).','[{"s": "The negative sign of the total energy of an electron moving in an orbit means that the electron is bound with the nucleus. Energy will thus be required to remove the electron from the hydrogen atom to a distance infinitely far away from its nucleus (or proton in hydrogen atom).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27809,68,516,'paragraph',NULL,'13. If an object undergoes a displacement ∆r in time ∆ t , its average velocity is given by','[{"s": "13. If an object undergoes a displacement ∆r in time ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", its", "t": "text"}, {"s": "average velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21883,56,141,'paragraph',NULL,'The derivation of Eqs. (12.17) – (12.19) involves the assumption that the electronic orbits are circular, though orbits under inverse square force are, in general elliptical. (Planets move in elliptical orbits under the inverse square gravitational force of the sun.) However, it was shown by the German physicist Arnold Sommerfeld (1868 – 1951) that, when the restriction of circular orbit is relaxed, these equations continue to hold even for elliptic orbits.','[{"s": "The derivation of Eqs. (12.17) – (12.19) involves the assumption that the electronic orbits are circular, though orbits under inverse square force are, in general elliptical. (Planets move in elliptical orbits under the inverse square gravitational force of the sun.) However, it was shown by the German physicist Arnold Sommerfeld (1868 – 1951) that, when the restriction of circular orbit is relaxed, these equations continue to hold even for elliptic orbits.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21884,56,142,'heading',3,'Orbit vs state (orbital picture) of electron in atom',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21885,56,143,'paragraph',NULL,'We are introduced to the Bohr Model of atom one time or the other in the course of physics. This model has its place in the history of quantum mechanics and particularly in explaining the structure of an atom. It has become a milestone since Bohr introduced the revolutionary idea of definite energy orbits for the electrons, contrary to the classical picture requiring an accelerating particle to radiate. Bohr also introduced the idea of quantisation of angular momentum of electrons moving in definite orbits. Thus it was a semi-classical picture of the structure of atom.','[{"s": "We are introduced to the Bohr Model of atom one time or the other in the course of physics. This model has its place in the history of quantum mechanics and particularly in explaining the structure of an atom. It has become a milestone since Bohr introduced the revolutionary idea of definite energy orbits for the electrons, contrary to the classical picture requiring an accelerating particle to radiate. Bohr also introduced the idea of quantisation of angular momentum of electrons moving in definite orbits. Thus it was a semi-classical picture of the structure of atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21886,56,144,'paragraph',NULL,'Now with the development of quantum mechanics, we have a better understanding of the structure of atom. Solutions of the Schrödinger wave equation assign a wave-like description to the electrons bound in an atom due to attractive forces of the protons.','[{"s": "Now with the development of quantum mechanics, we have a better understanding of the structure of atom. Solutions of the Schrödinger wave equation assign a wave-like description to the electrons bound in an atom due to attractive forces of the protons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21887,56,145,'paragraph',NULL,'An orbit of the electron in the Bohr model is the circular path of motion of an electron around the nucleus. But according to quantum mechanics, we cannot associate a definite path with the motion of the electrons in an atom. We can only talk about the probability of finding an electron in a certain region of space around the nucleus. This probability can be inferred from the one-electron wave function called the orbital. This function depends only on the coordinates of the electron.','[{"s": "An orbit of the electron in the Bohr model is the circular path of motion of an electron around the nucleus. But according to quantum mechanics, we cannot associate a definite path with the motion of the electrons in an atom. We can only talk about the probability of finding an electron in a certain region of space around the nucleus. This probability can be inferred from the one-electron wave function called the orbital. This function depends only on the coordinates of the electron.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21888,56,146,'paragraph',NULL,'It is therefore essential that we understand the subtle differences that exist in the two models:','[{"s": "It is therefore essential that we understand the subtle differences that exist in the two models:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21889,56,147,'paragraph',NULL,'- Bohr model is valid for only one-electron atoms/ions; an energy value, assigned to each orbit, depends on the principal quantum number n in this model. We know that energy associated with a stationary state of an electron depends on n only, for one-electron atoms/ions. For a multi-electron atom/ion, this is not true.','[{"s": "- Bohr model is valid for only one-electron atoms/ions; an energy value, assigned to each orbit, depends on the principal quantum number n in this model. We know that energy associated with a stationary state of an electron depends on n only, for one-electron atoms/ions. For a multi-electron atom/ion, this is not true.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21890,56,148,'paragraph',NULL,'- The solution of the Schrödinger wave equation, obtained for hydrogen-like atoms/ions, called the wave function, gives information about the probability of finding an electron in various regions around the nucleus. This orbital has no resemblance whatsoever with the orbit defined for an electron in the Bohr model.','[{"s": "- The solution of the Schrödinger wave equation, obtained for hydrogen-like atoms/ions, called the wave function, gives information about the probability of finding an electron in various regions around the nucleus. This orbital has no resemblance whatsoever with the orbit defined for an electron in the Bohr model.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21891,56,149,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 12.5 A 10 kg satellite circles earth once every 2 h in an orbit having a radius of 8000 km. Assuming that Bohr’s angular momentum postulate applies to satellites just as it does to an electron in the hydrogen atom, find the quantum number of the orbit of the satellite.','[{"s": "Example 12.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A 10 kg satellite circles earth once every 2 h in an orbit having a radius of 8000 km. Assuming that Bohr’s angular momentum postulate applies to satellites just as it does to an electron in the hydrogen atom, find the quantum number of the orbit of the satellite.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21892,56,150,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21893,56,151,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eq. (12.13), we have','[{"s": "From Eq. (12.13), we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21894,56,152,'paragraph',NULL,'m v n r n = nh/2π','[{"s": "m v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= nh/2π", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21895,56,153,'paragraph',NULL,'Here m = 10 kg and r n = 8 × 10 6 m. We have the time period T of the circling satellite as 2 h. That is T = 7200 s.','[{"s": "Here m = 10 kg and r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 8 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. We have the time period T of the circling satellite as 2 h. That is T = 7200 s.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21896,56,154,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus the velocity v n = 2π r n /T.','[{"s": "Thus the velocity v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2π r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/T.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21897,56,155,'paragraph',NULL,'The quantum number of the orbit of satellite','[{"s": "The quantum number of the orbit of satellite", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21898,56,156,'paragraph',NULL,'n = (2π r n ) 2 × m/(T × h).','[{"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (2π r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× m/(T × h).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21899,56,157,'paragraph',NULL,'Substituting the values,','[{"s": "Substituting the values,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(21900,56,158,'paragraph',NULL,'n = (2π × 8 × 10 6 m) 2 × 10/(7200 s × 6.64 × 10 –34 J s)','[{"s": "n = (2π × 8 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× 10/(7200 s × 6.64 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–34", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J s)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21901,56,159,'paragraph',NULL,'= 5.3 × 10 45','[{"s": "= 5.3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "45", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21902,56,160,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the quantum number for the satellite motion is extremely large! In fact for such large quantum numbers the results of quantisation conditions tend to those of classical physics.','[{"s": "Note that the quantum number for the satellite motion is extremely large! In fact for such large quantum numbers the results of quantisation conditions tend to those of classical physics.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21903,56,161,'heading',4,'12.4.1 Energy levels',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21904,56,162,'paragraph',NULL,'The energy of an atom is the least (largest negative value) when its electron is revolving in an orbit closest to the nucleus i.e., the one for which n = 1. For n = 2, 3, ... the absolute value of the energy E is smaller, hence the energy is progressively larger in the outer orbits. The lowest state of the atom, called the ground state , is that of the lowest energy, with the electron revolving in the orbit of smallest radius, the Bohr radius, a 0 . The energy of this state ( n = 1), E 1 is –13.6 eV. Therefore, the minimum energy required to free the electron from the ground state of the hydrogen atom is 13.6 eV. It is called the ionisation energy of the hydrogen atom. This prediction of the Bohr’s model is in excellent agreement with the experimental value of ionisation energy.','[{"s": "The energy of an atom is the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "least", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(largest negative value) when its electron is revolving in an orbit closest to the nucleus i.e., the one for which", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1. For", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2, 3, ... the absolute value of the energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is smaller, hence the energy is progressively larger in the outer orbits. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lowest", "t": "text"}, {"s": "state of the atom, called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ground state", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", is that of the lowest energy, with the electron revolving in the orbit of smallest radius, the Bohr radius,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The energy of this state (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is –13.6 eV. Therefore, the minimum energy required to free the electron from the ground state of the hydrogen atom is 13.6 eV. It is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ionisation energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the hydrogen atom. This prediction of the Bohr’s model is in excellent agreement with the experimental value of ionisation energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21905,56,163,'paragraph',NULL,'At room temperature, most of the hydrogen atoms are in ground state . When a hydrogen atom receives energy by processes such as electron collisions, the atom may acquire sufficient energy to raise the electron to higher energy states. The atom is then said to be in an excited state . From Eq. (12.19), for n = 2; the energy E 2 is –3.40 eV. It means that the energy required to excite an electron in hydrogen atom to its first excited state, is an energy equal to E 2 – E 1 = –3.40 eV – (–13.6) eV = 10.2 eV. Similarly, E 3 = –1.51 eV and E 3 – E 1 = 12.09 eV, or to excite the hydrogen atom from its ground state ( n = 1) to second excited state ( n = 3), 12.09 eV energy is required, and so on. From these excited states the electron can then fall back to a state of lower energy, emitting a photon in the process. Thus, as the excitation of hydrogen atom increases (that is as n increases) the value of minimum energy required to free the electron from the excited atom decreases.','[{"s": "At room temperature, most of the hydrogen atoms are in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ground state", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". When a hydrogen atom receives energy by processes such as electron collisions, the atom may acquire sufficient energy to raise the electron to higher energy states. The atom is then said to be in an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "excited state", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". From Eq. (12.19), for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2; the energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is –3.40 eV. It means that the energy required to excite an electron in hydrogen atom to its first excited state, is an energy equal to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –3.40 eV – (–13.6) eV = 10.2 eV. Similarly,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –1.51 eV and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 12.09 eV, or to excite the hydrogen atom from its ground state (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1) to second excited state (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3), 12.09 eV energy is required, and so on. From these excited states the electron can then fall back to a state of lower energy, emitting a photon in the process. Thus, as the excitation of hydrogen atom increases (that is as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "increases) the value of minimum energy required to free the electron from the excited atom decreases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21906,56,164,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Atoms-image40.png'),
(21907,56,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 12.8 The energy level diagram for the hydrogen atom. The electron in a hydrogen atom at room temperature spends most of its time in the ground state. To ionise a hydrogen atom an electron from the ground state, 13.6 eV of energy must be supplied. (The horizontal lines specify the presence of allowed energy states.)','[{"s": "Figure 12.8 The energy level diagram for the hydrogen atom. The electron in a hydrogen atom at room temperature spends most of its time in the ground state. To ionise a hydrogen atom an electron from the ground state, 13.6 eV of energy must be supplied. (The horizontal lines specify the presence of allowed energy states.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21908,56,166,'paragraph',NULL,'The energy level diagram* for the stationary states of a hydrogen atom, computed from Eq. (12.19), is given in Fig. 12.8. The principal quantum number n labels the stationary states in the ascending order of energy. In this diagram, the highest energy state corresponds to n =∞ in Eq, (12.19) and has an energy of 0 eV. This is the energy of the atom when the electron is completely removed ( r = ∞) from the nucleus and is at rest. Observe how the energies of the excited states come closer and closer together as n increases.','[{"s": "The energy level diagram* for the stationary states of a hydrogen atom, computed from Eq. (12.19), is given in Fig. 12.8. The principal quantum number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "labels the stationary states in the ascending order of energy. In this diagram, the highest energy state corresponds to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=∞ in Eq, (12.19) and has an energy of 0 eV. This is the energy of the atom when the electron is completely removed (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∞) from the nucleus and is at rest. Observe how the energies of the excited states come closer and closer together as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "increases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21909,56,167,'heading',3,'Franck – Hertz experiment',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21910,56,168,'paragraph',NULL,'The existence of discrete energy levels in an atom was directly verified in 1914 by James Franck and Gustav Hertz. They studied the spectrum of mercury vapour when electrons having different kinetic energies passed through the vapour. The electron energy was varied by subjecting the electrons to electric fields of varying strength. The electrons collide with the mercury atoms and can transfer energy to the mercury atoms. This can only happen when the energy of the electron is higher than the energy difference between an energy level of Hg occupied by an electron and a higher unoccupied level (see Figure). For instance, the difference between an occupied energy level of Hg and a higher unoccupied level is 4.9 eV. If an electron of having an energy of 4.9 eV or more passes through mercury, an electron in mercury atom can absorb energy from the bombarding electron and get excited to the higher level [Fig (a)]. The colliding electron’s kinetic energy would reduce by this amount.','[{"s": "The existence of discrete energy levels in an atom was directly verified in 1914 by James Franck and Gustav Hertz. They studied the spectrum of mercury vapour when electrons having different kinetic energies passed through the vapour. The electron energy was varied by subjecting the electrons to electric fields of varying strength. The electrons collide with the mercury atoms and can transfer energy to the mercury atoms. This can only happen when the energy of the electron is higher than the energy difference between an energy level of Hg occupied by an electron and a higher unoccupied level (see Figure). For instance, the difference between an occupied energy level of Hg and a higher unoccupied level is 4.9 eV. If an electron of having an energy of 4.9 eV or more passes through mercury, an electron in mercury atom can absorb energy from the bombarding electron and get excited to the higher level [Fig (a)]. The colliding electron’s kinetic energy would reduce by this amount.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21911,56,169,'image',NULL,'Fig_box',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Atoms-image49.png'),
(21912,56,170,'paragraph',NULL,'The excited electron would subsequently fall back to the ground state by emission of radiation [Fig. (b)]. The wavelength of emitted radiation is:','[{"s": "The excited electron would subsequently fall back to the ground state by emission of radiation [Fig. (b)]. The wavelength of emitted radiation is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21913,56,171,'paragraph',NULL,'Atoms-image3.png = 253 nm','[{"s": "Atoms-image3.png", "t": "img", "alt": "34"}, {"s": "= 253 nm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21914,56,172,'paragraph',NULL,'By direct measurement, Franck and Hertz found that the emission spectrum of mercury has a line corresponding to this wavelength. For this experimental verification of Bohr’s basic ideas of discrete energy levels in atoms and the process of photon emission, Frank and Hertz were awarded the Nobel prize in 1925','[{"s": "By direct measurement, Franck and Hertz found that the emission spectrum of mercury has a line corresponding to this wavelength. For this experimental verification of Bohr’s basic ideas of discrete energy levels in atoms and the process of photon emission, Frank and Hertz were awarded the Nobel prize in 1925", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21915,56,173,'heading',4,'12.5 The Line Spectra of the Hydrogen Atom',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21916,56,174,'paragraph',NULL,'According to the third postulate of Bohr’s model, when an atom makes a transition from the higher energy state with quantum number n i to the lower energy state with quantum number n f ( n f < n i ), the difference of energy is carried away by a photon of frequency ν if such that','[{"s": "According to the third postulate of Bohr’s model, when an atom makes a transition from the higher energy state with quantum number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to the lower energy state with quantum number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), the difference of energy is carried away by a photon of frequency ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "such that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21917,56,175,'paragraph',NULL,'h ν if = En i – En f (12.20)','[{"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "En", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "En", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(12.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21918,56,176,'paragraph',NULL,'Using Eq. (12.16), for En f and En i , we get','[{"s": "Using Eq. (12.16), for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "En", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "En", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
