(21924,56,182,'paragraph',NULL,'R = 1.03 × 10 7 m –1','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.03 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24930,62,180,'paragraph',NULL,'In place of Eq. (8.21), we can thus associate a potential energy W(r) at a distance r, such that','[{"s": "In place of Eq.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(8.21), we can thus associate a potential energy W(r) at a distance r, such that", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(21925,56,183,'paragraph',NULL,'This is a value very close to the value (1.097 × 10 7 m –1 ) obtained from the empirical Balmer formula. This agreement between the theoretical and experimental values of the Rydberg constant provided a direct and striking confirmation of the Bohr’s model.','[{"s": "This is a value very close to the value (1.097 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") obtained from the empirical Balmer formula. This agreement between the theoretical and experimental values of the Rydberg constant provided a direct and striking confirmation of the Bohr’s model.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21926,56,184,'paragraph',NULL,'Since both n f and n i are integers, this immediately shows that in transitions between different atomic levels, light is radiated in various discrete frequencies. For hydrogen spectrum, the Balmer formula corresponds to n f = 2 and n i = 3, 4, 5, etc. The results of the Bohr’s model suggested the presence of other series spectra for hydrogen atom–those corresponding to transitions resulting from n f = 1 and n i = 2, 3, etc.; n f = 3 and n i = 4, 5, etc., and so on. Such series were identified in the course of spectroscopic investigations and are known as the Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Brackett, and Pfund series. The electronic transitions corresponding to these series are shown in Fig. 12.9.','[{"s": "Since both", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are integers, this immediately shows that in transitions between different atomic levels, light is radiated in various discrete frequencies. For hydrogen spectrum, the Balmer formula corresponds to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3, 4, 5, etc. The results of the Bohr’s model suggested the presence of other series spectra for hydrogen atom–those corresponding to transitions resulting from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2, 3, etc.;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4, 5, etc., and so on. Such series were identified in the course of spectroscopic investigations and are known as the Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Brackett, and Pfund series. The electronic transitions corresponding to these series are shown in Fig. 12.9.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
