(21938,56,196,'paragraph',NULL,'= 913.4 n i 2 /(n i 2 –1) Å','[{"s": "= 913.4 n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/(n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1) Å", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21939,56,197,'paragraph',NULL,'Substituting n i = 2,3,4,5, we get λ 21 = 1218 Å, λ 31 = 1028 Å, λ 41 = 974.3 Å, and λ 51 = 951.4 Å.','[{"s": "Substituting n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2,3,4,5, we get λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1218 Å, λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1028 Å, λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "41", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 974.3 Å, and λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "51", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 951.4 Å.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21940,56,198,'heading',3,'12.6 De Broglie’s Explanation of Bohr’s Second Postulate of Quantisation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34852,80,285,'paragraph',NULL,'We have thus obtained the natural frequencies - the normal modes of oscillation of the system. The lowest possible natural frequency of a system is called its fundamental mode or the first harmonic. For the stretched string fixed at either end it is given by v =','[{"s": "We have thus obtained the natural frequencies - the normal modes of oscillation of the system. The lowest possible natural frequency of a system is called its fundamental mode or the first harmonic. For the stretched string fixed at either end it is given by v =", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21941,56,199,'paragraph',NULL,'Of all the postulates, Bohr made in his model of the atom, perhaps the most puzzling is his second postulate. It states that the angular momentum of the electron orbiting around the nucleus is quantised (that is, L n = nh/2 π; n = 1, 2, 3 …). Why should the angular momentum have only those values that are integral multiples of h/2 π? The French physicist Louis de Broglie explained this puzzle in 1923, ten years after Bohr proposed his model.','[{"s": "Of all the postulates, Bohr made in his model of the atom, perhaps the most puzzling is his second postulate. It states that the angular momentum of the electron orbiting around the nucleus is quantised (that is,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= nh/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1, 2, 3 …). Why should the angular momentum have only those values that are integral multiples of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π? The French physicist Louis de Broglie explained this puzzle in 1923, ten years after Bohr proposed his model.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21942,56,200,'paragraph',NULL,'We studied, in Chapter 11, about the de Broglie’s hypothesis that material particles, such as electrons, also have a wave nature. C. J. Davisson and L. H. Germer later experimentally verified the wave nature of electrons in 1927. Louis de Broglie argued that the electron in its circular orbit, as proposed by Bohr, must be seen as a particle wave. In analogy to waves travelling on a string, particle waves too can lead to standing waves under resonant conditions. From Chapter 15 of Class XI Physics textbook, we know that when a string is plucked, a vast number of wavelengths are excited. However only those wavelengths survive which have nodes at the ends and form the standing wave in the string. It means that in a string, standing waves are formed when the total distance travelled by a wave down the string and back is one wavelength, two wavelengths, or any integral number of wavelengths. Waves with other wavelengths interfere with themselves upon reflection and their amplitudes quickly drop to zero. For an electron moving in n th circular orbit of radius r n , the total distance is the circumference of the orbit, 2π r n . Thus','[{"s": "We studied, in Chapter 11, about the de Broglie’s hypothesis that material particles, such as electrons, also have a wave nature. C. J. Davisson and L. H. Germer later experimentally verified the wave nature of electrons in 1927. Louis de Broglie argued that the electron in its circular orbit, as proposed by Bohr, must be seen as a particle wave. In analogy to waves travelling on a string, particle waves too can lead to standing waves under resonant conditions. From Chapter 15 of Class XI Physics textbook, we know that when a string is plucked, a vast number of wavelengths are excited. However only those wavelengths survive which have nodes at the ends and form the standing wave in the string. It means that in a string, standing waves are formed when the total distance travelled by a wave down the string and back is one wavelength, two wavelengths, or any integral number of wavelengths. Waves with other wavelengths interfere with themselves upon reflection and their amplitudes quickly drop to zero. For an electron moving in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "circular orbit of radius", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the total distance is the circumference of the orbit, 2π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21943,56,201,'paragraph',NULL,'2π r n = n λ , n = 1, 2, 3... (12.24)','[{"s": "2π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1, 2, 3... (12.24)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21944,56,202,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 12.10 illustrates a standing particle wave on a circular orbit for n = 4, i.e., 2π r n = 4λ, where λ is the de Broglie wavelength of the electron moving in n th orbit. From Chapter 11, we have λ = h/p , where p is the magnitude of the electron’s momentum. If the speed of the electron is much less than the speed of light, the momentum is mv n . Thus, λ = h/mv n . From Eq. (12.24), we have','[{"s": "Figure 12.10 illustrates a standing particle wave on a circular orbit for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4, i.e., 2π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4λ, where λ is the de Broglie wavelength of the electron moving in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbit. From Chapter 11, we have λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= h/p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the magnitude of the electron’s momentum. If the speed of the electron is much less than the speed of light, the momentum is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= h/mv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "From Eq. (12.24), we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21945,56,203,'paragraph',NULL,'2π r n = n h/mv n or m v n r n = nh/2 π','[{"s": "2π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= n h/mv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= nh/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21946,56,204,'paragraph',NULL,'This is the quantum condition proposed by Bohr for the angular momentum of the electron [Eq. (12.13)]. In Section 12.5, we saw that this equation is the basis of explaining the discrete orbits and energy levels in hydrogen atom. Thus de Broglie hypothesis provided an explanation for Bohr’s second postulate for the quantisation of angular momentum of the orbiting electron. The quantised electron orbits and energy states are due to the wave nature of the electron and only resonant standing waves can persist.','[{"s": "This is the quantum condition proposed by Bohr for the angular momentum of the electron [Eq. (12.13)]. In Section 12.5, we saw that this equation is the basis of explaining the discrete orbits and energy levels in hydrogen atom. Thus de Broglie hypothesis provided an explanation for Bohr’s second postulate for the quantisation of angular momentum of the orbiting electron. The quantised electron orbits and energy states are due to the wave nature of the electron and only resonant standing waves can persist.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21947,56,205,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Atoms-image10.png'),
(21948,56,206,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 12.10 A standing wave is shown on a circular orbit where four de Broglie wavelengths fit into the circumference of the orbit.','[{"s": "Figure 12.10 A standing wave is shown on a circular orbit where four de Broglie wavelengths fit into the circumference of the orbit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21949,56,207,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The Bohr model is applicable to hydrogenic atoms. It cannot be extended even to mere two electron atoms such as helium. The analysis of atoms with more than one electron was attempted on the lines of Bohr’s model for hydrogenic atoms but did not meet with any success. Difficulty lies in the fact that each electron interacts not only with the positively charged nucleus but also with all other electrons.','[{"s": "(i) The Bohr model is applicable to hydrogenic atoms. It cannot be extended even to mere two electron atoms such as helium. The analysis of atoms with more than one electron was attempted on the lines of Bohr’s model for hydrogenic atoms but did not meet with any success. Difficulty lies in the fact that each electron interacts not only with the positively charged nucleus but also with all other electrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21950,56,208,'paragraph',NULL,'* Hydrogenic atoms are the atoms consisting of a nucleus with positive charge+Ze and a single electron, where Z is the proton number. Examples are hydrogenatom, singly ionised helium, doubly ionised lithium, and so forth. In theseatoms more complex electron-electron interactions are nonexistent','[{"s": "* Hydrogenic atoms are the atoms consisting of a nucleus with positive charge+Ze and a single electron, where Z is the proton number. Examples are hydrogenatom, singly ionised helium, doubly ionised lithium, and so forth. In theseatoms more complex electron-electron interactions are nonexistent", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21951,56,209,'paragraph',NULL,'The formulation of Bohr model involves electrical force between positively charged nucleus and electron. It does not include the electrical forces between electrons which necessarily appear in multi-electron atoms.','[{"s": "The formulation of Bohr model involves electrical force between positively charged nucleus and electron. It does not include the electrical forces between electrons which necessarily appear in multi-electron atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21952,56,210,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) While the Bohr’s model correctly predicts the frequencies of the light emitted by hydrogenic atoms, the model is unable to explain the relative intensities of the frequencies in the spectrum. In emission spectrum of hydrogen, some of the visible frequencies have weak intensity, others strong. Why? Experimental observations depict that some transitions are more favoured than others. Bohr’s model is unable to account for the intensity variations.','[{"s": "(ii) While the Bohr’s model correctly predicts the frequencies of the light emitted by hydrogenic atoms, the model is unable to explain the relative intensities of the frequencies in the spectrum. In emission spectrum of hydrogen, some of the visible frequencies have weak intensity, others strong. Why? Experimental observations depict that some transitions are more favoured than others. Bohr’s model is unable to account for the intensity variations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21953,56,211,'paragraph',NULL,'Bohr’s model presents an elegant picture of an atom and cannot be generalised to complex atoms. For complex atoms we have to use a new and radical theory based on Quantum Mechanics, which provides a more complete picture of the atomic structure.','[{"s": "Bohr’s model presents an elegant picture of an atom and cannot be generalised to complex atoms. For complex atoms we have to use a new and radical theory based on Quantum Mechanics, which provides a more complete picture of the atomic structure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21954,56,212,'heading',4,'Laser light',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21955,56,213,'paragraph',NULL,'Imagine a crowded market place or a railway platform with people entering a gate and going towards all directions. Their footsteps are random and there is no phase correlation between them. On the other hand, think of a large number of soldiers in a regulated march. Their footsteps are very well correlated. See figure here.','[{"s": "Imagine a crowded market place or a railway platform with people entering a gate and going towards all directions. Their footsteps are random and there is no phase correlation between them. On the other hand, think of a large number of soldiers in a regulated march. Their footsteps are very well correlated. See figure here.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21956,56,214,'paragraph',NULL,'This is similar to the difference between light emitted by an ordinary source like a candle or a bulb and that emitted by a laser. The acronym LASER stands for Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation. Since its development in 1960, it has entered into all areas of science and technology. It has found applications in physics, chemistry, biology, medicine, surgery, engineering, etc. There are low power lasers, with a power of 0.5 mW, called pencil lasers, which serve as pointers. There are also lasers of different power, suitable for delicate surgery of eye or glands in the stomach. Finally, there are lasers which can cut or weld steel.','[{"s": "This is similar to the difference between light emitted by an ordinary source like a candle or a bulb and that emitted by a laser. The acronym LASER stands for Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation. Since its development in 1960, it has entered into all areas of science and technology. It has found applications in physics, chemistry, biology, medicine, surgery, engineering, etc. There are low power lasers, with a power of 0.5 mW, called pencil lasers, which serve as pointers. There are also lasers of different power, suitable for delicate surgery of eye or glands in the stomach. Finally, there are lasers which can cut or weld steel.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21957,56,215,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Atoms-image15.png'),
(21958,56,216,'paragraph',NULL,'Light is emitted from a source in the form of packets of waves. Light coming out from an ordinary source contains a mixture of many wavelengths. There is also no phase relation between the various waves. Therefore, such light, even if it is passed through an aperture, spreads very fast and the beam size increases rapidly with distance. In the case of laser light, the wavelength of each packet is almost the same. Also the average length of the packet of waves is much larger. This means that there is better phase correlation over a longer duration of time. This results in reducing the divergence of a laser beam substantially.','[{"s": "Light is emitted from a source in the form of packets of waves. Light coming out from an ordinary source contains a mixture of many wavelengths. There is also no phase relation between the various waves. Therefore, such light, even if it is passed through an aperture, spreads very fast and the beam size increases rapidly with distance. In the case of laser light, the wavelength of each packet is almost the same. Also the average length of the packet of waves is much larger. This means that there is better phase correlation over a longer duration of time. This results in reducing the divergence of a laser beam substantially.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21959,56,217,'paragraph',NULL,'If there are N atoms in a source, each emitting light with intensity I , then the total intensity produced by an ordinary source is proportional to NI , whereas in a laser source, it is proportional to N 2 I . Considering that N is very large, we see that the light from a laser can be much stronger than that from an ordinary source.','[{"s": "If there are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atoms in a source, each emitting light with intensity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", then the total intensity produced by an ordinary source is proportional to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NI", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", whereas in a laser source, it is proportional to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Considering that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is very large, we see that the light from a laser can be much stronger than that from an ordinary source.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21960,56,218,'paragraph',NULL,'When astronauts of the Apollo missions visited the moon, they placed a mirror on its surface, facing the earth. Then scientists on the earth sent a strong laser beam, which was reflected by the mirror on the moon and received back on the earth. The size of the reflected laser beam and the time taken for the round trip were measured. This allowed a very accurate determination of (a) the extremely small divergence of a laser beam and (b) the distance of the moon from the earth.','[{"s": "When astronauts of the Apollo missions visited the moon, they placed a mirror on its surface, facing the earth. Then scientists on the earth sent a strong laser beam, which was reflected by the mirror on the moon and received back on the earth. The size of the reflected laser beam and the time taken for the round trip were measured. This allowed a very accurate determination of (a) the extremely small divergence of a laser beam and (b) the distance of the moon from the earth.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21961,56,219,'heading',3,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21962,56,220,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Atom, as a whole, is electrically neutral and therefore contains equal amount of positive and negative charges.','[{"s": "1. Atom, as a whole, is electrically neutral and therefore contains equal amount of positive and negative charges.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21963,56,221,'paragraph',NULL,'2. In Thomson’s model, an atom is a spherical cloud of positive charges with electrons embedded in it.','[{"s": "2. In Thomson’s model, an atom is a spherical cloud of positive charges with electrons embedded in it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21964,56,222,'paragraph',NULL,'3. In Rutherford’s model, most of the mass of the atom and all its positive charge are concentrated in a tiny nucleus (typically one by ten thousand the size of an atom), and the electrons revolve around it.','[{"s": "3. In Rutherford’s model, most of the mass of the atom and all its positive charge are concentrated in a tiny nucleus (typically one by ten thousand the size of an atom), and the electrons revolve around it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21965,56,223,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Rutherford nuclear model has two main difficulties in explaining the structure of atom: (a) It predicts that atoms are unstable because the accelerated electrons revolving around the nucleus must spiral into the nucleus. This contradicts the stability of matter. (b) It cannot explain the characteristic line spectra of atoms of different elements.','[{"s": "4. Rutherford nuclear model has two main difficulties in explaining the structure of atom: (a) It predicts that atoms are unstable because the accelerated electrons revolving around the nucleus must spiral into the nucleus. This contradicts the stability of matter. (b) It cannot explain the characteristic line spectra of atoms of different elements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21966,56,224,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Atoms of each element are stable and emit characteristic spectrum. The spectrum consists of a set of isolated parallel lines termed as line spectrum. It provides useful information about the atomic structure.','[{"s": "5. Atoms of each element are stable and emit characteristic spectrum. The spectrum consists of a set of isolated parallel lines termed as line spectrum. It provides useful information about the atomic structure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21968,56,226,'paragraph',NULL,'Lyman series: Atoms-image6.png ; n = 2, 3, 4,...','[{"s": "Lyman series:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Atoms-image6.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "; n = 2, 3, 4,...", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21969,56,227,'paragraph',NULL,'Balmer series: Atoms-image57.png ; n = 3, 4, 5,...','[{"s": "Balmer series:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Atoms-image57.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "; n = 3, 4, 5,...", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21970,56,228,'paragraph',NULL,'Paschen series: Atoms-image23.png ; n = 4, 5, 6,...','[{"s": "Paschen series:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Atoms-image23.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "; n = 4, 5, 6,...", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21971,56,229,'paragraph',NULL,'Brackett series: Atoms-image38.png ; n = 5, 6, 7,...','[{"s": "Brackett series:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Atoms-image38.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "; n = 5, 6, 7,...", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21972,56,230,'paragraph',NULL,'Pfund series: Atoms-image46.png ; n = 6, 7, 8,...','[{"s": "Pfund series:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Atoms-image46.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "; n = 6, 7, 8,...", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21973,56,231,'paragraph',NULL,'7. To explain the line spectra emitted by atoms, as well as the stability of atoms, Niel’s Bohr proposed a model for hydrogenic (single elctron) atoms. He introduced three postulates and laid the foundations of quantum mechanics:','[{"s": "7. To explain the line spectra emitted by atoms, as well as the stability of atoms, Niel’s Bohr proposed a model for hydrogenic (single elctron) atoms. He introduced three postulates and laid the foundations of quantum mechanics:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21974,56,232,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) In a hydrogen atom, an electron revolves in certain stable orbits (called stationary orbits) without the emission of radiant energy.','[{"s": "(a) In a hydrogen atom, an electron revolves in certain stable orbits (called stationary orbits) without the emission of radiant energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21975,56,233,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The stationary orbits are those for which the angular momentum is some integral multiple of h/2π. (Bohr’s quantisation condition.) That is L = nh/2π, where n is an integer called a quantum number.','[{"s": "(b) The stationary orbits are those for which the angular momentum is some integral multiple of h/2π. (Bohr’s quantisation condition.) That is L = nh/2π, where n is an integer called a quantum number.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21976,56,234,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The third postulate states that an electron might make a transition from one of its specified non-radiating orbits to another of lower energy. When it does so, a photon is emitted having energy equal to the energy difference between the initial and final states. The frequency (ν) of the emitted photon is then given by','[{"s": "(c) The third postulate states that an electron might make a transition from one of its specified non-radiating orbits to another of lower energy. When it does so, a photon is emitted having energy equal to the energy difference between the initial and final states. The frequency (ν) of the emitted photon is then given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21977,56,235,'paragraph',NULL,'hν = E i – E f','[{"s": "hν = E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21978,56,236,'paragraph',NULL,'An atom absorbs radiation of the same frequency the atom emits, in which case the electron is transferred to an orbit with a higher value of n.','[{"s": "An atom absorbs radiation of the same frequency the atom emits, in which case the electron is transferred to an orbit with a higher value of n.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21979,56,237,'paragraph',NULL,'E i + hν = E f','[{"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ hν = E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21980,56,238,'paragraph',NULL,'8. As a result of the quantisation condition of angular momentum, the electron orbits the nucleus at only specific radii. For a hydrogen atom it is given by','[{"s": "8. As a result of the quantisation condition of angular momentum, the electron orbits the nucleus at only specific radii. For a hydrogen atom it is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21981,56,239,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Atoms-image52.png'),
(21982,56,240,'paragraph',NULL,'The total energy is also quantised:','[{"s": "The total energy is also quantised:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21983,56,241,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Atoms-image36.png'),
(21984,56,242,'paragraph',NULL,'= –13.6 eV/n 2','[{"s": "= –13.6 eV/n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21985,56,243,'paragraph',NULL,'The n = 1 state is called ground state. In hydrogen atom the ground state energy is –13.6 eV. Higher values of n correspond to excited states (n > 1). Atoms are excited to these higher states by collisions with other atoms or electrons or by absorption of a photon of right frequency.','[{"s": "The n = 1 state is called ground state. In hydrogen atom the ground state energy is –13.6 eV. Higher values of n correspond to excited states (n > 1). Atoms are excited to these higher states by collisions with other atoms or electrons or by absorption of a photon of right frequency.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21986,56,244,'paragraph',NULL,'9. de Broglie’s hypothesis that electrons have a wavelength λ = h/mv gave an explanation for Bohr’s quantised orbits by bringing in the wave-particle duality. The orbits correspond to circular standing waves in which the circumference of the orbit equals a whole number of wavelengths.','[{"s": "9. de Broglie’s hypothesis that electrons have a wavelength λ = h/mv gave an explanation for Bohr’s quantised orbits by bringing in the wave-particle duality. The orbits correspond to circular standing waves in which the circumference of the orbit equals a whole number of wavelengths.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21988,56,246,'heading',3,'Points to Ponder',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21989,56,247,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Both the Thomson’s as well as the Rutherford’s models constitute an unstable system. Thomson’s model is unstable electrostatically, while Rutherford’s model is unstable because of electromagnetic radiation of orbiting electrons.','[{"s": "1. Both the Thomson’s as well as the Rutherford’s models constitute an unstable system. Thomson’s model is unstable electrostatically, while Rutherford’s model is unstable because of electromagnetic radiation of orbiting electrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21990,56,248,'paragraph',NULL,'2. What made Bohr quantise angular momentum (second postulate) and not some other quantity? Note, h has dimensions of angular momentum, and for circular orbits, angular momentum is a very relevant quantity. The second postulate is then so natural!','[{"s": "2. What made Bohr quantise angular momentum (second postulate) and not some other quantity? Note, h has dimensions of angular momentum, and for circular orbits, angular momentum is a very relevant quantity. The second postulate is then so natural!", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21991,56,249,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The orbital picture in Bohr’s model of the hydrogen atom was inconsistent with the uncertainty principle. It was replaced by modern quantum mechanics in which Bohr’s orbits are regions where the electron may be found with large probability.','[{"s": "3. The orbital picture in Bohr’s model of the hydrogen atom was inconsistent with the uncertainty principle. It was replaced by modern quantum mechanics in which Bohr’s orbits are regions where the electron may be found with large probability.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21992,56,250,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Unlike the situation in the solar system, where planet-planet gravitational forces are very small as compared to the gravitational force of the sun on each planet (because the mass of the sun is so much greater than the mass of any of the planets), the electron-electron electric force interaction is comparable in magnitude to the electron-nucleus electrical force, because the charges and distances are of the same order of magnitude. This is the reason why the Bohr’s model with its planet-like electron is not applicable to many electron atoms.','[{"s": "4. Unlike the situation in the solar system, where planet-planet gravitational forces are very small as compared to the gravitational force of the sun on each planet (because the mass of the sun is so much greater than the mass of any of the planets), the electron-electron electric force interaction is comparable in magnitude to the electron-nucleus electrical force, because the charges and distances are of the same order of magnitude. This is the reason why the Bohr’s model with its planet-like electron is not applicable to many electron atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21993,56,251,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Bohr laid the foundation of the quantum theory by postulating specific orbits in which electrons do not radiate. Bohr’s model include only one quantum number n. The new theory called quantum mechanics supportes Bohr’s postulate. However in quantum mechanics (more generally accepted), a given energy level may not correspond to just one quantum state. For example, a state is characterised by four quantum numbers (n, l, m, and s), but for a pure Coulomb potential (as in hydrogen atom) the energy depends only on n.','[{"s": "5. Bohr laid the foundation of the quantum theory by postulating specific orbits in which electrons do not radiate. Bohr’s model include only one quantum number n. The new theory called quantum mechanics supportes Bohr’s postulate. However in quantum mechanics (more generally accepted), a given energy level may not correspond to just one quantum state. For example, a state is characterised by four quantum numbers (n, l, m, and s), but for a pure Coulomb potential (as in hydrogen atom) the energy depends only on n.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21994,56,252,'paragraph',NULL,'6. In Bohr model, contrary to ordinary classical expectation, the frequency of revolution of an electron in its orbit is not connected to the frequency of spectral line. The later is the difference between two orbital energies divided by h. For transitions between large quantum numbers (n to n – 1, n very large), however, the two coincide as expected.','[{"s": "6. In Bohr model, contrary to ordinary classical expectation, the frequency of revolution of an electron in its orbit is not connected to the frequency of spectral line. The later is the difference between two orbital energies divided by h. For transitions between large quantum numbers (n to n – 1, n very large), however, the two coincide as expected.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21995,56,253,'paragraph',NULL,'7. Bohr’s semiclassical model based on some aspects of classical physics and some aspects of modern physics also does not provide a true picture of the simplest hydrogenic atoms. The true picture is quantum mechanical affair which differs from Bohr model in a number of fundamental ways. But then if the Bohr model is not strictly correct, why do we bother about it? The reasons which make Bohr’s model still useful are:','[{"s": "7. Bohr’s semiclassical model based on some aspects of classical physics and some aspects of modern physics also does not provide a true picture of the simplest hydrogenic atoms. The true picture is quantum mechanical affair which differs from Bohr model in a number of fundamental ways. But then if the Bohr model is not strictly correct, why do we bother about it? The reasons which make Bohr’s model still useful are:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21996,56,254,'paragraph',NULL,'(i)The model is based on just three postulates but accounts for almostall the general features of the hydrogen spectrum.','[{"s": "(i)The model is based on just three postulates but accounts for almostall the general features of the hydrogen spectrum.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21997,56,255,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii)The model incorporates many of the concepts we have learnt inclassical physics','[{"s": "(ii)The model incorporates many of the concepts we have learnt inclassical physics", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21998,56,256,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) The model demonstrates how a theoretical physicist occasionally must quite literally ignore certain problems of approach in hopes of being able to make some predictions. If the predictions of the theory or model agree with experiment, a theoretician then must somehow hope to explain away or rationalise the problems that were ignored along the way.','[{"s": "(iii) The model demonstrates how a theoretical physicist occasionally must quite literally ignore certain problems of approach in hopes of being able to make some predictions. If the predictions of the theory or model agree with experiment, a theoretician then must somehow hope to explain away or rationalise the problems that were ignored along the way.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21999,56,257,'heading',3,'Exercises',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22000,56,258,'paragraph',NULL,'12.1 Choose the correct alternative from the clues given at the end of each statement:','[{"s": "12.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Choose the correct alternative from the clues given at the end of each statement:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22001,56,259,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The size of the atom in Thomson’s model is .......... the atomic size in Rutherford’s model. (much greater than/no different from/much less than.)','[{"s": "(a) The size of the atom in Thomson’s model is .......... the atomic size in Rutherford’s model. (much greater than/no different from/much less than.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22002,56,260,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) In the ground state of .......... electrons are in stable equilibrium, while in .......... electrons always experience a net force. (Thomson’s model/ Rutherford’s model.)','[{"s": "(b) In the ground state of .......... electrons are in stable equilibrium, while in .......... electrons always experience a net force. (Thomson’s model/ Rutherford’s model.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22003,56,261,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) A classical atom based on .......... is doomed to collapse. (Thomson’s model/ Rutherford’s model.)','[{"s": "(c) A classical atom based on .......... is doomed to collapse. (Thomson’s model/ Rutherford’s model.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22004,56,262,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) An atom has a nearly continuous mass distribution in a .......... but has a highly non-uniform mass distribution in .......... (Thomson’s model/ Rutherford’s model.)','[{"s": "(d) An atom has a nearly continuous mass distribution in a .......... but has a highly non-uniform mass distribution in .......... (Thomson’s model/ Rutherford’s model.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22005,56,263,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) The positively charged part of the atom possesses most of the mass in .......... (Rutherford’s model/both the models.)','[{"s": "(e) The positively charged part of the atom possesses most of the mass in .......... (Rutherford’s model/both the models.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22006,56,264,'paragraph',NULL,'12.2 Suppose you are given a chance to repeat the alpha-particle scattering experiment using a thin sheet of solid hydrogen in place of the gold foil. (Hydrogen is a solid at temperatures below 14 K.) What results do you expect?','[{"s": "12.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Suppose you are given a chance to repeat the alpha-particle scattering experiment using a thin sheet of solid hydrogen in place of the gold foil. (Hydrogen is a solid at temperatures below 14 K.) What results do you expect?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22007,56,265,'paragraph',NULL,'12.3 What is the shortest wavelength present in the Paschen series of spectral lines?','[{"s": "12.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What is the shortest wavelength present in the Paschen series of spectral lines?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22008,56,266,'paragraph',NULL,'12.4 A difference of 2.3 eV separates two energy levels in an atom. What is the frequency of radiation emitted when the atom make a transition from the upper level to the lower level?','[{"s": "12.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A difference of 2.3 eV separates two energy levels in an atom. What is the frequency of radiation emitted when the atom make a transition from the upper level to the lower level?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22009,56,267,'paragraph',NULL,'12.5 The ground state energy of hydrogen atom is –13.6 eV. What are the kinetic and potential energies of the electron in this state?','[{"s": "12.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The ground state energy of hydrogen atom is –13.6 eV. What are the kinetic and potential energies of the electron in this state?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22011,56,269,'paragraph',NULL,'12.7 (a) Using the Bohr’s model calculate the speed of the electron in a hydrogen atom in the n = 1, 2, and 3 levels. (b) Calculate the orbital period in each of these levels.','[{"s": "12.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Using the Bohr’s model calculate the speed of the electron in a hydrogen atom in the n = 1, 2, and 3 levels. (b) Calculate the orbital period in each of these levels.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22012,56,270,'paragraph',NULL,'12.8 The radius of the innermost electron orbit of a hydrogen atom is 5.3×10 –11 m. What are the radii of the n = 2 and n =3 orbits?','[{"s": "12.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The radius of the innermost electron orbit of a hydrogen atom is 5.3×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. What are the radii of the n = 2 and n =3 orbits?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22013,56,271,'paragraph',NULL,'12.9 A 12.5 eV electron beam is used to bombard gaseous hydrogen at room temperature. What series of wavelengths will be emitted?','[{"s": "12.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A 12.5 eV electron beam is used to bombard gaseous hydrogen at room temperature. What series of wavelengths will be emitted?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22014,56,272,'paragraph',NULL,'12.10 In accordance with the Bohr’s model, find the quantum number that characterizes the earth’s revolution around the sun in an orbit of radius 1.5 × 10 11 m with orbital speed 3 × 10 4 m/s. (Mass of earth = 6.0 × 10 24 kg.)','[{"s": "12.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In accordance with the Bohr’s model, find the quantum number that characterizes the earth’s revolution around the sun in an orbit of radius 1.5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m with orbital speed 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m/s. (Mass of earth = 6.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "24", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22015,56,273,'heading',3,'Additional Exercises',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22016,56,274,'paragraph',NULL,'12.11 Answer the following questions, which help you understand the difference between Thomson’s model and Rutherford’s model better.','[{"s": "12.11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Answer the following questions, which help you understand the difference between Thomson’s model and Rutherford’s model better.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22017,56,275,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Is the average angle of deflection of α-particles by a thin gold foil predicted by Thomson’s model much less, about the same, or much greater than that predicted by Rutherford’s model?','[{"s": "(a) Is the average angle of deflection of α-particles by a thin gold foil predicted by Thomson’s model much less, about the same, or much greater than that predicted by Rutherford’s model?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22018,56,276,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Keeping other factors fixed, it is found experimentally that for small thickness t, the number of α-particles scattered at moderate angles is proportional to t. What clue does this linear dependence on t provide?','[{"s": "(c) Keeping other factors fixed, it is found experimentally that for small thickness t, the number of α-particles scattered at moderate angles is proportional to t. What clue does this linear dependence on t provide?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22019,56,277,'paragraph',NULL,'12.12 The gravitational attraction between electron and proton in a hydrogen atom is weaker than the coulomb attraction by a factor of about 10 –40 . An alternative way of looking at this fact is to estimate the radius of the first Bohr orbit of a hydrogen atom if the electron and proton were bound by gravitational attraction. You will find the answer interesting.','[{"s": "12.12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The gravitational attraction between electron and proton in a hydrogen atom is weaker than the coulomb attraction by a factor of about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–40", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". An alternative way of looking at this fact is to estimate the radius of the first Bohr orbit of a hydrogen atom if the electron and proton were bound by gravitational attraction. You will find the answer interesting.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22020,56,278,'paragraph',NULL,'12.13 Obtain an expression for the frequency of radiation emitted when a hydrogen atom de-excites from level n to level (n–1). For large n, show that this frequency equals the classical frequency of revolution of the electron in the orbit.','[{"s": "12.13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Obtain an expression for the frequency of radiation emitted when a hydrogen atom de-excites from level n to level (n–1). For large n, show that this frequency equals the classical frequency of revolution of the electron in the orbit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22021,56,279,'paragraph',NULL,'12.14 Classically, an electron can be in any orbit around the nucleus of an atom. Then what determines the typical atomic size? Why is an atom not, say, thousand times bigger than its typical size? The question had greatly puzzled Bohr before he arrived at his famous model of the atom that you have learnt in the text. To simulate what he might well have done before his discovery, let us play as follows with the basic constants of nature and see if we can get a quantity with the dimensions of length that is roughly equal to the known size of an atom (~ 10 –10 m).','[{"s": "12.14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Classically, an electron can be in any orbit around the nucleus of an atom. Then what determines the typical atomic size? Why is an atom not, say, thousand times bigger than its typical size? The question had greatly puzzled Bohr before he arrived at his famous model of the atom that you have learnt in the text. To simulate what he might well have done before his discovery, let us play as follows with the basic constants of nature and see if we can get a quantity with the dimensions of length that is roughly equal to the known size of an atom (~ 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22022,56,280,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Construct a quantity with the dimensions of length from the fundamental constants e, m e , and c. Determine its numerical value.','[{"s": "(a) Construct a quantity with the dimensions of length from the fundamental constants e, m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and c. Determine its numerical value.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27810,68,517,'paragraph',NULL,'v = Motion_in_a_Plane-image40.png . The velocity of an object at time t is the limiting value of the average velocity as ∆ t tends to zero :','[{"s": "v =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Plane-image40.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of an object at time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the limiting value of the average velocity as ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tends to zero :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27811,68,518,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-08 at 6.26.12 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image81.png'),
(27812,68,519,'paragraph',NULL,'. It can be written in unit vector notation as :','[{"s": ". It can be written in unit vector notation as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22023,56,281,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) You will find that the length obtained in (a) is many orders of magnitude smaller than the atomic dimensions. Further, it involves c. But energies of atoms are mostly in non-relativistic domain where c is not expected to play any role. This is what may have suggested Bohr to discard c and look for ‘something else’ to get the right atomic size. Now, the Planck’s constant h had already made its appearance elsewhere. Bohr’s great insight lay in recognising that h, m e , and e will yield the right atomic size. Construct a quantity with the dimension of length from h, m e , and e and confirm that its numerical value has indeed the correct order of magnitude.','[{"s": "(b) You will find that the length obtained in (a) is many orders of magnitude smaller than the atomic dimensions. Further, it involves c. But energies of atoms are mostly in non-relativistic domain where c is not expected to play any role. This is what may have suggested Bohr to discard c and look for ‘something else’ to get the right atomic size. Now, the Planck’s constant h had already made its appearance elsewhere. Bohr’s great insight lay in recognising that h, m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and e will yield the right atomic size. Construct a quantity with the dimension of length from h, m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and e and confirm that its numerical value has indeed the correct order of magnitude.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22024,56,282,'paragraph',NULL,'12.15 The total energy of an electron in the first excited state of the hydrogen atom is about –3.4 eV.','[{"s": "12.15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The total energy of an electron in the first excited state of the hydrogen atom is about –3.4 eV.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22025,56,283,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) What is the kinetic energy of the electron in this state?','[{"s": "(a) What is the kinetic energy of the electron in this state?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22026,56,284,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) What is the potential energy of the electron in this state?','[{"s": "(b) What is the potential energy of the electron in this state?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22027,56,285,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Which of the answers above would change if the choice of the zero of potential energy is changed?','[{"s": "(c) Which of the answers above would change if the choice of the zero of potential energy is changed?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22028,56,286,'paragraph',NULL,'12.16 If Bohr’s quantization postulate (angular momentum = nh/2π) is a basic law of nature, it should be equally valid for the case of planetary motion also. Why then do we never speak of quantization of orbits of planets around the sun?','[{"s": "12.16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If Bohr’s quantization postulate (angular momentum = nh/2π) is a basic law of nature, it should be equally valid for the case of planetary motion also. Why then do we never speak of quantization of orbits of planets around the sun?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22029,56,287,'paragraph',NULL,'12.17 Obtain the first Bohr’s radius and the ground state energy of a muonic hydrogen atom [i.e., an atom in which a negatively charged muon (µ – ) of mass about 207m e orbits around a proton].','[{"s": "12.17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Obtain the first Bohr’s radius and the ground state energy of a muonic hydrogen atom [i.e., an atom in which a negatively charged muon (µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of mass about 207m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "orbits around a proton].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22030,56,288,'paragraph',NULL,'EXEMPLAR QUESTIONS','[{"s": "EXEMPLAR QUESTIONS", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22031,56,289,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Taking the Bohr radius as a 0 =53 pm , the radius of Li ++ ion in its ground state, on the basis of Bohr\'s model, will be about','[{"s": "1. Taking the Bohr radius as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=53 pm", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the radius of Li", "t": "text"}, {"s": "++", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ion in its ground state, on the basis of Bohr\'s model, will be about", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22032,56,290,'paragraph',NULL,'1. 53 pm','[{"s": "1. 53 pm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22033,56,291,'paragraph',NULL,'2. 27 pm','[{"s": "2. 27 pm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22034,56,292,'paragraph',NULL,'3. 18 pm','[{"s": "3. 18 pm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22035,56,293,'paragraph',NULL,'4. 13 pm','[{"s": "4. 13 pm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22036,56,294,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The binding energy of a H-atom, considering an electron moving around a fixed nuclei (proton), is','[{"s": "2. The binding energy of a H-atom, considering an electron moving around a fixed nuclei (proton), is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22037,56,295,'paragraph',NULL,'B=-me 4 /8n 2 ε 0 2 h 2 (m = electron mass)','[{"s": "B=-me", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/8n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(m = electron mass)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22038,56,296,'paragraph',NULL,'If one decides to work in a frame of reference where the electron is at rest, the proton would be moving around it. By similar arguments, the binding energy would be','[{"s": "If one decides to work in a frame of reference where the electron is at rest, the proton would be moving around it. By similar arguments, the binding energy would be", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22039,56,297,'paragraph',NULL,'B=-Me 4 /8n 2 ε 0 2 h 2','[{"s": "B=-Me", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/8n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22040,56,298,'paragraph',NULL,'(M = proton mass)','[{"s": "(M = proton mass)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22041,56,299,'paragraph',NULL,'This last expression is not correct, because','[{"s": "This last expression is not correct, because", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22042,56,300,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) n would not be integral','[{"s": "(1) n would not be integral", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22043,56,301,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) Bohr-quantisation applies only to electron','[{"s": "(2) Bohr-quantisation applies only to electron", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22044,56,302,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) the frame in which the electron is at rest is not inertial','[{"s": "(3) the frame in which the electron is at rest is not inertial", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22045,56,303,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) the motion of the proton would not be in circular orbits, even approximately.','[{"s": "(4) the motion of the proton would not be in circular orbits, even approximately.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22046,56,304,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The simple Bohr model cannot be directly applied to calculate the energy levels of an atom with many electrons. This is because','[{"s": "3. The simple Bohr model cannot be directly applied to calculate the energy levels of an atom with many electrons. This is because", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22047,56,305,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) of the electrons not being subjected to a central force.','[{"s": "(1) of the electrons not being subjected to a central force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22048,56,306,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) of the electrons colliding with each other.','[{"s": "(2) of the electrons colliding with each other.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22049,56,307,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) of screening effects.','[{"s": "(3) of screening effects.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22050,56,308,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) the force between the nucleus and an electron will no longer be given by Coulomb\'s law.','[{"s": "(4) the force between the nucleus and an electron will no longer be given by Coulomb\'s law.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22051,56,309,'paragraph',NULL,'4. For the ground state, the electron in the H-atom has an angular momentum = h, according to the simple Bohr model. Angular momentum is a vector and hence there will be infinitely many orbits with the vector pointing in all possible directions. In actuality, this is not true,','[{"s": "4. For the ground state, the electron in the H-atom has an angular momentum = h, according to the simple Bohr model. Angular momentum is a vector and hence there will be infinitely many orbits with the vector pointing in all possible directions. In actuality, this is not true,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22052,56,310,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) because Bohr model gives incorrect values of angular momentum','[{"s": "(1) because Bohr model gives incorrect values of angular momentum", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22053,56,311,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) because only one of these would have a minimum energy','[{"s": "(2) because only one of these would have a minimum energy", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22054,56,312,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) angular momentum must be in the direction of spin of electron','[{"s": "(3) angular momentum must be in the direction of spin of electron", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22055,56,313,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) because electrons go around only in horizontal orbits','[{"s": "(4) because electrons go around only in horizontal orbits", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22056,56,314,'paragraph',NULL,'5. O 2 molecule consists of two oxygen atoms. In the molecule, nuclear force between the nuclei of the two atoms','[{"s": "5. O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecule consists of two oxygen atoms. In the molecule, nuclear force between the nuclei of the two atoms", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22057,56,315,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) is not important because nuclear forces are short-ranged','[{"s": "(1) is not important because nuclear forces are short-ranged", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22058,56,316,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) is as important as electrostatic force for binding the two atoms','[{"s": "(2) is as important as electrostatic force for binding the two atoms", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22059,56,317,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) cancels the repulsive electrostatic force between the nuclei','[{"s": "(3) cancels the repulsive electrostatic force between the nuclei", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22060,56,318,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) is not important because oxygen nucleus have equal number of neutrons and protons','[{"s": "(4) is not important because oxygen nucleus have equal number of neutrons and protons", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22061,56,319,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Two H atoms in the ground state collide inelastically. The maximum amount by which their combined kinetic energy is reduced is','[{"s": "6. Two H atoms in the ground state collide inelastically. The maximum amount by which their combined kinetic energy is reduced is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22062,56,320,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) 12.20 eV','[{"s": "(1) 12.20 eV", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22063,56,321,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) 20.40 eV','[{"s": "(2) 20.40 eV", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22064,56,322,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) 13.6 eV','[{"s": "(3) 13.6 eV", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22065,56,323,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) 27.2 eV','[{"s": "(4) 27.2 eV", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22066,56,324,'paragraph',NULL,'7. A set of atoms in an excited state decays','[{"s": "7. A set of atoms in an excited state decays", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22067,56,325,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) in general to any of the states with lower energy','[{"s": "(1) in general to any of the states with lower energy", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22068,56,326,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) into a lower state only when excited by an external electric field','[{"s": "(2) into a lower state only when excited by an external electric field", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22069,56,327,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) all together simultaneously into a lower state','[{"s": "(3) all together simultaneously into a lower state", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22070,56,328,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) to emit photons only when they collide','[{"s": "(4) to emit photons only when they collide", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22071,56,329,'paragraph',NULL,'8. An ionised H-molecule consists of an electron and two protons. The protons are separated by a small distance of the order of angstrom. In the ground state.','[{"s": "8. An ionised H-molecule consists of an electron and two protons. The protons are separated by a small distance of the order of angstrom. In the ground state.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22072,56,330,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) the electron would not move in circular orbits','[{"s": "(1) the electron would not move in circular orbits", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22073,56,331,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) the energy would be (2)4 times that of a H-atom','[{"s": "(2) the energy would be (2)4 times that of a H-atom", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22074,56,332,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) the electrons, orbit would go around the protons','[{"s": "(3) the electrons, orbit would go around the protons", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22075,56,333,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) the molecule will soon decay in a proton and a H-atom','[{"s": "(4) the molecule will soon decay in a proton and a H-atom", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22076,56,334,'paragraph',NULL,'9. Consider aiming a beam of free electrons towards free protons. When they scatter, an electron and a proton cannot combine to produce a H-atom.','[{"s": "9. Consider aiming a beam of free electrons towards free protons. When they scatter, an electron and a proton cannot combine to produce a H-atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22077,56,335,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) Because of energy conservation','[{"s": "(1) Because of energy conservation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22078,56,336,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) Without simultaneously releasing energy in the form of radiation','[{"s": "(2) Without simultaneously releasing energy in the form of radiation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22079,56,337,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) Because of momentum conservation','[{"s": "(3) Because of momentum conservation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22080,56,338,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) Because of angular momentum conservation','[{"s": "(4) Because of angular momentum conservation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22081,56,339,'paragraph',NULL,'10. The Bohr model for the spectra of a H-atom','[{"s": "10. The Bohr model for the spectra of a H-atom", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22082,56,340,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) will not be applicable to hydrogen in the molecular form','[{"s": "(1) will not be applicable to hydrogen in the molecular form", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22083,56,341,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) will not be applicanble as it is for a He-atom','[{"s": "(2) will not be applicanble as it is for a He-atom", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22084,56,342,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) is valid only at room temperature','[{"s": "(3) is valid only at room temperature", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22085,56,343,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) predicts continuous as well as discrete spectral lines','[{"s": "(4) predicts continuous as well as discrete spectral lines", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22086,56,344,'paragraph',NULL,'11. The Balmer series for the H-atom can be observed','[{"s": "11. The Balmer series for the H-atom can be observed", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22087,56,345,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) if we measure the frequencies of light emitted when an excited atom falls to the ground state','[{"s": "(1) if we measure the frequencies of light emitted when an excited atom falls to the ground state", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22088,56,346,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) if we measure the frequencies of light emitted due to transitions between excited states and the first excited state','[{"s": "(2) if we measure the frequencies of light emitted due to transitions between excited states and the first excited state", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22089,56,347,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) in any transition in a H-atom','[{"s": "(3) in any transition in a H-atom", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22090,56,348,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) as a sequence of frequencies with the higher frequencies getting closely packed','[{"s": "(4) as a sequence of frequencies with the higher frequencies getting closely packed", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22091,56,349,'paragraph',NULL,'12. Let En=−1me 4 /8ε 0 2 n 2 h 2 be the energy of the nth level of H-atom. If all the H-atoms are in the ground state and radiation of frequency (E 2 −E 1 )/ ℎ','[{"s": "12. Let En=−1me", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/8ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the energy of the nth level of H-atom. If all the H-atoms are in the ground state and radiation of frequency", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ℎ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22092,56,350,'paragraph',NULL,'falls on it,','[{"s": "falls on it,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22093,56,351,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) it will not be absorbed at all','[{"s": "(1) it will not be absorbed at all", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22094,56,352,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) some of atoms will move to the first excited state','[{"s": "(2) some of atoms will move to the first excited state", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22095,56,353,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) all atoms will be excited to the n = 2 state','[{"s": "(3) all atoms will be excited to the n = 2 state", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22096,56,354,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) no atoms will make a transition to the n = 3 state','[{"s": "(4) no atoms will make a transition to the n = 3 state", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22097,56,355,'paragraph',NULL,'13. The simple Bohr model is not applicable to He 4 atom because','[{"s": "13. The simple Bohr model is not applicable to He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atom because", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22098,56,356,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) He 4 is an inert gas','[{"s": "(1) He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is an inert gas", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22099,56,357,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) He 4 has neutrons in the nucleus','[{"s": "(2) He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has neutrons in the nucleus", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
