(22100,56,358,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) He 4 has one more electron','[{"s": "(3) He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has one more electron", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22101,56,359,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) electrons are not subject to central forces','[{"s": "(4) electrons are not subject to central forces", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22102,56,360,'paragraph',NULL,'14. The mass of a H-atom is less than the sum of the masses of a proton and electron why is this?','[{"s": "14. The mass of a H-atom is less than the sum of the masses of a proton and electron why is this?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22103,56,361,'paragraph',NULL,'15. Imagine removing one electron from He 4 and He 3 . Their energy levels, as worked out on the basis of Bohr model will be very close. Explain why?','[{"s": "15. Imagine removing one electron from He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and He", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Their energy levels, as worked out on the basis of Bohr model will be very close. Explain why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22104,56,362,'paragraph',NULL,'24. The first four spectral in the Lyman series of a H atom are','[{"s": "24. The first four spectral in the Lyman series of a H atom are", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22105,56,363,'paragraph',NULL,'λ=1218 Å , 1028 Å , 974.3 Å and 951.4 Å .','[{"s": "λ=1218", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Å", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 1028", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Å", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 974.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Å", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 951.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Å", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22106,56,364,'paragraph',NULL,'If instead of Hydrogen, we consider deuterium, calculate the shift in the wave length of these lines.','[{"s": "If instead of Hydrogen, we consider deuterium, calculate the shift in the wave length of these lines.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27813,68,520,'paragraph',NULL,'Motion_in_a_Plane-image80.png where','[{"s": "Motion_in_a_Plane-image80.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screen Shot 2019-06-08 at 6.26.24 PM"}, {"s": "where", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27814,68,521,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image72.png'),
(22107,56,365,'paragraph',NULL,'25. Deutrium was discovered in 1932 by Harold Urey by measuring the small change in wavelength for a particular transition in 1 H and 2 H. This is because, the wavelength of transition depend to a certain extent on the nuclear mass. If nuclear motion is taken into account, then the electrons and nucleus revolve around their common centre of mass.','[{"s": "25. Deutrium was discovered in 1932 by Harold Urey by measuring the small change in wavelength for a particular transition in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H. This is because, the wavelength of transition depend to a certain extent on the nuclear mass. If nuclear motion is taken into account, then the electrons and nucleus revolve around their common centre of mass.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22108,56,366,'paragraph',NULL,'Such a system is equivalent to a single particle with a reduced mass μ , revolving around the nucleus at a distance equal to the electron-nucleus separation. Here','[{"s": "Such a system is equivalent to a single particle with a reduced mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": "μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", revolving around the nucleus at a distance equal to the electron-nucleus separation. Here", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22109,56,367,'paragraph',NULL,'μ=m e M/(m e +M) , where M is the nuclear mass and m e is the electronic mass. Estimate the percentage difference in wavelenth for the 1st line of the Lyman series in 1 H and 2 H. (mass of 1 H nucleus is 1.6725×10 -27 kg, mass of 2 H nucleus is 3.3374×10 -27 kg, Mass of electron =9.109×10 -31 kg .)','[{"s": "μ=m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M/(m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+M)", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where M is the nuclear mass and m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the electronic mass. Estimate the percentage difference in wavelenth for the 1st line of the Lyman series in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H. (mass of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H nucleus is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1.6725×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-27", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg, mass of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H nucleus is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3.3374×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-27", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg, Mass of electron", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=9.109×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22110,56,368,'paragraph',NULL,'26. If a proton had a radius R and the charge was uniformly distributed, calculate using Bohr theory, the ground state energy of a H-atom when (i) R=0.1Å and (ii) R=10Å.','[{"s": "26. If a proton had a radius R and the charge was uniformly distributed, calculate using Bohr theory, the ground state energy of a H-atom when (i) R=0.1Å and (ii) R=10Å.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22111,56,369,'paragraph',NULL,'27. In the Auger process, an atom makes a transition to a lower state without emitting a photo. The excess energy is transferred to an outer electron which may be ejected by the atom (This is called an Auger, electron). Assuming the nucleus to be massive, calculate the kinetic energy of an n = 4 Auger electron emitted by Chromium by absorbing the energy from a n = 2 to n = 1 transition.','[{"s": "27. In the Auger process, an atom makes a transition to a lower state without emitting a photo. The excess energy is transferred to an outer electron which may be ejected by the atom (This is called an Auger, electron). Assuming the nucleus to be massive, calculate the kinetic energy of an n = 4 Auger electron emitted by Chromium by absorbing the energy from a n = 2 to n = 1 transition.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22112,56,370,'paragraph',NULL,'28. The inverse square law in electrostatic is |F|= e 2 (4πε 0 )r 2 for the force between an electron and a proton. The ( 1/r ) dependence of |F| can be understood in quantum theory as being due to the fact that the particle light (photon) is massless. If photons had a mass m p , force would be modified to |F|= e 2 (4π/ε 0 )r [ 1/r 2 + λ/r ]⋅exp(−λr) where λ =m p c /h and ℎ=ℎ/2π. Estimate the change in the ground state energy of a H-atom if m p were 10 -6 times the mass of an electron.','[{"s": "28. The inverse square law in electrostatic is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|F|=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for the force between an electron and a proton. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dependence of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|F|", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be understood in quantum theory as being due to the fact that the particle light (photon) is massless. If photons had a mass m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", force would be modified to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|F|=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4π/ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]⋅exp(−λr)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ℎ=ℎ/2π. Estimate the change in the ground state energy of a H-atom if m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "were 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "times the mass of an electron.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22113,56,371,'paragraph',NULL,'29. The Bohr model for the H-atom relies on the Coulomb\'s law of electrostatics. Coulomb\'s law has not directly been verified for very short distances of the order of angstroms. Supposing Coulomb\'s law between two opposite charge +q 1 , -q 2 is modified to |F|=q 1 q 2 /(4πε 0 ) 1/r 2 , r≥R 0','[{"s": "29. The Bohr model for the H-atom relies on the Coulomb\'s law of electrostatics. Coulomb\'s law has not directly been verified for very short distances of the order of angstroms. Supposing Coulomb\'s law between two opposite charge +q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", -q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is modified to |F|=q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/(4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") 1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", r≥R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22114,56,372,'paragraph',NULL,'=q 1 q 2 /4πε 0 1/R 2 0 (R 0 /r)ε, r≤R 0','[{"s": "=q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r)ε, r≤R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22115,56,373,'paragraph',NULL,'Calculate in such a case, the ground state energy of a H-atom, if E = 0.1, R 0 =1 Å .','[{"s": "Calculate in such a case, the ground state energy of a H-atom, if E = 0.1,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Å", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22116,57,0,'heading',2,'Chapter Three',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22117,57,1,'heading',1,'Current Electricity',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22118,57,2,'heading',2,'3.1 Introduction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22119,57,3,'paragraph',NULL,'In Chapter 1, all charges whether free or bound, were considered to be at rest. Charges in motion constitute an electric current. Such currents occur naturally in many situations. Lightning is one such phenomenon in which charges flow from the clouds to the earth through the atmosphere, sometimes with disastrous results. The flow of charges in lightning is not steady, but in our everyday life we see many devices where charges flow in a steady manner, like water flowing smoothly in a river. A torch and a cell-driven clock are examples of such devices. In the present chapter, we shall study some of the basic laws concerning steady electric currents.','[{"s": "In Chapter 1, all charges whether free or bound, were considered to be at rest. Charges in motion constitute an electric current. Such currents occur naturally in many situations. Lightning is one such phenomenon in which charges flow from the clouds to the earth through the atmosphere, sometimes with disastrous results. The flow of charges in lightning is not steady, but in our everyday life we see many devices where charges flow in a steady manner, like water flowing smoothly in a river. A torch and a cell-driven clock are examples of such devices. In the present chapter, we shall study some of the basic laws concerning steady electric currents.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22120,57,4,'heading',2,'3.2 Electric Current',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22122,57,6,'paragraph',NULL,'Current_Electricity-image19.png (3.1)','[{"s": "Current_Electricity-image19.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22123,57,7,'paragraph',NULL,'is defined to be the current across the area in the forward direction. (If it turn out to be a negative number, it implies a current in the backward direction.)','[{"s": "is defined to be the current across the area in the forward direction. (If it turn out to be a negative number, it implies a current in the backward direction.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22124,57,8,'paragraph',NULL,'Currents are not always steady and hence more generally, we define the current as follows. Let ∆Q be the net charge flowing across a cross-section of a conductor during the time interval ∆t [i.e., between times t and (t + ∆t)]. Then, the current at time t across the cross-section of the conductor is defined as the value of the ratio of ∆Q to ∆t in the limit of ∆t tending to zero,','[{"s": "Currents are not always steady and hence more generally, we define the current as follows. Let ∆Q be the net charge flowing across a cross-section of a conductor during the time interval ∆t [i.e., between times t and (t + ∆t)]. Then, the current at time t across the cross-section of the conductor is defined as the value of the ratio of ∆Q to ∆t in the limit of ∆t tending to zero,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22125,57,9,'paragraph',NULL,'Current_Electricity-image64.png (3.2)','[{"s": "Current_Electricity-image64.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22126,57,10,'paragraph',NULL,'In SI units, the unit of current is ampere. An ampere is defined through magnetic effects of currents that we will study in the following chapter. An ampere is typically the order of magnitude of currents in domestic appliances. An average lightning carries currents of the order of tens of thousands of amperes and at the other extreme, currents in our nerves are in microamperes.','[{"s": "In SI units, the unit of current is ampere. An ampere is defined through magnetic effects of currents that we will study in the following chapter. An ampere is typically the order of magnitude of currents in domestic appliances. An average lightning carries currents of the order of tens of thousands of amperes and at the other extreme, currents in our nerves are in microamperes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22127,57,11,'heading',2,'3.3 Electric Currents in Conductors',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22128,57,12,'paragraph',NULL,'An electric charge will experience a force if an electric field is applied. If it is free to move, it will thus move contributing to a current. In nature, free charged particles do exist like in upper strata of atmosphere called the ionosphere. However, in atoms and molecules, the negatively charged electrons and the positively charged nuclei are bound to each other and are thus not free to move. Bulk matter is made up of many molecules, a gram of water, for example, contains approximately 1022 molecules. These molecules are so closely packed that the electrons are no longer attached to individual nuclei. In some materials, the electrons will still be bound, i.e., they will not accelerate even if an electric field is applied. In other materials, notably metals, some of the electrons are practically free to move within the bulk material. These materials, generally called conductors, develop electric currents in them when an electric field is applied.','[{"s": "An electric charge will experience a force if an electric field is applied. If it is free to move, it will thus move contributing to a current. In nature, free charged particles do exist like in upper strata of atmosphere called the ionosphere. However, in atoms and molecules, the negatively charged electrons and the positively charged nuclei are bound to each other and are thus not free to move. Bulk matter is made up of many molecules, a gram of water, for example, contains approximately 1022 molecules. These molecules are so closely packed that the electrons are no longer attached to individual nuclei. In some materials, the electrons will still be bound, i.e., they will not accelerate even if an electric field is applied. In other materials, notably metals, some of the electrons are practically free to move within the bulk material. These materials, generally called conductors, develop electric currents in them when an electric field is applied.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27815,68,522,'paragraph',NULL,'When position of an object is plotted on a coordinate system, v is always tangent to the curve representing the path of the object.','[{"s": "When position of an object is plotted on a coordinate system, v is always tangent to the curve representing the path of the object.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22129,57,13,'paragraph',NULL,'If we consider solid conductors, then of course the atoms are tightly bound to each other so that the current is carried by the negatively charged electrons. There are, however, other types of conductors like electrolytic solutions where positive and negative charges both can move. In our discussions, we will focus only on solid conductors so that the current is carried by the negatively charged electrons in the background of fixed positive ions.','[{"s": "If we consider solid conductors, then of course the atoms are tightly bound to each other so that the current is carried by the negatively charged electrons. There are, however, other types of conductors like electrolytic solutions where positive and negative charges both can move. In our discussions, we will focus only on solid conductors so that the current is carried by the negatively charged electrons in the background of fixed positive ions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22130,57,14,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider first the case when no electric field is present. The electrons will be moving due to thermal motion during which they collide with the fixed ions. An electron colliding with an ion emerges with the same speed as before the collision. However, the direction of its velocity after the collision is completely random. At a given time, there is no preferential direction for the velocities of the electrons. Thus on the average, the number of electrons travelling in any direction will be equal to the number of electrons travelling in the opposite direction. So, there will be no net electric current.','[{"s": "Consider first the case when no electric field is present. The electrons will be moving due to thermal motion during which they collide with the fixed ions. An electron colliding with an ion emerges with the same speed as before the collision. However, the direction of its velocity after the collision is completely random. At a given time, there is no preferential direction for the velocities of the electrons. Thus on the average, the number of electrons travelling in any direction will be equal to the number of electrons travelling in the opposite direction. So, there will be no net electric current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22131,57,15,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image71.png'),
(22132,57,16,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.1 Charges +Q and –Q put at the ends of a metallic cylinder. The electrons will drift because of the electric field created to neutralise the charges. The current thus will stop after a while unless the charges +Q and –Q are continuously replenished.','[{"s": "Figure 3.1 Charges +Q and –Q put at the ends of a metallic cylinder. The electrons will drift because of the electric field created to neutralise the charges. The current thus will stop after a while unless the charges +Q and –Q are continuously replenished.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22133,57,17,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us now see what happens to such a piece of conductor if an electric field is applied. To focus our thoughts, imagine the conductor in the shape of a cylinder of radius R (Fig. 3.1). Suppose we now take two thin circular discs of a dielectric of the same radius and put positive charge +Q distributed over one disc and similarly –Q at the other disc. We attach the two discs on the two flat surfaces of the cylinder. An electric field will be created and is directed from the positive towards the negative charge. The electrons will be accelerated due to this field towards +Q. They will thus move to neutralise the charges. The electrons, as long as they are moving, will constitute an electric current. Hence in the situation considered, there will be a current for a very short while and no current thereafter.','[{"s": "Let us now see what happens to such a piece of conductor if an electric field is applied. To focus our thoughts, imagine the conductor in the shape of a cylinder of radius R (Fig. 3.1). Suppose we now take two thin circular discs of a dielectric of the same radius and put positive charge +Q distributed over one disc and similarly –Q at the other disc. We attach the two discs on the two flat surfaces of the cylinder. An electric field will be created and is directed from the positive towards the negative charge. The electrons will be accelerated due to this field towards +Q. They will thus move to neutralise the charges. The electrons, as long as they are moving, will constitute an electric current. Hence in the situation considered, there will be a current for a very short while and no current thereafter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22134,57,18,'paragraph',NULL,'We can also imagine a mechanism where the ends of the cylinder are supplied with fresh charges to make up for any charges neutralised by electrons moving inside the conductor. In that case, there will be a steady electric field in the body of the conductor. This will result in a continuous current rather than a current for a short period of time. Mechanisms, which maintain a steady electric field are cells or batteries that we shall study later in this chapter. In the next sections, we shall study the steady current that results from a steady electric field in conductors.','[{"s": "We can also imagine a mechanism where the ends of the cylinder are supplied with fresh charges to make up for any charges neutralised by electrons moving inside the conductor. In that case, there will be a steady electric field in the body of the conductor. This will result in a continuous current rather than a current for a short period of time. Mechanisms, which maintain a steady electric field are cells or batteries that we shall study later in this chapter. In the next sections, we shall study the steady current that results from a steady electric field in conductors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22135,57,19,'heading',2,'3.4 Ohm’s Law',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22137,57,21,'paragraph',NULL,'V ∝ I or, V = R I (3.3)','[{"s": "V ∝ I or, V = R I (3.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22139,57,23,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image18.png'),
(22140,57,24,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.2 Illustrating the relation R = ρl/A for a rectangular slab of length l and area of cross-section A.','[{"s": "Figure 3.2 Illustrating the relation R = ρl/A for a rectangular slab of length l and area of cross-section A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22141,57,25,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a conductor satisfying Eq. (3.3) to be in the form of a slab of length l and cross sectional area A [Fig. 3.2(a)]. Imagine placing two such identical slabs side by side [Fig. 3.2(b)], so that the length of the combination is 2l. The current flowing through the combination is the same as that flowing through either of the slabs. If V is the potential difference across the ends of the first slab, then V is also the potential difference across the ends of the second slab since the second slab is identical to the first and the same current I flows through both. The potential difference across the ends of the combination is clearly sum of the potential difference across the two individual slabs and hence equals 2V. The current through the combination is I and the resistance of the combination R C is [from Eq. (3.3)],','[{"s": "Consider a conductor satisfying Eq. (3.3) to be in the form of a slab of length l and cross sectional area A [Fig. 3.2(a)]. Imagine placing two such identical slabs side by side [Fig. 3.2(b)], so that the length of the combination is 2l. The current flowing through the combination is the same as that flowing through either of the slabs. If V is the potential difference across the ends of the first slab, then V is also the potential difference across the ends of the second slab since the second slab is identical to the first and the same current I flows through both. The potential difference across the ends of the combination is clearly sum of the potential difference across the two individual slabs and hence equals 2V. The current through the combination is I and the resistance of the combination R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is [from Eq. (3.3)],", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22142,57,26,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image119.png'),
(22143,57,27,'paragraph',NULL,'Georg Simon Ohm (1787–1854) German physicist, professor at Munich. Ohm was led to his law by an analogy between the conduction of heat: the electric field is analogous to the temperature gradient, and the electric current is analogous to the heat flow.','[{"s": "Georg Simon Ohm (1787–1854)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "German physicist, professor at Munich. Ohm was led to his law by an analogy between the conduction of heat: the electric field is analogous to the temperature gradient, and the electric current is analogous to the heat flow.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22144,57,28,'paragraph',NULL,'Current_Electricity-image63.png (3.4)','[{"s": "Current_Electricity-image63.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22145,57,29,'paragraph',NULL,'since V/I = R, the resistance of either of the slabs. Thus, doubling the length of a conductor doubles the resistance. In general, then resistance is proportional to length,','[{"s": "since V/I = R, the resistance of either of the slabs. Thus, doubling the length of a conductor doubles the resistance. In general, then resistance is proportional to length,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22146,57,30,'paragraph',NULL,'Current_Electricity-image97.png (3.5)','[{"s": "Current_Electricity-image97.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22147,57,31,'paragraph',NULL,'Next, imagine dividing the slab into two by cutting it lengthwise so that the slab can be considered as a combination of two identical slabs of length l, but each having a cross sectional area of A/2 [Fig. 3.2(c)].','[{"s": "Next, imagine dividing the slab into two by cutting it lengthwise so that the slab can be considered as a combination of two identical slabs of length l, but each having a cross sectional area of A/2 [Fig. 3.2(c)].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22148,57,32,'paragraph',NULL,'For a given voltage V across the slab, if I is the current through the entire slab, then clearly the current flowing through each of the two half-slabs is I/2. Since the potential difference across the ends of the half-slabs is V, i.e., the same as across the full slab, the resistance of each of the half-slabs R 1 is','[{"s": "For a given voltage V across the slab, if I is the current through the entire slab, then clearly the current flowing through each of the two half-slabs is I/2. Since the potential difference across the ends of the half-slabs is V, i.e., the same as across the full slab, the resistance of each of the half-slabs R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22149,57,33,'paragraph',NULL,'Current_Electricity-image15.png (3.6)','[{"s": "Current_Electricity-image15.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22152,57,36,'paragraph',NULL,'Combining Eqs. (3.5) and (3.7), we have','[{"s": "Combining Eqs. (3.5) and (3.7), we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
