(22160,57,44,'paragraph',NULL,'or, E = jρ (3.11)','[{"s": "or, E = jρ (3.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22163,57,47,'paragraph',NULL,'or, j = σ E (3.13)','[{"s": "or, j = σ E (3.13)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22164,57,48,'paragraph',NULL,'where σ ≡1/ρ is called the conductivity. Ohm’s law is often stated in an equivalent form, Eq. (3.13) in addition to Eq.(3.3). In the next section, we will try to understand the origin of the Ohm’s law as arising from the characteristics of the drift of electrons.','[{"s": "where σ ≡1/ρ is called the conductivity. Ohm’s law is often stated in an equivalent form, Eq. (3.13) in addition to Eq.(3.3). In the next section, we will try to understand the origin of the Ohm’s law as arising from the characteristics of the drift of electrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22165,57,49,'heading',2,'3.5 Drift of Electrons and the Origin of Resistivity',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22166,57,50,'paragraph',NULL,'As remarked before, an electron will suffer collisions with the heavy fixed ions, but after collision, it will emerge with the same speed but in random directions. If we consider all the electrons, their average velocity will be zero since their directions are random. Thus, if there are N electrons and the velocity of the ith electron (i = 1, 2, 3, ... N ) at a given time is vi, then','[{"s": "As remarked before, an electron will suffer collisions with the heavy fixed ions, but after collision, it will emerge with the same speed but in random directions. If we consider all the electrons, their average velocity will be zero since their directions are random. Thus, if there are N electrons and the velocity of the ith electron (i = 1, 2, 3, ... N ) at a given time is vi, then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22167,57,51,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image22.png'),
(22168,57,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.3 A schematic picture of an electron moving from a point A to another point B through repeated collisions, and straight line travel between collisions (full lines). If an electric field is applied as shown, the electron ends up at point B′ (dotted lines). A slight drift in a direction opposite the electric field is visible.','[{"s": "Figure 3.3 A schematic picture of an electron moving from a point A to another point B through repeated collisions, and straight line travel between collisions (full lines). If an electric field is applied as shown, the electron ends up at point B′ (dotted lines). A slight drift in a direction opposite the electric field is visible.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
