(22193,57,77,'paragraph',NULL,'We thus see that a very simple picture of electrical conduction reproduces Ohm’s law. We have, of course, made assumptions that τ and n are constants, independent of E. We shall, in the next section, discuss the limitations of Ohm’s law.','[{"s": "We thus see that a very simple picture of electrical conduction reproduces Ohm’s law. We have, of course, made assumptions that τ and n are constants, independent of E. We shall, in the next section, discuss the limitations of Ohm’s law.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22194,57,78,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 3.1 (a) Estimate the average drift speed of conduction electrons in a copper wire of cross-sectional area 1.0 × 10 –7 m 2 carrying a current of 1.5 A. Assume that each copper atom contributes roughly one conduction electron. The density of copper is 9.0 × 103 kg/m 3 , and its atomic mass is 63.5 u. (b) Compare the drift speed obtained above with, (i) thermal speeds of copper atoms at ordinary temperatures, (ii) speed of propagation of electric field along the conductor which causes the drift motion.','[{"s": "Example 3.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Estimate the average drift speed of conduction electrons in a copper wire of cross-sectional area 1.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "carrying a current of 1.5 A. Assume that each copper atom contributes roughly one conduction electron. The density of copper is 9.0 × 103 kg/m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and its atomic mass is 63.5 u. (b) Compare the drift speed obtained above with, (i) thermal speeds of copper atoms at ordinary temperatures, (ii) speed of propagation of electric field along the conductor which causes the drift motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22195,57,79,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22196,57,80,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The direction of drift velocity of conduction electrons is opposite to the electric field direction, i.e., electrons drift in the direction of increasing potential. The drift speed vd is given by Eq. (3.18)','[{"s": "(a) The direction of drift velocity of conduction electrons is opposite to the electric field direction, i.e., electrons drift in the direction of increasing potential. The drift speed vd is given by Eq. (3.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22197,57,81,'paragraph',NULL,'vd = (I/neA)','[{"s": "vd = (I/neA)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27816,68,523,'paragraph',NULL,'14. If the velocity of an object changes from v to v′in time ∆ t , then its average acceleration is given by:','[{"s": "14. If the velocity of an object changes from v to v′in time ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", then its", "t": "text"}, {"s": "average acceleration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is given by:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
