(22198,57,82,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, e = 1.6 × 10 –19 C, A = 1.0 × 10 –7 m 2 , I = 1.5 A. The density of conduction electrons, n is equal to the number of atoms per cubic metre (assuming one conduction electron per Cu atom as is reasonable from its valence electron count of one). A cubic metre of copper has a mass of 9.0 × 10 3 kg. Since 6.0 × 10 23 copper atoms have a mass of 63.5 g,','[{"s": "Now, e = 1.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C, A = 1.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", I = 1.5 A. The density of conduction electrons, n is equal to the number of atoms per cubic metre (assuming one conduction electron per Cu atom as is reasonable from its valence electron count of one). A cubic metre of copper has a mass of 9.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg. Since 6.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "23", "t": "text"}, {"s": "copper atoms have a mass of 63.5 g,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
