(22203,57,87,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1.1 × 10 –3 m s –1 = 1.1 mm s –1','[{"s": "= 1.1 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.1 mm s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22204,57,88,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) (i) At a temperature T, the thermal speed* of a copper atom of mass M is obtained from [<(1/2) Mv 2 > = (3/2) k B T ] and is thus typically of the order of Current_Electricity-image77.png , where k B is the Boltzmann constant. For copper at 300 K, this is about 2 × 10 2 m/s. This figure indicates the random vibrational speeds of copper atoms in a conductor. Note that the drift speed of electrons is much smaller, about 10 –5 times the typical thermal speed at ordinary temperatures.','[{"s": "(b) (i) At a temperature T, the thermal speed* of a copper atom of mass M is obtained from [<(1/2) Mv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> = (3/2) k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T ] and is thus typically of the order of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Current_Electricity-image77.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", where k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the Boltzmann constant. For copper at 300 K, this is about 2 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m/s. This figure indicates the random vibrational speeds of copper atoms in a conductor. Note that the drift speed of electrons is much smaller, about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "times the typical thermal speed at ordinary temperatures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22205,57,89,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) An electric field travelling along the conductor has a speed of an electromagnetic wave, namely equal to 3.0 × 10 8 m s (You will learn about this in Chapter 8). The drift speed is, in comparison, extremely small; smaller by a factor of 10 –11 .','[{"s": "(ii) An electric field travelling along the conductor has a speed of an electromagnetic wave, namely equal to 3.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "(You will learn about this in Chapter 8). The drift speed is, in comparison, extremely small; smaller by a factor of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–11", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22206,57,90,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 3.2','[{"s": "Example 3.2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22207,57,91,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) In Example 3.1, the electron drift speed is estimated to be only a few mm s –1 for currents in the range of a few amperes? How then is current established almost the instant a circuit is closed?','[{"s": "(a) In Example 3.1, the electron drift speed is estimated to be only a few mm s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for currents in the range of a few amperes? How then is current established almost the instant a circuit is closed?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22208,57,92,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The electron drift arises due to the force experienced by electrons in the electric field inside the conductor. But force should cause acceleration. Why then do the electrons acquire a steady average drift speed?','[{"s": "(b) The electron drift arises due to the force experienced by electrons in the electric field inside the conductor. But force should cause acceleration. Why then do the electrons acquire a steady average drift speed?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22209,57,93,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) If the electron drift speed is so small, and the electron’s charge is small, how can we still obtain large amounts of current in a conductor?','[{"s": "(c) If the electron drift speed is so small, and the electron’s charge is small, how can we still obtain large amounts of current in a conductor?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22210,57,94,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) When electrons drift in a metal from lower to higher potential, does it mean that all the ‘free’ electrons of the metal are moving in the same direction?','[{"s": "(d) When electrons drift in a metal from lower to higher potential, does it mean that all the ‘free’ electrons of the metal are moving in the same direction?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22211,57,95,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) Are the paths of electrons straight lines between successive collisions (with the positive ions of the metal) in the (i) absence of electric field, (ii) presence of electric field?','[{"s": "(e) Are the paths of electrons straight lines between successive collisions (with the positive ions of the metal) in the (i) absence of electric field, (ii) presence of electric field?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22212,57,96,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22213,57,97,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Electric field is established throughout the circuit, almost instantly (with the speed of light) causing at every point a local electron drift. Establishment of a current does not have to wait for electrons from one end of the conductor travelling to the other end. However, it does take a little while for the current to reach its steady value.','[{"s": "(a) Electric field is established throughout the circuit, almost instantly (with the speed of light) causing at every point a local electron drift. Establishment of a current does not have to wait for electrons from one end of the conductor travelling to the other end. However, it does take a little while for the current to reach its steady value.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22214,57,98,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Each ‘free’ electron does accelerate, increasing its drift speed until it collides with a positive ion of the metal. It loses its drift speed after collision but starts to accelerate and increases its drift speed again only to suffer a collision again and so on. On the average, therefore, electrons acquire only a drift speed.','[{"s": "(b) Each ‘free’ electron does accelerate, increasing its drift speed until it collides with a positive ion of the metal. It loses its drift speed after collision but starts to accelerate and increases its drift speed again only to suffer a collision again and so on. On the average, therefore, electrons acquire only a drift speed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22215,57,99,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Simple, because the electron number density is enormous, ~10 29 m –3 .','[{"s": "(c) Simple, because the electron number density is enormous, ~10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "29", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22216,57,100,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) By no means. The drift velocity is superposed over the large random velocities of electrons.','[{"s": "(d) By no means. The drift velocity is superposed over the large random velocities of electrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22217,57,101,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) In the absence of electric field, the paths are straight lines; in the presence of electric field, the paths are, in general, curved.','[{"s": "(e) In the absence of electric field, the paths are straight lines; in the presence of electric field, the paths are, in general, curved.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22218,57,102,'heading',4,'3.5.1 MOBILITY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22219,57,103,'paragraph',NULL,'As we have seen, conductivity arises from mobile charge carriers. In metals, these mobile charge carriers are electrons; in an ionised gas, they are electrons and positive charged ions; in an electrolyte, these can be both positive and negative ions.','[{"s": "As we have seen, conductivity arises from mobile charge carriers. In metals, these mobile charge carriers are electrons; in an ionised gas, they are electrons and positive charged ions; in an electrolyte, these can be both positive and negative ions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22221,57,105,'paragraph',NULL,'Current_Electricity-image94.png (3.24)','[{"s": "Current_Electricity-image94.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.24)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22222,57,106,'paragraph',NULL,'The SI unit of mobility is m 2 /Vs and is 10 4 of the mobility in practical units (cm 2 /Vs). Mobility is positive. From Eq. (3.17), we have','[{"s": "The SI unit of mobility is m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/Vs and is 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the mobility in practical units (cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/Vs). Mobility is positive. From Eq. (3.17), we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22223,57,107,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image38.png'),
(22224,57,108,'paragraph',NULL,'(3.25) Current_Electricity-image33.png','[{"s": "(3.25)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Current_Electricity-image33.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22225,57,109,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image28.png'),
(22227,57,111,'paragraph',NULL,'where τ is the average collision time for electrons.','[{"s": "where τ is the average collision time for electrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22228,57,112,'heading',3,'3.6 LIMITATIONS OF OHM’S LAW',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22229,57,113,'paragraph',NULL,'Although Ohm’s law has been found valid over a large class of materials, there do exist materials and devices used in electric circuits where the proportionality of V and I does not hold. The deviations broadly are one or more of the following types:','[{"s": "Although Ohm’s law has been found valid over a large class of materials, there do exist materials and devices used in electric circuits where the proportionality of V and I does not hold. The deviations broadly are one or more of the following types:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22230,57,114,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) V ceases to be proportional to I (Fig. 3.5).','[{"s": "(a) V ceases to be proportional to I (Fig. 3.5).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22231,57,115,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The relation between V and I depends on the sign of V. In other words, if I is the current for a certain V, then reversing the direction of V keeping its magnitude fixed, does not produce a current of the same magnitude as I in the opposite direction (Fig. 3.6). This happens, for example, in a diode which we will study in Chapter 14.','[{"s": "(b) The relation between V and I depends on the sign of V. In other words, if I is the current for a certain V, then reversing the direction of V keeping its magnitude fixed, does not produce a current of the same magnitude as I in the opposite direction (Fig. 3.6). This happens, for example, in a diode which we will study in Chapter 14.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22232,57,116,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image34.png'),
(22233,57,117,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.6 Characteristic curve of a diode. Note the different scales for negative and positive values of the voltage and current.','[{"s": "Figure 3.6 Characteristic curve of a diode. Note the different scales for negative and positive values of the voltage and current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22234,57,118,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image55.png'),
(22235,57,119,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.7 Variation of current versus voltage for GaAs.','[{"s": "Figure 3.7 Variation of current versus voltage for GaAs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22236,57,120,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The relation between V and I is not unique, i.e., there is more than one value of V for the same current I (Fig. 3.7). A material exhibiting such behaviour is GaAs.','[{"s": "(c) The relation between V and I is not unique, i.e., there is more than one value of V for the same current I (Fig. 3.7). A material exhibiting such behaviour is GaAs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22220,57,104,'paragraph',NULL,'An important quantity is the mobility µ defined as the magnitude of the drift velocity per unit electric field:','[{"s": "An important quantity is the mobility µ defined as the magnitude of the drift velocity per unit electric field:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041469", "2000042276", "2000110675"]',NULL),
(22237,57,121,'paragraph',NULL,'Materials and devices not obeying Ohm’s law in the form of Eq. (3.3) are actually widely used in electronic circuits. In this and a few subsequent chapters, however, we will study the electrical currents in materials that obey Ohm’s law.','[{"s": "Materials and devices not obeying Ohm’s law in the form of Eq. (3.3) are actually widely used in electronic circuits. In this and a few subsequent chapters, however, we will study the electrical currents in materials that obey Ohm’s law.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22238,57,122,'heading',3,'3.7 RESISTIVITY OF VARIOUS MATERIALS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22239,57,123,'paragraph',NULL,'The resistivities of various common materials are listed in Table 3.1. The materials are classified as conductors, semiconductors and insulators depending on their resistivities, in an increasing order of their values. Metals have low resistivities in the range of 10 –8 Ωm to 10 –6 Ωm. At the other end are insulators like ceramic, rubber and plastics having resistivities 10 18 times greater than metals or more. In between the two are the semiconductors. These, however, have resistivities characteristically decreasing with a rise in temperature. The resistivities of semiconductors are also affected by presence of small amount of impurities. This last feature is exploited in use of semiconductors for electronic devices.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "The resistivities of various common materials are listed in Table 3.1. The materials are classified as conductors, semiconductors and insulators depending on their resistivities, in an increasing order of their values. Metals have low resistivities in the range of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ωm to 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ωm. At the other end are insulators like ceramic, rubber and plastics having resistivities 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "times greater than metals or more. In between the two are the semiconductors. These, however, have resistivities characteristically decreasing with a rise in temperature. The resistivities of semiconductors are also affected by presence of small amount of impurities. This last feature is exploited in use of semiconductors for electronic devices.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22240,57,124,'image',NULL,'11',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image103.jpg'),
(22241,57,125,'paragraph',NULL,'Commercially produced resistors for domestic use or in laboratories are of two major types: wire bound resistors and carbon resistors. Wire bound resistors are made by winding the wires of an alloy, viz., manganin, constantan, nichrome or similar ones. The choice of these materials is dictated mostly by the fact that their resistivities are relatively insensitive to temperature. These resistances are typically in the range of a fraction of an ohm to a few hundred ohms.','[{"s": "Commercially produced resistors for domestic use or in laboratories are of two major types: wire bound resistors and carbon resistors. Wire bound resistors are made by winding the wires of an alloy, viz., manganin, constantan, nichrome or similar ones. The choice of these materials is dictated mostly by the fact that their resistivities are relatively insensitive to temperature. These resistances are typically in the range of a fraction of an ohm to a few hundred ohms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22242,57,126,'paragraph',NULL,'Resistors in the higher range are made mostly from carbon. Carbon resistors are compact, inexpensive and thus find extensive use in electronic circuits. Carbon resistors are small in size and hence their values are given using a colour code.','[{"s": "Resistors in the higher range are made mostly from carbon. Carbon resistors are compact, inexpensive and thus find extensive use in electronic circuits. Carbon resistors are small in size and hence their values are given using a colour code.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22243,57,127,'image',NULL,'12',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image91.jpg'),
(22245,57,129,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image86.png'),
(22247,57,131,'heading',3,'3.8 TEMPERATURE DEPENDENCE OF RESISTIVITY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22249,57,133,'paragraph',NULL,'ρ T = ρ 0 [1 + α (T–T 0 )] (3.26)','[{"s": "ρ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ρ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[1 + α (T–T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")] (3.26)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27818,68,525,'paragraph',NULL,'The acceleration a at any time t is the limiting value of Motion_in_a_Plane-image76.png as ∆ t →0 :','[{"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "acceleration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a at any time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the limiting value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Plane-image76.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "as ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→0 :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22250,57,134,'paragraph',NULL,'where ρ T is the resistivity at a temperature T and ρ 0 is the same at a reference temperature T 0 . α is called the temperature co-efficient of resistivity, and from Eq. (3.26), the dimension of α is (Temperature) –1 . For metals, α is positive and values of α for some metals at T 0 = 0°C are listed in Table 3.1.','[{"s": "where ρ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the resistivity at a temperature T and ρ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the same at a reference temperature T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". α is called the temperature co-efficient of resistivity, and from Eq. (3.26), the dimension of α is (Temperature)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For metals, α is positive and values of α for some metals at T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0°C are listed in Table 3.1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22251,57,135,'paragraph',NULL,'The relation of Eq. (3.26) implies that a graph of ρ T plotted against T would be a straight line. At temperatures much lower than 0°C, the graph, however, deviates considerably from a straight line (Fig. 3.9).','[{"s": "The relation of Eq. (3.26) implies that a graph of ρ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plotted against T would be a straight line. At temperatures much lower than 0°C, the graph, however, deviates considerably from a straight line (Fig. 3.9).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22253,57,137,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image20.png'),
(22254,57,138,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.9 Resistivity ρT of copper as a function of temperature T.','[{"s": "Figure 3.9 Resistivity ρT of copper as a function of temperature T.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22255,57,139,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image17.png'),
(22256,57,140,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.10 Resistivity ρT of nichrome as a function of absolute temperature T.','[{"s": "Figure 3.10 Resistivity ρT of nichrome as a function of absolute temperature T.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22257,57,141,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image102.png'),
(22258,57,142,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.11 Temperature dependence of resistivity for a typical semiconductor.','[{"s": "Figure 3.11 Temperature dependence of resistivity for a typical semiconductor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22259,57,143,'paragraph',NULL,'Some materials like Nichrome (which is an alloy of nickel, iron and chromium) exhibit a very weak dependence of resistivity with temperature (Fig. 3.10). Manganin and constantan have similar properties. These materials are thus widely used in wire bound standard resistors since their resistance values would change very little with temperatures.','[{"s": "Some materials like Nichrome (which is an alloy of nickel, iron and chromium) exhibit a very weak dependence of resistivity with temperature (Fig. 3.10). Manganin and constantan have similar properties. These materials are thus widely used in wire bound standard resistors since their resistance values would change very little with temperatures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22260,57,144,'paragraph',NULL,'Unlike metals, the resistivities of semiconductors decrease with increasing temperatures. A typical dependence is shown in Fig. 3.11.','[{"s": "Unlike metals, the resistivities of semiconductors decrease with increasing temperatures. A typical dependence is shown in Fig. 3.11.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22261,57,145,'paragraph',NULL,'We can qualitatively understand the temperature dependence of resistivity, in the light of our derivation of Eq. (3.23). From this equation, resistivity of a material is given by','[{"s": "We can qualitatively understand the temperature dependence of resistivity, in the light of our derivation of Eq. (3.23). From this equation, resistivity of a material is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22262,57,146,'paragraph',NULL,'Current_Electricity-image127.png (3.27)','[{"s": "Current_Electricity-image127.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.27)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22263,57,147,'paragraph',NULL,'ρ thus depends inversely both on the number n of free electrons per unit volume and on the average time τ between collisions. As we increase temperature, average speed of the electrons, which act as the carriers of current, increases resulting in more frequent collisions. The average time of collisions τ, thus decreases with temperature.','[{"s": "ρ thus depends inversely both on the number n of free electrons per unit volume and on the average time τ between collisions. As we increase temperature, average speed of the electrons, which act as the carriers of current, increases resulting in more frequent collisions. The average time of collisions τ, thus decreases with temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22265,57,149,'paragraph',NULL,'For insulators and semiconductors, however, n increases with temperature. This increase more than compensates any decrease in τ in Eq.(3.23) so that for such materials, ρ decreases with temperature.','[{"s": "For insulators and semiconductors, however, n increases with temperature. This increase more than compensates any decrease in τ in Eq.(3.23) so that for such materials, ρ decreases with temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27819,68,526,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image74.png'),
(27820,68,527,'paragraph',NULL,'In component form, we have :','[{"s": "In component form, we have :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27821,68,528,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-08 at 6.29.15 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image64.png'),
(27822,68,529,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image62.png'),
(27823,68,530,'paragraph',NULL,'15. If an object is moving in a plane with constant acceleration','[{"s": "15. If an object is moving in a plane with constant acceleration", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22267,57,151,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution When the current through the element is very small, heating effects can be ignored and the temperature T 1 of the element is the same as room temperature. When the toaster is connected to the supply, its initial current will be slightly higher than its steady value of 2.68 A. But due to heating effect of the current, the temperature will rise. This will cause an increase in resistance and a slight decrease in current. In a few seconds, a steady state will be reached when temperature will rise no further, and both the resistance of the element and the current drawn will achieve steady values. The resistance R 2 at the steady temperature T 2 is','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When the current through the element is very small, heating effects can be ignored and the temperature T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the element is the same as room temperature. When the toaster is connected to the supply, its initial current will be slightly higher than its steady value of 2.68 A. But due to heating effect of the current, the temperature will rise. This will cause an increase in resistance and a slight decrease in current. In a few seconds, a steady state will be reached when temperature will rise no further, and both the resistance of the element and the current drawn will achieve steady values. The resistance R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at the steady temperature T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22268,57,152,'paragraph',NULL,'R Current_Electricity-image48.png','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "Current_Electricity-image48.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22269,57,153,'paragraph',NULL,'Using the relation','[{"s": "Using the relation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22270,57,154,'paragraph',NULL,'R 2 = R 1 [1 + α (T 2 – T 1 )]','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[1 + α (T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22271,57,155,'paragraph',NULL,'with α = 1.70 × 10–4 °C –1 , we get','[{"s": "with α = 1.70 × 10–4 °C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22272,57,156,'paragraph',NULL,'T 2 – T Current_Electricity-image31.png = 820 °C','[{"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "Current_Electricity-image31.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 820 °C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22273,57,157,'paragraph',NULL,'that is, T 2 = (820 + 27.0) °C = 847 °C','[{"s": "that is, T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (820 + 27.0) °C = 847 °C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22274,57,158,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 3.4 The resistance of the platinum wire of a platinum resistance thermometer at the ice point is 5 Ω and at steam point is 5.39 Ω. When the thermometer is inserted in a hot bath, the resistance of the platinum wire is 5.795 Ω. Calculate the temperature of the bath.','[{"s": "Example 3.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The resistance of the platinum wire of a platinum resistance thermometer at the ice point is 5 Ω and at steam point is 5.39 Ω. When the thermometer is inserted in a hot bath, the resistance of the platinum wire is 5.795 Ω. Calculate the temperature of the bath.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22275,57,159,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution R 0 = 5 Ω, R 100 = 5.23 Ω and R t = 5.795 Ω','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5 Ω, R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "100", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5.23 Ω and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5.795 Ω", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22276,57,160,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, Current_Electricity-image113.png','[{"s": "Now,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Current_Electricity-image113.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22277,57,161,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image49.png'),
(22278,57,162,'paragraph',NULL,'= Current_Electricity-image82.png = 345.65 °C','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Current_Electricity-image82.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 345.65 °C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22279,57,163,'heading',3,'3.9 ELECTRICAL ENERGY, POWER',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22280,57,164,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a conductor with end points A and B, in which a current I is flowing from A to B. The electric potential at A and B are denoted by V(A) and V(B) respectively. Since current is flowing from A to B, V(A) > V(B) and the potential difference across AB is V = V(A) – V(B) > 0.','[{"s": "Consider a conductor with end points A and B, in which a current I is flowing from A to B. The electric potential at A and B are denoted by V(A) and V(B) respectively. Since current is flowing from A to B, V(A) > V(B) and the potential difference across AB is V = V(A) – V(B) > 0.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22281,57,165,'paragraph',NULL,'In a time interval ∆t, an amount of charge ∆Q = I ∆t travels from A to B. The potential energy of the charge at A, by definition, was Q V(A) and similarly at B, it is Q V(B). Thus, change in its potential energy ∆Upot is ∆Upot = Final potential energy – Initial potential energy','[{"s": "In a time interval ∆t, an amount of charge ∆Q = I ∆t travels from A to B. The potential energy of the charge at A, by definition, was Q V(A) and similarly at B, it is Q V(B). Thus, change in its potential energy ∆Upot is ∆Upot = Final potential energy – Initial potential energy", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22282,57,166,'paragraph',NULL,'= ∆Q[(V (B) – V (A)] = –∆Q V','[{"s": "= ∆Q[(V (B) – V (A)] = –∆Q V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22283,57,167,'paragraph',NULL,'= –I V∆t < 0 (3.28)','[{"s": "= –I V∆t < 0 (3.28)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22284,57,168,'paragraph',NULL,'If charges moved without collisions through the conductor, their kinetic energy would also change so that the total energy is unchanged. Conservation of total energy would then imply that,','[{"s": "If charges moved without collisions through the conductor, their kinetic energy would also change so that the total energy is unchanged. Conservation of total energy would then imply that,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22285,57,169,'paragraph',NULL,'∆K = –∆Upot (3.29)','[{"s": "∆K = –∆Upot (3.29)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22286,57,170,'paragraph',NULL,'that is,','[{"s": "that is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22287,57,171,'paragraph',NULL,'∆K = I V∆t > 0 (3.30)','[{"s": "∆K = I V∆t > 0 (3.30)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22289,57,173,'paragraph',NULL,'∆W = I V∆t (3.31)','[{"s": "∆W = I V∆t (3.31)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22290,57,174,'paragraph',NULL,'P = I V (3.32)','[{"s": "P = I V (3.32)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22291,57,175,'paragraph',NULL,'Using Ohm’s law V = IR, we get','[{"s": "Using Ohm’s law V = IR, we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22292,57,176,'paragraph',NULL,'P = I 2 R = V 2 /R (3.33)','[{"s": "P = I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R = V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/R (3.33)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22294,57,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Where does the power come from? As we have reasoned before, we need an external source to keep a steady current through the conductor. It is clearly this source which must supply this power. In the simple circuit shown with a cell (Fig.3.12), it is the chemical energy of the cell which supplies this power for as long as it can.','[{"s": "Where does the power come from? As we have reasoned before, we need an external source to keep a steady current through the conductor. It is clearly this source which must supply this power. In the simple circuit shown with a cell (Fig.3.12), it is the chemical energy of the cell which supplies this power for as long as it can.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22295,57,179,'paragraph',NULL,'The expressions for power, Eqs. (3.32) and (3.33), show the dependence of the power dissipated in a resistor R on the current through it and the voltage across it.','[{"s": "The expressions for power, Eqs. (3.32) and (3.33), show the dependence of the power dissipated in a resistor R on the current through it and the voltage across it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22296,57,180,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image105.png'),
(22297,57,181,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.12 Heat is produced in the resistor R which is connected across the terminals of a cell. The energy dissipated in the resistor R comes from the chemical energy of the electrolyte.','[{"s": "Figure 3.12 Heat is produced in the resistor R which is connected across the terminals of a cell. The energy dissipated in the resistor R comes from the chemical energy of the electrolyte.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22298,57,182,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (3.33) has an important application to power transmission. Electrical power is transmitted from power stations to homes and factories, which may be hundreds of miles away, via transmission cables. One obviously wants to minimise the power loss in the transmission cables connecting the power stations to homes and factories. We shall see now how this can be achieved. Consider a device R, to which a power P is to be delivered via transmission cables having a resistance Rc to be dissipated by it finally. If V is the voltage across R and I the current through it, then','[{"s": "Equation (3.33) has an important application to power transmission. Electrical power is transmitted from power stations to homes and factories, which may be hundreds of miles away, via transmission cables. One obviously wants to minimise the power loss in the transmission cables connecting the power stations to homes and factories. We shall see now how this can be achieved. Consider a device R, to which a power P is to be delivered via transmission cables having a resistance Rc to be dissipated by it finally. If V is the voltage across R and I the current through it, then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22299,57,183,'paragraph',NULL,'P = V I (3.34)','[{"s": "P = V I (3.34)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22300,57,184,'paragraph',NULL,'The connecting wires from the power station to the device has a finite resistance R c . The power dissipated in the connecting wires, which is wasted is P c with','[{"s": "The connecting wires from the power station to the device has a finite resistance R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The power dissipated in the connecting wires, which is wasted is P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22301,57,185,'paragraph',NULL,'P c = I 2 R c','[{"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22302,57,186,'paragraph',NULL,'Current_Electricity-image123.png (3.35)','[{"s": "Current_Electricity-image123.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.35)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22303,57,187,'paragraph',NULL,'from Eq. (3.32). Thus, to drive a device of power P, the power wasted in the connecting wires is inversely proportional to V 2 . The transmission cables from power stations are hundreds of miles long and their resistance Rc is considerable. To reduce P c , these wires carry current at enormous values of V and this is the reason for the high voltage danger signs on transmission lines — a common sight as we move away from populated areas. Using electricity at such voltages is not safe and hence at the other end, a device called a transformer lowers the voltage to a value suitable for use.','[{"s": "from Eq. (3.32). Thus, to drive a device of power P, the power wasted in the connecting wires is inversely proportional to V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The transmission cables from power stations are hundreds of miles long and their resistance Rc is considerable. To reduce P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", these wires carry current at enormous values of V and this is the reason for the high voltage danger signs on transmission lines — a common sight as we move away from populated areas. Using electricity at such voltages is not safe and hence at the other end, a device called a transformer lowers the voltage to a value suitable for use.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22304,57,188,'heading',3,'3.10 COMBINATION OF RESISTORS – SERIES AND PARALLEL',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22305,57,189,'paragraph',NULL,'The current through a single resistor R across which there is a potential difference V is given by Ohm’s law I = V/R. Resistors are sometimes joined together and there are simple rules for calculation of equivalent resistance of such combination.','[{"s": "The current through a single resistor R across which there is a potential difference V is given by Ohm’s law I = V/R. Resistors are sometimes joined together and there are simple rules for calculation of equivalent resistance of such combination.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22306,57,190,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image11.png'),
(22307,57,191,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.13 A series combination of two resistors R 1 and R 2 .','[{"s": "Figure 3.13 A series combination of two resistors R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22309,57,193,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image29.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(22310,57,194,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.14 A series combination of three resistors R 1 , R 2 , R 3 .','[{"s": "Figure 3.14 A series combination of three resistors R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22312,57,196,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image7.png'),
(22313,57,197,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.15 Two resistors R 1 and R 2 connected in parallel.','[{"s": "Figure 3.15 Two resistors R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "connected in parallel.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22314,57,198,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider two resistors R 1 and R 2 in series. The charge which leaves R 1 must be entering R 2 . Since current measures the rate of flow of charge, this means that the same current I flows through R 1 and R 2 . By Ohm’s law:','[{"s": "Consider two resistors R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in series. The charge which leaves R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "must be entering R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since current measures the rate of flow of charge, this means that the same current I flows through R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". By Ohm’s law:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22315,57,199,'paragraph',NULL,'Potential difference across R 1 = V 1 = I R 1 , and','[{"s": "Potential difference across R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22316,57,200,'paragraph',NULL,'Potential difference across R 2 = V 2 = I R 2 .','[{"s": "Potential difference across R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22317,57,201,'paragraph',NULL,'The potential difference V across the combination is V 1 +V 2 . Hence,','[{"s": "The potential difference V across the combination is V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Hence,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22318,57,202,'paragraph',NULL,'V = V 1 + V 2 = I (R 1 + R 2 ) (3.36)','[{"s": "V = V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I (R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (3.36)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22319,57,203,'paragraph',NULL,'This is as if the combination had an equivalent resistance R eq , which by Ohm’s law is','[{"s": "This is as if the combination had an equivalent resistance R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eq", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which by Ohm’s law is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22320,57,204,'paragraph',NULL,'Req Current_Electricity-image40.png = (R 1 + R 2 ) (3.37)','[{"s": "Req", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Current_Electricity-image40.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= (R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (3.37)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22321,57,205,'paragraph',NULL,'If we had three resistors connected in series, then similarly','[{"s": "If we had three resistors connected in series, then similarly", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22322,57,206,'paragraph',NULL,'V = I R 1 + I R 2 + I R 3 = I (R 1 + R 2 + R 3 ). (3.38)','[{"s": "V = I R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ I R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ I R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I (R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). (3.38)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22323,57,207,'paragraph',NULL,'This obviously can be extended to a series combination of any number n of resistors R 1 , R 2 ....., R n . The equivalent resistance R eq is','[{"s": "This obviously can be extended to a series combination of any number n of resistors R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "....., R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The equivalent resistance R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eq", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22324,57,208,'paragraph',NULL,'R eq = R 1 + R 2 + . . . + R n (3.39)','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eq", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ . . . + R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.39)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22325,57,209,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider now the parallel combination of two resistors (Fig. 3.15). The charge that flows in at A from the left flows out partly through R 1 and partly through R 2 . The currents I, I 1 , I 2 shown in the figure are the rates of flow of charge at the points indicated. Hence,','[{"s": "Consider now the parallel combination of two resistors (Fig. 3.15). The charge that flows in at A from the left flows out partly through R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and partly through R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The currents I, I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "shown in the figure are the rates of flow of charge at the points indicated. Hence,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22326,57,210,'paragraph',NULL,'I = I 1 + I 2 (3.40)','[{"s": "I = I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.40)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22327,57,211,'paragraph',NULL,'The potential difference between A and B is given by the Ohm’s law applied to R 1','[{"s": "The potential difference between A and B is given by the Ohm’s law applied to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22328,57,212,'paragraph',NULL,'V = I 2 R 1 (3.41)','[{"s": "V = I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.41)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22329,57,213,'paragraph',NULL,'Also, Ohm’s law applied to R 2 gives','[{"s": "Also, Ohm’s law applied to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22330,57,214,'paragraph',NULL,'V = I 2 R 2 (3.42)','[{"s": "V = I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.42)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
