(22469,57,353,'paragraph',NULL,'Gustav Robert Kirchhoff (1824 – 1887) German physicist, professor at Heidelberg and at Berlin. Mainly known for his development of spectroscopy, he also made many important contributions to mathe- matical physics, among them, his first and second rules for circuits.','[{"s": "Gustav Robert Kirchhoff (1824 – 1887)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "German physicist, professor at Heidelberg and at Berlin. Mainly known for his development of spectroscopy, he also made many important contributions to mathe- matical physics, among them, his first and second rules for circuits.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22470,57,354,'paragraph',NULL,'In Fig. (3.21), we had joined the positive terminals together and similarly the two negative ones, so that the currents I 1 , I 2 flow out of positive terminals. If the negative terminal of the second is connected to positive terminal of the first, Eqs. (3.75) and (3.76) would still be valid with ε 2 → –ε 2','[{"s": "In Fig. (3.21), we had joined the positive terminals together and similarly the two negative ones, so that the currents I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "flow out of positive terminals. If the negative terminal of the second is connected to positive terminal of the first, Eqs. (3.75) and (3.76) would still be valid with ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ –ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22471,57,355,'paragraph',NULL,'Equations (3.75) and (3.76) can be extended easily. If there are n cells of emf ε 1 , . . . ε n and of internal resistances r 1 ,... r n respectively, connected in parallel, the combination is equivalent to a single cell of emf ε eq and internal resistance r eq , such that','[{"s": "Equations (3.75) and (3.76) can be extended easily. If there are n cells of emf ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", . . . ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and of internal resistances r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",... r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively, connected in parallel, the combination is equivalent to a single cell of emf ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eq", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and internal resistance r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eq", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", such that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22472,57,356,'paragraph',NULL,'Current_Electricity-image46.png (3.77)','[{"s": "Current_Electricity-image46.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.77)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22473,57,357,'paragraph',NULL,'Current_Electricity-image16.png (3.78)','[{"s": "Current_Electricity-image16.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.78)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22474,57,358,'heading',3,'3.13 KIRCHHOFF’S RULES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22475,57,359,'paragraph',NULL,'Electric circuits generally consist of a number of resistors and cells interconnected sometimes in a complicated way. The formulae we have derived earlier for series and parallel combinations of resistors are not always sufficient to determine all the currents and potential differences in the circuit. Two rules, called Kirchhoff’s rules, are very useful for analysis of electric circuits.','[{"s": "Electric circuits generally consist of a number of resistors and cells interconnected sometimes in a complicated way. The formulae we have derived earlier for series and parallel combinations of resistors are not always sufficient to determine all the currents and potential differences in the circuit. Two rules, called Kirchhoff’s rules, are very useful for analysis of electric circuits.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22477,57,361,'paragraph',NULL,'V = ε + I r (3.79)','[{"s": "V = ε + I r (3.79)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22478,57,362,'paragraph',NULL,'Having clarified labelling, we now state the rules and the proof:','[{"s": "Having clarified labelling, we now state the rules and the proof:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22479,57,363,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Junction rule: At any junction, the sum of the currents entering the junction is equal to the sum of currents leaving the junction (Fig. 3.22).','[{"s": "(a) Junction rule: At any junction, the sum of the currents entering the junction is equal to the sum of currents leaving the junction (Fig. 3.22).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22480,57,364,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image70.png'),
(27834,68,541,'paragraph',NULL,'Motion in a plane can be treated as superposition of two separate simultaneous one-dimensional motions along two perpendicular directions','[{"s": "Motion in a plane can be treated as superposition of two separate simultaneous one-dimensional motions along two perpendicular directions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34853,80,286,'paragraph',NULL,'v / 2L , corresponding to n = 1 of Eq. (15.42). Here v is the speed of wave determined by the properties of the medium. The n = 2 frequency is called the second harmonic; n = 3 is the third harmonic and so on. We can label the various harmonics by the symbol νn ( n = 1, 2, ...).','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2L", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", corresponding to n = 1 of Eq. (15.42). Here v is the speed of wave determined by the properties of the medium. The n = 2 frequency is called the second harmonic; n = 3 is the third harmonic and so on. We can label the various harmonics by the symbol νn ( n = 1, 2, ...).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22482,57,366,'paragraph',NULL,'This applies equally well if instead of a junction of several lines, we consider a point in a line.','[{"s": "This applies equally well if instead of a junction of several lines, we consider a point in a line.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22483,57,367,'paragraph',NULL,'The proof of this rule follows from the fact that when currents are steady, there is no accumulation of charges at any junction or at any point in a line. Thus, the total current flowing in, (which is the rate at which charge flows into the junction), must equal the total current flowing out.','[{"s": "The proof of this rule follows from the fact that when currents are steady, there is no accumulation of charges at any junction or at any point in a line. Thus, the total current flowing in, (which is the rate at which charge flows into the junction), must equal the total current flowing out.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22485,57,369,'paragraph',NULL,'This rule is also obvious, since electric potential is dependent on the location of the point. Thus starting with any point if we come back to the same point, the total change must be zero. In a closed loop, we do come back to the starting point and hence the rule.','[{"s": "This rule is also obvious, since electric potential is dependent on the location of the point. Thus starting with any point if we come back to the same point, the total change must be zero. In a closed loop, we do come back to the starting point and hence the rule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22486,57,370,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 3.6 A battery of 10 V and negligible internal resistance is connected across the diagonally opposite corners of a cubical network consisting of 12 resistors each of resistance 1 Ω (Fig. 3.23). Determine the equivalent resistance of the network and the current along each edge of the cube.','[{"s": "Example 3.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A battery of 10 V and negligible internal resistance is connected across the diagonally opposite corners of a cubical network consisting of 12 resistors each of resistance 1 Ω (Fig. 3.23). Determine the equivalent resistance of the network and the current along each edge of the cube.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22487,57,371,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image109.png'),
(22488,57,372,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.23','[{"s": "Figure 3.23", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22489,57,373,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution The network is not reducible to a simple series and parallel combinations of resistors. There is, however, a clear symmetry in the problem which we can exploit to obtain the equivalent resistance of the network.','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The network is not reducible to a simple series and parallel combinations of resistors. There is, however, a clear symmetry in the problem which we can exploit to obtain the equivalent resistance of the network.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22490,57,374,'paragraph',NULL,'The paths AA′, AD and AB are obviously symmetrically placed in the network. Thus, the current in each must be the same, say, I. Further, at the corners A′, B and D, the incoming current I must split equally into the two outgoing branches. In this manner, the current in all the 12 edges of the cube are easily written down in terms of I, using Kirchhoff’s first rule and the symmetry in the problem.','[{"s": "The paths AA′, AD and AB are obviously symmetrically placed in the network. Thus, the current in each must be the same, say, I. Further, at the corners A′, B and D, the incoming current I must split equally into the two outgoing branches. In this manner, the current in all the 12 edges of the cube are easily written down in terms of I, using Kirchhoff’s first rule and the symmetry in the problem.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22491,57,375,'paragraph',NULL,'Next take a closed loop, say, ABCC′EA, and apply Kirchhoff’s second rule:','[{"s": "Next take a closed loop, say, ABCC′EA, and apply Kirchhoff’s second rule:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22492,57,376,'paragraph',NULL,'–IR – (1/2)IR – IR + ε = 0','[{"s": "–IR – (1/2)IR – IR + ε = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22493,57,377,'paragraph',NULL,'where R is the resistance of each edge and ε the emf of battery. Thus,','[{"s": "where R is the resistance of each edge and ε the emf of battery. Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22494,57,378,'paragraph',NULL,'ε = Current_Electricity-image8.png','[{"s": "ε =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Current_Electricity-image8.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22495,57,379,'paragraph',NULL,'The equivalent resistance Req of the network is','[{"s": "The equivalent resistance Req of the network is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22496,57,380,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image59.png'),
(22497,57,381,'paragraph',NULL,'For R = 1 Ω, Req = (5/6) Ω and for ε = 10 V, the total current (= 3I) in the network is 3I = 10 V/(5/6) Ω = 12 A, i.e., I = 4 A','[{"s": "For R = 1 Ω, Req = (5/6) Ω and for ε = 10 V, the total current (= 3I) in the network is 3I = 10 V/(5/6) Ω = 12 A, i.e., I = 4 A", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22498,57,382,'paragraph',NULL,'The current flowing in each edge can now be read off from the Fig. 3.23.','[{"s": "The current flowing in each edge can now be read off from the Fig. 3.23.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22499,57,383,'paragraph',NULL,'It should be noted that because of the symmetry of the network, the great power of Kirchhoff’s rules has not been very apparent in Example 3.6. In a general network, there will be no such simplification due to symmetry, and only by application of Kirchhoff’s rules to junctions and closed loops (as many as necessary to solve the unknowns in the network) can we handle the problem. This will be illustrated in Example 3.7.','[{"s": "It should be noted that because of the symmetry of the network, the great power of Kirchhoff’s rules has not been very apparent in Example 3.6. In a general network, there will be no such simplification due to symmetry, and only by application of Kirchhoff’s rules to junctions and closed loops (as many as necessary to solve the unknowns in the network) can we handle the problem. This will be illustrated in Example 3.7.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22500,57,384,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 3.7 Determine the current in each branch of the network shown in Fig. 3.24.','[{"s": "Example 3.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Determine the current in each branch of the network shown in Fig. 3.24.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
