(22502,57,386,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.24','[{"s": "Figure 3.24", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22503,57,387,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution Each branch of the network is assigned an unknown current to be determined by the application of Kirchhoff’s rules. To reduce the number of unknowns at the outset, the first rule of Kirchhoff is used at every junction to assign the unknown current in each branch. We then have three unknowns I1, I2 and I3 which can be found by applying the second rule of Kirchhoff to three different closed loops. Kirchhoff’s second rule for the closed loop ADCA gives,','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Each branch of the network is assigned an unknown current to be determined by the application of Kirchhoff’s rules. To reduce the number of unknowns at the outset, the first rule of Kirchhoff is used at every junction to assign the unknown current in each branch. We then have three unknowns I1, I2 and I3 which can be found by applying the second rule of Kirchhoff to three different closed loops. Kirchhoff’s second rule for the closed loop ADCA gives,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22504,57,388,'image',NULL,'13',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image79.jpg'),
(22505,57,389,'heading',3,'3.14 WHEATSTONE BRIDGE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22506,57,390,'paragraph',NULL,'As an application of Kirchhoff’s rules consider the circuit shown in Fig. 3.25, which is called the Wheatstone bridge. The bridge has four resistors R 1 , R 2 , R 3 and R 4 . Across one pair of diagonally opposite points (A and C in the figure) a source is connected. This (i.e., AC) is called the battery arm. Between the other two vertices, B and D, a galvanometer G (which is a device to detect currents) is connected. This line, shown as BD in the figure, is called the galvanometer arm.','[{"s": "As an application of Kirchhoff’s rules consider the circuit shown in Fig. 3.25, which is called the Wheatstone bridge. The bridge has four resistors R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Across one pair of diagonally opposite points (A and C in the figure) a source is connected. This (i.e., AC) is called the battery arm. Between the other two vertices, B and D, a galvanometer G (which is a device to detect currents) is connected. This line, shown as BD in the figure, is called the galvanometer arm.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22507,57,391,'paragraph',NULL,'For simplicity, we assume that the cell has no internal resistance. In general there will be currents flowing across all the resistors as well as a current Ig through G. Of special interest, is the case of a balanced bridge where the resistors are such that Ig = 0. We can easily get the balance condition, such that there is no current through G. In this case, the Kirchhoff’s junction rule applied to junctions D and B (see the figure) immediately gives us the relations I 1 = I 3 and I 2 = I 4 . Next, we apply Kirchhoff’s loop rule to closed loops ADBA and CBDC. The first loop gives','[{"s": "For simplicity, we assume that the cell has no internal resistance. In general there will be currents flowing across all the resistors as well as a current Ig through G. Of special interest, is the case of a balanced bridge where the resistors are such that Ig = 0. We can easily get the balance condition, such that there is no current through G. In this case, the Kirchhoff’s junction rule applied to junctions D and B (see the figure) immediately gives us the relations I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Next, we apply Kirchhoff’s loop rule to closed loops ADBA and CBDC. The first loop gives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22508,57,392,'paragraph',NULL,'–I 1 R 1 + 0 + I 2 R 2 = 0 (I g = 0) (3.81)','[{"s": "–I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 0 + I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 (I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0) (3.81)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22509,57,393,'paragraph',NULL,'and the second loop gives, upon using I 3 = I 1 , I 4 = I 2','[{"s": "and the second loop gives, upon using I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22510,57,394,'paragraph',NULL,'I 2 R 4 + 0 – I 1 R 3 = 0 (3.82)','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 0 – I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 (3.82)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22511,57,395,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eq. (3.81), we obtain,','[{"s": "From Eq. (3.81), we obtain,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
