(22606,57,490,'paragraph',NULL,'.','[{"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22607,57,491,'heading',3,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22608,57,492,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Current through a given area of a conductor is the net charge passing per unit time through the area.','[{"s": "1. Current through a given area of a conductor is the net charge passing per unit time through the area.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22609,57,493,'paragraph',NULL,'2. To maintain a steady current, we must have a closed circuit in which an external agency moves electric charge from lower to higher potential energy. The work done per unit charge by the source in taking the charge from lower to higher potential energy (i.e., from one terminal of the source to the other) is called the electromotive force, or emf, of the source. Note that the emf is not a force; it is the voltage difference between the two terminals of a source in open circuit.','[{"s": "2. To maintain a steady current, we must have a closed circuit in which an external agency moves electric charge from lower to higher potential energy. The work done per unit charge by the source in taking the charge from lower to higher potential energy (i.e., from one terminal of the source to the other) is called the electromotive force, or emf, of the source. Note that the emf is not a force; it is the voltage difference between the two terminals of a source in open circuit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22610,57,494,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Ohm’s law: The electric current I flowing through a substance is proportional to the voltage V across its ends, i.e., V ∝ I or V = RI, where R is called the resistance of the substance. The unit of resistance is ohm: 1Ω = 1 V A–1.','[{"s": "3. Ohm’s law: The electric current I flowing through a substance is proportional to the voltage V across its ends, i.e., V ∝ I or V = RI, where R is called the resistance of the substance. The unit of resistance is ohm: 1Ω = 1 V A–1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22611,57,495,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image83.png'),
(22612,57,496,'paragraph',NULL,'where ρ, called resistivity is a property of the material and depends on temperature and pressure.','[{"s": "where ρ, called resistivity is a property of the material and depends on temperature and pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22613,57,497,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Electrical resistivity of substances varies over a very wide range. Metals have low resistivity, in the range of 10–8 Ω m to 10–6 Ω m. Insulators like glass and rubber have 1022 to 1024 times greater resistivity. Semiconductors like Si and Ge lie roughly in the middle range of resistivity on a logarithmic scale.','[{"s": "5. Electrical resistivity of substances varies over a very wide range. Metals have low resistivity, in the range of 10–8 Ω m to 10–6 Ω m. Insulators like glass and rubber have 1022 to 1024 times greater resistivity. Semiconductors like Si and Ge lie roughly in the middle range of resistivity on a logarithmic scale.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22614,57,498,'paragraph',NULL,'6. In most substances, the carriers of current are electrons; in some cases, for example, ionic crystals and electrolytic liquids, positive and negative ions carry the electric current.','[{"s": "6. In most substances, the carriers of current are electrons; in some cases, for example, ionic crystals and electrolytic liquids, positive and negative ions carry the electric current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22615,57,499,'paragraph',NULL,'7. Current density j gives the amount of charge flowing per second per unit area normal to the flow,','[{"s": "7. Current density j gives the amount of charge flowing per second per unit area normal to the flow,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22616,57,500,'paragraph',NULL,'j = nq vd','[{"s": "j = nq vd", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22617,57,501,'paragraph',NULL,'where n is the number density (number per unit volume) of charge carriers each of charge q, and vd is the drift velocity of the charge carriers. For electrons q = – e. If j is normal to a cross-sectional area A and is constant over the area, the magnitude of the current I through the area is nevd A.','[{"s": "where n is the number density (number per unit volume) of charge carriers each of charge q, and vd is the drift velocity of the charge carriers. For electrons q = – e. If j is normal to a cross-sectional area A and is constant over the area, the magnitude of the current I through the area is nevd A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22618,57,502,'paragraph',NULL,'8. Using E = V/l, I = nevd A, and Ohm’s law, one obtains','[{"s": "8. Using E = V/l, I = nevd A, and Ohm’s law, one obtains", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22619,57,503,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image117.png'),
(22620,57,504,'paragraph',NULL,'The proportionality between the force eE on the electrons in a metal due to the external field E and the drift velocity vd (not acceleration) can be understood, if we assume that the electrons suffer collisions with ions in the metal, which deflect them randomly. If such collisions occur on an average at a time interval τ,','[{"s": "The proportionality between the force eE on the electrons in a metal due to the external field E and the drift velocity vd (not acceleration) can be understood, if we assume that the electrons suffer collisions with ions in the metal, which deflect them randomly. If such collisions occur on an average at a time interval τ,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22621,57,505,'paragraph',NULL,'vd = aτ = eEτ/m','[{"s": "vd = aτ = eEτ/m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22622,57,506,'paragraph',NULL,'where a is the acceleration of the electron. This gives','[{"s": "where a is the acceleration of the electron. This gives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22623,57,507,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image58.png'),
(22624,57,508,'paragraph',NULL,'9. In the temperature range in which resistivity increases linearly with temperature, the temperature coefficient of resistivity α is defined as the fractional increase in resistivity per unit increase in temperature.','[{"s": "9. In the temperature range in which resistivity increases linearly with temperature, the temperature coefficient of resistivity α is defined as the fractional increase in resistivity per unit increase in temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22625,57,509,'paragraph',NULL,'10. Ohm’s law is obeyed by many substances, but it is not a fundamental law of nature. It fails if','[{"s": "10. Ohm’s law is obeyed by many substances, but it is not a fundamental law of nature. It fails if", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22626,57,510,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) V depends on I non-linearly.','[{"s": "(a) V depends on I non-linearly.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22627,57,511,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) the relation between V and I depends on the sign of V for the same absolute value of V.','[{"s": "(b) the relation between V and I depends on the sign of V for the same absolute value of V.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22628,57,512,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The relation between V and I is non-unique.','[{"s": "(c) The relation between V and I is non-unique.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22629,57,513,'paragraph',NULL,'An example of (a) is when ρ increases with I (even if temperature is kept fixed). A rectifier combines features (a) and (b). GaAs shows the feature (c).','[{"s": "An example of (a) is when ρ increases with I (even if temperature is kept fixed). A rectifier combines features (a) and (b). GaAs shows the feature (c).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22630,57,514,'paragraph',NULL,'11. When a source of emf ε is connected to an external resistance R, the voltage Vext across R is given by','[{"s": "11. When a source of emf ε is connected to an external resistance R, the voltage Vext across R is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22631,57,515,'paragraph',NULL,'Vext = IR = Current_Electricity-image106.png','[{"s": "Vext = IR =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Current_Electricity-image106.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22632,57,516,'paragraph',NULL,'where r is the internal resistance of the source.','[{"s": "where r is the internal resistance of the source.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22633,57,517,'paragraph',NULL,'12. (a) Total resistance R of n resistors connected in series is given by','[{"s": "12. (a) Total resistance R of n resistors connected in series is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22634,57,518,'paragraph',NULL,'R = R1 + R2 +..... + Rn','[{"s": "R = R1 + R2 +..... + Rn", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22635,57,519,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Total resistance R of n resistors connected in parallel is given by','[{"s": "(b) Total resistance R of n resistors connected in parallel is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22636,57,520,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image5.png'),
(22637,57,521,'paragraph',NULL,'13. Kirchhoff’s Rules –','[{"s": "13. Kirchhoff’s Rules –", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22638,57,522,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Junction Rule: At any junction of circuit elements, the sum of currents entering the junction must equal the sum of currents leaving it.','[{"s": "(a) Junction Rule: At any junction of circuit elements, the sum of currents entering the junction must equal the sum of currents leaving it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22640,57,524,'paragraph',NULL,'14. The Wheatstone bridge is an arrangement of four resistances – R1, R2, R3, R4 as shown in the text. The null-point condition is given by','[{"s": "14. The Wheatstone bridge is an arrangement of four resistances – R1, R2, R3, R4 as shown in the text. The null-point condition is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22641,57,525,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image6.png'),
(22642,57,526,'paragraph',NULL,'using which the value of one resistance can be determined, knowing the other three resistances.','[{"s": "using which the value of one resistance can be determined, knowing the other three resistances.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22643,57,527,'paragraph',NULL,'15. The potentiometer is a device to compare potential differences. Since the method involves a condition of no current flow, the device can be used to measure potential difference; internal resistance of a cell and compare emf’s of two sources.','[{"s": "15. The potentiometer is a device to compare potential differences. Since the method involves a condition of no current flow, the device can be used to measure potential difference; internal resistance of a cell and compare emf’s of two sources.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22644,57,528,'paragraph',NULL,'Physical Quantity - Symbols - Dimensions - Unit - Remark','[{"s": "Physical Quantity - Symbols - Dimensions - Unit - Remark", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22645,57,529,'image',NULL,'14',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Current_Electricity-image99.jpg'),
(22646,57,530,'heading',3,'POINTS TO PONDER',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22647,57,531,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Current is a scalar although we represent current with an arrow. Currents do not obey the law of vector addition. That current is a scalar also follows from it’s definition. The current I through an area of cross-section is given by the scalar product of two vectors:','[{"s": "1. Current is a scalar although we represent current with an arrow. Currents do not obey the law of vector addition. That current is a scalar also follows from it’s definition. The current I through an area of cross-section is given by the scalar product of two vectors:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22648,57,532,'paragraph',NULL,'I = j . ∆S','[{"s": "I = j . ∆S", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22649,57,533,'paragraph',NULL,'where j and ∆S are vectors.','[{"s": "where j and ∆S are vectors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22650,57,534,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Refer to V-I curves of a resistor and a diode as drawn in the text. A resistor obeys Ohm’s law while a diode does not. The assertion that V = IR is a statement of Ohm’s law is not true. This equation defines resistance and it may be applied to all conducting devices whether they obey Ohm’s law or not. The Ohm’s law asserts that the plot of I versusV is linear i.e., R is independent of V.','[{"s": "2. Refer to V-I curves of a resistor and a diode as drawn in the text. A resistor obeys Ohm’s law while a diode does not. The assertion that V = IR is a statement of Ohm’s law is not true. This equation defines resistance and it may be applied to all conducting devices whether they obey Ohm’s law or not. The Ohm’s law asserts that the plot of I versusV is linear i.e., R is independent of V.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22651,57,535,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation E = ρ j leads to another statement of Ohm’s law, i.e., a conducting material obeys Ohm’s law when the resistivity of the material does not depend on the magnitude and direction of applied electric field.','[{"s": "Equation E = ρ j leads to another statement of Ohm’s law, i.e., a conducting material obeys Ohm’s law when the resistivity of the material does not depend on the magnitude and direction of applied electric field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22652,57,536,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Homogeneous conductors like silver or semiconductors like pure germanium or germanium containing impurities obey Ohm’s law within some range of electric field values. If the field becomes too strong, there are departures from Ohm’s law in all cases.','[{"s": "3. Homogeneous conductors like silver or semiconductors like pure germanium or germanium containing impurities obey Ohm’s law within some range of electric field values. If the field becomes too strong, there are departures from Ohm’s law in all cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22653,57,537,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Motion of conduction electrons in electric field E is the sum of (i) motion due to random collisions and (ii) that due to E. The motion due to random collisions averages to zero and does not contribute to vd(Chapter 11, Textbook of Class XI). vd , thus is only due to applied electric field on the electron.','[{"s": "4. Motion of conduction electrons in electric field E is the sum of (i) motion due to random collisions and (ii) that due to E. The motion due to random collisions averages to zero and does not contribute to vd(Chapter 11, Textbook of Class XI). vd , thus is only due to applied electric field on the electron.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22654,57,538,'paragraph',NULL,'5. The relation j = ρ v should be applied to each type of charge carriers separately. In a conducting wire, the total current and charge density arises from both positive and negative charges:','[{"s": "5. The relation j = ρ v should be applied to each type of charge carriers separately. In a conducting wire, the total current and charge density arises from both positive and negative charges:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22655,57,539,'paragraph',NULL,'j = ρ+ v+ + ρ– v–','[{"s": "j = ρ+ v+ + ρ– v–", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22656,57,540,'paragraph',NULL,'ρ = ρ+ + ρ–','[{"s": "ρ = ρ+ + ρ–", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22657,57,541,'paragraph',NULL,'Now in a neutral wire carrying electric current,','[{"s": "Now in a neutral wire carrying electric current,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22658,57,542,'paragraph',NULL,'ρ+ = – ρ–','[{"s": "ρ+ = – ρ–", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22659,57,543,'paragraph',NULL,'Further, v+ ~ 0 which gives','[{"s": "Further, v+ ~ 0 which gives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22660,57,544,'paragraph',NULL,'ρ = 0','[{"s": "ρ = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22661,57,545,'paragraph',NULL,'j = ρ– v','[{"s": "j = ρ– v", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22662,57,546,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the relation j = ρ v does not apply to the total current charge density.','[{"s": "Thus, the relation j = ρ v does not apply to the total current charge density.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22664,57,548,'heading',3,'EXERCISES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22665,57,549,'paragraph',NULL,'3.1 The storage battery of a car has an emf of 12 V. If the internal resistance of the battery is 0.4 Ω, what is the maximum current that can be drawn from the battery?','[{"s": "3.1 The storage battery of a car has an emf of 12 V. If the internal resistance of the battery is 0.4 Ω, what is the maximum current that can be drawn from the battery?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22666,57,550,'paragraph',NULL,'3.2 A battery of emf 10 V and internal resistance 3 Ω is connected to a resistor. If the current in the circuit is 0.5 A, what is the resistance of the resistor? What is the terminal voltage of the battery when the circuit is closed?','[{"s": "3.2 A battery of emf 10 V and internal resistance 3 Ω is connected to a resistor. If the current in the circuit is 0.5 A, what is the resistance of the resistor? What is the terminal voltage of the battery when the circuit is closed?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22667,57,551,'paragraph',NULL,'3.3 (a) Three resistors 1 Ω, 2 Ω, and 3 Ω are combined in series. What is the total resistance of the combination?','[{"s": "3.3 (a) Three resistors 1 Ω, 2 Ω, and 3 Ω are combined in series. What is the total resistance of the combination?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22668,57,552,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) If the combination is connected to a battery of emf 12 V and negligible internal resistance, obtain the potential drop across each resistor.','[{"s": "(b) If the combination is connected to a battery of emf 12 V and negligible internal resistance, obtain the potential drop across each resistor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22669,57,553,'paragraph',NULL,'3.4 (a) Three resistors 2 Ω, 4 Ω and 5 Ω are combined in parallel. What is the total resistance of the combination?','[{"s": "3.4 (a) Three resistors 2 Ω, 4 Ω and 5 Ω are combined in parallel. What is the total resistance of the combination?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22670,57,554,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) If the combination is connected to a battery of emf 20 V and negligible internal resistance, determine the current through each resistor, and the total current drawn from the battery.','[{"s": "(b) If the combination is connected to a battery of emf 20 V and negligible internal resistance, determine the current through each resistor, and the total current drawn from the battery.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22671,57,555,'paragraph',NULL,'3.5 At room temperature (27.0 °C) the resistance of a heating element is 100 Ω. What is the temperature of the element if the resistance is found to be 117 Ω, given that the temperature coefficient of the material of the resistor is 1.70 × 10–4 °C–1.','[{"s": "3.5 At room temperature (27.0 °C) the resistance of a heating element is 100 Ω. What is the temperature of the element if the resistance is found to be 117 Ω, given that the temperature coefficient of the material of the resistor is 1.70 × 10–4 °C–1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22672,57,556,'paragraph',NULL,'3.6 A negligibly small current is passed through a wire of length 15 m and uniform cross-section 6.0 × 10–7 m2, and its resistance is measured to be 5.0 Ω. What is the resistivity of the material at the temperature of the experiment?','[{"s": "3.6 A negligibly small current is passed through a wire of length 15 m and uniform cross-section 6.0 × 10–7 m2, and its resistance is measured to be 5.0 Ω. What is the resistivity of the material at the temperature of the experiment?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22673,57,557,'paragraph',NULL,'3.7 A silver wire has a resistance of 2.1 Ω at 27.5 °C, and a resistance of 2.7 Ω at 100 °C. Determine the temperature coefficient of resistivity of silver.','[{"s": "3.7 A silver wire has a resistance of 2.1 Ω at 27.5 °C, and a resistance of 2.7 Ω at 100 °C. Determine the temperature coefficient of resistivity of silver.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22734,57,618,'paragraph',NULL,'3.21 Determine the current drawn from a 12 V supply with internal resistance 0.5 Ω by the infinite network shown in figure . Each resistor has 1 Ω resistance.','[{"s": "3.21 Determine the current drawn from a 12 V supply with internal resistance 0.5 Ω by the infinite network shown in figure", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Each resistor has 1 Ω resistance.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041957", "2000110626"]',NULL),
(33916,78,477,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) A screw gauge has a pitch of 1.0 mm and 200 divisions on the circular scale. 2 Do you think it is possible to increase the accuracy of the screw gauge arbitrarily by increasing the number of divisions on the circular scale ?','[{"s": "(b) A screw gauge has a pitch of 1.0 mm and 200 divisions on the circular scale.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Do you think it is possible to increase the accuracy of the screw gauge arbitrarily by increasing the number of divisions on the circular scale ?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031063", "2000031787", "2000032118", "2000032736", "2000032814", "2000106042", "2000106060"]',NULL),
