(22679,57,563,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Determine the balance point of the bridge above if X and Y are interchanged.','[{"s": "(b) Determine the balance point of the bridge above if X and Y are interchanged.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22680,57,564,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) What happens if the galvanometer and cell are interchanged at the balance point of the bridge? Would the galvanometer show any current?','[{"s": "(c) What happens if the galvanometer and cell are interchanged at the balance point of the bridge? Would the galvanometer show any current?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22681,57,565,'paragraph',NULL,'3.11 A storage battery of emf 8.0 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω is being charged by a 120 V dc supply using a series resistor of 15.5 Ω. What is the terminal voltage of the battery during charging? What is the purpose of having a series resistor in the charging circuit?','[{"s": "3.11 A storage battery of emf 8.0 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω is being charged by a 120 V dc supply using a series resistor of 15.5 Ω. What is the terminal voltage of the battery during charging? What is the purpose of having a series resistor in the charging circuit?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22682,57,566,'paragraph',NULL,'3.12 In a potentiometer arrangement, a cell of emf 1.25 V gives a balance point at 35.0 cm length of the wire. If the cell is replaced by another cell and the balance point shifts to 63.0 cm, what is the emf of the second cell?','[{"s": "3.12 In a potentiometer arrangement, a cell of emf 1.25 V gives a balance point at 35.0 cm length of the wire. If the cell is replaced by another cell and the balance point shifts to 63.0 cm, what is the emf of the second cell?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22683,57,567,'paragraph',NULL,'3. 13 The number density of free electrons in a copper conductor estimated in Example 3.1 is 8.5 × 10 28 m –3 . How long does an electron take to drift from one end of a wire 3.0 m long to its other end? The area of cross-section of the wire is 2.0 × 10 –6 m 2 and it is carrying a current of 3.0 A.','[{"s": "3. 13 The number density of free electrons in a copper conductor estimated in Example 3.1 is 8.5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "28", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". How long does an electron take to drift from one end of a wire 3.0 m long to its other end? The area of cross-section of the wire is 2.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and it is carrying a current of 3.0 A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22684,57,568,'paragraph',NULL,'3.14 The earth\'s surface has a negative surface charge density of 10 -9 C m -2 . The potential difference of 400 kV between the top of the atmosphere and the surface results (due to the low conductivity of the lower atmosphere) in a current of only 1800 A over the entire globe. If there were no mechanism of sustaining atmospheric electric field, how much time (roughly) would be required to neutralise the earth\'s surface? (This never happens in practise because there is a mechanism to replenish electric charges, namely the continual thunderstorms and lightning in different parts of the globe). Current_Electricity-image36.png','[{"s": "3.14 The earth\'s surface has a negative surface charge density of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The potential difference of 400 kV between the top of the atmosphere and the surface results (due to the low conductivity of the lower atmosphere) in a current of only 1800 A over the entire globe. If there were no mechanism of sustaining atmospheric electric field, how much time (roughly) would be required to neutralise the earth\'s surface? (This never happens in practise because there is a mechanism to replenish electric charges, namely the continual thunderstorms and lightning in different parts of the globe).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Current_Electricity-image36.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22685,57,569,'paragraph',NULL,'3.15 (a) Six lead-acid type of secondary cells each of emf 2.0 V and internal resistance 0.015 Ω are joined in series to provide a supply to a resistance of 8.5 Ω. What are the current drawn from the supply and its terminal voltage?','[{"s": "3.15 (a) Six lead-acid type of secondary cells each of emf 2.0 V and internal resistance 0.015 Ω are joined in series to provide a supply to a resistance of 8.5 Ω. What are the current drawn from the supply and its terminal voltage?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22686,57,570,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) A secondary cell after long use has an emf of 1.9 V and a large internal resistance of 380 Ω. What maximum current can be drawn from the cell? Could the cell drive the starting motor of a car?','[{"s": "(b) A secondary cell after long use has an emf of 1.9 V and a large internal resistance of 380 Ω. What maximum current can be drawn from the cell? Could the cell drive the starting motor of a car?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22687,57,571,'paragraph',NULL,'3.16 Two wires of equal length, one of aluminium and the other of copper have the same resistance. Which of the two wires is lighter? Hence explain why aluminium wires are preferred for overhead power cables. (ρAl=2.63×10-8 Ω m, ρCu=1.72×10-8 Ω m, Relative density of Al=2,7 of Cu=8.9)','[{"s": "3.16 Two wires of equal length, one of aluminium and the other of copper have the same resistance. Which of the two wires is lighter? Hence explain why aluminium wires are preferred for overhead power cables.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(ρAl=2.63×10-8 Ω m, ρCu=1.72×10-8 Ω m, Relative density of Al=2,7 of Cu=8.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
