(22874,58,130,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) For a photoelectric device to operate, we require incident light energy E to be equal to or greater than the work function φ 0 0 0 = 2.30 eV) and Cs (with φ 0 = 2.14 eV). It will also operate with yellow-green light (with E = 2.26 eV) for Cs (with φ 0 = 2.14 eV) only. However, it will not operate with red light (with E = 1.64 eV) for any of these photosensitive materials.','[{"s": "(b) For a photoelectric device to operate, we require incident light energy E to be equal to or greater than the work function φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.30 eV) and Cs (with φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.14 eV). It will also operate with yellow-green light (with E = 2.26 eV) for Cs (with φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.14 eV) only. However, it will not operate with red light (with E = 1.64 eV) for any of these photosensitive materials.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22875,58,131,'heading',3,'11.8 Wave Nature of Matter',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26450,66,20,'paragraph',NULL,'A sharp needle when pressed against our skin pierces it. Our skin, however, remains intact when a blunt object with a wider contact area (say the back of a spoon) is pressed against it with the same force. If an elephant were to step on a man’s chest, his ribs would crack. A circus performer across whose chest a large, light but strong wooden plank is placed first, is saved from this accident. Such everyday experiences convince us that both the force and its coverage area are important. Smaller the area on which the force acts, greater is the impact. This impact is known as pressure.','[{"s": "A sharp needle when pressed against our skin pierces it. Our skin, however, remains intact when a blunt object with a wider contact area (say the back of a spoon) is pressed against it with the same force. If an elephant were to step on a man’s chest, his ribs would crack. A circus performer across whose chest a large, light but strong wooden plank is placed first, is saved from this accident. Such everyday experiences convince us that both the force and its coverage area are important. Smaller the area on which the force acts, greater is the impact. This impact is known as pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22878,58,134,'paragraph',NULL,'λ = Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image24.png (11.5)','[{"s": "λ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image24.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(11.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22881,58,137,'paragraph',NULL,'p = h ν /c (11.6)','[{"s": "p = h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(11.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22882,58,138,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore,','[{"s": "Therefore,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22883,58,139,'paragraph',NULL,'Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image1.png (11.7)','[{"s": "Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(11.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22884,58,140,'paragraph',NULL,'That is, the de Broglie wavelength of a photon given by Eq. (11.5) equals the wavelength of electromagnetic radiation of which the photon is a quantum of energy and momentum.','[{"s": "That is, the de Broglie wavelength of a photon given by Eq. (11.5) equals the wavelength of electromagnetic radiation of which the photon is a quantum of energy and momentum.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22885,58,141,'paragraph',NULL,'Clearly, from Eq. (11.5 ), λ is smaller for a heavier particle (large m ) or more energetic particle (large v ). For example, the de Broglie wavelength of a ball of mass 0.12 kg moving with a speed of 20 m s –1 is easily calculated:','[{"s": "Clearly, from Eq. (11.5 ), λ is smaller for a heavier particle (large", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") or more energetic particle (large", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). For example, the de Broglie wavelength of a ball of mass 0.12 kg moving with a speed of 20 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is easily calculated:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22886,58,142,'paragraph',NULL,'p = m v = 0.12 kg × 20 m s –1 = 2.40 kg m s –1','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.12 kg × 20 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.40 kg m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22887,58,143,'paragraph',NULL,'λ = Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image36.png = Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image17.png = 2.76 × 10 –34 m','[{"s": "λ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image36.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image17.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 2.76 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–34", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22888,58,144,'paragraph',NULL,'This wavelength is so small that it is beyond any measurement. This is the reason why macroscopic objects in our daily life do not show wave-like properties. On the other hand, in the sub-atomic domain, the wave character of particles is significant and measurable.','[{"s": "This wavelength is so small that it is beyond any measurement. This is the reason why macroscopic objects in our daily life do not show wave-like properties. On the other hand, in the sub-atomic domain, the wave character of particles is significant and measurable.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22890,58,146,'paragraph',NULL,'K = e V (11.8)','[{"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(11.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22891,58,147,'paragraph',NULL,'Now , K = Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image28.png m v 2 = Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image40.png , so that','[{"s": "Now", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image28.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "m v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image40.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", so that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22892,58,148,'paragraph',NULL,'p = Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image38.png (11.9)','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image38.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(11.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22896,58,152,'paragraph',NULL,'Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image48.png (11.11)','[{"s": "Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image48.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(11.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22897,58,153,'paragraph',NULL,'where V is the magnitude of accelerating potential in volts. For a 120 V accelerating potential, Eq. (11.11) gives λ = 0.112 nm. This wavelength is of the same order as the spacing between the atomic planes in crystals. This suggests that matter waves associated with an electron could be verified by crystal diffraction experiments analogous to X-ray diffraction. We describe the experimental verification of the de Broglie hypothesis in the next section. In 1929, de Broglie was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics for his discovery of the wave nature of electrons.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the magnitude of accelerating potential in volts. For a 120 V accelerating potential, Eq. (11.11) gives λ = 0.112 nm. This wavelength is of the same order as the spacing between the atomic planes in crystals. This suggests that matter waves associated with an electron could be verified by crystal diffraction experiments analogous to X-ray diffraction. We describe the experimental verification of the de Broglie hypothesis in the next section. In 1929, de Broglie was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics for his discovery of the wave nature of electrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22898,58,154,'heading',3,'A photo cell',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22899,58,155,'paragraph',NULL,'A photocell is a technological application of the photoelectric effect. It is a device whose electrical properties are affected by light. It is also sometimes called an electric eye. A photocell consists of a semi-cylindrical photo-sensitive metal plate C (emitter) and a wire loop A (collector) supported in an evacuated glass or quartz bulb. It is connected to the external circuit having a high-tension battery B and microammeter (µA) as shown in the Figure. Sometimes, instead of the plate C, a thin layer of photosensitive material is pasted on the inside of the bulb. A part of the bulb is left clean for the light to enter it.','[{"s": "A photocell is a technological application of the photoelectric effect. It is a device whose electrical properties are affected by light. It is also sometimes called an electric eye. A photocell consists of a semi-cylindrical photo-sensitive metal plate C (emitter) and a wire loop A (collector) supported in an evacuated glass or quartz bulb. It is connected to the external circuit having a high-tension battery B and microammeter (µA) as shown in the Figure. Sometimes, instead of the plate C, a thin layer of photosensitive material is pasted on the inside of the bulb. A part of the bulb is left clean for the light to enter it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22900,58,156,'paragraph',NULL,'When light of suitable wavelength falls on the emitter C, photoelectrons are emitted. These photoelectrons are drawn to the collector A. Photocurrent of the order of a few microampere can be normally obtained from a photo cell.','[{"s": "When light of suitable wavelength falls on the emitter C, photoelectrons are emitted. These photoelectrons are drawn to the collector A. Photocurrent of the order of a few microampere can be normally obtained from a photo cell.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22901,58,157,'paragraph',NULL,'A photocell converts a change in intensity of illumination into a change in photocurrent. This current can be used to operate control systems and in light measuring devices. A photocell of lead sulphide sensitive to infrared radiation is used in electronic ignition circuits.','[{"s": "A photocell converts a change in intensity of illumination into a change in photocurrent. This current can be used to operate control systems and in light measuring devices. A photocell of lead sulphide sensitive to infrared radiation is used in electronic ignition circuits.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22902,58,158,'image',NULL,'Box_Photocell',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image14.png'),
(22903,58,159,'paragraph',NULL,'A Photo cell','[{"s": "A Photo cell", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22904,58,160,'paragraph',NULL,'In scientific work, photo cells are used whenever it is necessary to measure the intensity of light. Light meters in photographic cameras make use of photo cells to measure the intensity of incident light. The photocells, inserted in the door light electric circuit, are used as automatic door opener. A person approaching a doorway may interrupt a light beam which is incident on a photocell. The abrupt change in photocurrent may be used to start a motor which opens the door or rings an alarm. They are used in the control of a counting device which records every interruption of the light beam caused by a person or object passing across the beam. So photocells help count the persons entering an auditorium, provided they enter the hall one by one. They are used for detection of traffic law defaulters: an alarm may be sounded whenever a beam of (invisible) radiation is intercepted.','[{"s": "In scientific work, photo cells are used whenever it is necessary to measure the intensity of light. Light meters in photographic cameras make use of photo cells to measure the intensity of incident light. The photocells, inserted in the door light electric circuit, are used as automatic door opener. A person approaching a doorway may interrupt a light beam which is incident on a photocell. The abrupt change in photocurrent may be used to start a motor which opens the door or rings an alarm. They are used in the control of a counting device which records every interruption of the light beam caused by a person or object passing across the beam. So photocells help count the persons entering an auditorium, provided they enter the hall one by one. They are used for detection of traffic law defaulters: an alarm may be sounded whenever a beam of (invisible) radiation is intercepted.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22905,58,161,'paragraph',NULL,'In burglar alarm, (invisible) ultraviolet light is continuously made to fall on a photocell installed at the doorway. A person entering the door interrupts the beam falling on the photocell. The abrupt change in photocurrent is used to start an electric bell ringing. In fire alarm, a number of photocells are installed at suitable places in a building. In the event of breaking out of fire, light radiations fall upon the photocell. This completes the electric circuit through an electric bell or a siren which starts operating as a warning signal.','[{"s": "In burglar alarm, (invisible) ultraviolet light is continuously made to fall on a photocell installed at the doorway. A person entering the door interrupts the beam falling on the photocell. The abrupt change in photocurrent is used to start an electric bell ringing. In fire alarm, a number of photocells are installed at suitable places in a building. In the event of breaking out of fire, light radiations fall upon the photocell. This completes the electric circuit through an electric bell or a siren which starts operating as a warning signal.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22906,58,162,'paragraph',NULL,'Photocells are used in the reproduction of sound in motion pictures and in the television camera for scanning and telecasting scenes. They are used in industries for detecting minor flaws or holes in metal sheets.','[{"s": "Photocells are used in the reproduction of sound in motion pictures and in the television camera for scanning and telecasting scenes. They are used in industries for detecting minor flaws or holes in metal sheets.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22907,58,163,'heading',4,'Louis Victor de Broglie (1892 – 1987)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22908,58,164,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image39.png'),
(22909,58,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Louis Victor de Broglie (1892 – 1987) French physicist who put forth revolutionary idea of wave nature of matter. This idea was developed by Erwin Schródinger into a full-fledged theory of quantum mechanics commonly known as wave mechanics. In 1929, he was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics for his discovery of the wave nature of electrons.','[{"s": "Louis Victor de Broglie (1892 – 1987)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "French physicist who put forth revolutionary idea of wave nature of matter. This idea was developed by Erwin Schródinger into a full-fledged theory of quantum mechanics commonly known as wave mechanics. In 1929, he was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics for his discovery of the wave nature of electrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22910,58,166,'paragraph',NULL,'The matter–wave picture elegantly incorporated the Heisenberg’s uncertainty principle . According to the principle, it is not possible to measure both the position and momentum of an electron (or any other particle) at the same time exactly. There is always some uncertainty (∆ x ) in the specification of position and some uncertainty (∆ p ) in the specification of momentum. The product of ∆ x and ∆ p is of the order of ö* (with ö = h /2π), i.e.,','[{"s": "The matter–wave picture elegantly incorporated the Heisenberg’s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "uncertainty principle", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". According to the principle, it is not possible to measure", "t": "text"}, {"s": "both", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the position and momentum of an electron (or any other particle)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at the same time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "exactly. There is always some uncertainty (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in the specification of position and some uncertainty (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in the specification of momentum. The product of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is of the order of ö* (with ö =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2π), i.e.,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22911,58,167,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ x ∆ p ≈ ö (11.12)','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≈ ö (11.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33797,78,358,'paragraph',NULL,'For example, the accuracy in measurement of mass 1.02 g is ± 0.01 g whereas another measurement 9.89 g is also accurate to ± 0.01 g.','[{"s": "For example, the accuracy in measurement of mass 1.02 g is ± 0.01 g whereas another measurement 9.89 g is also accurate to ± 0.01 g.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33798,78,359,'paragraph',NULL,'The relative error in 1.02 g is','[{"s": "The relative error in 1.02 g is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22912,58,168,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (11.12) allows the possibility that ∆ x is zero; but then ∆ p must be infinite in order that the product is non-zero. Similarly, if ∆ p is zero, ∆ x must be infinite. Ordinarily, both ∆ x and ∆ p are non-zero such that their product is of the order of ö.','[{"s": "Equation (11.12) allows the possibility that ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is zero; but then ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "must be infinite in order that the product is non-zero. Similarly, if ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is zero, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "must be infinite. Ordinarily, both ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are non-zero such that their product is of the order of ö.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22913,58,169,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, if an electron has a definite momentum p , (i.e.∆ p = 0), by the de Broglie relation, it has a definite wavelength λ. A wave of definite (single) wavelength extends all over space. By Born’s probability interpretation this means that the electron is not localised in any finite region of space. That is, its position uncertainty is infinite (∆ x → ∞), which is consistent with the uncertainty principle.','[{"s": "Now, if an electron has a definite momentum", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", (i.e.∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0), by the de Broglie relation, it has a definite wavelength λ. A wave of definite (single) wavelength extends all over space. By Born’s probability interpretation this means that the electron is not localised in any finite region of space. That is, its position uncertainty is infinite (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ ∞), which is consistent with the uncertainty principle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22914,58,170,'paragraph',NULL,'* A more rigorous treatment gives ∆x ∆p ≥ ö /2.','[{"s": "* A more rigorous treatment gives ∆x ∆p ≥ ö /2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22915,58,171,'paragraph',NULL,'In general, the matter wave associated with the electron is not extended all over space. It is a wave packet extending over some finite region of space. In that case ∆ x is not infinite but has some finite value depending on the extension of the wave packet. Also, you must appreciate that a wave packet of finite extension does not have a single wavelength. It is built up of wavelengths spread around some central wavelength.','[{"s": "In general, the matter wave associated with the electron is not extended all over space. It is a wave packet extending over some finite region of space. In that case ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is not infinite but has some finite value depending on the extension of the wave packet. Also, you must appreciate that a wave packet of finite extension does not have a single wavelength. It is built up of wavelengths spread around some central wavelength.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22916,58,172,'paragraph',NULL,'By de Broglie’s relation, then, the momentum of the electron will also have a spread – an uncertainty ∆ p . This is as expected from the uncertainty principle. It can be shown that the wave packet description together with de Broglie relation and Born’s probability interpretation reproduce the Heisenberg’s uncertainty principle exactly.','[{"s": "By de Broglie’s relation, then, the momentum of the electron will also have a spread – an uncertainty ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This is as expected from the uncertainty principle. It can be shown that the wave packet description together with de Broglie relation and Born’s probability interpretation reproduce the Heisenberg’s uncertainty principle exactly.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22917,58,173,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Dual_Nature_of_Matter_and_Radiation-image4.png'),
(22918,58,174,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 11.6 (a) The wave packet description of an electron. The wave packet corresponds to a spread of wavelength around some central wavelength (and hence by de Broglie relation, a spread in momentum). Consequently, it is associated with an uncertainty in position (∆x) and an uncertainty in momentum (∆p). (b) The matter wave corresponding to a definite momentum of an electron extends all over space. In this case, ∆p = 0 and ∆ x → ∞.','[{"s": "Figure 11.6 (a) The wave packet description of an electron. The wave packet corresponds to a spread of wavelength around some central wavelength (and hence by de Broglie relation, a spread in momentum). Consequently, it is associated with an uncertainty in position (∆x) and an uncertainty in momentum (∆p). (b) The matter wave corresponding to a definite momentum of an electron extends all over space. In this case, ∆p = 0 and ∆ x → ∞.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22919,58,175,'paragraph',NULL,'In Chapter 12, the de Broglie relation will be seen to justify Bohr’s postulate on quantisation of angular momentum of electron in an atom.','[{"s": "In Chapter 12, the de Broglie relation will be seen to justify Bohr’s postulate on quantisation of angular momentum of electron in an atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22920,58,176,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 11.6 shows a schematic diagram of (a) a localised wave packet, and (b) an extended wave with fixed wavelength.','[{"s": "Figure 11.6 shows a schematic diagram of (a) a localised wave packet, and (b) an extended wave with fixed wavelength.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22921,58,177,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.4 What is the de Broglie wavelength associated with (a) an electron moving with a speed of 5.4×10 6 m/s, and (b) a ball of mass 150 g travelling at 30.0 m/s?','[{"s": "Example 11.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What is the de Broglie wavelength associated with (a) an electron moving with a speed of 5.4×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m/s, and (b) a ball of mass 150 g travelling at 30.0 m/s?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22922,58,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22923,58,179,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) For the electron:','[{"s": "(a) For the electron:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22924,58,180,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass m = 9.11×10 –31 kg, speed v = 5.4×10 6 m/s. Then, momentum p = m v = 9.11×10 –31 (kg) × 5.4 × 10 6 (m/s)','[{"s": "Mass m = 9.11×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg, speed v = 5.4×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m/s. Then, momentum p = m v = 9.11×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(kg) × 5.4 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(m/s)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22925,58,181,'paragraph',NULL,'p = 4.92 × 10 –24 kg m/s','[{"s": "p = 4.92 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–24", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg m/s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
