(23049,58,305,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The number of photons entering the pupil of our eye per second corresponding to the minimum intensity of white light that we humans can perceive (10 -10 W m -2 ). Take the area of the pupil to be about 0.4 cm 2 , and the average frequency of white light to be about 6 x 10 14 Hz.','[{"s": "(b) The number of photons entering the pupil of our eye per second corresponding to the minimum intensity of white light that we humans can perceive (10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "W m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Take the area of the pupil to be about 0.4 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and the average frequency of white light to be about 6 x 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23050,58,306,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 11.26: Ultraviolet light of wavelength 2271 Å from a 100 W mercury source irradiates a photocell made of molybdenum metal. If the stopping potential is ~ 1.3 V, estimate the work function of the metal. How would the photo-cell respond to high intensity ( ~ 10 5 W m -2 ) red light of wavelength 6328 Å produced by a He-Ne laser?','[{"s": "Question 11.26: Ultraviolet light of wavelength 2271 Å from a 100 W mercury source irradiates a photocell made of molybdenum metal. If the stopping potential is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1.3 V, estimate the work function of the metal. How would the photo-cell respond to high intensity (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "W m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") red light of wavelength 6328 Å produced by a He-Ne laser?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23051,58,307,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 11.27: Monochromatic radiation of wavelength 640.2 nm (1 nm = 10 -9 m) from a neon lamp irradiates photosensitive material made of cesium on tungsten. The stopping voltage is measured to be 0.54 V. The source is replaced by an iron source and its 427.2 nm line irradiates the same photo-cell. Predict the new stopping voltage.','[{"s": "Question 11.27: Monochromatic radiation of wavelength 640.2 nm (1 nm = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m) from a neon lamp irradiates photosensitive material made of cesium on tungsten. The stopping voltage is measured to be 0.54 V. The source is replaced by an iron source and its 427.2 nm line irradiates the same photo-cell. Predict the new stopping voltage.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
