(23053,58,309,'paragraph',NULL,'A mercury lamp is a convenient source for studying the frequency dependence of photoelectric emission since it gives a number of spectral lines ranging from the UV to the red end of the visible spectrum. In our experiment with rubidium photo-cell, the following lines from a mercury sourcæ were used: λ 1 =3650 o A,λ 2 =4047 o A ,λ 3 =4358 o A ,λ 4 =5461 o A ,λ 5 =6907 o A The stopping voltages, respectively, were measured to be:','[{"s": "A mercury lamp is a convenient source for studying the frequency dependence of photoelectric emission since it gives a number of spectral lines ranging from the UV to the red end of the visible spectrum. In our experiment with rubidium photo-cell, the following lines from a mercury sourcæ were used:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=3650", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A,λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=4047", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=4358", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=5461", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=6907", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The stopping voltages, respectively, were measured to be:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23054,58,310,'paragraph',NULL,'V 01 =1.28 V, V 02 =0.95 V, V 03 =0.74 V, V 04 =0.16 V, V 05 =0 V','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "01", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=1.28 V, V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "02", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=0.95 V, V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "03", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=0.74 V, V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "04", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=0.16 V, V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "05", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=0 V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23055,58,311,'paragraph',NULL,'Determine the value of Planck\'s constant h, the threshold frequency, and work function for the material. [Note: You will notice that to get h from the data, you will need to know e (which you can take to be 1.6 x 10 -19 C). Experiments of this kind on Na, Li, K, etc. were performed by Millikan, who, using his own value of e (from the oil-drop experiment) confirmed Einstein\'s photoelectric equation and at the same time gave an independent estimate of the value of h.]','[{"s": "Determine the value of Planck\'s constant h, the threshold frequency, and work function for the material. [Note: You will notice that to get h from the data, you will need to know e (which you can take to be 1.6 x 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C). Experiments of this kind on Na, Li, K, etc. were performed by Millikan, who, using his own value of e (from the oil-drop experiment) confirmed Einstein\'s photoelectric equation and at the same time gave an independent estimate of the value of h.]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23056,58,312,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 11.29:','[{"s": "Question 11.29:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23057,58,313,'paragraph',NULL,'The work function for the following metals is given: Na: 2.75 eV; K: 2.30 eV; Mo: 4.17 eV; Ni: 5.15 eV. Which of these metals will not give photoelectric emission for a radiation of wavelength 3300 Å from a He-Cd laser placed I m away from the photocell? What happens if the laser is brought nearer and placed 50 cm away?','[{"s": "The work function for the following metals is given: Na: 2.75 eV; K: 2.30 eV; Mo: 4.17 eV; Ni: 5.15 eV. Which of these metals will not give photoelectric emission for a radiation of wavelength 3300 Å from a He-Cd laser placed I m away from the photocell? What happens if the laser is brought nearer and placed 50 cm away?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23058,58,314,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 11.30:','[{"s": "Question 11.30:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23059,58,315,'paragraph',NULL,'Light of intensity 10 -5 W m -2 falls on a sodium photo-cell of surface area 2 cm 2 . Assuming that the top 5 layers of sodium absorb the incident energy, estimate the time required for photoelectric emission in the wave-picture of radiation. The work function for the metal is given to be about 2 eV. What is the implication of your answer?','[{"s": "Light of intensity 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "W m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "falls on a sodium photo-cell of surface area 2 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Assuming that the top 5 layers of sodium absorb the incident energy, estimate the time required for photoelectric emission in the wave-picture of radiation. The work function for the metal is given to be about 2 eV. What is the implication of your answer?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
