(23062,58,318,'paragraph',NULL,'Crystal diffraction experiments can be performed using X-rays, or electrons accelerated through appropriate voltage. Which probe has greater energy? (For quantitative comparison, take the wavelength of the probe equal to I Å, which is of the order of inter-atomic s acing in the lattice) (rne= 9.11 x 10 -31 kg).','[{"s": "Crystal diffraction experiments can be performed using X-rays, or electrons accelerated through appropriate voltage. Which probe has greater energy? (For quantitative comparison, take the wavelength of the probe equal to I Å, which is of the order of inter-atomic s acing in the lattice) (rne= 9.11 x 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23063,58,319,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 11.32:','[{"s": "Question 11.32:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23064,58,320,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Obtain the de Broglie wavelength of a neutron of kinetic energy 150 eV. As you have seen in Exercise 11.31, an electron beam of this energy is suitable for crystal diffraction experiments. Would a neutron beam of the same energy be equally suitable? Explain. (m n = 1.675 x 10 -27 kg)','[{"s": "(a) Obtain the de Broglie wavelength of a neutron of kinetic energy 150 eV. As you have seen in Exercise 11.31, an electron beam of this energy is suitable for crystal diffraction experiments. Would a neutron beam of the same energy be equally suitable? Explain. (m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.675 x 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-27", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23065,58,321,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Obtain the de Broglie wavelength associated with thermal neutrons at room temperature (27 ° C). Hence explain why a fast neutron beam needs to be thermalized with the environment it can be used for neutron diffraction experiments.','[{"s": "(b) Obtain the de Broglie wavelength associated with thermal neutrons at room temperature (27", "t": "text"}, {"s": "°", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C). Hence explain why a fast neutron beam needs to be thermalized with the environment it can be used for neutron diffraction experiments.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23066,58,322,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 11.33:','[{"s": "Question 11.33:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23067,58,323,'paragraph',NULL,'An electron microscope uses electrons accelerated by a voltage of 50 W. Determine the de Broglie wavelength associated with the electrons. If other factors (such as numerical aperture, etc.) are taken to be roughly the same, how does the resolving power of an electron microscope compare with that of an optical microscope which uses yellow light?','[{"s": "An electron microscope uses electrons accelerated by a voltage of 50 W. Determine the de Broglie wavelength associated with the electrons. If other factors (such as numerical aperture, etc.) are taken to be roughly the same, how does the resolving power of an electron microscope compare with that of an optical microscope which uses yellow light?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
