(23178,59,0,'heading',2,'Chapter Six',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23179,59,1,'heading',1,'Electromagnetic Induction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23180,59,2,'heading',2,'6.1 Introduction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23181,59,3,'paragraph',NULL,'Electricity and magnetism were considered separate and unrelated phenomena for a long time. In the early decades of the nineteenth century, experiments on electric current by Oersted, Ampere and a few others established the fact that electricity and magnetism are inter-related. They found that moving electric charges produce magnetic fields. For example, an electric current deflects a magnetic compass needle placed in its vicinity. This naturally raises the questions like: Is the converse effect possible? Can moving magnets produce electric currents? Does the nature permit such a relation between electricity and magnetism? The answer is resounding yes! The experiments of Michael Faraday in England and Joseph Henry in USA, conducted around 1830, demonstrated conclusively that electric currents were induced in closed coils when subjected to changing magnetic fields. In this chapter, we will study the phenomena associated with changing magnetic fields and understand the underlying principles. The phenomenon in which electric current is generated by varying magnetic fields is appropriately called electromagnetic induction.','[{"s": "Electricity and magnetism were considered separate and unrelated phenomena for a long time. In the early decades of the nineteenth century, experiments on electric current by Oersted, Ampere and a few others established the fact that electricity and magnetism are inter-related. They found that moving electric charges produce magnetic fields. For example, an electric current deflects a magnetic compass needle placed in its vicinity. This naturally raises the questions like: Is the converse effect possible? Can moving magnets produce electric currents? Does the nature permit such a relation between electricity and magnetism? The answer is resounding yes! The experiments of Michael Faraday in England and Joseph Henry in USA, conducted around 1830, demonstrated conclusively that electric currents were induced in closed coils when subjected to changing magnetic fields. In this chapter, we will study the phenomena associated with changing magnetic fields and understand the underlying principles. The phenomenon in which electric current is generated by varying magnetic fields is appropriately called electromagnetic induction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23182,59,4,'paragraph',NULL,'When Faraday first made public his discovery that relative motion between a bar magnet and a wire loop produced a small current in the latter, he was asked, “What is the use of it?” His reply was: “What is the use of a new born baby?” The phenomenon of electromagnetic induction is not merely of theoretical or academic interest but also of practical utility. Imagine a world where there is no electricity – no electric lights, no trains, no telephones and no personal computers. The pioneering experiments of Faraday and Henry have led directly to the development of modern day generators and transformers. Today’s civilisation owes its progress to a great extent to the discovery of electromagnetic induction.','[{"s": "When Faraday first made public his discovery that relative motion between a bar magnet and a wire loop produced a small current in the latter, he was asked, “What is the use of it?” His reply was: “What is the use of a new born baby?” The phenomenon of electromagnetic induction is not merely of theoretical or academic interest but also of practical utility. Imagine a world where there is no electricity – no electric lights, no trains, no telephones and no personal computers. The pioneering experiments of Faraday and Henry have led directly to the development of modern day generators and transformers. Today’s civilisation owes its progress to a great extent to the discovery of electromagnetic induction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23183,59,5,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image90.png'),
(23184,59,6,'paragraph',NULL,'Josheph Henry [1797 – 1878] American experimental physicist, professor at Princeton University and first director of the Smithsonian Institution. He made important improvements in electro- magnets by winding coils of insulated wire around iron pole pieces and invented an electromagnetic motor and a new, efficient telegraph. He discoverd self-induction and investigated how currents in one circuit induce currents in another.','[{"s": "Josheph Henry [1797 – 1878] American experimental physicist, professor at Princeton University and first director of the Smithsonian Institution. He made important improvements in electro- magnets by winding coils of insulated wire around iron pole pieces and invented an electromagnetic motor and a new, efficient telegraph. He discoverd self-induction and investigated how currents in one circuit induce currents in another.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23185,59,7,'heading',2,'6.2 The Experiments of Faraday and Henry',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23186,59,8,'paragraph',NULL,'The discovery and understanding of electromagnetic induction are based on a long series of experiments carried out by Faraday and Henry. We shall now describe some of these experiments.','[{"s": "The discovery and understanding of electromagnetic induction are based on a long series of experiments carried out by Faraday and Henry. We shall now describe some of these experiments.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23187,59,9,'paragraph',NULL,'Experiment 6.1','[{"s": "Experiment 6.1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27835,68,542,'paragraph',NULL,'16. An object that is in flight after being projected is called a projectile . If an object is projected with initial velocity v o making an angle θ o with x- axis and if we assume its initial position to coincide with the origin of the coordinate system, then the position and velocity of the projectile at time t are given by :','[{"s": "16. An object that is in flight after being projected is called a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "projectile", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If an object is projected with initial velocity v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "making an angle θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "axis and if we assume its initial position to coincide with the origin of the coordinate system, then the position and velocity of the projectile at time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are given by :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23189,59,11,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image109.png'),
(23190,59,12,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.1 When the bar magnet is pushed towards the coil, the pointer in the galvanometer G deflects.','[{"s": "Figure 6.1 When the bar magnet is pushed towards the coil, the pointer in the galvanometer G deflects.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23191,59,13,'paragraph',NULL,'Experiment 6.2','[{"s": "Experiment 6.2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23192,59,14,'paragraph',NULL,'In Fig. 6.2 the bar magnet is replaced by a second coil C 2 connected to a battery. The steady current in the coil C 2 produces a steady magnetic field. As coil C 2 is moved towards the coil C 1 , the galvanometer shows a deflection. This indicates that electric current is induced in coil C 1 . When C 2 is moved away, the galvanometer shows a deflection again, but this time in the opposite direction. The deflection lasts as long as coil C 2 is in motion. When the coil C 2 is held fixed and C 1 is moved, the same effects are observed. Again, it is the relative motion between the coils that induces the electric current.','[{"s": "In Fig. 6.2 the bar magnet is replaced by a second coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "connected to a battery. The steady current in the coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "produces a steady magnetic field. As coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is moved towards the coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the galvanometer shows a deflection. This indicates that electric current is induced in coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". When C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is moved away, the galvanometer shows a deflection again, but this time in the opposite direction. The deflection lasts as long as coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is in motion. When the coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is held fixed and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is moved, the same effects are observed. Again, it is the relative motion between the coils that induces the electric current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23193,59,15,'paragraph',NULL,'* Wherever the term ‘coil’ or ‘loop’ is used, it is assumed that they are made up of conducting material and are prepared using wires which are coated with insulating material.','[{"s": "* Wherever the term ‘coil’ or ‘loop’ is used, it is assumed that they are made up of conducting material and are prepared using wires which are coated with insulating material.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23194,59,16,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image13.png'),
(23195,59,17,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.2 Current is induced in coil C 1 due to motion of the current carrying coil C 2 .','[{"s": "Figure 6.2 Current is induced in coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "due to motion of the current carrying coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23196,59,18,'paragraph',NULL,'Experiment 6.3','[{"s": "Experiment 6.3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23197,59,19,'paragraph',NULL,'The above two experiments involved relative motion between a magnet and a coil and between two coils, respectively. Through another experiment, Faraday showed that this relative motion is not an absolute requirement. Figure 6.3 shows two coils C 1 and C 2 held stationary. Coil C 1 is connected to galvanometer G while the second coil C 2 is connected to a battery through a tapping key K.','[{"s": "The above two experiments involved relative motion between a magnet and a coil and between two coils, respectively. Through another experiment, Faraday showed that this relative motion is not an absolute requirement. Figure 6.3 shows two coils C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "held stationary. Coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is connected to galvanometer G while the second coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is connected to a battery through a tapping key K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23199,59,21,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image105.png'),
(23200,59,22,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.3 Experimental set-up for Experiment 6.3.','[{"s": "Figure 6.3 Experimental set-up for Experiment 6.3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23201,59,23,'heading',2,'6.3 Magnetic Flux',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23203,59,25,'paragraph',NULL,'Φ B = B.A = BA cos θ (6.1)','[{"s": "Φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= B.A = BA cos θ (6.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23204,59,26,'paragraph',NULL,'where θ is angle between B and A. The notion of the area as a vector has been discussed earlier in Chapter 1. Equation (6.1) can be extended to curved surfaces and nonuniform fields.','[{"s": "where θ is angle between B and A. The notion of the area as a vector has been discussed earlier in Chapter 1. Equation (6.1) can be extended to curved surfaces and nonuniform fields.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23205,59,27,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image27.png'),
(23206,59,28,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.4 A plane of surface area A placed in a uniform magnetic field B.','[{"s": "Figure 6.4 A plane of surface area A placed in a uniform magnetic field B.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23207,59,29,'paragraph',NULL,'If the magnetic field has different magnitudes and directions at various parts of a surface as shown in Fig. 6.5, then the magnetic flux through the surface is given by','[{"s": "If the magnetic field has different magnitudes and directions at various parts of a surface as shown in Fig. 6.5, then the magnetic flux through the surface is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23208,59,30,'paragraph',NULL,'ϕ B =B 1 .dA 1 +B 2 .dA 2 +...=∑B i .dA i (6.2)','[{"s": "ϕ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".dA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".dA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+...=∑B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".dA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(6.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23209,59,31,'paragraph',NULL,'where ‘all’ stands for summation over all the area elements dA i comprising the surface and B i is the magnetic field at the area element dA i . The SI unit of magnetic flux is weber (Wb) or tesla meter squared (Tm 2 ). Magnetic flux is a scalar quantity.','[{"s": "where ‘all’ stands for summation over all the area elements dA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "comprising the surface and B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the magnetic field at the area element dA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The SI unit of magnetic flux is weber (Wb) or tesla meter squared (Tm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Magnetic flux is a scalar quantity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23210,59,32,'heading',3,'6.4 FARADAY’S LAW OF INDUCTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23211,59,33,'paragraph',NULL,'From the experimental observations, Faraday arrived at a conclusion that an emf is induced in a coil when magnetic flux through the coil changes with time. Experimental observations discussed in Section 6.2 can be explained using this concept.','[{"s": "From the experimental observations, Faraday arrived at a conclusion that an emf is induced in a coil when magnetic flux through the coil changes with time. Experimental observations discussed in Section 6.2 can be explained using this concept.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23212,59,34,'paragraph',NULL,'The motion of a magnet towards or away from coil C 1 in Experiment 6.1 and moving a current-carrying coil C 2 towards or away from coil C 1 in Experiment 6.2, change the magnetic flux associated with coil C 1 . The change in magnetic flux induces emf in coil C 1 . It was this induced emf which caused electric current to flow in coil C 1 and through the galvanometer. A plausible explanation for the observations of Experiment 6.3 is as follows: When the tapping key K is pressed, the current in coil C 2 (and the resulting magnetic field) rises from zero to a maximum value in a short time. Consequently, the magnetic flux through the neighbouring coil C 1 also increases. It is the change in magnetic flux through coil C 1 that produces an induced emf in coil C 1 . When the key is held pressed, current in coil C 2 is constant. Therefore, there is no change in the magnetic flux through coil C 1 and the current in coil C 1 drops to zero. When the key is released, the current in C 2 and the resulting magnetic field decreases from the maximum value to zero in a short time. This results in a decrease in magnetic flux through coil C 1 and hence again induces an electric current in coil C 1 *. The common point in all these observations is that the time rate of change of magnetic flux through a circuit induces emf in it. Faraday stated experimental observations in the form of a law called Faraday’s law of electromagnetic induction. The law is stated below.','[{"s": "The motion of a magnet towards or away from coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in Experiment 6.1 and moving a current-carrying coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "towards or away from coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in Experiment 6.2, change the magnetic flux associated with coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The change in magnetic flux induces emf in coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It was this induced emf which caused electric current to flow in coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and through the galvanometer. A plausible explanation for the observations of Experiment 6.3 is as follows: When the tapping key K is pressed, the current in coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(and the resulting magnetic field) rises from zero to a maximum value in a short time. Consequently, the magnetic flux through the neighbouring coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "also increases. It is the change in magnetic flux through coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "that produces an induced emf in coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". When the key is held pressed, current in coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is constant. Therefore, there is no change in the magnetic flux through coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the current in coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "drops to zero. When the key is released, the current in C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the resulting magnetic field decreases from the maximum value to zero in a short time. This results in a decrease in magnetic flux through coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and hence again induces an electric current in coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "*. The common point in all these observations is that the time rate of change of magnetic flux through a circuit induces emf in it. Faraday stated experimental observations in the form of a law called Faraday’s law of electromagnetic induction. The law is stated below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23213,59,35,'paragraph',NULL,'* Note that sensitive electrical instruments in the vicinity of an electromagnet can be damaged due to the induced emfs (and the resulting currents) when the electromagnet is turned on or off.','[{"s": "* Note that sensitive electrical instruments in the vicinity of an electromagnet can be damaged due to the induced emfs (and the resulting currents) when the electromagnet is turned on or off.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23214,59,36,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image77.png'),
(27836,68,543,'paragraph',NULL,'x = ( v o cos θ o ) t','[{"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23215,59,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.5 Magnetic field B i at the ith area element. dA i represents area vector of the i th area element.','[{"s": "Figure 6.5 Magnetic field B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at the ith area element. dA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "represents area vector of the i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "area element.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23216,59,38,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitude of the induced emf in a circuit is equal to the time rate of change of magnetic flux through the circuit.','[{"s": "The magnitude of the induced emf in a circuit is equal to the time rate of change of magnetic flux through the circuit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23218,59,40,'paragraph',NULL,'Electromagnetic_Induction-image64.png (6.3)','[{"s": "Electromagnetic_Induction-image64.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(6.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23219,59,41,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image65.png'),
(23220,59,42,'paragraph',NULL,'Michael Faraday [1791–1867] Faraday made numerous contributions to science, viz., the discovery of electromagnetic induction, the laws of electrolysis, benzene, and the fact that the plane of polarisation is rotated in an electric field. He is also credited with the invention of the electric motor, the electric generator and the transformer. He is widely regarded as the greatest experimental scientist of the nineteenth century.','[{"s": "Michael Faraday [1791–1867] Faraday made numerous contributions to science, viz., the discovery of electromagnetic induction, the laws of electrolysis, benzene, and the fact that the plane of polarisation is rotated in an electric field. He is also credited with the invention of the electric motor, the electric generator and the transformer. He is widely regarded as the greatest experimental scientist of the nineteenth century.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23221,59,43,'paragraph',NULL,'The negative sign indicates the direction of ε and hence the direction of current in a closed loop. This will be discussed in detail in the next section.','[{"s": "The negative sign indicates the direction of ε and hence the direction of current in a closed loop. This will be discussed in detail in the next section.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23223,59,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Electromagnetic_Induction-image83.png (6.4)','[{"s": "Electromagnetic_Induction-image83.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(6.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23224,59,46,'paragraph',NULL,'The induced emf can be increased by increasing the number of turns N of a closed coil.','[{"s": "The induced emf can be increased by increasing the number of turns N of a closed coil.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23225,59,47,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eqs. (6.1) and (6.2), we see that the flux can be varied by changing any one or more of the terms B, A and θ. In Experiments 6.1 and 6.2 in Section 6.2, the flux is changed by varying B. The flux can also be altered by changing the shape of a coil (that is, by shrinking it or stretching it) in a magnetic field, or rotating a coil in a magnetic field such that the angle θ between B and A changes. In these cases too, an emf is induced in the respective coils.','[{"s": "From Eqs. (6.1) and (6.2), we see that the flux can be varied by changing any one or more of the terms B, A and θ. In Experiments 6.1 and 6.2 in Section 6.2, the flux is changed by varying B. The flux can also be altered by changing the shape of a coil (that is, by shrinking it or stretching it) in a magnetic field, or rotating a coil in a magnetic field such that the angle θ between B and A changes. In these cases too, an emf is induced in the respective coils.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23226,59,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.1 Consider Experiment 6.2. (a) What would you do to obtain a large deflection of the galvanometer? (b) How would you demonstrate the presence of an induced current in the absence of a galvanometer?','[{"s": "Example 6.1 Consider Experiment 6.2. (a) What would you do to obtain a large deflection of the galvanometer? (b) How would you demonstrate the presence of an induced current in the absence of a galvanometer?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23227,59,49,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23228,59,50,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) To obtain a large deflection, one or more of the following steps can be taken: (i) Use a rod made of soft iron inside the coil C2, (ii) Connect the coil to a powerful battery, and (iii) Move the arrangement rapidly towards the test coil C1.','[{"s": "(a) To obtain a large deflection, one or more of the following steps can be taken: (i) Use a rod made of soft iron inside the coil C2, (ii) Connect the coil to a powerful battery, and (iii) Move the arrangement rapidly towards the test coil C1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23229,59,51,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Replace the galvanometer by a small bulb, the kind one finds in a small torch light. The relative motion between the two coils will cause the bulb to glow and thus demonstrate the presence of an induced current.','[{"s": "(b) Replace the galvanometer by a small bulb, the kind one finds in a small torch light. The relative motion between the two coils will cause the bulb to glow and thus demonstrate the presence of an induced current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23230,59,52,'paragraph',NULL,'In experimental physics one must learn to innovate. Michael Faraday who is ranked as one of the best experimentalists ever, was legendary for his innovative skills.','[{"s": "In experimental physics one must learn to innovate. Michael Faraday who is ranked as one of the best experimentalists ever, was legendary for his innovative skills.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23231,59,53,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.2 A square loop of side 10 cm and resistance 0.5 Ω is placed vertically in the east-west plane. A uniform magnetic field of 0.10 T is set up across the plane in the north-east direction. The magnetic field is decreased to zero in 0.70 s at a steady rate. Determine the magnitudes of induced emf and current during this time-interval.','[{"s": "Example 6.2 A square loop of side 10 cm and resistance 0.5 Ω is placed vertically in the east-west plane. A uniform magnetic field of 0.10 T is set up across the plane in the north-east direction. The magnetic field is decreased to zero in 0.70 s at a steady rate. Determine the magnitudes of induced emf and current during this time-interval.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23232,59,54,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution The angle θ made by the area vector of the coil with the magnetic field is 45°. From Eq. (6.1), the initial magnetic flux is','[{"s": "Solution The angle θ made by the area vector of the coil with the magnetic field is 45°. From Eq. (6.1), the initial magnetic flux is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23233,59,55,'paragraph',NULL,'Φ = BA cos θ','[{"s": "Φ = BA cos θ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23234,59,56,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image48.png'),
(23235,59,57,'paragraph',NULL,'Final flux, Φ min = 0','[{"s": "Final flux, Φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "min", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23236,59,58,'paragraph',NULL,'The change in flux is brought about in 0.70 s. From Eq. (6.3), the magnitude of the induced emf is given by','[{"s": "The change in flux is brought about in 0.70 s. From Eq. (6.3), the magnitude of the induced emf is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23237,59,59,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image52.png'),
(23238,59,60,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image39.png'),
(23239,59,61,'paragraph',NULL,'And the magnitude of the current is','[{"s": "And the magnitude of the current is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23240,59,62,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image51.png'),
(23241,59,63,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the earth’s magnetic field also produces a flux through the loop. But it is a steady field (which does not change within the time span of the experiment) and hence does not induce any emf.','[{"s": "Note that the earth’s magnetic field also produces a flux through the loop. But it is a steady field (which does not change within the time span of the experiment) and hence does not induce any emf.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23244,59,66,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23245,59,67,'paragraph',NULL,'Initial flux through the coil,','[{"s": "Initial flux through the coil,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23246,59,68,'paragraph',NULL,'Φ B (initial) = BA cos θ','[{"s": "Φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B (initial)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= BA cos θ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23247,59,69,'paragraph',NULL,'= 3.0 × 10 –5 × (π ×10 –2 ) × cos 0°','[{"s": "= 3.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× (π ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") × cos 0°", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23248,59,70,'paragraph',NULL,'= 3π × 10 –7 Wb','[{"s": "= 3π × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Wb", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23249,59,71,'paragraph',NULL,'Final flux after the rotation,','[{"s": "Final flux after the rotation,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23250,59,72,'paragraph',NULL,'Φ B (final) = 3.0 × 10 –5 × (π ×10 –2 ) × cos 180°','[{"s": "Φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B (final)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× (π ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") × cos 180°", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23251,59,73,'paragraph',NULL,'= –3π × 10 –7 Wb','[{"s": "= –3π × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Wb", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23252,59,74,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, estimated value of the induced emf is,','[{"s": "Therefore, estimated value of the induced emf is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23253,59,75,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image80.png'),
(23254,59,76,'paragraph',NULL,'= 500 × (6π × 10 –7 )/0.25','[{"s": "= 500 × (6π × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")/0.25", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23255,59,77,'paragraph',NULL,'= 3.8 × 10 –3 V','[{"s": "= 3.8 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23256,59,78,'paragraph',NULL,'I = ε/R = 1.9 × 10 –3 A','[{"s": "I = ε/R = 1.9 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23257,59,79,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the magnitudes of ε and I are the estimated values. Their instantaneous values are different and depend upon the speed of rotation at the particular instant.','[{"s": "Note that the magnitudes of ε and I are the estimated values. Their instantaneous values are different and depend upon the speed of rotation at the particular instant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23258,59,80,'heading',3,'6.5 LENZ’S LAW AND CONSERVATION OF ENERGY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23259,59,81,'paragraph',NULL,'In 1834, German physicist Heinrich Friedrich Lenz (1804-1865) deduced a rule, known as Lenz’s law which gives the polarity of the induced emf in a clear and concise fashion. The statement of the law is:','[{"s": "In 1834, German physicist Heinrich Friedrich Lenz (1804-1865) deduced a rule, known as Lenz’s law which gives the polarity of the induced emf in a clear and concise fashion. The statement of the law is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23260,59,82,'paragraph',NULL,'The polarity of induced emf is such that it tends to produce a current which opposes the change in magnetic flux that produced it.','[{"s": "The polarity of induced emf is such that it tends to produce a current which opposes the change in magnetic flux that produced it.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000043100", "2000043724", "2000112359"]',NULL),
(23261,59,83,'paragraph',NULL,'The negative sign shown in Eq. (6.3) represents this effect. We can understand Lenz’s law by examining Experiment 6.1 in Section 6.2.1. In Fig. 6.1, we see that the North-pole of a bar magnet is being pushed towards the closed coil. As the North-pole of the bar magnet moves towards the coil, the magnetic flux through the coil increases. Hence current is induced in the coil in such a direction that it opposes the increase in flux. This is possible only if the current in the coil is in a counter-clockwise direction with respect to an observer situated on the side of the magnet. Note that magnetic moment associated with this current has North polarity towards the North-pole of the approaching magnet. Similarly, if the North-pole of the magnet is being withdrawn from the coil, the magnetic flux through the coil will decrease. To counter this decrease in magnetic flux, the induced current in the coil flows in clockwise direction and its South-pole faces the receding North-pole of the bar magnet. This would result in an attractive force which opposes the motion of the magnet and the corresponding decrease in flux.','[{"s": "The negative sign shown in Eq. (6.3) represents this effect. We can understand Lenz’s law by examining Experiment 6.1 in Section 6.2.1. In Fig. 6.1, we see that the North-pole of a bar magnet is being pushed towards the closed coil. As the North-pole of the bar magnet moves towards the coil, the magnetic flux through the coil increases. Hence current is induced in the coil in such a direction that it opposes the increase in flux. This is possible only if the current in the coil is in a counter-clockwise direction with respect to an observer situated on the side of the magnet. Note that magnetic moment associated with this current has North polarity towards the North-pole of the approaching magnet. Similarly, if the North-pole of the magnet is being withdrawn from the coil, the magnetic flux through the coil will decrease. To counter this decrease in magnetic flux, the induced current in the coil flows in clockwise direction and its South-pole faces the receding North-pole of the bar magnet. This would result in an attractive force which opposes the motion of the magnet and the corresponding decrease in flux.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23262,59,84,'paragraph',NULL,'What will happen if an open circuit is used in place of the closed loop in the above example? In this case too, an emf is induced across the open ends of the circuit. The direction of the induced emf can be found using Lenz’s law. Consider Figs. 6.6 (a) and (b). They provide an easier way to understand the direction of induced currents. Note that the direction shown by Electromagnetic_Induction-image74.png and Electromagnetic_Induction-image58.png indicate the directions of the induced currents.','[{"s": "What will happen if an open circuit is used in place of the closed loop in the above example? In this case too, an emf is induced across the open ends of the circuit. The direction of the induced emf can be found using Lenz’s law. Consider Figs. 6.6 (a) and (b). They provide an easier way to understand the direction of induced currents. Note that the direction shown by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electromagnetic_Induction-image74.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electromagnetic_Induction-image58.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "indicate the directions of the induced currents.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23263,59,85,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image30.png'),
(23264,59,86,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.6 Illustration of Lenz’s law.','[{"s": "Figure 6.6 Illustration of Lenz’s law.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23265,59,87,'paragraph',NULL,'A little reflection on this matter should convince us on the correctness of Lenz’s law. Suppose that the induced current was in the direction opposite to the one depicted in Fig. 6.6(a). In that case, the South-pole due to the induced current will face the approaching North-pole of the magnet. The bar magnet will then be attracted towards the coil at an ever increasing acceleration. A gentle push on the magnet will initiate the process and its velocity and kinetic energy will continuously increase without expending any energy. If this can happen, one could construct a perpetual-motion machine by a suitable arrangement. This violates the law of conservation of energy and hence can not happen.','[{"s": "A little reflection on this matter should convince us on the correctness of Lenz’s law. Suppose that the induced current was in the direction opposite to the one depicted in Fig. 6.6(a). In that case, the South-pole due to the induced current will face the approaching North-pole of the magnet. The bar magnet will then be attracted towards the coil at an ever increasing acceleration. A gentle push on the magnet will initiate the process and its velocity and kinetic energy will continuously increase without expending any energy. If this can happen, one could construct a perpetual-motion machine by a suitable arrangement. This violates the law of conservation of energy and hence can not happen.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23266,59,88,'paragraph',NULL,'Now consider the correct case shown in Fig. 6.6(a). In this situation, the bar magnet experiences a repulsive force due to the induced current. Therefore, a person has to do work in moving the magnet. Where does the energy spent by the person go? This energy is dissipated by Joule heating produced by the induced current.','[{"s": "Now consider the correct case shown in Fig. 6.6(a). In this situation, the bar magnet experiences a repulsive force due to the induced current. Therefore, a person has to do work in moving the magnet. Where does the energy spent by the person go? This energy is dissipated by Joule heating produced by the induced current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23267,59,89,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.4','[{"s": "Example 6.4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23268,59,90,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.7 shows planar loops of different shapes moving out of or into a region of a magnetic field which is directed normal to the plane of the loop away from the reader. Determine the direction of induced current in each loop using Lenz’s law.','[{"s": "Figure 6.7 shows planar loops of different shapes moving out of or into a region of a magnetic field which is directed normal to the plane of the loop away from the reader. Determine the direction of induced current in each loop using Lenz’s law.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23269,59,91,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image113.png'),
(23270,59,92,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.7','[{"s": "Figure 6.7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23271,59,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23272,59,94,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The magnetic flux through the rectangular loop abcd increases, due to the motion of the loop into the region of magnetic field, The induced current must flow along the path bcdab so that it opposes the increasing flux.','[{"s": "(i) The magnetic flux through the rectangular loop abcd increases, due to the motion of the loop into the region of magnetic field, The induced current must flow along the path bcdab so that it opposes the increasing flux.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23273,59,95,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Due to the outward motion, magnetic flux through the triangular loop abc decreases due to which the induced current flows along bacb, so as to oppose the change in flux.','[{"s": "(ii) Due to the outward motion, magnetic flux through the triangular loop abc decreases due to which the induced current flows along bacb, so as to oppose the change in flux.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23274,59,96,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) As the magnetic flux decreases due to motion of the irregular shaped loop abcd out of the region of magnetic field, the induced current flows along cdabc, so as to oppose change in flux.','[{"s": "(iii) As the magnetic flux decreases due to motion of the irregular shaped loop abcd out of the region of magnetic field, the induced current flows along cdabc, so as to oppose change in flux.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23275,59,97,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that there are no induced current as long as the loops are completely inside or outside the region of the magnetic field.','[{"s": "Note that there are no induced current as long as the loops are completely inside or outside the region of the magnetic field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23277,59,99,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) A closed loop is held stationary in the magnetic field between the north and south poles of two permanent magnets held fixed. Can we hope to generate current in the loop by using very strong magnets?','[{"s": "(a) A closed loop is held stationary in the magnetic field between the north and south poles of two permanent magnets held fixed. Can we hope to generate current in the loop by using very strong magnets?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23278,59,100,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) A rectangular loop and a circular loop are moving out of a uniform magnetic field region (Fig. 6.8) to a field-free region with a constant velocity v. In which loop do you expect the induced emf to be constant during the passage out of the field region? The field is normal to the loops.','[{"s": "(c) A rectangular loop and a circular loop are moving out of a uniform magnetic field region (Fig. 6.8) to a field-free region with a constant velocity v. In which loop do you expect the induced emf to be constant during the passage out of the field region? The field is normal to the loops.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23279,59,101,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image62.png'),
(23280,59,102,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.8','[{"s": "Figure 6.8", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23281,59,103,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Predict the polarity of the capacitor in the situation described by Fig. 6.9.','[{"s": "(d) Predict the polarity of the capacitor in the situation described by Fig. 6.9.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23282,59,104,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image24.png'),
(23283,59,105,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.9','[{"s": "Figure 6.9", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23284,59,106,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23285,59,107,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) No. However strong the magnet may be, current can be induced only by changing the magnetic flux through the loop.','[{"s": "(a) No. However strong the magnet may be, current can be induced only by changing the magnetic flux through the loop.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23286,59,108,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) No current is induced in either case. Current can not be induced by changing the electric flux.','[{"s": "(b) No current is induced in either case. Current can not be induced by changing the electric flux.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23287,59,109,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The induced emf is expected to be constant only in the case of the rectangular loop. In the case of circular loop, the rate of change of area of the loop during its passage out of the field region is not constant, hence induced emf will vary accordingly.','[{"s": "(c) The induced emf is expected to be constant only in the case of the rectangular loop. In the case of circular loop, the rate of change of area of the loop during its passage out of the field region is not constant, hence induced emf will vary accordingly.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23288,59,110,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) The polarity of plate ‘A’ will be positive with respect to plate ‘B’ in the capacitor.','[{"s": "(d) The polarity of plate ‘A’ will be positive with respect to plate ‘B’ in the capacitor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23289,59,111,'heading',3,'6.6 MOTIONAL ELECTROMOTIVE FORCE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23290,59,112,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us consider a straight conductor moving in a uniform and time-independent magnetic field. Figure 6.10 shows a rectangular conductor PQRS in which the conductor PQ is free to move. The rod PQ is moved towards the left with a constant velocity v as shown in the figure. Assume that there is no loss of energy due to friction. PQRS forms a closed circuit enclosing an area that changes as PQ moves. It is placed in a uniform magnetic field B which is perpendicular to the plane of this system. If the length RQ = x and RS = l, the magnetic flux ΦB enclosed by the loop PQRS will be','[{"s": "Let us consider a straight conductor moving in a uniform and time-independent magnetic field. Figure 6.10 shows a rectangular conductor PQRS in which the conductor PQ is free to move. The rod PQ is moved towards the left with a constant velocity v as shown in the figure. Assume that there is no loss of energy due to friction. PQRS forms a closed circuit enclosing an area that changes as PQ moves. It is placed in a uniform magnetic field B which is perpendicular to the plane of this system. If the length RQ = x and RS = l, the magnetic flux ΦB enclosed by the loop PQRS will be", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23291,59,113,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image4.png'),
(23292,59,114,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.10 The arm PQ is moved to the left side, thus decreasing the area of the rectangular loop. This movement induces a current I as shown.','[{"s": "Figure 6.10 The arm PQ is moved to the left side, thus decreasing the area of the rectangular loop. This movement induces a current I as shown.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23293,59,115,'paragraph',NULL,'ΦB = Blx','[{"s": "ΦB = Blx", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23294,59,116,'paragraph',NULL,'Since x is changing with time, the rate of change of flux Φ B will induce an emf given by:','[{"s": "Since x is changing with time, the rate of change of flux Φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will induce an emf given by:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23295,59,117,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image93.png'),
(23296,59,118,'paragraph',NULL,'= Electromagnetic_Induction-image5.png (6.5)','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electromagnetic_Induction-image5.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(6.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27837,68,544,'paragraph',NULL,'y = ( v o sin θ o ) t − (1/2) g t 2','[{"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "− (1/2)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23297,59,119,'paragraph',NULL,'where we have used dx/dt = –v which is the speed of the conductor PQ. The induced emf Blv is called motional emf. Thus, we are able to produce induced emf by moving a conductor instead of varying the magnetic field, that is, by changing the magnetic flux enclosed by the circuit.','[{"s": "where we have used dx/dt = –v which is the speed of the conductor PQ. The induced emf Blv is called motional emf. Thus, we are able to produce induced emf by moving a conductor instead of varying the magnetic field, that is, by changing the magnetic flux enclosed by the circuit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23298,59,120,'paragraph',NULL,'It is also possible to explain the motional emf expression in Eq. (6.5) by invoking the Lorentz force acting on the free charge carriers of conductor PQ. Consider any arbitrary charge q in the conductor PQ. When the rod moves with speed v, the charge will also be moving with speed v in the magnetic field B. The Lorentz force on this charge is qvB in magnitude, and its direction is towards Q. All charges experience the same force, in magnitude and direction, irrespective of their position in the rod PQ.','[{"s": "It is also possible to explain the motional emf expression in Eq. (6.5) by invoking the Lorentz force acting on the free charge carriers of conductor PQ. Consider any arbitrary charge q in the conductor PQ. When the rod moves with speed v, the charge will also be moving with speed v in the magnetic field B. The Lorentz force on this charge is qvB in magnitude, and its direction is towards Q. All charges experience the same force, in magnitude and direction, irrespective of their position in the rod PQ.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23299,59,121,'paragraph',NULL,'The work done in moving the charge from P to Q is,','[{"s": "The work done in moving the charge from P to Q is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23300,59,122,'paragraph',NULL,'W = qvBl','[{"s": "W = qvBl", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23301,59,123,'paragraph',NULL,'Since emf is the work done per unit charge,','[{"s": "Since emf is the work done per unit charge,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23302,59,124,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image100.png'),
(23303,59,125,'paragraph',NULL,'= Blv','[{"s": "= Blv", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23304,59,126,'paragraph',NULL,'This equation gives emf induced across the rod PQ and is identical to Eq. (6.5). We stress that our presentation is not wholly rigorous. But it does help us to understand the basis of Faraday’s law when the conductor is moving in a uniform and time-independent magnetic field.','[{"s": "This equation gives emf induced across the rod PQ and is identical to Eq. (6.5). We stress that our presentation is not wholly rigorous. But it does help us to understand the basis of Faraday’s law when the conductor is moving in a uniform and time-independent magnetic field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23305,59,127,'paragraph',NULL,'On the other hand, it is not obvious how an emf is induced when a conductor is stationary and the magnetic field is changing – a fact which Faraday verified by numerous experiments. In the case of a stationary conductor, the force on its charges is given by','[{"s": "On the other hand, it is not obvious how an emf is induced when a conductor is stationary and the magnetic field is changing – a fact which Faraday verified by numerous experiments. In the case of a stationary conductor, the force on its charges is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23306,59,128,'paragraph',NULL,'F = q (E + v × B) = qE (6.6)','[{"s": "F = q (E + v × B) = qE (6.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23307,59,129,'paragraph',NULL,'since v = 0. Thus, any force on the charge must arise from the electric field term E alone. Therefore, to explain the existence of induced emf or induced current, we must assume that a time-varying magnetic field generates an electric field. However, we hasten to add that electric fields produced by static electric charges have properties different from those produced by time-varying magnetic fields. In Chapter 4, we learnt that charges in motion (current) can exert force/torque on a stationary magnet. Conversely, a bar magnet in motion (or more generally, a changing magnetic field) can exert a force on the stationary charge. This is the fundamental significance of the Faraday’s discovery. Electricity and magnetism are related.','[{"s": "since v = 0. Thus, any force on the charge must arise from the electric field term E alone. Therefore, to explain the existence of induced emf or induced current, we must assume that a time-varying magnetic field generates an electric field. However, we hasten to add that electric fields produced by static electric charges have properties different from those produced by time-varying magnetic fields. In Chapter 4, we learnt that charges in motion (current) can exert force/torque on a stationary magnet. Conversely, a bar magnet in motion (or more generally, a changing magnetic field) can exert a force on the stationary charge. This is the fundamental significance of the Faraday’s discovery. Electricity and magnetism are related.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23308,59,130,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.6 A metallic rod of 1 m length is rotated with a frequency of 50 rev/s, with one end hinged at the centre and the other end at the circumference of a circular metallic ring of radius 1 m, about an axis passing through the centre and perpendicular to the plane of the ring (Fig. 6.11). A constant and uniform magnetic field of 1 T parallel to the axis is present everywhere. What is the emf between the centre and the metallic ring?','[{"s": "Example 6.6 A metallic rod of 1 m length is rotated with a frequency of 50 rev/s, with one end hinged at the centre and the other end at the circumference of a circular metallic ring of radius 1 m, about an axis passing through the centre and perpendicular to the plane of the ring (Fig. 6.11). A constant and uniform magnetic field of 1 T parallel to the axis is present everywhere. What is the emf between the centre and the metallic ring?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
