(23380,59,202,'paragraph',NULL,'One obtains similar expressions for the inward motion from x = 2b to x = 0. One can appreciate the whole process by examining the sketch of various quantities displayed in Fig. 6.12(b).','[{"s": "One obtains similar expressions for the inward motion from x = 2b to x = 0. One can appreciate the whole process by examining the sketch of various quantities displayed in Fig. 6.12(b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23381,59,203,'heading',3,'6.8 EDDY CURRENTS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23382,59,204,'paragraph',NULL,'So far we have studied the electric currents induced in well defined paths in conductors like circular loops. Even when bulk pieces of conductors are subjected to changing magnetic flux, induced currents are produced in them. However, their flow patterns resemble swirling eddies in water. This effect was discovered by physicist Foucault (1819-1868) and these currents are called eddy currents.','[{"s": "So far we have studied the electric currents induced in well defined paths in conductors like circular loops. Even when bulk pieces of conductors are subjected to changing magnetic flux, induced currents are produced in them. However, their flow patterns resemble swirling eddies in water. This effect was discovered by physicist Foucault (1819-1868) and these currents are called eddy currents.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23383,59,205,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider the apparatus shown in Fig. 6.13. A copper plate is allowed to swing like a simple pendulum between the pole pieces of a strong magnet. It is found that the motion is damped and in a little while the plate comes to a halt in the magnetic field. We can explain this phenomenon on the basis of electromagnetic induction. Magnetic flux associated with the plate keeps on changing as the plate moves in and out of the region between magnetic poles. The flux change induces eddy currents in the plate. Directions of eddy currents are opposite when the plate swings into the region between the poles and when it swings out of the region.','[{"s": "Consider the apparatus shown in Fig. 6.13. A copper plate is allowed to swing like a simple pendulum between the pole pieces of a strong magnet. It is found that the motion is damped and in a little while the plate comes to a halt in the magnetic field. We can explain this phenomenon on the basis of electromagnetic induction. Magnetic flux associated with the plate keeps on changing as the plate moves in and out of the region between magnetic poles. The flux change induces eddy currents in the plate. Directions of eddy currents are opposite when the plate swings into the region between the poles and when it swings out of the region.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23384,59,206,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image20.png'),
(23385,59,207,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.13 Eddy currents are generated in the copper plate, while entering and leaving the region of magnetic field.','[{"s": "Figure 6.13 Eddy currents are generated in the copper plate, while entering and leaving the region of magnetic field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23386,59,208,'paragraph',NULL,'If rectangular slots are made in the copper plate as shown in Fig. 6.14, area available to the flow of eddy currents is less. Thus, the pendulum plate with holes or slots reduces electromagnetic damping and the plate swings more freely. Note that magnetic moments of the induced currents (which oppose the motion) depend upon the area enclosed by the currents (recall equation m = IA in Chapter 4).','[{"s": "If rectangular slots are made in the copper plate as shown in Fig. 6.14, area available to the flow of eddy currents is less. Thus, the pendulum plate with holes or slots reduces electromagnetic damping and the plate swings more freely. Note that magnetic moments of the induced currents (which oppose the motion) depend upon the area enclosed by the currents (recall equation m = IA in Chapter 4).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23387,59,209,'paragraph',NULL,'This fact is helpful in reducing eddy currents in the metallic cores of transformers, electric motors and other such devices in which a coil is to be wound over metallic core. Eddy currents are undesirable since they heat up the core and dissipate electrical energy in the form of heat. Eddy currents are minimised by using laminations of metal to make a metal core. The laminations are separated by an insulating material like lacquer. The plane of the laminations must be arranged parallel to the magnetic field, so that they cut across the eddy current paths. This arrangement reduces the strength of the eddy currents. Since the dissipation of electrical energy into heat depends on the square of the strength of electric current, heat loss is substantially reduced.','[{"s": "This fact is helpful in reducing eddy currents in the metallic cores of transformers, electric motors and other such devices in which a coil is to be wound over metallic core. Eddy currents are undesirable since they heat up the core and dissipate electrical energy in the form of heat. Eddy currents are minimised by using laminations of metal to make a metal core. The laminations are separated by an insulating material like lacquer. The plane of the laminations must be arranged parallel to the magnetic field, so that they cut across the eddy current paths. This arrangement reduces the strength of the eddy currents. Since the dissipation of electrical energy into heat depends on the square of the strength of electric current, heat loss is substantially reduced.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23388,59,210,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image44.png'),
(23389,59,211,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 6.14 Cutting slots in the copper plate reduces the effect of eddy currents.','[{"s": "Figure 6.14 Cutting slots in the copper plate reduces the effect of eddy currents.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23391,59,213,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Magnetic braking in trains: Strong electromagnets are situated above the rails in some electrically powered trains. When the electromagnets are activated, the eddy currents induced in the rails oppose the motion of the train. As there are no mechanical linkages, the braking effect is smooth.','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Magnetic braking in trains:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Strong electromagnets are situated above the rails in some electrically powered trains. When the electromagnets are activated, the eddy currents induced in the rails oppose the motion of the train. As there are no mechanical linkages, the braking effect is smooth.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23390,59,212,'paragraph',NULL,'Eddy currents are used to advantage in certain applications like:','[{"s": "Eddy currents are used to advantage in certain applications like:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000043016", "2000043151", "2000043522", "2000044203", "2000112291"]',NULL),
(23392,59,214,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Electromagnetic damping : Certain galvanometers have a fixed core made of nonmagnetic metallic material. When the coil oscillates, the eddy currents generated in the core oppose the motion and bring the coil to rest quickly.','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electromagnetic damping", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Certain galvanometers have a fixed core made of nonmagnetic metallic material. When the coil oscillates, the eddy currents generated in the core oppose the motion and bring the coil to rest quickly.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23393,59,215,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Induction furnace : Induction furnace can be used to produce high temperatures and can be utilised to prepare alloys, by melting the constituent metals. A high frequency alternating current is passed through a coil which surrounds the metals to be melted. The eddy currents generated in the metals produce high temperatures sufficient to melt it.','[{"s": "(iii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Induction furnace", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Induction furnace can be used to produce high temperatures and can be utilised to prepare alloys, by melting the constituent metals. A high frequency alternating current is passed through a coil which surrounds the metals to be melted. The eddy currents generated in the metals produce high temperatures sufficient to melt it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23394,59,216,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Electric power meters : The shiny metal disc in the electric power meter (analogue type) rotates due to the eddy currents. Electric currents are induced in the disc by magnetic fields produced by sinusoidally varying currents in a coil.','[{"s": "(iv)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electric power meters", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": The shiny metal disc in the electric power meter (analogue type) rotates due to the eddy currents. Electric currents are induced in the disc by magnetic fields produced by sinusoidally varying currents in a coil.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23395,59,217,'paragraph',NULL,'You can observe the rotating shiny disc in the power meter of your house.','[{"s": "You can observe the rotating shiny disc in the power meter of your house.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23396,59,218,'heading',3,'ELECTROMAGNETIC DAMPING',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23397,59,219,'paragraph',NULL,'Take two hollow thin cylindrical pipes of equal internal diameters made of aluminium and PVC, respectively. Fix them vertically with clamps on retort stands. Take a small cylinderical magnet having diameter slightly smaller than the inner diameter of the pipes and drop it through each pipe in such a way that the magnet does not touch the sides of the pipes during its fall. You will observe that the magnet dropped through the PVC pipe takes the same time to come out of the pipe as it would take when dropped through the same height without the pipe. Note the time it takes to come out of the pipe in each case. You will see that the magnet takes much longer time in the case of aluminium pipe. Why is it so? It is due to the eddy currents that are generated in the aluminium pipe which oppose the change in magnetic flux, i.e., the motion of the magnet. The retarding force due to the eddy currents inhibits the motion of the magnet. Such phenomena are referred to as electromagnetic damping. Note that eddy currents are not generated in PVC pipe as its material is an insulator whereas aluminium is a conductor.','[{"s": "Take two hollow thin cylindrical pipes of equal internal diameters made of aluminium and PVC, respectively. Fix them vertically with clamps on retort stands. Take a small cylinderical magnet having diameter slightly smaller than the inner diameter of the pipes and drop it through each pipe in such a way that the magnet does not touch the sides of the pipes during its fall. You will observe that the magnet dropped through the PVC pipe takes the same time to come out of the pipe as it would take when dropped through the same height without the pipe. Note the time it takes to come out of the pipe in each case. You will see that the magnet takes much longer time in the case of aluminium pipe. Why is it so? It is due to the eddy currents that are generated in the aluminium pipe which oppose the change in magnetic flux, i.e., the motion of the magnet. The retarding force due to the eddy currents inhibits the motion of the magnet. Such phenomena are referred to as electromagnetic damping. Note that eddy currents are not generated in PVC pipe as its material is an insulator whereas aluminium is a conductor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23398,59,220,'heading',3,'6.9 INDUCTANCE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23399,59,221,'paragraph',NULL,'An electric current can be induced in a coil by flux change produced by another coil in its vicinity or flux change produced by the same coil. These two situations are described separately in the next two sub-sections. However, in both the cases, the flux through a coil is proportional to the current. That is, Φ B α I.','[{"s": "An electric current can be induced in a coil by flux change produced by another coil in its vicinity or flux change produced by the same coil. These two situations are described separately in the next two sub-sections. However, in both the cases, the flux through a coil is proportional to the current. That is, Φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "α I.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23400,59,222,'paragraph',NULL,'Further, if the geometry of the coil does not vary with time then,','[{"s": "Further, if the geometry of the coil does not vary with time then,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23401,59,223,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image67.png'),
(23402,59,224,'paragraph',NULL,'For a closely wound coil of N turns, the same magnetic flux is linked with all the turns. When the flux Φ B through the coil changes, each turn contributes to the induced emf. Therefore, a term called flux linkage is used which is equal to NΦ B for a closely wound coil and in such a case','[{"s": "For a closely wound coil of N turns, the same magnetic flux is linked with all the turns. When the flux Φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "through the coil changes, each turn contributes to the induced emf. Therefore, a term called flux linkage is used which is equal to NΦ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for a closely wound coil and in such a case", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23403,59,225,'paragraph',NULL,'NΦ B ∝ I','[{"s": "NΦ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∝ I", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23404,59,226,'paragraph',NULL,'The constant of proportionality, in this relation, is called inductance. We shall see that inductance depends only on the geometry of the coil and intrinsic material properties. This aspect is akin to capacitance which for a parallel plate capacitor depends on the plate area and plate separation (geometry) and the dielectric constant K of the intervening medium (intrinsic material property).','[{"s": "The constant of proportionality, in this relation, is called inductance. We shall see that inductance depends only on the geometry of the coil and intrinsic material properties. This aspect is akin to capacitance which for a parallel plate capacitor depends on the plate area and plate separation (geometry) and the dielectric constant K of the intervening medium (intrinsic material property).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27838,68,545,'paragraph',NULL,'v x = v ox = v o cos θ o','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ox", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23405,59,227,'paragraph',NULL,'Inductance is a scalar quantity. It has the dimensions of [M L 2 T –2 A –2 ] given by the dimensions of flux divided by the dimensions of current. The SI unit of inductance is henry and is denoted by H. It is named in honour of Joseph Henry who discovered electromagnetic induction in USA, independently of Faraday in England.','[{"s": "Inductance is a scalar quantity. It has the dimensions of [M L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] given by the dimensions of flux divided by the dimensions of current. The SI unit of inductance is henry and is denoted by H. It is named in honour of Joseph Henry who discovered electromagnetic induction in USA, independently of Faraday in England.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23406,59,228,'heading',4,'6.9.2 SELF-INDUCTANCE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
