(23450,59,272,'paragraph',NULL,'We have already obtained the relation for the electrostatic energy stored per unit volume in a parallel plate capacitor (refer to Chapter 2, Eq. 2.77),','[{"s": "We have already obtained the relation for the electrostatic energy stored per unit volume in a parallel plate capacitor (refer to Chapter 2, Eq. 2.77),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23451,59,273,'paragraph',NULL,'Electromagnetic_Induction-image95.png (2.77)','[{"s": "Electromagnetic_Induction-image95.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.77)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23452,59,274,'paragraph',NULL,'In both the cases energy is proportional to the square of the field strength. Equations (6.20) and (2.77) have been derived for special cases: a solenoid and a parallel plate capacitor, respectively. But they are general and valid for any region of space in which a magnetic field or/and an electric field exist.','[{"s": "In both the cases energy is proportional to the square of the field strength. Equations (6.20) and (2.77) have been derived for special cases: a solenoid and a parallel plate capacitor, respectively. But they are general and valid for any region of space in which a magnetic field or/and an electric field exist.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23453,59,275,'heading',4,'6.9.1 MUTUAL INDUCTANCE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23454,59,276,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider Fig. 6.15 which shows two long co-axial solenoids each of length l. We denote the radius of the inner solenoid S 1 by r 1 and the number of turns per unit length by n 1 . The corresponding quantities for the outer solenoid S 2 are r 2 and n 2 , respectively. Let N 1 and N 2 be the total number of turns of coils S 1 and S 2 , respectively.','[{"s": "Consider Fig. 6.15 which shows two long co-axial solenoids each of length l. We denote the radius of the inner solenoid S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the number of turns per unit length by n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The corresponding quantities for the outer solenoid S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively. Let N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the total number of turns of coils S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23456,59,278,'paragraph',NULL,'N 1 ϕ 1 =M 12 I 2 (6.9)','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ϕ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(6.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23457,59,279,'paragraph',NULL,'M 12 is called the mutual inductance of solenoid S 1 with respect to solenoid S 2 . It is also referred to as the coefficient of mutual induction.','[{"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called the mutual inductance of solenoid S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with respect to solenoid S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is also referred to as the coefficient of mutual induction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23458,59,280,'paragraph',NULL,'For these simple co-axial solenoids it is possible to calculate M 12 . The magnetic field due to the current I 2 in S 2 is µ 0 n 2 I 2 . The resulting flux linkage with coil S 1 is,','[{"s": "For these simple co-axial solenoids it is possible to calculate M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The magnetic field due to the current I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The resulting flux linkage with coil S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23459,59,281,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image10.png'),
(23460,59,282,'paragraph',NULL,'Electromagnetic_Induction-image14.png (6.10) where n 1 l is the total number of turns in solenoid S 1 . Thus, from Eq. (6.9) and Eq. (6.10),','[{"s": "Electromagnetic_Induction-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(6.10) where n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l is the total number of turns in solenoid S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, from Eq. (6.9) and Eq. (6.10),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23461,59,283,'paragraph',NULL,'M 12 = µ 0 n 1 n 2 πr 12 l (6.11)','[{"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "πr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l (6.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23462,59,284,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that we neglected the edge effects and considered the magnetic field µ 0 n 2 I 2 to be uniform throughout the length and width of the solenoid S 2 . This is a good approximation keeping in mind that the solenoid is long, implying l >> r 2 .','[{"s": "Note that we neglected the edge effects and considered the magnetic field µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to be uniform throughout the length and width of the solenoid S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This is a good approximation keeping in mind that the solenoid is long, implying l >> r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23463,59,285,'paragraph',NULL,'We now consider the reverse case. A current I 1 is passed through the solenoid S 1 and the flux linkage with coil S 2 is,','[{"s": "We now consider the reverse case. A current I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is passed through the solenoid S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the flux linkage with coil S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23464,59,286,'paragraph',NULL,'N 2 Φ 2 = M 21 I 1 (6.12)','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(6.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23465,59,287,'paragraph',NULL,'M 21 is called the mutual inductance of solenoid S 2 with respect to solenoid S 1 .','[{"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called the mutual inductance of solenoid S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with respect to solenoid S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27839,68,546,'paragraph',NULL,'v y = v o sin θ o − g t','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23466,59,288,'paragraph',NULL,'The flux due to the current I 1 in S 1 can be assumed to be confined solely inside S 1 since the solenoids are very long. Thus, flux linkage with solenoid S 2 is','[{"s": "The flux due to the current I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be assumed to be confined solely inside S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "since the solenoids are very long. Thus, flux linkage with solenoid S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
