(23567,59,389,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 6.2:','[{"s": "Question 6.2:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23568,59,390,'paragraph',NULL,'Use Lenz\'s law to determine the direction of induced current in the situations described by Fig.','[{"s": "Use Lenz\'s law to determine the direction of induced current in the situations described by Fig.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23569,59,391,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) A wire of irregular shape turning into a circular shape','[{"s": "(a) A wire of irregular shape turning into a circular shape", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23570,59,392,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) A circular loop being deformed into a narrow straight wire.','[{"s": "(b) A circular loop being deformed into a narrow straight wire.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23571,59,393,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Induction-image102.png'),
(27841,68,548,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image6.png'),
(23572,59,394,'paragraph',NULL,'Q 3. A long solenoid with 15 turns per cm has a small loop of area 2.0 cm 2 placed inside the solenoid normal to its axis. If the current carried by the solenoid changes steadily from 2.0 A to 4.0 A in 0.1 s, what is the induced emf in the loop while the current is changing?','[{"s": "Q 3. A long solenoid with 15 turns per cm has a small loop of area 2.0 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "placed inside the solenoid normal to its axis. If the current carried by the solenoid changes steadily from 2.0 A to 4.0 A in 0.1 s, what is the induced emf in the loop while the current is changing?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23574,59,396,'paragraph',NULL,'Q 5. We are rotating a 1 m long metallic rod with an angular frequency of 400 rad s −1 with an axis normal to the rod passing through its one end. And on to the other end of the rod, it is connected with a circular metallic ring. There exist a uniform magnetic field of 0.5 T which is parallel to the axis everywhere. Find out the emf induced between the center and the ring.','[{"s": "Q 5. We are rotating a 1 m long metallic rod with an angular frequency of 400 rad", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with an axis normal to the rod passing through its one end. And on to the other end of the rod, it is connected with a circular metallic ring. There exist a uniform magnetic field of 0.5 T which is parallel to the axis everywhere. Find out the emf induced between the center and the ring.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23575,59,397,'paragraph',NULL,'Q 6. A circular coil of radius 8.0 cm and 20 turns is rotated about its vertical diameter with an angular speed of 50 rad s –1 in a uniform horizontal magnetic field of magnitude 3.0 × 10–2 T. Obtain the maximum and average emf induced in the coil. If the coil forms a closed loop of resistance 10 Ω, calculate the maximum value of current in the coil. Calculate the average power loss due to Joule heating. Where does this power come from?','[{"s": "Q 6. A circular coil of radius 8.0 cm and 20 turns is rotated about its vertical diameter with an angular speed of 50 rad s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in a uniform horizontal magnetic field of magnitude 3.0 × 10–2 T. Obtain the maximum and average emf induced in the coil. If the coil forms a closed loop of resistance 10 Ω, calculate the maximum value of current in the coil. Calculate the average power loss due to Joule heating. Where does this power come from?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23576,59,398,'paragraph',NULL,'Q 7. A horizontal straight wire 10 m long extending from east to west is falling with a speed of 5.0 m s –1 , at right angles to the horizontal component of the earth’s magnetic field, 0.30 × 10 –4 Wb m –2','[{"s": "Q 7. A horizontal straight wire 10 m long extending from east to west is falling with a speed of 5.0 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", at right angles to the horizontal component of the earth’s magnetic field, 0.30 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Wb m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23577,59,399,'paragraph',NULL,'Q 8. Current in a circuit falls from 5.0 A to 0.0 A in 0.1 s. If an average emf of 200 V induced, give an estimate of the self-inductance of the circuit.','[{"s": "Q 8. Current in a circuit falls from 5.0 A to 0.0 A in 0.1 s. If an average emf of 200 V induced, give an estimate of the self-inductance of the circuit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23578,59,400,'paragraph',NULL,'Q 9. A pair of adjacent coils has a mutual inductance of 1.5 H. If the current in one coil changes from 0 to 20 A in 0.5 s, what is the change of flux linkage with the other coil?','[{"s": "Q 9. A pair of adjacent coils has a mutual inductance of 1.5 H. If the current in one coil changes from 0 to 20 A in 0.5 s, what is the change of flux linkage with the other coil?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23579,59,401,'paragraph',NULL,'Q 10. A jet plane is traveling towards the west at a speed of 1800 km/h. What is the voltage difference developed between the ends of the wing having a span of 25 m, if the Earth’s magnetic field at the location has a magnitude of 5 × 10 –4 T and the dip angle is 30°.','[{"s": "Q 10. A jet plane is traveling towards the west at a speed of 1800 km/h. What is the voltage difference developed between the ends of the wing having a span of 25 m, if the Earth’s magnetic field at the location has a magnitude of 5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T and the dip angle is 30°.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
