(27845,68,552,'paragraph',NULL,'17. When an object follows a circular path at constant speed, the motion of the object is called uniform circular motion . The magnitude of its acceleration is a c = v 2 /R . The direction of a c is always towards the centre of the circle.','[{"s": "17. When an object follows a circular path at constant speed, the motion of the object is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "uniform circular motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The magnitude of its acceleration is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/R", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The direction of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is always towards the centre of the circle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23648,59,470,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 31. A metallic ring of mass m and radius l (ring being horizontal) is falling under gravity in a region having a magnetic field. If z is the vertical direction, the z-component of the magnetic field is B z =B 0 (1+λ z) . If R is the resistance of the ring and if the ring has reached a constant velocity, use the conservation of energy to determine v in terms of m, B, λ and acceleration due to gravity g.','[{"s": "Q. 31. A metallic ring of mass m and radius l (ring being horizontal) is falling under gravity in a region having a magnetic field. If z is the vertical direction, the z-component of the magnetic field is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1+λ z)", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If R is the resistance of the ring and if the ring has reached a constant velocity, use the conservation of energy to determine v in terms of m, B,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and acceleration due to gravity g.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23649,59,471,'paragraph',NULL,'Q. 32. A long solenoid S and n turns per meter, with a diameter a. At the center of this coil, we place a smaller coil of N turns and diameter b (where b<a). If the current in the solenoid increases linearly, with time, what is the induced emf appearing in the smaller coil. Plot graph showing the nature of variation in emf, if current varies as a function of','[{"s": "Q. 32. A long solenoid S and n turns per meter, with a diameter a. At the center of this coil, we place a smaller coil of N turns and diameter b (where b<a). If the current in the solenoid increases linearly, with time, what is the induced emf appearing in the smaller coil. Plot graph showing the nature of variation in emf, if current varies as a function of", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
