(23710,60,58,'heading',4,'8.3 ELECTROMAGNETIC WAVES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23711,60,59,'heading',4,'8.3.1 SOURCES OF ELECTROMAGNETIC WAVES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23713,60,61,'paragraph',NULL,'From the preceding discussion, it might appear easy to test the prediction that light is an electromagnetic wave. We might think that all we needed to do was to set up an ac circuit in which the current oscillate at the frequency of visible light, say, yellow light. But, alas, that is not possible. The frequency of yellow light is about 6 × 10 14 Hz, while the frequency that we get even with modern electronic circuits is hardly about 10 11 Hz. This is why the experimental demonstration of electromagnetic wave had to come in the low frequency region (the radio wave region), as in the Hertz’s experiment (1887).','[{"s": "From the preceding discussion, it might appear easy to test the prediction that light is an electromagnetic wave. We might think that all we needed to do was to set up an ac circuit in which the current oscillate at the frequency of visible light, say, yellow light. But, alas, that is not possible. The frequency of yellow light is about 6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz, while the frequency that we get even with modern electronic circuits is hardly about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz. This is why the experimental demonstration of electromagnetic wave had to come in the low frequency region (the radio wave region), as in the Hertz’s experiment (1887).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23714,60,62,'paragraph',NULL,'Hertz’s successful experimental test of Maxwell’s theory created a sensation and sparked off other important works in this field. Two important achievements in this connection deserve mention. Seven years after Hertz, Jagdish Chandra Bose, working at Calcutta (now Kolkata), succeeded in producing and observing electromagnetic waves of much shorter wavelength (25 mm to 5 mm). His experiment, like that of Hertz’s, was confined to the laboratory.','[{"s": "Hertz’s successful experimental test of Maxwell’s theory created a sensation and sparked off other important works in this field. Two important achievements in this connection deserve mention. Seven years after Hertz, Jagdish Chandra Bose, working at Calcutta (now Kolkata), succeeded in producing and observing electromagnetic waves of much shorter wavelength (25 mm to 5 mm). His experiment, like that of Hertz’s, was confined to the laboratory.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23715,60,63,'paragraph',NULL,'At around the same time, Guglielmo Marconi in Italy followed Hertz’s work and succeeded in transmitting electromagnetic waves over distances of many kilometres. Marconi’s experiment marks the beginning of the field of communication using electromagnetic waves.','[{"s": "At around the same time, Guglielmo Marconi in Italy followed Hertz’s work and succeeded in transmitting electromagnetic waves over distances of many kilometres. Marconi’s experiment marks the beginning of the field of communication using electromagnetic waves.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23716,60,64,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Waves-image22.png'),
(23717,60,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Heinrich Rudolf Hertz (1857 – 1894) German physicist who was the first to broadcast and receive radio waves. He produced electro-magnetic waves, sent them through space, and measured their wave-length and speed. He showed that the nature of their vibration, reflection and refraction was the same as that of light and heat waves, establishing their identity for the first time. He also pioneered research on discharge of electricity through gases, and discovered the photoelectric effect.','[{"s": "Heinrich Rudolf Hertz (1857 – 1894) German physicist who was the first to broadcast and receive radio waves. He produced electro-magnetic waves, sent them through space, and measured their wave-length and speed. He showed that the nature of their vibration, reflection and refraction was the same as that of light and heat waves, establishing their identity for the first time. He also pioneered research on discharge of electricity through gases, and discovered the photoelectric effect.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23718,60,66,'heading',4,'8.3.2 NATURE OF ELECTROMAGNETIC WAVES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23720,60,68,'paragraph',NULL,'In Fig. 8.4, we show a typical example of a plane electromagnetic wave propagating along the z direction (the fields are shown as a function of the z coordinate, at a given time t). The electric field E x is along the x-axis, and varies sinusoidally with z, at a given time. The magnetic field B y is along the y-axis, and again varies sinusoidally with z. The electric and magnetic fields E x and B y are perpendicular to each other, and to the direction z of propagation. We can write E x and B y as follows:','[{"s": "In Fig. 8.4, we show a typical example of a plane electromagnetic wave propagating along the z direction (the fields are shown as a function of the z coordinate, at a given time t). The electric field E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is along the x-axis, and varies sinusoidally with z, at a given time. The magnetic field B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is along the y-axis, and again varies sinusoidally with z. The electric and magnetic fields E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are perpendicular to each other, and to the direction z of propagation. We can write E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23721,60,69,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Waves-image34.png'),
(23722,60,70,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 8.4 A linearly polarised electromagnetic wave, propagating in the z-direction with the oscillating electric field E along the x-direction and the oscillating magnetic field B along the y-direction.','[{"s": "Figure 8.4 A linearly polarised electromagnetic wave, propagating in the z-direction with the oscillating electric field E along the x-direction and the oscillating magnetic field B along the y-direction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
