(23789,60,137,'paragraph',NULL,'Again, since the field in the light beam is sinusoidal, the peak magnetic field is','[{"s": "Again, since the field in the light beam is sinusoidal, the peak magnetic field is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23790,60,138,'paragraph',NULL,'B o =√2B rms = 1.4 × 10 –8 T . Note that although the energy in the magnetic field is equal to the energy in the electric field, the magnetic field strength is evidently very weak.','[{"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=√2B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.4 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Note that although the energy in the magnetic field is equal to the energy in the electric field, the magnetic field strength is evidently very weak.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23791,60,139,'heading',3,'8.4 ELECTROMAGNETIC SPECTRUM',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23793,60,141,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Waves-image2.png'),
(23794,60,142,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 8.5 The electromagnetic spectrum, with common names for various part of it. The various regions do not have sharply defined boundaries.','[{"s": "Figure 8.5 The electromagnetic spectrum, with common names for various part of it. The various regions do not have sharply defined boundaries.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23796,60,144,'heading',4,'8.4.1 RADIO WAVES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23797,60,145,'paragraph',NULL,'Radio waves are produced by the accelerated motion of charges in conducting wires. They are used in radio and television communication systems. They are generally in the frequency range from 500 kHz to about 1000 MHz. The AM (amplitude modulated) band is from 530 kHz to 1710 kHz. Higher frequencies upto 54 MHz are used for short wave bands. TV waves range from 54 MHz to 890 MHz. The FM (frequency modulated) radio band extends from 88 MHz to 108 MHz. Cellular phones use radio waves to transmit voice communication in the ultrahigh frequency (UHF) band. How these waves are transmitted and received is described in Chapter 15.','[{"s": "Radio waves are produced by the accelerated motion of charges in conducting wires. They are used in radio and television communication systems. They are generally in the frequency range from 500 kHz to about 1000 MHz. The AM (amplitude modulated) band is from 530 kHz to 1710 kHz. Higher frequencies upto 54 MHz are used for short wave bands. TV waves range from 54 MHz to 890 MHz. The FM (frequency modulated) radio band extends from 88 MHz to 108 MHz. Cellular phones use radio waves to transmit voice communication in the ultrahigh frequency (UHF) band. How these waves are transmitted and received is described in Chapter 15.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23798,60,146,'heading',4,'8.4.2 MICROWAVES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23800,60,148,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Waves-image37.png'),
(23801,60,149,'heading',3,'MICROWAVE OVEN',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23802,60,150,'paragraph',NULL,'The spectrum of electromagnetic radiation contains a part known as microwaves. These waves have frequency and energy smaller than visible light and wavelength larger than it. What is the principle of a microwave oven and how does it work?','[{"s": "The spectrum of electromagnetic radiation contains a part known as microwaves. These waves have frequency and energy smaller than visible light and wavelength larger than it. What is the principle of a microwave oven and how does it work?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23803,60,151,'paragraph',NULL,'One should use porcelain vessels and not metal containers in a microwave oven because of the danger of getting a shock from accumulated electric charges. Metals may also melt from heating. The porcelain container remains unaffected and cool, because its large molecules vibrate and rotate with much smaller frequencies, and thus cannot absorb microwaves. Hence, they do not get heated up.','[{"s": "One should use porcelain vessels and not metal containers in a microwave oven because of the danger of getting a shock from accumulated electric charges. Metals may also melt from heating. The porcelain container remains unaffected and cool, because its large molecules vibrate and rotate with much smaller frequencies, and thus cannot absorb microwaves. Hence, they do not get heated up.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23804,60,152,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the basic principle of a microwave oven is to generate microwave radiation of appropriate frequency in the working space of the oven where we keep food. This way energy is not wasted in heating up the vessel. In the conventional heating method, the vessel on the burner gets heated first, and then the food inside gets heated because of transfer of energy from the vessel. In the microwave oven, on the other hand, energy is directly delivered to water molecules which is shared by the entire food.','[{"s": "Thus, the basic principle of a microwave oven is to generate microwave radiation of appropriate frequency in the working space of the oven where we keep food. This way energy is not wasted in heating up the vessel. In the conventional heating method, the vessel on the burner gets heated first, and then the food inside gets heated because of transfer of energy from the vessel. In the microwave oven, on the other hand, energy is directly delivered to water molecules which is shared by the entire food.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23805,60,153,'heading',4,'8.4.3 INFRARED WAVES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23807,60,155,'heading',4,'8.4.4 VISIBLE RAYS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27847,68,554,'paragraph',NULL,'If T is the time period of revolution of the object in circular motion and ν is its frequency, we have ω = 2π ν, v = 2πνR, a c = 4π 2 ν 2 R','[{"s": "If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the time period of revolution of the object in circular motion and ν is its frequency, we have ω = 2π ν,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2πνR,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27848,68,555,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image1.png'),
(27849,68,556,'heading',3,'POINTS TO PONDER',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23808,60,156,'paragraph',NULL,'It is the most familiar form of electromagnetic waves. It is the part of the spectrum that is detected by the human eye. It runs from about 4 × 10 14 Hz to about 7 × 10 14 Hz or a wavelength range of about 700 – 400 nm. Visible light emitted or reflected from objects around us provides us information about the world. Our eyes are sensitive to this range of wavelengths. Different animals are sensitive to different range of wavelengths. For example, snakes can detect infrared waves, and the ‘visible’ range of many insects extends well into the utraviolet.','[{"s": "It is the most familiar form of electromagnetic waves. It is the part of the spectrum that is detected by the human eye. It runs from about 4 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz to about 7 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz or a wavelength range of about 700 – 400 nm. Visible light emitted or reflected from objects around us provides us information about the world. Our eyes are sensitive to this range of wavelengths. Different animals are sensitive to different range of wavelengths. For example, snakes can detect infrared waves, and the ‘visible’ range of many insects extends well into the utraviolet.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23809,60,157,'heading',4,'8.4.5 ULTRAVIOLET RAYS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23811,60,159,'paragraph',NULL,'Welders wear special glass goggles or face masks with glass windows to protect their eyes from large amount of UV produced by welding arcs. Due to its shorter wavelengths, UV radiations can be focussed into very narrow beams for high precision applications such as LASIK (Laser-assisted in situ keratomileusis) eye surgery. UV lamps are used to kill germs in water purifiers.','[{"s": "Welders wear special glass goggles or face masks with glass windows to protect their eyes from large amount of UV produced by welding arcs. Due to its shorter wavelengths, UV radiations can be focussed into very narrow beams for high precision applications such as LASIK (Laser-assisted in situ keratomileusis) eye surgery. UV lamps are used to kill germs in water purifiers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23812,60,160,'paragraph',NULL,'Ozone layer in the atmosphere plays a protective role, and hence its depletion by chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) gas (such as freon) is a matter of international concern.','[{"s": "Ozone layer in the atmosphere plays a protective role, and hence its depletion by chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) gas (such as freon) is a matter of international concern.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23813,60,161,'heading',4,'8.4.6 X-RAYS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23814,60,162,'paragraph',NULL,'Beyond the uv region of the electromagnetic spectrum lies the x-ray region. We are familiar with x-rays because of its medical applications. It covers wavelengths from about 10 –8 m (10 nm) down to 10 –13 m (10 –4 nm). One common way to generate X-rays is to bombard a metal target by high energy electrons. X-rays are used as a diagnostic tool in medicine and as a treatment for certain forms of cancer. Because x-rays damage or destroy living tissues and organisms, care must be taken to avoid unnecessary or over exposure.','[{"s": "Beyond the uv region of the electromagnetic spectrum lies the x-ray region. We are familiar with x-rays because of its medical applications. It covers wavelengths from about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m (10 nm) down to 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m (10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nm). One common way to generate X-rays is to bombard a metal target by high energy electrons. X-rays are used as a diagnostic tool in medicine and as a treatment for certain forms of cancer. Because x-rays damage or destroy living tissues and organisms, care must be taken to avoid unnecessary or over exposure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23815,60,163,'heading',4,'8.4.7 GAMMA RAYS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23816,60,164,'paragraph',NULL,'They lie in the upper frequency range of the electromagnetic spectrum and have wavelengths of from about 10 –10 m to less than 10 –14 m. This high frequency radiation is produced in nuclear reactions and also emitted by radioactive nuclei. They are used in medicine to destroy cancer cells.','[{"s": "They lie in the upper frequency range of the electromagnetic spectrum and have wavelengths of from about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m to less than 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. This high frequency radiation is produced in nuclear reactions and also emitted by radioactive nuclei. They are used in medicine to destroy cancer cells.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23817,60,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 8.1 summarises different types of electromagnetic waves, their production and detections. As mentioned earlier, the demarcation between different regions is not sharp and there are overlaps.','[{"s": "Table 8.1 summarises different types of electromagnetic waves, their production and detections. As mentioned earlier, the demarcation between different regions is not sharp and there are overlaps.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23818,60,166,'image',NULL,'315',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Waves-image6.jpg'),
(23819,60,167,'heading',3,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23820,60,168,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Maxwell found an inconsistency in the Ampere’s law and suggested the existence of an additional current, called displacement current, to remove this inconsistency. This displacement current is due to time-varying electric field and is given by','[{"s": "1. Maxwell found an inconsistency in the Ampere’s law and suggested the existence of an additional current, called displacement current, to remove this inconsistency. This displacement current is due to time-varying electric field and is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23821,60,169,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electromagnetic_Waves-image24.png'),
(23822,60,170,'paragraph',NULL,'and acts as a source of magnetic field in exactly the same way as conduction current.','[{"s": "and acts as a source of magnetic field in exactly the same way as conduction current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23823,60,171,'paragraph',NULL,'2. An accelerating charge produces electromagnetic waves. An electric charge oscillating harmonically with frequency ν, produces electromagnetic waves of the same frequency ν. An electric dipole is a basic source of electromagnetic waves.','[{"s": "2. An accelerating charge produces electromagnetic waves. An electric charge oscillating harmonically with frequency ν, produces electromagnetic waves of the same frequency ν. An electric dipole is a basic source of electromagnetic waves.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23824,60,172,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Electromagnetic waves with wavelength of the order of a few metres were first produced and detected in the laboratory by Hertz in 1887. He thus verified a basic prediction of Maxwell’s equations.','[{"s": "3. Electromagnetic waves with wavelength of the order of a few metres were first produced and detected in the laboratory by Hertz in 1887. He thus verified a basic prediction of Maxwell’s equations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23825,60,173,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Electric and magnetic fields oscillate sinusoidally in space and time in an electromagnetic wave. The oscillating electric and magnetic fields, E and B are perpendicular to each other, and to the direction of propagation of the electromagnetic wave. For a wave of frequency ν, wavelength λ, propagating along z-direction, we have','[{"s": "4. Electric and magnetic fields oscillate sinusoidally in space and time in an electromagnetic wave. The oscillating electric and magnetic fields, E and B are perpendicular to each other, and to the direction of propagation of the electromagnetic wave. For a wave of frequency ν, wavelength λ, propagating along z-direction, we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23826,60,174,'paragraph',NULL,'E = Ex (t) = E0 sin (kz – ω t )','[{"s": "E = Ex (t) = E0 sin (kz – ω t )", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23827,60,175,'paragraph',NULL,'= E0 sin Electromagnetic_Waves-image4.png','[{"s": "= E0 sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electromagnetic_Waves-image4.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23828,60,176,'paragraph',NULL,'B = By(t) = B0 sin (kz – ω t)','[{"s": "B = By(t) = B0 sin (kz – ω t)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23829,60,177,'paragraph',NULL,'= Electromagnetic_Waves-image18.png','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electromagnetic_Waves-image18.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23830,60,178,'paragraph',NULL,'They are related by E0/B0 = c.','[{"s": "They are related by E0/B0 = c.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23831,60,179,'paragraph',NULL,'5. The speed c of electromagnetic wave in vacuum is related to µ0 and ε0 (the free space permeability and permittivity constants) as follows: Electromagnetic_Waves-image27.png . The value of c equals the speed of light obtained from optical measurements.','[{"s": "5. The speed c of electromagnetic wave in vacuum is related to µ0 and ε0 (the free space permeability and permittivity constants) as follows:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electromagnetic_Waves-image27.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". The value of c equals the speed of light obtained from optical measurements.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23832,60,180,'paragraph',NULL,'Light is an electromagnetic wave; c is, therefore, also the speed of light. Electromagnetic waves other than light also have the same velocity c in free space.','[{"s": "Light is an electromagnetic wave; c is, therefore, also the speed of light. Electromagnetic waves other than light also have the same velocity c in free space.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23833,60,181,'paragraph',NULL,'The speed of light, or of electromagnetic waves in a material medium is given by Electromagnetic_Waves-image19.png','[{"s": "The speed of light, or of electromagnetic waves in a material medium is given by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electromagnetic_Waves-image19.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23834,60,182,'paragraph',NULL,'where µ is the permeability of the medium and ε its permittivity.','[{"s": "where µ is the permeability of the medium and ε its permittivity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23835,60,183,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Electromagnetic waves carry energy as they travel through space and this energy is shared equally by the electric and magnetic fields.','[{"s": "6. Electromagnetic waves carry energy as they travel through space and this energy is shared equally by the electric and magnetic fields.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23836,60,184,'paragraph',NULL,'Electromagnetic waves transport momentum as well. When these waves strike a surface, a pressure is exerted on the surface. If total energy transferred to a surface in time t is U, total momentum delivered to this surface is p = U/c.','[{"s": "Electromagnetic waves transport momentum as well. When these waves strike a surface, a pressure is exerted on the surface. If total energy transferred to a surface in time t is U, total momentum delivered to this surface is p = U/c.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23837,60,185,'paragraph',NULL,'7. The spectrum of electromagnetic waves stretches, in principle, over an infinite range of wavelengths. Different regions are known by different names; γ-rays, X-rays, ultraviolet rays, visible rays, infrared rays, microwaves and radio waves in order of increasing wavelength from 10 –2 Å or 10 –12 m to 10 6 m.','[{"s": "7. The spectrum of electromagnetic waves stretches, in principle, over an infinite range of wavelengths. Different regions are known by different names; γ-rays, X-rays, ultraviolet rays, visible rays, infrared rays, microwaves and radio waves in order of increasing wavelength from 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Å or 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m to 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23838,60,186,'paragraph',NULL,'They interact with matter via their electric and magnetic fields which set in oscillation charges present in all matter. The detailed interaction and so the mechanism of absorption, scattering, etc., depend on the wavelength of the electromagnetic wave, and the nature of the atoms and molecules in the medium.','[{"s": "They interact with matter via their electric and magnetic fields which set in oscillation charges present in all matter. The detailed interaction and so the mechanism of absorption, scattering, etc., depend on the wavelength of the electromagnetic wave, and the nature of the atoms and molecules in the medium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23839,60,187,'paragraph',NULL,'Points to Ponder','[{"s": "Points to Ponder", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23840,60,188,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The basic difference between various types of electromagnetic waves lies in their wavelengths or frequencies since all of them travel through vacuum with the same speed. Consequently, the waves differ considerably in their mode of interaction with matter.','[{"s": "1. The basic difference between various types of electromagnetic waves lies in their wavelengths or frequencies since all of them travel through vacuum with the same speed. Consequently, the waves differ considerably in their mode of interaction with matter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23841,60,189,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Accelerated charged particles radiate electromagnetic waves. The wavelength of the electromagnetic wave is often correlated with the characteristic size of the system that radiates. Thus, gamma radiation, having wavelength of 10–14 m to 10–15 m, typically originate from an atomic nucleus. X-rays are emitted from heavy atoms. Radio waves are produced by accelerating electrons in a circuit. A transmitting antenna can most efficiently radiate waves having a wavelength of about the same size as the antenna. Visible radiation emitted by atoms is, however, much longer in wavelength than atomic size.','[{"s": "2. Accelerated charged particles radiate electromagnetic waves. The wavelength of the electromagnetic wave is often correlated with the characteristic size of the system that radiates. Thus, gamma radiation, having wavelength of 10–14 m to 10–15 m, typically originate from an atomic nucleus. X-rays are emitted from heavy atoms. Radio waves are produced by accelerating electrons in a circuit. A transmitting antenna can most efficiently radiate waves having a wavelength of about the same size as the antenna. Visible radiation emitted by atoms is, however, much longer in wavelength than atomic size.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23842,60,190,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The oscillating fields of an electromagnetic wave can accelerate charges and can produce oscillating currents. Therefore, an apparatus designed to detect electromagnetic waves is based on this fact. Hertz original ‘receiver’ worked in exactly this way. The same basic principle is utilised in practically all modern receiving devices. High frequency electromagnetic waves are detected by other means based on the physical effects they produce on interacting with matter.','[{"s": "3. The oscillating fields of an electromagnetic wave can accelerate charges and can produce oscillating currents. Therefore, an apparatus designed to detect electromagnetic waves is based on this fact. Hertz original ‘receiver’ worked in exactly this way. The same basic principle is utilised in practically all modern receiving devices. High frequency electromagnetic waves are detected by other means based on the physical effects they produce on interacting with matter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23843,60,191,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Infrared waves, with frequencies lower than those of visible light, vibrate not only the electrons, but entire atoms or molecules of a substance. This vibration increases the internal energy and consequently, the temperature of the substance. This is why infrared waves are often called heat waves.','[{"s": "4. Infrared waves, with frequencies lower than those of visible light, vibrate not only the electrons, but entire atoms or molecules of a substance. This vibration increases the internal energy and consequently, the temperature of the substance. This is why infrared waves are often called heat waves.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23844,60,192,'paragraph',NULL,'5. The centre of sensitivity of our eyes coincides with the centre of the wavelength distribution of the sun. It is because humans have evolved with visions most sensitive to the strongest wavelengths from the sun.','[{"s": "5. The centre of sensitivity of our eyes coincides with the centre of the wavelength distribution of the sun. It is because humans have evolved with visions most sensitive to the strongest wavelengths from the sun.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
