(23988,61,0,'heading',2,'Chapter Two',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23989,61,1,'heading',1,'Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23990,61,2,'heading',2,'2.1 Introduction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23991,61,3,'paragraph',NULL,'In Chapters 6 and 8 (Class XI), the notion of potential energy was introduced. When an external force does work in taking a body from a point to another against a force like spring force or gravitational force, that work gets stored as potential energy of the body. When the external force is removed, the body moves, gaining kinetic energy and losing an equal amount of potential energy. The sum of kinetic and potential energies is thus conserved. Forces of this kind are called conservative forces. Spring force and gravitational force are examples of conservative forces.','[{"s": "In Chapters 6 and 8 (Class XI), the notion of potential energy was introduced. When an external force does work in taking a body from a point to another against a force like spring force or gravitational force, that work gets stored as potential energy of the body. When the external force is removed, the body moves, gaining kinetic energy and losing an equal amount of potential energy. The sum of kinetic and potential energies is thus conserved. Forces of this kind are called conservative forces. Spring force and gravitational force are examples of conservative forces.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23992,61,4,'paragraph',NULL,'Coulomb force between two (stationary) charges is also a conservative force. This is not surprising, since both have inverse-square dependence on distance and differ mainly in the proportionality constants – the masses in the gravitational law are replaced by charges in Coulomb’s law. Thus, like the potential energy of a mass in a gravitational field, we can define electrostatic potential energy of a charge in an electrostatic field.','[{"s": "Coulomb force between two (stationary) charges is also a conservative force. This is not surprising, since both have inverse-square dependence on distance and differ mainly in the proportionality constants – the masses in the gravitational law are replaced by charges in Coulomb’s law. Thus, like the potential energy of a mass in a gravitational field, we can define electrostatic potential energy of a charge in an electrostatic field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23993,61,5,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider an electrostatic field E due to some charge configuration. First, for simplicity, consider the field E due to a charge Q placed at the origin. Now, imagine that we bring a test charge q from a point R to a point P against the repulsive force on it due to the charge Q. With reference to Fig. 2.1, this will happen if Q and q are both positive or both negative. For definiteness, let us take Q, q > 0.','[{"s": "Consider an electrostatic field E due to some charge configuration. First, for simplicity, consider the field E due to a charge Q placed at the origin. Now, imagine that we bring a test charge q from a point R to a point P against the repulsive force on it due to the charge Q. With reference to Fig. 2.1, this will happen if Q and q are both positive or both negative. For definiteness, let us take Q, q > 0.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23994,61,6,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image97.png'),
(23995,61,7,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.1 A test charge q (> 0) is moved from the point R to the point P against the repulsive force on it by the charge Q (> 0) placed at the origin.','[{"s": "Figure 2.1 A test charge q (> 0) is moved from the point R to the point P against the repulsive force on it by the charge Q (> 0) placed at the origin.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23996,61,8,'paragraph',NULL,'Two remarks may be made here. First, we assume that the test charge q is so small that it does not disturb the original configuration, namely the charge Q at the origin (or else, we keep Q fixed at the origin by some unspecified force). Second, in bringing the charge q from R to P, we apply an external force Fext just enough to counter the repulsive electric force FE (i.e, Fext= –FE). This means there is no net force on or acceleration of the charge q when it is brought from R to P, i.e., it is brought with infinitesimally slow constant speed. In this situation, work done by the external force is the negative of the work done by the electric force, and gets fully stored in the form of potential energy of the charge q. If the external force is removed on reaching P, the electric force will take the charge away from Q – the stored energy (potential energy) at P is used to provide kinetic energy to the charge q in such a way that the sum of the kinetic and potential energies is conserved.','[{"s": "Two remarks may be made here. First, we assume that the test charge q is so small that it does not disturb the original configuration, namely the charge Q at the origin (or else, we keep Q fixed at the origin by some unspecified force). Second, in bringing the charge q from R to P, we apply an external force Fext just enough to counter the repulsive electric force FE (i.e, Fext= –FE). This means there is no net force on or acceleration of the charge q when it is brought from R to P, i.e., it is brought with infinitesimally slow constant speed. In this situation, work done by the external force is the negative of the work done by the electric force, and gets fully stored in the form of potential energy of the charge q. If the external force is removed on reaching P, the electric force will take the charge away from Q – the stored energy (potential energy) at P is used to provide kinetic energy to the charge q in such a way that the sum of the kinetic and potential energies is conserved.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23997,61,9,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, work done by external forces in moving a charge q from R to P is','[{"s": "Thus, work done by external forces in moving a charge q from R to P is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23998,61,10,'paragraph',NULL,'W RP =∫ P R F ext .dr=−∫ P R F E .dr (2.1)','[{"s": "W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=∫", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ext", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".dr=−∫", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".dr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(23999,61,11,'paragraph',NULL,'This work done is against electrostatic repulsive force and gets stored as potential energy.','[{"s": "This work done is against electrostatic repulsive force and gets stored as potential energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24000,61,12,'paragraph',NULL,'At every point in electric field, a particle with charge q possesses a certain electrostatic potential energy, this work done increases its potential energy by an amount equal to potential energy difference between points R and P.','[{"s": "At every point in electric field, a particle with charge q possesses a certain electrostatic potential energy, this work done increases its potential energy by an amount equal to potential energy difference between points R and P.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24002,61,14,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image100.png (2.2)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image100.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24003,61,15,'paragraph',NULL,'(Note here that this displacement is in an opposite sense to the electric force and hence work done by electric field is negative, i.e., –W RP .)','[{"s": "(Note here that this displacement is in an opposite sense to the electric force and hence work done by electric field is negative, i.e., –W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RP", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24004,61,16,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, we can define electric potential energy difference between two points as the work required to be done by an external force in moving (without accelerating) charge q from one point to another for electric field of any arbitrary charge configuration.','[{"s": "Therefore, we can define electric potential energy difference between two points as the work required to be done by an external force in moving (without accelerating) charge q from one point to another for electric field of any arbitrary charge configuration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24005,61,17,'paragraph',NULL,'Two important comments may be made at this stage:','[{"s": "Two important comments may be made at this stage:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24006,61,18,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The right side of Eq. (2.2) depends only on the initial and final positions of the charge. It means that the work done by an electrostatic field in moving a charge from one point to another depends only on the initial and the final points and is independent of the path taken to go from one point to the other. This is the fundamental characteristic of a conservative force. The concept of the potential energy would not be meaningful if the work depended on the path. The path-independence of work done by an electrostatic field can be proved using the Coulomb’s law. We omit this proof here.','[{"s": "(i) The right side of Eq. (2.2) depends only on the initial and final positions of the charge. It means that the work done by an electrostatic field in moving a charge from one point to another depends only on the initial and the final points and is independent of the path taken to go from one point to the other. This is the fundamental characteristic of a conservative force. The concept of the potential energy would not be meaningful if the work depended on the path. The path-independence of work done by an electrostatic field can be proved using the Coulomb’s law. We omit this proof here.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24007,61,19,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Equation (2.2) defines potential energy difference in terms of the physically meaningful quantity work. Clearly, potential energy so defined is undetermined to within an additive constant.What this means is that the actual value of potential energy is not physically significant; it is only the difference of potential energy that is significant. We can always add an arbitrary constant α to potential energy at every point, since this will not change the potential energy difference:','[{"s": "(ii) Equation (2.2) defines potential energy difference in terms of the physically meaningful quantity work. Clearly, potential energy so defined is undetermined to within an additive constant.What this means is that the actual value of potential energy is not physically significant; it is only the difference of potential energy that is significant. We can always add an arbitrary constant α to potential energy at every point, since this will not change the potential energy difference:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24008,61,20,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image99.png'),
(24009,61,21,'paragraph',NULL,'Count Alessandro Volta (1745 – 1827) Italian physicist, professor at Pavia. Volta established that the animal electri- city observed by Luigi Galvani, 1737–1798, in experiments with frog muscle tissue placed in contact with dissimilar metals, was not due to any exceptional property of animal tissues but was also generated whenever any wet body was sandwiched between dissimilar metals. This led him to develop the first voltaic pile, or battery, consisting of a large stack of moist disks of cardboard (electro-lyte) sandwiched between disks of metal (electrodes).','[{"s": "Count Alessandro Volta (1745 – 1827)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Italian physicist, professor at Pavia. Volta established that the animal electri- city observed by Luigi Galvani, 1737–1798, in experiments with frog muscle tissue placed in contact with dissimilar metals, was not due to any exceptional property of animal tissues but was also generated whenever any wet body was sandwiched between dissimilar metals. This led him to develop the first voltaic pile, or battery, consisting of a large stack of moist disks of cardboard (electro-lyte) sandwiched between disks of metal (electrodes).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24010,61,22,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image105.png'),
(24011,61,23,'paragraph',NULL,'Put it differently, there is a freedom in choosing the point where potential energy is zero. A convenient choice is to have electrostatic potential energy zero at infinity. With this choice, if we take the point R at infinity, we get from Eq. (2.2)','[{"s": "Put it differently, there is a freedom in choosing the point where potential energy is zero. A convenient choice is to have electrostatic potential energy zero at infinity. With this choice, if we take the point R at infinity, we get from Eq. (2.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24012,61,24,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image103.png (2.3)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image103.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24001,61,13,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, potential energy difference','[{"s": "Thus, potential energy difference", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040468", "2000040509", "2000040515", "2000040648", "2000040652", "2000041054", "2000041058", "2000041066", "2000041074", "2000041078", "2000041456", "2000110476", "2000110478", "2000110482", "2000110486", "2000110492", "2000110518", "2000110605"]',NULL),
(24013,61,25,'paragraph',NULL,'Since the point P is arbitrary, Eq. (2.3) provides us with a definition of potential energy of a charge q at any point. Potential energy of charge q at a point (in the presence of field due to any charge configuration) is the work done by the external force (equal and opposite to the electric force) in bringing the charge q from infinity to that point.','[{"s": "Since the point P is arbitrary, Eq. (2.3) provides us with a definition of potential energy of a charge q at any point. Potential energy of charge q at a point (in the presence of field due to any charge configuration) is the work done by the external force (equal and opposite to the electric force) in bringing the charge q from infinity to that point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24014,61,26,'heading',2,'2.2 Electrostatic Potential',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24015,61,27,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider any general static charge configuration. We define potential energy of a test charge q in terms of the work done on the charge q. This work is obviously proportional to q, since the force at any point is qE, where E is the electric field at that point due to the given charge configuration. It is, therefore, convenient to divide the work by the amount of charge q, so that the resulting quantity is independent of q. In other words, work done per unit test charge is characteristic of the electric field associated with the charge configuration. This leads to the idea of electrostatic potential V due to a given charge configuration. From Eq. (2.1), we get:','[{"s": "Consider any general static charge configuration. We define potential energy of a test charge q in terms of the work done on the charge q. This work is obviously proportional to q, since the force at any point is qE, where E is the electric field at that point due to the given charge configuration. It is, therefore, convenient to divide the work by the amount of charge q, so that the resulting quantity is independent of q. In other words, work done per unit test charge is characteristic of the electric field associated with the charge configuration. This leads to the idea of electrostatic potential V due to a given charge configuration. From Eq. (2.1), we get:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24017,61,29,'paragraph',NULL,'= V P −V R (= U P −U R/ q ) (2.4)','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24018,61,30,'paragraph',NULL,'where V P and V R are the electrostatic potentials at P and R, respectively. Note, as before, that it is not the actual value of potential but the potential difference that is physically significant. If, as before, we choose the potential to be zero at infinity, Eq. (2.4) implies:','[{"s": "where V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the electrostatic potentials at P and R, respectively. Note, as before, that it is not the actual value of potential but the potential difference that is physically significant. If, as before, we choose the potential to be zero at infinity, Eq. (2.4) implies:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24019,61,31,'paragraph',NULL,'Work done by an external force in bringing a unit positive charge from infinity to a point = electrostatic potential (V) at that point.','[{"s": "Work done by an external force in bringing a unit positive charge from infinity to a point = electrostatic potential (V) at that point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24020,61,32,'paragraph',NULL,'In other words, the electrostatic potential (V) at any point in a region with electrostatic field is the work done in bringing a unit positive charge (without acceleration) from infinity to that point.','[{"s": "In other words, the electrostatic potential (V) at any point in a region with electrostatic field is the work done in bringing a unit positive charge (without acceleration) from infinity to that point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24021,61,33,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image109.png'),
(24022,61,34,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.2 Work done on a test charge q by the electrostatic field due to any given charge configuration is independent of the path, and depends only on its initial and final positions.','[{"s": "Figure 2.2 Work done on a test charge q by the electrostatic field due to any given charge configuration is independent of the path, and depends only on its initial and final positions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24023,61,35,'heading',3,'2.3 POTENTIAL DUE TO A POINT CHARGE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24024,61,36,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a point charge Q at the origin (Fig. 2.3). For definiteness, take Q to be positive. We wish to determine the potential at any point P with position vector r from the origin. For that, we must calculate the work done in bringing a unit positive test charge from infinity to the point P. For Q > 0, the work done against the repulsive force on the test charge is positive. Since work done is independent of the path, we choose a convenient path – along the radial direction from infinity to the point P.','[{"s": "Consider a point charge Q at the origin (Fig. 2.3). For definiteness, take Q to be positive. We wish to determine the potential at any point P with position vector r from the origin. For that, we must calculate the work done in bringing a unit positive test charge from infinity to the point P. For Q > 0, the work done against the repulsive force on the test charge is positive. Since work done is independent of the path, we choose a convenient path – along the radial direction from infinity to the point P.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24025,61,37,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image106.png'),
(24026,61,38,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.3 Work done in bringing a unit positive test charge from infinity to the point P, against the repulsive force of charge Q (Q > 0), is the potential at P due to the charge Q.','[{"s": "Figure 2.3 Work done in bringing a unit positive test charge from infinity to the point P, against the repulsive force of charge Q (Q > 0), is the potential at P due to the charge Q.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24027,61,39,'paragraph',NULL,'At some intermediate point P′ on the path, the electrostatic force on a unit positive charge is Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image114.png (2.5)','[{"s": "At some intermediate point P′ on the path, the electrostatic force on a unit positive charge is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image114.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24028,61,40,'paragraph',NULL,'where Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image112.png is the unit vector along OP′. Work done against this force from r′ to r′ + ∆r′ is','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image112.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is the unit vector along OP′. Work done against this force from r′ to r′ + ∆r′ is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24029,61,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image119.png (2.6)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image119.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24030,61,42,'paragraph',NULL,'The negative sign appears because for ∆r′ < 0, ∆W is positive. Total work done (W) by the external force is obtained by integrating Eq. (2.6) from r′ = ∞ to r′ = r,','[{"s": "The negative sign appears because for ∆r′ < 0, ∆W is positive. Total work done (W) by the external force is obtained by integrating Eq. (2.6) from r′ = ∞ to r′ = r,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24031,61,43,'paragraph',NULL,'W=−∫ r∞Q/4πε 0 r \'2 dr\'=[ Q/4πε 0 r\' ] r∞ = Q/4πε 0 r (2.7)','[{"s": "W=−∫", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r∞Q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "\'2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dr\'=[", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r\'", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r∞", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24033,61,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image116.png (2.8)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image116.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24034,61,46,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (2.8) is true for any sign of the charge Q, though we considered Q > 0 in its derivation. For Q < 0, V < 0, i.e., work done (by the external force) per unit positive test charge in bringing it from infinity to the point is negative. This is equivalent to saying that work done by the electrostatic force in bringing the unit positive charge form infinity to the point P is positive. [This is as it should be, since for Q < 0, the force on a unit positive test charge is attractive, so that the electrostatic force and the displacement (from infinity to P) are in the same direction.] Finally, we note that Eq. (2.8) is consistent with the choice that potential at infinity be zero.','[{"s": "Equation (2.8) is true for any sign of the charge Q, though we considered Q > 0 in its derivation. For Q < 0, V < 0, i.e., work done (by the external force) per unit positive test charge in bringing it from infinity to the point is negative. This is equivalent to saying that work done by the electrostatic force in bringing the unit positive charge form infinity to the point P is positive. [This is as it should be, since for Q < 0, the force on a unit positive test charge is attractive, so that the electrostatic force and the displacement (from infinity to P) are in the same direction.] Finally, we note that Eq. (2.8) is consistent with the choice that potential at infinity be zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24035,61,47,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image118.png'),
(24036,61,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.4 Variation of potential V with r [in units of (Q/4πε 0 ) m -1 ] (blue curve) and field with r [in units of (Q/4πε 0 ) m -2 ] (black curve) for a point charge Q.','[{"s": "Figure 2.4 Variation of potential V with r [in units of (Q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] (blue curve) and field with r [in units of (Q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] (black curve) for a point charge Q.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24037,61,49,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure (2.4) shows how the electrostatic potential (∝ 1/r) and the electrostatic field (∝ 1/r 2 ) varies with r.','[{"s": "Figure (2.4) shows how the electrostatic potential (∝ 1/r) and the electrostatic field (∝ 1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") varies with r.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24038,61,50,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.1','[{"s": "Example 2.1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24039,61,51,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Calculate the potential at a point P due to a charge of 4 × 10 –7 C located 9 cm away.','[{"s": "(a) Calculate the potential at a point P due to a charge of 4 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C located 9 cm away.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24040,61,52,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Hence obtain the work done in bringing a charge of 2 × 10 –9 C from infinity to the point P. Does the answer depend on the path along which the charge is brought?','[{"s": "(b) Hence obtain the work done in bringing a charge of 2 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C from infinity to the point P. Does the answer depend on the path along which the charge is brought?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24041,61,53,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24042,61,54,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image121.png','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image121.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24043,61,55,'paragraph',NULL,'= 4 × 104 V','[{"s": "= 4 × 104 V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24044,61,56,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) W = qV = 2 × 10 –9 C × 4 × 10 4 V','[{"s": "(b) W = qV = 2 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C × 4 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24045,61,57,'paragraph',NULL,'= 8 × 10 –5 J','[{"s": "= 8 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24046,61,58,'paragraph',NULL,'No, work done will be path independent. Any arbitrary infinitesimal path can be resolved into two perpendicular displacements: One along r and another perpendicular to r. The work done corresponding to the later will be zero.','[{"s": "No, work done will be path independent. Any arbitrary infinitesimal path can be resolved into two perpendicular displacements: One along r and another perpendicular to r. The work done corresponding to the later will be zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24047,61,59,'heading',3,'2.4 POTENTIAL DUE TO AN ELECTRIC DIPOLE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24032,61,44,'paragraph',NULL,'This, by definition is the potential at P due to the charge Q','[{"s": "This, by definition is the potential at P due to the charge Q", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040501", "2000110489"]',NULL),
(24048,61,60,'paragraph',NULL,'As we learnt in the last chapter, an electric dipole consists of two charges q and –q separated by a (small) distance 2a. Its total charge is zero. It is characterised by a dipole moment vector p whose magnitude is q × 2a and which points in the direction from –q to q (Fig. 2.5). We also saw that the electric field of a dipole at a point with position vector r depends not just on the magnitude r, but also on the angle between r and p. Further, the field falls off, at large distance, not as 1/r 2 (typical of field due to a single charge) but as 1/r 3 . We, now, determine the electric potential due to a dipole and contrast it with the potential due to a single charge.','[{"s": "As we learnt in the last chapter, an electric dipole consists of two charges q and –q separated by a (small) distance 2a. Its total charge is zero. It is characterised by a dipole moment vector p whose magnitude is q × 2a and which points in the direction from –q to q (Fig. 2.5). We also saw that the electric field of a dipole at a point with position vector r depends not just on the magnitude r, but also on the angle between r and p. Further, the field falls off, at large distance, not as 1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(typical of field due to a single charge) but as 1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We, now, determine the electric potential due to a dipole and contrast it with the potential due to a single charge.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24049,61,61,'paragraph',NULL,'As before, we take the origin at the centre of the dipole. Now we know that the electric field obeys the superposition principle. Since potential is related to the work done by the field, electrostatic potential also follows the superposition principle. Thus, the potential due to the dipole is the sum of potentials due to the charges q and –q','[{"s": "As before, we take the origin at the centre of the dipole. Now we know that the electric field obeys the superposition principle. Since potential is related to the work done by the field, electrostatic potential also follows the superposition principle. Thus, the potential due to the dipole is the sum of potentials due to the charges q and –q", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24050,61,62,'paragraph',NULL,'V= 14πε 0 ( qr 1 − 1r 2 ) (2.9)','[{"s": "V=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "qr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24051,61,63,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image124.png'),
(24052,61,64,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.5 Quantities involved in the calculation of potential due to a dipole.','[{"s": "Figure 2.5 Quantities involved in the calculation of potential due to a dipole.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24053,61,65,'paragraph',NULL,'where r 1 and r 2 are the distances of the point P from q and –q, respectively.','[{"s": "where r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the distances of the point P from q and –q, respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24054,61,66,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, by geometry,','[{"s": "Now, by geometry,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24055,61,67,'paragraph',NULL,'r 2 1 =r 2 +a 2 −2ar cosθ','[{"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−2ar cosθ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24056,61,68,'paragraph',NULL,'r 2 2 =r 2 +a 2 +2ar cosθ (2.10)','[{"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+2ar cosθ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24057,61,69,'paragraph',NULL,'We take r much greater than a (r>>a) and retain terms only upto the first order in a/r','[{"s": "We take r much greater than a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(r>>a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and retain terms only upto the first order in a/r", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24058,61,70,'paragraph',NULL,'r 2 1 ≅r 2 (1− 2a cosθ/r ) (2.11)','[{"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≅r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1−", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2a cosθ/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24059,61,71,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly,','[{"s": "Similarly,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24060,61,72,'paragraph',NULL,'r 2 2 ≅r 2 (1+ 2a cosθ/r ) (2.12)','[{"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≅r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2a cosθ/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24061,61,73,'paragraph',NULL,'Using the Binomial theorem and retaining terms upto the first order in a/r ; we obtain,','[{"s": "Using the Binomial theorem and retaining terms upto the first order in a/r ; we obtain,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24062,61,74,'paragraph',NULL,'1/r 1 ≅ 1/r (1− 2a cosθ/r ) −1/2 ≅ 1/r (1+ a/r cosθ) [2.13(a)]','[{"s": "1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≅", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1−", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2a cosθ/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≅", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cosθ)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[2.13(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24063,61,75,'paragraph',NULL,'1/r 2 ≅ 1/r (1+ 2a cosθ/r ) −1/2 ≅ 1/r (1− a/r cosθ) [2.13(b)]','[{"s": "1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≅", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2a cosθ/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≅", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1−", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cosθ)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[2.13(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24065,61,77,'paragraph',NULL,'V= q/4πε0 2acosθ/r2=pcosθ/4πε0r 2 (2.14)','[{"s": "V=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q/4πε0 2acosθ/r2=pcosθ/4πε0r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.14)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24066,61,78,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, p cos θ = p.r^','[{"s": "Now, p cos θ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p.r^", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24067,61,79,'paragraph',NULL,'where Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image125.png is the unit vector along the position vector OP.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image125.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is the unit vector along the position vector OP.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24068,61,80,'paragraph',NULL,'The electric potential of a dipole is then given by','[{"s": "The electric potential of a dipole is then given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24069,61,81,'paragraph',NULL,'V= 1/4πε 0 p.ˆr/r 2 ; (r>>a) ; (r >> a) (2.15)','[{"s": "V=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p.ˆr/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ";", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(r>>a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; (r >> a) (2.15)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24071,61,83,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eq. (2.15), potential on the dipole axis (θ = 0, π ) is given by','[{"s": "From Eq. (2.15), potential on the dipole axis (θ = 0, π ) is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24072,61,84,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image126.png (2.16)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image126.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24073,61,85,'paragraph',NULL,'(Positive sign for θ = 0, negative sign for θ = π.) The potential in the equatorial plane (θ = π/2) is zero.','[{"s": "(Positive sign for θ = 0, negative sign for θ = π.) The potential in the equatorial plane (θ = π/2) is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24074,61,86,'paragraph',NULL,'The important contrasting features of electric potential of a dipole from that due to a single charge are clear from Eqs. (2.8) and (2.15):','[{"s": "The important contrasting features of electric potential of a dipole from that due to a single charge are clear from Eqs. (2.8) and (2.15):", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24075,61,87,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The potential due to a dipole depends not just on r but also on the angle between the position vector r and the dipole moment vector p. (It is, however, axially symmetric about p. That is, if you rotate the position vector r about p, keeping θ fixed, the points corresponding to P on the cone so generated will have the same potential as at P.)','[{"s": "(i) The potential due to a dipole depends not just on r but also on the angle between the position vector r and the dipole moment vector p. (It is, however, axially symmetric about p. That is, if you rotate the position vector r about p, keeping θ fixed, the points corresponding to P on the cone so generated will have the same potential as at P.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24076,61,88,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) The electric dipole potential falls off, at large distance, as 1/r2, not as 1/r, characteristic of the potential due to a single charge. (You can refer to the Fig. 2.5 for graphs of 1/r 2 versus r and 1/r versus r, drawn there in another context.)','[{"s": "(ii) The electric dipole potential falls off, at large distance, as 1/r2, not as 1/r, characteristic of the potential due to a single charge. (You can refer to the Fig. 2.5 for graphs of 1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "versus r and 1/r versus r, drawn there in another context.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24077,61,89,'heading',3,'2.5 POTENTIAL DUE TO A SYSTEM OF CHARGES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24078,61,90,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a system of charges q 1 , q 2 ,…, q n with position vectors r 1 , r 2 ,…, r n relative to some origin (Fig. 2.6). The potential V 1 at P due to the charge q 1 is','[{"s": "Consider a system of charges q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",…, q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with position vectors r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",…, r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "relative to some origin (Fig. 2.6). The potential V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at P due to the charge q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24079,61,91,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image127.png'),
(24080,61,92,'paragraph',NULL,'where r 1P is the distance between q 1 and P.','[{"s": "where r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the distance between q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and P.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24081,61,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, the potential V 2 at P due to q 2 and V 3 due to q 3 are given by','[{"s": "Similarly, the potential V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at P due to q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "due to q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24082,61,94,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image128.png , Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image129.png','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image128.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image129.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24083,61,95,'paragraph',NULL,'where r 2P and r 3P are the distances of P from charges q 2 and q 3 , respectively; and so on for the potential due to other charges. By the superposition principle, the potential V at P due to the total charge configuration is the algebraic sum of the potentials due to the individual charges','[{"s": "where r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the distances of P from charges q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively; and so on for the potential due to other charges. By the superposition principle, the potential V at P due to the total charge configuration is the algebraic sum of the potentials due to the individual charges", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24084,61,96,'paragraph',NULL,'V = V 1 + V 2 + ... + V n (2.17)','[{"s": "V = V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ... + V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24085,61,97,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1/4πε 0 (q1/r1P+q2/r2P+......+qn/rnP) (2.18)','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(q1/r1P+q2/r2P+......+qn/rnP)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24086,61,98,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image130.png'),
(24087,61,99,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.6 Potential at a point due to a system of charges is the sum of potentials due to individual charges.','[{"s": "Figure 2.6 Potential at a point due to a system of charges is the sum of potentials due to individual charges.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24088,61,100,'paragraph',NULL,'If we have a continuous charge distribution characterised by a charge density ρ(r), we divide it, as before, into small volume elements each of size ∆v and carrying a charge ρ∆v. We then determine the potential due to each volume element and sum (strictly speaking , integrate) over all such contributions, and thus determine the potential due to the entire distribution.','[{"s": "If we have a continuous charge distribution characterised by a charge density ρ(r), we divide it, as before, into small volume elements each of size ∆v and carrying a charge ρ∆v. We then determine the potential due to each volume element and sum (strictly speaking , integrate) over all such contributions, and thus determine the potential due to the entire distribution.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24090,61,102,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image86.png Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image87.png [2.19(a)]','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image86.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image87.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[2.19(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24092,61,104,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image88.png [2.19(b)]','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image88.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[2.19(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24093,61,105,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.2 Two charges 3 × 10 –8 C and –2 × 10 –8 C are located 15 cm apart. At what point on the line joining the two charges is the electric potential zero? Take the potential at infinity to be zero.','[{"s": "Example 2.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two charges 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C and –2 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C are located 15 cm apart. At what point on the line joining the two charges is the electric potential zero? Take the potential at infinity to be zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24094,61,106,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24095,61,107,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us take the origin O at the location of the positive charge. The line joining the two charges is taken to be the x-axis; the negative charge is taken to be on the right side of the origin (Fig. 2.7).','[{"s": "Let us take the origin O at the location of the positive charge. The line joining the two charges is taken to be the x-axis; the negative charge is taken to be on the right side of the origin (Fig. 2.7).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
