(24098,61,110,'paragraph',NULL,'1/4πε 0 [3×10 −8 /x×10 −2 −2×10 −8 /(15−x)×10 −2 ]= 0','[{"s": "1/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[3×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/x×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−2×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/(15−x)×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24099,61,111,'paragraph',NULL,'where x is in cm. That is,','[{"s": "where x is in cm. That is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24100,61,112,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image91.png'),
(24101,61,113,'paragraph',NULL,'which gives x = 9 cm.','[{"s": "which gives x = 9 cm.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24102,61,114,'paragraph',NULL,'If x lies on the extended line OA, the required condition is','[{"s": "If x lies on the extended line OA, the required condition is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24103,61,115,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image92.png'),
(24104,61,116,'paragraph',NULL,'which gives','[{"s": "which gives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24105,61,117,'paragraph',NULL,'x = 45 cm','[{"s": "x = 45 cm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24106,61,118,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, electric potential is zero at 9 cm and 45 cm away from the positive charge on the side of the negative charge. Note that the formula for potential used in the calculation required choosing potential to be zero at infinity.','[{"s": "Thus, electric potential is zero at 9 cm and 45 cm away from the positive charge on the side of the negative charge. Note that the formula for potential used in the calculation required choosing potential to be zero at infinity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24107,61,119,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.3 Figures 2.8 (a) and (b) show the field lines of a positive and negative point charge respectively.','[{"s": "Example 2.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Figures 2.8 (a) and (b) show the field lines of a positive and negative point charge respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24108,61,120,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image93.png'),
(24109,61,121,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.8','[{"s": "Figure 2.8", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24110,61,122,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Give the signs of the potential difference V P – V Q ; V B – V A .','[{"s": "(a) Give the signs of the potential difference V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24111,61,123,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Give the sign of the potential energy difference of a small negative charge between the points Q and P; A and B.','[{"s": "(b) Give the sign of the potential energy difference of a small negative charge between the points Q and P; A and B.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24112,61,124,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Give the sign of the work done by the field in moving a small positive charge from Q to P.','[{"s": "(c) Give the sign of the work done by the field in moving a small positive charge from Q to P.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24113,61,125,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Give the sign of the work done by the external agency in moving a small negative charge from B to A.','[{"s": "(d) Give the sign of the work done by the external agency in moving a small negative charge from B to A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24114,61,126,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) Does the kinetic energy of a small negative charge increase or decrease in going from B to A?','[{"s": "(e) Does the kinetic energy of a small negative charge increase or decrease in going from B to A?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24115,61,127,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24116,61,128,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) As Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image94.png , V P > V Q . Thus, (V P – V Q ) is positive. Also V B is less negative than V A . Thus, V B > V A or (V B – V A ) is positive.','[{"s": "(a) As", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image94.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, (V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is positive. Also V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is less negative than V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or (V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is positive.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24117,61,129,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) A small negative charge will be attracted towards positive charge. The negative charge moves from higher potential energy to lower potential energy. Therefore the sign of potential energy difference of a small negative charge between Q and P is positive.','[{"s": "(b) A small negative charge will be attracted towards positive charge. The negative charge moves from higher potential energy to lower potential energy. Therefore the sign of potential energy difference of a small negative charge between Q and P is positive.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24118,61,130,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, (P.E.) A > (P.E.) B and hence sign of potential energy differences is positive.','[{"s": "Similarly, (P.E.)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> (P.E.)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and hence sign of potential energy differences is positive.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24119,61,131,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) In moving a small positive charge from Q to P, work has to be done by an external agency against the electric field. Therefore, work done by the field is negative.','[{"s": "(c) In moving a small positive charge from Q to P, work has to be done by an external agency against the electric field. Therefore, work done by the field is negative.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24120,61,132,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) In moving a small negative charge from B to A work has to be done by the external agency. It is positive.','[{"s": "(d) In moving a small negative charge from B to A work has to be done by the external agency. It is positive.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24121,61,133,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) Due to force of repulsion on the negative charge, velocity decreases and hence the kinetic energy decreases in going from B to A.','[{"s": "(e) Due to force of repulsion on the negative charge, velocity decreases and hence the kinetic energy decreases in going from B to A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24122,61,134,'heading',4,'2.6.1 RELATION BETWEEN FIELD AND POTENTIAL',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24124,61,136,'paragraph',NULL,'This work equals the potential difference V A –V B .','[{"s": "This work equals the potential difference V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24125,61,137,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, |E|δ l = V – (V + δV)= – δV','[{"s": "Thus, |E|δ l = V – (V + δV)= – δV", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24126,61,138,'paragraph',NULL,'i.e., |E| =δV/δl (2.20)','[{"s": "i.e.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|E|", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=δV/δl (2.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24127,61,139,'paragraph',NULL,'Since δV is negative, δV = – |δV|. we can rewrite Eq (2.20) as','[{"s": "Since δV is negative, δV = – |δV|. we can rewrite Eq (2.20) as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24149,61,161,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider first the simple case of two charges q 1 and q 2 with position vector r 1 and r 2 relative to some origin. Let us calculate the work done (externally) in building up this configuration. This means that we consider the charges q 1 and q 2 initially at infinity and determine the work done by an external agency to bring the charges to the given locations. Suppose, first the charge q 1 is brought from infinity to the point r 1 . There is no external field against which work needs to be done, so work done in bringing q 1 from infinity to r 1 is zero. This charge produces a potential in space given by','[{"s": "Consider first the simple case of two charges q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with position vector r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "relative to some origin. Let us calculate the work done (externally) in building up this configuration. This means that we consider the charges q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "initially at infinity and determine the work done by an external agency to bring the charges to the given locations. Suppose, first the charge q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is brought from infinity to the point r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". There is no external field against which work needs to be done, so work done in bringing q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from infinity to r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is zero. This charge produces a potential in space given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24150,61,162,'paragraph',NULL,'V 1 = 1/4πε 0 q 1 /r 1 P','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24151,61,163,'paragraph',NULL,'where r 1P is the distance of a point P in space from the location of q 1 . From the definition of potential, work done in bringing charge q 2 from infinity to the point r 2 is q 2 times the potential at r 2 due to q 1 :','[{"s": "where r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the distance of a point P in space from the location of q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". From the definition of potential, work done in bringing charge q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from infinity to the point r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "times the potential at r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "due to q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24152,61,164,'paragraph',NULL,'work done on q2 =1/4πε 0 q 1 q 2 /r 12','[{"s": "work done on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q2 =1/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24153,61,165,'paragraph',NULL,'where r 12 is the distance between points 1 and 2.','[{"s": "where r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the distance between points 1 and 2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24155,61,167,'paragraph',NULL,'Obviously, if q 2 was brought first to its present location and q 1 brought later, the potential energy U would be the same. More generally, the potential energy expression, Eq. (2.22), is unaltered whatever way the charges are brought to the specified locations, because of path-independence of work for electrostatic force.','[{"s": "Obviously, if q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "was brought first to its present location and q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "brought later, the potential energy U would be the same. More generally, the potential energy expression, Eq. (2.22), is unaltered whatever way the charges are brought to the specified locations, because of path-independence of work for electrostatic force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24156,61,168,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (2.22) is true for any sign of q 1 and q 2 . If q 1 q 2 > 0, potential energy is positive. This is as expected, since for like charges (q 1 q 2 > 0), electrostatic force is repulsive and a positive amount of work is needed to be done against this force to bring the charges from infinity to a finite distance apart. For unlike charges (q 1 q 2 < 0), the electrostatic force is attractive. In that case, a positive amount of work is needed against this force to take the charges from the given location to infinity. In other words, a negative amount of work is needed for the reverse path (from infinity to the present locations), so the potential energy is negative.','[{"s": "Equation (2.22) is true for any sign of q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> 0, potential energy is positive. This is as expected, since for like charges (q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> 0), electrostatic force is repulsive and a positive amount of work is needed to be done against this force to bring the charges from infinity to a finite distance apart. For unlike charges (q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< 0), the electrostatic force is attractive. In that case, a positive amount of work is needed against this force to take the charges from the given location to infinity. In other words, a negative amount of work is needed for the reverse path (from infinity to the present locations), so the potential energy is negative.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24157,61,169,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image25.png'),
(24158,61,170,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.13 Potential energy of a system of charges q 1 and q 2 is directly proportional to the product of charges and inversely to the distance between them.','[{"s": "Figure 2.13 Potential energy of a system of charges q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is directly proportional to the product of charges and inversely to the distance between them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24159,61,171,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (2.22) is easily generalised for a system of any number of point charges. Let us calculate the potential energy of a system of three charges q 1 , q 2 and q3 located at r 1 , r 2 , r 3 , respectively. To bring q 1 first from infinity to r 1 , no work is required. Next we bring q 2 from infinity to r 2 . As before, work done in this step is','[{"s": "Equation (2.22) is easily generalised for a system of any number of point charges. Let us calculate the potential energy of a system of three charges", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and q3 located at r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively. To bring", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "first from infinity to r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", no work is required. Next we bring q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from infinity to r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". As before, work done in this step is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24160,61,172,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image26.png (2.23)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image26.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.23)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24161,61,173,'paragraph',NULL,'The charges q 1 and q 2 produce a potential, which at any point P is given by','[{"s": "The charges", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "produce a potential, which at any point P is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24162,61,174,'paragraph',NULL,'V 1,2 = 1/4πε 0 (q 1 /r 1 P+q 2 /r 2 P) (2.24)','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1,2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P+q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.24)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24163,61,175,'paragraph',NULL,'Work done next in bringing q 3 from infinity to the point r 3 is q 3 times V 1,2 at r 3','[{"s": "Work done next in bringing q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from infinity to the point r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "times V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1,2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24164,61,176,'paragraph',NULL,'q 3 V 1,2 (r 3 )= 1/4πε 0 (q 1 q 3 /r 13 +q 2 q 3 /r 23 ) (2.25)','[{"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1,2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "23", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.25)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24166,61,178,'paragraph',NULL,'U= 14πε 0 (q 1 q 2 /r 12 +q 1 q 2 /r 13 +q 2 q 3 /r 23 ) (2.26)','[{"s": "U=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "23", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.26)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24167,61,179,'paragraph',NULL,'Again, because of the conservative nature of the electrostatic force (or equivalently, the path independence of work done), the final expression for U, Eq. (2.26), is independent of the manner in which the configuration is assembled. The potential energy is characteristic of the present state of configuration, and not the way the state is achieved.','[{"s": "Again, because of the conservative nature of the electrostatic force (or equivalently, the path independence of work done), the final expression for U, Eq. (2.26), is independent of the manner in which the configuration is assembled. The potential energy is characteristic of the present state of configuration, and not the way the state is achieved.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24168,61,180,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image27.png'),
(24169,61,181,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.14 Potential energy of a system of three charges is given by Eq. (2.26), with the notation given','[{"s": "Figure 2.14 Potential energy of a system of three charges is given by Eq. (2.26), with the notation given", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24170,61,182,'paragraph',NULL,'in the figure.','[{"s": "in the figure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24165,61,177,'paragraph',NULL,'The total work done in assembling the charges at the given locations is obtained by adding the work done in different steps [Eq. (2.23) and Eq. (2.25)],','[{"s": "The total work done in assembling the charges at the given locations is obtained by adding the work done in different steps [Eq.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(2.23) and Eq. (2.25)],", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000040982"]',NULL),
(24171,61,183,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.4 Four charges are arranged at the corners of a square ABCD of side d, as shown in Fig. 2.15.(a) Find the work required to put together this arrangement. (b) A charge q 0 is brought to the centre E of the square, the four charges being held fixed at its corners. How much extra work is needed to do this?','[{"s": "Example 2.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Four charges are arranged at the corners of a square ABCD of side d, as shown in Fig. 2.15.(a) Find the work required to put together this arrangement. (b) A charge q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is brought to the centre E of the square, the four charges being held fixed at its corners. How much extra work is needed to do this?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24172,61,184,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image28.png'),
(24173,61,185,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.15','[{"s": "Figure 2.15", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24174,61,186,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24175,61,187,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Since the work done depends on the final arrangement of the charges, and not on how they are put together, we calculate work needed for one way of putting the charges at A, B, C and D. Suppose, first the charge +q is brought to A, and then the charges –q, +q, and –q are brought to B, C and D, respectively. The total work needed can be calculated in steps:','[{"s": "(a) Since the work done depends on the final arrangement of the charges, and not on how they are put together, we calculate work needed for one way of putting the charges at A, B, C and D. Suppose, first the charge +q is brought to A, and then the charges –q, +q, and –q are brought to B, C and D, respectively. The total work needed can be calculated in steps:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24176,61,188,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Work needed to bring charge +q to A when no charge is present elsewhere: this is zero.','[{"s": "(i) Work needed to bring charge +q to A when no charge is present elsewhere: this is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24177,61,189,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Work needed to bring –q to B when +q is at A. This is given by (charge at B) × (electrostatic potential at B due to charge +q at A)','[{"s": "(ii) Work needed to bring –q to B when +q is at A. This is given by (charge at B) × (electrostatic potential at B due to charge +q at A)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24178,61,190,'paragraph',NULL,'=+q×( q/4πε 0 d )=− q 2 /4πε 0 d','[{"s": "=+q×(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")=−", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24179,61,191,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Work needed to bring charge +q to C when +q is at A and –q is at B. This is given by (charge at C) × (potential at C due to charges at A and B)','[{"s": "(iii) Work needed to bring charge +q to C when +q is at A and –q is at B. This is given by (charge at C) × (potential at C due to charges at A and B)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24180,61,192,'paragraph',NULL,'=+q( +q/4πε 0 d√2+−q/4πε 0 d )','[{"s": "=+q(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d√2+−q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24181,61,193,'paragraph',NULL,'= q 2 /4πε 0 d(1−1/√2)','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d(1−1/√2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24182,61,194,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Work needed to bring –q to D when +q at A,–q at B, and +q at C. This is given by (charge at D) × (potential at D due to charges at A, B and C)','[{"s": "(iv) Work needed to bring –q to D when +q at A,–q at B, and +q at C. This is given by (charge at D) × (potential at D due to charges at A, B and C)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24183,61,195,'paragraph',NULL,'=−q( +q/4πε 0 d+−q/4πε 0 d√2+q/4πε 0 d )','[{"s": "=−q(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d+−q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d√2+q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24184,61,196,'paragraph',NULL,'= −q 2 /4πε 0 d(2−1/√2)','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d(2−1/√2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24185,61,197,'paragraph',NULL,'= −q 2 /4πε 0 d{(0)+(q)+(1−1/√2)+(2−1/√2)}','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d{(0)+(q)+(1−1/√2)+(2−1/√2)}", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24186,61,198,'paragraph',NULL,'= −q 2 /4πε 0 d(4−√2)','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d(4−√2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24187,61,199,'paragraph',NULL,'The work done depends only on the arrangement of the charges, and not how they are assembled. By definition, this is the total electrostatic energy of the charges.','[{"s": "The work done depends only on the arrangement of the charges, and not how they are assembled. By definition, this is the total electrostatic energy of the charges.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24188,61,200,'paragraph',NULL,'(Students may try calculating same work/energy by taking charges in any other order they desire and convince themselves that the energy will remain the same.)','[{"s": "(Students may try calculating same work/energy by taking charges in any other order they desire and convince themselves that the energy will remain the same.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24189,61,201,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The extra work necessary to bring a charge q0 to the point E when the four charges are at A, B, C and D is q 0 × (electrostatic potential at E due to the charges at A, B, C and D). The electrostatic potential at E is clearly zero since potential due to A and C is cancelled by that due to B and D. Hence, no work is required to bring any charge to point E.','[{"s": "(b) The extra work necessary to bring a charge q0 to the point E when the four charges are at A, B, C and D is q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× (electrostatic potential at E due to the charges at A, B, C and D). The electrostatic potential at E is clearly zero since potential due to A and C is cancelled by that due to B and D. Hence, no work is required to bring any charge to point E.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24190,61,202,'heading',2,'2.8 Potential Energy in an External Field',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24191,61,203,'heading',4,'2.8.1 POTENTIAL ENERGY OF A SINGLE CHARGE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24192,61,204,'paragraph',NULL,'In Section 2.7, the source of the electric field was specified – the charges and their locations - and the potential energy of the system of those charges was determined. In this section, we ask a related but a distinct question. What is the potential energy of a charge q in a given field? This question was, in fact, the starting point that led us to the notion of the electrostatic potential (Sections 2.1 and 2.2). But here we address this question again to clarify in what way it is different from the discussion in Section 2.7.','[{"s": "In Section 2.7, the source of the electric field was specified – the charges and their locations - and the potential energy of the system of those charges was determined. In this section, we ask a related but a distinct question. What is the potential energy of a charge q in a given field? This question was, in fact, the starting point that led us to the notion of the electrostatic potential (Sections 2.1 and 2.2). But here we address this question again to clarify in what way it is different from the discussion in Section 2.7.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24193,61,205,'paragraph',NULL,'The main difference is that we are now concerned with the potential energy of a charge (or charges) in an external field. The external field E is not produced by the given charge(s) whose potential energy we wish to calculate. E is produced by sources external to the given charge(s).The external sources may be known, but often they are unknown or unspecified; what is specified is the electric field E or the electrostatic potential V due to the external sources. We assume that the charge q does not significantly affect the sources producing the external field. This is true if q is very small, or the external sources are held fixed by other unspecified forces. Even if q is finite, its influence on the external sources may still be ignored in the situation when very strong sources far away at infinity produce a finite field E in the region of interest. Note again that we are interested in determining the potential energy of a given charge q (and later, a system of charges) in the external field; we are not interested in the potential energy of the sources producing the external electric field.','[{"s": "The main difference is that we are now concerned with the potential energy of a charge (or charges) in an external field. The external field E is not produced by the given charge(s) whose potential energy we wish to calculate. E is produced by sources external to the given charge(s).The external sources may be known, but often they are unknown or unspecified; what is specified is the electric field E or the electrostatic potential V due to the external sources. We assume that the charge q does not significantly affect the sources producing the external field. This is true if q is very small, or the external sources are held fixed by other unspecified forces. Even if q is finite, its influence on the external sources may still be ignored in the situation when very strong sources far away at infinity produce a finite field E in the region of interest. Note again that we are interested in determining the potential energy of a given charge q (and later, a system of charges) in the external field; we are not interested in the potential energy of the sources producing the external electric field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24194,61,206,'paragraph',NULL,'Potential energy of q at r in an external field = qV(r) (2.27)','[{"s": "Potential energy of q at r in an external field = qV(r) (2.27)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24195,61,207,'paragraph',NULL,'where V(r) is the external potential at the point r.','[{"s": "where V(r) is the external potential at the point r.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24196,61,208,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, if an electron with charge q = e = 1.6×10 –19 C is accelerated by a potential difference of ∆V = 1 volt, it would gain energy of q∆V = 1.6 × 10 –19 J. This unit of energy is defined as 1 electron volt or 1eV, i.e., 1 eV=1.6 × 10 –19 J. The units based on eV are most commonly used in atomic, nuclear and particle physics, (1 keV = 10 3 eV = 1.6 × 10 –16 J, 1 MeV = 10 6 eV = 1.6 × 10 –13 J, 1 GeV = 10 9 eV = 1.6 × 10 –10 J and 1 TeV = 10 12 eV = 1.6 × 10 –7 J). [This has already been defined on Page 117, XI Physics Part I, Table 6.1.]','[{"s": "Thus, if an electron with charge q = e = 1.6×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C is accelerated by a potential difference of ∆V = 1 volt, it would gain energy of q∆V = 1.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J. This unit of energy is defined as 1 electron volt or 1eV, i.e., 1 eV=1.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J. The units based on eV are most commonly used in atomic, nuclear and particle physics, (1 keV = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eV = 1.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J, 1 MeV = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eV = 1.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J, 1 GeV = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eV = 1.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J and 1 TeV = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eV = 1.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J). [This has already been defined on Page 117, XI Physics Part I, Table 6.1.]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24197,61,209,'heading',4,'2.8.2 POTENTIAL ENERGY OF A SYSTEM OF TWO CHARGES IN AN EXTERNAL FIELD',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24198,61,210,'paragraph',NULL,'Next, we ask: what is the potential energy of a system of two charges q 1 and q 2 located at r 1 and r 2 , respectively, in an external field? First, we calculate the work done in bringing the charge q 1 from infinity to r 1 . Work done in this step is q 1 V(r 1 ), using Eq. (2.27). Next, we consider the work done in bringing q 2 to r 2 . In this step, work is done not only against the external field E but also against the field due to q 1 .','[{"s": "Next, we ask: what is the potential energy of a system of two charges q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "located at r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively, in an external field? First, we calculate the work done in bringing the charge q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from infinity to r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Work done in this step is q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V(r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), using Eq. (2.27). Next, we consider the work done in bringing q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In this step, work is done not only against the external field E but also against the field due to q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24199,61,211,'paragraph',NULL,'Work done on q 2 against the external field','[{"s": "Work done on q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "against the external field", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24200,61,212,'paragraph',NULL,'= q 2 V (r 2 )','[{"s": "= q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V (r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24201,61,213,'paragraph',NULL,'Work done on q 2 against the field due to q 1','[{"s": "Work done on q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "against the field due to q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
