(24222,61,234,'heading',4,'2.8.3 POTENTIAL ENERGY OF A DIPOLE IN AN EXTERNAL FIELD',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24223,61,235,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a dipole with charges q 1 = +q and q 2 = –q placed in a uniform electric field E, as shown in Fig. 2.16.','[{"s": "Consider a dipole with charges q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= +q and q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –q placed in a uniform electric field E, as shown in Fig. 2.16.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24224,61,236,'paragraph',NULL,'As seen in the last chapter, in a uniform electric field, the dipole experiences no net force; but experiences a torque τ given by','[{"s": "As seen in the last chapter, in a uniform electric field, the dipole experiences no net force; but experiences a torque τ given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24226,61,238,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image6.png'),
(24227,61,239,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.16 Potential energy of a dipole in a uniform external field.','[{"s": "Figure 2.16 Potential energy of a dipole in a uniform external field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24228,61,240,'paragraph',NULL,'W=∫ θ1 θ0 τext(θ)d θ =∫ θ1 θ0 pEsinθdθ','[{"s": "W=∫", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "τext(θ)d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=∫", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pEsinθdθ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24229,61,241,'paragraph',NULL,'=pE(cosθ 0 −cosθ 1 ) (2.31)','[{"s": "=pE(cosθ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−cosθ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.31)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24230,61,242,'paragraph',NULL,'0 = π / 2. (Αn explanation for it is provided towards the end of discussion.) We can then write,','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= π / 2. (Αn explanation for it is provided towards the end of discussion.) We can then write,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24231,61,243,'paragraph',NULL,'U(θ)=pE(cos π / 2 −cosθ)=−pEcosθ=−p.E (2.32)','[{"s": "U(θ)=pE(cos", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−cosθ)=−pEcosθ=−p.E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.32)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24232,61,244,'paragraph',NULL,'This expression can alternately be understood also from Eq. (2.29). We apply Eq. (2.29) to the present system of two charges +q and –q. The potential energy expression then reads','[{"s": "This expression can alternately be understood also from Eq. (2.29). We apply Eq. (2.29) to the present system of two charges +q and –q. The potential energy expression then reads", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24233,61,245,'paragraph',NULL,'U\'(θ)=−pEcosθ−q 2 /4πε 0 ×2a=−p.E−q 2 /4πε 0 ×2a (2.33)','[{"s": "U\'(θ)=−pEcosθ−q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×2a=−p.E−q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×2a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.33)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24234,61,246,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, r 1 and r 2 denote the position vectors of +q and –q. Now, the potential difference between positions r 1 and r 2 2 to r 1 . The displacement parallel to the force is 2a cosθ. Thus, [V(r 1 )–V (r 2','[{"s": "Here, r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "denote the position vectors of +q and –q. Now, the potential difference between positions r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The displacement parallel to the force is 2a cosθ. Thus, [V(r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")–V (r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24235,61,247,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image7.png (2.34)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image7.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.34)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24236,61,248,'paragraph',NULL,'We note that U′ (θ) differs from U(θ) by a quantity which is just a constant for a given dipole. Since a constant is insignificant for potential energy, we can drop the second term in Eq. (2.34) and it then reduces to Eq. (2.32).','[{"s": "We note that U′ (θ) differs from U(θ) by a quantity which is just a constant for a given dipole. Since a constant is insignificant for potential energy, we can drop the second term in Eq. (2.34) and it then reduces to Eq. (2.32).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24237,61,249,'paragraph',NULL,'We can now understand why we took θ 0 =π/2. In this case, the work done against the external field E in bringing +q and – q are equal and opposite and cancel out, i.e., q [V (r 1 ) – V (r 2 )]=0.','[{"s": "We can now understand why we took θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=π/2. In this case, the work done against the external field E in bringing +q and – q are equal and opposite and cancel out, i.e., q [V (r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") – V (r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")]=0.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24238,61,250,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.6 A molecule of a substance has a permanent electric dipole moment of magnitude 10 –29 C m. A mole of this substance is polarised (at low temperature) by applying a strong electrostatic field of magnitude 10 6 V m –1 . The direction of the field is suddenly changed by an angle of 60º. Estimate the heat released by the substance in aligning its dipoles along the new direction of the field. For simplicity, assume 100% polarisation of the sample.','[{"s": "Example 2.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A molecule of a substance has a permanent electric dipole moment of magnitude 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–29", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m. A mole of this substance is polarised (at low temperature) by applying a strong electrostatic field of magnitude 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The direction of the field is suddenly changed by an angle of 60º. Estimate the heat released by the substance in aligning its dipoles along the new direction of the field. For simplicity, assume 100% polarisation of the sample.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24239,61,251,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution Here, dipole moment of each molecules = 10 –29 C m','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Here, dipole moment of each molecules = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–29", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24240,61,252,'paragraph',NULL,'As 1 mole of the substance contains 6 × 10 23 molecules, total dipole moment of all the molecules, p = 6 × 10 23 × 10 –29 C m = 6 × 10 –6 C m','[{"s": "As 1 mole of the substance contains 6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "23", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules, total dipole moment of all the molecules, p = 6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "23", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–29", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m = 6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24241,61,253,'paragraph',NULL,'Initial potential energy, U i = –pE cos θ = –6×10 -6 ×10 6 cos 0° = –6 J','[{"s": "Initial potential energy, U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –pE cos θ = –6×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos 0° = –6 J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24242,61,254,'paragraph',NULL,'Final potential energy (when θ = 60°), U f = –6×10 -6 ×10 6 cos 60° = –3 J','[{"s": "Final potential energy (when θ = 60°), U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –6×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos 60° = –3 J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24243,61,255,'paragraph',NULL,'Change in potential energy = –3 J – (–6J) = 3 J','[{"s": "Change in potential energy = –3 J – (–6J) = 3 J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24244,61,256,'paragraph',NULL,'So, there is loss in potential energy. This must be the energy released by the substance in the form of heat in aligning its dipoles.','[{"s": "So, there is loss in potential energy. This must be the energy released by the substance in the form of heat in aligning its dipoles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24245,61,257,'heading',3,'2.9 ELECTROSTATICS OF CONDUCTORS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24246,61,258,'paragraph',NULL,'Conductors and insulators were described briefly in Chapter 1. Conductors contain mobile charge carriers. In metallic conductors, these charge carriers are electrons. In a metal, the outer (valence) electrons part away from their atoms and are free to move. These electrons are free within the metal but not free to leave the metal. The free electrons form a kind of ‘gas’; they collide with each other and with the ions, and move randomly in different directions. In an external electric field, they drift against the direction of the field. The positive ions made up of the nuclei and the bound electrons remain held in their fixed positions. In electrolytic conductors, the charge carriers are both positive and negative ions; but the situation in this case is more involved – the movement of the charge carriers is affected both by the external electric field as also by the so-called chemical forces (see Chapter 3). We shall restrict our discussion to metallic solid conductors. Let us note important results regarding electrostatics of conductors.','[{"s": "Conductors and insulators were described briefly in Chapter 1. Conductors contain mobile charge carriers. In metallic conductors, these charge carriers are electrons. In a metal, the outer (valence) electrons part away from their atoms and are free to move. These electrons are free within the metal but not free to leave the metal. The free electrons form a kind of ‘gas’; they collide with each other and with the ions, and move randomly in different directions. In an external electric field, they drift against the direction of the field. The positive ions made up of the nuclei and the bound electrons remain held in their fixed positions. In electrolytic conductors, the charge carriers are both positive and negative ions; but the situation in this case is more involved – the movement of the charge carriers is affected both by the external electric field as also by the so-called chemical forces (see Chapter 3). We shall restrict our discussion to metallic solid conductors. Let us note important results regarding electrostatics of conductors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24247,61,259,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Inside a conductor, electrostatic field is zero','[{"s": "1. Inside a conductor, electrostatic field is zero", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24248,61,260,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a conductor, neutral or charged. There may also be an external electrostatic field. In the static situation, when there is no current inside or on the surface of the conductor, the electric field is zero everywhere inside the conductor. This fact can be taken as the defining property of a conductor. A conductor has free electrons. As long as electric field is not zero, the free charge carriers would experience force and drift. In the static situation, the free charges have so distributed themselves that the electric field is zero everywhere inside. Electrostatic field is zero inside a conductor.','[{"s": "Consider a conductor, neutral or charged. There may also be an external electrostatic field. In the static situation, when there is no current inside or on the surface of the conductor, the electric field is zero everywhere inside the conductor. This fact can be taken as the defining property of a conductor. A conductor has free electrons. As long as electric field is not zero, the free charge carriers would experience force and drift. In the static situation, the free charges have so distributed themselves that the electric field is zero everywhere inside. Electrostatic field is zero inside a conductor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24249,61,261,'paragraph',NULL,'2. At the surface of a charged conductor, electrostatic field must be normal to the surface at every point','[{"s": "2. At the surface of a charged conductor, electrostatic field must be normal to the surface at every point", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24250,61,262,'paragraph',NULL,'If E were not normal to the surface, it would have some non-zero component along the surface. Free charges on the surface of the conductor would then experience force and move. In the static situation, therefore, E should have no tangential component. Thus electrostatic field at the surface of a charged conductor must be normal to the surface at every point. (For a conductor without any surface charge density, field is zero even at the surface.) See result 5.','[{"s": "If E were not normal to the surface, it would have some non-zero component along the surface. Free charges on the surface of the conductor would then experience force and move. In the static situation, therefore, E should have no tangential component. Thus electrostatic field at the surface of a charged conductor must be normal to the surface at every point. (For a conductor without any surface charge density, field is zero even at the surface.) See result 5.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24251,61,263,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The interior of a conductor can have no excess charge in the static situation','[{"s": "3. The interior of a conductor can have no excess charge in the static situation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24252,61,264,'paragraph',NULL,'A neutral conductor has equal amounts of positive and negative charges in every small volume or surface element. When the conductor is charged, the excess charge can reside only on the surface in the static situation. This follows from the Gauss’s law. Consider any arbitrary volume element v inside a conductor. On the closed surface S bounding the volume element v, electrostatic field is zero. Thus the total electric flux through S is zero. Hence, by Gauss’s law, there is no net charge enclosed by S. But the surface S can be made as small as you like, i.e., the volume v can be made vanishingly small. This means there is no net charge at any point inside the conductor, and any excess charge must reside at the surface.','[{"s": "A neutral conductor has equal amounts of positive and negative charges in every small volume or surface element. When the conductor is charged, the excess charge can reside only on the surface in the static situation. This follows from the Gauss’s law. Consider any arbitrary volume element v inside a conductor. On the closed surface S bounding the volume element v, electrostatic field is zero. Thus the total electric flux through S is zero. Hence, by Gauss’s law, there is no net charge enclosed by S. But the surface S can be made as small as you like, i.e., the volume v can be made vanishingly small. This means there is no net charge at any point inside the conductor, and any excess charge must reside at the surface.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24253,61,265,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Electrostatic potential is constant throughout the volume of the conductor and has the same value (as inside) on its surface','[{"s": "4. Electrostatic potential is constant throughout the volume of the conductor and has the same value (as inside) on its surface", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24254,61,266,'paragraph',NULL,'This follows from results 1 and 2 above. Since E = 0 inside the conductor and has no tangential component on the surface, no work is done in moving a small test charge within the conductor and on its surface. That is, there is no potential difference between any two points inside or on the surface of the conductor. Hence, the result. If the conductor is charged, electric field normal to the surface exists; this means potential will be different for the surface and a point just outside the surface.','[{"s": "This follows from results 1 and 2 above. Since E = 0 inside the conductor and has no tangential component on the surface, no work is done in moving a small test charge within the conductor and on its surface. That is, there is no potential difference between any two points inside or on the surface of the conductor. Hence, the result. If the conductor is charged, electric field normal to the surface exists; this means potential will be different for the surface and a point just outside the surface.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24255,61,267,'paragraph',NULL,'In a system of conductors of arbitrary size, shape and charge configuration, each conductor is characterised by a constant value of potential, but this constant may differ from one conductor to the other.','[{"s": "In a system of conductors of arbitrary size, shape and charge configuration, each conductor is characterised by a constant value of potential, but this constant may differ from one conductor to the other.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24256,61,268,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Electric field at the surface of a charged conductor','[{"s": "5. Electric field at the surface of a charged conductor", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24257,61,269,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image8.png (2.35)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image8.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.35)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24258,61,270,'paragraph',NULL,'where σ is the surface charge density and Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image9.png is a unit vector normal to the surface in the outward direction.','[{"s": "where σ is the surface charge density and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image9.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is a unit vector normal to the surface in the outward direction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24259,61,271,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image10.png'),
(24260,61,272,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.17 The Gaussian surface (a pill box) chosen to derive Eq. (2.35) for electric field at the surface of a charged conductor.','[{"s": "Figure 2.17 The Gaussian surface (a pill box) chosen to derive Eq. (2.35) for electric field at the surface of a charged conductor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24261,61,273,'paragraph',NULL,'To derive the result, choose a pill box (a short cylinder) as the Gaussian surface about any point P on the surface, as shown in Fig. 2.17. The pill box is partly inside and partly outside the surface of the conductor. It has a small area of cross section δS and negligible height.','[{"s": "To derive the result, choose a pill box (a short cylinder) as the Gaussian surface about any point P on the surface, as shown in Fig. 2.17. The pill box is partly inside and partly outside the surface of the conductor. It has a small area of cross section δS and negligible height.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24262,61,274,'paragraph',NULL,'Just inside the surface, the electrostatic field is zero; just outside, the field is normal to the surface with magnitude E. Thus, the contribution to the total flux through the pill box comes only from the outside (circular) cross-section of the pill box. This equals ± EδS (positive for σ > 0, negative for σ < 0), since over the small area δS, E may be considered constant and E and δS are parallel or antiparallel. The charge enclosed by the pill box is σδS.','[{"s": "Just inside the surface, the electrostatic field is zero; just outside, the field is normal to the surface with magnitude E. Thus, the contribution to the total flux through the pill box comes only from the outside (circular) cross-section of the pill box. This equals ± EδS (positive for σ > 0, negative for σ < 0), since over the small area δS, E may be considered constant and E and δS are parallel or antiparallel. The charge enclosed by the pill box is σδS.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24263,61,275,'paragraph',NULL,'By Gauss’s law','[{"s": "By Gauss’s law", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24264,61,276,'paragraph',NULL,'EδS=|σ|δS/ε 0','[{"s": "EδS=|σ|δS/ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24265,61,277,'paragraph',NULL,'E=|α|ε 0 (2.36)','[{"s": "E=|α|ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.36)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24266,61,278,'paragraph',NULL,'Including the fact that electric field is normal to the surface, we get the vector relation, Eq. (2.35), which is true for both signs of σ. For σ > 0, electric field is normal to the surface outward; for σ < 0, electric field is normal to the surface inward.','[{"s": "Including the fact that electric field is normal to the surface, we get the vector relation, Eq. (2.35), which is true for both signs of σ. For σ > 0, electric field is normal to the surface outward; for σ < 0, electric field is normal to the surface inward.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24267,61,279,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Electrostatic shielding','[{"s": "6. Electrostatic shielding", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26452,66,22,'paragraph',NULL,'The normal force exerted by the fluid at a point may be measured. An idealised form of one such pressure-measuring device is shown in Fig. 10.1(b). It consists of an evacuated chamber with a spring that is calibrated to measure the force acting on the piston. This device is placed at a point inside the fluid. The inward force exerted by the fluid on the piston is balanced by the outward spring force and is thereby measured.','[{"s": "The normal force exerted by the fluid at a point may be measured. An idealised form of one such pressure-measuring device is shown in Fig. 10.1(b). It consists of an evacuated chamber with a spring that is calibrated to measure the force acting on the piston. This device is placed at a point inside the fluid. The inward force exerted by the fluid on the piston is balanced by the outward spring force and is thereby measured.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26453,66,23,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image56.png'),
(26454,66,24,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) (b)','[{"s": "(a) (b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26455,66,25,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 10.1 (a) The force exerted by the liquid in the beaker on the submerged object or on the','[{"s": "Fig. 10.1 (a) The force exerted by the liquid in the beaker on the submerged object or on the", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24268,61,280,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a conductor with a cavity, with no charges inside the cavity. A remarkable result is that the electric field inside the cavity is zero, whatever be the size and shape of the cavity and whatever be the charge on the conductor and the external fields in which it might be placed. We have proved a simple case of this result already: the electric field inside a charged spherical shell is zero. The proof of the result for the shell makes use of the spherical symmetry of the shell (see Chapter 1). But the vanishing of electric field in the (charge-free) cavity of a conductor is, as mentioned above, a very general result. A related result is that even if the conductor is charged or charges are induced on a neutral conductor by an external field, all charges reside only on the outer surface of a conductor with cavity.','[{"s": "Consider a conductor with a cavity, with no charges inside the cavity. A remarkable result is that the electric field inside the cavity is zero, whatever be the size and shape of the cavity and whatever be the charge on the conductor and the external fields in which it might be placed. We have proved a simple case of this result already: the electric field inside a charged spherical shell is zero. The proof of the result for the shell makes use of the spherical symmetry of the shell (see Chapter 1). But the vanishing of electric field in the (charge-free) cavity of a conductor is, as mentioned above, a very general result. A related result is that even if the conductor is charged or charges are induced on a neutral conductor by an external field, all charges reside only on the outer surface of a conductor with cavity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24270,61,282,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image42.png'),
(24271,61,283,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.18 The electric field inside a cavity of any conductor is zero. All charges reside only on the outer surface of a conductor with cavity. (There are no charges placed in the cavity.)','[{"s": "Figure 2.18 The electric field inside a cavity of any conductor is zero. All charges reside only on the outer surface of a conductor with cavity. (There are no charges placed in the cavity.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24272,61,284,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image43.png'),
(24273,61,285,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.19 Some important electrostatic properties of a conductor.','[{"s": "Figure 2.19 Some important electrostatic properties of a conductor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24274,61,286,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.7','[{"s": "Example 2.7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24275,61,287,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24276,61,288,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) This is because the comb gets charged by friction. The molecules in the paper gets polarised by the charged comb, resulting in a net force of attraction. If the hair is wet, or if it is rainy day, friction between hair and the comb reduces. The comb does not get charged and thus it will not attract small bits of paper.','[{"s": "(a) This is because the comb gets charged by friction. The molecules in the paper gets polarised by the charged comb, resulting in a net force of attraction. If the hair is wet, or if it is rainy day, friction between hair and the comb reduces. The comb does not get charged and thus it will not attract small bits of paper.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24277,61,289,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) To enable them to conduct charge (produced by friction) to the ground; as too much of static electricity accumulated may result in spark and result in fire.','[{"s": "(b) To enable them to conduct charge (produced by friction) to the ground; as too much of static electricity accumulated may result in spark and result in fire.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24278,61,290,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Reason similar to (b).','[{"s": "(c) Reason similar to (b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24279,61,291,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Current passes only when there is difference in potential.','[{"s": "(d) Current passes only when there is difference in potential.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24280,61,292,'heading',3,'2.10 DIELECTRICS AND POLARISATION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24281,61,293,'paragraph',NULL,'Dielectrics are non-conducting substances. In contrast to conductors, they have no (or negligible number of) charge carriers. Recall from Section 2.9 what happens when a conductor is placed in an external electric field. The free charge carriers move and charge distribution in the conductor adjusts itself in such a way that the electric field due to induced charges opposes the external field within the conductor. This happens until, in the static situation, the two fields cancel each other and the net electrostatic field in the conductor is zero. In a dielectric, this free movement of charges is not possible. It turns out that the external field induces dipole moment by stretching or re-orienting molecules of the dielectric. The collective effect of all the molecular dipole moments is net charges on the surface of the dielectric which produce a field that opposes the external field. Unlike in a conductor, however, the opposing field so induced does not exactly cancel the external field. It only reduces it. The extent of the effect depends on the nature of the dielectric. To understand the effect, we need to look at the charge distribution of a dielectric at the molecular level.','[{"s": "Dielectrics are non-conducting substances. In contrast to conductors, they have no (or negligible number of) charge carriers. Recall from Section 2.9 what happens when a conductor is placed in an external electric field. The free charge carriers move and charge distribution in the conductor adjusts itself in such a way that the electric field due to induced charges opposes the external field within the conductor. This happens until, in the static situation, the two fields cancel each other and the net electrostatic field in the conductor is zero. In a dielectric, this free movement of charges is not possible. It turns out that the external field induces dipole moment by stretching or re-orienting molecules of the dielectric. The collective effect of all the molecular dipole moments is net charges on the surface of the dielectric which produce a field that opposes the external field. Unlike in a conductor, however, the opposing field so induced does not exactly cancel the external field. It only reduces it. The extent of the effect depends on the nature of the dielectric. To understand the effect, we need to look at the charge distribution of a dielectric at the molecular level.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24282,61,294,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image44.png'),
(24283,61,295,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.20 Difference in behaviour of a conductor and a dielectric in an external electric field.','[{"s": "Figure 2.20 Difference in behaviour of a conductor and a dielectric in an external electric field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24284,61,296,'paragraph',NULL,'The molecules of a substance may be polar or non-polar. In a non-polar molecule, the centres of positive and negative charges coincide. The molecule then has no permanent (or intrinsic) dipole moment. Examples of non-polar molecules are oxygen (O 2 ) and hydrogen (H 2 ) molecules which, because of their symmetry, have no dipole moment. On the other hand, a polar molecule is one in which the centres of positive and negative charges are separated (even when there is no external field). Such molecules have a permanent dipole moment. An ionic molecule such as HCl or a molecule of water (H 2 O) are examples of polar molecules.','[{"s": "The molecules of a substance may be polar or non-polar. In a non-polar molecule, the centres of positive and negative charges coincide. The molecule then has no permanent (or intrinsic) dipole moment. Examples of non-polar molecules are oxygen (O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and hydrogen (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") molecules which, because of their symmetry, have no dipole moment. On the other hand, a polar molecule is one in which the centres of positive and negative charges are separated (even when there is no external field). Such molecules have a permanent dipole moment. An ionic molecule such as HCl or a molecule of water (H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O) are examples of polar molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24285,61,297,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image45.png'),
(24286,61,298,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.21 Some examples of polar and non-polar molecules.','[{"s": "Figure 2.21 Some examples of polar and non-polar molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24287,61,299,'paragraph',NULL,'In an external electric field, the positive and negative charges of a non-polar molecule are displaced in opposite directions. The displacement stops when the external force on the constituent charges of the molecule is balanced by the restoring force (due to internal fields in the molecule). The non-polar molecule thus develops an induced dipole moment. The dielectric is said to be polarised by the external field. We consider only the simple situation when the induced dipole moment is in the direction of the field and is proportional to the field strength. (Substances for which this assumption is true are called linear isotropic dielectrics.) The induced dipole moments of different molecules add up giving a net dipole moment of the dielectric in the presence of the external field.','[{"s": "In an external electric field, the positive and negative charges of a non-polar molecule are displaced in opposite directions. The displacement stops when the external force on the constituent charges of the molecule is balanced by the restoring force (due to internal fields in the molecule). The non-polar molecule thus develops an induced dipole moment. The dielectric is said to be polarised by the external field. We consider only the simple situation when the induced dipole moment is in the direction of the field and is proportional to the field strength. (Substances for which this assumption is true are called linear isotropic dielectrics.) The induced dipole moments of different molecules add up giving a net dipole moment of the dielectric in the presence of the external field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24288,61,300,'paragraph',NULL,'A dielectric with polar molecules also develops a net dipole moment in an external field, but for a different reason. In the absence of any external field, the different permanent dipoles are oriented randomly due to thermal agitation; so the total dipole moment is zero. When an external field is applied, the individual dipole moments tend to align with the field. When summed overall the molecules, there is then a net dipole moment in the direction of the external field, i.e., the dielectric is polarised. The extent of polarisation depends on the relative strength of two mutually opposite factors: the dipole potential energy in the external field tending to align the dipoles with the field and thermal energy tending to disrupt the alignment. There may be, in addition, the ‘induced dipole moment’ effect as for non-polar molecules, but generally the alignment effect is more important for polar molecules.','[{"s": "A dielectric with polar molecules also develops a net dipole moment in an external field, but for a different reason. In the absence of any external field, the different permanent dipoles are oriented randomly due to thermal agitation; so the total dipole moment is zero. When an external field is applied, the individual dipole moments tend to align with the field. When summed overall the molecules, there is then a net dipole moment in the direction of the external field, i.e., the dielectric is polarised. The extent of polarisation depends on the relative strength of two mutually opposite factors: the dipole potential energy in the external field tending to align the dipoles with the field and thermal energy tending to disrupt the alignment. There may be, in addition, the ‘induced dipole moment’ effect as for non-polar molecules, but generally the alignment effect is more important for polar molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24289,61,301,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image46.png'),
(24290,61,302,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.22 A dielectric develops a net dipole moment in an external electric field. (a) Non-polar molecules, (b) Polar molecules.','[{"s": "Figure 2.22 A dielectric develops a net dipole moment in an external electric field. (a) Non-polar molecules, (b) Polar molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24292,61,304,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image47.png (2.37)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image47.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.37)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24293,61,305,'paragraph',NULL,'where χ e is a constant characteristic of the dielectric and is known as the electric susceptibility of the dielectric medium.','[{"s": "where χ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a constant characteristic of the dielectric and is known as the electric susceptibility of the dielectric medium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24294,61,306,'paragraph',NULL,'It is possible to relate χ e to the molecular properties of the substance, but we shall not pursue that here.','[{"s": "It is possible to relate χ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to the molecular properties of the substance, but we shall not pursue that here.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26456,66,26,'paragraph',NULL,'walls is normal (perpendicular) to the surface at all points.','[{"s": "walls is normal (perpendicular) to the surface at all points.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24295,61,307,'paragraph',NULL,'The question is: how does the polarised dielectric modify the original external field inside it? Let us consider, for simplicity, a rectangular dielectric slab placed in a uniform external field E 0 parallel to two of its faces. The field causes a uniform polarisation P of the dielectric. Thus every volume element ∆v of the slab has a dipole moment P ∆v in the direction of the field. The volume element ∆v is macroscopically small but contains a very large number of molecular dipoles. Anywhere inside the dielectric, the volume element ∆v has no net charge (though it has net dipole moment). This is, because, the positive charge of one dipole sits close to the negative charge of the adjacent dipole. However, at the surfaces of the dielectric normal to the electric field, there is evidently a net charge density. As seen in Fig 2.23, the positive ends of the dipoles remain unneutralised at the right surface and the negative ends at the left surface. The unbalanced charges are the induced charges due to the external field.','[{"s": "The question is: how does the polarised dielectric modify the original external field inside it? Let us consider, for simplicity, a rectangular dielectric slab placed in a uniform external field E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallel to two of its faces. The field causes a uniform polarisation P of the dielectric. Thus every volume element ∆v of the slab has a dipole moment P ∆v in the direction of the field. The volume element ∆v is macroscopically small but contains a very large number of molecular dipoles. Anywhere inside the dielectric, the volume element ∆v has no net charge (though it has net dipole moment). This is, because, the positive charge of one dipole sits close to the negative charge of the adjacent dipole. However, at the surfaces of the dielectric normal to the electric field, there is evidently a net charge density. As seen in Fig 2.23, the positive ends of the dipoles remain unneutralised at the right surface and the negative ends at the left surface. The unbalanced charges are the induced charges due to the external field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24296,61,308,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the polarised dielectric is equivalent to two charged surfaces with induced surface charge densities, say σp and –σp. Clearly, the field produced by these surface charges opposes the external field. The total field in the dielectric is, thereby, reduced from the case when no dielectric is present. We should note that the surface charge density ±σp arises from bound (not free charges) in the dielectric.','[{"s": "Thus, the polarised dielectric is equivalent to two charged surfaces with induced surface charge densities, say σp and –σp. Clearly, the field produced by these surface charges opposes the external field. The total field in the dielectric is, thereby, reduced from the case when no dielectric is present. We should note that the surface charge density ±σp arises from bound (not free charges) in the dielectric.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24297,61,309,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image48.png'),
(24298,61,310,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.23 A uniformly polarised dielectric amounts to induced surface charge density, but no volume charge density.','[{"s": "Figure 2.23 A uniformly polarised dielectric amounts to induced surface charge density, but no volume charge density.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24299,61,311,'heading',3,'2.11 CAPACITORS AND CAPACITANCE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24300,61,312,'paragraph',NULL,'A capacitor is a system of two conductors separated by an insulator (Fig. 2.24). The conductors have charges, say Q 1 and Q 2 , and potentials V 1 and V 2 . Usually, in practice, the two conductors have charges Q and – Q, with potential difference V = V 1 – V 2 between them. We shall consider only this kind of charge configuration of the capacitor. (Even a single conductor can be used as a capacitor by assuming the other at infinity.) The conductors may be so charged by connecting them to the two terminals of a battery. Q is called the charge of the capacitor, though this, in fact, is the charge on one of the conductors – the total charge of the capacitor is zero.','[{"s": "A capacitor is a system of two conductors separated by an insulator (Fig. 2.24). The conductors have charges, say Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and potentials V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Usually, in practice, the two conductors have charges Q and – Q, with potential difference V = V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "between them. We shall consider only this kind of charge configuration of the capacitor. (Even a single conductor can be used as a capacitor by assuming the other at infinity.) The conductors may be so charged by connecting them to the two terminals of a battery. Q is called the charge of the capacitor, though this, in fact, is the charge on one of the conductors – the total charge of the capacitor is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24301,61,313,'paragraph',NULL,'The electric field in the region between the conductors is proportional to the charge Q. That is, if the charge on the capacitor is, say doubled, the electric field will also be doubled at every point. (This follows from the direct proportionality between field and charge implied by Coulomb’s law and the superposition principle.) Now, potential difference V is the work done per unit positive charge in taking a small test charge from the conductor 2 to 1 against the field. Consequently, V is also proportional to Q, and the ratio Q/V is a constant:','[{"s": "The electric field in the region between the conductors is proportional to the charge Q. That is, if the charge on the capacitor is, say doubled, the electric field will also be doubled at every point. (This follows from the direct proportionality between field and charge implied by Coulomb’s law and the superposition principle.) Now, potential difference V is the work done per unit positive charge in taking a small test charge from the conductor 2 to 1 against the field. Consequently, V is also proportional to Q, and the ratio Q/V is a constant:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24302,61,314,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image36.png (2.38)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image36.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.38)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24303,61,315,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image49.png'),
(24304,61,316,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.24 A system of two conductors separated by an insulator forms a capacitor.','[{"s": "Figure 2.24 A system of two conductors separated by an insulator forms a capacitor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26457,66,27,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) An idealised device for measuring pressure.','[{"s": "(b) An idealised device for measuring pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26458,66,28,'paragraph',NULL,'If F is the magnitude of this normal force on the piston of area A then the average pressure Pav is defined as the normal force acting per unit area.','[{"s": "If F is the magnitude of this normal force on the piston of area A then the average pressure Pav is defined as the normal force acting per unit area.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26459,66,29,'paragraph',NULL,'Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image58.png (10.1)','[{"s": "Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image58.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(10.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24305,61,317,'paragraph',NULL,'The constant C is called the capacitance of the capacitor. C is independent of Q or V, as stated above. The capacitance C depends only on the geometrical configuration (shape, size, separation) of the system of two conductors. [As we shall see later, it also depends on the nature of the insulator (dielectric) separating the two conductors.] The SI unit of capacitance is 1 farad (=1 coulomb volt-1) or 1 F = 1 C V –1 . A capacitor with fixed capacitance is symbolically shown as ---||---, while the one with variable capacitance is shown as Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image50.png .','[{"s": "The constant C is called the capacitance of the capacitor. C is independent of Q or V, as stated above. The capacitance C depends only on the geometrical configuration (shape, size, separation) of the system of two conductors. [As we shall see later, it also depends on the nature of the insulator (dielectric) separating the two conductors.] The SI unit of capacitance is 1 farad (=1 coulomb volt-1) or 1 F = 1 C V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". A capacitor with fixed capacitance is symbolically shown as ---||---, while the one with variable capacitance is shown as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image50.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24306,61,318,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (2.38) shows that for large C, V is small for a given Q. This means a capacitor with large capacitance can hold large amount of charge Q at a relatively small V. This is of practical importance. High potential difference implies strong electric field around the conductors. A strong electric field can ionise the surrounding air and accelerate the charges so produced to the oppositely charged plates, thereby neutralising the charge on the capacitor plates, at least partly. In other words, the charge of the capacitor leaks away due to the reduction in insulating power of the intervening medium.','[{"s": "Equation (2.38) shows that for large C, V is small for a given Q. This means a capacitor with large capacitance can hold large amount of charge Q at a relatively small V. This is of practical importance. High potential difference implies strong electric field around the conductors. A strong electric field can ionise the surrounding air and accelerate the charges so produced to the oppositely charged plates, thereby neutralising the charge on the capacitor plates, at least partly. In other words, the charge of the capacitor leaks away due to the reduction in insulating power of the intervening medium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24307,61,319,'paragraph',NULL,'The maximum electric field that a dielectric medium can withstand without break-down (of its insulating property) is called its dielectric strength; for air it is about 3 × 10 6 Vm –1 . For a separation between conductors of the order of 1 cm or so, this field corresponds to a potential difference of 3 × 10 4 V between the conductors. Thus, for a capacitor to store a large amount of charge without leaking, its capacitance should be high enough so that the potential difference and hence the electric field do not exceed the break-down limits. Put differently, there is a limit to the amount of charge that can be stored on a given capacitor without significant leaking. In practice, a farad is a very big unit; the most common units are its sub-multiples 1 µF = 10 –6 F, 1 nF = 10 –9 F, 1 pF = 10 –12 F, etc. Besides its use in storing charge, a capacitor is a key element of most ac circuits with important functions, as described in Chapter 7.','[{"s": "The maximum electric field that a dielectric medium can withstand without break-down (of its insulating property) is called its dielectric strength; for air it is about 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Vm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For a separation between conductors of the order of 1 cm or so, this field corresponds to a potential difference of 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V between the conductors. Thus, for a capacitor to store a large amount of charge without leaking, its capacitance should be high enough so that the potential difference and hence the electric field do not exceed the break-down limits. Put differently, there is a limit to the amount of charge that can be stored on a given capacitor without significant leaking. In practice, a farad is a very big unit; the most common units are its sub-multiples 1 µF = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F, 1 nF = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F, 1 pF = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F, etc. Besides its use in storing charge, a capacitor is a key element of most ac circuits with important functions, as described in Chapter 7.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
