(24351,61,363,'heading',3,'ELECTRIC DISPLACEMENT',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24352,61,364,'paragraph',NULL,'We have introduced the notion of dielectric constant and arrived at Eq. (2.54), without giving the explicit relation between the induced charge density σp and the polarisation P.','[{"s": "We have introduced the notion of dielectric constant and arrived at Eq. (2.54), without giving the explicit relation between the induced charge density σp and the polarisation P.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24353,61,365,'paragraph',NULL,'We take without proof the result that','[{"s": "We take without proof the result that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24354,61,366,'paragraph',NULL,'σP=P.n^','[{"s": "σP=P.n^", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24355,61,367,'paragraph',NULL,'where n^ is a unit vector along the outward normal to the surface. Above equation is general, true for any shape of the dielectric. For the slab in Fig. 2.23, P is along n^','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n^", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a unit vector along the outward normal to the surface. Above equation is general, true for any shape of the dielectric. For the slab in Fig. 2.23, P is along", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n^", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24356,61,368,'paragraph',NULL,'at the right surface and opposite to n^ at the left surface. Thus at the right surface, induced charge density is positive and at the left surface, it is negative, as guessed already in our qualitative discussion before. Putting the equation for electric field in vector form','[{"s": "at the right surface and opposite to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n^", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at the left surface. Thus at the right surface, induced charge density is positive and at the left surface, it is negative, as guessed already in our qualitative discussion before. Putting the equation for electric field in vector form", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24357,61,369,'paragraph',NULL,'E⋅n^=σ−P.n^ε 0 or (ε 0 E+P).n^=σ','[{"s": "E⋅n^=σ−P.n^ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or (ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E+P).n^=σ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24358,61,370,'paragraph',NULL,'The quantity ε 0 E + P is called the electric displacement and is denoted by D. It is a vector quantity. Thus,','[{"s": "The quantity ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E + P is called the electric displacement and is denoted by D. It is a vector quantity. Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24359,61,371,'paragraph',NULL,'D = ε 0 E + P, D n^ = σ,','[{"s": "D = ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E + P, D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n^", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= σ,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24360,61,372,'paragraph',NULL,'The significance of D is this : in vacuum, E is related to the free charge density σ. When a dielectric medium is present, the corresponding role is taken up by D. For a dielectric medium, it is D not E that is directly related to free charge density σ, as seen in above equation. Since P is in the same direction as E, all the three vectors P, E and D are parallel.','[{"s": "The significance of D is this : in vacuum, E is related to the free charge density σ. When a dielectric medium is present, the corresponding role is taken up by D. For a dielectric medium, it is D not E that is directly related to free charge density σ, as seen in above equation. Since P is in the same direction as E, all the three vectors P, E and D are parallel.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24361,61,373,'paragraph',NULL,'The ratio of the magnitudes of D and E is','[{"s": "The ratio of the magnitudes of D and E is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24362,61,374,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image76.png'),
(24363,61,375,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus,','[{"s": "Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24364,61,376,'paragraph',NULL,'D = ε 0 KE','[{"s": "D = ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "KE", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24365,61,377,'paragraph',NULL,'and P = D –ε 0 E = ε 0 (K –1)E','[{"s": "and P = D –ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E = ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(K –1)E", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24366,61,378,'paragraph',NULL,'This gives for the electric susceptibility χ e defined in Eq. (2.37)','[{"s": "This gives for the electric susceptibility χ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "defined in Eq. (2.37)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24367,61,379,'paragraph',NULL,'χ e =ε 0 (K–1)','[{"s": "χ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=ε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(K–1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24368,61,380,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.8 A slab of material of dielectric constant K has the same area as the plates of a parallel-plate capacitor but has a thickness (3/4)d, where d is the separation of the plates. How is the capacitance changed when the slab is inserted between the plates?','[{"s": "Example 2.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A slab of material of dielectric constant K has the same area as the plates of a parallel-plate capacitor but has a thickness (3/4)d, where d is the separation of the plates. How is the capacitance changed when the slab is inserted between the plates?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24369,61,381,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution Let E 0 = V 0 /d be the electric field between the plates when there is no dielectric and the potential difference is V0. If the dielectric is now inserted, the electric field in the dielectric will be E = E 0 /K. The potential difference will then be','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Let E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/d be the electric field between the plates when there is no dielectric and the potential difference is V0. If the dielectric is now inserted, the electric field in the dielectric will be E = E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/K. The potential difference will then be", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24370,61,382,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image66.png'),
(24371,61,383,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image67.png'),
(24372,61,384,'paragraph',NULL,'The potential difference decreases by the factor (K + 3)/4K while the free charge Q 0 on the plates remains unchanged. The capacitance thus increases','[{"s": "The potential difference decreases by the factor (K + 3)/4K while the free charge Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on the plates remains unchanged. The capacitance thus increases", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24373,61,385,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image68.png'),
(24374,61,386,'heading',3,'2.14 COMBINATION OF CAPACITORS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24375,61,387,'paragraph',NULL,'We can combine several capacitors of capacitance C 1 , C 2 ,…, C n to obtain a system with some effective capacitance C. The effective capacitance depends on the way the individual capacitors are combined. Two simple possibilities are discussed below.','[{"s": "We can combine several capacitors of capacitance C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",…, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to obtain a system with some effective capacitance C. The effective capacitance depends on the way the individual capacitors are combined. Two simple possibilities are discussed below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24376,61,388,'heading',4,'2.14.1 CAPACITORS IN SERIES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24377,61,389,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.26 shows capacitors C 1 and C 2 combined in series.','[{"s": "Figure 2.26 shows capacitors C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "combined in series.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26463,66,33,'paragraph',NULL,'Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image59.png (10.3)','[{"s": "Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image59.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(10.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26464,66,34,'paragraph',NULL,'The dimensions of density are [ML–3]. Its SI unit is kg m–3. It is a positive scalar quantity. A liquid is largely incompressible and its density is therefore, nearly constant at all pressures. Gases, on the other hand exhibit a large variation in densities with pressure.','[{"s": "The dimensions of density are [ML–3]. Its SI unit is kg m–3. It is a positive scalar quantity. A liquid is largely incompressible and its density is therefore, nearly constant at all pressures. Gases, on the other hand exhibit a large variation in densities with pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24379,61,391,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image56.png'),
(24380,61,392,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.26 Combination of two capacitors in series.','[{"s": "Figure 2.26 Combination of two capacitors in series.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24381,61,393,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image58.png'),
(24382,61,394,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.27 Combination of n capacitors in series.','[{"s": "Figure 2.27 Combination of n capacitors in series.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24383,61,395,'paragraph',NULL,'V = V 1 + V 2 = Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image59.png (2.55)','[{"s": "V = V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image59.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.55)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24384,61,396,'paragraph',NULL,'i.e., Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image60.png , (2.56)','[{"s": "i.e.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image60.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", (2.56)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24385,61,397,'paragraph',NULL,'Now we can regard the combination as an effective capacitor with charge Q and potential difference V. The effective capacitance of the combination is','[{"s": "Now we can regard the combination as an effective capacitor with charge Q and potential difference V. The effective capacitance of the combination is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24386,61,398,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image36.png (2.57)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image36.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.57)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24387,61,399,'paragraph',NULL,'We compare Eq. (2.57) with Eq. (2.56), and obtain','[{"s": "We compare Eq. (2.57) with Eq. (2.56), and obtain", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24388,61,400,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image62.png (2.58)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image62.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.58)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24389,61,401,'paragraph',NULL,'The proof clearly goes through for any number of capacitors arranged in a similar way. Equation (2.55), for n capacitors arranged in series, generalises to','[{"s": "The proof clearly goes through for any number of capacitors arranged in a similar way. Equation (2.55), for n capacitors arranged in series, generalises to", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24390,61,402,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image64.png (2.59)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image64.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.59)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24392,61,404,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image51.png (2.60)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image51.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.60)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24393,61,405,'heading',4,'2.14.2 CAPACITORS IN PARALLEL',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24394,61,406,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.28 (a) shows two capacitors arranged in parallel. In this case, the same potential difference is applied across both the capacitors. But the plate charges (±Q 1 ) on capacitor 1 and the plate charges (±Q 2 ) on the capacitor 2 are not necessarily the same:','[{"s": "Figure 2.28 (a) shows two capacitors arranged in parallel. In this case, the same potential difference is applied across both the capacitors. But the plate charges (±Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") on capacitor 1 and the plate charges (±Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") on the capacitor 2 are not necessarily the same:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24395,61,407,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image52.png'),
(24396,61,408,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.28 Parallel combination of (a) two capacitors, (b) n capacitors.','[{"s": "Figure 2.28 Parallel combination of (a) two capacitors, (b) n capacitors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24397,61,409,'paragraph',NULL,'Q 1 = C 1 V, Q 2 = C 2 V (2.61)','[{"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V, Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V (2.61)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24398,61,410,'paragraph',NULL,'The equivalent capacitor is one with charge','[{"s": "The equivalent capacitor is one with charge", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24399,61,411,'paragraph',NULL,'Q = Q 1 + Q 2 (2.62)','[{"s": "Q = Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.62)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
