(24400,61,412,'paragraph',NULL,'and potential difference V.','[{"s": "and potential difference V.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26465,66,35,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 10.1 Densities of some common fluids','[{"s": "Table 10.1 Densities of some common fluids", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24401,61,413,'paragraph',NULL,'Q = CV = C 1 V + C 2 V (2.63)','[{"s": "Q = CV = C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V + C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V (2.63)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24402,61,414,'paragraph',NULL,'The effective capacitance C is, from Eq. (2.63),','[{"s": "The effective capacitance C is, from Eq. (2.63),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24403,61,415,'paragraph',NULL,'C = C 1 + C 2 (2.64)','[{"s": "C = C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.64)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24404,61,416,'paragraph',NULL,'The general formula for effective capacitance C for parallel combination of n capacitors [Fig. 2.28 (b)] follows similarly,','[{"s": "The general formula for effective capacitance C for parallel combination of n capacitors [Fig. 2.28 (b)] follows similarly,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24405,61,417,'paragraph',NULL,'Q = Q1 + Q2 + ... + Qn (2.65)','[{"s": "Q = Q1 + Q2 + ... + Qn (2.65)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24406,61,418,'paragraph',NULL,'i.e., CV = C 1 V + C 2 V + ... C n V (2.66)','[{"s": "i.e., CV = C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V + C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V + ... C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V (2.66)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24407,61,419,'paragraph',NULL,'which gives','[{"s": "which gives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24408,61,420,'paragraph',NULL,'C = C 1 + C 2 + ... C n (2.67)','[{"s": "C = C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ... C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.67)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24409,61,421,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.9 A network of four 10 µF capacitors is connected to a 500 V supply, as shown in Fig. 2.29. Determine (a) the equivalent capacitance of the network and (b) the charge on each capacitor. (Note, the charge on a capacitor is the charge on the plate with higher potential, equal and opposite to the charge on the plate with lower potential.)','[{"s": "Example 2.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A network of four 10 µF capacitors is connected to a 500 V supply, as shown in Fig. 2.29. Determine (a) the equivalent capacitance of the network and (b) the charge on each capacitor. (Note, the charge on a capacitor is the charge on the plate with higher potential, equal and opposite to the charge on the plate with lower potential.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24410,61,422,'image',NULL,'1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image53.jpg'),
(24411,61,423,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24412,61,424,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) In the given network, C 1 , C 2 and C 3 are connected in series. The effective capacitance C′ of these three capacitors is given by','[{"s": "(a) In the given network, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are connected in series. The effective capacitance C′ of these three capacitors is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24413,61,425,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image54.png'),
(24414,61,426,'paragraph',NULL,'For C 1 = C 2 = C 3 = 10 µF, C′ = (10/3) µF. The network has C′ and C 4 connected in parallel. Thus, the equivalent capacitance C of the network is','[{"s": "For C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 µF, C′ = (10/3) µF. The network has C′ and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "connected in parallel. Thus, the equivalent capacitance C of the network is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24415,61,427,'paragraph',NULL,'C = C\' + C 4 =( 10/3 + 10)μF','[{"s": "C = C\' + C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10/3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 10)μF", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24416,61,428,'paragraph',NULL,'=13.3µF','[{"s": "=13.3µF", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24417,61,429,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Clearly, from the figure, the charge on each of the capacitors, C 1 , C 2 and C 3 is the same, say Q. Let the charge on C 4 be Q′. Now, since the potential difference across AB is Q/C 1 , across BC is Q/C 2 , across CD is Q/C 3 , we have','[{"s": "(b) Clearly, from the figure, the charge on each of the capacitors, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the same, say Q. Let the charge on C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be Q′. Now, since the potential difference across AB is Q/C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", across BC is Q/C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", across CD is Q/C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24418,61,430,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image107.png .','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image107.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24419,61,431,'paragraph',NULL,'Also, Q′/C 4 = 500 V.','[{"s": "Also, Q′/C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 500 V.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24420,61,432,'paragraph',NULL,'This gives for the given value of the capacitances,','[{"s": "This gives for the given value of the capacitances,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24421,61,433,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image110.png and','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image110.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24422,61,434,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image113.png'),
(24423,61,435,'heading',3,'2.15 ENERGY STORED IN A CAPACITOR',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24424,61,436,'paragraph',NULL,'A capacitor, as we have seen above, is a system of two conductors with charge Q and –Q. To determine the energy stored in this configuration, consider initially two uncharged conductors 1 and 2. Imagine next a process of transferring charge from conductor 2 to conductor 1 bit by bit, so that at the end, conductor 1 gets charge Q. By charge conservation, conductor 2 has charge –Q at the end (Fig 2.30 ).','[{"s": "A capacitor, as we have seen above, is a system of two conductors with charge Q and –Q. To determine the energy stored in this configuration, consider initially two uncharged conductors 1 and 2. Imagine next a process of transferring charge from conductor 2 to conductor 1 bit by bit, so that at the end, conductor 1 gets charge Q. By charge conservation, conductor 2 has charge –Q at the end (Fig 2.30 ).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24425,61,437,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image115.png'),
(24426,61,438,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 2.30 (a) Work done in a small step of building charge on conductor 1 from Q′ to Q′ + δ Q′. (b) Total work done in charging the capacitor may be viewed as stored in the energy of electric field between the plates.','[{"s": "Figure 2.30 (a) Work done in a small step of building charge on conductor 1 from Q′ to Q′ + δ Q′. (b) Total work done in charging the capacitor may be viewed as stored in the energy of electric field between the plates.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24427,61,439,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image117.png (2.68)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image117.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.68)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24428,61,440,'paragraph',NULL,'Since δ Q′ can be made as small as we like, Eq. (2.68) can be written as','[{"s": "Since δ Q′ can be made as small as we like, Eq. (2.68) can be written as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24429,61,441,'paragraph',NULL,'Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image120.png (2.69)','[{"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image120.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.69)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24430,61,442,'paragraph',NULL,'Equations (2.68) and (2.69) are identical because the term of second order in δQ′, i.e., δQ′ 2 /2C, is negligible, since δQ′ is arbitrarily small. The total work done (W) is the sum of the small work (δW) over the very large number of steps involved in building the charge Q′ from zero to Q.','[{"s": "Equations (2.68) and (2.69) are identical because the term of second order in δQ′, i.e., δQ′", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2C, is negligible, since δQ′ is arbitrarily small. The total work done (W) is the sum of the small work (δW) over the very large number of steps involved in building the charge Q′ from zero to Q.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24431,61,443,'paragraph',NULL,'W=∑ sum over all steps δW','[{"s": "W=∑", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sum over all steps", "t": "text"}, {"s": "δW", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24432,61,444,'paragraph',NULL,'=∑ sum over all steps 1/2C [(Q\'+δQ\') 2 −Q\' 2 ] (2.70)','[{"s": "=∑", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sum over all steps", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[(Q\'+δQ\')", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−Q\'", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] (2.70)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24433,61,445,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1/2C [{δQ\' 2 −0}+{(2δQ\') 2 −δQ\' 2 }+{(3δQ\') 2 −(2δQ\') 2 }+....+{Q 2 −(Q−δQ) 2 }]...(2.7)','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[{δQ\'", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−0}+{(2δQ\')", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−δQ\'", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "}+{(3δQ\')", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−(2δQ\')", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "}+....+{Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−(Q−δQ)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "}]...(2.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24434,61,446,'paragraph',NULL,'=1/2C[Q 2 −0]=Q 2 /2C..........(2.72)','[{"s": "=1/2C[Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−0]=Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2C..........(2.72)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
