(26467,66,37,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image64.png'),
(26468,66,38,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.1 The two thigh bones (femurs), each of cross-sectional area10 cm2 support the upper part of a human body of mass 40 kg. Estimate the average pressure sustained by the femurs.','[{"s": "Example 10.1 The two thigh bones (femurs), each of cross-sectional area10 cm2 support the upper part of a human body of mass 40 kg. Estimate the average pressure sustained by the femurs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24464,61,476,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose we have a large spherical conducting shell of radius R, on which we place a charge Q. This charge spreads itself uniformly all over the sphere. As we have seen in Section 1.14, the field outside the sphere is just that of a point charge Q at the centre; while the field inside the sphere vanishes. So the potential outside is that of a point charge; and inside it is constant, namely the value at he radius R. We thus have:','[{"s": "Suppose we have a large spherical conducting shell of radius R, on which we place a charge Q. This charge spreads itself uniformly all over the sphere. As we have seen in Section 1.14, the field outside the sphere is just that of a point charge Q at the centre; while the field inside the sphere vanishes. So the potential outside is that of a point charge; and inside it is constant, namely the value at he radius R. We thus have:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24465,61,477,'paragraph',NULL,'Potential inside conducting spherical shell of radius R carrying charge Q','[{"s": "Potential inside conducting spherical shell of radius R carrying charge Q", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24466,61,478,'paragraph',NULL,'= constant','[{"s": "= constant", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24467,61,479,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1 Q/4πε o R (2.78)','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 Q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.78)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24468,61,480,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image16.png'),
(24469,61,481,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, as shown in Fig. 2.32, let us suppose that in some way we introduce a small sphere of radius r, carrying some charge q, into the large one, and place it at the centre. The potential due to this new charge clearly has the following values at the radii indicated: Potential due to small sphere of radius r carrying charge','[{"s": "Now, as shown in Fig. 2.32, let us suppose that in some way we introduce a small sphere of radius r, carrying some charge q, into the large one, and place it at the centre. The potential due to this new charge clearly has the following values at the radii indicated: Potential due to small sphere of radius r carrying charge", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24470,61,482,'paragraph',NULL,'1q4πε 0 r at surface of small sphere','[{"s": "1q4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r at surface of small sphere", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24471,61,483,'paragraph',NULL,'1q4πε 0 R at large shell of radius R. (2.79)','[{"s": "1q4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R at large shell of radius R. (2.79)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24472,61,484,'paragraph',NULL,'Taking both charges q and Q into account we have for the total potential V and the potential difference the values','[{"s": "Taking both charges q and Q into account we have for the total potential V and the potential difference the values", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24473,61,485,'paragraph',NULL,'V(R)= 1/4πε o ( Q/R + q/R )','[{"s": "V(R)=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q/R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q/R", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24474,61,486,'paragraph',NULL,'V(r)= 1/4πε o ( Q/R + q/r )','[{"s": "V(r)=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q/R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24475,61,487,'paragraph',NULL,'V(r) − V(R)= q/4πε o ( 1/r − 1/R ) (2.80)','[{"s": "V(r) − V(R)=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/R", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.80)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24476,61,488,'paragraph',NULL,'Assume now that q is positive. We see that, independent of the amount of charge Q that may have accumulated on the larger sphere and even if it is positive, the inner sphere is always at a higher potential: the difference V(r )–V (R) is positive. The potential due to Q is constant upto radius R and so cancels out in the difference!','[{"s": "Assume now that q is positive. We see that, independent of the amount of charge Q that may have accumulated on the larger sphere and even if it is positive, the inner sphere is always at a higher potential: the difference V(r )–V (R) is positive. The potential due to Q is constant upto radius R and so cancels out in the difference!", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24477,61,489,'paragraph',NULL,'This means that if we now connect the smaller and larger sphere by a wire, the charge q on the former will immediately flow onto the matter, even though the charge Q may be quite large. The natural tendency is for positive charge to move from higher to lower potential. Thus, provided we are somehow able to introduce the small charged sphere into the larger one, we can in this way keep piling up larger and larger amount of charge on the latter. The potential (Eq. 2.78) at the outer sphere would also keep rising, at least until we reach the breakdown field of air.','[{"s": "This means that if we now connect the smaller and larger sphere by a wire, the charge q on the former will immediately flow onto the matter, even though the charge Q may be quite large. The natural tendency is for positive charge to move from higher to lower potential. Thus, provided we are somehow able to introduce the small charged sphere into the larger one, we can in this way keep piling up larger and larger amount of charge on the latter. The potential (Eq. 2.78) at the outer sphere would also keep rising, at least until we reach the breakdown field of air.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24478,61,490,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image15.png'),
(24479,61,491,'paragraph',NULL,'This is the principle of the van de Graaff generator. It is a machine capable of building up potential difference of a few million volts, and fields close to the breakdown field of air which is about 3 × 10 6 V/m. A schematic diagram of the van de Graaff generator is given in Fig. 2.33. A large spherical conducting shell (of few metres radius) is supported at a height several meters above the ground on an insulating column. A long narrow endless belt insulating material, like rubber or silk, is wound around two pulleys – one at ground level, one at the centre of the shell. This belt is kept continuously moving by a motor driving the lower pulley. It continuously carries positive charge, sprayed on to it by a brush at ground level, to the top. There it transfers its positive charge to another conducting brush connected to the large shell. Thus positive charge is transferred to the shell, where it spreads out uniformly on the outer surface. In this way, voltage differences of as much as 6 or 8 million volts (with respect to ground) can be built up.','[{"s": "This is the principle of the van de Graaff generator. It is a machine capable of building up potential difference of a few million volts, and fields close to the breakdown field of air which is about 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V/m. A schematic diagram of the van de Graaff generator is given in Fig. 2.33. A large spherical conducting shell (of few metres radius) is supported at a height several meters above the ground on an insulating column. A long narrow endless belt insulating material, like rubber or silk, is wound around two pulleys – one at ground level, one at the centre of the shell. This belt is kept continuously moving by a motor driving the lower pulley. It continuously carries positive charge, sprayed on to it by a brush at ground level, to the top. There it transfers its positive charge to another conducting brush connected to the large shell. Thus positive charge is transferred to the shell, where it spreads out uniformly on the outer surface. In this way, voltage differences of as much as 6 or 8 million volts (with respect to ground) can be built up.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24480,61,492,'heading',4,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24481,61,493,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Electrostatic force is a conservative force. Work done by an external force (equal and opposite to the electrostatic force) in bringing a charge q from a point R to a point P is VP – VR, which is the difference in potential energy of charge q between the final and initial points.','[{"s": "1. Electrostatic force is a conservative force. Work done by an external force (equal and opposite to the electrostatic force) in bringing a charge q from a point R to a point P is VP – VR, which is the difference in potential energy of charge q between the final and initial points.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24482,61,494,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Potential at a point is the work done per unit charge (by an external agency) in bringing a charge from infinity to that point. Potential at a point is arbitrary to within an additive constant, since it is the potential difference between two points which is physically significant. If potential at infinity is chosen to be zero; potential at a point with position vector r due to a point charge Q placed at the origin is given is given by','[{"s": "2. Potential at a point is the work done per unit charge (by an external agency) in bringing a charge from infinity to that point. Potential at a point is arbitrary to within an additive constant, since it is the potential difference between two points which is physically significant. If potential at infinity is chosen to be zero; potential at a point with position vector r due to a point charge Q placed at the origin is given is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24483,61,495,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image12.png'),
(24484,61,496,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The electrostatic potential at a point with position vector r due to a point dipole of dipole moment pplaced at the origin is','[{"s": "3. The electrostatic potential at a point with position vector r due to a point dipole of dipole moment pplaced at the origin is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24485,61,497,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image11.png'),
(24486,61,498,'paragraph',NULL,'The result is true also for a dipole (with charges –q and q separated by 2a) for r >> a.','[{"s": "The result is true also for a dipole (with charges –q and q separated by 2a) for r >> a.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24487,61,499,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image89.png'),
(24488,61,500,'paragraph',NULL,'where r1P is the distance between q1 and P, as and so on.','[{"s": "where r1P is the distance between q1 and P, as and so on.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24489,61,501,'paragraph',NULL,'5. An equipotential surface is a surface over which potential has a constant value. For a point charge, concentric spheres centred at a location of the charge are equipotential surfaces. The electric field E at a point is perpendicular to the equipotential surface through the point. E is in the direction of the steepest decrease of potential.','[{"s": "5. An equipotential surface is a surface over which potential has a constant value. For a point charge, concentric spheres centred at a location of the charge are equipotential surfaces. The electric field E at a point is perpendicular to the equipotential surface through the point. E is in the direction of the steepest decrease of potential.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24490,61,502,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Potential energy stored in a system of charges is the work done (by an external agency) in assembling the charges at their locations. Potential energy of two charges q1, q2 at r1, r2 is given by','[{"s": "6. Potential energy stored in a system of charges is the work done (by an external agency) in assembling the charges at their locations. Potential energy of two charges q1, q2 at r1, r2 is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24491,61,503,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image83.png'),
(24492,61,504,'paragraph',NULL,'where r12 is distance between q1 and q2.','[{"s": "where r12 is distance between q1 and q2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24493,61,505,'paragraph',NULL,'7. The potential energy of a charge q in an external potential V(r) is qV(r).','[{"s": "7. The potential energy of a charge q in an external potential V(r) is qV(r).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24494,61,506,'paragraph',NULL,'The potential energy of a dipole moment p in a uniform electric field E is –p.E.','[{"s": "The potential energy of a dipole moment p in a uniform electric field E is –p.E.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24495,61,507,'paragraph',NULL,'8. Electrostatics field E is zero in the interior of a conductor; just outside the surface of a charged conductor, E is normal to the surface given by Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image82.png where Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image85.png is the unit vector along the outward normal to the surface and σ is the surface charge density. Charges in a conductor can reside only at its surface. Potential is constant within and on the surface of a conductor. In a cavity within a conductor (with no charges), the electric field is zero.','[{"s": "8. Electrostatics field E is zero in the interior of a conductor; just outside the surface of a charged conductor, E is normal to the surface given by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image82.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image85.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is the unit vector along the outward normal to the surface and σ is the surface charge density. Charges in a conductor can reside only at its surface. Potential is constant within and on the surface of a conductor. In a cavity within a conductor (with no charges), the electric field is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24496,61,508,'paragraph',NULL,'C = Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image84.png','[{"s": "C =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image84.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24497,61,509,'paragraph',NULL,'where A is the area of each plate and d the separation between them.','[{"s": "where A is the area of each plate and d the separation between them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24498,61,510,'paragraph',NULL,'10. If the medium between the plates of a capacitor is filled with an insulating substance (dielectric), the electric field due to the charged plates induces a net dipole moment in the dielectric. This effect, called polarisation, gives rise to a field in the opposite direction. The net electric field inside the dielectric and hence the potential difference between the plates is thus reduced. Consequently, the capacitance C increases from its value C0 when there is no medium (vacuum),','[{"s": "10. If the medium between the plates of a capacitor is filled with an insulating substance (dielectric), the electric field due to the charged plates induces a net dipole moment in the dielectric. This effect, called polarisation, gives rise to a field in the opposite direction. The net electric field inside the dielectric and hence the potential difference between the plates is thus reduced. Consequently, the capacitance C increases from its value C0 when there is no medium (vacuum),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24499,61,511,'paragraph',NULL,'C = KC0','[{"s": "C = KC0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24500,61,512,'paragraph',NULL,'where K is the dielectric constant of the insulating substance.','[{"s": "where K is the dielectric constant of the insulating substance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24502,61,514,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Electrostatic_Potential_and_Capacitance-image79.png'),
(24504,61,516,'paragraph',NULL,'C = C1 + C2 + C3 + ...','[{"s": "C = C1 + C2 + C3 + ...", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
