(24635,61,647,'paragraph',NULL,'A cylindrical capacitor has two co-axial cylinders Of length 15 cm and radii 1.5 cm and 1.4 cm. The outer cylinder is earthed and the inner cylinder is given a charge Of 3.5 WC. Determine the capacitance Of the system and the potential of the inner cylinder. Neglect end effects (i.e., bending of field lines at the ends).','[{"s": "A cylindrical capacitor has two co-axial cylinders Of length 15 cm and radii 1.5 cm and 1.4 cm. The outer cylinder is earthed and the inner cylinder is given a charge Of 3.5 WC. Determine the capacitance Of the system and the potential of the inner cylinder. Neglect end effects (i.e., bending of field lines at the ends).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24636,61,648,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 2.33:','[{"s": "Question 2.33:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26469,66,39,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Total cross-sectional area of the femurs is A = 2 × 10 cm2 = 20 × 10–4 m2. The force acting on them is F = 40 kg wt = 400 N (taking g = 10 m s–2). This force is acting vertically down and hence, normally on the femurs. Thus, the average pressure is','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Total cross-sectional area of the femurs is A = 2 × 10 cm2 = 20 × 10–4 m2. The force acting on them is F = 40 kg wt = 400 N (taking g = 10 m s–2). This force is acting vertically down and hence, normally on the femurs. Thus, the average pressure is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26470,66,40,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image63.png'),
(24637,61,649,'paragraph',NULL,'A parallel plate capacitor is to be designed with a voltage rating 1 kV, using a material of dielectric constant 3 and dielectric strength about 107 Vm-l. (Dielectric strength is the maximum electric field a material can tolerate without breakdown, i.e., without starting to conduct electricity through partial ionisation.) For safety, we should like the field never to exceed, say 10% of the dielectric strength. What minimum area of the plates is required to have a capacitance Of 50 pF?','[{"s": "A parallel plate capacitor is to be designed with a voltage rating 1 kV, using a material of dielectric constant 3 and dielectric strength about 107 Vm-l. (Dielectric strength is the maximum electric field a material can tolerate without breakdown, i.e., without starting to conduct electricity through partial ionisation.) For safety, we should like the field never to exceed, say 10% of the dielectric strength. What minimum area of the plates is required to have a capacitance Of 50 pF?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24638,61,650,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 2.34:','[{"s": "Question 2.34:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24639,61,651,'paragraph',NULL,'Describe schematically the equipotential surfaces corresponding to','[{"s": "Describe schematically the equipotential surfaces corresponding to", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24640,61,652,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) a constant electric field in the z-direction,','[{"s": "(a) a constant electric field in the z-direction,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24641,61,653,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) a field that uniformly increases in magnitude but remains in a constant (say, z) direction,','[{"s": "(b) a field that uniformly increases in magnitude but remains in a constant (say, z) direction,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24642,61,654,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) a single positive charge at the origin, and','[{"s": "(c) a single positive charge at the origin, and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24643,61,655,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) a uniform grid consisting of long equally spaced parallel charged wires in a plane.','[{"s": "(d) a uniform grid consisting of long equally spaced parallel charged wires in a plane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24644,61,656,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 2.35:','[{"s": "Question 2.35:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24645,61,657,'paragraph',NULL,'In a Van de Graaff type generator a spherical metal shell is to be a 15 x 106 V electrode. The dielectric strength of the gas surrounding the electrode is 5 x 107 Vm-l. What is the minimum radius of the spherical shell required? (You will learn from this exercise why one cannot build an electrostatic generator using a very small shell that requires a small charge to acquire a high potential.)','[{"s": "In a Van de Graaff type generator a spherical metal shell is to be a 15 x 106 V electrode. The dielectric strength of the gas surrounding the electrode is 5 x 107 Vm-l. What is the minimum radius of the spherical shell required? (You will learn from this exercise why one cannot build an electrostatic generator using a very small shell that requires a small charge to acquire a high potential.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24646,61,658,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 2.36:','[{"s": "Question 2.36:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24647,61,659,'paragraph',NULL,'A small sphere of radius r 1 and charge q 1 is enclosed by a spherical shell of radius r 2 and charge q 2 . Show that if qt is positive, the charge will necessarily flow from the sphere to the shell (when the two are connected by a wire) no matter what the charge q 2 on the shell is.','[{"s": "A small sphere of radius r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and charge q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is enclosed by a spherical shell of radius r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and charge q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Show that if qt is positive, the charge will necessarily flow from the sphere to the shell (when the two are connected by a wire) no matter what the charge q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on the shell is.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24648,61,660,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 2.37:','[{"s": "Question 2.37:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24649,61,661,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer the following:','[{"s": "Answer the following:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24650,61,662,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The top of the atmosphere is at about 400 kV with respect to the surface of the earth, corresponding to an electric field that decreases with altitude. Near the surface of the earth, the field is about 100 V/m. Why then do we not get an electric shock as we step out of our house into the open? (Assume the house to be a steel cage so there is no field inside!)','[{"s": "(a) The top of the atmosphere is at about 400 kV with respect to the surface of the earth, corresponding to an electric field that decreases with altitude. Near the surface of the earth, the field is about 100 V/m. Why then do we not get an electric shock as we step out of our house into the open? (Assume the house to be a steel cage so there is no field inside!)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24651,61,663,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) A man fixes outside his house one evening a two-meter high insulating slab carrying on its top a large aluminum sheet of area 1m 2 . Will he get an electric shock if he touches the metal sheet the next morning?','[{"s": "(b) A man fixes outside his house one evening a two-meter high insulating slab carrying on its top a large aluminum sheet of area 1m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Will he get an electric shock if he touches the metal sheet the next morning?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24652,61,664,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The discharging current in the atmosphere due to the small conductivity of air is known to be 1800 A on average over the globe. Why then does the atmosphere not discharge itself completely in due course and become electrically neutral? In other words, what keeps the atmosphere charged?','[{"s": "(c) The discharging current in the atmosphere due to the small conductivity of air is known to be 1800 A on average over the globe. Why then does the atmosphere not discharge itself completely in due course and become electrically neutral? In other words, what keeps the atmosphere charged?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26471,66,41,'heading',4,'10.2.1 Pascal’s Law',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26472,66,42,'paragraph',NULL,'The French scientist Blaise Pascal observed that the pressure in a fluid at rest is the same at all points if they are at the same height. This fact may be demonstrated in a simple way.','[{"s": "The French scientist Blaise Pascal observed that the pressure in a fluid at rest is the same at all points if they are at the same height. This fact may be demonstrated in a simple way.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26473,66,43,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image69.png'),
(24653,61,665,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) What are the forms of energy into which the electrical energy of the atmosphere is dissipated during lightning? (Hint: The earth has an electric field of about 100 Vm-l at its surface in the downward direction, corresponding to a surface charge density= -10 -9 C m -2 . Due to the slight conductivity of the atmosphere up to about 50 km (beyond which it is a good conductor), about + 1800 C is pumped every second into the earth as a whole. The earth, however, does not get discharged since thunderstorms and lightning occurring continually all over the globe pump an equal amount of negative charge on the earth.)','[{"s": "(d) What are the forms of energy into which the electrical energy of the atmosphere is dissipated during lightning? (Hint: The earth has an electric field of about 100 Vm-l at its surface in the downward direction, corresponding to a surface charge density= -10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Due to the slight conductivity of the atmosphere up to about 50 km (beyond which it is a good conductor), about + 1800 C is pumped every second into the earth as a whole. The earth, however, does not get discharged since thunderstorms and lightning occurring continually all over the globe pump an equal amount of negative charge on the earth.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
