(24771,62,21,'paragraph',NULL,'Q ≡ The quotient ( T 2 /a 3 ) in units of 10 -34 y 2 m -3 .)','[{"s": "Q ≡ The quotient ( T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in units of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-34", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24772,62,22,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 3.32.28 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image99.png'),
(24773,62,23,'paragraph',NULL,'The law of areas can be understood as a consequence of conservation of angular momentum whch is valid for any central force . A central force is such that the force on the planet is along the vector joining the Sun and the planet. Let the Sun be at the origin and let the position and momentum of the planet be denoted by r and p respectively. Then the area swept out by the planet of mass m in time interval ∆t is (Fig. 8.2) ∆ A given by','[{"s": "The law of areas can be understood as a consequence of conservation of angular momentum whch is valid for any central force . A central force is such that the force on the planet is along the vector joining the Sun and the planet. Let the Sun be at the origin and let the position and momentum of the planet be denoted by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively. Then the area swept out by the planet of mass m in time interval ∆t is (Fig. 8.2) ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24774,62,24,'paragraph',NULL,'∆A = ½ ( r × v ∆t) (8.1)','[{"s": "∆A = ½ (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆t) (8.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24775,62,25,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence','[{"s": "Hence", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24776,62,26,'paragraph',NULL,'∆A /∆t =½ ( r × p )/m, (since v = p /m)','[{"s": "∆A /∆t =½ (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")/m, (since", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/m)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24777,62,27,'paragraph',NULL,'= L / (2 m) (8.2)','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ (2 m) (8.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26474,66,44,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 10.2 Proof of Pascal’s law. ABC-DEF is an element of the interior of a fluid at rest. This element is in the form of a right-angled prism. The element is small so that the effect of gravity can be ignored, but it has been enlarged for the sake of clarity.','[{"s": "Fig. 10.2 Proof of Pascal’s law. ABC-DEF is an element of the interior of a fluid at rest. This element is in the form of a right-angled prism. The element is small so that the effect of gravity can be ignored, but it has been enlarged for the sake of clarity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24779,62,29,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image98.png'),
(24780,62,30,'paragraph',NULL,'Johannes Kepler (1571–1630) was a scientist of German origin. He formulated the three laws of planetary motion based on the painstaking observations of Tycho Brahe and coworkers. Kepler himself was an assistant to Brahe and it took him sixteen long years to arrive at the three planetary laws. He is also known as the founder of geometrical optics, being the first to describe what happens to light after it enters a telescope.','[{"s": "Johannes Kepler (1571–1630)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "was a scientist of German origin. He formulated the three laws of planetary motion based on the painstaking observations of Tycho Brahe and coworkers. Kepler himself was an assistant to Brahe and it took him sixteen long years to arrive at the three planetary laws. He is also known as the founder of geometrical optics, being the first to describe what happens to light after it enters a telescope.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24781,62,31,'paragraph',NULL,'* Refer to information given in the Box on Page 182','[{"s": "* Refer to information given in the Box on Page 182", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24783,62,33,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The magnitude of the angular momentum at P is L p = m p r p v p , since inspection tells us that r p and v p are mutually perpendicular. Similarly, L A = m p r A v A . From angular momentum conservation','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The magnitude of the angular momentum at P is L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", since inspection tells us that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are mutually perpendicular. Similarly, L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". From angular momentum conservation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24784,62,34,'paragraph',NULL,'m p r p v p = m p r A v A','[{"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24785,62,35,'paragraph',NULL,'or Gravitation-image104.png','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image104.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24786,62,36,'paragraph',NULL,'Since r A > r p , v p > v A .','[{"s": "Since r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24787,62,37,'paragraph',NULL,'The area SBAC bounded by the ellipse and the radius vectors SB and SC is larger than SBPC in Fig. 8.1. From Kepler’s second law, equal areas are swept in equal times. Hence the planet will take a longer time to traverse BAC than CPB.','[{"s": "The area SBAC bounded by the ellipse and the radius vectors SB and SC is larger than SBPC in Fig. 8.1. From Kepler’s second law, equal areas are swept in equal times. Hence the planet will take a longer time to traverse BAC than CPB.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24788,62,38,'heading',3,'8.3 UNIVERSAL LAW OF GRAVITATION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24789,62,39,'paragraph',NULL,'Legend has it that observing an apple falling from a tree, Newton was inspired to arrive at an universal law of gravitation that led to an explanation of terrestrial gravitation as well as of Kepler’s laws. Newton’s reasoning was that the moon revolving in an orbit of radius R m was subject to a centripetal acceleration due to earth’s gravity of magnitude','[{"s": "Legend has it that observing an apple falling from a tree, Newton was inspired to arrive at an universal law of gravitation that led to an explanation of terrestrial gravitation as well as of Kepler’s laws. Newton’s reasoning was that the moon revolving in an orbit of radius R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "was subject to a centripetal acceleration due to earth’s gravity of magnitude", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24790,62,40,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image101.png'),
(24791,62,41,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.3)','[{"s": "(8.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24793,62,43,'heading',4,'Central Forces',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24794,62,44,'paragraph',NULL,'We know the time rate of change of the angular momentum of a single particle about the origin is','[{"s": "We know the time rate of change of the angular momentum of a single particle about the origin is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24795,62,45,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image106.png'),
(24796,62,46,'paragraph',NULL,'The angular momentum of the particle is conserved, if the torque Gravitation-image114.png due to the force F on it vanishes. This happens either when F is zero or when F is along r. We are interested in forces which satisfy the latter condition. Central forces satisfy this condition.','[{"s": "The angular momentum of the particle is conserved, if the torque", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image114.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "due to the force F on it vanishes. This happens either when F is zero or when F is along r. We are interested in forces which satisfy the latter condition. Central forces satisfy this condition.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24797,62,47,'paragraph',NULL,'A ‘central’ force is always directed towards or away from a fixed point, i.e., along the position vector of the point of application of the force with respect to the fixed point. (See Figure below.) Further, the magnitude of a central force F depends on r, the distance of the point of application of the force from the fixed point; F = F(r).','[{"s": "A ‘central’ force is always directed towards or away from a fixed point, i.e., along the position vector of the point of application of the force with respect to the fixed point. (See Figure below.) Further, the magnitude of a central force F depends on r, the distance of the point of application of the force from the fixed point; F = F(r).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24798,62,48,'paragraph',NULL,'In the motion under a central force the angular momentum is always conserved. Two important results follow from this:','[{"s": "In the motion under a central force the angular momentum is always conserved. Two important results follow from this:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24799,62,49,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) The motion of a particle under the central force is always confined to a plane.','[{"s": "(1) The motion of a particle under the central force is always confined to a plane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24800,62,50,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) The position vector of the particle with respect to the centre of the force (i.e. the fixed point) has a constant areal velocity. In other words the position vector sweeps out equal areas in equal times as the particle moves under the influence of the central force.','[{"s": "(2) The position vector of the particle with respect to the centre of the force (i.e. the fixed point) has a constant areal velocity. In other words the position vector sweeps out equal areas in equal times as the particle moves under the influence of the central force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24801,62,51,'paragraph',NULL,'An immediate application of the above discussion can be made to the motion of a planet under the gravitational force of the sun. For convenience the sun may be taken to be so heavy that it is at rest. The gravitational force of the sun on the planet is directed towards the sun. This force also satisfies the requirement F = F(r), since F = G m 1 m 2 /r 2 where m 1 and m 2 are respectively the masses of the planet and the sun and G is the universal constant of gravitation. The two results (1) and (2) described above, therefore, apply to the motion of the planet. In fact, the result (2) is the well-known second law of Kepler.','[{"s": "An immediate application of the above discussion can be made to the motion of a planet under the gravitational force of the sun. For convenience the sun may be taken to be so heavy that it is at rest. The gravitational force of the sun on the planet is directed towards the sun. This force also satisfies the requirement F = F(r), since F = G m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are respectively the masses of the planet and the sun and G is the universal constant of gravitation. The two results (1) and (2) described above, therefore, apply to the motion of the planet. In fact, the result (2) is the well-known second law of Kepler.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24802,62,52,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image111.png'),
(24803,62,53,'paragraph',NULL,'Tr is the trejectory of the particle under the central force. At a position P, the force is directed along OP, O is the centre of the force taken as the origin. In time ∆t, the particle moves from P to P′, arc PP′ = ∆s = v ∆t. The tangent PQ at P to the trajectory gives the direction of the velocity at P. The area swept in ∆t is the area of sector POP′ ≈ (r sin α ) PP′/2 = (r v sin a) ∆t/2.)','[{"s": "Tr is the trejectory of the particle under the central force. At a position P, the force is directed along OP, O is the centre of the force taken as the origin. In time ∆t, the particle moves from P to P′, arc PP′ = ∆s = v ∆t. The tangent PQ at P to the trajectory gives the direction of the velocity at P. The area swept in ∆t is the area of sector POP′ ≈ (r sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "α", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PP′/2 = (r v sin a) ∆t/2.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24804,62,54,'paragraph',NULL,'This clearly shows that the force due to earth’s gravity decreases with distance. If one assumes that the gravitational force due to the earth decreases in proportion to the inverse square of the distance from the centre of the earth, we will have','[{"s": "This clearly shows that the force due to earth’s gravity decreases with distance. If one assumes that the gravitational force due to the earth decreases in proportion to the inverse square of the distance from the centre of the earth, we will have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24805,62,55,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 3.30.45 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image113.png');
