(24832,62,82,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image84.png'),
(24833,62,83,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image85.png'),
(24834,62,84,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image86.png'),
(24835,62,85,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 8.5 Three equal masses are placed at the three vertices of the ∆ ABC. A mass 2m is placed at the centroid G.','[{"s": "Fig. 8.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Three equal masses are placed at the three vertices of the ∆ ABC. A mass 2m is placed at the centroid G.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24836,62,86,'paragraph',NULL,'From the principle of superposition and the law of vector addition, the resultant gravitational force F R on (2m) is','[{"s": "From the principle of superposition and the law of vector addition, the resultant gravitational force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on (2m) is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24837,62,87,'paragraph',NULL,'F R = F GA + F GB + F GC','[{"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "GA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "GB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "GC", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24838,62,88,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-05 at 5.58.29 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image88.png'),
(24839,62,89,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternatively, one expects on the basis of symmetry that the resultant force ought to be zero.','[{"s": "Alternatively, one expects on the basis of symmetry that the resultant force ought to be zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24840,62,90,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Now if the mass at vertex A is doubled then','[{"s": "(b) Now if the mass at vertex A is doubled then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24841,62,91,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image89.png'),
(24842,62,92,'paragraph',NULL,'For the gravitational force between an extended object (like the earth) and a point mass, Eq. (8.5) is not directly applicable. Each point mass in the extended object will exert a force on the given point mass and these force will not all be in the same direction. We have to add up these forces vectorially for all the point masses in the extended object to get the total force. This is easily done using calculus. For two special cases, a simple law results when you do that :','[{"s": "For the gravitational force between an extended object (like the earth) and a point mass, Eq. (8.5) is not directly applicable. Each point mass in the extended object will exert a force on the given point mass and these force will not all be in the same direction. We have to add up these forces vectorially for all the point masses in the extended object to get the total force. This is easily done using calculus. For two special cases, a simple law results when you do that :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24843,62,93,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) The force of attraction between a hollow spherical shell of uniform density and a point mass situated outside is just as if the entire mass of the shell is concentrated at the centre of the shell.','[{"s": "(1)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The force of attraction between a hollow spherical shell of uniform density and a point mass situated outside is just as if the entire mass of the shell is concentrated at the centre of the shell.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24844,62,94,'paragraph',NULL,'Qualitatively this can be understood as follows: Gravitational forces caused by the various regions of the shell have components along the line joining the point mass to the centre as well as along a direction prependicular to this line. The components prependicular to this line cancel out when summing over all regions of the shell leaving only a resultant force along the line joining the point to the centre. The magnitude of this force works out to be as stated above.','[{"s": "Qualitatively this can be understood as follows: Gravitational forces caused by the various regions of the shell have components along the line joining the point mass to the centre as well as along a direction prependicular to this line. The components prependicular to this line cancel out when summing over all regions of the shell leaving only a resultant force along the line joining the point to the centre. The magnitude of this force works out to be as stated above.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24845,62,95,'heading',4,'Newton’s Principia',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24846,62,96,'paragraph',NULL,'Kepler had formulated his third law by 1619. The announcement of the underlying universal law of gravitation came about seventy years later with the publication in 1687 of Newton’s masterpiece Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica , often simply called the Principia .','[{"s": "Kepler had formulated his third law by 1619. The announcement of the underlying universal law of gravitation came about seventy years later with the publication in 1687 of Newton’s masterpiece", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "often simply called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Principia", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24847,62,97,'paragraph',NULL,'Around 1685, Edmund Halley (after whom the famous Halley’s comet is named), came to visit Newton at Cambridge and asked him about the nature of the trajectory of a body moving under the influence of an inverse square law. Without hesitation Newton replied that it had to be an ellipse, and further that he had worked it out long ago around 1665 when he was forced to retire to his farm house from Cambridge on account of a plague outbreak. Unfortunately, Newton had lost his papers. Halley prevailed upon Newton to produce his work in book form and agreed to bear the cost of publication. Newton accomplished this feat in eighteen months of superhuman effort. The Principia is a singular scientific masterpiece and in the words of Lagrange it is “the greatest production of the human mind.” The Indian born astrophysicist and Nobel laureate S. Chandrasekhar spent ten years writing a treatise on the Principia . His book, Newton’s Principia for the Common Reader brings into sharp focus the beauty, clarity and breath taking economy of Newton’s methods.','[{"s": "Around 1685, Edmund Halley (after whom the famous Halley’s comet is named), came to visit Newton at Cambridge and asked him about the nature of the trajectory of a body moving under the influence of an inverse square law. Without hesitation Newton replied that it had to be an ellipse, and further that he had worked it out long ago around 1665 when he was forced to retire to his farm house from Cambridge on account of a plague outbreak. Unfortunately, Newton had lost his papers. Halley prevailed upon Newton to produce his work in book form and agreed to bear the cost of publication. Newton accomplished this feat in eighteen months of superhuman effort. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Principia", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a singular scientific masterpiece and in the words of Lagrange it is “the greatest production of the human mind.” The Indian born astrophysicist and Nobel laureate S. Chandrasekhar spent ten years writing a treatise on the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Principia", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "His book, Newton’s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Principia for the Common Reader", "t": "text"}, {"s": "brings into sharp focus the beauty, clarity and breath taking economy of Newton’s methods.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24848,62,98,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) The force of attraction due to a hollow spherical shell of uniform density, on a point mass situated inside it is zero.','[{"s": "(2)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The force of attraction due to a hollow spherical shell of uniform density, on a point mass situated inside it is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24849,62,99,'paragraph',NULL,'Qualitatively, we can again understand this result. Various regions of the spherical shell attract the point mass inside it in various directions. These forces cancel each other completely.','[{"s": "Qualitatively, we can again understand this result.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Various regions of the spherical shell attract the point mass inside it in various directions.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These forces cancel each other completely.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24850,62,100,'heading',2,'8.4 THE GRAVITATIONAL CONSTANT',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24851,62,101,'paragraph',NULL,'The value of the gravitational constant G entering the Universal law of gravitation can be determined experimentally and this was first done by English scientist Henry Cavendish in 1798. The apparatus used by him is schematically shown in figure.8.6','[{"s": "The value of the gravitational constant G entering the Universal law of gravitation can be determined experimentally and this was first done by English scientist Henry Cavendish in 1798. The apparatus used by him is schematically shown in figure.8.6", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24852,62,102,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image90.png'),
(24853,62,103,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 8.6 Schematic drawing of Cavendish’s experiment. S 1 and S 2 are large spheres which are kept on either side (shown shades) of the masses at A and B. When the big spheres are taken to the other side of the masses (shown by dotted circles), the bar AB rotates a little since the torque reverses direction. The angle of rotation can be measured experimentally.','[{"s": "Fig. 8.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Schematic drawing of Cavendish’s experiment. S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are large spheres which are kept on either side (shown shades) of the masses at A and B. When the big spheres are taken to the other side of the masses (shown by dotted circles), the bar AB rotates a little since the torque reverses direction. The angle of rotation can be measured experimentally.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24855,62,105,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image91.png'),
(24856,62,106,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.6)','[{"s": "(8.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24857,62,107,'paragraph',NULL,'If L is the length of the bar AB , then the torque arising out of F is F multiplied by L. At equilibrium, this is equal to the restoring torque and hence','[{"s": "If L is the length of the bar AB , then the torque arising out of F is F multiplied by L. At equilibrium, this is equal to the restoring torque and hence", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24858,62,108,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image92.png'),
(24859,62,109,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.7)','[{"s": "(8.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24860,62,110,'paragraph',NULL,'Observation of θ thus enables one to calculate G from this equation.','[{"s": "Observation of θ thus enables one to calculate G from this equation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24861,62,111,'paragraph',NULL,'Since Cavendish’s experiment, the measurement of G has been refined and the currently accepted value is','[{"s": "Since Cavendish’s experiment, the measurement of G has been refined and the currently accepted value is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24862,62,112,'paragraph',NULL,'G = 6.67×10 -11 N m 2 /kg 2 (8.8)','[{"s": "G = 6.67×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(8.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24863,62,113,'heading',2,'8.5 ACCELERATION DUE TO GRAVITY OF THE EARTH',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33310,77,289,'paragraph',NULL,'14. Carnot engine is a reversible engine operating between two temperatures T1 (source) and T2 (sink). 2 The Carnot cycle consists of two isothermal processes connected by two adiabatic processes. The efficiency of a Carnot engine is given by','[{"s": "14.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Carnot engine is a reversible engine operating between two temperatures T1 (source) and T2 (sink).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The Carnot cycle consists of two isothermal processes connected by two adiabatic processes. The efficiency of a Carnot engine is given by", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038164", "2000038967", "2000110342"]',NULL),
(24864,62,114,'paragraph',NULL,'The earth can be imagined to be a sphere made of a large number of concentric spherical shells with the smallest one at the centre and the largest one at its surface. A point outside the earth is obviously outside all the shells. Thus, all the shells exert a gravitational force at the point outside just as if their masses are concentrated at their common centre according to the result stated in section 8.3. The total mass of all the shells combined is just the mass of the earth. Hence, at a point outside the earth, the gravitational force is just as if its entire mass of the earth is concentrated at its centre.','[{"s": "The earth can be imagined to be a sphere made of a large number of concentric spherical shells with the smallest one at the centre and the largest one at its surface. A point outside the earth is obviously outside all the shells. Thus, all the shells exert a gravitational force at the point outside just as if their masses are concentrated at their common centre according to the result stated in section 8.3. The total mass of all the shells combined is just the mass of the earth. Hence, at a point outside the earth, the gravitational force is just as if its entire mass of the earth is concentrated at its centre.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24865,62,115,'paragraph',NULL,'For a point inside the earth, the situation is different. This is illustrated in Fig. 8.7.','[{"s": "For a point inside the earth, the situation is different. This is illustrated in Fig. 8.7.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24866,62,116,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image93.png'),
(24867,62,117,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 8.7 The mass m is in a mine located at a depth d below the surface of the Earth of mass M E and radius R E . We treat the Earth to be spherically symmetric.','[{"s": "Fig. 8.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The mass m is in a mine located at a depth d below the surface of the Earth of mass M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and radius R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We treat the Earth to be spherically symmetric.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24868,62,118,'paragraph',NULL,'Again consider the earth to be made up of concentric shells as before and a point mass m situated at a distance r from the centre. The point P lies outside the sphere of radius r. For the shells of radius greater than r, the point P lies inside. Hence according to result stated in the last section, they exert no gravitational force on mass m kept at P. The shells with radius ≤ r make up a sphere of radius r for which the point P lies on the surface. This smaller sphere therefore exerts a force on a mass m at P as if its mass M r is concentrated at the centre. Thus the force on the mass m at P has a magnitude','[{"s": "Again consider the earth to be made up of concentric shells as before and a point mass m situated at a distance r from the centre. The point P lies outside the sphere of radius r. For the shells of radius greater than r, the point P lies inside. Hence according to result stated in the last section, they exert no gravitational force on mass m kept at P. The shells with radius ≤ r make up a sphere of radius r for which the point P lies on the surface. This smaller sphere therefore exerts a force on a mass m at P as if its mass M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is concentrated at the centre. Thus the force on the mass m at P has a magnitude", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24869,62,119,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image94.png'),
(24870,62,120,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.9)','[{"s": "(8.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24871,62,121,'paragraph',NULL,'We assume that the entire earth is of uniform density and hence its mass is Gravitation-image20.png where M E is the mass of the earth R E is its radius and ρ is the density. On the other hand the mass of the sphere M r of radius r is Gravitation-image22.png and hence','[{"s": "We assume that the entire earth is of uniform density and hence its mass is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image20.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "where M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the mass of the earth R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is its radius and ρ is the density. On the other hand the mass of the sphere M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of radius r is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image22.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and hence", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24872,62,122,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image23.png'),
(24873,62,123,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image24.png'),
(24874,62,124,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.10)','[{"s": "(8.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24875,62,125,'paragraph',NULL,'If the mass m is situated on the surface of earth, then r = R E and the gravitational force on it is, from Eq. (8.10)','[{"s": "If the mass m is situated on the surface of earth, then r = R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the gravitational force on it is, from Eq. (8.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24876,62,126,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image25.png'),
(24877,62,127,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.11)','[{"s": "(8.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24879,62,129,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image26.png'),
(24880,62,130,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.12)','[{"s": "(8.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24881,62,131,'paragraph',NULL,'Acceleration g is readily measurable. R E is a known quantity. The measurement of G by Cavendish’s experiment (or otherwise), combined with knowledge of g and R E enables one to estimate M E from Eq. (8.12). This is the reason why there is a popular statement regarding Cavendish : “Cavendish weighed the earth”.','[{"s": "Acceleration g is readily measurable. R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a known quantity. The measurement of G by Cavendish’s experiment (or otherwise), combined with knowledge of g and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "enables one to estimate M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from Eq. (8.12). This is the reason why there is a popular statement regarding Cavendish : “Cavendish weighed the earth”.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24882,62,132,'heading',2,'8.6 ACCELERATION DUE TO GRAVITY BELOW AND ABOVE THE SURFACE OF EARTH',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24883,62,133,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a point mass m at a height h above the surface of the earth as shown in Fig. 8.8(a). The radius of the earth is denoted by R E . Since this point is outside the earth, its distance from the centre of the earth is (R E + h ). If F (h) denoted the magnitude of the force on the point mass m , we get from Eq. (8.5) :','[{"s": "Consider a point mass m at a height h above the surface of the earth as shown in Fig. 8.8(a). The radius of the earth is denoted by R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since this point is outside the earth, its distance from the centre of the earth is (R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h ). If F (h) denoted the magnitude of the force on the point mass m , we get from Eq. (8.5) :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
