(24911,62,161,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, as we go down below earth’s surface, the acceleration due gravity decreases by a factor Gravitation-image44.png The remarkable thing about acceleration due to earth’s gravity is that it is maximum on its surface decreasing whether you go up or down.','[{"s": "Thus, as we go down below earth’s surface, the acceleration due gravity decreases by a factor", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image44.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "The remarkable thing about acceleration due to earth’s gravity is that it is maximum on its surface decreasing whether you go up or down.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24912,62,162,'heading',2,'8.7 GRAVITATIONAL POTENTIAL ENERGY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24913,62,163,'paragraph',NULL,'We had discussed earlier the notion of potential energy as being the energy stored in the body at its given position. If the position of the particle changes on account of forces acting on it, then the change in its potential energy is just the amount of work done on the body by the force. As we had discussed earlier, forces for which the work done is independent of the path are the conservative forces.','[{"s": "We had discussed earlier the notion of potential energy as being the energy stored in the body at its given position. If the position of the particle changes on account of forces acting on it, then the change in its potential energy is just the amount of work done on the body by the force. As we had discussed earlier, forces for which the work done is independent of the path are the conservative forces.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24914,62,164,'paragraph',NULL,'The force of gravity is a conservative force and we can calculate the potential energy of a body arising out of this force, called the gravitational potential energy. Consider points close to the surface of earth, at distances from the surface much smaller than the radius of the earth. In such cases, the force of gravity is practically a constant equal to mg, directed towards the centre of the earth. If we consider a point at a height h 1 from the surface of the earth and another point vertically above it at a height h 2 from the surface, the work done in lifting the particle of mass m from the first to the second position is denoted by W 12','[{"s": "The force of gravity is a conservative force and we can calculate the potential energy of a body arising out of this force, called the gravitational potential energy. Consider points close to the surface of earth, at distances from the surface much smaller than the radius of the earth. In such cases, the force of gravity is practically a constant equal to mg, directed towards the centre of the earth. If we consider a point at a height h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from the surface of the earth and another point vertically above it at a height h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from the surface, the work done in lifting the particle of mass m from the first to the second position is denoted by W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24915,62,165,'paragraph',NULL,'W 12 = Force × displacement','[{"s": "W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= Force × displacement", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24916,62,166,'paragraph',NULL,'= mg (h 2 – h 1 ) (8.20)','[{"s": "= mg (h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (8.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24917,62,167,'paragraph',NULL,'If we associate a potential energy W(h) at a point at a height h above the surface such that','[{"s": "If we associate a potential energy W(h) at a point at a height h above the surface such that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24918,62,168,'paragraph',NULL,'W(h) = mgh + W o (8.21)','[{"s": "W(h) = mgh + W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(8.21)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24919,62,169,'paragraph',NULL,'(where W o = constant) ;','[{"s": "(where W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= constant) ;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24920,62,170,'paragraph',NULL,'then it is clear that','[{"s": "then it is clear that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24921,62,171,'paragraph',NULL,'W 12 = W(h 2 ) – W(h 1 ) (8.22)','[{"s": "W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= W(h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") – W(h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (8.22)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24922,62,172,'paragraph',NULL,'The work done in moving the particle is just the difference of potential energy between its final and initial positions.Observe that the constant W o cancels out in Eq. (8.22). Setting h = 0 in the last equation, we get W ( h = 0 ) = W o. . h = 0 means points on the surface of the earth. Thus, W o is the potential energy on the surface of the earth.','[{"s": "The work done in moving the particle is just the difference of potential energy between its final and initial positions.Observe that the constant W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cancels out in Eq. (8.22). Setting h = 0 in the last equation, we get W ( h = 0 ) = W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o.", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". h = 0 means points on the surface of the earth. Thus, W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the potential energy on the surface of the earth.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24923,62,173,'paragraph',NULL,'If we consider points at arbitrary distance from the surface of the earth, the result just derived is not valid since the assumption that the gravitational force mg is a constant is no longer valid. However, from our discussion we know that a point outside the earth, the force of gravitation on a particle directed towards the centre of the earth is','[{"s": "If we consider points at arbitrary distance from the surface of the earth, the result just derived is not valid since the assumption that the gravitational force mg is a constant is no longer valid. However, from our discussion we know that a point outside the earth, the force of gravitation on a particle directed towards the centre of the earth is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24924,62,174,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image45.png'),
(24925,62,175,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.23)','[{"s": "(8.23)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24926,62,176,'paragraph',NULL,'where M E = mass of earth, m = mass of the particle and r its distance from the centre of the earth. If we now calculate the work done in lifting a particle from r = r 1 to r = r 2 (r 2 > r 1 ) along a vertical path, we get instead of Eq. (8.20)','[{"s": "where M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= mass of earth, m = mass of the particle and r its distance from the centre of the earth. If we now calculate the work done in lifting a particle from r = r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to r = r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") along a vertical path, we get instead of Eq. (8.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24927,62,177,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image46.png'),
(24928,62,178,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image47.png'),
(24929,62,179,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.24)','[{"s": "(8.24)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24931,62,181,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image48.png'),
