(24933,62,183,'paragraph',NULL,'valid for r > R ,','[{"s": "valid for r > R ,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24934,62,184,'paragraph',NULL,'so that once again W 12 = W(r 2 ) – W(r 1 ). Setting r = infinity in the last equation, we get W ( r = infinity ) = W 1 . Thus, W 1 is the potential energy at infinity. One should note that only the difference of potential energy between two points has a definite meaning from Eqs. (8.22) and (8.24). One conventionally sets W 1 equal to zero, so that the potential energy at a point is just the amount of work done in displacing the particle from infinity to that point.','[{"s": "so that once again W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= W(r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") – W(r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Setting r = infinity in the last equation, we get W ( r = infinity ) = W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the potential energy at infinity. One should note that only the difference of potential energy between two points has a definite meaning from Eqs. (8.22) and (8.24). One conventionally sets W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "equal to zero, so that the potential energy at a point is just the amount of work done in displacing the particle from infinity to that point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24936,62,186,'paragraph',NULL,'Gravitation-image49.png (if we choose V = 0 as r → ∞ )','[{"s": "Gravitation-image49.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(if we choose V = 0 as r → ∞ )", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24937,62,187,'paragraph',NULL,'It should be noted that an isolated system of particles will have the total potential energy that equals the sum of energies (given by the above equation) for all possible pairs of its constituent particles. This is an example of the application of the superposition principle.','[{"s": "It should be noted that an isolated system of particles will have the total potential energy that equals the sum of energies (given by the above equation) for all possible pairs of its constituent particles. This is an example of the application of the superposition principle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24938,62,188,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 8.3 Find the potential energy of a system of four particles placed at the vertices of a square of side l. Also obtain the potential at the centre of the square.','[{"s": "Example 8.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Find the potential energy of a system of four particles placed at the vertices of a square of side l. Also obtain the potential at the centre of the square.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24939,62,189,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Consider four masses each of mass m at the corners of a square of side l; See Fig. 8.9. We have four mass pairs at distance l and two diagonal pairs at distance Gravitation-image50.png','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Consider four masses each of mass m at the corners of a square of side l; See Fig. 8.9. We have four mass pairs at distance l and two diagonal pairs at distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image50.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24940,62,190,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence,','[{"s": "Hence,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
