(24974,62,224,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 8.10','[{"s": "Fig. 8.10", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24975,62,225,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The projectile is acted upon by two mutually opposing gravitational forces of the two spheres. The neutral point N (see Fig. 8.10) is defined as the position where the two forces cancel each other exactly. If ON = r, we have','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The projectile is acted upon by two mutually opposing gravitational forces of the two spheres. The neutral point N (see Fig. 8.10) is defined as the position where the two forces cancel each other exactly. If ON = r, we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24976,62,226,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image67.png'),
(24977,62,227,'paragraph',NULL,'(6R – r) 2 = 4r 2','[{"s": "(6R – r)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24978,62,228,'paragraph',NULL,'6R – r = ±2r','[{"s": "6R – r = ±2r", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24979,62,229,'paragraph',NULL,'r = 2R or – 6R.','[{"s": "r = 2R or – 6R.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24980,62,230,'paragraph',NULL,'The neutral point r = – 6R does not concern us in this example. Thus ON = r = 2R. It is sufficient to project the particle with a speed which would enable it to reach N. Thereafter, the greater gravitational pull of 4M would suffice. The mechanical energy at the surface of M is','[{"s": "The neutral point r = – 6R does not concern us in this example. Thus ON = r = 2R. It is sufficient to project the particle with a speed which would enable it to reach N. Thereafter, the greater gravitational pull of 4M would suffice. The mechanical energy at the surface of M is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24981,62,231,'paragraph',NULL,'Gravitation-image68.png .','[{"s": "Gravitation-image68.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24982,62,232,'paragraph',NULL,'At the neutral point N, the speed approaches zero. The mechanical energy at N is purely potential.','[{"s": "At the neutral point N, the speed approaches zero. The mechanical energy at N is purely potential.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24983,62,233,'paragraph',NULL,'Gravitation-image69.png .','[{"s": "Gravitation-image69.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24984,62,234,'paragraph',NULL,'From the principle of conservation of mechanical energy','[{"s": "From the principle of conservation of mechanical energy", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24985,62,235,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image70.png'),
(24986,62,236,'paragraph',NULL,'or Gravitation-image71.png','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image71.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24987,62,237,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image72.png'),
(24988,62,238,'paragraph',NULL,'A point to note is that the speed of the projectile is zero at N, but is nonzero when it strikes the heavier sphere 4 M. The calculation of this speed is left as an exercise to the students.','[{"s": "A point to note is that the speed of the projectile is zero at N, but is nonzero when it strikes the heavier sphere 4 M. The calculation of this speed is left as an exercise to the students.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24989,62,239,'heading',2,'8.9 EARTH SATELLITES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24990,62,240,'paragraph',NULL,'Earth satellites are objects which revolve around the earth. Their motion is very similar to the motion of planets around the Sun and hence Kepler’s laws of planetary motion are equally applicable to them. In particular, their orbits around the earth are circular or elliptic. Moon is the only natural satellite of the earth with a near circular orbit with a time period of approximately 27.3 days which is also roughly equal to the rotational period of the moon about its own axis. Since, 1957, advances in technology have enabled many countries including India to launch artificial earth satellites for practical use in fields like telecommunication, geophysics and meteorology.','[{"s": "Earth satellites are objects which revolve around the earth. Their motion is very similar to the motion of planets around the Sun and hence Kepler’s laws of planetary motion are equally applicable to them. In particular, their orbits around the earth are circular or elliptic. Moon is the only natural satellite of the earth with a near circular orbit with a time period of approximately 27.3 days which is also roughly equal to the rotational period of the moon about its own axis. Since, 1957, advances in technology have enabled many countries including India to launch artificial earth satellites for practical use in fields like telecommunication, geophysics and meteorology.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24991,62,241,'paragraph',NULL,'We will consider a satellite in a circular orbit of a distance (R E + h) from the centre of the earth, where R E = radius of the earth. If m is the mass of the satellite and V its speed, the centripetal force required for this orbit is','[{"s": "We will consider a satellite in a circular orbit of a distance (R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ h) from the centre of the earth, where R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= radius of the earth. If m is the mass of the satellite and V its speed, the centripetal force required for this orbit is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24992,62,242,'paragraph',NULL,'F(centripetal) = Gravitation-image73.png','[{"s": "F(centripetal) =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image73.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24993,62,243,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.33)','[{"s": "(8.33)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24994,62,244,'paragraph',NULL,'directed towards the centre. This centripetal force is provided by the gravitational force, which is','[{"s": "directed towards the centre. This centripetal force is provided by the gravitational force, which is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24995,62,245,'paragraph',NULL,'F(gravitation) = Gravitation-image74.png','[{"s": "F(gravitation) =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image74.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24996,62,246,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.34)','[{"s": "(8.34)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24997,62,247,'paragraph',NULL,'where M E is the mass of the earth.','[{"s": "where M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the mass of the earth.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(24999,62,249,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image64.png'),
(25000,62,250,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.35)','[{"s": "(8.35)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25002,62,252,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image65.png'),
(25003,62,253,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.36)','[{"s": "(8.36)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25004,62,254,'paragraph',NULL,'Gravitation-image66.png . In every orbit, the satellite traverses a distance 2π(R E + h) with speed V. Its time period T therefore is','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image66.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". In every orbit, the satellite traverses a distance 2π(R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ h) with speed V. Its time period T therefore is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25005,62,255,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image57.png'),
(25006,62,256,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.37)','[{"s": "(8.37)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25007,62,257,'paragraph',NULL,'on substitution of value of V from Eq. (8.35). Squaring both sides of Eq. (8.37), we get','[{"s": "on substitution of value of V from Eq. (8.35). Squaring both sides of Eq. (8.37), we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25008,62,258,'paragraph',NULL,'T 2 = k ( R E + h) 3 (where k = 4 π 2 / GM E ) (8.38)','[{"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= k ( R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ h)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(where k = 4 π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ GM", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (8.38)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25009,62,259,'paragraph',NULL,'which is Kepler’s law of periods, as applied to motion of satellites around the earth. For a satellite very close to the surface of earth h can be neglected in comparison to R E in Eq. (8.38). Hence, for such satellites, T is T o , where','[{"s": "which is Kepler’s law of periods, as applied to motion of satellites around the earth. For a satellite very close to the surface of earth h can be neglected in comparison to R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in Eq. (8.38). Hence, for such satellites, T is T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25010,62,260,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image58.png'),
(25011,62,261,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.39)','[{"s": "(8.39)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25012,62,262,'paragraph',NULL,'If we substitute the numerical values g ≅ 9.8 m s -2 and R E = 6400 km., we get','[{"s": "If we substitute the numerical values g ≅ 9.8 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 6400 km., we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25013,62,263,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 3.01.49 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image59.png'),
(25014,62,264,'paragraph',NULL,'Which is approximately 85 minutes.','[{"s": "Which is approximately 85 minutes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25015,62,265,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 8.5 The planet Mars has two moons, phobos and delmos. (i) phobos has a period 7 hours, 39 minutes and an orbital radius of 9.4 ×10 3 km. Calculate the mass of mars. (ii) Assume that earth and mars move in circular orbits around the sun, with the martian orbit being 1.52 times the orbital radius of the earth. What is the length of the martian year in days ?','[{"s": "Example 8.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The planet Mars has two moons, phobos and delmos. (i) phobos has a period 7 hours, 39 minutes and an orbital radius of 9.4 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "km. Calculate the mass of mars. (ii) Assume that earth and mars move in circular orbits around the sun, with the martian orbit being 1.52 times the orbital radius of the earth. What is the length of the martian year in days ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25016,62,266,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer (i) We employ Eq. (8.38) with the sun’s mass replaced by the martian mass M m','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(i) We employ Eq. (8.38) with the sun’s mass replaced by the martian mass M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
