(25035,62,285,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image102.png'),
(25036,62,286,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image105.png'),
(25037,62,287,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image107.png'),
(25038,62,288,'paragraph',NULL,'Both methods yield almost the same answer, the difference between them being less than 1%.','[{"s": "Both methods yield almost the same answer, the difference between them being less than 1%.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25039,62,289,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 8.7 Express the constant k of Eq. (8.38) in days and kilometres. Given k = 10 –13 s 2 m –3 . The moon is at a distance of 3.84 × 10 5 km from the earth. Obtain its time-period of revolution in days.','[{"s": "Example 8.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Express the constant k of Eq. (8.38) in days and kilometres. Given k = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The moon is at a distance of 3.84 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "km from the earth. Obtain its time-period of revolution in days.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25040,62,290,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Given','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Given", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25041,62,291,'paragraph',NULL,'k = 10 –13 s 2 m –3','[{"s": "k = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25042,62,292,'paragraph',NULL,'= Gravitation-image110.png','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image110.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25043,62,293,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1.33 ×10 –14 d 2 km –3','[{"s": "= 1.33 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "km", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25044,62,294,'paragraph',NULL,'Using Eq. (8.38) and the given value of k, the time period of the moon is','[{"s": "Using Eq. (8.38) and the given value of k, the time period of the moon is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25045,62,295,'paragraph',NULL,'T 2 = (1.33 × 10 -14 )(3.84 × 10 5 ) 3','[{"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (1.33 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-14", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")(3.84 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25046,62,296,'paragraph',NULL,'T = 27.3 d','[{"s": "T = 27.3 d", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25047,62,297,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that Eq. (8.38) also holds for elliptical orbits if we replace (R E +h) by the semi-major axis of the ellipse. The earth will then be at one of the foci of this ellipse.','[{"s": "Note that Eq. (8.38) also holds for elliptical orbits if we replace (R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+h) by the semi-major axis of the ellipse. The earth will then be at one of the foci of this ellipse.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25048,62,298,'heading',2,'8.10 ENERGY OF AN ORBITING SATELLITE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25050,62,300,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 3.05.51 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image112.png'),
(25051,62,301,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image115.png'),
(25052,62,302,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.40)','[{"s": "(8.40)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25053,62,303,'paragraph',NULL,'Considering gravitational potential energy at infinity to be zero, the potential energy at distance (R E +h) from the centre of the earth is','[{"s": "Considering gravitational potential energy at infinity to be zero, the potential energy at distance (R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+h) from the centre of the earth is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25054,62,304,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image18.png'),
(25055,62,305,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.41)','[{"s": "(8.41)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25056,62,306,'paragraph',NULL,'The K.E is positive whereas the P.E is negative. However, in magnitude the K.E. is half the P.E, so that the total E is','[{"s": "The K.E is positive whereas the P.E is negative. However, in magnitude the K.E. is half the P.E, so that the total E is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25057,62,307,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image17.png'),
(25058,62,308,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.42)','[{"s": "(8.42)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25060,62,310,'paragraph',NULL,'When the orbit of a satellite becomes elliptic, both the K.E. and P.E. vary from point to point. The total energy which remains constant is negative as in the circular orbit case. This is what we expect, since as we have discussed before if the total energy is positive or zero, the object escapes to infinity. Satellites are always at finite distance from the earth and hence their energies cannot be positive or zero.','[{"s": "When the orbit of a satellite becomes elliptic, both the K.E. and P.E. vary from point to point. The total energy which remains constant is negative as in the circular orbit case. This is what we expect, since as we have discussed before if the total energy is positive or zero, the object escapes to infinity. Satellites are always at finite distance from the earth and hence their energies cannot be positive or zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25061,62,311,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 8.8 A 400 kg satellite is in a circular orbit of radius 2R E about the Earth. How much energy is required to transfer it to a circular orbit of radius 4R E ? What are the changes in the kinetic and potential energies ?','[{"s": "Example 8.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A 400 kg satellite is in a circular orbit of radius 2R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "about the Earth. How much energy is required to transfer it to a circular orbit of radius 4R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "? What are the changes in the kinetic and potential energies ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25062,62,312,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Initially,','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Initially,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25063,62,313,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image21.png'),
(25064,62,314,'paragraph',NULL,'While finally','[{"s": "While finally", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25065,62,315,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image19.png'),
(25066,62,316,'paragraph',NULL,'The change in the total energy is','[{"s": "The change in the total energy is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25067,62,317,'paragraph',NULL,'∆E = E f – E i','[{"s": "∆E = E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25068,62,318,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image14.png'),
(25069,62,319,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 3.08.11 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image13.png'),
(25070,62,320,'paragraph',NULL,'The kinetic energy is reduced and it mimics ∆E, namely, ∆K = K f – K i = – 3.13 × 10 9 J.','[{"s": "The kinetic energy is reduced and it mimics ∆E, namely, ∆K = K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 3.13 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25071,62,321,'paragraph',NULL,'The change in potential energy is twice the change in the total energy, namely','[{"s": "The change in potential energy is twice the change in the total energy, namely", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25072,62,322,'paragraph',NULL,'∆V = V f – V i = – 6.25 × 10 9 J','[{"s": "∆V = V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 6.25 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25073,62,323,'heading',2,'8.11 GEOSTATIONARY AND POLAR SATELLITES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25074,62,324,'paragraph',NULL,'An interesting phenomenon arises if in we arrange the value of (R E E + h) for this purpose works out to be large as compared to R E :','[{"s": "An interesting phenomenon arises if in we arrange the value of (R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ h) for this purpose works out to be large as compared to R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25075,62,325,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image16.png'),
(25076,62,326,'paragraph',NULL,'(8.43)','[{"s": "(8.43)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25077,62,327,'paragraph',NULL,'and for T = 24 hours, h works out to be 35800 km. which is much larger than R E . Satellites in a circular orbits around the earth in the equatorial plane with T = 24 hours are called Geostationery Satellites. Clearly, since the earth rotates with the same period, the satellite would appear fixed from any point on earth. It takes very powerful rockets to throw up a satellite to such large heights above the earth but this has been done in view of the several benefits of many practical applications.','[{"s": "and for T = 24 hours, h works out to be 35800 km. which is much larger than R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Satellites in a circular orbits around the earth in the equatorial plane with T = 24 hours are called Geostationery Satellites. Clearly, since the earth rotates with the same period, the satellite would appear fixed from any point on earth. It takes very powerful rockets to throw up a satellite to such large heights above the earth but this has been done in view of the several benefits of many practical applications.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25078,62,328,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image15.png'),
(25079,62,329,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 8.11 A Polar satellite. A strip on earth’s surface (shown shaded) is visible from the satellite during one cycle. For the next revolution of the satellite, the earth has rotated a little on its axis so that an adjacent strip becomes visible.','[{"s": "Fig. 8.11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A Polar satellite. A strip on earth’s surface (shown shaded) is visible from the satellite during one cycle. For the next revolution of the satellite, the earth has rotated a little on its axis so that an adjacent strip becomes visible.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25080,62,330,'paragraph',NULL,'It is known that electromagnetic waves above a certain frequency are not reflected from ionosphere. Radio waves used for radio broadcast which are in the frequency range 2 MHz to 10 MHz, are below the critical frequency. They are therefore reflected by the ionosphere.','[{"s": "It is known that electromagnetic waves above a certain frequency are not reflected from ionosphere. Radio waves used for radio broadcast which are in the frequency range 2 MHz to 10 MHz, are below the critical frequency. They are therefore reflected by the ionosphere.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25081,62,331,'heading',2,'8.12 WEIGHTLESSNESS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25082,62,332,'paragraph',NULL,'Weight of an object is the force with which the earth attracts it. We are conscious of our own weight when we stand on a surface, since the surface exerts a force opposite to our weight to keep us at rest. The same principle holds good when we measure the weight of an object by a spring balance hung from a fixed point e.g. the ceiling. The object would fall down unless it is subject to a force opposite to gravity. This is exactly what the spring exerts on the object. This is because the spring is pulled down a little by the gravitational pull of the object and in turn the spring exerts a force on the object vertically upwards.','[{"s": "Weight of an object is the force with which the earth attracts it. We are conscious of our own weight when we stand on a surface, since the surface exerts a force opposite to our weight to keep us at rest. The same principle holds good when we measure the weight of an object by a spring balance hung from a fixed point e.g. the ceiling. The object would fall down unless it is subject to a force opposite to gravity. This is exactly what the spring exerts on the object. This is because the spring is pulled down a little by the gravitational pull of the object and in turn the spring exerts a force on the object vertically upwards.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25083,62,333,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, imagine that the top end of the balance is no longer held fixed to the top ceiling of the room. Both ends of the spring as well as the object move with identical acceleration g. The spring is not stretched and does not exert any upward force on the object which is moving down with acceleration g due to gravity. The reading recorded in the spring balance is zero since the spring is not stretched at all. If the object were a human being, he or she will not feel his weight since there is no upward force on him. Thus, when an object is in free fall, it is weightless and this phenomenon is usually called the phenomenon of weightlessness.','[{"s": "Now, imagine that the top end of the balance is no longer held fixed to the top ceiling of the room. Both ends of the spring as well as the object move with identical acceleration g. The spring is not stretched and does not exert any upward force on the object which is moving down with acceleration g due to gravity. The reading recorded in the spring balance is zero since the spring is not stretched at all. If the object were a human being, he or she will not feel his weight since there is no upward force on him. Thus, when an object is in free fall, it is weightless and this phenomenon is usually called the phenomenon of weightlessness.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25084,62,334,'paragraph',NULL,'In a satellite around the earth, every part and parcel of the satellite has an acceleration towards the centre of the earth which is exactly the value of earth’s acceleration due to gravity at that position. Thus in the satellite everything inside it is in a state of free fall. This is just as if we were falling towards the earth from a height. Thus, in a manned satellite, people inside experience no gravity. Gravity for us defines the vertical direction and thus for them there are no horizontal or vertical directions, all directions are the same. Pictures of astronauts floating in a satellite show this fact.','[{"s": "In a satellite around the earth, every part and parcel of the satellite has an acceleration towards the centre of the earth which is exactly the value of earth’s acceleration due to gravity at that position. Thus in the satellite everything inside it is in a state of free fall. This is just as if we were falling towards the earth from a height. Thus, in a manned satellite, people inside experience no gravity. Gravity for us defines the vertical direction and thus for them there are no horizontal or vertical directions, all directions are the same. Pictures of astronauts floating in a satellite show this fact.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25085,62,335,'heading',2,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25086,62,336,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Newton’s law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force of attraction between any two particles of masses m 1 and m 2 separated by a distance r has the magnitude','[{"s": "1. Newton’s law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force of attraction between any two particles of masses m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "separated by a distance r has the magnitude", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25087,62,337,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Gravitation-image12.png'),
(25088,62,338,'paragraph',NULL,'where G is the universal gravitational constant, which has the value 6.672 ×10 –11 N m 2 kg –2 .','[{"s": "where G is the universal gravitational constant, which has the value 6.672 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25089,62,339,'paragraph',NULL,'2. If we have to find the resultant gravitational force acting on the particle m due to a number of masses M 1 , M 2 , ….M n etc. we use the principle of superposition. Let F 1 , F 2 , ….F n be the individual forces due to M 1 , M 2 , ….M n, each given by the law of gravitation. From the principle of superposition each force acts independently and uninfluenced by the other bodies. The resultant force F R is then found by vector addition','[{"s": "2. If we have to find the resultant gravitational force acting on the particle m due to a number of masses M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", ….M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "etc. we use the principle of superposition. Let F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", ….F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the individual forces due to M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", ….M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "each given by the law of gravitation. From the principle of superposition each force acts independently and uninfluenced by the other bodies. The resultant force F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is then found by vector addition", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25090,62,340,'paragraph',NULL,'F R = F 1 + F 2 + ……+ F n = Gravitation-image11.png','[{"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ……+ F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image11.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25091,62,341,'paragraph',NULL,'where the symbol ‘Σ’ stands for summation.','[{"s": "where the symbol ‘Σ’ stands for summation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25092,62,342,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Kepler’s laws of planetary motion state that','[{"s": "3. Kepler’s laws of planetary motion state that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25093,62,343,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) All planets move in elliptical orbits with the Sun at one of the focal points','[{"s": "(a) All planets move in elliptical orbits with the Sun at one of the focal points", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25094,62,344,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The radius vector drawn from the Sun to a planet sweeps out equal areas in equal time intervals. This follows from the fact that the force of gravitation on the planet is central and hence angular momentum is conserved.','[{"s": "(b) The radius vector drawn from the Sun to a planet sweeps out equal areas in equal time intervals. This follows from the fact that the force of gravitation on the planet is central and hence angular momentum is conserved.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25095,62,345,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The square of the orbital period of a planet is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis of the elliptical orbit of the planet','[{"s": "(c) The square of the orbital period of a planet is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis of the elliptical orbit of the planet", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25096,62,346,'paragraph',NULL,'The period T and radius R of the circular orbit of a planet about the Sun are related by','[{"s": "The period T and radius R of the circular orbit of a planet about the Sun are related by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
