(25153,63,12,'heading',4,'13.1 Introduction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25154,63,13,'paragraph',NULL,'Boyle discovered the law named after him in 1661. Boyle, Newton and several others tried to explain the behaviour of gases by considering that gases are made up of tiny atomic particles. The actual atomic theory got established more than 150 years later. Kinetic theory explains the behaviour of gases based on the idea that the gas consists of rapidly moving atoms or molecules. This is possible as the inter-atomic forces, which are short range forces that are important for solids and liquids, can be neglected for gases. The kinetic theory was developed in the nineteenth century by Maxwell, Boltzmann and others. It has been remarkably successful. It gives a molecular interpretation of pressure and temperature of a gas, and is consistent with gas laws and Avogadro’s hypothesis. It correctly explains specific heat capacities of many gases. It also relates measurable properties of gases such as viscosity, conduction and diffusion with molecular parameters, yielding estimates of molecular sizes and masses. This chapter gives an introduction to kinetic theory.','[{"s": "Boyle discovered the law named after him in 1661. Boyle, Newton and several others tried to explain the behaviour of gases by considering that gases are made up of tiny atomic particles. The actual atomic theory got established more than 150 years later. Kinetic theory explains the behaviour of gases based on the idea that the gas consists of rapidly moving atoms or molecules. This is possible as the inter-atomic forces, which are short range forces that are important for solids and liquids, can be neglected for gases. The kinetic theory was developed in the nineteenth century by Maxwell, Boltzmann and others. It has been remarkably successful. It gives a molecular interpretation of pressure and temperature of a gas, and is consistent with gas laws and Avogadro’s hypothesis. It correctly explains specific heat capacities of many gases. It also relates measurable properties of gases such as viscosity, conduction and diffusion with molecular parameters, yielding estimates of molecular sizes and masses. This chapter gives an introduction to kinetic theory.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25155,63,14,'heading',4,'13.2 Molecular Nature of Matter',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25156,63,15,'paragraph',NULL,'Richard Feynman, one of the great physicists of 20th century considers the discovery that “Matter is made up of atoms” to be a very significant one. Humanity may suffer annihilation (due to nuclear catastrophe) or extinction (due to environmental disasters) if we do not act wisely. If that happens, and all of scientific knowledge were to be destroyed then Feynman would like the ‘Atomic Hypothesis’ to be communicated to the next generation of creatures in the universe. Atomic Hypothesis: All things are made of atoms - little particles that move around in perpetual motion, attracting each other when they are a little distance apart, but repelling upon being squeezed into one another.','[{"s": "Richard Feynman, one of the great physicists of 20th century considers the discovery that “Matter is made up of atoms” to be a very significant one. Humanity may suffer annihilation (due to nuclear catastrophe) or extinction (due to environmental disasters) if we do not act wisely. If that happens, and all of scientific knowledge were to be destroyed then Feynman would like the ‘Atomic Hypothesis’ to be communicated to the next generation of creatures in the universe. Atomic Hypothesis: All things are made of atoms - little particles that move around in perpetual motion, attracting each other when they are a little distance apart, but repelling upon being squeezed into one another.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25157,63,16,'paragraph',NULL,'Speculation that matter may not be continuous, existed in many places and cultures. Kanada in India and Democritus in Greece had suggested that matter may consist of indivisible constituents. The scientific ‘Atomic Theory’ is usually credited to John Dalton. He proposed the atomic theory to explain the laws of definite and multiple proportions obeyed by elements when they combine into compounds. The first law says that any given compound has, a fixed proportion by mass of its constituents. The second law says that when two elements form more than one compound, for a fixed mass of one element, the masses of the other elements are in ratio of small integers.','[{"s": "Speculation that matter may not be continuous, existed in many places and cultures. Kanada in India and Democritus in Greece had suggested that matter may consist of indivisible constituents. The scientific ‘Atomic Theory’ is usually credited to John Dalton. He proposed the atomic theory to explain the laws of definite and multiple proportions obeyed by elements when they combine into compounds. The first law says that any given compound has, a fixed proportion by mass of its constituents. The second law says that when two elements form more than one compound, for a fixed mass of one element, the masses of the other elements are in ratio of small integers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25158,63,17,'heading',4,'Atomic Hypothesis in Ancient India and Greece',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25159,63,18,'paragraph',NULL,'Though John Dalton is credited with the introduction of atomic viewpoint in modern science, scholars in ancient India and Greece conjectured long before the existence of atoms and molecules. In the Vaiseshika school of thought in India founded by Kanada (Sixth century B.C.) the atomic picture was developed in considerable detail. Atoms were thought to be eternal, indivisible, infinitesimal and ultimate parts of matter. It was argued that if matter could be subdivided without an end, there would be no difference between a mustard seed and the Meru mountain. The four kinds of atoms (Paramanu — Sanskrit word for the smallest particle) postulated were Bhoomi (Earth), Ap (water), Tejas (fire) and Vayu (air) that have characteristic mass and other attributes, were propounded. Akasa (space) was thought to have no atomic structure and was continuous and inert. Atoms combine to form different molecules (e.g. two atoms combine to form a diatomic molecule dvyanuka, three atoms form a tryanuka or a triatomic molecule), their properties depending upon the nature and ratio of the constituent atoms. The size of the atoms was also estimated, by conjecture or by methods that are not known to us. The estimates vary. In Lalitavistara, a famous biography of the Buddha written mainly in the second century B.C., the estimate is close to the modern estimate of atomic size, of the order of 10–10 m.','[{"s": "Though John Dalton is credited with the introduction of atomic viewpoint in modern science, scholars in ancient India and Greece conjectured long before the existence of atoms and molecules. In the Vaiseshika school of thought in India founded by Kanada (Sixth century B.C.) the atomic picture was developed in considerable detail. Atoms were thought to be eternal, indivisible, infinitesimal and ultimate parts of matter. It was argued that if matter could be subdivided without an end, there would be no difference between a mustard seed and the Meru mountain. The four kinds of atoms (Paramanu — Sanskrit word for the smallest particle) postulated were Bhoomi (Earth), Ap (water), Tejas (fire) and Vayu (air) that have characteristic mass and other attributes, were propounded. Akasa (space) was thought to have no atomic structure and was continuous and inert. Atoms combine to form different molecules (e.g. two atoms combine to form a diatomic molecule dvyanuka, three atoms form a tryanuka or a triatomic molecule), their properties depending upon the nature and ratio of the constituent atoms. The size of the atoms was also estimated, by conjecture or by methods that are not known to us. The estimates vary. In Lalitavistara, a famous biography of the Buddha written mainly in the second century B.C., the estimate is close to the modern estimate of atomic size, of the order of 10–10 m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25160,63,19,'paragraph',NULL,'In ancient Greece, Democritus (Fourth century B.C.) is best known for his atomic hypothesis. The word ‘atom’ means ‘indivisible’ in Greek. According to him, atoms differ from each other physically, in shape, size and other properties and this resulted in the different properties of the substances formed by their combination. The atoms of water were smooth and round and unable to ‘hook’ on to each other, which is why liquid /water flows easily. The atoms of earth were rough and jagged, so they held together to form hard substances. The atoms of fire were thorny which is why it caused painful burns. These fascinating ideas, despite their ingenuity, could not evolve much further, perhaps because they were intuitive conjectures and speculations not tested and modified by quantitative experiments - the hallmark of modern science.','[{"s": "In ancient Greece, Democritus (Fourth century B.C.) is best known for his atomic hypothesis. The word ‘atom’ means ‘indivisible’ in Greek. According to him, atoms differ from each other physically, in shape, size and other properties and this resulted in the different properties of the substances formed by their combination. The atoms of water were smooth and round and unable to ‘hook’ on to each other, which is why liquid /water flows easily. The atoms of earth were rough and jagged, so they held together to form hard substances. The atoms of fire were thorny which is why it caused painful burns. These fascinating ideas, despite their ingenuity, could not evolve much further, perhaps because they were intuitive conjectures and speculations not tested and modified by quantitative experiments - the hallmark of modern science.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25161,63,20,'paragraph',NULL,'To explain the laws Dalton suggested, about 200 years ago, that the smallest constituents of an element are atoms. Atoms of one element are identical but differ from those of other elements. A small number of atoms of each element combine to form a molecule of the compound. Gay Lussac’s law, also given in early 19th century, states: When gases combine chemically to yield another gas, their volumes are in the ratios of small integers. Avogadro’s law (or hypothesis) says: Equal volumes of all gases at equal temperature and pressure have the same number of molecules. Avogadro’s law, when combined with Dalton’s theory explains Gay Lussac’s law. Since the elements are often in the form of molecules, Dalton’s atomic theory can also be referred to as the molecular theory of matter. The theory is now well accepted by scientists. However even at the end of the nineteenth century there were famous scientists who did not believe in atomic theory !','[{"s": "To explain the laws Dalton suggested, about 200 years ago, that the smallest constituents of an element are atoms. Atoms of one element are identical but differ from those of other elements. A small number of atoms of each element combine to form a molecule of the compound. Gay Lussac’s law, also given in early 19th century, states: When gases combine chemically to yield another gas, their volumes are in the ratios of small integers. Avogadro’s law (or hypothesis) says: Equal volumes of all gases at equal temperature and pressure have the same number of molecules. Avogadro’s law, when combined with Dalton’s theory explains Gay Lussac’s law. Since the elements are often in the form of molecules, Dalton’s atomic theory can also be referred to as the molecular theory of matter. The theory is now well accepted by scientists. However even at the end of the nineteenth century there were famous scientists who did not believe in atomic theory !", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25162,63,21,'paragraph',NULL,'From many observations, in recent times we now know that molecules (made up of one or more atoms) constitute matter. Electron microscopes and scanning tunnelling microscopes enable us to even see them. The size of an atom is about an angstrom (10 -10 m). In solids, which are tightly packed, atoms are spaced about a few angstroms (2 Å) apart. In liquids the separation between atoms is also about the same. In liquids the atoms are not as rigidly fixed as in solids, and can move around. This enables a liquid to flow. In gases the interatomic distances are in tens of angstroms. The average distance a molecule can travel without colliding is called the mean free path. The mean free path, in gases, is of the order of thousands of angstroms. The atoms are much freer in gases and can travel long distances without colliding. If they are not enclosed, gases disperse away. In solids and liquids the closeness makes the interatomic force important. The force has a long range attraction and a short range repulsion. The atoms attract when they are at a few angstroms but repel when they come closer. The static appearance of a gas is misleading. The gas is full of activity and the equilibrium is a dynamic one. In dynamic equilibrium, molecules collide and change their speeds during the collision. Only the average properties are constant.','[{"s": "From many observations, in recent times we now know that molecules (made up of one or more atoms) constitute matter. Electron microscopes and scanning tunnelling microscopes enable us to even see them. The size of an atom is about an angstrom (10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m). In solids, which are tightly packed, atoms are spaced about a few angstroms (2 Å) apart. In liquids the separation between atoms is also about the same. In liquids the atoms are not as rigidly fixed as in solids, and can move around. This enables a liquid to flow. In gases the interatomic distances are in tens of angstroms. The average distance a molecule can travel without colliding is called the mean free path. The mean free path, in gases, is of the order of thousands of angstroms. The atoms are much freer in gases and can travel long distances without colliding. If they are not enclosed, gases disperse away. In solids and liquids the closeness makes the interatomic force important. The force has a long range attraction and a short range repulsion. The atoms attract when they are at a few angstroms but repel when they come closer. The static appearance of a gas is misleading. The gas is full of activity and the equilibrium is a dynamic one. In dynamic equilibrium, molecules collide and change their speeds during the collision. Only the average properties are constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25163,63,22,'paragraph',NULL,'Atomic theory is not the end of our quest, but the beginning. We now know that atoms are not indivisible or elementary. They consist of a nucleus and electrons. The nucleus itself is made up of protons and neutrons. The protons and neutrons are again made up of quarks. Even quarks may not be the end of the story. There may be string like elementary entities. Nature always has surprises for us, but the search for truth is often enjoyable and the discoveries beautiful. In this chapter, we shall limit ourselves to understanding the behaviour of gases (and a little bit of solids), as a collection of moving molecules in incessant motion.','[{"s": "Atomic theory is not the end of our quest, but the beginning. We now know that atoms are not indivisible or elementary. They consist of a nucleus and electrons. The nucleus itself is made up of protons and neutrons. The protons and neutrons are again made up of quarks. Even quarks may not be the end of the story. There may be string like elementary entities. Nature always has surprises for us, but the search for truth is often enjoyable and the discoveries beautiful. In this chapter, we shall limit ourselves to understanding the behaviour of gases (and a little bit of solids), as a collection of moving molecules in incessant motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25164,63,23,'heading',4,'13.3 Behaviour of Gases',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25165,63,24,'paragraph',NULL,'Properties of gases are easier to understand than those of solids and liquids. This is mainly because in a gas, molecules are far from each other and their mutual interactions are negligible except when two molecules collide. Gases at low pressures and high temperatures much above that at which they liquefy (or solidify) approximately satisfy a simple relation between their pressure, temperature and volume given by (see Chapter 11)','[{"s": "Properties of gases are easier to understand than those of solids and liquids. This is mainly because in a gas, molecules are far from each other and their mutual interactions are negligible except when two molecules collide. Gases at low pressures and high temperatures much above that at which they liquefy (or solidify) approximately satisfy a simple relation between their pressure, temperature and volume given by (see Chapter 11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25166,63,25,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = KT (13.1)','[{"s": "PV = KT (13.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25167,63,26,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image31.gif'),
(25168,63,27,'paragraph',NULL,'John Dalton (1766 – 1844)','[{"s": "John Dalton (1766 – 1844)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25169,63,28,'paragraph',NULL,'He was an English chemist. When different types of atoms combine, they obey certain simple laws. Dalton’s atomic theory explains these laws in a simple way. He also gave a theory of colour blindness.','[{"s": "He was an English chemist. When different types of atoms combine, they obey certain simple laws. Dalton’s atomic theory explains these laws in a simple way. He also gave a theory of colour blindness.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25170,63,29,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image33.gif'),
(25171,63,30,'paragraph',NULL,'Amedeo Avogadro (1776 – 1856)','[{"s": "Amedeo Avogadro (1776 – 1856)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25172,63,31,'paragraph',NULL,'He made a brilliant guess that equal volumes of gases have equal number of molecules at the same temperature and pressure. This helped in understanding the combination of different gases in a very simple way. It is now called Avogadro’s hypothesis (or law). He also suggested that the smallest constituent of gases like hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen are not atoms but diatomic molecules.','[{"s": "He made a brilliant guess that equal volumes of gases have equal number of molecules at the same temperature and pressure. This helped in understanding the combination of different gases in a very simple way. It is now called Avogadro’s hypothesis (or law). He also suggested that the smallest constituent of gases like hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen are not atoms but diatomic molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25173,63,32,'paragraph',NULL,'for a given sample of the gas. Here T is the temperature in kelvin or (absolute) scale. K is aconstant for the given sample but varies with the volume of the gas. If we now bring in the idea of atoms or molecules, then K is proportional to the number of molecules, (say) N in the sample. We can write K = N k . Observation tells us that this k is same for all gases. It is calledBoltzmann constant and is denoted by k B.','[{"s": "for a given sample of the gas. Here T is the temperature in kelvin or (absolute) scale. K is aconstant for the given sample but varies with the volume of the gas. If we now bring in the idea of atoms or molecules, then K is proportional to the number of molecules, (say) N in the sample. We can write K = N k . Observation tells us that this k is same for all gases. It is calledBoltzmann constant and is denoted by k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25174,63,33,'image',NULL,'1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image32.png'),
(25175,63,34,'paragraph',NULL,'if P, V and T are same, then N is also same for all gases. This is Avogadro’s hypothesis, that the number of molecules per unit volume is the same for all gases at a fixed temperature and pressure. The number in 22.4 litres of any gas is 6.02 × 10 23 . This is known as Avogadro number and is denoted by N A . The mass of 22.4 litres of any gas is equal to its molecular weight in grams at S.T.P (standard temperature 273 K and pressure 1 atm). This amount of substance is called a mole (see Chapter 2 for a more precise definition). Avogadro had guessed the equality of numbers in equal volumes of gas at a fixed temperature and pressure from chemical reactions. Kinetic theory justifies this hypothesis. The perfect gas equation can be written as','[{"s": "if P, V and T are same, then N is also same for all gases. This is Avogadro’s hypothesis, that the number of molecules per unit volume is the same for all gases at a fixed temperature and pressure. The number in 22.4 litres of any gas is 6.02 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "23", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This is known as Avogadro number and is denoted by N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The mass of 22.4 litres of any gas is equal to its molecular weight in grams at S.T.P (standard temperature 273 K and pressure 1 atm). This amount of substance is called a mole (see Chapter 2 for a more precise definition). Avogadro had guessed the equality of numbers in equal volumes of gas at a fixed temperature and pressure from chemical reactions. Kinetic theory justifies this hypothesis. The perfect gas equation can be written as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25176,63,35,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = μ RT (13.3)','[{"s": "PV = μ RT (13.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25177,63,36,'paragraph',NULL,'where μ is the number of moles and R = N A k B is a universal constant. The temperature T is absolute temperature. Choosing kelvin scale for absolute temperature, R = 8.314 J mol –1 K –1.','[{"s": "where μ is the number of moles and R = N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a universal constant. The temperature T is absolute temperature. Choosing kelvin scale for absolute temperature, R = 8.314 J mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25178,63,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Here','[{"s": "Here", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25179,63,38,'image',NULL,'1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image32.png'),
(25180,63,39,'paragraph',NULL,'where M is the mass of the gas containing N molecules, M 0 is the molar mass and N A the Avogadro’s number. Using Eqs. (13.4) and (13.3) can also be written as','[{"s": "where M is the mass of the gas containing N molecules, M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the molar mass and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the Avogadro’s number. Using Eqs. (13.4) and (13.3) can also be written as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25181,63,40,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = k B NT or P = k B nT','[{"s": "PV = k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NT or P = k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25182,63,41,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image34.png'),
(25183,63,42,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.13.1 Real gases approach ideal gas behaviour at low pressures and high temperatures.','[{"s": "Fig.13.1 Real gases approach ideal gas behaviour at low pressures and high temperatures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25185,63,44,'image',NULL,'2',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image37.png'),
(25186,63,45,'paragraph',NULL,'where r is the mass density of the gas. A gas that satisfies Eq. (13.3) exactly at all pressures and temperatures is defined to be an ideal gas. An ideal gas is a simple theoretical model of a gas. No real gas is truly ideal. Fig. 13.1 shows departures from ideal gas behaviour for a real gas at three different temperatures. Notice that all curves approach the ideal gas behaviour for low pressures and high temperatures. At low pressures or high temperatures the molecules are far apart and molecular interactions are negligible. Without interactions the gas behaves like an ideal one. If we fix μ and T in Eq. (13.3), we get','[{"s": "where r is the mass density of the gas. A gas that satisfies Eq. (13.3) exactly at all pressures and temperatures is defined to be an ideal gas. An ideal gas is a simple theoretical model of a gas. No real gas is truly ideal. Fig. 13.1 shows departures from ideal gas behaviour for a real gas at three different temperatures. Notice that all curves approach the ideal gas behaviour for low pressures and high temperatures. At low pressures or high temperatures the molecules are far apart and molecular interactions are negligible. Without interactions the gas behaves like an ideal one. If we fix μ and T in Eq. (13.3), we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25187,63,46,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = constant (13.6)','[{"s": "PV = constant (13.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25188,63,47,'paragraph',NULL,'i.e., keeping temperature constant, pressure of a given mass of gas varies inversely with volume. This is the famous Boyle’s law. Fig. 13.2 shows comparison between experimental P-V curves and the theoretical curves predicted by Boyle’s law. Once again you see that the agreement is good at high temperatures and low pressures. Next, if you fix P, Eq. (13.1) shows that V α T i.e., for a fixed pressure, the volume of a gas is proportional to its absolute temperature T (Charles’ law). See Fig. 13.3.','[{"s": "i.e., keeping temperature constant, pressure of a given mass of gas varies inversely with volume. This is the famous Boyle’s law. Fig. 13.2 shows comparison between experimental P-V curves and the theoretical curves predicted by Boyle’s law. Once again you see that the agreement is good at high temperatures and low pressures. Next, if you fix P, Eq. (13.1) shows that V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "α", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T i.e., for a fixed pressure, the volume of a gas is proportional to its absolute temperature T (Charles’ law). See Fig. 13.3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25189,63,48,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image36.png'),
(25190,63,49,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.13.2 Experimental P-V curves (solid lines) for steam at three temperatures compared with Boyle’s law (dotted lines). P is in units of 22 atm and V in units of 0.09 litres.','[{"s": "Fig.13.2 Experimental P-V curves (solid lines) for steam at three temperatures compared with Boyle’s law (dotted lines). P is in units of 22 atm and V in units of 0.09 litres.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25191,63,50,'paragraph',NULL,'Finally, consider a mixture of non-interacting ideal gases: μ 1 moles of gas 1, μ 2 moles of gas 2, etc. in a vessel of volume V at temperature T and pressure P. It is then found that the equation of state of the mixture is :','[{"s": "Finally, consider a mixture of non-interacting ideal gases: μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "moles of gas 1, μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "moles of gas 2, etc. in a vessel of volume V at temperature T and pressure P. It is then found that the equation of state of the mixture is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25192,63,51,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = ( μ 1 + μ 2 +… ) RT (13.7)','[{"s": "PV = ( μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+… ) RT (13.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25193,63,52,'image',NULL,'3',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image42.png'),
(25194,63,53,'paragraph',NULL,'= P 1 + P 2 + … (13.9)','[{"s": "= P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ … (13.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25195,63,54,'paragraph',NULL,'Clearly P 1 = μ 1 R T/V is the pressure that gas 1 would exert at the same conditions of volume and temperature if no other gases were present. This is called the partial pressure of the gas. Thus, the total pressure of a mixture of ideal gases is the sum of partial pressures. This is Dalton’s law of partial pressures.','[{"s": "Clearly P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R T/V is the pressure that gas 1 would exert at the same conditions of volume and temperature if no other gases were present. This is called the partial pressure of the gas. Thus, the total pressure of a mixture of ideal gases is the sum of partial pressures. This is Dalton’s law of partial pressures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25196,63,55,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image40.png'),
(25197,63,56,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.3 Experimental T-V curves (solid lines) for CO2 at three pressures compared with Charles’ law (dotted lines). T is in units of 300 K and V in units of 0.13 litres.','[{"s": "Fig. 13.3 Experimental T-V curves (solid lines) for CO2 at three pressures compared with Charles’ law (dotted lines). T is in units of 300 K and V in units of 0.13 litres.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25198,63,57,'paragraph',NULL,'We next consider some examples which give us information about the volume occupied by the molecules and the volume of a single molecule.','[{"s": "We next consider some examples which give us information about the volume occupied by the molecules and the volume of a single molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25199,63,58,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.1 The density of water is 1000 kg m –3 . The density of water vapour at 100 °C and 1 atm pressure is 0.6 kg m –3 . The volume of a molecule multiplied by the total number gives ,what is called, molecular volume. Estimate the ratio (or fraction) of the molecular volume to the total volume occupied by the water vapour under the above conditions of temperature and pressure.','[{"s": "Example 13.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The density of water is 1000 kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The density of water vapour at 100 °C and 1 atm pressure is 0.6 kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The volume of a molecule multiplied by the total number gives ,what is called, molecular volume. Estimate the ratio (or fraction) of the molecular volume to the total volume occupied by the water vapour under the above conditions of temperature and pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25200,63,59,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer For a given mass of water molecules, the density is less if volume is large. So the volume of the vapour is 1000/0.6 = 1/(6 ×10 -4 ) times larger. If densities of bulk water and water molecules are same, then the fraction of molecular volume to the total volume in liquid state is 1. As volume in vapour state has increased, the fractional volume is less by the same amount, i.e. 6×10 -4 .','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For a given mass of water molecules, the density is less if volume is large. So the volume of the vapour is 1000/0.6 = 1/(6 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") times larger. If densities of bulk water and water molecules are same, then the fraction of molecular volume to the total volume in liquid state is 1. As volume in vapour state has increased, the fractional volume is less by the same amount, i.e. 6×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25201,63,60,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.2 Estimate the volume of a water molecule using the data in Example 13.1.','[{"s": "Example 13.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Estimate the volume of a water molecule using the data in Example 13.1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25202,63,61,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer In the liquid (or solid) phase, the molecules of water are quite closely packed. The density of water molecule may therefore, be regarded as roughly equal to the density of bulk water = 1000 kg m –3 . To estimate the volume of a water molecule, we need to know the mass of a single water molecule. We know that 1 mole of water has a mass approximately equal to (2 + 16)g = 18 g = 0.018 kg. Since 1 mole contains about 6 × 10 23 molecules (Avogadro’s number), the mass of a molecule of water is (0.018)/(6 × 10 23 ) kg = 3 × 10 –26 kg. Therefore, a rough estimate of the volume of a water molecule is as follows :','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the liquid (or solid) phase, the molecules of water are quite closely packed. The density of water molecule may therefore, be regarded as roughly equal to the density of bulk water = 1000 kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". To estimate the volume of a water molecule, we need to know the mass of a single water molecule. We know that 1 mole of water has a mass approximately equal to (2 + 16)g = 18 g = 0.018 kg. Since 1 mole contains about 6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "23", "t": "text"}, {"s": "molecules (Avogadro’s number), the mass of a molecule of water is (0.018)/(6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "23", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") kg = 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–26", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg. Therefore, a rough estimate of the volume of a water molecule is as follows :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25203,63,62,'paragraph',NULL,'Volume of a water molecule','[{"s": "Volume of a water molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25204,63,63,'paragraph',NULL,'= (3 × 10 –26 kg)/ (1000 kg m –3 )','[{"s": "= (3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–26", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg)/ (1000 kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25205,63,64,'paragraph',NULL,'= 3 × 10 –29 m 3','[{"s": "= 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–29", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25206,63,65,'paragraph',NULL,'= (4/3) p (Radius) 3','[{"s": "= (4/3) p (Radius)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25207,63,66,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence, Radius » 2 ×10 -10 m = 2 Å','[{"s": "Hence, Radius » 2 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m = 2 Å", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25208,63,67,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.3 What is the average distance between atoms (interatomic distance) in water? Use the data given in Examples 13.1 and 13.2.','[{"s": "Example 13.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What is the average distance between atoms (interatomic distance) in water? Use the data given in Examples 13.1 and 13.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33917,78,478,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The mean diameter of a thin brass rod is to be measured by vernier callipers. Why is a set of 100 measurements of the diameter expected to yield a more reliable estimate than a set of 5 measurements only ?','[{"s": "(c) The mean diameter of a thin brass rod is to be measured by vernier callipers.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Why is a set of 100 measurements of the diameter expected to yield a more reliable estimate than a set of 5 measurements only ?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031067", "2000031791", "2000032122", "2000032746", "2000032818", "2000106049", "2000106062"]',NULL),
(25209,63,68,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer : A given mass of water in vapour state has 1.67×10 3 times the volume of the same mass of water in liquid state (Ex. 13.1). This is also the increase in the amount of volume available for each molecule of water. When volume increases by 103 times the radius increases by V1/3 or 10 times, i.e., 10 × 2 Å = 20 Å. So the average distance is 2 × 20 = 40 Å.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": A given mass of water in vapour state has 1.67×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "times the volume of the same mass of water in liquid state (Ex. 13.1). This is also the increase in the amount of volume available for each molecule of water. When volume increases by 103 times the radius increases by V1/3 or 10 times, i.e., 10 × 2 Å = 20 Å. So the average distance is 2 × 20 = 40 Å.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25210,63,69,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.4 A vessel contains two nonreactive gases : neon (monatomic) and oxygen (diatomic). The ratio of their partial pressures is 3:2. Estimate the ratio of (i) number of molecules and (ii) mass density of neon and oxygen in the vessel. Atomic mass of Ne = 20.2 u, molecular mass of O 2 = 32.0 u.','[{"s": "Example 13.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A vessel contains two nonreactive gases : neon (monatomic) and oxygen (diatomic). The ratio of their partial pressures is 3:2. Estimate the ratio of (i) number of molecules and (ii) mass density of neon and oxygen in the vessel. Atomic mass of Ne = 20.2 u, molecular mass of O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 32.0 u.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25211,63,70,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Partial pressure of a gas in a mixture is the pressure it would have for the same volume and temperature if it alone occupied the vessel. (The total pressure of a mixture of non-reactive gases is the sum of partial pressures due to its constituent gases.) Each gas (assumed ideal) obeys the gas law. Since V and T are common to the two gases, we have P 1 V = μ 1 RT and P 2 V = μ 2 RT, i.e. (P 1 /P 2 ) = (μ 1 / μ 2 ). Here 1 and 2 refer to neon and oxygen respectively. Since (P 1 /P 2 ) = (3/2) (given), (μ 1 / μ 2 ) = 3/2.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Partial pressure of a gas in a mixture is the pressure it would have for the same volume and temperature if it alone occupied the vessel. (The total pressure of a mixture of non-reactive gases is the sum of partial pressures due to its constituent gases.) Each gas (assumed ideal) obeys the gas law. Since V and T are common to the two gases, we have P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V = μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RT and P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V = μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RT, i.e. (P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = (μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Here 1 and 2 refer to neon and oxygen respectively. Since (P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = (3/2) (given), (μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = 3/2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25212,63,71,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) By definition μ 1 = (N 1 /N A ) and μ 2 = (N 2 /N A ) where N 1 and N 2 are the number of molecules of 1 and 2, and N A is the Avogadro’s number. Therefore, (N 1 /N 2 ) = (μ 1 / μ 2 ) = 3/2.','[{"s": "(i) By definition μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") where N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the number of molecules of 1 and 2, and N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the Avogadro’s number. Therefore, (N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = (μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = 3/2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25213,63,72,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) We can also write μ 1 = (m 1 /M 1 ) and μ 2 = (m 2 /M 2 ) where m 1 and m 2 are the masses of 1 and 2; and M 1 and M 2 are their molecular masses. (Both m 1 and M 1 ; as well as m 2 and M 2 should be expressed in the same units). If r1 and r2 are the mass densities of 1 and 2 respectively, we have','[{"s": "(ii) We can also write μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") where m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the masses of 1 and 2; and M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are their molecular masses. (Both m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; as well as m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "should be expressed in the same units). If r1 and r2 are the mass densities of 1 and 2 respectively, we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25214,63,73,'image',NULL,'4',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image47.png'),
(25215,63,74,'heading',4,'13.4 KINETIC THEORY OF AN IDEAL GAS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27850,68,557,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The path length traversed by an object between two points is, in general, not the same as the magnitude of displacement. The displacement depends only on the end points; the path length (as the name implies) depends on the actual path. The two quantities are equal only if the object does not change its direction during the course of motion. In all other cases, the path length is greater than the magnitude of displacement.','[{"s": "1. The path length traversed by an object between two points is, in general, not the same as the magnitude of displacement. The displacement depends only on the end points; the path length (as the name implies) depends on the actual path. The two quantities are equal only if the object does not change its direction during the course of motion. In all other cases, the path length is greater than the magnitude of displacement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34039,78,600,'paragraph',NULL,'2.13 A physical quantity P is related to four observables a, b, c, and d as follows :','[{"s": "2.13", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A physical quantity P is related to four observables a, b, c, and d as follows :", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031071", "2000031815", "2000032126", "2000032740", "2000032822", "2000106063", "2000106115"]',NULL),
(25216,63,75,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic theory of gases is based on the molecular picture of matter. A given amount of gas is a collection of a large number of molecules (typically of the order of Avogadro’s number) that are in incessant random motion. At ordinary pressure and temperature, the average distance between molecules is a factor of 10 or more than the typical size of a molecule (2 Å). Thus, interaction between molecules is negligible and we can assume that they move freely in straight lines according to Newton’s first law. However, occasionally, they come close to each other, experience intermolecular forces and their velocities change. These interactions are called collisions. The molecules collide incessantly against each other or with the walls and change','[{"s": "Kinetic theory of gases is based on the molecular picture of matter. A given amount of gas is a collection of a large number of molecules (typically of the order of Avogadro’s number) that are in incessant random motion. At ordinary pressure and temperature, the average distance between molecules is a factor of 10 or more than the typical size of a molecule (2 Å). Thus, interaction between molecules is negligible and we can assume that they move freely in straight lines according to Newton’s first law. However, occasionally, they come close to each other, experience intermolecular forces and their velocities change. These interactions are called collisions. The molecules collide incessantly against each other or with the walls and change", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25217,63,76,'paragraph',NULL,'their velocities. The collisions are considered to be elastic. We can derive an expression for the pressure of a gas based on the kinetic theory. We begin with the idea that molecules of a gas are in incessant random motion, colliding against one another and with the walls of the container. All collisions between molecules among themselves or between molecules and the','[{"s": "their velocities. The collisions are considered to be elastic. We can derive an expression for the pressure of a gas based on the kinetic theory. We begin with the idea that molecules of a gas are in incessant random motion, colliding against one another and with the walls of the container. All collisions between molecules among themselves or between molecules and the", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25218,63,77,'paragraph',NULL,'walls are elastic. This implies that total kinetic energy is conserved. The total momentum is conserved as usual.','[{"s": "walls are elastic. This implies that total kinetic energy is conserved. The total momentum is conserved as usual.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25219,63,78,'heading',4,'13.4.1 Pressure of an Ideal Gas',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(25220,63,79,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a gas enclosed in a cube of side l. Take the axes to be parallel to the sides of the cube, as shown in Fig. 13.4. A molecule with velocity (v x , v y , v z ) hits the planar wall parallel to yzplane of area A (= l 2 ). Since the collision is elastic, the molecule rebounds with the same velocity; its y and z components of velocity do not change in the collision but the x component reverses sign. That is, the velocity after collision is (-v x , v y , v z ) . The change in momentum of the molecule is: –mv x – (mv x ) = – 2mv x . By the principle of conservation of momentum, the momentum imparted to the wall in the collision = 2mv x .','[{"s": "Consider a gas enclosed in a cube of side l. Take the axes to be parallel to the sides of the cube, as shown in Fig. 13.4. A molecule with velocity (v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") hits the planar wall parallel to yzplane of area A (= l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Since the collision is elastic, the molecule rebounds with the same velocity; its y and z components of velocity do not change in the collision but the x component reverses sign. That is, the velocity after collision is (-v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") . The change in momentum of the molecule is: –mv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– (mv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = – 2mv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". By the principle of conservation of momentum, the momentum imparted to the wall in the collision = 2mv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25221,63,80,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image43.png'),
(25222,63,81,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.4 Elastic collision of a gas molecule with the wall of the container.','[{"s": "Fig. 13.4 Elastic collision of a gas molecule with the wall of the container.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25223,63,82,'paragraph',NULL,'To calculate the force (and pressure) on the wall, we need to calculate momentum imparted to the wall per unit time. In a small time interval Δt , a molecule with x-component of velocity v x will hit the wall if it is within the distance v x Δt from the wall. That is, all molecules within the volume Av x Δt only can hit the wall in time Dt. But, on the average, half of these are moving towards the wall and the other half away from the wall. Thus, the number of molecules with velocity (v x , v y , v z ) hitting the wall in time','[{"s": "To calculate the force (and pressure) on the wall, we need to calculate momentum imparted to the wall per unit time. In a small time interval", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Δt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", a molecule with x-component of velocity v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will hit the wall if it is within the distance v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Δt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from the wall. That is, all molecules within the volume Av", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Δt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "only can hit the wall in time Dt. But, on the average, half of these are moving towards the wall and the other half away from the wall. Thus, the number of molecules with velocity (v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") hitting the wall in time", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25224,63,83,'paragraph',NULL,'Δt is','[{"s": "Δt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25225,63,84,'paragraph',NULL,'½A v x Δt n, where n is the number of molecules per unit volume. The total momentum transferred to the wall by these molecules in time Δt is:','[{"s": "½A v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Δt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n, where n is the number of molecules per unit volume. The total momentum transferred to the wall by these molecules in time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Δt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25226,63,85,'paragraph',NULL,'Q = (2mv x ) (½ n A v x Δt ) (13.10)','[{"s": "Q = (2mv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (½ n A v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Δt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (13.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25227,63,86,'paragraph',NULL,'The force on the wall is the rate of momentum transfer Q/ Δt and pressure is force per unit area :','[{"s": "The force on the wall is the rate of momentum transfer Q/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Δt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and pressure is force per unit area :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25228,63,87,'paragraph',NULL,'P = Q /(A Δt ) = n m v x 2 (3.11)','[{"s": "P = Q /(A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Δt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = n m v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25229,63,88,'paragraph',NULL,'Actually, all molecules in a gas do not have the same velocity; there is a distribution in velocities. The above equation, therefore, stands for pressure due to the group of molecules with speed vx in the x-direction and n stands for the number density of that group of molecules. The total pressure is obtained by summing over the contribution due to all groups:','[{"s": "Actually, all molecules in a gas do not have the same velocity; there is a distribution in velocities. The above equation, therefore, stands for pressure due to the group of molecules with speed vx in the x-direction and n stands for the number density of that group of molecules. The total pressure is obtained by summing over the contribution due to all groups:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25230,63,89,'paragraph',NULL,'P = n m v x 2 (13.12)','[{"s": "P = n m v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(13.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25231,63,90,'paragraph',NULL,'where v 2 x is the average of v x 2 . Now the gas is isotropic, i.e. there is no preferred direction of velocity of the molecules in the vessel.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the average of v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Now the gas is isotropic, i.e. there is no preferred direction of velocity of the molecules in the vessel.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25232,63,91,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, by symmetry,','[{"s": "Therefore, by symmetry,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25233,63,92,'image',NULL,'5',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image45.png'),
(25235,63,94,'paragraph',NULL,'P = (1/3) n m v 2 (13.14)','[{"s": "P = (1/3) n m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(13.14)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25236,63,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Some remarks on this derivation. First, though we choose the container to be a cube, the shape of the vessel really is immaterial. For a vessel of arbitrary shape, we can always choose a small infinitesimal (planar) area and carry through the steps above. Notice that both A and Δt do not appear in the final result. By Pascal’s law, given in Ch. 10, pressure in one portion of the gas in equilibrium is the same as anywhere else. Second, we have ignored any collisions inthe derivation. Though this assumption is difficult to justify rigorously, we can qualitatively see that it will not lead to erroneous results.','[{"s": "Some remarks on this derivation. First, though we choose the container to be a cube, the shape of the vessel really is immaterial. For a vessel of arbitrary shape, we can always choose a small infinitesimal (planar) area and carry through the steps above. Notice that both A and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Δt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "do not appear in the final result. By Pascal’s law, given in Ch. 10, pressure in one portion of the gas in equilibrium is the same as anywhere else. Second, we have ignored any collisions inthe derivation. Though this assumption is difficult to justify rigorously, we can qualitatively see that it will not lead to erroneous results.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25237,63,96,'paragraph',NULL,'The number of molecules hitting the wall in time Dt was found to be ½ n Av x Δt . Now the collisions are random and the gas is in a steady state. Thus, if a molecule with velocity (v x , v y , v z ) acquires a different velocity due to collision with some molecule, there will always be some other molecule with a different initial velocity which after a collision acquires the velocity (v x , v y , v z ).','[{"s": "The number of molecules hitting the wall in time Dt was found to be ½ n Av", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Δt", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Now the collisions are random and the gas is in a steady state. Thus, if a molecule with velocity (v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") acquires a different velocity due to collision with some molecule, there will always be some other molecule with a different initial velocity which after a collision acquires the velocity (v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25238,63,97,'paragraph',NULL,'If this were not so, the distribution of velocities would not remain steady. In any case we are finding v 2x . Thus, on the whole, molecular collisions (if they are not too frequent and the time spent in a collision is negligible compared to time between collisions) will not affect the calculation above.','[{"s": "If this were not so, the distribution of velocities would not remain steady. In any case we are finding", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, on the whole, molecular collisions (if they are not too frequent and the time spent in a collision is negligible compared to time between collisions) will not affect the calculation above.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25239,63,98,'heading',4,'13.4.2 Kinetic Interpretation of Temperature',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25240,63,99,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (13.14) can be written as','[{"s": "Equation (13.14) can be written as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25241,63,100,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = 1/3 nVmv 2','[{"s": "PV =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nVmv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25242,63,101,'paragraph',NULL,'PV= 2/3 N× 1/2 m¯¯¯v 2 ......(13.15b)','[{"s": "PV=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2/3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m¯¯¯v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "......(13.15b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25243,63,102,'paragraph',NULL,'where N (= nV) is the number of molecules in the sample. The quantity in the bracket is the average translational kinetic energy of the molecules in the gas. Since the internal energy E of an ideal gas is purely kinetic*,','[{"s": "where N (= nV) is the number of molecules in the sample. The quantity in the bracket is the average translational kinetic energy of the molecules in the gas. Since the internal energy E of an ideal gas is purely kinetic*,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25244,63,103,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = N× 1/2 m¯¯¯v 2 ......(13.16)','[{"s": "PV = N×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m¯¯¯v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "......(13.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25245,63,104,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (13.15) then gives :','[{"s": "Equation (13.15) then gives :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25246,63,105,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = (2/3) E ...........(13.17)','[{"s": "PV = (2/3) E ...........(13.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25247,63,106,'paragraph',NULL,'We are now ready for a kinetic interpretation of temperature. Combining Eq. (13.17) with the ideal gas Eq. (13.3), we get','[{"s": "We are now ready for a kinetic interpretation of temperature. Combining Eq. (13.17) with the ideal gas Eq. (13.3), we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25248,63,107,'paragraph',NULL,'E= 3/2 k B NT......(13.18)','[{"s": "E=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NT......(13.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25249,63,108,'paragraph',NULL,'or E/N =1/ 2 m¯¯¯v 2 ......(13.19)','[{"s": "or E/N =1/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m¯¯¯v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "......(13.19)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25251,63,110,'paragraph',NULL,'energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature, not on pressure or volume. With this interpretation of temperature, kinetic theory of an ideal gas is completely consistent with the ideal gas equation and the various gas laws based on it.','[{"s": "energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature, not on pressure or volume. With this interpretation of temperature, kinetic theory of an ideal gas is completely consistent with the ideal gas equation and the various gas laws based on it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25252,63,111,'paragraph',NULL,'For a mixture of non-reactive ideal gases, the total pressure gets contribution from each gas in the mixture. Equation (13.14) becomes','[{"s": "For a mixture of non-reactive ideal gases, the total pressure gets contribution from each gas in the mixture. Equation (13.14) becomes", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25253,63,112,'paragraph',NULL,'P= 1/3 (n 1 m 1 ¯¯¯¯¯v 12 +n 2 m 2 ¯¯¯¯¯v 22 +.....) …..(13.20)','[{"s": "P=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "¯¯¯¯¯v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "¯¯¯¯¯v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "22", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+.....) …..(13.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25254,63,113,'paragraph',NULL,'In equilibrium, the average kinetic energy of the molecules of different gases will be equal. That is,','[{"s": "In equilibrium, the average kinetic energy of the molecules of different gases will be equal. That is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25255,63,114,'paragraph',NULL,'1/2 m 1 ¯¯¯¯¯v 1/2 = 1/2 m 2 ¯¯¯¯¯v 2 2 = 3k B T/2','[{"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "¯¯¯¯¯v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "¯¯¯¯¯v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T/2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25256,63,115,'paragraph',NULL,'P = (n 1 + n 2 +… ) k B T .............(13.21)','[{"s": "P = (n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+… ) k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T .............(13.21)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25257,63,116,'paragraph',NULL,'which is Dalton’s law of partial pressures. From Eq. (13.19), we can get an idea of the typical speed of molecules in a gas. At a temperature T = 300 K, the mean square speed of a molecule in nitrogen gas is :','[{"s": "which is Dalton’s law of partial pressures. From Eq. (13.19), we can get an idea of the typical speed of molecules in a gas. At a temperature T = 300 K, the mean square speed of a molecule in nitrogen gas is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25258,63,117,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eq. (13.19), we can get an idea of the typical speed of molecules in a gas. At a temperature T = 300 K, the mean square speed of a molecule in nitrogen gas is :','[{"s": "From Eq. (13.19), we can get an idea of the typical speed of molecules in a gas. At a temperature T = 300 K, the mean square speed of a molecule in nitrogen gas is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25259,63,118,'paragraph',NULL,'m= M N2/ N A = 28/6.02×10 26 =4.56×10 −26 kg.','[{"s": "m=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N2/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "28/6.02×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "26", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=4.56×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−26", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25260,63,119,'paragraph',NULL,'v 2 ¯ = 3 k B T/m = (516 ) 2 m 2 s -2','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "¯", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3 k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T/m = (516", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27851,68,558,'paragraph',NULL,'2. In view of point 1 above, the average speed of an object is greater than or equal to the magnitude of the average velocity over a given time interval. The two are equal only if the path length is equal to the magnitude of displacement.','[{"s": "2. In view of point 1 above, the average speed of an object is greater than or equal to the magnitude of the average velocity over a given time interval. The two are equal only if the path length is equal to the magnitude of displacement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25261,63,120,'paragraph',NULL,'The square root of v 2 ¯ is known as root mean square (rms) speed and is denoted by vrms,( We can also write v 2 ¯ as < v 2 >.)','[{"s": "The square root of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "¯", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is known as root mean square (rms) speed and is denoted by vrms,( We can also write", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "¯", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as < v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25262,63,121,'paragraph',NULL,'v rms = 516 m s -1','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 516 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25265,63,124,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The important point to remember is that the average kinetic energy (per molecule) of any (ideal) gas (be it monatomic like argon, diatomic like chlorine or polyatomic) is always equal to (3/2) kBT. It depends only on temperature, and is independent of the nature of the gas.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The important point to remember is that the average kinetic energy (per molecule) of any (ideal) gas (be it monatomic like argon, diatomic like chlorine or polyatomic) is always equal to (3/2) kBT. It depends only on temperature, and is independent of the nature of the gas.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25266,63,125,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Since argon and chlorine both have the same temperature in the flask, the ratio of average kinetic energy (per molecule) of the two gases is 1:1.','[{"s": "(i) Since argon and chlorine both have the same temperature in the flask, the ratio of average kinetic energy (per molecule) of the two gases is 1:1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25267,63,126,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Now ½ m v 2rms = average kinetic energy per molecule = (3/2)k B T where m is the mass of a molecule of the gas. Therefore,','[{"s": "(ii) Now ½ m v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2rms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= average kinetic energy per molecule = (3/2)k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T where m is the mass of a molecule of the gas. Therefore,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25268,63,127,'paragraph',NULL,'(v 2rms ) Ar/ (v 2rms ) Cl = (m) Cl/ (m) Ar = (M) Cl/ (M) Ar = 70.9/39.9 =1.77','[{"s": "(v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2rms", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ar/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2rms", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(m)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(m)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ar", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(M)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(M)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ar", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "70.9/39.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=1.77", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25269,63,128,'paragraph',NULL,'where M denotes the molecular mass of the gas. (For argon, a molecule is just an atom of argon.)Taking square root of both sides, (v rms ) Ar/ (v rms ) Cl = 1.33','[{"s": "where M denotes the molecular mass of the gas. (For argon, a molecule is just an atom of argon.)Taking square root of both sides,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rms", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ar/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rms", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.33", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25270,63,129,'paragraph',NULL,'You should note that the composition of the mixture by mass is quite irrelevant to the above calculation. Any other proportion by mass of argon and chlorine would give the same answers to (i) and (ii), provided the temperature remains unaltered.','[{"s": "You should note that the composition of the mixture by mass is quite irrelevant to the above calculation. Any other proportion by mass of argon and chlorine would give the same answers to (i) and (ii), provided the temperature remains unaltered.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25271,63,130,'heading',4,'Founders of Kinetic Theory of Gases',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25272,63,131,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image48.png'),
(25273,63,132,'paragraph',NULL,'James Clerk Maxwell (1831 – 1879), born in Edinburgh, Scotland, was among the greatest physicists of the nineteenth century. He derived the thermal velocity distribution of molecules in a gas and was among the first to obtain reliable estimates of molecular parameters from measurable quantities like viscosity, etc. Maxwell’s greatest achievement was the unification of the laws of electricity and magnetism (discovered by Coulomb, Oersted, Ampere and Faraday) into a consistent set of equations now called Maxwell’s equations. From these he arrived at the most important conclusion that light is an electromagnetic wave. Interestingly, Maxwell did not agree with the idea (strongly suggested by the Faraday’s laws of electrolysis) that electricity was particulate in nature.','[{"s": "James Clerk Maxwell (1831 – 1879),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "born in Edinburgh, Scotland, was among the greatest physicists of the nineteenth century. He derived the thermal velocity distribution of molecules in a gas and was among the first to obtain reliable estimates of molecular parameters from measurable quantities like viscosity, etc. Maxwell’s greatest achievement was the unification of the laws of electricity and magnetism (discovered by Coulomb, Oersted, Ampere and Faraday) into a consistent set of equations now called Maxwell’s equations. From these he arrived at the most important conclusion that light is an electromagnetic wave. Interestingly, Maxwell did not agree with the idea (strongly suggested by the Faraday’s laws of electrolysis) that electricity was particulate in nature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25274,63,133,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image50.png'),
(25275,63,134,'paragraph',NULL,'Ludwig Boltzmann (1844 – 1906 ) born in Vienna, Austria, worked on the kinetic theory of gases independently of Maxwell. A firm advocate of atomism, that is basic to kinetic theory, Boltzmann provided a statistical interpretation of the Second Law of thermodynamics and the concept of entropy. He is regarded as one of the founders of classical statistical mechanics. The proportionality constant connecting energy and temperature in kinetic theory is known as Boltzmann’s constant in his honour.','[{"s": "Ludwig Boltzmann (1844 – 1906", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") born in Vienna, Austria, worked on the kinetic theory of gases independently of Maxwell. A firm advocate of atomism, that is basic to kinetic theory, Boltzmann provided a statistical interpretation of the Second Law of thermodynamics and the concept of entropy. He is regarded as one of the founders of classical statistical mechanics. The proportionality constant connecting energy and temperature in kinetic theory is known as Boltzmann’s constant in his honour.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25276,63,135,'heading',4,'Maxwell Distribution Function',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25277,63,136,'paragraph',NULL,'In a given mass of gas, the velocities of all molecules are not the same, even when bulk parameters like pressure, volume and temperature are fixed. Collisions change the direction and the speed of molecules. However in a state of equilibrium, the distribution of speeds is constant or fixed.','[{"s": "In a given mass of gas, the velocities of all molecules are not the same, even when bulk parameters like pressure, volume and temperature are fixed. Collisions change the direction and the speed of molecules. However in a state of equilibrium, the distribution of speeds is constant or fixed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25278,63,137,'image',NULL,'6',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image51.png'),
(25279,63,138,'paragraph',NULL,'Distributions are very important and useful when dealing with systems containing large number of objects. As an example consider the ages of different persons in a city. It is not feasible to deal with the age of each individual. We can divide the people into groups: children up to age 20 years, adults between ages of 20 and 60, old people above 60. If we want more detailed information we can choose smaller intervals, 0-1, 1-2,..., 99-100 of age groups. When the size of the interval becomes smaller, say half year, the number of persons in the interval will also reduce, roughly half the original number in the one year interval. The number of persons dN(x) in the age interval x and x+dx is proportional to dx or dN(x) = n x dx.','[{"s": "Distributions are very important and useful when dealing with systems containing large number of objects. As an example consider the ages of different persons in a city. It is not feasible to deal with the age of each individual. We can divide the people into groups: children up to age 20 years, adults between ages of 20 and 60, old people above 60. If we want more detailed information we can choose smaller intervals, 0-1, 1-2,..., 99-100 of age groups. When the size of the interval becomes smaller, say half year, the number of persons in the interval will also reduce, roughly half the original number in the one year interval. The number of persons dN(x) in the age interval x and x+dx is proportional to dx or dN(x) = n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dx.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25280,63,139,'paragraph',NULL,'We have used n x to denote the number of persons at the value of x. In a similar way the molecular speed distribution gives the number of molecules between the speeds v and v+ dv.','[{"s": "We have used n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to denote the number of persons at the value of x. In a similar way the molecular speed distribution gives the number of molecules between the speeds v and v+ dv.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25281,63,140,'paragraph',NULL,'dN(v) = 4pNa 3 e –bv 2 v 2 dv = n v dv.','[{"s": "dN(v) = 4pNa", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–bv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dv = n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dv.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25282,63,141,'paragraph',NULL,'This is called Maxwell distribution. The plot of nv against v is shown in the figure. The fraction of the molecules with speeds v and v+dv is equal to the area of the strip shown. The average of any quantity like v 2 is defined by the integral <v 2 >=(1/N)∫v 2 dN(v)=√ 3k B/ Tm','[{"s": "This is called Maxwell distribution. The plot of nv against v is shown in the figure. The fraction of the molecules with speeds v and v+dv is equal to the area of the strip shown. The average of any quantity like v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is defined by the integral", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">=(1/N)∫v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dN(v)=√", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Tm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25283,63,142,'paragraph',NULL,'which agrees with the result derived from more elementary considerations.','[{"s": "which agrees with the result derived from more elementary considerations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25284,63,143,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.6 Uranium has two isotopes of masses 235 and 238 units. If both are present in Uranium hexafluoride gas which would have the larger average speed ? If atomic mass of fluorine is 19 units, estimate the percentage difference in speeds at any temperature.','[{"s": "Example 13.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Uranium has two isotopes of masses 235 and 238 units. If both are present in Uranium hexafluoride gas which would have the larger average speed ? If atomic mass of fluorine is 19 units, estimate the percentage difference in speeds at any temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25285,63,144,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer At a fixed temperature the average energy = ½ m <v 2 > is constant. So smaller the mass of the molecule, faster will be the speed. The ratio of speeds is inversely proportional to the square root of the ratio of the masses. The masses are 349 and 352 units. So','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "At a fixed temperature the average energy = ½ m <v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> is constant. So smaller the mass of the molecule, faster will be the speed. The ratio of speeds is inversely proportional to the square root of the ratio of the masses. The masses are 349 and 352 units. So", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25286,63,145,'paragraph',NULL,'v 349 / v 352 = ( 352/ 349) 1/2 = 1.0044 .','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "349", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "352", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ( 352/ 349)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.0044 .", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25287,63,146,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence difference Kinetic_Theory-image52.png = 0.44 %.','[{"s": "Hence difference", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image52.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 0.44 %.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25288,63,147,'paragraph',NULL,'[ 235 U is the isotope needed for nuclear fission. To separate it from the more abundant isotope 238 U, the mixture is surrounded by a porous cylinder. The porous cylinder must be thick and narrow, so that the molecule wanders through individually, colliding with the walls of the long pore. The faster molecule will leak out more than the slower one and so there is more of the lighter molecule (enrichment) outside the porous cylinder (Fig. 13.5). The method is not very efficient and has to be repeated several times for sufficient enrichment.].','[{"s": "[", "t": "text"}, {"s": "235", "t": "text"}, {"s": "U is the isotope needed for nuclear fission. To separate it from the more abundant isotope", "t": "text"}, {"s": "238", "t": "text"}, {"s": "U, the mixture is surrounded by a porous cylinder. The porous cylinder must be thick and narrow, so that the molecule wanders through individually, colliding with the walls of the long pore. The faster molecule will leak out more than the slower one and so there is more of the lighter molecule (enrichment) outside the porous cylinder (Fig. 13.5). The method is not very efficient and has to be repeated several times for sufficient enrichment.].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25289,63,148,'paragraph',NULL,'When gases diffuse, their rate of diffusion is inversely proportional to square root of the masses (see Exercise 13.12 ). Can you guess the explanation from the above answer?','[{"s": "When gases diffuse, their rate of diffusion is inversely proportional to square root of the masses (see Exercise 13.12 ). Can you guess the explanation from the above answer?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25290,63,149,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image53.png'),
(25291,63,150,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.5 Molecules going through a porous wall.','[{"s": "Fig. 13.5 Molecules going through a porous wall.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25292,63,151,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.7 (a) When a molecule (or an elastic ball) hits a ( massive) wall, it rebounds with the same speed. When a ball hits a massive bat held firmly, the same thing happens. However, when the bat is moving towards the ball, the ball rebounds with a different speed. Does the ball move faster or slower? (Ch.6 will refresh your memory on elastic collisions.)','[{"s": "Example 13.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) When a molecule (or an elastic ball) hits a ( massive) wall, it rebounds with the same speed. When a ball hits a massive bat held firmly, the same thing happens. However, when the bat is moving towards the ball, the ball rebounds with a different speed. Does the ball move faster or slower? (Ch.6 will refresh your memory on elastic collisions.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25293,63,152,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) When gas in a cylinder is compressed by pushing in a piston, its temperature rises. Guess at an explanation of this in terms of kinetic theory using (a) above.','[{"s": "(b) When gas in a cylinder is compressed by pushing in a piston, its temperature rises. Guess at an explanation of this in terms of kinetic theory using (a) above.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25294,63,153,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) What happens when a compressed gas pushes a piston out and expands. What would you observe ?','[{"s": "(c) What happens when a compressed gas pushes a piston out and expands. What would you observe ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25295,63,154,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Sachin Tendulkar used a heavy cricket bat while playing. Did it help him in anyway ?','[{"s": "(d) Sachin Tendulkar used a heavy cricket bat while playing. Did it help him in anyway ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25296,63,155,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer (a) Let the speed of the ball be u relative to the wicket behind the bat. If the bat is moving towards the ball with a speed V relative to the wicket, then the relative speed of the ball to bat is V + u towards the bat. When the ball rebounds (after hitting the massive bat) its speed, relative to bat, is V + u moving away from the bat. So relative to the wicket the speed of the rebounding ball is V + (V + u) = 2V + u, moving away from the wicket. So the ball speeds up after the collision with the bat. The rebound speed will be less than u if the bat is not massive. For a molecule this would imply an increase in temperature.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Let the speed of the ball be u relative to the wicket behind the bat. If the bat is moving towards the ball with a speed V relative to the wicket, then the relative speed of the ball to bat is V + u towards the bat. When the ball rebounds (after hitting the massive bat) its speed, relative to bat, is V + u moving away from the bat. So relative to the wicket the speed of the rebounding ball is V + (V + u) = 2V + u, moving away from the wicket. So the ball speeds up after the collision with the bat. The rebound speed will be less than u if the bat is not massive. For a molecule this would imply an increase in temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25297,63,156,'paragraph',NULL,'You should be able to answer (b) (c) and (d) based on the answer to (a).','[{"s": "You should be able to answer (b) (c) and (d) based on the answer to (a).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25298,63,157,'paragraph',NULL,'(Hint: Note the correspondence, piston -> bat, cylinder -> wicket, molecule -> ball.)','[{"s": "(Hint: Note the correspondence, piston -> bat, cylinder -> wicket, molecule -> ball.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25299,63,158,'heading',4,'13.5 LAW OF EQUIPARTITION OF ENERGY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25300,63,159,'paragraph',NULL,'The kinetic energy of a single molecule is','[{"s": "The kinetic energy of a single molecule is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25301,63,160,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic_Theory-image54.png (13.22)','[{"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image54.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.22)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25302,63,161,'paragraph',NULL,'For a gas in thermal equilibrium at temperature T the average value of energy denoted by < Kinetic_Theory-image55.png > is','[{"s": "For a gas in thermal equilibrium at temperature T the average value of energy denoted by <", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image55.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "> is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25303,63,162,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic_Theory-image56.png (13.23)','[{"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image56.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.23)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25304,63,163,'paragraph',NULL,'Since there is no preferred direction, Eq. (13.23) implies','[{"s": "Since there is no preferred direction, Eq. (13.23) implies", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25305,63,164,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic_Theory-image21.png , Kinetic_Theory-image22.png ,','[{"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image21.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image22.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25306,63,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic_Theory-image23.png (13.24)','[{"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image23.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.24)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25307,63,166,'paragraph',NULL,'A molecule free to move in space needs three coordinates to specify its location. If it is constrained to move in a plane it needs two; and if constrained to move along a line, it needs just one coordinate to locate it. This can also be expressed in another way. We say that it has one degree of freedom for motion in a line, two for motion in a plane and three for motion in space. Motion of a body as a whole from one point to another is called translation. Thus, a molecule free to move in space has three translational degrees of freedom. Each translational degree of freedom contributes a term that contains square of some variable of motion, e.g., ½ mvx2 and similar terms in v y and v z','[{"s": "A molecule free to move in space needs three coordinates to specify its location. If it is constrained to move in a plane it needs two; and if constrained to move along a line, it needs just one coordinate to locate it. This can also be expressed in another way. We say that it has one degree of freedom for motion in a line, two for motion in a plane and three for motion in space. Motion of a body as a whole from one point to another is called translation. Thus, a molecule free to move in space has three translational degrees of freedom. Each translational degree of freedom contributes a term that contains square of some variable of motion, e.g., ½ mvx2 and similar terms in v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25308,63,167,'paragraph',NULL,'Molecules of a monatomic gas like argon have only translational degrees of freedom. But what about a diatomic gas such as O2 or N2? A molecule of O2 has three translational degrees of freedom. But in addition it can also rotate about its centre of mass. Figure 13.6 shows the two independent axes of rotation 1 and 2, normal to the axis joining the two oxygen atoms about which the molecule can rotate*. The molecule thus has two rotational degrees of freedom, each of which contributes a term to the total energy consisting of translational energy Kinetic_Theory-image24.png and rotational energy Kinetic_Theory-image25.png r.','[{"s": "Molecules of a monatomic gas like argon have only translational degrees of freedom. But what about a diatomic gas such as O2 or N2? A molecule of O2 has three translational degrees of freedom. But in addition it can also rotate about its centre of mass. Figure 13.6 shows the two independent axes of rotation 1 and 2, normal to the axis joining the two oxygen atoms about which the molecule can rotate*. The molecule thus has two rotational degrees of freedom, each of which contributes a term to the total energy consisting of translational energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image24.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and rotational energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image25.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "r.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25309,63,168,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic_Theory-image26.png (13.25)','[{"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image26.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.25)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25310,63,169,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image27.png'),
(25311,63,170,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.6 The two independent axes of rotation of a diatomic molecule','[{"s": "Fig. 13.6 The two independent axes of rotation of a diatomic molecule", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25312,63,171,'paragraph',NULL,'where ω1 and ω2 are the angular speeds about the axes 1 and 2 and I1, I2 are the corresponding moments of inertia. Note that each rotational degree of freedom contributes a term to the energy that contains square of a rotational variable of motion.','[{"s": "where ω1 and ω2 are the angular speeds about the axes 1 and 2 and I1, I2 are the corresponding moments of inertia. Note that each rotational degree of freedom contributes a term to the energy that contains square of a rotational variable of motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25313,63,172,'paragraph',NULL,'We have assumed above that the O2 molecule is a ‘rigid rotator’, i.e., the molecule does not vibrate. This assumption, though found to be true (at moderate temperatures) for O2, is not always valid. Molecules, like CO, even at moderate temperatures have a mode of vibration, i.e., its atoms oscillate along the interatomic axis like a one-dimensional oscillator, and contribute a vibrational energy term εv to the total energy:','[{"s": "We have assumed above that the O2 molecule is a ‘rigid rotator’, i.e., the molecule does not vibrate. This assumption, though found to be true (at moderate temperatures) for O2, is not always valid. Molecules, like CO, even at moderate temperatures have a mode of vibration, i.e., its atoms oscillate along the interatomic axis like a one-dimensional oscillator, and contribute a vibrational energy term εv to the total energy:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25314,63,173,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image28.png'),
(25315,63,174,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic_Theory-image29.png (13.26)','[{"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image29.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.26)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25316,63,175,'paragraph',NULL,'where k is the force constant of the oscillator and y the vibrational co-ordinate.','[{"s": "where k is the force constant of the oscillator and y the vibrational co-ordinate.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25317,63,176,'paragraph',NULL,'Once again the vibrational energy terms in Eq. (13.26) contain squared terms of vibrational variables of motion y and dy/dt .','[{"s": "Once again the vibrational energy terms in Eq. (13.26) contain squared terms of vibrational variables of motion y and dy/dt .", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25318,63,177,'paragraph',NULL,'At this point, notice an important feature in Eq.(13.26). While each translational and rotational degree of freedom has contributed only one ‘squared term’ in Eq.(13.26), one vibrational mode contributes two ‘squared terms’ : kinetic and potential energies.','[{"s": "At this point, notice an important feature in Eq.(13.26). While each translational and rotational degree of freedom has contributed only one ‘squared term’ in Eq.(13.26), one vibrational mode contributes two ‘squared terms’ : kinetic and potential energies.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27852,68,559,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The vector equations (4.33a) and (4.34a) do not involve any choice of axes. Of course, you can always resolve them along any two independent axes.','[{"s": "3. The vector equations (4.33a) and (4.34a) do not involve any choice of axes. Of course, you can always resolve them along any two independent axes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27853,68,560,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The kinematic equations for uniform acceleration do not apply to the case of uniform circular motion since in this case the magnitude of acceleration is constant but its direction is changing.','[{"s": "4. The kinematic equations for uniform acceleration do not apply to the case of uniform circular motion since in this case the magnitude of acceleration is constant but its direction is changing.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25319,63,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Each quadratic term occurring in the expression for energy is a mode of absorption of energy by the molecule. We have seen that in thermal equilibrium at absolute temperature T, for each translational mode of motion, the average energy is ½ kBT. The most elegant principle of classical statistical mechanics (first proved by Maxwell) states that this is so for each mode of energy: translational, rotational and vibrational. That is, in equilibrium, the total energy is equally distributed in all possible energy modes, with each mode having an average energy equal to ½ kBT. This is known as the law of equipartition of energy. Accordingly, each translational and rotational degree of freedom of a molecule contributes ½ kBT to the energy, while each vibrational frequency contributes 2 × ½ kBT = kBT , since a vibrational mode has both kinetic and potential energy modes.','[{"s": "Each quadratic term occurring in the expression for energy is a mode of absorption of energy by the molecule. We have seen that in thermal equilibrium at absolute temperature T, for each translational mode of motion, the average energy is ½ kBT. The most elegant principle of classical statistical mechanics (first proved by Maxwell) states that this is so for each mode of energy: translational, rotational and vibrational. That is, in equilibrium, the total energy is equally distributed in all possible energy modes, with each mode having an average energy equal to ½ kBT. This is known as the law of equipartition of energy. Accordingly, each translational and rotational degree of freedom of a molecule contributes ½ kBT to the energy, while each vibrational frequency contributes 2 × ½ kBT = kBT , since a vibrational mode has both kinetic and potential energy modes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25320,63,179,'paragraph',NULL,'The proof of the law of equipartition of energy is beyond the scope of this book. Here, we shall apply the law to predict the specific heats of gases theoretically. Later, we shall also discuss briefly, the application to specific heat of solids.','[{"s": "The proof of the law of equipartition of energy is beyond the scope of this book. Here, we shall apply the law to predict the specific heats of gases theoretically. Later, we shall also discuss briefly, the application to specific heat of solids.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
