(25325,63,184,'paragraph',NULL,'The molar specific heat at constant volume, Cv, is','[{"s": "The molar specific heat at constant volume, Cv, is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25326,63,185,'paragraph',NULL,'* Rotation along the line joining the atoms has very small moment of inertia and does not come into play for quantum mechanical reasons. See end of section 13.6.','[{"s": "*", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Rotation along the line joining the atoms has very small moment of inertia and does not come into play for quantum mechanical reasons. See end of section 13.6.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25327,63,186,'paragraph',NULL,'Cv (monatomic gas) = dU/dT = 3/2 RT (13.28)','[{"s": "Cv (monatomic gas) =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dU/dT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RT (13.28)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25328,63,187,'paragraph',NULL,'For an ideal gas,','[{"s": "For an ideal gas,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25329,63,188,'paragraph',NULL,'Cp – Cv = R (13.29)','[{"s": "Cp – Cv = R (13.29)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25330,63,189,'paragraph',NULL,'where Cp is the molar specific heat at constant pressure. Thus,','[{"s": "where Cp is the molar specific heat at constant pressure. Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25331,63,190,'paragraph',NULL,'Cp = 5/2 R (13.30)','[{"s": "Cp = 5/2 R (13.30)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25332,63,191,'paragraph',NULL,'The ratio of specific heats','[{"s": "The ratio of specific heats", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25333,63,192,'paragraph',NULL,'γ= C p/ C v = 5/3 (13.31)','[{"s": "γ=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5/3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(13.31)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25334,63,193,'heading',4,'13.6.2 Diatomic Gases',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25336,63,195,'paragraph',NULL,'U=5/2k B T×N A =5/2RT (13.32)','[{"s": "U=5/2k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T×N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=5/2RT (13.32)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25337,63,196,'paragraph',NULL,'The molar specific heats are then given by','[{"s": "The molar specific heats are then given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25338,63,197,'paragraph',NULL,'Cv (rigid diatomic) = 5/2R, Cp = 7/2R (13.33)','[{"s": "Cv (rigid diatomic) = 5/2R, Cp = 7/2R (13.33)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25339,63,198,'paragraph',NULL,'γ (rigid diatomic) = 7/5 (13.34)','[{"s": "γ (rigid diatomic) = 7/5 (13.34)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25340,63,199,'paragraph',NULL,'If the diatomic molecule is not rigid but has in addition a vibrational mode','[{"s": "If the diatomic molecule is not rigid but has in addition a vibrational mode", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25341,63,200,'paragraph',NULL,'U=(5/2k B T+k B T)N A =7/2RT','[{"s": "U=(5/2k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T+k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T)N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=7/2RT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25342,63,201,'paragraph',NULL,'Cv=7/2R, Cp=9/2R,γ=9/7R (13.35)','[{"s": "Cv=7/2R, Cp=9/2R,γ=9/7R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(13.35)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25343,63,202,'heading',4,'13.6.3 Polyatomic Gases',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25345,63,204,'paragraph',NULL,'U = (3/2) k B T + (3/2) k B T + f k B TN A','[{"s": "U = (3/2) k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T + (3/2) k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T + f k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "TN", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25346,63,205,'paragraph',NULL,'i.e., C v = (3 + f ) R, C p = (4 + f ) R,','[{"s": "i.e., C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (3 + f ) R, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (4 + f ) R,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25347,63,206,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic_Theory-image30.png (13.36)','[{"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image30.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.36)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25348,63,207,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that C p – C v = R is true for any ideal gas, whether mono, di or polyatomic.','[{"s": "Note that C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= R is true for any ideal gas, whether mono, di or polyatomic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25349,63,208,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 13.1 summarises the theoretical predictions for specific heats of gases ignoring any vibrational modes of motion. The values are in good agreement with experimental values of specific heats of several gases given in Table 13.2. Of course, there are discrepancies between predicted and actual values of specific heats of several other gases (not shown in the table), such as Cl 2 , C 2 H 6 and many other polyatomic gases. Usually, the experimental values for specific heats of these gases are greater than the predicted values as given in Table13.1 suggesting that the agreement can be improved by including vibrational modes of motion in the calculation. The law of equipartition of energy is, thus, well verified experimentally at ordinary temperatures.','[{"s": "Table 13.1 summarises the theoretical predictions for specific heats of gases ignoring any vibrational modes of motion. The values are in good agreement with experimental values of specific heats of several gases given in Table 13.2. Of course, there are discrepancies between predicted and actual values of specific heats of several other gases (not shown in the table), such as Cl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and many other polyatomic gases. Usually, the experimental values for specific heats of these gases are greater than the predicted values as given in Table13.1 suggesting that the agreement can be improved by including vibrational modes of motion in the calculation. The law of equipartition of energy is, thus, well verified experimentally at ordinary temperatures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25350,63,209,'image',NULL,'13.1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image11.png'),
(25351,63,210,'image',NULL,'13.2',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image12.png'),
(25352,63,211,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.8 A cylinder of fixed capacity 44.8 litres contains helium gas at standard temperature and pressure. What is the amount of heat needed to raise the temperature of the gas in the cylinder by 15.0 °C ? (R = 8.31 J mol –1 K –1 ).','[{"s": "Example 13.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A cylinder of fixed capacity 44.8 litres contains helium gas at standard temperature and pressure. What is the amount of heat needed to raise the temperature of the gas in the cylinder by 15.0 °C ? (R = 8.31 J mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25353,63,212,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Using the gas law PV = µRT, you can easily show that 1 mol of any (ideal) gas at standard temperature (273 K) and pressure (1 atm = 1.01 × 10 5 Pa) occupies a volume of 22.4 litres. This universal volume is called molar volume. Thus the cylinder in this example contains 2 mol of helium. Further, since helium is monatomic, its predicted (and observed) molar specific heat at constant volume, C v = (3/2) R, and molar specific heat at constant pressure, C p = (3/2) R + R = (5/2) R. Since the volume of the cylinder is fixed, the heat required is determined by C v . Therefore,','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Using the gas law PV = µRT, you can easily show that 1 mol of any (ideal) gas at standard temperature (273 K) and pressure (1 atm = 1.01 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Pa) occupies a volume of 22.4 litres. This universal volume is called molar volume. Thus the cylinder in this example contains 2 mol of helium. Further, since helium is monatomic, its predicted (and observed) molar specific heat at constant volume, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (3/2) R, and molar specific heat at constant pressure, C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (3/2) R + R = (5/2) R. Since the volume of the cylinder is fixed, the heat required is determined by C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Therefore,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25354,63,213,'paragraph',NULL,'Heat required = no. of moles × molar specific heat × rise in temperature','[{"s": "Heat required = no. of moles × molar specific heat × rise in temperature", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25355,63,214,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2 × 1.5 R × 15.0 = 45 R','[{"s": "= 2 × 1.5 R × 15.0 = 45 R", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25356,63,215,'paragraph',NULL,'= 45 × 8.31 = 374 J','[{"s": "= 45 × 8.31 = 374 J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25357,63,216,'heading',4,'13.6.4 Specific Heat Capacity of Solids',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22837,58,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.1 Monochromatic light of frequency 6.0 ×10 14 Hz is produced by a laser. The power emitted is 2.0 ×10 –3 W. (a) What is the energy of a photon in the light beam? (b) How many photons per second, on an average, are emitted by the source?','[{"s": "Example 11.1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Monochromatic light of frequency 6.0 ×10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "14", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Hz is produced by a laser.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The power emitted is 2.0 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "W. (a) What is the energy of a photon in the light beam? (b) How many photons per second, on an average, are emitted by the source?", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(25358,63,217,'paragraph',NULL,'We can use the law of equipartition of energy to determine specific heats of solids. Consider a solid of N atoms, each vibrating about its mean position. An oscillation in one dimension has average energy of 2 × ½ k B T = k B T . In three dimensions, the average energy is 3k B T. For a mole of solid, N = NA, and the total energy is','[{"s": "We can use the law of equipartition of energy to determine specific heats of solids. Consider a solid of N atoms, each vibrating about its mean position. An oscillation in one dimension has average energy of 2 × ½ k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T = k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T . In three dimensions, the average energy is 3k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T. For a mole of solid, N = NA, and the total energy is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25359,63,218,'paragraph',NULL,'U = 3 k B T × N A = 3 RT','[{"s": "U = 3 k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T × N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3 RT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25360,63,219,'paragraph',NULL,'Now at constant pressure ∆Q = ∆U + P∆V','[{"s": "Now at constant pressure ∆Q = ∆U + P∆V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25361,63,220,'paragraph',NULL,'= ∆U, since for a solid ∆V is negligible. Hence,','[{"s": "= ∆U, since for a solid ∆V is negligible. Hence,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
