(25365,63,224,'paragraph',NULL,'As Table 13.3 shows the prediction generally agrees with experimental values at ordinary temperature (Carbon is an exception).','[{"s": "As Table 13.3 shows the prediction generally agrees with experimental values at ordinary temperature (Carbon is an exception).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25366,63,225,'heading',4,'13.6.5 Specific Heat Capacity of Water',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25367,63,226,'paragraph',NULL,'We treat water like a solid. For each atom average energy is 3k B T. Water molecule has three atoms, two hydrogen and one oxygen. So it has','[{"s": "We treat water like a solid. For each atom average energy is 3k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T. Water molecule has three atoms, two hydrogen and one oxygen. So it has", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25368,63,227,'paragraph',NULL,'U = 3 × 3 k B T × N A = 9 RT','[{"s": "U = 3 × 3 k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T × N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 9 RT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25369,63,228,'paragraph',NULL,'and C = ∆Q/ ∆T =∆ U / ∆T = 9R .','[{"s": "and C = ∆Q/ ∆T =∆ U / ∆T = 9R .", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25370,63,229,'paragraph',NULL,'This is the value observed and the agreement is very good. In the calorie, gram, degree units, water is defined to have unit specific heat. As 1 calorie = 4.179 joules and one mole of water is 18 grams, the heat capacity per mole is ~ 75 J mol -1 K -1 ~ 9R. However with more complex molecules like alcohol or acetone the arguments, based on degrees of freedom, become more complicated.','[{"s": "This is the value observed and the agreement is very good. In the calorie, gram, degree units, water is defined to have unit specific heat. As 1 calorie = 4.179 joules and one mole of water is 18 grams, the heat capacity per mole is ~ 75 J mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~ 9R. However with more complex molecules like alcohol or acetone the arguments, based on degrees of freedom, become more complicated.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25371,63,230,'paragraph',NULL,'Lastly, we should note an important aspect of the predictions of specific heats, based on the classical law of equipartition of energy. The predicted specific heats are independent of temperature. As we go to low temperatures, however, there is a marked departure from this prediction. Specific heats of all substances approach zero as T -> 0. This is related to the fact that degrees of freedom get frozen and ineffective at low temperatures. According to classical physics, degrees of freedom must remain unchanged at all times. The behaviour of specific heats at low temperatures shows the inadequacy of classical physics and can be explained only by invoking quantum considerations, as was first shown by Einstein. Quantum mechanics requires a minimum, non-zero amount of energy before a degree of freedom comes into play. This is also the reason why vibrational degrees of freedom come into play only in some cases.','[{"s": "Lastly, we should note an important aspect of the predictions of specific heats, based on the classical law of equipartition of energy. The predicted specific heats are independent of temperature. As we go to low temperatures, however, there is a marked departure from this prediction. Specific heats of all substances approach zero as T -> 0. This is related to the fact that degrees of freedom get frozen and ineffective at low temperatures. According to classical physics, degrees of freedom must remain unchanged at all times. The behaviour of specific heats at low temperatures shows the inadequacy of classical physics and can be explained only by invoking quantum considerations, as was first shown by Einstein. Quantum mechanics requires a minimum, non-zero amount of energy before a degree of freedom comes into play. This is also the reason why vibrational degrees of freedom come into play only in some cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25372,63,231,'heading',4,'13.7 MEAN FREE PATH',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25374,63,233,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image15.png'),
(25375,63,234,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.7 The volume swept by a molecule in time ∆t in which any molecule will collide with it.','[{"s": "Fig. 13.7 The volume swept by a molecule in time ∆t in which any molecule will collide with it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25376,63,235,'paragraph',NULL,'Seeing is Believing','[{"s": "Seeing is Believing", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25377,63,236,'paragraph',NULL,'Can one see atoms rushing about. Almost but not quite. One can see pollen grains of a flower being pushed around by molecules of water. The size of the grain is ~ 10 -5 m. In 1827, a Scottish botanist Robert Brown, while examining, under a microscope, pollen grains of a flower suspended in water noticed that they continuously moved about in a zigzag, random fashion.','[{"s": "Can one see atoms rushing about. Almost but not quite. One can see pollen grains of a flower being pushed around by molecules of water. The size of the grain is ~ 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. In 1827, a Scottish botanist Robert Brown, while examining, under a microscope, pollen grains of a flower suspended in water noticed that they continuously moved about in a zigzag, random fashion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25378,63,237,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic theory provides a simple explanation of the phenomenon. Any object suspended in water is continuously bombarded from all sides by the water molecules. Since the motion of molecules is random, the number of molecules hitting the object in any direction is about the same as the number hitting in the opposite direction. The small difference between these molecular hits is negligible compared to the total number of hits for an object of ordinary size, and we do not notice any movement of the object.','[{"s": "Kinetic theory provides a simple explanation of the phenomenon. Any object suspended in water is continuously bombarded from all sides by the water molecules. Since the motion of molecules is random, the number of molecules hitting the object in any direction is about the same as the number hitting in the opposite direction. The small difference between these molecular hits is negligible compared to the total number of hits for an object of ordinary size, and we do not notice any movement of the object.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25379,63,238,'paragraph',NULL,'When the object is sufficiently small but still visible under a microscope, the difference in molecular hits from different directions is not altogether negligible, i.e. the impulses and the torques given to the suspended object through continuous bombardment by the molecules of the medium (water or some other fluid) do not exactly sum to zero. There is a net impulse and torque in this or that direction. The suspended object thus, moves about in a zigzag manner and tumbles about randomly. This motion called now ‘Brownian motion’ is a visible proof of molecular activity. In the last 50 years or so molecules have been seen by scanning tunneling and other special microscopes.','[{"s": "When the object is sufficiently small but still visible under a microscope, the difference in molecular hits from different directions is not altogether negligible, i.e. the impulses and the torques given to the suspended object through continuous bombardment by the molecules of the medium (water or some other fluid) do not exactly sum to zero. There is a net impulse and torque in this or that direction. The suspended object thus, moves about in a zigzag manner and tumbles about randomly. This motion called now ‘Brownian motion’ is a visible proof of molecular activity. In the last 50 years or so molecules have been seen by scanning tunneling and other special microscopes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25380,63,239,'paragraph',NULL,'In 1987 Ahmed Zewail, an Egyptian scientist working in USA was able to observe not only the molecules but also their detailed interactions. He did this by illuminating them with flashes of laser light for very short durations, of the order of tens of femtoseconds and photographing them. ( 1 femto- second = 10 -15 s ). One could study even the formation and breaking of chemical bonds. That is really seeing !','[{"s": "In 1987 Ahmed Zewail, an Egyptian scientist working in USA was able to observe not only the molecules but also their detailed interactions. He did this by illuminating them with flashes of laser light for very short durations, of the order of tens of femtoseconds and photographing them. ( 1 femto- second = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s ). One could study even the formation and breaking of chemical bonds. That is really seeing !", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25381,63,240,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose the molecules of a gas are spheres of diameter d. Focus on a single molecule with the average speed <v>. It will suffer collision with any molecule that comes within a distance d between the centres. In time ∆t, it sweeps a volume πd2 <v> ∆t wherein any other molecule will collide with it (see Fig. 13.7). If n is the number of molecules per unit volume, the molecule suffers nπd 2 <v> ∆t collisions in time ∆t. Thus the rate of collisions is nπd 2 <v> or the time between two successive collisions is on the average,','[{"s": "Suppose the molecules of a gas are spheres of diameter d. Focus on a single molecule with the average speed <v>. It will suffer collision with any molecule that comes within a distance d between the centres. In time ∆t, it sweeps a volume πd2 <v> ∆t wherein any other molecule will collide with it (see Fig. 13.7). If n is the number of molecules per unit volume, the molecule suffers nπd", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<v> ∆t collisions in time ∆t. Thus the rate of collisions is nπd", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<v> or the time between two successive collisions is on the average,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25382,63,241,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = 1/(nπ <v> d 2 ) ..........(13.38)','[{"s": "τ = 1/(nπ <v> d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") ..........(13.38)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25383,63,242,'paragraph',NULL,'The average distance between two successive collisions, called the mean free path l, is :','[{"s": "The average distance between two successive collisions, called the mean free path l, is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25384,63,243,'paragraph',NULL,'l = <v> /τ = 1/(nπd 2 ) ............(13.39)','[{"s": "l = <v> /τ = 1/(nπd", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") ............(13.39)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25385,63,244,'paragraph',NULL,'In this derivation, we imagined the other molecules to be at rest. But actually all molecules are moving and the collision rate is determined by the average relative velocity of the molecules. Thus we need to replace <v> by <v r > in Eq. (13.38). A more exact treatment gives','[{"s": "In this derivation, we imagined the other molecules to be at rest. But actually all molecules are moving and the collision rate is determined by the average relative velocity of the molecules. Thus we need to replace <v> by <v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> in Eq. (13.38). A more exact treatment gives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25386,63,245,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic_Theory-image16.png .............(13.40)','[{"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image16.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".............(13.40)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25387,63,246,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us estimate l and τ for air molecules with average speeds <v> = ( 485m/s). At STP','[{"s": "Let us estimate l and τ for air molecules with average speeds <v> = ( 485m/s). At STP", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25388,63,247,'paragraph',NULL,'n = Kinetic_Theory-image17.png','[{"s": "n =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image17.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25389,63,248,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2.7 × 10 25 m -3.','[{"s": "= 2.7 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "25", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25390,63,249,'paragraph',NULL,'Taking, d = 2 × 10 –10 m,','[{"s": "Taking, d = 2 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25391,63,250,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = 6.1 × 10 –10 s','[{"s": "τ = 6.1 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25392,63,251,'paragraph',NULL,'And l = 2.9 × 10 –7 m ≈ 1500d ..............(13.41)','[{"s": "And l = 2.9 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m ≈ 1500d ..............(13.41)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25394,63,253,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.9 Estimate the mean free path for a water molecule in water vapour at 373 K. Use information from Exercises 13.1 and Eq. (13.41) above.','[{"s": "Example 13.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Estimate the mean free path for a water molecule in water vapour at 373 K. Use information from Exercises 13.1 and Eq. (13.41) above.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25395,63,254,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The d for water vapour is same as that of air. The number density is inversely proportional to absolute temperature.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The d for water vapour is same as that of air. The number density is inversely proportional to absolute temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25396,63,255,'paragraph',NULL,'So Kinetic_Theory-image18.png','[{"s": "So", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image18.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25397,63,256,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence, mean free path Kinetic_Theory-image19.png','[{"s": "Hence, mean free path", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image19.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25398,63,257,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the mean free path is 100 times the interatomic distance ~ 40 Å = 4 ×10 -9 m calculated earlier. It is this large value of mean free path that leads to the typical gaseous behaviour. Gases can not be confined without a container.','[{"s": "Note that the mean free path is 100 times the interatomic distance ~ 40 Å = 4 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m calculated earlier. It is this large value of mean free path that leads to the typical gaseous behaviour. Gases can not be confined without a container.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25399,63,258,'paragraph',NULL,'Using, the kinetic theory of gases, the bulk measurable properties like viscosity, heat conductivity and diffusion can be related to the microscopic parameters like molecular size. It is through such relations that the molecular sizes were first estimated.','[{"s": "Using, the kinetic theory of gases, the bulk measurable properties like viscosity, heat conductivity and diffusion can be related to the microscopic parameters like molecular size. It is through such relations that the molecular sizes were first estimated.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25400,63,259,'heading',4,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25402,63,261,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = µ RT = kB NT','[{"s": "PV = µ RT = kB NT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25403,63,262,'paragraph',NULL,'where µ is the number of moles and N is the number of molecules. R and kB are universal constants.','[{"s": "where µ is the number of moles and N is the number of molecules. R and kB are universal constants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25404,63,263,'paragraph',NULL,'R = 8.314 J mol–1 K–1, kB = Kinetic_Theory-image20.png = 1.38 × 10–23 J K–1','[{"s": "R = 8.314 J mol–1 K–1, kB =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image20.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 1.38 × 10–23 J K–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25405,63,264,'paragraph',NULL,'Real gases satisfy the ideal gas equation only approximately, more so at low pressures and high temperatures.','[{"s": "Real gases satisfy the ideal gas equation only approximately, more so at low pressures and high temperatures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25406,63,265,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Kinetic theory of an ideal gas gives the relation','[{"s": "2. Kinetic theory of an ideal gas gives the relation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25407,63,266,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image1.png'),
(25409,63,268,'paragraph',NULL,'Kinetic_Theory-image3.png , Kinetic_Theory-image4.png Kinetic_Theory-image5.png','[{"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image3.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image4.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image5.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25401,63,260,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The ideal gas equation connecting pressure (P), volume (V) and absolute temperature (T ) is','[{"s": "1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The ideal gas equation connecting pressure (P), volume (V) and absolute temperature (T ) is", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000038375", "2000110022"]',NULL),
(25410,63,269,'paragraph',NULL,'This tells us that the temperature of a gas is a measure of the average kinetic energy of a molecule, independent of the nature of the gas or molecule. In a mixture of gases at a fixed temperature the heavier molecule has the lower average speed.','[{"s": "This tells us that the temperature of a gas is a measure of the average kinetic energy of a molecule, independent of the nature of the gas or molecule. In a mixture of gases at a fixed temperature the heavier molecule has the lower average speed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25411,63,270,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The translational kinetic energy','[{"s": "3. The translational kinetic energy", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25412,63,271,'paragraph',NULL,'E = Kinetic_Theory-image6.png kB NT.','[{"s": "E =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image6.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "kB NT.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25413,63,272,'paragraph',NULL,'This leads to a relation','[{"s": "This leads to a relation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25414,63,273,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = Kinetic_Theory-image7.png E','[{"s": "PV =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image7.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25415,63,274,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The law of equipartition of energy states that if a system is in equilibrium at absolute temperature T, the total energy is distributed equally in different energy modes of absorption, the energy in each mode being equal to ½ kB T. Each translational and rotational degree of freedom corresponds to one energy mode of absorption and has energy ½ kB T. Each vibrational frequency has two modes of energy (kinetic and potential) with corresponding energy equal to','[{"s": "4. The law of equipartition of energy states that if a system is in equilibrium at absolute temperature T, the total energy is distributed equally in different energy modes of absorption, the energy in each mode being equal to ½ kB T. Each translational and rotational degree of freedom corresponds to one energy mode of absorption and has energy ½ kB T. Each vibrational frequency has two modes of energy (kinetic and potential) with corresponding energy equal to", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25416,63,275,'paragraph',NULL,'2 × ½ kB T = kB T.','[{"s": "2 × ½ kB T = kB T.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25417,63,276,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Using the law of equipartition of energy, the molar specific heats of gases can be determined and the values are in agreement with the experimental values of specific heats of several gases. The agreement can be improved by including vibrational modes of motion.','[{"s": "5. Using the law of equipartition of energy, the molar specific heats of gases can be determined and the values are in agreement with the experimental values of specific heats of several gases. The agreement can be improved by including vibrational modes of motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25419,63,278,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image8.png'),
(25420,63,279,'paragraph',NULL,'where n is the number density and d the diameter of the molecule.','[{"s": "where n is the number density and d the diameter of the molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25421,63,280,'heading',4,'POINTS TO PONDER',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25422,63,281,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Pressure of a fluid is not only exerted on the wall. Pressure exists everywhere in a fluid. Any layer of gas inside the volume of a container is in equilibrium because the pressure is the same on both sides of the layer.','[{"s": "1. Pressure of a fluid is not only exerted on the wall. Pressure exists everywhere in a fluid. Any layer of gas inside the volume of a container is in equilibrium because the pressure is the same on both sides of the layer.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25423,63,282,'paragraph',NULL,'2. We should not have an exaggerated idea of the intermolecular distance in a gas. At ordinary pressures and temperatures, this is only 10 times or so the interatomic distance in solids and liquids. What is different is the mean free path which in a gas is 100 times the interatomic distance and 1000 times the size of the molecule.','[{"s": "2. We should not have an exaggerated idea of the intermolecular distance in a gas. At ordinary pressures and temperatures, this is only 10 times or so the interatomic distance in solids and liquids. What is different is the mean free path which in a gas is 100 times the interatomic distance and 1000 times the size of the molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25424,63,283,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The law of equipartition of energy is stated thus: the energy for each degree of freedom in thermal equilibrium is ½ kB T. Each quadratic term in the total energy expression of a molecule is to be counted as a degree of freedom. Thus, each vibrational mode gives 2 (not 1) degrees of freedom (kinetic and potential energy modes), corresponding to the energy 2 × ½ kB T = kB T.','[{"s": "3. The law of equipartition of energy is stated thus: the energy for each degree of freedom in thermal equilibrium is ½ kB T. Each quadratic term in the total energy expression of a molecule is to be counted as a degree of freedom. Thus, each vibrational mode gives 2 (not 1) degrees of freedom (kinetic and potential energy modes), corresponding to the energy 2 × ½ kB T = kB T.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25425,63,284,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Molecules of air in a room do not all fall and settle on the ground (due to gravity) because of their high speeds and incessant collisions. In equilibrium, there is a very slight increase in density at lower heights (like in the atmosphere). The effect is small since the potential energy (mgh) for ordinary heights is much less than the average kinetic energy ½ mv2 of the molecules.','[{"s": "4. Molecules of air in a room do not all fall and settle on the ground (due to gravity) because of their high speeds and incessant collisions. In equilibrium, there is a very slight increase in density at lower heights (like in the atmosphere). The effect is small since the potential energy (mgh) for ordinary heights is much less than the average kinetic energy ½ mv2 of the molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25426,63,285,'paragraph',NULL,'5. < v2 > is not always equal to ( < v >)2. The average of a squared quantity is not necessarily the square of the average. Can you find examples for this statement.','[{"s": "5. < v2 > is not always equal to ( < v >)2. The average of a squared quantity is not necessarily the square of the average. Can you find examples for this statement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
