(25427,63,286,'heading',4,'EXERCISES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25428,63,287,'paragraph',NULL,'13.1 Estimate the fraction of molecular volume to the actual volume occupied by oxygen gas at STP. Take the diameter of an oxygen molecule to be 3 Å.','[{"s": "13.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Estimate the fraction of molecular volume to the actual volume occupied by oxygen gas at STP. Take the diameter of an oxygen molecule to be 3 Å.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25429,63,288,'paragraph',NULL,'13.2 Molar volume is the volume occupied by 1 mol of any (ideal) gas at standard temperature and pressure (STP: 1 atmospheric pressure, 0 °C). Show that it is 22.4 litres.','[{"s": "13.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Molar volume is the volume occupied by 1 mol of any (ideal) gas at standard temperature and pressure (STP: 1 atmospheric pressure, 0 °C). Show that it is 22.4 litres.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25430,63,289,'paragraph',NULL,'13.3 Figure 13.8 shows a plot of PV/T versus P for 1.00× 10 -3 kg of oxygen gas at two different temperatures.','[{"s": "13.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Figure 13.8 shows a plot of PV/T versus P for 1.00×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg of oxygen gas at two different temperatures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25431,63,290,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Kinetic_Theory-image9.png'),
(25432,63,291,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 13.8','[{"s": "Fig. 13.8", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25433,63,292,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) What does the dotted plot signify?','[{"s": "(a) What does the dotted plot signify?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25434,63,293,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Which is true: T 1 > T 2 or T 1 < T 2 ?','[{"s": "(b) Which is true: T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25435,63,294,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) What is the value of PV/T where the curves meet on the y-axis?','[{"s": "(c) What is the value of PV/T where the curves meet on the y-axis?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25436,63,295,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) If we obtained similar plots for 1.00× 10 -3 kg of hydrogen, would we get the same value of PV/T at the point where the curves meet on the y-axis? If not, what mass of hydrogen yields the same value of PV/T (for the low-pressure and high-temperature region of the plot)? (Molecular mass of H 2 = 2.02 u, of O 2 = 32.0 u, R = 8.31 J mo1 –1 K –1 .)','[{"s": "(d) If we obtained similar plots for 1.00×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg of hydrogen, would we get the same value of PV/T at the point where the curves meet on the y-axis? If not, what mass of hydrogen yields the same value of PV/T (for the low-pressure and high-temperature region of the plot)? (Molecular mass of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.02 u, of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 32.0 u, R = 8.31 J mo1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25437,63,296,'paragraph',NULL,'13.4 An oxygen cylinder of volume 30 litres has an initial gauge pressure of 15 atm and a temperature of 27 °C. After some oxygen is withdrawn from the cylinder, the gauge pressure drops to 11 atm and its temperature drops to 17 °C. Estimate the mass of oxygen taken out of the cylinder (R = 8.31 J mol -1 K –1 , the molecular mass of','[{"s": "13.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An oxygen cylinder of volume 30 litres has an initial gauge pressure of 15 atm and a temperature of 27 °C. After some oxygen is withdrawn from the cylinder, the gauge pressure drops to 11 atm and its temperature drops to 17 °C. Estimate the mass of oxygen taken out of the cylinder (R = 8.31 J", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the molecular mass of", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25438,63,297,'paragraph',NULL,'O 2 = 32 u).','[{"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 32 u).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25439,63,298,'paragraph',NULL,'13.5 An air bubble of volume 1.0 cm 3 rises from the bottom of a lake 40 m deep at a temperature of 12 °C. To what volume does it grow when it reaches the surface, which is at a temperature of 35 °C?','[{"s": "13.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An air bubble of volume 1.0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rises from the bottom of a lake 40 m deep at a temperature of 12 °C. To what volume does it grow when it reaches the surface, which is at a temperature of 35 °C?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25440,63,299,'paragraph',NULL,'13.6 Estimate the total number of air molecules (inclusive of oxygen, nitrogen, water vapour and other constituents) in a room of capacity 25.0 m 3 at a temperature of 27 °C and 1 atm pressure.','[{"s": "13.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Estimate the total number of air molecules (inclusive of oxygen, nitrogen, water vapour and other constituents) in a room of capacity 25.0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at a temperature of 27 °C and 1 atm pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25441,63,300,'paragraph',NULL,'13.7 Estimate the average thermal energy of a helium atom at (i) room temperature (27 °C), (ii) the temperature on the surface of the Sun (6000 K), (iii) the temperature of 10 million kelvin (the typical core temperature in case of a star).','[{"s": "13.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Estimate the average thermal energy of a helium atom at (i) room temperature (27 °C), (ii) the temperature on the surface of the Sun (6000 K), (iii) the temperature of 10 million kelvin (the typical core temperature in case of a star).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25442,63,301,'paragraph',NULL,'13.8 Three vessels of equal capacity have gases at the same temperature and pressure. The first vessel contains neon (monatomic), the second contains chlorine (diatomic), and the third contains uranium hexafluoride (polyatomic). Do the vessels contain the equal number of respective molecules? Is the root mean square speed of molecules the same in the three cases? If not, in which case is v rms the largest?','[{"s": "13.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Three vessels of equal capacity have gases at the same temperature and pressure. The first vessel contains neon (monatomic), the second contains chlorine (diatomic), and the third contains uranium hexafluoride (polyatomic). Do the vessels contain the equal number of respective molecules? Is the root mean square speed of molecules the same in the three cases? If not, in which case is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the largest?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25443,63,302,'paragraph',NULL,'13.9 At what temperature is the root mean square speed of an atom in an argon gas cylinder equal to the RMS speed of a helium gas atom at – 20 °C? (atomic mass of Ar = 39.9 u, of He = 4.0 u).','[{"s": "13.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "At what temperature is the root mean square speed of an atom in an argon gas cylinder equal to the RMS speed of a helium gas atom at – 20 °C? (atomic mass of Ar = 39.9 u, of He = 4.0 u).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25444,63,303,'paragraph',NULL,'13.10 Estimate the mean free path and collision frequency of a nitrogen molecule in a cylinder containing nitrogen at 2.0 atm and temperature 17 °C. Take the radius of a nitrogen molecule to be roughly 1.0 Å. Compare the collision time with the time the molecule moves freely between two successive collisions (Molecular mass of','[{"s": "13.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Estimate the mean free path and collision frequency of a nitrogen molecule in a cylinder containing nitrogen at 2.0 atm and temperature 17 °C. Take the radius of a nitrogen molecule to be roughly 1.0 Å. Compare the collision time with the time the molecule moves freely between two successive collisions (Molecular mass of", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25445,63,304,'paragraph',NULL,'N 2 = 28.0 u).','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 28.0 u).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25446,63,305,'paragraph',NULL,'13.11 A metre long narrow bore held horizontally (and closed at one end) contains a 76 cm long mercury thread, which traps a 15 cm column of air. What happens if the tube is held vertically with the open end at the bottom?','[{"s": "13.11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A metre long narrow bore held horizontally (and closed at one end) contains a 76 cm long mercury thread, which traps a 15 cm column of air. What happens if the tube is held vertically with the open end at the bottom?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
