(25574,64,14,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) (ii) (iii)','[{"s": "(i) (ii) (iii)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25575,64,15,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 5.1(a)','[{"s": "Fig. 5.1(a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25576,64,16,'paragraph',NULL,'Another experiment by Galileo leading to the same conclusion involves a double inclined plane. A ball released from rest on one of the planes rolls down and climbs up the other. If the planes are smooth, the final height of the ball is nearly the same as the initial height (a little less but never greater). In the ideal situation, when friction is absent, the final height of the ball is the same as its initial height.','[{"s": "Another experiment by Galileo leading to the same conclusion involves a double inclined plane. A ball released from rest on one of the planes rolls down and climbs up the other. If the planes are smooth, the final height of the ball is nearly the same as the initial height (a little less but never greater). In the ideal situation, when friction is absent, the final height of the ball is the same as its initial height.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25577,64,17,'paragraph',NULL,'If the slope of the second plane is decreased and the experiment repeated, the ball will still reach the same height, but in doing so, it will travel a longer distance. In the limiting case, when the slope of the second plane is zero (i.e. is a horizontal) the ball travels an infinite distance. In other words, its motion never ceases. This is, of course, an idealised situation (Fig. 5.1(b)).','[{"s": "If the slope of the second plane is decreased and the experiment repeated, the ball will still reach the same height, but in doing so, it will travel a longer distance. In the limiting case, when the slope of the second plane is zero (i.e. is a horizontal) the ball travels an infinite distance. In other words, its motion never ceases. This is, of course, an idealised situation (Fig. 5.1(b)).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25578,64,18,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image37.png'),
(25579,64,19,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 5.1(b) The law of inertia was inferred by Galileo from observations of motion of a ball on a double inclined plane.','[{"s": "Fig. 5.1(b)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The law of inertia was inferred by Galileo from observations of motion of a ball on a double inclined plane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25580,64,20,'paragraph',NULL,'In practice, the ball does come to a stop after moving a finite distance on the horizontal plane, because of the opposing force of friction which can never be totally eliminated. However, if there were no friction, the ball would continue to move with a constant velocity on the horizontal plane.','[{"s": "In practice, the ball does come to a stop after moving a finite distance on the horizontal plane, because of the opposing force of friction which can never be totally eliminated. However, if there were no friction, the ball would continue to move with a constant velocity on the horizontal plane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25581,64,21,'paragraph',NULL,'Galileo thus, arrived at a new insight on motion that had eluded Aristotle and those who followed him. The state of rest and the state of uniform linear motion (motion with constant velocity) are equivalent. In both cases, there is no net force acting on the body. It is incorrect to assume that a net force is needed to keep a body in uniform motion. To maintain a body in uniform motion, we need to apply an external force to ecounter the frictional force, so that the two forces sum up to zero net external force.','[{"s": "Galileo thus, arrived at a new insight on motion that had eluded Aristotle and those who followed him. The state of rest and the state of uniform linear motion (motion with constant velocity) are equivalent. In both cases, there is no net force acting on the body. It is incorrect to assume that a net force is needed to keep a body in uniform motion. To maintain a body in uniform motion, we need to apply an external force to ecounter the frictional force, so that the two forces sum up to zero net external force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25582,64,22,'paragraph',NULL,'To summarise, if the net external force is zero, a body at rest continues to remain at rest and a body in motion continues to move with a uniform velocity. This property of the body is called inertia. Inertia means ‘resistance to change’ . A body does not change its state of rest or uniform motion, unless an external force compels it to change that state.','[{"s": "To summarise, if the net external force is zero, a body at rest continues to remain at rest and a body in motion continues to move with a uniform velocity. This property of the body is called inertia. Inertia means", "t": "text"}, {"s": "‘resistance to change’", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". A body does not change its state of rest or uniform motion, unless an external force compels it to change that state.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25583,64,23,'heading',4,'Ideas on Motion in Ancient Indian Science',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25584,64,24,'paragraph',NULL,'Ancient Indian thinkers had arrived at an elaborate system of ideas on motion. Force, the cause of motion, was thought to be of different kinds : force due to continuous pressure (nodan), as the force of wind on a sailing vessel; impact (abhighat), as when a potter’s rod strikes the wheel; persistent tendency (sanskara) to move in a straight line(vega) or restoration of shape in an elastic body; transmitted force by a string, rod, etc. The notion of (vega) in the Vaisesika theory of motion perhaps comes closest to the concept of inertia. Vega, the tendency to move in a straight line, was thought to be opposed by contact with objects including atmosphere, a parallel to the ideas of friction and air resistance. It was correctly summarised that the different kinds of motion (translational, rotational and vibrational) of an extended body arise from only the translational motion of its constituent particles. A falling leaf in the wind may have downward motion as a whole (patan) and also rotational and vibrational motion (bhraman, spandan), but each particle of the leaf at an instant only has a definite (small) displacement. There was considerable focus in Indian thought on measurement of motion and units of length and time. It was known that the position of a particle in space can be indicated by distance measured along three axes. Bhaskara (1150 A.D.) had introduced the concept of ‘instantaneous motion’ (tatkaliki gati), which anticipated the modern notion of instantaneous velocity using Differential Calculus. The difference between a wave and a current (of water) was clearly understood; a current is a motion of particles of water under gravity and fluidity while a wave results from the transmission of vibrations of water particles.','[{"s": "Ancient Indian thinkers had arrived at an elaborate system of ideas on motion. Force, the cause of motion, was thought to be of different kinds : force due to continuous pressure (nodan), as the force of wind on a sailing vessel; impact (abhighat), as when a potter’s rod strikes the wheel; persistent tendency (sanskara) to move in a straight line(vega) or restoration of shape in an elastic body; transmitted force by a string, rod, etc. The notion of (vega) in the Vaisesika theory of motion perhaps comes closest to the concept of inertia. Vega, the tendency to move in a straight line, was thought to be opposed by contact with objects including atmosphere, a parallel to the ideas of friction and air resistance. It was correctly summarised that the different kinds of motion (translational, rotational and vibrational) of an extended body arise from only the translational motion of its constituent particles. A falling leaf in the wind may have downward motion as a whole (patan) and also rotational and vibrational motion (bhraman, spandan), but each particle of the leaf at an instant only has a definite (small) displacement. There was considerable focus in Indian thought on measurement of motion and units of length and time. It was known that the position of a particle in space can be indicated by distance measured along three axes. Bhaskara (1150 A.D.) had introduced the concept of ‘instantaneous motion’ (tatkaliki gati), which anticipated the modern notion of instantaneous velocity using Differential Calculus. The difference between a wave and a current (of water) was clearly understood; a current is a motion of particles of water under gravity and fluidity while a wave results from the transmission of vibrations of water particles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25585,64,25,'heading',2,'5.4 NEWTON’S FIRST LAW OF MOTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25586,64,26,'paragraph',NULL,'Galileo’s simple, but revolutionary ideas dethroned Aristotelian mechanics. A new mechanics had to be developed. This task was accomplished almost single-handedly by Isaac Newton, one of the greatest scientists of all times.','[{"s": "Galileo’s simple, but revolutionary ideas dethroned Aristotelian mechanics. A new mechanics had to be developed. This task was accomplished almost single-handedly by Isaac Newton, one of the greatest scientists of all times.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25587,64,27,'paragraph',NULL,'Newton built on Galileo’s ideas and laid the foundation of mechanics in terms of three laws of motion that go by his name. Galileo’s law of inertia was his starting point which he formulated as the first law of motion :','[{"s": "Newton built on Galileo’s ideas and laid the foundation of mechanics in terms of three laws of motion that go by his name. Galileo’s law of inertia was his starting point which he formulated as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "first law of motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25591,64,31,'paragraph',NULL,'Two kinds of situations are encountered in the application of this law in practice. In some examples, we know that the net external force on the object is zero. In that case we can conclude that the acceleration of the object is zero. For example, a spaceship out in interstellar space, far from all other objects and with all its rockets turned off, has no net external force acting on it. Its acceleration, according to the first law, must be zero. If it is in motion, it must continue to move with a uniform velocity.','[{"s": "Two kinds of situations are encountered in the application of this law in practice. In some examples, we know that the net external force on the object is zero. In that case we can conclude that the acceleration of the object is zero. For example, a spaceship out in interstellar space, far from all other objects and with all its rockets turned off, has no net external force acting on it. Its acceleration, according to the first law, must be zero. If it is in motion, it must continue to move with a uniform velocity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25592,64,32,'heading',4,'Galileo Galilei (1564 - 1642)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25593,64,33,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image36.png'),
(30248,73,306,'paragraph',NULL,'Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image71.png … (9.31)','[{"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image71.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "… (9.31)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25594,64,34,'paragraph',NULL,'Galileo Galilei, born in Pisa, Italy in 1564 was a key figure in the scientific revolution in Europe about four centuries ago. Galileo proposed the concept of acceleration. From experiments on motion of bodies on inclined planes or falling freely, he contradicted the Aristotelian notion that a force was required to keep a body in motion, and that heavier bodies fall faster than lighter bodies under gravity. He thus arrived at the law of inertia that was the starting point of the subsequent epochal work of Isaac Newton.','[{"s": "Galileo Galilei, born in Pisa, Italy in 1564 was a key figure in the scientific revolution in Europe about four centuries ago. Galileo proposed the concept of acceleration. From experiments on motion of bodies on inclined planes or falling freely, he contradicted the Aristotelian notion that a force was required to keep a body in motion, and that heavier bodies fall faster than lighter bodies under gravity. He thus arrived at the law of inertia that was the starting point of the subsequent epochal work of Isaac Newton.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25595,64,35,'paragraph',NULL,'Galileo’s discoveries in astronomy were equally revolutionary. In 1609, he designed his own telescope (invented earlier in Holland) and used it to make a number of startling observations : mountains and depressions on the surface of the moon; dark spots on the sun; the moons of Jupiter and the phases of Venus. He concluded that the Milky Way derived its luminosity because of a large number of stars not visible to the naked eye. In his masterpiece of scientific reasoning : Dialogue on the Two Chief World Systems, Galileo advocated the heliocentric theory of the solar system proposed by Copernicus, which eventually got universal acceptance.','[{"s": "Galileo’s discoveries in astronomy were equally revolutionary. In 1609, he designed his own telescope (invented earlier in Holland) and used it to make a number of startling observations : mountains and depressions on the surface of the moon; dark spots on the sun; the moons of Jupiter and the phases of Venus. He concluded that the Milky Way derived its luminosity because of a large number of stars not visible to the naked eye. In his masterpiece of scientific reasoning : Dialogue on the Two Chief World Systems, Galileo advocated the heliocentric theory of the solar system proposed by Copernicus, which eventually got universal acceptance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25596,64,36,'paragraph',NULL,'With Galileo came a turning point in the very method of scientific inquiry. Science was no longer merely observations of nature and inferences from them. Science meant devising and doing experiments to verify or refute theories. Science meant measurement of quantities and a search for mathematical relations between them. Not undeservedly, many regard Galileo as the father of modern science.','[{"s": "With Galileo came a turning point in the very method of scientific inquiry. Science was no longer merely observations of nature and inferences from them. Science meant devising and doing experiments to verify or refute theories. Science meant measurement of quantities and a search for mathematical relations between them. Not undeservedly, many regard Galileo as the father of modern science.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25597,64,37,'paragraph',NULL,'More often, however, we do not know all the forces to begin with. In that case, if we know that an object is unaccelerated (i.e. it is either at rest or in uniform linear motion), we can infer from the first law that the net external force on the object must be zero. Gravity is everywhere. For terrestrial phenomena, in particular, every object experiences gravitational force due to the earth. Also objects in motion generally experience friction, viscous drag, etc. If then, on earth, an object is at rest or in uniform linear motion, it is not because there are no forces acting on it, but because the various external forces cancel out i.e. add up to zero net external force.','[{"s": "More often, however, we do not know all the forces to begin with. In that case, if we know that an object is unaccelerated (i.e. it is either at rest or in uniform linear motion), we can infer from the first law that the net external force on the object must be zero. Gravity is everywhere. For terrestrial phenomena, in particular, every object experiences gravitational force due to the earth. Also objects in motion generally experience friction, viscous drag, etc. If then, on earth, an object is at rest or in uniform linear motion, it is not because there are no forces acting on it, but because the various external forces cancel out i.e. add up to zero net external force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25598,64,38,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a book at rest on a horizontal surface Fig. (5.2(a)). It is subject to two external forces : the force due to gravity (i.e. its weight W ) acting downward and the upward force on the book by the table, the normal force R . R is a self-adjusting force. This is an example of the kind of situation mentioned above. The forces are not quite known fully but the state of motion is known. We observe the book to be at rest. Therefore, we conclude from the first law that the magnitude of R equals that of W . A statement often encountered is : “Since W = R , forces cancel and, therefore, the book is at rest”. This is incorrect reasoning. The correct statement is : “Since the book is observed to be at rest, the net external force on it must be zero, according to the first law. This implies that the normal force R must be equal and opposite to the weight W ” .','[{"s": "Consider a book at rest on a horizontal surface Fig. (5.2(a)). It is subject to two external forces : the force due to gravity (i.e. its weight", "t": "text"}, {"s": "W", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") acting downward and the upward force on the book by the table, the normal force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a self-adjusting force. This is an example of the kind of situation mentioned above. The forces are not quite known fully but the state of motion is known. We observe the book to be at rest. Therefore, we conclude from the first law that the magnitude of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "equals that of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "W", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". A statement often encountered is : “Since", "t": "text"}, {"s": "W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", forces cancel and, therefore, the book is at rest”. This is incorrect reasoning. The correct statement is : “Since the book is observed to be at rest, the net external force on it must be zero, according to the first law. This implies that the normal force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "must be equal and opposite to the weight", "t": "text"}, {"s": "W ”", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25599,64,39,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image39.png'),
(25600,64,40,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 5.2 (a) a book at rest on the table, and (b) a car moving with uniform velocity. The net force is zero in each case.','[{"s": "Fig. 5.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) a book at rest on the table, and (b) a car moving with uniform velocity. The net force is zero in each case.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30250,73,308,'paragraph',NULL,'P = P 1 + P 2 + P 3 + … (9.32)','[{"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ … (9.32)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25601,64,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider the motion of a car starting from rest, picking up speed and then moving on a smooth straight road with uniform speed (Fig. (5.2(b)). When the car is stationary, there is no net force acting on it. During pick-up, it accelerates. This must happen due to a net external force. Note, it has to be an external force. The acceleration of the car cannot be accounted for by any internal force. This might sound surprising, but it is true. The only conceivable external force along the road is the force of friction. It is the frictional force that accelerates the car as a whole. (You will learn about friction in section 5.9). When the car moves with constant velocity, there is no net external force.','[{"s": "Consider the motion of a car starting from rest, picking up speed and then moving on a smooth straight road with uniform speed (Fig. (5.2(b)). When the car is stationary, there is no net force acting on it. During pick-up, it accelerates. This must happen due to a net external force. Note, it has to be an external force. The acceleration of the car cannot be accounted for by any internal force. This might sound surprising, but it is true. The only conceivable external force along the road is the force of friction. It is the frictional force that accelerates the car as a whole. (You will learn about friction in section 5.9). When the car moves with constant velocity, there is no net external force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25602,64,42,'paragraph',NULL,'The property of inertia contained in the First law is evident in many situations. Suppose we are standing in a stationary bus and the driver starts the bus suddenly. We get thrown backward with a jerk. Why ? Our feet are in touch with the floor. If there were no friction, we would remain where we were, while the floor of the bus would simply slip forward under our feet and the back of the bus would hit us. However, fortunately, there is some friction between the feet and the floor. If the start is not too sudden, i.e. if the acceleration is moderate, the frictional force would be enough to accelerate our feet along with the bus. But our body is not strictly a rigid body. It is deformable, i.e. it allows some relative displacement between different parts. What this means is that while our feet go with the bus, the rest of the body remains where it is due to inertia. Relative to the bus, therefore, we are thrown backward. As soon as that happens, however, the muscular forces on the rest of the body (by the feet) come into play to move the body along with the bus. A similar thing happens when the bus suddenly stops. Our feet stop due to the friction which does not allow relative motion between the feet and the floor of the bus. But the rest of the body continues to move forward due to inertia. We are thrown forward. The restoring muscular forces again come into play and bring the body to rest.','[{"s": "The property of inertia contained in the First law is evident in many situations. Suppose we are standing in a stationary bus and the driver starts the bus suddenly. We get thrown backward with a jerk. Why ? Our feet are in touch with the floor. If there were no friction, we would remain where we were, while the floor of the bus would simply slip forward under our feet and the back of the bus would hit us. However, fortunately, there is some friction between the feet and the floor. If the start is not too sudden, i.e. if the acceleration is moderate, the frictional force would be enough to accelerate our feet along with the bus. But our body is not strictly a rigid body. It is deformable, i.e. it allows some relative displacement between different parts. What this means is that while our feet go with the bus, the rest of the body remains where it is due to inertia. Relative to the bus, therefore, we are thrown backward. As soon as that happens, however, the muscular forces on the rest of the body (by the feet) come into play to move the body along with the bus. A similar thing happens when the bus suddenly stops. Our feet stop due to the friction which does not allow relative motion between the feet and the floor of the bus. But the rest of the body continues to move forward due to inertia. We are thrown forward. The restoring muscular forces again come into play and bring the body to rest.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25603,64,43,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.1 An astronaut accidentally gets separated out of his small spaceship accelerating in inter stellar space at a constant rate of 100 m s –2 . What is the acceleration of the astronaut the instant after he is outside the spaceship ? (Assume that there are no nearby stars to exert gravitational force on him.)','[{"s": "Example 5.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An astronaut accidentally gets separated out of his small spaceship accelerating in inter stellar space at a constant rate of 100 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". What is the acceleration of the astronaut the instant after he is outside the spaceship ? (Assume that there are no nearby stars to exert gravitational force on him.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25604,64,44,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Since there are no nearby stars to exert gravitational force on him and the small spaceship exerts negligible gravitational attraction on him, the net force acting on the astronaut, once he is out of the spaceship, is zero. By the first law of motion the acceleration of the astronaut is zero.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Since there are no nearby stars to exert gravitational force on him and the small spaceship exerts negligible gravitational attraction on him, the net force acting on the astronaut, once he is out of the spaceship, is zero. By the first law of motion the acceleration of the astronaut is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25605,64,45,'heading',2,'5.5 NEWTON’S SECOND LAW OF MOTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25606,64,46,'paragraph',NULL,'The first law refers to the simple case when the net external force on a body is zero. The second law of motion refers to the general situation when there is a net external force acting on the body. It relates the net external force to the acceleration of the body.','[{"s": "The first law refers to the simple case when the net external force on a body is zero. The second law of motion refers to the general situation when there is a net external force acting on the body. It relates the net external force to the acceleration of the body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25607,64,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Momentum','[{"s": "Momentum", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25608,64,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Momentum of a body is defined to be the product of its mass m and velocity v p :','[{"s": "Momentum of a body is defined to be the product of its mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25609,64,49,'paragraph',NULL,'p = m v (5.1)','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(5.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25610,64,50,'paragraph',NULL,'Momentum is clearly a vector quantity. The following common experiences indicate the importance of this quantity for considering the effect of force on motion.','[{"s": "Momentum is clearly a vector quantity. The following common experiences indicate the importance of this quantity for considering the effect of force on motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25611,64,51,'paragraph',NULL,'• Suppose a light-weight vehicle (say a small car) and a heavy weight vehicle (say a loaded truck) are parked on a horizontal road. We all know that a much greater force is needed to push the truck than the car to bring them to the same speed in same time. Similarly, a greater opposing force is needed to stop a heavy body than a light body in the same time, if they are moving with the same speed.','[{"s": "• Suppose a light-weight vehicle (say a small car) and a heavy weight vehicle (say a loaded truck) are parked on a horizontal road. We all know that a much greater force is needed to push the truck than the car to bring them to the same speed in same time. Similarly, a greater opposing force is needed to stop a heavy body than a light body in the same time, if they are moving with the same speed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25612,64,52,'paragraph',NULL,'• If two stones, one light and the other heavy, are dropped from the top of a building, a person on the ground will find it easier to catch the light stone than the heavy stone. The mass of a body is thus an important parameter that determines the effect of force on its motion.','[{"s": "• If two stones, one light and the other heavy, are dropped from the top of a building, a person on the ground will find it easier to catch the light stone than the heavy stone. The mass of a body is thus an important parameter that determines the effect of force on its motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25613,64,53,'paragraph',NULL,'• Speed is another important parameter to consider. A bullet fired by a gun can easily pierce human tissue before it stops, resulting in casualty. The same bullet fired with moderate speed will not cause much damage. Thus for a given mass, the greater the speed, the greater is the opposing force needed to stop the body in a certain time. Taken together, the product of mass and velocity, that is momentum, is evidently a relevant variable of motion. The greater the change in the momentum in a given time, the greater is the force that needs to be applied.','[{"s": "• Speed is another important parameter to consider. A bullet fired by a gun can easily pierce human tissue before it stops, resulting in casualty. The same bullet fired with moderate speed will not cause much damage. Thus for a given mass, the greater the speed, the greater is the opposing force needed to stop the body in a certain time. Taken together, the product of mass and velocity, that is momentum, is evidently a relevant variable of motion. The greater the change in the momentum in a given time, the greater is the force that needs to be applied.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25614,64,54,'paragraph',NULL,'• A seasoned cricketer catches a cricket ball coming in with great speed far more easily than a novice, who can hurt his hands in the act. One reason is that the cricketer allows a longer time for his hands to stop the ball. As you may have noticed, he draws in the hands backward in the act of catching the ball (Fig. 5.3). The novice, on the other hand, keeps his hands fixed and tries to catch the ball almost instantly. He needs to provide a much greater force to stop the ball instantly, and this hurts. The conclusion is clear: force not only depends on the change in momentum, but also on how fast the change is brought about. The same change in momentum brought about in a shorter time needs a greater applied force. In short, the greater the rate of change of momentum, the greater is the force.','[{"s": "• A seasoned cricketer catches a cricket ball coming in with great speed far more easily than a novice, who can hurt his hands in the act. One reason is that the cricketer allows a longer time for his hands to stop the ball. As you may have noticed, he draws in the hands backward in the act of catching the ball (Fig. 5.3). The novice, on the other hand, keeps his hands fixed and tries to catch the ball almost instantly. He needs to provide a much greater force to stop the ball instantly, and this hurts. The conclusion is clear: force not only depends on the change in momentum, but also on how fast the change is brought about. The same change in momentum brought about in a shorter time needs a greater applied force. In short, the greater the rate of change of momentum, the greater is the force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25615,64,55,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image38.png'),
(25616,64,56,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 5.3 Force not only depends on the change in momentum but also on how fast the change is brought about. A seasoned cricketer draws in his hands during a catch, allowing greater time for the ball to stop and hence requires a smaller force.','[{"s": "Fig. 5.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Force not only depends on the change in momentum but also on how fast the change is brought about. A seasoned cricketer draws in his hands during a catch, allowing greater time for the ball to stop and hence requires a smaller force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25617,64,57,'paragraph',NULL,'• Observations confirm that the product of mass and velocity (i.e. momentum) is basic to the effect of force on motion. Suppose a fixed force is applied for a certain interval of time on two bodies of different masses, initially at rest, the lighter body picks up a greater speed than the heavier body. However, at the end of the time interval, observations show that each body acquires the same momentum. Thus the same force for the same time causes the same change in momentum for different bodies . This is a crucial clue to the second law of motion.','[{"s": "• Observations confirm that the product of mass and velocity (i.e. momentum) is basic to the effect of force on motion. Suppose a fixed force is applied for a certain interval of time on two bodies of different masses, initially at rest, the lighter body picks up a greater speed than the heavier body. However, at the end of the time interval, observations show that each body acquires the same momentum.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus the same force for the same time causes the same change in momentum for different bodies", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This is a crucial clue to the second law of motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25618,64,58,'paragraph',NULL,'• In the preceding observations, the vector character of momentum has not been evident. In the examples so far, momentum and change in momentum both have the same direction. But this is not always the case. Suppose a stone is rotated with uniform speed in a horizontal plane by means of a string, the magnitude of momentum is fixed, but its direction changes (Fig. 5.4). A force is needed to cause this change in momentum vector. This force is provided by our hand through the string. Experience suggests that our hand needs to exert a greater force if the stone is rotated at greater speed or in a circle of smaller radius, or both. This corresponds to greater acceleration or equivalently a greater rate of change in momentum vector. This suggests that the greater the rate of change in momentum vector the greater is the force applied.','[{"s": "• In the preceding observations, the vector character of momentum has not been evident. In the examples so far, momentum and change in momentum both have the same direction. But this is not always the case. Suppose a stone is rotated with uniform speed in a horizontal plane by means of a string, the magnitude of momentum is fixed, but its direction changes (Fig. 5.4). A force is needed to cause this change in momentum vector. This force is provided by our hand through the string. Experience suggests that our hand needs to exert a greater force if the stone is rotated at greater speed or in a circle of smaller radius, or both. This corresponds to greater acceleration or equivalently a greater rate of change in momentum vector. This suggests that the greater the rate of change in momentum vector the greater is the force applied.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25619,64,59,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image43.png'),
(25620,64,60,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 5.4 Force is necessary for changing the direction of momentum, even if its magnitude is constant. We can feel this while rotating a stone in a horizontal circle with uniform speed by means of a string.','[{"s": "Fig. 5.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Force is necessary for changing the direction of momentum, even if its magnitude is constant. We can feel this while rotating a stone in a horizontal circle with uniform speed by means of a string.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25621,64,61,'paragraph',NULL,'These qualitative observations lead to the second law of motion expressed by Newton as follows :','[{"s": "These qualitative observations lead to the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "second law of motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": "expressed by Newton as follows :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25623,64,63,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, if under the action of a force F for time interval ∆ t , the velocity of a body of mass m changes from v to v + ∆ v i.e. its initial momentum p = m v changes by Laws_of_Motion-image42.png . According to the Second Law,','[{"s": "Thus, if under the action of a force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for time interval ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the velocity of a body of mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "changes from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i.e. its initial momentum", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "changes by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image42.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". According to the Second Law,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25624,64,64,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image48.png'),
(25625,64,65,'paragraph',NULL,'where k is a constant of proportionality. Taking the limit ∆ t → 0, the term Laws_of_Motion-image46.png becomes the derivative or differential co-efficient of p with respect to t , denoted by Laws_of_Motion-image53.png . Thus','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a constant of proportionality. Taking the limit ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 0, the term", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image46.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "becomes the derivative or differential co-efficient of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with respect to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", denoted by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image53.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". Thus", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25626,64,66,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image49.png'),
(25627,64,67,'paragraph',NULL,'(5.2)','[{"s": "(5.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(25628,64,68,'paragraph',NULL,'For a body of fixed mass m ,','[{"s": "For a body of fixed mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25629,64,69,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image51.png'),
(25630,64,70,'paragraph',NULL,'(5.3)','[{"s": "(5.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25632,64,72,'paragraph',NULL,'F = k m a (5.4)','[{"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "k m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(5.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25633,64,73,'paragraph',NULL,'which shows that force is proportional to the product of mass m and acceleration a .','[{"s": "which shows that force is proportional to the product of mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and acceleration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25635,64,75,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image54.png'),
(25636,64,76,'paragraph',NULL,'(5.5)','[{"s": "(5.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25637,64,77,'paragraph',NULL,'In SI unit force is one that causes an acceleration of 1 m s -2 to a mass of 1 kg. This unit is known as newton : 1 N = 1 kg m s -2 .','[{"s": "In SI unit force is one that causes an acceleration of 1 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to a mass of 1 kg. This unit is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "newton", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": 1 N = 1 kg m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25638,64,78,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us note at this stage some important points about the second law :','[{"s": "Let us note at this stage some important points about the second law :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25639,64,79,'paragraph',NULL,'1. In the second law, F = 0 implies a = 0. The second law is obviously consistent with the first law.','[{"s": "1. In the second law,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 implies", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0. The second law is obviously consistent with the first law.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25641,64,81,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image56.png'),
(25642,64,82,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image58.png'),
(25643,64,83,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image60.png'),
(25644,64,84,'paragraph',NULL,'(5.6)','[{"s": "(5.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25631,64,71,'paragraph',NULL,'i.e the Second Law can also be written as','[{"s": "i.e the Second Law can also be written as", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000032978", "2000034489", "2000035429", "2000107588"]',NULL),
(25645,64,85,'paragraph',NULL,'This means that if a force is not parallel to the velocity of the body, but makes some angle with it, it changes only the component of velocity along the direction of force. The component of velocity normal to the force remains unchanged. For example, in the motion of a projectile under the vertical gravitational force, the horizontal component of velocity remains unchanged (Fig. 5.5).','[{"s": "This means that if a force is not parallel to the velocity of the body, but makes some angle with it, it changes only the component of velocity along the direction of force. The component of velocity normal to the force remains unchanged. For example, in the motion of a projectile under the vertical gravitational force, the horizontal component of velocity remains unchanged (Fig. 5.5).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25646,64,86,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The second law of motion given by Eq. (5.5) is applicable to a single point particle. The force F in the law stands for the net external force on the particle and a stands for acceleration of the particle. It turns out, however, that the law in the same form applies to a rigid body or, even more generally, to a system of particles. In that case, F refers to the total external force on the system and a refers to the acceleration of the system as a whole. More precisely, a is the acceleration of the centre of mass of the system about which we shall study in detail in chapter 7. Any internal forces in the system are not to be included in F .','[{"s": "3. The second law of motion given by Eq. (5.5) is applicable to a single point particle. The force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the law stands for the net external force on the particle and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "stands for acceleration of the particle. It turns out, however, that the law in the same form applies to a rigid body or, even more generally, to a system of particles. In that case,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "refers to the total external force on the system and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "refers to the acceleration of the system as a whole. More precisely,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the acceleration of the centre of mass of the system about which we shall study in detail in chapter 7.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Any internal forces in the system are not to be included in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25647,64,87,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image62.png'),
(25648,64,88,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 5.5 Acceleration at an instant is determined by the force at that instant. The moment after a stone is dropped out of an accelerated train, it has no horizontal acceleration or force, if air resistance is neglected. The stone carries no memory of its acceleration with the train a moment ago.','[{"s": "Fig. 5.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Acceleration at an instant is determined by the force at that instant. The moment after a stone is dropped out of an accelerated train, it has no horizontal acceleration or force, if air resistance is neglected. The stone carries no memory of its acceleration with the train a moment ago.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25649,64,89,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The second law of motion is a local relation which means that force F at a point in space (location of the particle) at a certain instant of time is related to a at that point at that instant. Acceleration here and now is determined by the force here and now, not by any history of the motion of the particle (See Fig. 5.5) .','[{"s": "4. The second law of motion is a local relation which means that force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at a point in space (location of the particle) at a certain instant of time is related to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at that point at that instant. Acceleration here and now is determined by the force here and now,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "not by any history of the motion of the particle (See Fig. 5.5)", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25650,64,90,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.2 A bullet of mass 0.04 kg moving with a speed of 90 m s –1 enters a heavy wooden block and is stopped after a distance of 60 cm. What is the average resistive force exerted by the block on the bullet?','[{"s": "Example 5.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A bullet of mass 0.04 kg moving with a speed of 90 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "enters a heavy wooden block and is stopped after a distance of 60 cm. What is the average resistive force exerted by the block on the bullet?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25651,64,91,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The retardation ‘ a ’ of the bullet (assumed constant) is given by','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The retardation ‘", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "’ of the bullet (assumed constant) is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25652,64,92,'paragraph',NULL,'Laws_of_Motion-image63.png = Laws_of_Motion-image64.png','[{"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image63.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image64.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25653,64,93,'paragraph',NULL,'The retarding force, by the second law of motion, is','[{"s": "The retarding force, by the second law of motion, is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25654,64,94,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.04 kg × 6750 m s -2 = 270 N','[{"s": "= 0.04 kg × 6750 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 270 N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25655,64,95,'paragraph',NULL,'The actual resistive force, and therefore, retardation of the bullet may not be uniform. The answer therefore, only indicates the average resistive force.','[{"s": "The actual resistive force, and therefore, retardation of the bullet may not be uniform. The answer therefore, only indicates the average resistive force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25656,64,96,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.3 The motion of a particle of mass m is described by y = Laws_of_Motion-image65.png . Find the force acting on the particle.','[{"s": "Example 5.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The motion of a particle of mass m is described by y =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image65.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". Find the force acting on the particle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25657,64,97,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer We know','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We know", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25658,64,98,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image21.png'),
(25659,64,99,'paragraph',NULL,'Now,','[{"s": "Now,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25660,64,100,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image23.png'),
(25661,64,101,'paragraph',NULL,'acceleration, Laws_of_Motion-image25.png','[{"s": "acceleration,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image25.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25662,64,102,'paragraph',NULL,'Then the force is given by Eq. (5.5)','[{"s": "Then the force is given by Eq. (5.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25663,64,103,'paragraph',NULL,'F = ma = mg','[{"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ma", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mg", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25664,64,104,'paragraph',NULL,'y is the position coordinate in the direction of g .','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the position coordinate in the direction of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25665,64,105,'paragraph',NULL,'Impulse','[{"s": "Impulse", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25669,64,109,'paragraph',NULL,'A large force acting for a short time to produce a finite change in momentum is called an impulsive force . In the history of science, impulsive forces were put in a conceptually different category from ordinary forces. Newtonian mechanics has no such distinction. Impulsive force is like any other force – except that it is large and acts for a short time.','[{"s": "A large force acting for a short time to produce a finite change in momentum is called an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "impulsive force", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In the history of science, impulsive forces were put in a conceptually different category from ordinary forces. Newtonian mechanics has no such distinction. Impulsive force is like any other force – except that it is large and acts for a short time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25671,64,111,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Change in momentum','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Change in momentum", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25672,64,112,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.15 × 12–(–0.15×12)','[{"s": "= 0.15 × 12–(–0.15×12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25673,64,113,'paragraph',NULL,'= 3.6 N s,','[{"s": "= 3.6 N s,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25674,64,114,'paragraph',NULL,'Impulse = 3.6 N s,','[{"s": "Impulse = 3.6 N s,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25675,64,115,'paragraph',NULL,'in the direction from the batsman to the bowler.','[{"s": "in the direction from the batsman to the bowler.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25676,64,116,'paragraph',NULL,'This is an example where the force on the ball by the batsman and the time of contact of the ball and the bat are difficult to know, but the impulse is readily calculated.','[{"s": "This is an example where the force on the ball by the batsman and the time of contact of the ball and the bat are difficult to know, but the impulse is readily calculated.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25677,64,117,'heading',2,'5.6 NEWTON’S THIRD LAW OF MOTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25678,64,118,'paragraph',NULL,'The second law relates the external force on a body to its acceleration. What is the origin of the external force on the body ? What agency provides the external force ? The simple answer in Newtonian mechanics is that the external force on a body always arises due to some other body. Consider a pair of bodies A and B . B gives rise to an external force on A . A natural question is: Does A in turn give rise to an external force on B ? In some examples, the answer seems clear. If you press a coiled spring, the spring is compressed by the force of your hand. The compressed spring in turn exerts a force on your hand and you can feel it. But what if the bodies are not in contact ? The earth pulls a stone downwards due to gravity. Does the stone exert a force on the earth ? The answer is not obvious since we hardly see the effect of the stone on the earth. The answer according to Newton is: Yes, the stone does exert an equal and opposite force on the earth. We do not notice it since the earth is very massive and the effect of a small force on its motion is negligible.','[{"s": "The second law relates the external force on a body to its acceleration. What is the origin of the external force on the body ? What agency provides the external force ? The simple answer in Newtonian mechanics is that the external force on a body always arises due to some other body. Consider a pair of bodies", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gives rise to an external force on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". A natural question is: Does", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in turn give rise to an external force on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "? In some examples, the answer seems clear. If you press a coiled spring, the spring is compressed by the force of your hand. The compressed spring in turn exerts a force on your hand and you can feel it. But what if the bodies are not in contact ? The earth pulls a stone downwards due to gravity. Does the stone exert a force on the earth ? The answer is not obvious since we hardly see the effect of the stone on the earth. The answer according to Newton is: Yes, the stone does exert an equal and opposite force on the earth. We do not notice it since the earth is very massive and the effect of a small force on its motion is negligible.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25667,64,107,'paragraph',NULL,'Impulse = Force × time duration','[{"s": "Impulse = Force × time duration", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000033094", "2000034554", "2000107554", "2000107596"]',NULL),
(25668,64,108,'paragraph',NULL,'= Change in momentum (5.7)','[{"s": "= Change in momentum", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(5.7)", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000033116", "2000034557", "2000107555", "2000107598"]',NULL),
(25679,64,119,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, according to Newtonian mechanics, force never occurs singly in nature. Force is the mutual interaction between two bodies. Forces always occur in pairs. Further, the mutual forces between two bodies are always equal and opposite. This idea was expressed by Newton in the form of the third law of motion .','[{"s": "Thus, according to Newtonian mechanics, force never occurs singly in nature. Force is the mutual interaction between two bodies. Forces always occur in pairs. Further, the mutual forces between two bodies are always equal and opposite. This idea was expressed by Newton in the form of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "third law of motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25680,64,120,'paragraph',NULL,'To every action, there is always an equal and opposite reaction .','[{"s": "To every action, there is always an equal and opposite reaction", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25681,64,121,'paragraph',NULL,'Newton’s wording of the third law is so crisp and beautiful that it has become a part of common language. For the same reason perhaps, misconceptions about the third law abound. Let us note some important points about the third law, particularly in regard to the usage of the terms : action and reaction .','[{"s": "Newton’s wording of the third law is so crisp and beautiful that it has become a part of common language. For the same reason perhaps, misconceptions about the third law abound. Let us note some important points about the third law, particularly in regard to the usage of the terms : action and reaction", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25682,64,122,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The terms action and reaction in the third law mean nothing else but ‘force’. Using different terms for the same physical concept can sometimes be confusing. A simple and clear way of stating the third law is as follows:','[{"s": "1. The terms action and reaction in the third law mean nothing else but ‘force’. Using different terms for the same physical concept can sometimes be confusing. A simple and clear way of stating the third law is as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25683,64,123,'paragraph',NULL,'Forces always occur in pairs. Force on a body A by B is equal and opposite to the force on the body B by A.','[{"s": "Forces always occur in pairs. Force on a body A by B is equal and opposite to the force on the body B by A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25684,64,124,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The terms action and reaction in the third law may give a wrong impression that action comes before reaction i.e action is the cause and reaction the effect. There is no cause-effect relation implied in the third law. The force on A by B and the force on B by A act at the same instant. By the same reasoning, any one of them may be called action and the other reaction.','[{"s": "2. The terms action and reaction in the third law may give a wrong impression that action comes before reaction i.e action is the cause and reaction the effect.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "There is no cause-effect relation implied in the third law. The force on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the force on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "act at the same instant.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "By the same reasoning, any one of them may be called action and the other reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25685,64,125,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Action and reaction forces act on different bodies, not on the same body. Consider a pair of bodies A and B . According to the third law,','[{"s": "3. Action and reaction forces act on different bodies, not on the same body. Consider a pair of bodies", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". According to the third law,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25686,64,126,'paragraph',NULL,'F AB = – F BA (5.8)','[{"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(5.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25687,64,127,'paragraph',NULL,'(force on A by B ) = – (force on B by A )','[{"s": "(force on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = – (force on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25688,64,128,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus if we are considering the motion of any one body ( A or B ), only one of the two forces is relevant. It is an error to add up the two forces and claim that the net force is zero.','[{"s": "Thus if we are considering the motion of any one body (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), only one of the two forces is relevant. It is an error to add up the two forces and claim that the net force is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25689,64,129,'paragraph',NULL,'However, if you are considering the system of two bodies as a whole, F AB and F BA are internal forces of the system ( A + B ). They add up to give a null force. Internal forces in a body or a system of particles thus cancel away in pairs. This is an important fact that enables the second law to be applicable to a body or a system of particles (See Chapter 7).','[{"s": "However, if you are considering the system of two bodies as a whole,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are internal forces of the system (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A + B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). They add up to give a null force. Internal forces in a body or a system of particles thus cancel away in pairs. This is an important fact that enables the second law to be applicable to a body or a system of particles (See Chapter 7).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25690,64,130,'heading',4,'Issac Newton(1642 - 1727)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25691,64,131,'image',NULL,'newton',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image27.png'),
(34856,80,289,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 15.13 shows the first six harmonics of a stretched string fixed at either end. A string need not vibrate in one of these modes only. Generally, the vibration of a string will be a superposition of different modes; some modes may be more strongly excited and some less. Musical instruments like sitar or violin are based on this principle. Where the string is plucked or bowed, determines which modes are more prominent than others.','[{"s": "Fig. 15.13 shows the first six harmonics of a stretched string fixed at either end. A string need not vibrate in one of these modes only. Generally, the vibration of a string will be a superposition of different modes; some modes may be more strongly excited and some less. Musical instruments like sitar or violin are based on this principle. Where the string is plucked or bowed, determines which modes are more prominent than others.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25692,64,132,'paragraph',NULL,'Isaac Newton was born in Woolsthorpe, England in 1642, the year Galileo died. His extraordinary mathematical ability and mechanical aptitude remained hidden from others in his school life. In 1662, he went to Cambridge for undergraduate studies. A plague epidemic in 1665 forced the university town to close and Newton had to return to his mother’s farm. There in two years of solitude, his dormant creativity blossomed in a deluge of fundamental discoveries in mathematics and physics : binomial theorem for negative and fractional exponents, the beginning of calculus, the inverse square law of gravitation, the spectrum of white light, and so on. Returning to Cambridge, he pursued his investigations in optics and devised a reflecting telescope.','[{"s": "Isaac Newton was born in Woolsthorpe, England in 1642, the year Galileo died. His extraordinary mathematical ability and mechanical aptitude remained hidden from others in his school life. In 1662, he went to Cambridge for undergraduate studies. A plague epidemic in 1665 forced the university town to close and Newton had to return to his mother’s farm. There in two years of solitude, his dormant creativity blossomed in a deluge of fundamental discoveries in mathematics and physics : binomial theorem for negative and fractional exponents, the beginning of calculus, the inverse square law of gravitation, the spectrum of white light, and so on. Returning to Cambridge, he pursued his investigations in optics and devised a reflecting telescope.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25693,64,133,'paragraph',NULL,'In 1684, encouraged by his friend Edmund Halley, Newton embarked on writing what was to be one of the greatest scientific works ever published : The Principia Mathematica. In it, he enunciated the three laws of motion and the universal law of gravitation, which explained all the three Kepler’s laws of planetary motion. The book was packed with a host of path-breaking achievements : basic principles of fluid mechanics, mathematics of wave motion, calculation of masses of the earth, the sun and other planets, explanation of the precession of equinoxes, theory of tides, etc. In 1704, Newton brought out another masterpiece Opticks that summarized his work on light and colour.','[{"s": "In 1684, encouraged by his friend Edmund Halley, Newton embarked on writing what was to be one of the greatest scientific works ever published : The Principia Mathematica. In it, he enunciated the three laws of motion and the universal law of gravitation, which explained all the three Kepler’s laws of planetary motion. The book was packed with a host of path-breaking achievements : basic principles of fluid mechanics, mathematics of wave motion, calculation of masses of the earth, the sun and other planets, explanation of the precession of equinoxes, theory of tides, etc. In 1704, Newton brought out another masterpiece Opticks that summarized his work on light and colour.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25694,64,134,'paragraph',NULL,'The scientific revolution triggered by Copernicus and steered vigorously ahead by Kepler and Galileo was brought to a grand completion by Newton. Newtonian mechanics unified terrestrial and celestial phenomena. The same mathematical equation governed the fall of an apple to the ground and the motion of the moon around the earth. The age of reason had dawned.','[{"s": "The scientific revolution triggered by Copernicus and steered vigorously ahead by Kepler and Galileo was brought to a grand completion by Newton. Newtonian mechanics unified terrestrial and celestial phenomena. The same mathematical equation governed the fall of an apple to the ground and the motion of the moon around the earth. The age of reason had dawned.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25695,64,135,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.5 Two identical billiard balls strike a rigid wall with the same speed but at different angles, and get reflected without any change in speed, as shown in Fig. 5.6. What is (i) the direction of the force on the wall due to each ball? (ii) the ratio of the magnitudes of impulses imparted to the balls by the wall ?','[{"s": "Example 5.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two identical billiard balls strike a rigid wall with the same speed but at different angles, and get reflected without any change in speed, as shown in Fig. 5.6. What is (i) the direction of the force on the wall due to each ball? (ii) the ratio of the magnitudes of impulses imparted to the balls by the wall ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25696,64,136,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image29.png'),
(25697,64,137,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 5.6','[{"s": "Fig. 5.6", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25698,64,138,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer An instinctive answer to (i) might be that the force on the wall in case (a) is normal to the wall, while that in case (b) is inclined at 30° to the normal. This answer is wrong. The force on the wall is normal to the wall in both cases.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An instinctive answer to (i) might be that the force on the wall in case (a) is normal to the wall, while that in case (b) is inclined at 30° to the normal. This answer is wrong. The force on the wall is normal to the wall in both cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25700,64,140,'paragraph',NULL,'Case (a)','[{"s": "Case (a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
