(25808,64,248,'paragraph',NULL,'These equations give a = Laws_of_Motion-image41.png m s –2 = 0.96 m s -2 and T = 27.1 N.','[{"s": "These equations give", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image41.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.96 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 27.1 N.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25809,64,249,'paragraph',NULL,'Rolling friction','[{"s": "Rolling friction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25810,64,250,'paragraph',NULL,'A body like a ring or a sphere rolling without slipping over a horizontal plane will suffer no friction, in principle. At every instant, there is just one point of contact between the body and the plane and this point has no motion relative to the plane. In this ideal situation, kinetic or static friction is zero and the body should continue to roll with constant velocity. We know, in practice, this will not happen and some resistance to motion (rolling friction) does occur, i.e. to keep the body rolling, some applied force is needed. For the same weight, rolling friction is much smaller (even by 2 or 3 orders of magnitude) than static or sliding friction. This is the reason why discovery of the wheel has been a major milestone in human history.','[{"s": "A body like a ring or a sphere rolling without slipping over a horizontal plane will suffer no friction, in principle. At every instant, there is just one point of contact between the body and the plane and this point has no motion relative to the plane. In this ideal situation, kinetic or static friction is zero and the body should continue to roll with constant velocity. We know, in practice, this will not happen and some resistance to motion (rolling friction) does occur, i.e. to keep the body rolling, some applied force is needed. For the same weight, rolling friction is much smaller (even by 2 or 3 orders of magnitude) than static or sliding friction. This is the reason why discovery of the wheel has been a major milestone in human history.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25811,64,251,'paragraph',NULL,'Rolling friction again has a complex origin, though somewhat different from that of static and sliding friction. During rolling, the surfaces in contact get momentarily deformed a little, and this results in a finite area (not a point) of the body being in contact with the surface. The net effect is that the component of the contact force parallel to the surface opposes motion.','[{"s": "Rolling friction again has a complex origin, though somewhat different from that of static and sliding friction. During rolling, the surfaces in contact get momentarily deformed a little, and this results in a finite area (not a point) of the body being in contact with the surface. The net effect is that the component of the contact force parallel to the surface opposes motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25812,64,252,'paragraph',NULL,'We often regard friction as something undesirable. In many situations, like in a machine with different moving parts, friction does have a negative role. It opposes relative motion and thereby dissipates power in the form of heat, etc. Lubricants are a way of reducing kinetic friction in a machine. Another way is to use ball bearings between two moving parts of a machine [Fig. 5.13(a)]. Since the rolling friction between ball bearings and the surfaces in contact is very small, power dissipation is reduced. A thin cushion of air maintained between solid surfaces in relative motion is another effective way of reducing friction (Fig. 5.13(a)).','[{"s": "We often regard friction as something undesirable. In many situations, like in a machine with different moving parts, friction does have a negative role. It opposes relative motion and thereby dissipates power in the form of heat, etc. Lubricants are a way of reducing kinetic friction in a machine. Another way is to use ball bearings between two moving parts of a machine [Fig. 5.13(a)]. Since the rolling friction between ball bearings and the surfaces in contact is very small, power dissipation is reduced. A thin cushion of air maintained between solid surfaces in relative motion is another effective way of reducing friction (Fig. 5.13(a)).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25813,64,253,'paragraph',NULL,'In many practical situations, however, friction is critically needed. Kinetic friction that dissipates power is nevertheless important for quickly stopping relative motion. It is made use of by brakes in machines and automobiles. Similarly, static friction is important in daily life. We are able to walk because of friction. It is impossible for a car to move on a very slippery road. On an ordinary road, the friction between the tyres and the road provides the necessary external force to accelerate the car.','[{"s": "In many practical situations, however, friction is critically needed. Kinetic friction that dissipates power is nevertheless important for quickly stopping relative motion. It is made use of by brakes in machines and automobiles. Similarly, static friction is important in daily life. We are able to walk because of friction. It is impossible for a car to move on a very slippery road. On an ordinary road, the friction between the tyres and the road provides the necessary external force to accelerate the car.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25814,64,254,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image44.png'),
(25815,64,255,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 5.13 Some ways of reducing friction. (a) Ball bearings placed between moving parts of a machine. (b) Compressed cushion of air between surfaces in relative motion.','[{"s": "Fig. 5.13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some ways of reducing friction. (a) Ball bearings placed between moving parts of a machine. (b) Compressed cushion of air between surfaces in relative motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25816,64,256,'heading',2,'5.10 CIRCULAR MOTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25817,64,257,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen in Chapter 4 that acceleration of a body moving in a circle of radius R with uniform speed v is v 2 / R directed towards the centre. According to the second law, the force f c providing this acceleration is :','[{"s": "We have seen in Chapter 4 that acceleration of a body moving in a circle of radius", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with uniform speed", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "directed towards the centre. According to the second law, the force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "providing this acceleration is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25818,64,258,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image45.png'),
(25819,64,259,'paragraph',NULL,'(5.16)','[{"s": "(5.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25820,64,260,'paragraph',NULL,'where m is the mass of the body. This force directed forwards the centre is called the centripetal force. For a stone rotated in a circle by a string, the centripetal force is provided by the tension in the string. The centripetal force for motion of a planet around the sun is the gravitational force on the planet due to the sun. For a car taking a circular turn on a horizontal road, the centripetal force is the force of friction.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the mass of the body. This force directed forwards the centre is called the centripetal force. For a stone rotated in a circle by a string, the centripetal force is provided by the tension in the string. The centripetal force for motion of a planet around the sun is the gravitational force on the planet due to the sun. For a car taking a circular turn on a horizontal road, the centripetal force is the force of friction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25821,64,261,'paragraph',NULL,'The circular motion of a car on a flat and banked road give interesting application of the laws of motion.','[{"s": "The circular motion of a car on a flat and banked road give interesting application of the laws of motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25822,64,262,'paragraph',NULL,'Motion of a car on a level road','[{"s": "Motion of a car on a level road", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25823,64,263,'paragraph',NULL,'Three forces act on the car (Fig. 5.14(a):','[{"s": "Three forces act on the car (Fig. 5.14(a):", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25824,64,264,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The weight of the car, mg','[{"s": "(i) The weight of the car,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mg", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25825,64,265,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Normal reaction, N','[{"s": "(ii) Normal reaction,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25826,64,266,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Frictional force, f','[{"s": "(iii) Frictional force,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25827,64,267,'paragraph',NULL,'As there is no acceleration in the vertical direction','[{"s": "As there is no acceleration in the vertical direction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25828,64,268,'paragraph',NULL,'N – mg = 0','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25829,64,269,'paragraph',NULL,'N = mg (5.17)','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(5.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25830,64,270,'paragraph',NULL,'The centripetal force required for circular motion is along the surface of the road, and is provided by the component of the contact force between road and the car tyres along the surface. This by definition is the frictional force. Note that it is the static friction that provides the centripetal acceleration. Static friction opposes the impending motion of the car moving away from the circle. Using equation (5.14) & (5.16) we get the result','[{"s": "The centripetal force required for circular motion is along the surface of the road, and is provided by the component of the contact force between road and the car tyres along the surface. This by definition is the frictional force. Note that it is the static friction that provides the centripetal acceleration. Static friction opposes the impending motion of the car moving away from the circle. Using equation (5.14) & (5.16) we get the result", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25831,64,271,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image47.png'),
(25832,64,272,'paragraph',NULL,'Laws_of_Motion-image50.png [ ∵ N = mg ]','[{"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image50.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[ ∵", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25833,64,273,'paragraph',NULL,'which is independent of the mass of the car.','[{"s": "which is independent of the mass of the car.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25834,64,274,'paragraph',NULL,'This shows that for a given value of µ s and R , there is a maximum speed of circular motion of the car possible, namely','[{"s": "This shows that for a given value of µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", there is a maximum speed of circular motion of the car possible, namely", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25835,64,275,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image52.png'),
(25836,64,276,'paragraph',NULL,'(5.18)','[{"s": "(5.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25837,64,277,'paragraph',NULL,'Motion of a car on a banked road','[{"s": "Motion of a car on a banked road", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25838,64,278,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-08 at 11.50.23 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image55.png'),
(25839,64,279,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 5.14 Circular motion of a car on (a) a level road, (b) a banked road.','[{"s": "Fig. 5.14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Circular motion of a car on (a) a level road, (b) a banked road.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(25841,64,281,'paragraph',NULL,'N cos θ = mg + f sin θ (5.19a)','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos θ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin θ (5.19a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25842,64,282,'paragraph',NULL,'The centripetal force is provided by the horizontal components of N and f .','[{"s": "The centripetal force is provided by the horizontal components of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25843,64,283,'paragraph',NULL,'N sin θ + f cos θ = Laws_of_Motion-image57.png','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin θ +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos θ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image57.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25844,64,284,'paragraph',NULL,'(5.19b)','[{"s": "(5.19b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25845,64,285,'paragraph',NULL,'But f ≤ µ sN','[{"s": "But", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f ≤ µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sN", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25846,64,286,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus to obtain v max we put','[{"s": "Thus to obtain", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "max", "t": "text"}, {"s": "we put", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25847,64,287,'paragraph',NULL,'f = µ s N.','[{"s": "f = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25848,64,288,'paragraph',NULL,'Then Eqs. (5.19a) and (5.19b) become','[{"s": "Then Eqs. (5.19a) and (5.19b) become", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25849,64,289,'paragraph',NULL,'N cos θ = mg + µ s N sin θ (5.20a)','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos θ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N sin θ (5.20a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25850,64,290,'paragraph',NULL,'N sin θ + µ s N cos θ= mv 2 /R (5.20b)','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin θ + µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N cos θ=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mv", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(5.20b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25851,64,291,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eq. (5.20a), we obtain','[{"s": "From Eq. (5.20a), we obtain", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25852,64,292,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image59.png'),
(25853,64,293,'paragraph',NULL,'Substituting value of N in Eq . (5.20b), we get','[{"s": "Substituting value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in Eq", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(5.20b), we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25854,64,294,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image61.png'),
(25855,64,295,'paragraph',NULL,'or Laws_of_Motion-image40.png','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image40.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25856,64,296,'paragraph',NULL,'(5.21)','[{"s": "(5.21)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25857,64,297,'paragraph',NULL,'Comparing this with Eq. (5.18) we see that maximum possible speed of a car on a banked road is greater than that on a flat road.','[{"s": "Comparing this with Eq. (5.18) we see that maximum possible speed of a car on a banked road is greater than that on a flat road.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25858,64,298,'paragraph',NULL,'For µ s = 0 in Eq. (5.21 ),','[{"s": "For µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in Eq. (5.21 ),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25859,64,299,'paragraph',NULL,'v o = ( R g tan θ ) ½ (5.22)','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "( R g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tan θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "½", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(5.22)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25860,64,300,'paragraph',NULL,'At this speed, frictional force is not needed at all to provide the necessary centripetal force. Driving at this speed on a banked road will cause little wear and tear of the tyres. The same equation also tells you that for v < v o , frictional force will be up the slope and that a car can be parked only if tan θ ≤ µ s .','[{"s": "At this speed, frictional force is not needed at all to provide the necessary centripetal force. Driving at this speed on a banked road will cause little wear and tear of the tyres. The same equation also tells you that for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v < v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", frictional force will be up the slope and that a car can be parked only if tan θ ≤ µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25861,64,301,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.10 A cyclist speeding at 18 km/h on a level road takes a sharp circular turn of radius 3 m without reducing the speed. The co-efficient of static friction between the tyres and the road is 0.1. Will the cyclist slip while taking the turn?','[{"s": "Example 5.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A cyclist speeding at 18 km/h on a level road takes a sharp circular turn of radius 3 m without reducing the speed. The co-efficient of static friction between the tyres and the road is 0.1. Will the cyclist slip while taking the turn?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25862,64,302,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer On an unbanked road, frictional force alone can provide the centripetal force needed to keep the cyclist moving on a circular turn without slipping. If the speed is too large, or if the turn is too sharp (i.e. of too small a radius) or both, the frictional force is not sufficient to provide the necessary centripetal force, and the cyclist slips. The condition for the cyclist not to slip is given by Eq. (5.18) :','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "On an unbanked road, frictional force alone can provide the centripetal force needed to keep the cyclist moving on a circular turn without slipping. If the speed is too large, or if the turn is too sharp (i.e. of too small a radius) or both, the frictional force is not sufficient to provide the necessary centripetal force, and the cyclist slips. The condition for the cyclist not to slip is given by Eq. (5.18) :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25863,64,303,'paragraph',NULL,'v 2 ≤ µs R g','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≤ µs", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R g", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25864,64,304,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, R = 3 m , g = 9.8 m s -2 , µs = 0.1. That is, µs R g = 2.94 m 2 s -2 . v = 18 km/h = 5 m s -1 ; i.e., v 2 = 25 m 2 s -2 . The condition is not obeyed. The cyclist will slip while taking the circular turn.','[{"s": "Now,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3 m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 9.8 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", µs = 0.1. That is, µs", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.94 m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". v =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "18 km/h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ";", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i.e.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 25 m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The condition is not obeyed. The cyclist will slip while taking the circular turn.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25866,64,306,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer On a banked road, the horizontal component of the normal force and the frictional force contribute to provide centripetal force to keep the car moving on a circular turn without slipping. At the optimum speed, the normal reaction’s component is enough to provide the needed centripetal force, and the frictional force is not needed. The optimum speed v o is given by Eq. (5.22):','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "On a banked road, the horizontal component of the normal force and the frictional force contribute to provide centripetal force to keep the car moving on a circular turn without slipping. At the optimum speed, the normal reaction’s component is enough to provide the needed centripetal force, and the frictional force is not needed. The optimum speed", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is given by Eq. (5.22):", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25867,64,307,'paragraph',NULL,'v o = (R g tan θ ) 1/2','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (R g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tan θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25868,64,308,'paragraph',NULL,'Here R = 300 m , θ = 15°, g = 9.8 m s -2 ; we have','[{"s": "Here", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 300 m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "15°,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9.8 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25869,64,309,'paragraph',NULL,'v o = 28.1 m s -1 .','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "28.1 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25870,64,310,'paragraph',NULL,'The maximum permissible speed v max is given by Eq. (5.21):','[{"s": "The maximum permissible speed", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "max", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is given by Eq. (5.21):", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25871,64,311,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image18.png'),
(25872,64,312,'heading',2,'5.11 SOLVING PROBLEMS IN MECHANICS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25874,64,314,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Draw a diagram showing schematically the various parts of the assembly of bodies, the links, supports, etc.','[{"s": "(i) Draw a diagram showing schematically the various parts of the assembly of bodies, the links, supports, etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25875,64,315,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Choose a convenient part of the assembly as one system.','[{"s": "(ii) Choose a convenient part of the assembly as one system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25865,64,305,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.11 A circular racetrack of radius 300 m is banked at an angle of 15°. If the coefficient of friction between the wheels of a race-car and the road is 0.2, what is the (a) optimum speed of the race-car to avoid wear and tear on its tyres, and (b) maximum permissible speed to avoid slipping ?','[{"s": "Example 5.11", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A circular racetrack of radius 300 m is banked at an angle of 15°.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "If the coefficient of friction between the wheels of a race-car and the road is 0.2, what is the (a) optimum speed of the race-car to avoid wear and tear on its tyres, and (b) maximum permissible speed to avoid slipping ?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034162", "2000035378", "2000107573"]',NULL),
(25876,64,316,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Draw a separate diagram which shows this system and all the forces on the system by the remaining part of the assembly. Include also the forces on the system by other agencies. Do not include the forces on the environment by the system . A diagram of this type is known as ‘a free-body diagram’. (Note this does not imply that the system under consideration is without a net force).','[{"s": "(iii) Draw a separate diagram which shows this system and all the forces on the system by the remaining part of the assembly. Include also the forces on the system by other agencies.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Do not include the forces on the environment by the system", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". A diagram of this type is known as ‘a free-body diagram’. (Note this does not imply that the system under consideration is without a net force).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25877,64,317,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) In a free-body diagram, include information about forces (their magnitudes and directions) that are either given or you are sure of (e.g., the direction of tension in a string along its length). The rest should be treated as unknowns to be determined using laws of motion.','[{"s": "(iv) In a free-body diagram, include information about forces (their magnitudes and directions) that are either given or you are sure of (e.g., the direction of tension in a string along its length). The rest should be treated as unknowns to be determined using laws of motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25878,64,318,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) If necessary, follow the same procedure for another choice of the system. In doing so, employ Newton’s third law. That is, if in the free-body diagram of A , the force on A due to B is shown as F, then in the free-body diagram of B , the force on B due to A should be shown as – F.','[{"s": "(v) If necessary, follow the same procedure for another choice of the system. In doing so, employ Newton’s third law. That is, if in the free-body diagram of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the force on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "due to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is shown as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "then in the free-body diagram of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the force on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "due to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "should be shown as –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25879,64,319,'paragraph',NULL,'The following example illustrates the above procedure :','[{"s": "The following example illustrates the above procedure :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25880,64,320,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.12 See Fig. 5.15. A wooden block of mass 2 kg rests on a soft horizontal floor. When an iron cylinder of mass 25 kg is placed on top of the block, the floor yields steadily and the block and the cylinder together go down with an acceleration of 0.1 m s –2 . What is the action of the block on the floor (a) before and (b) after the floor yields ? Take g = 10 m s –2 . Identify the action-reaction pairs in the problem.','[{"s": "Example 5.12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "See Fig. 5.15. A wooden block of mass 2 kg rests on a soft horizontal floor. When an iron cylinder of mass 25 kg is placed on top of the block, the floor yields steadily and the block and the cylinder together go down with an acceleration of 0.1 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". What is the action of the block on the floor (a) before and (b) after the floor yields ? Take g = 10 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Identify the action-reaction pairs in the problem.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25881,64,321,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25882,64,322,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The block is at rest on the floor. Its free-body diagram shows two forces on the block, the force of gravitational attraction by the earth equal to 2 × 10 = 20 N; and the normal force R of the floor on the block. By the First Law, the net force on the block must be zero i.e., R = 20 N. Using third law the action of the block (i.e. the force exerted on the floor by the block) is equal to 20 N and directed vertically downwards.','[{"s": "(a) The block is at rest on the floor. Its free-body diagram shows two forces on the block, the force of gravitational attraction by the earth equal to 2 × 10 = 20 N; and the normal force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the floor on the block. By the First Law, the net force on the block must be zero i.e.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 20 N. Using third law the action of the block (i.e. the force exerted on the floor by the block) is equal to 20 N and directed vertically downwards.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25884,64,324,'paragraph',NULL,'270 – R ′ = 27 × 0.1N','[{"s": "270 –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ = 27 × 0.1N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25885,64,325,'paragraph',NULL,'ie. R ′ = 267.3 N','[{"s": "ie.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ = 267.3 N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25886,64,326,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image19.png'),
(25887,64,327,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 5.15','[{"s": "Fig. 5.15", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25888,64,328,'paragraph',NULL,'By the third law, the action of the system on the floor is equal to 267.3 N vertically downward.','[{"s": "By the third law, the action of the system on the floor is equal to 267.3 N vertically downward.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25889,64,329,'paragraph',NULL,'Action-reaction pairs','[{"s": "Action-reaction pairs", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25890,64,330,'paragraph',NULL,'For (a): (i) the force of gravity (20 N) on the block by the earth (say, action); the force of gravity on the earth by the block (reaction) equal to 20 N directed upwards (not shown in the figure).','[{"s": "For (a): (i) the force of gravity (20 N) on the block by the earth (say, action); the force of gravity on the earth by the block (reaction) equal to 20 N directed upwards (not shown in the figure).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25891,64,331,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) the force on the floor by the block (action); the force on the block by the floor (reaction).','[{"s": "(ii) the force on the floor by the block (action); the force on the block by the floor (reaction).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25892,64,332,'paragraph',NULL,'For (b): (i) the force of gravity (270 N) on the system by the earth (say, action); the force of gravity on the earth by the system (reaction), equal to 270 N, directed upwards (not shown in the figure).','[{"s": "For (b): (i) the force of gravity (270 N) on the system by the earth (say, action); the force of gravity on the earth by the system (reaction), equal to 270 N, directed upwards (not shown in the figure).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25893,64,333,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) the force on the floor by the system (action); the force on the system by the floor (reaction). In addition, for (b), the force on the block by the cylinder and the force on the cylinder by the block also constitute an action-reaction pair.','[{"s": "(ii) the force on the floor by the system (action); the force on the system by the floor (reaction). In addition, for (b), the force on the block by the cylinder and the force on the cylinder by the block also constitute an action-reaction pair.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25894,64,334,'paragraph',NULL,'The important thing to remember is that an action-reaction pair consists of mutual forces which are always equal and opposite between two bodies. Two forces on the same body which happen to be equal and opposite can never constitute an action-reaction pair. The force of gravity on the mass in (a) or (b) and the normal force on the mass by the floor are not action-reaction pairs. These forces happen to be equal and opposite for (a) since the mass is at rest. They are not so for case (b), as seen already. The weight of the system is 270 N, while the normal force R ′ is 267.3 N.','[{"s": "The important thing to remember is that an action-reaction pair consists of mutual forces which are always equal and opposite between two bodies. Two forces on the same body which happen to be equal and opposite can never constitute an action-reaction pair. The force of gravity on the mass in (a) or (b) and the normal force on the mass by the floor are not action-reaction pairs. These forces happen to be equal and opposite for (a) since the mass is at rest. They are not so for case (b), as seen already. The weight of the system is 270 N, while the normal force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ is 267.3 N.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25895,64,335,'paragraph',NULL,'The practice of drawing free-body diagrams is of great help in solving problems in mechanics. It allows you to clearly define your system and consider all forces on the system due to objects that are not part of the system itself. A number of exercises in this and subsequent chapters will help you cultivate this practice.','[{"s": "The practice of drawing free-body diagrams is of great help in solving problems in mechanics. It allows you to clearly define your system and consider all forces on the system due to objects that are not part of the system itself. A number of exercises in this and subsequent chapters will help you cultivate this practice.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25896,64,336,'heading',2,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25897,64,337,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Aristotle’s view that a force is necessary to keep a body in uniform motion is wrong. A force is necessary in practice to counter the opposing force of friction.','[{"s": "1. Aristotle’s view that a force is necessary to keep a body in uniform motion is wrong. A force is necessary in practice to counter the opposing force of friction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25898,64,338,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Galileo extrapolated simple observations on motion of bodies on inclined planes, and arrived at the law of inertia. Newton’s first law of motion is the same law rephrased thus: “Everybody continues to be in its state of rest or of uniform motion in a straight line, unless compelled by some external force to act otherwise” . In simple terms, the First Law is “If external force on a body is zero, its acceleration is zero” .','[{"s": "2. Galileo extrapolated simple observations on motion of bodies on inclined planes, and arrived at the law of inertia. Newton’s first law of motion is the same law rephrased thus:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "“Everybody continues to be in its state of rest or of uniform motion in a straight line, unless compelled by some external force to act otherwise”", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In simple terms, the First Law is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "“If external force on a body is zero, its acceleration is zero”", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25899,64,339,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Momentum ( p ) of a body is the product of its mass (m) and velocity ( v ) :','[{"s": "3. Momentum (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of a body is the product of its mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(m)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25900,64,340,'paragraph',NULL,'p = m v','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25901,64,341,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Newton’s second law of motion :','[{"s": "4. Newton’s second law of motion :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25903,64,343,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image20.png'),
(25904,64,344,'paragraph',NULL,'where F is the net external force on the body and a its acceleration. We set the constant of proportionality k = 1 in SI units. Then','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the net external force on the body and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "its acceleration. We set the constant of proportionality", "t": "text"}, {"s": "k =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 in SI units. Then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25905,64,345,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image22.png'),
(25906,64,346,'paragraph',NULL,'The SI unit of force is newton : 1 N = 1 kg m s -2 .','[{"s": "The SI unit of force is newton : 1 N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 kg m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25907,64,347,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The second law is consistent with the First Law ( F = 0 implies a = 0)','[{"s": "(a) The second law is consistent with the First Law (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0 implies", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25908,64,348,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) It is a vector equation','[{"s": "(b) It is a vector equation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25909,64,349,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) It is applicable to a particle, and also to a body or a system of particles, provided F is the total external force on the system and a is the acceleration of the system as a whole.','[{"s": "(c) It is applicable to a particle, and also to a body or a system of particles, provided", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the total external force on the system and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the acceleration of the system as a whole.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25910,64,350,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) F at a point at a certain instant determines a at the same point at that instant. That is the Second Law is a local law; a at an instant does not depend on the history of motion.','[{"s": "(d)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at a point at a certain instant determines", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at the same point at that instant. That is the Second Law is a local law;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at an instant does not depend on the history of motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25911,64,351,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Impulse is the product of force and time which equals change in momentum.','[{"s": "5. Impulse is the product of force and time which equals change in momentum.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25912,64,352,'paragraph',NULL,'The notion of impulse is useful when a large force acts for a short time to produce a measurable change in momentum. Since the time of action of the force is very short, one can assume that there is no appreciable change in the position of the body during the action of the impulsive force.','[{"s": "The notion of impulse is useful when a large force acts for a short time to produce a measurable change in momentum. Since the time of action of the force is very short, one can assume that there is no appreciable change in the position of the body during the action of the impulsive force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25913,64,353,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Newton’s third law of motion:','[{"s": "6. Newton’s third law of motion:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25914,64,354,'paragraph',NULL,'To every action, there is always an equal and opposite reaction','[{"s": "To every action, there is always an equal and opposite reaction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25915,64,355,'paragraph',NULL,'In simple terms, the law can be stated thus :','[{"s": "In simple terms, the law can be stated thus :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25916,64,356,'paragraph',NULL,'Forces in nature always occur between pairs of bodies. Force on a body A by body B is equal and opposite to the force on the body B by A.','[{"s": "Forces in nature always occur between pairs of bodies. Force on a body A by body B is equal and opposite to the force on the body B by A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25917,64,357,'paragraph',NULL,'Action and reaction forces are simultaneous forces. There is no cause-effect relation between action and reaction. Any of the two mutual forces can be called action and the other reaction. Action and reaction act on different bodies and so they cannot be cancelled out. The internal action and reaction forces between different parts of a body do, however, sum to zero.','[{"s": "Action and reaction forces are simultaneous forces. There is no cause-effect relation between action and reaction. Any of the two mutual forces can be called action and the other reaction. Action and reaction act on different bodies and so they cannot be cancelled out. The internal action and reaction forces between different parts of a body do, however, sum to zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25918,64,358,'paragraph',NULL,'7. Law of Conservation of Momentum','[{"s": "7.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Law of Conservation of Momentum", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25919,64,359,'paragraph',NULL,'The total momentum of an isolated system of particles is conserved. The law follows from the second and third law of motion.','[{"s": "The total momentum of an isolated system of particles is conserved. The law follows from the second and third law of motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25920,64,360,'paragraph',NULL,'8. Friction','[{"s": "8.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Friction", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25921,64,361,'paragraph',NULL,'Frictional force opposes (impending or actual) relative motion between two surfaces in contact. It is the component of the contact force along the common tangent to the surface in contact. Static friction f s opposes impending relative motion; kinetic friction f k opposes actual relative motion. They are independent of the area of contact and satisfy the following approximate laws :','[{"s": "Frictional force opposes (impending or actual) relative motion between two surfaces in contact. It is the component of the contact force along the common tangent to the surface in contact. Static friction", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s opposes impending relative motion; kinetic friction", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "k opposes actual relative motion. They are independent of the area of contact and satisfy the following approximate laws :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25922,64,362,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image24.png'),
(25923,64,363,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image26.png'),
(25924,64,364,'paragraph',NULL,'µs (co-efficient of static friction) and µk (co-efficient of kinetic friction) are constants characteristic of the pair of surfaces in contact. It is found experimentally that µk is less than µs .','[{"s": "µs (co-efficient of static friction) and µk (co-efficient of kinetic friction) are constants characteristic of the pair of surfaces in contact. It is found experimentally that µk is less than µs .", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25925,64,365,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Laws_of_Motion-image28.png'),
(25926,64,366,'heading',4,'Points to ponder',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25927,64,367,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Force is not always in the direction of motion. Depending on the situation, F may be along v , opposite to v , normal to v or may make some other angle with v . In every case, it is parallel to acceleration.','[{"s": "1. Force is not always in the direction of motion. Depending on the situation,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "may be along", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", opposite to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", normal to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or may make some other angle with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In every case, it is parallel to acceleration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25928,64,368,'paragraph',NULL,'2. If v = 0 at an instant, i.e. if a body is momentarily at rest, it does not mean that force or acceleration are necessarily zero at that instant. For example, when a ball thrown upward reaches its maximum height, v = 0 but the force continues to be its weight mg and the acceleration is not zero but g.','[{"s": "2. If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 at an instant, i.e. if a body is momentarily at rest, it does not mean that force or acceleration are necessarily zero at that instant. For example, when a ball thrown upward reaches its maximum height,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 but the force continues to be its weight mg and the acceleration is not zero but g.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25929,64,369,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Force on a body at a given time is determined by the situation at the location of the body at that time. Force is not ‘carried’ by the body from its earlier history of motion. The moment after a stone is released out of an accelerated train, there is no horizontal force (or acceleration) on the stone, if the effects of the surrounding air are neglected. The stone then has only the vertical force of gravity.','[{"s": "3. Force on a body at a given time is determined by the situation at the location of the body at that time. Force is not ‘carried’ by the body from its earlier history of motion. The moment after a stone is released out of an accelerated train, there is no horizontal force (or acceleration) on the stone, if the effects of the surrounding air are neglected. The stone then has only the vertical force of gravity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25930,64,370,'paragraph',NULL,'4. In the second law of motion F = m a , F stands for the net force due to all material agencies external to the body. a is the effect of the force. m a should not be regarded as yet another force, besides F .','[{"s": "4. In the second law of motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "stands for the net force due to all material agencies external to the body.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the effect of the force. m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "should not be regarded as yet another force, besides", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25931,64,371,'paragraph',NULL,'5. The centripetal force should not be regarded as yet another kind of force. It is simply a name given to the force that provides inward radial acceleration to a body in circular motion. We should always look for some material force like tension, gravitational force, electrical force, friction, etc as the centripetal force in any circular motion.','[{"s": "5. The centripetal force should not be regarded as yet another kind of force. It is simply a name given to the force that provides inward radial acceleration to a body in circular motion. We should always look for some material force like tension, gravitational force, electrical force, friction, etc as the centripetal force in any circular motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25932,64,372,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Static friction is a self-adjusting force up to its limit µ s N (f s ≤ µ s N). Do not put f s = µ s N without being sure that the maximum value of static friction is coming into play.','[{"s": "6. Static friction is a self-adjusting force up to its limit µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N (f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≤ µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N). Do not put f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N without being sure that the maximum value of static friction is coming into play.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25933,64,373,'paragraph',NULL,'7. The familiar equation mg = R for a body on a table is true only if the body is in equilibrium. The two forces mg and R can be different (e.g. a body in an accelerated lift). The equality of mg and R has no connection with the third law.','[{"s": "7. The familiar equation mg = R for a body on a table is true only if the body is in equilibrium. The two forces mg and R can be different (e.g. a body in an accelerated lift). The equality of mg and R has no connection with the third law.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25934,64,374,'paragraph',NULL,'8. The terms ‘action’ and ‘reaction’ in the third Law of Motion simply stand for simultaneous mutual forces between a pair of bodies. Unlike their meaning in ordinary language, action does not precede or cause reaction. Action and reaction act on different bodies.','[{"s": "8. The terms ‘action’ and ‘reaction’ in the third Law of Motion simply stand for simultaneous mutual forces between a pair of bodies. Unlike their meaning in ordinary language, action does not precede or cause reaction. Action and reaction act on different bodies.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25935,64,375,'paragraph',NULL,'9. The different terms like ‘friction’, ‘normal reaction’ ‘tension’, ‘air resistance’, ‘viscous drag’, ‘thrust’, ‘buoyancy’, ‘weight’, ‘centripetal force’ all stand for ‘force’ in different contexts. For clarity, every force and its equivalent terms encountered in mechanics should be reduced to the phrase ‘force on A by B’.','[{"s": "9. The different terms like ‘friction’, ‘normal reaction’ ‘tension’, ‘air resistance’, ‘viscous drag’, ‘thrust’, ‘buoyancy’, ‘weight’, ‘centripetal force’ all stand for ‘force’ in different contexts. For clarity, every force and its equivalent terms encountered in mechanics should be reduced to the phrase ‘force on A by B’.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25936,64,376,'paragraph',NULL,'10. For applying the second law of motion, there is no conceptual distinction between inanimate and animate objects. An animate object such as a human also requires an external force to accelerate. For example, without the external force of friction, we cannot walk on the ground.','[{"s": "10. For applying the second law of motion, there is no conceptual distinction between inanimate and animate objects. An animate object such as a human also requires an external force to accelerate. For example, without the external force of friction, we cannot walk on the ground.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25937,64,377,'paragraph',NULL,'11. The objective concept of force in physics should not be confused with the subjective concept of the ‘feeling of force’. On a merry-go-around, all parts of our body are subject to an inward force, but we have a feeling of being pushed outward – the direction of impending motion.','[{"s": "11. The objective concept of force in physics should not be confused with the subjective concept of the ‘feeling of force’. On a merry-go-around, all parts of our body are subject to an inward force, but we have a feeling of being pushed outward – the direction of impending motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25938,65,0,'heading',2,'Chapter Five',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25939,65,1,'heading',1,'Magnetism and Matter',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25940,65,2,'heading',3,'5.1 INTRODUCTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25941,65,3,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetic phenomena are universal in nature. Vast, distant galaxies, the tiny invisible atoms, humans and beasts all are permeated through and through with a host of magnetic fields from a variety of sources. The earth’s magnetism predates human evolution. The word magnet is derived from the name of an island in Greece called magnesia where magnetic ore deposits were found, as early as 600 BC. Shepherds on this island complained that their wooden shoes (which had nails) at times stayed struck to the ground. Their iron-tipped rods were similarly affected. This attractive property of magnets made it difficult for them to move around.','[{"s": "Magnetic phenomena are universal in nature. Vast, distant galaxies, the tiny invisible atoms, humans and beasts all are permeated through and through with a host of magnetic fields from a variety of sources. The earth’s magnetism predates human evolution. The word magnet is derived from the name of an island in Greece called magnesia where magnetic ore deposits were found, as early as 600 BC. Shepherds on this island complained that their wooden shoes (which had nails) at times stayed struck to the ground. Their iron-tipped rods were similarly affected. This attractive property of magnets made it difficult for them to move around.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25942,65,4,'paragraph',NULL,'The directional property of magnets was also known since ancient times. A thin long piece of a magnet, when suspended freely, pointed in the north-south direction. A similar effect was observed when it was placed on a piece of cork which was then allowed to float in still water. The name lodestone (or loadstone) given to a naturally occurring ore of iron-magnetite means leading stone. The technological exploitation of this property is generally credited to the Chinese. Chinese texts dating 400 BC mention the use of magnetic needles for navigation on ships. Caravans crossing the Gobi desert also employed magnetic needles.','[{"s": "The directional property of magnets was also known since ancient times. A thin long piece of a magnet, when suspended freely, pointed in the north-south direction. A similar effect was observed when it was placed on a piece of cork which was then allowed to float in still water. The name lodestone (or loadstone) given to a naturally occurring ore of iron-magnetite means leading stone. The technological exploitation of this property is generally credited to the Chinese. Chinese texts dating 400 BC mention the use of magnetic needles for navigation on ships. Caravans crossing the Gobi desert also employed magnetic needles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25943,65,5,'paragraph',NULL,'A Chinese legend narrates the tale of the victory of the emperor Huang-ti about four thousand years ago, which he owed to his craftsmen (whom nowadays you would call engineers). These ‘engineers’ built a chariot on which they placed a magnetic figure with arms outstretched. Figure 5.1 is an artist’s description of this chariot. The figure swiveled around so that the finger of the statuette on it always pointed south. With this chariot, Huang-ti’s troops were able to attack the enemy from the rear in thick fog, and to defeat them.','[{"s": "A Chinese legend narrates the tale of the victory of the emperor Huang-ti about four thousand years ago, which he owed to his craftsmen (whom nowadays you would call engineers). These ‘engineers’ built a chariot on which they placed a magnetic figure with arms outstretched. Figure 5.1 is an artist’s description of this chariot. The figure swiveled around so that the finger of the statuette on it always pointed south. With this chariot, Huang-ti’s troops were able to attack the enemy from the rear in thick fog, and to defeat them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25944,65,6,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image49.png'),
(25945,65,7,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.1 The arm of the statuette mounted on the chariot always points south. This is an artist’s sketch of one of the earliest known compasses, thousands of years old.','[{"s": "Figure 5.1 The arm of the statuette mounted on the chariot always points south. This is an artist’s sketch of one of the earliest known compasses, thousands of years old.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25947,65,9,'paragraph',NULL,'In the present chapter, we take a look at magnetism as a subject in its own right.','[{"s": "In the present chapter, we take a look at magnetism as a subject in its own right.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25948,65,10,'paragraph',NULL,'Some of the commonly known ideas regarding magnetism are:','[{"s": "Some of the commonly known ideas regarding magnetism are:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25949,65,11,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The earth behaves as a magnet with the magnetic field pointing approximately from the geographic south to the north.','[{"s": "(i) The earth behaves as a magnet with the magnetic field pointing approximately from the geographic south to the north.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25950,65,12,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) When a bar magnet is freely suspended, it points in the north-south direction. The tip which points to the geographic north is called the north pole and the tip which points to the geographic south is called the south pole of the magnet.','[{"s": "(ii) When a bar magnet is freely suspended, it points in the north-south direction. The tip which points to the geographic north is called the north pole and the tip which points to the geographic south is called the south pole of the magnet.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25951,65,13,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) There is a repulsive force when north poles ( or south poles ) of two magnets are brought close together. Conversely, there is an attractive force between the north pole of one magnet and the south pole of the other.','[{"s": "(iii) There is a repulsive force when north poles ( or south poles ) of two magnets are brought close together. Conversely, there is an attractive force between the north pole of one magnet and the south pole of the other.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25952,65,14,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) We cannot isolate the north, or south pole of a magnet. If a bar magnet is broken into two halves, we get two similar bar magnets with somewhat weaker properties. Unlike electric charges, isolated magnetic north and south poles known as magnetic monopoles do not exist.','[{"s": "(iv) We cannot isolate the north, or south pole of a magnet. If a bar magnet is broken into two halves, we get two similar bar magnets with somewhat weaker properties. Unlike electric charges, isolated magnetic north and south poles known as magnetic monopoles do not exist.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25953,65,15,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) It is possible to make magnets out of iron and its alloys.','[{"s": "(v) It is possible to make magnets out of iron and its alloys.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25954,65,16,'paragraph',NULL,'We begin with a description of a bar magnet and its behaviour in an external magnetic field. We describe Gauss’s law of magnetism. We then follow it up with an account of the earth’s magnetic field. We next describe how materials can be classified on the basis of their magnetic properties. We describe para-, dia-, and ferromagnetism. We conclude with a section on electromagnets and permanent magnets.','[{"s": "We begin with a description of a bar magnet and its behaviour in an external magnetic field. We describe Gauss’s law of magnetism. We then follow it up with an account of the earth’s magnetic field. We next describe how materials can be classified on the basis of their magnetic properties. We describe para-, dia-, and ferromagnetism. We conclude with a section on electromagnets and permanent magnets.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25955,65,17,'heading',2,'5.2 The Bar Magnet',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25956,65,18,'paragraph',NULL,'One of the earliest childhood memories of the famous physicist Albert Einstein was that of a magnet gifted to him by a relative. Einstein was fascinated, and played endlessly with it. He wondered how the magnet could affect objects such as nails or pins placed away from it and not in any way connected to it by a spring or string.','[{"s": "One of the earliest childhood memories of the famous physicist Albert Einstein was that of a magnet gifted to him by a relative. Einstein was fascinated, and played endlessly with it. He wondered how the magnet could affect objects such as nails or pins placed away from it and not in any way connected to it by a spring or string.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25957,65,19,'paragraph',NULL,'We begin our study by examining iron filings sprinkled on a sheet of glass placed over a short bar magnet. The arrangement of iron filings is shown in Fig. 5.2.','[{"s": "We begin our study by examining iron filings sprinkled on a sheet of glass placed over a short bar magnet. The arrangement of iron filings is shown in Fig. 5.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25958,65,20,'paragraph',NULL,'The pattern of iron filings suggests that the magnet has two poles similar to the positive and negative charge of an electric dipole. As mentioned in the introductory section, one pole is designated the North pole and the other, the South pole. When suspended freely, these poles point approximately towards the geographic north and south poles, respectively. A similar pattern of iron filings is observed around a current carrying solenoid.','[{"s": "The pattern of iron filings suggests that the magnet has two poles similar to the positive and negative charge of an electric dipole. As mentioned in the introductory section, one pole is designated the North pole and the other, the South pole. When suspended freely, these poles point approximately towards the geographic north and south poles, respectively. A similar pattern of iron filings is observed around a current carrying solenoid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25959,65,21,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image45.png'),
(25960,65,22,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.2 The arrangement of iron filings surrounding a bar magnet. The pattern mimics magnetic field lines. The pattern suggests that the bar magnet is a magnetic dipole.','[{"s": "Figure 5.2 The arrangement of iron filings surrounding a bar magnet. The pattern mimics magnetic field lines. The pattern suggests that the bar magnet is a magnetic dipole.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25961,65,23,'heading',4,'5.2.1 THE MAGNETIC FIELD LINES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25962,65,24,'paragraph',NULL,'The pattern of iron filings permits us to plot the magnetic field lines*. This is shown both for the bar-magnet and the current-carrying solenoid in Fig. 5.3. For comparison refer to the Chapter 1, Figure 1.17(d). Electric field lines of an electric dipole are also displayed in Fig. 5.3(c). The magnetic field lines are a visual and intuitive realisation of the magnetic field. Their properties are:','[{"s": "The pattern of iron filings permits us to plot the magnetic field lines*. This is shown both for the bar-magnet and the current-carrying solenoid in Fig. 5.3. For comparison refer to the Chapter 1, Figure 1.17(d). Electric field lines of an electric dipole are also displayed in Fig. 5.3(c). The magnetic field lines are a visual and intuitive realisation of the magnetic field. Their properties are:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25963,65,25,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The magnetic field lines of a magnet (or a solenoid) form continuous closed loops. This is unlike the electric dipole where these field lines begin from a positive charge and end on the negative charge or escape to infinity.','[{"s": "(i) The magnetic field lines of a magnet (or a solenoid) form continuous closed loops. This is unlike the electric dipole where these field lines begin from a positive charge and end on the negative charge or escape to infinity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25964,65,26,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) The tangent to the field line at a given point represents the direction of the net magnetic field B at that point.','[{"s": "(ii) The tangent to the field line at a given point represents the direction of the net magnetic field B at that point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25965,65,27,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) The larger the number of field lines crossing per unit area, the stronger is the magnitude of the magnetic field B. In Fig. 5.3(a), B is larger around region ii than in region i .','[{"s": "(iii) The larger the number of field lines crossing per unit area, the stronger is the magnitude of the magnetic field B. In Fig. 5.3(a), B is larger around region ii than in region i .", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25966,65,28,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image1.png'),
(25967,65,29,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.3 The field lines of (a) a bar magnet, (b) a current-carrying finite solenoid and','[{"s": "Figure 5.3 The field lines of (a) a bar magnet, (b) a current-carrying finite solenoid and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25968,65,30,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) electric dipole. At large distances, the field lines are very similar. The curves','[{"s": "(c) electric dipole. At large distances, the field lines are very similar. The curves", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25969,65,31,'paragraph',NULL,'labelled i and ii are closed Gaussian surfaces.','[{"s": "labelled i and ii are closed Gaussian surfaces.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25970,65,32,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) The magnetic field lines do not intersect, for if they did, the direction of the magnetic field would not be unique at the point of intersection.','[{"s": "(iv) The magnetic field lines do not intersect, for if they did, the direction of the magnetic field would not be unique at the point of intersection.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25971,65,33,'paragraph',NULL,'One can plot the magnetic field lines in a variety of ways. One way is to place a small magnetic compass needle at various positions and note its orientation. This gives us an idea of the magnetic field direction at various points in space.','[{"s": "One can plot the magnetic field lines in a variety of ways. One way is to place a small magnetic compass needle at various positions and note its orientation. This gives us an idea of the magnetic field direction at various points in space.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25972,65,34,'paragraph',NULL,'* In some textbooks the magnetic field lines are called magnetic lines of force. This nomenclature is avoided since it can be confusing. Unlike electrostatics the field lines in magnetism do not indicate the direction of the force on a (moving) charge.','[{"s": "* In some textbooks the magnetic field lines are called magnetic lines of force. This nomenclature is avoided since it can be confusing. Unlike electrostatics the field lines in magnetism do not indicate the direction of the force on a (moving) charge.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25973,65,35,'heading',4,'5.2.4 THE ELECTROSTATIC ANALOG',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25974,65,36,'paragraph',NULL,'Comparison of Eqs. (5.2), (5.3) and (5.6) with the corresponding equations for electric dipole (Chapter 1), suggests that magnetic field at large distances due to a bar magnet of magnetic moment m can be obtained from the equation for electric field due to an electric dipole of dipole moment p, by making the following replacements:','[{"s": "Comparison of Eqs. (5.2), (5.3) and (5.6) with the corresponding equations for electric dipole (Chapter 1), suggests that magnetic field at large distances due to a bar magnet of magnetic moment m can be obtained from the equation for electric field due to an electric dipole of dipole moment p, by making the following replacements:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25975,65,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image26.png ,','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image26.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25976,65,38,'paragraph',NULL,'P → m','[{"s": "P → m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
