(25978,65,40,'paragraph',NULL,'In particular, we can write down the equatorial field (B E ) of a bar magnet at a distance r, for r >> l, where l is the size of the magnet:','[{"s": "In particular, we can write down the equatorial field (B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of a bar magnet at a distance r, for r >> l, where l is the size of the magnet:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25979,65,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image61.png (5.7)','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image61.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(5.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25980,65,42,'paragraph',NULL,'Likewise, the axial field (B A ) of a bar magnet for r >> l is:','[{"s": "Likewise, the axial field (B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of a bar magnet for r >> l is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25981,65,43,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image44.png (5.8)','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image44.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(5.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25982,65,44,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (5.8) is just Eq. (5.2) in the vector form. Table 5.1 summarises the analogy between electric and magnetic dipoles.','[{"s": "Equation (5.8) is just Eq. (5.2) in the vector form. Table 5.1 summarises the analogy between electric and magnetic dipoles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25983,65,45,'image',NULL,'19',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image51.jpg'),
(25984,65,46,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.4 What is the magnitude of the equatorial and axial fields due to a bar magnet of length 5.0 cm at a distance of 50 cm from its mid-point? The magnetic moment of the bar magnet is 0.40 A m2, the same as in Example 5.2.','[{"s": "Example 5.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What is the magnitude of the equatorial and axial fields due to a bar magnet of length 5.0 cm at a distance of 50 cm from its mid-point? The magnetic moment of the bar magnet is 0.40 A m2, the same as in Example 5.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25985,65,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution From Eq. (5.7)','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "From Eq. (5.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25986,65,48,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image25.png'),
(25987,65,49,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image13.png'),
(25988,65,50,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image63.png'),
(25989,65,51,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eq. (5.8), Magnetism_and_Matter-image33.png Magnetism_and_Matter-image34.png','[{"s": "From Eq. (5.8),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image33.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image34.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25990,65,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.5 Figure 5.5 shows a small magnetised needle P placed at a point O. The arrow shows the direction of its magnetic moment. The other arrows show different positions (and orientations of the magnetic moment) of another identical magnetised needle Q.','[{"s": "Example 5.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Figure 5.5 shows a small magnetised needle P placed at a point O. The arrow shows the direction of its magnetic moment. The other arrows show different positions (and orientations of the magnetic moment) of another identical magnetised needle Q.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25991,65,53,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) In which configuration the system is not in equilibrium?','[{"s": "(a) In which configuration the system is not in equilibrium?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25992,65,54,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) In which configuration is the system in (i) stable, and (ii) unstable equilibrium?','[{"s": "(b) In which configuration is the system in (i) stable, and (ii) unstable equilibrium?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25993,65,55,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Which configuration corresponds to the lowest potential energy among all the configurations shown?','[{"s": "(c) Which configuration corresponds to the lowest potential energy among all the configurations shown?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25994,65,56,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image15.png'),
(25995,65,57,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.5','[{"s": "Figure 5.5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25996,65,58,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25997,65,59,'paragraph',NULL,'Potential energy of the configuration arises due to the potential energy of one dipole (say, Q) in the magnetic field due to other (P). Use the result that the field due to P is given by the expression [Eqs. (5.7) and (5.8)]:','[{"s": "Potential energy of the configuration arises due to the potential energy of one dipole (say, Q) in the magnetic field due to other (P). Use the result that the field due to P is given by the expression [Eqs. (5.7) and (5.8)]:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25998,65,60,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image66.png (on the normal bisector)','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image66.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(on the normal bisector)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(25999,65,61,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image17.png (on the axis)','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image17.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(on the axis)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26000,65,62,'paragraph',NULL,'where mP is the magnetic moment of the dipole P.','[{"s": "where mP is the magnetic moment of the dipole P.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26001,65,63,'paragraph',NULL,'Equilibrium is stable when m Q is parallel to B P , and unstable when it is anti-parallel to B P .','[{"s": "Equilibrium is stable when m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is parallel to B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and unstable when it is anti-parallel to B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26002,65,64,'paragraph',NULL,'For instance for the configuration Q 3 for which Q is along the perpendicular bisector of the dipole P, the magnetic moment of Q is parallel to the magnetic field at the position 3. Hence Q 3 is stable.','[{"s": "For instance for the configuration Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for which Q is along the perpendicular bisector of the dipole P, the magnetic moment of Q is parallel to the magnetic field at the position 3. Hence Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is stable.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26003,65,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus,','[{"s": "Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26004,65,66,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) PQ 1 and PQ 2','[{"s": "(a) PQ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and PQ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26005,65,67,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) (i) PQ 3 , PQ 6 (stable); (ii) PQ 5 , PQ 4 (unstable)','[{"s": "(b) (i) PQ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", PQ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(stable); (ii) PQ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", PQ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(unstable)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26006,65,68,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) PQ 6','[{"s": "(c) PQ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26007,65,69,'heading',4,'5.2.2 BAR MAGNET AS AN EQUIVALENT SOLENOID',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26008,65,70,'paragraph',NULL,'In the previous chapter, we have explained how a current loop acts as a magnetic dipole (Section 4.10). We mentioned Ampere’s hypothesis that all magnetic phenomena can be explained in terms of circulating currents. Recall that the magnetic dipole moment m associated with a current loop was defined to be m = NIA where N is the number of turns in the loop, I the current and A the area vector (Eq. 4.30).','[{"s": "In the previous chapter, we have explained how a current loop acts as a magnetic dipole (Section 4.10). We mentioned Ampere’s hypothesis that all magnetic phenomena can be explained in terms of circulating currents. Recall that the magnetic dipole moment m associated with a current loop was defined to be m = NIA where N is the number of turns in the loop, I the current and A the area vector (Eq. 4.30).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26009,65,71,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image54.png'),
(26010,65,72,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.4 Calculation of (a) The axial field of a finite solenoid in order to demonstrate its similarity to that of a bar magnet. (b) A magnetic needle in a uniform magnetic field B. The arrangement may be used to determine either B or the magnetic moment m of the needle.','[{"s": "Figure 5.4 Calculation of (a) The axial field of a finite solenoid in order to demonstrate its similarity to that of a bar magnet. (b) A magnetic needle in a uniform magnetic field B. The arrangement may be used to determine either B or the magnetic moment m of the needle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26012,65,74,'paragraph',NULL,'To make this analogy more firm we calculate the axial field of a finite solenoid depicted in Fig. 5.4 (a). We shall demonstrate that at large distances this axial field resembles that of a bar magnet.','[{"s": "To make this analogy more firm we calculate the axial field of a finite solenoid depicted in Fig. 5.4 (a). We shall demonstrate that at large distances this axial field resembles that of a bar magnet.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26013,65,75,'paragraph',NULL,'Let the solenoid of Fig. 5.4(a) consists of n turns per unit length. Let its length be 2l and radius a. We can evaluate the axial field at a point P, at a distance r from the centre O of the solenoid. To do this, consider a circular element of thickness dx of the solenoid at a distance x from its centre. It consists of n dx turns. Let I be the current in the solenoid. In Section 4.6 of the previous chapter we have calculated the magnetic field on the axis of a circular current loop. From Eq. (4.13), the magnitude of the field at point P due to the circular element is','[{"s": "Let the solenoid of Fig. 5.4(a) consists of n turns per unit length. Let its length be 2l and radius a. We can evaluate the axial field at a point P, at a distance r from the centre O of the solenoid. To do this, consider a circular element of thickness dx of the solenoid at a distance x from its centre. It consists of n dx turns. Let I be the current in the solenoid. In Section 4.6 of the previous chapter we have calculated the magnetic field on the axis of a circular current loop. From Eq. (4.13), the magnitude of the field at point P due to the circular element is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26014,65,76,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image58.png'),
(26016,65,78,'paragraph',NULL,'B = μ 0 nIa 2 /2∫ l −l dx/[(r−x) 2 +a 2 ] 3/2','[{"s": "B = μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nIa", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2∫", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dx/[(r−x)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3/2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26017,65,79,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image18.png'),
(26018,65,80,'paragraph',NULL,'and','[{"s": "and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26019,65,81,'paragraph',NULL,'B= μ 0 nIa 2 /2r 3 ∫ l −l dx','[{"s": "B= μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nIa", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∫", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dx", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26020,65,82,'paragraph',NULL,'= μ 0 nI/2 2la 2 /r 3 (5.1)','[{"s": "= μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nI/2 2la", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(5.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26021,65,83,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the magnitude of the magnetic moment of the solenoid is, m = n (2l) I (πa 2 ) — (total number of turns × current × cross-sectional area). Thus,','[{"s": "Note that the magnitude of the magnetic moment of the solenoid is, m = n (2l) I (πa", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") — (total number of turns × current × cross-sectional area). Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26022,65,84,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image19.png (5.2)','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image19.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(5.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26024,65,86,'paragraph',NULL,'Some textbooks assign a magnetic charge (also called pole strength) +q m to the north pole and –q m to the south pole of a bar magnet of length 2l, and magnetic moment q m (2l). The field strength due to q m at a distance r from it is given by µ 0 q m /4πr 2 . The magnetic field due to the bar magnet is then obtained, both for the axial and the equatorial case, in a manner analogous to that of an electric dipole (Chapter 1). The method is simple and appealing. However, magnetic monopoles do not exist, and we have avoided this approach for that reason.','[{"s": "Some textbooks assign a magnetic charge (also called pole strength) +q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to the north pole and –q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to the south pole of a bar magnet of length 2l, and magnetic moment q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2l). The field strength due to q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at a distance r from it is given by µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4πr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The magnetic field due to the bar magnet is then obtained, both for the axial and the equatorial case, in a manner analogous to that of an electric dipole (Chapter 1). The method is simple and appealing. However, magnetic monopoles do not exist, and we have avoided this approach for that reason.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26025,65,87,'heading',4,'5.2.3 THE DIPOLE IN A UNIFORM MAGNETIC FIELD',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26026,65,88,'paragraph',NULL,'The pattern of iron filings, i.e., the magnetic field lines gives us an approximate idea of the magnetic field B. We may at times be required to determine the magnitude of B accurately. This is done by placing a small compass needle of known magnetic moment m and moment of inertia I and allowing it to oscillate in the magnetic field. This arrangement is shown in Fig. 5.4(b).','[{"s": "The pattern of iron filings, i.e., the magnetic field lines gives us an approximate idea of the magnetic field B. We may at times be required to determine the magnitude of B accurately. This is done by placing a small compass needle of known magnetic moment m and moment of inertia I and allowing it to oscillate in the magnetic field. This arrangement is shown in Fig. 5.4(b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26027,65,89,'paragraph',NULL,'The torque on the needle is [see Eq. (4.29)],','[{"s": "The torque on the needle is [see Eq. (4.29)],", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26028,65,90,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = m × B (5.3)','[{"s": "τ = m × B (5.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26030,65,92,'paragraph',NULL,'Here τ is restoring torque and θ is the angle between m and B.','[{"s": "Here τ is restoring torque and θ is the angle between m and B.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26031,65,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, in equilibrium Magnetism_and_Matter-image36.png','[{"s": "Therefore, in equilibrium", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image36.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26032,65,94,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image47.png'),
