(26144,65,206,'heading',3,'WHAT HAPPENS TO MY COMPASS NEEDLES AT THE POLES?',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26145,65,207,'paragraph',NULL,'A compass needle consists of a magnetic needle which floats on a pivotal point. When the compass is held level, it points along the direction of the horizontal component of the earth’s magnetic field at the location. Thus, the compass needle would stay along the magnetic meridian of the place. In some places on the earth there are deposits of magnetic minerals which cause the compass needle to deviate from the magnetic meridian. Knowing the magnetic declination at a place allows us to correct the compass to determine the direction of true north.','[{"s": "A compass needle consists of a magnetic needle which floats on a pivotal point. When the compass is held level, it points along the direction of the horizontal component of the earth’s magnetic field at the location. Thus, the compass needle would stay along the magnetic meridian of the place. In some places on the earth there are deposits of magnetic minerals which cause the compass needle to deviate from the magnetic meridian. Knowing the magnetic declination at a place allows us to correct the compass to determine the direction of true north.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26146,65,208,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image14.png'),
(26148,65,210,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.9 In the magnetic meridian of a certain place, the horizontal component of the earth’s magnetic field is 0.26G and the dip angle is 60°. What is the magnetic field of the earth at this location?','[{"s": "Example 5.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the magnetic meridian of a certain place, the horizontal component of the earth’s magnetic field is 0.26G and the dip angle is 60°. What is the magnetic field of the earth at this location?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26149,65,211,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26150,65,212,'paragraph',NULL,'It is given that H E = 0.26 G. From Fig. 5.11, we have','[{"s": "It is given that H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.26 G. From Fig. 5.11, we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26151,65,213,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image57.png'),
(26152,65,214,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image12.png'),
(26153,65,215,'paragraph',NULL,'= Magnetism_and_Matter-image56.png','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image56.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26154,65,216,'heading',3,'EARTH’S MAGNETIC FIELD',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26155,65,217,'paragraph',NULL,'It must not be assumed that there is a giant bar magnet deep inside the earth which is causing the earth’s magnetic field. Although there are large deposits of iron inside the earth, it is highly unlikely that a large solid block of iron stretches from the magnetic north pole to the magnetic south pole. The earth’s core is very hot and molten, and the ions of iron and nickel are responsible for earth’s magnetism. This hypothesis seems very probable. Moon, which has no molten core, has no magnetic field, Venus has a slower rate of rotation, and a weaker magnetic field, while Jupiter, which has the fastest rotation rate among planets, has a fairly strong magnetic field. However, the precise mode of these circulating currents and the energy needed to sustain them are not very well understood. These are several open questions which form an important area of continuing research.','[{"s": "It must not be assumed that there is a giant bar magnet deep inside the earth which is causing the earth’s magnetic field. Although there are large deposits of iron inside the earth, it is highly unlikely that a large solid block of iron stretches from the magnetic north pole to the magnetic south pole. The earth’s core is very hot and molten, and the ions of iron and nickel are responsible for earth’s magnetism. This hypothesis seems very probable. Moon, which has no molten core, has no magnetic field, Venus has a slower rate of rotation, and a weaker magnetic field, while Jupiter, which has the fastest rotation rate among planets, has a fairly strong magnetic field. However, the precise mode of these circulating currents and the energy needed to sustain them are not very well understood. These are several open questions which form an important area of continuing research.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26156,65,218,'paragraph',NULL,'The variation of the earth’s magnetic field with position is also an interesting area of study. Charged particles emitted by the sun flow towards the earth and beyond, in a stream called the solar wind. Their motion is affected by the earth’s magnetic field, and in turn, they affect the pattern of the earth’s magnetic field. The pattern of magnetic field near the poles is quite different from that in other regions of the earth.','[{"s": "The variation of the earth’s magnetic field with position is also an interesting area of study. Charged particles emitted by the sun flow towards the earth and beyond, in a stream called the solar wind. Their motion is affected by the earth’s magnetic field, and in turn, they affect the pattern of the earth’s magnetic field. The pattern of magnetic field near the poles is quite different from that in other regions of the earth.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26157,65,219,'paragraph',NULL,'The variation of earth’s magnetic field with time is no less fascinating. There are short term variations taking place over centuries and long term variations taking place over a period of a million years. In a span of 240 years from 1580 to 1820 AD, over which records are available, the magnetic declination at London has been found to change by 3.5°, suggesting that the magnetic poles inside the earth change position with time. On the scale of a million years, the earth’s magnetic fields has been found to reverse its direction. Basalt contains iron, and basalt is emitted during volcanic activity. The little iron magnets inside it align themselves parallel to the magnetic field at that place as the basalt cools and solidifies. Geological studies of basalt containing such pieces of magnetised region have provided evidence for the change of direction of earth’s magnetic field, several times in the past.','[{"s": "The variation of earth’s magnetic field with time is no less fascinating. There are short term variations taking place over centuries and long term variations taking place over a period of a million years. In a span of 240 years from 1580 to 1820 AD, over which records are available, the magnetic declination at London has been found to change by 3.5°, suggesting that the magnetic poles inside the earth change position with time. On the scale of a million years, the earth’s magnetic fields has been found to reverse its direction. Basalt contains iron, and basalt is emitted during volcanic activity. The little iron magnets inside it align themselves parallel to the magnetic field at that place as the basalt cools and solidifies. Geological studies of basalt containing such pieces of magnetised region have provided evidence for the change of direction of earth’s magnetic field, several times in the past.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26158,65,220,'heading',3,'5.5 MAGNETISATION AND MAGNETIC INTENSITY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26159,65,221,'paragraph',NULL,'The earth abounds with a bewildering variety of elements and compounds. In addition, we have been synthesising new alloys, compounds and even elements. One would like to classify the magnetic properties of these substances. In the present section, we define and explain certain terms which will help us to carry out this exercise.','[{"s": "The earth abounds with a bewildering variety of elements and compounds. In addition, we have been synthesising new alloys, compounds and even elements. One would like to classify the magnetic properties of these substances. In the present section, we define and explain certain terms which will help us to carry out this exercise.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26160,65,222,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen that a circulating electron in an atom has a magnetic moment. In a bulk material, these moments add up vectorially and they can give a net magnetic moment which is non-zero. We define magnetisation M of a sample to be equal to its net magnetic moment per unit volume:','[{"s": "We have seen that a circulating electron in an atom has a magnetic moment. In a bulk material, these moments add up vectorially and they can give a net magnetic moment which is non-zero. We define magnetisation M of a sample to be equal to its net magnetic moment per unit volume:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26161,65,223,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image9.png (5.11)','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image9.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(5.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26162,65,224,'paragraph',NULL,'M is a vector with dimensions L –1 A and is measured in a units of A m –1 .','[{"s": "M is a vector with dimensions L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A and is measured in a units of A m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26163,65,225,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a long solenoid of n turns per unit length and carrying a current I. The magnetic field in the interior of the solenoid was shown to be given by','[{"s": "Consider a long solenoid of n turns per unit length and carrying a current I. The magnetic field in the interior of the solenoid was shown to be given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26164,65,226,'paragraph',NULL,'B 0 = µ 0 nI (5.12)','[{"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nI (5.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29003,71,136,'paragraph',NULL,'R = λ N (13.16)','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(13.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26165,65,227,'paragraph',NULL,'If the interior of the solenoid is filled with a material with non-zero magnetisation, the field inside the solenoid will be greater than B 0 . The net B field in the interior of the solenoid may be expressed as','[{"s": "If the interior of the solenoid is filled with a material with non-zero magnetisation, the field inside the solenoid will be greater than B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The net B field in the interior of the solenoid may be expressed as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26166,65,228,'paragraph',NULL,'B = B 0 + B m (5.13)','[{"s": "B = B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(5.13)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26167,65,229,'paragraph',NULL,'where B m is the field contributed by the material core. It turns out that this additional field B m is proportional to the magnetisation M of the material and is expressed as','[{"s": "where B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the field contributed by the material core. It turns out that this additional field B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is proportional to the magnetisation M of the material and is expressed as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26168,65,230,'paragraph',NULL,'B m = µ 0 M (5.14)','[{"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M (5.14)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26169,65,231,'paragraph',NULL,'where µ 0 is the same constant (permittivity of vacuum) that appears in Biot-Savart’s law.','[{"s": "where µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the same constant (permittivity of vacuum) that appears in Biot-Savart’s law.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26170,65,232,'paragraph',NULL,'It is convenient to introduce another vector field H, called the magnetic intensity, which is defined by','[{"s": "It is convenient to introduce another vector field H, called the magnetic intensity, which is defined by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26171,65,233,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image32.png (5.15)','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image32.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(5.15)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26172,65,234,'paragraph',NULL,'where H has the same dimensions as M and is measured in units of A m –1 .','[{"s": "where H has the same dimensions as M and is measured in units of A m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26173,65,235,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the total magnetic field B is written as','[{"s": "Thus, the total magnetic field B is written as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26174,65,236,'paragraph',NULL,'B = µ 0 (H + M) (5.16)','[{"s": "B = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(H + M) (5.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26175,65,237,'paragraph',NULL,'We repeat our defining procedure. We have partitioned the contribution to the total magnetic field inside the sample into two parts: one, due to external factors such as the current in the solenoid. This is represented by H. The other is due to the specific nature of the magnetic material, namely M. The latter quantity can be influenced by external factors. This influence is mathematically expressed as','[{"s": "We repeat our defining procedure. We have partitioned the contribution to the total magnetic field inside the sample into two parts: one, due to external factors such as the current in the solenoid. This is represented by H. The other is due to the specific nature of the magnetic material, namely M. The latter quantity can be influenced by external factors. This influence is mathematically expressed as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26176,65,238,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image4.png (5.17)','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image4.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(5.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26178,65,240,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image8.png (5.18)','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image8.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(5.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26179,65,241,'paragraph',NULL,'= µ0 µr H','[{"s": "= µ0 µr H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26180,65,242,'paragraph',NULL,'= µ H (5.19)','[{"s": "= µ H (5.19)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26182,65,244,'paragraph',NULL,'µ = µ 0 µ r =µ 0 (1+χ).','[{"s": "µ = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1+χ).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26183,65,245,'paragraph',NULL,'The three quantities χ, µ r and µ are interrelated and only one of them is independent. Given one, the other two may be easily determined.','[{"s": "The three quantities χ, µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and µ are interrelated and only one of them is independent. Given one, the other two may be easily determined.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26184,65,246,'image',NULL,'20',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image48.jpg'),
(26185,65,247,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.10 A solenoid has a core of a material with relative permeability 400. The windings of the solenoid are insulated from the core and carry a current of 2A. If the number of turns is 1000 per metre, calculate (a) H, (b) M, (c) B and (d) the magnetising current Im.','[{"s": "Example 5.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A solenoid has a core of a material with relative permeability 400. The windings of the solenoid are insulated from the core and carry a current of 2A. If the number of turns is 1000 per metre, calculate (a) H, (b) M, (c) B and (d) the magnetising current Im.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26186,65,248,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26187,65,249,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The field H is dependent of the material of the core, and is','[{"s": "(a) The field H is dependent of the material of the core, and is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26188,65,250,'paragraph',NULL,'H = nI = 1000 × 2.0 = 2 ×10 3 A/m.','[{"s": "H = nI = 1000 × 2.0 = 2 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A/m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26189,65,251,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The magnetic field B is given by','[{"s": "(b) The magnetic field B is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26190,65,252,'paragraph',NULL,'B = µ r µ 0 H','[{"s": "B = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26191,65,253,'paragraph',NULL,'= 400 × 4π ×10 –7 (N/A 2 ) × 2 × 10 3 (A/m)','[{"s": "= 400 × 4π ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(N/A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") × 2 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(A/m)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26192,65,254,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1.0 T','[{"s": "= 1.0 T", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26193,65,255,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Magnetisation is given by','[{"s": "(c) Magnetisation is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26194,65,256,'paragraph',NULL,'M = (B– µ 0 H)/ µ 0','[{"s": "M = (B– µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H)/ µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26195,65,257,'paragraph',NULL,'= (µ r µ 0 H–µ 0 H)/µ 0 = (µ r – 1)H = 399 × H','[{"s": "= (µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H–µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H)/µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– 1)H = 399 × H", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26196,65,258,'paragraph',NULL,'≅ 8 × 10 5 A/m','[{"s": "≅ 8 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A/m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26197,65,259,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) The magnetising current IM is the additional current that needs to be passed through the windings of the solenoid in the absence of the core which would give a B value as in the presence of the core. Thus B= µ r n 0 (I + I M ). Using I = 2A, B = 1 T, we get I M = 794 A.','[{"s": "(d) The magnetising current IM is the additional current that needs to be passed through the windings of the solenoid in the absence of the core which would give a B value as in the presence of the core. Thus B= µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(I + I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Using I = 2A, B = 1 T, we get I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 794 A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26198,65,260,'heading',3,'5.6 MAGNETIC PROPERTIES OF MATERIALS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26200,65,262,'image',NULL,'21',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image65.jpg'),
(26201,65,263,'heading',4,'5.6.1 DIAMAGNETISM',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26203,65,265,'paragraph',NULL,'5 . When placed in a non-uniform magnetic field, the bar will tend to move from high to low field.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". When placed in a non-uniform magnetic field, the bar will tend to move from high to low field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26205,65,267,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image11.png'),
(26206,65,268,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.12 Behaviour of magnetic field lines near a','[{"s": "Figure 5.12 Behaviour of magnetic field lines near a", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26207,65,269,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) diamagnetic,','[{"s": "(a) diamagnetic,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26208,65,270,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) paramagnetic substance.','[{"s": "(b) paramagnetic substance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26209,65,271,'paragraph',NULL,'Some diamagnetic materials are bismuth, copper, lead, silicon, nitrogen (at STP), water and sodium chloride. Diamagnetism is present in all the substances. However, the effect is so weak in most cases that it gets shifted by other effects like paramagnetism, ferromagnetism, etc.','[{"s": "Some diamagnetic materials are bismuth, copper, lead, silicon, nitrogen (at STP), water and sodium chloride. Diamagnetism is present in all the substances. However, the effect is so weak in most cases that it gets shifted by other effects like paramagnetism, ferromagnetism, etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26210,65,272,'paragraph',NULL,'The most exotic diamagnetic materials are superconductors. These are metals, cooled to very low temperatures which exhibits both perfect conductivity and perfect diamagnetism. Here the field lines are completely expelled! χ = –1 and µ r = 0. A superconductor repels a magnet and (by Newton’s third law) is repelled by the magnet. The phenomenon of perfect diamagnetism in superconductors is called the Meissner effect, after the name of its discoverer. Superconducting magnets can be gainfully exploited in variety of situations, for example, for running magnetically levitated superfast trains.','[{"s": "The most exotic diamagnetic materials are superconductors. These are metals, cooled to very low temperatures which exhibits both perfect conductivity and perfect diamagnetism. Here the field lines are completely expelled! χ = –1 and µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0. A superconductor repels a magnet and (by Newton’s third law) is repelled by the magnet. The phenomenon of perfect diamagnetism in superconductors is called the Meissner effect, after the name of its discoverer. Superconducting magnets can be gainfully exploited in variety of situations, for example, for running magnetically levitated superfast trains.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26211,65,273,'heading',4,'5.6.2 PARAMAGNETISM',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26212,65,274,'paragraph',NULL,'Paramagnetic substances are those which get weakly magnetised when placed in an external magnetic field. They have tendency to move from a region of weak magnetic field to strong magnetic field, i.e., they get weakly attracted to a magnet.','[{"s": "Paramagnetic substances are those which get weakly magnetised when placed in an external magnetic field. They have tendency to move from a region of weak magnetic field to strong magnetic field, i.e., they get weakly attracted to a magnet.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26213,65,275,'paragraph',NULL,'The individual atoms (or ions or molecules) of a paramagnetic material possess a permanent magnetic dipole moment of their own. On account of the ceaseless random thermal motion of the atoms, no net magnetisation is seen. In the presence of an external field B 0 , which is strong enough, and at low temperatures, the individual atomic dipole moment can be made to align and point in the same direction as B 0 . Figure 5.12(b) shows a bar of paramagnetic material placed in an external field. The field lines gets concentrated inside the material, and the field inside is enhanced. In most cases, as is evident from Table 5.2, this enhancement is slight, being one part in 10 5 . When placed in a non-uniform magnetic field, the bar will tend to move from weak field to strong.','[{"s": "The individual atoms (or ions or molecules) of a paramagnetic material possess a permanent magnetic dipole moment of their own. On account of the ceaseless random thermal motion of the atoms, no net magnetisation is seen. In the presence of an external field B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which is strong enough, and at low temperatures, the individual atomic dipole moment can be made to align and point in the same direction as B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Figure 5.12(b) shows a bar of paramagnetic material placed in an external field. The field lines gets concentrated inside the material, and the field inside is enhanced. In most cases, as is evident from Table 5.2, this enhancement is slight, being one part in 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". When placed in a non-uniform magnetic field, the bar will tend to move from weak field to strong.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26214,65,276,'paragraph',NULL,'Some paramagnetic materials are aluminium, sodium, calcium, oxygen (at STP) and copper chloride. Experimentally, one finds that the magnetisation of a paramagnetic material is inversely proportional to the absolute temperature T,','[{"s": "Some paramagnetic materials are aluminium, sodium, calcium, oxygen (at STP) and copper chloride. Experimentally, one finds that the magnetisation of a paramagnetic material is inversely proportional to the absolute temperature T,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26215,65,277,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image31.png [5.20(a)]','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image31.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[5.20(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26216,65,278,'paragraph',NULL,'or equivalently, using Eqs. (5.12) and (5.17)','[{"s": "or equivalently, using Eqs. (5.12) and (5.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26217,65,279,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image39.png [5.20(b)]','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image39.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[5.20(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26219,65,281,'heading',4,'5.6.3 FERROMAGNETISM',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26220,65,282,'paragraph',NULL,'Ferromagnetic substances are those which gets strongly magnetised when placed in an external magnetic field. They have strong tendency to move from a region of weak magnetic field to strong magnetic field, i.e., they get strongly attracted to a magnet.','[{"s": "Ferromagnetic substances are those which gets strongly magnetised when placed in an external magnetic field. They have strong tendency to move from a region of weak magnetic field to strong magnetic field, i.e., they get strongly attracted to a magnet.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26221,65,283,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image52.png'),
(26222,65,284,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.13','[{"s": "Figure 5.13", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26223,65,285,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Randomly oriented domains, (b) Aligned domains.','[{"s": "(a) Randomly oriented domains, (b) Aligned domains.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26224,65,286,'paragraph',NULL,'The individual atoms (or ions or molecules) in a ferromagnetic material possess a dipole moment as in a paramagnetic material. However, they interact with one another in such a way that they spontaneously align themselves in a common direction over a macroscopic volume called domain. The explanation of this cooperative effect requires quantum mechanics and is beyond the scope of this textbook. Each domain has a net magnetisation. Typical domain size is 1mm and the domain contains about 10 11 atoms. In the first instant, the magnetisation varies randomly from domain to domain and there is no bulk magnetisation. This is shown in Fig. 5.13(a). When we apply an external magnetic field B 0 , the domains orient themselves in the direction of B 0 and simultaneously the domain oriented in the direction of B 0 grow in size. This existence of domains and their motion in B 0 are not speculations. One may observe this under a microscope after sprinkling a liquid suspension of powdered ferromagnetic substance of samples. This motion of suspension can be observed. Figure 5.12(b) shows the situation when the domains have aligned and amalgamated to form a single ‘giant’ domain.','[{"s": "The individual atoms (or ions or molecules) in a ferromagnetic material possess a dipole moment as in a paramagnetic material. However, they interact with one another in such a way that they spontaneously align themselves in a common direction over a macroscopic volume called domain. The explanation of this cooperative effect requires quantum mechanics and is beyond the scope of this textbook. Each domain has a net magnetisation. Typical domain size is 1mm and the domain contains about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atoms. In the first instant, the magnetisation varies randomly from domain to domain and there is no bulk magnetisation. This is shown in Fig. 5.13(a). When we apply an external magnetic field B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the domains orient themselves in the direction of B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and simultaneously the domain oriented in the direction of B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "grow in size. This existence of domains and their motion in B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are not speculations. One may observe this under a microscope after sprinkling a liquid suspension of powdered ferromagnetic substance of samples. This motion of suspension can be observed. Figure 5.12(b) shows the situation when the domains have aligned and amalgamated to form a single ‘giant’ domain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26225,65,287,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, in a ferromagnetic material the field lines are highly concentrated. In non-uniform magnetic field, the sample tends to move towards the region of high field. We may wonder as to what happens when the external field is removed. In some ferromagnetic materials the magnetisation persists. Such materials are called hard magnetic materials or hard ferromagnets. Alnico, an alloy of iron, aluminium, nickel, cobalt and copper, is one such material. The naturally occurring lodestone is another. Such materials form permanent magnets to be used among other things as a compass needle. On the other hand, there is a class of ferromagnetic materials in which the magnetisation disappears on removal of the external field. Soft iron is one such material. Appropriately enough, such materials are called soft ferromagnetic materials. There are a number of elements, which are ferromagnetic: iron, cobalt, nickel, gadolinium, etc. The relative magnetic permeability is >1000!','[{"s": "Thus, in a ferromagnetic material the field lines are highly concentrated. In non-uniform magnetic field, the sample tends to move towards the region of high field. We may wonder as to what happens when the external field is removed. In some ferromagnetic materials the magnetisation persists. Such materials are called hard magnetic materials or hard ferromagnets. Alnico, an alloy of iron, aluminium, nickel, cobalt and copper, is one such material. The naturally occurring lodestone is another. Such materials form permanent magnets to be used among other things as a compass needle. On the other hand, there is a class of ferromagnetic materials in which the magnetisation disappears on removal of the external field. Soft iron is one such material. Appropriately enough, such materials are called soft ferromagnetic materials. There are a number of elements, which are ferromagnetic: iron, cobalt, nickel, gadolinium, etc. The relative magnetic permeability is >1000!", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26226,65,288,'paragraph',NULL,'The ferromagnetic property depends on temperature. At high enough temperature, a ferromagnet becomes a paramagnet. The domain structure disintegrates with temperature. This disappearance of magnetisation with temperature is gradual. It is a phase transition reminding us of the melting of a solid crystal. The temperature of transition from ferromagnetic to paramagnetism is called the Curie temperature Tc. Table 5.4 lists the Curie temperature of certain ferromagnets. The susceptibility above the Curie temperature, i.e., in the paramagnetic phase is described by,','[{"s": "The ferromagnetic property depends on temperature. At high enough temperature, a ferromagnet becomes a paramagnet. The domain structure disintegrates with temperature. This disappearance of magnetisation with temperature is gradual. It is a phase transition reminding us of the melting of a solid crystal. The temperature of transition from ferromagnetic to paramagnetism is called the Curie temperature Tc. Table 5.4 lists the Curie temperature of certain ferromagnets. The susceptibility above the Curie temperature, i.e., in the paramagnetic phase is described by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26227,65,289,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetism_and_Matter-image55.png (5.21)','[{"s": "Magnetism_and_Matter-image55.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(5.21)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26228,65,290,'image',NULL,'22',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image46.jpg'),
(26229,65,291,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.11 A domain in ferromagnetic iron is in the form of a cube of side length 1µm. Estimate the number of iron atoms in the domain and the maximum possible dipole moment and magnetisation of the domain. The molecular mass of iron is 55 g/mole and its density is 7.9 g/cm 3 . Assume that each iron atom has a dipole moment of 9.27×10 –24 A m 2 .','[{"s": "Example 5.11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A domain in ferromagnetic iron is in the form of a cube of side length 1µm. Estimate the number of iron atoms in the domain and the maximum possible dipole moment and magnetisation of the domain. The molecular mass of iron is 55 g/mole and its density is 7.9 g/cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Assume that each iron atom has a dipole moment of 9.27×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–24", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26230,65,292,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution The volume of the cubic domain is','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The volume of the cubic domain is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26231,65,293,'paragraph',NULL,'V = (10–6 m) 3 = 10–18 m 3 = 10 –12 cm 3','[{"s": "V = (10–6 m)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10–18 m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26232,65,294,'paragraph',NULL,'Its mass is volume × density = 7.9 g cm –3 × 10 –12 cm 3 = 7.9 × 10 –12 g','[{"s": "Its mass is volume × density = 7.9 g cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 7.9 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26233,65,295,'paragraph',NULL,'It is given that Avagadro number (6.023 × 10 23 ) of iron atoms have a mass of 55 g. Hence, the number of atoms in the domain is','[{"s": "It is given that Avagadro number (6.023 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "23", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of iron atoms have a mass of 55 g. Hence, the number of atoms in the domain is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
