(26235,65,297,'paragraph',NULL,'The maximum possible dipole moment mmax is achieved for the (unrealistic) case when all the atomic moments are perfectly aligned. Thus,','[{"s": "The maximum possible dipole moment mmax is achieved for the (unrealistic) case when all the atomic moments are perfectly aligned. Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26236,65,298,'paragraph',NULL,'m max = (8.65 × 10 10 ) × (9.27 × 10 –24 )','[{"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "max", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (8.65 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") × (9.27 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–24", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26237,65,299,'paragraph',NULL,'= 8.0 × 10 –13 A m 2','[{"s": "= 8.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26238,65,300,'paragraph',NULL,'The consequent magnetisation is','[{"s": "The consequent magnetisation is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26239,65,301,'paragraph',NULL,'M max = m max /Domain volume','[{"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "max", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "max", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/Domain volume", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26240,65,302,'paragraph',NULL,'= 8.0 × 10 –13 A m 2 /10 –18 m 3','[{"s": "= 8.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26241,65,303,'paragraph',NULL,'= 8.0 × 10 5 Am –1','[{"s": "= 8.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Am", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26242,65,304,'paragraph',NULL,'The relation between B and H in ferromagnetic materials is complex. It is often not linear and it depends on the magnetic history of the sample. Figure 5.14 depicts the behaviour of the material as we take it through one cycle of magnetisation. Let the material be unmagnetised initially. We place it in a solenoid and increase the current through the solenoid. The magnetic field B in the material rises and saturates as depicted in the curve Oa. This behaviour represents the alignment and merger of domains until no further enhancement is possible. It is pointless to increase the current (and hence the magnetic intensity H) beyond this. Next, we decrease H and reduce it to zero. At H = 0, B ≠ 0. This is represented by the curve ab. The value of B at H = 0 is called retentivity or remanence. In Fig. 5.14, B R ~ 1.2 T, where the subscript R denotes retentivity. The domains are not completely randomised even though the external driving field has been removed. Next, the current in the solenoid is reversed and slowly increased. Certain domains are flipped until the net field inside stands nullified. This is represented by the curve bc. The value of H at c is called coercivity. In Fig. 5.14 H c ~ –90 A m –1 . As the reversed current is increased in magnitude, we once again obtain saturation. The curve cd depicts this. The saturated magnetic field Bs ~ 1.5 T. Next, the current is reduced (curve de) and reversed (curve ea). The cycle repeats itself. Note that the curve Oa does not retrace itself as H is reduced. For a given value of H, B is not unique but depends on previous history of the sample. This phenomenon is called hysterisis. The word hysterisis means lagging behind (and not ‘history’).','[{"s": "The relation between B and H in ferromagnetic materials is complex. It is often not linear and it depends on the magnetic history of the sample. Figure 5.14 depicts the behaviour of the material as we take it through one cycle of magnetisation. Let the material be unmagnetised initially. We place it in a solenoid and increase the current through the solenoid. The magnetic field B in the material rises and saturates as depicted in the curve Oa. This behaviour represents the alignment and merger of domains until no further enhancement is possible. It is pointless to increase the current (and hence the magnetic intensity H) beyond this. Next, we decrease H and reduce it to zero. At H = 0, B ≠ 0. This is represented by the curve ab. The value of B at H = 0 is called retentivity or remanence. In Fig. 5.14, B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~ 1.2 T, where the subscript R denotes retentivity. The domains are not completely randomised even though the external driving field has been removed. Next, the current in the solenoid is reversed and slowly increased. Certain domains are flipped until the net field inside stands nullified. This is represented by the curve bc. The value of H at c is called coercivity. In Fig. 5.14 H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~ –90 A m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". As the reversed current is increased in magnitude, we once again obtain saturation. The curve cd depicts this. The saturated magnetic field Bs ~ 1.5 T. Next, the current is reduced (curve de) and reversed (curve ea). The cycle repeats itself. Note that the curve Oa does not retrace itself as H is reduced. For a given value of H, B is not unique but depends on previous history of the sample. This phenomenon is called hysterisis. The word hysterisis means lagging behind (and not ‘history’).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26243,65,305,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image22.png'),
(26244,65,306,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.14 The magnetic hysteresis loop is the B-H curve for ferromagnetic materials.','[{"s": "Figure 5.14 The magnetic hysteresis loop is the B-H curve for ferromagnetic materials.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26245,65,307,'paragraph',NULL,'Hysterisis in magnetic materials:','[{"s": "Hysterisis in magnetic materials:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
