(26269,65,331,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Gauss’s law for magnetism states that the net magnetic flux through any closed surface is zero','[{"s": "4. Gauss’s law for magnetism states that the net magnetic flux through any closed surface is zero", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26270,65,332,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image21.png'),
(26271,65,333,'paragraph',NULL,'5. The earth’s magnetic field resembles that of a (hypothetical) magnetic dipole located at the centre of the earth. The pole near the geographic north pole of the earth is called the north magnetic pole. Similarly, the pole near the geographic south pole is called the south magnetic pole. This dipole is aligned making a small angle with the rotation axis of the earth. The magnitude of the field on the earth’s surface ≈ 4 × 10–5 T.','[{"s": "5. The earth’s magnetic field resembles that of a (hypothetical) magnetic dipole located at the centre of the earth. The pole near the geographic north pole of the earth is called the north magnetic pole. Similarly, the pole near the geographic south pole is called the south magnetic pole. This dipole is aligned making a small angle with the rotation axis of the earth. The magnitude of the field on the earth’s surface ≈ 4 × 10–5 T.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26272,65,334,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Three quantities are needed to specify the magnetic field of the earth on its surface – the horizontal component, the magnetic declination, and the magnetic dip. These are known as the elements of the earth’s magnetic field.','[{"s": "6. Three quantities are needed to specify the magnetic field of the earth on its surface – the horizontal component, the magnetic declination, and the magnetic dip. These are known as the elements of the earth’s magnetic field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26273,65,335,'paragraph',NULL,'7. Consider a material placed in an external magnetic field B0. The magnetic intensity is defined as,','[{"s": "7. Consider a material placed in an external magnetic field B0. The magnetic intensity is defined as,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26274,65,336,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image50.png'),
(26275,65,337,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnetisation M of the material is its dipole moment per unit volume.','[{"s": "The magnetisation M of the material is its dipole moment per unit volume.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26276,65,338,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnetic field B in the material is,','[{"s": "The magnetic field B in the material is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26277,65,339,'paragraph',NULL,'B = µ0 (H + M)','[{"s": "B = µ0 (H + M)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26278,65,340,'paragraph',NULL,'8. For a linear material M = χ H. So that B = µ H and χ is called the magnetic susceptibility of the material. The three quantities, χ, the relative magnetic permeability µr, and the magnetic permeability µare related as follows:','[{"s": "8. For a linear material M = χ H. So that B = µ H and χ is called the magnetic susceptibility of the material. The three quantities, χ, the relative magnetic permeability µr, and the magnetic permeability µare related as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26279,65,341,'paragraph',NULL,'µ = µ0 µr','[{"s": "µ = µ0 µr", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26280,65,342,'paragraph',NULL,'µr = 1+ χ','[{"s": "µr = 1+ χ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26281,65,343,'paragraph',NULL,'9. Magnetic materials are broadly classified as: diamagnetic, paramagnetic, and ferromagnetic. For diamagnetic materials χ is negative and small and for paramagnetic materials it is positive and small. Ferromagnetic materials have large χ and are characterised by non-linear relation between B and H.They show the property of hysteresis.','[{"s": "9. Magnetic materials are broadly classified as: diamagnetic, paramagnetic, and ferromagnetic. For diamagnetic materials χ is negative and small and for paramagnetic materials it is positive and small. Ferromagnetic materials have large χ and are characterised by non-linear relation between B and H.They show the property of hysteresis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26282,65,344,'paragraph',NULL,'10. Substances, which at room temperature, retain their ferromagnetic property for a long period of time are called permanent magnets.','[{"s": "10. Substances, which at room temperature, retain their ferromagnetic property for a long period of time are called permanent magnets.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26283,65,345,'image',NULL,'23',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image16.jpg'),
(26284,65,346,'heading',4,'POINTS TO PONDER',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26285,65,347,'paragraph',NULL,'1. A satisfactory understanding of magnetic phenomenon in terms of moving charges/currents was arrived at after 1800 AD. But technological exploitation of the directional properties of magnets predates this scientific understanding by two thousand years. Thus, scientific understanding is not a necessary condition for engineering applications. Ideally, science and engineering go hand-in-hand, one leading and assisting the other in tandem.','[{"s": "1. A satisfactory understanding of magnetic phenomenon in terms of moving charges/currents was arrived at after 1800 AD. But technological exploitation of the directional properties of magnets predates this scientific understanding by two thousand years. Thus, scientific understanding is not a necessary condition for engineering applications. Ideally, science and engineering go hand-in-hand, one leading and assisting the other in tandem.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26286,65,348,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Magnetic monopoles do not exist. If you slice a magnet in half, you get two smaller magnets. On the other hand, isolated positive and negative charges exist. There exists a smallest unit of charge, for example, the electronic charge with value |e| = 1.6 ×10–19 C. All other charges are integral multiples of this smallest unit charge. In other words, charge is quantised. We do not know why magnetic monopoles do not exist or why electric charge is quantised.','[{"s": "2. Magnetic monopoles do not exist. If you slice a magnet in half, you get two smaller magnets. On the other hand, isolated positive and negative charges exist. There exists a smallest unit of charge, for example, the electronic charge with value |e| = 1.6 ×10–19 C. All other charges are integral multiples of this smallest unit charge. In other words, charge is quantised. We do not know why magnetic monopoles do not exist or why electric charge is quantised.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26287,65,349,'paragraph',NULL,'3. A consequence of the fact that magnetic monopoles do not exist is that the magnetic field lines are continuous and form closed loops. In contrast, the electrostatic lines of force begin on a positive charge and terminate on the negative charge (or fade out at infinity).','[{"s": "3. A consequence of the fact that magnetic monopoles do not exist is that the magnetic field lines are continuous and form closed loops. In contrast, the electrostatic lines of force begin on a positive charge and terminate on the negative charge (or fade out at infinity).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34351,79,271,'paragraph',NULL,'Although the size of the spot is very small, it plays an important role in determining the limit of resolution of optical instruments like a telescope or a microscope. For the two stars to be just resolved','[{"s": "Although the size of the spot is very small, it plays an important role in determining the limit of resolution of optical instruments like a telescope or a microscope.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For the two stars to be just resolved", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031782", "2000032080", "2000033036", "2000112645"]',NULL),
(26288,65,350,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The earth’s magnetic field is not due to a huge bar magnet inside it. The earth’s core is hot and molten. Perhaps convective currents in this core are responsible for the earth’s magnetic field. As to what ‘dynamo’ effect sustains this current, and why the earth’s field reverses polarity every million years or so, we do not know.','[{"s": "4. The earth’s magnetic field is not due to a huge bar magnet inside it. The earth’s core is hot and molten. Perhaps convective currents in this core are responsible for the earth’s magnetic field. As to what ‘dynamo’ effect sustains this current, and why the earth’s field reverses polarity every million years or so, we do not know.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26289,65,351,'paragraph',NULL,'5. A miniscule difference in the value of χ, the magnetic susceptibility, yields radically different behaviour: diamagnetic versus paramagnetic. For diamagnetic materials χ = –10–5 whereas χ = +10–5 for paramagnetic materials.','[{"s": "5. A miniscule difference in the value of χ, the magnetic susceptibility, yields radically different behaviour: diamagnetic versus paramagnetic. For diamagnetic materials χ = –10–5 whereas χ = +10–5 for paramagnetic materials.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26290,65,352,'paragraph',NULL,'6. There exists a perfect diamagnet, namely, a superconductor. This is a metal at very low temperatures. In this case χ = –1, µr = 0, µ = 0. The external magnetic field is totally expelled. Interestingly, this material is also a perfect conductor. However, there exists no classical theory which ties these two properties together. A quantum-mechanical theory by Bardeen, Cooper, and Schrieffer (BCS theory) explains these effects. The BCS theory was proposed in1957 and was eventually recognised by a Nobel Prize in physics in 1970.','[{"s": "6. There exists a perfect diamagnet, namely, a superconductor. This is a metal at very low temperatures. In this case χ = –1, µr = 0, µ = 0. The external magnetic field is totally expelled. Interestingly, this material is also a perfect conductor. However, there exists no classical theory which ties these two properties together. A quantum-mechanical theory by Bardeen, Cooper, and Schrieffer (BCS theory) explains these effects. The BCS theory was proposed in1957 and was eventually recognised by a Nobel Prize in physics in 1970.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26291,65,353,'paragraph',NULL,'7. The phenomenon of magnetic hysteresis is reminiscent of similar behaviour concerning the elastic properties of materials. Strain may not be proportional to stress; here H and B (or M) are not linearly related. The stress-strain curve exhibits hysteresis and area enclosed by it represents the energy dissipated per unit volume. A similar interpretation can be given to the B-H magnetic hysteresis curve.','[{"s": "7. The phenomenon of magnetic hysteresis is reminiscent of similar behaviour concerning the elastic properties of materials. Strain may not be proportional to stress; here H and B (or M) are not linearly related. The stress-strain curve exhibits hysteresis and area enclosed by it represents the energy dissipated per unit volume. A similar interpretation can be given to the B-H magnetic hysteresis curve.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26292,65,354,'paragraph',NULL,'8. Diamagnetism is universal. It is present in all materials. But it is weak and hard to detect if the substance is para- or ferromagnetic.','[{"s": "8. Diamagnetism is universal. It is present in all materials. But it is weak and hard to detect if the substance is para- or ferromagnetic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26293,65,355,'paragraph',NULL,'9. We have classified materials as diamagnetic, paramagnetic, and ferromagnetic. However, there exist additional types of magnetic material such as ferrimagnetic, anti-ferromagnetic, spin glass, etc. with properties which are exotic and mysterious.','[{"s": "9. We have classified materials as diamagnetic, paramagnetic, and ferromagnetic. However, there exist additional types of magnetic material such as ferrimagnetic, anti-ferromagnetic, spin glass, etc. with properties which are exotic and mysterious.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26294,65,356,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image3.png'),
(26295,65,357,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image3.png'),
(26296,65,358,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image3.png'),
(26297,65,359,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image3.png'),
(26298,65,360,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image3.png'),
(26299,65,361,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Magnetism_and_Matter-image3.png'),
