(26503,66,73,'paragraph',NULL,'This is a 100% increase in pressure from surface level. At a depth of 1 km, the increase in pressure is 100 atm! Submarines are designed to withstand such enormous pressures.','[{"s": "This is a 100% increase in pressure from surface level. At a depth of 1 km, the increase in pressure is 100 atm! Submarines are designed to withstand such enormous pressures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26504,66,74,'heading',4,'10.2.3 Atmospheric Pressure and Gauge Pressure',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26505,66,75,'paragraph',NULL,'The pressure of the atmosphere at any point is equal to the weight of a column of air of unit cross-sectional area extending from that point to the top of the atmosphere. At sea level, it is 1.013 × 105 Pa (1 atm). Italian scientist Evangelista Torricelli (1608–1647) devised for the first time a method for measuring atmospheric pressure. A long glass tube closed at one end and filled with mercury is inverted into a trough of mercury as shown in Fig.10.5 (a). This device is known as ‘mercury barometer’. The space above the mercury column in the tube contains only mercury vapour whose pressure P is so small that it may be neglected. Thus, the pressure at Point A=0. The pressure inside the coloumn at Point B must be the same as the pressure at Point C, which is atmospheric pressure, Pa.','[{"s": "The pressure of the atmosphere at any point is equal to the weight of a column of air of unit cross-sectional area extending from that point to the top of the atmosphere. At sea level, it is 1.013 × 105 Pa (1 atm). Italian scientist Evangelista Torricelli (1608–1647) devised for the first time a method for measuring atmospheric pressure. A long glass tube closed at one end and filled with mercury is inverted into a trough of mercury as shown in Fig.10.5 (a). This device is known as ‘mercury barometer’. The space above the mercury column in the tube contains only mercury vapour whose pressure P is so small that it may be neglected. Thus, the pressure at Point A=0. The pressure inside the coloumn at Point B must be the same as the pressure at Point C, which is atmospheric pressure, Pa.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26506,66,76,'paragraph',NULL,'Pa = ρgh (10.8)','[{"s": "Pa = ρgh (10.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26508,66,78,'paragraph',NULL,'In the experiment it is found that the mercury column in the barometer has a height of about 76 cm at sea level equivalent to one atmosphere (1 atm). This can also be obtained using the value of ρ in Eq. (10.8). A common way of stating pressure is in terms of cm or mm of mercury (Hg). A pressure equivalent of 1 mm is called a torr (after Torricelli).','[{"s": "In the experiment it is found that the mercury column in the barometer has a height of about 76 cm at sea level equivalent to one atmosphere (1 atm). This can also be obtained using the value of ρ in Eq. (10.8). A common way of stating pressure is in terms of cm or mm of mercury (Hg). A pressure equivalent of 1 mm is called a torr (after Torricelli).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26509,66,79,'paragraph',NULL,'1 torr = 133 Pa.','[{"s": "1 torr = 133 Pa.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26510,66,80,'paragraph',NULL,'The mm of Hg and torr are used in medicine and physiology. In meteorology, a common unit is the bar and millibar.','[{"s": "The mm of Hg and torr are used in medicine and physiology. In meteorology, a common unit is the bar and millibar.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26511,66,81,'paragraph',NULL,'1 bar = 105 Pa','[{"s": "1 bar = 105 Pa", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26513,66,83,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image72.png'),
(26514,66,84,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 10.5 (a) The mercury barometer.','[{"s": "Fig 10.5 (a) The mercury barometer.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(26515,66,85,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image75.png'),
(26516,66,86,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The open tube manometer','[{"s": "(b) The open tube manometer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26517,66,87,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 10.5 Two pressure measuring devices.','[{"s": "Fig 10.5 Two pressure measuring devices.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26518,66,88,'paragraph',NULL,'Pressure is same at the same level on both sides of the U-tube containing a fluid. For liquids, the density varies very little over wide ranges in pressure and temperature and we can treat it safely as a constant for our present purposes. Gases on the other hand, exhibits large variations of densities with changes in pressure and temperature. Unlike gases, liquids are, therefore, largely treated as incompressible.','[{"s": "Pressure is same at the same level on both sides of the U-tube containing a fluid. For liquids, the density varies very little over wide ranges in pressure and temperature and we can treat it safely as a constant for our present purposes. Gases on the other hand, exhibits large variations of densities with changes in pressure and temperature. Unlike gases, liquids are, therefore, largely treated as incompressible.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26519,66,89,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.3 The density of the atmosphere at sea level is 1.29 kg/m3. Assume that it does not change with altitude. Then how high would the atmosphere extend?','[{"s": "Example 10.3 The density of the atmosphere at sea level is 1.29 kg/m3. Assume that it does not change with altitude. Then how high would the atmosphere extend?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26520,66,90,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer We use Eq. (10.7)','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We use Eq. (10.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26521,66,91,'paragraph',NULL,'ρgh = 1.29 kg m–3 × 9.8 m s2 × h m = 1.01 × 105 Pa','[{"s": "ρgh = 1.29 kg m–3 × 9.8 m s2 × h m = 1.01 × 105 Pa", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26522,66,92,'paragraph',NULL,'∴ h = 7989 m ≈ 8 km','[{"s": "∴ h = 7989 m ≈ 8 km", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26524,66,94,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Here h = 1000 m and ρ = 1.03 × 103 kg m-3.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Here h = 1000 m and ρ = 1.03 × 103 kg m-3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26525,66,95,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) From Eq. (10.6), absolute pressure','[{"s": "(a) From Eq. (10.6), absolute pressure", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26526,66,96,'paragraph',NULL,'P = Pa + ρgh','[{"s": "P = Pa + ρgh", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26527,66,97,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1.01 × 105 Pa','[{"s": "= 1.01 × 105 Pa", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26528,66,98,'paragraph',NULL,'+ 1.03 × 103 kg m–3 × 10 m s–2 × 1000 m','[{"s": "+ 1.03 × 103 kg m–3 × 10 m s–2 × 1000 m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26529,66,99,'paragraph',NULL,'= 104.01 × 105 Pa','[{"s": "= 104.01 × 105 Pa", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26530,66,100,'paragraph',NULL,'≈ 104 atm','[{"s": "≈ 104 atm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26531,66,101,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Gauge pressure is P − Pa = ρgh = Pg','[{"s": "(b) Gauge pressure is P − Pa = ρgh = Pg", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26532,66,102,'paragraph',NULL,'Pg = 1.03 × 103 kg m–3 × 10 ms2 × 1000 m','[{"s": "Pg = 1.03 × 103 kg m–3 × 10 ms2 × 1000 m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26533,66,103,'paragraph',NULL,'= 103 × 105 Pa','[{"s": "= 103 × 105 Pa", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26534,66,104,'paragraph',NULL,'≈ 103 atm','[{"s": "≈ 103 atm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26535,66,105,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The pressure outside the submarine is P = Pa + ρgh and the pressure inside it is Pa. Hence, the net pressure acting on the window is gauge pressure, Pg = ρgh. Since the area of the window is A = 0.04 m2, the force acting on it is','[{"s": "(c) The pressure outside the submarine is P = Pa + ρgh and the pressure inside it is Pa. Hence, the net pressure acting on the window is gauge pressure, Pg = ρgh. Since the area of the window is A = 0.04 m2, the force acting on it is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26536,66,106,'paragraph',NULL,'F = Pg A = 103 × 105 Pa × 0.04 m2 = 4.12 × 105 N','[{"s": "F = Pg A = 103 × 105 Pa × 0.04 m2 = 4.12 × 105 N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26537,66,107,'heading',4,'10.2.4 Hydraulic Machines',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26538,66,108,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us now consider what happens when we change the pressure on a fluid contained in a vessel. Consider a horizontal cylinder with a piston and three vertical tubes at different points [Fig. 10.6 (a)]. The pressure in the horizontal cylinder is indicated by the height of liquid column in the vertical tubes. It is necessarily the same in all. If we push the piston, the fluid level rises in all the tubes, again reaching the same level in each one of them.','[{"s": "Let us now consider what happens when we change the pressure on a fluid contained in a vessel. Consider a horizontal cylinder with a piston and three vertical tubes at different points [Fig. 10.6 (a)]. The pressure in the horizontal cylinder is indicated by the height of liquid column in the vertical tubes. It is necessarily the same in all. If we push the piston, the fluid level rises in all the tubes, again reaching the same level in each one of them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26539,66,109,'paragraph',NULL,'Archemedes’ Principle','[{"s": "Archemedes’ Principle", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26540,66,110,'paragraph',NULL,'Fluid appears to provide partial support to the objects placed in it. When a body is wholly or partially immersed in a fluid at rest, the fluid exerts pressure on the surface of the body in contact with the fluid. The pressure is greater on lower surfaces of the body than on the upper surfaces as pressure in a fluid increases with depth. The resultant of all the forces is an upward force called buoyant force. Suppose that a cylindrical body is immersed in the fluid. The upward force on the bottom of the body is more than the downward force on its top. The fluid exerts a resultant upward force or buoyant force on the body equal to (P 2 – P 1 ) × A (Fig. 10.3). We have seen in equation 10.4 that (P2-P1)A = ρghA. Now, hA is the volume of the solid and ρhA is the weight of an equivaliant volume of the fluid. (P2-P1)A = mg. Thus, the upward force exerted is equal to the weight of the displaced fluid.','[{"s": "Fluid appears to provide partial support to the objects placed in it. When a body is wholly or partially immersed in a fluid at rest, the fluid exerts pressure on the surface of the body in contact with the fluid. The pressure is greater on lower surfaces of the body than on the upper surfaces as pressure in a fluid increases with depth. The resultant of all the forces is an upward force called buoyant force. Suppose that a cylindrical body is immersed in the fluid. The upward force on the bottom of the body is more than the downward force on its top. The fluid exerts a resultant upward force or buoyant force on the body equal to (P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") × A (Fig. 10.3). We have seen in equation 10.4 that (P2-P1)A = ρghA. Now, hA is the volume of the solid and ρhA is the weight of an equivaliant volume of the fluid. (P2-P1)A = mg. Thus, the upward force exerted is equal to the weight of the displaced fluid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26541,66,111,'paragraph',NULL,'The result holds true irrespective of the shape of the object and here cylindrical object is considered only for convenience. This is Archimedes’ principle. For totally immersed objects the volume of the fluid displaced by the object is equal to its own volume. If the density of the immersed object is more than that of the fluid, the object will sink as the weight of the body is more than the upward thrust. If the density of the object is less than that of the fluid, it floats in the fluid partially submerged. To calculate the volume submerged, suppose the total volume of the object is Vs and a part Vp of it is submerged in the fluid. Then, the upward force which is the weight of the displaced fluid is ρfgVp, which must equal the weight of the body; ρsgVs = ρfgVpor ρs/ρf = Vp/Vs The apparent weight of the floating body is zero.','[{"s": "The result holds true irrespective of the shape of the object and here cylindrical object is considered only for convenience. This is Archimedes’ principle. For totally immersed objects the volume of the fluid displaced by the object is equal to its own volume. If the density of the immersed object is more than that of the fluid, the object will sink as the weight of the body is more than the upward thrust. If the density of the object is less than that of the fluid, it floats in the fluid partially submerged. To calculate the volume submerged, suppose the total volume of the object is Vs and a part Vp of it is submerged in the fluid. Then, the upward force which is the weight of the displaced fluid is ρfgVp, which must equal the weight of the body; ρsgVs = ρfgVpor ρs/ρf = Vp/Vs The apparent weight of the floating body is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26542,66,112,'paragraph',NULL,'This principle can be summarised as; ‘the loss of weight of a body submerged (partially or fully) in a fluid is equal to the weight of the fluid displaced’.','[{"s": "This principle can be summarised as; ‘the loss of weight of a body submerged (partially or fully) in a fluid is equal to the weight of the fluid displaced’.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26543,66,113,'paragraph',NULL,'This indicates that when the pressure on the cylinder was increased, it was distributed uniformly throughout. We can say whenever external pressure is applied on any part of a fluid contained in a vessel, it is transmitted undiminished and equally in all directions. This is another form of the Pascal’s law and it has many applications in daily life.','[{"s": "This indicates that when the pressure on the cylinder was increased, it was distributed uniformly throughout. We can say whenever external pressure is applied on any part of a fluid contained in a vessel, it is transmitted undiminished and equally in all directions. This is another form of the Pascal’s law and it has many applications in daily life.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26544,66,114,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image77.png'),
(26545,66,115,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 10.6 (a) Whenever external pressure is applied on any part of a fluid in a vessel, it is equally transmitted in all directions.','[{"s": "Fig 10.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Whenever external pressure is applied on any part of a fluid in a vessel, it is equally transmitted in all directions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26546,66,116,'paragraph',NULL,'Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image79.png is transmitted throughout the liquid to the larger cylinder attached with a larger piston of area A2, which results in an upward force of P × A2. Therefore, the piston is capable of supporting a large force (large weight of, say a car, or a truck, placed on the platform) F2 = PA2 = Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image81.png . By changing the force at A1, the platform can be moved up or down. Thus, the applied force has been increased by a factor of Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image83.png and this factor is the mechanical advantage of the device. The example below clarifies it.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image79.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is transmitted throughout the liquid to the larger cylinder attached with a larger piston of area A2, which results in an upward force of P × A2. Therefore, the piston is capable of supporting a large force (large weight of, say a car, or a truck, placed on the platform) F2 = PA2 =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image81.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". By changing the force at A1, the platform can be moved up or down. Thus, the applied force has been increased by a factor of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image83.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and this factor is the mechanical advantage of the device. The example below clarifies it.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26547,66,117,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image84.png'),
(26548,66,118,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 10.6 (b) Schematic diagram illustrating the principle behind the hydraulic lift, a device used to lift heavy loads.','[{"s": "Fig 10.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(b) Schematic diagram illustrating the principle behind the hydraulic lift, a device used to lift heavy loads.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26549,66,119,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.5 Two syringes of different cross-sections (without needles) filled with water are connected with a tightly fitted rubber tube filled with water. Diameters of the smaller piston and larger piston are 1.0 cm and 3.0 cm respectively. (a) Find the force exerted on the larger piston when a force of 10 N is applied to the smaller piston. (b) If the smaller piston is pushed in through 6.0 cm, how much does the larger piston move out?','[{"s": "Example 10.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two syringes of different cross-sections (without needles) filled with water are connected with a tightly fitted rubber tube filled with water. Diameters of the smaller piston and larger piston are 1.0 cm and 3.0 cm respectively. (a) Find the force exerted on the larger piston when a force of 10 N is applied to the smaller piston. (b) If the smaller piston is pushed in through 6.0 cm, how much does the larger piston move out?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26550,66,120,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer (a) Since pressure is transmitted undiminished throughout the fluid,','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Since pressure is transmitted undiminished throughout the fluid,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26551,66,121,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image85.png'),
(26552,66,122,'paragraph',NULL,'= 90 N','[{"s": "= 90 N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26553,66,123,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Water is considered to be perfectly incompressible. Volume covered by the movement of smaller piston inwards is equal to volume moved outwards due to the larger piston.','[{"s": "(b) Water is considered to be perfectly incompressible. Volume covered by the movement of smaller piston inwards is equal to volume moved outwards due to the larger piston.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26554,66,124,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image86.png'),
(26555,66,125,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image43.png'),
(26556,66,126,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.67 × 10-2 m = 0.67 cm','[{"s": "= 0.67 × 10-2 m = 0.67 cm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26557,66,127,'paragraph',NULL,'Note, atmospheric pressure is common to both pistons and has been ignored.','[{"s": "Note, atmospheric pressure is common to both pistons and has been ignored.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26558,66,128,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.6 In a car lift compressed air exerts a force F1 on a small piston having a radius of 5.0 cm. This pressure is transmitted to a second piston of radius 15 cm (Fig 10.7). If the mass of the car to be lifted is 1350 kg, calculate F1. What is the pressure necessary to accomplish this task? (g = 9.8 ms -2 ).','[{"s": "Example 10.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In a car lift compressed air exerts a force F1 on a small piston having a radius of 5.0 cm. This pressure is transmitted to a second piston of radius 15 cm (Fig 10.7). If the mass of the car to be lifted is 1350 kg, calculate F1. What is the pressure necessary to accomplish this task? (g = 9.8 ms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26559,66,129,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Since pressure is transmitted undiminished throughout the fluid,','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Since pressure is transmitted undiminished throughout the fluid,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26560,66,130,'image',NULL,'eq',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image45.png'),
(26561,66,131,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1470 N ≈ 1.5 × 103 N','[{"s": "= 1470 N ≈ 1.5 × 103 N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26562,66,132,'paragraph',NULL,'The air pressure that will produce this force is','[{"s": "The air pressure that will produce this force is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26563,66,133,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image47.png'),
(26564,66,134,'paragraph',NULL,'This is almost double the atmospheric pressure.','[{"s": "This is almost double the atmospheric pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26565,66,135,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image49.png'),
(26566,66,136,'paragraph',NULL,'Archimedes (287–212 B.C.)','[{"s": "Archimedes (287–212 B.C.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26567,66,137,'paragraph',NULL,'Archimedes was a Greek philosopher, mathematician, scientist and engineer. He invented the catapult and devised a system of pulleys and levers to handle heavy loads. The king of his native city Syracuse, Hiero II, asked him to determine if his gold crown was alloyed with some cheaper metal, such as silver without damaging the crown. The partial loss of weight he experienced while lying in his bathtub suggested a solution to him. According to legend, he ran naked through the streets of Syracuse, exclaiming “Eureka, eureka!”, which means “I have found it, I have found it!”','[{"s": "Archimedes was a Greek philosopher, mathematician, scientist and engineer. He invented the catapult and devised a system of pulleys and levers to handle heavy loads. The king of his native city Syracuse, Hiero II, asked him to determine if his gold crown was alloyed with some cheaper metal, such as silver without damaging the crown. The partial loss of weight he experienced while lying in his bathtub suggested a solution to him. According to legend, he ran naked through the streets of Syracuse, exclaiming “Eureka, eureka!”, which means “I have found it, I have found it!”", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26568,66,138,'paragraph',NULL,'Hydraulic brakes in automobiles also work on the same principle. When we apply a little force on the pedal with our foot the master piston moves inside the master cylinder, and the pressure caused is transmitted through the brake oil to act on a piston of larger area. A large force acts on the piston and is pushed down expanding the brake shoes against brake lining. In this way, a small force on the pedal produces a large retarding force on the wheel. An important advantage of the system is that the pressure set up by pressing pedal is transmitted equally to all cylinders attached to the four wheels so that the braking effort is equal on all wheels.','[{"s": "Hydraulic brakes in automobiles also work on the same principle. When we apply a little force on the pedal with our foot the master piston moves inside the master cylinder, and the pressure caused is transmitted through the brake oil to act on a piston of larger area. A large force acts on the piston and is pushed down expanding the brake shoes against brake lining. In this way, a small force on the pedal produces a large retarding force on the wheel. An important advantage of the system is that the pressure set up by pressing pedal is transmitted equally to all cylinders attached to the four wheels so that the braking effort is equal on all wheels.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26569,66,139,'heading',4,'10.3 STREAMLINE FLOW',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26570,66,140,'paragraph',NULL,'So far we have studied fluids at rest. The study of the fluids in motion is known as fluid dynamics. When a water tap is turned on slowly, the water flow is smooth initially, but loses its smoothness when the speed of the outflow is increased. In studying the motion of fluids, we focus our attention on what is happening to various fluid particles at a particular point in space at a particular time. The flow of the fluid is said to be steady if at any given point, the velocity of each passing fluid particle remains constant in time. This does not mean that the velocity at different points in space is same. The velocity of a particular particle may change as it moves from one point to another. That is, at some other point the particle may have a different velocity, but every other particle which passes the second point behaves exactly as the previous particle that has just passed that point. Each particle follows a smooth path, and the paths of the particles do not cross each other.','[{"s": "So far we have studied fluids at rest. The study of the fluids in motion is known as fluid dynamics. When a water tap is turned on slowly, the water flow is smooth initially, but loses its smoothness when the speed of the outflow is increased. In studying the motion of fluids, we focus our attention on what is happening to various fluid particles at a particular point in space at a particular time. The flow of the fluid is said to be steady if at any given point, the velocity of each passing fluid particle remains constant in time. This does not mean that the velocity at different points in space is same. The velocity of a particular particle may change as it moves from one point to another. That is, at some other point the particle may have a different velocity, but every other particle which passes the second point behaves exactly as the previous particle that has just passed that point. Each particle follows a smooth path, and the paths of the particles do not cross each other.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26571,66,141,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image51.png'),
(26572,66,142,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 10.7 The meaning of streamlines. (a) A typical trajectory of a fluid particle.','[{"s": "Fig. 10.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The meaning of streamlines. (a) A typical trajectory of a fluid particle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26573,66,143,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) A region of streamline flow.','[{"s": "(b) A region of streamline flow.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26574,66,144,'paragraph',NULL,'The path taken by a fluid particle under a steady flow is a streamline. It is defined as a curve whose tangent at any point is in the direction of the fluid velocity at that point. Consider the path of a particle as shown in Fig.10.7 (a), the curve describes how a fluid particle moves with time. The curve PQ is like a permanent map of fluid flow, indicating how the fluid streams. No two streamlines can cross, for if they do, an oncoming fluid particle can go either one way or the other and the flow would not be steady. Hence, in steady flow, the map of flow is stationary in time. How do we draw closely spaced streamlines ? If we intend to show streamline of every flowing particle, we would end up with a continuum of lines. Consider planes perpendicular to the direction of fluid flow e.g., at three points P, R and Q in Fig.10.7 (b). The plane pieces are so chosen that their boundaries be determined by the same set of streamlines. This means that number of fluid particles crossing the surfaces as indicated at P, R and Q is the same. If area of cross-sections at these points are AP,AR and AQ and speeds of fluid particles are vP, vR and vQ, then mass of fluid ∆mP crossing at AP in a small interval of time ∆t is ρPAPvP ∆t. Similarly mass of fluid ∆mR flowing or crossing at AR in a small interval of time ∆t is ρRARvR ∆t and mass of fluid ∆mQ is ρQAQvQ ∆t crossing at AQ. The mass of liquid flowing out equals the mass flowing in, holds in all cases. Therefore,','[{"s": "The path taken by a fluid particle under a steady flow is a streamline. It is defined as a curve whose tangent at any point is in the direction of the fluid velocity at that point. Consider the path of a particle as shown in Fig.10.7 (a), the curve describes how a fluid particle moves with time. The curve PQ is like a permanent map of fluid flow, indicating how the fluid streams. No two streamlines can cross, for if they do, an oncoming fluid particle can go either one way or the other and the flow would not be steady. Hence, in steady flow, the map of flow is stationary in time. How do we draw closely spaced streamlines ? If we intend to show streamline of every flowing particle, we would end up with a continuum of lines. Consider planes perpendicular to the direction of fluid flow e.g., at three points P, R and Q in Fig.10.7 (b). The plane pieces are so chosen that their boundaries be determined by the same set of streamlines. This means that number of fluid particles crossing the surfaces as indicated at P, R and Q is the same. If area of cross-sections at these points are AP,AR and AQ and speeds of fluid particles are vP, vR and vQ, then mass of fluid ∆mP crossing at AP in a small interval of time ∆t is ρPAPvP ∆t. Similarly mass of fluid ∆mR flowing or crossing at AR in a small interval of time ∆t is ρRARvR ∆t and mass of fluid ∆mQ is ρQAQvQ ∆t crossing at AQ. The mass of liquid flowing out equals the mass flowing in, holds in all cases. Therefore,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26575,66,145,'paragraph',NULL,'ρPAPvP∆t = ρRARvR∆t = ρQAQvQ∆t (10.9)','[{"s": "ρPAPvP∆t = ρRARvR∆t = ρQAQvQ∆t (10.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26576,66,146,'paragraph',NULL,'For flow of incompressible fluids','[{"s": "For flow of incompressible fluids", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26577,66,147,'paragraph',NULL,'ρP = ρR = ρQ','[{"s": "ρP = ρR = ρQ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26578,66,148,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (10.9) reduces to','[{"s": "Equation (10.9) reduces to", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26579,66,149,'paragraph',NULL,'APvP = ARvR = AQvQ (10.10)','[{"s": "APvP = ARvR = AQvQ (10.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26581,66,151,'paragraph',NULL,'Av = constant (10.11)','[{"s": "Av = constant (10.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26582,66,152,'paragraph',NULL,'Av gives the volume flux or flow rate and remains constant throughout the pipe of flow. Thus, at narrower portions where the streamlines are closely spaced, velocity increases and its vice versa. From (Fig 10.7b) it is clear that AR > AQ or vR < vQ, the fluid is accelerated while passing from R to Q. This is associated with a change in pressure in fluid flow in horizontal pipes.','[{"s": "Av gives the volume flux or flow rate and remains constant throughout the pipe of flow. Thus, at narrower portions where the streamlines are closely spaced, velocity increases and its vice versa. From (Fig 10.7b) it is clear that AR > AQ or vR < vQ, the fluid is accelerated while passing from R to Q. This is associated with a change in pressure in fluid flow in horizontal pipes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26583,66,153,'paragraph',NULL,'Steady flow is achieved at low flow speeds. Beyond a limiting value, called critical speed, this flow loses steadiness and becomes turbulent. One sees this when a fast flowing stream encounters rocks, small foamy whirlpool-like regions called ‘white water rapids are formed.','[{"s": "Steady flow is achieved at low flow speeds. Beyond a limiting value, called critical speed, this flow loses steadiness and becomes turbulent. One sees this when a fast flowing stream encounters rocks, small foamy whirlpool-like regions called ‘white water rapids are formed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26584,66,154,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image53.png'),
(26585,66,155,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 10.8 (a) Some streamlines for fluid flow.','[{"s": "Fig. 10.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Some streamlines for fluid flow.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26586,66,156,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) A jet of air striking a flat plate placed perpendicular to it. This is an example','[{"s": "(b) A jet of air striking a flat plate placed perpendicular to it. This is an example", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26587,66,157,'paragraph',NULL,'of turbulent flow.','[{"s": "of turbulent flow.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26588,66,158,'heading',4,'10.4 BERNOULLI’S PRINCIPLE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26589,66,159,'paragraph',NULL,'Fluid flow is a complex phenomenon. But we can obtain some useful properties for steady or streamline flows using the conservation of energy.','[{"s": "Fluid flow is a complex phenomenon. But we can obtain some useful properties for steady or streamline flows using the conservation of energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26590,66,160,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a fluid moving in a pipe of varying cross-sectional area. Let the pipe be at varying heights as shown in Fig. 10.9. We now suppose that an incompressible fluid is flowing through the pipe in a steady flow. Its velocity must change as a consequence of equation of continuity. A force is required to produce this acceleration, which is caused by the fluid surrounding it, the pressure must be different in different regions. Bernoulli’s equation is a general expression that relates the pressure difference between two points in a pipe to both velocity changes (kinetic energy change) and elevation (height) changes (potential energy change). The Swiss Physicist Daniel Bernoulli developed this relationship in 1738.','[{"s": "Consider a fluid moving in a pipe of varying cross-sectional area. Let the pipe be at varying heights as shown in Fig. 10.9. We now suppose that an incompressible fluid is flowing through the pipe in a steady flow. Its velocity must change as a consequence of equation of continuity. A force is required to produce this acceleration, which is caused by the fluid surrounding it, the pressure must be different in different regions. Bernoulli’s equation is a general expression that relates the pressure difference between two points in a pipe to both velocity changes (kinetic energy change) and elevation (height) changes (potential energy change). The Swiss Physicist Daniel Bernoulli developed this relationship in 1738.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26591,66,161,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider the flow at two regions 1 (i.e., BC) and 2 (i.e., DE). Consider the fluid initially lying between B and D. In an infinitesimal time interval ∆t, this fluid would have moved. Suppose v1 is the speed at B and v2 at D, then fluid initially at B has moved a distance v1∆t to C (v1∆t is small enough to assume constant cross-section along BC). In the same interval ∆t the fluid initially at D moves to E, a distance equal to v2∆t. Pressures P1 and P2 act as shown on the plane faces of areas A1 and A2 binding the two regions. The work done on the fluid at left end (BC) is W1 = P1A1(v1∆t) = P1∆V. Since the same volume ∆V passes through both the regions (from the equation of continuity) the work done by the fluid at the other end (DE) is W2 = P2A2(v2∆t) = P2∆V or, the work done on the fluid is –P2∆V. So the total work done on the fluid is','[{"s": "Consider the flow at two regions 1 (i.e., BC) and 2 (i.e., DE). Consider the fluid initially lying between B and D. In an infinitesimal time interval ∆t, this fluid would have moved. Suppose v1 is the speed at B and v2 at D, then fluid initially at B has moved a distance v1∆t to C (v1∆t is small enough to assume constant cross-section along BC). In the same interval ∆t the fluid initially at D moves to E, a distance equal to v2∆t. Pressures P1 and P2 act as shown on the plane faces of areas A1 and A2 binding the two regions. The work done on the fluid at left end (BC) is W1 = P1A1(v1∆t) = P1∆V. Since the same volume ∆V passes through both the regions (from the equation of continuity) the work done by the fluid at the other end (DE) is W2 = P2A2(v2∆t) = P2∆V or, the work done on the fluid is –P2∆V. So the total work done on the fluid is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26592,66,162,'paragraph',NULL,'W1 – W2 = (P1− P2) ∆V','[{"s": "W1 – W2 = (P1− P2) ∆V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26593,66,163,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image55.png'),
(26594,66,164,'paragraph',NULL,'Daniel Bernoulli (1700–1782)','[{"s": "Daniel Bernoulli (1700–1782)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26595,66,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Daniel Bernoulli was a Swiss scientist and mathematician, who along with Leonard Euler had the distinction of winning the French Academy prize for mathematics 10 times. He also studied medicine and served as a professor of anatomy and botany for a while at Basle, Switzerland. His most well-known work was in hydrodynamics, a subject he developed from a single principle: the conservation of energy. His work included calculus, probability, the theory of vibrating strings, and applied mathematics. He has been called the founder of mathematical physics.','[{"s": "Daniel Bernoulli was a Swiss scientist and mathematician, who along with Leonard Euler had the distinction of winning the French Academy prize for mathematics 10 times. He also studied medicine and served as a professor of anatomy and botany for a while at Basle, Switzerland. His most well-known work was in hydrodynamics, a subject he developed from a single principle: the conservation of energy. His work included calculus, probability, the theory of vibrating strings, and applied mathematics. He has been called the founder of mathematical physics.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26596,66,166,'paragraph',NULL,'Part of this work goes into changing the kinetic energy of the fluid, and part goes into changing the gravitational potential energy. If the density of the fluid is ρ and ∆m = ρA1v1∆t = ρ∆V is the mass passing through the pipe in time ∆t, then change in gravitational potential energy is','[{"s": "Part of this work goes into changing the kinetic energy of the fluid, and part goes into changing the gravitational potential energy. If the density of the fluid is ρ and ∆m = ρA1v1∆t = ρ∆V is the mass passing through the pipe in time ∆t, then change in gravitational potential energy is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26597,66,167,'paragraph',NULL,'∆U = ρg∆V (h2 − h1)','[{"s": "∆U = ρg∆V (h2 − h1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26598,66,168,'paragraph',NULL,'The change in its kinetic energy is','[{"s": "The change in its kinetic energy is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(26599,66,169,'paragraph',NULL,'∆K = Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image24.png ρ ∆V (v22 − v12)','[{"s": "∆K =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Mechanical_Properties_of_Fluids-image24.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "ρ ∆V (v22 − v12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
