(27243,67,239,'paragraph',NULL,'This work is stored in the wire in the form of elastic potential energy (U). Therefore the elastic potential energy per unit volume of the wire (u) is','[{"s": "This work is stored in the wire in the form of elastic potential energy (U). Therefore the elastic potential energy per unit volume of the wire (u) is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27244,67,240,'paragraph',NULL,'u = Mechanical_Properties_of_Solids-image11.png × Mechanical_Properties_of_Solids-image12.png (9.15)','[{"s": "u =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Mechanical_Properties_of_Solids-image11.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Mechanical_Properties_of_Solids-image12.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(9.15)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27245,67,241,'heading',4,'9.7 Applications of elastic behaviour of materials',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27246,67,242,'paragraph',NULL,'The elastic behaviour of materials plays an important role in everyday life. All engineering designs require precise knowledge of the elastic behaviour of materials. For example while designing a building, the structural design of the columns, beams and supports require knowledge of strength of materials used. Have you ever thought why the beams used in construction of bridges, as supports etc. have a cross-section of the type I? Why does a heap of sand or a hill have a pyramidal shape? Answers to these questions can be obtained from the study of structural engineering which is based on concepts developed here.','[{"s": "The elastic behaviour of materials plays an important role in everyday life. All engineering designs require precise knowledge of the elastic behaviour of materials. For example while designing a building, the structural design of the columns, beams and supports require knowledge of strength of materials used. Have you ever thought why the beams used in construction of bridges, as supports etc. have a cross-section of the type I? Why does a heap of sand or a hill have a pyramidal shape? Answers to these questions can be obtained from the study of structural engineering which is based on concepts developed here.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34353,79,273,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 10.19 A parallel beam of light is incident on a convex lens. Because of diffraction effects, the beam gets focused to a spot of radius ≈ 0.61 λ f/a.','[{"s": "Figure 10.19 A parallel beam of light is incident on a convex lens.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Because of diffraction effects, the beam gets focused to a spot of radius", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≈", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0.61", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f/a.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031814", "2000032087", "2000033079", "2000112647"]',NULL),
(34358,79,278,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.6 Assume that light of wavelength 6000Å is coming from a star. What is the limit of resolution of a telescope whose objective has a diameter of 100 inch?','[{"s": "Example 10.6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Assume that light of wavelength 6000Å is coming from a star.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What is the limit of resolution of a telescope whose objective has a diameter of 100 inch?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031817", "2000032095", "2000033083", "2000112649"]',NULL),
(34374,79,294,'paragraph',NULL,'Two objects whose images are closer than this distance will not be resolved, they will be seen as one . The corresponding minimum separation, d min, in the object plane is given by','[{"s": "Two objects whose images are closer than this distance will not be resolved, they will be seen as one", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The corresponding minimum separation,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "min, in the object plane is given by", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031822", "2000032099", "2000033086", "2000112650"]',NULL),
(27247,67,243,'paragraph',NULL,'Cranes used for lifting and moving heavy loads from one place to another have a thick metal rope to which the load is attached. The rope is pulled up using pulleys and motors. Suppose we want to make a crane, which has a lifting capacity of 10 tonnes or metric tons (1 metric ton = 1000 kg). How thick should the steel rope be? We obviously want that the load does not deform the rope permanently. Therefore, the extension should not exceed the elastic limit. From Table 9.1, we find that mild steel has a yield strength (σy) of about 300 × 10 6 N m –2 . Thus, the area of cross-section (A) of the rope should at least be','[{"s": "Cranes used for lifting and moving heavy loads from one place to another have a thick metal rope to which the load is attached. The rope is pulled up using pulleys and motors. Suppose we want to make a crane, which has a lifting capacity of 10 tonnes or metric tons (1 metric ton = 1000 kg). How thick should the steel rope be? We obviously want that the load does not deform the rope permanently. Therefore, the extension should not exceed the elastic limit. From Table 9.1, we find that mild steel has a yield strength (σy) of about 300 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, the area of cross-section (A) of the rope should at least be", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27248,67,244,'paragraph',NULL,'A ≥ W/σy = Mg/σy (9.16)','[{"s": "A ≥ W/σy = Mg/σy (9.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27249,67,245,'paragraph',NULL,'= (10 4 kg × 9.8 m s -2 )/(300 × 10 6 N m -2 )','[{"s": "= (10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg × 9.8 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")/(300 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27250,67,246,'paragraph',NULL,'= 3.3 × 10 -4 m 2','[{"s": "= 3.3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27251,67,247,'paragraph',NULL,'corresponding to a radius of about 1 cm for a rope of circular cross-section. Generally a large margin of safety (of about a factor of ten in the load) is provided. Thus a thicker rope of radius about 3 cm is recommended. A single wire of this radius would practically be a rigid rod. So the ropes are always made of a number of thin wires braided together, like in pigtails, for ease in manufacture, flexibility and strength.','[{"s": "corresponding to a radius of about 1 cm for a rope of circular cross-section. Generally a large margin of safety (of about a factor of ten in the load) is provided. Thus a thicker rope of radius about 3 cm is recommended. A single wire of this radius would practically be a rigid rod. So the ropes are always made of a number of thin wires braided together, like in pigtails, for ease in manufacture, flexibility and strength.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27252,67,248,'paragraph',NULL,'A bridge has to be designed such that it can withstand the load of the flowing traffic, the force of winds and its own weight. Similarly, in the design of buildings the use of beams and columns is very common. In both the cases, the overcoming of the problem of bending of beam under a load is of prime importance. The beam should not bend too much or break. Let us consider the case of a beam loaded at the centre and supported near its ends as shown in','[{"s": "A bridge has to be designed such that it can withstand the load of the flowing traffic, the force of winds and its own weight. Similarly, in the design of buildings the use of beams and columns is very common. In both the cases, the overcoming of the problem of bending of beam under a load is of prime importance. The beam should not bend too much or break. Let us consider the case of a beam loaded at the centre and supported near its ends as shown in", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27253,67,249,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 9.8. A bar of length l, breadth b, and depth d when loaded at the centre by a load W sags by an amount given by','[{"s": "Fig. 9.8. A bar of length l, breadth b, and depth d when loaded at the centre by a load W sags by an amount given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27254,67,250,'paragraph',NULL,'δ = W l 3 /(4bd 3 Y) (9.17)','[{"s": "δ = W l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/(4bd", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Y) (9.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27255,67,251,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Mechanical_Properties_of_Solids-image29.png'),
(27256,67,252,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 9.8 A beam supported at the ends and loaded at the centre.','[{"s": "Fig. 9.8 A beam supported at the ends and loaded at the centre.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27257,67,253,'paragraph',NULL,'This relation can be derived using what you have already learnt and a little calculus. From Eq. (9.16), we see that to reduce the bending for a given load, one should use a material with a large Young’s modulus Y. For a given material, increasing the depth d rather than the breadth b is more effective in reducing the bending, since δ is proportional to d -3 and only to b -1 (of course the length l of the span should be as small as possible). But on increasing the depth, unless the load is exactly at the right place (difficult to arrange in a bridge with moving traffic), the deep bar may bend as shown in Fig. 9.9(b). This is called buckling. To avoid this, a common compromise is the cross-sectional shape shown in Fig. 9.9(c). This section provides a large load-bearing surface and enough depth to prevent bending. This shape reduces the weight of the beam without sacrificing the strength and hence reduces the cost.','[{"s": "This relation can be derived using what you have already learnt and a little calculus. From Eq. (9.16), we see that to reduce the bending for a given load, one should use a material with a large Young’s modulus Y. For a given material, increasing the depth d rather than the breadth b is more effective in reducing the bending, since δ is proportional to d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and only to b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(of course the length l of the span should be as small as possible). But on increasing the depth, unless the load is exactly at the right place (difficult to arrange in a bridge with moving traffic), the deep bar may bend as shown in Fig. 9.9(b). This is called buckling. To avoid this, a common compromise is the cross-sectional shape shown in Fig. 9.9(c). This section provides a large load-bearing surface and enough depth to prevent bending. This shape reduces the weight of the beam without sacrificing the strength and hence reduces the cost.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
