(27264,67,260,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 9.10 Pillars or columns: (a) a pillar with rounded ends, (b) Pillar with distributed ends.','[{"s": "Fig. 9.10 Pillars or columns: (a) a pillar with rounded ends, (b) Pillar with distributed ends.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34382,79,302,'paragraph',NULL,'If the medium between the object and the objective lens is not air but a medium of refractive index n , Eq. (10.29) gets modified to','[{"s": "If the medium between the object and the objective lens is not air but a medium of refractive index", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "n", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", Eq.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(10.29) gets modified to", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031828", "2000032105", "2000033103"]',NULL),
(27265,67,261,'paragraph',NULL,'The answer to the question why the maximum height of a mountain on earth is ~10 km can also be provided by considering the elastic properties of rocks. A mountain base is not under uniform compression and this provides some shearing stress to the rocks under which they can flow. The stress due to all the material on the top should be less than the critical shearing stress at which the rocks flow.','[{"s": "The answer to the question why the maximum height of a mountain on earth is ~10 km can also be provided by considering the elastic properties of rocks. A mountain base is not under uniform compression and this provides some shearing stress to the rocks under which they can flow. The stress due to all the material on the top should be less than the critical shearing stress at which the rocks flow.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27266,67,262,'paragraph',NULL,'At the bottom of a mountain of height h, the force per unit area due to the weight of the mountain is hρg where ρ is the density of the material of the mountain and g is the acceleration due to gravity. The material at the bottom experiences this force in the vertical direction, and the sides of the mountain are free. Therefore, this is not a case of pressure or bulk compression. There is a shear component, approximately hρg itself. Now the elastic limit for a typical rock is 30 × 10 7 N m -2 3 kg m -3 gives','[{"s": "At the bottom of a mountain of height h, the force per unit area due to the weight of the mountain is hρg where ρ is the density of the material of the mountain and g is the acceleration due to gravity. The material at the bottom experiences this force in the vertical direction, and the sides of the mountain are free. Therefore, this is not a case of pressure or bulk compression. There is a shear component, approximately hρg itself. Now the elastic limit for a typical rock is 30 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27267,67,263,'paragraph',NULL,'hρg = 30 × 10 7 N m -2 .','[{"s": "hρg = 30 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27268,67,264,'paragraph',NULL,'h = 30 × 10 7 N m -2 /(3 × 10 3 kg m -3 × 10 m s -2 )','[{"s": "h = 30 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/(3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× 10 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27269,67,265,'paragraph',NULL,'= 10 km','[{"s": "= 10 km", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27270,67,266,'paragraph',NULL,'which is more than the height of Mt. Everest!','[{"s": "which is more than the height of Mt. Everest!", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27271,67,267,'heading',4,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27272,67,268,'paragraph',NULL,'2. For small deformations, stress is directly proportional to the strain for many materials. This is known as Hooke’s law. The constant of proportionality is called modulus of elasticity. Three elastic moduli viz., Young’s modulus, shear modulus and bulk modulus are used to describe the elastic behaviour of objects as they respond to deforming forces that act on them.','[{"s": "2. For small deformations, stress is directly proportional to the strain for many materials. This is known as Hooke’s law. The constant of proportionality is called modulus of elasticity. Three elastic moduli viz., Young’s modulus, shear modulus and bulk modulus are used to describe the elastic behaviour of objects as they respond to deforming forces that act on them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27273,67,269,'paragraph',NULL,'A class of solids called elastomers does not obey Hooke’s law.','[{"s": "A class of solids called elastomers does not obey Hooke’s law.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27274,67,270,'paragraph',NULL,'3. When an object is under tension or compression, the Hooke’s law takes the form','[{"s": "3. When an object is under tension or compression, the Hooke’s law takes the form", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27275,67,271,'paragraph',NULL,'F/A = Y∆L/L','[{"s": "F/A = Y∆L/L", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27276,67,272,'paragraph',NULL,'where ∆L/L is the tensile or compressive strain of the object, F is the magnitude of the applied force causing the strain, A is the cross-sectional area over which F is applied (perpendicular to A) and Y is the Young’s modulus for the object. The stress is F/A.','[{"s": "where ∆L/L is the tensile or compressive strain of the object, F is the magnitude of the applied force causing the strain, A is the cross-sectional area over which F is applied (perpendicular to A) and Y is the Young’s modulus for the object. The stress is F/A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27277,67,273,'paragraph',NULL,'4. A pair of forces when applied parallel to the upper and lower faces, the solid deforms so that the upper face moves sideways with respect to the lower. The horizontal displacement ∆L of the upper face is perpendicular to the vertical height L. This type of deformation is called shear and the corresponding stress is the shearing stress. This type of stress is possible only in solids.','[{"s": "4. A pair of forces when applied parallel to the upper and lower faces, the solid deforms so that the upper face moves sideways with respect to the lower. The horizontal displacement ∆L of the upper face is perpendicular to the vertical height L. This type of deformation is called shear and the corresponding stress is the shearing stress. This type of stress is possible only in solids.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27278,67,274,'paragraph',NULL,'In this kind of deformation the Hooke’s law takes the form','[{"s": "In this kind of deformation the Hooke’s law takes the form", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27279,67,275,'paragraph',NULL,'F/A = G × ∆L/L','[{"s": "F/A = G × ∆L/L", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27280,67,276,'paragraph',NULL,'where ∆L is the displacement of one end of object in the direction of the applied force F, and G is the shear modulus.','[{"s": "where ∆L is the displacement of one end of object in the direction of the applied force F, and G is the shear modulus.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27281,67,277,'paragraph',NULL,'5. When an object undergoes hydraulic compression due to a stress exerted by a surrounding fluid, the Hooke’s law takes the form','[{"s": "5. When an object undergoes hydraulic compression due to a stress exerted by a surrounding fluid, the Hooke’s law takes the form", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27282,67,278,'paragraph',NULL,'p = B (∆V/V),','[{"s": "p = B (∆V/V),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34791,80,224,'paragraph',NULL,'The net displacement is then, by the principle of superposition, given by 2','[{"s": "The net displacement is then, by the principle of superposition, given by", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040490", "2000109213"]',NULL),
(27283,67,279,'paragraph',NULL,'where p is the pressure (hydraulic stress) on the object due to the fluid, ∆V/V (the volume strain) is the absolute fractional change in the object’s volume due to that pressure and B is the bulk modulus of the object.','[{"s": "where p is the pressure (hydraulic stress) on the object due to the fluid, ∆V/V (the volume strain) is the absolute fractional change in the object’s volume due to that pressure and B is the bulk modulus of the object.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27284,67,280,'heading',4,'Points to ponder',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27285,67,281,'paragraph',NULL,'1. In the case of a wire, suspended from celing and stretched under the action of a weight (F) suspended from its other end, the force exerted by the ceiling on it is equal and opposite to the weight. However, the tension at any cross-section A of the wire is just F and not 2F. Hence, tensile stress which is equal to the tension per unit area is equal to F/A.','[{"s": "1. In the case of a wire, suspended from celing and stretched under the action of a weight (F) suspended from its other end, the force exerted by the ceiling on it is equal and opposite to the weight. However, the tension at any cross-section A of the wire is just F and not 2F. Hence, tensile stress which is equal to the tension per unit area is equal to F/A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27286,67,282,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Hooke’s law is valid only in the linear part of stress-strain curve.','[{"s": "2. Hooke’s law is valid only in the linear part of stress-strain curve.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27287,67,283,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The Young’s modulus and shear modulus are relevant only for solids since only solids have lengths and shapes.','[{"s": "3. The Young’s modulus and shear modulus are relevant only for solids since only solids have lengths and shapes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27288,67,284,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Bulk modulus is relevant for solids, liquid and gases. It refers to the change in volume when every part of the body is under the uniform stress so that the shape of the body remains unchanged.','[{"s": "4. Bulk modulus is relevant for solids, liquid and gases. It refers to the change in volume when every part of the body is under the uniform stress so that the shape of the body remains unchanged.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27289,67,285,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Metals have larger values of Young’s modulus than alloys and elastomers. A material with large value of Young’s modulus requires a large force to produce small changes in its length.','[{"s": "5. Metals have larger values of Young’s modulus than alloys and elastomers. A material with large value of Young’s modulus requires a large force to produce small changes in its length.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27290,67,286,'paragraph',NULL,'6. In daily life, we feel that a material which stretches more is more elastic, but it a is misnomer. In fact material which stretches to a lesser extent for a given load is considered to be more elastic.','[{"s": "6. In daily life, we feel that a material which stretches more is more elastic, but it a is misnomer. In fact material which stretches to a lesser extent for a given load is considered to be more elastic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27291,67,287,'paragraph',NULL,'7. In general, a deforming force in one direction can produce strains in other directions also. The proportionality between stress and strain in such situations cannot be described by just one elastic constant. For example, for a wire under longitudinal strain, the lateral dimensions (radius of cross section) will undergo a small change, which is described by another elastic constant of the material (called Poisson ratio).','[{"s": "7. In general, a deforming force in one direction can produce strains in other directions also. The proportionality between stress and strain in such situations cannot be described by just one elastic constant. For example, for a wire under longitudinal strain, the lateral dimensions (radius of cross section) will undergo a small change, which is described by another elastic constant of the material (called Poisson ratio).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27292,67,288,'paragraph',NULL,'8. Stress is not a vector quantity since, unlike a force, the stress cannot be assigned a specific direction. Force acting on the portion of a body on a specified side of a section has a definite direction.','[{"s": "8. Stress is not a vector quantity since, unlike a force, the stress cannot be assigned a specific direction. Force acting on the portion of a body on a specified side of a section has a definite direction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27293,68,0,'heading',2,'4.1 INTRODUCTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27294,68,1,'paragraph',NULL,'In the last chapter we developed the concepts of position, displacement, velocity and acceleration that are needed to describe the motion of an object along a straight line. We found that the directional aspect of these quantities can be taken care of by + and – signs, as in one dimension only two directions are possible. But in order to describe motion of an object in two dimensions (a plane) or three dimensions (space), we need to use vectors to describe the above-mentioned physical quantities. Therefore, it is first necessary to learn the language of vectors. What is a vector? How to add, subtract and multiply vectors ? What is the result of multiplying a vector by a real number ? We shall learn this to enable us to use vectors for defining velocity and acceleration in a plane. We then discuss motion of an object in a plane. As a simple case of motion in a plane, we shall discuss motion with constant acceleration and treat in detail the projectile motion. Circular motion is a familiar class of motion that has a special significance in daily-life situations. We shall discuss uniform circular motion in some detail.','[{"s": "In the last chapter we developed the concepts of position, displacement, velocity and acceleration that are needed to describe the motion of an object along a straight line. We found that the directional aspect of these quantities can be taken care of by + and – signs, as in one dimension only two directions are possible. But in order to describe motion of an object in two dimensions (a plane) or three dimensions (space), we need to use vectors to describe the above-mentioned physical quantities. Therefore, it is first necessary to learn the language of vectors. What is a vector? How to add, subtract and multiply vectors ? What is the result of multiplying a vector by a real number ? We shall learn this to enable us to use vectors for defining velocity and acceleration in a plane. We then discuss motion of an object in a plane. As a simple case of motion in a plane, we shall discuss motion with constant acceleration and treat in detail the projectile motion. Circular motion is a familiar class of motion that has a special significance in daily-life situations. We shall discuss uniform circular motion in some detail.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27295,68,2,'paragraph',NULL,'The equations developed in this chapter for motion in a plane can be easily extended to the case of three dimensions.','[{"s": "The equations developed in this chapter for motion in a plane can be easily extended to the case of three dimensions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27296,68,3,'heading',2,'4.2 SCALARS AND VECTORS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34838,80,271,'paragraph',NULL,'Clearly, the distance between any two successive nodes is λ2 . In the same way, the positions of antinodes (where the amplitude is the largest) are given by the largest value of sin kx :','[{"s": "Clearly, the distance between any two successive nodes is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "λ2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In the same way, the positions of antinodes (where the amplitude is the largest) are given by the largest value of sin kx :", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000040493", "2000109227"]',NULL),
(27297,68,4,'paragraph',NULL,'In physics, we can classify quantities as scalars or vectors. Basically, the difference is that a direction is associated with a vector but not with a scalar. A scalar quantity is a quantity with magnitude only. It is specified completely by a single number, along with the proper unit. Examples are : the distance between two points, mass of an object, the temperature of a body and the time at which a certain event happened. The rules for combining scalars are the rules of ordinary algebra. Scalars can be added, subtracted, multiplied and divided just as the ordinary numbers*. For example, if the length and breadth of a rectangle are 1.0 m and 0.5 m respectively, then its perimeter is the sum of the lengths of the four sides, 1.0 m + 0.5 m +1.0 m + 0.5 m = 3.0 m. The length of each side is a scalar and the perimeter is also a scalar. Take another example: the maximum and minimum temperatures on a particular day are 35.6 °C and 24.2 °C respectively. Then, the difference between the two temperatures is 11.4 °C. Similarly, if a uniform solid cube of aluminium of side 10 cm has a mass of 2.7 kg, then its volume is 10 –3 m 3 (a scalar) and its density is 2.7×10 3 kg m –3 (a scalar).','[{"s": "In physics, we can classify quantities as scalars or vectors. Basically, the difference is that a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "direction", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is associated with a vector but not with a scalar. A scalar quantity is a quantity with magnitude only. It is specified completely by a single number, along with the proper unit. Examples are : the distance between two points, mass of an object, the temperature of a body and the time at which a certain event happened. The rules for combining scalars are the rules of ordinary algebra. Scalars can be added, subtracted, multiplied and divided just as the ordinary numbers*. For example, if the length and breadth of a rectangle are 1.0 m and 0.5 m respectively, then its perimeter is the sum of the lengths of the four sides, 1.0 m + 0.5 m +1.0 m + 0.5 m = 3.0 m. The length of each side is a scalar and the perimeter is also a scalar. Take another example: the maximum and minimum temperatures on a particular day are 35.6 °C and 24.2 °C respectively. Then, the difference between the two temperatures is 11.4 °C. Similarly, if a uniform solid cube of aluminium of side 10 cm has a mass of 2.7 kg, then its volume is 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a scalar) and its density is 2.7×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a scalar).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27298,68,5,'paragraph',NULL,'A vector quantity is a quantity that has both a magnitude and a direction and obeys the triangle law of addition or equivalently the parallelogram law of addition . So, a vector is specified by giving its magnitude by a number and its direction. Some physical quantities that are represented by vectors are displacement, velocity, acceleration and force.','[{"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "quantity is a quantity that has both a magnitude and a direction and obeys the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "triangle law of addition", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or equivalently the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallelogram law of addition", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". So, a vector is specified by giving its magnitude by a number and its direction. Some physical quantities that are represented by vectors are displacement, velocity, acceleration and force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27299,68,6,'paragraph',NULL,'To represent a vector, we use a bold face type in this book. Thus, a velocity vector can be represented by a symbol v . Since bold face is difficult to produce, when written by hand, a vector is often represented by an arrow placed over a letter, say Motion_in_a_Plane-image138.png . Thus, both v and Motion_in_a_Plane-image138.png represent the velocity vector. The magnitude of a vector is often called its absolute value, indicated by | v | = v. Thus, a vector is represented by a bold face, e.g. by A, a, p, q, r, ... x, y , with respective magnitudes denoted by light face A, a, p, q, r, ... x, y.','[{"s": "To represent a vector, we use a bold face type in this book. Thus, a velocity vector can be represented by a symbol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since bold face is difficult to produce, when written by hand, a vector is often represented by an arrow placed over a letter, say", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Plane-image138.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". Thus, both", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Plane-image138.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "represent the velocity vector. The magnitude of a vector is often called its absolute value, indicated by |", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "| = v. Thus, a vector is represented by a bold face, e.g. by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A, a, p, q, r, ... x, y", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", with respective magnitudes denoted by light face A, a, p, q, r, ... x, y.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27300,68,7,'heading',3,'4.2.1 POSITION AND DISPLACEMENT VECTORS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27301,68,8,'paragraph',NULL,'To describe the position of an object moving in a plane, we need to choose a convenient point, say O as origin. Let P and P′ be the positions of the object at time t and t′, respectively [Fig. 4.1(a)]. We join O and P by a straight line. Then, OP is the position vector of the object at time t. An arrow is marked at the head of this line. It is represented by a symbol r , i.e. OP = r . Point P′ is represented by another position vector, OP ′ denoted by r ′. The length of the vector r represents the magnitude of the vector and its direction is the direction in which P lies as seen from O. If the object moves from P to P′, the vector PP ′ (with tail at P and tip at P′) is called the displacement vector corresponding to motion from point P (at time t) to point P′ (at time t′).','[{"s": "To describe the position of an object moving in a plane, we need to choose a convenient point, say O as origin. Let P and P′ be the positions of the object at time t and t′, respectively [Fig. 4.1(a)]. We join O and P by a straight line. Then,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the position vector of the object at time t. An arrow is marked at the head of this line. It is represented by a symbol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", i.e.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Point P′ is represented by another position vector,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ denoted by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′. The length of the vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "represents the magnitude of the vector and its direction is the direction in which P lies as seen from O. If the object moves from P to P′, the vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "PP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ (with tail at P and tip at P′) is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "displacement vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "corresponding to motion from point P (at time t) to point P′ (at time t′).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27302,68,9,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image140.png'),
(27303,68,10,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.1 (a) Position and displacement vectors. (b) Displacement vector PQ and different courses of motion.','[{"s": "Fig. 4.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Position and displacement vectors. (b) Displacement vector PQ and different courses of motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27860,69,3,'paragraph',NULL,'In our discussions, we shall treat the objects in motion as point objects. This approximation is valid so far as the size of the object is much smaller than the distance it moves in a reasonable duration of time. In a good number of situations in real-life, the size of objects can be neglected and they can be considered as point-like objects without much error.','[{"s": "In our discussions, we shall treat the objects in motion as point objects. This approximation is valid so far as the size of the object is much smaller than the distance it moves in a reasonable duration of time. In a good number of situations in real-life, the size of objects can be neglected and they can be considered as point-like objects without much error.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34848,80,281,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the possible wavelengths of stationary waves are constrained by the relation 3','[{"s": "Thus, the possible wavelengths of stationary waves are constrained by the relation", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040497", "2000109228"]',NULL),
(27304,68,11,'paragraph',NULL,'It is important to note that displacement vector is the straight line joining the initial and final positions and does not depend on the actual path undertaken by the object between the two positions. For example, in Fig. 4.1(b), given the initial and final positions as P and Q, the displacement vector is the same P Q for different paths of journey, say PABCQ, PDQ, and PBEFQ. Therefore, the magnitude of displacement is either less or equal to the path length of an object between two points . This fact was emphasised in the previous chapter also while discussing motion along a straight line.','[{"s": "It is important to note that displacement vector is the straight line joining the initial and final positions and does not depend on the actual path undertaken by the object between the two positions. For example, in Fig. 4.1(b), given the initial and final positions as P and Q, the displacement vector is the same", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q for different paths of journey, say PABCQ, PDQ, and PBEFQ. Therefore, the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "magnitude of displacement is either less or equal to the path length of an object between two points", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This fact was emphasised in the previous chapter also while discussing motion along a straight line.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27305,68,12,'heading',3,'4.2.2 EQUALITY OF VECTORS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27306,68,13,'paragraph',NULL,'Two vectors A and B are said to be equal if, and only if, they have the same magnitude and the same direction.**','[{"s": "Two vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are said to be equal if, and only if, they have the same magnitude and the same direction.**", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27307,68,14,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image145.png'),
(27308,68,15,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.2 (a) Two equal vectors A and B. (b) Two vectors A� and B� are unequal though they are of the same length.','[{"s": "Fig. 4.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Two equal vectors A and B. (b) Two vectors A� and B� are unequal though they are of the same length.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27309,68,16,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.2(a) shows two equal vectors A and B . We can easily check their equality. Shift B parallel to itself until its tail Q coincides with that of A, i.e. Q coincides with O. Then, since their tips S and P also coincide, the two vectors are said to be equal. In general, equality is indicated as A = B . Note that in Fig. 4.2(b), vectors A ′ and B ′ have the same magnitude but they are not equal because they have different directions. Even if we shift B ′ parallel to itself so that its tail Q′ coincides with the tail O′ of A ′, the tip S′ of B ′ does not coincide with the tip P′ of A ′.','[{"s": "Figure 4.2(a) shows two equal vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We can easily check their equality. Shift", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallel to itself until its tail Q coincides with that of A, i.e. Q coincides with O. Then, since their tips S and P also coincide, the two vectors are said to be equal. In general, equality is indicated as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Note that in Fig. 4.2(b), vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ have the same magnitude but they are not equal because they have different directions. Even if we shift", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ parallel to itself so that its tail Q′ coincides with the tail O′ of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′, the tip S′ of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ does not coincide with the tip P′ of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27310,68,17,'paragraph',NULL,'* Addition and subtraction of scalars make sense only for quantities with same units. However, you can multiply and divide scalars of different units.','[{"s": "* Addition and subtraction of scalars make sense only for quantities with same units. However, you can multiply and divide scalars of different units.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27311,68,18,'paragraph',NULL,'** In our study, vectors do not have fixed locations. So displacing a vector parallel to itself leaves the vector unchanged. Such vectors are called free vectors. However, in some physical applications, location or line of application of a vector is important. Such vectors are called localised vectors.','[{"s": "** In our study, vectors do not have fixed locations. So displacing a vector parallel to itself leaves the vector unchanged. Such vectors are called free vectors. However, in some physical applications, location or line of application of a vector is important. Such vectors are called localised vectors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(27312,68,19,'heading',2,'4.3 MULTIPLICATION OF VECTORS BY REAL NUMBERS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27313,68,20,'paragraph',NULL,'Multiplying a vector A with a positive number λ gives a vector whose magnitude is changed by the factor λ but the direction is the same as that of A :','[{"s": "Multiplying a vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with a positive number λ gives a vector whose magnitude is changed by the factor λ but the direction is the same as that of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27314,68,21,'paragraph',NULL,'|λ A| = λ | A | if λ > 0.','[{"s": "|λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A|", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= λ |", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "| if λ > 0.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27315,68,22,'paragraph',NULL,'For example, if A is multiplied by 2, the resultant vector 2 A is in the same direction as A and has a magnitude twice of | A | as shown in Fig. 4.3(a).','[{"s": "For example, if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is multiplied by 2, the resultant vector 2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is in the same direction as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and has a magnitude twice of |", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "| as shown in Fig. 4.3(a).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27316,68,23,'paragraph',NULL,'Multiplying a vector A by a negative number −λ gives another vector whose direction is opposite to the direction of A and whose magnitude is λ times | A |.','[{"s": "Multiplying a vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by a negative number −λ gives another vector whose direction is opposite to the direction of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and whose magnitude is λ times |", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34850,80,283,'paragraph',NULL,'with corresponding frequencies 3','[{"s": "with corresponding frequencies", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040523", "2000109231"]',NULL),
(27317,68,24,'paragraph',NULL,'Multiplying a given vector A by negative numbers, say –1 and –1.5, gives vectors as shown in Fig 4.3(b).','[{"s": "Multiplying a given vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by negative numbers, say –1 and –1.5, gives vectors as shown in Fig 4.3(b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27318,68,25,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 11.07.05 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image143.png'),
(27319,68,26,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.3 (a) Vector A and the resultant vector after multiplying A by a positive number 2. (b) Vector A and resultant vectors after multiplying it by a negative number –1 and –1.5.','[{"s": "Fig. 4.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Vector A and the resultant vector after multiplying A by a positive number 2. (b) Vector A and resultant vectors after multiplying it by a negative number –1 and –1.5.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27320,68,27,'paragraph',NULL,'The factor λ by which a vector A is multiplied could be a scalar having its own physical dimension. Then, the dimension of λ A is the product of the dimensions of λ and A . For example, if we multiply a constant velocity vector by duration (of time), we get a displacement vector.','[{"s": "The factor λ by which a vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is multiplied could be a scalar having its own physical dimension. Then, the dimension of λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the product of the dimensions of λ and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For example, if we multiply a constant velocity vector by duration (of time), we get a displacement vector.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27321,68,28,'heading',2,'4.4 ADDITION AND SUBTRACTION OF VECTORS — GRAPHICAL METHOD',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27322,68,29,'paragraph',NULL,'As mentioned in section 4.2, vectors, by definition, obey the triangle law or equivalently, the parallelogram law of addition. We shall now describe this law of addition using the graphical method. Let us consider two vectors A and B that lie in a plane as shown in Fig. 4.4(a). The lengths of the line segments representing these vectors are proportional to the magnitude of the vectors. To find the sum A + B , we place vector B so that its tail is at the head of the vector A , as in Fig. 4.4(b). Then, we join the tail of A to the head of B . This line OQ represents a vector R, that is, the sum of the vectors A and B . Since, in this procedure of vector addition, vectors are arranged head to tail, this graphical method is called the head-to-tail method . The two vectors and their resultant form three sides of a triangle, so this method is also known as triangle method of vector addition . If we find the resultant of B + A as in Fig. 4.4(c), the same vector R is obtained. Thus, vector addition is commutative :','[{"s": "As mentioned in section 4.2, vectors, by definition, obey the triangle law or equivalently, the parallelogram law of addition. We shall now describe this law of addition using the graphical method. Let us consider two vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "that lie in a plane as shown in Fig. 4.4(a). The lengths of the line segments representing these vectors are proportional to the magnitude of the vectors. To find the sum", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we place vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "so that its tail is at the head of the vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", as in Fig. 4.4(b). Then, we join the tail of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to the head of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This line OQ represents a vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "that is, the sum of the vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since, in this procedure of vector addition, vectors are arranged head to tail, this graphical method is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "head-to-tail method", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The two vectors and their resultant form three sides of a triangle, so this method is also known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "triangle method of vector addition", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If we find the resultant of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as in Fig. 4.4(c), the same vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is obtained. Thus, vector addition is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "commutative", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27323,68,30,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 11.08.15 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image150.png'),
(27324,68,31,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.4 (a) Vectors A and B. (b) Vectors A and B added graphically. (c) Vectors B and A added graphically. (d) Illustrating the associative law of vector addition.','[{"s": "Fig. 4.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Vectors A and B. (b) Vectors A and B added graphically. (c) Vectors B and A added graphically. (d) Illustrating the associative law of vector addition.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27325,68,32,'paragraph',NULL,'A + B = B + A (4.1)','[{"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27326,68,33,'paragraph',NULL,'The addition of vectors also obeys the associative law as illustrated in Fig. 4.4(d). The result of adding vectors A and B first and then adding vector C is the same as the result of adding B and C first and then adding vector A :','[{"s": "The addition of vectors also obeys the associative law as illustrated in Fig. 4.4(d). The result of adding vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "first and then adding vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the same as the result of adding", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "first and then adding vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27327,68,34,'paragraph',NULL,'( A + B ) + C = A + ( B + C ) (4.2)','[{"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (4.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27328,68,35,'paragraph',NULL,'What is the result of adding two equal and opposite vectors ? Consider two vectors A and – A shown in Fig. 4.3(b). Their sum is A + (– A ). Since the magnitudes of the two vectors are the same, but the directions are opposite, the resultant vector has zero magnitude and is represented by 0 called a null vector or a zero vector :','[{"s": "What is the result of adding two equal and opposite vectors ? Consider two vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "shown in Fig. 4.3(b). Their sum is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ (–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Since the magnitudes of the two vectors are the same, but the directions are opposite, the resultant vector has zero magnitude and is represented by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "called a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "null vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "zero vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27329,68,36,'paragraph',NULL,'A – A = 0 | 0 |= 0 (4.3)','[{"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|= 0 (4.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27330,68,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Since the magnitude of a null vector is zero, its direction cannot be specified.','[{"s": "Since the magnitude of a null vector is zero, its direction cannot be specified.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27331,68,38,'paragraph',NULL,'The null vector also results when we multiply a vector A by the number zero. The main properties of 0 are :','[{"s": "The null vector also results when we multiply a vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by the number zero. The main properties of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27332,68,39,'paragraph',NULL,'A + 0 = A','[{"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27333,68,40,'paragraph',NULL,'λ 0 = 0','[{"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27334,68,41,'paragraph',NULL,'0 A = 0 (4.4)','[{"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27335,68,42,'paragraph',NULL,'What is the physical meaning of a zero vector? Consider the position and displacement vectors in a plane as shown in Fig. 4.1(a). Now suppose that an object which is at P at time t, moves to P′ and then comes back to P. Then, what is its displacement? Since the initial and final positions coincide, the displacement is a “null vector”.','[{"s": "What is the physical meaning of a zero vector? Consider the position and displacement vectors in a plane as shown in Fig. 4.1(a). Now suppose that an object which is at P at time t, moves to P′ and then comes back to P. Then, what is its displacement? Since the initial and final positions coincide, the displacement is a “null vector”.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27336,68,43,'paragraph',NULL,'Subtraction of vectors can be defined in terms of addition of vectors. We define the difference of two vectors A and B as the sum of two vectors A and – B :','[{"s": "Subtraction of vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be defined in terms of addition of vectors. We define the difference of two vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as the sum of two vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27337,68,44,'paragraph',NULL,'A – B = A + (– B ) (4.5)','[{"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ (–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (4.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27338,68,45,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image147.png'),
(27339,68,46,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.5 (a) Two vectors A and B, – B is also shown. (b) Subtracting vector B from vector A – the result is R 2 . For comparison, addition of vectors A and B, i.e. R 1 is also shown.','[{"s": "Fig. 4.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Two vectors A and B, – B is also shown. (b) Subtracting vector B from vector A – the result is R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For comparison, addition of vectors A and B, i.e. R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is also shown.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27340,68,47,'paragraph',NULL,'It is shown in Fig 4.5. The vector – B is added to vector A to get R 2 = ( A – B ). The vector R 1 = A + B is also shown in the same figure for comparison. We can also use the parallelogram method to find the sum of two vectors. Suppose we have two vectors A and B . To add these vectors, we bring their tails to a common origin O as shown in Fig. 4.6(a). Then we draw a line from the head of A parallel to B and another line from the head of B parallel to A to complete a parallelogram OQSP. Now we join the point of the intersection of these two lines to the origin O. The resultant vector R is directed from the common origin O along the diagonal (OS) of the parallelogram [Fig. 4.6(b)]. In Fig.4.6(c), the triangle law is used to obtain the resultant of A and B and we see that the two methods yield the same result. Thus, the two methods are equivalent.','[{"s": "It is shown in Fig 4.5. The vector –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is added to vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to get", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). The vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is also shown in the same figure for comparison. We can also use the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallelogram method", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to find the sum of two vectors. Suppose we have two vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". To add these vectors, we bring their tails to a common origin O as shown in Fig. 4.6(a). Then we draw a line from the head of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallel to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and another line from the head of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallel to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to complete a parallelogram OQSP. Now we join the point of the intersection of these two lines to the origin O. The resultant vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is directed from the common origin O along the diagonal (OS) of the parallelogram [Fig. 4.6(b)]. In Fig.4.6(c), the triangle law is used to obtain the resultant of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and we see that the two methods yield the same result. Thus, the two methods are equivalent.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27341,68,48,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image156.png'),
(27342,68,49,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.6 (a) Two vectors A and B with their tails brought to a common origin. (b) The sum A+ B obtained using the parallelogram method. (c) The parallelogram method of vector addition is equivalent to the triangle method.','[{"s": "Fig. 4.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Two vectors A and B with their tails brought to a common origin. (b) The sum A+ B obtained using the parallelogram method. (c) The parallelogram method of vector addition is equivalent to the triangle method.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27344,68,51,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image153.png'),
(27345,68,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.7','[{"s": "Fig. 4.7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27861,69,4,'paragraph',NULL,'In Kinematics , we study ways to describe motion without going into the causes of motion. What causes motion described in this chapter and the next chapter forms the subject matter of Chapter 5.','[{"s": "In", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinematics", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we study ways to describe motion without going into the causes of motion. What causes motion described in this chapter and the next chapter forms the subject matter of Chapter 5.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27346,68,53,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The velocity of the rain and the wind are represented by the vectors v r and v w in Fig. 4.7 and are in the direction specified by the problem. Using the rule of vector addition, we see that the resultant of v r and v w is R as shown in the figure. The magnitude of R is','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The velocity of the rain and the wind are represented by the vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in Fig. 4.7 and are in the direction specified by the problem. Using the rule of vector addition, we see that the resultant of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "w", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as shown in the figure. The magnitude of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27347,68,54,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image161.png'),
(27348,68,55,'paragraph',NULL,'The direction θ that R makes with the vertical is given by','[{"s": "The direction θ that R makes with the vertical is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27349,68,56,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image158.png'),
(27350,68,57,'paragraph',NULL,'Or, Motion_in_a_Plane-image160.png','[{"s": "Or,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Plane-image160.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27351,68,58,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, the boy should hold his umbrella in the vertical plane at an angle of about 19 o with the vertical towards the east.','[{"s": "Therefore, the boy should hold his umbrella in the vertical plane at an angle of about 19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with the vertical towards the east.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27352,68,59,'heading',2,'4.5 RESOLUTION OF VECTORS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27353,68,60,'paragraph',NULL,'Let a and b be any two non-zero vectors in a plane with different directions and let A be another vector in the same plane(Fig. 4.8). A can be expressed as a sum of two vectors — one obtained by multiplying a by a real number and the other obtained by multiplying b by another real number. To see this, let O and P be the tail and head of the vector A . Then, through O, draw a straight line parallel to a , and through P, a straight line parallel to b . Let them intersect at Q. Then, we have','[{"s": "Let", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be any two non-zero vectors in a plane with different directions and let", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be another vector in the same plane(Fig. 4.8).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be expressed as a sum of two vectors — one obtained by multiplying", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by a real number and the other obtained by multiplying", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by another real number. To see this, let O and P be the tail and head of the vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Then, through O, draw a straight line parallel to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and through P, a straight line parallel to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Let them intersect at Q. Then, we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27354,68,61,'paragraph',NULL,'A = OP = O Q + Q P (4.6)','[{"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q + Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27355,68,62,'paragraph',NULL,'But since O Q is parallel to a , and Q P is parallel to b , we can write :','[{"s": "But since", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q is parallel to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is parallel to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we can write :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27356,68,63,'paragraph',NULL,'O Q = λ a , and Q P = µ b (4.7)','[{"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q = λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27357,68,64,'paragraph',NULL,'where λ and µ are real numbers.','[{"s": "where λ and µ are real numbers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27358,68,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, A = λ a + µ b (4.8)','[{"s": "Therefore,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27359,68,66,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image163.png'),
(27360,68,67,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 4.8 (a) Two non-colinear vectors a and b. (b) Resolving a vector A in terms of vectors a and b.','[{"s": "Fig. 4.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Two non-colinear vectors a and b. (b) Resolving a vector A in terms of vectors a and b.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27361,68,68,'paragraph',NULL,'We say that A has been resolved into two component vectors λ a and µ b along a and b respectively. Using this method one can resolve a given vector into two component vectors along a set of two vectors – all the three lie in the same plane. It is convenient to resolve a general vector along the axes of a rectangular coordinate system using vectors of unit magnitude. These are called unit vectors that we discuss now. A unit vector is a vector of unit magnitude and points in a particular direction. It has no dimension and unit. It is used to specify a direction only. Unit vectors along the x-, y- and z-axes of a rectangular coordinate system are denoted by','[{"s": "We say that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has been resolved into two component vectors λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "along", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively. Using this method one can resolve a given vector into two component vectors along a set of two vectors – all the three lie in the same plane. It is convenient to resolve a general vector along the axes of a rectangular coordinate system using vectors of unit magnitude. These are called unit vectors that we discuss now. A unit vector is a vector of unit magnitude and points in a particular direction. It has no dimension and unit. It is used to specify a direction only. Unit vectors along the x-, y- and z-axes of a rectangular coordinate system are denoted by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27862,69,5,'heading',2,'3.2 POSITION, PATH LENGTH AND DISPLACEMENT',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
