(27719,68,426,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image89.png'),
(27720,68,427,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The time taken to return to the same level is','[{"s": "(b) The time taken to return to the same level is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27721,68,428,'paragraph',NULL,'T f = (2 v o sin θ o )/g = (2× 28 × sin 30° )/9.8 = 28/9.8 s = 2.9 s','[{"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (2 v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")/g = (2× 28 × sin 30° )/9.8 = 28/9.8 s = 2.9 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27722,68,429,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The distance from the thrower to the point where the ball returns to the same level is','[{"s": "(c) The distance from the thrower to the point where the ball returns to the same level is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27723,68,430,'paragraph',NULL,'R','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27724,68,431,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image87.png'),
(27725,68,432,'heading',4,'Neglecting air resistance - what does the assumption really mean?',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27727,68,434,'paragraph',NULL,'In order to avoid air resistance, we will have to perform the experiment in vacuum or under low pressure, which is not easy. When we use a phrase like ‘neglect air resistance’, we imply that the change in parameters such as range, height etc. is much smaller than their values without air resistance. The calculation without air resistance is much simpler than that with air resistance.','[{"s": "In order to avoid air resistance, we will have to perform the experiment in vacuum or under low pressure, which is not easy. When we use a phrase like ‘neglect air resistance’, we imply that the change in parameters such as range, height etc. is much smaller than their values without air resistance. The calculation without air resistance is much simpler than that with air resistance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27728,68,435,'heading',2,'4.11 UNIFORM CIRCULAR MOTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27729,68,436,'paragraph',NULL,'When an object follows a circular path at a constant speed, the motion of the object is called uniform circular motion . The word “uniform” refers to the speed, which is uniform (constant) throughout the motion. Suppose an object is moving with uniform speed v in a circle of radius R as shown in Fig. 4.19. Since the velocity of the object is changing continuously in direction, the object undergoes acceleration. Let us find the magnitude and the direction of this acceleration.','[{"s": "When an object follows a circular path at a constant speed, the motion of the object is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "uniform circular motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The word “uniform” refers to the speed, which is uniform (constant) throughout the motion. Suppose an object is moving with uniform speed v in a circle of radius R as shown in Fig. 4.19. Since the velocity of the object is changing continuously in direction, the object undergoes acceleration. Let us find the magnitude and the direction of this acceleration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27730,68,437,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image93.png'),
(27732,68,439,'paragraph',NULL,'Let r and r ′ be the position vectors and v and v ′ the velocities of the object when it is at point P and P′ as shown in Fig. 4.19(a). By definition, velocity at a point is along the tangent at that point in the direction of motion. The velocity vectors v and v ′ are as shown in Fig. 4.19(a1). ∆ v is obtained in Fig. 4.19 (a2) using the triangle law of vector addition. Since the path is circular, v is perpendicular to r and so is v ′ to r ′. Therefore, ∆ v is perpendicular to ∆ r . Since average acceleration is along ∆ v','[{"s": "Let", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ be the position vectors and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ the velocities of the object when it is at point P and P′ as shown in Fig. 4.19(a). By definition, velocity at a point is along the tangent at that point in the direction of motion. The velocity vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ are as shown in Fig. 4.19(a1). ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is obtained in Fig. 4.19 (a2) using the triangle law of vector addition. Since the path is circular,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is perpendicular to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and so is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′. Therefore, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is perpendicular to ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since average acceleration is along ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27733,68,440,'paragraph',NULL,'Motion_in_a_Plane-image91.png , the average acceleration Motion_in_a_Plane-image76.png is perpendicular to ∆r . If we place ∆ v on the line that bisects the angle between r and r ′, we see that it is directed towards the centre of the circle. Figure 4.19(b) shows the same quantities for smaller time interval. ∆ v and hence','[{"s": "Motion_in_a_Plane-image91.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the average acceleration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Plane-image76.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is perpendicular to ∆r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If we place ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on the line that bisects the angle between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′, we see that it is directed towards the centre of the circle. Figure 4.19(b) shows the same quantities for smaller time interval. ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and hence", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27734,68,441,'paragraph',NULL,'Motion_in_a_Plane-image76.png is again directed towards the centre. In Fig. 4.19(c), ∆t→0 and the average acceleration becomes the instantaneous acceleration. It is directed towards the centre*. Thus, we find that the acceleration of an object in uniform circular motion is always directed towards the centre of the circle. Let us now find the magnitude of the acceleration.','[{"s": "Motion_in_a_Plane-image76.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is again directed towards the centre. In Fig. 4.19(c), ∆t→0 and the average acceleration becomes the instantaneous acceleration. It is directed towards the centre*. Thus, we find that the acceleration of an object in uniform circular motion is always directed towards the centre of the circle. Let us now find the magnitude of the acceleration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27735,68,442,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitude of a is, by definition, given by','[{"s": "The magnitude of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is, by definition, given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27736,68,443,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-08 at 6.08.07 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image85.png'),
(27737,68,444,'paragraph',NULL,'* In the limit ∆t→0, ∆r becomes perpendicular to r. In this limit ∆v→ 0 and is consequently also perpendicular to V. Therefore, the acceleration is directed towards the centre, at each point of the circular path.','[{"s": "* In the limit ∆t→0, ∆r becomes perpendicular to r. In this limit ∆v→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0 and is consequently also perpendicular to V. Therefore, the acceleration is directed towards the centre, at each point of the circular path.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27738,68,445,'paragraph',NULL,'Let the angle between position vectors r and r ′ be ∆θ. Since the velocity vectors v and v ′ are always perpendicular to the position vectors, the angle between them is also ∆θ . Therefore, the triangle CPP′ formed by the position vectors and the triangle GHI formed by the velocity vectors v , v ′ and ∆ v are similar (Fig. 4.19a). Therefore, the ratio of the base-length to side-length for one of the triangles is equal to that of the other triangle. That is :','[{"s": "Let the angle between position vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ be ∆θ. Since the velocity vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ are always perpendicular to the position vectors, the angle between them is also ∆θ . Therefore, the triangle CPP′ formed by the position vectors and the triangle GHI formed by the velocity vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are similar (Fig. 4.19a). Therefore, the ratio of the base-length to side-length for one of the triangles is equal to that of the other triangle. That is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
