(27758,68,465,'paragraph',NULL,'but ∆s = R ∆θ. Therefore :','[{"s": "but ∆s = R ∆θ. Therefore :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27759,68,466,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image101.png'),
(27760,68,467,'paragraph',NULL,'v = R ω (4.46)','[{"s": "v = R ω (4.46)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27761,68,468,'paragraph',NULL,'We can express centripetal acceleration a c in terms of angular speed :','[{"s": "We can express centripetal acceleration a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in terms of angular speed :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27762,68,469,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image60.png'),
(27763,68,470,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Plane-image53.png'),
(27764,68,471,'paragraph',NULL,'(4.47)','[{"s": "(4.47)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27863,69,6,'paragraph',NULL,'Earlier you learnt that motion is change in position of an object with time. In order to specify position, we need to use a reference point and a set of axes. It is convenient to choose a rectangular coordinate system consisting of three mutually perpenducular axes, labelled X-, Y-, and Z- axes. The point of intersection of these three axes is called origin (O) and serves as the reference point . The coordinates ( x, y. z ) of an object describe the position of the object with respect to this coordinate system. To measure time, we position a clock in this system. This coordinate system along with a clock constitutes a frame of reference .','[{"s": "Earlier you learnt that motion is change in position of an object with time. In order to specify position, we need to use a reference point and a set of axes. It is convenient to choose a rectangular coordinate system consisting of three mutually perpenducular axes, labelled X-, Y-, and Z- axes. The point of intersection of these three axes is called origin (O) and serves as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reference point", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The coordinates (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x, y. z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of an object describe the position of the object with respect to this coordinate system. To measure time, we position a clock in this system. This coordinate system along with a clock constitutes a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "frame of reference", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27864,69,7,'paragraph',NULL,'If one or more coordinates of an object change with time, we say that the object is in motion. Otherwise, the object is said to be at rest with respect to this frame of reference.','[{"s": "If one or more coordinates of an object change with time, we say that the object is in motion. Otherwise, the object is said to be at rest with respect to this frame of reference.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27865,69,8,'paragraph',NULL,'The choice of a set of axes in a frame of reference depends upon the situation. For example, for describing motion in one dimension, we need only one axis. To describe motion in two/three dimensions, we need a set of two/three axes.','[{"s": "The choice of a set of axes in a frame of reference depends upon the situation. For example, for describing motion in one dimension, we need only one axis. To describe motion in two/three dimensions, we need a set of two/three axes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27866,69,9,'paragraph',NULL,'Description of an event depends on the frame of reference chosen for the description. For example, when you say that a car is moving on a road, you are describing the car with respect to a frame of reference attached to you or to the ground. But with respect to a frame of reference attached with a person sitting in the car, the car is at rest.','[{"s": "Description of an event depends on the frame of reference chosen for the description. For example, when you say that a car is moving on a road, you are describing the car with respect to a frame of reference attached to you or to the ground. But with respect to a frame of reference attached with a person sitting in the car, the car is at rest.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27867,69,10,'paragraph',NULL,'To describe motion along a straight line, we can choose an axis, say X -axis, so that it coincides with the path of the object. We then measure the position of the object with reference to a conveniently chosen origin, say O, as shown in Fig. 3.1. Positions to the right of O are taken as positive and to the left of O, as negative. Following this convention, the position coordinates of point P and Q in Fig. 3.1 are +360 m and +240 m. Similarly, the position coordinate of point R is –120 m.','[{"s": "To describe motion along a straight line, we can choose an axis, say", "t": "text"}, {"s": "X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-axis, so that it coincides with the path of the object. We then measure the position of the object with reference to a conveniently chosen origin, say O, as shown in Fig. 3.1. Positions to the right of O are taken as positive and to the left of O, as negative. Following this convention, the position coordinates of point P and Q in Fig. 3.1 are +360 m and +240 m. Similarly, the position coordinate of point R is –120 m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27868,69,11,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image59.png'),
(27869,69,12,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.1 x-axis, origin and positions of a car at different times.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x-axis, origin and positions of a car at different times.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27870,69,13,'heading',3,'PATH LENGTH',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27871,69,14,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider the motion of a car along a straight line. We choose the x -axis such that it coincides with the path of the car’s motion and origin of the axis as the point from where the car started moving, i.e. the car was at x = 0 at t = 0 (Fig.3.1). Let P, Q and R represent the positions of the car at different instants of time. Consider two cases of motion. In the first case, the car moves from O to P. Then the distance moved by the car is OP = +360 m. This distance is called the path length traversed by the car . In the second case, the car moves from O to P and then moves back from P to Q. During this course of motion, the path length traversed is OP + PQ = + 360 m + (+120 m) = + 480 m. Path length is a scalar quantity — a quantity that has a magnitude only and no direction (see Chapter 4).','[{"s": "Consider the motion of a car along a straight line. We choose the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-axis such that it coincides with the path of the car’s motion and origin of the axis as the point from where the car started moving, i.e. the car was at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 (Fig.3.1). Let P, Q and R represent the positions of the car at different instants of time. Consider two cases of motion. In the first case, the car moves from O to P. Then the distance moved by the car is OP = +360 m.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This distance is called the path length", "t": "text"}, {"s": "traversed by the car", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the second case, the car moves from O to P and then moves back from P to Q. During this course of motion, the path length traversed is OP + PQ = + 360 m + (+120 m) = + 480 m. Path length is a scalar quantity — a quantity that has a magnitude only and no direction (see Chapter 4).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27872,69,15,'heading',3,'DISPLACEMENT',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22782,58,38,'paragraph',NULL,'The interpretation of the observation in terms of photoelectrons is straightforward. All the photoelectrons emitted from the metal do not have the same energy. Photoelectric current is zero when the stopping potential is sufficient to repel even the most energetic photoelectrons, with the maximum kinetic energy ( K max ), so that','[{"s": "The interpretation of the observation in terms of photoelectrons is straightforward.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "All the photoelectrons emitted from the metal do not have the same energy. Photoelectric current is zero when the stopping potential is sufficient to repel even the most energetic photoelectrons, with the maximum kinetic energy (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "max", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), so that", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(27873,69,16,'paragraph',NULL,'It is useful to define another quantity displacement as the change in position. Let x 1 and x 2 be the positions of an object at time t 1 and t 2 . Then its displacement, denoted by ∆ x , in time ∆ t = ( t 2 - t 1 ), is given by the difference between the final and initial positions :','[{"s": "It is useful to define another quantity displacement as the change in position. Let", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the positions of an object at time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Then its displacement, denoted by ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", in time ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), is given by the difference between the final and initial positions :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27874,69,17,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ x = x 2 – x 1','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27875,69,18,'paragraph',NULL,'(We use the Greek letter delta (∆) to denote a change in a quantity.)','[{"s": "(We use the Greek letter delta (∆) to denote a change in a quantity.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27876,69,19,'paragraph',NULL,'If x 2 > x 1 , ∆ x is positive; and if x 2 < x 1 , ∆ x is negative.','[{"s": "If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is positive; and if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is negative.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27877,69,20,'paragraph',NULL,'Displacement has both magnitude and direction. Such quantities are represented by vectors. You will read about vectors in the next chapter. Presently, we are dealing with motion along a straight line (also called rectilinear motion ) only. In one-dimensional motion, there are only two directions (backward and forward, upward and downward) in which an object can move, and these two directions can easily be specified by + and – signs. For example, displacement of the car in moving from O to P is :','[{"s": "Displacement has both magnitude and direction. Such quantities are represented by vectors. You will read about vectors in the next chapter. Presently, we are dealing with motion along a straight line (also called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rectilinear motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") only. In one-dimensional motion, there are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "only two", "t": "text"}, {"s": "directions (backward and forward, upward and downward) in which an object can move, and these two directions can easily be specified by + and – signs. For example, displacement of the car in moving from O to P is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27878,69,21,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ x = x 2 – x 1 = (+360 m) – 0 m = +360 m','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (+360 m) – 0 m = +360 m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27879,69,22,'paragraph',NULL,'The displacement has a magnitude of 360 m and is directed in the positive x direction as indicated by the + sign. Similarly, the displacement of the car from P to Q is 240 m – 360 m = – 120 m. The negative sign indicates the direction of displacement. Thus, it is not necessary to use vector notation for discussing motion of objects in one-dimension.','[{"s": "The displacement has a magnitude of 360 m and is directed in the positive", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "direction as indicated by the + sign. Similarly, the displacement of the car from P to Q is 240 m – 360 m = – 120 m. The negative sign indicates the direction of displacement. Thus, it is not necessary to use vector notation for discussing motion of objects in one-dimension.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27880,69,23,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitude of displacement may or may not be equal to the path length traversed by an object. For example, for motion of the car from O to P, the path length is +360 m and the displacement is +360 m. In this case, the magnitude of displacement (360 m) is equal to the path length (360 m). But consider the motion of the car from O to P and back to Q. In this case, the path length = (+360 m) + (+120 m) = + 480 m. However, the displacement = (+240 m) – (0 m) = + 240 m. Thus, the magnitude of displacement (240 m) is not equal to the path length (480 m).','[{"s": "The magnitude of displacement may or may not be equal to the path length traversed by an object.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For example, for motion of the car from O to P, the path length is +360 m and the displacement is +360 m. In this case, the magnitude of displacement (360 m) is equal to the path length (360 m). But consider the motion of the car from O to P and back to Q. In this case, the path length = (+360 m) + (+120 m) = + 480 m. However, the displacement = (+240 m) – (0 m) = + 240 m. Thus, the magnitude of displacement (240 m) is not equal to the path length (480 m).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27881,69,24,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitude of the displacement for a course of motion may be zero but the corresponding path length is not zero . For example, if the car starts from O, goes to P and then returns to O, the final position coincides with the initial position and the displacement is zero. However, the path length of this journey is OP + PO = 360 m + 360 m = 720 m.','[{"s": "The magnitude of the displacement for a course of motion may be zero but the corresponding path length is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "not zero", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For example, if the car starts from O, goes to P and then returns to O, the final position coincides with the initial position and the displacement is zero. However, the path length of this journey is OP + PO = 360 m + 360 m = 720 m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35025,80,458,'paragraph',NULL,'17. Beats arise when two waves having slightly different frequencies, ν1 and ν2 and comparable amplitudes, are superposed . 2 The beat frequency is','[{"s": "17.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Beats arise when two waves having slightly different frequencies, ν1 and ν2 and comparable amplitudes, are superposed", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The beat frequency is", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041109"]',NULL),
(27882,69,25,'paragraph',NULL,'Motion of an object can be represented by a position-time graph as you have already learnt about it. Such a graph is a powerful tool to represent and analyse different aspects of motion of an object. For motion along a straight line, say X -axis, only x -coordinate varies with time and we have an x - t graph. Let us first consider the simple case in which an object is stationary, e.g. a car standing still at x = 40 m. The position-time graph is a straight line parallel to the time axis, as shown in Fig. 3.2(a).','[{"s": "Motion of an object can be represented by a position-time graph as you have already learnt about it. Such a graph is a powerful tool to represent and analyse different aspects of motion of an object. For motion along a straight line, say", "t": "text"}, {"s": "X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-axis, only", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-coordinate varies with time and we have an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "graph. Let us first consider the simple case in which an object is stationary, e.g. a car standing still at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 40 m. The position-time graph is a straight line parallel to the time axis, as shown in Fig. 3.2(a).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27883,69,26,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image61.png'),
(27884,69,27,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.2 Position-time graph of (a) stationary object, and (b) an object in uniform motion.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Position-time graph of (a) stationary object, and (b) an object in uniform motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27885,69,28,'paragraph',NULL,'If an object moving along the straight line covers equal distances in equal intervals of time, it is said to be in uniform motion along a straight line. Fig. 3.2(b) shows the position-time graph of such a motion.','[{"s": "If an object moving along the straight line covers equal distances in equal intervals of time, it is said to be in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "uniform motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": "along a straight line. Fig. 3.2(b) shows the position-time graph of such a motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27886,69,29,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 9.06.09 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image60.png'),
(27887,69,30,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.3 Position-time graph of a car.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Position-time graph of a car.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27888,69,31,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, let us consider the motion of a car that starts from rest at time t = 0 s from the origin O and picks up speed till t = 10 s and thereafter moves with uniform speed till t = 18 s. Then the brakes are applied and the car stops at t = 20 s and x = 296 m. The position-time graph for this case is shown in Fig. 3.3. We shall refer to this graph in our discussion in the following sections.','[{"s": "Now, let us consider the motion of a car that starts from rest at time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 s from the origin O and picks up speed till", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 s and thereafter moves with uniform speed till", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 18 s. Then the brakes are applied and the car stops at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 20 s and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 296 m. The position-time graph for this case is shown in Fig. 3.3. We shall refer to this graph in our discussion in the following sections.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27889,69,32,'heading',2,'3.3 AVERAGE VELOCITY AND AVERAGE SPEED',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27891,69,34,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image63.png'),
(27892,69,35,'paragraph',NULL,'(3.1)','[{"s": "(3.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27893,69,36,'paragraph',NULL,'where x 2 and x 1 are the positions of the object at time t 2 and t 1 , respectively. Here the bar over the symbol for velocity is a standard notation used to indicate an average quantity. The SI unit for velocity is m/s or m s –1 , although km h –1 is used in many everyday applications.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the positions of the object at time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively. Here the bar over the symbol for velocity is a standard notation used to indicate an average quantity. The SI unit for velocity is m/s or m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", although km h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is used in many everyday applications.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27894,69,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Like displacement, average velocity is also a vector quantity. But as explained earlier, for motion in a straight line, the directional aspect of the vector can be taken care of by + and – signs and we do not have to use the vector notation for velocity in this chapter.','[{"s": "Like displacement, average velocity is also a vector quantity. But as explained earlier, for motion in a straight line, the directional aspect of the vector can be taken care of by + and – signs and we do not have to use the vector notation for velocity in this chapter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27895,69,38,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 9.08.49 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image62.png'),
(27897,69,40,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider the motion of the car in Fig. 3.3. The portion of the x-t graph between t = 0 s and t = 8 s is blown up and shown in Fig. 3.4. As seen from the plot, the average velocity of the car between time t = 5 s and t = 7 s is :','[{"s": "Consider the motion of the car in Fig. 3.3. The portion of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x-t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "graph between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 s and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 8 s is blown up and shown in Fig. 3.4. As seen from the plot, the average velocity of the car between time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5 s and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 7 s is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27898,69,41,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 9.09.53 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image66.png'),
(27899,69,42,'paragraph',NULL,'Geometrically, this is the slope of the straight line P 1 P 2 connecting the initial position P 1 to the final position P 2 as shown in Fig. 3.4.','[{"s": "Geometrically, this is the slope of the straight line P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "connecting the initial position P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to the final position P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as shown in Fig. 3.4.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27900,69,43,'paragraph',NULL,'The average velocity can be positive or negative depending upon the sign of the displacement. It is zero if the displacement is zero. Fig. 3.5 shows the x-t graphs for an object, moving with positive velocity (Fig. 3.5a), moving with negative velocity (Fig. 3.5b) and at rest (Fig. 3.5c).','[{"s": "The average velocity can be positive or negative depending upon the sign of the displacement. It is zero if the displacement is zero. Fig. 3.5 shows the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x-t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "graphs for an object, moving with positive velocity (Fig. 3.5a), moving with negative velocity (Fig. 3.5b) and at rest (Fig. 3.5c).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27901,69,44,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image65.png'),
(27902,69,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.5 Position-time graph for an object (a) moving with positive velocity, (b) moving with negative velocity, and (c) at rest.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Position-time graph for an object (a) moving with positive velocity, (b) moving with negative velocity, and (c) at rest.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27903,69,46,'paragraph',NULL,'Average velocity as defined above involves only the displacement of the object. We have seen earlier that the magnitude of displacement may be different from the actual path length. To describe the rate of motion over the actual path, we introduce another quantity called average speed .','[{"s": "Average velocity as defined above involves only the displacement of the object. We have seen earlier that the magnitude of displacement may be different from the actual path length. To describe the rate of motion over the actual path, we introduce another quantity called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "average speed", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27905,69,48,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image71.png'),
(27906,69,49,'paragraph',NULL,'(3.2)','[{"s": "(3.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27907,69,50,'paragraph',NULL,'Average speed has obviously the same unit (m s –1 ) as that of velocity. But it does not tell us in what direction an object is moving. Thus, it is always positive (in contrast to the average velocity which can be positive or negative). If the motion of an object is along a straight line and in the same direction , the magnitude of displacement is equal to the total path length. In that case, the magnitude of average velocity is equal to the average speed. This is not always the case, as you will see in the following example.','[{"s": "Average speed has obviously the same unit (m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") as that of velocity. But it does not tell us in what direction an object is moving. Thus, it is always positive (in contrast to the average velocity which can be positive or negative). If the motion of an object is along a straight line and in the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "same direction", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the magnitude of displacement is equal to the total path length. In that case, the magnitude of average velocity is equal to the average speed. This is not always the case, as you will see in the following example.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27908,69,51,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 3.1 A car is moving along a straight line, say OP in Fig. 3.1. It moves from O to P in 18 s and returns from P to Q in 6.0 s. What are the average velocity and average speed of the car in going (a) from O to P ? and (b) from O to P and back to Q ?','[{"s": "Example 3.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A car is moving along a straight line, say OP in Fig. 3.1. It moves from O to P in 18 s and returns from P to Q in 6.0 s. What are the average velocity and average speed of the car in going (a) from O to P ? and (b) from O to P and back to Q ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27909,69,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer (a)','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27910,69,53,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image69.png'),
(27911,69,54,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image76.png'),
(27912,69,55,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image72.png'),
(27913,69,56,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image74.png'),
(27914,69,57,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, in this case the average speed is equal to the magnitude of the average velocity.','[{"s": "Thus, in this case the average speed is equal to the magnitude of the average velocity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27915,69,58,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) In this case,','[{"s": "(b) In this case,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27916,69,59,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 9.14.15 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image77.png'),
(27904,69,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Average speed is defined as the total path length travelled divided by the total time interval during which the motion has taken place:','[{"s": "Average speed", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is defined as the total path length travelled divided by the total time interval during which the motion has taken place:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000038943", "2000039775", "2000040396", "2000106125", "2000106140", "2000106156", "2000106196"]',NULL),
(27917,69,60,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, in this case the average speed is not equal to the magnitude of the average velocity. This happens because the motion here involves change in direction so that the path length is greater than the magnitude of displacement. This shows that speed is, in general, greater than the magnitude of the velocity.','[{"s": "Thus, in this case the average speed is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "not", "t": "text"}, {"s": "equal to the magnitude of the average velocity. This happens because the motion here involves change in direction so that the path length is greater than the magnitude of displacement. This shows that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "speed is, in general, greater than the magnitude of the velocity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27919,69,62,'heading',2,'3.4 INSTANTANEOUS VELOCITY AND SPEED',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27920,69,63,'paragraph',NULL,'The average velocity tells us how fast an object has been moving over a given time interval but does not tell us how fast it moves at different instants of time during that interval. For this, we define instantaneous velocity or simply velocity v at an instant t .','[{"s": "The average velocity tells us how fast an object has been moving over a given time interval but does not tell us how fast it moves at different instants of time during that interval. For this, we define", "t": "text"}, {"s": "instantaneous velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or simply velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at an instant", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27922,69,65,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image79.png'),
(27923,69,66,'paragraph',NULL,'(3.3a)','[{"s": "(3.3a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27924,69,67,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image81.png'),
(27925,69,68,'paragraph',NULL,'(3.3b)','[{"s": "(3.3b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27926,69,69,'paragraph',NULL,'where the symbol Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image83.png stands for the operation of taking limit as ∆t → 0 of the quantity on its right. In the language of calculus, the quantity on the right hand side of Eq. (3.3a) is the differential coefficient of x with respect to t and is denoted by Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image85.png (see Appendix 3.1). It is the rate of change of position with respect to time, at that instant.','[{"s": "where the symbol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image83.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "stands for the operation of taking limit as ∆t → 0 of the quantity on its right. In the language of calculus, the quantity on the right hand side of Eq. (3.3a) is the differential coefficient of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with respect to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and is denoted by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image85.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(see Appendix 3.1). It is the rate of change of position with respect to time, at that instant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27927,69,70,'paragraph',NULL,'We can use Eq. (3.3a) for obtaining the value of velocity at an instant either graphically or numerically . Suppose that we want to obtain graphically the value of velocity at time t = 4 s (point P) for the motion of the car represented in Fig. 3.3. The figure has been redrawn in Fig.3.6 choosing different scales to facilitate the calculation. Let us take ∆ t = 2 s centred at t = 4 s. Then, by the definition of the average velocity, the slope of line P 1 P 2 ( Fig. 3.6) gives the value of average velocity over the interval 3 s to 5 s. Now, we decrease the value of ∆ t from 2 s to 1 s. Then line P 1 P 2 becomes Q 1 Q 2 and its slope gives the value of the average velocity over the interval 3.5 s to 4.5 s. In the limit ∆ t → 0, the line P 1 P 2 becomes tangent to the position-time curve at the point P and the velocity at t = 4 s is given by the slope of the tangent at that point. It is difficult to show this process graphically. But if we use numerical method to obtain the value of the velocity, the meaning of the limiting process becomes clear x = 0.08 t 3 . Table 3.1 gives the value of ∆ x /∆ t calculated for ∆ t equal to 2.0 s, 1.0 s, 0.5 s, 0.1 s and 0.01 s centred at t = 4.0 s. The second and third columns give the value of t 1 = Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image86.png and Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image87.png and the fourth and the fifth columns give the corresponding values of x , i.e. x ( t 1 ) = 0.08 t 1 3 and x ( t 2 ) = 0.08 t 2 3 . The sixth column lists the difference ∆ x = x ( t 2 ) – x ( t 1 ) and the last column gives the ratio of ∆ x and ∆ t , i.e. the average velocity corresponding to the value of ∆ t listed in the first column.','[{"s": "We can use Eq. (3.3a) for obtaining the value of velocity at an instant either", "t": "text"}, {"s": "graphically", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "numerically", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Suppose that we want to obtain graphically the value of velocity at time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4 s (point P) for the motion of the car represented in Fig. 3.3. The figure has been redrawn in Fig.3.6 choosing different scales to facilitate the calculation. Let us take ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2 s centred at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4 s. Then, by the definition of the average velocity, the slope of line P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "( Fig. 3.6) gives the value of average velocity over the interval 3 s to 5 s. Now, we decrease the value of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from 2 s to 1 s. Then line P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "becomes Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and its slope gives the value of the average velocity over the interval 3.5 s to 4.5 s. In the limit ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 0, the line P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "becomes tangent to the position-time curve at the point P and the velocity at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4 s is given by the slope of the tangent at that point.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is difficult to show this process graphically. But if we use numerical method to obtain the value of the velocity, the meaning of the limiting process becomes clear", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.08", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Table 3.1 gives the value of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "calculated for ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "equal to 2.0 s, 1.0 s, 0.5 s, 0.1 s and 0.01 s centred at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.0 s. The second and third columns give the value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image86.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image87.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and the fourth and the fifth columns give the corresponding values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", i.e.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = 0.08 t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = 0.08 t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The sixth column lists the difference ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and the last column gives the ratio of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", i.e. the average velocity corresponding to the value of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "listed in the first column.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27928,69,71,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image88.png'),
(27930,69,73,'paragraph',NULL,'We see from Table 3.1 that as we decrease the value of ∆ t from 2.0 s to 0.010 s, the value of the average velocity approaches the limiting value 3.84 m s –1 which is the value of velocity at t = 4.0 s, i.e. the value of dx/dt at t = 4.0 s. In this manner, we can calculate the velocity at each instant for motion of the car shown in Fig. 3.3. For this case, the variation of velocity with time is found to be as shown in Fig. 3.7.','[{"s": "We see from Table 3.1 that as we decrease the value of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from 2.0 s to 0.010 s, the value of the average velocity approaches the limiting value 3.84 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which is the value of velocity at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.0 s, i.e. the value of dx/dt at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.0 s. In this manner, we can calculate the velocity at each instant for motion of the car shown in Fig. 3.3. For this case, the variation of velocity with time is found to be as shown in Fig. 3.7.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27931,69,74,'heading',5,'Table 3.1 Limiting value of ∆x/∆t at t = 4 s',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27932,69,75,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image44.png'),
(27933,69,76,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 9.21.26 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image46.png'),
(27934,69,77,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.7 Velocity–time graph corresponding to motion shown in Fig. 3.3.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Velocity–time graph corresponding to motion shown in Fig. 3.3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27935,69,78,'paragraph',NULL,'The graphical method for the determination of the instantaneous velocity is always not a convenient method. For this, we must carefully plot the position–time graph and calculate the value of average velocity as ∆ t becomes smaller and smaller. It is easier to calculate the value of velocity at different instants if we have data of positions at different instants or exact expression for the position as a function of time. Then, we calculate ∆ x /∆ t from the data for decreasing the value of ∆ t and find the limiting value as we have done in Table 3.1 or use differential calculus for the given expression and calculate dx/dt at different instants as done in the following example.','[{"s": "The graphical method for the determination of the instantaneous velocity is always not a convenient method. For this, we must carefully plot the position–time graph and calculate the value of average velocity as ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "becomes smaller and smaller. It is easier to calculate the value of velocity at different instants if we have data of positions at different instants or exact expression for the position as a function of time. Then, we calculate ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from the data for decreasing the value of ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and find the limiting value as we have done in Table 3.1 or use differential calculus for the given expression and calculate dx/dt at different instants as done in the following example.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27937,69,80,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer In notation of differential calculus, the velocity is','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In notation of differential calculus, the velocity is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27938,69,81,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image48.png'),
(27939,69,82,'paragraph',NULL,'At t = 0 s, v = 0 m s –1 and at t = 2.0 s, v = 10 m s -1 .','[{"s": "At", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 s,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.0 s,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27940,69,83,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image50.png'),
(27941,69,84,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image52.png'),
(27942,69,85,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image54.png'),
(27943,69,86,'paragraph',NULL,'From Fig. 3.7, we note that during the period t =10 s to 18 s the velocity is constant. Between period t =18 s to t = 20 s, it is uniformly decreasing and during the period t = 0 s to t = 10 s, it is increasing. Note that for uniform motion, velocity is the same as the average velocity at all instants .','[{"s": "From Fig. 3.7, we note that during the period", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=10 s to 18 s the velocity is constant. Between period", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=18 s to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 20 s, it is uniformly decreasing and during the period", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 s to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 s, it is increasing.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Note that for uniform motion, velocity is the same as the average velocity at all instants", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27944,69,87,'paragraph',NULL,'Instantaneous speed or simply speed is the magnitude of velocity. For example, a velocity of + 24.0 m s –1 and a velocity of – 24.0 m s –1 — both have an associated speed of 24.0 m s -1 . It should be noted that though average speed over a finite interval of time is greater or equal to the magnitude of the average velocity, instantaneous speed at an instant is equal to the magnitude of the instantaneous velocity at that instant. Why so ?','[{"s": "Instantaneous speed", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or simply speed is the magnitude of velocity. For example, a velocity of + 24.0 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and a velocity of – 24.0 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "— both have an associated speed of 24.0 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It should be noted that though average speed over a finite interval of time is greater or equal to the magnitude of the average velocity, instantaneous speed at an instant is equal to the magnitude of the instantaneous velocity at that instant. Why so ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27945,69,88,'heading',2,'3.5 ACCELERATION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29008,71,141,'paragraph',NULL,'Marie Sklodowska Curie (1867-1934) Born in Poland. She is recognised both as a physicist and as a chemist. The discovery of radioactivity by Henri Becquerel in 1896 inspired Marie and her husband Pierre Curie in their researches and analyses which led to the isolation of radium and polonium elements. She was the first person to be awarded two Nobel Prizes- for Physics in 1903 and for Chemistry in 1911.','[{"s": "Marie Sklodowska Curie (1867-1934)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Born in Poland. She is recognised both as a physicist and as a chemist. The discovery of radioactivity by Henri Becquerel in 1896 inspired Marie and her husband Pierre Curie in their researches and analyses which led to the isolation of radium and polonium elements. She was the first person to be awarded two Nobel Prizes- for Physics in 1903 and for Chemistry in 1911.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29009,71,142,'paragraph',NULL,'1 curie = 1 Ci = 3.7 × 10 10 decays per second','[{"s": "1 curie = 1 Ci = 3.7 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decays per second", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29010,71,143,'paragraph',NULL,'= 3.7 × 10 10 Bq','[{"s": "= 3.7 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Bq", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27946,69,89,'paragraph',NULL,'The velocity of an object, in general, changes during its course of motion. How to describe this change? Should it be described as the rate of change in velocity with distance or with time ? This was a problem even in Galileo’s time. It was first thought that this change could be described by the rate of change of velocity with distance. But, through his studies of motion of freely falling objects and motion of objects on an inclined plane, Galileo concluded that the rate of change of velocity with time is a constant of motion for all objects in free fall. On the other hand, the change in velocity with distance is not constant – it decreases with the increasing distance of fall. This led to the concept of acceleration as the rate of change of velocity with time.','[{"s": "The velocity of an object, in general, changes during its course of motion. How to describe this change? Should it be described as the rate of change in velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "? This was a problem even in Galileo’s time. It was first thought that this change could be described by the rate of change of velocity with distance. But, through his studies of motion of freely falling objects and motion of objects on an inclined plane, Galileo concluded that the rate of change of velocity with time is a constant of motion for all objects in free fall. On the other hand, the change in velocity with distance is not constant – it decreases with the increasing distance of fall. This led to the concept of acceleration as the rate of change of velocity with time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27947,69,90,'paragraph',NULL,'The average acceleration Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image55.png over a time interval is defined as the change of velocity divided by the time interval :','[{"s": "The average acceleration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image55.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "over a time interval is defined as the change of velocity divided by the time interval :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27948,69,91,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image56.png'),
(27949,69,92,'paragraph',NULL,'(3.4)','[{"s": "(3.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27950,69,93,'paragraph',NULL,'where v 2 and v 1 are the instantaneous velocities or simply velocities at time t 2 and t 1 . It is the average change of velocity per unit time. The SI unit of acceleration is m s –2 .','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the instantaneous velocities or simply velocities at time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is the average change of velocity per unit time. The SI unit of acceleration is m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27951,69,94,'paragraph',NULL,'On a plot of velocity versus time, the average acceleration is the slope of the straight line connecting the points corresponding to ( v 2 , t 2 ) and ( v 1 , t 1','[{"s": "On a plot of velocity versus time, the average acceleration is the slope of the straight line connecting the points corresponding to (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27952,69,95,'paragraph',NULL,'0 s - 10 s Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image57.png','[{"s": "0 s - 10 s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image57.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
