(27955,69,98,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image25.png'),
(27956,69,99,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.8 Acceleration as a function of time for motion represented in Fig. 3.3.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Acceleration as a function of time for motion represented in Fig. 3.3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27958,69,101,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image27.png'),
(27959,69,102,'paragraph',NULL,'(3.5)','[{"s": "(3.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27960,69,103,'paragraph',NULL,'The acceleration at an instant is the slope of the tangent to the v–t curve at that instant. For the v–t curve shown in Fig. 3.7, we can obtain acceleration at every instant of time. The resulting a – t curve is shown in Fig. 3.8. We see that the acceleration is nonuniform over the period 0 s to 10 s. It is zero between 10 s and 18 s and is constant with value -12 m s –2 between 18 s and 20 s. When the acceleration is uniform, obviously, it equals the average acceleration over that period.','[{"s": "The acceleration at an instant is the slope of the tangent to the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v–t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "curve at that instant. For the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v–t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "curve shown in Fig. 3.7, we can obtain acceleration at every instant of time. The resulting", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a – t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "curve is shown in Fig. 3.8. We see that the acceleration is nonuniform over the period 0 s to 10 s. It is zero between 10 s and 18 s and is constant with value -12 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "between 18 s and 20 s. When the acceleration is uniform, obviously, it equals the average acceleration over that period.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27957,69,100,'paragraph',NULL,'Instantaneous acceleration is defined in the same way as the instantaneous velocity','[{"s": "Instantaneous acceleration", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is defined in the same way as the instantaneous velocity", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000039026", "2000039040", "2000039052", "2000039064", "2000039072", "2000039096", "2000039783", "2000039935", "2000039938", "2000039947", "2000039950", "2000039962", "2000039987", "2000040448", "2000106130", "2000106131", "2000106145", "2000106163", "2000106164", "2000106199"]',NULL),
(27961,69,104,'paragraph',NULL,'Since velocity is a quantity having both magnitude and direction, a change in velocity may involve either or both of these factors. Acceleration, therefore, may result from a change in speed (magnitude), a change in direction or changes in both. Like velocity, acceleration can also be positive, negative or zero. Position-time graphs for motion with positive, negative and zero acceleration are shown in Figs. 3.9 (a), (b) and (c), respectively. As an exercise, identify in Fig. 3.3, the regions of the curve that correspond to these three cases.','[{"s": "Since velocity is a quantity having both magnitude and direction, a change in velocity may involve either or both of these factors. Acceleration, therefore, may result from a change in speed (magnitude), a change in direction or changes in both. Like velocity, acceleration can also be positive, negative or zero. Position-time graphs for motion with positive, negative and zero acceleration are shown in Figs. 3.9 (a), (b) and (c), respectively.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "As an exercise, identify in Fig. 3.3, the regions of the curve that correspond to these three cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27962,69,105,'paragraph',NULL,'Although acceleration can vary with time, our study in this chapter will be restricted to motion with constant acceleration. In this case, the average acceleration equals the constant value of acceleration during the interval. If the velocity of an object is v o at t = 0 and v at time t , we have','[{"s": "Although acceleration can vary with time, our study in this chapter will be restricted to motion with constant acceleration. In this case, the average acceleration equals the constant value of acceleration during the interval. If the velocity of an object is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27963,69,106,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image29.png'),
(27964,69,107,'paragraph',NULL,'(3.6)','[{"s": "(3.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27965,69,108,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image31.png'),
(27966,69,109,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.9','[{"s": "Fig. 3.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27967,69,110,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us see how velocity-time graph looks like for some simple cases. Fig. 3.10 shows velocity-time graph for motion with constant acceleration for the following cases :','[{"s": "Let us see how velocity-time graph looks like for some simple cases. Fig. 3.10 shows velocity-time graph for motion with constant acceleration for the following cases :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27968,69,111,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image33.png'),
(27969,69,112,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.10 1 . Between times 0 to t 1 , its moves in positive x - direction and between t 1 and t 2 it moves in the opposite direction.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Between times 0 to t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", its moves in positive x - direction and between t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "it moves in the opposite direction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27970,69,113,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) An object is moving in a positive direction with a positive acceleration, for example the motion of the car in Fig. 3.3 between t = 0 s and t = 10 s.','[{"s": "(a) An object is moving in a positive direction with a positive acceleration, for example the motion of the car in Fig. 3.3 between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 s and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 s.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27971,69,114,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) An object is moving in positive direction with a negative acceleration, for example, motion of the car in Fig 3.3 between t = 18 s and 20 s.','[{"s": "(b) An object is moving in positive direction with a negative acceleration, for example, motion of the car in Fig 3.3 between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 18 s and 20 s.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27972,69,115,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) An object is moving in negative direction with a negative acceleration, for example the motion of a car moving from O in Fig. 3.1 in negative x- direction with increasing speed.','[{"s": "(c) An object is moving in negative direction with a negative acceleration, for example the motion of a car moving from O in Fig. 3.1 in negative", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "direction with increasing speed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27973,69,116,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) An object is moving in positive direction till time t 1 , and then turns back with the same negative acceleration, for example the motion of a car from point O to point Q in Fig. 3.1 till time t 1 with decreasing speed and turning back and moving with the same negative acceleration.','[{"s": "(d) An object is moving in positive direction till time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and then turns back with the same negative acceleration, for example the motion of a car from point O to point Q in Fig. 3.1 till time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with decreasing speed and turning back and moving with the same negative acceleration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27974,69,117,'paragraph',NULL,'An interesting feature of a velocity-time graph for any moving object is that the area under the curve represents the displacement over a given time interval . A general proof of this statement requires use of calculus. We can, however, see that it is true for the simple case of an object moving with constant velocity u . Its velocity-time graph is as shown in Fig. 3.11.','[{"s": "An interesting feature of a velocity-time graph for any moving object is that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the area under the curve represents the displacement over a given time interval", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". A general proof of this statement requires use of calculus. We can, however, see that it is true for the simple case of an object moving with constant velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "u", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Its velocity-time graph is as shown in Fig. 3.11.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27975,69,118,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image35.png'),
(27976,69,119,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.11 Area under v–t curve equals displacement of the object over a given time interval.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Area under v–t curve equals displacement of the object over a given time interval.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27977,69,120,'paragraph',NULL,'The v-t curve is a straight line parallel to the time axis and the area under it between t = 0 and t = T is the area of the rectangle of height u and base T . Therefore, area = u × T = uT which is the displacement in this time interval. How come in this case an area is equal to a distance? Think! Note the dimensions of quantities on the two coordinate axes, and you will arrive at the answer.','[{"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v-t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "curve is a straight line parallel to the time axis and the area under it between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the area of the rectangle of height", "t": "text"}, {"s": "u", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and base", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Therefore, area =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "u", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "uT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which is the displacement in this time interval. How come in this case an area is equal to a distance? Think! Note the dimensions of quantities on the two coordinate axes, and you will arrive at the answer.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27978,69,121,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the x-t, v-t , and a-t graphs shown in several figures in this chapter have sharp kinks at some points implying that the functions are not differentiable at these points. In any realistic situation, the functions will be differentiable at all points and the graphs will be smooth .','[{"s": "Note that the x-t, v-t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and a-t graphs shown in several figures in this chapter have sharp kinks at some points implying that the functions are not differentiable at these points. In any realistic situation, the functions will be differentiable at all points and the graphs will be smooth", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27979,69,122,'paragraph',NULL,'What this means physically is that acceleration and velocity cannot change values abruptly at an instant. Changes are always continuous.','[{"s": "What this means physically is that acceleration and velocity cannot change values abruptly at an instant. Changes are always continuous.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27980,69,123,'heading',2,'3.6 KINEMATIC EQUATIONS FOR UNIFORMLY ACCELERATED MOTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27981,69,124,'paragraph',NULL,'For uniformly accelerated motion, we can derive some simple equations that relate displacement ( x ), time taken ( t ), initial velocity ( v 0 ), final velocity ( v ) and acceleration ( a ). Equation (3.6) already obtained gives a relation between final and initial velocities v and v 0 of an object moving with uniform acceleration a :','[{"s": "For uniformly accelerated motion, we can derive some simple equations that relate displacement (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), time taken (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), initial velocity (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), final velocity (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and acceleration (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Equation (3.6) already obtained gives a relation between final and initial velocities", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of an object moving with uniform acceleration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27982,69,125,'paragraph',NULL,'v = v 0 + at (3.6)','[{"s": "v = v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27983,69,126,'paragraph',NULL,'This relation is graphically represented in Fig. 3.12.','[{"s": "This relation is graphically represented in Fig. 3.12.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
