(28075,69,218,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the second method is better since we do not have to worry about the path of the motion as the motion is under constant acceleration.','[{"s": "Note that the second method is better since we do not have to worry about the path of the motion as the motion is under constant acceleration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28076,69,219,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 3.5 Free-fall : Discuss the motion of an object under free fall. Neglect air resistance.','[{"s": "Example 3.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Free-fall : Discuss the motion of an object under free fall. Neglect air resistance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28077,69,220,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer An object released near the surface of the Earth is accelerated downward under the influence of the force of gravity. The magnitude of acceleration due to gravity is represented by g. If air resistance is neglected, the object is said to be in free fall . If the height through which the object falls is small compared to the earth’s radius, g can be taken to be constant, equal to 9.8 m s –2 . Free fall is thus a case of motion with uniform acceleration.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An object released near the surface of the Earth is accelerated downward under the influence of the force of gravity. The magnitude of acceleration due to gravity is represented by g. If air resistance is neglected, the object is said to be in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "free fall", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If the height through which the object falls is small compared to the earth’s radius,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be taken to be constant, equal to 9.8 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Free fall is thus a case of motion with uniform acceleration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28078,69,221,'paragraph',NULL,'We assume that the motion is in y -direction, more correctly in – y- direction because we choose upward direction as positive. Since the acceleration due to gravity is always downward, it is in the negative direction and we have','[{"s": "We assume that the motion is in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-direction, more correctly in –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "direction because we choose upward direction as positive. Since the acceleration due to gravity is always downward, it is in the negative direction and we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28079,69,222,'paragraph',NULL,'a = – g = – 9.8 m s –2','[{"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 9.8 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28080,69,223,'paragraph',NULL,'The object is released from rest at y = 0. Therefore, v 0 = 0 and the equations of motion become:','[{"s": "The object is released from rest at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0. Therefore,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 and the equations of motion become:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28081,69,224,'paragraph',NULL,'v = 0 – g t = –9.8 t m s –1','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –9.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28082,69,225,'paragraph',NULL,'y = 0 – ½ g t 2 = –4.9 t 2 m','[{"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 – ½", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –4.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28083,69,226,'paragraph',NULL,'v 2 = 0 – 2 g y = –19.6 y m 2 s –2','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 – 2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –19.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28084,69,227,'paragraph',NULL,'These equations give the velocity and the distance travelled as a function of time and also the variation of velocity with distance. The variation of acceleration, velocity, and distance, with time have been plotted in Fig. 3.14(a), (b) and (c).','[{"s": "These equations give the velocity and the distance travelled as a function of time and also the variation of velocity with distance. The variation of acceleration, velocity, and distance, with time have been plotted in Fig. 3.14(a), (b) and (c).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28085,69,228,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 9.41.07 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image40.png'),
(28086,69,229,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 9.41.18 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image21.png'),
(28087,69,230,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.14 Motion of an object under free fall. (a) Variation of acceleration with time.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion of an object under free fall. (a) Variation of acceleration with time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28088,69,231,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Variation of velocity with time.','[{"s": "(b) Variation of velocity with time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28089,69,232,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Variation of distance with time t','[{"s": "(c) Variation of distance with time t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28091,69,234,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Let us divide the time interval of motion of an object under free fall into many equal intervals Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image22.png and find out the distances traversed during successive intervals of time. Since initial velocity is zero, we have','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Let us divide the time interval of motion of an object under free fall into many equal intervals", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image22.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and find out the distances traversed during successive intervals of time. Since initial velocity is zero, we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28092,69,235,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 9.43.53 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image24.png'),
(28093,69,236,'paragraph',NULL,'Using this equation, we can calculate the position of the object after different time intervals, 0, τ, 2τ, 3τ… which are given in second column of Table 3.2. If we take (–1/ 2) g τ 2 as y 0 — the position coordinate after first time interval τ, then third column gives the positions in the unit of y o . The fourth column gives the distances traversed in successive τs. We find that the distances are in the simple ratio 1: 3: 5: 7: 9: 11… as shown in the last column. This law was established by Galileo Galilei (1564-1642) who was the first to make quantitative studies of free fall.','[{"s": "Using this equation, we can calculate the position of the object after different time intervals, 0, τ, 2τ, 3τ… which are given in second column of Table 3.2. If we take (–1/ 2)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "— the position coordinate after first time interval τ, then third column gives the positions in the unit of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The fourth column gives the distances traversed in successive τs. We find that the distances are in the simple ratio 1: 3: 5: 7: 9: 11… as shown in the last column. This law was established by Galileo Galilei (1564-1642) who was the first to make quantitative studies of free fall.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28094,69,237,'heading',5,'Table 3.2',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28095,69,238,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 9.53.25 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image26.png'),
(28096,69,239,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 3.7 Stopping distance of vehicles : When brakes are applied to a moving vehicle, the distance it travels before stopping is called stopping distance. It is an important factor for road safety and depends on the initial velocity (v 0 ) and the braking capacity, or deceleration, –a that is caused by the braking. Derive an expression for stopping distance of a vehicle in terms of v o and a.','[{"s": "Example 3.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Stopping distance of vehicles : When brakes are applied to a moving vehicle, the distance it travels before stopping is called stopping distance. It is an important factor for road safety and depends on the initial velocity (v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and the braking capacity, or deceleration, –a that is caused by the braking. Derive an expression for stopping distance of a vehicle in terms of v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and a.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28097,69,240,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Let the distance travelled by the vehicle before it stops be d s . Then, using equation of motion v 2 = v o 2 + 2 ax , and noting that v = 0, we have the stopping distance','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Let the distance travelled by the vehicle before it stops be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Then, using equation of motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ax", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and noting that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0, we have the stopping distance", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28098,69,241,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image28.png'),
(28099,69,242,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the stopping distance is proportional to the square of the initial velocity. Doubling the initial velocity increases the stopping distance by a factor of 4 (for the same deceleration).','[{"s": "Thus, the stopping distance is proportional to the square of the initial velocity. Doubling the initial velocity increases the stopping distance by a factor of 4 (for the same deceleration).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28100,69,243,'paragraph',NULL,'For the car of a particular make, the braking distance was found to be 10 m, 20 m, 34 m and 50 m corresponding to velocities of 11, 15, 20 and 25 m/s which are nearly consistent with the above formula.','[{"s": "For the car of a particular make, the braking distance was found to be 10 m, 20 m, 34 m and 50 m corresponding to velocities of 11, 15, 20 and 25 m/s which are nearly consistent with the above formula.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28101,69,244,'paragraph',NULL,'Stopping distance is an important factor considered in setting speed limits, for example, in school zones. t','[{"s": "Stopping distance is an important factor considered in setting speed limits, for example, in school zones. t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28102,69,245,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 3.8 Reaction time : When a situation demands our immediate action, it takes some time before we really respond. Reaction time is the time a person takes to observe, think and act. For example, if a person is driving and suddenly a boy appears on the road, then the time elapsed before he slams the brakes of the car is the reaction time. Reaction time depends on complexity of the situation and on an individual.','[{"s": "Example 3.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Reaction time : When a situation demands our immediate action, it takes some time before we really respond. Reaction time is the time a person takes to observe, think and act. For example, if a person is driving and suddenly a boy appears on the road, then the time elapsed before he slams the brakes of the car is the reaction time. Reaction time depends on complexity of the situation and on an individual.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28103,69,246,'paragraph',NULL,'You can measure your reaction time by a simple experiment. Take a ruler and ask your friend to drop it vertically through the gap between your thumb and forefinger (Fig. 3.15). After you catch it, find the distance d travelled by the ruler. In a particular case, d was found to be 21.0 cm. Estimate reaction time.','[{"s": "You can measure your reaction time by a simple experiment. Take a ruler and ask your friend to drop it vertically through the gap between your thumb and forefinger (Fig. 3.15). After you catch it, find the distance d travelled by the ruler. In a particular case, d was found to be 21.0 cm. Estimate reaction time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28104,69,247,'image',NULL,'fig3.15',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image30.png'),
(28105,69,248,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.15 Measuring the reaction time.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Measuring the reaction time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28106,69,249,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The ruler drops under free fall. Therefore, v o = 0, and a = – g = –9.8 m s –2 . The distance travelled d and the reaction time t r are related by','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The ruler drops under free fall. Therefore,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0, and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –9.8 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The distance travelled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the reaction time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are related by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28107,69,250,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image32.png'),
(28108,69,251,'paragraph',NULL,'Or, Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image34.png','[{"s": "Or,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image34.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28109,69,252,'paragraph',NULL,'Given d = 21.0 cm and g = 9.8 m s –2 the reaction time is','[{"s": "Given", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 21.0 cm and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 9.8 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the reaction time is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28110,69,253,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image18.png'),
(28111,69,254,'heading',2,'3.7 RELATIVE VELOCITY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28112,69,255,'paragraph',NULL,'You must be familiar with the experience of travelling in a train and being overtaken by another train moving in the same direction as you are. While that train must be travelling faster than you to be able to pass you, it does seem slower to you than it would be to someone standing on the ground and watching both the trains. In case both the trains have the same velocity with respect to the ground, then to you the other train would seem to be not moving at all. To understand such observations, we now introduce the concept of relative velocity.','[{"s": "You must be familiar with the experience of travelling in a train and being overtaken by another train moving in the same direction as you are. While that train must be travelling faster than you to be able to pass you, it does seem slower to you than it would be to someone standing on the ground and watching both the trains. In case both the trains have the same velocity with respect to the ground, then to you the other train would seem to be not moving at all. To understand such observations, we now introduce the concept of relative velocity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28113,69,256,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider two objects A and B moving uniformly with average velocities v A and v B in one dimension, say along x -axis. (Unless otherwise specified, the velocities mentioned in this chapter are measured with reference to the ground). If x A (0) and x B (0) are positions of objects A and B , respectively at time t = 0, their positions x A ( t ) and x B ( t ) at time t are given by:','[{"s": "Consider two objects", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "moving uniformly with average velocities", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in one dimension, say along", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-axis. (Unless otherwise specified, the velocities mentioned in this chapter are measured with reference to the ground). If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) are positions of objects", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively at time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0, their positions", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") at time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are given by:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28114,69,257,'paragraph',NULL,'x A ( t ) = x A (0) + v A t (3.12a)','[{"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.12a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28115,69,258,'paragraph',NULL,'x B ( t ) = x B (0) + v B t (3.12b)','[{"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.12b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28116,69,259,'paragraph',NULL,'Then, the displacement from object A to object B is given by','[{"s": "Then, the displacement from object", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to object", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28117,69,260,'paragraph',NULL,'x BA ( t ) = x B ( t ) – x A ( t )','[{"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28118,69,261,'paragraph',NULL,'= [ x B (0) – x A (0) ] + ( v B – v A ) t . (3.13)','[{"s": "= [", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) ] + (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". (3.13)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28119,69,262,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (3.13) is easily interpreted. It tells us that as seen from object A , object B has a velocity v B – v A because the displacement from A to B changes steadily by the amount v B – v A in each unit of time. We say that the velocity of object B relative to object A is v B – v A :','[{"s": "Equation (3.13) is easily interpreted. It tells us that as seen from object", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", object", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has a velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "because the displacement from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "changes steadily by the amount", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in each unit of time. We say that the velocity of object", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "relative to object A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28120,69,263,'paragraph',NULL,'v BA = v B – v A (3.14a)','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.14a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28121,69,264,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, velocity of object A relative to object B is:','[{"s": "Similarly, velocity of object", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "relative to object B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28122,69,265,'paragraph',NULL,'v AB = v A – v B (3.14b)','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.14b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28123,69,266,'paragraph',NULL,'This shows: v BA = – v AB (3.14c)','[{"s": "This shows:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(3.14c)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
