(28125,69,268,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.16 Position-time graphs of two objects with equal velocities.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Position-time graphs of two objects with equal velocities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28126,69,269,'paragraph',NULL,'Now we consider some special cases :','[{"s": "Now we consider some special cases :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28127,69,270,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) If v B = v A , v B – v A = 0. Then, from Eq. (3.13), x B ( t ) – x A ( t ) = x B (0) – x A (0). Therefore, the two objects stay at a constant distance ( x B (0) – x A (0)) apart, and their position–time graphs are straight lines parallel to each other as shown in Fig. 3.16. The relative velocity v AB or v BA is zero in this case.','[{"s": "(a) If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0. Then, from Eq. (3.13),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0). Therefore, the two objects stay at a constant distance (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0)) apart, and their position–time graphs are straight lines parallel to each other as shown in Fig. 3.16. The relative velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is zero in this case.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28128,69,271,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) If v A > v B , v B – v A is negative. One graph is steeper than the other and they meet at a common point. For example, suppose v A = 20 m s -1 and x A (0) = 10 m; and v B = 10 m s -1 , x B (0) = 40 m; then the time at which they meet is t = 3 s (Fig. 3.17). At this instant they are both at a position x A ( t ) = x B ( t ) = 70 m. Thus, object A overtakes object B at this time. In this case, v BA = 10 m s –1 – 20 m s –1 = – 10 m s –1 = – v AB .','[{"s": "(b) If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is negative. One graph is steeper than the other and they meet at a common point. For example, suppose", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 20 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) = 10 m; and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) = 40 m; then the time at which they meet is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3 s (Fig. 3.17). At this instant they are both at a position", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = 70 m. Thus, object", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "overtakes object", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at this time. In this case,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– 20 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 10 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28129,69,272,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Suppose v A and v B are of opposite signs. For example, if in the above example object A is moving with 20 m s –1 starting at x A (0) = 10 m and object B is moving with – 10 m s –1 starting at x B (0) = 40 m, the two objects meet at t = 1 s (Fig. 3.18). The velocity of B relative to A , v BA = [–10 – (20)] m s –1 = –30 m s –1 = – v AB . In this case, the magnitude of v BA or v AB ( = 30 m s –1 ) is greater than the magnitude of velocity of A or that of B . If the objects under consideration are two trains, then for a person sitting on either of the two, the other train seems to go very fast.','[{"s": "(c) Suppose", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are of opposite signs. For example, if in the above example object", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is moving with 20 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "starting at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) = 10 m and object", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is moving with – 10 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "starting at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0) = 40 m, the two objects meet at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1 s (Fig. 3.18). The velocity of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "relative to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [–10 – (20)] m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –30 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In this case, the magnitude of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "( = 30 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is greater than the magnitude of velocity of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or that of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If the objects under consideration are two trains, then for a person sitting on either of the two, the other train seems to go very fast.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28130,69,273,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that Eq. (3.14) are valid even if v A and v B represent instantaneous velocities.','[{"s": "Note that Eq. (3.14) are valid even if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "represent instantaneous velocities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28131,69,274,'image',NULL,'C3.17',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image20.png'),
(28132,69,275,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.17 Position-time graphs of two objects with unequal velocities, showing the time of meeting.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Position-time graphs of two objects with unequal velocities, showing the time of meeting.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28133,69,276,'image',NULL,'C3.18',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image2.png'),
(28134,69,277,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.18 Position-time graphs of two objects with velocities in opposite directions, showing the time of meeting.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Position-time graphs of two objects with velocities in opposite directions, showing the time of meeting.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28135,69,278,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 3.9 Two parallel rail tracks run north-south. Train A moves north with a speed of 54 km h –1 , and train B moves south with a speed of 90 km h –1 . What is the','[{"s": "Example 3.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two parallel rail tracks run north-south. Train A moves north with a speed of 54 km h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and train B moves south with a speed of 90 km h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". What is the", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28136,69,279,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) velocity of B with respect to A ?,','[{"s": "(a) velocity of B with respect to A ?,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28137,69,280,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) velocity of ground with respect to B ?, and','[{"s": "(b) velocity of ground with respect to B ?, and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28138,69,281,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) velocity of a monkey running on the roof of the train A against its motion (with a velocity of 18 km h –1 with respect to the train A) as observed by a man standing on the ground ?','[{"s": "(c) velocity of a monkey running on the roof of the train A against its motion (with a velocity of 18 km h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with respect to the train A) as observed by a man standing on the ground ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28139,69,282,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Choose the positive direction of x -axis to be from south to north. Then,','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Choose the positive direction of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-axis to be from south to north. Then,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28140,69,283,'paragraph',NULL,'v A = + 54 km h –1 = 15 m s –1','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= + 54 km h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 15 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28141,69,284,'paragraph',NULL,'v B = – 90 km h –1 = – 25 m s –1','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 90 km h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 25 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28142,69,285,'paragraph',NULL,'Relative velocity of B with respect to A = v B – v A = – 40 m s –1 , i.e. the train B appears to A to move with a speed of 40 m s –1 from north to south.','[{"s": "Relative velocity of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with respect to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – 40 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", i.e. the train", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "appears to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to move with a speed of 40 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from north to south.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28143,69,286,'paragraph',NULL,'B = 0 – v B = 25 m s –1 .','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 25 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28144,69,287,'paragraph',NULL,'In (c), let the velocity of the monkey with respect to ground be v M . Relative velocity of the monkey with respect to A,','[{"s": "In (c), let the velocity of the monkey with respect to ground be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Relative velocity of the monkey with respect to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28145,69,288,'paragraph',NULL,'v MA = v M – v A = –18 km h –1 =–5 ms –1 v M = (15 – 5) m s –1 = 10 m s –1 .','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "MA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –18 km h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=–5 ms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (15 – 5) m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28146,69,289,'heading',2,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28147,69,290,'paragraph',NULL,'1. An object is said to be in motion if its position changes with time. The position of the object can be specified with reference to a conveniently chosen origin. For motion in a straight line, position to the right of the origin is taken as positive and to the left as negative.','[{"s": "1. An object is said to be in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": "if its position changes with time. The position of the object can be specified with reference to a conveniently chosen origin. For motion in a straight line, position to the right of the origin is taken as positive and to the left as negative.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28148,69,291,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Path length is defined as the total length of the path traversed by an object.','[{"s": "2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Path length", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is defined as the total length of the path traversed by an object.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28149,69,292,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Displacement is the change in position : ∆ x = x 2 – x 1 . Path length is greater or equal to the magnitude of the displacement between the same points.','[{"s": "3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Displacement", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the change in position :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Path length is greater or equal to the magnitude of the displacement between the same points.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28150,69,293,'paragraph',NULL,'4. An object is said to be in uniform motion in a straight line if its displacement is equal in equal intervals of time. Otherwise, the motion is said to be non-uniform.','[{"s": "4. An object is said to be in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "uniform motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in a straight line if its displacement is equal in equal intervals of time. Otherwise, the motion is said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "non-uniform.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28151,69,294,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Average velocity is the displacement divided by the time interval in which the displacement occurs :','[{"s": "5.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Average velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the displacement divided by the time interval in which the displacement occurs :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28152,69,295,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image4.png'),
(28153,69,296,'paragraph',NULL,'On an x-t graph, the average velocity over a time interval is the slope of the line connecting the initial and final positions corresponding to that interval.','[{"s": "On an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x-t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "graph, the average velocity over a time interval is the slope of the line connecting the initial and final positions corresponding to that interval.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28154,69,297,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Average Speed is the ratio of total path length traversed and the corresponding time interval.','[{"s": "6.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Average Speed", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the ratio of total path length traversed and the corresponding time interval.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29012,71,145,'paragraph',NULL,'T 1/2 = Nuclei-image102.png = Nuclei-image74.png (13.17)','[{"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image102.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image74.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28157,69,300,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image6.png'),
(28158,69,301,'paragraph',NULL,'The velocity at a particular instant is equal to the slope of the tangent drawn on position-time graph at that instant.','[{"s": "The velocity at a particular instant is equal to the slope of the tangent drawn on position-time graph at that instant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28159,69,302,'paragraph',NULL,'8. Average acceleration is the change in velocity divided by the time interval during which the change occurs :','[{"s": "8.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Average acceleration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the change in velocity divided by the time interval during which the change occurs :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28160,69,303,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image8.png'),
(28161,69,304,'paragraph',NULL,'9. Instantaneous acceleration is defined as the limit of the average acceleration as the time interval ∆ t goes to zero :','[{"s": "9.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Instantaneous acceleration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is defined as the limit of the average acceleration as the time interval ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "goes to zero :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28162,69,305,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image10.png'),
(28163,69,306,'paragraph',NULL,'The acceleration of an object at a particular time is the slope of the velocity-time graph at that instant of time. For uniform motion, acceleration is zero and the x-t graph is a straight line inclined to the time axis and the v-t graph is a straight line parallel to the time axis. For motion with uniform acceleration, x-t graph is a parabola while the v-t graph is a straight line inclined to the time axis.','[{"s": "The acceleration of an object at a particular time is the slope of the velocity-time graph at that instant of time. For uniform motion, acceleration is zero and the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x-t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "graph is a straight line inclined to the time axis and the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v-t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "graph is a straight line parallel to the time axis. For motion with uniform acceleration,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x-t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "graph is a parabola while the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v-t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "graph is a straight line inclined to the time axis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28164,69,307,'paragraph',NULL,'10. The area under the velocity-time curve between times t 1 and t 2 is equal to the displacement of the object during that interval of time.','[{"s": "10. The area under the velocity-time curve between times", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is equal to the displacement of the object during that interval of time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28165,69,308,'paragraph',NULL,'11. For objects in uniformly accelerated rectilinear motion, the five quantities, displacement x , time taken t , initial velocity v 0 , final velocity v and acceleration a are related by a set of simple equations called kinematic equations of motion :','[{"s": "11. For objects in uniformly accelerated rectilinear motion, the five quantities, displacement", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", time taken", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", initial velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", final velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and acceleration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are related by a set of simple equations called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kinematic equations of motion :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28166,69,309,'paragraph',NULL,'v = v 0 + at','[{"s": "v = v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ at", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28167,69,310,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image12.png'),
(28168,69,311,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image14.png'),
(28169,69,312,'paragraph',NULL,'if the position of the object at time t = 0 is 0. If the particle starts at x = x 0 , x in above equations is replaced by ( x – x 0 ).','[{"s": "if the position of the object at time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 is 0. If the particle starts at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x = x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in above equations is replaced by (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x – x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28170,69,313,'image',NULL,'T3.3',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Motion_in_a_Straight_line-image1.png'),
(28171,69,314,'heading',3,'POINTS TO PONDER',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28172,69,315,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The path length traversed by an object between two points is, in general, not the same as the magnitude of displacement. The displacement depends only on the end points; the path length (as the name implies) depends on the actual path. In one dimension, the two quantities are equal only if the object does not change its direction during the course of motion. In all other cases, the path length is greater than the magnitude of displacement.','[{"s": "1. The path length traversed by an object between two points is, in general, not the same as the magnitude of displacement. The displacement depends only on the end points; the path length (as the name implies) depends on the actual path. In one dimension, the two quantities are equal only if the object does not change its direction during the course of motion. In all other cases, the path length is greater than the magnitude of displacement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28173,69,316,'paragraph',NULL,'2. In view of point 1 above, the average speed of an object is greater than or equal to the magnitude of the average velocity over a given time interval. The two are equal only if the path length is equal to the magnitude of displacement.','[{"s": "2. In view of point 1 above, the average speed of an object is greater than or equal to the magnitude of the average velocity over a given time interval. The two are equal only if the path length is equal to the magnitude of displacement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28174,69,317,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The origin and the positive direction of an axis are a matter of choice. You should first specify this choice before you assign signs to quantities like displacement, velocity and acceleration.','[{"s": "3. The origin and the positive direction of an axis are a matter of choice. You should first specify this choice before you assign signs to quantities like displacement, velocity and acceleration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28175,69,318,'paragraph',NULL,'4. If a particle is speeding up, acceleration is in the direction of velocity; if its speed is decreasing, acceleration is in the direction opposite to that of the velocity. This statement is independent of the choice of the origin and the axis.','[{"s": "4. If a particle is speeding up, acceleration is in the direction of velocity; if its speed is decreasing, acceleration is in the direction opposite to that of the velocity. This statement is independent of the choice of the origin and the axis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28176,69,319,'paragraph',NULL,'5. The sign of acceleration does not tell us whether the particle’s speed is increasing or decreasing. The sign of acceleration (as mentioned in point 3) depends on the choice of the positive direction of the axis. For example, if the vertically upward direction is chosen to be the positive direction of the axis, the acceleration due to gravity is negative. If a particle is falling under gravity, this acceleration, though negative, results in increase in speed. For a particle thrown upward, the same negative acceleration (of gravity) results in decrease in speed.','[{"s": "5. The sign of acceleration does not tell us whether the particle’s speed is increasing or decreasing. The sign of acceleration (as mentioned in point 3) depends on the choice of the positive direction of the axis. For example, if the vertically upward direction is chosen to be the positive direction of the axis, the acceleration due to gravity is negative. If a particle is falling under gravity, this acceleration, though negative, results in increase in speed. For a particle thrown upward, the same negative acceleration (of gravity) results in decrease in speed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28177,69,320,'paragraph',NULL,'6. The zero velocity of a particle at any instant does not necessarily imply zero acceleration at that instant. A particle may be momentarily at rest and yet have non-zero acceleration. For example, a particle thrown up has zero velocity at its uppermost point but the acceleration at that instant continues to be the acceleration due to gravity.','[{"s": "6. The zero velocity of a particle at any instant does not necessarily imply zero acceleration at that instant. A particle may be momentarily at rest and yet have non-zero acceleration. For example, a particle thrown up has zero velocity at its uppermost point but the acceleration at that instant continues to be the acceleration due to gravity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28178,69,321,'paragraph',NULL,'7. In the kinematic equations of motion [Eq. (3.11)], the various quantities are algebraic, i.e. they may be positive or negative. The equations are applicable in all situations (for one dimensional motion with constant acceleration) provided the values of different quantities are substituted in the equations with proper signs.','[{"s": "7. In the kinematic equations of motion [Eq. (3.11)], the various quantities are algebraic, i.e. they may be positive or negative. The equations are applicable in all situations (for one dimensional motion with constant acceleration) provided the values of different quantities are substituted in the equations with proper signs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28179,69,322,'paragraph',NULL,'8. The definitions of instantaneous velocity and acceleration (Eqs. (3.3) and (3.5)) are exact and are always correct while the kinematic equations (Eq. (3.11)) are true only for motion in which the magnitude and the direction of acceleration are constant during the course of motion.','[{"s": "8. The definitions of instantaneous velocity and acceleration (Eqs. (3.3) and (3.5)) are exact and are always correct while the kinematic equations (Eq. (3.11)) are true only for motion in which the magnitude and the direction of acceleration are constant during the course of motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28180,70,0,'heading',2,'Chapter Four',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28181,70,1,'heading',1,'Moving Charges and Magnetism',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28182,70,2,'heading',2,'4.1 Introduction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28183,70,3,'paragraph',NULL,'Both Electricity and Magnetism have been known for more than 2000 years. However, it was only about 200 years ago, in 1820, that it was realised that they were intimately related*. During a lecture demonstration in the summer of 1820, Danish physicist Hans Christian Oersted noticed that a current in a straight wire caused a noticeable deflection in a nearby magnetic compass needle. He investigated this phenomenon. He found that the alignment of the needle is tangential to an imaginary circle which has the straight wire as its centre and has its plane perpendicular to the wire. This situation is depicted in Fig.4.1(a). It is noticeable when the current is large and the needle sufficiently close to the wire so that the earth’s magnetic field may be ignored. Reversing the direction of the current reverses the orientation of the needle [Fig. 4.1(b)]. The deflection increases on increasing the current or bringing the needle closer to the wire. Iron filings sprinkled around the wire arrange themselves in concentric circles with the wire as the centre [Fig. 4.1(c)]. Oersted concluded that moving charges or currents produced a magnetic field in the surrounding space','[{"s": "Both Electricity and Magnetism have been known for more than 2000 years. However, it was only about 200 years ago, in 1820, that it was realised that they were intimately related*. During a lecture demonstration in the summer of 1820, Danish physicist Hans Christian Oersted noticed that a current in a straight wire caused a noticeable deflection in a nearby magnetic compass needle. He investigated this phenomenon. He found that the alignment of the needle is tangential to an imaginary circle which has the straight wire as its centre and has its plane perpendicular to the wire. This situation is depicted in Fig.4.1(a). It is noticeable when the current is large and the needle sufficiently close to the wire so that the earth’s magnetic field may be ignored. Reversing the direction of the current reverses the orientation of the needle [Fig. 4.1(b)]. The deflection increases on increasing the current or bringing the needle closer to the wire. Iron filings sprinkled around the wire arrange themselves in concentric circles with the wire as the centre [Fig. 4.1(c)]. Oersted concluded that moving charges or currents produced a magnetic field in the surrounding space", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28184,70,4,'paragraph',NULL,'Following this, there was intense experimentation. In 1864, the laws obeyed by electricity and magnetism were unified and formulated by James Maxwell who then realised that light was electromagnetic waves. Radio waves were discovered by Hertz, and produced by J.C.Bose and G. Marconi by the end of the 19th century. A remarkable scientific and technological progress took place in the 20th century. This was due to our increased understanding of electromagnetism and the invention of devices for production, amplification, transmission and detection of electromagnetic waves.','[{"s": "Following this, there was intense experimentation. In 1864, the laws obeyed by electricity and magnetism were unified and formulated by James Maxwell who then realised that light was electromagnetic waves. Radio waves were discovered by Hertz, and produced by J.C.Bose and G. Marconi by the end of the 19th century. A remarkable scientific and technological progress took place in the 20th century. This was due to our increased understanding of electromagnetism and the invention of devices for production, amplification, transmission and detection of electromagnetic waves.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28185,70,5,'paragraph',NULL,'* See the box in Chapter 1, Page 3.','[{"s": "* See the box in Chapter 1, Page 3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28186,70,6,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image43.png'),
(28187,70,7,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.1 The magnetic field due to a straight long current-carrying wire. The wire is perpendicular to the plane of the paper. A ring of compass needles surrounds the wire. The orientation of the needles is shown when (a) the current emerges out of the plane of the paper, (b) the current moves into the plane of the paper. (c) The arrangement of iron filings around the wire. The darkened ends of the needle represent north poles. The effect of the earth’s magnetic field is neglected.','[{"s": "Figure 4.1 The magnetic field due to a straight long current-carrying wire. The wire is perpendicular to the plane of the paper. A ring of compass needles surrounds the wire. The orientation of the needles is shown when (a) the current emerges out of the plane of the paper, (b) the current moves into the plane of the paper. (c) The arrangement of iron filings around the wire. The darkened ends of the needle represent north poles. The effect of the earth’s magnetic field is neglected.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28188,70,8,'paragraph',NULL,'In this chapter, we will see how magnetic field exerts forces on moving charged particles, like electrons, protons, and current-carrying wires.','[{"s": "In this chapter, we will see how magnetic field exerts forces on moving charged particles, like electrons, protons, and current-carrying wires.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28190,70,10,'paragraph',NULL,'In this and subsequent Chapter on magnetism, we adopt the following convention: A current or a field (electric or magnetic) emerging out of the plane of the paper is depicted by a dot (). A current or a field going into the plane of the paper is depicted by a cross ( Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image30.png )*. Figures. 4.1(a) and 4.1(b) correspond to these two situations, respectively.','[{"s": "In this and subsequent Chapter on magnetism, we adopt the following convention: A current or a field (electric or magnetic) emerging out of the plane of the paper is depicted by a dot (). A current or a field going into the plane of the paper is depicted by a cross (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image30.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ")*. Figures. 4.1(a) and 4.1(b) correspond to these two situations, respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28191,70,11,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image121.png'),
(28192,70,12,'paragraph',NULL,'Hans Christian Oersted (1777–1851) Danish physicist and chemist, professor at Copenhagen. He observed that a compass needle suffers a deflection when placed near a wire carrying an electric current. This discovery gave the first empirical evidence of a connection between electric and magnetic phenomena.','[{"s": "Hans Christian Oersted (1777–1851)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Danish physicist and chemist, professor at Copenhagen. He observed that a compass needle suffers a deflection when placed near a wire carrying an electric current. This discovery gave the first empirical evidence of a connection between electric and magnetic phenomena.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28193,70,13,'heading',2,'4.2 Magnetic Force',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28194,70,14,'heading',4,'4.2.1 SOURCES AND FIELDS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28195,70,15,'paragraph',NULL,'Before we introduce the concept of a magnetic field B, we shall recapitulate what we have learnt in Chapter 1 about the electric field E. We have seen that the interaction between two charges can be considered in two stages. The charge Q, the source of the field, produces an electric field E, where','[{"s": "Before we introduce the concept of a magnetic field B, we shall recapitulate what we have learnt in Chapter 1 about the electric field E. We have seen that the interaction between two charges can be considered in two stages. The charge Q, the source of the field, produces an electric field E, where", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28196,70,16,'paragraph',NULL,'E=Q ˆr/(4πε 0 )r 2 (4.1)','[{"s": "E=Q ˆr/(4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28197,70,17,'paragraph',NULL,'where ˆr is unit vector along r, and the field E is a vector field. A charge q interacts with this field and experiences a force F given by','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ˆr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is unit vector along r, and the field E is a vector field. A charge q interacts with this field and experiences a force F given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28198,70,18,'paragraph',NULL,'* A dot appears like the tip of an arrow pointed at you, a cross is like the feathered tail of an arrow moving away from you.','[{"s": "* A dot appears like the tip of an arrow pointed at you, a cross is like the feathered tail of an arrow moving away from you.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28199,70,19,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image23.png'),
(28200,70,20,'paragraph',NULL,'Hendrik Antoon Lorentz (1853 – 1928) Dutch theoretical physicist, professor at Leiden. He investigated the relationship between electricity, magnetism, and mechanics. In order to explain the observed effect of magnetic fields on emitters of light (Zeeman effect), he postulated the existence of electric charges in the atom, for which he was awarded the Nobel Prize in 1902. He derived a set of transformation equations (known after him, as Lorentz transformation equations) by some tangled mathematical arguments, but he was not aware that these equations hinge on a new concept of space and time.','[{"s": "Hendrik Antoon Lorentz (1853 – 1928)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Dutch theoretical physicist, professor at Leiden. He investigated the relationship between electricity, magnetism, and mechanics. In order to explain the observed effect of magnetic fields on emitters of light (Zeeman effect), he postulated the existence of electric charges in the atom, for which he was awarded the Nobel Prize in 1902. He derived a set of transformation equations (known after him, as Lorentz transformation equations) by some tangled mathematical arguments, but he was not aware that these equations hinge on a new concept of space and time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28201,70,21,'paragraph',NULL,'F=qE=qQˆr/(4πε 0 )r 2 (4.2)','[{"s": "F=qE=qQˆr/(4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28202,70,22,'paragraph',NULL,'As pointed out in the Chapter 1, the field E is not just an artefact but has a physical role. It can convey energy and momentum and is not established instantaneously but takes finite time to propagate. The concept of a field was specially stressed by Faraday and was incorporated by Maxwell in his unification of electricity and magnetism. In addition to depending on each point in space, it can also vary with time, i.e., be a function of time. In our discussions in this chapter, we will assume that the fields do not change with time.','[{"s": "As pointed out in the Chapter 1, the field E is not just an artefact but has a physical role. It can convey energy and momentum and is not established instantaneously but takes finite time to propagate. The concept of a field was specially stressed by Faraday and was incorporated by Maxwell in his unification of electricity and magnetism. In addition to depending on each point in space, it can also vary with time, i.e., be a function of time. In our discussions in this chapter, we will assume that the fields do not change with time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28203,70,23,'paragraph',NULL,'The field at a particular point can be due to one or more charges. If there are more charges the fields add vectorially. You have already learnt in Chapter 1 that this is called the principle of superposition. Once the field is known, the force on a test charge is given by Eq. (4.2).','[{"s": "The field at a particular point can be due to one or more charges. If there are more charges the fields add vectorially. You have already learnt in Chapter 1 that this is called the principle of superposition. Once the field is known, the force on a test charge is given by Eq. (4.2).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28204,70,24,'paragraph',NULL,'Just as static charges produce an electric field, the currents or moving charges produce (in addition) a magnetic field, denoted by B (r), again a vector field. It has several basic properties identical to the electric field. It is defined at each point in space (and can in addition depend on time). Experimentally, it is found to obey the principle of superposition: the magnetic field of several sources is the vector addition of magnetic field of each individual source.','[{"s": "Just as static charges produce an electric field, the currents or moving charges produce (in addition) a magnetic field, denoted by B (r), again a vector field. It has several basic properties identical to the electric field. It is defined at each point in space (and can in addition depend on time). Experimentally, it is found to obey the principle of superposition: the magnetic field of several sources is the vector addition of magnetic field of each individual source.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28205,70,25,'heading',4,'4.2.2 MAGNETIC FIELD, LORENTZ FORCE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28207,70,27,'paragraph',NULL,'F = q [ E (r) + v × B (r)] ≡ F electric +F magnetic (4.3)','[{"s": "F = q [ E (r) + v × B (r)] ≡ F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "electric", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "magnetic", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28208,70,28,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) It depends on q, v and B (charge of the particle, the velocity and the magnetic field). Force on a negative charge is opposite to that on a positive charge.','[{"s": "(i) It depends on q, v and B (charge of the particle, the velocity and the magnetic field). Force on a negative charge is opposite to that on a positive charge.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28209,70,29,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) The magnetic force q [ v × B ] includes a vector product of velocity and magnetic field. The vector product makes the force due to magnetic field vanish (become zero) if velocity and magnetic field are parallel or anti-parallel. The force acts in a (sideways) direction perpendicular to both the velocity and the magnetic field. Its direction is given by the screw rule or right hand rule for vector (or cross) product as illustrated in Fig. 4.2.','[{"s": "(ii) The magnetic force q [ v × B ] includes a vector product of velocity and magnetic field. The vector product makes the force due to magnetic field vanish (become zero) if velocity and magnetic field are parallel or anti-parallel. The force acts in a (sideways) direction perpendicular to both the velocity and the magnetic field. Its direction is given by the screw rule or right hand rule for vector (or cross) product as illustrated in Fig. 4.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28210,70,30,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image27.png'),
(28211,70,31,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.2 The direction of the magnetic force acting on a charged particle. (a) The force on a positively charged particle with velocity v and making an angle θ with the magnetic field B is given by the right-hand rule. (b) A moving charged particle q is deflected in an opposite sense to –q in the presence of magnetic field.','[{"s": "Figure 4.2 The direction of the magnetic force acting on a charged particle. (a) The force on a positively charged particle with velocity v and making an angle θ with the magnetic field B is given by the right-hand rule. (b) A moving charged particle q is deflected in an opposite sense to –q in the presence of magnetic field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28212,70,32,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) The magnetic force is zero if charge is not moving (as then |v|= 0). Only a moving charge feels the magnetic force.','[{"s": "(iii) The magnetic force is zero if charge is not moving (as then |v|= 0). Only a moving charge feels the magnetic force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28214,70,34,'paragraph',NULL,'Dimensionally, we have [B] = [F/qv] and the unit of B are Newton second / (coulomb metre). This unit is called tesla (T) named after Nikola Tesla (1856 – 1943). Tesla is a rather large unit. A smaller unit (non-SI) called gauss (=10 –4 tesla) is also often used. The earth’s magnetic field is about 3.6 × 10 –5 T. Table','[{"s": "Dimensionally, we have [B] = [F/qv] and the unit of B are Newton second / (coulomb metre). This unit is called tesla (T) named after Nikola Tesla (1856 – 1943). Tesla is a rather large unit. A smaller unit (non-SI) called gauss (=10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tesla) is also often used. The earth’s magnetic field is about 3.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T. Table", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28215,70,35,'image',NULL,'16',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image60.jpg'),
(28216,70,36,'heading',4,'4.2.3 MAGNETIC FORCE ON A CURRENT-CARRYING CONDUCTOR',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28217,70,37,'paragraph',NULL,'We can extend the analysis for force due to magnetic field on a single moving charge to a straight rod carrying current. Consider a rod of a uniform cross-sectional area A and length l. We shall assume one kind of mobile carriers as in a conductor (here electrons). Let the number density of these mobile charge carriers in it be n. Then the total number of mobile charge carriers in it is nlA. For a steady current I in this conducting rod, we may assume that each mobile carrier has an average drift velocity vd (see Chapter 3). In the presence of an external magnetic field B, the force on these carriers is:','[{"s": "We can extend the analysis for force due to magnetic field on a single moving charge to a straight rod carrying current. Consider a rod of a uniform cross-sectional area A and length l. We shall assume one kind of mobile carriers as in a conductor (here electrons). Let the number density of these mobile charge carriers in it be n. Then the total number of mobile charge carriers in it is nlA. For a steady current I in this conducting rod, we may assume that each mobile carrier has an average drift velocity vd (see Chapter 3). In the presence of an external magnetic field B, the force on these carriers is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28218,70,38,'paragraph',NULL,'F = (nlA)q v d × B','[{"s": "F = (nlA)q v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28219,70,39,'paragraph',NULL,'where q is the value of the charge on a carrier. Now nq v d is the current density j and |(nq v d )|A is the current I (see Chapter 3 for the discussion of current and current density). Thus,','[{"s": "where q is the value of the charge on a carrier. Now nq v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the current density j and |(nq v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")|A is the current I (see Chapter 3 for the discussion of current and current density). Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28220,70,40,'paragraph',NULL,'F = [(nq v d )lA] × B = [ jAl ] ×B','[{"s": "F = [(nq v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")lA] × B = [ jAl ] ×B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28221,70,41,'paragraph',NULL,'= Il × B (4.4)','[{"s": "= Il × B (4.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28223,70,43,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (4.4) holds for a straight rod. In this equation, B is the external magnetic field. It is not the field produced by the current-carrying rod. If the wire has an arbitrary shape we can calculate the Lorentz force on it by considering it as a collection of linear strips dlj and summing','[{"s": "Equation (4.4) holds for a straight rod. In this equation, B is the external magnetic field. It is not the field produced by the current-carrying rod. If the wire has an arbitrary shape we can calculate the Lorentz force on it by considering it as a collection of linear strips dlj and summing", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28224,70,44,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image9.png'),
(28225,70,45,'paragraph',NULL,'This summation can be converted to an integral in most cases.','[{"s": "This summation can be converted to an integral in most cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28226,70,46,'heading',3,'ON PERMITTIVITY AND PERMEABILITY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28227,70,47,'paragraph',NULL,'In the universal law of gravitation, we say that any two point masses exert a force on each other which is proportional to the product of the masses m 1 , m 2 and inversely proportional to the square of the distancer between them. We write it as F = Gm 1 m 2 /r 2 where G is the universal constant of gravitation. Similarly, in Coulomb’s law of electrostatics we write the force between two point charges q 1 , q 2 , separated by a distance r as F = kq 2 q 1 /r 2 where k is a constant of proportionality. In SI units, k is taken as 1/4πε where ε is the permittivity of the medium. Also in magnetism, we get another constant, which in SI units, is taken as µ/4π where µ is the permeability of the medium.','[{"s": "In the universal law of gravitation, we say that any two point masses exert a force on each other which is proportional to the product of the masses m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and inversely proportional to the square of the distancer between them. We write it as F = Gm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where G is the universal constant of gravitation. Similarly, in Coulomb’s law of electrostatics we write the force between two point charges q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", separated by a distance r as F = kq", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where k is a constant of proportionality. In SI units, k is taken as 1/4πε where ε is the permittivity of the medium. Also in magnetism, we get another constant, which in SI units, is taken as µ/4π where µ is the permeability of the medium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28228,70,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Although G, ε and µ arise as proportionality constants, there is a difference between gravitational force and electromagnetic force. While the gravitational force does not depend on the intervening medium, the electromagnetic force depends on the medium between the two charges or magnets. Hence, while G is a universal constant, ε and µ depend on the medium. They have different values for different media. The product εµ turns out to be related to the speed v of electromagnetic radiation in the medium through εµ=1/ v 2 .','[{"s": "Although G, ε and µ arise as proportionality constants, there is a difference between gravitational force and electromagnetic force. While the gravitational force does not depend on the intervening medium, the electromagnetic force depends on the medium between the two charges or magnets. Hence, while G is a universal constant, ε and µ depend on the medium. They have different values for different media. The product εµ turns out to be related to the speed v of electromagnetic radiation in the medium through εµ=1/ v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28229,70,49,'paragraph',NULL,'Electric permittivity ε is a physical quantity that describes how an electric field affects and is affected by a medium. It is determined by the ability of a material to polarise in response to an applied field, and thereby to cancel, partially, the field inside the material. Similarly, magnetic permeability µ is the ability of a substance to acquire magnetisation in magnetic fields. It is a measure of the extent to which magnetic field can penetrate matter.','[{"s": "Electric permittivity ε is a physical quantity that describes how an electric field affects and is affected by a medium. It is determined by the ability of a material to polarise in response to an applied field, and thereby to cancel, partially, the field inside the material. Similarly, magnetic permeability µ is the ability of a substance to acquire magnetisation in magnetic fields. It is a measure of the extent to which magnetic field can penetrate matter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28230,70,50,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 4.1 A straight wire of mass 200 g and length 1.5 m carries a current of 2 A. It is suspended in mid-air by a uniform horizontal magnetic field B (Fig. 4.3). What is the magnitude of the magnetic field?','[{"s": "Example 4.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A straight wire of mass 200 g and length 1.5 m carries a current of 2 A. It is suspended in mid-air by a uniform horizontal magnetic field B (Fig. 4.3). What is the magnitude of the magnetic field?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28231,70,51,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image50.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(28232,70,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.3','[{"s": "Figure 4.3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28233,70,53,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28234,70,54,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eq. (4.4), we find that there is an upward force F, of magnitude IlB,. For mid-air suspension, this must be balanced by the force due to gravity:','[{"s": "From Eq. (4.4), we find that there is an upward force F, of magnitude IlB,. For mid-air suspension, this must be balanced by the force due to gravity:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28235,70,55,'paragraph',NULL,'m g = I lB','[{"s": "m g = I lB", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
