(28242,70,62,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution The velocity v of particle is along the x-axis, while B, the magnetic field is along the y-axis, so v × B is along the z-axis (screw rule or right-hand thumb rule). So, (a) for electron it will be along –z axis. (b) for a positive charge (proton) the force is along +z axis.','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The velocity v of particle is along the x-axis, while B, the magnetic field is along the y-axis, so v × B is along the z-axis (screw rule or right-hand thumb rule). So, (a) for electron it will be along –z axis. (b) for a positive charge (proton) the force is along +z axis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28243,70,63,'heading',3,'4.3 MOTION IN A MAGNETIC FIELD',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28244,70,64,'paragraph',NULL,'We will now consider, in greater detail, the motion of a charge moving in a magnetic field. We have learnt in Mechanics (see Class XI book, Chapter 6) that a force on a particle does work if the force has a component along (or opposed to) the direction of motion of the particle. In the case of motion of a charge in a magnetic field, the magnetic force is perpendicular to the velocity of the particle. So no work is done and no change in the magnitude of the velocity is produced (though the direction of momentum may be changed). [Notice that this is unlike the force due to an electric field, qE, which can have a component parallel (or antiparallel) to motion and thus can transfer energy in addition to momentum.]','[{"s": "We will now consider, in greater detail, the motion of a charge moving in a magnetic field. We have learnt in Mechanics (see Class XI book, Chapter 6) that a force on a particle does work if the force has a component along (or opposed to) the direction of motion of the particle. In the case of motion of a charge in a magnetic field, the magnetic force is perpendicular to the velocity of the particle. So no work is done and no change in the magnitude of the velocity is produced (though the direction of momentum may be changed). [Notice that this is unlike the force due to an electric field, qE, which can have a component parallel (or antiparallel) to motion and thus can transfer energy in addition to momentum.]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28245,70,65,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image106.png'),
(28246,70,66,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.5 Circular motion','[{"s": "Figure 4.5 Circular motion", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28248,70,68,'paragraph',NULL,'If velocity has a component along B, this component remains unchanged as the motion along the magnetic field will not be affected by the magnetic field. The motion in a plane perpendicular to B is as before a circular one, thereby producing a helical motion (Fig. 4.6).','[{"s": "If velocity has a component along B, this component remains unchanged as the motion along the magnetic field will not be affected by the magnetic field. The motion in a plane perpendicular to B is as before a circular one, thereby producing a helical motion (Fig. 4.6).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28249,70,69,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image90.png'),
(28250,70,70,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.6 Helical motion','[{"s": "Figure 4.6 Helical motion", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28252,70,72,'paragraph',NULL,'m v 2 /r = q v B, which gives','[{"s": "m v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r = q v B, which gives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28255,70,75,'paragraph',NULL,'ω = 2π ν = q B/ m [4.6(a)]','[{"s": "ω = 2π ν = q B/ m [4.6(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28256,70,76,'paragraph',NULL,'which is independent of the velocity or energy . Here ν is the frequency of rotation. The independence of ν from energy has important application in the design of a cyclotron (see Section 4.4.2).','[{"s": "which is independent of the velocity or energy . Here ν is the frequency of rotation. The independence of ν from energy has important application in the design of a cyclotron (see Section 4.4.2).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28257,70,77,'paragraph',NULL,'The time taken for one revolution is T= 2π/ω ≡ 1/ν. If there is a component of the velocity parallel to the magnetic field (denoted by v || ), it will make the particle move along the field and the path of the particle would be a helical one (Fig. 4.6). The distance moved along the magnetic field in one rotation is called pitch p. Using Eq. [4.6 (a)], we have','[{"s": "The time taken for one revolution is T= 2π/ω ≡ 1/ν. If there is a component of the velocity parallel to the magnetic field (denoted by v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "||", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), it will make the particle move along the field and the path of the particle would be a helical one (Fig. 4.6). The distance moved along the magnetic field in one rotation is called pitch p. Using Eq. [4.6 (a)], we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28258,70,78,'paragraph',NULL,'p = v || T = 2πm v || / q B [4.6(b)]','[{"s": "p = v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "||", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T = 2πm v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "||", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ q B [4.6(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28259,70,79,'paragraph',NULL,'The radius of the circular component of motion is called the radius of the helix.','[{"s": "The radius of the circular component of motion is called the radius of the helix.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28261,70,81,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution Using Eq. (4.5) we find','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Using Eq. (4.5) we find", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28262,70,82,'paragraph',NULL,'r = m v / (qB) = 9 ×10 -31 kg × 3 × 10 7 m s –1 / ( 1.6 × 10 –19 C × 6 × 10 –4 T)','[{"s": "r = m v / (qB) = 9 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg × 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ ( 1.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C × 6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28263,70,83,'paragraph',NULL,'= 26 × 10 –2 m = 26 cm','[{"s": "= 26 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m = 26 cm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28264,70,84,'paragraph',NULL,'ν = v / (2 πr) = 2×10 6 s –1 = 2×10 6 Hz =2 MHz.','[{"s": "ν = v / (2 πr) = 2×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hz =2 MHz.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28253,70,73,'paragraph',NULL,'r = m v / qB (4.5)','[{"s": "r = m v / qB (4.5)", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(28265,70,85,'paragraph',NULL,'E = (½ )mv 2 = (½ ) 9 × 10 -31 kg × 9 × 10 14 m 2 /s 2 = 40.5 ×10 –17 J','[{"s": "E = (½ )mv 2 = (½ ) 9 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg × 9 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 40.5 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28266,70,86,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image124.png 4×10 –16 J = 2.5 keV.','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image124.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "4×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J = 2.5 keV.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28267,70,87,'heading',3,'HELICAL MOTION OF CHARGED PARTICLES AND AURORA BOREALIS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28268,70,88,'paragraph',NULL,'In polar regions like Alaska and Northern Canada, a splendid display of colours is seen in the sky. The appearance of dancing green pink lights is fascinating, and equally puzzling. An explanation of this natural phenomenon is now found in physics, in terms of what we have studied here.','[{"s": "In polar regions like Alaska and Northern Canada, a splendid display of colours is seen in the sky. The appearance of dancing green pink lights is fascinating, and equally puzzling. An explanation of this natural phenomenon is now found in physics, in terms of what we have studied here.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28269,70,89,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a charged particle of mass m and charge q, entering a region of magnetic field B with an initial velocity v. Let this velocity have a component vp parallel to the magnetic field and a component vn normal to it. There is no force on a charged particle in the direction of the field. Hence the particle continues to travel with the velocity v p parallel to the field. The normal component v n of the particle results in a Lorentz force (v n × B) which is perpendicular to both vn and B. As seen in Section 4.3.1 the particle thus has a tendency to perform a circular motion in a plane perpendicular to the magnetic field. When this is coupled with the velocity parallel to the field, the resulting trajectory will be a helix along the magnetic field line, as shown in Figure (a) here. Even if the field line bends, the helically moving particle is trapped and guided to move around the field line. Since the Lorentz force is normal to the velocity of each point, the field does no work on the particle and the magnitude of velocity remains the same.','[{"s": "Consider a charged particle of mass m and charge q, entering a region of magnetic field B with an initial velocity v. Let this velocity have a component vp parallel to the magnetic field and a component vn normal to it. There is no force on a charged particle in the direction of the field. Hence the particle continues to travel with the velocity v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallel to the field. The normal component v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the particle results in a Lorentz force (v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× B) which is perpendicular to both vn and B. As seen in Section 4.3.1 the particle thus has a tendency to perform a circular motion in a plane perpendicular to the magnetic field. When this is coupled with the velocity parallel to the field, the resulting trajectory will be a helix along the magnetic field line, as shown in Figure (a) here. Even if the field line bends, the helically moving particle is trapped and guided to move around the field line. Since the Lorentz force is normal to the velocity of each point, the field does no work on the particle and the magnitude of velocity remains the same.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28270,70,90,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image104.png'),
(28271,70,91,'paragraph',NULL,'During a solar flare, a large number of electrons and protons are ejected from the sun. Some of them get trapped in the earth’s magnetic field and move in helical paths along the field lines. The field lines come closer to each other near the magnetic poles; see figure (b). Hence the density of charges increases near the poles. These particles collide with atoms and molecules of the atmosphere. Excited oxygen atoms emit green light and excited nitrogen atoms emits pink light. This phenomenon is called Aurora Borealis in physics.','[{"s": "During a solar flare, a large number of electrons and protons are ejected from the sun. Some of them get trapped in the earth’s magnetic field and move in helical paths along the field lines. The field lines come closer to each other near the magnetic poles; see figure (b). Hence the density of charges increases near the poles. These particles collide with atoms and molecules of the atmosphere. Excited oxygen atoms emit green light and excited nitrogen atoms emits pink light. This phenomenon is called Aurora Borealis in physics.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
