(28386,70,206,'paragraph',NULL,'∮B.dl=μ o I [4.17(a)]','[{"s": "∮B.dl=μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I [4.17(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28387,70,207,'paragraph',NULL,'where I is the total current through the surface. The integral is taken over the closed loop coinciding with the boundary C of the surface. The relation above involves a sign-convention, given by the right-hand rule. Let the fingers of the right-hand be curled in the sense the boundary is traversed in the loop integral ÜB.dl. Then the direction of the thumb gives the sense in which the current I is regarded as positive.','[{"s": "where I is the total current through the surface. The integral is taken over the closed loop coinciding with the boundary C of the surface. The relation above involves a sign-convention, given by the right-hand rule. Let the fingers of the right-hand be curled in the sense the boundary is traversed in the loop integral ÜB.dl. Then the direction of the thumb gives the sense in which the current I is regarded as positive.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28388,70,208,'paragraph',NULL,'For several applications, a much simplified version of Eq. [4.17(a)] proves sufficient. We shall assume that, in such cases, it is possible to choose the loop (called an amperian loop) such that at each point of the loop, either','[{"s": "For several applications, a much simplified version of Eq. [4.17(a)] proves sufficient. We shall assume that, in such cases, it is possible to choose the loop (called an amperian loop) such that at each point of the loop, either", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28389,70,209,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) B is tangential to the loop and is a non-zero constant B, or','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is tangential to the loop and is a non-zero constant B, or", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28390,70,210,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) B is normal to the loop, or','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is normal to the loop, or", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28391,70,211,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) B vanishes.','[{"s": "(iii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "vanishes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28392,70,212,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, let L be the length (part) of the loop for which B is tangential. Let I e be the current enclosed by the loop. Then, Eq. (4.17) reduces to,','[{"s": "Now, let L be the length (part) of the loop for which B is tangential. Let I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the current enclosed by the loop. Then, Eq. (4.17) reduces to,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28393,70,213,'paragraph',NULL,'BL =µ 0 I e [4.17(b)]','[{"s": "BL =µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[4.17(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28395,70,215,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image20.png'),
(28396,70,216,'paragraph',NULL,'Andre Ampere (1775 –1836) Andre Marie Ampere was a French physicist, mathematician and chemist who founded the science of electrodynamics. Ampere was a child prodigy who mastered advanced mathematics by the age of 12. Ampere grasped the significance of Oersted’s discovery. He carried out a large series of experiments to explore the relationship between current electricity and magnetism. These investigations culminated in 1827 with the publication of the ‘Mathematical Theory of Electrodynamic Pheno- mena Deduced Solely from Experiments’. He hypo- thesised that all magnetic phenomena are due to circulating electric currents. Ampere was humble and absent-minded. He once forgot an invitation to dine with the Emperor Napoleon. He died of pneumonia at the age of 61. His gravestone bears the epitaph: Tandem Felix (Happy at last).','[{"s": "Andre Ampere (1775 –1836)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Andre Marie Ampere was a French physicist, mathematician and chemist who founded the science of electrodynamics. Ampere was a child prodigy who mastered advanced mathematics by the age of 12. Ampere grasped the significance of Oersted’s discovery. He carried out a large series of experiments to explore the relationship between current electricity and magnetism. These investigations culminated in 1827 with the publication of the ‘Mathematical Theory of Electrodynamic Pheno- mena Deduced Solely from Experiments’. He hypo- thesised that all magnetic phenomena are due to circulating electric currents. Ampere was humble and absent-minded. He once forgot an invitation to dine with the Emperor Napoleon. He died of pneumonia at the age of 61. His gravestone bears the epitaph: Tandem Felix (Happy at last).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28397,70,217,'paragraph',NULL,'B × 2πr = µ 0 I,','[{"s": "B × 2πr = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28398,70,218,'paragraph',NULL,'B = µ 0 I/ (2πr) (4.18)','[{"s": "B = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I/ (2πr) (4.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28399,70,219,'paragraph',NULL,'The above result for the infinite wire is interesting from several points of view.','[{"s": "The above result for the infinite wire is interesting from several points of view.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28400,70,220,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) It implies that the field at every point on a circle of radius r, (with the wire along the axis), is same in magnitude. In other words, the magnetic field possesses what is called a cylindrical symmetry. The field that normally can depend on three coordinates depends only on one: r. Whenever there is symmetry, the solutions simplify.','[{"s": "(i) It implies that the field at every point on a circle of radius r, (with the wire along the axis), is same in magnitude. In other words, the magnetic field possesses what is called a cylindrical symmetry. The field that normally can depend on three coordinates depends only on one: r. Whenever there is symmetry, the solutions simplify.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28401,70,221,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) The field direction at any point on this circle is tangential to it. Thus, the lines of constant magnitude of magnetic field form concentric circles. Notice now, in Fig. 4.1(c), the iron filings form concentric circles. These lines called magnetic field lines form closed loops. This is unlike the electrostatic field lines which originate from positive charges and end at negative charges. The expression for the magnetic field of a straight wire provides a theoretical justification to Oersted’s experiments.','[{"s": "(ii) The field direction at any point on this circle is tangential to it. Thus, the lines of constant magnitude of magnetic field form concentric circles. Notice now, in Fig. 4.1(c), the iron filings form concentric circles. These lines called magnetic field lines form closed loops. This is unlike the electrostatic field lines which originate from positive charges and end at negative charges. The expression for the magnetic field of a straight wire provides a theoretical justification to Oersted’s experiments.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28402,70,222,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Another interesting point to note is that even though the wire is infinite, the field due to it at a non-zero distance is not infinite. It tends to blow up only when we come very close to the wire. The field is directly proportional to the current and inversely proportional to the distance from the (infinitely long) current source.','[{"s": "(iii) Another interesting point to note is that even though the wire is infinite, the field due to it at a non-zero distance is not infinite. It tends to blow up only when we come very close to the wire. The field is directly proportional to the current and inversely proportional to the distance from the (infinitely long) current source.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28403,70,223,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) There exists a simple rule to determine the direction of the magnetic field due to a long wire. This rule, called the right-hand rule*, is:','[{"s": "(iv) There exists a simple rule to determine the direction of the magnetic field due to a long wire. This rule, called the right-hand rule*, is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28404,70,224,'paragraph',NULL,'Grasp the wire in your right hand with your extended thumb pointing in the direction of the current. Your fingers will curl around in the direction of the magnetic field.','[{"s": "Grasp the wire in your right hand with your extended thumb pointing in the direction of the current. Your fingers will curl around in the direction of the magnetic field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28405,70,225,'paragraph',NULL,'Ampere’s circuital law is not new in content from Biot-Savart law. Both relate the magnetic field and the current, and both express the same physical consequences of a steady electrical current. Ampere’s law is to Biot-Savart law, what Gauss’s law is to Coulomb’s law. Both, Ampere’s and Gauss’s law relate a physical quantity on the periphery or boundary (magnetic or electric field) to another physical quantity, namely, the source, in the interior (current or charge). We also note that Ampere’s circuital law holds for steady currents which do not fluctuate with time. The following example will help us understand what is meant by the term enclosed current.','[{"s": "Ampere’s circuital law is not new in content from Biot-Savart law. Both relate the magnetic field and the current, and both express the same physical consequences of a steady electrical current. Ampere’s law is to Biot-Savart law, what Gauss’s law is to Coulomb’s law. Both, Ampere’s and Gauss’s law relate a physical quantity on the periphery or boundary (magnetic or electric field) to another physical quantity, namely, the source, in the interior (current or charge). We also note that Ampere’s circuital law holds for steady currents which do not fluctuate with time. The following example will help us understand what is meant by the term enclosed current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28407,70,227,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image117.png'),
(28408,70,228,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.15','[{"s": "Figure 4.15", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28409,70,229,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution (a) Consider the case r > a. The Amperian loop, labelled 2, is a circle concentric with the cross-section. For this loop,','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Consider the case r > a. The Amperian loop, labelled 2, is a circle concentric with the cross-section. For this loop,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28410,70,230,'paragraph',NULL,'L = 2 π r','[{"s": "L = 2 π r", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28411,70,231,'paragraph',NULL,'I e = Current enclosed by the loop = I','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= Current enclosed by the loop = I", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28412,70,232,'paragraph',NULL,'The result is the familiar expression for a long straight wire','[{"s": "The result is the familiar expression for a long straight wire", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28413,70,233,'paragraph',NULL,'B (2π r) =µ 0 I','[{"s": "B (2π r) =µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28414,70,234,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image72.png [4.19(a)]','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image72.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[4.19(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28415,70,235,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image115.png (r > a)','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image115.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(r > a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28416,70,236,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Consider the case r < a. The Amperian loop is a circle labelled 1. For this loop, taking the radius of the circle to be r,','[{"s": "(b) Consider the case r < a. The Amperian loop is a circle labelled 1. For this loop, taking the radius of the circle to be r,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28417,70,237,'paragraph',NULL,'L = 2 π r','[{"s": "L = 2 π r", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28418,70,238,'paragraph',NULL,'Now the current enclosed Ie is not I, but is less than this value. Since the current distribution is uniform, the current enclosed is,','[{"s": "Now the current enclosed Ie is not I, but is less than this value. Since the current distribution is uniform, the current enclosed is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28419,70,239,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image19.png'),
(28420,70,240,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image59.png'),
(28421,70,241,'paragraph',NULL,'Using Ampere’s law, Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image24.png','[{"s": "Using Ampere’s law,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image24.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28422,70,242,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image37.png [4.19(b)]','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image37.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[4.19(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28423,70,243,'paragraph',NULL,'B ∝ r (r < a)','[{"s": "B ∝ r (r < a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28424,70,244,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image69.png'),
(28425,70,245,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.16','[{"s": "Figure 4.16", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28426,70,246,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure (4.16) shows a plot of the magnitude of B with distance r from the centre of the wire. The direction of the field is tangential to the respective circular loop (1 or 2) and given by the right-hand rule described earlier in this section.','[{"s": "Figure (4.16) shows a plot of the magnitude of B with distance r from the centre of the wire. The direction of the field is tangential to the respective circular loop (1 or 2) and given by the right-hand rule described earlier in this section.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28427,70,247,'paragraph',NULL,'This example possesses the required symmetry so that Ampere’s law can be applied readily.','[{"s": "This example possesses the required symmetry so that Ampere’s law can be applied readily.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28428,70,248,'paragraph',NULL,'* Note that there are two distinct right-hand rules: One which gives the direction of B on the axis of current-loop and the other which gives direction of B for a straight conducting wire. Fingers and thumb play different roles in the two.','[{"s": "*", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Note that there are two distinct right-hand rules: One which gives the direction of B on the axis of current-loop and the other which gives direction of B for a straight conducting wire. Fingers and thumb play different roles in the two.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28429,70,249,'paragraph',NULL,'It should be noted that while Ampere’s circuital law holds for any loop, it may not always facilitate an evaluation of the magnetic field in every case. For example, for the case of the circular loop discussed in Section 4.6, it cannot be applied to extract the simple expression B = µ 0 I/2R [Eq. (4.16)] for the field at the centre of the loop. However, there exists a large number of situations of high symmetry where the law can be conveniently applied. We shall use it in the next section to calculate the magnetic field produced by two commonly used and very useful magnetic systems: the solenoid and the toroid.','[{"s": "It should be noted that while Ampere’s circuital law holds for any loop, it may not always facilitate an evaluation of the magnetic field in every case. For example, for the case of the circular loop discussed in Section 4.6, it cannot be applied to extract the simple expression B = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I/2R [Eq. (4.16)] for the field at the centre of the loop. However, there exists a large number of situations of high symmetry where the law can be conveniently applied. We shall use it in the next section to calculate the magnetic field produced by two commonly used and very useful magnetic systems: the solenoid and the toroid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28430,70,250,'heading',3,'4.8 THE SOLENOID AND THE TOROID',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28431,70,251,'paragraph',NULL,'The solenoid and the toroid are two pieces of equipment which generate magnetic fields. The television uses the solenoid to generate magnetic fields needed. The synchrotron uses a combination of both to generate the high magnetic fields required. In both, solenoid and toroid, we come across a situation of high symmetry where Ampere’s law can be conveniently applied.','[{"s": "The solenoid and the toroid are two pieces of equipment which generate magnetic fields. The television uses the solenoid to generate magnetic fields needed. The synchrotron uses a combination of both to generate the high magnetic fields required. In both, solenoid and toroid, we come across a situation of high symmetry where Ampere’s law can be conveniently applied.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28432,70,252,'heading',4,'4.8.1 THE SOLENOID',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28433,70,253,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall discuss a long solenoid. By long solenoid we mean that the solenoid’s length is large compared to its radius. It consists of a long wire wound in the form of a helix where the neighbouring turns are closely spaced. So each turn can be regarded as a circular loop. The net magnetic field is the vector sum of the fields due to all the turns. Enamelled wires are used for winding so that turns are insulated from each other.','[{"s": "We shall discuss a long solenoid. By long solenoid we mean that the solenoid’s length is large compared to its radius. It consists of a long wire wound in the form of a helix where the neighbouring turns are closely spaced. So each turn can be regarded as a circular loop. The net magnetic field is the vector sum of the fields due to all the turns. Enamelled wires are used for winding so that turns are insulated from each other.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28434,70,254,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image119.png'),
(28435,70,255,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.17 (a) The magnetic field due to a section of the solenoid which has been stretched out for clarity. Only the exterior semi-circular part is shown. Notice how the circular loops between neighbouring turns tend to cancel. (b) The magnetic field of a finite solenoid.','[{"s": "Figure 4.17 (a) The magnetic field due to a section of the solenoid which has been stretched out for clarity. Only the exterior semi-circular part is shown. Notice how the circular loops between neighbouring turns tend to cancel. (b) The magnetic field of a finite solenoid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28436,70,256,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.17 displays the magnetic field lines for a finite solenoid. We show a section of this solenoid in an enlarged manner in Fig. 4.17(a). Figure 4.17(b) shows the entire finite solenoid with its magnetic field. In Fig. 4.17(a), it is clear from the circular loops that the field between two neighbouring turns vanishes. In Fig. 4.17(b), we see that the field at the interior mid-point P is uniform, strong and along the axis of the solenoid. The field at the exterior mid-point Q is weak and moreover is along the axis of the solenoid with no perpendicular or normal component. As the solenoid is made longer it appears like a long cylindrical metal sheet. Figure 4.18 represents this idealised picture. The field outside the solenoid approaches zero. We shall assume that the field outside is zero. The field inside becomes everywhere parallel to the axis.','[{"s": "Figure 4.17 displays the magnetic field lines for a finite solenoid. We show a section of this solenoid in an enlarged manner in Fig. 4.17(a). Figure 4.17(b) shows the entire finite solenoid with its magnetic field. In Fig. 4.17(a), it is clear from the circular loops that the field between two neighbouring turns vanishes. In Fig. 4.17(b), we see that the field at the interior mid-point P is uniform, strong and along the axis of the solenoid. The field at the exterior mid-point Q is weak and moreover is along the axis of the solenoid with no perpendicular or normal component. As the solenoid is made longer it appears like a long cylindrical metal sheet. Figure 4.18 represents this idealised picture. The field outside the solenoid approaches zero. We shall assume that the field outside is zero. The field inside becomes everywhere parallel to the axis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28437,70,257,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image33.png'),
(28438,70,258,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.18 The magnetic field of a very long solenoid. We consider a rectangular Amperian loop abcd to determine the field.','[{"s": "Figure 4.18 The magnetic field of a very long solenoid. We consider a rectangular Amperian loop abcd to determine the field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28439,70,259,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a rectangular Amperian loop abcd. Along cd the field is zero as argued above. Along transverse sections bc and ad, the field component is zero. Thus, these two sections make no contribution. Let the field along ab be B. Thus, the relevant length of the Amperian loop is, L = h.','[{"s": "Consider a rectangular Amperian loop abcd. Along cd the field is zero as argued above. Along transverse sections bc and ad, the field component is zero. Thus, these two sections make no contribution. Let the field along ab be B. Thus, the relevant length of the Amperian loop is, L = h.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28441,70,261,'paragraph',NULL,'BL = µ 0 Ie, B h = µ 0 I (n h)','[{"s": "BL = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ie, B h = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I (n h)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28442,70,262,'paragraph',NULL,'B = µ 0 n I (4.20)','[{"s": "B = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n I (4.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28443,70,263,'paragraph',NULL,'The direction of the field is given by the right-hand rule. The solenoid is commonly used to obtain a uniform magnetic field. We shall see in the next chapter that a large field is possible by inserting a soft iron core inside the solenoid.','[{"s": "The direction of the field is given by the right-hand rule. The solenoid is commonly used to obtain a uniform magnetic field. We shall see in the next chapter that a large field is possible by inserting a soft iron core inside the solenoid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28444,70,264,'heading',4,'4.8.2 THE TOROID',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28445,70,265,'paragraph',NULL,'The toroid is a hollow circular ring on which a large number of turns of a wire are closely wound. It can be viewed as a solenoid which has been bent into a circular shape to close on itself. It is shown in Fig. 4.19(a) carrying a current I. We shall see that the magnetic field in the open space inside (point P) and exterior to the toroid (point Q) is zero. The field B inside the toroid is constant in magnitude for the ideal toroid of closely wound turns.','[{"s": "The toroid is a hollow circular ring on which a large number of turns of a wire are closely wound. It can be viewed as a solenoid which has been bent into a circular shape to close on itself. It is shown in Fig. 4.19(a) carrying a current I. We shall see that the magnetic field in the open space inside (point P) and exterior to the toroid (point Q) is zero. The field B inside the toroid is constant in magnitude for the ideal toroid of closely wound turns.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28446,70,266,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image58.png'),
(28447,70,267,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.19 (a) A toroid carrying a current I. (b) A sectional view of the toroid. The magnetic field can be obtained at an arbitrary distance r from the centre O of the toroid by Ampere’s circuital law. The dashed lines labelled 1, 2 and 3 are three circular Amperian loops.','[{"s": "Figure 4.19 (a) A toroid carrying a current I. (b) A sectional view of the toroid. The magnetic field can be obtained at an arbitrary distance r from the centre O of the toroid by Ampere’s circuital law. The dashed lines labelled 1, 2 and 3 are three circular Amperian loops.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28448,70,268,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.19(b) shows a sectional view of the toroid. The direction of the magnetic field inside is clockwise as per the right-hand thumb rule for circular loops. Three circular Amperian loops 1, 2 and 3 are shown by dashed lines. By symmetry, the magnetic field should be tangential to each of them and constant in magnitude for a given loop. The circular areas bounded by loops 2 and 3 both cut the toroid: so that each turn of current carrying wire is cut once by the loop 2 and twice by the loop 3.','[{"s": "Figure 4.19(b) shows a sectional view of the toroid. The direction of the magnetic field inside is clockwise as per the right-hand thumb rule for circular loops. Three circular Amperian loops 1, 2 and 3 are shown by dashed lines. By symmetry, the magnetic field should be tangential to each of them and constant in magnitude for a given loop. The circular areas bounded by loops 2 and 3 both cut the toroid: so that each turn of current carrying wire is cut once by the loop 2 and twice by the loop 3.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(28449,70,269,'paragraph',NULL,'Let the magnetic field along loop 1 be B 1 in magnitude. Then in Ampere’s circuital law [Eq. 4.17(a)], L = 2π r 1 .','[{"s": "Let the magnetic field along loop 1 be B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in magnitude. Then in Ampere’s circuital law [Eq. 4.17(a)], L = 2π r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28450,70,270,'paragraph',NULL,'However, the loop encloses no current, so I e = 0. Thus,','[{"s": "However, the loop encloses no current, so I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0. Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28451,70,271,'paragraph',NULL,'B 1 (2 π r 1 ) = µ 0 (0), B 1 = 0','[{"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2 π r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(0), B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28452,70,272,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the magnetic field at any point P in the open space inside the toroid is zero.','[{"s": "Thus, the magnetic field at any point P in the open space inside the toroid is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28453,70,273,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall now show that magnetic field at Q is likewise zero. Let the magnetic field along loop 3 be B 3 . Once again from Ampere’s law L = 2 π r 3 . However, from the sectional cut, we see that the current coming out of the plane of the paper is cancelled exactly by the current going into it. Thus, I e = 0, and B 3 = 0. Let the magnetic field inside the solenoid be B. We shall now consider the magnetic field at S. Once again we employ Ampere’s law in the form of Eq. [4.17 (a)]. We find, L = 2π r.','[{"s": "We shall now show that magnetic field at Q is likewise zero. Let the magnetic field along loop 3 be B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Once again from Ampere’s law L = 2 π r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". However, from the sectional cut, we see that the current coming out of the plane of the paper is cancelled exactly by the current going into it. Thus, I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0, and B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0. Let the magnetic field inside the solenoid be B. We shall now consider the magnetic field at S. Once again we employ Ampere’s law in the form of Eq. [4.17 (a)]. We find, L = 2π r.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28454,70,274,'paragraph',NULL,'The current enclosed Ie is (for N turns of toroidal coil) N I.','[{"s": "The current enclosed Ie is (for N turns of toroidal coil) N I.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28455,70,275,'paragraph',NULL,'B (2πr) = µ 0 NI','[{"s": "B (2πr) = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "NI", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28456,70,276,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image105.png (4.21)','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image105.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(4.21)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28457,70,277,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall now compare the two results: for a toroid and solenoid. We re-express Eq. (4.21) to make the comparison easier with the solenoid result given in Eq. (4.20). Let r be the average radius of the toroid and n be the number of turns per unit length. Then','[{"s": "We shall now compare the two results: for a toroid and solenoid. We re-express Eq. (4.21) to make the comparison easier with the solenoid result given in Eq. (4.20). Let r be the average radius of the toroid and n be the number of turns per unit length. Then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28458,70,278,'paragraph',NULL,'N = 2πr n = (average) perimeter of the toroid','[{"s": "N = 2πr n = (average) perimeter of the toroid", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28459,70,279,'paragraph',NULL,'× number of turns per unit length','[{"s": "× number of turns per unit length", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28460,70,280,'paragraph',NULL,'and thus,','[{"s": "and thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28461,70,281,'paragraph',NULL,'B = µ 0 n I, (4.22)','[{"s": "B = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n I, (4.22)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28462,70,282,'paragraph',NULL,'i.e., the result for the solenoid!','[{"s": "i.e., the result for the solenoid!", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28463,70,283,'paragraph',NULL,'In an ideal toroid the coils are circular. In reality the turns of the toroidal coil form a helix and there is always a small magnetic field external to the toroid.','[{"s": "In an ideal toroid the coils are circular. In reality the turns of the toroidal coil form a helix and there is always a small magnetic field external to the toroid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28464,70,284,'heading',3,'MAGNETIC CONFINEMENT',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28465,70,285,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen in Section 4.3 (see also the box on helical motion of charged particles earlier in this chapter) that orbits of charged particles are helical. If the magnetic field is non-uniform, but does not change much during one circular orbit, then the radius of the helix will decrease as it enters stronger magnetic field and the radius will increase when it enters weaker magnetic fields. We consider two solenoids at a distance from each other, enclosed in an evacuated container (see figure below where we have not shown the container). Charged particles moving in the region between the two solenoids will start with a small radius. The radius will increase as field decreases and the radius will decrease again as field due to the second solenoid takes over. The solenoids act as a mirror or reflector. [See the direction of F as the particle approaches coil 2 in the figure. It has a horizontal component against the forward motion.] This makes the particles turn back when they approach the solenoid. Such an arrangement will act like magnetic bottle or magnetic container. The particles will never touch the sides of the container. Such magnetic bottles are of great use in confining the high energy plasma in fusion experiments. The plasma will destroy any other form of material container because of its high temperature. Another useful container is a toroid. Toroids are expected to play a key role in thetokamak, an equipment for plasma confinement in fusion power reactors. There is an international collaboration called the International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor (ITER), being set up in France, for achieving controlled fusion, of which India is a collaborating nation. For details of ITER collaboration and the project, you may visit http://www.iter.org.','[{"s": "We have seen in Section 4.3 (see also the box on helical motion of charged particles earlier in this chapter) that orbits of charged particles are helical. If the magnetic field is non-uniform, but does not change much during one circular orbit, then the radius of the helix will decrease as it enters stronger magnetic field and the radius will increase when it enters weaker magnetic fields. We consider two solenoids at a distance from each other, enclosed in an evacuated container (see figure below where we have not shown the container). Charged particles moving in the region between the two solenoids will start with a small radius. The radius will increase as field decreases and the radius will decrease again as field due to the second solenoid takes over. The solenoids act as a mirror or reflector. [See the direction of F as the particle approaches coil 2 in the figure. It has a horizontal component against the forward motion.] This makes the particles turn back when they approach the solenoid. Such an arrangement will act like magnetic bottle or magnetic container. The particles will never touch the sides of the container. Such magnetic bottles are of great use in confining the high energy plasma in fusion experiments. The plasma will destroy any other form of material container because of its high temperature. Another useful container is a toroid. Toroids are expected to play a key role in thetokamak, an equipment for plasma confinement in fusion power reactors. There is an international collaboration called the International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor (ITER), being set up in France, for achieving controlled fusion, of which India is a collaborating nation. For details of ITER collaboration and the project, you may visit http://www.iter.org.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28466,70,286,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image63.png'),
(28467,70,287,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 4.9 A solenoid of length 0.5 m has a radius of 1 cm and is made up of 500 turns. It carries a current of 5 A. What is the magnitude of the magnetic field inside the solenoid?','[{"s": "Example 4.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A solenoid of length 0.5 m has a radius of 1 cm and is made up of 500 turns. It carries a current of 5 A. What is the magnitude of the magnetic field inside the solenoid?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28468,70,288,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution The number of turns per unit length is,','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The number of turns per unit length is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28469,70,289,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image53.png turns/m','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image53.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "turns/m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28470,70,290,'paragraph',NULL,'The length l = 0.5 m and radius r = 0.01 m. Thus, l/a = 50 i.e., l >> a.','[{"s": "The length l = 0.5 m and radius r = 0.01 m. Thus, l/a = 50 i.e., l >> a.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28471,70,291,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence, we can use the long solenoid formula, namely, Eq. (4.20)','[{"s": "Hence, we can use the long solenoid formula, namely, Eq. (4.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28472,70,292,'paragraph',NULL,'B = µ 0 n I','[{"s": "B = µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n I", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28473,70,293,'paragraph',NULL,'= 4π × 10 –7 × 10 3 × 5','[{"s": "= 4π × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× 5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28474,70,294,'paragraph',NULL,'= 6.28 × 10 –3 T','[{"s": "= 6.28 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28475,70,295,'heading',3,'4.9 FORCE BETWEEN TWO PARALLEL CURRENTS,THE AMPERE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28476,70,296,'paragraph',NULL,'We have learnt that there exists a magnetic field due to a conductor carrying a current which obeys the Biot-Savart law. Further, we have learnt that an external magnetic field will exert a force on a current-carrying conductor. This follows from the Lorentz force formula. Thus, it is logical to expect that two current-carrying conductors placed near each other will exert (magnetic) forces on each other. In the period 1820-25, Ampere studied the nature of this magnetic force and its dependence on the magnitude of the current, on the shape and size of the conductors, as well as, the distances between the conductors. In this section, we shall take the simple example of two parallel current- carrying conductors, which will perhaps help us to appreciate Ampere’s painstaking work.','[{"s": "We have learnt that there exists a magnetic field due to a conductor carrying a current which obeys the Biot-Savart law. Further, we have learnt that an external magnetic field will exert a force on a current-carrying conductor. This follows from the Lorentz force formula. Thus, it is logical to expect that two current-carrying conductors placed near each other will exert (magnetic) forces on each other. In the period 1820-25, Ampere studied the nature of this magnetic force and its dependence on the magnitude of the current, on the shape and size of the conductors, as well as, the distances between the conductors. In this section, we shall take the simple example of two parallel current- carrying conductors, which will perhaps help us to appreciate Ampere’s painstaking work.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35027,80,460,'paragraph',NULL,'18. The Doppler effect is a change in the observed frequency of a wave when the source (S) or the observer (O) or both move(s) relative to the medium. For sound the observed frequency ν is given in terms of the source frequency νo by','[{"s": "18.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The Doppler effect is a change in the observed frequency of a wave when the source (S) or the observer (O) or both move(s) relative to the medium.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For sound the observed frequency ν is given in terms of the source frequency νo by", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041113"]',NULL),
(28477,70,297,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.20 shows two long parallel conductors a and b separated by a distance d and carrying (parallel) currents I a and I b , respectively. The conductor ‘a’ produces, the same magnetic field Ba at all points along the conductor ‘b’. The right-hand rule tells us that the direction of this field is downwards (when the conductors are placed horizontally). Its magnitude is given by Eq. [4.19(a)] or from Ampere’s circuital law,','[{"s": "Figure 4.20 shows two long parallel conductors a and b separated by a distance d and carrying (parallel) currents I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively. The conductor ‘a’ produces, the same magnetic field Ba at all points along the conductor ‘b’. The right-hand rule tells us that the direction of this field is downwards (when the conductors are placed horizontally). Its magnitude is given by Eq. [4.19(a)] or from Ampere’s circuital law,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28478,70,298,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image56.png'),
(28479,70,299,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.20 Two long straight parallel conductors carrying steady currents Ia and Ib and separated by a distance d. Ba is the magnetic field set up by conductor ‘a’ at conductor ‘b’.','[{"s": "Figure 4.20 Two long straight parallel conductors carrying steady currents Ia and Ib and separated by a distance d. Ba is the magnetic field set up by conductor ‘a’ at conductor ‘b’.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28480,70,300,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image39.png'),
(28482,70,302,'paragraph',NULL,'F ba = I b L B a','[{"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ba", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28485,70,305,'paragraph',NULL,'F ba = –F ab (4.24)','[{"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ba", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ab", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.24)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28486,70,306,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that this is consistent with Newton’s third Law. Thus, at least for parallel conductors and steady currents, we have shown that the Biot-Savart law and the Lorentz force yield results in accordance with Newton’s third Law*.','[{"s": "Note that this is consistent with Newton’s third Law. Thus, at least for parallel conductors and steady currents, we have shown that the Biot-Savart law and the Lorentz force yield results in accordance with Newton’s third Law*.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28487,70,307,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen from above that currents flowing in the same direction attract each other. One can show that oppositely directed currents repel each other. Thus,Parallel currents attract, and antiparallel currents repel.','[{"s": "We have seen from above that currents flowing in the same direction attract each other. One can show that oppositely directed currents repel each other. Thus,Parallel currents attract, and antiparallel currents repel.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28488,70,308,'paragraph',NULL,'This rule is the opposite of what we find in electrostatics. Like (same sign) charges repel each other, but like (parallel) currents attract each other.','[{"s": "This rule is the opposite of what we find in electrostatics. Like (same sign) charges repel each other, but like (parallel) currents attract each other.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28489,70,309,'paragraph',NULL,'Let fba represent the magnitude of the force Fba per unit length. Then, from Eq. (4.23),','[{"s": "Let fba represent the magnitude of the force Fba per unit length. Then, from Eq. (4.23),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28490,70,310,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image25.png (4.25)','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image25.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(4.25)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28491,70,311,'paragraph',NULL,'The above expression is used to define the ampere (A), which is one of the seven SI base units.','[{"s": "The above expression is used to define the ampere (A), which is one of the seven SI base units.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28492,70,312,'paragraph',NULL,'The ampere is the value of that steady current which, when maintained in each of the two very long, straight, parallel conductors of negligible cross-section, and placed one metre apart in vacuum, would produce on each of these conductors a force equal to 2 × 10 –7 newtons per metre of length.','[{"s": "The ampere is the value of that steady current which, when maintained in each of the two very long, straight, parallel conductors of negligible cross-section, and placed one metre apart in vacuum, would produce on each of these conductors a force equal to 2 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "newtons per metre of length.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28483,70,303,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image65.png (4.23)','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image65.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(4.23)", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(28493,70,313,'paragraph',NULL,'This definition of the ampere was adopted in 1946. It is a theoretical definition. In practice, one must eliminate the effect of the earth’s magnetic field and substitute very long wires by multiturn coils of appropriate geometries. An instrument called the current balance is used to measure this mechanical force.','[{"s": "This definition of the ampere was adopted in 1946. It is a theoretical definition. In practice, one must eliminate the effect of the earth’s magnetic field and substitute very long wires by multiturn coils of appropriate geometries. An instrument called the current balance is used to measure this mechanical force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28494,70,314,'paragraph',NULL,'The SI unit of charge, namely, the coulomb, can now be defined in terms of the ampere.','[{"s": "The SI unit of charge, namely, the coulomb, can now be defined in terms of the ampere.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28495,70,315,'paragraph',NULL,'When a steady current of 1A is set up in a conductor, the quantity of charge that flows through its cross-section in 1s is one coulomb (1C).','[{"s": "When a steady current of 1A is set up in a conductor, the quantity of charge that flows through its cross-section in 1s is one coulomb (1C).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28496,70,316,'paragraph',NULL,'* It turns out that when we have time-dependent currents and/or charges in motion, Newton’s third law may not hold for forces between charges and/or conductors. An essential consequence of the Newton’s third law in mechanics is conservation of momentum of an isolated system. This, however, holds even for the case of time-dependent situations with electromagnetic fields, provided the momentum carried by fields is also taken into account.','[{"s": "* It turns out that when we have time-dependent currents and/or charges in motion, Newton’s third law may not hold for forces between charges and/or conductors. An essential consequence of the Newton’s third law in mechanics is conservation of momentum of an isolated system. This, however, holds even for the case of time-dependent situations with electromagnetic fields, provided the momentum carried by fields is also taken into account.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28497,70,317,'heading',3,'ROGET’S SPIRAL FOR ATTRACTION BETWEEN PARALLEL CURRENTS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28498,70,318,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnetic effects are generally smaller than electric effects. As a consequence, the force between currents is rather small, because of the smallness of the factor µ. Hence it is difficult to demonstrate attraction or repulsion between currents. Thus, for 5 A current in each wire at a separation of 1cm, the force per metre would be 5 × 10 –4 N, which is about 50 mg weight. It would be like pulling a wire by a string going over a pulley to which a 50 mg weight is attached. The displacement of the wire would be quite unnoticeable.','[{"s": "Magnetic effects are generally smaller than electric effects. As a consequence, the force between currents is rather small, because of the smallness of the factor µ. Hence it is difficult to demonstrate attraction or repulsion between currents. Thus, for 5 A current in each wire at a separation of 1cm, the force per metre would be 5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N, which is about 50 mg weight. It would be like pulling a wire by a string going over a pulley to which a 50 mg weight is attached. The displacement of the wire would be quite unnoticeable.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28499,70,319,'paragraph',NULL,'With the use of a soft spring, we can increase the effective length of the parallel current and by using mercury, we can make the displacement of even a few mm observable very dramatically. You will also need a constant-current supply giving a constant current of about 5 A.','[{"s": "With the use of a soft spring, we can increase the effective length of the parallel current and by using mercury, we can make the displacement of even a few mm observable very dramatically. You will also need a constant-current supply giving a constant current of about 5 A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28500,70,320,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image116.png'),
(28501,70,321,'paragraph',NULL,'Take a soft spring whose natural period of oscillations is about 0.5 – 1s. Hang it vertically and attach a pointed tip to its lower end, as shown in the figure here. Take some mercury in a dish and adjust the spring such that the tip is just above the mercury surface. Take the DC current source, connect one of its terminals to the upper end of the spring, and dip the other terminal in mercury. If the tip of the spring touches mercury, the circuit is completed through mercury.','[{"s": "Take a soft spring whose natural period of oscillations is about 0.5 – 1s. Hang it vertically and attach a pointed tip to its lower end, as shown in the figure here. Take some mercury in a dish and adjust the spring such that the tip is just above the mercury surface. Take the DC current source, connect one of its terminals to the upper end of the spring, and dip the other terminal in mercury. If the tip of the spring touches mercury, the circuit is completed through mercury.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28502,70,322,'paragraph',NULL,'Let the DC source be put off to begin with. Let the tip be adjusted so that it just touches the mercury surface. Switch on the constant current supply, and watch the fascinating outcome. The spring shrinks with a jerk, the tip comes out of mercury (just by a mm or so), the circuit is broken, the current stops, the spring relaxes and tries to come back to its original position, the tip again touches mercury establishing a current in the circuit, and the cycle continues with tick, tick, tick,... In the beginning, you may require some small adjustments to get a good effect.','[{"s": "Let the DC source be put off to begin with. Let the tip be adjusted so that it just touches the mercury surface. Switch on the constant current supply, and watch the fascinating outcome. The spring shrinks with a jerk, the tip comes out of mercury (just by a mm or so), the circuit is broken, the current stops, the spring relaxes and tries to come back to its original position, the tip again touches mercury establishing a current in the circuit, and the cycle continues with tick, tick, tick,... In the beginning, you may require some small adjustments to get a good effect.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28503,70,323,'paragraph',NULL,'Keep your face away from mercury vapour as it is poisonous. Do not inhale mercury vapour for long.','[{"s": "Keep your face away from mercury vapour as it is poisonous. Do not inhale mercury vapour for long.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2691,24,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 13.8 The Calvin cycle proceeds in three stages : (1) carboxylation, during which CO2 combines with ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate; (2) reduction, during which carbohydrate is formed at the expense of the photochemically made ATP and NADPH; and (3) regeneration during which the CO2 acceptor ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate is formed again so that the cycle continues','[{"s": "Figure 13.8", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The Calvin cycle proceeds in three stages : (1) carboxylation, during which CO2 combines with ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate; (2) reduction, during which carbohydrate is formed at the expense of the photochemically made ATP and NADPH; and (3) regeneration during which the CO2 acceptor ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate is formed again so that the cycle continues", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(28504,70,324,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 4.10 The horizontal component of the earth’s magnetic field at a certain place is 3.0 ×10 –5 T and the direction of the field is from the geographic south to the geographic north. A very long straight conductor is carrying a steady current of 1A. What is the force per unit length on it when it is placed on a horizontal table and the direction of the current is (a) east to west; (b) south to north?','[{"s": "Example 4.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The horizontal component of the earth’s magnetic field at a certain place is 3.0 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T and the direction of the field is from the geographic south to the geographic north. A very long straight conductor is carrying a steady current of 1A. What is the force per unit length on it when it is placed on a horizontal table and the direction of the current is (a) east to west; (b) south to north?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28505,70,325,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution F = Il × B','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F = Il × B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28506,70,326,'paragraph',NULL,'F = IlB sinθ','[{"s": "F = IlB sinθ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28507,70,327,'paragraph',NULL,'The force per unit length is','[{"s": "The force per unit length is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28508,70,328,'paragraph',NULL,'f = F/l = I B sinθ','[{"s": "f = F/l = I B sinθ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28509,70,329,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) When the current is flowing from east to west,','[{"s": "(a) When the current is flowing from east to west,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28510,70,330,'paragraph',NULL,'θ = 90°','[{"s": "θ = 90°", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28511,70,331,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence,','[{"s": "Hence,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28512,70,332,'paragraph',NULL,'f = I B','[{"s": "f = I B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28513,70,333,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1 × 3 × 10 –5 = 3 × 10 –5 N m–1','[{"s": "= 1 × 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N m–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28514,70,334,'paragraph',NULL,'This is larger than the value 2×10 –7 Nm–1 quoted in the definition of the ampere. Hence it is important to eliminate the effect of the earth’s magnetic field and other stray fields while standardising the ampere.','[{"s": "This is larger than the value 2×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nm–1 quoted in the definition of the ampere. Hence it is important to eliminate the effect of the earth’s magnetic field and other stray fields while standardising the ampere.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28515,70,335,'paragraph',NULL,'The direction of the force is downwards. This direction may be obtained by the directional property of cross product of vectors.','[{"s": "The direction of the force is downwards. This direction may be obtained by the directional property of cross product of vectors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28516,70,336,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) When the current is flowing from south to north,','[{"s": "(b) When the current is flowing from south to north,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28517,70,337,'paragraph',NULL,'θ = 0','[{"s": "θ = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28518,70,338,'paragraph',NULL,'f = 0','[{"s": "f = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
