(28520,70,340,'heading',3,'4.10 TORQUE ON CURRENT LOOP, MAGNETIC DIPOLE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28521,70,341,'heading',4,'4.10.1 TORQUE ON A RECTANGULAR CURRENT LOOP IN A UNIFORM MAGNETIC FIELD',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28522,70,342,'paragraph',NULL,'We now show that a rectangular loop carrying a steady current I and placed in a uniform magnetic field experiences a torque. It does not experience a net force. This behaviour is analogous to that of electric dipole in a uniform electric field (Section 1.12).','[{"s": "We now show that a rectangular loop carrying a steady current I and placed in a uniform magnetic field experiences a torque. It does not experience a net force. This behaviour is analogous to that of electric dipole in a uniform electric field (Section 1.12).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28523,70,343,'paragraph',NULL,'We first consider the simple case when the rectangular loop is placed such that the uniform magnetic field B is in the plane of the loop. This is illustrated in Fig. 4.21(a).','[{"s": "We first consider the simple case when the rectangular loop is placed such that the uniform magnetic field B is in the plane of the loop. This is illustrated in Fig. 4.21(a).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28524,70,344,'paragraph',NULL,'The field exerts no force on the two arms AD and BC of the loop. It is perpendicular to the arm AB of the loop and exerts a force F 1 on it which is directed into the plane of the loop. Its magnitude is,','[{"s": "The field exerts no force on the two arms AD and BC of the loop. It is perpendicular to the arm AB of the loop and exerts a force F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on it which is directed into the plane of the loop. Its magnitude is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28525,70,345,'paragraph',NULL,'F 1 = I b B','[{"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I b B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28526,70,346,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, it exerts a force F2 on the arm CD and F2 is directed out of the plane of the paper.','[{"s": "Similarly, it exerts a force F2 on the arm CD and F2 is directed out of the plane of the paper.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28527,70,347,'paragraph',NULL,'F 2 = I b B = F 1','[{"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I b B = F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28529,70,349,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image44.png'),
(28530,70,350,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image1.png'),
(28531,70,351,'paragraph',NULL,'= I A B (4.26)','[{"s": "= I A B (4.26)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28532,70,352,'paragraph',NULL,'where A = ab is the area of the rectangle.','[{"s": "where A = ab is the area of the rectangle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28533,70,353,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image95.png'),
(28534,70,354,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.21 (a) A rectangular current-carrying coil in uniform magnetic field. The magnetic moment mpoints downwards. The torque τ is along the axis and tends to rotate the coil anticlockwise. (b) The couple acting on the coil.','[{"s": "Figure 4.21 (a) A rectangular current-carrying coil in uniform magnetic field. The magnetic moment mpoints downwards. The torque τ is along the axis and tends to rotate the coil anticlockwise. (b) The couple acting on the coil.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28535,70,355,'paragraph',NULL,'We next consider the case when the plane of the loop, is not along the magnetic field, but makes an angle with it. We take the angle between the field and the normal to the coil to be angle θ (The previous case corresponds to θ = π/2). Figure 4.22 illustrates this general case.','[{"s": "We next consider the case when the plane of the loop, is not along the magnetic field, but makes an angle with it. We take the angle between the field and the normal to the coil to be angle θ (The previous case corresponds to θ = π/2). Figure 4.22 illustrates this general case.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28536,70,356,'paragraph',NULL,'The forces on the arms BC and DA are equal, opposite, and act along the axis of the coil, which connects the centres of mass of BC and DA. Being collinear along the axis they cancel each other, resulting in no net force or torque. The forces on arms AB and CD are F 1 and F 2 . They too are equal and opposite, with magnitude,','[{"s": "The forces on the arms BC and DA are equal, opposite, and act along the axis of the coil, which connects the centres of mass of BC and DA. Being collinear along the axis they cancel each other, resulting in no net force or torque. The forces on arms AB and CD are F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". They too are equal and opposite, with magnitude,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28537,70,357,'paragraph',NULL,'F 1 = F 2 = I b B','[{"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I b B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28538,70,358,'paragraph',NULL,'But they are not collinear! This results in a couple as before. The torque is, however, less than the earlier case when plane of loop was along the magnetic field. This is because the perpendicular distance between the forces of the couple has decreased. Figure 4.22(b) is a view of the arrangement from the AD end and it illustrates these two forces constituting a couple. The magnitude of the torque on the loop is,','[{"s": "But they are not collinear! This results in a couple as before. The torque is, however, less than the earlier case when plane of loop was along the magnetic field. This is because the perpendicular distance between the forces of the couple has decreased. Figure 4.22(b) is a view of the arrangement from the AD end and it illustrates these two forces constituting a couple. The magnitude of the torque on the loop is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28539,70,359,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image36.png'),
(28540,70,360,'paragraph',NULL,'= I ab B sin θ','[{"s": "= I ab B sin θ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28541,70,361,'paragraph',NULL,'= I A B sin θ (4.27)','[{"s": "= I A B sin θ (4.27)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28542,70,362,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image40.png'),
(28543,70,363,'paragraph',NULL,'FIGURE 4.22 (a) The area vector of the loop ABCD makes an arbitrary angle θ with the magnetic field. (b) Top view of the loop. The forces F 1 and F 2 acting on the arms AB and CD are indicated.','[{"s": "FIGURE 4.22 (a) The area vector of the loop ABCD makes an arbitrary angle θ with the magnetic field. (b) Top view of the loop. The forces F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "acting on the arms AB and CD are indicated.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28546,70,366,'paragraph',NULL,'τ=m×B (4.29)','[{"s": "τ=m×B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.29)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28547,70,367,'paragraph',NULL,'This is analogous to the electrostatic case (Electric dipole of dipole moment pe in an electric field E).','[{"s": "This is analogous to the electrostatic case (Electric dipole of dipole moment pe in an electric field E).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28548,70,368,'paragraph',NULL,'τ=p×E','[{"s": "τ=p×E", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28549,70,369,'paragraph',NULL,'As is clear from Eq. (4.28), the dimensions of the magnetic moment are [A][L 2 ] and its unit is Am 2 .','[{"s": "As is clear from Eq. (4.28), the dimensions of the magnetic moment are [A][L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] and its unit is Am", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28545,70,365,'paragraph',NULL,'where the direction of the area vector A is given by the right-hand thumb rule and is directed into the plane of the paper in Fig. 4.21. Then as the angle between m and B is θ , Eqs. (4.26) and (4.27) can be expressed by one expression','[{"s": "where the direction of the area vector A is given by the right-hand thumb rule and is directed into the plane of the paper in Fig. 4.21.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Then as the angle between m and B is θ , Eqs. (4.26) and (4.27) can be expressed by one expression", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(28550,70,370,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eq. (4.29), we see that the torque τ vanishes when m is either parallel or antiparallel to the magnetic field B. This indicates a state of equilibrium as there is no torque on the coil (this also applies to any object with a magnetic moment m). When m and B are parallel the equilibrium is a stable one. Any small rotation of the coil produces a torque which brings it back to its original position. When they are antiparallel, the equilibrium is unstable as any rotation produces a torque which increases with the amount of rotation. The presence of this torque is also the reason why a small magnet or any magnetic dipole aligns itself with the external magnetic field.','[{"s": "From Eq. (4.29), we see that the torque τ vanishes when m is either parallel or antiparallel to the magnetic field B. This indicates a state of equilibrium as there is no torque on the coil (this also applies to any object with a magnetic moment m). When m and B are parallel the equilibrium is a stable one. Any small rotation of the coil produces a torque which brings it back to its original position. When they are antiparallel, the equilibrium is unstable as any rotation produces a torque which increases with the amount of rotation. The presence of this torque is also the reason why a small magnet or any magnetic dipole aligns itself with the external magnetic field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28552,70,372,'paragraph',NULL,'m = N I A (4.30)','[{"s": "m = N I A (4.30)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28553,70,373,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 4.11 A 100 turn closely wound circular coil of radius 10 cm carries a current of 3.2 A. (a) What is the field at the centre of the coil? (b) What is the magnetic moment of this coil?','[{"s": "Example 4.11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A 100 turn closely wound circular coil of radius 10 cm carries a current of 3.2 A. (a) What is the field at the centre of the coil? (b) What is the magnetic moment of this coil?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28554,70,374,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28555,70,375,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) From Eq. (4.16)','[{"s": "(a) From Eq. (4.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28556,70,376,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image118.png'),
(28557,70,377,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, N = 100; I = 3.2 A, and R = 0.1 m. Hence,','[{"s": "Here, N = 100; I = 3.2 A, and R = 0.1 m. Hence,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28558,70,378,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image77.png Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image14.png (using π × 3.2 = 10)','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image77.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(using π × 3.2 = 10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28559,70,379,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2 × 10–3 T','[{"s": "= 2 × 10–3 T", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28560,70,380,'paragraph',NULL,'The direction is given by the right-hand thumb rule.','[{"s": "The direction is given by the right-hand thumb rule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28561,70,381,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The magnetic moment is given by Eq. (4.30),','[{"s": "(b) The magnetic moment is given by Eq. (4.30),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28562,70,382,'paragraph',NULL,'m = N I A = N I π r 2 = 100 × 3.2 × 3.14 × 10 –2 = 10 A m 2','[{"s": "m = N I A = N I π r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 100 × 3.2 × 3.14 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 A m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28563,70,383,'paragraph',NULL,'The direction is once again given by the right-hand thumb rule.','[{"s": "The direction is once again given by the right-hand thumb rule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28564,70,384,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image108.png [from Eq. (4.29)]','[{"s": "(c)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image108.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[from Eq. (4.29)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28565,70,385,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image67.png'),
(28566,70,386,'paragraph',NULL,'Initially, θ = 0. Thus, initial torque τ i = 0. Finally, θ = π/2 (or 90º). Thus, final torque τ f = m B = 10 × 2 = 20 N m.','[{"s": "Initially, θ = 0. Thus, initial torque τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0. Finally, θ = π/2 (or 90º). Thus, final torque τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m B = 10 × 2 = 20 N m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28567,70,387,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) From Newton’s second law,','[{"s": "(d) From Newton’s second law,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28568,70,388,'paragraph',NULL,'I Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image49.png','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image49.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28569,70,389,'paragraph',NULL,'where I is the moment of inertia of the coil. From chain rule,','[{"s": "where I is the moment of inertia of the coil. From chain rule,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28570,70,390,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image28.png'),
