(28573,70,393,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 4.12','[{"s": "Example 4.12", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28574,70,394,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) A current-carrying circular loop lies on a smooth horizontal plane. Can a uniform magnetic field be set up in such a manner that the loop turns around itself (i.e., turns about the vertical axis).','[{"s": "(a) A current-carrying circular loop lies on a smooth horizontal plane. Can a uniform magnetic field be set up in such a manner that the loop turns around itself (i.e., turns about the vertical axis).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28575,70,395,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) A current-carrying circular loop is located in a uniform external magnetic field. If the loop is free to turn, what is its orientation of stable equilibrium? Show that in this orientation, the flux of the total field (external field + field produced by the loop) is maximum.','[{"s": "(b) A current-carrying circular loop is located in a uniform external magnetic field. If the loop is free to turn, what is its orientation of stable equilibrium? Show that in this orientation, the flux of the total field (external field + field produced by the loop) is maximum.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28576,70,396,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) A loop of irregular shape carrying current is located in an external magnetic field. If the wire is flexible, why does it change to a circular shape?','[{"s": "(c) A loop of irregular shape carrying current is located in an external magnetic field. If the wire is flexible, why does it change to a circular shape?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28577,70,397,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28578,70,398,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) No, because that would require τ to be in the vertical direction. But τ = I A × B, and since A of the horizontal loop is in the vertical direction, τ would be in the plane of the loop for any B.','[{"s": "(a) No, because that would require τ to be in the vertical direction. But τ = I A × B, and since A of the horizontal loop is in the vertical direction, τ would be in the plane of the loop for any B.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28579,70,399,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Orientation of stable equilibrium is one where the area vector A of the loop is in the direction of external magnetic field. In this orientation, the magnetic field produced by the loop is in the same direction as external field, both normal to the plane of the loop, thus giving rise to maximum flux of the total field.','[{"s": "(b) Orientation of stable equilibrium is one where the area vector A of the loop is in the direction of external magnetic field. In this orientation, the magnetic field produced by the loop is in the same direction as external field, both normal to the plane of the loop, thus giving rise to maximum flux of the total field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28580,70,400,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) It assumes circular shape with its plane normal to the field to maximise flux, since for a given perimeter, a circle encloses greater area than any other shape.','[{"s": "(c) It assumes circular shape with its plane normal to the field to maximise flux, since for a given perimeter, a circle encloses greater area than any other shape.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28581,70,401,'heading',4,'4.10.2 CIRCULAR CURRENT LOOP AS A MAGNETIC DIPOLE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28582,70,402,'paragraph',NULL,'In this section, we shall consider the elementary magnetic element: the current loop. We shall show that the magnetic field (at large distances) due to current in a circular current loop is very similar in behaviour to the electric field of an electric dipole. In Section 4.6, we have evaluated the magnetic field on the axis of a circular loop, of a radius R, carrying a steady current I. The magnitude of this field is [(Eq. (4.15)],','[{"s": "In this section, we shall consider the elementary magnetic element: the current loop. We shall show that the magnetic field (at large distances) due to current in a circular current loop is very similar in behaviour to the electric field of an electric dipole. In Section 4.6, we have evaluated the magnetic field on the axis of a circular loop, of a radius R, carrying a steady current I. The magnitude of this field is [(Eq. (4.15)],", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28583,70,403,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image81.png'),
(28584,70,404,'paragraph',NULL,'and its direction is along the axis and given by the right-hand thumb rule (Fig. 4.12). Here, x is the distance along the axis from the centre of the loop. For x >> R, we may drop the R 2 term in the denominator. Thus,','[{"s": "and its direction is along the axis and given by the right-hand thumb rule (Fig. 4.12). Here, x is the distance along the axis from the centre of the loop. For x >> R, we may drop the R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "term in the denominator. Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28585,70,405,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image41.png'),
(28586,70,406,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the area of the loop A = πR 2 . Thus,','[{"s": "Note that the area of the loop A = πR", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28587,70,407,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image98.png'),
(28588,70,408,'paragraph',NULL,'As earlier, we define the magnetic moment m to have a magnitude IA,','[{"s": "As earlier, we define the magnetic moment m to have a magnitude IA,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28589,70,409,'paragraph',NULL,'m = I A . Hence,','[{"s": "m = I A . Hence,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28590,70,410,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image91.png'),
(28591,70,411,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image34.png [4.31(a)]','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image34.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[4.31(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28592,70,412,'paragraph',NULL,'The expression of Eq. [4.31(a)] is very similar to an expression obtained earlier for the electric field of a dipole. The similarity may be seen if we substitute, Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image34.png','[{"s": "The expression of Eq. [4.31(a)] is very similar to an expression obtained earlier for the electric field of a dipole. The similarity may be seen if we substitute,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image34.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28593,70,413,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image4.png'),
(28594,70,414,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image82.png (electrostatic dipole)','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image82.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(electrostatic dipole)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28595,70,415,'paragraph',NULL,'(electrostatic field)','[{"s": "(electrostatic field)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28596,70,416,'paragraph',NULL,'We then obtain,','[{"s": "We then obtain,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28597,70,417,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image76.png'),
(28598,70,418,'paragraph',NULL,'which is precisely the field for an electric dipole at a point on its axis. considered in Chapter 1, Section 1.10 [Eq. (1.20)].','[{"s": "which is precisely the field for an electric dipole at a point on its axis. considered in Chapter 1, Section 1.10 [Eq. (1.20)].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28599,70,419,'paragraph',NULL,'It can be shown that the above analogy can be carried further. We had found in Chapter 1 that the electric field on the perpendicular bisector of the dipole is given by [See Eq.(1.21)], Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image13.png','[{"s": "It can be shown that the above analogy can be carried further. We had found in Chapter 1 that the electric field on the perpendicular bisector of the dipole is given by [See Eq.(1.21)],", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28600,70,420,'paragraph',NULL,'where x is the distance from the dipole. If we replace p with m and in the above expression, we obtain the result for B for a point in the plane of the loop at a distance x from the centre. Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image32.png','[{"s": "where x is the distance from the dipole. If we replace p with m and in the above expression, we obtain the result for B for a point in the plane of the loop at a distance x from the centre.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image32.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28601,70,421,'paragraph',NULL,'The results given by Eqs. [4.31(a)] and [4.31(b)] become exact for a point magnetic dipole.','[{"s": "The results given by Eqs. [4.31(a)] and [4.31(b)] become exact for a point magnetic dipole.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28602,70,422,'paragraph',NULL,'The results obtained above can be shown to apply to any planar loop: a planar current loop is equivalent to a magnetic dipole of dipole moment m = I A, which is the analogue of electric dipole moment p. Note, however, a fundamental difference: an electric dipole is built up of two elementary units — the charges (or electric monopoles). In magnetism, a magnetic dipole (or a current loop) is the most elementary element. The equivalent of electric charges, i.e., magnetic monopoles, are not known to exist.','[{"s": "The results obtained above can be shown to apply to any planar loop: a planar current loop is equivalent to a magnetic dipole of dipole moment m = I A, which is the analogue of electric dipole moment p. Note, however, a fundamental difference: an electric dipole is built up of two elementary units — the charges (or electric monopoles). In magnetism, a magnetic dipole (or a current loop) is the most elementary element. The equivalent of electric charges, i.e., magnetic monopoles, are not known to exist.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28603,70,423,'paragraph',NULL,'We have shown that a current loop (i) produces a magnetic field (see Fig. 4.12) and behaves like a magnetic dipole at large distances, and (ii) is subject to torque like a magnetic needle. This led Ampere to suggest that all magnetism is due to circulating currents. This seems to be partly true and no magnetic monopoles have been seen so far. However, elementary particles such as an electron or a proton also carry an intrinsic magnetic moment, not accounted by circulating currents.','[{"s": "We have shown that a current loop (i) produces a magnetic field (see Fig. 4.12) and behaves like a magnetic dipole at large distances, and (ii) is subject to torque like a magnetic needle. This led Ampere to suggest that all magnetism is due to circulating currents. This seems to be partly true and no magnetic monopoles have been seen so far. However, elementary particles such as an electron or a proton also carry an intrinsic magnetic moment, not accounted by circulating currents.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28604,70,424,'heading',4,'4.10.3 THE MAGNETIC DIPOLE MOMENT OF A REVOLVING ELECTRON',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28605,70,425,'paragraph',NULL,'In Chapter 12 we shall read about the Bohr model of the hydrogen atom. You may perhaps have heard of this model which was proposed by the Danish physicist Niels Bohr in 1911 and was a stepping stone to a new kind of mechanics, namely, quantum mechanics. In the Bohr model, the electron (a negatively charged particle) revolves around a positively charged nucleus much as a planet revolves around the sun. The force in the former case is electrostatic (Coulomb force) while it is gravitational for the planet-Sun case. We show this Bohr picture of the electron in Fig. 4.23.','[{"s": "In Chapter 12 we shall read about the Bohr model of the hydrogen atom. You may perhaps have heard of this model which was proposed by the Danish physicist Niels Bohr in 1911 and was a stepping stone to a new kind of mechanics, namely, quantum mechanics. In the Bohr model, the electron (a negatively charged particle) revolves around a positively charged nucleus much as a planet revolves around the sun. The force in the former case is electrostatic (Coulomb force) while it is gravitational for the planet-Sun case. We show this Bohr picture of the electron in Fig. 4.23.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28606,70,426,'paragraph',NULL,'The electron of charge (–e) (e = + 1.6 × 10 –19 C) performs uniform circular motion around a stationary heavy nucleus of charge +Ze. This constitutes a current I, where,','[{"s": "The electron of charge (–e) (e = + 1.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C) performs uniform circular motion around a stationary heavy nucleus of charge +Ze. This constitutes a current I, where,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28607,70,427,'paragraph',NULL,'I= e/T (4.32)','[{"s": "I=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e/T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.32)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28609,70,429,'paragraph',NULL,'T= 2πr / v (4.33)','[{"s": "T=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2πr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.33)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28610,70,430,'paragraph',NULL,'Substituting in Eq. (4.32), we have I = ev/2πr.','[{"s": "Substituting in Eq. (4.32), we have I = ev/2πr.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28611,70,431,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image61.png'),
(28612,70,432,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.23 In the Bohr model of hydrogen-like atoms, the negatively charged electron is revolving with uniform speed around a centrally placed positively charged (+Z e) nucleus. The uniform circular motion of the electron constitutes a current. The direction of the magnetic moment is into the plane of the paper and is indicated separately by ⊗.','[{"s": "Figure 4.23 In the Bohr model of hydrogen-like atoms, the negatively charged electron is revolving with uniform speed around a centrally placed positively charged (+Z e) nucleus. The uniform circular motion of the electron constitutes a current. The direction of the magnetic moment is into the plane of the paper and is indicated separately by ⊗.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28613,70,433,'paragraph',NULL,'There will be a magnetic moment, usually denoted by µl, associated with this circulating current. From Eq. (4.28) its magnitude is, µl = Iπr2 = evr/2.','[{"s": "There will be a magnetic moment, usually denoted by µl, associated with this circulating current. From Eq. (4.28) its magnitude is, µl = Iπr2 = evr/2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28608,70,428,'paragraph',NULL,'and T is the time period of revolution. Let r be the orbital radius of the electron, and v the orbital speed. Then,','[{"s": "and T is the time period of revolution.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Let r be the orbital radius of the electron, and v the orbital speed. Then,", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(28614,70,434,'paragraph',NULL,'The direction of this magnetic moment is into the plane of the paper in Fig. 4.23. [This follows from the right-hand rule discussed earlier and the fact that the negatively charged electron is moving anticlockwise, leading to a clockwise current.] Multiplying and dividing the right-hand side of the above expression by the electron mass me, we have,','[{"s": "The direction of this magnetic moment is into the plane of the paper in Fig. 4.23. [This follows from the right-hand rule discussed earlier and the fact that the negatively charged electron is moving anticlockwise, leading to a clockwise current.] Multiplying and dividing the right-hand side of the above expression by the electron mass me, we have,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28615,70,435,'paragraph',NULL,'μl=e/2m e (mevr)','[{"s": "μl=e/2m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(mevr)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28616,70,436,'paragraph',NULL,'=e/2m e l [4.34(a)]','[{"s": "=e/2m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l [4.34(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28617,70,437,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, l is the magnitude of the angular momentum of the electron about the central nucleus (“orbital” angular momentum). Vectorially,','[{"s": "Here, l is the magnitude of the angular momentum of the electron about the central nucleus (“orbital” angular momentum). Vectorially,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28618,70,438,'paragraph',NULL,'μl=−e/2m e l [4.34(b)]','[{"s": "μl=−e/2m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[4.34(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28619,70,439,'paragraph',NULL,'The negative sign indicates that the angular momentum of the electron is opposite in direction to the magnetic moment. Instead of electron with charge (–e), if we had taken a particle with charge (+q), the angular momentum and magnetic moment would be in the same direction. The ratio','[{"s": "The negative sign indicates that the angular momentum of the electron is opposite in direction to the magnetic moment. Instead of electron with charge (–e), if we had taken a particle with charge (+q), the angular momentum and magnetic moment would be in the same direction. The ratio", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28620,70,440,'paragraph',NULL,'μ l /l=e/2m e (4.35)','[{"s": "μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/l=e/2m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(4.35)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28621,70,441,'paragraph',NULL,'is called the gyromagnetic ratio and is a constant. Its value is 8.8 × 10 10 C /kg for an electron, which has been verified by experiments.','[{"s": "is called the gyromagnetic ratio and is a constant. Its value is 8.8 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C /kg for an electron, which has been verified by experiments.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28622,70,442,'paragraph',NULL,'The fact that even at an atomic level there is a magnetic moment, confirms Ampere’s bold hypothesis of atomic magnetic moments. This according to Ampere, would help one to explain the magnetic properties of materials. Can one assign a value to this atomic dipole moment? The answer is Yes. One can do so within the Bohr model. Bohr hypothesised that the angular momentum assumes a discrete set of values, namely,','[{"s": "The fact that even at an atomic level there is a magnetic moment, confirms Ampere’s bold hypothesis of atomic magnetic moments. This according to Ampere, would help one to explain the magnetic properties of materials. Can one assign a value to this atomic dipole moment? The answer is Yes. One can do so within the Bohr model. Bohr hypothesised that the angular momentum assumes a discrete set of values, namely,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28623,70,443,'paragraph',NULL,'l= n ℎ/2π (4.36)','[{"s": "l=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ℎ/2π (4.36)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28624,70,444,'paragraph',NULL,'where n is a natural number, n = 1, 2, 3, .... and h is a constant named after Max Planck (Planck’s constant) with a value h = 6.626 × 10 –34','[{"s": "where n is a natural number, n = 1, 2, 3, .... and h is a constant named after Max Planck (Planck’s constant) with a value h = 6.626 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–34", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28625,70,445,'paragraph',NULL,'(μl) min =e/4πm e h','[{"s": "(μl)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "min", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=e/4πm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28626,70,446,'paragraph',NULL,'=1.60×10 -19 ×6.63×10 -34 /4×3.14×9.11×10 -31','[{"s": "=1.60×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×6.63×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-34", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4×3.14×9.11×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-31", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28627,70,447,'paragraph',NULL,'=9.27×10 -24 Am 2','[{"s": "=9.27×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-24", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Am", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28628,70,448,'paragraph',NULL,'(4.37)','[{"s": "(4.37)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28629,70,449,'paragraph',NULL,'where the subscript ‘min’ stands for minimum. This value is called the Bohr magneton.','[{"s": "where the subscript ‘min’ stands for minimum. This value is called the Bohr magneton.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28630,70,450,'paragraph',NULL,'Any charge in uniform circular motion would have an associated magnetic moment given by an expression similar to Eq. (4.34). This dipole moment is labelled as the orbital magnetic moment. Hence, the subscript ‘l’ in µl. Besides the orbital moment, the electron has an intrinsic magnetic moment, which has the same numerical value as given in Eq. (4.37). It is called the spin magnetic moment. But we hasten to add that it is not as though the electron is spinning. The electron is an elementary particle and it does not have an axis to spin around like a top or our earth. Nevertheless, it does possess this intrinsic magnetic moment. The microscopic roots of magnetism in iron and other materials can be traced back to this intrinsic spin magnetic moment.','[{"s": "Any charge in uniform circular motion would have an associated magnetic moment given by an expression similar to Eq. (4.34). This dipole moment is labelled as the orbital magnetic moment. Hence, the subscript ‘l’ in µl. Besides the orbital moment, the electron has an intrinsic magnetic moment, which has the same numerical value as given in Eq. (4.37). It is called the spin magnetic moment. But we hasten to add that it is not as though the electron is spinning. The electron is an elementary particle and it does not have an axis to spin around like a top or our earth. Nevertheless, it does possess this intrinsic magnetic moment. The microscopic roots of magnetism in iron and other materials can be traced back to this intrinsic spin magnetic moment.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28631,70,451,'heading',3,'4.11 THE MOVING COIL GALVANOMETER',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28632,70,452,'paragraph',NULL,'Currents and voltages in circuits have been discussed extensively in Chapters 3. But how do we measure them? How do we claim that current in a circuit is 1.5 A or the voltage drop across a resistor is 1.2 V? Figure 4.24 exhibits a very useful instrument for this purpose: the moving coil galvanometer (MCG). It is a device whose principle can be understood on the basis of our discussion in Section 4.10.','[{"s": "Currents and voltages in circuits have been discussed extensively in Chapters 3. But how do we measure them? How do we claim that current in a circuit is 1.5 A or the voltage drop across a resistor is 1.2 V? Figure 4.24 exhibits a very useful instrument for this purpose: the moving coil galvanometer (MCG). It is a device whose principle can be understood on the basis of our discussion in Section 4.10.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28633,70,453,'paragraph',NULL,'The galvanometer consists of a coil, with many turns, free to rotate about a fixed axis (Fig. 4.24), in a uniform radial magnetic field. There is a cylindrical soft iron core which not only makes the field radial but also increases the strength of the magnetic field. When a current flows through the coil, a torque acts on it. This torque is given by Eq. (4.26) to be','[{"s": "The galvanometer consists of a coil, with many turns, free to rotate about a fixed axis (Fig. 4.24), in a uniform radial magnetic field. There is a cylindrical soft iron core which not only makes the field radial but also increases the strength of the magnetic field. When a current flows through the coil, a torque acts on it. This torque is given by Eq. (4.26) to be", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28634,70,454,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = NI AB','[{"s": "τ = NI AB", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28635,70,455,'paragraph',NULL,'where the symbols have their usual meaning. Since the field is radial by design, we have taken sin θ = 1 in the above expression for the torque. The magnetic torque NIAB tends to rotate the coil. A spring Sp provides a counter torque kφ that balances the magnetic torque NIAB; resulting in a steady angular deflection φ. In equilibrium','[{"s": "where the symbols have their usual meaning. Since the field is radial by design, we have taken sin θ = 1 in the above expression for the torque. The magnetic torque NIAB tends to rotate the coil. A spring Sp provides a counter torque kφ that balances the magnetic torque NIAB; resulting in a steady angular deflection φ. In equilibrium", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28636,70,456,'paragraph',NULL,'kφ = NI AB','[{"s": "kφ = NI AB", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28637,70,457,'paragraph',NULL,'where k is the torsional constant of the spring; i.e. the restoring torque per unit twist. The deflection φ is indicated on the scale by a pointer attached to the spring. We have','[{"s": "where k is the torsional constant of the spring; i.e. the restoring torque per unit twist. The deflection φ is indicated on the scale by a pointer attached to the spring. We have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28638,70,458,'paragraph',NULL,'Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image51.png (4.38)','[{"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image51.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(4.38)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28639,70,459,'paragraph',NULL,'The quantity in brackets is a constant for a given galvanometer.','[{"s": "The quantity in brackets is a constant for a given galvanometer.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28640,70,460,'paragraph',NULL,'The galvanometer can be used in a number of ways. It can be used as a detector to check if a current is flowing in the circuit. We have come across this usage in the Wheatstone’s bridge arrangement. In this usage the neutral position of the pointer (when no current is flowing through the galvanometer) is in the middle of the scale and not at the left end as shown in Fig.4.24. Depending on the direction of the current, the pointer’s deflection is either to the right or the left.','[{"s": "The galvanometer can be used in a number of ways. It can be used as a detector to check if a current is flowing in the circuit. We have come across this usage in the Wheatstone’s bridge arrangement. In this usage the neutral position of the pointer (when no current is flowing through the galvanometer) is in the middle of the scale and not at the left end as shown in Fig.4.24. Depending on the direction of the current, the pointer’s deflection is either to the right or the left.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28642,70,462,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image97.png'),
(28643,70,463,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.24 The moving coil galvanometer. Its elements are described in the text. Depending on the requirement, this device can be used as a current detector or for measuring the value of the current (ammeter) or voltage (voltmeter).','[{"s": "Figure 4.24 The moving coil galvanometer. Its elements are described in the text. Depending on the requirement, this device can be used as a current detector or for measuring the value of the current (ammeter) or voltage (voltmeter).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28644,70,464,'paragraph',NULL,'R G r s / (R G + r s ) ; r s if RG >> r s','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ (R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") ; r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "if RG >> r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30014,73,72,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 9.1 Suppose that the lower half of the concave mirror’s reflecting surface in Fig. 9.5 is covered with an opaque (non-reflective) material. What effect will this have on the image of an object placed in front of the mirror?','[{"s": "Example 9.1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Suppose that the lower half of the concave mirror’s reflecting surface in Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "9.5 is covered with an opaque (non-reflective) material. What effect will this have on the image of an object placed in front of the mirror?", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(28646,70,466,'paragraph',NULL,'ϕ/I=NAB/k (4.39)','[{"s": "ϕ/I=NAB/k (4.39)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28647,70,467,'paragraph',NULL,'A convenient way for the manufacturer to increase the sensitivity is to increase the number of turns N. We choose galvanometers having sensitivities of value, required by our experiment.','[{"s": "A convenient way for the manufacturer to increase the sensitivity is to increase the number of turns N. We choose galvanometers having sensitivities of value, required by our experiment.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28649,70,469,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image103.png'),
(28650,70,470,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.25 Conversion of a galvanometer (G) to an ammeter by the introduction of a shunt resistance rsof very small value in parallel.','[{"s": "Figure 4.25 Conversion of a galvanometer (G) to an ammeter by the introduction of a shunt resistance rsof very small value in parallel.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28651,70,471,'paragraph',NULL,'R G + R ; R : large','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ R ; R : large", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28652,70,472,'paragraph',NULL,'The scale of the voltmeter is calibrated to read off the voltage value with ease. We define the voltage sensitivity as the deflection per unit voltage. From Eq. (4.38),','[{"s": "The scale of the voltmeter is calibrated to read off the voltage value with ease. We define the voltage sensitivity as the deflection per unit voltage. From Eq. (4.38),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28653,70,473,'paragraph',NULL,'ϕ/V= (NAB/k) I/V = (NAB/k)1/R (4.40)','[{"s": "ϕ/V= (NAB/k) I/V = (NAB/k)1/R (4.40)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(28654,70,474,'paragraph',NULL,'An interesting point to note is that increasing the current sensitivity may not necessarily increase the voltage sensitivity. Let us take Eq. (4.39) which provides a measure of current sensitivity. If N → 2N, i.e., we double the number of turns, then','[{"s": "An interesting point to note is that increasing the current sensitivity may not necessarily increase the voltage sensitivity. Let us take Eq. (4.39) which provides a measure of current sensitivity. If N → 2N, i.e., we double the number of turns, then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28655,70,475,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image101.png'),
(28656,70,476,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image109.png'),
(28657,70,477,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.26 Conversion of a galvanometer (G) to a voltmeter by the introduction of a resistance R of large','[{"s": "Figure 4.26 Conversion of a galvanometer (G) to a voltmeter by the introduction of a resistance R of large", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28658,70,478,'paragraph',NULL,'value in series.','[{"s": "value in series.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28659,70,479,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image110.png'),
(28660,70,480,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the current sensitivity doubles. However, the resistance of the galvanometer is also likely to double, since it is proportional to the length of the wire. In Eq. (4.40), N →2N, and R →2R, thus the voltage sensitivity,','[{"s": "Thus, the current sensitivity doubles. However, the resistance of the galvanometer is also likely to double, since it is proportional to the length of the wire. In Eq. (4.40), N →2N, and R →2R, thus the voltage sensitivity,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28661,70,481,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image75.png'),
(28662,70,482,'paragraph',NULL,'remains unchanged. So in general, the modification needed for conversion of a galvanometer to an ammeter will be different from what is needed for converting it into a voltmeter.','[{"s": "remains unchanged. So in general, the modification needed for conversion of a galvanometer to an ammeter will be different from what is needed for converting it into a voltmeter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22639,57,523,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Loop Rule: The algebraic sum of changes in potential around any closed loop must be zero.','[{"s": "(b) Loop Rule: The algebraic sum of changes in potential around any closed loop must be zero.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041941", "2000110643"]',NULL),
(28664,70,484,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) is a galvanometer with a resistance RG = 60.00 Ω; (b) is a galvanometer described in (a) but converted to an ammeter by a shunt resistance rs = 0.02 Ω; (c) is an ideal ammeter with zero resistance?','[{"s": "(a) is a galvanometer with a resistance RG = 60.00 Ω; (b) is a galvanometer described in (a) but converted to an ammeter by a shunt resistance rs = 0.02 Ω; (c) is an ideal ammeter with zero resistance?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28665,70,485,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image107.png'),
(28666,70,486,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.27','[{"s": "Figure 4.27", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28667,70,487,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28668,70,488,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Total resistance in the circuit is,','[{"s": "(a) Total resistance in the circuit is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28669,70,489,'paragraph',NULL,'R G +3=63 Ω . Hence, I = 3/63 = 0.048 A.','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+3=63 Ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Hence, I = 3/63 = 0.048 A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28670,70,490,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Resistance of the galvanometer converted to an ammeter is,','[{"s": "(b) Resistance of the galvanometer converted to an ammeter is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28671,70,491,'paragraph',NULL,'R G r s /R G +r s = 60Ω×0.02Ω/(60+0.02)Ω ≃ 0.02Ω','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 60Ω×0.02Ω/(60+0.02)Ω ≃ 0.02Ω", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28672,70,492,'paragraph',NULL,'Total resistance in the circuit is,','[{"s": "Total resistance in the circuit is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28673,70,493,'paragraph',NULL,'0.02Ω+3Ω=3.02Ω . Hence, I = 3/3.02 = 0.99 A.','[{"s": "0.02Ω+3Ω=3.02Ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Hence, I = 3/3.02 = 0.99 A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28674,70,494,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) For the ideal ammeter with zero resistance,','[{"s": "(c) For the ideal ammeter with zero resistance,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28675,70,495,'paragraph',NULL,'I = 3/3 = 1.00 A','[{"s": "I = 3/3 = 1.00 A", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28676,70,496,'heading',3,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28678,70,498,'paragraph',NULL,'F = q (v × B + E)','[{"s": "F = q (v × B + E)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28680,70,500,'paragraph',NULL,'2. A straight conductor of length l and carrying a steady current I experiences a force F in a uniform external magnetic field B,','[{"s": "2. A straight conductor of length l and carrying a steady current I experiences a force F in a uniform external magnetic field B,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28681,70,501,'paragraph',NULL,'F = I l × B','[{"s": "F = I l × B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28682,70,502,'paragraph',NULL,'where|l| = l and the direction of l is given by the direction of the current.','[{"s": "where|l| = l and the direction of l is given by the direction of the current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28683,70,503,'paragraph',NULL,'3. In a uniform magnetic field B, a charge q executes a circular orbit in a plane normal to B. Its frequency of uniform circular motion is called the cyclotron frequency and is given by:','[{"s": "3. In a uniform magnetic field B, a charge q executes a circular orbit in a plane normal to B. Its frequency of uniform circular motion is called the cyclotron frequency and is given by:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28684,70,504,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image26.png'),
(28685,70,505,'paragraph',NULL,'This frequency is independent of the particle’s speed and radius. This fact is exploited in a machine, the cyclotron, which is used to accelerate charged particles.','[{"s": "This frequency is independent of the particle’s speed and radius. This fact is exploited in a machine, the cyclotron, which is used to accelerate charged particles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28686,70,506,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The Biot-Savart law asserts that the magnetic field dB due to an element dl carrying a steady current I at a point P at a distance r from the current element is:','[{"s": "4. The Biot-Savart law asserts that the magnetic field dB due to an element dl carrying a steady current I at a point P at a distance r from the current element is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28687,70,507,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image80.png'),
(28688,70,508,'paragraph',NULL,'To obtain the total field at P, we must integrate this vector expression over the entire length of the conductor.','[{"s": "To obtain the total field at P, we must integrate this vector expression over the entire length of the conductor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
