(28677,70,497,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The total force on a charge q moving with velocity v in the presence of magnetic and electric fields B and E, respectively is called the Lorentz force . I t is given by the expression:','[{"s": "1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The total force on a charge q moving with velocity v in the presence of magnetic and electric fields B and E, respectively is called the Lorentz force", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t is given by the expression:", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(28693,70,513,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Ampere’s Circuital Law: Let an open surface S be bounded by a loop C. Then the Ampere’s law states that Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image123.png where I refers to the current passing through S. The sign of I is determined from the right-hand rule. We have discussed a simplified form of this law. If B is directed along the tangent to every point on the perimeter L of a closed curve and is constant in magnitude along perimeter then,','[{"s": "6. Ampere’s Circuital Law: Let an open surface S be bounded by a loop C. Then the Ampere’s law states that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image123.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "where I refers to the current passing through S. The sign of I is determined from the right-hand rule. We have discussed a simplified form of this law. If B is directed along the tangent to every point on the perimeter L of a closed curve and is constant in magnitude along perimeter then,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28694,70,514,'paragraph',NULL,'BL = µ0 Ie','[{"s": "BL = µ0 Ie", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28695,70,515,'paragraph',NULL,'where Ie is the net current enclosed by the closed circuit.','[{"s": "where Ie is the net current enclosed by the closed circuit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28696,70,516,'paragraph',NULL,'7. The magnitude of the magnetic field at a distance R from a long, straight wire carrying a current I is given by:','[{"s": "7. The magnitude of the magnetic field at a distance R from a long, straight wire carrying a current I is given by:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28697,70,517,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image62.png'),
(28698,70,518,'paragraph',NULL,'The field lines are circles concentric with the wire.','[{"s": "The field lines are circles concentric with the wire.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28699,70,519,'paragraph',NULL,'8. The magnitude of the field B inside a long solenoid carrying a current I is','[{"s": "8. The magnitude of the field B inside a long solenoid carrying a current I is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28700,70,520,'paragraph',NULL,'B = µ0nI','[{"s": "B = µ0nI", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28701,70,521,'paragraph',NULL,'where n is the number of turns per unit length. For a toroid one obtains,','[{"s": "where n is the number of turns per unit length. For a toroid one obtains,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28702,70,522,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image112.png'),
(28703,70,523,'paragraph',NULL,'where N is the total number of turns and r is the average radius.','[{"s": "where N is the total number of turns and r is the average radius.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28704,70,524,'paragraph',NULL,'9. Parallel currents attract and anti-parallel currents repel.','[{"s": "9. Parallel currents attract and anti-parallel currents repel.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28706,70,526,'paragraph',NULL,'m = N I A','[{"s": "m = N I A", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28707,70,527,'paragraph',NULL,'and the direction of m is given by the right-hand thumb rule : curl the palm of your right hand along the loop with the fingers pointing in the direction of the current. The thumb sticking out gives the direction of m (and A)','[{"s": "and the direction of m is given by the right-hand thumb rule : curl the palm of your right hand along the loop with the fingers pointing in the direction of the current. The thumb sticking out gives the direction of m (and A)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28708,70,528,'paragraph',NULL,'When this loop is placed in a uniform magnetic field B, the force F on it is: F = 0','[{"s": "When this loop is placed in a uniform magnetic field B, the force F on it is: F = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28709,70,529,'paragraph',NULL,'And the torque on it is,','[{"s": "And the torque on it is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28710,70,530,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = m × B','[{"s": "τ = m × B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28711,70,531,'paragraph',NULL,'In a moving coil galvanometer, this torque is balanced by a counter-torque due to a spring, yielding','[{"s": "In a moving coil galvanometer, this torque is balanced by a counter-torque due to a spring, yielding", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28712,70,532,'paragraph',NULL,'kφ = NI AB','[{"s": "kφ = NI AB", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28713,70,533,'paragraph',NULL,'where φ is the equilibrium deflection and k the torsion constant of the spring.','[{"s": "where φ is the equilibrium deflection and k the torsion constant of the spring.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28714,70,534,'paragraph',NULL,'11. An electron moving around the central nucleus has a magnetic moment µl given by:','[{"s": "11. An electron moving around the central nucleus has a magnetic moment µl given by:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28715,70,535,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image21.png'),
(28716,70,536,'paragraph',NULL,'where l is the magnitude of the angular momentum of the circulating electron about the central nucleus. The smallest value of µl is called the Bohr magneton µB and it is µB = 9.27×10–24 J/T','[{"s": "where l is the magnitude of the angular momentum of the circulating electron about the central nucleus. The smallest value of µl is called the Bohr magneton µB and it is µB = 9.27×10–24 J/T", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28717,70,537,'paragraph',NULL,'12. A moving coil galvanometer can be converted into a ammeter by introducing a shunt resistance rs, of small value in parallel. It can be converted into a voltmeter by introducing a resistance of a large value in series.','[{"s": "12. A moving coil galvanometer can be converted into a ammeter by introducing a shunt resistance rs, of small value in parallel. It can be converted into a voltmeter by introducing a resistance of a large value in series.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28718,70,538,'image',NULL,'18',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image55.jpg'),
(28719,70,539,'heading',4,'POINTS TO PONDER',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28720,70,540,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Electrostatic field lines originate at a positive charge and terminate at a negative charge or fade at infinity. Magnetic field lines always form closed loops.','[{"s": "1. Electrostatic field lines originate at a positive charge and terminate at a negative charge or fade at infinity. Magnetic field lines always form closed loops.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28721,70,541,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The discussion in this Chapter holds only for steady currents which do not vary with time.When currents vary with time Newton’s third law is valid only if momentum carried by the electromagnetic field is taken into account.','[{"s": "2. The discussion in this Chapter holds only for steady currents which do not vary with time.When currents vary with time Newton’s third law is valid only if momentum carried by the electromagnetic field is taken into account.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28722,70,542,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Recall the expression for the Lorentz force,','[{"s": "3. Recall the expression for the Lorentz force,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28723,70,543,'paragraph',NULL,'F = q (v × B + E)','[{"s": "F = q (v × B + E)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28724,70,544,'paragraph',NULL,'This velocity dependent force has occupied the attention of some of the greatest scientific thinkers. If one switches to a frame with instantaneous velocity v, the magnetic part of the force vanishes. The motion of the charged particle is then explained by arguing that there exists an appropriate electric field in the new frame. We shall not discuss the details of this mechanism. However, we stress that the resolution of this paradox implies that electricity and magnetism are linked phenomena (electromagnetism) and that the Lorentz force expression does not imply a universal preferred frame of reference in nature.','[{"s": "This velocity dependent force has occupied the attention of some of the greatest scientific thinkers. If one switches to a frame with instantaneous velocity v, the magnetic part of the force vanishes. The motion of the charged particle is then explained by arguing that there exists an appropriate electric field in the new frame. We shall not discuss the details of this mechanism. However, we stress that the resolution of this paradox implies that electricity and magnetism are linked phenomena (electromagnetism) and that the Lorentz force expression does not imply a universal preferred frame of reference in nature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28725,70,545,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Ampere’s Circuital law is not independent of the Biot-Savart law. It can be derived from the Biot-Savart law. Its relationship to the Biot-Savart law is similar to the relationship between Gauss’s law and Coulomb’s law.','[{"s": "4. Ampere’s Circuital law is not independent of the Biot-Savart law. It can be derived from the Biot-Savart law. Its relationship to the Biot-Savart law is similar to the relationship between Gauss’s law and Coulomb’s law.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28726,70,546,'heading',3,'EXERCISES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28727,70,547,'paragraph',NULL,'4.1 A circular coil of wire consisting of 100 turns, each of radius 8.0 cm carries a current of 0.40 A. What is the magnitude of the magnetic field B at the centre of the coil?','[{"s": "4.1 A circular coil of wire consisting of 100 turns, each of radius 8.0 cm carries a current of 0.40 A. What is the magnitude of the magnetic field B at the centre of the coil?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28728,70,548,'paragraph',NULL,'4.2 A long straight wire carries a current of 35 A. What is the magnitude of the field B at a point 20 cm from the wire?','[{"s": "4.2 A long straight wire carries a current of 35 A. What is the magnitude of the field B at a point 20 cm from the wire?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28729,70,549,'paragraph',NULL,'4.3 A long straight wire in the horizontal plane carries a current of 50 A in north to south direction. Give the magnitude and direction of B at a point 2.5 m east of the wire.','[{"s": "4.3 A long straight wire in the horizontal plane carries a current of 50 A in north to south direction. Give the magnitude and direction of B at a point 2.5 m east of the wire.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28730,70,550,'paragraph',NULL,'4.4 A horizontal overhead power line carries a current of 90 A in east to west direction. What is the magnitude and direction of the magnetic field due to the current 1.5 m below the line?','[{"s": "4.4 A horizontal overhead power line carries a current of 90 A in east to west direction. What is the magnitude and direction of the magnetic field due to the current 1.5 m below the line?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28731,70,551,'paragraph',NULL,'4.5 What is the magnitude of magnetic force per unit length on a wire carrying a current of 8 A and making an angle of 30º with the direction of a uniform magnetic field of 0.15 T?','[{"s": "4.5 What is the magnitude of magnetic force per unit length on a wire carrying a current of 8 A and making an angle of 30º with the direction of a uniform magnetic field of 0.15 T?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28734,70,554,'paragraph',NULL,'4.8 A closely wound solenoid 80 cm long has 5 layers of windings of 400 turns each. The diameter of the solenoid is 1.8 cm. If the current carried is 8.0 A, estimate the magnitude of B inside the solenoid near its centre.','[{"s": "4.8 A closely wound solenoid 80 cm long has 5 layers of windings of 400 turns each. The diameter of the solenoid is 1.8 cm. If the current carried is 8.0 A, estimate the magnitude of B inside the solenoid near its centre.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28735,70,555,'paragraph',NULL,'4.9 A square coil of side 10 cm consists of 20 turns and carries a current of 12 A. The coil is suspended vertically and the normal to the plane of the coil makes an angle of 30º with the direction of a uniform horizontal magnetic field of magnitude 0.80 T. What is the magnitude of torque experienced by the coil?','[{"s": "4.9 A square coil of side 10 cm consists of 20 turns and carries a current of 12 A. The coil is suspended vertically and the normal to the plane of the coil makes an angle of 30º with the direction of a uniform horizontal magnetic field of magnitude 0.80 T. What is the magnitude of torque experienced by the coil?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28736,70,556,'paragraph',NULL,'4.10 Two moving coil meters, M1 and M2 have the following particulars:','[{"s": "4.10 Two moving coil meters, M1 and M2 have the following particulars:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28737,70,557,'paragraph',NULL,'R1 = 10 Ω, N1 = 30,','[{"s": "R1 = 10 Ω, N1 = 30,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28738,70,558,'paragraph',NULL,'A1 = 3.6 × 10–3 m2, B1 = 0.25 T','[{"s": "A1 = 3.6 × 10–3 m2, B1 = 0.25 T", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28739,70,559,'paragraph',NULL,'R2 = 14 Ω, N2 = 42,','[{"s": "R2 = 14 Ω, N2 = 42,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28740,70,560,'paragraph',NULL,'A2 = 1.8 × 10–3 m2, B2 = 0.50 T','[{"s": "A2 = 1.8 × 10–3 m2, B2 = 0.50 T", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28741,70,561,'paragraph',NULL,'(The spring constants are identical for the two meters).','[{"s": "(The spring constants are identical for the two meters).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28742,70,562,'paragraph',NULL,'Determine the ratio of (a) current sensitivity and (b) voltage sensitivity of M2 and M1.','[{"s": "Determine the ratio of (a) current sensitivity and (b) voltage sensitivity of M2 and M1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28743,70,563,'paragraph',NULL,'4.11 In a chamber, a uniform magnetic field of 6.5 G (1 G = 10 –4 T) is maintained. An electron is shot into the field with a speed of 4.8 × 106 m s –1 –19 C, me = 9.1×10 –31 kg)','[{"s": "4.11 In a chamber, a uniform magnetic field of 6.5 G (1 G = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T) is maintained. An electron is shot into the field with a speed of 4.8 × 106 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "–19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C, me = 9.1×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28744,70,564,'paragraph',NULL,'4.12 In Exercise 4.11 obtain the frequency of revolution of the electron in its circular orbit. Does the answer depend on the speed of the electron? Explain.','[{"s": "4.12 In Exercise 4.11 obtain the frequency of revolution of the electron in its circular orbit. Does the answer depend on the speed of the electron? Explain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28745,70,565,'paragraph',NULL,'4.13 (a) A circular coil of 30 turns and radius 8.0 cm carrying a current of 6.0 A is suspended vertically in a uniform horizontal magnetic field of magnitude 1.0 T. The field lines make an angle of 60° with the normal of the coil. Calculate the magnitude of the counter torque that must be applied to prevent the coil from turning.','[{"s": "4.13 (a) A circular coil of 30 turns and radius 8.0 cm carrying a current of 6.0 A is suspended vertically in a uniform horizontal magnetic field of magnitude 1.0 T. The field lines make an angle of 60° with the normal of the coil. Calculate the magnitude of the counter torque that must be applied to prevent the coil from turning.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28746,70,566,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Would your answer change, if the circular coil in (a) were replaced by a planar coil of some irregular shape that encloses the same area? (All other particulars are also unaltered.)','[{"s": "(b) Would your answer change, if the circular coil in (a) were replaced by a planar coil of some irregular shape that encloses the same area? (All other particulars are also unaltered.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28747,70,567,'paragraph',NULL,'4.14 Two concentric circular coils X and Y of radii 16 cm and 10 cm, respectively, lie in the same vertical plane containing the north to south direction. Coil X has 20 turns and carries a current of 16 A; coil Y has 25 turns and carries a current of 18 A. The sense of the current in X is anticlockwise, and clockwise in Y, for an observer looking at the coils facing west. Give the magnitude and direction of the net magnetic field due to the coils at their centre.','[{"s": "4.14 Two concentric circular coils X and Y of radii 16 cm and 10 cm, respectively, lie in the same vertical plane containing the north to south direction. Coil X has 20 turns and carries a current of 16 A; coil Y has 25 turns and carries a current of 18 A. The sense of the current in X is anticlockwise, and clockwise in Y, for an observer looking at the coils facing west. Give the magnitude and direction of the net magnetic field due to the coils at their centre.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28748,70,568,'paragraph',NULL,'4.15 A magnetic field of 100 G (1 G=10 -4 T) is required which is uniform in a region of linear dimension about 10 cm and area of cross-section about 10 -3 m 2 . The maximum current carrying capacity of a given coil of wire is 15 A and the number of turns per unit length that can be wound round a core is at most 1000 turns m -1 . Suggest some appropriate design particulars of a solenoid for the required purpose. Assume the core is not ferromagnetic.','[{"s": "4.15 A magnetic field of 100 G (1 G=10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T) is required which is uniform in a region of linear dimension about 10 cm and area of cross-section about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The maximum current carrying capacity of a given coil of wire is 15 A and the number of turns per unit length that can be wound round a core is at most 1000 turns m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Suggest some appropriate design particulars of a solenoid for the required purpose. Assume the core is not ferromagnetic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28749,70,569,'paragraph',NULL,'4.16 For a circular coil of radius R and N turns carrying current I, the magnitude of the magnetic field at a point on its axis at a distance x from its centre is given by,','[{"s": "4.16 For a circular coil of radius R and N turns carrying current I, the magnitude of the magnetic field at a point on its axis at a distance x from its centre is given by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28750,70,570,'paragraph',NULL,'B=μ 0 IR 2 N/2(x 2 +R 2 ) 3/2','[{"s": "B=μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "IR", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N/2(x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3/2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28751,70,571,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Show that this reduces to the familiar result for field at the centre of the coil.','[{"s": "(a) Show that this reduces to the familiar result for field at the centre of the coil.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28752,70,572,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Consider two parallel co-axial circular coils of equal radius R, and number of turns N, carrying equal currents in the same direction, and separated by a distance R. Show that the field on the axis around the mid-point between the coils is uniform over a distance that is small as compared to R, and is given by, B=0.72-μ 0 BNI/R , approximately.','[{"s": "(b) Consider two parallel co-axial circular coils of equal radius R, and number of turns N, carrying equal currents in the same direction, and separated by a distance R. Show that the field on the axis around the mid-point between the coils is uniform over a distance that is small as compared to R, and is given by,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B=0.72-μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BNI/R", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", approximately.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28753,70,573,'paragraph',NULL,'[Such an arrangement to produce a nearly uniform magnetic field over a small region is known as Helmholtz coils.]','[{"s": "[Such an arrangement to produce a nearly uniform magnetic field over a small region is known as Helmholtz coils.]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28754,70,574,'paragraph',NULL,'4.17 A toroid has a core (non-ferromagnetic) of inner radius 25 cm and outer radius 26 cm, around which 3500 turns of a wire are wound. If the current in the wire is 11 A, what is the magnetic field (a) outside the toroid, (b) inside the core of the toroid, and (c) in the empty space surrounded by the toroid.','[{"s": "4.17 A toroid has a core (non-ferromagnetic) of inner radius 25 cm and outer radius 26 cm, around which 3500 turns of a wire are wound. If the current in the wire is 11 A, what is the magnetic field (a) outside the toroid, (b) inside the core of the toroid, and (c) in the empty space surrounded by the toroid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28755,70,575,'paragraph',NULL,'4.18 Answer the following questions:','[{"s": "4.18 Answer the following questions:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28756,70,576,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) A magnetic field that varies in magnitude from point to point but has a constant direction (east to west) is set up in a chamber. A charged particle enters the chamber and travels undeflected along a straight path with constant speed. What can you say about the initial velocity of the particle?','[{"s": "(a) A magnetic field that varies in magnitude from point to point but has a constant direction (east to west) is set up in a chamber. A charged particle enters the chamber and travels undeflected along a straight path with constant speed. What can you say about the initial velocity of the particle?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28757,70,577,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) A charged particle enters an environment of a strong and non-uniform magnetic field varying from point to point both in magnitude and direction, and comes out of it following a complicated trajectory. Would its final speed equal the initial speed if it suffered no collisions with the environment?','[{"s": "(b) A charged particle enters an environment of a strong and non-uniform magnetic field varying from point to point both in magnitude and direction, and comes out of it following a complicated trajectory. Would its final speed equal the initial speed if it suffered no collisions with the environment?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28758,70,578,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) An electron travelling west to east enters a chamber having a uniform electrostatic field in north to south direction. Specify the direction in which a uniform magnetic field should be set up to prevent the electron from deflecting from its straight line path.','[{"s": "(c) An electron travelling west to east enters a chamber having a uniform electrostatic field in north to south direction. Specify the direction in which a uniform magnetic field should be set up to prevent the electron from deflecting from its straight line path.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28759,70,579,'paragraph',NULL,'4.19. An electron emitted by a heated cathode and accelerated through a potential difference of 2.0 kV, enters a region with uniform magnetic field of 0.15 T. Determine the trajectory of the electron if the field (a) is transverse to its initial velocity, (b) makes an angle of 30°C with the initial velocity.','[{"s": "4.19. An electron emitted by a heated cathode and accelerated through a potential difference of 2.0 kV, enters a region with uniform magnetic field of 0.15 T. Determine the trajectory of the electron if the field (a) is transverse to its initial velocity, (b) makes an angle of 30°C with the initial velocity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28760,70,580,'paragraph',NULL,'4.20 A magnetic field set up using Helmholtz coils (described in Exercise 4.16) is uniform in a small region and has a magnitude of 0.75 T. In the same region, a uniform electrostatic field is maintained in a direction normal to the common axis of the coils. A narrow beam of (single species) charged particles all accelerated through 15 kV enters this region in a direction perpendicular to both the axis of the coils and electrostatic field. If the beam remains undeflected when the electrostatic field is 9.0×10 −5 V m −1 , make a simple guess as to what the beam contains. Why is the answer not unique?','[{"s": "4.20 A magnetic field set up using Helmholtz coils (described in Exercise 4.16) is uniform in a small region and has a magnitude of 0.75 T. In the same region, a uniform electrostatic field is maintained in a direction normal to the common axis of the coils. A narrow beam of (single species) charged particles all accelerated through 15 kV enters this region in a direction perpendicular to both the axis of the coils and electrostatic field. If the beam remains undeflected when the electrostatic field is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9.0×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", make a simple guess as to what the beam contains. Why is the answer not unique?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28761,70,581,'paragraph',NULL,'4.21 A straight horizontal conducting rod of length 0.45 m and mass 60 g is suspended by two vertical wires at its ends. A current of 5.0 A is set up in the rod through the wires. (a) What magnetic field should be set up normal to the conductor in order that the tension in the wires is zero? (b) What will be the total tension in the wires if the direction of current is reversed keeping the magnetic field same as before? (Ignore the mass of the wires.) g=9.8 ms -2 .','[{"s": "4.21 A straight horizontal conducting rod of length 0.45 m and mass 60 g is suspended by two vertical wires at its ends. A current of 5.0 A is set up in the rod through the wires. (a) What magnetic field should be set up normal to the conductor in order that the tension in the wires is zero? (b) What will be the total tension in the wires if the direction of current is reversed keeping the magnetic field same as before? (Ignore the mass of the wires.) g=9.8 ms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28762,70,582,'paragraph',NULL,'4.22 The wires which connect the battery of an automobile to its starting motor carry a current of 300 A (for a short time). What is the force per unit length between the wires if they are 70 cm long and 1.5 cm apart? Is the force attractive or repulsive?','[{"s": "4.22 The wires which connect the battery of an automobile to its starting motor carry a current of 300 A (for a short time). What is the force per unit length between the wires if they are 70 cm long and 1.5 cm apart? Is the force attractive or repulsive?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28763,70,583,'paragraph',NULL,'4.23 A uniform magnetic field of 1.5 T exists in a cylindrical region of radius 10.0 cm, its direction parallel to its axis along east to west. A wire carrying current of 7.0 A in the north to south direction passes through this region. What is the magnitude and direction of the force on the wire if,','[{"s": "4.23 A uniform magnetic field of 1.5 T exists in a cylindrical region of radius 10.0 cm, its direction parallel to its axis along east to west. A wire carrying current of 7.0 A in the north to south direction passes through this region. What is the magnitude and direction of the force on the wire if,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28764,70,584,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) the wire intersects the axis,','[{"s": "(a) the wire intersects the axis,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28765,70,585,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) the wire is turned from N-S to northeast-northwest direction,','[{"s": "(b) the wire is turned from N-S to northeast-northwest direction,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28766,70,586,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) the wire in the N-S direction is lowered from the axis by a distance of 6.0 cm?','[{"s": "(c) the wire in the N-S direction is lowered from the axis by a distance of 6.0 cm?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
