(28861,70,681,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image52.png'),
(28862,70,682,'paragraph',NULL,'4.29 Five long wires A, B, C, D, and E, each carrying current I are arranged to form edges of a pentagonal prism as shown in Fig. 4.6. Each carries current out of the plane of the paper.','[{"s": "4.29 Five long wires A, B, C, D, and E, each carrying current I are arranged to form edges of a pentagonal prism as shown in Fig. 4.6. Each carries current out of the plane of the paper.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28863,70,683,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) What will be magnetic induction at a point on the axis O? Axis is at a distance R from each wire.','[{"s": "(a) What will be magnetic induction at a point on the axis O? Axis is at a distance R from each wire.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28864,70,684,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) What will be the field if current in one of the wires (say A) is switched off?','[{"s": "(b) What will be the field if current in one of the wires (say A) is switched off?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28865,70,685,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) What if current in one of the wire (say) A is reversed?','[{"s": "(c) What if current in one of the wire (say) A is reversed?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28866,70,686,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Moving_Charges_and_Magnetism-image47.png'),
(28867,71,0,'heading',2,'Chapter Thirteen',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28868,71,1,'heading',1,'Nuclei',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28869,71,2,'heading',3,'13.1 Introduction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28870,71,3,'paragraph',NULL,'In the previous chapter, we have learnt that in every atom, the positive charge and mass are densely concentrated at the centre of the atom forming its nucleus. The overall dimensions of a nucleus are much smaller than those of an atom. Experiments on scattering of α-particles demonstrated that the radius of a nucleus was smaller than the radius of an atom by a factor of about 10 4 . This means the volume of a nucleus is about 10 –12 times the volume of the atom. In other words, an atom is almost empty. If an atom is enlarged to the size of a classroom, the nucleus would be of the size of pinhead. Nevertheless, the nucleus contains most (more than 99.9%) of the mass of an atom.','[{"s": "In the previous chapter, we have learnt that in every atom, the positive charge and mass are densely concentrated at the centre of the atom forming its nucleus. The overall dimensions of a nucleus are much smaller than those of an atom. Experiments on scattering of α-particles demonstrated that the radius of a nucleus was smaller than the radius of an atom by a factor of about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This means the volume of a nucleus is about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "times the volume of the atom. In other words, an atom is almost empty. If an atom is enlarged to the size of a classroom, the nucleus would be of the size of pinhead. Nevertheless, the nucleus contains most (more than 99.9%) of the mass of an atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28871,71,4,'paragraph',NULL,'Does the nucleus have a structure, just as the atom does? If so, what are the constituents of the nucleus? How are these held together? In this chapter, we shall look for answers to such questions. We shall discuss various properties of nuclei such as their size, mass and stability, and also associated nuclear phenomena such as radioactivity, fission and fusion.','[{"s": "Does the nucleus have a structure, just as the atom does? If so, what are the constituents of the nucleus? How are these held together? In this chapter, we shall look for answers to such questions. We shall discuss various properties of nuclei such as their size, mass and stability, and also associated nuclear phenomena such as radioactivity, fission and fusion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28872,71,5,'heading',3,'13.2 Atomic Masses and Composition of Nucleus',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28873,71,6,'paragraph',NULL,'The mass of an atom is very small, compared to a kilogram; for example, the mass of a carbon atom, 12 C, is 1.992647 × 10 –26 kg. Kilogram is not a very convenient unit to measure such small quantities. Therefore, a different mass unit is used for expressing atomic masses. This unit is the atomic mass unit (u), defined as 1/12 th of the mass of the carbon ( 12 C) atom . According to this definition','[{"s": "The mass of an atom is very small, compared to a kilogram; for example, the mass of a carbon atom,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C, is 1.992647 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–26", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg. Kilogram is not a very convenient unit to measure such small quantities. Therefore, a different mass unit is used for expressing atomic masses. This unit is the atomic mass unit (u), defined as 1/12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the mass of the carbon (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C) atom", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "According to this definition", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28874,71,7,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image85.png'),
(28875,71,8,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image84.png'),
(28876,71,9,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image63.png (13.1)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image63.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28877,71,10,'paragraph',NULL,'The atomic masses of various elements expressed in atomic mass unit (u) are close to being integral multiples of the mass of a hydrogen atom. There are, however, many striking exceptions to this rule. For example, the atomic mass of chlorine atom is 35.46 u.','[{"s": "The atomic masses of various elements expressed in atomic mass unit (u) are close to being integral multiples of the mass of a hydrogen atom. There are, however, many striking exceptions to this rule. For example, the atomic mass of chlorine atom is 35.46 u.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28879,71,12,'paragraph',NULL,'= Nuclei-image41.png','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image41.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28880,71,13,'paragraph',NULL,'= 35.47 u','[{"s": "= 35.47 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28881,71,14,'paragraph',NULL,'which agrees with the atomic mass of chlorine.','[{"s": "which agrees with the atomic mass of chlorine.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28882,71,15,'paragraph',NULL,'Even the lightest element, hydrogen has three isotopes having masses 1.0078 u, 2.0141 u, and 3.0160 u. The nucleus of the lightest atom of hydrogen, which has a relative abundance of 99.985%, is called the proton. The mass of a proton is','[{"s": "Even the lightest element, hydrogen has three isotopes having masses 1.0078 u, 2.0141 u, and 3.0160 u. The nucleus of the lightest atom of hydrogen, which has a relative abundance of 99.985%, is called the proton. The mass of a proton is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28883,71,16,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image148.png (13.2)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image148.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28884,71,17,'paragraph',NULL,'This is equal to the mass of the hydrogen atom (= 1.00783u), minus the mass of a single electron ( m e = 0.00055 u). The other two isotopes of hydrogen are called deuterium and tritium. Tritium nuclei, being unstable, do not occur naturally and are produced artificially in laboratories.','[{"s": "This is equal to the mass of the hydrogen atom (= 1.00783u), minus the mass of a single electron (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.00055 u). The other two isotopes of hydrogen are called deuterium and tritium. Tritium nuclei, being unstable, do not occur naturally and are produced artificially in laboratories.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28885,71,18,'paragraph',NULL,'The positive charge in the nucleus is that of the protons. A proton carries one unit of fundamental charge and is stable. It was earlier thought that the nucleus may contain electrons, but this was ruled out later using arguments based on quantum theory. All the electrons of an atom are outside the nucleus. We know that the number of these electrons outside the nucleus of the atom is Z , the atomic number. The total charge of the atomic electrons is thus (– Ze ), and since the atom is neutral, the charge of the nucleus is (+ Ze ). The number of protons in the nucleus of the atom is, therefore, exactly Z , the atomic number.','[{"s": "The positive charge in the nucleus is that of the protons. A proton carries one unit of fundamental charge and is stable. It was earlier thought that the nucleus may contain electrons, but this was ruled out later using arguments based on quantum theory. All the electrons of an atom are outside the nucleus. We know that the number of these electrons outside the nucleus of the atom is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the atomic number. The total charge of the atomic electrons is thus (–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ze", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), and since the atom is neutral, the charge of the nucleus is (+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ze", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). The number of protons in the nucleus of the atom is, therefore, exactly", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the atomic number.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28886,71,19,'heading',4,'Discovery of Neutron',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28887,71,20,'paragraph',NULL,'Since the nuclei of deuterium and tritium are isotopes of hydrogen, they must contain only one proton each. But the masses of the nuclei of hydrogen, deuterium and tritium are in the ratio of 1:2:3. Therefore, the nuclei of deuterium and tritium must contain, in addition to a proton, some neutral matter. The amount of neutral matter present in the nuclei of these isotopes, expressed in units of mass of a proton, is approximately equal to one and two, respectively. This fact indicates that the nuclei of atoms contain, in addition to protons, neutral matter in multiples of a basic unit. This hypothesis was verified in 1932 by James Chadwick who observed emission of neutral radiation when beryllium nuclei were bombarded with alpha-particles (α-particles are helium nuclei, to be discussed in a later section). It was found that this neutral radiation could knock out protons from light nuclei such as those of helium, carbon and nitrogen. The only neutral radiation known at that time was photons (electromagnetic radiation). Application of the principles of conservation of energy and momentum showed that if the neutral radiation consisted of photons, the energy of photons would have to be much higher than is available from the bombardment of beryllium nuclei with α-particles. The clue to this puzzle, which Chadwick satisfactorily solved, was to assume that the neutral radiation consists of a new type of neutral particles called neutrons . From conservation of energy and momentum, he was able to determine the mass of new particle ‘as very nearly the same as mass of proton’.','[{"s": "Since the nuclei of deuterium and tritium are isotopes of hydrogen, they must contain only one proton each. But the masses of the nuclei of hydrogen, deuterium and tritium are in the ratio of 1:2:3. Therefore, the nuclei of deuterium and tritium must contain, in addition to a proton, some neutral matter. The amount of neutral matter present in the nuclei of these isotopes, expressed in units of mass of a proton, is approximately equal to one and two, respectively. This fact indicates that the nuclei of atoms contain, in addition to protons, neutral matter in multiples of a basic unit. This hypothesis was verified in 1932 by James Chadwick who observed emission of neutral radiation when beryllium nuclei were bombarded with alpha-particles (α-particles are helium nuclei, to be discussed in a later section). It was found that this neutral radiation could knock out protons from light nuclei such as those of helium, carbon and nitrogen. The only neutral radiation known at that time was photons (electromagnetic radiation). Application of the principles of conservation of energy and momentum showed that if the neutral radiation consisted of photons, the energy of photons would have to be much higher than is available from the bombardment of beryllium nuclei with α-particles. The clue to this puzzle, which Chadwick satisfactorily solved, was to assume that the neutral radiation consists of a new type of neutral particles called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "neutrons", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". From conservation of energy and momentum, he was able to determine the mass of new particle ‘as very nearly the same as mass of proton’.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28888,71,21,'paragraph',NULL,'The mass of a neutron is now known to a high degree of accuracy. It is','[{"s": "The mass of a neutron is now known to a high degree of accuracy. It is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28889,71,22,'paragraph',NULL,'m n = 1.00866 u = 1.6749×10 –27 kg (13.3)','[{"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.00866 u = 1.6749×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–27", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg (13.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28890,71,23,'paragraph',NULL,'Chadwick was awarded the 1935 Nobel Prize in Physics for his discovery of the neutron.','[{"s": "Chadwick was awarded the 1935 Nobel Prize in Physics for his discovery of the neutron.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28891,71,24,'paragraph',NULL,'A free neutron, unlike a free proton, is unstable. It decays into a proton, an electron and a antineutrino (another elementary particle), and has a mean life of about 1000s. It is, however, stable inside the nucleus.','[{"s": "A free neutron, unlike a free proton, is unstable. It decays into a proton, an electron and a antineutrino (another elementary particle), and has a mean life of about 1000s. It is, however, stable inside the nucleus.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28893,71,26,'paragraph',NULL,'Z - atomic number = number of protons [13.4(a)]','[{"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atomic number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= number of protons [13.4(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28894,71,27,'paragraph',NULL,'N - neutron number = number of neutrons [13.4(b)]','[{"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "neutron number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= number of neutrons [13.4(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28895,71,28,'paragraph',NULL,'A - mass number = Z + N = total number of protons and neutrons [13.4(c)]','[{"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mass number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= total number of protons and neutrons [13.4(c)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28896,71,29,'paragraph',NULL,'One also uses the term nucleon for a proton or a neutron. Thus the number of nucleons in an atom is its mass number A.','[{"s": "One also uses the term nucleon for a proton or a neutron. Thus the number of nucleons in an atom is its mass number A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28897,71,30,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclear species or nuclides are shown by the notation Nuclei-image133.png where X is the chemical symbol of the species. For example, the nucleus of gold is denoted by Nuclei-image62.png . It contains 197 nucleons, of which 79 are protons and the rest118 are neutrons.','[{"s": "Nuclear species or nuclides are shown by the notation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image133.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "where X is the chemical symbol of the species. For example, the nucleus of gold is denoted by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image62.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". It contains 197 nucleons, of which 79 are protons and the rest118 are neutrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28898,71,31,'paragraph',NULL,'The composition of isotopes of an element can now be readily explained. The nuclei of isotopes of a given element contain the same number of protons, but differ from each other in their number of neutrons. Deuterium, Nuclei-image90.png , which is an isotope of hydrogen, contains one proton and one neutron. Its other isotope tritium, Nuclei-image43.png , contains one proton and two neutrons. The element gold has 32 isotopes, ranging from A =173 to A = 204. We have already mentioned that chemical properties of elements depend on their electronic structure. As the atoms of isotopes have identical electronic structure they have identical chemical behaviour and are placed in the same location in the periodic table.','[{"s": "The composition of isotopes of an element can now be readily explained. The nuclei of isotopes of a given element contain the same number of protons, but differ from each other in their number of neutrons. Deuterium,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image90.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", which is an isotope of hydrogen, contains one proton and one neutron. Its other isotope tritium,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image43.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", contains one proton and two neutrons. The element gold has 32 isotopes, ranging from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=173 to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 204. We have already mentioned that chemical properties of elements depend on their electronic structure. As the atoms of isotopes have identical electronic structure they have identical chemical behaviour and are placed in the same location in the periodic table.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28899,71,32,'paragraph',NULL,'All nuclides with same mass number A are called isobars . For example, the nuclides Nuclei-image43.png and Nuclei-image65.png are isobars. Nuclides with same neutron number N but different atomic number Z , for example Nuclei-image83.png and Nuclei-image135.png , are called isotones .','[{"s": "All nuclides with same mass number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "isobars", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For example, the nuclides", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image43.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image65.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "are isobars. Nuclides with same neutron number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "but different atomic number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", for example", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image83.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image135.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "isotones", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28900,71,33,'heading',3,'13.3 Size of the Nucleus',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28901,71,34,'paragraph',NULL,'As we have seen in Chapter 12, Rutherford was the pioneer who postulated and established the existence of the atomic nucleus. At Rutherford’s suggestion, Geiger and Marsden performed their classic experiment: on the scattering of α-particles from thin gold foils. Their experiments revealed that the distance of closest approach to a gold nucleus of an α-particle of kinetic energy 5.5 MeV is about 4.0 × 10 –14 m. The scattering of α-particle by the gold sheet could be understood by Rutherford by assuming that the coulomb repulsive force was solely responsible for scattering. Since the positive charge is confined to the nucleus, the actual size of the nucleus has to be less than 4.0 × 10 –14 m.','[{"s": "As we have seen in Chapter 12, Rutherford was the pioneer who postulated and established the existence of the atomic nucleus. At Rutherford’s suggestion, Geiger and Marsden performed their classic experiment: on the scattering of α-particles from thin gold foils. Their experiments revealed that the distance of closest approach to a gold nucleus of an α-particle of kinetic energy 5.5 MeV is about 4.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. The scattering of α-particle by the gold sheet could be understood by Rutherford by assuming that the coulomb repulsive force was solely responsible for scattering. Since the positive charge is confined to the nucleus, the actual size of the nucleus has to be less than 4.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28892,71,25,'paragraph',NULL,'The composition of a nucleus can now be described using the following terms and symbols:','[{"s": "The composition of a nucleus can now be described using the following terms and symbols:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000045376", "2000046119", "2000111990"]',NULL),
(28902,71,35,'paragraph',NULL,'If we use α-particles of higher energies than 5.5 MeV, the distance of closest approach to the gold nucleus will be smaller and at some point the scattering will begin to be affected by the short range nuclear forces, and differ from Rutherford’s calculations. Rutherford’s calculations are based on pure coulomb repulsion between the positive charges of the α-particle and the gold nucleus. From the distance at which deviations set in, nuclear sizes can be inferred.','[{"s": "If we use α-particles of higher energies than 5.5 MeV, the distance of closest approach to the gold nucleus will be smaller and at some point the scattering will begin to be affected by the short range nuclear forces, and differ from Rutherford’s calculations. Rutherford’s calculations are based on pure coulomb repulsion between the positive charges of the α-particle and the gold nucleus. From the distance at which deviations set in, nuclear sizes can be inferred.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28903,71,36,'paragraph',NULL,'By performing scattering experiments in which fast electrons, instead of α-particles, are projectiles that bombard targets made up of various elements, the sizes of nuclei of various elements have been accurately measured.','[{"s": "By performing scattering experiments in which fast electrons, instead of α-particles, are projectiles that bombard targets made up of various elements, the sizes of nuclei of various elements have been accurately measured.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28905,71,38,'paragraph',NULL,'R = R 0 A 1/3 (13.5)','[{"s": "R = R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(13.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28906,71,39,'paragraph',NULL,'where R 0 = 1.2 × 10 –15 m (=1.2 fm; 1 fm = 10 –15 m). This means the volume of the nucleus, which is proportional to R 3 is proportional to A . Thus the density of nucleus is a constant, independent of A, for all nuclei. Different nuclei are like a drop of liquid of constant density. The density of nuclear matter is approximately 2.3 × 10 17 kg m –3 . This density is very large compared to ordinary matter, say water, which is 10 3 kg m –3 . This is understandable, as we have already seen that most of the atom is empty. Ordinary matter consisting of atoms has a large amount of empty space.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.2 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m (=1.2 fm; 1 fm = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m). This means the volume of the nucleus, which is proportional to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is proportional to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus the density of nucleus is a constant, independent of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for all nuclei. Different nuclei are like a drop of liquid of constant density. The density of nuclear matter is approximately 2.3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This density is very large compared to ordinary matter, say water, which is 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This is understandable, as we have already seen that most of the atom is empty. Ordinary matter consisting of atoms has a large amount of empty space.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28907,71,40,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.1 Given the mass of iron nucleus as 55.85u and A=56, find the nuclear density?','[{"s": "Example 13.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Given the mass of iron nucleus as 55.85u and A=56, find the nuclear density?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
