(28909,71,42,'paragraph',NULL,'m Fe = 55.85, u = 9.27 × 10 –26 kg','[{"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Fe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 55.85, u = 9.27 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–26", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28910,71,43,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclear density = Nuclei-image146.png = Nuclei-image53.png','[{"s": "Nuclear density =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image146.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image53.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28911,71,44,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2.29 × 10 17 kg m –3','[{"s": "= 2.29 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28912,71,45,'paragraph',NULL,'The density of matter in neutron stars (an astrophysical object) is comparable to this density. This shows that matter in these objects has been compressed to such an extent that they resemble a big nucleus.','[{"s": "The density of matter in neutron stars (an astrophysical object) is comparable to this density. This shows that matter in these objects has been compressed to such an extent that they resemble a big nucleus.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28913,71,46,'heading',3,'13.4 Mass-Energy and Nuclear Binding Energy',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28914,71,47,'heading',4,'13.4.1 Mass – Energy',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28915,71,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Einstein showed from his theory of special relativity that it is necessary to treat mass as another form of energy. Before the advent of this theory of special relativity it was presumed that mass and energy were conserved separately in a reaction. However, Einstein showed that mass is another form of energy and one can convert mass-energy into other forms of energy, say kinetic energy and vice-versa.','[{"s": "Einstein showed from his theory of special relativity that it is necessary to treat mass as another form of energy. Before the advent of this theory of special relativity it was presumed that mass and energy were conserved separately in a reaction. However, Einstein showed that mass is another form of energy and one can convert mass-energy into other forms of energy, say kinetic energy and vice-versa.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28916,71,49,'paragraph',NULL,'Einstein gave the famous mass-energy equivalence relation','[{"s": "Einstein gave the famous mass-energy equivalence relation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28917,71,50,'paragraph',NULL,'E = mc 2 (13.6)','[{"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(13.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28904,71,37,'paragraph',NULL,'It has been found that a nucleus of mass number A has a radius','[{"s": "It has been found that a nucleus of mass number", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "has a radius", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000045431", "2000046123", "2000111991"]',NULL),
(28918,71,51,'paragraph',NULL,'Here the energy equivalent of mass m is related by the above equation and c is the velocity of light in vacuum and is approximately equal to 3×10 8 m s –1','[{"s": "Here the energy equivalent of mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is related by the above equation and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the velocity of light in vacuum and is approximately equal to 3×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28920,71,53,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28921,71,54,'paragraph',NULL,'Energy, E = 10 –3 × ( 3 × 10 8 ) 2 J','[{"s": "Energy, E = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× ( 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28922,71,55,'paragraph',NULL,'E = 10 –3 × 9 × 10 16 = 9 × 10 13 J','[{"s": "E = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× 9 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 9 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28923,71,56,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, if one gram of matter is converted to energy, there is a release of enormous amount of energy.','[{"s": "Thus, if one gram of matter is converted to energy, there is a release of enormous amount of energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28924,71,57,'paragraph',NULL,'Experimental verification of the Einstein’s mass-energy relation has been achieved in the study of nuclear reactions amongst nucleons, nuclei, electrons and other more recently discovered particles. In a reaction the conservation law of energy states that the initial energy and the final energy are equal provided the energy associated with mass is also included. This concept is important in understanding nuclear masses and the interaction of nuclei with one another. They form the subject matter of the next few sections.','[{"s": "Experimental verification of the Einstein’s mass-energy relation has been achieved in the study of nuclear reactions amongst nucleons, nuclei, electrons and other more recently discovered particles. In a reaction the conservation law of energy states that the initial energy and the final energy are equal provided the energy associated with mass is also included. This concept is important in understanding nuclear masses and the interaction of nuclei with one another. They form the subject matter of the next few sections.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28925,71,58,'heading',4,'13.4.2 Nuclear binding energy',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28926,71,59,'paragraph',NULL,'In Section 13.2 we have seen that the nucleus is made up of neutrons and protons. Therefore it may be expected that the mass of the nucleus is equal to the total mass of its individual protons and neutrons. However, the nuclear mass M is found to be always less than this . For example, let us consider Nuclei-image28.png ; a nucleus which has 8 neutrons and 8 protons. We have','[{"s": "In Section 13.2 we have seen that the nucleus is made up of neutrons and protons. Therefore it may be expected that the mass of the nucleus is equal to the total mass of its individual protons and neutrons. However, the nuclear mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is found to be always less than this", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For example, let us consider", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image28.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "; a nucleus which has 8 neutrons and 8 protons. We have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28927,71,60,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass of 8 neutrons = 8 × 1.00866 u','[{"s": "Mass of 8 neutrons = 8 × 1.00866 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28928,71,61,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass of 8 protons = 8 × 1.00727 u','[{"s": "Mass of 8 protons = 8 × 1.00727 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28929,71,62,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass of 8 electrons = 8 × 0.00055 u','[{"s": "Mass of 8 electrons = 8 × 0.00055 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28930,71,63,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore the expected mass of Nuclei-image28.png nucleus','[{"s": "Therefore the expected mass of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image28.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "nucleus", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28931,71,64,'paragraph',NULL,'= 8 × 2.01593 u = 16.12744 u.','[{"s": "= 8 × 2.01593 u = 16.12744 u.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28932,71,65,'paragraph',NULL,'The atomic mass of Nuclei-image96.png found from mass spectroscopy experiments is seen to be 15.99493 u. Substracting the mass of 8 electrons (8 × 0.00055 u) from this, we get the experimental mass of Nuclei-image88.png nucleus to be 15.99053 u.','[{"s": "The atomic mass of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image96.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "found from mass spectroscopy experiments is seen to be 15.99493 u. Substracting the mass of 8 electrons (8 × 0.00055 u) from this, we get the experimental mass of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image88.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "nucleus to be 15.99053 u.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28919,71,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.2 Calculate the energy equivalent of 1 g of substance.','[{"s": "Example 13.2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Calculate the energy equivalent of 1 g of substance.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000045380", "2000046127", "2000111993"]',NULL),
(35052,80,485,'paragraph',NULL,'15.6 A bat emits an ultrasonic sound of frequency 1000 kHz in air . If the sound meets a water surface, what is the wavelength of (a) the reflected sound, (b) the transmitted sound? Speed of sound in air is 340 m/sec and in water 1486 m/sec.','[{"s": "15.6 A bat emits an ultrasonic sound of frequency 1000 kHz in air", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "If the sound meets a water surface, what is the wavelength of (a) the reflected sound, (b) the transmitted sound? Speed of sound in air is 340 m/sec and in water 1486 m/sec.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041141"]',NULL),
(35083,80,516,'paragraph',NULL,'15.15 A one metre-long tube open at one end, with a movable piston at the other end, shows resonance with a fixed frequency source (a tuning fork of frequency 340 Hz) when the tube length is 25.5 cm or 79.3 cm. Estimate the speed of sound in air at the temperature of the experiment. The edge effects may be neglected.','[{"s": "15.15 A one metre-long tube open at one end, with a movable piston at the other end, shows resonance with a fixed frequency source (a tuning fork of frequency 340 Hz) when the tube length is 25.5 cm or 79.3 cm.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Estimate the speed of sound in air at the temperature of the experiment. The edge effects may be neglected.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041145"]',NULL),
(28934,71,67,'paragraph',NULL,'What is the meaning of the mass defect? It is here that Einstein’s equivalence of mass and energy plays a role. Since the mass of the oxygen nucleus is less that the sum of the masses of its constituents (8 protons and 8 neutrons, in the unbound state), the equivalent energy of the oxygen nucleus is less than that of the sum of the equivalent energies of its constituents. If one wants to break the oxygen nucleus into 8 protons and 8 neutrons, this extra energy ∆ M c 2 , has to supplied. This energy required E b is related to the mass defect by','[{"s": "What is the meaning of the mass defect? It is here that Einstein’s equivalence of mass and energy plays a role. Since the mass of the oxygen nucleus is less that the sum of the masses of its constituents (8 protons and 8 neutrons, in the unbound state), the equivalent energy of the oxygen nucleus is less than that of the sum of the equivalent energies of its constituents. If one wants to break the oxygen nucleus into 8 protons and 8 neutrons, this extra energy ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", has to supplied. This energy required", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is related to the mass defect by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28935,71,68,'paragraph',NULL,'E b = ∆ M c 2 (13.8)','[{"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(13.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
