(28948,71,81,'paragraph',NULL,'The energy needed to separate Nuclei-image69.png into its constituents is thus 127.5 MeV/c2.','[{"s": "The energy needed to separate", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image69.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "into its constituents is thus 127.5 MeV/c2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28949,71,82,'paragraph',NULL,'If a certain number of neutrons and protons are brought together to form a nucleus of a certain charge and mass, an energy E b will be released in the process. The energy E b is called the binding energy of the nucleus. If we separate a nucleus into its nucleons, we would have to supply a total energy equal to E b , to those particles. Although we cannot tear apart a nucleus in this way, the nuclear binding energy is still a convenient measure of how well a nucleus is held together. A more useful measure of the binding between the constituents of the nucleus is the binding energy per nucleon , E bn , which is the ratio of the binding energy E b of a nucleus to the number of the nucleons, A, in that nucleus:','[{"s": "If a certain number of neutrons and protons are brought together to form a nucleus of a certain charge and mass, an energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will be released in the process. The energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "binding energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the nucleus. If we separate a nucleus into its nucleons, we would have to supply a total energy equal to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", to those particles. Although we cannot tear apart a nucleus in this way, the nuclear binding energy is still a convenient measure of how well a nucleus is held together. A more useful measure of the binding between the constituents of the nucleus is the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "binding energy per nucleon", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bn", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which is the ratio of the binding energy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of a nucleus to the number of the nucleons, A, in that nucleus:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28950,71,83,'paragraph',NULL,'E bn = E b / A (13.9)','[{"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(13.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28951,71,84,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image51.png'),
(28953,71,86,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 13.1 is a plot of the binding energy per nucleon E bn versus the mass number A','[{"s": "Figure 13.1 is a plot of the binding energy per nucleon", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "versus the mass number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28954,71,87,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) the binding energy per nucleon, E bn , is practically constant, i.e. practically independent of the atomic number for nuclei of middle mass number ( 30 < A < 170). The curve has a maximum of about 8.75 MeV for A = 56 and has a value of 7.6 MeV for A = 238.','[{"s": "(i) the binding energy per nucleon,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bn", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", is practically constant, i.e. practically independent of the atomic number for nuclei of middle mass number ( 30 < A < 170). The curve has a maximum of about 8.75 MeV for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 56 and has a value of 7.6 MeV for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 238.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28955,71,88,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii ) E bn is lower for both light nuclei ( A <30) and heavy nuclei ( A >170).','[{"s": "(ii", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is lower for both light nuclei (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<30) and heavy nuclei (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">170).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28956,71,89,'paragraph',NULL,'We can draw some conclusions from these two observations:','[{"s": "We can draw some conclusions from these two observations:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28957,71,90,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The force is attractive and sufficiently strong to produce a binding energy of a few MeV per nucleon.','[{"s": "(i) The force is attractive and sufficiently strong to produce a binding energy of a few MeV per nucleon.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28958,71,91,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) The constancy of the binding energy in the range 30 < A < 170 is a consequence of the fact that the nuclear force is short-ranged. Consider a particular nucleon inside a sufficiently large nucleus. It will be under the influence of only some of its neighbours, which come within the range of the nuclear force. If any other nucleon is at a distance more than the range of the nuclear force from the particular nucleon it will have no influence on the binding energy of the nucleon under consideration. If a nucleon can have a maximum of p neighbours within the range of nuclear force, its binding energy would be proportional to p . Let the binding energy of the nucleus be pk , where k is a constant having the dimensions of energy. If we increase A by adding nucleons they will not change the binding energy of a nucleon inside. Since most of the nucleons in a large nucleus reside inside it and not on the surface, the change in binding energy per nucleon would be small. The binding energy per nucleon is a constant and is approximately equal to pk . The property that a given nucleon influences only nucleons close to it is also referred to as saturation property of the nuclear force.','[{"s": "(ii) The constancy of the binding energy in the range 30 < A < 170 is a consequence of the fact that the nuclear force is short-ranged. Consider a particular nucleon inside a sufficiently large nucleus. It will be under the influence of only some of its neighbours, which come within the range of the nuclear force. If any other nucleon is at a distance more than the range of the nuclear force from the particular nucleon it will have no influence on the binding energy of the nucleon under consideration. If a nucleon can have a maximum of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "neighbours within the range of nuclear force, its binding energy would be proportional to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Let the binding energy of the nucleus be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pk", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a constant having the dimensions of energy. If we increase", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by adding nucleons they will not change the binding energy of a nucleon inside. Since most of the nucleons in a large nucleus reside inside it and not on the surface, the change in binding energy per nucleon would be small. The binding energy per nucleon is a constant and is approximately equal to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pk", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The property that a given nucleon influences only nucleons close to it is also referred to as saturation property of the nuclear force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28961,71,94,'heading',3,'13.5 Nuclear Force',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28962,71,95,'paragraph',NULL,'The force that determines the motion of atomic electrons is the familiar Coulomb force. In Section 13.4, we have seen that for average mass nuclei the binding energy per nucleon is approximately 8 MeV, which is much larger than the binding energy in atoms. Therefore, to bind a nucleus together there must be a strong attractive force of a totally different kind. It must be strong enough to overcome the repulsion between the (positively charged) protons and to bind both protons and neutrons into the tiny nuclear volume. We have already seen that the constancy of binding energy per nucleon can be understood in terms of its short-range. Many features of the nuclear binding force are summarised below. These are obtained from a variety of experiments carried out during 1930 to 1950.','[{"s": "The force that determines the motion of atomic electrons is the familiar Coulomb force. In Section 13.4, we have seen that for average mass nuclei the binding energy per nucleon is approximately 8 MeV, which is much larger than the binding energy in atoms. Therefore, to bind a nucleus together there must be a strong attractive force of a totally different kind. It must be strong enough to overcome the repulsion between the (positively charged) protons and to bind both protons and neutrons into the tiny nuclear volume. We have already seen that the constancy of binding energy per nucleon can be understood in terms of its short-range. Many features of the nuclear binding force are summarised below. These are obtained from a variety of experiments carried out during 1930 to 1950.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28963,71,96,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image124.png'),
(35154,80,587,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 11: A transverse harmonic wave on a string is described by','[{"s": "Question 11: A transverse harmonic wave on a string is described by", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041165"]',NULL),
(35295,81,58,'paragraph',NULL,'The above equation provides a motivation for the definitions of work and kinetic energy. The left side of the equation is the difference in the quantity ‘half the mass times the square of the speed’ from its initial value to its final value. We call each of these quantities the ‘kinetic energy’, denoted by K. The right side is a product of the displacement and the component of the force along the displacement. This quantity is called ‘work’ and is denoted by W. Eq. (6.2b) is then','[{"s": "The above equation provides a motivation for the definitions of work and kinetic energy. The left side of the equation is the difference in the quantity ‘half the mass times the square of the speed’ from its initial value to its final value. We call each of these quantities the ‘kinetic energy’, denoted by K. The right side is a product of the displacement and the component of the force along the displacement.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This quantity is called ‘work’ and is denoted by W. Eq.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(6.2b) is then", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034237", "2000034296", "2000034316", "2000034380", "2000035382", "2000035409", "2000036231", "2000036235", "2000036238", "2000036243", "2000036247", "2000036278", "2000108489", "2000108498", "2000108526", "2000108601", "2000108611"]',NULL),
(35309,81,72,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) From the work-energy theorem','[{"s": "(b) From the work-energy theorem", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034241", "2000035412", "2000108490", "2000108613"]',NULL),
(28964,71,97,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 13.2 Potential energy of a pair of nucleons as a function of their separation. For a separation greater than r 0 , the force is attractive and for separations less than r 0 , the force is strongly repulsive.','[{"s": "Figure 13.2 Potential energy of a pair of nucleons as a function of their separation. For a separation greater than r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the force is attractive and for separations less than r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the force is strongly repulsive.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28965,71,98,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The nuclear force is much stronger than the Coulomb force acting between charges or the gravitational forces between masses. The nuclear binding force has to dominate over the Coulomb repulsive force between protons inside the nucleus. This happens only because the nuclear force is much stronger than the coulomb force. The gravitational force is much weaker than even Coulomb force.','[{"s": "(i) The nuclear force is much stronger than the Coulomb force acting between charges or the gravitational forces between masses. The nuclear binding force has to dominate over the Coulomb repulsive force between protons inside the nucleus. This happens only because the nuclear force is much stronger than the coulomb force. The gravitational force is much weaker than even Coulomb force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28966,71,99,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) The nuclear force between two nucleons falls rapidly to zero as their distance is more than a few femtometres. This leads to saturation of forces in a medium or a large-sized nucleus, which is the reason for the constancy of the binding energy per nucleon.','[{"s": "(ii) The nuclear force between two nucleons falls rapidly to zero as their distance is more than a few femtometres. This leads to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "saturation of forces", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in a medium or a large-sized nucleus, which is the reason for the constancy of the binding energy per nucleon.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28967,71,100,'paragraph',NULL,'A rough plot of the potential energy between two nucleons as a function of distance is shown in the Fig. 13.2. The potential energy is a minimum at a distance r 0 of about 0.8 fm. This means that the force is attractive for distances larger than 0.8 fm and repulsive if they are separated by distances less than 0.8 fm.','[{"s": "A rough plot of the potential energy between two nucleons as a function of distance is shown in the Fig. 13.2. The potential energy is a minimum at a distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of about 0.8 fm. This means that the force is attractive for distances larger than 0.8 fm and repulsive if they are separated by distances less than 0.8 fm.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28968,71,101,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) The nuclear force between neutron-neutron, proton-neutron and proton-proton is approximately the same. The nuclear force does not depend on the electric charge.','[{"s": "(iii) The nuclear force between neutron-neutron, proton-neutron and proton-proton is approximately the same. The nuclear force does not depend on the electric charge.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28969,71,102,'paragraph',NULL,'Unlike Coulomb’s law or the Newton’s law of gravitation there is no simple mathematical form of the nuclear force.','[{"s": "Unlike Coulomb’s law or the Newton’s law of gravitation there is no simple mathematical form of the nuclear force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28970,71,103,'heading',3,'13.6 Radioactivity',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28971,71,104,'paragraph',NULL,'A. H. Becquerel discovered radioactivity in 1896 purely by accident. While studying the fluorescence and phosphorescence of compounds irradiated with visible light, Becquerel observed an interesting phenomenon. After illuminating some pieces of uranium-potassium sulphate with visible light, he wrapped them in black paper and separated the package from a photographic plate by a piece of silver. When, after several hours of exposure, the photographic plate was developed, it showed blackening due to something that must have been emitted by the compound and was able to penetrate both black paper and the silver.','[{"s": "A. H. Becquerel discovered radioactivity in 1896 purely by accident. While studying the fluorescence and phosphorescence of compounds irradiated with visible light, Becquerel observed an interesting phenomenon. After illuminating some pieces of uranium-potassium sulphate with visible light, he wrapped them in black paper and separated the package from a photographic plate by a piece of silver. When, after several hours of exposure, the photographic plate was developed, it showed blackening due to something that must have been emitted by the compound and was able to penetrate both black paper and the silver.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28972,71,105,'paragraph',NULL,'Experiments performed subsequently showed that radioactivity was a nuclear phenomenon in which an unstable nucleus undergoes a decay. This is referred to as radioactive decay . Three types of radioactive decay occur in nature :','[{"s": "Experiments performed subsequently showed that radioactivity was a nuclear phenomenon in which an unstable nucleus undergoes a decay. This is referred to as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "radioactive decay", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Three types of radioactive decay occur in nature :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28973,71,106,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) α-decay in which a helium nucleus Nuclei-image15.png is emitted;','[{"s": "(i) α-decay in which a helium nucleus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image15.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is emitted;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28975,71,108,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) γ-decay in which high energy (hundreds of keV or more) photons are emitted.','[{"s": "(iii) γ-decay in which high energy (hundreds of keV or more) photons are emitted.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28976,71,109,'paragraph',NULL,'Each of these decay will be considered in subsequent sub-sections.','[{"s": "Each of these decay will be considered in subsequent sub-sections.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28977,71,110,'heading',4,'13.6.1 Law of radioactive decay',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28974,71,107,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) β-decay in which electrons or positrons (particles with the same mass as electrons, but with a charge exactly opposite to that of electron) are emitted;','[{"s": "(ii) β-decay in which electrons or positrons (particles with the same mass as electrons, but with a charge exactly opposite to that of electron) are emitted;", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000045410", "2000046157", "2000112003"]',NULL),
(28979,71,112,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image36.png'),
(28980,71,113,'paragraph',NULL,'or, ∆ N /∆ t = λ N , (13.10)','[{"s": "or, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", (13.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28981,71,114,'paragraph',NULL,'where λ is called the radioactive decay constant or disintegration constant .','[{"s": "where λ is called the radioactive", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decay constant", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "disintegration constant", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28982,71,115,'paragraph',NULL,'The change in the number of nuclei in the sample* is d N = – ∆ N in time ∆ t . Thus the rate of change of N is (in the limit ∆ t → 0)','[{"s": "The change in the number of nuclei in the sample* is d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= – ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in time ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus the rate of change of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is (in the limit ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 0)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28983,71,116,'paragraph',NULL,'* ∆N is the number of nuclei that decay, and hence is always positive. dN is the change in N, which may have either sign. Here it is negative, because out of original N nuclei, ∆N have decayed, leaving (N–∆N) nuclei.','[{"s": "* ∆N is the number of nuclei that decay, and hence is always positive. dN is the change in N, which may have either sign. Here it is negative, because out of original N nuclei, ∆N have decayed, leaving (N–∆N) nuclei.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28984,71,117,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image143.png'),
(28985,71,118,'paragraph',NULL,'or, Nuclei-image23.png = –λdt','[{"s": "or,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image23.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= –λdt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28987,71,120,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image81.png (13.11)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image81.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(28988,71,121,'paragraph',NULL,'or, ln N − ln N 0 = −λ ( t – t 0 ) (13.12)','[{"s": "or, ln", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "− ln", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= −λ (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (13.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
