(29035,71,168,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29036,71,169,'paragraph',NULL,'By definition of half-life, half of the initial sample will remain undecayed after 12.5 y. In the next 12.5 y, one-half of these nuclei would have decayed. Hence, one fourth of the sample of the initial pure tritium will remain undecayed.','[{"s": "By definition of half-life, half of the initial sample will remain undecayed after 12.5 y. In the next 12.5 y, one-half of these nuclei would have decayed. Hence, one fourth of the sample of the initial pure tritium will remain undecayed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29037,71,170,'heading',4,'13.6.2 Alpha decay',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29034,71,167,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.5 Tritium has a half-life of 12.5 y undergoing beta decay.','[{"s": "Example 13.5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Tritium has a half-life of 12.5 y undergoing beta decay.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]','marked','["2000045540", "2000046501", "2000112021"]',NULL),
(35618,81,381,'paragraph',NULL,'where v is the instantaneous velocity when the force is F .','[{"s": "where", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "v", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is the instantaneous velocity when the force is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "F", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000035052", "2000108474", "2000108600"]',NULL),
(29040,71,173,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image9.png → Nuclei-image17.png + Nuclei-image14.png (13.20)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image9.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image17.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29041,71,174,'paragraph',NULL,'From Einstein’s mass-energy equivalance relation [Eq. (13.6)] and energy conservation, it is clear that this spontaneous decay is possible only when the total mass of the decay products is less than the mass of the initial nucleus. This difference in mass appears as kinetic energy of the products. By referring to a table of nuclear masses, one can check that the total mass of Nuclei-image26.png and Nuclei-image14.png is indeed less than that of Nuclei-image47.png .','[{"s": "From Einstein’s mass-energy equivalance relation [Eq. (13.6)] and energy conservation, it is clear that this spontaneous decay is possible only when the total mass of the decay products is less than the mass of the initial nucleus. This difference in mass appears as kinetic energy of the products. By referring to a table of nuclear masses, one can check that the total mass of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image26.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is indeed less than that of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image47.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29042,71,175,'paragraph',NULL,'The disintegration energy or the Q -value of a nuclear reaction is the difference between the initial mass energy and the total mass energy of the decay products. For α-decay','[{"s": "The disintegration energy or the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-value of a nuclear reaction is the difference between the initial mass energy and the total mass energy of the decay products. For α-decay", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29043,71,176,'paragraph',NULL,'Q = ( m X – m Y – m He ) c 2 (13.21)','[{"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "He", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(13.21)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29044,71,177,'paragraph',NULL,'Q is also the net kinetic energy gained in the process or, if the initial nucleus X is at rest, the kinetic energy of the products. Clearly, Q > 0 for exothermic processes such as α-decay.','[{"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is also the net kinetic energy gained in the process or, if the initial nucleus X is at rest, the kinetic energy of the products. Clearly,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> 0 for exothermic processes such as α-decay.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29045,71,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.6 We are given the following atomic masses:','[{"s": "Example 13.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We are given the following atomic masses:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29046,71,179,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image49.png = 238.05079 u Nuclei-image2.png = 4.00260 u','[{"s": "Nuclei-image49.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 238.05079 u", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image2.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 4.00260 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29047,71,180,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image140.png = 234.04363 u Nuclei-image33.png = 1.00783 u','[{"s": "Nuclei-image140.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 234.04363 u", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image33.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 1.00783 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29048,71,181,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image48.png = 237.05121 u','[{"s": "Nuclei-image48.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 237.05121 u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29049,71,182,'paragraph',NULL,'Here the symbol Pa is for the element protactinium (Z = 91).','[{"s": "Here the symbol Pa is for the element protactinium (Z = 91).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29050,71,183,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Calculate the energy released during the alpha decay of Nuclei-image49.png .','[{"s": "(a) Calculate the energy released during the alpha decay of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image49.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29051,71,184,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Show that Nuclei-image49.png can not spontaneously emit a proton.','[{"s": "(b) Show that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image49.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "can not spontaneously emit a proton.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29052,71,185,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29053,71,186,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The alpha decay of Nuclei-image136.png is given by Eq. (13.20). The energy released in this process is given by','[{"s": "(a) The alpha decay of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image136.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is given by Eq. (13.20). The energy released in this process is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29054,71,187,'paragraph',NULL,'Q = (M U – M Th – M He ) c 2','[{"s": "Q = (M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "He", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29055,71,188,'paragraph',NULL,'Substituting the atomic masses as given in the data, we find','[{"s": "Substituting the atomic masses as given in the data, we find", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29056,71,189,'paragraph',NULL,'Q = (238.05079 – 234.04363 – 4.00260)u × c 2','[{"s": "Q = (238.05079 – 234.04363 – 4.00260)u × c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29057,71,190,'paragraph',NULL,'= (0.00456 u) c 2','[{"s": "= (0.00456 u) c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29058,71,191,'paragraph',NULL,'= (0.00456 u) (931.5 MeV/u)','[{"s": "= (0.00456 u) (931.5 MeV/u)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29059,71,192,'paragraph',NULL,'= 4.25 MeV.','[{"s": "= 4.25 MeV.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29060,71,193,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) If Nuclei-image49.png spontaneously emits a proton, the decay process would be','[{"s": "(b) If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image49.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "spontaneously emits a proton, the decay process would be", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29061,71,194,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image49.png → Nuclei-image147.png + Nuclei-image33.png','[{"s": "Nuclei-image49.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image147.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image33.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29062,71,195,'paragraph',NULL,'The Q for this process to happen is','[{"s": "The Q for this process to happen is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29063,71,196,'paragraph',NULL,'= (M U – M Pa – M H ) c 2','[{"s": "= (M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "U", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Pa", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29064,71,197,'paragraph',NULL,'= (238.05079 – 237.05121 – 1.00783) u × c 2','[{"s": "= (238.05079 – 237.05121 – 1.00783) u × c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29065,71,198,'paragraph',NULL,'= (– 0.00825 u) c 2','[{"s": "= (– 0.00825 u) c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29066,71,199,'paragraph',NULL,'= – (0.00825 u)(931.5 MeV/u)','[{"s": "= – (0.00825 u)(931.5 MeV/u)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29067,71,200,'paragraph',NULL,'= – 7.68 MeV','[{"s": "= – 7.68 MeV", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29068,71,201,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the Q of the process is negative and therefore it cannot proceed spontaneously. We will have to supply an energy of 7.68 MeV to a Nuclei-image49.png nucleus to make it emit a proton.','[{"s": "Thus, the Q of the process is negative and therefore it cannot proceed spontaneously. We will have to supply an energy of 7.68 MeV to a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image49.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "nucleus to make it emit a proton.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29069,71,202,'heading',4,'13.6.3 Beta deca',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29070,71,203,'heading',4,'In beta decay, a nucleus spontaneously emits an electron (β− decay) or a positron (β+ decay).A common example of β− decay is',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29071,71,204,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image115.png (13.22)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image115.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.22)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29072,71,205,'paragraph',NULL,'and that of β + decay is','[{"s": "and that of β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decay is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29073,71,206,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image89.png (13.23)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image89.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.23)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29074,71,207,'paragraph',NULL,'The decays are governed by the Eqs. (13.14) and (13.15), so that one can never predict which nucleus will undergo decay, but one can characterize the decay by a half-life T 1/2 . For example, T 1/2 for the decays above is respectively 14.3 d and 2.6y. The emission of electron in β − decay is accompanied by the emission of an antineutrino ( Nuclei-image127.png ); in β + decay, instead, a neutrino (ν) is generated. Neutrinos are neutral particles with very small (possiblly, even zero) mass compared to electrons. They have only weak interaction with other particles. They are, therefore, very difficult to detect, since they can penetrate large quantity of matter (even earth) without any interaction.','[{"s": "The decays are governed by the Eqs. (13.14) and (13.15), so that one can never predict", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nucleus will undergo decay, but one can characterize the decay by a half-life", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For example,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for the decays above is respectively 14.3 d and 2.6y. The emission of electron in β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decay is accompanied by the emission of an antineutrino (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image127.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "); in β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decay, instead, a neutrino (ν) is generated. Neutrinos are neutral particles with very small (possiblly, even zero) mass compared to electrons. They have only weak interaction with other particles. They are, therefore, very difficult to detect, since they can penetrate large quantity of matter (even earth) without any interaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29076,71,209,'paragraph',NULL,'n → p + e – + Nuclei-image70.png (13.24)','[{"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image70.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.24)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29077,71,210,'paragraph',NULL,'while for β + decay, it is the conversion of proton into neutron','[{"s": "while for β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decay, it is the conversion of proton into neutron", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29078,71,211,'paragraph',NULL,'p → n + e + + ν (13.25)','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ν (13.25)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29079,71,212,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that while a free neutron decays to proton, the decay of proton to neutron [Eq. (13.25)] is possible only inside the nucleus, since proton has smaller mass than neutron.','[{"s": "Note that while a free neutron decays to proton, the decay of proton to neutron [Eq. (13.25)] is possible only inside the nucleus, since proton has smaller mass than neutron.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29080,71,213,'heading',4,'13.6.4 Gamma decay',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29082,71,215,'paragraph',NULL,'Typically, a gamma ray is emitted when a α or β decay results in a daughter nucleus in an excited state. This then returns to the ground state by a single photon transition or successive transitions involving more than one photon. A familiar example is the successive emmission of gamma rays of energies 1.17 MeV and 1.33 MeV from the deexcitation of Nuclei-image116.png nuclei formed from β − decay of Nuclei-image77.png .','[{"s": "Typically, a gamma ray is emitted when a α or β decay results in a daughter nucleus in an excited state. This then returns to the ground state by a single photon transition or successive transitions involving more than one photon. A familiar example is the successive emmission of gamma rays of energies 1.17 MeV and 1.33 MeV from the deexcitation of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image116.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "nuclei formed from β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decay of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image77.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29083,71,216,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image122.png'),
(29084,71,217,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 13.4 β-decay of Nuclei-image25.png Nuclei-image25.png .','[{"s": "Figure 13.4 β-decay of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image25.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image25.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29085,71,218,'heading',3,'13.7 Nuclear Energy',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29086,71,219,'paragraph',NULL,'The curve of binding energy per nucleon E bn , given in Fig. 13.1, has a long flat middle region between A = 30 and A = 170. In this region the binding energy per nucleon is nearly constant (8.0 MeV). For the lighter nuclei region, A < 30, and for the heavier nuclei region, A > 170, the binding energy per nucleon is less than 8.0 MeV, as we have noted earlier. Now, the greater the binding energy, the less is the total mass of a bound system, such as a nucleus. Consequently, if nuclei with less total binding energy transform to nuclei with greater binding energy, there will be a net energy release. This is what happens when a heavy nucleus decays into two or more intermediate mass fragments ( fission ) or when light nuclei fuse into a havier nucleus ( fusion .)','[{"s": "The curve of binding energy per nucleon", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bn", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", given in Fig. 13.1, has a long flat middle region between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 30 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 170. In this region the binding energy per nucleon is nearly constant (8.0 MeV). For the lighter nuclei region,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< 30, and for the heavier nuclei region,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> 170, the binding energy per nucleon is less than 8.0 MeV, as we have noted earlier. Now, the greater the binding energy, the less is the total mass of a bound system, such as a nucleus. Consequently, if nuclei with less total binding energy transform to nuclei with greater binding energy, there will be a net energy release. This is what happens when a heavy nucleus decays into two or more intermediate mass fragments (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "fission", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") or when light nuclei fuse into a havier nucleus (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "fusion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29087,71,220,'paragraph',NULL,'Exothermic chemical reactions underlie conventional energy sources such as coal or petroleum. Here the energies involved are in the range of electron volts. On the other hand, in a nuclear reaction, the energy release is of the order of MeV. Thus for the same quantity of matter, nuclear sources produce a million times more energy than a chemical source. Fission of 1 kg of uranium, for example, generates 10 14 J of energy; compare it with burning of 1 kg of coal that gives 10 7 J.','[{"s": "Exothermic chemical reactions underlie conventional energy sources such as coal or petroleum. Here the energies involved are in the range of electron volts. On the other hand, in a nuclear reaction, the energy release is of the order of MeV. Thus for the same quantity of matter, nuclear sources produce a million times more energy than a chemical source. Fission of 1 kg of uranium, for example, generates 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J of energy; compare it with burning of 1 kg of coal that gives 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29088,71,221,'heading',4,'13.7.1 Fission',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(29089,71,222,'paragraph',NULL,'New possibilities emerge when we go beyond natural radioactive decays and study nuclear reactions by bombarding nuclei with other nuclear particles such as proton, neutron, α-particle, etc.','[{"s": "New possibilities emerge when we go beyond natural radioactive decays and study nuclear reactions by bombarding nuclei with other nuclear particles such as proton, neutron, α-particle, etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29091,71,224,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image128.png (13.26)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image128.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.26)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29092,71,225,'paragraph',NULL,'The same reaction can produce other pairs of intermediate mass fragments','[{"s": "The same reaction can produce other pairs of intermediate mass fragments", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29093,71,226,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image76.png (13.27)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image76.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.27)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29094,71,227,'paragraph',NULL,'Or, as another example,','[{"s": "Or, as another example,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29095,71,228,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image66.png (13.28)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image66.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.28)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29096,71,229,'paragraph',NULL,'The fragment products are radioactive nuclei; they emit β particles in succession to achieve stable end products.','[{"s": "The fragment products are radioactive nuclei; they emit β particles in succession to achieve stable end products.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29097,71,230,'paragraph',NULL,'The energy released (the Q value ) in the fission reaction of nuclei like uranium is of the order of 200 MeV per fissioning nucleus. This is estimated as follows:','[{"s": "The energy released (the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "value ) in the fission reaction of nuclei like uranium is of the order of 200 MeV per fissioning nucleus. This is estimated as follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29098,71,231,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us take a nucleus with A = 240 breaking into two fragments each of A = 120. Then','[{"s": "Let us take a nucleus with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 240 breaking into two fragments each of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 120. Then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29099,71,232,'paragraph',NULL,'E bn for A = 240 nucleus is about 7.6 MeV,','[{"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 240 nucleus is about 7.6 MeV,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29100,71,233,'paragraph',NULL,'E bn for the two A = 120 fragment nuclei is about 8.5 MeV.','[{"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bn", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for the two", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 120 fragment nuclei is about 8.5 MeV.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29102,71,235,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence the total gain in binding energy is 240×0.9 or 216 MeV.','[{"s": "Hence the total gain in binding energy is 240×0.9 or 216 MeV.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29104,71,237,'heading',4,'13.7.2 Nuclear reactor',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29105,71,238,'paragraph',NULL,'Notice one fact of great importance in the fission reactions given in Eqs. (13.26) to (13.28). There is a release of extra neutron (s) in the fission process. Averagely, 2½ neutrons are released per fission of uranium nucleus. It is a fraction since in some fission events 2 neutrons are produced, in some 3, etc. The extra neutrons in turn can initiate fission processes, producing still more neutrons, and so on. This leads to the possibility of a chain reaction, as was first suggested by Enrico Fermi. If the chain reaction is controlled suitably, we can get a steady energy output. This is what happens in a nuclear reactor. If the chain reaction is uncontrolled, it leads to explosive energy output, as in a nuclear bomb.','[{"s": "Notice one fact of great importance in the fission reactions given in Eqs. (13.26) to (13.28). There is a release of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "extra", "t": "text"}, {"s": "neutron (s) in the fission process. Averagely, 2½ neutrons are released per fission of uranium nucleus. It is a fraction since in some fission events 2 neutrons are produced, in some 3, etc. The extra neutrons in turn can initiate fission processes, producing still more neutrons, and so on. This leads to the possibility of a chain reaction, as was first suggested by Enrico Fermi. If the chain reaction is controlled suitably, we can get a steady energy output. This is what happens in a nuclear reactor. If the chain reaction is uncontrolled, it leads to explosive energy output, as in a nuclear bomb.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29090,71,223,'paragraph',NULL,'A most important neutron-induced nuclear reaction is fission. An example of fission is when a uranium isotope Nuclei-image94.png bombarded with a neutron breaks into two intermediate mass nuclear fragments','[{"s": "A most important neutron-induced nuclear reaction is fission.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An example of fission is when a uranium isotope", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image94.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "bombarded with a neutron breaks into two intermediate mass nuclear fragments", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000045577", "2000046810", "2000112071"]',NULL),
(29106,71,239,'paragraph',NULL,'There is, however, a hurdle in sustaining a chain reaction, as described here. It is known experimentally that slow neutrons (thermal neutrons) are much more likely to cause fission in Nuclei-image59.png than fast neutrons. Also fast neutrons liberated in fission would escape instead of causing another fission reaction.','[{"s": "There is, however, a hurdle in sustaining a chain reaction, as described here. It is known experimentally that slow neutrons (thermal neutrons) are much more likely to cause fission in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image59.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "than fast neutrons. Also fast neutrons liberated in fission would escape instead of causing another fission reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29107,71,240,'paragraph',NULL,'The average energy of a neutron produced in fission of Nuclei-image59.png is 2 MeV. These neutrons unless slowed down will escape from the reactor without interacting with the uranium nuclei, unless a very large amount of fissionable material is used for sustaining the chain reaction. What one needs to do is to slow down the fast neutrons by elastic scattering with light nuclei. In fact, Chadwick’s experiments showed that in an elastic collision with hydrogen the neutron almost comes to rest and proton carries away the energy. This is the same situation as when a marble hits head-on an identical marble at rest. Therefore, in reactors, light nuclei called moderators are provided along with the fissionable nuclei for slowing down fast neutrons. The moderators commonly used are water, heavy water (D 2 O) and graphite. The Apsara reactor at the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC), Mumbai, uses water as moderator. The other Indian reactors, which are used for power production, use heavy water as moderator.','[{"s": "The average energy of a neutron produced in fission of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image59.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is 2 MeV. These neutrons unless slowed down will escape from the reactor without interacting with the uranium nuclei, unless a very large amount of fissionable material is used for sustaining the chain reaction. What one needs to do is to slow down the fast neutrons by elastic scattering with light nuclei. In fact, Chadwick’s experiments showed that in an elastic collision with hydrogen the neutron almost comes to rest and proton carries away the energy. This is the same situation as when a marble hits head-on an identical marble at rest. Therefore, in reactors, light nuclei called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "moderators", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are provided along with the fissionable nuclei for slowing down fast neutrons. The moderators commonly used are water, heavy water (D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O) and graphite. The Apsara reactor at the Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC), Mumbai, uses water as moderator. The other Indian reactors, which are used for power production, use heavy water as moderator.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29108,71,241,'paragraph',NULL,'Because of the use of moderator, it is possible that the ratio, K , of number of fission produced by a given generation of neutrons to the number of fission of the preceeding generation may be greater than one. This ratio is called the multiplication factor; it is the measure of the growth rate of the neutrons in the reactor. For K = 1, the operation of the reactor is said to be critical , which is what we wish it to be for steady power operation. If K becomes greater than one, the reaction rate and the reactor power increases exponentially. Unless the factor K is brought down very close to unity, the reactor will become supercritical and can even explode. The explosion of the Chernobyl reactor in Ukraine in 1986 is a sad reminder that accidents in a nuclear reactor can be catastrophic.','[{"s": "Because of the use of moderator, it is possible that the ratio,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", of number of fission produced by a given generation of neutrons to the number of fission of the preceeding generation may be greater than one. This ratio is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "multiplication factor;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "it is the measure of the growth rate of the neutrons in the reactor. For", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1, the operation of the reactor is said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "critical", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which is what we wish it to be for steady power operation. If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "becomes greater than one, the reaction rate and the reactor power increases exponentially. Unless the factor", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is brought down very close to unity, the reactor will become supercritical and can even explode. The explosion of the Chernobyl reactor in Ukraine in 1986 is a sad reminder that accidents in a nuclear reactor can be catastrophic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29109,71,242,'paragraph',NULL,'The reaction rate is controlled through control-rods made out of neutron-absorbing material such as cadmium. In addition to control rods, reactors are provided with safety rods which, when required, can be inserted into the reactor and K can be reduced rapidly to less than unity.','[{"s": "The reaction rate is controlled through control-rods made out of neutron-absorbing material such as cadmium. In addition to control rods, reactors are provided with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "safety rods", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which, when required, can be inserted into the reactor and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be reduced rapidly to less than unity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29110,71,243,'paragraph',NULL,'The more abundant isotope Nuclei-image13.png in naturally occurring uranium is non-fissionable. When it captures a neutron, it produces the highly radioactive plutonium through these reactions','[{"s": "The more abundant isotope", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "in naturally occurring uranium is non-fissionable. When it captures a neutron, it produces the highly radioactive plutonium through these reactions", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29111,71,244,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image111.png'),
(29112,71,245,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image37.png (13.29)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image37.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.29)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29113,71,246,'paragraph',NULL,'Plutonium undergoes fission with slow neutrons.','[{"s": "Plutonium undergoes fission with slow neutrons.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29114,71,247,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 13.5 shows the schematic diagram of a nuclear reactor based on thermal neutron fission. The core of the reactor is the site of nuclear fission. It contains the fuel elements in suitably fabricated form. The fuel may be say enriched uranium (i.e., one that has greater abundance of Nuclei-image59.png than naturally occurring uranium). The core contains a moderator to slow down the neutrons. The core is surrounded by a reflector to reduce leakage. The energy (heat) released in fission is continuously removed by a suitable coolant . A containment vessel prevents the escape of radioactive fission products. The whole assembly is shielded to check harmful radiation from coming out. The reactor can be shut down by means of rods (made of, for example, cadmium) that have high absorption of neutrons. The coolant transfers heat to a working fluid which in turn may produce stream. The steam drives turbines and generates electricity.','[{"s": "Figure 13.5 shows the schematic diagram of a nuclear reactor based on thermal neutron fission. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "core", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the reactor is the site of nuclear fission. It contains the fuel elements in suitably fabricated form. The fuel may be say enriched uranium (i.e., one that has greater abundance of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image59.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "than naturally occurring uranium). The core contains a moderator to slow down the neutrons. The core is surrounded by a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reflector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to reduce leakage. The energy (heat) released in fission is continuously removed by a suitable", "t": "text"}, {"s": "coolant", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". A containment vessel prevents the escape of radioactive fission products. The whole assembly is shielded to check harmful radiation from coming out. The reactor can be shut down by means of rods (made of, for example, cadmium) that have high absorption of neutrons. The coolant transfers heat to a working fluid which in turn may produce stream. The steam drives turbines and generates electricity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29115,71,248,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image109.png'),
(29116,71,249,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 13.5 Schematic diagram of a nuclear reactor based on thermal neutron fission.','[{"s": "Figure 13.5 Schematic diagram of a nuclear reactor based on thermal neutron fission.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29117,71,250,'paragraph',NULL,'Like any power reactor, nuclear reactors generate considerable waste products. But nuclear wastes need special care for treatment since they are radioactive and hazardous. Elaborate safety measures, both for reactor operation as well as handling and reprocessing the spent fuel, are required. These safety measures are a distinguishing feature of the Indian Atomic Energy programme. An appropriate plan is being evolved to study the possibility of converting radioactive waste into less active and short-lived material.','[{"s": "Like any power reactor, nuclear reactors generate considerable waste products. But nuclear wastes need special care for treatment since they are radioactive and hazardous. Elaborate safety measures, both for reactor operation as well as handling and reprocessing the spent fuel, are required. These safety measures are a distinguishing feature of the Indian Atomic Energy programme. An appropriate plan is being evolved to study the possibility of converting radioactive waste into less active and short-lived material.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29118,71,251,'heading',4,'India’s atomic energy programme',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29119,71,252,'paragraph',NULL,'The atomic energy programme in India was launched around the time of independence under the leadership of Homi J. Bhabha (1909-1966). An early historic achievement was the design and construction of the first nuclear reactor in India (named Apsara) which went critical on August 4, 1956. It used enriched uranium as fuel and water as moderator. Following this was another notable landmark: the construction of CIRUS (Canada India Research U.S.) reactor in 1960. This 40 MW reactor used natural uranium as fuel and heavy water as moderator. Apsara and CIRUS spurred research in a wide range of areas of basic and applied nuclear science. An important milestone in the first two decades of the programme was the indigenous design and construction of the plutonium plant at Trombay, which ushered in the technology of fuel reprocessing (separating useful fissile and fertile nuclear materials from the spent fuel of a reactor) in India. Research reactors that have been subsequently commissioned include ZERLINA, PURNIMA (I, II and III), DHRUVA and KAMINI. KAMINI is the country’s first large research reactor that uses U-233 as fuel. As the name suggests, the primary objective of a research reactor is not generation of power but to provide a facility for research on different aspects of nuclear science and technology. Research reactors are also an excellent source for production of a variety of radioactive isotopes that find application in diverse fields: industry, medicine and agriculture.','[{"s": "The atomic energy programme in India was launched around the time of independence under the leadership of Homi J. Bhabha (1909-1966). An early historic achievement was the design and construction of the first nuclear reactor in India (named Apsara) which went critical on August 4, 1956. It used enriched uranium as fuel and water as moderator. Following this was another notable landmark: the construction of CIRUS (Canada India Research U.S.) reactor in 1960. This 40 MW reactor used natural uranium as fuel and heavy water as moderator. Apsara and CIRUS spurred research in a wide range of areas of basic and applied nuclear science. An important milestone in the first two decades of the programme was the indigenous design and construction of the plutonium plant at Trombay, which ushered in the technology of fuel reprocessing (separating useful fissile and fertile nuclear materials from the spent fuel of a reactor) in India. Research reactors that have been subsequently commissioned include ZERLINA, PURNIMA (I, II and III), DHRUVA and KAMINI. KAMINI is the country’s first large research reactor that uses U-233 as fuel. As the name suggests, the primary objective of a research reactor is not generation of power but to provide a facility for research on different aspects of nuclear science and technology. Research reactors are also an excellent source for production of a variety of radioactive isotopes that find application in diverse fields: industry, medicine and agriculture.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29120,71,253,'paragraph',NULL,'The main objectives of the Indian Atomic Energy programme are to provide safe and reliable electric power for the country’s social and economic progress and to be self-reliant in all aspects of nuclear technology. Exploration of atomic minerals in India undertaken since the early fifties has indicated that India has limited reserves of uranium, but fairly abundant reserves of thorium. Accordingly, our country has adopted a three-stage strategy of nuclear power generation. The first stage involves the use of natural uranium as a fuel, with heavy water as moderator. The Plutonium-239 obtained from reprocessing of the discharged fuel from the reactors then serves as a fuel for the second stage — the fast breeder reactors. They are so called because they use fast neutrons for sustaining the chain reaction (hence no moderator is needed) and, besides generating power, also breed more fissile species (plutonium) than they consume. The third stage, most significant in the long term, involves using fast breeder reactors to produce fissile Uranium-233 from Thorium-232 and to build power reactors based on them.','[{"s": "The main objectives of the Indian Atomic Energy programme are to provide safe and reliable electric power for the country’s social and economic progress and to be self-reliant in all aspects of nuclear technology. Exploration of atomic minerals in India undertaken since the early fifties has indicated that India has limited reserves of uranium, but fairly abundant reserves of thorium. Accordingly, our country has adopted a three-stage strategy of nuclear power generation. The first stage involves the use of natural uranium as a fuel, with heavy water as moderator. The Plutonium-239 obtained from reprocessing of the discharged fuel from the reactors then serves as a fuel for the second stage — the fast breeder reactors. They are so called because they use fast neutrons for sustaining the chain reaction (hence no moderator is needed) and, besides generating power, also breed more fissile species (plutonium) than they consume. The third stage, most significant in the long term, involves using fast breeder reactors to produce fissile Uranium-233 from Thorium-232 and to build power reactors based on them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29121,71,254,'paragraph',NULL,'India is currently well into the second stage of the programme and considerable work has also been done on the third — the thorium utilisation — stage. The country has mastered the complex technologies of mineral exploration and mining, fuel fabrication, heavy water production, reactor design, construction and operation, fuel reprocessing, etc. Pressurised Heavy Water Reactors (PHWRs) built at different sites in the country mark the accomplishment of the first stage of the programme. India is now more than self-sufficient in heavy water production. Elaborate safety measures both in the design and operation of reactors, as also adhering to stringent standards of radiological protection are the hallmark of the Indian Atomic Energy Programme.','[{"s": "India is currently well into the second stage of the programme and considerable work has also been done on the third — the thorium utilisation — stage. The country has mastered the complex technologies of mineral exploration and mining, fuel fabrication, heavy water production, reactor design, construction and operation, fuel reprocessing, etc. Pressurised Heavy Water Reactors (PHWRs) built at different sites in the country mark the accomplishment of the first stage of the programme. India is now more than self-sufficient in heavy water production. Elaborate safety measures both in the design and operation of reactors, as also adhering to stringent standards of radiological protection are the hallmark of the Indian Atomic Energy Programme.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29122,71,255,'heading',4,'13.7.3 Nuclear fusion – energy generation in stars',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29124,71,257,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image92.png + e + + ν + 0.42 MeV [13.29(a)]','[{"s": "Nuclei-image92.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ν + 0.42 MeV [13.29(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29125,71,258,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image21.png + n + 3.27 MeV [13.29(b)]','[{"s": "Nuclei-image21.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 3.27 MeV [13.29(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29126,71,259,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image56.png + 4.03 MeV [13.29(c)]','[{"s": "Nuclei-image56.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "+ 4.03 MeV [13.29(c)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29127,71,260,'paragraph',NULL,'In the first reaction, two protons combine to form a deuteron and a positron with a release of 0.42 MeV energy. In reaction [13.29(b)], two deuterons combine to form the light isotope of helium. In reaction (13.29c), two deuterons combine to form a triton and a proton. For fusion to take place, the two nuclei must come close enough so that attractive short-range nuclear force is able to affect them. However, since they are both positively charged particles, they experience coulomb repulsion. They, therefore, must have enough energy to overcome this coulomb barrier. The height of the barrier depends on the charges and radii of the two interacting nuclei. It can be shown, for example, that the barrier height for two protons is ~ 400 keV, and is higher for nuclei with higher charges. We can estimate the temperature at which two protons in a proton gas would (averagely) have enough energy to overcome the coulomb barrier:','[{"s": "In the first reaction, two protons combine to form a deuteron and a positron with a release of 0.42 MeV energy. In reaction [13.29(b)], two deuterons combine to form the light isotope of helium. In reaction (13.29c), two deuterons combine to form a triton and a proton. For fusion to take place, the two nuclei must come close enough so that attractive short-range nuclear force is able to affect them. However, since they are both positively charged particles, they experience coulomb repulsion. They, therefore, must have enough energy to overcome this coulomb barrier. The height of the barrier depends on the charges and radii of the two interacting nuclei. It can be shown, for example, that the barrier height for two protons is ~ 400 keV, and is higher for nuclei with higher charges. We can estimate the temperature at which two protons in a proton gas would (averagely) have enough energy to overcome the coulomb barrier:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29128,71,261,'paragraph',NULL,'(3/2) k T = K = 400 keV, which gives T ~ 3 × 10 9 K.','[{"s": "(3/2)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "k T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 400 keV, which gives T ~ 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29129,71,262,'paragraph',NULL,'When fusion is achieved by raising the temperature of the system so that particles have enough kinetic energy to overcome the coulomb repulsive behaviour, it is called thermonuclear fusion .','[{"s": "When fusion is achieved by raising the temperature of the system so that particles have enough kinetic energy to overcome the coulomb repulsive behaviour, it is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "thermonuclear fusion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29130,71,263,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermonuclear fusion is the source of energy output in the interior of stars. The interior of the sun has a temperature of 1.5×10 7 K, which is considerably less than the estimated temperature required for fusion of particles of average energy. Clearly, fusion in the sun involves protons whose energies are much above the average energy.','[{"s": "Thermonuclear fusion is the source of energy output in the interior of stars. The interior of the sun has a temperature of 1.5×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K, which is considerably less than the estimated temperature required for fusion of particles of average energy. Clearly, fusion in the sun involves protons whose energies are much above the average energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29131,71,264,'paragraph',NULL,'The fusion reaction in the sun is a multi-step process in which the hydrogen is burned into helium. Thus, the fuel in the sun is the hydrogen in its core. The proton-proton (p, p) cycle by which this occurs is represented by the following sets of reactions:','[{"s": "The fusion reaction in the sun is a multi-step process in which the hydrogen is burned into helium. Thus, the fuel in the sun is the hydrogen in its core. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "proton-proton", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(p, p)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cycle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by which this occurs is represented by the following sets of reactions:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29132,71,265,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image92.png + e + + ν + 0.42 MeV (i)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image92.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ν + 0.42 MeV (i)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29133,71,266,'paragraph',NULL,'e + + e – → γ + γ + 1.02 MeV (ii)','[{"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ γ + γ + 1.02 MeV (ii)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29134,71,267,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image130.png + γ + 5.49 MeV (iii)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image130.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "+ γ + 5.49 MeV (iii)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29135,71,268,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclei-image144.png + 12.86 MeV (iv) (13.30)','[{"s": "Nuclei-image144.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "+ 12.86 MeV (iv) (13.30)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29136,71,269,'paragraph',NULL,'For the fourth reaction to occur, the first three reactions must occur twice, in which case two light helium nuclei unite to form ordinary helium nucleus. If we consider the combination 2(i) + 2(ii) + 2(iii) +(iv), the net effect is','[{"s": "For the fourth reaction to occur, the first three reactions must occur twice, in which case two light helium nuclei unite to form ordinary helium nucleus. If we consider the combination 2(i) + 2(ii) + 2(iii) +(iv), the net effect is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29137,71,270,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image6.png'),
(29138,71,271,'paragraph',NULL,'or Nuclei-image45.png (13.31)','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image45.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(13.31)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29139,71,272,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, four hydrogen atoms combine to form an Nuclei-image19.png atom with a release of 26.7 MeV of energy.','[{"s": "Thus, four hydrogen atoms combine to form an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image19.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "atom with a release of 26.7 MeV of energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29140,71,273,'paragraph',NULL,'Helium is not the only element that can be synthesized in the interior of a star. As the hydrogen in the core gets depleted and becomes helium, the core starts to cool. The star begins to collapse under its own gravity which increases the temperature of the core. If this temperature increases to about 10 8 K, fusion takes place again, this time of helium nuclei into carbon. This kind of process can generate through fusion higher and higher mass number elements. But elements more massive than those near the peak of the binding energy curve in Fig. 13.1 cannot be so produced.','[{"s": "Helium is not the only element that can be synthesized in the interior of a star. As the hydrogen in the core gets depleted and becomes helium, the core starts to cool. The star begins to collapse under its own gravity which increases the temperature of the core. If this temperature increases to about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K, fusion takes place again, this time of helium nuclei into carbon. This kind of process can generate through fusion higher and higher mass number elements. But elements more massive than those near the peak of the binding energy curve in Fig. 13.1 cannot be so produced.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29141,71,274,'paragraph',NULL,'The age of the sun is about 5×10 9 y and it is estimated that there is enough hydrogen in the sun to keep it going for another 5 billion years. After that, the hydrogen burning will stop and the sun will begin to cool and will start to collapse under gravity, which will raise the core temperature. The outer envelope of the sun will expand, turning it into the so called red giant .','[{"s": "The age of the sun is about 5×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y and it is estimated that there is enough hydrogen in the sun to keep it going for another 5 billion years. After that, the hydrogen burning will stop and the sun will begin to cool and will start to collapse under gravity, which will raise the core temperature. The outer envelope of the sun will expand, turning it into the so called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "red giant", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29142,71,275,'heading',4,'Nuclear holocaust',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29143,71,276,'paragraph',NULL,'In a single uranium fission about 0.9×235 MeV (≈200 MeV) of energy is liberated. If each nucleus of about 50 kg of 235 U undergoes fission the amount of energy involved is about 4 × 10 15 J. This energy is equivalent to about 20,000 tons of TNT, enough for a superexplosion. Uncontrolled release of large nuclear energy is called an atomic explosion. On August 6, 1945 an atomic device was used in warfare for the first time. The US dropped an atom bomb on Hiroshima, Japan. The explosion was equivalent to 20,000 tons of TNT. Instantly the radioactive products devastated 10 sq km of the city which had 3,43,000 inhabitants. Of this number 66,000 were killed and 69,000 were injured; more than 67% of the city’s structures were destroyed.','[{"s": "In a single uranium fission about 0.9×235 MeV (≈200 MeV) of energy is liberated. If each nucleus of about 50 kg of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "235", "t": "text"}, {"s": "U undergoes fission the amount of energy involved is about 4 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J. This energy is equivalent to about 20,000 tons of TNT, enough for a superexplosion. Uncontrolled release of large nuclear energy is called an atomic explosion. On August 6, 1945 an atomic device was used in warfare for the first time. The US dropped an atom bomb on Hiroshima, Japan. The explosion was equivalent to 20,000 tons of TNT. Instantly the radioactive products devastated 10 sq km of the city which had 3,43,000 inhabitants. Of this number 66,000 were killed and 69,000 were injured; more than 67% of the city’s structures were destroyed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29144,71,277,'paragraph',NULL,'High temperature conditions for fusion reactions can be created by exploding a fission bomb. Super-explosions equivalent to 10 megatons of explosive power of TNT were tested in 1954. Such bombs which involve fusion of isotopes of hydrogen, deuterium and tritium are called hydrogen bombs. It is estimated that a nuclear arsenal sufficient to destroy every form of life on this planet several times over is in position to be triggered by the press of a button. Such a nuclear holocaust will not only destroy the life that exists now but its radioactive fallout will make this planet unfit for life for all times. Scenarios based on theoretical calculations predict a long nuclear winter , as the radioactive waste will hang like a cloud in the earth’s atmosphere and will absorb the sun’s radiation.','[{"s": "High temperature conditions for fusion reactions can be created by exploding a fission bomb. Super-explosions equivalent to 10 megatons of explosive power of TNT were tested in 1954. Such bombs which involve fusion of isotopes of hydrogen, deuterium and tritium are called hydrogen bombs. It is estimated that a nuclear arsenal sufficient to destroy every form of life on this planet several times over is in position to be triggered by the press of a button. Such a nuclear holocaust will not only destroy the life that exists now but its radioactive fallout will make this planet unfit for life for all times. Scenarios based on theoretical calculations predict a long", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nuclear winter", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", as the radioactive waste will hang like a cloud in the earth’s atmosphere and will absorb the sun’s radiation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29145,71,278,'heading',4,'13.7.4 Controlled thermonuclear fusion',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29146,71,279,'paragraph',NULL,'The natural thermonuclear fusion process in a star is replicated in a thermonuclear fusion device. In controlled fusion reactors, the aim is to generate steady power by heating the nuclear fuel to a temperature in the range of 10 8 K. At these temperatures, the fuel is a mixture of positive ions and electrons (plasma). The challenge is to confine this plasma, since no container can stand such a high temperature. Several countries around the world including India are developing techniques in this connection. If successful, fusion reactors will hopefully supply almost unlimited power to humanity.','[{"s": "The natural thermonuclear fusion process in a star is replicated in a thermonuclear fusion device. In controlled fusion reactors, the aim is to generate steady power by heating the nuclear fuel to a temperature in the range of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K. At these temperatures, the fuel is a mixture of positive ions and electrons (plasma). The challenge is to confine this plasma, since no container can stand such a high temperature. Several countries around the world including India are developing techniques in this connection. If successful, fusion reactors will hopefully supply almost unlimited power to humanity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29147,71,280,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.7 Answer the following questions:','[{"s": "Example 13.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Answer the following questions:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29148,71,281,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Are the equations of nuclear reactions (such as those given in Section 13.7) ‘balanced’ in the sense a chemical equation (e.g., 2H 2 + O 2 → 2 H 2 O) is? If not, in what sense are they balanced on both sides?','[{"s": "(a) Are the equations of nuclear reactions (such as those given in Section 13.7) ‘balanced’ in the sense a chemical equation (e.g., 2H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "→ 2 H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O) is? If not, in what sense are they balanced on both sides?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29149,71,282,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) If both the number of protons and the number of neutrons are conserved in each nuclear reaction, in what way is mass converted into energy (or vice-versa) in a nuclear reaction?','[{"s": "(b) If both the number of protons and the number of neutrons are conserved in each nuclear reaction, in what way is mass converted into energy (or vice-versa) in a nuclear reaction?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29150,71,283,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) A general impression exists that mass-energy interconversion takes place only in nuclear reaction and never in chemical reaction. This is strictly speaking, incorrect. Explain.','[{"s": "(c) A general impression exists that mass-energy interconversion takes place only in nuclear reaction and never in chemical reaction. This is strictly speaking, incorrect. Explain.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29151,71,284,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29152,71,285,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) A chemical equation is balanced in the sense that the number of atoms of each element is the same on both sides of the equation. A chemical reaction merely alters the original combinations of atoms. In a nuclear reaction, elements may be transmuted. Thus, the number of atoms of each element is not necessarily conserved in a nuclear reaction. However, the number of protons and the number of neutrons are both separately conserved in a nuclear reaction. [Actually, even this is not strictly true in the realm of very high energies – what is strictly conserved is the total charge and total ‘baryon number’. We need not pursue this matter here.]','[{"s": "(a) A chemical equation is balanced in the sense that the number of atoms of each element is the same on both sides of the equation. A chemical reaction merely alters the original combinations of atoms. In a nuclear reaction, elements may be transmuted. Thus, the number of atoms of each element is not necessarily conserved in a nuclear reaction. However, the number of protons and the number of neutrons are both separately conserved in a nuclear reaction. [Actually, even this is not strictly true in the realm of very high energies – what is strictly conserved is the total charge and total ‘baryon number’. We need not pursue this matter here.]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29153,71,286,'paragraph',NULL,'In nuclear reactions (e.g., Eq. 13.26), the number of protons and the number of neutrons are the same on the two sides of the equation.','[{"s": "In nuclear reactions (e.g., Eq. 13.26), the number of protons and the number of neutrons are the same on the two sides of the equation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29154,71,287,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) We know that the binding energy of a nucleus gives a negative contribution to the mass of the nucleus (mass defect). Now, since proton number and neutron number are conserved in a nuclear reaction, the total rest mass of neutrons and protons is the same on either side of a reaction. But the total binding energy of nuclei on the left side need not be the same as that on the right hand side. The difference in these binding energies appears as energy released or absorbed in a nuclear reaction. Since binding energy contributes to mass, we say that the difference in the total mass of nuclei on the two sides get converted into energy or vice-versa. It is in these sense that a nuclear reaction is an example of mass-energy interconversion.','[{"s": "(b) We know that the binding energy of a nucleus gives a negative contribution to the mass of the nucleus (mass defect). Now, since proton number and neutron number are conserved in a nuclear reaction, the total rest mass of neutrons and protons is the same on either side of a reaction. But the total binding energy of nuclei on the left side need not be the same as that on the right hand side. The difference in these binding energies appears as energy released or absorbed in a nuclear reaction. Since binding energy contributes to mass, we say that the difference in the total mass of nuclei on the two sides get converted into energy or vice-versa. It is in these sense that a nuclear reaction is an example of mass-energy interconversion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29155,71,288,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) From the point of view of mass-energy interconversion, a chemical reaction is similar to a nuclear reaction in principle. The energy released or absorbed in a chemical reaction can be traced to the difference in chemical (not nuclear) binding energies of atoms and molecules on the two sides of a reaction. Since, strictly speaking, chemical binding energy also gives a negative contribution (mass defect) to the total mass of an atom or molecule, we can equally well say that the difference in the total mass of atoms or molecules, on the two sides of the chemical reaction gets converted into energy or vice-versa. However, the mass defects involved in a chemical reaction are almost a million times smaller than those in a nuclear reaction.This is the reason for the general impression, (which is incorrect) that mass-energy interconversion does not take place in a chemical reaction.','[{"s": "(c) From the point of view of mass-energy interconversion, a chemical reaction is similar to a nuclear reaction in principle. The energy released or absorbed in a chemical reaction can be traced to the difference in chemical (not nuclear) binding energies of atoms and molecules on the two sides of a reaction. Since, strictly speaking, chemical binding energy also gives a negative contribution (mass defect) to the total mass of an atom or molecule, we can equally well say that the difference in the total mass of atoms or molecules, on the two sides of the chemical reaction gets converted into energy or vice-versa. However, the mass defects involved in a chemical reaction are almost a million times smaller than those in a nuclear reaction.This is the reason for the general impression, (which is incorrect) that mass-energy interconversion does not take place in a chemical reaction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29156,71,289,'heading',3,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29157,71,290,'paragraph',NULL,'1. An atom has a nucleus. The nucleus is positively charged. The radius of the nucleus is smaller than the radius of an atom by a factor of 10 4 . More than 99.9% mass of the atom is concentrated in the nucleus.','[{"s": "1. An atom has a nucleus. The nucleus is positively charged. The radius of the nucleus is smaller than the radius of an atom by a factor of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". More than 99.9% mass of the atom is concentrated in the nucleus.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29158,71,291,'paragraph',NULL,'2. On the atomic scale, mass is measured in atomic mass units (u). By definition, 1 atomic mass unit (1u) is 1/12 th mass of one atom of 12 C;','[{"s": "2. On the atomic scale, mass is measured in atomic mass units (u). By definition, 1 atomic mass unit (1u) is 1/12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mass of one atom of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29159,71,292,'paragraph',NULL,'1u = 1.660563 × 10 –27 kg.','[{"s": "1u = 1.660563 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–27", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29160,71,293,'paragraph',NULL,'3. A nucleus contains a neutral particle called neutron. Its mass is almost the same as that of proton','[{"s": "3. A nucleus contains a neutral particle called neutron. Its mass is almost the same as that of proton", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29161,71,294,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The atomic number Z is the number of protons in the atomic nucleus of an element. The mass number A is the total number of protons and neutrons in the atomic nucleus; A = Z+N; Here N denotes the number of neutrons in the nucleus.','[{"s": "4. The atomic number Z is the number of protons in the atomic nucleus of an element. The mass number A is the total number of protons and neutrons in the atomic nucleus; A = Z+N; Here N denotes the number of neutrons in the nucleus.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29162,71,295,'paragraph',NULL,'A nuclear species or a nuclide is represented as Nuclei-image145.png , where X is the chemical symbol of the species.','[{"s": "A nuclear species or a nuclide is represented as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image145.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", where X is the chemical symbol of the species.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29163,71,296,'paragraph',NULL,'Nuclides with the same atomic number Z, but different neutron number N are called isotopes. Nuclides with the same A are isobars and those with the same N are isotones.','[{"s": "Nuclides with the same atomic number Z, but different neutron number N are called isotopes. Nuclides with the same A are isobars and those with the same N are isotones.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29164,71,297,'paragraph',NULL,'Most elements are mixtures of two or more isotopes. The atomic mass of an element is a weighted average of the masses of its isotopes. The masses are the relative abundances of the isotopes.','[{"s": "Most elements are mixtures of two or more isotopes. The atomic mass of an element is a weighted average of the masses of its isotopes. The masses are the relative abundances of the isotopes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29165,71,298,'paragraph',NULL,'5. A nucleus can be considered to be spherical in shape and assigned a radius. Electron scattering experiments allow determination of the nuclear radius; it is found that radii of nuclei fit the formula','[{"s": "5. A nucleus can be considered to be spherical in shape and assigned a radius. Electron scattering experiments allow determination of the nuclear radius; it is found that radii of nuclei fit the formula", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29166,71,299,'paragraph',NULL,'R = R 0 A 1/3 ,','[{"s": "R = R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29167,71,300,'paragraph',NULL,'where R 0 = a constant = 1.2 fm. This implies that the nuclear density is independent of A. It is of the order of 10 17 kg/m 3 .','[{"s": "where R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= a constant = 1.2 fm. This implies that the nuclear density is independent of A. It is of the order of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg/m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29168,71,301,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Neutrons and protons are bound in a nucleus by the short-range strong nuclear force. The nuclear force does not distinguish between neutron and proton.','[{"s": "6. Neutrons and protons are bound in a nucleus by the short-range strong nuclear force. The nuclear force does not distinguish between neutron and proton.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29169,71,302,'paragraph',NULL,'7. The nuclear mass M is always less than the total mass, Σm, of its constituents. The difference in mass of a nucleus and its constituents is called the mass defect,','[{"s": "7. The nuclear mass M is always less than the total mass, Σm, of its constituents. The difference in mass of a nucleus and its constituents is called the mass defect,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29170,71,303,'paragraph',NULL,'∆M = (Z m p + (A – Z)m n ) – M','[{"s": "∆M = (Z m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ (A – Z)m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") – M", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29171,71,304,'paragraph',NULL,'Using Einstein’s mass energy relation, we express this mass difference in terms of energy as','[{"s": "Using Einstein’s mass energy relation, we express this mass difference in terms of energy as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29172,71,305,'paragraph',NULL,'∆E b = ∆M c2','[{"s": "∆E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∆M c2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29173,71,306,'paragraph',NULL,'The energy ∆E b represents the binding energy of the nucleus. In the mass number range A = 30 to 170, the binding energy per nucleon is nearly constant, about 8 MeV/nucleon.','[{"s": "The energy ∆E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "represents the binding energy of the nucleus. In the mass number range A = 30 to 170, the binding energy per nucleon is nearly constant, about 8 MeV/nucleon.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29174,71,307,'paragraph',NULL,'8. Energies associated with nuclear processes are about a million times larger than chemical process.','[{"s": "8. Energies associated with nuclear processes are about a million times larger than chemical process.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29175,71,308,'paragraph',NULL,'9. The Q-value of a nuclear process is','[{"s": "9. The Q-value of a nuclear process is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29176,71,309,'paragraph',NULL,'Q = final kinetic energy – initial kinetic energy.','[{"s": "Q = final kinetic energy – initial kinetic energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29177,71,310,'paragraph',NULL,'Due to conservation of mass-energy, this is also,','[{"s": "Due to conservation of mass-energy, this is also,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29178,71,311,'paragraph',NULL,'Q = (sum of initial masses – sum of final masses)c 2','[{"s": "Q = (sum of initial masses – sum of final masses)c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30252,73,310,'paragraph',NULL,'m = m 1 m 2 m 3 ... (9.33)','[{"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "... (9.33)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29180,71,313,'paragraph',NULL,'11. Law of radioactive decay : N (t) = N(0) e – λt','[{"s": "11. Law of radioactive decay : N (t) = N(0) e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29182,71,315,'paragraph',NULL,'The half-life T 1/2 of a radionuclide is the time in which N has been reduced to one-half of its initial value. The mean life τ is the time at which N has been reduced to e –1 of its initial value','[{"s": "The half-life T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of a radionuclide is the time in which N has been reduced to one-half of its initial value. The mean life τ is the time at which N has been reduced to e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of its initial value", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29183,71,316,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image117.png'),
(29184,71,317,'paragraph',NULL,'12. Energy is released when less tightly bound nuclei are transmuted into more tightly bound nuclei. In fission, a heavy nucleus like Nuclei-image93.png breaks into two smaller fragments, e.g., Nuclei-image114.png','[{"s": "12. Energy is released when less tightly bound nuclei are transmuted into more tightly bound nuclei. In fission, a heavy nucleus like", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image93.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "breaks into two smaller fragments, e.g.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image114.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29185,71,318,'paragraph',NULL,'13. The fact that more neutrons are produced in fission than are consumed gives the possibility of a chain reaction with each neutron that is produced triggering another fission. The chain reaction is uncontrolled and rapid in a nuclear bomb explosion. It is controlled and steady in a nuclear reactor. In a reactor, the value of the neutron multiplication factor k is maintained at 1.','[{"s": "13. The fact that more neutrons are produced in fission than are consumed gives the possibility of a chain reaction with each neutron that is produced triggering another fission. The chain reaction is uncontrolled and rapid in a nuclear bomb explosion. It is controlled and steady in a nuclear reactor. In a reactor, the value of the neutron multiplication factor k is maintained at 1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29186,71,319,'paragraph',NULL,'14. In fusion, lighter nuclei combine to form a larger nucleus. Fusion of hydrogen nuclei into helium nuclei is the source of energy of all stars including our sun.','[{"s": "14. In fusion, lighter nuclei combine to form a larger nucleus. Fusion of hydrogen nuclei into helium nuclei is the source of energy of all stars including our sun.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29187,71,320,'image',NULL,'530',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Nuclei-image101.png'),
(29188,71,321,'heading',3,'Points to Ponder',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29189,71,322,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The density of nuclear matter is independent of the size of the nucleus. The mass density of the atom does not follow this rule.','[{"s": "1. The density of nuclear matter is independent of the size of the nucleus. The mass density of the atom does not follow this rule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29190,71,323,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The radius of a nucleus determined by electron scattering is found to be slightly different from that determined by alpha-particle scattering. This is because electron scattering senses the charge distribution of the nucleus, whereas alpha and similar particles sense the nuclear matter.','[{"s": "2. The radius of a nucleus determined by electron scattering is found to be slightly different from that determined by alpha-particle scattering. This is because electron scattering senses the charge distribution of the nucleus, whereas alpha and similar particles sense the nuclear matter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29191,71,324,'paragraph',NULL,'3. After Einstein showed the equivalence of mass and energy, E = mc 2 , we cannot any longer speak of separate laws of conservation of mass and conservation of energy, but we have to speak of a unified law of conservation of mass and energy. The most convincing evidence that this principle operates in nature comes from nuclear physics. It is central to our understanding of nuclear energy and harnessing it as a source of power. Using the principle, Q of a nuclear process (decay or reaction) can be expressed also in terms of initial and final masses.','[{"s": "3. After Einstein showed the equivalence of mass and energy, E = mc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we cannot any longer speak of separate laws of conservation of mass and conservation of energy, but we have to speak of a unified law of conservation of mass and energy. The most convincing evidence that this principle operates in nature comes from nuclear physics. It is central to our understanding of nuclear energy and harnessing it as a source of power. Using the principle, Q of a nuclear process (decay or reaction) can be expressed also in terms of initial and final masses.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29192,71,325,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The nature of the binding energy (per nucleon) curve shows that exothermic nuclear reactions are possible, when two light nuclei fuse or when a heavy nucleus undergoes fission into nuclei with intermediate mass.','[{"s": "4. The nature of the binding energy (per nucleon) curve shows that exothermic nuclear reactions are possible, when two light nuclei fuse or when a heavy nucleus undergoes fission into nuclei with intermediate mass.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30253,73,311,'paragraph',NULL,'Such a system of combination of lenses is commonly used in designing lenses for cameras, microscopes, telescopes and other optical instruments.','[{"s": "Such a system of combination of lenses is commonly used in designing lenses for cameras, microscopes, telescopes and other optical instruments.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29194,71,327,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Although the binding energy (per nucleon) curve is smooth and slowly varying, it shows peaks at nuclides like 4 He, 16 O etc. This is considered as evidence of atom-like shell structure in nuclei.','[{"s": "6. Although the binding energy (per nucleon) curve is smooth and slowly varying, it shows peaks at nuclides like", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "He,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O etc. This is considered as evidence of atom-like shell structure in nuclei.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29195,71,328,'paragraph',NULL,'7. Electrons and positron are a particle-antiparticle pair. They are identical in mass; their charges are equal in magnitude and opposite. (It is found that when an electron and a positron come together, they annihilate each other giving energy in the form of gamma-ray photons.)','[{"s": "7. Electrons and positron are a particle-antiparticle pair. They are identical in mass; their charges are equal in magnitude and opposite. (It is found that when an electron and a positron come together, they annihilate each other giving energy in the form of gamma-ray photons.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29196,71,329,'paragraph',NULL,'8. In β - -decay (electron emission), the particle emitted along with electron is anti-neutrino ( Nuclei-image70.png ). On the other hand, the particle emitted in β + -decay (positron emission) is neutrino (ν). Neutrino and anti-neutrino are a particle-antiparticle pair. There are anti particles associated with every particle. What should be antiproton which is the anti particle of the proton?','[{"s": "8. In β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-decay (electron emission), the particle emitted along with electron is anti-neutrino (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image70.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "). On the other hand, the particle emitted in β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-decay (positron emission) is neutrino (ν). Neutrino and anti-neutrino are a particle-antiparticle pair. There are anti particles associated with every particle. What should be antiproton which is the anti particle of the proton?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29197,71,330,'paragraph',NULL,'9. A free neutron is unstable ( Nuclei-image1.png ). But a similar free proton decay is not possible, since a proton is (slightly) lighter than a neutron.','[{"s": "9. A free neutron is unstable (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "). But a similar free proton decay is not possible, since a proton is (slightly) lighter than a neutron.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29198,71,331,'paragraph',NULL,'10. Gamma emission usually follows alpha or beta emission. A nucleus in an excited (higher) state goes to a lower state by emitting a gamma photon. A nucleus may be left in an excited state after alpha or beta emission. Successive emission of gamma rays from the same nucleus (as in case of 60 Ni, Fig. 13.4) is a clear proof that nuclei also have discrete energy levels as do the atoms.','[{"s": "10. Gamma emission usually follows alpha or beta emission. A nucleus in an excited (higher) state goes to a lower state by emitting a gamma photon. A nucleus may be left in an excited state after alpha or beta emission. Successive emission of gamma rays from the same nucleus (as in case of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "60", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ni, Fig. 13.4) is a clear proof that nuclei also have discrete energy levels as do the atoms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29199,71,332,'paragraph',NULL,'11. Radioactivity is an indication of the instability of nuclei. Stability requires the ratio of neutron to proton to be around 1:1 for light nuclei. This ratio increases to about 3:2 for heavy nuclei. (More neutrons are required to overcome the effect of repulsion among the protons.) Nuclei which are away from the stability ratio, i.e., nuclei which have an excess of neutrons or protons are unstable. In fact, only about 10% of knon isotopes (of all elements), are stable. Others have been either artificially produced in the laboratory by bombarding α, p, d, n or other particles on targets of stable nuclear species or identified in astronomical observations of matter in the universe.','[{"s": "11. Radioactivity is an indication of the instability of nuclei. Stability requires the ratio of neutron to proton to be around 1:1 for light nuclei. This ratio increases to about 3:2 for heavy nuclei. (More neutrons are required to overcome the effect of repulsion among the protons.) Nuclei which are away from the stability ratio, i.e., nuclei which have an excess of neutrons or protons are unstable. In fact, only about 10% of knon isotopes (of all elements), are stable. Others have been either artificially produced in the laboratory by bombarding α, p, d, n or other particles on targets of stable nuclear species or identified in astronomical observations of matter in the universe.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
