(29568,72,176,'paragraph',NULL,'Eq. (14.11) gives the acceleration of a particle in SHM. The same equation can again be obtained directly by differentiating velocity v(t) given by Eq. (14.9) with respect to time:','[{"s": "Eq. (14.11) gives the acceleration of a particle in SHM. The same equation can again be obtained directly by differentiating velocity v(t) given by Eq. (14.9) with respect to time:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29569,72,177,'paragraph',NULL,'Oscillations-image97.png (14.12)','[{"s": "Oscillations-image97.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29571,72,179,'paragraph',NULL,'x(t) = A cos ωt, v(t) = – ω Asin ωt, a(t)=–ω 2 A cos ωt The corresponding plots are shown in Fig. 14.13. All quantities vary sinusoidally with time; only their maxima differ and the different plots differ in phase. x varies between –A to A; v(t) varies from –ωA to ωA and a(t) from –ω 2 A to ω 2 A. With respect to displacement plot, velocity plot has a phase difference of π/2 and acceleration plot has a phase difference of π.','[{"s": "x(t) = A cos ωt, v(t) = – ω Asin ωt, a(t)=–ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A cos ωt The corresponding plots are shown in Fig. 14.13. All quantities vary sinusoidally with time; only their maxima differ and the different plots differ in phase. x varies between –A to A; v(t) varies from –ωA to ωA and a(t) from –ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A to ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A. With respect to displacement plot, velocity plot has a phase difference of π/2 and acceleration plot has a phase difference of π.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29572,72,180,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image79.png'),
(29575,72,183,'paragraph',NULL,'x = 5 cos [2π t + π/4].','[{"s": "x = 5 cos [2π t + π/4].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29576,72,184,'paragraph',NULL,'At t = 1.5 s, calculate the (a) displacement, (b) speed and (c) acceleration of the body.','[{"s": "At t = 1.5 s, calculate the (a) displacement, (b) speed and (c) acceleration of the body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29577,72,185,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29578,72,186,'paragraph',NULL,'At t = 1.5 s','[{"s": "At t = 1.5 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29579,72,187,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) displacement = (5.0 m) cos [(2π s –1 ) ×1.5 s + π/4]','[{"s": "(a) displacement = (5.0 m) cos [(2π s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") ×1.5 s + π/4]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29580,72,188,'paragraph',NULL,'= (5.0 m) cos [(3π + π/4)]','[{"s": "= (5.0 m) cos [(3π + π/4)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29581,72,189,'paragraph',NULL,'= –5.0 × 0.707 m','[{"s": "= –5.0 × 0.707 m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29582,72,190,'paragraph',NULL,'= –3.535 m','[{"s": "= –3.535 m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29583,72,191,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Using Eq. (14.9), the speed of the body','[{"s": "(b) Using Eq. (14.9), the speed of the body", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29584,72,192,'paragraph',NULL,'= – (5.0 m)(2π s –1 ) sin [(2π s –1 ) ×1.5 s + π/4]','[{"s": "= – (5.0 m)(2π s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") sin [(2π s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") ×1.5 s + π/4]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29585,72,193,'paragraph',NULL,'= – (5.0 m)(2π s –1 ) sin [(3π + π/4)]','[{"s": "= – (5.0 m)(2π s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") sin [(3π + π/4)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29586,72,194,'paragraph',NULL,'= 10π × 0.707 m s –1','[{"s": "= 10π × 0.707 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29587,72,195,'paragraph',NULL,'= 22 m s –1','[{"s": "= 22 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29589,72,197,'paragraph',NULL,'= –(2π s –1 ) 2 × displacement','[{"s": "= –(2π s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× displacement", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29590,72,198,'paragraph',NULL,'= – 2π s –1 ) 2 × (–3.535 m)','[{"s": "= – 2π s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× (–3.535 m)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29591,72,199,'paragraph',NULL,'= 140 ms –2','[{"s": "= 140 ms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29592,72,200,'heading',4,'14.6 Force law for simple harmonic motion',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29594,72,202,'paragraph',NULL,'F (t) = ma','[{"s": "F (t) = ma", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29595,72,203,'paragraph',NULL,'= –mω 2 x(t)','[{"s": "= –mω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x(t)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29596,72,204,'paragraph',NULL,'i.e., F (t) = –k x (t) (14.13)','[{"s": "i.e., F (t) = –k x (t) (14.13)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29597,72,205,'paragraph',NULL,'where k = mω 2 (14.14a)','[{"s": "where k = mω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(14.14a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29598,72,206,'paragraph',NULL,'or Oscillations-image42.png (14.14b)','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Oscillations-image42.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.14b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29599,72,207,'paragraph',NULL,'Like acceleration, force is always directed towards the mean position—hence it is sometimes called the restoring force in SHM. To summarise the discussion so far, simple harmonic motion can be defined in two equivalent ways, either by Eq. (14.4) for displacement or by Eq. (14.13) that gives its force law. Going from Eq. (14.4) to Eq. (14.13) required us to differentiate two times. Likewise, by integrating the force law Eq. (14.13) two times, we can get back Eq. (14.4).','[{"s": "Like acceleration, force is always directed towards the mean position—hence it is sometimes called the restoring force in SHM. To summarise the discussion so far, simple harmonic motion can be defined in two equivalent ways, either by Eq. (14.4) for displacement or by Eq. (14.13) that gives its force law. Going from Eq. (14.4) to Eq. (14.13) required us to differentiate two times. Likewise, by integrating the force law Eq. (14.13) two times, we can get back Eq. (14.4).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29600,72,208,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the force in Eq. (14.13) is linearly proportional to x(t). A particle oscillating under such a force is, therefore, calling a linear harmonic oscillator. In the real world, the force may contain small additional terms proportional to x 2 , x 3 , etc. These then are called non-linear oscillators.','[{"s": "Note that the force in Eq. (14.13) is linearly proportional to x(t). A particle oscillating under such a force is, therefore, calling a linear harmonic oscillator. In the real world, the force may contain small additional terms proportional to x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", etc. These then are called non-linear oscillators.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29602,72,210,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image94.png'),
(29603,72,211,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.14','[{"s": "Fig. 14.14", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29604,72,212,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Let the mass be displaced by a small distance x to the right side of the equilibrium position, as shown in Fig. 14.15. Under this situation the spring on the left side gets','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Let the mass be displaced by a small distance x to the right side of the equilibrium position, as shown in Fig. 14.15. Under this situation the spring on the left side gets", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29605,72,213,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image35.png'),
(29606,72,214,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.15','[{"s": "Fig. 14.15", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29607,72,215,'paragraph',NULL,'elongated by a length equal to x and that on the right side gets compressed by the same length. The forces acting on the mass are then,','[{"s": "elongated by a length equal to x and that on the right side gets compressed by the same length. The forces acting on the mass are then,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29608,72,216,'paragraph',NULL,'F1 = –k x (force exerted by the spring on the left side, trying to pull the mass towards the mean position)','[{"s": "F1 = –k x (force exerted by the spring on the left side, trying to pull the mass towards the mean position)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29609,72,217,'paragraph',NULL,'F2 = –k x (force exerted by the spring on the right side, trying to push the mass towards the mean position)','[{"s": "F2 = –k x (force exerted by the spring on the right side, trying to push the mass towards the mean position)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29610,72,218,'paragraph',NULL,'The net force, F, acting on the mass is then given by,','[{"s": "The net force, F, acting on the mass is then given by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29611,72,219,'paragraph',NULL,'F = –2kx','[{"s": "F = –2kx", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
