(29631,72,239,'paragraph',NULL,'Oscillations-image17.png (14.18)','[{"s": "Oscillations-image17.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30261,73,319,'paragraph',NULL,'The image formed by the first lens serves as the object for the second. This is at a distance of (15 – 5) cm = 10 cm to the right of the second lens. Though the image is real, it serves as a virtual object for the second lens, which means that the rays appear to come from it for the second lens.','[{"s": "The image formed by the first lens serves as the object for the second. This is at a distance of (15 – 5) cm = 10 cm to the right of the second lens. Though the image is real, it serves as a virtual object for the second lens, which means that the rays appear to come from it for the second lens.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29632,72,240,'paragraph',NULL,'Observe that both kinetic energy and potential energy in SHM are seen to be always positive in Fig. 14.16. Kinetic energy can, of course, be never negative, since it is proportional to the square of speed. Potential energy is positive by choice of the undermined constant in potential energy. Both kinetic energy and potential energy peak twice during each period of SHM. For x = 0, the energy is kinetic; at the extremes x = ±A, it is all potential energy. In the course of motion between these limits, kinetic energy increases at the expense of potential energy or vice-versa.','[{"s": "Observe that both kinetic energy and potential energy in SHM are seen to be always positive in Fig. 14.16. Kinetic energy can, of course, be never negative, since it is proportional to the square of speed. Potential energy is positive by choice of the undermined constant in potential energy. Both kinetic energy and potential energy peak twice during each period of SHM. For x = 0, the energy is kinetic; at the extremes x = ±A, it is all potential energy. In the course of motion between these limits, kinetic energy increases at the expense of potential energy or vice-versa.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29633,72,241,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image87.png'),
(29635,72,243,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 14.7 A block whose mass is 1 kg is fastened to a spring. The spring has a spring constant of 50 N m –1 . The block is pulled to a distance x = 10 cm from its equilibrium position at x = 0 on a frictionless surface from rest at t = 0. Calculate the kinetic, potential and total energies of the block when it is 5 cm away from the mean position.','[{"s": "Example 14.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A block whose mass is 1 kg is fastened to a spring. The spring has a spring constant of 50 N m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The block is pulled to a distance x = 10 cm from its equilibrium position at x = 0 on a frictionless surface from rest at t = 0. Calculate the kinetic, potential and total energies of the block when it is 5 cm away from the mean position.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29636,72,244,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The block executes SHM, its angular frequency, as given by Eq. (14.14b), is','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The block executes SHM, its angular frequency, as given by Eq. (14.14b), is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29637,72,245,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image10.png'),
(29638,72,246,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image55.png'),
(29639,72,247,'paragraph',NULL,'= 7.07 rad s –1','[{"s": "= 7.07 rad s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29640,72,248,'paragraph',NULL,'Its displacement at any time t is then given by,','[{"s": "Its displacement at any time t is then given by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29641,72,249,'paragraph',NULL,'x(t) = 0.1 cos (7.07t)','[{"s": "x(t) = 0.1 cos (7.07t)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29642,72,250,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, when the particle is 5 cm away from the mean position, we have','[{"s": "Therefore, when the particle is 5 cm away from the mean position, we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29643,72,251,'paragraph',NULL,'0.05 = 0.1 cos (7.07t)','[{"s": "0.05 = 0.1 cos (7.07t)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29644,72,252,'paragraph',NULL,'Or cos (7.07t) = 0.5 and hence','[{"s": "Or cos (7.07t) = 0.5 and hence", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29645,72,253,'paragraph',NULL,'sin (7.07t) Oscillations-image41.png = 0.866','[{"s": "sin (7.07t)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Oscillations-image41.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 0.866", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29646,72,254,'paragraph',NULL,'Then, the velocity of the block at x = 5 cm is','[{"s": "Then, the velocity of the block at x = 5 cm is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29647,72,255,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.1 × 7.07 × 0.866 m s –1','[{"s": "= 0.1 × 7.07 × 0.866 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29648,72,256,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.61 m s –1','[{"s": "= 0.61 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29649,72,257,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence the K.E. of the block,','[{"s": "Hence the K.E. of the block,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29650,72,258,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image61.png'),
(29651,72,259,'paragraph',NULL,'= ½[1kg × (0.6123 m s –1 ) 2 ]','[{"s": "= ½[1kg × (0.6123 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29652,72,260,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.19 J','[{"s": "= 0.19 J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29653,72,261,'paragraph',NULL,'The P.E. of the block,','[{"s": "The P.E. of the block,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29654,72,262,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image54.png'),
(29655,72,263,'paragraph',NULL,'= ½(50 N m–1 × 0.05 m × 0.05 m)','[{"s": "= ½(50 N m–1 × 0.05 m × 0.05 m)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29656,72,264,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.0625 J','[{"s": "= 0.0625 J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29657,72,265,'paragraph',NULL,'The total energy of the block at x = 5 cm,','[{"s": "The total energy of the block at x = 5 cm,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29658,72,266,'paragraph',NULL,'= K.E. + P.E.','[{"s": "= K.E. + P.E.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29659,72,267,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.25 J','[{"s": "= 0.25 J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29660,72,268,'paragraph',NULL,'we also know that at maximum displacement, K.E. is zero and hence the total energy of the system is equal to the P.E. Therefore, the total energy of the system,','[{"s": "we also know that at maximum displacement, K.E. is zero and hence the total energy of the system is equal to the P.E. Therefore, the total energy of the system,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29661,72,269,'paragraph',NULL,'= ½(50 N m–1 × 0.1 m × 0.1 m )','[{"s": "= ½(50 N m–1 × 0.1 m × 0.1 m )", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29662,72,270,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.25 J','[{"s": "= 0.25 J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30262,73,320,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image63.png'),
(29663,72,271,'paragraph',NULL,'which is same as the sum of the two energies at a displacement of 5 cm. This is in conformity with the principle of conservation of energy.','[{"s": "which is same as the sum of the two energies at a displacement of 5 cm. This is in conformity with the principle of conservation of energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29664,72,272,'heading',4,'14.8.2 The Simple Pendulum',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29665,72,273,'paragraph',NULL,'It is said that Galileo measured the periods of a swinging chandelier in a church by his pulse beats. He observed that the motion of the chandelier was periodic. The system is a kind of pendulum. You can also make your own pendulum by tying a piece of stone to a long unstretchable thread, approximately 100 cm long. Suspend your pendulum from a suitable support so that it is free to oscillate. Displace the stone to one side by a small distance and let it go. The stone executes a to and fro motion, it is periodic with a period of about two seconds.','[{"s": "It is said that Galileo measured the periods of a swinging chandelier in a church by his pulse beats. He observed that the motion of the chandelier was periodic. The system is a kind of pendulum. You can also make your own pendulum by tying a piece of stone to a long unstretchable thread, approximately 100 cm long. Suspend your pendulum from a suitable support so that it is free to oscillate. Displace the stone to one side by a small distance and let it go. The stone executes a to and fro motion, it is periodic with a period of about two seconds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29666,72,274,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall show that this periodic motion is simple harmonic for small displacements from the mean position. Consider simple pendulum — a small bob of mass m tied to an inextensible massless string of length L. The other end of the string is fixed to a rigid support. The bob oscillates in a plane about the vertical line through the support. Fig. 14.18(a) shows this system. Fig. 14.18(b) is a kind of ‘free-body’ diagram of the simple pendulum showing the forces acting on the bob.','[{"s": "We shall show that this periodic motion is simple harmonic for small displacements from the mean position. Consider simple pendulum — a small bob of mass m tied to an inextensible massless string of length L. The other end of the string is fixed to a rigid support. The bob oscillates in a plane about the vertical line through the support. Fig. 14.18(a) shows this system. Fig. 14.18(b) is a kind of ‘free-body’ diagram of the simple pendulum showing the forces acting on the bob.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29667,72,275,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image56.png'),
(29668,72,276,'paragraph',NULL,'(a)','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29669,72,277,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image113.png'),
(29670,72,278,'paragraph',NULL,'(b)','[{"s": "(b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29671,72,279,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.18 (a) A bob oscillating about its mean position. (b) The radial force T-mg cosθ provides centripetal force but no torque about the support. The tangential force mg sinθ provides the restoring torque.','[{"s": "Fig. 14.18 (a) A bob oscillating about its mean position. (b) The radial force T-mg cosθ provides centripetal force but no torque about the support. The tangential force mg sinθ provides the restoring torque.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29672,72,280,'paragraph',NULL,'Let θ be the angle made by the string with the vertical. When the bob is at the mean position, θ = 0','[{"s": "Let θ be the angle made by the string with the vertical. When the bob is at the mean position, θ = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29673,72,281,'paragraph',NULL,'There are only two forces acting on the bob; the tension T along the string and the vertical force due to gravity (=mg). The force mg can be resolved into the component mg cosθ along the string and mg sinθ perpendicular to it. Since the motion of the bob is along a circle of length L and centre at the support point, the bob has a radial acceleration (ω 2 L) and also a tangental acceleration; the latter arises since motion along the arc of the circle is not uniform. The radial acceleration is provided by the net radial force T –mg cosθ, while the tangential acceleration is provided by mg sinθ. It is more convenient to work with torque about the support since the radial force gives zero torque. Torque τ about the support is entirely provided by the tangental component of force','[{"s": "There are only two forces acting on the bob; the tension T along the string and the vertical force due to gravity (=mg). The force mg can be resolved into the component mg cosθ along the string and mg sinθ perpendicular to it. Since the motion of the bob is along a circle of length L and centre at the support point, the bob has a radial acceleration (ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L) and also a tangental acceleration; the latter arises since motion along the arc of the circle is not uniform. The radial acceleration is provided by the net radial force T –mg cosθ, while the tangential acceleration is provided by mg sinθ. It is more convenient to work with torque about the support since the radial force gives zero torque. Torque τ about the support is entirely provided by the tangental component of force", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29674,72,282,'paragraph',NULL,'SHM - how small should the amplitude be?','[{"s": "SHM - how small should the amplitude be?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29675,72,283,'paragraph',NULL,'When you perform the experiment to determine the time period of a simple pendulum, your teacher tells you to keep the amplitude small. But have you ever asked how small is small? Should the amplitude to 5 0 , 2 0 , 1 0 , or 0.5 0 ? Or could it be 10 0 , 20 0 , or 30 0 ?','[{"s": "When you perform the experiment to determine the time period of a simple pendulum, your teacher tells you to keep the amplitude small. But have you ever asked how small is small? Should the amplitude to 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", or 0.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "? Or could it be 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 20", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", or 30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30263,73,321,'paragraph',NULL,'or v 2 = ∞','[{"s": "or v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∞", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30264,73,322,'paragraph',NULL,'The virtual image is formed at an infinite distance to the left of the second lens. This acts as an object for the third lens.','[{"s": "The virtual image is formed at an infinite distance to the left of the second lens. This acts as an object for the third lens.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30265,73,323,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image65.png'),
(30266,73,324,'paragraph',NULL,'or Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image66.png','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image66.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29676,72,284,'paragraph',NULL,'To appreciate this, it would be better to measure the time period for different amplitudes, up to large amplitudes. Of course, for large oscillations, you will have to take care that the pendulum oscillates in a vertical plane. Let us denote the time period for small-amplitude oscillations as T (0) and write the time period for amplitude θ 0 as T(θ 0 ) = cT (0), where c is the multiplying factor. If you plot a graph of c versus θ 0 , you will get values somewhat like this:','[{"s": "To appreciate this, it would be better to measure the time period for different amplitudes, up to large amplitudes. Of course, for large oscillations, you will have to take care that the pendulum oscillates in a vertical plane. Let us denote the time period for small-amplitude oscillations as T (0) and write the time period for amplitude θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as T(θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = cT (0), where c is the multiplying factor. If you plot a graph of c versus θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", you will get values somewhat like this:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29677,72,285,'paragraph',NULL,'θ 0 : 20 0 45 0 50 0 70 0 90 0','[{"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": 20", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "45", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "50", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "70", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "90", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29678,72,286,'paragraph',NULL,'c : 1.02 1.04 1.05 1.10 1.18','[{"s": "c : 1.02 1.04 1.05 1.10 1.18", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29679,72,287,'paragraph',NULL,'This means that the error in the time period is about 2% at an amplitude of 20 0 , 5% at an amplitude of 50 0 , and 10% at an amplitude of 70 0 and 18% at an amplitude of 90 0 .','[{"s": "This means that the error in the time period is about 2% at an amplitude of 20", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 5% at an amplitude of 50", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and 10% at an amplitude of 70", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 18% at an amplitude of 90", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29680,72,288,'paragraph',NULL,'0 over its low amplitude value, you could very well keep the amplitude to be 50° in your experiments.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "over its low amplitude value, you could very well keep the amplitude to be 50° in your experiments.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29681,72,289,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = –L (mg sinθ ) (14.22)','[{"s": "τ = –L (mg sinθ ) (14.22)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29682,72,290,'paragraph',NULL,'This is the restoring torque that tends to reduce angular displacement — hence the negative sign. By Newton’s law of rotational motion,','[{"s": "This is the restoring torque that tends to reduce angular displacement — hence the negative sign. By Newton’s law of rotational motion,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29683,72,291,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = I α (14.23)','[{"s": "τ = I α (14.23)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29684,72,292,'paragraph',NULL,'where I is the moment of inertia of the system about the support and α is the angular acceleration. Thus,','[{"s": "where I is the moment of inertia of the system about the support and α is the angular acceleration. Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29685,72,293,'paragraph',NULL,'I α = –m g sin θ L (14.24)','[{"s": "I α = –m g sin θ L (14.24)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29686,72,294,'paragraph',NULL,'Or,','[{"s": "Or,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29687,72,295,'paragraph',NULL,'α = Oscillations-image28.png (14.25)','[{"s": "α =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Oscillations-image28.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.25)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29688,72,296,'paragraph',NULL,'We can simplify Eq. (14.25) if we assume that the displacement θ is small. We know that sin θ can be expressed as,','[{"s": "We can simplify Eq. (14.25) if we assume that the displacement θ is small. We know that sin θ can be expressed as,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29689,72,297,'paragraph',NULL,'Oscillations-image75.png (14.26)','[{"s": "Oscillations-image75.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.26)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29690,72,298,'paragraph',NULL,'where θ is in radians.','[{"s": "where θ is in radians.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29691,72,299,'paragraph',NULL,'Now if θ is small, sin θ can be approximated by θ and Eq. (14.25) can then be written as,','[{"s": "Now if θ is small, sin θ can be approximated by θ and Eq. (14.25) can then be written as,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29692,72,300,'paragraph',NULL,'Oscillations-image40.png (14.27)','[{"s": "Oscillations-image40.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.27)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29693,72,301,'paragraph',NULL,'In Table 14.1, we have listed the angle θ in degrees, its equivalent in radians, and the value of the function sin θ . From this table it can be seen that for θ as large as 20 degrees, sin θ is nearly the same as θ expressed in radians.','[{"s": "In Table 14.1, we have listed the angle θ in degrees, its equivalent in radians, and the value of the function sin θ . From this table it can be seen that for θ as large as 20 degrees, sin θ is nearly the same as θ expressed in radians.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29694,72,302,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image5.png'),
(29695,72,303,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 14.1 sin θ as a function of angle θ','[{"s": "Table 14.1 sin θ as a function of angle θ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29696,72,304,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (14.27) is mathematically, identical to Eq. (14.11) except that the variable is angular displacement. Hence we have proved that for small θ, the motion of the bob is simple harmonic. From Eqs. (14.27) and (14.11),','[{"s": "Equation (14.27) is mathematically, identical to Eq. (14.11) except that the variable is angular displacement. Hence we have proved that for small θ, the motion of the bob is simple harmonic. From Eqs. (14.27) and (14.11),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29697,72,305,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image73.png'),
(29698,72,306,'paragraph',NULL,'and','[{"s": "and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
