(29780,72,388,'paragraph',NULL,'This is just half of the decay period for amplitude. This is not surprising, because, according to Eqs. (14.33) and (14.35), energy depends on the square of the amplitude. Notice that there is a factor of 2 in the exponents of the two exponentials.','[{"s": "This is just half of the decay period for amplitude. This is not surprising, because, according to Eqs. (14.33) and (14.35), energy depends on the square of the amplitude. Notice that there is a factor of 2 in the exponents of the two exponentials.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29781,72,389,'heading',4,'14.10 Forced oscillations and resonance',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29782,72,390,'paragraph',NULL,'When a system (such as a simple pendulum or a block attached to a spring) is displaced from its equilibrium position and released, it oscillates with its natural frequency ω, and the oscillations are called free oscillations. All free oscillations eventually die out because of the ever present damping forces. However, an external agency can maintain these oscillations. These are called forced or driven oscillations. We consider the case when the external force is itself periodic, with a frequency ω d called the driven frequency. The most important fact of forced periodic oscillations is that the system oscillates not with its natural frequency ω, but at the frequency ω d of the external agency; the free oscillations die out due to damping. The most familiar example of forced oscillation is when a child in a garden swing periodically presses his feet against the ground (or someone else periodically gives the child a push) to maintain the oscillations.','[{"s": "When a system (such as a simple pendulum or a block attached to a spring) is displaced from its equilibrium position and released, it oscillates with its natural frequency ω, and the oscillations are called free oscillations. All free oscillations eventually die out because of the ever present damping forces. However, an external agency can maintain these oscillations. These are called forced or driven oscillations. We consider the case when the external force is itself periodic, with a frequency ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "called the driven frequency. The most important fact of forced periodic oscillations is that the system oscillates not with its natural frequency ω, but at the frequency ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the external agency; the free oscillations die out due to damping. The most familiar example of forced oscillation is when a child in a garden swing periodically presses his feet against the ground (or someone else periodically gives the child a push) to maintain the oscillations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29783,72,391,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose an external force F(t) of amplitude F o that varies periodically with time is applied to a damped oscillator. Such a force can be represented as,','[{"s": "Suppose an external force F(t) of amplitude F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "that varies periodically with time is applied to a damped oscillator. Such a force can be represented as,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29784,72,392,'paragraph',NULL,'F(t) = F o cos ω d t (14.36)','[{"s": "F(t) = F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t (14.36)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29785,72,393,'paragraph',NULL,'The motion of a particle under the combined action of a linear restoring force, damping force and a time dependent driving force represented by Eq. (14.36) is given by,','[{"s": "The motion of a particle under the combined action of a linear restoring force, damping force and a time dependent driving force represented by Eq. (14.36) is given by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
