(29794,72,402,'paragraph',NULL,'where m is the mass of the particle and v0 and x0 are the velocity and the displacement of the particle at time t = 0, which is the moment when we apply the periodic force. Equation (14.39) shows that the amplitude of the forced oscillator depends on the (angular) frequency of the driving force. We can see a different behaviour of the oscillator when ωd is far from ω and when it is close to ω. We consider these two cases.','[{"s": "where m is the mass of the particle and v0 and x0 are the velocity and the displacement of the particle at time t = 0, which is the moment when we apply the periodic force. Equation (14.39) shows that the amplitude of the forced oscillator depends on the (angular) frequency of the driving force. We can see a different behaviour of the oscillator when ωd is far from ω and when it is close to ω. We consider these two cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29795,72,403,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Small Damping, Driving Frequency far from Natural Frequency : In this case, ω d b will be much smaller than m(ω 2 –ω d2 ), and we can neglect that term. Then Eq. (14.39) reduces to','[{"s": "(a) Small Damping, Driving Frequency far from Natural Frequency : In this case, ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b will be much smaller than m(ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), and we can neglect that term. Then Eq. (14.39) reduces to", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29796,72,404,'paragraph',NULL,'Oscillations-image8.png (14.40)','[{"s": "Oscillations-image8.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.40)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29798,72,406,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image114.png'),
(29799,72,407,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.21 The displacement amplitude of a forced oscillator as a function of the angular frequency of the driving force. The amplitude is the greatest at ω d /ω =1, the resonance condition. The three curves correspond to different extents of damping present in the system. The curves 1 and 3 correspond to minimum and maximum damping in the system.','[{"s": "Fig. 14.21 The displacement amplitude of a forced oscillator as a function of the angular frequency of the driving force. The amplitude is the greatest at ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ω =1, the resonance condition. The three curves correspond to different extents of damping present in the system. The curves 1 and 3 correspond to minimum and maximum damping in the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29800,72,408,'paragraph',NULL,'If we go on changing the driving frequency, the amplitude tends to infinity when it equals the natural frequency. But this is the ideal case of zero damping, a case which never arises in a real system as the damping is never perfectly zero. You must have experienced in a swing that when the timing of your push exactly matches with the time period of the swing, your swing gets the maximum amplitude. This amplitude is large, but not infinity, because there is always some damping in your swing. This will become clear in the (b).','[{"s": "If we go on changing the driving frequency, the amplitude tends to infinity when it equals the natural frequency. But this is the ideal case of zero damping, a case which never arises in a real system as the damping is never perfectly zero. You must have experienced in a swing that when the timing of your push exactly matches with the time period of the swing, your swing gets the maximum amplitude. This amplitude is large, but not infinity, because there is always some damping in your swing. This will become clear in the (b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29801,72,409,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Driving Frequency Close to Natural Frequency : If ω d is very close to ω , m (ω 2 –ω 2d ) would be much less than ω d b, for any reasonable value of b, then Eq. (14.39) reduces to','[{"s": "(b) Driving Frequency Close to Natural Frequency : If ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is very close to ω , m (ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2d", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") would be much less than ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b, for any reasonable value of b, then Eq. (14.39) reduces to", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29802,72,410,'paragraph',NULL,'Oscillations-image11.png (14.41)','[{"s": "Oscillations-image11.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.41)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29804,72,412,'paragraph',NULL,'In our daily life, we encounter phenomena which involve resonance. Your experience with swings is a good example of resonance. You might have realised that the skill in swinging to greater heights lies in the synchronisation of the rhythm of pushing against the ground with the natural frequency of the swing.','[{"s": "In our daily life, we encounter phenomena which involve resonance. Your experience with swings is a good example of resonance. You might have realised that the skill in swinging to greater heights lies in the synchronisation of the rhythm of pushing against the ground with the natural frequency of the swing.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29805,72,413,'paragraph',NULL,'To illustrate this point further, let us consider a set of five simple pendulums of assorted lengths suspended from a common rope as shown in Fig. 14.22. The pendulums 1 and 4 have the same lengths and the others have different lengths. Now, let us set pendulum 1 into motion. The energy from this pendulum gets transferred to other pendulums through the connecting rope and they start oscillating. The driving force is provided through the connecting rope. The frequency of this force is the frequency with which pendulum 1 oscillates. If we observe the response of pendulums 2, 3 and 5, they first start oscillating with their natural frequencies of oscillations and different amplitudes, but this motion is gradually damped and not sustained. Their frequencies of oscillation gradually change, and ultimately, they oscillate with the frequency of pendulum 1, i.e., the frequency of the driving force but with different amplitudes. They oscillate with small amplitudes. The response of pendulum 4 is in contrast to this set of pendulums. It oscillates with the same frequency as that of pendulum 1 and its amplitude gradually picks up and becomes very large. A resonance-like response is seen. This happens because in this the condition for resonance is satisfied, i.e. the natural frequency of the system coincides with that of the driving force.','[{"s": "To illustrate this point further, let us consider a set of five simple pendulums of assorted lengths suspended from a common rope as shown in Fig. 14.22. The pendulums 1 and 4 have the same lengths and the others have different lengths. Now, let us set pendulum 1 into motion. The energy from this pendulum gets transferred to other pendulums through the connecting rope and they start oscillating. The driving force is provided through the connecting rope. The frequency of this force is the frequency with which pendulum 1 oscillates. If we observe the response of pendulums 2, 3 and 5, they first start oscillating with their natural frequencies of oscillations and different amplitudes, but this motion is gradually damped and not sustained. Their frequencies of oscillation gradually change, and ultimately, they oscillate with the frequency of pendulum 1, i.e., the frequency of the driving force but with different amplitudes. They oscillate with small amplitudes. The response of pendulum 4 is in contrast to this set of pendulums. It oscillates with the same frequency as that of pendulum 1 and its amplitude gradually picks up and becomes very large. A resonance-like response is seen. This happens because in this the condition for resonance is satisfied, i.e. the natural frequency of the system coincides with that of the driving force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29806,72,414,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image15.png'),
(29807,72,415,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.22 Five simple pendulums of different lengths suspended from a common support.','[{"s": "Fig. 14.22 Five simple pendulums of different lengths suspended from a common support.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29808,72,416,'heading',4,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29809,72,417,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The motion that repeats itself is called periodic motion.','[{"s": "1. The motion that repeats itself is called periodic motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29810,72,418,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The period T is the time required for one complete oscillation, or cycle. It is related to the frequency ν by,','[{"s": "2. The period T is the time required for one complete oscillation, or cycle. It is related to the frequency ν by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29811,72,419,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image72.png'),
(29812,72,420,'paragraph',NULL,'The frequency ν of periodic or oscillatory motion is the number of oscillations per unit time. In the SI, it is measured in hertz :','[{"s": "The frequency ν of periodic or oscillatory motion is the number of oscillations per unit time. In the SI, it is measured in hertz :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29813,72,421,'paragraph',NULL,'1 hertz = 1 Hz = 1 oscillation per second = 1s–1','[{"s": "1 hertz = 1 Hz = 1 oscillation per second = 1s–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29815,72,423,'paragraph',NULL,'x (t) = A cos (ωt + φ ) (displacement),','[{"s": "x (t) = A cos (ωt + φ ) (displacement),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29816,72,424,'paragraph',NULL,'in which A is the amplitude of the displacement, the quantity (ωt + φ ) is the phase of the motion, and φ is the phase constant. The angular frequency ω is related to the period and frequency of the motion by,','[{"s": "in which A is the amplitude of the displacement, the quantity (ωt + φ ) is the phase of the motion, and φ is the phase constant. The angular frequency ω is related to the period and frequency of the motion by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29817,72,425,'paragraph',NULL,'Oscillations-image20.png (angular frequency).','[{"s": "Oscillations-image20.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(angular frequency).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29818,72,426,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Simple harmonic motion can also be viewed as the projection of uniform circular motion on the diameter of the circle in which the latter motion occurs.','[{"s": "4. Simple harmonic motion can also be viewed as the projection of uniform circular motion on the diameter of the circle in which the latter motion occurs.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29820,72,428,'paragraph',NULL,'v (t) = –ωA sin (ωt + φ ) (velocity),','[{"s": "v (t) = –ωA sin (ωt + φ ) (velocity),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29821,72,429,'paragraph',NULL,'a (t) = –ω2A cos (ωt + φ )','[{"s": "a (t) = –ω2A cos (ωt + φ )", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29822,72,430,'paragraph',NULL,'= –ω2x (t) (acceleration),','[{"s": "= –ω2x (t) (acceleration),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29823,72,431,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus we see that both velocity and acceleration of a body executing simple harmonic motion are periodic functions, having the velocity amplitude vm=ω A and acceleration amplitude am =ω2A, respectively.','[{"s": "Thus we see that both velocity and acceleration of a body executing simple harmonic motion are periodic functions, having the velocity amplitude vm=ω A and acceleration amplitude am =ω2A, respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29824,72,432,'paragraph',NULL,'6. The force acting in a simple harmonic motion is proportional to the displacement and is always directed towards the centre of motion.','[{"s": "6. The force acting in a simple harmonic motion is proportional to the displacement and is always directed towards the centre of motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29825,72,433,'paragraph',NULL,'8. A particle of mass m oscillating under the influence of Hooke’s law restoring force given by F = – k x exhibits simple harmonic motion with','[{"s": "8. A particle of mass m oscillating under the influence of Hooke’s law restoring force given by F = – k x exhibits simple harmonic motion with", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29826,72,434,'paragraph',NULL,'Oscillations-image102.png (angular frequency)','[{"s": "Oscillations-image102.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(angular frequency)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29827,72,435,'paragraph',NULL,'Oscillations-image100.png (period)','[{"s": "Oscillations-image100.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(period)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29828,72,436,'paragraph',NULL,'Such a system is also called a linear oscillator.','[{"s": "Such a system is also called a linear oscillator.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29829,72,437,'paragraph',NULL,'9. The motion of a simple pendulum swinging through small angles is approximately simple harmonic. The period of oscillation is given by,','[{"s": "9. The motion of a simple pendulum swinging through small angles is approximately simple harmonic. The period of oscillation is given by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29830,72,438,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image68.png'),
(29831,72,439,'paragraph',NULL,'10. The mechanical energy in a real oscillating system decreases during oscillations because external forces, such as drag, inhibit the oscillations and transfer mechanical energy to thermal energy. The real oscillator and its motion are then said to be damped. If the damping force is given by Fd = –bv, where v is the velocity of the oscillator and b is a damping constant, then the displacement of the oscillator is given by,','[{"s": "10. The mechanical energy in a real oscillating system decreases during oscillations because external forces, such as drag, inhibit the oscillations and transfer mechanical energy to thermal energy. The real oscillator and its motion are then said to be damped. If the damping force is given by Fd = –bv, where v is the velocity of the oscillator and b is a damping constant, then the displacement of the oscillator is given by,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29832,72,440,'paragraph',NULL,'x (t) = A e–bt/2m cos (ω′t + φ )','[{"s": "x (t) = A e–bt/2m cos (ω′t + φ )", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29833,72,441,'paragraph',NULL,'where ω′, the angular frequency of the damped oscillator, is given by','[{"s": "where ω′, the angular frequency of the damped oscillator, is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29834,72,442,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image93.png'),
(29835,72,443,'paragraph',NULL,'If the damping constant is small then ω′ ≈ ω, where ω is the angular frequency of the undamped oscillator. The mechanical energy E of the damped oscillator is given by','[{"s": "If the damping constant is small then ω′ ≈ ω, where ω is the angular frequency of the undamped oscillator. The mechanical energy E of the damped oscillator is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29836,72,444,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image13.png'),
(29837,72,445,'paragraph',NULL,'11. If an external force with angular frequency ωd acts on an oscillating system with natural angular frequency ω, the system oscillates with angular frequency ωd. The amplitude of oscillations is the greatest when','[{"s": "11. If an external force with angular frequency ωd acts on an oscillating system with natural angular frequency ω, the system oscillates with angular frequency ωd. The amplitude of oscillations is the greatest when", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29838,72,446,'paragraph',NULL,'ωd = ω','[{"s": "ωd = ω", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29839,72,447,'paragraph',NULL,'a condition called resonance.','[{"s": "a condition called resonance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29840,72,448,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image48.png'),
(29841,72,449,'heading',4,'Points to ponder',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29842,72,450,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The period T is the least time after which motion repeats itself. Thus, motion repeats itself after nT where n is an integer.','[{"s": "1. The period", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "least time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "after which motion repeats itself. Thus, motion repeats itself after", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is an integer.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29843,72,451,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Every periodic motion is not simple harmonic motion. Only that periodic motion governed by the force law F = – k x is simple harmonic.','[{"s": "2. Every periodic motion is not simple harmonic motion. Only that periodic motion governed by the force law", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F = – k x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is simple harmonic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29844,72,452,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Circular motion can arise due to an inverse-square law force (as in planetary motion) as well as due to simple harmonic force in two dimensions equal to: – m ω2 r . In the latter case, the phases of motion, in two perpendicular directions ( x and y ) must differ by π/2. Thus, for example, a particle subject to a force – m ω2 r with initial position (0, A ) and velocity (ω A , 0) will move uniformly in a circle of radius A .','[{"s": "3. Circular motion can arise due to an inverse-square law force (as in planetary motion) as well as due to simple harmonic force in two dimensions equal to: –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In the latter case, the phases of motion, in two perpendicular directions (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") must differ by π/2. Thus, for example, a particle subject to a force –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with initial position (0,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and velocity (ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", 0) will move uniformly in a circle of radius", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29845,72,453,'paragraph',NULL,'4. For linear simple harmonic motion with a given ω, two initial conditions are necessary and sufficient to determine the motion completely. The initial conditions may be (i) initial position and initial velocity or (ii) amplitude and phase or (iii) energy and phase.','[{"s": "4. For linear simple harmonic motion with a given ω, two initial conditions are necessary and sufficient to determine the motion completely. The initial conditions may be (i) initial position and initial velocity or (ii) amplitude and phase or (iii) energy and phase.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29846,72,454,'paragraph',NULL,'5. From point 4 above, given amplitude or energy, phase of motion is determined by the initial position or initial velocity.','[{"s": "5. From point 4 above, given amplitude or energy, phase of motion is determined by the initial position or initial velocity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29847,72,455,'paragraph',NULL,'6. A combination of two simple harmonic motions with arbitrary amplitudes and phases is not necessarily periodic. It is periodic only if frequency of one motion is an integral multiple of the other’s frequency. However, a periodic motion can always be expressed as a sum of infinite number of harmonic motions with appropriate amplitudes.','[{"s": "6. A combination of two simple harmonic motions with arbitrary amplitudes and phases is not necessarily periodic. It is periodic only if frequency of one motion is an integral multiple of the other’s frequency. However, a periodic motion can always be expressed as a sum of infinite number of harmonic motions with appropriate amplitudes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29848,72,456,'paragraph',NULL,'7. The period of SHM does not depend on amplitude or energy or the phase constant. Contrast this with the periods of planetary orbits under gravitation (Kepler’s third law).','[{"s": "7. The period of SHM does not depend on amplitude or energy or the phase constant. Contrast this with the periods of planetary orbits under gravitation (Kepler’s third law).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29849,72,457,'paragraph',NULL,'8. The motion of a simple pendulum is simple harmonic for small angular displacement.','[{"s": "8. The motion of a simple pendulum is simple harmonic for small angular displacement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29850,72,458,'paragraph',NULL,'9. For motion of a particle to be simple harmonic, its displacement x must be expressible in either of the following forms :','[{"s": "9. For motion of a particle to be simple harmonic, its displacement", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "must be expressible in either of the following forms :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29851,72,459,'paragraph',NULL,'x = A cos ω t + B sin ω t','[{"s": "x = A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29852,72,460,'paragraph',NULL,'x = A cos (ω t + α ), x = B sin (ω t + β )','[{"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos (ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ α ),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin (ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ β )", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29853,72,461,'paragraph',NULL,'The three forms are completely equivalent (any one can be expressed in terms of any other two forms).','[{"s": "The three forms are completely equivalent (any one can be expressed in terms of any other two forms).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29854,72,462,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, damped simple harmonic motion [Eq. (14.31)] is not strictly simple harmonic. It is approximately so only for time intervals much less than 2m/b where b is the damping constant.','[{"s": "Thus, damped simple harmonic motion [Eq. (14.31)] is not strictly simple harmonic. It is approximately so only for time intervals much less than", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2m/b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the damping constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29855,72,463,'paragraph',NULL,'10. In forced oscillations, the steady state motion of the particle (after the forced oscillations die out) is simple harmonic motion whose frequency is the frequency of the driving frequency ωd, not the natural frequency ω of the particle.','[{"s": "10. In forced oscillations, the steady state motion of the particle (after the forced oscillations die out) is simple harmonic motion whose frequency is the frequency of the driving frequency ωd, not the natural frequency ω of the particle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29856,72,464,'paragraph',NULL,'11. In the ideal case of zero damping, the amplitude of simple harmonic motion at resonance is infinite. Since all real systems have some damping, however small, this situation is never observed.','[{"s": "11. In the ideal case of zero damping, the amplitude of simple harmonic motion at resonance is infinite. Since all real systems have some damping, however small, this situation is never observed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29857,72,465,'paragraph',NULL,'12. Under forced oscillation, the phase of harmonic motion of the particle differs from the phase of the driving force.','[{"s": "12. Under forced oscillation, the phase of harmonic motion of the particle differs from the phase of the driving force.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29858,72,466,'heading',4,'Exercises',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29859,72,467,'paragraph',NULL,'14.1 Which of the following examples represent periodic motion?','[{"s": "14.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Which of the following examples represent periodic motion?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29860,72,468,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) A swimmer completing one (return) trip from one bank of a river to the other and back.','[{"s": "(a) A swimmer completing one (return) trip from one bank of a river to the other and back.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29861,72,469,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) A freely suspended bar magnet displaced from its N-S direction and released.','[{"s": "(b) A freely suspended bar magnet displaced from its N-S direction and released.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29862,72,470,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) A hydrogen molecule rotating about its centre of mass.','[{"s": "(c) A hydrogen molecule rotating about its centre of mass.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29863,72,471,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) An arrow released from a bow.','[{"s": "(d) An arrow released from a bow.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29864,72,472,'paragraph',NULL,'14.2 Which of the following examples represent (nearly) simple harmonic motion and which represent periodic but not simple harmonic motion?','[{"s": "14.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Which of the following examples represent (nearly) simple harmonic motion and which represent periodic but not simple harmonic motion?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29865,72,473,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) the rotation of the earth about its axis.','[{"s": "(a) the rotation of the earth about its axis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29866,72,474,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) the motion of an oscillating mercury column in a U-tube.','[{"s": "(b) the motion of an oscillating mercury column in a U-tube.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29867,72,475,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) the motion of a ball bearing inside a smooth curved bowl, when released from a point slightly above the lowermost point.','[{"s": "(c) the motion of a ball bearing inside a smooth curved bowl, when released from a point slightly above the lowermost point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29868,72,476,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) general vibrations of a polyatomic molecule about its equilibrium position.','[{"s": "(d) general vibrations of a polyatomic molecule about its equilibrium position.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29869,72,477,'paragraph',NULL,'14.3 Fig. 14.23 depicts four x-t plots for linear motion of a particle. Which of the plots represent periodic motion? What is the period of motion (in case of periodic motion)?','[{"s": "14.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Fig. 14.23 depicts four x-t plots for linear motion of a particle. Which of the plots represent periodic motion? What is the period of motion (in case of periodic motion)?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29870,72,478,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image53.png'),
(29871,72,479,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Oscillations-image6.png'),
(29872,72,480,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.23','[{"s": "Fig. 14.23", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29873,72,481,'paragraph',NULL,'14.4 Which of the following functions of time represent (a) simple harmonic, (b) periodic but not simple harmonic, and (c) non-periodic motion? Give period for each case of periodic motion (ω is any positive constant):','[{"s": "14.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Which of the following functions of time represent (a) simple harmonic, (b) periodic but not simple harmonic, and (c) non-periodic motion? Give period for each case of periodic motion (ω is any positive constant):", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29874,72,482,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) sin ωt – cos ωt','[{"s": "(a) sin ωt – cos ωt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29875,72,483,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) sin3 ωt','[{"s": "(b) sin3 ωt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29876,72,484,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) 3 cos (π/4 – 2ωt)','[{"s": "(c) 3 cos (π/4 – 2ωt)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29877,72,485,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) cos ωt + cos 3ωt + cos 5ωt','[{"s": "(d) cos ωt + cos 3ωt + cos 5ωt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
