(29941,72,549,'paragraph',NULL,'at time t = 0. Determine the amplitude of the resulting oscillations in terms of the parameters ω, X 0 and v 0 . [Hint: Start with the equation x=acos(ωt+θ) and note that the initial velocity is negative.]','[{"s": "at time t = 0. Determine the amplitude of the resulting oscillations in terms of the parameters ω,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "X", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". [Hint: Start with the equation x=acos(ωt+θ) and note that the initial velocity is negative.]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29942,73,0,'heading',2,'Chapter Nine',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29943,73,1,'heading',1,'Ray Optics and Optical Instruments',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29944,73,2,'heading',3,'9.1 Introduction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29945,73,3,'paragraph',NULL,'Nature has endowed the human eye (retina) with the sensitivity to detect electromagnetic waves within a small range of the electromagnetic spectrum. Electromagnetic radiation belonging to this region of the spectrum (wavelength of about 400 nm to 750 nm) is called light. It is mainly through light and the sense of vision that we know and interpret the world around us.','[{"s": "Nature has endowed the human eye (retina) with the sensitivity to detect electromagnetic waves within a small range of the electromagnetic spectrum. Electromagnetic radiation belonging to this region of the spectrum (wavelength of about 400 nm to 750 nm) is called light. It is mainly through light and the sense of vision that we know and interpret the world around us.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29946,73,4,'paragraph',NULL,'There are two things that we can intuitively mention about light from common experience. First, that it travels with enormous speed and second, that it travels in a straight line. It took some time for people to realise that the speed of light is finite and measurable. Its presently accepted value in vacuum is c = 2.99792458 × 10 8 m s –1 . For many purposes, it suffices to take c = 3 × 10 8 m s –1 . The speed of light in vacuum is the highest speed attainable in nature.','[{"s": "There are two things that we can intuitively mention about light from common experience. First, that it travels with enormous speed and second, that it travels in a straight line. It took some time for people to realise that the speed of light is finite and measurable. Its presently accepted value in vacuum is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.99792458 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For many purposes, it suffices to take", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The speed of light in vacuum is the highest speed attainable in nature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30271,73,329,'paragraph',NULL,'In the quadrilateral AQNR, two of the angles (at the vertices Q and R) are right angles. Therefore, the sum of the other angles of the quadrilateral is 180°.','[{"s": "In the quadrilateral AQNR, two of the angles (at the vertices Q and R) are right angles. Therefore, the sum of the other angles of the quadrilateral is 180°.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30272,73,330,'paragraph',NULL,'∠ A + ∠QNR = 180°','[{"s": "∠", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ∠QNR = 180°", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30273,73,331,'paragraph',NULL,'From the triangle QNR,','[{"s": "From the triangle QNR,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30274,73,332,'paragraph',NULL,'r 1 + r 2 + ∠QNR = 180°','[{"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∠QNR = 180°", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29947,73,5,'paragraph',NULL,'The intuitive notion that light travels in a straight line seems to contradict what we have learnt in Chapter 8, that light is an electromagnetic wave of wavelength belonging to the visible part of the spectrum. How to reconcile the two facts? The answer is that the wavelength of light is very small compared to the size of ordinary objects that we encounter commonly (generally of the order of a few cm or larger). In this situation, as you will learn in Chapter 10, a light wave can be considered to travel from one point to another, along a straight line joining them. The path is called a ray of light, and a bundle of such rays constitutes a beam of light.','[{"s": "The intuitive notion that light travels in a straight line seems to contradict what we have learnt in Chapter 8, that light is an electromagnetic wave of wavelength belonging to the visible part of the spectrum. How to reconcile the two facts? The answer is that the wavelength of light is very small compared to the size of ordinary objects that we encounter commonly (generally of the order of a few cm or larger). In this situation, as you will learn in Chapter 10, a light wave can be considered to travel from one point to another, along a straight line joining them. The path is called a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ray", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of light, and a bundle of such rays constitutes a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "beam", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29948,73,6,'paragraph',NULL,'In this chapter, we consider the phenomena of reflection, refraction and dispersion of light, using the ray picture of light. Using the basic laws of reflection and refraction, we shall study the image formation by plane and spherical reflecting and refracting surfaces. We then go on to describe the construction and working of some important optical instruments, including the human eye.','[{"s": "In this chapter, we consider the phenomena of reflection, refraction and dispersion of light, using the ray picture of light. Using the basic laws of reflection and refraction, we shall study the image formation by plane and spherical reflecting and refracting surfaces. We then go on to describe the construction and working of some important optical instruments, including the human eye.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29949,73,7,'heading',4,'Particle model of light',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29950,73,8,'paragraph',NULL,'Newton’s fundamental contributions to mathematics, mechanics, and gravitation often blind us to his deep experimental and theoretical study of light. He made pioneering contributions in the field of optics. He further developed the corpuscular model of light proposed by Descartes. It presumes that light energy is concentrated in tiny particles called corpuscles . He further assumed that corpuscles of light were massless elastic particles. With his understanding of mechanics, he could come up with a simple model of reflection and refraction. It is a common observation that a ball bouncing from a smooth plane surface obeys the laws of reflection. When this is an elastic collision, the magnitude of the velocity remains the same. As the surface is smooth, there is no force acting parallel to the surface, so the component of momentum in this direction also remains the same. Only the component perpendicular to the surface, i.e., the normal component of the momentum, gets reversed in reflection. Newton argued that smooth surfaces like mirrors reflect the corpuscles in a similar manner.','[{"s": "Newton’s fundamental contributions to mathematics, mechanics, and gravitation often blind us to his deep experimental and theoretical study of light. He made pioneering contributions in the field of optics. He further developed the corpuscular model of light proposed by Descartes. It presumes that light energy is concentrated in tiny particles called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "corpuscles", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". He further assumed that corpuscles of light were massless elastic particles. With his understanding of mechanics, he could come up with a simple model of reflection and refraction. It is a common observation that a ball bouncing from a smooth plane surface obeys the laws of reflection. When this is an elastic collision, the magnitude of the velocity remains the same. As the surface is smooth, there is no force acting parallel to the surface, so the component of momentum in this direction also remains the same. Only the component perpendicular to the surface, i.e., the normal component of the momentum, gets reversed in reflection. Newton argued that smooth surfaces like mirrors reflect the corpuscles in a similar manner.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29951,73,9,'paragraph',NULL,'In order to explain the phenomena of refraction, Newton postulated that the speed of the corpuscles was greater in water or glass than in air. However, later on it was discovered that the speed of light is less in water or glass than in air.','[{"s": "In order to explain the phenomena of refraction, Newton postulated that the speed of the corpuscles was greater in water or glass than in air. However, later on it was discovered that the speed of light is less in water or glass than in air.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29952,73,10,'paragraph',NULL,'In the field of optics, Newton – the experimenter, was greater than Newton – the theorist. He himself observed many phenomena, which were difficult to understand in terms of particle nature of light. For example, the colours observed due to a thin film of oil on water. Property of partial reflection of light is yet another such example. Everyone who has looked into the water in a pond sees image of the face in it, but also sees the bottom of the pond. Newton argued that some of the corpuscles, which fall on the water, get reflected and some get transmitted. But what property could distinguish these two kinds of corpuscles? Newton had to postulate some kind of unpredictable, chance phenomenon, which decided whether an individual corpuscle would be reflected or not. In explaining other phenomena, however, the corpuscles were presumed to behave as if they are identical. Such a dilemma does not occur in the wave picture of light. An incoming wave can be divided into two weaker waves at the boundary between air and water.','[{"s": "In the field of optics, Newton – the experimenter, was greater than Newton – the theorist. He himself observed many phenomena, which were difficult to understand in terms of particle nature of light. For example, the colours observed due to a thin film of oil on water. Property of partial reflection of light is yet another such example. Everyone who has looked into the water in a pond sees image of the face in it, but also sees the bottom of the pond. Newton argued that some of the corpuscles, which fall on the water, get reflected and some get transmitted. But what property could distinguish these two kinds of corpuscles? Newton had to postulate some kind of unpredictable, chance phenomenon, which decided whether an individual corpuscle would be reflected or not. In explaining other phenomena, however, the corpuscles were presumed to behave as if they are identical. Such a dilemma does not occur in the wave picture of light. An incoming wave can be divided into two weaker waves at the boundary between air and water.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29953,73,11,'heading',3,'9.2 Reflection of Light by Spherical Mirrors',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29955,73,13,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image105.png'),
(29956,73,14,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.1 The incident ray, reflected ray and the normal to the reflecting surface lie in the same plane.','[{"s": "Figure 9.1 The incident ray, reflected ray and the normal to the reflecting surface lie in the same plane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29957,73,15,'paragraph',NULL,'We have already studied that the geometric centre of a spherical mirror is called its pole while that of a spherical lens is called its optical centre. The line joining the pole and the centre of curvature of the spherical mirror is known as the principal axis . In the case of spherical lenses, the principal axis is the line joining the optical centre with its principal focus as you will see later.','[{"s": "We have already studied that the geometric centre of a spherical mirror is called its pole while that of a spherical lens is called its optical centre. The line joining the pole and the centre of curvature of the spherical mirror is known as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "principal axis", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In the case of spherical lenses, the principal axis is the line joining the optical centre with its principal focus as you will see later.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29958,73,16,'heading',4,'9.2.1 Sign convention',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29959,73,17,'paragraph',NULL,'To derive the relevant formulae for reflection by spherical mirrors and refraction by spherical lenses, we must first adopt a sign convention for measuring distances. In this book, we shall follow the Cartesian sign convention . According to this convention, all distances are measured from the pole of the mirror or the optical centre of the lens. The distances measured in the same direction as the incident light are taken as positive and those measured in the direction opposite to the direction of incident light are taken as negative (Fig. 9.2). The heights measured upwards with respect to x -axis and normal to the principal axis ( x -axis) of the mirror/lens are taken as positive (Fig. 9.2). The heights measured downwards are taken as negative.','[{"s": "To derive the relevant formulae for reflection by spherical mirrors and refraction by spherical lenses, we must first adopt a sign convention for measuring distances. In this book, we shall follow the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cartesian sign convention", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". According to this convention, all distances are measured from the pole of the mirror or the optical centre of the lens. The distances measured in the same direction as the incident light are taken as positive and those measured in the direction opposite to the direction of incident light are taken as negative (Fig. 9.2). The heights measured upwards with respect to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-axis and normal to the principal axis (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-axis) of the mirror/lens are taken as positive (Fig. 9.2). The heights measured downwards are taken as negative.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29960,73,18,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image108.png'),
(29961,73,19,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.2 The Cartesian Sign Convention.','[{"s": "Figure 9.2 The Cartesian Sign Convention.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29962,73,20,'paragraph',NULL,'With a common accepted convention, it turns out that a single formula for spherical mirrors and a single formula for spherical lenses can handle all different cases.','[{"s": "With a common accepted convention, it turns out that a single formula for spherical mirrors and a single formula for spherical lenses can handle all different cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29963,73,21,'heading',4,'9.2.2 Focal length of spherical mirrors',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29964,73,22,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.3 shows what happens when a parallel beam of light is incident on (a) a concave mirror, and (b) a convex mirror. We assume that the rays are paraxial principal focus of the mirror. If the parallel paraxial beam of light were incident, making some angle with the principal axis, the reflected rays would converge (or appear to diverge) from a point in a plane through F normal to the principal axis. This is called the focal plane of the mirror [Fig. 9.3(c)].','[{"s": "Figure 9.3 shows what happens when a parallel beam of light is incident on (a) a concave mirror, and (b) a convex mirror. We assume that the rays are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "paraxial", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "principal focus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the mirror. If the parallel paraxial beam of light were incident, making some angle with the principal axis, the reflected rays would converge (or appear to diverge) from a point in a plane through F normal to the principal axis. This is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "focal plane", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the mirror [Fig. 9.3(c)].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29965,73,23,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image107.png'),
(29966,73,24,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image113.png'),
(29967,73,25,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image111.png'),
(29968,73,26,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.3 Focus of a concave and convex mirror.','[{"s": "Figure 9.3 Focus of a concave and convex mirror.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29969,73,27,'paragraph',NULL,'The distance between the focus F and the pole P of the mirror is called the focal length of the mirror, denoted by f . We now show that f = R /2, where R is the radius of curvature of the mirror. The geometry of reflection of an incident ray is shown in Fig. 9.4.','[{"s": "The distance between the focus F and the pole P of the mirror is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "focal length", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the mirror, denoted by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We now show that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2, where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the radius of curvature of the mirror. The geometry of reflection of an incident ray is shown in Fig. 9.4.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29970,73,28,'paragraph',NULL,'Let C be the centre of curvature of the mirror. Consider a ray parallel to the principal axis striking the mirror at M. Then CM will be perpendicular to the mirror at M. Let θ be the angle of incidence, and MD be the perpendicular from M on the principal axis. Then,','[{"s": "Let C be the centre of curvature of the mirror. Consider a ray parallel to the principal axis striking the mirror at M. Then CM will be perpendicular to the mirror at M. Let θ be the angle of incidence, and MD be the perpendicular from M on the principal axis. Then,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29971,73,29,'paragraph',NULL,'∠MCP = θ and ∠MFP = 2θ','[{"s": "∠MCP = θ and ∠MFP = 2θ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29972,73,30,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image118.png'),
(29973,73,31,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.4 Geometry of reflection of an incident ray on (a) concave spherical mirror, and (b) convex spherical mirror.','[{"s": "Figure 9.4 Geometry of reflection of an incident ray on (a) concave spherical mirror, and (b) convex spherical mirror.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29974,73,32,'paragraph',NULL,'Now,','[{"s": "Now,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29975,73,33,'paragraph',NULL,'tanθ = Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image115.png and tan 2θ = Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image121.png (9.1)','[{"s": "tanθ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image115.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and tan 2θ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image121.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(9.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29976,73,34,'paragraph',NULL,'For small θ, which is true for paraxial rays, tanθ ≈ θ, tan 2θ ≈ 2θ. Therefore, Eq. (9.1) gives','[{"s": "For small θ, which is true for paraxial rays, tanθ ≈ θ, tan 2θ ≈ 2θ. Therefore, Eq. (9.1) gives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
