(29981,73,39,'heading',4,'9.2.3 The mirror equation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29982,73,40,'paragraph',NULL,'If rays emanating from a point actually meet at another point after reflection and/or refraction, that point is called the image of the first point. The image is real if the rays actually converge to the point; it is virtual if the rays do not actually meet but appear to diverge from the point when produced backwards. An image is thus a point-to-point correspondence with the object established through reflection and/or refraction.','[{"s": "If rays emanating from a point actually meet at another point after reflection and/or refraction, that point is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "image", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the first point. The image is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "real", "t": "text"}, {"s": "if the rays actually converge to the point; it is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "virtual", "t": "text"}, {"s": "if the rays do not actually meet but appear to diverge from the point when produced backwards. An image is thus a point-to-point correspondence with the object established through reflection and/or refraction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29983,73,41,'paragraph',NULL,'In principle, we can take any two rays emanating from a point on an object, trace their paths, find their point of intersection and thus, obtain the image of the point due to reflection at a spherical mirror. In practice, however, it is convenient to choose any two of the following rays:','[{"s": "In principle, we can take any two rays emanating from a point on an object, trace their paths, find their point of intersection and thus, obtain the image of the point due to reflection at a spherical mirror. In practice, however, it is convenient to choose any two of the following rays:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29984,73,42,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The ray from the point which is parallel to the principal axis. The reflected ray goes through the focus of the mirror.','[{"s": "(i) The ray from the point which is parallel to the principal axis. The reflected ray goes through the focus of the mirror.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29985,73,43,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image127.png'),
(29986,73,44,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.5 Ray diagram for image','[{"s": "Figure 9.5 Ray diagram for image", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29988,73,46,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) The ray passing through (or directed towards) the focus of the concave mirror or appearing to pass through (or directed towards) the focus of a convex mirror. The reflected ray is parallel to the principal axis.','[{"s": "(iii) The ray passing through (or directed towards) the focus of the concave mirror or appearing to pass through (or directed towards) the focus of a convex mirror. The reflected ray is parallel to the principal axis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29989,73,47,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) The ray incident at any angle at the pole. The reflected ray follows laws of reflection.','[{"s": "(iv) The ray incident at any angle at the pole. The reflected ray follows laws of reflection.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29990,73,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.5 shows the ray diagram considering three rays. It shows the image A′B′ (in this case, real) of an object AB formed by a concave mirror. It does not mean that only three rays emanate from the point A. An infinite number of rays emanate from any source, in all directions. Thus, point A′ is image point of A if every ray originating at point A and falling on the concave mirror after reflection passes through the point A′.','[{"s": "Figure 9.5 shows the ray diagram considering three rays. It shows the image A′B′ (in this case, real) of an object AB formed by a concave mirror. It does not mean that only three rays emanate from the point A. An infinite number of rays emanate from any source, in all directions. Thus, point A′ is image point of A if every ray originating at point A and falling on the concave mirror after reflection passes through the point A′.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29992,73,50,'paragraph',NULL,'From Fig. 9.5, the two right-angled triangles A′B′F and MPF are similar. (For paraxial rays, MP can be considered to be a straight line perpendicular to CP.) Therefore,','[{"s": "From Fig. 9.5, the two right-angled triangles A′B′F and MPF are similar. (For paraxial rays, MP can be considered to be a straight line perpendicular to CP.) Therefore,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29993,73,51,'image',NULL,'111',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image129.png'),
(29994,73,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (9.6) is a relation involving magnitude of distances. We now apply the sign convention. We note that light travels from the object to the mirror MPn. Hence this is taken as the positive direction. To reach the object AB, image A′B′ as well as the focus F from the pole P, we have to travel opposite to the direction of incident light. Hence, all the three will have negative signs. Thus,','[{"s": "Equation (9.6) is a relation involving magnitude of distances. We now apply the sign convention. We note that light travels from the object to the mirror MPn. Hence this is taken as the positive direction. To reach the object AB, image A′B′ as well as the focus F from the pole P, we have to travel opposite to the direction of incident light. Hence, all the three will have negative signs. Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
