(30046,73,104,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, it can be seen that for u = –29 m, –19 m and –9 m, the speed with which the image appears to move is','[{"s": "Similarly, it can be seen that for u = –29 m, –19 m and –9 m, the speed with which the image appears to move is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30047,73,105,'paragraph',NULL,'Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image23.png respectively.','[{"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image23.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30048,73,106,'paragraph',NULL,'Although the jogger has been moving with a constant speed, the speed of his/her image appears to increase substantially as he/she moves closer to the mirror. This phenomenon can be noticed by any person sitting in a stationary car or a bus. In case of moving vehicles, a similar phenomenon could be observed if the vehicle in the rear is moving closer with a constant speed.','[{"s": "Although the jogger has been moving with a constant speed, the speed of his/her image appears to increase substantially as he/she moves closer to the mirror. This phenomenon can be noticed by any person sitting in a stationary car or a bus. In case of moving vehicles, a similar phenomenon could be observed if the vehicle in the rear is moving closer with a constant speed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30049,73,107,'heading',3,'9.3 Refraction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30050,73,108,'paragraph',NULL,'When a beam of light encounters another transparent medium, a part of light gets reflected back into the first medium while the rest enters the other. A ray of light represents a beam. The direction of propagation of an obliquely incident (0°< i < 90°) ray of light that enters the other medium, changes at the interface of the two media. This phenomenon is called refraction of light . Snell experimentally obtained the following laws of refraction:','[{"s": "When a beam of light encounters another transparent medium, a part of light gets reflected back into the first medium while the rest enters the other. A ray of light represents a beam. The direction of propagation of an obliquely incident (0°<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< 90°) ray of light that enters the other medium, changes at the interface of the two media. This phenomenon is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "refraction of light", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Snell experimentally obtained the following laws of refraction:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30051,73,109,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) The incident ray, the refracted ray and the normal to the interface at the point of incidence, all lie in the same plane.','[{"s": "(i) The incident ray, the refracted ray and the normal to the interface at the point of incidence, all lie in the same plane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30053,73,111,'paragraph',NULL,'Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image24.png (9.10)','[{"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image24.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(9.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30055,73,113,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image25.png'),
(30056,73,114,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.8 Refraction and reflection of light.','[{"s": "Figure 9.8 Refraction and reflection of light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30277,73,335,'paragraph',NULL,'The total deviation δ is the sum of deviations at the two faces,','[{"s": "The total deviation δ is the sum of deviations at the two faces,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30278,73,336,'paragraph',NULL,'δ = ( i – r 1 ) + ( e – r 2 )','[{"s": "δ = (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") + (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e – r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30279,73,337,'paragraph',NULL,'that is,','[{"s": "that is,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30057,73,115,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eq. (9.10), if n 21 > 1, r < i , i.e., the refracted ray bends towards the normal. In such a case medium 2 is said to be optically denser (or denser , in short) than medium 1. On the other hand, if n 21 <1, r > i , the refracted ray bends away from the normal. This is the case when incident ray in a denser medium refracts into a rarer medium.','[{"s": "From Eq. (9.10), if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> 1,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", i.e., the refracted ray bends towards the normal. In such a case medium 2 is said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "optically denser", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "denser", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", in short) than medium 1. On the other hand, if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<1,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the refracted ray bends away from the normal. This is the case when incident ray in a denser medium refracts into a rarer medium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30058,73,116,'paragraph',NULL,'Note: Optical density should not be confused with mass density, which is mass per unit volume. It is possible that mass density of an optically denser medium may be less than that of an optically rarer medium (optical density is the ratio of the speed of light in two media). For example, turpentine and water. Mass density of turpentine is less than that of water but its optical density is higher.','[{"s": "Note:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Optical density should not be confused with mass density, which is mass per unit volume. It is possible that mass density of an optically denser medium may be less than that of an optically rarer medium (optical density is the ratio of the speed of light in two media). For example, turpentine and water. Mass density of turpentine is less than that of water but its optical density is higher.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30059,73,117,'paragraph',NULL,'If n 21 is the refractive index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1 and n 12 the refractive index of medium 1 with respect to medium 2, then it should be clear that','[{"s": "If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the refractive index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the refractive index of medium 1 with respect to medium 2, then it should be clear that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30060,73,118,'paragraph',NULL,'Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image26.png (9.11)','[{"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image26.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(9.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30061,73,119,'paragraph',NULL,'It also follows that if n 32 is the refractive index of medium 3 with respect to medium 2 then n 32 = n 31 × n 12 , where n 31 is the refractive index of medium 3 with respect to medium 1.','[{"s": "It also follows that if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "32", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the refractive index of medium 3 with respect to medium 2 then", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "32", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the refractive index of medium 3 with respect to medium 1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30062,73,120,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image27.png'),
