(30191,73,249,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eqs. (9.19) and (9.20), we get','[{"s": "From Eqs. (9.19) and (9.20), we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30192,73,250,'paragraph',NULL,'Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image38.png (9.22)','[{"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image38.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(9.22)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30194,73,252,'paragraph',NULL,'BO = – u , DI = + v , we get','[{"s": "BO = –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "u", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", DI = +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30195,73,253,'paragraph',NULL,'Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image39.png (9.23)','[{"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image39.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(9.23)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30196,73,254,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (9.23) is the familiar thin lens formula . Though we derived it for a real image formed by a convex lens, the formula is valid for both convex as well as concave lenses and for both real and virtual images.','[{"s": "Equation (9.23) is the familiar", "t": "text"}, {"s": "thin lens formula", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Though we derived it for a real image formed by a convex lens, the formula is valid for both convex as well as concave lenses and for both real and virtual images.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30197,73,255,'paragraph',NULL,'It is worth mentioning that the two foci, F and F′, of a double convex or concave lens are equidistant from the optical centre. The focus on the side of the (original) source of light is called the first focal point , whereas the other is called the second focal point .','[{"s": "It is worth mentioning that the two foci, F and F′, of a double convex or concave lens are equidistant from the optical centre. The focus on the side of the (original) source of light is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "first focal point", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", whereas the other is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "second focal point", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30198,73,256,'paragraph',NULL,'To find the image of an object by a lens, we can, in principle, take any two rays emanating from a point on an object; trace their paths using the laws of refraction and find the point where the refracted rays meet (or appear to meet). In practice, however, it is convenient to choose any two of the following rays:','[{"s": "To find the image of an object by a lens, we can, in principle, take any two rays emanating from a point on an object; trace their paths using the laws of refraction and find the point where the refracted rays meet (or appear to meet). In practice, however, it is convenient to choose any two of the following rays:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30200,73,258,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) A ray of light, passing through the optical centre of the lens, emerges without any deviation after refraction.','[{"s": "(ii) A ray of light, passing through the optical centre of the lens, emerges without any deviation after refraction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30201,73,259,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) A ray of light passing through the first principal focus (for a convex lens) or appearing to meet at it (for a concave lens) emerges parallel to the principal axis after refraction.','[{"s": "(iii) A ray of light passing through the first principal focus (for a convex lens) or appearing to meet at it (for a concave lens) emerges parallel to the principal axis after refraction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30202,73,260,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image40.png'),
(30203,73,261,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.19 Tracing rays through (a) convex lens (b) concave lens.','[{"s": "Figure 9.19 Tracing rays through (a) convex lens (b) concave lens.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30204,73,262,'paragraph',NULL,'Figures 9.19(a) and (b) illustrate these rules for a convex and a concave lens, respectively. You should practice drawing similar ray diagrams for different positions of the object with respect to the lens and also verify that the lens formula, Eq. (9.23), holds good for all cases.','[{"s": "Figures 9.19(a) and (b) illustrate these rules for a convex and a concave lens, respectively. You should practice drawing similar ray diagrams for different positions of the object with respect to the lens and also verify that the lens formula, Eq. (9.23), holds good for all cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30205,73,263,'paragraph',NULL,'Here again it must be remembered that each point on an object gives out infinite number of rays. All these rays will pass through the same image point after refraction at the lens.','[{"s": "Here again it must be remembered that each point on an object gives out infinite number of rays. All these rays will pass through the same image point after refraction at the lens.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30206,73,264,'paragraph',NULL,'Magnification ( m ) produced by a lens is defined, like that for a mirror, as the ratio of the size of the image to that of the object. Proceeding in the same way as for spherical mirrors, it is easily seen that for a lens','[{"s": "Magnification (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") produced by a lens is defined, like that for a mirror, as the ratio of the size of the image to that of the object. Proceeding in the same way as for spherical mirrors, it is easily seen that for a lens", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30207,73,265,'paragraph',NULL,'m = ℎ\'/ℎ = v/u (9.24)','[{"s": "m = ℎ\'/ℎ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v/u", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(9.24)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30208,73,266,'paragraph',NULL,'When we apply the sign convention, we see that, for erect (and virtual) image formed by a convex or concave lens, m is positive, while for an inverted (and real) image, m is negative.','[{"s": "When we apply the sign convention, we see that, for erect (and virtual) image formed by a convex or concave lens,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is positive, while for an inverted (and real) image,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is negative.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30209,73,267,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 9.7 A magician during a show makes a glass lens with n = 1.47 disappear in a trough of liquid. What is the refractive index of the liquid? Could the liquid be water?','[{"s": "Example 9.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A magician during a show makes a glass lens with n = 1.47 disappear in a trough of liquid. What is the refractive index of the liquid? Could the liquid be water?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30210,73,268,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30211,73,269,'paragraph',NULL,'The refractive index of the liquid must be equal to 1.47 in order to make the lens disappear. This me ans n 1 = n 2 . Th is gives 1/f =0 or f → ∞. The lens in the liquid will act like a plane sheet of glass. No, the liquid is not water. It could be glycerine.','[{"s": "The refractive index of the liquid must be equal to 1.47 in order to make the lens disappear. This me", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ans n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is gives 1/f =0 or f → ∞. The lens in the liquid will act like a plane sheet of glass. No, the liquid is not water. It could be glycerine.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30212,73,270,'heading',4,'9.5.3 Power of a lens',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30213,73,271,'paragraph',NULL,'Power of a lens is a measure of the convergence or divergence, which a lens introduces in the light falling on it. Clearly, a lens of shorter focal length bends the incident light more, while converging it in case of a convex lens and diverging it in case of a concave lens. The power P of a lens is defined as the tangent of the angle by which it converges or diverges a beam of light falling at unit distant from the optical centre (Fig. 9.20).','[{"s": "Power of a lens is a measure of the convergence or divergence, which a lens introduces in the light falling on it. Clearly, a lens of shorter focal length bends the incident light more, while converging it in case of a convex lens and diverging it in case of a concave lens. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "power", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of a lens is defined as the tangent of the angle by which it converges or diverges a beam of light falling at unit distant from the optical centre (Fig. 9.20).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30214,73,272,'paragraph',NULL,'Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image41.png or Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image31.png for small value of δ. Thus,','[{"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image41.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image31.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "for small value of δ. Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
