(30293,73,351,'paragraph',NULL,'Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image53.png (9.38)','[{"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image53.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(9.38)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30294,73,352,'paragraph',NULL,'The angles A and D m can be measured experimentally. Equation (9.38) thus provides a method of determining refractive index of the material of the prism.','[{"s": "The angles", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be measured experimentally. Equation (9.38) thus provides a method of determining refractive index of the material of the prism.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30295,73,353,'paragraph',NULL,'For a small angle prism, i.e., a thin prism, D m is also very small, and we get','[{"s": "For a small angle prism, i.e., a thin prism,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is also very small, and we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30296,73,354,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image55.png'),
(30297,73,355,'paragraph',NULL,'D m = ( n 21 –1) A','[{"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30298,73,356,'paragraph',NULL,'It implies that, thin prisms do not deviate light much.','[{"s": "It implies that, thin prisms do not deviate light much.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30299,73,357,'heading',4,'9.7 Dispersion by a Prism',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30300,73,358,'paragraph',NULL,'It has been known for a long time that when a narrow beam of sunlight, usually called white light, is incident on a glass prism, the emergent light is seen to be consisting of several colours. There is actually a continuous variation of colour, but broadly, the different colours that appear in sequence are: violet, indigo, blue, green, yellow, orange and red (given by the acronym VIBGYOR). The red light bends the least, while the violet light bends the most (Fig. 9.25).','[{"s": "It has been known for a long time that when a narrow beam of sunlight, usually called white light, is incident on a glass prism, the emergent light is seen to be consisting of several colours. There is actually a continuous variation of colour, but broadly, the different colours that appear in sequence are: violet, indigo, blue, green, yellow, orange and red (given by the acronym VIBGYOR). The red light bends the least, while the violet light bends the most (Fig. 9.25).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30301,73,359,'paragraph',NULL,'The phenomenon of splitting of light into different colours is known as dispersion . The pattern of colour components of light is called the spectrum of light. The word spectrum is now used in a much more general sense: we discussed in Chapter 8 the electro- magnetic spectrum over the large range of wavelengths, from γ-rays to radio waves, of which the spectrum of light (visible spectrum) is only a small part.','[{"s": "The phenomenon of splitting of light into different colours is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dispersion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The pattern of colour components of light is called the spectrum of light. The word", "t": "text"}, {"s": "spectrum", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is now used in a much more general sense: we discussed in Chapter 8 the electro- magnetic spectrum over the large range of wavelengths, from γ-rays to radio waves, of which the spectrum of light (visible spectrum) is only a small part.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30302,73,360,'paragraph',NULL,'Though the reason for appearance of spectrum is now common knowledge, it was a matter of much debate in the history of physics. Does the prism itself create colour in some way or does it only separate the colours already present in white light?','[{"s": "Though the reason for appearance of spectrum is now common knowledge, it was a matter of much debate in the history of physics. Does the prism itself create colour in some way or does it only separate the colours already present in white light?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30303,73,361,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image116.png'),
(30304,73,362,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.25 Dispersion of sunlight or white light on passing through a glass prism. The relative deviation of different colours shown is highly exaggerated.','[{"s": "Figure 9.25 Dispersion of sunlight or white light on passing through a glass prism. The relative deviation of different colours shown is highly exaggerated.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30305,73,363,'paragraph',NULL,'In a classic experiment known for its simplicity but great significance, Isaac Newton settled the issue once for all. He put another similar prism, but in an inverted position, and let the emergent beam from the first prism fall on the second prism (Fig. 9.26). The resulting emergent beam was found to be white light. The explanation was clear— the first prism splits the white light into its component colours, while the inverted prism recombines them to give white light. Thus, white light itself consists of light of different colours, which are separated by the prism.','[{"s": "In a classic experiment known for its simplicity but great significance, Isaac Newton settled the issue once for all. He put another similar prism, but in an inverted position, and let the emergent beam from the first prism fall on the second prism (Fig. 9.26). The resulting emergent beam was found to be white light. The explanation was clear— the first prism splits the white light into its component colours, while the inverted prism recombines them to give white light. Thus, white light itself consists of light of different colours, which are separated by the prism.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30306,73,364,'paragraph',NULL,'It must be understood here that a ray of light, as defined mathematically, does not exist. An actual ray is really a beam of many rays of light. Each ray splits into component colours when it enters the glass prism. When those coloured rays come out on the other side, they again produce a white beam.','[{"s": "It must be understood here that a ray of light, as defined mathematically, does not exist. An actual ray is really a beam of many rays of light. Each ray splits into component colours when it enters the glass prism. When those coloured rays come out on the other side, they again produce a white beam.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30307,73,365,'image',NULL,'114',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image119.png'),
(30308,73,366,'paragraph',NULL,'FIGURE 9.26 Schematic diagram of Newton’s classic','[{"s": "FIGURE 9.26 Schematic diagram of Newton’s classic", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30309,73,367,'paragraph',NULL,'experiment on dispersion of white light.','[{"s": "experiment on dispersion of white light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30310,73,368,'paragraph',NULL,'We now know that colour is associated with wavelength of light. In the visible spectrum, red light is at the long wavelength end (~700 nm) while the violet light is at the short wavelength end (~ 400 nm). Dispersion takes place because the refractive index of medium for different wavelengths (colours) is different. For example, the bending of red component of white light is least while it is most for the violet. Equivalently, red light travels faster than violet light in a glass prism. Table 9.2 gives the refractive indices for different wavelength for crown glass and flint glass. Thick lenses could be assumed as made of many prisms, therefore, thick lenses show chromatic aberration due to dispersion of light. When white light passes through thick lenses, red and blue colours focus at different points. This phenomena is known as chromatic aberration.','[{"s": "We now know that colour is associated with wavelength of light. In the visible spectrum, red light is at the long wavelength end (~700 nm) while the violet light is at the short wavelength end (~ 400 nm). Dispersion takes place because the refractive index of medium for different wavelengths (colours) is different. For example, the bending of red component of white light is least while it is most for the violet. Equivalently, red light travels faster than violet light in a glass prism. Table 9.2 gives the refractive indices for different wavelength for crown glass and flint glass. Thick lenses could be assumed as made of many prisms, therefore, thick lenses show", "t": "text"}, {"s": "chromatic aberration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "due to dispersion of light. When white light passes through thick lenses, red and blue colours focus at different points. This phenomena is known as chromatic aberration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30311,73,369,'image',NULL,'115',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image122.png'),
(30312,73,370,'paragraph',NULL,'The variation of refractive index with wavelength may be more pronounced in some media than the other. In vacuum, of course, the speed of light is independent of wavelength. Thus, vacuum (or air approximately) is a non-dispersive medium in which all colours travel with the same speed. This also follows from the fact that sunlight reaches us in the form of white light and not as its components. On the other hand, glass is a dispersive medium.','[{"s": "The variation of refractive index with wavelength may be more pronounced in some media than the other. In vacuum, of course, the speed of light is independent of wavelength. Thus, vacuum (or air approximately) is a non-dispersive medium in which all colours travel with the same speed. This also follows from the fact that sunlight reaches us in the form of white light and not as its components. On the other hand, glass is a dispersive medium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30313,73,371,'heading',3,'9.8 Some Natural Phenomena due to Sunlight',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30314,73,372,'paragraph',NULL,'The Interplay of light with things around us gives rise to several beautiful phenomena. The spectacles of colours that we see around us all the time is possible due to sunlight. The blue of the sky, white clouds, the red-hue at sunrise and sunset, the rainbow, the brilliant colours of some pearls, shells, and wings of birds, are just a few of the natural wonders we are used to. We describe some of them here from the point of view of physics.','[{"s": "The Interplay of light with things around us gives rise to several beautiful phenomena. The spectacles of colours that we see around us all the time is possible due to sunlight. The blue of the sky, white clouds, the red-hue at sunrise and sunset, the rainbow, the brilliant colours of some pearls, shells, and wings of birds, are just a few of the natural wonders we are used to. We describe some of them here from the point of view of physics.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30315,73,373,'heading',4,'9.8.1 The rainbow',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30317,73,375,'paragraph',NULL,'In order to understand the formation of rainbows, consider Fig. (9.27(a). Sunlight is first refracted as it enters a raindrop, which causes the different wavelengths (colours) of white light to separate. Longer wangelength of light (red) are bent the least while the shorter wavelength (violet) are bent the most. Next, these component rays strike the inner surface of the water drop and get internally reflected if the angle between the refracted ray and normal to the drop surface is greater then the critical angle (48° in this case). The reflected light is refracted again when it comes out of the drop, as shown in the figure. It is found that the violet light emerges at an angle of 40° related to the incoming sunlight and red light emerges at an angle of 42°. For other colours, angles lie in between these two values.','[{"s": "In order to understand the formation of rainbows, consider Fig. (9.27(a). Sunlight is first refracted as it enters a raindrop, which causes the different wavelengths (colours) of white light to separate. Longer wangelength of light (red) are bent the least while the shorter wavelength (violet) are bent the most. Next, these component rays strike the inner surface of the water drop and get internally reflected if the angle between the refracted ray and normal to the drop surface is greater then the critical angle (48° in this case). The reflected light is refracted again when it comes out of the drop, as shown in the figure. It is found that the violet light emerges at an angle of 40° related to the incoming sunlight and red light emerges at an angle of 42°. For other colours, angles lie in between these two values.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30318,73,376,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.27(b) explains the formation of primary rainbow. We see that red light from drop 1 and violet light from drop 2 reach the observer’s eye. The violet from drop 1 and red light from drop 2 are directed at level above or below the observer. Thus the observer sees a rainbow with red colour on the top and violet on the bottom. The primary rainbow is a result of three-step process, that is, refraction, reflection and refraction.','[{"s": "Figure 9.27(b) explains the formation of primary rainbow. We see that red light from drop 1 and violet light from drop 2 reach the observer’s eye. The violet from drop 1 and red light from drop 2 are directed at level above or below the observer. Thus the observer sees a rainbow with red colour on the top and violet on the bottom. The primary rainbow is a result of three-step process, that is, refraction, reflection and refraction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30320,73,378,'image',NULL,'116',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image124.png'),
(30321,73,379,'paragraph',NULL,'FIGURE 9.27 Rainbow: (a) The sun rays incident on a water drop get refracted twice and reflected internally by a drop; (b) Enlarged view of internal reflection and refraction of a ray of light inside a drop forming primary rainbow; and (c) secondary rainbow is formed by rays undergoing internal reflection twice inside the drop.','[{"s": "FIGURE 9.27 Rainbow: (a) The sun rays incident on a water drop get refracted twice and reflected internally by a drop; (b) Enlarged view of internal reflection and refraction of a ray of light inside a drop forming primary rainbow; and (c) secondary rainbow is formed by rays undergoing internal reflection twice inside the drop.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30322,73,380,'heading',4,'9.8.2 Scattering of light',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30323,73,381,'paragraph',NULL,'As sunlight travels through the earth’s atmosphere, it gets scattered (changes its direction) by the atmospheric particles. Light of shorter wavelengths is scattered much more than light of longer wavelengths. (The amount of scattering is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the wavelength. This is known as Rayleigh scattering). Hence, the bluish colour predominates in a clear sky, since blue has a shorter wave­ length than red and is scattered much more strongly. In fact, violet gets scattered even more than blue, having a shorter wavelength. But since our eyes are more sensitive to blue than violet, we see the sky blue.','[{"s": "As sunlight travels through the earth’s atmosphere, it gets", "t": "text"}, {"s": "scattered", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(changes its direction) by the atmospheric particles. Light of shorter wavelengths is scattered much more than light of longer wavelengths. (The amount of scattering is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the wavelength. This is known as Rayleigh scattering). Hence, the bluish colour predominates in a clear sky, since blue has a shorter wave­ length than red and is scattered much more strongly. In fact, violet gets scattered even more than blue, having a shorter wavelength. But since our eyes are more sensitive to blue than violet, we see the sky blue.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30324,73,382,'paragraph',NULL,'Large particles like dust and water droplets present in the atmosphere behave differently. The relevant quantity here is the relative size of the wavelength of light λ, and the scatterer (of typical size, say , a ). For a << λ, one has Rayleigh scattering which is proportional to 1/λ 4 . For a >> λ, i.e., large scattering objects (for example, raindrops, large dust or ice particles) this is not true; all wavelengths are scattered nearly equally. Thus, clouds which have droplets of water with a >> λ are generally white.','[{"s": "Large particles like dust and water droplets present in the atmosphere behave differently. The relevant quantity here is the relative size of the wavelength of light λ, and the scatterer (of typical size, say", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). For", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<< λ, one has Rayleigh scattering which is proportional to 1/λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">> λ, i.e., large scattering objects (for example, raindrops, large dust or ice particles) this is not true; all wavelengths are scattered nearly equally. Thus, clouds which have droplets of water with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">> λ are generally white.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30325,73,383,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image126.png'),
(30326,73,384,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.28 Sunlight travels through a longer distance in the atmosphere at sunset and sunrise.','[{"s": "Figure 9.28 Sunlight travels through a longer distance in the atmosphere at sunset and sunrise.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30328,73,386,'heading',3,'9.9 Optical Instruments',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30329,73,387,'paragraph',NULL,'A number of optical devices and instruments have been designed utilising reflecting and refracting properties of mirrors, lenses and prisms. Periscope, kaleidoscope, binoculars, telescopes, microscopes are some examples of optical devices and instruments that are in common use. Our eye is, of course, one of the most important optical device the nature has endowed us with. Starting with the eye, we then go on to describe the principles of working of the microscope and the telescope.','[{"s": "A number of optical devices and instruments have been designed utilising reflecting and refracting properties of mirrors, lenses and prisms. Periscope, kaleidoscope, binoculars, telescopes, microscopes are some examples of optical devices and instruments that are in common use. Our eye is, of course, one of the most important optical device the nature has endowed us with. Starting with the eye, we then go on to describe the principles of working of the microscope and the telescope.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30330,73,388,'heading',4,'9.9.1 The eye',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30331,73,389,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.29 (a) shows the eye. Light enters the eye through a curved front surface, the cornea. It passes through the pupil which is the central hole in the iris. The size of the pupil can change under control of muscles. The light is further focussed by the eye lens on the retina. The retina is a film of nerve fibres covering the curved back surface of the eye. The retina contains rods and cones which sense light intensity and colour, respectively, and transmit electrical signals via the optic nerve to the brain which finally processes this information. The shape (curvature) and therefore the focal length of the lens can be modified somewhat by the ciliary muscles. For example, when the muscle is relaxed, the focal length is about 2.5 cm and objects at infinity are in sharp focus on the retina. When the object is brought closer to the eye, in order to maintain the same image-lens distance (≅ 2.5 cm), the focal length of the eye lens becomes shorter by the action of the ciliary muscles. This property of the eye is called accommodation . If the object is too close to the eye, the lens cannot curve enough to focus the image on to the retina, and the image is blurred. The closest distance for which the lens can focus light on the retina is called the least distance of distinct vision , or the near point . The standard value for normal vision is taken as 25 cm. (Often the near point is given the symbol D.) This distance increases with age, because of the decreasing effectiveness of the ciliary muscle and the loss of flexibility of the lens. The near point may be as close as about 7 to 8 cm in a child ten years of age, and may increase to as much as 200 cm at 60 years of age. Thus, if an elderly person tries to read a book at about 25 cm from the eye, the image appears blurred. This condition (defect of the eye) is called presbyopia . It is corrected by using a converging lens for reading.','[{"s": "Figure 9.29 (a) shows the eye. Light enters the eye through a curved front surface, the cornea. It passes through the pupil which is the central hole in the iris. The size of the pupil can change under control of muscles. The light is further focussed by the eye lens on the retina. The retina is a film of nerve fibres covering the curved back surface of the eye. The retina contains rods and cones which sense light intensity and colour, respectively, and transmit electrical signals via the optic nerve to the brain which finally processes this information. The shape (curvature) and therefore the focal length of the lens can be modified somewhat by the ciliary muscles. For example, when the muscle is relaxed, the focal length is about 2.5 cm and objects at infinity are in sharp focus on the retina. When the object is brought closer to the eye, in order to maintain the same image-lens distance (≅ 2.5 cm), the focal length of the eye lens becomes shorter by the action of the ciliary muscles. This property of the eye is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "accommodation", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If the object is too close to the eye, the lens cannot curve enough to focus the image on to the retina, and the image is blurred. The closest distance for which the lens can focus light on the retina is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "least distance of distinct vision", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", or the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "near point", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The standard value for normal vision is taken as 25 cm. (Often the near point is given the symbol D.) This distance increases with age, because of the decreasing effectiveness of the ciliary muscle and the loss of flexibility of the lens. The near point may be as close as about 7 to 8 cm in a child ten years of age, and may increase to as much as 200 cm at 60 years of age. Thus, if an elderly person tries to read a book at about 25 cm from the eye, the image appears blurred. This condition (defect of the eye) is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "presbyopia", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is corrected by using a converging lens for reading.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30332,73,390,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, our eyes are marvellous organs that have the capability to interpret incoming electromagnetic waves as images through a complex process. These are our greatest assets and we must take proper care to protect them. Imagine the world without a pair of functional eyes. Yet many amongst us bravely face this challenge by effectively overcoming their limitations to lead a normal life. They deserve our appreciation for their courage and conviction.','[{"s": "Thus, our eyes are marvellous organs that have the capability to interpret incoming electromagnetic waves as images through a complex process. These are our greatest assets and we must take proper care to protect them. Imagine the world without a pair of functional eyes. Yet many amongst us bravely face this challenge by effectively overcoming their limitations to lead a normal life. They deserve our appreciation for their courage and conviction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30333,73,391,'paragraph',NULL,'In spite of all precautions and proactive action, our eyes may develop some defects due to various reasons. We shall restrict our discussion to some common optical defects of the eye. For example, the light from a distant object arriving at the eye-lens may get converged at a point in front of the retina. This type of defect is called nearsightedness or myopia . This means that the eye is producing too much convergence in the incident beam. To compensate this, we interpose a concave lens between the eye and the object, with the diverging effect desired to get the image focussed on the retina [Fig. 9.29(b)].','[{"s": "In spite of all precautions and proactive action, our eyes may develop some defects due to various reasons. We shall restrict our discussion to some common optical defects of the eye. For example, the light from a distant object arriving at the eye-lens may get converged at a point in front of the retina. This type of defect is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nearsightedness", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "myopia", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This means that the eye is producing too much convergence in the incident beam. To compensate this, we interpose a concave lens between the eye and the object, with the diverging effect desired to get the image focussed on the retina [Fig. 9.29(b)].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30334,73,392,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, if the eye-lens focusses the incoming light at a point behind the retina, a convergent lens is needed to compensate for the defect in vision. This defect is called farsightedness or hypermetropia [Fig. 9.29(c)].','[{"s": "Similarly, if the eye-lens focusses the incoming light at a point behind the retina, a convergent lens is needed to compensate for the defect in vision. This defect is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "farsightedness", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hypermetropia", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[Fig. 9.29(c)].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30335,73,393,'paragraph',NULL,'Another common defect of vision is called astigmatism. This occurs when the cornea is not spherical in shape. For example, the cornea could have a larger curvature in the vertical plane than in the horizontal plane or vice-versa. If a person with such a defect in eye-lens looks at a wire mesh or a grid of lines, focussing in either the vertical or the horizontal plane may not be as sharp as in the other plane. Astigmatism results in lines in one direction being well focussed while those in a perpendicular direction may appear distorted [Fig. 9.29(d)]. Astigmatism can be corrected by using a cylindrical lens of desired radius of curvature with an appropriately directed axis. This defect can occur along with myopia or hypermetropia.','[{"s": "Another common defect of vision is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "astigmatism.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This occurs when the cornea is not spherical in shape. For example, the cornea could have a larger curvature in the vertical plane than in the horizontal plane or vice-versa. If a person with such a defect in eye-lens looks at a wire mesh or a grid of lines, focussing in either the vertical or the horizontal plane may not be as sharp as in the other plane. Astigmatism results in lines in one direction being well focussed while those in a perpendicular direction may appear distorted [Fig. 9.29(d)]. Astigmatism can be corrected by using a cylindrical lens of desired radius of curvature with an appropriately directed axis. This defect can occur along with myopia or hypermetropia.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30336,73,394,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image128.png'),
(30337,73,395,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image18.png'),
(30338,73,396,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image17.png'),
(30339,73,397,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image21.png'),
(30340,73,398,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 9.10 What focal length should the reading spectacles have for a person for whom the least distance of distinct vision is 50 cm?','[{"s": "Example 9.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What focal length should the reading spectacles have for a person for whom the least distance of distinct vision is 50 cm?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30341,73,399,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution The distance of normal vision is 25 cm. So if a book is at u = –25 cm, its image should be formed at v = –50 cm. Therefore, the desired focal length is given by','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The distance of normal vision is 25 cm. So if a book is at u = –25 cm, its image should be formed at v = –50 cm. Therefore, the desired focal length is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30342,73,400,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image19.png'),
(30343,73,401,'paragraph',NULL,'or Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image14.png','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30344,73,402,'paragraph',NULL,'or f = + 50 cm (convex lens).','[{"s": "or f = + 50 cm (convex lens).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30346,73,404,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The far point of a myopic person is 80 cm in front of the eye. What is the power of the lens required to enable him to see very distant objects clearly?','[{"s": "(a) The far point of a myopic person is 80 cm in front of the eye. What is the power of the lens required to enable him to see very distant objects clearly?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30347,73,405,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) In what way does the corrective lens help the above person? Does the lens magnify very distant objects? Explain carefully.','[{"s": "(b) In what way does the corrective lens help the above person? Does the lens magnify very distant objects? Explain carefully.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30348,73,406,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The above person prefers to remove his spectacles while reading a book. Explain why?','[{"s": "(c) The above person prefers to remove his spectacles while reading a book. Explain why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30349,73,407,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30350,73,408,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Solving as in the previous example, we find that the person should use a concave lens of focal length = – 80 cm, i.e., of power = – 1.25 dioptres.','[{"s": "(a) Solving as in the previous example, we find that the person should use a concave lens of focal length = – 80 cm, i.e., of power = – 1.25 dioptres.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30351,73,409,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) No. The concave lens, in fact, reduces the size of the object, but the angle subtended by the distant object at the eye is the same as the angle subtended by the image (at the far point) at the eye. The eye is able to see distant objects not because the corrective lens magnifies the object, but because it brings the object (i.e., it produces virtual image of the object) at the far point of the eye which then can be focussed by the eye-lens on the retina.','[{"s": "(b) No. The concave lens, in fact, reduces the size of the object, but the angle subtended by the distant object at the eye is the same as the angle subtended by the image (at the far point) at the eye. The eye is able to see distant objects not because the corrective lens magnifies the object, but because it brings the object (i.e., it produces virtual image of the object) at the far point of the eye which then can be focussed by the eye-lens on the retina.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30352,73,410,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 9.12 (a) The near point of a hypermetropic person is 75 cm from the eye. What is the power of the lens required to enable the person to read clearly a book held at 25 cm from the eye? (b) In what way does the corrective lens help the above person? Does the lens magnify objects held near the eye? (c) The above person prefers to remove the spectacles while looking at the sky. Explain why?','[{"s": "Example 9.12 (a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The near point of a hypermetropic person is 75 cm from the eye. What is the power of the lens required to enable the person to read clearly a book held at 25 cm from the eye? (b) In what way does the corrective lens help the above person? Does the lens magnify objects held near the eye? (c) The above person prefers to remove the spectacles while looking at the sky. Explain why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30353,73,411,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30354,73,412,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) u = – 25 cm, v = – 75 cm','[{"s": "(a) u = – 25 cm, v = – 75 cm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30355,73,413,'paragraph',NULL,'1/f = 1/25 – 1/75, i.e., f = 37.5 cm.','[{"s": "1/f = 1/25 – 1/75, i.e., f = 37.5 cm.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30356,73,414,'paragraph',NULL,'The corrective lens needs to have a converging power of +2.67 dioptres.','[{"s": "The corrective lens needs to have a converging power of +2.67 dioptres.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30357,73,415,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The corrective lens produces a virtual image (at 75 cm) of an object at 25 cm. The angular size of this image is the same as that of the object. In this sense the lens does not magnify the object but merely brings the object to the near point of the hypermetric eye, which then gets focussed on the retina. However, the angular size is greater than that of the same object at the near point (75 cm) viewed without the spectacles.','[{"s": "(b) The corrective lens produces a virtual image (at 75 cm) of an object at 25 cm. The angular size of this image is the same as that of the object. In this sense the lens does not magnify the object but merely brings the object to the near point of the hypermetric eye, which then gets focussed on the retina. However, the angular size is greater than that of the same object at the near point (75 cm) viewed without the spectacles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30358,73,416,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) A hypermetropic eye may have normal far point i.e., it may have enough converging power to focus parallel rays from infinity on the retina of the shortened eyeball. Wearing spectacles of converging lenses (used for near vision) will amount to more converging power than needed for parallel rays. Hence the person prefers not to use the spectacles for far objects.','[{"s": "(c) A hypermetropic eye may have normal far point i.e., it may have enough converging power to focus parallel rays from infinity on the retina of the shortened eyeball. Wearing spectacles of converging lenses (used for near vision) will amount to more converging power than needed for parallel rays. Hence the person prefers not to use the spectacles for far objects.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30359,73,417,'heading',4,'9.9.2 The microscope',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30360,73,418,'paragraph',NULL,'A simple magnifier or microscope is a converging lens of small focal length (Fig. 9.30). In order to use such a lens as a microscope, the lens is held near the object, one focal length away or less, and the eye is positioned close to the lens on the other side. The idea is to get an erect, magnified and virtual image of the object at a distance so that it can be viewed comfortably, i.e., at 25 cm or more. If the object is at a distance f , the image is at infinity. However, if the object is at a distance slightly less than the focal length of the lens, the image is virtual and closer than infinity. Although the closest comfortable distance for viewing the image is when it is at the near point (distance D ≅ 25 cm), it causes some strain on the eye. Therefore, the image formed at infinity is often considered most suitable for viewing by the relaxed eye. We show both cases, the first in Fig. 9.30(a), and the second in Fig. 9.30(b) and (c).','[{"s": "A simple magnifier or microscope is a converging lens of small focal length (Fig. 9.30). In order to use such a lens as a microscope, the lens is held near the object, one focal length away or less, and the eye is positioned close to the lens on the other side. The idea is to get an erect, magnified and virtual image of the object at a distance so that it can be viewed comfortably, i.e., at 25 cm or more. If the object is at a distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the image is at infinity. However, if the object is at a distance slightly less than the focal length of the lens, the image is virtual and closer than infinity. Although the closest comfortable distance for viewing the image is when it is at the near point (distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≅ 25 cm), it causes some strain on the eye. Therefore, the image formed at infinity is often considered most suitable for viewing by the relaxed eye. We show both cases, the first in Fig. 9.30(a), and the second in Fig. 9.30(b) and (c).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30361,73,419,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image13.png'),
(30362,73,420,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.30 A simple microscope; (a) the magnifying lens is located such that the image is at the near point, (b) the angle subtanded by the object, is the same as that at the near point, and (c) the object near the focal point of the lens; the image is far off but closer than infinity.','[{"s": "Figure 9.30 A simple microscope; (a) the magnifying lens is located such that the image is at the near point, (b) the angle subtanded by the object, is the same as that at the near point, and (c) the object near the focal point of the lens; the image is far off but closer than infinity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30363,73,421,'paragraph',NULL,'The linear magnification m , for the image formed at the near point D , by a simple microscope can be obtained by using the relation','[{"s": "The linear magnification", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", for the image formed at the near point", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", by a simple microscope can be obtained by using the relation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30364,73,422,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image16.png'),
(30365,73,423,'paragraph',NULL,'Now according to our sign convention, v is negative, and is equal in magnitude to D. Thus, the magnification is','[{"s": "Now according to our sign convention,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is negative, and is equal in magnitude to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, the magnification is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30366,73,424,'paragraph',NULL,'Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image15.png (9.39)','[{"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image15.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(9.39)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30367,73,425,'paragraph',NULL,'Since D is about 25 cm, to have a magnification of six, one needs a convex lens of focal length, f = 5 cm.','[{"s": "Since", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is about 25 cm, to have a magnification of six, one needs a convex lens of focal length,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5 cm.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(346,3,37,'paragraph',NULL,'While it is doubtful if any new species are being added (through speciation) into the earth’s treasury of species, there is no doubt about their continuing losses. The biological wealth of our planet has been declining rapidly and the accusing finger is clearly pointing to human activities. The colonisation of tropical Pacific Islands by humans is said to have led to the extinction of more than 2,000 species of native birds. The IUCN Red List (2004) documents the extinction of 784 species (including 338 vertebrates, 359 invertebrates and 87 plants) in the last 500 years. Some examples of recent extinctions include the dodo (Mauritius), quagga (Africa), thylacine (Australia), Steller’s Sea Cow (Russia) and three subspecies (Bali, Javan, Caspian) of tiger. The last twenty years alone have witnessed the disappearance of 27 species. Careful analysis of records shows that extinctions across taxa are not random; some groups like amphibians appear to be more vulnerable to extinction. Adding to the grim scenario of extinctions is the fact that more than 15,500 species world-wide are facing the threat of extinction. Presently, 12 per cent of all bird species, 23 per cent of all mammal species, 32 per cent of all amphibian species and 31per cent of all gymnosperm species in the world face the threat of extinction.','[{"s": "While it is doubtful if any new species are being added (through speciation) into the earth’s treasury of species, there is no doubt about their continuing losses. The biological wealth of our planet has been declining rapidly and the accusing finger is clearly pointing to human activities. The colonisation of tropical Pacific Islands by humans is said to have led to the extinction of more than 2,000 species of native birds.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The IUCN Red List (2004) documents the extinction of 784 species (including 338 vertebrates, 359 invertebrates and 87 plants) in the last 500 years.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Some examples of recent extinctions include the dodo (Mauritius), quagga (Africa), thylacine (Australia), Steller’s Sea Cow (Russia) and three subspecies (Bali, Javan, Caspian) of tiger. The last twenty years alone have witnessed the disappearance of 27 species. Careful analysis of records shows that extinctions across taxa are not random; some groups like amphibians appear to be more vulnerable to extinction. Adding to the grim scenario of extinctions is the fact that more than 15,500 species world-wide are facing the threat of extinction. Presently, 12 per cent of all bird species, 23 per cent of all mammal species, 32 per cent of all amphibian species and 31per cent of all gymnosperm species in the world face the threat of extinction.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["370907", "370911", "370915"]',NULL),
(30368,73,426,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that m = h ′/ h where h is the size of the object and h D for comfortable viewing. (Note that this is not the angle actually subtended by the object at the eye, which is h/u. ) What a single-lens simple magnifier achieves is that it allows the object to be brought closer to the eye than D .','[{"s": "Note that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the size of the object and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for comfortable viewing. (Note that this is not the angle actually subtended by the object at the eye, which is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h/u.", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") What a single-lens simple magnifier achieves is that it allows the object to be brought closer to the eye than", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30369,73,427,'paragraph',NULL,'We will now find the magnification when the image is at infinity. In this case we will have to obtained the angular magnification. Suppose the object has a height h . The maximum angle it can subtend, and be clearly visible (without a lens), is when it is at the near point, i.e., a distance D. The angle subtended is then given by','[{"s": "We will now find the magnification when the image is at infinity. In this case we will have to obtained the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "angular", "t": "text"}, {"s": "magnification. Suppose the object has a height", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The maximum angle it can subtend, and be clearly visible (without a lens), is when it is at the near point, i.e., a distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The angle subtended is then given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30370,73,428,'paragraph',NULL,'tan Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image12.png ≈ θ o (9.40)','[{"s": "tan", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image12.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "≈ θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(9.40)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30371,73,429,'paragraph',NULL,'We now find the angle subtended at the eye by the image when the object is at u . From the relations','[{"s": "We now find the angle subtended at the eye by the image when the object is at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "u", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". From the relations", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
