(30385,73,443,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image87.png'),
(30386,73,444,'paragraph',NULL,'Here h ′ is the size of the first image, the object size being h and f o being the focal length of the objective. The first image is formed near the focal point of the eyepiece. The distance L, i.e., the distance between the second focal point of the objective and the first focal point of the eyepiece (focal length f e ) is called the tube length of the compound microscope.','[{"s": "Here", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ is the size of the first image, the object size being", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "being the focal length of the objective. The first image is formed near the focal point of the eyepiece. The distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i.e., the distance between the second focal point of the objective and the first focal point of the eyepiece (focal length", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is called the tube length of the compound microscope.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30387,73,445,'paragraph',NULL,'As the first inverted image is near the focal point of the eyepiece, we use the result from the discussion above for the simple microscope to obtain the (angular) magnification m e due to it [Eq. (9.39)], when the final image is formed at the near point, is','[{"s": "As the first inverted image is near the focal point of the eyepiece, we use the result from the discussion above for the simple microscope to obtain the (angular) magnification", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "due to it [Eq. (9.39)], when the final image is formed at the near point, is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30388,73,446,'paragraph',NULL,'Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image86.png [9.44(a)]','[{"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image86.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "[9.44(a)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30389,73,447,'paragraph',NULL,'When the final image is formed at infinity, the angular magnification due to the eyepiece [Eq. (9.42)] is','[{"s": "When the final image is formed at infinity, the angular magnification due to the eyepiece [Eq. (9.42)] is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30390,73,448,'paragraph',NULL,'m e = ( D / f e ) [9.44(b)]','[{"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") [9.44(b)]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30392,73,450,'paragraph',NULL,'Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image89.png (9.45)','[{"s": "Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image89.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(9.45)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30393,73,451,'paragraph',NULL,'Clearly, to achieve a large magnification of a small object (hence the name microscope), the objective and eyepiece should have small focal lengths. In practice, it is difficult to make the focal length much smaller than 1 cm. Also large lenses are required to make L large.','[{"s": "Clearly, to achieve a large magnification of a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "small", "t": "text"}, {"s": "object (hence the name microscope), the objective and eyepiece should have small focal lengths. In practice, it is difficult to make the focal length much smaller than 1 cm. Also large lenses are required to make L large.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30394,73,452,'paragraph',NULL,'For example, with an objective with f o = 1.0 cm, and an eyepiece with focal length f e = 2.0 cm, and a tube length of 20 cm, the magnification is','[{"s": "For example, with an objective with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1.0 cm, and an eyepiece with focal length", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2.0 cm, and a tube length of 20 cm, the magnification is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30395,73,453,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image88.png'),
(30396,73,454,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image85.png'),
(30397,73,455,'paragraph',NULL,'Various other factors such as illumination of the object, contribute to the quality and visibility of the image. In modern microscopes, multi-component lenses are used for both the objective and the eyepiece to improve image quality by minimising various optical aberrations (defects) in lenses.','[{"s": "Various other factors such as illumination of the object, contribute to the quality and visibility of the image. In modern microscopes, multi-component lenses are used for both the objective and the eyepiece to improve image quality by minimising various optical aberrations (defects) in lenses.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30398,73,456,'heading',4,'9.9.3 Telescope',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30399,73,457,'paragraph',NULL,'The telescope is used to provide angular magnification of distant objects (Fig. 9.32). It also has an objective and an eyepiece. But here, the objective has a large focal length and a much larger aperture than the eyepiece. Light from a distant object enters the objective and a real image is formed in the tube at its second focal point. The eyepiece magnifies this image producing a final inverted image. The magnifying power m is the ratio of the angle β subtended at the eye by the final image to the angle α which the object subtends at the lens or the eye. Hence','[{"s": "The telescope is used to provide angular magnification of distant objects (Fig. 9.32). It also has an objective and an eyepiece. But here, the objective has a large focal length and a much larger aperture than the eyepiece. Light from a distant object enters the objective and a real image is formed in the tube at its second focal point. The eyepiece magnifies this image producing a final inverted image. The magnifying power", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the ratio of the angle β subtended at the eye by the final image to the angle α which the object subtends at the lens or the eye. Hence", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30401,73,459,'paragraph',NULL,'In this case, the length of the telescope tube is f o + f e .','[{"s": "In this case, the length of the telescope tube is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30402,73,460,'paragraph',NULL,'Terrestrial telescopes have, in addition, a pair of inverting lenses to make the final image erect. Refracting telescopes can be used both for terrestrial and astronomical observations. For example, consider a telescope whose objective has a focal length of 100 cm and the eyepiece a focal length of 1 cm. The magnifying power of this telescope is m = 100/1 = 100.','[{"s": "Terrestrial telescopes have, in addition, a pair of inverting lenses to make the final image erect. Refracting telescopes can be used both for terrestrial and astronomical observations. For example, consider a telescope whose objective has a focal length of 100 cm and the eyepiece a focal length of 1 cm. The magnifying power of this telescope is m = 100/1 = 100.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30403,73,461,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us consider a pair of stars of actual separation 1′ (one minute of arc). The stars appear as though they are separated by an angle of 100 × 1′ = 100′ =1.67°.','[{"s": "Let us consider a pair of stars of actual separation 1′ (one minute of arc). The stars appear as though they are separated by an angle of 100 × 1′ = 100′ =1.67°.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30405,73,463,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image131.png'),
(30407,73,465,'paragraph',NULL,'For these reasons, modern telescopes use a concave mirror rather than a lens for the objective. Telescopes with mirror objectives are called reflecting telescopes. There is no chromatic aberration in a mirror. Mechanical support is much less of a problem since a mirror weighs much less than a lens of equivalent optical quality, and can be supported over its entire back surface, not just over its rim. One obvious problem with a reflecting telescope is that the objective mirror focusses light inside the telescope tube. One must have an eyepiece and the observer right there, obstructing some light (depending on the size of the observer cage). This is what is done in the very large 200 inch (~5.08 m) diameters, Mt. Palomar telescope, California. The viewer sits near the focal point of the mirror, in a small cage. Another solution to the problem is to deflect the light being focussed by another mirror. One such arrangement using a convex secondary mirror to focus the incident light, which now passes through a hole in the objective primary mirror, is shown in Fig. 9.33. This is known as a Cassegrain telescope, after its inventor. It has the advantages of a large focal length in a short telescope. The largest telescope in India is in Kavalur, Tamil Nadu. It is a 2.34 m diameter reflecting telescope (Cassegrain). It was ground, polished, set up, and is being used by the Indian Institute of Astrophysics, Bangalore. The largest reflecting telescopes in the world are the pair of Keck telescopes in Hawaii, USA, with a reflector of 10 metre in diameter.','[{"s": "For these reasons, modern telescopes use a concave mirror rather than a lens for the objective. Telescopes with mirror objectives are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reflecting", "t": "text"}, {"s": "telescopes. There is no chromatic aberration in a mirror. Mechanical support is much less of a problem since a mirror weighs much less than a lens of equivalent optical quality, and can be supported over its entire back surface, not just over its rim. One obvious problem with a reflecting telescope is that the objective mirror focusses light inside the telescope tube. One must have an eyepiece and the observer right there, obstructing some light (depending on the size of the observer cage). This is what is done in the very large 200 inch (~5.08 m) diameters, Mt. Palomar telescope, California. The viewer sits near the focal point of the mirror, in a small cage. Another solution to the problem is to deflect the light being focussed by another mirror. One such arrangement using a convex secondary mirror to focus the incident light, which now passes through a hole in the objective primary mirror, is shown in Fig. 9.33. This is known as a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cassegrain", "t": "text"}, {"s": "telescope, after its inventor. It has the advantages of a large focal length in a short telescope. The largest telescope in India is in Kavalur, Tamil Nadu. It is a 2.34 m diameter reflecting telescope (Cassegrain). It was ground, polished, set up, and is being used by the Indian Institute of Astrophysics, Bangalore. The largest reflecting telescopes in the world are the pair of Keck telescopes in Hawaii, USA, with a reflector of 10 metre in diameter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30408,73,466,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image130.png'),
(30409,73,467,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.33 Schematic diagram of a reflecting telescope (Cassegrain).','[{"s": "Figure 9.33 Schematic diagram of a reflecting telescope (Cassegrain).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30410,73,468,'heading',3,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30411,73,469,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Reflection is governed by the equation ∠i = ∠r′ and refraction by the Snell’s law, sini/sinr = n, where the incident ray, reflected ray, refracted ray and normal lie in the same plane. Angles of incidence, reflection and refraction are i, r ′ and r, respectively.','[{"s": "1. Reflection is governed by the equation ∠i = ∠r′ and refraction by the Snell’s law, sini/sinr = n, where the incident ray, reflected ray, refracted ray and normal lie in the same plane. Angles of incidence, reflection and refraction are i, r ′ and r, respectively.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30412,73,470,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The critical angle of incidence i c for a ray incident from a denser to rarer medium, is that angle for which the angle of refraction is 90°. For i > i c , total internal reflection occurs. Multiple internal reflections in diamond (i c ≅ 24.4°), totally reflecting prisms and mirage, are some examples of total internal reflection. Optical fibres consist of glass fibres coated with a thin layer of material of lower refractive index. Light incident at an angle at one end comes out at the other, after multiple internal reflections, even if the fibre is bent.','[{"s": "2. The critical angle of incidence i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for a ray incident from a denser to rarer medium, is that angle for which the angle of refraction is 90°. For i > i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", total internal reflection occurs. Multiple internal reflections in diamond (i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≅ 24.4°), totally reflecting prisms and mirage, are some examples of total internal reflection. Optical fibres consist of glass fibres coated with a thin layer of material of lower refractive index. Light incident at an angle at one end comes out at the other, after multiple internal reflections, even if the fibre is bent.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30406,73,464,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.32 A refracting telescope.','[{"s": "Figure 9.32 A refracting telescope.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(30413,73,471,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Cartesian sign convention: Distances measured in the same direction as the incident light are positive; those measured in the opposite direction are negative. All distances are measured from the pole/optic centre of the mirror/lens on the principal axis. The heights measured upwards above x-axis and normal to the principal axis of the mirror/lens are taken as positive. The heights measured downwards are taken as negative.','[{"s": "3. Cartesian sign convention: Distances measured in the same direction as the incident light are positive; those measured in the opposite direction are negative. All distances are measured from the pole/optic centre of the mirror/lens on the principal axis. The heights measured upwards above x-axis and normal to the principal axis of the mirror/lens are taken as positive. The heights measured downwards are taken as negative.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30414,73,472,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Mirror equation:','[{"s": "4. Mirror equation:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30415,73,473,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image117.png'),
(30416,73,474,'paragraph',NULL,'where u and v are object and image distances, respectively and f is the focal length of the mirror. f is (approximately) half the radius of curvature R. f is negative for concave mirror; f is positive for a convex mirror.','[{"s": "where u and v are object and image distances, respectively and f is the focal length of the mirror. f is (approximately) half the radius of curvature R. f is negative for concave mirror; f is positive for a convex mirror.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30418,73,476,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image114.png'),
(30419,73,477,'paragraph',NULL,'where D m is the angle of minimum deviation.','[{"s": "where D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the angle of minimum deviation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30420,73,478,'paragraph',NULL,'6. For refraction through a spherical interface (from medium 1 to 2 of refractive index n 1 and n 2 , respectively)','[{"s": "6. For refraction through a spherical interface (from medium 1 to 2 of refractive index n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
