(30439,73,497,'heading',3,'Exercises',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30440,73,498,'paragraph',NULL,'9.1 A small candle, 2.5 cm in size is placed at 27 cm in front of a concave mirror of radius of curvature 36 cm. At what distance from the mirror should a screen be placed in order to obtain a sharp image? Describe the nature and size of the image. If the candle is moved closer to the mirror, how would the screen have to be moved?','[{"s": "9.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A small candle, 2.5 cm in size is placed at 27 cm in front of a concave mirror of radius of curvature 36 cm. At what distance from the mirror should a screen be placed in order to obtain a sharp image? Describe the nature and size of the image. If the candle is moved closer to the mirror, how would the screen have to be moved?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30441,73,499,'paragraph',NULL,'9.2 A 4.5 cm needle is placed 12 cm away from a convex mirror of focal length 15 cm. Give the location of the image and the magnification. Describe what happens as the needle is moved farther from the mirror.','[{"s": "9.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A 4.5 cm needle is placed 12 cm away from a convex mirror of focal length 15 cm. Give the location of the image and the magnification. Describe what happens as the needle is moved farther from the mirror.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30442,73,500,'paragraph',NULL,'9.3 A tank is filled with water to a height of 12.5 cm. The apparent depth of a needle lying at the bottom of the tank is measured by a microscope to be 9.4 cm. What is the refractive index of water? If water is replaced by a liquid of refractive index 1.63 up to the same height, by what distance would the microscope have to be moved to focus on the needle again?','[{"s": "9.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A tank is filled with water to a height of 12.5 cm. The apparent depth of a needle lying at the bottom of the tank is measured by a microscope to be 9.4 cm. What is the refractive index of water? If water is replaced by a liquid of refractive index 1.63 up to the same height, by what distance would the microscope have to be moved to focus on the needle again?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30443,73,501,'paragraph',NULL,'9.4 Figures 9.31(a) and (b) show the refraction of a ray in air incident at 60° with the normal to a glass-air and water-air interface, respectively. Predict the angle of refraction in the glass when the angle of incidence in water is 45° with the normal to a water-glass interface [Fig. 9.31(c)].','[{"s": "9.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Figures 9.31(a) and (b) show the refraction of a ray in air incident at 60° with the normal to a glass-air and water-air interface, respectively. Predict the angle of refraction in the glass when the angle of incidence in water is 45° with the normal to a water-glass interface [Fig. 9.31(c)].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30444,73,502,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Ray_Optics_and_Optical_Instruments-image64.png'),
(30445,73,503,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.31','[{"s": "Figure 9.31", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30446,73,504,'paragraph',NULL,'9.5 A small bulb is placed at the bottom of a tank containing water to a depth of 80cm. What is the area of the surface of water through which light from the bulb can emerge out? Refractive index of water is 1.33. (Consider the bulb to be a point source.)','[{"s": "9.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A small bulb is placed at the bottom of a tank containing water to a depth of 80cm. What is the area of the surface of water through which light from the bulb can emerge out? Refractive index of water is 1.33. (Consider the bulb to be a point source.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30447,73,505,'paragraph',NULL,'9.6 A prism is made of glass of unknown refractive index. A parallel beam of light is incident on a face of the prism. The angle of minimum deviation is measured to be 40°. What is the refractive index of the material of the prism? The refracting angle of the prism is 60°. If the prism is placed in water (refractive index 1.33), predict the new angle of minimum deviation of a parallel beam of light.','[{"s": "9.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A prism is made of glass of unknown refractive index. A parallel beam of light is incident on a face of the prism. The angle of minimum deviation is measured to be 40°. What is the refractive index of the material of the prism? The refracting angle of the prism is 60°. If the prism is placed in water (refractive index 1.33), predict the new angle of minimum deviation of a parallel beam of light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30448,73,506,'paragraph',NULL,'9.7 Double-convex lenses are to be manufactured from a glass of refractive index 1.55 with both faces of the same radius of curvature. What is the radius of curvature required if the focal length is to be 20cm?','[{"s": "9.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Double-convex lenses are to be manufactured from a glass of refractive index 1.55 with both faces of the same radius of curvature. What is the radius of curvature required if the focal length is to be 20cm?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30449,73,507,'paragraph',NULL,'9.8 A beam of light converges at a point P. Now a lens is placed in the path of the convergent beam 12cm from P. At what point does the beam converge if the lens is (a) a convex lens of focal length 20cm, and (b) a concave lens of focal length 16cm?','[{"s": "9.8 A beam of light converges at a point P. Now a lens is placed in the path of the convergent beam 12cm from P. At what point does the beam converge if the lens is (a) a convex lens of focal length 20cm, and (b) a concave lens of focal length 16cm?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30450,73,508,'paragraph',NULL,'9.9 An object of size 3.0cm is placed 14cm in front of a concave lens of focal length 21cm. Describe the image produced by the lens. What happens if the object is moved further away from the lens?','[{"s": "9.9 An object of size 3.0cm is placed 14cm in front of a concave lens of focal length 21cm. Describe the image produced by the lens. What happens if the object is moved further away from the lens?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30436,73,494,'paragraph',NULL,'9 . Magnifying power m of a telescope is the ratio of the angle β subtended at the eye by the image to the angle α subtended at the eye by the object.','[{"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Magnifying power m of a telescope is the ratio of the angle β subtended at the eye by the image to the angle α subtended at the eye by the object.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(30451,73,509,'paragraph',NULL,'9.10 What is the focal length of a convex lens of focal length 30cm in contact with a concave lens of focal length 20cm? Is the system a converging or a diverging lens? Ignore the thickness of the lenses.','[{"s": "9.10 What is the focal length of a convex lens of focal length 30cm in contact with a concave lens of focal length 20cm? Is the system a converging or a diverging lens? Ignore the thickness of the lenses.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30452,73,510,'paragraph',NULL,'9.11: A compound microscope consists of an objective lens of focal length 2.0 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 6.25 cm separated by a distance of 15 cm. How far from the objective should an object be placed in order to obtain the final image at (a) the least distance of distinct vision (25 cm), and (b) at infinity? What is the magnifying power of the microscope in each case?','[{"s": "9.11: A compound microscope consists of an objective lens of focal length 2.0 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 6.25 cm separated by a distance of 15 cm. How far from the objective should an object be placed in order to obtain the final image at (a) the least distance of distinct vision (25 cm), and (b) at infinity? What is the magnifying power of the microscope in each case?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30453,73,511,'paragraph',NULL,'9.12: A person with a normal near point (25 cm) using a compound microscope with objective of focal length 8.0 mm and an eyepiece of focal length 2.5 cm can bring an object placed at 9.0 mm from the objective in sharp focus. What is the separation between the two lenses? Calculate the magnifying power of the microscope.','[{"s": "9.12: A person with a normal near point (25 cm) using a compound microscope with objective of focal length 8.0 mm and an eyepiece of focal length 2.5 cm can bring an object placed at 9.0 mm from the objective in sharp focus. What is the separation between the two lenses? Calculate the magnifying power of the microscope.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30454,73,512,'paragraph',NULL,'9.13: A small telescope has an objective lens of focal length 144 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 6.0 cm. What is the magnifying power of the telescope? What is the separation between the objective and the eyepiece?','[{"s": "9.13: A small telescope has an objective lens of focal length 144 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 6.0 cm. What is the magnifying power of the telescope? What is the separation between the objective and the eyepiece?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
