(22845,58,101,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.2 The work function of caesium is 2.14 eV . Find (a) the threshold frequency for caesium, and (b) the wavelength of the incident light if the photocurrent is brought to zero by a stopping potential of 0.60 V.','[{"s": "Example 11.2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The work function of caesium is 2.14 eV", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Find (a) the threshold frequency for caesium, and (b) the wavelength of the incident light if the photocurrent is brought to zero by a stopping potential of 0.60 V.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(30795,74,173,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us understand how reverse current suddenly increases at the breakdown voltage. We know that reverse current is due to the flow of electrons (minority carriers) from p → n and holes from n → p. As the reverse bias voltage is increased, the electric field at the junction becomes significant. When the reverse bias voltage V = V z , then the electric field strength is high enough to pull valence electrons from the host atoms on the p-side which are accelerated to n-side. These electrons account for high current observed at the breakdown. The emission of electrons from the host atoms due to the high electric field is known as internal field emission or field ionisation. The electric field required for field ionisation is of the order of 10 6 V/m.','[{"s": "Let us understand how reverse current suddenly increases at the breakdown voltage. We know that reverse current is due to the flow of electrons (minority carriers) from p → n and holes from n → p. As the reverse bias voltage is increased, the electric field at the junction becomes significant. When the reverse bias voltage V =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", then the electric field strength is high enough to pull valence electrons from the host atoms on the p-side which are accelerated to n-side. These electrons account for high current observed at the breakdown. The emission of electrons from the host atoms due to the high electric field is known as internal field emission or field ionisation. The electric field required for field ionisation is of the order of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V/m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30796,74,174,'paragraph',NULL,'Zener diode as a voltage regulator','[{"s": "Zener diode as a voltage regulator", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30797,74,175,'paragraph',NULL,'We know that when the ac input voltage of a rectifier fluctuates, its rectified output also fluctuates. To get a constant dc voltage from the dc unregulated output of a rectifier, we use a Zener diode. The circuit diagram of a voltage regulator using a Zener diode is shown in Fig. 14.22.','[{"s": "We know that when the ac input voltage of a rectifier fluctuates, its rectified output also fluctuates. To get a constant dc voltage from the dc unregulated output of a rectifier, we use a Zener diode. The circuit diagram of a voltage regulator using a Zener diode is shown in Fig. 14.22.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(30798,74,176,'paragraph',NULL,'The unregulated dc voltage (filtered output of a rectifier) is connected to the Zener diode through a series resistance R s such that the Zener diode is reverse biased. If the input voltage increases, the current through R s and Zener diode also increases. This increases the voltage drop across R s without any change in the voltage across the Zener diode. This is because in the breakdown region, Zener voltage remains constant even though the current through the Zener diode changes. Similarly, if the input voltage decreases, the current through R s and Zener diode also decreases. The voltage drop across R s decreases without any change in the voltage across the Zener diode. Thus any increase/decrease in the input voltage results in, increase/decrease of the voltage drop across R s without any change in voltage across the Zener diode. Thus the Zener diode acts as a voltage regulator. We have to select the Zener diode according to the required output voltage and accordingly the series resistance R s .','[{"s": "The unregulated dc voltage (filtered output of a rectifier) is connected to the Zener diode through a series resistance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "such that the Zener diode is reverse biased. If the input voltage increases, the current through", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Zener diode also increases. This increases the voltage drop across", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "without any change in the voltage across the Zener diode. This is because in the breakdown region, Zener voltage remains constant even though the current through the Zener diode changes. Similarly, if the input voltage decreases, the current through", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Zener diode also decreases. The voltage drop across", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decreases without any change in the voltage across the Zener diode. Thus any increase/decrease in the input voltage results in, increase/decrease of the voltage drop across", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "without any change in voltage across the Zener diode. Thus the Zener diode acts as a voltage regulator. We have to select the Zener diode according to the required output voltage and accordingly the series resistance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30799,74,177,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image87.png'),
(30800,74,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.22 Zener diode as DC voltage regulator','[{"s": "Figure 14.22 Zener diode as DC voltage regulator", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30801,74,179,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 14.5 In a Zener regulated power supply a Zener diode with V Z = 6.0 V is used for regulation. The load current is to be 4.0 mA and the unregulated input is 10.0 V. What should be the value of series resistor R S ?','[{"s": "Example 14.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In a Zener regulated power supply a Zener diode with V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 6.0 V is used for regulation. The load current is to be 4.0 mA and the unregulated input is 10.0 V. What should be the value of series resistor R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30802,74,180,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30803,74,181,'paragraph',NULL,'The value of R S should be such that the current through the Zener diode is much larger than the load current. This is to have good load regulation. Choose Zener current as five times the load current, i.e., I Z = 20 mA. The total current through R S is, therefore, 24 mA. The voltage drop across R S is 10.0 – 6.0 = 4.0 V. This gives R S = 4.0V/(24 × 10 –3 ) A = 167 Ω. The nearest value of carbon resistor is 150 Ω. So, a series resistor of 150 Ω is appropriate. Note that slight variation in the value of the resistor does not matter, what is important is that the current I Z should be sufficiently larger than I L .','[{"s": "The value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "should be such that the current through the Zener diode is much larger than the load current. This is to have good load regulation. Choose Zener current as five times the load current, i.e.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 20 mA. The total current through", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is, therefore, 24 mA. The voltage drop across", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is 10.0 – 6.0 = 4.0 V. This gives", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.0V/(24 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") A = 167 Ω. The nearest value of carbon resistor is 150 Ω. So, a series resistor of 150 Ω is appropriate. Note that slight variation in the value of the resistor does not matter, what is important is that the current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "should be sufficiently larger than", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30804,74,182,'heading',4,'14.8.2 Optoelectronic junction devices',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30805,74,183,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen so far, how a semiconductor diode behaves under applied electrical inputs. In this section, we learn about semiconductor diodes in which carriers are generated by photons (photo-excitation). All these devices are called optoelectronic devices . We shall study the functioning of the following optoelectronic devices:','[{"s": "We have seen so far, how a semiconductor diode behaves under applied electrical inputs. In this section, we learn about semiconductor diodes in which carriers are generated by photons (photo-excitation). All these devices are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "optoelectronic devices", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We shall study the functioning of the following optoelectronic devices:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30806,74,184,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Photodiodes used for detecting optical signal (photodetectors).','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Photodiodes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "used for detecting optical signal (photodetectors).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30807,74,185,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Light emitting diodes (LED) which convert electrical energy into light.','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Light emitting diodes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(LED) which convert electrical energy into light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30808,74,186,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Photovoltaic devices which convert optical radiation into electricity ( solar cells ).','[{"s": "(iii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Photovoltaic devices", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which convert optical radiation into electricity (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "solar cells", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30809,74,187,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Photodiode','[{"s": "(i) Photodiode", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30811,74,189,'paragraph',NULL,'It is easier to observe the change in the current with change in the light intensity, if a reverse bias is applied. Thus photodiode can be used as a photodetector to detect optical signals. The circuit diagram used for the measurement of I-V characteristics of a photodiode is shown in Fig. 14.23(a) and a typical I-V characteristics in Fig. 14.23(b).','[{"s": "It is easier to observe the change in the current with change in the light intensity, if a reverse bias is applied. Thus photodiode can be used as a photodetector to detect optical signals. The circuit diagram used for the measurement of I-V characteristics of a photodiode is shown in Fig. 14.23(a) and a typical I-V characteristics in Fig. 14.23(b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30812,74,190,'image',NULL,'140',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image15.png'),
(30813,74,191,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.23 (a) An illuminated photodiode under reverse bias , (b) I-V characteristics of a photodiode for different illumination intensity I 4 > I 3 > I 2 > I 1 .','[{"s": "Figure 14.23 (a) An illuminated photodiode under reverse bias , (b) I-V characteristics of a photodiode for different illumination intensity I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30814,74,192,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 14.6 The current in the forward bias is known to be more (~mA) than the current in the reverse bias (~µA). What is the reason then to operate the photodiodes in reverse bias?','[{"s": "Example 14.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The current in the forward bias is known to be more (~mA) than the current in the reverse bias (~µA). What is the reason then to operate the photodiodes in reverse bias?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30815,74,193,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution Consider the case of an n-type semiconductor. Obviously, the majority carrier density ( n ) is considerably larger than the minority hole density p (i.e., n >> p ). On illumination, let the excess electrons and holes generated be ∆n and ∆p, respectively:','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Consider the case of an n-type semiconductor. Obviously, the majority carrier density (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is considerably larger than the minority hole density", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(i.e.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">>", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). On illumination, let the excess electrons and holes generated be ∆n and ∆p, respectively:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30816,74,194,'paragraph',NULL,'n′ = n + ∆n','[{"s": "n′ = n + ∆n", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30817,74,195,'paragraph',NULL,'p ′ = p + ∆ p','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30818,74,196,'paragraph',NULL,'Here n ′ and p ′ are the electron and hole concentrations* at any particular illumination and n and p are carriers concentration when there is no illumination. Remember ∆ n = ∆ p and n >> p . Hence, the fractional change in the majority carriers (i.e., ∆ n / n ) would be much less than that in the minority carriers (i.e., ∆ p/p ). In general, we can state that the fractional change due to the photo-effects on the minority carrier dominated reverse bias current is more easily measurable than the fractional change in the forward bias current. Hence, photodiodes are preferably used in the reverse bias condition for measuring light intensity.','[{"s": "Here", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ are the electron and hole concentrations* at any particular illumination and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are carriers concentration when there is no illumination. Remember ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">>", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Hence, the fractional change in the majority carriers (i.e., ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") would be much less than that in the minority carriers (i.e., ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p/p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). In general, we can state that the fractional change due to the photo-effects on the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "minority carrier dominated reverse bias current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is more easily measurable than the fractional change in the forward bias current. Hence, photodiodes are preferably used in the reverse bias condition for measuring light intensity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30819,74,197,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Light emitting diode','[{"s": "(ii) Light emitting diode", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30820,74,198,'paragraph',NULL,'It is a heavily doped p-n junction which under forward bias emits spontaneous radiation. The diode is encapsulated with a transparent cover so that emitted light can come out.','[{"s": "It is a heavily doped p-n junction which under forward bias emits spontaneous radiation. The diode is encapsulated with a transparent cover so that emitted light can come out.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30821,74,199,'paragraph',NULL,'When the diode is forward biased, electrons are sent from n → p (where they are minority carriers) and holes are sent from p → n (where they are minority carriers). At the junction boundary the concentration of minority carriers increases compared to the equilibrium concentration (i.e., when there is no bias). Thus at the junction boundary on either side of the junction, excess minority carriers are there which recombine with majority carriers near the junction. On recombination, the energy is released in the form of photons. Photons with energy equal to or slightly less than the band gap are emitted. When the forward current of the diode is small, the intensity of light emitted is small. As the forward current increases, intensity of light increases and reaches a maximum. Further increase in the forward current results in decrease of light intensity. LEDs are biased such that the light emitting efficiency is maximum.','[{"s": "When the diode is forward biased, electrons are sent from n → p (where they are minority carriers) and holes are sent from p → n (where they are minority carriers). At the junction boundary the concentration of minority carriers increases compared to the equilibrium concentration (i.e., when there is no bias). Thus at the junction boundary on either side of the junction, excess minority carriers are there which recombine with majority carriers near the junction. On recombination, the energy is released in the form of photons. Photons with energy equal to or slightly less than the band gap are emitted. When the forward current of the diode is small, the intensity of light emitted is small. As the forward current increases, intensity of light increases and reaches a maximum. Further increase in the forward current results in decrease of light intensity. LEDs are biased such that the light emitting efficiency is maximum.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30822,74,200,'paragraph',NULL,'The V-I characteristics of a LED is similar to that of a Si junction diode. But the threshold voltages are much higher and slightly different for each colour. The reverse breakdown voltages of LEDs are very low, typically around 5V. So care should be taken that high reverse voltages do not appear across them.','[{"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V-I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "characteristics of a LED is similar to that of a Si junction diode. But the threshold voltages are much higher and slightly different for each colour. The reverse breakdown voltages of LEDs are very low, typically around 5V. So care should be taken that high reverse voltages do not appear across them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30823,74,201,'paragraph',NULL,'LEDs that can emit red, yellow, orange, green and blue light are commercially available. The semiconductor used for fabrication of visible LEDs must at least have a band gap of 1.8 eV (spectral range of visible light is from about 0.4 µm to 0.7 µm, i.e., from about 3 eV to 1.8 eV). The compound semiconductor Gallium Arsenide – Phosphide (GaAs 1– x P x ) is used for making LEDs of different colours. GaAs 0.6 P 0.4 ( E g ~ 1.9 eV) is used for red LED. GaAs ( E g ~ 1.4 eV) is used for making infrared LED. These LEDs find extensive use in remote controls, burglar alarm systems, optical communication, etc. Extensive research is being done for developing white LEDs which can replace incandescent lamps.','[{"s": "LEDs that can emit red, yellow, orange, green and blue light are commercially available. The semiconductor used for fabrication of visible LEDs must at least have a band gap of 1.8 eV (spectral range of visible light is from about 0.4 µm to 0.7 µm, i.e., from about 3 eV to 1.8 eV). The compound semiconductor Gallium Arsenide – Phosphide (GaAs", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is used for making LEDs of different colours. GaAs", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~ 1.9 eV) is used for red LED. GaAs (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~ 1.4 eV) is used for making infrared LED. These LEDs find extensive use in remote controls, burglar alarm systems, optical communication, etc. Extensive research is being done for developing white LEDs which can replace incandescent lamps.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30824,74,202,'paragraph',NULL,'LEDs have the following advantages over conventional incandescent low power lamps:','[{"s": "LEDs have the following advantages over conventional incandescent low power lamps:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30825,74,203,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Low operational voltage and less power.','[{"s": "(i) Low operational voltage and less power.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30826,74,204,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Fast action and no warm-up time required.','[{"s": "(ii) Fast action and no warm-up time required.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30827,74,205,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) The bandwidth of emitted light is 100 Å to 500 Å or in other words it is nearly (but not exactly) monochromatic.','[{"s": "(iii) The bandwidth of emitted light is 100 Å to 500 Å or in other words it is nearly (but not exactly) monochromatic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30828,74,206,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) Long life and ruggedness.','[{"s": "(iv) Long life and ruggedness.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30829,74,207,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) Fast on-off switching capability.','[{"s": "(v) Fast on-off switching capability.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30830,74,208,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Solar cell','[{"s": "(iii) Solar cell", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30831,74,209,'paragraph',NULL,'A solar cell is basically a p-n junction which generates emf when solar radiation falls on the p-n junction. It works on the same principle (photovoltaic effect) as the photodiode, except that no external bias is applied and the junction area is kept much larger for solar radiation to be incident because we are interested in more power.','[{"s": "A solar cell is basically a p-n junction which generates emf when solar radiation falls on the p-n junction. It works on the same principle (photovoltaic effect) as the photodiode, except that no external bias is applied and the junction area is kept much larger for solar radiation to be incident because we are interested in more power.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30832,74,210,'paragraph',NULL,'A simple p-n junction solar cell is shown in Fig. 14.24.','[{"s": "A simple p-n junction solar cell is shown in Fig. 14.24.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30833,74,211,'paragraph',NULL,'A p-Si wafer of about 300 µm is taken over which a thin layer (~0.3 µm) of n-Si is grown on one-side by diffusion process. The other side of p-Si is coated with a metal (back contact). On the top of n-Si layer, metal finger electrode (or metallic grid) is deposited. This acts as a front contact. The metallic grid occupies only a very small fraction of the cell area (<15%) so that light can be incident on the cell from the top.','[{"s": "A p-Si wafer of about 300 µm is taken over which a thin layer (~0.3 µm) of n-Si is grown on one-side by diffusion process. The other side of p-Si is coated with a metal (back contact). On the top of n-Si layer, metal finger electrode (or metallic grid) is deposited. This acts as a front contact. The metallic grid occupies only a very small fraction of the cell area (<15%) so that light can be incident on the cell from the top.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30834,74,212,'paragraph',NULL,'The generation of emf by a solar cell, when light falls on, it is due to the following three basic processes: generation, separation and collection— (i) generation of e-h pairs due to light (with h ν > E g photovoltage','[{"s": "The generation of emf by a solar cell, when light falls on, it is due to the following three basic processes: generation, separation and collection— (i) generation of e-h pairs due to light (with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "photovoltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30835,74,213,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image25.png'),
(30836,74,214,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.24 (a) Typical p-n junction solar cell; (b) Cross-sectional view.','[{"s": "Figure 14.24 (a) Typical p-n junction solar cell; (b) Cross-sectional view.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30837,74,215,'paragraph',NULL,'When an external load is connected as shown in the Fig. 14.25(a) a photocurrent I L flows through the load. A typical I-V characteristics of a solar cell is shown in the Fig. 14.25(b).','[{"s": "When an external load is connected as shown in the Fig. 14.25(a) a photocurrent", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "flows through the load. A typical", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I-V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "characteristics of a solar cell is shown in the Fig. 14.25(b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30838,74,216,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the I – V characteristics of solar cell is drawn in the fourth quadrant of the coordinate axes. This is because a solar cell does not draw current but supplies the same to the load.','[{"s": "Note that the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "characteristics of solar cell is drawn in the fourth quadrant of the coordinate axes. This is because a solar cell does not draw current but supplies the same to the load.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30839,74,217,'paragraph',NULL,'Semiconductors with band gap close to 1.5 eV are ideal materials for solar cell fabrication. Solar cells are made with semiconductors like Si ( E g = 1.1 eV), GaAs ( E g = 1.43 eV), CdTe ( E g = 1.45 eV), CuInSe 2 ( E g = 1.04 eV), etc. The important criteria for the selection of a material for solar cell fabrication are (i) band gap (~1.0 to 1.8 eV), (ii) high optical absorption (~10 4 cm –1 ), (iii) electrical conductivity, (iv) availability of the raw material, and (v) cost. Note that sunlight is not always required for a solar cell. Any light with photon energies greater than the bandgap will do. Solar cells are used to power electronic devices in satellites and space vehicles and also as power supply to some calculators. Production of low-cost photovoltaic cells for large-scale solar energy is a topic for research.','[{"s": "Semiconductors with band gap close to 1.5 eV are ideal materials for solar cell fabrication. Solar cells are made with semiconductors like Si (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.1 eV), GaAs (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.43 eV), CdTe (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.45 eV), CuInSe", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.04 eV), etc. The important criteria for the selection of a material for solar cell fabrication are (i) band gap (~1.0 to 1.8 eV), (ii) high optical absorption (~10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), (iii) electrical conductivity, (iv) availability of the raw material, and (v) cost. Note that sunlight is not always required for a solar cell.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Any", "t": "text"}, {"s": "light with photon energies greater than the bandgap will do. Solar cells are used to power electronic devices in satellites and space vehicles and also as power supply to some calculators. Production of low-cost photovoltaic cells for large-scale solar energy is a topic for research.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30840,74,218,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image5.png'),
(30842,74,220,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 14.7 Why are Si and GaAs are preferred materials for solar cells?','[{"s": "Example 14.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Why are Si and GaAs are preferred materials for solar cells?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30843,74,221,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution The solar radiation spectrum received by us is shown in Fig. 14.26.','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The solar radiation spectrum received by us is shown in Fig. 14.26.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30844,74,222,'image',NULL,'141',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image44.png'),
(30845,74,223,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.26','[{"s": "Figure 14.26", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30846,74,224,'paragraph',NULL,'The maxima is near 1.5 eV. For photo-excitation, hν > E g . Hence, semiconductor with band gap ~1.5 eV or lower is likely to give better solar conversion efficiency. Silicon has E g ~ 1.1 eV while for GaAs it is ~1.53 eV. In fact, GaAs is better (in spite of its higher band gap) than Si because of its relatively higher absorption coefficient. If we choose materials like CdS or CdSe (E g ~ 2.4 eV), we can use only the high energy component of the solar energy for photo-conversion and a significant part of energy will be of no use.','[{"s": "The maxima is near 1.5 eV. For photo-excitation, hν > E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Hence, semiconductor with band gap ~1.5 eV or lower is likely to give better solar conversion efficiency. Silicon has E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~ 1.1 eV while for GaAs it is ~1.53 eV. In fact, GaAs is better (in spite of its higher band gap) than Si because of its relatively higher absorption coefficient. If we choose materials like CdS or CdSe (E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~ 2.4 eV), we can use only the high energy component of the solar energy for photo-conversion and a significant part of energy will be of no use.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30847,74,225,'paragraph',NULL,'The question arises: why we do not use material like PbS (E g ~ 0.4 eV) which satisfy the condition hν > E g for ν maxima corresponding to the solar radiation spectra? If we do so, most of the solar radiation will be absorbed on the top-layer of solar cell and will not reach in or near the depletion region. For effective electron-hole separation, due to the junction field, we want the photo-generation to occur in the junction region only.','[{"s": "The question arises: why we do not use material like PbS (E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~ 0.4 eV) which satisfy the condition hν > E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for ν maxima corresponding to the solar radiation spectra? If we do so, most of the solar radiation will be absorbed on the top-layer of solar cell and will not reach in or near the depletion region. For effective electron-hole separation, due to the junction field, we want the photo-generation to occur in the junction region only.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30848,74,226,'heading',4,'14.9 Junction Transistor',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30849,74,227,'paragraph',NULL,'The credit of inventing the transistor in the year 1947 goes to J. Bardeen and W.H. Brattain of Bell Telephone Laboratories, U.S.A. That transistor was a point-contact transistor. The first junction transistor consisting of two back-to-back p-n junctions was invented by William Schockley in 1951.','[{"s": "The credit of inventing the transistor in the year 1947 goes to J. Bardeen and W.H. Brattain of Bell Telephone Laboratories, U.S.A. That transistor was a point-contact transistor. The first junction transistor consisting of two back-to-back p-n junctions was invented by William Schockley in 1951.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30850,74,228,'paragraph',NULL,'As long as only the junction transistor was known, it was known simply as transistor. But over the years new types of transistors were invented and to differentiate it from the new ones it is now called the Bipolar Junction Transistor (BJT). Even now, often the word transistor is used to mean BJT when there is no confusion. Since our study is limited to only BJT, we shall use the word transistor for BJT without any ambiguity.','[{"s": "As long as only the junction transistor was known, it was known simply as transistor. But over the years new types of transistors were invented and to differentiate it from the new ones it is now called the Bipolar Junction Transistor (BJT). Even now, often the word transistor is used to mean BJT when there is no confusion. Since our study is limited to only BJT, we shall use the word transistor for BJT without any ambiguity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30851,74,229,'heading',4,'14.9.1 TRANSISTOR: STRUCTURE AND ACTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30852,74,230,'paragraph',NULL,'A transistor has three doped regions forming two p-n junctions between them. Obviously, there are two types of transistors, as shown in Fig. 14.27.','[{"s": "A transistor has three doped regions forming two p-n junctions between them. Obviously, there are two types of transistors, as shown in Fig. 14.27.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30853,74,231,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) n-p-n transistor: Here two segments of n-type semiconductor (emitter and collector) are separated by a segment of p-type semiconductor (base).','[{"s": "(i) n-p-n transistor:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Here two segments of n-type semiconductor (emitter and collector) are separated by a segment of p-type semiconductor (base).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30854,74,232,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) p-n-p transistor:','[{"s": "(ii) p-n-p transistor:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30855,74,233,'paragraph',NULL,'The schematic representations of an n-p-n and a p-n-p configuration are shown in Fig. 14.27(a). All the three segments of a transistor have different thickness and their doping levels are also different. In the schematic symbols used for representing p-n-p and n-p-n transistors [Fig. 14.27(b)] the arrowhead shows the direction of conventional current in the transistor. A brief description of the three segments of a transistor is given below:','[{"s": "The schematic representations of an n-p-n and a p-n-p configuration are shown in Fig. 14.27(a). All the three segments of a transistor have different thickness and their doping levels are also different. In the schematic symbols used for representing p-n-p and n-p-n transistors [Fig. 14.27(b)] the arrowhead shows the direction of conventional current in the transistor. A brief description of the three segments of a transistor is given below:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30856,74,234,'paragraph',NULL,'• Emitter : This is the segment on one side of the transistor shown in Fig. 14.27(a). It is of moderate size and heavily doped . It supplies a large number of majority carriers for the current flow through the transistor.','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Emitter", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": This is the segment on one side of the transistor shown in Fig. 14.27(a). It is of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "moderate size", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "heavily doped", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It supplies a large number of majority carriers for the current flow through the transistor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30857,74,235,'image',NULL,'142',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image27.png'),
(30859,74,237,'paragraph',NULL,'• Collector : This segment collects a major portion of the majority carriers supplied by the emitter. The collector side is moderately doped and larger in size as compared to the emitter .','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Collector", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": This segment collects a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "major", "t": "text"}, {"s": "portion of the majority carriers supplied by the emitter. The collector side is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "moderately doped", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "larger", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in size as compared to the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "emitter", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(3446,30,39,'paragraph',NULL,'Pollen grains are rich in nutrients. It has become a fashion in recent years to use pollen tablets as food supplements. In western countries, a large number of pollen products in the form of tablets and syrups are available in the market. Pollen consumption has been claimed to increase the performance of athletes and race horses (Figure 2.6).','[{"s": "Pollen grains are rich in nutrients.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It has become a fashion in recent years to use pollen tablets as food supplements. In western countries, a large number of pollen products in the form of tablets and syrups are available in the market.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Pollen consumption has been claimed to increase the performance of athletes and race horses (Figure 2.6).", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(30860,74,238,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen earlier in the case of a p-n junction, that there is a formation of depletion region across the junction. In case of a transistor depletion regions are formed at the emitter base-junction and the base-collector junction. For understanding the action of a transistor, we have to consider the nature of depletion regions formed at these junctions. The charge carriers move across different regions of the transistor when proper voltages are applied across its terminals.','[{"s": "We have seen earlier in the case of a p-n junction, that there is a formation of depletion region across the junction. In case of a transistor depletion regions are formed at the emitter base-junction and the base-collector junction. For understanding the action of a transistor, we have to consider the nature of depletion regions formed at these junctions. The charge carriers move across different regions of the transistor when proper voltages are applied across its terminals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30861,74,239,'paragraph',NULL,'The biasing of the transistor is done differently for different uses. The transistor can be used in two distinct ways. Basically, it was invented to function as an amplifier, a device which produces a enlarged copy of a signal. But later its use as a switch acquired equal importance. We shall study both these functions and the ways the transistor is biased to achieve these mutually exclusive functions.','[{"s": "The biasing of the transistor is done differently for different uses. The transistor can be used in two distinct ways. Basically, it was invented to function as an amplifier, a device which produces a enlarged copy of a signal. But later its use as a switch acquired equal importance. We shall study both these functions and the ways the transistor is biased to achieve these mutually exclusive functions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30862,74,240,'paragraph',NULL,'First we shall see what gives the transistor its amplifying capabilities. The transistor works as an amplifier, with its emitter-base junction forward biased and the base-collector junction reverse biased. This situation is shown in Fig. 14.28, where V CC and V EE are used for creating the respective biasing. When the transistor is biased in this way it is said to be in active state.We represent the voltage between emitter and base as V EB and that between the collector and the base as V CB V EE , and V CC , respectively. In circuits, where emitter is the common terminal, the power supply between the base and the emitter is represented as V BB and that between collector and emitter as V CC .','[{"s": "First we shall see what gives the transistor its amplifying capabilities. The transistor works as an amplifier, with its emitter-base junction forward biased and the base-collector junction reverse biased. This situation is shown in Fig. 14.28, where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "EE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are used for creating the respective biasing. When the transistor is biased in this way it is said to be in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "active", "t": "text"}, {"s": "state.We represent the voltage between emitter and base as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "EB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and that between the collector and the base as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "EE", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively. In circuits, where emitter is the common terminal, the power supply between the base and the emitter is represented as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and that between collector and emitter as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30864,74,242,'image',NULL,'143',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image84.png'),
(30865,74,243,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.28 Bias Voltage applied on: (a) p-n-p transistor and (b) n-p-n transistor.','[{"s": "Figure 14.28 Bias Voltage applied on: (a) p-n-p transistor and (b) n-p-n transistor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30866,74,244,'paragraph',NULL,'It is interesting to note that due to forward bias a large current enters the emitter-base junction, but most of it is diverted to adjacent reverse-biased base-collector junction and the current coming out of the base becomes a very small fraction of the current that entered the junction. If we represent the hole current and the electron current crossing the forward biased junction by I h and I e respectively then the total current in a forward biased diode is the sum I h + I e . We see that the emitter current I E = I h + I e but the base current I B << I h + I e , because a major part of I E goes to collector instead of coming out of the base terminal. The base current is thus a small fraction of the emitter current.','[{"s": "It is interesting to note that due to forward bias a large current enters the emitter-base junction, but most of it is diverted to adjacent reverse-biased base-collector junction and the current coming out of the base becomes a very small fraction of the current that entered the junction. If we represent the hole current and the electron current crossing the forward biased junction by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively then the total current in a forward biased diode is the sum", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We see that the emitter current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "but the base current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", because a major part of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "goes to collector instead of coming out of the base terminal. The base current is thus a small fraction of the emitter current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30868,74,246,'paragraph',NULL,'I E = I C + I B (14.7)','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(14.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30869,74,247,'paragraph',NULL,'We also see that I C ≈ I E .','[{"s": "We also see that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≈", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30870,74,248,'paragraph',NULL,'Our description of the direction of motion of the holes is identical with the direction of the conventional current. But the direction of motion of electrons is just opposite to that of the current. Thus in a p-n-p transistor the current enters from emitter into base whereas in a n-p-n transistor it enters from the base into the emitter. The arrowhead in the emitter shows the direction of the conventional current.','[{"s": "Our description of the direction of motion of the holes is identical with the direction of the conventional current. But the direction of motion of electrons is just opposite to that of the current. Thus in a p-n-p transistor the current enters from emitter into base whereas in a n-p-n transistor it enters from the base into the emitter. The arrowhead in the emitter shows the direction of the conventional current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30871,74,249,'paragraph',NULL,'The description about the paths followed by the majority and minority carriers in a n-p-n is exactly the same as that for the p-n-p transistor. But the current paths are exactly opposite, as shown in Fig. 14.28. In Fig. 14.28(b) the electrons are the majority carriers supplied by the n-type emitter region. They cross the thin p-base region and are able to reach the collector to give the collector current, I C . From the above description we can conclude that in the active state of the transistor the emitter-base junction acts as a low resistance while the base collector acts as a high resistance.','[{"s": "The description about the paths followed by the majority and minority carriers in a n-p-n is exactly the same as that for the p-n-p transistor. But the current paths are exactly opposite, as shown in Fig. 14.28. In Fig. 14.28(b) the electrons are the majority carriers supplied by the n-type emitter region. They cross the thin p-base region and are able to reach the collector to give the collector current,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". From the above description we can conclude that in the active state of the transistor the emitter-base junction acts as a low resistance while the base collector acts as a high resistance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30872,74,250,'heading',4,'14.9.2 BASIC TRANSISTOR CIRCUIT CONFIGURATIONS AND TRANSISTOR CHARACTERISTICS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30873,74,251,'paragraph',NULL,'In a transistor, only three terminals are available, viz., Emitter (E), Base (B) and Collector (C). Therefore, in a circuit the input/output connections have to be such that one of these (E, B or C) is common to both the input and the output. Accordingly, the transistor can be connected in either of the following three configurations:','[{"s": "In a transistor, only three terminals are available, viz.,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Emitter", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(E),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Base", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(B) and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Collector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(C). Therefore, in a circuit the input/output connections have to be such that one of these (E, B or C) is common to both the input and the output. Accordingly, the transistor can be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "connected", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in either of the following three configurations:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30874,74,252,'paragraph',NULL,'Common Emitter (CE), Common Base (CB), Common Collector (CC)','[{"s": "Common Emitter (CE), Common Base (CB), Common Collector (CC)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30875,74,253,'paragraph',NULL,'The transistor is most widely used in the CE configuration and we shall restrict our discussion to only this configuration. Since more commonly used transistors are n-p-n Si transistors, we shall confine our discussion to such transistors only. With p-n-p transistors the polarities of the external power supplies are to be inverted.','[{"s": "The transistor is most widely used in the CE configuration and we shall restrict our discussion to only this configuration. Since more commonly used transistors are n-p-n Si transistors, we shall confine our discussion to such transistors only. With p-n-p transistors the polarities of the external power supplies are to be inverted.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30876,74,254,'paragraph',NULL,'Common emitter transistor characteristics','[{"s": "Common emitter transistor characteristics", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30877,74,255,'paragraph',NULL,'When a transistor is used in CE configuration, the input is between the base and the emitter and the output is between the collector and the emitter. The variation of the base current I B with the base-emitter voltage V BE is called the input characteristic . Similarly, the variation of the collector current I C with the collector-emitter voltage V CE is called the output characteristic . You will see that the output characteristics are controlled by the input characteristics. This implies that the collector current changes with the base current.','[{"s": "When a transistor is used in CE configuration, the input is between the base and the emitter and the output is between the collector and the emitter. The variation of the base current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with the base-emitter voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "input characteristic", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Similarly, the variation of the collector current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with the collector-emitter voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "output characteristic", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". You will see that the output characteristics are controlled by the input characteristics. This implies that the collector current changes with the base current.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30878,74,256,'paragraph',NULL,'The input and the output characteristics of an n-p-n transistors can be studied by using the circuit shown in Fig. 14.29.','[{"s": "The input and the output characteristics of an n-p-n transistors can be studied by using the circuit shown in Fig. 14.29.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30879,74,257,'paragraph',NULL,'To study the input characteristics of the transistor in C E configuration, a curve is plotted between the base current I B against the base-emitter voltage V BE . The collector-emitter voltage V CE is kept fixed while studying the dependence of I B on V BE . We are interested to obtain the input characteristic when the transistor is in active state. So the collector-emitter voltage V CE is kept large enough to make the base collector junction reverse biased. Since V CE = V CB + V BE and for Si transistor V BE is 0.6 to 0.7 V, V CE must be sufficiently larger than 0.7 V. Since the transistor is operated as an amplifier over large range of V CE , the reverse bias across the base-collector junction is high most of the time. Therefore, the input characteristics may be obtained for V CE somewhere in the range of 3 V to 20 V. Since the increase in V CE appears as increase in V CB , its effect on I B is negligible . As a consequence, input characteristics for various values of V CE will give almost identical curves. Hence, it is enough to determine only one input characteristics . The input characteristics of a transistor is as shown in Fig. 14.30(a).','[{"s": "To study the input characteristics of the transistor in C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "configuration, a curve is plotted between the base current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "against the base-emitter voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The collector-emitter voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is kept fixed while studying the dependence of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We are interested to obtain the input characteristic when the transistor is in active state. So the collector-emitter voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is kept large enough to make the base collector junction reverse biased. Since", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and for Si transistor", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is 0.6 to 0.7 V,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "must be sufficiently larger than 0.7 V. Since the transistor is operated as an amplifier over large range of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the reverse bias across the base-collector junction is high most of the time. Therefore, the input characteristics may be obtained for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "somewhere in the range of 3 V to 20 V. Since", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the increase in V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "appears as increase in V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CB", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", its effect on I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is negligible", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". As a consequence, input characteristics for various values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will give almost identical curves. Hence,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "it is enough to determine only one input characteristics", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The input characteristics of a transistor is as shown in Fig. 14.30(a).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30880,74,258,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image46.png'),
(30881,74,259,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.29 Circuit arrangement for studying the input and output characteristics of n-p-n transistor in CE configuration.','[{"s": "Figure 14.29 Circuit arrangement for studying the input and output characteristics of n-p-n transistor in CE configuration.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30882,74,260,'paragraph',NULL,'The output characteristic is obtained by observing the variation of I C as V CE is varied keeping I B constant. It is obvious that if V BE is increased by a small amount, both hole current from the emitter region and the electron current from the base region will increase. As a consequence both I B and I C will increase proportionately. This shows that when I B increases I C also increases. The plot of I C versus V CE for different fixed values of I B gives one output characteristic. So there will be different output characteristics corresponding to different values of I B as shown in Fig. 14.30(b).','[{"s": "The output characteristic is obtained by observing the variation of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is varied keeping", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "constant. It is obvious that if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is increased by a small amount, both hole current from the emitter region and the electron current from the base region will increase. As a consequence both", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will increase proportionately. This shows that when", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "increases", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "also increases. The plot of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "versus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for different fixed values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gives one output characteristic. So there will be different output characteristics corresponding to different values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as shown in Fig. 14.30(b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30883,74,261,'paragraph',NULL,'The linear segments of both the input and output characteristics can be used to calculate some important ac parameters of transistors as shown below.','[{"s": "The linear segments of both the input and output characteristics can be used to calculate some important ac parameters of transistors as shown below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30884,74,262,'image',NULL,'144',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image71.png'),
(30885,74,263,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.30 (a) Typical input characteristics, and (b) Typical output characteristics.','[{"s": "Figure 14.30 (a) Typical input characteristics, and (b) Typical output characteristics.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30886,74,264,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Input resistance (r i ) : This is defined as the ratio of change in base-emitter voltage (∆ V BE ) to the resulting change in base current (∆ I B ) at constant collector-emitter voltage ( V CE ). This is dynamic (ac resistance) and as can be seen from the input characteristic, its value varies with the operating current in the transistor:','[{"s": "(i) Input resistance (r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": This is defined as the ratio of change in base-emitter voltage (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") to the resulting change in base current (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") at constant collector-emitter voltage (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). This is dynamic (ac resistance) and as can be seen from the input characteristic, its value varies with the operating current in the transistor:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30887,74,265,'paragraph',NULL,'Semiconductor_Electronics-image72.png (14.8)','[{"s": "Semiconductor_Electronics-image72.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30888,74,266,'paragraph',NULL,'The value of r i can be anything from a few hundreds to a few thousand ohms.','[{"s": "The value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be anything from a few hundreds to a few thousand ohms.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30889,74,267,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Output resistance (r o ): This is defined as the ratio of change in collector-emitter voltage (∆ V CE ) to the change in collector current (∆ I C ) at a constant base current I B .','[{"s": "(ii) Output resistance (r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "):", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is defined as the ratio of change in collector-emitter voltage (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") to the change in collector current (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") at a constant base current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30890,74,268,'paragraph',NULL,'Semiconductor_Electronics-image6.png (14.9)','[{"s": "Semiconductor_Electronics-image6.png", "t": "img", "alt": "iamge1"}, {"s": "(14.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30891,74,269,'paragraph',NULL,'The output characteristics show that initially for very small values of V CE , I C increases almost linearly. This happens because the base-collector junction is not reverse biased and the transistor is not in active state. In fact, the transistor is in the saturation state and the current is controlled by the supply voltage V CC (=V CE ) in this part of the characteristic. When V CE is more than that required to reverse bias the base-collector junction, I C increases very little with V CE . The reciprocal of the slope of the linear part of the output characteristic gives the values of r o. The output resistance of the transistor is mainly controlled by the bias of the base-collector junction. The high magnitude of the output resistance (of the order of 100 kΩ) is due to the reverse-biased state of this diode. This also explains why the resistance at the initial part of the characteristic, when the transistor is in saturation state, is very low.','[{"s": "The output characteristics show that initially for very small values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "increases almost linearly. This happens because the base-collector junction is not reverse biased and the transistor is not in active state. In fact, the transistor is in the saturation state and the current is controlled by the supply voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(=V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in this part of the characteristic. When", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is more than that required to reverse bias the base-collector junction,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "increases very little with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The reciprocal of the slope of the linear part of the output characteristic gives the values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o. The output resistance of the transistor is mainly controlled by the bias of the base-collector junction. The high magnitude of the output resistance (of the order of 100 kΩ) is due to the reverse-biased state of this diode. This also explains why the resistance at the initial part of the characteristic, when the transistor is in saturation state, is very low.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30892,74,270,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Current amplification factor (β): This is defined as the ratio of the change in collector current to the change in base current at a constant collector-emitter voltage ( V CE','[{"s": "(iii) Current amplification factor (β):", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is defined as the ratio of the change in collector current to the change in base current at a constant collector-emitter voltage (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30893,74,271,'paragraph',NULL,'Semiconductor_Electronics-image70.png (14.10)','[{"s": "Semiconductor_Electronics-image70.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30895,74,273,'paragraph',NULL,'If we simply find the ratio of I C and I B we get what is called dc β of the transistor. Hence,','[{"s": "If we simply find the ratio of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "we get what is called dc β of the transistor. Hence,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30896,74,274,'paragraph',NULL,'Semiconductor_Electronics-image3.png (14.11)','[{"s": "Semiconductor_Electronics-image3.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30897,74,275,'paragraph',NULL,'Since I C increases with I B almost linearly and I C = 0 when I B = 0, the values of both β dc and β ac are nearly equal. So, for most calculations β dc can be used. Both β ac and β dc vary with V CE and I B (or I C ) slightly.','[{"s": "Since", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "increases with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "almost linearly and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 when", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0, the values of both β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are nearly equal. So, for most calculations β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be used. Both β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "vary with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") slightly.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30898,74,276,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 14.8 From the output characteristics shown in Fig. 14.30(b), calculate the values of β ac and β dc of the transistor when V CE I C = 4.0 mA.','[{"s": "Example 14.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "From the output characteristics shown in Fig. 14.30(b), calculate the values of β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the transistor when", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.0 mA.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30899,74,277,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30900,74,278,'paragraph',NULL,'Semiconductor_Electronics-image47.png , Semiconductor_Electronics-image39.png','[{"s": "Semiconductor_Electronics-image47.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Semiconductor_Electronics-image39.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30901,74,279,'paragraph',NULL,'For determining β ac and β dc at the stated values of V CE and I C one can proceed as follows. Consider any two characteristics for two values of I B which lie above and below the given value of I C . Here I C = 4.0 mA. (Choose characteristics for I B = 30 and 20 µA.) At V CE = 10 V we read the two values of I C from the graph. Then','[{"s": "For determining β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at the stated values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "one can proceed as follows. Consider any two characteristics for two values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which lie above and below the given value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Here", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.0 mA. (Choose characteristics for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 30 and 20 µA.) At", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 V we read the two values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from the graph. Then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30902,74,280,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ I B = (30 – 20) µA = 10 µA, ∆ I C = (4.5 – 3.0) mA = 1.5 mA','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (30 – 20) µA = 10 µA, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (4.5 – 3.0) mA = 1.5 mA", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30903,74,281,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, β ac = 1.5 mA/ 10 µA = 150','[{"s": "Therefore, β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.5 mA/ 10 µA = 150", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30904,74,282,'paragraph',NULL,'For determining β dc, either estimate the value of I B corresponding to I C = 4.0 mA at V CE = 10 V or calculate the two values of β dc for the two characteristics chosen and find their mean.','[{"s": "For determining β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "either estimate the value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "corresponding to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.0 mA at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 V or calculate the two values of β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for the two characteristics chosen and find their mean.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30905,74,283,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, for I C = 4.5 mA and I B = 30 µA,','[{"s": "Therefore, for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.5 mA and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 30 µA,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30906,74,284,'paragraph',NULL,'β dc = 4.5 mA/ 30 µA = 150','[{"s": "β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4.5 mA/ 30 µA = 150", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30907,74,285,'paragraph',NULL,'and for I C = 3.0 mA and I B = 20 µA','[{"s": "and for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3.0 mA and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 20 µA", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30908,74,286,'paragraph',NULL,'β dc =3.0 mA / 20 µA = 150','[{"s": "β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=3.0 mA / 20 µA = 150", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30909,74,287,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence, β dc =(150 + 150) /2 = 150','[{"s": "Hence, β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=(150 + 150) /2 = 150", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30910,74,288,'heading',4,'14.9.3 TRANSISTOR AS A DEVICE',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30912,74,290,'paragraph',NULL,'When the transistor is used in the cutoff or saturation state it acts as a switch . On the other hand for using the transistor as an amplifier , it has to operate in the active region.','[{"s": "When the transistor is used in the cutoff or saturation state it acts as a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "switch", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". On the other hand for using the transistor as an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "amplifier", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", it has to operate in the active region.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30913,74,291,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Transistor as a switch','[{"s": "(i) Transistor as a switch", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30914,74,292,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall try to understand the operation of the transistor as a switch by analysing the behaviour of the base-biased transistor in CE configuration as shown in Fig. 14.31(a).','[{"s": "We shall try to understand the operation of the transistor as a switch by analysing the behaviour of the base-biased transistor in CE configuration as shown in Fig. 14.31(a).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30915,74,293,'paragraph',NULL,'Applying Kirchhoff’s voltage rule to the input and output sides of this circuit, we get','[{"s": "Applying Kirchhoff’s voltage rule to the input and output sides of this circuit, we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30916,74,294,'paragraph',NULL,'V BB = I B R B + V BE (14.12)','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(14.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30917,74,295,'paragraph',NULL,'and','[{"s": "and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30918,74,296,'paragraph',NULL,'V CE = V CC – I C R C. (14.13)','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(14.13)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30919,74,297,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall treat V BB as the dc input voltage V i and V CE as the dc output voltage V O . So, we have','[{"s": "We shall treat", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as the dc input voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as the dc output voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". So, we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30920,74,298,'paragraph',NULL,'V i = I B R B + V BE and','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30921,74,299,'paragraph',NULL,'V o = V CC – I C R C .','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30922,74,300,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us see how V o changes as V i increases from zero onwards. In the case of Si transistor, as long as input V i is less than 0.6 V, the transistor will be in cut off state and current I C will be zero.','[{"s": "Let us see how", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "changes as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "increases from zero onwards. In the case of Si transistor, as long as input", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is less than 0.6 V, the transistor will be in cut off state and current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will be zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30923,74,301,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence V o = V CC','[{"s": "Hence", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30924,74,302,'paragraph',NULL,'When V i becomes greater than 0.6 V the transistor is in active state with some current I C in the output path and the output V o decrease as the term I C R C increases. With increase of V i , I C increases almost linearly and so V o decreases linearly till its value becomes less than about 1.0 V.','[{"s": "When", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "becomes greater than 0.6 V the transistor is in active state with some current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the output path and the output", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decrease as the term", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "increases. With increase of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "increases almost linearly and so", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decreases linearly till its value becomes less than about 1.0 V.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30925,74,303,'image',NULL,'145',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image50.png'),
(30927,74,305,'paragraph',NULL,'Beyond this, the change becomes non linear and transistor goes into saturation state. With further increase in V i the output voltage is found to decrease further towards zero though it may never become zero. If we plot the V o vs V i curve, [also called the transfer characteristics of the base-biased transistor (Fig. 14.31(b)], we see that between cut off state and active state and also between active state and saturation state there are regions of non-linearity showing that the transition from cutoff state to active state and from active state to saturation state are not sharply defined.','[{"s": "Beyond this, the change becomes non linear and transistor goes into saturation state. With further increase in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the output voltage is found to decrease further towards zero though it may never become zero. If we plot the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "vs", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "curve, [also called the transfer characteristics of the base-biased transistor (Fig. 14.31(b)], we see that between cut off state and active state and also between active state and saturation state there are regions of non-linearity showing that the transition from cutoff state to active state and from active state to saturation state are not sharply defined.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30928,74,306,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us see now how the transistor is operated as a switch. As long as V i is low and unable to forward-bias the transistor, V o is high (at V CC ). If V i is high enough to drive the transistor into saturation, then V o is low , very near to zero. When the transistor is not conducting it is said to be switched off and when it is driven into saturation it is said to be switched on . This shows that if we define low and high states as below and above certain voltage levels corresponding to cutoff and saturation of the transistor, then we can say that a low input switches the transistor off and a high input switches it on. Alternatively, we can say that a low input to the transistor gives a high output and a high input gives a low output. The switching circuits are designed in such a way that the transistor does not remain in active state.','[{"s": "Let us see now how the transistor is operated as a switch. As long as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "low", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and unable to forward-bias the transistor,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "high", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "high", "t": "text"}, {"s": "enough to drive the transistor into saturation, then", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "low", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", very near to zero. When the transistor is not conducting it is said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "switched off", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and when it is driven into saturation it is said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "switched on", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This shows that if we define low and high states as below and above certain voltage levels corresponding to cutoff and saturation of the transistor, then we can say that a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "low", "t": "text"}, {"s": "input switches the transistor off and a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "high", "t": "text"}, {"s": "input switches it on. Alternatively, we can say that a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "low", "t": "text"}, {"s": "input to the transistor gives a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "high", "t": "text"}, {"s": "output and a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "high", "t": "text"}, {"s": "input gives a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "low", "t": "text"}, {"s": "output. The switching circuits are designed in such a way that the transistor does not remain in active state.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30929,74,307,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Transistor as an amplifier','[{"s": "(ii) Transistor as an amplifier", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(21129,55,99,'paragraph',NULL,'The instantaneous power supplied to the inductor is','[{"s": "The instantaneous power supplied to the inductor is", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000044833", "2000045593", "2000112750"]',NULL),
(30930,74,308,'paragraph',NULL,'For using the transistor as an amplifier we will use the active region of the V o versus V i curve. The slope of the linear part of the curve represents the rate of change of the output with the input. It is negative because the output is V CC – I C R C and not I C R C . That is why as input voltage of the CE amplifier increases its output voltage decreases and the output is said to be out of phase with the input. If we consider ∆ V o and ∆ V i as small changes in the output and input voltages then ∆ V o /∆ V i is called the small signal voltage gain A V of the amplifier.','[{"s": "For using the transistor as an amplifier we will use the active region of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "versus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "curve. The slope of the linear part of the curve represents the rate of change of the output with the input. It is negative because the output is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and not", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". That is why as input voltage of the CE amplifier increases its output voltage decreases and the output is said to be out of phase with the input. If we consider ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as small changes in the output and input voltages then ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called the small signal voltage gain", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the amplifier.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30931,74,309,'paragraph',NULL,'If the V BB voltage has a fixed value corresponding to the mid point of the active region, the circuit will behave as a CE amplifier with voltage gain ∆ V o / ∆ V i . We can express the voltage gain A V in terms of the resistors in the circuit and the current gain of the transistor as follows.','[{"s": "If the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "voltage has a fixed value corresponding to the mid point of the active region, the circuit will behave as a CE amplifier with voltage gain ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We can express the voltage gain", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in terms of the resistors in the circuit and the current gain of the transistor as follows.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30932,74,310,'paragraph',NULL,'We have, V o = V CC – I C R C','[{"s": "We have,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30933,74,311,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, ∆ V o = 0 – R C ∆ I C','[{"s": "Therefore, ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30934,74,312,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, from V i = I B R B + V BE','[{"s": "Similarly, from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30935,74,313,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ V i = R B ∆ I B + ∆ V BE','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30936,74,314,'paragraph',NULL,'But ∆ V BE is negligibly small in comparison to ∆ I B R B in this circuit.','[{"s": "But ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is negligibly small in comparison to ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in this circuit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30938,74,316,'paragraph',NULL,'A V = – R C ∆ I C / R B ∆ I B','[{"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30939,74,317,'paragraph',NULL,'= –β ac ( R C /R B ) (14.14)','[{"s": "= –β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (14.14)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30941,74,319,'heading',4,'14.9.4 TRANSISTOR AS AN AMPLIFIER (CE-CONFIGURATION)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(27918,69,61,'paragraph',NULL,'If the car in Example 3.1 moves from O to P and comes back to O in the same time interval, average speed is 20 m/s but the average velocity is zero !','[{"s": "If the car in Example 3.1 moves from O to P and comes back to O in the same time interval, average speed is 20 m/s but the average velocity is zero !", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000038970", "2000038995", "2000038998", "2000039016", "2000039029", "2000039747", "2000039759", "2000039779", "2000039922", "2000039943", "2000039958", "2000039969", "2000039977", "2000040388", "2000040400", "2000106126", "2000106137", "2000106141", "2000106146", "2000106147", "2000106153", "2000106157", "2000106158", "2000106194"]',NULL),
(30937,74,315,'paragraph',NULL,'So, the voltage gain of this CE amplifier (Fig. 14.32) is given by','[{"s": "So, the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "voltage gain", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "of this", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "CE amplifier", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "14.32) is given by", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000112944"]',NULL),
(30943,74,321,'paragraph',NULL,'If a small sinusoidal voltage with amplitude v s is superposed on the dc base bias by connecting the source of that signal in series with the V BB supply, then the base current will have sinusoidal variations superimposed on the value of I B . As a consequence the collector current also will have sinusoidal variations superimposed on the value of I C , producing in turn corresponding change in the value of V O . We can measure the ac variations across the input and output terminals by blocking the dc voltages by large capacitors.','[{"s": "If a small sinusoidal voltage with amplitude", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is superposed on the dc base bias by connecting the source of that signal in series with the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "supply, then the base current will have sinusoidal variations superimposed on the value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". As a consequence the collector current also will have sinusoidal variations superimposed on the value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", producing in turn corresponding change in the value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We can measure the ac variations across the input and output terminals by blocking the dc voltages by large capacitors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30944,74,322,'paragraph',NULL,'In the discription of the amplifier given above we have not considered any ac signal. In general, amplifiers are used to amplify alternating signals. Now let us superimpose an ac input signal v i (to be amplified) on the bias V BB (dc) as shown in Fig. 14.32. The output is taken between the collector and the ground.','[{"s": "In the discription of the amplifier given above we have not considered any ac signal. In general, amplifiers are used to amplify alternating signals. Now let us superimpose an ac input signal", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(to be amplified) on the bias", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(dc) as shown in Fig. 14.32. The output is taken between the collector and the ground.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30945,74,323,'paragraph',NULL,'The working of an amplifier can be easily understood, if we first assume that v i','[{"s": "The working of an amplifier can be easily understood, if we first assume that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30946,74,324,'paragraph',NULL,'V cc = V CE + I c R L (14.15)','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(14.15)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30947,74,325,'paragraph',NULL,'Likewise, the input loop gives','[{"s": "Likewise, the input loop gives", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30948,74,326,'paragraph',NULL,'V BB = V BE + I B R B (14.16)','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(14.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30949,74,327,'paragraph',NULL,'When v i is not zero, we get','[{"s": "When", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is not zero, we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30950,74,328,'paragraph',NULL,'V BE + v i = V BE + I B R B + ∆ I B (R B + r i )','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30951,74,329,'paragraph',NULL,'The change in V BE can be related to the input resistance r i I B . Hence','[{"s": "The change in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be related to the input resistance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Hence", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30952,74,330,'paragraph',NULL,'v i = ∆ I B (R B + r i )','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30953,74,331,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image28.png'),
(30954,74,332,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.32 A simple circuit of a CE-transistor amplifier.','[{"s": "Figure 14.32 A simple circuit of a CE-transistor amplifier.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30955,74,333,'paragraph',NULL,'= r ∆ I B','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30956,74,334,'paragraph',NULL,'The change in I B causes a change in I c . We define a parameter β ac , which is similar to the β dc defined in Eq. (14.11), as','[{"s": "The change in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "causes a change in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We define a parameter β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which is similar to the β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "defined in Eq. (14.11), as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30957,74,335,'paragraph',NULL,'Semiconductor_Electronics-image66.png (14.17)','[{"s": "Semiconductor_Electronics-image66.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30958,74,336,'paragraph',NULL,'which is also known as the ac current gain A i . Usually β ac is close to β dc in the linear region of the output characteristics.','[{"s": "which is also known as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac current gain A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Usually β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is close to β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the linear region of the output characteristics.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30959,74,337,'paragraph',NULL,'The change in I c due to a change in I B causes a change in V CE and the voltage drop across the resistor R L because V CC is fixed. These changes can be given by Eq. (14.15) as','[{"s": "The change in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "due to a change in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "causes a change in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the voltage drop across the resistor", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "because", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is fixed. These changes can be given by Eq. (14.15) as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30960,74,338,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ V CC = ∆ V CE + R L ∆ I C = 0','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30961,74,339,'paragraph',NULL,'or ∆ V CE = – R L ∆ I C','[{"s": "or ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30962,74,340,'paragraph',NULL,'The change in V CE is the output voltage v 0 . From Eq. (14.10), we get','[{"s": "The change in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the output voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". From Eq. (14.10), we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30963,74,341,'paragraph',NULL,'v 0 = ∆ V CE = –β ac R L ∆ I B','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= –β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30965,74,343,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image76.png'),
(30966,74,344,'paragraph',NULL,'Semiconductor_Electronics-image14.png (14.18)','[{"s": "Semiconductor_Electronics-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30967,74,345,'paragraph',NULL,'ac in the CE configuration. Here we have also seen the voltage gain A v . Therefore the power gain A p can be expressed as the product of the current gain and voltage gain. Mathematically','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the CE configuration. Here we have also seen the voltage gain", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Therefore the power gain", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be expressed as the product of the current gain and voltage gain. Mathematically", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30968,74,346,'paragraph',NULL,'A p = β ac × A v (14.19)','[{"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(14.19)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30969,74,347,'paragraph',NULL,'Since β ac and A v are greater than 1, we get ac power gain. However it should be realised that transistor is not a power generating device. The energy for the higher ac power at the output is supplied by the battery.','[{"s": "Since β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are greater than 1, we get ac power gain. However it should be realised that transistor is not a power generating device. The energy for the higher ac power at the output is supplied by the battery.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30970,74,348,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 14.9 In Fig. 14.31(a), the V BB supply can be varied from 0V to 5.0 V. The Si transistor has β dc = 250 and R B = 100 kΩ, R C = 1 KΩ, V CC = 5.0V. Assume that when the transistor is saturated, V CE = 0V and V BE = 0.8V. Calculate (a) the minimum base current, for which the transistor will reach saturation. Hence, (b) determine V 1 when the transistor is ‘switched on’. (c) find the ranges of V 1 for which the transistor is ‘switched off’ and ‘switched on’.','[{"s": "Example 14.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In Fig. 14.31(a), the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "supply can be varied from 0V to 5.0 V. The Si transistor has β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 250 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 100 kΩ,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1 KΩ,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5.0V. Assume that when the transistor is saturated,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0V and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.8V. Calculate (a) the minimum base current, for which the transistor will reach saturation. Hence, (b) determine V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "when the transistor is ‘switched on’. (c) find the ranges of V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for which the transistor is ‘switched off’ and ‘switched on’.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30971,74,349,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30972,74,350,'paragraph',NULL,'Given at saturation V CE = 0V, V BE = 0.8V','[{"s": "Given at saturation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0V,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.8V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30973,74,351,'paragraph',NULL,'V CE = V CC – I C R C','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30974,74,352,'paragraph',NULL,'I C = V CC / R C = 5.0V/1.0kΩ = 5.0 mA','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5.0V/1.0kΩ = 5.0 mA", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30975,74,353,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore I B = I C /β = 5.0 mA/250 = 20µA','[{"s": "Therefore", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/β = 5.0 mA/250 = 20µA", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30976,74,354,'paragraph',NULL,'The input voltage at which the transistor will go into saturation is given by','[{"s": "The input voltage at which the transistor will go into saturation is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30977,74,355,'paragraph',NULL,'V IH = V BB = I B R B + V BE','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "IH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30978,74,356,'paragraph',NULL,'= 20µA × 100 kΩ + 0.8V = 2.8V','[{"s": "= 20µA × 100 kΩ + 0.8V = 2.8V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30979,74,357,'paragraph',NULL,'The value of input voltage below which the transistor remains cutoff is given by','[{"s": "The value of input voltage below which the transistor remains cutoff is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30980,74,358,'paragraph',NULL,'V IL = 0.6V, V IH = 2.8V','[{"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "IL", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.6V,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "IH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.8V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30981,74,359,'paragraph',NULL,'Between 0.0V and 0.6V, the transistor will be in the ‘switched off’ state. Between 2.8V and 5.0V, it will be in ‘switched on’ state.','[{"s": "Between 0.0V and 0.6V, the transistor will be in the ‘switched off’ state. Between 2.8V and 5.0V, it will be in ‘switched on’ state.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30982,74,360,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the transistor is in active state when I B varies from 0.0mA to 20mA. In this range, I C = β I B I C Semiconductor_Electronics-image43.png β I B .','[{"s": "Note that the transistor is in active state when", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "varies from 0.0mA to 20mA. In this range,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Semiconductor_Electronics-image43.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30983,74,361,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 14.10 For a CE transistor amplifier, the audio signal voltage across the collector resistance of 2.0 kΩ is 2.0 V. Suppose the current amplification factor of the transistor is 100, What should be the value of R B in series with V BB supply of 2.0 V if the dc base current has to be 10 times the signal current. Also calculate the dc drop across the collector resistance. (Refer to Fig. 14.33).','[{"s": "Example 14.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For a CE transistor amplifier, the audio signal voltage across the collector resistance of 2.0 kΩ is 2.0 V. Suppose the current amplification factor of the transistor is 100, What should be the value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in series with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "supply of 2.0 V if the dc base current has to be 10 times the signal current. Also calculate the dc drop across the collector resistance. (Refer to Fig. 14.33).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30984,74,362,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution The output ac voltage is 2.0 V. So, the ac collector current i C = 2.0/2000 = 1.0 mA. The signal current through the base is, therefore given by i B = i C /β = 1.0 mA/100 = 0.010 mA. The dc base current has to be 10× 0.010 = 0.10 mA.','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The output ac voltage is 2.0 V. So, the ac collector current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.0/2000 = 1.0 mA. The signal current through the base is, therefore given by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/β = 1.0 mA/100 = 0.010 mA. The dc base current has to be 10× 0.010 = 0.10 mA.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30985,74,363,'paragraph',NULL,'From Eq.14.16, R B = ( V BB - V BE ) /I B. Assuming V BE = 0.6 V,','[{"s": "From Eq.14.16,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Assuming", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.6 V,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30986,74,364,'paragraph',NULL,'R B = (2.0 – 0.6 )/0.10 = 14 kΩ.','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (2.0 – 0.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")/0.10 = 14 kΩ.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30987,74,365,'paragraph',NULL,'The dc collector current I C = 100×0.10 = 10 mA.','[{"s": "The dc collector current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 100×0.10 = 10 mA.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30988,74,366,'heading',4,'14.9.5 FEEDBACK AMPLIFIER AND TRANSISTOR OSCILLATOR',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30989,74,367,'paragraph',NULL,'In an amplifier, we have seen that a sinusoidal input is given which appears as an amplified signal in the output. This means that an external input is necessary to sustain ac signal in the output for an amplifier. In an oscillator, we get ac output without any external input signal. In other words, the output in an oscillator is self-sustained . To attain this, an amplifier is taken. A portion of the output power is returned back (feedback) to the input in phase with the starting power (this process is termed positive feedback) as shown in Fig. 14.33(a). The feedback can be achieved by inductive coupling (through mutual inductance) or LC or RC networks. Different types of oscillators essentially use different methods of coupling the output to the input (feedback network), apart from the resonant circuit for obtaining oscillation at a particular frequency. For understanding the oscillator action, we consider the circuit shown in Fig. 14.33(b) in which the feedback is accomplished by inductive coupling from one coil winding ( T 1 ) to another coil winding ( T 2 ). Note that the coils T 2 and T 1 are wound on the same core and hence are inductively coupled through their mutual inductance. As in an amplifier, the base-emitter junction is forward biased while the base-collector junction is reverse biased. Detailed biasing circuits actually used have been omitted for simplicity.','[{"s": "In an amplifier, we have seen that a sinusoidal input is given which appears as an amplified signal in the output. This means that an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "external input is necessary to sustain ac signal in the output for an amplifier.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In an oscillator, we get ac output without any external input signal. In other words, the output in an oscillator is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "self-sustained", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". To attain this, an amplifier is taken. A portion of the output power is returned back (feedback) to the input", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in phase", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with the starting power (this process is termed", "t": "text"}, {"s": "positive feedback)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as shown in Fig. 14.33(a). The feedback can be achieved by inductive coupling (through mutual inductance) or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "LC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "networks. Different types of oscillators essentially use different methods of coupling the output to the input (feedback network), apart from the resonant circuit for obtaining oscillation at a particular frequency. For understanding the oscillator action, we consider the circuit shown in Fig. 14.33(b) in which the feedback is accomplished by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "inductive coupling", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from one coil winding (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") to another coil winding (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Note that the coils", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are wound on the same core and hence are inductively coupled through their mutual inductance. As in an amplifier, the base-emitter junction is forward biased while the base-collector junction is reverse biased. Detailed biasing circuits actually used have been omitted for simplicity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30990,74,368,'image',NULL,'146',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image68.png'),
(30991,74,369,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.33 (a) Principle of a transistor amplifier with positive feedback working as an oscillator and (b) Tuned collector oscillator, (c) Rise and fall (or built up) of current Ic and Ie due to the inductive coupling.','[{"s": "Figure 14.33 (a) Principle of a transistor amplifier with positive feedback working as an oscillator and (b) Tuned collector oscillator, (c) Rise and fall (or built up) of current Ic and Ie due to the inductive coupling.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30992,74,370,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us try to understand how oscillations are built. Suppose switch S 1 is put on to apply proper bias for the first time. Obviously, a surge of collector current flows in the transistor. This current flows through the coil T 2 where terminals are numbered 3 and 4 [Fig. 14.33(b)]. This current does not reach full amplitude instantaneously but increases from X to Y, as shown in Fig. [14.33(c)(i)]. The inductive coupling between coil T 2 and coil T 1 now causes a current to flow in the emitter circuit (note that this actually is the ‘feedback’ from input to output). As a result of this positive feedback, this current (in T 1 ; emitter current) also increases from X´ to Y´ [Fig. 14.33(c)(ii)]. The current in T 2 (collector current) connected in the collector circuit acquires the value Y when the transistor becomes saturated . This means that maximum collector current is flowing and can increase no further. Since there is no further change in collector current, the magnetic field around T 2 ceases to grow. As soon as the field becomes static, there will be no further feedback from T 2 to T 1 . Without continued feedback, the emitter current begins to fall. Consequently, collector current decreases from Y towards Z [Fig. 14.33(c)(i)]. However, a decrease of collector current causes the magnetic field to decay around the coil T 2 . Thus, T 1 is now seeing a decaying field in T 2 (opposite from what it saw when the field was growing at the initial start operation). This causes a further decrease in the emitter current till it reaches Z′when the transistor is cut-off . This means that both I E and I C cease to flow . Therefore, the transistor has reverted back to its original state (when the power was first switched on). The whole process now repeats itself. That is, the transistor is driven to saturation, then to cut-off, and then back to saturation. The time for change from saturation to cut-off and back is determined by the constants of the tank circuit or tuned circuit (inductance L of coil T 2 and C connected in parallel to it). The resonance frequency (ν) of this tuned circuit determines the frequency at which the oscillator will oscillate.','[{"s": "Let us try to understand how oscillations are built. Suppose switch S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is put", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to apply proper bias for the first time. Obviously, a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "surge", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of collector current flows in the transistor. This current flows through the coil", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where terminals are numbered 3 and 4 [Fig. 14.33(b)]. This current does not reach full amplitude instantaneously but increases from X to Y, as shown in Fig. [14.33(c)(i)]. The inductive coupling between coil", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and coil", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "now causes a current to flow in the emitter circuit (note that this actually is the ‘feedback’ from input to output). As a result of this positive feedback, this current (in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; emitter current) also increases from X´ to Y´ [Fig. 14.33(c)(ii)]. The current in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(collector current) connected in the collector circuit acquires the value Y when the transistor becomes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "saturated", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This means that maximum collector current is flowing and can increase no further. Since there is no further change in collector current, the magnetic field around", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ceases to grow. As soon as the field becomes static, there will be no further feedback from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Without continued feedback, the emitter current begins to fall. Consequently, collector current decreases from Y towards Z [Fig. 14.33(c)(i)]. However, a decrease of collector current causes the magnetic field to decay around the coil", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is now seeing a decaying field in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(opposite from what it saw when the field was growing at the initial", "t": "text"}, {"s": "start", "t": "text"}, {"s": "operation). This causes a further decrease in the emitter current till it reaches Z′when the transistor is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cut-off", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This means that both", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cease to flow", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Therefore, the transistor has reverted back to its original state (when the power was first switched on). The whole process now repeats itself. That is, the transistor is driven to saturation, then to cut-off, and then back to saturation. The time for change from saturation to cut-off and back is determined by the constants of the tank circuit or tuned circuit (inductance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of coil", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "connected in parallel to it). The resonance frequency (ν) of this tuned circuit determines the frequency at which the oscillator will oscillate.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30993,74,371,'paragraph',NULL,'Semiconductor_Electronics-image45.png (14.20)','[{"s": "Semiconductor_Electronics-image45.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(14.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30994,74,372,'paragraph',NULL,'In the circuit of Fig. 14.33(b), the tank or tuned circuit is connected in the collector side. Hence, it is known as tuned collector oscillator . If the tuned circuit is on the base side, it will be known as tuned base oscillator . There are many other types of tank circuits (say RC ) or feedback circuits giving different types of oscillators like Colpitt’s oscillator, Hartley oscillator, RC -oscillator.','[{"s": "In the circuit of Fig. 14.33(b), the tank or tuned circuit is connected in the collector side. Hence, it is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tuned collector oscillator", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If the tuned circuit is on the base side, it will be known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tuned base oscillator", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". There are many other types of tank circuits (say", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RC", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") or feedback circuits giving different types of oscillators like Colpitt’s oscillator, Hartley oscillator,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-oscillator.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30995,74,373,'heading',3,'14.10 Digital Electronics and Logic Gates',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30327,73,385,'paragraph',NULL,'At sunset or sunrise, the sun’s rays have to pass through a larger distance in the atmosphere (Fig. 9.28). Most of the blue and other shorter wavelengths are removed by scattering. The least scattered light reaching our eyes, therefore, the sun looks reddish. This explains the reddish appearance of the sun and full moon near the horizon.','[{"s": "At sunset or sunrise, the sun’s rays have to pass through a larger distance in the atmosphere (Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "9.28). Most of the blue and other shorter wavelengths are removed by scattering. The least scattered light reaching our eyes, therefore, the sun looks reddish. This explains the reddish appearance of the sun and full moon near the horizon.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(30996,74,374,'paragraph',NULL,'In electronics circuits like amplifiers, oscillators, introduced to you in earlier sections, the signal (current or voltage) has been in the form of continuous, time-varying voltage or current. Such signals are called continuous or analog signals . A typical analog signal is shown in Figure. 14.27(a). Fig. 14.27(b) shows a pulse waveform in which only discrete values of voltages are possible. It is convenient to use binary numbers to represent such signals. A binary number has only two digits ‘0’ (say, 0V) and ‘1’ (say, 5V). In digital electronics we use only these two levels of voltage as shown in Fig. 14.27(b). Such signals are called Digital Signals . In digital circuits only two values (represented by 0 or 1) of the input and output voltage are permissible.','[{"s": "In electronics circuits like amplifiers, oscillators, introduced to you in earlier sections, the signal (current or voltage) has been in the form of continuous, time-varying voltage or current. Such signals are called continuous or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "analog signals", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". A typical analog signal is shown in Figure. 14.27(a). Fig. 14.27(b) shows a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pulse waveform", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in which only discrete values of voltages are possible. It is convenient to use binary numbers to represent such signals. A binary number has only two digits ‘0’ (say, 0V) and ‘1’ (say, 5V). In digital electronics we use only these two levels of voltage as shown in Fig. 14.27(b). Such signals are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Digital Signals", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In digital circuits only two values (represented by 0 or 1) of the input and output voltage are permissible.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30997,74,375,'paragraph',NULL,'This section is intended to provide the first step in our understanding of digital electronics. We shall restrict our study to some basic building blocks of digital electronics (called Logic Gates ) which process the digital signals in a specific manner. Logic gates are used in calculators, digital watches, computers, robots, industrial control systems, and in telecommunications.','[{"s": "This section is intended to provide the first step in our understanding of digital electronics. We shall restrict our study to some basic building blocks of digital electronics (called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Logic Gates", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") which process the digital signals in a specific manner. Logic gates are used in calculators, digital watches, computers, robots, industrial control systems, and in telecommunications.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30998,74,376,'paragraph',NULL,'A light switch in your house can be used as an example of a digital circuit. The light is either ON or OFF depending on the switch position. When the light is ON, the output value is ‘1’. When the light is OFF the output value is ‘0’. The inputs are the position of the light switch. The switch is placed either in the ON or OFF position to activate the light.','[{"s": "A light switch in your house can be used as an example of a digital circuit. The light is either ON or OFF depending on the switch position. When the light is ON, the output value is ‘1’. When the light is OFF the output value is ‘0’. The inputs are the position of the light switch. The switch is placed either in the ON or OFF position to activate the light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30999,74,377,'heading',4,'14.10.1 Logic gates',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31001,74,379,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) NOT gate','[{"s": "(i) NOT gate", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31002,74,380,'paragraph',NULL,'This is the most basic gate, with one input and one output. It produces a ‘1’ output if the input is ‘0’ and vice-versa. That is, it produces an inverted version of the input at its output. This is why it is also known as an inverter . The commonly used symbol together with the truth table for this gate is given in Fig. 14.28.','[{"s": "This is the most basic gate, with one input and one output. It produces a ‘1’ output if the input is ‘0’ and vice-versa. That is, it produces an inverted version of the input at its output. This is why it is also known as an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "inverter", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The commonly used symbol together with the truth table for this gate is given in Fig. 14.28.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31003,74,381,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) OR Gate','[{"s": "(ii) OR Gate", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31005,74,383,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image32.png'),
(31006,74,384,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.27 (a) Analog signal, (b) Digital signal.','[{"s": "Figure 14.27 (a) Analog signal, (b) Digital signal.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
