(31062,74,440,'paragraph',NULL,'FIGURE 14.34','[{"s": "FIGURE 14.34", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31063,74,441,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) NOR Gate','[{"s": "(v) NOR Gate", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31064,74,442,'paragraph',NULL,'It has two or more inputs and one output. A NOT- operation applied after OR gate gives a NOT-OR gate (or simply NOR gate). Its output Y is ‘1’ only when both inputs A and B are ‘0’, i.e., neither one input nor the other is ‘1’. The symbol and truth table for NOR gate is given in Fig. 14.35.','[{"s": "It has two or more inputs and one output.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A NOT- operation applied", "t": "text"}, {"s": "after", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OR gate gives a NOT-OR gate (or simply NOR gate). Its output Y is ‘1’ only when both inputs A and B are ‘0’, i.e., neither one input", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nor", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the other is ‘1’. The symbol and truth table for NOR gate is given in Fig. 14.35.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31065,74,443,'image',NULL,'155',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Semiconductor_Electronics-image86.png'),
(31066,74,444,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.35 (a) Logic symbol, (b) Truth table of NOR gate.','[{"s": "Figure 14.35 (a) Logic symbol, (b) Truth table of NOR gate.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31067,74,445,'paragraph',NULL,'NOR gates are considered as universal gates because you can obtain all the gates like AND, OR, NOT by using only NOR gates (Exercises 14.14 and 14.15).','[{"s": "NOR gates are considered as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "universal", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gates because you can obtain all the gates like AND, OR, NOT by using only NOR gates (Exercises 14.14 and 14.15).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31068,74,446,'heading',4,'FASTER AND SMALLER: THE FUTURE OF COMPUTER TECHNOLOGY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31069,74,447,'paragraph',NULL,'The Integrated Chip (IC) is at the heart of all computer systems. In fact ICs are found in almost all electrical devices like cars, televisions, CD players, cell phones etc. The miniaturisation that made the modern personal computer possible could never have happened without the IC. ICs are electronic devices that contain many transistors, resistors, capacitors, connecting wires – all in one package. You must have heard of the microprocessor . The microprocessor is an IC that processes all information in a computer, like keeping track of what keys are pressed, running programmes, games etc. The IC was first invented by Jack Kilky at Texas Instruments in 1958 and he was awarded Nobel Prize for this in 2000. ICs are produced on a piece of semiconductor crystal (or chip) by a process called photolithography . Thus, the entire Information Technology (IT) industry hinges on semiconductors. Over the years, the complexity of ICs has increased while the size of its features continued to shrink. In the past five decades, a dramatic miniaturisation in computer technology has made modern day computers faster and smaller . In the 1970s, Gordon Moore, co-founder of INTEL, pointed out that the memory capacity of a chip (IC) approximately doubled every one and a half years. This is popularly known as Moore’s law . The number of transistors per chip has risen exponentially and each year computers are becoming more powerful, yet cheaper than the year before. It is intimated from current trends that the computers available in 2020 will operate at 40 GHz (40,000 MHz) and would be much smaller, more efficient and less expensive than present day computers. The explosive growth in the semiconductor industry and computer technology is best expressed by a famous quote from Gordon Moore: “If the auto industry advanced as rapidly as the semiconductor industry, a Rolls Royce would get half a million miles per gallon, and it would be cheaper to throw it away than to park it”.','[{"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Integrated Chip", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(IC) is at the heart of all computer systems. In fact ICs are found in almost all electrical devices like cars, televisions, CD players, cell phones etc. The miniaturisation that made the modern personal computer possible could never have happened without the IC. ICs are electronic devices that contain many transistors, resistors, capacitors, connecting wires – all in one package. You must have heard of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "microprocessor", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The microprocessor is an IC that processes all information in a computer, like keeping track of what keys are pressed, running programmes, games etc. The IC was first invented by Jack Kilky at Texas Instruments in 1958 and he was awarded Nobel Prize for this in 2000. ICs are produced on a piece of semiconductor crystal (or chip) by a process called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "photolithography", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, the entire Information Technology (IT) industry hinges on semiconductors. Over the years, the complexity of ICs has increased while the size of its features continued to shrink. In the past five decades, a dramatic miniaturisation in computer technology has made modern day computers", "t": "text"}, {"s": "faster and smaller", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In the 1970s, Gordon Moore, co-founder of INTEL, pointed out that the memory capacity of a chip (IC) approximately doubled every one and a half years. This is popularly known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Moore’s law", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The number of transistors per chip has risen exponentially and each year computers are becoming more powerful, yet cheaper than the year before. It is intimated from current trends that the computers available in 2020 will operate at 40 GHz (40,000 MHz) and would be much smaller, more efficient and less expensive than present day computers. The explosive growth in the semiconductor industry and computer technology is best expressed by a famous quote from Gordon Moore: “If the auto industry advanced as rapidly as the semiconductor industry, a Rolls Royce would get half a million miles per gallon, and it would be cheaper to throw it away than to park it”.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31070,74,448,'heading',3,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31071,74,449,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Semiconductors are the basic materials used in the present solid state electronic devices like diode, transistor, ICs, etc.','[{"s": "1. Semiconductors are the basic materials used in the present solid state electronic devices like diode, transistor, ICs, etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31072,74,450,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Lattice structure and the atomic structure of constituent elements decide whether a particular material will be insulator, metal or semiconductor.','[{"s": "2. Lattice structure and the atomic structure of constituent elements decide whether a particular material will be insulator, metal or semiconductor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31073,74,451,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Metals have low resistivity (10 –2 to 10 –8 Ωm), insulators have very high resistivity (>10 8 Ωm –1 ), while semiconductors have intermediate values of resistivity.','[{"s": "3. Metals have low resistivity (10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ωm), insulators have very high resistivity (>10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ωm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), while semiconductors have intermediate values of resistivity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31074,74,452,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Semiconductors are elemental (Si, Ge) as well as compound (GaAs, CdS, etc.).','[{"s": "4. Semiconductors are elemental (Si, Ge) as well as compound (GaAs, CdS, etc.).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31075,74,453,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Pure semiconductors are called ‘intrinsic semiconductors’. The presence of charge carriers (electrons and holes) is an ‘intrinsic’ property of the material and these are obtained as a result of thermal excitation. The number of electrons (n e ) is equal to the number of holes (n h ) in intrinsic conductors. Holes are essentially electron vacancies with an effective positive charge.','[{"s": "5. Pure semiconductors are called ‘intrinsic semiconductors’. The presence of charge carriers (electrons and holes) is an ‘intrinsic’ property of the material and these are obtained as a result of thermal excitation. The number of electrons (n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is equal to the number of holes (n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in intrinsic conductors. Holes are essentially electron vacancies with an effective positive charge.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31076,74,454,'paragraph',NULL,'6. The number of charge carriers can be changed by ‘doping’ of a suitable impurity in pure semiconductors. Such semiconductors are known as extrinsic semiconductors. These are of two types (n-type and p-type).','[{"s": "6. The number of charge carriers can be changed by ‘doping’ of a suitable impurity in pure semiconductors. Such semiconductors are known as extrinsic semiconductors. These are of two types (n-type and p-type).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31077,74,455,'paragraph',NULL,'7. In n-type semiconductors, n e >> n h while in p-type semiconductors n h >> n e .','[{"s": "7. In n-type semiconductors, n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">> n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "while in p-type semiconductors n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">> n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31078,74,456,'paragraph',NULL,'8. n-type semiconducting Si or Ge is obtained by doping with pentavalent atoms (donors) like As, Sb, P, etc., while p-type Si or Ge can be obtained by doping with trivalent atom (acceptors) like B, Al, In etc.','[{"s": "8. n-type semiconducting Si or Ge is obtained by doping with pentavalent atoms (donors) like As, Sb, P, etc., while p-type Si or Ge can be obtained by doping with trivalent atom (acceptors) like B, Al, In etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31079,74,457,'paragraph',NULL,'9. n e n h = n i 2 in all cases. Further, the material possesses an overall charge neutrality.','[{"s": "9. n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in all cases. Further, the material possesses an overall charge neutrality.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31372,75,38,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us try to understand what rotation is, what characterises rotation. You may notice that in rotation of a rigid body about a fixed axis, every particle of the body moves in a circle, which lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis and has its centre on the axis . Fig. 7.4 shows the rotational motion of a rigid body about a fixed axis (the z-axis of the frame of reference). Let P 1 be a particle of the rigid body, arbitrarily chosen and at a distance r 1 from fixed axis. The particle P 1 describes a circle of radius r 1 with its centre C 1 on the fixed axis. The circle lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis. The figure also shows another particle P 2 of the rigid body, P 2 is at a distance r 2 from the fixed axis. The particle P 2 moves in a circle of radius r 2 and with centre C 2 on the axis. This circle, too, lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis. Note that the circles described by P 1 and P 2 may lie in different planes; both these planes, however, are perpendicular to the fixed axis. For any particle on the axis like P 3 , r = 0. Any such particle remains stationary while the body rotates. This is expected since the axis of rotation is fixed.','[{"s": "Let us try to understand what rotation is, what characterises rotation. You may notice that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in rotation of a rigid body about a fixed axis, every particle of the body moves in a circle, which lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis and has its centre on the axis", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Fig. 7.4 shows the rotational motion of a rigid body about a fixed axis (the z-axis of the frame of reference). Let P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be a particle of the rigid body, arbitrarily chosen and at a distance r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from fixed axis. The particle P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "describes a circle of radius r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with its centre C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on the fixed axis. The circle lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis. The figure also shows another particle P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the rigid body, P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is at a distance r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from the fixed axis. The particle P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "moves in a circle of radius r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and with centre C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on the axis. This circle, too, lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis. Note that the circles described by P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "may lie in different planes; both these planes, however, are perpendicular to the fixed axis. For any particle on the axis like P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", r = 0. Any such particle remains stationary while the body rotates. This is expected since the axis of rotation is fixed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31080,74,458,'paragraph',NULL,'10. There are two distinct band of energies (called valence band and conduction band) in which the electrons in a material lie. Valence band energies are low as compared to conduction band energies. All energy levels in the valence band are filled while energy levels in the conduction band may be fully empty or partially filled. The electrons in the conduction band are free to move in a solid and are responsible for the conductivity. The extent of conductivity depends upon the energy gap (E g ) between the top of valence band (E V ) and the bottom of the conduction band E C . The electrons from valence band can be excited by heat, light or electrical energy to the conduction band and thus, produce a change in the current flowing in a semiconductor.','[{"s": "10. There are two distinct band of energies (called valence band and conduction band) in which the electrons in a material lie. Valence band energies are low as compared to conduction band energies. All energy levels in the valence band are filled while energy levels in the conduction band may be fully empty or partially filled. The electrons in the conduction band are free to move in a solid and are responsible for the conductivity. The extent of conductivity depends upon the energy gap (E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") between the top of valence band (E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and the bottom of the conduction band E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The electrons from valence band can be excited by heat, light or electrical energy to the conduction band and thus, produce a change in the current flowing in a semiconductor.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31081,74,459,'paragraph',NULL,'11. For insulators E g > 3 eV, for semiconductors E g is 0.2 eV to 3 eV, while for metals E g ≈ 0.','[{"s": "11. For insulators E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> 3 eV, for semiconductors E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is 0.2 eV to 3 eV, while for metals E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≈ 0.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31082,74,460,'paragraph',NULL,'12. p-n junction is the ‘key’ to all semiconductor devices. When such a junction is made, a ‘depletion layer’ is formed consisting of immobile ion-cores devoid of their electrons or holes. This is responsible for a junction potential barrier.','[{"s": "12. p-n junction is the ‘key’ to all semiconductor devices. When such a junction is made, a ‘depletion layer’ is formed consisting of immobile ion-cores devoid of their electrons or holes. This is responsible for a junction potential barrier.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31083,74,461,'paragraph',NULL,'13. By changing the external applied voltage, junction barriers can be changed. In forward bias (n-side is connected to negative terminal of the battery and p-side is connected to the positive), the barrier is decreased while the barrier increases in reverse bias. Hence, forward bias current is more (mA) while it is very small (µA) in a p-n junction diode.','[{"s": "13. By changing the external applied voltage, junction barriers can be changed. In forward bias (n-side is connected to negative terminal of the battery and p-side is connected to the positive), the barrier is decreased while the barrier increases in reverse bias. Hence, forward bias current is more (mA) while it is very small (µA) in a p-n junction diode.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31084,74,462,'paragraph',NULL,'14. Diodes can be used for rectifying an ac voltage (restricting the ac voltage to one direction). With the help of a capacitor or a suitable filter, a dc voltage can be obtained.','[{"s": "14. Diodes can be used for rectifying an ac voltage (restricting the ac voltage to one direction). With the help of a capacitor or a suitable filter, a dc voltage can be obtained.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31085,74,463,'paragraph',NULL,'15. There are some special purpose diodes.','[{"s": "15. There are some special purpose diodes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31086,74,464,'paragraph',NULL,'16. Zener diode is one such special purpose diode. In reverse bias, after a certain voltage, the current suddenly increases (breakdown voltage) in a Zener diode. This property has been used to obtain voltage regulation.','[{"s": "16. Zener diode is one such special purpose diode. In reverse bias, after a certain voltage, the current suddenly increases (breakdown voltage) in a Zener diode. This property has been used to obtain voltage regulation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31087,74,465,'paragraph',NULL,'18. There are some special circuits which handle the digital data consisting of 0 and 1 levels. This forms the subject of Digital Electronics.','[{"s": "18. There are some special circuits which handle the digital data consisting of 0 and 1 levels. This forms the subject of Digital Electronics.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31088,74,466,'paragraph',NULL,'19. The important digital circuits performing special logic operations are called logic gates. These are: OR, AND, NOT, NAND, and NOR gates.','[{"s": "19. The important digital circuits performing special logic operations are called logic gates. These are: OR, AND, NOT, NAND, and NOR gates.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31089,74,467,'heading',3,'Points to Ponder',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31090,74,468,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The energy bands (E C or E V ) in the semiconductors are space delocalised which means that these are not located in any specific place inside the solid. The energies are the overall averages. When you see a picture in which E C or E V are drawn as straight lines, then they should be respectively taken simply as the bottom of conduction band energy levels and top of valence band energy levels.','[{"s": "1. The energy bands (E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in the semiconductors are space delocalised which means that these are not located in any specific place inside the solid. The energies are the overall averages. When you see a picture in which E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are drawn as straight lines, then they should be respectively taken simply as the bottom of conduction band energy levels and top of valence band energy levels.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31373,75,39,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image296.png'),
(29715,72,323,'paragraph',NULL,'The constant of proportionality, k, is called the spring constant, its value is governed by the elastic properties of the spring. A stiff spring has large k and a soft spring has small k. Equation (14.19) is same as the force law for SHM and therefore the system executes a simple harmonic motion. From Eq. (14.14) we have,','[{"s": "The constant of proportionality, k, is called the spring constant, its value is governed by the elastic properties of the spring.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A stiff spring has large k and a soft spring has small k. Equation (14.19) is same as the force law for SHM and therefore the system executes a simple harmonic motion.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "From Eq. (14.14) we have,", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(31091,74,469,'paragraph',NULL,'2. In elemental semiconductors (Si or Ge), the n-type or p-type semiconductors are obtained by introducing ‘dopants’ as defects. In compound semiconductors, the change in relative stoichiometric ratio can also change the type of semiconductor. For example, in ideal GaAs the ratio of Ga:As is 1:1 but in Ga-rich or As-rich GaAs it could respectively be Ga 1.1 As 0.9 or Ga 0.9 As 1.1 . In general, the presence of defects control the properties of semiconductors in many ways.','[{"s": "2. In elemental semiconductors (Si or Ge), the n-type or p-type semiconductors are obtained by introducing ‘dopants’ as defects. In compound semiconductors, the change in relative stoichiometric ratio can also change the type of semiconductor. For example, in ideal GaAs the ratio of Ga:As is 1:1 but in Ga-rich or As-rich GaAs it could respectively be Ga", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "As", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or Ga", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "As", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In general, the presence of defects control the properties of semiconductors in many ways.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31092,74,470,'paragraph',NULL,'3. In modern day circuit, many logical gates or circuits are integrated inone single ‘Chip’. These are known as Intgrated circuits (IC).','[{"s": "3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In modern day circuit, many logical gates or circuits are integrated inone single ‘Chip’.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These are known as Intgrated circuits (IC).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31093,74,471,'heading',3,'Exercises',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31094,74,472,'paragraph',NULL,'14.1 In an n-type silicon, which of the following statement is true:','[{"s": "14.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In an n-type silicon, which of the following statement is true:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31095,74,473,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Electrons are majority carriers and trivalent atoms are the dopants.','[{"s": "(a) Electrons are majority carriers and trivalent atoms are the dopants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31096,74,474,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Electrons are minority carriers and pentavalent atoms are the dopants.','[{"s": "(b) Electrons are minority carriers and pentavalent atoms are the dopants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31097,74,475,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Holes are minority carriers and pentavalent atoms are the dopants.','[{"s": "(c) Holes are minority carriers and pentavalent atoms are the dopants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31098,74,476,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Holes are majority carriers and trivalent atoms are the dopants.','[{"s": "(d) Holes are majority carriers and trivalent atoms are the dopants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31099,74,477,'paragraph',NULL,'14.2 Which of the statements given in Exercise 14.1 is true for p-type semiconductos','[{"s": "14.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Which of the statements given in Exercise 14.1 is true for p-type semiconductos", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31100,74,478,'paragraph',NULL,'14.3 Carbon, silicon and germanium have four valence electrons each. These are characterised by valence and conduction bands separated by energy band gap respectively equal to ( E g ) C , ( E g ) Si and ( E g ) Ge . Which of the following statements is true?','[{"s": "14.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Carbon, silicon and germanium have four valence electrons each. These are characterised by valence and conduction bands separated by energy band gap respectively equal to (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Si", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ge", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Which of the following statements is true?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31101,74,479,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) ( E g ) Si < ( E g ) Ge < ( E g ) C','[{"s": "(a) (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Si", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ge", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31102,74,480,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) ( E g ) C < ( E g ) Ge > ( E g ) Si','[{"s": "(b) (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ge", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Si", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31103,74,481,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) ( E g ) C > ( E g ) Si > ( E g ) Ge','[{"s": "(c) (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Si", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ge", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31104,74,482,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) ( E g ) C = ( E g ) Si = ( E g ) Ge','[{"s": "(d) (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Si", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ge", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31105,74,483,'paragraph',NULL,'14.4 In an unbiased p-n junction, holes diffuse from the p-region to n-region because','[{"s": "14.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In an unbiased p-n junction, holes diffuse from the p-region to n-region because", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31106,74,484,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) free electrons in the n-region attract them.','[{"s": "(a) free electrons in the n-region attract them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31107,74,485,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) they move across the junction by the potential difference.','[{"s": "(b) they move across the junction by the potential difference.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31108,74,486,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) hole concentration in p-region is more as compared to n-region.','[{"s": "(c) hole concentration in p-region is more as compared to n-region.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31109,74,487,'paragraph',NULL,'14.5 When a forward bias is applied to a p-n junction, it','[{"s": "14.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When a forward bias is applied to a p-n junction, it", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31110,74,488,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) raises the potential barrier.','[{"s": "(a) raises the potential barrier.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31111,74,489,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) reduces the majority carrier current to zero.','[{"s": "(b) reduces the majority carrier current to zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31112,74,490,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) lowers the potential barrier.','[{"s": "(c) lowers the potential barrier.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31113,74,491,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) None of the above.','[{"s": "(d) None of the above.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31114,74,492,'paragraph',NULL,'14.6 For transistor action, which of the following statements are correct:','[{"s": "14.6 For transistor action, which of the following statements are correct:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31115,74,493,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Base, emitter and collector regions should have similar size and doping concentrations.','[{"s": "(a) Base, emitter and collector regions should have similar size and doping concentrations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31116,74,494,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The base region must be very thin and lightly doped.','[{"s": "(b) The base region must be very thin and lightly doped.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31117,74,495,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The emitter junction is forward biased and collector junction is reverse biased.','[{"s": "(c) The emitter junction is forward biased and collector junction is reverse biased.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31118,74,496,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Both the emitter junction as well as the collector junction are forward biased.','[{"s": "(d) Both the emitter junction as well as the collector junction are forward biased.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31119,74,497,'paragraph',NULL,'14.7 For a transistor amplifier, the voltage gain','[{"s": "14.7 For a transistor amplifier, the voltage gain", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31120,74,498,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) remains constant for all frequencies.','[{"s": "(a) remains constant for all frequencies.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31121,74,499,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) is high at high and low frequencies and constant in the middle frequency range.','[{"s": "(b) is high at high and low frequencies and constant in the middle frequency range.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31122,74,500,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) is low at high and low frequencies and constant at mid frequencies.','[{"s": "(c) is low at high and low frequencies and constant at mid frequencies.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31123,74,501,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) None of the above','[{"s": "(d) None of the above", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31124,74,502,'paragraph',NULL,'rectifier for the same lnput frequency?','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "rectifier for the same lnput frequency?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31125,74,503,'paragraph',NULL,'14.9: for a CE-transistor amplifier, the audio signal voltage across the collected resistance of 2 KΩ 2 V. Suppose the current ampliiation factor ofthe transistor is 10, find the input signal voltage and base current, the base resistances 1 KΩ .','[{"s": "14.9: for a CE-transistor amplifier, the audio signal voltage across the collected resistance of 2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "KΩ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 V. Suppose the current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ampliiation factor ofthe transistor is 10, find the input signal voltage and base current, the base resistances 1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "KΩ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
