(31129,74,507,'paragraph',NULL,'14.12 The number of silicon atoms per m 3 is 5 × 10 28 . This is doped simultaneously with 5 × 10 22 atoms per m 3 of Arsenic and 5 × 10 20 per m 3 atoms of Indium. Calculate the number of electrons and holes. Given that n i = 1.5 × 10 16 m –3 . Is the material n-type or p-type?','[{"s": "14.12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The number of silicon atoms per m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is 5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "28", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This is doped simultaneously with 5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "22", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atoms per m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of Arsenic and 5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "20", "t": "text"}, {"s": "per m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atoms of Indium. Calculate the number of electrons and holes. Given that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Is the material n-type or p-type?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31130,74,508,'paragraph',NULL,'14.13 In an intrinsic semiconductor the energy gap E g is 1.2eV. Its hole mobility is much smaller than electron mobility and independent of temperature. What is the ratio between conductivity at 600K and that at 300K? Assume that the temperature dependence of intrinsic carrier concentration n i is given by n i =n 0 exp[ −E g / 2 K B T ] where n 0 is a constant.','[{"s": "14.13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In an intrinsic semiconductor the energy gap", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is 1.2eV. Its hole mobility is much smaller than electron mobility and independent of temperature. What is the ratio between conductivity at 600K and that at 300K? Assume that the temperature dependence of intrinsic carrier concentration", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is given by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "exp[", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31131,74,509,'paragraph',NULL,'14.14 In a p-n junction diode, the current I can be expressed as l=l 0 exp[ eV/2k B T −1]','[{"s": "14.14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In a p-n junction diode, the current I can be expressed as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l=l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "exp[", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eV/2k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31132,74,510,'paragraph',NULL,'where I 0 is called the reverse saturation current, V is the voltage across the diode and is positive for forward bias and negative for reverse bias, and I is the current through the diode, k B is the Boltzmann constant (8.6×10 –5 eV/K) and T is the absolute temperature. If for a given diode I 0 = 5 × 10 –12 A and T = 300 K, then','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called the reverse saturation current,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the voltage across the diode and is positive for forward bias and negative for reverse bias, and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the current through the diode,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the Boltzmann constant (8.6×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "eV/K) and T is the absolute temperature. If for a given diode", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A and T = 300 K, then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
