(31354,75,20,'heading',2,'7.1 INTRODUCTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31355,75,21,'paragraph',NULL,'In the earlier chapters we primarily considered the motion of a single particle. (A particle is ideally represented as a point mass having no size.) We applied the results of our study even to the motion of bodies of finite size, assuming that motion of such bodies can be described in terms of the motion of a particle.','[{"s": "In the earlier chapters we primarily considered the motion of a single particle. (A particle is ideally represented as a point mass having no size.) We applied the results of our study even to the motion of bodies of finite size, assuming that motion of such bodies can be described in terms of the motion of a particle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31356,75,22,'paragraph',NULL,'Any real body which we encounter in daily life has a finite size. In dealing with the motion of extended bodies (bodies of finite size) often the idealised model of a particle is inadequate. In this chapter we shall try to go beyond this inadequacy. We shall attempt to build an understanding of the motion of extended bodies. An extended body, in the first place, is a system of particles. We shall begin with the consideration of motion of the system as a whole. The centre of mass of a system of particles will be a key concept here. We shall discuss the motion of the centre of mass of a system of particles and usefulness of this concept in understanding the motion of extended bodies.','[{"s": "Any real body which we encounter in daily life has a finite size. In dealing with the motion of extended bodies (bodies of finite size) often the idealised model of a particle is inadequate. In this chapter we shall try to go beyond this inadequacy. We shall attempt to build an understanding of the motion of extended bodies. An extended body, in the first place, is a system of particles. We shall begin with the consideration of motion of the system as a whole. The centre of mass of a system of particles will be a key concept here. We shall discuss the motion of the centre of mass of a system of particles and usefulness of this concept in understanding the motion of extended bodies.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31357,75,23,'paragraph',NULL,'A large class of problems with extended bodies can be solved by considering them to be rigid bodies. Ideally a rigid body is a body with a perfectly definite and unchanging shape. The distances between all pairs of particles of such a body do not change. It is evident from this definition of a rigid body that no real body is truly rigid, since real bodies deform under the influence of forces. But in many situations the deformations are negligible. In a number of situations involving bodies such as wheels, tops, steel beams, molecules and planets on the other hand, we can ignore that they warp (twist out of shape), bend or vibrate and treat them as rigid.','[{"s": "A large class of problems with extended bodies can be solved by considering them to be rigid bodies.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ideally a rigid body is a body with a perfectly definite and unchanging shape. The distances between all pairs of particles of such a body do not change.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is evident from this definition of a rigid body that no real body is truly rigid, since real bodies deform under the influence of forces. But in many situations the deformations are negligible. In a number of situations involving bodies such as wheels, tops, steel beams, molecules and planets on the other hand, we can ignore that they warp (twist out of shape), bend or vibrate and treat them as rigid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31358,75,24,'heading',3,'7.1.1 WHAT KIND OF MOTION CAN A RIGID BODY HAVE?',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31359,75,25,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us try to explore this question by taking some examples of the motion of rigid bodies. Let us begin with a rectangular block sliding down an inclined plane without any sidewise movement. The block is taken as a rigid body. Its motion down the plane is such that all the particles of the body are moving together, i.e. they have the same velocity at any instant of time. The rigid body here is in pure translational motion (Fig. 7.1).','[{"s": "Let us try to explore this question by taking some examples of the motion of rigid bodies. Let us begin with a rectangular block sliding down an inclined plane without any sidewise movement. The block is taken as a rigid body. Its motion down the plane is such that all the particles of the body are moving together, i.e. they have the same velocity at any instant of time. The rigid body here is in pure translational motion (Fig. 7.1).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31360,75,26,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image129.png'),
(31361,75,27,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 7.1 Translational (sliding) motion of a block down an inclined plane.','[{"s": "Fig 7.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Translational (sliding) motion of a block down an inclined plane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31362,75,28,'paragraph',NULL,'(Any point like P 1 or P 2 of the block moves with the same velocity at any instant of time.)','[{"s": "(Any point like P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the block moves with the same velocity at any instant of time.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31363,75,29,'paragraph',NULL,'In pure translational motion at any instant of time, all particles of the body have the same velocity.','[{"s": "In pure translational motion at any instant of time, all particles of the body have the same velocity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31364,75,30,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider now the rolling motion of a solid metallic or wooden cylinder down the same inclined plane (Fig. 7.2). The rigid body in this problem, namely the cylinder, shifts from the top to the bottom of the inclined plane, and thus, seems to have translational motion. But as Fig. 7.2 shows, all its particles are not moving with the same velocity at any instant. The body, therefore, is not in pure translational motion. Its motion is translational plus ‘something else.’','[{"s": "Consider now the rolling motion of a solid metallic or wooden cylinder down the same inclined plane (Fig. 7.2). The rigid body in this problem, namely the cylinder, shifts from the top to the bottom of the inclined plane, and thus, seems to have translational motion. But as Fig. 7.2 shows, all its particles are not moving with the same velocity at any instant. The body, therefore, is not in pure translational motion. Its motion is translational plus ‘something else.’", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31365,75,31,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image171.png'),
(31366,75,32,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.2 Rolling motion of a cylinder. It is not pure translational motion. Points P 1 , P 2 , P 3 and P 4 have different velocities (shown by arrows) at any instant of time. In fact, the velocity of the point of contact P 3 is zero at any instant, if the cylinder rolls without slipping.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Rolling motion of a cylinder. It is not pure translational motion. Points P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "have different velocities (shown by arrows) at any instant of time. In fact, the velocity of the point of contact P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is zero at any instant, if the cylinder rolls without slipping.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31367,75,33,'paragraph',NULL,'In order to understand what this ‘something else’ is, let us take a rigid body so constrained that it cannot have translational motion. The most common way to constrain a rigid body so that it does not have translational motion is to fix it along a straight line. The only possible motion of such a rigid body is rotation . The line or fixed axis about which the body is rotating is its axis of rotation . If you look around, you will come across many examples of rotation about an axis, a ceiling fan, a potter’s wheel, a giant wheel in a fair, a merry-go-round and so on (Fig 7.3(a) and (b)).','[{"s": "In order to understand what this ‘something else’ is, let us take a rigid body so constrained that it cannot have translational motion. The most common way to constrain a rigid body so that it does not have translational motion is to fix it along a straight line. The only possible motion of such a rigid body is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rotation", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The line or fixed axis about which the body is rotating is its", "t": "text"}, {"s": "axis of rotation", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If you look around, you will come across many examples of rotation about an axis, a ceiling fan, a potter’s wheel, a giant wheel in a fair, a merry-go-round and so on (Fig 7.3(a) and (b)).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31368,75,34,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 4.03.58 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image62.png'),
(31369,75,35,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.3 Rotation about a fixed axis','[{"s": "Fig. 7.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Rotation about a fixed axis", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31370,75,36,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) A ceiling fan','[{"s": "(a) A ceiling fan", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31371,75,37,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) A potter’s wheel.','[{"s": "(b) A potter’s wheel.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31374,75,40,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.4 A rigid body rotation about the z-axis (Each point of the body such as P 1 or P 2 describes a circle with its centre (C 1 or C 2 ) on the axis of rotation. The radius of the circle (r 1 or r 2 ) is the perpendicular distance of the point (P 1 or P 2 ) from the axis. A point on the axis like P 3 remains stationary).','[{"s": "Fig. 7.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A rigid body rotation about the z-axis (Each point of the body such as P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "describes a circle with its centre (C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") on the axis of rotation. The radius of the circle (r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is the perpendicular distance of the point (P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") from the axis. A point on the axis like P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "remains stationary).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31375,75,41,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image243.png'),
(31376,75,42,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.5 (a) A spinning top (The point of contact of the top with the ground, its tip O, is fixed.)','[{"s": "Fig. 7.5 (a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A spinning top (The point of contact of the top with the ground, its tip O, is fixed.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31377,75,43,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image207.png'),
(31378,75,44,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.5 (b) An oscillating table fan with rotating blades. The pivot of the fan, point O, is fixed. The blades of the fan are under rotational motion, whereas, the axis of rotation of the fan blades is oscillating.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.5 (b)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An oscillating table fan with rotating blades. The pivot of the fan, point O, is fixed. The blades of the fan are under rotational motion, whereas, the axis of rotation of the fan blades is oscillating.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31379,75,45,'paragraph',NULL,'In some examples of rotation, however, the axis may not be fixed. A prominent example of this kind of rotation is a top spinning in place [Fig. 7.5(a)]. (We assume that the top does not slip from place to place and so does not have translational motion.) We know from experience that the axis of such a spinning top moves around the vertical through its point of contact with the ground, sweeping out a cone as shown in Fig. 7.5(a). (This movement of the axis of the top around the vertical is termed precession .) Note, the point of contact of the top with ground is fixed . The axis of rotation of the top at any instant passes through the point of contact. Another simple example of this kind of rotation is the oscillating table fan or a pedestal fan [Fig.7.5(b)]. You may have observed that the axis of rotation of such a fan has an oscillating (sidewise) movement in a horizontal plane about the vertical through the point at which the axis is pivoted (point O in Fig. 7.5(b)).','[{"s": "In some examples of rotation, however, the axis may not be fixed. A prominent example of this kind of rotation is a top spinning in place [Fig. 7.5(a)]. (We assume that the top does not slip from place to place and so does not have translational motion.) We know from experience that the axis of such a spinning top moves around the vertical through its point of contact with the ground, sweeping out a cone as shown in Fig. 7.5(a). (This movement of the axis of the top around the vertical is termed", "t": "text"}, {"s": "precession", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".) Note, the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "point of contact of the top with ground is fixed", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The axis of rotation of the top at any instant passes through the point of contact. Another simple example of this kind of rotation is the oscillating table fan or a pedestal fan [Fig.7.5(b)]. You may have observed that the axis of rotation of such a fan has an oscillating (sidewise) movement in a horizontal plane about the vertical through the point at which the axis is pivoted (point O in Fig. 7.5(b)).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31380,75,46,'paragraph',NULL,'While the fan rotates and its axis moves sidewise, this point is fixed. Thus, in more general cases of rotation, such as the rotation of a top or a pedestal fan, one point and not one line, of the rigid body is fixed. In this case the axis is not fixed, though it always passes through the fixed point. In our study, however, we mostly deal with the simpler and special case of rotation in which one line (i.e. the axis) is fixed. Thus, for us rotation will be about a fixed axis only unless stated otherwise.','[{"s": "While the fan rotates and its axis moves sidewise, this point is fixed. Thus, in more general cases of rotation, such as the rotation of a top or a pedestal fan,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "one point and not one line,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the rigid body is fixed. In this case the axis is not fixed, though it always passes through the fixed point. In our study, however, we mostly deal with the simpler and special case of rotation in which one line (i.e. the axis) is fixed. Thus, for us rotation will be about a fixed axis only unless stated otherwise.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31381,75,47,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image214.png'),
(31382,75,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.6 (a) Motion of a rigid body which is pure translation.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.6 (a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Motion of a rigid body which is pure translation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31383,75,49,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image158.png'),
(31384,75,50,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.6(b)','[{"s": "Fig. 7.6(b)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31385,75,51,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 7.6 (a) and 7.6 (b) illustrate different motions of the same body. Note P is an arbitrary point of the body; O is the centre of mass of the body, which is defined in the next section. Suffice to say here that the trajectories of O are the translational trajectories Tr 1 and Tr 2 of the body. The positions O and P at three different instants of time are shown by O 1 , O 2 , and O 3 , and P 1 , P 2 and P 3 , respectively, in both Figs. 7.6 (a) and (b) . As seen from Fig. 7.6(a), at any instant the velocities of any particles like O and P of the body are the same in pure translation. Notice, in this case the orientation of OP, i.e. the angle OP makes with a fixed direction, say the horizontal, remains the same, i.e. α 1 = α 2 = α 3 . Fig. 7.6 (b) illustrates a case of combination of translation and rotation. In this case, at any instants the velocities of O and P differ. Also, α 1 , α 2 and α 3 may all be different.','[{"s": "Fig 7.6 (a) and 7.6 (b) illustrate different motions of the same body. Note P is an arbitrary point of the body; O is the centre of mass of the body, which is defined in the next section. Suffice to say here that the trajectories of O are the translational trajectories Tr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and Tr", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the body. The positions O and P at three different instants of time are shown by O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively, in both Figs. 7.6 (a) and (b) . As seen from Fig. 7.6(a), at any instant the velocities of any particles like O and P of the body are the same in pure translation. Notice, in this case the orientation of OP, i.e. the angle OP makes with a fixed direction, say the horizontal, remains the same, i.e. α", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= α", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= α", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Fig. 7.6 (b) illustrates a case of combination of translation and rotation. In this case, at any instants the velocities of O and P differ. Also, α", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", α", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and α", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "may all be different.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31386,75,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, for us rotation will be about a fixed axis only unless stated otherwise.','[{"s": "Thus, for us rotation will be about a fixed axis only unless stated otherwise.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31387,75,53,'paragraph',NULL,'The rolling motion of a cylinder down an inclined plane is a combination of rotation about a fixed axis and translation. Thus, the ‘something else’ in the case of rolling motion which we referred to earlier is rotational motion. You will find Fig. 7.6(a) and (b) instructive from this point of view. Both these figures show motion of the same body along identical translational trajectory. In one case, Fig. 7.6(a), the motion is a pure translation; in the other case [Fig. 7.6(b)] it is a combination of translation and rotation. (You may try to reproduce the two types of motion shown, using a rigid object like a heavy book.)','[{"s": "The rolling motion of a cylinder down an inclined plane is a combination of rotation about a fixed axis and translation. Thus, the ‘something else’ in the case of rolling motion which we referred to earlier is rotational motion. You will find Fig. 7.6(a) and (b) instructive from this point of view. Both these figures show motion of the same body along identical translational trajectory. In one case, Fig. 7.6(a), the motion is a pure translation; in the other case [Fig. 7.6(b)] it is a combination of translation and rotation. (You may try to reproduce the two types of motion shown, using a rigid object like a heavy book.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31388,75,54,'paragraph',NULL,'We now recapitulate the most important observations of the present section: The motion of a rigid body which is not pivoted or fixed in some way is either a pure translation or a combination of translation and rotation. The motion of a rigid body which is pivoted or fixed in some way is rotation. The rotation may be about an axis that is fixed (e.g. a ceiling fan) or moving (e.g. an oscillating table fan [Fig.7.5(b)]). We shall, in the present chapter, consider rotational motion about a fixed axis only.','[{"s": "We now recapitulate the most important observations of the present section:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The motion of a rigid body which is not pivoted or fixed in some way is either a pure translation or a combination of translation and rotation. The motion of a rigid body which is pivoted or fixed in some way is rotation.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The rotation may be about an axis that is fixed (e.g. a ceiling fan) or moving (e.g. an oscillating table fan [Fig.7.5(b)]). We shall, in the present chapter, consider rotational motion about a fixed axis only.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31389,75,55,'heading',2,'7.2 CENTRE OF MASS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31390,75,56,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall first see what the centre of mass of a system of particles is and then discuss its significance. For simplicity we shall start with a two particle system. We shall take the line joining the two particles to be the x- axis.','[{"s": "We shall first see what the centre of mass of a system of particles is and then discuss its significance. For simplicity we shall start with a two particle system. We shall take the line joining the two particles to be the x- axis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31391,75,57,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image63.png'),
(31392,75,58,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.7','[{"s": "Fig. 7.7", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31394,75,60,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image249.png'),
(31395,75,61,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.1)','[{"s": "(7.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31396,75,62,'paragraph',NULL,'In Eq. (7.1), X can be regarded as the mass-weighted mean of x 1 and x 2 . If the two particles have the same mass m 1 = m 2 = m , then','[{"s": "In Eq. (7.1), X can be regarded as the mass-weighted mean of x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If the two particles have the same mass m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31397,75,63,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image54.png'),
(31398,75,64,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, for two particles of equal mass the centre of mass lies exactly midway between them.','[{"s": "Thus, for two particles of equal mass the centre of mass lies exactly midway between them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31399,75,65,'paragraph',NULL,'If we have n particles of masses m 1 , m 2 , ...mn respectively, along a straight line taken as the x- axis, then by definition the position of the centre of the mass of the system of particles is given by.','[{"s": "If we have n particles of masses m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", ...mn respectively, along a straight line taken as the x- axis, then by definition the position of the centre of the mass of the system of particles is given by.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31400,75,66,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 4.12.21 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image212.png'),
(31401,75,67,'paragraph',NULL,'where x 1 , x 2 ,...x n are the distances of the particles from the origin ; is also measured from the same origin. The symbol ⅀ Systems_Of_Particles-image44.png (the Greek letter sigma) denotes summation, in this case over n particles. The sum','[{"s": "where x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",...x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the distances of the particles from the origin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; is also measured from the same origin. The symbol ⅀", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image44.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(the Greek letter sigma) denotes summation, in this case over n particles. The sum", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31402,75,68,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image178.png'),
(31403,75,69,'paragraph',NULL,'is the total mass of the system.','[{"s": "is the total mass of the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31404,75,70,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose that we have three particles, not lying in a straight line. We may define x– and y–axes in the plane in which the particles lie and represent the positions of the three particles by coordinates (x 1 ,y 1 ), (x 2 ,y 2 ) and (x 3 ,y 3 ) respectively. Let the masses of the three particles be m 1 , m 2 and m 3 respectively. The centre of mass C of the system of the three particles is defined and located by the coordinates (X, Y) given by','[{"s": "Suppose that we have three particles, not lying in a straight line. We may define x– and y–axes in the plane in which the particles lie and represent the positions of the three particles by coordinates (x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), (x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and (x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") respectively. Let the masses of the three particles be m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively. The centre of mass C of the system of the three particles is defined and located by the coordinates (X, Y) given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31405,75,71,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image68.png'),
(31406,75,72,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.3a)','[{"s": "(7.3a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
