(31464,75,130,'paragraph',NULL,'The centre of mass C is shown in the figure. Note that it is not the geometric centre of the triangle OAB. Why?','[{"s": "The centre of mass C is shown in the figure. Note that it is not the geometric centre of the triangle OAB. Why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31465,75,131,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.2 Find the centre of mass of a triangular lamina.','[{"s": "Example 7.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Find the centre of mass of a triangular lamina.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31466,75,132,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The lamina (∆LMN) may be subdivided into narrow strips each parallel to the base (MN) as shown in Fig. 7.10','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The lamina (∆LMN) may be subdivided into narrow strips each parallel to the base (MN) as shown in Fig. 7.10", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31467,75,133,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 7.44.19 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image145.png'),
(31468,75,134,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.10','[{"s": "Fig. 7.10", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31469,75,135,'paragraph',NULL,'By symmetry each strip has its centre of mass at its midpoint. If we join the midpoint of all the strips we get the median LP. The centre of mass of the triangle as a whole therefore, has to lie on the median LP. Similarly, we can argue that it lies on the median MQ and NR. This means the centre of mass lies on the point of concurrence of the medians, i.e. on the centroid G of the triangle.','[{"s": "By symmetry each strip has its centre of mass at its midpoint. If we join the midpoint of all the strips we get the median LP. The centre of mass of the triangle as a whole therefore, has to lie on the median LP. Similarly, we can argue that it lies on the median MQ and NR. This means the centre of mass lies on the point of concurrence of the medians, i.e. on the centroid G of the triangle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31470,75,136,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.3 Find the centre of mass of a uniform L-shaped lamina (a thin flat plate) with dimensions as shown. The mass of the lamina is 3 kg.','[{"s": "Example 7.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Find the centre of mass of a uniform L-shaped lamina (a thin flat plate) with dimensions as shown. The mass of the lamina is 3 kg.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31471,75,137,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Choosing the X and Y axes as shown in Fig. 7.11 we have the coordinates of the vertices of the L-shaped lamina as given in the figure. We can think of the L-shape to consist of 3 squares each of length 1m. The mass of each square is 1kg, since the lamina is uniform. The centres of mass C 1 , C 2 and C 3 of the squares are, by symmetry, their geometric centres and have coordinates (1/2,1/2), (3/2,1/2), (1/2,3/2) respectively. We take the masses of the squares to be concentrated at these points. The centre of mass of the whole L shape (X, Y) is the centre of mass of these mass points.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Choosing the X and Y axes as shown in Fig. 7.11 we have the coordinates of the vertices of the L-shaped lamina as given in the figure. We can think of the L-shape to consist of 3 squares each of length 1m. The mass of each square is 1kg, since the lamina is uniform. The centres of mass C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the squares are, by symmetry, their geometric centres and have coordinates (1/2,1/2), (3/2,1/2), (1/2,3/2) respectively. We take the masses of the squares to be concentrated at these points. The centre of mass of the whole L shape (X, Y) is the centre of mass of these mass points.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
