(31497,75,163,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.10)','[{"s": "(7.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31498,75,164,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the total mass of a system of particles times the acceleration of its centre of mass is the vector sum of all the forces acting on the system of particles.','[{"s": "Thus, the total mass of a system of particles times the acceleration of its centre of mass is the vector sum of all the forces acting on the system of particles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31480,75,146,'paragraph',NULL,'Equipped with the definition of the centre of mass, we are now in a position to discuss its physical importance for a system of n particles. We may rewrite Eq.(7.4d) as','[{"s": "Equipped with the definition of the centre of mass, we are now in a position to discuss its physical importance for a system of n particles.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "We may rewrite Eq.(7.4d) as", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000035192"]',NULL),
(31483,75,149,'paragraph',NULL,'Differentiating the two sides of the equation with respect to time we get','[{"s": "Differentiating the two sides of the equation with respect to time we get", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000035990"]',NULL),
(31499,75,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Note when we talk of the force F 1 on the first particle, it is not a single force, but the vector sum of all the forces on the first particle; likewise for the second particle etc. Among these forces on each particle there will be external forces exerted by bodies outside the system and also internal forces exerted by the particles on one another. We know from Newton’s third law that these internal forces occur in equal and opposite pairs and in the sum of forces of Eq. (7.10), their contribution is zero. Only the external forces contribute to the equation. We can then rewrite Eq. (7.10) as','[{"s": "Note when we talk of the force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on the first particle, it is not a single force, but the vector sum of all the forces on the first particle; likewise for the second particle etc. Among these forces on each particle there will be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "external", "t": "text"}, {"s": "forces exerted by bodies outside the system and also", "t": "text"}, {"s": "internal", "t": "text"}, {"s": "forces exerted by the particles on one another. We know from Newton’s third law that these internal forces occur in equal and opposite pairs and in the sum of forces of Eq. (7.10), their contribution is zero. Only the external forces contribute to the equation. We can then rewrite Eq. (7.10) as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31500,75,166,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image268.png'),
(31501,75,167,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.11)','[{"s": "(7.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31502,75,168,'paragraph',NULL,'where F ext represents the sum of all external forces acting on the particles of the system.','[{"s": "where F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ext", "t": "text"}, {"s": "represents the sum of all external forces acting on the particles of the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31503,75,169,'paragraph',NULL,'Eq. (7.11) states that the centre of mass of a system of particles moves as if all the mass of the system was concentrated at the centre of mass and all the external forces were applied at that point.','[{"s": "Eq. (7.11) states that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the centre of mass of a system of particles moves as if all the mass of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "system was concentrated at the centre of mass and all the external forces were applied at that point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31504,75,170,'paragraph',NULL,'Notice, to determine the motion of the centre of mass no knowledge of internal forces of the system of particles is required; for this purpose we need to know only the external forces.','[{"s": "Notice, to determine the motion of the centre of mass no knowledge of internal forces of the system of particles is required; for this purpose we need to know only the external forces.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31505,75,171,'paragraph',NULL,'To obtain Eq. (7.11) we did not need to specify the nature of the system of particles. The system may be a collection of particles in which there may be all kinds of internal motions, or it may be a rigid body which has either pure translational motion or a combination of translational and rotational motion. Whatever is the system and the motion of its individual particles, the centre of mass moves according to Eq. (7.11).','[{"s": "To obtain Eq. (7.11) we did not need to specify the nature of the system of particles. The system may be a collection of particles in which there may be all kinds of internal motions, or it may be a rigid body which has either pure translational motion or a combination of translational and rotational motion. Whatever is the system and the motion of its individual particles, the centre of mass moves according to Eq. (7.11).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31506,75,172,'paragraph',NULL,'Instead of treating extended bodies as single particles as we have done in earlier chapters, we can now treat them as systems of particles. We can obtain the translational component of their motion, i.e. the motion of the centre of mass of the system, by taking the mass of the whole system to be concentrated at the centre of mass and all the external forces on the system to be acting at the centre of mass.','[{"s": "Instead of treating extended bodies as single particles as we have done in earlier chapters, we can now treat them as systems of particles. We can obtain the translational component of their motion, i.e. the motion of the centre of mass of the system, by taking the mass of the whole system to be concentrated at the centre of mass and all the external forces on the system to be acting at the centre of mass.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31507,75,173,'paragraph',NULL,'This is the procedure that we followed earlier in analysing forces on bodies and solving problems without explicitly outlining and justifying the procedure.We now realise that in earlier studies we assumed, without saying so, that rotational motion and/or internal motion of the particles were either absent or negligible. We no longer need to do this. We have not only found the justification of the procedure we followed earlier; but we also have found how to describe and separate the translational motion of (1) a rigid body which may be rotating as well, or (2) a system of particles with all kinds of internal motion.','[{"s": "This is the procedure that we followed earlier in analysing forces on bodies and solving problems without explicitly outlining and justifying the procedure.We now realise that in earlier studies we assumed, without saying so, that rotational motion and/or internal motion of the particles were either absent or negligible. We no longer need to do this. We have not only found the justification of the procedure we followed earlier; but we also have found how to describe and separate the translational motion of (1) a rigid body which may be rotating as well, or (2) a system of particles with all kinds of internal motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31508,75,174,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 7.49.45 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image261.png'),
(31509,75,175,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.12 The centre of mass of the fragments of the projectile continues along the same parabolic path which it would have followed if there were no explosion.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The centre of mass of the fragments of the projectile continues along the same parabolic path which it would have followed if there were no explosion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31510,75,176,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.12 is a good illustration of Eq. (7.11). A projectile, following the usual parabolic trajectory, explodes into fragments midway in air. The forces leading to the explosion are internal forces. They contribute nothing to the motion of the centre of mass. The total external force, namely, the force of gravity acting on the body, is the same before and after the explosion. The centre of mass under the influence of the external force continues, therefore, along the same parabolic trajectory as it would have followed if there were no explosion.','[{"s": "Figure 7.12 is a good illustration of Eq. (7.11). A projectile, following the usual parabolic trajectory, explodes into fragments midway in air. The forces leading to the explosion are internal forces. They contribute nothing to the motion of the centre of mass. The total external force, namely, the force of gravity acting on the body, is the same before and after the explosion. The centre of mass under the influence of the external force continues, therefore, along the same parabolic trajectory as it would have followed if there were no explosion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31511,75,177,'heading',2,'7.4 LINEAR MOMENTUM OF A SYSTEM OF PARTICLES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31512,75,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us recall that the linear momentum of a particle is defined as','[{"s": "Let us recall that the linear momentum of a particle is defined as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31513,75,179,'paragraph',NULL,'p = m v (7.12)','[{"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31514,75,180,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us also recall that Newton’s second law written in symbolic form for a single particle is','[{"s": "Let us also recall that Newton’s second law written in symbolic form for a single particle is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31515,75,181,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image299.png'),
(31516,75,182,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.13)','[{"s": "(7.13)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31517,75,183,'paragraph',NULL,'where F is the force on the particle. Let us consider a system of n particles with masses m 1 , m 2 ,...m n respectively and velocities v 1 ,v 2 ,..........v n respectively. The particles may be interacting and have external forces acting on them. The linear momentum of the first particle is m 1 v 1 , of the second particle is m 2 v 2 and so on.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the force on the particle. Let us consider a system of n particles with masses m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",...m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively and velocities v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",..........v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively. The particles may be interacting and have external forces acting on them. The linear momentum of the first particle is m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", of the second particle is m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and so on.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31518,75,184,'paragraph',NULL,'For the system of n particles, the linear momentum of the system is defined to be the vector sum of all individual particles of the system,','[{"s": "For the system of n particles, the linear momentum of the system is defined to be the vector sum of all individual particles of the system,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31519,75,185,'paragraph',NULL,'P = p 1 +p 2 +.........+p n','[{"s": "P = p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+.........+p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31520,75,186,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image40.png'),
(31521,75,187,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.14)','[{"s": "(7.14)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31522,75,188,'paragraph',NULL,'Comparing this with Eq. (7.8)','[{"s": "Comparing this with Eq. (7.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31523,75,189,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image217.png'),
(31524,75,190,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.15)','[{"s": "(7.15)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31525,75,191,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the total momentum of a system of particles is equal to the product of the total mass of the system and the velocity of its centre of mass . Differentiating Eq. (7.15) with respect to time,','[{"s": "Thus,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the total momentum of a system of particles is equal to the product of the total mass of the system and the velocity of its centre of mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Differentiating Eq. (7.15) with respect to time,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31526,75,192,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image218.png'),
(31527,75,193,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.16)','[{"s": "(7.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31528,75,194,'paragraph',NULL,'Comparing Eq.(7.16) and Eq. (7.11),','[{"s": "Comparing Eq.(7.16) and Eq. (7.11),", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31529,75,195,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image66.png'),
(31530,75,196,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.17)','[{"s": "(7.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31531,75,197,'paragraph',NULL,'This is the statement of Newton’s second law of motion extended to a system of particles.','[{"s": "This is the statement of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Newton’s second law of motion extended to a system of particles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31532,75,198,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose now, that the sum of external forces acting on a system of particles is zero. Then from Eq.(7.17)','[{"s": "Suppose now, that the sum of external forces acting on a system of particles is zero. Then from Eq.(7.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31533,75,199,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 7.55.41 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image34.png'),
(31534,75,200,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.18a)','[{"s": "(7.18a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31536,75,202,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that on account of the internal forces, i.e. the forces exerted by the particles on one another, the individual particles may have complicated trajectories. Yet, if the total external force acting on the system is zero, the centre of mass moves with a constant velocity, i.e., moves uniformly in a straight line like a free particle.','[{"s": "Note that on account of the internal forces, i.e. the forces exerted by the particles on one another, the individual particles may have complicated trajectories. Yet, if the total external force acting on the system is zero, the centre of mass moves with a constant velocity, i.e., moves uniformly in a straight line like a free particle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31537,75,203,'paragraph',NULL,'The vector Eq. (7.18a) is equivalent to three scalar equations,','[{"s": "The vector Eq. (7.18a) is equivalent to three scalar equations,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31538,75,204,'paragraph',NULL,'P x = c 1 , P y = c 2 and P z = c 3 (7.18 b)','[{"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.18 b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31539,75,205,'paragraph',NULL,'Here P x , P y and P z are the components of the total linear momentum vector P along the x–, y– and z–axes respectively; c 1 , c 2 and c 3 are constants.','[{"s": "Here P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the components of the total linear momentum vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "along the x–, y– and z–axes respectively; c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are constants.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31540,75,206,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 7.57.11 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image50.png'),
(31541,75,207,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.13 (a) A heavy nucleus radium (Ra) splits into a lighter nucleus radon (Rn) and an alpha particle (nucleus of helium atom). The CM of the system is in uniform motion.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) A heavy nucleus radium (Ra) splits into a lighter nucleus radon (Rn) and an alpha particle (nucleus of helium atom). The CM of the system is in uniform motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31542,75,208,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The same spliting of the heavy nucleus radium (Ra) with the centre of mass at rest. The two product particles fly back to back.','[{"s": "(b) The same spliting of the heavy nucleus radium (Ra) with the centre of mass at rest. The two product particles fly back to back.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31543,75,209,'paragraph',NULL,'As an example, let us consider the radioactive decay of a moving unstable particle, like the nucleus of radium. A radium nucleus disintegrates into a nucleus of radon and an alpha particle. The forces leading to the decay are internal to the system and the external forces on the system are negligible. So the total linear momentum of the system is the same before and after decay. The two particles produced in the decay, the radon nucleus and the alpha particle, move in different directions in such a way that their centre of mass moves along the same path along which the original decaying radium nucleus was moving [Fig. 7.13(a)].','[{"s": "As an example, let us consider the radioactive decay of a moving unstable particle, like the nucleus of radium. A radium nucleus disintegrates into a nucleus of radon and an alpha particle. The forces leading to the decay are internal to the system and the external forces on the system are negligible. So the total linear momentum of the system is the same before and after decay. The two particles produced in the decay, the radon nucleus and the alpha particle, move in different directions in such a way that their centre of mass moves along the same path along which the original decaying radium nucleus was moving [Fig. 7.13(a)].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31544,75,210,'paragraph',NULL,'If we observe the decay from the frame of reference in which the centre of mass is at rest, the motion of the particles involved in the decay looks particularly simple; the product particles move back to back with their centre of mass remaining at rest as shown in Fig.7.13 (b).','[{"s": "If we observe the decay from the frame of reference in which the centre of mass is at rest, the motion of the particles involved in the decay looks particularly simple; the product particles move back to back with their centre of mass remaining at rest as shown in Fig.7.13 (b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31545,75,211,'paragraph',NULL,'In many problems on the system of particles, as in the above radioactive decay problem, it is convenient to work in the centre of mass frame rather than in the laboratory frame of reference.','[{"s": "In many problems on the system of particles, as in the above radioactive decay problem, it is convenient to work in the centre of mass frame rather than in the laboratory frame of reference.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31546,75,212,'paragraph',NULL,'In astronomy, binary (double) stars is a common occurrence. If there are no external forces, the centre of mass of a double star moves like a free particle, as shown in Fig.7.14 (a). The trajectories of the two stars of equal mass are also shown in the figure; they look complicated. If we go to the centre of mass frame, then we find that there the two stars are moving in a circle, about the centre of mass, which is at rest. Note that the position of the stars have to be diametrically opposite to each other [Fig. 7.14(b)]. Thus in our frame of reference, the trajectories of the stars are a combination of (i) uniform motion in a straight line of the centre of mass and (ii) circular orbits of the stars about the centre of mass.','[{"s": "In astronomy, binary (double) stars is a common occurrence. If there are no external forces, the centre of mass of a double star moves like a free particle, as shown in Fig.7.14 (a). The trajectories of the two stars of equal mass are also shown in the figure; they look complicated. If we go to the centre of mass frame, then we find that there the two stars are moving in a circle, about the centre of mass, which is at rest. Note that the position of the stars have to be diametrically opposite to each other [Fig. 7.14(b)]. Thus in our frame of reference, the trajectories of the stars are a combination of (i) uniform motion in a straight line of the centre of mass and (ii) circular orbits of the stars about the centre of mass.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31547,75,213,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-06 at 7.58.41 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image143.png'),
(31548,75,214,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.14 (a) Trajectories of two stars, S1 (dotted line) and S2 (solid line) forming a binary system with their centre of mass C in uniform motion.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Trajectories of two stars, S1 (dotted line) and S2 (solid line) forming a binary system with their centre of mass C in uniform motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31549,75,215,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The same binary system, with the centre of mass C at rest.','[{"s": "(b) The same binary system, with the centre of mass C at rest.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31550,75,216,'paragraph',NULL,'As can be seen from the two examples, separating the motion of different parts of a system into motion of the centre of mass and motion about the centre of mass is a very useful technique that helps in understanding the motion of the system.','[{"s": "As can be seen from the two examples,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "separating", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the motion of different parts of a system into motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the centre of mass and motion about the centre of mass", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a very useful technique that helps in understanding the motion of the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31551,75,217,'heading',2,'7.5 VECTOR PRODUCT OF TWO VECTORS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31552,75,218,'paragraph',NULL,'We are already familiar with vectors and their use in physics. In chapter 6 (Work, Energy, Power) we defined the scalar product of two vectors. An important physical quantity, work, is defined as a scalar product of two vector quantities, force and displacement.','[{"s": "We are already familiar with vectors and their use in physics. In chapter 6 (Work, Energy, Power) we defined the scalar product of two vectors. An important physical quantity, work, is defined as a scalar product of two vector quantities, force and displacement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31553,75,219,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall now define another product of two vectors. This product is a vector. Two important quantities in the study of rotational motion, namely, moment of a force and angular momentum, are defined as vector products.','[{"s": "We shall now define another product of two vectors. This product is a vector. Two important quantities in the study of rotational motion, namely, moment of a force and angular momentum, are defined as vector products.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31554,75,220,'paragraph',NULL,'Definition of Vector Product','[{"s": "Definition of Vector Product", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31555,75,221,'paragraph',NULL,'A vector product of two vectors a and b is a vector c such that','[{"s": "A vector product of two vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "such that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31556,75,222,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) magnitude of c = c Systems_Of_Particles-image101.png where a and b are magnitudes of a and b and θ is the angle between the two vectors.','[{"s": "(i) magnitude of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image101.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "where a and b are magnitudes of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and θ is the angle between the two vectors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31557,75,223,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) c is perpendicular to the plane containing a and b .','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is perpendicular to the plane containing", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31558,75,224,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) if we take a right handed screw with its head lying in the plane of a and b and the screw perpendicular to this plane, and if we turn the head in the direction from a to b , then the tip of the screw advances in the direction of c . This right handed screw rule is illustrated in Fig. 7.15a.','[{"s": "(iii) if we take a right handed screw with its head lying in the plane of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the screw perpendicular to this plane, and if we turn the head in the direction from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", then the tip of the screw advances in the direction of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This right handed screw rule is illustrated in Fig. 7.15a.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31559,75,225,'paragraph',NULL,'Alternately, if one curls up the fingers of right hand around a line perpendicular to the plane of the vectors a and b and if the fingers are curled up in the direction from a to b , then the stretched thumb points in the direction of c , as shown in Fig. 7.15b.','[{"s": "Alternately, if one curls up the fingers of right hand around a line perpendicular to the plane of the vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and if the fingers are curled up in the direction from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", then the stretched thumb points in the direction of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", as shown in Fig. 7.15b.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31560,75,226,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 5.17.44 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image166.png'),
(31561,75,227,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.15 (a) Rule of the right handed screw for defining the direction of the vector product of two vectors.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Rule of the right handed screw for defining the direction of the vector product of two vectors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31562,75,228,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Rule of the right hand for defining the direction of the vector product.','[{"s": "(b) Rule of the right hand for defining the direction of the vector product.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31563,75,229,'paragraph',NULL,'A simpler version of the right hand rule is the following : Open up your right hand palm and curl the fingers pointing from a to b . Your stretched thumb points in the direction of c .','[{"s": "A simpler version of the right hand rule is the following : Open up your right hand palm and curl the fingers pointing from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Your stretched thumb points in the direction of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31564,75,230,'paragraph',NULL,'It should be remembered that there are two angles between any two vectors a and b . In Fig. 7.15 (a) or (b) they correspond to θ (as shown) and (360 0 – θ). While applying either of the above rules, the rotation should be taken through the smaller angle (<180 0 ) between a and b . It is θ here.','[{"s": "It should be remembered that there are two angles between any two vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In Fig. 7.15 (a) or (b) they correspond to θ (as shown) and (360", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– θ). While applying either of the above rules, the rotation should be taken through the smaller angle (<180", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is θ here.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31565,75,231,'paragraph',NULL,'Because of the cross (×) used to denote the vector product, it is also referred to as cross product.','[{"s": "Because of the cross (×) used to denote the vector product, it is also referred to as cross product.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31566,75,232,'paragraph',NULL,'• Note that scalar product of two vectors is commutative as said earlier, a . b = b.a','[{"s": "• Note that scalar product of two vectors is commutative as said earlier,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b.a", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31567,75,233,'paragraph',NULL,'The vector product, however, is not commutative, i.e. a × b ≠ b × a','[{"s": "The vector product, however, is not commutative, i.e.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≠", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31568,75,234,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitude of both a × b and b × a is the same ( ab sin θ ); also, both of them are perpendicular to the plane of a and b . But the rotation of the right-handed screw in case of a × b is from a to b , whereas in case of b × a it is from b to a . This means the two vectors are in opposite directions. We have','[{"s": "The magnitude of both", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the same ( ab sin θ ); also, both of them are perpendicular to the plane of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". But the rotation of the right-handed screw in case of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", whereas in case of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "it is from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This means the two vectors are in opposite directions. We have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31569,75,235,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image269.png'),
(31570,75,236,'paragraph',NULL,'• Another interesting property of a vector product is its behaviour under reflection. Under reflection (i.e. on taking the plane mirror image) we have Systems_Of_Particles-image306.png . As a result all the components of a vector change sign and thus Systems_Of_Particles-image220.png Systems_Of_Particles-image302.png . What happens to a × b under reflection?','[{"s": "• Another interesting property of a vector product is its behaviour under reflection. Under reflection (i.e. on taking the plane mirror image) we have", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image306.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". As a result all the components of a vector change sign and thus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image220.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image302.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". What happens to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "under reflection?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31571,75,237,'paragraph',NULL,'a × b Systems_Of_Particles-image216.png','[{"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image216.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31572,75,238,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, a × b does not change sign under reflection.','[{"s": "Thus,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "does not change sign under reflection.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31573,75,239,'paragraph',NULL,'• Both scalar and vector products are distributive with respect to vector addition.','[{"s": "• Both scalar and vector products are distributive with respect to vector addition.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31574,75,240,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus,','[{"s": "Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31575,75,241,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image74.png'),
(31576,75,242,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image298.png'),
(31577,75,243,'paragraph',NULL,'• We may write c = a × b in the component form. For this we first need to obtain some elementary cross products:','[{"s": "• We may write", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the component form. For this we first need to obtain some elementary cross products:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31578,75,244,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) a × a = 0 ( 0 is a null vector, i.e. a vector with zero magnitude)','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a null vector, i.e. a vector with zero magnitude)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31579,75,245,'paragraph',NULL,'This follows since magnitude of a × a is Systems_Of_Particles-image204.png .','[{"s": "This follows since magnitude of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image204.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31580,75,246,'paragraph',NULL,'From this follow the results','[{"s": "From this follow the results", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31581,75,247,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Systems_Of_Particles-image265.png','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image265.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31582,75,248,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Systems_Of_Particles-image100.png','[{"s": "(ii)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image100.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31583,75,249,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the magnitude of Systems_Of_Particles-image93.png is sin90 0 or 1, since Systems_Of_Particles-image179.png and Systems_Of_Particles-image8.png both have unit magnitude and the angle between them is 90 0 . Thus, Systems_Of_Particles-image93.png is a unit vector. A unit vector perpendicular to the plane of Systems_Of_Particles-image179.png and and related to them by the right hand screw rule is Systems_Of_Particles-image237.png . Hence, the above result. You may verify similarly,','[{"s": "Note that the magnitude of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image93.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is sin90", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or 1, since", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image179.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image8.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "both have unit magnitude and the angle between them is 90", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image93.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is a unit vector. A unit vector perpendicular to the plane of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image179.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and related to them by the right hand screw rule is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image237.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". Hence, the above result. You may verify similarly,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31584,75,250,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image42.png'),
(31585,75,251,'paragraph',NULL,'From the rule for commutation of the cross product, it follows:','[{"s": "From the rule for commutation of the cross product, it follows:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31586,75,252,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image285.png'),
(31587,75,253,'paragraph',NULL,'Note if Systems_Of_Particles-image92.png occur cyclically in the above vector product relation, the vector product is positive. If Systems_Of_Particles-image92.png do not occur in cyclic order, the vector product is negative.','[{"s": "Note if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image92.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "occur cyclically in the above vector product relation, the vector product is positive. If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image92.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "do not occur in cyclic order, the vector product is negative.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31588,75,254,'paragraph',NULL,'Now,','[{"s": "Now,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31589,75,255,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image275.png'),
(31590,75,256,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image17.png'),
(31591,75,257,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image197.png'),
(31592,75,258,'paragraph',NULL,'We have used the elementary cross products in obtaining the above relation. The expression for a × b can be put in a determinant form which is easy to remember.','[{"s": "We have used the elementary cross products in obtaining the above relation. The expression for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be put in a determinant form which is easy to remember.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31593,75,259,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image310.png'),
(31594,75,260,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.4 Find the scalar and vector products of two vectors. a = (3iˆ – 4jˆ + 5kˆ ) and b = (– 2iˆ + jˆ – 3kˆ )','[{"s": "Example 7.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Find the scalar and vector products of two vectors. a = (3iˆ – 4jˆ + 5kˆ ) and b = (– 2iˆ + jˆ – 3kˆ )", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31595,75,261,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31596,75,262,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 5.51.33 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image266.png'),
(31597,75,263,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image274.png'),
(31598,75,264,'paragraph',NULL,'Note Systems_Of_Particles-image291.png','[{"s": "Note", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image291.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31599,75,265,'heading',2,'7.6 ANGULAR VELOCITY AND ITS RELATION WITH LINEAR VELOCITY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31600,75,266,'paragraph',NULL,'In this section we shall study what is angular velocity and its role in rotational motion. We have seen that every particle of a rotating body moves in a circle. The linear velocity of the particle is related to the angular velocity. The relation between these two quantities involves a vector product which we learnt about in the last section.','[{"s": "In this section we shall study what is angular velocity and its role in rotational motion. We have seen that every particle of a rotating body moves in a circle. The linear velocity of the particle is related to the angular velocity. The relation between these two quantities involves a vector product which we learnt about in the last section.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31601,75,267,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us go back to Fig. 7.4. As said above, in rotational motion of a rigid body about a fixed axis, every particle of the body moves in a circle, which lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis and has its centre on the axis. In Fig. 7.16 we redraw Fig. 7.4, showing a typical particle (at a point P) of the rigid body rotating about a fixed axis (taken as the z-axis). The particle describes a circle with a centre C on the axis. The radius of the circle is r, the perpendicular distance of the point P from the axis. We also show the linear velocity vector v of the particle at P. It is along the tangent at P to the circle.','[{"s": "Let us go back to Fig. 7.4. As said above, in rotational motion of a rigid body about a fixed axis, every particle of the body moves in a circle, which lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis and has its centre on the axis. In Fig. 7.16 we redraw Fig. 7.4, showing a typical particle (at a point P) of the rigid body rotating about a fixed axis (taken as the z-axis). The particle describes a circle with a centre C on the axis. The radius of the circle is r, the perpendicular distance of the point P from the axis. We also show the linear velocity vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the particle at P. It is along the tangent at P to the circle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31602,75,268,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image231.png'),
(31603,75,269,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.16 Rotation about a fixed axis. (A particle (P) of the rigid body rotating about the fixed (z-) axis moves in a circle with centre (C) on the axis.)','[{"s": "Fig. 7.16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Rotation about a fixed axis. (A particle (P) of the rigid body rotating about the fixed (z-) axis moves in a circle with centre (C) on the axis.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31604,75,270,'paragraph',NULL,'Let P′ be the position of the particle after an interval of time ∆t (Fig. 7.16). The angle PCP′ describes the angular displacement ∆θ of the particle in time ∆t. The average angular velocity of the particle over the interval ∆t is ∆θ/∆t. As ∆t tends to zero (i.e. takes smaller and smaller values), the ratio ∆θ/∆t approaches a limit which is the instantaneous angular velocity dθ/dt of the particle at the position P. We denote the instantaneous angular velocity by ω (the Greek letter omega). We know from our study of circular motion that the magnitude of linear velocity v of a particle moving in a circle is related to the angular velocity of the particle ω by the simple relation Systems_Of_Particles-image186.png , where r is the radius of the circle.','[{"s": "Let P′ be the position of the particle after an interval of time ∆t (Fig. 7.16). The angle PCP′ describes the angular displacement ∆θ of the particle in time ∆t. The average angular velocity of the particle over the interval ∆t is ∆θ/∆t. As ∆t tends to zero (i.e. takes smaller and smaller values), the ratio ∆θ/∆t approaches a limit which is the instantaneous angular velocity dθ/dt of the particle at the position P. We denote the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "instantaneous angular velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by ω (the Greek letter omega). We know from our study of circular motion that the magnitude of linear velocity v of a particle moving in a circle is related to the angular velocity of the particle ω by the simple relation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image186.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", where r is the radius of the circle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31605,75,271,'paragraph',NULL,'We observe that at any given instant the relation Systems_Of_Particles-image270.png applies to all particles of the rigid body. Thus for a particle at a perpendicular distance r i from the fixed axis, the linear velocity at a given instant v i is given by','[{"s": "We observe that at any given instant the relation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image270.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "applies to all particles of the rigid body. Thus for a particle at a perpendicular distance r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from the fixed axis, the linear velocity at a given instant v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31606,75,272,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image233.png'),
(31607,75,273,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.19)','[{"s": "(7.19)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31608,75,274,'paragraph',NULL,'The index i runs from 1 to n, where n is the total number of particles of the body.','[{"s": "The index i runs from 1 to n, where n is the total number of particles of the body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(31609,75,275,'paragraph',NULL,'For particles on the axis, Systems_Of_Particles-image199.png , and hence v = ω r = 0. Thus, particles on the axis are stationary. This verifies that the axis is fixed.','[{"s": "For particles on the axis,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image199.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", and hence v = ω r = 0. Thus, particles on the axis are stationary. This verifies that the axis is fixed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31610,75,276,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that we use the same angular velocity ω for all the particles. We therefore, refer to ω as the angular velocity of the whole body .','[{"s": "Note that we use the same angular velocity ω for all the particles.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We therefore, refer to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as the angular velocity of the whole body", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31611,75,277,'paragraph',NULL,'We have characterised pure translation of a body by all parts of the body having the same velocity at any instant of time. Similarly, we may characterise pure rotation by all parts of the body having the same angular velocity at any instant of time . Note that this characterisation of the rotation of a rigid body about a fixed axis is just another way of saying as in Sec. 7.1 that each particle of the body moves in a circle, which lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis and has the centre on the axis.','[{"s": "We have characterised pure translation of a body by all parts of the body having the same velocity at any instant of time. Similarly, we may characterise pure rotation by all parts of the body having the same angular velocity at any instant of time", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Note that this characterisation of the rotation of a rigid body about a fixed axis is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "just another way", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of saying as in Sec. 7.1 that each particle of the body moves in a circle, which lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis and has the centre on the axis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31612,75,278,'paragraph',NULL,'In our discussion so far the angular velocity appears to be a scalar. In fact, it is a vector. We shall not justify this fact, but we shall accept it. For rotation about a fixed axis, the angular velocity vector lies along the axis of rotation, and points out in the direction in which a right handed screw would advance, if the head of the screw is rotated with the body. (See Fig. 7.17a).','[{"s": "In our discussion so far the angular velocity appears to be a scalar. In fact, it is a vector. We shall not justify this fact, but we shall accept it. For rotation about a fixed axis, the angular velocity vector lies along the axis of rotation, and points out in the direction in which a right handed screw would advance, if the head of the screw is rotated with the body. (See Fig. 7.17a).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31613,75,279,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitude of this vector is Systems_Of_Particles-image287.png referred as above.','[{"s": "The magnitude of this vector is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image287.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "referred as above.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31614,75,280,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image20.png'),
(31615,75,281,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.17 (a) If the head of a right handed screw rotates with the body, the screw advances in the direction of the angular velocity ω. If the sense (clockwise or anticlockwise) of rotation of the body changes, so does the direction of ω.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) If the head of a right handed screw rotates with the body, the screw advances in the direction of the angular velocity ω. If the sense (clockwise or anticlockwise) of rotation of the body changes, so does the direction of ω.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31616,75,282,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image131.png'),
(31617,75,283,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.17 (b) The angular velocity vector ω is directed along the fixed axis as shown. The linear velocity of the particle at P is v = ω × r. It is perpendicular to both ω and r and is directed along the tangent to the circle described by the particle.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(b) The angular velocity vector ω is directed along the fixed axis as shown. The linear velocity of the particle at P is v = ω × r. It is perpendicular to both ω and r and is directed along the tangent to the circle described by the particle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31618,75,284,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall now look at what the vector product ω × r corresponds to. Refer to Fig. 7.17(b) which is a part of Fig. 7.16 reproduced to show the path of the particle P. The figure shows the vector ω directed along the fixed (z–) axis and also the position vector r = OP of the particle at P of the rigid body with respect to the origin O. Note that the origin is chosen to be on the axis of rotation.','[{"s": "We shall now look at what the vector product ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "corresponds to. Refer to Fig. 7.17(b) which is a part of Fig. 7.16 reproduced to show the path of the particle P. The figure shows the vector ω directed along the fixed (z–) axis and also the position vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the particle at P of the rigid body with respect to the origin O. Note that the origin is chosen to be on the axis of rotation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31619,75,285,'paragraph',NULL,'Now ω × r = ω × OP = ω × (OC + CP)','[{"s": "Now ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OP =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(OC + CP)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31620,75,286,'paragraph',NULL,'But ω × OC = 0 as ω is along OC','[{"s": "But ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OC =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0 as ω is along", "t": "text"}, {"s": "OC", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31621,75,287,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence ω × r = ω × CP','[{"s": "Hence ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CP", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31622,75,288,'paragraph',NULL,'The vector ω × CP is perpendicular to ω, i.e. to the z-axis and also to CP , the radius of the circle described by the particle at P. It is therefore, along the tangent to the circle at P. Also, the magnitude of ω × CP CP are perpendicular to each other. We shall denote CP by Systems_Of_Particles-image35.png and not by r , as we did earlier.','[{"s": "The vector ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is perpendicular to ω, i.e. to the z-axis and also to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CP", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the radius of the circle described by the particle at P. It is therefore, along the tangent to the circle at P. Also, the magnitude of ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "CP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are perpendicular to each other. We shall denote", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image35.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and not by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", as we did earlier.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31624,75,290,'paragraph',NULL,'v = ω × r (7.20)','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31625,75,291,'paragraph',NULL,'In fact, the relation, Eq. (7.20), holds good even for rotation of a rigid body with one point fixed, such as the rotation of the top [Fig. 7.6(a)]. In this case r represents the position vector of the particle with respect to the fixed point taken as the origin.','[{"s": "In fact, the relation, Eq. (7.20), holds good even for rotation of a rigid body with one point fixed, such as the rotation of the top [Fig. 7.6(a)]. In this case", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "represents the position vector of the particle with respect to the fixed point taken as the origin.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31626,75,292,'paragraph',NULL,'We note that for rotation about a fixed axis, the direction of the vector ω does not change with time. Its magnitude may, however, change from instant to instant. For the more general rotation, both the magnitude and the direction of ω may change from instant to instant.','[{"s": "We note that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for rotation about a fixed axis, the direction of the vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "does not change with time. Its magnitude may, however, change from instant to instant. For the more general rotation, both the magnitude and the direction of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "may change from instant to instant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31627,75,293,'heading',3,'7.6.1 ANGULAR ACCELERATION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31628,75,294,'paragraph',NULL,'You may have noticed that we are developing the study of rotational motion along the lines of the study of translational motion with which we are already familiar. Analogous to the kinetic variables of linear displacement (s) and velocity ( v ) in translational motion, we have angular displacement (θ) and angular velocity (ω) in rotational motion. It is then natural to define in rotational motion the concept of angular acceleration in analogy with linear acceleration defined as the time rate of change of velocity in translational motion. We define angular acceleration α as the time rate of change of angular velocity; Thus,','[{"s": "You may have noticed that we are developing the study of rotational motion along the lines of the study of translational motion with which we are already familiar. Analogous to the kinetic variables of linear displacement", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(s)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and velocity (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") in translational motion, we have angular displacement (θ) and angular velocity (ω) in rotational motion. It is then natural to define in rotational motion the concept of angular acceleration in analogy with linear acceleration defined as the time rate of change of velocity in translational motion. We define angular acceleration α as the time rate of change of angular velocity; Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31629,75,295,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image144.png'),
(31630,75,296,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.21)','[{"s": "(7.21)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31632,75,298,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image87.png'),
(31633,75,299,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.22)','[{"s": "(7.22)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31634,75,300,'heading',2,'7.7 TORQUE AND ANGULAR MOMENTUM',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31635,75,301,'paragraph',NULL,'In this section, we shall acquaint ourselves with two physical quantities (torque and angular momentum) which are defined as vector products of two vectors. These as we shall see, are especially important in the discussion of motion of systems of particles, particularly rigid bodies.','[{"s": "In this section, we shall acquaint ourselves with two physical quantities (torque and angular momentum) which are defined as vector products of two vectors. These as we shall see, are especially important in the discussion of motion of systems of particles, particularly rigid bodies.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31636,75,302,'heading',3,'7.7.1 MOMENT OF FORCE (TORQUE)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31637,75,303,'paragraph',NULL,'We have learnt that the motion of a rigid body, in general, is a combination of rotation and translation. If the body is fixed at a point or along a line, it has only rotational motion. We know that force is needed to change the translational state of a body, i.e. to produce linear acceleration. We may then ask, what is the analogue of force in the case of rotational motion? To look into the question in a concrete situation let us take the example of opening or closing of a door. A door is a rigid body which can rotate about a fixed vertical axis passing through the hinges. What makes the door rotate? It is clear that unless a force is applied the door does not rotate. But any force does not do the job. A force applied to the hinge line cannot produce any rotation at all, whereas a force of given magnitude applied at right angles to the door at its outer edge is most effective in producing rotation. It is not the force alone, but how and where the force is applied is important in rotational motion.','[{"s": "We have learnt that the motion of a rigid body, in general, is a combination of rotation and translation. If the body is fixed at a point or along a line, it has only rotational motion. We know that force is needed to change the translational state of a body, i.e. to produce linear acceleration. We may then ask, what is the analogue of force in the case of rotational motion? To look into the question in a concrete situation let us take the example of opening or closing of a door. A door is a rigid body which can rotate about a fixed vertical axis passing through the hinges. What makes the door rotate? It is clear that unless a force is applied the door does not rotate. But any force does not do the job. A force applied to the hinge line cannot produce any rotation at all, whereas a force of given magnitude applied at right angles to the door at its outer edge is most effective in producing rotation. It is not the force alone, but how and where the force is applied is important in rotational motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31639,75,305,'image',NULL,'718',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image23.png'),
(31642,75,308,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = r × F (7.23)','[{"s": "τ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.23)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31643,75,309,'paragraph',NULL,'The moment of force (or torque) is a vector quantity. The symbol τ stands for the Greek letter tau. The magnitude of τ is','[{"s": "The moment of force (or torque) is a vector quantity. The symbol τ stands for the Greek letter tau. The magnitude of τ is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31644,75,310,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = r F sinθ (7.24a)','[{"s": "τ = r F sinθ (7.24a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31645,75,311,'paragraph',NULL,'where r is the magnitude of the position vector r , i.e. the length OP, F is the magnitude of force F and θ is the angle between r and F as shown.','[{"s": "where r is the magnitude of the position vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", i.e. the length OP, F is the magnitude of force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and θ is the angle between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as shown.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31646,75,312,'paragraph',NULL,'Moment of force has dimensions M L 2 T -2 . Its dimensions are the same as those of work or energy. It is, however, a very different physical quantity than work. Moment of a force is a vector, while work is a scalar. The SI unit of moment of force is newton metre (N m). The magnitude of the moment of force may be written','[{"s": "Moment of force has dimensions M L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Its dimensions are the same as those of work or energy. It is, however, a very different physical quantity than work. Moment of a force is a vector, while work is a scalar. The SI unit of moment of force is newton metre (N m). The magnitude of the moment of force may be written", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31647,75,313,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image183.png'),
(31648,75,314,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.24b)','[{"s": "(7.24b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31649,75,315,'paragraph',NULL,'or Systems_Of_Particles-image28.png','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image28.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31650,75,316,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.24c)','[{"s": "(7.24c)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31651,75,317,'paragraph',NULL,'where Systems_Of_Particles-image11.png = r sinθ is the perpendicular distance of the line of action of F from the origin and Systems_Of_Particles-image209.png is the component of F in the direction perpendicular to r . Note that τ = 0 if r = 0, F = 0 or θ = 0 0 or 180 0 . Thus, the moment of a force vanishes if either the magnitude of the force is zero, or if the line of action of the force passes through the origin.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image11.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= r sinθ is the perpendicular distance of the line of action of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from the origin and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image209.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is the component of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the direction perpendicular to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Note that τ = 0 if r = 0, F = 0 or θ = 0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or 180", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, the moment of a force vanishes if either the magnitude of the force is zero, or if the line of action of the force passes through the origin.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31652,75,318,'paragraph',NULL,'One may note that since r × F is a vector product, properties of a vector product of two vectors apply to it. If the direction of F is reversed, the direction of the moment of force is reversed. If directions of both r and F are reversed, the direction of the moment of force remains the same.','[{"s": "One may note that since", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a vector product, properties of a vector product of two vectors apply to it. If the direction of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is reversed, the direction of the moment of force is reversed. If directions of both", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are reversed, the direction of the moment of force remains the same.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31653,75,319,'heading',3,'7.7.1 MOMENT OF FORCE (TORQUE)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31654,75,320,'paragraph',NULL,'We have learnt that the motion of a rigid body, in general, is a combination of rotation and translation. If the body is fixed at a point or along a line, it has only rotational motion. We know that force is needed to change the translational state of a body, i.e. to produce linear acceleration. We may then ask, what is the analogue of force in the case of rotational motion? To look into the question in a concrete situation let us take the example of opening or closing of a door. A door is a rigid body which can rotate about a fixed vertical axis passing through the hinges. What makes the door rotate? It is clear that unless a force is applied the door does not rotate. But any force does not do the job. A force applied to the hinge line cannot produce any rotation at all, whereas a force of given magnitude applied at right angles to the door at its outer edge is most effective in producing rotation. It is not the force alone, but how and where the force is applied is important in rotational motion.','[{"s": "We have learnt that the motion of a rigid body, in general, is a combination of rotation and translation. If the body is fixed at a point or along a line, it has only rotational motion. We know that force is needed to change the translational state of a body, i.e. to produce linear acceleration. We may then ask, what is the analogue of force in the case of rotational motion? To look into the question in a concrete situation let us take the example of opening or closing of a door. A door is a rigid body which can rotate about a fixed vertical axis passing through the hinges. What makes the door rotate? It is clear that unless a force is applied the door does not rotate. But any force does not do the job. A force applied to the hinge line cannot produce any rotation at all, whereas a force of given magnitude applied at right angles to the door at its outer edge is most effective in producing rotation. It is not the force alone, but how and where the force is applied is important in rotational motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31655,75,321,'paragraph',NULL,'The rotational analogue of force in linear motion is moment of force . It is also referred to as torque or couple . (We shall use the words moment of force and torque interchangeably.) We shall first define the moment of force for the special case of a single particle. Later on we shall extend the concept to systems of particles including rigid bodies. We shall also relate it to a change in the state of rotational motion, i.e. is angular acceleration of a rigid body.','[{"s": "The rotational analogue of force in linear motion is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "moment of force", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is also referred to as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "torque", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "couple", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". (We shall use the words moment of force and torque interchangeably.) We shall first define the moment of force for the special case of a single particle. Later on we shall extend the concept to systems of particles including rigid bodies. We shall also relate it to a change in the state of rotational motion, i.e. is angular acceleration of a rigid body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31656,75,322,'image',NULL,'718',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image23.png'),
(31657,75,323,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.18 τ = r × F , τ is perpendicular to the plane containing r and F , and its direction is given by the right handed screw rule.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "τ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", τ is perpendicular to the plane containing", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and its direction is given by the right handed screw rule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31658,75,324,'paragraph',NULL,'If a force acts on a single particle at a point P whose position with respect to the origin O is given by the position vector r (Fig. 7.18), the moment of the force acting on the particle with respect to the origin O is defined as the vector product','[{"s": "If a force acts on a single particle at a point P whose position with respect to the origin O is given by the position vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Fig. 7.18), the moment of the force acting on the particle with respect to the origin O is defined as the vector product", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31659,75,325,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = r × F (7.23)','[{"s": "τ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.23)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31660,75,326,'paragraph',NULL,'The moment of force (or torque) is a vector quantity. The symbol τ stands for the Greek letter tau. The magnitude of τ is','[{"s": "The moment of force (or torque) is a vector quantity. The symbol τ stands for the Greek letter tau. The magnitude of τ is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31661,75,327,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = r F sinθ (7.24a)','[{"s": "τ = r F sinθ (7.24a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31662,75,328,'paragraph',NULL,'where r is the magnitude of the position vector r , i.e. the length OP, F is the magnitude of force F and θ is the angle between r and F as shown.','[{"s": "where r is the magnitude of the position vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", i.e. the length OP, F is the magnitude of force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and θ is the angle between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as shown.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31663,75,329,'paragraph',NULL,'Moment of force has dimensions M L 2 T -2 . Its dimensions are the same as those of work or energy. It is, however, a very different physical quantity than work. Moment of a force is a vector, while work is a scalar. The SI unit of moment of force is newton metre (N m). The magnitude of the moment of force may be written','[{"s": "Moment of force has dimensions M L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Its dimensions are the same as those of work or energy. It is, however, a very different physical quantity than work. Moment of a force is a vector, while work is a scalar. The SI unit of moment of force is newton metre (N m). The magnitude of the moment of force may be written", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31664,75,330,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image183.png'),
(31665,75,331,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.24b)','[{"s": "(7.24b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
