(31717,75,383,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall assume not only Newton’s third law of motion, i.e. the forces between any two particles of the system are equal and opposite, but also that these forces are directed along the line joining the two particles. In this case the contribution of the internal forces to the total torque on the system is zero, since the torque resulting from each action-reaction pair of forces is zero. We thus have, τ int = 0 and therefore τ = τ ext .','[{"s": "We shall assume not only Newton’s third law of motion, i.e. the forces between any two particles of the system are equal and opposite, but also that these forces are directed along the line joining the two particles. In this case the contribution of the internal forces to the total torque on the system is zero, since the torque resulting from each action-reaction pair of forces is zero. We thus have, τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "int", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and therefore τ = τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ext", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31718,75,384,'paragraph',NULL,'Since','[{"s": "Since", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31719,75,385,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 6.14.38 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image213.png'),
(31720,75,386,'paragraph',NULL,', it follows from Eq. (7.28a) that','[{"s": ", it follows from Eq. (7.28a) that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31721,75,387,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 6.14.45 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image118.png'),
(31722,75,388,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.28 b)','[{"s": "(7.28 b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31723,75,389,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the time rate of the total angular momentum of a system of particles about a point (taken as the origin of our frame of reference) is equal to the sum of the external torques (i.e. the torques due to external forces) acting on the system taken about the same point . Eq. (7.28 b) is the generalisation of the single particle case of Eq. (7.23) to a system of particles. Note that when we have only one particle, there are no internal forces or torques. Eq.(7.28 b) is the rotational analogue of','[{"s": "Thus,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the time rate of the total angular momentum of a system of particles about a point", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(taken as the origin of our frame of reference)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is equal to the sum of the external torques", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(i.e. the torques due to external forces)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "acting on the system taken about the same point", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Eq. (7.28 b) is the generalisation of the single particle case of Eq. (7.23) to a system of particles. Note that when we have only one particle, there are no internal forces or torques. Eq.(7.28 b) is the rotational analogue of", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31724,75,390,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image1.png'),
(31725,75,391,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.17)','[{"s": "(7.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31726,75,392,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that like Eq.(7.17), Eq.(7.28b) holds good for any system of particles, whether it is a rigid body or its individual particles have all kinds of internal motion.','[{"s": "Note that like Eq.(7.17), Eq.(7.28b) holds good for any system of particles, whether it is a rigid body or its individual particles have all kinds of internal motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31727,75,393,'paragraph',NULL,'Conservation of angular momentum','[{"s": "Conservation of angular momentum", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31728,75,394,'paragraph',NULL,'If τ ext = 0 , Eq. (7.28b) reduces to','[{"s": "If τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ext", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", Eq. (7.28b) reduces to", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31729,75,395,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image185.png'),
(31730,75,396,'paragraph',NULL,'or L = constant. (7.29a)','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= constant. (7.29a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31732,75,398,'paragraph',NULL,'L x = K 1 , L y = K 2 and L z = K 3 (7.29 b)','[{"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.29 b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31733,75,399,'paragraph',NULL,'Here K 1 , K 2 and K 3 are constants; L x , L y and L z are the components of the total angular momentum vector L along the x,y and z axes respectively. The statement that the total angular momentum is conserved means that each of these three components is conserved.','[{"s": "Here K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are constants; L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the components of the total angular momentum vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "along the x,y and z axes respectively. The statement that the total angular momentum is conserved means that each of these three components is conserved.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31734,75,400,'paragraph',NULL,'Eq. (7.29a) is the rotational analogue of Eq. (7.18a), i.e. the conservation law of the total linear momentum for a system of particles. Like Eq. (7.18a), it has applications in many practical situations. We shall look at a few of the interesting applications later on in this chapter.','[{"s": "Eq. (7.29a) is the rotational analogue of Eq. (7.18a), i.e. the conservation law of the total linear momentum for a system of particles. Like Eq. (7.18a), it has applications in many practical situations. We shall look at a few of the interesting applications later on in this chapter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31736,75,402,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Here Systems_Of_Particles-image259.png','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Here", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image259.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31737,75,403,'paragraph',NULL,'and Systems_Of_Particles-image161.png .','[{"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image161.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31738,75,404,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall use the determinant rule to find the torque Systems_Of_Particles-image301.png','[{"s": "We shall use the determinant rule to find the torque", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image301.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31739,75,405,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image155.png'),
(31740,75,406,'paragraph',NULL,'or Systems_Of_Particles-image80.png','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image80.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31741,75,407,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.6 Show that the angular momentum about any point of a single particle moving with constant velocity remains constant throughout the motion.','[{"s": "Example 7.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Show that the angular momentum about any point of a single particle moving with constant velocity remains constant throughout the motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31742,75,408,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Let the particle with velocity v be at point P at some instant t. We want to calculate the angular momentum of the particle about an arbitrary point O.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Let the particle with velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be at point P at some instant t. We want to calculate the angular momentum of the particle about an arbitrary point O.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31743,75,409,'image',NULL,'719',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image67.png'),
(31744,75,410,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 7.19','[{"s": "Fig 7.19", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31745,75,411,'paragraph',NULL,'The angular momentum is l = r × m v . Its magnitude is mvr sinθ, where θ is the angle between r and v as shown in Fig. 7.19. Although the particle changes position with time, the line of direction of v remains the same and hence OM = r sin θ. is a constant.','[{"s": "The angular momentum is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Its magnitude is mvr sinθ, where θ is the angle between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as shown in Fig. 7.19. Although the particle changes position with time, the line of direction of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "remains the same and hence OM = r sin θ. is a constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31746,75,412,'paragraph',NULL,'Further, the direction of l is perpendicular to the plane of r and v . It is into the page of the figure.This direction does not change with time.','[{"s": "Further,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the direction of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is perpendicular to the plane of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is into the page of the figure.This direction does not change with time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31747,75,413,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, l remains the same in magnitude and direction and is therefore conserved. Is there any external torque on the particle?','[{"s": "Thus,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "remains the same in magnitude and direction and is therefore conserved. Is there any external torque on the particle?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31748,75,414,'heading',2,'7.8 EQUILIBRIUM OF A RIGID BODY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31749,75,415,'paragraph',NULL,'We are now going to concentrate on the motion of rigid bodies rather than on the motion of general systems of particles.','[{"s": "We are now going to concentrate on the motion of rigid bodies rather than on the motion of general systems of particles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31750,75,416,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall recapitulate what effect the external forces have on a rigid body. (Henceforth we shall omit the adjective ‘external’ because unless stated otherwise, we shall deal with only external forces and torques.) The forces change the translational state of the motion of the rigid body, i.e. they change its total linear momentum in accordance with Eq. (7.17). But this is not the only effect the forces have. The total torque on the body may not vanish. Such a torque changes the rotational state of motion of the rigid body, i.e. it changes the total angular momentum of the body in accordance with Eq. (7.28 b).','[{"s": "We shall recapitulate what effect the external forces have on a rigid body. (Henceforth we shall omit the adjective ‘external’ because unless stated otherwise, we shall deal with only external forces and torques.) The forces change the translational state of the motion of the rigid body, i.e. they change its total linear momentum in accordance with Eq. (7.17). But this is not the only effect the forces have. The total torque on the body may not vanish. Such a torque changes the rotational state of motion of the rigid body, i.e. it changes the total angular momentum of the body in accordance with Eq. (7.28 b).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31751,75,417,'paragraph',NULL,'A rigid body is said to be in mechanical equilibrium, if both its linear momentum and angular momentum are not changing with time, or equivalently, the body has neither linear acceleration nor angular acceleration. This means','[{"s": "A rigid body is said to be in mechanical equilibrium, if both its linear momentum and angular momentum are not changing with time, or equivalently, the body has neither linear acceleration nor angular acceleration. This means", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31752,75,418,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) the total force, i.e. the vector sum of the forces, on the rigid body is zero;','[{"s": "(1) the total force, i.e. the vector sum of the forces, on the rigid body is zero;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31753,75,419,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image172.png'),
(31754,75,420,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.30a)','[{"s": "(7.30a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31755,75,421,'paragraph',NULL,'If the total force on the body is zero, then the total linear momentum of the body does not change with time. Eq. (7.30a) gives the condition for the translational equilibrium of the body.','[{"s": "If the total force on the body is zero, then the total linear momentum of the body does not change with time. Eq. (7.30a) gives the condition for the translational equilibrium of the body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31757,75,423,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 7.58.11 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image13.png'),
(31758,75,424,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.30b)','[{"s": "(7.30b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31759,75,425,'paragraph',NULL,'If the total torque on the rigid body is zero, the total angular momentum of the body does not change with time. Eq. (7.30 b) gives the condition for the rotational equilibrium of the body.','[{"s": "If the total torque on the rigid body is zero, the total angular momentum of the body does not change with time. Eq. (7.30 b) gives the condition for the rotational equilibrium of the body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31760,75,426,'paragraph',NULL,'One may raise a question, whether the rotational equilibrium condition [Eq. 7.30(b)] remains valid, if the origin with respect to which the torques are taken is shifted. One can show that if the translational equilibrium condition [Eq. 7.30(a)] holds for a rigid body, then such a shift of origin does not matter, i.e. the rotational equilibrium condition is independent of the location of the origin about which the torques are taken. Example 7.7 gives a proof of this result in a special case of a couple, i.e. two forces acting on a rigid body in translational equilibrium. The generalisation of this result to n forces is left as an exercise.','[{"s": "One may raise a question, whether the rotational equilibrium condition [Eq. 7.30(b)] remains valid, if the origin with respect to which the torques are taken is shifted. One can show that if the translational equilibrium condition [Eq. 7.30(a)] holds for a rigid body, then such a shift of origin does not matter, i.e. the rotational equilibrium condition is independent of the location of the origin about which the torques are taken. Example 7.7 gives a proof of this result in a special case of a couple, i.e. two forces acting on a rigid body in translational equilibrium. The generalisation of this result to n forces is left as an exercise.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31761,75,427,'paragraph',NULL,'Eq. (7.30a) and Eq. (7.30b), both, are vector equations. They are equivalent to three scalar equations each. Eq. (7.30a) corresponds to','[{"s": "Eq. (7.30a) and Eq. (7.30b), both, are vector equations. They are equivalent to three scalar equations each. Eq. (7.30a) corresponds to", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31762,75,428,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 7.58.49 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image56.png'),
(31763,75,429,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.31a)','[{"s": "(7.31a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31764,75,430,'paragraph',NULL,'where F ix , F iy and F iz are respectively the x, y and z components of the forces F i . Similarly, Eq. (7.30b) is equivalent to three scalar equations','[{"s": "where F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ix", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "iy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "iz", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are respectively the x, y and z components of the forces", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Similarly, Eq. (7.30b) is equivalent to three scalar equations", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31765,75,431,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 7.58.55 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image18.png'),
(31766,75,432,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.31b)','[{"s": "(7.31b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31767,75,433,'paragraph',NULL,'where τ ix , τ iy and τ iz are respectively the x, y and z components of the torque τ i .','[{"s": "where τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ix", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "iy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "iz", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are respectively the x, y and z components of the torque τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31768,75,434,'paragraph',NULL,'Eq. (7.31a) and (7.31b) give six independent conditions to be satisfied for mechanical equilibrium of a rigid body. In a number of problems all the forces acting on the body are coplanar. Then we need only three conditions to be satisfied for mechanical equilibrium. Two of these conditions correspond to translational equilibrium; the sum of the components of the forces along any two perpendicular axes in the plane must be zero. The third condition corresponds to rotational equilibrium. The sum of the components of the torques along any axis perpendicular to the plane of the forces must be zero.','[{"s": "Eq. (7.31a) and (7.31b) give six independent conditions to be satisfied for mechanical equilibrium of a rigid body. In a number of problems all the forces acting on the body are coplanar. Then we need only three conditions to be satisfied for mechanical equilibrium. Two of these conditions correspond to translational equilibrium; the sum of the components of the forces along any two perpendicular axes in the plane must be zero. The third condition corresponds to rotational equilibrium. The sum of the components of the torques along any axis perpendicular to the plane of the forces must be zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31769,75,435,'paragraph',NULL,'The conditions of equilibrium of a rigid body may be compared with those for a particle, which we considered in earlier chapters. Since consideration of rotational motion does not apply to a particle, only the conditions for translational equilibrium (Eq. 7.30 a) apply to a particle. Thus, for equilibrium of a particle the vector sum of all the forces on it must be zero. Since all these forces act on the single particle, they must be concurrent. Equilibrium under concurrent forces was discussed in the earlier chapters.','[{"s": "The conditions of equilibrium of a rigid body may be compared with those for a particle, which we considered in earlier chapters. Since consideration of rotational motion does not apply to a particle, only the conditions for translational equilibrium (Eq. 7.30 a) apply to a particle. Thus, for equilibrium of a particle the vector sum of all the forces on it must be zero. Since all these forces act on the single particle, they must be concurrent. Equilibrium under concurrent forces was discussed in the earlier chapters.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31770,75,436,'paragraph',NULL,'A body may be in partial equilibrium, i.e., it may be in translational equilibrium and not in rotational equilibrium, or it may be in rotational equilibrium and not in translational equilibrium.','[{"s": "A body may be in partial equilibrium, i.e., it may be in translational equilibrium and not in rotational equilibrium, or it may be in rotational equilibrium and not in translational equilibrium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31771,75,437,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a light (i.e. of negligible mass) rod (AB) as shown in Fig. 7.20(a). At the two ends (A and B) of which two parallel forces, both equal in magnitude and acting along same direction are applied perpendicular to the rod.','[{"s": "Consider a light (i.e. of negligible mass) rod (AB) as shown in Fig. 7.20(a). At the two ends (A and B) of which two parallel forces, both equal in magnitude and acting along same direction are applied perpendicular to the rod.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31772,75,438,'image',NULL,'720(a)',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image278.png'),
(31773,75,439,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.20 (a)','[{"s": "Fig. 7.20 (a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31774,75,440,'paragraph',NULL,'Let C be the midpoint of AB, CA = CB = a. the moment of the forces at A and B will both be equal in magnitude (aF), but opposite in sense as shown. The net moment on the rod will be zero. The system will be in rotational equilibrium, but it will not be in translational equilibrium; Systems_Of_Particles-image109.png','[{"s": "Let C be the midpoint of AB, CA = CB = a. the moment of the forces at A and B will both be equal in magnitude (aF), but opposite in sense as shown. The net moment on the rod will be zero. The system will be in rotational equilibrium, but it will not be in translational equilibrium;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image109.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31775,75,441,'image',NULL,'720(b)',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image140.png'),
(31776,75,442,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.20 (b)','[{"s": "Fig. 7.20 (b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31777,75,443,'paragraph',NULL,'The force at B in Fig. 7.20(a) is reversed in Fig. 7.20(b). Thus, we have the same rod with two forces of equal magnitude but acting in opposite diretions applied perpendicular to the rod, one at end A and the other at end B. Here the moments of both the forces are equal, but they are not opposite; they act in the same sense and cause anticlockwise rotation of the rod. The total force on the body is zero; so the body is in translational equilibrium; but it is not in rotational equilibrium. Although the rod is not fixed in any way, it undergoes pure rotation (i.e. rotation without translation).','[{"s": "The force at B in Fig. 7.20(a) is reversed in Fig. 7.20(b). Thus, we have the same rod with two forces of equal magnitude but acting in opposite diretions applied perpendicular to the rod, one at end A and the other at end B. Here the moments of both the forces are equal, but they are not opposite; they act in the same sense and cause anticlockwise rotation of the rod. The total force on the body is zero; so the body is in translational equilibrium; but it is not in rotational equilibrium. Although the rod is not fixed in any way, it undergoes pure rotation (i.e. rotation without translation).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31778,75,444,'paragraph',NULL,'A pair of forces of equal magnitude but acting in opposite directions with different lines of action is known as a couple or torque . A couple produces rotation without translation.','[{"s": "A pair of forces of equal magnitude but acting in opposite directions with different lines of action is known as a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "couple", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "torque", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". A couple produces rotation without translation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31779,75,445,'paragraph',NULL,'When we open the lid of a bottle by turning it, our fingers are applying a couple to the lid [Fig. 7.21(a)]. Another known example is a compass needle in the earth’s magnetic field as shown in the Fig. 7.21(b). The earth’s magnetic field exerts equal forces on the north and south poles. The force on the North Pole is towards the north, and the force on the South Pole is toward the south. Except when the needle points in the north-south direction; the two forces do not have the same line of action. Thus there is a couple acting on the needle due to the earth’s magnetic field.','[{"s": "When we open the lid of a bottle by turning it, our fingers are applying a couple to the lid [Fig. 7.21(a)]. Another known example is a compass needle in the earth’s magnetic field as shown in the Fig. 7.21(b). The earth’s magnetic field exerts equal forces on the north and south poles. The force on the North Pole is towards the north, and the force on the South Pole is toward the south. Except when the needle points in the north-south direction; the two forces do not have the same line of action. Thus there is a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "couple", "t": "text"}, {"s": "acting on the needle due to the earth’s magnetic field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31780,75,446,'image',NULL,'721a',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image110.png'),
(31781,75,447,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.21 (a) Our fingers apply a couple to turn the lid.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Our fingers apply a couple to turn the lid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31782,75,448,'image',NULL,'721b',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image16.png'),
(31783,75,449,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.21 (b) The Earth’s magnetic field exerts equal and opposite forces on the poles of a compass needle. These two forces form a couple.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(b) The Earth’s magnetic field exerts equal and opposite forces on the poles of a compass needle. These two forces form a couple.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31784,75,450,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.7 Show that moment of a couple does not depend on the point about which you take the moments.','[{"s": "Example 7.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Show that moment of a couple does not depend on the point about which you take the moments.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31785,75,451,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31786,75,452,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image36.png'),
(31787,75,453,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.22','[{"s": "Fig. 7.22", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31788,75,454,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a couple as shown in Fig. 7.22 acting on a rigid body. The forces F and - F act respectively at points B and A. These points have position vectors r 1 and r 2 with respect to origin O. Let us take the moments of the forces about the origin.','[{"s": "Consider a couple as shown in Fig. 7.22 acting on a rigid body. The forces", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and -", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "act respectively at points B and A. These points have position vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with respect to origin O. Let us take the moments of the forces about the origin.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31789,75,455,'paragraph',NULL,'The moment of the couple = sum of the moments of the two forces making the couple','[{"s": "The moment of the couple = sum of the moments of the two forces making the couple", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31790,75,456,'paragraph',NULL,'= r 1 × (– F) + r 2 × F','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× (–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31791,75,457,'paragraph',NULL,'= r 2 × F – r 1 × F','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31792,75,458,'paragraph',NULL,'= ( r 2 – r 1 ) × F','[{"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31793,75,459,'paragraph',NULL,'But r 1 + AB = r 2 , and hence AB = r 2 – r 1 .','[{"s": "But", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and hence", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31794,75,460,'paragraph',NULL,'The moment of the couple, therefore, is AB × F.','[{"s": "The moment of the couple, therefore, is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31795,75,461,'paragraph',NULL,'Clearly this is independent of the origin, the point about which we took the moments of the forces.','[{"s": "Clearly this is independent of the origin, the point about which we took the moments of the forces.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31796,75,462,'heading',3,'7.8.1 PRINCIPLE OF MOMENTS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31797,75,463,'paragraph',NULL,'An ideal lever is essentially a light (i.e. of negligible mass) rod pivoted at a point along its length. This point is called the fulcrum. A see-saw on the children’s playground is a typical example of a lever. Two forces F 1 and F 2 , parallel to each other and usually perpendicular to the lever, as shown here, act on the lever at distances d 1 and d 2 respectively from the fulcrum as shown in Fig. 7.23.','[{"s": "An ideal lever is essentially a light (i.e. of negligible mass) rod pivoted at a point along its length. This point is called the fulcrum. A see-saw on the children’s playground is a typical example of a lever. Two forces F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", parallel to each other and usually perpendicular to the lever, as shown here, act on the lever at distances d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively from the fulcrum as shown in Fig. 7.23.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31798,75,464,'image',NULL,'723',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image112.png'),
(31799,75,465,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.23','[{"s": "Fig. 7.23", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31800,75,466,'paragraph',NULL,'The lever is a system in mechanical equilibrium. Let R be the reaction of the support at the fulcrum; R is directed opposite to the forces F 1 and F 2 . For translational equilibrium,','[{"s": "The lever is a system in mechanical equilibrium. Let", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the reaction of the support at the fulcrum;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is directed opposite to the forces F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For translational equilibrium,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31801,75,467,'paragraph',NULL,'R – F 1 – F 2 = 0 (i)','[{"s": "R – F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 (i)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31802,75,468,'paragraph',NULL,'For considering rotational equilibrium we take the moments about the fulcrum; the sum of moments must be zero,','[{"s": "For considering rotational equilibrium we take the moments about the fulcrum; the sum of moments must be zero,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31803,75,469,'paragraph',NULL,'d 1 F 1 – d 2 F 2 = 0 (ii)','[{"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 (ii)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31804,75,470,'paragraph',NULL,'Normally the anticlockwise (clockwise) moments are taken to be positive (negative). Note R acts at the fulcrum itself and has zero moment about the fulcrum.','[{"s": "Normally the anticlockwise (clockwise) moments are taken to be positive (negative). Note R acts at the fulcrum itself and has zero moment about the fulcrum.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31805,75,471,'paragraph',NULL,'In the case of the lever force F 1 is usually some weight to be lifted. It is called the load and its distance from the fulcrum d 1 is called the load arm. Force F 2 is the effort applied to lift the load; distance d 2 of the effort from the fulcrum is the effort arm .','[{"s": "In the case of the lever force F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is usually some weight to be lifted. It is called the load and its distance from the fulcrum d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called the load arm. Force F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the effort applied to lift the load; distance d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the effort from the fulcrum is the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "effort arm", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31806,75,472,'paragraph',NULL,'Eq. (ii) can be written as','[{"s": "Eq. (ii) can be written as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31807,75,473,'paragraph',NULL,'d 1 F 1 = d 2 F 2 (7.32a)','[{"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.32a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31808,75,474,'paragraph',NULL,'or load arm × load = effort arm × effort','[{"s": "or load arm × load = effort arm × effort", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31810,75,476,'paragraph',NULL,'M.A. = Systems_Of_Particles-image210.png (7.32b)','[{"s": "M.A. =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image210.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(7.32b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31811,75,477,'paragraph',NULL,'If the effort arm d 2 is larger than the load arm, the mechanical advantage is greater than one. Mechanical advantage greater than one means that a small effort can be used to lift a large load. There are several examples of a lever around you besides the see-saw. The beam of a balance is a lever. Try to find more such examples and identify the fulcrum, the effort and effort arm, and the load and the load arm of the lever in each case.','[{"s": "If the effort arm d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is larger than the load arm, the mechanical advantage is greater than one. Mechanical advantage greater than one means that a small effort can be used to lift a large load. There are several examples of a lever around you besides the see-saw. The beam of a balance is a lever. Try to find more such examples and identify the fulcrum, the effort and effort arm, and the load and the load arm of the lever in each case.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31812,75,478,'paragraph',NULL,'You may easily show that the principle of moment holds even when the parallel forces F 1 and F 2 are not perpendicular, but act at some angle, to the lever.','[{"s": "You may easily show that the principle of moment holds even when the parallel forces F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are not perpendicular, but act at some angle, to the lever.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31813,75,479,'heading',3,'7.8.2 CENTRE OF GRAVITY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31814,75,480,'paragraph',NULL,'Many of you may have the experience of balancing your notebook on the tip of a finger. Figure 7.24 illustrates a similar experiment that you can easily perform. Take an irregular-shaped cardboard having mass M and a narrow tipped object like a pencil. You can locate by trial and error a point G on the cardboard where it can be balanced on the tip of the pencil. (The cardboard remains horizontal in this position.) This point of balance is the centre of gravity (CG) of the cardboard. The tip of the pencil provides a vertically upward force due to which the cardboard is in mechanical equilibrium. As shown in the Fig. 7.24, the reaction of the tip is equal and opposite to M g and hence the cardboard is in translational equilibrium. It is also in rotational equilibrium; if it were not so, due to the unbalanced torque it would tilt and fall. There are torques on the card board due to the forces of gravity like m 1 g , m 2 g …. etc, acting on the individual particles that make up the cardboard.','[{"s": "Many of you may have the experience of balancing your notebook on the tip of a finger. Figure 7.24 illustrates a similar experiment that you can easily perform. Take an irregular-shaped cardboard having mass M and a narrow tipped object like a pencil. You can locate by trial and error a point G on the cardboard where it can be balanced on the tip of the pencil. (The cardboard remains horizontal in this position.) This point of balance is the centre of gravity (CG) of the cardboard. The tip of the pencil provides a vertically upward force due to which the cardboard is in mechanical equilibrium. As shown in the Fig. 7.24, the reaction of the tip is equal and opposite to M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and hence the cardboard is in translational equilibrium. It is also in rotational equilibrium; if it were not so, due to the unbalanced torque it would tilt and fall. There are torques on the card board due to the forces of gravity like m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "…. etc, acting on the individual particles that make up the cardboard.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31815,75,481,'image',NULL,'724',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image27.png'),
(31817,75,483,'paragraph',NULL,'The CG of the cardboard is so located that the total torque on it due to the forces m 1 g , m 2 g …. etc. is zero.','[{"s": "The CG of the cardboard is so located that the total torque on it due to the forces m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "…. etc. is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31818,75,484,'paragraph',NULL,'If r i is the position vector of the ith particle of an extended body with respect to its CG, then the torque about the CG, due to the force of gravity on the particle is τ i = r i × m i g . The total gravitational torque about the CG is zero, i.e.','[{"s": "If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the position vector of the ith particle of an extended body with respect to its CG, then the torque about the CG, due to the force of gravity on the particle is τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The total gravitational torque about the CG is zero, i.e.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31819,75,485,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image7.png'),
(31820,75,486,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.33)','[{"s": "(7.33)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(31821,75,487,'paragraph',NULL,'We may therefore, define the CG of a body as that point where the total gravitational torque on the body is zero.','[{"s": "We may therefore, define the CG of a body as that point where the total gravitational torque on the body is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2902,26,33,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.2 Bryophytes: A liverwort – Marchantia (a) Female thallus (b) Male thallus Mosses – (c) Funaria, gametophyte and sporophyte (d) Sphagnum gametophyte','[{"s": "Figure 3.2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Bryophytes: A liverwort – Marchantia (a) Female thallus (b) Male thallus Mosses – (c) Funaria, gametophyte and sporophyte (d) Sphagnum gametophyte", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["30339", "30340", "30341"]',NULL),
(31809,75,475,'paragraph',NULL,'The above equation expresses the principle of moments for a lever. Incidentally the ratio F 1 /F 2 is called the Mechanical Advantage (M.A.);','[{"s": "The above equation expresses the principle of moments for a lever.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Incidentally the ratio F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called the Mechanical Advantage (M.A.);", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(31822,75,488,'paragraph',NULL,'We notice that in Eq. (7.33), g is the same for all particles, and hence it comes out of the summation. This gives, since g is non-zero, Systems_Of_Particles-image33.png = 0 . Remember that the position vectors ( r i ) are taken with respect to the CG. Now, in accordance with the reasoning given below Eq. (7.4a) in Sec. 7.2, if the sum is zero, the origin must be the centre of mass of the body. Thus, the centre of gravity of the body coincides with the centre of mass in uniform gravity or gravity-free space. We note that this is true because the body being small, g does not vary from one point of the body to the other. If the body is so extended that g varies from part to part of the body, then the centre of gravity and centre of mass will not coincide. Basically, the two are different concepts. The centre of mass has nothing to do with gravity. It depends only on the distribution of mass of the body.','[{"s": "We notice that in Eq. (7.33),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the same for all particles, and hence it comes out of the summation. This gives, since", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is non-zero,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image33.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Remember that the position vectors (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") are taken with respect to the CG. Now, in accordance with the reasoning given below Eq. (7.4a) in Sec. 7.2, if the sum is zero, the origin must be the centre of mass of the body. Thus, the centre of gravity of the body coincides with the centre of mass in uniform gravity or gravity-free space. We note that this is true because the body being small,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "does not vary from one point of the body to the other. If the body is so extended that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "varies from part to part of the body, then the centre of gravity and centre of mass will not coincide. Basically, the two are different concepts. The centre of mass has nothing to do with gravity. It depends only on the distribution of mass of the body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31823,75,489,'image',NULL,'725',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image229.png'),
(31824,75,490,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.25 Determining the centre of gravity of a body of irregular shape. The centre of gravity G lies on the vertical AA 1 through the point of suspension of the body A.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.25", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Determining the centre of gravity of a body of irregular shape. The centre of gravity G lies on the vertical AA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "through the point of suspension of the body A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31825,75,491,'paragraph',NULL,'In Sec. 7.2 we found out the position of the centre of mass of several regular, homogeneous objects. Obviously the method used there gives us also the centre of gravity of these bodies, if they are small enough.','[{"s": "In Sec. 7.2 we found out the position of the centre of mass of several regular, homogeneous objects. Obviously the method used there gives us also the centre of gravity of these bodies, if they are small enough.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31826,75,492,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.25 illustrates another way of determining the CG of an irregular shaped body like a cardboard. If you suspend the body from some point like A, the vertical line through A passes through the CG. We mark the vertical AA 1 . We then suspend the body through other points like B and C. The intersection of the verticals gives the CG. Explain why the method works. Since the body is small enough, the method allows us to determine also its centre of mass.','[{"s": "Figure 7.25 illustrates another way of determining the CG of an irregular shaped body like a cardboard. If you suspend the body from some point like A, the vertical line through A passes through the CG. We mark the vertical AA", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We then suspend the body through other points like B and C. The intersection of the verticals gives the CG. Explain why the method works. Since the body is small enough, the method allows us to determine also its centre of mass.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31828,75,494,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31829,75,495,'image',NULL,'726',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image76.png'),
(31830,75,496,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.26','[{"s": "Fig. 7.26", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31831,75,497,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 7.26 shows the rod AB, the positions of the knife edges K 1 and K 2 , the centre of gravity of the rod at G and the suspended load at P.','[{"s": "Figure 7.26 shows the rod AB, the positions of the knife edges K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the centre of gravity of the rod at G and the suspended load at P.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31832,75,498,'paragraph',NULL,'Note the weight of the rod W acts at its centre of gravity G. The rod is uniform in cross section and homogeneous; hence G is at the centre of the rod; AB = 70 cm. AG = 35 cm, AP = 30 cm, PG = 5 cm, AK 1 = BK 2 = 10 cm and K 1 G = K 2 G = 25 cm. Also, W= weight of the rod = 4.00 kg and W 1 1 and R 2 are the normal reactions of the support at the knife edges.','[{"s": "Note the weight of the rod W acts at its centre of gravity G. The rod is uniform in cross section and homogeneous; hence G is at the centre of the rod; AB = 70 cm. AG = 35 cm, AP = 30 cm, PG = 5 cm, AK", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= BK", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 cm and K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G = K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G = 25 cm. Also, W= weight of the rod = 4.00 kg and W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the normal reactions of the support at the knife edges.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31833,75,499,'paragraph',NULL,'For translational equilibrium of the rod,','[{"s": "For translational equilibrium of the rod,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31834,75,500,'paragraph',NULL,'R 1 +R 2 –W 1 –W = 0 (i)','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–W = 0 (i)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31835,75,501,'paragraph',NULL,'Note W 1 and W act vertically down and R 1 and R 2 act vertically up.','[{"s": "Note W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and W act vertically down and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "act vertically up.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31836,75,502,'paragraph',NULL,'For considering rotational equilibrium, we take moments of the forces. A convenient point to take moments about is G. The moments of R 2 and W 1 are anticlockwise (+ve), whereas the moment of R 1 is clockwise (-ve).','[{"s": "For considering rotational equilibrium, we take moments of the forces. A convenient point to take moments about is G. The moments of R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are anticlockwise (+ve), whereas the moment of R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is clockwise (-ve).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31837,75,503,'paragraph',NULL,'For rotational equilibrium,','[{"s": "For rotational equilibrium,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31838,75,504,'paragraph',NULL,'–R 1 (K 1 G) + W 1 (PG) + R 2 (K 2 G) = 0 (ii)','[{"s": "–R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G) + W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(PG) + R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "G) = 0 (ii)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31839,75,505,'paragraph',NULL,'It is given that W = 4.00g N and W 1 = 6.00g N, where g = acceleration due to gravity. We take g = 9.8 m/s 2 .','[{"s": "It is given that W = 4.00g N and W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 6.00g N, where g = acceleration due to gravity. We take g = 9.8 m/s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31840,75,506,'paragraph',NULL,'With numerical values inserted, from (i)','[{"s": "With numerical values inserted, from (i)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31841,75,507,'paragraph',NULL,'R 1 + R 2 – 4.00g – 6.00g = 0','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– 4.00g – 6.00g = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31842,75,508,'paragraph',NULL,'or R 1 + R 2 = 10.00g N (iii)','[{"s": "or R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10.00g N (iii)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31843,75,509,'paragraph',NULL,'= 98.00 N','[{"s": "= 98.00 N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
