(31868,75,534,'heading',2,'7.9 MOMENT OF INERTIA',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31869,75,535,'paragraph',NULL,'We have already mentioned that we are developing the study of rotational motion parallel to the study of translational motion with which we are familiar. We have yet to answer one major question in this connection. What is the analogue of mass in rotational motion? We shall attempt to answer this question in the present section. To keep the discussion simple, we shall consider rotation about a fixed axis only. Let us try to get an expression for the kinetic energy of a rotating body . We know that for a body rotating about a fixed axis, each particle of the body moves in a circle with linear velocity given by Eq. (7.19). (Refer to Fig. 7.16). For a particle at a distance from the axis, the linear velocity is Systems_Of_Particles-image22.png . The kinetic energy of motion of this particle is','[{"s": "We have already mentioned that we are developing the study of rotational motion parallel to the study of translational motion with which we are familiar. We have yet to answer one major question in this connection.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What is the analogue of mass in rotational motion?", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We shall attempt to answer this question in the present section. To keep the discussion simple, we shall consider rotation about a fixed axis only. Let us try to get an expression for the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kinetic energy of a rotating body", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We know that for a body rotating about a fixed axis, each particle of the body moves in a circle with linear velocity given by Eq. (7.19). (Refer to Fig. 7.16). For a particle at a distance from the axis, the linear velocity is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image22.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". The kinetic energy of motion of this particle is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31870,75,536,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image219.png'),
(31871,75,537,'paragraph',NULL,'where m i is the mass of the particle. The total kinetic energy K of the body is then given by the sum of the kinetic energies of individual particles,','[{"s": "where m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the mass of the particle. The total kinetic energy K of the body is then given by the sum of the kinetic energies of individual particles,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31872,75,538,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image283.png'),
(31873,75,539,'paragraph',NULL,'Here n is the number of particles in the body. Note ω is the same for all particles. Hence, taking ω out of the sum,','[{"s": "Here n is the number of particles in the body. Note ω is the same for all particles. Hence, taking ω out of the sum,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31874,75,540,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image71.png'),
