(31882,75,548,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image264.png'),
(31883,75,549,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, m is the mass of the body and v is its velocity. We have already noted the analogy between angular velocity ω (in respect of rotational motion about a fixed axis) and linear velocity v (in respect of linear motion). It is then evident that the parameter, moment of inertia I, is the desired rotational analogue of mass in linear motion. In rotation (about a fixed axis), the moment of inertia plays a similar role as mass does in linear motion.','[{"s": "Here, m is the mass of the body and v is its velocity. We have already noted the analogy between angular velocity ω (in respect of rotational motion about a fixed axis) and linear velocity v (in respect of linear motion). It is then evident that the parameter, moment of inertia I, is the desired rotational analogue of mass in linear motion. In rotation (about a fixed axis), the moment of inertia plays a similar role as mass does in linear motion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31884,75,550,'paragraph',NULL,'We now apply the definition Eq. (7.34), to calculate the moment of inertia in two simple cases.','[{"s": "We now apply the definition Eq. (7.34), to calculate the moment of inertia in two simple cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31885,75,551,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Consider a thin ring of radius R and mass M, rotating in its own plane around its centre with angular velocity ω. Each mass element of the ring is at a distance R from the axis, and moves with a speed Rω. The kinetic energy is therefore,','[{"s": "(a) Consider a thin ring of radius R and mass M, rotating in its own plane around its centre with angular velocity ω. Each mass element of the ring is at a distance R from the axis, and moves with a speed Rω. The kinetic energy is therefore,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31886,75,552,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image160.png'),
(31887,75,553,'paragraph',NULL,'Comparing with Eq. (7.35) we get I = MR 2 for the ring.','[{"s": "Comparing with Eq. (7.35) we get I = MR", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for the ring.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31888,75,554,'image',NULL,'728',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image188.png'),
(31890,75,556,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Next, take a rigid rod of negligible mass of length of length l with a pair of small masses, rotating about an axis through the centre of mass perpendicular to the rod (Fig. 7.28). Each mass M/2 is at a distance l/2 from the axis. The moment of inertia of the masses is therefore given by','[{"s": "(b) Next, take a rigid rod of negligible mass of length of length l with a pair of small masses, rotating about an axis through the centre of mass perpendicular to the rod (Fig. 7.28). Each mass M/2 is at a distance l/2 from the axis. The moment of inertia of the masses is therefore given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31891,75,557,'paragraph',NULL,'(M/2) (l/2) 2 + (M/2)(l/2) 2','[{"s": "(M/2) (l/2)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ (M/2)(l/2)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31892,75,558,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, for the pair of masses, rotating about the axis through the centre of mass perpendicular to the rod','[{"s": "Thus, for the pair of masses, rotating about the axis through the centre of mass perpendicular to the rod", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31893,75,559,'paragraph',NULL,'I = Ml 2 / 4','[{"s": "I = Ml", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ 4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31894,75,560,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 7.1 simply gives the moment of inertia of various familiar regular shaped bodies about specific axes. (The derivations of these expressions are beyond the scope of this textbook and you will study them in higher classes.)','[{"s": "Table 7.1 simply gives the moment of inertia of various familiar regular shaped bodies about specific axes. (The derivations of these expressions are beyond the scope of this textbook and you will study them in higher classes.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31897,75,563,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the moment of inertia of a rigid body depends on the mass of the body, its shape and size; distribution of mass about the axis of rotation, and the position and orientation of the axis of rotation.','[{"s": "Thus, the moment of inertia of a rigid body depends on the mass of the body, its shape and size; distribution of mass about the axis of rotation, and the position and orientation of the axis of rotation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31898,75,564,'paragraph',NULL,'From the definition, Eq. (7.34), we can infer that the dimensions of moments of inertia are ML 2 and its SI units are kg m 2 .','[{"s": "From the definition, Eq. (7.34), we can infer that the dimensions of moments of inertia are ML", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and its SI units are kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31889,75,555,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.28 A light rod of length l with a pair of masses rotating about an axis through the centre of mass of the system and perpendicular to the rod. 3 The total mass of the system is M.','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7.28", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A light rod of length l with a pair of masses rotating about an axis through the centre of mass of the system and perpendicular to the rod.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The total mass of the system is M.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(31899,75,565,'paragraph',NULL,'The property of this extremely important quantity I, as a measure of rotational inertia of the body, has been put to a great practical use. The machines, such as steam engine and the automobile engine, etc., that produce rotational motion have a disc with a large moment of inertia, called a flywheel . Because of its large moment of inertia, the flywheel resists the sudden increase or decrease of the speed of the vehicle. It allows a gradual change in the speed and prevents jerky motions, thereby ensuring a smooth ride for the passengers on the vehicle.','[{"s": "The property of this extremely important quantity I, as a measure of rotational inertia of the body, has been put to a great practical use. The machines, such as steam engine and the automobile engine, etc., that produce rotational motion have a disc with a large moment of inertia, called a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "flywheel", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Because of its large moment of inertia, the flywheel resists the sudden increase or decrease of the speed of the vehicle. It allows a gradual change in the speed and prevents jerky motions, thereby ensuring a smooth ride for the passengers on the vehicle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31900,75,566,'heading',2,'7.10 THEOREMS OF PERPENDICULAR AND PARALLEL AXES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31901,75,567,'paragraph',NULL,'These are two useful theorems relating to moment of inertia. We shall first discuss the theorem of perpendicular axes and its simple yet instructive application in working out the moments of inertia of some regular-shaped bodies.','[{"s": "These are two useful theorems relating to moment of inertia. We shall first discuss the theorem of perpendicular axes and its simple yet instructive application in working out the moments of inertia of some regular-shaped bodies.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31902,75,568,'heading',5,'Table 7.1 Moments of inertia of some regular shaped bodies about specific axes3',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31903,75,569,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 7.43.47 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image256.png'),
(31904,75,570,'heading',4,'Theorem of perpendicular axes',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31905,75,571,'paragraph',NULL,'This theorem is applicable to bodies which are planar. In practice this means the theorem applies to flat bodies whose thickness is very small compared to their other dimensions (e.g. length, breadth or radius). Fig. 7.29 illustrates the theorem. It states that the moment of inertia of a planar body (lamina) about an axis perpendicular to its plane is equal to the sum of its moments of inertia about two perpendicular axes concurrent with perpendicular axis and lying in the plane of the body.','[{"s": "This theorem is applicable to bodies which are planar. In practice this means the theorem applies to flat bodies whose thickness is very small compared to their other dimensions (e.g. length, breadth or radius). Fig. 7.29 illustrates the theorem. It states that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the moment of inertia of a planar body (lamina) about an axis perpendicular to its plane is equal to the sum of its moments of inertia about two perpendicular axes concurrent with perpendicular axis and lying in the plane of the body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31906,75,572,'image',NULL,'729',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image104.png'),
(31907,75,573,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.29 Theorem of perpendicular axes applicable to a planar body; x and y axes are two perpendicular axes in the plane and the z-axis is perpendicular to the plane.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.29", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Theorem of perpendicular axes applicable to a planar body; x and y axes are two perpendicular axes in the plane and the z-axis is perpendicular to the plane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31908,75,574,'paragraph',NULL,'The figure shows a planar body. An axis perpendicular to the body through a point O is taken as the z-axis. Two mutually perpendicular axes lying in the plane of the body and concurrent with z-axis, i.e., passing through O, are taken as the x and y-axes. The theorem states that','[{"s": "The figure shows a planar body. An axis perpendicular to the body through a point O is taken as the z-axis. Two mutually perpendicular axes lying in the plane of the body and concurrent with z-axis, i.e., passing through O, are taken as the x and y-axes. The theorem states that", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31909,75,575,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image127.png'),
(31910,75,576,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.36)','[{"s": "(7.36)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31911,75,577,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us look at the usefulness of the theorem through an example.','[{"s": "Let us look at the usefulness of the theorem through an example.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31913,75,579,'image',NULL,'730',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image83.png'),
(31914,75,580,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.30 Moment of inertia of a disc about a diameter, given its moment of inertia about the perpendicular axis through its centre.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Moment of inertia of a disc about a diameter, given its moment of inertia about the perpendicular axis through its centre.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31915,75,581,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer We assume the moment of inertia of the disc about an axis perpendicular to it and through its centre to be known; it is MR 2 /2, where M is the mass of the disc and R is its radius (Table 7.1)','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We assume the moment of inertia of the disc about an axis perpendicular to it and through its centre to be known; it is MR", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2, where M is the mass of the disc and R is its radius (Table 7.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31916,75,582,'paragraph',NULL,'The disc can be considered to be a planar body. Hence the theorem of perpendicular axes is applicable to it. As shown in Fig. 7.30, we take three concurrent axes through the centre of the disc, O, as the x–, y– and z–axes; x– and y–axes lie in the plane of the disc and z–axis is perpendicular to it. By the theorem of perpendicular axes,','[{"s": "The disc can be considered to be a planar body. Hence the theorem of perpendicular axes is applicable to it. As shown in Fig. 7.30, we take three concurrent axes through the centre of the disc, O, as the x–, y– and z–axes; x– and y–axes lie in the plane of the disc and z–axis is perpendicular to it. By the theorem of perpendicular axes,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31917,75,583,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image127.png'),
(31918,75,584,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, x and y axes are along two diameters of the disc, and by symmetry the moment of inertia of the disc is the same about any diameter. Hence','[{"s": "Now, x and y axes are along two diameters of the disc, and by symmetry the moment of inertia of the disc is the same about any diameter. Hence", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31919,75,585,'paragraph',NULL,'I x = I y','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31920,75,586,'paragraph',NULL,'and I z = 2I x','[{"s": "and I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31921,75,587,'paragraph',NULL,'But I z = MR 2 /2','[{"s": "But I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= MR", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31922,75,588,'paragraph',NULL,'So finally, I x = I z /2 = MR 2 /4','[{"s": "So finally, I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2 = MR", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31924,75,590,'paragraph',NULL,'Find similarly the moment of inertia of a ring about any of its diameters. Will the theorem be applicable to a solid cylinder?','[{"s": "Find similarly the moment of inertia of a ring about any of its diameters. Will the theorem be applicable to a solid cylinder?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
