(32135,75,801,'heading',3,'7.13.1 CONSERVATION OF ANGULAR MOMENTUM',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32136,75,802,'paragraph',NULL,'We are now in a position to revisit the principle of conservation of angular momentum in the context of rotation about a fixed axis. From Eq. (7.45c), if the external torque is zero,','[{"s": "We are now in a position to revisit the principle of conservation of angular momentum in the context of rotation about a fixed axis. From Eq. (7.45c), if the external torque is zero,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32137,75,803,'paragraph',NULL,'L z = Iω = constant (7.46)','[{"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= Iω = constant (7.46)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32138,75,804,'paragraph',NULL,'For symmetric bodies, from Eq. (7.44d), L z may be replaced by L .(L and L z are respectively the magnitudes of L and L z .)','[{"s": "For symmetric bodies, from Eq. (7.44d), L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "may be replaced by L .(L and L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are respectively the magnitudes of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(29797,72,405,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.21 shows the dependence of the displacement amplitude of an oscillator on the angular frequency of the driving force for different amounts of damping present in the system. It may be noted that in all cases the amplitude is the greatest when ω d /ω = 1. The curves in this figure show that smaller the damping, the taller and narrower is the resonance peak.','[{"s": "Fig. 14.21 shows the dependence of the displacement amplitude of an oscillator on the angular frequency of the driving force for different amounts of damping present in the system. It may be noted that in all cases the amplitude is the greatest when ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/ω = 1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The curves in this figure show that smaller the damping, the taller and narrower is the resonance peak.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(32140,75,806,'image',NULL,'736a',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image239.png'),
(32141,75,807,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 7.36 (a) A demonstration of conservation of angular momentum. A girl sits on a swivel chair and stretches her arms/brings her arms closer to the body.','[{"s": "Fig 7.36", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) A demonstration of conservation of angular momentum. A girl sits on a swivel chair and stretches her arms/brings her arms closer to the body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32142,75,808,'image',NULL,'736b',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image115.png'),
(32143,75,809,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig 7.36 (b) An acrobat employing the principle of conservation of angular momentum in her performance.','[{"s": "Fig 7.36", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(b) An acrobat employing the principle of conservation of angular momentum in her performance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32144,75,810,'paragraph',NULL,'A circus acrobat and a diver take advantage of this principle. Also, skaters and classical, Indian or western, dancers performing a pirouette (a spinning about a tip–top) on the toes of one foot display ‘mastery’ over this principle. Can you explain?','[{"s": "A circus acrobat and a diver take advantage of this principle. Also, skaters and classical, Indian or western, dancers performing a pirouette (a spinning about a tip–top) on the toes of one foot display ‘mastery’ over this principle. Can you explain?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32145,75,811,'heading',2,'7.14 ROLLING MOTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32146,75,812,'paragraph',NULL,'One of the most common motions observed in daily life is the rolling motion. All wheels used in transportation have rolling motion. For specificness we shall begin with the case of a disc, but the result will apply to any rolling body rolling on a level surface. We shall assume that the disc rolls without slipping. This means that at any instant of time the bottom of the disc which is in contact with the surface is at rest on the surface.','[{"s": "One of the most common motions observed in daily life is the rolling motion. All wheels used in transportation have rolling motion. For specificness we shall begin with the case of a disc, but the result will apply to any rolling body rolling on a level surface. We shall assume that the disc rolls without slipping. This means that at any instant of time the bottom of the disc which is in contact with the surface is at rest on the surface.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32147,75,813,'paragraph',NULL,'We have remarked earlier that rolling motion is a combination of rotation and translation. We know that the translational motion of a system of particles is the motion of its centre of mass.','[{"s": "We have remarked earlier that rolling motion is a combination of rotation and translation. We know that the translational motion of a system of particles is the motion of its centre of mass.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32148,75,814,'image',NULL,'737',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image159.png'),
(32149,75,815,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 7.37 The rolling motion (without slipping) of a disc on a level surface. Note at any instant, the point of contact P 0 of the disc with the surface is at rest; the centre of mass of the disc moves with velocity, v cm . The disc rotates with angular velocity ω about its axis which passes through C; v cm =Rω, where R is the radius of the disc.','[{"s": "Fig. 7.37", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The rolling motion (without slipping) of a disc on a level surface. Note at any instant, the point of contact P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the disc with the surface is at rest; the centre of mass of the disc moves with velocity, v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The disc rotates with angular velocity ω about its axis which passes through C; v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=Rω, where R is the radius of the disc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32150,75,816,'paragraph',NULL,'Let v cm be the velocity of the centre of mass and therefore the translational velocity of the disc. Since the centre of mass of the rolling disc is at its geometric centre C (Fig. 7. 37), v cm is the velocity of C. It is parallel to the level surface. The rotational motion of the disc is about its symmetry axis, which passes through C. Thus, the velocity of any point of the disc, like P 0 , P 1 or P 2 , consists of two parts, one is the translational velocity v cm and the other is the linear velocity v r on account of rotation. The magnitude of v r is v r = rω, where ω is the angular velocity of the rotation of the disc about the axis and r is the distance of the point from the axis (i.e. from C). The velocity v r is directed perpendicular to the radius vector of the given point with respect to C. In Fig. 7.37, the velocity of the point P 2 ( v 2 ) and its components v r and v cm are shown; v r here is perpendicular to CP 2 . It is easy to show that v z is perpendicular to the line P O P 2 . Therefore the line passing through P O and parallel to ω is called the instantaneous axis of rotation.','[{"s": "Let", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the velocity of the centre of mass and therefore the translational velocity of the disc. Since the centre of mass of the rolling disc is at its geometric centre C (Fig. 7. 37),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the velocity of C. It is parallel to the level surface. The rotational motion of the disc is about its symmetry axis, which passes through C. Thus, the velocity of any point of the disc, like P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", consists of two parts, one is the translational velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the other is the linear velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on account of rotation. The magnitude of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= rω, where ω is the angular velocity of the rotation of the disc about the axis and r is the distance of the point from the axis (i.e. from C). The velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is directed perpendicular to the radius vector of the given point with respect to C. In Fig. 7.37, the velocity of the point P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and its components", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are shown;", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "here is perpendicular to CP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is easy to show that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is perpendicular to the line P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Therefore the line passing through P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and parallel to ω is called the instantaneous axis of rotation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32152,75,818,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image190.png'),
(32153,75,819,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.47)','[{"s": "(7.47)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32154,75,820,'paragraph',NULL,'Incidentally, this means that the velocity of point P 1 at the top of the disc ( v 1 ) has a magnitude v cm + Rω or 2 v cm and is directed parallel to the level surface. The condition (7.47) applies to all rolling bodies.','[{"s": "Incidentally, this means that the velocity of point P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at the top of the disc (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") has a magnitude v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ Rω or 2 v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and is directed parallel to the level surface. The condition (7.47) applies to all rolling bodies.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32155,75,821,'heading',3,'7.14.1 KINETIC ENERGY OF ROLLING MOTION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32156,75,822,'paragraph',NULL,'Our next task will be to obtain an expression for the kinetic energy of a rolling body. The kinetic energy of a rolling body can be separated into kinetic energy of translation and kinetic energy of rotation. This is a special case of a general result for a system of particles, according to which the kinetic energy of a system of particles (K) can be separated into the kinetic energy of translational motion of the centre of mass (MV 2 /2) and kinetic energy of rotational motion about the centre of mass of the system of particles (K′). Thus,','[{"s": "Our next task will be to obtain an expression for the kinetic energy of a rolling body. The kinetic energy of a rolling body can be separated into kinetic energy of translation and kinetic energy of rotation. This is a special case of a general result for a system of particles, according to which the kinetic energy of a system of particles (K) can be separated into the kinetic energy of translational motion of the centre of mass (MV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2) and kinetic energy of rotational motion about the centre of mass of the system of particles (K′). Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32157,75,823,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image245.png'),
(32158,75,824,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.48)','[{"s": "(7.48)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32160,75,826,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image304.png'),
(32161,75,827,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.49a)','[{"s": "(7.49a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32162,75,828,'paragraph',NULL,'Substituting I = mk 2 where k = the corresponding radius of gyration of the body and v cm = R ω, we get','[{"s": "Substituting I = mk", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where k = the corresponding radius of gyration of the body and v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= R ω, we get", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32163,75,829,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image276.png'),
(32164,75,830,'paragraph',NULL,'or Systems_Of_Particles-image228.png','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image228.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32165,75,831,'paragraph',NULL,'(7.49b)','[{"s": "(7.49b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32168,75,834,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer We assume conservation of energy of the rolling body, i.e. there is no loss of energy due to friction etc. The potential energy lost by the body in rolling down the inclined plane (= mgh) must, therefore, be equal to kinetic energy gained. (See Fig.7.38) Since the bodies start from rest the kinetic energy gained is equal to the final kinetic energy of the bodies. From Eq. (7.49b), Systems_Of_Particles-image150.png , where v is the final velocity of (the centre of mass of) the body. Equating K and mgh,','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We assume conservation of energy of the rolling body, i.e. there is no loss of energy due to friction etc. The potential energy lost by the body in rolling down the inclined plane (= mgh) must, therefore, be equal to kinetic energy gained. (See Fig.7.38) Since the bodies start from rest the kinetic energy gained is equal to the final kinetic energy of the bodies. From Eq. (7.49b),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image150.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", where v is the final velocity of (the centre of mass of) the body. Equating K and mgh,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32169,75,835,'image',NULL,'738',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image311.png'),
(32170,75,836,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig.7.38','[{"s": "Fig.7.38", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32171,75,837,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image252.png'),
(32172,75,838,'paragraph',NULL,'or Systems_Of_Particles-image230.png','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image230.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32173,75,839,'paragraph',NULL,'Note is independent of the mass of the rolling body;','[{"s": "Note is independent of the mass of the rolling body;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32174,75,840,'paragraph',NULL,'For a ring, k 2 = R 2','[{"s": "For a ring, k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32175,75,841,'paragraph',NULL,'Systems_Of_Particles-image295.png ,','[{"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image295.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32176,75,842,'paragraph',NULL,'= Systems_Of_Particles-image88.png','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image88.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32177,75,843,'paragraph',NULL,'For a solid cylinder k 2 = R 2 /2','[{"s": "For a solid cylinder k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32178,75,844,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image234.png'),
(32179,75,845,'paragraph',NULL,'= Systems_Of_Particles-image254.png','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image254.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32180,75,846,'paragraph',NULL,'For a solid sphere k 2 = 2R 2 /5','[{"s": "For a solid sphere k", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32181,75,847,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image82.png'),
(32182,75,848,'paragraph',NULL,'= Systems_Of_Particles-image206.png','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image206.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32183,75,849,'paragraph',NULL,'From the results obtained it is clear that among the three bodies the sphere has the greatest and the ring has the least velocity of the centre of mass at the bottom of the inclined plane.','[{"s": "From the results obtained it is clear that among the three bodies the sphere has the greatest and the ring has the least velocity of the centre of mass at the bottom of the inclined plane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32184,75,850,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose the bodies have the same mass. Which body has the greatest rotational kinetic energy while reaching the bottom of the inclined plane?','[{"s": "Suppose the bodies have the same mass. Which body has the greatest rotational kinetic energy while reaching the bottom of the inclined plane?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32185,75,851,'heading',2,'SUMMARY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32186,75,852,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Ideally, a rigid body is one for which the distances between different particles of the body do not change, even though there are forces on them.','[{"s": "1. Ideally, a rigid body is one for which the distances between different particles of the body do not change, even though there are forces on them.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32187,75,853,'paragraph',NULL,'2. A rigid body fixed at one point or along a line can have only rotational motion. A rigid body not fixed in some way can have either pure translational motion or a combination of translational and rotational motions.','[{"s": "2. A rigid body fixed at one point or along a line can have only rotational motion. A rigid body not fixed in some way can have either pure translational motion or a combination of translational and rotational motions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32188,75,854,'paragraph',NULL,'3. In rotation about a fixed axis, every particle of the rigid body moves in a circle which lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis and has its centre on the axis. Every Point in the rotating rigid body has the same angular velocity at any instant of time.','[{"s": "3. In rotation about a fixed axis, every particle of the rigid body moves in a circle which lies in a plane perpendicular to the axis and has its centre on the axis. Every Point in the rotating rigid body has the same angular velocity at any instant of time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32189,75,855,'paragraph',NULL,'4. In pure translation, every particle of the body moves with the same velocity at any instant of time.','[{"s": "4. In pure translation, every particle of the body moves with the same velocity at any instant of time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32190,75,856,'paragraph',NULL,'5. Angular velocity is a vector. Its magnitude is ω = dθ/dt and it is directed along the axis of rotation. For rotation about a fixed axis, this vector ω has a fixed direction.','[{"s": "5. Angular velocity is a vector. Its magnitude is ω = dθ/dt and it is directed along the axis of rotation. For rotation about a fixed axis, this vector ω has a fixed direction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32191,75,857,'paragraph',NULL,'6. The vector or cross product of two vector a and b is a vector written as a × b','[{"s": "6. The vector or cross product of two vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a vector written as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32192,75,858,'paragraph',NULL,'v = ω × r , where r is the position vector of the particle with respect to an origin along the fixed axis. The relation applies even to more general rotation of a rigid body with one point fixed. In that case r is the position vector of the particle with respect to the fixed point taken as the origin.','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the position vector of the particle with respect to an origin along the fixed axis. The relation applies even to more general rotation of a rigid body with one point fixed. In that case", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the position vector of the particle with respect to the fixed point taken as the origin.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32193,75,859,'paragraph',NULL,'8. The centre of mass of a system of n particles is defined as the point whose position vector is','[{"s": "8. The centre of mass of a system of n particles is defined as the point whose position vector is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32194,75,860,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image279.png'),
(32195,75,861,'paragraph',NULL,'9. Velocity of the centre of mass of a system of particles is given by V = P /M, where P is the linear momentum of the system. The centre of mass moves as if all the mass of the system is concentrated at this point and all the external forces act at it. If the total external force on the system is zero, then the total linear momentum of the system is constant.','[{"s": "9. Velocity of the centre of mass of a system of particles is given by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/M, where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the linear momentum of the system. The centre of mass moves as if all the mass of the system is concentrated at this point and all the external forces act at it. If the total external force on the system is zero, then the total linear momentum of the system is constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32196,75,862,'paragraph',NULL,'10. The angular momentum of a system of n particles about the origin is','[{"s": "10. The angular momentum of a system of n particles about the origin is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32197,75,863,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image111.png'),
(32198,75,864,'paragraph',NULL,'The torque or moment of force on a system of n particles about the origin is','[{"s": "The torque or moment of force on a system of n particles about the origin is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32199,75,865,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image31.png'),
(32200,75,866,'paragraph',NULL,'The force F i acting on the i th particle includes the external as well as internal forces.','[{"s": "The force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "acting on the i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "th", "t": "text"}, {"s": "particle includes the external as well as internal forces.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32201,75,867,'paragraph',NULL,'Assuming Newton’s third law of motion and that forces between any two particles act along the line joining the particles, we can show τ int = 0 and','[{"s": "Assuming Newton’s third law of motion and that forces between any two particles act along the line joining the particles, we can show τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "int", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32202,75,868,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 6.39.20 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image132.png'),
(32203,75,869,'paragraph',NULL,'11. A rigid body is in mechanical equilibrium if','[{"s": "11. A rigid body is in mechanical equilibrium if", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32204,75,870,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) it is in translational equilibrium, i.e., the total external force on it is zero : Systems_Of_Particles-image313.png , and','[{"s": "(1) it is in translational equilibrium, i.e., the total external force on it is zero :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image313.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", and", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32205,75,871,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) it is in rotational equilibrium, i.e. the total external torque on it is zero :','[{"s": "(2) it is in rotational equilibrium, i.e. the total external torque on it is zero :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32206,75,872,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 6.40.14 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image297.png'),
(32207,75,873,'paragraph',NULL,'12. The centre of gravity of an extended body is that point where the total gravitational torque on the body is zero.','[{"s": "12. The centre of gravity of an extended body is that point where the total gravitational torque on the body is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32208,75,874,'paragraph',NULL,'13. The moment of intertia of a rigid body about an axis is defined by the formula Systems_Of_Particles-image70.png where r i is the perpendicular distance of the ith point of the body from the axis. The kinetic energy of rotation is Systems_Of_Particles-image96.png .','[{"s": "13. The moment of intertia of a rigid body about an axis is defined by the formula", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image70.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "where r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the perpendicular distance of the ith point of the body from the axis. The kinetic energy of rotation is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image96.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32209,75,875,'paragraph',NULL,'14. The theorem of parallel axes: Systems_Of_Particles-image174.png , allows us to determine the moment of intertia of a rigid body about an axis as the sum of the moment of inertia of the body about a parallel axis through its centre of mass and the product of mass and square of the perpendicular distance between these two axes.','[{"s": "14. The theorem of parallel axes:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image174.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", allows us to determine the moment of intertia of a rigid body about an axis as the sum of the moment of inertia of the body about a parallel axis through its centre of mass and the product of mass and square of the perpendicular distance between these two axes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32210,75,876,'paragraph',NULL,'15. Rotation about a fixed axis is directly analogous to linear motion in respect of kinematics and dynamics.','[{"s": "15. Rotation about a fixed axis is directly analogous to linear motion in respect of kinematics and dynamics.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32211,75,877,'paragraph',NULL,'16. For a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis (say, z-axis) of rotation, L z = Iω, where I is the moment of inertia about z-axis. In general, the angular momentum L for such a body is not along the axis of rotation. Only if the body is symmetric about the axis of rotation, L is along the axis of rotation. In that case, Systems_Of_Particles-image232.png . The angular acceleration of a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis is given by Iα = τ. If the external torque τ acting on the body is zero, the component of angular momentum about the fixed axis (say, z-axis), L z (=Iω) of such a rotating body is constant.','[{"s": "16. For a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis (say, z-axis) of rotation, L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= Iω, where I is the moment of inertia about z-axis. In general, the angular momentum", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for such a body is not along the axis of rotation. Only if the body is symmetric about the axis of rotation,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is along the axis of rotation. In that case,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image232.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". The angular acceleration of a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis is given by Iα = τ. If the external torque τ acting on the body is zero, the component of angular momentum about the fixed axis (say, z-axis), L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(=Iω) of such a rotating body is constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32212,75,878,'paragraph',NULL,'17. For rolling motion without slipping v cm = Rω, where v cm is the velocity of translation (i.e. of the centre of mass), R is the radius and m is the mass of the body. The kinetic energy of such a rolling body is the sum of kinetic energies of translation and rotation: Systems_Of_Particles-image5.png .','[{"s": "17. For rolling motion without slipping v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= Rω, where v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the velocity of translation (i.e. of the centre of mass), R is the radius and m is the mass of the body. The kinetic energy of such a rolling body is the sum of kinetic energies of translation and rotation:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image5.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32213,75,879,'image',NULL,'Summary_table',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Systems_Of_Particles-image251.png'),
(32214,75,880,'heading',3,'POINTS TO PONDER',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32215,75,881,'paragraph',NULL,'1. To determine the motion of the centre of mass of a system no knowledge of internal forces of the system is required. For this purpose we need to know only the external forces on the body.','[{"s": "1. To determine the motion of the centre of mass of a system no knowledge of internal forces of the system is required. For this purpose we need to know only the external forces on the body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32216,75,882,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Separating the motion of a system of particles as the motion of the centre of mass, (i.e., the translational motion of the system) and motion about (i.e. relative to) the centre of mass of the system is a useful technique in dynamics of a system of particles. One example of this technique is separating the kinetic energy of a system of particles K as the kinetic energy of the system about its centre of mass K′ and the kinetic energy of the centre of mass MV 2 /2,','[{"s": "2. Separating the motion of a system of particles as the motion of the centre of mass, (i.e., the translational motion of the system) and motion about (i.e. relative to) the centre of mass of the system is a useful technique in dynamics of a system of particles. One example of this technique is separating the kinetic energy of a system of particles K as the kinetic energy of the system about its centre of mass K′ and the kinetic energy of the centre of mass MV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32217,75,883,'paragraph',NULL,'K = K′ + MV 2 /2','[{"s": "K = K′ + MV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32218,75,884,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Newton’s Second Law for finite sized bodies (or systems of particles) is based in Newton’s Second Law and also Newton’s Third Law for particles.','[{"s": "3. Newton’s Second Law for finite sized bodies (or systems of particles) is based in Newton’s Second Law and also Newton’s Third Law for particles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32219,75,885,'paragraph',NULL,'4. To establish that the time rate of change of the total angular momentum of a system of particles is the total external torque in the system, we need not only Newton’s second law for particles, but also Newton’s third law with the provision that the forces between any two particles act along the line joining the particles.','[{"s": "4. To establish that the time rate of change of the total angular momentum of a system of particles is the total external torque in the system, we need not only Newton’s second law for particles, but also Newton’s third law with the provision that the forces between any two particles act along the line joining the particles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32220,75,886,'paragraph',NULL,'5. The vanishing of the total external force and the vanishing of the total external torque are independent conditions. We can have one without the other. In a couple, total external force is zero, but total torque is non-zero.','[{"s": "5. The vanishing of the total external force and the vanishing of the total external torque are independent conditions. We can have one without the other. In a couple, total external force is zero, but total torque is non-zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32221,75,887,'paragraph',NULL,'6. The total torque on a system is independent of the origin if the total external force is zero.','[{"s": "6. The total torque on a system is independent of the origin if the total external force is zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32223,75,889,'paragraph',NULL,'8. The angular momentum L and the angular velocity ω are not necessarily parallel vectors. However, for the simpler situations discussed in this chapter when rotation is about a fixed axis which is an axis of symmetry of the rigid body, the relation L = Iω holds good, where I is the moment of the inertia of the body about the rotation axis.','[{"s": "8. The angular momentum", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the angular velocity ω are not necessarily parallel vectors. However, for the simpler situations discussed in this chapter when rotation is about a fixed axis which is an axis of symmetry of the rigid body, the relation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= Iω holds good, where I is the moment of the inertia of the body about the rotation axis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32224,75,890,'heading',2,'EXERCISES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32225,75,891,'paragraph',NULL,'7.1 Give the location of the centre of mass of a (i) sphere, (ii) cylinder, (iii) ring, and (iv) cube, each of uniform mass density. Does the centre of mass of a body necessarily lie inside the body ?','[{"s": "7.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Give the location of the centre of mass of a (i) sphere, (ii) cylinder, (iii) ring, and (iv) cube, each of uniform mass density. Does the centre of mass of a body necessarily lie inside the body ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32226,75,892,'paragraph',NULL,'7.2 In the HCl molecule, the separation between the nuclei of the two atoms is about 1.27 Å (1 Å = 10 -10 m). Find the approximate location of the CM of the molecule, given that a chlorine atom is about 35.5 times as massive as a hydrogen atom and nearly all the mass of an atom is concentrated in its nucleus.','[{"s": "7.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the HCl molecule, the separation between the nuclei of the two atoms is about 1.27 Å (1 Å = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m). Find the approximate location of the CM of the molecule, given that a chlorine atom is about 35.5 times as massive as a hydrogen atom and nearly all the mass of an atom is concentrated in its nucleus.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32227,75,893,'paragraph',NULL,'7.3 A child sits stationary at one end of a long trolley moving uniformly with a speed V on a smooth horizontal floor. If the child gets up and runs about on the trolley in any manner, what is the speed of the CM of the (trolley + child) system ?','[{"s": "7.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A child sits stationary at one end of a long trolley moving uniformly with a speed V on a smooth horizontal floor. If the child gets up and runs about on the trolley in any manner, what is the speed of the CM of the (trolley + child) system ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32228,75,894,'paragraph',NULL,'7.4 Show that the area of the triangle contained between the vectors a and b is one half of the magnitude of a × b .','[{"s": "7.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Show that the area of the triangle contained between the vectors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is one half of the magnitude of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32229,75,895,'paragraph',NULL,'7.5 Show that a. ( b × c ) is equal in magnitude to the volume of the parallelepiped formed on the three vectors , a , b and c .','[{"s": "7.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Show that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is equal in magnitude to the volume of the parallelepiped formed on the three vectors ,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32230,75,896,'paragraph',NULL,'7.6 Find the components along the x, y, z axes of the angular momentum l of a particle, whose position vector is r with components x, y, z and momentum is p with components p x , p y and p z . Show that if the particle moves only in the x-y plane the angular momentum has only a z-component.','[{"s": "7.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Find the components along the x, y, z axes of the angular momentum", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of a particle, whose position vector is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with components x, y, z and momentum is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with components p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Show that if the particle moves only in the x-y plane the angular momentum has only a z-component.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32231,75,897,'paragraph',NULL,'7.7 Two particles, each of mass m and speed v, travel in opposite directions along parallel lines separated by a distance d. Show that the angular momentum vector of the two particle system is the same whatever be the point about which the angular momentum is taken.','[{"s": "7.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two particles, each of mass m and speed v, travel in opposite directions along parallel lines separated by a distance d. Show that the angular momentum vector of the two particle system is the same whatever be the point about which the angular momentum is taken.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32232,75,898,'paragraph',NULL,'7.8 A non-uniform bar of weight W is suspended at rest by two strings of negligible weight as shown in Fig.7.39. The angles made by the strings with the vertical are 36.9° and 53.1° respectively. The bar is 2 m long. Calculate the distance d of the centre of gravity of the bar from its left end.','[{"s": "7.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A non-uniform bar of weight W is suspended at rest by two strings of negligible weight as shown in Fig.7.39. The angles made by the strings with the vertical are 36.9° and 53.1° respectively. The bar is 2 m long. Calculate the distance d of the centre of gravity of the bar from its left end.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
