(32347,75,1013,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 7. 11. Torques of equal magnitude are applied to a hollow cylinder and a solid sphere, both having the same mass and radius. The cylinder is free to rotate about its standard axis of symmetry, and the sphere is free to rotate about an axis passing through its center. Which of the two will acquire a greater angular speed after a given time.','[{"s": "Question 7. 11. Torques of equal magnitude are applied to a hollow cylinder and a solid sphere, both having the same mass and radius. The cylinder is free to rotate about its standard axis of symmetry, and the sphere is free to rotate about an axis passing through its center. Which of the two will acquire a greater angular speed after a given time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32348,75,1014,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 7. 12 A solid cylinder of mass 20 kg rotates about its axis with angular speed 100 rad s –1 . The radius of the cylinder is 0.25 m. What is the kinetic energy associated with the rotation of the cylinder? What is the magnitude of angular momentum of the cylinder about its axis?','[{"s": "Question 7. 12 A solid cylinder of mass 20 kg rotates about its axis with angular speed 100 rad s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The radius of the cylinder is 0.25 m. What is the kinetic energy associated with the rotation of the cylinder? What is the magnitude of angular momentum of the cylinder about its axis?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32349,75,1015,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 7. 13. (a) A child stands at the center of a turntable with his arms outstretched. The turntable is set rotating with an angular speed of 40 rev/min. How much is the angular speed of the child if he folds his hands back and thereby reduces his moment of inertia to 2/5 times the initial value? Assume that the turntable rotates without friction.','[{"s": "Question 7. 13. (a) A child stands at the center of a turntable with his arms outstretched. The turntable is set rotating with an angular speed of 40 rev/min. How much is the angular speed of the child if he folds his hands back and thereby reduces his moment of inertia to 2/5 times the initial value? Assume that the turntable rotates without friction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32350,75,1016,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Show that the child’s new kinetic energy of rotation is more than the initial kinetic energy of rotation. How do you account for this increase in kinetic energy?','[{"s": "(b) Show that the child’s new kinetic energy of rotation is more than the initial kinetic energy of rotation. How do you account for this increase in kinetic energy?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32351,75,1017,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 7. 14. A rope of negligible mass is wound around a hollow cylinder of mass 3 kg and radius 40 cm. What is the angular acceleration of the cylinder if the rope is pulled with a force of 30 N? What is the linear acceleration of the rope? Assume that there is no slipping.','[{"s": "Question 7. 14. A rope of negligible mass is wound around a hollow cylinder of mass 3 kg and radius 40 cm. What is the angular acceleration of the cylinder if the rope is pulled with a force of 30 N? What is the linear acceleration of the rope? Assume that there is no slipping.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32353,75,1019,'paragraph',NULL,'Note: Uniform angular velocity in the absence of friction implies zero torque. In practice, applied torque is needed to counter frictional torque). Assume that the engine is 100% efficient.','[{"s": "Note: Uniform angular velocity in the absence of friction implies zero torque. In practice, applied torque is needed to counter frictional torque). Assume that the engine is 100% efficient.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32354,75,1020,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 7. 16. From a uniform disk of radius R, a circular hole of radius R/2 is cut out. The centre of the hole is at R/2 from the centre of the original disc. Locate the centre of gravity of the resulting flat body.','[{"s": "Question 7. 16. From a uniform disk of radius R, a circular hole of radius R/2 is cut out. The centre of the hole is at R/2 from the centre of the original disc. Locate the centre of gravity of the resulting flat body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32355,75,1021,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 7. 17. A meter stick is balanced on a knife-edge at its center. When two coins, each of mass 5g are put one on top of the other at the 12.0 cm mark, the stick is found to be balanced at 45.0 cm. What is the mass of the meter stick?','[{"s": "Question 7. 17. A meter stick is balanced on a knife-edge at its center. When two coins, each of mass 5g are put one on top of the other at the 12.0 cm mark, the stick is found to be balanced at 45.0 cm. What is the mass of the meter stick?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32356,75,1022,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 7. 18. A solid sphere rolls down two different inclined planes of the same heights but different angles of inclination, (a) Will it reach the bottom with the same speed in each case? (b) Will it take longer to roll down one plane than the other? (c) If so, which one and why?','[{"s": "Question 7. 18. A solid sphere rolls down two different inclined planes of the same heights but different angles of inclination, (a) Will it reach the bottom with the same speed in each case? (b) Will it take longer to roll down one plane than the other? (c) If so, which one and why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32357,75,1023,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 7. 19. A hoop of radius 2 m weighs 100 kg. It rolls along a horizontal floor so that its center of mass has a speed of 20 cm/s. How much work has to be done to stop it?','[{"s": "Question 7. 19. A hoop of radius 2 m weighs 100 kg. It rolls along a horizontal floor so that its center of mass has a speed of 20 cm/s. How much work has to be done to stop it?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32358,75,1024,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 7. 20. The oxygen molecule has a mass of 5.30 x 10 -26 kg and a moment of inertia of 1.94 x 10 -46 kg m 2 about an axis through its center perpendicular to the lines joining the two atoms. Suppose the mean speed of such a molecule in a gas is 500 m/s and that its kinetic energy of rotation is two-thirds of its kinetic energy of translation. Find the average angular velocity of the molecule.','[{"s": "Question 7. 20. The oxygen molecule has a mass of 5.30 x 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-26", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg and a moment of inertia of 1.94 x 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-46", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "about an axis through its center perpendicular to the lines joining the two atoms. Suppose the mean speed of such a molecule in a gas is 500 m/s and that its kinetic energy of rotation is two-thirds of its kinetic energy of translation. Find the average angular velocity of the molecule.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
