(32514,76,32,'paragraph',NULL,'or PV = µRT (11.2)','[{"s": "or PV = µRT (11.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32515,76,33,'paragraph',NULL,'where, µ is the number of moles in the sample of gas and R is called universal gas constant:','[{"s": "where, µ is the number of moles in the sample of gas and R is called universal gas constant:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32516,76,34,'paragraph',NULL,'R = 8.31 J mol–1 K–1','[{"s": "R = 8.31 J mol–1 K–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32517,76,35,'paragraph',NULL,'In Eq. 11.2, we have learnt that the pressure and volume are directly proportional to temperature : PV ∝ T. This relationship allows a gas to be used to measure temperature in a constant volume gas thermometer. Holding the volume of a gas constant, it gives P ∝T. Thus, with a constant-volume gas thermometer, temperature is read in terms of pressure. A plot of pressure versus temperature gives a straight line in this case, as shown in Fig. 11.2.','[{"s": "In Eq. 11.2, we have learnt that the pressure and volume are directly proportional to temperature : PV ∝ T. This relationship allows a gas to be used to measure temperature in a constant volume gas thermometer. Holding the volume of a gas constant, it gives P ∝T. Thus, with a constant-volume gas thermometer, temperature is read in terms of pressure. A plot of pressure versus temperature gives a straight line in this case, as shown in Fig. 11.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32518,76,36,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image54.png'),
(32519,76,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.3 A plot of pressure versus temperature and extrapolation of lines for low density gases indicates the same absolute zero temperature.','[{"s": "Fig. 11.3 A plot of pressure versus temperature and extrapolation of lines for low density gases indicates the same absolute zero temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32520,76,38,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image59.png'),
(32522,76,40,'paragraph',NULL,'The size of unit in Kelvin and Celsius temperature scales is the same. So, temperature on these scales are related by','[{"s": "The size of unit in Kelvin and Celsius temperature scales is the same. So, temperature on these scales are related by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32523,76,41,'paragraph',NULL,'T = tC + 273.15 (11.3)','[{"s": "T = tC + 273.15 (11.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32524,76,42,'heading',4,'11.5 Thermal Expansion',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32525,76,43,'paragraph',NULL,'You may have observed that sometimes sealed bottles with metallic lids are so tightly screwed that one has to put the lid in hot water for some time to open it. This would allow the metallic lid to expand, thereby loosening it to unscrew easily. In case of liquids, you may have observed that mercury in a thermometer rises, when the thermometer is put in slightly warm water. If we take out the thermometer from the warm water the level of mercury falls again. Similarly, in case of gases, a balloon partially inflated in a cool room may expand to full size when placed in warm water. On the other hand, a fully inflated balloon when immersed in cold water would start shrinking due to contraction of the air inside.','[{"s": "You may have observed that sometimes sealed bottles with metallic lids are so tightly screwed that one has to put the lid in hot water for some time to open it. This would allow the metallic lid to expand, thereby loosening it to unscrew easily. In case of liquids, you may have observed that mercury in a thermometer rises, when the thermometer is put in slightly warm water. If we take out the thermometer from the warm water the level of mercury falls again. Similarly, in case of gases, a balloon partially inflated in a cool room may expand to full size when placed in warm water. On the other hand, a fully inflated balloon when immersed in cold water would start shrinking due to contraction of the air inside.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32526,76,44,'paragraph',NULL,'It is our common experience that most substances expand on heating and contract on cooling. A change in the temperature of a body causes change in its dimensions. The increase in the dimensions of a body due to the increase in its temperature is called thermal expansion. The expansion in length is called linear expansion. The expansion in area is called area expansion. The expansion in volume is called volume expansion (Fig. 11.5).','[{"s": "It is our common experience that most substances expand on heating and contract on cooling. A change in the temperature of a body causes change in its dimensions. The increase in the dimensions of a body due to the increase in its temperature is called thermal expansion. The expansion in length is called linear expansion. The expansion in area is called area expansion. The expansion in volume is called volume expansion (Fig. 11.5).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32527,76,45,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image58.png'),
(32528,76,46,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image64.png'),
(32529,76,47,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image62.png'),
(32530,76,48,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image69.png'),
(32531,76,49,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Linear expansion (b) Area expansion (c) Volume expansion','[{"s": "(a) Linear expansion (b) Area expansion (c) Volume expansion", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32532,76,50,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.5 Thermal Expansion.','[{"s": "Fig. 11.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermal Expansion.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32533,76,51,'paragraph',NULL,'If the substance is in the form of a long rod, then for small change in temperature, ∆T, the fractional change in length, ∆l/l, is directly proportional to ∆T.','[{"s": "If the substance is in the form of a long rod, then for small change in temperature, ∆T, the fractional change in length, ∆l/l, is directly proportional to ∆T.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32534,76,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image65.png (11.4)','[{"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image65.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(11.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32535,76,53,'paragraph',NULL,'where α1 is known as the coefficient of linear expansion (or linear expansivity) and is characteristic of the material of the rod. In Table 11.1, typical average values of the coefficient of linear expansion for some material in the temperature range 0 °C to 100 °C are given. From this Table, compare the value of αl for glass and copper. We find that copper expands about five times more than glass for the same rise in temperature. Normally, metals expand more and have relatively high values of α l .','[{"s": "where α1 is known as the coefficient of linear expansion (or linear expansivity) and is characteristic of the material of the rod. In Table 11.1, typical average values of the coefficient of linear expansion for some material in the temperature range 0 °C to 100 °C are given. From this Table, compare the value of αl for glass and copper. We find that copper expands about five times more than glass for the same rise in temperature. Normally, metals expand more and have relatively high values of α", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32536,76,54,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 11.1 Values of coefficient of linear expansion for some material','[{"s": "Table 11.1 Values of coefficient of linear expansion for some material", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32537,76,55,'image',NULL,'11.1',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image67.png'),
(32539,76,57,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image74.png (11.5)','[{"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image74.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(11.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32521,76,39,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.4 Comparison of the Kelvin, Celsius and Fahrenheit temperature scales.','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "11.4 Comparison of the Kelvin, Celsius and Fahrenheit temperature scales.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000037562", "2000110187"]',NULL),
(32540,76,58,'paragraph',NULL,'Here αV is also a characteristic of the substance but is not strictly a constant. It depends in general on temperature (Fig 11.6). It is seen that αV becomes constant only at a high temperature.','[{"s": "Here αV is also a characteristic of the substance but is not strictly a constant. It depends in general on temperature (Fig 11.6). It is seen that αV becomes constant only at a high temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32541,76,59,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image76.png'),
(32542,76,60,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.6 Coefficient of volume expansion of copper as a function of temperature.','[{"s": "Fig. 11.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Coefficient of volume expansion of copper as a function of temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32543,76,61,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 11.2 gives the values of coefficient of volume expansion of some common substances in the temperature range 0–100 °C. You can see that thermal expansion of these substances (solids and liquids) is rather small, with material, like pyrex glass and invar (a special iron-nickel alloy) having particularly low values of αV. From this Table we find that the value of αv for alcohol (ethanol) is more than mercury and expands more than mercury for the same rise in temperature.','[{"s": "Table 11.2 gives the values of coefficient of volume expansion of some common substances in the temperature range 0–100 °C. You can see that thermal expansion of these substances (solids and liquids) is rather small, with material, like pyrex glass and invar (a special iron-nickel alloy) having particularly low values of αV. From this Table we find that the value of αv for alcohol (ethanol) is more than mercury and expands more than mercury for the same rise in temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32544,76,62,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 11.2 Values of coefficient of volume expansion for some substances','[{"s": "Table 11.2 Values of coefficient of volume expansion for some substances", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32545,76,63,'image',NULL,'11.2',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image78.png'),
(32546,76,64,'paragraph',NULL,'Water exhibits an anomalous behaviour; it contracts on heating between 0 °C and 4 °C. The volume of a given amount of water decreases as it is cooled from room temperature, until its temperature reaches 4 °C, [Fig. 11.7(a)]. Below 4 °C, the volume increases, and therefore, the density decreases [Fig. 11.7(b)].','[{"s": "Water exhibits an anomalous behaviour; it contracts on heating between 0 °C and 4 °C. The volume of a given amount of water decreases as it is cooled from room temperature, until its temperature reaches 4 °C, [Fig. 11.7(a)]. Below 4 °C, the volume increases, and therefore, the density decreases [Fig. 11.7(b)].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32547,76,65,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image79.png'),
(32548,76,66,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) (b)','[{"s": "(a) (b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32549,76,67,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.7 Thermal expansion of water.','[{"s": "Fig. 11.7 Thermal expansion of water.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32550,76,68,'paragraph',NULL,'This means that water has the maximum density at 4 °C. This property has an important environmental effect: bodies of water, such as lakes and ponds, freeze at the top first. As a lake cools toward 4 °C, water near the surface loses energy to the atmosphere, becomes denser, and sinks; the warmer, less dense water near the bottom rises. However, once the colder water on top reaches temperature below 4 °C, it becomes less dense and remains at the surface, where it freezes. If water did not have this property, lakes and ponds would freeze from the bottom up, which would destroy much of their animal and plant life.','[{"s": "This means that water has the maximum density at 4 °C. This property has an important environmental effect: bodies of water, such as lakes and ponds, freeze at the top first. As a lake cools toward 4 °C, water near the surface loses energy to the atmosphere, becomes denser, and sinks; the warmer, less dense water near the bottom rises. However, once the colder water on top reaches temperature below 4 °C, it becomes less dense and remains at the surface, where it freezes. If water did not have this property, lakes and ponds would freeze from the bottom up, which would destroy much of their animal and plant life.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32551,76,69,'paragraph',NULL,'Gases, at ordinary temperature, expand more than solids and liquids. For liquids, the coefficient of volume expansion is relatively independent of the temperature. However, for gases it is dependent on temperature. For an ideal gas, the coefficient of volume expansion at constant pressure can be found from the ideal gas equation:','[{"s": "Gases, at ordinary temperature, expand more than solids and liquids. For liquids, the coefficient of volume expansion is relatively independent of the temperature. However, for gases it is dependent on temperature. For an ideal gas, the coefficient of volume expansion at constant pressure can be found from the ideal gas equation:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
