(32563,76,81,'paragraph',NULL,'αv = 3α l (11.9)','[{"s": "αv = 3α", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(11.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32564,76,82,'paragraph',NULL,'What happens by preventing the thermal expansion of a rod by fixing its ends rigidly? Clearly, the rod acquires a compressive strain due to the external forces provided by the rigid support at the ends. The corresponding stress set up in the rod is called thermal stress. For example, consider a steel rail of length 5 m and area of cross-section 40 cm2 that is prevented from expanding while the temperature rises by 10 °C. The coefficient of linear expansion of steel is αl(steel) = 1.2 × 10–5 K–1. Thus, the compressive strain is Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image40.png = αl(steel) ∆T = 1.2 × 10–5 × 10=1.2 × 10–4. Youngs modulus of steel is Y (steel) = 2 × 1011 N m–2. Therefore, the thermal stress developed is Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image42.png 2.4 × 107 N m–2, which corresponds to an external force of','[{"s": "What happens by preventing the thermal expansion of a rod by fixing its ends rigidly? Clearly, the rod acquires a compressive strain due to the external forces provided by the rigid support at the ends. The corresponding stress set up in the rod is called thermal stress. For example, consider a steel rail of length 5 m and area of cross-section 40 cm2 that is prevented from expanding while the temperature rises by 10 °C. The coefficient of linear expansion of steel is αl(steel) = 1.2 × 10–5 K–1. Thus, the compressive strain is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image40.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= αl(steel) ∆T = 1.2 × 10–5 × 10=1.2 × 10–4. Youngs modulus of steel is Y (steel) = 2 × 1011 N m–2. Therefore, the thermal stress developed is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image42.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "2.4 × 107 N m–2, which corresponds to an external force of", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32565,76,83,'paragraph',NULL,'∆F = AYsteel Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image44.png = 2.4 × 107 × 40 × 10–4 j 105N. If two such steel rails, fixed at their outer ends, are in contact at their inner ends, a force of this magnitude can easily bend the rails.','[{"s": "∆F = AYsteel", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image44.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 2.4 × 107 × 40 × 10–4 j 105N. If two such steel rails, fixed at their outer ends, are in contact at their inner ends, a force of this magnitude can easily bend the rails.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32566,76,84,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.1 Show that the coefficient of area expansion, ( ∆ A/A)/ ∆ T, of a rectangular sheet of the solid is twice its linear expansivity, αl .','[{"s": "Example 11.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Show that the coefficient of area expansion, (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A/A)/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T, of a rectangular sheet of the solid is twice its linear expansivity,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "αl", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32567,76,85,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32568,76,86,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image46.png'),
(32569,76,87,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.8','[{"s": "Fig. 11.8", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32570,76,88,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a rectangular sheet of the solid material of length a and breadth b (Fig. 11.8 ). When the temperature increases by ∆T, a increases by ∆a = αl a∆T and b increases by ∆b = αlb ∆T. From Fig. 11.8, the increase in area','[{"s": "Consider a rectangular sheet of the solid material of length a and breadth b (Fig. 11.8 ). When the temperature increases by ∆T, a increases by ∆a = αl a∆T and b increases by ∆b = αlb ∆T. From Fig. 11.8, the increase in area", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32571,76,89,'paragraph',NULL,'∆A = ∆A1 +∆A2 + ∆A3','[{"s": "∆A = ∆A1 +∆A2 + ∆A3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32572,76,90,'paragraph',NULL,'∆A = a ∆b + b ∆a + (∆a) (∆b)','[{"s": "∆A = a ∆b + b ∆a + (∆a) (∆b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32573,76,91,'paragraph',NULL,'= a αlb ∆T + b αl a ∆T + (αl)2 ab (∆T)2','[{"s": "= a αlb ∆T + b αl a ∆T + (αl)2 ab (∆T)2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32574,76,92,'paragraph',NULL,'= αl ab ∆T (2 + αl ∆T) = αl A ∆T (2 + αl ∆T)','[{"s": "= αl ab ∆T (2 + αl ∆T) = αl A ∆T (2 + αl ∆T)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32575,76,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Since αl Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image36.png 10–5 K–1, from Table 11.1, the product αl ∆T for fractional temperature is small in comparison with 2 and may be neglected.','[{"s": "Since αl", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image36.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "10–5 K–1, from Table 11.1, the product αl ∆T for fractional temperature is small in comparison with 2 and may be neglected.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32576,76,94,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence,','[{"s": "Hence,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32577,76,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image48.png t','[{"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image48.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32579,76,97,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32580,76,98,'paragraph',NULL,'Given, T1 = 27 °C','[{"s": "Given, T1 = 27 °C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32581,76,99,'paragraph',NULL,'LT1 = 5.231 m','[{"s": "LT1 = 5.231 m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32582,76,100,'paragraph',NULL,'LT2 = 5.243 m','[{"s": "LT2 = 5.243 m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32583,76,101,'paragraph',NULL,'So,','[{"s": "So,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
