(32588,76,106,'paragraph',NULL,'Take some water in a vessel and start heating it on a burner. Soon you will notice that bubbles begin to move upward. As the temperature is raised the motion of water particles increases till it becomes turbulent as water starts boiling. What are the factors on which the quantity of heat required to raise the temperature of a substance depend? In order to answer this question in the first step, heat a given quantity of water to raise its temperature by, say 20 °C and note the time taken. Again take the same amount of water and raise its temperature by 40 °C using the same source of heat. Note the time taken by using a stopwatch. You will find it takes about twice the time and therefore, double the quantity of heat required raising twice the temperature of same amount of water.','[{"s": "Take some water in a vessel and start heating it on a burner. Soon you will notice that bubbles begin to move upward. As the temperature is raised the motion of water particles increases till it becomes turbulent as water starts boiling. What are the factors on which the quantity of heat required to raise the temperature of a substance depend? In order to answer this question in the first step, heat a given quantity of water to raise its temperature by, say 20 °C and note the time taken. Again take the same amount of water and raise its temperature by 40 °C using the same source of heat. Note the time taken by using a stopwatch. You will find it takes about twice the time and therefore, double the quantity of heat required raising twice the temperature of same amount of water.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32589,76,107,'paragraph',NULL,'In the second step, now suppose you take double the amount of water and heat it, using the same heating arrangement, to raise the temperature by 20 °C, you will find the time taken is again twice that required in the first step.','[{"s": "In the second step, now suppose you take double the amount of water and heat it, using the same heating arrangement, to raise the temperature by 20 °C, you will find the time taken is again twice that required in the first step.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32590,76,108,'paragraph',NULL,'In the third step, in place of water, now heat the same quantity of some oil, say mustard oil, and raise the temperature again by 20 °C. Now note the time by the same stopwatch. You will find the time taken will be shorter and therefore, the quantity of heat required would be less than that required by the same amount of water for the same rise in temperature.','[{"s": "In the third step, in place of water, now heat the same quantity of some oil, say mustard oil, and raise the temperature again by 20 °C. Now note the time by the same stopwatch. You will find the time taken will be shorter and therefore, the quantity of heat required would be less than that required by the same amount of water for the same rise in temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32591,76,109,'paragraph',NULL,'The above observations show that the quantity of heat required to warm a given substance depends on its mass, m, the change in temperature, ∆T and the nature of substance. The change in temperature of a substance, when a given quantity of heat is absorbed or rejected by it, is characterised by a quantity called the heat capacity of that substance. We define heat capacity, S of a substance as','[{"s": "The above observations show that the quantity of heat required to warm a given substance depends on its mass, m, the change in temperature, ∆T and the nature of substance. The change in temperature of a substance, when a given quantity of heat is absorbed or rejected by it, is characterised by a quantity called the heat capacity of that substance. We define heat capacity, S of a substance as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32592,76,110,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image49.png (11.10)','[{"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image49.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(11.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32593,76,111,'paragraph',NULL,'where ∆Q is the amount of heat supplied to the substance to change its temperature from T to T + ∆T.','[{"s": "where ∆Q is the amount of heat supplied to the substance to change its temperature from T to T + ∆T.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32594,76,112,'paragraph',NULL,'You have observed that if equal amount of heat is added to equal masses of different substances, the resulting temperature changes will not be the same. It implies that every substance has a unique value for the amount of heat absorbed or given off to change the temperature of unit mass of it by one unit. This quantity is referred to as the specific heat capacity of the substance.','[{"s": "You have observed that if equal amount of heat is added to equal masses of different substances, the resulting temperature changes will not be the same. It implies that every substance has a unique value for the amount of heat absorbed or given off to change the temperature of unit mass of it by one unit. This quantity is referred to as the specific heat capacity of the substance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32596,76,114,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image50.png (11.11)','[{"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image50.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(11.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32597,76,115,'paragraph',NULL,'The specific heat capacity is the property of the substance which determines the change in the temperature of the substance (undergoing no phase change) when a given quantity of heat is absorbed (or given off) by it. It is defined as the amount of heat per unit mass absorbed or given off by the substance to change its temperature by one unit. It depends on the nature of the substance and its temperature. The SI unit of specific heat capacity is J kg –1 K –1 .','[{"s": "The specific heat capacity is the property of the substance which determines the change in the temperature of the substance (undergoing no phase change) when a given quantity of heat is absorbed (or given off) by it. It is defined as the amount of heat per unit mass absorbed or given off by the substance to change its temperature by one unit. It depends on the nature of the substance and its temperature. The SI unit of specific heat capacity is J kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32598,76,116,'paragraph',NULL,'If the amount of substance is specified in terms of moles µ, instead of mass m in kg, we can define heat capacity per mole of the substance by','[{"s": "If the amount of substance is specified in terms of moles µ, instead of mass m in kg, we can define heat capacity per mole of the substance by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32599,76,117,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image15.png (11.12)','[{"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image15.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(11.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32600,76,118,'paragraph',NULL,'where C is known as molar specific heat capacity of the substance. Like S, C also depends on the nature of the substance and its temperature. The SI unit of molar specific heat capacity is J mol –1 K –1 .','[{"s": "where C is known as molar specific heat capacity of the substance. Like S, C also depends on the nature of the substance and its temperature. The SI unit of molar specific heat capacity is J mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32601,76,119,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 11.3 Specific heat capacity of some substances at room temperature and atmospheric pressure','[{"s": "Table 11.3 Specific heat capacity of some substances at room temperature and atmospheric pressure", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32602,76,120,'image',NULL,'11.3',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image17.png'),
(32603,76,121,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 11.4 Molar specific heat capacities of some gases','[{"s": "Table 11.4 Molar specific heat capacities of some gases", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32604,76,122,'image',NULL,'11.4',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image19.png'),
(32605,76,123,'heading',4,'11.7 Calorimetry',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32608,76,126,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.3 A sphere of 0.047 kg aluminium is placed for sufficient time in a vessel containing boiling water, so that the sphere is at 100 °C. It is then immediately transfered to 0.14 kg copper calorimeter containing 0.25 kg water at 20 °C. The temperature of water rises and attains a steady state at 23 °C. Calculate the specific heat capacity of aluminium.','[{"s": "Example 11.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A sphere of 0.047 kg aluminium is placed for sufficient time in a vessel containing boiling water, so that the sphere is at 100 °C. It is then immediately transfered to 0.14 kg copper calorimeter containing 0.25 kg water at 20 °C. The temperature of water rises and attains a steady state at 23 °C. Calculate the specific heat capacity of aluminium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32609,76,127,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer In solving this example, we shall use the fact that at a steady state, heat given by an aluminium sphere will be equal to the heat absorbed by the water and calorimeter.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In solving this example, we shall use the fact that at a steady state, heat given by an aluminium sphere will be equal to the heat absorbed by the water and calorimeter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32610,76,128,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass of aluminium sphere (m1) = 0.047 kg','[{"s": "Mass of aluminium sphere (m1) = 0.047 kg", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32611,76,129,'paragraph',NULL,'Initial temperature of aluminium sphere = 100 °C','[{"s": "Initial temperature of aluminium sphere = 100 °C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32612,76,130,'paragraph',NULL,'Final temperature = 23 °C','[{"s": "Final temperature = 23 °C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32613,76,131,'paragraph',NULL,'Change in temperature (∆T)=(100 °C-23°C)= 77 °C','[{"s": "Change in temperature (∆T)=(100 °C-23°C)= 77 °C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32614,76,132,'paragraph',NULL,'Let specific heat capacity of aluminium be s Al .','[{"s": "Let specific heat capacity of aluminium be s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Al", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32615,76,133,'paragraph',NULL,'The amount of heat lost by the aluminium sphere = Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image21.png','[{"s": "The amount of heat lost by the aluminium sphere =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image21.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32616,76,134,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass of water (m2) = 0.25 kg','[{"s": "Mass of water (m2) = 0.25 kg", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32617,76,135,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass of calorimeter (m3) = 0.14 kg','[{"s": "Mass of calorimeter (m3) = 0.14 kg", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32618,76,136,'paragraph',NULL,'Initial temperature of water and calorimeter=20 °C','[{"s": "Initial temperature of water and calorimeter=20 °C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32619,76,137,'paragraph',NULL,'Final temperature of the mixture = 23 °C','[{"s": "Final temperature of the mixture = 23 °C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32620,76,138,'paragraph',NULL,'Change in temperature (∆T2) = 23 °C – 20 °C = 3 °C','[{"s": "Change in temperature (∆T2) = 23 °C – 20 °C = 3 °C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32621,76,139,'paragraph',NULL,'= 4.18 × 103 J kg –1 K –1','[{"s": "= 4.18 × 103 J kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32622,76,140,'paragraph',NULL,'Specific heat capacity of copper calorimeter','[{"s": "Specific heat capacity of copper calorimeter", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32623,76,141,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.386 × 103 J kg –1 K –1','[{"s": "= 0.386 × 103 J kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32624,76,142,'paragraph',NULL,'The amount of heat gained by water and calorimeter = m2 sw ∆T2 + m3scu∆T2','[{"s": "The amount of heat gained by water and calorimeter = m2 sw ∆T2 + m3scu∆T2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32625,76,143,'paragraph',NULL,'= (m2sw + m3scu) (∆T2)','[{"s": "= (m2sw + m3scu) (∆T2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32626,76,144,'paragraph',NULL,'Specific heat capacity of water (sw)','[{"s": "Specific heat capacity of water (sw)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32627,76,145,'paragraph',NULL,'= (0.25 kg × 4.18 × 103 J kg–1 K –1 + 0.14 kg ×','[{"s": "= (0.25 kg × 4.18 × 103 J kg–1 K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 0.14 kg ×", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32628,76,146,'paragraph',NULL,'0.386 × 103 J kg –1 K –1 ) (23 °C – 20 °C)','[{"s": "0.386 × 103 J kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (23 °C – 20 °C)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32629,76,147,'paragraph',NULL,'In the steady state heat lost by the aluminium sphere = heat gained by water + heat gained by calorimeter.','[{"s": "In the steady state heat lost by the aluminium sphere = heat gained by water + heat gained by calorimeter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32630,76,148,'paragraph',NULL,'So, 0.047 kg × sAl × 77 °C','[{"s": "So, 0.047 kg × sAl × 77 °C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32631,76,149,'paragraph',NULL,'= (0.25 kg × 4.18 × 103 J kg –1 K –1 + 0.14 kg ×','[{"s": "= (0.25 kg × 4.18 × 103 J kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 0.14 kg ×", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32632,76,150,'paragraph',NULL,'0.386 × 103 J kg –1 K –1 )(3 °C)','[{"s": "0.386 × 103 J kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")(3 °C)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32633,76,151,'paragraph',NULL,'sAl = 0.911 kJ kg –1 K –1 t','[{"s": "sAl = 0.911 kJ kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32634,76,152,'heading',4,'11.8 Change of State',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32635,76,153,'paragraph',NULL,'Matter normally exists in three states: solid, liquid and gas. A transition from one of these states to another is called a change of state. Two common changes of states are solid to liquid and liquid to gas (and, vice versa). These changes can occur when the exchange of heat takes place between the substance and its surroundings. To study the change of state on heating or cooling, let us perform the following activity.','[{"s": "Matter normally exists in three states: solid, liquid and gas. A transition from one of these states to another is called a change of state. Two common changes of states are solid to liquid and liquid to gas (and, vice versa). These changes can occur when the exchange of heat takes place between the substance and its surroundings. To study the change of state on heating or cooling, let us perform the following activity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32636,76,154,'paragraph',NULL,'Take some cubes of ice in a beaker. Note the temperature of ice. Start heating it slowly on a constant heat source. Note the temperature after every minute. Continuously stir the mixture of water and ice. Draw a graph between temperature and time (Fig. 11.9). You will observe no change in the temperature as long as there is ice in the beaker. In the above process, the temperature of the system does not change even though heat is being continuously supplied. The heat supplied is being utilised in changing the state from solid (ice) to liquid (water).','[{"s": "Take some cubes of ice in a beaker. Note the temperature of ice. Start heating it slowly on a constant heat source. Note the temperature after every minute. Continuously stir the mixture of water and ice. Draw a graph between temperature and time (Fig. 11.9). You will observe no change in the temperature as long as there is ice in the beaker. In the above process, the temperature of the system does not change even though heat is being continuously supplied. The heat supplied is being utilised in changing the state from solid (ice) to liquid (water).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32637,76,155,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image23.png'),
(32639,76,157,'paragraph',NULL,'Take a slab of ice. Take a metallic wire and fix two blocks, say 5 kg each, at its ends. Put the wire over the slab as shown in Fig. 11.10. You will observe that the wire passes through the ice slab. This happens due to the fact that just below the wire, ice melts at lower temperature due to increase in pressure. When the wire has passed, water above the wire freezes again. Thus, the wire passes through the slab and the slab does not split. This phenomenon of refreezing is called regelation. Skating is possible on snow due to the formation of water under the skates. Water is formed due to the increase of pressure and it acts as a lubricant.','[{"s": "Take a slab of ice. Take a metallic wire and fix two blocks, say 5 kg each, at its ends. Put the wire over the slab as shown in Fig. 11.10. You will observe that the wire passes through the ice slab. This happens due to the fact that just below the wire, ice melts at lower temperature due to increase in pressure. When the wire has passed, water above the wire freezes again. Thus, the wire passes through the slab and the slab does not split. This phenomenon of refreezing is called regelation. Skating is possible on snow due to the formation of water under the skates. Water is formed due to the increase of pressure and it acts as a lubricant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32640,76,158,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image25.png'),
(32641,76,159,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.10','[{"s": "Fig. 11.10", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32642,76,160,'paragraph',NULL,'After the whole of ice gets converted into water and as we continue further heating, we shall see that temperature begins to rise (Fig.11.9). The temperature keeps on rising till it reaches nearly 100 °C when it again becomes steady. The heat supplied is now being utilised to change water from liquid state to vapour or gaseous state.','[{"s": "After the whole of ice gets converted into water and as we continue further heating, we shall see that temperature begins to rise (Fig.11.9). The temperature keeps on rising till it reaches nearly 100 °C when it again becomes steady. The heat supplied is now being utilised to change water from liquid state to vapour or gaseous state.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32643,76,161,'paragraph',NULL,'The change of state from liquid to vapour (or gas) is called vaporisation. It is observed that the temperature remains constant until the entire amount of the liquid is converted into vapour. That is, both the liquid and vapour states of the substance coexist in thermal equilibrium, during the change of state from liquid to vapour. The temperature at which the liquid and the vapour states of the substance coexist is called its boiling point. Let us do the following activity to understand the process of boiling of water.','[{"s": "The change of state from liquid to vapour (or gas) is called vaporisation. It is observed that the temperature remains constant until the entire amount of the liquid is converted into vapour. That is, both the liquid and vapour states of the substance coexist in thermal equilibrium, during the change of state from liquid to vapour. The temperature at which the liquid and the vapour states of the substance coexist is called its boiling point. Let us do the following activity to understand the process of boiling of water.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32644,76,162,'paragraph',NULL,'Take a round-bottom flask, more than half filled with water. Keep it over a burner and fix a thermometer and steam outlet through the cork of the flask (Fig. 11.11). As water gets heated in the flask, note first that the air, which was dissolved in the water, will come out as small bubbles. Later, bubbles of steam will form at the bottom but as they rise to the cooler water near the top, they condense and disappear. Finally, as the temperature of the entire mass of the water reaches 100 °C, bubbles of steam reach the surface and boiling is said to occur. The steam in the flask may not be visible but as it comes out of the flask, it condenses as tiny droplets of water, giving a foggy appearance.','[{"s": "Take a round-bottom flask, more than half filled with water. Keep it over a burner and fix a thermometer and steam outlet through the cork of the flask (Fig. 11.11). As water gets heated in the flask, note first that the air, which was dissolved in the water, will come out as small bubbles. Later, bubbles of steam will form at the bottom but as they rise to the cooler water near the top, they condense and disappear. Finally, as the temperature of the entire mass of the water reaches 100 °C, bubbles of steam reach the surface and boiling is said to occur. The steam in the flask may not be visible but as it comes out of the flask, it condenses as tiny droplets of water, giving a foggy appearance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32645,76,163,'heading',4,'Triple Point',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32646,76,164,'paragraph',NULL,'The temperature of a substance remains constant during its change of state (phase change). A graph between the temperature T and the Pressure P of the substance is called a phase diagram or P – T diagram. The following figure shows the phase diagram of water and CO2. Such a phase diagram divides the P – T plane into a solid-region, the vapour-region and the liquid-region. The regions are separated by the curves such as sublimation curve (BO), fusion curve (AO) and vaporisation curve (CO). The points on sublimation curve represent states in which solid and vapour phases coexist. The point on the sublimation curve BO represent states in which the solid and vapour phases co-exist. Points on the fusion curve AO represent states in which solid and liquid phase coexist. Points on the vapourisation curve CO represent states in which the liquid and vapour phases coexist. The temperature and pressure at which the fusion curve, the vaporisation curve and the sublimation curve meet and all the three phases of a substance coexist is called the triple point of the substance. For example the triple point of water is represented by the temperature 273.16 K and pressure 6.11×10–3 Pa.','[{"s": "The temperature of a substance remains constant during its change of state (phase change). A graph between the temperature T and the Pressure P of the substance is called a phase diagram or P – T diagram. The following figure shows the phase diagram of water and CO2. Such a phase diagram divides the P – T plane into a solid-region, the vapour-region and the liquid-region. The regions are separated by the curves such as sublimation curve (BO), fusion curve (AO) and vaporisation curve (CO). The points on sublimation curve represent states in which solid and vapour phases coexist. The point on the sublimation curve BO represent states in which the solid and vapour phases co-exist. Points on the fusion curve AO represent states in which solid and liquid phase coexist. Points on the vapourisation curve CO represent states in which the liquid and vapour phases coexist. The temperature and pressure at which the fusion curve, the vaporisation curve and the sublimation curve meet and all the three phases of a substance coexist is called the triple point of the substance. For example the triple point of water is represented by the temperature 273.16 K and pressure 6.11×10–3 Pa.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32647,76,165,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image27.png'),
(32648,76,166,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) (b)','[{"s": "(a) (b)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32649,76,167,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.11: Pressure-temperature phase diagrams for (a) water and (b) CO2 (not to the scale).','[{"s": "Fig. 11.11: Pressure-temperature phase diagrams for (a) water and (b) CO2 (not to the scale).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32650,76,168,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image28.png'),
(32651,76,169,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.11 Boiling process .','[{"s": "Fig. 11.11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Boiling process", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32652,76,170,'paragraph',NULL,'If now the steam outlet is closed for a few seconds to increase the pressure in the flask, you will notice that boiling stops. More heat would be required to raise the temperature (depending on the increase in pressure) before boiling begins again. Thus boiling point increases with increase in pressure.','[{"s": "If now the steam outlet is closed for a few seconds to increase the pressure in the flask, you will notice that boiling stops. More heat would be required to raise the temperature (depending on the increase in pressure) before boiling begins again. Thus boiling point increases with increase in pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32653,76,171,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us now remove the burner. Allow water to cool to about 80 °C. Remove the thermometer and steam outlet. Close the flask with the airtight cork. Keep the flask turned upside down on the stand. Pour ice-cold water on the flask. Water vapours in the flask condense reducing the pressure on the water surface inside the flask. Water begins to boil again, now at a lower temperature. Thus boiling point decreases with decrease in pressure.','[{"s": "Let us now remove the burner. Allow water to cool to about 80 °C. Remove the thermometer and steam outlet. Close the flask with the airtight cork. Keep the flask turned upside down on the stand. Pour ice-cold water on the flask. Water vapours in the flask condense reducing the pressure on the water surface inside the flask. Water begins to boil again, now at a lower temperature. Thus boiling point decreases with decrease in pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32654,76,172,'paragraph',NULL,'This explains why cooking is difficult on hills. At high altitudes, atmospheric pressure is lower, reducing the boiling point of water as compared to that at sea level. On the other hand, boiling point is increased inside a pressure cooker by increasing the pressure. Hence cooking is faster. The boiling point of a substance at standard atmospheric pressure is called its normal boiling point.','[{"s": "This explains why cooking is difficult on hills. At high altitudes, atmospheric pressure is lower, reducing the boiling point of water as compared to that at sea level. On the other hand, boiling point is increased inside a pressure cooker by increasing the pressure. Hence cooking is faster. The boiling point of a substance at standard atmospheric pressure is called its normal boiling point.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32655,76,173,'paragraph',NULL,'However, all substances do not pass through the three states: solid-liquid-gas. There are certain substances which normally pass from the solid to the vapour state directly and vice versa. The change from solid state to vapour state without passing through the liquid state is called sublimation, and the substance is said to sublime. Dry ice (solid CO2) sublimes, so also iodine. During the sublimation process both the solid and vapour states of a substance coexist in thermal equilibrium.','[{"s": "However, all substances do not pass through the three states: solid-liquid-gas. There are certain substances which normally pass from the solid to the vapour state directly and vice versa. The change from solid state to vapour state without passing through the liquid state is called sublimation, and the substance is said to sublime. Dry ice (solid CO2) sublimes, so also iodine. During the sublimation process both the solid and vapour states of a substance coexist in thermal equilibrium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32656,76,174,'heading',4,'11.8.1 Latent Heat',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(32657,76,175,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 11.5 Temperatures of the change of state and latent heats for various substances at 1 atm pressure (not to scale).','[{"s": "Table 11.5 Temperatures of the change of state and latent heats for various substances at 1 atm pressure (not to scale).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32658,76,176,'image',NULL,'11.5',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image29.png'),
(32659,76,177,'paragraph',NULL,'The heat required during a change of state depends upon the heat of transformation and the mass of the substance undergoing a change of state. Thus, if mass m of a substance undergoes a change from one state to the other, then the quantity of heat required is given by','[{"s": "The heat required during a change of state depends upon the heat of transformation and the mass of the substance undergoing a change of state. Thus, if mass m of a substance undergoes a change from one state to the other, then the quantity of heat required is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32660,76,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Q = m L','[{"s": "Q = m L", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32661,76,179,'paragraph',NULL,'or L = Q/m (11.13)','[{"s": "or L = Q/m (11.13)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32662,76,180,'paragraph',NULL,'where L is known as latent heat and is a characteristic of the substance. Its SI unit is J kg –1 . The value of L also depends on the pressure. Its value is usually quoted at standard atmospheric pressure. The latent heat for a solid-liquid state change is called the latent heat of fusion (Lf), and that for a liquid-gas state change is called the latent heat of vaporisation (Lv). These are often referred to as the heat of fusion and the heat of vaporisation. A plot of temperature versus heat for a quantity of water is shown in Fig. 11.12. The latent heats of some substances, their freezing and boiling points, are given in Table 11.5.','[{"s": "where L is known as latent heat and is a characteristic of the substance. Its SI unit is J kg", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The value of L also depends on the pressure. Its value is usually quoted at standard atmospheric pressure. The latent heat for a solid-liquid state change is called the latent heat of fusion (Lf), and that for a liquid-gas state change is called the latent heat of vaporisation (Lv). These are often referred to as the heat of fusion and the heat of vaporisation. A plot of temperature versus heat for a quantity of water is shown in Fig. 11.12. The latent heats of some substances, their freezing and boiling points, are given in Table 11.5.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32663,76,181,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image30.png'),
(32664,76,182,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.12 Temperature versus heat for water at','[{"s": "Fig. 11.12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Temperature versus heat for water at", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32665,76,183,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that when heat is added (or removed) during a change of state, the temperature remains constant. Note in Fig. 11.12 that the slopes of the phase lines are not all the same, which indicate that specific heats of the various states are not equal. For water, the latent heat of fusion and vaporisation are Lf = 3.33 × 105 J kg–1 and Lv = 22.6 × 105 J kg–1, respectively. That is, 3.33 × 105 J of heat is needed to melt 1 kg ice at 0 °C, and 22.6 × 105 J of heat is needed to convert 1 kg water into steam at 100 °C. So, steam at 100 °C carries 22.6 × 105 J kg–1 more heat than water at 100 °C. This is why burns from steam are usually more serious than those from boiling water.','[{"s": "Note that when heat is added (or removed) during a change of state, the temperature remains constant. Note in Fig. 11.12 that the slopes of the phase lines are not all the same, which indicate that specific heats of the various states are not equal. For water, the latent heat of fusion and vaporisation are Lf = 3.33 × 105 J kg–1 and Lv = 22.6 × 105 J kg–1, respectively. That is, 3.33 × 105 J of heat is needed to melt 1 kg ice at 0 °C, and 22.6 × 105 J of heat is needed to convert 1 kg water into steam at 100 °C. So, steam at 100 °C carries 22.6 × 105 J kg–1 more heat than water at 100 °C. This is why burns from steam are usually more serious than those from boiling water.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
