(32809,76,327,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation 11.23 enables you to calculate the time of cooling of a body through a particular range of temperature.','[{"s": "Equation 11.23 enables you to calculate the time of cooling of a body through a particular range of temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32810,76,328,'paragraph',NULL,'For small temperature differences, the rate of cooling, due to conduction, convection, and radiation combined, is proportional to the difference in temperature. It is a valid approximation in the transfer of heat from a radiator to a room, the loss of heat through the wall of a room, or the cooling of a cup of tea on the table.','[{"s": "For small temperature differences, the rate of cooling, due to conduction, convection, and radiation combined, is proportional to the difference in temperature. It is a valid approximation in the transfer of heat from a radiator to a room, the loss of heat through the wall of a room, or the cooling of a cup of tea on the table.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32811,76,329,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image43.png'),
(32812,76,330,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.20 Verification of Newton’s Law of cooling.','[{"s": "Fig. 11.20", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Verification of Newton’s Law of cooling.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32813,76,331,'paragraph',NULL,'Newton’s law of cooling can be verified with the help of the experimental set-up shown in Fig. 11.20(a). The set-up consists of a double- walled vessel (V) containing water between the two walls. A copper calorimeter (C) containing hot water is placed inside the double-walled vessel. Two thermometers through the corks are used to note the temperatures T2 of water in calorimeter and T1 of hot water in between the double walls, respectively. Temperature of hot water in the calorimeter is noted after equal intervals of time. A graph is plotted between loge (T2–T1) [or ln(T2–T1)] and time (t). The nature of the graph is observed to be a straight line having a negative slope as shown in Fig. 11.20(b). This is in support of Eq. 11.22.','[{"s": "Newton’s law of cooling can be verified with the help of the experimental set-up shown in Fig. 11.20(a). The set-up consists of a double- walled vessel (V) containing water between the two walls. A copper calorimeter (C) containing hot water is placed inside the double-walled vessel. Two thermometers through the corks are used to note the temperatures T2 of water in calorimeter and T1 of hot water in between the double walls, respectively. Temperature of hot water in the calorimeter is noted after equal intervals of time. A graph is plotted between loge (T2–T1) [or ln(T2–T1)] and time (t). The nature of the graph is observed to be a straight line having a negative slope as shown in Fig. 11.20(b). This is in support of Eq. 11.22.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32814,76,332,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.8 A pan filled with hot food cools from 94 °C to 86 °C in 2 minutes when the room temperature is at 20 °C. How long will it take to cool from 71 °C to 69 °C?','[{"s": "Example 11.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A pan filled with hot food cools from 94 °C to 86 °C in 2 minutes when the room temperature is at 20 °C. How long will it take to cool from 71 °C to 69 °C?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32815,76,333,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The average temperature of 94 °C and 86 °C is 90 °C, which is 70 °C above the room temperature. Under these conditions the pan cools 8 °C in 2 minutes.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The average temperature of 94 °C and 86 °C is 90 °C, which is 70 °C above the room temperature. Under these conditions the pan cools 8 °C in 2 minutes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32816,76,334,'paragraph',NULL,'Using Eq. (11.21), we have','[{"s": "Using Eq. (11.21), we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32817,76,335,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image45.png'),
(32818,76,336,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image47.png'),
(32819,76,337,'paragraph',NULL,'The average of 69 °C and 71 °C is 70 °C, which is 50 °C above room temperature. K is the same for this situation as for the original.','[{"s": "The average of 69 °C and 71 °C is 70 °C, which is 50 °C above room temperature. K is the same for this situation as for the original.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32820,76,338,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image31.png = K (50 °C)','[{"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image31.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= K (50 °C)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32821,76,339,'paragraph',NULL,'When we divide above two equations, we have','[{"s": "When we divide above two equations, we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32822,76,340,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image32.png'),
(32823,76,341,'paragraph',NULL,'Time = 0.7 min','[{"s": "Time = 0.7 min", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32824,76,342,'paragraph',NULL,'= 42 s','[{"s": "= 42 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32825,76,343,'heading',4,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32826,76,344,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Heat is a form of energy that flows between a body and its surrounding medium by virtue of temperature difference between them. The degree of hotness of the body is quantitatively represented by temperature.','[{"s": "1. Heat is a form of energy that flows between a body and its surrounding medium by virtue of temperature difference between them. The degree of hotness of the body is quantitatively represented by temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32827,76,345,'paragraph',NULL,'2. A temperature-measuring device (thermometer) makes use of some measurable property (called thermometric property) that changes with temperature. Different thermometers lead to different temperature scales. To construct a temperature scale, two fixed points are chosen and assigned some arbitrary values of temperature. The two numbers fix the origin of the scale and the size of its unit.','[{"s": "2. A temperature-measuring device (thermometer) makes use of some measurable property (called thermometric property) that changes with temperature. Different thermometers lead to different temperature scales. To construct a temperature scale, two fixed points are chosen and assigned some arbitrary values of temperature. The two numbers fix the origin of the scale and the size of its unit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32828,76,346,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The Celsius temperature ( t C) and the Farenheit temperare ( t F)are related by','[{"s": "3. The Celsius temperature (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C) and the Farenheit temperare (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F)are related by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32829,76,347,'paragraph',NULL,'t F = (9/5) t C + 32','[{"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F = (9/5)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C + 32", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32830,76,348,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The ideal gas equation connecting pressure ( P ), volume ( V ) and absolute temperature ( T ) is :','[{"s": "4. The ideal gas equation connecting pressure (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), volume (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and absolute temperature (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32831,76,349,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = µ RT','[{"s": "PV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= µ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RT", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32832,76,350,'paragraph',NULL,'where µ is the number of moles and R is the universal gas constant.','[{"s": "where µ is the number of moles and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the universal gas constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32833,76,351,'paragraph',NULL,'5. In the absolute temperature scale, the zero of the scale corresponds to the temperature where every substance in nature has the least possible molecular activity. The Kelvin absolute temperature scale ( T ) has the same unit size as the Celsius scale ( T c ), but differs in the origin :','[{"s": "5. In the absolute temperature scale, the zero of the scale corresponds to the temperature where every substance in nature has the least possible molecular activity. The Kelvin absolute temperature scale (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") has the same unit size as the Celsius scale (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c ), but differs in the origin :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32834,76,352,'paragraph',NULL,'T C = T – 273.15','[{"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– 273.15", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32835,76,353,'paragraph',NULL,'6. The coefficient of linear expansion (αl ) and volume expansion (αv ) are defined by the relations :','[{"s": "6. The coefficient of linear expansion (αl ) and volume expansion (αv ) are defined by the relations :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32836,76,354,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image13.png'),
(32837,76,355,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image14.png'),
(32838,76,356,'paragraph',NULL,'where ∆ l and ∆ V denote the change in length l and volume V for a change of temperature ∆ T . The relation between them is :','[{"s": "where ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "denote the change in length", "t": "text"}, {"s": "l", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and volume", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for a change of temperature ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The relation between them is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32839,76,357,'paragraph',NULL,'αv = 3 αl','[{"s": "αv = 3 αl", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32840,76,358,'paragraph',NULL,'7. The specific heat capacity of a substance is defined by','[{"s": "7. The specific heat capacity of a substance is defined by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32841,76,359,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image16.png'),
(32842,76,360,'paragraph',NULL,'where m is the mass of the substance and ∆ Q is the heat required to change its temperature by ∆ T . The molar specific heat capacity of a substance is defined by','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the mass of the substance and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the heat required to change its temperature by ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The molar specific heat capacity of a substance is defined by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32843,76,361,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image18.png'),
(32844,76,362,'paragraph',NULL,'where µ is the number of moles of the substance.','[{"s": "where µ is the number of moles of the substance.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32845,76,363,'paragraph',NULL,'8. The latent heat of fusion ( L f) is the heat per unit mass required to change a substance from solid into liquid at the same temperature and pressure. The latent heat of vaporisation ( L v) is the heat per unit mass required to change a substance from liquid to the vapour state without change in the temperature and pressure.','[{"s": "8. The latent heat of fusion (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f) is the heat per unit mass required to change a substance from solid into liquid at the same temperature and pressure. The latent heat of vaporisation (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v) is the heat per unit mass required to change a substance from liquid to the vapour state without change in the temperature and pressure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32846,76,364,'paragraph',NULL,'9. The three modes of heat transfer are conduction, convection and radiation.','[{"s": "9. The three modes of heat transfer are conduction, convection and radiation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32847,76,365,'paragraph',NULL,'10. In conduction, heat is transferred between neighbouring parts of a body through molecular collisions, without any flow of matter. For a bar of length L and uniform cross section A with its ends maintained at temperatures T C and T D, the rate of flow of heat H is :','[{"s": "10. In conduction, heat is transferred between neighbouring parts of a body through molecular collisions, without any flow of matter. For a bar of length", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and uniform cross section", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with its ends maintained at temperatures", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D, the rate of flow of heat", "t": "text"}, {"s": "H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32848,76,366,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image20.png'),
(32849,76,367,'paragraph',NULL,'where K is the thermal conductivity of the material of the bar.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the thermal conductivity of the material of the bar.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32851,76,369,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image22.png'),
(32852,76,370,'paragraph',NULL,'Where T 1 is the temperature of the surrounding medium and T 2 is the temperature of the body.','[{"s": "Where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1 is the temperature of the surrounding medium and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2 is the temperature of the body.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32853,76,371,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image24.png'),
(32854,76,372,'heading',4,'Points to Ponder',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32855,76,373,'paragraph',NULL,'1. The relation connecting Kelvin temperature (T ) and the Celsius temperature','[{"s": "1. The relation connecting Kelvin temperature (T ) and the Celsius temperature", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32856,76,374,'paragraph',NULL,'t c.','[{"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32857,76,375,'paragraph',NULL,'T = t c + 273.15','[{"s": "T =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 273.15", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32858,76,376,'paragraph',NULL,'and the assignment T = 273.16 K for the triple point of water are exact relations (by choice). With this choice, the Celsius temperature of the melting point of water and boiling point of water (both at 1 atm pressure) are very close to, but not exactly equal to 0 °C and 100 °C respectively. In the original Celsius scale, these latter fixed points were exactly at 0 °C and 100 °C (by choice), but now the triple point of water is the preferred choice for the fixed point because it has a unique temperature.','[{"s": "and the assignment T = 273.16 K for the triple point of water are exact relations (by choice). With this choice, the Celsius temperature of the melting point of water and boiling point of water (both at 1 atm pressure) are very close to, but not exactly equal to 0 °C and 100 °C respectively. In the original Celsius scale, these latter fixed points were exactly at 0 °C and 100 °C (by choice), but now the triple point of water is the preferred choice for the fixed point because it has a unique temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32859,76,377,'paragraph',NULL,'2. A liquid in equilibrium with vapour has the same pressure and temperature throughout the system; the two phases in equilibrium differ in their molar volume (i.e. density). This is true for a system with any number of phases in equilibrium.','[{"s": "2. A liquid in equilibrium with vapour has the same pressure and temperature throughout the system; the two phases in equilibrium differ in their molar volume (i.e. density). This is true for a system with any number of phases in equilibrium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32860,76,378,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Heat transfer always involves temperature difference between two systems or two parts of the same system. Any energy transfer that does not involve temperature difference in some way is not heat.','[{"s": "3. Heat transfer always involves temperature difference between two systems or two parts of the same system. Any energy transfer that does not involve temperature difference in some way is not heat.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32861,76,379,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Convection involves flow of matter within a fluid due to unequal temperatures of its parts. A hot bar placed under a running tap loses heat by conduction between the surface of the bar and water and not by convection within water.','[{"s": "4. Convection involves flow of matter within a fluid due to unequal temperatures of its parts. A hot bar placed under a running tap loses heat by conduction between the surface of the bar and water and not by convection within water.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32862,76,380,'heading',4,'Exercises',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32863,76,381,'paragraph',NULL,'11.1 The triple points of neon and carbon dioxide are 24.57 K and 216.55 K respectively. Express these temperatures on the Celsius and Fahrenheit scales.','[{"s": "11.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The triple points of neon and carbon dioxide are 24.57 K and 216.55 K respectively. Express these temperatures on the Celsius and Fahrenheit scales.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32864,76,382,'paragraph',NULL,'11.2 Two absolute scales A and B have triple points of water defined to be 200 A and 350 B. What is the relation between T A and T B ?','[{"s": "11.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two absolute scales A and B have triple points of water defined to be 200 A and 350 B. What is the relation between", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(32865,76,383,'paragraph',NULL,'11.3 The electrical resistance in ohms of a certain thermometer varies with temperature according to the approximate law: R=R o [1+α(T−T o )]','[{"s": "11.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The electrical resistance in ohms of a certain thermometer varies with temperature according to the approximate law:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R=R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[1+α(T−T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32866,76,384,'paragraph',NULL,'The resistance is 101.6 Ω at the triple-point of water 273.16 K, and 165.5 Ω at the normal melting point of lead (600.5 K). What is the temperature when the resistance is 123.4 Ω?','[{"s": "The resistance is 101.6 Ω at the triple-point of water 273.16 K, and 165.5 Ω at the normal melting point of lead (600.5 K). What is the temperature when the resistance is 123.4 Ω?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32867,76,385,'paragraph',NULL,'11.4 Answer the following :','[{"s": "11.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Answer the following :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32868,76,386,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) The triple-point of water is a standard fixed point in modern thermometry. Why? What is wrong in taking the melting point of ice and the boiling point of water as standard fixed points (as was originally done in the Celsius scale)?','[{"s": "(a) The triple-point of water is a standard fixed point in modern thermometry. Why? What is wrong in taking the melting point of ice and the boiling point of water as standard fixed points (as was originally done in the Celsius scale)?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32869,76,387,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) There were two fixed points in the original Celsius scale as mentioned above which were assigned the number 0 °C and 100 °C respectively. On the absolute scale, one of the fixed points is the triple-point of water, which on the Kelvin absolute scale is assigned the number 273.16 K. What is the other fixed point on this (Kelvin) scale?','[{"s": "(b) There were two fixed points in the original Celsius scale as mentioned above which were assigned the number 0 °C and 100 °C respectively. On the absolute scale, one of the fixed points is the triple-point of water, which on the Kelvin absolute scale is assigned the number 273.16 K. What is the other fixed point on this (Kelvin) scale?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32870,76,388,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The absolute temperature (Kelvin scale) T is related to the temperature t c on the Celsius scale by t c = T – 273.15.','[{"s": "(c) The absolute temperature (Kelvin scale) T is related to the temperature", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on the Celsius scale by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= T – 273.15.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32871,76,389,'paragraph',NULL,'Why do we have 273.15 in this relation, and not 273.16?','[{"s": "Why do we have 273.15 in this relation, and not 273.16?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32872,76,390,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) What is the temperature of the triple-point of water on an absolute scale whose unit interval size is equal to that of the Fahrenheit scale?','[{"s": "(d) What is the temperature of the triple-point of water on an absolute scale whose unit interval size is equal to that of the Fahrenheit scale?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32873,76,391,'paragraph',NULL,'11.5 Two ideal gas thermometers A and B use oxygen and hydrogen respectively. The following observations are made :','[{"s": "11.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two ideal gas thermometers A and B use oxygen and hydrogen respectively. The following observations are made :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32874,76,392,'paragraph',NULL,'Triple-point of water','[{"s": "Triple-point of water", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32875,76,393,'paragraph',NULL,'1.250×10 5 Pa','[{"s": "1.250×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Pa", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32876,76,394,'paragraph',NULL,'0.200×10 5 Pa','[{"s": "0.200×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Pa", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32877,76,395,'paragraph',NULL,'1.797×10 5 Pa','[{"s": "1.797×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Pa", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32878,76,396,'paragraph',NULL,'0.28×10 5 Pa','[{"s": "0.28×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Pa", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32879,76,397,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) What is the absolute temperature of normal melting point of sulphur as read by thermometers A and B?','[{"s": "(a) What is the absolute temperature of normal melting point of sulphur as read by thermometers A and B?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32880,76,398,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) What do you think is the reason behind the slight difference in answers of thermometers A and B? (The thermometers are not faulty). What further procedure is needed in the experiment to reduce the discrepancy between the two readings?','[{"s": "(b) What do you think is the reason behind the slight difference in answers of thermometers A and B? (The thermometers are not faulty). What further procedure is needed in the experiment to reduce the discrepancy between the two readings?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32881,76,399,'paragraph',NULL,'11.6 A steel tape 1 m long is correctly calibrated for a temperature of 27.0 °C. The length of a steel rod measured by this tape is found to be 63.0 cm on a hot day when the temperature is 45.0 °C. What is the actual length of the steel rod on that day? What is the length of the same steel rod on a day when the temperature is 27.0 °C? Coefficient of linear expansion of steel = 1.20 × 10 −5 K −1 .','[{"s": "11.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A steel tape 1 m long is correctly calibrated for a temperature of 27.0 °C. The length of a steel rod measured by this tape is found to be 63.0 cm on a hot day when the temperature is 45.0 °C. What is the actual length of the steel rod on that day? What is the length of the same steel rod on a day when the temperature is 27.0 °C? Coefficient of linear expansion of steel = 1.20 ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32882,76,400,'paragraph',NULL,'11.7 A large steel wheel is to be fitted on to a shaft of the same material. At 27 °C, the outer diameter of the shaft is 8.70 cm and the diameter of the central hole in the wheel is 8.69 cm. The shaft is cooled using ‘dry ice’. At what temperature of the shaft does the wheel slip on the shaft? Assume the coefficient of linear expansion of the steel to be constant over the required temperature range : α steel = 1.20×10 −5 K −1 .','[{"s": "11.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A large steel wheel is to be fitted on to a shaft of the same material. At 27 °C, the outer diameter of the shaft is 8.70 cm and the diameter of the central hole in the wheel is 8.69 cm. The shaft is cooled using ‘dry ice’. At what temperature of the shaft does the wheel slip on the shaft? Assume the coefficient of linear expansion of the steel to be constant over the required temperature range :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "α", "t": "text"}, {"s": "steel", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.20×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32883,76,401,'paragraph',NULL,'11.8 A hole is drilled in a copper sheet. The diameter of the hole is 4.24 cm at 27.0 °C. What is the change in the diameter of the hole when the sheet is heated to 227 °C? Coefficient of linear expansion of copper = 1.70 × 10 −5 K −1 .','[{"s": "11.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A hole is drilled in a copper sheet. The diameter of the hole is 4.24 cm at 27.0 °C. What is the change in the diameter of the hole when the sheet is heated to 227 °C? Coefficient of linear expansion of copper = 1.70 ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32884,76,402,'paragraph',NULL,'11.9 A brass wire 1.8 m long at 27 °C is held taut with little tension between two rigid supports. If the wire is cooled to a temperature of –39 °C, what is the tension developed in the wire, if its diameter is 2.0 mm? Co-efficient of linear expansion of brass = 2.0×10 −5 K −1 ; Young’s modulus of brass = 0.91 × 10 11 Pa .','[{"s": "11.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A brass wire 1.8 m long at 27 °C is held taut with little tension between two rigid supports. If the wire is cooled to a temperature of –39 °C, what is the tension developed in the wire, if its diameter is 2.0 mm? Co-efficient of linear expansion of brass =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2.0×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; Young’s modulus of brass =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0.91 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Pa", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(32885,76,403,'paragraph',NULL,'11.10 A brass rod of length 50 cm and diameter 3.0 mm is joined to a steel rod of the same length and diameter. What is the change in length of the combined rod at 250 °C, if the original lengths are at 40.0 °C? Is there a ‘thermal stress’ developed at the junction? The ends of the rod are free to expand. (Co-efficient of linear expansion of brass = 2.0×10 −5 K −1 , steel = 1.2×10 −5 K −1 ).','[{"s": "11.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A brass rod of length 50 cm and diameter 3.0 mm is joined to a steel rod of the same length and diameter. What is the change in length of the combined rod at 250 °C, if the original lengths are at 40.0 °C? Is there a ‘thermal stress’ developed at the junction? The ends of the rod are free to expand. (Co-efficient of linear expansion of brass =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2.0×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", steel =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1.2×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
