(33110,77,89,'paragraph',NULL,'As Table 12.1 shows, the experimentally measured values which generally agrees with predicted value 3R at ordinary temperatures. (Carbon is an exception.) The agreement is known to break down at low temperatures.','[{"s": "As Table 12.1 shows, the experimentally measured values which generally agrees with predicted value 3R at ordinary temperatures. (Carbon is an exception.) The agreement is known to break down at low temperatures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33111,77,90,'paragraph',NULL,'Specific heat capacity of water','[{"s": "Specific heat capacity of water", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33112,77,91,'paragraph',NULL,'The old unit of heat was calorie. One calorie was earlier defined to be the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of 1g of water by 1°C. With more precise measurements, it was found that the specific heat of water varies slightly with temperature. Figure 12.5 shows this variation in the temperature range 0 to 100 °C.','[{"s": "The old unit of heat was calorie. One calorie was earlier defined to be the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of 1g of water by 1°C. With more precise measurements, it was found that the specific heat of water varies slightly with temperature. Figure 12.5 shows this variation in the temperature range 0 to 100 °C.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33113,77,92,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermodynamics-image13.png'),
(33114,77,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 12.5 Variation of specific heat capacity of water with temperature.','[{"s": "Fig. 12.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Variation of specific heat capacity of water with temperature.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33115,77,94,'paragraph',NULL,'For a precise definition of calorie, it was, therefore, necessary to specify the unit temperature interval. One calorie is defined to be the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of 1g of water from 14.5 °C to 15.5°C. Since heat is just a form of energy, it is preferable to use the unit joule, J. In SI units, the specific heat capacity of water is 4186 J kg–1 K–1 i.e. 4.186 J g–1 K–1. The so called mechanical equivalent of heat defined as the amount of work needed to produce 1 cal of heat is in fact just a conversion factor between two different units of energy : calorie to joule. Since in SI units, we use the unit joule for heat, work or any other form of energy, the term mechanical equivalent is now superfluous and need not be used.','[{"s": "For a precise definition of calorie, it was, therefore, necessary to specify the unit temperature interval. One calorie is defined to be the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of 1g of water from 14.5 °C to 15.5°C. Since heat is just a form of energy, it is preferable to use the unit joule, J. In SI units, the specific heat capacity of water is 4186 J kg–1 K–1 i.e. 4.186 J g–1 K–1. The so called mechanical equivalent of heat defined as the amount of work needed to produce 1 cal of heat is in fact just a conversion factor between two different units of energy : calorie to joule. Since in SI units, we use the unit joule for heat, work or any other form of energy, the term mechanical equivalent is now superfluous and need not be used.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33117,77,96,'paragraph',NULL,'Cp – Cv = R (12.8)','[{"s": "Cp – Cv = R (12.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33118,77,97,'paragraph',NULL,'where Cp and Cv are molar specific heat capacities of an ideal gas at constant pressure and volume respectively and R is the universal gas constant. To prove the relation, we begin with Eq. (12.3) for 1 mole of the gas :','[{"s": "where Cp and Cv are molar specific heat capacities of an ideal gas at constant pressure and volume respectively and R is the universal gas constant. To prove the relation, we begin with Eq. (12.3) for 1 mole of the gas :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33119,77,98,'paragraph',NULL,'∆Q = ∆U + P ∆V','[{"s": "∆Q = ∆U + P ∆V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33120,77,99,'paragraph',NULL,'If ∆Q is absorbed at constant volume, ∆V = 0','[{"s": "If ∆Q is absorbed at constant volume, ∆V = 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
