(33285,77,264,'paragraph',NULL,'which generally agrees with experiment at ordinary temperatures.','[{"s": "which generally agrees with experiment at ordinary temperatures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33286,77,265,'paragraph',NULL,'Calorie is the old unit of heat. 1 calorie is the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of 1 g of water from 14.5 °C to 15.5 °C. 1 cal = 4.186 J.','[{"s": "Calorie is the old unit of heat. 1 calorie is the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of 1 g of water from 14.5 °C to 15.5 °C. 1 cal = 4.186 J.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33287,77,266,'paragraph',NULL,'5. For an ideal gas, the molar specific heat capacities at constant pressure and volume satisfy the relation','[{"s": "5. For an ideal gas, the molar specific heat capacities at constant pressure and volume satisfy the relation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33288,77,267,'paragraph',NULL,'Cp – Cv = R','[{"s": "Cp – Cv = R", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33289,77,268,'paragraph',NULL,'where R is the universal gas constant.','[{"s": "where R is the universal gas constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33290,77,269,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Equilibrium states of a thermodynamic system are described by state variables. The value of a state variable depends only on the particular state, not on the path used to arrive at that state. Examples of state variables are pressure (P ), volume (V ), temperature (T ), and mass (m ). Heat and work are not state variables. An Equation of State (like the ideal gas equation PV = µ RT ) is a relation connecting different state variables.','[{"s": "6. Equilibrium states of a thermodynamic system are described by state variables. The value of a state variable depends only on the particular state, not on the path used to arrive at that state. Examples of state variables are pressure (P ), volume (V ), temperature (T ), and mass (m ). Heat and work are not state variables. An Equation of State (like the ideal gas equation PV = µ RT ) is a relation connecting different state variables.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33291,77,270,'paragraph',NULL,'7. A quasi-static process is an infinitely slow process such that the system remains in thermal and mechanical equilibrium with the surroundings throughout. In a quasi-static process, the pressure and temperature of the environment can differ from those of the system only infinitesimally.','[{"s": "7. A quasi-static process is an infinitely slow process such that the system remains in thermal and mechanical equilibrium with the surroundings throughout. In a quasi-static process, the pressure and temperature of the environment can differ from those of the system only infinitesimally.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33292,77,271,'paragraph',NULL,'8. In an isothermal expansion of an ideal gas from volume V1 to V2 at temperature T the heat absorbed (Q) equals the work done (W ) by the gas, each given by','[{"s": "8. In an isothermal expansion of an ideal gas from volume V1 to V2 at temperature T the heat absorbed (Q) equals the work done (W ) by the gas, each given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
