(33302,77,281,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermodynamics-image66.png'),
(33303,77,282,'paragraph',NULL,'12. The second law of thermodynamics disallows some processes consistent with the First Law of Thermodynamics. It states','[{"s": "12. The second law of thermodynamics disallows some processes consistent with the First Law of Thermodynamics. It states", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33304,77,283,'paragraph',NULL,'Kelvin-Planck statement','[{"s": "Kelvin-Planck statement", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33305,77,284,'paragraph',NULL,'No process is possible whose sole result is the absorption of heat from a reservoir and complete conversion of the heat into work.','[{"s": "No process is possible whose sole result is the absorption of heat from a reservoir and complete conversion of the heat into work.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33306,77,285,'paragraph',NULL,'Clausius statement','[{"s": "Clausius statement", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33307,77,286,'paragraph',NULL,'No process is possible whose sole result is the transfer of heat from a colder object to a hotter object.','[{"s": "No process is possible whose sole result is the transfer of heat from a colder object to a hotter object.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33308,77,287,'paragraph',NULL,'Put simply, the Second Law implies that no heat engine can have efficiency η equal to 1 or no refrigerator can have co-efficient of performance α equal to infinity.','[{"s": "Put simply, the Second Law implies that no heat engine can have efficiency η equal to 1 or no refrigerator can have co-efficient of performance α equal to infinity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(30841,74,219,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.25 (a) A typical illuminated p-n junction solar cell; (b) I-V characteristics of a solar cell.','[{"s": "Figure 14.25 (a) A typical illuminated p-n junction solar cell;", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "(b) I-V characteristics of a solar cell.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046308", "2000046316", "2000046320", "2000046443", "2000046500", "2000046865", "2000046870", "2000046954", "2000046959", "2000046961", "2000046965", "2000046971", "2000046987", "2000046993", "2000047112", "2000047113", "2000047115", "2000112899", "2000112916", "2000112955", "2000112956"]',NULL),
(33309,77,288,'paragraph',NULL,'13. A process is reversible if it can be reversed such that both the system and the surroundings return to their original states, with no other change anywhere else in the universe. Spontaneous processes of nature are irreversible. The idealised reversible process is a quasi-static process with no dissipative factors such as friction, viscosity, etc.','[{"s": "13. A process is reversible if it can be reversed such that both the system and the surroundings return to their original states, with no other change anywhere else in the universe. Spontaneous processes of nature are irreversible. The idealised reversible process is a quasi-static process with no dissipative factors such as friction, viscosity, etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33311,77,290,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermodynamics-image55.png (Carnot engine)','[{"s": "Thermodynamics-image55.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(Carnot engine)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33312,77,291,'paragraph',NULL,'No engine operating between two temperatures can have efficiency greater than that of the Carnot engine.','[{"s": "No engine operating between two temperatures can have efficiency greater than that of the Carnot engine.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33313,77,292,'paragraph',NULL,'15. If Q > 0, heat is added to the system','[{"s": "15. If Q > 0, heat is added to the system", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33314,77,293,'paragraph',NULL,'If Q < 0, heat is removed to the system','[{"s": "If Q < 0, heat is removed to the system", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33315,77,294,'paragraph',NULL,'If W > 0, Work is done by the system','[{"s": "If W > 0, Work is done by the system", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33316,77,295,'paragraph',NULL,'If W < 0, Work is done on the system','[{"s": "If W < 0, Work is done on the system", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33317,77,296,'heading',4,'Points to ponder',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33318,77,297,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Temperature of a body is related to its average internal energy, not to the kinetic energy of motion of its centre of mass. A bullet fired from a gun is not at a higher temperature because of its high speed.','[{"s": "1. Temperature of a body is related to its average internal energy, not to the kinetic energy of motion of its centre of mass. A bullet fired from a gun is not at a higher temperature because of its high speed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33319,77,298,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Equilibrium in thermodynamics refers to the situation when macroscopic variables describing the thermodynamic state of a system do not depend on time. Equilibrium of a system in mechanics means the net external force and torque on the system are zero.','[{"s": "2. Equilibrium in thermodynamics refers to the situation when macroscopic variables describing the thermodynamic state of a system do not depend on time. Equilibrium of a system in mechanics means the net external force and torque on the system are zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33320,77,299,'paragraph',NULL,'3. In a state of thermodynamic equilibrium, the microscopic constituents of a system are not in equilibrium (in the sense of mechanics).','[{"s": "3. In a state of thermodynamic equilibrium, the microscopic constituents of a system are not in equilibrium (in the sense of mechanics).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33321,77,300,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Heat capacity, in general, depends on the process the system goes through when heat is supplied.','[{"s": "4. Heat capacity, in general, depends on the process the system goes through when heat is supplied.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33322,77,301,'paragraph',NULL,'5. In isothermal quasi-static processes, heat is absorbed or given out by the system even though at every stage the gas has the same temperature as that of the surrounding reservoir. This is possible because of the infinitesimal difference in temperature between the system and the reservoir.','[{"s": "5. In isothermal quasi-static processes, heat is absorbed or given out by the system even though at every stage the gas has the same temperature as that of the surrounding reservoir. This is possible because of the infinitesimal difference in temperature between the system and the reservoir.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33323,77,302,'image',NULL,'ttt',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Thermodynamics-image36.png'),
(33324,77,303,'heading',4,'Exercises',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33325,77,304,'paragraph',NULL,'12.1 A geyser heats water flowing at the rate of 3.0 litres per minute from 27 °C to 77 °C. If the geyser operates on a gas burner, what is the rate of consumption of the fuel if its heat of combustion is 4.0 × 10 4 J/g?','[{"s": "12.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A geyser heats water flowing at the rate of 3.0 litres per minute from 27 °C to 77 °C. If the geyser operates on a gas burner, what is the rate of consumption of the fuel if its heat of combustion is 4.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J/g?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33326,77,305,'paragraph',NULL,'12.2 What amount of heat must be supplied to 2.0 × 10 −2 kg of nitrogen (at room temperature) to raise its temperature by 4°C at constant pressure? (Molecular mass of N 2 = 28; R = 8.3 J mol −1 K −1 )','[{"s": "12.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What amount of heat must be supplied to 2.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg of nitrogen (at room temperature) to raise its temperature by 4°C at constant pressure? (Molecular mass of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 28; R = 8.3 J", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mol", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33327,77,306,'paragraph',NULL,'12.3 Explain why','[{"s": "12.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Explain why", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33328,77,307,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Two bodies at different temperatures T 1 and T 2 , if brought in thermal contact, do not necessarily settle to the mean temperature ( T 1 +T 2/2 ) .','[{"s": "(a) Two bodies at different temperatures T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", if brought in thermal contact, do not necessarily settle to the mean temperature", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33329,77,308,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The coolant in a chemical or a nuclear plant (i.e., the liquid used to prevent the different parts of a plant from getting too hot) should have high specific heat.','[{"s": "(b) The coolant in a chemical or a nuclear plant (i.e., the liquid used to prevent the different parts of a plant from getting too hot) should have high specific heat.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33330,77,309,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Air pressure in a car tyre increases during driving.','[{"s": "(c) Air pressure in a car tyre increases during driving.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33331,77,310,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) The climate of a harbour town is more temperate than that of a town in a desert at the same latitude.','[{"s": "(d) The climate of a harbour town is more temperate than that of a town in a desert at the same latitude.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33332,77,311,'paragraph',NULL,'12.4 A cylinder with a movable piston contains 3 moles of hydrogen at standard temperature and pressure. The walls of the cylinder are made of a heat insulator, and the piston is insulated by having a pile of sand on it. By what factor does the pressure of the gas increase if the gas is compressed to half of its original volume?','[{"s": "12.4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A cylinder with a movable piston contains 3 moles of hydrogen at standard temperature and pressure. The walls of the cylinder are made of a heat insulator, and the piston is insulated by having a pile of sand on it. By what factor does the pressure of the gas increase if the gas is compressed to half of its original volume?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33334,77,313,'paragraph',NULL,'12.6 Two cylinders A and B of equal capacity are connected to each other via a stopcock. A contains a gas at standard temperature and pressure. B is completely evacuated. The entire system is thermally insulated. The stopcock is suddenly opened. Answer the following :','[{"s": "12.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two cylinders A and B of equal capacity are connected to each other via a stopcock. A contains a gas at standard temperature and pressure. B is completely evacuated. The entire system is thermally insulated. The stopcock is suddenly opened. Answer the following :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33335,77,314,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) What is the final pressure of the gas in A and B?','[{"s": "(a) What is the final pressure of the gas in A and B?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33336,77,315,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) What is the change in the internal energy of the gas?','[{"s": "(b) What is the change in the internal energy of the gas?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33337,77,316,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) What is the change in the temperature of the gas?','[{"s": "(c) What is the change in the temperature of the gas?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33338,77,317,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Do the intermediate states of the system (before settling to the final equilibrium state) lie on its P-V-T surface?','[{"s": "(d) Do the intermediate states of the system (before settling to the final equilibrium state) lie on its P-V-T surface?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33339,77,318,'paragraph',NULL,'12.7 A steam engine delivers 5.4×10 8 J of work per minute and services 3.6 × 10 9 J of heat per minute from its boiler. What is the efficiency of the engine? How much heat is wasted per minute?','[{"s": "12.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A steam engine delivers 5.4×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of work per minute and services 3.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J of heat per minute from its boiler. What is the efficiency of the engine? How much heat is wasted per minute?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33340,77,319,'paragraph',NULL,'12.8 An electric heater supplies heat to a system at a rate of 100W. If the system performs work at a rate of 75 joules per second. At what rate is the internal energy increasing?','[{"s": "12.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An electric heater supplies heat to a system at a rate of 100W. If the system performs work at a rate of 75 joules per second. At what rate is the internal energy increasing?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
