(33475,78,36,'paragraph',NULL,'We employ units for some physical quantities that can be derived from the seven base units (Appendix A 6). Some derived units in terms of the SI base units are given in (Appendix A 6.1). Some SI derived units are given special names (Appendix A 6.2 ) and some derived SI units make use of these units with special names and the seven base units (Appendix A 6.3). These are given in Appendix A 6.2 and A 6.3 for your ready reference. Other units retained for general use are given in Table 2.2.','[{"s": "We employ units for some physical quantities that can be derived from the seven base units (Appendix A 6). Some derived units in terms of the SI base units are given in (Appendix A 6.1). Some SI derived units are given special names (Appendix A 6.2 ) and some derived SI units make use of these units with special names and the seven base units (Appendix A 6.3). These are given in Appendix A 6.2 and A 6.3 for your ready reference. Other units retained for general use are given in Table 2.2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33476,78,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Common SI prefixes and symbols for multiples and sub-multiples are given in Appendix A2. General guidelines for using symbols for physical quantities, chemical elements and nuclides are given in Appendix A7 and those for SI units and some other units are given in Appendix A8 for your guidance and ready reference.','[{"s": "Common SI prefixes and symbols for multiples and sub-multiples are given in Appendix A2. General guidelines for using symbols for physical quantities, chemical elements and nuclides are given in Appendix A7 and those for SI units and some other units are given in Appendix A8 for your guidance and ready reference.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33477,78,38,'heading',2,'2.3 MEASUREMENT OF LENGTH',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33478,78,39,'paragraph',NULL,'You are already familiar with some direct methods for the measurement of length. For example, a metre scale is used for lengths from 10 –3 m to 10 2 m. A vernier callipers is used for lengths to an accuracy of 10 –4 m. A screw gauge and a spherometer can be used to measure lengths as less as to 10 –5 m. To measure lengths beyond these ranges, we make use of some special indirect methods.','[{"s": "You are already familiar with some direct methods for the measurement of length. For example, a metre scale is used for lengths from 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m to 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. A vernier callipers is used for lengths to an accuracy of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. A screw gauge and a spherometer can be used to measure lengths as less as to 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. To measure lengths beyond these ranges, we make use of some special indirect methods.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33479,78,40,'heading',3,'2.3.1 Measurement of Large Distances',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33480,78,41,'paragraph',NULL,'Large distances such as the distance of a planet or a star from the earth cannot be measured directly with a metre scale. An important method in such cases is the parallax method .','[{"s": "Large distances such as the distance of a planet or a star from the earth cannot be measured directly with a metre scale. An important method in such cases is the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallax method", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33481,78,42,'paragraph',NULL,'When you hold a pencil in front of you against some specific point on the background (a wall) and look at the pencil first through your left eye A (closing the right eye) and then look at the pencil through your right eye B (closing the left eye), you would notice that the position of the pencil seems to change with respect to the point on the wall. This is called parallax . The distance between the two points of observation is called the basis . In this example, the basis is the distance between the eyes.','[{"s": "When you hold a pencil in front of you against some specific point on the background (a wall) and look at the pencil first through your left eye A (closing the right eye) and then look at the pencil through your right eye B (closing the left eye), you would notice that the position of the pencil seems to change with respect to the point on the wall. This is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallax", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The distance between the two points of observation is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "basis", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In this example, the basis is the distance between the eyes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(33482,78,43,'paragraph',NULL,'To measure the distance D of a faraway planet S by the parallax method, we observe it from two different positions (observatories) A and B on the Earth, separated by distance AB = b at the same time as shown in Fig. 2.2. We measure the angle between the two directions along which the planet is viewed at these two points. The ∠ASB in Fig. 2.2 represented by symbol θ is called the parallax angle or parallactic angle .','[{"s": "To measure the distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of a faraway planet S by the parallax method, we observe it from two different positions (observatories) A and B on the Earth, separated by distance AB =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at the same time as shown in Fig. 2.2. We measure the angle between the two directions along which the planet is viewed at these two points. The ∠ASB in Fig. 2.2 represented by symbol θ is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallax angle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallactic angle", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33483,78,44,'paragraph',NULL,'As the planet is very far away,b/D<<1, and therefore, θ is very small. Then we approximately take AB as an arc of length b of a circle with centre at S and the distance D as the radius AS = BS so that AB = b = D θ where θ is in radians.','[{"s": "As the planet is very far away,b/D<<1, and therefore,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is very small. Then we approximately take AB as an arc of length", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of a circle with centre at S and the distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as the radius AS = BS so that AB =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ where θ is in radians.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33484,78,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Units_And_Measurements-image27.png (2.1)','[{"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image27.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.1)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33485,78,46,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image60.png'),
(33486,78,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 2.2 Parallax method.','[{"s": "Fig. 2.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Parallax method.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33487,78,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Having determined D , we can employ a similar method to determine the size or angular diameter of the planet. If d is the diameter of the planet and α the angular size of the planet (the angle subtended by d at the earth), we have','[{"s": "Having determined", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we can employ a similar method to determine the size or angular diameter of the planet. If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the diameter of the planet and α the angular size of the planet (the angle subtended by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at the earth), we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33488,78,49,'paragraph',NULL,'α = d/D (2.2)','[{"s": "α =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d/D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33489,78,50,'paragraph',NULL,'The angle α can be measured from the same location on the earth. It is the angle between the two directions when two diametrically opposite points of the planet are viewed through the telescope. Since D is known, the diameter d of the planet can be determined using Eq. (2.2).','[{"s": "The angle α can be measured from the same location on the earth. It is the angle between the two directions when two diametrically opposite points of the planet are viewed through the telescope. Since", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is known, the diameter", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the planet can be determined using Eq. (2.2).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33491,78,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33492,78,53,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) We have 360 0 = 2π rad','[{"s": "(a) We have 360", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2π rad", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33493,78,54,'paragraph',NULL,'1 0 = (π /180) rad = 1.745×10 –2 rad','[{"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (π /180) rad = 1.745×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rad", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33494,78,55,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) 1 0 = 60′ = 1.745×10 –2 rad','[{"s": "(b) 1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 60′ = 1.745×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rad", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33495,78,56,'paragraph',NULL,'1′ = 2.908×10 –4 rad ≅ 2.91×10 –4 rad','[{"s": "1′ = 2.908×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rad ≅ 2.91×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rad", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33496,78,57,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) 1′ = 60″ = 2.908×10 –4 rad','[{"s": "(c) 1′ = 60″ = 2.908×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rad", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33497,78,58,'paragraph',NULL,'1″ = 4.847×10 –4 rad ≅ 4.85×10 –6 rad','[{"s": "1″ = 4.847×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rad ≅ 4.85×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rad", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33498,78,59,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.2 A man wishes to estimate the distance of a nearby tower from him. He stands at a point A in front of the tower C and spots a very distant object O in line with AC. He then walks perpendicular to AC up to B, a distance of 100 m, and looks at O and C again. Since O is very distant, the direction BO is practically the same as AO; but he finds the line of sight of C shifted from the original line of sight by an angle θ = 40 0 (θ is known as ‘parallax’) estimate the distance of the tower C from his original position A.','[{"s": "Example 2.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A man wishes to estimate the distance of a nearby tower from him. He stands at a point A in front of the tower C and spots a very distant object O in line with AC. He then walks perpendicular to AC up to B, a distance of 100 m, and looks at O and C again. Since O is very distant, the direction BO is practically the same as AO; but he finds the line of sight of C shifted from the original line of sight by an angle θ = 40", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(θ is known as ‘parallax’) estimate the distance of the tower C from his original position A.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33499,78,60,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image19.png'),
(33500,78,61,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 2.3','[{"s": "Fig. 2.3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33501,78,62,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33502,78,63,'paragraph',NULL,'We have, parallax angle θ = 40 0','[{"s": "We have, parallax angle θ = 40", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33503,78,64,'paragraph',NULL,'From Fig. 2.3, AB = AC tan θ','[{"s": "From Fig. 2.3, AB = AC tan θ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33504,78,65,'paragraph',NULL,'AC = AB/tanθ = 100 m/tan 40 0','[{"s": "AC = AB/tanθ = 100 m/tan 40", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33505,78,66,'paragraph',NULL,'= 100 m/0.8391 = 119 m','[{"s": "= 100 m/0.8391 = 119 m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33506,78,67,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.3 The moon is observed from two diametrically opposite points A and B on Earth. The angle θ subtended at the moon by the two directions of observation is 1 o 54′. Given the diameter of the Earth to be about 1.276 × 10 7 m, compute the distance of the moon from the Earth.','[{"s": "Example 2.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The moon is observed from two diametrically opposite points A and B on Earth. The angle θ subtended at the moon by the two directions of observation is 1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "54′. Given the diameter of the Earth to be about 1.276 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m, compute the distance of the moon from the Earth.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33507,78,68,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33508,78,69,'paragraph',NULL,'We have θ = 1° 54′ = 114′','[{"s": "We have θ = 1° 54′ = 114′", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
