(33524,78,85,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image36.png'),
(33525,78,86,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image1.png'),
(33526,78,87,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image61.png'),
(33527,78,88,'heading',3,'2.3.2 Estimation of Very Small Distances: Size of a Molecule',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33528,78,89,'paragraph',NULL,'To measure a very small size, like that of a molecule (10 –8 m to 10 –10 -10 m). Hence an optical microscope cannot resolve particles with sizes smaller than this. Instead of visible light, we can use an electron beam. Electron beams can be focussed by properly designed electric and magnetic fields. The resolution of such an electron microscope is limited finally by the fact that electrons can also behave as waves ! (You will learn more about this in class XII). The wavelength of an electron can be as small as a fraction of an angstrom. Such electron microscopes with a resolution of 0.6 Å have been built. They can almost resolve atoms and molecules in a material. In recent times, tunnelling microscopy has been developed in which again the limit of resolution is better than an angstrom. It is possible to estimate the sizes of molecules.','[{"s": "To measure a very small size, like that of a molecule (10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m to 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "-10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m). Hence an optical microscope cannot resolve particles with sizes smaller than this. Instead of visible light, we can use an electron beam. Electron beams can be focussed by properly designed electric and magnetic fields. The resolution of such an electron microscope is limited finally by the fact that electrons can also behave as waves ! (You will learn more about this in class XII). The wavelength of an electron can be as small as a fraction of an angstrom. Such electron microscopes with a resolution of 0.6 Å have been built. They can almost resolve atoms and molecules in a material. In recent times, tunnelling microscopy has been developed in which again the limit of resolution is better than an angstrom. It is possible to estimate the sizes of molecules.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33529,78,90,'paragraph',NULL,'A simple method for estimating the molecular size of oleic acid is given below. Oleic acid is a soapy liquid with large molecular size of the order of 10 –9 m.','[{"s": "A simple method for estimating the molecular size of oleic acid is given below. Oleic acid is a soapy liquid with large molecular size of the order of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33530,78,91,'paragraph',NULL,'The idea is to first form mono-molecular layer of oleic acid on water surface.','[{"s": "The idea is to first form mono-molecular layer of oleic acid on water surface.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1106,11,86,'paragraph',NULL,'These are membrane bound vesicular structures formed by the process of packaging in the golgi apparatus. The isolated lysosomal vesicles have been found to be very rich in almost all types of hydrolytic enzymes (hydrolases – lipases, proteases, carbohydrases) optimally active at the acidic pH.These enzymes are capable of digesting carbohydrates, proteins, lipids and nucleic acids.','[{"s": "These are membrane bound vesicular structures formed by the process of packaging in the golgi apparatus. The isolated lysosomal vesicles have been found to be very rich in almost all types of hydrolytic enzymes (hydrolases – lipases, proteases, carbohydrases) optimally active at the acidic pH.These enzymes are capable of digesting carbohydrates, proteins, lipids and nucleic acids.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["80915", "80919", "80924", "80928"]',NULL),
(33531,78,92,'paragraph',NULL,'We dissolve 1 cm 3 of oleic acid in alcohol to make a solution of 20 cm 3 . Then we take 1 cm 3 of this solution and dilute it to 20 cm 3 , using alcohol. So, the concentration of the solution is equal to Units_And_Measurements-image56.png of oleic acid/cm 3 of solution. Next we lightly sprinkle some lycopodium powder on the surface of water in a large trough and we put one drop of this solution in the water. The oleic acid drop spreads into a thin, large and roughly circular film of molecular thickness on water surface. Then, we quickly measure the diameter of the thin film to get its area A . Suppose we have dropped n drops in the water. Initially, we determine the approximate volume of each drop ( V cm 3 ).','[{"s": "We dissolve 1 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of oleic acid in alcohol to make a solution of 20 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Then we take 1 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of this solution and dilute it to 20 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", using alcohol. So, the concentration of the solution is equal to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image56.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "of oleic acid/cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of solution. Next we lightly sprinkle some lycopodium powder on the surface of water in a large trough and we put one drop of this solution in the water. The oleic acid drop spreads into a thin, large and roughly circular film of molecular thickness on water surface. Then, we quickly measure the diameter of the thin film to get its area", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Suppose we have dropped", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "drops in the water. Initially, we determine the approximate volume of each drop (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33532,78,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Volume of n drops of solution','[{"s": "Volume of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "drops of solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33533,78,94,'paragraph',NULL,'= nV cm 3','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nV", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33534,78,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Amount of oleic acid in this solution','[{"s": "Amount of oleic acid in this solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33535,78,96,'paragraph',NULL,'= Units_And_Measurements-image16.png','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image16.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33536,78,97,'paragraph',NULL,'This solution of oleic acid spreads very fast on the surface of water and forms a very thin layer of thickness t . If this spreads to form a film of area A cm 2 , then the thickness of the film','[{"s": "This solution of oleic acid spreads very fast on the surface of water and forms a very thin layer of thickness", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If this spreads to form a film of area", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", then the thickness of the film", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33537,78,98,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image34.png'),
(33538,78,99,'paragraph',NULL,'or, Units_And_Measurements-image31.png (2.3)','[{"s": "or,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image31.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(2.3)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33539,78,100,'paragraph',NULL,'If we assume that the film has mono-molecular thickness, then this becomes the size or diameter of a molecule of oleic acid. The value of this thickness comes out to be of the order of 10 –9 m.','[{"s": "If we assume that the film has mono-molecular thickness, then this becomes the size or diameter of a molecule of oleic acid. The value of this thickness comes out to be of the order of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33540,78,101,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.5 If the size of a nucleus (in the range of 10 –15 to 10 –14 m) is scaled up to the tip of a sharp pin, what roughly is the size of an atom ? Assume tip of the pin to be in the range 10 –5 m to 10 –4 m.','[{"s": "Example 2.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If the size of a nucleus (in the range of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m) is scaled up to the tip of a sharp pin, what roughly is the size of an atom ? Assume tip of the pin to be in the range 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m to 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33541,78,102,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The size of a nucleus is in the range of 10 –15 m and 10 –14 m. The tip of a sharp pin is taken to be in the range of 10 –5 m and 10 –4 m. Thus we are scaling up by a factor of 10 10 . An atom roughly of size 10 –10 m will be scaled up to a size of 1 m. Thus a nucleus in an atom is as small in size as the tip of a sharp pin placed at the centre of a sphere of radius about a metre long. t','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The size of a nucleus is in the range of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m and 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. The tip of a sharp pin is taken to be in the range of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m and 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. Thus we are scaling up by a factor of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". An atom roughly of size 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m will be scaled up to a size of 1 m. Thus a nucleus in an atom is as small in size as the tip of a sharp pin placed at the centre of a sphere of radius about a metre long. t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33542,78,103,'heading',3,'2.3.3 Range of Lengths',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33543,78,104,'paragraph',NULL,'The sizes of the objects we come across in the universe vary over a very wide range. These may vary from the size of the order of 10 –14 m of the tiny nucleus of an atom to the size of the order of 10 26 m of the extent of the observable universe. Table 2.3 gives the range and order of lengths and sizes of some of these objects.','[{"s": "The sizes of the objects we come across in the universe vary over a very wide range. These may vary from the size of the order of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m of the tiny nucleus of an atom to the size of the order of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "26", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m of the extent of the observable universe. Table 2.3 gives the range and order of lengths and sizes of some of these objects.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33544,78,105,'paragraph',NULL,'We also use certain special length units for short and large lengths. These are','[{"s": "We also use certain special length units for short and large lengths. These are", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33545,78,106,'paragraph',NULL,'1 fermi = 1 f = 10 –15 m','[{"s": "1 fermi = 1 f = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33546,78,107,'paragraph',NULL,'1 angstrom = 1 Å = 10 –10 m','[{"s": "1 angstrom = 1 Å = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33547,78,108,'paragraph',NULL,'1 astronomical unit = 1 AU (average distance of the Sun from the Earth)','[{"s": "1 astronomical unit = 1 AU (average distance of the Sun from the Earth)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33548,78,109,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1.496 × 10 11 m','[{"s": "= 1.496 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33549,78,110,'paragraph',NULL,'1 light year = 1 ly = 9.46 × 10 15 m (distance that light travels with velocity of 3 × 10 8 m s –1 in 1 year)','[{"s": "1 light year = 1 ly = 9.46 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m (distance that light travels with velocity of 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in 1 year)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33550,78,111,'paragraph',NULL,'1 parsec = 3.08 × 10 16 m (Parsec is the distance at which average radius of earth’s orbit subtends an angle of 1 arc second)','[{"s": "1 parsec = 3.08 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m (Parsec is the distance at which average radius of earth’s orbit subtends an angle of 1 arc second)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33551,78,112,'heading',2,'2.4 MEASUREMENT OF MASS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33552,78,113,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass is a basic property of matter. It does not depend on the temperature, pressure or location of the object in space. The SI unit of mass is kilogram (kg). The prototypes of the International standard kilogram supplied by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures (BIPM) are available in many other laboratories of different countries. In India, this is available at the National Physical Laboratory (NPL), New Delhi.','[{"s": "Mass is a basic property of matter. It does not depend on the temperature, pressure or location of the object in space. The SI unit of mass is kilogram (kg). The prototypes of the International standard kilogram supplied by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures (BIPM) are available in many other laboratories of different countries. In India, this is available at the National Physical Laboratory (NPL), New Delhi.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33553,78,114,'paragraph',NULL,'While dealing with atoms and molecules, the kilogram is an inconvenient unit. In this case, there is an important standard unit of mass, called the unified atomic mass unit (u), which has been established for expressing the mass of atoms as','[{"s": "While dealing with atoms and molecules, the kilogram is an inconvenient unit. In this case, there is an important standard unit of mass, called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "unified atomic mass unit", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(u), which has been established for expressing the mass of atoms as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33554,78,115,'paragraph',NULL,'1 unified atomic mass unit = 1u','[{"s": "1 unified atomic mass unit = 1u", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33555,78,116,'paragraph',NULL,'= (1/12) of the mass of an atom of carbon-12 isotope Units_And_Measurements-image62.png including the mass of electrons','[{"s": "= (1/12) of the mass of an atom of carbon-12 isotope", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image62.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "including the mass of electrons", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33556,78,117,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1.66 × 10 –27 kg','[{"s": "= 1.66 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–27", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33557,78,118,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass of commonly available objects can be determined by a common balance like the one used in a grocery shop. Large masses in the universe like planets, stars, etc., based on Newton’s law of gravitation can be measured by using gravitational method (See Chapter 8). For measurement of small masses of atomic/sub-atomic particles etc., we make use of mass spectrograph in which radius of the trajectory is proportional to the mass of a charged particle moving in uniform electric and magnetic field.','[{"s": "Mass of commonly available objects can be determined by a common balance like the one used in a grocery shop. Large masses in the universe like planets, stars, etc., based on Newton’s law of gravitation can be measured by using gravitational method (See Chapter 8). For measurement of small masses of atomic/sub-atomic particles etc., we make use of mass spectrograph in which radius of the trajectory is proportional to the mass of a charged particle moving in uniform electric and magnetic field.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33558,78,119,'heading',3,'2.4.1 Range of Masses',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33559,78,120,'paragraph',NULL,'The masses of the objects, we come across in the universe, vary over a very wide range. These may vary from tiny mass of the order of 10 -30 kg of an electron to the huge mass of about 10 55 kg of the known universe. Table 2.4 gives the range and order of the typical masses of various objects.','[{"s": "The masses of the objects, we come across in the universe, vary over a very wide range. These may vary from tiny mass of the order of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg of an electron to the huge mass of about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "55", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg of the known universe. Table 2.4 gives the range and order of the typical masses of various objects.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33560,78,121,'heading',5,'Table 2.3 Range and order of lengths',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33561,78,122,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image35.png'),
(33562,78,123,'heading',5,'Table 2.4 Range and order of masses',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33563,78,124,'heading',2,'2.5 MEASUREMENT OF TIME',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33564,78,125,'paragraph',NULL,'To measure any time interval we need a clock. We now use an atomic standard of time , which is based on the periodic vibrations produced in a cesium atom. This is the basis of the cesium clock , sometimes called atomic clock , used in the national standards. Such standards are available in many laboratories. In the cesium atomic clock, the second is taken as the time needed for 9,192,631,770 vibrations of the radiation corresponding to the transition between the two hyperfine levels of the ground state of cesium-133 atom. The vibrations of the cesium atom regulate the rate of this cesium atomic clock just as the vibrations of a balance wheel regulate an ordinary wristwatch or the vibrations of a small quartz crystal regulate a quartz wristwatch.','[{"s": "To measure any time interval we need a clock. We now use an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atomic standard of time", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which is based on the periodic vibrations produced in a cesium atom. This is the basis of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cesium", "t": "text"}, {"s": "clock", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", sometimes called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atomic clock", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", used in the national standards. Such standards are available in many laboratories. In the cesium atomic clock, the second is taken as the time needed for 9,192,631,770 vibrations of the radiation corresponding to the transition between the two hyperfine levels of the ground state of cesium-133 atom. The vibrations of the cesium atom regulate the rate of this cesium atomic clock just as the vibrations of a balance wheel regulate an ordinary wristwatch or the vibrations of a small quartz crystal regulate a quartz wristwatch.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33565,78,126,'paragraph',NULL,'The cesium atomic clocks are very accurate. In principle they provide portable standard. The national standard of time interval ‘second’ as well as the frequency is maintained through four cesium atomic clocks. A cesium atomic clock is used at the National Physical Laboratory (NPL), New Delhi to maintain the Indian standard of time.','[{"s": "The cesium atomic clocks are very accurate. In principle they provide portable standard. The national standard of time interval ‘second’ as well as the frequency is maintained through four cesium atomic clocks. A cesium atomic clock is used at the National Physical Laboratory (NPL), New Delhi to maintain the Indian standard of time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33566,78,127,'paragraph',NULL,'In our country, the NPL has the responsibility of maintenance and improvement of physical standards, including that of time, frequency, etc. Note that the Indian Standard Time (IST) is linked to this set of atomic clocks. The efficient cesium atomic clocks are so accurate that they impart the uncertainty in time realisation as ± 1 × 10 –13 , i.e. 1 part in 10 13 . This implies that the uncertainty gained over time by such a device is less than 1 part in 10 13 ; they lose or gain no more than 3 µs in one year. In view of the tremendous accuracy in time measurement, the SI unit of length has been expressed in terms the path length light travels in certain interval of time (1/299, 792, 458 of a second) (Table 2.1).','[{"s": "In our country, the NPL has the responsibility of maintenance and improvement of physical standards, including that of time, frequency, etc. Note that the Indian Standard Time (IST) is linked to this set of atomic clocks. The efficient cesium atomic clocks are so accurate that they impart the uncertainty in time realisation as ± 1 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–13", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", i.e. 1 part in 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "13", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This implies that the uncertainty gained over time by such a device is less than 1 part in 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; they lose or gain no more than 3 µs in one year. In view of the tremendous accuracy in time measurement, the SI unit of length has been expressed in terms the path length light travels in certain interval of time (1/299, 792, 458 of a second) (Table 2.1).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33567,78,128,'paragraph',NULL,'The time interval of events that we come across in the universe vary over a very wide range. Table 2.5 gives the range and order of some typical time intervals.','[{"s": "The time interval of events that we come across in the universe vary over a very wide range. Table 2.5 gives the range and order of some typical time intervals.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33568,78,129,'paragraph',NULL,'You may notice that there is an interesting coincidence between the numbers appearing in Tables 2.3 and 2.5. Note that the ratio of the longest and shortest lengths of objects in our universe is about 10 41 . Interestingly enough, the ratio of the longest and shortest time intervals associated with the events and objects in our universe is also about 10 41 . This number, 10 41 comes up again in Table 2.4, which lists typical masses of objects. The ratio of the largest and smallest masses of the objects in our universe is about (10 41 ) 2 . Is this a curious coincidence between these large numbers purely accidental ?','[{"s": "You may notice that there is an interesting coincidence between the numbers appearing in Tables 2.3 and 2.5. Note that the ratio of the longest and shortest lengths of objects in our universe is about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "41", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Interestingly enough, the ratio of the longest and shortest time intervals associated with the events and objects in our universe is also about 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "41", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This number, 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "41", "t": "text"}, {"s": "comes up again in Table 2.4, which lists typical masses of objects. The ratio of the largest and smallest masses of the objects in our universe is about (10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "41", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Is this a curious coincidence between these large numbers purely accidental ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33569,78,130,'heading',2,'2.6 ACCURACY, PRECISION OF INSTRUMENTS AND ERRORS IN MEASUREMENT',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33570,78,131,'paragraph',NULL,'Measurement is the foundation of all experimental science and technology. The result of every measurement by any measuring instrument contains some uncertainty. This uncertainty is called error . Every calculated quantity which is based on measured values, also has an error. We shall distinguish between two terms: accuracy and precision . The accuracy of a measurement is a measure of how close the measured value is to the true value of the quantity. Precision tells us to what resolution or limit the quantity is measured.','[{"s": "Measurement is the foundation of all experimental science and technology. The result of every measurement by any measuring instrument contains some uncertainty. This uncertainty is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "error", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Every calculated quantity which is based on measured values, also has an error. We shall distinguish between two terms:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "accuracy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "precision", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The accuracy of a measurement is a measure of how close the measured value is to the true value of the quantity. Precision tells us to what resolution or limit the quantity is measured.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33571,78,132,'paragraph',NULL,'The accuracy in measurement may depend on several factors, including the limit or the resolution of the measuring instrument. For example, suppose the true value of a certain length is near 3.678 cm. In one experiment, using a measuring instrument of resolution 0.1 cm, the measured value is found to be 3.5 cm, while in another experiment using a measuring device of greater resolution, say 0.01 cm, the length is determined to be 3.38 cm. The first measurement has more accuracy (because it is closer to the true value) but less precision (its resolution is only 0.1 cm), while the second measurement is less accurate but more precise. Thus every measurement is approximate due to errors in measurement. In general, the errors in measurement can be broadly classified as (a) systematic errors and (b) random errors.','[{"s": "The accuracy in measurement may depend on several factors, including the limit or the resolution of the measuring instrument. For example, suppose the true value of a certain length is near 3.678 cm. In one experiment, using a measuring instrument of resolution 0.1 cm, the measured value is found to be 3.5 cm, while in another experiment using a measuring device of greater resolution, say 0.01 cm, the length is determined to be 3.38 cm. The first measurement has more accuracy (because it is closer to the true value) but less precision (its resolution is only 0.1 cm), while the second measurement is less accurate but more precise. Thus every measurement is approximate due to errors in measurement. In general, the errors in measurement can be broadly classified as (a) systematic errors and (b) random errors.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33572,78,133,'heading',5,'Table 2.5 Range and order of time intervals',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33573,78,134,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image15.png'),
(33574,78,135,'paragraph',NULL,'Systematic errors','[{"s": "Systematic errors", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33575,78,136,'paragraph',NULL,'The systematic errors are those errors that tend to be in one direction, either positive or negative. Some of the sources of systematic errors are :','[{"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "systematic errors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are those errors that tend to be in one direction, either positive or negative. Some of the sources of systematic errors are :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33576,78,137,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Instrumental errors that arise from the errors due to imperfect design or calibration of the measuring instrument, zero error in the instrument, etc. For example, the temperature graduations of a thermometer may be inadequately calibrated (it may read 104 °C at the boiling point of water at STP whereas it should read 100 °C); in a vernier callipers the zero mark of vernier scale may not coincide with the zero mark of the main scale, or simply an ordinary metre scale may be worn off at one end.','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Instrumental errors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "that arise from the errors due to imperfect design or calibration of the measuring instrument, zero error in the instrument, etc. For example, the temperature graduations of a thermometer may be inadequately calibrated (it may read 104 °C at the boiling point of water at STP whereas it should read 100 °C); in a vernier callipers the zero mark of vernier scale may not coincide with the zero mark of the main scale, or simply an ordinary metre scale may be worn off at one end.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33577,78,138,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Imperfection in experimental technique or procedure To determine the temperature of a human body, a thermometer placed under the armpit will always give a temperature lower than the actual value of the body temperature. Other external conditions (such as changes in temperature, humidity, wind velocity, etc.) during the experiment may systematically affect the measurement.','[{"s": "(b)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Imperfection in experimental technique", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "procedure", "t": "text"}, {"s": "To determine the temperature of a human body, a thermometer placed under the armpit will always give a temperature lower than the actual value of the body temperature. Other external conditions (such as changes in temperature, humidity, wind velocity, etc.) during the experiment may systematically affect the measurement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33578,78,139,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Personal errors that arise due to an individual’s bias, lack of proper setting of the apparatus or individual’s carelessness in taking observations without observing proper precautions, etc. For example, if you, by habit, always hold your head a bit too far to the right while reading the position of a needle on the scale, you will introduce an error due to parallax .','[{"s": "(c)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Personal errors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "that arise due to an individual’s bias, lack of proper setting of the apparatus or individual’s carelessness in taking observations without observing proper precautions, etc. For example, if you, by habit, always hold your head a bit too far to the right while reading the position of a needle on the scale, you will introduce an error due to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "parallax", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33579,78,140,'paragraph',NULL,'Systematic errors can be minimised by improving experimental techniques, selecting better instruments and removing personal bias as far as possible. For a given set-up, these errors may be estimated to a certain extent and the necessary corrections may be applied to the readings.','[{"s": "Systematic errors can be minimised by improving experimental techniques, selecting better instruments and removing personal bias as far as possible. For a given set-up, these errors may be estimated to a certain extent and the necessary corrections may be applied to the readings.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33580,78,141,'paragraph',NULL,'Random errors','[{"s": "Random errors", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33582,78,143,'paragraph',NULL,'Least count error','[{"s": "Least count error", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33583,78,144,'paragraph',NULL,'The smallest value that can be measured by the measuring instrument is called its least count . All the readings or measured values are good only up to this value.','[{"s": "The smallest value that can be measured by the measuring instrument is called its", "t": "text"}, {"s": "least count", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". All the readings or measured values are good only up to this value.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33584,78,145,'paragraph',NULL,'The least count error is the error associated with the resolution of the instrument. For example, a vernier callipers has the least count as 0.01cm; a spherometer may have a least count of 0.001 cm. Least count error belongs to the category of random errors but within a limited size; it occurs with both systematic and random errors. If we use a metre scale for measurement of length, it may have graduations at 1 mm division scale spacing or interval.','[{"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "least count error", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the error associated with the resolution of the instrument. For example, a vernier callipers has the least count as 0.01cm; a spherometer may have a least count of 0.001 cm. Least count error belongs to the category of random errors but within a limited size; it occurs with both systematic and random errors. If we use a metre scale for measurement of length, it may have graduations at 1 mm division scale spacing or interval.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33585,78,146,'paragraph',NULL,'Using instruments of higher precision, improving experimental techniques, etc., we can reduce the least count error. Repeating the observations several times and taking the arithmetic mean of all the observations, the mean value would be very close to the true value of the measured quantity.','[{"s": "Using instruments of higher precision, improving experimental techniques, etc., we can reduce the least count error. Repeating the observations several times and taking the arithmetic mean of all the observations, the mean value would be very close to the true value of the measured quantity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33586,78,147,'heading',3,'2.6.1 Absolute Error, Relative Error and Percentage Error',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33587,78,148,'paragraph',NULL,'(a)','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33588,78,149,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose the values obtained in several measurements are a 1 , a 2 , a 3 ...., a n . The arithmetic mean of these values is taken as the best possible value of the quantity under the given conditions of measurement as :','[{"s": "Suppose the values obtained in several measurements are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "...., a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The arithmetic mean of these values is taken as the best possible value of the quantity under the given conditions of measurement as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33589,78,150,'paragraph',NULL,'a mean = (a 1 +a 2 +a 3 +...+a n ) / n (2.4)','[{"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+...+a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") / n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.4)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33590,78,151,'paragraph',NULL,'or,','[{"s": "or,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33591,78,152,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image44.png'),
(33592,78,153,'paragraph',NULL,'(2.5)','[{"s": "(2.5)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33593,78,154,'paragraph',NULL,'This is because, as explained earlier, it is reasonable to suppose that individual measurements are as likely to overestimate as to underestimate the true value of the quantity.','[{"s": "This is because, as explained earlier, it is reasonable to suppose that individual measurements are as likely to overestimate as to underestimate the true value of the quantity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33594,78,155,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitude of the difference between the individual measurement and the true value of the quantity is called the absolute error of the measurement . This is denoted by |∆ a |. In absence of any other method of knowing true value, we considered arithmatic mean as the true value. Then the errors in the individual measurement values from the true value, are','[{"s": "The magnitude of the difference between the individual measurement and the true value of the quantity is called the absolute error of the measurement", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This is denoted by |∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|. In absence of any other method of knowing true value, we considered arithmatic mean as the true value. Then the errors in the individual measurement values from the true value, are", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33595,78,156,'paragraph',NULL,'∆a 1 = a 1 – a mean ,','[{"s": "∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33596,78,157,'paragraph',NULL,'∆a 2 = a 2 – a mean ,','[{"s": "∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33597,78,158,'paragraph',NULL,'.... .... ....','[{"s": ".... .... ....", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33598,78,159,'paragraph',NULL,'.... .... ....','[{"s": ".... .... ....", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33599,78,160,'paragraph',NULL,'∆a n = a n – a mean','[{"s": "∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33600,78,161,'paragraph',NULL,'The ∆a calculated above may be positive in certain cases and negative in some other cases. But absolute error |∆ a | will always be positive.','[{"s": "The ∆a calculated above may be positive in certain cases and negative in some other cases. But absolute error |∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "| will always be positive.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33601,78,162,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The arithmetic mean of all the absolute errors is taken as the final or mean absolute error of the value of the physical quantity a . It is represented by ∆a mean .','[{"s": "(b) The arithmetic mean of all the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "absolute errors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is taken as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "final", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean absolute error", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the value of the physical quantity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is represented by ∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33602,78,163,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus,','[{"s": "Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33603,78,164,'paragraph',NULL,'∆a mean = (|∆ a 1 |+|∆ a 2 |+|∆ a 3 |+...+ |∆ a n |)/ n (2.6)','[{"s": "∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (|∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|+|∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|+|∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|+...+ |∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|)/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.6)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33604,78,165,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image52.png'),
(33605,78,166,'paragraph',NULL,'|∆ a i |/ n (2.7)','[{"s": "|∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.7)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33606,78,167,'paragraph',NULL,'If we do a single measurement, the value we get may be in the range a mean ± ∆a mean','[{"s": "If we do a single measurement, the value we get may be in the range a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33607,78,168,'paragraph',NULL,'i.e. a = a mean ± ∆a mean','[{"s": "i.e. a = a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33608,78,169,'paragraph',NULL,'or,','[{"s": "or,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33609,78,170,'paragraph',NULL,'a mean – ∆a mean ≤ a ≤ a mean + ∆a mean (2.8)','[{"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– ∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≤ a ≤ a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.8)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33610,78,171,'paragraph',NULL,'This implies that any measurement of the physical quantity a is likely to lie between (a mean + ∆a mean ) and (a mean − ∆a mean ).','[{"s": "This implies that any measurement of the physical quantity a is likely to lie between (a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") and (a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "− ∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33611,78,172,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Instead of the absolute error, we often use the relative error or the percentage error (δa). T he relative error is the ratio of the mean absolute error ∆amean to the mean value amean of the quantity measured .','[{"s": "(c) Instead of the absolute error, we often use the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "relative error", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "percentage error", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(δa). T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "he relative error is the ratio of the mean absolute error", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆amean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to the mean value", "t": "text"}, {"s": "amean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the quantity measured", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33612,78,173,'paragraph',NULL,'Relative error = ∆a mean /a mean (2.9)','[{"s": "Relative error = ∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33613,78,174,'paragraph',NULL,'When the relative error is expressed in per cent, it is called the percentage error (δa).','[{"s": "When the relative error is expressed in per cent, it is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "percentage error", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(δa).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33614,78,175,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, Percentage error','[{"s": "Thus, Percentage error", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33615,78,176,'paragraph',NULL,'δa = (∆a mean /a mean ) × 100% (2.10)','[{"s": "δa = (∆a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") × 100% (2.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33616,78,177,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us now consider an example.','[{"s": "Let us now consider an example.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33617,78,178,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.6 Two clocks are being tested against a standard clock located in a national laboratory. At 12:00:00 noon by the standard clock, the readings of the two clocks are :','[{"s": "Example 2.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two clocks are being tested against a standard clock located in a national laboratory. At 12:00:00 noon by the standard clock, the readings of the two clocks are :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33618,78,179,'paragraph',NULL,'Clock 1 Clock 2','[{"s": "Clock 1 Clock 2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33619,78,180,'paragraph',NULL,'Monday 12:00:05 10:15:06','[{"s": "Monday 12:00:05 10:15:06", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33620,78,181,'paragraph',NULL,'Tuesday 12:01:15 10:14:59','[{"s": "Tuesday 12:01:15 10:14:59", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33621,78,182,'paragraph',NULL,'Wednesday 11:59:08 10:15:18','[{"s": "Wednesday 11:59:08 10:15:18", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33622,78,183,'paragraph',NULL,'Thursday 12:01:50 10:15:07','[{"s": "Thursday 12:01:50 10:15:07", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33623,78,184,'paragraph',NULL,'Friday 11:59:15 10:14:53','[{"s": "Friday 11:59:15 10:14:53", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33624,78,185,'paragraph',NULL,'Saturday 12:01:30 10:15:24','[{"s": "Saturday 12:01:30 10:15:24", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33625,78,186,'paragraph',NULL,'Sunday 12:01:19 10:15:11','[{"s": "Sunday 12:01:19 10:15:11", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33626,78,187,'paragraph',NULL,'If you are doing an experiment that requires precision time interval measurements, which of the two clocks will you prefer ?','[{"s": "If you are doing an experiment that requires precision time interval measurements, which of the two clocks will you prefer ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33627,78,188,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33628,78,189,'paragraph',NULL,'The range of variation over the seven days of observations is 162 s for clock 1, and 31 s for clock 2. The average reading of clock 1 is much closer to the standard time than the average reading of clock 2. The important point is that a clock’s zero error is not as significant for precision work as its variation, because a ‘zero-error’ can always be easily corrected. Hence clock 2 is to be preferred to clock 1.','[{"s": "The range of variation over the seven days of observations is 162 s for clock 1, and 31 s for clock 2. The average reading of clock 1 is much closer to the standard time than the average reading of clock 2. The important point is that a clock’s zero error is not as significant for precision work as its variation, because a ‘zero-error’ can always be easily corrected. Hence clock 2 is to be preferred to clock 1.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33629,78,190,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.7 We measure the period of oscillation of a simple pendulum. In successive measurements, the readings turn out to be 2.63 s, 2.56 s, 2.42 s, 2.71s and 2.80 s. Calculate the absolute errors, relative error or percentage error.','[{"s": "Example 2.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We measure the period of oscillation of a simple pendulum. In successive measurements, the readings turn out to be 2.63 s, 2.56 s, 2.42 s, 2.71s and 2.80 s. Calculate the absolute errors, relative error or percentage error.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33630,78,191,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The mean period of oscillation of the pendulum','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The mean period of oscillation of the pendulum", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33631,78,192,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image32.png'),
(33632,78,193,'paragraph',NULL,'= Units_And_Measurements-image30.png','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image30.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33633,78,194,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2.624 s','[{"s": "= 2.624 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33634,78,195,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2.62 s','[{"s": "= 2.62 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33635,78,196,'paragraph',NULL,'As the periods are measured to a resolution of 0.01 s, all times are to the second decimal; it is proper to put this mean period also to the second decimal.','[{"s": "As the periods are measured to a resolution of 0.01 s, all times are to the second decimal; it is proper to put this mean period also to the second decimal.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33636,78,197,'paragraph',NULL,'The errors in the measurements are','[{"s": "The errors in the measurements are", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33637,78,198,'paragraph',NULL,'2.63 s – 2.62 s = 0.01 s','[{"s": "2.63 s – 2.62 s = 0.01 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33638,78,199,'paragraph',NULL,'2.56 s – 2.62 s = – 0.06 s','[{"s": "2.56 s – 2.62 s = – 0.06 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33639,78,200,'paragraph',NULL,'2.42 s – 2.62 s = – 0.20 s','[{"s": "2.42 s – 2.62 s = – 0.20 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33640,78,201,'paragraph',NULL,'2.71 s – 2.62 s = 0.09 s','[{"s": "2.71 s – 2.62 s = 0.09 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33641,78,202,'paragraph',NULL,'2.80 s – 2.62 s = 0.18 s','[{"s": "2.80 s – 2.62 s = 0.18 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33642,78,203,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the errors have the same units as the quantity to be measured.','[{"s": "Note that the errors have the same units as the quantity to be measured.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33643,78,204,'paragraph',NULL,'The arithmetic mean of all the absolute errors (for arithmetic mean, we take only the magnitudes) is','[{"s": "The arithmetic mean of all the absolute errors (for arithmetic mean, we take only the magnitudes) is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33644,78,205,'paragraph',NULL,'∆Τ mean = [(0.01+ 0.06+0.20+0.09+0.18)s]/5','[{"s": "∆Τ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [(0.01+ 0.06+0.20+0.09+0.18)s]/5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33645,78,206,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.54 s/5','[{"s": "= 0.54 s/5", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33646,78,207,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0.11 s','[{"s": "= 0.11 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33647,78,208,'paragraph',NULL,'That means, the period of oscillation of the simple pendulum is (2.62 ± 0.11) s i.e. it lies between (2.62 + 0.11) s and (2.62 – 0.11) s or between 2.73 s and 2.51 s. As the arithmetic mean of all the absolute errors is 0.11 s, there is already an error in the tenth of a second. Hence there is no point in giving the period to a hundredth. A more correct way will be to write','[{"s": "That means, the period of oscillation of the simple pendulum is (2.62 ± 0.11) s i.e. it lies between (2.62 + 0.11) s and (2.62 – 0.11) s or between 2.73 s and 2.51 s. As the arithmetic mean of all the absolute errors is 0.11 s, there is already an error in the tenth of a second. Hence there is no point in giving the period to a hundredth. A more correct way will be to write", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33648,78,209,'paragraph',NULL,'T = 2.6 ± 0.1 s','[{"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.6 ± 0.1 s", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33649,78,210,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the last numeral 6 is unreliable, since it may be anything between 5 and 7. We indicate this by saying that the measurement has two significant figures. In this case, the two significant figures are 2, which is reliable and 6, which has an error associated with it. You will learn more about the significant figures in section 2.7.','[{"s": "Note that the last numeral 6 is unreliable, since it may be anything between 5 and 7. We indicate this by saying that the measurement has two significant figures. In this case, the two significant figures are 2, which is reliable and 6, which has an error associated with it. You will learn more about the significant figures in section 2.7.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33650,78,211,'paragraph',NULL,'For this example, the relative error or the percentage error is','[{"s": "For this example, the relative error or the percentage error is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33651,78,212,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image50.png'),
(33652,78,213,'heading',3,'2.6.2 Combination of Errors',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33653,78,214,'paragraph',NULL,'If we do an experiment involving several measurements, we must know how the errors in all the measurements combine. For example, mass density is obtained by deviding mass by the volume of the substance. If we have errors in the measurement of mass and of the sizes or dimensions, we must know what the error will be in the density of the substance. To make such estimates, we should learn how errors combine in various mathematical operations. For this, we use the following procedure.','[{"s": "If we do an experiment involving several measurements, we must know how the errors in all the measurements combine. For example, mass density is obtained by deviding mass by the volume of the substance. If we have errors in the measurement of mass and of the sizes or dimensions, we must know what the error will be in the density of the substance. To make such estimates, we should learn how errors combine in various mathematical operations. For this, we use the following procedure.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33654,78,215,'heading',4,'How will you measure the length of a line?',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33655,78,216,'paragraph',NULL,'What a naïve question, at this stage, you might say! But what if it is not a straight line? Draw a zigzag line in your copy, or on the blackboard. Well, not too difficult again. You might take a thread, place it along the line, open up the thread, and measure its length.','[{"s": "What a naïve question, at this stage, you might say! But what if it is not a straight line? Draw a zigzag line in your copy, or on the blackboard. Well, not too difficult again. You might take a thread, place it along the line, open up the thread, and measure its length.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(613,5,135,'paragraph',NULL,'A number of enzymes require metal ions for their activity which form coordination bonds with side chains at the active site and at the same time form one or more cordination bonds with the substrate, e.g., zinc is a cofactor for the proteolytic enzyme carboxypeptidase.','[{"s": "A number of enzymes require metal ions for their activity which form coordination bonds with side chains at the active site and at the same time form one or more cordination bonds with the substrate, e.g., zinc is a cofactor for the proteolytic enzyme carboxypeptidase.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(33656,78,217,'paragraph',NULL,'Now imagine that you want to measure the length of a national highway, a river, the railway track between two stations, or the boundary between two states or two nations. If you take a string of length 1 metre or 100 metre, keep it along the line, shift its position every time, the arithmetic of man-hours of labour and expenses on the project is not commensurate with the outcome. Moreover, errors are bound to occur in this enormous task. There is an interesting fact about this. France and Belgium share a common international boundary, whose length mentioned in the official documents of the two countries differs substantially!','[{"s": "Now imagine that you want to measure the length of a national highway, a river, the railway track between two stations, or the boundary between two states or two nations. If you take a string of length 1 metre or 100 metre, keep it along the line, shift its position every time, the arithmetic of man-hours of labour and expenses on the project is not commensurate with the outcome. Moreover, errors are bound to occur in this enormous task. There is an interesting fact about this. France and Belgium share a common international boundary, whose length mentioned in the official documents of the two countries differs substantially!", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33657,78,218,'paragraph',NULL,'Go one step beyond and imagine the coastline where land meets sea. Roads and rivers have fairly mild bends as compared to a coastline. Even so, all documents, including our school books, contain information on the length of the coastline of Gujarat or Andhra Pradesh, or the common boundary between two states, etc. Railway tickets come with the distance between stations printed on them. We have ‘milestones’ all along the roads indicating the distances to various towns. So, how is it done?','[{"s": "Go one step beyond and imagine the coastline where land meets sea. Roads and rivers have fairly mild bends as compared to a coastline. Even so, all documents, including our school books, contain information on the length of the coastline of Gujarat or Andhra Pradesh, or the common boundary between two states, etc. Railway tickets come with the distance between stations printed on them. We have ‘milestones’ all along the roads indicating the distances to various towns. So, how is it done?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33658,78,219,'paragraph',NULL,'One has to decide how much error one can tolerate and optimise cost-effectiveness. If you want smaller errors, it will involve high technology and high costs. Suffice it to say that it requires fairly advanced level of physics, mathematics, engineering and technology. It belongs to the areas of fractals, which has lately become popular in theoretical physics. Even then one doesn’t know how much to rely on the figure that props up, as is clear from the story of France and Belgium. Incidentally, this story of the France-Belgium discrepancy appears on the first page of an advanced Physics book on the subject of fractals and chaos!','[{"s": "One has to decide how much error one can tolerate and optimise cost-effectiveness. If you want smaller errors, it will involve high technology and high costs. Suffice it to say that it requires fairly advanced level of physics, mathematics, engineering and technology. It belongs to the areas of fractals, which has lately become popular in theoretical physics. Even then one doesn’t know how much to rely on the figure that props up, as is clear from the story of France and Belgium. Incidentally, this story of the France-Belgium discrepancy appears on the first page of an advanced Physics book on the subject of fractals and chaos!", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33659,78,220,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Error of a sum or a difference','[{"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Error of a sum or a difference", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33660,78,221,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose two physical quantities A and B have measured values A ± ∆ A , B ± ∆ B respectively where ∆ A and ∆ B are their absolute errors. We wish to find the error ∆ Z in the sum','[{"s": "Suppose two physical quantities", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "have measured values", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively where ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are their absolute errors. We wish to find the error ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the sum", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33661,78,222,'paragraph',NULL,'Z = A + B .','[{"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33662,78,223,'paragraph',NULL,'We have by addition, Z ± ∆ Z','[{"s": "We have by addition,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33663,78,224,'paragraph',NULL,'= ( A ± ∆ A ) + ( B ± ∆ B ).','[{"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A ±", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") + (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33664,78,225,'paragraph',NULL,'The maximum possible error in Z','[{"s": "The maximum possible error in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33665,78,226,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ Z = ∆ A + ∆ B','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33666,78,227,'paragraph',NULL,'For the difference Z = A – B , we have','[{"s": "For the difference", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33667,78,228,'paragraph',NULL,'Z ± ∆ Z = (A ± ∆ A ) – ( B ± ∆ B )','[{"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") – (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33668,78,229,'paragraph',NULL,'= ( A – B ) ± ∆ A ± ∆ B','[{"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") ± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33669,78,230,'paragraph',NULL,'or, ± ∆ Z = ± ∆ A ± ∆ B','[{"s": "or, ± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33670,78,231,'paragraph',NULL,'The maximum value of the error ∆ Z is again ∆ A + ∆ B .','[{"s": "The maximum value of the error ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is again ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33671,78,232,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence the rule : When two quantities are added or subtracted, the absolute error in the final result is the sum of the absolute errors in the individual quantities.','[{"s": "Hence the rule", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When two quantities are added or subtracted, the absolute error in the final result is the sum of the absolute errors in the individual quantities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33672,78,233,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.8 The temperatures of two bodies measured by a thermometer are','[{"s": "Example 2.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The temperatures of two bodies measured by a thermometer are", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33673,78,234,'paragraph',NULL,'t 1 = 20 o C ± 0.5 o C and t 2 = 50 o C ± 0.5 o C.','[{"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 20", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C ± 0.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C and t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 50", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C ± 0.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33674,78,235,'paragraph',NULL,'Calculate the temperature difference and the error theirin.','[{"s": "Calculate the temperature difference and the error theirin.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33675,78,236,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer t ′ = t 2 – t 1 = (50 o C±0.5 o C)– (20 o C±0.5 o C)','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (50", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C±0.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C)– (20", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C±0.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33676,78,237,'paragraph',NULL,'t ′ = 30 o C ± 1 o C','[{"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′ = 30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C ± 1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33677,78,238,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Error of a product or a quotient','[{"s": "(b) Error of a product or a quotient", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33678,78,239,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose Z = AB and the measured values of A and B are A ± ∆ A and B ± ∆ B . Then','[{"s": "Suppose", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the measured values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33679,78,240,'paragraph',NULL,'Z ± ∆ Z = ( A ± ∆ A ) ( B ± ∆ B )','[{"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33680,78,241,'paragraph',NULL,'= AB ± B ∆ A ± A ∆ B ± ∆ A ∆ B .','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "±", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "±", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "± ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33681,78,242,'paragraph',NULL,'Dividing LHS by Z and RHS by AB we have,','[{"s": "Dividing LHS by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and RHS by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "AB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "we have,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(33682,78,243,'paragraph',NULL,'1±(∆ Z/Z ) = 1 ± (∆ A / A ) ± (∆ B / B ) ± (∆ A / A )(∆ B / B ).','[{"s": "1±(∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z/Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = 1 ± (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") ± (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") ± (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")(∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33683,78,244,'paragraph',NULL,'Since ∆ A and ∆ B are small, we shall ignore their product.','[{"s": "Since ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are small, we shall ignore their product.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33684,78,245,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence the maximum relative error','[{"s": "Hence the maximum relative error", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33685,78,246,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ Z / Z = (∆A/A) + (∆B/B).','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (∆A/A) + (∆B/B).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33686,78,247,'paragraph',NULL,'You can easily verify that this is true for division also.','[{"s": "You can easily verify that this is true for division also.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33687,78,248,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence the rule : When two quantities are multiplied or divided, the relative error in the result is the sum of the relative errors in the multipliers.','[{"s": "Hence the rule", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When two quantities are multiplied or divided, the relative error in the result is the sum of the relative errors in the multipliers.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33688,78,249,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.9 The resistance R = V/I where V = (100 ± 5)V and I = (10 ± 0.2)A. Find the percentage error in R.','[{"s": "Example 2.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The resistance R = V/I where V = (100 ± 5)V and I = (10 ± 0.2)A. Find the percentage error in R.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33691,78,252,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer (a)','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33692,78,253,'paragraph',NULL,'The equivalent resistance of series combination','[{"s": "The equivalent resistance of series combination", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33693,78,254,'paragraph',NULL,'R =R 1 + R 2 = (100 ± 3) ohm + (200 ± 4) ohm','[{"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (100 ± 3) ohm + (200 ± 4) ohm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33694,78,255,'paragraph',NULL,'= 300 ± 7 ohm.','[{"s": "= 300 ± 7 ohm.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33695,78,256,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The equivalent resistance of parallel combination','[{"s": "(b) The equivalent resistance of parallel combination", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33696,78,257,'paragraph',NULL,'Units_And_Measurements-image55.png = 66.7 ohm','[{"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image55.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "= 66.7 ohm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33697,78,258,'paragraph',NULL,'Then, from Units_And_Measurements-image13.png','[{"s": "Then, from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33698,78,259,'paragraph',NULL,'we get,','[{"s": "we get,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33699,78,260,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image42.png'),
(33700,78,261,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image5.png'),
(33701,78,262,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image8.png'),
(33702,78,263,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1.8','[{"s": "= 1.8", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33703,78,264,'paragraph',NULL,'Then, Units_And_Measurements-image33.png','[{"s": "Then,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image33.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33704,78,265,'paragraph',NULL,'(Here, ∆R is expresed as 1.8 instead of 2 to keep in confirmity with the rules of significant figures.) t','[{"s": "(Here, ∆R is expresed as 1.8 instead of 2 to keep in confirmity with the rules of significant figures.) t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33705,78,266,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) Error in case of a measured quantity raised to a power','[{"s": "(c) Error in case of a measured quantity raised to a power", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33706,78,267,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose Z = A 2 ,','[{"s": "Suppose", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33707,78,268,'paragraph',NULL,'Then,','[{"s": "Then,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33708,78,269,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ Z / Z = (∆ A / A ) + (∆ A / A ) = 2 (∆ A / A ). Hence, the relative error in A 2 is two times the error in A .','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") + (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = 2 (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Hence, the relative error in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is two times the error in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33709,78,270,'paragraph',NULL,'In general, if Z = A p B q /C r','[{"s": "In general, if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33710,78,271,'paragraph',NULL,'Then,','[{"s": "Then,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33711,78,272,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ Z / Z = p (∆ A / A ) + q (∆ B/B ) + r (∆ C / C ).','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B/B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33712,78,273,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence the rule : The relative error in a physical quantity raised to the power k is the k times the relative error in the individual quantity.','[{"s": "Hence the rule", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The relative error in a physical quantity raised to the power k is the k times the relative error in the individual quantity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33714,78,275,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The relative error in Z is ∆ Z / Z = 4(∆ A / A ) +(1/3) (∆ B/B ) + (∆ C / C ) + (3/2) (∆D/ D ).','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The relative error in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4(∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") +(1/3) (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B/B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") + (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") + (3/2) (∆D/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33716,78,277,'paragraph',NULL,'Units_And_Measurements-image54.png Measured value of L is 20.0 cm known to 1 mm accuracy and time for 100 oscillations of the pendulum is found to be 90 s using a wrist watch of 1 s resolution. What is the accuracy in the determination of g ?','[{"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image54.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "Measured value of L is 20.0 cm known to 1 mm accuracy and time for 100 oscillations of the pendulum is found to be 90 s using a wrist watch of 1 s resolution. What is the accuracy in the determination of g ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33717,78,278,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33718,78,279,'paragraph',NULL,'g = 4π 2 L / T 2','[{"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33719,78,280,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image57.png'),
(33720,78,281,'paragraph',NULL,'and Units_And_Measurements-image22.png . Therefore, Units_And_Measurements-image2.png . The errors in both L and t are the least count errors. Therefore,','[{"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image22.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". Therefore,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image2.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". The errors in both", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are the least count errors. Therefore,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33721,78,282,'paragraph',NULL,'(∆ g/g ) = (∆ L/L ) + 2(∆ T/T )','[{"s": "(∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g/g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L/L", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") + 2(∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T/T", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33722,78,283,'paragraph',NULL,'= Units_And_Measurements-image20.png','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image20.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33723,78,284,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the percentage error in g is','[{"s": "Thus, the percentage error in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33724,78,285,'paragraph',NULL,'100 (∆ g/g ) = 100(∆ L/L ) + 2 × 100 (∆ T/T )','[{"s": "100 (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g/g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") = 100(∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L/L", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") + 2 × 100 (∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T/T", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33725,78,286,'paragraph',NULL,'= 3%','[{"s": "= 3%", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33726,78,287,'heading',2,'2.7 SIGNIFICANT FIGURES',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33727,78,288,'paragraph',NULL,'As discussed above, every measurement involves errors. Thus, the result of measurement should be reported in a way that indicates the precision of measurement. Normally, the reported result of measurement is a number that includes all digits in the number that are known reliably plus the first digit that is uncertain. The reliable digits plus the first uncertain digit are known as significant digits or significant figures . If we say the period of oscillation of a simple pendulum is 1.62 s, the digits 1 and 6 are reliable and certain, while the digit 2 is uncertain. Thus, the measured value has three significant figures. The length of an object reported after measurement to be 287.5 cm has four significant figures, the digits 2, 8, 7 are certain while the digit 5 is uncertain. Clearly, reporting the result of measurement that includes more digits than the significant digits is superfluous and also misleading since it would give a wrong idea about the precision of measurement.','[{"s": "As discussed above, every measurement involves errors. Thus, the result of measurement should be reported in a way that indicates the precision of measurement. Normally, the reported result of measurement is a number that includes all digits in the number that are known reliably plus the first digit that is uncertain. The reliable digits plus the first uncertain digit are known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "significant", "t": "text"}, {"s": "digits", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "significant figures", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If we say the period of oscillation of a simple pendulum is 1.62 s, the digits 1 and 6 are reliable and certain, while the digit 2 is uncertain. Thus, the measured value has three significant figures. The length of an object reported after measurement to be 287.5 cm has four significant figures, the digits 2, 8, 7 are certain while the digit 5 is uncertain. Clearly, reporting the result of measurement that includes more digits than the significant digits is superfluous and also misleading since it would give a wrong idea about the precision of measurement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33728,78,289,'paragraph',NULL,'The rules for determining the number of significant figures can be understood from the following examples. Significant figures indicate, as already mentioned, the precision of measurement which depends on the least count of the measuring instrument. A choice of change of different units does not change the number of significant digits or figures in a measurement. This important remark makes most of the following observations clear:','[{"s": "The rules for determining the number of significant figures can be understood from the following examples. Significant figures indicate, as already mentioned, the precision of measurement which depends on the least count of the measuring instrument.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A choice of change of different units does not change the number of significant digits or figures in a measurement.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This important remark makes most of the following observations clear:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(198,2,98,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 4.13 Examples of Mollusca :(a) Pila (b) Octopus','[{"s": "Figure 4.13 Examples of Mollusca :(a) Pila (b) Octopus", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(33729,78,290,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) For example, the length 2.308 cm has four significant figures. But in different units, the same value can be written as 0.02308 m or 23.08 mm or 23080 µm.','[{"s": "(1) For example, the length 2.308 cm has four significant figures. But in different units, the same value can be written as 0.02308 m or 23.08 mm or 23080 µm.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33730,78,291,'paragraph',NULL,'All these numbers have the same number of significant figures (digits 2, 3, 0, 8), namely four. This shows that the location of decimal point is of no consequence in determining the number of significant figures.','[{"s": "All these numbers have the same number of significant figures (digits 2, 3, 0, 8), namely four. This shows that the location of decimal point is of no consequence in determining the number of significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33731,78,292,'paragraph',NULL,'The example gives the following rules :','[{"s": "The example gives the following rules :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33732,78,293,'paragraph',NULL,'• All the non-zero digits are significant.','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "All the non-zero digits are significant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33733,78,294,'paragraph',NULL,'• All the zeros between two non-zero digits are significant, no matter where the decimal point is, if at all.','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "All the zeros between two non-zero digits are significant, no matter where the decimal point is, if at all.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33734,78,295,'paragraph',NULL,'• If the number is less than 1, the zero(s) on the right of decimal point but to the left of the first non-zero digit are not significant. [In 0 . 00 2308, the underlined zeroes are not significant].','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If the number is less than 1, the zero(s) on the right of decimal point but to the left of the first non-zero digit are not significant.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[In", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "00", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2308, the underlined zeroes are not significant].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33735,78,296,'paragraph',NULL,'• The terminal or trailing zero(s) in a number without a decimal point are not significant.','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The terminal or trailing zero(s) in a number without a decimal point are not significant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33736,78,297,'paragraph',NULL,'[Thus 123 m = 12300 cm = 123000 mm has three significant figures, the trailing zero(s) being not significant.] However, you can also see the next observation.','[{"s": "[Thus 123 m = 12300 cm = 123000 mm has", "t": "text"}, {"s": "three", "t": "text"}, {"s": "significant figures, the trailing zero(s) being not significant.] However, you can also see the next observation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33737,78,298,'paragraph',NULL,'• The trailing zero(s) in a number with a decimal point are significant.','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The trailing zero(s) in a number with a decimal point are significant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33738,78,299,'paragraph',NULL,'[The numbers 3.500 or 0.06900 have four significant figures each.]','[{"s": "[The numbers 3.500 or 0.06900 have four significant figures each.]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33739,78,300,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) There can be some confusion regarding the trailing zero(s). Suppose a length is reported to be 4.700 m. It is evident that the zeroes here are meant to convey the precision of measurement and are, therefore, significant. [If these were not, it would be superfluous to write them explicitly, the reported measurement would have been simply 4.7 m]. Now suppose we change units, then','[{"s": "(2) There can be some confusion regarding the trailing zero(s). Suppose a length is reported to be 4.700 m. It is evident that the zeroes here are meant to convey the precision of measurement and are, therefore, significant. [If these were not, it would be superfluous to write them explicitly, the reported measurement would have been simply 4.7 m]. Now suppose we change units, then", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33740,78,301,'paragraph',NULL,'4.700 m = 470.0 cm = 4700 mm = 0.004700 km','[{"s": "4.700 m = 470.0 cm = 4700 mm = 0.004700 km", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33741,78,302,'paragraph',NULL,'Since the last number has trailing zero(s) in a number with no decimal, we would conclude erroneously from observation (1) above that the number has two significant figures, while in fact, it has four significant figures and a mere change of units cannot change the number of significant figures.','[{"s": "Since the last number has trailing zero(s) in a number with no decimal, we would conclude erroneously from observation (1) above that the number has", "t": "text"}, {"s": "two", "t": "text"}, {"s": "significant figures, while in fact, it has four significant figures and a mere change of units cannot change the number of significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33742,78,303,'paragraph',NULL,'(3) To remove such ambiguities in determining the number of significant figures, the best way is to report every measurement in scientific notation (in the power of 10). In this notation, every number is expressed as a × 10 b , where a is a number between 1 and 10, and b is any positive or negative exponent (or power) of 10. In order to get an approximate idea of the number, we may round off the number a to 1 (for a ≤ 5) and to 10 (for 5< a ≤ 10). Then the number can be expressed approximately as 10 b in which the exponent (or power) b of 10 is called order of magnitude of the physical quantity. When only an estimate is required, the quantity is of the order of 10 b . For example, the diameter of the earth (1.28×10 7 m) is of the order of 10 7 m with the order of magnitude 7. The diameter of hydrogen atom (1.06 ×10 –10 m) is of the order of 10 –10 m, with the order of magnitude –10. Thus, the diameter of the earth is 17 orders of magnitude larger than the hydrogen atom.','[{"s": "(3)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "To remove such ambiguities in determining the number of significant figures, the best way is to report every measurement in scientific notation (in the power of 10).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In this notation, every number is expressed as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "× 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a number between 1 and 10, and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is any positive or negative exponent (or power) of 10. In order to get an approximate idea of the number, we may round off the number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to 1 (for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≤ 5) and to 10 (for 5<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a ≤", "t": "text"}, {"s": "10). Then the number can be expressed approximately as 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in which the exponent (or power) b of 10 is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "order of magnitude", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the physical quantity. When only an estimate is required, the quantity is of the order of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For example, the diameter of the earth (1.28×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m) is of the order of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m with the order of magnitude 7. The diameter of hydrogen atom (1.06 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m) is of the order of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m, with the order of magnitude –10. Thus, the diameter of the earth is 17 orders of magnitude larger than the hydrogen atom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33743,78,304,'paragraph',NULL,'It is often customary to write the decimal after the first digit. Now the confusion mentioned in (a) above disappears :','[{"s": "It is often customary to write the decimal after the first digit. Now the confusion mentioned in (a) above disappears :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33744,78,305,'paragraph',NULL,'4.700 m = 4.700 × 10 2 cm','[{"s": "4.700 m = 4.700 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33745,78,306,'paragraph',NULL,'= 4.700 × 10 3 mm = 4.700 × 10 –3 km','[{"s": "= 4.700 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mm = 4.700 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "km", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33746,78,307,'paragraph',NULL,'The power of 10 is irrelevant to the determination of significant figures. However, all zeroes appearing in the base number in the scientific notation are significant. Each number in this case has four significant figures.','[{"s": "The power of 10 is irrelevant to the determination of significant figures. However, all zeroes appearing in the base number in the scientific notation are significant. Each number in this case has", "t": "text"}, {"s": "four", "t": "text"}, {"s": "significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33747,78,308,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, in the scientific notation, no confusion arises about the trailing zero(s) in the base number a . They are always significant.','[{"s": "Thus, in the scientific notation, no confusion arises about the trailing zero(s) in the base number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". They are always significant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33748,78,309,'paragraph',NULL,'(4) The scientific notation is ideal for reporting measurement. But if this is not adopted, we use the rules adopted in the preceding example :','[{"s": "(4) The scientific notation is ideal for reporting measurement. But if this is not adopted, we use the rules adopted in the preceding example :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33749,78,310,'paragraph',NULL,'• For a number greater than 1, without any decimal, the trailing zero(s) are not significant.','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For a number greater than 1, without any decimal, the trailing zero(s) are not significant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33750,78,311,'paragraph',NULL,'• For a number with a decimal, the trailing zero(s) are significant.','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For a number with a decimal, the trailing zero(s) are significant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33751,78,312,'paragraph',NULL,'(5) The digit 0 conventionally put on the left of a decimal for a number less than 1 (like 0.1250) is never significant. However, the zeroes at the end of such number are significant in a measurement.','[{"s": "(5) The digit 0 conventionally put on the left of a decimal for a number less than 1 (like 0.1250) is never significant. However, the zeroes at the end of such number are significant in a measurement.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33752,78,313,'paragraph',NULL,'(6) The multiplying or dividing factors which are neither rounded numbers nor numbers representing measured values are exact and have infinite number of significant digits. For example in Units_And_Measurements-image17.png or s = 2π r , the factor 2 is an exact number and it can be written as 2.0, 2.00 or 2.0000 as required. Similarly, in Units_And_Measurements-image26.png , n is an exact number.','[{"s": "(6) The multiplying or dividing factors which are neither rounded numbers nor numbers representing measured values are exact and have infinite number of significant digits. For example in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image17.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "or s = 2π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the factor 2 is an exact number and it can be written as 2.0, 2.00 or 2.0000 as required. Similarly, in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image26.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is an exact number.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33753,78,314,'heading',3,'2.7.1 Rules for Arithmetic Operations with Significant Figures',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33754,78,315,'paragraph',NULL,'The result of a calculation involving approximate measured values of quantities (i.e. values with limited number of significant figures) must reflect the uncertainties in the original measured values. It cannot be more accurate than the original measured values themselves on which the result is based. In general, the final result should not have more significant figures than the original data from which it was obtained. Thus, if mass of an object is measured to be, say, 4.237 g (four significant figures) and its volume is measured to be 2.51 cm 3 , then its density, by mere arithmetic division, is 1.68804780876 g/cm 3 upto 11 decimal places. It would be clearly absurd and irrelevant to record the calculated value of density to such a precision when the measurements on which the value is based, have much less precision. The following rules for arithmetic operations with significant figures ensure that the final result of a calculation is shown with the precision that is consistent with the precision of the input measured values :','[{"s": "The result of a calculation involving approximate measured values of quantities (i.e. values with limited number of significant figures) must reflect the uncertainties in the original measured values. It cannot be more accurate than the original measured values themselves on which the result is based. In general, the final result should not have more significant figures than the original data from which it was obtained. Thus, if mass of an object is measured to be, say, 4.237 g (four significant figures) and its volume is measured to be 2.51 cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", then its density, by mere arithmetic division, is 1.68804780876 g/cm", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "upto 11 decimal places. It would be clearly absurd and irrelevant to record the calculated value of density to such a precision when the measurements on which the value is based, have much less precision. The following rules for arithmetic operations with significant figures ensure that the final result of a calculation is shown with the precision that is consistent with the precision of the input measured values :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33755,78,316,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) In multiplication or division, the final result should retain as many significant figures as are there in the original number with the least significant figures.','[{"s": "(1)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In multiplication or division, the final result should retain as many significant figures as are there in the original number with the least significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33756,78,317,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, in the example above, density should be reported to three significant figures.','[{"s": "Thus, in the example above, density should be reported to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "three", "t": "text"}, {"s": "significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33757,78,318,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image39.png'),
(33758,78,319,'paragraph',NULL,'8 m s -1 (one significant figure) and one year (1y = 365.25 d) has 3.1557 × 10 7 s ( five significant figures), the light year is 9.47 × 10 15 m ( three significant figures).','[{"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(one significant figure) and one year (1y = 365.25 d) has 3.1557 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "five", "t": "text"}, {"s": "significant figures), the light year is 9.47 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "three", "t": "text"}, {"s": "significant figures).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33760,78,321,'paragraph',NULL,'For example, the sum of the numbers 436.32 g, 227.2 g and 0.301 g by mere arithmetic addition, is 663.821 g. But the least precise measurement (227.2 g) is correct to only one decimal place. The final result should, therefore, be rounded off to 663.8 g.','[{"s": "For example, the sum of the numbers 436.32 g, 227.2 g and 0.301 g by mere arithmetic addition, is 663.821 g. But the least precise measurement (227.2 g) is correct to only one decimal place. The final result should, therefore, be rounded off to 663.8 g.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33761,78,322,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, the difference in length can be expressed as :','[{"s": "Similarly, the difference in length can be expressed as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33762,78,323,'paragraph',NULL,'0.307 m – 0.304 m = 0.003 m = 3 × 10 –3 m.','[{"s": "0.307 m – 0.304 m = 0.003 m = 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33763,78,324,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that we should not use the rule (1) applicable for multiplication and division and write 664 g as the result in the example of addition and 3.00 × 10 –3 m in the example of subtraction . They do not convey the precision of measurement properly. For addition and subtraction, the rule is in terms of decimal places.','[{"s": "Note that we should not use the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rule", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1) applicable for multiplication and division and write 664 g as the result in the example of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "addition", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 3.00 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m in the example of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "subtraction", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". They do not convey the precision of measurement properly. For addition and subtraction, the rule is in terms of decimal places.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33764,78,325,'heading',3,'2.7.2 Rounding off the Uncertain Digits',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33765,78,326,'paragraph',NULL,'The result of computation with approximate numbers, which contain more than one uncertain digit, should be rounded off. The rules for rounding off numbers to the appropriate significant figures are obvious in most cases. A number 2.74 6 rounded off to three significant figures is 2.75, while the number 2.743 would be 2.74. The rule by convention is that the preceding digit is raised by 1 if the insignificant digit to be dropped (the underlined digit in this case) is more than 5, and is left unchanged if the latter is less than 5 . But what if the number is 2.745 in which the insignificant digit is 5. Here, the convention is that if the preceding digit is even, the insignificant digit is simply dropped and, if it is odd, the preceding digit is raised by 1. Then, the number 2.745 rounded off to three significant figures becomes 2.74. On the other hand, the number 2.735 rounded off to three significant figures becomes 2.74 since the preceding digit is odd.','[{"s": "The result of computation with approximate numbers, which contain more than one uncertain digit, should be rounded off. The rules for rounding off numbers to the appropriate significant figures are obvious in most cases. A number 2.74", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rounded off to three significant figures is 2.75, while the number 2.743 would be 2.74. The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rule", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by convention is that the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "preceding digit is raised by 1 if the insignificant digit to be dropped (the underlined digit in this case) is more than 5, and is left unchanged if the latter is less than 5", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". But what if the number is 2.745 in which the insignificant digit is 5. Here, the convention is that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "if the preceding digit is even, the insignificant digit is simply dropped and, if it is odd, the preceding digit is raised by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1. Then, the number 2.745 rounded off to three significant figures becomes 2.74. On the other hand, the number 2.735 rounded off to three significant figures becomes 2.74 since the preceding digit is odd.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33766,78,327,'paragraph',NULL,'In any involved or complex multi-step calculation, you should retain, in intermediate steps, one digit more than the significant digits and round off to proper significant figures at the end of the calculation. Similarly, a number known to be within many significant figures, such as in 2.99792458 × 10 8 m/s for the speed of light in vacuum, is rounded off to an approximate value 3 × 10 8 m/s , which is often employed in computations. Finally, remember that exact numbers that appear in formulae like 2 π in Units_And_Measurements-image37.png have a large (infinite) number of significant figures. The value of π = 3.1415926.... is known to a large number of significant figures. You may take the value as 3.142 or 3.14 for π, with limited number of significant figures as required in specific cases.','[{"s": "In any involved or complex multi-step calculation, you should retain, in intermediate steps, one digit more than the significant digits and round off to proper significant figures at the end of the calculation. Similarly, a number known to be within many significant figures, such as in 2.99792458 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m/s for the speed of light in vacuum, is rounded off to an approximate value 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m/s , which is often employed in computations. Finally, remember that exact numbers that appear in formulae like 2 π in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image37.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "have a large (infinite) number of significant figures. The value of π = 3.1415926.... is known to a large number of significant figures. You may take the value as 3.142 or 3.14 for π, with limited number of significant figures as required in specific cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33768,78,329,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The number of significant figures in the measured length is 4. The calculated area and the volume should therefore be rounded off to 4 significant figures.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The number of significant figures in the measured length is 4. The calculated area and the volume should therefore be rounded off to 4 significant figures.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33769,78,330,'paragraph',NULL,'Surface area of the cube = 6(7.203) 2 m 2','[{"s": "Surface area of the cube = 6(7.203)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33770,78,331,'paragraph',NULL,'= 311.299254 m 2','[{"s": "= 311.299254 m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33771,78,332,'paragraph',NULL,'= 311.3 m 2','[{"s": "= 311.3 m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33772,78,333,'paragraph',NULL,'Volume of the cube = (7.203) 3 m 3','[{"s": "Volume of the cube = (7.203)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33807,78,368,'paragraph',NULL,'The nature of a physical quantity is described by its dimensions. All the physical quantities represented by derived units can be expressed in terms of some combination of seven fundamental or base quantities. We shall call these base quantities as the seven dimensions of the physical world, which are denoted with square brackets [ ]. Thus, length has the dimension [L], mass [M], time [T], electric current [A], thermodynamic temperature [K], luminous intensity [cd], and amount of substance [mol]. The dimensions of a physical quantity are the powers (or exponents) to which the base quantities are raised to represent that quantity . Note that using the square brackets [ ] round a quantity means that we are dealing with ‘ the dimensions of ’ the quantity.','[{"s": "The nature of a physical quantity is described by its dimensions. All the physical quantities represented by derived units can be expressed in terms of some combination of seven fundamental or base quantities. We shall call these base quantities as the seven dimensions of the physical world, which are denoted with square brackets [ ]. Thus, length has the dimension [L], mass [M], time [T], electric current [A], thermodynamic temperature [K], luminous intensity [cd], and amount of substance [mol].", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The dimensions of a physical quantity are the powers (or exponents) to which the base quantities are raised to represent that quantity", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Note that using the square brackets [ ] round a quantity means that we are dealing with ‘", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the dimensions of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "’", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the quantity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33808,78,369,'paragraph',NULL,'In mechanics, all the physical quantities can be written in terms of the dimensions [L], [M] and [T]. For example, the volume occupied by an object is expressed as the product of length, breadth and height, or three lengths. Hence the dimensions of volume are [L] × [L] × [L] = [L] 3 = [L 3 ]. As the volume is independent of mass and time, it is said to possess zero dimension in mass [M°], zero dimension in time [T°] and three dimensions in length.','[{"s": "In mechanics, all the physical quantities can be written in terms of the dimensions [L], [M] and [T]. For example, the volume occupied by an object is expressed as the product of length, breadth and height, or three lengths. Hence the dimensions of volume are [L] × [L] × [L] = [L]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]. As the volume is independent of mass and time, it is said to possess zero dimension in mass [M°], zero dimension in time [T°] and three dimensions in length.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33809,78,370,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, force, as the product of mass and acceleration, can be expressed as','[{"s": "Similarly, force, as the product of mass and acceleration, can be expressed as", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33810,78,371,'paragraph',NULL,'Force = mass × acceleration','[{"s": "Force = mass × acceleration", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33811,78,372,'paragraph',NULL,'= mass × (length)/(time) 2','[{"s": "= mass × (length)/(time)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33812,78,373,'paragraph',NULL,'The dimensions of force are [M] [L]/[T] 2 = [M L T –2 ]. Thus, the force has one dimension in mass, one dimension in length, and –2 dimensions in time. The dimensions in all other base quantities are zero.','[{"s": "The dimensions of force are [M] [L]/[T]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= [M L T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]. Thus, the force has one dimension in mass, one dimension in length, and –2 dimensions in time. The dimensions in all other base quantities are zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33813,78,374,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that in this type of representation, the magnitudes are not considered. It is the quality of the type of the physical quantity that enters. Thus, a change in velocity, initial velocity, average velocity, final velocity, and speed are all equivalent in this context. Since all these quantities can be expressed as length/time, their dimensions are [L]/[T] or [L T –1 ].','[{"s": "Note that in this type of representation, the magnitudes are not considered. It is the quality of the type of the physical quantity that enters. Thus, a change in velocity, initial velocity, average velocity, final velocity, and speed are all equivalent in this context. Since all these quantities can be expressed as length/time, their dimensions are [L]/[T] or [L T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33814,78,375,'heading',2,'2.9 DIMENSIONAL FORMULAE AND DIMENSIONAL EQUATIONS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33817,78,378,'paragraph',NULL,'[ V ] = [M 0 L 3 T 0 ]','[{"s": "[", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] = [M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33818,78,379,'paragraph',NULL,'[ v ] = [M 0 L T –1 ]','[{"s": "[", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] = [M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33819,78,380,'paragraph',NULL,'[ F ] = [M L T –2 ]','[{"s": "[", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] = [M L T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33820,78,381,'paragraph',NULL,'[ρ] = [M L –3 T 0 ]','[{"s": "[ρ] = [M L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33821,78,382,'paragraph',NULL,'The dimensional equation can be obtained from the equation representing the relations between the physical quantities. The dimensional formulae of a large number and wide variety of physical quantities, derived from the equations representing the relationships among other physical quantities and expressed in terms of base quantities are given in Appendix 9 for your guidance and ready reference.','[{"s": "The dimensional equation can be obtained from the equation representing the relations between the physical quantities. The dimensional formulae of a large number and wide variety of physical quantities, derived from the equations representing the relationships among other physical quantities and expressed in terms of base quantities are given in Appendix 9 for your guidance and ready reference.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33822,78,383,'heading',2,'2.10 DIMENSIONAL ANALYSIS AND ITS APPLICATIONS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33823,78,384,'paragraph',NULL,'The recognition of concepts of dimensions, which guide the description of physical behaviour is of basic importance as only those physical quantities can be added or subtracted which have the same dimensions. A thorough understanding of dimensional analysis helps us in deducing certain relations among different physical quantities and checking the derivation, accuracy and dimensional consistency or homogeneity of various mathematical expressions. When magnitudes of two or more physical quantities are multiplied, their units should be treated in the same manner as ordinary algebraic symbols. We can cancel identical units in the numerator and denominator. The same is true for dimensions of a physical quantity. Similarly, physical quantities represented by symbols on both sides of a mathematical equation must have the same dimensions.','[{"s": "The recognition of concepts of dimensions, which guide the description of physical behaviour is of basic importance as only those physical quantities can be added or subtracted which have the same dimensions. A thorough understanding of dimensional analysis helps us in deducing certain relations among different physical quantities and checking the derivation, accuracy and dimensional consistency or homogeneity of various mathematical expressions. When magnitudes of two or more physical quantities are multiplied, their units should be treated in the same manner as ordinary algebraic symbols. We can cancel identical units in the numerator and denominator. The same is true for dimensions of a physical quantity. Similarly, physical quantities represented by symbols on both sides of a mathematical equation must have the same dimensions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33824,78,385,'heading',3,'2.10.1 Checking the Dimensional Consistency of Equations',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33825,78,386,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitudes of physical quantities may be added together or subtracted from one another only if they have the same dimensions. In other words, we can add or subtract similar physical quantities. Thus, velocity cannot be added to force, or an electric current cannot be subtracted from the thermodynamic temperature. This simple principle called the principle of homogeneity of dimensions in an equation is extremely useful in checking the correctness of an equation. If the dimensions of all the terms are not same, the equation is wrong. Hence, if we derive an expression for the length (or distance) of an object, regardless of the symbols appearing in the original mathematical relation, when all the individual dimensions are simplified, the remaining dimension must be that of length. Similarly, if we derive an equation of speed, the dimensions on both the sides of equation, when simplified, must be of length/time, or [L T –1 ].','[{"s": "The magnitudes of physical quantities may be added together or subtracted from one another only if they have the same dimensions. In other words, we can add or subtract similar physical quantities. Thus, velocity cannot be added to force, or an electric current cannot be subtracted from the thermodynamic temperature. This simple principle called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the principle of homogeneity of dimensions", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in an equation is extremely useful in checking the correctness of an equation. If the dimensions of all the terms are not same, the equation is wrong. Hence, if we derive an expression for the length (or distance) of an object, regardless of the symbols appearing in the original mathematical relation, when all the individual dimensions are simplified, the remaining dimension must be that of length. Similarly, if we derive an equation of speed, the dimensions on both the sides of equation, when simplified, must be of length/time, or [L T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33826,78,387,'paragraph',NULL,'Dimensions are customarily used as a preliminary test of the consistency of an equation, when there is some doubt about the correctness of the equation. However, the dimensional consistency does not guarantee correct equations. It is uncertain to the extent of dimensionless quantities or functions. The arguments of special functions, such as the trigonometric, logarithmic and exponential functions must be dimensionless. A pure number, ratio of similar physical quantities, such as angle as the ratio (length/length), refractive index as the ratio (speed of light in vacuum/speed of light in medium) etc., has no dimensions.','[{"s": "Dimensions are customarily used as a preliminary test of the consistency of an equation, when there is some doubt about the correctness of the equation. However, the dimensional consistency does not guarantee correct equations. It is uncertain to the extent of dimensionless quantities or functions. The arguments of special functions, such as the trigonometric, logarithmic and exponential functions must be dimensionless. A pure number, ratio of similar physical quantities, such as angle as the ratio (length/length), refractive index as the ratio (speed of light in vacuum/speed of light in medium) etc., has no dimensions.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33828,78,389,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Units_And_Measurements-image9.png'),
