(33914,78,475,'paragraph',NULL,'2.8 Answer the following :','[{"s": "2.8", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Answer the following :", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031059", "2000031150", "2000032114", "2000032728", "2000032810", "2000106041", "2000106059"]',NULL),
(33930,78,491,'paragraph',NULL,'The percentage errors of measurement in a, b, c and d are 1%, 3%, 4% and 2%, respectively. What is the percentage error in the quantity P ? If the value of P calculated using the above relation turns out to be 3.763, to what value should you round off the result ?','[{"s": "The percentage errors of measurement in a, b, c and d are 1%, 3%, 4% and 2%, respectively. What is the percentage error in the quantity P ? If the value of P calculated using the above relation turns out to be 3.763, to what value should you round off the result ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33931,78,492,'paragraph',NULL,'2.14 A book with many printing errors contains four different formulas for the displacement y of a particle undergoing a certain periodic motion :','[{"s": "2.14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A book with many printing errors contains four different formulas for the displacement y of a particle undergoing a certain periodic motion :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33932,78,493,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) y = a sin 2π t/T','[{"s": "(a) y = a sin 2π t/T", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33933,78,494,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) y = a sin vt','[{"s": "(b) y = a sin vt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33934,78,495,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) y = (a/T) sin t/a','[{"s": "(c) y = (a/T) sin t/a", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33935,78,496,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Units_And_Measurements-image59.png','[{"s": "(d)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image59.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33936,78,497,'paragraph',NULL,'(a = maximum displacement of the particle, v = speed of the particle. T = time-period of motion). Rule out the wrong formulas on dimensional grounds.','[{"s": "(a = maximum displacement of the particle, v = speed of the particle. T = time-period of motion). Rule out the wrong formulas on dimensional grounds.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33937,78,498,'paragraph',NULL,'2.15 A famous relation in physics relates ‘moving mass’ m to the ‘rest mass’ mo of a particle in terms of its speed v and the speed of light, c. (This relation first arose as a consequence of special relativity due to Albert Einstein). A boy recalls the relation almost correctly but forgets where to put the constant c. He writes :','[{"s": "2.15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A famous relation in physics relates ‘moving mass’ m to the ‘rest mass’ mo of a particle in terms of its speed v and the speed of light, c. (This relation first arose as a consequence of special relativity due to Albert Einstein). A boy recalls the relation almost correctly but forgets where to put the constant c. He writes :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33938,78,499,'paragraph',NULL,'Units_And_Measurements-image14.png .','[{"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33939,78,500,'paragraph',NULL,'Guess where to put the missing c.','[{"s": "Guess where to put the missing c.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33940,78,501,'paragraph',NULL,'2.16 The unit of length convenient on the atomic scale is known as an angstrom and is denoted by Å: 1 Å = 10 –10 m. The size of a hydrogen atom is about 0.5 Å. What is the total atomic volume in m 3 of a mole of hydrogen atoms ?','[{"s": "2.16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The unit of length convenient on the atomic scale is known as an angstrom and is denoted by Å: 1 Å = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. The size of a hydrogen atom is about 0.5 Å. What is the total atomic volume in m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of a mole of hydrogen atoms ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33941,78,502,'paragraph',NULL,'2.17 One mole of an ideal gas at standard temperature and pressure occupies 22.4 L (molar volume). What is the ratio of molar volume to the atomic volume of a mole of hydrogen ? (Take the size of hydrogen molecule to be about 1 Å). Why is this ratio so large ?','[{"s": "2.17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "One mole of an ideal gas at standard temperature and pressure occupies 22.4 L (molar volume). What is the ratio of molar volume to the atomic volume of a mole of hydrogen ? (Take the size of hydrogen molecule to be about 1 Å). Why is this ratio so large ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33942,78,503,'paragraph',NULL,'2.18 Explain this common observation clearly : If you look out of the window of a fast moving train, the nearby trees, houses etc. seem to move rapidly in a direction opposite to the train’s motion, but the distant objects (hill tops, the Moon, the stars etc.) seem to be stationary. (In fact, since you are aware that you are moving, these distant objects seem to move with you).','[{"s": "2.18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Explain this common observation clearly : If you look out of the window of a fast moving train, the nearby trees, houses etc. seem to move rapidly in a direction opposite to the train’s motion, but the distant objects (hill tops, the Moon, the stars etc.) seem to be stationary. (In fact, since you are aware that you are moving, these distant objects seem to move with you).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33943,78,504,'paragraph',NULL,'2.19 The principle of ‘parallax’ in section 2.3.1 is used in the determination of distances of very distant stars. The baseline AB is the line joining the Earth’s two locations six months apart in its orbit around the Sun. That is, the baseline is about the diameter of the Earth’s orbit ≈ 3 × 10 11 m. However, even the nearest stars are so distant that with such a long baseline, they show parallax only of the order of 1” (second) of arc or so. A parsec is a convenient unit of length on the astronomical scale. It is the distance of an object that will show a parallax of 1” (second of arc) from opposite ends of a baseline equal to the distance from the Earth to the Sun. How much is a parsec in terms of metres ?','[{"s": "2.19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The principle of ‘parallax’ in section 2.3.1 is used in the determination of distances of very distant stars. The baseline AB is the line joining the Earth’s two locations six months apart in its orbit around the Sun. That is, the baseline is about the diameter of the Earth’s orbit ≈ 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. However, even the nearest stars are so distant that with such a long baseline, they show parallax only of the order of 1” (second) of arc or so. A parsec is a convenient unit of length on the astronomical scale. It is the distance of an object that will show a parallax of 1” (second of arc) from opposite ends of a baseline equal to the distance from the Earth to the Sun. How much is a parsec in terms of metres ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33944,78,505,'paragraph',NULL,'2.20 The nearest star to our solar system is 4.29 light years away. How much is this distance in terms of parsecs? How much parallax would this star (named Alpha Centauri) show when viewed from two locations of the Earth six months apart in its orbit around the Sun ?','[{"s": "2.20", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The nearest star to our solar system is 4.29 light years away. How much is this distance in terms of parsecs? How much parallax would this star (named Alpha Centauri) show when viewed from two locations of the Earth six months apart in its orbit around the Sun ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33945,78,506,'paragraph',NULL,'2.21 Precise measurements of physical quantities are a need of science. For example, to ascertain the speed of an aircraft, one must have an accurate method to find its positions at closely separated instants of time. This was the actual motivation behind the discovery of radar in World War II. Think of different examples in modern science where precise measurements of length, time, mass etc. are needed. Also, wherever you can, give a quantitative idea of the precision needed.','[{"s": "2.21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Precise measurements of physical quantities are a need of science. For example, to ascertain the speed of an aircraft, one must have an accurate method to find its positions at closely separated instants of time. This was the actual motivation behind the discovery of radar in World War II. Think of different examples in modern science where precise measurements of length, time, mass etc. are needed. Also, wherever you can, give a quantitative idea of the precision needed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33946,78,507,'paragraph',NULL,'2.22 Just as precise measurements are necessary in science, it is equally important to be able to make rough estimates of quantities using rudimentary ideas and common observations. Think of ways by which you can estimate the following (where an estimate is difficult to obtain, try to get an upper bound on the quantity) :','[{"s": "2.22", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Just as precise measurements are necessary in science, it is equally important to be able to make rough estimates of quantities using rudimentary ideas and common observations. Think of ways by which you can estimate the following (where an estimate is difficult to obtain, try to get an upper bound on the quantity) :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33947,78,508,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) the total mass of rain-bearing clouds over India during the Monsoon','[{"s": "(a) the total mass of rain-bearing clouds over India during the Monsoon", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33948,78,509,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) the mass of an elephant','[{"s": "(b) the mass of an elephant", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33949,78,510,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) the wind speed during a storm','[{"s": "(c) the wind speed during a storm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33950,78,511,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) the number of strands of hair on your head','[{"s": "(d) the number of strands of hair on your head", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33951,78,512,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) the number of air molecules in your classroom.','[{"s": "(e) the number of air molecules in your classroom.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33952,78,513,'paragraph',NULL,'2.23 The Sun is a hot plasma (ionized matter) with its inner core at a temperature exceeding 10 7 K, and its outer surface at a temperature of about 6000 K. At these high temperatures, no substance remains in a solid or liquid phase. In what range do you expect the mass density of the Sun to be, in the range of densities of solids and liquids or gases ? Check if your guess is correct from the following data : mass of the Sun = 2.0 ×10 30 kg, radius of the Sun = 7.0 × 10 8 m.','[{"s": "2.23", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The Sun is a hot plasma (ionized matter) with its inner core at a temperature exceeding 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K, and its outer surface at a temperature of about 6000 K. At these high temperatures, no substance remains in a solid or liquid phase. In what range do you expect the mass density of the Sun to be, in the range of densities of solids and liquids or gases ? Check if your guess is correct from the following data : mass of the Sun = 2.0 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg, radius of the Sun = 7.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33953,78,514,'paragraph',NULL,'2.24 When the planet Jupiter is at a distance of 824.7 million kilometers from the Earth, its angular diameter is measured to be 35.72” of arc. Calculate the diameter of Jupiter.','[{"s": "2.24", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When the planet Jupiter is at a distance of 824.7 million kilometers from the Earth, its angular diameter is measured to be 35.72” of arc. Calculate the diameter of Jupiter.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33954,78,515,'heading',3,'Additional Exercises',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33955,78,516,'paragraph',NULL,'2.25 A man walking briskly in rain with speed v must slant his umbrella forward making an angle θ with the vertical. A student derives the following relation between θ and v : tan θ = v and checks that the relation has a correct limit: as v → 0, θ → 0, as expected. (We are assuming there is no strong wind and that the rain falls vertically for a stationary man). Do you think this relation can be correct ? If not, guess the correct relation.','[{"s": "2.25", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A man walking briskly in rain with speed v must slant his umbrella forward making an angle θ with the vertical. A student derives the following relation between θ and v : tan θ = v and checks that the relation has a correct limit: as v → 0, θ → 0, as expected. (We are assuming there is no strong wind and that the rain falls vertically for a stationary man). Do you think this relation can be correct ? If not, guess the correct relation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33956,78,517,'paragraph',NULL,'2.26 It is claimed that two cesium clocks, if allowed to run for 100 years, free from any disturbance, may differ by only about 0.02 s. What does this imply for the accuracy of the standard cesium clock in measuring a time-interval of 1 s ?','[{"s": "2.26", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is claimed that two cesium clocks, if allowed to run for 100 years, free from any disturbance, may differ by only about 0.02 s. What does this imply for the accuracy of the standard cesium clock in measuring a time-interval of 1 s ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33957,78,518,'paragraph',NULL,'2.27 Estimate the average mass density of a sodium atom assuming its size to be about 2.5 Å. (Use the known values of Avogadro’s number and the atomic mass of sodium). Compare it with the mass density of sodium in its crystalline phase : 970 kg m –3 . Are the two densities of the same order of magnitude ? If so, why ?','[{"s": "2.27", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Estimate the average mass density of a sodium atom assuming its size to be about 2.5 Å. (Use the known values of Avogadro’s number and the atomic mass of sodium). Compare it with the mass density of sodium in its crystalline phase : 970 kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Are the two densities of the same order of magnitude ? If so, why ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33958,78,519,'paragraph',NULL,'2.28 The unit of length convenient on the nuclear scale is a fermi : 1 f = 10 –15 m. Nuclear sizes obey roughly the following empirical relation :','[{"s": "2.28", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The unit of length convenient on the nuclear scale is a fermi : 1 f = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. Nuclear sizes obey roughly the following empirical relation :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33959,78,520,'paragraph',NULL,'r = r 0 A 1/3','[{"s": "r = r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/3", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33960,78,521,'paragraph',NULL,'where r is the radius of the nucleus, A its mass number, and ro is a constant equal to about, 1.2 f. Show that the rule implies that nuclear mass density is nearly constant for different nuclei. Estimate the mass density of sodium nucleus. Compare it with the average mass density of a sodium atom obtained in Exercise. 2.27.','[{"s": "where r is the radius of the nucleus, A its mass number, and ro is a constant equal to about, 1.2 f. Show that the rule implies that nuclear mass density is nearly constant for different nuclei. Estimate the mass density of sodium nucleus. Compare it with the average mass density of a sodium atom obtained in Exercise. 2.27.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33961,78,522,'paragraph',NULL,'2.29 A LASER is a source of very intense, monochromatic, and unidirectional beam of light. These properties of a laser light can be exploited to measure long distances. The distance of the Moon from the Earth has been already determined very precisely using a laser as a source of light. A laser light beamed at the Moon takes 2.56 s to return after reflection at the Moon’s surface. How much is the radius of the lunar orbit around the Earth ?','[{"s": "2.29", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A LASER is a source of very intense, monochromatic, and unidirectional beam of light. These properties of a laser light can be exploited to measure long distances. The distance of the Moon from the Earth has been already determined very precisely using a laser as a source of light. A laser light beamed at the Moon takes 2.56 s to return after reflection at the Moon’s surface. How much is the radius of the lunar orbit around the Earth ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33962,78,523,'paragraph',NULL,'2.30 A SONAR (sound navigation and ranging) uses ultrasonic waves to detect and locate objects under water. In a submarine equipped with a SONAR, the time delay between generation of a probe wave and the reception of its echo after reflection from an enemy submarine is found to be 77.0 s. What is the distance of the enemy submarine? (Speed of sound in water = 1450 m s –1 ).','[{"s": "2.30", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A SONAR (sound navigation and ranging) uses ultrasonic waves to detect and locate objects under water. In a submarine equipped with a SONAR, the time delay between generation of a probe wave and the reception of its echo after reflection from an enemy submarine is found to be 77.0 s. What is the distance of the enemy submarine? (Speed of sound in water = 1450 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33963,78,524,'paragraph',NULL,'2.31 The farthest objects in our Universe discovered by modern astronomers are so distant that light emitted by them takes billions of years to reach the Earth. These objects (known as quasars) have many puzzling features, which have not yet been satisfactorily explained. What is the distance in km of a quasar from which light takes 3.0 billion years to reach us?','[{"s": "2.31", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The farthest objects in our Universe discovered by modern astronomers are so distant that light emitted by them takes billions of years to reach the Earth. These objects (known as quasars) have many puzzling features, which have not yet been satisfactorily explained. What is the distance in km of a quasar from which light takes 3.0 billion years to reach us?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33964,78,525,'paragraph',NULL,'2.32 It is a well known fact that during a total solar eclipse the disk of the moon almost completely covers the disk of the Sun. From this fact and from the information you can gather from examples 2.3 and 2.4, determine the approximate diameter of the moon.','[{"s": "2.32", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is a well known fact that during a total solar eclipse the disk of the moon almost completely covers the disk of the Sun. From this fact and from the information you can gather from examples 2.3 and 2.4, determine the approximate diameter of the moon.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(33965,78,526,'paragraph',NULL,'2.33 A great physicist of this century (P.A.M. Dirac) loved playing with numerical values of Fundamental constants of nature. This led him to an interesting observation. Dirac found that from the basic constants of atomic physics (c, e, mass of electron, mass of proton) and the gravitational constant G, he could arrive at a number with the dimension of time. Further, it was a very large number, its magnitude being close to the present estimate on the age of the universe (~15 billion years). From the table of fundamental constants in this book, try to see if you too can construct this number (or any other interesting number you can think of ). If its coincidence with the age of the universe were significant, what would this imply for the constancy of fundamental constants?','[{"s": "2.33", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A great physicist of this century (P.A.M. Dirac) loved playing with numerical values of Fundamental constants of nature. This led him to an interesting observation. Dirac found that from the basic constants of atomic physics (c, e, mass of electron, mass of proton) and the gravitational constant G, he could arrive at a number with the dimension of time. Further, it was a very large number, its magnitude being close to the present estimate on the age of the universe (~15 billion years). From the table of fundamental constants in this book, try to see if you too can construct this number (or any other interesting number you can think of ). If its coincidence with the age of the universe were significant, what would this imply for the constancy of fundamental constants?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
