(34051,78,612,'paragraph',NULL,'Guess where to put the missing c.','[{"s": "Guess where to put the missing c.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34052,78,613,'paragraph',NULL,'2.16 The unit of length convenient on the atomic scale is known as an angstrom and is denoted by Å: 1 Å = 10 –10 m. The size of a hydrogen atom is about 0.5 Å. What is the total atomic volume in m 3 of a mole of hydrogen atoms?','[{"s": "2.16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The unit of length convenient on the atomic scale is known as an angstrom and is denoted by Å: 1 Å = 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. The size of a hydrogen atom is about 0.5 Å. What is the total atomic volume in m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of a mole of hydrogen atoms?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34053,78,614,'paragraph',NULL,'2.17 One mole of an ideal gas at standard temperature and pressure occupies 22.4 L (molar volume). What is the ratio of molar volume to the atomic volume of a mole of hydrogen? (Take the size of the hydrogen molecule to be about 1 Å). Why is this ratio so large?','[{"s": "2.17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "One mole of an ideal gas at standard temperature and pressure occupies 22.4 L (molar volume). What is the ratio of molar volume to the atomic volume of a mole of hydrogen? (Take the size of the hydrogen molecule to be about 1 Å). Why is this ratio so large?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34054,78,615,'paragraph',NULL,'2.18 Explain this common observation clearly: If you look out of the window of a fast-moving train, the nearby trees, houses, etc. seem to move rapidly in a direction opposite to the train’s motion, but the distant objects (hilltops, the Moon, the stars, etc.) seem to be stationary. (In fact, since you are aware that you are moving, these distant objects seem to move with you).','[{"s": "2.18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Explain this common observation clearly: If you look out of the window of a fast-moving train, the nearby trees, houses, etc. seem to move rapidly in a direction opposite to the train’s motion, but the distant objects (hilltops, the Moon, the stars, etc.) seem to be stationary. (In fact, since you are aware that you are moving, these distant objects seem to move with you).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34055,78,616,'paragraph',NULL,'2.19 The principle of ‘parallax’ in section 2.3.1 is used in the determination of distances of very distant stars. The baseline AB is the line joining the Earth’s two locations six months apart in its orbit around the Sun. That is, the baseline is about the diameter of the Earth’s orbit ≈ 3 × 10 11 m. However, even the nearest stars are so distant that with such a long baseline, they show parallax only of the order of 1” (second) of arc or so. A parsec is a convenient unit of length on the astronomical scale. It is the distance of an object that will show a parallax of 1” (second of arc) from opposite ends of a baseline equal to the distance from the Earth to the Sun. How much is a parsec in terms of metres?','[{"s": "2.19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The principle of ‘parallax’ in section 2.3.1 is used in the determination of distances of very distant stars. The baseline AB is the line joining the Earth’s two locations six months apart in its orbit around the Sun. That is, the baseline is about the diameter of the Earth’s orbit ≈ 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. However, even the nearest stars are so distant that with such a long baseline, they show parallax only of the order of 1” (second) of arc or so. A parsec is a convenient unit of length on the astronomical scale. It is the distance of an object that will show a parallax of 1” (second of arc) from opposite ends of a baseline equal to the distance from the Earth to the Sun. How much is a parsec in terms of metres?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34056,78,617,'paragraph',NULL,'2.20 The nearest star to our solar system is 4.29 light-years away. How much is this distance in terms of parsecs? How much parallax would this star (named Alpha Centauri) show when viewed from two locations of the Earth six months apart in its orbit around the Sun?','[{"s": "2.20", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The nearest star to our solar system is 4.29 light-years away. How much is this distance in terms of parsecs? How much parallax would this star (named Alpha Centauri) show when viewed from two locations of the Earth six months apart in its orbit around the Sun?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34057,78,618,'heading',5,'Note: In stating numerical answers, take care of significant figures.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(212,2,112,'paragraph',NULL,'Hemichordata was earlier considered as a sub-phylum under phylum Chordata. But now it is placed as a separate phylum under non-chordata. Hemichordates have a rudimentary structure in the collar region called stomochord , a structure similar to notochord.','[{"s": "Hemichordata was earlier considered as a sub-phylum under phylum Chordata. But now it is placed as a separate phylum under non-chordata. Hemichordates have a rudimentary structure in the collar region called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "stomochord", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", a structure similar to notochord.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(34058,78,619,'paragraph',NULL,'2.21 Precise measurements of physical quantities are a need of science. For example, to ascertain the speed of an aircraft, one must have an accurate method to find its positions at closely separated instants of time. This was the actual motivation behind the discovery of radar in World War II. Think of different examples in modern science where precise measurements of length, time, mass, etc. are needed. Also, wherever you can, give a quantitative idea of the precision needed.','[{"s": "2.21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Precise measurements of physical quantities are a need of science. For example, to ascertain the speed of an aircraft, one must have an accurate method to find its positions at closely separated instants of time. This was the actual motivation behind the discovery of radar in World War II. Think of different examples in modern science where precise measurements of length, time, mass, etc. are needed. Also, wherever you can, give a quantitative idea of the precision needed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34059,78,620,'paragraph',NULL,'2.22 Just as precise measurements are necessary for science, it is equally important to be able to make rough estimates of quantities using rudimentary ideas and common observations. Think of ways by which you can estimate the following (where an estimate is difficult to obtain, try to get an upper bound on the quantity) :','[{"s": "2.22", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Just as precise measurements are necessary for science, it is equally important to be able to make rough estimates of quantities using rudimentary ideas and common observations. Think of ways by which you can estimate the following (where an estimate is difficult to obtain, try to get an upper bound on the quantity) :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
