(34215,79,135,'paragraph',NULL,'we will have constructive interference and the resultant intensity will be 4 I 0 ; the sign ~ between S 1 P and S 2 P represents the difference between S 1 P and S 2 P. On the other hand, if the point P is such that the path difference,','[{"s": "we will have constructive interference and the resultant intensity will be 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; the sign", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~", "t": "text"}, {"s": "between S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P represents the difference between S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P. On the other hand, if the point P is such that the path difference,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34216,79,136,'paragraph',NULL,'S 1 P ~ S 2 P = ( n + Wave_Optics-image3.png ) λ ( n = 0, 1, 2, 3, ...) (10.11)','[{"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P = (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Wave_Optics-image3.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0, 1, 2, 3, ...) (10.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34217,79,137,'paragraph',NULL,'we will have destructive interference and the resultant intensity will be zero. Now, for any other arbitrary point G (Fig. 10.10) let the phase difference between the two displacements be φ . Thus, if the displacement produced by S 1 is given by','[{"s": "we will have", "t": "text"}, {"s": "destructive interference", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the resultant intensity will be zero. Now, for any other arbitrary point G (Fig. 10.10) let the phase difference between the two displacements be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, if the displacement produced by S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34218,79,138,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image53.png'),
(34219,79,139,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 10.10 Locus of points for which S 1 P – S 2 P is equal to zero, ±λ , ± 2 λ , ± 3 λ .','[{"s": "Figure 10.10 Locus of points for which S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P – S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P is equal to zero,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "±λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "±", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "±", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34220,79,140,'paragraph',NULL,'y 1 = a cos ω t','[{"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34221,79,141,'paragraph',NULL,'then, the displacement produced by S 2 would be','[{"s": "then, the displacement produced by S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "would be", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34222,79,142,'paragraph',NULL,'y 2 = a cos ( ω t + φ )','[{"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34223,79,143,'paragraph',NULL,'and the resultant displacement will be given by','[{"s": "and the resultant displacement will be given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34224,79,144,'paragraph',NULL,'y = y 1 + y 2','[{"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34225,79,145,'paragraph',NULL,'= a [cos ω t + cos ( ω t + φ) ]','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[cos", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ cos (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34226,79,146,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2 a cos ( φ /2) cos ( ω t + φ /2)','[{"s": "= 2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2) cos (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34227,79,147,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image54.png'),
(34229,79,149,'paragraph',NULL,'I = 4 I 0 cos 2 (φ /2) (10.12)','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2) (10.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34231,79,151,'paragraph',NULL,'Now if the two sources are coherent (i.e., if the two needles are going up and down regularly) then the phase difference φ at any point will not change with time and we will have a stable interference pattern; i.e., the positions of maxima and minima will not change with time. However, if the two needles do not maintain a constant phase difference, then the interference pattern will also change with time and, if the phase difference changes very rapidly with time, the positions of maxima and minima will also vary rapidly with time and we will see a “time-averaged” intensity distribution. When this happens, we will observe an average intensity that will be given by','[{"s": "Now if the two sources are coherent (i.e., if the two needles are going up and down regularly) then the phase difference", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at any point will not change with time and we will have a stable interference pattern; i.e., the positions of maxima and minima will not change with time. However, if the two needles do not maintain a constant phase difference, then the interference pattern will also change with time and, if the phase difference changes very rapidly with time, the positions of maxima and minima will also vary rapidly with time and we will see a “time-averaged” intensity distribution. When this happens, we will observe an average intensity that will be given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34232,79,152,'paragraph',NULL,'Wave_Optics-image55.png (10.13)','[{"s": "Wave_Optics-image55.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(10.13)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34233,79,153,'paragraph',NULL,'where angular brackets represent time averaging. Indeed it is shown in Section 7.2 that if φ ( t ) varies randomly with time, the time-averaged quantity < cos 2 ( φ /2) > will be 1/2. This is also intuitively obvious because the function cos 2 ( φ /2) will randomly vary between 0 and 1 and the average value will be 1/2. The resultant intensity will be given by','[{"s": "where angular brackets represent time averaging. Indeed it is shown in Section 7.2 that if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") varies randomly with time, the time-averaged quantity < cos", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2) > will be 1/2. This is also intuitively obvious because the function cos", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2) will randomly vary between 0 and 1 and the average value will be 1/2. The resultant intensity will be given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34234,79,154,'paragraph',NULL,'I = 2 I 0 (10.14)','[{"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(10.14)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34235,79,155,'paragraph',NULL,'at all points.','[{"s": "at all points.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34236,79,156,'paragraph',NULL,'When the phase difference between the two vibrating sources changes rapidly with time, we say that the two sources are incoherent and when this happens the intensities just add up. This is indeed what happens when two separate light sources illuminate a wall.','[{"s": "When the phase difference between the two vibrating sources changes rapidly with time, we say that the two sources are incoherent and when this happens the intensities just add up. This is indeed what happens when two separate light sources illuminate a wall.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34237,79,157,'heading',3,'10.5 Interference of Light Waves and Young’s Experiment',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34238,79,158,'paragraph',NULL,'We will now discuss interference using light waves. If we use two sodium lamps illuminating two pinholes (Fig. 10.11) we will not observe any interference fringes. This is because of the fact that the light wave emitted from an ordinary source (like a sodium lamp) undergoes abrupt phase changes in times of the order of 10 –10 seconds. Thus the light waves coming out from two independent sources of light will not have any fixed phase relationship and would be incoherent, when this happens, as discussed in the previous section, the intensities on the screen will add up.','[{"s": "We will now discuss interference using light waves. If we use two sodium lamps illuminating two pinholes (Fig. 10.11) we will not observe any interference fringes. This is because of the fact that the light wave emitted from an ordinary source (like a sodium lamp) undergoes abrupt phase changes in times of the order of 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "seconds. Thus the light waves coming out from two independent sources of light will not have any fixed phase relationship and would be incoherent, when this happens, as discussed in the previous section, the intensities on the screen will add up.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34239,79,159,'paragraph',NULL,'The British physicist Thomas Young used an ingenious technique to “lock” the phases of the waves emanating from S 1 and S 2 . He made two pinholes S 1 and S 2 (very close to each other) on an opaque screen [Fig. 10.12(a)]. These were illuminated by another pinholes that was in turn, lit by a bright source. Light waves spread out from S and fall on both S 1 and S 2 . S 1 and S 2 then behave like two coherent sources because light waves coming out from S 1 and S 2 are derived from the same original source and any abrupt phase change in S will manifest in exactly similar phase changes in the light coming out from S 1 and S 2 . Thus, the two sources S 1 and S 2 will be locked in phase; i.e., they will be coherent like the two vibrating needle in our water wave example [Fig. 10.8(a)].','[{"s": "The British physicist Thomas Young used an ingenious technique to “lock” the phases of the waves emanating from S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". He made two pinholes S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(very close to each other) on an opaque screen [Fig. 10.12(a)]. These were illuminated by another pinholes that was in turn, lit by a bright source. Light waves spread out from S and fall on both S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "then behave like two coherent sources because light waves coming out from S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are derived from the same original source and any abrupt phase change in S will manifest in exactly similar phase changes in the light coming out from S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, the two sources S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "locked", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in phase; i.e., they will be coherent like the two vibrating needle in our water wave example [Fig. 10.8(a)].", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34240,79,160,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image56.png'),
(34241,79,161,'paragraph',NULL,'FIGURE 10.11 If two sodium lamps illuminate two pinholes S1 and S2, the intensities will add up and no interference fringes will be observed on the screen.','[{"s": "FIGURE 10.11 If two sodium lamps illuminate two pinholes S1 and S2, the intensities will add up and no interference fringes will be observed on the screen.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34242,79,162,'image',NULL,'121',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image23.png'),
(34243,79,163,'paragraph',NULL,'FIGURE 10.12 Young’s arrangement to produce interference pattern.','[{"s": "FIGURE 10.12 Young’s arrangement to produce interference pattern.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34244,79,164,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus spherical waves emanating from S 1 and S 2 will produce interference fringes on the screen GG ′ , as shown in Fig. 10.12(b). The positions of maximum and minimum intensities can be calculated by using the analysis given in Section 10.4 where we had shown that for an arbitrary point P on the line GG ′ [Fig. 10.12(b)] to correspond to a maximum, we must have','[{"s": "Thus spherical waves emanating from S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will produce interference fringes on the screen GG", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", as shown in Fig. 10.12(b). The positions of maximum and minimum intensities can be calculated by using the analysis given in Section 10.4 where we had shown that for an arbitrary point P on the line GG", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[Fig. 10.12(b)] to correspond to a maximum, we must have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
