(34268,79,188,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 10.13 Computer generated fringe pattern produced by two point source S 1 and S 2 on the screen GG ′ (Fig. 10.12); (a) and (b) correspond to d = 0.005 mm and 0.025 mm, respectively (both figures correspond to D = 5 cm and λ = 5 × 10 –5 cm.) (Adopted from OPTICS by A. Ghatak, Tata McGraw Hill Publishing Co. Ltd., New Delhi, 2000.)','[{"s": "Figure 10.13 Computer generated fringe pattern produced by two point source S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on the screen GG", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Fig. 10.12); (a) and (b) correspond to d = 0.005 mm and 0.025 mm, respectively (both figures correspond to D = 5 cm and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cm.) (Adopted from OPTICS by A. Ghatak, Tata McGraw Hill Publishing Co. Ltd., New Delhi, 2000.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34269,79,189,'paragraph',NULL,'In the double-slit experiment shown in Fig. 10.12(b), we have taken the source hole S on the perpendicular bisector of the two slits, which is shown as the line SO. What happens if the source S is slightly away from the perpendicular bisector. Consider that the source is moved to some new point S ′ and suppose that Q is the mid-point of S 1 and S 2 . If the angle S ′ QS is φ , then the central bright fringe occurs at an angle – φ , on the other side. Thus, if the source S is on the perpendicular bisector, then the central fringe occurs at O, also on the perpendicular bisector. If S is shifted by an angle φ to point S ′ , then the central fringe appears at a point O ′ at an angle – φ , which means that it is shifted by the same angle on the other side of the bisector. This also means that the source S ′ , the mid-point Q and the point O ′ of the central fringe are in a straight line.','[{"s": "In the double-slit experiment shown in Fig. 10.12(b), we have taken the source hole S on the perpendicular bisector of the two slits, which is shown as the line SO. What happens if the source S is slightly away from the perpendicular bisector. Consider that the source is moved to some new point S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and suppose that Q is the mid-point of S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If the angle S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′", "t": "text"}, {"s": "QS is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", then the central bright fringe occurs at an angle –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", on the other side. Thus, if the source S is on the perpendicular bisector, then the central fringe occurs at O, also on the perpendicular bisector. If S is shifted by an angle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to point S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", then the central fringe appears at a point", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at an angle –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which means that it is shifted by the same angle on the other side of the bisector. This also means that the source S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the mid-point Q and the point O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "′", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the central fringe are in a straight line.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34270,79,190,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image12.png'),
(34271,79,191,'paragraph',NULL,'We end this section by quoting from the Nobel lecture of Dennis Gabor*','[{"s": "We end this section by quoting from the Nobel lecture of Dennis Gabor*", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34272,79,192,'paragraph',NULL,'The wave nature of light was demonstrated convincingly for the first time in 1801 by Thomas Young by a wonderfully simple experiment. He let a ray of sunlight into a dark room, placed a dark screen in front of it, pierced with two small pinholes, and beyond this, at some distance, a white screen. He then saw two darkish lines at both sides of a bright line, which gave him sufficient encouragement to repeat the experiment, this time with spirit flame as light source, with a little salt in it to produce the bright yellow sodium light. This time he saw a number of dark lines, regularly spaced; the first clear proof that light added to light can produce darkness. This phenomenon is called interference. Thomas Young had expected it because he believed in the wave theory of light.','[{"s": "The wave nature of light was demonstrated convincingly for the first time in 1801 by Thomas Young by a wonderfully simple experiment. He let a ray of sunlight into a dark room, placed a dark screen in front of it, pierced with two small pinholes, and beyond this, at some distance, a white screen. He then saw two darkish lines at both sides of a bright line, which gave him sufficient encouragement to repeat the experiment, this time with spirit flame as light source, with a little salt in it to produce the bright yellow sodium light. This time he saw a number of dark lines, regularly spaced; the first clear proof that light added to light can produce darkness. This phenomenon is called interference. Thomas Young had expected it because he believed in the wave theory of light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34273,79,193,'paragraph',NULL,'*Dennis Gabor received the 1971 Nobel Prize in Physics for discovering theprinciples of holography','[{"s": "*Dennis Gabor received the 1971 Nobel Prize in Physics for discovering theprinciples of holography", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34274,79,194,'paragraph',NULL,'We should mention here that the fringes are straight lines although S 1 and S 2 are point sources. If we had slits instead of the point sources (Fig. 10.14), each pair of points would have produced straight line fringes resulting in straight line fringes with increased intensities.','[{"s": "We should mention here that the fringes are straight lines although S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are point sources. If we had slits instead of the point sources (Fig. 10.14), each pair of points would have produced straight line fringes resulting in straight line fringes with increased intensities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34275,79,195,'image',NULL,'122',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image14.png'),
(34277,79,197,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.3 Two slits are made one millimetre apart and the screen is placed one metre away. What is the fringe separation when blue-green light of wavelength 500 nm is used?','[{"s": "Example 10.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two slits are made one millimetre apart and the screen is placed one metre away. What is the fringe separation when blue-green light of wavelength 500 nm is used?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
