(34279,79,199,'paragraph',NULL,'= 5 × 10 –4 m = 0.5 mm','[{"s": "= 5 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m = 0.5 mm", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34281,79,201,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) the screen is moved away from the plane of the slits;','[{"s": "(a) the screen is moved away from the plane of the slits;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34282,79,202,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) the (monochromatic) source is replaced by another (monochromatic) source of shorter wavelength;','[{"s": "(b) the (monochromatic) source is replaced by another (monochromatic) source of shorter wavelength;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34283,79,203,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) the separation between the two slits is increased;','[{"s": "(c) the separation between the two slits is increased;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34284,79,204,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) the source slit is moved closer to the double-slit plane;','[{"s": "(d) the source slit is moved closer to the double-slit plane;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34285,79,205,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) the width of the source slit is increased;','[{"s": "(e) the width of the source slit is increased;", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34286,79,206,'paragraph',NULL,'(In each operation, take all parameters, other than the one specified, to remain unchanged.)','[{"s": "(In each operation, take all parameters, other than the one specified, to remain unchanged.)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34287,79,207,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34288,79,208,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Angular separation of the fringes remains constant (= λ / d','[{"s": "(a) Angular separation of the fringes remains constant (=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34289,79,209,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The separation of the fringes (and also angular separation) decreases. See, however, the condition mentioned in (d) below.','[{"s": "(b) The separation of the fringes (and also angular separation) decreases. See, however, the condition mentioned in (d) below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34290,79,210,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The separation of the fringes (and also angular separation) decreases. See, however, the condition mentioned in (d) below.','[{"s": "(c) The separation of the fringes (and also angular separation) decreases. See, however, the condition mentioned in (d) below.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34291,79,211,'paragraph',NULL,'(d) Let s be the size of the source and S s/S < λ / d should be satisfied; otherwise, interference patterns produced by different parts of the source overlap and no fringes are seen. Thus, as S decreases (i.e., the source slit is brought closer), the interference pattern gets less and less sharp, and when the source is brought too close for this condition to be valid, the fringes disappear. Till this happens, the fringe separation remains fixed.','[{"s": "(d) Let", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the size of the source and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "s/S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "should be satisfied; otherwise, interference patterns produced by different parts of the source overlap and no fringes are seen. Thus, as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decreases (i.e., the source slit is brought closer), the interference pattern gets less and less sharp, and when the source is brought too close for this condition to be valid, the fringes disappear. Till this happens, the fringe separation remains fixed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34292,79,212,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) Same as in (d). As the source slit width increases, fringe pattern gets less and less sharp. When the source slit is so wide that the condition s/S ≤ λ / d is not satisfied, the interference pattern disappears.','[{"s": "(e) Same as in (d). As the source slit width increases, fringe pattern gets less and less sharp. When the source slit is so wide that the condition", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s/S ≤ λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is not satisfied, the interference pattern disappears.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1541,16,47,'paragraph',NULL,'Many fungi belonging to the genera Microsporum, Trichophyton and Epidermophyton are responsible for ringworms which is one of the most common infectious diseases in man Appearance of dry, scaly lesions on various parts of the body such as skin, nails and scalp (Figure 8.3) are the main symptoms of the disease. These lesions are accompanied by intense itching. Heat and moisture help these fungi to grow, which makes them thrive in skin folds such as those in the groin or between the toes. Ringworms are generally acquired from soil or by using towels, clothes or even the comb of infected individuals.','[{"s": "Many fungi belonging to the genera Microsporum, Trichophyton and Epidermophyton are responsible for", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ringworms", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "which is one of the most common infectious diseases in man Appearance of dry, scaly lesions on various parts of the body such as skin, nails and scalp (Figure 8.3) are the main symptoms of the disease.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "These lesions are accompanied by intense itching. Heat and moisture help these fungi to grow, which makes them thrive in skin folds such as those in the groin or between the toes.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ringworms are generally acquired from soil or by using towels, clothes or even the comb of infected individuals.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["301730", "301734", "301735", "301749", "301750"]',NULL),
(34293,79,213,'paragraph',NULL,'(f) The interference patterns due to different component colours of white light overlap (incoherently). The central bright fringes for different colours are at the same position. Therefore, the central fringe is white. For a point P for which S2P –S1P = λ b/2, where λ ≈ 4000 Å) represents the wavelength for the blue colour, the blue component will be absent and the fringe will appear red in colour. Slightly farther away where S2Q–S1Q = λ b = λ r/2 where λ r ( ≈ 8000 Å) is the wavelength for the red colour, the fringe will be predominantly blue.','[{"s": "(f) The interference patterns due to different component colours of white light overlap (incoherently). The central bright fringes for different colours are at the same position. Therefore, the central fringe is white. For a point P for which S2P –S1P =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b/2, where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "≈", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4000 Å) represents the wavelength for the blue colour, the blue component will be absent and the fringe will appear red in colour. Slightly farther away where S2Q–S1Q =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r/2 where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≈", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8000 Å) is the wavelength for the red colour, the fringe will be predominantly blue.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34294,79,214,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the fringe closest on either side of the central white fringe is red and the farthest will appear blue. After a few fringes, no clear fringe pattern is seen.','[{"s": "Thus, the fringe closest on either side of the central white fringe is red and the farthest will appear blue. After a few fringes, no clear fringe pattern is seen.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34295,79,215,'heading',3,'10.6 Diffraction',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34296,79,216,'paragraph',NULL,'If we look clearly at the shadow cast by an opaque object, close to the region of geometrical shadow, there are alternate dark and bright regions just like in interference. This happens due to the phenomenon of diffraction. Diffraction is a general characteristic exhibited by all types of waves, be it sound waves, light waves, water waves or matter waves. Since the wavelength of light is much smaller than the dimensions of most obstacles; we do not encounter diffraction effects of light in everyday observations. However, the finite resolution of our eye or of optical instruments such as telescopes or microscopes is limited due to the phenomenon of diffraction. Indeed the colours that you see when a CD is viewed is due to diffraction effects. We will now discuss the phenomenon of diffraction.','[{"s": "If we look clearly at the shadow cast by an opaque object, close to the region of geometrical shadow, there are alternate dark and bright regions just like in interference. This happens due to the phenomenon of diffraction. Diffraction is a general characteristic exhibited by all types of waves, be it sound waves, light waves, water waves or matter waves. Since the wavelength of light is much smaller than the dimensions of most obstacles; we do not encounter diffraction effects of light in everyday observations. However, the finite resolution of our eye or of optical instruments such as telescopes or microscopes is limited due to the phenomenon of diffraction. Indeed the colours that you see when a CD is viewed is due to diffraction effects. We will now discuss the phenomenon of diffraction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34297,79,217,'heading',4,'10.6.1 The single slit',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34298,79,218,'paragraph',NULL,'In the discussion of Young’s experiment, we stated that a single narrow slit acts as a new source from which light spreads out. Even before Young, early experimenters – including Newton – had noticed that light spreads out from narrow holes and slits. It seems to turn around corners and enter regions where we would expect a shadow. These effects, known as diffraction , can only be properly understood using wave ideas. After all, you are hardly surprised to hear sound waves from someone talking around a corner!','[{"s": "In the discussion of Young’s experiment, we stated that a single narrow slit acts as a new source from which light spreads out. Even before Young, early experimenters – including Newton – had noticed that light spreads out from narrow holes and slits. It seems to turn around corners and enter regions where we would expect a shadow. These effects, known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "diffraction", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", can only be properly understood using wave ideas. After all, you are hardly surprised to hear sound waves from someone talking around a corner!", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34299,79,219,'paragraph',NULL,'When the double slit in Young’s experiment is replaced by a single narrow slit (illuminated by a monochromatic source), a broad pattern with a central bright region is seen. On both sides, there are alternate dark and bright regions, the intensity becoming weaker away from the centre (Fig. 10.16). To understand this, go to Fig. 10.15, which shows a parallel beam of light falling normally on a single slit LN of width a . The diffracted light goes on to meet a screen. The midpoint of the slit is M.','[{"s": "When the double slit in Young’s experiment is replaced by a single narrow slit (illuminated by a monochromatic source), a broad pattern with a central bright region is seen. On both sides, there are alternate dark and bright regions, the intensity becoming weaker away from the centre (Fig. 10.16). To understand this, go to Fig. 10.15, which shows a parallel beam of light falling normally on a single slit LN of width", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The diffracted light goes on to meet a screen. The midpoint of the slit is M.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34300,79,220,'paragraph',NULL,'A straight line through M perpendicular to the slit plane meets the screen at C. We want the intensity at any point P on the screen. As before, straight lines joining P to the different points L,M,N, etc., can be treated as parallel, making an angle θ with the normal MC.','[{"s": "A straight line through M perpendicular to the slit plane meets the screen at C. We want the intensity at any point P on the screen. As before, straight lines joining P to the different points L,M,N, etc., can be treated as parallel, making an angle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with the normal MC.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34301,79,221,'paragraph',NULL,'The basic idea is to divide the slit into much smaller parts, and add their contributions at P with the proper phase differences. We are treating different parts of the wavefront at the slit as secondary sources. Because the incoming wavefront is parallel to the plane of the slit, these sources are in phase.','[{"s": "The basic idea is to divide the slit into much smaller parts, and add their contributions at P with the proper phase differences. We are treating different parts of the wavefront at the slit as secondary sources. Because the incoming wavefront is parallel to the plane of the slit, these sources are in phase.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34302,79,222,'paragraph',NULL,'From Fig. 10.15,','[{"s": "From Fig. 10.15,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34303,79,223,'paragraph',NULL,'NP – LP = NQ','[{"s": "NP – LP = NQ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34304,79,224,'paragraph',NULL,'= a sin θ','[{"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34305,79,225,'paragraph',NULL,'≈ a θ (for smaller angles) (10.21)','[{"s": "≈", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(for smaller angles) (10.21)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34306,79,226,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, if two points M 1 and M 2 in the slit plane are separated by y , the path difference M 2 P – M 1 P ≈ y θ . We now have to sum up equal, coherent contributions from a large number of sources, each with a different phase. This calculation was made by Fresnel using integral calculus, so we omit it here. The main features of the diffraction pattern can be understood by simple arguments.','[{"s": "Similarly, if two points M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the slit plane are separated by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the path difference M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P – M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≈", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We now have to sum up equal, coherent contributions from a large number of sources, each with a different phase. This calculation was made by Fresnel using integral calculus, so we omit it here. The main features of the diffraction pattern can be understood by simple arguments.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34307,79,227,'paragraph',NULL,'At the central point C on the screen, the angle θ is zero. All path differences are zero and hence all the parts of the slit contribute in phase. This gives maximum intensity at C. Experimental observation shown in Fig. 10.15 indicates that the intensity has a central maximum at θ = 0 and other secondary maxima at θ ≈ ( n +1/2) λ / a , and has minima (zero intensity) at θ ≈ n λ / a n = ±1, ±2, ±3, .... It is easy to see why it has minima at these values of angle. Consider first the angle θ where the path difference a θ is λ . Then,','[{"s": "At the central point", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "on the screen, the angle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is zero. All path differences are zero and hence all the parts of the slit contribute in phase. This gives maximum intensity at C. Experimental observation shown in Fig. 10.15 indicates that the intensity has a central maximum at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 and other secondary maxima at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≈ (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+1/2)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", and has minima (zero intensity) at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≈", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ±1, ±2, ±3, .... It is easy to see why it has minima at these values of angle. Consider first the angle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "where the path difference", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Then,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34308,79,228,'paragraph',NULL,'Wave_Optics-image16.png . (10.22)','[{"s": "Wave_Optics-image16.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". (10.22)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34309,79,229,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, divide the slit into two equal halves LM and MN each of size a /2. For every point M 1 in LM, there is a point M 2 in MN such that M 1 M 2 = a /2. The path difference between M 1 and M 2 at P = M 2 P – M 1 P = θ a /2 = λ /2 for the angle chosen. This means that the contributions from M 1 and M 2 are 180° out of phase and cancel in the direction θ = λ / a . Contributions from the two halves of the slit LM and MN, therefore, cancel each other. Equation (10.22) gives the angle at which the intensity falls to zero. One can similarly show that the intensity is zero for θ = n λ / a , with n being any integer (except zero!). Notice that the angular size of the central maximum increases when the slit width a decreases.','[{"s": "Now, divide the slit into two equal halves LM and MN each of size", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2. For every point M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in LM, there is a point M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in MN such that M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2. The path difference between M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at P = M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P – M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2 =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2 for the angle chosen. This means that the contributions from M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are 180° out of phase and cancel in the direction", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Contributions from the two halves of the slit LM and MN, therefore, cancel each other. Equation (10.22) gives the angle at which the intensity falls to zero. One can similarly show that the intensity is zero for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "being any integer (except zero!). Notice that the angular size of the central maximum increases when the slit width", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decreases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
