(34383,79,303,'paragraph',NULL,'Wave_Optics-image38.png (10.30)','[{"s": "Wave_Optics-image38.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(10.30)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34384,79,304,'paragraph',NULL,'The product n sin β is called the numerical aperture and is sometimes marked on the objective.','[{"s": "The product", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "numerical aperture", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and is sometimes marked on the objective.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34386,79,306,'paragraph',NULL,'There is a likelihood of confusion between resolution and magnification, and similarly between the role of a telescope and a microscope to deal with these parameters. A telescope produces images of far objects nearer to our eye. Therefore objects which are not resolved at far distance, can be resolved by looking at them through a telescope. A microscope, on the other hand, magnifies objects (which are near to us) and produces their larger image. We may be looking at two stars or two satellites of a far-away planet, or we may be looking at different regions of a living cell. In this context, it is good to remember that a telescope resolves whereas a microscope magnifies.','[{"s": "There is a likelihood of confusion between resolution and magnification, and similarly between the role of a telescope and a microscope to deal with these parameters. A telescope produces images of far objects nearer to our eye. Therefore objects which are not resolved at far distance, can be resolved by looking at them through a telescope. A microscope, on the other hand, magnifies objects (which are near to us) and produces their larger image. We may be looking at two stars or two satellites of a far-away planet, or we may be looking at different regions of a living cell. In this context, it is good to remember that a telescope resolves whereas a microscope magnifies.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34387,79,307,'heading',4,'10.6.4 The validity of ray optics',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34388,79,308,'paragraph',NULL,'An aperture (i.e., slit or hole) of size a illuminated by a parallel beam sends diffracted light into an angle of approximately ≈ λ / a . This is the angular size of the bright central maximum. In travelling a distance z , the diffracted beam therefore acquires a width z λ / a due to diffraction. It is interesting to ask at what value of z the spreading due to diffraction becomes comparable to the size a of the aperture. We thus approximately equate z λ / a with a . This gives the distance beyond which divergence of the beam of width a becomes significant. Therefore,','[{"s": "An aperture (i.e., slit or hole) of size", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "illuminated by a parallel beam sends diffracted light into an angle of approximately", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≈ λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This is the angular size of the bright central maximum. In travelling a distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the diffracted beam therefore acquires a width", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "due to diffraction. It is interesting to ask at what value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the spreading due to diffraction becomes comparable to the size", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the aperture. We thus approximately equate", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This gives the distance beyond which divergence of the beam of width", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "becomes significant. Therefore,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34389,79,309,'paragraph',NULL,'Wave_Optics-image21.png (10.31)','[{"s": "Wave_Optics-image21.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(10.31)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34390,79,310,'paragraph',NULL,'We define a quantity z F called the Fresnel distance by the following equation','[{"s": "We define a quantity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Fresnel distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "by the following equation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34391,79,311,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image22.png'),
(34392,79,312,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (10.31) shows that for distances much smaller than z F , the spreading due to diffraction is smaller compared to the size of the beam. It becomes comparable when the distance is approximately z F . For distances much greater than z F , the spreading due to diffraction dominates over that due to ray optics (i.e., the size a of the aperture). Equation (10.31) also shows that ray optics is valid in the limit of wavelength tending to zero.','[{"s": "Equation (10.31) shows that for distances much smaller than", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the spreading due to diffraction is smaller compared to the size of the beam. It becomes comparable when the distance is approximately", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For distances much greater than", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the spreading due to diffraction dominates over that due to ray optics (i.e., the size", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the aperture). Equation (10.31) also shows that ray optics is valid in the limit of wavelength tending to zero.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34393,79,313,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.7 For what distance is ray optics a good approximation when the aperture is 3 mm wide and the wavelength is 500 nm?','[{"s": "Example 10.7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For what distance is ray optics a good approximation when the aperture is 3 mm wide and the wavelength is 500 nm?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34394,79,314,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution Wave_Optics-image24.png','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Wave_Optics-image24.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34395,79,315,'paragraph',NULL,'This example shows that even with a small aperture, diffraction spreading can be neglected for rays many metres in length. Thus, ray optics is valid in many common situations.','[{"s": "This example shows that even with a small aperture, diffraction spreading can be neglected for rays many metres in length. Thus, ray optics is valid in many common situations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34396,79,316,'heading',3,'10.7 Polarisation',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34397,79,317,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider holding a long string that is held horizontally, the other end of which is assumed to be fixed. If we move the end of the string up and down in a periodic manner, we will generate a wave propagating in the + x direction (Fig. 10.21). Such a wave could be described by the following equation','[{"s": "Consider holding a long string that is held horizontally, the other end of which is assumed to be fixed. If we move the end of the string up and down in a periodic manner, we will generate a wave propagating in the +", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "direction (Fig. 10.21). Such a wave could be described by the following equation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34398,79,318,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image26.png'),
(34399,79,319,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 10.21 (a) The curves represent the displacement of a string at t = 0 and at t = ∆ t, respectively when a sinusoidal wave is propagating in the +x-direction. (b) The curve represents the time variation of the displacement at x = 0 when a sinusoidal wave is propagating in the +x-direction. At x = ∆ x, the time variation of the displacement will be slightly displaced to the right.','[{"s": "Figure 10.21 (a) The curves represent the displacement of a string at t = 0 and at t =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t, respectively when a sinusoidal wave is propagating in the +x-direction. (b) The curve represents the time variation of the displacement at x = 0 when a sinusoidal wave is propagating in the +x-direction. At x =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x, the time variation of the displacement will be slightly displaced to the right.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34400,79,320,'paragraph',NULL,'y ( x,t ) = a sin ( kx – ω t ) (10.32)','[{"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x,t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kx", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (10.32)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34401,79,321,'paragraph',NULL,'where a and ω ( = 2 πν ) represent the amplitude and the angular frequency of the wave, respectively; further,','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "πν", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") represent the amplitude and the angular frequency of the wave, respectively; further,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34402,79,322,'paragraph',NULL,'Wave_Optics-image28.png (10.33)','[{"s": "Wave_Optics-image28.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(10.33)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34403,79,323,'paragraph',NULL,'represents the wavelength associated with the wave. We had discussed propagation of such waves in Chapter 15 of Class XI textbook. Since the displacement (which is along the y direction) is at right angles to the direction of propagation of the wave, we have what is known as a transverse wave . Also, since the displacement is in the y direction, it is often referred to as a y -polarised wave. Since each point on the string moves on a straight line, the wave is also referred to as a linearly polarised wave. Further, the string always remains confined to the x-y plane and therefore it is also referred to as a plane polarised wave .','[{"s": "represents the wavelength associated with the wave. We had discussed propagation of such waves in Chapter 15 of Class XI textbook. Since the displacement (which is along the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "direction) is at right angles to the direction of propagation of the wave, we have what is known as a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "transverse wave", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Also, since the displacement is in the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "direction, it is often referred to as a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-polarised wave. Since each point on the string moves on a straight line, the wave is also referred to as a linearly polarised wave. Further, the string always remains confined to the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x-y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plane and therefore it is also referred to as a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plane polarised wave", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34404,79,324,'paragraph',NULL,'In a similar manner we can consider the vibration of the string in the x - z plane generating a z -polarised wave whose displacement will be given by','[{"s": "In a similar manner we can consider the vibration of the string in the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plane generating a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-polarised wave whose displacement will be given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34405,79,325,'paragraph',NULL,'z ( x,t ) = a sin ( kx – ω t ) (10.34)','[{"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x,t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kx", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") (10.34)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34406,79,326,'paragraph',NULL,'It should be mentioned that the linearly polarised waves [described by Eqs. (10.33) and (10.34)] are all transverse waves; i.e., the displacement of each point of the string is always at right angles to the direction of propagation of the wave. Finally, if the plane of vibration of the string is changed randomly in very short intervals of time, then we have what is known as an unpolarised wave . Thus, for an unpolarised wave the displacement will be randomly changing with time though it will always be perpendicular to the direction of propagation.','[{"s": "It should be mentioned that the linearly polarised waves [described by Eqs. (10.33) and (10.34)] are all transverse waves; i.e., the displacement of each point of the string is always at right angles to the direction of propagation of the wave. Finally, if the plane of vibration of the string is changed randomly in very short intervals of time, then we have what is known as an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "unpolarised wave", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, for an unpolarised wave the displacement will be randomly changing with time though it will always be perpendicular to the direction of propagation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34407,79,327,'paragraph',NULL,'Light waves are transverse in nature; i.e., the electric field associated with a propagating light wave is always at right angles to the direction of propagation of the wave. This can be easily demonstrated using a simple polaroid. You must have seen thin plastic like sheets, which are called polaroids . A polaroid consists of long chain molecules aligned in a particular direction. The electric vectors (associated with the propagating light wave) along the direction of the aligned molecules get absorbed. Thus, if an unpolarised light wave is incident on such a polaroid then the light wave will get linearly polarised with the electric vector oscillating along a direction perpendicular to the aligned molecules; this direction is known as the pass-axis of the polaroid.','[{"s": "Light waves are transverse in nature; i.e., the electric field associated with a propagating light wave is always at right angles to the direction of propagation of the wave. This can be easily demonstrated using a simple polaroid. You must have seen thin plastic like sheets, which are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "polaroids", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". A polaroid consists of long chain molecules aligned in a particular direction. The electric vectors (associated with the propagating light wave) along the direction of the aligned molecules get absorbed. Thus, if an unpolarised light wave is incident on such a polaroid then the light wave will get linearly polarised with the electric vector oscillating along a direction perpendicular to the aligned molecules; this direction is known as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pass-axis", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the polaroid.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34408,79,328,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, if the light from an ordinary source (like a sodium lamp) passes through a polaroid sheet P 1, it is observed that its intensity is reduced by half. Rotating P 1 has no effect on the transmitted beam and transmitted intensity remains constant. Now, let an identical piece of polaroid P 2 be placed before P 1 . As expected, the light from the lamp is reduced in intensity on passing through P 2 alone. But now rotating P 1 has a dramatic effect on the light coming from P 2 . In one position, the intensity transmitted by P 2 followed by P 1 is nearly zero. When turned by 90° from this position, P 1 transmits nearly the full intensity emerging from P 2 (Fig. 10.22).','[{"s": "Thus, if the light from an ordinary source (like a sodium lamp) passes through a polaroid sheet", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "it is observed that its intensity is reduced by half. Rotating", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has no effect on the transmitted beam and transmitted intensity remains constant. Now, let an identical piece of polaroid", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be placed before", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". As expected, the light from the lamp is reduced in intensity on passing through", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "alone. But now rotating P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has a dramatic effect on the light coming from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In one position, the intensity transmitted by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "followed by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is nearly zero. When turned by 90° from this position,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "transmits nearly the full intensity emerging from", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Fig. 10.22).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1065,11,45,'paragraph',NULL,'Glycocalyx differs in composition and thickness among different bacteria. It could be a loose sheath called the slime layer in some, while in others it may be thick and tough, called the capsule . The cell wall determines the shape of the cell and provides a strong structural support to prevent the bacterium from bursting or collapsing.','[{"s": "Glycocalyx differs in composition and thickness among different bacteria. It could be a loose sheath called the slime layer in some, while in others it may be thick and tough, called the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "capsule", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cell wall", "t": "text"}, {"s": "determines the shape of the cell and provides a strong structural support to prevent the bacterium from bursting or collapsing.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(34411,79,331,'paragraph',NULL,'where I 0 is the intensity of the polarized light after passing through P 1 . This is known as Malus’ law . The above discussion shows that the intensity coming out of a single polaroid is half of the incident intensity. By putting a second polaroid, the intensity can be further controlled from 50% to zero of the incident intensity by adjusting the angle between the pass-axes of two polaroids.','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the intensity of the polarized light after passing through", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Malus’ law", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The above discussion shows that the intensity coming out of a single polaroid is half of the incident intensity. By putting a second polaroid, the intensity can be further controlled from 50% to zero of the incident intensity by adjusting the angle between the pass-axes of two polaroids.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34412,79,332,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image30.png'),
(34413,79,333,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 10.22 (a) Passage of light through two polaroids P 2 and P 1 . The transmitted fraction falls from 1 to 0 as the angle between them varies from 0° to 90°. Notice that the light seen through a single polaroid P 1 does not vary with angle. (b) Behaviour of the electric vector when light passes through two polaroids. The transmitted polarisation is the component parallel to the polaroid axis.The double arrows show the oscillations of the electric vector.','[{"s": "Figure 10.22 (a) Passage of light through two polaroids P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The transmitted fraction falls from 1 to 0 as the angle between them varies from 0° to 90°. Notice that the light seen through a single polaroid P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "does not vary with angle. (b) Behaviour of the electric vector when light passes through two polaroids. The transmitted polarisation is the component parallel to the polaroid axis.The double arrows show the oscillations of the electric vector.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34414,79,334,'paragraph',NULL,'Polaroids can be used to control the intensity, in sunglasses, windowpanes, etc. Polaroids are also used in photographic cameras and 3D movie cameras.','[{"s": "Polaroids can be used to control the intensity, in sunglasses, windowpanes, etc. Polaroids are also used in photographic cameras and 3D movie cameras.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34415,79,335,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.8 Discuss the intensity of transmitted light when a polaroid sheet is rotated between two crossed polaroids?','[{"s": "Example 10.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Discuss the intensity of transmitted light when a polaroid sheet is rotated between two crossed polaroids?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34416,79,336,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution Let I 0 be the intensity of polarised light after passing through the first polariser P 1 . Then the intensity of light after passing through second polariser P 2 will be','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Let I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the intensity of polarised light after passing through the first polariser P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Then the intensity of light after passing through second polariser P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will be", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34417,79,337,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image32.png'),
(34418,79,338,'paragraph',NULL,'where θ is the angle between pass axes of P 1 and P 2 . Since P 1 and P 3 are crossed the angle between the pass axes of P 2 and P 3 3 will be','[{"s": "where θ is the angle between pass axes of P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are crossed the angle between the pass axes of P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will be", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34419,79,339,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image34.png'),
(34420,79,340,'paragraph',NULL,'= I 0 cos 2 θ sin 2 θ =(I 0 /4) sin 2 2θ','[{"s": "= I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ =(I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/4) sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2θ", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34421,79,341,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, the transmitted intensity will be maximum when θ = π/4.','[{"s": "Therefore, the transmitted intensity will be maximum when θ = π/4.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34422,79,342,'heading',4,'10.7.1 Polarisation by scattering',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34423,79,343,'paragraph',NULL,'The light from a clear blue portion of the sky shows a rise and fall of intensity when viewed through a polaroid which is rotated. This is nothing but sunlight, which has changed its direction (having been scattered) on encountering the molecules of the earth’s atmosphere. As Fig. 10.23(a) shows, the incident sunlight is unpolarised. The dots stand for polarisation perpendicular to the plane of the figure. The double arrows show polarisation in the plane of the figure. (There is no phase relation between these two in unpolarised light). Under the influence of the electric field of the incident wave the electrons in the molecules acquire components of motion in both these directions. We have drawn an observer looking at 90° to the direction of the sun. Clearly, charges accelerating parallel to the double arrows do not radiate energy towards this observer since their acceleration has no transverse component. The radiation scattered by the molecule is therefore represented by dots. It is polarised perpendicular to the plane of the figure. This explains the polarisation of scattered light from the sky.','[{"s": "The light from a clear blue portion of the sky shows a rise and fall of intensity when viewed through a polaroid which is rotated. This is nothing but sunlight, which has changed its direction (having been scattered) on encountering the molecules of the earth’s atmosphere. As Fig. 10.23(a) shows, the incident sunlight is unpolarised. The dots stand for polarisation perpendicular to the plane of the figure. The double arrows show polarisation in the plane of the figure. (There is no phase relation between these two in unpolarised light). Under the influence of the electric field of the incident wave the electrons in the molecules acquire components of motion in both these directions. We have drawn an observer looking at 90° to the direction of the sun. Clearly, charges accelerating parallel to the double arrows do not radiate energy towards this observer since their acceleration has no transverse component. The radiation scattered by the molecule is therefore represented by dots. It is polarised perpendicular to the plane of the figure. This explains the polarisation of scattered light from the sky.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34424,79,344,'image',NULL,'125',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image18.png'),
(34426,79,346,'paragraph',NULL,'The scattering of light by molecules was intensively investigated by C.V. Raman and his collaborators in Kolkata in the 1920s. Raman was awarded the Nobel Prize for Physics in 1930 for this work.','[{"s": "The scattering of light by molecules was intensively investigated by C.V. Raman and his collaborators in Kolkata in the 1920s. Raman was awarded the Nobel Prize for Physics in 1930 for this work.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34427,79,347,'heading',3,'A SPECIAL CASE OF TOTAL TRANSMISSION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34428,79,348,'paragraph',NULL,'When light is incident on an interface of two media, it is observed that some part of it gets reflected and some part gets transmitted. Consider a related question: Is it possible that under some conditions a monochromatic beam of light incident on a surface (which is normally reflective) gets completely transmitted with no reflection? To your surprise, the answer is yes.','[{"s": "When light is incident on an interface of two media, it is observed that some part of it gets reflected and some part gets transmitted. Consider a related question: Is it possible that under some conditions a monochromatic beam of light incident on a surface (which is normally reflective) gets completely transmitted with no reflection? To your surprise, the answer is yes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34429,79,349,'image',NULL,'Fig_10_25',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image19.png'),
(34430,79,350,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us try a simple experiment and check what happens. Arrange a laser, a good polariser, a prism and screen as shown in the figure here.','[{"s": "Let us try a simple experiment and check what happens. Arrange a laser, a good polariser, a prism and screen as shown in the figure here.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34431,79,351,'paragraph',NULL,'Let the light emitted by the laser source pass through the polariser and be incident on the surface of the prism at the Brewster’s angle of incidence i B . Now rotate the polariser carefully and you will observe that for a specific alignment of the polariser, the light incident on the prism is completely transmitted and no light is reflected from the surface of the prism. The reflected spot will completely vanish.','[{"s": "Let the light emitted by the laser source pass through the polariser and be incident on the surface of the prism at the Brewster’s angle of incidence i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Now rotate the polariser carefully and you will observe that for a specific alignment of the polariser, the light incident on the prism is completely transmitted and no light is reflected from the surface of the prism. The reflected spot will completely vanish.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34432,79,352,'heading',4,'10.7.2 Polarisation by reflection',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34433,79,353,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 10.23(b) shows light reflected from a transparent medium, say, water. As before, the dots and arrows indicate that both polarisations are present in the incident and refracted waves. We have drawn a situation in which the reflected wave travels at right angles to the refracted wave. The oscillating electrons in the water produce the reflected wave. These move in the two directions transverse to the radiation from wave in the medium, i.e., the refracted wave . The arrows are parallel to the direction of the reflected wave. Motion in this direction does not contribute to the reflected wave. As the figure shows, the reflected light is therefore linearly polarised perpendicular to the plane of the figure (represented by dots). This can be checked by looking at the reflected light through an analyser. The transmitted intensity will be zero when the axis of the analyser is in the plane of the figure, i.e., the plane of incidence.','[{"s": "Figure 10.23(b) shows light reflected from a transparent medium, say, water. As before, the dots and arrows indicate that both polarisations are present in the incident and refracted waves. We have drawn a situation in which the reflected wave travels at right angles to the refracted wave. The oscillating electrons in the water produce the reflected wave. These move in the two directions transverse to the radiation from wave in the medium, i.e., the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "refracted", "t": "text"}, {"s": "wave", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The arrows are parallel to the direction of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reflected", "t": "text"}, {"s": "wave. Motion in this direction does not contribute to the reflected wave. As the figure shows, the reflected light is therefore linearly polarised perpendicular to the plane of the figure (represented by dots). This can be checked by looking at the reflected light through an analyser. The transmitted intensity will be zero when the axis of the analyser is in the plane of the figure, i.e., the plane of incidence.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34434,79,354,'paragraph',NULL,'When unpolarised light is incident on the boundary between two transparent media, the reflected light is polarised with its electric vector perpendicular to the plane of incidence when the refracted and reflected rays make a right angle with each other. Thus we have seen that when reflected wave is perpendicular to the refracted wave, the reflected wave is a totally polarised wave. The angle of incidence in this case is called Brewster’s angle and is denoted by i B . We can see that i B is related to the refractive index of the denser medium. Since we have i B + r = π /2, we get from Snell’s law','[{"s": "When unpolarised light is incident on the boundary between two transparent media, the reflected light is polarised with its electric vector perpendicular to the plane of incidence when the refracted and reflected rays make a right angle with each other. Thus we have seen that when reflected wave is perpendicular to the refracted wave, the reflected wave is a totally polarised wave. The angle of incidence in this case is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Brewster’s angle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and is denoted by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We can see that", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is related to the refractive index of the denser medium. Since we have", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2, we get from Snell’s law", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34435,79,355,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Wave_Optics-image20.png'),
(34436,79,356,'paragraph',NULL,'Wave_Optics-image2.png (10.36)','[{"s": "Wave_Optics-image2.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(10.36)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34438,79,358,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.9 Unpolarised light is incident on a plane glass surface. What should be the angle of incidence so that the reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular to each other?','[{"s": "Example 10.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Unpolarised light is incident on a plane glass surface. What should be the angle of incidence so that the reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular to each other?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34439,79,359,'paragraph',NULL,'Solution For i + r to be equal to π/2, we should have tan iB = µ = 1.5. This gives i B','[{"s": "Solution", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For i + r to be equal to π/2, we should have tan iB = µ = 1.5. This gives i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34440,79,360,'paragraph',NULL,'For simplicity, we have discussed scattering of light by 90°, and reflection at the Brewster angle. In this special situation, one of the two perpendicular components of the electric field is zero. At other angles, both components are present but one is stronger than the other. There is no stable phase relationship between the two perpendicular components since these are derived from two perpendicular components of an unpolarised beam. When such light is viewed through a rotating analyser, one sees a maximum and a minimum of intensity but not complete darkness. This kind of light is called partially polarised .','[{"s": "For simplicity, we have discussed scattering of light by 90°, and reflection at the Brewster angle. In this special situation, one of the two perpendicular components of the electric field is zero. At other angles, both components are present but one is stronger than the other. There is no stable phase relationship between the two perpendicular components since these are derived from two perpendicular components of an unpolarised beam. When such light is viewed through a rotating analyser, one sees a maximum and a minimum of intensity but not complete darkness. This kind of light is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "partially polarised", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34441,79,361,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us try to understand the situation. When an unpolarised beam of light is incident at the Brewster’s angle on an interface of two media, only part of light with electric field vector perpendicular to the plane of incidence will be reflected. Now by using a good polariser, if we completely remove all the light with its electric vector perpendicular to the plane of incidence and let this light be incident on the surface of the prism at Brewster’s angle, you will then observe no reflection and there will be total transmission of light.','[{"s": "Let us try to understand the situation. When an unpolarised beam of light is incident at the Brewster’s angle on an interface of two media, only part of light with electric field vector perpendicular to the plane of incidence will be reflected. Now by using a good polariser, if we completely remove all the light with its electric vector perpendicular to the plane of incidence and let this light be incident on the surface of the prism at Brewster’s angle, you will then observe no reflection and there will be total transmission of light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34442,79,362,'paragraph',NULL,'We began this chapter by pointing out that there are some phenomena which can be explained only by the wave theory. In order to develop a proper understanding, we first described how some phenomena like reflection and refraction, which were studied on this basis of Ray Optics in Chapter 9, can also be understood on the basis of Wave Optics. Then we described Young’s double slit experiment which was a turning point in the study of optics. Finally, we described some associated points such as diffraction, resolution, polarisation, and validity of ray optics. In the next chapter, you will see how new experiments led to new theories at the turn of the century around 1900 A.D.','[{"s": "We began this chapter by pointing out that there are some phenomena which can be explained only by the wave theory. In order to develop a proper understanding, we first described how some phenomena like reflection and refraction, which were studied on this basis of Ray Optics in Chapter 9, can also be understood on the basis of Wave Optics. Then we described Young’s double slit experiment which was a turning point in the study of optics. Finally, we described some associated points such as diffraction, resolution, polarisation, and validity of ray optics. In the next chapter, you will see how new experiments led to new theories at the turn of the century around 1900 A.D.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34443,79,363,'heading',3,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34444,79,364,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Huygens’ principle tells us that each point on a wavefront is a source of secondary waves, which add up to give the wavefront at a later time.','[{"s": "1. Huygens’ principle tells us that each point on a wavefront is a source of secondary waves, which add up to give the wavefront at a later time.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34445,79,365,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Huygens’ construction tells us that the new wavefront is the forward envelope of the secondary waves. When the speed of light is independent of direction, the secondary waves are spherical. The rays are then perpendicular to both the wavefronts and the time of travel is the same measured along any ray. This principle leads to the well known laws of reflection and refraction.','[{"s": "2. Huygens’ construction tells us that the new wavefront is the forward envelope of the secondary waves. When the speed of light is independent of direction, the secondary waves are spherical. The rays are then perpendicular to both the wavefronts and the time of travel is the same measured along any ray. This principle leads to the well known laws of reflection and refraction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34446,79,366,'paragraph',NULL,'3. The principle of superposition of waves applies whenever two or more sources of light illuminate the same point. When we consider the intensity of light due to these sources at the given point, there is an interference term in addition to the sum of the individual intensities. But this term is important only if it has a non-zero average, which occurs only if the sources have the same frequency and a stable phase difference.','[{"s": "3. The principle of superposition of waves applies whenever two or more sources of light illuminate the same point. When we consider the intensity of light due to these sources at the given point, there is an interference term in addition to the sum of the individual intensities. But this term is important only if it has a non-zero average, which occurs only if the sources have the same frequency and a stable phase difference.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34447,79,367,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Young’s double slit of separation d gives equally spaced fringes of angular separation λ /d. The source, mid-point of the slits, and central bright fringe lie in a straight line. An extended source will destroy the fringes if it subtends angle more than λ /d at the slits.','[{"s": "4. Young’s double slit of separation d gives equally spaced fringes of angular separation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/d. The source, mid-point of the slits, and central bright fringe lie in a straight line. An extended source will destroy the fringes if it subtends angle more than", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/d at the slits.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34448,79,368,'paragraph',NULL,'5. A single slit of width a gives a diffraction pattern with a central maximum. The intensity falls to zero at angles of Wave_Optics-image4.png etc., with successively weaker secondary maxima in between. Diffraction limits the angular resolution of a telescope to λ /D where D is the diameter. Two stars closer than this give strongly overlapping images. Similarly, a microscope objective subtending angle 2 β at the focus, in a medium of refractive index n, will just separate two objects spaced at a distance λ /(2n sin β) , which is the resolution limit of a microscope. Diffraction determines the limitations of the concept of light rays. A beam of width atravels a distance a 2 / λ , called the Fresnel distance, before it starts to spread out due to diffraction.','[{"s": "5. A single slit of width a gives a diffraction pattern with a central maximum. The intensity falls to zero at angles of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Wave_Optics-image4.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "etc., with successively weaker secondary maxima in between. Diffraction limits the angular resolution of a telescope to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/D where D is the diameter. Two stars closer than this give strongly overlapping images. Similarly, a microscope objective subtending angle 2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at the focus, in a medium of refractive index n, will just separate two objects spaced at a distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/(2n sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "β)", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which is the resolution limit of a microscope. Diffraction determines the limitations of the concept of light rays. A beam of width atravels a distance a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", called the Fresnel distance, before it starts to spread out due to diffraction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34449,79,369,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Natural light, e.g., from the sun is unpolarised. This means the electric vector takes all possible directions in the transverse plane, rapidly and randomly, during a measurement. A polaroid transmits only one component (parallel to a special axis). The resulting light is called linearly polarised or plane polarised. When this kind of light is viewed through a second polaroid whose axis turns through 2 π , two maxima and minima of intensity are seen. Polarised light can also be produced by reflection at a special angle (called the Brewster angle) and by scattering through π /2 in the earth’s atmosphere.','[{"s": "6. Natural light, e.g., from the sun is unpolarised. This means the electric vector takes all possible directions in the transverse plane, rapidly and randomly, during a measurement. A polaroid transmits only one component (parallel to a special axis). The resulting light is called linearly polarised or plane polarised. When this kind of light is viewed through a second polaroid whose axis turns through 2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", two maxima and minima of intensity are seen. Polarised light can also be produced by reflection at a special angle (called the Brewster angle) and by scattering through", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2 in the earth’s atmosphere.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34450,79,370,'heading',3,'Points to Ponder',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34451,79,371,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Waves from a point source spread out in all directions, while light was seen to travel along narrow rays. It required the insight and experiment of Huygens, Young and Fresnel to understand how a wave theory could explain all aspects of the behaviour of light.','[{"s": "1. Waves from a point source spread out in all directions, while light was seen to travel along narrow rays. It required the insight and experiment of Huygens, Young and Fresnel to understand how a wave theory could explain all aspects of the behaviour of light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34452,79,372,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The crucial new feature of waves is interference of amplitudes from different sources which can be both constructive and destructive, as shown in Young’s experiment.','[{"s": "2. The crucial new feature of waves is interference of amplitudes from different sources which can be both constructive and destructive, as shown in Young’s experiment.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34453,79,373,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Diffraction phenomena define the limits of ray optics. The limit of the ability of microscopes and telescopes to distinguish very close objects is set by the wavelength of light.','[{"s": "3. Diffraction phenomena define the limits of ray optics. The limit of the ability of microscopes and telescopes to distinguish very close objects is set by the wavelength of light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34454,79,374,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Most interference and diffraction effects exist even for longitudinal waves like sound in air. But polarisation phenomena are special to transverse waves like light waves.','[{"s": "4. Most interference and diffraction effects exist even for longitudinal waves like sound in air. But polarisation phenomena are special to transverse waves like light waves.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34455,79,375,'heading',3,'Exercises',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34456,79,376,'paragraph',NULL,'10.1 Monochromatic light of wavelength 589 nm is incident from the air on a water surface. What are the wavelength, frequency, and speed of (a) reflected, and (b) refracted light? The Refractive index of water is 1.33.','[{"s": "10.1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Monochromatic light of wavelength 589 nm is incident from the air on a water surface. What are the wavelength, frequency, and speed of (a) reflected, and (b) refracted light? The Refractive index of water is 1.33.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34457,79,377,'paragraph',NULL,'10.2 What is the shape of the wavefront in each of the following cases:','[{"s": "10.2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What is the shape of the wavefront in each of the following cases:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34458,79,378,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) Light diverging from a point source.','[{"s": "(a) Light diverging from a point source.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34459,79,379,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Light emerging out of a convex lens when a point source is placed at its focus.','[{"s": "(b) Light emerging out of a convex lens when a point source is placed at its focus.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34460,79,380,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The portion of the wavefront of the light from a distant star intercepted by the Earth.','[{"s": "(c) The portion of the wavefront of the light from a distant star intercepted by the Earth.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34461,79,381,'paragraph',NULL,'10.3 (a) The refractive index of glass is 1.5. What is the speed of light in glass? (Speed of light in a vacuum is 3.0 × 10 8 m s –1 )','[{"s": "10.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) The refractive index of glass is 1.5. What is the speed of light in glass? (Speed of light in a vacuum is 3.0 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34462,79,382,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Is the speed of light in glass independent of the color of light? If not, which of the two colors red and violet travels slower in a glass prism?','[{"s": "(b) Is the speed of light in glass independent of the color of light? If not, which of the two colors red and violet travels slower in a glass prism?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
