(34472,79,392,'heading',3,'Additional Exercises',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34473,79,393,'paragraph',NULL,'10.11 The 6563 Å H α line emitted by hydrogen in a star is found to be red-shifted by 15 Å. Estimate the speed with which the star is receding from the Earth.','[{"s": "10.11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The 6563 Å H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "α", "t": "text"}, {"s": "line emitted by hydrogen in a star is found to be red-shifted by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Å. Estimate the speed with which the star is receding from the Earth.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34474,79,394,'paragraph',NULL,'10.12 Explain how Corpuscular theory predicts the speed of light in a medium, say, water, to be greater than the speed of light in a vacuum. Is the prediction confirmed by experimental determination of the speed of light in water? If not, which alternative picture of light is consistent with the experiment?','[{"s": "10.12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Explain how Corpuscular theory predicts the speed of light in a medium, say, water, to be greater than the speed of light in a vacuum. Is the prediction confirmed by experimental determination of the speed of light in water? If not, which alternative picture of light is consistent with the experiment?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34475,79,395,'paragraph',NULL,'10.13 You have learned in the text how Huygens’ principle leads to the laws of reflection and refraction. Use the same principle to deduce directly that a point object placed in front of a plane mirror produces a virtual image whose distance from the mirror is equal to the object distance from the mirror.','[{"s": "10.13", "t": "text"}, {"s": "You have learned in the text how Huygens’ principle leads to the laws of reflection and refraction. Use the same principle to deduce directly that a point object placed in front of a plane mirror produces a virtual image whose distance from the mirror is equal to the object distance from the mirror.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34476,79,396,'paragraph',NULL,'10.14 Let us list some of the factors, which could possibly influence the speed of wave propagation:','[{"s": "10.14", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Let us list some of the factors, which could possibly influence the speed of wave propagation:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34477,79,397,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) nature of the source.','[{"s": "(i) nature of the source.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34478,79,398,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) the direction of propagation.','[{"s": "(ii) the direction of propagation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34479,79,399,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) the motion of the source and/or observer.','[{"s": "(iii) the motion of the source and/or observer.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34480,79,400,'paragraph',NULL,'(iv) wavelength.','[{"s": "(iv) wavelength.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34481,79,401,'paragraph',NULL,'(v) the intensity of the wave.','[{"s": "(v) the intensity of the wave.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34482,79,402,'paragraph',NULL,'On which of these factors, if any, does','[{"s": "On which of these factors, if any, does", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34483,79,403,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) the speed of light in vacuum,','[{"s": "(a) the speed of light in vacuum,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34484,79,404,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) the speed of light in a medium (say, glass or water), depends?','[{"s": "(b) the speed of light in a medium (say, glass or water), depends?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34485,79,405,'paragraph',NULL,'10.15 For sound waves, the Doppler formula for frequency shift differs slightly between the two situations: (i) source at rest; observer moving, and (ii) source moving; observer at rest. The exact Doppler formulas for the case of light waves in vacuum are, however, strictly identical for these situations. Explain why this should be so. Would you expect the formulas to be strictly identical to the two situations in case of light traveling in a medium?','[{"s": "10.15", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For sound waves, the Doppler formula for frequency shift differs slightly between the two situations: (i) source at rest; observer moving, and (ii) source moving; observer at rest. The exact Doppler formulas for the case of light waves in vacuum are, however, strictly identical for these situations. Explain why this should be so. Would you expect the formulas to be strictly identical to the two situations in case of light traveling in a medium?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34487,79,407,'paragraph',NULL,'10.17 Answer the following questions:','[{"s": "10.17", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Answer the following questions:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34488,79,408,'paragraph',NULL,'(a) In a single slit diffraction experiment, the width of the slit is made double the original width. How does this affect the size and intensity of the central diffraction band?','[{"s": "(a) In a single slit diffraction experiment, the width of the slit is made double the original width. How does this affect the size and intensity of the central diffraction band?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34489,79,409,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) In what way is diffraction from each slit related to the interference pattern in a double-slit experiment?','[{"s": "(b) In what way is diffraction from each slit related to the interference pattern in a double-slit experiment?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34490,79,410,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) When a tiny circular obstacle is placed in the path of light from a distant source, a bright spot is seen at the center of the shadow of the obstacle. Explain why?','[{"s": "(c) When a tiny circular obstacle is placed in the path of light from a distant source, a bright spot is seen at the center of the shadow of the obstacle. Explain why?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34491,79,411,'paragraph',NULL,'(e) Ray optics is based on the assumption that light travels in a straight line. Diffraction effects (observed when light propagates through small apertures/slits or around small obstacles) disprove this assumption. Yet the ray optics assumption is so commonly used in an understanding location and several other properties of images in optical instruments. What is the justification?','[{"s": "(e) Ray optics is based on the assumption that light travels in a straight line. Diffraction effects (observed when light propagates through small apertures/slits or around small obstacles) disprove this assumption. Yet the ray optics assumption is so commonly used in an understanding location and several other properties of images in optical instruments. What is the justification?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34492,79,412,'paragraph',NULL,'10.18 Two towers on top of two hills are 40 km apart. The line joining them passes 50 m above a hill halfway between the towers. What is the longest wavelength of radio waves, which can be sent between the towers without appreciable diffraction effects?','[{"s": "10.18", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two towers on top of two hills are 40 km apart. The line joining them passes 50 m above a hill halfway between the towers. What is the longest wavelength of radio waves, which can be sent between the towers without appreciable diffraction effects?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34493,79,413,'paragraph',NULL,'10.19 A parallel beam of light of wavelength 500 nm falls on a narrow slit and the resulting diffraction pattern is observed on a screen 1 m away. It is observed that the first minimum is at a distance of 2.5 mm from the center of the screen. Find the width of the slit.','[{"s": "10.19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A parallel beam of light of wavelength 500 nm falls on a narrow slit and the resulting diffraction pattern is observed on a screen 1 m away. It is observed that the first minimum is at a distance of 2.5 mm from the center of the screen. Find the width of the slit.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
