(34692,80,125,'heading',4,'15.4 The speed of a travelling wave',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34669,80,102,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 15.2 A wave travelling along a string is described by,','[{"s": "Example 15.2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A wave travelling along a string is described by,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040378", "2000041474"]',NULL),
(34693,80,126,'paragraph',NULL,'To determine the speed of propagation of a travelling wave, we can fix our attention on any particular point on the wave (characterised by some value of the phase) and see how that point moves in time. It is convenient to look at the motion of the crest of the wave. Fig. 15.8 gives the shape of the wave at two instants of time, which differ by a small time internal ∆t. The entire wave pattern is seen to shift to the right (positive direction of x-axis) by a distance ∆x. In particular, the crest shown by a dot ( Waves-image12.png ) moves a','[{"s": "To determine the speed of propagation of a travelling wave, we can fix our attention on any particular point on the wave (characterised by some value of the phase) and see how that point moves in time. It is convenient to look at the motion of the crest of the wave. Fig. 15.8 gives the shape of the wave at two instants of time, which differ by a small time internal ∆t. The entire wave pattern is seen to shift to the right (positive direction of x-axis) by a distance ∆x. In particular, the crest shown by a dot (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image12.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ") moves a", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34694,80,127,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image32.png'),
(34695,80,128,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 15.8 Progression of a harmonic wave from time t to t + ∆t. where ∆t is a small interval. The wave pattern as a whole shifts to the right. The crest of the wave (or a point with any fixed phase) moves right by the distance ∆x in time ∆t.','[{"s": "Fig. 15.8 Progression of a harmonic wave from time t to t + ∆t. where ∆t is a small interval. The wave pattern as a whole shifts to the right. The crest of the wave (or a point with any fixed phase) moves right by the distance ∆x in time ∆t.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34696,80,129,'paragraph',NULL,'distance ∆x in time ∆t. The speed of the wave is then ∆x/∆t. We can put the dot ( Waves-image12.png ) on a point with any other phase. It will move with the same speed v (otherwise the wave pattern will not remain fixed). The motion of a fixed phase point on the wave is given by','[{"s": "distance ∆x in time ∆t. The speed of the wave is then ∆x/∆t. We can put the dot (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image12.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ") on a point with any other phase. It will move with the same speed v (otherwise the wave pattern will not remain fixed). The motion of a fixed phase point on the wave is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34697,80,130,'paragraph',NULL,'kx – ωt = constant (15.10)','[{"s": "kx – ωt = constant (15.10)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34698,80,131,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, as time t changes, the position x of the fixed phase point must change so that the phase remains constant. Thus,','[{"s": "Thus, as time t changes, the position x of the fixed phase point must change so that the phase remains constant. Thus,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34699,80,132,'paragraph',NULL,'kx – ωt = k(x+∆x) – ω(t+∆t)','[{"s": "kx – ωt = k(x+∆x) – ω(t+∆t)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34700,80,133,'paragraph',NULL,'or k ∆x – ω ∆t =0','[{"s": "or k ∆x – ω ∆t =0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34702,80,135,'paragraph',NULL,'Waves-image69.png (15.11)','[{"s": "Waves-image69.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(15.11)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34704,80,137,'paragraph',NULL,'Waves-image67.png (15.12)','[{"s": "Waves-image67.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(15.12)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34705,80,138,'paragraph',NULL,'Eq. (15.12), a general relation for all progressive waves, shows that in the time required for one full oscillation by any constituent of the medium, the wave pattern travels a distance equal to the wavelength of the wave. It should be noted that the speed of a mechanical wave is determined by the inertial (linear mass density for strings, mass density in general) and elastic properties (Young’s modulus for linear media/ shear modulus, bulk modulus) of the medium. The medium determines the speed; Eq. (15.12) then relates wavelength to frequency for the given speed. Of course, as remarked earlier, the medium can support both transverse and longitudinal waves, which will have different speeds in the same medium. Later in this chapter, we shall obtain specific expressions for the speed of mechanical waves in some media.','[{"s": "Eq. (15.12), a general relation for all progressive waves, shows that in the time required for one full oscillation by any constituent of the medium, the wave pattern travels a distance equal to the wavelength of the wave. It should be noted that the speed of a mechanical wave is determined by the inertial (linear mass density for strings, mass density in general) and elastic properties (Young’s modulus for linear media/ shear modulus, bulk modulus) of the medium. The medium determines the speed; Eq. (15.12) then relates wavelength to frequency for the given speed. Of course, as remarked earlier, the medium can support both transverse and longitudinal waves, which will have different speeds in the same medium. Later in this chapter, we shall obtain specific expressions for the speed of mechanical waves in some media.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34706,80,139,'heading',4,'15.4.1 Speed of a Transverse Wave on Stretched String',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34707,80,140,'paragraph',NULL,'The speed of a mechanical wave is determined by the restoring force setup in the medium when it is disturbed and the inertial properties (mass density) of the medium. The speed is expected to be directly related to the former and inversely to the latter. For waves on a string, the restoring force is provided by the tension T in the string. The inertial property will in this case be linear mass density µ, which is mass m of the string divided by its length L. Using Newton’s Laws of Motion, an exact formula for the wave speed on a string can be derived, but this derivation is outside the scope of this book. We shall, therefore, use dimensional analysis. We already know that dimensional analysis alone can never yield the exact formula. The overall dimensionless constant is always left undetermined by dimensional analysis.','[{"s": "The speed of a mechanical wave is determined by the restoring force setup in the medium when it is disturbed and the inertial properties (mass density) of the medium. The speed is expected to be directly related to the former and inversely to the latter. For waves on a string, the restoring force is provided by the tension T in the string. The inertial property will in this case be linear mass density µ, which is mass m of the string divided by its length L. Using Newton’s Laws of Motion, an exact formula for the wave speed on a string can be derived, but this derivation is outside the scope of this book. We shall, therefore, use dimensional analysis. We already know that dimensional analysis alone can never yield the exact formula. The overall dimensionless constant is always left undetermined by dimensional analysis.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34708,80,141,'paragraph',NULL,'The dimension of µ is [ML –1 ] and that of T is like force, namely [MLT –2 ]. We need to combine these dimensions to get the dimension of speed v [LT –1 ]. Simple inspection shows that the quantity T/µ has the relevant dimension','[{"s": "The dimension of µ is [ML", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] and that of T is like force, namely [MLT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]. We need to combine these dimensions to get the dimension of speed v [LT", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]. Simple inspection shows that the quantity T/µ has the relevant dimension", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34709,80,142,'image',NULL,'equation',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image72.png'),
(34710,80,143,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus if T and µ are assumed to be the only relevant physical quantities,','[{"s": "Thus if T and µ are assumed to be the only relevant physical quantities,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34711,80,144,'paragraph',NULL,'v = C √ T/μ (15.13)','[{"s": "v = C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "√", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T/μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(15.13)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34713,80,146,'paragraph',NULL,'v = √ T/μ (15.14)','[{"s": "v =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "√", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T/μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(15.14)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34714,80,147,'paragraph',NULL,'Note the important point that the speed v depends only on the properties of the medium T and µ (T is a property of the stretched string arising due to an external force). It does not depend on wavelength or frequency of the wave itself. In higher studies, you will come across waves whose speed is not independent of frequency of the wave. Of the two parameters λ and ν the source of disturbance determines the frequency of the wave generated. Given the speed of the wave in the medium and the frequency Eq. (15.12) then fixes the wavelength','[{"s": "Note the important point that the speed v depends only on the properties of the medium T and µ (T is a property of the stretched string arising due to an external force). It does not depend on wavelength or frequency of the wave itself. In higher studies, you will come across waves whose speed is not independent of frequency of the wave. Of the two parameters λ and ν the source of disturbance determines the frequency of the wave generated. Given the speed of the wave in the medium and the frequency Eq. (15.12) then fixes the wavelength", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34715,80,148,'paragraph',NULL,'Waves-image93.png (15.15)','[{"s": "Waves-image93.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(15.15)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34716,80,149,'paragraph',NULL,'Propagation of a pulse on a rope','[{"s": "Propagation of a pulse on a rope", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34717,80,150,'paragraph',NULL,'You can easily see the motion of a pulse on a rope. You can also see its reflection from a rigid boundary and measure its velocity of travel. You will need a rope of diameter 1 to 3 cm, two hooks and some weights. You can perform this experiment in your classroom or laboratory.','[{"s": "You can easily see the motion of a pulse on a rope. You can also see its reflection from a rigid boundary and measure its velocity of travel. You will need a rope of diameter 1 to 3 cm, two hooks and some weights. You can perform this experiment in your classroom or laboratory.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34718,80,151,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image53.png'),
(34719,80,152,'paragraph',NULL,'Take a long rope or thick string of diameter 1 to 3 cm, and tie it to hooks on opposite walls in a hall or laboratory. Let one end pass on a hook and hang some weight (about 1 to 5 kg) to it. The walls may be about 3 to 5 m apart.','[{"s": "Take a long rope or thick string of diameter 1 to 3 cm, and tie it to hooks on opposite walls in a hall or laboratory. Let one end pass on a hook and hang some weight (about 1 to 5 kg) to it. The walls may be about 3 to 5 m apart.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34720,80,153,'paragraph',NULL,'Take a stick or a rod and strike the rope hard at a point near one end. This creates a pulse on the rope which now travels on it. You can see it reaching the end and reflecting back from it. You can check the phase relation between the incident pulse and reflected pulse. You can easily watch two or three reflections before the pulse dies out. You can take a stopwatch and find the time for the pulse to travel the distance between the walls, and thus measure its velocity. Compare it with that obtained from Eq. (15.14).','[{"s": "Take a stick or a rod and strike the rope hard at a point near one end. This creates a pulse on the rope which now travels on it. You can see it reaching the end and reflecting back from it. You can check the phase relation between the incident pulse and reflected pulse. You can easily watch two or three reflections before the pulse dies out. You can take a stopwatch and find the time for the pulse to travel the distance between the walls, and thus measure its velocity. Compare it with that obtained from Eq. (15.14).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34721,80,154,'paragraph',NULL,'This is also what happens with a thin metallic string of a musical instrument. The major difference is that the velocity on a string is fairly high because of low mass per unit length, as compared to that on a thick rope. The low velocity on a rope allows us to watch the motion and make measurements beautifully.','[{"s": "This is also what happens with a thin metallic string of a musical instrument. The major difference is that the velocity on a string is fairly high because of low mass per unit length, as compared to that on a thick rope. The low velocity on a rope allows us to watch the motion and make measurements beautifully.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34722,80,155,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 15.3 A steel wire 0.72 m long has a mass of 5.0 ×10 –3 kg. If the wire is under a tension of 60 N, what is the speed of transverse waves on the wire ?','[{"s": "Example 15.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A steel wire 0.72 m long has a mass of 5.0 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg. If the wire is under a tension of 60 N, what is the speed of transverse waves on the wire ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34723,80,156,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34724,80,157,'paragraph',NULL,'Mass per unit length of the wire,','[{"s": "Mass per unit length of the wire,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34725,80,158,'paragraph',NULL,'μ= 5.0×10 −3 kg/0.72 m','[{"s": "μ=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5.0×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg/0.72 m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34726,80,159,'paragraph',NULL,'= 6.9 ×10 –3 kg m –1','[{"s": "= 6.9 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34727,80,160,'paragraph',NULL,'Tension, T = 60 N','[{"s": "Tension, T = 60 N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34728,80,161,'paragraph',NULL,'The speed of wave on the wire is given by','[{"s": "The speed of wave on the wire is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34729,80,162,'paragraph',NULL,'v= √ T/μ =√ 60 N6.9×10 −3 kg m −1 =93 ms −1','[{"s": "v=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "√", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T/μ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=√", "t": "text"}, {"s": "60 N6.9×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=93 ms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34730,80,163,'heading',4,'15.4.2 Speed of a Longitudinal Wave (Speed of Sound)',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34731,80,164,'paragraph',NULL,'In a longitudinal wave, the constituents of the medium oscillate forward and backward in the direction of propagation of the wave. We have already seen that the sound waves travel in the form of compressions and rarefactions of small volume elements of air. The elastic property that determines the stress under compressional strain is the bulk modulus of the medium defined by (see Chapter 9)','[{"s": "In a longitudinal wave, the constituents of the medium oscillate forward and backward in the direction of propagation of the wave. We have already seen that the sound waves travel in the form of compressions and rarefactions of small volume elements of air. The elastic property that determines the stress under compressional strain is the bulk modulus of the medium defined by (see Chapter 9)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34732,80,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Waves-image77.png (15.16)','[{"s": "Waves-image77.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(15.16)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34733,80,166,'paragraph',NULL,'Here, the change in pressure ∆P produces a volumetric strain ΔV/V . B has the same dimension as pressure and given in SI units in terms of pascal (Pa). The inertial property relevant for the propagation of wave is the mass density ρ, with dimensions [ML –3 ]. Simple inspection reveals that quantity B/ρ has the relevant dimension:','[{"s": "Here, the change in pressure ∆P produces a volumetric strain", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ΔV/V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". B has the same dimension as pressure and given in SI units in terms of pascal (Pa). The inertial property relevant for the propagation of wave is the mass density ρ, with dimensions [ML", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]. Simple inspection reveals that quantity B/ρ has the relevant dimension:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34734,80,167,'paragraph',NULL,'Waves-image71.png (15.17)','[{"s": "Waves-image71.png", "t": "img", "alt": "eq1"}, {"s": "(15.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34735,80,168,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, if B and ρ are considered to be the only relevant physical quantities,','[{"s": "Thus, if B and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ρ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are considered to be the only relevant physical quantities,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34736,80,169,'paragraph',NULL,'v = C √ B/ρ (15.18)','[{"s": "v = C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "√", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B/ρ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(15.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34737,80,170,'paragraph',NULL,'where, as before, C is the undetermined constant from dimensional analysis. The exact derivation shows that C=1. Thus, the general formula for longitudinal waves in a medium is:','[{"s": "where, as before, C is the undetermined constant from dimensional analysis. The exact derivation shows that C=1. Thus, the general formula for longitudinal waves in a medium is:", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34738,80,171,'paragraph',NULL,'v = √ B/ρ (15.19)','[{"s": "v =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "√", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B/ρ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(15.19)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34739,80,172,'paragraph',NULL,'For a linear medium, like a solid bar, the lateral expansion of the bar is negligible and we may consider it to be only under longitudinal strain. In that case, the relevant modulus of elasticity is Young’s modulus, which has the same dimension as the Bulk modulus. Dimensional analysis for this case is the same as before and yields a relation like Eq. (15.18), with an undetermined C, which the exact derivation shows to be unity. Thus, the speed of longitudinal waves in a solid bar is given by','[{"s": "For a linear medium, like a solid bar, the lateral expansion of the bar is negligible and we may consider it to be only under longitudinal strain. In that case, the relevant modulus of elasticity is Young’s modulus, which has the same dimension as the Bulk modulus. Dimensional analysis for this case is the same as before and yields a relation like Eq. (15.18), with an undetermined C, which the exact derivation shows to be unity. Thus, the speed of longitudinal waves in a solid bar is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34740,80,173,'paragraph',NULL,'v = √ γ/ρ (15.20)','[{"s": "v =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "√", "t": "text"}, {"s": "γ/ρ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(15.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34741,80,174,'paragraph',NULL,'where Y is the Young’s modulus of the material of the bar. Table 15.1 gives the speed of sound in some media.','[{"s": "where Y is the Young’s modulus of the material of the bar. Table 15.1 gives the speed of sound in some media.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34742,80,175,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image3.png'),
(34743,80,176,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 15.1 Speed of Sound in some Media','[{"s": "Table 15.1 Speed of Sound in some Media", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34744,80,177,'paragraph',NULL,'Liquids and solids generally have higher speed of sound than gases. [Note for solids, the speed being referred to is the speed of longitudinal waves in the solid]. This happens because they are much more difficult to compress than gases and so have much higher values of bulk modulus. Now, see Eq. (15.19). Solids and liquids have higher mass densities ( Waves-image60.png ) than gases. But the corresponding increase in both the modulus (B) of solids and liquids is much higher. This is the reason why the sound waves travel faster in solids and liquids.','[{"s": "Liquids and solids generally have higher speed of sound than gases. [Note for solids, the speed being referred to is the speed of longitudinal waves in the solid]. This happens because they are much more difficult to compress than gases and so have much higher values of bulk modulus. Now, see Eq. (15.19). Solids and liquids have higher mass densities (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image60.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ") than gases. But the corresponding increase in both the modulus (B) of solids and liquids is much higher. This is the reason why the sound waves travel faster in solids and liquids.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34745,80,178,'paragraph',NULL,'We can estimate the speed of sound in a gas in the ideal gas approximation. For an ideal gas, the pressure P, volume V and temperature T are related by (see Chapter 11).','[{"s": "We can estimate the speed of sound in a gas in the ideal gas approximation. For an ideal gas, the pressure P, volume V and temperature T are related by (see Chapter 11).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34746,80,179,'paragraph',NULL,'PV = Nk B T (15.21)','[{"s": "PV = Nk", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T (15.21)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
