(34895,80,328,'paragraph',NULL,'which may be written as :','[{"s": "which may be written as :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34896,80,329,'paragraph',NULL,'s = [2 a cos ω b t] cos ω a t (15.47)','[{"s": "s = [2 a cos ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t] cos ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t (15.47)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34897,80,330,'paragraph',NULL,'If |ω 1 – ω 2 | <<ω 1 , ω 2 , ω a >> ω b , th','[{"s": "If |ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "| <<ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": ">> ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", th", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34898,80,331,'paragraph',NULL,'where','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34899,80,332,'paragraph',NULL,'ω b = Waves-image26.png and ω a = Waves-image36.png','[{"s": "ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image26.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image36.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34900,80,333,'paragraph',NULL,'Now if we assume |ω 1 – ω 2 | <<ω 1 , which means ωa >> ωb, we can interpret Eq. (15.47) as follows. The resultant wave is oscillating with the average angular frequency ωa; however its amplitude is not constant in time, unlike a pure harmonic wave. The amplitude is the largest when the term cos ωb t takes its limit +1 or –1. In other words, the intensity of the resultant wave waxes and wanes with a frequency which is 2ω b = ω 1 – ω 2 . Since ω = 2πν, the beat frequency νbeat, is given by','[{"s": "Now if we assume |ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "| <<ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", which means ωa >> ωb, we can interpret Eq. (15.47) as follows. The resultant wave is oscillating with the average angular frequency ωa; however its amplitude is not constant in time, unlike a pure harmonic wave. The amplitude is the largest when the term cos ωb t takes its limit +1 or –1. In other words, the intensity of the resultant wave waxes and wanes with a frequency which is 2ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "b", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since ω = 2πν, the beat frequency νbeat, is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34901,80,334,'paragraph',NULL,'ν beat = ν 1 – ν 2 (15.48)','[{"s": "ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "beat", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(15.48)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34902,80,335,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 15.16 illustrates the phenomenon of beats for two harmonic waves of frequencies 11 Hz and 9 Hz. The amplitude of the resultant wave shows beats at a frequency of 2 Hz.','[{"s": "Fig. 15.16 illustrates the phenomenon of beats for two harmonic waves of frequencies 11 Hz and 9 Hz. The amplitude of the resultant wave shows beats at a frequency of 2 Hz.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34903,80,336,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image108.png'),
(34904,80,337,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 15.16 Superposition of two harmonic waves, one of frequency 11 Hz (a), and the other of frequency 9Hz (b), giving rise to beats of frequency 2 Hz, as shown in (c).','[{"s": "Fig. 15.16 Superposition of two harmonic waves, one of frequency 11 Hz (a), and the other of frequency 9Hz (b), giving rise to beats of frequency 2 Hz, as shown in (c).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34905,80,338,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 15.6 Two sitar strings A and B playing the note ‘Dha’ are slightly out of tune and produce beats of frequency 5 Hz. The tension of the string B is slightly increased and the beat frequency is found to decrease to 3 Hz. What is the original frequency of B if the frequency of A is 427 Hz ?','[{"s": "Example 15.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Two sitar strings A and B playing the note ‘Dha’ are slightly out of tune and produce beats of frequency 5 Hz. The tension of the string B is slightly increased and the beat frequency is found to decrease to 3 Hz. What is the original frequency of B if the frequency of A is 427 Hz ?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34906,80,339,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34907,80,340,'paragraph',NULL,'Increase in the tension of a string increases its frequency. If the original frequency of B (ν B ) were greater than that of A (ν A ), further increase in ν B should have resulted in an increase in the beat frequency. But the beat frequency is found to decrease. This shows that ν B < ν A . Since ν A – ν B = 5 Hz, and ν A = 427 Hz, we get ν B = 422 Hz.','[{"s": "Increase in the tension of a string increases its frequency. If the original frequency of B (ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") were greater than that of A (ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), further increase in ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "should have resulted in an increase in the beat frequency. But the beat frequency is found to decrease. This shows that ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 5 Hz, and ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 427 Hz, we get ν", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 422 Hz.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34908,80,341,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image106.png'),
(34909,80,342,'heading',4,'Musical Pillars',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34910,80,343,'paragraph',NULL,'Temples often have some pillars portraying human figures playing musical instru- ments, but seldom do these pillars themselves produce music. At the Nellaiappar temple in Tamil Nadu, gentle taps on a cluster of pillars carved out of a single piece of rock produce the basic notes of Indian classical music, viz. Sa, Re, Ga, Ma, Pa, Dha, Ni, Sa. Vibrations of these pillars depend on elasticity of the stone used, its density and shape.','[{"s": "Temples often have some pillars portraying human figures playing musical instru- ments, but seldom do these pillars themselves produce music. At the Nellaiappar temple in Tamil Nadu, gentle taps on a cluster of pillars carved out of a single piece of rock produce the basic notes of Indian classical music, viz. Sa, Re, Ga, Ma, Pa, Dha, Ni, Sa. Vibrations of these pillars depend on elasticity of the stone used, its density and shape.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34911,80,344,'paragraph',NULL,'Musical pillars are categorised into three types: The first is called the Shruti Pillar, as it can produce the basic notes — the “swaras”. The second type is the Gana Thoongal, which generates the basic tunes that make up the “ragas”. The third variety is the Laya Thoongal pillars that produce “taal” (beats) when tapped. The pillars at the Nellaiappar temple are a combination of the Shruti and Laya types.','[{"s": "Musical pillars are categorised into three types: The first is called the Shruti Pillar, as it can produce the basic notes — the “swaras”. The second type is the Gana Thoongal, which generates the basic tunes that make up the “ragas”. The third variety is the Laya Thoongal pillars that produce “taal” (beats) when tapped. The pillars at the Nellaiappar temple are a combination of the Shruti and Laya types.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34912,80,345,'paragraph',NULL,'Archaeologists date the Nelliappar temple to the 7th century and claim it was built by successive rulers of the Pandyan dynasty.','[{"s": "Archaeologists date the Nelliappar temple to the 7th century and claim it was built by successive rulers of the Pandyan dynasty.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34913,80,346,'paragraph',NULL,'The musical pillars of Nelliappar and several other temples in southern India like those at Hampi (picture), Kanyakumari, and Thiruvananthapuram are unique to the country and have no parallel in any other part of the world.','[{"s": "The musical pillars of Nelliappar and several other temples in southern India like those at Hampi (picture), Kanyakumari, and Thiruvananthapuram are unique to the country and have no parallel in any other part of the world.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34914,80,347,'paragraph',NULL,'15.8 Doppler effect','[{"s": "15.8 Doppler effect", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34916,80,349,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall analyse changes in frequency under three different situations: (1) observer is stationary but the source is moving, (2) observer is moving but the source is stationary, and (3) both the observer and the source are moving. The situations (1) and (2) differ from each other because of the absence or presence of relative motion between the observer and the medium. Most waves require a medium for their propagation; however, electromagnetic waves do not require any medium for propagation. If there is no medium present, the Doppler shifts are same irrespective of whether the source moves or the observer moves, since there is no way of distinction between the two situations.','[{"s": "We shall analyse changes in frequency under three different situations: (1) observer is stationary but the source is moving, (2) observer is moving but the source is stationary, and (3) both the observer and the source are moving. The situations (1) and (2) differ from each other because of the absence or presence of relative motion between the observer and the medium. Most waves require a medium for their propagation; however, electromagnetic waves do not require any medium for propagation. If there is no medium present, the Doppler shifts are same irrespective of whether the source moves or the observer moves, since there is no way of distinction between the two situations.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34917,80,350,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image1.png'),
(34918,80,351,'heading',4,'Reflection of sound in an open pipe',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34919,80,352,'paragraph',NULL,'When a high pressure pulse of air travelling down an open pipe reaches the other end, its momentum drags the air out into the open, where pressure falls rapidly to the atmospheric pressure. As a result the air following after it in the tube is pushed out. The low pressure at the end of the tube draws air from further up the tube. The air gets drawn towards the open end forcing the low pressure region to move upwards. As a result a pulse of high pressure air travelling down the tube turns into a pulse of low pressure air travelling up the tube. We say a pressure wave has been reflected at the open end with a change in phase of 1800. Standing waves in an open pipe organ like the flute is a result of this phenomenon.','[{"s": "When a high pressure pulse of air travelling down an open pipe reaches the other end, its momentum drags the air out into the open, where pressure falls rapidly to the atmospheric pressure. As a result the air following after it in the tube is pushed out. The low pressure at the end of the tube draws air from further up the tube. The air gets drawn towards the open end forcing the low pressure region to move upwards. As a result a pulse of high pressure air travelling down the tube turns into a pulse of low pressure air travelling up the tube. We say a pressure wave has been reflected at the open end with a change in phase of 1800. Standing waves in an open pipe organ like the flute is a result of this phenomenon.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34920,80,353,'paragraph',NULL,'Compare this with what happens when a pulse of high pressure air arrives at a closed end: it collides and as a result pushes the air back in the opposite direction. Here, we say that the pressure wave is reflected, with no change in phase.','[{"s": "Compare this with what happens when a pulse of high pressure air arrives at a closed end: it collides and as a result pushes the air back in the opposite direction. Here, we say that the pressure wave is reflected, with no change in phase.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
