(34958,80,391,'paragraph',NULL,'the observer counts n crests and the observer records the period of the wave as equal to T given by','[{"s": "the observer counts n crests and the observer records the period of the wave as equal to T given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34959,80,392,'paragraph',NULL,'T=T 0 (1+ v s −v o / v+v o )=T 0 ( v s +v / v+v o ) (15.54)','[{"s": "T=T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(1+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v+v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")=T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v+v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(15.54)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34962,80,395,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider a passenger sitting in a train moving on a straight track. Suppose she hears a whistle sounded by the driver of the train. What frequency will she measure or hear? Here both the observer and the source are moving with the same velocity, so there will be no shift in frequency and the passenger will note the natural frequency. But an observer outside who is stationary with respect to the track will note a higher frequency if the train is approaching him and a lower frequency when it recedes from him.','[{"s": "Consider a passenger sitting in a train moving on a straight track. Suppose she hears a whistle sounded by the driver of the train. What frequency will she measure or hear? Here both the observer and the source are moving with the same velocity, so there will be no shift in frequency and the passenger will note the natural frequency. But an observer outside who is stationary with respect to the track will note a higher frequency if the train is approaching him and a lower frequency when it recedes from him.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34963,80,396,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image17.png'),
(34961,80,394,'paragraph',NULL,'Waves-image89.png (15.55)','[{"s": "Waves-image89.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(15.55)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040833"]',NULL),
(34964,80,397,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 15.18 Doppler effect when both the source and observer are moving with different velocities.','[{"s": "Fig. 15.18 Doppler effect when both the source and observer are moving with different velocities.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(34965,80,398,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that we have defined the direction from the observer to the source as the positive direction. Therefore, if the observer is moving towards the source, v o has a positive (numerical) value whereas if O is moving away from S, v o has a negative value. On the other hand, if S is moving away from O, vs has a positive value whereas if it is moving towards O, vs has a negative value. The sound emitted by the source travels in all directions. It is that part of sound coming towards the observer which the observer receives and detects. Therefore, the relative velocity of sound with respect to the observer is v+v o in all cases.','[{"s": "Note that we have defined the direction from the observer to the source as the positive direction. Therefore, if the observer is moving towards the source, v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has a positive (numerical) value whereas if O is moving away from S, v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has a negative value. On the other hand, if S is moving away from O, vs has a positive value whereas if it is moving towards O, vs has a negative value. The sound emitted by the source travels in all directions. It is that part of sound coming towards the observer which the observer receives and detects. Therefore, the relative velocity of sound with respect to the observer is v+v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in all cases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34967,80,400,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34968,80,401,'paragraph',NULL,'(1) The observer is at rest and the source is moving with a speed of 200 ms –1 . Since this is comparable with the velocity of sound, 330 m s –1 , we must use Eq. (15.50) and not the approximate Eq. (15.51). Since the source is approaching a stationary target, v o = 0, and vs must be replaced by –v s . Thus, we have','[{"s": "(1) The observer is at rest and the source is moving with a speed of 200 ms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Since this is comparable with the velocity of sound, 330 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", we must use Eq. (15.50) and not the approximate Eq. (15.51). Since the source is approaching a stationary target, v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0, and vs must be replaced by –v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, we have", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34969,80,402,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image105.png'),
(34970,80,403,'paragraph',NULL,'v = 1000 Hz × [1 – 200 m s –1 /330 ms –1 ] –1','[{"s": "v = 1000 Hz × [1 – 200 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/330 ms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34971,80,404,'paragraph',NULL,'= 2540 Hz','[{"s": "= 2540 Hz", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34972,80,405,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) The target is now the source (because it is the source of echo) and the rocket’s detector is now the detector or observer (because it detects echo). Thus, v s = 0 and v o has a positive value. The frequency of the sound emitted by the source (the target) is v, the frequency intercepted by the target and not v o . Therefore, the frequency as registered by the rocket is','[{"s": "(2) The target is now the source (because it is the source of echo) and the rocket’s detector is now the detector or observer (because it detects echo). Thus, v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 and v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has a positive value. The frequency of the sound emitted by the source (the target) is v, the frequency intercepted by the target and not v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Therefore, the frequency as registered by the rocket is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34973,80,406,'paragraph',NULL,'v′ = Waves-image5.png','[{"s": "v′ =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image5.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34974,80,407,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image10.png'),
(34975,80,408,'paragraph',NULL,'4080 Hz','[{"s": "4080 Hz", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34976,80,409,'heading',4,'Application of Doppler effect',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34977,80,410,'paragraph',NULL,'The change in frequency caused by a moving object due to Doppler effect is used to measure their velocities in diverse areas such as military, medical science, astrophysics, etc. It is also used by police to check over-speeding of vehicles.','[{"s": "The change in frequency caused by a moving object due to Doppler effect is used to measure their velocities in diverse areas such as military, medical science, astrophysics, etc. It is also used by police to check over-speeding of vehicles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34978,80,411,'paragraph',NULL,'A sound wave or electromagnetic wave of known frequency is sent towards a moving object. Some part of the wave is reflected from the object and its frequency is detected by the monitoring station. This change in frequency is called Doppler shift.','[{"s": "A sound wave or electromagnetic wave of known frequency is sent towards a moving object. Some part of the wave is reflected from the object and its frequency is detected by the monitoring station. This change in frequency is called Doppler shift.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34979,80,412,'paragraph',NULL,'It is used at airports to guide aircraft, and in the military to detect enemy aircraft. Astrophysicists use it to measure the velocities of stars.','[{"s": "It is used at airports to guide aircraft, and in the military to detect enemy aircraft. Astrophysicists use it to measure the velocities of stars.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34980,80,413,'paragraph',NULL,'Doctors use it to study heart beats and blood flow in different parts of the body. Here they use ulltrasonic waves, and in common practice, it is called sonography. Ultrasonic waves enter the body of the person, some of them are reflected back, and give information about motion of blood and pulsation of heart valves, as well as pulsation of the heart of the foetus. In the case of heart, the picture generated is called echocardiogram.','[{"s": "Doctors use it to study heart beats and blood flow in different parts of the body. Here they use ulltrasonic waves, and in common practice, it is called sonography. Ultrasonic waves enter the body of the person, some of them are reflected back, and give information about motion of blood and pulsation of heart valves, as well as pulsation of the heart of the foetus. In the case of heart, the picture generated is called echocardiogram.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34981,80,414,'heading',4,'Summary',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34982,80,415,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Mechanical waves can exist in material media and are governed by Newton’s Laws.','[{"s": "1. Mechanical waves can exist in material media and are governed by Newton’s Laws.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34983,80,416,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Transverse waves are waves in which the particles of the medium oscillate perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation.','[{"s": "2. Transverse waves are waves in which the particles of the medium oscillate perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34984,80,417,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Longitudinal waves are waves in which the particles of the medium oscillate along the direction of wave propagation.','[{"s": "3. Longitudinal waves are waves in which the particles of the medium oscillate along the direction of wave propagation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34985,80,418,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Progressive wave is a wave that moves from one point of medium to another.','[{"s": "4. Progressive wave is a wave that moves from one point of medium to another.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34986,80,419,'paragraph',NULL,'5. The displacement in a sinusoidal wave propagating in the positive x direction is given by','[{"s": "5. The displacement in a sinusoidal wave propagating in the positive x direction is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34987,80,420,'paragraph',NULL,'y (x, t) = a sin (kx – ωt + φ)','[{"s": "y (x, t) = a sin (kx – ωt + φ)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34988,80,421,'paragraph',NULL,'where a is the amplitude of the wave, k is the angular wave number, ω is the angular frequency, (kx – ωt + φ) is the phase, and φ is the phase constant or phase angle.','[{"s": "where a is the amplitude of the wave, k is the angular wave number, ω is the angular frequency, (kx – ωt + φ) is the phase, and φ is the phase constant or phase angle.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34989,80,422,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Wavelength λ of a progressive wave is the distance between two consecutive points of the same phase at a given time. In a stationary wave, it is twice the distance between two consecutive nodes or antinodes.','[{"s": "6. Wavelength λ of a progressive wave is the distance between two consecutive points of the same phase at a given time. In a stationary wave, it is twice the distance between two consecutive nodes or antinodes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34990,80,423,'paragraph',NULL,'7. Period T of oscillation of a wave is defined as the time any element of the medium takes to move through one complete oscillation. It is related to the angular frequencyω through the relation','[{"s": "7. Period T of oscillation of a wave is defined as the time any element of the medium takes to move through one complete oscillation. It is related to the angular frequencyω through the relation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34991,80,424,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image103.png'),
(34992,80,425,'paragraph',NULL,'8. Frequency v of a wave is defined as 1/T and is related to angular frequency by','[{"s": "8. Frequency v of a wave is defined as 1/T and is related to angular frequency by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34993,80,426,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image100.png'),
(34994,80,427,'paragraph',NULL,'9. Speed of a progressive wave is given by Waves-image80.png','[{"s": "9. Speed of a progressive wave is given by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image80.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34995,80,428,'paragraph',NULL,'10. The speed of a transverse wave on a stretched string is set by the properties of the string. The speed on a string with tension T and linear mass density µ is','[{"s": "10. The speed of a transverse wave on a stretched string is set by the properties of the string. The speed on a string with tension T and linear mass density µ is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(34996,80,429,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image4.png'),
(34998,80,431,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image68.png'),
(34999,80,432,'paragraph',NULL,'The speed of longitudinal waves in a metallic bar is','[{"s": "The speed of longitudinal waves in a metallic bar is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35000,80,433,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image2.png'),
(35001,80,434,'paragraph',NULL,'For gases, since B = γP, the speed of sound is','[{"s": "For gases, since B = γP, the speed of sound is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35002,80,435,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image29.png'),
(35003,80,436,'paragraph',NULL,'12. When two or more waves traverse simultaneously in the same medium, the displacement of any element of the medium is the algebraic sum of the displacements due to each wave. This is known as the principle of superposition of waves','[{"s": "12. When two or more waves traverse simultaneously in the same medium, the displacement of any element of the medium is the algebraic sum of the displacements due to each wave. This is known as the principle of superposition of waves", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35004,80,437,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image90.png'),
(1301,13,66,'paragraph',NULL,'The pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted because the biomass of fishes far exceeds that of phytoplankton. Isn’t that a paradox? How would you explain this?','[{"s": "The pyramid of biomass in sea is generally inverted because the biomass of fishes far exceeds that of phytoplankton.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Isn’t that a paradox? How would you explain this?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["360816"]',NULL),
(35005,80,438,'paragraph',NULL,'13. Two sinusoidal waves on the same string exhibit interference, adding or cancelling according to the principle of superposition. If the two are travelling in the same direction and have the same amplitude a and frequency but differ in phase by a phase constant φ, the result is a single wave with the same frequency ω :','[{"s": "13. Two sinusoidal waves on the same string exhibit interference, adding or cancelling according to the principle of superposition. If the two are travelling in the same direction and have the same amplitude a and frequency but differ in phase by a phase constant φ, the result is a single wave with the same frequency ω :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35006,80,439,'paragraph',NULL,'y (x, t) = Waves-image84.png','[{"s": "y (x, t) =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image84.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35007,80,440,'paragraph',NULL,'If φ = 0 or an integral multiple of 2π, the waves are exactly in phase and the interference is constructive; if φ = π, they are exactly out of phase and the interference is destructive.','[{"s": "If φ = 0 or an integral multiple of 2π, the waves are exactly in phase and the interference is constructive; if φ = π, they are exactly out of phase and the interference is destructive.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35008,80,441,'paragraph',NULL,'14. A travelling wave, at a rigid boundary or a closed end, is reflected with a phase reversal but the reflection at an open boundary takes place without any phase change.','[{"s": "14. A travelling wave, at a rigid boundary or a closed end, is reflected with a phase reversal but the reflection at an open boundary takes place without any phase change.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35009,80,442,'paragraph',NULL,'For an incident wave','[{"s": "For an incident wave", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35010,80,443,'paragraph',NULL,'yi (x, t) = a sin (kx – ωt )','[{"s": "yi (x, t) = a sin (kx – ωt )", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35011,80,444,'paragraph',NULL,'the reflected wave at a rigid boundary is','[{"s": "the reflected wave at a rigid boundary is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35012,80,445,'paragraph',NULL,'yr (x, t) = – a sin (kx + ωt )','[{"s": "yr (x, t) = – a sin (kx + ωt )", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35013,80,446,'paragraph',NULL,'For reflection at an open boundary','[{"s": "For reflection at an open boundary", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35014,80,447,'paragraph',NULL,'yr (x,t ) = a sin (kx + ωt)','[{"s": "yr (x,t ) = a sin (kx + ωt)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35015,80,448,'paragraph',NULL,'15. The interference of two identical waves moving in opposite directions produces standing waves. For a string with fixed ends, the standing wave is given by','[{"s": "15. The interference of two identical waves moving in opposite directions produces standing waves. For a string with fixed ends, the standing wave is given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35016,80,449,'paragraph',NULL,'y (x, t) = [2a sin kx ] cos ωt','[{"s": "y (x, t) = [2a sin kx ] cos ωt", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35017,80,450,'paragraph',NULL,'Standing waves are characterised by fixed locations of zero displacement called nodes and fixed locations of maximum displacements called antinodes. The separation between two consecutive nodes or antinodes is λ/2.','[{"s": "Standing waves are characterised by fixed locations of zero displacement called nodes and fixed locations of maximum displacements called antinodes. The separation between two consecutive nodes or antinodes is λ/2.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35018,80,451,'paragraph',NULL,'A stretched string of length L fixed at both the ends vibrates with frequencies given by','[{"s": "A stretched string of length L fixed at both the ends vibrates with frequencies given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35019,80,452,'paragraph',NULL,'v Waves-image30.png n = 1, 2, 3, ...','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image30.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "n = 1, 2, 3, ...", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35020,80,453,'paragraph',NULL,'The set of frequencies given by the above relation are called the normal modes of oscillation of the system. The oscillation mode with lowest frequency is called the fundamental mode or the first harmonic. The second harmonic is the oscillation mode with n = 2 and so on.','[{"s": "The set of frequencies given by the above relation are called the normal modes of oscillation of the system. The oscillation mode with lowest frequency is called the fundamental mode or the first harmonic. The second harmonic is the oscillation mode with n = 2 and so on.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35021,80,454,'paragraph',NULL,'A pipe of length L with one end closed and other end open (such as air columns) vibrates with frequencies given by','[{"s": "A pipe of length L with one end closed and other end open (such as air columns) vibrates with frequencies given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35022,80,455,'paragraph',NULL,'v Waves-image102.png , n = 0, 1, 2, 3, ...','[{"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image102.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", n = 0, 1, 2, 3, ...", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35023,80,456,'paragraph',NULL,'The set of frequencies represented by the above relation are the normal modes of oscillation of such a system. The lowest frequency given by v/4L is the fundamental mode or the first harmonic.','[{"s": "The set of frequencies represented by the above relation are the normal modes of oscillation of such a system. The lowest frequency given by v/4L is the fundamental mode or the first harmonic.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35024,80,457,'paragraph',NULL,'16. A string of length L fixed at both ends or an air column closed at one end and open at the other end or open at both the ends, vibrates with certain frequencies called their normal modes. Each of these frequencies is a resonant frequency of the system.','[{"s": "16. A string of length L fixed at both ends or an air column closed at one end and open at the other end or open at both the ends, vibrates with certain frequencies called their normal modes. Each of these frequencies is a resonant frequency of the system.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35026,80,459,'paragraph',NULL,'νbeat = ν1 ~ ν2','[{"s": "νbeat = ν1 ~ ν2", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(188,2,88,'paragraph',NULL,'Examples: Economically important insects – Apis (Honey bee), Bombyx (Silkworm), Laccifer (Lac insect)','[{"s": "Examples: Economically important insects – Apis (Honey bee), Bombyx (Silkworm), Laccifer (Lac insect)", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(35028,80,461,'paragraph',NULL,'v = vo Waves-image107.png','[{"s": "v = vo", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image107.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35029,80,462,'paragraph',NULL,'here v is the speed of sound through the medium, vo is the velocity of observer relative to the medium, and vs is the source velocity relative to the medium. In using this formula, velocities in the direction OS should be treated as positive and those opposite to it should be taken to be negative.','[{"s": "here v is the speed of sound through the medium, vo is the velocity of observer relative to the medium, and vs is the source velocity relative to the medium. In using this formula, velocities in the direction OS should be treated as positive and those opposite to it should be taken to be negative.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35030,80,463,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Waves-image55.png'),
(35031,80,464,'heading',4,'Points to Ponder',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35032,80,465,'paragraph',NULL,'1. A wave is not a motion of matter as a whole in a medium. The wind is different from the sound wave in the air. The former involves motion of air from one place to the other. The latter involves compressions and rarefactions of layers of air.','[{"s": "1. A wave is not a motion of matter as a whole in a medium. The wind is different from the sound wave in the air. The former involves motion of air from one place to the other. The latter involves compressions and rarefactions of layers of air.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35033,80,466,'paragraph',NULL,'2. In a wave, energy and not the matter is transferred from one point to the other.','[{"s": "2. In a wave, energy and not the matter is transferred from one point to the other.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35034,80,467,'paragraph',NULL,'3. In a mechanical wave, energy transfer takes place because of the coupling through elastic forces between neighbouring oscillating parts of the medium.','[{"s": "3. In a mechanical wave, energy transfer takes place because of the coupling through elastic forces between neighbouring oscillating parts of the medium.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35035,80,468,'paragraph',NULL,'4. Transverse waves can propagate only in medium with the shear modulus of elasticity. Longitudinal waves need bulk modulus of elasticity and are, therefore, possible in all media, solids, liquids and gases.','[{"s": "4. Transverse waves can propagate only in medium with the shear modulus of elasticity. Longitudinal waves need bulk modulus of elasticity and are, therefore, possible in all media, solids, liquids and gases.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35036,80,469,'paragraph',NULL,'5. In a harmonic progressive wave of a given frequency, all particles have the same amplitude but different phases at a given instant of time. In a stationary wave, all particles between two nodes have the same phase at a given instant but have different amplitudes.','[{"s": "5. In a harmonic progressive wave of a given frequency, all particles have the same amplitude but different phases at a given instant of time. In a stationary wave, all particles between two nodes have the same phase at a given instant but have different amplitudes.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35037,80,470,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Relative to an observer at rest in a medium the speed of a mechanical wave in that medium (v) depends only on elastic and other properties (such as mass density) of the medium. It does not depend on the velocity of the source.','[{"s": "6. Relative to an observer at rest in a medium the speed of a mechanical wave in that medium (v) depends only on elastic and other properties (such as mass density) of the medium. It does not depend on the velocity of the source.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35038,80,471,'paragraph',NULL,'7. For an observer moving with velocity v o relative to the medium, the speed of a wave is obviously different from v and is given by v±v o .','[{"s": "7. For an observer moving with velocity", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "relative to the medium, the speed of a wave is obviously different from v and is given by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v±v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35039,80,472,'paragraph',NULL,'Exercises','[{"s": "Exercises", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35040,80,473,'paragraph',NULL,'15.1 A string of mass 2.50 kg is under a tension of 200 N. The length of the stretched string is 20.0 m. If the transverse jerk is struck at one end of the string, how long does the disturbance take to reach the other end?','[{"s": "15.1 A string of mass 2.50 kg is under a tension of 200 N. The length of the stretched string is 20.0 m. If the transverse jerk is struck at one end of the string, how long does the disturbance take to reach the other end?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35041,80,474,'paragraph',NULL,'15.2 A stone dropped from the top of a tower of height 300 m splashes into the water of a pond near the base of the tower. When is the splash heard at the top given that the speed of sound in air is 340 m/sec?','[{"s": "15.2 A stone dropped from the top of a tower of height 300 m splashes into the water of a pond near the base of the tower. When is the splash heard at the top given that the speed of sound in air is 340 m/sec?", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
