(35515,81,278,'paragraph',NULL,'while the external force F does work Work_Energy_and_Power-image77.png . If the block is moved from an initial displacement x i to a final displacement x f , the work done by the spring force W s is','[{"s": "while the external force F does work", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Work_Energy_and_Power-image77.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". If the block is moved from an initial displacement x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to a final displacement x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the work done by the spring force W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35516,81,279,'paragraph',NULL,'.','[{"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35517,81,280,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image78.png'),
(35518,81,281,'paragraph',NULL,'(6.17)','[{"s": "(6.17)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35519,81,282,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus the work done by the spring force depends only on the end points. Specifically, if the block is pulled from x i and allowed to return to x i ; Work_Energy_and_Power-image79.png','[{"s": "Thus the work done by the spring force depends only on the end points. Specifically, if the block is pulled from x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and allowed to return to x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ";", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Work_Energy_and_Power-image79.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35520,81,283,'paragraph',NULL,'= 0 (6.18)','[{"s": "= 0 (6.18)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35521,81,284,'paragraph',NULL,'The work done by the spring force in a cyclic process is zero. We have explicitly demonstrated that the spring force (i) is position dependent only as first stated by Hooke, (F s = − kx); (ii) does work which only depends on the initial and final positions, e.g. Eq. (6.17). Thus, the spring force is a conservative force .','[{"s": "The work done by the spring force in a cyclic process is zero. We have explicitly demonstrated that the spring force (i) is position dependent only as first stated by Hooke, (F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= − kx); (ii) does work which only depends on the initial and final positions, e.g. Eq. (6.17). Thus, the spring force is a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "conservative force", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35523,81,286,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image69.png'),
(35524,81,287,'paragraph',NULL,'(6.19)','[{"s": "(6.19)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35525,81,288,'paragraph',NULL,'You may easily verify that − dV/dx = − k x, the spring force. If the block of mass m in Fig. 6.7 is extended to x m and released from rest, then its total mechanical energy at any arbitrary point x, where x lies between – x m and + x m, will be given by','[{"s": "You may easily verify that − dV/dx = − k x, the spring force. If the block of mass m in Fig. 6.7 is extended to x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and released from rest, then its total mechanical energy at any arbitrary point x, where x lies between – x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and + x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will be given by", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35526,81,289,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image70.png'),
(35527,81,290,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image71.png'),
(35528,81,291,'paragraph',NULL,'where v m is the maximum speed.','[{"s": "where v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the maximum speed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35529,81,292,'paragraph',NULL,'or Work_Energy_and_Power-image58.png','[{"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Work_Energy_and_Power-image58.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35530,81,293,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that k/m has the dimensions of [T -2 ] and our equation is dimensionally correct. The kinetic energy gets converted to potential energy and vice versa, however, the total mechanical energy remains constant. This is graphically depicted in Fig. 6.8.','[{"s": "Note that k/m has the dimensions of [T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] and our equation is dimensionally correct. The kinetic energy gets converted to potential energy and vice versa, however, the total mechanical energy remains constant. This is graphically depicted in Fig. 6.8.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35531,81,294,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image60.png'),
(35532,81,295,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.8 Parabolic plots of the potential energy V and kinetic energy K of a block attached to a spring obeying Hooke’s law. The two plots are complementary, one decreasing as the other increases. The total mechanical energy E = K + V remains constant.','[{"s": "Fig. 6.8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Parabolic plots of the potential energy V and kinetic energy K of a block attached to a spring obeying Hooke’s law. The two plots are complementary, one decreasing as the other increases. The total mechanical energy E = K + V remains constant.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35534,81,297,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer At maximum compression the kinetic energy of the car is converted entirely into the potential energy of the spring.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "At maximum compression the kinetic energy of the car is converted entirely into the potential energy of the spring.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35535,81,298,'paragraph',NULL,'The kinetic energy of the moving car is','[{"s": "The kinetic energy of the moving car is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35536,81,299,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image61.png'),
(35537,81,300,'image',NULL,'Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 4.24.19 PM',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image62.png'),
(35538,81,301,'paragraph',NULL,'K = 1.25 × 10 4 J','[{"s": "K = 1.25 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35539,81,302,'paragraph',NULL,'where we have converted 18 km h –1 to 5 m s –1 [It is useful to remember that 36 km h –1 = 10 m s –1 ] . At maximum compression x m , the potential energy V of the spring is equal to the kinetic energy K of the moving car from the principle of conservation of mechanical energy.','[{"s": "where we have converted 18 km h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to 5 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "[It is useful to remember that 36 km h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 10 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". At maximum compression x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the potential energy V of the spring is equal to the kinetic energy K of the moving car from the principle of conservation of mechanical energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35540,81,303,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image64.png'),
(35541,81,304,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1.25 × 10 4 J','[{"s": "= 1.25 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35542,81,305,'paragraph',NULL,'We obtain','[{"s": "We obtain", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35543,81,306,'paragraph',NULL,'x m = 2.00 m','[{"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2.00 m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35544,81,307,'paragraph',NULL,'We note that we have idealised the situation. The spring is considered to be massless. The surface has been considered to possess negligible friction.','[{"s": "We note that we have idealised the situation. The spring is considered to be massless. The surface has been considered to possess negligible friction.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35545,81,308,'paragraph',NULL,'We conclude this section by making a few remarks on conservative forces.','[{"s": "We conclude this section by making a few remarks on conservative forces.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35546,81,309,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Information on time is absent from the above discussions. In the example considered above, we can calculate the compression, but not the time over which the compression occurs. A solution of Newton’s Second Law for this system is required for temporal information.','[{"s": "(i) Information on time is absent from the above discussions. In the example considered above, we can calculate the compression, but not the time over which the compression occurs. A solution of Newton’s Second Law for this system is required for temporal information.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35547,81,310,'paragraph',NULL,'(ii) Not all forces are conservative. Friction, for example, is a non-conservative force. The principle of conservation of energy will have to be modified in this case. This is illustrated in Example 6.9.','[{"s": "(ii) Not all forces are conservative. Friction, for example, is a non-conservative force. The principle of conservation of energy will have to be modified in this case. This is illustrated in Example 6.9.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35548,81,311,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) The zero of the potential energy is arbitrary. It is set according to convenience. For the spring force we took V(x) = 0, at x = 0, i.e. the unstretched spring had zero potential energy. For the constant gravitational force mg, we took V = 0 on the earth’s surface. In a later chapter we shall see that for the force due to the universal law of gravitation, the zero is best defined at an infinite distance from the gravitational source. However, once the zero of the potential energy is fixed in a given discussion, it must be consistently adhered to throughout the discussion. You cannot change horses in midstream !','[{"s": "(iii) The zero of the potential energy is arbitrary. It is set according to convenience. For the spring force we took V(x) = 0, at x = 0, i.e. the unstretched spring had zero potential energy. For the constant gravitational force mg, we took V = 0 on the earth’s surface. In a later chapter we shall see that for the force due to the universal law of gravitation, the zero is best defined at an infinite distance from the gravitational source. However, once the zero of the potential energy is fixed in a given discussion, it must be consistently adhered to throughout the discussion. You cannot change horses in midstream !", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35549,81,312,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.9 Consider Example 6.8 taking the coefficient of friction, µ, to be 0.5 and calculate the maximum compression of the spring.','[{"s": "Example 6.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Consider Example 6.8 taking the coefficient of friction, µ, to be 0.5 and calculate the maximum compression of the spring.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35550,81,313,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer In presence of friction, both the spring force and the frictional force act so as to oppose the compression of the spring as shown in Fig. 6.9.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In presence of friction, both the spring force and the frictional force act so as to oppose the compression of the spring as shown in Fig. 6.9.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35551,81,314,'paragraph',NULL,'We invoke the work-energy theorem, rather than the conservation of mechanical energy.','[{"s": "We invoke the work-energy theorem, rather than the conservation of mechanical energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
