(35563,81,326,'paragraph',NULL,'where we take the positive square root since x m is positive. Putting in numerical values we obtain','[{"s": "where we take the positive square root since x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is positive. Putting in numerical values we obtain", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35564,81,327,'paragraph',NULL,'x m = 1.35 m','[{"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1.35 m", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35566,81,329,'paragraph',NULL,'If the two forces on the body consist of a conservative force F c and a non-conservative force F nc , the conservation of mechanical energy formula will have to be modified. By the WE theorem','[{"s": "If the two forces on the body consist of a conservative force F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and a non-conservative force F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nc", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the conservation of mechanical energy formula will have to be modified. By the WE theorem", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35567,81,330,'paragraph',NULL,'(F c + F nc ) ∆x = ∆K','[{"s": "(F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nc", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") ∆x = ∆K", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35568,81,331,'paragraph',NULL,'But Fc ∆x = − ∆V','[{"s": "But Fc ∆x = − ∆V", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35569,81,332,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence, ∆(K + V) = F nc ∆x','[{"s": "Hence, ∆(K + V) = F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆x", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35570,81,333,'paragraph',NULL,'∆E = F nc ∆x','[{"s": "∆E = F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆x", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35571,81,334,'paragraph',NULL,'where E is the total mechanical energy. Over the path this assumes the form','[{"s": "where E is the total mechanical energy. Over the path this assumes the form", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35572,81,335,'paragraph',NULL,'E f − E i = W nc','[{"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "− E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nc", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35573,81,336,'paragraph',NULL,'where W nc is the total work done by the non-conservative forces over the path. Note that unlike the conservative force, W nc depends on the particular path i to f.','[{"s": "where W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the total work done by the non-conservative forces over the path. Note that unlike the conservative force, W", "t": "text"}, {"s": "nc", "t": "text"}, {"s": "depends on the particular path i to f.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35574,81,337,'heading',2,'6.10 VARIOUS FORMS OF ENERGY : THE LAW OF CONSERVATION OF ENERGY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35575,81,338,'paragraph',NULL,'In the previous section we have discussed mechanical energy. We have seen that it can be classified into two distinct categories : one based on motion, namely kinetic energy; the other on configuration (position), namely potential energy. Energy comes in many a forms which transform into one another in ways which may not often be clear to us.','[{"s": "In the previous section we have discussed mechanical energy. We have seen that it can be classified into two distinct categories : one based on motion, namely kinetic energy; the other on configuration (position), namely potential energy. Energy comes in many a forms which transform into one another in ways which may not often be clear to us.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35576,81,339,'heading',3,'6.10.1 HEAT',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35577,81,340,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen that the frictional force is not a conservative force. However, work is associated with the force of friction, Example 6.5. A block of mass m sliding on a rough horizontal surface with speed v 0 comes to a halt over a distance x 0 . The work done by the force of kinetic friction f over x 0 is –f x 0 . By the work-energy theorem Work_Energy_and_Power-image111.png If we confine our scope to mechanics, we would say that the kinetic energy of the block is ‘lost’ due to the frictional force. On examination of the block and the table we would detect a slight increase in their temperatures. The work done by friction is not ‘lost’, but is transferred as heat energy. This raises the internal energy of the block and the table. In winter, in order to feel warm, we generate heat by vigorously rubbing our palms together. We shall see later that the internal energy is associated with the ceaseless, often random, motion of molecules. A quantitative idea of the transfer of heat energy is obtained by noting that 1 kg of water releases about 42000 J of energy when it cools by10 °C.','[{"s": "We have seen that the frictional force is not a conservative force. However, work is associated with the force of friction, Example 6.5. A block of mass m sliding on a rough horizontal surface with speed v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "comes to a halt over a distance x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The work done by the force of kinetic friction f over x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is –f x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". By the work-energy theorem", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Work_Energy_and_Power-image111.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "If we confine our scope to mechanics, we would say that the kinetic energy of the block is ‘lost’ due to the frictional force. On examination of the block and the table we would detect a slight increase in their temperatures. The work done by friction is not ‘lost’, but is transferred as heat energy. This raises the internal energy of the block and the table. In winter, in order to feel warm, we generate heat by vigorously rubbing our palms together. We shall see later that the internal energy is associated with the ceaseless, often random, motion of molecules. A quantitative idea of the transfer of heat energy is obtained by noting that 1 kg of water releases about 42000 J of energy when it cools by10 °C.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35578,81,341,'heading',3,'6.10.2 CHEMICAL ENERGY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35579,81,342,'paragraph',NULL,'One of the greatest technical achievements of humankind occurred when we discovered how to ignite and control fire. We learnt to rub two flint stones together (mechanical energy), got them to heat up and to ignite a heap of dry leaves (chemical energy), which then provided sustained warmth. A matchstick ignites into a bright flame when struck against a specially prepared chemical surface. The lighted matchstick, when applied to a firecracker, results in a spectacular display of sound and light.','[{"s": "One of the greatest technical achievements of humankind occurred when we discovered how to ignite and control fire. We learnt to rub two flint stones together (mechanical energy), got them to heat up and to ignite a heap of dry leaves (chemical energy), which then provided sustained warmth. A matchstick ignites into a bright flame when struck against a specially prepared chemical surface. The lighted matchstick, when applied to a firecracker, results in a spectacular display of sound and light.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(917,9,40,'paragraph',NULL,'Alveoli are the primary sites of exchange of gases. Exchange of gases also occur between blood and tissues. O 2 and CO 2 are exchanged in these sites by simple diffusion mainly based on pressure/concentration gradient. Solubility of the gases as well as the thickness of the membranes involved in diffusion are also some important factors that can affect the rate of diffusion.','[{"s": "Alveoli are the primary sites of exchange of gases.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Exchange of gases also occur between blood and tissues.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and CO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "are exchanged in these sites by simple diffusion mainly based on pressure/concentration gradient.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Solubility of the gases as well as the thickness of the membranes involved in diffusion are also some important factors that can affect the rate of diffusion.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(35580,81,343,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemical energy arises from the fact that the molecules participating in the chemical reaction have different binding energies. A stable chemical compound has less energy than the separated parts. A chemical reaction is basically a rearrangement of atoms. If the total energy of the reactants is more than the products of the reaction, heat is released and the reaction is said to be an exothermic reaction. If the reverse is true, heat is absorbed and the reaction is endothermic . Coal consists of carbon and a kilogram of it when burnt releases about 3 × 10 7 J of energy.','[{"s": "Chemical energy arises from the fact that the molecules participating in the chemical reaction have different binding energies. A stable chemical compound has less energy than the separated parts. A chemical reaction is basically a rearrangement of atoms. If the total energy of the reactants is more than the products of the reaction, heat is released and the reaction is said to be an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "exothermic", "t": "text"}, {"s": "reaction. If the reverse is true, heat is absorbed and the reaction is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "endothermic", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Coal consists of carbon and a kilogram of it when burnt releases about 3 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J of energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35581,81,344,'paragraph',NULL,'Chemical energy is associated with the forces that give rise to the stability of substances. These forces bind atoms into molecules, molecules into polymeric chains, etc. The chemical energy arising from the combustion of coal, cooking gas, wood and petroleum is indispensable to our daily existence.','[{"s": "Chemical energy is associated with the forces that give rise to the stability of substances. These forces bind atoms into molecules, molecules into polymeric chains, etc. The chemical energy arising from the combustion of coal, cooking gas, wood and petroleum is indispensable to our daily existence.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35582,81,345,'heading',3,'6.10.3 ELECTRICAL ENERGY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35583,81,346,'paragraph',NULL,'The flow of electrical current causes bulbs to glow, fans to rotate and bells to ring. There are laws governing the attraction and repulsion of charges and currents, which we shall learn later. Energy is associated with an electric current. An urban Indian household consumes about 200 J of energy per second on an average.','[{"s": "The flow of electrical current causes bulbs to glow, fans to rotate and bells to ring. There are laws governing the attraction and repulsion of charges and currents, which we shall learn later. Energy is associated with an electric current. An urban Indian household consumes about 200 J of energy per second on an average.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35584,81,347,'heading',3,'6.10.4 THE EQUIVALENCE OF MASS AND ENERGY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35585,81,348,'paragraph',NULL,'Till the end of the nineteenth century, physicists believed that in every physical and chemical process, the mass of an isolated system is conserved. Matter might change its phase, e.g. glacial ice could melt into a gushing stream, but matter is neither created nor destroyed; Albert Einstein (1879-1955) however, showed that mass and energy are equivalent and are related by the relation','[{"s": "Till the end of the nineteenth century, physicists believed that in every physical and chemical process, the mass of an isolated system is conserved. Matter might change its phase, e.g. glacial ice could melt into a gushing stream, but matter is neither created nor destroyed; Albert Einstein (1879-1955) however, showed that mass and energy are equivalent and are related by the relation", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35586,81,349,'paragraph',NULL,'E = m c 2 (6.20)','[{"s": "E = m c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(6.20)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35587,81,350,'paragraph',NULL,'where c, the speed of light in vacuum is approximately 3 ×10 8 m s –1 . Thus, a staggering amount of energy is associated with a mere kilogram of matter','[{"s": "where c, the speed of light in vacuum is approximately 3 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus, a staggering amount of energy is associated with a mere kilogram of matter", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(35588,81,351,'paragraph',NULL,'E = 1× (3 ×10 8 ) 2 J = 9 ×10 16 J.','[{"s": "E = 1× (3 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J = 9 ×10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "16", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35589,81,352,'paragraph',NULL,'This is equivalent to the annual electrical output of a large (3000 MW) power generating station.','[{"s": "This is equivalent to the annual electrical output of a large (3000 MW) power generating station.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35590,81,353,'heading',3,'6.10.5 NUCLEAR ENERGY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35591,81,354,'paragraph',NULL,'The most destructive weapons made by man, the fission and fusion bombs are manifestations of the above equivalence of mass and energy [Eq. (6.20)]. On the other hand the explanation of the life-nourishing energy output of the sun is also based on the above equation. In this case effectively four light hydrogen nuclei fuse to form a helium nucleus whose mass is less than the sum of the masses of the reactants. This mass difference, called the mass defect ∆m is the source of energy (∆m)c 2 . In fission, a heavy nucleus like uranium Work_Energy_and_Power-image114.png , is split by a neutron into lighter nuclei. Once again the final mass is less than the initial mass and the mass difference translates into energy, which can be tapped to provide electrical energy as in nuclear power plants (controlled nuclear fission) or can be employed in making nuclear weapons (uncontrolled nuclear fission). Strictly, the energy ∆E released in a chemical reaction can also be related to the mass defect ∆m = ∆E/c 2 . However, for a chemical reaction, this mass defect is much smaller than for a nuclear reaction. Table 6.3 lists the total energies for a variety of events and phenomena.','[{"s": "The most destructive weapons made by man, the fission and fusion bombs are manifestations of the above equivalence of mass and energy [Eq. (6.20)]. On the other hand the explanation of the life-nourishing energy output of the sun is also based on the above equation. In this case effectively four light hydrogen nuclei fuse to form a helium nucleus whose mass is less than the sum of the masses of the reactants. This mass difference, called the mass defect ∆m is the source of energy (∆m)c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In fission, a heavy nucleus like uranium", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Work_Energy_and_Power-image114.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screen Shot 2019-06-09 at 4.36.45 PM"}, {"s": ", is split by a neutron into lighter nuclei. Once again the final mass is less than the initial mass and the mass difference translates into energy, which can be tapped to provide electrical energy as in nuclear power plants (controlled nuclear fission) or can be employed in making nuclear weapons (uncontrolled nuclear fission). Strictly, the energy ∆E released in a chemical reaction can also be related to the mass defect ∆m = ∆E/c", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". However, for a chemical reaction, this mass defect is much smaller than for a nuclear reaction. Table 6.3 lists the total energies for a variety of events and phenomena.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35592,81,355,'heading',5,'Table 6.3 Approximate energy associated with various phenomena',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35593,81,356,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image117.png'),
(35594,81,357,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.10 Examine Tables 6.1-6.3 and express (a) The energy required to break one bond in DNA in eV; (b) The kinetic energy of an air molecule (10 —21 J) in eV; (c) The daily intake of a human adult in kilocalories.','[{"s": "Example 6.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Examine Tables 6.1-6.3 and express (a) The energy required to break one bond in DNA in eV; (b) The kinetic energy of an air molecule (10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "—21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J) in eV; (c) The daily intake of a human adult in kilocalories.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35595,81,358,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer (a) Energy required to break one bond of DNA is','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(a) Energy required to break one bond of DNA is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35596,81,359,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image120.png'),
(35597,81,360,'paragraph',NULL,'Note 0.1 eV = 100 meV (100 millielectron volt).','[{"s": "Note 0.1 eV = 100 meV (100 millielectron volt).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35598,81,361,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The kinetic energy of an air molecule is','[{"s": "(b) The kinetic energy of an air molecule is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35599,81,362,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image122.png'),
(35600,81,363,'paragraph',NULL,'This is the same as 6.2 meV.','[{"s": "This is the same as 6.2 meV.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35601,81,364,'paragraph',NULL,'(c) The average human consumption in a day is','[{"s": "(c) The average human consumption in a day is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35602,81,365,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image125.png'),
(35603,81,366,'paragraph',NULL,'We point out a common misconception created by newspapers and magazines. They mention food values in calories and urge us to restrict diet intake to below 2400 calories. What they should be saying is kilocalories (kcal) and not calories. A person consuming 2400 calories a day will soon starve to death! 1 food calorie is 1 kcal.','[{"s": "We point out a common misconception created by newspapers and magazines. They mention food values in calories and urge us to restrict diet intake to below 2400 calories. What they should be saying is kilocalories (kcal) and not calories. A person consuming 2400 calories a day will soon starve to death! 1 food calorie is 1 kcal.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35604,81,367,'heading',3,'6.10.6 THE PRINCIPLE OF CONSERVATION OF ENERGY',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35605,81,368,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen that the total mechanical energy of the system is conserved if the forces doing work on it are conservative. If some of the forces involved are non-conservative, part of the mechanical energy may get transformed into other forms such as heat, light and sound. However, the total energy of an isolated system does not change, as long as one accounts for all forms of energy. Energy may be transformed from one form to another but the total energy of an isolated system remains constant. Energy can neither be created, nor destroyed.','[{"s": "We have seen that the total mechanical energy of the system is conserved if the forces doing work on it are conservative. If some of the forces involved are non-conservative, part of the mechanical energy may get transformed into other forms such as heat, light and sound. However, the total energy of an isolated system does not change, as long as one accounts for all forms of energy. Energy may be transformed from one form to another but the total energy of an isolated system remains constant. Energy can neither be created, nor destroyed.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35606,81,369,'paragraph',NULL,'Since the universe as a whole may be viewed as an isolated system, the total energy of the universe is constant. If one part of the universe loses energy, another part must gain an equal amount of energy.','[{"s": "Since the universe as a whole may be viewed as an isolated system, the total energy of the universe is constant. If one part of the universe loses energy, another part must gain an equal amount of energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35607,81,370,'paragraph',NULL,'The principle of conservation of energy cannot be proved. However, no violation of this principle has been observed. The concept of conservation and transformation of energy into various forms links together various branches of physics, chemistry and life sciences. It provides a unifying, enduring element in our scientific pursuits. From engineering point of view all electronic, communication and mechanical devices rely on some forms of energy transformation.','[{"s": "The principle of conservation of energy cannot be proved. However, no violation of this principle has been observed. The concept of conservation and transformation of energy into various forms links together various branches of physics, chemistry and life sciences. It provides a unifying, enduring element in our scientific pursuits. From engineering point of view all electronic, communication and mechanical devices rely on some forms of energy transformation.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35608,81,371,'heading',2,'6.11 POWER',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35609,81,372,'paragraph',NULL,'Often it is interesting to know not only the work done on an object, but also the rate at which this work is done. We say a person is physically fit if he not only climbs four floors of a building but climbs them fast. Power is defined as the time rate at which work is done or energy is transferred.','[{"s": "Often it is interesting to know not only the work done on an object, but also the rate at which this work is done. We say a person is physically fit if he not only climbs four floors of a building but climbs them fast.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Power", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is defined as the time rate at which work is done or energy is transferred.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35611,81,374,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image18.png'),
(35613,81,376,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image17.png'),
(35614,81,377,'paragraph',NULL,'(6.21)','[{"s": "(6.21)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35610,81,373,'paragraph',NULL,'The average power of a force is defined as the ratio of the work, W, to the total time t taken','[{"s": "The average power of a force is defined as the ratio of the work, W, to the total time t taken", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000035040", "2000108471", "2000108533"]',NULL),
(35616,81,379,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image21.png'),
(35617,81,380,'paragraph',NULL,'= F.v (6.22)','[{"s": "= F.v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(6.22)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35619,81,382,'paragraph',NULL,'Power, like work and energy, is a scalar quantity. Its dimensions are [ML 2 T –3 ]. In the SI, its unit is called a watt (W). The watt is 1 J s –1 . The unit of power is named after James Watt, one of the innovators of the steam engine in the eighteenth century.','[{"s": "Power, like work and energy, is a scalar quantity. Its dimensions are [ML", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]. In the SI, its unit is called a watt (W). The watt is 1 J s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The unit of power is named after James Watt, one of the innovators of the steam engine in the eighteenth century.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35620,81,383,'paragraph',NULL,'There is another unit of power, namely the horse-power (hp)','[{"s": "There is another unit of power, namely the horse-power (hp)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35621,81,384,'paragraph',NULL,'1 hp = 746 W','[{"s": "1 hp = 746 W", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35622,81,385,'paragraph',NULL,'This unit is still used to describe the output of automobiles, motorbikes, etc.','[{"s": "This unit is still used to describe the output of automobiles, motorbikes, etc.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35623,81,386,'paragraph',NULL,'We encounter the unit watt when we buy electrical goods such as bulbs, heaters and refrigerators. A 100 watt bulb which is on for 10 hours uses 1 kilowatt hour (kWh) of energy.','[{"s": "We encounter the unit watt when we buy electrical goods such as bulbs, heaters and refrigerators. A 100 watt bulb which is on for 10 hours uses 1 kilowatt hour (kWh) of energy.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35624,81,387,'paragraph',NULL,'100 (watt) × 10 (hour)','[{"s": "100 (watt) × 10 (hour)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35625,81,388,'paragraph',NULL,'= 1000 watt hour','[{"s": "= 1000 watt hour", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35626,81,389,'paragraph',NULL,'=1 kilowatt hour (kWh)','[{"s": "=1 kilowatt hour (kWh)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35627,81,390,'paragraph',NULL,'= 10 3 (W) × 3600 (s)','[{"s": "= 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(W) × 3600 (s)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35628,81,391,'paragraph',NULL,'= 3.6 × 10 6 J','[{"s": "= 3.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35629,81,392,'paragraph',NULL,'Our electricity bills carry the energy consumption in units of kWh. Note that kWh is a unit of energy and not of power.','[{"s": "Our electricity bills carry the energy consumption in units of kWh. Note that kWh is a unit of energy and not of power.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35630,81,393,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.11 An elevator can carry a maximum load of 1800 kg (elevator + passengers) is moving up with a constant speed of 2ms –1 . The frictional force opposing the motion is 4000 N. Determine the minimum power delivered by the motor to the elevator in watts as well as in horse power.','[{"s": "Example 6.11", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An elevator can carry a maximum load of 1800 kg (elevator + passengers) is moving up with a constant speed of 2ms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The frictional force opposing the motion is 4000 N. Determine the minimum power delivered by the motor to the elevator in watts as well as in horse power.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35631,81,394,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The downward force on the elevator is','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The downward force on the elevator is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35632,81,395,'paragraph',NULL,'F = m g + F f = (1800 × 10) + 4000 = 22000 N','[{"s": "F = m g + F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= (1800 × 10) + 4000 = 22000 N", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35633,81,396,'paragraph',NULL,'The motor must supply enough power to balance this force. Hence,','[{"s": "The motor must supply enough power to balance this force. Hence,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35634,81,397,'paragraph',NULL,'P = F. v = 22000 × 2 = 44000 W = 59 hp','[{"s": "P =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F. v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 22000 × 2 = 44000 W = 59 hp", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35635,81,398,'heading',2,'6.12 COLLISIONS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35636,81,399,'paragraph',NULL,'In physics we study motion (change in position). At the same time, we try to discover physical quantities, which do not change in a physical process. The laws of momentum and energy conservation are typical examples. In this section we shall apply these laws to a commonly encountered phenomena, namely collisions. Several games such as billiards, marbles or carrom involve collisions.We shall study the collision of two masses in an idealised form.','[{"s": "In physics we study motion (change in position). At the same time, we try to discover physical quantities, which do not change in a physical process. The laws of momentum and energy conservation are typical examples. In this section we shall apply these laws to a commonly encountered phenomena, namely collisions. Several games such as billiards, marbles or carrom involve collisions.We shall study the collision of two masses in an idealised form.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(1303,13,68,'paragraph',NULL,'However, there are certain limitations of ecological pyramids such as it does not take into account the same species belonging to two or more trophic levels. It assumes a simple food chain, something that almost never exists in nature; it does not accommodate a food web. Moreover, saprophytes are not given any place in ecological pyramids even though they play a vital role in the ecosystem.','[{"s": "However, there are certain limitations of ecological pyramids such as it does not take into account the same species belonging to two or more trophic levels. It assumes a simple food chain, something that almost never exists in nature; it does not accommodate a food web. Moreover, saprophytes are not given any place in ecological pyramids even though they play a vital role in the ecosystem.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(35638,81,401,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image19.png'),
(35639,81,402,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.10 Collision of mass m 1 , with a stationary mass m 2 .','[{"s": "Fig. 6.10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Collision of mass m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", with a stationary mass m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35640,81,403,'paragraph',NULL,'The masses m 1 and m 2 fly-off in different directions. We shall see that there are relationships, which connect the masses, the velocities and the angles.','[{"s": "The masses m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "fly-off in different directions. We shall see that there are relationships, which connect the masses, the velocities and the angles.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35641,81,404,'heading',3,'6.12.1 ELASTIC AND INELASTIC COLLISIONS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35642,81,405,'paragraph',NULL,'In all collisions the total linear momentum is conserved; the initial momentum of the system is equal to the final momentum of the system. One can argue this as follows. When two objects collide, the mutual impulsive forces acting over the collision time ∆t cause a change in their respective momenta :','[{"s": "In all collisions the total linear momentum is conserved; the initial momentum of the system is equal to the final momentum of the system. One can argue this as follows. When two objects collide, the mutual impulsive forces acting over the collision time ∆t cause a change in their respective momenta :", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35643,81,406,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ p 1 = F 12 ∆t','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35644,81,407,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ p 2 = F 21 ∆t','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆t", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35645,81,408,'paragraph',NULL,'where F 12 is the force exerted on the first particle by the second particle. F 21 is likewise the force exerted on the second particle by the first particle. Now from Newton’s third law, F 12 = − F 21 . This implies','[{"s": "where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the force exerted on the first particle by the second particle.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is likewise the force exerted on the second particle by the first particle. Now from Newton’s third law,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= −", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "21", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". This implies", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35646,81,409,'paragraph',NULL,'∆ p 1 + ∆ p 2 = 0','[{"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35647,81,410,'paragraph',NULL,'The above conclusion is true even though the forces vary in a complex fashion during the collision time ∆t. Since the third law is true at every instant, the total impulse on the first object is equal and opposite to that on the second.','[{"s": "The above conclusion is true even though the forces vary in a complex fashion during the collision time ∆t. Since the third law is true at every instant, the total impulse on the first object is equal and opposite to that on the second.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35649,81,412,'heading',3,'6.12.2 COLLISIONS IN ONE DIMENSION',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35650,81,413,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider first a completely inelastic collision in one dimension. Then, in Fig. 6.10,','[{"s": "Consider first a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "completely inelastic collision", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in one dimension. Then, in Fig. 6.10,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
