(35686,81,449,'paragraph',NULL,'The first mass comes to rest and pushes off the second mass with its initial speed on collision.','[{"s": "The first mass comes to rest and pushes off the second mass with its initial speed on collision.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35687,81,450,'paragraph',NULL,'Case II : If one mass dominates, e.g. m 2 > > m 1 v 1f ~ − v 1i v 2f ~ 0','[{"s": "Case II :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If one mass dominates, e.g. m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> > m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~ − v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "~ 0", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35688,81,451,'paragraph',NULL,'The heavier mass is undisturbed while the lighter mass reverses its velocity.','[{"s": "The heavier mass is undisturbed while the lighter mass reverses its velocity.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35689,81,452,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.12 Slowing down of neutrons : In a nuclear reactor a neutron of high speed (typically 10 7 m s –1 ) must be slowed to 10 3 m s –1 so that it can have a high probability of interacting with isotope Work_Energy_and_Power-image83.png and causing it to fission. Show that a neutron can lose most of its kinetic energy in an elastic collision with a light nuclei like deuterium or carbon which has a mass of only a few times the neutron mass. The material making up the light nuclei, usually heavy water (D 2 O) or graphite, is called a moderator.','[{"s": "Example 6.12 Slowing down of neutrons", "t": "text"}, {"s": ":", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In a nuclear reactor a neutron of high speed (typically 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "7", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") must be slowed to 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "so that it can have a high probability of interacting with isotope", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Work_Energy_and_Power-image83.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and causing it to fission. Show that a neutron can lose most of its kinetic energy in an elastic collision with a light nuclei like deuterium or carbon which has a mass of only a few times the neutron mass. The material making up the light nuclei, usually heavy water (D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O) or graphite, is called a moderator.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35690,81,453,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The initial kinetic energy of the neutron is','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The initial kinetic energy of the neutron is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35691,81,454,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image80.png'),
(35692,81,455,'paragraph',NULL,'while its final kinetic energy from Eq. (6.27)','[{"s": "while its final kinetic energy from Eq. (6.27)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35693,81,456,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image128.png'),
(35694,81,457,'paragraph',NULL,'The fractional kinetic energy lost is','[{"s": "The fractional kinetic energy lost is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35695,81,458,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image127.png'),
(35696,81,459,'paragraph',NULL,'while the fractional kinetic energy gained by the moderating nuclei K 2f /K 1i is','[{"s": "while the fractional kinetic energy gained by the moderating nuclei K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35697,81,460,'paragraph',NULL,'f 2 = 1 − f 1 (elastic collision)','[{"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1 − f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(elastic collision)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35698,81,461,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image112.png'),
(35699,81,462,'paragraph',NULL,'One can also verify this result by substituting from Eq. (6.28).','[{"s": "One can also verify this result by substituting from Eq. (6.28).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35700,81,463,'paragraph',NULL,'For deuterium m 2 = 2m 1 and we obtain f 1 = 1/9 while f 2 = 8/9. Almost 90% of the neutron’s energy is transferred to deuterium. For carbon f 1 = 71.6% and f 2 = 28.4%. In practice, however, this number is smaller since head-on collisions are rare.','[{"s": "For deuterium m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and we obtain f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 1/9 while f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 8/9. Almost 90% of the neutron’s energy is transferred to deuterium. For carbon f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 71.6% and f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 28.4%. In practice, however, this number is smaller since head-on collisions are rare.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35701,81,464,'paragraph',NULL,'If the initial velocities and final velocities of both the bodies are along the same straight line, then it is called a one-dimensional collision, or head-on collision. In the case of small spherical bodies, this is possible if the direction of travel of body 1 passes through the centre of body 2 which is at rest. In general, the collision is two-dimensional, where the initial velocities and the final velocities lie in a plane.','[{"s": "If the initial velocities and final velocities of both the bodies are along the same straight line, then it is called a one-dimensional collision, or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "head-on collision.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the case of small spherical bodies, this is possible if the direction of travel of body 1 passes through the centre of body 2 which is at rest. In general, the collision is two-dimensional, where the initial velocities and the final velocities lie in a plane.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35702,81,465,'heading',3,'6.12.3 COLLISIONS IN TWO DIMENSIONS',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35703,81,466,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.10 also depicts the collision of a moving mass m 1 with the stationary mass m 2 . Linear momentum is conserved in such a collision. Since momentum is a vector this implies three equations for the three directions {x, y, z}. Consider the plane determined by the final velocity directions of m 1 and m 2','[{"s": "Fig. 6.10 also depicts the collision of a moving mass m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with the stationary mass m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Linear momentum is conserved in such a collision. Since momentum is a vector this implies three equations for the three directions {x, y, z}. Consider the plane determined by the final velocity directions of m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35704,81,467,'paragraph',NULL,'m 1 v 1i = m 1 v 1f cos θ 1 + m 2 v 2f cos θ 2 (6.29)','[{"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(6.29)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35705,81,468,'paragraph',NULL,'0 = m 1 v 1f sin θ 1 − m 2 v 2f sin θ 2 (6.30)','[{"s": "0 = m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "− m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sin θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(6.30)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35706,81,469,'paragraph',NULL,'One knows {m 1 , m 2 , v 1i } in most situations. There are thus four unknowns {v 1f , v 2f , θ 1 and θ 2 }, and only two equations. If θ 1 = θ 2 = 0, we regain Eq. (6.24) for one dimensional collision.','[{"s": "One knows {m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "} in most situations. There are thus four unknowns {v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1f", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2f", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "}, and only two equations. If θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0, we regain Eq. (6.24) for one dimensional collision.", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35707,81,470,'paragraph',NULL,'If, further the collision is elastic,','[{"s": "If, further the collision is elastic,", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35708,81,471,'image',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'/ncert/physics/images/Work_Energy_and_Power-image109.png'),
(35709,81,472,'paragraph',NULL,'(6.31)','[{"s": "(6.31)", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35710,81,473,'paragraph',NULL,'We obtain an additional equation. That still leaves us one equation short. At least one of the four unknowns, say θ 1 , must be made known for the problem to be solvable. For example, θ 1 can be determined by moving a detector in an angular fashion from the x to the y axis. Given {m 1 , m 2 , v 1i , θ 1 } we can determine {v 1f , v 2f , θ 2 } from Eqs. (6.29)-(6.31).','[{"s": "We obtain an additional equation. That still leaves us one equation short. At least one of the four unknowns, say θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", must be made known for the problem to be solvable. For example, θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "can be determined by moving a detector in an angular fashion from the x to the y axis. Given {m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "} we can determine {v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1f", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2f", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "} from Eqs. (6.29)-(6.31).", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(35712,81,475,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer From momentum conservation, since the masses are equal','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "From momentum conservation, since the masses are equal", "t": "text"}]',NULL,NULL,NULL),
