(26074,65,136,'paragraph',NULL,'The situation is radically different for magnetic fields which are continuous and form closed loops. Examine the Gaussian surfaces represented by i or ii in Fig 5.3(a) or Fig. 5.3(b). Both cases visually demonstrate that the number of magnetic field lines leaving the surface is balanced by the number of lines entering it. The net magnetic flux is zero for both the surfaces . This is true for any closed surface.','[{"s": "The situation is radically different for magnetic fields which are continuous and form closed loops. Examine the Gaussian surfaces represented by i or ii in Fig 5.3(a) or Fig. 5.3(b). Both cases visually demonstrate that the number of magnetic field lines leaving the surface is balanced by the number of lines entering it.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The net magnetic flux is zero for both the surfaces", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is true for any closed surface.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(21832,56,90,'paragraph',NULL,'The Lyman series is in the ultraviolet, and the Paschen and Brackett series are in the infrared region.','[{"s": "The Lyman series is in the ultraviolet, and the Paschen and Brackett series are in the infrared region.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2842,25,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Spraying juvenile conifers with GAs hastens the maturity period, thus leading to early seed production . Gibberellins also promotes bolting (internode elongation just prior to flowering) in beet, cabbages and many plants with rosette habit.','[{"s": "Spraying juvenile conifers with GAs hastens the maturity period, thus leading to early seed production", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gibberellins also promotes bolting (internode elongation just prior to flowering) in beet, cabbages and many plants with rosette habit.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(30011,73,69,'paragraph',NULL,'We have derived here the mirror equation, Eq. (9.7), and the magnification formula, Eq. (9.9), for the case of real, inverted image formed by a concave mirror. With the proper use of sign convention, these are, In fact, valid for all the cases of reflection by a spherical mirror (concave or convex) whether the image formed is real or virtual. Figure 9.6 shows the ray diagrams for virtual image formed by a concave and convex mirror. 2 You should verify that Eqs. (9.7) and (9.9) are valid for these cases as well.','[{"s": "We have derived here the mirror equation, Eq. (9.7), and the magnification formula, Eq. (9.9), for the case of real, inverted image formed by a concave mirror. With the proper use of sign convention, these are, In fact, valid for all the cases of reflection by a spherical mirror (concave or convex) whether the image formed is real or virtual.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Figure 9.6 shows the ray diagrams for virtual image formed by a concave and convex mirror.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "You should verify that Eqs. (9.7) and (9.9) are valid for these cases as well.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2210,21,89,'paragraph',NULL,'Leaves are often modified to perform functions other than photosynthesis. They are converted into tendrils for climbing as in peas or into spines for defence as in cacti (Figure 5.10 a, b). The fleshy leaves of onion and garlic store food (Figure 5.10c). In some plants such as Australian acacia, the leaves are small and short-lived. The petioles in these plants expand, become green and synthesise food. Leaves of certain insectivorous plants such as pitcher plant, venus-fly trap are also modified leaves.','[{"s": "Leaves are often modified to perform functions other than photosynthesis. They are converted into tendrils for climbing as in peas or into spines for defence as in cacti (Figure 5.10 a, b). The fleshy leaves of onion and garlic store food (Figure 5.10c). In some plants such as Australian acacia, the leaves are small and short-lived. The petioles in these plants expand, become green and synthesise food.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Leaves of certain insectivorous plants such as pitcher plant, venus-fly trap are also modified leaves.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(32389,75,1055,'paragraph',NULL,'is the angular momentum of the system about the center of mass with velocities taken relative to the center of mass? Remember r’i = ri – R; rest of the notation is the standard notation used in the chapter. Note L′ and MR × V can be said to be angular momenta, respectively, about and of the center of mass of the system of particles.','[{"s": "is the angular momentum of the system about the center of mass with velocities taken relative to the center of mass?", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Remember r’i = ri – R; rest of the notation is the standard notation used in the chapter. Note L′ and MR × V can be said to be angular momenta, respectively, about and of the center of mass of the system of particles.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(1652,17,14,'paragraph',NULL,'Each lobule contains one to three highly coiled seminiferous tubules in which sperms are produced . Each seminiferous tubule is lined on its inside by two types of cells called male germ cells (spermatogonia) and Sertoli cells (Figure 3.2 ). The male germ cells undergo meiotic divisions finally leading to sperm formation, while Sertoli cells provide nutrition to the germ cells. The regions outside the seminiferous tubules called interstitial spaces, contain small blood vessels and interstitial cells or Leydig cells (Figure 3.2). Leydig cells synthesise and secrete testicular hormones called androgens. Other immunologically competent cells are also present.','[{"s": "Each lobule contains one to three highly coiled", "t": "text"}, {"s": "seminiferous", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tubules", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in which sperms are produced", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Each seminiferous tubule is lined on its inside by two types of cells called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "male germ cells", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(spermatogonia) and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Sertoli cells", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Figure 3.2 ).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The male germ cells undergo meiotic divisions finally leading to sperm formation, while Sertoli cells provide nutrition to the germ cells.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The regions outside the seminiferous tubules called interstitial spaces, contain small blood vessels and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "interstitial cells", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "or", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Leydig cells", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Figure 3.2).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Leydig cells synthesise and secrete testicular hormones called androgens.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Other immunologically competent cells are also present.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["251220", "251238", "251239"]',NULL),
(1611,16,117,'paragraph',NULL,'Drugs like barbiturates, amphetamines, benzodiazepines, and other similar drugs, that are normally used as medicines to help patients cope with mental illnesses like depression and insomnia, are often abused . Morphine is a very effective sedative and painkiller, and is very useful in patients who have undergone surgery. Several plants, fruits and seeds having hallucinogenic properties have been used for hundreds of years in folk-medicine, religious ceremonies and rituals all over the globe. When these are taken for a purpose other than medicinal use or in amounts/frequency that impairs one’s physical, physiological or psychological functions, it constitutes drug abuse.','[{"s": "Drugs like barbiturates, amphetamines, benzodiazepines, and other similar drugs, that are normally used as medicines to help patients cope with mental illnesses like depression and insomnia, are often abused .", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Morphine is a very effective sedative and painkiller, and is very useful in patients who have undergone surgery.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Several plants, fruits and seeds having hallucinogenic properties have been used for hundreds of years in folk-medicine, religious ceremonies and rituals all over the globe.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When these are taken for a purpose other than medicinal use or in amounts/frequency that impairs one’s physical, physiological or psychological functions, it constitutes drug abuse.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["301481", "301719"]',NULL),
(3348,29,29,'paragraph',NULL,'In fermentation, say by yeast, the incomplete oxidation of glucose is achieved under anaerobic conditions by sets of reactions where pyruvic acid is converted to CO 2 and ethanol. The enzymes, pyruvic acid decarboxylase and alcohol dehydrogenase catalyse these reactions. Other organisms like some bacteria produce lactic acid from pyruvic acid. The steps involved are shown in Figure 14.2. In animal cells also, like muscles during exercise, when oxygen is inadequate for cellular respiration pyruvic acid is reduced to lactic acid by lactate dehydrogenase. The reducing agent is NADH+H + which is reoxidised to NAD + in both the processes.','[{"s": "In fermentation, say by yeast, the incomplete oxidation of glucose is achieved under anaerobic conditions by sets of reactions where pyruvic acid is converted to CO", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and ethanol.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The enzymes, pyruvic acid decarboxylase and alcohol dehydrogenase catalyse these reactions. Other organisms like some bacteria produce lactic acid from pyruvic acid. The steps involved are shown in Figure 14.2. In animal cells also, like muscles during exercise, when oxygen is inadequate for cellular respiration pyruvic acid is reduced to lactic acid by lactate dehydrogenase.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The reducing agent is NADH+H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "which is reoxidised to NAD", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "in both the processes.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["141308"]',NULL),
(3864,33,95,'paragraph',NULL,'Do you remember studying cross sections of roots, stems and leaves of higher plants and studying the vascular system? The higher plants have highly specialised vascular tissues – xylem and phloem. Xylem is associated with translocation of mainly water, mineral salts, some organic nitrogen and hormones, from roots to the aerial parts of the plants. The phloem translocates a variety of organic and inorganic solutes, mainly from the leaves to other parts of the plants.','[{"s": "Do you remember studying cross sections of roots, stems and leaves of higher plants and studying the vascular system? The higher plants have highly specialised vascular tissues – xylem and phloem.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Xylem is associated with translocation of mainly water, mineral salts, some organic nitrogen and hormones, from roots to the aerial parts of the plants.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The phloem translocates a variety of organic and inorganic solutes, mainly from the leaves to other parts of the plants.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["111320"]',NULL),
(3163,27,86,'paragraph',NULL,'Occasionally, a single gene product may produce more than one effect. For example, starch synthesis in pea seeds is controlled by one gene. It has two alleles ( B and b ). Starch is synthesised effectively by BB homozygotes and therefore, large starch grains are produced. In contrast, bb homozygotes have lesser efficiency in starch synthesis and produce smaller starch grains. After maturation of the seeds, BB seeds are round and the bb seeds are wrinkled. Heterozygotes produce round seeds, and so B seems to be the dominant allele. But, the starch grains produced are of intermediate size in Bb seeds. So if starch grain size is considered as the phenotype, then from this angle, the alleles show incomplete dominance.','[{"s": "Occasionally, a single gene product may produce more than one effect. For example, starch synthesis in pea seeds is controlled by one gene. It has two alleles (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "B", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "b", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "). Starch is synthesised effectively by", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "homozygotes and therefore, large starch grains are produced.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In contrast,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bb", "t": "text"}, {"s": "homozygotes have lesser efficiency in starch synthesis and produce smaller starch grains. After maturation of the seeds,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "seeds are round and the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "bb", "t": "text"}, {"s": "seeds are wrinkled. Heterozygotes produce round seeds, and so", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "seems to be the dominant allele. But, the starch grains produced are of intermediate size in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Bb", "t": "text"}, {"s": "seeds. So if starch grain size is considered as the phenotype, then from this angle, the alleles show incomplete dominance.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(1921,20,52,'paragraph',NULL,'In 1928, Frederick Griffith, in a series of experiments with Streptococcus pneumoniae (bacterium responsible for pneumonia), witnessed a miraculous transformation in the bacteria . During the course of his experiment, a living organism (bacteria) had changed in physical form.','[{"s": "In 1928, Frederick Griffith, in a series of experiments with Streptococcus pneumoniae (bacterium responsible for pneumonia), witnessed a miraculous transformation in the bacteria", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "During the course of his experiment, a living organism (bacteria) had changed in physical form.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["282410"]',NULL),
(29560,72,168,'paragraph',NULL,'where the negative sign shows that v (t) has a direction opposite to the positive direction of x-axis. Eq. (14.9) gives the instantaneous velocity of a particle executing SHM, where displacement is given by Eq. (14.4). We can, of course, obtain this equation without using geometrical argument, directly by differentiating (Eq. 14.4) with respect of t:','[{"s": "where the negative sign shows that v (t) has a direction opposite to the positive direction of x-axis. Eq. (14.9) gives the instantaneous velocity of a particle executing SHM, where displacement is given by Eq. (14.4).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We can, of course, obtain this equation without using geometrical argument, directly", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "by differentiating (Eq. 14.4) with respect of t:", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
