(28952,71,85,'paragraph',NULL,'We can think of binding energy per nucleon as the average energy per nucleon needed to separate a nucleus into its individual nucleons.','[{"s": "We can think of binding energy per nucleon as the average energy per nucleon needed to separate a nucleus into its individual nucleons.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000045395", "2000046141", "2000111996"]',NULL),
(1696,17,58,'paragraph',NULL,'The process of fusion of a sperm with an ovum is called fertilisation. During fertilisation, a sperm comes in contact with the zona pellucida layer of the ovum (Figure 3.10) and induces changes in the membrane that block the entry of additional sperms. Thus, it ensures that only one sperm can fertilise an ovum. The secretions of the acrosome help the sperm enter into the cytoplasm of the ovum through the zona pellucida and the plasma membrane. This induces the completion of the meiotic division of the secondary oocyte. The second meiotic division is also unequal and results in the formation of a second polar body and a haploid ovum (ootid). Soon the haploid nucleus of the sperms and that of the ovum fuse together to form a diploid zygote. How many chromosomes will be there in the zygote?','[{"s": "The process of fusion of a sperm with an ovum is called fertilisation.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "During fertilisation, a sperm comes in contact with the zona pellucida layer of the ovum (Figure 3.10) and induces changes in the membrane that block the entry of additional sperms. Thus, it ensures that only one sperm can fertilise an ovum.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The secretions of the acrosome help the sperm enter into the cytoplasm of the ovum through the zona pellucida and the plasma membrane. This induces the completion of the meiotic division of the secondary oocyte.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The second meiotic division is also unequal and results in the formation of a second polar body and a haploid ovum (ootid). Soon the haploid nucleus of the sperms and that of the ovum fuse together to form a diploid zygote.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "How many chromosomes will be there in the zygote?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["251223", "251252"]',NULL),
(26055,65,117,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) From Eq. (5.6), the most stable position is θ = 0° and the most unstable position is θ = 180°. Work done is given by','[{"s": "(b) From Eq. (5.6), the most stable position is θ = 0° and the most unstable position is θ = 180°.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Work done is given by", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3467,30,60,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Autogamy : In this type, pollination is achieved within the same flower. Transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of the same flower (Figure 2.9a). In a normal flower which opens and exposes the anthers and the stigma, complete autogamy is rather rare. Autogamy in such flowers requires synchrony in pollen release and stigma receptivity and also, the anthers and the stigma should lie close to each other so that self-pollination can occur. Some plants such as Viola (common pansy), Oxalis, and Commelina produce two types of flowers – chasmogamous flowers which are similar to flowers of other species with exposed anthers and stigma, and cleistogamous flowers which do not open at all (Figure 2.9c) I n such flowers, the anthers and stigma lie close to each other. When anthers dehisce in the flower buds, pollen grains come in contact with the stigma to effect pollination. Thus, cleistogamous flowers are invariably autogamous as there is no chance of cross-pollen landing on the stigma. Cleistogamous flowers produce assured seed-set even in the absence of pollinators. Do you think that cleistogamy is advantageous or disadvantageous to the plant? Why?','[{"s": "(i)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Autogamy", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": In this type, pollination is achieved within the same flower. Transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of the same flower (Figure 2.9a). In a normal flower which opens and exposes the anthers and the stigma, complete autogamy is rather rare. Autogamy in such flowers requires synchrony in pollen release and stigma receptivity and also, the anthers and the stigma should lie close to each other so that self-pollination can occur.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Some plants such as Viola (common pansy), Oxalis, and Commelina produce two types of flowers –", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "chasmogamous", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "flowers which are similar to flowers of other species with exposed anthers and stigma, and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "cleistogamous", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "flowers which do not open at all (Figure 2.9c)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "I", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "n such flowers, the anthers and stigma lie close to each other. When anthers dehisce in the flower buds, pollen grains come in contact with the stigma to effect pollination.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, cleistogamous flowers are invariably autogamous as there is no chance of cross-pollen landing on the stigma. Cleistogamous flowers produce assured seed-set even in the absence of pollinators.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Do you think that cleistogamy is advantageous or disadvantageous to the plant? Why?", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
