(2281,21,160,'paragraph',NULL,'Morphology_Of_Flowering_Plants-image8.png for zygomorphic nature of flower. Fusion is indicated by enclosing the figure within bracket and adhesion by a line drawn above the symbols of the floral parts. A floral diagram provides information about the number of parts of a flower, their arrangement and the relation they have with one another (Figure 5.20). The position of the mother axis with respect to the flower is represented by a dot on the top of the floral diagram. Calyx, corolla, androecium and gynoecium are drawn in successive whorls, calyx being the outermost and the gynoecium being in the centre. Floral formula also shows cohesion and adhesion within parts of whorls and between whorls. The floral diagram and floral formula in Figure 5.20 represents the mustard plant (Family: Brassicaceae).','[{"s": "Morphology_Of_Flowering_Plants-image8.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "for zygomorphic nature of flower. Fusion is indicated by enclosing the figure within bracket and adhesion by a line drawn above the symbols of the floral parts. A floral diagram provides information about the number of parts of a flower, their arrangement and the relation they have with one another (Figure 5.20). The position of the mother axis with respect to the flower is represented by a dot on the top of the floral diagram. Calyx, corolla, androecium and gynoecium are drawn in successive whorls, calyx being the outermost and the gynoecium being in the centre. Floral formula also shows cohesion and adhesion within parts of whorls and between whorls.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The floral diagram and floral formula in Figure 5.20 represents the mustard plant (Family: Brassicaceae).", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2217,21,96,'paragraph',NULL,'In cymose type of inflorescence the main axis terminates in a flower, hence is limited in growth.The flowers are borne in a basipetal order (Figure 5.12).','[{"s": "In cymose type of inflorescence the main axis terminates in a flower, hence is limited in growth.The flowers are borne in a basipetal order (Figure 5.12).", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2049,20,180,'paragraph',NULL,'tRNA has an anticodon loop that has bases complementary to the code, and it also has an amino acid acceptor end to which it binds to amino acids. tRNAs are specific for each amino acid (Figure 6.12). For initiation, there is another specific tRNA that is referred to as initiator tRNA . There are no tRNAs for stop codons. In figure 6.12, the secondary structure of tRNA has been depicted that looks like a clover-leaf. In actual structure, the tRNA is a compact molecule which looks like inverted L.','[{"s": "tRNA has an", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "anticodon loop", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "that has bases complementary to the code, and it also has an", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "amino acid acceptor end", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "to which it binds to amino acids. tRNAs are specific for each amino acid (Figure 6.12).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For initiation, there is another specific tRNA that is referred to as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "initiator", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tRNA", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". There are no tRNAs for stop codons.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In figure 6.12, the secondary structure of tRNA has been depicted that looks like a clover-leaf. In actual structure, the tRNA is a compact molecule which looks like inverted L.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["282731"]',NULL),
(2666,24,68,'paragraph',NULL,'When only PS I is functional, the electron is circulated within the photosystem and the phosphorylation occurs due to cyclic flow of electrons (Figure 13.6). A possible location where this could be happening is in the stroma lamellae. While the membrane or lamellae of the grana have both PS I and PS II the stroma lamellae membranes lack PS II as well as NADP reductase enzyme. The excited electron does not pass on to NADP + but is cycled back to the PS I complex through the electron transport chain (Figure 13.6). The cyclic flow hence, results only in the synthesis of ATP, but not of NADPH + H + . Cyclic photophosphorylation also occurs when only light of wavelengths beyond 680 nm are available for excitation.','[{"s": "When only PS I is functional, the electron is circulated within the photosystem and the phosphorylation occurs due to cyclic flow of electrons (Figure 13.6).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A possible location where this could be happening is in the stroma lamellae.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "While the membrane or lamellae of the grana have both PS I and PS II the stroma lamellae membranes lack PS II as well as NADP reductase enzyme.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The excited electron does not pass on to NADP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "but is cycled back to the PS I complex through the electron transport chain (Figure 13.6).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The cyclic flow hence, results only in the synthesis of ATP, but not of NADPH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Cyclic photophosphorylation also occurs when only light of wavelengths beyond 680 nm are available for excitation.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["131334"]',NULL),
(2087,20,218,'paragraph',NULL,'Methodologies : The methods involved two major approaches. One approach focused on identifying all the genes that are expressed as RNA (referred to as Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs). The other took the blind approach of simply sequencing the whole set of genome that contained all the coding and non-coding sequence, and later assigning different regions in the sequence with functions (a term referred to as Sequence Annotation ). For sequencing, the total DNA from a cell is isolated and converted into random fragments of relatively smaller sizes (recall DNA is a very long polymer, and there are technical limitations in sequencing very long pieces of DNA) and cloned in suitable host using specialised vectors. The cloning resulted into amplification of each piece of DNA fragment so that it subsequently could be sequenced with ease. The commonly used hosts were bacteria and yeast, and the vectors were called as BAC (bacterial artificial chromosomes), and YAC (yeast artificial chromosomes).','[{"s": "Methodologies :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The methods involved two major approaches.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "One approach focused on identifying all the genes that are expressed as RNA (referred to as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Expressed Sequence Tags", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(ESTs).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The other took the blind approach of simply sequencing the whole set of genome that contained all the coding and non-coding sequence, and later assigning different regions in the sequence with functions (a term referred to as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Sequence Annotation", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ").", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For sequencing, the total DNA from a cell is isolated and converted into random fragments of relatively smaller sizes (recall DNA is a very long polymer, and there are technical limitations in sequencing very long pieces of DNA) and cloned in suitable host using specialised vectors. The cloning resulted into amplification of each piece of DNA fragment so that it subsequently could be sequenced with ease.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The commonly used hosts were bacteria and yeast, and the vectors were called as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "BAC", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(bacterial artificial chromosomes), and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "YAC", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(yeast artificial chromosomes).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["282614", "282704", "282717"]',NULL),
(3250,27,173,'paragraph',NULL,'Down’s Syndrome : The cause of this genetic disorder is the presence of an additional copy of the chromosome number 21 (trisomy of 21).This disorder was first described by Langdon Down (1866). The affected individual is short statured with small round head, furrowed tongue and partially open mouth (Figure 5.16). Palm is broad with characteristic palm crease. Physical, psychomotor and mental development is retarded.','[{"s": "Down’s Syndrome :", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The cause of this genetic disorder is the presence of an additional copy of the chromosome number 21 (trisomy of 21).This disorder was first described by Langdon Down (1866).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The affected individual is short statured with small round head, furrowed tongue and partially open mouth (Figure 5.16).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Palm is broad with characteristic palm crease. Physical, psychomotor and mental development is retarded.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["271701", "271711", "271798"]',NULL),
(3495,30,88,'paragraph',NULL,'Pollen-pistil Interaction : Pollination does not guarantee the transfer of the right type of pollen (compatible pollen of the same species as the stigma). Often, pollen of the wrong type, either from other species or from the same plant (if it is self-incompatible), also land on the stigma. The pistil has the ability to recognise the pollen, whether it is of the right type (compatible) or of the wrong type (incompatible). If it is of the right type, the pistil accepts the pollen and promotes post-pollination events that leads to fertilisation. If the pollen is of the wrong type, the pistil rejects the pollen by preventing pollen germination on the stigma or the pollen tube growth in the style. The ability of the pistil to recognise the pollen followed by its acceptance or rejection is the result of a continuous dialogue between pollen grain and the pistil. This dialogue is mediated by chemical components of the pollen interacting with those of the pistil. It is only in recent years that botanists have been able to identify some of the pollen and pistil components and the interactions leading to the recognition, followed by acceptance or rejection.','[{"s": "Pollen-pistil Interaction", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": Pollination does not guarantee the transfer of the right type of pollen (compatible pollen of the same species as the stigma). Often, pollen of the wrong type, either from other species or from the same plant (if it is self-incompatible), also land on the stigma. The pistil has the ability to recognise the pollen, whether it is of the right type (compatible) or of the wrong type (incompatible). If it is of the right type, the pistil accepts the pollen and promotes post-pollination events that leads to fertilisation.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If the pollen is of the wrong type, the pistil rejects the pollen by preventing pollen germination on the stigma or the pollen tube growth in the style.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The ability of the pistil to recognise the pollen followed by its acceptance or rejection is the result of a continuous dialogue between pollen grain and the pistil. This dialogue is mediated by chemical components of the pollen interacting with those of the pistil. It is only in recent years that botanists have been able to identify some of the pollen and pistil components and the interactions leading to the recognition, followed by acceptance or rejection.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(32362,75,1028,'paragraph',NULL,'Question 7. 24. A bullet of mass 10 g and speed 500 m/s is fired into a door and gets embedded exactly at the center of the door. The door is 1.0 m wide and weighs 12 kg. It is hinged at one end and rotates about a vertical axis practically without friction. Find the angular speed of the door just after the bullet embeds into it.(Hint: The moment of inertia of the door about the vertical axis at one end is ML 2 /3.)','[{"s": "Question 7. 24.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A bullet of mass 10 g and speed 500 m/s is fired into a door and gets embedded exactly at the center of the door.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The door is 1.0 m wide and weighs 12 kg. It is hinged at one end and rotates about a vertical axis practically without friction. Find the angular speed of the door just after the bullet embeds into it.(Hint: The moment of inertia of the door about the vertical axis at one end is ML", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/3.)", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(30182,73,240,'paragraph',NULL,'The point where image of an object placed at infinity is formed is called the focus F, of the lens and the distance f gives its focal length . 2 A lens has two foci, F and F′, on either side of it (Fig. 9.19). By the sign convention,','[{"s": "The point where image of an object placed at infinity is formed is called the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "focus", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "F, of the lens and the distance", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "gives its", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "focal length", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A lens has two foci, F and F′, on either side of it (Fig. 9.19). By the sign convention,", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3891,33,122,'paragraph',NULL,'Usually the lower surface of a dorsiventral (often dicotyledonous) leaf has a greater number of stomata while in an isobilateral (often monocotyledonous) leaf they are about equal on both surfaces. Transpiration is affected by several external factors: temperature, light, humidity, wind speed. Plant factors that affect transpiration include number and distribution of stomata, per cent of open stomata, water status of the plant, canopy structure etc.','[{"s": "Usually the lower surface of a dorsiventral (often dicotyledonous) leaf has a greater number of stomata while in an isobilateral (often monocotyledonous) leaf they are about equal on both surfaces.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Transpiration is affected by several external factors: temperature, light, humidity, wind speed.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Plant factors that affect transpiration include number and distribution of stomata, per cent of open stomata, water status of the plant, canopy structure etc.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["111321"]',NULL),
(21517,55,487,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) Eddy currents : The alternating magnetic flux induces eddy currents in the iron core and causes heating. The effect is reduced by using a laminated core.','[{"s": "(iii)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Eddy currents", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ": The alternating magnetic flux induces eddy currents in the iron core and causes heating.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The effect is reduced by using a laminated core.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000045240", "2000112686"]',NULL),
(2398,22,48,'paragraph',NULL,'The hindbrain comprises pons , cerebellum and medulla (also called the medulla oblongata). Pons consists of fibre tracts that interconnect different regions of the brain. Cerebellum has very convoluted surface in order to provide the additional space for many more neurons. The medulla of the brain is connected to the spinal cord. The medulla contains centres which control respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions.','[{"s": "The hindbrain comprises", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "pons", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ",", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "cerebellum", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "medulla", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(also called the medulla oblongata).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Pons consists of fibre tracts that interconnect different regions of the brain.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Cerebellum has very convoluted surface in order to provide the additional space for many more neurons. The medulla of the brain is connected to the spinal cord. The medulla contains centres which control respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3242,27,165,'paragraph',NULL,'Thalassemia : This is also an autosome-linked recessive blood disease transmitted from parents to the offspring when both the partners are unaffected carrier for the gene (or heterozygous). The defect could be due to either mutation or deletion which ultimately results in reduced rate of synthesis of one of the globin chains (a and b chains) that make up haemoglobin. This causes the formation of abnormal haemoglobin molecules resulting into anaemia which is characteristic of the disease. Thalassemia can be classified according to which chain of the haemoglobin molecule is affected. In a Thalassemia, production of a globin chain is affected while in b Thalassemia, production of b globin chain is affected. a Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes HBA1 and HBA2 on chromosome 16 of each parent and it is observed due to mutation or deletion of one or more of the four genes. The more genes affected, the less alpha globin molecules produced. While b Thalassemia is controlled by a single gene HBB on chromosome 11 of each parent and occurs due to mutation of one or both the genes. Thalassemia differs from sickle-cell anaemia in that the former is a quantitative problem of synthesising too few globin molecules while the latter is a qualitative problem of synthesising an incorrectly functioning globin.','[{"s": "Thalassemia :", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is also an autosome-linked recessive blood disease transmitted from parents to the offspring when both the partners are unaffected carrier for the gene (or heterozygous).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The defect could be due to either mutation or deletion which ultimately results in reduced rate of synthesis of one of the globin chains (a and b chains) that make up haemoglobin. This causes the formation of abnormal haemoglobin molecules resulting into anaemia which is characteristic of the disease. Thalassemia can be classified according to which chain of the haemoglobin molecule is affected. In a Thalassemia, production of a globin chain is affected while in b Thalassemia, production of b globin chain is affected. a Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes HBA1 and HBA2 on chromosome 16 of each parent and it is observed due to mutation or deletion of one or more of the four genes. The more genes affected, the less alpha globin molecules produced. While b Thalassemia is controlled by a single gene HBB on chromosome 11 of each parent and occurs due to mutation of one or both the genes.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thalassemia differs from sickle-cell anaemia in that the former is a quantitative problem of synthesising too few globin molecules while the latter is a qualitative problem of synthesising an incorrectly functioning globin.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["271709", "271729"]',NULL),
(28679,70,499,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnetic force q (v × B) is normal to v and work done by it is zero.','[{"s": "The magnetic force q (v × B) is normal to v and work done by it is zero.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(22311,57,195,'paragraph',NULL,'Two or more resistors are said to be in parallel if one end of all the resistors is joined together and similarly the other ends joined together (Fig . 3.15).','[{"s": "Two or more resistors are said to be in parallel if one end of all the resistors is joined together and similarly the other ends joined together (Fig", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3.15).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041545", "2000042688"]',NULL),
(2227,21,106,'paragraph',NULL,'Based on the position of calyx, corolla and androecium in respect of the ovary on thalamus, the flowers are described as hypogynous, perigynous and epigynous (Figure 5.13). In the hypogynous flower the gynoecium occupies the highest position while the other parts are situated below it. The ovary in such flowers is said to be superior , e.g., mustard, china rose and brinjal. If gynoecium is situated in the centre and other parts of the flower are located on the rim of the thalamus almost at the same level, it is called perigynous . The ovary here is said to be half inferior, e.g., plum, rose, peach. In epigynous flowers, the margin of thalamus grows upward enclosing the ovary completely and getting fused with it, the other parts of flower arise above the ovary. Hence, the ovary is said to be inferior as in flowers of guava and cucumber, and the ray florets of sunflower.','[{"s": "Based on the position of calyx, corolla and androecium in respect of the ovary on thalamus, the flowers are described as hypogynous, perigynous and epigynous (Figure 5.13).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "hypogynous", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "flower the gynoecium occupies the highest position while the other parts are situated below it. The ovary in such flowers is said to be", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "superior", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", e.g., mustard, china rose and brinjal.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "If gynoecium is situated in the centre and other parts of the flower are located on the rim of the thalamus almost at the same level, it is called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "perigynous", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". The ovary here is said to be half inferior, e.g., plum, rose, peach.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In", "t": "text"}, {"s": "epigynous", "t": "text"}, {"s": "flowers, the margin of thalamus grows upward enclosing the ovary completely and getting fused with it, the other parts of flower arise above the ovary.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hence, the ovary is said to be", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "inferior", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "as in flowers of guava and cucumber, and the ray florets of sunflower.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["51070", "51071"]',NULL),
(30247,73,305,'paragraph',NULL,'The derivation is valid for any number of thin lenses in contact. 2 If several thin lenses of focal length f 1 , f 2 , f 3 ,... are in contact, the effective focal length of their combination is given by 2','[{"s": "The derivation is valid for any number of thin lenses in contact.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "If several thin lenses of focal length", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ",", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ",", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ",... are in contact, the effective focal length of their combination is given by", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
