(31827,75,493,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 7.8 A metal bar 70 cm long and 4.00 kg in mass supported on two knife-edges placed 10 cm from each end. A 6.00 kg load is suspended at 30 cm from one end. Find the reactions at the knife-edges. (Assume the bar to be of uniform cross section and homogeneous.)','[{"s": "Example 7.8", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A metal bar 70 cm long and 4.00 kg in mass supported on two knife-edges placed 10 cm from each end.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A 6.00 kg load is suspended at 30 cm from one end. Find the reactions at the knife-edges. (Assume the bar to be of uniform cross section and homogeneous.)", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2394,22,44,'paragraph',NULL,'The forebrain consists of cerebrum, thalamus and hypothalamus (Figure 21.4). Cerebrum forms the major part of the human brain. A deep cleft divides the cerebrum longitudinally into two halves, which are termed as the left and right cerebral hemispheres . The hemispheres are connected by a tract of nerve fibres called corpus callosum . The layer of cells which covers the cerebral hemisphere is called cerebral cortex and is thrown into prominent folds. The cerebral cortex is referred to as the grey matter due to its greyish appearance. The neuron cell bodies are concentrated here giving the colour. The cerebral cortex contains motor areas, sensory areas and large regions that are neither clearly sensory nor motor in function. These regions called as the association areas are responsible for complex functions like intersensory associations, memory and communication. Fibres of the tracts are covered with the myelin sheath, which constitute the inner part of cerebral hemisphere. They give an opaque white appearance to the layer and, hence, is called the white matter. The cerebrum wraps around a structure called thalamus, which is a major coordinating centre for sensory and motor signaling. Another very important part of the brain called hypothalamus lies at the base of the thalamus. The hypothalamus contains a number of centres which control body temperature, urge for eating and drinking. It also contains several groups of neurosecretory cells, which secrete hormones called hypothalamic hormones. The inner parts of cerebral hemispheres and a group of associated deep structures like amygdala, hippocampus, etc., form a complex structure called the limbic lobe or limbic system. Along with the hypothalamus, it is involved in the regulation of sexual behaviour, expression of emotional reactions (e.g., excitement, pleasure, rage and fear), and motivation.','[{"s": "The forebrain consists of cerebrum, thalamus and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "hypothalamus", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Figure 21.4).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Cerebrum forms the major part of the human brain. A deep cleft divides the cerebrum longitudinally into two halves, which are termed as the left and right", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cerebral hemispheres", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The hemispheres are connected by a tract of nerve fibres called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "corpus callosum", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The layer of cells which covers the cerebral hemisphere is called cerebral cortex and is thrown into prominent folds. The cerebral cortex is referred to as the grey matter due to its greyish appearance. The neuron cell bodies are concentrated here giving the colour. The cerebral cortex contains motor areas, sensory areas and large regions that are neither clearly sensory nor motor in function. These regions called as the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "association areas", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are responsible for complex functions like intersensory associations, memory and communication. Fibres of the tracts are covered with the myelin sheath, which constitute the inner part of cerebral hemisphere. They give an opaque white appearance to the layer and, hence, is called the white matter. The cerebrum wraps around a structure called thalamus, which is a major coordinating centre for sensory and motor signaling. Another very important part of the brain called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hypothalamus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lies at the base of the thalamus.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The hypothalamus contains a number of centres which control body temperature, urge for eating and drinking.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It also contains several groups of neurosecretory cells, which secrete hormones called hypothalamic hormones.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The inner parts of cerebral hemispheres and a group of associated deep structures like amygdala, hippocampus, etc., form a complex structure called the limbic lobe or limbic system. Along with the hypothalamus, it is involved in the regulation of sexual behaviour, expression of emotional reactions (e.g., excitement, pleasure, rage and fear), and motivation.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["210732", "211517"]',NULL),
(26147,65,209,'paragraph',NULL,'So what happens if we take our compass to the magnetic pole? At the poles, the magnetic field lines are converging or diverging vertically so that the horizontal component is negligible. If the needle is only capable of moving in a horizontal plane, it can point along any direction, rendering it useless as a direction finder. What one needs in such a case is a dip needle which is a compass pivoted to move in a vertical plane containing the magnetic field of the earth. The needle of the compass then shows the angle which the magnetic field makes with the vertical. At the magnetic poles such a needle will point straight down.','[{"s": "So what happens if we take our compass to the magnetic pole?", "t": "text"}, {"s": "At the poles, the magnetic field lines are converging or diverging vertically so that the horizontal component is negligible.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the needle is only capable of moving in a horizontal plane, it can point along any direction, rendering it useless as a direction finder. What one needs in such a case is a dip needle which is a compass pivoted to move in a vertical plane containing the magnetic field of the earth. The needle of the compass then shows the angle which the magnetic field makes with the vertical. At the magnetic poles such a needle will point straight down.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2921,26,52,'paragraph',NULL,'These gametophytes require cool, damp, shady places to grow. Because of this specific restricted requirement and the need for water for fertilisation, the spread of living pteridophytes is limited and restricted to narrow geographical regions. The gametophytes bear male and female sex organs called antheridia and archegonia, respectively. Water is required for transfer of antherozoids – the male gametes released from the antheridia, to the mouth of archegonium. Fusion of male gamete with the egg present in the archegonium result in the formation of zygote. Zygote thereafter produces a multicellular well-differentiated sporophyte which is the dominant phase of the pteridophytes. In majority of the pteridophytes all the spores are of similar kinds; such plants are called homosporous . Genera like Selaginella and Salvinia which produce two kinds of spores, macro (large) and micro (small) spores, are known as heterosporous . The megaspores and microspores germinate and give rise to female and male gametophytes, respectively. The female gametophytes in these plants are retained on the parent sporophytes for variable periods. The development of the zygotes into young embryos take place within the female gametophytes. This event is a precursor to the seed habit considered an important step in evolution.','[{"s": "These gametophytes require cool, damp, shady places to grow. Because of this specific restricted requirement and the need for water for fertilisation, the spread of living pteridophytes is limited and restricted to narrow geographical regions. The gametophytes bear male and female sex organs called antheridia and archegonia, respectively.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Water is required for transfer of antherozoids – the male gametes released from the antheridia, to the mouth of archegonium.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Fusion of male gamete with the egg present in the archegonium result in the formation of zygote.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Zygote thereafter produces a multicellular well-differentiated sporophyte which is the dominant phase of the pteridophytes.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In majority of the pteridophytes all the spores are of similar kinds; such plants are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "homosporous", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Genera like Selaginella and Salvinia which produce two kinds of spores, macro (large) and micro (small) spores, are known as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "heterosporous", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The megaspores and microspores germinate and give rise to female and male gametophytes, respectively. The female gametophytes in these plants are retained on the parent sporophytes for variable periods. The development of the zygotes into young embryos take place within the female gametophytes. This event is a precursor to the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "seed habit", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "considered an important step in evolution.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
