(2003,20,134,'paragraph',NULL,'An intriguing question is that how is the RNA polymerases able to catalyse all the three steps, which are initiation, elongation and termination. The RNA polymerase is only capable of catalysing the process of elongation. It associates transiently with initiation-factor (σ) and termination-factor (ρ) to initiate and terminate the transcription, respectively. Association with these factors alter the specificity of the RNA polymerase to either initiate or terminate (Figure 6.10).','[{"s": "An intriguing question is that how is the RNA polymerases able to catalyse all the three steps, which are initiation, elongation and termination. The RNA polymerase is only capable of catalysing the process of elongation. It associates transiently with", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "initiation-factor", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(σ) and termination-factor (ρ) to initiate and terminate the transcription, respectively.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Association with these factors alter the specificity of the RNA polymerase to either initiate or terminate (Figure 6.10).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["282709"]',NULL),
(1886,20,17,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us recapitulate the chemical structure of a polynucleotide chain (DNA or RNA). A nucleotide has three components – a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar (ribose in case of RNA, and deoxyribose for DNA), and a phosphate group. There are two types of nitrogenous bases – Purines (Adenine and Guanine), and Pyrimidines (Cytosine, Uracil and Thymine). Cytosine is common for both DNA and RNA and Thymine is present in DNA. Uracil is present in RNA at the place of Thymine. A nitrogenous base is linked to the OH of 1\'C pentose sugar through a N-glycosidic linkage to form a nucleoside, such as adenosine or deoxyadenosine, guanosine or deoxyguanosine, cytidine or deoxycytidine and uridine or deoxythymidine. When a phosphate group is linked to OH of 5\'C of a nucleoside through phosphoester linkage, a corresponding nucleotide (or deoxynucleotide depending upon the type of sugar present) is formed.','[{"s": "Let us recapitulate the chemical structure of a polynucleotide chain (DNA or RNA).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A nucleotide has three components – a nitrogenous base, a pentose sugar (ribose in case of RNA, and deoxyribose for DNA), and a phosphate group.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "There are two types of nitrogenous bases – Purines (Adenine and Guanine), and Pyrimidines (Cytosine, Uracil and Thymine). Cytosine is common for both DNA and RNA", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and Thymine is present in DNA. Uracil is present in RNA at the place of Thymine.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A nitrogenous base is linked to the OH of 1\'C pentose sugar through a N-glycosidic linkage to form a nucleoside, such as adenosine or deoxyadenosine, guanosine or deoxyguanosine, cytidine or deoxycytidine and uridine or deoxythymidine.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When a phosphate group is linked to OH of 5\'C of a nucleoside through phosphoester linkage, a corresponding nucleotide (or deoxynucleotide depending upon the type of sugar present) is formed.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["282402"]',NULL),
