(29991,73,49,'paragraph',NULL,'We now derive the mirror equation or the relation between the object distance ( u ), image distance ( v ) and the focal length ( f ).','[{"s": "We now derive the mirror equation or the relation between the object distance (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "u", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "), image distance (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "v", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") and the focal length (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ").", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(1662,17,24,'paragraph',NULL,'The oviducts (fallopian tubes), uterus and vagina constitute the female accessory ducts. Each fallopian tube is about 10-12 cm long and extends from the periphery of each ovary to the uterus (Figure 3.3b), the part closer to the ovary is the funnel-shaped infundibulum . The edges of the infundibulum possess finger-like projections called fimbriae , which help in collection of the ovum after ovulation. The infundibulum leads to a wider part of the oviduct called ampulla . The last part of the oviduct, isthmus has a narrow lumen and it joins the uterus.','[{"s": "The oviducts (fallopian tubes), uterus and vagina constitute the female accessory ducts.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Each fallopian tube is about 10-12 cm long and extends from the periphery of each ovary to the uterus (Figure 3.3b), the part closer to the ovary is the funnel-shaped", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "infundibulum", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". The edges of the infundibulum possess finger-like projections called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "fimbriae", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", which help in collection of the ovum after ovulation. The infundibulum leads to a wider part of the oviduct called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ampulla", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". The last part of the oviduct,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "isthmus", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "has a narrow lumen and it joins the uterus.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["251246"]',NULL),
(3478,30,71,'paragraph',NULL,'Pollination by wind is more common amongst abiotic pollinations. Wind pollination also requires that the pollen grains are light and non-sticky so that they can be transported in wind currents. They often possess well-exposed stamens (so that the pollens are easily dispersed into wind currents, Figure 2.10) and large often-feathery stigma to easily trap air-borne pollen grains. Wind-pollinated flowers often have a single ovule in each ovary and numerous flowers packed into an inflorescence; a familiar example is the corn cob – the tassels you see are nothing but the stigma and style which wave in the wind to trap pollen grains. Wind-pollination is quite common in grasses.','[{"s": "Pollination by wind is more common amongst abiotic pollinations.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Wind pollination also requires that the pollen grains are light and non-sticky so that they can be transported in wind currents.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "They often possess well-exposed stamens (so that the pollens are easily dispersed into wind currents, Figure 2.10) and large often-feathery stigma to easily trap air-borne pollen grains.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Wind-pollinated flowers often have a single ovule in each ovary and numerous flowers packed into an inflorescence; a familiar example is the corn cob – the tassels you see are nothing but the stigma and style which wave in the wind to trap pollen grains. Wind-pollination is quite common in grasses.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(30404,73,462,'paragraph',NULL,'The main considerations with an astronomical telescope are its light gathering power and its resolution or resolving power. The former clearly depends on the area of the objective. With larger diameters, fainter objects can be observed. The resolving power, or the ability to observe two objects distinctly, which are in very nearly the same direction, also depends on the diameter of the objective. So, the desirable aim in optical telescopes is to make them with objective of large diameter. The largest lens objective in use has a diameter of 40 inch (~1.02 m). It is at the Yerkes Observatory in Wisconsin, USA. Such big lenses tend to be very heavy and therefore, difficult to make and support by their edges. Further, it is rather difficult and expensive to make such large sized lenses which form images that are free from any kind of chromatic aberration and distortions.','[{"s": "The main considerations with an astronomical telescope are its light gathering power and its resolution or resolving power.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The former clearly depends on the area of the objective.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "With larger diameters, fainter objects can be observed. The resolving power, or the ability to observe two objects distinctly, which are in very nearly the same direction, also depends on the diameter of the objective. So, the desirable aim in optical telescopes is to make them with objective of large diameter. The largest lens objective in use has a diameter of 40 inch (~1.02 m). It is at the Yerkes Observatory in Wisconsin, USA. Such big lenses tend to be very heavy and therefore, difficult to make and support by their edges. Further, it is rather difficult and expensive to make such large sized lenses which form images that are free from any kind of chromatic aberration and distortions.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3569,31,31,'paragraph',NULL,'Fibres and fibroblasts are compactly packed in the dense connective tissues. Orientation of fibres show a regular or irregular pattern and are called dense regular and dense irregular tissues. In the dense regular connective tissues, the collagen fibres are present in rows between many parallel bundles of fibres. Tendons, which attach skeletal muscles to bones and ligaments which attach one bone to another are examples of this tissue. Dense irregular connective tissue has fibroblasts and many fibres (mostly collagen) that are oriented differently (Figure 7.5). This tissue is present in the skin. Cartilage, bones and blood are various types of specialised connective tissues.','[{"s": "Fibres and fibroblasts are compactly packed in the dense connective tissues.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Orientation of fibres show a regular or irregular pattern and are called dense regular and dense irregular tissues.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the dense regular connective tissues, the collagen fibres are present in rows between many parallel bundles of fibres.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Tendons, which attach skeletal muscles to bones and ligaments which attach one bone to another are examples of this tissue.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Dense irregular connective tissue has fibroblasts and many fibres (mostly collagen) that are oriented differently", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Figure 7.5). This tissue is present in the skin. Cartilage, bones and blood are various types of specialised connective tissues.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["70735", "70743", "70744", "70771", "70775", "70909"]',NULL),
