(25059,62,309,'paragraph',NULL,'The total energy of an circularly orbiting satellite is thus negative, with the potential energy being negative but twice is magnitude of the positive kinetic energy.','[{"s": "The total energy of an circularly orbiting satellite is thus negative, with the potential energy being negative but twice is magnitude of the positive kinetic energy.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(28260,70,80,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 4.3 What is the radius of the path of an electron (mass 9 × 10 -31 kg and charge 1.6 × 10 –19 C) moving at a speed of 3 ×10 7 m/s in a magnetic field of 6 × 10 –4 T perpendicular to it? What is its frequency? Calculate its energy in keV. ( 1 eV = 1.6 × 10 –19 J).','[{"s": "Example 4.3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What is the radius of the path of an electron (mass 9 × 10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "-31", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "kg and charge 1.6 × 10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–19", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "C) moving at a speed of 3 ×10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "7", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "m/s in a magnetic field of 6 × 10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "T perpendicular to it? What is its frequency?", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Calculate its energy in keV. ( 1 eV = 1.6 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–19", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J).", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2831,25,84,'paragraph',NULL,'In most higher plants, the growing apical bud inhibits the growth of the lateral (axillary) buds, a phenomenon called apical dominance . Removal of shoot tips (decapitation) usually results in the growth of lateral buds (Figure 15.11). It is widely applied in tea plantations, hedge-making. Can you explain why?','[{"s": "In most higher plants, the growing apical bud inhibits the growth of the lateral (axillary) buds, a phenomenon called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "apical dominance", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". Removal of shoot tips (decapitation) usually results in the growth of lateral buds (Figure 15.11). It is widely applied in tea plantations, hedge-making.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Can you explain why?", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(29481,72,89,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 14.4 The location of the particle in SHM at the discrete values t = 0, T/4, T/2, 3T/4, T, 5T/4. 2 The time after which motion repeats itself is T. T will remain fixed, no matter what location you choose as the initial (t = 0) location. The speed is maximum for zero displacement (at x = 0) and zero at the extremes of motion.','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14.4 The location of the particle in SHM at the discrete values t = 0, T/4, T/2, 3T/4, T, 5T/4.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The time after which motion repeats itself is T. T will remain fixed, no matter what location you choose as the initial (t = 0) location. The speed is maximum for zero displacement (at x = 0) and zero at the extremes of motion.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
