(18430,51,309,'paragraph',NULL,'Two solutions having same osmotic pressure at a given temperature are called isotonic solutions . 2 When such solutions are separated by semipermeable membrane no osmosis occurs between them. For examplmembrane e, the osmotic pressure associated with the fluid inside the blood cell is equivalent to that of 0.9% (mass/volume) sodium chloride solution, called normal saline solution and it is safe to inject intravenously. On the other hand, if we place the cells in a solution containing more than 0.9% (mass/volume) sodium chloride, water will flow out of the cells and they would shrink. Such a solution is called hypertonic . If the salt concentration is less than 0.9% (mass/volume), the solution is said to be hypotonic . In this case, water will flow into the cells if placed in this solution and they would swell.','[{"s": "Two solutions having same osmotic pressure at a given temperature are called isotonic solutions", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When such solutions are separated by semipermeable membrane no osmosis occurs between them.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For examplmembrane e, the osmotic pressure associated with the fluid inside the blood cell is equivalent to that of 0.9% (mass/volume) sodium chloride solution, called normal saline solution and it is safe to inject intravenously. On the other hand, if we place the cells in a solution containing more than 0.9% (mass/volume) sodium chloride, water will flow out of the cells and they would shrink. Such a solution is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hypertonic", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If the salt concentration is less than 0.9% (mass/volume), the solution is said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hypotonic", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In this case, water will flow into the cells if placed in this solution and they would swell.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["60933", "160937"]',NULL),
(3338,29,19,'paragraph',NULL,'During the process of respiration, oxygen is utilised, and carbon dioxide, water and energy are released as products. The combustion reaction requires oxygen. But some cells live where oxygen may or may not be available. Can you think of such situations (and organisms) where O2 is not available? There are sufficient reasons to believe that the first cells on this planet lived in an atmosphere that lacked oxygen. Even among present-day living organisms, we know of several that are adapted to anaerobic conditions. Some of these organisms are facultative anaerobes, while in others the requirement for anaerobic condition is obligate. In any case, all living organisms retain the enzymatic machinery to partially oxidise glucose without the help of oxygen. This breakdown of glucose to pyruvic acid is called glycolysis.','[{"s": "During the process of respiration, oxygen is utilised, and carbon dioxide, water and energy are released as products. The combustion reaction requires oxygen. But some cells live where oxygen may or may not be available. Can you think of such situations (and organisms) where O2 is not available? There are sufficient reasons to believe that the first cells on this planet lived in an atmosphere that lacked oxygen. Even among present-day living organisms, we know of several that are adapted to anaerobic conditions. Some of these organisms are facultative anaerobes, while in others the requirement for anaerobic condition is obligate. In any case, all living organisms retain the enzymatic machinery to partially oxidise glucose without the help of oxygen.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This breakdown of glucose to pyruvic acid is called glycolysis.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["141310"]',NULL),
(3635,31,97,'paragraph',NULL,'The alimentary canal present in the body cavity is divided into three regions: foregut, midgut and hindgut (Figure 7.16). The mouth opens into a short tubular pharynx, leading to a narrow tubular passage called oesophagus. This in turn opens into a sac like structure called crop used for storing of food. The crop is followed by gizzard or proventriculus. It has an outer layer of thick circular muscles and thick inner cuticle forming six highly chitinous plate called teeth. Gizzard helps in grinding the food particles. The entire foregut is lined by cuticle. A ring of 6-8 blind tubules called hepatic or gastric caeca is present at the junction of foregut and midgut, which secrete digestive juice. At the junction of midgut and hindgut is present another ring of 100-150 yellow coloured thin filamentous Malpighian tubules. They help in removal of excretory products from haemolymph. The hindgut is broader than midgut and is differentiated into ileum, colon and rectum. The rectum opens out through anus.','[{"s": "The alimentary canal present in the body cavity is divided into three regions: foregut, midgut and hindgut (Figure 7.16).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The mouth opens into a short tubular pharynx, leading to a narrow tubular passage called oesophagus. This in turn opens into a sac like structure called crop used for storing of food. The crop is followed by gizzard or proventriculus.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It has an outer layer of thick circular muscles and thick inner cuticle forming six highly chitinous plate called teeth.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gizzard helps in grinding the food particles.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The entire foregut is lined by cuticle.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A ring of 6-8 blind tubules called hepatic or gastric caeca is present at the junction of foregut and midgut, which secrete digestive juice. At the junction of midgut and hindgut is present another ring of 100-150 yellow coloured thin filamentous Malpighian tubules.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "They help in removal of excretory products from haemolymph. The hindgut is broader than midgut and is differentiated into ileum, colon and rectum. The rectum opens out through anus.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["70716", "70726", "70767"]',NULL),
(3921,33,152,'paragraph',NULL,'Food, primarily sucrose, is transported by the vascular tissue phloem from a source to a sink. Usually the source is understood to be that part of the plant which synthesises the food, i.e., the leaf, and sink, the part that needs or stores the food. But, the source and sink may be reversed depending on the season, or the plant’s needs. Sugar stored in roots may be mobilised to become a source of food in the early spring when the buds of trees, act as sink; they need energy for growth and development of the photosynthetic apparatus. Since the source-sink relationship is variable, the direction of movement in the phloem can be upwards or downwards, i.e., bi-directional . This contrasts with that of the xylem where the movement is always unidirectional , i.e., upwards . Hence, unlike one-way flow of water in transpiration, food in phloem sap can be transported in any required direction so long as there is a source of sugar and a sink able to use, store or remove the sugar.','[{"s": "Food, primarily sucrose, is transported by the vascular tissue phloem from a source to a sink. Usually the source is understood to be that part of the plant which synthesises the food, i.e., the leaf, and sink, the part that needs or stores the food. But, the source and sink may be reversed depending on the season, or the plant’s needs. Sugar stored in roots may be mobilised to become a source of food in the early spring when the buds of trees, act as sink; they need energy for growth and development of the photosynthetic apparatus.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Since the source-sink relationship is variable, the direction of movement in the phloem can be upwards or downwards, i.e.,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "bi-directional", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". This contrasts with that of the xylem where the movement is always", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "unidirectional", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", i.e., upwards", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Hence, unlike one-way flow of water in transpiration, food in phloem sap can be transported in any required direction so long as there is a source of sugar and a sink able to use, store or remove the sugar.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["111322", "111328"]',NULL),
(24503,61,515,'paragraph',NULL,'In the parallel combination, the total capacitance C is:','[{"s": "In the parallel combination, the total capacitance C is:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000110609"]',NULL),
(3701,32,25,'paragraph',NULL,'Perhaps, the most obvious and technically complicated feature of all living organisms is this ability to sense their surroundings or environment and respond to these environmental stimuli which could be physical, chemical or biological. We sense our environment through our sense organs. Plants respond to external factors like light, water, temperature, other organisms, pollutants, etc. All organisms, from the prokaryotes to the most complex eukaryotes can sense and respond to environmental cues. Photoperiod affects reproduction in seasonal breeders, both plants and animals. All organisms handle chemicals entering their bodies. All organisms therefore, are ‘aware’ of their surroundings. Human being is the only organism who is aware of himself, i.e., has self-consciousness. Consciousness therefore, becomes the defining property of living organisms.','[{"s": "Perhaps, the most obvious and technically complicated feature of all living organisms is this ability to sense their surroundings or environment and respond to these environmental stimuli which could be physical, chemical or biological. We sense our environment through our sense organs. Plants respond to external factors like light, water, temperature, other organisms, pollutants, etc. All organisms, from the prokaryotes to the most complex eukaryotes can sense and respond to environmental cues. Photoperiod affects reproduction in seasonal breeders, both plants and animals. All organisms handle chemicals entering their bodies. All organisms therefore, are ‘aware’ of their surroundings. Human being is the only organism who is aware of himself, i.e., has self-consciousness.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Consciousness therefore, becomes the defining property of living organisms.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(29625,72,233,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the potential energy of a particle executing simple harmonic motion is also periodic, with period T/2, being zero at the mean position and maximum at the extreme displacements. 2','[{"s": "Thus, the potential energy of a particle executing simple harmonic motion is also periodic, with period T/2, being zero at the mean position and maximum at the extreme displacements.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3526,30,119,'paragraph',NULL,'In most plants, by the time the fruit develops from the ovary, other floral parts degenerate and fall off. However, in a few species such as apple, strawberry, cashew, etc., the thalamus also contributes to fruit formation. Such fruits are called false fruits (Figure 2.15b). Most fruits however develop only from the ovary and are called true fruits . Although in most of the species, fruits are the results of fertilisation, there are a few species in which fruits develop without fertilisation. Such fruits are called parthenocarpic fruits . Banana is one such example. Parthenocarpy can be induced through the application of growth hormones and such fruits are seedless.','[{"s": "In most plants, by the time the fruit develops from the ovary, other floral parts degenerate and fall off.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "However, in a few species such as apple, strawberry, cashew, etc., the thalamus also contributes to fruit formation. Such fruits are called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "false fruits", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Figure 2.15b).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Most fruits however develop only from the ovary and are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "true fruits", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Although in most of the species, fruits are the results of fertilisation, there are a few species in which fruits develop without fertilisation. Such fruits are called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "parthenocarpic fruits", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". Banana is one such example.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Parthenocarpy can be induced through the application of growth hormones and such fruits are seedless.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["241621"]',NULL),
(32088,75,754,'paragraph',NULL,'We have studied in section 7.7, the angular momentum of a system of particles. We already know from there that the time rate of total angular momentum of a system of particles about a point is equal to the total external torque on the system taken about the same point. When the total external torque is zero, the total angular momentum of the system is conserved.','[{"s": "We have studied in section 7.7, the angular momentum of a system of particles. We already know from there that the time rate of total angular momentum of a system of particles about a point is equal to the total external torque on the system taken about the same point.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When the total external torque is zero, the total angular momentum of the system is conserved.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(17155,49,248,'paragraph',NULL,'Table 7.5: Oxoacids of Phosphorus 3','[{"s": "Table 7.5: Oxoacids of Phosphorus 3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["214206", "214241"]',NULL),
(1915,20,46,'paragraph',NULL,'In eukaryotes, this organisation is much more complex. There is a set of positively charged, basic proteins called histones . A protein acquires charge depending upon the abundance of amino acids residues with charged side chains. Histones are rich in the basic amino acid residues lysine and arginine. Both the amino acid residues carry positive charges in their side chains. Histones are organised to form a unit of eight molecules called histone octamer . The negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form a structure called nucleosome (Figure 6.4 a). A typical nucleosome contains 200 bp of DNA helix. Nucleosomes constitute the repeating unit of a structure in nucleus called chromatin , thread-like stained (coloured) bodies seen in nucleus. The nucleosomes in chromatin are seen as ‘beads-on-string’ structure when viewed under electron microscope (EM) (Figure 6.4 b).','[{"s": "In eukaryotes, this organisation is much more complex. There is a set of positively charged, basic proteins called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "histones", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A protein acquires charge depending upon the abundance of amino acids residues with charged side chains.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Histones are rich in the basic amino acid residues lysine and arginine.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Both the amino acid residues carry positive charges in their side chains.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Histones are organised to form a unit of eight molecules called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "histone octamer", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". The negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form a structure called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "nucleosome", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Figure 6.4 a). A typical nucleosome contains 200 bp of DNA helix. Nucleosomes constitute the repeating unit of a structure in nucleus called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "chromatin", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", thread-like stained (coloured) bodies seen in nucleus. The nucleosomes in chromatin are seen as ‘beads-on-string’ structure when viewed under electron microscope (EM) (Figure 6.4 b).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["282403", "282713", "282729"]',NULL),
(32249,75,915,'paragraph',NULL,'7.21 A solid cylinder rolls up an inclined plane of angle of inclination 30°. At the bottom of the inclined plane the centre of mass of the cylinder has a speed of 5 m/s.','[{"s": "7.21", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A solid cylinder rolls up an inclined plane of angle of inclination 30°.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "At the bottom of the inclined plane the centre of mass of the cylinder has a speed of 5 m/s.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(26204,65,266,'paragraph',NULL,'The simplest explanation for diamagnetism is as follows. Electrons in an atom orbiting around nucleus possess orbital angular momentum. These orbiting electrons are equivalent to current-carrying loop and thus possess orbital magnetic moment. Diamagnetic substances are the ones in which resultant magnetic moment in an atom is zero. 2 When magnetic field is applied, those electrons having orbital magnetic moment in the same direction slow down and those in the opposite direction speed up. This happens due to induced current in accordance with Lenz’s law which you will study in Chapter 6. Thus, the substance develops a net magnetic moment in direction opposite to that of the applied field and hence repulsion.','[{"s": "The simplest explanation for diamagnetism is as follows. Electrons in an atom orbiting around nucleus possess orbital angular momentum. These orbiting electrons are equivalent to current-carrying loop and thus possess orbital magnetic moment.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Diamagnetic substances are the ones in which resultant magnetic moment in an atom is zero.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When magnetic field is applied, those electrons having orbital magnetic moment in the same direction slow down and those in the opposite direction speed up.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This happens due to induced current in accordance with Lenz’s law which you will study in Chapter 6. Thus, the substance develops a net magnetic moment in direction opposite to that of the applied field and hence repulsion.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2838,25,91,'paragraph',NULL,'Auxins also induce parthenocarpy, e.g., in tomatoes. They are widely used as herbicides. 2, 4-D, widely used to kill dicotyledonous weeds, does not affect mature monocotyledonous plants. It is used to prepare weed-free lawns by gardeners. Auxin also controls xylem differentiation and helps in cell division.','[{"s": "Auxins also induce parthenocarpy, e.g., in tomatoes.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "They are widely used as herbicides. 2, 4-D, widely used to kill dicotyledonous weeds, does not affect mature monocotyledonous plants. It is used to prepare weed-free lawns by gardeners.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Auxin also controls xylem differentiation and helps in cell division.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["151918"]',NULL),
(22663,57,547,'paragraph',NULL,'6. Kirchhoff\'s junction rule is based on conservation of charge and the outgoing currents add up and are equal to incoming current at a junction . Bending or reorienting the wire does not change the validity of Kirchhoff\'s junction rule.','[{"s": "6.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kirchhoff\'s junction rule is based on conservation of charge and the outgoing currents add up and are equal to incoming current at a junction", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Bending or reorienting the wire does not change the validity of Kirchhoff\'s junction rule.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041945", "2000110618"]',NULL),
(21921,56,179,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (12.21) is the Rydberg formula, for the spectrum of the hydrogen atom. In this relation, if we take n f = 2 and n i = 3, 4, 5..., it reduces to a form similar to Eq. (12.10) for the Balmer series. The Rydberg constant R is readily identified to be','[{"s": "Equation (12.21) is the Rydberg formula, for the spectrum of the hydrogen atom. In this relation, if we take", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 3, 4, 5..., it reduces to a form similar to Eq.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(12.10) for the Balmer series.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The Rydberg constant", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is readily identified to be", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(28406,70,226,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 4.8 Figure 4.15 shows a long straight wire of a circular cross-section (radius a) carrying steady current I . The current I is uniformly distributed across this cross-section. Calculate the magnetic field in the region r < a and r > a.','[{"s": "Example 4.8", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Figure 4.15 shows a long straight wire of a circular cross-section (radius a) carrying steady current I", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The current I is uniformly distributed across this cross-section. Calculate the magnetic field in the region r < a and r > a.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(31623,75,289,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, ω × r is a vector of magnitude ω Systems_Of_Particles-image242.png and is along the tangent to the circle described by the particle at P. The linear velocity vector v at P has the same magnitude and direction. Thus,','[{"s": "Thus, ω ×", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a vector of magnitude ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Systems_Of_Particles-image242.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and is along the tangent to the circle described by the particle at P.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The linear velocity vector", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at P has the same magnitude and direction.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus,", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2912,26,43,'paragraph',NULL,'The Pteridophytes include horsetails and ferns. Pteridophytes are used for medicinal purposes and as soil-binders. They are also frequently grown as ornamentals. Evolutionarily, they are the first terrestrial plants to possess vascular tissues – xylem and phloem. You shall study more about these tissues in Chapter 6. The pteridophytes are found in cool, damp, shady places though some may flourish well in sandy-soil conditions.','[{"s": "The Pteridophytes include horsetails and ferns.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Pteridophytes are used for medicinal purposes and as soil-binders. They are also frequently grown as ornamentals. Evolutionarily, they are the first terrestrial plants to possess vascular tissues – xylem and phloem. You shall study more about these tissues in Chapter 6. The pteridophytes are found in cool, damp, shady places though some may flourish well in sandy-soil conditions.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(29926,72,534,'paragraph',NULL,'14.18 A cylindrical piece of cork of density of base area A and height hfloats in a liquid of density Oscillations-image70.png','[{"s": "14.18", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A cylindrical piece of cork of density of base area A and height hfloats in a liquid of density", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Oscillations-image70.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2095,20,226,'paragraph',NULL,'(iii) The total number of genes is estimated at 30,000–much lower than previous estimates of 80,000 to 1,40,000 genes. Almost all (99.9 per cent) nucleotide bases are exactly the same in all people.','[{"s": "(iii) The total number of genes is estimated at 30,000–much lower than previous estimates of 80,000 to 1,40,000 genes.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Almost all (99.9 per cent) nucleotide bases are exactly the same in all people.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3699,32,23,'paragraph',NULL,'Reproduction, likewise, is a characteristic of living organisms . In multicellular organisms, reproduction refers to the production of progeny possessing features more or less similar to those of parents. Invariably and implicitly we refer to sexual reproduction. Organisms reproduce by asexual means also. Fungi multiply and spread easily due to the millions of asexual spores they produce. In lower organisms like yeast and hydra, we observe budding. In Planaria (flat worms), we observe true regeneration, i.e., a fragmented organism regenerates the lost part of its body and becomes, a new organism. The fungi, the filamentous algae, the protonema of mosses, all easily multiply by fragmentation. When it comes to unicellular organisms like bacteria, unicellular algae or Amoeba, reproduction is synonymous with growth, i.e., increase in number of cells. We have already defined growth as equivalent to increase in cell number or mass. Hence, we notice that in single-celled organisms, we are not very clear about the usage of these two terms – growth and reproduction. Further, there are many organisms which do not reproduce (mules, sterile worker bees, infertile human couples, etc). Hence, reproduction also cannot be an all-inclusive defining characteristic of living organisms. Of course, no non-living object is capable of reproducing or replicating by itself.','[{"s": "Reproduction, likewise, is a characteristic of living organisms", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In multicellular organisms, reproduction refers to the production of progeny possessing features more or less similar to those of parents. Invariably and implicitly we refer to sexual reproduction. Organisms reproduce by asexual means also. Fungi multiply and spread easily due to the millions of asexual spores they produce. In lower organisms like yeast and hydra, we observe budding. In Planaria (flat worms), we observe true regeneration, i.e., a fragmented organism regenerates the lost part of its body and becomes, a new organism.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The fungi, the filamentous algae, the protonema of mosses, all easily multiply by fragmentation. When it comes to unicellular organisms like bacteria, unicellular algae or Amoeba, reproduction is synonymous with growth, i.e., increase in number of cells.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "We have already defined growth as equivalent to increase in cell number or mass. Hence, we notice that in single-celled organisms, we are not very clear about the usage of these two terms – growth and reproduction. Further, there are many organisms which do not reproduce (mules, sterile worker bees, infertile human couples, etc). Hence, reproduction also cannot be an all-inclusive defining characteristic of living organisms. Of course, no non-living object is capable of reproducing or replicating by itself.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2318,21,197,'paragraph',NULL,'Fruits : berry or capsule','[{"s": "Fruits", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ": berry or capsule", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3659,31,121,'paragraph',NULL,'The vascular system of frog is well-developed closed type. Frogs have a lymphatic system also. The blood vascular system involves heart, blood vessels and blood. The lymphatic system consists of lymph, lymph channels and lymph nodes. Heart is a muscular structure situated in the upper part of the body cavity. It has three chambers, two atria and one ventricle and is covered by a membrane called pericardium. A triangular structure called sinus venosus joins the right atrium. It receives blood through the major veins called vena cava. The ventricle opens into a sac-like conus arteriosus on the ventral side of the heart. The blood from the heart is carried to all parts of the body by the arteries (arterial system). The veins collect blood from different parts of body to the heart and form the venous system. Special venous connection between liver and intestine as well as the kidney and lower parts of the body are present in frogs. The former is called hepatic portal system and the latter is called renal portal system. The blood is composed of plasma and cells. The blood cells are RBC (red blood cells) or erythrocytes, WBC (white blood cells) or leucocytes and platelets. RBC’s are nucleated and contain red coloured pigment namely haemoglobin. The lymph is different from blood. It lacks few proteins and RBCs. The blood carries nutrients, gases and water to the respective sites during the circulation. The circulation of blood is achieved by the pumping action of the muscular heart.','[{"s": "The vascular system of frog is well-developed closed type. Frogs have a lymphatic system also. The blood vascular system involves heart, blood vessels and blood. The lymphatic system consists of lymph, lymph channels and lymph nodes. Heart is a muscular structure situated in the upper part of the body cavity.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It has three chambers, two atria and one ventricle and is covered by a membrane called pericardium.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A triangular structure called sinus venosus joins the right atrium. It receives blood through the major veins called vena cava. The ventricle opens into a sac-like conus arteriosus on the ventral side of the heart. The blood from the heart is carried to all parts of the body by the arteries (arterial system). The veins collect blood from different parts of body to the heart and form the venous system. Special venous connection between liver and intestine as well as the kidney and lower parts of the body are present in frogs.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The former is called hepatic portal system and the latter is called renal portal system.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The blood is composed of plasma and cells. The blood cells are RBC (red blood cells) or erythrocytes, WBC (white blood cells) or leucocytes and platelets.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "RBC’s are nucleated and contain red coloured pigment namely haemoglobin.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The lymph is different from blood. It lacks few proteins and RBCs. The blood carries nutrients, gases and water to the respective sites during the circulation. The circulation of blood is achieved by the pumping action of the muscular heart.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["70717", "70788", "70789"]',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_blocks` (`id`, `chapter_id`, `idx`, `type`, `level`, `text`, `content`, `status`, `pyq_ids`, `image_url`) VALUES
(2654,24,56,'paragraph',NULL,'In photosystem II the reaction centre chlorophyll a absorbs 680 nm wavelength of red light causing electrons to become excited and jump into an orbit farther from the atomic nucleus. These electrons are picked up by an electron acceptor which passes them to an electrons transport system consisting of cytochromes (Figure 13.5). This movement of electrons is downhill, in terms of an oxidation-reduction or redox potential scale. The electrons are not used up as they pass through the electron transport chain, but are passed on to the pigments of photosystem PS I. Simultaneously, electrons in the reaction centre of PS I are also excited when they receive red light of wavelength 700 nm and are transferred to another accepter molecule that has a greater redox potential. These electrons then are moved downhill again, this time to a molecule of energy-rich NADP + .','[{"s": "In photosystem II the reaction centre chlorophyll a absorbs 680 nm wavelength of red light causing electrons to become excited and jump into an orbit farther from the atomic nucleus.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "These electrons are picked up by an electron acceptor which passes them to an electrons transport system consisting of cytochromes (Figure 13.5). This movement of electrons is downhill, in terms of an oxidation-reduction or redox potential scale. The electrons are not used up as they pass through the electron transport chain, but are passed on to the pigments of photosystem PS I. Simultaneously, electrons in the reaction centre of PS I are also excited when they receive red light of wavelength 700 nm and are transferred to another accepter molecule that has a greater redox potential. These electrons then are moved downhill again, this time to a molecule of energy-rich NADP", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(1767,18,50,'paragraph',NULL,'There are 12 pairs of ribs. Each rib is a thin flat bone connected dorsally to the vertebral column and ventrally to the sternum. It has two articulation surfaces on its dorsal end and is hence called bicephalic. First seven pairs of ribs are called true ribs. Dorsally, they are attached to the thoracic vertebrae and ventrally connected to the sternum with the help of hyaline cartilage. The 8th, 9th and 10th pairs of ribs do not articulate directly with the sternum but join the seventh rib with the help of hyaline cartilage. These are called vertebrochondral (false) ribs. Last 2 pairs (11th and 12th) of ribs are not connected ventrally and are therefore, called floating ribs. Thoracic vertebrae, ribs and sternum together form the rib cage (Figure 20.8).','[{"s": "There are 12 pairs of ribs. Each rib is a thin flat bone connected dorsally to the vertebral column and ventrally to the sternum.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It has two articulation surfaces on its dorsal end and is hence called bicephalic.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "First seven pairs of ribs are called true ribs. Dorsally, they are attached to the thoracic vertebrae and ventrally connected to the sternum with the help of hyaline cartilage.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The 8th, 9th and 10th pairs of ribs do not articulate directly with the sternum but join the seventh rib with the help of hyaline cartilage.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These are called vertebrochondral (false) ribs. Last 2 pairs (11th and 12th) of ribs are not connected ventrally and are therefore, called floating ribs.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thoracic vertebrae, ribs and sternum together form the rib cage (Figure 20.8).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["200651", "201613"]',NULL),
(3633,31,95,'paragraph',NULL,'The abdomen in both males and females consists of 10 segments. In females, the 7th sternum is boat shaped and together with the 8th and 9th sterna forms a brood or genital pouch whose anterior part contains female gonopore, spermathecal pores and collateral glands. In males, genital pouch or chamber lies at the hind end of abdomen bounded dorsally by 9th and 10th terga and ventrally by the 9th sternum. It contains dorsal anus, ventral male genital pore and gonapophysis. Males bear a pair of short, thread-like anal styles which are absent in females. In both sexes, the 10th segment bears a pair of jointed filamentous structures called anal cerci.','[{"s": "The abdomen in both males and females consists of 10 segments. In females, the 7th sternum is boat shaped and together with the 8th and 9th sterna forms a brood or genital pouch whose anterior part contains female gonopore, spermathecal pores and collateral glands. In males, genital pouch or chamber lies at the hind end of abdomen bounded dorsally by 9th and 10th terga and ventrally by the 9th sternum. It contains dorsal anus, ventral male genital pore and gonapophysis.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Males bear a pair of short, thread-like anal styles which are absent in females. In both sexes, the 10th segment bears a pair of jointed filamentous structures called anal cerci.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["70712", "70759", "70774"]',NULL),
(2270,21,149,'paragraph',NULL,'Generally, monocotyledonous seeds are endospermic but some as in orchids are non-endospermic. In the seeds of cereals such as maize the seed coat is membranous and generally fused with the fruit wall. The endosperm is bulky and stores food. The outer covering of endosperm separates the embryo by a proteinous layer called aleurone layer . The embryo is small and situated in a groove at one end of the endosperm. It consists of one large and shield shaped cotyledon known as scutellum and a short axis with a plumule and a radicle. The plumule and radicle are enclosed in sheaths which are called coleoptile and coleorhiza respectively (Figure 5.19).','[{"s": "Generally, monocotyledonous seeds are endospermic but some as in orchids are non-endospermic. In the seeds of cereals such as maize the seed coat is membranous and generally fused with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "the fruit wall. The endosperm is bulky and stores food. The outer covering of endosperm separates the embryo by a proteinous layer called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "aleurone layer", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The embryo is small and situated in a groove at one end of the endosperm.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It consists of one large and shield shaped cotyledon known as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "scutellum", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and a short axis with a plumule and a radicle.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The plumule and radicle are enclosed in sheaths which are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "coleoptile", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "coleorhiza", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively (Figure 5.19).", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3389,29,70,'paragraph',NULL,'Glucose is the favoured substrate for respiration. All carbohydrates are usually first converted into glucose before they are used for respiration. Other substrates can also be respired, as has been mentioned earlier, but then they do not enter the respiratory pathway at the first step. See Figure 14.6 to see the points of entry of different substrates in the respiratory pathway. Fats would need to be broken down into glycerol and fatty acids first. If fatty acids were to be respired they would first be degraded to acetyl CoA and enter the pathway. Glycerol would enter the pathway after being converted to PGAL. The proteins would be degraded by proteases and the individual amino acids (after deamination) depending on their structure would enter the pathway at some stage within the Krebs’ cycle or even as pyruvate or acetyl CoA.','[{"s": "Glucose is the favoured substrate for respiration. All carbohydrates are usually first converted into glucose before they are used for respiration. Other substrates can also be respired, as has been mentioned earlier, but then they do not enter the respiratory pathway at the first step. See Figure 14.6 to see the points of entry of different substrates in the respiratory pathway. Fats would need to be broken down into glycerol and fatty acids first. If fatty acids were to be respired they would first be degraded to acetyl CoA and enter the pathway. Glycerol would enter the pathway after being converted to PGAL.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The proteins would be degraded by proteases and the individual amino acids (after deamination) depending on their structure would enter the pathway at some stage within the Krebs’ cycle or even as pyruvate or acetyl CoA.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(28663,70,483,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 4.13 In the circuit (Fig. 4.27) the current is to be measured. What is the value of the current if the ammeter shown','[{"s": "Example 4.13", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In the circuit (Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4.27) the current is to be measured.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What is the value of the current if the ammeter shown", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2290,21,169,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.21 Pisum sativum (pea) plant : (a) Flowering twig (b) Flower (c) Petals (d) Reproductive parts (e) L.S.carpel (f) Floral diagram','[{"s": "Figure 5.21", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Pisum sativum (pea) plant : (a) Flowering twig (b) Flower (c) Petals (d) Reproductive parts (e) L.S.carpel (f) Floral diagram", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3302,28,42,'paragraph',NULL,'Infections or diseases which are transmitted through sexual intercourse are collectively called sexually transmitted infections (STI) or venereal diseases (VD) or reproductive tract infections (RTI). Gonorrhoea, syphilis, genital herpes, chlamydiasis, genital warts, trichomoniasis, hepatitis-B and of course, the most discussed infection in the recent years, HIV leading to AIDS are some of the common STIs . Among these, HIV infection is most dangerous and is discussed in detail in Chapter 8.','[{"s": "Infections or diseases which are transmitted through sexual intercourse are collectively called sexually transmitted infections (STI) or venereal diseases (VD) or reproductive tract infections (RTI).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Gonorrhoea, syphilis, genital herpes, chlamydiasis, genital warts, trichomoniasis, hepatitis-B and of course, the most discussed infection in the recent years, HIV leading to AIDS are some of the common STIs", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Among these, HIV infection is most dangerous and is discussed in detail in Chapter 8.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["261106", "261107", "261108", "261122", "261125"]',NULL),
(1695,17,57,'paragraph',NULL,'During copulation (coitus) semen is released by the penis into the vagina (insemination). The motile sperms swim rapidly, pass through the cervix, enter into the uterus and finally reach the ampullary region of the fallopian tube (Figure 3.11b). The ovum released by the ovary is also transported to the ampullary region where fertilisation takes place. Fertilisation can only occur if the ovum and sperms are transported simultaneously to the ampullary region . This is the reason why not all copulations lead to fertilisation and pregnancy.','[{"s": "During copulation (coitus) semen is released by the penis into the vagina (insemination).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The motile sperms swim rapidly, pass through the cervix, enter into the uterus and finally reach the ampullary region of the fallopian tube (Figure 3.11b). The ovum released by the ovary is also transported to the ampullary region where fertilisation takes place.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Fertilisation can only occur if the ovum and sperms are transported simultaneously to the ampullary region", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This is the reason why not all copulations lead to fertilisation and pregnancy.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(31881,75,547,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that the parameter I is independent of the magnitude of the angular velocity. 2 It is a characteristic of the rigid body and the axis about which it rotates.','[{"s": "Note that the parameter I is independent of the magnitude of the angular velocity.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is a characteristic of the rigid body and the axis about which it rotates.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2816,25,69,'paragraph',NULL,'The plant growth regulators (PGRs) are small, simple molecules of diverse chemical composition. They could be indole compounds (indole-3-acetic acid, IAA); adenine derivatives (N6-furfurylamino purine, kinetin), derivatives of carotenoids (abscisic acid, ABA); terpenes (gibberellic acid, GA3) or gases (ethylene, C2H4). Plant growth regulators are variously described as plant growth substances, plant hormones or phytohormones in literature.','[{"s": "The plant growth regulators (PGRs) are small, simple molecules of diverse chemical composition.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "They could be indole compounds (indole-3-acetic acid, IAA); adenine derivatives (N6-furfurylamino purine, kinetin), derivatives of carotenoids (abscisic acid, ABA); terpenes (gibberellic acid, GA3) or gases (ethylene, C2H4).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Plant growth regulators are variously described as plant growth substances, plant hormones or phytohormones in literature.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(29504,72,112,'paragraph',NULL,'ω is called the angular frequency of SHM. Its S.I. unit is radians per second. Since the frequency of oscillations is simply 1/T, ω is 2π times the frequency of oscillation. Two simple harmonic motions may have the same A and φ, but different ω, as seen in Fig. 14.8. In this plot the curve (b) has half the period and twice the frequency of the curve (a).','[{"s": "ω is called the angular frequency of SHM. Its S.I. unit is radians per second.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Since the frequency of oscillations is simply 1/T, ω is 2π times the frequency of oscillation.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Two simple harmonic motions may have the same A and φ, but different ω, as seen in Fig. 14.8. In this plot the curve (b) has half the period and twice the frequency of the curve (a).", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2903,26,34,'paragraph',NULL,'The plant body of bryophytes is more differentiated than that of algae. It is thallus-like and prostrate or erect, and attached to the substratum by unicellular or multicellular rhizoids. They lack true roots, stem or leaves. They may possess root-like, leaf-like or stem-like structures. The main plant body of the bryophyte is haploid. It produces gametes, hence is called a gametophyte . The sex organs in bryophytes are multicellular. The male sex organ is called antheridium . They produce biflagellate antherozoids . The female sex organ called archegonium is flask-shaped and produces a single egg. The antherozoids are released into water where they come in contact with archegonium. An antherozoid fuses with the egg to produce the zygote. Zygotes do not undergo reduction division immediately. They produce a multicellular body called a sporophyte . The sporophyte is not free-living but attached to the photosynthetic gametophyte and derives nourishment from it. Some cells of the sporophyte undergo reduction division (meiosis) to produce haploid spores. These spores germinate to produce gametophyte.','[{"s": "The plant body of bryophytes is more differentiated than that of algae. It is thallus-like and prostrate or erect, and attached to the substratum by unicellular or multicellular rhizoids. They lack true roots, stem or leaves. They may possess root-like, leaf-like or stem-like structures.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The main plant body of the bryophyte is haploid. It produces gametes, hence is called a", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "gametophyte", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The sex organs in bryophytes are multicellular. The male sex organ is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "antheridium", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". They produce biflagellate", "t": "text"}, {"s": "antherozoids", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The female sex organ called archegonium is flask-shaped and produces a single egg. The antherozoids are released into water where they come in contact with archegonium.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "An antherozoid fuses with the egg to produce the zygote. Zygotes do not undergo reduction division immediately. They produce a multicellular body called a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "sporophyte", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The sporophyte is not free-living but attached to the photosynthetic gametophyte and derives nourishment from it.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Some cells of the sporophyte undergo reduction division (meiosis) to produce haploid spores. These spores germinate to produce gametophyte.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(30761,74,139,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.16 Experimental circuit arrangement for studying V-I characteristics of a p-n junction diode (a) in forward bias, (b) in reverse bias. (c) Typical V-I characteristics of a silicon diode.','[{"s": "Figure 14.16 Experimental circuit arrangement for studying V-I characteristics of a p-n junction diode (a) in forward bias, (b) in reverse bias.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(c) Typical V-I characteristics of a silicon diode.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046964"]',NULL),
(3363,29,44,'paragraph',NULL,'The TCA cycle starts with the condensation of acetyl group with oxaloacetic acid (OAA) and water to yield citric acid (Figure 14.3). The reaction is catalysed by the enzyme citrate synthase and a molecule of CoA is released. Citrate is then isomerised to isocitrate. It is followed by two successive steps of decarboxylation, leading to the formation of α-ketoglutaric acid and then succinyl-CoA. In the remaining steps of citric acid cycle, succinyl-CoA is oxidised to OAA allowing the cycle to continue. During the conversion of succinyl-CoA to succinic acid a molecule of GTP is synthesised. This is a substrate level phosphorylation. In a coupled reaction GTP is converted to GDP with the simultaneous synthesis of ATP from ADP. Also there are three points in the cycle where NAD + is reduced to NADH + H + and one point where FAD + is reduced to FADH 2 . The continued oxidation of acetyl CoA via the TCA cycle requires the continued replenishment of oxaloacetic acid, the first member of the cycle. In addition it also requires regeneration of NAD + and FAD + from NADH and FADH 2 respectively. The summary equation for this phase of respiration may be written as follows:','[{"s": "The TCA cycle starts with the condensation of acetyl group with oxaloacetic acid (OAA) and water to yield citric acid (Figure 14.3).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The reaction is catalysed by the enzyme citrate synthase and a molecule of CoA is released.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Citrate is then isomerised to isocitrate.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is followed by two successive steps of decarboxylation, leading to the formation of α-ketoglutaric acid and then succinyl-CoA.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In the remaining steps of citric acid cycle, succinyl-CoA is oxidised to OAA allowing the cycle to continue. During the conversion of succinyl-CoA to succinic acid a molecule of GTP is synthesised. This is a substrate level phosphorylation.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In a coupled reaction GTP is converted to GDP with the simultaneous synthesis of ATP from ADP. Also there are three points in the cycle where NAD", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is reduced to NADH + H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and one point where FAD", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is reduced to FADH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The continued oxidation of acetyl CoA via the TCA cycle requires the continued replenishment of oxaloacetic acid, the first member of the cycle. In addition it also requires regeneration of NAD", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and FAD", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from NADH and FADH", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively. The summary equation for this phase of respiration may be written as follows:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["141217", "141219", "141221"]',NULL),
(29562,72,170,'paragraph',NULL,'The method of reference circle can be similarly used for obtaining instantaneous acceleration of a particle undergoing SHM. We know that the centripetal acceleration of a particle P in uniform circular motion has a magnitude v 2 /A or ω 2 A, and it is directed towards the centre i.e., the direction is along PO. The instantaneous acceleration of the projection particle P′ is then (See Fig. 3 14.12)','[{"s": "The method of reference circle can be similarly used for obtaining instantaneous acceleration of a particle undergoing SHM. We know that the centripetal acceleration of a particle P in uniform circular motion has a magnitude v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/A or ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A, and it is directed towards the centre i.e., the direction is along PO.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The instantaneous acceleration of the projection particle P′ is then (See Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "14.12)", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(26199,65,261,'paragraph',NULL,'The discussion in the previous section helps us to classify materials as diamagnetic, paramagnetic or ferromagnetic. In terms of the susceptibility χ, a material is diamagnetic if χ is negative, para- if χ is positive and small, and ferro- if χ is large and positive.','[{"s": "The discussion in the previous section helps us to classify materials as diamagnetic, paramagnetic or ferromagnetic.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In terms of the susceptibility χ, a material is diamagnetic if χ is negative, para- if χ is positive and small, and ferro- if χ is large and positive.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(31977,75,643,'paragraph',NULL,'The corresponding kinematic equations for rotational motion with uniform angular acceleration are: 3','[{"s": "The corresponding kinematic equations for rotational motion with uniform angular acceleration are:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2883,26,14,'paragraph',NULL,'The algae reproduce by vegetative, asexual and sexual methods. Vegetative reproduction is by fragmentation. Each fragment develops into a thallus. Asexual reproduction is by the production of different types of spores, the most common being the zoospores . They are flagellated (motile) and on germination gives rise to new plants. Sexual reproduction takes place through fusion of two gametes. These gametes can be flagellated and similar in size (as in Ulothrix) or non-flagellated (non-motile) but similar in size (as in Spirogyra). Such reproduction is called isogamous . Fusion of two gametes dissimilar in size, as in species of Eudorina is termed as anisogamous . Fusion between one large, non-motile (static) female gamete and a smaller, motile male gamete is termed oogamous , e.g., Volvox, Fucus.','[{"s": "The algae reproduce by vegetative, asexual and sexual methods. Vegetative reproduction is by fragmentation. Each fragment develops into a thallus. Asexual reproduction is by the production of different types of spores, the most common being the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "zoospores", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". They are flagellated (motile) and on germination gives rise to new plants. Sexual reproduction takes place through fusion of two gametes.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These gametes can be flagellated and similar in size (as in Ulothrix) or non-flagellated (non-motile) but similar in size (as in Spirogyra).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Such reproduction is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "isogamous", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Fusion of two gametes dissimilar in size, as in species of Eudorina is termed as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "anisogamous", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Fusion between one large, non-motile (static) female gamete and a smaller, motile male gamete is termed", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "oogamous", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", e.g., Volvox, Fucus.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2650,24,52,'paragraph',NULL,'Light reactions or the ‘Photochemical’ phase include light absorption, water splitting, oxygen release, and the formation of high-energy chemical intermediates, ATP and NADPH. Several protein complexes are involved in the process. The pigments are organised into two discrete photochemical light harvesting complexes (LHC) within the Photosystem I (PS I) and Photosystem II (PS II). These are named in the sequence of their discovery, and not in the sequence in which they function during the light reaction. The LHC are made up of hundreds of pigment molecules bound to proteins. Each photosystem has all the pigments (except one molecule of chlorophyll a) forming a light harvesting system also called antennae (Figure 13.4). These pigments help to make photosynthesis more efficient by absorbing different wavelengths of light. The single chlorophyll a molecule forms the reaction centre. The reaction centre is different in both the photosystems. In PS I the reaction centre chlorophyll a has an absorption peak at 700 nm, hence is called P700 , while in PS II it has absorption maxima at 680 nm, and is called P680 .','[{"s": "Light reactions or the ‘Photochemical’ phase include light absorption, water splitting, oxygen release, and the formation of high-energy chemical intermediates, ATP and NADPH.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Several protein complexes are involved in the process. The pigments are organised into two discrete photochemical light", "t": "text"}, {"s": "harvesting complexes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(LHC) within the Photosystem I (PS I) and Photosystem II (PS II).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These are named in the sequence of their discovery, and not in the sequence in which they function during the light reaction. The LHC are made up of hundreds of pigment molecules bound to proteins. Each photosystem has all the pigments (except one molecule of chlorophyll a) forming a light harvesting system also called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "antennae", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Figure 13.4). These pigments help to make photosynthesis more efficient by absorbing different wavelengths of light. The single chlorophyll a molecule forms the reaction centre.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The reaction centre is different in both the photosystems.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In PS I the reaction centre chlorophyll a has an absorption peak at 700 nm, hence is called", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "P700", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ",", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "while in PS II it has absorption maxima at 680 nm, and is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P680", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["131330"]',NULL),
(3372,29,53,'paragraph',NULL,'When the electrons pass from one carrier to another via complex I to IV in the electron transport chain, they are coupled to ATP synthase (complex V) for the production of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate. The number of ATP molecules synthesised depends on the nature of the electron donor. Oxidation of one molecule of NADH gives rise to 3 molecules of ATP, while that of one molecule of FADH 2 produces 2 molecules of ATP. Although the aerobic process of respiration takes place only in the presence of oxygen, the role of oxygen is limited to the terminal stage of the process. Yet, the presence of oxygen is vital, since it drives the whole process by removing hydrogen from the system. Oxygen acts as the final hydrogen acceptor. Unlike photophosphorylation where it is the light energy that is utilised for the production of proton gradient required for phosphorylation, in respiration it is the energy of oxidation-reduction utilised for the same process. It is for this reason that the process is called oxidative phosphorylation.','[{"s": "When the electrons pass from one carrier to another via complex I to IV in the electron transport chain, they are coupled to ATP synthase (complex V) for the production of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate. The number of ATP molecules synthesised depends on the nature of the electron donor.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Oxidation of one molecule of NADH gives rise to 3 molecules of ATP, while that of one molecule of FADH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "produces 2 molecules of ATP.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Although the aerobic process of respiration takes place only in the presence of oxygen, the role of oxygen is limited to the terminal stage of the process. Yet, the presence of oxygen is vital, since it drives the whole process by removing hydrogen from the system. Oxygen acts as the final hydrogen acceptor.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Unlike photophosphorylation where it is the light energy that is utilised for the production of proton gradient required for phosphorylation, in respiration it is the energy of oxidation-reduction utilised for the same process. It is for this reason that the process is called oxidative phosphorylation.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["141113", "141118"]',NULL),
