(29593,72,201,'paragraph',NULL,'Using Newton’s second law of motion, and the expression for acceleration of a particle undergoing SHM (Eq . 14.11), the force acting on a particle of mass m in SHM is','[{"s": "Using Newton’s second law of motion, and the expression for acceleration of a particle undergoing SHM (Eq", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14.11), the force acting on a particle of mass m in SHM is", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2951,26,82,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Sporophytic generation is represented only by the one-celled zygote. There are no free-living sporophytes. Meiosis in the zygote results in the formation of haploid spores. The haploid spores divide mitotically and form the gametophyte. The dominant, photosynthetic phase in such plants is the free-living gametophyte. This kind of life cycle is termed as haplontic. Many algae such as Volvox, Spirogyra and some species of Chlamydomonas represent this pattern (Figure 3.7 a).','[{"s": "1. Sporophytic generation is represented only by the one-celled zygote. There are no free-living sporophytes.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Meiosis in the zygote results in the formation of haploid spores.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The haploid spores divide mitotically and form the gametophyte. The dominant, photosynthetic phase in such plants is the free-living gametophyte. This kind of life cycle is termed as haplontic.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Many algae such as Volvox, Spirogyra and some species of Chlamydomonas represent this pattern (Figure 3.7 a).", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2848,25,101,'paragraph',NULL,'Ethylene is used to initiate flowering and for synchronising fruit-set in pineapples. It also induces flowering in mango. Since ethylene regulates so many physiological processes, it is one of the most widely used PGR in agriculture. The most widely used compound as source of ethylene is ethephon. Ethephon in an aqueous solution is readily absorbed and transported within the plant and releases ethylene slowly. Ethephon hastens fruit ripening in tomatoes and apples and accelerates abscission in flowers and fruits (thinning of cotton, cherry, walnut). It promotes female flowers in cucumbers thereby increasing the yield.','[{"s": "Ethylene is used to initiate flowering and for synchronising fruit-set in pineapples.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It also induces flowering in mango. Since ethylene regulates so many physiological processes, it is one of the most widely used PGR in agriculture. The most widely used compound as source of ethylene is ethephon. Ethephon in an aqueous solution is readily absorbed and transported within the plant and releases ethylene slowly. Ethephon hastens fruit ripening in tomatoes and apples and accelerates abscission in flowers and fruits (thinning of cotton, cherry, walnut).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It promotes female flowers in cucumbers thereby increasing the yield.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["151806"]',NULL),
(2663,24,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Living organisms have the capability of extracting energy from oxidisable substances and store this in the form of bond energy. Special substances like ATP, carry this energy in their chemical bonds. The process through which ATP is synthesised by cells (in mitochondria and chloroplasts) is named phosphorylation. Photo-phosphorylation is the synthesis of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate in the presence of light. When the two photosystems work in a series, first PS II and then the PS I, a process called non-cyclic photo-phosphorylation occurs. The two photosystems are connected through an electron transport chain, as seen earlier – in the Z scheme. Both ATP and NADPH + H + are synthesised by this kind of electron flow (Figure 13.5).','[{"s": "Living organisms have the capability of extracting energy from oxidisable substances and store this in the form of bond energy. Special substances like ATP, carry this energy in their chemical bonds. The process through which ATP is synthesised by cells (in mitochondria and chloroplasts) is named phosphorylation.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Photo-phosphorylation is the synthesis of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate in the presence of light.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When the two photosystems work in a series, first PS II and then the PS I, a process called non-cyclic photo-phosphorylation occurs. The two photosystems are connected through an electron transport chain, as seen earlier – in the Z scheme.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Both ATP and NADPH", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "H", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "are synthesised by this kind of electron flow (Figure 13.5).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["131334"]',NULL),
