(21794,56,52,'paragraph',NULL,'The kinetic energy ( K ) and electrostatic potential energy ( U ) of the electron in hydrogen atom are','[{"s": "The kinetic energy (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "K", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") and electrostatic potential energy (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "U", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") of the electron in hydrogen atom are", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2239,21,118,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 5.15 Types of aestivation in corolla : (a) Valvate (b) Twisted (c) Imbricate (d) Vexillary','[{"s": "Figure 5.15", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Types of aestivation in corolla : (a) Valvate (b) Twisted (c) Imbricate (d) Vexillary", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2384,22,34,'paragraph',NULL,'A nerve impulse is transmitted from one neuron to another through junctions called synapses. A synapse is formed by the membranes of a pre-synaptic neuron and a post-synaptic neuron, which may or may not be separated by a gap called synaptic cleft . There are two types of synapses, namely, electrical synapses and chemical synapses. At electrical synapses, the membranes of pre- and post-synaptic neurons are in very close proximity. Electrical current can flow directly from one neuron into the other across these synapses. Transmission of an impulse across electrical synapses is very similar to impulse conduction along a single axon. Impulse transmission across an electrical synapse is always faster than that across a chemical synapse. Electrical synapses are rare in our system.','[{"s": "A nerve impulse is transmitted from one neuron to another through junctions called synapses. A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "synapse", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is formed by the membranes of a pre-synaptic neuron and a post-synaptic neuron, which may or may not be separated by a gap called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "synaptic cleft", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". There are two types of synapses, namely, electrical synapses and chemical synapses. At electrical synapses, the membranes of pre- and post-synaptic neurons are in very close proximity. Electrical current can flow directly from one neuron into the other across these synapses. Transmission of an impulse across electrical synapses is very similar to impulse conduction along a single axon.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Impulse transmission across an electrical synapse is always faster than that across a chemical synapse.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Electrical synapses are rare in our system.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3293,28,33,'paragraph',NULL,'The Medical Termination of Pregnancy (Amendment) Act, 2017 was enacted by the government of India with the intension of reducing the incidence of illegal abortion and consequent maternal mortality and morbidity. According to this Act, a pregnancy may be terminated on certain considered grounds within the first 12 weeks of pregnancy on the opinion of one registered medical practitioner. If the pregnancy has lasted more than 12 weeks, but fewer than 24 weeks, two registered medical practitioners must be of the opinion, formed in good faith, that the required ground exist. The grounds for such termination of pregnancies are:','[{"s": "The Medical Termination of Pregnancy (Amendment) Act, 2017 was enacted by the government of India with the intension of reducing the incidence of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "illegal abortion", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and consequent maternal mortality and morbidity. According to this Act, a pregnancy may be terminated on certain considered grounds within the first 12 weeks of pregnancy on the opinion of one registered medical practitioner. If the pregnancy has lasted more than 12 weeks, but fewer than 24 weeks, two registered medical practitioners must be of the opinion, formed in good faith, that the required ground exist. The grounds for such termination of pregnancies are:", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(30319,73,377,'paragraph',NULL,'When light rays undergoes two internal reflections inside a raindrop, instead of one as in the primary rainbow, a secondary rainbow is formed as shown in Fig. 2 9.27(c). It is due to four-step process. The intensity of light is reduced at the second reflection and hence the secondary rainbow is fainter than the primary rainbow. Further, the order of the colours is reversed in it as is clear from Fig. 9.27(c).','[{"s": "When light rays undergoes", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "two", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "internal reflections inside a raindrop, instead of", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "one", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "as in the primary rainbow, a secondary rainbow is formed as shown in Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "9.27(c). It is due to four-step process. The intensity of light is reduced at the second reflection and hence the secondary rainbow is fainter than the primary rainbow. Further, the order of the colours is reversed in it as is clear from Fig. 9.27(c).", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
