(24553,61,565,'paragraph',NULL,'Q 2.4) A spherical conductor of radius 12 cm has a charge of 1.6 x 10 -7 C distributed uniformly on its surface. What is the electric field','[{"s": "Q 2.4) A spherical conductor of radius 12 cm has a charge of 1.6 x 10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "-7", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "C distributed uniformly on its surface.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What is the electric field", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000110475"]',NULL),
(26177,65,239,'paragraph',NULL,'where χ, a dimensionless quantity, is appropriately called the magnetic susceptibility. It is a measure of how a magnetic material responds to an external field. Table 5.2 lists χ for some elements. It is small and positive for materials, which are called paramagnetic. It is small and negative for materials, which are termed diamagnetic. In the latter case M and H are opposite in direction. From Eqs. (5.16) and (5.17) we obtain,','[{"s": "where χ, a dimensionless quantity, is appropriately called the magnetic susceptibility. It is a measure of how a magnetic material responds to an external field. Table 5.2 lists χ for some elements. It is small and positive for materials, which are called paramagnetic.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is small and negative for materials, which are termed diamagnetic.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In the latter case M and H are opposite in direction. From Eqs. (5.16) and (5.17) we obtain,", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2073,20,204,'paragraph',NULL,'Regulation of lac operon by repressor is referred to as negative regulation . Lac operon is under control of positive regulation as well, but it is beyond the scope of discussion at this level.','[{"s": "Regulation of lac operon by repressor is referred to as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "negative", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "regulation", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Lac operon is under control of positive regulation as well, but it is beyond the scope of discussion at this level.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3500,30,93,'paragraph',NULL,'As you shall learn in the chapter on plant breeding (Chapter 9), a breeder is interested in crossing different species and often genera to combine desirable characters to produce commercially ‘superior’ varieties. Artificial hybridisation is one of the major approaches of crop improvement programme. In such crossing experiments it is important to make sure that only the desired pollen grains are used for pollination and the stigma is protected from contamination (from unwanted pollen). This is achieved by emasculation and bagging techniques.','[{"s": "As you shall learn in the chapter on plant breeding (Chapter 9), a breeder is interested in crossing different species and often genera to combine desirable characters to produce commercially ‘superior’ varieties.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Artificial hybridisation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is one of the major approaches of crop improvement programme.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In such crossing experiments it is important to make sure that only the desired pollen grains are used for pollination and the stigma is protected from contamination (from unwanted pollen).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This is achieved by emasculation and bagging techniques.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["241832", "242028"]',NULL),
(2823,25,76,'paragraph',NULL,'F. Skoog and his co-workers observed that from the internodal segments of tobacco stems the callus (a mass of undifferentiated cells) proliferated only if, in addition to auxins the nutrients medium was supplemented with one of the following: extracts of vascular tissues, yeast extract, coconut milk or DNA. Miller et al. (1955), later identified and crystallised the cytokinesis promoting active substance that they termed kinetin.','[{"s": "F. Skoog and his co-workers observed that from the internodal segments of tobacco stems the callus (a mass of undifferentiated cells) proliferated only if, in addition to auxins the nutrients medium was supplemented with one of the following: extracts of vascular tissues, yeast extract, coconut milk or DNA. Miller et al. (1955), later identified and crystallised the cytokinesis promoting active substance that they termed kinetin.", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3523,30,116,'paragraph',NULL,'In angiosperms, the seed is the final product of sexual reproduction. It is often described as a fertilised ovule. Seeds are formed inside fruits. A seed typically consists of seed coat(s), cotyledon(s) and an embryo axis. The cotyledons (Figure 2.15a) of the embryo are simple structures, generally thick and swollen due to storage of food reserves (as in legumes). Mature seeds may be non-albuminous or ex-albuminous . Non-albuminous seeds have no residual endosperm as it is completely consumed during embryo development (e.g., pea, groundnut). Albuminous seeds retain a part of endosperm as it is not completely used up during embryo development (e.g., wheat, maize, barley, castor) . Occasionally, in some seeds such as black pepper and beet, remnants of nucellus are also persistent. This residual, persistent nucellus is the perisperm .','[{"s": "In angiosperms, the seed is the final product of sexual reproduction. It is often described as a fertilised ovule. Seeds are formed inside fruits. A seed typically consists of seed coat(s), cotyledon(s) and an embryo axis. The cotyledons (Figure 2.15a) of the embryo are simple structures, generally thick and swollen due to storage of food reserves (as in legumes). Mature seeds may be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "non-albuminous", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ex-albuminous", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Non-albuminous seeds have no residual endosperm as it is completely consumed during embryo development (e.g., pea, groundnut). Albuminous seeds retain a part of endosperm as it is not completely used up during embryo development (e.g., wheat, maize, barley, castor)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Occasionally, in some seeds such as black pepper and beet, remnants of nucellus are also persistent.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This residual, persistent nucellus is the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "perisperm", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["241622", "241624", "241625", "241636"]',NULL),
(1924,20,55,'paragraph',NULL,'Griffith was able to kill bacteria by heating them. He observed that heat-killed S strain bacteria injected into mice did not kill them. When he injected a mixture of heat-killed S and live R bacteria, the mice died. Moreover, he recovered living S bacteria from the dead mice.','[{"s": "Griffith was able to kill bacteria by heating them.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "He observed that heat-killed S strain bacteria injected into mice did not kill them.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When he injected a mixture of heat-killed S and live R bacteria, the mice died. Moreover, he recovered living S bacteria from the dead mice.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
