(2322,21,201,'paragraph',NULL,'Many plants belonging to this family are source of food (tomato, brinjal, potato), spice (chilli); medicine (belladonna, ashwagandha); fumigatory (tobacco); ornamentals (petunia).','[{"s": "Many plants belonging to this family are source of food (tomato, brinjal, potato), spice (chilli); medicine (belladonna, ashwagandha); fumigatory (tobacco); ornamentals (petunia).", "t": "hl"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(3346,29,27,'paragraph',NULL,'Pyruvic acid is then the key product of glycolysis. What is the metabolic fate of pyruvate? This depends on the cellular need. There are three major ways in which different cells handle pyruvic acid produced by glycolysis. These are lactic acid fermentation, alcoholic fermentation and aerobic respiration. Fermentation takes place under anaerobic conditions in many prokaryotes and unicellular eukaryotes. For the complete oxidation of glucose to CO 2 and H 2 O, however, organisms adopt Krebs’ cycle which is also called as aerobic respiration. This requires O 2 supply.','[{"s": "Pyruvic acid is then the key product of glycolysis. What is the metabolic fate of pyruvate? This depends on the cellular need. There are three major ways in which different cells handle pyruvic acid produced by glycolysis. These are lactic acid fermentation, alcoholic fermentation and aerobic respiration.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Fermentation takes place under anaerobic conditions in many prokaryotes and unicellular eukaryotes.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For the complete oxidation of glucose to CO", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O, however, organisms adopt Krebs’ cycle which is also called as aerobic respiration. This requires O", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "supply.", "t": "text"}]','marked',NULL,NULL),
(2424,22,74,'paragraph',NULL,'The ears perform two sensory functions, hearing and maintenance of body balance. Anatomically, the ear can be divided into three major sections called the outer ear , the middle ear and the inner ear (Figure 21.7). The outer ear consists of the pinna and external auditory meatus (canal). The pinna collects the vibrations in the air which produce sound. The external auditory meatus leads inwards and extends up to the tympanic membrane (the ear drum ). There are very fine hairs and wax-secreting glands in the skin of the pinna and the meatus. T he tympanic membrane is composed of connective tissues covered with skin outside and with mucus membrane inside. The middle ear contains three ossicles called malleus , incus and stapes which are attached to one another in a chain-like fashion. The malleus is attached to the tympanic membrane and the stapes is attached to the oval window of the cochlea. The ear ossicles increase the efficiency of transmission of sound waves to the inner ear. An Eustachian tube connects the middle ear cavity with the pharynx. The Eustachian tube helps in equalising the pressures on either sides of the ear drum.','[{"s": "The ears perform two sensory functions, hearing and maintenance of body balance. Anatomically, the ear can be divided into three major sections called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "outer ear", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "middle ear", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "inner ear", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Figure 21.7). The outer ear consists of the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pinna", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "external auditory meatus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(canal). The pinna collects the vibrations in the air which produce sound. The external auditory meatus leads inwards and extends up to the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "tympanic membrane", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ear drum", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}, {"s": "There are very fine hairs and wax-secreting glands in the skin of the pinna and the meatus. T", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "he tympanic membrane is composed of connective tissues covered with skin outside and with mucus membrane inside. The middle ear contains three ossicles called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "malleus", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "incus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "stapes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "which are attached to one another in a chain-like fashion.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The malleus is attached to the tympanic membrane and the stapes is attached to the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "oval window", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "of the cochlea.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The ear ossicles increase the efficiency of transmission of sound waves to the inner ear.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Eustachian tube", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "connects the middle ear cavity with the pharynx.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The Eustachian tube helps in equalising the pressures on either sides of the ear drum.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["211716", "211717"]',NULL),
