(21314,55,284,'paragraph',NULL,'For values of ω other than ω 0 , the amplitude of the current is less than the maximum value. Suppose we choose a value of ω for which the current amplitude is Alternating_Current-image116.png times its maximum value. At this value, the power dissipated by the circuit becomes half. From the curve in Fig. (7.16), we see that there are two such values of ω, say, ω 1 and ω 2 ,one greater and the other smaller than ω0 and symmetrical about ω 0 . We may write','[{"s": "For values of ω other than ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the amplitude of the current is less than the maximum value.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Suppose we choose a value of ω for which the current amplitude is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Alternating_Current-image116.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "times its maximum value. At this value, the power dissipated by the circuit becomes half. From the curve in Fig. (7.16), we see that there are two such values of ω, say, ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",one greater and the other smaller than ω0 and symmetrical about ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We may write", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000044963", "2000112653"]',NULL),
(21336,55,306,'paragraph',NULL,'The ratio Alternating_Current-image155.png is also called the quality factor, Q of the circuit .','[{"s": "The ratio", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Alternating_Current-image155.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is also called the quality factor, Q of the circuit", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000044967", "2000112654"]',NULL),
(21365,55,335,'paragraph',NULL,'The average power over a cycle is given by the average of the two terms in R.H.S. 3 of Eq. (7.37). It is only the second term which is time-dependent. Its average is zero (the positive half of the cosine cancels the negative half). Therefore,','[{"s": "The average power over a cycle is given by the average of the two terms in R.H.S.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "of Eq. (7.37). It is only the second term which is time-dependent. Its average is zero (the positive half of the cosine cancels the negative half). Therefore,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000045096", "2000112657"]',NULL),
(21380,55,350,'paragraph',NULL,'We can improve the power factor (tending to 1) by making Z tend to R. Let us understand, with the help of a phasor diagram (Fig. 7.17) how this can be achieved. Let us resolve I into two components. Ip along the applied voltage V and Iq perpendicular to the applied voltage. Iq as you have learnt in Section 7.7, is called the wattless component since corresponding to this component of current, there is no power loss. IP is known as the power component because it is in phase with the voltage and corresponds to power loss in the circuit.','[{"s": "We can improve the power factor (tending to 1) by making Z tend to R. Let us understand, with the help of a phasor diagram (Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "7.17) how this can be achieved. Let us resolve I into two components. Ip along the applied voltage V and Iq perpendicular to the applied voltage. Iq as you have learnt in Section 7.7, is called the wattless component since corresponding to this component of current, there is no power loss. IP is known as the power component because it is in phase with the voltage and corresponds to power loss in the circuit.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000045104", "2000112663"]',NULL),
(21492,55,462,'paragraph',NULL,'When an alternating voltage is applied to the primary, the resulting current produces an alternating magnetic flux which links the secondary and induces an emf in it. The value of this emf depends on the number of turns in the secondary. We consider an ideal transformer in which the primary has negligible resistance and all the flux in the core links both primary and secondary windings. Let φ be the flux in each turn in the core at time t due to current in the primary when a voltage v p is applied to it.','[{"s": "When an alternating voltage is applied to the primary, the resulting current produces an alternating magnetic flux which links the secondary and induces an emf in it.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The value of this emf depends on the number of turns in the secondary.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We consider an ideal transformer in which the primary has negligible resistance and all the flux in the core links both primary and secondary windings.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Let φ be the flux in each turn in the core at time t due to current in the primary when a voltage v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is applied to it.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000045217", "2000112667"]',NULL),
(21507,55,477,'paragraph',NULL,'Although some energy is always lost, this is a good approximation, since a well designed transformer may have an efficiency of more than 95%. 2 Combining Eqs. (7.47) and (7.48), we have','[{"s": "Although some energy is always lost, this is a good approximation, since a well designed transformer may have an efficiency of more than 95%.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Combining Eqs. (7.47) and (7.48), we have", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000045221", "2000112668"]',NULL),
(21813,56,71,'paragraph',NULL,'We might expect that the frequencies of the light emitted by a particular element would exhibit some regular pattern. Hydrogen is the simplest atom and therefore, has the simplest spectrum. In the observed spectrum, however, at first sight, there does not seem to be any resemblance of order or regularity in spectral lines. But the spacing between lines within certain sets of the hydrogen spectrum decreases in a regular way (Fig. 12.5). Each of these sets is called a spectral series . In 1885, the first such series was observed by a Swedish school teacher Johann Jakob Balmer (1825–1898) in the visible region of the hydrogen spectrum. This series is called Balmer series (Fig. 12.6). The line with the longest wavelength, 656.3 nm in the red is called Hα; the next line with wavelength 486.1 nm in the blue-green is called Hβ, the third line 434.1 nm in the violet is called Hγ; and so on. As the wavelength decreases, the lines appear closer together and are weaker in intensity. Balmer found a simple empirical formula for the observed wavelengths','[{"s": "We might expect that the frequencies of the light emitted by a particular element would exhibit some regular pattern. Hydrogen is the simplest atom and therefore, has the simplest spectrum. In the observed spectrum, however, at first sight, there does not seem to be any resemblance of order or regularity in spectral lines. But the spacing between lines within certain sets of the hydrogen spectrum decreases in a regular way (Fig. 12.5). Each of these sets is called a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "spectral series", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". In 1885, the first such series was observed by a Swedish school teacher Johann Jakob Balmer (1825–1898) in the visible region of the hydrogen spectrum. This series is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Balmer series", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(Fig. 12.6). The line with the longest wavelength, 656.3 nm in the red is called Hα; the next line with wavelength 486.1 nm in the blue-green is called Hβ, the third line 434.1 nm in the violet is called Hγ; and so on. As the wavelength decreases, the lines appear closer together and are weaker in intensity.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Balmer found a simple empirical formula for the observed wavelengths", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000044295", "2000044300", "2000044305", "2000044310", "2000044315", "2000044321", "2000044325", "2000044330", "2000044336", "2000044340", "2000044344", "2000044355", "2000044359", "2000044365", "2000044370", "2000044384", "2000044389", "2000044394", "2000044398", "2000044408", "2000044413", "2000044447", "2000044472", "2000044535", "2000044550", "2000044555", "2000044609", "2000044615", "2000044619", "2000045398", "2000045452", "2000045462", "2000045724", "2000045731", "2000045756", "2000045778", "2000045783", "2000045789", "2000045813", "2000045823", "2000045833", "2000045858", "2000045891", "2000046420", "2000046424", "2000046429", "2000046435", "2000046450", "2000112373", "2000112375", "2000112378", "2000112379", "2000112380", "2000112382", "2000112384", "2000112385", "2000112387", "2000112388", "2000112394", "2000112395", "2000112397", "2000112400", "2000112403", "2000112438", "2000112442"]',NULL),
(21788,56,46,'paragraph',NULL,'The Rutherford nuclear model of the atom which involves classical concepts, pictures the atom as an electrically neutral sphere consisting of a very small, massive and positively charged nucleus at the centre surrounded by the revolving electrons in their respective dynamically stable orbits. The electrostatic force of attraction, F e between the revolving electrons and the nucleus provides the requisite centripetal force ( F c ) to keep them in their orbits. Thus, for a dynamically stable orbit in a hydrogen atom','[{"s": "The Rutherford nuclear model of the atom which involves classical concepts, pictures the atom as an electrically neutral sphere consisting of a very small, massive and positively charged nucleus at the centre surrounded by the revolving electrons in their respective dynamically stable orbits. The electrostatic force of attraction,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "between the revolving electrons and the nucleus provides the requisite centripetal force (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "c", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") to keep them in their orbits.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, for a dynamically stable orbit in a hydrogen atom", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000044423", "2000044428", "2000044438", "2000044453", "2000044467", "2000044476", "2000044482", "2000044487", "2000044491", "2000044497", "2000044511", "2000044546", "2000044565", "2000044570", "2000044575", "2000044581", "2000044589", "2000044600", "2000044604", "2000045457", "2000045472", "2000045716", "2000045720", "2000045746", "2000045750", "2000045763", "2000045767", "2000045819", "2000045867", "2000045884", "2000045887", "2000046404", "2000046409", "2000046414", "2000046439", "2000046444", "2000112364", "2000112366", "2000112368", "2000112369", "2000112370", "2000112372", "2000112374", "2000112377", "2000112381", "2000112383", "2000112386", "2000112389", "2000112390", "2000112391", "2000112392", "2000112393", "2000112396", "2000112398", "2000112399", "2000112402", "2000112439", "2000112440", "2000112441", "2000112445", "2000112446", "2000112447", "2000112448"]',NULL),
(21861,56,119,'paragraph',NULL,'Bohr’s second postulate of quantisation [Eq. (12.11)] says that the allowed values of angular momentum are integral multiples of h /2π.','[{"s": "Bohr’s second postulate of quantisation [Eq.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(12.11)] says that the allowed values of angular momentum are integral multiples of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2π.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000044501", "2000112367"]',NULL),
(21764,56,22,'paragraph',NULL,'The scattering data shown in Fig. 12.3 can be analysed by employing Rutherford’s nuclear model of the atom. As the gold foil is very thin, it can be assumed that α-particles will suffer not more than one scattering during their passage through it. Therefore, computation of the trajectory of an alpha-particle scattered by a single nucleus is enough. Alpha-particles are nuclei of helium atoms and, therefore, carry two units, 2 e , of positive charge and have the mass of the helium atom. The charge of the gold nucleus is Z e , where Z is the atomic number of the atom; for gold Z = 79. Since the nucleus of gold is about 50 times heavier than an α-particle, it is reasonable to assume that it remains stationary throughout the scattering process. Under these assumptions, the trajectory of an alpha-particle can be computed employing Newton’s second law of motion and the Coulomb’s law for electrostatic force of repulsion between the alpha-particle and the positively charged nucleus.','[{"s": "The scattering data shown in Fig. 12.3 can be analysed by employing Rutherford’s nuclear model of the atom. As the gold foil is very thin, it can be assumed that α-particles will suffer not more than one scattering during their passage through it. Therefore, computation of the trajectory of an alpha-particle scattered by a single nucleus is enough.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Alpha-particles are nuclei of helium atoms and, therefore, carry two units, 2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "e", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", of positive charge and have the mass of the helium atom.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The charge of the gold nucleus is Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", where", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the atomic number of the atom; for gold", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 79. Since the nucleus of gold is about 50 times heavier than an α-particle, it is reasonable to assume that it remains stationary throughout the scattering process. Under these assumptions, the trajectory of an alpha-particle can be computed employing Newton’s second law of motion and the Coulomb’s law for electrostatic force of repulsion between the alpha-particle and the positively charged nucleus.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000044520", "2000044585", "2000044595", "2000044625", "2000044630", "2000045467", "2000045880", "2000046394", "2000112371", "2000112376", "2000112443", "2000112444"]',NULL),
(21775,56,33,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 12.2 In a Geiger-Marsden experiment, what is the distance of closest approach to the nucleus of a 7.7 MeV α-particle before it comes momentarily to rest and reverses its direction?','[{"s": "Example 12.2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In a Geiger-Marsden experiment, what is the distance of closest approach to the nucleus of a 7.7 MeV α-particle before it comes momentarily to rest and reverses its direction?", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000112401"]',NULL),
(22248,57,132,'paragraph',NULL,'The resistivity of a material is found to be dependent on the temperature. Different materials do not exhibit the same dependence on temperatures. Over a limited range of temperatures, that is not too large, the resistivity of a metallic conductor is approximately given by,','[{"s": "The resistivity of a material is found to be dependent on the temperature. Different materials do not exhibit the same dependence on temperatures.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Over a limited range of temperatures, that is not too large, the resistivity of a metallic conductor is approximately given by,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041343", "2000041366", "2000041373", "2000041377", "2000041401", "2000041413", "2000041421", "2000041429", "2000041441", "2000041445", "2000041505", "2000041521", "2000041618", "2000041622", "2000041626", "2000041630", "2000041997", "2000042017", "2000042248", "2000042252", "2000042268", "2000042272", "2000042292", "2000042324", "2000042810", "2000042916", "2000043850", "2000043882", "2000110619", "2000110633", "2000110635", "2000110642", "2000110681", "2000110731", "2000110735"]',NULL),
(22181,57,65,'paragraph',NULL,'Because of the drift, there will be net transport of charges across any area perpendicular to E. Consider a planar area A, located inside the conductor such that the normal to the area is parallel to E (Fig. 3.4). Then because of the drift, in an infinitesimal amount of time ∆t, all electrons to the left of the area at distances upto |vd|∆t would have crossed the area. If n is the number of free electrons per unit volume in the metal, then there are n ∆t |v d |A such electrons. Since each electron carries a charge –e, the total charge transported across this area A to the right in time ∆t is –ne A|v d |∆t. E is directed towards the left and hence the total charge transported along E across the area is negative of this. The amount of charge crossing the area A in time ∆t is by definition [Eq. (3.2)] I ∆t, where I is the magnitude of the current. Hence,','[{"s": "Because of the drift, there will be net transport of charges across any area perpendicular to E. Consider a planar area A, located inside the conductor such that the normal to the area is parallel to E (Fig. 3.4). Then because of the drift, in an infinitesimal amount of time ∆t, all electrons to the left of the area at distances upto |vd|∆t would have crossed the area. If n is the number of free electrons per unit volume in the metal, then there are n ∆t |v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|A such electrons. Since each electron carries a charge –e, the total charge transported across this area A to the right in time ∆t is –ne A|v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "|∆t. E is directed towards the left and hence the total charge transported along E across the area is negative of this.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The amount of charge crossing the area A in time ∆t is by definition", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "[Eq. (3.2)]", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I ∆t, where I is the magnitude of the current. Hence,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000040538", "2000040758", "2000041362", "2000041393", "2000041405", "2000041417", "2000041433", "2000041437", "2000041465", "2000041481", "2000041582", "2000041586", "2000041590", "2000041592", "2000041614", "2000041961", "2000041989", "2000042005", "2000042032", "2000042260", "2000042280", "2000042284", "2000042288", "2000042300", "2000042732", "2000042767", "2000042843", "2000042884", "2000043866", "2000043890", "2000043968", "2000110620", "2000110621", "2000110632", "2000110646", "2000110650", "2000110652", "2000110657", "2000110660", "2000110663", "2000110664", "2000110666", "2000110667", "2000110668", "2000110671", "2000110673", "2000110674", "2000110676", "2000110678", "2000110682", "2000110732", "2000110734", "2000110742", "2000110743", "2000110748", "2000111337"]',NULL),
(22121,57,5,'paragraph',NULL,'Imagine a small area held normal to the direction of flow of charges. Both the positive and the negative charges may flow forward and backward across the area. In a given time interval t, let q + be the net amount (i.e., forward minus backward) of positive charge that flows in the forward direction across the area. Similarly, let q – be the net amount of negative charge flowing across the area in the forward direction. The net amount of charge flowing across the area in the forward direction in the time interval t, then, is q = q + – q – . This is proportional to t for steady current and the quotient.','[{"s": "Imagine a small area held normal to the direction of flow of charges. Both the positive and the negative charges may flow forward and backward across the area. In a given time interval t, let q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the net amount (i.e., forward minus backward) of positive charge that flows in the forward direction across the area. Similarly, let q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the net amount of negative charge flowing across the area in the forward direction. The net amount of charge flowing across the area in the forward direction in the time interval t, then, is q = q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "– q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is proportional to t for steady current and the quotient.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040992", "2000041969", "2000110637"]',NULL),
(22476,57,360,'paragraph',NULL,'Given a circuit, we start by labelling currents in each resistor by a symbol, say I, and a directed arrow to indicate that a current I flows along the resistor in the direction indicated. If ultimately I is determined to be positive, the actual current in the resistor is in the direction of the arrow. If I turns out to be negative, the current actually flows in a direction opposite to the arrow. Similarly, for each source (i.e., cell or some other source of electrical power) the positive and negative electrodes are labelled, as well as, a directed arrow with a symbol for the current flowing through the cell. This will tell us the potential difference, V = V (P) – V (N) = ε – I r [Eq. (3.57) between the positive terminal P and the negative terminal N; I here is the current flowing from N to P through the cell]. If, while labelling the current I through the cell one goes from P to N, then of course','[{"s": "Given a circuit, we start by labelling currents in each resistor by a symbol, say I, and a directed arrow to indicate that a current I flows along the resistor in the direction indicated. If ultimately I is determined to be positive, the actual current in the resistor is in the direction of the arrow. If I turns out to be negative, the current actually flows in a direction opposite to the arrow. Similarly, for each source (i.e., cell or some other source of electrical power) the positive and negative electrodes are labelled, as well as, a directed arrow with a symbol for the current flowing through the cell. This will tell us the potential difference, V = V (P) – V (N) = ε – I r [Eq. (3.57) between the positive terminal P and the negative terminal N; I here is the current flowing from N to P through the cell].", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If, while labelling the current I through the cell one goes from P to N, then of course", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041323", "2000041327", "2000041355", "2000041359", "2000041397", "2000041509", "2000041513", "2000041517", "2000041576", "2000041673", "2000041710", "2000041714", "2000041981", "2000042013", "2000042036", "2000042317", "2000042320", "2000042322", "2000042741", "2000042775", "2000042852", "2000042900", "2000042920", "2000042924", "2000042936", "2000043976", "2000043981", "2000043984", "2000043988", "2000043993", "2000110627", "2000110630", "2000110634", "2000110647", "2000110654", "2000110656", "2000110662", "2000110665", "2000110741", "2000110747"]',NULL),
(22136,57,20,'paragraph',NULL,'A basic law regarding flow of currents was discovered by G.S. Ohm in 1828, long before the physical mechanism responsible for flow of currents was discovered. Imagine a conductor through which a current I is flowing and let V be the potential difference between the ends of the conductor. Then Ohm’s law states that','[{"s": "A basic law regarding flow of currents was discovered by G.S. Ohm in 1828, long before the physical mechanism responsible for flow of currents was discovered. Imagine a conductor through which a current I is flowing and let V be the potential difference between the ends of the conductor.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Then Ohm’s law states that", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041319", "2000041425", "2000041598", "2000041602", "2000041606", "2000041973", "2000042296", "2000042904", "2000110641", "2000110661"]',NULL),
(22138,57,22,'paragraph',NULL,'where the constant of proportionality R is called the resistance of the conductor. The SI units of resistance is ohm, and is denoted by the symbol Ω. The resistance R not only depends on the material of the conductor but also on the dimensions of the conductor. The dependence of R on the dimensions of the conductor can easily be determined as follows.','[{"s": "where the constant of proportionality R is called the resistance of the conductor.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The SI units of resistance is ohm, and is denoted by the symbol Ω. The resistance R not only depends on the material of the conductor but also on the dimensions of the conductor. The dependence of R on the dimensions of the conductor can easily be determined as follows.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041331", "2000041977", "2000110645"]',NULL),
(22150,57,34,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, halving the area of the cross-section of a conductor doubles the resistance. In general, then the resistance R is inversely proportional to the cross-sectional area,','[{"s": "Thus, halving the area of the cross-section of a conductor doubles the resistance. In general, then the resistance R is inversely proportional to the cross-sectional area,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041335", "2000041985", "2000110649"]',NULL),
(22308,57,192,'paragraph',NULL,'Two resistors are said to be in series if only one of their end points is joined (Fig . 3.13). If a third resistor is joined with the series combination of the two (Fig. 3.14), then all three are said to be in series. Clearly, we can extend this definition to series combination of any number of resistors.','[{"s": "Two resistors are said to be in series if only one of their end points is joined (Fig", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3.13). If a third resistor is joined with the series combination of the two (Fig. 3.14), then all three are said to be in series. Clearly, we can extend this definition to series combination of any number of resistors.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041339", "2000041385", "2000041389", "2000041485", "2000041489", "2000041493", "2000041497", "2000041552", "2000041698", "2000041937", "2000041993", "2000042044", "2000042264", "2000042314", "2000042336", "2000042692", "2000042756", "2000042799", "2000042807", "2000042834", "2000042868", "2000042876", "2000042932", "2000043858", "2000043862", "2000110622", "2000110624", "2000110629", "2000110631", "2000110638", "2000110639", "2000110644", "2000110648", "2000110655", "2000110745"]',NULL),
(22153,57,37,'paragraph',NULL,'Current_Electricity-image9.png (3.8)','[{"s": "Current_Electricity-image9.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(3.8)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041351", "2000042009", "2000110653"]',NULL),
(22156,57,40,'paragraph',NULL,'where the constant of proportionality ρ depends on the material of the conductor but not on its dimensions. ρ is called resistivity.','[{"s": "where the constant of proportionality ρ", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "depends on the material of the conductor but not on its dimensions. ρ is called resistivity.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041381", "2000042024", "2000110659"]',NULL),
(22159,57,43,'paragraph',NULL,'Current per unit area (taken normal to the current), I/A, is called current density and is denoted by j. The SI units of the current density are A/m 2 . Further, if E is the magnitude of uniform electric field in the conductor whose length is l, then the potential difference V across its ends is El. Using these, the last equation reads E l = j ρ l','[{"s": "Current per unit area (taken normal to the current), I/A, is called current density and is denoted by j.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The SI units of the current density are A/m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Further, if E is the magnitude of uniform electric field in the conductor whose length is l, then the potential difference V across its ends is El. Using these, the last equation reads E l = j ρ l", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041409", "2000041573", "2000042240", "2000042795", "2000042912", "2000043874", "2000110640", "2000110669", "2000110733", "2000110746"]',NULL),
(22161,57,45,'paragraph',NULL,'The above relation for magnitudes E and j can indeed be cast in a vector form. The current density, (which we have defined as the current through unit area normal to the current) is also directed along E, and is also a vector j(≡ j E/E). Thus, the last equation can be written as,','[{"s": "The above relation for magnitudes E and j can indeed be cast in a vector form. The current density, (which we have defined as the current through unit area normal to the current) is also directed along E, and is also a vector j(≡ j E/E).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, the last equation can be written as,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041457", "2000042244", "2000110670"]',NULL),
(22226,57,110,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.5 The dashed line represents the linear Ohm’s law. The solid line is the voltage V versus currentI for a good conductor.','[{"s": "Figure 3.5 The dashed line represents the linear Ohm’s law.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The solid line is the voltage V versus currentI for a good conductor.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041473", "2000042304", "2000110677"]',NULL),
(22244,57,128,'paragraph',NULL,'The resistors have a set of co-axial coloured rings on them whose significance are listed in Table 3.2. The first two bands from the end indicate the first two significant figures of the resistance in ohms. The third band indicates the decimal multiplier (as listed in Table 3.2). The last band stands for tolerance or possible variation in percentage about the indicated values. Sometimes, this last band is absent and that indicates a tolerance of 20% (Fig. 3.8). For example, if the four colours are orange, blue, yellow and gold, the resistance value is 36 × 10 4 Ω, with a tolerence value of 5%.','[{"s": "The resistors have a set of co-axial coloured rings on them whose significance are listed in Table 3.2.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The first two bands from the end indicate the first two significant figures of the resistance in ohms. The third band indicates the decimal multiplier (as listed in Table 3.2). The last band stands for tolerance or possible variation in percentage about the indicated values. Sometimes, this last band is absent and that indicates a tolerance of 20% (Fig. 3.8). For example, if the four colours are orange, blue, yellow and gold, the resistance value is 36 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Ω, with a tolerence value of 5%.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041477", "2000042308", "2000110679"]',NULL),
(22246,57,130,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 3.8 Colour coded resistors (a) (22 × 10 2 Ω) ± 10%, (b) (47 × 10 Ω) ± 5%.','[{"s": "Figure 3.8 Colour coded resistors (a) (22 × 10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Ω) ± 10%, (b) (47 × 10 Ω) ± 5%.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041501", "2000042311", "2000110680"]',NULL),
(22252,57,136,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (3.26) thus, can be used approximately over a limited range of T around any reference temperature T 0 , where the graph can be approximated as a straight line.','[{"s": "Equation (3.26) thus, can be used approximately over a limited range of T around any reference temperature T", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "0", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", where the graph can be approximated as a straight line.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041525", "2000042326", "2000110736"]',NULL),
(22264,57,148,'paragraph',NULL,'In a metal, n is not dependent on temperature to any appreciable extent and thus the decrease in the value of τ with rise in temperature causes ρ to increase as we have observed.','[{"s": "In a metal, n is not dependent on temperature to any appreciable extent and thus the decrease in the value of τ with rise in temperature causes ρ to increase as we have observed.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041529", "2000042328", "2000110737"]',NULL),
(22742,57,626,'paragraph',NULL,'3.23 Figure shows a potentiometer circuit for comparison of two resistances. The balance point with a standard resistor R=10.0 Ω is found to be 58.3 cm, while that with the unknown resistance X is 68.5 cm. Determine the value of X. What might you do if you failed to find a balance point with the given cell of emf ε?','[{"s": "3.23 Figure shows a potentiometer circuit for comparison of two resistances.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The balance point with a standard resistor R=10.0 Ω is found to be 58.3 cm, while that with the unknown resistance X is 68.5 cm. Determine the value of X. What might you do if you failed to find a balance point with the given cell of emf ε?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041965", "2000110636"]',NULL),
(22266,57,150,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 3.3 An electric toaster uses nichrome for its heating element. When a negligibly small current passes through it, its resistance at room temperature (27.0 °C) is found to be 75.3 Ω . When the toaster is connected to a 230 V supply, the current settles, after a few seconds, to a steady value of 2.68 A. What is the steady temperature of the nichrome element? The temperature coefficient of resistance of nichrome averaged over the temperature range involved, is 1.70 × 10 –4 °C –1 .','[{"s": "Example 3.3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "An electric toaster uses nichrome for its heating element.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When a negligibly small current passes through it, its resistance at room temperature (27.0 °C) is found to be 75.3 Ω", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When the toaster is connected to a 230 V supply, the current settles, after a few seconds, to a steady value of 2.68 A. What is the steady temperature of the nichrome element? The temperature coefficient of resistance of nichrome averaged over the temperature range involved, is 1.70 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "°C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041532", "2000042330", "2000110738"]',NULL),
(22288,57,172,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, in case charges were moving freely through the conductor under the action of electric field, their kinetic energy would increase as they move. We have, however, seen earlier that on the average, charge carriers do not move with acceleration but with a steady drift velocity. This is because of the collisions with ions and atoms during transit. During collisions, the energy gained by the charges thus is shared with the atoms. The atoms vibrate more vigorously, i.e., the conductor heats up. Thus, in an actual conductor, an amount of energy dissipated as heat in the conductor during the time interval ∆t is,','[{"s": "Thus, in case charges were moving freely through the conductor under the action of electric field, their kinetic energy would increase as they move. We have, however, seen earlier that on the average, charge carriers do not move with acceleration but with a steady drift velocity. This is because of the collisions with ions and atoms during transit. During collisions, the energy gained by the charges thus is shared with the atoms. The atoms vibrate more vigorously, i.e., the conductor heats up.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, in an actual conductor, an amount of energy dissipated as heat in the conductor during the time interval ∆t is,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041537", "2000042332", "2000110739"]',NULL),
(22293,57,177,'paragraph',NULL,'as the power loss (“ohmic loss”) in a conductor of resistance R carrying a current I. It is this power which heats up, for example, the coil of an electric bulb to incandescence, radiating out heat and light.','[{"s": "as the power loss (“ohmic loss”) in a conductor of resistance R carrying a current I.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It is this power which heats up, for example, the coil of an electric bulb to incandescence, radiating out heat and light.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041540", "2000042334", "2000110740"]',NULL),
