(23439,59,261,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.10 (a) Obtain the expression for the magnetic energy stored in a solenoid in terms of magnetic field B, area A and length l of the solenoid. (b) How does this magnetic energy compare with the electrostatic energy stored in a capacitor?','[{"s": "Example 6.10 (a) Obtain the expression for the magnetic energy stored in a solenoid in terms of magnetic field B, area A and length l of the solenoid.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(b) How does this magnetic energy compare with the electrostatic energy stored in a capacitor?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000043395", "2000112270", "2000112299"]',NULL),
(23455,59,277,'paragraph',NULL,'When a current I 2 is set up through S 2 , it in turn sets up a magnetic flux through S 1 . Let us denote it by Φ 1 . The corresponding flux linkage with solenoid S 1 is','[{"s": "When a current I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is set up through S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", it in turn sets up a magnetic flux through S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Let us denote it by Φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The corresponding flux linkage with solenoid S", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000043399", "2000112272", "2000112301"]',NULL),
(23478,59,300,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.9 Two concentric circular coils, one of small radius r 1 and the other of large radius r 2 , such that r 1 << r 2 , are placed co-axially with centres coinciding. Obtain the mutual inductance of the arrangement.','[{"s": "Example 6.9 Two concentric circular coils, one of small radius r", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and the other of large radius r", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", such that r", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "<< r", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", are placed co-axially with centres coinciding.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Obtain the mutual inductance of the arrangement.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000043419", "2000112273", "2000112304"]',NULL),
(23488,59,310,'paragraph',NULL,'Now, let us recollect Experiment 6.3 in Section 6.2. In that experiment, emf is induced in coil C 1 wherever there was any change in current through coil C 2 . Let Φ 1 be the flux through coil C 1 (say of N 1 turns) when current in coil C 2 is I 2 .','[{"s": "Now, let us recollect Experiment 6.3 in Section 6.2.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In that experiment, emf is induced in coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "wherever there was any change in current through coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Let Φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the flux through coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(say of N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "turns) when current in coil C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000043499", "2000112274", "2000112305"]',NULL),
(23573,59,395,'paragraph',NULL,'Q 4. A rectangular wire loop of sides 8 cm and 2 cm with a small cut is moving out of a region of the uniform magnetic field of magnitude 0.3 T directed normal to the loop. What is the emf developed across the cut if the velocity of the loop is 1 cm s–1 in a direction normal to the (a) longer side, (b) shorter side of the loop? For how long does the induced voltage last in each case?','[{"s": "Q 4.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A rectangular wire loop of sides 8 cm and 2 cm with a small cut is moving out of a region of the uniform magnetic field of magnitude 0.3 T directed normal to the loop.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What is the emf developed across the cut if the velocity of the loop is 1 cm s–1 in a direction normal to the (a) longer side, (b) shorter side of the loop? For how long does the induced voltage last in each case?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000043506", "2000112276", "2000112361"]',NULL),
(23799,60,147,'paragraph',NULL,'Microwaves (short-wavelength radio waves), with frequencies in the gigahertz (GHz) range, are produced by special vacuum tubes (called klystrons, magnetrons and Gunn diodes). Due to their short wavelengths, they are suitable for the radar systems used in aircraft navigation. Radar also provides the basis for the speed guns used to time fast balls, tennis- serves, and automobiles. Microwave ovens are an interesting domestic application of these waves. In such ovens, the frequency of the microwaves is selected to match the resonant frequency of water molecules so that energy from the waves is transferred efficiently to the kinetic energy of the molecules. This raises the temperature of any food containing water.','[{"s": "Microwaves (short-wavelength radio waves), with frequencies in the gigahertz (GHz) range, are produced by special vacuum tubes (called klystrons, magnetrons and Gunn diodes). Due to their short wavelengths, they are suitable for the radar systems used in aircraft navigation. Radar also provides the basis for the speed guns used to time fast balls, tennis- serves, and automobiles.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Microwave ovens are an interesting domestic application of these waves.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In such ovens, the frequency of the microwaves is selected to match the resonant frequency of water molecules so that energy from the waves is transferred efficiently to the kinetic energy of the molecules.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This raises the temperature of any food containing water.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000044085", "2000044095", "2000044213", "2000044762", "2000044832"]',NULL),
(23712,60,60,'paragraph',NULL,'How are electromagnetic waves produced? Neither stationary charges nor charges in uniform motion (steady currents) can be sources of electromagnetic waves. The former produces only electrostatic fields, while the latter produces magnetic fields that, however, do not vary with time. It is an important result of Maxwell’s theory that accelerated charges radiate electromagnetic waves. The proof of this basic result is beyond the scope of this book, but we can accept it on the basis of rough, qualitative reasoning. Consider a charge oscillating with some frequency. (An oscillating charge is an example of accelerating charge.) This produces an oscillating electric field in space, which produces an oscillating magnetic field, which in turn, is a source of oscillating electric field, and so on. The oscillating electric and magnetic fields thus regenerate each other, so to speak, as the wave propagates through the space. The frequency of the electromagnetic wave naturally equals the frequency of oscillation of the charge. The energy associated with the propagating wave comes at the expense of the energy of the source – the accelerated charge.','[{"s": "How are electromagnetic waves produced? Neither stationary charges nor charges in uniform motion (steady currents) can be sources of electromagnetic waves. The former produces only electrostatic fields, while the latter produces magnetic fields that, however, do not vary with time.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is an important result of Maxwell’s theory that accelerated charges radiate electromagnetic waves.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The proof of this basic result is beyond the scope of this book, but we can accept it on the basis of rough, qualitative reasoning. Consider a charge oscillating with some frequency. (An oscillating charge is an example of accelerating charge.) This produces an oscillating electric field in space, which produces an oscillating magnetic field, which in turn, is a source of oscillating electric field, and so on. The oscillating electric and magnetic fields thus regenerate each other, so to speak, as the wave propagates through the space. The frequency of the electromagnetic wave naturally equals the frequency of oscillation of the charge. The energy associated with the propagating wave comes at the expense of the energy of the source – the accelerated charge.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000044089", "2000044092", "2000044099", "2000044102", "2000044106", "2000044109", "2000044112", "2000044115", "2000044118", "2000044121", "2000044127", "2000044130", "2000044205", "2000044217", "2000044228", "2000044232", "2000044241", "2000044247", "2000044256", "2000044259", "2000044262", "2000044268", "2000044271", "2000044274", "2000044277", "2000044598", "2000044602", "2000044610", "2000044743", "2000044747", "2000044751", "2000044755", "2000044758", "2000044766", "2000044770", "2000044778", "2000044782", "2000044786", "2000044790", "2000044794", "2000044798", "2000044802", "2000044805", "2000044808", "2000044816", "2000044820", "2000044823", "2000044843", "2000044851", "2000044983", "2000044991", "2000044995", "2000044998", "2000045002", "2000045006", "2000045015", "2000045018", "2000045022", "2000045026", "2000045030", "2000045033", "2000045037"]',NULL),
(23792,60,140,'paragraph',NULL,'At the time Maxwell predicted the existence of electromagnetic waves, the only familiar electromagnetic waves were the visible light waves. The existence of ultraviolet and infrared waves was barely established. By the end of the nineteenth century, X-rays and gamma rays had also been discovered. We now know that, electromagnetic waves include visible light waves, X-rays, gamma rays, radio waves, microwaves, ultraviolet and infrared waves. The classification of em waves according to frequency is the electromagnetic spectrum (Fig. 2 8.5). There is no sharp division between one kind of wave and the next. The classification is based roughly on how the waves are produced and/or detected.','[{"s": "At the time Maxwell predicted the existence of electromagnetic waves, the only familiar electromagnetic waves were the visible light waves. The existence of ultraviolet and infrared waves was barely established. By the end of the nineteenth century, X-rays and gamma rays had also been discovered.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We now know that, electromagnetic waves include visible light waves, X-rays, gamma rays, radio waves, microwaves, ultraviolet and infrared waves.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The classification of em waves according to frequency is the electromagnetic spectrum (Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "8.5). There is no sharp division between one kind of wave and the next.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The classification is based roughly on how the waves are produced and/or detected.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000044124", "2000044209", "2000044221", "2000044253", "2000044265", "2000044774", "2000044813", "2000044838", "2000045041", "2000045045"]',NULL),
(23719,60,67,'paragraph',NULL,'It can be shown from Maxwell’s equations that electric and magnetic fields in an electromagnetic wave are perpendicular to each other, and to the direction of propagation. 2 It appears reasonable, say from our discussion of the displacement current. Consider Fig. 8.2. The electric field inside the plates of the capacitor is directed perpendicular to the plates. The magnetic field this gives rise to via the displacement current is along the perimeter of a circle parallel to the capacitor plates. So B and E are perpendicular in this case. This is a general feature.','[{"s": "It can be shown from Maxwell’s equations that electric and magnetic fields in an electromagnetic wave are perpendicular to each other, and to the direction of propagation.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It appears reasonable, say from our discussion of the displacement current. Consider Fig. 8.2. The electric field inside the plates of the capacitor is directed perpendicular to the plates. The magnetic field this gives rise to via the displacement current is along the perimeter of a circle parallel to the capacitor plates.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "So B and E are perpendicular in this case.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This is a general feature.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000044594", "2000044847", "2000044987", "2000045010"]',NULL),
(24391,61,403,'paragraph',NULL,'Following the same steps as for the case of two capacitors, we get the general formula for effective capacitance of a series combination of n capacitors:','[{"s": "Following the same steps as for the case of two capacitors, we get the general formula for effective capacitance of a series combination of n capacitors:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040443", "2000040455", "2000040459", "2000040462", "2000040471", "2000040474", "2000040486", "2000040492", "2000040528", "2000040532", "2000040536", "2000040540", "2000040544", "2000040548", "2000040552", "2000040556", "2000040560", "2000040563", "2000040567", "2000040571", "2000040668", "2000040672", "2000040676", "2000040684", "2000040688", "2000040692", "2000040696", "2000040700", "2000040704", "2000040708", "2000040712", "2000040716", "2000040720", "2000040724", "2000040728", "2000040986", "2000040994", "2000040998", "2000041006", "2000041010", "2000041013", "2000041018", "2000041039", "2000041046", "2000041050", "2000041086", "2000041090", "2000041098", "2000041102", "2000041106", "2000041114", "2000041118", "2000041122", "2000041130", "2000041134", "2000041138", "2000041142", "2000041384", "2000041436", "2000041440", "2000041444", "2000041484", "2000041488", "2000041500", "2000042275", "2000042279", "2000110474", "2000110487", "2000110488", "2000110490", "2000110497", "2000110499", "2000110500", "2000110501", "2000110502", "2000110503", "2000110504", "2000110505", "2000110506", "2000110509", "2000110510", "2000110511", "2000110512", "2000110514", "2000110515", "2000110522", "2000110601", "2000110602", "2000110604", "2000110606", "2000110607"]',NULL),
(24291,61,303,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus in either case, whether polar or non-polar, a dielectric develops a net dipole moment in the presence of an external field. The dipole moment per unit volume is called polarisation and is denoted by P. For linear isotropic dielectrics,','[{"s": "Thus in either case, whether polar or non-polar, a dielectric develops a net dipole moment in the presence of an external field.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The dipole moment per unit volume is called polarisation and is denoted by P. For linear isotropic dielectrics,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000040480", "2000041021", "2000041480"]',NULL),
(24154,61,166,'paragraph',NULL,'Since electrostatic force is conservative, this work gets stored in the form of potential energy of the system. Thus, the potential energy of a system of two charges q 1 and q 2 is U = 1/4πε 0 q 1 q 2 /r 12 (2.22)','[{"s": "Since electrostatic force is conservative, this work gets stored in the form of potential energy of the system.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus, the potential energy of a system of two charges q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "U =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/4πε", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.22)", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000040447", "2000040483", "2000040495", "2000040521", "2000040607", "2000040632", "2000040656", "2000040864", "2000040872", "2000040978", "2000041062", "2000041070", "2000041094", "2000041368", "2000041376", "2000041412", "2000041424", "2000041448", "2000041464", "2000041472", "2000041496", "2000042255", "2000042259", "2000110483", "2000110491", "2000110493", "2000110507", "2000110513", "2000110516", "2000110519", "2000110520", "2000110521", "2000110610"]',NULL),
(24016,61,28,'paragraph',NULL,'Work done by external force in bringing a unit positive charge from point R to P','[{"s": "Work done by external force in bringing a unit positive charge from point R to P", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040477", "2000110484"]',NULL),
(24064,61,76,'paragraph',NULL,'Using Eqs. (2.9) and (2.13) and p = 2qa, we get','[{"s": "Using Eqs.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(2.9) and (2.13) and p = 2qa, we get", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000040524", "2000110494"]',NULL),
(24070,61,82,'paragraph',NULL,'Equation (2.15) is, as indicated, approximately true only for distances large compared to the size of the dipole, so that higher order terms in a/r are negligible. For a point dipole p at the origin, Eq. (2.15) is, however, exact.','[{"s": "Equation (2.15) is, as indicated, approximately true only for distances large compared to the size of the dipole, so that higher order terms in a/r are negligible.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For a point dipole p at the origin, Eq. (2.15) is, however, exact.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000040576", "2000110495"]',NULL),
(24089,61,101,'paragraph',NULL,'We have seen in Chapter 1 that for a uniformly charged spherical shell, the electric field outside the shell is as if the entire charge is concentrated at the centre. Thus, the potential outside the shell is given by','[{"s": "We have seen in Chapter 1 that for a uniformly charged spherical shell, the electric field outside the shell is as if the entire charge is concentrated at the centre.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, the potential outside the shell is given by", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040580", "2000110508"]',NULL),
(24091,61,103,'paragraph',NULL,'where q is the total charge on the shell and R its radius. The electric field inside the shell is zero. This implies (Section 2.6) that potential is constant inside the shell (as no work is done in moving a charge inside the shell), and, therefore, equals its value at the surface, which is','[{"s": "where q is the total charge on the shell and R its radius. The electric field inside the shell is zero.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This implies (Section 2.6) that potential is constant inside the shell (as no work is done in moving a charge inside the shell), and, therefore, equals its value at the surface, which is", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040583", "2000111336"]',NULL),
(24123,61,135,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider two closely spaced equipotential surfaces A and B (Fig. 2.12) with potential values V and V + δ V, where δ V is the change in V in the direction of the electric field E. Let P be a point on the surface B. δ l is the perpendicular distance of the surface A from P. Imagine that a unit positive charge is moved along this perpendicular from the surface B to surface A against the electric field. The work done in this process is |E|δ l.','[{"s": "Consider two closely spaced equipotential surfaces A and B (Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2.12) with potential values V and V + δ V, where δ V is the change in V in the direction of the electric field E. Let P be a point on the surface B. δ l is the perpendicular distance of the surface A from P. Imagine that a unit positive charge is moved along this perpendicular from the surface B to surface A against the electric field.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The work done in this process is |E|δ l.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000040588", "2000110600"]',NULL),
(24132,61,144,'paragraph',NULL,'(i) Electric field is in the direction in which the potential decreases steepest.','[{"s": "(i) Electric field is in the direction in which the potential decreases steepest.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040736", "2000110608"]',NULL),
(24970,62,220,'paragraph',NULL,'Using the value of g and R E , numerically (V i ) min ≈11.2 km/s. This is called the escape speed, sometimes loosely called the escape velocity.','[{"s": "Using the value of g and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", numerically (V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")", "t": "text"}, {"s": "min", "t": "text"}, {"s": "≈11.2 km/s.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is called the escape speed, sometimes loosely called the escape velocity.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034175", "2000034279", "2000034351", "2000034375", "2000035395", "2000035414", "2000035426", "2000035753", "2000035760", "2000035764", "2000035849", "2000035854", "2000036314", "2000036325", "2000036327", "2000109688", "2000109689", "2000109691", "2000109693", "2000109705", "2000109708", "2000109712", "2000109783"]',NULL),
(24138,61,150,'paragraph',NULL,'Now the electric field lines for a single charge q are radial lines starting from or ending at the charge, depending on whether q is positive or negative. Clearly, the electric field at every point is normal to the equipotential surface passing through that point. This is true in general: for any charge configuration, equipotential surface through a point is normal to the electric field at that point. The proof of this statement is simple.','[{"s": "Now the electric field lines for a single charge q are radial lines starting from or ending at the charge, depending on whether q is positive or negative. Clearly, the electric field at every point is normal to the equipotential surface passing through that point.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is true in general: for any charge configuration, equipotential surface through a point is normal to the electric field at that point.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The proof of this statement is simple.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000040740", "2000111332"]',NULL),
(24143,61,155,'paragraph',NULL,'If the field were not normal to the equipotential surface, it would have non-zero component along the surface. To move a unit test charge against the direction of the component of the field, work would have to be done. But this is in contradiction to the definition of an equipotential surface: there is no potential difference between any two points on the surface and no work is required to move a test charge on the surface. The electric field must, therefore, be normal to the equipotential surface at every point. Equipotential surfaces offer an alternative visual picture in addition to the picture of electric field lines around a charge configuration.','[{"s": "If the field were not normal to the equipotential surface, it would have non-zero component along the surface. To move a unit test charge against the direction of the component of the field, work would have to be done.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "But this is in contradiction to the definition of an equipotential surface: there is no potential difference between any two points on the surface and no work is required to move a test charge on the surface.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The electric field must, therefore, be normal to the equipotential surface at every point. Equipotential surfaces offer an alternative visual picture in addition to the picture of electric field lines around a charge configuration.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000040744"]',NULL),
(24225,61,237,'paragraph',NULL,'τ = p×E (2.30) which will tend to rotate it (unless p is parallel or antiparallel to E). Suppose an external torque τext is applied in such a manner that it just neutralises this torque and rotates it in the plane of paper from angle θ 0 to angle θ 1 at an infinitesimal angular speed and without angular acceleration. The amount of work done by the external torque will be given by','[{"s": "τ = p×E (2.30) which will tend to rotate it (unless p is parallel or antiparallel to E). Suppose an external torque τext is applied in such a manner that it just neutralises this torque and rotates it in the plane of paper from angle θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to angle θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at an infinitesimal angular speed and without angular acceleration.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The amount of work done by the external torque will be given by", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040990"]',NULL),
(24269,61,281,'paragraph',NULL,'The proofs of the results noted in Fig. 2.18 are omitted here, but we note their important implication. Whatever be the charge and field configuration outside, any cavity in a conductor remains shielded from outside electric influence: the field inside the cavity is always zero . This is known as electrostatic shielding. The effect can be made use of in protecting sensitive instruments from outside electrical influence. Figure 2.19 gives a summary of the important electrostatic properties of a conductor.','[{"s": "The proofs of the results noted in Fig. 2.18 are omitted here, but we note their important implication.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Whatever be the charge and field configuration outside, any cavity in a conductor remains shielded from outside electric influence: the field inside the cavity is always zero", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is known as electrostatic shielding. The effect can be made use of in protecting sensitive instruments from outside electrical influence. Figure 2.19 gives a summary of the important electrostatic properties of a conductor.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041001"]',NULL),
(24309,61,321,'paragraph',NULL,'12.25). A parallel plate capacitor consists of two large plane parallel conducting plates separated by a small distance (Fig. We first take the intervening medium between the plates to be vacuum. The effect of a dielectric medium between the plates is discussed in the next section. Let A be the area of each plate and d the separation between them. The two plates have charges Q and –Q. Since d is much smaller than the linear dimension of the plates (d 2 << A), we can use the result on electric field by an infinite plane sheet of uniform surface charge density (Section 1.15). Plate 1 has surface charge density σ = Q/A and plate 2 has a surface charge density –σ. Using Eq. (1.33), the electric field in different regions is:','[{"s": "12.25).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A parallel plate capacitor consists of two large plane parallel conducting plates separated by a small distance (Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "We first take the intervening medium between the plates to be vacuum. The effect of a dielectric medium between the plates is discussed in the next section. Let A be the area of each plate and d the separation between them. The two plates have charges Q and –Q. Since d is much smaller than the linear dimension of the plates (d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "<< A), we can use the result on electric field by an infinite plane sheet of uniform surface charge density (Section 1.15). Plate 1 has surface charge density σ = Q/A and plate 2 has a surface charge density –σ. Using Eq. (1.33), the electric field in different regions is:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041026"]',NULL),
(24316,61,328,'paragraph',NULL,'In the inner region between the plates 1 and 2, the electric fields due to the two charged plates add up, giving','[{"s": "In the inner region between the plates 1 and 2, the electric fields due to the two charged plates add up, giving", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041030"]',NULL),
(24321,61,333,'paragraph',NULL,'The capacitance C of the parallel plate capacitor is then','[{"s": "The capacitance C of the parallel plate capacitor is then", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041034"]',NULL),
(24343,61,355,'paragraph',NULL,'The capacitance C, with dielectric between the plates, is then','[{"s": "The capacitance C, with dielectric between the plates, is then", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041042"]',NULL),
(24349,61,361,'paragraph',NULL,'is called the dielectric constant of the substance. As remarked before, from Eq. (2.49), it is clear that K is greater than 1. From Eqs. (2.46) and (2. 51)','[{"s": "is called the dielectric constant of the substance. As remarked before, from Eq. (2.49), it is clear that K is greater than 1. From Eqs.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(2.46) and (2.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "51)", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041082"]',NULL),
(24378,61,390,'paragraph',NULL,'The left plate of C 1 and the right plate of C 2 are connected to two terminals of a battery and have charges Q and –Q , respectively. It then follows that the right plate of C 1 has charge –Q and the left plate of C 2 has charge Q. If this was not so, the net charge on each capacitor would not be zero. This would result in an electric field in the conductor connecting C 1 and C 2 . Charge would flow until the net charge on both C 1 and C 2 is zero and there is no electric field in the conductor connecting C 1 and C 2 . Thus, in the series combination, charges on the two plates (±Q) are the same on each capacitor . The total potential drop V across the combination is the sum of the potential drops V 1 and V 2 across C 1 and C 2 , respectively.','[{"s": "The left plate of C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the right plate of C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "are connected to two terminals of a battery and have charges Q and –Q , respectively. It then follows that the right plate of C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has charge –Q and the left plate of C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "has charge Q. If this was not so, the net charge on each capacitor would not be zero. This would result in an electric field in the conductor connecting C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Charge would flow until the net charge on both C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is zero and there is no electric field in the conductor connecting C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, in the series combination, charges on the two plates (±Q) are the same on each capacitor", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The total potential drop V across the combination is the sum of the potential drops V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "across C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", respectively.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041110"]',NULL),
(24437,61,449,'paragraph',NULL,'This is not surprising since integration is nothing but summation of a large number of small terms. We can write the final result, Eq.','[{"s": "This is not surprising since integration is nothing but summation of a large number of small terms.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We can write the final result, Eq.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041400"]',NULL),
(24440,61,452,'paragraph',NULL,'Since electrostatic force is conservative, this work is stored in the form of potential energy of the system. For the same reason, the final result for potential energy [Eq. (2.73)] is independent of the manner in which the charge configuration of the capacitor is built up. When the capacitor discharges, this stored-up energy is released. It is possible to view the potential energy of the capacitor as ‘stored’ in the electric field between the plates. To see this, consider for simplicity, a parallel plate capacitor [of area A (of each plate) and separation d between the plates].','[{"s": "Since electrostatic force is conservative, this work is stored in the form of potential energy of the system.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For the same reason, the final result for potential energy [Eq. (2.73)] is independent of the manner in which the charge configuration of the capacitor is built up.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When the capacitor discharges, this stored-up energy is released.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It is possible to view the potential energy of the capacitor as ‘stored’ in the electric field between the plates. To see this, consider for simplicity, a parallel plate capacitor [of area A (of each plate) and separation d between the plates].", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041432"]',NULL),
(24448,61,460,'paragraph',NULL,'Note that Ad is the volume of the region between the plates (where electric field alone exists). If we define energy density as energy stored per unit volume of space, Eq (2.76) shows that Energy density of electric field, u =(1/2)ε 0 E 2 (2.77)','[{"s": "Note that Ad is the volume of the region between the plates (where electric field alone exists).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If we define energy density as energy stored per unit volume of space, Eq (2.76) shows that Energy density of electric field, u =(1/2)ε", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "0", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "E", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(2.77)", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000041492"]',NULL),
(24450,61,462,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.10 (a) A 900 pF capacitor is charged by 100 V battery [Fig. 2.31(a)]. How much electrostatic energy is stored by the capacitor? (b) The capacitor is disconnected from the battery and connected to another 900 pF capacitor [Fig. 2.31(b)]. What is the electrostatic energy stored by the system?','[{"s": "Example 2.10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(a) A 900 pF capacitor is charged by 100 V battery [Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2.31(a)]. How much electrostatic energy is stored by the capacitor? (b) The capacitor is disconnected from the battery and connected to another 900 pF capacitor [Fig. 2.31(b)]. What is the electrostatic energy stored by the system?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000042271"]',NULL),
(24501,61,513,'paragraph',NULL,'11. For capacitors in the series combination, the total capacitance C is given by','[{"s": "11.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For capacitors in the series combination, the total capacitance C is given by", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000110480"]',NULL),
(24533,61,545,'paragraph',NULL,'2.5 A parallel plate capacitor with air between the plates has a capacitance of 8 pF (1pF = 10–12 F). What will be the capacitance if the distance between the plates is reduced by half, and the space between them is filled with a substance of dielectric constant 6?','[{"s": "2.5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A parallel plate capacitor with air between the plates has a capacitance of 8 pF (1pF = 10–12 F).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What will be the capacitance if the distance between the plates is reduced by half, and the space between them is filled with a substance of dielectric constant 6?", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000110611"]',NULL),
(24592,61,604,'paragraph',NULL,'Two charged conducting spheres of radii a and b are connected to each other by a wire. What is the ratio of electric fields at the surfaces of the two spheres? use the result obtained to explain why charge density on the sharp and pointed ends of a conductor is higher than on its flatter portions.','[{"s": "Two charged conducting spheres of radii a and b are connected to each other by a wire.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What is the ratio of electric fields at the surfaces of the two spheres? use the result obtained to explain why charge density on the sharp and pointed ends of a conductor is higher than on its flatter portions.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000110481"]',NULL),
(25130,62,380,'paragraph',NULL,'3. In Kepler’s third law (see Eq. (8.1) and T 2 = K S R 3 . The constant K S is the same for all planets in circular orbits. This applies to satellites orbiting the Earth [(Eq. (8.38)].','[{"s": "3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In Kepler’s third law (see Eq.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(8.1) and T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The constant K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the same for all planets in circular orbits. This applies to satellites orbiting the Earth [(Eq. (8.38)].", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034155", "2000034164", "2000034168", "2000034263", "2000034275", "2000034299", "2000035284", "2000035288", "2000035296", "2000035357", "2000035399", "2000035402", "2000035422", "2000035748", "2000035756", "2000035812", "2000035820", "2000035828", "2000035832", "2000035862", "2000035889", "2000036293", "2000036303", "2000036307", "2000036310", "2000036318", "2000036321", "2000109679", "2000109682", "2000109690", "2000109700", "2000109710", "2000109713"]',NULL),
(24935,62,185,'paragraph',NULL,'We have calculated the potential energy at a point of a particle due to gravitational forces on it due to the earth and it is proportional to the mass of the particle. The gravitational potential due to the gravitational force of the earth is defined as the potential energy of a particle of unit mass at that point. From the earlier discussion, we learn that the gravitational potential energy associated with two particles of masses m 1 and m 2 separated by distance by a distance r is given by .','[{"s": "We have calculated the potential energy at a point of a particle due to gravitational forces on it due to the earth and it is proportional to the mass of the particle.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The gravitational potential due to the gravitational force of the earth is defined as the potential energy of a particle of unit mass at that point. From the earlier discussion, we learn that the gravitational potential energy associated with two particles of masses m", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and m", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "separated by distance by a distance r is given by", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034172", "2000034191", "2000034195", "2000034295", "2000034303", "2000034339", "2000034347", "2000034355", "2000034387", "2000034395", "2000035237", "2000035252", "2000035264", "2000035353", "2000035381", "2000035386", "2000035390", "2000035744", "2000035776", "2000035788", "2000035800", "2000036297", "2000109684", "2000109692", "2000109699", "2000109703", "2000109706", "2000109709", "2000109714", "2000109779", "2000109780"]',NULL),
(24778,62,28,'paragraph',NULL,'where v is the velocity, L is the angular momentum equal to ( r × p ). For a central force, which is directed along r , L is a constant as the planet goes around. Hence, ∆A /∆t is a constant according to the last equation. This is the law of areas. Gravitation is a central force and hence the law of areas follows.','[{"s": "where", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "v", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is the velocity,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "L", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is the angular momentum equal to (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "r", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "×", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "p", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ").", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For a central force, which is directed along", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a constant as the planet goes around. Hence, ∆A /∆t is a constant according to the last equation. This is the law of areas. Gravitation is a central force and hence the law of areas follows.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034216", "2000034379", "2000035245", "2000035260", "2000035272", "2000035410", "2000035418", "2000035772", "2000035784", "2000035792", "2000036260", "2000036267", "2000109675", "2000109701", "2000109776", "2000109781"]',NULL),
(24792,62,42,'paragraph',NULL,'where V is the speed of the moon related to the time period T by the relation Gravitation-image109.png . The time period T is about 27.3 days and R m was already known then to be about 3.84 × 10­ 8 m. If we substitute these numbers in Eq. (8.3), we get a value of a m much smaller than the value of acceleration due to gravity g on the surface of the earth, arising also due to earth’s gravitational attraction.','[{"s": "where V is the speed of the moon related to the time period T by the relation", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image109.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ". The time period T is about 27.3 days and R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "was already known then to be about 3.84 × 10­", "t": "text"}, {"s": "8", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m. If we substitute these numbers in Eq. (8.3), we get a value of a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "much smaller than the value of acceleration due to gravity g on the surface of the earth, arising also due to earth’s gravitational attraction.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034219", "2000034223", "2000034227", "2000034231", "2000034240", "2000034244", "2000034247", "2000034323", "2000034359", "2000034363", "2000034367", "2000034383", "2000034391", "2000035276", "2000035280", "2000035304", "2000035369", "2000035373", "2000035376", "2000035724", "2000035728", "2000035732", "2000035736", "2000035768", "2000035780", "2000035804", "2000035808", "2000035816", "2000035824", "2000035836", "2000035841", "2000035845", "2000035858", "2000035870", "2000035874", "2000035882", "2000035886", "2000036271", "2000036275", "2000036279", "2000036283", "2000109681", "2000109683", "2000109686", "2000109694", "2000109696", "2000109704", "2000109777", "2000109778", "2000109782", "2000109784", "2000109785"]',NULL),
(24763,62,13,'paragraph',NULL,'2. Law of areas : The line that joins any planet to the sun sweeps equal areas in equal intervals of time (Fig. 8.2). This law comes from the observations that planets appear to move slower when they are farther from the sun than when they are nearer.','[{"s": "2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Law of areas :", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The line that joins any planet to the sun sweeps equal areas in equal intervals of time (Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "8.2). This law comes from the observations that planets appear to move slower when they are farther from the sun than when they are nearer.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000035241"]',NULL),
(24766,62,16,'paragraph',NULL,'3. Law of periods : The square of the time period of revolution of a planet is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis of the ellipse traced out by the planet.','[{"s": "3.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Law of periods :", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The square of the time period of revolution of a planet is proportional to the cube of the semi-major axis of the ellipse traced out by the planet.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000035256"]',NULL),
(24782,62,32,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 8.1 Let the speed of the planet at the perihelion P in Fig. 8.1(a) be v P and the Sun-planet distance SP be r P . Relate {r P , v P } to the corresponding quantities at the aphelion {r A, v A }. Will the planet take equal times to traverse BAC and CPB ?','[{"s": "Example 8.1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Let the speed of the planet at the perihelion P in Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "8.1(a) be v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the Sun-planet distance SP be r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Relate {r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "} to the corresponding quantities at the aphelion {r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "}. Will the planet take equal times to traverse BAC and CPB ?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000035878"]',NULL),
(24810,62,60,'paragraph',NULL,'Every body in the universe attracts every other body with a force which is directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.','[{"s": "Every body in the universe attracts every other body with a force which is directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000109685"]',NULL),
(27649,68,356,'paragraph',NULL,'After the object has been projected, the acceleration acting on it is that due to gravity which is directed vertically downward:','[{"s": "After the object has been projected, the acceleration acting on it is that due to gravity which is directed vertically downward:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000033520", "2000107299"]',NULL),
(24817,62,67,'paragraph',NULL,'where G is the universal gravitational constant, Gravitation-image121.png is the unit vector from m 1 to m 2 and r = r 2 – r 1 as shown in Fig. 8.3.','[{"s": "where G is the universal gravitational constant,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image121.png", "t": "img", "alt": "Screen Shot 2019-06-05 at 5.46.38 PM"}, {"s": "is the unit vector from m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as shown in Fig. 8.3.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000109697"]',NULL),
(24854,62,104,'paragraph',NULL,'The bar AB has two small lead spheres attached at its ends. The bar is suspended from a rigid support by a fine wire. Two large lead spheres are brought close to the small ones but on opposite sides as shown. The big spheres attract the nearby small ones by equal and opposite force as shown. There is no net force on the bar but only a torque which is clearly equal to F times the length of the bar,where F is the force of attraction between a big sphere and its neighbouring small sphere. Due to this torque, the suspended wire gets twisted till such time as the restoring torque of the wire equals the gravitational torque . If θ is the angle of twist of the suspended wire, the restoring torque is proportional to θ, equal to τθ. Where τ is the restoring couple per unit angle of twist. τ can be measured independently e.g. by applying a known torque and measuring the angle of twist. The gravitational force between the spherical balls is the same as if their masses are concentrated at their centres. Thus if d is the separation between the centres of the big and its neighbouring small ball, M and m their masses, the gravitational force between the big sphere and its neighouring small ball is.','[{"s": "The bar AB has two small lead spheres attached at its ends. The bar is suspended from a rigid support by a fine wire. Two large lead spheres are brought close to the small ones but on opposite sides as shown. The big spheres attract the nearby small ones by equal and opposite force as shown. There is no net force on the bar but only a torque which is clearly equal to F times the length of the bar,where F is the force of attraction between a big sphere and its neighbouring small sphere. Due to this torque, the suspended wire gets twisted till such time as the restoring torque of the wire equals the gravitational torque . If θ is the angle of twist of the suspended wire, the restoring torque is proportional to θ, equal to τθ. Where τ is the restoring couple per unit angle of twist. τ can be measured independently e.g. by applying a known torque and measuring the angle of twist. The gravitational force between the spherical balls is the same as if their masses are concentrated at their centres.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus if d is the separation between the centres of the big and its neighbouring small ball, M and m their masses, the gravitational force between the big sphere and its neighouring small ball is.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000109707"]',NULL),
(24878,62,128,'paragraph',NULL,'The acceleration experienced by the mass m, which is usually denoted by the symbol g is related to F by Newton’s 2 nd law by relation F = mg. Thus','[{"s": "The acceleration experienced by the mass m, which is usually denoted by the symbol g is related to F by Newton’s 2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "nd", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "law by relation F = mg.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000109711"]',NULL),
