(24891,62,141,'paragraph',NULL,'This is clearly less than the value of g on the surface of earth: Gravitation-image1.png For Gravitation-image2.png we can expand the RHS of Eq . (8.14) :','[{"s": "This is clearly less than the value of g on the surface of earth:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image1.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "For", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Gravitation-image2.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "we can expand the RHS of Eq", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". (8.14) :", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000109786"]',NULL),
(25408,63,267,'paragraph',NULL,'where n is number density of molecules, m the mass of the molecule and Kinetic_Theory-image2.png is the mean of squared speed. Combined with the ideal gas equation it yields a kinetic interpretation of temperature .','[{"s": "where n is number density of molecules, m the mass of the molecule and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Kinetic_Theory-image2.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is the mean of squared speed.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Combined with the ideal gas equation it yields a kinetic interpretation of temperature", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000037964", "2000037968", "2000038094", "2000038102", "2000038106", "2000038114", "2000038122", "2000038125", "2000038177", "2000038181", "2000038196", "2000038213", "2000038216", "2000038379", "2000038728", "2000038732", "2000038800", "2000110008", "2000110012", "2000110018", "2000110020", "2000110024", "2000110028", "2000110033", "2000110036", "2000110037", "2000110038", "2000110039", "2000110041", "2000110046", "2000110096", "2000110098"]',NULL),
(25335,63,194,'paragraph',NULL,'As explained earlier, a diatomic molecule treated as a rigid rotator, like a dumbbell, has 5 degrees of freedom: 3 translational and 2 rotational. Using the law of equipartition of energy, the total internal energy of a mole of such a gas is','[{"s": "As explained earlier, a diatomic molecule treated as a rigid rotator, like a dumbbell, has 5 degrees of freedom: 3 translational and 2 rotational. Using the law of equipartition of energy, the total internal energy of a mole of such a gas is", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000037972", "2000037976", "2000037988", "2000038188", "2000038200", "2000038364", "2000038368", "2000038748", "2000038756", "2000038776", "2000110010", "2000110042", "2000110045", "2000110097"]',NULL),
(25418,63,277,'paragraph',NULL,'6. The mean free path l is the average distance covered by a molecule between two successive collisions :','[{"s": "6.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The mean free path l is the average distance covered by a molecule between two successive collisions :", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000037980", "2000037992", "2000038098", "2000038129", "2000038137", "2000038724", "2000038779", "2000038783", "2000038788", "2000110017", "2000110023", "2000110025", "2000110032"]',NULL),
(25184,63,43,'paragraph',NULL,'where n is the number density, i.e. number of molecules per unit volume. kB is the Boltzmann constant introduced above. Its value in SI units is 1.38 × 10 –23 J K –1 . Another useful form of Eq. (13.3) is','[{"s": "where n is the number density, i.e. number of molecules per unit volume. kB is the Boltzmann constant introduced above. Its value in SI units is 1.38 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–23", "t": "text"}, {"s": "J K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Another useful form of Eq.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(13.3) is", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037984", "2000038736", "2000110026"]',NULL),
(25234,63,93,'paragraph',NULL,'where v is the speed and v 2 denotes the mean of the squared speed . Thus','[{"s": "where v is the speed and v", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "denotes the mean of the squared speed", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038110", "2000038739", "2000110027"]',NULL),
(27661,68,368,'paragraph',NULL,'The components of velocity at time t can be obtained using Eq. 2 ( 4.33b) :','[{"s": "The components of velocity at time t can be obtained using Eq.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4.33b) :", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000033532", "2000107300"]',NULL),
(25250,63,109,'paragraph',NULL,'i.e., the average kinetic energy of a molecule is proportional to the absolute temperature of the gas; it is independent of pressure, volume or the nature of the ideal gas. This is a fundamental result relating temperature, a macroscopic measurable parameter of a gas (a thermodynamic variable as it is called) to a molecular quantity, namely the average kinetic energy of a molecule. The two domains are connected by the Boltzmann constant. We note in passing that Eq. (13.18) tells us that internal','[{"s": "i.e., the average kinetic energy of a molecule is proportional to the absolute temperature of the gas; it is independent of pressure, volume or the nature of the ideal gas.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This is a fundamental result relating temperature, a macroscopic measurable parameter of a gas (a thermodynamic variable as it is called) to a molecular quantity, namely the average kinetic energy of a molecule. The two domains are connected by the Boltzmann constant. We note in passing that Eq. (13.18) tells us that internal", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038118", "2000038744", "2000038751", "2000038760", "2000038763", "2000038768", "2000110011", "2000110013", "2000110014", "2000110021", "2000110029", "2000110030", "2000110031", "2000110034", "2000110099", "2000110103"]',NULL),
(25263,63,122,'paragraph',NULL,'The speed is of the order of the speed of sound in air. It follows from Eq. (13.19) that at the same temperature, lighter molecules have greater rms speed.','[{"s": "The speed is of the order of the speed of sound in air.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It follows from Eq. (13.19) that at the same temperature, lighter molecules have greater rms speed.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038133", "2000038790", "2000110035"]',NULL),
(25264,63,123,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 13.5 A flask contains argon and chlorine in the ratio of 2:1 by mass. The temperature of the mixture is 27 °C. Obtain the ratio of (i) average kinetic energy per molecule, and (ii) root mean square speed v rms of the molecules of the two gases. Atomic mass of argon = 39.9 u; Molecular mass of chlorine = 70.9 u.','[{"s": "Example 13.5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A flask contains argon and chlorine in the ratio of 2:1 by mass.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The temperature of the mixture is 27 °C. Obtain the ratio of (i) average kinetic energy per molecule, and (ii) root mean square speed v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rms", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the molecules of the two gases. Atomic mass of argon = 39.9 u; Molecular mass of chlorine = 70.9 u.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038184", "2000038771", "2000110007", "2000110040", "2000110043"]',NULL),
(25323,63,182,'paragraph',NULL,'The molecule of a monatomic gas has only three translational degrees of freedom. Thus, the average energy of a molecule at temperature T is (3/2)kBT . The total internal energy of a mole of such a gas is','[{"s": "The molecule of a monatomic gas has only three translational degrees of freedom.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus, the average energy of a molecule at temperature T is (3/2)kBT . The total internal energy of a mole of such a gas is", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038192", "2000110009", "2000110044"]',NULL),
(25344,63,203,'paragraph',NULL,'In general a polyatomic molecule has 3 translational, 3 rotational degrees of freedom and a certain number (f) of vibrational modes. According to the law of equipartition of energy, it is easily seen that one mole of such a gas has','[{"s": "In general a polyatomic molecule has 3 translational, 3 rotational degrees of freedom and a certain number (f) of vibrational modes.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "According to the law of equipartition of energy, it is easily seen that one mole of such a gas has", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000038205", "2000110015", "2000110095"]',NULL),
(25373,63,232,'paragraph',NULL,'Molecules in a gas have rather large speeds of the order of the speed of sound. Yet a gas leaking from a cylinder in a kitchen takes considerable time to diffuse to the other corners of the room. The top of a cloud of smoke holds together for hours. This happens because molecules in a gas have a finite though small size, so they are bound to undergo collisions. As a result, they cannot move straight unhindered; their paths keep getting incessantly deflected.','[{"s": "Molecules in a gas have rather large speeds of the order of the speed of sound.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Yet a gas leaking from a cylinder in a kitchen takes considerable time to diffuse to the other corners of the room. The top of a cloud of smoke holds together for hours. This happens because molecules in a gas have a finite though small size, so they are bound to undergo collisions. As a result, they cannot move straight unhindered; their paths keep getting incessantly deflected.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038209", "2000110016"]',NULL),
(25393,63,252,'paragraph',NULL,'As expected, the mean free path given by Eq. (13.40) depends inversely on the number density and the size of the molecules. In a highly evacuated tube n is rather small and the mean free path can be as large as the length of the tube.','[{"s": "As expected, the mean free path given by Eq.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(13.40) depends inversely on the number density and the size of the molecules.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In a highly evacuated tube n is rather small and the mean free path can be as large as the length of the tube.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038371", "2000110019"]',NULL),
(25588,64,28,'paragraph',NULL,'Every body continues to be in its state of rest or of uniform motion in a straight line unless compelled by some external force to act otherwise.','[{"s": "Every body continues to be in its state of rest or of uniform motion in a straight line unless compelled by some external force to act otherwise.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000033091", "2000034182", "2000035413", "2000107579"]',NULL),
(25840,64,280,'paragraph',NULL,'We can reduce the contribution of friction to the circular motion of the car if the road is banked (Fig. 5.14(b)). Since there is no acceleration along the vertical direction, the net force along this direction must be zero. Hence,','[{"s": "We can reduce the contribution of friction to the circular motion of the car if the road is banked (Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "5.14(b)). Since there is no acceleration along the vertical direction, the net force along this direction must be zero. Hence,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000032935", "2000033082", "2000033087", "2000033119", "2000033146", "2000034158", "2000034493", "2000034525", "2000034538", "2000034545", "2000034562", "2000034565", "2000034590", "2000034609", "2000034631", "2000034642", "2000034650", "2000034674", "2000035371", "2000035433", "2000035437", "2000035442", "2000107569", "2000107572", "2000107575", "2000107576", "2000107584", "2000107589", "2000107590", "2000107594", "2000107600", "2000107681", "2000107682", "2000107684", "2000107685"]',NULL),
(27687,68,394,'paragraph',NULL,'The horizontal distance travelled by a projectile from its initial position (x = y = 0) to the position where it passes y = 0 during its fall is called the horizontal range , R. It is the distance travelled during the time of flight T f . Therefore, the range R is','[{"s": "The horizontal distance travelled by a projectile from its initial position (x = y = 0) to the position where it passes y = 0 during its fall is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "horizontal range", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", R. It is the distance travelled during the time of flight T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Therefore, the range R is", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000033591", "2000107302"]',NULL),
(25737,64,177,'paragraph',NULL,'In other words, the resultant of any two forces say F 1 and F 2 , obtained by the parallelogram law of forces must be equal and opposite to the third force, F 3 . As seen in Fig . 5.7, the three forces in equilibrium can be represented by the sides of a triangle with the vector arrows taken in the same sense . The result can be generalised to any number of forces. A particle is in equilibrium under the action of forces F 1 , F 2 ,... F n if they can be represented by the sides of a closed n-sided polygon with arrows directed in the same sense.','[{"s": "In other words, the resultant of any two forces say", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", obtained by the parallelogram law of forces must be equal and opposite to the third force,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". As seen in Fig", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5.7, the three forces in equilibrium can be represented by the sides of a triangle with the vector arrows taken in the same sense", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The result can be generalised to any number of forces. A particle is in equilibrium under the action of forces", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",...", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "if they can be represented by the sides of a closed n-sided polygon with arrows directed in the same sense.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000032951", "2000033149", "2000034550", "2000034581", "2000034619", "2000035375", "2000035405", "2000107559", "2000107560", "2000107570", "2000107580", "2000107604"]',NULL),
(25589,64,29,'paragraph',NULL,'The state of rest or uniform linear motion both imply zero acceleration. The first law of motion can, therefore, be simply expressed as:','[{"s": "The state of rest or uniform linear motion both imply zero acceleration.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The first law of motion can, therefore, be simply expressed as:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000032939", "2000032943", "2000032999", "2000033010", "2000033046", "2000033066", "2000033122", "2000033137", "2000034134", "2000034138", "2000034142", "2000034146", "2000034150", "2000034186", "2000034190", "2000034202", "2000034497", "2000034531", "2000034533", "2000034541", "2000034577", "2000034594", "2000034598", "2000034613", "2000034622", "2000034635", "2000034654", "2000034658", "2000034662", "2000035367", "2000035383", "2000035387", "2000035394", "2000035417", "2000107562", "2000107564", "2000107571", "2000107581", "2000107582", "2000107585", "2000107587", "2000107591", "2000107597", "2000107605", "2000107607", "2000107609", "2000107677", "2000107678", "2000107683"]',NULL),
(25590,64,30,'paragraph',NULL,'If the net external force on a body is zero, its acceleration is zero. Acceleration can be non zero only if there is a net external force on the body.','[{"s": "If the net external force on a body is zero, its acceleration is zero.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Acceleration can be non zero only if there is a net external force on the body.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000032947", "2000034194", "2000035421", "2000107583"]',NULL),
(25622,64,62,'paragraph',NULL,'The rate of change of momentum of a body is directly proportional to the applied force and takes place in the direction in which the force acts .','[{"s": "The rate of change of momentum of a body is directly proportional to the applied force and takes place in the direction in which the force acts", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000032970", "2000034198", "2000035425", "2000107586"]',NULL),
(25634,64,74,'paragraph',NULL,'The unit of force has not been defined so far. In fact, we use Eq. (5.4) to define the unit of force. We, therefore, have the liberty to choose any constant value for k . For simplicity, we choose k = 1. The second law then is','[{"s": "The unit of force has not been defined so far. In fact, we use Eq. (5.4) to define the unit of force. We, therefore, have the liberty to choose any constant value for", "t": "text"}, {"s": "k", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For simplicity, we choose", "t": "text"}, {"s": "k =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The second law then is", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000033019", "2000034514", "2000107592", "2000107601"]',NULL),
(25640,64,80,'paragraph',NULL,'2. The second law of motion is a vector law . It is equivalent to three equations, one for each component of the vectors :','[{"s": "2.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The second law of motion is a vector law", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is equivalent to three equations, one for each component of the vectors :", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000033024", "2000034519", "2000107552", "2000107593"]',NULL),
(25666,64,106,'paragraph',NULL,'We sometimes encounter examples where a large force acts for a very short duration producing a finite change in momentum of the body. For example, when a ball hits a wall and bounces back, the force on the ball by the wall acts for a very short time when the two are in contact, yet the force is large enough to reverse the momentum of the ball. Often, in these situations, the force and the time duration are difficult to ascertain separately. However, the product of force and time, which is the change in momentum of the body remains a measurable quantity. This product is called impulse:','[{"s": "We sometimes encounter examples where a large force acts for a very short duration producing a finite change in momentum of the body. For example, when a ball hits a wall and bounces back, the force on the ball by the wall acts for a very short time when the two are in contact, yet the force is large enough to reverse the momentum of the ball. Often, in these situations, the force and the time duration are difficult to ascertain separately. However, the product of force and time, which is the change in momentum of the body remains a measurable quantity.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This product is called impulse:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000033028", "2000034521", "2000107553", "2000107595"]',NULL),
(25670,64,110,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.4 A batsman hits back a ball straight in the direction of the bowler without changing its initial speed of 12 m s –1 . If the mass of the ball is 0.15 kg, determine the impulse imparted to the ball. (Assume linear motion of the ball)','[{"s": "Example 5.4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A batsman hits back a ball straight in the direction of the bowler without changing its initial speed of 12 m s", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "If the mass of the ball is 0.15 kg, determine the impulse imparted to the ball. (Assume linear motion of the ball)", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000033125", "2000034569", "2000107556", "2000107599"]',NULL),
(27697,68,404,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 4.7 Galileo, in his book Two new sciences, stated that “for elevations which exceed or fall short of 45° by equal amounts, the ranges are equal”. Prove this statement.','[{"s": "Example 4.7", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Galileo, in his book Two new sciences, stated that “for elevations which exceed or fall short of 45° by equal amounts, the ranges are equal”.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Prove this statement.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034037", "2000107312"]',NULL),
(25699,64,139,'paragraph',NULL,'How to find the force on the wall? The trick is to consider the force (or impulse) on the ball due to the wall using the second law, and then use the third law to answer (i). Let u be the speed of each ball before and after collision with the wall, and m the mass of each ball. Choose the x and y axes as shown in the figure, and consider the change in momentum of the ball in each case :','[{"s": "How to find the force on the wall? The trick is to consider the force (or impulse) on the ball due to the wall using the second law, and then use the third law to answer (i). Let", "t": "text"}, {"s": "u", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be the speed of each ball before and after collision with the wall, and m the mass of each ball.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Choose the x and y axes as shown in the figure, and consider the change in momentum of the ball in each case :", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000033140", "2000034602", "2000107557", "2000107602"]',NULL),
(25719,64,159,'paragraph',NULL,'The total momentum of an isolated system of interacting particles is conserved.','[{"s": "The total momentum of an isolated system of interacting particles is conserved.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000033143", "2000034606", "2000107558", "2000107603"]',NULL),
(25761,64,201,'paragraph',NULL,'Two other common forces are tension in a string and the force due to spring. When a spring is compressed or extended by an external force, a restoring force is generated. This force is usually proportional to the compression or elongation (for small displacements). The spring force F is written as F = – k x where x is the displacement and k is the force constant. The negative sign denotes that the force is opposite to the displacement from the unstretched state. For an inextensible string, the force constant is very high. The restoring force in a string is called tension. It is customary to use a constant tension T throughout the string. This assumption is true for a string of negligible mass.','[{"s": "Two other common forces are tension in a string and the force due to spring. When a spring is compressed or extended by an external force, a restoring force is generated. This force is usually proportional to the compression or elongation (for small displacements).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The spring force", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "F", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is written as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "F = – k x", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "where", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "x", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is the displacement and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "k", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is the force constant.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The negative sign denotes that the force is opposite to the displacement from the unstretched state. For an inextensible string, the force constant is very high. The restoring force in a string is called tension. It is customary to use a constant tension", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "throughout the string. This assumption is true for a string of negligible mass.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000033152", "2000034625", "2000107561", "2000107606"]',NULL),
(25764,64,204,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us return to the example of a body of mass m at rest on a horizontal table. The force of gravity (mg) is cancelled by the normal reaction force (N) of the table. Now suppose a force F is applied horizontally to the body. We know from experience that a small applied force may not be enough to move the body. But if the applied force F were the only external force on the body, it must move with acceleration F/m, however small. Clearly, the body remains at rest because some other force comes into play in the horizontal direction and opposes the applied force F, resulting in zero net force on the body. This force fs parallel to the surface of the body in contact with the table is known as frictional force, or simply friction (Fig. 5.10(a)). The subscript stands for static friction to distinguish it from kinetic friction fk that we consider later (Fig. 5.10(b)). Note that static friction does not exist by itself. When there is no applied force, there is no static friction. It comes into play the moment there is an applied force. As the applied force F increases, f s also increases, remaining equal and opposite to the applied force (up to a certain limit), keeping the body at rest. Hence, it is called static friction . Static friction opposes impending motion . The term impending motion means motion that would take place (but does not actually take place) under the applied force, if friction were absent.','[{"s": "Let us return to the example of a body of mass m at rest on a horizontal table. The force of gravity (mg) is cancelled by the normal reaction force (N) of the table. Now suppose a force F is applied horizontally to the body. We know from experience that a small applied force may not be enough to move the body. But if the applied force F were the only external force on the body, it must move with acceleration F/m, however small. Clearly, the body remains at rest because some other force comes into play in the horizontal direction and opposes the applied force F, resulting in zero net force on the body. This force fs parallel to the surface of the body in contact with the table is known as frictional force, or simply friction (Fig. 5.10(a)). The subscript stands for static friction to distinguish it from kinetic friction fk that we consider later (Fig. 5.10(b)).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Note that static friction does not exist by itself.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When there is no applied force, there is no static friction. It comes into play the moment there is an applied force. As the applied force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "increases,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "f", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "also increases, remaining equal and opposite to the applied force (up to a certain limit), keeping the body at rest. Hence, it is called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "static friction", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Static friction opposes", "t": "text"}, {"s": "impending motion", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The term impending motion means motion that would take place (but does not actually take place) under the applied force, if friction were absent.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034125", "2000034639", "2000107563", "2000107608"]',NULL),
(25766,64,206,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 5.10 Static and sliding friction: (a) Impending motion of the body is opposed by static friction. When external force exceeds the maximum limit of static friction, the body begins to move. (b) Once the body is in motion, it is subject to sliding or kinetic friction which opposes relative motion between the two surfaces in contact. Kinetic friction is usually less than the maximum value of static friction.','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5.10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Static and sliding friction: (a) Impending motion of the body is opposed by static friction.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When external force exceeds the maximum limit of static friction, the body begins to move. (b) Once the body is in motion, it is subject to sliding or kinetic friction which opposes relative motion between the two surfaces in contact. Kinetic friction is usually less than the maximum value of static friction.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034128", "2000034646", "2000107565", "2000107679"]',NULL),
(25767,64,207,'paragraph',NULL,'We know from experience that as the applied force exceeds a certain limit, the body begins to move. It is found experimentally that the limiting value of static friction Laws_of_Motion-image3.png is independent of the area of contact and varies with the normal force (N) approximately as :','[{"s": "We know from experience that as the applied force exceeds a certain limit, the body begins to move. It is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "found experimentally that the limiting value of static friction", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Laws_of_Motion-image3.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is independent of the area of contact and varies with the normal force", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(N)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "approximately as :", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034131", "2000034666", "2000107566", "2000107680"]',NULL),
(25783,64,223,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.8 See Fig. 3 5.11. A mass of 4 kg rests on a horizontal plane.The plane is gradually inclined until at an angle θ = 15° with the horizontal, the mass just begins to slide. What is the coefficient of static friction between the block and the surface ?','[{"s": "Example 5.8", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "See Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "5.11. A mass of 4 kg rests on a horizontal plane.The plane is gradually inclined until at an angle θ = 15° with the horizontal, the mass just begins to slide.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What is the coefficient of static friction between the block and the surface ?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034174", "2000034670", "2000107567"]',NULL),
(25795,64,235,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.9 What is the acceleration of the block and trolley system shown in a Fig. 5 5.12(a), if the coefficient of kinetic friction between the trolley and the surface is 0.04? What is the tension in the string? (Take g = 10 m s -2 ). Neglect the mass of the string.','[{"s": "Example 5.9", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What is the acceleration of the block and trolley system shown in a Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "5.12(a), if the coefficient of kinetic friction between the trolley and the surface is 0.04? What is the tension in the string? (Take g = 10 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Neglect the mass of the string.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034154", "2000035363", "2000107568"]',NULL),
(25873,64,313,'paragraph',NULL,'The three laws of motion that you have learnt in this chapter are the foundation of mechanics. You should now be able to handle a large variety of problems in mechanics. A typical problem in mechanics usually does not merely involve a single body under the action of given forces. More often, we will need to consider an assembly of different bodies exerting forces on each other. Besides, each body in the assembly experiences the force of gravity. When trying to solve a problem of this type, it is useful to remember the fact that we can choose any part of the assembly and apply the laws of motion to that part provided we include all forces on the chosen part due to the remaining parts of the assembly. We may call the chosen part of the assembly as the system and the remaining part of the assembly (plus any other agencies of forces) as the environment. We have followed the same method in solved examples. To handle a typical problem in mechanics systematically, one should use the following steps :','[{"s": "The three laws of motion that you have learnt in this chapter are the foundation of mechanics. You should now be able to handle a large variety of problems in mechanics. A typical problem in mechanics usually does not merely involve a single body under the action of given forces. More often, we will need to consider an assembly of different bodies exerting forces on each other. Besides, each body in the assembly experiences the force of gravity.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When trying to solve a problem of this type, it is useful to remember the fact that we can choose any part of the assembly and apply the laws of motion to that part provided we include all forces on the chosen part due to the remaining parts of the assembly.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "We may call the chosen part of the assembly as the system and the remaining part of the assembly (plus any other agencies of forces) as the environment. We have followed the same method in solved examples. To handle a typical problem in mechanics systematically, one should use the following steps :", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034166", "2000035389", "2000107574"]',NULL),
(25883,64,323,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) The system (block + cylinder) accelerates downwards with 0.1 m s -2 . The free-body diagram of the system shows two forces on the system : the force of gravity due to the earth (270 N); and the normal force R ′ by the floor. Note, the free-body diagram of the system does not show the internal forces between the block and the cylinder. Applying the second law to the system,','[{"s": "(b) The system (block + cylinder) accelerates downwards with 0.1 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The free-body diagram of the system shows two forces on the system : the force of gravity due to the earth (270 N); and the normal force", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "R", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "′ by the floor.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Note, the free-body diagram of the system does not show the internal forces between the block and the cylinder. Applying the second law to the system,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034170", "2000035396", "2000107577"]',NULL),
(25902,64,342,'paragraph',NULL,'The rate of change of momentum of a body is proportional to the applied force and takes place in the direction in which the force acts. Thus','[{"s": "The rate of change of momentum of a body is proportional to the applied force and takes place in the direction in which the force acts.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034178", "2000035408", "2000107578"]',NULL),
(26131,65,193,'paragraph',NULL,'There is one more quantity of interest. If a magnetic needle is perfectly balanced about a horizontal axis so that it can swing in a plane of the magnetic meridian, the needle would make an angle with the horizontal (Fig. 5.10). This is known as the angle of dip (also known as inclination) . Thus, dip is the angle that the total magnetic field B E of the earth makes with the surface of the earth. Figure 5.11 shows the magnetic meridian plane at a point P on the surface of the earth. The plane is a section through the earth. The total magnetic field at P can be resolved into a horizontal component H E and a vertical component Z E . The angle that B E makes with H E is the angle of dip, I.','[{"s": "There is one more quantity of interest. If a magnetic needle is perfectly balanced about a horizontal axis so that it can swing in a plane of the magnetic meridian, the needle would make an angle with the horizontal (Fig. 5.10).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is known as the angle of dip (also known as inclination)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, dip is the angle that the total magnetic field B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the earth makes with the surface of the earth. Figure 5.11 shows the magnetic meridian plane at a point P on the surface of the earth. The plane is a section through the earth. The total magnetic field at P can be resolved into a horizontal component H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and a vertical component Z", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The angle that B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "makes with H", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the angle of dip, I.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000043248", "2000043252", "2000043284", "2000043336", "2000043344", "2000043462", "2000043482", "2000043503", "2000043765", "2000043769", "2000043773", "2000043777", "2000043789", "2000043833", "2000111097", "2000111102", "2000111109", "2000111116", "2000111119", "2000111121", "2000111123", "2000111124", "2000111125"]',NULL),
(26011,65,73,'paragraph',NULL,'The resemblance of magnetic field lines for a bar magnet and a solenoid suggest that a bar magnet may be thought of as a large number of circulating currents in analogy with a solenoid . Cutting a bar magnet in half is like cutting a solenoid. We get two smaller solenoids with weaker magnetic properties. The field lines remain continuous, emerging from one face of the solenoid and entering into the other face. One can test this analogy by moving a small compass needle in the neighbourhood of a bar magnet and a current-carrying finite solenoid and noting that the deflections of the needle are similar in both cases.','[{"s": "The resemblance of magnetic field lines for a bar magnet and a solenoid suggest that a bar magnet may be thought of as a large number of circulating currents in analogy with a solenoid", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Cutting a bar magnet in half is like cutting a solenoid. We get two smaller solenoids with weaker magnetic properties. The field lines remain continuous, emerging from one face of the solenoid and entering into the other face. One can test this analogy by moving a small compass needle in the neighbourhood of a bar magnet and a current-carrying finite solenoid and noting that the deflections of the needle are similar in both cases.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000043240", "2000043245", "2000043260", "2000043300", "2000043372", "2000043376", "2000043396", "2000043454", "2000043458", "2000043466", "2000043470", "2000043474", "2000043478", "2000043486", "2000043507", "2000043737", "2000043745", "2000043749", "2000043753", "2000043757", "2000043761", "2000043781", "2000043785", "2000043793", "2000043797", "2000043805", "2000043817", "2000043821", "2000043825", "2000043829", "2000043837", "2000044063", "2000044069", "2000111095", "2000111096", "2000111098", "2000111099", "2000111100", "2000111101", "2000111103", "2000111104", "2000111106", "2000111107", "2000111112", "2000111113", "2000111114", "2000111115", "2000111117", "2000111118", "2000111126", "2000111167", "2000111168", "2000111170", "2000111171", "2000111172"]',NULL),
(27716,68,423,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 4.9 A cricket ball is thrown at a speed of 28 m s –1 in a direction 30° above the horizontal. Calculate (a) the maximum height, (b) the time taken by the ball to return to the same level, and (c) the distance from the thrower to the point where the ball returns to the same level.','[{"s": "Example 4.9", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A cricket ball is thrown at a speed of 28 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in a direction 30° above the horizontal.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Calculate (a) the maximum height, (b) the time taken by the ball to return to the same level, and (c) the distance from the thrower to the point where the ball returns to the same level.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000107259", "2000107317"]',NULL),
(26250,65,312,'paragraph',NULL,'The hysteresis curve (Fig. 5.14) allows us to select suitable materials for permanent magnets. The material should have high retentivity so that the magnet is strong and high coercivity so that the magnetisation is not erased by stray magnetic fields, temperature fluctuations or minor mechanical damage. Further, the material should have a high permeability. Steel is one-favoured choice. It has a slightly smaller retentivity than soft iron but this is outweighed by the much smaller coercivity of soft iron. Other suitable materials for permanent magnets are alnico, cobalt steel and ticonal.','[{"s": "The hysteresis curve (Fig. 5.14) allows us to select suitable materials for permanent magnets.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The material should have high retentivity so that the magnet is strong and high coercivity so that the magnetisation is not erased by stray magnetic fields, temperature fluctuations or minor mechanical damage.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Further, the material should have a high permeability. Steel is one-favoured choice. It has a slightly smaller retentivity than soft iron but this is outweighed by the much smaller coercivity of soft iron. Other suitable materials for permanent magnets are alnico, cobalt steel and ticonal.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000043256", "2000043809", "2000043813", "2000111110", "2000111120", "2000111122", "2000111127"]',NULL),
(25946,65,8,'paragraph',NULL,'In the previous chapter we have learned that moving charges or electric currents produce magnetic fields. This discovery, which was made in the early part of the nineteenth century is credited to Oersted, Ampere, Biot and Savart, among others.','[{"s": "In the previous chapter we have learned that moving charges or electric currents produce magnetic fields.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This discovery, which was made in the early part of the nineteenth century is credited to Oersted, Ampere, Biot and Savart, among others.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000043498"]',NULL),
(26015,65,77,'paragraph',NULL,'The magnitude of the total field is obtained by summing over all the elements — in other words by integrating from x = – l to x = + l. Thus,','[{"s": "The magnitude of the total field is obtained by summing over all the elements — in other words by integrating from x = – l to x = + l. Thus,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000043840"]',NULL),
(26023,65,85,'paragraph',NULL,'This is also the far axial magnetic field of a bar magnet which one may obtain experimentally. Thus, a bar magnet and a solenoid produce similar magnetic fields. The magnetic moment of a bar magnet is thus equal to the magnetic moment of an equivalent solenoid that produces the same magnetic field.','[{"s": "This is also the far axial magnetic field of a bar magnet which one may obtain experimentally. Thus, a bar magnet and a solenoid produce similar magnetic fields.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The magnetic moment of a bar magnet is thus equal to the magnetic moment of an equivalent solenoid that produces the same magnetic field.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000044065"]',NULL),
(26029,65,91,'paragraph',NULL,'In magnitude τ = mB sinθ','[{"s": "In magnitude τ = mB sinθ", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000111105"]',NULL),
(26034,65,96,'paragraph',NULL,'This represents a simple harmonic motion. 4 The square of the angular frequency is ω 2 = mB/I and the time period is,','[{"s": "This represents a simple harmonic motion.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The square of the angular frequency is ω", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= mB/I and the time period is,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000111108"]',NULL),
(26037,65,99,'paragraph',NULL,'An expression for magnetic potential energy can also be obtained on lines similar to electrostatic potential energy. 2','[{"s": "An expression for magnetic potential energy can also be obtained on lines similar to electrostatic potential energy.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000111111"]',NULL),
(26050,65,112,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 5.2 A short bar magnet placed with its axis at 30° with an external field of 800 G experiences a torque of 0.016 Nm. 2 (a) What is the magnetic moment of the magnet? (b) What is the work done in moving it from its most stable to most unstable position? (c) The bar magnet is replaced by a solenoid of cross-sectional area 2 × 10 –4 m 2 and 1000 turns, but of the same magnetic moment. Determine the current flowing through the solenoid.','[{"s": "Example 5.2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A short bar magnet placed with its axis at 30° with an external field of 800 G experiences a torque of 0.016 Nm.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(a) What is the magnetic moment of the magnet? (b) What is the work done in moving it from its most stable to most unstable position? (c) The bar magnet is replaced by a solenoid of cross-sectional area 2 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and 1000 turns, but of the same magnetic moment. Determine the current flowing through the solenoid.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000111169"]',NULL),
(26507,66,77,'paragraph',NULL,'where ρ is the density of mercury and h is the height of the mercury column in the tube.','[{"s": "where ρ is the density of mercury and h is the height of the mercury column in the tube.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000037513", "2000037671", "2000038311", "2000038735", "2000109804", "2000109830"]',NULL),
(26784,66,354,'paragraph',NULL,'Another interesting consequence of surface tension is that the pressure inside a spherical drop Fig. 10.20(a) is more than the pressure outside. Suppose a spherical drop of radius r is in equilibrium. If its radius increase by ∆r. The extra surface energy is','[{"s": "Another interesting consequence of surface tension is that the pressure inside a spherical drop Fig. 10.20(a) is more than the pressure outside. Suppose a spherical drop of radius r is in equilibrium. If its radius increase by ∆r.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The extra surface energy is", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000037518", "2000037522", "2000037534", "2000037611", "2000037614", "2000037627", "2000037635", "2000037647", "2000037763", "2000037766", "2000037771", "2000037775", "2000037779", "2000037782", "2000037811", "2000037814", "2000037819", "2000038163", "2000038267", "2000038271", "2000038275", "2000038278", "2000038282", "2000038287", "2000038291", "2000038294", "2000038335", "2000038339", "2000038343", "2000038347", "2000038351", "2000038367", "2000038372", "2000038376", "2000038647", "2000038683", "2000038691", "2000038703", "2000038727", "2000038740", "2000038747", "2000039329", "2000039332", "2000039337", "2000039340", "2000039344", "2000039348", "2000039353", "2000109788", "2000109789", "2000109792", "2000109793", "2000109795", "2000109797", "2000109799", "2000109800", "2000109805", "2000109809", "2000109810", "2000109813", "2000109819", "2000109820", "2000109821", "2000109824", "2000109827", "2000109829", "2000109837", "2000109839", "2000109844", "2000109845", "2000109847", "2000109848", "2000109850", "2000109918", "2000109919", "2000109920", "2000109921", "2000109922", "2000109923"]',NULL),
(27929,69,72,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 3.6 Determining velocity from position-time graph. Velocity at t = 4 s is the slope of the tangent to the graph at that instant.','[{"s": "Fig. 3.6", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Determining velocity from position-time graph.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Velocity at t = 4 s is the slope of the tangent to the graph at that instant.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000039056", "2000039927", "2000040408", "2000106128", "2000106143", "2000106161", "2000106197"]',NULL),
(26512,66,82,'paragraph',NULL,'An open tube manometer is a useful instrument for measuring pressure differences. It consists of a U-tube containing a suitable liquid i.e., a low density liquid (such as oil) for measuring small pressure differences and a high density liquid (such as mercury) for large pressure differences. One end of the tube is open to the atmosphere and the other end is connected to the system whose pressure we want to measure [see Fig. 10.5 (b)]. The pressure P at A is equal to pressure at point B. What we normally measure is the gauge pressure, which is P − Pa, given by Eq. (10.8) and is proportional to manometer height h.','[{"s": "An open tube manometer is a useful instrument for measuring pressure differences. It consists of a U-tube containing a suitable liquid i.e., a low density liquid (such as oil) for measuring small pressure differences and a high density liquid (such as mercury) for large pressure differences. One end of the tube is open to the atmosphere and the other end is connected to the system whose pressure we want to measure [see Fig. 10.5 (b)].", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The pressure P at A is equal to pressure at point B.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What we normally measure is the gauge pressure, which is P − Pa, given by Eq. (10.8) and is proportional to manometer height h.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037524", "2000037707", "2000038315", "2000039285", "2000109806", "2000109831"]',NULL),
(26523,66,93,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.4 At a depth of 1000 m in an ocean (a) what is the absolute pressure? (b) What is the gauge pressure? (c) Find the force acting on the window of area 20 cm × 20 cm of a submarine at this depth, the interior of which is maintained at sea-level atmospheric pressure. (The density of sea water is 1.03 × 10 3 kg m -3 , g = 10 m s –2 .)','[{"s": "Example 10.4 At a depth of 1000 m in an ocean (a) what is the absolute pressure?", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "(b) What is the gauge pressure? (c) Find the force acting on the window of area 20 cm × 20 cm of a submarine at this depth, the interior of which is maintained at sea-level atmospheric pressure. (The density of sea water is 1.03 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "kg m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-3", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", g = 10 m s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".)", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037528", "2000037715", "2000038319", "2000039290", "2000109807", "2000109833"]',NULL),
(26580,66,150,'paragraph',NULL,'which is called the equation of continuity and it is a statement of conservation of mass in flow of incompressible fluids. 2 In general','[{"s": "which is called the equation of continuity and it is a statement of conservation of mass in flow of incompressible fluids.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In general", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037538", "2000037699", "2000037703", "2000037743", "2000038034", "2000038323", "2000038743", "2000039304", "2000109787", "2000109808", "2000109818", "2000109832", "2000109834", "2000109916"]',NULL),
