(29002,71,135,'paragraph',NULL,'This is equivalent to the law of radioactivity decay, since you can integrate Eq. (13.15) to get back Eq. (13.14). Clearly, R 0 = λ N 0 is the decay rate at t = 0. The decay rate R at a certain time t and the number of undecayed nuclei N at the same time are related by','[{"s": "This is equivalent to the law of radioactivity decay, since you can integrate Eq. (13.15) to get back Eq. (13.14). Clearly,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is the decay rate at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The decay rate", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "R", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "at a certain time", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "t", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and the number of undecayed nuclei", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "N", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "at the same time are related by", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000045499", "2000046472", "2000112017"]',NULL),
(29011,71,144,'paragraph',NULL,'Different radionuclides differ greatly in their rate of decay. A common way to characterize this feature is through the notion of half-life . Half-life of a radionuclide (denoted by T 1/2 ) is the time it takes for a sample that has initially, say N 0 radionuclei to reduce to N 0 N = N 0 /2 and t = T 1/2 in Eq. (13.14), we get','[{"s": "Different radionuclides differ greatly in their rate of decay. A common way to characterize this feature is through the notion of", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "half-life", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Half-life of a radionuclide (denoted by", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is the time it takes for a sample that has initially, say", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "radionuclei to reduce to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2 and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1/2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in Eq. (13.14), we get", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000045533", "2000046483", "2000112018"]',NULL),
(29014,71,147,'paragraph',NULL,'Another related measure is the average or mean life τ. This again can be obtained from Eq. (13.14). The number of nuclei which decay in the time interval t to t + ∆ t is R ( t )∆ t (= λ N 0 e –λt ∆ t ). Each of them has lived for time t . Thus the total life of all these nuclei would be t λ N 0 e –λt ∆ t . It is clear that some nuclei may live for a short time while others may live longer. Therefore to obtain the mean life, we have to sum (or integrate) this expression over all times from 0 to Nuclei-image80.png , and divide by the total number N 0 of nuclei at t = 0. Thus,','[{"s": "Another related measure is the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "average", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "mean life", "t": "text"}, {"s": "τ. This again can be obtained from Eq. (13.14). The number of nuclei which decay in the time interval", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(= λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–λt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). Each of them has lived for time", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Thus the total life of all these nuclei would be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–λt", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is clear that some nuclei may live for a short time while others may live longer.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Therefore to obtain the mean life, we have to sum (or integrate) this expression over all times from 0 to", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image80.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", and divide by the total number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of nuclei at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "t", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0. Thus,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000045537", "2000046487", "2000112020"]',NULL),
(29038,71,171,'paragraph',NULL,'A well-known example of alpha decay is the decay of uranium Nuclei-image13.png to thorium Nuclei-image142.png with the emission of a helium nucleus Nuclei-image14.png Nuclei-image47.png → Nuclei-image26.png + Nuclei-image14.png (α-decay) (13.19)','[{"s": "A well-known example of alpha decay is the decay of uranium", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image13.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "to thorium", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image142.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "with the emission of a helium nucleus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image47.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "→", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image26.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image14.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(α-decay) (13.19)", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000045544", "2000046504", "2000112024"]',NULL),
(29039,71,172,'paragraph',NULL,'In α-decay, the mass number of the product nucleus (daughter nucleus) is four less than that of the decaying nucleus (parent nucleus), while the atomic number decreases by two. In general, α-decay of a parent nucleus Nuclei-image9.png results in a daughter nucleus Nuclei-image17.png','[{"s": "In α-decay, the mass number of the product nucleus (daughter nucleus) is four less than that of the decaying nucleus (parent nucleus), while the atomic number decreases by two.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In general, α-decay of a parent nucleus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image9.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "results in a daughter nucleus", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Nuclei-image17.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]','marked','["2000045548", "2000046510", "2000112028"]',NULL),
(29075,71,208,'paragraph',NULL,'In both β − and β + decay, the mass number A remains unchanged. In β − decay, the atomic number Z of the nucleus goes up by 1, while in β + decay Z goes down by 1. The basic nuclear process underlying β − decay is the conversion of neutron to proton','[{"s": "In both β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "−", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "decay, the mass number", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "remains unchanged.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In β", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "−", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "decay, the atomic number", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Z", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "of the nucleus goes up by 1, while in β", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "+", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "decay Z goes down by 1. The basic nuclear process underlying β", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "−", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "decay is the conversion of neutron to proton", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000045559", "2000046513", "2000112029"]',NULL),
(29103,71,236,'paragraph',NULL,'The disintegration energy in fission events first appears as the kinetic energy of the fragments and neutrons. Eventually it is transferred to the surrounding matter appearing as heat. The source of energy in nuclear reactors, which produce electricity, is nuclear fission. The enormous energy released in an atom bomb comes from uncontrolled nuclear fission. We discuss some details in the next section how a nuclear reactor functions.','[{"s": "The disintegration energy in fission events first appears as the kinetic energy of the fragments and neutrons.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Eventually it is transferred to the surrounding matter appearing as heat.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The source of energy in nuclear reactors, which produce electricity, is nuclear fission.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The enormous energy released in an atom bomb comes from uncontrolled nuclear fission. We discuss some details in the next section how a nuclear reactor functions.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000045599", "2000111982", "2000112075"]',NULL),
(29179,71,312,'paragraph',NULL,'10. Radioactivity is the phenomenon in which nuclei of a given species transform by giving out α or β or γ rays; α-rays are helium nuclei; β-rays are electrons. γ-rays are electromagnetic radiation of wavelengths shorter than X-rays;','[{"s": "10.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Radioactivity is the phenomenon in which nuclei of a given species transform by giving out α or β or γ rays; α-rays are helium nuclei;", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "β-rays are electrons. γ-rays are electromagnetic radiation of wavelengths shorter than X-rays;", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000045615", "2000111980", "2000112078"]',NULL),
(29181,71,314,'paragraph',NULL,'where λ is the decay constant or disintegration constant.','[{"s": "where λ is the decay constant or disintegration constant.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000045618", "2000111983", "2000112079"]',NULL),
(29193,71,326,'paragraph',NULL,'5. For fusion, the light nuclei must have sufficient initial energy to overcome the coulomb potential barrier. That is why fusion requires very high temperatures.','[{"s": "5.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For fusion, the light nuclei must have sufficient initial energy to overcome the coulomb potential barrier.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "That is why fusion requires very high temperatures.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000045622", "2000111984", "2000112080"]',NULL),
