(29225,71,358,'paragraph',NULL,'13.7 A radioactive isotope has a half-life of T years. How long will it take the activity to reduce to a) 3.125%, b) 1% of its original value?','[{"s": "13.7", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A radioactive isotope has a half-life of T years.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "How long will it take the activity to reduce to a) 3.125%, b) 1% of its original value?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046105", "2000111986"]',NULL),
(29233,71,366,'paragraph',NULL,'13.13 The radionuclide 11 C decays according to','[{"s": "13.13 The radionuclide", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "11", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "C decays according to", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000046112", "2000111987"]',NULL),
(29292,71,425,'paragraph',NULL,'1. Suppose we consider a large number of containers each containing initially 10000 atoms of a radioactive material with a half life of 1yr. After 1yr,','[{"s": "1.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Suppose we consider a large number of containers each containing initially 10000 atoms of a radioactive material with a half life of 1yr.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "After 1yr,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046132", "2000111988"]',NULL),
(29479,72,87,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, simple harmonic motion (SHM) is not any periodic motion but one in which displacement is a sinusoidal function of time. Fig. 14.4 shows the positions of a particle executing SHM at discrete value of time, each interval of time being T/4, where T is the period of motion. Fig. 14.5 plots the graph of x versus t, which gives the values of displacement as a continuous function of time. The quantities A, ω and φ which characterize a given SHM have standard names, as summarised in Fig. 14.6. Let us understand these quantities.','[{"s": "Thus, simple harmonic motion (SHM) is not any periodic motion but one in which displacement is a sinusoidal function of time.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Fig. 14.4 shows the positions of a particle executing SHM at discrete value of time, each interval of time being T/4, where T is the period of motion. Fig. 14.5 plots the graph of x versus t, which gives the values of displacement as a continuous function of time. The quantities A, ω and φ which characterize a given SHM have standard names, as summarised in Fig. 14.6. Let us understand these quantities.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000039652", "2000039656", "2000039660", "2000039664", "2000039668", "2000039676", "2000039680", "2000039684", "2000039688", "2000039692", "2000039714", "2000039750", "2000039754", "2000039758", "2000039762", "2000039766", "2000039770", "2000039778", "2000039782", "2000039790", "2000039798", "2000039810", "2000039821", "2000039829", "2000039873", "2000039877", "2000039881", "2000039885", "2000039897", "2000039901", "2000040136", "2000040139", "2000040144", "2000040156", "2000040160", "2000040168", "2000040176", "2000040181", "2000040192", "2000040196", "2000040201", "2000040209", "2000040264", "2000040272", "2000040276", "2000040280", "2000040284", "2000040288", "2000040292", "2000040296", "2000040308", "2000040312", "2000040316", "2000040573", "2000040577", "2000040589", "2000040597", "2000040621", "2000040625", "2000040637", "2000040641", "2000040645", "2000041123", "2000041127"]',NULL),
(29612,72,220,'paragraph',NULL,'Hence the force acting on the mass is proportional to the displacement and is directed towards the mean position; therefore, the motion executed by the mass is simple harmonic. The time period of oscillations is, 2','[{"s": "Hence the force acting on the mass is proportional to the displacement and is directed towards the mean position; therefore, the motion executed by the mass is simple harmonic.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The time period of oscillations is,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000039672", "2000039738", "2000039742", "2000039853", "2000040188", "2000040300", "2000040553", "2000040557", "2000040565", "2000040613", "2000040629", "2000041119"]',NULL);
