(30270,73,328,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 9.23 shows the passage of light through a triangular prism ABC. The angles of incidence and refraction at the first face AB are i and r 1 , while the angle of incidence (from glass to air) at the second face AC is r 2 and the angle of refraction or emergence e. The angle between the emergent ray RS and the direction of the incident ray PQ is called the angle of deviation , δ.','[{"s": "Figure 9.23 shows the passage of light through a triangular prism ABC.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The angles of incidence and refraction at the first face AB are", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", while the angle of incidence (from glass to air) at the second face AC is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and the angle of refraction or emergence", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The angle between the emergent ray RS and the direction of the incident ray PQ is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "angle of deviation", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", δ.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031912", "2000031916", "2000031919", "2000031927", "2000031931", "2000032043", "2000032047", "2000032051", "2000032058", "2000032113", "2000032145", "2000032149", "2000032171", "2000032189", "2000032491", "2000032496", "2000032504", "2000032520", "2000032531", "2000032936", "2000033000", "2000033013", "2000033777", "2000112763", "2000112781", "2000112784", "2000112785", "2000112786", "2000112792", "2000112809", "2000112882", "2000112883", "2000112884"]',NULL),
(29954,73,12,'paragraph',NULL,'We are familiar with the laws of reflection. The angle of reflection (i.e., the angle between reflected ray and the normal to the reflecting surface or the mirror) equals the angle of incidence (angle between incident ray and the normal) . These laws are valid at each point on any reflecting surface whether plane or curved. However, we shall restrict our discussion to the special case of curved surfaces, that is, spherical surfaces. The normal in this case is to be taken as normal to the tangent to surface at the point of incidence. That is, the normal is along the radius, the line joining the centre of curvature of the mirror to the point of incidence.','[{"s": "We are familiar with the laws of reflection.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The angle of reflection (i.e., the angle between reflected ray and the normal to the reflecting surface or the mirror) equals the angle of incidence (angle between incident ray and the normal)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "These laws are valid at each point on any reflecting surface whether plane or curved.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "However, we shall restrict our discussion to the special case of curved surfaces, that is, spherical surfaces. The normal in this case is to be taken as normal to the tangent to surface at the point of incidence. That is, the normal is along the radius, the line joining the centre of curvature of the mirror to the point of incidence.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031935", "2000031939", "2000031943", "2000031947", "2000031951", "2000031955", "2000032003", "2000032019", "2000032054", "2000032062", "2000032066", "2000032070", "2000032074", "2000032090", "2000032094", "2000032121", "2000032125", "2000032129", "2000032137", "2000032141", "2000032151", "2000032159", "2000032169", "2000032177", "2000032183", "2000032186", "2000032433", "2000032437", "2000032441", "2000032445", "2000032449", "2000032481", "2000032484", "2000032487", "2000032499", "2000032508", "2000032512", "2000032523", "2000032892", "2000032919", "2000032945", "2000032976", "2000032979", "2000033002", "2000033009", "2000033721", "2000033725", "2000033737", "2000033741", "2000033745", "2000033749", "2000033753", "2000033757", "2000033761", "2000112755", "2000112756", "2000112757", "2000112758", "2000112759", "2000112761", "2000112765", "2000112766", "2000112768", "2000112770", "2000112771", "2000112772", "2000112773", "2000112776", "2000112777", "2000112778", "2000112779", "2000112780", "2000112782", "2000112783", "2000112788", "2000112789", "2000112791", "2000112793", "2000112794", "2000112795", "2000112796", "2000112797", "2000112798", "2000112799", "2000112800", "2000112801", "2000112802", "2000112803", "2000112805", "2000112806", "2000112807", "2000112808", "2000112876", "2000112877", "2000112878", "2000112879", "2000112881", "2000112885", "2000112887", "2000112888", "2000112889", "2000112890", "2000112892"]',NULL),
(30115,73,173,'paragraph',NULL,'We have so far considered refraction at a plane interface. We shall now consider refraction at a spherical interface between two transparent media. An infinitesimal part of a spherical surface can be regarded as planar and the same laws of refraction can be applied at every point on the surface. Just as for reflection by a spherical mirror, the normal at the point of incidence is perpendicular to the tangent plane to the spherical surface at that point and, therefore, passes through its centre of curvature. We first consider refraction by a single spherical surface and follow it by thin lenses. A thin lens is a transparent optical medium bounded by two surfaces; at least one of which should be spherical. Applying the formula for image formation by a single spherical surface successively at the two surfaces of a lens, we shall obtain the lens maker’s formula and then the lens formula.','[{"s": "We have so far considered refraction at a plane interface. We shall now consider refraction at a spherical interface between two transparent media. An infinitesimal part of a spherical surface can be regarded as planar and the same laws of refraction can be applied at every point on the surface. Just as for reflection by a spherical mirror, the normal at the point of incidence is perpendicular to the tangent plane to the spherical surface at that point and, therefore, passes through its centre of curvature. We first consider refraction by a single spherical surface and follow it by thin lenses. A thin lens is a transparent optical medium bounded by two surfaces; at least one of which should be spherical. Applying the formula for image formation by a single spherical surface successively at the two surfaces of a lens, we shall obtain the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "lens maker’s formula", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and then the lens formula.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031967", "2000031971", "2000031975", "2000031983", "2000031987", "2000031991", "2000031995", "2000031999", "2000032007", "2000032011", "2000032078", "2000032082", "2000032086", "2000032098", "2000032102", "2000032106", "2000032110", "2000032133", "2000032157", "2000032453", "2000032457", "2000032462", "2000032465", "2000032469", "2000032472", "2000032476", "2000032515", "2000032898", "2000032904", "2000032941", "2000032964", "2000032984", "2000032992", "2000033729", "2000112769", "2000112775", "2000112804", "2000112810", "2000112875", "2000112891"]',NULL),
(30740,74,118,'paragraph',NULL,'When an external voltage V is applied across a semiconductor diode such that p-side is connected to the positive terminal of the battery and n-side to the negative terminal [Fig. 14.13(a)], it is said to be forward biased .','[{"s": "When an external voltage", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is applied across a semiconductor diode such that p-side is connected to the positive terminal of the battery and n-side to the negative terminal [Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "14.13(a)], it is said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "forward biased", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046493", "2000046497", "2000046503", "2000046523", "2000046526", "2000046547", "2000046550", "2000046562", "2000046565", "2000046571", "2000046867", "2000046869", "2000046871", "2000046955", "2000046956", "2000046957", "2000046958", "2000046960", "2000046972", "2000046976", "2000046979", "2000046982", "2000046983", "2000046988", "2000046990", "2000046991", "2000046995", "2000047109", "2000047110", "2000047111", "2000047114", "2000047116", "2000047117", "2000112895", "2000112896", "2000112897", "2000112900", "2000112904", "2000112905", "2000112909", "2000112920", "2000112927", "2000112928", "2000112930", "2000112933", "2000112935", "2000112936", "2000112938", "2000112942", "2000112943", "2000112945", "2000112946", "2000112948", "2000112959", "2000113018", "2000113027", "2000113030"]',NULL),
(30665,74,43,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.1 The energy band positions in a semiconductor at 0 K. The upper band, called the conduction band, consists of infinitely large number of closely spaced energy states. The lower band, called the valence band, consists of closely spaced completely filled energy states.','[{"s": "Figure 14.1 The energy band positions in a semiconductor at 0 K. The upper band, called the conduction band, consists of infinitely large number of closely spaced energy states.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The lower band, called the valence band, consists of closely spaced completely filled energy states.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046365", "2000112966"]',NULL),
(30670,74,48,'paragraph',NULL,'Case III: This situation is shown in Fig. 14.2(c). Here a finite but small band gap ( E g < 3 eV) exists. Because of the small band gap, at room temperature some electrons from valence band can acquire enough energy to cross the energy gap and enter the conduction band . These electrons (though small in numbers) can move in the conduction band. Hence, the resistance of semiconductors is not as high as that of the insulators.','[{"s": "Case III:", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This situation is shown in Fig. 14.2(c). Here a finite but small band gap (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": "< 3 eV) exists.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Because of the small band gap, at room temperature some electrons from valence band can acquire enough energy to cross the energy gap and enter the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "conduction", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "band", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "These electrons (though small in numbers) can move in the conduction band. Hence, the resistance of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "semiconductors", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is not as high as that of the insulators.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046402", "2000113023"]',NULL),
(30673,74,51,'paragraph',NULL,'We shall take the most common case of Ge and Si whose lattice structure is shown in Fig. 14.3. These structures are called the diamond-like structures. Each atom is surrounded by four nearest neighbours. We know that Si and Ge have four valence electrons. In its crystalline structure, every Si or Ge atom tends to share one of its four valence electrons with each of its four nearest neighbour atoms, and also to take share of one electron from each such neighbour. These shared electron pairs are referred to as forming a covalent bond or simply a valence bond As the temperature increases, more thermal energy becomes available to these electrons and some of these electrons may break–away (becoming free electrons contributing to conduction). ’ The thermal energy effectively ionises only a few atoms in the crystalline lattice and creates a vacancy in the bond as shown in Fig. 14.5(a). The neighbourhood, from which the free electron (with charge – q ) has come out leaves a vacancy with an effective charge (+ q ). This vacancy with the effective positive electronic charge is called a hole . The hole behaves as an apparent free particle with effective positive charge.','[{"s": "We shall take the most common case of Ge and Si whose lattice structure is shown in Fig. 14.3. These structures are called the diamond-like structures. Each atom is surrounded by four nearest neighbours. We know that Si and Ge have four valence electrons. In its crystalline structure, every Si or Ge atom tends", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to share", "t": "text"}, {"s": "one of its four valence electrons with each of its four nearest neighbour atoms, and also", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to take share", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of one electron from each such neighbour. These shared electron pairs are referred to as forming a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "covalent bond", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or simply a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "valence bond", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "As the temperature increases, more thermal energy becomes available to these electrons and some of these electrons may break–away (becoming", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "free", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "electrons contributing to conduction).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "’", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The thermal energy effectively ionises only a few atoms in the crystalline lattice and creates a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "vacancy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "in the bond as shown in Fig. 14.5(a). The neighbourhood, from which the free electron (with charge –", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") has come out leaves a vacancy with an effective charge (+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). This", "t": "text"}, {"s": "vacancy", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with the effective positive electronic charge is called a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hole", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The hole behaves as an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "apparent free particle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with effective positive charge.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046410", "2000113017"]',NULL),
(30683,74,61,'paragraph',NULL,'An intrinsic semiconductor will behave like an insulator at T = 0 K as shown in Fig. 14.6(a). It is the thermal energy at higher temperatures ( T > 0K), which excites some electrons from the valence band to the conduction band. These thermally excited electrons at T > 0 K, partially occupy the conduction band. Therefore, the energy-band diagram of an intrinsic semiconductor will be as shown in Fig. 14.6(b). Here, some electrons are shown in the conduction band. These have come from the valence band leaving equal number of holes there.','[{"s": "An intrinsic semiconductor will behave like an insulator at", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "T", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "= 0 K as shown in Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "14.6(a). It is the thermal energy at higher temperatures (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> 0K), which excites some electrons from the valence band to the conduction band. These thermally excited electrons at", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "> 0 K, partially occupy the conduction band. Therefore, the energy-band diagram of an intrinsic semiconductor will be as shown in Fig. 14.6(b). Here, some electrons are shown in the conduction band. These have come from the valence band leaving equal number of holes there.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046413", "2000113019"]',NULL),
(30688,74,66,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 14.1 C, Si and Ge have same lattice structure. Why is C insulator while Si and Ge intrinsic semiconductors?','[{"s": "Example 14.1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "C, Si and Ge have same lattice structure.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Why is C insulator while Si and Ge intrinsic semiconductors?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046421"]',NULL),
(30698,74,76,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose we dope Si or Ge with a pentavalent element as shown in Fig . 14.7. When an atom of +5 valency element occupies the position of an atom in the crystal lattice of Si, four of its electrons bond with the four silicon neighbours while the fifth remains very weakly bound to its parent atom. This is because the four electrons participating in bonding are seen as part of the effective core of the atom by the fifth electron. As a result the ionisation energy required to set this electron free is very small and even at room temperature it will be free to move in the lattice of the semiconductor. For example, the energy required is ~ 0.01 eV for germanium, and 0.05 eV for silicon, to separate this electron from its atom . This is in contrast to the energy required to jump the forbidden band (about 0.72 eV for germanium and about 1.1 eV for silicon) at room temperature in the intrinsic semiconductor. Thus, the pentavalent dopant is donating one extra electron for conduction and hence is known as donor impurity. The number of electrons made available for conduction by dopant atoms depends strongly upon the doping level and is independent of any increase in ambient temperature. On the other hand, the number of free electrons (with an equal number of holes) generated by Si atoms, increases weakly with temperature.','[{"s": "Suppose we dope Si or Ge with a pentavalent element as shown in Fig", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14.7. When an atom of +5 valency element occupies the position of an atom in the crystal lattice of Si, four of its electrons bond with the four silicon neighbours while the fifth remains very weakly bound to its parent atom. This is because the four electrons participating in bonding are seen as part of the effective core of the atom by the fifth electron. As a result the ionisation energy required to set this electron free is very small and even at room temperature it will be free to move in the lattice of the semiconductor. For example, the energy required is ~ 0.01 eV for germanium, and 0.05 eV for silicon, to separate this electron from its atom", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is in contrast to the energy required to jump the forbidden band (about 0.72 eV for germanium and about 1.1 eV for silicon) at room temperature in the intrinsic semiconductor.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus, the pentavalent dopant is donating one extra electron for conduction and hence is known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "donor", "t": "text"}, {"s": "impurity. The number of electrons made available for conduction by dopant atoms depends strongly upon the doping level and is independent of any increase in ambient temperature. On the other hand, the number of free electrons (with an equal number of holes) generated by Si atoms, increases weakly with temperature.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046432"]',NULL),
(30702,74,80,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, with proper level of doping the number of conduction electrons can be made much larger than the number of holes. Hence in an extrinsic semiconductor doped with pentavalent impurity, electrons become the majority carriers and holes the minority carriers . These semiconductors are, therefore, known as n -type semiconductors . For n-type semiconductors, we have,','[{"s": "Thus, with proper level of doping the number of conduction electrons can be made much larger than the number of holes. Hence in an extrinsic semiconductor doped with pentavalent impurity, electrons become the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "majority carriers", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and holes the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "minority carriers", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ". These semiconductors are, therefore, known as n", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "-type semiconductors", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For n-type semiconductors, we have,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046506"]',NULL),
(32719,76,237,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.6 What is the temperature of the steel-copper junction in the steady state of the system shown in Fig. 2 –1 K –1 –1 K –1 ).','[{"s": "Example 11.6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What is the temperature of the steel-copper junction in the steady state of the system shown in Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038434", "2000110292"]',NULL),
(30705,74,83,'paragraph',NULL,'This is obtained when Si or Ge is doped with a trivalent impurity like Al, B, In, etc. The dopant has one valence electron less than Si or Ge and, therefore, this atom can form covalent bonds with neighbouring three Si atoms but does not have any electron to offer to the fourth Si atom. So the bond between the fourth neighbour and the trivalent atom has a vacancy or hole as shown in Fig. 14.8. Since the neighbouring Si atom in the lattice wants an electron in place of a hole, an electron in the outer orbit of an atom in the neighbourhood may jump to fill this vacancy, leaving a vacancy or hole at its own site. Thus the hole is available for conduction. Note that the trivalent foreign atom becomes effectively negatively charged when it shares fourth electron with neighbouring Si atom. Therefore, the dopant atom of p-type material can be treated as core of one negative charge along with its associated hole as shown in Fig. 14.8(b). It is obvious that one acceptor atom gives one hole . These holes are in addition to the intrinsically generated holes while the source of conduction electrons is only intrinsic generation. Thus, for such a material, the holes are the majority carriers and electrons are minority carriers. Therefore, extrinsic semiconductors doped with trivalent impurity are called p -type semiconductors . For p-type semiconductors, the recombination process will reduce the number ( n i )of intrinsically generated electrons to n e . We have, for p-type semiconductors','[{"s": "This is obtained when Si or Ge is doped with a trivalent impurity like Al, B, In, etc.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The dopant has one valence electron less than Si or Ge and, therefore, this atom can form covalent bonds with neighbouring three Si atoms but does not have any electron to offer to the fourth Si atom. So the bond between the fourth neighbour and the trivalent atom has a vacancy or hole as shown in Fig. 14.8. Since the neighbouring Si atom in the lattice wants an electron in place of a hole, an electron in the outer orbit of an atom in the neighbourhood may jump to fill this vacancy, leaving a vacancy or hole at its own site. Thus the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hole", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is available for conduction. Note that the trivalent foreign atom becomes effectively negatively charged when it shares fourth electron with neighbouring Si atom. Therefore, the dopant atom of p-type material can be treated as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "core of one negative charge", "t": "text"}, {"s": "along with its associated hole as shown in Fig. 14.8(b). It is obvious that one", "t": "text"}, {"s": "acceptor", "t": "text"}, {"s": "atom gives one", "t": "text"}, {"s": "hole", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". These holes are in addition to the intrinsically generated holes while the source of conduction electrons is only intrinsic generation. Thus, for such a material, the holes are the majority carriers and electrons are minority carriers. Therefore, extrinsic semiconductors doped with trivalent impurity are called p", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-type semiconductors", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". For p-type semiconductors, the recombination process will reduce the number (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ")of intrinsically generated electrons to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". We have, for p-type semiconductors", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046512"]',NULL),
(30725,74,103,'paragraph',NULL,'Two important processes occur during the formation of a p-n junction: diffusion and drift . We know that in an n-type semiconductor, the concentration of electrons (number of electrons per unit volume) is more compared to the concentration of holes. Similarly, in a p-type semiconductor, the concentration of holes is more than the concentration of electrons. During the formation of p-n junction, and due to the concentration gradient across p-, and n- sides, holes diffuse from p-side to n-side (p → n) and electrons diffuse from n-side to p-side (n → p). This motion of charge carries gives rise to diffusion current across the junction.','[{"s": "Two important processes occur during the formation of a p-n junction:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "diffusion", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "drift", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "We know that in an n-type semiconductor, the concentration of electrons (number of electrons per unit volume) is more compared to the concentration of holes. Similarly, in a p-type semiconductor, the concentration of holes is more than the concentration of electrons. During the formation of p-n junction, and due to the concentration gradient across p-, and n- sides, holes diffuse from p-side to n-side (p → n) and electrons diffuse from n-side to p-side (n → p). This motion of charge carries gives rise to diffusion current across the junction.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046518"]',NULL),
(30730,74,108,'paragraph',NULL,'Initially, diffusion current is large and drift current is small. As the diffusion process continues, the space-charge regions on either side of the junction extend, thus increasing the electric field strength and hence drift current. This process continues until the diffusion current equals the drift current. Thus a p-n junction is formed. In a p-n junction under equilibrium there is no net current.','[{"s": "Initially, diffusion current is large and drift current is small. As the diffusion process continues, the space-charge regions on either side of the junction extend, thus increasing the electric field strength and hence drift current. This process continues until the diffusion current equals the drift current. Thus a p-n junction is formed.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In a p-n junction under equilibrium there is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "no net", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "current.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000046532"]',NULL),
(30731,74,109,'paragraph',NULL,'The loss of electrons from the n-region and the gain of electron by the p-region causes a difference of potential across the junction of the two regions. The polarity of this potential is such as to oppose further flow of carriers so that a condition of equilibrium exists. Figure 14.11 shows the p-n junction at equilibrium and the potential across the junction. The n-material has lost electrons, and p material has acquired electrons. The n material is thus positive relative to the p material. Since this potential tends to prevent the movement of electron from the n region into the p region, it is often called a barrier potential .','[{"s": "The loss of electrons from the n-region and the gain of electron by the p-region causes a difference of potential across the junction of the two regions. The polarity of this potential is such as to oppose further flow of carriers so that a condition of equilibrium exists. Figure 14.11 shows the p-n junction at equilibrium and the potential across the junction.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The n-material has lost electrons, and p material has acquired electrons.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The n material is thus positive relative to the p material.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Since this potential tends to prevent the movement of electron from the n region into the p region, it is often called a", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "barrier potential", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000046538"]',NULL),
(30738,74,116,'paragraph',NULL,'The direction of arrow indicates the conventional direction of current (when the diode is under forward bias). The equilibrium barrier potential can be altered by applying an external voltage V across the diode. The situation of p-n junction diode under equilibrium (without bias) is shown in Fig. 14.11(a) and (b).','[{"s": "The direction of arrow indicates the conventional direction of current (when the diode is under forward bias).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The equilibrium barrier potential can be altered by applying an external voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "across the diode. The situation of p-n junction diode under equilibrium (without bias) is shown in Fig. 14.11(a) and (b).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046866"]',NULL),
(30741,74,119,'paragraph',NULL,'The applied voltage mostly drops across the depletion region and the voltage drop across the p-side and n-side of the junction is negligible. (This is because the resistance of the depletion region – a region where there are no charges – is very high compared to the resistance of n-side and p-side.) The direction of the applied voltage ( V ) is opposite to the built-in potential V 0 . As a result, the depletion layer width decreases and the barrier height is reduced [Fig. 14.13(b)]. The effective barrier height under forward bias is ( V 0 – V ).','[{"s": "The applied voltage mostly drops across the depletion region and the voltage drop across the p-side and n-side of the junction is negligible. (This is because the resistance of the depletion region – a region where there are no charges – is very high compared to the resistance of n-side and p-side.) The direction of the applied voltage (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") is opposite to the built-in potential", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". As a result, the depletion layer width decreases and the barrier height is reduced [Fig. 14.13(b)].", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The effective barrier height under forward bias is (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "0", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ").", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000046945"]',NULL),
(30745,74,123,'paragraph',NULL,'Due to the applied voltage, electrons from n-side cross the depletion region and reach p-side (where they are minority carries). Similarly, holes from p-side cross the junction and reach the n-side (where they are minority carries). This process under forward bias is known as minority carrier injection. At the junction boundary, on each side, the minority carrier concentration increases significantly compared to the locations far from the junction.','[{"s": "Due to the applied voltage, electrons from n-side cross the depletion region and reach p-side (where they are minority carries).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Similarly, holes from p-side cross the junction and reach the n-side (where they are minority carries). This process under forward bias is known as minority carrier injection. At the junction boundary, on each side, the minority carrier concentration increases significantly compared to the locations far from the junction.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046962"]',NULL),
(30751,74,129,'paragraph',NULL,'When an external voltage ( V ) is applied across the diode such that n-side is positive and p-side is negative, it is said to be reverse biased [Fig.14.15(a)]. The applied voltage mostly drops across the depletion region. The direction of applied voltage is same as the direction of barrier potential. As a result, the barrier height increases and the depletion region widens due to the change in the electric field. The effective barrier height under reverse bias is ( V 0 + V ), [Fig. 14.15(b)]. This suppresses the flow of electrons from n → p and holes from p → n. Thus, diffusion current, decreases enormously compared to the diode under forward bias.','[{"s": "When an external voltage (", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") is applied across the diode such that n-side is positive and p-side is negative, it is said to be", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "reverse biased", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "[Fig.14.15(a)].", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The applied voltage mostly drops across the depletion region. The direction of applied voltage is same as the direction of barrier potential.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "As a result, the barrier height increases and the depletion region widens due to the change in the electric field.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The effective barrier height under reverse bias is (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "), [Fig. 14.15(b)]. This suppresses the flow of electrons from n → p and holes from p → n. Thus, diffusion current, decreases enormously compared to the diode under forward bias.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046963"]',NULL),
(30762,74,140,'paragraph',NULL,'The above discussion shows that the p-n junction diode primerly allows the flow of current only in one direction (forward bias). The forward bias resistance is low as compared to the reverse bias resistance. This property is used for rectification of ac voltages as discussed in the next section. For diodes, we define a quantity called dynamic resistance as the ratio of small change in voltage ∆V to a small change in current ∆I:','[{"s": "The above discussion shows that the p-n junction diode primerly allows the flow of current only in one direction (forward bias).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The forward bias resistance is low as compared to the reverse bias resistance. This property is used for rectification of ac voltages as discussed in the next section. For diodes, we define a quantity called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dynamic resistance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "as the ratio of small change in voltage ∆V to a small change in current ∆I:", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046981"]',NULL),
(31000,74,378,'paragraph',NULL,'A gate is a digital circuit that follows curtain logical relationship between the input and output voltages. Therefore, they are generally known as logic gates — gates because they control the flow of information. The five common logic gates used are NOT, AND, OR, NAND, NOR. Each logic gate is indicated by a symbol and its function is defined by a truth table that shows all the possible input logic level combinations with their respective output logic levels. Truth tables help understand the behaviour of logic gates. These logic gates can be realised using semiconductor devices.','[{"s": "A gate is a digital circuit that follows curtain", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "logical", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "relationship between the input and output voltages.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Therefore, they are generally known as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "logic gates", "t": "text"}, {"s": "— gates because they control the flow of information. The five common logic gates used are NOT, AND, OR, NAND, NOR. Each logic gate is indicated by a symbol and its function is defined by a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "truth table", "t": "text"}, {"s": "that shows all the possible input logic level combinations with their respective output logic levels. Truth tables help understand the behaviour of logic gates. These logic gates can be realised using semiconductor devices.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000046984", "2000112941", "2000112961", "2000113020"]',NULL),
(30777,74,155,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.18 (a) Half-wave rectifier circuit, (b) Input ac voltage and output voltage waveforms from the rectifier circuit.','[{"s": "Figure 14.18 (a) Half-wave rectifier circuit, (b) Input ac voltage and output voltage waveforms from the rectifier circuit.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000046989"]',NULL),
(30778,74,156,'paragraph',NULL,'Therefore, in the positive half-cycle of ac there is a current through the load resistor R L and we get an output voltage, as shown in Fig. 14.18(b), whereas there is no current in the negative half-cycle. In the next positive half-cycle, again we get the output voltage. Thus, the output voltage, though still varying, is restricted to only one direction and is said to be rectified . Since the rectified output of this circuit is only for half of the input ac wave it is called as half-wave rectifier .','[{"s": "Therefore, in the positive", "t": "text"}, {"s": "half-cycle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of ac there is a current through the load resistor", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and we get an output voltage, as shown in Fig. 14.18(b), whereas there is no current in the negative half-cycle. In the next positive half-cycle, again we get the output voltage. Thus, the output voltage, though still varying, is restricted to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "only one direction", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and is said to be", "t": "text"}, {"s": "rectified", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Since the rectified output of this circuit is only for half of the input ac wave it is called as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "half-wave rectifier", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000047108"]',NULL),
(33076,77,55,'paragraph',NULL,'The general principle of conservation of energy then implies that 7 (their are 7 questions first 3 in first line and next 4 in second line)','[{"s": "The general principle of conservation", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "of energy then implies that", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "7", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(their are 7 questions first 3 in first line and next 4 in second line)", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038148", "2000038290", "2000110300", "2000110352"]',NULL),
(30782,74,160,'paragraph',NULL,'The rectified voltage is in the form of pulses of the shape of half sinusoids. Though it is unidirectional it does not have a steady value. To get steady dc output from the pulsating voltage normally a capacitor is connected across the output terminals (parallel to the load R L ). One can also use an inductor in series with R L for the same purpose. Since these additional circuits appear to filter out the ac ripple and give a pure dc voltage, so they are called filters.','[{"s": "The rectified voltage is in the form of pulses of the shape of half sinusoids. Though it is unidirectional it does not have a steady value. To get steady dc output from the pulsating voltage normally a capacitor is connected across the output terminals (parallel to the load", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "). One can also use an inductor in series with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "for the same purpose.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Since these additional circuits appear to", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "filter", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "out the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "ac ripple", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and give a", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "pure dc", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "voltage, so they are called filters.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000113029"]',NULL),
(30790,74,168,'paragraph',NULL,'It is a special purpose semiconductor diode, named after its inventor C. Zener. It is designed to operate under reverse bias in the breakdown region and used as a voltage regulator . The symbol for Zener diode is shown in Fig. 14.21(a).','[{"s": "It is a special purpose semiconductor diode, named after its inventor C. Zener. It is designed to operate under reverse bias in the breakdown region and used as a voltage regulator", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The symbol for Zener diode is shown in Fig. 14.21(a).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000113032"]',NULL),
(30791,74,169,'paragraph',NULL,'Zener diode is fabricated by heavily doping both p-, and n- sides of the junction. Due to this, depletion region formed is very thin (<10 –6 m) and the electric field of the junction is extremely high (~5×10 6 V/m) even for a small reverse bias voltage of about 5V. The I-V characteristics of a Zener diode is shown in Fig. 14.21(b). It is seen that when the applied reverse bias voltage( V ) reaches the breakdown voltage ( V z ) of the Zener diode, there is a large change in the current. Note that after the breakdown voltage V z , a large change in the current can be produced by almost insignificant change in the reverse bias voltage. In other words, Zener voltage remains constant, even though current through the Zener diode varies over a wide range. This property of the Zener diode is used for regulating supply voltages so that they are constant.','[{"s": "Zener diode is fabricated by heavily doping both p-, and n- sides of the junction.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Due to this, depletion region formed is very thin (<10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "m) and the electric field of the junction is extremely high (~5×10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "V/m) even for a small reverse bias voltage of about 5V.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The I-V characteristics of a Zener diode is shown in Fig. 14.21(b). It is seen that when the applied reverse bias voltage(", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") reaches the breakdown voltage (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of the Zener diode, there is a large change in the current. Note that after the breakdown voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "z", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", a large change in the current can be produced by almost insignificant change in the reverse bias voltage. In other words, Zener voltage remains constant, even though current through the Zener diode varies over a wide range. This property of the Zener diode is used for regulating supply voltages so that they are constant.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000113033"]',NULL),
(30810,74,188,'paragraph',NULL,'A Photodiode is again a special purpose p-n junction diode fabricated with a transparent window to allow light to fall on the diode. It is operated under reverse bias. When the photodiode is illuminated with light (photons) with energy ( h ν) greater than the energy gap ( E g ) of the semiconductor, then electron-hole pairs are generated due to the absorption of photons. The diode is fabricated such that the generation of e-h pairs takes place in or near the depletion region of the diode. Due to electric field of the junction, electrons and holes are separated before they recombine. The direction of the electric field is such that electrons reach n-side and holes reach p-side. Electrons are collected on n-side and holes are collected on p-side giving rise to an emf. When an external load is connected, current flows. The magnitude of the photocurrent depends on the intensity of incident light (photocurrent is proportional to incident light intensity).','[{"s": "A Photodiode is again a special purpose p-n junction diode fabricated with a transparent window to allow light to fall on the diode.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It is operated under reverse bias. When the photodiode is illuminated with light (photons) with energy (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ν) greater than the energy gap (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "g", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") of the semiconductor, then electron-hole pairs are generated due to the absorption of photons. The diode is fabricated such that the generation of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "e-h", "t": "text"}, {"s": "pairs takes place in or near the depletion region of the diode. Due to electric field of the junction, electrons and holes are separated before they recombine. The direction of the electric field is such that electrons reach n-side and holes reach p-side. Electrons are collected on n-side and holes are collected on p-side giving rise to an emf. When an external load is connected, current flows.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The magnitude of the photocurrent depends on the intensity of incident light (photocurrent is proportional to incident light intensity).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000112898"]',NULL),
(30858,74,236,'paragraph',NULL,'• Base : This is the central segment . It is very thin and lightly doped .','[{"s": "•", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Base", "t": "text"}, {"s": ": This is the central segment", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is very thin", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "lightly doped", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000112902"]',NULL),
(30863,74,241,'paragraph',NULL,'Let us see now the paths of current carriers in the transistor with emitter-base junction forward biased and base-collector junction reverse biased. The heavily doped emitter has a high concentration of majority carriers, which will be holes in a p-n-p transistor and electrons in an n-p-n transistor . These majority carriers enter the base region in large numbers. The base is thin and lightly doped. So the majority carriers there would be few. In a p-n-p transistor the majority carriers in the base are electrons since base is of n-type semiconductor. The large number of holes entering the base from the emitter swamps the small number of electrons there. As the base collector-junction is reverse-biased, these holes, which appear as minority carriers at the junction, can easily cross the junction and enter the collector. The holes in the base could move either towards the base terminal to combine with the electrons entering from outside or cross the junction to enter into the collector and reach the collector terminal. The base is made thin so that most of the holes find themselves near the reverse-biased base-collector junction and so cross the junction instead of moving to the base terminal.','[{"s": "Let us see now the paths of current carriers in the transistor with emitter-base junction forward biased and base-collector junction reverse biased. The heavily doped emitter has a high concentration of majority carriers, which will be holes in a p-n-p transistor and electrons in an n-p-n transistor", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "These majority carriers enter the base region in large numbers.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The base is thin and lightly doped. So the majority carriers there would be few. In a p-n-p transistor the majority carriers in the base are electrons since base is of n-type semiconductor. The large number of holes entering the base from the emitter swamps the small number of electrons there. As the base collector-junction is reverse-biased, these holes, which appear as minority carriers at the junction, can easily cross the junction and enter the collector. The holes in the base could move either towards the base terminal to combine with the electrons entering from outside or cross the junction to enter into the collector and reach the collector terminal. The base is made thin so that most of the holes find themselves near the reverse-biased base-collector junction and so cross the junction instead of moving to the base terminal.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000112907"]',NULL),
(30867,74,245,'paragraph',NULL,'The current entering into the emitter from outside is equal to the emitter current I E . Similarly the current emerging from the base terminal is I B and that from collector terminal is I C . It is obvious from the above description and also from a straight forward application of Kirchhoff’s law to Fig. 14.28(a) that the emitter current is the sum of collector current and base current:','[{"s": "The current entering into the emitter from outside is equal to the emitter current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Similarly the current emerging from the base terminal is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and that from collector terminal is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is obvious from the above description and also from a straight forward application of Kirchhoff’s law to Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "14.28(a) that the emitter current is the sum of collector current and base current:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000112929"]',NULL),
(30894,74,272,'paragraph',NULL,'This is also known as small signal current gain and its value is very large.','[{"s": "This is also known as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "small signal current gain", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and its value is very large.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000112932"]',NULL),
(30911,74,289,'paragraph',NULL,'The transistor can be used as a device application depending on the configuration used (namely CB, CC and CE), the biasing of the E-B and B-C junction and the operation region namely cutoff, active region and saturation. As mentioned earlier we have confined only to the CE configuration and will be concentrating on the biasing and the operation region to understand the working of a device.','[{"s": "The transistor can be used as a device application depending on the configuration used (namely CB, CC and CE), the biasing of the E-B and B-C junction and the operation region namely cutoff, active region and saturation.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "As mentioned earlier we have confined only to the CE configuration and will be concentrating on the biasing and the operation region to understand the working of a device.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000112934"]',NULL),
(30926,74,304,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 14.31 (a) Base-biased transistor in CE configuration, (b) Transfer characteristic.','[{"s": "Figure 14.31 (a) Base-biased transistor in CE configuration, (b) Transfer characteristic.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000112937"]',NULL),
(30940,74,318,'paragraph',NULL,'where β ac is equal to ∆ I C /∆ I B from Eq. (14.10). Thus the linear portion of the active region of the transistor can be exploited for the use in amplifiers. Transistor as an amplifier (CE configuration) is discussed in detail in the next section.','[{"s": "where β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "ac", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is equal to ∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/∆", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from Eq.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(14.10).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus the linear portion of the active region of the transistor can be exploited for the use in amplifiers. Transistor as an amplifier (CE configuration) is discussed in detail in the next section.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000112950"]',NULL),
(30942,74,320,'paragraph',NULL,'To operate the transistor as an amplifier it is necessary to fix its operating point somewhere in the middle of its active region . If we fix the value of V BB corresponding to a point in the middle of the linear part of the transfer curve then the dc base current I B would be constant and corresponding collector current I C will also be constant. The dc voltage V CE = V CC - I C R C would also remain constant. The operating values of V CE and I B determine the operating point, of the amplifier.','[{"s": "To operate the transistor as an amplifier it is necessary to fix its operating point somewhere in the middle of its active region", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If we fix the value of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "BB", "t": "text"}, {"s": "corresponding to a point in the middle of the linear part of the transfer curve then the dc base current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "would be constant and corresponding collector current", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "will also be constant. The dc voltage", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CC", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": "would also remain constant. The operating values of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "CE", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "I", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "determine the operating point, of the amplifier.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000112952"]',NULL),
(31004,74,382,'paragraph',NULL,'An OR gate has two or more inputs with one output. The logic symbol and truth table are shown in Fig. 14.29. The output Y is 1 when either input A or input B or both are 1s, that is, if any of the input is high, the output is high.','[{"s": "An", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "OR", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "gate has two or more inputs with one output.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The logic symbol and truth table are shown in Fig. 14.29. The output Y is 1 when either input A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "input B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "both are 1s, that is, if any of the input is high, the output is high.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000112962"]',NULL),
(31015,74,393,'paragraph',NULL,'FIGURE 14.29 (a) Logic symbol (b) Truth table of OR gate.','[{"s": "FIGURE 14.29 (a) Logic symbol (b) Truth table of OR gate.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000112965"]',NULL),
(31875,75,541,'paragraph',NULL,'We define a new parameter characterising the rigid body, called the moment of inertia I , given by 3','[{"s": "We define a new parameter characterising the rigid body, called the moment of inertia I , given by", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000035034", "2000035088", "2000035100", "2000035119", "2000035123", "2000035143", "2000035204", "2000035208", "2000035212", "2000035216", "2000035220", "2000035224", "2000035228", "2000035232", "2000035236", "2000035661", "2000035669", "2000035689", "2000035693", "2000035924", "2000035929", "2000035946", "2000035950", "2000035978", "2000035986", "2000036400", "2000037160"]',NULL),
(31895,75,561,'paragraph',NULL,'As the mass of a body resists a change in its state of linear motion, it is a measure of its inertia in linear motion. Similarly, as the moment of inertia about a given axis of rotation resists a change in its rotational motion, it can be regarded as a measure of rotational inertia of the body; it is a measure of the way in which different parts of the body are distributed at different distances from the axis. Unlike the mass of a body, the moment of inertia is not a fixed quantity but depends on distribution of mass about the axis of rotation, and the orientation and position of the axis of rotation with respect to the body as a whole. As a measure of the way in which the mass of a rotating rigid body is distributed with respect to the axis of rotation, we can define a new parameter, the radius of gyration . It is related to the moment of inertia and the total mass of the body.','[{"s": "As the mass of a body resists a change in its state of linear motion, it is a measure of its inertia in linear motion. Similarly, as the moment of inertia about a given axis of rotation resists a change in its rotational motion, it can be regarded as a measure of rotational inertia of the body; it is a measure of the way in which different parts of the body are distributed at different distances from the axis. Unlike the mass of a body, the moment of inertia is not a fixed quantity but depends on distribution of mass about the axis of rotation, and the orientation and position of the axis of rotation with respect to the body as a whole.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "As a measure of the way in which the mass of a rotating rigid body is distributed with respect to the axis of rotation, we can define a new parameter, the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "radius of gyration", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "It is related to the moment of inertia and the total mass of the body.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000035038", "2000035042", "2000035046", "2000035053", "2000035067", "2000035072", "2000035164", "2000035168", "2000035172", "2000035261", "2000035293", "2000035297", "2000035301", "2000035305", "2000035636", "2000035640", "2000035657", "2000035673", "2000035685", "2000035954", "2000035958", "2000035962", "2000035966", "2000035970", "2000035974", "2000036344", "2000036392", "2000036396", "2000037196"]',NULL),
(31393,75,59,'paragraph',NULL,'Let the distances of the two particles be x 1 and x 2 respectively from some origin O. Let m 1 and m 2 be respectively the masses of the two particles. The centre of mass of the system is that point C which is at a distance X from O, where X is given by 3','[{"s": "Let the distances of the two particles be x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "respectively from some origin O. Let m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "be respectively the masses of the two particles.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The centre of mass of the system is that point C which is at a distance X from O, where X is given by", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000035049", "2000035080", "2000035096", "2000035180", "2000035184", "2000035344", "2000035348", "2000035352", "2000035934", "2000035982"]',NULL),
(31731,75,397,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, if the total external torque on a system of particles is zero, then the total angular momentum of the system is conserved, i.e. 9 remains constant. Eq. (7.29a) is equivalent to three scalar equations,','[{"s": "Thus, if the total external torque on a system of particles is zero, then the total angular momentum of the system is conserved, i.e.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "9", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "remains constant. Eq. (7.29a) is equivalent to three scalar equations,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000035058", "2000035084", "2000035092", "2000035151", "2000035249", "2000035253", "2000035265", "2000035644", "2000035648", "2000035652", "2000035677", "2000035681", "2000036324", "2000037156"]',NULL),
(32139,75,805,'paragraph',NULL,'This then is the required form, for fixed axis rotation, of Eq. (7.29a), which expresses the general law of conservation of angular momentum of a system of particles. Eq. (7.46) applies to many situations that we come across in daily life. You may do this experiment with your friend. Sit on a swivel chair (a chair with a seat, free to rotate about a pivot) with your arms folded and feet not resting on, i.e., away from, the ground. Ask your friend to rotate the chair rapidly. While the chair is rotating with considerable angular speed stretch your arms horizontally. What happens? Your angular speed is reduced. If you bring back your arms closer to your body, the angular speed increases again. This is a situation where the principle of conservation of angular momentum is applicable. If friction in the rotational mechanism is neglected, there is no external torque about the axis of rotation of the chair and hence Iω is constant. Stretching the arms increases I about the axis of rotation, resulting in decreasing the angular speed ω. Bringing the arms closer to the body has the opposite effect.','[{"s": "This then is the required form, for fixed axis rotation, of Eq.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(7.29a), which expresses the general law of conservation of angular momentum of a system of particles.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Eq. (7.46) applies to many situations that we come across in daily life. You may do this experiment with your friend. Sit on a swivel chair (a chair with a seat, free to rotate about a pivot) with your arms folded and feet not resting on, i.e., away from, the ground. Ask your friend to rotate the chair rapidly. While the chair is rotating with considerable angular speed stretch your arms horizontally. What happens? Your angular speed is reduced. If you bring back your arms closer to your body, the angular speed increases again. This is a situation where the principle of conservation of angular momentum is applicable. If friction in the rotational mechanism is neglected, there is no external torque about the axis of rotation of the chair and hence Iω is constant. Stretching the arms increases I about the axis of rotation, resulting in decreasing the angular speed ω. Bringing the arms closer to the body has the opposite effect.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000035063", "2000035127", "2000035131", "2000035632", "2000035665", "2000035938", "2000035942"]',NULL),
(31638,75,304,'paragraph',NULL,'The rotational analogue of force in linear motion is moment of force . It is also referred to as torque or couple . (We shall use the words moment of force and torque interchangeably.) We shall first define the moment of force for the special case of a single particle. Later on we shall extend the concept to systems of particles including rigid bodies. We shall also relate it to a change in the state of rotational motion, i.e. is angular acceleration of a rigid body.','[{"s": "The rotational analogue of force in linear motion is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "moment of force", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". It is also referred to as", "t": "text"}, {"s": "torque", "t": "text"}, {"s": "or", "t": "text"}, {"s": "couple", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(We shall use the words moment of force and torque interchangeably.)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "We shall first define the moment of force for the special case of a single particle. Later on we shall extend the concept to systems of particles including rigid bodies. We shall also relate it to a change in the state of rotational motion, i.e. is angular acceleration of a rigid body.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000035076", "2000035155", "2000035309", "2000035701", "2000035713", "2000035919", "2000037240"]',NULL),
(31641,75,307,'paragraph',NULL,'If a force acts on a single particle at a point P whose position with respect to the origin O is given by the position vector r (Fig. 7.18), the moment of the force acting on the particle with respect to the origin O is defined as the vector product','[{"s": "If a force acts on a single particle at a point P whose position with respect to the origin O is given by the position vector", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "r", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(Fig. 7.18), the moment of the force acting on the particle with respect to the origin O is defined as the vector product", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000036384"]',NULL),
(32606,76,124,'paragraph',NULL,'A system is said to be isolated if no exchange or transfer of heat occurs between the system and its surroundings. When different parts of an isolated system are at different temperature, a quantity of heat transfers from the part at higher temperature to the part at lower temperature. The heat lost by the part at higher temperature is equal to the heat gained by the part at lower temperature.','[{"s": "A system is said to be isolated if no exchange or transfer of heat occurs between the system and its surroundings.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When different parts of an isolated system are at different temperature, a quantity of heat transfers from the part at higher temperature to the part at lower temperature.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The heat lost by the part at higher temperature is equal to the heat gained by the part at lower temperature.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037566", "2000037569", "2000037577", "2000037621", "2000037637", "2000037641", "2000037645", "2000037649", "2000037653", "2000037657", "2000037713", "2000037726", "2000037734", "2000037750", "2000037762", "2000037767", "2000037770", "2000037963", "2000038107", "2000038111", "2000038115", "2000038199", "2000038202", "2000038206", "2000038210", "2000038215", "2000038218", "2000038223", "2000038231", "2000038234", "2000038238", "2000110146", "2000110147", "2000110148", "2000110150", "2000110153", "2000110156", "2000110157", "2000110160", "2000110162", "2000110165", "2000110168", "2000110169", "2000110174", "2000110180", "2000110183", "2000110184", "2000110198", "2000110209", "2000110291", "2000110293", "2000110295"]',NULL),
(32538,76,56,'paragraph',NULL,'Similarly, we consider the fractional change in volume , Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image70.png , of a substance for temperature change ∆T and define the coefficient of volume expansion (or volume expansivity), Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image72.png as','[{"s": "Similarly, we consider the fractional change in volume", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ",", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image70.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ", of a substance for temperature change ∆T and define the coefficient of volume expansion (or volume expansivity),", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thermal_Properties_Of_Matter-image72.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "as", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037558", "2000037573", "2000037581", "2000037593", "2000037882", "2000037886", "2000037890", "2000037894", "2000037898", "2000037902", "2000037906", "2000038175", "2000038179", "2000038183", "2000038186", "2000038190", "2000038470", "2000110149", "2000110152", "2000110164", "2000110166", "2000110170", "2000110172", "2000110178", "2000110181", "2000110182", "2000110185", "2000110186", "2000110188", "2000110189", "2000110190", "2000110191", "2000110195", "2000110196", "2000110197", "2000110203", "2000110204", "2000110205", "2000110211", "2000110288", "2000110298"]',NULL),
(32557,76,75,'paragraph',NULL,'There is a simple relation between the coefficient of volume expansion (αv) and coefficient of linear expansion (αl). Imagine a cube of length, l, that expands equally in all directions, when its temperature increases by ∆T . We have','[{"s": "There is a simple relation between the coefficient of volume expansion (αv) and coefficient of linear expansion (αl).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Imagine a cube of length, l, that expands equally in all directions, when its temperature increases by ∆T", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We have", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037601", "2000110192"]',NULL),
(32578,76,96,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.2 A blacksmith fixes an iron ring on the rim of the wooden wheel of a horse cart . The diameter of the rim and the iron ring are 5.243 m and 5.231 m, respectively at 27 °C. To what temperature should the ring be heated so as to fit the rim of the wheel?','[{"s": "Example 11.2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A blacksmith fixes an iron ring on the rim of the wooden wheel of a horse cart", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The diameter of the rim and the iron ring are 5.243 m and 5.231 m, respectively at 27 °C. To what temperature should the ring be heated so as to fit the rim of the wheel?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037954", "2000110193"]',NULL),
(32607,76,125,'paragraph',NULL,'Calorimetry means measurement of heat. When a body at higher temperature is brought in contact with another body at lower temperature, the heat lost by the hot body is equal to the heat gained by the colder body, provided no heat is allowed to escape to the surroundings . A device in which heat measurement can be done is called a calorimeter. It consists of a metallic vessel and stirrer of the same material, like copper or aluminium. The vessel is kept inside a wooden jacket, which contains heat insulating material, like glass wool etc. The outer jacket acts as a heat shield and reduces the heat loss from the inner vessel. There is an opening in the outer jacket through which a mercury thermometer can be inserted into the calorimeter (Fig. 11.20). The following example provides a method by which the specific heat capacity of a given solid can be determinated by using the principle, heat gained is equal to the heat lost.','[{"s": "Calorimetry means measurement of heat.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "When a body at higher temperature is brought in contact with another body at lower temperature, the heat lost by the hot body is equal to the heat gained by the colder body, provided no heat is allowed to escape to the surroundings", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A device in which heat measurement can be done is called a calorimeter. It consists of a metallic vessel and stirrer of the same material, like copper or aluminium. The vessel is kept inside a wooden jacket, which contains heat insulating material, like glass wool etc. The outer jacket acts as a heat shield and reduces the heat loss from the inner vessel. There is an opening in the outer jacket through which a mercury thermometer can be inserted into the calorimeter (Fig. 11.20). The following example provides a method by which the specific heat capacity of a given solid can be determinated by using the principle, heat gained is equal to the heat lost.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037967", "2000038426", "2000038454", "2000038903", "2000110199", "2000110200", "2000110201", "2000110206", "2000110289"]',NULL),
(32638,76,156,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.9 A plot of temperature versus time showing the changes in the state of ice on heating (not to scale).','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "11.9 A plot of temperature versus time showing the changes in the state of ice on heating (not to scale).", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000037971", "2000110202"]',NULL),
(32666,76,184,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 11.4 When 0.15 kg of ice at 0 °C is mixed with 0.30 kg of water at 50 °C in a container, the resulting temperature is 6.7 °C. Calculate the heat of fusion of ice. (swater = 4186 J kg–1 K–1)','[{"s": "Example 11.4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When 0.15 kg of ice at 0 °C is mixed with 0.30 kg of water at 50 °C in a container, the resulting temperature is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "6.7 °C. Calculate the heat of fusion of ice. (swater = 4186 J kg–1 K–1)", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037975", "2000110207"]',NULL),
(32713,76,231,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 11.14 Steady state heat flow by conduction in a bar with its two ends maintained at temperatures TC and TD; (TC > TD).3','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "11.14 Steady state heat flow by conduction in a bar with its two ends maintained at temperatures TC and TD; (TC > TD).3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000038170", "2000110210"]',NULL),
(32755,76,273,'paragraph',NULL,'Convection is a mode of heat transfer by actual motion of matter. It is possible only in fluids. Convection can be natural or forced. In natural convection, gravity plays an important part. When a fluid is heated from below, the hot part expands and, therefore, becomes less dense. Because of buoyancy, it rises and the upper colder part replaces it. This again gets heated, rises up and is replaced by the relatively colder part of the fluid. The process goes on. This mode of heat transfer is evidently different from conduction. Convection involves bulk transport of different parts of the fluid.','[{"s": "Convection is a mode of heat transfer by actual motion of matter. It is possible only in fluids. Convection can be natural or forced.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In natural convection, gravity plays an important part.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "When a fluid is heated from below, the hot part expands and, therefore, becomes less dense. Because of buoyancy, it rises and the upper colder part replaces it. This again gets heated, rises up and is replaced by the relatively colder part of the fluid. The process goes on. This mode of heat transfer is evidently different from conduction. Convection involves bulk transport of different parts of the fluid.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038450", "2000110294"]',NULL),
(32764,76,282,'paragraph',NULL,'We find that black bodies absorb and emit radiant energy better than bodies of lighter colours . This fact finds many applications in our daily life. We wear white or light coloured clothes in summer, so that they absorb the least heat from the Sun. However, during winter, we use dark coloured clothes, which absorb heat from the sun and keep our body warm. The bottoms of utensils for cooking food are blackened so that they absorb maximum heat from fire and transfer it to the vegetables to be cooked.','[{"s": "We find that black bodies absorb and emit radiant energy better than bodies of lighter colours", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "This fact finds many applications in our daily life. We wear white or light coloured clothes in summer, so that they absorb the least heat from the Sun. However, during winter, we use dark coloured clothes, which absorb heat from the sun and keep our body warm. The bottoms of utensils for cooking food are blackened so that they absorb maximum heat from fire and transfer it to the vegetables to be cooked.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000110145", "2000110299"]',NULL),
(32774,76,292,'paragraph',NULL,'Energy can be transferred by radiation over large distances, without a medium (i.e., in vacuum). The total electromagnetic energy radiated by a body at absolute temperature T is proportional to its size, its ability to radiate (called emissivity) and most importantly to its temperature. For a body, which is a perfect radiator, the energy emitted per unit time (H) is given by 6','[{"s": "Energy can be transferred by radiation over large distances, without a medium (i.e., in vacuum). The total electromagnetic energy radiated by a body at absolute temperature T is proportional to its size, its ability to radiate (called emissivity) and most importantly to its temperature.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For a body, which is a perfect radiator, the energy emitted per unit time (H) is given by", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000110151", "2000110213"]',NULL),
(32776,76,294,'paragraph',NULL,'where A is the area and T is the absolute temperature of the body. This relation obtained experimentally by Stefan and later proved theoretically by Boltzmann is known as Stefan-Boltzmann law and the constant σ is called Stefan-Boltzmann constant. Its value in SI units is 5.67 × 10–8 W m–2 K–4. Most bodies emit only a fraction of the rate given by Eq. 11.16. A substance like lamp black comes close to the limit. One, therefore, defines a dimensionless fraction e called emissivity and writes,','[{"s": "where A is the area and T is the absolute temperature of the body.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This relation obtained experimentally by Stefan and later proved theoretically by Boltzmann is known as Stefan-Boltzmann law and the constant σ is called Stefan-Boltzmann constant. Its value in SI units is 5.67 × 10–8 W m–2 K–4.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Most bodies emit only a fraction of the rate given by Eq. 11.16. A substance like lamp black comes close to the limit. One, therefore, defines a dimensionless fraction e called emissivity and writes,", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000110154"]',NULL),
(32780,76,298,'paragraph',NULL,'For a body with emissivity e, the relation modifies to 2','[{"s": "For a body with emissivity e, the relation modifies to", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000110163"]',NULL),
(32795,76,313,'paragraph',NULL,'The above activity shows that a hot body loses heat to its surroundings in the form of heat radiation. The rate of loss of heat depends on the difference in temperature between the body and its surroundings. Newton was the first to study, in a systematic manner, the relation between the heat lost by a body in a given enclosure and its temperature.','[{"s": "The above activity shows that a hot body loses heat to its surroundings in the form of heat radiation. The rate of loss of heat depends on the difference in temperature between the body and its surroundings.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Newton was the first to study, in a systematic manner, the relation between the heat lost by a body in a given enclosure and its temperature.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000110167"]',NULL),
(32796,76,314,'paragraph',NULL,'According to Newton’s law of cooling, the rate of loss of heat, – dQ/dt of the body is directly proportional to the difference of temperature ∆T = (T2–T1) of the body and the surroundings. The law holds good only for small difference of temperature. Also, the loss of heat by radiation depends upon the nature of the surface of the body and the area of the exposed surface. We can write','[{"s": "According to Newton’s law of cooling, the rate of loss of heat, – dQ/dt of the body is directly proportional to the difference of temperature ∆T = (T2–T1) of the body and the surroundings. The law holds good only for small difference of temperature. Also, the loss of heat by radiation depends upon the nature of the surface of the body and the area of the exposed surface.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "We can write", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000110173"]',NULL),
(32850,76,368,'paragraph',NULL,'11. Newton’s Law of Cooling says that the rate of cooling of a body is proportional to the excess temperature of the body over the surroundings :','[{"s": "11.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Newton’s Law of Cooling says that the rate of cooling of a body is proportional to the excess temperature of the body over the surroundings :", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000110175"]',NULL),
(32886,76,404,'paragraph',NULL,'11.11 The coefficient of volume expansion of glycerine is 49×(10) −5 K −1 . What is the fractional change in its density for a 30 °C rise in temperature?','[{"s": "11.11", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The coefficient of volume expansion of glycerine is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "49×(10)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "−5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "K", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "−1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "What is the fractional change in its density for a 30 °C rise in temperature?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000110177"]',NULL),
(33078,77,57,'paragraph',NULL,'i.e. the energy (∆Q) supplied to the system goes in partly to increase the internal energy of the system (∆U) and the rest in work on the environment (∆W). Equation (12.1) is known as the First Law of Thermodynamics. 2 It is simply the general law of conservation of energy applied to any system in which the energy transfer from or to the surroundings is taken into account.','[{"s": "i.e. the energy (∆Q) supplied to the system goes in partly to increase the internal energy of the system (∆U) and the rest in work on the environment (∆W).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equation (12.1) is known as the First Law of Thermodynamics.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It is simply the general law of conservation of energy applied to any system in which the energy transfer from or to the surroundings is taken into account.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037760", "2000037861", "2000038116", "2000038144", "2000038168", "2000038189", "2000038295", "2000038938", "2000110301", "2000110304", "2000110339", "2000110340", "2000110345", "2000110353", "2000110419", "2000110420", "2000110430"]',NULL),
(33084,77,63,'paragraph',NULL,'If the system is a gas in a cylinder with a movable piston, the gas in moving the piston does work. Since force is pressure times area, and area times displacement is volume, work done by the system against a constant pressure P is','[{"s": "If the system is a gas in a cylinder with a movable piston, the gas in moving the piston does work.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Since force is pressure times area, and area times displacement is volume, work done by the system against a constant pressure P is", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000037776", "2000038298", "2000110302", "2000110346"]',NULL),
(33097,77,76,'paragraph',NULL,'We expect ∆Q and, therefore, heat capacity S to be proportional to the mass of the substance. Further, it could also depend on the temperature, i.e., a different amount of heat may be needed for a unit rise in temperature at different temperatures. To define a constant characteristic of the substance and independent of its amount, we divide S by the mass of the substance m in kg :','[{"s": "We expect ∆Q and, therefore, heat capacity S to be proportional to the mass of the substance. Further, it could also depend on the temperature, i.e., a different amount of heat may be needed for a unit rise in temperature at different temperatures.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "To define a constant characteristic of the substance and independent of its amount, we divide S by the mass of the substance m in kg :", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000037753", "2000038302", "2000110305", "2000110347"]',NULL),
(33116,77,95,'paragraph',NULL,'As already remarked, the specific heat capacity depends on the process or the conditions under which heat capacity transfer takes place. For gases, for example, we can define two specific heats : specific heat capacity at constant volume and specific heat capacity at constant pressure. For an ideal gas, we have a simple relation. 2','[{"s": "As already remarked, the specific heat capacity depends on the process or the conditions under which heat capacity transfer takes place. For gases, for example, we can define two specific heats : specific heat capacity at constant volume and specific heat capacity at constant pressure.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For an ideal gas, we have a simple relation.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000037764", "2000038172", "2000038306", "2000038337", "2000038573", "2000038959", "2000110306", "2000110331", "2000110336", "2000110348", "2000110421"]',NULL),
(33122,77,101,'paragraph',NULL,'where the subscript v is dropped in the last step, since U of an ideal gas depends only on temperature. (The subscript denotes the quantity kept fixed.) If, on the other hand, ∆Q is absorbed at constant pressure,','[{"s": "where the subscript v is dropped in the last step, since U of an ideal gas depends only on temperature. (The subscript denotes the quantity kept fixed.)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If, on the other hand, ∆Q is absorbed at constant pressure,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000037768", "2000038341", "2000110307", "2000110349"]',NULL),
(33145,77,124,'paragraph',NULL,'A process in which the temperature of the system is kept fixed throughout is called an isothermal process. The expansion of a gas in a metallic cylinder placed in a large reservoir of fixed temperature is an example of an isothermal process. (Heat transferred from the reservoir to the system does not materially affect the temperature of the reservoir, because of its very large heat capacity.) In isobaric processes the pressure is constant while in isochoric processes the volume is constant. Finally, if the system is insulated from the surroundings and no heat flows between the system and the surroundings, the process is adiabatic. The definitions of these special processes are summarised in Table. 12.2','[{"s": "A process in which the temperature of the system is kept fixed throughout is called an isothermal process.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The expansion of a gas in a metallic cylinder placed in a large reservoir of fixed temperature is an example of an isothermal process. (Heat transferred from the reservoir to the system does not materially affect the temperature of the reservoir, because of its very large heat capacity.) In isobaric processes the pressure is constant while in isochoric processes the volume is constant. Finally, if the system is insulated from the surroundings and no heat flows between the system and the surroundings, the process is adiabatic. The definitions of these special processes are summarised in Table. 12.2", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037780", "2000038314", "2000110310", "2000110351"]',NULL),
(33151,77,130,'paragraph',NULL,'In an isobaric process, P is fixed. Work done by the gas is','[{"s": "In an isobaric process, P is fixed.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Work done by the gas is", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037816", "2000038331", "2000110311", "2000110354"]',NULL),
(33155,77,134,'paragraph',NULL,'In an isochoric process, V is constant. 2 No work is done on or by the gas. From Eq. (12.1), the heat absorbed by the gas goes entirely to change its internal energy and its temperature. The change in temperature for a given amount of heat is determined by the specific heat of the gas at constant volume.','[{"s": "In an isochoric process, V is constant.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "No work is done on or by the gas. From Eq. (12.1), the heat absorbed by the gas goes entirely to change its internal energy and its temperature. The change in temperature for a given amount of heat is determined by the specific heat of the gas at constant volume.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037833", "2000038345", "2000110313", "2000110355"]',NULL),
(33858,78,419,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.17 Consider a simple pendulum, having a bob attached to a string, that oscillates under the action of the force of gravity. 3 Suppose that the period of oscillation of the simple pendulum depends on its length (l), mass of the bob (m) and acceleration due to gravity (g). Derive the expression for its time period using method of dimensions.','[{"s": "Example 2.17", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Consider a simple pendulum, having a bob attached to a string, that oscillates under the action of the force of gravity.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Suppose that the period of oscillation of the simple pendulum depends on its length (l), mass of the bob (m) and acceleration due to gravity (g). Derive the expression for its time period using method of dimensions.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031051", "2000031138", "2000032104", "2000032154", "2000032191", "2000032798", "2000106039", "2000106057"]',NULL),
(33164,77,143,'paragraph',NULL,'where in the second step we have made use of the ideal gas equation PV = µ RT and taken the constants out of the integral. For an ideal gas, internal energy depends only on temperature. Thus, there is no change in the internal energy of an ideal gas in an isothermal process. The First Law of Thermodynamics then implies that heat supplied to the gas equals the work done by the gas : Q = W. Note from Eq. (12.12) that for V2 > V1, W > 0; and for V2 < V1, W < 0. That is, in an isothermal expansion, the gas absorbs heat and does work while in an isothermal compression, work is done on the gas by the environment and heat is released.','[{"s": "where in the second step we have made use of the ideal gas equation PV = µ RT and taken the constants out of the integral.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For an ideal gas, internal energy depends only on temperature.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Thus, there is no change in the internal energy of an ideal gas in an isothermal process. The First Law of Thermodynamics then implies that heat supplied to the gas equals the work done by the gas : Q = W. Note from Eq. (12.12) that for V2 > V1, W > 0; and for V2 < V1, W < 0. That is, in an isothermal expansion, the gas absorbs heat and does work while in an isothermal compression, work is done on the gas by the environment and heat is released.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037895", "2000038569", "2000110314", "2000110422"]',NULL),
(33166,77,145,'paragraph',NULL,'In a cyclic process, the system returns to its initial state. 2 Since internal energy is a state variable, ∆U = 0 for a cyclic process. From Eq. (12.1), the total heat absorbed equals the work done by the system.','[{"s": "In a cyclic process, the system returns to its initial state.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Since internal energy is a state variable, ∆U = 0 for a cyclic process. From Eq. (12.1), the total heat absorbed equals the work done by the system.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037879", "2000038576", "2000110317", "2000110424"]',NULL),
(33168,77,147,'paragraph',NULL,'In an adiabatic process, the system is insulated from the surroundings and heat absorbed or released is zero. 2 From Eq. (12.1), we see that work done by the gas results in decrease in its internal energy (and hence its temperature for an ideal gas). We quote without proof (the result that you will learn in higher courses) that for an adiabatic process of an ideal gas.','[{"s": "In an adiabatic process, the system is insulated from the surroundings and heat absorbed or released is zero.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "From Eq. (12.1), we see that work done by the gas results in decrease in its internal energy (and hence its temperature for an ideal gas). We quote without proof (the result that you will learn in higher courses) that for an adiabatic process of an ideal gas.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000037883", "2000038581", "2000110319", "2000110426"]',NULL),
(33176,77,155,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 12.8 P-V curves for isothermal and adiabatic processes of an ideal gas . 3','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "12.8 P-V curves for isothermal and adiabatic processes of an ideal gas", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000038108", "2000038593", "2000110322", "2000110429"]',NULL),
(33194,77,173,'paragraph',NULL,'The cycle is repeated again and again to get useful work for some purpose. The discipline of thermodynamics has its roots in the study of heat engines. A basic question relates to the efficiency of a heat engine. The efficiency (η) of a heat engine is defined by','[{"s": "The cycle is repeated again and again to get useful work for some purpose. The discipline of thermodynamics has its roots in the study of heat engines.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A basic question relates to the efficiency of a heat engine. The efficiency (η) of a heat engine is defined by", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000038112", "2000038932", "2000110324", "2000110431"]',NULL),
(33196,77,175,'paragraph',NULL,'where Q1 is the heat input i.e., the heat absorbed by the system in one complete cycle and W is the work done on the environment in a cycle. In a cycle, a certain amount of heat (Q2) may also be rejected to the environment. Then, according to the First Law of Thermodynamics, over one complete cycle, 2','[{"s": "where Q1 is the heat input i.e., the heat absorbed by the system in one complete cycle and W is the work done on the environment in a cycle. In a cycle, a certain amount of heat (Q2) may also be rejected to the environment.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Then, according to the First Law of Thermodynamics, over one complete cycle,", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000038120", "2000038935", "2000110325"]',NULL),
(33218,77,197,'paragraph',NULL,'In a heat engine, heat cannot be fully converted to work; likewise a refrigerator cannot work without some external work done on the system, i.e., the coefficient of performance in Eq. (12.21) cannot be infinite.','[{"s": "In a heat engine, heat cannot be fully converted to work; likewise a refrigerator cannot work without some external work done on the system, i.e., the coefficient of performance in Eq.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(12.21) cannot be infinite.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038124", "2000038944", "2000110327"]',NULL),
(33230,77,209,'paragraph',NULL,'A thermodynamic process (state i → state f ) is reversible if the process can be turned back such that both the system and the surroundings return to their original states, with no other change anywhere else in the universe. From the preceding discussion, a reversible process is an idealised notion. A process is reversible only if it is quasi-static (system in equilibrium with the surroundings at every stage) and there are no dissipative effects. For example, a quasi-static isothermal expansion of an ideal gas in a cylinder fitted with a frictionless movable piston is a reversible process.','[{"s": "A thermodynamic process (state i → state f ) is reversible if the process can be turned back such that both the system and the surroundings return to their original states, with no other change anywhere else in the universe.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "From the preceding discussion, a reversible process is an idealised notion. A process is reversible only if it is quasi-static (system in equilibrium with the surroundings at every stage) and there are no dissipative effects. For example, a quasi-static isothermal expansion of an ideal gas in a cylinder fitted with a frictionless movable piston is a reversible process.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038128", "2000038947", "2000110328"]',NULL),
(33241,77,220,'paragraph',NULL,'(b) Step 2 → 3 Adiabatic expansion of the gas from (P2, V2, T1) to (P3, V3, T2) Work done by the gas, using Eq. (12.16), is','[{"s": "(b) Step 2 → 3 Adiabatic expansion of the gas from (P2, V2, T1) to (P3, V3, T2) Work done by the gas,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "using Eq.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(12.16), is", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038132", "2000038950", "2000110333"]',NULL),
(33264,77,243,'paragraph',NULL,'Thermodynamics-image5.png (Carnot engine) (12.32) 2','[{"s": "Thermodynamics-image5.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(Carnot engine) (12.32)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000038140", "2000038953", "2000110335"]',NULL),
(33265,77,244,'paragraph',NULL,'We have already seen that a Carnot engine is a reversible engine. 2 Indeed it is the only reversible engine possible that works between two reservoirs at different temperatures. Each step of the Carnot cycle given in Fig. 12.11 can be reversed. This will amount to taking heat Q 2 from the cold reservoir at T 2 , doing work W on the system, and transferring heat Q 1 to the hot reservoir. This will be a reversible refrigerator.','[{"s": "We have already seen that a Carnot engine is a reversible engine.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Indeed it is the only reversible engine possible that works between two reservoirs at different temperatures. Each step of the Carnot cycle given in Fig. 12.11 can be reversed. This will amount to taking heat Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "from the cold reservoir at T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", doing work W on the system, and transferring heat Q", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to the hot reservoir. This will be a reversible refrigerator.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038156", "2000038956", "2000110338"]',NULL),
(33301,77,280,'paragraph',NULL,'11. In a refrigerator or a heat pump, the system extracts heat Q2 from the cold reservoir and releases Q1 amount of heat to the hot reservoir, with work W done on the system. The co-efficient of performance of a refrigerator is given by','[{"s": "11.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In a refrigerator or a heat pump, the system extracts heat Q2 from the cold reservoir and releases Q1 amount of heat to the hot reservoir, with work W done on the system.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The co-efficient of performance of a refrigerator is given by", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038160", "2000038963", "2000110341"]',NULL),
(33333,77,312,'paragraph',NULL,'12.5 In changing the state of a gas adiabatically from an equilibrium state A to another equilibrium state B, an amount of work equal to 22.3 J is done on the system. If the gas is taken from state A to B via a process in which the net heat absorbed by the system is 9.35 cal, how much is the net work done by the system in the latter case? (Take 1 cal = 4.19 J)','[{"s": "12.5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In changing the state of a gas adiabatically from an equilibrium state A to another equilibrium state B, an amount of work equal to 22.3 J is done on the system.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "If the gas is taken from state A to B via a process in which the net heat absorbed by the system is 9.35 cal, how much is the net work done by the system in the latter case? (Take 1 cal = 4.19 J)", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038180", "2000038971", "2000110343"]',NULL),
(33341,77,320,'paragraph',NULL,'12.9 A thermodynamic system is taken from an original state to an intermediate state by the linear process shown in Fig. 3 (12.13)','[{"s": "12.9", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A thermodynamic system is taken from an original state to an intermediate state by the linear process shown in Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(12.13)", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000038185", "2000110316", "2000110344"]',NULL),
(33463,78,24,'paragraph',NULL,'In SI, there are seven base units as given in Table 2.1. Besides the seven base units, there are two more units that are defined for (a) plane angle dθ as the ratio of length of arc d s to the radius r and (b) solid angle dΩ as the ratio of the intercepted area d A of the spherical surface, described about the apex O as the centre, to the square of its radius r , as shown in Fig. 2.1(a) and (b) respectively. The unit for plane angle is radian with the symbol rad and the unit for the solid angle is steradian with the symbol sr. Both these are dimensionless quantities.','[{"s": "In SI, there are seven base units as given in Table 2.1. Besides the seven base units, there are two more units that are defined for (a) plane angle dθ as the ratio of length of arc d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to the radius", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and (b) solid angle dΩ as the ratio of the intercepted area d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the spherical surface, described about the apex O as the centre, to the square of its radius", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", as shown in Fig. 2.1(a) and (b) respectively. The unit for plane angle is radian with the symbol rad and the unit for the solid angle is steradian with the symbol sr.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Both these are dimensionless quantities.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000030992", "2000030998", "2000031075", "2000031795", "2000031803", "2000032134", "2000032138", "2000032750", "2000032806", "2000032826", "2000106043", "2000106061", "2000106064"]',NULL),
(33490,78,51,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.1 Calculate the angle of (a) 1 0 (degree) (b) 1′ (minute of arc or arcmin) and (c) 1″(second of arc or arc second) in radians. Use 360 0 =2π rad, 1 0 =60′ and 1′ = 60 ″','[{"s": "Example 2.1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Calculate the angle of (a) 1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "0", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(degree) (b) 1′ (minute of arc or arcmin) and (c) 1″(second of arc or arc second) in radians.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Use 360", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=2π rad, 1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "0", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=60′ and 1′ = 60 ″", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000030995", "2000031079", "2000031799", "2000032142", "2000032754", "2000032837", "2000106044", "2000106065"]',NULL),
(33581,78,142,'paragraph',NULL,'The random errors are those errors, which occur irregularly and hence are random with respect to sign and size. These can arise due to random and unpredictable fluctuations in experimental conditions (e.g. unpredictable fluctuations in temperature, voltage supply, mechanical vibrations of experimental set-ups, etc), personal (unbiased) errors by the observer taking readings, etc. For example, when the same person repeats the same observation, it is very likely that he may get different readings everytime.','[{"s": "The", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "random errors", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "are those errors, which occur irregularly and hence are random with respect to sign and size.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "These can arise due to random and unpredictable fluctuations in experimental conditions (e.g. unpredictable fluctuations in temperature, voltage supply, mechanical vibrations of experimental set-ups, etc), personal (unbiased) errors by the observer taking readings, etc. For example, when the same person repeats the same observation, it is very likely that he may get different readings everytime.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031001", "2000031084", "2000031807", "2000032146", "2000032758", "2000032841", "2000106045", "2000106066"]',NULL),
(33689,78,250,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer The percentage error in V is 5% and in I it is 2%. The total error in R would therefore be 5% + 2% = 7%.','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The percentage error in", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "V", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "is 5% and in", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "I", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "it is 2%.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The total error in", "t": "text"}, {"s": "R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "would therefore be 5% + 2% = 7%.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031004", "2000031088", "2000031811", "2000032150", "2000032762", "2000032845", "2000106046", "2000106067"]',NULL),
(33690,78,251,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.10 Two resistors of resistances R 1 = 100±3 ohm and R 2 = 200 ± 4 ohm are connected (a) in series, (b) in parallel. Find the equivalent resistance of the (a) series combination, (b) parallel combination. Use for (a) the relation R =R 1 + R 2, and for (b) Units_And_Measurements-image21.png and Units_And_Measurements-image45.png','[{"s": "Example 2.10", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Two resistors of resistances R", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "= 100±3 ohm and R", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "= 200 ± 4 ohm are connected (a) in series, (b) in parallel.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Find the equivalent resistance of the (a) series combination, (b) parallel combination. Use for (a) the relation R =R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ R", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and for (b)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image21.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "and", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Units_And_Measurements-image45.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}]','marked','["2000031008", "2000031092", "2000031820", "2000032158", "2000032766", "2000032849", "2000106047", "2000106069"]',NULL),
(33713,78,274,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.11 Find the relative error in Z, if Z = A 4 B 1/3 /CD 3/2 .','[{"s": "Example 2.11", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Find the relative error in Z, if Z = A", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "B", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "1/3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "/CD", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3/2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031012", "2000031096", "2000031826", "2000032162", "2000032770", "2000032857", "2000106048", "2000106071"]',NULL),
(33715,78,276,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.12 The period of oscillation of a simple pendulum is 2','[{"s": "Example 2.12", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The period of oscillation of a simple pendulum is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031016", "2000031100", "2000031830", "2000032166", "2000032774", "2000032861", "2000106050", "2000106112"]',NULL),
(33759,78,320,'paragraph',NULL,'(2) In addition or subtraction, the final result should retain as many decimal places as are there in the number with the least decimal places.','[{"s": "(2)", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In addition or subtraction, the final result should retain as many decimal places as are there in the number with the least decimal places.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031021", "2000031109", "2000031834", "2000032170", "2000032778", "2000106032", "2000106051", "2000106113"]',NULL),
(33767,78,328,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 2.13 Each side of a cube is measured to be 7.203 m. What are the total surface area and the volume of the cube to appropriate significant figures?','[{"s": "Example 2.13", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Each side of a cube is measured to be 7.203 m. What are the total surface area and the volume of the cube to appropriate significant figures?", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031025", "2000031038", "2000031104", "2000031113", "2000031122", "2000031142", "2000031838", "2000032174", "2000032719", "2000032732", "2000032782", "2000106033", "2000106052", "2000106068", "2000106070", "2000106114"]',NULL),
(33815,78,376,'paragraph',NULL,'The expression which shows how and which of the base quantities represent the dimensions of a physical quantity is called the dimensional formula of the given physical quantity. 2 For example, the dimensional formula of the volume is [M° L 3 T°], and that of speed or velocity is [M° L T -1 ]. Similarly, [M° L T –2 ] is the dimensional formula of acceleration and [M L –3 T°] that of mass density.','[{"s": "The expression which shows how and which of the base quantities represent the dimensions of a physical quantity is called the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "dimensional formula", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "of the given physical quantity.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For example, the dimensional formula of the volume is [M° L", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "T°], and that of speed or velocity is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "[M° L T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "]. Similarly, [M° L T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] is the dimensional formula of acceleration and [M L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T°] that of mass density.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031029", "2000031118", "2000032088", "2000032178", "2000032786", "2000106034", "2000106053", "2000106116"]',NULL),
(33816,78,377,'paragraph',NULL,'An equation obtained by equating a physical quantity with its dimensional formula is called the dimensional equation of the physical quantity. Thus, the dimensional equations are the equations, which represent the dimensions of a physical quantity in terms of the base quantities. For example, the dimensional equations of volume [ V ], speed [ v ], force [ F ] and mass density [ρ] may be expressed as','[{"s": "An equation obtained by equating a physical quantity with its dimensional formula is called the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "dimensional equation", "t": "text"}, {"s": "of the physical quantity.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, the dimensional equations are the equations, which represent the dimensions of a physical quantity in terms of the base quantities.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "For example, the dimensional equations of volume [", "t": "text"}, {"s": "V", "t": "text"}, {"s": "], speed [", "t": "text"}, {"s": "v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "], force [", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] and mass density [ρ] may be expressed as", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031033", "2000031126", "2000032092", "2000032182", "2000032790", "2000106035", "2000106054"]',NULL),
(33855,78,416,'paragraph',NULL,'Answer Every correct formula or equation must have the same dimensions on both sides of the equation. Also, only quantities with the same physical dimensions can be added or subtracted. The dimensions of the quantity on the right side are [M 2 L 3 T –3 ] for (a); [M L 2 T –2 ] for (b) and (d); [M L T –2 ] for (c). The quantity on the right side of (e) has no proper dimensions since two quantities of different dimensions have been added. Since the kinetic energy K has the dimensions of [M L 2 T –2 ], formulas (a), (c) and (e) are ruled out. Note that dimensional arguments cannot tell which of the two, (b) or (d), is the correct formula. For this, one must turn to the actual definition of kinetic energy (see Chapter 6). The correct formula for kinetic energy is given by (b).','[{"s": "Answer", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Every correct formula or equation must have the same dimensions on both sides of the equation. Also, only quantities with the same physical dimensions can be added or subtracted. The dimensions of the quantity on the right side are [M", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] for (a); [M L", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] for (b) and (d); [M L T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] for (c). The quantity on the right side of (e) has no proper dimensions since two quantities of different dimensions have been added.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Since the kinetic energy", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "K", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "has the dimensions of [M L", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "T", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "], formulas (a), (c) and (e) are ruled out.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Note that dimensional arguments cannot tell which of the two, (b) or (d), is the correct formula. For this, one must turn to the actual definition of kinetic energy (see Chapter 6). The correct formula for kinetic energy is given by (b).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031046", "2000031134", "2000032101", "2000032188", "2000032833", "2000106038", "2000106056"]',NULL),
(33894,78,455,'paragraph',NULL,'2.2 Fill in the blanks by suitable conversion of units','[{"s": "2.2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Fill in the blanks by suitable conversion of units", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031055", "2000031146", "2000032109", "2000032724", "2000032802", "2000106040", "2000106058"]',NULL),
(34230,79,150,'paragraph',NULL,'If φ = 0, ± 2 π , ± 4 π ,… which corresponds to the condition given by Eq. (10.10) we will have constructive interference leading to maximum intensity. On the other hand, if φ = ± π , ± 3 π , ± 5 π … [which corresponds to the condition given by Eq. (10.11)] we will have destructive interference leading to zero intensity.','[{"s": "If", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0,", "t": "text"}, {"s": "±", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "±", "t": "text"}, {"s": "4", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",… which corresponds to the condition given by Eq.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(10.10) we will have constructive interference leading to maximum intensity.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "On the other hand, if", "t": "text"}, {"s": "φ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "±", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "±", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": ",", "t": "text"}, {"s": "±", "t": "text"}, {"s": "5", "t": "text"}, {"s": "π", "t": "text"}, {"s": "… [which corresponds to the condition given by Eq. (10.11)] we will have destructive interference leading to zero intensity.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031723", "2000031980", "2000032516", "2000112570"]',NULL),
(34276,79,196,'paragraph',NULL,'Figure 10.14 Photograph and the graph of the intensity distribution in Young’s double-slit experiment.','[{"s": "Figure 10.14 Photograph and the graph of the intensity distribution in Young’s double-slit experiment.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031727", "2000031735", "2000031743", "2000031747", "2000031755", "2000031763", "2000031786", "2000031790", "2000031798", "2000031801", "2000031905", "2000031920", "2000031924", "2000031928", "2000031940", "2000032016", "2000032064", "2000032072", "2000032076", "2000032084", "2000032091", "2000032184", "2000032190", "2000032193", "2000032196", "2000032199", "2000032202", "2000032205", "2000032220", "2000032222", "2000032225", "2000032519", "2000032527", "2000032532", "2000032548", "2000032552", "2000032567", "2000032594", "2000032602", "2000033038", "2000033044", "2000112552", "2000112554", "2000112560", "2000112561", "2000112562", "2000112563", "2000112566", "2000112567", "2000112569", "2000112573", "2000112576", "2000112577", "2000112578", "2000112579", "2000112580", "2000112581", "2000112582", "2000112583", "2000112586", "2000112587", "2000112589", "2000112590", "2000112641", "2000112642", "2000112646", "2000112648"]',NULL),
(34264,79,184,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus dark and bright bands appear on the screen, as shown in Fig. 10.13. Such bands are called fringes . Equations (10.18) and (10.19) show that dark and bright fringes are equally spaced and the distance between two consecutive bright and dark fringes is given by 3','[{"s": "Thus dark and bright bands appear on the screen, as shown in Fig. 10.13. Such bands are called", "t": "text"}, {"s": "fringes", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Equations (10.18) and (10.19) show that dark and bright fringes are equally spaced and the distance between two consecutive bright and dark fringes is given by", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031731", "2000031988", "2000032524", "2000112574"]',NULL),
(34266,79,186,'paragraph',NULL,'which is the expression for the fringe width . 2 Obviously, the central point O (in Fig. 10.12) will be bright because S 1 O = S 2 O and it will correspond to n = 0 [Eq. (10.18)]. If we consider the line perpendicular to the plane of the paper and passing through O [i.e., along the y -axis] then all points on this line will be equidistant from S 1 and S 2 and we will have a bright central fringe which is a straight line as shown in Fig. 10.13. In order to determine the shape of the interference pattern on the screen we note that a particular fringe would correspond to the locus of points with a constant value of S 2 P – S 1 P. Whenever this constant is an integral multiple of λ , the fringe will be bright and whenever it is an odd integral multiple of λ /2 it will be a dark fringe. Now, the locus of the point P lying in the x - y plane such that S 2 P – S 1 P (= ∆) is a constant, is a hyperbola. Thus the fringe pattern will strictly be a hyperbola; however, if the distance D is very large compared to the fringe width, the fringes will be very nearly straight lines as shown in Fig. 10.13.','[{"s": "which is the expression for the", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "fringe width", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Obviously, the central point O (in Fig. 10.12) will be bright because S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O = S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "O and it will correspond to", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0 [Eq. (10.18)]. If we consider the line perpendicular to the plane of the paper and passing through O [i.e., along the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-axis] then all points on this line will be equidistant from S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and we will have a bright central fringe which is a straight line as shown in Fig. 10.13. In order to determine the shape of the interference pattern on the screen we note that a particular fringe would correspond to the locus of points with a constant value of S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P – S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P. Whenever this constant is an integral multiple of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the fringe will be bright and whenever it is an odd integral multiple of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2 it will be a dark fringe. Now, the locus of the point P lying in the", "t": "text"}, {"s": "x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "-", "t": "text"}, {"s": "y", "t": "text"}, {"s": "plane such that S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P – S", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P (=", "t": "text"}, {"s": "∆)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is a constant, is a hyperbola. Thus the fringe pattern will strictly be a hyperbola; however, if the distance", "t": "text"}, {"s": "D", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is very large compared to the fringe width, the fringes will be very nearly straight lines as shown in Fig. 10.13.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031739", "2000031996", "2000032555", "2000112575"]',NULL),
(34280,79,200,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 10.4 What is the eff ect on the interference fringes in a Young’s double-slit experiment due to each of the following operations: 4','[{"s": "Example 10.4", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What is the eff ect on the interference fringes in a Young’s double-slit experiment due to each of the following operations:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "4", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031759", "2000032020", "2000032590", "2000112584"]',NULL),
(34314,79,234,'paragraph',NULL,'The first two-thirds of the slit can therefore be divided into two halves which have a λ /2 path difference. The contributions of these two halves cancel in the same manner as described earlier. Only the remaining one-third of the slit contributes to the intensity at a point between the two minima. Clearly, this will be much weaker than the central maximum (where the entire slit contributes in phase). One can similarly show that there are maxima at ( n + 1/2) λ / a with n = 2, 3, etc. These become weaker with increasing n , since only one-fifth, one-seventh, etc., of the slit contributes in these cases. The photograph and intensity pattern corresponding to it is shown in Fig. 10.16.','[{"s": "The first two-thirds of the slit can therefore be divided into two halves which have a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/2 path difference. The contributions of these two halves cancel in the same manner as described earlier. Only the remaining one-third of the slit contributes to the intensity at a point between the two minima.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Clearly, this will be much weaker than the central maximum (where the entire slit contributes in phase).", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "One can similarly show that there are maxima at (", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "+ 1/2)", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/", "t": "text"}, {"s": "a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 2, 3, etc. These become weaker with increasing", "t": "text"}, {"s": "n", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", since only one-fifth, one-seventh, etc., of the slit contributes in these cases. The photograph and intensity pattern corresponding to it is shown in Fig. 10.16.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031767", "2000032024", "2000032598", "2000112585"]',NULL),
(34385,79,305,'paragraph',NULL,'The resolving power of the microscope is given by the reciprocal of the minimum separation of two points seen as distinct. It can be seen from Eq. (10.30) that the resolving power can be increased by choosing a medium of higher refractive index. Usually an oil having a refractive index close to that of the objective glass is used. Such an arrangement is called an ‘oil immersion objective’ . Notice that it is not possible to make sin β larger than unity . Thus, we see that the resolving power of a microscope is basically determined by the wavelength of the light used.','[{"s": "The resolving power of the microscope is given by the reciprocal of the minimum separation of two points seen as distinct. It can be seen from Eq. (10.30) that the resolving power can be increased by choosing a medium of higher refractive index. Usually an oil having a refractive index close to that of the objective glass is used. Such an arrangement is called an", "t": "text"}, {"s": "‘oil immersion objective’", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". Notice that it is not possible to make sin", "t": "text"}, {"s": "β", "t": "text"}, {"s": "larger than unity", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, we see that the resolving power of a microscope is basically determined by the wavelength of the light used.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031864", "2000032108", "2000033106"]',NULL),
(34409,79,329,'paragraph',NULL,'The above experiment can be easily understood by assuming that light passing through the polaroid P 2 gets polarised along the pass-axis of P 2 . If the pass-axis of P 2 makes an angle θ with the pass-axis of P 1 , then when the polarised beam passes through the polaroid P 2 , the component E cos θ (along the pass-axis of P 2 ) will pass through P 2 . Thus, as we rotate the polaroid P 1 (or P 2 ), the intensity will vary as: 2','[{"s": "The above experiment can be easily understood by assuming that light passing through the polaroid", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "gets polarised along the pass-axis of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". If the pass-axis of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "makes an angle", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "with the pass-axis of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", then when the polarised beam passes through the polaroid", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the component", "t": "text"}, {"s": "E", "t": "text"}, {"s": "cos", "t": "text"}, {"s": "θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "(along the pass-axis of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") will pass through", "t": "text"}, {"s": "P", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, as we rotate the polaroid", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "P", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(or", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "P", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "), the intensity will vary as:", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031873", "2000032112", "2000033110"]',NULL),
(34425,79,345,'paragraph',NULL,'FIGURE 10.23 (a) Polarisation of the blue scattered light from the sky. The incident sunlight is unpolarised (dots and arrows). A typical molecule is shown. It scatters light by 90° polarised normal to the plane of the paper (dots only). (b) Polarisation of light reflected from a transparent medium at the Brewster angle (reflected ray perpendicular to refracted ray).','[{"s": "FIGURE 10.23 (a) Polarisation of the blue scattered light from the sky.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The incident sunlight is unpolarised (dots and arrows). A typical molecule is shown. It scatters light by 90° polarised normal to the plane of the paper (dots only). (b) Polarisation of light reflected from a transparent medium at the Brewster angle (reflected ray perpendicular to refracted ray).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031884", "2000032128", "2000112553"]',NULL),
(34437,79,357,'paragraph',NULL,'This is known as Brewster’s law .','[{"s": "This is known as", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Brewster’s law", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031908", "2000032187", "2000112555"]',NULL),
(34464,79,384,'paragraph',NULL,'10.5 In Young’s double-slit experiment using monochromatic light of wavelength λ , the intensity of light at a point on the screen where path difference is λ , is K units. 2 What is the intensity of light at a point where path difference is λ /3?','[{"s": "10.5", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In Young’s double-slit experiment using monochromatic light of wavelength", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", the intensity of light at a point on the screen where path difference is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ", is", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "K", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "units.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What is the intensity of light at a point where path difference is", "t": "text"}, {"s": "λ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/3?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031956", "2000032208", "2000112556"]',NULL),
(34465,79,385,'paragraph',NULL,'10.6 A beam of light consisting of two wavelengths, 650 nm, and 520 nm, is used to obtain interference fringes in Young’s double-slit experiment. 2','[{"s": "10.6", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A beam of light consisting of two wavelengths, 650 nm, and 520 nm, is used to obtain interference fringes in Young’s double-slit experiment.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000031960", "2000032211", "2000112557"]',NULL),
(34468,79,388,'paragraph',NULL,'10.7 In a double-slit experiment the angular width of a fringe is found to be 0.2° on a screen placed 1 m away. 3 The wavelength of light used is 600 nm. What will be the angular width of the fringe if the entire experimental apparatus is immersed in water? Take the refractive index of water to be 4/3.','[{"s": "10.7", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In a double-slit experiment the angular width of a fringe is found to be 0.2° on a screen placed 1 m away.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The wavelength of light used is 600 nm. What will be the angular width of the fringe if the entire experimental apparatus is immersed in water? Take the refractive index of water to be 4/3.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000031972", "2000032214", "2000112559"]',NULL),
(34486,79,406,'paragraph',NULL,'10.16 In a double-slit experiment using the light of wavelength 600 nm, the angular width of a fringe formed on a distant screen is 0.1°. What is the spacing between the two slits?','[{"s": "10.16", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "In a double-slit experiment using the light of wavelength 600 nm, the angular width of a fringe formed on a distant screen is 0.1°.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "What is the spacing between the two slits?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000032068", "2000032217", "2000112564"]',NULL),
(34712,80,145,'paragraph',NULL,'where C is the undetermined constant of dimensional analysis. In the exact formula, it turms out, C=1. The speed of transverse waves on a stretched string is given by 2','[{"s": "where C is the undetermined constant of dimensional analysis.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "In the exact formula, it turms out, C=1. The speed of transverse waves on a stretched string is given by", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040354", "2000040374", "2000040406", "2000040414", "2000040418", "2000040430", "2000040442", "2000040517", "2000040526", "2000040539", "2000040546", "2000040550", "2000040554", "2000040558", "2000040566", "2000040570", "2000040829", "2000040837", "2000040841", "2000040845", "2000040854", "2000040857", "2000040861", "2000041117", "2000041153", "2000041161", "2000041188", "2000041194", "2000041506", "2000041530", "2000109210", "2000109215", "2000109216", "2000109219", "2000109220", "2000109225", "2000109233", "2000109240", "2000109241", "2000109250", "2000109252", "2000109254", "2000109307", "2000109310"]',NULL),
(34620,80,53,'paragraph',NULL,'For mathematical description of a travelling wave, we need a function of both position x and time t. Such a function at every instant should give the shape of the wave at that instant. Also, at every given location, it should describe the motion of the constituent of the medium at that location. If we wish to describe a sinusoidal travelling wave (such as the one shown in Fig. 15.3) the corresponding function must also be sinusoidal. For convenience, we shall take the wave to be transverse so that if the position of the constituents of the medium is denoted by x, the displacement from the equilibrium position may be denoted by y. A sinusoidal travelling wave is then described by:','[{"s": "For mathematical description of a travelling wave, we need a function of both position x and time t. Such a function at every instant should give the shape of the wave at that instant. Also, at every given location, it should describe the motion of the constituent of the medium at that location. If we wish to describe a sinusoidal travelling wave (such as the one shown in Fig. 15.3) the corresponding function must also be sinusoidal. For convenience, we shall take the wave to be transverse so that if the position of the constituents of the medium is denoted by x, the displacement from the equilibrium position may be denoted by y.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "A sinusoidal travelling wave is then described by:", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040358", "2000041182"]',NULL),
(34626,80,59,'paragraph',NULL,'To understand why Equation (15.2) represents a sinusoidal travelling wave, take a fixed instant, say t = t0. Then, the argument of the sine function in Equation (15.2) is simply kx + constant. Thus, the shape of the wave (at any fixed instant) as a function of Waves-image7.png is a sine wave. Similarly, take a fixed location, say x = x0. Then, the argument of the sine function in Equation (15.2) is constant -ωt. The displacement y, at a fixed location, thus, varies sinusoidally with time. That is, the constituents of the medium at different positions execute simple harmonic motion. Finally, as t increases, x must increase in the positive direction to keep kx – ωt + φ constant. Thus, Eq. (15.2) represents a sinusiodal (harmonic) wave travelling along the positive direction of the x-axis. On the other hand, a function','[{"s": "To understand why Equation (15.2) represents a sinusoidal travelling wave, take a fixed instant, say t = t0.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Then, the argument of the sine function in Equation (15.2) is simply kx + constant. Thus, the shape of the wave (at any fixed instant) as a function of", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Waves-image7.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "is a sine wave. Similarly, take a fixed location, say x = x0. Then, the argument of the sine function in Equation (15.2) is constant -ωt. The displacement y, at a fixed location, thus, varies sinusoidally with time. That is, the constituents of the medium at different positions execute simple harmonic motion. Finally, as t increases, x must increase in the positive direction to keep kx – ωt + φ constant. Thus, Eq. (15.2) represents a sinusiodal (harmonic) wave travelling along the positive direction of the x-axis.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "On the other hand, a function", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040362", "2000041185"]',NULL),
(34627,80,60,'paragraph',NULL,'Waves-image87.png (15.4) represents a wave travelling in the negative direction of x-axis. Fig. (15.5) gives the names of the various physical quantities appearing in Eq. (15.2) that we now interpret.','[{"s": "Waves-image87.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": "(15.4) represents a wave travelling in the negative direction of x-axis.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Fig. (15.5) gives the names of the various physical quantities appearing in Eq. (15.2) that we now interpret.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000040366", "2000041191"]',NULL),
(34997,80,430,'paragraph',NULL,'11. Sound waves are longitudinal mechanical waves that can travel through solids, liquids, or gases. The speed v of sound wave in a fluid having bulk modulus B and density ρ is','[{"s": "11. Sound waves are longitudinal mechanical waves that can travel through solids, liquids, or gases.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "speed v of sound wave in a fluid having bulk modulus B and density ρ is", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000040370", "2000040461", "2000040470", "2000040627", "2000040849", "2000040997", "2000041093", "2000041121", "2000041125", "2000041129", "2000041133", "2000041137", "2000041179", "2000109222", "2000109235", "2000109238", "2000109245", "2000109253", "2000109255", "2000109311", "2000109312", "2000109316"]',NULL),
