(34966,80,399,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 15.7 A rocket is moving at a speed of 200 m s –1 towards a stationary target . 3 While moving, it emits a wave of frequency 1000 Hz. Some of the sound reaching the target gets reflected back to the rocket as an echo. Calculate (1) the frequency of the sound as detected by the target and (2) the frequency of the echo as detected by the rocket.','[{"s": "Example 15.7", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A rocket is moving at a speed of 200 m s", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "towards a stationary target", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "While moving, it emits a wave of frequency 1000 Hz. Some of the sound reaching the target gets reflected back to the rocket as an echo. Calculate (1) the frequency of the sound as detected by the target and (2) the frequency of the echo as detected by the rocket.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000041002"]',NULL),
(35664,81,427,'paragraph',NULL,'By performing this experiment in a vertical direction, all the three difficulties vanish. Take two balls, one of which is heavier (basketball/football/volleyball) and the other lighter (tennis ball/rubber ball/table tennis ball). First take only the heavier ball and drop it vertically from some height, say 1 m. Note to which it rises . This gives the velocities near the floor or ground, just before and just after the bounce (by using Work_Energy_and_Power-image92.png ). Hence you will get the coefficient of restitution.','[{"s": "By performing this experiment in a vertical direction, all the three difficulties vanish. Take two balls, one of which is heavier (basketball/football/volleyball) and the other lighter (tennis ball/rubber ball/table tennis ball). First take only the heavier ball and drop it vertically from some height, say 1 m. Note to which it rises", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This gives the velocities near the floor or ground, just before and just after the bounce (by using", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Work_Energy_and_Power-image92.png", "t": "img", "alt": ""}, {"s": ").", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hence you will get the coefficient of restitution.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034249", "2000035064", "2000108477", "2000108605"]',NULL),
(35360,81,123,'paragraph',NULL,'If the displacements are allowed to approach zero, then the number of terms in the sum increases without limit, but the sum approaches a definite value equal to the area under the curve in Fig. 6.3(b). Then the work done is','[{"s": "If the displacements are allowed to approach zero, then the number of terms in the sum increases without limit, but the sum approaches a definite value equal to the area under the curve in Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6.3(b). Then the work done is", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034253", "2000035453", "2000108492"]',NULL),
(35364,81,127,'paragraph',NULL,'where ‘lim’ stands for the limit of the sum when ∆x tends to zero. Thus, for a varying force the work done can be expressed as a definite integral of force over displacement (see also Appendix 3.1).','[{"s": "where ‘lim’ stands for the limit of the sum when ∆x tends to zero.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus, for a varying force", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "the work done can be expressed as a definite integral of force over displacement (see also Appendix 3.1).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034256", "2000035474", "2000108493"]',NULL),
(35367,81,130,'paragraph',NULL,'Fig. 6.3 (a) The shaded rectangle represents the work done by the varying force F(x), over the small displacement ∆x, ∆W = F(x) ∆x. 2 (b) adding the areas of all the rectangles we find that for ∆x → 0, the area under the curve is exactly equal to the work done by F(x).','[{"s": "Fig.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "6.3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "(a) The shaded rectangle represents the work done by the varying force F(x), over the small displacement ∆x, ∆W = F(x) ∆x.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "", "t": "br"}, {"s": "(b) adding the areas of all the rectangles we find that for ∆x → 0, the area under the curve is exactly equal to the work done by F(x).", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034260", "2000035477", "2000108495"]',NULL),
(35468,81,231,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.7 A bob of mass m is suspended by a light string of length L It is imparted a horizontal velocity v o at the lowest point A such that it completes a semi-circular trajectory in the vertical plane with the string becoming slack only on reaching the topmost point, C. This is shown in Fig. 6.6. Obtain an expression for (i) v o ; (ii) the speeds at points B and C; (iii) the ratio of the kinetic energies (K B /K C ) at B and C. Comment on the nature of the trajectory of the bob after it reaches the point C.','[{"s": "Example 6.7", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "A bob of mass m is suspended by a light string of length L", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "It is imparted a horizontal velocity v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "at the lowest point A such that it completes a semi-circular trajectory in the vertical plane with the string becoming slack only on reaching the topmost point, C. This is shown in Fig. 6.6. Obtain an expression for (i) v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "o", "t": "text"}, {"s": "; (ii) the speeds at points B and C; (iii) the ratio of the kinetic energies (K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "B", "t": "text"}, {"s": "/K", "t": "text"}, {"s": "C", "t": "text"}, {"s": ") at B and C. Comment on the nature of the trajectory of the bob after it reaches the point C.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034264", "2000034444", "2000034448", "2000034669", "2000034874", "2000035036", "2000035358", "2000036227", "2000036266", "2000036270", "2000108462", "2000108464", "2000108479", "2000108488", "2000108519", "2000108529", "2000108531", "2000108612"]',NULL),
(35384,81,147,'paragraph',NULL,'We are now familiar with the concepts of work and kinetic energy to prove the work-energy theorem for a variable force. We confine ourselves to one dimension. The time rate of change of kinetic energy is','[{"s": "We are now familiar with the concepts of work and kinetic energy to prove the work-energy theorem for a variable force.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "We confine ourselves to one dimension. The time rate of change of kinetic energy is", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034268", "2000034300", "2000034321", "2000034839", "2000034866", "2000035379", "2000035480", "2000036255", "2000108461", "2000108463", "2000108483", "2000108496", "2000108499", "2000108505", "2000108517", "2000108524", "2000108607"]',NULL),
(35391,81,154,'paragraph',NULL,'Integrating from the initial position (x i ) to final position ( x f ), we have','[{"s": "Integrating from the initial position (x", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "i", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ") to final position ( x", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "f", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "), we have", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034272", "2000036211", "2000108501"]',NULL),
(35398,81,161,'paragraph',NULL,'Thus, the WE theorem is proved for a variable force.','[{"s": "Thus, the WE theorem is proved for a variable force.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034276", "2000036215", "2000108503"]',NULL),
(35418,81,181,'paragraph',NULL,'The negative sign indicates that the gravitational force is downward. When released, the ball comes down with an increasing speed. Just before it hits the ground, its speed is given by the kinematic relation,','[{"s": "The negative sign indicates that the gravitational force is downward. When released, the ball comes down with an increasing speed.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Just before it hits the ground, its speed is given by the kinematic relation,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034292", "2000036259", "2000108504"]',NULL),
(35447,81,210,'paragraph',NULL,'The total mechanical energy of a system is conserved if the forces, doing work on it, are conservative .','[{"s": "The total mechanical energy of a system is conserved if the forces, doing work on it, are conservative", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034333", "2000034661", "2000034831", "2000034996", "2000035008", "2000035016", "2000035028", "2000035032", "2000036251", "2000036274", "2000108458", "2000108481", "2000108482", "2000108484", "2000108497", "2000108500", "2000108509", "2000108510", "2000108512", "2000108515", "2000108516", "2000108525", "2000108527"]',NULL),
(35424,81,187,'paragraph',NULL,'Physically, the notion of potential energy is applicable only to the class of forces where work done against the force gets ‘stored up’ as energy. When external constraints are removed, it manifests itself as kinetic energy. Mathematically, (for simplicity, in one dimension) the potential energy V(x) is defined if the force F(x) can be written as','[{"s": "Physically, the notion of potential energy is applicable only to the class of forces where work done against the force gets ‘stored up’ as energy. When external constraints are removed, it manifests itself as kinetic energy.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Mathematically, (for simplicity, in one dimension) the potential energy V(x) is defined if the force F(x) can be written as", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034336", "2000034452", "2000034835", "2000034843", "2000034855", "2000034870", "2000034992", "2000035004", "2000035012", "2000035020", "2000035024", "2000036222", "2000036262", "2000036287", "2000108478", "2000108487", "2000108494", "2000108502", "2000108507", "2000108514", "2000108521", "2000108602", "2000108610", "2000108614"]',NULL),
