(35429,81,192,'paragraph',NULL,'The dimensions of potential energy are [ML 2 T –2 ] and the unit is joule (J), the same as kinetic energy or work. To reiterate, the change in potential energy, for a conservative force, ∆V is equal to the negative of the work done by the force','[{"s": "The dimensions of potential energy are [ML", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "T", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "] and the unit is joule (J), the same as kinetic energy or work.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "To reiterate, the change in potential energy, for a conservative force, ∆V is equal to the negative of the work done by the force", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034653", "2000036319", "2000108511"]',NULL),
(35437,81,200,'paragraph',NULL,'The above equations imply that','[{"s": "The above equations imply that", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034657", "2000036336", "2000108513"]',NULL),
(35456,81,219,'paragraph',NULL,'The constant force is a special case of a spatially dependent force F(x). Hence, the mechanical energy is conserved . Thus','[{"s": "The constant force is a special case of a spatially dependent force F(x).", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Hence, the mechanical energy is conserved", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "Thus", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034665", "2000108459", "2000108518"]',NULL),
(35499,81,262,'paragraph',NULL,'The spring force is an example of a variable force which is conservative. Fig. 6.7 shows a block attached to a spring and resting on a smooth horizontal surface. The other end of the spring is attached to a rigid wall. The spring is light and may be treated as massless. In an ideal spring, the spring force F s is proportional to x where x is the displacement of the block from the equilibrium position. The displacement could be either positive [Fig. 6.7(b)] or negative [Fig. 6.7(c)]. This force law for the spring is called Hooke’s law and is mathematically stated as','[{"s": "The spring force is an example of a variable force which is conservative. Fig. 6.7 shows a block attached to a spring and resting on a smooth horizontal surface. The other end of the spring is attached to a rigid wall. The spring is light and may be treated as massless. In an ideal spring, the spring force F", "t": "text"}, {"s": "s", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is proportional to x where x is the displacement of the block from the equilibrium position. The displacement could be either positive [Fig. 6.7(b)] or negative [Fig. 6.7(c)].", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This force law for the spring is called Hooke’s law and is mathematically stated as", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034827", "2000108465", "2000108520"]',NULL),
(35502,81,265,'paragraph',NULL,'Suppose that we pull the block outwards as in Fig. 3 6.7(b). If the extension is x m , the work done by the spring force is','[{"s": "Suppose that we pull the block outwards as in Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "3", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6.7(b). If the extension is x", "t": "text"}, {"s": "m", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the work done by the spring force is", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034846", "2000108466", "2000108522"]',NULL),
(35522,81,285,'paragraph',NULL,'We define the potential energy V(x) of the spring to be zero when block and spring system is in the equilibrium position. For an extension (or compression) x the above analysis suggests that','[{"s": "We define the potential energy V(x) of the spring to be zero when block and spring system is in the equilibrium position.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "For an extension (or compression) x the above analysis suggests that", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000034859", "2000108468", "2000108528"]',NULL),
(35533,81,296,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.8 To simulate car accidents, auto manufacturers study the collisions of moving cars with mounted springs of different spring constants. Consider a typical simulation with a car of mass 1000 kg moving with a speed 18.0 km/h on a smooth road and colliding with a horizontally mounted spring of spring constant 6.25 × 10 3 N m –1 . What is the maximum compression of the spring ?','[{"s": "Example 6.8", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "To simulate car accidents, auto manufacturers study the collisions of moving cars with mounted springs of different spring constants.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Consider a typical simulation with a car of mass 1000 kg moving with a speed 18.0 km/h on a smooth road and colliding with a horizontally mounted spring of spring constant 6.25 × 10", "t": "text"}, {"s": "3", "t": "text"}, {"s": "N m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "–1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". What is the maximum compression of the spring ?", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000034862", "2000108469", "2000108530"]',NULL),
(35565,81,328,'paragraph',NULL,'which, as expected, is less than the result in Example 6.8.','[{"s": "which, as expected, is less than the result in Example 6.8.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000035000", "2000108470", "2000108532"]',NULL),
(35612,81,375,'paragraph',NULL,'The instantaneous power is defined as the limiting value of the average power as time interval approaches zero,','[{"s": "The instantaneous power is defined as the limiting value of the average power as time interval approaches zero,", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000035044", "2000108472", "2000108534"]',NULL),
(35615,81,378,'paragraph',NULL,'The work dW done by a force F for a displacement d r is dW = F. d r . The instantaneous power can also be expressed as','[{"s": "The work dW done by a force F for a displacement d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is dW =", "t": "text"}, {"s": "F.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "d", "t": "text"}, {"s": "r", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The instantaneous power can also be expressed as", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000035048", "2000108473", "2000108535"]',NULL),
(35637,81,400,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider two masses m 1 and m 2 . The particle m 1 is moving with speed v 1i , the subscript ‘i’ implying initial. We can consider m 2 to be at rest. No loss of generality is involved in making such a selection. In this situation the mass m 1 collides with the stationary mass m 2 and this is depicted in Fig. 6.10.','[{"s": "Consider two masses m", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "1", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "and m", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "2", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "The particle m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "is moving with speed v", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1i", "t": "text"}, {"s": ", the subscript ‘i’ implying initial. We can consider m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "to be at rest. No loss of generality is involved in making such a selection. In this situation the mass m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "collides with the stationary mass m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "and this is depicted in Fig. 6.10.", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000035056", "2000108475", "2000108603"]',NULL),
(35648,81,411,'paragraph',NULL,'On the other hand, the total kinetic energy of the system is not necessarily conserved. The impact and deformation during collision may generate heat and sound. Part of the initial kinetic energy is transformed into other forms of energy. A useful way to visualise the deformation during collision is in terms of a ‘compressed spring’. If the ‘spring’ connecting the two masses regains its original shape without loss in energy, then the initial kinetic energy is equal to the final kinetic energy but the kinetic energy during the collision time ∆t is not constant. Such a collision is called an elastic collision . On the other hand the deformation may not be relieved and the two bodies could move together after the collision. A collision in which the two particles move together after the collision is called a completely inelastic collision . The intermediate case where the deformation is partly relieved and some of the initial kinetic energy is lost is more common and is appropriately called an inelastic collision .','[{"s": "On the other hand, the total kinetic energy of the system is not necessarily conserved. The impact and deformation during collision may generate heat and sound. Part of the initial kinetic energy is transformed into other forms of energy. A useful way to visualise the deformation during collision is in terms of a ‘compressed spring’.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "If the ‘spring’ connecting the two masses regains its original shape without loss in energy, then the initial kinetic energy is equal to the final kinetic energy but the kinetic energy during the collision time ∆t is not constant. Such a collision is called an", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "elastic collision", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "On the other hand the deformation may not be relieved and the two bodies could move together after the collision. A collision in which the two particles move together after the collision is called a", "t": "text"}, {"s": "completely inelastic collision", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}, {"s": "The intermediate case where the deformation is partly relieved and some of the initial kinetic energy is lost is more common and is appropriately called an", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "inelastic collision", "t": "hl"}, {"s": ".", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000035060", "2000108476", "2000108604"]',NULL),
(35667,81,430,'paragraph',NULL,'Consider next an elastic collision. Using the above nomenclature with θ 1 = θ 2 = 0, the momentum and kinetic energy conservation equations are','[{"s": "Consider next an elastic collision.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Using the above nomenclature with θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 0, the momentum and kinetic energy conservation equations are", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000035068", "2000108480", "2000108606"]',NULL),
(35711,81,474,'paragraph',NULL,'Example 6.13 Consider the collision depicted in Fig. 6.10 to be between two billiard balls with equal masses m 1 = m 2 . The first ball is called the cue while the second ball is called the target. The billiard player wants to ‘sink’ the target ball in a corner pocket, which is at an angle θ 2 = 37°. Assume that the collision is elastic and that friction and rotational motion are not important. Obtain θ 1 .','[{"s": "Example 6.13", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "Consider the collision depicted in Fig.", "t": "hl"}, {"s": "6.10 to be between two billiard balls with equal masses m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= m", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": ". The first ball is called the cue while the second ball is called the target. The billiard player wants to ‘sink’ the target ball in a corner pocket, which is at an angle θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "2", "t": "text"}, {"s": "= 37°. Assume that the collision is elastic and that friction and rotational motion are not important. Obtain θ", "t": "text"}, {"s": "1", "t": "text"}, {"s": ".", "t": "text"}]','marked','["2000035362", "2000108485", "2000108608"]',NULL),
(35753,81,516,'paragraph',NULL,'4. The work done by a force can be calculated sometimes even if the exact nature of the force is not known. This is clear from Example 6.2 where the WE theorem is used in such a situation.','[{"s": "4. The work done by a force can be calculated sometimes even if the exact nature of the force is not known.", "t": "text"}, {"s": "This is clear from Example 6.2 where the WE theorem is used in such a situation.", "t": "hl"}]','marked','["2000035385", "2000108486", "2000108609"]',NULL);
