(200348,'biology','Choose the correct option- \n\nA) Muscle fibre contracts by the sliding of thick filaments over thin filaments. \n\nB) During sarcomere contraction A band shortens. \n\nC) 2 ATPs are used in one contraction of the muscle fibre. \n\nD) Reaction time of fibres is same in all the muscles.','Only C','Muscle fibre contracts by the sliding of thin filaments over thick filaments, during sarcomere contraction A band retains its length and reaction time of fibres varies in different muscles. NCERT Class 11th pg. 305 para1)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Muscle 2','Locomotion and Movement','1','mcq','both A and B','Only C','C and D','both B and D','NEW','11-305','11-220',NULL),
(200349,'biology','Muscle fibres are classified as red and white on the basis of-','Myoglobin content','Muscles are classified as Red and White fibres based primarily on the amount of red coloured myoglobin pigment in them. NCERT class 11th, pg 308 para 1)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Muscle 2','Locomotion and Movement','1','mcq','Mitochondria content','Myoglobin content','Mode of respiration','Pigment present in the muscle fibre','NEW','11-308','11-223',NULL),
(200350,'biology','Choose the correct option','Cardiac muscles are branched','Option 1 is correct, 2 is wrong because the coordinated movement of cilia are required for removal of dust particles from trachea and option 3 in incorrect because Euglena moves with the help of flagella and cilia. [ The coordinated movements of cilia in the trachea help us in removing dust particles. Flagellar movement helps in the swimming of spermatozoa, maintenance of water current in the canal system of sponges and in locomotion of Protozoans like Euglena] NCERT class 11th, pg 303','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Muscle 2','Locomotion and Movement','1','mcq','Cardiac muscles are branched','Coordinated movement of muscles help in removal of dust particles in trachea','Cilia helps in locomotion of Euglena','Both A and C','NEW','11-303','11-218',NULL),
(200351,'biology','Which is correct about, Visceral muscles:','Do not have intercalated discs and smooth under microscope','Visceral muscles do not exhibit any striation and are smooth in appearance. NCERT class 11th, pg 303','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Muscle 2','Locomotion and Movement','1','mcq','Striated under microscope','Smooth under microscope','Do not have intercalated discs','Do not have intercalated discs and smooth under microscope','NEW','11-303','11-218',NULL),
(200352,'biology','Muscle bundles are held together by','Collagenous connective tissue','Each organised skeletal muscle in our body is made of a number of muscle bundles or fascicles held together by a common collagenous connective tissue layer called fascia. NCERT Class 11th pg. 304.','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Muscle 2','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Muscle fibres','Collagenous connective tissue','Loose connective tissue','Sarcolemma','NEW','11-304','11-219',NULL),
(200353,'biology','Choose the correct option','Nervous system does not control the activity of cardiac muscles directly','1st option is incorrect because microfilaments are involved in ameboid movements not macrofilamnets. 2nd option is incorrect because visceral muscles are present in the inner wall of the hollow organs, and last option is incorrect because the actins are thinner as compared to myosin. (Reference: NCERT Class 11th pg. 307. para 1)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Muscle 2','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Macrofillaments are involved in the ameboid movement','Visceral muscles are located in the outer walls of hollow organs','Nervous system does not control the activity of cardiac muscles directly','Actin fillaments are thicker as compared to myosin filaments','NEW','11-307','11-222',NULL),
(200401,'chemistry','Gibbs energy change for a feasible reaction in metallurgy should be?','Negative','The criterion for the feasibility of a thermal reduction is that at a given temperature Gibbs energy change of the reaction must be negative. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 156 paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'1','mcq','Positive','Negative','Neutral','Zero','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200402,'chemistry','Which factor affect the Gibbs energy convert to negative, if enthalpy change is positive :','Increasing the temperature','If ∆S is positive, on increasing the temperature (T), the value of T∆S increases so that ∆H < T∆S. In this situation ∆G will become negative on increasing temperature. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 156 paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'3','mcq','Decreasing the temperature','Increasing the temperature','Keeping the temperature constant','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200403,'chemistry','Select the correct statement regarding Ellingham Diagram.','Any metal will not reduce the oxide of other metals which lies above it in the Ellingham diagram','The graphical representation of Gibbs energy was first used by H.J.T. Ellingham. This provides a sound basis for considering the choice of reducing agent in the reduction of oxides. This is known as Ellingham Diagram. Such diagrams help us in predicting the feasibility of thermal reduction of an ore. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'2','mcq','Any metal will reduce the oxide of other metal lie above it in Ellingham diagram','According to Ellingham diagram , Al will not reduce MgO at temperature below 1350°c','According to Ellingham diagram, Al will reduce MgO at temperature below 1350°c','Any metal will not reduce the oxide of other metals which lies above it in the Ellingham diagram','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200404,'chemistry','Plots of ∆fG vs T in Ellingham Diagram slopes downward for the reaction:','C + 1/2 O₂ → CO','In this reaction ∆S increases , Hence as the temperature increases, T∆S increases and hence ∆G(∆H – T∆S) decreases . In other words , the slope of the curve of formation decreases , However for all other oxides it increases. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 ).','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg + 1/2 O₂ →  MgO','2Ag + 1/2 O₂ → Ag₂O','C + 1/2 O₂ → CO','CO + 1/2O₂ →  CO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200405,'chemistry','Ellingham Diagram represents:','Change of ∆G with temperature','Gibbs energy (∆rGV) vs T plots (schematic) for the formation of some oxides per mole of oxygen consumed (Ellingham diagram). (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 156 paragraph last).','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'1','mcq','Change of ∆G with temperature','Change of ∆H with temperature','Change of ∆G with pressure','Change of (∆G–T∆S) with temperature','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200406,'chemistry','A→ CaO +MgO + B (calcination) When the colorless gas B is passed through lime water, initially a milky solution is obtained and on excess passage of gas B throughout the lime water, milkiness disappears.What is A and B?','CaCO₃.MgCO₃ , CO₂','CaCO₃.MgCO₃ → CaO +MgO + 2CO₂ (calcination) , CO₂ + Ca(OH)₂(lime water) → CaCO₃ + H₂O (milkiness) , CaCO₃ + H₂O + CO₂ → Ca(HCO₃)₂ (milkiness disappears) (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 155 paragraph last).','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'3','mcq','CaCO₃ , CO','MgCO₃ , SO₂','CaCO₃.MgCO₃ , CO₂','CaSO₄.MgCO₃ , SO₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200407,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct on the basis of the Ellingham diagram for Carbon?Refer diagram(0601)','The formation of CO₂ is energetically more favorable up to 710°C, but above 710°C the formation of CO is preferred','The Ellingham diagram is a graphical representation of the thermodynamic stability of oxides with respect to their formation from the corresponding elements. For carbon, the Ellingham diagram shows that up to 710°C, the formation of CO₂ is energetically more favorable. However, above 710°C, the formation of CO becomes more favorable. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'3','mcq','The formation of CO₂ is energetically more favorable at all temperatures','The formation of CO is energetically more favorable at all temperatures','The formation of CO₂ is energetically more favorable up to 710°C, but above 710°C the formation of CO is preferred','The formation of CO is energetically more favorable up to 710°C, but above 710°C the formation of CO₂ is preferred','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200408,'chemistry','Looking at the diagram: At what approximate temperature,  zinc and carbon have equal affinity for oxygen ? Refer diagram(0602)','1000°c','At approx 1000°c lines(C , CO) and (Zn , ZnO) intersect ; at this temperature zinc and carbon have equal affinity for oxygen. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'3','mcq','1000°c','1500°c','500°c','1200°c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200409,'chemistry','Looking at the diagram :At this temperature  ∆fG of the reaction is ZnO + C—> Zn + CO\ndiagram(0602)','Zero','At approx 1000°c , as zinc and carbon have equal affinity for oxygen , ∆fG of the reaction is zero. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'3','mcq','Positive','Negative','Zero','Nothing can be said','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200410,'chemistry','In the reaction of Ellingham Diagram, which gas is used?','O₂','The graphical representation of Gibbs energy was first used by H.J.T. Ellingham. This provides a sound basis for considering the choice of reducing agent in the reduction of oxides. This is known as Ellingham Diagram. Such diagrams help us in predicting the feasibility of thermal reduction of an ore. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂','O₂','CO₂','CO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200411,'chemistry','Why some lines are completely straight and some are not in Ellingham Diagram?','Change in phase','Each plot is a straight line expect when some changes take place in phase that is solid to liquid or liquid to gas takes place the temperature at which a change occurs is indicated by an increase in the slope on the positive side. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'2','mcq','Change in phase','Change in temperature','Condensation','Evaporation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200412,'chemistry','Complete oxidation of C produced:','CO₂','There may also be complete oxidation of carbon to CO₂ 1/2 C(s) + 1/2O₂(g) —>1/2CO₂ (g) .(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 paragraph last).','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'1','mcq','CO','CO₂','O₂','All of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200413,'biology','Label the following diagram showing contractile proteins.(2003)','An actin (thin) filament-a, Myosin monomer (Meromyosin)-b','Figure 20.3 NCERT Class 11 page no: 306 (a) An actin (thin) filament (b) Myosin monomer (Meromyosin)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Structure of Contractile Proteins','Locomotion and Movement','1','match','An actin (thin) filament,a','Myosin monomer (Meromyosin),b',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-306','11-221','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/200413q.png'),
(200414,'chemistry','What should be the ∆rG value of the both oxidation and reduction reactions combines?','Large negative','The role of reducing agent is to provide ∆rG negative and large enough to make the sum of ∆rG of the two reactions, i.e, oxidation of the reducing agent and reduction of the metal oxide negative.(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'3','mcq','Large positive','Large negative','Small negative','Small positive','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/200414q.png'),
(200415,'chemistry','Why in the formation of oxides, ∆S tend to be negative?','Because of decrease of molecular randomness','2xM(s) + O2(g) → 2MxO(s)\n\nIn this reaction, gas is consumed in the formation of oxide hence, molecular randomness decreases in the formation of oxide which leades to a negative value of ∆S as a result sign of T∆S term in equation (6.14) becomes positive. Subsequently ∆fGV shifts towards higher side despite rising T. The result is positive slope in the curve for most of the reactions for the formation of MxO(s). (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 158 paragraph 2).','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'3','mcq','Because of increase of ∆H value','Because of increase of T','Because of increase of molecular randomness','Because of decrease of molecular randomness','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/200415q.png'),
(200416,'chemistry','Select the correct statement for Ellingham Diagram','Each curve is the straight line except when some change takes place in phase (s—>l or l—>g)','Each curve is a straight line except when some change takes place in phase . (s—>l or l—>g) ,the temperature at which such changes occurs, is indicated by an increase in slope on +ve side.(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157).','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'2','mcq','The temperature at which such changes occurs, is indicated by an increase in slope on -ve side','Each curve is not a straight line except when some change takes place in phase . (s—>l or l—>g)','Each curve is the straight line except when some change takes place in phase (s—>l or l—>g)','All are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200417,'chemistry','Select the correct statements (Ellingham Diagram)\na. If the rising of temperature cause the graph crosses the line( ∆fG = 0 ) below this new T,then  ∆G  will be negative and oxide will be stable.\nb. If the rising of temperature cause the graph crosses the line( ∆fG = 0 ) above this new T,then  ∆G  will be positive and oxide will be unstable.\nc. If the rising of temperature cause the graph crosses the line( ∆fG = 0 ) above this new T,then  ∆G  will be negative and oxide will be unstable.\nd. If the rising of temperature cause the graph crosses the line( ∆fG = 0 ) below this new T,then  ∆G  will be positive and oxide will be stable.','Only a and b','Theoretically all oxides can be decomposed to give the metal and  dioxygen if a sufficiently high temperature can be obtained. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a','Only a and b','Only c and d','All statements are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200418,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about the advantages of roasting of sulfide ore before reduction is not true?','Carbon and hydrogen are suitable reducing agent for metal sulfides','Some metal oxides get reduced easily while others are very difficult to be reduced (reduction means electron gain by the metal ion). In any case, heating is required. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 156)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'3','mcq','∆fG of the sulphide is greater in CS2 and H₂S','∆fG is negative for roasting of sulfide ore to oxide','Roasting of the sulfide to oxide is thermodynamically feasible','Carbon and hydrogen are suitable reducing agent for metal sulfides','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200419,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding temperature at which carbon reduces FeO to iron and produces CO: refer diagram(0603)','About the temperature at point A','A metal oxide placed higher in the diagram can be reduced by the element involved in the formation of the oxide placed lower in the diagram this condition is fulfilled above temperature at point A. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Thermodynamic principles of metallurgy',NULL,'3','mcq','Below the temperature at point A','Approximately at the temperature corresponding to point A','Above the temperature at point A but below the temperature at point D','About the temperature at point A','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200501,'chemistry','Iron ores are subjected to calcination/roasting for the removal of?','To remove H₂O','After concentration, mixture of oxide ores of iron (Fe2O3, Fe3O4) is subjected to calcination/roasting to remove water, to decompose carbonates and to oxidise sulphides. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 159 paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Applications',NULL,'2','mcq','To remove O₂','To remove H₂O','Both of these','None of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200502,'chemistry','Why Iron ores are subjected to calcination/roasting?','To oxidize sulphides','After concentration, mixture of oxide ores of iron (Fe2O3, Fe3O4) is subjected to calcination/roasting to remove water, to decompose carbonates and to oxidise sulphides.  (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 159 paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Applications',NULL,'1','mcq','To reduce sulphides','To oxidize sulphides','To oxidize carbonated','To reduce carbonates','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200503,'chemistry','Iron oxides are reduced to its metal in:','Blast furnace','These are mixed with limestone and coke and fed into a Blast furnace from its top, in which the oxide is reduced to the metal. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 159 paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Applications',NULL,'2','mcq','Reverberatory furnace','Blast furnace','Electrolytic cell','None of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200504,'chemistry','Which form of carbon is added to reduce Fe','CO','The CO and heat move to the upper part of the furnace. In upper part, the temperature is lower and the iron oxides (Fe2O3 and Fe3O4) coming from the top are reduced in steps to FeO. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 160 paragraph first)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Applications',NULL,'2','mcq','CO','Coke','Peat','Graphite','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200505,'chemistry','The lowest portion of blast furnace gives the temperature of about ?','2200 K','A blast of hot air is blown from the bottom of the furnace by burning coke in the lower portion to give temperature upto about 2200K.(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 160 paragraph 1st)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Applications',NULL,'2','mcq','1570 K','1270 K','2200 K','1900 K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200506,'chemistry','Burning of _____ gives the heat required for the oxidation of Fe in blast furnace.','Coke','A blast of hot air is blown from the bottom of the furnace by burning coke in the lower portion to give temperature upto about 2200K. The burning of coke, therefore, supplies most of the heat required in the process. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 160 paragraph first)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Applications',NULL,'1','mcq','Slag','Flux','Coke','Limestone','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200507,'chemistry','What Iron oxides are added to the blast furnace for reduction?','Fe₂O₃ , Fe₃O₄','The iron oxides (Fe₂O₃ and Fe₃O₄) coming from the top are reduced in steps to FeO. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 160 paragraph first)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Applications',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe₂O₃ , FeO','Fe₃O₄ , FeO','Fe₂O₃ , Fe₃O₄','FeO , FeCO₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200508,'chemistry','The mixture of ore, Coke and ______ is added to blast furnace.','Limestone','The ore limestone and coke and fed into a Blast furnace from its top . (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 159 paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Applications',NULL,'1','mcq','Slag','Flux','Coke','Limestone','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200509,'chemistry','How the reduction takes place in the case of Iron oxides in blast furnace?','Fe₂O₃—>Fe₃O₄—>FeO','Fe₂O₃ is first reduced to Fe₃O₄ and then to FeO . (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 160 paragraph first)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Applications',NULL,'3','mcq','Fe₃O₄—>Fe₂O₃—>FeO','FeO—>Fe₂O₄—>Fe₃O₄','Fe₂O₃—>Fe₃O₄—>FeO','Fe₂O₃—>FeO₃O₄—>Fe₃O₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(200510,'chemistry','At what temperature ranges iron oxides inter convert to one another?','500 K - 800 K','At 500 – 800 K (lower temperature range in the blast furnace),Fe₂O₃ is first reduced to Fe3O4 and then to FeO . (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 160 paragraph first)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Applications',NULL,'2','mcq','900 K - 1500 K','500 K - 800 K','1500 K - 2100 K','2100 K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
