(201001,'biology','Points of contact between bones are called-','Joints','Joints are points of contact between bones, or between bones and cartilages..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 312;paragraph No.1)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Joints','Locomotion and Movement','1','mcq','Tendons','Joints','Ligaments','None','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201002,'biology','Points of contact between bones and cartilages are called-','Joints','Joints are points of contact between bones, or between bones and cartilages..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 312;paragraph No.1)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Joints','Locomotion and Movement','1','mcq','Tendons','Joints','Ligaments','None','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201003,'biology','There are _____ types of Joints.','3','Joints have been classified into three major structural forms, namely, fibrous, cartilaginous and synovial..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 312;paragraph No.1)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Joints','Locomotion and Movement','1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201004,'biology','Which joints allow no movement?','Fibrous','Fibrous joints do not allow any movement. This type of joint is shown by the flat skull bones which fuse end-to-end with the help of dense fibrous connective tissues.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 312;paragraph No.2)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Joints','Locomotion and Movement','1','mcq','Cartilagenous','Fibrous','Synovial','Ball and socket joint','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201005,'biology','Which joint allows limited movement?','Cartilagenous','In cartilaginous joints, the bones involved are joined together with the help of cartilages. The joint between the adjacent vertebrae in the vertebral column is of this pattern and it permits limited movements..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 312;paragraph No.3)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Joints','Locomotion and Movement','1','mcq','Cartilagenous','Fibrous','Synovial','Ball and socket joint','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201006,'chemistry','In the extraction of copper from its sulphide ore, the metal is finally obtained by the reduction of cuprous oxide with:','Copper (I) sulphide','It is an example of auto reduction. 2Cu₂O + Cu₂S 6Cu + SO₂. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 156 , line 3) (NEET 2015, 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon monoxide','Copper (I) sulphide','Sulphur dioxide','Iron (II) sulphide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201007,'chemistry','The metal oxide which cannot be reduced to metal by carbon is:','Al₂O₃','Oxides of less reactive metals (like PbO, ZnO, Fe₂O₃) can be reduced by carbon. While oxides of very reactive metals (like Al₂O₃) can be reduced only by the electrolytic method. According to Ellingham diagram,  C is placed lower than Al so can\'t reduce Al . (Karnataka NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Al₂O₃','PbO','ZnO','Fe₂O₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201008,'chemistry','Extraction of gold and silver involves leaching with CN–ion. Silver is later recovered by:','displacement with Zn','Extraction of gold and silver involves leaching the metal with CN– and the metals silver and gold are later recovered by displacement method. 4M(s) + 8CN⁻(aq) + 2H₂O(aq) + O₂(g) 4[M(CN)₂]⁻(aq) + 4OH⁻(aq)2[M(CN)₂]⁻(aq) + Zn(s) 2M(s) + [Zn(CN)₄]²⁻(aq) (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 164, paragraph 1)  (NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Distillation','Zone refining','Displacement with Zn','Liquation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201009,'chemistry','Identify the correct statement from the following :\n\n(a) Wrought iron is impure iron with 4% carbon.\n\n(b) Blister copper has blistered appearance due to evolution of CO₂\n\n(c) Vapour phase refining is carried out for Nickel by van Arkel method.\n\n(d) Pig iron can be moulded into a variety of shapes','d','(a) Pig iron is impure iron with 4% carbon.\n(b) Blister copper has blistered appearance due to evolution of SO₂\n(c) Vapour phase refining is carried out for nickel by Mond’s process \n(d) Pig iron can be moulded into a variety of shapes. (NEET 2020)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201010,'chemistry','Match items of Column I with the items of Column II and assign the correct code :\n Column I Column II\n(A) Cyanide process (i) Ultrapure Ge\n(B) Froth floatation (ii) Dressing of ZnS process\n(C) Electrolytic (iii) Extraction of Alreduction\n(D) Zone refining (iv) Extraction of Au(v) Purification of Ni','A (iv), B (ii), C (iii), D (i)','Cyanide process-Extraction of Au;  Broth floatation-Dressing of ZnS ; Electrolytic reduction-Extraction of Al; Zone refining- Ultra pure Ge. (Reference: NCERT class 12 unit 6 , refining portion )\n(NEET 2016)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','A (i), B (ii) ,C (iii), D (iv)','A (iii), B (iv), C (v), D (i)','A (iv), B (ii), C (iii), D (i)','A (ii) , B (iii) ,C (i), D (v)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201011,'chemistry','Purest form of iron is:','Wrought iron','Wrought Iron is the purest form of iron. For pure carbon steel (iron-carbon alloys), the carbon content of steel is between 0.002 percent and 2.14 percent by weight. (NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cast iron','Hard steel','Wrought iron','Stainless steel','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201012,'chemistry','Thomas slag is:','Tetra calcium phosphate','The tetra calcium phosphate is known as Thomas slag. The chemical formula of Thomas slag is Ca₄(PO₄)₂O . It is used as a phosphorus fertilizer because it is soluble in water and contains a high amount of phosphorus. (NEET 2004)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Calcium silicate','Calcium Phosphate','Tetra calcium phosphate','Calcium ammonium phosphate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201013,'chemistry','Corundum is:','Al₂O₃','Corundum is a crystalline form of aluminium oxide typically containing traces of iron, titanium, vanadium and chromium. (NEET 2007)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','SrO₂','Al₂O₃','CaCl₂','Cu₂Cl₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201014,'chemistry','Heating of carbonate ores to remove carbon is called as:','Calcination','The root of the word calcination refers to its most prominent use, which is to remove carbon from limestone through combustion to yield calcium oxide (quicklime). (NEET 2010)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Roasting','Calcination','Smelting','Fluxing','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201015,'chemistry','Sulphide ores are common for the metals:','Ag, Cu and Pb','Sulfide minerals are the source of various precious metals, most notably gold, silver, and platinum. (NEET 2007)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ag, Cu and Pb','Ag, Cu and Sn','Ag, Mg and Pb','Al, Cu and Pb','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201016,'chemistry','Which element occurs freely in nature?','Nitrogen','The nitrogen which exists free in nature which is made up of 78% of Earth\'s atmosphere and naturally found in mineral deposits, soil and organic compounds. (NEET 2012)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Nitrogen','Sulphur','Phosphorus','Magnesium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201017,'chemistry','Silver obtained by argentiferous lead is purified by:','Cupellation','If metal possess the impurity or another metal which forms volatile oxide. Then cupellation method is used. (NEET 2013)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Distillation','Froth Floatation','Cupellation','Reacting with KCN','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201018,'chemistry','Which process used in purification of Bauxite?','Hall\'s Method','The Bayer process is the principal industrial means of refining bauxite to produce alumina (aluminium oxide) and was developed by Carl Josef Bayer. (NEET 2005)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Hall\'s method','Bayer\'s method','Serpeck\'s method','Frankland\'s method','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201019,'chemistry','Method used for obtaining highly pure silicon used as a semiconductor material, is:','Zone refining','Silicon is purified by zone-refining process because the impurities present in it are more soluble in the liquid phase than in the solid phase. (NEET 2017)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidation','Electrochemical','Crystallization','Zone refining','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201020,'chemistry','The method of zone refining of metals is based on the principle of:','Greater solubility of the impurity in the molten state than in the solid','The method of zone refining of metals is based on the principle of greater solubility of the impurity in the molten state than in the solid. Elements which are used as semiconductors like Si, Ge, Ga etc. are refined by this method. (NEET 2003)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Greater solubility of the impurity in the molten state than in the solid','Greater mobility of the pure metal than that of the impurity','Higher melting point of the impurity than that of the pure metal','Greater noble character of the solid metal than that of the impurity','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201101,'chemistry','What is the difference between minerals and ores?  \n \n a)Minerals come from nonmetals, ores come from metals. \n \n b)Minerals can be found in earth\'s crust and ores are made in laboratory conditions. \n \n c)Minerals are naturally occurring chemical substance in the earth\'s crust, out of many minerals, a few are viable to be used as source of the metal that can be found in it, and they are known as ores. \n \n d)Minerals that are  to be used as a source of a metal which is part of it,are called ores.','c,d','For obitaining a particular metal,first we look for mineral which are naturally occurring chemical substances in the earth\'s crust and are obtained through mining.Out of  many minerals in which a metal may be found,only a  few are viable to be used as source of that metal. Such minerals are known as ores. ( Reference:NCERT class 12, unit 6,page 152,paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','c,d','a,c','a,d','b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201102,'chemistry','Which is ore of Aluminium? \n \n a)Bauxite \n \n b)Siderite \n \n c)Malachnite \n \n d)Cryolite','a,d','Bauxite AlOx(OH)3-2x\nCryolite Na3AlF6\n\n(Reference:NCERT class 12,unit 6, page 152 table 6.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,d','a,c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201103,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nA: For the purpose of extraction,for iron,usually are used oxide ores. \n \nR: They don\'t produce polluting gasses','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','For the purpose of extraction, bauxite is chosen for aluminium. For iron, usually the oxide ores which are abundant and do not produce polluting gases (like SO2 that is produced in case of iron pyrites).\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12,unit 6,page 153,paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201104,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\n\nA: Hydraulic Washing is a type of gravity separation. \n \nR: It\'s based on the difference between specific gravities of the ores and the gangue particles.','Both statement A and statement R are true and R is a correct explanation of statement A','Hydraulic Wasghing is based on the difference between specific gravities of the ore and the gangue particles. It is therefore a type of gravity separation. In one such process, an upward stream of running water is used to wash the powdered ore. The lighter gangue particles are washed away and the heavier ore particles are left behind.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12, unit 6 page 153, paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both statement A and statement R are true and R is a correct explanation of statement A','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/201104q.png'),
(201105,'chemistry','Conentration of Bauxite could be carried at \n \n a) 478 K, 36 bar \n \n b)273K,27 bar \n \n c)423 K,46 bar \n \n d) 519 K 35 bar','a,d','Bauxite is the principal ore of aluminium. It usually contains SiO2,iron oxides and titanium oxide (TiO2) as impurities. Concentration is carried out by heating the powdered ore with a concentrated solution  of NaOH at 473 – 523 K and 35 – 36 bar pressure.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12,unit 6, page 154, paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,d','a,b','c,b','c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201106,'chemistry','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\n\nA: Sulphide ores are converted to oxide before reduction. \n \nR: To extract metals from ore, we must convert to a form ,suitable for reduction to metal.','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','To extract metal from concentrated ore, it must be converted to a form which is suitable for reduction to metal. Usually sulphide ores are converted to oxide before reduction because oxides are easier to reduce.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12, unit 6, page 155, paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/201106q.png'),
(201107,'chemistry','At what temperature is Coke burning in blast furnace? \n \n a)T=1270 K \n \n b)T>1070 K \n \n c)T<1000 K','a only','Burning of Coke 2170K\n(Reference: NCERT class 12, unit 6, page 160, fig. 6.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,c','c,b','a only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/201107q.png'),
(201108,'chemistry','Which metals are used in zone refining ? \n\n a)germanium\n\n b)galium \n \n c)indium \n \n d)strontium','a,b,c','Zone refining is very useful for producing semiconductor and other metals of very high purity, e.g., germanium, silicon, boron, gallium and indium.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12, unit 6, page 165, paragraph first)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b,c','d,c,b','d,c,a','a,d,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201109,'chemistry','Which elements are extracted by electrolytic refining? \n \n a)Copper \n \n b)Zinc \n \n c)Lead \n \n d)Magnesium','a,b','Copper is refined using an electrolytic method. Anodes are of impure copper and pure copper strips are taken as cathode. The electrolyte is acidified solution of copper sulphate and the net result of electrolysis is the transfer of copper in pure form from the anode to the cathode:\nAnode: Cu → Cu2+ + 2 e–\nCathode: Cu2+ + 2e– → Cu (6.51)\nImpurities from the blister copper deposit as anode mud which contains antimony, selenium, tellurium, silver, gold and platinum; recovery of these elements may meet the cost of refining. Zinc may also be refined this way.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12, unit 6, page 164, paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','c.b','a,b','d,a','c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201201,'chemistry','In the extraction of chlorine by electrolysis of brine ............','oxidation of CI- ion to chlorine gas occurs','In the electrolysis of an aqueous NaCl solution,H2 gas is evolved at the cathode and chlorine at the anode. The solution contains NaOH and is therefore, basic in nature. It is always better to prepare chlorine by Down\'s process. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 168 , )','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','oxidation of CI- ion to chlorine gas occurs','reduction of CI- ion to chlorine gas occurs','for overall reaction ΔG has negative value','a displacement reaction takes place','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201202,'chemistry','When copper ore is mixed with silica, in a reverberatory furnace copper matte is produced. The copper matte contains','sulphides of copper (I) and iron (II)','In the furnace, iron oxide ‘slags of’ as iron silicate is formed. Copper is produced in the form of copper matte. This contains Cu2S and FeS. \n\nIn the furnace, iron oxide ‘slags of’ as iron silicate is formed. Copper is produced in the form of copper matte. This contains Cu2S and FeS. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 161 paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','sulphides of copper (II) and iron (II)','sulphides of copper (II) and iron (III)','sulphides of copper (I) and iron (II)','sulphides of copper (I) and iron (III)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201203,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions is an example of autoreduction?','Cu2O +1/2Cu2S ----> 3Cu + 1/2SO2','An autoreduction reaction is a type of redox reaction in which a substance acts as both an oxidizing agent and a reducing agent. In other words, the substance is both oxidized and reduced in the same reaction.\n\nAmong the given reactions, only the reaction:\n\nCu2O +1/2Cu2S ----> 3Cu + 1/2SO2\n\nis an example of autoreduction. In this reaction, both Cu2O and Cu2S act as both oxidizing and reducing agents. Cu2O is reduced to Cu while Cu2S is oxidized to SO2.\n\nThe other reactions involve a transfer of electrons from one reactant to another, but not autoreduction.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe3O4 +  4CO--> 3Fe + 4CO2','Cu2O + C ---> 2Cu  + CO','Cu2+ + Fe -----> Cu + Fe2+','Cu2O +1/2Cu2S ----> 3Cu + 1/2SO2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201204,'chemistry','A number of elements are available in earth’s crust but most abundant elements are .............','Al and Fe','Among metals, aluminium is the most abundant. In fact, it is the third most abundant element in earth’s crust (8.3% approx. by weight). It is a major component of many igneous minerals including mica and clays. Many gemstones are impure forms of Al2O3. For example, gems ‘ruby’ and ‘sapphire’ have Cr and Co respectively as impurity. "For iron, usually the oxide ores which are abundant.  (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 page 152 paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Al and Fe','Al and Cu','Fe and Cu','Cu and Ag','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201205,'chemistry','Zone refining is based on the principle that ..........','impurities are more soluble in molten metal than in solid metal.','Zone refining is the method for an element in which impurities present have chemical properties close to the properties of those elements. The method of zone refining of metals is based on the principle of greater solubility of the impurity in the molten state than in the solid. Elements which are used as semiconductors like Si, Ge, Ga etc. are refined by this method. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 168 , Exercise 6.11)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','impurities of low boiling metals can be separated by distillation.','impurities are more soluble in molten metal than in solid metal.','different components of a mixture are differently adsorbed on an adsorbent.','vapours of volatile compound can be decomposed in pure metal.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201206,'chemistry','In the extraction of copper from its sulphide ore, the metal is formed by the reduction of Cu2O with','Cu2S','In the extraction of copper from its sulphide ore, copper metal is obtained by the reduction of copper (I) oxide (Cu2O) with copper (I) sulphide (Cu2S) at high temperature. The overall reaction is:\n\n2Cu2O + Cu2S → 6Cu + SO2\n\nDuring the reaction, copper (I) sulphide acts as a reducing agent and is oxidized to sulfur dioxide (SO2). Copper (I) oxide is simultaneously reduced to copper (Cu) metal.\n\nThe solidified 98% copper obtained has blistered appearance due to the evolution of SO2 and so it is called blister copper. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 162 paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','FeS','CO','Cu2S','SO2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201207,'chemistry','Brine is electrolysed by using inert electrodes. The reaction at anode is ......... Refer diagram(n0605)','a','The possible reactions occurring at the anode during the electrolysis of brine (NaCl solution) using inert electrodes are:\n\nCl⁻(aq) → 1/2Cl₂(g) + e⁻ ; E° = -1.36 V\n2H₂O(l) → O₂(g) + 4H⁺(aq) + 4e⁻ ; E° = -1.23 V\nAccording to the standard electrode potential values, the oxidation of chloride ions has a more negative E° value than the oxidation of water molecules. Therefore, the oxidation of chloride ions to chlorine gas is thermodynamically favored over the oxidation of water to oxygen gas.\n\nHowever, the kinetic factors also play an important role in determining the product of electrolysis. The oxidation of water molecules to oxygen gas is a relatively slow process compared to the oxidation of chloride ions to chlorine gas. This means that even though the oxidation of water has a higher standard electrode potential, it occurs at a slower rate than the oxidation of chloride ions.\n\nAs a result, during the electrolysis of brine, even though water molecules theoretically should get oxidized at the anode, the oxidation of chloride ions is preferred because it occurs more rapidly. Hence, chlorine gas is produced instead of oxygen gas at the anode.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/201207q.png'),
(210404,'biology','In a resting state, the neuron cell membrane is impermeable to-','Both A and B','When a neuron is not conducting any impulse, i.e., Resting, the axonal membrane is comparatively more permeable to potassium ions (K+ ) and nearly impermeable to sodium ions (Na+ ). Similarly, the membrane is impermeable to negatively charged proteins present in the axoplasm. Consequently, the axoplasm inside the axon therefore have high concentration of k + and negatively charged proteins and low concentration of Na+.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2) .','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','negatively charged proteins in axoplasm','Sodium ion','Potassium ion','Both A and B','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(250703,'chemistry','Methanol is a colourless liquid and boils at?','337 K','Methanol is a colourless liquid and boils at 337 K.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'1','mcq','337 K','250 K','550 K','227 K','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(201208,'chemistry','In the metallurgy of aluminium ............','graphide anode is oxidised to carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide','In the metallurgy of aluminum, the electrolysis is performed in a steel vessel lined with carbon. The cathode is made of carbon, while the anode is made of graphite. During the electrolysis process, aluminum ions (Al3+) are reduced at the cathode to form aluminum metal (Al), while the graphite anode is oxidized to produce carbon dioxide (CO2) and carbon monoxide (CO).\n\nThe chemical reaction occurring during this process is represented as:\n\n2Al2O3 + 3C → 4Al + 3CO2 + 2CO\n\nThis process is known as the Hall-Heroult process. The electrolytic reactions at the cathode and anode are as follows:\n\nAt the cathode: Al3+ + 3e- → Al (reduction reaction)\n\nAt the anode: C (graphite) + O2- → CO2 + 4e- (oxidation reaction)\n2C (graphite) + O2- → 2CO (oxidation reaction)\n\nThe oxidation state of oxygen changes from -2 to 0 during the oxidation reactions occurring at the anode, where oxygen combines with carbon to form CO and CO2. However, the overall reaction involved in the process does not involve a net change in the oxidation state of oxygen.\n\nHence, during the electrolysis of aluminum, aluminum ions are reduced at the cathode to form aluminum metal, while graphite anode is oxidized to produce carbon dioxide and carbon monoxide.\n\n (Reference: NCERT  , class 12 , unit 6 , page 163 paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Al3+ is oxidised to Al (s).','graphide anode is oxidised to carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide','oxidation state of oxygen changes in the reaction at anode','oxidation state of oxygen changes in the overall reaction involved in the process','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201209,'chemistry','Electrolytic refining is used to purify which of the following metals?','Cu and Zn','Electrolytic refining is commonly used to purify copper and zinc. The process involves using an impure metal as the anode and a pure metal as the cathode, with an electrolyte solution in between. When an electric current is passed through the solution, the impurities in the anode are oxidized and dissolve into the electrolyte, while the pure metal is deposited onto the cathode. The impurities that dissolve into the electrolyte settle to the bottom and form a sludge, which is known as the anode mud.\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 165 , paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu and Zn','Ge and Si','Zr and Ti','Zn and Hg','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201210,'chemistry','Extraction of gold and silver involves leaching the metal with CN- ion.The metal is recovered by ......','displacement of metal by some other metal from the complex ion.','In the extraction of gold and silver, the metal is first leached with the CN- ion to form a metal-cyanide complex. The metal is then recovered by displacement of the metal from the complex ion by some other metal, such as zinc. This is an oxidation reaction, as the metal is being oxidized to a higher oxidation state, and the reducing agent, in this case, zinc, is being oxidized to its higher oxidation state. The overall reaction involves the transfer of electrons from the reducing agent to the metal ion, resulting in the reduction of the metal ion to its elemental form and the oxidation of the reducing agent.\n\n4 Au(s) + 8 CN⁻(aq) + 2 H₂O(l) + O₂(g) → 4 [Au(CN)₂]⁻(aq) + 4 OH⁻(aq)\n\n4 [Au(CN)₂]⁻(aq) + Zn(s) → 2 [Zn(CN)₄]²⁻(aq) + 4 Au(s)\n(Reference: NCERT  , class 12 , unit 6 , page 164 paragraph first)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','displacement of metal by some other metal from the complex ion.','roasting of metal complex.','calcination followed by roasting.','thermal decomposition of metal complex.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201211,'chemistry','Choose the correct option of temperature at which carbon reduces FeO to iron and produces CO. Refer diagram(n0606)','Above temperature at point A','The reduction of FeO to iron by carbon and the production of carbon monoxide occurs above temperature at point A, but below temperature at point D. This is because the Ellingham diagram shows that the formation of FeO has a more negative value of Gibbs free energy compared to the formation of CO, above point A. \n\nThis means that at temperatures above point A, FeO can be reduced by carbon to form Fe and CO can be produced as a byproduct of the reaction. \n\nHowever, at temperatures above point D, the formation of CO has a more negative value of Gibbs free energy compared to the formation of FeO, which means that carbon will reduce CO to form CO2 instead of reducing FeO to form Fe.\n\n\n (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Below temperature at point A','Approximately at the temperature corresponding to point A','Above temperature at point A but below temperature at point D','Above temperature at point A','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/201211q.png'),
(201212,'chemistry','Below point ‘A’ FeO can .............. Refer diagram(n0606)','be reduced by carbon monoxide only','Below point A, the Gibbs free energy change for the formation of CO₂ from CO is more negative than the Gibbs free energy change for the formation of FeO. This means that carbon monoxide (CO) can reduce FeO to Fe metal, but carbon (C) cannot because the Gibbs free energy change for the formation of CO₂ from C is not as negative as the one for CO. Therefore, below point A, FeO can only be reduced by carbon monoxide (CO) and not by both carbon monoxide and carbon (C) or carbon alone. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','be reduced by carbon monoxide only','be reduced by both carbon monoxide and carbon.','be reduced by carbon only.','not be reduced by both carbon and carbon monoxide.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/201212q.png'),
(201213,'chemistry','For the reduction of FeO at the temperature corresponding to point D, which of the following statements is correct?Refer diagram(n0606)','ΔG value for the overall reduction reaction with carbon monoxide is zero','At point D, the Gibbs free energy change curve for the formation of CO₂ from CO intersects with the Gibbs free energy change curve for the formation of FeO from Fe. This means that the overall Gibbs free energy change for the reduction of FeO with CO is zero, indicating that the reaction is at equilibrium. Therefore, statement (a) "ΔG value for the overall reduction reaction with carbon monoxide is zero" is correct. Statements (b), (c), and (d) are incorrect because they suggest that the Gibbs free energy change is positive, which would not be consistent with an equilibrium point on the diagram. (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 157 Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ΔG value for the overall reduction reaction with carbon monoxide is zero','ΔG value for the overall reduction reaction with a mixture of 1 mol carbon and 1 mol oxygen is positive.','ΔG value for the overall reduction reaction with a mixture of 2 mol carbon and\n1 mol oxygen will be positive.','ΔG value for the overall reduction reaction with carbon monoxide is negative','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/201213q.png'),
(201214,'chemistry','Which of the following options are correct?\n\n(a) Cast iron is obtained by remelting pig iron with scrap iron and coke using hot air blast.\n\n(b) In extraction of silver, silver is extracted as cationic complex.\n\n(c) Nickel is purified by zone refining.\n\n(d) Zr and Ti are purified by van Arkel method.','a,d','(a) Cast iron is obtained by remelting pig iron with scrap iron and coke using hot air blast - This statement is correct. Cast iron is obtained by remelting pig iron along with scrap iron and coke using a hot air blast in a blast furnace.\n\n(b) In extraction of silver, silver is extracted as cationic complex - This statement is incorrect. Silver is extracted as an anionic complex [Ag(CN)₂]⁻ in the extraction process.\n\n(c) Nickel is purified by zone refining - This statement is incorrect. Nickel is purified by a method called vapour phase refining or Mond process, which involves heating the impure nickel with carbon monoxide to form a volatile complex.\n\n(d) Zr and Ti are purified by van Arkel method - This statement is correct. Zirconium and Titanium are purified by the Van Arkel method, which involves the formation and subsequent decomposition of a volatile metal iodide.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 165 , paragraph last)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201215,'chemistry','In the extraction of aluminium by Hall-Heroult process, purified Al2O3 is mixed with CaF2 to\n\n(a) lower the melting point of Al2O3\n\n(b) increase the conductivity of molten mixture.\n\n(c) reduce Al3+ into Al(s)\n\n(d) acts as catalyst','a,b','In the Hall-Heroult process for the extraction of aluminum, Al2O3 is mixed with cryolite (Na3AlF6) and calcium fluoride (CaF2) to form a molten electrolyte. The addition of cryolite and calcium fluoride serves two main purposes:\n\n(a) Lower the melting point of Al2O3: The melting point of pure Al2O3 is very high (around 2050°C), which makes it difficult and energy-intensive to melt it. However, by adding cryolite and calcium fluoride, the melting point of the mixture is lowered to around 950°C, which reduces the energy requirements for the process.\n\n(b) Increase the conductivity of the molten mixture: The addition of cryolite and calcium fluoride also increases the conductivity of the molten mixture, allowing for the flow of electrical current through the electrolyte. This is essential for the electrolysis process, as it allows for the reduction of Al3+ ions into aluminum metal at the cathode.\n\nTherefore, the correct options are (a) and (b). Options (c) and (d) are incorrect. Calcium fluoride does not directly reduce Al3+ ions into aluminum metal, and it does not act as a catalyst in the process.\n(Reference: NCERT  , class 12 , unit 6 , page 163 paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201216,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct about the role of substances added in the froth floatation process?\n\n(a) Collectors enhance the non-wettability of the mineral particles.\n\n(b) Collectors enhance the wettability of gangue particles.\n\n(c) By using depressants in the process two sulphide ores can be separated.\n\n(d) Froth stabilisers decrease wettability of gangue.','a,c','The correct options are (a) and (c). Option (b) is incorrect, as collectors actually enhance the wettability of the mineral particles, not the gangue particles. Option (d) is also incorrect, as froth stabilizers do not affect the wettability of the gangue or mineral particles, but rather help to stabilize the froth and prevent it from breaking down.\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 page 154 paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201217,'chemistry','In the froth floatation process, zinc sulphide and lead sulphide can be separated by ...............\n\na. using collectors\n\nb. adjusting the proportion of oil to water\n\nc. using depressant\n\nd. using froth stabilisers','b,c','The correct answer is (b) and (c). In the froth floatation process, zinc sulphide and lead sulphide can be separated by using a depressant, such as NaCN, which selectively prevents ZnS from coming to the froth. Additionally, adjusting the proportion of oil to water can also aid in the separation process. Therefore, both options (b) and (c) are correct choices. Collectors and froth stabilisers are also used in froth floatation, but they are not specifically relevant to the separation of zinc sulphide and lead sulphide.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 page 154 paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201218,'chemistry','Common impurities present in bauxite are ........\n\na. CuO\nb. ZnO\nc. Fe2O3\nd. SiO2','c,d','The correct answer is (c) and (d). Bauxite is an ore of aluminum and it commonly contains impurities such as iron oxide (Fe2O3) and silica (SiO2). These impurities can make the extraction of aluminum from bauxite more difficult and can also affect the quality of the final aluminum product. While copper oxide (CuO) and zinc oxide (ZnO) can also be present in bauxite, they are not considered to be common impurities. Therefore, options (a) and (b) are incorrect.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201219,'chemistry','Which of the following ores are concentrated by froth floatation?\n\na. Hematite\nb. Galena\nc. Copper pyrites\nd. Magnetite','b,c','The correct answer is (b) Galena and (c) Copper pyrites. Froth flotation is a method commonly used for the concentration of sulfide ores, such as galena (PbS) and copper pyrites (CuFeS2). Hematite (Fe2O3) and magnetite (Fe3O4) are oxide ores and are generally concentrated by magnetic separation. So, option (a) and (d) are incorrect.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201220,'chemistry','For the metallurgical process of which of the ores calcined ore can be reduced by carbon?\n\n(a) Haematite \n\n(b) Calamine \n\n(c) Iron pyrites \n\n(d) Sphalerite','a,b','Haematite (Fe2O3) and calamine (ZnO) are oxide ores of iron and zinc, respectively. These oxide ores can be reduced by carbon in the metallurgical process, as carbon can reduce the oxide to the metal. On the other hand, iron pyrites (FeS2) and sphalerite (ZnS) are sulfide ores of iron and zinc, respectively.\n\n Sulfide ores cannot be reduced by carbon, and other methods such as roasting or smelting are used for their reduction.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 168)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(180806,'chemistry','Assertion: In collision theory activation energy and proper orientation of the molecules together determine the criteria for an effective collision.  \nReason: Collision theory considers atoms / molecules to be hard spheres and ignores their structural aspects.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference NCERT Class 12; Page 117; Paragraph 6)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Collision theory of chemical reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-117','12-083',NULL),
(201221,'chemistry','The main reactions occurring in blast furnace during extraction of iron from haematite ore .......... Refer diagram(n0607)','a,d','Slag is formed by the reaction between the impurities in the iron ore and the flux (CaO) added to the furnace. The slag floats on top of the molten iron and is removed.\n\nSo, the main reactions occurring in the blast furnace during the extraction of iron from hematite ore are:\n\n(a) Fe2O3 + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO2\nThis reaction represents the reduction of hematite to iron.\n\n(d) CaO + SiO2 → CaSiO3\nThis reaction represents the formation of slag, which helps in the removal of impurities from the iron ore.\nRefer diagram(ne0605). (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 160 )','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/201221q.png'),
(201301,'chemistry','Cyanide process is used for the extraction of[AIEEE 2002]','silver','In the extraction of gold and silver, the metal is first leached with the CN- ion to form a metal-cyanide complex. The metal is then recovered by displacement of the metal from the complex ion by some other metal, such as zinc. This is an oxidation reaction, as the metal is being oxidized to a higher oxidation state, and the reducing agent, in this case, zinc, is being oxidized to its higher oxidation state. The overall reaction involves the transfer of electrons from the reducing agent to the metal ion, resulting in the reduction of the metal ion to its elemental form and the oxidation of the reducing agent.\n\n4 Au(s) + 8 CN⁻(aq) + 2 H₂O(l) + O₂(g) → 4 [Au(CN)₂]⁻(aq) + 4 OH⁻(aq)\n\n4 [Au(CN)₂]⁻(aq) + Zn(s) → 2 [Zn(CN)₄]²⁻(aq) + 4 Au(s)\n(Reference: NCERT  , class 12 , unit 6 , page 164 paragraph first)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','barium','silver','boron','zinc','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201302,'chemistry','Aluminium is extracted by the electrolysis of [AIEEE 2002]','alumina','Aluminium is extracted by the electrolysis of alumina (Al2O3) dissolved in molten cryolite (Na3AlF6). The process takes place in a specialized cell called the Hall-Héroult cell, where a large electric current is passed through the molten mixture to separate the aluminium from the oxygen.\n\nBauxite is the main source of alumina, but it must be refined and processed to extract the alumina before electrolysis can occur.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','alumina','bauxite','molten cryolite','alumina fixed with molten cryolite','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201303,'chemistry','Which of the following ores is best concentrated by froth-floatation method? [AIEEE 2004]','Galena','Types of ores that can be concentrated by froth flotation method are sulphide ores. Examples are PbS (sulphide galena) and ZnS (zinc blende or sphalerite). (Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 154 paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Magnetite','Cassiterite','Galena','Malachite','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201304,'chemistry','During the process of electrolytic refining of copper, some metals present as impurity settle as anode mud. These are [AIEEE 2005]','Ag and Au','During the process of electrolytic refining of copper, some metals present as impurity settle as anode mud. These impurities include mainly silver (Ag), gold (Au), platinum (Pt), and some other precious metals.\n\nDuring the electrolysis process, copper ions are reduced to copper metal at the cathode, while impurities present in the anode dissolve into the electrolyte and settle as anode mud at the bottom of the cell. The anode mud contains a mixture of metals including Ag, Au, Pt','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe and Ni','Ag and Au','Pb and Zn','Se and Ag','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201305,'chemistry','Which of the following factors is of no significance for roasting sulphide ores to the oxides and not subjecting the sulphide ores to carbon reduction directly? [AIEEE 2008]','Metal sulphides are less stable than the corresponding oxides','"Metal sulphides are less stable than the corresponding oxides." This is because roasting sulphide ores to the oxides involves heating the ores in the presence of oxygen, which causes the sulphide to be converted to oxide. This process is preferred over subjecting the sulphide ores to carbon reduction directly because the latter can lead to the formation of toxic gases such as carbon monoxide and sulfur dioxide. The other factors listed in the question, such as the thermodynamic stability of metal sulphides compared to CS2 or the volatility of CO2 compared to CS2, are not directly relevant to the process of roasting sulphide ores to the oxides.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Metal sulphides are thermodynamically more stable than CS2','CO2 is thermodynamically more stable than CS2','Metal sulphides are less stable than the corresponding oxides','CO2 is more volatile than CS2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201306,'chemistry','Which method of purification is represented in the following equation? Refer diagram(n0608)','van Arkel method','The van Arkel method is a process used to purify certain metals, including zirconium. The method involves the formation and decomposition of a volatile metal iodide compound, which allows for the separation of impurities from the desired metal. The steps involved in the van Arkel method for zirconium purification are as follows: Zirconium is heated with iodine to form zirconium tetraiodide (ZrI4). Zirconium tetraiodide is heated to a high temperature (~1800 K) under reduced pressure, causing it to decompose into pure zirconium metal and iodine gas. The zirconium metal is then collected and purified further by remelting and casting into ingots. The assertion (A) is true because zirconium can indeed be purified by the van Arkel method. The reason (R) is also true because ZrI4 is a volatile compound that decomposes at a high temperature of ~1800 K, which allows for the extraction of pure zirconium metal.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Zone refining','Cupellation','Polling','van Arkel method','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/201306q.png'),
(201307,'chemistry','In the Hall-Herould process, aluminium is formed at the cathode. The cathode is made out of','Carbon','In the Hall-Heroult process, aluminum is produced by the electrolysis of alumina dissolved in molten cryolite. The cathode is typically made of carbon, while the anode is made of carbon or graphite. When an electric current is passed through the cell, aluminum ions are reduced at the carbon cathode, forming liquid aluminum metal. The carbon anodes react with oxygen to form carbon dioxide, which is released as a gas. Therefore, the cathode is made out of carbon in the Hall-Heroult process\n\nAt the cathode: Al3+ + 3 e- → Al\n\nThis reduction reaction occurs at the carbon cathode, where aluminum ions are reduced to form liquid aluminum metal.\n\nAt the anode: 2 O2- → O2 + 4 e-\n\nThis oxidation reaction occurs at the carbon anode, where oxygen ions are oxidized to form oxygen gas. Additionally, carbon anodes react with oxygen to form carbon dioxide, which is released as a gas.','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','platinum','Carbon','pure aluminum','copper','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201308,'biology','Select the correct statement concerning to disorders of muscles in humans. (Karnataka NEET 2013)','Failure of neuromuscular transmission in myasthenia gravis can prevent normal swallowing','Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder in which autoantibodies bind to cholinergic receptors on muscle cells and impairs the ability of the neurotransmitter acetylcholine to induce muscular contraction. This leads to fatigue, weakening andparalysis of skeletal muscles of mouth and throat which may prevent normal swallow.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 312;paragraph No.5).','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Failure of neuromuscular transmission in myasthenia gravis can prevent normal swallowing','Accumulation of urea and creatine in the joints causes their inflammation','An overdose of vitamin D causes osteoporosis','Rapid contractions of skeletal muscles cause muscle dystrophy.','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201309,'biology','Select the correct statement regarding the specific disorder of muscular or skeletal system. (2012)','Osteoporosis – Decrease in bone mass and higher chances of fractures with advancing age','Myasthenia gravis: Auto immune disorder affecting neuromuscular junction leading to fatigue, weakening and paralysis of skeletal muscle.\nMuscular dystrophy: Progressive degeneration of skeletal muscle mostly\ndue to genetic disorder.\nTetany: Rapid spasms (wild contractions) in muscle due to low Ca++ in\nbody fluid.\nArthritis: Inflammation of joints.\nOsteoporosis: Age-related disorder characterised by decreased bone mass\nand increased chances of fractures. Decreased levels of estrogen is a\ncommon cause.\nGout: Inflammation of joints due to accumulation of uric acid crystals.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 312;paragraph No.5).','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Muscular dystrophy – Age related shortening of muscle','Osteoporosis – Decrease in bone mass and higher chances of fractures with advancing age','Myasthenia gravis – Autoimmune disorder which inhibits sliding of myosin filaments','Gout – Inflammation of joints due to extra deposition of calcium','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201310,'biology','During muscular contraction which of the following events occur? \n(a) \'H\' zone disappears \n(b) \'A\' band widens \n(c) \'I\' band reduces in width \n(d) Myosine hydrolyzes ATP, releasing the ADP and Pi \n(e) Z-lines attached to actins are pulled inwards Choose the correct answer from the options given below (NEET 2021)','(a), (c), (d), (e) only','During muscle contraction, the cross bridges pull the thin filaments towards the center of A band. The Z line attached to the actins are also pulled inwards thereby causing a shortening of the sarcomere. The I bands get reduced, whereas the \'A\' bands retain the length. Myosin head acts as ATPase. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 307;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','(b), (d), (e), (a) only','(a), (c), (d), (e) only','(a), (b), (c), (d) only','(b), (c), (d), (e) only','NEW','11-307','11-222',NULL),
(201311,'biology','Match the following (NEET 2021)','Scapula-Triangular flat bone,Cranium-Fibrous joints,Sternum-Flat bone,Vertebral column-Cartilaginous joints','Scapula is a large triangular flat bone situated in the dorsal part of thorax between second and seventh rib. Fibrous joints do not allow movement and they are present in cranium. Sternum is a flat bone on the ventral midline of thorax. The bones in vertebrae are joined together with the help of cartilage. (Reference: 310, 311,312)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','3','match','Scapula,Triangular flat bone','Cranium,Fibrous joints','Sternum,Flat bone','Vertebral column,Cartilaginous joints','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201312,'biology','Which of the following is present between the adjacent bones of the vertebral column? (NEET 2022)','cartilage','In cartilaginous joints, the bones involved are joined together with the help of cartilages. The joint between the adjacent vertebrae in the vertebral column is of this pattern and it permits limited movements.\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 312; paragraph No.3).','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Areolar tissue','smooth muscle','intercalated disc','cartilage','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201313,'biology','Given below are two statements:\n\nStatement I: Autoimmune disorder is a condition where body defense mechanism recognizes its own cells as foreign bodies.\n\nStatement II : Rheumatoid arthritis is a condition where body does not attack self cells.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','autoimmune disorder is a condition where body defense \nmechanism recognises its own cells as foreign bodies. Sometimes, due to genetic and other unknown \nreasons, the body attacks self- cells.\nRheumatoid arthritis is an example where body attacks self cells (synovial membrane).\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page 153; paragraph No.3).','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201314,'biology','Which of the following is a correct match for disease and its symptoms?','Arthritis – Inflammed joints','Myasthenia gravis: Auto immune disorder affecting neuromuscular junction leading to fatigue, weakening and paralysis of skeletal muscle.\nMuscular dystrophy: Progressive degeneration of skeletal muscle mostly due to genetic disorder.\nTetany: Rapid spasms (wild contractions) in muscle due to low Ca++ in\nbody fluid.\nArthritis: Inflammation of joints.\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 312; paragraph No.4).','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Arthritis – Inflammed joints','Tetany – High Ca2+ level causing rapid spasms','Myasthenia gravis – Genetic disorder resulting in weakening and paralysis of skeletal muscle','Muscular dystrophy – An auto immune disorder causing progressive degeneration of skeletal muscle','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201315,'biology','Given below are two statement : one is labeled as Assertion (A) and the other is labeled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A):\nOsteoporosis is characterized by decreased bone mass andan  increased chance of fractures.\n\nReason (R):\nA common cause of osteoporosis is increased levels of estrogen.\n\nIn light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','Osteoporosis: Age-related disorder characterised by decreased bone mass\nand increased chances of fractures. Decreased levels of estrogen is a\ncommon cause.\n\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 312; paragraph No.4).','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) of explanation is the correct (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','11-304','11-219',NULL),
(201316,'biology','Which of the following statements are correct regarding skeletal muscle?\nA. Muscle bundles are held together by collagenous connective tissue layer called fascicle.\nB. Sarcoplasmic reticulum of muscle fibre is a store house of calcium ions.\nC. Striated appearance of skeletal muscle fibre is due to distribution pattern of actin and myosin proteins.\nD. M line is considered as functional unit of contraction called sarcomere.\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(1) B and C only','Muscle bundles are held together by collagenous connective tissue layer called fascia. (Reference: Class XI NCERT page 304, para 2)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','(1) B and C only','(2) A, C and D only','(3) C and D only','(4) A, B and C only','NEW','11-304','11-210',NULL),
(201317,'biology','A deltoid ridge occurs in- [NEET 1990]','Humerus','Humerus has a V shaped deltoid ridge for deltoid muscle attachment. (reference  NCERT class XI page 311, para 2)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Radius','Ulna','femur','Humerus','NEW','11-311','11-226',NULL),
(201318,'biology','Match List I with List II.\nList I (Type of Joint) - List II (Found between)\nA. Cartilaginous Joint - I. Between flat skull bones\nB. Ball and Socket Joint - II. Between adjacent vertebrae in vertebral column\nC. Fibrous Joint - III. Between carpal and metacarpal of thumb\nD. Saddle Joint - IV. Between Humerus and Pectoral girdle\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(1) A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','(1) A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','(2) A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II','(3) A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I','(4) A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201319,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \nList-I List-II\n(A) Palm bones (I) Phalanges\n(B) Wrist bones (II) Metacarpals\n(C) Ankle bones (III) Carpals\n(D) Digit bones (IV) Tarsals\n Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(4) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Previous Year Questions 2','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','(1) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)','(2) (A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)','(3) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','(4) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','NEW','11-311','11-226',NULL),
(201401,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\na) Bauxite is chosen for the extraction of iron.\n\nb) The scientific process used for isolation of the metal from its ore is known as metallurgy.\n\nc) An ore rarely contains only a desired substance.\n\nd) The purification of the metal is one of the major steps in the extraction and isolation of metals from ores.','b,c','a) Incorrect. Bauxite is chosen for the extraction of aluminium, not iron.  "For the purpose of extraction, bauxite is chosen for aluminium."\n\nb) Correct. "The entire scientific and technological process used for isolation of the metal from its ore is known as metallurgy."\n\nc) Correct.  "An ore rarely contains only a desired substance. It is usually contaminated with earthly or undesired materials known as gangue."\n\nd) Correct. "The extraction and isolation of metals from ores involves the following major steps: Concentration of the ore, Isolation of the metal from its concentrated ore, and Purification of the metal." \n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 153)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201402,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\na. Bauxite is the ore used for the extraction of aluminium.\n\nb. Gangue refers to the desired substance present in an ore.\n\nc. Magnetic separation is based on the difference in magnetic properties of the ore components.\n\nd. In gravity separation, an upward stream of running water is used to wash the powdered ore.','a,c','"For the purpose of extraction, bauxite is chosen for aluminium."\n\n"An ore rarely contains only a desired substance. It is usually contaminated with earthly or undesired materials known as gangue."\n\n"This is based on differences in magnetic properties of the ore components. If either the ore or the gangue is attracted towards magnetic field, then the separation is carried out by this method."\n\n"In one such process, an upward stream of running water is used to wash the powdered ore. The lighter gangue particles are washed away and the heavier ore particles are left behind."\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 153)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201403,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are incorrect regarding the froth floatation method of concentration?\n\na.This method is used for removing gangue from sulphide ores.\n\nb. Collectors enhance non-wettability of the mineral particles.\n\nc. Froth stabilisers are used to make gangue particles wet by oils.\n\nd. In the case of an ore containing ZnS and PbS, NaCN is used as a depressant for ZnS.','c,d','Froth stabilisers are used to stabilise the froth, not to make gangue particles wet by oils.\n\nIn the case of an ore containing ZnS and PbS, NaCN is used as a depressant for PbS, not for ZnS.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 153)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201404,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct about leaching?\n\na. Leaching is a common process used for extracting metals from insoluble ores.\n\nb. In leaching of alumina from bauxite, concentrated NaOH is used for digestion.\n\nc. Seeding is the process of adding a freshly prepared sample of hydrated Al2O3 to induce precipitation.\n\nd. In the metallurgy of silver and gold, the metal is leached with a concentrated solution of NaCl  or KCl.','b,c','"Leaching is often used if the ore is soluble in some suitable solvent. ... Bauxite is the principal ore of aluminium. It usually contains SiO2, iron oxides and titanium oxide (TiO2) as impurities. Concentration is carried out by heating the powdered ore with a concentrated solution of NaOH at 473 – 523 K and 35 – 36 bar pressure. This process is called digestion. ... At this stage, small amount of freshly prepared sample of hydrated Al2O3 is added to the solution. This is called seeding. It induces the precipitation."\n\nIn the metallurgy of silver and gold, the metal is leached with a concentrated solution of NaCN or KCN.\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 154)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(180807,'chemistry','Collision theory is applicable to','Biomolecular reactions','It is not applicable to unimolecular reaction. The basic requirement of  collision theory is that the reacting species must come together and collide in order for the reaction to occur.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Collision theory of chemical reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','First order reactions','Zero order reactions','Biomolecular reactions','Intramolecular reactions','NEW','12-117','12-083',NULL),
(22906,'chemistry','An electron in 5th excited state of H-atom is de-excited to ground state. The maximum number of lines in visible region are','4','6 → 2, 5 → 2, 4 → 2, 3 → 2, these transitions result in visible line','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Numerical questions',NULL,'1','mcq','5','15','4','10','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(201405,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about Gibbs energy and metallurgical transformations are correct?\n\na. Gibbs energy is the most significant term in understanding metallurgical transformations.\n\nb. The criterion for the feasibility of a thermal reduction is that at a given temperature, the Gibbs energy change of the reaction must be negative.\n\nc. The change in Gibbs energy, ∆G for any process at any specified temperature, is described by the equation: ∆G = ∆H + T∆S.','a,b','"Gibbs energy is the most significant term."\n\n"The criterion for the feasibility of a thermal reduction is that at a given temperature Gibbs energy change of the reaction must be negative."\n\n"The change in Gibbs energy, ∆G for any process at any specified temperature, is described by the equation: ∆G = ∆H – T∆S"\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 156)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','c only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201406,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct regarding the reduction of iron oxides in the blast furnace?\n\na. The reduction of iron oxides takes place at different temperature ranges.\n\nb. The temperature in the lower portion of the blast furnace can reach up to 2200K due to the burning of coke.\n\nc. In the upper part of the furnace, Fe2O3 and Fe3O4 coming from the top are directly reduced to iron.\n\nd. Limestone is added to remove silicate impurities of the ore as gangue.','a,b','"Reduction of iron oxides takes place at different temperature ranges." \n\n"A blast of hot air is blown from the bottom of the furnace by burning coke in the lower portion to give temperature up to about 2200K."\n\nIn the upper part of the furnace, Fe2O3 and Fe3O4 coming from the top are directly reduced to FeO\n\nLimestone is added to remove silicate impurities of the ore as slag.\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 159)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201407,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\na) At temperatures above 1073K, coke will reduce FeO\n\nb) At about 1673K, ∆rG0 value for the reaction 2FeO→2Fe+O2 is -341 kJmol-1\n\nc) The iron obtained from Blast furnace contains about 4% carbon and many impurities\n\nd) Cast iron is made by melting pig iron with scrap iron and coke using cold air blast','a,c','a) "Above 1073 K in the range of temperature 900-1500 K coke will reduce FeO and will itself be oxidized to CO."\nc) "The iron obtained from Blast furnace contains about 4% carbon and many impurities in smaller amount (e.g., S, P, Si, Mn)."\nb) "At about 1673K (1400oC) ∆rG0 value for the reaction: 2FeO→2Fe+O2 is +341 kJmol-1" is correct, and \n\nd) "Cast iron is made by melting pig iron with scrap iron and coke using hot air blast"\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 161)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201408,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\na, In the Hall-Heroult process, purified Al2O3 is mixed with CaO.\n\nb.The fused mixture in the Hall-Heroult process is electrolysed using steel electrodes.\n\nc. Carbon anodes are consumed during the Hall-Heroult process due to their reaction with oxygen.\n\nd. The overall reaction during the Hall-Heroult process is 2Al2O3 + 3Na → 4Al + 3Na2O.','b,c','purified Al2O3 is mixed with Na3AlF6 or CaF2 which lowers the melting point of the mixture and brings conductivity."\n"Steel vessel with lining of carbon acts as cathode and graphite anode is used."\n"This way for each kg of aluminium produced, about 0.5 kg of carbon anode is burnt away."\n"The overall reaction may be written as: 2Al2O3 + 3C → 4Al + 3CO2\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 163)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201409,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct regarding metallurgical processes?\n\na. Distillation is useful for high boiling metals like zinc and mercury.\n\nb. Liquation is a method used to separate low melting metal impurities from the high melting metal.\n\nc. Electrolytic refining is a method where impure metal is used as a cathode and a strip of the same metal in pure form is used as an anode.\n\nd. Copper is not refined using an electrolytic method.','b,c','a. Incorrect. "This is very useful for low boiling metals like zinc and mercury."\n\nb. Correct. "In this method a low melting metal like tin can be made to flow on a sloping surface. In this way, it is separated from higher melting impurities."\n\nc. Correct. "In this method, the impure metal is made to act as anode. A strip of the same metal in pure form is used as cathode."\n\nd. Incorrect. "Copper is refined using an electrolytic method."\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 164)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201410,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\na. Impurities are more soluble in the solid state of the metal than in the melt.\n\nb. Zone refining is useful for producing semiconductor and other metals of very high purity.\n\nc. The mobile heater in zone refining is fixed at the end where impurities get concentrated.\n\nd.This method is not useful for producing germanium, silicon, boron, gallium, and indium.','b,c','Zone refining is based on the principle that the impurities are more soluble in the melt than in the solid state of the metal...This method is very useful for producing semiconductor and other metals of very high purity, e.g., germanium, silicon, boron, gallium and indium. A mobile heater surrounding the rod of impure metal is fixed at its one end...Impurities get concentrated at one end."\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 165)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','none','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201411,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct regarding the Mond Process and van Arkel Method?\n\na. In the Mond Process, nickel is heated with carbon monoxide to form a volatile complex named as nickel tetracarbonyl.\n\nb. The nickel tetracarbonyl is decomposed at lower temperature to obtain pure metal.\n\nc. The van Arkel Method is useful for removing all the oxygen and nitrogen present in the form of impurity in certain metals like Zr and Ti.\n\nd. In the van Arkel Method, the metal iodide is decomposed on a tungsten filament, electrically heated to about 800K.','a,c','"In this process, nickel is heated in a stream of carbon monoxide forming a volatile complex named as nickel tetracarbonyl. This compex is decomposed at higher temperature to obtain pure metal." (Mond Process)\n"This method is very useful for removing all the oxygen and nitrogen present in the form of impurity in certain metals like Zr and Ti. The crude metal is heated in an evacuated vessel with iodine. The metal iodide being more covalent, volatilises" (van Arkel Method)\n\nThe metal iodide is decomposed on a tungsten filament, electrically\nheated to about 1800K. \n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 165)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201412,'chemistry','A: Concentration of ores involves several steps and selection of these steps depends upon the differences in physical properties of the compound of the metal present and that of the gangue.\n\nR: The process of concentration of ores is based on the difference between specific gravities of the ore and the gangue particles.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','"Removal of the unwanted materials (e.g., sand, clays, etc.) from the ore is known as concentration, dressing or benefaction. Concentration of ores involves several steps and selection of these steps depends upon the differences in physical properties of the compound of the metal present and that of the gangue." \n\nGravity seperation is based on the difference between specific gravities of the ore and the gangue particles. \n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 153)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201413,'chemistry','A: In froth floatation process, collectors enhance nonwettability of the mineral particles.\n\nR: Froth stabilisers stabilise the froth in froth floatation process.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','"Collectors (e.g., pine oils, fatty acids, xanthates, etc.) enhance nonwettability of the mineral particles and froth stabilisers (e.g., cresols, aniline) stabilise the froth."\n\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 153)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201414,'chemistry','A: Froth is formed in froth floatation process which carries the mineral particles.\n\nR: Mineral particles become wet by water while gangue particles become wet by oils in froth floatation process.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','The mineral particles become wet by oils while the gangue particles by water. A rotating paddle agitates the mixture and draws air in it. As a result, froth is formed which carries the mineral particles. The froth is light and is skimmed off. It is then dried for recovery of the ore particles.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 153)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201415,'chemistry','A: In froth floatation process, the froth is skimmed off and dried for recovery of the ore particles.\n\nR: The mineral particles become wet by water in froth floatation process.','Assertion is true but the reason is false','The mineral particles become wet by oils while the gangue particles by water. A rotating paddle agitates the mixture and draws air in it. As a result, froth is formed which carries the mineral particles. The froth is light and is skimmed off. It is then dried for recovery of the ore particles.\n(Reference: NCERT class 12 , unit 6 , page 153)','Inorganic chemistry >> General principles and processes of isolation of Elements >> AR and MSQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but the reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(201501,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Streaming of protoplasm is seen in Amoeba; \n\nR: Paramecium uses tentacles to capture prey.','A is true but R is false','Streaming of protoplasm in the unicellular organisms like Amoeba is a simple form of movement.\nParamecium uses cilia to obtain food which is moved through cyto-pharynx. It also helps in locomotion (NCERT Class11 pg.302)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-302','11-217',NULL),
(201502,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Muscle originates from MESODERM, \n\nR: Muscle tissue have properties like excitibility, contractility, elasticity','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Muscle is derived from mesoderm and it is a specialised tissue with ability to get excited, contract, extend,etc. It helps in locomotion and movement of higher organisms. (NCERT Class 11 pg 303)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-303','11-218',NULL),
(201503,'biology','Read the following statements about muscles, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect? \n\n1. Skeletal muscles are striated and involuntary in nature. \n\n2. Smooth muscles are also called Visceral Muscles. \n\n3. Smooth muscles are not under voluntary control.\n\n4. Skeletal muscles help to maintain body posture.','Only 1 is incorrect.','Smooth muscles are striated muscles which are voluntary in nature i.e. they under our voluntary control. They do help in movement and maintain the body posture. (NCERT class 11 pg. 303)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Only 1, 2 are correct','Only 1 is incorrect.','2 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-303','11-218',NULL),
(201504,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Amoeba shows streaming of protoplasm, \n\nR: Macrophages and leucocytes also exhibit amoeboid movement','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Amoeba are unicellular organisms which show streaming of protoplasm as simple movements. Similar movement is seen in macrophages and leucocytes in our body because of cytoskeleton filaments. (NCERT Class 11 pg 303)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-303','11-218',NULL),
(250718,'biology','Lack of menstruation is observed in all of the following situations except:','When hygiene and nutrition is adequate','Lack of menstruation may be indicative of pregnancy. However, it may also be caused due to some other underlying causes like stress, poor health etc. Reference  : NCERT Page 51','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Menstrual Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','When the released ovum is fertilised','Due to some other underlying causes like stress, poor health etc.','During pregnancy','When hygiene and nutrition is adequate','NEW','12-051','12-035',NULL),
(201505,'biology','Read the following statements about movement and locomotion, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?: \n\n1. Movement of tongue requires involvement of muscular system only. \n\n2. Our tongue is made of muscles.\n\n3. Passage of ova through fallopian tube requires ciliary movement.','Only 1 is incorrect','Tongue is made of muscles, so its movement requires synchronised involvement of muscular, skeletal and neural system. Also the beating movement of cilia helps to move ova through fallopian tube.  (NCERT Class 1 pg. 303)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect','NEW','11-303','11-218',NULL),
(201506,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Cardiac muscles are branching, striated muscle, \n\nR: Cardiac muscles are present in cardia of stomach.','A is true but R is false','Cardiac muscles are branching, striated muscles present in the heart. They have auto-excitibilty features which helps to make the heart beat whole throughout life. (NCERT class 11 pg. 304)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-304','11-219',NULL),
(201507,'biology','Read the following statements about muscles, which of the following is/are true or false?\n\n1. Skeletal muscles are made up of muscle bundles/fascicles. \n\n2. Each fascicle is held together by a fascia. \n\n3. Each muscle fibre is lined by sarcolemma containing sarcoplasm. \n\n4. Sarcoplasm is the storehouse of Ca+2 in the muscle.','Only 4 is false.','A skeletal muscle is a striated muscle containing a number of bundles called fascicles. The bundles are held together by a covering called fascia. Each bundle has number of muscle fibres lined by a covering called sarcolemma. Sarcoplasmic reticulum is the storehouse of Ca+2 ions, which is needed during contraction of muscle. (NCERT Class 11 pg 304)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are false','Only 1. is true','Only 1. and  3. are true','Only 4 is false.','NEW','11-304','11-219',NULL),
(201508,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: A muscle fibre is a syncitium. \n\nR: Sarcoplasm has many nuclei','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','A muscle fibre is a syncitium because it is lined by sarcolemma (plasma membrane) containing sarcoplasm with many nuclei. Syncitium means "MULTI-NUCLEATED" (NCERT class 11 pg 304)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-304','11-219',NULL),
(201509,'biology','Read the following statements about muscle fibre ultrastructure, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. Myofilaments are composed of actin and myosin.\n\n2. Muscle fibre show non-alternating irregular light and dark bands. \n\n3. In the muscle fibre, sarcoplasm is the store of Ca+2 ions. \n\n4. Muscle fibre show alternating light and dark bands.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Each muscle fibre contains large number of parallel arranged filaments called myo-filaments or myofibrils. The myofibrils are actin and myosin. Also calcium ions are stored inside sarcoplasmic reticulum (NCERT class 11 pg 304)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','2, 3 and 4 are incorrect.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','3 and 4 are correct','2, 3 and 4 are correct.','NEW','11-304','11-219',NULL),
(201510,'biology','Read the following statements about muscle fibre ultrastructure, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. Z line bisects I line. \n\n2. M menbrane bisects A band. \n\n3. Z line is the functional unit of relaxation. \n\n4. Distance between 2 Z lines is also called sarcomere','1, 2, and 4 are correct','Z line is an elastic fibre which bisects the I line formed by thin filament. So, the Z line is also the unit of CONTRACTION. Statement 3 is incorrect; Rest all statements are correct (NCERT Class 11 pg 305)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Only 3 is correct','1 and 2 are incorrect','1, 2, and 4 are correct','1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-305','11-220',NULL),
(201511,'biology','Read the following statements about ultrastructure of muscle fibres, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n1. H band is equal to the sarcomere.\n\n2. H band is equal to A band. \n\n3. It is equal to the central part of A band which is not overlapped by thin filament.','Only 3 is correct','H zone is the central part of the thick filament (A band) which is not overlapped by the thin filaments. (NCERT Class 11 pg 305)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Only 2 and 3 is incorrect','Only 2 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 3 is correct','NEW','11-305','11-220',NULL),
(201512,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: I band is formed by thin filaments, \n\nR: I band is bisected by Z line','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','I band is formed by thin filament called actin and hence called ISOTROPIC BAND. I band is bisected by the Z line which is a unit of contraction. (NCERT class 11 pg 305)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-305','11-220',NULL),
(201513,'biology','Read the following statements about ultrastructure of muscle fibres, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. I band is also called anisotropic band. \n\n2. Myosin constitutes the anisotropic band. \n\n3. Distance between two Z line is half of a sarcomere. \n\n4. H zone is seen in central part of thin filament','1, 3 and 4 are incorrect','I band is called Isotropic band, Distance between z line is equal to sarcomere, H band is seen in middle part of thick filament. (NCERT class 11 pg 305)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are correct','1, 3 and 4 are incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-305','11-220',NULL),
(201514,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: F actin is a monomer, \n\nR: F actins aggregate to form G actin','Both A and R are false','F actin is a polymer. G actins are monomers which aggregate to form F actin (NCERT class 11 page 306)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-306','11-221',NULL),
(201515,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Troponin masks myosin binding sites on actin, \n\nR: This happens during contracting','A is true but R is false','Troponin masks the myosin binding sites on actin when the muscle is at rest because this prevents any actin-myosin bridge formation. (NCERT Class 11 pg 306)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-306','11-221',NULL),
(201516,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Meromyosins are monomers of thick filament, \n\nR: Meromyosins are covered by troponin.','A is true but R is false','Many meromyosin join togerther to form myosin (thick filament). Troponin masks the myosin binding site on actin at resting state of muscle. (NCERT Class 11 pg 306)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-306','11-221',NULL),
(201517,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Meromyosins have two component HMM and LMM, \n\nR: LMM has ATPase enzyme','A is true but R is false','Meromyosins have HMM and LMM components. However the ATPase enzyme is present in globular head of HMM part. (NCERT Class 11 pg 306)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-306','11-221',NULL),
(201518,'biology','Read the following statements about contraction of muscle fibres, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. Muscle contraction is exhibited by SLIDING FILAMENT theory. \n\n2. Ca+2 levels decrease during muscle contraction. \n\n3. Action potential travels from moton neuron to muscle via neuro-muscular junction. \n\n4. At the neuromuscular junction, the neurotransmitter is Acetyl Choline.','1, 3 and 4 are correct','Ca+2 levels increase during muscle contraction because large concentration of calcium ions is released into the sarcoplasm. (NCERT class 11 pg 307)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','2, 3 and 4 are incorrect','1 and 4 are incorrect','1, 3 and 4 are correct','2 and 4 are incorrect.','NEW','11-307','11-222',NULL),
(201519,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: During contraction, there is shortening of sarcomere, \n\nR: A band retains its length.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','During contraction, the Z lines attached to the I bands are pulled inwards leading to shortening of sarcomere. Also, the A band is equal to thick filament length, hence the length of A band is retained. (NCERT Class 11 pg 307)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-307','11-222',NULL),
(201520,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: ATP hydrolysis helps in cross-bridge formation, \n\nR: The energy is used to connect the actin to the myosin.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','The globular head of meromyosin has ATPase whoch hydrolyses ATP. The energy released helps the myosin to get attached to myosin binding site on actin leading to cross-bridge formation. (NCERT Class 11 pg 307)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-307','11-222',NULL),
(201601,'biology','Read the following statements about contraction of muscle fibres, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. ATPase and ATP hydrolysis leads to cross-bridge formation. \n\n2. Re-Attachment of ATP to head of myosin helps to break cross-bridge. \n\n3. Release of ADP helps to contract muscle fibre\n\n4. \'A\' band retains length during contraction','1,2 and 4 are correct','ADP + Pi is formed by hydrolysis of ATP by globular head of myosin. Now after contraction, the ADP+Pi is released which helps to make the myosin relaxed. Hence release of ADP does not help to contract. (NCERT Class 11 pg 307)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 is incorrect','1,2 and 4 are correct','1 and 4 are incorrect.','NEW','11-307','11-222',NULL),
(201602,'biology','Read the following statements about contraction of muscle fibres, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. Masking of actin is associated with Ca+2 going back to sarcoplasmic reticulum. \n\n2. I band regains its length upon relaxation. \n\n3. Myoglobin stores O₂ and contains plenty of ER.','Only 3. is false','Muscles (especially cardiac muscles) contain a red coloured O₂ storing pigment named MYOGLOBIN which has plenty of mitochonfria. It is a red coloured pigment. (NCERT Class 1 pg. 308)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Only 1. is true','Only 3. is true','Both 1. and 2. are false','Only 3. is false','NEW','11-308','11-223',NULL),
(201603,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Repeated activation and contraction causes muscle fatigue.\n\nR: Repeated activation leads to formation of glycolic acid.','A is true but R is false','Repeated stimulation of muscles leads to lack of O₂ which in turn leads to anaerobic glycolysis and formation of LACTIC ACID. Accumulation of lactic acid in muscles lead to fatigue and cramps. (NCERT Class 11 pg 308)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-308','11-223',NULL),
(201604,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: White fibres contain plenty of myoglobin. \n\nR: They have less number of sarcoplasmic reticulum','Both A and R are false','White muscle fibres contain lesser number of myoglobin but higher number of sarcoplasmic reticulum. They also contain less number of mitochondria. (NCERT Class 11 pg 308)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-308','11-223',NULL),
(201605,'biology','Read the following statements about ultrastructure of muscle fibres, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. Troponin masks myosin binding site on actin. \n\n2. ATP hydrolysis allow unmasking. \n\n3. Entry of Ca+2 into Sarcoplasmic reticulum causes re-masking. \n\n4. ATP hydrolysis is caused by Carbonic Anhydrase.','Only 4 is false.','Troponin masks the myosin binding sites on actin when the muscle is at rest because this prevents any actin-myosin bridge formation. Now ATPase hydrolyses ATP and this allows cross-bridge formation by unmasking. During relaxation, the calcium ions go back into sarcoplasmic reticulum leading to re-masking if the site. ATP hydrolysis is caused by an enzyme named ATPase. (Not carbonic anhydrase) (NCERT Class 11 pg 308)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Only 1. is true','Both 2. and 4. are true','1. and 2. are false','Only 4 is false.','NEW','11-308','11-223',NULL),
(201606,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Human newborn baby has 206 bones. \n\nR: Some of the bones fuse in adults to form 200 bones in total','Both A and R are false','Human newborn baby has 300 bones in total. some of the bones fuse to form total 206 bones in adults. (NCERT CLASS 11 pg 309)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-309','11-224',NULL),
(201607,'biology','Read the following statements about human skeletal system, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. Axial skeleton of a human body contains 22 bones.\n\n2. Axial skeleton comprises the vertebrae, skull, sternum and ribs. \n\n3. Skull has facial and cranial bones. \n\n4. Frontal bone is located in the skull','2, 3 and 4 are correct','Axial skeleton of human body has 80 bones constituted by ribs, sternum, vertebrae and skull. (NCERT Class 11 pg 309)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','2, 3 and 4 are correct','1, 2 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-309','11-224',NULL),
(201608,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Cranial bones protect the brain. \n\nR: Frontal, temporal and mandible are cranial bones','A is true but R is false','Cranial bones cover and protect the brain from injury. However the cranial bones are frontal, temporal, parietal and occipital, sphenoid and ethmoid bone. (NCERT Class 11 pg 309)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-309','11-224',NULL),
(201609,'biology','Read the following statements about human skeletal system, which of the following is/are true or false? \n\n1. There are 3 ear ossicles in each ear. \n\n2. The ear osscicle attenuate sound waves \n\n3. Malleus, incus and stapes are ossicles','Only 2 is false','Ossicles are responsible for amplification of sound waves so that we can hear low amplitude sounds. (NCERT Class 11 pg 309)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','1 and 2 are true.','Only 1. is false','Both 2 and 3 are false','Only 2 is false','NEW','11-309','11-224',NULL),
(201610,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA. Each vertebrae contains a central hollow part called neural canal. \n\nR: The spinal cord travels ventral to the vertebral bodies.','A is true but R is false','Spinal cord travels through the neural canal (central hollow part) of the vertebrae. (NCERT Class 11 pg 310)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-310','11-225',NULL),
(201611,'biology','Read the following statements about vertebral column, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. 7 Cervial vertebrae  \n\n2. 12 lumbar vertebrae \n\n3. 5 Thoracic vertebrae \n\n4. 1 fused sacrum \n\n5. 6 coccyx bones(Consider adult human being)','2, 3 and 5 are false','In an adult human, there are 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar , 1 fused sacrum and 1 fused coccyx bones as part of vertebrae. (NCERT Class 11 pg 310)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','1, 3 and 4 are true.','1. and 4. are false','Only 2. is true','2, 3 and 5 are false','NEW','11-310','11-225',NULL),
(201612,'biology','Following are statements about function of vertebral column: \n\n1. Protection of spinal cord. \n\n2. Supporting the head and providing attachment to back muscles. \n\n3. Maintaining posture while sitting and standing. \n\n4. Providing attachment to biceps and triceps. \n\n5. Release of hormones for growth.','Both 4 and 5 are incorrect.','Vertebral column helps to protect spinal cord, maintanence of back posture and site for attachment of back muscles. Biceps and triceps muscles are attached to the humerus and radius bone. Growth hormone [GH] is released from anterior pituitary (not vertebrae) (NCERT Class 11 pg 310)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Both 4 and 5 are incorrect.','Only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-310','11-225',NULL),
(201613,'biology','Read the following statements about human axial skeletal system, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. Adult humans have 12 pairs of rib. \n\n2. Sternum is a flat bone present in dorsal part of thorax. \n\n3. Last 2 pairs of ribs are connected ventrally to the sternum.','Both 2 and 3 are false','Sternum is a flat bone present in ventral part of thorax. Floating ribs (last 2 pairs) are not connected to the sternum ventrally. (NCERT Class 11 pg 310)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','1 and 3 are true.','Only 1 is false','Both 2 and 3 are false','Only 2 is true','NEW','11-310','11-225',NULL),
(201614,'biology','Read the following statements about human axial skeletal system, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. Humans have 12 pair of ribs. \n\n2. First 7 pairs are called false ribs as they attach vertebrae to the sternum. \n\n3. 8th and 9th pair are called floating ribs.','2 and 3 are false','First 7 pair of ribs are called true ribs whoh connect thoracic vertebrae to sternum. False ribs are the last 2 pairs (11th and 12th pair) (NCERT Class 11 pg 310)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Only 1. is false','1 and 3 are true','2 and 3 are false','Both 1 and 2 are true.','NEW','11-310','11-225',NULL),
(220546,'biology','In the given diagram , Anterior pituitary is shown by- diagram (2203)','d','Figure 22.2 NCERT Class 11 page no: 333 Representation of pituitary and its relationship with hypothalamus','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> The Pituitary Gland',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-333','11-241','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/220546q.png'),
(201615,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Appendicular skeleton is constituted by the upper and lower limbs. \n\nR: Femur is the longest bone which connects to the radius and ulna.','A is true but R is false','Femur is the longest bone which articulates to the hip bone one side and to the tibiaandfibula on the other side. (NCERT Class 11 pg 311)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-311','11-226',NULL),
(201616,'biology','Read the following statements about appendicular skeletal system, which of the following is/are correct or incorrect?\n\n1. Scapula is a rectangular bone extending from 2nd to 7th rib. \n\n2. Scapula forms the pelvic girdle. \n\n3. Scapula articluates with the clavicle in the lumbar region.','1, 2, 3 are false','Scapula is a triangular bone extending from 2nd to 7th rib. It forms the pectoral girdle. It articulates with the clavivle near the cervical region. (NCERT Class 11 pg 311)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Only 1 is true','Both 2 and 3 are true','1, 2, 3 are false','Only 3 is false','NEW','11-311','11-226',NULL),
(201617,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA. Each coxal bone is formed by fusion of ilium, ischium and patella. \n\nR: Acetabulum articulates with the humerus in lower limb.','Both A and R are false','Each coxal bone is formed by ilium, ischium and pubis. The acetabulum articulates with head of femur in lower limb. (NCERT Class 11 pg 311)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-311','11-226',NULL),
(201618,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA. Clavicle has acromion process which articulates with cervical vertebrae. \n\nR: Scapula articulates with clavivle and extends along the floating ribs.','Both A and R are false','Acromion process is present in the scapula. Scapula extends from 2nd to 7th rib (i.e. along the true ribs) (NCERT Class 11 pg 311)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-311','11-226',NULL),
(201619,'biology','Read the following statements about joints in human body, which of the following is/are true or false?\n\n1. Fibrous joints allow maximum range of movements. \n\n2. Joint between cranial bones is a synovial joint. \n\n3. Knee joint is a hinge type of fibrous joint.','1, 2, 3 are false','Fibrous joints do not allow any movement. Joint between cranial bones is fibrous joint. Knee joint is a hinge synovial joint. (NCERT Class 11 pg 312)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Only 1 is false','1, 2, 3 are false','2 and 3 are true','Only 2 is true.','NEW','11-311','11-226',NULL),
(201620,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA. Cartilaginous joints have lesser range of mobility than synovial joint. \n\nR: In cartilaginous joint, bones are joined together with cartilages.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Cartilaginous joints are less mobile than synovial joint because the bones (in cartilagenous joints) are held closely by tight catilages. for example: inter-vertebral joints. (NCERT Class 11 pg 312)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201621,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA. Synovial joints show max range of movements. \n\nR: The synovial cavity is filled with cerebro-spinal fluid which allows considerable movement.','A is true but R is false','The synovial cavity between articular surfaces is filled with SYNOVIAL FLUID (not CSF). This privides lubricating effect and hence max range of movement. (NCERT Class 11 pg 312)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201622,'biology','Read the following statements about joints in human body, which of the following is/are true or false?\n\n1. Humerus and pectoral girdle is a saddle type of syovial joint. \n\n2. Knee joint is a cartilagenous type of hinge joint. \n\n3. Atlas and axis are pivot joint which helps to rotate the head.','Only 3 is true','Humerus and pectoral girdle is a ball-socket type of synovial joint. The knee joint is hinge type of synovial joint (not cartilagenous joint). (NCERT Class 1 pg 312)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Only 3 is true','Both 1 and 3 are false','Only 2 is true','Both 1 and 2 are true.','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201623,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder due to presence of auto-antibodies in NMJ.\n\nR: In myasthenia, the muscles become hyper-excitable and rigid.','A is true but R is false','Due to auto-antibodies, there is reduced impulse transmission to muscles which lead to paralysis and weakening of skeletal muscles. (NCERT Class 11 pg 312)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(201624,'biology','Read the following statements about diseases of skeletal system, which of the following is/are true or false?\n\n1. Osteoporosis is common in older women. \n\n2. Hypo-calcemia leads to tetany and rapid spasms. \n\n3. Muscular dystrophy is a genetic condition due to mutation of certain chromosomes. \n\n4. Rickets occurs due to excess vitamin D.','Only 4 is false.','Osteoporosis is seen in post-menopausal women due to reduced estrogen effect. Low calcium levels cause tetany. Muscular dystrophy is a genetic condition. \nRickets occurs due to vitamin D deficiency.\n(NCERT Class 11 pg 312)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','3','mcq','Only 1 is true','Both 2 and 3 are false','Only 4 is false.','Only 3 is false','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(220547,'biology','In the given diagram , Posterior pituitary is shown by- diagram (2203)','c','Figure 22.2 NCERT Class 11 page no: 333 Representation of pituitary and its relationship with hypothalamus','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> The Pituitary Gland',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-333','11-241','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/220547q.png'),
(201625,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA. Gout is a painful joint disorder. \n\nR: There is inflammation of joint due to accumulation calcium-oxalate crystals.','A is true but R is false','Gout is an Inflammatory disorder of joints due to accumulation of uric acid crystals. (NCERT class 11th, pg 312)','Human Physiology >> Locomotion and Movement >>  Indepth Questions (Syllabus Reduction)','Locomotion and Movement','2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-312','11-227',NULL),
(210101,'chemistry','The p-block elements are placed in which groups of the periodic table?','13 to 18','The p-block elements are placed in groups 13 to 18 of the periodic table.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','14 to 17','13 to 18','12 to 15','13 to 17','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210102,'chemistry','Which P-block element doesn\'t have ns² np¹⁻⁶ electronic configuration?','He','The valence shell electronic configuration of p-block elements is ns² np¹⁻⁶ (except He which has 1s² configuration). \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','O','Ar','He','P','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210103,'chemistry','Where are P-block elements placed in the periodic table?','Extreme right','The p-block elements are placed in which groups of the periodic table extreme right.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Extreme left','Extreme bottom','Extreme right','Below periodic table','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210104,'chemistry','Which among the following doesn\'t have the significant effect on the properties of elements of p-block?','Presence of s-orbital in lighter members','The absence of d-orbitals in second period and presence of d or d and f orbitals in heavier elements (starting from third period onwards) have significant effects on the properties of elements.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Presence of d-orbitals in heavier members','Presence of f-orbital in heavier members','Presence of s-orbital in lighter members','Absence of d-orbitals in heavier members','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210105,'chemistry','In which period d-orbitals are absent?','2nd','The absence of d-orbitals in second period and presence of d or d and f orbitals in heavier elements (starting from third period onwards) have significant effects on the properties of elements.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','2nd','3rd','4th','5th','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210106,'chemistry','From which period filling of f orbitals begin?','6th','The absence of d-orbitals in second period and presence of d or d and f orbitals in heavier elements (starting from third period onwards) have significant effects on the properties of elements.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','3rd','4th','5th','6th','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210107,'chemistry','Which among the following is a group-15 element?','Moscovium','Moscovium is a group-15 element. Livermorium is a group-16 element.. All the others are group-17 elements.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Livermorium','Moscovium','Tennessine','Astatine','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210108,'chemistry','Phosphorous belongs to which family?','Apatite','Phosphorous belongs to Apatite family\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Apatite','Hematite','Pyrite','Calcite','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210109,'chemistry','How many metalloids are present in group-15?','2','Arsenic and antimony are metalloids.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210110,'chemistry','How many non-metals are present in group-15?','2','Nitrogen and phosphorus are non-metals, arsenic and antimony metalloids and bismuth is a metal.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210111,'chemistry','Which among the following is a metal?','Bismuth','Bismuth is a metal. Arsenic and antimony are metalloids.Phosphorus are non-metals\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Arsenic','Antimony','Bismuth','Phosphorous','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210112,'chemistry','In the formula Ca₉(PO₄)₆.CaX₂, what X cannot be?','Br','Phosphorus occurs in minerals of the apatite family, Ca₉(PO₄)₆.CaX₂ (X = F, Cl or OH) (e.g.,Ca₉(PO₄)₆.CaF₂ fluorapatite) which are the main components of phosphate rocks.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','F','Br','Cl','OH','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(210201,'chemistry','In which form is nitrogen found in plants and animals?','Protein','Nitrogen is found in the form of proteins in plants and animals.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 170; Paragraph No.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','Protein','Carbohydrates','Lipids','Fats','NEW','12-170',NULL,NULL),
(210202,'chemistry','Which are the main components of phosphate rocks?','Ca₉(PO₄)₆.CaF₂','Phosphorus occurs in minerals of the apatite family, Ca₉(PO₄)₆.CaX₂ (X = F, Cl or OH) (e.g.,Ca₉(PO₄)₆.CaF₂ fluorapatite) which are the main components of phosphate rocks.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','Ca₉(PO₄)₆.CaF₂','H₃PO₄','Ca₉ (PO₄ )₆.CaCl₂','Ca₉(PO₄)₆.CaBr₂','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210203,'chemistry','Among which of the following phosphorus is not an essential constituent ?','Soil','Phosphorus is an essential constituent of animal and plant matter. It is present in bones as well as in living cells. Phosphoproteins are present in milk and eggs.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','Plants','Animals','Living cells','Soil','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210204,'chemistry','What are arsenic, antimony and bismuth found mainly as?','Sulphide minerals','Arsenic, antimony and bismuth are found mainly as sulphide minerals.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','Zinc minerals','Sulphide minerals','Aluminum minerals','Phosphorus minerals','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(220548,'biology','The Pituitary Gland is attached to hypothalamus by ______','Stalk','The Pituitary Gland is attached to hypothalamus by Stalk.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> The Pituitary Gland',NULL,'1','mcq','Sella Tursica','Sella Fabricus','Stalk','Podium','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(210205,'chemistry','Moscovium is a  ________  element.','synthetic and radioactive','Moscovium is a synthetic, radioactive element. \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','naturally occuring and radioactive','synthetic and radioactive','naturally occuring','synthetic','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210206,'chemistry','What is the symbol of Moscovium?','Mc','Moscovium is a synthetic radioactive element. Its symbol is Mc, atomic number 115,\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','Mc','Ms','Mr','Mv','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210207,'chemistry','What is the electronic configuration of Mc?','[Rn]5f¹⁴6d¹⁰7s²7p³','Moscovium is a synthetic radioactive element. Its symbol is Mc, atomic number 115, and its electronic configuration is [Rn],5f¹⁴,6d¹⁰,7s²,7p³ .\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','[Rn]5f¹⁴6d¹⁰7s²7p¹','[Rn]5f¹⁴6d¹⁰7s²7p⁶','[Rn]5f¹⁴6d¹⁰7s²7p³','[Rn]5f¹⁴6d¹⁰7s²','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210208,'chemistry','Match the electronic configurations.','P-[Ne]3s²3p³, As-[Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p³, Sb-[Kr]4d¹⁰5s²5p³, Bi-[Xe]4f¹⁴5d¹⁰6s²6p³','The coprrect match of electronic configurations is P-[Ne]3s²3p³, As-[Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p³, Sb-[Kr]4d¹⁰5s²5p³, Bi-[Xe]4f¹⁴5d¹⁰6s²6p³\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; Table 7.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'3','match','P, [Ne]3s²3p³','As, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p³','Sb, [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²5p³','Bi, [Xe]4f¹⁴5d¹⁰6s²6p³','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210209,'biology','Impulses from CNS to skeletal muscles are relayed by______ neural system.','Somatic','The somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles while the autonomic neural system transmits impulses from the CNS to the involuntary organs and smooth muscles of the body.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 316;paragraph no.3)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Human Neural System','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Somatic','Autonomic','Sympathetic','Parasympathetic','NEW','11-316','11-231',NULL),
(210210,'biology','Impulses from CNS to smooth muscles and involuntary organs are relayed by______ neural system.','Autonomic','The somatic neural system relays impulses from the CNS to skeletal muscles while the autonomic neural system transmits impulses from the CNS to the involuntary organs and smooth muscles of the body.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 316;paragraph no.3)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Human Neural System','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Autonomic','peripheral','Sympethatic','Central','NEW','11-316','11-231',NULL),
(210211,'biology','Parasympathetic and sympathetic neural systems are part of-','Autonomic nervous system','The autonomic neural system is further classified into sympathetic neural system and parasympathetic nervous systems.\n(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 316;paragraph no.3)Stem.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Human Neural System','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','Central Nervous system','Peripheral Nervous system','Autonomic nervous system','Limbic system','NEW','11-316','11-231',NULL),
(210212,'biology','ANS has ___ components.','2','The autonomic neural system is further classified into sympathetic neural system and parasympathetic neural system.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 316;paragraph no.3).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Human Neural System','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-316','11-231',NULL),
(210213,'biology','_______nervous system is the part of the peripheral nervous system that comprises the whole complex of nerves, fibres, ganglia, and plexuses by which impulses travel from the central nervous system to the viscera and from the viscera to the central nervous system.','Visceral','Visceral nervous system is the part of the peripheral nervous system that comprises the whole complex of nerves, fibres, ganglia, and plexuses by which impulses travel from the central nervous system to the viscera and from the viscera to the central nervous system..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 316;paragraph no.4)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Human Neural System','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Sympathetic','Autonomic','Somatic','Visceral','NEW','11-316','11-231',NULL),
(210301,'chemistry','What is the valence shell electronic configuration of group 15 elements?','ns² np³','The valence shell electronic configuration of group 15 elements is ns² np³ \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; topic 7.1.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Electronic Configuration',NULL,'1','mcq','ns² np¹','ns² np³','ns² np⁶','ns² np⁵','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210302,'chemistry','In Group 15 elements, the s-orbital is completely filled and p-orbitals are ________ filled, making their electronic configuration extra stable.','completely half','Covalent and ionic (in a particular state) radii increase in size down the group. There is a considerable increase in covalent radius from N to P.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; topic 7.1.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Electronic Configuration',NULL,'1','mcq','completely filled','completely half','partially filled','completely empty','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210303,'chemistry','From N to P,  what is the trend of radii?','There is considerable increase','Covalent and ionic (in a particular state) radii increase in size down the group. There is a considerable increase in covalent radius from N to P. . \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; topic 7.1.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Electronic Configuration',NULL,'1','mcq','There is considerable decrease','There is considerable increase','There is rapid decrease','There is no change','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210304,'chemistry','From As to Bi only a ________ in covalent radius is observed.','small increase','Covalent and ionic (in a particular state) radii increase in size down the group. There is a considerable increase in covalent radius from N to P. However, from As to Bi only a small increase in covalent radius is observed. This is due to the presence of completely filled d and/or f orbitals in heavier members. \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; topic 7.1.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Electronic Configuration',NULL,'1','mcq','considerable decrease','considerable increase','no change','small increase','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210305,'biology','The long fibre with a branched distal end in a neuron is-','Axon','The axon is a long fibre, the distal end of which is branched.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','Cell body','Dendrites','Axon','Terminal buttons','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210306,'biology','Dendrites carry impulse-','Towards cell body','Dendrites transmit impulses towards the cell body..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','Towards cell body','Away from cell body','Both from the body to other neurons and from other neurons to the cell body','The don\'t carry impulses','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210307,'biology','Each branch of an axon terminates as a-','Synaptic knob','The axon is a long fibre, the distal end of which is branched. Each branch terminates as a bulb-like structure called synaptic knob which possess synaptic vesicles containing chemicals called neurotransmitters..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Synaptic knob','Synaptic cleft','Synaptic bulb','Axon hillock','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210308,'biology','Synaptic vesicles in synaptic knobs contain-','Neurotransmitters','The axon is a long fibre, the distal end of which is branched. Each branch terminates as a bulb-like structure called synaptic knob which possess synaptic vesicles containing chemicals called neurotransmitters..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Hormones','Secretions','Neurotransmitters','Paracrine Peptides','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210309,'biology','Axons carry impulse-','Away from cell body','The axons transmit nerve impulses away from the cell body to a synapse or to a neuromuscular junction.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','Towards cell body','Away from cell body','Both from the body to other neurons and from other neurons to the cell body','The don\'t carry impulses','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210310,'biology','Multipolar neurons have-','1 axon,2 or more dendrites','Multipolar (with one axon and two or more dendrites; found in the cerebral cortex).(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','1 axon,1 dendrite','2 axons,1 dendrite','1 axon,2 or more dendrites','1 axon,no dendrites','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210311,'biology','Multipolar neurons are in-','Cerebral cortex','Multipolar (with one axon and two or more dendrites; found in the cerebral cortex).(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','Embryonic stage','Retina','Cerebral cortex','Spinal cord','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210312,'biology','Bipolar neurons have-','1 axon,1 dendrite','Bipolar (with one axon and one dendrite, found in the retina of eye.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','1 axon,1 dendrite','2 axons,1 dendrite','1 axon,2 or more dendrites','1 axon,no dendrites','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210313,'biology','Bipolar neurons are in-','Retina','Bipolar (with one axon and one dendrite, found in the retina of eye.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','Medulla','Retina','Cerebral cortex','Spinal cord','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210314,'biology','Unipolar neurons have-','1 axon','Unipolar (cell body with one axon only; found usually in the embryonic stage).(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','1 axon,1 dendrite','1 axon','1 axon,2 or more dendrites','2 axons,1 dendrite','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210315,'biology','Unipolar neurons are in-','Embryonic stage','Unipolar (cell body with one axon only; found usually in the embryonic stage).(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','Embryonic stage','Retina','Cerebral cortex','Spinal cord','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210316,'biology','Myelinated nerves are enveloped by-','Schwann cells that form myelin sheath','There are two types of axons, namely, myelinated and unmyelinated. The myelinated nerve fibres are enveloped with schwann cells, which form a myelin sheath around the axon.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','Schwann cells that form myelin sheath','Schwann cells that don\'t form myelin sheath','Synaptic Cleft','Synaptic bulb','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210317,'biology','The gap between two adjacent myelin sheaths is called-','Nodes of ranvier','The gaps between two adjacent myelin sheaths are called nodes of ranvier.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Nodes of ranvier','Synaptic knob','Synaptic Cleft','Synaptic bulb','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210318,'biology','Unmyelinated nerves are enveloped by-','Schwann cells that don\'t form myelin sheath','Unmyelinated nerve fibre is enclosed by a schwann cell that does not form a myelin sheath around the axon.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','Schwann cells that form myelin sheath','Schwann cells that don\'t form myelin sheath','Synaptic Cleft','Synaptic bulb','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210319,'biology','Myelinated nerves are found in-','Cranial and spinal nerves','Myelinated nerve fibres are found in spinal and cranial nerves.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','ANS only','Cranial nerves and ans','Cranial and spinal nerves','ANS and spinal nerves','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210320,'biology','Unmyelinated nerves are found in-','ANS and somatic neural system','Unmyelinated nerve fibre is enclosed by a schwann cell that does not form a myelin sheath around the axon, and is commonly found in autonomous and the somatic neural systems.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','ANS and somatic neural system','Cranial nerves and ans','Cranial and spinal nerves','ANS and spinal nerves','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
