(210331,'biology','Which neurons are usually present in the embryonic stage?','Unipolar','Based on the number of axon and dendrites, the neurons are divided into three types, i.e., multipolar (with one axon and two or more dendrites; found in the cerebral cortex), bipolar (with one axon and one dendrite, found in the retina of eye) and unipolar (cell body with one axon only; found usually in the embryonic stage)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Bipolar','Unipolar','Multipolar','Omnipolar','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210332,'biology','Which neurons are usually present in the cerebral cortex?','Multipolar','Based on the number of axon and dendrites, the neurons are divided into three types, i.e., multipolar (with one axon and two or more dendrites; found in the cerebral cortex), bipolar (with one axon and one dendrite, found in the retina of eye) and unipolar (cell body with one axon only; found usually in the embryonic stage)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Bipolar','Unipolar','Multipolar','Omnipolar','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210333,'biology','Which neurons are present in the retina?','Bipolar','Based on the number of axon and dendrites, the neurons are divided into three types, i.e., multipolar (with one axon and two or more dendrites; found in the cerebral cortex), bipolar (with one axon and one dendrite, found in the retina of eye) and unipolar (cell body with one axon only; found usually in the embryonic stage)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Bipolar','Unipolar','Multipolar','Omnipolar','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210334,'biology','There are _____ types of neurons based on the number of axons and dendrites they have.','Three','Based on the number of axon and dendrites, the neurons are divided into three types, i.e., multipolar (with one axon and two or more dendrites; found in the cerebral cortex), bipolar (with one axon and one dendrite, found in the retina of eye) and unipolar (cell body with one axon only; found usually in the embryonic stage)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','One','Two','Three','Four','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210335,'biology','Assertion: Nerves are divided into two types myelinated and non myelinated nerves\n \nReason: Myelinated nerve fibers are those  in which the schwann cells forms myelin sheath around axon while the non myelinated is because of  schwann cell that does not form myelin sheath around the axons','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Nerves are divided into two types myelinated and non Myelinated. Myelinated nerve fibers are those  in which the schwann cells forms myelin sheath around axon and gives a white appearance while the non myelinated is because of  schwann cell that does not form myelin sheath around the axon. This gives a grey appearance ( Reference NCERT class 11 page 317','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Neuron','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210401,'chemistry','What is the expected order of successive ionization enthalpies of group 15?','∆ᵢH₁ < ∆ᵢH₂ < ∆ᵢH₃','Because of the extra stable half-filled p orbitals electronic configuration and smaller size, the ionisation enthalpy of the group 15 elements is much greater than that of group 14 elements in the corresponding periods. The order of successive ionisation enthalpies, as expected is ∆ᵢH₁ < ∆ᵢH₂ < ∆ᵢH₃\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; Table 7.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Ionization Enthalpy and Electronegativity',NULL,'3','mcq','∆ᵢH₁ < ∆ᵢH₂ < ∆ᵢH₃','∆ᵢH₁ = ∆ᵢH₂ < ∆ᵢH₃','∆ᵢH₁ < ∆ᵢH₂ > ∆ᵢH₃','∆ᵢH₁ > ∆ᵢH₂ < ∆ᵢH₃','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210402,'chemistry','Ionization energy of group 15 elements is _____ than/to group 14 elements.','greater','Due to half filled p subshell ,the ionization energy of group 15 elements is greater ,because half filled or completey filled subshell are stable , they need more energy to remove electron\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 171; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Ionization Enthalpy and Electronegativity',NULL,'1','mcq','equal','greater','smaller','slightly smaller','NEW','12-171',NULL,NULL),
(210403,'chemistry','What is the trend of electronegativity value?','It decreases down the group','The electronegativity value, in general, decreases down the group with increasing atomic size. However, amongst the heavier  elements, the difference is not that much pronounced. \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Ionization Enthalpy and Electronegativity',NULL,'1','mcq','It increases down the group','It decreases down the group','It remains same throughout the group','It increases slightly down the group','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210405,'biology','In a resting state, the axoplasm contains-','High potassium concentration','When a neuron is not conducting any impulse, i.e., Resting, the axonal membrane is comparatively more permeable to potassium ions (K+ ) and nearly impermeable to sodium ions (Na+ ). Similarly, the membrane is impermeable to negatively charged proteins present in the axoplasm. Consequently, the axoplasm inside the axon contains high concentration of k + and negatively charged proteins and low concentration of Na+ . (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2) .','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','High potassium concentration','High sodium concentration','Low protein concentration','High protein concentration','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210406,'biology','In a resting state, the axoplasm contains-','Low sodium concentration','When a neuron is not conducting any impulse, i.e., Resting, the axonal membrane is comparatively more permeable to potassium ions (K+ ) and nearly impermeable to sodium ions (Na+ ). Similarly, the membrane is impermeable to negatively charged proteins present in the axoplasm. Consequently, the axoplasm inside the axon contains high concentration of k + and negatively charged proteins and low concentration of Na+ .(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Low potassium concentration','Low sodium concentration','Low protein concentration','high sodium concentration.','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210407,'biology','In a resting state, the fluid outside the axon contains-','High sodium concentration','In contrast, the fluid outside the axon contains a low concentration of k + , a high concentration of Na+ and thus form a concentration gradient. (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','High potassium concentration','High sodium concentration','High protein concentration','Low protein concentration','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210408,'biology','In a resting state, the fluid outside the axon contains-','Low potassium concentration','In contrast, the fluid outside the axon contains a low concentration of k + , a high concentration of Na+ and thus form a concentration gradient..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Low potassium concentration','Low sodium concentration','Low protein concentration','High protein concentration','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210409,'biology','Sodium-potassium pump transports___sodium ions outwards for__potassium ions into the axon cell.','3,2','These ionic gradients across the resting membrane are maintained by the active transport of ions by the sodium-potassium pump which transports 3 na + outwards for 2 k + into the cell.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2)E cell.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','2,4','2,3','3,2','4,2','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210410,'biology','The concentration gradient across the axonal membrane in a resting neuron is maintained by-','Active transport','These ionic gradients across the resting membrane are maintained by the active transport of ions by the sodium-potassium pump which transports 3 na + outwards for 2 k + into the cell..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Passive transport','Active transport','Osmosis','Diffusion','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210411,'biology','The concentration gradient across the axonal membrane in a resting neuron is maintained by-','Sodium-potassium pump','These ionic gradients across the resting membrane are maintained by the active transport of ions by the sodium-potassium pump which transports 3 na + outwards for 2 k + into the cell..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Sodium-potassium pump','Sodium-calcium pump','Potassium-Calcium pump','Potassium-Chlorine','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210412,'biology','In a resting state, outer surface of axonal membrane possesses a _______ charge.','Positive','In a resting state, the outer surface of the axonal membrane possesses a positive charge while its inner surface becomes negatively charged and therefore is polarised. .(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Positive','Negative','Neutral','Can\'t be determined','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210413,'biology','In a resting state, inner surface of axonal membrane possesses a _______ charge.','Negative','In a resting state, the outer surface of the axonal membrane possesses a positive charge while its inner surface becomes negatively charged and therefore is polarised. .(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Positive','Negative','Neutral','Can\'t be determined','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210414,'biology','The electrical potential difference across the resting plasma membrane is called-','Resting potential','The electrical potential difference across the resting plasma membrane is called as the resting potential.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 318;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Action potential','Resting potential','Neutral potential','Graded potential','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(210415,'biology','Application of a stimulus causes neuron cell membrane to become freely permeable to-','Sodium ion','When a stimulus is applied at a site on the polarised membrane, the membrane at the site a becomes freely permeable to Na+.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 318;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Potassium ion','Sodium ion','Chlorine ion','Calcium ion','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(210416,'biology','Application of a stimulus causes inner surface of neuron cell membrane to become-','Positive','When a stimulus is applied at a site on the polarised membrane, the membrane at the site a becomes freely permeable to Na+ . This leads to a rapid influx of Na+ followed by the reversal of the polarity at that site, i.e., The outer surface of the membrane becomes negatively charged and the inner side becomes positively charged..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 318; paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Positive','Negative','Neutral','All of above','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(210417,'biology','Application of a stimulus causes outer surface of neuron cell membrane to become-','Negative','When a stimulus is applied at a site on the polarised membrane, the membrane at the site a becomes freely permeable to Na+ . This leads to a rapid influx of Na+ followed by the reversal of the polarity at that site, i.e., The outer surface of the membrane becomes negatively charged and the inner side becomes positively charged..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 318;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Positive','Negative','Neutral','All of above','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(210418,'biology','Application of a stimulus causes neuron cell membrane to become-','Depolarised','When a stimulus is applied at a site (Figure 21.2 e.g., point A) on the polarised membrane, the membrane at the site A becomes freely permeable to Na+. This leads to a rapid influx of Na+ followed by the reversal of the polarity at that site, i.e., the outer surface of the membrane becomes negatively charged and the inner side becomes positively charged. The polarity of the membrane at the site A is thus reversed and hence depolarised.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 318;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Polarised','Depolarised','Repolarised','Hyperpolarise','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(210419,'biology','The electrical potential difference across the neuron cell membrane after application of stimulus is called-','Action potential','The electrical potential difference across the plasma membrane at the site a is called the action potential, which is in fact termed as a nerve impulse..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 318;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Action potential','Resting potential','Neutral potential','Graded potential','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(210420,'biology','The rise in the stimulus-induced permeability to Na+ is-','Extremely short lived','The rise in the stimulus-induced permeability to Na+ is extremely short lived.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 318;paragraph no.1).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','For several hours','For several days','depends on the axonal length','Extremely short lived','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(210421,'biology','_______ diffuses outside the membrane and restores the resting potential of the membrane at the site of excitation.','Potassium ion','The rise in the stimulus-induced permeability to Na+ is extremely short lived. It is quickly followed by a rise in permeability to K+ . Within a fraction of a second, K+ diffuses outside the membrane and restores the resting potential of the membrane at the site of excitation and the fibre becomes once more responsive to further stimulation..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 318;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Potassium ion','Sodium ion','Calcium ion','Chloride ion','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(210422,'biology','Which ion is represented by \'a\' in the diagram ? (2111)','Sodium ion','Figure 21.2 NCERT class 1 page no: 318 diagram matic representation of impulse conduction through an axon (at points a and b)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Potassium ion','Sodium ion','Calcium ion','Chloride ion','NEW','11-318','11-233','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/210422q.png'),
(210423,'biology','Ion channels present on the neural membrane are ___________ to different ions.','Selectively permeable','Ion channels present on the neural membrane are selectively permeable to different ions.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Generation and Conduction of Nerve Impulse','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Semi- permeable','Impermeable','Totally permeable','Selectively permeable','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210501,'chemistry','What is the atomicity of all the elements of group 15?','Polyatomic','All the elements of this group are polyatomic.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Polyatomic','Diatomic','Monoatomic','Triatomic','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210502,'chemistry','What is the trend of the boiling points of group 15 elements?','It first increases then decreases down the group','The boiling points, in general, increase from top to bottom in the group but the melting point increases upto antimony and then decreases upto bismuth.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Physical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','It increases down the group','It decreases down the group','It remains same throughout the group','It first increases then decreases down the group','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210503,'chemistry','What is the trend of the melting points of group 15?','It first increases then decreases down the group','The melting points, in general, increase from top to bottom in the group but the melting point increases upto arsenic and then decreases upto bismuth.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','It increases down the group','It decreases down the group','It remains same throughout the group','It first increases then decreases down the group','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210504,'chemistry','What is the trend of the metallic character of group 15 elements?','It increases down the group','The tendency to exhibit -3 oxidation state decreases down the group due to increase in size and metallic character.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','It increases down the group','It decreases down the group','It remains same throughout the group','It first increases then decreases down the group','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210505,'chemistry','Except nitrogen, what do all the elements show?','Allotropy','Except nitrogen, all the elements show allotropy.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Physical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Metallic character','Allotropy','Polytropy','Non-metallic character','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
