(210509,'biology','Upon generation of an action potential,_______move towards the axonal membrane and fuse with it.','Presynaptic vesicles','Upon generation of an action potential, presynaptic  vesicles moves towards the axonal membrane and fuse with the plasma membrane and release their neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 319;paragraph no.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Transmission of Impulses','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Presynaptic tubule','Presynaptic vesicles','Postsynaptic vesicles','Postsynaptic tubule','NEW','11-319','11-234',NULL),
(210510,'biology','Upon generation of an action potential, synaptic vesicles move towards the axonal membrane and fuse with it and release their neurotransmitters in the________________.','Synaptic cleft','Upon generation of an action potential, synaptic vesicles move towards the axonal membrane and fuse with it and release their neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 319-320;paragraph no.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Transmission of Impulses','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','Axoplasm','Synaptic cleft','Synaptic Terminal','Axon hillock','NEW','11-320','11-235',NULL),
(210511,'biology','Neurotransmitters released in the synaptic cleft bind with specific_______ on the postsynaptic membrane.','Receptors','The released neurotransmitters bind to their specific receptors, present on the postsynaptic membrane. This binding opens ion channels allowing the entry of ions which can generate a new potential in the postsynaptic neuron..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 320;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Transmission of Impulses','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Receptors','Sensory','Synaptic Terminal','Axon hillock','NEW','11-320','11-235',NULL),
(210512,'biology','The new potential developed after release of neurotransmitters is-','Either excitatory or inhibitory','The new potential developed after the release of neurotransmitters is either excitatory or inhibitory .(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 320;paragraph no.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Transmission of Impulses','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Excitatory','Inhibitory','Either excitatory or inhibitory','Does not  transmit any signals','NEW','11-320','11-235',NULL),
(210513,'biology','Label the parts of axon terminal in the diagram . (2112)','Axon-a,Synaptic vesicles-b,Receptors-c,Axon terminal-d','Axon-a,\nSynaptic vesicles-b,\nReceptors-c,\nAxon terminal-d\nFigure 21.3 NCERT class 11 page no:319 diagram showing axon terminal and synapse','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Transmission of Impulses','Neural Control and Coordination','2','match','Axon,a','Synaptic vesicles,b','Receptors,c','Axon terminal,d','NEW','11-319','11-234','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/210513q.png'),
(210514,'biology','In the given diagram,synaptic vesicles are shown by \n(2113)','b','Figure 21.3 NCERT class 11 page no:319 diagram showing axon terminal and synapse','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Transmission of Impulses','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-319','11-234','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/210514q.png'),
(210515,'biology','In the diagram , receptors are shown by (2114)','C','Figure 21.3 NCERT class 11 page no:319 diagram showing axon terminal and synapse','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Transmission of Impulses','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-319','11-234','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/210515q.png'),
(210601,'chemistry','What are the common oxidation states of the elements in group 15?','-3, +3, +5','The common oxidation states of these elements are –3, +3 and +5.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','-2, +1, +5','-3, +4, +5','-3, +3, +5','-2, +3, +4','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210602,'chemistry','How does the tendency to show -3 oxidation state varies down the group?','It decreases down the group','Down the group size and metallic character increase,which increase the abilty to  remove electron rather addition of electrons\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','It first increases then decreases down the group','It first decreases then increases down the group','It decreases down the group','It remains same down the group','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210603,'chemistry','Which element hardly forms any compound in –3 oxidation state?','Bismuth','The tendency to exhibit –3 oxidation state decreases down the group due to increase in size and metallic character. In fact last member of the group, bismuth hardly forms any compound in –3 oxidation state. \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Bismuth','Arsenic','Phospohorus','Nitrogen','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210604,'chemistry','How does the stability of +5 oxidation state varies down the group?','It decreases down the group','The stability of +5 oxidation state decreases down the group. The only well characterised Bi (V) compound is BiF₅ . The stability of +5  oxidation state decreases and that of +3 state increases (due to inert pair effect) down the group. \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','It first increases then decreases down the group','It first decreases then increases down the group','It decreases down the group','It remains same down the group','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210605,'chemistry','Which is the only well characterised Bi(V) compound?','BiF₅','The only well characterised Bi(V) compound is BiF₅.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','BiF₅','BiI₅','BiBr₅','BiCl₅','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(250719,'biology','During follicular phase, changes in ovary and uterus are induced by changes in the levels of :','Pituitary and Ovarian hormones','During follicular phase, the primary follicles in the ovary grow to become a fully mature Graafian follicle and simultaneously the endometrium of uterus regenerates through proliferation Reference  : NCERT Page 51','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Menstrual Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','Pituitary hormones only','Ovarian hormones','Pituitary and Ovarian hormones','Uterine','NEW','12-051','12-035',NULL),
(210606,'chemistry','How does the stability of +3 oxidation state varies down the group?','It increases down the group','Inert pair effect increase down the group ,inert pair effect prevents ns electrons to be removed due to poor screening effect of d-subshell, so only np electrons remove and show +3 oxidation state\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','It increases down the group','It decreases down the group','It remains same throughout the group','It first increases then decreases down the group','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210607,'chemistry','Besides +5 oxidation state, nitrogen exhibits which oxidation states also when it reacts with oxygen?','1+, 2+, 4+','Besides +5 oxidation state, nitrogen exhibits + 1, + 2, + 4 oxidation states also when it reacts with oxygen.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','1+, 2+, 3+','2+, 2+, 4+','1+, 2+, 4+','1+, 2+, 5+','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210608,'chemistry','Why Nitrogen can\'t make binary compounds with halogens?','Due to absence of d-subshell','Nitrogen can\'t make compounds in +5 oxidation state with halogens ,as Nitrogen do not have d -subshell to accomodate the electrons from other elements.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Due to absence of p-subshell','Due to absence of d-subshell','Due to absence of s-subshell','Due to absence of completely filled s-subshell','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210609,'chemistry','Which oxidation states are shown by phosphorous in some oxoacids?','1+ and 4+','Phosphorus also shows 1+ and 4+ oxidation states in some oxoacids.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','1+ and 3+','1+ and 4+','2+ and 4+','3+ and 4+','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210610,'chemistry','Match the following.','Nitrogen-Disproportionate in acid solution, Phosphorus-Disproportionate in alkali and acid solutions, Arsenic-No disporportionation, d-orbitals-Absent in nitrogen','In the case of nitrogen, all oxidation states from +1 to +4 tend to disproportionate in acid solution. Similarly, in case of phosphorus nearly all intermediate oxidation states disproportionate into +5 and –3 both in alkali and acid. However +3 oxidation state in case of arsenic, antimony and bismuth becomes increasingly stable with respect to disproportionation.d subshell starts from 3rd period nitrogen being present in second doesn\'t have \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','match','Nitrogen,Disproportionate in acid solution','Phosphorus,Disproportionate in alkali and acid solutions','Arsenic,No disporportionation','d-orbitals,Absent in nitrogen','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210611,'chemistry','What is the maximum covalence of Nitrogen?','Four','The maximum covalence of Nitrogen is 4.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Four','Three','Five','One','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210612,'chemistry','Which group 15 element can\'t expand it\'s octet ?','Nitrogen','Except nitrogen, all other members have empty d-subshell, which can be used to accomodate the electrons from other elements.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Nitrogen','Phosphorus','Bismuth','Arsenic','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210613,'chemistry','Which is not the anomalous property of nitrogen?','Compounds in +5 oxidation state with halogens','Nitrogen differs from the rest of the members of this group due to its small size, high electronegativity, high ionisation enthalpy and non-availability of d orbitals. Nitrogen does not form compounds in +5 oxidation state with halogens as nitrogen does not have d-orbitals to accommodate electrons from other elements to form bonds.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Absence of d-subshell','Compounds in +5 oxidation state with halogens','Very high ionization energy','3+ oxidation state','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210614,'chemistry','In Nitrogen, which obitals are available for bonding?','One s and three p-orbitals','Nitrogen is restricted to a maximum covalency of 4 since only four (one s and three p) orbitals are available for bonding. \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Two s and three p-orbitals','One s and three p-orbitals','One s and two p-orbitals','Two s and two p-orbitals','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210615,'chemistry','Nitrogen has unique ability to form which multiple bonds with itself and with other elements?','pπ-pπ','Nitrogen differs from the rest of the members of this group due to its small size, high electronegativity, high ionisation enthalpy and non-availability of d orbitals. Nitrogen has unique ability to form pπ-pπ multiple bonds with itself and with other elements having small size and high electronegativity (e.g., C, O).\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','spπ-pπ','spπ-spπ','pπ-pπ','sp2π-sp2π','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210616,'chemistry','Which among the following bonding is not observed in heavier members of Group-15 elements?','pπ-pπ','The atomic orbitals of heavier members of group 15 elements are so large and diffused that they can not do effective overlap.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','dπ-pπ','pπ-pπ','sπ-pπ','sπ-sπ','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210617,'chemistry','Match the following.','Nitrogen-triple bond, oxygen-double bond, Arsenic-single bond, Bismuth-metallic bond','The correct match is Nitrogen-triple bond, oxygen-double bond, Arsenic-single bond, Bismuth-metallic bond\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 172; Paragraph No.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','match','Nitrogen,triple bond','Oxygen,double bond','Arsenic,single bond','Bismuth,metallic bond','NEW','12-172',NULL,NULL),
(210618,'chemistry','The single N–N bond is ________ than the single P–P bond.','weaker','The single N–N bond is weaker than the single P–P bond because of high interelectronic repulsion of the non-bonding electrons, owing to the small bond length. \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','stronger','weaker','of same strength','slightly stronger','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210619,'chemistry','The catenation tendency is ________ in nitrogen.','weaker','The single N–N bond is weaker than the single P–P bond because of high interelectronic repulsion of the non-bonding electrons, owing to the small bond length. As a result the catenation tendency is weaker in nitrogen.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','stronger','weaker','of same strength','slightly stronger','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210620,'chemistry','The absence of which orbital affects the chemistry of Nitrogen?','d-orbital','Due to unavailibilty of d-subshell in nitrogen it is uable to show pπ-dπ bonding \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','p-orbital','s-orbital','d-orbital','f-orbital','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210621,'chemistry','Which bonding is nitrogen unable to show?','pπ-dπ','Due to unavailibilty of d-subshell in nitrogen it is uable to show pπ-dπ bonding \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','pπ-dπ','pπ-pπ','pπ-sπ','sπ-sπ','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210622,'chemistry','What type of π-bonding is present in R₃P=O?','pπ-dπ','Besides restricting its covalency to four, nitrogen cannot form dπ –pπ bond as the heavier elements can e.g., R₃P=O\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','pπ-dπ','pπ-pπ','pπ-sπ','sπ-dπ','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210623,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and Reason (R)\n\nAssertion : Phosphorus and arsenic can form dπ-dπ bond with transition metals\n\nReason : Their compounds act like as ligands','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','P(C₂H₅)₃, As(C₆H₅)₃, being having lone pair on P and As, it can act as donor and make bond with transition metals\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210624,'chemistry','What type of hydrides are formed by members of Group-15?','EH₃','Group-15 elements form EH₃ type of hydrides.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','EH₃','EH','EH₂','EH₄','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210625,'chemistry','Consider the following:\n\n(a) The stability of hydrides decreases from NH₃ to BiH₃. \n(b) The reducing character of the hydrides increases down the group.\n(c)  Ammonia is a strong reducing agent. \n(d) BiH₃ is the strongest reducing agent amongst all the hydrides. \n\nWhich of the following options are correct?','(a), (b), (d)','Reactivity towards hydrogen: All the elements of Group 15 form hydrides of the type EH₃ where E = N, P, As, Sb or Bi. The hydrides show regular gradation in their properties. The stability of hydrides decreases from NH₃ to BiH₃ which can be observed from their bond dissociation enthalpy. Consequently, the reducing character of the hydrides increases. Ammonia is only a mild reducing agent while BiH₃ is the strongest reducing agent amongst all the hydrides.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','(a), (c), (d)','(a), (b), (c)','(a), (b), (d)','(b), (c), (d)','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210626,'chemistry','What is the order of basicity?','NH₃ > PH₃ > AsH₃ > SbH₃','Order if basicity is NH₃ > PH₃ > AsH₃ > SbH₃. The stability of hydrides decreases from NH₃ to BiH₃ which can be observed from their bond dissociation enthalpy. Consequently, the reducing character of the hydrides increases. Thus, acidity increases down the group.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','order','1,NH₃','2,PH₃','3,AsH₃','4,SbH₃','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210627,'chemistry','Which bonding in nitrogen is present in solid as well as liquid state.?','Hydrogen bonding','NH₃ exhibits hydrogen bonding in solid as well as liquid state.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Non-metallic bonding','Metallic bonding','Hydrogen bonding','Van dar waals forces','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210628,'chemistry','Match the following:','NH₃-Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛH°=389 kJ/mol, PH₃-Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛ=322 kJ/mol, AsH₃-Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛH°=297 kJ/mol, SbH₃-Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛH°=255 kJ/mol','The correct match is NH₃-Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛH°=389 kJ/mol, PH₃-Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛ=322 kJ/mol, AsH₃-Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛH°=297 kJ/mol, SbH₃-Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛH°=255 kJ/mol\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Table No.7.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','match','NH₃,Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛH°=389 kJ/mol','PH₃,Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛH°=322 kJ/mol','AsH₃,Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛH°=297 kJ/mol','SbH₃,Δ𝒹ᵢₛₛH°=255 kJ/mol','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210629,'chemistry','Consider the following:\n\n(a) All these elements form two types of oxides: E₂O₂ and E₂O₅. \n(b) The oxide in the higher oxidation state of the element is more acidic than that of lower oxidation state. \n(c) Their acidic character decreases down the group.\n(d) The oxides of the type E₂O₃ of nitrogen and phosphorus are purely basic. \n\nWhich of the following options are correct regarding group 15 elements reactivty with oxygen?','(b), (c)','All these elements form two types of oxides: E₂O₃ and E₂O₅. The oxide in the higher oxidation state of the element is more acidic than that of lower oxidation state. Their acidic character decreases down the group. The oxides of the type E₂O₃ of nitrogen and phosphorus are purely acidic, that of arsenic and antimony amphoteric and those of bismuth predominantly basic. \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 173; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','(a), (c), (d)','(b), (c)','(a), (b)','(b), (c), (d)','NEW','12-173',NULL,NULL),
(210630,'chemistry','Consider the following:\n\n(a) Grouip-15 elements react to form two series of halides: EX₃ and EX₅. \n(b) Nitrogen forms pentahalide. \n(c) Pentahalides are more covalent than trihalides.\n(d) Trihalides except BiF₃ are predominantly covalent in nature.\n\nWhich of the following options are correct regarding group 15 elements reactivty with halogen?','(a), (c), (d)','Grouip-15 elements react to form two series of halides: EX₃ and EX₅. . Nitrogen does not form pentahalide due to non-availability of the d orbitals in its valence shell. Pentahalides are more covalent than trihalides. This is due to the fact that in pentahalides +5 oxidation state exists while in the case of trihalides +3 oxidation state exists. Trihalides except BiF₃ are predominantly covalent in nature.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175, Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','(a), (c), (d)','(a), (b), (c)','(a), (b), (d)','(b), (c), (d)','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(210631,'chemistry','What do binary compounds of group 15 elements with metals exhibit?','3- oxidation state','All these elements react with metals to form their binary compounds exhibiting –3 oxidation state,\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 174,paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','5- oxidation state','3- oxidation state','2- oxidation state','1- oxidation state','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(210632,'chemistry','Despite of ability of nitrogen to show 5+ oxidation, still unable to make pentahalides. What is the reason?','Due to absence of d-subshell','Nitrogen with n=2 has s and p orbitals only it does not have d-subshell to expand its covalence beyond 4\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 174, example 7.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Due to presence of d-subshell','Due to absence of d-subshell','Due to absence of p-subshell','Due to presence of half-filled p-orbitals','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(210633,'chemistry','Why is the boiling point of PH₃ has lower than NH₃?','Because of hydrogen bonding','Unlike NH₃, PH₃ molecules are not associated through hydrogen bonding in liquid state. That is why the boiling point of PH₃ is lower than NH₃.\n(NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 174, example 7.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group -15 Elements - Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Because of dipole forces','Because of hydrogen bonding','Because of covalent bond','Because of dispersion forces','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(210701,'chemistry','Dinitrogen is produced commercially by the liquefaction and _________ of air.','Fractional distillation','Dinitrogen is produced commercially by the liquefaction and Fractional distillation of air.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 174, Topic 7.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'1','mcq','Fractional distillation','Vaccum distillation','Steam distillation','Paper chromatography','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(210702,'chemistry','Match the following','Liquid dinitrogen-Boiling point:77.2K, Liquid Oxygen-Boiling point;90K, Ammonia-Boiing point;239.7 K, Nitric acid-Boiling point:355.6 K','The correct match is Liquid dinitrogen-Boiling point:77.2K, Liquid Oxygen-Boiling point:90K, Ammonia-Boiing point:239.7 K, Nitric acid-Boiling point:355.6 K\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 174, Topic 7.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'3','match','Liquid dinitrogen,Boiling point:77.2K','Liquid Oxygen,Boiling point:90K','Ammonia, Boiing point:239.7 K','Nitric acid,Boiling point:355.6 K','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(210703,'chemistry','In the laboratory preparation of dinitrogen, what is the aqueous solution of ammonium chloride treated with?','Sodium nitrite','In the laboratory, dinitrogen is prepared by treating an aqueous solution of ammonium chloride with sodium nitrite.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 174, Topic 7.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'2','mcq','Sodium nitrite','Potassium nitrate','Calcium nitrate','Barium nitrate','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(210704,'chemistry','What does thermal decomposition of ammonium dichromate give?','Cr₂O₃','The thermal decomposition of ammonium dichromate gives N₂ and Cr₂O₃\n(NH₄)₂Cr₂O₇ → N₂ + Cr₂O₃ + H₂O\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 174, Topic 7.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'2','mcq','Cr₂O₃','Cr₃O₂','Cr₂O₇','CrO','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(210705,'chemistry','Complete the equation: \nNH₄CI(aq) + NaNO₂ (aq) → _________ + 2H₂O(l) + NaCl (aq).','N₂ (g)','In the laboratory, dinitrogen is prepared by treating an aqueous solution of ammonium chloride with sodium nitrite.\nNH₄CI (aq) + NaNO₂ (aq) → N₂ (g) + 2H₂O (l) + NaCl (aq). \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 174, Topic 7.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'2','mcq','N₂O (g)','NO₂ (g)','HNO₃ (g)','N₂ (g)','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(210706,'chemistry','Which is the correct reaction for thermal decomposition of sodium or barium azide?','Ba(N₃)₂→ Ba + 3N₂','The reaction for thermal decomposition of sodium or barium azide is-\nBa(N₃)₂→ Ba + 3N₂\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 174, Topic 7.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'3','mcq','Ba(N₃)₂→ Ba + N₂','Ba(N₃)₂→ Ba + 3N₂','Ba(N₃)₂→ Ba + 4N₂','Ba(N₃)₂→ Ba + 2N₂','NEW','12-174',NULL,NULL),
(210707,'chemistry','Which among the folowing doesn\'t describe dinitrogen gas?','Toxic','Dinitrogen is a colourless, odourless, tasteless and non-toxic gas.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175, Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'1','mcq','Colourless','Odourless','Tasteless','Toxic','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(210708,'chemistry','What are the two stable isotopes of nitrogen?','¹⁴N,¹⁵N','The two stable isotopes of Nitrogen are ¹⁴N and ¹⁵N\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175, Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'1','mcq','¹⁴N,¹⁵N','¹³N,¹⁴N','¹³N,¹⁵N','¹⁴N,¹⁶N','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(210709,'chemistry','Dinitrogen is ______ at room temprature','inert','Dinitrogen is Inert at room temprature\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175, Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'1','mcq','inert','reactive','monoatomic','toxic','NEW','12-175',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/210709q.png'),
(210710,'chemistry','6Li  +  N₂   →  2Li₃N\nWhat type of nitride is Li₃N?','Ionic nitride','At higher temperatures, it directly combines with some metals to form predominantly ionic nitrides and with non-metals, covalent nitrides. Li₃N is an ionic nitride.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175, Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'2','mcq','Covalent niride','Ionic nitride','Amphoteric nitride','Acidic nitride','NEW','12-175',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/210710q.png'),
(210711,'chemistry','Nitrogen combines with hydrogen at about 773 K in the presence of a catalyst to form ammonia. What is this process called?','Haber’s Process','Nitrogen combines with hydrogen at about 773 K in the presence of a catalyst (Haber’s Process) to form ammonia:\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g)  ⇄ 2NH₃(g);\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175, Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'1','mcq','Brown Ring Test','Contact Process','Haber’s Process','Deacon’s process:','NEW','12-175',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/210711q.png'),
(210712,'chemistry','What is formed when Dinitrogen combines with dioxygen?','Nitric oxide','Dinitrogen combines with dioxygen to form \nN₂ + O₂ (g) ⇄  2NO (g)\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175, Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'2','mcq','Nitrogen','Nitric acid','Dinitrogen oxide','Nitric oxide','NEW','12-175',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/210712q.png'),
(210713,'chemistry','Which one of the following doesn\'t have use of Dinitrogen?','Produce various nitrogenous fertilisers','The main use of dinitrogen is in the manufacture of ammonia and other industrial chemicals containing nitrogen, (e.g., calcium cyanamide).  It also finds use where an inert atmosphere is required (e.g., in iron and steel industry, inert diluent for reactive chemicals). Liquid dinitrogen is used as a refrigerant to preserve biological materials, food items and in cryosurgery.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175, Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'3','mcq','NH₃ synthesis','Iron and steel industry','Refrigerant and cryosurgery','Produce various nitrogenous fertilisers','NEW','12-175',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/210713q.png'),
(210714,'chemistry','What is the reaction of thermal decomposition of sodium azide?','2NaN₃ → 2Na+3N₂','Thermal decomposition of sodium azide gives dinitrogen gas.\n2NaN₂ → 2Na+3N₂\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175, Example no. 7.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Dinitrogen',NULL,'2','mcq','2NaN → 2Na + N₂','2NaN₃ → 2Na+3N₂','2NaN₂ → 2Na + 2N₂','2Na₂N₂ → 4Na + 2N₂','NEW','12-175',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/210714q.png'),
