(210721,'biology','Which of the following is in direct contact with the Brain?','Pia matter','Pia matter is the innermost layer of the meninges, this means that it is directly connected to the brain and provide support to the brain.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','Arachnoid matter','Pia matter','Dura matter','Sub-arachnoid matter','NEW','11-320','11-235',NULL),
(210722,'biology','Which layer of the meninges is closet to the skull?','Dura matter','Since Dura matter is the outermost layer of the meninges, aa fall would most likely affect the dura matter first before it causes damage to other structures in the brain.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','Arachnoid matter','Pia matter','Dura matter','Sub-arachnoid matter','NEW','11-320','11-235',NULL),
(210723,'biology','What does the vertebral column hold?','Spinal cord','The vertebral column or the spinal Column, houses the spinal cord','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','1','mcq','Brainstem','Meninges','Medulla','Spinal cord','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(210724,'biology','The nerve centers which controls the body temperature and the urge for eating are contained in','Hypothalamus','The nerve centers which control the body temperature and the urge for eating are contained in the hypothalamus.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','Hypothalamus','pons','Cerebellum','Thalamus','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(210725,'biology','What influences the vestibular apparatus?','Gravity','The inner ear also contains a complex system located above the cochlea called vestibular apparatus. It is influenced by gravity and movements, and helps us in maintaining balance of the body and posture.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','Gravity','Sound','Temperature','Vibrations','NEW','11-328',NULL,NULL),
(210726,'biology','What is the initial event in an action potential?','Release of Neurotransmitter','Since nerve impulses originate after a neurotransmitter is recieved, we can assume that release of a neurotransmitter is the first step in the formation of an impulse.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','Depolarization','Entry of Na+ ions','Release of Neurotransmitter','Repolarization','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(210727,'biology','Which part of the brain is responsible for balance and co-ordination of movements?','Cerebellum','Part of the brain is responsible for balance and co-ordination of movements is called the cerebellum','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','Frontal lobe','Cerebellum','Medually','Corpus Callosum','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(210728,'biology','Which of the following is a neurotransmitter?','Serotonin','Serotonin is a neurotransmitter. On the other hand, insulin,Gastrin and Secretin are hormones.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','Serotonin','Insulin','Secretin','Gastrin','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(210729,'biology','Which of the following is non myelinated?','Somatic nerves','This is because Somatic nerves are the only one which are not myelinated. All the other nerves have myelination around them. This myelination is provided by the Schwann Cells.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','Spinal nerves','Nerves covered with Schwann cells','Somatic nerves','Cranial nerves','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210730,'biology','Ions responsible for the nerve impulse can freely move through the neuron cell membrane.','FALSE','Ions can move through the neuron cell membrane due to selective permeability. If these ions were allowed to freely move, the generation of an impulse would not be possible.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(210731,'biology','The cerebral aqueduct eventually enters the spinal cord','TRUE','The cerebral aqueduct eventually enters the spinal cord at the spinal canal. This duct contains the CSF (Cerebrospinal fluid) which is responsible for the removal of waste from the brain. Additionally, it also helps equalize the weight of the brain.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(210732,'biology','Which part of the brain is responsible for thermoregulation ?','Hypothalamus','hypothalamus is responsible for thermoregulation.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','Medulla oblongata','Cerebrum','Hypothalamus','Corpus callosum','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(210733,'biology','The secretion of gastric juice is controlled by','Medulla','The secretion of gastric juice is controlled by medulla.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','Cerebellum','ANS','Cerebrum','Medulla','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(210734,'biology','The cerebral cortex is','The outer layer of cerebrum, called gray matter','The cerebral cortex is the outer layer of cerebrum, called gray matter.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> CNS 2','Neural Control and Coordination','3','mcq','The outer layer of cerebrum, called white matter','The inner layer of cerebrum, called white matter','The outer layer of cerebrum, called gray matter','The inner layer of cerebrum, called gray matter','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(210801,'chemistry','Ammonia is present in _________ quantities in air.','small','Ammonia is present in small quantities in air and soil where it is formed by the decay of nitrogenous organic matter \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ammonia - Preparation',NULL,'1','mcq','small','very large','large','medium','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(210802,'chemistry','What does decomposition of urea give?','Ammonia','Ammonia is present in small quantities in air and soil where it is formed by the decay of nitrogenous organic matter e.g., urea.\nNH₂CONH₂ + 2H₂O  → (NH₄)₂CO₃ ⇄ 2NH₃ + H₂O + CO₂\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ammonia - Preparation',NULL,'1','mcq','Ammonia','Dinitrogen','Nitrates','Nitrites','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(210803,'chemistry','What does the decomposition of ammonium salts with caustic soda give?','Ammonia','On a small scale ammonia is obtained from ammonium salts which decompose when treated with caustic soda.\n(NH₄)₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → 2NH₃ + 2H₂O + Na₂SO₄\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 175)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ammonia - Preparation',NULL,'1','mcq','Nitrogen','Ammonia','Nitrates','Nitrites','NEW','12-175',NULL,NULL),
(210804,'chemistry','On a large scale, ammonia is manufactured by Haber’s process. What is the ∆ғH (kJ/mol) of reaction?','-46.1 kJ/mol','On a large scale, ammonia is manufactured by Haber’s process. The heat of formation is -46.1 kJ/mol\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 176)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ammonia - Preparation',NULL,'3','mcq','-46.1 kJ/mol','-26.1 kJ/mol','-46.9 kJ/mol','-26.9 kJ/mol','NEW','12-176',NULL,NULL),
(210805,'chemistry','In accordance with Le Chatelier’s principle, _________ pressure would favour the formation of ammonia.','high','In accordance with Le Chatelier’s principle, high pressure would favour the formation of ammonia.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 176)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ammonia - Preparation',NULL,'1','mcq','high','low','very low','extremely high','NEW','12-176',NULL,NULL),
(210806,'chemistry','Which is not the optimum conditions for the manufacturing of ammonia ?','Pressure 200 Pa','The optimum conditions for the production of ammonia are a pressure of 200 × 10⁵ Pa (about 200 atm), a temperature of ~ 700 K and the use of a catalyst such as iron oxide with small amounts of K₂O and Al₂O₃ to increase the rate of attainment of equilibrium.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 176)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ammonia - Preparation',NULL,'1','mcq','Pressure 200 atm','Temprature 700 K','Fe₂O₃, K₂O, Al₂O₃ as catalyst','Pressure 200 Pa','NEW','12-176',NULL,NULL),
(210807,'chemistry','A catalyst can help in ______ the rate of attainment of _________.','increase, equilibrium','The optimum conditions for the production of ammonia are a pressure of 200 × 10⁵ Pa (about 200 atm), a temperature of ~ 700 K and the use of a catalyst such as iron oxide with small amounts of K₂O and Al₂O₃ to increase the rate of attainment of equilibrium.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 176)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Ammonia - Preparation',NULL,'1','mcq','increase, completion','decrease, completion','decrease, equilibrium','increase, equilibrium','NEW','12-176',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/210807q.png'),
