(211303,'chemistry','White phosphorus is a _______ white _____ solid.','translucent, waxy','White phosphorus is a translucent white waxy solid. It is poisonous, insoluble in water but soluble in carbon disulphide and glows in dark (chemiluminescence). It dissolves in boiling NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere giving PH₃.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 180)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus Allotropic Forms',NULL,'1','mcq','translucent, waxy','translucent, oily','transparent, waxy','transparent, oily','NEW','12-180',NULL,NULL),
(211304,'chemistry','White phosphorus is-','insoluble in water but soluble in carbon disulphide','White phosphorus is a translucent white waxy solid. It is poisonous, insoluble in water but soluble in carbon disulphide and glows in dark (chemiluminescence).\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 180)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus Allotropic Forms',NULL,'1','mcq','soluble in water and soluble in carbon disulphide','insoluble in water and insoluble in carbon disulphide','insoluble in water but soluble in carbon disulphide','soluble in water but insoluble in carbon disulphide','NEW','12-180',NULL,NULL),
(211305,'chemistry','Consider the following:\n\n(a) Black phosphorus is less stable. \n(b) Phosphorus is more reactive than the other solid phases under normal conditions. \n(c) Phosphorus catches fire in air to give dense white fumes of P₄O₁₀.\n(d) White Phosphorus consists of discrete tetrahedral P₄ molecule\n\nWhich of the following options are correct ?','(b), (c) and (d)','White phosphorus is less stable due to presence of angular strain that\'s why more reactive. P₄ molecule is cyclic having tetrahedral geometry \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus Allotropic Forms',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) and (b)','(a), (b) and (d)','(b), (c) and (d)','(a) and (c)','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211306,'chemistry','What is obtained by heating white phosphorus at 573K in an inert atmosphere for several days?','Red phosphorus','Red phosphorus is obtained by heating white phosphorus at 573K in an inert atmosphere for several days.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus Allotropic Forms',NULL,'2','mcq','White phosphorus','Brown phosphorus','Black phosphorus','Red phosphorus','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211307,'chemistry','When red phosphorus is heated under high pressure, a series of phases of _________ phosphorus is formed.','black','When red phosphorus is heated under high pressure, a series of phases of black phosphorus is formed.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus Allotropic Forms',NULL,'1','mcq','black','white','red','brown','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211308,'chemistry','Consider the following:\n\n(a) Red phosphorus possesses iron grey lustre.\n(b) Red phosphorus is odourless, non-poisonous and insoluble in water as well as in carbon disulphide.\n(c) Chemically, red phosphorus is much less reactive than white phosphorus.\n(d) Red phosphorus glows in the dark.\n\nWhich of the following options are false?','(d)','Red phosphorus is obtained by heating white phosphorus at 573K in an inert atmosphere for several days. When red phosphorus is heated under high pressure, a series of phases of black phosphorus is formed. Red phosphorus possesses iron grey lustre. It is odourless, non- poisonous and insoluble in water as well as in carbon disulphide. Chemically, red phosphorus is much less reactive than white phosphorus. It does not glow in the dark.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus Allotropic Forms',NULL,'3','mcq','(a)','(b)','(c)','(d)','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211309,'chemistry','How many forms of black phosphorous are present?','Two','Black phosphorus has two forms α-black and β-black phosphorous \n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus Allotropic Forms',NULL,'1','mcq','One','Two','Three','Four','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
