(211329,'biology','The cerebellum integrates information from which part of the cochlea?','Semicircular canals','The vestibular apparatus is composed of three semicircular canals and the otolith. These help in providing balance and coordination. However impulses from these receptors is first transmitted to the cerebellum where they can be processed by the brain.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> The Ear 2','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','External acoustic meatus','Semicircular canals','Cochlea','Organ of corti','OLD','11-328','11-237',NULL),
(211401,'chemistry','Phosphine is prepared by the reaction of calcium phosphide with water or _________.','dilute HCl','Phosphine is prepared by the reaction of calcium phosphide with water or dilute HCl. \nCa₃P₂ + 6H₂O → 3Ca(OH)₂ + 2PH₃ \nCa₃P₂ + 6HCl → 3CaCl₂ + 2PH₃\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphine',NULL,'1','mcq','dilute HCl','conc. HCl','dilute H₂SO₄','conc. H₂SO₄','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211402,'chemistry','In the laboratory, phosphine is prepared by heating _________ with concentrated NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere of CO₂.','white phosphorus','In the laboratory, phosphine is prepared by heating white phosphorus with concentrated NaOH solution in an inert atmosphere of CO₂.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphine',NULL,'1','mcq','white phosphorus','red phosphorus','black phosphorus','blue phosphorus','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211403,'chemistry','Complete the following reaction: \nP₄ + 3NaOH + 3H₂O → _________ + 3NaH₃PO₂.','PH₃','Phosphorus get dissolved in NaOH giving PH₃.\nP₄ + 3NaOH + 3H₂O → PH₃ + 3NaH₃PO₂\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphine',NULL,'2','mcq','PH₂','PH₃','PH₅','PH','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211404,'chemistry','What is the name of NaH₂PO₂?','Sodium hypophosphite','The name of NaH₂PO₂ is Sodium hypophosphite\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphine',NULL,'1','mcq','Sodium phosphite','Sodium hypophosphite','Sodium hypophosphate','Sodium phosphate','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211405,'chemistry','The properties of phosphine are:\n\na. It is a colourless gas with rotten fish smell. \nb. It is slightly poisonous.\nc. It explodes in contact with traces of oxidising agents like HNO₃, Cl₂ and Br₂ vapours. \nd. It is slightly soluble in water. \n\nWhich of the following option is false?','b','Phosphine is highly poisonous.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphine',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211406,'chemistry','Match the following.','PH₃ on reacting with metal salts-Phosphides, PH₃ on reaction with acids-Phosphonium salts, Holme\'s signal-PH₃, PH₃-Lewis base','The correct match is PH₃ on reacting with metal salts-Phosphides, PH₃ on reaction with acids-Phosphonium salts, Holme\'s signal-PH₃, PH₃-Lewis base\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphine',NULL,'3','match','PH₃ on reacting with metal salts,Phosphides','PH₃ on reactin with acids,Phosphonium salts','Holme\'s signal,PH₃','PH₃,Lewis base','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211407,'chemistry','Choose the wrong statement\n\na. PH₃ is basic in nature\nb. Bond angle in PH₄⁺ is higher than PH₃ \nc. On reaction with acids PH₃ form phosphides\nd. White phosphorus on reaction with conc.NaOH in presence of CO₂ give PH₃','Only c','Phosphine forms phosphides when it is absorbed in copper sulphate or mercuric chloride solution,\n3CuSO₄ + 2PH₃ → Cu₃P₂ + 3H₂SO₄\n3HgCl₂ + 2PH₃ → Hg₃P₂ + 6HCl\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphine',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only c','Only d','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211408,'chemistry','PH₃ is also used in Smoke______','screen','Containers containing calcium carbide and calcium phosphide are pierced and thrown in the sea when the gases evolved burn and serve as a signal. It is also used in smoke screens.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 181)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphine',NULL,'1','mcq','lights','screen','generator','signals','NEW','12-181',NULL,NULL),
(211409,'biology','Alzheimer’s disease in humans is associated with the deficiency of','Acetylcholine','Alzheimer’s disease is a neurological disease characterized by progressive loss of intellectual ability. The disease, which is named after german physician alois alzheimer (1864-1915), is associated with general shrinkage of the brain tissue, with deposits of β-amyloid protein and abnormal filaments composed of tau protein in the brain, and changes in the neurotransmitter systems within the brain that include a loss in the activity of cholinergic neurons (neurons releasing acetylcholine). Some inherited forms are associated with a genetic locus on chromosome 21','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Glutamic acid','Acetylcholine','Gamma aminobutyric acid','Dopamine','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211410,'biology','During the propagation of a nerve impulse, the action potential results from the movement of [AIPMT 2008]','Na+ ions from extracellular fluid to intracellular fluid','Action potential is the change in electrical potential that occurs across a plasma membrane during the passage of a nerve impulse. As an impulse travels in a wave-like manner along the axon of a nerve, it causes a localized and transient switch in electric potential across the membrane from –60 mv (millivolts; the resting potential) to + 45 mv. It is due to the fact that the sodium channels open and the potassium channels remain closed. As a result, sodium channels permit the influx of Na+ by diffusion from extracellular fluid to intracellular fluid..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 318;paragraph no.2).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','K+ ions from intracellular fluid to extracellular fluid','Na+ ions from extracellular fluid to intracellular fluid','K+ ions from extracellular fluid to intracellular fluid','Na+ ions from intracellular fluid to extracellular fluid','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(211411,'biology','During the transmission of nerve impulse through a nerve fibre, the potential on the inner side of the plasma membrane has which type of electric charge? [AIPMT 2007]','First negative, then positive and again back to negative','The signal that travels along the length of a nerve fibre and is the means by which information is transmitted through the nervous system is called nerve impulse. During resting phase, inside of the plasma membrane is negatively charged (polarized, -70 mv, resting potential). As the stimulus reaches it becomes positively charged (+45 mv, action potential). This condition is known as depolarization of nerve fibres. As impulse passes away, it regains its original ionic distribution and becomes repolarized..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','First positive, then negative and continue to be negative','First negative, then positive and continue to be positive','First positive, then negative and again back to positive','First negative, then positive and again back to negative','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211412,'biology','Which one of the following does not act as a neurotransmitter? [AIPMT 2006]','Cortisone','Neurotransmitters are chemicals that are used to relay, amplify and modulate electrical signals between a neuron and another cell. Substances that act as neurotransmitters can be categorized into three major groups: (i) amino acids (primarily glutamic acid, gaba, aspartic acid and glycine), (ii) peptides (vasopressin, somatostatin, neurotensin, etc.), And (iii) monoamines (norepinephrine, dopamine and serotonin) plus acetylcholine. Cortisone is a glucocorticoid steroid hormone, produced by the adrenal glands and has anti-inflammatory and immune-system suppressing properties.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Cortisone','Acetylcholine','Epinephrine','Norepinephrine','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211413,'biology','Parkinson’s disease (characterized by tremors and progressive rigidity of limbs) is caused by degeneration of brain neurons that are involved in movement control and make use of neurotransmitter [AIPMT 2005]','Dopamine','Parkinson\'s disease is caused by degeneration of neurons in Substantia Nigra tract which are essentially dopaminergic.\nDopamine is required by the striatum to controls muscle tones and coordinates it movements.\nTherefore, when dopamine levels are too low, people find it harder to get things done, to control their movements.\nAs dopamine levels progressively drop in patients with the disease, their symptoms gradually become more severe.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Acetylcholine','Norepinephrine','Dopamine','GABA.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211414,'biology','In the resting state of the neural membrane, diffusion due to concentration gradients, if allowed, would drive [AIPMT 2004]','Na+ into the cell','In the resting state of neurons, if diffusion is allowed due to concentration gradient of ions, it would lead to movement of Na+ ions into the cell. This is because at resting state the concentration of sodium ions is lesser inside the nerve cell as compared to the outside of the cell. Similarly, the concentration of potassium ions is more on the inside of the cell than the outside. Since diffusion is movement of ions from a region of higher concentration to the region of lower contraction, Na+ will move into the cell while K+ will move out of the cell.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','K+ into the cell','K+ and Na+ out of the cell','Na+ into the cell','Na+ out of the cell','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211415,'biology','What used to be described as nissl’s granules in a nerve cell are now identified as [AIPMT 2003]','Ribosomes','Cell body of a nerve cell contains basophilic granules called nissl’s granules. These granules appear to be cisternae of rough endoplasmic reticulum with numerous attached and free ribosomes. They probably synthesise proteins for the cell.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Cell metabolites','Fat granules','Ribosomes','Mitochondria','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(350208,'biology','Ecologists assume that over a period of time, the organism had evolved adaptations to optimise its survival and reproduction in its habitat through ________','Natural selection','Ecologistics assume that over a period of time, the organism had evolved adaptations to optimise its survival and reproduction in its habitat through natural selection.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:221, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Organism and its Environment',NULL,'2','mcq','Predation','Migration','Natural selection','Regulation','NEW','12-221',NULL,NULL),
(211416,'biology','Which of the following statement is correct for node of ranvier of nerve? [AIPMT 2002]','Myelin sheath is discontinuous','At the level of node of ranvier the myelin sheath is discontinuous but not the neurilemma lining. Actually myelin sheath is an integral part of schwann cell – which form a continuous neurilemmal covering. Each schwann cell wrap-around the neurite to form concentric layers of plasma membrane. But at the level of junction between two schwann cells myelin cannot be formed and thus a gap appears..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Neurilemma is discontinuous','Myelin sheath is discontinuous','Both neurilemma and myelin sheath are discontinuous','Covered by myelin sheath','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211417,'biology','Depolarization of axolemma during nerve conduction takes place because of \n[AIPMT 2000]','Na+ move inside','Depolarization of a nerve cell membrane occurs during the passage of an action potential along the axon where the nerve is transmitting an impulse. During depolarization, the Voltage-gated Na+ channels open, and Na+ rush into the axon. Entry of sodium ions leads to depolarization (reversal of polarity) of the nerve membrane. This membrane depolarization causes the opening of voltage-gated K+ channels, allowing K+ ions to exit and repolarization of the membrane.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Equal amount of Na+ and K+ move out across axolemma','Na+ move inside','Na+ move outside','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211418,'biology','The junction between the axon of one neuron and the dendrite of the next is called [AIPMT 1999]','A synapse','Synapse is the close proximity of the axon of one neuron and the dendrite or cyton of another neuron with a gap of just about 200 å in between. A nerve impulse is transmitted across the synapse by the release from the presynaptic membrane of neurotransmitter, which diffuses across the synaptic cleft to the postsynaptic membrane. This triggers the propagation of the impulse from the dendrite along the length of the postsynaptic neuron..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 319;paragraph no.1).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Constant bridge','Junction point','A joint','A synapse','NEW','11-319','11-234',NULL),
(211419,'biology','Which of the following is regarded as a unit of nervous tissue? [AIPMT 1999]','Neurons','Neurons or nerve cells are the structural and functional unit of nervous system. These have a special structure but vary greatly in size and shape. Each neuron has a cell body which encloses cytoplasm and has a nucleus. A number of processes arise from the cell body. There is usually a single axon and a variable number of dendrites. The medullated nerve fibres is composed of a shining, white, fatty substance called myelin.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Neurons','Myelin sheath','Axons','Dendrites','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211420,'biology','A saggital section of human brain is shown here Identify at least twomlabels from A-D (2115) (NEET 2013)','A-cerebrum, C-Pons','Reference Fig. 21.4 NCERT class XI page 320','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 1','Neural Control and Coordination','2','mcq','C-Midbrain, D- Cerebellum','A-cerebrum, C-Pons','C-corpus callosum, D-Medulla','A-cerebral hemispheres, B-Cerebellum','NEW','11-320','11-235','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/211420q.png'),
(211501,'chemistry','Phosphorus forms two types of halides, PX₃ (X = F, Cl, Br, I) and _________.','PX₅','Phosphorus forms two types of halides, PX₃ (X = F, Cl, Br, I) and PX₅ (X = F, Cl, Br).\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 182)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus halides-Phosphorus trichloride',NULL,'2','mcq','PX₂','PX₃','PX₅','PX','NEW','12-182',NULL,NULL),
(211502,'chemistry','Phosphorus halide is obtained by passing dry chlorine over heated _________ phosphorus.','white','Phosphorus halide is obtained by passing dry chlorine over heated white phosphorus.\nP₄ + 6Cl₂ → 4PCl₃\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 182)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus halides-Phosphorus trichloride',NULL,'1','mcq','black','white','red','colourless','NEW','12-182',NULL,NULL),
(211503,'chemistry','Phosphorus trichloride is a _________ oily liquid.','colourless','Phosphorus halide is a colourless oily liquid and hydrolyses in the presence of moisture.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 182)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus halides-Phosphorus trichloride',NULL,'1','mcq','pale yellow','white','yellow','colourless','NEW','12-182',NULL,NULL),
(211504,'chemistry','Phosphorus halide reacts with organic compounds containing which groups?','-OH','Phosphorus halide reacts with organic compounds containing -OH group.\n3CH₃COOH + PCl₃ → 3CH₃COCl + H₃PO₃\n3C₂H₅OH + PCl₃ → 3C₂H₅Cl + H₃PO₃\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 182)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus halides-Phosphorus trichloride',NULL,'2','mcq','-OH','-COOH','-OCH₃','-OOCH₃','NEW','12-182',NULL,NULL),
(211505,'chemistry','What is the shape of Phosphorous halide?','Pyramidal','The shape of phosphorous halide is Pyramidal.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 182)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus halides-Phosphorus trichloride',NULL,'1','mcq','Pyramidal','Triangular','Hexagonal','Octagonal','NEW','12-182',NULL,NULL),
(211506,'chemistry','What is the hybridization of Phosphorous halide?','sp³','The hybridisation of Phosphorous halide is sp³\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 182)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus halides-Phosphorus trichloride',NULL,'2','mcq','sp²','sp','sp³','sp³d','NEW','12-182',NULL,NULL),
(211507,'biology','The nissl’s granules of nerves cell are made up of [AIPMT 1997]','Ribosome','Cell body of a nerve cell contains basophilic granules called nissl’s granules. These granules appear to be cisternae of rough endoplasmic reticulum with numerous attached and free ribosomes. They probably synthesise proteins for the cell.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Dna','Rna','Ribosome','Protein.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211508,'biology','Which part of the brain is responsible for thermoregulation? [NEET 2019]','Hypothalamus','Hypothalamus is thermoregulatory centre. Hence it is called “thermostat” of the body. It keeps body temperature at roughly 37°c by means of a complex thermostat system..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 321;paragraph no.1).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Medulla oblongata','Cerebrum','Hypothalamus','Corpus callosum','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(180808,'chemistry','The rate of the chemical reaction doubles for an increase of 10K absolute temperature from 298K. Calculate Ea.','52.9kJmol⁻¹','It is given that T₁ = 298K                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    so, T₂ = (298 + 10)K = 308K.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               We also know that the rate of reaction doubles when the temperature is increased by 10 K.                                                                                                                                                                \nTherefore, let us take the value of k₁ = k and that of k₂ = 2k.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Also, R = 8.314 J K ⁻¹ mol⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            logk₂/k₁ = Ea/2.303R(1/T₁ - 1/T₂)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         log2/1 = Ea/2.303R(1/298 - 1/308) \n​Ea = 52.9kJmol⁻¹.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Collision theory of chemical reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','42.9kJmol⁻¹','62.9kJmol⁻¹','72.9kJmol⁻¹','52.9kJmol⁻¹','NEW','12-117','12-084',NULL),
(211509,'biology','Which of the following structures or regions is incorrectly paired with its functions? [NEET 2018]','Limbic : consists of fibre tracts system that interconnect different regions of brain controls movement','Certain components of the cerebrum and diencephalon constitute the limbic system. It is sometimes called the emotional brain because it controls emotional behaviour expressed in the form of joy, sorrow, fear, fight, friendship, liking and disliking. It also controls food habits and sex behaviours necessary for survival of the individual.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Medulla : controls respiration oblongata and cardiovascular reflexes','Limbic : consists of fibre tracts system that interconnect different regions of brain controls movement','Hypothalamus : production of releasing hormones and regulation of temperature, hunger and thirst','Corpus callosum : band of fibers connecting left and right cerebral hemispheres','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211510,'biology','Which of the following regions of the brain is incorrectly paired with its function? [AIPMT 2015 Cancelled]','Cerebellum - language comprehension',': language comprehension is a function of cerebrum. Cerebellum coordinates and controls rapid muscular activities such as running, typing, etc. Although it does not initiate such voluntary movements, but it is an important centre for coordinating movements and for controlling posture and balance. Cerebellum’s function is almost exclusively motor; but it is also implicated in some forms of learning.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Corpus callosum - communication between the left and right cerebral cortices','Cerebrum - calculation and contemplation','Medulla oblongata - homeostatic control','Cerebellum - language comprehension','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211511,'biology','Injury localized to the hypothalamus would most likely disrupt [AIPMT 2014]','Regulation of body temperature','Hypothalamus lies at the base of the thalamus. It provides anatomical connection between the nervous and endocrine systems by its relationship to the pituitary gland. Hypothalamus is thermoregulatory centre. Hence, it is called “thermostat” of the body. It keeps body temperature at roughly 37°c by means of a complex thermostat system. Any localised injury to hypothalamus will, hence, disrupt regulation of body temperature..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 321;paragraph no.1).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Short - term memory','Coordination during locomotion','Executive functions, such as decision making','Regulation of body temperature','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211512,'biology','The human hind brain comprises three parts, one of which is [AIPMT 2012]','Cerebellum','Brain is the anterior most part of central nervous system. Human brain can be divided into three parts: forebrain, midbrain and hindbrain. Human hindbrain comprises pons, cerebellum and medulla (also called the medulla oblongata)..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 321;paragraph no.3).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Spinal cord','Corpus callosum','Cerebellum','Hypothalamus.','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211513,'biology','Select the incorrect statement regarding synapses: [NEET 2022]','Impulse transmission across a chemical synapse is always faster than that across an electrical synapse','Impulse transmission across an electrical synapse is always faster \nthan that across a chemical synapse.\nChemical synapses use chemicals for transmission which are known as neurotransmitters. The membranes of presynaptic and postsynaptic neurons are in close proximity in an electrical synapse.\nIn an electrical synapse, the transmission of the impulse occurs in the form of an electrical current from one neuron to the next neuron.\n(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.3).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','The membranes of presynaptic and postsynaptic neurons are in close proximity in an electrical \nsynapse.','Electrical current can flow directly from one neuron into the other across the electrical synapse.','Chemical synapses use neurotransmitters','Impulse transmission across a chemical synapse is always faster than that across an electrical synapse','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211514,'biology','Which of the following is not a connective tissue? [NEET 2022]','Neuroglia','Neuroglia are the supportive cells of nervous tissue. They make up more than half the volume of \nneural tissue. Neurons, the unit of neural system are excitable cells.\nCartilage and blood are specialised type of connective tissues.\nAdipose tissue is a type of loose connective tissue (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 105;paragraph no.3).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Blood','Adipose tissue','Cartilage','Neuroglia','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211515,'biology','Brainstem of human brain consists of : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(1) Mid-brain, Pons and Medulla Oblongata','Mid-brain, Pons and Medulla Oblongata','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) Mid-brain, Pons and Medulla Oblongata','(2) Forebrain, Cerebellum and Pons','(3) Thalamus, Hypothalamus and Corpora quadrigemina','(4) Amygdala, Hippocampus and Corpus Callosum','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211516,'biology','Given below are two statements : \n Statement I :-\n The nose contains mucus – coated receptors which \nare specialised for receiving the sense of smell and \nare called olfactory receptors. \nStatement II :-\n Wall of the eye ball has three layers. The external \nlayer is called choroid (dense connective tissue), middle layer is sclera (thin pigmented layer) and \ninternal layer is retina (ganglion cells, bipolar cells \nand photoreceptor cells). \n In the light of the above statements, choose the \ncorrect answer from the options given below:','(1) Statement I is true but statement II is false','Statement I: The nose contains mucus-coated receptors that are specialized for receiving the sense of smell, which are called olfactory receptors. This statement is true.\n\nStatement II: The statement about the layers of the eyeball is not mentioned in option 1, so we cannot determine its correctness based on the given options.\n\nTherefore, if option 1 is the correct answer, it means that statement I is true.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(1) Statement I is true but statement II is false','(2) Statement I is false but Statement II is true','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are true','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','NEW','11-323',NULL,NULL),
(211517,'biology','The parts of human brain that helps in regulation of sexual behaviour, expression of excitement, pleasure,rage, fear etc. are: [ NEET 2023 ]','(4) Limbic system and hypothalamus','The hypothalamus, a vital part of the brain, plays a central role in controlling various physiological processes, including sexual behavior and the expression of emotions. Therefore, the limbic system and hypothalamus are closely involved in the regulation of these aspects of human brain function.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(1) Corpora quadrigemina and hippocampus','(2) Brain stem and epithalamus','(3) Corpus callosum and thalamus','(4) Limbic system and hypothalamus','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211518,'biology','Match List I with List II with respect in human eye.\nList I - List II\nA. Fovea - I. Visible coloured portion of eye that regulates\ndiameter of pupil.\n\nB. Iris - II. External layer of eye formed of dense\nconnective tissue.\n\nC. Blind spot - III. Point of greatest visual acuity or resolution.\n\nD. Sclera - IV. Point where optic nerve leaves the eyeball and\nphotoreceptor cells are absent.\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(4) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(1) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','(2) A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II','(3) A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV','(4) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','NEW','11-324',NULL,NULL),
(211601,'chemistry','What is prepared by the reaction of white phosphorus with excess of dry chlorine?','Phosphorus pentachloride','Phosphorus pentachloride is prepared by the reaction of white phosphorus with excess of dry chlorine.\nP₄ + 10Cl₂ → 4PCl₅\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 183)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus halides-Phosphorus pentachloride',NULL,'2','mcq','Phosphorus trichloride','Phosphorus pentachloride','Phosphorus tetrachloride','Phosphorus chloride','NEW','12-183',NULL,NULL),
(211602,'chemistry','Phosphorus pentachloride can also be prepared by the action of _________ on phosphorus.','SO₂Cl₂','Phosphorus pentachloride can also be prepared by the action of _________ on phosphorus.\nP₄ + 10SO₂Cl₂ → 4PCl₅ + 10SO₂\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 183)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus halides-Phosphorus pentachloride',NULL,'3','mcq','SO₂Cl','SO₂Cl₂','SOCl₂','SOCl','NEW','12-183',NULL,NULL),
(211603,'chemistry','What is PCl₅?','A yellowish white powder','PCl₅ is a yellowish white powder.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 183)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus halides-Phosphorus pentachloride',NULL,'1','mcq','A yellowish white powder','A yellowish white oily compound','A transparent oiy compound','A colourless solid','NEW','12-183',NULL,NULL),
(211604,'chemistry','In moist air, what does pentachloride hydrolyses to ?','POCl₃','PCl₅ is a yellowish white powder and in moist air, it hydrolyses to POCl₃ and finally gets converted to phosphoric acid.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 183)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus halides-Phosphorus pentachloride',NULL,'3','mcq','POCl₂','PO₂Cl₂','POCl','POCl₃','NEW','12-183',NULL,NULL),
(211605,'chemistry','Which of the following is true.\n\na. PCl₅ is used in the synthesis of some organic compounds, e.g., C₂H₅Cl, CH₃COCl.\nb. In gaseous and liquid phases, PCl₅ has a trigonal bipyramidal structure.\nc. In PCl₅, the three equatorial P–Cl bonds are equivalent, while the two axial bonds are longer than equatorial bonds.\nd. PCl₃ hydrolyses in the presence of moisture giving fumes of Cl₂.','a, b, c','PCl₅  is used in the synthesis of some organic compounds, e.g.,C₂H₅Cl, CH₃COCl. In gaseous and liquid phases, PCl₅  has a trigonal bipyramidal structure. The three equatorial P–Cl bonds are equivalent, while the two axial bonds are longer than equatorial bonds.  This is due to the fact that the axial bond pairs suffer more repulsion as compared to equatorial bond pairs. PCl₃ hydrolyses in the presence of moisture giving fumes of HCl.\n(Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 183)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Phosphorus halides-Phosphorus pentachloride',NULL,'3','mcq','a, b, d','a, b, c','a, c, d','b, c, d','NEW','12-183',NULL,NULL),
(211606,'biology','The crystal of lead zirconate is a key component of [AIIMS  2003]','Sonography','Lead zirconate is a key component of sonography. These crystals are housed in a transducer which gets excited and starts vibrating when an electric potential is applied to it. These vibrations are the source of ultrasound.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Electroencephalography','Electrocardiography','Magnetoencephalography','Sonography','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211607,'biology','Excessive stimulation of vagus nerve in humans may lead to [AIIMS 2003]','Irregular contraction of diaphragm','Vagus nerve is a mixed nerve. It controls the visceral sensations and visceral movements, e.g. Respiratory movements.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Hoarse voice','Peptic ulcers','Efficient digestion of proteins','Irregular contraction of diaphragm','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211608,'biology','A person is wearing spectacles with concave lenses for correcting vision. While not using the glasses, the image of a distant object in his case will be formed [AIIMS  2003]','In front of the retina','Concave lenses correct the eye condition of nearsightedness i.e. Myopia by bringing the light rays to a focus on retina. In such cases light rays converge at a point in front of the retina','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','On the blind spot','Behind the retina','In front of the retina','On the yellow spot','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211609,'biology','Unidirectional transmission of a nerve impulse through nerve fibre is due to the fact that [AIIMS 2004]','Neurotransmitters are released by the axon endings and not by dendrites.','Transmission of nerve impulse is always from axon of one neuron to the dendrite of another neuron i.e. Unidirectional because neurotransmitters are produced by axons and not by dendrites..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 319;paragraph no.2).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Nerve fibre is insulated by a medullary sheath.','Sodium pump starts operating only at the cyton and then continues into the nerve fibre','Neurotransmitters are released by dendrites and not by axon endings.','Neurotransmitters are released by the axon endings and not by dendrites.','NEW','11-319','11-234',NULL),
(211610,'biology','A person, who shows unpredictable moods, outbursts of emotion, quarrelsome behaviour and conflicts with others, is suffering from [AIIMS 2006]','Borderline personality disorder (bpd)','Borderline personality disorder is an emotionally unstable personality disorder characterized by impulsivity, unpredictable moods, outburst of emotion, behavioural explosions, quarrelsome behaviour and conflicts with others','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Borderline personality disorder (bpd)','Mood disorder','Addictive disorder','Schizophrenia','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211611,'biology','Which of the following is an eye disease?','Glaucoma','Glaucoma is an eye disease characterized by increased ocular pressure within the eyeball. Glaucoma is a group of diseases of the optic nerve involving loss of retinal ganglion cells in a characteristic pattern of optic neuropathy. Untreated glaucoma leads to permanent damage of the optic nerve and resultant visual field loss that can progress to blindness.Measles is a highly infectious viral disease that usually spread by droplet infection. Bronchitis is the inflammation of the membrane lining of the bronchial tubes','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Hepatitis','Measles','Glaucoma','Bronchitis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(220549,'biology','Oxytocin and vasopressin are synthesized by ________ while stored and released by________.','Hypothalamus, pars nervosa','Oxytocin and vasopressin are synthesized by Hypothalamus while stored and released by pars nervosa','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> The Pituitary Gland',NULL,'2','mcq','Pars medialis, hypothalamus','Pars nervosa, Hypothalamus','Hypothalamus, pars nervosa','Hypothalamus, pars medialis','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(211612,'biology','Hearing impairment affects which part of brain? [AIIMS 2007]','Temporal lobe','Forebrain is the largest part of the brain consisting of two halves called cerebral hemispheres separated by longitudinal fissures. Each cerebral hemisphere is divided into four lobes-frontal lobe, parietal lobe, temporal lobe and occipital lobe. Temporal lobe has cells that bring to consciousness, the sensations of hearing and smell. The frontal lobe has centers that are concerned with voluntary movements and personality. The parietal lobe is concerned with general sensations like temperature, touch, pressure, pain, and proprioception. The occipital lobe has centers of visual sense.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Frontal lobe','Parietal lobe','Temporal lobe','Cerebellum','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211613,'biology','The black pigment in the eye which reduces the internal reflection is located in [AIIMS  2007]','Retina','The inner layer of the posterior two-thirds of the eyeball consists of a light sensitive layer, called retina that possesses two types of photoreceptors called the rods and the cone cells. Retina reduces the internal reflection so any damage to it leads to greater internal reflection of light often causing an increase in light sensitivity..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 324;paragraph no.2).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Retina','Iris','Cornea','Sclerotic','NEW','11-324',NULL,NULL),
(211614,'biology','Fenestra ovalis is the opening of [AIIMS  2012]','Tympanic cavity','Fenestra ovalis is membrane covered opening leading from tympanic cavity into the vestibule of the internal ear.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cranium','Tympanum','Tympanic cavity','Brain','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211615,'biology','Which of the following ions are required for nerve conduction ? [AIIMS  2016]','Na+ and K+','.(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.2).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ca++, Na+ and K+','Ca++ and mg++','Mg++ and K+','Na+ and K+','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211616,'biology','Multipolar nerve cells are present in [AIIMS 2012]','Brain','Multipolar nerve cells are present in the cerebral cortex (brain), unipolar neurons are present in embryological stages during neural tube formation, while bipolar neurons are present in the retina and cochlea, Dorsal root ganglion is a pseudounipolar neuron..(Reference  : NCERT biology class 11; page 317;paragraph no.1).','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cochlea','Dorsal root ganglia of spinal cord','Retina of eye','Brain','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211617,'biology','Neurons receive signals through their __________ and send signals to other neurons through their __________. [AIIMS 2013]','Cell bodies and dendrites ... Axons','Dendrites generally receive inputs and conduct signals toward the cell body, whereas axons conduct signals away from the cell body.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Dendrites ... Receptors','End feet ... Cell bodies and dendrites','Cell bodies and dendrites ... Axons','Transmitter vesicles ... Axons','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211618,'biology','Assertion : Transmission of nerve impulse across a synapse is accomplished by neurotransmitters. \n\nReason : Transmission across a synapse usually requires neurotransmitters because there is a small space, i.e., Synaptic cleft, that separates one neuron from another.\n[AIIMS   1999]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Transmission of nerve impulse access synapses is accomplishes by neurotransmitter because synapses comprises of a synaptic cleft between the end of one nerve fibres and the beginning of the next.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211619,'biology','Assertion : Tongue is a gustatory receptor. \n\nReason : Receptors for gustatory sensations are located in taste buds.\n[AIIMS 2000]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Gustatory Receptors are chemoreceptors, enclosed within taste buds.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211620,'biology','Assertion : Astigmatism is due to uneven curvature of lens. \n\nReason : It is treated with cylindrical lenses. \n[AIIMS  2007]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Astigmatism is a kind of defect of vision in which the image of an object is distorted. It is because all the light rays do not come to focus on retina. It is due to abnormal curvature of the lens . It can be corrected by wearing cylindrical lenses','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211621,'biology','Assertion : The brainstem contains centres for controlling activities. \n\nReason : Brainstem is very sensitive.\n[AIIMS  2012]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The brainstem consists of pons varoli, medulla oblongata, midbrain and diencephalon. The brainstem is the connection between brain and spinal cord. It contains centres for controlling many vital activities like respiration, body temperature, urge for eating and drinking etc. It also carries nerve tracts between the spinal cord and the higher brain structure.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(180804,'chemistry','Assertion: Formation of methanol from bromoethane depends upon the orientation of reactant molecules.                                                         Reason: The proper orientation of reactant molecules lead to bond formation.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','The proper orientation of reactant molecules lead to bond formation whereas improper orientation makes them simply bounce bak and no products are formed.(Reference NCERT Class 12; Page 117; Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Collision theory of chemical reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-117','12-083',NULL),
(211622,'biology','Assertion : The chemical stored in the synaptic vesicles are termed as neurotransmitters. \n\nReason : Synaptic vesicles release these chemicals in the synaptic cleft. \n[AIIMS 2013]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The axon terminal of the neuron contains many membrane bound vesicles called synaptic vesicles, in its cytoplasm. Within these vesicles, chemical substances such as adrenaline and acetylcholine remain stored. These chemicals are called neurotransmitters, because they help to transmit nerve impulses across the synapses. When a nerve impulse passes the axon terminal, its synaptic vesicles release their stored chemicals to the synaptic cleft.These diffuse through the cleft to reach the membrane of the next neuron, stimulating the latter. This causes the nerve impulse to be transmitted along the next neuron.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211623,'biology','Assertion: The imbalance in concentration of Na+ , K+ and proteins generates resting potential. \n\nReason: To maintain the unequal distribution of Na+ and K+ , the neurons use electrical energy. \n[AIIMS  2002, 2015]','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Resting potential is due to differential distribution of ions on two sides of cell membrane.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211624,'biology','Assertion : The axonal membrane of the neuron is more permeable to sodium ion (Na+ ) and nearly impermeable to potassium (K+ ). \n\nReason : In a resting state neuron does not conduct any impulse. \n[AIIMS  2016]','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','The axonal membrane of neuron is more permeable to potassium ions (K+ ) and nearly impermeable to sodium ions (Na+ ). In a resting state neuron does not conduct any impulse. In the resting state the period during which a neuron is not conducting the fluids outside the cell membrane carry a relatively high positive charge. The fluids inside the cell membrane. Carry a less positive, or relatively negative, charge.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211625,'biology','Assertion : The cerebellum is related with skillful voluntary movement and involuntary activity like body balance, equilibrium, etc. \n\nReason : It is part of hind brain and it is situated behind the pons.\n [AIIMS  2010, 2017]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Hindbrain consists of cerebellum located dorsally to medulla oblongata and pons varolii. It contains centres for maintenance of posture and equilibrium of the body and for the muscle tone. All activities of the cerebellum are involuntary but may involve learning in their early stages','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211626,'biology','A diagram showing axon terminal and synapse is given Identify correctly at least two of A-D (2112) (AIPMT 2013)','A- Receptor, C- synaptic vesicles','Figure 21.3 NCERT class 11 page no:319 diagram showing axon terminal and synapse','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >> Previous year AIIMS questions',NULL,'2','mcq','B-synaptic connection D-K+','A- Neurotransmitter, B-synaptic cleft D-K+','C- Neurotransmitters, C-Ca++','A- Receptor, C- synaptic vesicles','NEW','11-319','11-234','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/211626q.png'),
(211701,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of phosphorus in Hypophosphorous H₃PO₂?','1','Let the oxidation state be x. \nOxidation state of Hydrogen is +1 and that of Oxygen is -2\nTherefore, for H₃PO₂ we have \n3(1) + x +2(-2) = 0\n3 + x - 4 = 0\nx-1 = 0\nx = 1\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'2','mcq','2','1','-1','-2','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(211702,'chemistry','In oxoacids phosphorus is __________ surrounded by other atoms.','tetrahedrally','In oxoacids phosphorus is tetrahedrally surrounded by other atoms.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'2','mcq','trigonally','tetrahedrally','octahedrally','linerally','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(211703,'chemistry','All these acids contain at least ______ P=O bond and _____ P–OH bond.','one, one','All these acids contain at least one P=O bond and one P–OH bond.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'3','mcq','two, one','two, two','one, two','one, one','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(211704,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of P in H₃PO₂?','1+','Let the oxidation state be x. \nOxidation state of Hydrogen is +1 and that of Oxygen is -2\nTherefore, for H₃PO₂ we have \n3(1) + x +2(-2) = 0\n3 + x - 4 = 0\nx-1 = 0\nx = 1\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'2','mcq','1+','2+','3+','4+','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(211705,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of P in H₃PO₃?','3+','Let the oxidation state be x. \nOxidation state of Hydrogen is +1 and that of Oxygen is -2\nTherefore, for H₃PO₃ we have \n3(1) + x +(-2) = 0\n3 + x - 6 = 0\nx-3 = 0\nx = 3\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'2','mcq','2+','1+','3+','4+','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(211706,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of P in H₃PO₄?','5+','Let the oxidation state be x. \nOxidation state of Hydrogen is +1 and that of Oxygen is -2\nTherefore, for H₃PO₄ we have \n3(1) + x + 4(-2) = 0\n3 + x - 8 = 0\nx - 5 = 0\nx = 5\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'2','mcq','1+','2+','5+','4+','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(211707,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of P in H₄P₂O₅?','3+','Let the oxidation state be x. \nOxidation state of Hydrogen is +1 and that of Oxygen is -2\nTherefore, for  H₄P₂O₅ we have \n4(1) + 2x +5(-2) = 0\n4 + 2x - 10 = 0\n2x - 6 = 0\nx = 3\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'2','mcq','1+','2+','3+','4+','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(211708,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of P in H₄P₂O₆?','4+','Let the oxidation state be x. \nOxidation state of Hydrogen is +1 and that of Oxygen is -2\nTherefore, for H₄P₂O₆ we have \n4(1) + 2x +6(-2) = 0\n4 + 2x - 12 = 0\n2x - 8 = 0\nx = 4\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'2','mcq','2+','1+','3+','4+','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(211709,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of P in H₄P₂O₇?','5+','Let the oxidation state be x. \nOxidation state of Hydrogen is +1 and that of Oxygen is -2\nTherefore, for H₄P₂O₇ we have \n4(1) + 2x + 7(-2) = 0\n4 + 2x - 14 = 0\n2x - 10 = 0\nx = 5\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'2','mcq','1+','2+','5+','4+','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(211710,'chemistry','What is the number of P-OH bonds in H₃PO₄?','3','There are 3 P-OH bonds in H₃PO₄.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 184 ,Table 7.5)\ndiagram(ne2104)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-184',NULL,NULL),
(211711,'chemistry','What is H₄P₂O₇ the formula of?','Pyrophosphoric acid','H₄P₂O₇ is the formula of Pyrophosphoric acid\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 185 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'2','mcq','Pyrophosphoric acid','Pyrophosphorous acid','Orthophosphorous acid','Orthophosphoric acid','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(211712,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct about acids which contain P–H bond?','They have strong reducing properties','The acids which contain P–H bond have strong reducing properties.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 185 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'1','mcq','They have strong oxidising properties','They have strong reducing properties','They have weak reducing properties','They have weak oxidising properties','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(211713,'chemistry','Which one is more reducing in nature?','H₃PO₂','More the P-H bonds, more will be the reducing ability. H₃PO₂ have two P-H bonds and H₃PO₃ has one.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 185 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'2','mcq','H₃PO₂','H₃PO₃','Both have equal reducing ability','Both are non reducing','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(211714,'chemistry','What is the basicity of H₃PO₄?','3','Basicity is related to number of replaceable hydrogens. More the P-OH bonds, more will be the basicity\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 185 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Oxo-acids of Phosphorus',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL),
(211715,'biology','Read the following statements about HUMAN EAR; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Ear helps in hearing and helps to maintain balance. \n\n2. Pinna is a part of middle ear. \n\n3. Tympanic membrane separates the middle ear and inner ear. \n\n4. Majority part of ear is located in the temporal bone.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Ear helps in hearing and maintaining balance. The pinna/auricle is a part of the outer ear, the outer ear is separated by the tympanic membrane from the middle ear. Temporal bone houses the hearing apparatus. (NCERT Class 11 pg 325 section 21.6.2 fig. 21.7)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect.','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(211716,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Middle ear has three ossicles called malleus, incus and stapes. \n\nR: The main function of the ossicles is to attenuate the sound wave reaching ear.','A is true but R is false','The three ossicles in middle ear is malleus, incus and stapes. The bony ossicles helps in increasing the efficiency of wave transmission to the inner ear, thereby amplifies the sound. (NCERT Class 11 pg 325 section 21.6.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(211717,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Eustachian tube connect the external ear to the pharynx. \n\nR: It helps to maintain a constant difference in pressure between middle ear and external ear.','Both A and R are false.','Eustachian tube connects between the anterior wall of middle ear and the pharynx. Its main function is equalising pressure on either side of tympanic membrane. (NCERT Class 11 pg 325 section 21.6.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(211718,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Inner ear contains both bony labyrinth and membranous labyrinth. \n\nR: The membranous labyrinth is filled with perilymph that helps in perception of sound.','A is true but R is false','Inner ear has bony and membranous labyrinth which produces, utricle, saccule, semi-circular canals and cochlea. The membranous labyrinth is filled with endolymph and surrounded by perilymph. (NCERT Class 11 pg 325 section 21.6.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(211719,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The cochlea is divided by 3 membranes into 2 chambers. \n\nR: The membranes are Ranvier\'s membrane, Basiliar membrane and Tectorial membrane','Both A and R are false.','Cochlea is divided by 2 membranes into 3 chambers namely scala vestibuli, scala media and scala tympani. The mebranes are Reissner\'s membrane and Basiliar membrane. (NCERT Class 11 pg 326 fig. 21.8)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-326',NULL,NULL),
(211720,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Hair cells of organ of corti on basilar membrane act as auditory receptors. \n\nR: Basiliar membrane separates scala media and scala tympani.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','The organ of corti is present on the basilar membrane. It contains hair cells which act as AUDITORY receptors. The basiliar mebrane separates the scala media and scala tympani. (NCERT Class 11 pg 326 paragraph 2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-326',NULL,NULL),
(211721,'biology','Read the following statements about INNER EAR; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Tectorial membrane is present over the hair in the nose. \n\n2. Organ of corti is present on the Reissner\'s membrane. \n\n3. Perilymph flows in scala media. \n\n4. Scala tympani ends at round window.','1, 2 and 3 are incorrect','Tectorial membrane is present over the hair cells of organ of corti in the inner ear. Organ of corti is located over the Basiliar membrane. Endolymph flows in scala media. (NCERT Class 11 pg 326 paragraph 2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 4 are correct','1, 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-326',NULL,NULL),
(211722,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The semi-circular canals are oriented at 60 degree to one another. \n\nR: Semi-circular canal is a part of cochlear apparatus responsible for maintainence of hearing.','Both A and R are false.','There are 3 semi-circular canals. The canals are located at 90 degrees to one another. These canals form a part of vestibular apparatus responsible for maintainence of balance. (NCERT Class 11 pg 326 paragraph 3)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-326',NULL,NULL),
(211723,'biology','Read the following statements about INNER EAR; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Base of semi-circular canals forms the ampulla. \n\n2. Organ of corti is located on Reissner\'s membrane. \n\n3. Endolymph flows in Scala media. \n\n4. The inner ear has membranous and bony labyrinth.','Only 2 is incorrect','Base of semi-circular canal forms a swollen structure called AMPULLA. Organ of corti is located on the basilar membrane. Endolymph flows in scala media. Inner ear has membranous and bony labyrinth. (NCERT Class 11 pg 326 paragraph 2,3; pg 327 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-327',NULL,NULL),
(211724,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Sound waves received by ear is analysed in the visual cortex of brain. \n\nR: The ear drum passes the sound wave from the external ear directly to the inner ear .','Both A and R are false.','Sound waves received by ear is analysed by the auditory cortex and then sound is recognised by humans. Ear drum passes the sound wave from external ear to the ossicles and then to the inner ear. (NCERT Class 11 pg 327 section 20.6.2.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-327',NULL,NULL),
(211725,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The bony ossicles are responsible for the efficiency of sound wave transmission. \n\nR: The vibrations are transmitted from the ossicles to the inner ear via the round window.','A is true but R is false','Bony ossicles in middle ear help in amplification of the sound wave and enhancing their transmission. The ossicles transmit the sound from middle ear to inner ear via the oval window. (NCERT Class 11 pg 325 section 21.6.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(211726,'biology','Read the following statements about BRAIN and SENSORY ORGANS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Medulla controls the gastric secretions. \n\n2. Eyes are situated in pyramid shaped orbits. \n\n3. Ears are located in the occipital bone. \n\n4. The hypothalamus regulates the body temperature.','Only 3 is incorrect','Medulla oblongata is responsible for controlling gastric secretions (NCERT Class 11 pg 321 section 21.4.3). Eyes are situated in the pyramidal orbits (NCERT Class 11 pg 323 section 21.6.1). Ears are located in the TEMPORAL BONE (NCERT Class 11 pg 325 fig 21.7). The hypothalamus regulates body temperature. (NCERT Class pg 321 section 21.4.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211727,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Synaptic cleft is present between the scala media and scala tympani. \n\nR: In a synaptic cleft ,chemical synapses are faster than electrical synapses.','Both A and R are false.','Synaptic cleft is a fluid filled space produced due to proximity between 2 neurons. Neurotransmitters are released into this space which help in transmitting the action potential across the synapse. Chemical synapses are slower than electrical synapses. (NCERT Class 11 pg 319 paragraph 2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-319','11-234',NULL),
(211728,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In Resting potential, the inner surface of axons are negatively charged. \n\nR: 3Na+ is transported out and 2K+ are transported in by Na-K ATPase pump and is responsible for maintaining negativity','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','In resting potential, 3Na+ is transported out and 2K+ are transported in by Na-K ATPase pump leading to net loss of cations. This causes negtive charge in the inner surface of axons of neurons (NCERT Class 11 pg 318 paragraph 1 )','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(211729,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Multipolar neurons are seen in cerebral cortex and retina. \n\nR: These neurons have multiple axons and dendrites.','Both A and R are false.','Multipolar NEURONS are seen in cerebral cortex. Bipolar NEURONS are seen in retina. They have multiple axons and dendrites. (NCERT Class 11 pg 317 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(180805,'chemistry','Rate = PZe⁻ᴱᵃ/ᴿᵀ . Here P stands for','Steric factor','(Reference NCERT Class 12; Page 117; Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Collision theory of chemical reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Collision frequency','Effective collisions','Steric factor','Activation energy','NEW','12-117','12-083',NULL),
(211730,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Autonomic nervous system transmit impulses from PNS to voluntary organs. \n\nR: Autonomic nervous system is a part of CNS.','Both A and R are false.','ANS transmits impulses via nerves from CNS to involuntary organs like heart, lungs and kidneys. ANS is a part of Peripheral Nervous System (PNS). (NCERT Class 11 pg 316 section 21.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-316','11-231',NULL),
(211731,'biology','Read the following statements about NERVES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. During depolarisation, the axon is permeable to K+ ions. \n\n2. Unipolar neurons are seen in embryonic stages. \n\n3. Gaps between myelin sheath is called Nodes of Bertini. \n\n4. Granular bodies in the cyton are called Nissl\'s granules.','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','During depolarisation, the axon becomes freely permeable to Na+ such that rapid influx of Na+ occurs into axons. Gaps between myelin sheath over axons is called NODES OF RANVIER. Granular bodies in the cyton are called Nissl\'s granules(NCERT Class 11 pg 317 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211732,'biology','Assertion: The retina contains two types of photoreceptors Rods and Cones. \n\nReason: These are functional for photopic and scotopic vision respectively.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The question implies that rods allow us to view photopic vision which is incorrect as the word respectively is given at the end of the reason, rods helps in dark(scotopic) and cones in light(photopic) vision.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-324',NULL,NULL),
(211733,'biology','Assertion: The human eye contains a blindspot. \n\nReason: This is due to the  presence of the optic nerve and vessels entering the eye.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Reason is incorrect because the optic nerve characteristically exits the eye, it does not enter the eye.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-324',NULL,NULL),
(211734,'biology','Assertion: The dissociation of retinal from opsin results in the change of the structure of the opsin pigment and vision is appreciated. \n\nReason: This causes a change in the permeability of the of the membrane and generation  of an impulse','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Light induces dissociation of the retinal from opsin resulting in changes in the structure of the opsin. This causes membrane permeability changes and generation of impulse','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-324',NULL,NULL),
(211735,'biology','Assertion: The external ear consists of the Pinna and the eustachian tube. \n\nReason: Both of these provide protection from mechanical damage to ear','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','The Eustachian tube is present in the middle ear and serves the function of equalizing air pressure in the middle ear. The external ear comprises of pinna and external auditory canal','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(211736,'biology','Assertion: The Eustachian tube equalizes the pressure in the middle ear. \n\nReason: The Eustachian tube connects the middle ear to the Larynx','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Eustachian tube connects the middle ear to the Pharynx','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-325',NULL,NULL),
(211737,'biology','Assertion: The Organ of Corti is a structure located on the outer membrane of the cochlea. \n\nReason: Because it contains static receptors','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Organ of corti is located on the basillar membrane in the inner ear and contains auditory receptors.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-326',NULL,NULL),
(211738,'biology','Assertion: The crista and macula are specifc receptors of the vestibular system. \n\nReason: They are responsible for the maintainance of balance and posture.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The saccule and utricle contain a projecting ridge called macula. The crista and macula are the specific receptors of the vestibular apparatus responsible for maintenance of balance of the body and posture.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-327',NULL,NULL),
(180809,'biology','Fish have ______circulation','Single circulation','In fishes the heart pumps out deoxygenated blood which is oxygenated by the gills and supplied to the body parts from where deoxygenated blood is returned to the heart (single circulation). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Circulatory pathways','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Single circulation','Double circulation','Incomplete double circulation','Open circulation','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(211739,'biology','Assertion: Nerve impulses in the ear are conducted to the temporal lobe via auditory pathway. \n\nReason: They directly transmit hearing impulses to the auditory cortex of the brain, located in the temporal lobe.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','These impulses are transmitted by the afferent fibres via auditory nerves to the auditory cortex of the brain, where the impulses are analysed and the sound is recognised','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-327',NULL,NULL),
(211740,'biology','Assertion: The Hypothalamus contains centers for thermoregulation. \n\nReason: Neurosecretory cells of hypothalamus secrete hypothalamic hormones.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Hormones are not responsible for the regulation of body temperature because it contains centre for temperature regulation that controls body temperature. Hypothalamic hormones signal pituitary gland to secrete hormones,but these do not regulate temperature regulation.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211741,'biology','Assertion: Myelinated nerves are faster at conducting impulses. \n\nReason: This is due to Saltatory conduction in the nodes of Ranvier.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Saltatory conduction means, a jumping impulse. This means that the impulse instead of travelling across the entire neuron would jump from one node of Ranvier to the adjacent along the direction of impulse . This allows for a faster conduction along the nodes of Ranvier in myelinated neurons.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211801,'chemistry','Group-16 elements are also known as ?','chalcogens','Oxygen, sulphur, selenium, tellurium, polonium and livermorium constitute Group 16 of the periodic table. This is sometimes known as group of chalcogens.\n(Reference (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 185 Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','halogens','chalcogens','coinage elements','inert elements','NEW','12-185',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(211802,'chemistry','What is the abundance of sulphur in earth\'s crust?','0.03-0.1%','The abundance of sulphur in the earth’s crust is only 0.03-0.1%.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 186 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','0.1-0.23%','0.01-0.05%','0.2-0.33%','0.03-0.1%','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(211803,'chemistry','Match the following','Gypsum-CaSO₄.2H₂O, Epsom-MgSO₄.7H₂O, Baryte-BaSO₄, Galena-PbS','The correct match is Gypsum-CaSO₄.2H₂O, Epsom-MgSO₄.7H₂O, Baryte-BaSO₄, Galena-PbS\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 186 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'3','match','Gypsum, CaSO₄.2H₂O','Epsom, MgSO₄.7H₂O','Baryte,BaSO₄','Galena,PbS','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(211804,'chemistry','Which one is not the source of S?','Paper','Combined sulphur exists primarily as sulphates such as gypsum CaSO₄.2H₂O, epsom salt MgSO₄.7H₂O, baryte BaSO₄ and sulphides such as galena PbS, zinc blende ZnS, copper pyrites CuFeS₂. \n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 186 Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'1','mcq','Paper','Zinc blende','Egg','Hair','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(211805,'chemistry','Match the following','Po-Decay product of Th and U, Lv-A synthetic radioactive material, Atomic number 116-Lv, Selenides-Ores of Se','The correct match is Po-Decay product of Th and U, Lv-A synthetic radioactive material, Atomic number 116-Lv, Selenides-Ores of Se\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 186 Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 16 Elements - Occurance',NULL,'3','match','Po,Decay product of Th and U','Lv,A synthetic radioactive material','Atomic number 116,Lv','Selenides,Ores of Se','NEW','12-186',NULL,NULL),
(211806,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: PNS comprises of all the nerves associated with the CNS (brain and spinal cord). \n\nR: Afferent neurons of CNS transmit regulatory impulses from CNS to the involuntary organs.','A is true but R is false','Efferent fibres of PNS transmit regulatory impulses from the CNS to the involuntary organs and the smooth muscles of the body. And the afferent fibres take up impulses from organs/tissues to the CNS. (NCERT Class 11 pg 316 section 21.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-316','11-231',NULL),
(211807,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: A neuron is the structural and functional unit of the human nervous system. \n\nR: The cell body of neurons contain granular structures called Nissl\'s granules.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A neuron is a structural and functional unit of nervous system and has mainly three major parts, namely, cell body, dendrites and axon.The cell body contains cytoplasm with typical cell organelles and certain granular bodies called Nissl’s granules. (NCERT Class 11 pg 316 section 21.3)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-316','11-231',NULL),
(211808,'biology','Read the following statements about NEURONS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Distal end of axons of neurons are un-branched. \n\n2. The terminal ends are bulbous and contain neurotransmitters. \n\n3. Multipolar neurons are seen in embryonic stages. \n\n4. Bipolar neurons are seen in cornea of the eye.','Only 2 is correct','The axon of a neuron is a long fibre, the distal end of which is branched. Multipolar neurons are seen in cerebral cortex. Bipolar neurons are seen in retina of eye. (NCERT Class 11 pg 317 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Only 2 is correct','Both 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211809,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: The branched end of the axons are known as Nodes of Ranvier. \n\nR: The branched ends contain synaptic vesicles containing Schwann Cells.','Both A and R are false.','The branched end of axons of neurons are called axon terminals containing bulbs known as SYNAPTIC KNOBS. They contains vesicles with neurotransmitters. (NCERT Class 11 pg 317 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211810,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In resting state, the axoplasm inside neuron has high concentration of Na+ ions. \n\nR:  In this stage, the axonal membrane is relatively permeable to Na+ than K+.','Both A and R are false.','In resting stage, the axoplasm inside neuron has high concentration of K+ and low concentration of Na+. In this state, the axonal membrane is relatively more permeable to K+ than to Na+. (NCERT Class 11 pg 317 section 21.3.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211811,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: There is an ionic gradient across the axonal membrane in resting state. \n\nR: The gradient is maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase pump that pumps out 3Na+ and pumps in 2K+.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','The ionic gradients across the resting membrane are maintained by the active transport of ions by the sodium-potassium pump which transports 3Na+ outwards for 2 K+ into the cell. (NCERT Class 11 pg 317 section 21.3.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211812,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: During depolarisation stage, the axon becomes freely permeable to Na+ such that their is free influx of K+ into the axon. \n\nR: During this stage, the inner surface of axon becomes negatively charged and outer surface positively charged.','Both A and R are false.','During depolarisation, the axon becomes freely permeable to Na+ such that rapid influx of Na+ occurs into axons such that the inner surface of axons becomes positive and outer surface of axon becomes negative. (NCERT Class 11 pg 318 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(211813,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: After deoplarisation stage, comes repolarisation stage. \n\nR: Hyperpolarisation is characterised by rapid influx of Na+ ions into the axons such that inner surface becomes negative and outer surface is positive.','A is true but R is false','Depolarisation is shortly followed by re-polarisation stage characterised by outflow of K+ ions outside the axonal membrane restoring the inner negative and out positive state of the axon. (NCERT Class 11 pg 318 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(211814,'biology','Read the following statements about TRANSMISSION OF IMPULSE; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Neuron becomes responsive to further stimulus only after resting membrane potential is achieved. \n\n2. Resting membrane potential is characterised by positive inner surface and negative outer surface of axon. \n\n3. During depolarisation, axon is freely permeable to K+. \n\n4. Depolarisation is followed by outflow of Na+ ions to restore resting potential.','2, 3 and 4 are incorrect','Resting membrane potential is characterised by inner surface negativity and outer surface positivity. During depolarisation, the axon is freely permeable to Na+ ions leading to its inflow. Depolarisation is followed by K+ ions outflow to restore resting membrane potential. (NCERT Class 11 pg 318 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 is correct','2, 3 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-318','11-233',NULL),
(211815,'biology','Read the following statements about SYNAPSE; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Synapse is formed by pre-synaptic neuron and post-synaptic neuron/muscle. \n\n2. The gap in a synapse is called synaptic rigor. \n\n3. Synapses are only of electrical type. \n\n4. Synapses help to conduct action potential from one neuron to another.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Synapse is formed by close proximity of presynaptic and postsynaptic neuron or muscle. The gap in a synapse is called synaptic cleft. Synapses can be either electrical or chemical in type. They help to transfer action potential from one neuron to another. (NCERT Class 11 pg 319 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-319','11-234',NULL),
(211816,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Electrical synapses are faster than chemical synapses in transmitting action potential. \n\nR: Electrical synapses are more commonly found in human body.','A is true but R is false','Electrical synapses are much faster than chemical impulses in transmitting impulses from one neuron to another, but electrical synapses are less seen in the human body. (NCERT Class 11 pg 319 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-319','11-234',NULL),
(211817,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Neurotransmitters are released in the synaptic cleft which help in transmitting impulse across synapse. \n\nR: Synaptic cleft is an air-filled narrow space between 2 neurons.','A is true but R is false','Synaptic cleft is a fluid filled space produced due to proximity between 2 neurons. Neurotransmitters are released into this space which help in transmitting the action potential across the synapse. (NCERT Class 11 pg 319 paragraph 2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-319','11-234',NULL),
(211818,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Axon terminals are bulb-like structure containing vesicles filled with neurotransmitters. \n\nR: When Action potential reaches axon terminal the synaptic vesicles release the neurotransmitters into the next neuron on their own.','A is true but R is false','When AP reaches the axon terminal, it stimulates the movement of the synaptic vesicles towards the membrane where they fuse with the plasma and release the neurotransmitters in the synaptic cleft. (NCERT Class 11 pg 319 paragraph 2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-319','11-234',NULL),
(211819,'biology','Read the following statements about NERVOUS SYSTEM; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Potential generated in the post-synaptic neuron is always excitatory. \n\n2. PNS comprises of the nerves connecting the CNS to various organs. \n\n3. Depolarisation of nerves refers to inflow of Na+ ions into axon. \n\n4. Axon terminals are cylinder shaped called synaptic knobs.','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect.','Post synaptic action potentials can be both excitatory or inhibitory depending on the type of neurotransmitter released in the synapse. Axon terminals are bulb shaped and known as synaptic knobs.(NCERT Class 11 pg 317 paragraph 1,319,320 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect.','NEW','11-319','11-234',NULL),
(211820,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Human brain is well protected inside the cranium. \n\nR: Brain is covered by 4 layers of meninges namely dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater and falx cerebri.','A is true but R is false','Human brain is well protected by the skull. Inside the skull, the brain is covered by cranial meninges consisting of an outer layer called dura mater, a very thin middle layer called arachnoid and an inner layer called pia mater. So, there are 3 layers of meninges. (NCERT Class 11 pg 320 section 21.4)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-320','11-235',NULL),
(211821,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Cerebrum is divided longitudinally into 2 cerebral hemispheres. \n\nR: The two cerebral hemispheres are connected by a tract of nerve fibres called corpus cavernosum.','A is true but R is false','Cerebrum is divided by a longitudinal cleft into two cerebral hemispheres which are connected by a tract of nerve fibres called corpus callosum. (NCERT Class 11 pg 320 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-320','11-235',NULL),
(211822,'biology','Read the following statements about HUMAN BRAIN; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Forebrain consists of cerebrum, thalamus, medulla oblongata and hypothalamus. \n\n2. Association areas of cerebrum perform functions like memory, communication. \n\n3. Hypothalamus lies superior to thalamus. \n\n4. Thalamus is also known as the temperature regulatory centre.','1, 3 and 4 are incorrect','Forebrain consists of cerebrum, thalamus and hypothalamus. Hypothalamus lies at the base of thalamus. Hypothalamus is known as the temperature regulatory centre of the body. Association areas of cerebrum help in complex functions like memory, speech, communication, etc. (NCERT Class 11 pg 321 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','1, 3 and 4 are incorrect','1, 2 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211823,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Hypothalamus controls our urge for eating and drinking. \n\nR: Hypothalamus also secretes neuro-secretory hormones that are stored in posterior pituitary and released in blood.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Hypothalamus performs various functions such as temperature regulation, urge for eating (appetite) and drinking (thirst). It even secretes hormones like ADH and oxytocin that are stored in posterior pituitary and released into blood. (NCERT Class 11 pg 321 section 21.4.1)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211824,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Aqueduct of Sylvius passes through the midbrain. \n\nR: The round swellings on the dorsal midbrain is called corpora quadrigemina.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Cerebral aqueduct (Aqueduct of sylvius) is a canal that passes through the midbrain. There are 4 sweelings on dorsal part of midbrain called corpora quadrigemina. (NCERT Class 11 pg 321 section 21.4.2)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211825,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Medulla contains various centres of regulation controlling respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions. \n\nR: Pons is a part of midbrain that connects various structures of the brain.','A is true but R is false','The medulla of the brain is connected to the spinal cord. The medulla contains centres which control respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions. The pons is a part of hindbrain that connects various part of the brain. (NCERT Class 11 pg 321 section 21.4.3)','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-321','11-236',NULL),
(211826,'biology','What is the function of the myelin sheath in a neuron?','Transmission of nerve impulses','Main function of myelin sheath is Transmission of nerve impulses.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Initiation of nerve impulses','Transmission of nerve impulses','Protection of the neuron','Maintenance of cell shape','NEW','11-317','11-232',NULL),
(211827,'biology','Which part of the brain is involved in the formation of long-term memories?','Hippocampus','Hippocampus is involved in the formation of long-term memories.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Hippocampus','Amygdala','Thalamus','Hypothalamus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(211828,'biology','In the context of neural pathways, what is the function of the cerebellum?','Coordination of voluntary movements and balance','In the context of neural pathways, cerebellum carries out Coordination of voluntary movements and balance.','Human Physiology >> Neural Control and Coordination >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Control of basic survival functions','Integration of sensory information and initiation of motor responses','Coordination of voluntary movements and balance','Processing visual information','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(180101,'chemistry','Which branch of chemistry helps to understand how chemical reactions occur?','Chemical Kinetics','Chemical kinetics helps us to understand how chemical reactions occur(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 95 ; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Thermodynamics','Chemical Kinetics','Redox','Equilibriun','NEW','12-095','12-061',NULL),
