(180201,'chemistry','Precipitation of AgCl occurs','Very fast','Ionic reaction occur very fast . For example- precipitation of AgCl occurs instantaneously by mixing of aqueous solutions of silver nitrate and sodium chloride.On the other hand, some reactions are very slow, for example, rusting of iron in the presence of air and moisture(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 96 ; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','Very fast','Slow','Constant rate','Moderate','NEW','12-096','12-062',NULL),
(180202,'chemistry','Inversion of cane sugar and hydrolysis of strach occurs at','Moderate rate','Inversion of cane sugar and hydrolysis of starch occurs at moderate speed(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 96 ; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','Constant rate','Moderate rate','Slow rate','very fast rate','NEW','12-096','12-062',NULL),
(180203,'chemistry','Rate of reaction can be defined as','All of the above','(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 96 ; Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'2','mcq','Change in concentration of reactant or product in unit time.','Rate of decrease in concentration of any one of the reactants','Rate of increase in concentration of any one of the products','All of the above','NEW','12-096','12-062',NULL),
(180204,'chemistry','At constant volume, a hypothetical reaction R→P. Which of the following is correct?','Both a and b','One mole of reactant R produces one mole of the product P. At t1, concentration of R = R1\n  and, concentration of P = P1\nAt t2, concentration of R = R2\n  and, concentration of P = P2\nso, ∆t =t2-t1\n     ∆[R] =[R2]-[R1] and ∆[P] =[ P2]-[P1]. So rate of disappearance= -∆[R]/∆t and rate of appearance= ∆[P]/∆t.(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 96 ; Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'2','mcq','Rate of disappearance of R = -∆[R]/∆t','Rate of appearance of P=∆[P]/∆t.','Both a and b','Rate of appearance of P=-∆[P]/∆t.','NEW','12-096','12-062',NULL),
(180205,'chemistry','Average rate depends upon','All of the above','Average rate depends upon the change in concentration of reactants or products and the time taken for that change to occur.(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 97 ; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'2','mcq','Change in concentration of reactants','Change in concentration of products','Time taken for change to occur','All of the above','NEW','12-097','12-063',NULL),
(180206,'chemistry','Unit of rate of a reaction','All of the above','Unit of rate is concentration/time\nIf concentration is in mol L⁻¹ then unit is mol L⁻¹s⁻¹. In case of gaseous reaction, concentration of gases is expressed in terms of their partial pressures then unit = atm s⁻¹(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 97 ; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','Concentration time⁻¹','mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','atm s⁻¹','All of the above','NEW','12-097','12-063',NULL),
(180207,'chemistry','Consider a reaction ,Hg(l) + Cl₂(g)→HgCl₂(s). Write rate of given reaction.','Rate of reaction = -∆[Hg]/∆t = -∆[Cl₂]/∆t = ∆[HgCl₂]/∆t','When stoichiometric coefficients of reactants and products are same then rate of reaction is given as-\nRate of reaction = -∆[Hg]/∆t= -∆[Cl₂]/∆t= ∆[HgCl₂]/∆t.(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 99 ; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'2','mcq','Rate of reaction = -∆[Hg]/∆t = -1∆[Cl₂]/2∆t = 1∆[HgCl₂]/2∆t.','Rate of reaction = -∆[Hg]/∆t = ∆[Cl₂]/∆t = ∆[HgCl₂]/∆t','Rate of reaction = -∆[Hg]/∆t = -1∆[Cl₂]/2∆t = 1∆[HgCl₂]/2∆t','Rate of reaction = -∆[Hg]/∆t = -∆[Cl₂]/∆t = ∆[HgCl₂]/∆t','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(180208,'chemistry','Consider a reaction, 2HI(g)→H₂(g) + I₂(g). Write rate of given reaction.','Rate of reaction = -1∆[HI]/2∆t = ∆[H₂]/∆t = ∆[I₂]/∆t.','Rate of consumption of HI is twice the rate of the formation of H₂ or I₂, to make them equal, the term ∆[HI] is divided by 2.(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 99 ; Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'2','mcq','Rate of reaction = -∆[HI]/∆t = ∆[H₂]/∆t = ∆[I₂]/∆t.','Rate of reaction = -1∆[HI]/2∆t = ∆[H₂]/∆t = ∆[I₂]/∆t.','Rate of reaction = -1∆[HI]/2∆t = 1∆[H₂]/2∆t = ∆[I₂]/∆t.','Rate of reaction = 1∆[HI]/2∆t = 1∆[H₂]/2∆t = ∆[I₂]/∆t.','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(180209,'chemistry','For a gaseous reaction at constant temperature, relation between concentration and the partial pressure of species','Directly proportional','For a gaseous reaction, rate can be expressed as a rate of change in partial pressure of reactant or product.(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 99 ; Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','Inversely proportional','Directly proportional','Partial pressure doesn\'t related with concentration','Partial pressure remains constant','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(180210,'chemistry','Rate constant in case of first order reaction is','Independent of concentration units','The rate constant of a first order reaction has just a time unit.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','Inversely proportional to the concentration units','Independent of concentration units','Directly proportional to concentration units','Inversely proportional to the square of concentration units','NEW','12-100','12-066',NULL),
(180211,'chemistry','From the concentrations of C₄H₉Cl (butyl chloride) at different times given below, calculate the average rate of the reaction:\nC₄H₉Cl + H2O → C₄H₉OH + HCl\nduring different intervals of time diagram(1818)','1.90 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹ to 0.4 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','(Reference:  NCERT 12th chemistry ; Page 97 ; Example 4.1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Rate of reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','1.90 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹ to 0.4 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','1.90 × 10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹ to 0.4 × 10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','1.90 × 10⁻⁶ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹ to 0.4 × 10⁻⁶ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','1.90 × 10⁻⁷ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹ to 0.4 × 10⁻⁷ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','NEW','12-097','12-063','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/180211q.png'),
