(180324,'biology','__________ are associated with allergic reactions.','Eosinophils','Eosinophils (2-3 %) resist infections and are also associated with allergic reactions. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Neutrophils','Eosinophils','Lymphocytes','Basophils','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(180325,'biology','_________ form 20-25% of WBCs.','Lymphocytes','Lymphocytes (20-25 %) are of two major types – ‘B’ and ‘T’ forms. Both B and T lymphocytes are responsible for immune responses of the body. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Neutrophils','Eosinophils','Lymphocytes','Basophils','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180326,'biology','Platelets are also called______','Thrombocytes','Platelets also called thrombocytes, are cell fragments produced from megakaryocytes (precursor cells-special cells in the bone marrow). They are called so because their major role is to form thrombus or a clot.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Thrombocytes','Megakaryocytes','Erythrocytes','Lymphocytes','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180327,'biology','Platelets are produced from________','Megakaryocytes','Platelets also called thrombocytes, are cell fragments produced from megakaryocytes (special cells in the bone marrow). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Megakaryocytes','Erythrocytes','Lymphocytes','Blast cell','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180328,'biology','What is the average number of platelets per mm⁻ ³ of blood in an adult man ?','1,500,00-3,500,00','Platelets also called thrombocytes, are cell fragments produced from megakaryocytes (special cells in the bone marrow). Blood normally contains 1,500,00-3,500,00 platelets ᵐᵐ³.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','4,500,00-8,500,00','500,00-1,500,00','1,500,00-3,500,00','2,500,00-4,500,00','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180329,'biology','Reduction in platelets leads to-\n(i)Anemia \n(ii)Clotting disorders\n(iii)Bleeding disorders\n(iv)Leukemia','ii and iii','Platelets can release a variety of substances most of which are involved in the coagulation or clotting of blood. A reduction in their number can lead to bleeding and clotting disorders which will lead to excessive loss of blood from the body. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','i and iii','i, ii and iv','ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(220550,'biology','Which part of the adrenal gland secretes glucocorticoids?','Adrenal cortex','Adrenal cortex secretes glucocorticoids.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> The Pituitary Gland',NULL,'1','mcq','Adrenal cortex','Adrenal medulla','Both','None','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(180330,'biology','What percentage of blood is constituted by the formed elements?','45','Erythrocytes, leukocytes and platelets are collectively called formed elements and they constitute nearly 45 percent of the blood. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 2','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','30','40','45','35','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(180331,'biology','Why are RBCs red in colour?','Hemoglobin protein is made up subunits called hemes','RBCs are formed in the red bone marrow in adults. RBCs are devoid of nucleus in most mammals and are biconcave in shape. They have a red coloured, iron containing complex protein called haemoglobin, hence the colour and name of these cells. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','They have copper containing complex','Hemoglobin protein is made up subunits called hemes','They have magnesium containing complex','They have zinc containing complex','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(180332,'biology','Which cells among the following have the shortest life span?','Leukocytes','Leukocytes are generally short lived. Granulocytes like neutrophils, basophils and eosinophils with life spans from 6 hours to few days, agranulocytes like lymphocytes and monocytes with months to years. RBCs have life span of around 120 days and platelets with 5-10 days.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Platelets','Mast cells','Erythrocytes','Leukocytes','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(180333,'biology','Match the differential percentage range of each type of leukocyte.','Neutrophils-60-65%,  Monocytes 6-8%,  Eosinophils-2-3%,  Lymphocytes-20-25%','Differential count is the individual percentage of each leukocyte out of the total WBCs. Neutrophils are the most abundant cells (60-65 %) of the total WBCs and basophils are the least (0.5-1 percent) among them. Neutrophils and monocytes (6-8 %) are phagocytic cells which destroy foreign organisms entering the body. Eosinophils (2-3 %) resist infections and are also associated with allergic reactions. Lymphocytes (20-25 %) are of two major types – ‘B’ and ‘T’ forms (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','3','match','Neutrophils,60-65%','Monocytes,6-8%','Eosinophils,2-3%','Lymphocytes,20-25%','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(180334,'biology','Assertion: Excessive blood loss is seen when there is reduction of platelets.\n\nReason: Platelets release substances involved in coagulation and help arrest bleeding','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Platelets can release a variety of substances most of which are involved in the coagulation or clotting of blood. A reduction in their number can lead to clotting disorders which will lead to excessive loss of blood from the body. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180335,'biology','Main function of the Red blood cells are','Transport of respiratory gases','These molecules play a significant role in transport of\nrespiratory gases. RBCs mainly transport oxygen and carbon dioxide (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','1','mcq','Transport of Nutrients','Transport of Ions','Transport of respiratory gases','All of the above','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(180336,'biology','How many types of Lymphocytes are present in blood?','2','Lymphocytes (20-25 per cent) are of\ntwo major types – ‘B’ and ‘T’ forms. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','1','mcq','2','3','5','1','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(180337,'biology','Match the following','RBC-Erythrocyte,WBC-Leucocyte,Platelet-Thrombocyte,Lymphocytes-immune response','RBC-Erythrocyte,WBC-Leucocyte,Platelet-Thrombocyte,Lymphocytes-immune response(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279-280; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Formed elements','Body fluids and circulation','2','match','RBC,Erythrocyte','WBC,Leucocyte','Platelet,Thrombocyte','Lymphocytes,immune response','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(180401,'chemistry','In the rate equation. Rate = k [A]ˣ [B]ʸ. In this equation, x and y indicates','Both b and c','Exponents of the concentration terms are not the same as their stoichiometric coefficients.(Reference:  NCERT 12th; Page 102 ; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Order of a reaction',NULL,'2','mcq','Stoichiometric coefficients of balanced chemical equation.','How sensitive the rate is to the change in concentration of A and B.','Sum of x and y gives order of that chemical reaction.','Both b and c','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(180402,'chemistry','Order of a chemical reaction is','Power of concentration of reactants','The sum of powers of the concentration of the reactants in the rate law expression is called the order of that chemical reaction.(Reference:  NCERT 12th; Page 102 ; Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Order of a reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','Concentration of reactants','Rate of the reaction','Power of concentration of reactants','Concentration of products','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(180403,'chemistry','Assertion: For a zero order reaction, the rate of reaction is independent of concentration of reactants.                                                                                                  Reason:  Order of a reaction can be 0,1,2,3 and even a fraction.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 102 ; Paragraph 6)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Order of a reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-102','12-071',NULL),
(180404,'chemistry','The reactions taking place in single step are called as','Elementary reaction','Single step reactions are known as elementary reactions.(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 102 ; Paragraph 7)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Order of a reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','Elementary reaction','Complex reaction','Disproportion reaction','Coupling reaction','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(180810,'biology','Which organ in fish oxygenates the deoxygenated blood?','Gills','In fishes the heart pumps out deoxygenated blood which is oxygenated by the gills and supplied to the body parts from where deoxygenated blood is returned to the heart (single circulation). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Circulatory pathways','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Fins','Mouth','Heart','Gills','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(180405,'chemistry','Match up.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Reaction.                                           Unit of rate constant.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        1.Zero order reaction.                     (a) mol⁻¹L s⁻¹.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         2.First order reaction.                     (b) mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             3.Second order reaction.                (c) s⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          4.Third order reaction                     (d) mol⁻²L²s⁻¹','1-(b), 2-(c), 3-(a), 4-(d)','(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 103 ; Table 4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Order of a reaction',NULL,'3','match','1,(b)','2,(c)','3,(a)','4,(d)','NEW','12-103','12-069',NULL),
(180406,'chemistry','Second order reaction rate constant-','k = 2.3 × 10 mol⁻¹ L s⁻¹','Solved by checking the unit of rate constant. Second order reaction unit = mol⁻¹ L s⁻¹(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 103 ;Example 4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Order of a reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','k=3×10⁻⁴ s⁻¹','k = 2.3 × 10 mol⁻¹ L s⁻¹','k=1.2×10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹','k=3.2×10⁻⁴ s','NEW','12-103','12-069',NULL),
(180407,'chemistry','The unit of rate and rate constant are same for a','Zero order reaction','For a zero order reaction, rate=k[A]⁰=k','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Order of a reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','Zero order reaction','First order reaction','Second order reaction','Third order reaction','NEW','12-105','12-071',NULL),
(180408,'chemistry','A sequence of elementary reactions gives us the products, the reaction are called','Complex reaction','When a sequence of elementary reactions gives us the products, the reaction are called complex reactions.(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 102 ; Paragraph 7)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Order of a reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','Elementary reaction','Complex reaction','Ionic reactions','Covalent reactions','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(180409,'chemistry','Calculate the overall order of a reaction which has the rate expression (a) Rate = k [A]¹/²[B]³/²','2','The sum of powers of the concentration of the reactants in the rate law expression is called the order of that chemical reaction.(Reference:  NCERT 12th; Page 102 ; Example 4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Order of a reaction',NULL,'2','mcq','1','0','2','3','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
