(180411,'chemistry','10.0 L flask contains 64 g of oxygen at 21°C. (Assume O2 gas is behaving ideally). The\nthe pressure inside the flask in the bar is (Given R = 0.0831 L bar K-1 mol-1)','4.82','Solution: We know for ideal gas\nPV = nRT\nP = 64/32 (0.0831x 291/ 10)\nP = 2 (0.0831x 291/ 10)\nP = 2( 2.41)\nP = 4.82\nPressure of O2 gas inside the flask = 4.82 bar','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Order of a reaction',NULL,'2','mcq','2.5','4.82','49.8','58.5','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(180412,'biology','Blood group AB individual can receive blood from- \n(i)Donor group A \n(ii)Donor group B \n(iii)Donor group O \n(iv)Donor group AB','i, ii, iii and iv','Blood group AB has no antibodies, so they can receive blood from any blood group.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','i and ii','i, ii and iii','i, ii and iv','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180413,'biology','Which blood group has no RBC antigens?','O','Blood group O has RBCs with neither A nor B antigen on them.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','O','A','B','AB','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180414,'biology','Blood group O individual has _________antibodies.\n(i)Ag  A  \n(ii)Ag B  \n(iii)Anti-B \n(iv)Anti-A','iii and iv','Blood group O has both antibodies A and B in their plasma.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','i and ii','iii and iv','i, ii and iv','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180415,'biology','Blood group O individual can receive blood from which of the following-','None of the above','Blood group O can receive blood from only blood group O because of no antigens, so no chances of clumping at all unlike A or B which contain respective antigens which react with anti- A and B antibodies present in blood group O. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Donor group B','Donor group A','Donor group AB','None of the above','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180416,'biology','Blood group O individual can never receive blood from which of the following-','All of them','Blood group O can receive blood from only blood group O because of no antigens, so no chances of clumping at all unlike A or B which contain respective antigens which react with anti- A and B antibodies present in blood group O.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Donor group A','Donor group B','Donor group AB','All of them','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180417,'biology','Which blood group makes an individual a universal donor?','O','Blood group O can theoretically donate blood to any blood group because of no antigens on the RBC surface, so chances of clumping is less compared to the presence of antigens in other blood groups. Although the blood group O has both anti-A and B antibodies, the clumping can be tolerated especially in emergency cases when only blood group O is present.  group ‘O’ blood can be donated to persons with any other blood group and hence ‘O’ group individuals are called ‘universal donors’.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1; Paragraph no.:5)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','A','B','AB','O','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180418,'biology','Which blood group makes an individual a universal recipient?','AB','Blood group AB contains both antigens A and B on their RBC surface and no antibodies in their plasma, so they can theoretically receive blood from any blood group, so the name universal recipient. Persons with ‘AB’ group can accept blood from persons with AB as well as the other groups of blood. Therefore, such persons are called ‘universal recipients’.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1; Paragraph no:5)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','A','B','AB','O','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180419,'biology','Assertion: Blood of human beings differ in certain aspects. \n\nReason: One such difference is measured qualititatively as blood grouping','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Blood of human beings differ in certain aspects though it appears to be similar. Various types of blood grouping have been done. Two such groupings – the ABO and Rh – are widely used all over the world. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180420,'biology','Blood group A individual has _____ antibodies in their plasma.','Anti-B','Blood group A contain anti-B antibodies in its plasma.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Anti-B','Anti-A','Antigen B','Antigen A','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180421,'biology','Blood group B individual has _____on the RBC surface.','Antigen B','Blood group B contains antigen B on their RBC surface and antibodies anti-A in their plasma. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Anti-B','Anti-A','Antigen B','Antigen A','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180422,'biology','Blood group A +ve individuals should never receive blood from _____among the following.','Donor group B -ve','The anti-A antibodies of donor blood group B will react with the antigen A on recipient RBCs destroying them and vice-versa. Thus causing grouping mismatch effects.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Donor group A +ve','Donor group A -ve','Donor group B -ve','Donor group O -ve','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180423,'biology','Blood group AB individuals should never receive blood from only_____among the following.','None of the above, they can recieve blood from all ABO groups','Blood group AB has no antibodies, so they can receive blood from any blood group.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Donor group A','Donor group B','Donor group AB','None of the above, they can recieve blood from all ABO groups','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180424,'biology','Fill in the blanks. According to ABO blood grouping, there are ____types of RBC antigens and ___types of blood groups, and ___ types of RBCs.','2,4,3','Two types of RBC antigens - Ag A and Ag B.   Four type of blood groups A,B, AB, O. Three types of RBCs with antigens A, B and one with no antigens (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> ABO grouping','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','2,3,4','4,3,2','2,4,3','3,4,2','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(180501,'chemistry','Assertion: The number of reacting species taking part in an elementary reaction is called molecularity of reaction.                                                    Reason: Reacting species must collide simultaneously in order to bring about chemical reaction.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Collision is important in order to bring about chemical reaction between reacting species.(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 103 ; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Molecularity of a reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-103','12-069',NULL),
(180502,'chemistry','Match up the following','Unimolecular-NH₄NO₂ → N₂ + 2H₂O,Biomolecular-2HI → H₂ + I ₂,Trimolecular-2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂','Unimolecular reaction involves one reacting species.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \nBiomolecular reaction involve simultaneously collision between two species.                                                                                                                                                                                         \nTrimolecular or termolecular reactions involve simultaneously collision between 3 reacting species(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 104 ; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Molecularity of a reaction',NULL,'3','match','Unimolecular,NH₄NO₂ → N₂ + 2H₂O','Biomolecular,2HI → H₂ + I ₂','Trimolecular,2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂',NULL,'NEW','12-104','12-070',NULL),
(180503,'chemistry','Assertion: The reaction with the molecularity three are very rare and slow to proceed.                                                                                 Reason: The probability of more than three molecules can collide and react simultaneously is very small.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Reaction with molecularity three are very rare because more than three molecules can\'t collide simultaneously.(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 104 ; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Molecularity of a reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-104','12-070',NULL),
(180504,'chemistry','The rate of the reaction is controlled by slowest step in a reaction is known as____.','Rate determining step','Rate of complex reaction is determined by the slowest step called rate determining step.(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 104 ; Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Molecularity of a reaction',NULL,'1','fillup','Ra,te de,ter,mi,ni,ng st,ep, fa,ste,st st,e,p',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-104','12-070',NULL),
(180811,'biology','In amphibians and reptiles, left atrium receives oxygenated blood from-','Gills,Lungs and skin','In amphibians and reptiles, the left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the gills/lungs/skin and the right atrium gets the deoxygenated blood from other body parts. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Circulatory pathways','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Gills,Lungs and skin','Lungs, Heart and Brain','Skin, Gut and Kidneys','Kidneys, Brain and Gills','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(180505,'chemistry','Choose the correct option:.                                                                                                                                                                    1. Order of a reaction is an experimental quantity.                                                                                                                                    2. Order can be zero or even a fraction.                                                                                                                                                   3. Molecularity can be zero.                                                                                                                                                                   4. Order is applicable to elementary as well as complex reactions.                                                                                                            5. Molecularity is applicable for both elementary and complex reactions.','1, 2, 4 are correct','(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 104 ; Paragraph 6)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Molecularity of a reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','All statements are correct.','1, 2, 4 are correct','1, 3, 4 are correct','2, 3, 5 are correct.','NEW','12-104','12-068',NULL),
(180506,'chemistry','For complex reactions, order is given by','Both b and c','Rate of complex reaction is determined by slowest step known as rate determining step.(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 105 ; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Molecularity of a reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','Fastest step','Slowest step','Rate determining step','Both b and c','NEW','12-105','12-070',NULL),
(180507,'chemistry','For a reaction.                                                                                                                                                                                                      A + B → Product.                                                                                                                                                                                 The rate law is given by ,  rate = k[A]¹/²[B]².                                                                                                                                             What is the order if the reaction.','(5/2)','Order of reaction = sum of the power of the concentration of reactants.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Molecularity of a reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','(2/5)','(3/5)','(5/2)','(4/5)','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(180508,'chemistry','The conversion of molecules X to Y follows second order kinetics. If concentration of X is increased to three times how will it affect the rate of formation of Y?','9 times','The given reaction is:-\nX⟶Y\n\nSince it follows second order kinetics so,\n(Rate)1=K[X]²  −(i)\nwhere K= rate constant\n            [X]= concentration of X\nIf the concentration of [X] is made to increase 3 times then rate will increase by 9 times as given by:-\n(Rate) 2=K[3X]²  =9K[X]² −(ii)\n(Rate)2 =9×(Rate)1\n​So, the rate of formation of Y will increase by 9 times','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Molecularity of a reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','3 times','9 times','6 times','4 times','NEW','12-106','12-071',NULL),
(180509,'chemistry','For a reaction x + y → z, rate ∝ |X|. What is (i) molecularity and (ii) order of reaction?','(i)2, (ii) 1','Molecularity is the number of species involved in chemical reaction while order is the sum of power of concentration of reactants in rate law','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Molecularity of a reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','(i)2, (ii) 1','(i)2,(ii) 2','(i) 1, (ii) 1','(i) 1, (ii) 2','NEW','12-104','12-069',NULL),
(180510,'chemistry','For a unimolecular reaction','The order and molecularity of the slowest step are equal to one','The order and molecularity of the slowest step are equal to one','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Molecularity of a reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','The order and molecularity of the slowest step are equal to one','Molecularity of the reaction can be zero, one or two','Molecularity of the reaction can be determined only experimentally','More than one reacting species are involved in one step','NEW','12-104','12-069',NULL),
(180601,'chemistry','Assertion: Instantaneous rate of reaction is equal to dx/dt.                                                                                                                                                                   Reason: It is the rate of reaction at any particular instant of time.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Instantaneous rate of a reaction is equal to the small change in concentration (dx) during a small interval of the time (dt) at that particular instant of time divided by time interval','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-098','12-063',NULL),
(180602,'chemistry','In zero order reaction the rate of reaction is proportional','Zero power of concentration of reactants.','Zero order reaction means that the rate of reaction is proportional to zero power of the concentration of reactants.(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 105 ; Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Integrated rate equations',NULL,'1','mcq','One power of concentration of reactants','Zero power of concentration of reactants.','Two power of concentration of reactants','1/2 power of concentration of reactants','NEW','12-105','12-071',NULL),
