(180711,'chemistry','By increasing the temperature or decreasing the activation energy, rate of reaction will','Increase','From Arrhenius equation, increasing the temperature or decreasing the activation energy will result in an increase in rate of the reaction and an exponential increase in the rate constant.(Reference NCERT Class 12; Page 114; Paragraph 4).','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Temperature dependence of the rate of a reaction',NULL,'1','mcq','Increase','Decrease','Remains constant','None of these','NEW','12-114','12-080',NULL),
(180712,'chemistry','The rate constants of a reaction at 500K and 700K are 0.02s⁻¹ and 0.07s⁻¹ respectively. Calculate the values of Ea.','18230.8J','log k₂/k₁ = Ea/2.303R[T₂ - T₁ /T₁T₂].                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      \nlog 0.07/0.02 = (Ea/2.303×8.314JK⁻¹mol⁻¹)[700-500/700×500].                                                                                                                                                                                                                                0.544 = Ea × 5.714 × 10/ 19.15.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Ea = 0.544 × 19.15/5.714 × 10⁻⁴ = 18230.8J (Reference NCERT Class 12; Page 115; Example 4.9)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Temperature dependence of the rate of a reaction',NULL,'2','mcq','19230.8J','18000.8J','18230.8J','17300.3J','NEW','12-115','12-081',NULL),
(180713,'chemistry','Assertion: A catalyst increases the rate of a reaction without itself undergoing any permanent chemical change.                                                    Reason: MnO₂ catalyses 2KClO₃ → 2KCl + 3O₂ and increase its rate considerably.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','A catalyst is a substance which increases the rate of a reaction without itself undergoing any permanent chemical change because catalyst reduces the activation energy between reactants and products.(Reference NCERT Class 12; Page 116; Paragraph 3).','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Temperature dependence of the rate of a reaction',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-116','12-082',NULL),
(180801,'chemistry','Assertion: Collision theory of chemical reactions is based on kinetic theory of gases.                                                                                  Reason: According to this theory, the reactant molecules are assumed to be hard spheres and reaction is postulated to occur when molecules collide with each other.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference NCERT Class 12; Page 116; Paragraph 5 ; Page 117; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Collision theory of chemical reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-116','12-082',NULL),
(180802,'chemistry','Assertion: According to the collision theory of chemical reaction rate is directly proportional to the number of collision per second.                   Reason: All collisions result in the formation of the products.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','The increase in collision frequency brings in an increase in effective collisions and thus rate of reaction increases. All collisions are not effective and does not result in the formation of the products.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Collision theory of chemical reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-117','12-082',NULL),
(180803,'chemistry','Assertion: All collisions do not lead to the formation of products.                                                                                                               Reason: The collisions in which molecules collide with sufficient kinetic energy and proper orientation, so as to facilitate breaking of bonds between reacting species and formation of new bonds to form products.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','(Reference NCERT Class 12; Page 117; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Collision theory of chemical reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-117','12-082',NULL),
(100306,'chemistry','What does hydrolysis of alkali metal superoxide give?','Metal ion, hydroxide ion, hydrogen peroxide and oxygen gas','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1 Reactions)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Metal ion, hydroxide ion and oxygen gas','Metal ion and hydroxide ion only','Metal ion, hydroxide ion and hydrogen peroxide','Metal ion, hydroxide ion, hydrogen peroxide and oxygen gas','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(180812,'biology','What type of circulation is seen in all reptiles except crocodiles?','Incomplete double circulation','In amphibians and reptiles, the left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the gills/lungs/skin and the right atrium gets the deoxygenated blood from other body parts. However, they get mixed up in the single ventricle which pumps out mixed blood (incomplete double circulation). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Circulatory pathways','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Single circulation','Double circulation','Incomplete double circulation','Partially single circulation','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(180813,'biology','In birds and mammals, right side of heart receives-','Deoxygenated blood','In birds and mammals, oxygenated and deoxygenated blood received by the left and right atria respectively passes on to the ventricles of the same sides. The ventricles pump it out without any mixing up, i.e., two separate circulatory pathways are present in these organisms, hence, these animals have double circulation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Circulatory pathways','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Deoxygenated blood','Oxygenated blood','Mixed blood','Partially oxygenated blood','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(180814,'biology','In birds and mammals, oxygenated blood passes from______ to _____','Left atrium, Left ventricle','In birds and mammals, oxygenated and deoxygenated blood received by the left and right atria respectively passes on to the ventricles of the same sides. The ventricles pump it out without any mixing up, i.e., two separate circulatory pathways are present in these organisms, hence, these animals have double circulation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Circulatory pathways','Body fluids and circulation','1','mcq','Right atrium, Right ventricle','Left atrium, Left ventricle','Right ventricle, Right atrium','Left ventricle, Left atrium','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(180815,'biology','How many types of circulatory systems are seen in animals?','2','The circulatory patterns are of two types – open or closed. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Circulatory pathways','Body fluids and circulation','1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(180816,'biology','Why is the closed circulatory system more advantageous than the open type?','Because the fluid flow is better regulated','Closed circulatory system in which the blood pumped by the heart is always circulated through a closed network of blood vessels. This pattern is considered to be more advantageous as the flow of fluid can be more precisely regulated.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Circulatory pathways','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Because the fluid flow is better regulated','Because the fluid flow is unidirectional','Because the fluid flow is always in the same speed','Because the fluid flow is always of the same force','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(180817,'biology','Assertion: Double circulation is called so because of 2 different compartments of circulation.\n\nReason: The 2 atria and 2 ventricles are seperated by an septum and make sure the oxygenated and deoxygenated are pumped without mixing up','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In birds and mammals, oxygenated and deoxygenated blood received by the left and right atria respectively passes on to the ventricles of the same sides. The ventricles pump it out without any mixing up, i.e., two separate circulatory pathways are present in these organisms, hence, these animals have double circulation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Circulatory pathways','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
