(180905,'chemistry','For the reaction:\n2A+B→A₂B, the rate =k[A][B]² with k=2.0×10⁻⁶mol⁻² L² s⁻¹. Calculate the initial rate of the reaction when [A]=0.1molL⁻¹ ,[B]=0.2 molL⁻¹ .Calculate the rate of reaction after [A] is reduced to 0.06 molL⁻¹','3.89×10⁻⁹molL⁻¹ s⁻¹','The rate law expression is as follows: 2A+B→A₂B                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      The rate constant k=2.0×10⁻⁶ mol⁻² L² s⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 When [A]=0.1molL⁻¹, [B]=0.2 molL⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             The initial rate of the reaction is,  Rate =2.0×10⁻⁶ × (0.1) × (0.2)² = 8.0×10⁻⁹ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹  (Reference NCERT Class 12; page 119; Exercise 4.2)                                                                                                                                                                                                      After [A] is reduced to 0.06 molL⁻⁻¹ , the rate of reaction is as follows:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Rate =2.0×10⁻⁶ × 0.06 × (0.18)² =3.89 × 10⁻⁹ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','3.89×10⁻⁹molL⁻¹ s⁻¹','6.89×10⁻⁹molL⁻¹ s⁻¹','5.89×10⁻⁹molL⁻¹ s⁻¹','4.89×10⁻⁹molL⁻¹ s⁻¹','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(100307,'chemistry','What is the color of oxides and peroxides when they are pure?','Colourless','The oxides and the peroxides are colourless when pure.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Colourless','Blue','Yellow','Orange','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(180906,'chemistry','The decomposition of NH₃ on platinum surface is zero order reaction. What are the rates of production of N₂ and H₂ if k = 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹?','2.5×10⁻⁴M/s and 7.5×10⁻⁴M/s','2NH₃ →N₂ + 3H₂                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Rate of the reaction = -1/2d[NH₃]/dt = d[N₂]/dt = 1/3d[H₂]/dt = k                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       For a zero order reaction, Rate =k=2.5×10 ⁻⁴M/s                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Rate of production of N₂ = d[N₂]/dt = 2.5×10 ⁻⁴M/s                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Rate of production of H₂ = 1/3d[H₂]/dt = 3 x 2.5×10 ⁻⁴M/s  = 7.5×10 ⁻⁴M/s. (Reference NCERT Class 12; page 119; Exercise 4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','2.5×10⁻⁴M/s and 2.5×10⁻⁴M/s','2.5×10⁻⁴M/s and 7.5×10⁻⁴M/s','7.5×10⁻⁴M/s and 7.5×10⁻⁴M/s','2.5×10⁻⁴M/s and 8.5×10⁻⁴M/s','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(180907,'chemistry','The decomposition of dimethyl ether leads to the formation of CH₄, H₂ and CO and the reaction rate is given by                                                                                                                                                          Rate = k [CH₃OCH₃]³/²                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 The rate of reaction is followed by increase in pressure in a closed vessel, so the rate can also be expressed in terms of the partial pressure\nof dimethyl ether, i.e.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Rate = k (pCH₃OCH₃)³/²                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           If the pressure is measured in bar and time in minutes, then what are the units of rate and rate constants?','Rate = k(bar)³/²  and Rate constant = bar⁻¹/²min⁻¹','Rate = bar/min =unit of rate = bar min⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Rate =  k [CH₃OCH₃]³/² = k(bar)³/²                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               k = bar min⁻¹/(bar)³/² = bar⁻¹/²min⁻¹(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 119; Exercise 4.4)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','Rate = (bar)³/²  and Rate constant = bar⁻¹/²min⁻¹','Rate = k(bar)³/²  and Rate constant = bar⁻¹/²min⁻¹','Rate = k(bar)³/²  and Rate constant = bar min⁻¹','Rate = k(bar)  and Rate constant = bar⁻¹/²min⁻¹','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/180907q.png'),
(180908,'chemistry','The factors that affect the rate of a chemical reaction','All of the above','The factors that affect reaction rates are:.\nConcentration or pressure of a reactant.\nTemperature.\nNature of the reactants.(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 119; Exercise 4.5)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Concentration or pressure of a reactant.','Temperature','Nature of the reactants','All of the above','NEW','12-097','12-063',NULL),
(180909,'chemistry','A reaction is second order with respect to a reactant. How is the rate of reaction affected if the concentration of the reactant is doubled','4 times','If the concentration of the reactant is doubled, the rate of reaction becomes 4 times.(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 119; Exercise 4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','2 times','3 times','4 times','6 times','NEW','12-100','12-066',NULL),
(180910,'chemistry','A reaction is second order with respect to a reactant. How is the rate of reaction affected if the concentration of the reactant is reduced to half','one fourth','If the concentration of the reactant is reduced to half, the rate of reaction becomes one fourth.(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 119; Exercise 4.6)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','one fourth','one third','half','one sixth','NEW','12-100','12-066',NULL),
(180911,'chemistry','What is the effect of temperature on the rate constant of a reaction?','Rate constant of a reaction increases with increase in temperature','With increase in temperature, the rate of the reaction and the rate constant increases.(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 119; Exercise 4.7)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Rate constant of a reaction increases with increase in temperature','Rate constant of a reaction decrease with increase in temperature','Does not change with temperature','Becomes four times for every ten degree rise in temperature.','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(180912,'chemistry','In a pseudo first order reaction in water, the following results were obtained:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Calculate the average rate of reaction between the time interval 30 to 60 seconds. Refer diagram(n1807)','4.67×10⁻³ M/s','The average rate of reaction between the time interval 30 to 60 is                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  0.31−0.17/60-30 = 4.67×10⁻³ M/s.(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 119; Exercise 4.8)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','5.67×10⁻³ M/s','3.67×10⁻³ M/s','4.67×10⁻³ M/s','4.67×10⁻⁴ M/s','NEW','12-112','12-078','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/180912q.png'),
(180913,'chemistry','A reaction is first order in A and second order in B. Write the differential rate equation.','Rate =k[A][B]²','The differential rate equation is as follows:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Rate =k[A][B]²(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 119; Exercise 4.9)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','Rate =k[A][B]','Rate =k[A][B]²','Rate =k[A]²','Rate =k[B]²','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(110708,'biology','Apoplastic movement is dependent on the gradient.','TRUE','The apoplastic movement of water occurs exclusively through the intercellular spaces and the walls of the cells. Movement through the apoplast does not involve crossing the cell membrane. This movement is dependent on the gradient.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 184','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(180914,'chemistry','A reaction is first order in A and second order in B. How is the rate affected on increasing the concentration of B three\ntimes?','9 times','The differential rate equation is as follows:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Rate =k[A][B]²                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              On increasing the concentration of B three times, rate becomes 9 times.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        (d[B])² = (3)² = 9(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 119; Exercise 4.9)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','9 times','8 times','6 times','3 times','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(180915,'chemistry','A reaction is first order in A and second order in B. How is the rate affected when the concentrations of both A and B\nare doubled?','8 times','The differential rate equation is as follows:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Rate =k[A][B]²                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              When the concentrations of both A and B are doubled, rate becomes 8 times.                                                                                                                                                                                                              d[A](d[B])² = 2x(2)² = 8 (Reference NCERT Class 12; page 119; Exercise 4.9)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','9 times','8 times','3 times','2 times','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(180916,'chemistry','In a reaction between A and B, the initial rate of reaction (r₀)  was measured for different initial concentrations of A and B as given below:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    What is the order of the reaction with respect to A and B? diagram(n1808)','1.5 and 0','Rate =k[A]ᵖ[B]ᵠ                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Rate₁ = k(0.20)ᵖ (0.30)ᵠ = 5.07×10⁻⁵.....(i)\nRate₂ =  k(0.20)ᵖ (0.10)ᵠ = 5.07×10⁻⁵ ......(ii)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Divide equation (i) by equation (ii)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  \nRate₁/Rate₂ = (0.30)ᵠ/ (0.10)ᵠ = 1\n3ᵠ = 1                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   q = 0                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Hence, the reaction is zero order in B.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    Rate₃ =(0.40)ᵖ (0.05)ᵠ = 1.43×10⁻⁴.......(iii)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 Divide equation (iii) by (ii)\n Rate₃/Rate₂ = (0.40)ᵖ/(0.20)ᵖ =  1.43×10⁻⁴/ 5.07×10⁻⁵                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              2ᵖ = 2.8                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       p = 1.5(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 120; Exercise 4.10)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','1.5 and 0','0 and 1.5','1.5 and 1.5','0 and 0','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/180916q.png'),
(180917,'chemistry','The following results have been obtained during the kinetic studies of the reaction:                                                                                                                                                                                                           2A + B → C + D                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Determine the rate law and the rate constant for the reaction. diagram(n1809)','Rate =k[A][B]² and k=6.0 L²mol⁻²min⁻¹','The rate law expression is, rate=k[A]₀ᵖ[B]₀ᵠ                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     From experiments I and IV, we get                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Rate₁ = k(0.1)ᵖ (0.1)ᵠ = 6.0×10⁻³.....(i)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    Rate₄ = k(0.4)ᵖ (0.1)ᵠ = 2.40×10⁻².....(ii)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Divide equation (ii) by equation (i)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      (0.4)ᵖ/(0.1)ᵖ = 2.40×10⁻²/ 6.0×10⁻³ = 4                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        4ᵖ = 4                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                p = 1                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            From experiments II and III, we get                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Rate₂ = k(0.3)ᵖ (0.2)ᵠ = 7.20×10⁻².......(iii)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Rate₃ = k(0.3)ᵖ (0.4)ᵠ = 2.88×10⁻¹........(iv)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Divide equation (iv) by equation (iii)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         (0.4)ᵠ/(0.2)ᵠ  = 2.88×10⁻¹/ 7.20×10⁻² = 4                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           2ᵠ = 4                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 q = 2                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   The rate law expression is, rate=  k[A][B]²                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Substitute values in equation (i)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                6.0×10⁻³ =  k[0.1][0.1]²                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       k=6.0 L²mol⁻²min⁻¹(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 120; Exercise 4.11)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','Rate =k[A][B]² and k=6.0 L²mol⁻²min⁻¹','Rate =k[A]²[B] and k=6.0 L²mol⁻²min⁻¹','Rate =k[A]²[B]² and k=6.0 L²mol⁻²min⁻¹','Rate =k[A][B]² and k=1.0 L²mol⁻²min⁻¹','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/180917q.png'),
(180918,'chemistry','The reaction between A and B is first order with respect to A and zero order\nwith respect to B. Fill in the blanks in the following table (a, b, c): diagram(1809)','a = 0.2 molL⁻¹, b = 8.0×10⁻² mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹, c = 0.1molL⁻¹','The rate law expression is: \nRate=k[A][B]⁰                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Rate=k[A]                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                2.0×10⁻² = k[0.1] \nk=0.2min ⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        From second experiment, 4.0×10⁻² = 0.2[A]                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             [A] = 0.2 molL⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                From third experiment                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 Rate = 0.2×0.4 = 8.0×10⁻² mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     From fourth experiment, 2.0×10⁻² = 0.2[A]                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      [A] = 0.1 molL⁻¹ (Reference NCERT Class 12; page 120; Exercise 4.12)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','a = 0.1 molL⁻¹, b = 8.0×10⁻² mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹, c = 0.2molL⁻¹','a = 0.2 molL⁻¹, b = 8.0×10⁻² mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹, c = 0.1molL⁻¹','a = 0.2 molL⁻¹, b = 6.0×10⁻² mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹, c = 0.1molL⁻¹','a = 0.2 molL⁻¹, b = 8.0×10⁻² mol L⁻¹ min⁻¹, c = 0.2molL⁻¹','NEW','12-102','12-068','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/180918q.png'),
(180919,'chemistry','Calculate the half-life of a first order reaction from rate constants 200 s⁻¹','3.465×10⁻³','The relationship between the half life period and the rate constant is, t₁/₂ = 0.693/k                                                                                                                                                                                                            t₁/₂ = 0.693/200 =  3.465×10⁻³ s(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 120; Exercise 4.13)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','6.465×10⁻³','4.465×10⁻³','2.465×10⁻³','3.465×10⁻³','NEW','12-111','12-076',NULL),
(180920,'chemistry','Calculate the half-life of a first order reaction from rate constants 2 min⁻¹','0.3465mins','The relationship between the half life period and the rate constant is, tDecay constant k= = 0.693/k                                                                                                                                                                                                            t₁/₂ = 0.693/2 = 0.3465mins(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 120; Exercise 4.13)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.3465mins','0.1465mins','0.6465mins','0.9465mins','NEW','12-111','12-076',NULL),
(180921,'chemistry','Calculate the half-life of a first order reaction from rate constants 4 year⁻¹','0.1732years','The relationship between the half life period and the rate constant is, t₁/₂ = 0.693/k                                                                                                                                                                                                            t₁/₂ = 0.693/4 = 0.1732years(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 120; Exercise 4.13)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.4732years','0.2732years','0.3732years','0.1732years','NEW','12-111','12-076',NULL),
(180922,'chemistry','The half-life for radioactive decay of 14C is 5730 years. An archaeological artifact containing wood had only 80% of the 14C found in a living tree. Estimate\nthe age of the sample.','1846years','Decay constant k= 0.693/t₁/₂ = 0.693/5730years = 1.209×10⁻⁴/years                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       The rate of counts is proportional to the number of C-14 atoms in the sample.                                                                                                                                                                                                                   N₀ = 100, N = 80                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   The age of the sample t = 2.303/k log(N₀/N)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        t = 2.303/ 1.209×10⁻⁴ log(100/80) = 1846years(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 120; Exercise 4.14)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','1846years','1800years','1946years','1446years','NEW','12-111','12-076',NULL),
(180923,'chemistry','The experimental data for decomposition of N₂O₅                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        [2N₂O₅ → 4NO₂ + O₂]                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              in gas phase at 318K are given below:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Find the half-life period for the reaction and what is the rate law ? diagram(n1810)','1432s and Rate=k[N₂O₅]','Slope = -k/2.303 = -2.10 x 10⁻⁴                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   k = 4.84×10⁻⁴/s                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Half life period t₁/₂ = 0.693/k = 0.693/4.84×10⁻⁴ = 1432s(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 120; Exercise 4.15)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','1400s and Rate=k[N₂O₅]²','1432s and Rate=k[N₂O₅]','1400s and Rate=k[N₂O₅]','1432s and Rate=k[N₂O₅]²','NEW','12-111','12-076','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/180923q.png'),
(180924,'chemistry','The rate constant for a first order reaction is 60 s⁻¹. How much time will it take to reduce the initial concentration of the reactant to its 1/16th\nvalue?','4.6×10⁻²seconds','Given that the rate constant for a first order reaction is 60 s⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Let a M be the initial concentration                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Final concentration will be a/16 M.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Rate constant, k=60/s                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    t = 2.303/k log([A]₀/[A])                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   t = 2.303/60 log(a/a/16)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        t=4.6×10⁻²seconds(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 121; Exercise 4.16)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','4.6×10⁻¹seconds','4.6×10⁻³seconds','5.6×10⁻²seconds','4.6×10⁻²seconds','NEW','12-111','12-076',NULL),
(180925,'chemistry','During nuclear explosion, one of the products is ⁹⁰Sr with half-life of 28.1 years. If 1μg of ⁹⁰Sr was absorbed in the bones of a newly born\nbaby instead of calcium, how much of it will remain after 10 years and 60 years if it is not lost metabolically','x=0.782μg and y=0.228μg','k= 0.693/t₁/₂                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               k= 0.693/28.1 = 0.0247/year                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               i) After 10 years, let xμ be the concentration of  ⁹⁰Sr.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             k = 2.303/t log([A]₀/[A])                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 0.0247 = 2.303/10 years log(1/x)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 x=0.782μg                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       ii) After 60 years, let yμ be the concentration of  ⁹⁰Sr.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       k = 2.303/t log([A]₀/[A])                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               0.0247 = 2.303/60 years log(1/y)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         y=0.228μg (Reference NCERT Class 12; page 121; Exercise 4.17)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','x=0.228μg and y=0.782μg','x=0.228μg and y=0.228μg','x=0.782μg and y=0.228μg','x=0.782μg and y=0.782μg','NEW','12-111','12-076',NULL),
(180926,'chemistry','For a first order reaction, show that time required for 99% completion is twice the time required for the completion of 90% of reaction.','t₉₉ = 2t₉₀','For a first order reaction, we have                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      t = 2.303/k log([A]₀/[A])                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Let a M be the initial concentration.\nWhen reaction is 99% complete,                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         [A]=a - 99a/100 = a/100                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             t₉₉ = 2.303/k log(a/0.01a)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    When reaction is 90% complete,                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 [A]=a - 90a/100 = a/10                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              t₉₀ = 2.303/k log(a/0.1a)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          t₉₀ = 2.303/k×1 ......(2)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Divide equation (1) by (2), we get                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       t₉₉/t₉₀ = 2                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    t₉₉ = 2t₉₀ (Reference NCERT Class 12; page 121; Exercise 4.18)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','t₉₉ = 6t₉₀','t₉₉ = 4t₉₀','t₉₉ = 2t₉₀','t₉₉ = 3t₉₀','NEW','12-111','12-076',NULL),
(180927,'chemistry','A first order reaction takes 40 min for 30% decomposition. Calculate t₁/₂','77.7 min','Let a M be the initial concentration. After 40 minutes, the concentration is a−                                                                                                                                                                                                                        30a/100 = 0.70a                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       k= 2.303/t log([A]₀/[A])                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        k = 2.303/40 log(a/0.70)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     k=8.92×10⁻³/min                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   The half life period, t₁/₂ = 0.693/k = 0.693/8.92×10⁻³ 77.7min (Reference NCERT Class 12; page 121; Exercise 4.19)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','97.7 min','67.7 min','87.7 min','77.7 min','NEW','12-111','12-076',NULL),
(180928,'chemistry','For the decomposition of azoisopropane to hexane and nitrogen at 543K, the following data are obtained                                                                                                                                             Calculate the rate constant. diagram(n1811)','2.21×10⁻³s⁻¹','(CH₃)₂CHN=N(CH₃)₂ → N₂ + C₆H₁₄                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      t = 0           P₀                            0        0                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 t = t𝒻          P₀- p                         p       p                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Total pressure at t = t𝒻 = (P₀- p) + p + p = P₀ + p                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 Pₜ = P₀ + p                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       p = Pₜ - P₀                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                For first order reaction,K=2.303/tlog(P₀/P₀-p) = 2.303/t log(P₀/2P₀-Pₜ)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                At t=360s, K=2.303/360slog(35/2×35−54)=2.175×10⁻³s⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       At t=720s, K=2.303/720slog(35/2×35−63)=2.235×10⁻³ s⁻¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           K = K + K/2 = (2.175×10⁻³ + 2.235×10⁻³)/2 = 2.21×10⁻³s⁻¹ (Reference NCERT Class 12; page 121; Exercise 4.20)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','1.21×10⁻³s⁻¹','2.21×10⁻³s⁻¹','4.21×10⁻³s⁻¹','3.21×10⁻³s⁻¹','NEW','12-111','12-076','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/180928q.png'),
(180929,'chemistry','The following data were obtained during the first order thermal decomposition of SO₂Cl₂ at a constant volume                                                                                                                                                             SO₂Cl₂ (g) → SO₂(g) + Cl₂(g)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Calculate the rate of the reaction when total pressure is 0.65 atm. diagram(n1812)','7.805×10⁻⁴ atm/s','For a first order reaction, we have:  K=2.303/tlog(P₀/2P₀-Pₜ)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        K=2.303/100log(0.5/2 x 0.5 - 0.6)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 k=2.23×10⁻²³/s                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Rate =kPSO₂Cl₂                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        The total pressure is 0.65 atm.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    PSO₂Cl₂ = 2P₀-Pₜ = 2 x 0.5 - 0.65 = 0.35 atm                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Hence, rate =2.23×10⁻³ x 0.35 = 7.805×10⁻⁴ atm/s(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 121; Exercise 4.21)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','6.805×10⁻⁴ atm/s','7.805×10⁻⁴ atm/s','9.805×10⁻⁴ atm/s','8.805×10⁻⁴ atm/s','NEW','12-111','12-076','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/180929q.png'),
(180930,'chemistry','The rate constant for the decomposition of N₂O₅ at various temperatures is given below:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          Draw a graph between ln k and 1/T and calculate the values of A and Ea. Predict the rate constant at 30° and 50°C. diagram(n1813)','k₁ = 0.003873/s and k₂ = 0.004028/s','lnk = lnA−Ea/RT                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              \n y=mx+c\nFrom the graph                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     m=−191.4\nc=−4.922                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Hence, lnk=−4.922 + (-191.4)/T                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                At 30⁰C or 303 K,                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         lnk = −4.922 + (-191.4)/303                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         lnk=−5.515\nk=0.003873/s                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   At 50⁰C  or 323 K                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     lnk = −4.922 + (-191.4)/323                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         lnk=−5.55\nk=0.004028/s(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 121; Exercise 4.22)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','k₁ = 0.003873/s and k₂ = 0.004028/s','k₂ = 0.003873/s and k₁ = 0.004028/s','k₁ = 0.003873/s and k₂ = 0.003873/s','k₁ = 0.004028/s and k₂ = 0.004028/s','NEW','12-109','12-074','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/180930q.png'),
(180931,'chemistry','The rate constant for the decomposition of hydrocarbons is 2.418 × 10⁻⁵/s  at 546 K. If the energy of activation is 179.9 kJ/mol, what will be the value of pre-exponential factor','3.90×10¹²/s','The expression for the preexponentail factor can be obtained from Arrhenius equation.                                                                                                                                                                                                      logk = logA − Ea/2.303RT                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        logA = logk + Ea/2.303RT                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 logA = log2.418 x 10⁻⁵ + 179.9 x 10³/2.303 x 8.314 x 546                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            logA=12.592 The pre-exponential factor, A=3.90×10¹²/s(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 121; Exercise 4.23)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','1.90×10¹²/s','2.90×10¹²/s','4.90×10¹²/s','3.90×10¹²/s','NEW','12-114','12-080',NULL),
(180932,'chemistry','Consider a certain reaction A → Products with k = 2.0 × 10⁻²s⁻¹. Calculate the concentration of A remaining after 100 s if the initial concentration of A is 1.0 mol L⁻¹.','0.135M','The integrated rate expression for the first order reaction is as follows:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     k= 2.303/t log([A]₀/[A])                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         2 x 10⁻² =  2.303/t log(1.0/[A])                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           log1.0/[A] = 0.868                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                [A]=0.135M\nHence, the concentration of A remaining after 100 seconds is 0.135 M.(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 121; Exercise 4.24)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','0.335M','0.035M','0.135M','0.235M','NEW','12-100','12-066',NULL),
(180933,'chemistry','Sucrose decomposes in acid solution into glucose and fructose according to the first order rate law, with t₁/₂ = 3.00 hours. What fraction of sample of sucrose remains after 8 hours ?','0.158 M','k= 0.693/t₁/₂ = 0.693/3.0 = 0.231/hr                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Let initial concetration of sucrose be 1 M.\nAfter 8 hours, the concentration will be 1−x. x represents the amount of sucrose decomposed                                                                                                                                                                                         k= 2.303/t log([A]₀/[A])                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               0.231 = 2.303/8 log(x/1-x)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                log(x/1-x) = 0.8024                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 1/1-x = 6.345                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               x=0.842\nAfter 8 hours, the concentration of sucrose left is 1−0.842=0.158 M(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 121; Exercise 4.25)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','0.358 M','0.158 M','0.058 M','0.258 M','NEW','12-111','12-076',NULL),
(180934,'chemistry','The decomposition of hydrocarbon follows the equation\nk = (4.5 × 10¹¹s⁻¹) e⁻²⁸⁰⁰⁰ᴷ/ᵀ. Calculate Ea.','232.792kJ/mol','The expression for k is as follows:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    k = (4.5 × 10¹¹s⁻¹) e⁻²⁸⁰⁰⁰ᴷ/ᵀ                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 Comparing it with Arrhenius equation, we get                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              k=Ae⁻ᴱᵃ/ᴿᵀ                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Ea/R = 28000K                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       The activation energy = Ea = 28000×8.314=232792J/mol=232.792kJ/mol(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 121; Exercise 4.26)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','132.792kJ/mol','232.792kJ/mol','332.792kJ/mol','432.792kJ/mol','NEW','12-114','12-080',NULL),
(180935,'chemistry','The rate constant for the first order decomposition of H₂O₂ is given by the\nfollowing equation:\nlog k = 14.34 – 1.25 × 104K/T\nCalculate Ea for this reaction and at what temperature will its half-period\nbe 256 minutes?','239.339kJ/mol and 669 K','The expression for the rate constant is as follows:\nlogk=14.34−1.25×10⁴K/T....(i)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Comparing it with Arrhenius equation, we get-                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                logk = logA − Ea/2.303RT                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Therefore, Ea/2.303R = 1.25 x 10⁴                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    Ea =  1.25 x 10⁴ × 2.303 × 8.314                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           The activation energy = Ea = 239339J/mol = 239.339kJ/mol                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Half life period, t₁/₂ = 256min = 256 × 60 sec                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         k = 0.693/t₁/₂ = 0.693/265 x 60                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                k=4.51×10⁻⁵/s                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Substitute in equation (i), we get-                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              log4.51 × 10⁻⁵  =14.341.25 × 10⁴ K/T                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         −4.35=14.341.25×10⁴K/T                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       T=669 K\nHence, the temperature at which the half-life period is 256 minutes is 669 K.(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 122; Exercise 4.27)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','239.339kJ/mol and 669 K','239.339kJ/mol and 600 K','209.339kJ/mol and 669 K','200.339kJ/mol and 669 K','NEW','12-114','12-080',NULL),
(180936,'chemistry','The decomposition of A into product has value of k as 4.5 × 10³ s⁻¹ at 10°C and energy of activation 60 kJ mol⁻¹. At what temperature would k be 1.5 × 10⁴s⁻¹?','24.02⁰C','It is given that the decomposition of A into product has value of k=4.5×10³/s at 10⁰C                                                                                                                                                                                                           log(K\'/k) = Ea/2.303R(T\'-T/T\'T)\nlog(1.5 x 10⁴/4.5 x 10³) = 60 x 10³/2.303 x 8.314(T\'-283/283T\') \n0.5228=3132.62(T\'-283/283T\')                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         T\'-283 = 0.0472T\'                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 T\' = 297.02 K                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       T\' = 297.02K−273=24.02⁰C(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 122; Exercise 4.28)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','14.02⁰C','24.02⁰C','20.02⁰C','34.02⁰C','NEW','12-114','12-080','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/180936q.png'),
(180937,'chemistry','The time required for 10% completion of a first order reaction at 298K is equal to that required for its 25% completion at 308K. If the value of A is 4 × 10¹⁰s⁻¹. Calculate k at 318K and Ea.','1.042×10⁻²/s  and 76.623kJ/mol','For 10% completion of the reaction, we have                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 \nk(298) = 2.303/t log100/90  ....(i)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               For 25% completion of the reaction, we have                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  k(308) = 2.303/t log100/75  .....(ii)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Divide equation (ii) with (i)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    log2.73 = Ea/2.303 x 8.314 x (308-298/308 x 298)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Ea = 76623J/mol=76.623kJ/mol                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              But, logk = logA − Ea/2.303RT                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     logk(318K) = log4×10¹⁰ - 76623/2.303 x 8.314 x 318 =−1.9823                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        k(318) = 1.042×10⁻²/s (Reference NCERT Class 12; page 122; Exercise 4.29)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','1.042×10⁻²/s  and 76.623kJ/mol','2.042×10⁻²/s  and 96.623kJ/mol','1.042×10⁻²/s  and 96.623kJ/mol','2.042×10⁻²/s  and 76.623kJ/mol','NEW','12-114','12-080','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/180937q.png'),
(180938,'chemistry','The rate of a reaction quadruples when the temperature changes from 293 K to 313 K. Calculate the energy of activation of the reaction assuming that it does not change with temperature','52.86kJ/mol.','The Arrhenius equation at two different temperatures is as follows:                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          log(k\'/k) = Ea/2.303R[1/T - 1/T\']                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           k\'/k = 4                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       log(4) = Ea/2.303R[1/293 - 1/313]                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    The energy of activation, Ea = 52863.3J/mol = 52.86kJ/mol.(Reference NCERT Class 12; page 122; Exercise 4.30)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','82.86kJ/mol.','52.86kJ/mol.','42.86kJ/mol.','72.86kJ/mol.','NEW','12-114','12-080','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/180938q.png'),
(180939,'biology','The size of the heart of an individual is approximately estimated by the size of that individual\'s clenched fist.','TRUE','The heart has the size of a clenched fist. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 283; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Human circulatory system','Body fluids and circulation','1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-283','11-198',NULL),
(180940,'biology','Why is the SAN called the pacemaker of the heart?  \n(i)Initiating the contractions \n(ii)Maintaining the rhythm\n (iii)Dampening the action potential \n(iv)Filtering the current','i and ii','The SAN can generate the maximum number of action potentials, i.e., 70-75 /min  , and is responsible for initiating and maintaining the rhythmic contractile activity of the heart. Therefore, it is called the pacemaker. Our heart normally beats 70-75 times in a minute (average 72 beats /min ). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 284; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Human circulatory system','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','iii and iv','i and ii','i, ii and iv','i, ii and iii','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181001,'chemistry','For the chemical reaction, N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) the correct option is','-d[N₂]/dt = d[NH₃]/2dt','N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  rate of reaction is given as                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      -d[N₂]/dt = -d[H₂]/3dt = d[NH₃]/2dt','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','d[H₂]/3dt = d[NH₃]/2dt','3d[H₂]/dt = 2d[NH₃]/dt','-d[N₂]/dt = d[NH₃]/2dt','-1/3d[H₂]/dt = -1/2d[NH₃]/dt','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(181002,'chemistry','The rate of the reaction : 2N₂O₅ → 4NO₂ + O₂ can be written in three ways.                                                                                                                                                                                                                            -d[N₂O₅]/dt = k[N₂O₅]                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     d[NO₂]/dt = k\'[N₂O₅]                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          d[O₂]/dt = k\'\'[N₂O₅] The relationship between k and k′ and between k\nand k′′ are','k ′ = 2k , k ′′ = k/2','The expression for the rate of the reaction can be written as :                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                -d[N₂O₅]/2dt = -d[NO₂]/4dt = d[O₂]/dt                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 k(N₂O₅)/2 = k\'(N₂O₅)/4 = k\'\'(N₂O₅)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    k/2 = k\'/4 = k\'\'                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   k\' = 2k, k\'\' = k/2                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Thus, the relationship between k and k\' and between k and k\'\' are k\' = 2k; k\'\'= k/2.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','k ′ = 2k , k ′′ = k/4','k ′ = 2k , k ′′ = 2k','k ′ = 2k , k ′′ = k/2','k ′ = k/2 , k ′′ = k/2','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(181003,'chemistry','For the reaction N₂O₅(g) → 2NO₂(g) + 1/2O₂(g) the value of rate of disappearance of N₂O₅ is given as 6.25 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹. The rate of formation of NO₂ and O₂ is given respectively as','1.25 × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ and\n3.125 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹','For the reaction, N₂O₅(g) → 2NO₂(g) + 1/2O₂(g)                                                                                                                       \n The value of rate of disappearance of N₂O₅ is given 6.25 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹. From the stoichiometry of the reaction.                                             \n -d[N₂O₅]/dt = d[NO₂]/2dt                                                                                                                                            \n d[NO₂]/2dt = 2[-d[N₂O₅]/dt]                                                                                                                                      \n where dx/dt stands for rate  of appearance or disappearance of species x with respect to time.                                                                      \n Therefore rate of formation of NO₂ and O₂ is "1.25 × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ and\n 3.125 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹".','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','6.25 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ and\n6.25 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹','1.25 × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ and\n3.125 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹','6.25 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ and\n3.125 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹','1.25 × 10⁻² mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ and\n6.25 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(181004,'chemistry','For the reaction, N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g) d[NH₃]/dt =  2 ×10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹ , the value of -d[H₂]/dt would be','3 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','d[NH₃]/2dt =  1 ×10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹                                                                                                                                    \n d[NH₃]/2dt = -d[H₂]/3dt                                                                                                                                                  \n -d[H₂]/dt =  3 ×10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','4 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','6 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','1 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','3 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(181005,'chemistry','In the reaction,\nBrO₃⁻(aq) + 5Br⁻(aq) + 6H⁺(aq) → 3Br₂(l) + 3H₂O(l)\nThe rate of appearance of bromine (Br₂) is related to rate of disappearance of bromide ions as','d[Br₂]/dt = -3d[Br⁻]/5dt','For the given reaction,                                                                                                                                              \n Rate of appearance of bromine (Br₂) = +d[Br₂]/3dt                                                                                                                  \n Rate of disappearance of bromine (Br⁻) = -d[Br⁻]/5dt                                                                                                                 \n Therefore, +d[Br₂]/3dt = -d[Br⁻]/5dt                                                                                                                                      \n +d[Br₂]/dt = -3d[Br⁻]/5dt','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','d[Br₂]/dt = -5d[Br⁻]/3dt','d[Br₂]/dt = 5d[Br⁻]/3dt','d[Br₂]/dt = 3d[Br⁻]/5dt','d[Br₂]/dt = -3d[Br⁻]/5dt','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(181006,'chemistry','Consider the reaction : N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g). The equality relationship between  d[NH₃]/dt and -d[H₂]/dt','d[NH₃]/dt = -2d[H₂]/3dt','N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g)                                                                                                                                         \n From state law, -d[N₂]/dt = -d[H₂]/3dt = d[NH₃]/2dt                                                                                                            \n Equating 2nd and 3rd term                                                                                                                                            \n d[H₂]/dt = -3d[NH₃]/2dt','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','d[NH₃]/dt = -1/d[H₂]/3dt','d[NH₃]/dt = -2d[H₂]/3dt','d[NH₃]/dt = -3d[H₂]/2dt','d[NH₃]/dt = -d[H₂]/dt','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(181007,'chemistry','For the reaction, 2A + B → 3C + D, which of the following does not express the reaction rate?','-d[C]/3dt','C is not representing the rate because the product side cannot have a negative sign in the expression.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','-d[A]/2dt','-d[C]/3dt','-d[B]/dt','d[D]/dt','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(181008,'chemistry','3A → 2B, rate of reaction, +d[B]/dt is equal to','-2d[A]/3dt','3A→2B\nFor the given reaction we can write                                                                                                                            \n rate of reaction: +d[B]/dt = -2d[A]/3dt','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','-3d[A]/2dt','-2d[A]/3dt','-d[A]/3dt','2d[A]/dt','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(181009,'chemistry','For the reaction, BrO₃⁻(aq) + 3Br⁻(aq) + H⁺(aq) → 5Br₂(l) + 3H₂O(l)                                                                                                \n Which of the following relations correctly represents the consumption and formation of products?','d[Br⁻]/dt =-3d[Br₂]/5dt','BrO₃⁻(aq) + 3Br⁻(aq) + H⁺(aq) → 5Br₂(l) + 3H₂O(l)                                                                                                                               \n Applying rate law,                                                                                                                                                    \n -d[Br⁻]/3dt = d[Br₂]/5dt                                                                                                                                             \n d[Br⁻]/dt = -3d[Br₂]/5dt(Reference:  NCERT 12th ; Page 99 ; Paragraph 6)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','d[Br⁻]/dt =-3d[Br₂]/5dt','d[Br⁻]/dt =3d[Br₂]/5dt','d[Br⁻]/dt =-5d[Br₂]/3dt','d[Br⁻]/dt =5d[Br₂]/3dt','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(181010,'chemistry','For the reaction H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g), the rate of reaction is expressed as','Rate of reaction = ∆[HI]/2∆t = -∆[H₂]/∆t = -∆[I₂]/∆t.','For the reaction:                                                                                                                                                     \n Rate of reaction = ∆[HI]/2∆t = -∆[H₂]/∆t = -∆[I₂]/∆t.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Rate of reaction = -∆[HI]/∆t = ∆[H₂]/∆t = ∆[I₂]/2∆t.','Rate of reaction = ∆[HI]/2∆t = -∆[H₂]/∆t = -∆[I₂]/∆t.','Rate of reaction = ∆[HI]/2∆t = ∆[H₂]/∆t = ∆[I₂]/∆t.','None of these.','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(181011,'chemistry','Mechanism of a hypothetical reaction,\nX2 + Y2 → 2XY, is given below :\n(i) X2 → X + X (fast)\n(ii) X + Y2 ⇌ XY + Y (slow)\n(iii) X + Y → XY (fast)                                                                                                                                             \n The overall order of the reaction will be','1.5','Take reaction (i) and (iii) to be equilibrium reaction where value of k𝒻 >>> kᵦ                                                                                                           \n Therefore,[X]²/[X₂] = Kₑ\'                                                                                                                                     \n [X] = Kₑ\'⁰.⁵ x [X₂]⁰.⁵ ...(A)                                                                                                                                        \n Since reaction (ii) is the slowest step, so the rate of this reaction will be reaction rate for the main reaction                                              \n r = Kₑ[X][Y₂]  ......(B)                                                                                                                                       Substituting equation (A) in equation (B)                                                                                                                           We get r = KₑKₑ\'⁰.⁵ x [X₂]⁰.⁵[Y₂]                                                                                                                                     Let, KₑKₑ\'= K                                                                                                                                                            r = K[X₂]⁰.⁵[Y₂]                                                                                                                                                  Hence, order of reaction is 1 + 0.5=1.5','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2','0','1.5','1','NEW','12-099','12-065',NULL),
(181012,'chemistry','The decomposition of phosphine (PH₃) on tungsten at low pressure is a first-order reaction. It is because the','Rate is proportional to the surface coverage','The decomposition of PH₃  on tungsten at low pressure is a first order reaction because surface area coverage is proportional to partial pressure of PH₃. Hence, rate is proportional to the surface coverage.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Rate is proportional to the surface coverage','Rate is inversely proportional to the surface coverage','Rate is independent of the surface coverage','Rate of decomposition is very slow','NEW','12-106','12-072',NULL),
(181013,'chemistry','The rate constant of the reaction A→ B is 0.6 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹. If the concentration of A is 5 M, then concentration of B after 20 minutes is','0.72','The rate constant has unit mole per second which indicates zero order reaction.                                                                                                                                                                                                                    x = kt                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       The concentration of B after 20 minutes is 20 × 60 × 0.6 × 10⁻³= 0.72M','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.72','0.36','1.08','3.6','NEW','12-100','12-066',NULL),
(181014,'chemistry','For a reaction between A and B the order with respect to A is 2 and the order with respect to B is 3. The concentrations of both A and B are doubled, the rate will increase by a factor of','32','Given, Order of A=2;\nOrder of B=3;                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Rate ∝ [A]²[B]³                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      If both concentrations of A and B are doubled.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Rate ∝ [2A]²[2B]³                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Rate𝒻 ∝ 32[A]²[B]³                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Rate increases by a factor of 32.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','12','16','32','10','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181015,'chemistry','In a reaction, A + B → product, rate is doubled when the concentration of B is doubled, and rate increases by a factor of 8 when the concentration of both the reactants (A and B) are doubled, rate law for the reaction can be written as','Rate = k [A]²[B]','When conc of B is doubled, the rate is doubled. So, Order wrt [B] is 1.\n\nThe rate increases by a factor of 8 when conc of [A] and [B] are doubled. So Order wrt [A] is 2.\n\nOverall Rate is                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Rate = k [A]²[B]¹                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               Hence, the correct option is B.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Rate = k [A][B]²','Rate = k [A]²[B]','Rate = k [A][B]','Rate = k [A]²[B]²','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181016,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements for the order of a reaction is incorrect?','Order of reaction is always whole number','If rate is given by: rate =k[A]ˣ[B]ʸ  then the order =x+y. This order can be a positive, negative no., zero or even fractional. It can be determined only experimentally and does not depend on stoichiometric coefficients of reactants.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Order can be determined only experimentally','Order is not influenced by stoichiometric coefficient of the reactants','Order of a reaction is sum of power to the concentration terms of reactants to express the rate of reaction','Order of reaction is always whole number','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181017,'chemistry','The unit of rate constant for a zero order reaction is','mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','Unit of K = mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','mol L⁻¹s⁻¹','L mol⁻¹s⁻¹','L²mol⁻²s⁻¹','s⁻¹','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181018,'chemistry','During the kinetic study of the reaction,\n2A + B → C + D, following results were obtained : diagram(1813)                                                                                                           \n Based on the above data which one of the following is correct?','Rate = k[A][B]²','In Exp 1 and 4 ( Keeping the Conc. of [B] constant), when the concentration of A is quadrapled, the rate is also quadrapled. So Order with respect to A is 1   \n In Exp 2 and 3 ( Keeping the Conc. of [A] constant), when the concentration of B is doubled, the rate is quadrapled. So Order with respect to B is 2.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Rate = k[A]²[B]','Rate = k[A][B]','Rate = k[A]²[B]²','Rate = k[A][B]²','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181018q.png'),
(181019,'chemistry','For the reaction, A + B → products, it is observed\nthat\n(i) on doubling the initial concentration of A only, the rate of reaction is also doubled and\n(ii) on doubling the initial concentration of both A and B, there is a change by a factor of 8 in the rate of the reaction.\nThe rate of this reaction is given by','Rate = k[A][B]²','The given reaction is A+B⟶ Product\nLet us suppose the rate law of this reaction is:-                                                                                                                      \n Rate = k[A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ where K is a rate constant.\na and b are order of the reaction with respect to the reactants A and B respectively.\nGiven that,\nWhen [A] is doubled, the rate of the reaction is also doubled, so the reaction is first order w.r.t.A and hence a=1                                                                     \n When [A],[B] is doubled, the rate of reaction becomes 8 times. Now,                                                                                                     \n Rate\' = k[2A]¹[2B]ᵇ ...(ii)                                                                                                                                                 \nRate = k[A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ....(iii)                                                                                                                                         \n Now, ∵ New rate of reaction is 8 times, so dividing (ii) by (iii) :-                                                                                                \n 8 = 2.2ᵇ                                                                                                                                                              \n 2³ = 2.2ᵇ                                                                                                                                                           \n 2³ = 2¹⁺ᵇ                                                                                                                                                       \n b = 2                                                                                                                                                                \n So, order of reaction w.r.t to B is 2                                                                                                                               \n So, Rate=K[A][B]²','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Rate = k[A]²[B]','Rate = k[A][B]','Rate = k[A]²[B]²','Rate = k[A][B]²','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(181020,'chemistry','The bromination of acetone that occurs in acid solution is represented by this equation.\nCH₃COCH₃(aq) + Br₂(aq) → CH₃COCH₂Br(aq)+ H⁺(aq) + Br⁻(aq)\nThese kinetic data were obtained for given reaction concentrations.                                                                                           Based on these data, the rate equation is ..... Refer diagram(n1814)','Rate = k [CH₃COCH₃][H⁺]','Here, Rate of disappearance of Br₂ = Rate of reaction                                                                                                                \n On comparing (1) & (2)\nOn doubling the concentration of Br₂, there is no change in the rate of reaction.                                                                                     \n ⇒ order wrt H₂ = 1                                                                                                                                                 \n On comparing (3) & (4)                                                                                                                                                \n [CH₃COCH₃] → 4[CH₃COCH₃]/3 and [H⁺] → 2[H⁺]                                                                                                                       \n the rate became 8/3times of itself                                                                                                                                \n ⇒order wrt CH₃COCH₃ = 1                                                                                                                                                   \n ∴ Rate equation ⇒ rate = K[CH₃COCH₃][H⁺]','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Rate = k [CH₃COCH₃][Br₂][H⁺]²','Rate = k [CH₃COCH₃][Br₂][H⁺]','Rate = k [CH₃COCH₃][H⁺]','Rate = k [CH₃COCH₃][Br₂]','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181020q.png'),
(181021,'chemistry','The reaction of hydrogen and iodine monochloride is given as :\nH₂(g) + 2ICl(g) → 2HCl(g) + I₂(g)\nThis reaction is of first order with respect to H₂(g) and ICl(g), following mechanisms were proposed diagram                                   \nWhich of the above mechanism(s) can be consistent with the given information about the reaction? (1815)','B only','According to question the given reaction is first order in H₂(g) and also first order in ICl(g) so, the slowest (rate determining) step in the reaction should involve H₂(g) & ICl(g) one mole each.\nThe mechanism II is seeming consistent with the conditions stated as:-                                                                                                              \n H₂(g) + 2ICl(g) → 2HCl(g) + I₂(g) (slow)                                                                                                                             \n Rate=K[H₂][ICl]                                                                                                                                                                         \n & HI(g)+ICl(g)⟶HCl(g)+I₂(g) (fast)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A and B both','Neither A nor B','A only','B only','NEW','12-104','12-070','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181021q.png'),
(181022,'chemistry','The rate of reaction between two reactants A and B decreases by a factor of 4 if the concentration of reactant B is doubled. The order of this reaction with respect to reactant B is','-2','Let us assume the chemical reaction:-\nnA+yB⟶Product\n\nLet the rate of the reaction:-                                                                                                                                       \n Rate₁ = k[A]ⁿ[B]ʸ...(a)                                                                                                                                                \n When the conc. of B is doubled;                                                                                                                                       \n Rate₂ = Rate₁/4 =  k[A]ⁿ[2B]ʸ...(b)                                                                                                                                   \nNow divide equation a by b:-                                                                                                                                       \n 4 = 1/2ʸ                                                                                                                                                                                  \n 2² = 1/2ʸ                                                                                                                                                          \n y=−2 with respect to B                                                                                                                                              \n i.e., the order of the reaction with respect to reactant B is −2','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2','-2','-1','1','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181023,'chemistry','If the rate of the reaction is equal to the rate constant, the order of the reaction is','0','If the rate of the reaction is equal to the rate constant , the order of the reaction is. For zero order reaction r = k','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Previous year questions',NULL,'1','mcq','0','1','2','3','NEW','12-103','12-068',NULL),
(181101,'chemistry','The half life period of a first order chemical reaction\n is 6.93 minutes. The time required for the\n completion of 99% of the chemical reaction will be\n (log 2 = 0.301) [AIEEE-2009]','46.06 minutes','t₁/₂ = ln2/k \nK = 2.303 × 0.301/6.93 \nAlso , t = (2.303/k) × log(a/a-0.99a)\nt = (2.303/2.303×0.301)6.93log(1/0.01) = 46.06 minutes','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','23.03 minutes','46.06 minutes','460.6 minutes','230.3 minutes','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181102,'chemistry','Consider the reaction\nCl₂ (aq) + H₂S (aq) → S(s) + 2H⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq)\nThe rate equation for this reaction is\nrate = k [Cl₂] [H₂S]\nWhich of these mechanisms is/are consistent with\n this rate equation?\nA. Cl₂ + H₂S → H⁺ + Cl⁻ + Cl⁺ + HS⁻ (slow)\nCl⁺ + HS⁻ → H⁺ + Cl⁻ + S (fast)\nB. H₂S ⇌ H⁺ + HS⁻ (fast equilibrium)\nCl₂ + HS⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + H⁺ + S (slow)\n [AIEEE-2010]','A only','Rate depends only on slow step','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','A only','B only','Both (A) & (B)','Neither (A) nor (B)','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(181103,'chemistry','The time for half life period of a certain reaction\n A → Products is 1 h. When the initial\n concentration of the reactant \'A\', is 2.0 mol /L,\n how much time does it take for its concentration\n to come from 0.50 to 0.25 mol /L if it is a zero\n order reaction? [AIEEE-2010] Refer diagram(n1831)','0.25 h','k = a/t₁/₂ = 2/2×1 = 1 \nt = c₀ - cₜ /k = 0.5 - 0.25/1 = 0.25h','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','1 h','4 h','0.5 h','0.25 h','NEW','12-111','12-077','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181103q.png'),
(181104,'chemistry','A reactant (A) forms two products :\nA  —k₁→ B , Activation energy Ea₁\nA  —k₂→ C , Activation energy Ea₂\nIf Ea₂ = 2Ea₁ , then k₁ and k₂ are related as\n [AIEEE-2011]','1','Refer diagram(ne1826)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181105,'chemistry','For a first order reaction, (A) → products, the\n concentration of A changes from 0.1 M to 0.025 M\n in 40 minutes. The rate of reaction when the\n concentration of A is 0.01M, is [AIEEE-2012]','3.47 × 10⁻⁴ M/min','Refer diagram(ne1827)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','3.47 × 10⁻⁴ M/min','3.47 × 10⁻⁵ M/min','1.73 × 10⁻⁴ M/min','1.73 × 10⁻⁵ M/min','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181106,'chemistry','The rate of a reaction doubles when its temperature\n changes from 300 K to 310 K. Activation energy of\n such a reaction will be [JEE (Main)-2013]\n (R = 8.314 J/Kmol and log2 = 0.301)','53.6 kJ /mol','log k₂/k₁ = (Eₐ/2.303R) [ 1/T₁- 1/T₂] = (Eₐ/2.303 × 8.314)[ 1/300 - 1/310] = (0.301× 2.303× 8.314× 300 ×310)/10 = 53.6 kJ /mol','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','53.6 kJ /mol','48.6 kJ /mol','58.5 kJ /mol','60.5 kJ /mol','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181107,'chemistry','For the non-stoichiometre reaction 2A + B → C +\nD, the following kinetic data were obtained in three\n separate experiments, all at 298 K.The rate law for the formation of C is.... refer diagram(n1832)','dC/dt = k[A]','Refer diagram(ne1828)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','dC/dt = k[A][B]','dC/dt = k[A]²[B]','dC/dt = k[A][B]²','dC/dt = k[A]','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181107q.png'),
(181108,'chemistry','Higher order (>3) reactions are rare due to\n [JEE (Main)-2015]','Low probability of simultaneous collision of all\n the reacting species','Higher order greater than 3 for reaction is rare\n because there is low probability of simultaneous\n collision of all the reacting species.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Low probability of simultaneous collision of all\n the reacting species','Increase in entropy and activation energy as\n more molecules are involved','Shifting of equilibrium towards reactants due to\n elastic collisions','Loss of active species on collision','NEW','12-116','12-082','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/181108q.png'),
(181109,'chemistry','Decomposition of H₂O₂ follows a first order\n reaction. In fifty minutes, the concentration of H₂O₂\n decreases from 0.5 to 0.25 M in one such\n decomposition. When the concentration of H₂O₂\n reaches 0.05 M, the rate of formation of O₂ will be\n [JEE (Main)-2016]','6.93 × 10⁻⁴ mol /min','Rate = K[H₂O₂\n] = 0.693 × 0.05 / 25\nRate of formation of  H₂O₂\n = 0.693 × 0.05 / 2 × 25 = 6.93 × 10⁻⁴ mol /min','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','6.93 × 10⁻⁴ mol /min','2.66 L /min at STP','1.34 × 10⁻² mol /min','6.93 × 10⁻² mol /min','NEW','12-106','12-072','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/181109q.png'),
(181110,'chemistry','Two reactions R₁ and R₂ have identical pre-\nexponential factors. Activation energy of R₁\n exceeds that of R₂ by 10 kJ /mol. If k₁ and k₂ are\n rate constants for reactions R₁ and R₂ respectively\n at 300 K, then ln(k₂/k₁) is equal to\n (R = 8.314 J /mole K) [JEE (Main)-2017]','4','Refer diagram(ne1829)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','6','4','8','12','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181111,'chemistry','At 518°C, the rate of decomposition of a sample of\n gaseous acetaldehyde, initially at a pressure of\n 363 torr, was 1.00 torr /s when 5% had reacted\n and 0.5 torr /s when 33% had reacted. The order\n of the reaction is [JEE (Main)-2018]','2','Assume the order of reaction with respect to\n acetaldehyde is x.\nCondition-1 :\nRate = k[CH₃CHO]ˣ\n1 = k[363 × 0.95]ˣ\n1 = k[344.85]ˣ ...(i)\nCondition-2 :\n0.5 = k[363 × 0.67]ˣ\n0.5 = k[243.21]ˣ ...(ii)\nDivide equation (i) by (ii),\n1/0.5 = (344.85/243.21)ˣ => 2 = (1.414)ˣ','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','2','3','1','0','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(181112,'chemistry','The following results were obtained during kinetic\n studies of the reaction ; 2 A + B →Products\nThe time (in minutes) required to consume half of\n A is .... Refer diagram(n1833)','5','From experiment I and II, it is observed that order\n of reaction w.r.t. 3 is zero.\nFrom experiment II and III,\n1.386 × 10⁻² /6.93 × 10⁻³ = (0.2/0.1)^α\n→ α = 1\nRate = K[A]¹\n6.93 × 10⁻³ = K(0.1)\nK = 6.93 × 10⁻²\nFor 2A + B → products\n2Kt = ln [A]₀/[A]\nt₁/₂ = 0.693/2K = 0.693/6.93 × 10⁻² × 2 = 5','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','100','1','5','10','NEW','12-111','12-077','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181112q.png'),
(181113,'chemistry','For the reaction, 2A + B → products, when the\n concentration of A and B both were doubled, the\n rate of the reaction increased from 0.3 mol /Ls to 2.4 mol /Ls. When the concentration of A\n alone is doubled, the rate increased from 0.3 mol\n/Ls to 0.6 mol /Ls. Which one of the following\n statements is correct? [JEE (Main)-2019]','Order of the reaction with respect to B is 2','r = K[A]ˣ [B]ʸ\nr₂/r₁ = 2ˣ2ʸ = 8 → x + y = 3\nr₃/r₁ = 2ˣ = 2 , x = 1\ny = 2.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Order of the reaction with respect to A is 2','Order of the reaction with respect to B is 1','Order of the reaction with respect to B is 2','Total order of the reaction is 4','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(181114,'chemistry','Consider the given plots for a reaction obeying\n Arrhenius equation (0°C < T < 300°C) : (k and Ea\n are rate constant and activation energy,\n respectively)','Both I and II are correct','K = Ae^-E /RT\nSo, as Ea increases, K decreases and as T increases, K increases.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','I is wrong but II is right','Both I and II are correct','Both I and II are wrong','I is right but II is wrong','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181201,'chemistry','From the rate expression for the following reactions, determine their order of reaction and the dimensions of the rate constants : 3NO(g) → N₂O(g) ; Rate = k[NO]²','2 & L/ mol s','Order = 2\nDimensions of k = rate / [NO]² = (mol / Ls) / (mol/L)² = L/ mol s','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','2 & L/ mol s','1 & L/ mol s','2 & L/ mol','2 & L mol /s','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(181202,'chemistry','From the rate expression for the following reactions, determine their order of reaction and the dimensions of the rate constants : H₂O₂(aq) + 3I⁻(aq) + 2H⁺ →  2H₂O(l) + I₃⁻ ; rate = k[H₂O₂][I⁻]','2 & L /mol s','Order = 2, Dimensions of k = L /mol s','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','2 & L /mol s','1 & L/ mol s','2 & L/ mol','2 & L mol /s','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(181203,'chemistry','From the rate expression for the following reactions, determine their order of reaction and the dimensions of the rate constants : CH₃CHO(g) → CH₄(g) + CO(g), rate = [CH₃CHO]³/²','3/2 & √L/√(mol s )','Order = 3/2 , Dimensions of k = Rate / [CH₃CHO]³/²  = (mol/Ls)/(mol/L)³/² = √L/√(mol s )','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','3/2 & √L/√(mol s )','3/2 & L/√(mol s )','3 & √L/(mol s )','√3/2 & L/√(mol s )','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(181204,'chemistry','From the rate expression for the following reactions, determine their order of reaction and the dimensions of the rate constants : C₂H₅Cl(g) → C₂H₄(g) + HCl(g) ; rate = k[C₂H₅Cl]','1 & 1/s','Order = 1 , Dimensions of k = Rate / [C₂H₅Cl] = (mol/Ls)/(mol/L) = 1/s','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','1 & 1/s','2 & 1/s','1 & 1s','1 & 2/s','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(181205,'chemistry','For the reaction : 2A + B → A₂B the rate = k[A][B]² with k = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶  L² /mol² s .Calculate the initial rate of the reaction when [A] = 0.1 mol /L [B] = 0.2 mol /L','8 × 10 ⁻⁹ mol /Ls','Initial rate = k[A][B]² = (2 × 10⁻⁶)(0.1) × (0.2)²  = 8 × 10 ⁻⁹ mol /Ls','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','8 × 10 ⁻⁹ mol /Ls','8 × 10 ⁹ mol /Ls','7 × 10 ⁻⁹ mol /Ls','6 × 10 ⁻⁹ mol /Ls','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181206,'chemistry','For the reaction : 2A + B → A₂B the rate = k[A][B]² with k = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶  L² /mol² s .[A] = 0.1 mol /L [B] = 0.2 mol /L, Calculate the rate of reaction after [A] is reduced to 0.06 mol /L.','3.89 × 10⁻⁹ mol/Ls','When [A] is reduced from 0.10 mol /L to 0.06 mol /L, i.e. 0.04 mol /L of A has reacted. Then according to equation, amount of B reacted is half of A \n[B] = 1/2 × 0.04 mol/L = 0.02 mol/L\nHence [B] left  = 0.2-0.02 = 0.18 mol/L\nNow, again rate = k[A][B]²\nRate =  (2 × 10⁻⁶)(0.06)(0.18)² = 3.89 × 10⁻⁹ mol/Ls','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','3.89 × 10⁻⁹ mol/Ls','3.89 × 10⁹ mol/Ls','10⁻⁹ mol/Ls','1.23 × 10⁻⁹ mol/Ls','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181207,'chemistry','The decomposition of NH₃ on platinum surface is zero order reaction. What are the rates of production of N₂ and H₂ if k = 2.5 × 10⁻⁴ mol /L s?','2.5 × 10⁻⁴ mol / Ls & 7.5 × 10⁻⁴ mol / Ls','Refer diagram(ne1832)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','2.5 × 10⁻⁴ mol / Ls & 7.5 × 10⁻⁴ mol / Ls','2.5 × 10⁻⁴ mol / Ls & 7.5 × 10⁴ mol / Ls','2.5 × 10⁴ mol / Ls & 7.5 × 10⁴ mol / Ls','2.5 × 10⁴ mol / Ls & 7.5 × 10⁻⁴ mol / Ls','NEW','12-105','12-071',NULL),
(181208,'chemistry','The decomposition of dimethyl ether leads to the formation of CH₄, H₂ and CO and the reaction rate is given by\nRate = k[CH₃OCH₃]³/²\nThe rate of reaction is followed by increase in pressure in closed vessel, so the rate can also be expressed in terms of the partial pressure of dimethyl ether, i.e.,\nRate = K(pCH₃OCH₃)³/²\nIf the pressure is measured in bar and time in minutes, then what are the units of rate and rate constants ?','bar / min & 1/√bar × min','In terms of pressure,\nUnits of rate = bar /min\nUnits of k = rate / (pCH₃OCH₃)³/² = (bar/min) / bar³/² = 1/√bar × min.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','bar / min & 1/√bar × min','bar / min & 1/bar × min','bar / min & √bar × min','bar / min & 1/√bar','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181209,'chemistry','A reaction is second order with respect to a reactant. How is the rate of reaction affected if the concentration of the reactant is doubled?','4 times','Rate = kR²\nIf [A] = 2R, rate = k(2R)² = 4kR² i.e. rate of reaction increases 4 times when concentration is doubled','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','4 times','1/4 times','8 times','1/8 times','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181210,'chemistry','A reaction is second order with respect to a reactant. How is the rate of reaction affected if the concentration of the reactant is reduced to half ?','1/4th times','If [A] = 1/2R , rate = k(R/2)² = kR²/4 , i.e. rate becomes 1/4th when concentration is 1/2.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'1','mcq','1/4th times','4 times','8 times','1/8 times','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181211,'chemistry','In a pseudo first order hydrolysis of ester in water, the following results were obtained : Calculate the average rate of reaction between the time interval 30 to 60 seconds.','4.67 × 10⁻³ mol/Ls','Average rate during the interval 30-60 sec\nRate = - C₂- C₁ / t₂ - t₁ = 0.17-0.31/60-30 =  4.67 × 10⁻³ mol/Ls','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','4.67 × 10⁻³ mol/Ls','4.67 × 10³ mol/Ls','2.34 × 10⁻³ mol/Ls','2.37 × 10³ mol/Ls','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(181212,'chemistry','In a pseudo first order hydrolysis of ester in water, the following results were obtained : Calculate the pseudo first order rate constant for the hydrolysis of ester.','1.98 × 10⁻² /s','Refer diagram(ne1833)','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Exercises',NULL,'2','mcq','1.98 × 10⁻² /s','2 × 10² /s','1.98 × 10⁻⁴ /s','19 × 10⁻² /s','NEW','12-112','12-078','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/181212q.png'),
