(181307,'chemistry','Consider the Arrhenius equation given below and mark the correct option. k =Ae^-Ea/RT','Rate constant increases exponentially with decreasing activation energy and increasing temperature.','K= Ae^-Ea/RT from the equation it is clear that value of rate constant k increases exponentially with decrease in activation energy Ea and increase in temperature.\nAs Ea decrease, -Ea/RT increases and k increase \nAs T increases , -Ea/RT decreases and k increases','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Rate constant increases exponentially with increasing activation energy _ and decreasing temperature.','Rate constant decreases exponentially with increasing activation energy and decreasing temperature.','Rate constant increases exponentially with decreasing activation energy and decreasing temperature.','Rate constant increases exponentially with decreasing activation energy and increasing temperature.','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(181308,'chemistry','A graph of volume of hydrogen released vs time for the reaction between zinc and dil. HCl is shown below. On the basis of this, mark the correct option. Refer diagram(n1843)','c','Average rate of reaction up to 40 seconds on the basis of the graph is C , average rate of the reaction can be determined by change in concentration of H₂ upon from (0,0) to (40,V₃) = V₃-0/40-0 = V₃/40','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-101','12-067','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/181308q.png'),
(181401,'chemistry','A : Rate of reaction depends upon the concentration of the reactants.\nR : The order of reaction can be negative with respect to a substance present in the reaction.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','Rate = -dC/dt α C and for zero order reaction,\n→ order can be positive negative or zero.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-102','12-068',NULL),
(181402,'chemistry','A : NO₂ + CO —slow→ CO₂ + NO\n , Rate = K[NO₂]\n²\nR : Rate does not depend upon [CO] because it is involved in the first step.','Both A and R are false.','R = K[NO₂][CO]\nAll reactant concentration term (in slow state) must be included in Rate law.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-101','12-067',NULL),
(181403,'chemistry','A : If temperature does not affect the rate of reaction, Ea = 0.\nR : Less the activation energy, slower will be the reaction.','A is true but R is false.','Lesser the activation energy, faster will be the rate.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(181404,'chemistry','A : The rate constant of first order reaction is used to calculate population if growth rate is given.\nR : The rate constant is independent of concentration for first order reaction.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','Rate = -dC/dt = kC or k = (2.303/t)log[A₀/Aₜ] and k = 0.693/t₁/₂','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-106','12-072',NULL),
(181405,'chemistry','A : A positive catalyst increases the rate of reaction.\nR : A positive catalyst alters reaction mechanism and decreases activation energy.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Positive catalyst favours rate of reaction by decreasing activation energy.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-119','12-082',NULL),
(181406,'chemistry','A : The molecularity of reaction can never be fractional.\nR : Molecularity is the number of molecules needed to form activated complex, which will never be fractional.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Molecularity means number of molecules participate [in an elementary reaction] in a reaction to form activated\n complex.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-104','12-070',NULL),
(181407,'chemistry','A : The decomposition of gaseous N₂O₅ follows first order kinetics.\nR : The plot of log of its partial pressure versus time is linear with slope, -k\n/2.303 and having intercept equal\n to P.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','k = (2.303/t)log[P₀/Pₜ]\nlogPₜ = -(k/2.303)t + logP₀','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-106','12-072',NULL),
(181408,'chemistry','A : Order and molecularity of a reaction are always equal.\nR : Complex reactions takes place in steps and fastest step determine the molecularity of reaction.','Both A and R are false.','Order can be fractional but molecularity can never be fractional.\nSlowest rate determines the molecularity of reaction.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-101','12-070',NULL),
(181409,'chemistry','A : Rate constant increases with temperature.\nR : Rate of exothermic reaction increases with temperature.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','Rate constant α temperature\nRate always increases with increase in temperature.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(181410,'chemistry','A : Hydrolysis of ester in acidic medium follows first order kinetics.\nR : Hydrolysis of ester is independent of the concentration of acid used.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Hydrolysis of ester is an example of pseudo first order reaction.\n∵ Concentration of acid is large','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-096','12-062',NULL),
(181411,'chemistry','A : The rate constant for zero order reaction is equal to rate of reaction.\nR : t₁/₂  for zero order reaction is directly proportional to initial concentration.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','Rate = KC°\nRate = K\nt₁/₂ = C°/2K','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-111','12-077',NULL),
(181412,'chemistry','A : For exothermic reaction,\n∆H = Ea (forward) – Ea (backward).\nR : The value of activation energy for forward direction is less than activation energy for backward reaction.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','∆H = Ea(forward) – Ea(backward) < 0\n[Ea < Eb]\n[∆H = –ve → exothermic reaction]','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(181413,'chemistry','A : Arrhenius parameter\n (A) = P(steric factor) × z (collision frequency)\nR : On increasing temperature, the value of A increases.','A is true but R is false.','A = P(steric factor) × Z(collision frequency)\nA is constant.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(181414,'chemistry','A : In presence of +ve catalyst, activation energy & threshold energy decreases.\nR : Minimum energy required to permit a reaction is known as threshold energy.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','Positive catalyst favours rate of reaction by decreasing activation energy and threshold energy.\n2nd statement is a definition of threshold energy.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-119','12-082',NULL),
(181415,'chemistry','A : When temperature becomes infinite then the value of rate constant is maximum.\nR : A is also known as maximum rate constant.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','K = Ae^-Ea/RT\nT → infinity\nEa/RT = 0\ne⁰ = 1\nK = A\n[∵ K increases as temperature increases.]\n[Kmax = A = maximum rate constant]','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> Assertion & Reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','NEW','12-112','12-078',NULL),
(181416,'biology','The reasons for angina are?','Conditions gradually affecting the blood flow','Angina occurs due to conditions that affect the blood flow. Heart failure is not the same as cardiac arrest (when the heart stops beating) or a heart attack (when the heart muscle is suddenly damaged by an inadequate blood supply). High blood pressure conditions lead to hypertension(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 288; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Disorders of circulatory system','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Conditions affecting conduction system of the heart','Conditions gradually affecting the blood flow','Conditions causing sudden stopping of the heart due to absence of impulse','Conditions raising the blood pressure of heart','NEW','11-288','11-203',NULL),
(181501,'biology','Erythropoiesis occurs in\n[2015 Cancelled]','Red Bone marrow','Erythropoiesis is the formation of red blood cells. In humans during various stages of life the formation of RBCs takes place in various parts of body. In embryonic stage when the foetus is developing in womb of mother RBCs are manufactured in the mesoderm of yolk sac upto 3 months, after 3 months  liver and spleen are the site of formation, when the foetus completes 4.5 to 5 months erythropoiesis starts in bone marrow along with liver and spleen. In children it occurs in bone marrow, liver and spleen. In adults after 18 to 20 yrs it occurs EXCLUSIVELY in bone marrow.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Spleen','Red Bone marrow','Kidney','Liver','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(250704,'chemistry','Which of the following statement(s) is/are false?\n\n i. Methanol is highly poisonous in nature.\n ii. Ingestion of methanol cam cause blindness.\n iii. Methanol is used in paints, varnishes and for making formaldehyde.\n iv. None of the above.','iv','It is highly poisonous in nature. Ingestion of even small quantities of methanol can cause blindness and large quantities causes even death. Methanol is used as a solvent in paints, varnishes and chiefly for making formaldehyde.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'2','mcq','ii','i and iii','ii and iii','iv','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(181502,'biology','Person with blood group AB is considered as universal recipient because he has [NEET 2014]','Both A and B antigens on RBC but no antibodies in the plasma','Individuals with AB blood group have both antigen A and B on their RBCs, and no antibodies for either of the antigen in their plasma. Type O individuals are without A and B antigens on their RBCs, but have antibodies for both these antigens in their plasma. Individuals with blood group AB can receive blood of A, B or O group, while those with blood group O can donate blood to anyone. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and B antigens on RBC but no antibodies in the plasma','Both A and B antibodies in the plasma','No antigen on RBC and no antibody in the plasma','Both A and B antigens in the plasma but no antibodies.','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(181503,'biology','Which one of the following human organs is often called the “graveyard” of RBCs? [Mains 2012]','Spleen','Spleen is a vertebrate organ, lying behind the stomach, that is basically a collection of lymphoid tissue. Its functions include producing lymphocytes and destroying foreign particles. It acts as a reservoir for RBCs and can regulate the number in circulation. It is also the site for the breakdown of worn out RBCs and thus is known as “graveyard” of RBCs. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Gallbladder','Kidney','Spleen','Liver','NEW','11-279','11-193',NULL),
(181504,'biology','The haemoglobin content per 100 mL of blood of a normal healthy human adult is. [Mains 2010]','12 - 16 gm','A healthy individual has 12-16 gms of haemoglobin in every 100 ml of blood. These molecules play a significant role in transport of respiratory gases. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','5 - 11 gm','25 - 30 gm','17 - 20 gm','12 - 16 gm','NEW','11-279','11-193',NULL),
(181505,'biology','There is no DNA in [AIPMT 2009]','Mature RBCs','RBCs are formed in the red bone marrow in the adults. RBCs are devoid of nucleus in most of the mammals and are biconcave in shape. They have a red coloured, iron containing complex protein called haemoglobin, hence the colour and name of these cells. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Mature RBCs','A mature spermatozoan','Hair root','An enucleated ovum','NEW','11-279','11-193',NULL),
(181506,'biology','Globulins contained in human blood plasma are primarily involved in [AIPMT 2009]','Defence mechanisms of body','Globulins in human blood plasma are primarily involved in defence mechanisms of body. Globulins like immunoglobulins act as antibodies that destroy bacteria, viruses and toxic substances that may enter into the blood from outside. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Osmotic balance of body fluids','O₂ transport in the blood','Clotting of blood','Defence mechanisms of body','NEW','11-279','11-193',NULL),
(181507,'biology','The most active phagocytic white blood cells are [AIPMT 2008]','Neutrophils and monocytes','Phagocytes are cells that are able to engulf and breakdown foreign particles, cell debris and disease producing microorganisms. Neutrophils and monocytes(type of white blood cells) are the most active phagocytic cells. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Eosinophils and lymphocytes','Neutrophils and monocytes','Neutrophils and eosinophils','Lymphocytes and macrophages','NEW','11-279','11-193',NULL),
(181508,'biology','A drop of each of the following, is placed separately on four slides. Which of them will not coagulate? [ AIPMT 2007]','Blood serum','Blood serum is blood plasma from which the fibrin and clotting factors have been removed by centrifugation or vigorous stirring, so that it cannot clot. Serum containing a specific antibody or antitoxin may be used in the treatment or prevention of certain infections. Such serum is generally derived from a non-human mammal (e.g., a horse). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Blood serum','Sample from the thoracic duct of lymphatic system','Whole blood from pulmonary vein','Blood plasma','NEW','11-279','11-193',NULL),
(181509,'biology','You are required to draw blood from a patient and to keep it in a test tube for analysis of blood corpuscles and plasma. You are also provided with the following four types of test tubes. Which of these you will not use for the purpose? [AIPMT 2004]','Test tube containing calcium bicarbonate','Calcium ions cause activation certain inactive factors to active form thus causing coagulation of blood. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 281; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Test tube containing calcium bicarbonate','Chilled test tube','Test tube containing heparin','Test tube containing sugar solution','NEW','11-281','11-196',NULL),
(181510,'biology','The life span of human WBC is approximately [AIPMT 1997]','Less than 10 days','WBCs (also called leukocytes) are rounded or irregular colourless cells with a nucleus. They can change their shape and are capable of amoeboid movement. Leucocytes, formed in lymph nodes and red bone marrow, can produce antibodies and move through the walls of vessels to migrate to the sites of injuries, where they surround and isolate dead tissue, foreign bodies and bacteria. They survive for a few days generally 3-4 days after which they die and get phagocytized in blood, liver and lymph nodes. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Between 20 to 30 days','More than 4 months','Less than 10 days','Between 30 to 40 days','NEW','11-279','11-193',NULL),
(181511,'biology','Vitamin K is required for [AIPMT 1997]','Vitamin K is essential for blood clotting as it is necessary for the synthesis of prothrombin in the liver.','Vitamin K is essential for blood clotting as it is necessary for the synthesis of prothrombin in the liver. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 281; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Change of prothrombin into thrombin','Vitamin K is essential for blood clotting as it is necessary for the synthesis of prothrombin in the liver.','Change of fibrinogen to fibrin','Formation of thromboplastin','NEW','11-281','11-196',NULL),
(250705,'chemistry','___________ is obtained commercially by fermentation.','Ethanol','Ethanol, C₂H₅OH, is obtained commercially by fermentation.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'1','mcq','Ethanol','Methanol','Propanol','Butanol','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(181512,'biology','Component of blood responsible for producing antibodies is [AIPMT 1997]','Lymphocytes','Lymphocytes have a very large, rounded nucleus and scanty cytoplasm. They are nonmotile and nonphagocytic. They secrete antibodies to destroy microbes and their toxins, reject grafts and kill tumour cells. They also help in healing of injuries. Thrombocytes aid in clotting of blood. Monocytes are phagocytic in nature and erythrocytes (RBCs) transport gases in the body. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Thrombocytes','Monocytes','Erythrocytes','Lymphocytes','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181513,'biology','The lymph serves to [AIPMT 1995]','Return the interstitial fluid to the blood','Lymph (also called tissue fluid in the intercellular spaces) is the colourless liquid found within the lymphatic system. An important function of lymph is to return interstitial fluid back to the blood. The interstitial fluid is the filtered form of the blood without the cellular components and plasma proteins. It consists of water containing dissolved materials. It receives CO₂ , nitrogenous waste products, hormones and other synthetic substances from the tissue cells and enters the lymph capillaries to discharge them into blood.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Return the interstitial fluid to the blood','Return the WBCs and RBCs to the lymph nodes','Transport CO₂  to the lungs','Transport O₂ to the brain','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(181514,'biology','The heart sound ‘dup’ is produced when [AIPMT 1994]','Semi-lunar valves at the base of aorta get closed','Second heart sound i.e., dup is caused by the closure of the semilunar valves and marks the end of ventricular systole.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 285; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Mitral valve is closed','Semi-lunar valves at the base of aorta get closed','Tricuspid valve is opened','Mitral valve is opened','NEW','11-285','11-200',NULL),
(181515,'biology','In mammals, which blood vessel would normally carry largest amount of urea? [NEET-I 2016]','Hepatic vein','Hepatic vein carries largest amount of urea. Urea is produced in liver. Hepatic vein transports liver’s deoxygenated blood to heart for oxygenation. A unique vascular connection exists between the digestive tract and liver called hepatic portal system. The hepatic portal vein carries blood from intestine to the liver before it is delivered to the systemic circulation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 286; Paragraph No. 7)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Hepatic vein','Hepatic portal vein','Renal vein','Dorsal aorta','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181516,'biology','Which one of the following animals has two separate circulatory pathways? [NEET 2015]','Whale','Whale is a mammal and in mammals, two separate circulatory pathways are found — systemic circulation and pulmonary circulation. Oxygenated and deoxygenated bloods received by the left and right atria respectively pass on to the left and right ventricles. Thus, oxygenated and deoxygenated bloods are not mixed. This is referred to as double circulation.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Whale','Shark','Frog','Lizard','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(181517,'biology','Fastest distribution of some injectable material/ medicine and with no risk of any kind can be achieved by injecting it into the [Mains 2010]','Veins','Intravenous injection is given for rapid distribution of drugs/substance. Intra-muscular injection is given for producing local effect.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Muscles','Arteries','Veins','Lymph vessels','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181518,'biology','Which vertebrate organ receives only oxygenated blood? [AIPMT 1996]','Spleen','Spleen receives only oxygenated blood from the heart through splenic artery. The liver receives a blood supply from two sources. The first is the hepatic artery which delivers oxygenated blood from the general circulation. The second is the hepatic portal vein delivering deoxygenated blood from the small intestine containing nutrients. The blood flows through the liver tissue to the hepatic cells where many metabolic functions take place. The blood drains out of the liver via the hepatic vein. Gill and lung receive deoxygenated blood as these are the organs where oxygenation of blood takes place. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 286; Paragraph No. 7)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Spleen','Liver','Gill','Lung','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181519,'biology','In veins, valves are present to check backward flow of blood flowing at [AIPMT 1995]','Low pressure','Veins carry blood at low pressure as compared to blood carried by arteries. Arteries carry blood from the heart whose function is to pump blood at high pressure so that blood can reach each and every part of the body. Veins carry blood from tissues to the heart, so they carry blood at low pressure. Valves are present in the veins to prevent backflow of blood due to force of gravity.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 284; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Atmospheric pressure','High pressure','Low pressure','All of these','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181520,'biology','Blood capillaries are made of [AIPMT1993]','Endothelium only','The wall of capillaries is very thin (usually less than one micron) and have numerous minute pores and made up of only endothelium. Exchange of material takes place between blood and tissue fluid across the endothelial membrane of capillaries through active diffusion.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Endothelium, connective tissue and muscle fibres','Endothelium and muscle fibres','Endothelium and connective tissue','Endothelium only','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181521,'biology','Persons with \'AB\' blood group are called as "Universal recipients". This is due to [NEET 2021]','Absence of antibodies, anti-A and anti-B in plasma','Individuals with AB blood group have both antigen A and B on their RBCs, and no antibodies for either of the antigen in their plasma. Type O individuals are without A and B antigens on their RBCs, but have antibodies for both these antigens in their plasma. Individuals with blood group AB can receive blood of A, B or O group, while those with blood group O can donate blood to anyone. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Absence of antibodies, anti-A and anti-B in plasma','Absence of antigens A and B on the surface of RBCs','Absence of antigens A and B in plasma','Presence of antibodies, anti-A and anti-B on RBCs','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(181522,'biology','Which enzyme is responsible for the conversion of inactive fibrinogens to fibrins? [NEET 2021]','Thrombin','Fibrins are formed by the conversion of inactive fibrinogens in the plasma by the enzyme thrombin.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 281; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 1','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Thrombokinase','Thrombin','Renin','Epinephrine','NEW','11-281','11-196',NULL),
(181601,'biology','Wall of blood capillary is formed of [AIPMT 1991]','Endothelial cells','The wall of capillaries is very thin (usually less than one micron) and have numerous minute pores and made up of only endothelium. Exchange of material takes place between blood and tissue fluid across the endothelial membrane of capillaries through active diffusion.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 286; Paragraph No. 6)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Haemocytes','Parietal cells','Endothelial cells','Oxyntic cells','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181602,'biology','Splenic artery arises from [AIPMT 1991]','Coeliac artery','The splenic artery is the blood vessel that supplies oxygenated blood to the spleen. It branches from the coeliac artery and follows a course superior to the pancreas. The coeliac artery is the first major branch of the abdominal aorta and branches from the aorta around the level of the T12 vertebra in humans. It is one of three anterior/ midline branches of the abdominal aorta. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 286; Paragraph No. 7)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Anterior mesenteric artery','Coeliac artery','Posterior mesenteric artery','Intestinal artery','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181603,'biology','How do parasympathetic neural signals affect the working of the heart? [AIPMT 2014]','Reduce both heart rate and cardiac output','A special neural centre in medulla oblongata can moderate the cardiac function through autonomic nervous system (ANS). Neural signals through the sympathetic nerves (part of ANS) can increase the rate of heart beat, the strength of ventricular contraction and thereby the cardiac output. Parasympathetic neural signals (component of ANS) decrease the rate of heart beat, speed of conduction of action potential and thereby the cardiac output.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 287; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Reduce both heart rate and cardiac output','Heart rate is increased without affecting the cardiac output','Both heart rate and cardiac output increase','Heart rate decreases but cardiac output increases','NEW','11-287','11-202',NULL),
(181604,'biology','An adult human with average health has systolic and diastolic pressures as [AIPMT 1998]','120 mm Hg and 80 mm Hg','The temporary rise in blood pressure during the contraction of the heart is called systolic pressure and the temporary fall in blood pressure during relaxation of the heart is called diastolic pressure. Blood pressure is expressed as the ratio of the systolic pressure over the diastolic pressure. For a healthy resting adult person, the average systolic/diastolic pressures are 120/80 mm Hg. Aorta is directly supplied by left ventricle thus, the blood pressure in aorta is highest during systole of left ventricle. During it, left ventricle contracts and pushes blood into aorta. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 287; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','120 mm Hg and 80 mm Hg','50 mm Hg and 80 mm Hg','80 mm Hg and 80 mm Hg','70 mm Hg and 120 mm Hg','NEW','11-287','11-202',NULL),
(181605,'biology','Serum differs from blood in-[NEET-II 2016]','Lacking clotting factors','Serum is the fluid that separates from blood plasma on centrifugation. Serum is essentially similar in composition to plasma but lacks fibrinogen and other substances that are used in the coagulation process. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Lacking globulins','Lacking albumins','Lacking clotting factors','Lacking antibodies','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181606,'biology','A certain road accident patient with unknown blood group needs immediate blood transfusion. His one doctor friend at once offers his blood. What was the blood group of the donor? [AIPMT 2012]','Blood group O','The blood group was O. The person having O blood group is universal donor. It lacks both antigens ‘A’ and ‘B’ thus does not cause agglutination or clumping of blood cells when transfused into person with any of the four blood groups. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Blood group B','Blood group A','Blood group O','Blood group AB','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(181607,'biology','Which one of the following plasma proteins is involved in the coagulation of blood? [AIPMT 2011]','Fibrinogen','Blood plasma is a faint yellow, slightly alkaline and somewhat viscous fluid. The plasma contains a number of proteins: serum albumin, serum globulins, properdin, prothrombin and fibrinogen. Prothrombin and fibrinogen play an important role in blood clotting. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Albumin','Serum amylase','Globulin','Fibrinogen','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181608,'biology','Which type of white blood cells are concerned with the release of histamine and the natural anticoagulant heparin? [AIPMT 2008]','Basophils','Basophils have nucleus which is three-lobed and have less number of coarse granules. Their granules take basic stain and release heparin, histamine and serotonin. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Eosinophils','Monocytes','Neutrophils','Basophils','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181609,'biology','What is correct for blood group O?','No antigens but both a and b antibodies are present','Individuals with AB blood group have both antigen A and B on their RBCs, and no antibodies for either of the antigen in their plasma. Type O individuals are without A and B antigens on their RBCs, but have antibodies for both these antigens in their plasma. Individuals with blood group AB can receive blood of A, B or O group, while those with blood group O can donate blood to anyone. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','No antigens but both a and b antibodies are present','A antigen and b antibody present','Both antigens are absent','A and B antigens and a, b antibodies present','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(250706,'chemistry','Glucose and fructose undergo fermentation in the presence of an enzyme, ____________.','Zymase','Glucose and fructose undergo fermentation in the presence of another enzyme, zymase.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'1','mcq','Zymase','Invertase','Glycosidase','Carbohydrase','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(181610,'biology','Child death may occur in the marriage of- [AIPMT 1988]','RH⁺ man and Rh– woman','Rh factor plays a crucial role in child’s birth born out of a marriage between Rh– woman and a RH⁺ man. In such a case, the mother becomes sensitive while carrying a first RH⁺ child within her womb. The reason for such sensitivity is that some of the RBCs from the developing embryo get into the bloodstream of the mother during development, causing her to produce anti-Rh antibodies. In fact, the first child of such parents is nearly normal and is delivered safely. However, if such a mother gets pregnant again, the subsequent RH⁺ foetuses will be exposed to the anti-Rh antibodies produced by the mother. As a result serious damage to the red blood cells of the developing embryo will occur causing haemolytic disease of the newborn (HDN) or erythroblastosis fetalis. This disease leads to the death of the developing embryo before birth or after parturition. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 281; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','RH⁺ man and RH⁺ woman','RH⁺ man and Rh– woman','Rh– man and Rh– woman','Rh– man and RH⁺ woman','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181611,'biology','Which of the following statements is true for lymph? [AIPMT 2002]','Blood – RBCs and some proteins','As the blood passes through the capillaries in tissues, some water along with many small water soluble substances move out into the spaces between the cells of tissues leaving the larger proteins and most of the formed elements in the blood vessels. This fluid released out is called the interstitial fluid or tissue fluid. An elaborate network of vessels called the lymphatic system collects this fluid and drains it back to the major veins. The fluid present in the lymphatic system is called the lymph. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','WBC + serum','Blood – RBCs and some proteins','RBCs + WBCs + plasma','RBCs + proteins + platelets','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(181612,'biology','What would be the heart rate of a person if the cardiac output is 5 L, blood volume in the ventricles at the end of diastole is 100 mL and at the end of ventricular systole is 50 mL ? [NEET 2019]','100 beats per minute','The stroke volume multiplied by the heart rate (no. of beats per min.) gives the cardiac output. \nSo End Systolic volume is 50 ml(100ml -50 ml) and \n5L/50ml= 5000/50= 100 beats(every minute);\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 285; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','125 beats per minute','50 beats per minute','75 beats per minute','100 beats per minute','NEW','11-285','11-200',NULL),
(181613,'biology','Blood pressure in the mammalian aorta is maximum during- [AIPMT 2011]','Systole of the left ventricle','The temporary rise in blood pressure during the contraction of the heart is called systolic pressure and the temporary fall in blood pressure during relaxation of the heart is called diastolic pressure. Blood pressure is expressed as the ratio of the systolic pressure over the diastolic pressure. For a healthy resting adult person, the average systolic/diastolic pressures are 120/80 mmHg. Aorta is directly supplied by left ventricle thus, the blood pressure in aorta is highest during systole of left ventricle. During it, left ventricle contracts and pushes blood into aorta. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 287; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Systole of the left ventricle','Diastole of the right atrium','Systole of the left atrium','Diastole of the right ventricle','NEW','11-287','11-202',NULL),
(181614,'biology','In a standard ECG which one of the following alphabets is the correct representation of the respective activity of the human heart? [AIPMT 2009]','P - depolarisation of the atria','In the given diagram the P-wave represents the electrical excitation (or depolarisation) of the atria, which leads to the contraction of both the atria. The QRS complex represents the depolarisation of the ventricles, which initiates the ventricular contraction. The contraction starts shortly after Q and marks the beginning of the systole. The T-wave represents the return of the ventricles from excited to normal state (repolarisation). The end of the T-wave marks the end of systole. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 286; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','S - start of systole','T - end of diastole','P - depolarisation of the atria','R - repolarization of ventricles','NEW','11-287','11-202',NULL),
(181615,'biology','Bundle of His is a network of [AIPMT 2003]','Muscle fibres found only in the ventricle wall','Bundle of His is a part of heart. A bundle of nodal fibres, atrioventricular bundle (AV bundle), continues from the atrioventricular node (AVN) and passes through the atrioventricular septa. It emerges on the top of the interventricular septum and immediately divides into a right and left bundle, which give rise to minute fibres throughout the ventricular musculature of the respective sides called Purkinje fibres. These fibres along with right and left bundles are known as Bundle of His. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 284; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Muscle fibres distributed throughout the heart walls','Muscle fibres found only in the ventricle wall','Nerve fibres distributed in ventricles','Nerve fibres found throughout the heart','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181616,'biology','Impulse of heart beat originates from [AIPMT 2002]','SA node','SA (sinoatrial) node is a specialised bundle of neurons located in the upper part of the right atrium of the heart. SA node is the natural cardiac pacemaker from which the heartbeat originates. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 284; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','SA node','AV node','Vagus nerve','Cardiac nerve','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181617,'biology','Tricuspid valve is found in between [AIPMT 1989]','Right auricle and right ventricle','Tricuspid valve is the valve in the heart between the right atrium and right ventricle. It consists of three cusps that channel the flow of blood from the atrium to the ventricle. When the right ventricle contracts, forcing blood into the pulmonary artery, the tricuspid valve closes the aperture to the atrium, thereby preventing any backflow of blood. The valve reopens to allow blood to flow from the atrium into the ventricle. Thus, if tricuspid valve is partially non-functional the flow of blood into the pulmonary artery will be reduced (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 283; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Sinus venosus and right auricle','Right auricle and right ventricle','Left ventricle and left auricle','Ventricle and aorta','NEW','11-283','11-198',NULL),
(250707,'chemistry','Enzyme, ____________ is found in yeast.','Zymase','Glucose and fructose undergo fermentation in the presence of another enzyme, zymase, which is found in yeast.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'1','mcq','Zymase','Invertase','Glycosidase','Carbohydrase','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(181618,'biology','Blood pressure in the pulmonary artery is [NEET-I 2016]','More than that in the pulmonary vein','As the ventricular pressure increases further, the semilunar valves guarding the pulmonary artery (right side) and the aorta (left side) are forced open, allowing the blood in the ventricles to flow through these vessels into the circulatory pathways. As a result the travelled pressure is greater in the pulmonary artery.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 285; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','More than that in the pulmonary vein','Less than that in the venae cavae','Same as that in the aorta','More than that in the carotid','NEW','11-285','11-200',NULL),
(181619,'biology','Arteries are best defined as the vessels which [AIPMT 2011]','Carry blood away from the heart to different organs','Arteries and veins are main blood vessels. Arteries carry blood from the heart to different body parts. Veins bring blood from different body parts to the heart. The blood flows strictly by a fixed route through Blood Vessels—the arteries and veins. All the arteries except the pulmonary artery carry oxygenated blood away from heart and all the veins except the pulmonary veins carry deoxygenated blood towards the heart.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 286; Paragraph No. 6)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Supply oxygenated blood to the different organs','Carry blood away from the heart to different organs','Break up into capillaries which reunite to form a vein','Carry blood from one visceral organ to another visceral organ','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181620,'biology','Difference between pulmonary artery and pulmonary vein is that, the pulmonary artery has [AIPMT 2000]','Thicker walls','An artery has thick and more elastic wall but its lumen is narrow as compared to vein. Pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs for oxygenation. Pulmonary vein carries oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left auricle. Basically, each artery and vein consists of three layers: an inner lining of squamous endothelium, the tunica intima, a middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibres, the tunica media, and an external layer of fibrous connective tissue with collagen fibres, the tunica externa. The tunica media is comparatively thin in the veins. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 286; Paragraph No. 6)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','No endothelium','Valves','Thicker walls','Oxygenated blood','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181621,'biology','Arteries carry oxygenated blood except [AIPMT 1989]','Pulmonary','Pulmonary artery carries the blood from the right ventricle of the heart to the lungs for oxygenation so it carries deoxygenated blood. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 286; Paragraph No. 7)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Pulmonary','Cardiac','Hepatic','Systemic','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181622,'biology','Which one of the following statements is correct regarding blood pressure? [AIPMT 2011]','190/110 mm Hg may harm vital organs like brain and kidney','Hypertension is the term for blood pressure that is higher than normal (120/80). In this measurement 120 mm Hg (millimetres of mercury pressure) is the systolic, or pumping, pressure and 80 mm Hg is the diastolic, or resting, pressure. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 287; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','130/90 mm Hg is considered high and requires treatment','100/55 mm Hg is considered an ideal blood pressure','105/50 mm Hg makes one very active','190/110 mm Hg may harm vital organs like brain and kidney','NEW','11-287','11-202',NULL),
(181623,'biology','The thickening of walls of arteries is called  [ AIPMT 1999]','Arteriosclerosis','Arteriosclerosis is the hardening of arteries and arterioles due to thickening of the fibrous tissue, and the consequent loss of elasticity. In this disease, calcium salts precipitate with the cholesterol. This calcification ultimately makes the wall of arteries stiff and rigid.Coronary Artery Disease (CAD): Coronary Artery Disease, often referred to as atherosclerosis, affects the vessels that supply blood to the heart muscle. It is caused by deposits of calcium, fat, cholesterol and fibrous tissues, which makes the lumen of arteries narrower. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 288; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Arteriosclerosis','Arthritis','Aneurysm','Both (b) and (c)','NEW','11-288','11-203',NULL),
(181624,'biology','Which of the following blood group can be given to any patient ? [AIPMT 1999]','O','Blood group ‘O’ is universal donor. Group ‘O’ blood, with no antigens, can be given to anyone. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Table 18.1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','A','B','O','AB','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(181625,'biology','Thickening of arteries due to cholesterol deposition is [AIPMT 2007]','arteriosclerosis','High proportion of cholesterol in blood leads to deposition of cholesterol on the walls of blood vessels. This causes the arteries to lose their elasticity and get stiffened. This is called arteriosclerosis or hardening of arteries. Coronary Artery Disease (CAD): Coronary Artery Disease, often referred to as atherosclerosis, affects the vessels that supply blood to the heart muscle. It is caused by deposits of calcium, fat, cholesterol and fibrous tissues, which makes the lumen of arteries narrower. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 288; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','arteriosclerosis','arthritis','aneurysm','cardiac arrest','NEW','11-288','11-203',NULL),
(181626,'biology','A man whose blood group is not known meets with a serious accident and needs blood transfusion immediately, which one of the blood groups readily available in the hospital will be safe for transfusion? [AIPMT 2010]','O, Rh-','According to ABO system of blood grouping, O type blood can be given to person of all types of blood, i.e., O, A, B and AB. Hence, a person with O type blood is called universal donor. According to Rh system of blood grouping, most of the people have blood in which there is a substance called Rh factor. Rh stands for rhesus. This type of blood can be donated only in rarest of rare chances. In India, about 97% of people are RH⁺ . So, if an RH⁺ person is transfused with RH⁺ blood, then it is safe. But if an Rh negative (Rh– ) person is transfused with RH⁺ blood then he/she will develop anti Rh factor i.e., antibodies in his/her blood, and there might be no harm is, but may kill the recipient if a second RH⁺ transfusion is done.But in case a RH⁺ person is transfused with Rh- blood, there will be reaction as the donor plasma is devoid of the Rh factor and recipient plasma is devoid of Anti-Rh antibodies.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','O, Rh-','O, RH⁺','AB, Rh–','AB, RH⁺','NEW','11-280','11-179',NULL),
(181627,'biology','With Reference   to the blood in a normal person, which one of the following statements is correct? [AIPMT 2010]','The blood has more platelets than WBC','The number of blood platelets per cubic mm in human blood is 3 lacs while the number of WBCs are 5000/cubic mm of blood. Veins are as complex as the arteries. Veins and arteries both are types of blood vessels. Arteries carry blood from heart to different organs while vein carries blood from different organs to heart. At any given time in a healthy human, the blood amount is same in both, as the circulation of blood never stops.Blood consists of two parts:The plasma (water, proteins, inorganic salts and other elements) constitutes 55-60% of blood while cellular part constitutes 40-45% of total blood. WBCs are produced in red bone marrow, lymph nodes and sometimes even in liver and spleen. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279 and 280)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Compared to arteries, veins are less numerous and hold less of the body’s blood at any given time','Blood cells constitute about 70 percent of the total volume of the blood','White blood cells (WBC) are made by lymph nodes only','The blood has more platelets than WBC','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181628,'biology','A vein possesses a large lumen because [AIPMT 1990]','Tunica media is a thin coat','Wall of a vein consists of tunica externa, tunica media and tunica interna. All these layers are also present in the wall of artery. However, in the wall of a vein, the elastic membrane of tunica interna is relatively thin, and muscle fibres and elastic fibres in tunica media are fewer. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 286; Paragraph No. 6)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Tunica media and tunica externa form a single coat','Tunica interna and tunica media form a single coat','Tunica interna, tunica media and tunica externa are thin','Tunica media is a thin coat','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181629,'biology','In veins, valves are present to check backward flow of blood flowing at [AIPMT 1995]','Low pressure','Veins carry blood at low pressure as compared to blood carried by arteries. Arteries carry blood from the heart whose function is to pump blood at high pressure so that blood can reach each and every part of the body. Veins carry blood from tissues to the heart, so they carry blood at low pressure. Valves are present in the veins to prevent backflow of blood due to force of gravity. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 284; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Atmospheric pressure','High pressure','Low pressure','All of these','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181630,'biology','Rate of heart beat is determined by [AIPMT 1999]','SA node','The SAN can generate the maximum number of action potentials, i.e., 70-75 /min  , and is responsible for initiating and maintaining the rhythmic contractile activity of the heart. Therefore, it is called the pacemaker. Our heart normally beats 70-75 times in a minute (average 72 beats /min ). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 284; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Purkinje fibres','Papillary muscles','AV node','SA node','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181631,'biology','Given below are two statements :\n\nStatement I : The coagulum is formed of network of threads called thrombins.\n\nStatement II : Spleen is the graveyard of erythrocytes.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, \nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below[NEET 2022]','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct, because a coagulum is composed of a network of threads called fibrins, and spleen is the graveyard of RBCs as RBCs get destroyed there.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','NEW','11-281','11-196',NULL),
(181632,'biology','Which one of the following statements is correct?[NEET 2022]','Blood moves freely from atrium to the ventricle during joint diastole.','The SA node generates an action potential to stimulate atrial contraction,The tricuspid and the bicuspid valves open due to the pressure exerted by the blood present in the atria and decrease in ventricular pressure, Decrease in ventricular pressure causes closing of the semilunar valves','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','The atrio-ventricular node (AVN) generates an action potential to stimulate atrial contraction','The tricuspid and the bicuspid valves open due to the pressure exerted by the simultaneous contraction of the atria','Blood moves freely from atrium to the ventricle during joint diastole.','Increased ventricular pressure causes closing of the semilunar valves','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181633,'biology','Match List-I with List-II\n[NEET 2022]','Glycogen- Storage product, Globulin-Antibody,Steroids-Hormone,Thrombin-Biocatalyst','Glycogen is a storage form of carbohydrate in the liver,\nGlobulin(immunoglobulin) are protein components of Antibodies,,\nSteroids are mostly hormones examples are Testosterone, cortisol, estrogen and progestrone\nThrombin is a clotting factor that converts fibrinogen to active fibrin.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','match','Glycogen, Storage product','Globulin, Antibody','Steroids,Hormone','Thrombin,Biocatalyst','NEW','11-281','11-196',NULL),
(181634,'biology','Which of the following statements are correct?\nA. Basophils are most abundant cells of the total WBCs\nB. Basophils secrete histamine, serotonin and heparin\nC. Basophils are involved in inflammatory response\nD. Basophils have kidney shaped nucleus\nE. Basophils are agranulocytes\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(2) B and C only','Basophils (0.5 to 1%) are granulocytes with S-shape nuclei and it secretes histamine, heparin and serotonin etc.,','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','(1) C and E only','(2) B and C only','(3) A and B only','(4) D and E only','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181635,'biology','Arrange the following formed elements in the decreasing order of their abundance in blood in humans : \n (a) Platelets (b) Neutrophils \n (c) Erythrocytes (d) Eosinophils (NEET 2022)\n (e) Monocytes \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below : [ RE NEET 2022]','(1) (c), (a), (b), (e), (d)','Neutrophils: 40% to 60%\nLymphocytes: 20% to 40%\nMonocytes: 2% to 8%\nEosinophils: 1% to 4%\nBasophils: 0.5% to 1%','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','(1) (c), (a), (b), (e), (d)','(2) (c), (b), (a), (e), (d)','(3) (d), (e), (b), (a), (c)','(4) (a), (c), (b), (d), (e)','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(250708,'chemistry','Name the enzyme which involves in the given reaction.\ndiagram(n2568)','Zymase','Glucose and fructose undergo fermentation in the presence of another enzyme, zymase, which is found in yeast.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'3','mcq','Invertase','Zymase','Amylase','Maltase','NEW','12-344','12-214','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/250708q.png'),
(181636,'biology','Math List I with List II.\nList I - List II \nA. P-wave - I. Beginning of systole\nB. Q-wave - II. Repolarisation of ventricles\nC. QRS complex - III. Depolarisation of atria\nD. T-wave - IV. Depolarisation of ventricles\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023]','(4) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','(1) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','(2) A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','(3) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','(4) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181637,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \nList-I List-II\n(A) Eosinophils (I) 6 – 8%\n(B) Lymphocytes (II) 2 – 3%\n(C) Neutrophils (III) 20 – 25%\n(D) Monocytes (IV) 60 – 65 %\n Choose the correct answer from the options given \nbelow : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) A-(II), B-(III), C-(IV), D-(I)','A-(II), B-(III), C-(IV), D-(I)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','(1) A-(IV), B-(I), C-(II), D-(III)','(2) A-(IV), B-(I), C-(III), D-(II)','(3) A-(II), B-(III), C-(IV), D-(I)','(4) A-(II), B-(III), C-(I), D-(IV)','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181638,'biology','Match the following columns and select the correct option 1. Eosinophils 2. Basophils 3. Neutrophils 4. lymphocytes (i) Immune response (ii) phagocytosis (iii) Release histaminase, destructive enzymes (iv) Release granules containing histamine (NEET 2020)','1-(iii) 2- (iv) 3- (ii) 4 (i)','Lymphocytes (20-25%) are of two major types B and T Lymphocytes. Both B and T Lymphocytes are responsible for immune responses of the body.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','match','1-(i) 2- (iv) 3- (iii) 4 (ii)','1-(i) 2- (ii) 3- (iii) 4 (iv)','1-(ii) 2- (iii) 3- (i) 4 (iv)','1-(iii) 2- (iv) 3- (ii) 4 (i)','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181639,'biology','Bundle of His is a part of which one of the following organs in humans','Heart','.The action potential is conducted to the ventricular side by the AVN and AV bundle from where the bundle of His transmits it through the entire ventricular musculature.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','1','mcq','Heart','kidney','Pancreas','brain','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181640,'biology','Inadequate supply of oxygen to heart muscles leads to symptom of acute chest pain. This disorder of circulatory system is defined as','Angina pectoris','Angina: It is also called ‘angina pectoris’. A symptom of acute chest pain appears when no enough oxygen is reaching the heart muscle. Angina can occur in men and women of any age but it is more common among the middle-aged and elderly. It occurs due to conditions that affect the blood flow.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Angina pectoris','Cardiac arrest','Heart Failure','Coronary Heart disease','NEW','11-288','11-203',NULL),
(181641,'biology','Match the following columns and select the correct option 1. Eosinophils 2. Basophils 3. Neutrophils 4. lymphocytes (i) Immune response (ii) phagocytosis (iii) Release histaminase, destructive enzymes (iv) Release granules containing histamine (NEET 2020)','1-(iii) 2- (iv) 3- (ii) 4 (i)','Lymphocytes (20-25%) are of two major types B and T Lymphocytes. Both B and T Lymphocytes are responsible for immune responses of the body.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','1-(i) 2- (iv) 3- (iii) 4 (ii)','1-(i) 2- (ii) 3- (iii) 4 (iv)','1-(ii) 2- (iii) 3- (i) 4 (iv)','1-(iii) 2- (iv) 3- (ii) 4 (i)','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181642,'biology','Which one of the following is correct  (NEET 2015)','3. Blood = Plasma + RBC + WBC + platelets','Plasma is a straw coloured, viscous fluid constituting nearly 55 per cent of the blood. 90-92 per cent of plasma is water and proteins contribute 6-8 per cent of it. Fibrinogen, globulins and albumins are the major proteins. Fibrinogens are needed for clotting or coagulation of blood. Globulins primarily are involved in defense mechanisms of the body and the albumins help in osmotic balance. (Reference: NCERT Class XI page 279\' para 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','1. Serum= Blood+ Fibrinogen','2. Lymph= Plasma + RBC + WBC','3. Blood = Plasma + RBC + WBC + platelets','4. Plasma = Blood-lymphocytes','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181643,'biology','Adult human RBC\'s are enucleate. Which of the following statement(s) is/are most appropriate explanation for this feature?\n\n\n(a) They do not need to reproduce.\n(b) They are somatic cells.\n(c) They do not metabolize.\n(d) All their internal space is available for oxygen transport.','a, c and d','The circular mammalian erythrocyte is synthesized in the bone marrow. After synthesis, it undergoes a process called enucleation in which the nucleus is removed. The absence of a nucleus allows the red blood cells to contain more hemoglobin and hence all their internal space is available for oxygen transport in order to the body tissues. As they do not have any organelle, it does not show any metabolic activity and multiplication','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','a, c and d','only a','only d','b and c','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181702,'biology','Assertion : Persons suffering from haemophilia fail to produce blood clotting factor VIII. \n\nReason : Prothrombin producing platelets in such persons are found in very low concentration.\n[AIIMS 2005]','Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Haemophilia is caused by lack of activity of blood clotting factor VIII or IX and they show platelet function disorder. Platelets also called thrombocytes, are cell fragments produced from megakaryocytes (special cells in the bone marrow). They are called so because their major role is to form thrombus or a clot.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 280; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(181703,'biology','Assertion : Smaller the organism higher is the rate of metabolism per gram weight. \n\nReason : The heart rate of a six month old baby is much higher than that of an old person. \n[AIIMS 2007]','Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','The basal metabolic rate is defined as the energy requirement of human body at rest. BMR of smallest animals are generally higher than larger animals. Peoples with higher metabolism means that they have higher heart rate. Heart rate of baby is 70-190 times/minute, whereas adults (including serious) is 60-100 times/ minute. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 284; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181704,'biology','Assertion : Blood pressure is arterial blood pressure. \n\nReason : Blood pressure is measured by sphygmomanometer.\n [AIIMS 2000]','Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Blood pressure is the arterial pressure of blood exerted on the wall of arteries with each heartbeat. It is measured form the brachial artery in the elbow pit. It is expressed as systolic pressure (mm/Hg) diastolic pressure (mm/Hg) Arterial (superficial; arteries) blood pressure is measured by sphygmomanometer.  In this measurement 120 mm Hg (millimetres of mercury pressure) is the systolic, or pumping, pressure and 80 mm Hg is the diastolic, or resting, pressure. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 287; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-287','11-202',NULL),
(181705,'biology','Assertion : Blood is not red coloured in the insects. \n\nReason : Insect blood has no role in O₂ transport. \n[AIIMS 2012, 2013]','Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Insect blood is not red colored as there are no RBCs in the insect blood. It does not play any role in transport of oxygen. Insects have tracheal respiration. RBCs are formed in the red bone marrow in the adults. RBCs are devoid of nucleus in most of the mammals and are biconcave in shape. They have a red coloured, iron containing complex protein called haemoglobin, hence the colour and name of these cells. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-279','11-194','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/181705q.png'),
(181706,'biology','Assertion : Lub is a heart sound which is produced during each cardiac cycle. \n\nReason : It is associated with the closure of the tricuspid and bicuspid valves. \n[AIIMS 2016]','Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Lub and dub are two heart sounds, which occurs due to the closure of cuspid valves and semilunar valves respectively. Lub is the first heart sound which is formed due to closure of atrioventricular valves at the beginning of ventricular systole. It is low pitched of long duration (0.15 sec).(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 285; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-285','11-200','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/181706q.png'),
(181707,'biology','Assertion: WBCs accumulate at site of wounds by diapedesis. \n\nReason: It is squeezing of leucocytes from endothelium. \n[AIIMS 2002]','Assertion and Reason are correct, Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','WBC’s are wandering cells capable of coming out of blood capillaries by amoeboid movement called diapedesis.We have two main categories of WBCs – granulocytes and agranulocytes. Neutrophils, eosinophils and basophils are different types of granulocytes, while lymphocytes and monocytes are the agranulocytes. Neutrophils and monocytes (6-8 %) are phagocytic cells which destroy foreign organisms entering the body. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Assertion and Reason are correct, Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181708,'biology','Assertion : When there is a fall in the blood pressure due to loss of blood volume, this is compensated by vasoconstriction of veins. \n\nReason : Veins hold the extra amount of blood which can be shifted to the arteries as required. \n[AIIMS 2010, 2015]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','When the blood pressure of an individual decreases due to loss of blood volume, then vasoconstriction of veins occurs. This shifts the little amount of blood from veins to arteries.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-287','11-202',NULL),
(181709,'biology','RBCs do not occur in','cockroach','RBCs do not occur in blood of cockroach. It contains haemocoel immersed in blood called haemolymph','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Frog','cow','camel','cockroach','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181710,'biology','Anti-serum contains [AIIMS 1997]','Antibodies','Anti-serum is a blood serum that contains antibodies. Serum is the fluid that separates from blood plasma on centrifugation. Serum is essentially similar in composition to plasma but lacks fibrinogen and other substances that are used in the coagulation process. But it contains the antibodies.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Antigens','Antibodies','Leucocytes','RBCs','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181711,'biology','Which enzyme induces lysis of fibrinogen to fibrin during clotting mechanism ?','Thrombin','During blood clotting, lysis of fibrinogen to fibrin occurs by plasma thrombin. Later on fibrin entangles corpuscles to form a clot. Thrombin is not normally found in circulating blood, but instead it is present in its inactive precursor prothrombin. The conversion of prothrombin to thrombin requires blood platelets, calcium ions and thromboplastin.Note, lysis of fibrinogen requires thrombin, but lysing of fibrin requires plasmin. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 281; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Plasmin','Thrombin','Fibrin','Trypsin','NEW','11-283','11-198',NULL),
(181712,'biology','Which of the following chamber of heart has the thickest muscular wall ? [AIIMS 1999]','Left ventricle','Left ventricle of the heart has the thickest wall because it has to pump the blood to the farthest end of the body.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Left auricle','Left ventricle','Right ventricle','Right auricle','NEW','11-283','11-198',NULL),
(181713,'biology','In which of the following pairs the two items mean one and the same thing? [AIIMS 2004]','SA node - Pacemaker','Malleus is hammer shaped; lymphocytes are type of leukocytes and hemophilia is bleeder’s disease (genetic) disorder). SA node acts as pacemaker because it functions as originator of cardiac impulse. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 284; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Malleus - Anvil','SA node - Pacemaker','Leukocytes - Lymphocytes','Haemophilia - Blood cancer','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181714,'biology','An artificial pacemaker is implanted subcutaneously and connected to the heart in patients [AIIMS 2004]','With irregularity in the heart rhythm','Artificial pacemaker is implanted to the patients where heart beat level drops abnormally low (30-40) due to disease or operations. The SAN can generate the maximum number of action potentials, i.e., 70-75 /min  , and is responsible for initiating and maintaining the rhythmic contractile activity of the heart. Therefore, it is called the natural pacemaker. Our heart normally beats 70-75 times in a minute (average 72 beats /min ). If it fails, an artificial pacemaker may be needed.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 284; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Having 90% blockage of the three main coronary arteries','Having a very high blood pressure','With irregularity in the heart rhythm','Suffering from arteriosclerosis','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181715,'biology','Hirudin is [AIIMS 2006]','A protein produced from transgenic Brassica napus, which prevents blood clotting','Hirudin is an anticlotting agent produced by transgenic Brassica napus(mustard). Prothrombin and fibrinogen play an important role in blood clotting. Hirudin acts by preventing the clotting factors like the above(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','A protein produced by Hordeum vulgare, which is rich in lysine','A toxic molecule isolated from Gossypium hirsutum, which reduces human fertility','A protein produced from transgenic Brassica napus, which prevents blood clotting','An antibiotic produced by a genetically engineered bacterium, Escherichia coli','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181716,'biology','The component of blood which prevents its coagulation in the blood vessels is [AIIMS 2007]','Heparin','Blood contains an anticoagulant called heparin, which prevents the activation of prothrombin. The latter is an inactive globulin which is required during blood coagulation. Heparin is released from the mast-cell granules (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Haemoglobin','Plasma','Thrombin','Heparin','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181717,'biology','A malfunction of the lymph nodes would most likely interfere with the [AIIMS 2009]','Filtering of bacteria from the lymph','The lymphatic system not only regulates fluid amounts in the blood but also helps to fight infection. The lymph nodes produce white blood cells and filter harmful bacteria, that is why they tend to enlarge when we have an infection. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 282; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Release of  CO₂  into the lymph','Filtering of glucose from the lymph','Release of O₂ into the lymph','Filtering of bacteria from the lymph','NEW','11-282','11-197',NULL),
(181718,'biology','Which of the following organs is the blood bank? [AIIMS 2011]','Spleen','Spleen is called as the blood bank of our body because it holds the reserve of blood in case of haemorragic shock. It is the largest lymphatic organ that acts as an emergency blood bank for our body and also help remove debris from the blood stream.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Heart','Lungs','Spleen','Liver','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181719,'biology','Bulk of  CO₂ released from body tissues into the blood is present as [AIIMS 2015]','Bicarbonate in blood plasma and RBCs','Plasma also contains small amounts of minerals like Na+ , Ca++, Mg++, HCO3 – , Cl– , etc. Glucose, amino acids, lipids, etc., are also present in the plasma as they are always in transit in the body. Factors for coagulation or clotting of blood are also present in the plasma in an inactive form. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 279; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','70% carbamino-haemoglobin and 30% as bicarbonate','Carbamino-haemoglobin in RBCs','Bicarbonate in blood plasma and RBCs','Both b and c','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181720,'biology','Which one of the following is a matching pair of a certain body feature and its value/count in a normal human adult?\n [AIIMS 2003, 2008, 2011]','Blood sugar (fasting)- 70-100 mg/100 ml','The normal range of urea in the blood is 6-20 mg/dL (2.5 to 7.1 mmol/L).\nThe total blood volume in an average adult body is 4.7 - 5.5 Litres.\nErythrocyte Sedimentation Rate (ESR) is the rate at which Red blood cells sediment in one hour. The normal range of ESR in Wintrobe method is 0-9 mm/hr for males and 0-20 mm/hr for females.\nThe normal range of blood sugar level (fasting) for a non-diabetic person is 70 mg/100 ml.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Urea- 5-10 mg / 100 ml of blood','Blood sugar (fasting)- 70-100 mg/100 ml','Total blood volume- 5-6 litres','ESR in Wintrobe method- 9-15 mm in males and 20-34 mm in females','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181721,'biology','Match the items given in column I with those in Column II and select correct options ggiven below:a. Fibrinogen b. Globulin c. albumin  i. Osmotic balance ii. blood clotting iii. defence mechanism','4. a-ii, b-iii, c-i','Fibrinogens are needed for clotting of blood, globulins help in defence of body against pathogens. Albumin maintain osmotic balance. (NCERT Class 11 pg 279)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','match','. a-iii, b-ii, c-i','2. a-ii, b-i, c-iii','3. a-i, b-ii, c-iii','4. a-ii, b-iii, c-i','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181722,'biology','The correct statement in respect of protein haemoglobin that it maintains','2. Acts as an oxygen carrier in the blood','A healthy individual has 12-16 gms of haemoglobin in every 100 ml of blood. These molecules play a significant role in transport of respiratory gases. RBCs have an average life span of 120 days after which they are destroyed in the spleen (graveyard of RBCs).','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','1','mcq','1. Maintains blood sugar level','2. Acts as an oxygen carrier in the blood','3. Forms antibodies and offers resistance to diseases','4. Functions as a catalyst to biological reactions.','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(250709,'chemistry','In wine making, grapes are the source of?','Both a and b','In wine making, grapes are the source of sugars and yeast.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'1','mcq','Sugars','Yeast','Oxygen','Both a and b','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(181723,'biology','Figure shows a schematic plan of blood circulation in humans with labels A to D. Identify labels and give its functions (1811) (AIPMT 2013)','2. C Vena cava- takes blood from parts to right auricle lungs pO2 = 45mm Hg','NCERT class XI figure 18.4 286-287 para 5)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','1. B. pulmonary artery- takes blood from heart to lungs pO2 = 90mm Hg','2. C Vena cava- takes blood from parts to right auricle lungs pO2 = 45mm Hg','3. D-Dorsal aorta-takes blood from heart to body parts pO2 = 95mm Hg','4. A- pulmonary vein - takes impure blood from body parts pO2 = 60mm Hg','NEW','11-287','11-202','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/181723q.png'),
(181801,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \nA: Blood is a specialised muscular tissue. \n\nR: Blood is composed of fluid matrix, plasma and skeletal muscle fibres.','Both A and R are false','Blood is a specialised connective tissue composed of fluid matrix, plasma and formed elements such as RBCs, WBCs, Platelets, etc. They do not contain muscular elements. (NCERT class 11 pg 278)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-278','11-193','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/181801q.png'),
(181802,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Lymph is a body fluid that helps in transport of substances. \n\nR: Lymph travels through blood vessels all over the body.','A is true but R is false','Lymph is a body fluid responsible for transporting substances through the interstitial spaces. Howeer lymph does not travel through blood vessels all over the body. There are specific vessels called lymphatic channels through which they flow. (NCERT Class 11 pg 278)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-278','11-193','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/181802q.png'),
(181803,'biology','Read the following statements about BLOOD;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Plasma is a red colored, thin fluid. \n\n2. 50% of plasma is constituted by water.\n\n3. Fibrinogen, globulins and albumin are important proteins in plasma.','Only 3 is correct','Plasma is a straw coloured viscous fluid. 90-92% of plasma is constituted by water. (NCERT Class 11 pg 278)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','NEW','11-278','11-193','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/181803q.png'),
(181804,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Blood helps to transport essential nutrients to cells of the human body. \n\nR: Blood helps to remove waste material from cells','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','Blood is a specialised connective tissue responsible for provision of essential nutrients to cells and removal of waste from cells in the body of higher organisms. (NCERT Class 11 pg 278)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-278','11-193','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/181804q.png'),
(181805,'biology','Read the following statements about proteins in blood; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Fibrinogens are needed for defense mechanism of body. \n\n2. Globulins helps to clot blood. \n\n3. Albumin maintain osmotic balance.','Only 3 is correct','Fibrinogens are needed for clotting of blood, globulins help in defence of body against pathogens. Albumin maintain osmotic balance. (NCERT Class 11 pg 279)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 is correct','Only 3 is incorrect','Only 3 is correct','NEW','11-279','11-194','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/181805q.png'),
(181806,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Plasma contains activated clotting factors in physiological conditions. \n\nR: This is to prevent clotting of blood during bleeding.','Both A and R are false','Plasma contains clotting factors in inactivated form in normal physiological conditions. This prevents clotting of blood when there is no bleeding. (NCERT Class 11 pg 279)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-279','11-194','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/181806q.png'),
(181807,'biology','Read the following statements about FORMED ELEMENTS;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Normal value of RBCs in human blood is 3-3.5 million/ul of blood. \n\n2. Platelets helps defend the body against pathogens. \n\n3. RBC are anucleate bi-concave cells.','Only 3 is correct','RBCs are devoid of nucleus in most of the mammals\nand are biconcave in shape. Normal value of RBCs is 5-5.5 million/microlitre of blood. Platelets helps to clot blood in case of bleeding. (NCERT Class 11 pg 279)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 2 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181808,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Leukocytes are colourless, nucleate cells. \n\nR: Leukocytes are also called White Blood Cells (WBCs)','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','Leukocytes are nucleate cells also known as WBCs. They are colourless due to lack of hemoglobin in them. (NCERT Class 11 pg 279)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181809,'biology','Read the following statements about FORMED ELEMENTS;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Basophils are phagocytic cells. \n\n2. Eosinophils are associated with allergy. \n\n3. Monocytes are largest leukocytes.','Only 1 is incorrect','Basophils are leukocytes which secrete histamine, serotonin and heparin and involved in inflammatory reactions. They are not phagocytic. (NCERT Class 11 pg 279)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181810,'biology','Read the following statements about FORMED ELEMENTS in blood;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Platelets are derived from reticulocytes. \n\n2. Normal range of platelets is 1.5-3.5 Trillion/ul of blood. \n\n3. Platelets involved in coagulation during bleeding.','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Platelets are fragrments of precursor cells known as megakaryocytes. (NCERT Class 1 pg 280)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(181811,'biology','Read the following statements about BLOOD GROUPING;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. ABO grouping was given by Karl Landsteiner. \n\n2. Blood group A has B antigen on RBC surface. \n\n3. O+ is the universal donor.','Both  2 and 3 are incorrect','Blood group A has A antigen on RBC surface. O- is the universal donor blood group because RBC surface doesnt have AandB antigen. Also there is no Rh antigen. (NCERT Class 11 pg 280)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Both  2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 2 is correct','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(181812,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Blood group B can be given blood group AB. \n\nR: AB blood group has no antigen on RBC surface.','Both A and R are false','Blood group B recipient can be given either B or O group blood.  AB blood group has both A and B antigen on RBC surface. (NCERT CLass 11 pg 280)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(181813,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA : AB- is the universal recipient blood group. \n\nR: O+ is the universal donor blood group.','Both A and R are false','O- is the universal donor blood group because it doesn have A or B or Rh antigen on the RBCs, AB+ is the universal recipient as this individual can accept blood from all blood groups (NCERT Class 11 pg 280)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(181814,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Rh incompatibility is seen with RH⁺ mother and Rh- child. \n\nR: Rh refers to Rheumatic monkey antigen','Both A and R are false','Rh incompatibility is seen in Rh- mother and RH⁺ child. Rh refers to Rhesus monkey antigen present in blood of some individuals. (NCERT Class 11 pg 281)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-281','11-195',NULL),
(181815,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Rh incompatibility is seen with Rh- mother and RH⁺ child. \n\nR: In Rh incompatibility, the child suffers from Polycythemia vera due to maternal antigens destroying RBCs of child.','A is true but R is false','The maternal anti-bodies in succesive pregnancies travel to foetus\' blood and destroys the RBCs leading to anemia and jaundice, a condition called ERYTHREOBLASTOSIS FOETALIS. (NCERT Class 11 pg 281)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-281','11-195',NULL),
(181816,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Clot is formed of fibrin meshwork containing live and viable formed elements of blood. \n\nR: Clots helps in excessive bleeding when blood vessels are injured.','Both A and R are false','Clot contains fibrin meshwork containing dead and damaged formed elements of blood. Clot prevents excessive bleeding and prevents loss of blood. (NCERT Class 11 pg 281)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-281','11-195',NULL),
(181817,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Calcium ion helps in clotting mechanism. \n\nR: Magnesium helps activate various clotting factors.','A is true but R is false','Calcium ion helps to activate the clotting factors leading to clotting and prevention of blood loss. (NCERT Class 11 pg 281)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-281','11-195',NULL),
(181818,'biology','Read the following statements about LYMPH;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Lymph helps to absorb fat in the small intestine. \n\n2. Lymph is a red coloured viscous fluid. \n\n3. Lymph partly helps in immune responses of the body.','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Lymph is a colourless fluid passing through the lymphatic vessels responsible for absorption of fats and immune responses of the body. (NCERT Class 11 pg 282)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Only 2 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 3 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-282','11-196',NULL),
(181819,'biology','Read the following statements about CIRCULATION;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Annelids have open circulatory system. \n\n2. Arthropods have closed circulatory system. \n\n3. Humans have double circulatory system.','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Arthropods and mollusc have open circulatory pathways. And annelids and chordates have closed circulatory pathways. (NCERT Class 11 pg 282)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 1 is correct','NEW','11-282','11-196',NULL),
(250710,'chemistry','Fermentation takes place in ____________ conditions.','Anaerobic','Fermentation takes place in anaerobic conditions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'1','mcq','Anaerobic','Aerobic','Acidic','Basic','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(181820,'biology','Choose the CORRECT option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Crocodiles have 3 chambered heart. \n\nR: Crocodiles have 1 atria and 2 ventricles, as they are reptiles','Both A and R are false','All birds, mammals and members of the crocodile family have a 4 chambered heart with 2 ventricles and 2 atria (NCERT Class 11 pg 282)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-282','11-196',NULL),
(181821,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Open circulatory system involves blood flow through definite blood vessels. \n\nR: It helps to precisely regulate blood flow as compared to closed circulatory system.','Both A and R are false','Open circulatory system involve blood flow from heart into open spaces called sinusoids. Closed circulatory system regulates blood flow more precisely than open system due to flow inside blood vessels. (NCERT Class 11 pg 282)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-282','11-196',NULL),
(181822,'biology','Read the following statements about HEART and CIRCULATION;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Heart is derived from endoderm. \n\n2. Human circulatory system is closed. \n\n3. Human heart has 2 auricles and 2 ventricles.','Only 1 is incorrect','Heart is derived from mesoderm out of the three germ layers of the embryo. (NCERT Class 11 pg 283)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-283','11-197',NULL),
(181823,'biology','Read the following statements about HEART;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Inter-atrial septum separates atria and ventricle of right side. \n\n2. A-V septum separates the atria and ventricle of either side. \n\n3. Ventricular walls are thicker than that of atria.','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Inter-atrial septum separates the right atrium from left atrium. (NCERT Class 11 pg 283)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 1 is correct','NEW','11-283','11-197',NULL),
(181824,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: A-V valve between right atrium and right ventricle is called tricuspid valve. \n\nR: Tricuspid valve has 3 muscular flaps or cusps.','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','The tricuspid valve separates the right atrium from right ventricle and named so because of presence of 3 muscular flaps or cusps. (NCERT Class 11 pg 283)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-283','11-197',NULL),
(181825,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Bi-cuspid valve separates left atrium from right ventricle. \n\nR: Inability of adequate closure of this valve causes aortic murmur.','Both A and R are false','Bi-cuspid valve separates left atrium from left ventricle. Inadequate closure of this valve leads to mitral murmur (a.k.a. mitral regurgitation) (NCERT Class 11 pg 283)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-283','11-197',NULL),
(181826,'biology','Read the following statements about NODES IN HEART;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. AV node is called the main cardiac pacemaker. \n\n2. SA node is present in left ventricle near AV septum. \n\n3. SA node generates action potential at 70-75 times per min.','Only 3 is correct','SA node is the cardiac pacemaker as it generates max number of action potentials per minute. It is located at right upper corner of right atrium. (NCERT Class 11 pg 284)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-284','11-198',NULL),
(181827,'biology','Read the following statements about NODES IN HEART;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. SA node is auto-excitable. \n\n2. AV node generates action potential at 70-75 times per minute.  \n\n3. Impulses pass from AV node to SA node.','Only 1 is correct','SA node generates action potential at the rate of 70-75 times per minute. The impulses pass from SA node to AV node and then to Bundle of His and finally to Purkinjie fibres. (NCERT Class 11 pg 284)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Only 3 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Only 1 is incorrect','NEW','11-284','11-198',NULL),
(181828,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Joint diastole means when only the atria is relaxed. \n\nR: At that time, blood flows from ventricles to aorta and pulmonary artery.','Both A and R are false','Joint diastole means all the 4 chambers of heart are relaxed. During this phase, blood flows from atria to the ventricles passively without any contraction. (NCERT Class 11 pg 284)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-284','11-198',NULL),
(181829,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Atrial systole increases blood flow to ventricles by 70%. \n\nR: Atrium contract when impulses pass from AV node to Purkinjie fibres.','Both A and R are false','Atrial systole increases blood flow to ventricles by 30%. Atrial systole occurs when SA node generates impulses that flow from SA node to AV node. (NCERT Class 11 pg 284)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-284','11-198',NULL),
(181830,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: During ventricular diastole, semilunar valves prevent backflow of blood from ventricles into atrium. \n\nR: Semilunar valves are present in between the superior vena cava and inferior vena cava.','Both A and R are false','During ventricular diastole, semilunar valves prevent back flow of blood from aorta and pulomonary artery into ventricles. Semilunar valves are present between aorta and left venrticle and pulmonary artery and right ventricle. (NCERT Class 11 pg 285)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-285','11-199',NULL),
(181831,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Cardiac output = Stroke volume  x Heart rate. \n\nR: Normal value of cardiac output is 70 ml per minute.','A is true but R is false','Cardiac output = 70 * 72 = 5040 mL (approx 5 litres) (NCERT Class 11 pg 285)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-285','11-199',NULL),
(181832,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Cardiac sounds are produced due to closure of valves. \n\nR: First heart sound (lub) is heard after closure of both semilunar and AV valves.','A is true but R is false','Lub (1st heart sound) is heard after closure of Atrio-ventricular valves (i.e. tricuspid and bi-cuspid valves) (NCERT Class 11 pg 285)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-285','11-199',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(181833,'biology','Read the following statements about ECG;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. ECG was invented by Einthoven. \n\n2. It graphically represents the electrical activity of heart. \n\n3. The machine is called Electro-cardio-gram.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','The machine is called ELECTRO-CARDIO-GRAPH. whereas the graphical representation is called ELECTRO-CARDIO-GRAM. (NCERT Class 11 pg 285).','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 3 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-285','11-199',NULL),
(181834,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: The cardiac output increases, while running\n\nR: The heart rate remains constant but stroke volume increases during running.','A is true but R is false','Both stroke volume and Heart rate increases during running. This leads to increase in cardiac output and increased blood flow to muscles during running. (NCERT Class 11 pg 285)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-285','11-199',NULL),
(181835,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: P wave of ECG refers to electrical hyperpolarisation of atria. \n\nR: At that stage, the chambers are in joint diastole whole throughout P wave.','Both A and R are false','P wave in ECG refers to electrical depolarisation of the heart. During P wav, the ventricles remain relaxed but atria contract and increase blood flow to ventricles by 30%. (NCERT Class 11 pg 286)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-286','11-200',NULL),
(181836,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: QRS complex refers to ventricular depolarisation. \n\nR: During QRS complex, the ventricles get filled with blood from atria.','A is true but R is false','During QRS complex, the ventricles contract such that blood flows from ventricles to aorta and pulmonary artery. During that phase, the A-V valves are closed and atria and ventricles do not communicate. (NCERT Class 11 pg 286)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-286','11-200',NULL),
(181837,'biology','Read the following statements about BLOOD VESSELS;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Tunica intima is the innermost layer of blood vessels. \n\n2. Tunica externa is made up of fibrous tissue with collagen. \n\n3. Inner epithelial lining of blood vessel is keratinised stratified squamous epithelium.','Only 3 is incorrect','Inner lining epithelium of blood vessels is simple squamous epithelium also known as ENDOTHELIUM. Basically, each artery and vein consists of three layers: an inner lining of squamous endothelium, the tunica intima, a middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibres, the tunica media, and an external layer of fibrous connective tissue with collagen fibres, the tunica externa. The tunica media is comparatively thin in the veins (NCERT Class 11 pg 286)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-286','11-200',NULL),
(181838,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Humans have closed and double circulatory system. \n\nR: In humans,blood flows through pulmonary and systemic circulation during each ventricular systole in closed vessels','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Humans have closed, double circulation. Blood in humans travels through definite vessels and are passed through both pulonary circulation and systemic circulation. (NCERT Class 11 pg 286)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-286','11-200',NULL),
(250711,'chemistry','Which gas is released during fermentation.','Carbon dioxide','Carbon dioxide is released during fermentation.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxygen','Nitrogen dioxide','Carbon dioxide','Sulphur dioxide','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(181839,'biology','Read the following statements about PORTAL SYSTEM;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Hepatic portal vein transports blood from intestine to liver. \n\n2. This type of portal system is also seen in pineal gland.\n\n3. Portal system leads to triple blood supply to liver.','Only 1 is correct','Liver has dual blood supply. One supply from the hepatic artery and the other from the portal vein. Portal circulation is also seen in pituitary (a.k.a Hypophyseal portal circulation) (NCERT Class 11 pg 287)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 3 is correct','Only 1 is correct','Only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-287','11-201',NULL),
(181840,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Parasympathetic system helps to reduce cardiac activity (Heart rate, contractions, etc) \n\nR: Cardiac centre is located in midbrain.','A is true but R is false','Regulatory cardiac centre is located in medulla oblongata which controls cardiac ativity through the autonomic nervous system. (NCERT Class 11 pg 287)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-287','11-201',NULL),
(181841,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Hypertension refers to elevated blood pressures. \n\nR: A person suffers from hypertension when his/her blood pressure becomes greater than 120/80 mm of H₂O.','A is true but R is false','Hypertension occurs when blood pressure in a person becomes greater than 120/80 mm Hg (mercury not water). (NCERT Class 11 pg 287)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-287','11-201',NULL),
(181842,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Adrenal medullary hormones can reduce cardiac output. \n\nR: Glucocorticoids are released from adrenal medulla.','Both A and R are false','Adrenal medullary hormones always increase cardiac output. Glucorticoids (cortisol, cortisones) are released from adrenal cortex. Adrenaline/Noradrenaline is released from adrenal medulla. (NCERT Class 11 pg 287).','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-287','11-201',NULL),
(181843,'biology','Read the following statements about VASCULAR DISEASES;Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Coronary Artery disease is also known as ATHEROSCLEROSIS. \n\n2. Coronary Artery Disease affects the blood vessels supplying stomach and intestine. \n\n3. There is deposition of cholestrol, fats, calcium in the vascular tissues.','Only 2 is incorrect','CAD involves narrowing of lumen of the coronary artery which supplies O₂ to the heart muscles. (NCERT Class 11 pg 288)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 2 is correct','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are correct','NEW','11-288','11-202',NULL),
(181844,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Angina refers to acute chest pain. \n\nR: Angina Pectoris is also called Heart Attack','A is true but R is false','Angina occurs associated with atherosclerosis when enough O₂ does not reach heart muscles leading to irritation of the nerves and resultant acute chest pain.The pain occurig in Myocardial Infarction(Heart Attack) is a symptom, and is called Angina Pectoris. (NCERT class 11 pg 288)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct, and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-288','11-202',NULL),
(181845,'biology','Read the following statements about HEART FAILURE; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n\n1. Heart failure refers to inability of heart to pump effectively. \n\n2. Dyspnea is a major symptom of left heart failure. \n\n3. Heart failure is synonymous to cardiac arrest.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Dyspnea refers to shortness/difficulty of breathing associated with pulmonary/lung congestion associated with heart failure. Heart failure is not the same as cardiac arrest. (NCERT Class 11 pg 288)','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181901,'biology','Adult human RBC\'s are enucleate. Which of the following statement(s) is/are most appropriate explanation for this feature?\n\n\n(a) They do not need to reproduce.\n(b) They are somatic cells.\n(c) They do not metabolize.\n(d) All their internal space is available for oxygen transport.','a, c and d','The circular mammalian erythrocyte is synthesized in the bone marrow. After synthesis, it undergoes a process called enucleation in which the nucleus is removed. The absence of a nucleus allows the red blood cells to contain more hemoglobin and hence all their internal space is available for oxygen transport in order to the body tissues. As they do not have any organelle, it does not show any metabolic activity and multiplication','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','a, c and d','only a','only d','b and c','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181902,'biology','Which of the following is correct for Chordae Tendiae? \n(a)Chordae tendineae are the tendons that connect the tricuspid valve and mitral valve to the wall of ventricles.\n(b) They prevent backflow of blood, from ventricles to atria\n(e)Keeps the tricuspid and mitral valves in a closed position.\n(d)Keeps the semilunar valves in a closed position.\n(c)Chordae tendineae are the tendons that connect the semilunar valves to the wall of ventricles.','a, b, e','Chordae tendineae are the tendons that connect tricuspid valve and mitral valve to the wall of ventricles. They prevent backflow of blood, from ventricles to atria, by keeping these valves in closed position. The chordae tendinae (CT) are strong, fibrous connections between the valve leaflets and the papillary muscles. These are attached to the leaflets on to the ventricular side and prevent the cusps from swinging back into the atrial cavity during systole.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','a, b, e','a, and b','a, b, c','b,c and d','NEW','11-283','11-198',NULL),
(181903,'biology','Chordae tendineae are the tendons that connect______with the cardiac muscles.','The tricuspid valve divides the right atrium from the right ventricle','The tricuspid valve divides the right atrium from the right ventricle\n\n\nChordae tendineae are the tendons that connect tricuspid valve and mitral valve to the wall of ventricles. They prevent backflow of blood, from ventricles to atria, by keeping these valves in closed position.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','The semilunar valve separates the left atrium from the left ventricle','The tricuspid valve divides the right atrium from the right ventricle','Aortic and pulmonary valves control the flow of blood into the heart','The pulmonary valves are supported by chordae tendineae so that the regurgitation of blood into the atria during ventricular contraction does not occur','NEW','11-283','11-198',NULL),
(181904,'biology','First branch of the aortic arch is','brachiocephalic trunk','The brachiocephalic trunk is the first branch of the aortic arch and supplies blood to the right arm and right head and neck. The left common carotid artery is the second branch of the aortic arch, which supplies blood to the left side of the head of the neck','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','brachiocephalic trunk','common Carotid artery','Descending aorta','left subclavian artery','NEW','11-283','11-198',NULL),
(181905,'biology','Which of the following does not arise from the aorta?','Sigmoid artery','the sigmoid arteries are 2–5 branches of the inferior mesenteric artery that are distributed to the distal descending colon and the sigmoid colon.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Sigmoid artery','common Carotid artery','ascending aorta','left subclavian artery','NEW','11-283','11-198',NULL),
(181906,'biology','Apex of the heart is tilted towards the','Downwards and to the left','The heart is located in the middle of the thoracic cavity, oriented obliquely, with the apex of the heart pointing down and to the left','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Downwards and to the left','Upwards and to the left','Downwards and to the right','Upwards to the right','NEW','11-283','11-198',NULL),
(181907,'biology','If due to some injury, the chordae tendineae of the tricuspid valve of the human heart is partially non-functional, what will be the immediate effect?','The flow of blood into the pulmonary artery will be reduced','When the chordae tendineae contract, they pull all three cusp down, allowing blood to flow into the right ventricle and opening the tricuspid valve. If the chordae tendineae are injured, a backflow of blood into the atria will occur immediately.\nAs a result, blood flow into the pulmonary artery will be diminished since blood will continue to return to the atria.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','The flow of blood into the pulmonary artery will be reduced','The blood will tend to flow back into the left atrium','The flow of blood into the aorta will be slowed down','The pacemaker will stop working','NEW','11-283','11-198',NULL),
(181908,'biology','Which two of the following changes (a-d) usually tend to occur in plain dwellers when they move to higher altitudes (3500m or more)?\n(a) Increase in red blood cell size\n(b) Increase in red blood cell production\n(c) Increase in breathing rate\n(d) Increase in thrombocyte count','b and c','When a plain dweller moves to a higher altitude (3500m or above) then altitude sickness occurs. The symptoms include nausea, fatigue, and heart palpitations because of low atmospheric pressure and the inability of the body to take enough oxygen. Thus, to cope up with this body shows Physiological adaptation which includes-\n\n1) Increase in RBCs production\n\n2) The decrease in binding capacity of hemoglobin\n\n3) By increasing the breathing rate','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','b and c','a and b','b and d','a and d','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181909,'biology','Where are papillary muscles located?','Ventricles of the heart','The papillary muscles of the heart are pillar-like muscles seen within the cavity of the ventricles, attached to their walls. They have an integral role in proper cardiac valvular function.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Ventricles of the heart','Arteries','veins','Atrium of the heart','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181910,'biology','Breakdown product of haemoglobin is','Bilirubin','Haemoglobin molecule liberated from red blood cells are broken down into subunits of heme, an iron containing portion and globin, a protein. The heme further decomposes into iron and a greenish pigment called biliverdin. Biliverdin eventually is converted to a orange pigment called bilirubin.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Bilirubin','Biliverdin,','calcium','ferretin','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181911,'biology','Presence of RBCs in Urine is called','Hematuria','Hematuria is blood in the urine. Alkaptonuria, or black urine disease, is a very rare inherited disorder that prevents the body fully breaking down two protein building blocks (amino acids) called tyrosine and phenylalanine.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Alkaptonuria','Urothiasis','Proteinuria','Hematuria','NEW','11-288','11-203',NULL),
(181912,'biology','Carbonic Anhydrase occurs in','RBCs','carbonic anhydrase, enzyme found in red blood cells, gastric mucosa, pancreatic cells, and renal tubules that catalyzes the interconversion of carbon dioxide (CO2) and carbonic acid (H2CO3). Carbonic anhydrase plays an important role in respiration by influencing CO2 transport in the blood.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','RBCs','lymphocytes','blood plasma','leucocytes','NEW','11-286','11-201',NULL),
(181913,'biology','The neurogenic heart is the characteristic feature of','arthropods','The neurogenic heart is a characteristic feature of most arthropods and some annelids. In this the heart beat is initiated by a nerve impulse coming from a nerve ganglion situated near the heart.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','humans','Rabbits','rats','arthropods','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181914,'biology','Cells formed in the bone marrow include','RBCs and leucocytes','There are two types of bone marrow which are characterized by their color :\nRed bone marrow produces blood cells (hematopoiesis).\nIt creates red blood cells (RBCs) that carry oxygen and white blood cells (WBCs) and platelets that prevent infection.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','RBCs','RBCs and leucocytes','Leucocytes','lymphocytes','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181915,'biology','Component of blood responsible for producing antibodies is','Lymphocytes','Lymphocytes have a very large, rounded nucleus and scanty cytoplasm. They are non motile and non phagocytic. They secrete antibodies to destroy microbes and their toxins, reject graphs and kill tumor cells. They also help in healing of injuries. Thrombocytes aid in clotting of blood. Monocytes are phagocytic in nature. RBCs transport gases in the body.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Thrombocytes','Monocytes','Erythrocytes','Lymphocytes','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181916,'biology','Removal of calcium from freshly collected blood would','prevent clotting','Clotting requires calcium ions (Ca2+), which is why blood banks use a chelating agent to bind the calcium in donated blood, so the blood will not clot in the bag.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','prevent clotting','cause delayed clotting','cause immediate clotting','prevent destruction of haemoglobin','NEW','11-281','11-196',NULL),
(181917,'biology','The pacesetter in the heart is called','Atrio-ventricular node','The cardiac impulse is initiated in the sino-atrial node also known as natural pacemaker. It then travels to the atrio-ventricular node. From there through the Purkinje fibres to the ventricles. The atrio-ventricular node regulates the rhythm of the heart rate mainly. The delay in conduction of impulse in this node changes the duration of contraction between the atria and the ventricles. Hence AV node is called as the pace-setter.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Atrio-ventricular node','Sino-atrial node','Purkinje fibres','papillary muscles','NEW','11-284','11-197',NULL),
(181918,'biology','Which is the principal cation in the plasma of the blood?','Sodium','Sodium is the main cation of plasma and Chloride is the main anion. Potassium, calcium and magnesium are present in small amounts.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','3','mcq','Sodium','Potassium','Calcium','magnesium','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181919,'biology','Which statement of true for WBC?','Can squeeze through blood capillaries','WBCs can change their shape and are capable of amoeboid movement. This enables them to squeeze out of blood capillaries into tissues. This process is known as diapedesis.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','In deficiency, cancer is caused','Non-nucleated','Can squeeze through blood capillaries','Manufactured in thymus','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(181920,'biology','Which of the following has an open Circulatory system?','Periplaneta','Periplaneta has open Circulatory system.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','Octopus','Pheretima','Periplaneta','Hirudinaria','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(181921,'biology','The genotype of B blood group father of an O blood group child is','IO IB','The father with B group can have the genotype IOIB or IBIB . The child with blood group O will have the genotype ii, one from each parent. Hence the father has the genotype IOIB.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','IO IO','IO IB','IO IA','IB IB','NEW','11-280','11-195',NULL),
(181922,'biology','The hormonal regulation of cardiac output is','mediated by the adrenal medulla','Adrenal medullary hormones\ncan also increase the cardiac output','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','1','mcq','mediated by the adrenal cortex','mediated by thyroid','mediated by the adrenal medulla','mediated by pineal','NEW','11-285','11-200',NULL),
(181923,'biology','The reason why tricuspid and bicuspid valves are closed is','attempted backflow of blood into the atria','Ventricular systole increases the ventricular pressure causing the closure of tricuspid and bicuspid valves due to\nattempted backflow of blood into the atria.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','ventricular relaxation','attempted backflow of blood into the atria','Atrial systole','ventricular filling','NEW','11-285','11-200',NULL),
(181924,'biology','Which of these events will not take place if the semilunar valves did not function?','Production of heart sound','During each cardiac cycle two prominent sounds are produced which\ncan be easily heard through a stethoscope. The first heart sound (lub) is\nassociated with the closure of the tricuspid and bicuspid valves whereas\nthe second heart sound (dub) is associated with the closure of the\nsemilunar valves','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','ventricular contraction','Production of heart sound','blood flow','atrial contraction','NEW','11-284','11-199',NULL),
(181925,'biology','Blood enters into the heart because of muscles of','atria relax','Blood enters the heart through large veins called as vena cava. Blood enters the heart when the muscles of atria relax and it is pumped out when it is contracted.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','ventricles contract','atria relax','ventricles relax','contraction of the vena cava','NEW','11-283','11-198',NULL),
(181926,'biology','Match the following columns and select the correct option 1. Eosinophils 2. Basophils 3. Neutrophils 4. lymphocytes (i) Immune response (ii) phagocytosis (iii) Release histaminase, destructive enzymes (iv) Release granules containing histamine (NEET 2020)','1-(iii) 2- (iv) 3- (ii) 4 (i)','Lymphocytes (20-25%) are of two major types B and T Lymphocytes. Both B and T Lymphocytes are responsible for immune responses of the body.','Human Physiology >> Body Fluids and circulation >> Previous Year Questions 2','Body fluids and circulation','2','mcq','1-(i) 2- (iv) 3- (iii) 4 (ii)','1-(i) 2- (ii) 3- (iii) 4 (iv)','1-(ii) 2- (iii) 3- (i) 4 (iv)','1-(iii) 2- (iv) 3- (ii) 4 (i)','NEW','11-279','11-194',NULL),
(170101,'chemistry','Electrochemistry is the study of the production of electricity from the energy released during _________ chemical reactions and the use of electrical energy to bring about non-spontaneous chemical transformations.','spontaneous','Electrochemistry is the study of the production of electricity from the energy released during spontaneous chemical reactions and the use of electrical energy to bring about non-spontaneous chemical transformations. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 65; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Introduction',NULL,'1','fillup','spon,tan,eous,non,spo,ntan,eous',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-065','12-031',NULL),
(170102,'chemistry','Batteries and fuel cells convert ____ energy into mechanical energy and are used on a large scale in various instruments and devices.','chemical','Batteries and fuel cells convert chemical energy into electrical energy and are used on a large scale in various instruments and devices.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 65; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Introduction',NULL,'1','fillup','che,mi,cal,mecha,ni,cal',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-065','12-031',NULL),
(170103,'chemistry','The study of electrochemistry is important for creating new technologies that are _____.','eco friendly','The reactions carried out electrochemically can be energy efficient and less polluting. Therefore, the study of electrochemistry is important for creating new technologies that are eco-friendly.  \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 65; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','polluting','harmful','eco friendly','free of cost','NEW','12-065','12-031',NULL),
(170104,'chemistry','The transmission of sensory signals through cells to the brain and vice versa and communication between the cells are known to have _____ origin.','electrochemical','The transmission of sensory signals through cells to the brain and vice versa and communication between the cells are known to have an electrochemical origin.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 65; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Introduction',NULL,'1','fillup','ele,ctro,chem,ical,mec,han.ical',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-065','12-031',NULL),
(170105,'biology','Exchange of O₂ with CO₂  in the body is called ________','Respiration','This process of exchange of O₂ from the atmosphere with CO₂ produced by the cells is called breathing, commonly known as respiration. NCERT biology class 11, pg 268.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Introduction',NULL,'1','fillup','Res,pir,ati,on,er,fy',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-268','11-183',NULL),
(170201,'chemistry','Daniell cell converts the _____ energy liberated during the redox reaction to electrical energy.','chemical','Daniell cell converts the chemical energy liberated during the redox reaction to electrical energy.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrochemical Cells',NULL,'1','fillup','che,mi,cal,ele,ctri,cal',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(170202,'chemistry','Daniell cell has an electrical potential equal to ____ V when the concentration of cations is unity.','1.1','Daniell cell has an electrical potential equal to 1.1 V when the concentration of cations is unity.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrochemical Cells',NULL,'1','mcq','0.1','1','1.1','0','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(170203,'chemistry','Redox reaction of ___ cell is Zn(s) + Cu⁺²(aq) --> Zn⁺²(aq) + Cu(s).','All of these','Redox reaction of Daniell (Devices derived from them when concentration is unity such as galvanic/voltaic cell) cell is Zn(s) + Cu⁺²(aq) --> Zn⁺²(aq) + Cu(s).\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrochemical Cells',NULL,'2','mcq','Galvanic','Voltaic','Daniell','All of these','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(170204,'chemistry','Another name for the galvanic cell is ___ cell.','Voltaic','Another name for the galvanic cell is the Voltaic cell.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrochemical Cells',NULL,'1','mcq','Daniell','Dry','Voltaic','Both (b) and (c)','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(170205,'chemistry','When the concentration of Zn⁺² and Cu⁺² is unity then such a device is called ___.','Both (a) and (c)','When the concentration of Zn⁺² and Cu⁺² ions is unity (1 mol dm⁻³)*. Such a device is called a galvanic or a voltaic cell.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrochemical Cells',NULL,'2','mcq','Voltaic cell','Battery','Galvanic cell','Both (a) and (c)','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(170206,'chemistry','Which is the device for using electrical energy to carry non-spontaneous chemical reactions?','Electrolytic cell','An electrolytic cell is a device for using electrical energy to carry non-spontaneous chemical reactions.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrochemical Cells',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrolytic cell','Battery','Dry cell','Galvanic cell','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(170207,'chemistry','With reference to the Daniell cell, when Eₑₓₜₑᵣₙₐₗ < 1.1V \n i) Electrons flow from Zn rod to Cu rod hence current flows from Cu to Zn.\n(ii) Zn dissolves at the anode and copper deposits at the cathode.','Both statements are correct','Current flows opposite direction of electron flow. So electrons flow from Zn rod to Cu rod hence current flows from Cu to Zn. Hence, Zn dissolves at the anode, and copper deposits at the cathode.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrochemical Cells',NULL,'2','mcq','Only statement (i) is correct','Only statement (ii) is correct','Both statements are incorrect','Both statements are correct','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(170208,'chemistry','With reference to the Daniell cell, when Eₑₓₜ = 1.1 V\n(i) No flow of electrons or current.\n(ii) No chemical reaction.','Both statements are correct','The reaction continues to take place till the opposing voltage reaches the value of 1.1 V when the reaction stops altogether and no current flows through the cell. So no chemical reactions occur as well. \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrochemical Cells',NULL,'2','mcq','Only statement (i) is correct','Only statement (ii) is correct','Both statements are incorrect','Both statements are correct','NEW','12-066','12-032','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/170208q.png'),
(170301,'chemistry','A galvanic cell is an electrochemical cell that converts the X energy of a spontaneous redox reaction into Z energy. What are X and Z respectively?','chemical,electrical','A galvanic cell is an electrochemical cell that converts the chemical energy of a spontaneous redox reaction into electrical energy. The Gibbs energy of the spontaneous redox reaction is converted into electrical work.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'2','mcq','chemical,mechanical','chemical,electrical','mechanical,electrical','electrical,chemical','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170302,'chemistry','In a Galvanic cell, the Gibbs energy of the ___ redox reaction is converted into ___ work which may be used for running a motor or other electrical gadgets like heater, fan, geyser, etc.','spontaneous, electrical','A galvanic cell is an electrochemical cell that converts the chemical energy of a spontaneous redox reaction into electrical energy. In this device, the Gibbs energy of the spontaneous redox reaction is converted into electrical work which may be used for running a motor or other electrical gadgets like heater, fan, geyser, etc.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','mcq','non-spontaneous, electrical','spontaneous, mechanical','spontaneous, electrical','non-spontaneous, mechanical','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(250712,'chemistry','The action of zymase is inhibited once the percentage of alcohol formed exceeds?','14 Percent','The action of zymase is inhibited once the percentage of alcohol formed exceeds 14 percent.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'1','mcq','10 Percent','20 Percent','14 Percent','40 Percent','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(170303,'chemistry','Galvanic cell reaction is a combination of two half-reactions whose addition gives the overall cell reaction:\n(i) Cu⁺² + 2e⁻ --> Cu(s) \n(ii) Zn(s) --> Zn⁺² + 2e⁻    \nHere (i) and (ii) reactions are :','reduction half-reaction,oxidation half-reaction','Galvanic cell reaction is a combination of two half-reactions whose addition gives the overall cell reaction:\n(i) Cu⁺² + 2e⁻ --> Cu(s) \n(ii) Zn(s) --> Zn⁺² + 2e⁻    \nOverall reaction is Zn(s) + Cu⁺²(aq) ® Zn⁺² (aq) + Cu(s)\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'3','mcq','oxidation half-reaction,reduction half-reaction','reduction half-reaction,oxidation half-reaction','reduction half-reaction,reduction half-reaction','oxidation half-reaction,oxidation half-reaction','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170304,'chemistry','In Galvanic cell, the reduction half reaction occurs on the ____ electrode while the oxidation half reaction occurs on the zinc electrode.','copper','The reduction half-reaction occurs on the copper electrode while the oxidation half-reaction occurs on the zinc electrode. These two portions of the cell are also called half-cells or redox couples. The copper electrode may be called the reduction half cell and the zinc electrode, the oxidation half-cell.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','fillup','co,pp,er,zi,nc',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170305,'chemistry','In the Galvanic cell, the reduction half-reaction occurs on the cooper electrode while the oxidation half-reaction occurs on the zinc electrode. These two portions of the cell are also called as______.','Half-cells or redox couples','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'2','mcq','half-cells','redox couples','Half-cells or redox couples','None of these','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170306,'chemistry','Oxidation and reduction portions of a Galvanic cell are connected by which of the followings?','metallic wire','Each half-cell consists of a metallic electrode dipped into an electrolyte. The two half-cells are connected by a metallic wire through a voltmeter and a switch externally.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','mcq','metallic wire','plastic thread','non-metallic wire','non-conducting wire','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170307,'chemistry','In a galvanic cell, the electrolytes of the two half-cells are connected internally through a ___.','salt bridge','The electrolytes of the two half-cells are connected internally through a salt bridge. \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','mcq','wire','salt bridge','non-conducting material','None of these','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170308,'chemistry','We do not require a salt bridge when both the electrodes are dipped in the same electrolyte solution. The given statement is:','completely correct','Sometimes, both the electrodes of a Daniell cell dip in the same electrolyte solution, and in such cases we do not require a salt bridge.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'2','mcq','completely incorrect','completely correct','partially correct','insufficient information','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170309,'chemistry','What do you call the potential difference which develops between the electrode and the electrolyte?','electrode potential','At equilibrium, there is a separation of charges and depending on the tendencies of the two opposing reactions, the electrode may be positively or negatively charged with respect to the solution. A potential difference develops between the electrode and the electrolyte which is called electrode potential.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','mcq','voltage','potential','standard electrode potential','electrode potential','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170310,'chemistry','When the concentrations of all the species involved in a half-cell are unity then the electrode potential is known as?','standard electrode potential','When the concentrations of all the species involved in a half-cell are unity, then the electrode potential is known as standard electrode potential.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','mcq','electrode potential','standard electrode potential','potential','voltage','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170311,'chemistry','According to the IUPAC convention, standard reduction potentials are called _____.','standard electrode potentials','When the concentrations of all the species involved in a half-cell are unity then the electrode potential is known as standard electrode potential. According to IUPAC convention, standard reduction potentials are now called standard electrode potentials.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','mcq','standard electrode potentials','electrode potential','potential','voltage','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170312,'chemistry','In a galvanic cell, the half-cell in which oxidation takes place is called ___and it has a negative potential with respect to the solution.','anode','In a galvanic cell, the half-cell in which oxidation takes place is called anode and it has a negative potential with respect to the solution. The other half-cell in which reduction takes place is called cathode and it has a\npositive potential with respect to the solution.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','fillup','ano,de,cath,ode',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170313,'chemistry','The half-cell in which reduction takes place is called ____ and it has a  positive potential with respect to the solution.','cathode','In a galvanic cell, the half-cell in which oxidation takes place is called anode and it has a negative potential with respect to the solution. The other half-cell in which reduction takes place is called cathode and it has a positive potential with respect to the solution.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','fillup','cat,ho,de,ano,de',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170314,'chemistry','In galvanic cells, the electrons flow from the ____ electrode.','negative','There exists a potential difference between the two electrodes and as soon as the switch is in the on position the electrons flow from the negative electrode to the positive electrode.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','fillup','ne,ga,tive,pos,it,ive',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170315,'chemistry','The direction of current flow is ___ to that of electron flow.','opposite','There exists a potential difference between the two electrodes and as soon as the switch is in the on position the electrons flow from the negative electrode to the positive electrode. The direction of current flow is opposite to that of electron flow.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','fillup','op,po,si,te,pa,ra,llel',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170316,'chemistry','The potential difference between the two electrodes of a galvanic cell is called the ___ potential','cell','The potential difference between the two electrodes of a galvanic cell is called the cell potential and is measured in volts.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','fillup','ce,ll,el,ec,tro,de',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(170317,'chemistry','The cell ___ is the difference between the electrode potentials (reduction potentials) of the cathode and anode.','potential','The cell potential is the difference between the electrode potentials (reduction potentials) of the cathode and anode. It is called the cell electromotive force (emf) of the cell when no current.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','fillup','po,ten,tial,cap,aci,ty',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(170318,'chemistry','Cell _____  is called the cell emf of the cell when no current is drawn through the cell.','potential','The cell potential is the difference between the electrode potentials (reduction potentials) of the cathode and anode. It is called the cell electromotive force (emf) of the cell when no current is drawn through the cell.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','fillup','po,ten,tial,conc,ent,ra,tion',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(170319,'chemistry','It is now an accepted convention that we keep the anode on the A and the cathode on the B while representing the galvanic cell. Match A and B','A-left,B-right','It is now an accepted convention that we keep the anode on thEₗₑբₜ and the cathode on thEᵣᵢ₉ₕₜ\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'3','match','A,left','B,right',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(170320,'chemistry','A galvanic cell is generally represented by putting a ____ line between metal and electrolyte solution and putting a double vertical line between the two electrolytes connected by a salt bridge.','vertical','While representing the galvanic cell. A galvanic cell is generally represented by putting a vertical line between metal and electrolyte solution and putting a double vertical line between the two electrolytes connected by a salt bridge.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','fillup','ver,tic,al,hor,izo,nt,al',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(170321,'chemistry','____ = Eᵣᵢ₉ₕₜ – Eₗₑբₜ','Ecell','The emf of the cell is positive and is given by the potential of the half-cell on thEᵣᵢ₉ₕₜ-hand side minus the potential of the half-cell on thEₗₑբₜ-hand side i.e. E𝒸ₑₗₗ = Eᵣᵢ₉ₕₜ – Eₗₑfₜ\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','fillup','Ece,ll,Eca,tho,de',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(170322,'chemistry','Which of the following options are correct regarding the reaction :\nA : 2Ag⁺(aq) + 2e– --> 2Ag(s) \nB:  Cu(s) --> Cu⁺²(aq) + 2e–','A happens at cathode and B at anode','Half-cell reactions:\nCathode (reduction): 2Ag⁺(aq) + 2e–----> 2Ag(s)\nAnode (oxidation): Cu(s) ----->Cu⁺²(aq) + 2e–\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'2','mcq','A happens at anode and B at cathode','A happens at cathode and B at anode','Both at cathode','Both at anode','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(170323,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\na. Galvanic cell is spontaneous\nb. Electrolytic cell is non-spontaneous','Both','A galvanic cell is an electrochemical cell that converts the chemical energy of a spontaneous redox reaction into electrical energy. An electrolytic cell is a device for using electrical energy to carry out non-spontaneous chemical reactions.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph No.2, Page 67; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both','none of these','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(170324,'chemistry','Gibbs free energy for Galvanic cell at constant temperature and pressure is','negative','As in galvanic cell, spontaneous redox reaction happens hence it has negative Gibbs free energy\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'1','mcq','positive','negative','zero','information insufficient','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170325,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\na.  In a galvanic cell, the anode is negative\nb. In an electrolytic cell, the anode is positive','Both','The anode is where oxidation occurs. In an electrolytic cell, the negative charge is on the cathode, so the positive is on the anode, while the positive charge is on the anode. Since the reaction at the anode is the source of electrons for the current, the anode is the negative terminal for the galvanic cell.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 67; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both','none of these','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(170326,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion: EMF of the cell is the potential difference between the electrode potentials of the cathode and anode when no current is drawn through the cell\nReason: Anode is kept on the right and cathode on the left while representing the galvanic cell','Assertion is true but Reason is false','The cell potential is the difference between the electrode potentials (reduction potentials) of the cathode and anode. It is called the cell electromotive force (emf) of the cell when no current is drawn through the cell. It is now an accepted convention that we keep the anode on the left and the cathode on the right while representing the galvanic cell. These two sentences don\'t relate to each other.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(170327,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion : Current stops flowing when E𝒸ₑₗₗ = 0\nReason : Equilibrium of the  cell is attained','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','At equilibrium, the potential value at both electrodes is the same, and therefore no current flows.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Galvanic Cells',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(110709,'biology','The apoplast does not provide any barrier to water movement and water movement is through','Mass flow','The apoplast does not provide any barrier to water movement and water movement is through mass flow.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 184','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Without any gradient.','Osmosis','Bulk flow','Mass flow','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(170328,'biology','Which of the following set of muscles are involved in process of normal breathing?','Diaphragm and intercoastal muscles.','The process of breathing is initiated by the contraction of diaphragm which increases the volume of thoracic chamber in the antero-posterior axis. \nThe contraction of external inter-costal muscles lifts up the ribs and the sternum causing an increase in the volume of the thoracic chamber in the dorso-ventral axis (NCERT Class 11, Page 270). Abdominal muscles are involved during forced breathing.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'1','mcq','Diaphragm and intercoastal muscles.','Diaphragm and abdominal muscles','Abdominal and intercoastal muscle','Diaphragm, intercoastal and abdominal muscles.','NEW','11-270','11-185',NULL),
(170329,'biology','A batsman during a cricket match was running between the wickets. In the mean time, which muscles would he was using to breathe?','Diaphragm, intercoastal muscles and abdominal Muscles','As the cricker was running and we know that during running or exercise body metabolic demands increases and body needs more O₂ \nand we have the ability to increase the strength of inspiration and expiration with the help of additional muscles in the abdomen. (NCERT Class 11, Page 271) \nSo he would have used diaphragm, intercoastal muscles and additionally abdominal muscles to breath and fulfill his increased O₂ demands.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'3','mcq','Diaphragm and intercoastal muscles','Diaphragm, intercoastal muscles and abdominal Muscles','Intercoastal and abdominal muscles','Diaphragm and abdominal muscles','NEW','11-271','11-186',NULL),
(170330,'biology','Which of the following machine is used for assessing lung volumes and capacities in lab?','Spirometer','The volume of air involved in breathing movements can be estimated by using a spirometer which helps in clinical assessment of pulmonary functions. (NCERT Class 11, Page  271). \nSphygmomanometer is used for measuring Blood pressure.\nStethoscope is used for Heart and respiratory problems. ECG machine is used for Heart problems.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'1','mcq','Spirometer','Sphygmomanometer','Stethoscope will be used.','ECG Machine','NEW','11-271','11-186',NULL),
(170331,'biology','Choose the incorrect statement.','Average residual volume is 2500mL to 3000 mL.','Residual Volume (RV) is Volume of air remaining in the lungs even after a forcible expiration. This averages 1100 mL to 1200 mL. (NCERT Class 11, Page (272)','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'1','mcq','On an average, a healthy adult breathes 12-16 times/minute.','A spirometer is used to assess pulmonary functions.','A healthy man has a tidal volume of 500ml','Average residual volume is 2500mL to 3000 mL.','NEW','11-272','11-187',NULL),
(170332,'biology','A student is sitting quietly on chair in his classroom and he is listening to biology lecture along taking some notes. The volume of air, he inspired or expired during lecture is equal to ______ per breath.','0.5 L','Explanation: Volume of air inspired or expired during a normal respiration is called Tidal volume. It is approx. 500 mL or 0.5 L(NCERT Class 11, Page 271) . As the student is sitting and he is listening and writing  only. So it is very obvious that he is breathing normally which is equal to tidal volume i-e 500 mL and 0.5 L.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'3','mcq','500 L','0.5 L','2.5-3 L','1 L','NEW','11-271','11-186',NULL),
(170333,'biology','Normally a healthy man inspires or expires approximately ————- volume of air in a minute','6 L to 8 L of air per minute.','Explanation:As we know that the amount of air we breathe in or out normally is equal to tidal volume i-e 500 mL or  0.5 L per breath. \nNow,we also know that the normal breathing rate is 12-16 breaths per minute. \nMultiply Tidal volume with the rate of breaths per minute i-e 500x(12 to 16) = 6000 to 8000ml which is 6 to 8L, \n(NCERT Class 11, Page 271).(NCERT .bio.11 pg#271)','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'1','mcq','6 L to 8 L of air per minute.','2500 mL to 3000 mL of air per minute.','1000 mL to 1100 mL of air per minute.','1500 mL to 2000 mL of air per minute','NEW','11-271','11-186',NULL),
(170334,'biology','A spirometer shows following readings.\n\nTidal volume(TV)=500mL.\n\nExpiratory reserve volume(ERV)=1000 mL.\n\nInspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV)=3000 mL.\n\nThe expiratory capacity (EC) after a normal inspiration is?','1500 mL','Explanation: Expiratory Capacity (EC): Total volume of air a person can expire after a normal inspiration.This includes tidal volume\nand expiratory reserve volume and is equal to TV+ERV.(NCERT Class 11, Page 272). So EC=500+1000=1500 mL','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'3','mcq','1500 mL','4000 mL','3500 mL','500 mL','NEW','11-272','11-187',NULL),
(170335,'biology','What is total lung capacity of 70 year old male patient whoose spirometer shows the following readings: \n\nTidal volume(TV)=400mL\n\nExpiratory reserve volume(ERV)=1400mL\n\nInspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV)=1000 mL\n\nResidual Volume (RV)=1100','3.9 L','Explanation: Total Lung Capacity (TLC) is total volume of air accommodated in the lungs at the end of a forced inspiration. This includes RV, ERV, TV and IRV. (NCERT Class 11, Page 272). So TLC=400+1400+1000+1100=3900 mL or 3.9 L.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'3','mcq','3.9 L','4.5 L','3 L','2 L','NEW','11-272','11-187',NULL),
(170336,'biology','All of the following are correct except:','Vital Capacity (VC) is equal to sum of  ERV, TV and RV','Vital Capacity (VC): The maximum volume of air a person can breathe in after a forced expiration. This includes ERV, TV and IRV or the maximum volume of air a person can breathe out after a forced inspiration. (NCERT Class 11, Page 272)','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'3','mcq','Total Lung capacity is the highest parameter in spirometry','Vital Capacity (VC) is equal to sum of  ERV, TV and RV','Total Lung Capacity is equal to Vital capacity + residual volume.','Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) is equal to Volume of air that will remain in the lungs after a normal expiration.','NEW','11-272','11-187',NULL),
(170337,'biology','A Hiker at top of mount everest will develop altitude sickness(headache and rapid breathing) without suplemental oxygen. What could be the reason of this?','The partial pressure of O₂ is less at top of mount everest.','Explanation: As we know that Exchange of gases also occur between blood and tissues. O₂ and CO₂  are exchanged in these sites by simple diffusion mainly based on pressure/concentration gradient. (NCERT Class 11, Page 272). Now we also know that with increase in height atmospheric pressure decreases. So as the climber is at the top of mount evert that\'s why he will develop rapid breathing and headache due to decrease O₂ supply from the atmosphere.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'3','mcq','The partial pressure of CO₂  is more at top of mount everest.','The partial pressure of O₂ is less at top of mount everest.','The partial pressure of O₂ is more at top of mount everest.','The partial pressure of CO₂  is less at top of mount everest.','NEW','11-272','11-187',NULL),
(170338,'biology','O₂ reaches blood by passing through diffusion membrane, in following order:','Squamous epithelium of alveoli, basement membranes and capillaries endothelium.','The diffusion membrane is made up of three major layers namely, the thin squamous epithelium of alveoli, the endothelium of alveolar capillaries and the basement substance (composed of a thin basement membrane supporting the squamous epithelium and the basement membrane surrounding \nthe single layer endothelial cells of capillaries) in between them.(NCERT Class 11, Page 273.Also Look Fig 17.4 carefully)','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'1','mcq','Squamous epithelium of alveoli, capillaries endothelium and basement membranes.','Squamous epithelium of alveoli, basement membranes and capillaries endothelium.','Squamous epithelium of alveoli ,capillary endothelium and basement membranes','Basement membranes, capillary endothelium, and squamous epithelium of alveoli','NEW','11-273','11-188',NULL),
(170339,'biology','Regarding respiration, Choose the incorrect pair.','Tracheal Respiration---Humans','Explanation: Mechanisms of breathing vary among different groups of animals depending mainly on their habitats and levels of organisation.\n(NCERT Class 11, Page 268). Fishes respire through special vascularised structures called gills (branchial respiration)\nHumans and Reptiles respire through vascularised bags called lungs (pulmonary respiration).\nFrogs respire through lungs and also frogs can respire through their moist skin (cutaneous respiration)','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Human Respiratory System',NULL,'1','mcq','Branchial Respiration---fish','Tracheal Respiration---Humans','Pulmonary Respiration---Reptiles','Cutaneous Respiration---Frogs','NEW','11-268','11-183',NULL),
(170401,'chemistry','According to the convention, a half-cell called standard hydrogen electrode represented by Pt(s) | H₂(g) | H⁺(aq) is assigned\na ___ potential at all temperatures.','zero','Standard hydrogen electrode has zero potential at all temperatures.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'1','fillup','ze,ro,non,ze,ro',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(170402,'chemistry','The standard hydrogen electrode consists of a platinum electrode coated with platinum __','black','The standard hydrogen electrode consists of a platinum electrode coated with platinum black.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'1','fillup','bla,ck,sil,ver',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(170403,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\na.  If the concentrations of the oxidized and the reduced form of the species in the right-hand half-cell are unity, then the cell potential is equal to standard electrode potentials\nb. In a standard hydrogen electrode, the concentration of both the reduced and oxidized forms of hydrogen is maintained at unity','Both statements are correct','The standard hydrogen electrode consists of a platinum electrode coated with platinum black. The electrode is dipped in an acidic solution and pure hydrogen gas is bubbled through it. The concentration of both the reduced and oxidized forms of hydrogen is maintained at unity.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 68; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both statements are correct','None of these','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(170404,'chemistry','The measured emf of the cell:\nPt(s) | H2(g, 1 bar) | H⁺(aq, 1 M) || Cu⁺² (aq, 1 M) | Cu is 0.34 V and it is also the value for the standard electrode potential of the half-cell corresponding to the reaction:\nCu⁺² (aq, 1M) + 2 e– ----> Cu(s)\nSimilarly, the measured emf of the cell:\nPt(s) | H2(g, 1 bar) | H+ (aq, 1 M) || Zn⁺² (aq, 1M) | Zn is -0.76 V corresponding to the standard electrode potential of the half-cell reaction:\nZn⁺² (aq, 1 M) + 2e– ----> Zn(s)\nThe positive value of the standard electrode potential in the first case indicates that','Cu⁺² ions-get reduced more easily than H+ ions,hydrogen ions-cannot oxidise Cu,Cu-does not dissolve in HCl,hydrogen gas-can reduce copper ion','The positive value of the standard electrode potential in the first case indicates that Cu⁺² ions get reduced more easily than H⁺ ions\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 69; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'2','match','Cu⁺² ions, get reduced more easily than H⁺ ions','hydrogen ions, cannot oxidize Cu','Cu,does not dissolve in HCl','hydrogen gas,can reduce copper ion','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(170405,'chemistry','Which of the metals are used as inert electrodes?','Both','Sometimes metals like platinum or gold are used as inert electrodes. They do not participate in the reaction but provide their surface for oxidation or reduction reactions and for the conduction of electrons.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 69; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'1','mcq','platinum','gold','Both','None of these','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(170406,'chemistry','Which electrodes do not participate in the reaction but provide their surface for oxidation or reduction reactions and for the conduction of electrons?','Inert','Sometimes metals like platinum or gold are used as inert electrodes. They do not participate in the reaction but provide their surface for oxidation or reduction reactions and for the conduction of electrons.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 69; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'1','mcq','Acidic','Inert','Basic','none of these','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(170407,'chemistry','If the standard electrode potential of an electrode is ___  than zero then its reduced form is more stable compared to hydrogen gas','greater','If the standard electrode potential of an electrode is greater than zero then its reduced form is more stable compared to hydrogen gas\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 70; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'1','fillup','gre,ater,le,ss,er',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(170408,'chemistry','If the standard electrode potential is ___ then hydrogen gas is more stable than the reduced form of the species.','negative','If the standard electrode potential is negative then hydrogen gas is more stable than the reduced form of the species.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 70; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'1','fillup','ne,ga,ti,ve,po,sit,ive',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(170409,'chemistry','The standard electrode potential for ___ is the highest.','fluorine','The standard electrode potential for fluorine is the highest indicating that fluorine gas (F2) has the maximum tendency to get reduced to fluoride ions (F⁻) and therefore fluorine gas is the strongest oxidizing agent and fluoride ion is the weakest reducing agent.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 70; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'1','fillup','flu,or,ine,ca,rb,on',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(170410,'chemistry','Arrange the following according to their tendency to get reduced in increasing order.','Fluorine gas >Lithium ion > Fluoride ion > Lithium metal','Lower the standard electrode potential value, greater the reducing power.\n \nFluorine gas (F₂) has the maximum tendency to get reduced to fluoride ions (F⁻) and therefore fluorine gas is the strongest oxidizing agent and fluoride ion is the weakest reducing agent. \n\nLithium metal is the most powerful reducing agent in an aqueous solution - Lithium ions possess greater hydration energy and have a tendency to lose electrons; hence lithium is considered a strong reducing agent.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 71; Table No. 3.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'3','order','1,Fluorine gas','2,Lithium ion','3,Fluoride ion','4,Lithium metal','NEW','12-071.','12-037.',NULL),
(170411,'chemistry','Which cells are extensively used for determining the pH of solutions, solubility product, the equilibrium constant, and other thermodynamic properties and for potentiometric titrations?','Electrochemical cells','Electrochemical cells are extensively used for determining the pH of solutions, solubility product, equilibrium constant and other thermodynamic properties and for potentiometric titrations.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 70; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','Dry cell','Standard cell','Electrochemical cells','Standard hydrogen electrode','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(170412,'chemistry','Can you store copper sulphate solutions in a zinc pot?','No','Copper is less reactive than zinc. Zinc will replace the copper from its salt solution. When the copper sulphate solution is stored in a zinc pot, copper is replaced by zinc, in the copper sulphate solution. So copper sulphate solution cannot be stored in a zinc pot.  \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 70; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'2','mcq','Yes','No','In presence of HCl','In presence of kerosene oil','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(170413,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion: A standard hydrogen electrode is also called a reversible electrode\nReason: Standard hydrogen electrode can act both as anode as well as cathode in an electrochemical cell','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','At 298 K the emf of the cell, standard hydrogen electrode second half-cell constructed by taking standard hydrogen electrode as anode (reference half-cell) and the other half-cell as cathode, gives the reduction potential of the other half-cell.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 69; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(170414,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion : Cu⁺² ions get reduced more easily than H⁺ ions\nReason : Standard electrode potential of copper is 0.34 V','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The positive value of the standard electrode potential in the first case indicates that Cu⁺² ions get reduced more easily than H⁺ ions\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 69; Paragraph No. 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(170415,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\nAssertion : Lithium has the lowest electrode potential\nReason : Lithium ion is the strongest oxidising agent','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Lithium has the lowest electrode potential indicating that lithium ion is the weakest oxidising agent while lithium metal is the most powerful reducing agent in an aqueous solution.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 70; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(170416,'chemistry','What is the order of these in the electrode potential table?','F > Cu⁺² > H⁺ > Li⁺','The table indicates that F is at the top and Li⁺ is at the bottom\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 71; Table No. 3.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'3','order','1,F','2,Cu⁺²','3,H⁺','4,Li⁺','NEW','12-071','12-037','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/170416q.png'),
(170417,'chemistry','What is the order of tendency of these to oxidise other ?','F > Cu⁺² > H⁺ > Li⁺','The higher the reduction potential, the more oxidising that element/ion\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 71; Table No. 3.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of Electrode Potential',NULL,'3','order','1,F','2,Cu⁺²','3,H⁺','4,Li⁺','NEW','12-071','12-037',NULL),
(170501,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct ?\na. A negative E⁻ means that the redox couple is a stronger reducing agent than the H⁺/H₂ couple.\nb. A positive E⁻ means that the redox couple is a weaker reducing agent than the H⁺/H₂ couple','Both statements are correct','A negative E⁻ means that the redox couple is a stronger reducing agent than the H⁺/H₂ couple while a positive E⁻ means that the redox couple is a weaker reducing agent than the H⁺/H₂ couple\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 71; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Both statements are correct','Both statements are incorrect','NEW','12-071','12-037',NULL),
(170502,'chemistry','A ____ E⁰ means that the redox couple is a stronger reducing agent than the H⁺/H₂ couple.','negative','A negative E⁰ means that the redox couple is a stronger reducing agent than the H⁺/H₂  couple. A positive E⁰ means that the redox couple is a weaker reducing agent than the H⁺/H₂ couple.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 71)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'1','fillup','ne,gat,ive,pos,it,ive',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-071','12-037',NULL),
(170503,'chemistry','A ___ E⁰ means that the redox couple is a weaker reducing agent than the H⁺/H₂ couple.','positive','A negative E⁰ means that the redox couple is a stronger reducing agent than the H⁺/H₂  couple. A positive E⁰ means that the redox couple is a weaker reducing agent than the H⁺/H₂ couple.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 71)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'1','fillup','po,sit,ive,neg,at,ive',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-071','12-037',NULL),
(170504,'chemistry','E₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎ = E⁰₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎  –RT/nF lnQ . \n\nWhich equation is this?','Nernst equation','Nernst equation can be written as: E₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎ = E⁰₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎  –RT/nF lnQ\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 72)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'2','mcq','Gibbs equation','Nernst equation','Equilibrium constant equation','Cell potential equation','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(170505,'chemistry','Mg(s) + 2Ag⁺(0.0001M) →  Mg⁺²(0.130M) + 2Ag(s)\n\nWhat will be the E₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎ if E⁰₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎ = 3.17 V?','2.96 V','E𝒸ₑₗₗ=E⁰- 0.0591/2 ln (Mg⁺²) / (Ag⁺²)     \n\nE𝒸ₑₗₗ= 3.17 - 0.0591/2 log[0.130/10⁻⁸] = 2.96V \n  \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 73; Example No. 3.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'2','mcq','2.96 V','3.6 V','0 V','1.96 V','NEW','12-073','12-039',NULL),
(170506,'chemistry','The standard electrode potential for Daniell cell is 1.1V. What is the standard Gibbs energy for the reaction:\n\nZn(s) + Cu⁺²(aq) --> Zn⁺²(aq) + Cu(s)','–212.27 kJ mol⁻¹','ΔG⁰ = – nFE⁰ ₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎\n\nIn the above equation n is 2, F = 96487 C mol⁻¹ and E⁰₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎ = 1.1 V.  \n\nTherefore, ΔG⁰  = – 2 × 1.1V × 96487 C mol⁻¹    = – 212.27 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 74; Example No. 3.3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'2','mcq','-112.27 kJ mol⁻¹','112.27 kJ mol⁻¹','212.27 kJ mol⁻¹','–212.27 kJ mol⁻¹','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(170507,'chemistry','Calculate the potential of hydrogen electrode in contact with a solution whose pH is 10.','-0.591V','Given pH=10,  we know that pH=−log[H⁺]    also for hydrogen electrode : E 𝒸ₑₗₗ=E⁰ − 0.0591/1 ​log [H⁺/∣H₂∣]   (no of electron included =1)  E=0   E cell = −0.0591/1 ​ log (1/10⁻¹⁰ )\n E𝒸ₑₗₗ =-0.591V         ​\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 75; Example 3.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'3','mcq','-0.591V','0.591V','-0.0591V','0.0591V','NEW','12-075','12-041',NULL),
(170508,'chemistry','What is the emf of the cell in which the following reaction takes place:  Ni(s) + 2Ag⁺ (0.002 M) -->  Ni⁺² (0.160 M) + 2Ag(s)  Given that E⁰₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎ = 1.05 V','0.91V','Given, [Ag⁺]=0.002M        [Ni⁺²]=0.160M    n=2    \nAccording to the Nernst equation:    E𝒸ₑₗₗ =E⁰₍𝒸ₑₗₗ₎ − 0.0591/2  log [Ni⁺²] / [Ag ⁺²]²   \n∴ E𝒸ₑₗₗ =1.05− 0.0591/2​ log [0.160/(0.002)²]       E𝒸ₑₗₗ= 0.91V\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 75; Example 3.5)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'2','mcq','1.91V','-0.91V','0.91V','-1.91V','NEW','12-075','12-041',NULL),
(170509,'chemistry','Calculate the equilibrium constant of the reaction:\nCu(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) -----> Cu⁺²(aq) + 2Ag(s)','3.92 × 10¹⁵','E⁰𝒸ₑₗₗ=(0.059 V)/2 log K𝒸 = 0.46 V\nor log K𝒸 = 15.6\nK𝒸 = 3.92 X 10¹⁵\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 74; Example No. 3.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'2','mcq','1.5 × 10¹⁵','39.2 × 10¹⁵','3.92 × 10¹⁵','15.6','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(170510,'chemistry','Arrange the following metals in the order in which they displace each other from their salts','Mg >  Al > Zn > Fe','A metal having stronger reducing power displaces another metal having weaker reducing power from its salt solution. The increasing order of reducing power is Cu<Fe<Zn<Al<Mg. Thus, Mg can displace Al from its salt solution but Al cannot displace Mg. The order in which the metals displace each other from their salt solutions is Mg>Al>Zn>Fe\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'3','order','1,Mg','2,Al','3,Zn','4,Fe','NEW','12-071','12-037',NULL),
(170511,'chemistry','Given the standard electrode potentials, \n\nK⁺/K=-2.93 V, Hg⁺²/Hg =0.79V, Mg⁺²/Mg=-2.37V, Cr⁺³/Cr=-0.74V. \n\nArrange these metals in their increasing order of reducing power.','K⁺/K > Mg⁺²/Mg > Cr⁺³/Cr > Hg⁺²/Hg','The higher the oxidation potential more easily it is oxidized and hence greater the reducing power. \n\nThus, the increasing order of reducing power will be Hg<Cr<Mg<K\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'3','order','1,K⁺/K','2,Mg⁺²/Mg','3,Cr⁺³/Cr','4,Hg⁺²/Hg','NEW','12-071','12-037',NULL),
(170512,'chemistry','Calculate the standard cell potentials of the galvanic cells in which the following reactions take place\n\n2Cr(s)+3Cd⁺²(aq)→2Cr⁺³aq) + Cd(s)\n\nGiven E⁰ Cr⁺³ / Cr = − 0.74 V , E⁰ Cd⁺²/ Cd = − 0.40 V','0.34 V','E𝒸ₑₗₗ=E⁰ᵣᵢ₉ₕₜ-E⁰ₗₑfₜ=-0.4-(-0.74)=0.34 V\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'2','mcq','0.34 V','-0.34 V','3.4 V','-3.4 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(170513,'chemistry','Calculate the standard cell potentials of the galvanic cells in which the following reactions take place :\n\nFe⁺² (aq)+Ag⁺(aq)→Fe⁺³aq)+Ag(s)','0.03 V','E⁰ =E⁰𝒸ₐₜₕₒₔₑ − E⁰ ₐₙₒₔₑ  = 0.80−(−0.77)=0.03 V\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 93; Exercise No. 3.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'2','mcq','- 0.03 V','0.03 V','0.3 V','-1.57 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(170514,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R).\n\nAssertion : To obtain maximum work from a galvanic cell charge has to passed reversibly\n\nReason : the reversible work done by a galvanic cell is equal to decrease in its Gibbs energy','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','If we want to obtain maximum work from a galvanic cell then the charge has to be passed reversibly.\n \nThe reversible work done by a galvanic cell is equal to a decrease in its Gibbs energy\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 74; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(170515,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\nAssertion : When a copper wire is dipped in silver nitrate solution, there is change in the color of the solution\n\nReason : Copper can displace silver from its salt solution','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Cu is more reactive than Ag hence it displaces silver from its salt solution.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 71; Table No. 3.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Nernst Equation',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Assertion is false but Reason is true','NEW','12-071','12-037',NULL),
(250713,'chemistry','If air gets into fermentation mixture, the oxygen of air oxidises ethanol to?','Ethanoic Acid','If air gets into fermentation mixture, the oxygen of air oxidises ethanol to ethanoic acid.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 6)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'1','mcq','Phenoxide Ion','Ethanoic Acid','Acetic Acid','Carbon dioxide','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(170601,'chemistry','The electrical resistance is measured in terms of?','ohm','The electrical resistance is represented by the symbol ‘R’ and it is measured in ohm (Ω) which in terms of SI base units is equal to (kg m²)/(S³ A²). \n\nIt can be measured with the help of a Wheatstone bridge with which you are familiar from your study of physics.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 75; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','ohm','volt','ohm⁻¹','ampere','NEW','12-075','12-041',NULL),
(170602,'chemistry','The electrical resistance of any object is directly proportional to which of the following.','length','The electrical resistance of any object is directly proportional to its length, l, and inversely proportional to its area of cross-section, A.\n \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 75; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','area','length','volume','diameter','NEW','12-075','12-041',NULL),
(170603,'chemistry','The constant of proportionality, r (Greek, rho), is called ___.','resistivity','The constant of proportionality, r (Greek, rho), is called resistivity (specific resistance).\n \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 75; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','fillup','resi,sti,vi,ty,con,du,ct,ivity',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-075','12-041',NULL),
(170604,'chemistry','What is the inverse of resistance, R, is called?','conductance','The inverse of resistance, R, is called conductance, G, \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 75; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','resistance','conductivity','conductance','resistivity','NEW','12-075','12-041',NULL),
(170605,'chemistry','What is the SI unit of conductance?','siemens','The SI unit of conductance is siemens, represented by the symbol ‘S’ and is equal to 1/ohm (also known as mho) or Ω–1.  \n   \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 75; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','volt','ohm','siemens','ampere','NEW','12-075','12-041',NULL),
(170606,'chemistry','What is the inverse of resistivity is called?','conductivity','The inverse of resistivity, called conductivity (specific conductance) is represented by the symbol, k (Greek, kappa), Conductivity has SI units of siemens per metre (S/m).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 75; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','conductivity','resistance','conductance','resistivity','NEW','12-075','12-041',NULL),
(170607,'chemistry','Materials are classified into conductors, insulators and semiconductors depending on the magnitude of their _____.','conductivity','Materials are classified into conductors, insulators and semiconductors depending on the magnitude of their conductivity. \n \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 76; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','fillup','con,du,ct,ivity,res,is,tance',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(170608,'chemistry','Metals and their ___ have very large conductivity and are known as conductors.','alloys','Metals and their alloys have very large conductivity and are known as conductors.\n  \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 76; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','fillup','al,lo,ys,iso,to,pes',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(170609,'chemistry','Match the following.','graphite-conductor,glass-insulator,silicon-semiconductor,','Conductors: Sodium, Copper, Silver, Gold, Iron, Graphite\n\nInsulators: Glass, Teflon\n\nSemiconductors: CuO, Si, Ge\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 76; Table 3.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'3','match','graphite,conductor','glass,insulator','silicon,semiconductor',NULL,'NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(170610,'chemistry','Which of the nonmetals is electronically conducted?','All of these','Certain non-metals like carbon-black, graphite and some organic polymers like polyaniline, polypyrrole, and polythiophene are also electronically conducting.\n  \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 76; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon-black','Graphite','Polyaniline','All of these','NEW','12-076','12-042','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/170610q.png'),
(170611,'chemistry','Which of the following have very low conductivity are known as insulators?','glass, ceramics','Substances like glass,ceramics, etc., having very low conductivity are known as insulators. \n  \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 76; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','glass, ceramics','glass, graphite','graphite, ceramics','carbon-black,glass','NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(170612,'chemistry','Substances like silicon, doped silicon and gallium arsenide having conductivity between conductors and insulators are called ____ and are important electronic materials.','semiconductors','Substances like silicon, doped silicon, and gallium arsenide having conductivity between conductors and insulators are called semiconductors and are important electronic materials.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 76; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','fillup','sem,ico,nduct,ors,con,du,ctors',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(170613,'chemistry','Certain materials called _____ by definition have zero resistivity or infinite conductivity.','superconductors','Certain materials called superconductors by definition have zero resistivity or infinite conductivity.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 76; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','fillup','sup,erco,ndu,ctors,sup,erco,nduc,tors',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(170614,'chemistry','Earlier, only metals and their alloys at very low temperatures (0 to 15 K) were known to behave as superconductors, but nowadays a number of ceramic materials and mixed oxides are also known to show superconductivity at temperatures as high as X, What is x here?','150 K','Earlier, only metals and their alloys at very low temperatures (0 to 15 K) were known to behave as superconductors, but nowadays a number of ceramic materials and mixed oxides are also known to show superconductivity at temperatures as high as 150 K.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 76; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','50K','100K','150 K','200K','NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(170615,'chemistry','Electrical conductance through metals is called metallic or electronic conductance and is due to the movement of  ________.','electrons','Electrical conductance through metals is called metallic or electronic conductance and is due to the movement of electrons.\n \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 76; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'1','fillup','ele,ctro,ns,protons',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(250714,'chemistry','Ethanol is a colourless liquid with boiling point?','351 K','Ethanol is a colourless liquid with boiling point 351 K.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 7)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'1','mcq','351 K','420 K','235 K','531 K','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(170616,'chemistry','The electronic conductance depends on;\n\n(i) the nature and structure of the metal\n\n(ii) the number of valence electrons per atom\n\n(iii) temperature (it decreases with increase of temperature)','all of them','The electronic conductance depends on;\n\n(i) the nature and structure of the metal\n\n(ii) the number of valence electrons per atom\n\n(iii) temperature (it decreases with the increase of temperature)  \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 76; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Conductance of Electrolytic Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','i & ii only','ii & iii only','i & iii only','all of them','NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(170701,'chemistry','Accurate measurement of an unknown resistance in an ionic solution can be performed on a _____.','wheatstonebridge','Accurate measurement of an unknown resistance in an ionic solution can be performed on a Wheatstone bridge. \n  \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 77; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,'1','fillup','at,st,bri,whe,dge,one',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(170702,'chemistry','Which of the following options will you need to determine the resistance of a conductivity cell?','ρ l/A, l/kA','R=ρl/A=l/kA, ρ=1/k\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 77; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','ρ l/A, l/kA','l/kA, ρ A/l','l/A','ρ A/l','NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(170703,'chemistry','The quantity l/A is called ____ constant denoted by the symbol, G.','cell','The quantity l/A is called cell constant denoted by the symbol, G. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 78; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,'1','fillup','ce,ll,gra,vit,atio,nal',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-078','12-044',NULL),
(170704,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct?\n\n(a) Cell constant depends on the distance between the electrodes and their area of cross-section  \n\n(b) Cell constant has the dimension of length\n\n(c) Cell constant has the dimension of length²','Only a','Cell constant:\n\n(i) depends on the distance between the electrodes and their area of cross-section\n  \n(ii) has the dimension of length⁻¹.  \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 78; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only a & c','Both a & b','Only b','NEW','12-078','12-044',NULL),
(170705,'chemistry','The Wheatstone bridge is fed by an oscillator, a source of a.c. power in the audio frequency range X to Z cycles per second. What are X and Z respectively?','550 ,5000','The Wheatstone bridge is fed by an oscillator, a source of a.c. power in the audio frequency range 550 to 5000 cycles per second.\n \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 78; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','550 ,5000','500, 5500','500. 5000','550, 5500','NEW','12-078','12-044',NULL),
(170706,'chemistry','The conductivity of the solution is equal to?','Cell constant /Resistance','Once the cell constant and the resistance of the solution in the cell is determined, the conductivity of the solution is given by the equation:\n\nk=Cell constant /Resistance\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 78; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Resistance/Cell constant','Cell constant /Resistance','Cell constant','Resistance','NEW','12-078','12-044',NULL),
(170707,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct?\n\na. The conductivity of solutions of different electrolytes in the same solvent and at a given temperature differs due to the charge and size of the ions in which they dissociate\n\nb. Molar conductivity is related to the conductivity of the solution by the equation: ᴧₘ=conductivity/concentration\n\nc. The conductivity of solutions of different electrolytes in the same solvent and at a given temperature differs due to the change of concentration of ions','All of these','The conductivity of solutions of different electrolytes in the same solvent and at a given temperature differs due to the charge and size of the ions in which they dissociate, the concentration of ions, or the ease with which the ions move under a potential gradient.  \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 78; Paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Only b','Only b and c','All of these','NEW','12-078','12-044','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/170707q.png'),
(170708,'chemistry','Which of the following is equal to molar conductivity?','conductivity/concentration','Molar conductivity, ᴧₘ = k/C.  \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 79; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','conductivity/concentration','cell constant /resistance','concentration/conductivity','none of these','NEW','12-079','12-045','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/170708q.png'),
(170709,'chemistry','The resistance of a conductivity cell filled with 0.1 mol/L KCl solution is 100 Ω. \n\nIf the resistance of the same cell when filled with 0.02 mol/L KCl solution is 520 Ω.\n\nCalculate the conductivity and molar conductivity of 0.02 mol/L KCl solution.\n \nThe conductivity of 0.1 mol/L KCl solution is 1.29 S/m.','124 S cm²/mol','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 79; Example No. 3.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','-124 S cm²/ mol','124 S cm²/mol','114 S cm²/ mol','24 S cm²/ mol','NEW','12-079','12-045','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/170709q.png'),
(170710,'chemistry','The electrical resistance of a column of 0.05 mol/L NaOH solution of diameter 1 cm and length 50 cm is 5.55 × 10³ ohm.\n \nCalculate its resistivity, conductivity and molar conductivity.','229.6 x 10⁻⁴ S m²/mol','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 80; Example No. 3.5)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Measurement of the Conductivity of Ionic Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','229.6 S cm²/mol','22.9 S cm²/ mol','229.6 x 10⁻⁴ S m²/mol','129.6 S m²/ mol','NEW','12-080','12-046','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/170710q.png'),
(170801,'chemistry','Which of the following changes with the concentration of the electrolyte?\n\na) conductivity\n\nb) molar conductivity\n\nc) critical voltage','All of these','Conductivity and molar conductivity and critical voltage change with the concentration of the electrolyte.\n \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 80; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only c','Only b and c','All of these','NEW','12-080','12-046',NULL),
(170802,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\na. Conductivity always decreases with a decrease in concentration both, for weak and strong electrolytes.\n\nb. Number of ions per unit volume that carry the current in a solution decreases on dilution.\n\nc. Conductivity always increases with a decrease in concentration both, for weak and strong electrolytes.','Only a and b','Conductivity always decreases with a decrease in concentration both, for weak and strong electrolytes. \n\nThis can be explained by the fact that the number of ions per unit volume that carry the current in a solution decreases on dilution.\n  \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 80; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only b','Only c','NEW','12-080','12-046',NULL),
(170803,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\na. The conductivity of a solution at any given concentration is the conductance of one unit volume of solution kept between two platinum electrodes with a unit area of cross section and at a distance of unit length.\n\nb. G = kA/l (G= Conductance, k = conductivity, A = area of cross-section, l = length)\n\nc. G = l/kA (G= Conductance, k = conductivity, A = area of cross-section, l = length)','Only a and b','The conductivity of a solution at any given concentration is the conductance of one unit volume of solution kept between two platinum electrodes with unit area of cross section and at a distance of unit length. \n\nThis is clear from the equation: G=k A/l = k. ((both A and l are unity in their appropriate units in m or cm)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 81; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only a and c','Only c','Only a','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(170804,'chemistry','What is the relation between molar conductivity( Λₘ) and G,k,A and l ? \n\n(G= Conductance, k = conductivity, A = area of cross section, l = length)','Λ ₘ=κA/l','Molar conductivity of a solution at a given concentration is the conductance of the volume V of solution containing one mole of electrolyte kept between two electrodes with area of cross-section A and distance of unit length. \n\nTherefore, Λₘ =κA/l\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 81; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'2','mcq','Λₘ =κA','Λₘ =κ','Λ ₘ=κA/l','none of these','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(170805,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\na. Molar conductivity increases with a decrease in concentration. \n\nb. Decrease in k (conductivity) on dilution of a solution is more than compensated by an increase in its volume.\n\nc. Molar conductivity increases with an increase in concentration.','Only a and b','Molar conductivity increases with a decrease in concentration. \n\nThis is because the total volume, V, of the solution containing one mole of electrolyte also increases \n \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 81; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only b','Only c','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(170806,'chemistry','When concentration approaches zero, the molar conductivity is known as _____ molar conductivity.','limiting','When concentration approaches zero, the molar conductivity is known as limiting molar conductivity. \n \nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 81; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'1','fillup','limi,ti,ng,un,lim,it,ed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(170807,'chemistry','Kohlrausch examined Λ ⁰ ₘ (limiting molar conductivity) values for a number of strong electrolytes and observed certain regularities. \n\nHe noted that the difference in  Λ ⁰ ₘ of the electrolytes NaX and KX for any X is nearly ____','constant','Kohlrausch examined Λ ⁰ ₘ values for a number of strong electrolytes and observed certain regularities.\n \nHe noted that the difference in Λ ⁰ ₘ of the electrolytes NaX and KX for any X is nearly constant. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 83; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'1','fillup','con,sta,nt,ze,ro',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(170808,'chemistry','Kohlrausch law of _____ migration of ions.','independent','Kohlrausch law of independent migration of ions. \n\nThe law states that the limiting molar conductivity of an electrolyte can be represented as the sum of the individual contributions of the anion and cation of the electrolyte.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 83; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'1','fillup','in,de,pend,ent,de,pen,de,nt',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(170809,'chemistry','Weak electrolytes like acetic acid have lower degree of dissociation at higher concentrations and hence for such electrolytes, \nthe change in  limiting molar conductivity with dilution is due to _____ in the degree of dissociation','increase','Weak electrolytes like acetic acid have a lower degree of dissociation at higher concentrations and hence for such electrolytes, the change in ᴧₘ with dilution is due to increase in the degree of dissociation and consequently the number of ions in total volume of solution that contains 1 mol of electrolyte. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 83; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'1','fillup','incr,eas,es,decr,ea,se',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(170810,'chemistry','Λ ⁰ ₘ (Limiting molar conductivity for NaCl, HCl and NaAc) are 126.4, 425.9 and 91.0 S cm² /mol respectively. \n\nCalculate Λ ⁰  ( limiting molar conductivity) for HAc.','390.5 S cm² /mol','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 84; example No. 3.8)\ndiagram(ne1704)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'2','mcq','-300.5 S cm² /mol','3.90 S cm² /mol','-39.05 S cm² /mol','390.5 S cm² /mol','NEW','12-084','12-050',NULL),
(170811,'chemistry','The conductivity of 0.001028 mol/L acetic acid is 4.95 × 10⁻⁵ S/ cm. \n\nCalculate its dissociation constant if ᴧ⁰ₘ for acetic acid is 390.5 S cm²/ mol.','1.78 × 10⁻⁵ mol/l','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 84; example No. 3.9)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'2','mcq','1.78 × 10⁻⁵ mol/l','178 × 10⁻⁵ mol/l','17.8 × 10⁻⁵ mol/l','0.178 × 10⁻⁵ mol/l','NEW','12-084','12-050',NULL),
(170812,'chemistry','Conductivity of a solution ____ because the number of ions in unit volume decreases','decreases','The conductivity of a solution is directly proportional to the number of ions present in a unit volume of the solution because current is carried forward by the ions. \nWith dilution number of ions in unit volume decreases so that conductivity also decreases. \n\nHence with dilution conductivity decreases.  \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 80; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Variation of Conductivity and Molar Conductivity with Concentration',NULL,'1','fillup','decr,ea,ses,incr,eas,es',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-080','12-046',NULL),
(170901,'chemistry','In an electrolytic cell ______ source of voltage is used to bring about a chemical reaction','external','In an electrolytic cell, an external source of voltage is used to bring about a chemical reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 85; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','fillup','ex,ter,nal,int,ern,al',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(170902,'chemistry','Which of the following metals is produced on large scale by electrochemical reduction of their respective cations where no suitable chemical reducing agents are available for this purpose ?','All of these','Many metals like Na, Mg, Al, etc. are produced on large scale by electrochemical reduction of their respective cations where no suitable chemical reducing agents are available for this purpose.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 85; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Na','Mg','Al','All of these','NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(170903,'chemistry','Which of the following metals are produced by the electrolysis of their fused chlorides?\n\na) Sodium\n\nb) Magnesium\n\nc) Copper','Only a and b','Sodium and magnesium metals are produced by the electrolysis of their fused chlorides.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 85; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(170904,'chemistry','Aluminium is produced by the electrolysis of aluminium oxide in presence of ____','cryolite','Aluminium is produced by the electrolysis of aluminium oxide in presence of cryolite\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 85; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','fillup','cr,yo,li,te,zi,nc',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(170905,'chemistry','___ was the first scientist who described the quantitative aspects of electrolysis','Faraday','Michael Faraday was the first scientist who described the quantitative aspects of electrolysis\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 85; Paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','fillup','Fa,ra,day,koh,lr,aush',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(170906,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n\na. According to Faraday’s first law, the amount of chemical reaction which occurs at any electrode during electrolysis by a current is proportional to the quantity of electricity passed through the electrolyte\n\nb.  According to Faraday’s second law, amounts of different substances liberated by the same quantity of electricity passing through the electrolytic solution are proportional to their chemical equivalent weights\n\nc. According to Faraday’s third law, amounts of different substances liberated by the same quantity of electricity passing through the electrolytic solution are proportional to their chemical equivalent density','Only a and b','Faraday’s Laws of Electrolysis\n\n(a) First Law: The amount of chemical reaction which occurs at any electrode during electrolysis by a current is proportional to the quantity of electricity passed through the electrolyte (solution or melt).\n\n(b) Second Law: The amounts of different substances liberated by the same quantity of electricity passing through the electrolytic solution are proportional to their chemical equivalent weights (Atomic Mass of Metal ÷ Number of electrons required to reduce the cation).\n\n(c) There is no Faraday\'s third law\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 85; Paragraph 5)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(170907,'chemistry','The amount of electricity (or charge) required for oxidation or reduction depends on the ____ of the electrode reaction','stoichiometry','The amount of electricity (or charge) required for oxidation or reduction depends on the stoichiometry of the electrode reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 86; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Electrolytic cells and electrolysis',NULL,'1','fillup','stoi,chio,metry,im,pur,ity',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171001,'chemistry','A battery is a ___ cell','galvanic','Any battery (actually it may have one or more than one cell connected in series) or cell that we use as a source of electrical energy is basically a galvanic cell where the chemical energy of the redox reaction is converted into electrical energy\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 88; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','fillup','ga,lv,an,ic,el,ec,tro,lytic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(171002,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct?\n\na. In the primary batteries, the reaction occurs only once, and after use over a period of time battery becomes dead and cannot be reused again\n\nb. The most familiar example of a primary battery is the dry cell\n\nc. Statement (a) opposes for the secondary batteries','All of these','In the primary batteries, the reaction occurs only once, and after use over a period of time battery becomes dead and cannot be reused again. \n\nThe most familiar example of this type is the dry cell (known as the Leclanche cell after its discoverer) which is used commonly in our transistors and clocks\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 88; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(171003,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct for the primary batteries?\n\na. The cell consists of a zinc container that also acts as an anode\n\nb. The cathode is a carbon (graphite) rod surrounded by powdered manganese dioxide and carbon\n\nc. The space between the electrodes is filled with a moist paste of ammonium chloride and zinc chloride','All of these','The cell consists of a zinc container that also acts as an anode and the cathode is a carbon (graphite) rod surrounded by powdered manganese dioxide and carbon (Fig.3.8). \n\nThe space between the electrodes is filled with a moist paste of ammonium chloride (NH₄Cl) and zinc chloride (ZnCl₂).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 88; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(171004,'chemistry','____ cell is suitable for low current devices  like hearing aids, watches','Mercury','Mercury cell, is suitable for low current devices like hearing aids, watches.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 88; Paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','fillup','Me,rc,ury,chro,mi,um',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(171005,'chemistry','The cell potential in mercury cell is approximately','1.35 V','The cell potential of mercury cell is approximately 1.35 V and remains constant during its life as the overall reaction does not involve any ion in solution whose concentration can change during its life time\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 89; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','mcq','1.35 V','135 V','35 V','13.5 V','NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171006,'chemistry','A secondary cell after use can be recharged by passing current through it in the ___ direction so that it can be used again','opposite','A secondary cell after use can be recharged by passing current through it in the opposite direction so that it can be used again.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 89; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','fillup','op,po,site,sa,me',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171007,'chemistry','The most important secondary cell is the ____ storage battery','lead','The most important secondary cell is the lead storage battery  commonly used in automobiles and invertors\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 89; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','fillup','le,ad,ca,rb,on',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171008,'chemistry','What percent solution of sulphuric acid is used as an electrolyte in lead storage battery?','38%','A 38% solution of sulphuric acid is used as an electrolyte.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 89; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','mcq','38%','8%','48%','28%','NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171009,'chemistry','Which cell has longer life than the lead storage cell but more expensive to manufacture?','Nickel-cadmium','Nickel-cadmium cell has a longer life than the lead storage cell but is more expensive to manufacture\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 90; Paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','mcq','Nickel-cadmium','Nickel-Argon','Argon-cadmium','The Lead storage cell','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171010,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct?\n\na. Production of electricity by thermal plants is not a very efficient method and is a major source of pollution\n\nb. In thermal plants, the chemical energy of fossil fuels is used for converting water into high-pressure steam\n\nc. High pressure steam is used to run a turbine to produce electricity','All of these','Production of electricity by thermal plants is not a very efficient method and is a major source of pollution. \n\nIn such plants, the chemical energy (heat of combustion) of fossil fuels (coal, gas, or oil) is first used for converting water into high-pressure steam. \n\nThis is then used to run a turbine to produce electricity.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 90; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'3','mcq','Only a and b','Only b and c','Only a and c','All of these','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171011,'chemistry','Galvanic cells that are designed to convert the energy of combustion of fuels like hydrogen, methane, methanol, etc. directly into electrical energy are called ___  cells','Fuel','Galvanic cells that are designed to convert the energy of combustion of fuels like hydrogen, methane, methanol, etc. directly into electrical energy are called fuel cells.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 90; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','fillup','Fu,el,Ele,ctro,lytic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171012,'chemistry','Hydrogen- oxygen cell was used for providing electrical power in the ____ space programme.','Apollo','The hydrogen-oxygen cell is one of the most successful fuel cells that uses the reaction of hydrogen with oxygen to form water. \n\nThe cell was used for providing electrical power in the Apollo space programme.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 90; Paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Batteries,fuel cells and corrosion',NULL,'1','fillup','Ap,ol,lo,te,sla',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171101,'chemistry','The standard electrode potential (E°) values of Al⁺³/Al, Ag⁺/Ag, K⁺/K and Cr⁺³/Cr are –1.66 V, 0.80 V, –2.93 V and –0.74 V, respectively. \n\nThe correct decreasing order of reducing power of the metal is','K > Al > Cr > Ag','The higher the value of reduction  E°, the stronger the oxidizing power. \n\nThus, the decreasing order of reducing the power of the metal is K > Al > Cr > Ag\n(NEET Odisha 2019)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','order','1,K','2,Al','3,Cr','4,Ag','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171102,'chemistry','A button cell used in watches function as following :\n\nZn(s) + Ag₂O(s) + H₂O(l) ⇌  2Ag(s) + Zn⁺² (aq) = 2OH⁻ (aq)\n\nIf half cell potentials are :\n\nZn⁺² (aq) +2e⁻ → Zn , E° = – 0.76 V\n\nAg₂O(s) + H₂O (l) +2e⁻  → 2Ag(s) + 2OH⁻ (aq) ,   E° = 0.34 V\n\nThe cell potential will be :','1.10 V','E°cell = E°ₒₚ. + E°ᵣₚ \n\n= 0.76 + 0.34 = 1.10 V\n\n(NEET 2013)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.10 V','0.84 V','0.42 V','-0.42 V','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(171103,'chemistry','Standard reduction potentials of the half reactions are given below :\n\nF₂(g)+2e⁻ → 2F⁻ (aq.),  E° = + 2.85 V \n\nCl₂(g)+2e⁻ → 2Cl⁻ (aq.) , E° = + 1.36 V\n\nBr₂(g)+2e⁻ → 2Br ⁻ (aq.), ; E° = + 1.06 V\n\nI₂(s)+2e⁻ → 2l⁻ (aq.), E° = + 0.53 V\n\nThe strongest oxidising and reducing agents respectively are :','F₂ and I⁻','Less positive the value of reduction potential, stronger will be the reducing agent thus, I⁻ is strongest  reducing agent. \n\nMore positive, the value of reduction potential shows good oxidising properties thus, strongest oxidising agent is F₂\n\n(NEET 2012)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','F₂ and I⁻','Br₂ and Cl⁻','Cl₂ and Br⁻','Cl₂ and I₂','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171104,'chemistry','Standard electrode potentials of three metals X, Y and Z are –1.2 V, + 0.5 V and – 3.0 V respectively.\n \nThe order of  reducing power of these metals will be','Z > X > Y','More negative the value of reduction potential, stronger will be the reducing agent. \n\nSo, Z (–3.0 V) > X (–1.2 V) > Y (+ 0.5 V)\n\n(NEET 2011)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','order','1,Z','2,X','3,Y',NULL,'NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171106,'chemistry','A solution contains Fe⁺², Fe⁺³ and I⁻ ions. \n\nThis solution was treated with iodine at 35°C. \n\nE° for Fe⁺³/Fe⁺² is + 0.77 V and E° for I₂/2I⁻ = 0.536 V. \n\nThe favourable redox reaction is','I⁻ will be oxidised to I₂','Since the reduction potential of Fe⁺³/ Fe⁺²  is greater than that of I₂/2I⁻, will be reduced and I⁻ will be oxidised.\n\n(NEET 2011)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I₂ will be reduced to I⁻','there will be no redox reaction','I⁻ will be oxidised to I₂','Fe⁺² will be oxidised to Fe⁺³','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171107,'chemistry','Consider the following relations for emf of an electrochemical cell.\n\n(i) EMF of cell = (Oxidation potential of anode) – (Reduction potential of cathode)\n\n(ii) EMF of cell = (Oxidation potential of anode) + (Reduction potential of cathode)\n\n(iii)EMF of cell = (Reduction potential of anode) + (Reduction potential of cathode)\n\n(iv) EMF of cell = (Oxidation potential of anode) – (Oxidation potential of cathode)\n\nWhich of the above relations are correct?','(ii) and (iv)','EMF of a cell = Reduction potential of cathode – Reduction potential of anode\n\n= Reduction potential of cathode + Oxidation potential of anode\n\n= Oxidation potential of anode – Oxidation potential of cathode.\n\n(NEET 2010)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(iii) and (i)','(i) and (ii)','(iii) and (iv)','(ii) and (iv)','NEW','12-069','12-035',NULL),
(171108,'chemistry','On the basis of the following E° values, the strongest oxidizing agent is \n\n[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ →  [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ + 1e⁻ , E° = –0.35 V\n\nFe⁺² → Fe⁺³ + 1e⁻ , ; E° = –0.77 V','Fe⁺³','The higher the +ve reduction potential, the stronger will be the oxidizing agent. \n\nOxidizing agent oxidizes other compounds and gets itself reduced easily. \n\nThus, Fe⁺³ is the strongest oxidizing agent.\n\n(NEET 2008)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe⁺³','[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻','[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻','Fe⁺','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171109,'chemistry','A hypothetical electrochemical cell is shown below :\n\nA/A⁺(xM )||B⁺(yM)/B \n\nThe emf measured is + 0.20 V. \n\nThe cell reaction is','A + B⁺ → A⁺ + B','From the given expression :\n\nAt anode : A → A⁺ + e⁻  (oxidation)\n\nAt cathode : B⁺ + e⁻ →  B (reduction)\n\nOverall reaction is :A + B⁺ → A⁺ + B\n\n(NEET 2006)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A + B⁺ → A⁺ + B','A⁺ + B → A + B⁺','A⁺ + e⁻ → A; B⁺ + e⁻ → B','the cell reaction cannot be predicted','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(171110,'chemistry','E° Fe⁺²/Fe = -0.44 V and E° Fe⁺³/Fe⁺² = 0.771 V, \n\nthe standard EMF of the reaction \n\nFe + 2Fe⁺³+ → 3Fe⁺²  will be','1.212 V','For the cell reaction\n\nFe + 2Fe⁺³+ → 3Fe⁺²\n\nAnode reaction is Fe→Fe⁺² +2e ⁻\n\nCathode reaction is 2Fe⁺³ +2e⁻  →2Fe⁺²\n\nE° cathode -  E°anode = (E° = reduction potential)\n\n= 0.771−(−0.441) = 1.212 V\n   \n(NEET 2006)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0.111 V','0.330 V','1.653 V','1.212 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171111,'chemistry','Standard electrode potentials are Fe⁺²/Fe;  \n\nE° = –0.44 and Fe⁺³/Fe⁺²; E° = 0.77. \n\nFe⁺², Fe⁺³ and Fe blocks are kept together, then','Fe⁺³ decreases','Fe⁺³ decreases as it would like to be reduced\n\n(NEET 2001)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe⁺³ increases','Fe⁺³ decreases','Fe⁺²/Fe⁺³ remains unchanged','Fe⁺² decreases','NEW','12-070','12-036','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/171111q.png'),
(171112,'chemistry','Electrode potential for the following half-cell  reactions are\n\nZn → Zn⁺² + 2e⁻ , E° = + 0.76 V; \n\nFe → Fe⁺² + 2e⁻, E° = + 0.44 V.\n\nThe EMF for the cell reaction \n\nFe⁺² + Zn → Zn⁺² + Fe will be','0.32 V','E°cell = E°cathode – E°anode\n\n= -0.44 - (-0.76) = 0.32 V\n\n(NEET 1996)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','- 0.32 V','0.32 V','1.20V','- 1.20 V','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(171113,'chemistry','An electrochemical cell is set up as :  \n\nPt; H₂ (1 atm)|HCl(0.1 M) || CH₃COOH (0.1 M) |H₂ (1 atm); Pt. \n\nThe e.m.f. of this cell will not be zero, because','pH of 0.1 M HCl and 0.1 M CH₃COOH is not same','Since it is a concentration cell and the concentration of H⁺ ions in two electrolyte solutions (HCl and CH₃COOH) are different i.e., pH of 0.1 M HCl and 0.1 M CH₃COOH is not the same.\n\nTherefore e.m.f. of this cell will not be zero.\n\n(NEET 1995)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','acids used in two compartments are different','e.m.f. depends on molarities of acids used','the temperature is constant','pH of 0.1 M HCl and 0.1 M CH₃COOH is not same','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(171114,'chemistry','Standard reduction potentials at 25°C of Li⁺|Li, Ba⁺²|Ba, Na⁺|Na and Mg⁺²|Mg are –3.05, –2.90, –2.71 and –2.37 volt respectively. \n\nWhich one of the following is the strongest oxidising agent?','Mg⁺²','More positive or less negative the reduction potential value, the stronger is the oxidising agent.\n\n(NEET 1994)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Na⁺','Mg⁺²','Li⁺','Ba⁺²','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171115,'chemistry','A solution of potassium bromide is treated with each of the following. \n\nWhich one would liberate bromine?','Chlorine','A stronger oxidising agent (Cl₂) displaces a weaker oxidising agent (Br₂) from its salt solution.\n\n(NEET 1993)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrogen iodide','Sulphur dioxide','Chlorine','Iodine','NEW','12-071','12-037',NULL),
(171116,'chemistry','For the cell reaction :\n\n2Fe⁺³ (aq) + 2I⁻(aq) 2Fe⁺²(aq) + I₂(aq)\n\n[Given that Faraday constant, F = 96500 C/mol]\n\nE°cell = 0.24 V at 298 K. \n\nThe standard Gibbs’ energy (ΔG°)of the cell reaction is :','-46.32 kJ mol⁻¹','The standard Gibbs’ energy,\n\n(ΔG°) = –nFE°cell. Value of n = 2\n\nΔG° = – 2 × 96500 × 0.24 = – 46320 J \n\n= – 46.32 kJ/mol\n\n(NEET 2019)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','-23.16 kJ mol⁻¹','-23.16 kJ mol⁻¹','46.32 kJ mol⁻¹','-46.32 kJ mol⁻¹','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(171117,'chemistry','For a cell involving one electron, E°cell = 0.59 V at  298 K, the equilibrium constant for the cell reaction is\n \n[Given that 2 303RT/F = 0.059 V  at T = 298 K]','1 x 10¹⁰','According to Nernst equation,  \n\nE𝒸ₑₗₗ =  E°cell -0.059/n x log Q꜀\n\nAt equilibrium E𝒸ₑₗₗ = 0, Q꜀ = K꜀\n\nE°cell = 0.059/n x log K꜀\n\n0.59 = 0.059 x log K꜀\n\nK꜀ = antilog 10 = 1 x 10¹⁰\n\n(NEET 2019)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1 x10²','1 x 10²⁰','1 x 10¹⁰','1 x 10⁵','NEW','12-073','12-039',NULL),
(171118,'chemistry','In the electrochemical cell : \n\nZn|ZnSO₄(0.01 M)||CuSO₄(1.0 M)|Cu, the emf of this Daniell cell is E₁. \n\nWhen the concentration of ZnSO₄ is changed to 1.0 M and that of CuSO₄ changed to 0.01 M, the emf changes to E₂.  \n\nFrom the followings, which one is the relationship between E₁ and E₂? (Given, RT/F = 0.059)','E₁ > E₂','E𝒸ₑₗₗ = E°cell - 0.059 x log [Zn⁺²/Cu⁺²]x 1/n\n\nE₁ =  E°cell - 0.059/2 x log 0.01/1\n\nE₁ =  E°cell - 0.059/2 x (-2) = E° + 0.059\n\nE₂ =  E°cell - 0.059/2 x log 1/0.01  = E° - 0.059\n\nHence E₁ > E₂\n\n(NEET 2017, 2003)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','E₁ > E₂','E₁ < E₂','E₁ = E₂','E₁ = 0 ≠ E₂','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(171119,'chemistry','If the E°cell for a given reaction has a negative value, which of the following gives the correct relationships for the values of ΔG° and Kₑq?','ΔG°> 0; Keq < 1','ΔG° = –nFE°cell\n\nIf E°cell = – ve then ΔG° = +ve i.e.; ΔG° > 0.\n\nΔG° = –nRT ln Keq\n\nFor ΔG° = +ve, Keq = –ve i.e., Keq < 1.\n\n(NEET-II 2016, 2011)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ΔG°> 0; Keq < 1','ΔG° > 0 ; Keq > 1','ΔG° < 0 Keq > 1','ΔG° < 0 Keq < 1','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171120,'chemistry','The pressure of H₂ required to make the potential of  H₂ electrode zero in pure water at 298 K is','10⁻¹⁴ atm',': pH = 7 for water.\n\n–log[H⁺] = 7 ⇒ [H⁺] = 10⁻⁷\n\n2H⁺ (aq) + 2e⁻ → H₂ (g)\n\nE𝒸ₑₗₗ = E°cell - 0.059/2 x  log ₚH₂/[H⁺]²  \n\n0 = 0 - 0.059/2 x log pH₂/[10⁻⁷]² \n\nlog ₚH₂/(10⁻⁷)² = 0\n\nHence ₚH₂ = 10⁻¹⁴ atm\n\n(NEET-I 2016)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','10⁻⁴ atm','10⁻¹⁰ atm','10⁻¹⁴ atm','10⁻¹² atm','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(171121,'chemistry','A hydrogen gas electrode is made by dipping platinum wire in a solution of HCl of pH = 10 and  by passing hydrogen gas around the platinum wire  at one atm pressure.\n \nThe oxidation potential of  electrode would be','0.59 V','E  ₕ₂/ₕ⁺  = 0 - 0.059/2 x log (10⁻¹⁰)²/1\n\nE  ₕ₂/ₕ⁺ = 0.59 V\n\n(NEET 2013)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.118 V','1.18 V','0.059 V','0.59 V','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(171122,'chemistry','Consider the half-cell reduction reaction\n\nMn⁺² + 2e⁻ → Mn , , E° = – 1.18 V\n\nMn⁺²  → Mn⁺³ = 1e⁻, , E° = – 1.51 V\n\nThe E° for the reaction,\n\n3Mn⁺² → Mn + 2Mn⁺³\n\nand possibility of the forward reaction are  respectively','– 2.69 V and no','Mn⁺² + 2e⁻-  → Mn , , E° = – 1.18 V\n\nMn⁺²  → Mn⁺³ = 1e⁻, , E° = – 1.51 V\n\nBy adding the two reactions, we get\n\n3Mn⁺² → Mn + 2Mn⁺³ , E° = -2.96 V\n\nSince the E° value is negative, so the process is non-spontaneous as ΔG° is positive\n\n(NEET Karnataka 2013)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','– 4.18 V and yes','+ 0.33 V and yes','+ 2.69 V and no','– 2.69 V and no','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171123,'chemistry','The Gibbs’ energy for the decomposition of Al₂O₃ at 500 °C is as follows\n\n2/3 Al₂O₃ → 4/3 Al + O₂ , ΔG = 960 kJ/mol\n\nThe potential difference needed for the electrolytic reduction of aluminium oxide (Al₂O₃ ) at 500 °C is  at least','2.5 V','ΔG° = – nFE°\n\nF = 96500, ΔG° = + 960 × 10³J/mol\n\n2/3 Al₂O₃ → 4/3 Al + O₂\n\nTotal number of Al atoms in  Al₂O₃ = 4/3\n\nAl⁺³ + 3e⁻ → Al\n\nAs 3e⁻  change occur for each Al-atom\n\nn = 4/3 x 3 = 4\n\nE° = -  ΔG°/nF = 960 x 1000/ 4 x 96500 = -2.5 V\n\n(NEET 2012)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4.5 V','5 V','3 V','2.5 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171124,'chemistry','The electrode potentials for,\n\nCu⁺²(aq)  + e⁻ → Cu⁺(aq)  and Cu⁺ + e⁻ →Cu(s) \n\nare + 0.15 V and + 0.50 V respectively. \n\nThe value of E° for  Cu⁺²/Cu will be','0.325 V','1) Cu⁺²(aq)  + e⁻ → Cu⁺(aq) E° = 0.15 V\n\n2) Cu⁺ + e⁻ →Cu(s), E° = + 0.500 V\n\n Cu⁺²(aq)  + 2e →Cu(s) ,E° = ?\n\nNow, ΔG° = ΔG₁° + ΔG₂°\n\n–nFE° = –n₁FE°₁ – n₂FE°₂\n\nE° = (n₁E₁° + n₂E₂°)/n\n\nE° = (1 x 0.15 + 1 x 0.5)/2 = 0.325 V\n\n(NEET 2011)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0.500 V','0.325 V','0.650V','0.150 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171125,'chemistry','For the reduction of silver ions with copper metal, the standard cell potential was found to be + 0.46 V at 25 °C. \n\nThe value of standard Gibbs energy, ΔG° will be (F = 96500 C/ mol)','– 89.0 kJ','The cell reaction can be written as\n\nCu + 2Ag⁺²  → Cu⁺² + 2Ag\n\nWe know, ΔG° = – nFE°cell\n\n= – 2 × 96500 × 0.46= – 88780 J\n\n= – 88.78 kJ ≈ – 89 kJ\n\n(NEET 2010)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','– 89.0 kJ','– 89.0 J','– 44.5 kJ','– 98.0 kJ','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171126,'chemistry','Given\n\nCu⁺²  + 2e⁻ → Cu E° = 0.337  V\n\nCu⁺² + e⁻ → Cu⁺, E° = + 0.153 V\n\nElectrode potential, E° for the reaction, \n\nCu⁺  + e →Cu will be','0.52 V','1) Cu⁺²  + 2e⁻ → Cu E° = 0.337  V\n\n2) Cu⁺² + e⁻ → Cu⁺, E°= + 0.153 V\n\n3) Cu⁺  + e →C , E° = ?\n\nNow,\n\n−(n₃FE₃) = −(n₁ FE₁)−(−n₂FE₂)\n\nE₃ = 2 × E₁– E₂\n\nE₃ =( 2 x 0.337) -(0.153) = 0.52 V\n\n(NEET 2009)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0.90 V','0.30 V','0.38 V','0.52 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171127,'chemistry','Standard free energies of formation (in kJ/mol) at  298 K are –237.2, –394.4 and –8.2 for H₂O(l) , CO₂(g) and pentane(g) respectively. \n\nThe value of E°cell for the pentane-oxygen fuel cell is','1.0968 V','(NEET 2008) \n\ndiagram(ne1701)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1.0968 V','0.0968 V','1.968 V','2.0968 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171128,'chemistry','The equilibrium constant of the reaction :\n\nCu(s) +   Ag⁺² → Cu⁺²(aq) + Ag (s)\n\nE° = 0.46 V at 298 K is','K꜀ = 4 x 10¹⁵','For a cell reaction in equilibrium at 298 K,\n\nE°cell = 0.059/n x log K꜀\n\n where K꜀ = equilibrium constant, n = number of electrons involved in the electrochemical cell reaction.\n\nGiven, E°cell = 0.46 V, n = 2\n\n∴  0.46 = 0.059/2 x logK꜀\n\nlogK꜀ = 2 x 0.46/0.059 = 15.57 \n\nK꜀ = 4 x 10¹⁵\n\n(NEET 2007)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','K꜀ = 7 x 10¹⁵','K꜀ = 4 x 10¹⁵','5 x 10¹⁰','4 x 10¹⁰','NEW','12-073','12-039',NULL),
(171129,'chemistry','The standard e.m.f. of a galvanic cell involving cell reaction with n = 2 is found to be 0.295 V at 25°C.  \n\nThe equilibrium constant of the reaction would be','1 x 10¹⁰','According to the Nernst equation, \n \nE𝒸ₑₗₗ =  E°cell -0.059/n x log Q꜀\n\nAt equilibrium E𝒸ₑₗₗ = 0, Q꜀ = K꜀\n\nE°cell = 0.059/2 x log K꜀\n\n0.295 = 0.059/2 x log K꜀\n\nK꜀ = antilog 10 = 1 x 10¹⁰\n\n(NEET 2004)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2 x 10¹⁰','1 x 10¹⁰','4 x 10¹⁰','1.5 x 10¹⁰','NEW','12-073','12-039',NULL),
(171130,'chemistry','On the basis of the information available from the reaction,\n\n4/3 Al + O₂ → 2/3 Al₂O₃, \n\nΔG = –827 kJ/mol of O₂, the minimum e.m.f. required to carry out an electrolysis of  Al₂O₃ is (F = 96500 C/mol)','2.14 V','ΔG° = – nFE°\n\nF = 96500, ΔG° = - 827 × 10³J/mol\n\nE° = ΔG°/nF = -827000/386000 = 2.14 V\n\n(NEET 2003)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.14 V','4.28 V','6.42 V','8.56 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171131,'chemistry','For the disproportionation of copper \n\n2 Cu⁺ → Cu⁺² + Cu, E° is (Given : E° for Cu⁺²/Cu is 0.34 V and E° for Cu⁺²/Cu+ is 0.15 V)','0.38 V','(NEET 2000) \n\ndiagram(ne1702)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0.49 V','–0.19 V','0.38 V','–0.38 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171132,'chemistry','E° for the cell, \n\nZn | Zn⁺²(aq) ||Cu⁺²(aq) | Cu is 1.10 V  at 25°C, the equilibrium constant for the reaction  \n\nZn + Cu⁺²(aq) → Cu + Zn⁺²(aq)  is of the order','10³⁷','According to Nernst equation,  \n\nE𝒸ₑₗₗ =  E°cell -0.059/n x log Q꜀\n\nAt equilibrium E𝒸ₑₗₗ = 0, Q꜀ = K꜀\n\nE°cell = 0.059/2 x log K꜀\n\n1.1 = 0.059/2 x log K꜀\n\nK꜀ = 1.9 x 10¹⁰\n\n(NEET 1997)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','10²','10¹⁷','10¹⁸','10³⁷','NEW','12-073','12-039',NULL),
(171133,'chemistry','Following limiting molar conductivities are given as :\n\nλₘ⁰ (H₂SO₄ ) = x S cm²/mol\n\nλₘ⁰ (K₂SO₄ ) = y S cm²/mol\n\nλₘ⁰ CH₃COOK) = z S cm²/mol\n\nλₘ⁰ (in S cm²/ mol) for CH3COOH will be','(x-y)/2 + z','(NEET Odisha 2019) diagram(ne1703)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','x - y + 2z','x+y - z','x-y + z','(x-y)/2 + z','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(171134,'chemistry','The molar conductivity of a 0.5 mol/dm³ solution of AgNO₃ with electrolytic conductivity of 5.76 × 10⁻³ S cm⁻¹ at 298 K is','11.52 S cm²/mol','λₘ = k x 1000/ Molarity(M)\n\n= 5.76 x 10⁻³ S cm⁻¹ x 1000/0.5 mol cm = 11.52 S cm²/mol\n\n(NEET-II 2016)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2.88 S cm²/mol','11.52 S cm²/mol','0.086 S cm²/mol','28.8 S cm²/mol','NEW','12-079','12-045',NULL),
(171135,'chemistry','At 25 °C molar conductance of 0.1 molar aqueous solution of ammonium hydroxide is  9.54 /ohm cm² /mol and at infinite dilution, its molar conductance is 238 /ohmcm² /mol. \n\nThe degree of ionization of ammonium hydroxide at the  same concentration and temperature is','4.01%','Degree of dissociation (α)\n\nα = Molar conductivity at conc./ Molar conductivity as infinite dilution\n\nα = 9.54/238 = 0.04008 = 4.01%\n\n(NEET 2013)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','4.01%','40.80%','2.08%','20.80%','NEW','12-084','12-050',NULL),
(171136,'chemistry','Molar conductivities (λₘ⁰) at infinite dilution of NaCl, HCl and CH₃COONa are 126.4, 425.9 and 91.0 S cm²/mol respectively. \n\n(λₘ⁰) for CH₃COOH will be','390.5 S cm²/mol','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 84; example No. 3.8)\n\n(NEET 2021, 2012, 1997)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','425.5 S cm²/mol','180.5 S cm²/mol','290.8 S cm²/mol','390.5 S cm²/mol','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(171137,'chemistry','An increase in equivalent conductance of a strong  electrolyte with dilution is mainly due to','increase in ionic mobility of ions','Strong electrolytes are completely ionized at all concentrations. \n\nOn increasing dilution, the no. of ions remains the same but the ionic mobility increases, and the equivalent conductance increases.\n\n(NEET 2010)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','increase in ionic mobility of ions','100% ionisation of electrolyte at normal dilution','increase in both i.e., number of ions and ionic mobility of ions','increase in number of ions','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(171138,'chemistry','The equivalent conductance of M/32 solution of a weak monobasic acid is 8.0 mho cm² and at infinite dilution is 400 mho cm². \n\nThe dissociation constant of this acid is','1.25 x 10⁻⁵','Given, λ = 8 mho cm² λₘ⁰ = 400 mho cm²\n\ndegree of dissociation α = λ/λₘ⁰\n\nα = 8/400 = 2 x 10⁻²\n\nDissociation constant, K = Ca²\n\nGiven, C = M/32\n\nK = 1/32 x 2 x 10⁻² 2 x 10⁻² = 1.25 x 10⁻⁵\n\n(NEET 2009)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.25 x 10⁻⁶','1.25 x 10⁻⁵','1.25 x 10⁻⁴','6.25 x 10⁻⁴','NEW','12-084','12-050',NULL),
(171139,'chemistry','Kohlrausch’s law states that at','infinite dilution, each ion makes definite contribution to conductance of an electrolyte whatever be the nature of the other ion of the electrolyte','At infinite dilution, when dissociation is complete each ion makes a definite contribution towards molar conductance of the electrolyte irrespective of the nature of the other ion with which it is associated and that the molar conductance of any electrolyte at infinite dilution is given by the sum of the contributions of two ions. \n\nThis is called Kohlrausch’s law.\n\n(NEET 2008)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','infinite dilution, each ion makes definite contribution to conductance of an electrolyte whatever be the nature of the other ion of the electrolyte','infinite dilution, each ion makes definite contribution to equivalent conductance of an electrolyte, whatever be the nature of the other  ion of the electrolyte','finite dilution, each ion makes definite contribution to equivalent conductance of an electrolyte, whatever be the nature of the other ion of the electrolyte','finite dilution each ion makes definite contribution to equivalent conductance of an electrolyte depending on the nature of the other ion of the electrolyte','NEW','12-083','12-049','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/171139q.png'),
(171140,'chemistry','The specific conductance of a 0.1 N KCl solution at 23°C is 0.012/ohm cm \n\nThe resistance of cell containing the solution at the same temperature was found to be 55 ohm. \n\nThe cell constant will be','0.66/cm','k = 0.012/ohm cm\n\nR = 55 ohm, Conductance = 1/R = 1/55 mho\n\ncell constant (l/a) = Specific conductance / conductance \n\n= 0.012 x 55 = 0.66/cm\n\n(NEET 1999)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.918/cm','0.66/cm','1.142/cm','1.12/cm','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(171141,'chemistry','On heating one end of a piece of a metal, the other end becomes hot because of','energised electrons moving to the other end','Conductivity of heat in metals is due to the presence of free electrons, which move due to increase in temperature.\n\n(NEET 1995)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','energised electrons moving to the other end','minor perturbation in the energy of atoms','resistance of the metal','mobility of atoms in the metal','NEW','12-076','12-042',NULL),
(171142,'chemistry','On electrolysis of dil. sulphuric acid using platinum (Pt) electrode, the product obtained at anode will be','Oxygen gas','During electrolysis of dilute sulphuric acid O₂ will be liberated at anode.\n\n(NEET 2020)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrogen gas','Oxygen gas','H₂S gas','SO₂ gas','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171143,'chemistry','The number of Faradays (F) required to produce 20 g of calcium from molten CaCl₂ (Atomic mass of Ca = 40 g / mol) is','1','Ca⁺² + 2e⁻ → Ca \n\nFor 40 g of Ca, 2 moles of electrons(2F) will be needed, therefore for 20 g 1 mole of electrons (1F) will be needed.\n\nThus, one Faraday is required to produce 20 g of calcium from molten CaCl₂.\n\n(NEET 2020)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171144,'chemistry','During the electrolysis of molten sodium chloride, the time required to produce 0.10 mol of chlorine gas using a current of 3 amperes is','110 minutes','(NEET-II 2016) \ndiagram(ne1705)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','55 minutes','110 minutes','220 minutes','330 minutes','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171145,'chemistry','When 0.1 mol MnO₄⁻² is oxidised, the quantity of electricity required to completely oxidise MnO₄⁻² to MnO₄⁻','9650 C','To oxidise 1 mole of MnO₄⁻², 96500 C charge is required, for 0.1 mole 9650 C is required\n\n(NEET 2014)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','96500 C','2 x 96500 C','9650 C','96.50 C','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171146,'chemistry','The weight of silver (at. wt. = 108) displaced by a quantity of electricity which displaces 5600 mL of O₂ at STP will be','108 g','Let weight of silver = Wₐ and weight of oxygen = Wo  \n\nAccording to Faraday’s second law,\n\nWₐ/Eₐ = Wo/Eo\n\nor Wₐ/108 = 8/8 \n\nWₐ = 108 g\n\n(NEET 2014)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','5.4 g','54 g','10.8 g','108 g','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171147,'chemistry','How many grams of cobalt metal will be deposited when a solution of cobalt(II) chloride is electrolyzed with a current of 10 amperes for 109 minutes? \n\n(1 Faraday = 96,500 C; Atomic mass of Co = 59 u)','20','w = ITE/96500 \n\n= 10 x 109 x 60 x 59/(96500 x 2) = 20 g \n\n(NEET Karnataka 2013)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','4','40','20','0.66','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171148,'chemistry','Al₂O₃ is reduced by electrolysis at low potentials and high currents. \n\nIf 4.0 × 10⁴ amperes of current is passed through molten Al₂O₃ for 6 hours, what mass of aluminium is produced? \n\n(Assume 100% current efficiency, at. mass of Al = 27 g /mol)','8.1 × 10⁴ g','Applying E = Z × 96500\n\n27/3 = Z x 96500\n\nZ = 9/96500\n\nNow applying the formula, w = Z × I × t\n\nw = 9/96500 x 4 x 10⁴ x 60 x 6 x 60 = 8.1 × 10⁴ g\n\n(NEET 2009)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','8.1 × 10⁴ g','8.1 x 10³ g','4 x 10³ g','4 x 10⁴ g','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171149,'chemistry','4.5 g of aluminium (at. mass 27 amu) is deposited at cathode from Al⁺³ solution by a certain quantity of electric charge. \n\nThe volume of hydrogen produced at STP from H⁺ ions in solution by the same quantity of electric charge will be','5.6 L','We know that, \n\n1 Faraday charge liberates 1 eq. of substance.This is the Faraday law\n\neq. wt. of Al = 9\n\nNo. of eq. of Al = wt. of Al/eq. wt = 4.5/9 = 0.5\n\nNo. of Faradays required = 0.5\n\nNo. of eq. of H₂ produced = 0.5 eq.\n\nVolume occupied by 1 eq. of H₂ = 11.2 L\n\nVolume occupied by 0.5 eq. of H₂ = 11.2 × 0.5= 5.6 L at STP\n\n(NEET 2005)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','44.8 L','22.4 L','11.2 L','5.6 L','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171150,'chemistry','In electrolysis of NaCl when Pt electrode is taken then H₂ is liberated at cathode while with Hg cathode it forms sodium amalgam. The reason for this is','more voltage is required to reduce H⁺ at Hg than at Pt','(NEET 2002) \ndiagram(ne1706)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Hg is more inert than Pt','more voltage is required to reduce H⁺ at Hg than at Pt','Na is dissolved in Hg while it does not dissolve in Pt','conc. of H⁺ ions is larger when Pt electrode is taken','NEW','12-090','12-056',NULL),
(171151,'chemistry','A 5 ampere current is passed through a solution of zinc sulphate for 40 minutes. The amount of zinc deposited at the cathode is','4.065 g','Current (I) = 5 ampere and time (t) = 40 minutes = 2400 seconds. \n\nAmount of electricity passed (Q) = I × t\n\n= 5 × 2400 = 12000 C\n\nNow, Zn⁺² + 2e⁻ →  Zn  (1 mole = 65.39 g)\n\nSince, two charges (i.e., 2 × 96500 C) deposits 65.39 g of \n\nzinc, therefore 12000 C will deposit\n\n= 65.39 x 12000/193000 = 4.065 g of zinc\n\n(NEET 1996)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.4065 g','65.04 g','40.65 g','4.065 g','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171152,'chemistry','Sodium is made by the electrolysis of a molten mixture of about 40% NaCl and 60% CaCl₂ because','this mixture has a lower melting point than NaCl.','Sodium is obtained by electrolytic reduction of its chloride. \n\nThe melting point of chloride of sodium is high so in order to lower its melting point, calcium chloride is added to it.\n(NEET 1995)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Previous years questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ca⁺² can reduce NaCl to Na','Ca⁺² can displace Na from NaCl','CaCl₂ helps in conduction of electricity','this mixture has a lower melting point than NaCl.','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171201,'chemistry','If an external opposite potential is applied in the galvanic cell and increased slowly, what will happen?\n\nChoose the correct option.\n\na) The reaction continues to take place till the opposing voltage reaches the value 1.1 V\n\nb) Any further increase in the external potential will not start again the reaction\n\nc) The reaction continues to take place till the opposing voltage reaches the value 0 V','Only a','If an external opposite potential is applied in the galvanic cell and increased slowly, we find that the reaction continues to take place till the opposing voltage reaches the value 1.1 V when the reaction stops altogether and no current flows through the cell. \n\nAny further increase in the external potential again starts the reaction but in the opposite direction. \nIt now functions as an electrolytic cell.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a','Only b and c','Only c','Only a and b','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(171202,'chemistry','Which one is the correct option from the given diagram?(n1703)','Eₑₓₜ < 1.1 V','When Eₑₓₜ< 1.1 V\n\n(i) Electrons flow from Zn rod to Cu rod hence current flows from Cu to Zn.\n\n(ii) Zn dissolves at the anode and copper deposits at the cathode.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Diagram 3.2a)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Eₑₓₜ < 1.1 V','Eₑₓₜ > 1.1 V','Eₑₓₜ = 1.1 V','Eₑₓₜ = 0','NEW','12-066','12-032','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171202q.png'),
(171203,'chemistry','Which one is the correct option from the given diagram?(n1704)','Eₑₓₜ = 1.1 V','When Eₑₓₜ = 1.1 V\n\n(i) No flow of electrons or current.\n\n(ii) No chemical reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Diagram 3.2b)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Eₑₓₜ < 1.1 V','Eₑₓₜ > 1.1 V','Eₑₓₜ = 1.1 V','Eₑₓₜ = 0','NEW','12-066','12-032','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171203q.png'),
(171204,'chemistry','Which one is the correct option from the given diagram?(n1705)','Eₑₓₜ > 1.1 V','When Eₑₓₜ > 1.1 V\n\n(i) Electrons flow from Cu to Zn and current flows from Zn to Cu.\n\n(ii) Zinc is deposited at the zinc electrode and copper dissolves at copper electrode.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Diagram 3.2c)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Eₑₓₜ < 1.1 V','Eₑₓₜ > 1.1 V','Eₑₓₜ = 1.1 V','Eₑₓₜ = 0','NEW','12-066','12-032','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171204q.png'),
(171205,'chemistry','We can construct an innumerable number of _________ cells on the pattern of the Daniell cell by taking combinations of different half-cells.','galvanic','When Eₑₓₜ > 1.1 V\n\n(i) Electrons flow from Cu to Zn and current flows from Zn to Cu.\n\n(ii) Zinc is deposited at the zinc electrode and copper dissolves at copper electrode.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 66; Diagram 3.2c)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','fillup','gal,ele,van,ctr,ic,al',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(171206,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true for the electrochemical reactions? \n\na) The use of electrical energy is to bring about spontaneous chemical transformations.\n\nb) Batteries convert chemical energy into electrical energy.\n\nc) The reactions carried out electrochemically can be highly polluting.\n\nd) The transmission of sensory signals through cells to the brain and vice versa and communication between the cells are known to have an electrochemical origin.\n\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Only b and d','a) The use of electrical energy to bring about non-spontaneous chemical transformations. \n\nThe option mentions spontaneous so it is not the correct answer.\n\nb) Batteries and fuel cells convert chemical energy into electrical energy and are used on a large scale in various instruments and devices. \n\nSo it is the correct option.\n\nc) The reactions carried out electrochemically can be energy efficient and less polluting. \n\nSo highly polluting is not the correct option. \n\nd) The transmission of sensory signals through cells to the brain and vice versa and communication between the cells is known to have an electrochemical origin. \n\nIt is written in the reference paragraph so is the correct option.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 65; Paragraph No.1)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a and b','Only c and d','Only a and c','Only b and d','NEW','12-065','12-031',NULL),
(171207,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n\nAssertion (A): Standard hydrogen electrode has zero potential at all temperatures.\n\nReason (R): The concentration of both the reduced and oxidized forms of hydrogen is maintained at unity.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','We cannot measure the potential value of individual half-cells. \n\nWe can measure only the difference between the two half-cell potentials that give the emf of the cell. \n\nThe reaction of the standard hydrogen electrode is a half-cell reaction and measured as a standard cell with a value of zero at all temperatures. \n\nBoth (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation (A)\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 68; Paragraph No.2)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(171208,'chemistry','Match the following.','Galvanic cell-has an electrical potential equal to 1.1 V, Electrolytic cell-a device for using electrical energy to carry non-spontaneous chemical reactions, Standard hydrogen electrode has an electrical potential equal to 0 V, Conductivity cell-is used when a solution cannot be connected to the bridge like a metallic wire or other solid conductors.','a) The galvanic cell converts chemical energy into electrical energy. \n\nThis energy has a potential value of 1.1 V when the concentration of Zn⁺² and Cu⁺² ions is unity (1 mol dm⁻³).\n\nb) When the reaction goes in the opposite direction in a galvanic cell, it acts like an electrolytic cell, which is a device for using electrical energy to carry non-spontaneous chemical reactions.\n\nc) The standard hydrogen electrode is a half-cell, which does not have a potential value. \n\nIt has a standard potential of zero as a standard electrode value. \n\nd) For measuring the resistance of an ionic solution we face two problems. \n\nFirstly, passing direct current (DC) changes the composition of the solution. \n\nSecondly, a solution cannot be connected to the bridge like a metallic wire or other solid conductors. \nThe second solution is solved by using a conductivity cell.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 66; Paragraph No.1, Page No. 66; Paragraph No. 2, Page No. 68; Paragraph No. 2, Page No. 77; Paragraph No. 4.)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','match','Galvanic cell,has an electrical potential equal to 1.1 V','Electrolytic cell,a device for using electrical energy to carry non-spontaneous chemical reactions','Standard hydrogen electrode,has an electrical potential equal to 0 V','Conductivity cell,is used when a solution cannot be connected to the bridge like a metallic wire or other solid conductors','NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(171209,'chemistry','The graph shows molar conductivity vs concentration in the aqueous solution of two components.\nWhat can you conclude from this graph? (171209Q)','Line A is weak electrolyte and line B is strong electrolyte','Molar conductivity increases with a decrease in concentration. \n\nFor strong electrolytes, λₘ increases slowly with dilution. \n\nWeak electrolytes like acetic acid have a lower degree of dissociation at higher concentrations and hence for such electrolytes, the change in λₘ with dilution is due to an increase in the degree of dissociation. \n\nIn such cases λₘ increases steeply as the figure shows on dilution, especially near lower concentrations.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 81; Paragraph No.3, Page No. 83; Paragraph 5.)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Line A is weak electrolyte and line B is strong electrolyte','Line B is weak electrolyte and line B is weak electrolyte','Line A and B both are strong electrolytes','Line A and B both are weak electrolytes','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(171210,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true for the batteries? \n\na) Primary batteries are rechargeable.\n\nb) The reaction occurs only once at secondary batteries.\n\nc) The dry cell is a primary battery.\n\nd) On charging the Lead storage battery, the reaction is reversed.','Only (c) and (d)','a) In the primary batteries, the reaction occurs only once, and after use over a period of time battery becomes dead and cannot be reused again. \n\nSo it can’t be recharged which is not the correct answer.\n\nb) A secondary cell after use can be recharged by passing a current through it in the opposite direction so that it can be used again. \n\nIt is a rechargeable battery and can be used many times. So it is not the correct answer. \n\nc) The most familiar example of a primary battery is a dry cell. So this option is the correct answer. \n\nd) On charging the Lead storage battery the reaction is reversed and PbSO₄(s) on the anode and cathode are converted into Pb and PbO2, respectively. So this option is correct. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 88; Paragraph No.4, Page No. 89; Paragraph No.2,)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (a) and (b)','Only (c) and (d)','Only (b) and (c)','All the options except (d)','NEW','12-088','12-054',NULL),
(171211,'chemistry','In a galvanic cell, metal atoms of the electrode have a tendency to go into the solution as ions and leave behind the electrons at the electrode trying to make it ________charged.','negatively','At each electrode-electrolyte interface there is a tendency of metal ions from the solution to deposit on the metal electrode trying to make it positively charged. \n\nAt the same time, metal atoms of the electrode have a tendency to go into the solution as ions and leave behind the electrons at the electrode trying to make it negatively charged.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page No. 67; Paragraph No.4)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','fillup','ne,posi,ga,tive,ly',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-067','12-033','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/171211q.png'),
(171301,'chemistry','Consider the statements S1 and S2 :\nS1 : Conductivity always increases with decrease\nin the concentration of electrolyte.\nS2 : Molar conductivity always increases with\n decrease in the concentration of electrolyte.\nThe correct option among the following is','S1 is wrong and S2 is correct','Conductivity of an electrolyte is the conductance of\n1 cm³ of the given electrolyte. So, it increases with\nthe increase of concentration of electrolyte. Molar\nconductivity (Om) is the conductance of a solution\ncontaining 1 mole of the electrolyte. It increases\nwith the decrease of concentration (i) due to\nincrease in interionic attraction for strong\nelectrolytes and (ii) due to decrease in degree of\nionisation for weak electrolytes. Therefore, (S1) is\nwrong and (S2) is correct.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','S1 is wrong and S2 is correct','S1 is correct and S2 is wrong','Both S1 and S2 are wrong','Both S1 and S2 are correct','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(171302,'chemistry','The decreasing order of electrical conductivity of the\nfollowing aqueous solutions is: [JEE (Main)-2019]\n 0.1 M Formic acid (A),\n0.1 M Acetic acid (B),\n0.1 M Benzoic acid (C).','A > C > B','So order of conductivity is A > C > B. Refer diagram(ne1710)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','A > B > C','A > C > B','C > B > A','C > A > B','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(171304,'chemistry','Which one of the following graphs between molar\nconductivity (λm) versus √C is correct?\n[JEE (Main)-2019] refer diagram(n1714)','3','Refer diagram(ne1711)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-081','12-047','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171304q.png'),
(171305,'chemistry','A solution of Ni(NO₃)₂ is electrolysed between\nplatinum electrodes using 0.1 Faraday electricity.\nHow many mole of Ni will be deposited at the\ncathode? [JEE (Main)-2019]','0.05','0.1 F of electricity is passed through Ni(NO₃)₂ solution. \n→ Amount of Ni deposited = 0.1 eq\nMoles = 0.1/2 = 0.05','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0.2','0.15','0.1','0.05','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171306,'chemistry','The standard Gibbs energy for the given cell\nreaction in kJ /mol at 298 K is\nZn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) →  Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s),\nE° = 2 V at 298 K\n(Faraday’s constant, F = 96000 C /mol)\n[JEE (Main)-2019]','-384','∆G° = – nFE°cell\n= –2 × (96000) × 2 V\n= –384 kJ/mole','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','192','384','-384','-192','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171307,'chemistry','Calculate the standard cell potential (in V) of the\ncell in which following reaction takes place :\nFe²⁺(aq) + Ag⁺(aq) → Fe³⁺(aq) + Ag(s)\nGiven that\nE°Ag⁺/Ag  = xV \nE°Fe²⁺/Fe = yV\nE° Fe³⁺/Fe = zV','x + 2y – 3z','Fe²⁺(aq) + Ag⁺(aq) → Fe³⁺(aq) + Ag(s)\nE°Cell = E°Ag⁺/Ag  - E° Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺\n\nTo calculate refer diagram(ne1712)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','x – y','x + y – z','x + 2y – 3z','x – z','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171308,'chemistry','The strongest oxidizing agent is.... refer diagram(n1715)','S₂O₈²⁻','More positive is the reduction potential stronger is the oxidising agent.\nReduction potential is maximum for S₂O₈²⁻.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Br₂','Au³⁺','S₂O₈²⁻','O₂','NEW','12-070','12-036','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171308q.png'),
(171309,'chemistry','Λ°m for NaCl, HCl and NaA are 126.4, 425.9 and\n100.5 S cm²/mol, respectively. If the conductivity\nof 0.001 M HA is 5 × 10⁻⁵ S /cm, degree of\ndissociation of HA is [JEE (Main)-2019]','0.125','Λ°m (NaCl) = 126.4 S cm² /mol\nΛ°m (HCl) = 425.9 S cm² /mol\nΛ°m (NaA) = 100.5 S cm² /mol\nΛ°m (HA) = 425.9 – 126.4 + 100.5 = 400 S cm² /mol\nK(HA) = 5 × 10⁻⁵ S /cm\n\nΛᶜm = k × 1000/Molarity = 5 × 10⁻⁵ × 1000/0.001 = 50\n\nα = Λᶜm /Λ°m = 50/400 = 0.125','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','0.25','0.125','0.5','0.75','NEW','12-084','12-050',NULL),
(171310,'chemistry','The standard electrode potential E°\nand its\ntemperature coefficient\ndE°/dT\nfor a cell are 2 V and\n -5 × 10⁻⁴ V/K at 300 K respectively. The cell\nreaction is\nZn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺ (aq) + Cu(s)\nThe standard reaction enthalpy (∆r\nH°\n) at 300 K in\nkJ /mol is,\n[Use R = 8 J/K mol and F = 96,000 C /mol]','-412.8','∆r\nH° = -nFE° + nFTdE°/dT\n\nCell reaction:\n\nZn(s) + Cu²⁺ (aq) → Zn²⁺ (aq) +  Cu(s)\n∆r\nH°= –2 × 96000 (2 + 300 × 5 × 10⁻⁴)\n= – 2 × 96000 (2 + 0.15)\n= – 412.8 × 103 J/mol\n∆r\nH°= – 412.8 kJ/mol','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','206.4','-384','-412.8','192','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171311,'chemistry','Given the equilibrium constant :\nK꜀ of the reaction :\nCu(s) + 2Ag⁺ (aq) → Cu²⁺ (aq) + 2Ag(s) is\n10 × 10¹⁵, calculate the E⁰cell of this reaction at\n298 K','0.4736 V','E°cell = (0.059/n)logKc\n          = (0.059/2) log 10¹⁶\n          = 0.472 V','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0.4736 mV','0.4736 V','0.04736 V','0.04736 mV','NEW','12-073','12-039',NULL),
(171312,'chemistry','For the cell Zn(s)|Zn²⁺(aq)||Mx⁺(aq)| M(s), different\nhalf cells and their standard electrode potentials\nare given below:\nIf E°Zn²⁺ /Zn = 0.76 V, which cathode will give a\nmaximum value of E°cell per electron transferred? refer diagram(1716)','Au³⁺/Au','Ecell = E°RP(Cathode) - E°RP(Anode)\nAll electrodes act as cathode w.r.t. Zn so the ion\nwhich has highest reduction potential will give\nmaximum value of E°cell so Au³⁺/Au produce highest\nE°cell.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe²⁺/Fe','Ag⁺/Ag','Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺','Au³⁺/Au','NEW','12-070','12-036','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171312q.png'),
(171313,'chemistry','In the cell\nPt(s)|H₂(g,1bar)|HCl(aq)|AgCl(s)|Ag(s)|Pt(s) the cell\npotential is 0.92 V when a 10⁻⁶ molal HCl solution\nis used. The standard electrode potential of (AgCl/\nAg,Cl⁻) electrode is\n{ Given, 2.303RT/F = 0.06V at 298 K\n} \n[JEE (Main)-2019]','0.20 V','Refer diagram(ne1713)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0.20 V','0.40 V','0.76 V','0.94 V','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(171315,'chemistry','The anodic half-cell of lead-acid battery is recharged\nusing electricity of 0.05 Faraday. The amount of\nPbSO₄ electrolyzed in g during the process is\n(Molar mass of PbSO₄ = 303 g /mol)\n[JEE (Main)-2019]','7.6','PbSO₄ → Pb⁴⁺ + 2e⁻\n\nw =  ZIt = 303 × 0.05 F / 2F = 7.6g','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','7.6','15.2','11.4','22.8','NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171316,'chemistry','How long (approximate) should water be\nelectrolysed by passing through 100 amperes\ncurrent so that the oxygen released can\ncompletely burn 27.66 g of diborane? (Atomic\nweight of B = 10.8 u) [JEE (Main)-2018]','3.2 hours','B₂H₆ + 3O₂ → B₂O₃ + 3H₂O\n27.66 of B₂H₆ = 1 mole of B₂H₆ which requires three\nmoles of oxygen (O2) for complete burning\n6H₂O → 6H₂+ 3O₂ (On electrolysis)\nNumber of faradays = 12 = Amount of charge\n12 × 96500 = i × t\n12 × 96500 = 100 × t\nt = 12 × 96500 /100 × 3600 hours\nt = 3.2 hours','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','6.4 hours','0.8 hours','3.2 hours','1.6 hours','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171317,'chemistry','Among the following, the strongest reducing agent\nis [JEE (Main)-2017] refer diagram(n1711)','Cr','Positive E° is for Cr, hence it is strongest reducing\nagent.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Cr³⁺','Cl⁻','Cr','Mn²⁺','NEW','12-070','12-036','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171317q.png'),
(171318,'chemistry','Galvanization is applying a coating of\n[JEE (Main)-2016]','Zn','Galvanisation is coating a layer of Zn.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Cr','Cu','Zn','Pb','NEW','12-091','12-057',NULL),
(171319,'chemistry','Two faraday of electricity is passed through a\nsolution of CuSO₄. The mass of copper deposited\nat the cathode is (at. mass of Cu = 63.5 amu)\n[JEE (Main)-2015]','63.5 g','Cu⁺² + 2e → Cu\nSo, 2 F charge deposite 1 mol of Cu. Mass\ndeposited = 63.5 g.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','0 g','63.5 g','2 g','127 g','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171320,'chemistry','Given below are the half-cell reactions\nMn²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Mn; E° = -1.18 V\n(Mn³⁺ + e⁻ → Mn²⁺); E° = + 1.51 V\nThe E° for 3 Mn²⁺ → Mn + 2Mn³⁺ will be\n[JEE (Main)-2014]','–2.69 V; the reaction will not occur','(1) Mn²⁺ + 2e →  Mn; E° = –1.18V ;\n   ∆G₁° = -2F( -1.18) =  2.36F\n(2) Mn³⁺ + e → Mn²⁺ ; E° = +1.51 V;\n∆G°₂ = -f(1.51) = -1.51\nf\n(1) - 2(2) \n3Mn²⁺  → Mn + 2Mn³⁺ ;\n∆G°₃ = ∆G₁° - 2∆G°₂ \n= [2.36 – 2(–1.51)] F\n= (2.36 + 3.02) F\n= 5.38 F\nBut ∆G°₃ = -2FE°\n→ 5.38F = –2FE°\n→ E° = –2.69 V\nAs E° value is negative reaction is non spontaneous.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','–2.69 V; the reaction will not occur','–2.69 V; the reaction will occur','–0.33 V; the reaction will not occur','–0.33 V; the reaction will occur','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171321,'chemistry','The metal that cannot be obtained by electrolysis\nof an aqueous solution of its salts is\n[JEE (Main)-2014]','Ca','On electrolysis only in case of Ca²⁺ salt aqueous\nsolution H₂ gas discharge at Cathode.\nCase of Cr\nAt cathode : Cr³⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cr\nSo, Cr is deposited.\nCase of Ag\nAt cathode : Ag+ + e⁻ → Ag\nSo, Ag is deposited.\nCase of Cu\nAt cathode : Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu\nCase of Ca²⁺\nAt cathode : H₂O + e⁻ → 1/2 H₂ + OH⁻','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Ag','Ca','Cu','Cr','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171322,'chemistry','The equivalent conductance of NaCl at\nconcentration C and at infinite dilution are λ꜀ and\nλ∞, respectively. The correct relationship between\nλ꜀ and λ∞ is given as (where the constant B is\npositive) [JEE (Main)-2014]','λ꜀ = λ∞ - (B)√C','According to Debye-Huckel-Onsager equation,\nλ꜀ = λ∞ - A√C \nHere A = B \n→λ꜀ = λ∞ - B√C','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','λ꜀ = λ∞ + (B)C','λ꜀ = λ∞ - (B)C','λ꜀ = λ∞ - (B)√C','λ꜀ = λ∞ + (B)√C','NEW','12-083','12-049',NULL),
(171323,'chemistry','Resistance of 0.2 M solution of an electrolyte is\n50Ω . The specific conductance of the solution is\n1.4 S /m. The resistance of 0.5 M solution of the\nsame electrolyte is 280 Ω. The molar conductivity\nof 0.5 M solution of the electrolyte in S m² /mol\nis [JEE (Main)-2014]','5 × 10⁻⁴','For 0.2 M solution\nR = 50Ω\nσ = 1.4 S /m = 1.4 × 10⁻² S /cm\nσ = 1/ρ = 1/1.4 × 10⁻² Ω cm\n\nR = ρ(l/a) \nl/a = R/ρ = 50 × 1.4 × 10⁻² \n\nFor 0.5 M solution\nR = 280 Ω\nσ = ?\n\nl/a = 50 × 1.4 × 10⁻²  \nR = ρ(l/a) \n1/ρ = l/Ra\nσ = (1/280)50 × 1.4 × 10⁻²  = 2.5 × 10⁻³ S/cm\n \nNow, λm = σ × 1000/M = 2.5 × 10⁻³ × 1000/0.5  = 5 S Cm²/mol = 5 × 10⁻⁴ S m²/mol.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','5 × 10⁻⁴','5 × 10⁻³','5 × 10³','5 × 10²','NEW','12-079','12-045',NULL),
(171324,'chemistry','Based on the data given below , strongest oxidising\nagent will be [JEE (Main)-2013]  Refer diagram(n1711)','MnO₄⁻','Of the given values of standard reduction potential,\nthe value of E°MnO₄⁻ /Mn²⁺ is highest. Therefore,\nMnO₄⁻\nis the strongest reducing agent.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl⁻','Cr³⁺','Mn²⁺','MnO₄⁻','NEW','12-070','12-036','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/171324q.png'),
(171325,'chemistry','The standard reduction potentials for Zn²⁺/Zn,\nNi²⁺/Ni, and Fe²⁺/Fe are – 0.76, – 0.23 and – 0.44 V\nrespectively. The reaction X + Y²⁺ → X²⁺ + Y will\nbe spontaneous when [AIEEE-2012]','X = Zn, Y = Ni','X = Zn, Y = Ni','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','X = Ni, Y = Zn','X = Fe, Y = Zn','X = Zn, Y = Ni','X = Ni, Y = Fe','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171326,'chemistry','Resistance of 0.2 M solution of an electrolyte is\n50 Ω. The specific conductance of the solution is\n1.3 S /m. If resistance of the 0.4 M solution of the\nsame electrolyte is 260Ω.  Its molar conductivity is\n[AIEEE-2011]','6.25 × 10⁻⁴ S m² /mol','Case-I\n\nG = 1/R = 1/50S (S = Sieman)\nK = G × cell constant\n\nCell constant = K/G = (1.3 S/m / 1S) × 50 = 65 /m\n\nCase-II\n\nλm = k / molarity = G × Cell constant/ Molarity = S × 65 /m /260 × 0.4 × mol/litre = (1/260)(S × 65/m / 0.4 mol × 1000³) = 6.25 × 10⁻⁴ S m²/mol.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','62.5 S m² /mol','6250 S m² /mol','6.25 × 10⁻⁴ S m² /mol','625 × 10⁻⁴ S m² /mol','NEW','12-079','12-045',NULL),
(171327,'chemistry','The Gibbs energy for the decomposition Al₂O₃ at\n500°C is as follows\n2/3Al₂O₃ → 4/3 Al + O₂ ,∆ᵣG = 966 KJ/mol\nThe potential difference needed for electrolytic\nreduction of Al₂O₃ at 500°C is at least\n[AIEEE-2010]','2.5 V','2/3Al₂O₃ → 4/3 Al + O₂ \n∆G = 966 kJ/mol\n→ 4e⁻ are involved\n→ ∆G = – nFE\n966 × 10³ = –4 × 96500 × E\nE = 966 V / 4 × 965 = -2.5 V\n→ 2.5 V potential difference is required','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','5.0 V','4.5 V','3.0 V','2.5 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171328,'chemistry','The value of standard electrode potential for the\nchange, 3 Fe(aq)\n + e⁻  → Fe²⁺ (aq) will be\n[AIEEE-2010] refer diagram(n1713)','0.770 V','Refer diagram(ne1714)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0.385 V','0.770 V','– 0.270 V','– 0.072 V','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171329,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\nA: Contraction of external intercostal muscles increase the volume of thoracic cavity in dorso-ventral axis.\n \nR: Intercostal muscle contraction pulls the ribs and sternum upwards and forwards.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','The contraction of external inter-costal muscles lifts up the ribs and the sternum causing an increase in the volume of the thoracic chamber in the dorso-ventral axis. (NCERT Class 11 pg 271 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-271','11-186',NULL),
(171401,'chemistry','For the given cell, Mg|Mg²⁺||Cu²⁺ || Cu\n(a) Mg is cathode\n(b) Cu is cathode\n(c) The cell reaction Mg + Cu²⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Cu\n(d) Cu is the oxidizing agent','b,c','Left side of cell reaction represents oxidation half-cell i.e., oxidation of Mg and right side of cell represents reduction half-cell reactions i.e., reduction of copper.\n(ii) Cu is reduced and reduction occurs at cathode.\n(iii) Mg is oxidized and oxidation occurs at anode.\n(iv) Whole cell reaction can be written as : Refer diagram(ne1715)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','12-067','12-033',NULL),
(171402,'chemistry','Molar conductivity of ionic solution depends on .\n(a) temperature\n(b) distance between electrodes\n(c) concentration of electrolytes in solution\n(d) surface area of electrodes','a,c','Molar conductivity of ionic solution depends on temperature and concentration of electrolytes in solution.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(171403,'chemistry','Conductivity K, is equal to\na) (1/R)(1/A)\nb) G*/R\nc) Λm\nd) 1/A','a,b','k = (1/R)(1/A) or G*/R','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-078','12-044',NULL),
(171404,'chemistry','What will happen during the electrolysis of aqueous solution of CuSO₄ in the presence of Cu electrodes?\n(a) Copper will deposit at cathode.\n(b) Copper will dissolve at anode.\n(c) Oxygen will be released at anode.\n(d) Copper will deposit at anode.','a,b','Electrolysis of CuSO₄ can be represented by two half-cell reactions these occurring at cathode and anode respectively as\nAt cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu(s)\nAt anode   :  Cu(s) → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻\nCathode will witness deposit of Cu and witness will witness it\'s dissolution.\nHere, Cu will deposit at cathode while copper will dissolved at anode. Hence, options (a) and (b) are the correct choices.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171405,'chemistry','What will happen during the electrolysis of aqueous solution of CuSO₄ by using platinum electrodes?\n(a) Copper will deposit at cathode.\n(b) Copper will deposit at anode.\n(c) Oxygen will be released at anode.\n(d) Copper will dissolve at anode.','a,c','Refer diagram(ne1716)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171406,'chemistry','Refer diagram(n1717)','a,c','However, the sum of molar conductivities of constituent ions gives the molar conductivity of water but here NH₄OH is a weak electrolyte of which complete decomposition is not possible. Refer diagram(ne1717)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-081','12-047',NULL),
(250715,'chemistry','_______________ is used as a solvent in paint industry and in the preparation of a number of carbon compounds?','Ethanol','It is used as a solvent in paint industry and in the preparation of a number of carbon compounds.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 344; Paragraph No. 7)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Some Commercially Important Alcohols',NULL,'1','mcq','Ethanol','Methanol','Propanol','Butanol','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(171407,'chemistry','Conductivity of an electrolytic solution depends on:\n(a) nature of electrolyte \n(b) concentration of electrolyte\n(c) power of AC source\n(d) distance between the electrodes','a,b','Conductivity of electrolyte solution is due to presence of mobile ions in the solution. This type of conductance is known as ionic conductance. Conductivity of these type of solutions depend upon\n(i) the nature of electrolyte added\n(ii) size of the ion produced and their solvation\n(iii) concentration of electrolyte\n(iv) nature of solvent and its viscosity\n(v) temperature\nWhile power of source or distance between electrodes has no effect on conductivity of electrolyte solution.\nHence, options (a) and (b) are the correct choices.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(171408,'chemistry','E°cell = 1.1 V for Daniell cell. Which of the following expressions are correct description of state of equilibrium in this cell?\n(a) 1.1 = Kc \n(b) (2.303RT/2F)logKc = 1.1\n(c) log Kc = 2.2/0.059 \n(d) logKc = 1.1','b,c','E°cell = 2.303 RT logKc / nF = (0.059/2)logKc\n1.1 = (0.059/2)logKc\nlogKc = 2.2/0.059','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-073','12-039',NULL),
(171409,'chemistry','E°cell for some half-cell reactions are given below. On the basis of these marks the correct answer will be\n(i) H⁺(aq) + e⁻→ 1/2H₂(g) ; E°cell = 0.00 V\n(ii) 2H₂O(l) → O₂(g) + 4H⁺(aq) +4e⁻ ; E°cell = 1.23 V\n(iii) 2SO₄²⁻(aq) →  S₂O₈²⁻(aq) + 2e⁻ ; E°cell = 1.96 V\n(a) In dilute sulphuric acid solution, hydrogen will be reduced at cathode.\n(b) In concentrated sulphuric acid solution, water will be oxidized at anode.\n(c) In dilute sulphuric acid solution, water will be oxidized at anode.\n(d) In dilute sulphuric acid solution, SO²⁻ ion will be oxidized to tetrathionate ion at anode.','a,c','In dilute sulphuric acid solution, hydrogen will be reduced at cathode.\nH⁺ + e⁻ → 1/2 H₂ and H₂O is oxidised at anode.\n2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ , while in concentrated solution of sulphuric acid, SO²⁻ ions are oxidized to tetrathionate (SO₂) ions.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171410,'chemistry','The positive value of the standard electrode potential of Cu⁺²/Cu indicates that\n(a) this redox couple is a stronger reduction agent than the H/H₂ couple\n(b) this redox couple is a stronger oxidizing agent than H⁺/H₂\n(c) Cu can displace H₂ from acid\n(d) Cu cannot displace H₂  from acid','b,d','The Lesser the E° value of redox couple, the higher the reducing power ’.\nCu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu ; E° = 0.34 V\n2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂ ;  E° = 0.00 V\nSince, 2 H⁺/H₂ has lesser SRP than Cu⁺²/Cu redox couple. Therefore,\n(i) This redox couple is a stronger oxidizing agent than H⁺/H₂\n(ii) Cu cannot displace H₂ from acid.\nHence, (b) and (d) are correct.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,d','a,d','c,d','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171411,'chemistry','In the electrolysis of aqueous sodium chloride solution, which of the half-cell reaction will occur at anode? Refer diagram(n1718)','iv','During electrolysis of aqueous\nNaCl → Na⁺ + Cl⁻\nH₂O → H⁺ + OH⁻\nNa⁺ + e⁻ → Na ( E°cell = -2.71V)\nH⁺ + e⁻  → (1/2)H₂ ( E°cell = 0.00)\nAt cathode,\nH₂O + e⁻ →  (1/2)H₂ + OH⁻ \nAt anode , two reaction are possible\n Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + e⁻ ; E°cell = 1.36 V\n2H₂O → O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ ; E°cell = 1.23 V\n(ii)The reaction at anode with lower value of E° should be preferred, but oxidation of O₂ is kinetically slow process and needs high voltage thus reaction (i) takes place.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','i','ii','iii','iv','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171412,'chemistry','Refer diagram(n1719)','b','Refer diagram(ne1718)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(171413,'chemistry','While charging the lead storage battery','PbSO₄ anode is reduced to Pb','While charging the lead storage battery the reaction occurring on cell is reversed and PbSO₄(s) on anode and cathode is converted into Pb and PbO₂ respectively.\nHence, option (a) is the correct choice The electrode reactions are as follows:\nAt cathode: PbSO₄(s) + 2e⁻ → Pb(s) + SO₄²⁻(aq) (Reduction)\nAt Anode : PbSO₄(s) + 2H₂O → PbO₂(s) + SO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ + 2e⁻ (Oxidation)\n\nOverall reaction: 2PbSO₄(s) + 2H₂O → Pb(s) + PbO₂(s) + 4H⁺(aq) + 2SO₄²⁻(aq)','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','PbSO₄ anode is reduced to Pb','PbSO₄ cathode is reduced to Pb','PbSO₄ cathode is oxidized to Pb','PbSO₄ anode is oxidized to PbO₂','NEW','12-089','12-055',NULL),
(171414,'chemistry','The cell constant of a conductivity cell','remains constant for a cell','The cell constant of a conductivity cell (a) remains constant for a cell.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','changes with change of electrolyte','changes with change, of concentration of electrolyte','changes with temperature of electrolyte','remains constant for a cell','NEW','12-078','12-044',NULL),
(171415,'chemistry','The quantity of charge required to obtain one mole of aluminium from Al₂O₃ is','3F','Al₂O₃ → Al³⁺ + 3O²⁻\nAl³⁻ +3e⁻ → Al(for 1 mole)\n3F charge is required to obtain 1 mole Al from Al₂O₃.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','1F','6F','3F','2F','NEW','12-085','12-051',NULL),
(171416,'chemistry','Using given below find strongest reduction agent. Refer diagram(n1720)','Cr³⁺','A negative value of standard reduction potential for Cr³⁺ to Cr means that the redox couple is a stronger reducing agent','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl⁻','Cr','Cr³⁺','Mn²⁺','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171417,'chemistry','Find out which of the following is the strongest oxidizing agent. Refer diagram(n1721)','MnO₄⁻','The higher the positive value of standard reduction potential of metal ion, the higher will be its oxidizing capacity.Since, E°MnO₄⁻/Mn²⁺ has value equal to 1.51 V hence it is the strongest oxidizing agent.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl⁻','Mn²⁺','MnO₄⁻','Cr³⁺','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171418,'chemistry','Find out in which option the order of reducing power is correct. Refer diagram(n1722)','Mn²⁺ < Cl⁻ < Cr₂O₇²⁻ < MnO₄⁻','The lower the reduction potential, the higher is the reducing power.\nThe order of reducing power is Mn²⁺ < Cl⁻ < Cr₂O₇²⁻ < MnO₄⁻','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Cr³⁺ < Cl⁻ < Mn²⁺ < Cr','Mn²⁺ < Cl⁻ < Cr₂O₇²⁻ < MnO₄⁻','Cr³⁺ < Cl₄⁻ < Cr₂O₇²⁻ < MnO₄⁻','Mn²⁺ < Cr³⁺ < Cl⁻ < Cr','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171419,'chemistry','Find out the most stable ion in its reduced form. Refer diagram(n1723)','Mn²⁺','Mn²⁺ is most stale in its reduced form due to highest E° value.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl⁻','Cr³⁻','Cr','Mn²⁺','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171420,'chemistry','Find out the most stable oxidized species. Refer diagram(n1724)','Cr³⁺','Due to lowest value of E°, Cr³⁺ is most stable in its oxidised form.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Cr³⁺','MnO₄⁻','Cr₂O₇²⁻','Mn²⁺','NEW','12-070','12-036',NULL),
(171421,'chemistry','Which of the following statement about solutions of electrolytes is not correct?','Conductivity does not depend upon salvation of ions present in solution.','Conductivity depends upon salvation of ions present in the solution. The greater the salvation of ions, the lesser is the conductivity.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Conductivity of solution depends upon size of ions.','Conductivity depends upon viscosity of solution.','Conductivity does not depend upon salvation of ions present in solution.','Conductivity of solution increases with temperature.','NEW','12-077','12-043',NULL),
(171422,'chemistry','An electrochemical cell can behave like an electrolytic cell when','Eext < Ecell','If an external opposite potential is applied on the galvanic cell and increased reaction continues to take place till the opposing voltage reaches the value 1.1 V. At this stage no current flow through the cell and if there is any further increase in the external potential then reaction starts functioning in opposite direction.Hence, this works as an electrolytic cell.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Ecell = 0','Ecell > Eext','Eext < Ecell','Ecell = Eext','NEW','12-066','12-032',NULL),
(171423,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not correct about an inert electrode in a cell?','It provides surface for redox reaction.','Inert electrode does not participate in redox reaction and acts only as source or sink for electrons. It provides surface either for oxidation or for reduction reaction.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','It does not participate in the cell reaction.','It provides surface either for oxidation or for reduction reaction.','It provides surface for conduction of electrons.','It provides surface for redox reaction.','NEW','12-086','12-052',NULL),
(171424,'chemistry','The difference between the electrode potentials of two electrodes when no current is drawn through the cell is called','cell emf','EMF is the difference between the electrode potentials of two electrodes • when no current is drawn through the cell.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','cell potential','cell emf','potential difference','cell voltage','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(171425,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is correct?','Ecell is an intensive property while ∆ᵣG of cell reaction is an extensive property','Ecell is an intensive property as it does not depend upon mass of species (number of particles) but ∆ᵣG of the cell reaction is an extensive property because this depends upon mass of species (number of particles).','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Ecell and ∆ᵣG of cell reaction both are extensive properties','Ecell and ∆ᵣG of cell reaction both are intensive properties','Ecell is an intensive property while ∆ᵣG of cell reaction is an extensive property','Ecell is an extensive property while ∆ᵣG of cell reaction is an intensive property','NEW','12-074','12-040',NULL),
(171426,'chemistry','Electrode potential for Mg electrode varies according to the equation. Refer diagram(n1725)','b','EMg²⁺/Mg = E°Mg²⁺/Mg - (0.059/2) log[Mg²⁺]\nComparing this equation with the equation of straight line y = mx + c\nThe graph of Mg²⁺/Mg versus log[Mg²⁺] is a straight with a positive slope and intercept Mg²⁺/Mg.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-072','12-038',NULL),
(171427,'chemistry','Which cell will measure standard electrode potential of copper electrode?','Pt(s) l H₂(g,1bar) l H⁺ (aq.,1M) ll Cu²⁺(aq.,1M)lCu','When copper electrode is connected to standard hydrogen electrode, it acts as cathode and its standard electrode potential can be measured.\nE° = E°R - E°L = E°R - 0 = E°R\nPt(s) l H₂(g,1bar) l H⁺ (aq.,1M) ll Cu²⁺(aq.,1M)lCu\nTo calculate the standard electrode potential of the given cell it is coupled with the standard hydrogen electrode in which pressure of hydrogen gas is one bar and the cone, of H⁺ ion in the solution is one molar and also the concentrations of the oxidized and the reduced forms of the species in the right hand half-cell are unity.','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','Pt(s) l H₂(g,0.1bar) l H⁺ (aq.,1M) ll Cu²⁺(aq.,1M)lCu','Pt(s) l H₂(g,1bar) l H⁺ (aq.,1M) ll Cu²⁺(aq.,2M)lCu','Pt(s) l H₂(g,1bar) l H⁺ (aq.,1M) ll Cu²⁺(aq.,1M)lCu','Pt(s) l H₂(g,1bar) l H⁺ (aq.,0.1M) ll Cu²⁺(aq.,1M)lCu','NEW','12-068','12-034',NULL),
(171428,'chemistry','Predict the products of electrolysis in each of the following : An aqueous solution of AgNO₃ with silver electrodes.','Ag & Ag⁺','AgNO₃(s) + aq → Ag⁺(aq) + NO₃⁻(aq)\n H₂O ⇌ H⁺ + OH⁻\nAt cathode: Ag⁺ ions have lower discharge potential than H⁺ ions. Hence, Ag⁺ ions will be deposited as Ag in preference to H⁺ ions.\nAg⁺(aq) + e⁻  → Ag(s)\n\nAt anode: As Ag anode is attacked by NO₃⁻ ions, Ag of the anode will dissolve to form Ag⁺ ions in the solution.\n\nAg(s) → Ag⁺(aq) +e⁻','Physical Chemistry >> Electrochemistry >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','Ag & Ag⁺','Ag & Ag','Cu & Ag⁺','Ag⁺ & Cu','NEW','12-087','12-053',NULL),
(171429,'biology','Blood carbon dioxide levels determine the pH of other body fluids as well as blood, including the pH of cerebrospinal fluid. How does this enable the organism to control breathing?','The medulla, which is in contact with cerebrospinal fluid, monitors pH and uses this measure to control breathing.','Organism controls breathing with help of medulla, which is in contact with cerebrospinal fluid, monitors pH and uses this measure to control breathing.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduced)',NULL,'3','mcq','The brain directly measures and monitors carbon dioxide and causes breathing changes accordingly.','The medulla, which is in contact with cerebrospinal fluid, monitors pH and uses this measure to control breathing.','The brain alters the pH of the cerebrospinal fluid to force the animal to retain more or less carbon dioxide.','Stretch receptors in the lungs cause the medulla to speed up or slow breathing.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(171430,'biology','Which of the following does not stimulate the medullary breathing center chemosensitive neurons?','low blood oxygen','Blood Oxygen does not have significant effect in stimulating the medullary breathing center chemosensitive neurons.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduced)',NULL,'2','mcq','low blood pH','high blood carbon dioxide','low blood oxygen','high spinal fluid acidity','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(171431,'biology','A person suffers punctures in his chest cavity in an accident, without any damages to the lungs, it\'s effect could','rapid increase in breathing rate','This is because the chest cavity plays a crucial role in the mechanics of breathing. Punctures in the chest cavity can disrupt the normal negative pressure that allows the lungs to expand and contract during the breathing process. To compensate for this disruption and maintain adequate oxygen exchange, the body would typically increase the breathing rate to enhance air intake and exhalation. This is a protective response to ensure sufficient oxygen supply, even if the lungs are not directly damaged.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduced)',NULL,'3','mcq','reduce breathing rate','rapid increase in breathing rate','no change in Respiration','cessation of breathing','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(171432,'biology','These cells which do not respire','erythrocytes','Erythrocytes, or red blood cells, do not respire in the traditional sense. They lack mitochondria, which are the cellular organelles responsible for aerobic respiration. Because they lack mitochondria, red blood cells do not consume oxygen for energy production and, therefore, do not respire in the same way that other cells do.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduced)',NULL,'3','mcq','epidermal cells','sieve cells','cortical cells','erythrocytes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(171433,'biology','What is the primary function of the diaphragm during inhalation?','To contract and increase the volume of the thoracic cavity','Diaphragm contracts and increases the volume of the thoracic cavity during inspiration','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduced)',NULL,'2','mcq','To contract and decrease the volume of the thoracic cavity','To relax and increase the volume of the thoracic cavity','To contract and increase the volume of the thoracic cavity','To relax and decrease the volume of the thoracic cavity','NEW','11-271','11-186',NULL),
(171434,'biology','What is the function of the epiglottis in the respiratory system?','cover over larynx','During swallowing of food, glottis can be covered by a thin elastic cartilaginous flap called epiglottis to prevent the entry of food into larynx','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduced)',NULL,'2','mcq','cover over alveoli','cover over bronchioles','cover over larynx','cover over lungs','NEW','11-269','11-184',NULL),
(171435,'biology','In the context of gaseous exchange, which of the following best describes Dalton\'s law?','Total pressure is the sum of the partial pressures of individual gases.','Dalton\'s Law of Partial Pressures states that in a mixture of non-reacting gases, the total pressure exerted by the mixture is equal to the sum of the partial pressures of individual gases. In other words, each gas in a mixture exerts pressure independently as if it were the only gas present, and the total pressure is the sum of these individual pressures.','Human Physiology >> Breathing and Exchange of Gases >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduced)',NULL,'3','mcq','Total pressure is the sum of the partial pressures of individual gases.','The rate of diffusion of a gas is inversely proportional to its molecular weight','Gases always combine in simple whole number ratios.','Volume and temperature are directly proportional','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
