(250819,'biology','Which of the following structures fuse together to form a diploid zygote?','Haploid nucleus of the sperms,haploid ovum','Haploid nucleus of the sperms and haploid ovum fuse together to form zygote. Reference   : NCERT Page 51','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Fertilisation and Implantation',NULL,'2','mcq','Haploid nucleus of the sperms,diploid ovum','Diploid nucleus of the sperms,haploid ovum','Diploid nucleus of the sperms,diploid ovum','Haploid nucleus of the sperms,haploid ovum','NEW','12-051','12-035',NULL),
(250820,'biology','Assertion: 50 % of sperms carry the X chromosome while the other 50 percent carry the Y chromosome\n \nReason: In the male gametes (sperms) the sex chromosome could be either X or Y','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','the chromosome pattern in the human female is XX and that in the male is XY. Therefore, all the haploid gametes (ova) produced by the female have the sex chromosome X whereas in the male gametes (sperms) the sex chromosome could be either X or Y, hence, 50 % of sperms carry the X chromosome while the other 50 per\ncent carry the Y. After fusion of the male and female gametes the zygote would carry either XX or XY depending on whether the sperm carrying X or Y fertilised the ovum. The zygote carrying XX would develop into a female\nbaby and XY would form a male\nReference  : NCERT class12th, chapter 3, page 52, paragraph 2.','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Fertilisation and Implantation',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but reason is fase','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-052','12-036',NULL),
(250821,'biology','After fusion of the male and female gametes the zygote would carry ______ depending on whether the sperm carrying X or Y fertilised the ovum.','Either XX or XY','After fusion of the male and female gametes the zygote would carry either XX or XY depending on whether the sperm carrying X or Y fertilised the ovum.Reference   : NCERT Page 51','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Fertilisation and Implantation',NULL,'2','mcq','XX only','XY only','XX and XY both','Either XX or XY','NEW','12-051','12-035',NULL),
(250822,'biology','The zygote carrying XX would develop into a _____ baby and XY would form a _____ baby','Female,male','The zygote carrying XX would develop into a female baby and XY would form a male baby. Reference   : NCERT Page 51','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Fertilisation and Implantation',NULL,'2','mcq','Male,female','Female,male','Male,male','Female,female','NEW','12-051','12-035',NULL),
(250823,'biology','Scientifically it is correct to say that the sex of the baby is determined by the mother and not by the father!','FALSE','The sex of the baby is determined by the father and not by the mother! Because mother has XX chromosomes while the father has XY chromosomes. Reference   : NCERT Page 51','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Fertilisation and Implantation',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-051','12-035',NULL),
(250824,'biology','Choose the incorrect statement:','The embryo with 2 to 4 blastomeres is called a morula','The embryo with 8 to 16 blastomeres is called a morula. Reference   : NCERT Page 51','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Fertilisation and Implantation',NULL,'2','mcq','The mitotic division starts as the zygote moves through the isthmus of the oviduct called cleavage towards the uterus and forms 2, 4, 8, 16 daughter cells called blastomeres','The embryo with 2 to 4 blastomeres is called a morula','The morula continues to divide and transforms into blastocyst','The embryo with 8 to 16 blastomeres is called a morula','NEW','12-051','12-035',NULL),
(250825,'biology','The morula continues to divide and transforms into _______ as it moves further into the uterus','Blastocyst','The morula continues to divide and transforms into blastocyst as it moves further into the uterus. Reference   : NCERT Page 51','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Fertilisation and Implantation',NULL,'2','mcq','Blastomeres','Blastocyst','Trophoblast','Trophocyst','NEW','12-051','12-035',NULL),
(250826,'biology','The blastomeres in the blastocyst are arranged into an outer layer called ________ and an inner group of cells attached to trophoblast called __________','Trophoblast,innercell mass','The blastomeres in the blastocyst are arranged into an outer layer called trophoblast and an inner group of cells attached to trophoblast called inner cell mass. Reference   : NCERT Page 52','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Fertilisation and Implantation',NULL,'3','mcq','Innercell mass,trophoblast','Trophocyst,innercell mass','Trophoblast,innercell mass','Innercell mass,trophocyst','NEW','12-052','12-036',NULL),
(250827,'biology','Choose the correct fate of trophoblast layer and inner cell mass:','The trophoblast layer then gets attached to the endometrium and the inner cell mass gets differentiated as the embryo','The trophoblast layer then gets attached to the endometrium and the inner cell mass gets differentiated as the embryo. Reference   : NCERT Page 52','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Fertilisation and Implantation',NULL,'2','mcq','The trophoblast layer then gets attached to the endometrium and the inner cell mass gets differentiated as the embryo','The trophoblast layer then gets differentiated as the embryo and the inner cell mass gets attached to the myometrium','The trophoblast layer then gets differentiated as the embryo and the inner cell mass gets attached to the myometrium','The trophoblast layer then gets differentiated as the embryo and the inner cell mass gets attached to the endometrium','NEW','12-052','12-036',NULL),
(250828,'biology','What is implantation?','After attachment of the trophoblast, the uterine cells divide rapidly and covers the blastocyst. As a result, the blastocyst becomes embedded in the endometrium of the uterus. This is called implantation','After attachment of the trophoblast, the uterine cells divide rapidly and covers the blastocyst. As a result, the blastocyst becomes embedded in the endometrium of the uterus. This is called implantation. Reference   : NCERT Page 52','Reproduction >> Human Reproduction >> Fertilisation and Implantation',NULL,'2','mcq','After attachment of the trophoblast, the uterine cells divide rapidly and covers the blastocyst. As a result, the blastocyst becomes embedded in the myometrium of the uterus. This is called implantation','After attachment of the trophoblast, the uterine cells divide rapidly and covers the blastocyst. As a result, the blastocyst becomes embedded in the endometrium of the uterus. This is called implantation','After attachment of the trophoblast, the uterine cells divide rapidly and covers the blastocyst. As a result, the blastocyst becomes embedded in the perimetrium of the uterus. This is called implantation','After attachment of the trophoblast, the uterine cells divide rapidly and covers the morula. As a result, the blastocyst becomes embedded in the endometrium of the uterus. This is called implantation','NEW','12-052','12-036',NULL),
