(251304,'chemistry','Benzyl alcohol → Benzoic acid\n\nThe reaction can be carried out by ?','Acidic KMnO4','Acidic KMnO₄ oxidises primary alcohol into carboxylic acids. Other reagent oxidises to aldehydes Pyridinium chlorochromate is also known as Correy\'s reagent. It oxidises primary alcohol to aldehydes and not carboxylic acids A better reagent for oxidation of primary alcohols to aldehydes in good yield is pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC). (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 340; Paragraph No. 2) CrO₃ in anhydrous medium','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Conversion Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Sulphuric acid','Acidic KMnO4','Phosphoric acid','Acetic acid','NEW','12-340','12-210',NULL),
(251401,'chemistry','During dehydration of alcohols to alkenes by heating with conc. sulphuric acid, the initiation step is [2003]','protonation of alcohol molecule','Alcohols undergo dehydration (removal of a molecule of water) to form alkenes on treating with a protic acid. Protonation of alcohol group is the first step during dehydration.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 338; Paragraph No. 6) . Refer diagram(ne2568)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','formation of carbocation','elimination of water','formation of an ester','protonation of alcohol molecule','NEW','12-338','12-208',NULL),
(251402,'chemistry','Among the following compounds which can be dehydrated very easily?','CH₃CH₂C(CH₃)(OH)CH₂CH₃','Dehydration leads to formation of cabocation. The tertiary carbocation is more stable than secondary or primary . Higher the stability of intermediate, higher is the reactivity of the reactant molecule. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 338; Paragraph No. 4) . Refer diagram(ne2569)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₂OH','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH(OH)CH₃','CH₃CH₂C(CH₃)(OH)CH₂CH₃','CH₃CH₂CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₂OH','NEW','12-338','12-208',NULL),
(251403,'chemistry','The best reagent to convert pent-3-en-2-ol into pent-3-en-2-one is:','Pyridinium chlorochromate','Pyridinium chlorochromate will oxidise primary and secondary  alcohols to aldehyde or ketones respectively.\n\nPyridinium chlorochromate is also know as Correy\'s reagent. It oxidises primary alcohol to aldehydes and not carboxylic acids\nA better reagent for oxidation of primary alcohols to aldehydes in good yield is pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 340; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Pyridinium chlorochromate','Chromic anhydride in glacial acetic acid','Acidic dichromate','Acidic permanganate','NEW','12-340','12-210',NULL),
(251404,'chemistry','HBr reacts with CH₂=CH-OCH₃ under anhydrous conditions at room temperature to give','CH₃-CHBr-OCH₃','Cleavage of C-O bond in ethers takes place with HBr and HI because these reagents are sufficiently acidic.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 348; Paragraph No. 1). Refer diagram(ne2570)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','BrCH₂-CH₂-OCH₃','CH₃-CHBr-OCH₃','CH₃CHO and CH₃Br','BrCH₂CHO and CH₃OH','NEW','12-348','12-218',NULL),
(251405,'chemistry','Among the following in the given diagram(n2588) the one that gives positive iodoform test upon reaction with I₂ and NaOH is','b','It is an example of iodoform test and this test is given by those alcohols which have CH₃CH(OH)– group. methyl alcohol do not give this test.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-343','12-213','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251405q.png'),
(251406,'chemistry','The structure of the compound that gives a tribromo derivatives on treatment with bromine water is :  Refer diagram(n2589)','c','When phenol is treated with bromine water, 2,4,6-tribromophenol is formed.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 342; Paragraph No. 3). Refer diagram(ne2571)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-342','12-212','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251406q.png'),
(251407,'chemistry','The electrophile involved in the given reaction diagram(n2590) is:','dichlorocarbene','On treating phenol with chloroform in the presence of sodium hydroxide, a –CHO group is introduced at ortho position of benzene ring. This reaction is known as Reimer - Tiemann reaction.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 343; Paragraph No. 1). Refer diagram(ne2572)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','trichloromethyl anion','formyl cation','dichloromethyl cation','dichlorocarbene','NEW','12-343','12-213','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251407q.png'),
(251408,'chemistry','In the following sequence of reactions , the compound D is: . Refer diagram(n2591)','n-propyl alcohol','The given reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne2573)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','butanal','n-butyl alcohol','n-propyl alcohol','propanal','NEW','12-341','12-211','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251408q.png'),
(251409,'chemistry','Phenol, when it first reacts with concentrated sulphuric acid and then with concentrated nitric acid, gives','o-nitrophenol','The given reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne2574)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','2,4,6- trinitrobenzene','o-nitrophenol','p-nitrophenol','nitrobenzene','NEW','12-341','12-211',NULL),
(251410,'chemistry','The major product obtained on interaction of phenol with NaOH and carbon dioxide is:','Salicylic acid','The mechanism of Kolbe reaction proceeds through the nucleophilic addition of phenoxide to carbon dioxide, yielding the salicylate. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 342; Paragraph No. 3) . Refer diagram(ne2575)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Salicylaldehyde','Salicylic acid','Phthalic acid','Benzoic acid','NEW','12-342','12-212',NULL),
(251411,'chemistry','From amongst the following alcohols, the one that would react fastest with conc. HCl and anhydrous ZnCl₂, is','2-Methylpropan-2-ol','Alcohols are soluble in Lucas reagent (conc. HCl and ZnCl₂) while their halides are immiscible and produce turbidity in solution. The reaction proceeds through the formation of carbocation.\n\nTertiary alcohol react fastest  because it forms most stable carbocation , therefore 2-Methylpropan-2-ol is correct\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 338; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','2-Butanol','2-Methylpropan-2-ol','2-Methylpropanol','1-Butanol','NEW','12-338','12-208',NULL),
(251412,'chemistry','Consider thiol anion(RS⁻) and alkoxy anion (RO⁻). Which of the following statements is correct?','RS⁻ is less basic but more nucleophillic than RO⁻','Nucleophilic strength is directly proportional to electronegativity of an atom. \nBasicity depends on the strength of H--Metal bond.Thus, RS⁻ is less basic but more nucleophillic than RO⁻','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','RS⁻ is less basic but more nucleophillic than RO⁻','RS⁻ is more basic and more nucleophilic than RO⁻','RS⁻ is more basic but less nucleophillic than RO⁻','RS⁻ is less basic and less nucleophilic than RO⁻','NEW','12-341','12-211',NULL),
(251413,'chemistry','The correct order of acid strength of the following compounds:\n\na. Phenol\nb. p-Cresol\nc. m-Nitrophenol\nd. p-Nitrophenol','d > c > a > b','Presence of electron withdrawing group on phenol increases the acidic strength, while electron donating group decreasing the acidic strength','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','d > c > a > b','b > d > a > c','a > b > d > c','c > b > a > d','NEW','12-337','12-207',NULL),
(251414,'chemistry','Consider the following reaction:\nC₂H₅OH + H₂SO₄---> Product\n\nAmong the following, which one cannot be formed as a product under any conditions?','Acetylene','The products formed are given in the reaction diagram(ne2576)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Ethylene','Acetylene','Diethyl ether','Ethylhydrogen sulphate','NEW','12-338','12-208',NULL),
(251415,'chemistry','Arrange the following compounds in order of decreasing acidity :  Refer diagram(n2592)','III > I > II > IV','Presence of electron withdrawing group on phenol increases the acidic strength, while electron donating group decreasing the acidic strength','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','II > IV > I > III','I > II > III > IV','III > I > II > IV','IV > III > I > II','NEW','12-337','12-207','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251415q.png'),
(251416,'chemistry','The most suitable reagent for the conversion of R-CH₂OH---> R-CHO is:','PCC','Pyridinium chlorochromate is also know as Correy\'s reagent. It oxidises primary alcohol to aldehydes and not carboxylic acids\nA better reagent for oxidation of primary alcohols to aldehydes in good yield is pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 340; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','KMnO₄','K₂Cr₂O₇','CrO₃','PCC','NEW','12-339','12-209',NULL),
(251417,'chemistry','Sodium phenoxide when heated with CO₂ under pressure at 125ᵒC yields a product which on acetylation produces C. The major product C is? Refer diagram(n2593)','a','Acetylation of salicylic acid produces aspirin.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 338; Paragraph No. 1). Refer diagram(ne2577)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-338','12-208','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251417q.png'),
(251418,'chemistry','Phenol on treatment with CO₂ in the presence of NaOH followed by acidification produces compound C as the major product. C on treatment with (CH₃CO)₂O in the presence of catalytic amount of H₂SO₄ produces: Refer diagram(n2594)','a','Acetylation of salicylic acid produces aspirin.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 338; Paragraph No. 1). Refer diagram(ne2577)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-338','12-208','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/251418q.png'),
(251419,'biology','Choose the correct statement/statements-:\n\n1.  Structure of a sperm has a head , middle piece and tail.\n\n2. The acrosome contains enzymes.\n\n3. Sperm head contains a diploid nucleus that later undergoes meiosis \n\n4. The entire structure of a sperm is covered by acrosome','2 only','The sperm  is a microscopic structure composed of a head, neck, a middle piece and a tail . A plasma membrane envelops the whole body of sperm. The sperm head contains an elongated haploid nucleus, the anterior portion of which is covered by a cap-like structure, acrosome. The acrosome is filled with enzymes that help fertilisation of the ovum.\n(Reference   NCERT BIO 12th , page no 48, 1st paragraph )','Reproduction>> Human reproduction >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2 only','1 and 4','1 and 2','1,3 and 4','NEW','12-048','12-032',NULL),
(251420,'biology','ASSERTION-: Tail of the sperm helps with the motility \n\nREASON-: It contains dynein protein that aids in motility and derives energy from mitochondria in the body of sperm','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Tail helps with the motility as it derives its energy from the Mitochondria present in the body of the sperm , and the dynein protein helps in motility by contraction\n(Reference   NCERT BIO 12th , page no 48, 1st paragraph )','Reproduction>> Human reproduction >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is NOT the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false.','Assertion is false but reason is true.','NEW','12-048','12-032',NULL),
(251501,'chemistry','Assertion: Williamson synthesis is preferred over acidic dehydration for the preparation of unsymmetrical ethers.\n\nReason: In acidic dehydration, substitution reaction to give an ether is associated with the formation of an alkene.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The assertion is true, and the reason is a correct explanation of why Williamson synthesis is preferred over acidic dehydration for the preparation of unsymmetrical ethers. In Williamson synthesis, an alkyl halide is allowed to react with sodium alkoxide, leading to the formation of the desired ether. This method is particularly useful for the preparation of unsymmetrical ethers, as it allows the use of different alkyl halides and alkoxides.\n\nOn the other hand, acidic dehydration is not appropriate for the preparation of unsymmetrical ethers, as it favors the formation of alkenes, and the substitution reaction leading to the formation of the desired ether is limited to primary alkyl groups.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-345','12-215',NULL),
(251502,'chemistry','Assertion: Alcohols undergo dehydration in the presence of protic acids\n\nReason: The formation of the reaction product depends on the reaction conditions.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Alcohols undergo dehydration in the presence of protic acids, and the formation of the reaction product depends on the reaction conditions.\n\nFor example, ethanol is dehydrated to ethene in the presence of sulfuric acid at 443 K. At 413 K, ethoxyethane is the main product.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-338','12-208',NULL),
(251503,'chemistry','Assertion: In wine making, grapes are the source of sugars and yeast. \n\nReason: The action of zymase is inhibited once the percentage of alcohol formed exceeds 12 percent.','Assertion is true but reason is false','When grapes are crushed, the sugar and yeast come in contact and fermentation starts, releasing carbon dioxide. However, if air gets into the fermentation mixture, the oxygen in the air oxidizes ethanol to ethanoic acid, which destroys the taste of the alcoholic drink. Additionally, the action of zymase is inhibited once the percentage of alcohol formed exceeds 14 percent.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(251504,'chemistry','Assertion: Ethanol is a colourless liquid with a boiling point of 351 K\n\nReason: It is used as a solvent in the paint industry.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Ethanol is a common solvent in the paint industry and is also used in the preparation of a number of carbon compounds. It has a boiling point of 351 K and is a colorless liquid.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(251505,'chemistry','Assertion: Ethanol is made unfit for drinking by denaturation.\n\nReason: Denaturation of alcohol involves mixing copper sulphate and pyridine to make it unfit for consumption.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The commercial alcohol is made unfit for drinking\nby mixing in it some copper sulphate (to give it a colour) and pyridine\n(a foul smelling liquid). It is known as denaturation of alcohol.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(251506,'chemistry','Assertion: Methanol is commonly known as ‘wood spirit’ and it is used as a solvent in paints, varnishes, and for making formaldehyde.\n\nReason: Methanol is produced today by catalytic hydrogenation of carbon monoxide at high pressure and temperature in the presence of a ZnO-Cr2O3 catalyst.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Methanol is commonly known as ‘wood spirit’ and it is used as a solvent in paints, varnishes, and for making formaldehyde.\n\nMethanol is produced today by catalytic hydrogenation of carbon monoxide at high pressure and temperature in the presence of a ZnO-Cr2O3 catalyst. Methanol is a colorless liquid that boils at 337 K and is highly poisonous in nature, with even small quantities of ingestion causing blindness and large quantities leading to death.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-344','12-214',NULL),
(151417,'chemistry','A : The coordination number of ionic compound depends upon radius ratio.\n\nR : Higher the coordination number higher will be stability.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','C.N. of ionic compounds depends upon radius ratio, more the radius ratio, more will be C.N. and higher will be\n stability.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(251507,'chemistry','Assertion: Oxidation of phenol with chromic acid produces a conjugated diketone known as benzoquinone. \n\nReason: In the presence of air, phenols are slowly oxidised to dark coloured mixtures containing quinones.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Phenols are easily oxidized due to the presence of the hydroxyl group (-OH) which makes the benzene ring electron-rich, making it more susceptible to oxidation. The oxidation of phenol with chromic acid produces benzoquinone, which is a conjugated diketone. The presence of air causes the oxidation of phenols to form dark coloured mixtures containing quinones.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-343','12-213',NULL),
(251508,'chemistry','Assertion: On treating phenol with chloroform in the presence of sodium hydroxide, a –CHO group is introduced at the ortho position of the benzene ring.\n\nReason: The negative charge on the oxygen atom in the phenoxide ion is responsible for its increased reactivity towards electrophilic aromatic substitution.','Assertion is true but reason is false','On treating phenol with chloroform in the presence of sodium hydroxide, a –CHO group is introduced at the ortho position of the benzene ring. This reaction is known as the Reimer-Tiemann reaction. The intermediate substituted benzal chloride is hydrolysed in the presence of alkali to produce salicylaldehyde.\nReason: The Reimer-Tiemann reaction is a nucleophilic substitution reaction where the nucleophile is the phenoxide ion generated by the reaction of phenol with sodium hydroxide.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-343','12-213',NULL),
(251509,'chemistry','Assertion: The usual halogenation of benzene takes place in the presence of a Lewis acid, such as FeBr3. \n\nReason: In the case of phenol, the polarisation of the bromine molecule takes place even in the absence of a Lewis acid.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The usual halogenation of benzene takes place in the presence of a Lewis acid, such as FeBr3. In the case of phenol, the polarisation of the bromine molecule takes place even in the absence of a Lewis acid. It is due to the highly activating effect of –OH group attached to the benzene ring.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-342','12-212',NULL),
(251510,'chemistry','Assertion: Nitration of phenol with dilute nitric acid at low temperature (298 K) yields a mixture of ortho and para nitrophenols.\n\nReason: Concentrated nitric acid converts phenol to 2,4,6-trinitrophenol, also known as picric acid','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Nitration of phenol with dilute nitric acid at low temperature (298 K) yields a mixture of ortho and para nitrophenols. The ortho and para isomers can be separated by steam distillation. o-Nitrophenol is steam volatile due to intramolecular hydrogen bonding while p-nitrophenol is less volatile due to intermolecular hydrogen bonding which causes the association of molecules.\n\nConcentrated nitric acid converts phenol to 2,4,6-trinitrophenol, also known as picric acid,','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-341','12-211',NULL),
(251511,'chemistry','Assertion: Tertiary alcohols do not undergo oxidation reaction. \n\nReason: Under strong reaction conditions such as strong oxidizing agents (KMnO4) and elevated temperatures, cleavage of various C-C bonds takes place and a mixture of carboxylic acids is formed','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Tertiary alcohols do not have any alpha-hydrogen atoms and hence cannot undergo oxidation.\n Tertiary alcohols do not undergo oxidation reaction. Under strong reaction conditions such as strong oxidizing agents (KMnO4) and elevated temperatures, cleavage of various C-C bonds takes place and a mixture of carboxylic acids containing a lesser number of carbon atoms is formed.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-340','12-210',NULL),
(251512,'chemistry','Assertion: Phenol is manufactured from the hydrocarbon, cumene.\n\nReason: Cumene is oxidised in the presence of air to cumene hydroperoxide which is converted to phenol and acetone by treating it with dilute acid.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Phenol is manufactured from the hydrocarbon, cumene.Cumene is oxidised in the presence of air to cumene hydroperoxide. It is converted to phenol and acetone by treating it with dilute acid.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-332','12-202',NULL),
(251513,'chemistry','Assertion: A methanol poisoned patient is treated by giving intravenous infusions of diluted ethanol. \n\nReason: Ethanol competitively inhibits the enzyme alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH), which is responsible for the oxidation of methanol to formaldehyde','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Ethanol competitively inhibits the enzyme alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH), which is responsible for the oxidation of methanol to formaldehyde. This allows time for the kidneys to excrete the methanol and prevents the accumulation of toxic levels of methanol metabolites. Intravenous infusions of diluted ethanol are therefore used as an antidote for methanol poisoning.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-340','12-210',NULL),
(251514,'chemistry','Assertion: Strong oxidizing agents such as acidified potassium permanganate are used for getting carboxylic acids from alcohols directly\n\nReason: Secondary alcohols are not easily oxidized to aldehydes','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Strong oxidizing agents such as acidified potassium permanganate are used for getting carboxylic acids from alcohols directly. CrO3 in anhydrous medium is used as the oxidizing agent for the isolation of aldehydes. A better reagent for oxidation of primary alcohols to aldehydes in good yield is pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC), a complex of chromium trioxide with pyridine and HCl. Secondary alcohols are oxidized to ketones by chromic anhydride (CrO3).\nSecondary alcohols are not easily oxidized to aldehydes because they are more stable due to the presence of two alkyl groups attached to the carbonyl carbon.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-340','12-210',NULL),
(251515,'chemistry','Assertion: Alcohols and phenols have higher boiling points than hydrocarbons\n\nReason: The –OH group in alcohols and phenols is involved in intermolecular hydrogen bonding','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The –OH group in alcohols and phenols is involved in intermolecular hydrogen bonding, which results in the formation of hydrogen bonds between molecules. This hydrogen bonding leads to a stronger attractive force between the molecules, which requires more energy to break the intermolecular forces and results in higher boiling points.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-333','12-203',NULL),
(251516,'chemistry','Assertion: The reduction of ketones to secondary alcohols is more difficult than the reduction of aldehydes to primary alcohols.\nReason: Ketones have two alkyl groups attached to the carbonyl carbon, which makes it less reactive towards reduction than aldehydes','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Ketones have two alkyl groups attached to the carbonyl carbon, which makes it less reactive towards reduction than aldehydes, which have only one alkyl group. Additionally, ketones tend to form stable enolate intermediates during reduction, which can undergo further reactions leading to complex mixtures of products.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-329','12-199',NULL),
(251517,'chemistry','Assertion: The bond angle in alcohols is slightly less than the tetrahedral angle due to the repulsion between unshared electron pairs of oxygen.\n\nReason: The unshared electron pairs of oxygen cause repulsion in alcohols, which results in a bond angle slightly less than the tetrahedral angle.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The bond angle in alcohols is slightly less than the tetrahedral\nangle (109°-28′). It is due to the repulsion between the unshared\nelectron pairs of oxygen.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-329','12-199',NULL),
(251518,'chemistry','Assertion: Alcohols can be formed by the addition of water to an alkene in a way opposite to the Markovnikov\'s rule.\n\nReason: This reaction yields alcohol in excellent yield.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','By hydroboration–oxidation: The addition of borane to the double bond takes place in such a manner that the boron atom gets attached to the sp2  carbon carrying greater number of hydrogen atoms. The alcohol so formed looks as if it has been formed by the addition of water to the alkene in a way opposite to the Markovnikov’s rule. In this reaction, alcohol is obtained in excellent yield.','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Assertion & Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','12-330','12-200',NULL),
(251519,'biology','Choose the correct statement/statements-:\n\n1. Parturation is the expulsion of fetus induced by a complex endocrine mechanism \n\n2. The signals for parturition originate from the fully developed foetus and the placenta which induce mild uterine contractions called foetal ejection reflex. \n\n3. Estrogen, HCG and oxytocin are responsible for contractions \n\n4. First movement of the fetus and hair on its head are usually observed during the 5th month','2 and 4','The first movements of the foetus and appearance of hair on the head are usually observed during the fifth month.Parturition is induced by a complex neuroendocrine mechanism. The signals for parturition originate from the fully developed foetus and the placenta which induce mild uterine contractions called foetal ejection reflex. This triggers release of oxytocin from the maternal pituitary. Oxytocin acts on the uterine muscle and causes stronger uterine contractions, which in turn stimulates further secretion of oxytocin. The stimulatory reflex between the uterine contraction and oxytocin secretion continues resulting in stronger and stronger contractions. This leads to expulsion of the baby out of the uterus through the birth canal – parturition. Soon after the infant is delivered, the placenta is also expelled out of the uterus.Parturition is the expulsion of fetus induced by a complex neuroendocrine mechanism,Only oxytocin is responsible for contractions.\n  (Reference   NCERT BIO 12th , page no 54, 3rd paragraph  )','Reproduction>> Human reproduction >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','1,2 and 3','1,2 and 4','1 and 4','2 and 4','NEW','12-054','12-038',NULL),
(251520,'biology','ASSERTION-: Mammary glands undergo differentiation during pregnancy \n\nREASON-: Colostrum contains several factors essential for growth and development.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is NOT the correct explanation of the assertion.','Mammary Glands undergo differentiation during pregnancy under hormonal influence of prolactin, estrogen and progestrone \n  (Reference   NCERT BIO 12th , page no 54, 4th paragraph  )','Reproduction>> Human reproduction >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is NOT the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false.','Assertion is false but reason is true.','NEW','12-054','12-038',NULL),
(251521,'biology','ASSERTION-: Humans are sexually reproducing mammals\n\nREASON-: Humans are viviparous and nulliparous creatures','Assertion is true but reason is false.','The statement given as reason is false as viviparous is a characteristic of humans which means bearing an offspring which has developed inside the body \nNulliparous is a term used when a woman hasn\'t concieved any children .\n  (Reference   NCERT BIO 12th , page no 55, Summary  )','Reproduction>> Human reproduction >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is NOT the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false.','Assertion is false but reason is true.','NEW','12-055','12-039',NULL),
(251522,'biology','ASSERTION-: Seminiferous tubules are coiled structures \n\nREASON-: They are lined by Sertoli cells','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is NOT the correct explanation of the assertion.','The production of sperms in the testes takes place in a highly coiled structure called the seminiferous tubules.\nEach lobule contains one to three highly coiled seminiferous tubules in which sperms are produced. Each seminiferous tubule is lined on its inside by two types of cells called male germ cells (spermatogonia) and Sertoli cells.\n  (Reference   NCERT BIO 12th , page no 55, Summary)','Reproduction>> Human reproduction >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is NOT the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false.','Assertion is false but reason is true.','NEW','12-055','12-039',NULL),
(251523,'biology','Choose the correct statement/statements-:\n\n1. Ovarian follicles are present outside the stroma \n\n2. Ovaries are responsible for other functions also apart from producing gametes \n\n3. The female genital tract includes mons pubis, labia majora ,labia minora and clitoris \n\n4. Mammary gland growth during puberty are the primary sexual characteristics','2 only','- Ovarian follicles are embedded inside the stroma \n- Ovaries also produce several steroid hormones (ovarian hormones).\nThe female external genitalia includes mons pubis, labia majora ,labia minora ,clitoris and hymen. NOT GENITAL TRACT.\n- Mammary glands are the secondary  sexual characteristics \n  (Reference   NCERT BIO 12th , page no 55, Summary  )','Reproduction>> Human reproduction >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','2 and 3','2 only','3 only','2,3 and 4','NEW','12-055','12-039',NULL),
(160101,'chemistry','What concentration of fluoride ion causes the tooth to become mottled?','1.5 ppm','fluoride ions in water prevents tooth decay, while 1.5 ppm causes the tooth to become mottled\nReference : [NCERT Class 12 Page - 35 Paragraph 1]','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Introduction and Types of Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','1 ppm','1.5 ppm','2 ppm','1.75 ppm','NEW','12-035','12-001',NULL),
(160102,'chemistry','Incorrect statement about homogeneous mixtures?','These always contain a solute and a solvent','Solutions are homogeneous mixtures of two or more than two components. \nReference : (NCERT Class 12 Page - 35 Paragraph - 3','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Introduction and Types of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','These have uniform composition throughout the mixture','These always contain a solute and a solvent','These have same chemical properties throughout the mixture','These show same physical properties throughout the mixture','NEW','12-035','12-001',NULL),
(160103,'chemistry','Solvent is the component that is','which determines the physical state of solution','Solvent determines the physical state in which solution exists. \nReference : (NCERT Class 12 Page - 35 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Introduction and Types of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','whose quantity no  effect in solutions','which is responsible for the chemical properties of solution','which determines the physical state of solution','which is present in small quantity.','NEW','12-035','12-001',NULL),
(160104,'chemistry','Which of the following is not an example of a homogenous solution?','Benzene in water','As benzene is insoluble in water it does not form homogenous solution with water. \nReference - (NCERT Class - 12 Page - 36 Table - 2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Introduction and Types of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','Air','Brass','Amalgam','Benzene in water','NEW','12-036','12-002',NULL),
(160105,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct example of solid solution in which the solute is in gas phase?','Hydrogen in palladium','Solution of hydrogen in palladium is an example of solid solution in which solute is a gas. \nReference : (NCERT Class - 12 Page - 36 Table 2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Introduction and Types of Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Copper dissolved in gold','Camphor in Nitrogen gas','Hydrogen in palladium','sulphuric acid in water','NEW','12-036','12-002',NULL),
(160106,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct example of solid solution in which the solute is in liquid phase?','Amalgam of mercury with sodium','Reference : (NCERT Class - 12 Page - 36 Table 2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Introduction and Types of Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Amalgam of mercury with sodium','Chloroform mixed with Nitrogen gas','Glucose dissolved in water','Ethanol dissolved in water','NEW','12-036','12-002',NULL),
(160107,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct example of liquid solution in which the solute is in liquid phase?','Ethanol dissolved in water','Reference : (NCERT Class - 12 Page - 36 Table 2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Introduction and Types of Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Camphor in Nitrogen gas','Ethanol dissolved in water','Amalgam of mercury with sodium','Chloroform mixed with Nitrogen gas','NEW','12-036','12-002',NULL),
(160108,'chemistry','Which of the following is the correct example of gaseous solution in which the solute is in solid phase?','Camphor in Nitrogen gas','Reference : (NCERT Class - 12 Page - 36 Table 2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Introduction and Types of Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Chloroform mixed with Nitrogen gas','Amalgam of mercury with sodium','Camphor in Nitrogen gas','Ethanol dissolved in water','NEW','12-036','12-002',NULL),
(160109,'biology','Biochemical method of digestion happens through','Chemical Reactions','Chemical Reactions between Food particles and Enzymes (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 261; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Chemical Reactions','Grinding','Decomposition','Fragmentation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160110,'biology','What substance present in food cannot be utilised in original form by our body?','Bio-macromolecules','Biomacromolecules in food cannot be utilised by our body in their original form. They have to be broken down and converted into simple substances in the digestive system. This process of conversion of complex food substances to simple absorbable forms is called digestion and is carried out by our digestive system by mechanical and biochemical methods. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Introduction',NULL,'1','fillup','Bio,-macro,mole,cules',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160111,'biology','Molecular physiology became almost synonymous with','Biochemistry and biophysics','Molecular physiology became almost synonymous with biochemistry and biophysics. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 255; Paragraph No.1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Biochemistry and biophysics','Astrology and physiology','Physiology and cytochemistry','Cytogenetics and molecular biology only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160112,'biology','Who discovered organ of corti ?','Alfonso corti','ALFONSO CORTI, the Italian anatomist, was born in 1822. Corti began his scientific career studying the cardiovascular systems of reptiles. Later, he turned his attention to the mammalian auditory system. In 1851, he published a paper describing a structure located on the basilar membrane of the cochlea containing hair cells that convert sound vibrations into nerve impulses, the organ of Corti. He died in the year 1888. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No.1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Alfonso corti','Gregor Corti','Corti Catherine','Pavlov Corti','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160113,'biology','Choose the correct statements. \n(i)Food is one of the basic requirements of all living organisms \n\n(ii)The major components of our food are carbohydrates, proteins and fats.\n\n(iii)Vitamins and minerals are also required in large quantities.\n\n(iv)Food provides energy and organic materials for growth and repair of tissues.','(i), (ii), (iv)','Food is one of the basic requirements of all living organisms. The major components of our food are carbohydrates, proteins and fats. Vitamins and minerals are also required in small quantities. Food provides energy and organic materials for the growth and repair of tissues. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iv)','Both (iii) and (iv)','Only (iii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160114,'biology','Choose the correct statements.\n\n(i)The water we take in, plays an important role in metabolic processes and also prevents dehydration of the body. \n\n(ii)Biomacromolecules in food can be utilised by our body in their original form without digestion.\n\n(iii)Biomacromolecules need not be broken down and converted into simple substances in the digestive system. \n\n(iv)Digestion is carried out by our digestive system by mechanical and biochemical methods.','Both (i) and (iv)','The water we take in plays an important role in metabolic processes and also prevents dehydration of the body. Biomacromolecules in food cannot be utilised by our body in their original form. They have to be broken down and converted into simple substances in the digestive system. This process of conversion of complex food substances to simple absorbable forms is called digestion and is carried out by our digestive system by mechanical and biochemical methods. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (i) and (iv)','Both (iii) and (iv)','Only (iii)','(i), (ii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160201,'biology','Alimentary canal begins with an anterior opening called','Mouth','The alimentary canal starts with the mouth. As the process of digestion begins with the chewing of food and the breakdown of complex molecules into small molecules. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Mouth','Anus','Oesophagus','Intestine','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160202,'biology','Alimentary canal opens out posteriorly through the _______','Anus','The alimentary canal begins with an anterior opening – the mouth, and it opens out posteriorly through the anus. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Large intestine','Anus','Rectum','Appendix','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160203,'biology','Mouth leads to the _____','Oral Cavity','The part next to the mouth in the alimentary canal is Oral Cavity also called the buccal cavity. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Stomach','Oral Cavity','Esophagus','Both b and c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160204,'biology','The oral cavity has a number of ______ and a_______Tongue','Teeth,Muscular','Several teeth and a muscular tongue is present in the oral cavity. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Cells,Vascular','Teeth,Muscular','Tongue,Muscular','villi,bifurcated','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160205,'biology','Each tooth is embedded in a socket of ____ bone','Jaw','All teeth are embedded in the sockets of the jaw bone. Each tooth is embedded in a socket of the jaw bone. This type of attachment is called thecodont. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Jaw','Occipital','Femoral','Frontal','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160206,'biology','All tooth are embedded in a socket of jaw bone. type of attachment is called ________','Thecodont','Each tooths are embedded in a socket of the jaw bone. This type of attachment is called thecodont. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','fillup','Th,ec,od,ont,di',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160207,'biology','Majority of mammals including human being forms two sets of teeth during their life which are?','Deciduous Teeth and Adult Teeth','The two sets of human teeth formed during the lifetime are Deciduous teeth and Adult Tooths  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Deciduous Teeth and Adult Teeth','Permanent Teeth and Adult teeth','Both a and b','Temporary teeth and  Deciduous teeth','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160208,'biology','Temporary milk teeth are also called','Deciduous Teeth','Deciduous Teeth are another name for Temporary milk teeth. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','fillup','Dec,idu,ous Te,e,th',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160209,'biology','Adult teeth are also called as','Permanent Teeth','Adult teeth are also called permanent teeth. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Permanent Teeth','Temporary milk teeth','Deciduous Teeth','Primary teeth','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160210,'biology','Human being forms two sets of teeth during their life this type of dentition is called ________','Diphyodont','Diphyodont is when two types of dentition are formed in a lifetime. While Thecodont refers to the attachment of tooths embedded in the socket of the jaw bone. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Diphyodont','Thecodont','Monophyodont','All the Above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160211,'biology','An adult human has _______','32 permanent teeth','32 permanent teeth are present in adult human beings. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','30 permanent teeth','32 permanent teeth','32 temporary teeth','34 Deciduous Teeth','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160212,'biology','An adult human has 32 permanent teeth which are of _____ different types','4','Four different types of teeth are present in adult human beings having 32 permanent teeth. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','6','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(160213,'biology','32 permanent teeth which are of four different types. This type of dentition is called','Heterodont','Incisors (I), canine (C), premolars (PM) and molars (M) are the four different types of teeth in adult humans. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Thecodont','Heterodont','Diphyodont','Monophyodont','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160214,'biology','Which among the following isn\'t a type of permanent teeth in adult human ?','None of the Above','Incisors (I), canine (C), premolars (PM) and molars (M) are the four different types of teeth in adult humans. 3rd molar and premolars appear only once and have only permanent tooth (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','All Incisors','Canine tooth','All the Premolars','None of the Above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160215,'biology','In the dentition formula \'2102\',indicating diphyodont or deciduous teeth, \nZero represents-\n \n(i)Canine \n(ii)Incisors\n(iii)Molars \n(iv)Premolars','only (iv)','Incisors (I), canine (C), premolars (PM), and molars (M) are the four different types of teeth in adult humans.\nfor the formula \'2102\', it means there are 2 incisors, 1 canine, 0 premolar, and 2 molars. Hence, there is no 2nd set of premolar and they cannot be replaced if injured, they erupt late, and are never replaced by new teeth. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','(iii), (iv)','only (iv)','(i), (ii)','(i), (ii), (iii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160216,'biology','Arrangement of teeth in each half of the upper and lower jaw is in the order of?','I, C, PM, M','Incisors (I), canine (C), premolars (PM) and molars (M) are the four an different types of teeth an in adult humans. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','I, C, M, PM','I, C, PM, M','PM, M, I, C','M, I, C, PM','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160217,'biology','Which one of the following correctly represents adult dental formula?','2123/2123','2123/2123 is the dental formula for Adult humans. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','3131/3121','2133/2123','2123/2123','3133/3133','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160218,'biology','In dental formula 2123 denotes which the following teeth pattern respectively','I,C,PM,M','Dental Formula applies in the order of I, C, PM  and  M. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','M,PM,C,I','I,C,PM,M','PM,C,I,C','I,M,C,PM','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160219,'biology','How many incisors are present in one half of the upper quadrant of  teeth?','2','For one-half of upper or lower teeth, the formula is 2123 in the order of I, C, PM, and M. So incisors are 2 in number. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160220,'biology','The hard chewing surface of the teeth is made up of?','Enamel','The hard chewing surface of the teeth, made up of enamel, helps in the mastication of food. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Gums','Enamel','Odontoclast','Dentine','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160221,'biology','The process of chewing in the mouth is called ?','Mastication','The hard chewing surface of the teeth, made up of enamel, helps in the mastication of food. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Mastication','Regurgitation','Swallowing','Chewing','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160222,'biology','_______ is a freely movable muscular organ','Tongue','The tongue is a muscular organ and it is freely movable. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Tongue','Teeth','Nose','Ear','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160223,'biology','Tongue is attached to the floor of the oral cavity by ______','Frenulum','The tongue is a freely movable muscular organ attached to the floor of the oral cavity by the frenulum. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','fillup','Fre,nu,l,um',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160224,'biology','The upper surface of the tongue has small projections called _____','Papillae','Papillae are the small projections On the Upper surface of the tongue. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Papillae','Frenulum','Gums','Buds','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160225,'biology','Some of the Papillae also bear taste buds','TRUE','The upper surface of the tongue has small projections called papillae, some of which bear taste buds. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160226,'biology','The oral cavity immediately leads into a short ______','Pharynx','The oral cavity leads into a short pharynx which serves as a common passage for food and air. The oesophagus and the trachea (windpipe) open into the pharynx. A canrtilaginous flap called epiglottis prevents the entry of food into the glottis – opening of the windpipe – during swallowing. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 2) 05)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','mcq','Pharynx','Larynx','Oesophagus','Trachea','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160227,'biology','Match the following parts of the diagram (1604)','A-Incisor,B-Canine,C-Premolars,D-Molars','An adult human has 32 permanent teeth which are of four different types (Heterodont\ndentition), namely, incisors (I), canine (C), premolars (PM) and molars\n(M). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Figure 16.2) 06)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Oral Cavity',NULL,'1','match','A,Incisor','B,Canine','C,Premolars','D,Molars','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/160227q.png'),
(160301,'chemistry','18 g of sucrose is dissolved in 162 g of water. Calculate the mass percentage of solution?','10%','We know that mass percentage = (Mass of the component in the solution / Total mass of the solution and total mass of solution) x 100  \nmass of sucrose (solute) + mass of solvent (water) = (18+162)g = 180g \nMass of solute = 18 \nMass percentage =18/180 x 100= 10%. \nReference - (NCERT Class-12 Page - 36 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','18%','20%','10%','15%','NEW','12-036','12-002',NULL),
(151501,'chemistry','Which of the following defects decrease the density?\n \n \n \n (a) Interstitial defect \n \n (b) Vacancy defect\n \n (c) Frenkel defect \n \n (d) Schottky defect','b,d','Vacancy and Schottky defect which lead to decrease the density both are the types of a stoichiometric defect. In case of Frenkel defect and interstitial defect, there is no change in density of substance.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160302,'chemistry','Calculate the mole fraction of ethylene glycol in a solution containing 20% of C₂H₆O₂ by mass.','0.068','we have 100 g of solution (one can start with any amount of solution because the results obtained will be the same). \nSolution will contain 20 g of ethylene glycol and 80 g of water .\nMolar mass of C₂H₆O₂ = (12 × 2) + (1 × 6) + 1(6 × 2) = 62 g mol⁻¹ . \nMoles of C₂H₆O₂ = 20 g/ 62 g mol = 0.322 mol \nMoles of water = 80 g/ 18 g mol = 4.444 mol \n=> X(glycol) =  0.322mol/(0.322 mol + 4.444 mol) = 0.068.\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.932','0.068','4.444','0.322','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160303,'chemistry','Calculate the molarity of a solution containing 5g of NaOH in 450 ml solution.','0.278 mol/L','Moles of NaOH = 5 g/ 40 g mol = 0.125 mol, \nVolume of the solution in litres = 450 mL / 1000 mL-1,\nUsing above derivations Molarity =0.125 mol × 1000 mL /450 mL = 0.278 M = 0.278 mol L⁻¹. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.175 mol/L','0.278 mol/L','0.144 mol/L','0.458 mol/L','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160304,'chemistry','Calculate molality of 2.5 g of ethanoic acid in 75 g of benzene.','0.556 mol/kg','Moles of ethanoic acid =  2.5 g/ 60 g mol = 0.0417 mol, \n\nMass of benzene in kg= 75/1000= 0.075 kg\n\nUsing above derivations \n\nMolality =0.0417/0.075 \n\n= 0.556m\n\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.556 mol/kg','0.666 mol/kg','0.656 mol/kg','0.665 mol/kg','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160305,'chemistry','Concentration terms which are independent of temperature is/are','mole fraction and molality','Mass %, ppm, mole fraction and molality are independent of temperature, whereas molarity is a function of temperature. This is because volume depends on temperature and the mass does not. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','mole fraction and molarity','mole fraction and molality','only molarity','only  molality','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160306,'chemistry','If two bottles A and B contain 1 M and 1 m aqueous solution','B is more concentrated than A','1 M H₂SO₄ means 1 mole H₂SO₄ in 1000 cc of solution whereas, 1 m H₂SO₄ means 1 mole H₂SO₄ in 1000 g of water Total volume of 1 m solution will be greater than 1000 cc due to extra mole of H₂SO₄ . Hence, number of moles per lOO P cc will be less than 1 mole. Thus, 1 M is less concentrated than 1 m.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','A is more concentrated than B','B is more concentrated than A','Concentration of A is equal to concentration of B','It is not possible to compare the concentration','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160307,'chemistry','25.3 g of sodium carbonate,Na₂CO₃ is dissolved in enough water to make 250 ml of solution. if sodium carbonate dissociates completely, molar concentration of sodium ion,  Na⁺ and carbonate ions, CO respectively are (molar mass of Na₂CO₃ =106 mol-1 )','1.910 M and 0.955 M','Molarity of Na₂CO₃ = 25.3/106 x 1000/250 = 0.955M \nAfter dissociation 2  Na⁺ ions will be produced so conc. of [ Na⁺] = 2 x 0.955 = 1.910 M\n Similarly conc. of [CO3]²⁻ will be = 0.955M as it will dissociate into single ion. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','1.90 M and 1.910 M','1.910 M and 0.955 M','0.955 M and 1.910 M','0.977 M and 0.810 M','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160308,'chemistry','How many gram of concentrated nitric acid solution should be used to prepare 250 ml of 2.0 m HNO₃ ? the concentrated acid is 70% HNO₃ .','45.0 g cone. HNO₃','Given molarity of solution = 2 \nVolume of the solution = 250 mL/1000mL = 1/4 L\nMolar mass of HNO₃ = 1+ 14 + 3x 16= 63 g mol-1 \nMolarity = (Weight of HNO₃/ Molecular mass of HNO₃) x volume of solution (in L) Weight of HNO₃ = molarity x molecular mass x volume (in L) = 2 mol/L x 63 g mol x 1/4L = 31.5g It is weight of 100% HNO₃. But the given acid contains 70% of HNO₃ 70% HNO₃ means 70 g HNO₃ is present in l00g solution  31.5g HNO₃ will be present in = 100/70 x 31.5 = 45 gm of solution. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','50.0 g cone. HNO₃','70.0 g cone. HNO₃','90.0 g cone. HNO₃','45.0 g cone. HNO₃','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160309,'chemistry','What is the mole fraction of the solute in a 1m aqueous solution','0.0177','Molality can be written as m = 1000 x n/ N x M .\nSo, 1 = 1000 x n /N x 18 -> n/N = 18/1000 \nWe also know n/n + N = 18/1018 = 0.0177.   \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.77','1.077','0.0017','0.0177','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160310,'chemistry','If the mole fraction of a solute in 1 kg benzene is 0.2 then molality of the solute is','3.2','Let number of moles of solute in solution = x \nMoles of benzene in solution = 1000/78 = 12.82 moles, \nMole fraction of solute = 0.2 = x/(x + 12.82) .\nOn solving x = 3.2 \nMolality = 3.2/1kg weight of solvent = 3.2m. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','3.2','2.3','4.3','3.6','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160311,'chemistry','6.02 x 10²⁰ molecules of urea are present in 100 ml of its solution. the concentration of solution is','0.01 M','Given number of molecules of urea = 6.02 x 10²⁰\nNumber of moles = 6.02  x 10²⁰/ 6.02 x 10²³ = 10⁻³ \nVolume of solution = 100 mL = 0.1L, \nConc. of urea = 10⁻³/0.1 = 10⁻²M = 0.01M. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.02 M','0.01 M','0.001 M','0.1 M','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160312,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason.\n\n(A) molarity of a solution in liquid state changes with temperature. \n\n(R) the volume of a solution changes with change in temperature.','Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A','Mass %, ppm, mole fraction and molality are independent of temperature, whereas molarity is a function of temperature. This is because volume depends on temperature and the mass does not. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 5 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct R is not the correct explanation of A','A is correct R is incorrect','A is incorrect, R is correct.','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160313,'chemistry','4l of 0.02 M aqueous solution of NaCl was diluted by adding 1 l of water. the molarity of the resultant solution is (NCERT exemplar)','0.016','Given M₁ = 0.02M V₁=4L M₂=? V₂=1+4 = 5L so, \nM₁V₁=M₂V₂ => \n0.02 x 4 = M₂ x 5 => M₂ = 0.016M. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.011','0.01','0.012','0.016','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(151502,'chemistry','Frenkel defect is also known as\n \n \n \n (a) stoichiometric defect \n \n (b) dislocation defect\n \n (c) impurity defect \n \n (d) non-stoichiometric defect','a,b','In Frenkel defect, dislocation of cations takes place and there is no change in stoichiometry of the crystal.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160314,'chemistry','Calculate the mass of urea required in making 2.5 kg of 0.25 molal aqueous solution.','37 g','Mass of solvent = 1000 g \nMolar mass of urea = 60 g mol-1 \n0.25 mole of urea = 0.25 x 60 = 15 g \nTotal mass of solution = (1000 + 15)g = 1.015 kg => 1.015 kg of solution contain urea= 15 g  \nso, 2.5 kg of solution will contain urea= 15/1.015 x 2.5  = 37g. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','37 g','32g','35g','39g','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160315,'chemistry','Concentrated nitric acid used in laboratory work is 68% nitric acid by mass in aqueous solution. What should be the molarity of such a sample of the nitric acid, if the density of the solution is 1.504 g ml ?','16.23M','68% HNO₃ means 68 g of nitric acid in 100 g solution. \nDensity of the solution = 1.504 g mL Moles of HNO₃ = 68/63 = 1.079\nSo, Volume of the solution = 100/1.504 = 66.5mL = 0.0665L \nMolarity of the solution = 1.079/0.0665 = 16.23M. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','16.23M','15.23M','15.00M','17.23M','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160316,'chemistry','Which of the following is a increases with increase in temperature.\n\na. Mass %\n\nb. ppm \n\nc. mole fraction \n\nd. molality','none of the above','Mass %, ppm, mole fraction and molality are independent of temperature, whereas molarity is a function of temperature. This is because volume depends on temperature and the mass does not. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','none of the above','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160317,'chemistry','Calculate the molarity of the following solution: 30 g of CO(NO₃)₂. 6H₂O in 4.3 l of solution.','0.023M','Moles of solute = 30/291 = 0.103 (291 molar mass of solute)\nMolarity = no. of moles /vol (L) = 0.103/4.3 = 0.023M. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','0.030M','0.023M','0.032M','0.020M','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160318,'chemistry','Calculate the mass percentage of benzene (C₆H₆) and carbon tetrachloride (CCl₄) if 22 g of benzene is dissolved in 122 g of carbon tetrachloride.','15.28% & 84.72%','Mass percentage of benzene = [22/(22+122)] x 100 = 15.28% \nSimilarly mass percentage carbon tetrachloride = 100-15.28 = 84.72%. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-37)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','84.72% & 15.28%','15% & 85%','15.28% & 84.72%','85% & 15%','NEW','12-037','12-003',NULL),
(160319,'chemistry','Calculate the mole fraction of benzene in solution containing 30% by mass in carbon tetrachloride.','0.458','Take 100g of solution then 30g is benzene and 70g is CCl₄. \nMoles of  CCl₄ = 70/154 = 0.454 mol, moles of benzene = 30/78 = 0.384, \nmole fraction of benzene = 0.384/(0.384 +0.454) = 0.458. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.384','0.454','0.458','0.511','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160320,'chemistry','Calculate molality of Kl. if the density of 20% (mass/mass) aqueous Kl is 1.202 g/ml.','1.506','Let us take 100 gm of solution which has 20 gm of KI and 80 gm of water.\nSo molality = 20/166 x 0.08 = 1.506 m. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.506','1.605','1.56','1.65','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160321,'chemistry','A solution of glucose in water is labelled as 10% w/w, what would be the molality','0.62','10% means 10g of glucose in 100g sol. so, water = 90gm\nMolality = 10 x 1000/90 x 180 = 0.62m. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.65','0.52','0.56','0.62','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160322,'chemistry','How many ml of 0.1 m HCl are required to react completely with 1 g mixture of Na₂CO₃ and NaHCO₃ containing equimolar amounts of both?','157.8','Let the amount of Na₂CO₃ be xg, So the amount of NaHCO₃ will be equal to (1 - x)g ,\nMoles of Na₂CO₃ = Moles of NaHCO₃(Since the mixture is equimolar)\n => x/106 = 1-x/84 => x = 0.558g, \n\nMoles of Na₂CO₃ = 0.00526, Moles of NaHCO₃ = 0.053. \nIt is clear that for 1 mol of Na₂CO₃ 2 mol of HCl is required. Ssimilarly for 1 mol of NaHCO₃ 1 mol of HCl is required.\nSo number of moles required of HCl = 2(0.00526) + 0.0053 = 0.01578 mol.\n \nIt is given that molarity of HCl is 0.1 which means 0.1 mol of HCl in 1L of solution. \nThus required volume : = 0.01578/0.1= 0.1578L=157.8mL. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','158.7','157.8','158','157','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160323,'chemistry','A solution is obtained by mixing 300g of 25% solution and 400 g of 40% solution by mass. calculate the mass percentage of the resulting solution.','33.50%','Solute 1 : 25% of 300 gm => 25/100 x 300 = 75gm, \nSolute 2: 40% of 400 gm = 160gm ,\nSo total amount of solute = 75 + 160 = 235 g. \nMass percentage of solute is : 235/700 x 100 = 33.50%\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-37)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','33.50%','66.50%','25%','40%','NEW','12-037','12-003',NULL),
(160324,'chemistry','we have 100 g of solution (one can start with any amount of solution because the results obtained will be the same). Solution will contain 20 g of ethylene glycol and 80 g of water. Molar mass of C₂H₆O₂ = 12 × 2 + 1 × 6 + 16 × 2 = 62 g mol⁻¹ . Moles of C₂H₆O₂ = 20 g/ 62 g mol = 0.322 mol Moles of water = 80 g/ 18 g mol = 4.444 mol => x(glycol) =  0.322mol/0.322 mol + 4.444 mol = 0.068 Similarly, x(water) 4.444 mol /0.322 mol + 4.444 mol = 0.932 Mole fraction of water can also be Calculated as: 1 – 0.068 = 0.932.','5','Molarity = no. of moles/vol \n=> vol = 1000gm/1.25 =800 mL \n=> M = 92/23 x 0.8L = 5M. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','4','5','2','1','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160325,'chemistry','If the solubility product of CuS is 6 × 10⁻¹⁶, calculate the Maximum molarity of CuS in aqueous solution','2.45 x 10⁻⁸','The dissociation equation of CuS is given by => CuS ------> Cu²⁺ + S²⁻\nSo Kₛₚ = Cu²⁺ x S²⁻ = s x s = s² => s = 2.45 x 10⁻⁸ =M ( Maximum molarity = solubility). \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','2.45 x 10⁻⁸','2.45 x 10⁻⁶','2.45 x 10⁻⁷','2.45 x 10⁻⁴','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160326,'chemistry','Nalorphene (C₁₉H₂₁NO₃), similar to morphine, is used to combat withdrawal symptoms in narcotic users. dose of nalorphene generally given is 1.5 mg. Calculate the mass of 1.5 × 10⁻³ m aqueous solution required for the above dose','3.2','Moles of neoprene = 1.5 × 10⁻³/311 = 4.82 x 10⁻⁶, \nMass of solvent in kg = 4.82 x 10⁻⁶/1.5 × 10⁻³ = 3.2 x 10⁻³ kg = 3.2g. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','3.2','2.3','6.4','4.6','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160327,'chemistry','Calculate the amount of benzoic acid required for preparing 250 ml of 0.15 m solution in methanol.','4.575','Molar mass of benzoic acid = 122u, \nMoles of solute = 0.15 x 250/1000 = 0.0375mol \nRequired mass of benzoic acid = 0.0375 x 122 = 4.575g. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','4.755','4.575','4.557','5.475','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160328,'chemistry','Calculate the mass percentage of aspirin (C₉H₈O₄ ) in acetonitrile (CH₃CN) when 6.5 g of C₉H₈O₄ is dissolved in 450 g of CH₃CN','1.42%','Mass percentage = 6.5/456.5 x 100 = 1.42%.\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-37)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','1.35%','1.45%','1.32%','1.42%','NEW','12-037','12-003',NULL),
(160329,'chemistry','Aerosol are colloids which contain (a) A solid dispersed in a liquid (b) A solid dispersed in a gas (c) A liquid dispersed in a gas (d) A liquid in another liquid	2.013 bar','b, c','Aerosol contains small particles of liquid or solid dispersed in a gas\n(NCERT Class 12 Page - 35 )','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','a, b','b, c','a, c','NEW','12-035','12-001',NULL),
(160330,'chemistry','Kͪ value for Ar(g), CO₂ g), HCHO (g) and CH₄ (g) are 40.39, 1.67, 1.83×10⁻⁵and 0.413 respectively. Arrange these gases in the order of their increasing solubility','Ar < CO₂ < CH₄ < HCHO','Higher the value of Kh , lower is the solubility of gas in the liquid','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ar < CH₄ < CO₂ < HCHO','Ar < CO₂ < CH₄ < HCHO','HCHO < CO₂ < CH₄ < Ar','HCHO < CH₄ < CO₂ < Ar','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160331,'chemistry','Mercury in metal amalgams is an example of','Liquid in Solid','In this mixture, the distributed substance is in the liquid state and the medium in which the dispersed phase is dispersed is in the solid state. The dispersion phase in the case of amalgam is mercury and the dispersion medium is usually a solid metal.\nReference : (NCERT Class 12 Page - 35 )','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','Liquid in Solid','Liquid in Liquid','solid in liquid','liquid in gas','NEW','12-035','12-001',NULL),
(160332,'chemistry','According to Henry law, Solubility is','inversely proportional to K(henry constant)','According To this law, “At constant temperature, the solubility of a gas in a given volume of liquid is directly proportional to the pressure of the gas over the liquid. m∝ P =m = KP or m/p = K (At constant temperature) Here K= Henry’s constant (Function of natural gas) When solubility is expressed as its mole fraction (X) P ∝ X or P = K X','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','is equal to K(henry constant)','is independent of K(henry constant)','inversely proportional to K(henry constant)','directly proportional to K(henry constant)','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160333,'chemistry','How is Normality related to Molarity?','N = Molarity × Number of H⁺ or OH⁻ ion.','Normality can be described as a multiple of molarity. While Molarity refers to the concentration of a compound or ion in a solution, normality refers to the molar concentration only of the acid component or only of the base component of the solution','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','N = Molarity × Number of H⁺ or OH⁻ ion.','N xMolarity = Number of H⁺ or OH⁻ ion.','N = Molarity × Number of H⁺ only','N = Molarity × Number of OH⁻ ion only.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160334,'chemistry','1 Demal solution is','1 Molar > 1 molal.','When one mole of a solute is present in one litre of solution at 0°C it is called one demal solution','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','1 Molar = 1 molal.','1 Molar > 1 molal.','1 Molar + 1 molal.','1 Molar < 1 molal.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160335,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a example of non Ideal solutions','Hexane + heptane','Hexane + heptane is a solution in which solute-solute and solvent-solvent interactions are almost similar to solute solvent interactions (A – B = A – A or B – B interactions) and such solutions satisfy the following requirements: 1. They obey Raoult’s law for all ranges of concentrations and temperature. 2. ΔH (mix) = 03.  ΔV(mix) = 04. (V · P)obs = (V · P)exp. (B · P)obs = (V · P)exp.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','Acetone + carbon disulphide,','Carbon tetrachloride + chloroform or Toluene','Hexane + heptane','Methyl Alcohol + water','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160336,'chemistry','Values of Henry\'s Law Constant for mercury in Water at 298 K','0.413','Reference : (NCERT Class-12 page 42 Table 2.2','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Concentration of Solutions',NULL,'3','mcq','0.413','0.611','0.623','0.428','NEW','12-042','12-008',NULL),
(160337,'biology','Which among the following is Incorrect?','In duodenum no glands are present in submucosa','The sub-mucosal layer is formed of loose connective tissues containing nerves, blood and lymph vessels. In the duodenum, glands are also present in the sub-mucosa. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Paragraph No. 2)  08)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'1','mcq','In duodenum no glands are present in submucosa','The innermost layer lining the lumen of the alimentary canal is the mucosa.','Mucosa layer forms irregular folds (rugae) in stomach','Mucosa is thrown into small finger-like foldings called villi in the small intestine','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160338,'biology','The cells lining the villi produce numerous microscopic projections called','Microvilli','The cells lining the villi produce numerous microscopic projections called microvilli giving a brush border appearance. The innermost layer lining the lumen of the alimentary canal is the mucosa. This layer forms irregular folds (rugae) in the stomach and small finger-like foldings called villi in the small intestine (Figure 16.5). The cells lining the villi produce numerous microscopic projections called microvilli giving a brush border appearance. These modifications increase the surface area enormously. Villi are supplied with a network of capillaries and a large lymph vessel called the lacteal. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Paragraph No. 2)  09)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'1','fillup','mi,cro,vil,li,mac',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160339,'biology','Microvilli giving a brush border appearance in small intestine','TRUE','The cells lining the villi produce numerous microscopic projections called microvilli giving a brush border appearance. The innermost layer lining the lumen of the alimentary canal is the mucosa. This layer forms irregular folds (rugae) in the stomach and small finger-like foldings called villi in the small intestine (Figure 16.5). The cells lining the villi produce numerous microscopic projections called microvilli giving a brush border appearance. These modifications increase the surface area enormously. Villi are supplied with a network of capillaries and a large lymph vessel called the lacteal. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Paragraph No. 2) 09)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151405,'chemistry','A : If a tetrad axis is passed through the unit cell of NaCl and all ions are removed which are touching to tetrad  axis then the formula of NaCl becomes Na₃Cl₄.\n\nR : Only one Na⁺ is removed not the Cl⁻.','Both A and R are false.','If tetrad axis are passed through the unit cell of NaCl two Cl⁻ ions and one Na⁺ will be removed. Formula unit will be\n\nCl⁻ = 8(1/8) + 4(1/2) = 3\n\nNa⁺ = (1/4)12 = 3 \n\ni.e. Na₃Cl₃ → NaCl\nBoth statements are wrong. Refer diagram (ne1515)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160340,'biology','Villi are supplied with \n\n(i)Network of capillaries \n(ii)Large lymph vessel called the lacteal \n(iii)Nephrons','Both (i) and (ii)','The cells lining the villi produce numerous microscopic projections called microvilli giving a brush border appearance. The innermost layer lining the lumen of the alimentary canal is the mucosa. This layer forms irregular folds (rugae) in the stomach and small finger-like foldings called villi in the small intestine (Figure 16.5). The cells lining the villi produce numerous microscopic projections called microvilli giving a brush border appearance. These modifications increase the surface area enormously. Villi are supplied with a network of capillaries and a large lymph vessel called the lacteal. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Paragraph No. 2) 09)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (i) and (ii)','Both (i) and (iii)','Both (ii) and (iii)','only (ii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160341,'biology','Mucosal epithelium has________cells which secrete mucus that help in lubrication','Goblet','The mucosal epithelium has goblet cells which secrete mucus that help in lubrication. Mucosa also forms glands in the stomach (gastric glands) and crypts in between the bases of villi in the intestine (crypts of Lieberkuhn). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 260; Paragraph No. 1) 08)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'1','mcq','Goblet','Casket','Basket','Leaflet','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160342,'biology','Statement I :  Stomach contains mucosa that forms gastric glands \n\nStatement II : Crypts in the mucosa in between the bases of villi in the intestine called as crypts of Lieberkuhn\n\nChoose the correct options regarding two statements:','Both I and II are correct','The mucosal epithelium has goblet cells which secrete mucus that help in lubrication. Mucosa also forms glands in the stomach (gastric glands) and crypts in between the bases of villi in the intestine (crypts of Lieberkuhn). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 260; Paragraph No. 1) 08)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'1','mcq','Only I is correct','Both I and II are correct','Only II is correct','Both I and II are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160343,'biology','Match the following parts of the diagram (1602)','a-Fundus,b-Cardiac,c-Body,d-Pyloric','A muscular sphincter (gastro-oesophageal) regulates the opening of the oesophagus into the stomach. The stomach, located in the upper left portion of the abdominal cavity, has four major parts – a cardiac portion into which the oesophagus opens, a fundic region, a body (main central region) and a pyloric portion which opens into the first part of small intestine  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 260; Figure 16.3) 07)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'1','match','a,Fundus','b,Cardiac','c,Body','d,Pyloric','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/160343q.png'),
(160344,'biology','Match the following parts of the diagram (1603)','a-Serosa,b-Muscularis,c-Submucosa,d-Mucosa','The wall of the alimentary canal from the oesophagus to the rectum possesses four layers namely serosa, muscularis, sub-mucosa and mucosa. Serosa is the outermost layer and is made up of a thin mesothelium (epithelium of visceral organs) with some connective tissues. Muscularis is formed by smooth muscles usually arranged into an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer. An oblique muscle layer may be present in some regions. The submucosal layer is formed of loose connective tissues containing nerves, blood and lymph vessels. In the duodenum, glands are also present in the sub-mucosa. The innermost layer lining the lumen of the alimentary canal is the mucosa. This layer forms irregular folds (rugae) in the stomach and small finger-like foldings called villi in the small intestine  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 261; Figure 16.4)08)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'1','match','a,Serosa','b,Muscularis','c,Submucosa','d,Mucosa','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/160344q.png'),
(160345,'biology','Choose the correct statements \n\n(i)The human digestive system consists of the alimentary canal only. \n\n(ii)The alimentary canal begins with an anterior opening – the anus, and it opens out posteriorly through the mouth. \n\n(iii)The mouth leads to the buccal cavity or oral cavity.\n\n(iv)The oral cavity has a number of teeth and a muscular tongue.','Both (iii) and (iv)','The human digestive system consists of the alimentary canal and the associated glands. The alimentary canal begins with an anterior opening – the mouth, and it opens out posteriorly through the anus. The mouth leads to the buccal cavity or oral cavity. The oral cavity has several teeth and a muscular tongue. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 257; Paragraph No. 2,3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (iii) and (iv)','Only (iii)','Both (ii) and (iii)','(i), (ii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160346,'biology','Choose the wrong statements \n\n(i)Each tooth is embedded in a socket of jaw bone. This type of attachment is called thecodont. \n\n(ii)Majority of mammals including human being forms two sets of teeth during their life, a set of temporary milk or deciduous teeth replaced by a set of permanent or adult teeth. This type of dentition is called diphyodont. \n\n(iii)An adult human has 20 permanent teeth which are of four different types (Heterodont dentition), namely, incisors (I), canine (C), premolars (PM) and molars (M). \n\n(iv)Arrangement of teeth in each half of the upper and lower jaw in the order I, C, PM, M is represented by a dental formula which in human is 2123/2123 .','Only (iii)','Each tooth is embedded in a socket of the jaw bone. This type of attachment is called thecodont. The majority of mammals including human beings form two sets of teeth during their life, a set of temporary milk or deciduous teeth replaced by a set of permanent or adult teeth. This type of dentition is called diphyodont. An adult human has 32 permanent teeth which are of four different types (Heterodont dentition), namely, incisors (I), canine (C), premolars (PM) and molars (M). The arrangement of teeth in each half of the upper and lower jaw in the order I, C, PM, and M is represented by a dental formula which in humans is 2123/2123.','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (iii)','Both (ii) and (iii)','Both (i) and (ii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160347,'biology','Choose the false statements \n\n(i)The hard chewing surface of the teeth, made up of enamel, helps in chewing and mastication of food. \n\n(ii)The tongue is a freely movable muscular organ attached to the roof of the oral cavity by the frenulum. \n\n(iii) The lower surface of the tongue has small projections called papillae, some of which bear taste buds.','Both (ii) and (iii)','The hard chewing surface of the teeth, made up of enamel, helps in the mastication of food. The tongue is a freely movable muscular organ attached to the FLOOR of the oral cavity by the frenulum. The UPPER surface of the tongue has small projections called papillae, some of which bear taste buds. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (ii) and (iii)','Both (i) and (ii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160348,'biology','Choose the correct statements \n\n(i) The oral cavity leads into a long pharynx which serves as a common passage for food and air.  \n\n(ii)The oesophagus and the trachea (wind pipe) open into the pharynx. \n\n(iii)A cartilaginous flap called epiglottis prevents the entry of food into the glottis – opening of the wind pipe – during swallowing. \n\n(iv)The oesophagus is a thin, long tube which extends posteriorly passing through the neck, thorax and diaphragm and leads to a ‘M’ shaped bag called the stomach','Both (ii) and (iii)','The oral cavity leads into a short pharynx which serves as a common passage for food and air. The oesophagus and the trachea (windpipe) open into the pharynx. A cartilaginous flap called epiglottis prevents the entry of food into the glottis – opening of the windpipe – during swallowing. The oesophagus is a thin, long tube which extends posteriorly passing through the neck, thorax and diaphragm and leading to a ‘J’ shaped bag. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 258; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (ii) and (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160349,'biology','Choose the wrong statements  \n\n(i)A cartilagenous sphincter (gastro-oesophageal) regulates the opening of oesophagus into the stomach. \n\n(ii)The stomach, located in the lower left portion of the abdominal cavity, has three major parts. \n\n(iii)The stomach consists of a cardiac portion into which the oesophagus opens. \n\n(iv)Body of the stomach is the main central region of the stomach\n\n(v) Pyloric portion of the stomach opens into the first part of  the small intestine.','Both (i) and (ii)','A MUSCULAR sphincter (gastro-oesophageal) regulates the opening of the oesophagus into the stomach. The stomach, located in the UPPER left portion of the abdominal cavity, has four major parts – a cardiac portion into which the oesophagus opens, a fundic region, a body (main central region) and a pyloric portion which opens into the first part of the small intestine. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (i) and (ii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160350,'biology','Choose the correct statements regarding the small intestine\n\n(i) Small intestine is distinguishable into three regions. \n\n(ii) A ‘J’ shaped duodenum \n\n(iii) A short coiled middle portion jejunum \n\n(iv) A highly coiled ileum.','Both (i) and (iv)','The small intestine is distinguishable into three regions, a ‘C’ shaped duodenum, a long coiled middle portion jejunum and a highly coiled ileum. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (i) and (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (iii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160351,'biology','Choose the correct statements \n\n(i)The opening of the stomach into the duodenum is guarded by the pyloric sphincter. \n\n(ii) Jejunum opens into the large intestine. \n\n(iii) Large intestine consists of caecum, colon and rectum . \n\n(iv)Colon is a small blind sac which hosts some symbiotic micro-organism','Both (i) and (iii)','The opening of the stomach into the duodenum is guarded by the pyloric sphincter.The ileum opens into the large intestine. It consists of the caecum, colon and rectum. The caecum is a small blind sac which hosts some symbiotic micro-organisms. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (i) and (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160352,'biology','Choose the correct statements \n\n(i) A narrow finger-like tubular projection, the vermiform appendix which is a vestigial organ, arises from the colon. \n\n(ii)The colon opens into the caecum. \n\n(iii)The colon is divided into four parts – an ascending, a transverse, descending part and a sigmoid colon. \n\n(iv)The caecum opens into the rectum which opens out through the anus.','Only (iii)','A narrow finger-like tubular projection, the vermiform appendix which is a vestigial organ, arises from the caecum. The caecum opens into the colon. The colon is divided into four parts – an ascending, a transverse, descending part and a sigmoid colon. The descending part opens into the rectum which opens out through the anus.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160353,'biology','Choose the correct statements \n\n(i) The wall of alimentary canal from oesophagus to rectum possesses three layers. \n\n(ii) serosa, muscularis, sub-mucosa and mucosa. are the layers of wall of alimentary canal. \n\n(iii)Serosa is the outermost layer and is made up of a thin mesothelium (epithelium of visceral organs) with some connective tissues. \n\n(iv) Sub mucosa is formed by smooth muscles usually arranged into an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer.','Both (ii) and (iii)','The wall of alimentary canal from oesophagus to rectum possesses four layers namely serosa, muscularis, sub-mucosa and mucosa. Serosa is the outermost layer and is made up of a thin mesothelium (epithelium of visceral organs) with some connective tissues. Muscularis is formed by smooth muscles usually arranged into an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (ii) and (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160354,'biology','Choose the false statements. \n\n(i) Serosa is the innermost layer and is made up of a thin mesothelium (epithelium of visceral organs) with some connective tissues. \n\n(ii)Muscularis is formed by smooth muscles usually arranged into an outer circular and an inner longitudinal layer. An oblique muscle layer may be present in some regions. \n\n(iii) The sub- mucosal layer is formed by dense connective tissues containing nerves, blood and lymph vessels. \n\n(iv)The outermost layer lining the lumen of the alimentary canal is the mucosa.','All of them','Serosa is the outermost layer and is made up of a thin mesothelium (epithelium of visceral organs) with some connective tissues. Muscularis is formed by smooth muscles usually arranged into an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer. An oblique muscle layer may be present in some regions. The sub-mucosal layer is formed of loose connective tissues containing nerves, blood and lymph vessels. In the duodenum, glands are also present in the sub-mucosa. The innermost layer lining the lumen of the alimentary canal is the mucosa. This layer forms irregular folds (rugae) in the stomach and small finger-like foldings called villi in the small intestine (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','All of them','Both (i) and (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','Only (ii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100309,'chemistry','What is the colour of superoxides?','Yellow or orange','The superoxides are yellow or orange in colour. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Yellow','Orange','Colourless','Yellow or orange','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160355,'biology','Choose the statement which is not correct. \n\n(i)In duodenum, glands are also present in sub-mucosa. \n\n(ii) Sub-mucosa layer forms irregular folds (rugae) in the stomach and small finger-like foldings called villi in the small intestine. \n\n(iii)The innermost layer lining the lumen of the alimentary canal is the mucosa. \n\n(iv)Muscularis is formed by smooth muscles.','Only (ii)','Serosa is the outermost layer and is made up of a thin mesothelium (epithelium of visceral organs) with some connective tissues. Muscularis is formed by smooth muscles usually arranged into an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer. An oblique muscle layer may be present in some regions. The sub-mucosal layer is formed of loose connective tissues containing nerves, blood and lymph vessels. In the duodenum, glands are also present in the sub-mucosa. The innermost layer lining the lumen of the alimentary canal is the mucosa. This layer forms irregular folds (rugae) in the stomach and small finger-like foldings called villi in the small intestine (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 259; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (ii)','Both (i) and (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160356,'biology','Choose the correct statement  \n\n(i) Brush bordered modifications in alimentary canal increase the surface area enormously. \n\n(ii)Villi are supplied with a network of capillaries and a large lymph vessel called the lacteal. \n\n(iii)Mucosal epithelium has goblet cells which secrete mucus that help in lubrication. \n\n(iv)Mucosa also forms glands in the stomach (gastric glands) and crypts in between the bases of villi in the intestine (crypts of Lieberkuhn).','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','Microvilli give a brush border appearance. These modifications increase the surface area enormously. Villi are supplied with a network of capillaries and a large lymph vessel called the lacteal. The mucosal epithelium has goblet cells which secrete mucus that help in lubrication. Mucosa also forms glands in the stomach (gastric glands) and crypts in between the bases of villi in the intestine (crypts of Lieberkuhn). All four layers show modifications in different parts of the alimentary canal. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 260; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Understanding the Alimentary Canal',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iv)','(i), (ii), (iv)','Only (ii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160401,'chemistry','Solubility of a substance is its Maximum amount that can be dissolved in a specified amount of solvent at a specified temperature.','Maximum','Solubility of a substance is its Maximum amount that can be dissolved in a specified amount of solvent at a specified temperature. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-39 Paragraph - Last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','Minimum','Maximum','either','both','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160402,'chemistry','Solubility depends on','Nature of solute and solvent','It depends upon the nature of solute and solvent as well as temperature and pressure. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-39 Paragraph - Last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','Nature of solute','Nature of solvent','Nature of solute and solvent','Time given','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160403,'chemistry','If in a nearly saturated solution, the dissolution process is endothermic (∆Sol H > 0), then  with rise in temperature, solubility should','increase','In general, if in a nearly saturated solution, the dissolution process is endothermic (∆sol H > 0), the solubility should increase with rise in temperature and if it is exothermic (∆sol H < 0) the solubility should decrease. These trends are also observed experimentally. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','increase','decrease','equal','may increase or decrease','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160404,'chemistry','If in a nearly saturated solution, the dissolution process is  exothermic (∆Sol H < 0)  then  with rise in temperature, solubility should','decrease','In general, if in a nearly saturated solution, the dissolution process is endothermic (∆sol H > 0), the solubility should increase with rise in temperature and if it is exothermic (∆sol H < 0) the solubility should decrease. These trends are also observed experimentally. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','increase','decrease','equal','may increase or decrease','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160405,'chemistry','A solution in which no more solute can be dissolved at the same temperature and pressure is called a _______ solution','saturated','Such a solution in which no more solute can be dissolved at the same temperature and pressure is called a saturated solution. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','resaturated','saturated','supersaturated','unsaturated','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160406,'chemistry','An _______ solution is one in which more solute can be dissolved at the same temperature.','Unsaturated','An unsaturated solution is one in which more solute can be dissolved at the same temperature. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','Resaturated','Saturated','Supersaturated','Unsaturated','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160407,'chemistry','The solution which is in dynamic equilibrium with undissolved solute is the ______ solution','Saturated','The solution which is in dynamic equilibrium with undissolved solute is the saturated solution. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','Resaturated','Saturated','Supersaturated','Unsaturated','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160408,'chemistry','In ______solution the concentration of solute in such a solution is its solubility.','Saturated','In saturated solution the concentration of solute in such a solution is its solubility.\n Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','Resaturated','Saturated','Supersaturated','Unsaturated','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160409,'chemistry','Pressure does not have any significant effect on solubility of _____ in liquids.','Solids','Pressure does not have any significant effect on solubility of solids in liquids. It is so because solids and liquids are highly incompressible and practically remain unaffected by changes in pressure. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 6)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','Solids','liquids','gases','plasma','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160410,'chemistry','Solubility of gases in liquids is greatly affected by ______','Pressure and temperature','Solubility of gases in liquids is greatly affected by pressure and temperature. The solubility of gases increase with increase of pressure. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','Pressure','Temperature','Volume','Pressure and temperature','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160411,'chemistry','The solubility of gases increase with______ of pressure.','Increase','Solubility of gases in liquids is greatly affected by pressure and temperature. The solubility of gases increase with increase of pressure. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','Increase','decrease','all the time','remain same','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160412,'chemistry','Who was the first to give a quantitative relation between pressure and solubility of a gas in a solvent','Henry','Henry was the first to give a quantitative relation between pressure and solubility of a gas in a solvent. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','Henry','charles','Boyle','Dalton','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160413,'chemistry','The incorrect statement about solubility?','It always increases with increase in pressure','It depends upon the nature of solute and solvent as well as temperature and pressure. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-39 Paragraph - Last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','It depends upon the nature of solute and solvent','It depends upon temperature and pressure','It shows the Maximum amount of solute present in a definite amount of solvent','It always increases with increase in pressure','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160414,'chemistry','Which of the following is incorrect statement','In saturated solution, the solution is in dynamic equilibrium with dissolved solute','In saturated solution, the solution is in dynamic equilibrium with undissolved solute. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','In saturated solution, the solution is in dynamic equilibrium with dissolved solute','Dissolution and crystallisation are opposite processes','When dissolution and crystallisation occur with same rate, a saturated solution is obtained','When rate of dissolution = rate of crystallisation, no more solute can be added to it','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160415,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following \n\nAssertion (A) : polar solute dissolves in polar solvents and nonpolar solute dissolves in non-polar solvents. \n\nReason (R) : like dissolves like. \n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:"','Both A and R are correct, R is the correct explanation of A.','In general, a solute dissolves in a solvent if the intermolecular interactions are similar in the two or we may say like dissolves like. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct, R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are correct, R is not the correct explanation of A','A is correct, R is incorrect.','A is incorrect, R is correct','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160416,'chemistry','In case of which type of solution concentration of solute shows its solubility?','Saturated solution','In saturated solution the concentration of solute in such a solution is its solubility. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','Supersaturated solution','Unsaturated solution','Saturated solution','None of these','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160417,'chemistry','20 g of glucose is dissolved in 50 ml of water and some pressure is applied on the surface of solution formed. it will result in','No effect on solubility','Pressure does not have any significant effect on solubility of solids in liquids. It is so because solids and liquids are highly incompressible and practically remain unaffected by changes in pressure. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 6)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','Increased solubility','decreased solubility','slight increase in solubility','No effect on solubility','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160418,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is true about Henry’s law?','The solubility of a gas in a liquid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of the gas present above the surface of liquid or solution','Henry’s law. The law states that at a constant temperature, the solubility of a gas in a liquid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of the gas present above the surface of liquid or solution. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','The solubility of a gas in a liquid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of the gas present above the surface of liquid or solution','The solubility of solid in a liquid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of the solid present above the surface of liquid or solution','The solubility of a liquid in gas is directly proportional to the partial pressure of liquid present above the surface of gas','The solubility of a gas in solid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of gas present above the surface of solid','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160419,'chemistry','The value of henry’s law constant for argon (Ar), carbon dioxide (CO₂ ), methane (CH₄ ) and formaldehyde (HCHO) are respectively 40.3 k bar, 1.67 k bar, 0.413 K. bar and 1.83 x 10⁻⁵ K. bar at 298 K. the correct order of their solubility is','Ar < CO₂ < CH₄ < HCHO','Higher the value of Henry’s law constant, lower is the solubility of gas in water (or in liquid solvent). \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - Last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','HCHO < CO₂ < CH₄ < Ar','Ar < CH₄ < CO₂ < HCHO','Ar < CO₂ < CH₄ < HCHO','Ar < CO₂ < HCHO < CH₄','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160420,'chemistry','Ratio of O₂ and N₂ in the air is 1:4. find out the ratio of their solubilities in terms of mole fractions of O₂ and N₂ dissolved in water at atmospheric pressure and room temperature. kₕ (O₂) = 4.40x 10⁷torr  kₕ(O₂)=8.80x 10⁷ torr','1:2','pO₂ = 1/5 x 1 = 0.2 bar pN₂= 4/5 x 1 = 0.8 => p= Kₕx => x(O₂):x(N₂) = 0.2 x 8.8. x 10⁷/0.8 x 4.40 x 10⁷ = 1:2. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','2:1','1:2','1:1','1:3','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160421,'chemistry','Soft drinks and soda water bottles are sealed under high pressure','To increase the solubility of CO²','To increase the solubility of CO² in soft drinks and soda water, the bottle is sealed under high pressure. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-42 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','To increase their taste','To increase the solubility of CO²','To decrease temperature of the gas dissolved','To keep them well preserved','NEW','12-042','12-008',NULL),
(160422,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is incorrect?','Due to high pressure, solubility of atmospheric gases in the blood decreases','Underwater divers must cope with high concentrations of dissolved gases while breathing air at high pressure underwater. Increased pressure increases the solubility of atmospheric gases in blood. When the divers come towards surface, the pressure gradually decreases. This releases the dissolved gases and leads to the formation of bubbles of Nitrogen in the blood. This blocks capillaries and creates a medical condition known as bends, which are painful and dangerous to life. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-42 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','Pressure is higher under water than at the surface of a sea','Low surface pressure is responsible for the formation of bends in the body of scuba divers','Bends are actually the bubbles of Nitrogen in the blood','Due to high pressure, solubility of atmospheric gases in the blood decreases','NEW','12-042','12-008',NULL),
(2000044095,'physics','An antenna of length $25\\,m$ is mounted on top of a building of height $75\\,m$. Then the maximum wavelength of the transmission signal is close to :','A','Length of antenna $$\\geq \\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}$$<br>$$\\therefore \\quad \\lambda \\leq 4 \\times 25$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\lambda  \\le 100\\,m.$','Application of EM Waves','Electromagnetic Waves','Hard',NULL,'$100\\,m$','$200\\,m$','$300\\,m$','$400\\,m$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160423,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is true about bends?','Capillaries are blocked and it create medical condition known as bends','Underwater divers must cope with high concentrations of dissolved gases while breathing air at high pressure underwater. Increased pressure increases the solubility of atmospheric gases in blood. When the divers come towards surface, the pressure gradually decreases. This releases the dissolved gases and leads to the formation of bubbles of Nitrogen in the blood. This blocks capillaries and creates a medical condition known as bends, which are painful and dangerous to life. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-42 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','Capillaries are blocked and it create medical condition known as bends','These are safe to life','These are bubbles of Helium in the blood','These are bubble of  Hydrogen in blood','NEW','12-042','12-008',NULL),
(160424,'chemistry','Anoxia is a condition, generally seen in climbers because of','Low partial pressure of Oxygen at high altitudes','At high altitudes the partial pressure of Oxygen is less than that at the ground level. This leads to low concentrations of Oxygen in the blood and tissues of people living at high altitudes or climbers. Low blood Oxygen causes climbers to become weak and unable to think clearly, symptoms of a condition known as anoxia. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-43 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','Low partial pressure of Oxygen at high altitudes','High partial pressure of Oxygen at high altitudes','High concentration of air at high altitudes','High concentration of Oxygen at high altitudes','NEW','12-043','12-009','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/160424q.png'),
(160425,'chemistry','What is the composition of gases in the tanks used by the scuba divers?','11.7% Helium 56.2% Nitrogen 32.1% Oxygen','To avoid bends, as well as, the toxic effects of high concentrations of Nitrogen in the blood, the tanks used by scuba divers are filled with air diluted with Helium (11.7% Helium, 56.2% Nitrogen and 32.1% Oxygen). \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-43 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','5% Helium 56.2% Nitrogen 20% Oxygen','1% Helium 56.2% Nitrogen 28% Oxygen','11.7% Helium 56.2% Nitrogen 20.1% Oxygen','11.7% Helium 56.2% Nitrogen 32.1% Oxygen','NEW','12-043','12-009',NULL),
(160426,'chemistry','What does the slope of the line represent in the given diagram(n1601)','Henry’s law constant','The straight line represents constant thus it is Henry\'s constant. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'3','mcq','Partial pressure of the gas in vapour phase (p)','Mole fraction of gas in the solution (%)','Henry’s law constant','General gas law','NEW','12-041','12-007','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/160426q.png'),
(160427,'chemistry','On dissolving sugar in water at room temperature, solution feels cool to touch. under which of the following cases dissolution of sugar will be most rapid?','Powdered sugar in hot water','Dissolution of sugar in water will be most rapid, when powdered sugar is dissolved in hot water because powdered form can easily insert in the vacancies of liquid particles. Further dissolution of sugar in water is an endothermic process (as cooling is produced). Hence, high temperature will favour the dissolution of sugar in water. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-43 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','Sugar crystals in cold water','Sugar crystals in hot water','Powdered sugar in cold water','Powdered sugar in hot water','NEW','12-043','12-009',NULL),
(160428,'chemistry','Maximum amount of a solid solute that can be dissolved in a specified amount of a given liquid solvent does not depend upon','Pressure','Maximum amount of solid that can be dissolved in a specified amount of a given solvent does not depend upon pressure. This is because solid and liquid are highly incompressible and practically remain unaffected by change in pressure. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-40 Paragraph - 6)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'2','mcq','Temperature','Nature of solute','Pressure','Nature of solvent','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(160429,'chemistry','Henry’s law constant for the solubility of methane in benzene at 298 k is 4.27 x 10⁵ mm Hg. calculate the solubility of methane in benzene at 298 k under 760 mmHg.','1.78x 10⁻³','From Henry’s law p = Kₕx => x = p/Kₕ => 760 mm Hg / 4.27 x 10⁵ = 1.78 x 10⁻³. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-41 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Solubility',NULL,'1','mcq','1.78x 10⁻³','1.78x 10⁻²','0.178','1.78','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(160501,'chemistry','Which of the following is not the characteristic of solutions of liquids and solids in liquid?','Both solute and solvent are non-volatile','solutions of liquids and solids in a liquid. Such solutions may contain one or more volatile components. Generally, the liquid solvent is volatile. The solute may or may not be volatile. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-43 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'2','mcq','These may contain one or more than one volatile component','Generally, the liquid solvent is volatile','The solute may or may not be volatile','Both solute and solvent are non-volatile','NEW','12-043','12-009',NULL),
(160502,'chemistry','When a binary solution of two volatile liquids is taken in a closed vessel, then','In the closed vessel, both the components would evaporate and an equilibrium would be established between vapour phase and liquid phase','Let us consider a binary solution of two volatile liquids and denote the two components as 1 and 2. When taken in a closed vessel, both the components would evaporate and eventually an equilibrium would be established between vapour phase and the liquid phase. Let the total vapour pressure at this stage be ptotal and p₁ and p₂ be the partial vapour pressures of the two components 1 and 2 respectively. These partial pressures are related to the mole fractions x₁ and x₂ of the two components 1 and 2 respectively. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-43 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'2','mcq','In the closed vessel, both the components would evaporate and an equilibrium would be established between vapour phase and liquid phase','In the closed vessel, both the components would evaporate but equilibrium would not be established between vapour phase and liquid phase','In the closed vessel, none of components would evaporate and no equilibrium would be established between vapour phase and liquid phase','In closed vessel, more volatile of the two will evaporate','NEW','12-043','12-009',NULL),
(160503,'chemistry','The total pressure (ptotal ) over the solution phase in the container will be the sum of the partial pressures of the components of the solution. this statement belongs to...','Dalton’s law of partial pressure','According to Dalton’s law of partial pressures, the total pressure ( ptotal ) over the solution phase in the container will be the sum of the partial pressures of the components of the solution. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-44 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'1','mcq','Raoult’s law','Henry’s law','Dalton’s law of partial pressure','General Gas law','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(160504,'chemistry','What does point A signifies in the diagram(n1602)','Vapour pressure of pure solvent','Vapour pressure of pure solvent. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-44 Paragraph - 6)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'3','mcq','Vapour pressure of solute','Vapour pressure of pure solvent','Vapour pressure of solution','Depression in vapour pressure','NEW','12-044','12-010','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/160504q.png'),
(160505,'chemistry','One component of a solution follows Raoult’s law over the entire range 0 < x1 <1. the second component must follow Raoult’s law in the range when x2 is','1>x2>0','Depending on the vapour pressures of the pure components 1 and 2, total vapour pressure over the solution decreases or increases with the increase of the mole fraction of component 1.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-44 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'1','mcq','Close to zero','close to 1','1>x2>0','0.5>x2>0','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(160506,'chemistry','Vapour pressure of chloroform (CHCl₃ ) and dichloromethane (CH₂Cl₂ ) at 298 K are 200 mm Hg and 415 mm Hg respectively. calculate the vapour pressure of the solution prepared by mixing 25.5 g of CHCl₃ and 40 g of CH₂Cl₂.','347.9 mm Hg','Molar mass of CH₂Cl₂ = 12×1+1×2+ 35.5×2= 85 g mol⁻¹ Molar mass of CHCl₃ = 12×1+1×1+ 35.5×3= 119.5 g mol-1 Moles of CH₂Cl₂ = 40 g/ 85 g mol⁻¹  = 0.47 mol Moles of CHCl₃ = 1 25.5 g/ 119.5 g mol⁻¹  = 0.213 mol Total number of moles = 0.47 + 0.213 = 0.683 mol CH₂Cl₂  x = 0.47 mol/0.683 mol = 0.688 CHCl₃ x = 1.00 – 0.688 = 0.312, ptotal = p⁰₁ + (p⁰₂ – p⁰₁ ) x₂ = 200 + (415 – 200) × 0.688 = 200 + 147.9 = 347.9 mm Hg','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'2','mcq','347.9 mm Hg','355.1 mmHg','340.7 mm Hg','488 mm Hg','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(160507,'chemistry','At equilibrium vapour phase will be always rich in the component which is  ______','More Volatile','At equilibrium, vapour phase will be always rich in the component which is more volatile. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-45 Paragraph - Last)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'1','mcq','More Volatile','Less Volatile','Volatility has no effect','More quantity','NEW','12-045','12-011',NULL),
(160508,'chemistry','Which law/laws state that the partial pressure of the volatile component or gas is directly proportional to its mole fraction in solution?','Henry’s law and Raoult’s law','If we compare the equations for Raoult’s law and Henry’s law, it can be seen that the partial pressure of the volatile component or gas is directly proportional to its mole fraction in solution. Only the proportionality constant KH differs from p1 . Thus, Raoult’s law becomes a special case of Henry’s law in which KH becomes equal to p1. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-46 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'1','mcq','Raoult’s law and Dalton’s law of partial pressure','Henry’s law and Dalton’s law of partial pressure','Dalton’s law of partial pressure and General gas law','Henry’s law and Raoult’s law','NEW','12-046','12-012',NULL),
(160509,'chemistry','Raoult’s law becomes a special case of Henry’s law when?','Kₕ = p₁','If we compare the equations for Raoult’s law and Henry’s law, it can be seen that the partial pressure of the volatile component or gas is directly proportional to its mole fraction in solution. Only the proportionality constant Kₕ differs from p₁ . Thus, Raoult’s law becomes a special case of Henry’s law in which Kₕ becomes equal to p₁. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-46 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'1','mcq','Kₕ = p₁','Kₕ > p₁','p₁> Kₕ','Not possible','NEW','12-046','12-012',NULL),
(160510,'chemistry','Interpret the correct statement for the following diagram(n1603)','Vapour pressure of the solution in beaker A is more than in the beaker B','Decrease in the vapour pressure of the solvent on account of the presence of solute in the solvent (a) evaporation of the molecules of the solvent from its surface is denoted by , (b) in a solution, solute particles have been denoted by and they also occupy part of the surface area. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-46 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'3','mcq','Vapour pressure of the solution in beaker A is more than in the beaker B','Vapour pressure of the solution in beaker A is less than in the beaker B','Vapour pressure of the solution in both the beakers is same','it is not possible to determine','NEW','12-046','12-012','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/160510q.png'),
(160511,'chemistry','Decrease in the vapour pressure of the solvent depends on','Quantity of non-volatile solute','The decrease in the vapour pressure of solvent depends on the quantity of non-volatile solute present in the solution, irrespective of its nature.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-46 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'1','mcq','Nature of non-volatile solute','Quantity of non-volatile solute','Nature of solution','Quantity of solution','NEW','12-046','12-012',NULL),
(160512,'chemistry','If a solution obeys Raoult\'s law for all concentrations, its vapour pressure would vary ______ from zero to the vapour pressure of the pure solvent.','Linearly','If a solution obeys Raoult\'s law for all concentrations, its vapour pressure would vary linearly from zero to the vapour pressure of the pure solvent. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'1','mcq','Linearly','Straight','Irregular','Zig Zag','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160513,'chemistry','According to raoult’s law, the proportionality constant is equal to the _______','Vapour pressure of pure solvent','Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'1','mcq','Vapour pressure of pure solvent','Vapour pressure of pure solute','Vapour pressure of pure solution','Do not depend on Vapour pressure','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160514,'chemistry','The vapour pressure of pure liquids A and B are 450 and 700 mm Hg respectively, at 350 K . Find out the composition of the liquid mixture if total vapour pressure is 600 mm Hg.','Xₐ = 0.4 & Xᵦ = 0.6','Let the composition of liquid A (mole fraction) be Xₐ & Xᵦ = 1-Xₐ, \nUsing Raoult’s law 600 = (450 x Xₐ) + 700 (1-Xₐ) => 600-700 = 450Xₐ-700Xₐ=> Xₐ = 0.4 & Xᵦ = 0.6. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-44 Paragraph - 6)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'2','mcq','Xₐ = 0.4 & Xᵦ = 0.6','Xₐ = 0.3 & Xb = 0.7','Xₐ = 0.5 & Xᵦ = 0.5','Xₐ = 0.2 & Xᵦ = 0.8','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(160515,'chemistry','Heptane and Octane form an ideal solution. at 373 K, the vapour pressures of the two liquid components are 105.2 KPa and 46.8 KPa respectively. what will be the vapour pressure of a mixture of 26.0 g of heptane and 35 g of Octane?','73.43','Moles of heptane = 26/100 = 0.26 mol, moles of Octane = 35/114 = 0.31, Mole fraction of heptane = 0.456 and mole fraction of Octane = 0.544, vapour pressure of heptane = 0.456x105.2= 47.97KPa & vapour pressure of Octane = 0.544x46.8=25.46KPa,So total pressure of solution = 47.97 + 25.46 = 73.43 KPa. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page 47 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'2','mcq','73.43','43.73','47.97','46.8','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160516,'chemistry','The vapour pressure of water is 12.3 KPa at 300 K. calculate vapour pressure of 1 molal solution of a non-volatile solute in it.','12','Moles in 1000g of water = 55.55 mol,Mole fraction of solute = 1/1+55.55 = 0.0177, x = pᵒ𝓌-p/pᵒ𝓌 = 12.3-p/12.3 = 0.0177 => p =12.083KPa. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-46 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'2','mcq','11.7','13','11','12','NEW','12-046','12-012',NULL),
(160517,'chemistry','Calculate the mass of a non-volatile solute (molar mass 40 g/mol) which should be dissolved in 114 g Octane to reduce its vapour pressure to 80%','10','After adding solute to Octane, the vapour pressure becomes = 80/100 x p⁰ = 0.8p⁰, moles of Octane = 114/114 = 1, Using Raoult\'s law p⁰-p/p⁰=x => p⁰-0.8p⁰/p⁰=w/40/w/40+1 =w=10g. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-46 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'2','mcq','12','10','20','15','NEW','12-046','12-012',NULL),
(160518,'chemistry','A solution containing 30 g of non-volatile solute exactly in 90 g of water has a vapour pressure of 2.8 KPa at 298 K. further, 18 g of water is then added to the solution and the new vapour pressure becomes 2.9 KPa at 298 K. Calculate vapour pressure of water. (m=23)','3.53','p⁰/2.8 = 5M+30/5M => p⁰/2.8 = 5(23)+30/5(23) = 3.53KPa. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-46 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'1','mcq','3.53','2.32','4.54','1.23','NEW','12-046','12-012',NULL),
(160519,'chemistry','Vapour pressure of water at 293 K is 17.535 mm Hg. calculate the vapour pressure of water at 293 K when 25 g of glucose is dissolved in 450 g of water.','17.44','Moles of glucose = 25/180 = 0.139 mol, moles of water = 450/18 = 25 mol, Now p⁰-p/p⁰ = n₂/n₂ + n₁ => 17.535-p/17.535 = 0.139/0.139+25 => p=17.44 mm Hg. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-46 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'2','mcq','25','13.2','17.44','18','NEW','12-046','12-012',NULL),
(160520,'chemistry','100 g of liquid A (molar mass 140 g/mol) was dissolved in 1000 g of liquid B (molar mass 180 g/mol). The vapour pressure of pure liquid B was found to be 500 torr. Calculate the vapour pressure of pure liquid A in the solution if the total vapour pressure of the solution is 475 torr','280.7','number of moles of liquid A = 100/140 = 0.714 and moles of liquid B = 1000/180 = 5.556, Mole fraction of A (xₐ) = 0.714/0.714 + 5.556 = 0.114 mole fraction of B (xᵦ) = 5.556/5.556 + 0.714 =0.866, Pₜₒₜₐₗ = P⁰ₐxₐ +P⁰ᵦxᵦ => 475 = P⁰ₐ x 0.114 + 500 x 0.866 => P⁰ₐ = 280.7 torr. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-44 Paragraph - 6)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Vapour pressure',NULL,'2','mcq','280.7','282','287','277','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(160521,'biology','The intestinal mucosal epithelium has _____ cells which secrete mucus.','Goblet','The intestinal mucosal epithelium has goblet cells which secrete mucus. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 5)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'1','mcq','Goblet','Casket','Gauntlet','Bracelet','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160522,'biology','The secretions of the brush border cells of the mucosa along with the secretions of the goblet cells constitute \n(i)Intestinal juice \n(ii)Succus entericus \n(iii)Pancreatic juice  \n(iv)Bile juice','Both (i) and (ii)','The secretions of the brush border cells of the mucosa along with the secretions of the goblet cells constitute the intestinal juice or succus entericus. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 5)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (i) and (ii)','Both (i) and (iii)','Both (ii) and (iii)','Both (iii) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160523,'biology','_______juice contains a variety of enzymes like disaccharidases (e.g., maltase), dipeptidases, lipases, nucleosidases','Intestinal','Intestinal juice contains a variety of enzymes like disaccharidases (e.g., maltase), dipeptidases, lipases, nucleosidases, etc. The mucus along with the bicarbonates from the pancreas protects the intestinal mucosa from acid as well as provides an alkaline medium (pH 7.8) for enzymatic activities. Submucosal glands (Brunner’s glands) also help in this. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 5)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'1','fillup','Inte,sti,nal',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160524,'biology','Carbohydrates in the chyme are hydrolysed by pancreatic ______ into disaccharides','Amylase','Carbohydrates in the chyme are hydrolysed by pancreatic amylase into disaccharides. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 263; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'1','mcq','Trypsin','Amylase','HCL','Chymotrypsinogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160525,'biology','Fats are broken down by _______ with the help of bile into di-and monoglycerides.','Lipases','Fats are broken down by lipases with the help of bile into di-and monoglycerides. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 263; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'1','mcq','Amylase','Chymotrypsin','Lipases','Trypsin','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160526,'biology','Nucleases in the pancreatic juice acts on nucleic acids to form \n(i)Nucleotides \n(ii)Nucleosides \n(iii)RNA \n(iv)DNA','Both (i) and (ii)','Nucleases in the pancreatic juice act on nucleic acids to form nucleotides and nucleosides. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 263; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (i) and (ii)','Both (i) and (iii)','Both (ii) and (iii)','Both (iii) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160527,'biology','Functions of large intestine\n\n(i)Absorption of some water, minerals and certain drugs \n\n(ii) Secretion of mucus which helps in adhering the waste (undigested) particles together and lubricating it for an easy passage \n\n(iii)Secretion of enzymes amylase \n\n(iv)Secretion of enzyme pepsin','Both (i) and (ii)','No significant digestive activity occurs in the large intestine. The\nfunctions of the large intestine are:\n(i) absorption of some water, minerals and certain drugs;\n(ii) secretion of mucus which helps in adhering the waste (undigested)\nparticles together and lubricate it for easy passage.\nThe undigested, unabsorbed substances called faeces enter the caecum\nof the large intestine through the ileocecal valve, which prevents the backflow of\nthe faecal matter. It is temporarily stored in the rectum till defecation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 264; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (i) and (ii)','Both (i) and (iii)','Both (ii) and (iii)','Both (iii) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160528,'biology','Which of the following is incorrect regarding feacal matter?','Renin is involved in protein digestion','The undigested, unabsorbed substances called faeces enter the caecum of the large intestine through the ileocecal valve, which prevents the backflow of the faecal matter. It is temporarily stored in the rectum till defecation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 264; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'1','mcq','The undigested, unabsorbed substances called faeces','Faeces enters into the caecum of the large intestine through ileocecal valve,','Ileocecal valve  prevents the backflow of the faecal matter','Renin is involved in protein digestion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151406,'chemistry','A : A particle at the corner of CCP unit cell has  1/8th of its contribution to the unit cell.\n\nR : In any space lattice, the corner of the unit cell is always shared by the eight unit cell.','A is true but R is false.','Assertion is true but reason is wrong because unit cell is always shared by eight unit cell only for cubic crystal not for all space lattice','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160529,'biology','The activities of the gastrointestinal tract are under the control of? \n(i)Neural control \n(ii)Hormonal control \n(iii)Respiration \n(iv)Vascular','Both (i) and (ii)','The activities of the gastrointestinal tract are under neural and hormonal control for proper coordination of different parts. The sight, smell and/or the presence of food in the oral cavity can stimulate the secretion of saliva. Gastric and intestinal secretions are also, similarly, stimulated by neural signals. The muscular activities of different parts of the alimentary canal can also be moderated by neural mechanisms, both local and through CNS. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 264; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (i) and (ii)','Both (i) and (iii)','Both (ii) and (iii)','Both (iii) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160530,'biology','Hormonal control of the secretion of digestive juices is carried out by local hormones produced by the \n(i)Gastric mucosa \n(ii)Intestinal mucosa \n(iii)Submucosal \n(iv) serosa','Both (i) and (ii)','Hormonal control of the secretion of digestive juices is carried out by local hormones produced by the gastric and intestinal mucosa. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 264; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (i) and (ii)','Both (i) and (iii)','Both (ii) and (iii)','Both (iii) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160531,'biology','Na+,K+,CL-,HCO3- are the _________ present in saliva','Electrolytes','Na+, K+ etc are electrolytes present in saliva. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 261; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'2','mcq','Electrolytes','Enzymes','Amylases','Lysozymes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160532,'biology','The pH of salivary amylase is ________  and  _________','6.8  and  Nearly neutral','The pH of saliva is nearly neutral and equal to 6.8. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'2','mcq','9.2  and  Basic','1.8  and  Acidic','7.1  and  Basic','6.8  and  Nearly neutral','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160533,'biology','Saliva acts as an antibacterial agent due to the presence of','Lysozyme','Saliva is composed of a variety of electrolytes, Lysozyme which is an antibacterial agent and includes sodium, potassiumum, calcium, magnesium, bicarbonate, and phosphates. Also found in saliva are immunoglobulins, proteins, enzymes, mucins, and nitrogenous products, such as urea and ammonia. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'2','mcq','Enzymes','Lysozyme','Electrolytes','Amylase','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160534,'biology','Peristalsis is','Wave of muscular contraction','Peristalsis is a series of wavelike muscle contractions that moves food to different processing stations in the digestive tract. The process of peristalsis begins in the oesophagus when a bolus of food is swallowed. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 261; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'2','mcq','Wave of muscular expansion','Wave of muscular elongation','Wave of muscular contraction','Wave of movement','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160535,'biology','The peristalsis helps in movement of bolus in the oesophagus. The process is______','Involuntary','Peristalsis is an involuntary process. But swallowing is a voluntary process. Mucus in saliva helps in lubricating and adhering the masticated food particles into a bolus. The bolus is then conveyed into the pharynx and then into the oesophagus by swallowing or deglutition. The bolus further passes down through the oesophagus by successive waves of muscular contractions called peristalsis. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'2','mcq','Voluntary','Involuntary','Skeletal','Depends on size of bolus','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160536,'biology','Bilirubin and biliverdin are','Bile Pigments','The two most important bile pigments are bilirubin, which is orange or yellow, and its oxidized form biliverdin, which is green. Mixed with the intestinal contents, they give a brown colour to the faeces. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'2','mcq','Enzymes','Bile Pigments','Bile salts','Phospholipids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160537,'biology','Match the following','Trypsin-Proteins,Pancreatic Amylase-Carbohydrates,Lipase-Fats','Trypsin acts on proteins to form peptones and dipeptides. Pancreatic amylase acts on starch to form disaccharides like maltase. Lipase acts on Fats to form monoglycerides. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 263; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','match','Trypsin,proteins','Pancreatic Amylase,Carbohydrates','Lipase,Fats',NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160538,'biology','Choose the statement that are not correct \n\n(i) The process of digestion is accomplished by mechanical and chemical processes. \n\n(ii)The buccal cavity performs two major functions, mastication of food and facilitation of swallowing. \n\n(iii)The teeth with the help of HCl masticate and mix up the food thoroughly. \n\n(iv) Mucus in saliva helps in lubricating and adhering the masticated food particles into a bolus.','Only (iii)','The process of digestion is accomplished by mechanical and chemical processes. The buccal cavity performs two major functions, mastication of food and facilitation of swallowing. The teeth and the tongue with the help of saliva masticate and mix up the food thoroughly. Mucus in saliva helps in lubricating and adhering the masticated food particles into a bolus. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 261; Paragraph No. 3,4)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (i) and (ii)','Only (iii)','(i), (iii), (iv)','Both (ii) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160539,'biology','Choose the false statement/(s)\n\n(i) The bolus is conveyed into the larynx and then into the oesophagus by swallowing or deglutition \n\n(ii)The bolus passes down the oesophagus by successive waves of muscular contractions called peristalsis. \n\n(iii)The gastro-oesophageal sphincter controls the passage of food into the stomach.\n\n(iv)The saliva secreted into the oral cavity contains electrolytes and enzymes, salivary amylase and lysozyme.','Only (i)','Mucus in saliva helps in lubricating and adhering the masticated food particles into a bolus. The bolus is then conveyed into the pharynx and then into the oesophagus by swallowing or deglutition. The bolus further passes down through the oesophagus by successive waves of muscular contractions called peristalsis. The gastro-oesophageal sphincter controls the passage of food into the stomach. The saliva secreted into the oral cavity contains electrolytes and enzymes, salivary amylase and lysozyme. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 261; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (ii)','(i), (iii), (iv)','Only (i)','Both (ii) and (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151503,'chemistry','In which of the following arrangements octahedral voids are formed?\n \n \n \n (a) hcp \n \n (b) bcc \n \n (c) simple cubic \n \n (d) fcc','a,d','In hcp and fcc arrangement, octahedral voids are formed. In fcc, the octahedral voids are observed at edge and centre of cube while in bcc and simple cubic, no any octahedral voids are observed. In bcc, cubic voids formed.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160540,'biology','Choose the statements that are not true  \n\n(i)The chemical process of digestion is initiated in the oral cavity by the hydrolytic action of the carbohydrate splitting enzyme. \n\n(ii) Starch is hydrolysed by salivary amylase (optimum pH 6.8) into a disaccharide – sucrose. \n\n(iii) Lysozyme present in saliva acts as an antibacterial agent that prevents infections. \n\n(iv)About 70 % of starch is hydrolysed by salivary amylase (optimum pH 6.8) into a disaccharide – sucrose','Both (ii) and (iv)','The chemical process of digestion is initiated in the oral cavity by the hydrolytic action of the carbohydrate-splitting enzyme, the salivary amylase. About 30 % of starch is hydrolysed here by this enzyme (optimum pH 6.8) into a disaccharide – maltose. Lysozyme present in saliva acts as an antibacterial agent that prevents infections. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 261,262; Paragraph No. 4,1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (ii) and (iv)','Both (i) and (ii)','Only (iii)','(i), (iii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160541,'biology','Choose the false statements \n\n(i) The sub-mucosa of stomach has gastric glands. \n\n(ii)Gastric glands have three major types of cells. \n\n(iii)Mucus neck cells which secrete mucus \n\n(iv) parietal or chief cells secrete the proenzyme pepsinogen.','Both (i) and (iv)','The mucosa of stomach has gastric glands. Gastric glands have three major types of cells namely -\n(i) mucus neck cells which secrete mucus;\n(ii) peptic or chief cells which secrete the proenzyme pepsinogen; and\n(iii) parietal or oxyntic cells which secrete HCl and intrinsic factor (factor essential for the absorption of vitamin B12). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (i) and (iv)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (iii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160542,'biology','Choose the correct statements \n\n(i) The stomach stores the food for 7-8 hours. \n\n(ii)The food mixes thoroughly with the acidic gastric juice of the stomach by the churning movements of its muscular wall and is called the chyme. \n\n(iii)The proenzyme pepsinogen, on exposure to hydrochloric acid gets converted into the active enzyme pepsin, the proteolytic enzyme of the stomach. \n\n(iv)Pepsin converts proteins into proteoses and peptones (peptides).','(ii), (iii), (iv)','The stomach stores the food for 4-5 hours. The food mixes thoroughly with the acidic gastric juice of the stomach by the churning movements of its muscular wall and is called the chyme. The proenzyme pepsinogen, on exposure to hydrochloric acid gets converted into the active enzyme pepsin, the proteolytic enzyme of the stomach. Pepsin converts proteins into proteoses and peptones (peptides).(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (i) and (iv)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (iii), (iv)','Both (i) and (iii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160543,'biology','Choose the correct statements \n\n(i) The mucus and bicarbonates present in the gastric juice play an important role in lubrication and protection of the mucosal epithelium from excoriation by the highly concentrated hydrochloric acid \n\n(ii)HCl provides the acidic pH (pH 2.8) optimal for pepsins. \n\n(iii)Rennin is a proteolytic enzyme found in gastric juice of infants  \n\n(iv)Rennin helps in the digestion of milk proteins','(i), (iii), (iv)','The mucus and bicarbonates present in gastric juice play an important role in lubrication and protection of the mucosal epithelium from excoriation by highly concentrated hydrochloric acid. HCl provides the acidic pH (pH 1.8) optimal for pepsins. Rennin is a proteolytic enzyme found in the gastric juice of infants which helps in the digestion of milk proteins. Small amounts of lipases are also secreted by gastric glands. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (i) and (iii)','Both (i) and (iv)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (iii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160544,'biology','Choose the false statements  \n\n(i) Single type of movement  generated by the muscularis layer of the small intestine. \n\n(ii)The movements help in a thorough mixing up of the food with various secretions in the intestine and thereby facilitate digestion. \n\n(iii)The bile, pancreatic juice and the intestinal juice are the secretions released into the stomach. \n\n(iv)Pancreatic juice and bile are released through the hepato-pancreatic duct.','Both (i) and (iii)','Various types of movements are generated by the muscularis layer of the small intestine. These movements help in a thorough mixing up of the food with various secretions in the intestine and thereby facilitate digestion. The bile, pancreatic juice and intestinal juice are the secretions released into the small intestine. Pancreatic juice and bile are released through the hepato-pancreatic duct. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (i) and (iii)','Both (i) and (iv)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (iii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160545,'biology','How many of given enzymes are present in pancreatic juice.      \nsalivary amylase, Lysozyme , pepsinogen,  pepsin, Rennin, lipases, trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, enterokinase, procarboxypeptidases, amylases, nucleases.','6','The pancreatic juice contains inactive enzymes – trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidases, amylases, lipases and nucleases. Trypsinogen is activated by an enzyme, enterokinase, secreted by the intestinal mucosa into active trypsin, which in turn activates the other enzymes in the pancreatic juice.','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','6','4','5','7','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160546,'biology','How many of the following are components of bile juice ?    \nsalivary amylase, Lysozyme , phospholipids,  pepsinogen,  pepsin, Rennin, bile pigments,  lipases, trypsinogen, bilirubin,  bili-verdin, chymotrypsinogen, enterokinase, procarboxypeptidases, amylases, bile salts, nucleases, cholesterol.','6','The bile released into the duodenum contains bile pigments (bilirubin and biliverdin), bile salts, cholesterol and phospholipids but no enzymes. Bile helps in the emulsification of fats, i.e., breaking down the fats into very small micelles. Bile also activates lipases. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','6','4','5','7','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160547,'biology','How many of given enzymes are present in bile juice?\nsalivary amylase, Lysozyme , phospholipids,  pepsinogen,  pepsin, Rennin, bile pigments,  lipases, trypsinogen, bilirubin,  bili-verdin, chymotrypsinogen, enterokinase, procarboxypeptidases, amylases, bile salts, nucleases, cholesterol.','Zero','The bile released into the duodenum contains bile pigments (bilirubin and biliverdin), bile salts, cholesterol and phospholipids but no enzymes. Bile helps in the emulsification of fats, i.e., breaking down the fats into very small micelles. Bile also activates lipases. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Zero','One','Two','Six','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2000112356,'physics','The energy stored in a coil of inductance $80\\,mH$ carrying a current of $2.5\\,A$ is','C','Given,<br>$L = 80mH = 80 \\times {10^{ - 3}}H,I = 2.5\\,A$<br>$\\therefore$ Energy stored,<br>$E=\\dfrac{1}{2} L I^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 80 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 2.5 \\times 2.5=0.25 J$','Energy Stored in an Inductor','Electromagnetic Induction','Easy',NULL,'$1.25\\,J$','$0.75\\,J$','$0.25\\,J$','$0.50\\,J$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160548,'biology','Choose the correct statements \n\n(i)Trypsinogen is activated by an enzyme, enterokinase, secreted by the gastric mucosa into active trypsin, which in turn activates the other enzymes in the pancreatic juice.  \n\n(ii) The bile released into the duodenum contains bile pigments (bilirubin and bili-verdin), bile salts, cholesterol and phospholipids but no enzymes. \n\n(iii) Bile salts helps in emulsification of fats, i.e., breaking down of the fats into very small micelles. \n\n(iv)Bile also activates lipases.','(ii), (iii), (iv)','The pancreatic juice contains inactive enzymes – trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidases, amylases, lipases and nucleases. Trypsinogen is activated by an enzyme, enterokinase, secreted by the intestinal mucosa into active trypsin, which in turn activates the other enzymes in the pancreatic juice. The bile released into the duodenum contains bile pigments (bilirubin and biliverdin), bile salts, cholesterol and phospholipids but no enzymes. Bile helps in the emulsification of fats, i.e., breaking down the fats into very small micelles. Bile also activates lipases. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii), (iii), (iv)','Only (iv)','Both (ii) and (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160549,'biology','Choose the incorrect statements \n\n(i) The duct of gall bladder (cystic duct) along with the hepatic duct from the liver forms the common bile duct. \n\n(ii)The bile duct and the pancreatic duct open together into the jejunum as the common hepato-pancreatic duct. \n\n(iii) hepato-pancreatic duct which is guarded by a sphincter called the sphincter of Oddi. \n\n(iv)the bile duct and the pancreatic duct open together into the duodenum as the common hepato-pancreatic duct','Only (ii)','The duct of the gall bladder (cystic duct) along with the hepatic duct from the liver forms the common bile duct. The bile duct and the pancreatic duct open together into the duodenum as the common hepato-pancreatic duct which is guarded by a sphincter called the sphincter of Oddi. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 261; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (ii)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','Both (ii) and (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160550,'biology','Choose the correct statement  \n\n(i)Proteins, proteoses and peptones (partially hydrolysed proteins) in the chyme reaching the intestine are acted upon by the proteolytic enzymes of bile juice. \n\n(ii)Carbohydrates in the chyme are hydrolysed by pancreatic amylase into trisaccharides and polysaccharides.\n\n(iii)Fats are broken down by lipases in bile with the help of bile into tri-and diglycerides. \n\n(iv) Nucleases in the pancreatic juice acts on nucleic acids to form nucleotides and nucleosides','Only (iv)','Proteins, proteoses and peptones (partially hydrolysed proteins) in the chyme reaching the intestine are acted upon by the proteolytic enzymes of pancreatic juice \nCarbohydrates in the chyme are hydrolysed by pancreatic amylase into disaccharides.\nFats are broken down by lipases with the help of bile into di-and monoglycerides.\nNucleases in the pancreatic juice act on nucleic acids to form nucleotides and nucleosides. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 263; Paragraph No. 1,2,3,4)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (iv)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','Both (ii) and (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160551,'biology','Choose the correct statement \n\n(i)The final steps in digestion occurs far away from the mucosal epithelial cells of the intestine.\n\n(ii)The breakdown of biomacromolecules mentioned above occurs in the region before the jejunum of the small intestine.\n\n(iii)The simple substances thus formed are absorbed in the jejunum and ileum regions of the small intestine.\n\n(iv) The undigested and unabsorbed substances are stored in the large intestine and never excreted.','Both (ii) and (iii)','The final steps in digestion occur very close to the mucosal epithelial cells of the intestine.\nDipeptides gets converted in to Amino acids through action of Dipeptidases. \nMaltose gets converted in to two molecules of Glucose through action of Maltase\nLactose gets converted in to Glucose + Galactose through action of Lactase\nSucrose gets converted in to Glucose+ Fructose through action of Sucrase                                     \nDi and Monoglycerides gets converted in to Fatty acids + Glycerol through action of  Lipases. \nThe breakdown of biomacromolecules mentioned above occurs in the duodenum (region preceding the jejunum)region of the small intestine. The simple substances thus formed are absorbed in the jejunum and ileum regions of the small intestine. The undigested and unabsorbed substances are passed on to the large intestine. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 263; Paragraph No. 5,6)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii), (iii), (iv)','Only (iv)','Both (ii) and (iii)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160552,'biology','Choose the false statements \n\n(i)No significant digestive activity occurs in the small intestine. \n\n(ii)The functions of large intestine are absorption of some water, minerals and certain drugs and secretion of mucus which helps in adhering the waste (undigested) particles together and lubricating it for an easy passage. \n\n(iii)The undigested, unabsorbed substances called faeces enters into the colon of the large intestine through ileo-caecal valve, which prevents the back flow of the faecal matter. \n\n(iv) Faeces is temporarily stored in the rectum till defaecation.','Both (i) and (iii)','No significant digestive activity occurs in the large intestine. The functions of the large intestine are:\n\n(i) absorption of some water, minerals and certain drugs;\n\n(ii) secretion of mucus which helps in adhering the waste (undigested)\nparticles together and lubricate it for easy passage. The undigested, unabsorbed substances called faeces enter the caecum of the large intestine through the ileocecal valve, which prevents the backflow of the faecal matter. It is temporarily stored in the rectum till defecation. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 264; Paragraph No. 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (i) and (iii)','Only (iv)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160553,'biology','Choose the statements that are not false\n\n(i)The activities of the gastro-intestinal tract are under neural control only for proper coordination of different parts.\n\n(ii)The sight, smell and/or the presence of food in the oral cavity can stimulate the secretion of saliva. \n\n(iii)Gastric and intestinal secretions are also, stimulated by neural signals. \n\n(iv)The muscular activities of different parts of the alimentary canal can also be moderated by neural mechanisms, both local and through CNS.','(ii), (iii), (iv)','The activities of the gastrointestinal tract are under neural and hormonal control for proper coordination of different parts. The sight, smell and/or the presence of food in the oral cavity can stimulate the secretion of saliva. Gastric and intestinal secretions are also, similarly, stimulated by neural signals. The muscular activities of different parts of the alimentary canal can also be moderated by neural mechanisms, both local and through CNS. Hormonal control of the secretion of digestive juices is carried out by local hormones produced by the gastric and intestinal mucosa. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 264; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','Only (iv)','Both (ii) and (iii)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160554,'biology','Choose the statements that are not correct\n\n(i) The muscular activities of different parts of the alimentary canal can also be moderated by neural mechanisms, only through CNS. \n\n(ii)Hormonal control of the secretion of digestive juices is carried out by local hormones produced by the gastric and intestinal mucosa. \n\n(iii) Heat is the ultimate form of all energies \n\n(iv) Joule (J) is the amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of 10 g of water by 1 °C.','Both (i) and (iv)','The activities of the gastrointestinal tract are under neural and hormonal control for proper coordination of different parts. The sight, smell and/or the presence of food in the oral cavity can stimulate the secretion of saliva. Gastric and intestinal secretions are also, similarly, stimulated by neural signals. The muscular activities of different parts of the alimentary canal can also be moderated by neural mechanisms, both local and through CNS. Hormonal control of the secretion of digestive juices is carried out by local hormones produced by the gastric and intestinal mucosa. The energy requirements of animals, and the energy content of food, are expressed in terms of a measure of heat energy because heat is the ultimate form of all energies. This is often measured as calorie (cal) or joule (J), which is the amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of 1 g of water by 1 °C. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 264; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (iv)','Both (i) and (iv)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','Both (ii) and (iii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160555,'biology','Choose the wrong statement \n\n(i)One kilo calorie is the amount of energy required to raise the temperature of 10 kg of water by 1 °C. \n\n(ii) Physiologists, traditionally refer to kcal as the Calorie or Joule (always capitalised). \n\n(iii) Nutritionists commonly use kilocalorie (kcal) or kilo joule (kJ). \n\n(iv) The amount of heat liberated from complete combustion of 10 g food in a bomb calorimeter (a closed metal chamber filled with oxygen) is its gross calorific or physiological energy value.','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','The energy requirements of animals, and the energy content of food, are expressed in terms of a measure of heat energy because heat is the ultimate form of all energies. This is often measured as calorie (cal) or joule (J), which is the amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of 1 g of water by 1 °C. Since this value is a tiny amount of energy, physiologists commonly use kilocalories (kcal) or kilojoule (kJ). One kilo calorie is the amount of energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of water by 1 °C. Nutritionists, traditionally refer to kcal as the Calorie or Joule (always capitalised). The amount of heat liberated from the complete combustion of 1 g of food in a bomb calorimeter (a closed metal chamber filled with oxygen) is its gross calorific or gross energy value. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 264; yellow box)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','Only (iv)','(ii), (iii), (iv)','Both (ii) and (iii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160556,'biology','Choose the correct statement\n\n(i)Absorption is the process by which the end products of digestion pass through the intestinal mucosa into the blood or lymph. \n\n(ii)Transport of water depends upon the osmotic gradient. \n\n(iii)Small amounts of disaccharides like glucose, amino acids and some electrolytes like chloride ions are generally absorbed by simple diffusion. \n\n(iv)Active transport occurs against the concentration gradient','(i), (ii),(iv)','Absorption is the process by which the end products of digestion pass through the intestinal mucosa into the blood or lymph. It is carried out by passive, active or facilitated transport mechanisms. Small amounts of monosaccharides like glucose, and amino acids and some electrolytes like chloride ions are generally absorbed by simple diffusion. The passage of these substances into the blood depends upon the concentration gradients. However, some substances like glucose and amino acids are absorbed with the help of carrier proteins. This mechanism is called facilitated transport. The transport of water depends upon the osmotic gradient. Active transport occurs against the concentration gradient and hence requires energy. Various nutrients like amino acids, monosaccharides like glucose, and electrolytes like Na+ are absorbed into the blood by this mechanism. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 264; Paragraph No. 4)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Digestion of food',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','Only (iv)','(i), (ii),(iv)','Both (ii) and (iii)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160601,'chemistry','Liquid-liquid solutions can be classified into ideal and non-ideal solutions on the basis of raoult’s law','Raoult’s law','Liquid-liquid solutions can be classified into ideal and non-ideal solutions on the basis of Raoult’s law. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Raoult’s law','Henry\'s law','Charles law','Boyle law','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160602,'chemistry','The solutions which obey raoult’s law over the entire range of concentration are known as _____ solutions','Ideal','The solutions which obey Raoult’s law over the entire range of concentration are known as ideal solutions. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ideal','non-ideal','TRUE','FALSE','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160603,'chemistry','The enthalpy of mixing of the pure components to form the solution is _____','Zero','The enthalpy of mixing of the pure components to form the solution is zero. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Zero','one','two','three','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160604,'chemistry','The volume of mixing of the pure components to form the solution is _____','Zero','The enthalpy & volume of mixing of the pure components to form the solution is zero. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Zero','one','two','three','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160605,'chemistry','When a solution does not obey raoult’s law over the entire range of concentration, then it is called _______','non ideal solution','When a solution does not obey Raoult’s law over the entire range of concentration, then it is called non-ideal solution. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 6)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','non ideal solution','ideal solution','true  solution','false  solution','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160606,'chemistry','The cause for these deviations lie in the nature of ________ at the molecular level','Interaction','The cause for these deviations lie in the nature of interactions at the molecular level. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 7)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','Interaction','Intraaction','Hydrogen bonding','Dipole - Dipole','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160607,'chemistry','In case of positive deviation from raoult’s law, A-B interactions are ______than those between A-A or B-B','weaker','In case of positive deviation from Raoult’s law, A-B interactions are weaker than those between A-A or B-B. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 7)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','weaker','stronger','equal','May be weaker or stronger','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160608,'chemistry','In case of negative deviations from raoult’s law, the intermolecular attractive forces between A-A and B-B are _______ than those between A-B','weaker','In case of negative deviations from Raoult’s law, the intermolecular attractive forces between A-A and B-B are weaker than those between A-B. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','weaker','stronger','equal','May be weaker or stronger','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160609,'chemistry','What leads to decrease in vapour pressure?','negative deviation','In case of negative deviations from Raoult’s law, the intermolecular attractive forces between A-A and B-B are weaker than those between A-B and leads to decrease in vapour pressure resulting in negative deviations.\nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','positive deviation','negative deviation','no deviation','Both A & B','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160610,'chemistry','What leads to increase in vapour pressure?','positive deviation','In case of positive deviation from Raoult’s law, A-B interactions are weaker than those between A-A or B-B. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 7)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','positive deviation','negative deviation','no deviation','Both A & B','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160611,'chemistry','Examples of solutions are nearly ideal in behaviour?','n-hexane and n-heptane','A perfectly ideal solution is rare but some solutions are nearly ideal in behaviour. Solution of n-hexane and n-heptane, bromoethane and chloroethane, benzene and toluene, etc. fall into this category. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','n-hexane and n-heptane','ethanol and acetone','carbon disulphide and acetone','acetone and benzene','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(160612,'chemistry','Which solutions show negative deviation?','phenol and aniline','In case of negative deviations from Raoult’s law, the intermolecular attractive forces between A-A and B-B are weaker than those between A-B and leads to decrease in vapour pressure resulting in negative deviations. An example of this type is a mixture of phenol and aniline. In this case the intermolecular hydrogen bonding between phenolic proton and lone pair on Nitrogen atom of aniline is stronger than the respective intermolecular hydrogen bonding between similar molecules. Similarly, a mixture of chloroform and acetone forms a solution with negative deviation from Raoult’s law. This is because chloroform molecule is able to form hydrogen bond with acetone molecule.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-48 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Ideal & Non-ideal solutions',NULL,'1','mcq','phenol and aniline','ethanol and acetone','benzene and toluene','carbon disulphide and acetone','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(160701,'chemistry','Which among the following are colligative properties','relative lowering of vapour pressure','(1) relative lowering of vapour pressure of the solvent (2) depression of freezing point of the solvent (3) elevation of boiling point of the solvent and (4) Osmotic pressure of the solution. All these properties depend on the number of solute particles irrespective of their nature relative to the total number of particles present in the solution. Such properties are called colligative properties. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'1','mcq','relative lowering of vapour pressure','viscosity','surface tension','solubility','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160702,'chemistry','__________ properties depend on the number of solute particles irrespective of their nature relative to the total number of particles present in the solution.','Colligative','(1) relative lowering of vapour pressure of the solvent (2) depression of freezing point of the solvent (3) elevation of boiling point of the solvent and (4) Osmotic pressure of the solution. All these properties depend on the number of solute particles irrespective of their nature relative to the total number of particles present in the solution. Such properties are called colligative properties. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Colligative','Concentration','Irrespect on these','Vapor pressure','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160703,'chemistry','Colligative properties depend on','The number of solute particles in solution','(1) relative lowering of vapour pressure of the solvent (2) depression of freezing point of the solvent (3) elevation of boiling point of the solvent and (4) Osmotic pressure of the solution. All these properties depend on the number of solute particles irrespective of their nature relative to the total number of particles present in the solution. Such properties are called colligative properties. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 Paragraph - 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','The nature of the solute particles dissolved in solution','The number of solute particles in solution','The physical properties of the solute particles dissolved in solution','The nature of solvent particles','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160704,'chemistry','When concentration of a salt solution is increased','Boiling point increases, while vapour pressure decreases','Increase in the concentration of salt solution means more solute (salt) is present and hence, more surface sites are occupied by solute molecules, That’s why vapour pressure decreases and a higher temperature is required to boil the solution, i.e. boiling point increases.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-51 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Boiling point increases, while vapour pressure decreases','Boiling point decreases, while vapour pressure increases','Freezing point decreases, while vapour pressure increases','Freezing point increases, while vapour pressure decreases','NEW','12-051','12-017',NULL),
(160705,'chemistry','Which of the following units is useful in relating concentration of solution with its vapour pressure?','Mole fraction','According to Raoult’s law, p = p° x XA. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Mole fraction','Parts per million','Mass percentage','Molality','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160706,'chemistry','The vapour pressure of pure benzene at a certain temperature is 0.850 bar. a non-volatile, non-electrolyte solid weighing 0.5 g when added to 39.0 g of benzene (molar mass 78 g/mol). vapour pressure of the solution, then, is 0.845 bar. what is the molar mass of the solid substance?','170 g/mol','The various quantities known to us are as follows pᵒ₁= 0.850 bar; p = 0.845 bar; M₁ = 78 g/mol; w₂= 0.5 g; w₁= 39 g Substituting these values in equation => 0.850-0.845/0.850 = 0.5/M₂ x 78/39 => M₂ = 170 g/mol. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-50 Paragraph - numerical)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'1','mcq','170 g/mol','180 g/mol','175 g/mol','160 g/mol','NEW','12-050','12-016',NULL),
(151504,'chemistry','Which of the following cannot be regarded as molecular solid?\n \n (a) SiC \n \n (b) AIN\n \n (c) Diamond \n \n (d) I₂','a,b,c','SiC, AIN and diamond are examples of network solid as they have three dimensional structure while, I₂ is a molecular solid, because such solid particles are held together by dipole-dipole interactions. SiC and AIN are interstitial solids.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,c','a,b,d','b,c,d','only b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160707,'chemistry','What does point a and b represent in the following diagram(n1606)','Elevation of boiling point','Let Tᵒᵦ be the boiling point of pure solvent and Tᵦ be the boiling point of solution. The increase in the boiling point  ∆Tᵦ = Tᵒᵦ - Tᵦ is known as elevation of boiling point. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-51 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Elevation of vapour pressure','Elevation of boiling point','Elevation of freezing point','Elevation of pressure','NEW','12-051','12-017','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/160707q.png'),
(160708,'chemistry','Identify (i), (ii) and (iii) in the following diagram(n1607)','(i) Frozen solvent (ii) Liquid solvent (iii) Solution','When a non-volatile solid is added to the solvent its vapour pressure decreases and now it would become equal to that of solid solvent at lower temperature. Thus, the freezing point of the solvent decreases. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-52 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) Solution (ii) Frozen solvent (iii) Liquid solvent','(i) Frozen solvent (ii) Solution (iii) Liquid solvent','(i) Frozen solvent (ii) Liquid solvent (iii) Solution','(i) Solution (ii) Liquid solvent (iii) Frozen solvent','NEW','12-052','12-018','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/160708q.png'),
(160709,'chemistry','During the depression of freezing point experiment an equilibrium is established between the molecules of','liquid solvent and solid solvent','When a non-volatile solid is added to the solvent its vapour pressure decreases and now it would become equal to that of solid solvent at lower temperature. Thus, the freezing point of the solvent decreases. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-52 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','liquid solvent and solid solvent','liquid solute and solid solvent','liquid solute and solid solute','liquid solvent and solid solute','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(160710,'chemistry','What happens to freezing point of benzene when naphthalene is added?','Decreases','When a non-volatile solid is added to the solvent its vapour pressure decreases and now it would become equal to that of solid solvent at lower temperature. Thus, the freezing point of the solvent decreases. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-52 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Increases','Decreases','Remains unchanged','First decreases and then increases','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(160711,'chemistry','The order of boiling points of four equimolar aqueous solutions is C < B < A < D. the correct order of their freezing points is','D< B < A<C','Elevation in boiling point and depression in freezing point, both are the colligative properties, i.e. depend only upon the number of particles present in solution. In other words, as the number of particles increases, boiling point increases but freezing point decreases. \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','D< B> A>C','D< B < A<C','D> B> A<C','D=B> A<C','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160712,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following \n\nAssertion (A) melting point of a substance is used for testing the purity of the substance. \n\nReason (R) there is no other method to determine the purity of substance. \n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:"','A is correct, R is incorrect.','A pure component has sharp melting point. If any impurity is present, it lowers the melting point of the substance. Other methods like chromatography etc., can also be used to check the purity of substances.  \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct, R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct, R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is correct, R is incorrect.','A is incorrect, R is correct','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160713,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is/are correct for Kf ? i. depends upon the nature of solute. ii. kᵣ is known as freezing point depression constant/molal depression constant. iii. Kf is not known as cryoscopic constant','Only II','The proportionality constant K j depends on the nature of solvent and is known as freezing point depression constant or molal depression constant or cryoscopic constant. Unit of K is K kg/mol . \nReference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-53 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Colligative properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Only I','Only II','Both I and II','i, II and III','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160801,'chemistry','Quantitatively, the extent to which a solute is dissociated or associated can be expressed by ________','Van\'t Hoff factor','Quantitatively, the extent to which a solute is dissociated or associated can be expressed by van’t Hoff factor i. This factor has been defined as ratio of normal molar mass to experimentally determined molar mass or as the ratio of observed colligative property to the Calculated colligative property. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'1','mcq','Van\'t Hoff factor','Pascal Constant','Faraday Constant','General gas constant','NEW','12-053','12-019',NULL),
(160802,'chemistry','Which of the following will have same value of Van\'t Hoff factor as that of Al₂(SO₄)₃ ?','K₄ [Fe(CN) ₆ ]','Quantitatively, the extent to which a solute is dissociated or associated can be expressed by van’t Hoff factor i. This factor has been defined as ratio of normal molar mass to experimentally determined molar mass or as the ratio of observed colligative property to the Calculated colligative property. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'1','mcq','AlCl₃','Al(OH)₃','BCl₃','K₄ [Fe(CN) ₆ ]','NEW','12-061','12-027',NULL),
(160803,'chemistry','Arrange the following aqueous solutions in the order of their increasing boiling points. \n\ni. 10⁻² m NaCl \n\nii.10⁻³ m MgCl₂ \n\niii. 10⁻⁴ m urea \n\niv. 10⁻⁴ m NaCl','I>II>IV>III','More the value of /, more will be the elevation in boiling point. Again, more dilute solution has low boiling point.  Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-58 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'3','mcq','I>II>IV>III','I>II>III>IV','II>I>III>IV','IV>I>III>II','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160804,'chemistry','58.5 g of NaCl and 180 g of glucose were separately dissolved in 1000 ml of water. identify the correct statement regarding the elevation of boiling point (BP) of the resulting solution.','NaCl solution will show higher elevation of boiling point','Both solutions have same molality but values of i i.e. van’t Hoff factor for NaCl and glucose are 2 and 1 respectively. Hence, NaCl will show higher elevation in boiling point. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-58 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'2','mcq','NaCl solution will show higher elevation of boiling point','glucose solution will show higher elevation of boiling point','both the solutions will show equal elevation of boiling point','the boiling point elevation will be shown by none of the solutions','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160805,'chemistry','Which of the following has lowest freezing point?','1%CaCl₂','Depression in freezing point is a colligative property (i.e. depends only upon the number of particles of solutes). Thus, the compound which produces Maximum number of ions will have the lowest freezing point. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-58 Paragraph - 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'3','mcq','1% sucrose','1%NaCl','1%CaCl₂','1% glucose','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160806,'chemistry','Which of the following would exert Maximum Osmotic pressure?','Decinormal aluminium sulphate','The Osmotic pressure of a solution is the excess pressure that must be applied to a solution to prevent osmosis, i.e., to stop the passage of solvent molecules through a semipermeable membrane into the solution Osmotic pressure is a colligative property as it depends on the number of solute molecules and not on their identity. For dilute solutions, it has been found experimentally that Osmotic pressure is proportional to the molarity, C of the solution at a given temperature T.  Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-55 Paragraph - 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'2','mcq','Decinormal aluminium sulphate','Decinormal barium chloride','Decinormal sodium chloride','A solution obtained by mixing equal volumes of (b) and (c)','NEW','12-055','12-021',NULL),
(160807,'chemistry','Compound a undergoes tetramerization in the given solvent. the Van\'t Hoff factor is','0.25','Quantitatively, the extent to which a solute is dissociated or associated can be expressed by van’t Hoff factor i. This factor has been defined as ratio of normal molar mass to experimentally determined molar mass or as the ratio of observed colligative property to the Calculated colligative property. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'2','mcq','2','0.125','0.5','0.25','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160808,'chemistry','Pure benzene freezes at 5.3°C. a solution of 0.223 g of phenylacetic acid in 4.4 g of benzene (k𝒻 =5.12 kg mol-1 ) freezes at 4.47°C. from this observation, one can conclude that','phenylacetic acid dimerises in benzene','Quantitatively, the extent to which a solute is dissociated or associated can be expressed by van’t Hoff factor i. This factor has been defined as ratio of normal molar mass to experimentally determined molar mass or as the ratio of observed colligative property to the Calculated colligative property. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'3','mcq','phenylacetic acid exists as such in benzene','phenylacetic acid undergoes partial ionisation in benzene','phenylacetic acid undergoes complete ionisation in benzene','phenylacetic acid dimerises in benzene','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160809,'chemistry','19.5 g of CH₂FCOOH is dissolved in 500 g of water. the depression in the freezing point of water observed is 1.00 C. calculate the Van\'t Hoff factor','1.0753','moles of CH₂FCOOH = 19.5/78 = 0.25,We need to assume volume of solution to be nearly equal to 500 mL. (as 500 g water is 500 mL). Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary) present), i = 1/0.93= 1.0753','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'2','mcq','1.0753','1.1','1.0521','1.0222','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160810,'chemistry','Determine the amount of CaCl₂ (i = 2.47) dissolved in 2.5 litre of water such that its Osmotic pressure is 0.75 atm at 27°C','3.42','pi = i x (w/MxV) x RT =>w = 0.75 x 111 x 2.5/2.47 x 0.0821 x 300 = 3.42g. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'2','mcq','0.75','2.47','3.42','2.5','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(160811,'chemistry','Determine the Osmotic pressure of a solution prepared by dissolving 25 mg of K₂SO₄ in 2 litre of water at 25° C, assuming that it is completely dissociated','5.27 x 10⁻³','3 ions are produced, so the value of i will be 3, pi = i CRT = 3 x 25 x 10⁻³ x 0.082 x 298/174 x 2 = 5.27 x 10⁻³. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'2','mcq','5.45 x 10⁻³','5.27 x 10⁻³','6 x 10⁻³','5.72 x 10⁻³','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160812,'chemistry','2 g of benzoic acid (C₆H₅COOH) dissolved in 25 g of benzene shows a depression in freezing point equal to 1.62 k. molal depression constant for benzene is 4.9  kg/mol. what is the percentage association of acid if it forms dimer in solution?','99.20%','M₂ = 4.9 x 2 x 1000/25 x 1.62K = 241.98 g/mol, Now consider the following equilibrium for the acid: 2C₆H₅COOH ----> (C₆H₅COOH)₂, If x represents the degree of association of the solute then we would have (1 – x ) mol of benzoic acid left in unassociated form and correspondingly x/2 as associated moles of benzoic acid at equilibrium.Therefore, total number of moles of particles at equilibrium is: 1-x+x/2 = 1-x/2 => i = 122/241.98 => x/2 = 1 -122/241.98 = 0.496 => x = 0.992 => percentage = 0.992 x 100 = 99.2%. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'2','mcq','9.92%','9.99%','0.99%','99.20%','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160813,'chemistry','0.6 ml of acetic acid (CH₃COOH), having density 1.06 g ml⁻¹ , is dissolved in 1 litre of water. the depression in freezing point observed for this strength of acid was 0.0205°C. calculate the Van\'t Hoff factor','1.041','Number of moles of acetic acid = 0.6 x 1.06/60 = 0.0106 mol, molality = 0.0106/1 = 0.0106 mol/kg, ∆T𝒻= 1.86 K kg/mol × 0.0106 mol/kg = 0.0197 K, i = 0.0205/0.0197 = 1.041. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-61 Paragraph - Summary)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Abnormal Molar Mass',NULL,'2','mcq','1.041','0.6','1.86','1.06','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(160814,'biology','In the stomach, gastric acid is secreted by the[NEET-I 2016]','Parietal cells','Parietal cells are large cells present on the side walls of the gastric glands. They lie against the basement membrane. They secrete hydrochloric acid and Castle’s intrinsic gastric factor that helps in the absorption of vitamin B12 in the ileum. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 2)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Peptic cells','Acidic cells','Gastrin secreting cells','Parietal cells','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160815,'biology','The enzyme that is not present in succus entericus is \n[AIPMT 2015]','Nuclease','Succus entericus or intestinal juice (pH = 7.8) refers to the secretion of glands of the small intestine. It contains many enzymes viz maltase, isomaltase, lipase, lactase, α-dextrinase, enterokinase, aminopeptidase, nucleotidase, nucleosidase, etc., for the digestion of carbohydrates, proteins, fats, nucleic acids etc. Enzyme nuclease is not a digestive enzyme. It is not present in any digestive juice. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 5)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Nucleosidase','Lipase','Maltase','Nuclease','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160816,'biology','Gastric juice of infants contains[AIPMT 2015 Cancelled]','Pepsinogen, lipase, rennin','The secretion of the cells of the gastric glands form gastric juice with pH 2 to 3.7. It contains two proenzymes, pepsinogen and prorennin and enzyme gastric lipase, mucous and hydrochloric acid. Rennin (chymosin) is responsible for clotting milk by acting on soluble milk protein caseinogen, and converting it into insoluble casein. This ensures that milk remains in stomach long enough to be acted on by protein digesting enzymes. Rennin’s concentration is highest in young mammals (as their primary diet is milk) which reduces gradually with age. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Pepsinogen, lipase, rennin','Amylase, rennin, pepsinogen','Maltase, pepsinogen, rennin','Nuclease, pepsinogen, lipase','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160817,'biology','Which of the following statements is not correct?[AIPMT 2015 Cancelled]','Brunner’s glands are present in the submucosa of stomach and secrete pepsinogen','The Brunner’s glands are branched tubular glands which occur only in the duodenum. They secrete alkaline watery fluid, a little enzyme and mucus. They open into the crypts of Lieberkuhn. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 5)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxyntic cells are present in the mucosa of stomach and secrete HCl','Acini are present in the pancreas and secrete carboxypeptidase','Brunner’s glands are present in the submucosa of stomach and secrete pepsinogen','Goblet cells are present in the mucosa of intestine and secrete mucus','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160818,'biology','Which enzymes are likely to act on the baked potatoes eaten by a man, starting from the mouth and as it moves down the alimentary canal?\n[Karnataka NEET 2013]','Salivary amylase → Pancreatic amylase → Disaccharidases','About 30 % of starch is hydrolysed here by the enzyme, Salivary Amylase\n(optimum pH 6.8) into a disaccharide – maltose. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Pancreatic amylase → Salivary amylase → Lipases','Disaccharidase like maltase → Lipases → Nucleases','Salivary amylase → Pancreatic amylase → Disaccharidases','Salivary maltase → Carboxypeptidase →Trypsinogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160819,'biology','Succus entericus is referred to as [NEET 2021]','Intestinal juice','Succus entericus also called, intestinal juice is a fluid that is secreted in small quantities in the small intestine. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 262; Paragraph No. 5)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Chyme','Pancreatic juice','Intestinal juice','Gastric juice','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160820,'biology','Sphincter of oddi is present a [NEET 2021]','Junction of hepato-pancreatic duct and duodenum','"The bile secreted by the hepatic cells passes through the hepatic duct. The bile secreted by the hepatic cells passes through the hepatic ducts and is stored and concentrated in a thin muscular sac called the gall bladder. The duct of the gall bladder (cystic duct) along with the hepatic duct from the liver forms the common bile duct  The bile duct and the pancreatic duct open together into the duodenum as the common hepato-pancreatic duct which is guarded by a sphincter called the sphincter of Oddi. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 260,261; Paragraph No. 4,1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Junction of jejunum and duodenum','Ileo-caecal junction','Junction of hepato-pancreatic duct and duodenum','Gastro-oesophageal junction','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160821,'biology','Which one of the following statements is true\nregarding digestion and absorption of food in\nhumans?[NEET 2009]','Fructose and amino acids are absorbed through\nintestinal mucosa with the help of carrier ions\nlike Na+.','Glucose and galactose get absorbed by active transport. The sodium pump of the cell membrane helps in the active take up of glucose and galactose. Fructose gets absorbed by facilitated diffusion which involves specific transmembrane carriers for transfer. Amino acids get absorbed by active transport coupled with active sodium transport. They also enter the bloodstream.','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Fructose and amino acids are absorbed through\nintestinal mucosa with the help of carrier ions\nlike Na+.','Chylomicrons are small lipoprotein particles\nthat are transported from intestine into blood\ncapillaries.','About 60% of starch is hydrolysed by salivary\namylase in our mouth.','Oxyntic cells in our stomach secrete the\nproenzyme pepsinogen.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160822,'biology','Which of the following functions is not performed by secretions from salivary glands? [NEET 2022]','Digestion of disaccharides','Digestion of disaccharides is the correct answer because digestion of polysaccharides like starch occurs in mouth and digestion of disaccharides occurs in small intestine. Saliva contains mucus which helps in the lubrication of oral cavity. Saliva contains an antibacterial agent-lysozyme so that it controls bacterial population in mouth. Digestion of complex carbohydrates are performed by secretions from salivary glands.','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Control bacterial population in mouth','Digestion of complex carbohydrates','Lubrication of oral cavity','Digestion of disaccharides','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160823,'biology','Given below are two statements: [NEET 2022]\nStatement I: Fatty acids and glycerols cannot be absorbed into the blood.\n\nStatement II: Specialized lymphatic capillaries called lacteals carry chylomicrons into lymphatic vessels and ultimately into the blood.\n In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Fatty acids and glycerol being insoluble in water, cannot be absorbed into the blood. They are first incorporated into small droplets called micelles which move into the intestinal mucosa. They are re-formed into very small protein coated fat globules called chylomicrons which are transported into the lymph vessels (lacteals) in the villi. These lymph vessels ultimately release the absorbed substances into the blood stream.','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct Answer','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160824,'biology','Which one of the following is a protein deficiency disease?[AIPMT 1998]','Kwashiorkor','Kwashiorkor is a protein deficiency disease. Its symptoms are underweight, stunted growth, poor brain development, loss of appetite, anaemia, protruding belly, slender legs and bulging eyes. Night blindness is the inability to see in dim light or at night. It occurs due to a deficiency of vitamin A. Eczema is a common itchy skin disease characterized by reddening and vesicle formation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 266; Paragraph No. 3)a','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Kwashiorkor','Night blindness','Eczema','Cirrhosis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160825,'biology','Select the correct sequential steps regarding \nabsorption of fatty acids and glycerol, in intestine. \n (A) Micelles are reformed into small protein coated \nfat globules called chylomicrons. \n (B) Micelles move into intestinal mucosa. \n (C) Fatty acids and glycerol are incorporated into \nsmall droplets called micelles. \n (D) Lacteals release the absorbed substances into \nblood stream. \n (E) Chylomicrons are transported into lacteals. \n Choose the correct answer from the options given \nbelow : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) (C), (B), (A), (E), (D)','Fatty acids and glycerols being insoluble, cannot be transported into the blood. They are emulsified by the bile juice which converts it into micelles and later on they convert it into chylomicrons which are coated by protein and absorb and transported by lymph vessels and release them into the bloodstream.','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(3) (C), (B), (A), (E), (D)','(1) (A), (E), (B), (D), (C)','(2) (D), (E), (B), (C), (A)','(4) (B), (C), (E), (A), (D)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160826,'biology','Given below are two statements : \n Statement I :-\n Goblet cells are unicellular glands. \nStatement II :-\n Earwax is the secretion of exocrine gland. \n In the light of the above statements, choose the \ncorrect answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Statement I: Goblet cells are unicellular glands. Statement II: Earwax is the secretion of an exocrine gland.\n\nBoth statements are true. Goblet cells are specialized epithelial cells that secrete mucus and are considered unicellular glands. Earwax, also known as cerumen, is produced by the ceruminous glands, which are exocrine glands located in the ear canal.\n\nTherefore, both statement I and statement II are true.','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are true','(1) Statement I is true but Statement II is false','(2) Statement I is false but Statement II is true','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160827,'biology','Once the undigested and unabsorbed substances enter the caecum, their backflow is prevented by [ NEET 2023]','(1) Ileo-caecal valve','The undigested & unabsorbed substances called faecal matter (faeces) enter into the caecum of large intastine through lleo-caecal valve which prevent the backflow.','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Ileo-caecal valve','(2) Gastro-oesophageal sphincter','(3) Pyloric sphincter','(4) Sphincter of Oddi','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160828,'biology','Role of enamel is to : [ RE NEET 2022]','(2) Masticate the food.','Enamel plays a crucial role in mastication by protecting the tooth, resisting acidic erosion, facilitating chewing, transferring pressure to dentin, and preventing tooth sensitivity.','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Connect crown of tooth with its root.','(2) Masticate the food.','(3) Form bolus.','(4) Give basic shape to the teeth.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160829,'biology','Math List I with List II.\nList I List II \nA. CCK I. Kidney\nB. GIP II. Heart\nC. ANF III. Gastric gland\nD. ADH IV. Pancreas\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023]','(4) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','Human Physiology >> Digestion  and  Absorption >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I','(2) A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','(3) A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I','(4) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(160901,'chemistry','Which of the following is dependent on volume?','Molarity','Molarity is a function of temperature as volume depends on temperature.  Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Molarity','Molality','Weight percentage','Mole fraction','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160902,'chemistry','What is the mole fraction of the solute in a 1.00 m aqueous solution?','0.0177',': 1 molal aqueous solution means 1 mole of solute is present in 1000 g of water. ∴ => x solute 1/1 +1000 /18 = 1/56.5 = 0 0177. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','1.77','0.177','0.0177','0.0324','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160903,'chemistry','How many grams of concentrated nitric acid solution should be used to prepare 250 ml of 2.0 M  HNO₃? the concentrated acid is 70%  HNO₃','45.0 g conc. HNO₃','Molarity = w HNO₃ x 1000/M HNO₃ x Vol. = w HNO₃ = 63 x 2 x 250/1000 = 63/2 => Mass of acid = 63/2 x 100/70 = 45. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','55.0 g conc. HNO₃','70.0 g conc. HNO₃','45.0 g conc. HNO₃','90.0 g conc. HNO₃','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160904,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds can be used as antifreeze in automobile radiators?','Glycol','Ethylene glycol is used as antifreeze in car radiators','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Methyl alcohol','Glycol','Nitrophenol','Ethyl alcohol','NEW','12-037','12-003',NULL),
(160905,'chemistry','Concentrated aqueous sulphuric acid is 98% H₂SO₄ by mass and has a density of 1.80 g ml⁻¹. volume of acid required to make one litre of 0.1 M H₂SO₄ solution is','5.55 mL','H₂SO₄ is 98% by weight. Weight of H₂SO₄ = 98 g, Weight of solution = 100g. => vol = mass/density = 100/1.80 mL = 55.55mL = 0.05555L. =>Molarity = 98/98 x 0.055 = 18.02M => V = 1000 x 0.1/18.02 = 5.55 mL. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','16.65 mL','22.20 mL','5.55 mL','11.10 mL','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160906,'chemistry','The mole fraction of the solute in one molal aqueous solution is','0.018','1 molal aqueous solution means 1 mole of solute present in 1 kg of H₂O. 1 mole of solute present in 1000/18 moless of H₂O. x(solute)=1/1000/18 +1 = 18/1018 = 0.018. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.009','0.018','0.027','0.036','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160907,'chemistry','2.5 litre of 1 M NaOH solution is mixed with another 3 litre of 0.5 M NaOH solution. then find out molarity of resultant solution.','0.73 M','Molecular weight of NaOH = 40 g mol⁻¹, 2.5 litre of 1 M NaOH solution contain 40 g mol⁻¹ × 1 mol L⁻¹ × 2.5 L = 40 × 2.5 g of NaOH 3 litre of 0.5 M NaOH solution contain 40 g mol⁻¹ × 0.5 mol L⁻¹ × 3 L = 40 × 0.5 × 3 g of NaOH If these two solutions are mixed, the volume of the resultant solution = (2.5 + 3) = 5.5 litre. 5.5 litre of the resultant solution contain = 40(2.5 + 1.5) g of NaOH 1 litre of the resultant solution contain 40 x 4/40 x 5.5 = 0.73M. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','0.80 M','0.50 M','0.73 M','0.10 M','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160908,'chemistry','How many g of dibasic acid (mol. weight 200) should be present in 100 ml of the aqueous solution to give strength of 0.1 N','1 g','The strength of the solution is 0.1 N. (Equivalent weight = 200/2 = 100). w = 100 x 0.1 x 100/1000 = 1g. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','10 g','1 g','2 g','20 g','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160909,'chemistry','What is the molarity of H₂SO₄ solution, that has a density 1.84 g/cc at 35°C and contains 98% by weight?','18.4 M','98% H₂SO₄ by weight means 98 g of H₂SO₄ is present in 100 g of solution. Therefore, its weight is 98 and moles of H₂SO₄ => 98/98 = 1, vol = mass/density = 100/1.83 =54.35mL, Molarity = 1 x 1000/54.35 = 18.4M. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','18.4 M','4.18 M','1.84 M','184 M','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160910,'chemistry','The concentration unit, independent of temperature, would be','molality','The molality involves weights of the solute and the solvent. Since the weight does not change with the temperature, therefore molality does not depend upon the temperature. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 39 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','normality','volume percentage','molality','molarity','NEW','12-039','12-005',NULL),
(160911,'chemistry','How many grams of CH₃OH should be added to water to prepare 150 ml solution of 2 M CH₃OH?','9.6','2 M CH₃OH = { 2/ 1000} x 150 x 32 = 9.6 g. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page - 38 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','13.2','9.6','2.4 × 10³','2.4','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(160912,'chemistry','In water saturated air, the mole fraction of water vapour is 0.02. if the total pressure of the saturated air is 1.2 atm, the partial pressure of dry air is','1.176 atm','p𝓌ₐₜₑᵣ ᵥₐₚₒᵤᵣ = x𝓌ₐₜₑᵣ ᵥₐₚₒᵤᵣ × Pₜₒₜₐₗ = 0.02 × 1.2 = 0.024 atm, Pₜₒₜₐₗ = p𝓌ₐₜₑᵣ ᵥₐₚₒᵤᵣ + p𝒹ᵣᵧ ₐᵢᵣ => p𝒹ᵣᵧ ₐᵢᵣ = 1.2-0.024 = 1.176atm. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-49 Paragraph - 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','3.4 atm','1.76 atm','1.176 atm','0.98 atm','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(160913,'chemistry','Pₐ and pᵦ are the vapour pressures of pure liquid components, A and B, respectively of an ideal binary solution. if xₐ represents the mole fraction of component A, the total pressure of the solution will be','pᵦ + xₐ ( pₐ – pᵦ)','According to Raoult’s law, P = xApA + xBpB . For binary solutions, xA + xB = 1, xB = 1 – xA. Reference : (NCERT Class-12 Page-47 Paragraph - 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','pₐ + xₐ(pᵦ – pₐ)','pₐ + xₐ ( pₐ – pᵦ)','pᵦ + xₐ(p – pₐ)','pᵦ + xₐ ( pₐ – pᵦ)','NEW','12-047','12-013',NULL),
(161001,'chemistry','Select the correct statement.\n\n(i) Sodium chloride is used in rat poison .\n\n(ii)Ethylene glycol is used as a freezing agent in car to cool down the engine .\n\n(iii)Generally solvent determines the physical state in which the solution exists.\n\n(iv)Binary solution usually contains two components namely the solute and the solvent.','(ii) (iii) and (iv) are correct.','Sodium fluoride is used in rat poison. (NCERT class 12; Chapter Solutions ; Page 35 ; Paragraph 1)\nEthylene glycol, an antifreeze, is used in cars for cooling the engine. (NCERT class 12; Chapter Solutions ; Page 37 ; Paragraph 1)\nGenerally, the component that is present in the largest quantity is known as solvent. Solvent determines the physical state in which solution exists. One or more components present in the solution other than solvent are called solutes. In this Unit we shall consider only binary solutions (i.e.consisting of two components)(NCERT class 12; Chapter Solutions ; Page 35 ; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (iii) and (iv) is correct','(i) (ii) and (iii) are correct','only (ii) and (iii) is correct','(ii) (iii) and (iv) are correct.','NEW','12-035','12-001',NULL),
(161002,'chemistry','Chloroform + oxygen is an example of','Liquid in gas','NCERT Class-12 Page-36 Table - 2.1','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Gas in liquid','Liquid in liquid','Gas in gas','Liquid in gas','NEW','12-036','12-002',NULL),
(161003,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true?\n\n(i)20%(v/v) methanol solution means 20 mL of methanol in 100mL of water.\n\n(ii)Mass by volume is the common concentration term used by pharmacists and in medicines.\n\n(iii)For a solution with \'n\' number of components the sum of all mole fractions is less than 1.\n\n(iv)The concentration of pollutant in water is often expressed in ppb.','(ii) and (iii) only are correct','20% (v/v) methanol means 20mL of methanol in water such that volume of the solution is 100mL. (NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions )\nAnother unit which is commonly used in medicine and pharmacy is mass by volume percentage.(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ; page 37; paragraph 2)\nIt can be shown that in a given solution sum of all the mole fractions is unity, i.e. x1+ x2 + .................. + xi= 1(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ; page 37; paragraph 8)\nThe concentration of pollutants in water or atmosphere is often expressed in terms of μg mL–1 or ppm.(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ; page 37; paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) (ii) and (iv) are correct.','All except (ii) are correct','(ii) and (iii) only are correct','(i) and (iv) only are correct.','NEW','12-037','12-003',NULL),
(161004,'chemistry','Select the correct option regarding alloys.','Bronze is an alloy of Cu and Sn','Example, the properties of brass (mixture of copper and zinc) are quite different from those of German silver (mixture of copper, zinc and nickel) or bronze (mixture of copper and tin);(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ; page 35; paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Brass is a mixture of Cu and Sn','German silver is an alloy of Cu ,Zn and Ni)','Bronze is a mixture of Cu and Ni','Bronze is an alloy of Cu and Sn','NEW','12-035','12-001',NULL),
(161005,'chemistry','Find the mole fraction of NaOH and water solution where 5 g NaOH is present in 0.1kg of water','0.022','Mole fraction = (no of moles of the component )/ (sum of mole fraction of all the components )\nNo of moles of NaOH = 5/40\nNo of moles of water = 100/18 (since 0.1kg=100g)\nHence mole fraction of NaOH = 0.125/(0.125+5.55)=0.022(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions )','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0.002','0.022','0.22','2.22','NEW','12-037','12-003',NULL),
(161006,'chemistry','40g of Ethyl alcohol dissolved in 60g of water . Find mass percentage of ethanol.','40%','Mass percentage = (mass of the substance / total mass )x100\nMass of ethanol = 40g ; mass of water = 60g ; total mass = 100\nHence mass % of ethanol = (40/100)100 = 40%(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter solutions)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','50%','40%','20%','60%','NEW','12-036','12-002',NULL),
(161007,'chemistry','Assertion : In both Raoults law and Henrys law partial pressure of the volatile component or gas is directly proportional to its mole fraction in solution.\n\nReason :Raoults law is a special case of Henrys law where the constant of proportionality in either becomes equal .','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','If we compare the equations for Raoult’s law and Henry’s law, it can be seen that the partial pressure of the volatile component or gas is directly proportional to its mole fraction in solution. Only the proportionality constant KH differs from p10 . Thus, Raoult’s law becomes a special case of Henry’s law in which KH becomes equal to p1 0(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ;page no 46 paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is false but reason is true.','NEW','12-046','12-012',NULL),
(161008,'chemistry','500mL of 0.4M HCl is mixed with 500mL of 0.1M H₂SO₄ . Find the concentration of H+ of the resulting solution','0.3 moles/L','No of moles / volume of solution in litres = Molarity \nThen no of moles = Molarity x volume of solution in litres \n\nHere 500mL of 0.4 M HCl is mixed . Hence no of moles of HCl = 0.4x0.5 = 0.2\n1 mole of HCl gives one mole of H⁺ ions . Therefore 0.2 moles of HCl can give 0.2 moles H+ ions .{Since 500mL =0.5L}\n\nSimilarly 500mL of 0.1M H₂SO₄ is present . That means no of moles of H₂SO₄ = 0.5 x 0.1 = 0.05 {Since 500mL = 0.5L}\n1 mole of H₂SO₄ can give 2 moles of H⁺ .Hence 0.05 moles of H₂SO₄ can give 0.05 x 2 moles of H⁺ i .e 0.1moles of H⁺\n\nHence total no of moles of H⁺ in solution is 0.1+0.2 = 0.3 ; Total volume = 500mL + 500mL = 1000mL = 1L\nConcentration = no of moles / volume = 0.3/1 = 0.3 moles /L(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter solutions)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0.3 moles/L','0.4 moles/L','.0.1 moles/L','0.2 moles/L','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(161009,'chemistry','Which of the following are true regarding Henrys law constant \n\n(i)It depends on temperature .\n\n(ii)Henrys law constant Kₕ is a universal constant .\n\n(iii) The relation between Kₕ and partial pressure is Kₕ = partial pressure of the gas /mole fraction (Kh = P/X ,X is mole fraction)\n\n(iv)Kh is inversly proportional to solubility','only (i) (iii) and (iv) are true ; (ii) is false','Here Kh is the Henry’s law constant. Different gases have different Kh values at the same temperature . This suggests that KH is a function of the nature of the gas. It is obvious from equation  that higher the value of Kh at a given pressure, the lower is the solubility of the gas in the liquid.\n(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ;page no 41 paragraph 3 and 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only (i) and (ii) are true ; (iii) and (iv) are false','only (i) and (ii) and (iii) are true ;(iv) are false','only (ii) and (iii) are true ; i) and (iv) are false','only (i) (iii) and (iv) are true ; (ii) is false','NEW','12-041','12-007',NULL),
(161010,'chemistry','In the following diagram the slope of the line represents diagram(n1601)','Henrys law constant','(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ;page no 41 fig 2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Henrys law constant','Universal constant','Raults law constant','Partial pressure','NEW','12-041','12-007','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/161010q.png'),
(161011,'chemistry','What amount of water in mL must be added to 300mL of 0.1M Nitric acid to make reduce its concentration to 0.3M','100mL','The dilution formula for molarity is MV=constant\nM₁V₁ = M₂V₂\n300 x 0.1 = 0.3 x V\nV=100 mL(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter solutions)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','50mL','100mL','150mL','200mL','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(161012,'chemistry','Which of the following relation is false','Pᴛ = P°₂+ (P°₂ - P°₁)X₂','Pᴛ = P₁ + P₂ ..............(1)\nPᴛ = P°₁X₁ + P°₂X₂........(2)\n\nSince X₁ + X₂ = 1\nPᴛ = P°₁X₁ + P°₂(1- X₁)\nPᴛ =  P°₁X₁ +P°₂ - P°₂X₁\nPᴛ = P°₂ + X₁(P°₁ -  P°₂)\nPᴛ = P°₁X₁ + P°₂(1- X₁).......(3)\n\nPᴛ = P°₁(1- X₂) + P°₂X₂\nPᴛ=  P°₁ -  P°₁X₂ + P°₂X₂\nPᴛ = P°₁ +  X₂(P°₂ -  P°₁)......(4)\n(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter solutions','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Pᴛ = P₁ + P₂','Pᴛ = P°₁+ (P°₂ - P°₁)X₂','Pᴛ = P°₁X₁ + P°₂X₂','Pᴛ = P°₂+ (P°₂ - P°₁)X₂','NEW','12-044','12-010',NULL),
(161013,'chemistry','Select the correct statement regarding solubility \n\n(i)Solubility of gases in liquid is affected by pressure but not temperature\n\n(i)Solubility of solid in liqiuid is affected by pressure but not temperature\n\n(iii)Solubility of gases in liquid is affected by both temperature and pressure \n\n(iv)Solubility of solid in liquid is affected only by temperature not pressure.','(iv) and (iii) are true ; (ii) and (i) are false','Solubility of gases in liquids is greatly affected by pressure and temperature.(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ;page no 40 paragraph 7)\nThe solubility of a solid in a liquid is significantly affected by temperature changes. Pressure does not have any significant effect on solubility of solids in  liquids. It is so because solids and liquids are highly incompressible and practically remain unaffected by changes in pressure(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ;page no 40 paragraph 6 and 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) and (ii) are true ; (iii) and (iv) are false','(i) and (iv) are true ; (iii) and (ii) are false','(ii) and (iii) are true ; (i) and (iv) are false','(iv) and (iii) are true ; (ii) and (i) are false','NEW','12-040','12-006',NULL),
(161014,'chemistry','Which of the following is true?','Solute which dissociate in solution will show lower mass than their actual mass','Solutes which dissociate in solution exhibit molar mass lower than the actual molar mass and those which associate show higher molar mass than their actual values\n(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter solutions ; Summary ; pg no 61 paragraph 2 )','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Solute which dissociate in solution will show lower mass than their actual mass','Solutes which associate will show lower molecular mass thann their actual mass','Solutes wont show any change in their molecular masses whether they dissociate or associate','Solutes which associate will show lower molecular mass than their actual mass','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161015,'chemistry','45g of H₂SO₄ is mixed in 500mL of water find the molarity of the solution','1M','Molarity = no of moles of solute / Volume of solution in L\nHere solute is  H₂SO₄ ; Given mass of  H₂SO₄ = 45g and molar mass of  H₂SO₄ is 98 g \nHence no of moles of  H₂SO₄  is 45/98 (Given mass / Molar mass)\nWhich gives 0.5 moles\nVolume of water = 500mL = 0.5L\nMolarity = 0.5/ 0.5 = 1M\n(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter solutions)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1M','2M','1.5M','2.5M','NEW','12-038','12-004',NULL),
(161016,'chemistry','Name (i) , (ii) and (iii) in figure diagram(n1607)','(i) - Frozen solvent ; (ii) liquid solvent ; (iii) - solution','(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter solutions , pg numebr 52 , figure - 2.8)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i)- Frozen solvent ; (iii) - Liquid solvent ; (ii) - Solution','(i) - Solute ; (ii) - Solution ;(iii) - liquid solvent','(i) - Frozen solvent ; (ii) liquid solvent ; (iii) - solution','(i) - Solution ; (ii) - Solute ; (iii) - liquid solvent','NEW','12-052','12-018','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/161016q.png'),
(161017,'chemistry','For a solution obeying Raults Law which of the following are true?\n\n(i) If a solution obeys Raults law for all concentrations , its vapour pressure would vary linearly from zero to the vapour pressure of the pure solvent\n\n(ii) In general for any solution the partial pressure of each volatile component in the solution is directly proportional to its mole fraction .\n\n(iii) P°₁=P₁X₁  ;P°₂=P₂X₂\n\n(iv) diagram(n1608)','(i) , (ii) and (iv) are true , (iii) is false','If a solution obeys Raoult\'s law for all concentrations, its vapour pressure would vary linearly from zero to the vapour pressure of the pure solvent. (NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ;page no 47 under figure 2.5)\nFor any solution the partial vapour pressure of each volatile component in the solution is directly proportional to its mole fraction . (NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ;page no 46 ;paragraph :5)\nP₁=P°₁X₁  ;P₂=P°₂X₂\ndiagram(ne1608)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) and (ii) are true ; (iii) and (iv) are false','(i) , (ii) and (iv) are true , (iii) is false','(i) , (ii) and (iv) are false ; (iii) is true','(i) , (ii) and (iii) are true ; (iv) is false','NEW','12-046','12-012','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/161017q.png'),
(151505,'chemistry','Which of the following features are not shown by quartz glass?\n \n (a) This is a crystalline solid\n \n (b) Refractive index is same in all the directions\n \n (c) This has definite heat of fusion\n \n (d) This is also called super cooled liquid','a,c','Quartz glass is an amorphous solid so it has not definite heat of fusion. This is due to short range order of molecule while quartz glass is also known as super cooled liquid and isotropic in nature.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161018,'chemistry','Assertion : Decrease in vapour pressure produced by adding 1.0 mol of sucrose to 1 kg of water is nearly similar to that produced by adding 1.0 mol urea to 1 kg water provided temperature is same\n\nReason : The decrease in vapour pressure of solvent depends on the quantity of non volatile solute irrespective of its nature .','Both assertion and reason is true and reason is the correct explanation for assertion','The decrease in the vapour pressure of solvent depends on the quantity of non-volatile solute present in the solution, irrespective of its nature. For example, decrease in the vapour pressure of water by adding 1.0 mol of sucrose to one kg of water is nearly similar to that produced by adding 1.0 mol of urea to the same quantity of water at the same temperature..','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is true reason is false','Both assertion and reason is true but reason is not the correct explanation for assertion.','Both assertion and reason is true and reason is the correct explanation for assertion','Assertion is false reason is true','NEW','12-046','12-012',NULL),
(161019,'chemistry','If a solution of a NaOH  in a solvent (whose molar mass is 40) contain 20% by mass of solute ,calculate its mole fraction','0.2','Mass percentage is 20 ie 20g  is present in 80g of solvent\nMole fraction = n/n+N (N =No of moles of solvent ; n = no of moles of solute )\nn = 20/40 = 0.5 ;N = 80/40 =2\nMole fraction = 0.5/2+0.5 = 0.5/2.5 = 0.2','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.2','0.25','0.3','0.15','NEW','12-037','12-003',NULL),
(161020,'chemistry','Assertion :In a solution containing several non volatile solute the lowering of vapour pressure depends on the sum of the mole fraction of solutes\n\nReason : Relative lowering of vapour pressure is equal to the mole fraction of the solute.','Both assertion and reason is true and reason is the correct explanation for assertion','The expression on the left hand side of the equation as mentioned earlier is called relative lowering of vapour pressure and is equal to the mole fraction of the solute.','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is false reason is true','Both assertion and reason is true and reason is the correct explanation for assertion','Both assertion and reason is true but reason is not the correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is true reason is false','NEW','12-050','12-016',NULL),
(161021,'chemistry','If 64g of  a non volatile substance of molecular weight 32g added to 108g water then the relative lowering of vapour pressure is ?','0.25','Vapour pressure after adding a solute according to Raults law is P₁=P°₁X₁ \nVapour pressure of pure solvent is P₁\nThen lowering of vapour pressure is  (P₁  - P°₁X₁) = P₁X₂\nRelative lowering of vapour pressure is = P₁X₂/P₁ = X₂\nX₂ = n/N+n (N =No of moles of solvent ; n = no of moles of solute )\nn= (64/32) =2 ; N=(108/18) =6\nX₂ = 2/(2+6) = 2/8 = 0.25','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.2','0.25','0.1125','0.125','NEW','12-050','12-016',NULL),
(161022,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true\n\n(i) Azeotropic mixtures can be formed by both ideal and non ideal solutions .\n\n(ii)A mixture of methanol and water is an example of a non ideal mixture showing positive deiviation.\n\n(iii)ΔH mix and ΔV mix are > 0 for solutions showing negative deviation .\n\n(iv)The boiling point of 68% Nitric acid and 32% water by mass azeortopic mixture has a boiling point of 398.5K','only (ii) is true ;(i) (iii) and (iv) are false','Only non ideal solutions can form azeortopic mixtures. \nMixture of methanol and water forms an ideal solution showing positive deviation .(Just like a mixture of ethanol and water)\nΔH mix and ΔV mix are < 0 for solutions showing negative deviation .\nThe solutions that show large negative deviation from Raoult’s law form maximum boiling azeotrope at a specific composition. Nitric acid and water is an example of this class of azeotrope. This azeotrope has the approximate composition, 68% nitric acid and 32% water by mass, with a boiling point of 393.5 K(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ; page 49; paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) (ii) (iii) are true (iv) is false','(ii) and (iii) are true ;(i) and (iv) are false','(i) and (iv) are true but (ii) and (iii) are false','only (ii) is true ;(i) (iii) and (iv) are false','NEW','12-048','12-014',NULL),
(161023,'chemistry','Find the depresion in freezing point for 0.3m NaCl  solution . Kf for water is 1.86℃/m','1.116','ΔTf =iK𝒻m\ni = 2 for NaCl ; Kf = 1.86℃/m  ;m=0.3\nΔTf =iK𝒻m = 1.116\n(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.16','0.18','1.116','0.1116','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(161024,'chemistry','Assertion :When a non volatile solute is added to the solvent the vapour pressure decreases and hence the freezing point of the solvent increases.\n\nReason: A solution will freeze when the vapour pressure equals the vapour pressure of the pure solid solvent','Reason is true but assertion is false','The freezing point of a substance may be defined as the temperature at which the vapour pressure of the substance in its liquid phase is equal to its vapour pressure in the solid phase. A solution will freeze when its vapour pressure equals the vapour pressure of the pure solid solvent  . According to Raoult’s law, when a non-volatile solid is added to the solvent its vapour pressure decreases and now it would become equal to that of solid solvent at lower temperature. Thus, the freezing point of the solvent decreases(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ; page 52; paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','Reason is true but assertion is false','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation for assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','NEW','12-052','12-018',NULL),
(161025,'chemistry','Assertion : Certain compunds gets associated when dissolved in a solvent\n\nReason : This normally happens in solvents of high dielectric constant.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Molecules of ethanoic acid (acetic acid) dimerise in benzene due to hydrogen bonding. This normally happens in solvents of low dielectric constan.(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ; page 58; paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Reason is true but assertion is false','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation for assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161026,'chemistry','Assertion :The techique of osmotic pressure for determination of molar mass of solute is particularly useful for biomolecules\n\nReason:Biomolecules are generally not stable at higher temperature','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation for assertion','The technique of osmotic pressure for determination of molar mass of solutes is particularly useful for biomolecules as they are generally not stable at higher temperatures and polymers have poor solubility.(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ; page 56; paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Reason is true but assertion is false','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation for assertion','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(161027,'chemistry','The fluid inside blood cell has the same osmotic pressure as that of the following','0.9%(M/V) NaCl solution','For example, the osmotic pressure associated with the fluid inside the blood cell is equivalent to that of 0.9% (mass/ volume) sodium chloride solution, called normal saline solution and it is safe to inject intravenously(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ; page 56; paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','0.8%(M/V) NaCl solution','0.95%(M/V) NaCl solution','0.9%(M/V) NaCl solution','both 2 and 3','NEW','12-056','12-022',NULL),
(161028,'chemistry','Assertion The preservation of meat by salting is due to osmosis\n\nReason:Through the process of osmosis, a bacterium on salted meat or candid fruit loses water, shrivels and dies','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation for assertion','The preservation of meat by salting and of fruits by adding sugar protects against bacterial action. Through the process of osmosis, a bacterium on salted meat or candid fruit loses water, shrivels and dies(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions ; page 57; paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation for assertion','Reason is true but assertion is false','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation for assertion','NEW','12-057','12-023',NULL),
(161029,'chemistry','Find the relative lowering of vapour pressure in 20% aqueous solution of sucrose .','0.033','20% Aqueous solution sucrose means  10g urea i+ 90g water (solution  is 100g)\nn (nof of moles of solute , in this case it is urea)=10/60 = 1/6\nN (no of moles of water )= 90/18 = 5 \nΔP =X₂ = (1/6)/{5+(1/6)} = 0.17/5.17 = 0.033\nNCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0.033','0.022','0.003','0.33','NEW','12-049','12-015',NULL),
(161030,'chemistry','Find the vanthoff factor for the following , if dissociation/association  is 100%  ;\n(i)Al₂(SO₄)₃\n(ii)HClO₄','5,2','Al₂(SO₄)₃ is a salt and HClO₄ is a strong acid  both of which complete dissociates.\nHence the vanthoff factor will be the number of ions itself\nFor Al₂(SO₄)₃ when it dissociates the number of ions will be 5 and for HClO₄ when it dissociates the number of ions will be 2\n(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1,4','1,5','2,4','5,2','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161031,'chemistry','If the percentage dissociation for acetic acid is 80 find its vanthoffs factor','1.8','If a molecule dissociates then , if dissociation is alpha\n  X      🠒    Y \n 1              n\n -α             α\n 1 - α        nα\nTotal number of molecules after dissociation (1-α + nα)\n                                                                       (1 + α(n-1))\ni = no of molecules after dissociation / No of molecules before dissociation = (1 + α(n-1))/1\n\nfor acetic acid n = 2\n\nGiven percentage dissociation is 80 hence  α = 0.8\n\ni = 1 + 0.8(2-1) = 1.8\n(NCERT class 12 chemistry ;chapter Solutions)','Physical chemistry >> Solutions >> Advanced questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.8','2','1.9','1.7','NEW','12-058','12-024',NULL),
(161032,'biology','Assertion : Certain drugs are absorbed in the mouth. \n\nReason : The mucosa of mouth and lower side of tongue has blood capillaries.','Both Assertion and Reason  are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Certain drugs coming in contact with the mucosa of the mouth and lower side of the tongue are absorbed into the blood capillaries lining them. .( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  265; Table 16.1 )','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason  are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161033,'biology','Assertion: Small intestine is the principal organ for absorption.\n \nReason: Absorption of water, simple sugars and alcohol take place in small intestine.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The small intestine is the principal organ for absorption. The final products of digestion such as glucose, fructose,  fatty acid, glycerol and amino acids are absorbed here. ( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  265; Table 16.1 )','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161034,'biology','Consider following statement regarding protein energy malnutition (PEM) \n\n(i)PEM usually affects adults.\n\n(ii) Marasmus is included in PEM while kwashiorkor is not included in PEM.\n\n(iii) Marasmus occurs if mother milk is replaced by other food poor in carbohydrates.','i, ii and iii are incorrect.','PEM affects infants and children . Marasmus and kwashiorkor are included in PEM.Marasmus occurs if mother milk is replaced  by other food poor in protein and calorie .( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  266; Paragraph 1 ,2 and 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii and iii are incorrect.','iii is correct while i and ii are incorrect.','i and ii are correct while iii is incorrect.','i ,ii and iii are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161035,'biology','Consider following statement regarding Marasmus.\n\n(i) Marasmus is produced by simultaneous deficiency of proteins and calories.\n\n(ii) In Marasmus growth rate and body weight does not decline.\n\n(iii) Growth and development of brain and mental faculties are not impaired.','ii and iii are incorrect.','Marasmus is produced by a simultaneous deficiency of proteins and calories. Growth rate and body weight decline considerably in Marasmus.  Even growth and development of the brain and mental faculties are impaired. .( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  266; Paragraph 2)','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, iii are incorrect','i ,ii and iii are correct.','Only i and ii is correct.','ii and iii are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161036,'biology','Assertion: In Kwashiorkor there is thinning of limbs, wasting of muscles and failure of growth and brain development. \n\nReason: Kwashiorkor is produced by protein deficiency accompanied by calorie deficiency.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Kwashiorkor is produced by protein deficiency unaccompanied by calorie deficiency. Kwashiorkor shows wasting of muscles, thinning of limbs, failure of growth and brain development. (Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  266; Paragraph 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161037,'biology','Consider following statement regarding alimentary canal in man. \n\n(i) Alimentary canal consists of mouth and buccal cavity only. \n\n(ii) Alimentary canal begins at mouth and terminates at  small intestine.\n\n(iii) The mouth is the beginning of the alimentary canal and leads into oral cavity.','Only iii is correct.','The alimentary canal consists of the mouth, buccal cavity, pharynx, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, rectum and anus. ( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  266; Paragraph 2). The alimentary canal begins with the anterior opening mouth and posteriorly through the anus. The mouth leads into the oral cavity. ( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  257; Paragraph 3)','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only ii is correct.','Both i and ii are correct','i , ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161038,'biology','Assertion: The backflow of fecal matter in the other part of alimentary canal is prevented by large intestine. \n\nReason: Large intestine is guarded by illeocaecal valve.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion.','The undigested food (faeces) enters the caecum of the large intestine through the ileocecal valve, which prevents the backflow of the faecal matter. .( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  267; Paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161039,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding digestion in the duodenum:\n\n(i) Nucleotides are converted into Nucleosides.\n\n(ii) Maltose is converted into three molecules of glucose.\n\n(iii) Di and Monoglycerides are converted into fatty acids and glycerol.','Both i and iii are correct while ii is incorrect.','Nucleotides are converted into Nucleosides. Maltose is converted into two molecules of glucose. Di and Monoglycerides are converted into fatty acids and glycerol. ( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  263)','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii and iii are incorrect.','i , ii and iii are correct','Both i and iii are correct while ii is incorrect.','Only iii is incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161040,'biology','Assertion : In the chyme proteins, peptones and proteoses are converted into dipeptides. \n\nReason: In chyme, carbohydrates are hydrolyzed into disaccharides.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','In the chyme proteins, peptones and proteoses are converted into dipeptides. whereas carbohydrates are hydrolyzed into disaccharides. ( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  263)','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161041,'biology','Assertion: The digestion of fats occurs in small intestine.\n\nReason: The lipases are present in the bile which is present in the duodenum part of small intestine.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Fats are broken down by lipases(from pancreatic juice and succus entericus) with the help of bile salts(bile is devoid of enzymes) into di-and monoglycerides. ( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  263)','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161042,'biology','Assertion : Maximum digestive substances are absorbed in the jejunum and illeum. \n\nReason : Microvilli modification of villi in small intestine increase the surface area and it contains simple food substances that are broken down','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The simple substances thus formed are absorbed in the jejunum and ileum regions of the small intestine. ( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  263)The cells lining the villi produce numerous microscopic projections called microvilli giving a brush border appearance. These modifications increase the surface area enormously( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  260)','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161043,'biology','Consider following statement regarding food :\n\n(i) Food is one of the basic requirement of all living organisms. \n\n(ii) Major components of food are carbohydrates and proteins only.\n\n(iii) Food provides energy and organic material for growth and repair of tissues.','Both i and iii are correct while ii is incorrect.','Food is one of the basic requirements of all living organisms. The major components of our food are carbohydrates, proteins and fats. Food provides energy and organic materials for the growth and repair of tissues. ( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  257)','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only i is correct.','Both ii and iii are correct','Both i and iii are correct while ii is incorrect.','i , ii and iii are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(161044,'biology','Assertion : The type of attachment of teeth in man is thecodont. \n\nReason : Each tooth is embedded in the socket of jaw bone.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion.','Each tooth in man is embedded in a socket of the jaw bone. This type of attachment is called thecodont. ( Reference   NCERT Biology Class11; Page NO  257 paragraph 3 )','Human Physiology >> Digestion and Absorption >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380101,'biology','The Environment Protection Act was passed in?','1986','On 9th January, 1986 Government of India passed an Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 to protect and improve the quality of our environment (air, water and soil). NCERT, 12th Biology , Chapter 16,Page 270','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','1986','1992','1995','2014','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/380101q.png'),
(311622,'biology','Which of the following is not an objective of Biofortification in crops?(NEET 2021)','Improve resistance to diseases','Biofortication is used to improve the macronutrient (proteins and vitamins) and micro nutrient content','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Improve micronutrient and mineral content','Improve protein content','Improve resistance to diseases','Improve vitamin content','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380102,'biology','Pollution is an undesirable change in?\n \ni.Physical characteristics of air, land, water and soil. \n\nii.Chemical characteristics of the earth.\n\niii. Biological Characteristics..\n\niv. Psychological characteristics','i, ii  and  iii','Pollution is any change in physical, chemical, or biological characteristics of the environment that has potentiality to harm human life, life of other desirable species, natural resources, cultural assets and industries. Another type of pollution is increase in CO₂ and other greenhouse gases and a decrease in stratospheric ozone on global scale which would be affecting air, water and land resources, biological diversity and human health. Thus pollution results in damage to biological effectiveness. NCERT, 12th Biology , Chapter 16,Page 270','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','i only','ii only','i, ii  and  iii','i, ii, iii , iv','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380103,'biology','Agents, that are responsible for pollution are known as?','Pollutants','A pollutant is a substance or energy introduced into the environment that has undesired effects, or adversely affects the usefulness of a resource. NCERT, 12th Biology , Chapter 16,Page 270','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Pollutants','Contaminants','Infectious agents','Infiltration agents','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380104,'biology','Which exerts tremendous pressure on natural resources?','Increase in demand for food, water, home, electricity, roads, automobiles and numerous other commodities.','Human population size has grown enormously over the last hundred years. This means increase in demand for food, water, home, electricity, roads, automobiles and numerous other commodities. These demands are exerting tremendous pressure on our natural resources, and are also contributing to pollution of air, water and soil.  NCERT, 12th Biology , Chapter 16,Page 270','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Increase in demand for employment and handmade goods','Increase in use of eco-friendly and biodegradable material','Increase in demand for political power','Increase in demand for food, water, home, electricity, roads, automobiles and numerous other commodities.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380105,'biology','IUCN stands for:','International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural resources','IUCN-International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural resources.\nThe IUCN is an international organization that works in the field of conservation of the world’s flora and fauna. This is an important international body, especially in light of the IUCN Red List.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural resources','International Union Council for Natural resources','Indian Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural resources','International Union for Control on Noise pollution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380106,'biology','The need of the hour is to \n \ni.Stop promoting use of eco friemdly goods\n \nii.Check the depletion of our precious natural resources. \n\niii.Check the degradation of our precious natural resources.\n\niv.Control pollution without halting the process of development.\n\nv.Promote use of non biodegradable material','ii, iii, iv','The need of the hour is to check the degradation and depletion of our precious natural resources and pollution without halting the process of development.NCERT, 12th Biology , Chapter 16,Page 270','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','ii only','ii, iii, iv','i,ii,iii,iv,v','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380201,'biology','Air pollutants affects on which of the following:\n\ni) Growth of crop\n\nii) Agricultural yield\n\niii)Respiratory system of living beings\n\niv)Mortality rate of a population','i, ii, iii, iv','Air pollutants affect the growth, yields of crop, death of plant, respiratory system of human and animals and many more lives and process in the environment. Air pollution predispose an individual to respiratory diseases and increases the mortality rate directly or indirectly, NCERT, 12th Biology , Chapter 16,Page 270','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Air Pollution and Control',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','I,ii','i, ii ,iii','i, ii, iii, iv','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380202,'biology','The harmful effect of pollution depends upon? \n\ni.The concentration of Pollutants.\n\nii.Duration of Exposure. \n\niii.Physiology of organism','i, ii,iii are correct','The harmful effect of pollution depends upon Concentration of Pollutants, Duration of Exposure, physiology of the organism, potency of pollutant\nNCERT, 12th Biology, Chapter 16,Page 271','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Air Pollution and Control',NULL,'2','mcq','i only is correct','ii only is correct','i and ii are correct','i, ii,iii are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380203,'biology','The electrostatic precipitator removes impurities upto?','99%','There are several ways of removing particulate matter; the most widely used of which is the electrostatic precipitator, which can remove over 99 % particulate matter present in the exhaust from a thermal power plant. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 271','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Air Pollution and Control',NULL,'2','mcq','100%','99%','90%','78 %','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380204,'biology','According to CPCB (Central Pollution Control Board) which particulate in respect to size(in diameter) is really dangerous for human health?','Particulate size 2.5 micrometers or less in diameter (PM 2.5)','According to Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB), particulate size 2.5 micrometers or less in diameter (PM 2.5) are responsible for causing the greatest harm to human health. These fine particulates can be inhaled deep into the lungs and can cause breathing and respiratory symptoms, irritation, inflammations and damage to the lungs and premature deaths.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Air Pollution and Control',NULL,'1','mcq','Particulate size 2.5 micrometers or less in diameter (PM 2.5)','Particulate size above 2.5 micrometres in diameter (PM 2.5)','Particulates equal to 2.5 micrometres or slight above','Particulates above 5 micrometres','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380205,'biology','CPCB stands for?','Central Pollution Control Board','The Central Pollution Control Board of India is a statutory organisation under the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change,  NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 271','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Air Pollution and Control',NULL,'1','mcq','Central Population Control Board','Central Pollution Control Bureau','Central Pollutants Control Board','Central Pollution Control Board','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380206,'biology','Particulate size 2.5 micrometres or less in diameter (PM 2.5) are responsible for \n\ni. Breathing  and  Respiratory symptoms \n\nii.Irritation and Inflammation \n\niii. Damage to lungs \n\niv. Premature death','i, ii, iii,iv','According to CPCB, particulate size 2.5 micrometers or less in diameter (PM 2.5) are responsible for causing greatest harm to human health. These fine particulates can be inhaled deep into the lungs and can cause breathing and respiratory symptoms, like irritation and inflammations and damage to the lungs and premature deaths.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Air Pollution and Control',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','i, ii and iii','ii and iv','i, ii, iii,iv','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110710,'biology','Mass flow of water occurs due to','Adhesive and cohesive property','As water evaporates into the intercellular spaces or the atmosphere, tension develop in the continuous stream of water in the apoplast, hence mass flow of water occurs due to the adhesive and cohesive properties of water.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 184','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Adhesive property of water','Cohesive property of water','Adhesive and cohesive property','Due to the concentration of water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380207,'biology','Automobiles are major cause of atmospheric pollution. What are methods to prevent pollution caused by them?\n\ni. Use of Catalytic Converters \n\nii. Proper maintenance of automobiles \n\niii. Use lead free petrol/diesel','i, ii and iii','Automobiles are a major cause for atmospheric pollution atleast in\nthe metro cities. As the number of vehicles increase on the streets, this\nproblem is now shifting to the other cities too. Proper maintenance of\nautomobiles along with use of lead-free petrol or diesel can reduce the\npollutants they emit.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Air Pollution and Control',NULL,'2','mcq','i only','ii only','i, ii and iii','ii and iii','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380208,'biology','The Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act came into force in?','1981','The Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981 is an Act of the Parliament of India to control and prevent air pollution in India','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Air Pollution and Control',NULL,'2','mcq','1981','1987','1881','2005','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380209,'biology','Which of the following conditions caused by Noise pollution? \n\ni. Sleeplessness \n\nii. Decrease in heart beat \n\niiii.Altered the breathing patterns \n\niv. decreases stress','i and iii','Exposure to prolonged or excessive noise has been shown to cause a range of health problems ranging from stress, poor concentration, productivity losses in the workplace, and communication difficulties and fatigue from lack of sleep, to more serious issues such as cardiovascular disease, cognitive impairment, tinnitus','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Air Pollution and Control',NULL,'2','mcq','i and iii','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380210,'biology','According to CPCB (Central Pollution Control Board) the particulate size(in diameter) really dangerous for human health is?','2.5 micrometers or less','According to CPCB, particulate size 2.5 micrometers or less in diameter (PM 2.5) are responsible for causing greatest harm to human health. These fine particulates can be inhaled deep into the lungs and can cause breathing and respiratory symptoms, like irritation and inflammations and damage to the lungs and premature deaths.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Air Pollution and Control',NULL,'3','mcq','2.5 micrometers or less','2.5 millimeters or more','2.0 micrometers or more','5.2 micrometres or less','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
