(380302,'biology','Label the components in the given diagram .(3802)','A-Dirty air,B-Clean air,C-Water/lime spray,D-Particulate matter','A-Dirty air,\nB-Clean air,\nC-Water/lime spray,\nD-Particulate matte.\n\n(NCERT 12th biology, page 271, Figure 16.1-scrubber.)','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Water pollution and its control',NULL,'3','match','A,Dirty air','B,Clean air','C,Water/lime spray','D,Particulate matter','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/380302q.png'),
(380303,'biology','________ is the aging of a water body due to nutrient enrichment of its water.','Accelerated eutrophication','Accelerated eutrophication is the aging of a water body due to nutrient enrichment of its water. This is the condition due to the movement of the chemicals containing nitrates and phosphates in the water system. It can be natural or artificial.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Water pollution and its control',NULL,'3','mcq','Accelerated eutrophication','Biofortification','Algal bloom','Biomagnification','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380304,'biology','Arrange the following in increasing sequence of DDT in an aquatic food chain.','1-Water,2-Zooplankton,3-Smallfish,4-Fish eating birds','The concentration of DDT is increased at successive trophic levels; say if it starts at 0.003 ppb in water, it flows in the manner Water(DDT 0.003 ppb)< Zooplankton (DDT 0.04ppm)<Small fish (DDT 0.5ppm)<Large fish(DDT 2ppm)<Fish-eating birds(DDT 25ppm), thus in this way it ultimately reaches 25 ppm ppm in fish-eating birds','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Water pollution and its control',NULL,'3','match','1,Water','2,Zooplankton','3,Small fish','4,Fish eating birds','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380305,'biology','BOD stands for?','Biochemical O₂ Demand','Biochemical O₂ demand (BOD) represents the amount of O₂ consumed by bacteria and other microorganisms while they decompose organic matter under aerobic (O₂ is present) conditions at a specified temperature.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Water pollution and its control',NULL,'1','mcq','Biochemical O₂ Demand','Biological O₂ deficit','Biological Oxidation Deed','Biochemical Oxidation Degree','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380306,'biology','Water hyacinths are the world’s most problematic aquatic weed, they are also called ________?','Terror of Bengal','The beautiful mauve-colored flowers found on very appealingly-shaped floating plants in water bodies are these plants which were introduced into India for their lovely flowers have caused havoc by their excessive growth by causing blocks in our waterways. They grow faster than our ability to remove them. These are plants of water hyacinth (Eichhornia crassipes), the world’s most problematic aquatic weed, also called ‘Terror of Bengal’.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Water pollution and its control',NULL,'2','mcq','Killing weed','Terror of Bengal','Terror of west Bengal','hazardous weed','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(380307,'biology','Sewage from our homes as well as from hospitals is likely to contain many undesirable pathogenic may cause an outbreak of serious diseases, such as__________','Typhoid','Sewage from our homes as well as from hospitals are likely to contain many undesirable pathogenic micro- organisms, and its disposal into a water without proper treatment may cause outbreak of serious diseases, such as, dysentery, typhoid, jaundice, cholera, etc.\n\n(NCERT 12th, chapter 16, page 276, paragraph 2.)','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Water pollution and its control',NULL,'2','mcq','AIDS','Sickle cell anemia','Typhoid','Haemophilia','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
