(3,'biology','Which one of the statements is not valid for aerosols\n(NEET 2017)','They cause increased agricultural productivity','Aerosol are harmful environmental pollutants which have negative effects on Agriculture. Aerosol is the colloid of fine solid particles in the air such as fog. It is type of pollutant that hinders the sunlight to reach the plants and thus, plants are not able to trap light for photosynthesis. In the absence of photosynthesis the agricultural productivity get decreased.\n\nThe following are the environmental, agricultural and health issues caused by aerosols:\n- They are harmful to human health\n- They alter rainfall and monsoon patterns\n- They have a negative impact on agricultural land causing a decrease in agricultural productivity.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','They alter rainfall and monsoon patterns','They cause increased agricultural productivity','They have negative impact on agricultural land','They are harmful to human health','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381002,'biology','Biochemical O₂ Demand (BOD) may not be a good index for pollution of water bodies receiving effluents from- \n(NEET2 2016)','Petroleum Industry','Biochemical O₂ Demand (BOD) is the measure of O₂ required by aerobic decomposers for biological degradation of the biodegradable organic materials. BOD indicates the °of organic pollution in water. Petroleum is not degraded by decomposer microbes.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Domestic Sewage','Dairy Industry','Petroleum Industry','Sugar Industry','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381003,'biology','The highest DDT concentration in aquatic food chain shall occur in \n(NEET2 2016)','Seagull','DDT is Non-biodegradable and is not metabolised within the body of an organism rather it accumulates 8n the fat tissues therefore its concentration goes on increasing from one tropic level to another of a food chain, highest amount being present in top consumer i.e., it shows biomagnification. In an aquatic food chain sea gull is the top carnivore therefore will possess highest concentration of DDT.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Phytoplankton','Seagull','Crab','Eel','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381004,'biology','Depletion of which gas in the atmosphere can lead to an increase incidence of skin cancer \n(NEET1 2016)','Ozone','Ozone Layer or shield is present in stratosphere. It functions at the shield against strong UV radiations coming from sun. UV radiations are harmful and may cause mutations in living organisms. Thining of Ozone layer increases the amount of UV radiations reaching the earth. It would increase occurrence of cataract, skin cancers, dimming of eye sight, photobuming, deficient functioning of immune system etc.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Ammonia','Methane','Nitrous Oxide','Ozone','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381005,'biology','The Joint Forest Management(JFM) concept was introduced in India during (NEET1 2016)','1980s','In India, Joint Forest Management was started in 1980s. Village and tribal communities are being involved in development and protection of degraded forests on share basis.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','1980s','1990s','1960s','1970s','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381006,'biology','A river with an inflow of domestic sewage rich in organic waste may result in \n[NEET1 2016]','Death of fish due to lack of Oxygen','High amount of organic waste in a lake will trigger and activate decomposer microbes which will decompose organic waste. Biochemical O₂ Demand (BOD) of this lake will shoot up and decomposers will utilise the most of dissolved O₂ present in lake. Consequently the level of dissolved O₂ will go down to alarming extent. Aquatic animals like fish which totally depend on the O₂ dissolved in the water will ultimately die.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','An increased production of fish due to biodegradable nutrients','Death of fish due to lack of Oxygen','Drying of river very soon due to algal bloom','Increased population of aquatic food web organisms','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381007,'biology','Acid rain is caused by increase in the atmospheric concentration of \n(NEET 2015)','SO₂ and NO₂','Acid rain is rainfall and other forms of precipitation with a pH of less than 5. Acid rain is caused by large scale emission of acidic gases into the atmosphere from thermal power plants, industries and automobiles. The common ones are sulphur dioxide, nitrogen oxides, volatile organic carbon(VOCs) and hydrogen chloride. Sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides are changed in the atmosphere into sulphuric acid and nitric acid by combining with O₂ and water which then fall on earth in the form of acid rain.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₂ and CO','O₃ and dust','SO₂ and NO₂','SO₃ and CO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381008,'biology','Eutrophication of water bodies leading to killing of fishes is mainly due to non-availability of \n(NEET 2015)','Oxygen','Eutrophication is the excessive growth of algae, plants and animals in water bodies due to the nutrient enrichment particularly with nitrogen and phosphate. Nutrients present in sewage, agricultural wastes and fertilisers cause dense growth of plants and plaktonic algae. The excessive growth of plaktonic algae that cause colouration of water is called algal bloom, which is toxic to animals and humans. Eutrophic water bodies also support excessive growth of floating plants. Algal blooms and floating plants cut off light from submerged plants, resulting in their death. There is drastic decrease in O₂ replenishment inside water. Non availability of O₂ results in death of aquatic plants and animals such as fish, which further adds to organic loading of water. Decomposition is replaced by putrefraction which is anaerobic, leading to absence of O₂ in water and death of aquatic animals.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Essential minerals','Oxygen','Food','Light','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381009,'biology','The UN conference of parties on climate change in the year 2012 was held at \n[NEET 2015]','Doha','The UN conference of parties on climate change in the year 2012 was held at Doha','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Lima','Warsaw','Durban','Doha','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381010,'biology','Which of the following are the most suitable indicators of SO₂ pollution in the environment \n(NEET 2015)','Lichens','Lichens are very sensitive of SO₂ pollution. They are completely destroyed at places where there is SO₂ pollution in atmosphere. Therefore they act as very good indicator of SO₂ pollution.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Algae','Fungi','Lichens','Conifers','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110711,'biology','The symplastic system is the system of?','Interconnected protoplasts','The symplastic system is the system of interconnected protoplasts.Neighbouring cells are connected through cytoplasmic strands that extend through plasmodesmata\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 185','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Connection of cell membranes','Interconnected protoplasts','Connection between cytoplasm','none of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381011,'biology','An increase in the concentration of the toxicant at successive tropic levels is known as \n(NEET 2015)','Biomagnification','Heavy metals and persistent pesticides (e.g., chlorinated hydrocarbons like DDT)  pass into Food Chain and increase in amount per unit weight of organisms with the rise of trophic Level due to the accumulation in fat. The phenomenon is called biomagnification/bioconcentration/biological amplification, e.g., 0.003 parts per billion of DDT in water becomes 0.003 ppm in phytoplankton, 0.04 ppm in zooplankton, 0.5 be p.m. in clams and small fish, 2.0 ppm in Predator fish and 25 ppm in fish eating birds like sea gulls.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Bio transformation','Biogeochemical cycling','Biomagnification','Biodeterioration','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381012,'biology','The UN Conference of Parties on climate change in the year 2011 was held in \n(NEET 2015 Cancelled)','South Africa','The UN Conference of Parties on climate change in the year 2011 was held in South Africa.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Peru','Qatar','Poland','South Africa','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381013,'biology','Which of the following is not one of the prime health risks associated with greater UV radiations through the atmosphere due to depletion of stratospheric ozone \n(NEET 2015 Cancelled)','Increased liver cancer','Ultraviolet radiations are of three types- UV-C (100 - 200 nm), UV-B (280 - 320nm) and UV-A (320 - 390nm) sorter ultraviolet radiations you VC are absorbed by atmosphere the longer ones you\'ve a are not much harmful the intermediate or UV B are harmful as well as capable of DNA damage and mutation; UV radiation of wavelengths shorter than UV-B, are almost completely absorbed by Earth’s atmosphere, given that the ozone layer is intact. But, UV-B damages DNA and mutation may occur. It causes aging of skin, damage to skin cells and various types of skin cancers. In human eye, cornea absorbs UV-B radiation, and a high dose of UV-B causes inflammation of cornea, called snow-blindness, cataract, etc. Such exposure may permanently damage the cornea.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Damage to eyes','Increased liver cancer','Increased skin cancer','Reduced immune system','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381014,'biology','High value of BOD (Biochemical O₂ Demand) indicates that \n(NEET 2015 Cancelled)','Both Consumption of organic matter in the water is higher by the microbes and Water is highly polluted.','Biochemical O₂ Demand or BOD is the O₂ required for microbial decomposition of a unit mass of organic remains. The °of impurity of water due to organic matter is measured in terms of BOD. A higher BOD of a river indicates that water is highly polluted.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Water is less polluted','Consumption of organic matter in the water is higher by the microbes','Water is highly polluted','Both Consumption of organic matter in the water is higher by the microbes and Water is highly polluted.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381015,'biology','Rachel Carson\'s famous book "Silent Spring" is related to \n(NEET 2015 Cancelled)','Pesticide Pollution','Rachel Carson\'s famous book "Silent Spring" is related to pesticide pollution','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Population explosion','Ecosystem management','Pesticide Pollution','Noise Pollution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(381016,'biology','A location with luxuriant growth of the lichens on the tree indicates that the \n(NEET 2014)','Location is not polluted','Lichens are very sensitive of SO₂ pollution. They are completely destroyed at places where there is SO₂ pollution in atmosphere. Therefore they act as very good indicator of SO₂ pollution.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Trees are very healthy','Trees are heavily infested','Location is highly polluted','Location is not polluted','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381017,'biology','The zone of atmosphere in which ozone layer is present is called \n(NEET 2014)','Stratosphere','Ozone Layer or shield is present in stratosphere. It functions at the shield against strong UV radiations coming from sun. UV radiations are harmful and may cause mutations in living organisms. Thining of Ozone layer increases the amount of UV radiations reaching the earth. It would increase occurrence of cataract, skin cancers, dimming of eye sight, photobuming, deficient functioning of immune system etc.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Ionosphere','Mesosphere','Stratosphere','Troposephere','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381018,'biology','The scrubber in the exhaust of a chemical industrial plant removes \n(NEET 2014)','Gases like sulphur dioxide','Dust separation is carried out by scrubbers. They are of two types dry and wet. Both can be used to separate particulate matter by passing through dry or wet packing material but more commonly they are employed by removing gaseous pollutants like SO₂.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Gases like sulphur dioxide','Particulate matter of size 5 micrometers or above','Gases like ozone and methane','Particulate matter of size 2.5 micrometers or less','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381019,'biology','Kyoto protocol was endorsed at \n(NEET 2013)','CoP - 3','Kyoto Protocol is an international Treaty to reduce emission of greenhouse gases which was adopted at the third session of conference of Parties (CoP - 3) to the UNFCCC (United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change) in 1997, in Tokyo, Japan.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','CoP - 6','CoP - 4','CoP - 3','CoP - 5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381020,'biology','Global Warming can be controlled by \n[NEET 2013]','Reducing deforestation, cutting down use of fossil fuel.','Global Warming can be controlled by reducing deforestation, cutting down use of fossil fuel. Reducing deforestation, cutting down the use of fossil fuel.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Increasing deforestation, slowing down the growth of human population','Increasing deforestation, reducing efficiency of energy usage','Reducing deforestation, cutting down use of fossil fuel.','Reducing reforestation, increasing the use of fossil fuel','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381021,'biology','The Air Prevention and Control of Pollution Act came into force in \n(NEET 2013)','1981','The Air Prevention and Control of Pollution Act came into force in 1981.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','1985','1990','1975','1981','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381022,'biology','The second commitment period for Kyoto Protocol was decided at \n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Doha','International conference held in Kyoto, Japan obtained commitments from different countries for reducing overall Greenhouse gas emissions at level 5 % below than that in 1990 by 2008-2012. \nIn Doha, Qatar on 8th December 2012 the "Doha amendment the Kyoto protocol was adopted. The second commitment period is from January 1st 2013 to 31st December 2020.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Durban','Bali','Doha','Cancum','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110712,'biology','Neighbouring cells are connected through __________ that extend through plasmodesmata','Cytoplasmic strands','Neighbouring cells are connected through cytoplasmic strands that extend through plasmodesmata.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 185','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Cytoplasmic strands','Chromatin interconnection','Cell membrane extends','Cell organelles','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381023,'biology','The climate of the world is threatened by\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','Increasing amount of atmospheric  CO₂','CO₂  is a Greenhouse gas with warming effect of 60%. Greenhouse gases are essential for keeping the earthworm and hospitable. They prevent a sustainable part of longwave radiation emitted by Earth to escape into space and radiate a part of this energy back to the earth. The phenomenon is called greenhouse flux. Because of greenhouse flux, the mean annual temperature of the earth 15 °Celsius. Recently the concentration of greenhouse gases have started rising that is resulting in increasing the mean Global temperatur. It is called global warming. Deforestation has reduced  CO₂  assimilation. The excess remains in the air. Excessive use of fossil fuel is adding more CO₂ to atmosphere. This causes greenhouse effect.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Decreasing amount of atmospheric CO₂','Increasing amount of atmospheric  CO₂','Decreasing amount of atmospheric carbon monoxide','Increasing concentration of atmospheric oxygen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381024,'biology','Which one of the following is not correct with regard to the harmful effects of particulate matter of the size 2.5 micrometers or less? \n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','It can directly enter into our circulatory system','Particulate matter consists of soot, flyash, dust spores, pollen grains etc. Particulate matter is differentiated into settleable (larger than 10 mm and remaining in the air for less than one day) and suspended (less than 10 mm and remaining in the air for more than one day to several weeks). Particles of 2.5μm and lesser diameter (PM 2.5) are the most harmful to human health (as %ral pollution control board or CPCB). They pass Deep into the lungs causing breathing and respiratory problems, irritation, inflammation and damage to lungs resulting in premature death. It cannot directly enter the circulatory system but indirectly through respiratory system.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','It can cause respiratory problems','It can directly enter into our circulatory system','It can cause inflammation and damage to the lungs','It can be inhaled into the lungs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381101,'biology','World Ozone Day is celebrated on \n(NEET 2018)','16th September','World Ozone Day is celebrated on 16th April to promote the awareness of ozone and its depletion in people.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','5th June','21st April','16th September','22nd April','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381102,'biology','In stratosphere, which of the following elements acts as a catalyst in degradation of ozone and release of molecular oxygen? \n(NEET 2018)','Cl','UV rays act on chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) releasing active chlorine (Cl, ClO) which further reacts with ozone in sequential manner thereby converting it into oxygen.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','He','Cl','Fe','Xe','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381103,'biology','Which of the following methods is the most suitable for disposal of nuclear waste? [NEET 2019]','Bury the waste within rocks deep below earth\'s surface','Burying the waste within rocks deep below earth\'s surface is most suitable for disposal of nuclear waste.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Bury the waste within rocks deep below earth\'s surface','Shoot the waste into space','Bury the waste under Antarctic ice-cover','Dump the waste within rocks under ocean','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381104,'biology','On the basis of given Assertion(A) and Reason(R), choose the correct option \n(AIIMS 2015)\n\nAssertion(A): BOD of a river polluted by sewage is more than 20 ppm. \n\nReason(R): Polluted river contains excess of organic matter.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Degree of impurity of water due to organic matter is measured in terms of BOD. BOD is the O₂ in milligrams required for five days in one litre of water at 20°C for the microorganisms to metabolise organic waste. It is measured in the milligrams of O₂ taken up in one litre of sewage. The effluent that is legally allowed to be discharged into a river or stream should be such that BOD at 20°C should not be more than 20 ppm i.e., 20 mg per litre.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381105,'biology','Dobson units are used to measure thickness of:\n[NEET 2021]','Ozone','Dobson units are used to measure the thickness of ozone','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Troposphere','CFCs','Stratosphere','Ozone','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381106,'biology','The device which can remove particulate matter present in the exhaust from a thermal power plant is :\n(NEET 2022)','Electrostatic Precipitator','Electrostatic precipitator can remove over 99% particulate matter present in the exhaust from a thermal power plant. Catalytic converters are fitted into automobiles for reducing emission of poisonous gases. STPs are associated with sewage treatment.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','STP','Incinerator','Electrostatic Precipitator','Catalytic Convertor','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381107,'biology','Which one of the following will accelerate phosphorus cycle?\n(NEET 2022)','Weathering of rocks','Phosphorus cycle is a sedimentary cycle. Reservoir pool of phosphorus in ecosystem is the earth’s crust or lithosphere. Weathering of rocks accelerate phosphorus cycle.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Burning of fossil fuels','Volcanic activity','Weathering of rocks','Rain fall and storms','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381108,'biology','The entire fleet of buses in Delhi were converted to CNG from diesel. In Reference   to this, which one of the following statements is false?\n(NEET 2022)','The same diesel engine is used in CNG buses making the cost of conversion low','CNG is cheaper then petrol and it burns more efficiently unlike petrol or diesel. It also cannot be adulterated like diesel and petrol. The same diesel engine cannot be used in CNG buses for making the cost conversion low','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','CNG burns more efficiently than diesel','The same diesel engine is used in CNG buses making the cost of conversion low','It is cheaper than diesel','It cannot be adulterated like diesel','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381109,'biology','In-situ conservation refers to:\n(NEET 2022)','conserve and protect the whole ecosystem','When we conserve and protect the whole ecosystem, its biodiversity at all levels is protected. This is in-situ or on site conservation strategy','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','conserve and protect the whole ecosystem','Conserve only high-risk species','Conserve only endangered species','Conserve only extinct species','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381110,'biology','Detritivores breakdown detritus into smaller particles. This process is called:\n(NEET 2022)','Fragmentation','Detritivores (eg. earthworm) break down detritus into smaller particles. This process is called fragmentation','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Catabolism','Fragmentation','Humification','Decomposition','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381111,'biology','Given below are two statements: \n\nStatements I : In a scrubber the exhaust from the thermal plant is passed through the electric wires to charge the dust particles. \n\nStatement II : Particulate matter (PM 2.5) cannot be removed by scrubber but can be removed by an electrostatic precipitator.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :\n\n(NEET 2022)','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','Scrubber is used by the industries which produce SO₂ as a by product. The limestone present in slurry of scrubber remove SO₂ from the exhaust. Electrostatic precipitator is the most effective device to remove 99% of particulate matter, ‘even PM 2.5’ present in the exhaust.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381112,'biology','What will happen if fresh water lake gets contaminated by addition of polluted water with high BOD?\n(1) Number of submerged aquatic plants in the lake will increase\n(2) Number of aquatic animals in the lake will increase\n(3) Amount of dissolved oxygen in the lake will decrease\n(4) The lake will remain unaffected [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','3','Higher BOD of water indicates higher amount of biodegradable organic materials present in it. Since BOD is inversely proportional to the dissolved oxygen (DO), therefore, the dissolved oxygen in the lake will decrease','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381113,'biology','Given below are two statements:\nStatement I:Electrostatic precipitator is most widely used in thermal power plant .\nStatement II: Electrostatic precipitator in thermal power plant removes ionising radiations.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below: [NEET 2023]','(2) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.','Electrostatic precipitators are indeed widely used in thermal power plants to remove particulate matter (dust and other particles) from the flue gases before they are released into the atmosphere. Statement II is incorrect. Electrostatic precipitators are not used to remove ionizing radiations.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','(2) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.','(3) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381114,'biology','Match List Iwith List II .\nList I(Cells)-  List II(Secretion)\n\nA. Peptic cells - I. Mucus\nB. Goblet cells - II. Bile juice\nC. Oxyntic cells - III. Proenzyme pepsinogen\nD. Hepatic cells - IV. HCl and intrinsic factor for absorption of vitamin B12\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(2) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV','(2) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','(3) A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','(4) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381115,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \nList-I List-II\n(A) Deforestation (I) Responsible for heating of Earth\'s surface and atmosphere \n(B) Reforestation (II) Conversion of forested areas to non-forested areas \n(C) Green-house effect (III) Natural ageing of lake by nutrient enrichment of its water \n(D) Eutrophication (IV) Process of restoring a forest that once existed but was removed \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(4) A-(II), B-(IV), C-(I), D-(III)','A-(II), B-(IV), C-(I), D-(III)','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) A-(IV), B-(III), C-(II), D-(I)','(2) A-(I), B-(II), C-(III), D-(IV)','(3) A-(III), B-(I), C-(II), D-(IV)','(4) A-(II), B-(IV), C-(I), D-(III)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381116,'biology','Consider the following statements (A-D) about organic farming\n\nA. Utilizes genetically modified crops like Bt cotton\n\nB. Uses only naturally produced inputs like compost\n\nC. Does not use pesticides and urea\n\nD.Produces vegetables rich in vitamins and minerals\n\nWhich of the above statements is correct?','(B) and (C) only','(B) and (C) only','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(A) and (B) only','(B), (C) and (D)','(C) and (D) only','(B) and (C) only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381117,'biology','If an agricultural field is liberally irrigated for a prolonged period of time, it is likely to face the problem of:','Salinity','PAGE 283, XII NCERT','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Metal Toxicity','Alkalinity','Acidity','Salinity','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381201,'biology','Select the correct statements from the options given below','The need of the hour is to check the degradation and depletion of our precious natural resources and pollution without halting the process of development.','Human population size has grown enormously over the last hundred years. This means increase in demand for food, water, home, electricity, roads, automobiles and numerous other commodities. These demands are exerting tremendous pressure on our natural resources, and are also contributing to pollution of air, water and soil. The need of the hour is to check the degradation and depletion of our precious natural resources and pollution without halting the process of development. \n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 270','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Human population size has not grown significatly over the last hundred years','With increase in population, there is a decrease in demand for food, water, home, electricity, roads, automobiles and numerous other commodities.','There is decrease in demands and decrease in pressure on our natural resources, and are also contributing to prevention pollution of air, water and soil.','The need of the hour is to check the degradation and depletion of our precious natural resources and pollution without halting the process of development.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381202,'biology','Assertion: The Government of India has passed the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986\n\nReason: With increasing pollution, population and depletion of natural resources,the act was passed as a stategy of to control environmental\npollution,','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','In order to control environmental pollution, the Government of India has passed the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 to protect and improve the quality of our environment (air, water and soil).NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 270','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110713,'biology','How is symplast movement done ?','Intercellular movement is through the plasmodesmata','During symplastic movement, the water travels through the cells – their cytoplasm; intercellular movement is through the plasmodesmata.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 185','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','The water travels through the cell wall','Intercellular movement is through the plasmodesmata','Both a and b','Through cell membrane','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381203,'biology','Assertion: The mortality rate is high in areas with bad air quality and decrease crop yield \n\nReason: We are dependant on environment for respiration and breathing','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','We are dependent on air for our respiratory needs. Air pollutants cause injury to all living organisms. They reduce growth and yield of crops and cause premature death of plants. Air pollutants also deleteriously affect the respiratory system of humans and of animals. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 270','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381204,'biology','Select the correct statements from the options given below','The electrostatic precipitator can remove over 99 % particulate matter present in the exhaust from a\nthermal power plant.','Harmful effects depend on the concentration of pollutants, duration of exposure and the organism. Smokestacks of thermal power plants, smelters and other industries release particulate and gaseous air pollutants together with harmless gases, such as nitrogen, oxygen, etc. These pollutants must be separated filtered out before releasing the harmless gases into the atmosphere. There are several ways of removing particulate matter; the most widely used of which is the electrostatic precipitator, which can\nremove over 99 % particulate matter present in the exhaust from a thermal power plant.  NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 271','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Useful effects depend on the concentration of pollutants, duration of exposure and the organism.','Smokestacks of thermal power plants, smelters and other industries never release particulate matter and gaseous air pollutants','The electrostatic precipitator can remove over 99 % particulate matter present in the exhaust from a\nthermal power plant.','There are several ways of removing particulate matter; the least widely used of which is the electrostatic precipitator','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381205,'biology','Select the correct statements regarding particulate matter and its management from the options given below','Particulate matter that are very very small and are not removed by these precipitators are more harmful.','The Electrostatic precipitator has electrode wires that are maintained at several thousand volts, which produce a corona that releases electrons. These electrons attach to dust particles giving them a net negative charge. Thecollecting plates are grounded and attract the charged dust particles.\nThe velocity of air between the plates must be low enough to allow the dust to fall. A scrubber can remove gases like sulphur dioxide. In a scrubber, the exhaust is passed through a spray of water or lime. Recently we have realised the dangers of particulate matter that are very very small and are not removed by these precipitators.precipitators. According to Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB), particulate size 2.5 micrometers or less in diameter (PM 2.5) are responsible for causing the greatest harm to human health. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 271','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The Electrostatic precipitator has electrode wires that are maintained at several thousand volts, which produce a corona that releases protons.','The protons attach to dust particles giving them a net positive charge and the collecting plates are grounded to attract the charged dust particles','The velocity of air between the plates must be high enough to allow the dust to fall and the scrubber removes only CO₂  by passing through lime','Particulate matter that are very very small and are not removed by these precipitators are more harmful.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381206,'biology','Assertion: Particulate size 2.5 micrometers or less in diameter (PM 2.5) are responsible for causing the greatest harm to human health\n\nReason:  These fine particulates cannot be inhaled into the lungs and are expired out.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','According to Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB), particulate size 2.5 micrometers or less in diameter (PM 2.5) are responsible for causing the greatest harm to human health. These fine particulates can be inhaled deep into the lungs and can cause breathing and respiratory symptoms, irritation, inflammations and damage to the lungs and premature deaths. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 271','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381207,'biology','Select the correct statements regarding Air pollution from the options given below:','As the exhaust passes through the catalytic converter, unburnt hydrocarbons are converted into  CO₂  and water, and carbon monoxide and nitric oxide are changed to  CO₂  and nitrogen gas, respectively.','Automobiles are a major cause for atmospheric pollution atleast in the metro cities. As the number of vehicles increase on the streets, this problem is now shifting to the other cities too. Proper maintenance of automobiles along with use of lead-free petrol or diesel can reduce the pollutants they emit. Catalytic converters, having expensive metals namely platinum-palladium and rhodium as the catalysts, are fitted into automobiles for reducing emission of poisonous gases. As the exhaust passes through the catalytic converter, unburnt hydrocarbons are converted into  CO₂  and water, and carbon monoxide and nitric oxide are changed to  CO₂  and nitrogen gas, respectively.   NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 272','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Automobiles are a minor cause for atmospheric pollution atleast in the metro cities','Proper maintenance of automobiles along with use of lead-filled petrol or diesel can reduce the pollutants they emit.','Catalytic converters, having cheap metals namely platinum-palladium and rhodium as the catalysts, are fitted into automobiles for reducing emission of poisonous gases.','As the exhaust passes through the catalytic converter, unburnt hydrocarbons are converted into  CO₂  and water, and carbon monoxide and nitric oxide are changed to  CO₂  and nitrogen gas, respectively.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381208,'biology','Assertion: Motor vehicles equipped with catalytic converter should use unleaded petrol \n\nReason: Lead in the petrol inactivates the catalyst present in catalytic converter','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Motor vehicles equipped with catalytic converter should use unleaded petrol because lead in the petrol inactivates the catalyst.NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 272','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381209,'biology','Select the correct statements from the options given below','Noise is undesired high level of sound. We have got used to associating loud sounds with pleasure and entertainment not realising that noise causes psychological and physiological disorders in humans.','In India, the Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act came into force in 1981, but was amended in 1987 to include noise as an air pollutant. Noise is undesired high level of sound. We have got used to associating loud sounds with pleasure and entertainment not realising that noise causes psychological and physiological disorders in humans. The bigger the city, the bigger the function, the greater the noise!! A brief exposure to extremely high sound level, 150 dB or more generated by take off of a jet plane or rocket, may damage ear drums thus permanently impairing hearing ability.NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 272','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','In India, the Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act came into force in 1987, but was amended in 1981 to include noise as an air pollutant.','Noise is undesired high level of sound. We have got used to associating loud sounds with pleasure and entertainment not realising that noise causes psychological and physiological disorders in humans.','The bigger the city, the bigger the function, the lesser the noise!! A brief exposure to extremely high sound level,','200 dB or less generated by take off of a jet plane or rocket, may damage ear drums thus permanently impairing hearing ability.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381210,'biology','Select the incorrect statement regarding noise pollution from the options given below','Even chronic exposure to a relatively lower noise level of cities may temporarily damage hearing abilities of humans.','Even chronic exposure to a relatively lower noise level of cities may permanently damage hearing abilities of humans. Noise also causes sleeplessness, increased heart beat, altered breathing pattern, thus considerably stressing humans. Considering the many dangerous effects of noise pollution can you identify the unnecessary sources of noise pollution around you which can be reduced immediately without any financial loss to anybody?\nReduction of noise in our industries can be affected by use of sound- absorbent materials or by muffling noise.Stringent following of laws laid down in relation to noise like delimitation of horn-free zones around hospitals and schools, permissible sound-levels of crackers and of loud- speakers, timings after which loudspeakers cannot be played, etc., need to be enforced to protect ourselves from noise pollution.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Even chronic exposure to a relatively lower noise level of cities may temporarily damage hearing abilities of humans.','Noise also causes sleeplessness, increased heart beat, altered breathing pattern, thus considerably stressing humans.','Reduction of noise in our industries can be affected by use of sound- absorbent materials or by muffling noise.','Stringent following of laws laid down in relation to noise like delimitation of horn-free zones around hospitals and schools, need to be enforced to protect ourselves from noise pollution.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381211,'biology','Select the correct statement regarding control of vehicular pollution in India from the options given below','Air pollution problems in Delhi became so serious that a public interest litigation (PIL) was filed in the Supreme Court of India.','Delhi leads the country in its levels of air-pollution – it has more cars than the states of Gujarat and West Bengal put together. In the 1990s, Delhi ranked fourth among the 41 most polluted cities of the world. Air pollution problems in Delhi became so serious that a public interest litigation (PIL) was filed in the Supreme Court of India. After being censured very strongly by the Supreme Court, under its directives, the government was asked to take, within a specified time period, appropriate measures, including switching over the entire fleet of public transport, NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 272','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Kolkata leads the country in its levels of air-pollution – it has more cars than the states of Gujarat and UP put together','Delhi ranked fourth among the 50 most polluted cities of the world.','Air pollution problems in Delhi became so serious that a public interest litigation (PIL) was filed in the Supreme Court of India.','After being censured very strongly by the Supreme Court, under its directives, the government was asked to take, within a specified time period, appropriate measures, including switching over the entire fleet of private transport,','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381212,'biology','Assertion: The government had to switch over the entire fleet of public buses, from diesel to compressed natural gas (CNG).\n\nReason: Air pollution problems in Delhi became so serious that a public interest litigation (PIL) was filed in the Supreme Court of India and measures were taken under its directive','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Air pollution problems in Delhi became so serious that a public interest litigation (PIL) was filed in the Supreme Court of India. After being censured very strongly by the Supreme Court, under its directives, the government was asked to take, within a specified time period, appropriate measures, including switching over the entire fleet of public transport, i.e., buses, from diesel to compressed natural gas (CNG).NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 272','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381213,'biology','Assertion: CNG is a more efficient fuel than petrol\n\nReason: CNG is cheap and burns efficiently, unlike petrol or diesel, in the automobiles and very little of it is left unburnt , but more chances of being adulterated','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','CNG burns most efficiently, unlike petrol or diesel, in the automobiles and very little of it is left unburnt. Moreover, CNG is cheaper than petrol or diesel, cannot be siphoned off by thieves and adulterated like petrol or diesel.NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 273','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(70704,'chemistry','Choose the correct option --> \nI. A bare proton is very reactive and  creates bond to the oxygen atom of a solvent water molecule to give hydronium ion. \nII. Hydronium ion has trigonal planar geometry.','I is true but II is false','A bare proton, H⁺ is very reactive and cannot exist freely in aqueous solutions. Thus, it bonds to the oxygen atom of a solvent water molecule to give trigonal pyramidal hydronium ion.\n (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 214 ; paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Acids, Bases and Salts',NULL,'2','mcq','Both are true','Both are false','II is true but I is false','I is true but II is false','NEW','11-214','11-190',NULL),
(381214,'biology','Select the correct options regarding measures taken to control Air pollution','Parallel steps were taken in Delhi for reducing vehicular pollution include phasing out of old vehicles, use of unleaded petrol, use of low-sulphur petrol and diesel, use of catalytic converters in vehicles, application of stringent pollution-level norms for vehicles.','The main problem with switching over to CNG is the difficulty of laying down pipelines to deliver CNG through distribution points/pumps and ensuring uninterrupted supply. Simultaneously parallel steps taken in Delhi for reducing vehicular pollution include phasing out of old vehicles, use of unleaded petrol, use of low-sulphur petrol and diesel, use of catalytic converters in vehicles, application of stringent pollution-level norms for vehicles, etc.\nThe Government of India through a new auto fuel policy has laid out a roadmap to cut down vehicular pollution in Indian cities. More stringent norms for fuels means steadily reducing the sulphur and aromatic content in petrol and diesel fuels.NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 273','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The Government of India through a new auto fuel policy has laid out a roadmap to bring up vehicular pollution in Indian cities','The main problem with switching over from CNG to petrol is the difficulty of laying down pipelines to deliver CNG through distribution points/pumps and ensuring uninterrupted supply','Parallel steps were taken in Delhi for reducing vehicular pollution include phasing out of old vehicles, use of unleaded petrol, use of low-sulphur petrol and diesel, use of catalytic converters in vehicles, application of stringent pollution-level norms for vehicles.','More stringent norms for fuels means steadily raising the sulphur and aromatic content in petrol and diesel fuels.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381215,'biology','Select the correct options regarding measures taken to control Air pollution','The goal, according to the roadmap, is to reduce sulphur to 50 ppm in petrol and diesel and bring down the level to 35 %.','Euro III norms, for example, stipulate that sulphur be controlled at 350 parts-per-million (ppm) in diesel and 150 ppm in petrol. Aromatic hydrocarbons are to be contained at 42 % of the concerned fuel. The goal, according to the roadmap, is to reduce sulphur to 50 ppm in petrol and diesel and bring down the level to 35 %. Corresponding to the fuel, vehicle engines will also need to be upgraded. Mass Emission Standards (Bharat Stage II which is equivalent to Euro-II norms) are no more applicable in any of the cities of India.\nNCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 273','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Euro II norms stipulate that sulphur be controlled at 350 parts-per-million (ppm) in diesel and 150 ppm in petrol.  Aromatic hydrocarbons are to be contained at 42 % of the concerned fuel.','The goal, according to the roadmap, is to reduce sulphur to 50 ppm in petrol and diesel and bring down the level to 35 %.','Corresponding to the fuel, vehicle engines need not to be upgraded.','Mass Emission Standards (Bharat Stage II which is equivalent to Euro-II norms) are applied in all of the cities of India.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381216,'biology','Select the correct options regarding measures taken to control Air pollution','According to an estimate, a substantial fall in CO₂  and sulphurdioxide level has been found in Delhi between 1997 and 2005.','Bharat stage IV norms have been applied to 4 wheelers, 3 wheelers and 2 wheelers throughout the country since April 2017. Thanks to the efforts made, the air quality of Delhi has significantly\nimproved. According to an estimate, a substantial fall in CO₂  and SO₂ level has been found in Delhi between 1997 and 2005.\nAll the buses of Delhi were converted to run on CNG by the end of 2002\nNCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 273','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Bharat stage IV norms have been applied to 4 wheelers, 3 wheelers and 2 wheelers throughout the country since  June 2017','Thanks to the efforts made for controlling Air pollution, the air quality of Delhi has significantly deproved.','According to an estimate, a substantial fall in CO₂  and sulphurdioxide level has been found in Delhi between 1997 and 2005.','All the buses of Delhi were converted to run on CNG by the end of 2012','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381217,'biology','Select the correct options regarding Water pollution and its control','The Government of India has passed the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 to safeguard our water resources','Realising the importance of maintaining the cleanliness of the water bodies, the Government of India has passed the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 to safeguard our water resources A mere 0.1 % impurities make domestic sewage unfit for human use. Solids are\nrelatively easy to remove, what is most difficult to remove are dissolved salts such as nitrates, phosphates, and other nutrients, and toxic metal ions and organic compounds.  Domestic sewage primarily contains biodegradable organic matter, which readily decomposes –thanks to bacteria and other micro-organisms, NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 274','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The Government of India has passed the Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 to safeguard our water resources','A mere 1 % % impurities make domestic sewage unfit for human use','Solids are relatively difficult to remove, what is easy to remove are dissolved salts such as nitrates, phosphates, and other nutrients, and toxic metal ions and organic compounds','Domestic sewage primarily contains non-biodegradable inorganic matter, which cannot be decomposed by bacteria','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381218,'biology','Assertion:  Domestic sewage primarily contains biodegradable organic matter, which readily decomposes ,thanks to bacteria and other micro-organisms,\n\nReason: Bacteria can multiply using these organic substances as substrates and hence utilise some of the components of sewage.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Domestic sewage primarily contains biodegradable organic matter, which readily decomposes –thanks to bacteria and other micro-organisms, which can multiply using these organic substances as substrates and hence utilise some of the components of sewage. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 275','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381219,'biology','Which of the following is incorrect regarding impurities found in water','Dissolved materials do not include nitrates, ammonia,phosphate,calcium and sodium','Impurities constituting a mere 0.1%, make water unsuitable for domestic purposes;\nSuspended solids include sand, silt and clay;\nColloidal material includes fecal matter, clothes and paper material with bacteria;\nDissolved materials includes nitrates, ammonia,phosphate,calcium and sodium;NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 274','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Impurities constituting a mere 0.1%, make water unsuitable for domestic purposes','Suspended solids include sand, silt and clay','Colloidal material includes fecal matter, clothes and paper material with bacteria','Dissolved materials do not include nitrates, ammonia,phosphate,calcium and sodium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381220,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding impurities found in water','Algal bloom imparts a distinct colour to the water bodies due to excessive planktonic growth','Micro-organisms involved in biodegradation of organic matter in the receiving water body consume a lot of oxygen, and as a result there is a sharp decline in dissolved O₂ downstream from the point of sewage discharge. This causes mortality of fish(in other words increases the mortality of fish) and other aquatic creatures. Presence of large amounts of nutrients in waters also causes excessive growth of planktonic (free-floating) algae, called an algal bloom which imparts a distinct colour to the water bodies; NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 275','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Micro-organisms involved in biodegradation of organic matter in the receiving water body consume a lot of CO₂','There is a sharp rise in dissolved O₂ downstream from the point of sewage discharge','Increase in BOD, decreases the mortality of aquatic life','Algal bloom imparts a distinct colour to the water bodies due to excessive planktonic growth','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381221,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding impurities found in water','Water Hyacinth is called the "Terror of Bengal"','Algal blooms cause deterioration of the water quality and fish mortality. Some bloom-forming algae are extremely toxic to human beings and animals. You may have seen the beautiful mauve-colored flowers found on very appealingly-shaped floating plants in water bodies. These plants which were introduced into India for their lovely flowers have caused havoc by their excessive growth by causing blocks in our waterways. They grow faster than our ability to remove them. These are plants of water hyacinth (Eichhornia crassipes), the world’s most problematic aquatic weed, also called ‘Terror of Bengal, NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 275','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Algal blooms causes enhancement of the water quality and fish mortality','Water Hyacinth were introduced into India for their lovely flowers have caused enhancement in purity by their excessive growth and relieving blocks caused by impurities','The water hyacinth (Eichhornia indica) grows faster than our ability to remove them and is the world’s most problematic aquatic weed,','Water Hyacinth is called the "Terror of Bengal"','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381222,'biology','Assertion: Water Hyacinth  (Eichhornia crassipes) is the most problematic weed in the world\n\nReason: It grows faster than our ability to remove them and causes havoc by their excessive growth blocking our waterways.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','The water hyacinth (Eichhornia crassipes) grows faster than our ability to remove them and is the world’s most problematic aquatic weed, These plants which were introduced into India for their lovely flowers have caused havoc by their excessive growth by causing blocks in our waterways. They grow faster than our ability to remove them. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 275','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381223,'biology','Select the correct option regarding Domestic Waste and industrial effluents','A few toxic substances, often present in industrial waste waters, can undergo Biomagnification in the aquatic food chain','Sewage from our homes as well as from hospitals are likely to contain many undesirable pathogenic microorganisms, and its disposal into a water without proper treatment may cause outbreak of serious diseases, such as, dysentery, typhoid, jaundice, cholera, etc. Unlike domestic sewage, waste water from industries like petroleum, paper manufacturing, metal extraction and processing, chemical manufacturing, etc., often contain toxic substances, notably, heavy metals (defined as elements with density > 5 g/cm3 such as mercury, cadmium, copper, lead, etc.) and a variety of organic compounds.\nA few toxic substances, often present in industrial waste waters, can undergo biological magnification (Biomagnification) in the aquatic food chain. Biomagnification refers to increase in concentration of the toxicant at successive trophic levels. This happens because a toxic substance accumulated by an organism cannot be metabolised or excreted, and is thus passed on to the next higher trophic level. This phenomenon is wellknown for mercury and DDT. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 276','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sewage from our homes as well as from hospitals are less likely to contain pathogenic microorganisms, and their disposal into a water without proper treatment is safe','Domestic waste from homes often contain toxic substances, notably, heavy metals (defined as elements with density > 5 g/cc such as mercury, cadmium, copper, lead, etc.) and a variety of organic compounds.','A few toxic substances, often present in industrial waste waters, can undergo Biomagnification in the aquatic food chain','Biofortification refers to increase in concentration of the toxicant at sucessive trophic levels','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381224,'biology','Assertion: In Biomagnification,there is an increase in concentration of the toxicant at successive trophic levels. \n\nReason: The toxic substance accumulated by an organism cannot be metabolised or excreted, gets passed on to the lower trophic level.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Biomagnification refers to increase in concentration of the toxicant at successive trophic levels. This happens because a toxic substance accumulated by an organism cannot be metabolised or excreted, and is thus passed on to the next higher trophic level. This phenomenon is wellknown for mercury and DDT. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 276','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381225,'biology','Select the correct option regarding Domestic Waste and industrial effluents','In accelerated eutrophication, the natural aging of a lake may be accelerated by pollutants from man’s activities like effluents from the industries and homes.','Eutrophication is the natural aging of a lake by nutrient enrichment of its water. In a young lake the water is cold and clear, supporting little life. With time, streams draining into the lake introduce nutrients such as nitrogen and phosphorus, which encourage the growth of aquatic organisms. As the lake’s fertility increases, plant and animal life burgeons, and organic remains begin to be deposited on the lake bottom. Over the centuries, as silt and organic debris pile up, the lake grows shallower and warmer, with warm-water organisms supplanting those that thrive in a cold environment. Marsh plants take root in the shallows and begin to fill in the original lake basin. Eventually, the lake gives way to large masses of floating plants (bog), finally converting into land. Depending on climate, size of the lake and other factors, the natural aging of a lake may span thousands of years. However, pollutants from man’s activities like effluents from the industries and homes can radically accelerate the aging process. This phenomenon has been called Cultural or Accelerated Eutrophication. During the past century, lakes in many parts of the earth have been severely eutrophied by sewage and agricultural and industrial wastes. The prime contaminants are nitrates and phosphates, which act as plant nutrients. They overstimulate the growth of algae, causing unsightly scum and unpleasant odours, and robbing the water of dissolved O₂ vital to other aquatic life. At the same time, other pollutants flowing into a lake may poison whole populations of fish, whose decomposing remains further deplete the water’s dissolved O₂ content. In such fashion, a lake can literally choke to death. Heated (thermal) wastewaters flowing out of electricity-generating units, e.g., thermal power plants, constitute another important category of pollutants. Thermal waste water eliminates or reduces the number of organisms sensitive to high temperature, and may enhance the growth of plants and fish in extremely cold areas but, only after causing damage to the indigenous flora and fauna. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 276 and 277','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Eutrophication is the artificial aging of a lake by nutrient enrichment of its water by introducing nutrients such as nitrogen and phosphorus, which encourage the growth of aquatic organisms.','As the lake’s fertility increases,the quality of water improves and impurities keep decreasing','In accelerated eutrophication, the natural aging of a lake may be accelerated by pollutants from man’s activities like effluents from the industries and homes.','Heated (thermal) wastewaters flowing out of electricity-generating units, constitute another important category of pollutants. Thermal waste water reduces the number of organisms sensitive to high temperature,enhancing the growth of plants and fish in extremely cold areas  causing damage to the indigenous flora and fauna.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381226,'biology','Assertion: DDT causes decline in Bird populations\n\nReason: DDT interferes with Na metabolism and causes the thinning of eggshells and premature breaking of eggshells','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','High concentrations of DDT disturb calcium metabolism in birds, which causes thinning of eggshell and their premature breaking, eventually causing decline in bird populations. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 276','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381227,'biology','Assertion: As the fertility of lake increases, the impurity concentration decreases and water quality improves\n\nReason: As the lake ages, the water becomes cold and clear.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','In a young lake the water is cold and clear, supporting little life. With time, streams draining into the lake introduce nutrients such as nitrogen and phosphorus, which encourage the growth of aquatic organisms. As the lake’s fertility increases, plant and animal life burgeons, and organic remains begin to be deposited on the lake bottom NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 276','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381228,'biology','Assertion: As the lake fertility increases, the lake ages and there is accelerated aging with pollution\n\nReason: The silt and organic deposit in the base and support growth of marshy plants and speedy conversion to land','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','As the lake’s fertility increases, plant and animal life burgeons, and organic remains begin to be deposited on the lake bottom. Over the centuries, as silt and organic debris pile up, the lake grows shallower and warmer, with warm-water organisms supplanting those that thrive in a cold environment. In accelerated eutrophication, the natural aging of a lake may be accelerated by pollutants from man’s activities like effluents from the industries and homes.Over the centuries, as silt and organic debris pile up, the lake grows shallower and warmer, with warm-water organisms supplanting those that thrive in a cold environment. Marsh plants take root in the shallows and begin to fill in the original lake basin. Eventually, the lake gives way to large masses of floating plants (bog), finally converting into land. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 276','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381229,'biology','Assertion: The plant and algal growth is accelerated by industrial effluents as they contain high concentrations of nitrates and phosphates.\n\nReason: They overstimulate growth of algae being prime nutrients, and scum overgrowth with foul smell and accelerated aging by depleting the oxygen','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','The prime contaminants are nitrates and phosphates, which act as plant nutrients. They overstimulate the growth of algae, causing unsightly scum and unpleasant odours, and robbing the water of dissolved O₂ vital to other aquatic life. At the same time, other pollutants flowing into a lake may poison whole populations of fish, whose decomposing remains further deplete the water’s dissolved O₂ content.NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 277','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381230,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding integrated waste management','The cleaning occurs in two stages incliuding  the conventional sedimentation, filtering and chlorine treatments first and six connected marshes over 60 hectares of marshland with biological agents that assimilate the heavy pollutants','Wastewater including sewage can be treated in an integrated manner, by utilising a mix of artificial and natural processes. An example of such an initiative is the town of Arcata, situated along the Northern coast of California. Collaborating with biologists from the Humboldt\nState University, the townspeople created an integrated waste water treatment process within a natural system. The cleaning occurs in two stages – (a) the conventional sedimentation, filtering and chlorine treatments are given. After this stage, lots of dangerous pollutants like dissolved heavy metals still remain. To combat this, an innovative approach was taken and (b) the biologists developed a series of six connected marshes over 60 hectares of marshland. Appropriate plants, algae, fungi and bacteria were seeded into this area, which neutralise, absorb and assimilate the pollutants. Hence, as the water flows through the marshes, it gets purified naturally.The marshes also constitute a sanctuary, with a high level of biodiversity in the form of fishes, animals and birds that now reside there.\nA citizens group called Friends of the Arcata Marsh (FOAM) are responsible for the upkeep and safeguarding of this wonderful project.NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 276 and 277','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Wastewater including sewage cannot be treated in an integrated manner, by utilising a mix of artificial and natural processes.','An integrated wate management was found effective at Arcata, Southern California in association with Harvard University, using natural systems','The cleaning occurs in two stages incliuding  the conventional sedimentation, filtering and chlorine treatments first and six connected marshes over 60 hectares of marshland with biological agents that assimilate the heavy pollutants','However, the biodiversity is less and is managed by a citizen group called FOAM(Federation of Arcata Marsh)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381231,'biology','Assertion: Human excreta can be decomposed by sanitary methods in a sustainable method using less or very minimal or no water method in a sustainable manner.\n\nReason:  The key point is that , human excreta can be recycled into a resource (as natural fertiliser), which reduces the need for chemical fertilisers','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Ecological sanitation is a sustainable system for handling human excreta, using dry composting toilets. This is a practical, hygienic, efficient and cost-effective solution to human waste disposal. The key point to note here is that with this composting method, human excreta can be recycled into a resource (as natural fertiliser), which reduces the need for chemical fertilisers. There are working ‘EcoSan’ toilets in many areas of Kerala and Sri Lanka.NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 277 and 278','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381232,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding Solid Wastes','Sanitary landfills are a substitute for open-burning dumps where wastes are dumped in a depression or trench after compaction, and covered with dirt everyday','Solid wastes refer to everything that goes out in trash. Municipal solid wastes are wastes from homes, offices, stores, schools, hospitals, etc., that are collected and disposed by the municipality. The municipal solid wastes generally comprise paper, food wastes, plastics, glass, metals, rubber, leather, textile, etc. Burning reduces the volume of the wastes, although it is generally not burnt to completion and open dumps often serve as the breeding ground for rats and flies. Sanitary landfills were adopted as the substitute for open-burning dumps. In a sanitary landfill, wastes are dumped in a depression or trench after compaction, and covered with dirt everyday. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 278','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Solid wastes does not  refer to everything that goes out in trash from homes, offices, stores, schools, hospitals, etc.,\nthat are collected and disposed by the municipality.','The industrial effluents generally comprise paper, food wastes, plastics, glass, metals, rubber, leather, textile, etc.','Burning increases the volume of the wastes, because it is not burnt to completion and open dumps often serve as the breeding ground for rats and flies','Sanitary landfills are a substitute for open-burning dumps where wastes are dumped in a depression or trench after compaction, and covered with dirt everyday','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381233,'biology','Assertion: Burning increases the volume of wastes because wastes are not burnt to completion\n\nReason: Burning in open dumps, is protective against infestation of rodents and other vectors','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Burning reduces the volume of the wastes, although it is generally not burnt to completion and open dumps often serve as the breeding ground for rats and flies. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 278','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381234,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding Solid Wastes','The kabadiwallahs and rag-pickers do a great job of separation of materials for recycling','Landfills are also not really much of a\nsolution since the amount of garbage generation specially in the metros\nhas increased so much that these sites are getting filled too. Also there\nis danger of seepage of chemicals, etc., from these landfills polluting the\nunderground water resources.\nA solution to all this can only be in human beings becoming more\nsensitive to these environment issues. All waste that we generate can\nbe categorised into three types – (a) bio-degradable, (b) recyclable and\n(c) the non-biodegradable. It is important that all garbage generated is\nsorted. What can be reused or recycled should be separated out; our\nkabadiwallahs and rag-pickers do a great job of separation of materials\nfor recycling. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 278','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Landfills are a permanent solution since the amount of garbage generation specially in the metros has been decreasing so much that these sites are getting free','There is danger of seepage of chemicals, from these burning pits polluting the underground water resources.','All waste that we generate can be categorised into three types bio-degradable, unbiodegradable and non-biodegradable','The kabadiwallahs and rag-pickers do a great job of separation of materials for recycling','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381235,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding Solid Wastes','We contribute heavily to environmental pollution when we accept grocery in polystyrene bags','The biodegradable materials can be put into deep pits in the ground and be left for natural breakdown. That leaves only the nonbiodegradable to be disposed off. The need to reduce our garbage generation should be a prime goal, instead, we are increasing the use of non-biodegradable products. Just pick any readymade packet of any ‘good quality’ eatable, say a biscuit packet, and study the packaging – do you see the number of protective layers used? Note that atleast one layer is of plastic. We have started packaging even our daily use products like milk and water in polybags!! In cities, fruits and vegetables can be bought packed in beautiful polysterene and plastic packaging – we pay so much and what do we do? Contribute heavily to environmental pollution. State Governments across the country are trying to push for reduction in use of plastics and use of eco-friendly packaging. We can do our bit by carrying cloth or other natural fibre carry-bags when we go shopping and by refusing polythene bags.NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 278','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The non biodegradable materials can be put into deep pits in the ground and be left for natural breakdown.','We contribute heavily to environmental pollution when we accept grocery in polystyrene bags','Central Governments across the country are trying to push for reduction in use of plastics and use of eco-friendly packaging.','We can prevent environmental pollution by carrying polystyrene carry-bags when we go shopping and by natural fibre bags.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381236,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding Solid Wastes','Unlike developed countries, which have specifically built facilities for recycling of e-wastes, recycling in developing countries often involves manual participation and exposure to gtoxic substances in woekplace','Hospitals generate hazardous wastes that contain disinfectants and other harmful chemicals, and also pathogenic micro-organisms. Such wastes also require careful treatment and disposal. The use of incinerators is crucial to disposal of hospital waste.\nIrreparable computers and other electronic goods are known as electronic wastes (e-wastes). E-wastes are burried in landfills or incinerated. Over half of the e-wastes generated in the developed world are exported to developing countries, mainly to China, India and Pakistan, where metals like copper, iron, silicon, nickel and gold are recovered during recycling process. Unlike developed countries, which have specifically built facilities for recycling of e-wastes, recycling in developing countries often involves manual participation thus exposing workers to toxic substances present in e-wastes. Recycling is the only solution for the treatment of e-waste, provided it is carried out in an environment friendly manner.\n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 279','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Industries generate hazardous wastes that contain disinfectants and other harmful chemicals, and also pathogenic micro-organisms and incineration is the best methos of dealing with them','Over half of the e-wastes generated in the developing world are exported to developed countries, mainly to China, India and Pakistan, where metals like copper, iron, silicon, nickel and gold are recovered during recycling process','Reusing is the only solution for the treatment of e-waste, provided it is carried out in an environmentf riendly manner.','Unlike developed countries, which have specifically built facilities for recycling of e-wastes, recycling in developing countries often involves manual participation and exposure to gtoxic substances in woekplace','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381237,'biology','Which of the following is incorrect regarding Plastic Waste Management','In collaboration with GRD College of Technology and the Bangalore City Corporation, Ahmed Khan proved that blends of Polyblend and bitumen, when used to lay roads,  enhanced the bitumen’s water repellant properties, and helped to increase road life by a factor of three.','A plastic sack manufacturer in Bangalore has managed to find the ideal solution to the ever-increasing problem of accumulating plastic waste. Ahmed Khan, aged 57 years old, has been producing plastic sacks for 20 years. About 8 years ago, he realised that plastic waste was a real problem. Polyblend, a fine powder of recycled modified plastic, was developed then by his company. This mixture is mixed with the bitumen that is used to lay roads. In collaboration with R.V.College of Engineering and the Bangalore City Corporation, Ahmed Khan proved that blends of Polyblend and bitumen, when used to lay roads, enhanced the bitumen’s water repellant properties, and helped to increase road life by a factor of three. The raw material for creating Polyblend is any plastic film waste. So, against the price of Rs. 0.40 per kg that rag pickers had been getting for plastic waste, Khan now offers Rs.6.NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 279','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ahmed Khan realised that plastic waste was a real problem and Polyblend, a fine powder of recycled modified plastic, was developed then by his company.','Polyblend, a fine powder of recycled modified plastic, was developed then by his company. This mixture is mixed with the bitumen that is used to lay roads.','In collaboration with GRD College of Technology and the Bangalore City Corporation, Ahmed Khan proved that blends of Polyblend and bitumen, when used to lay roads,  enhanced the bitumen’s water repellant properties, and helped to increase road life by a factor of three.','The raw material for creating Polyblend is any plastic film waste.\nSo, against the price of Rs. 0.40 per kg that rag pickers had been getting\nfor plastic waste, Khan now offers Rs.6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381238,'biology','Assertion:  Innovations like Polyblend might help avoid being reduce plastic waste.\n\nReason: We will soon be running short of plastic waste in our country, to produce Polyblend if all the plastic waste is channeled into innovative strategies like Polyblend','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Using Khan’s technique, by the year 2002, more than 40 kms of road in Bangalore has already been laid. At this rate, Khan will soon be running short of plastic waste in Bangalore, to produce Polyblend. Thanks to innovations like Polyblend, we might still avoid being smothered by plastic waste. The Real reason being Polyblend manufacturing requires large amount of plastics, which can be channeled into innovations instead of negative impact like pollution. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 279','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381239,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding Agricultural Waste and its management','Increasing use of chemical fertilizers can lead to aging of a lake by accelerated eutrophication','In the wake of green revolution, use of inorganic fertilisers and pesticides has increased manifold for enhancing crop production. Pesticides, herbicides, fungicides, etc., are being increasingly used. These incidentally, are also toxic to non-target organisms,that are important components of the soil ecosystem. Do you think these can be biomagnified in the terrestrial ecosystems? We know what the addition of increasing amounts of chemical fertilisers can do to aquatic ecosystems vis-à-vis eutrophication. The current problems in agriculture are, therefore, extremely grave Integrated organic farming is a cyclical, zero-waste procedure, where waste products from one process are cycled in as nutrients for other processes. This allows the maximum utilisation of resource and increases the efficiency of production. NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 279','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Use of inorganic fertilisers and pesticides has increased manifold for enhancing crop production and affects only target organisms that are not a part of soil ecosystem','Increasing use of chemical fertilizers can lead to aging of a lake by accelerated eutrophication','Integrated organic farming is a cyclical, zero-waste procedure, where waste products from one process are cycled in as nutrients for other processes allowing the minimum utilisation of resource and decrease in the efficiency of production','In the wake of green revolution, the use of organic and sustainable farming has increased manifold for enhancing crop production.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381240,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding integrated waste management','Enthusiastic about spreading information and help on the practice of integrated organic farming, Ramesh has created the Haryana Kisan Welfare Club, with a current membership of 5000 farmers.','Ramesh Chandra Dagar, a farmer in Sonipat,Haryana, is doing just this. He includes bee-keeping, dairy management, water harvesting, composting and agriculture in a chain of processes, which support each other and allow an extremely economical and sustainable venture. There is no need to use chemical fertilisers for crops, as cattle excreta (dung) are used as manure. Crop waste is used to create compost, which can be used as a natural fertiliser or can be used to generate natural gas for satisfying the energy needs of the farm. Enthusiastic about spreading information and help on the practice of integrated organic farming, Dagar has created the Haryana Kisan Welfare Club, with a current membership of 5000 farmers.\nNCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 280.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ramesh Chandar Segar, a farmer in Chandigarh,Haryana, includes bee-keeping, dairy management, water harvesting, composting and agriculture in a chain of processes, which support each other and allow an extremely economical and sustainable venture','Enthusiastic about spreading information and help on the practice of integrated organic farming, Ramesh has created the Haryana Kisan Welfare Club, with a current membership of 5000 farmers.','We need to use chemical fertilisers for crops, as cattle excreta (dung) is insufficient in this method','Integrated Organic Farming is a zero waste procedure as there is no cycling of nutrients for other processes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381241,'biology','Which of the following is incorrect about Radioactive waste','Radiation, that is given off by nuclear waste is less damaging to organisms, because it causes mutations at a very low rate and is considered safe source of energy','Initially, nuclear energy was hailed as a non-polluting way for generating electricity. Later on, it was realised that the use of nuclear energy has two very serious inherent problems. The first is accidental leakage, as occurred in the Three Mile Island and Chernobyl incidents and the second is safe disposal of radioactive wastes. \nRadiation, that is given off by nuclear waste is extremely damaging to organisms, because it causes mutations at a very high rate. At high doses, nuclear radiation is lethal but at lower doses, it creates various disorders, the most frequent of all being cancer. Therefore, nuclear waste is an extremely potent pollutant and has to be dealt with utmost caution. It has been recommended that storage of nuclear waste, after sufficient pre-treatment, should be done in suitably shielded containers buried within the rocks, about 500 m deep below the earth’s surface. However, this method of disposal is meeting stiff opposition from the public.\n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 280.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The use of nuclear energy has two serious problems that include  accidental leakage, and their safe\ndisposal','Radiation, that is given off by nuclear waste is less damaging to organisms, because it causes mutations at a very low rate and is considered safe source of energy','At high doses, nuclear radiation is lethal but at lower doses, it creates various disorders, the most frequent of all being cancer.','Nuclear waste must be stored after sufficient pre-treatment, in suitably shielded containers and buried within the rocks, 500 m deep below the earth’s surface.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381242,'biology','Assertion: Nuclear source of energy is no longer considered feasible and safe\n\nReason: Nuclear waste management and accidental leakage can be very catastrophic.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Initially, nuclear energy was hailed as a non-polluting way for generating electricity. Later on, it was realised that the use of nuclear energy has two very serious inherent problems. The first is accidental leakage, as occurred in the Three Mile Island and Chernobyl incidents and the second is safe disposal of radioactive wastes.NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 280.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151407,'chemistry','A : Glass belongs to the category of covalent network solid.\n\nR : Unit cell of glass is hexagonal.','Both A and R are false.','Glass belongs to amorphous solid not have an unit cell. Glass is not a covalent network solid but is classified as an amorphous solid, which means that it lacks a long-range order in its atomic arrangement. Covalent network solids, on the other hand, have a highly ordered 3D network of covalent bonds.\n\nUnlike crystalline solids, glasses do not have a unit cell or a repeating pattern of atoms. Instead, the atoms in glass are arranged in a disordered manner.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(51037,'chemistry','In diagram given below, it is seen that gases show ideal behaviour when the volume is _________ (n514)','large','It is seen that gases show ideal behaviour when the volume is large\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 152; Figure  No. 5.12)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','large','small','constant','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/51037q.png'),
(381243,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding the Greenhouse effect?','Greenhouse gases absorb longwave infrared radiation from the earth and emit it again towards Earth,The cycle continues until there is no longwave radiation left to emit','The term ‘Greenhouse effect’ has been derived from a phenomenon that occurs in a greenhouse. Have you ever seen a greenhouse? It looks like a small glass house and is used for growing plants especially during winter. In a greenhouse the glass panel lets the light in, but does not allow heat to escape. Therefore, the greenhouse warms up, very much like inside a car that has been parked in the sun for a few hours.\nGreenhouse gases absorb longwave infrared radiation from the earth and emit it again towards Earth,The cycle continues until there is no longwave radiation left to emit\nThe greenhouse effect is a naturally occurring phenomenon that is responsible for heating of Earth’s surface and atmosphere.You would be surprised to know that surprised to know that without greenhouse effect the average temperature\nat surface of Earth would have been a chilly –18 °C rather than the present average of 15 °C.NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 280, 281','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A greenhouse is a large glass house and is used for growing plants especially during summer., with glass panel lets that the light in, but does not allow heat to escape','The greenhouse effect is a manmade phenomenon that is responsible for heating of Earth’s surface and atmosphere.','Greenhouse gases absorb longwave infrared radiation from the earth and emit it again towards Earth,The cycle continues until there is no longwave radiation left to emit','Without greenhouse effect the average temperature at surface of Earth would have been a chilly –15 °C rather than the present average of 18 degree','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381244,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding Greenhouse effect?','Relative contribution of various greenhouse gases to total global warming is highest with CO₂ (60%), methane(20%),CFCs(14%), nitrous oxide (6%)','Clouds and gases reflect about ONE-FOURTH of the incoming solar radiation, and absorb some of it but ALMOST HALF of incoming solar radiation falls on Earth’s surface heating it, while a small proportion is reflected back. Earth’s surface re-emits heat in the form of infrared radiation but part of this does not escape into space asatmospheric gases (e.g.,  CO₂ , methane, etc.) absorb a major fraction of it. The molecules of these gases radiate heat energy, and a major part of which again comes to Earth’s surface, thus heating it up once again.\nThis cycle is repeated many a times. The above-mentioned gases –  CO₂  and methane – are commonly known as greenhouse gases because they are responsible for the greenhouse effect. Relative contribution of various greenhouse gases to total global warming is highest with CO₂ (60%),and then methane(20%),CFCs(14%), nitrous oxide (6%). Increase in the level of greenhouse gases has led to considerable heating of Earth leading to global warming. During the past century, the temperature of Earth has increased by 0.6 °C,most of it during the last three decades.\nNCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 281','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Clouds and gases reflect about half of the incoming solar radiation, and absorb some of it but almost one fourth of incoming solar radiation falls on Earth’s surface heating it, while a small proportion is reflected back.','Earth’s surface re-emits heat in the form of infrared radiation but part of this does not escape into space asatmospheric gases (e.g., oxygen, ethane, etc.) absorb a major fraction of it.','Relative contribution of various greenhouse gases to total global warming is highest with CO₂ (60%), methane(20%),CFCs(14%), nitrous oxide (6%)','Increase in the level of greenhouse gases has led to considerable heating of Earth leading to global warming. During the past century, the temperature of Earth has increased by 6 °C,most of it during the last three decades','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381245,'biology','Which of the following is incorrect regarding Greenhouse effect ?','The measures to control global warming include use of fossil fuel, energy usage, reducing afforestation, planting trees and slowing down the growth of human population.','Scientists believe that this rise in temperature is leading to deleterious changes in the environment and resulting in odd climatic changes (e.g. El Nino effect, and the opposite effect is La Nina) , thus leading to increased melting of polar ice caps as well as of other places like the Himalayan snow caps. Over many years, this will result in a rise in sea level that can submerge many coastal areas. The total spectrum of changes that global warming can bring about is a subject that is still under active research. How can we control global warming? The measures include cutting down use of fossil fuel, improving efficiency of energy usage, reducing deforestation, planting trees and slowing down the growth of human population. International initiatives are also being taken to reduce the emission of greenhouse gases into the atmosphere.\n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 281, 282','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Scientists believe that this rise in temperature is leading to deleterious changes in the environment and resulting in odd climatic changes like El-Nino and La-Nina and increased melting of snow caps','Over many years, Greenhouse effect and global warming will result in a rise in sea level that can submerge many coastal areas and cause their disappearance','The measures to control global warming include use of fossil fuel, energy usage, reducing afforestation, planting trees and slowing down the growth of human population.','International initiatives are also being taken to reduce the emission of greenhouse gases into the atmosphere.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381246,'biology','Which of the following statements regarding Ozone depletion is correct','The ozone hole over Antarctica develops each year between late August and early October and the ozone layer is thinnest here','Bad’ ozone, formed in the lower atmosphere (troposphere) that harms plants and animals. There is ‘good’ ozone also; this ozone is found in the upper part of the atmosphere called the stratosphere, and it acts as a shield absorbing ultraviolet radiation from the sun. UV rays are highly injurious to living organisms since DNA and proteins of living organisms preferentially absorb UV rays, and its high energy breaks the chemical bonds within these molecules. The thickness of the ozone in a column of air from the ground to the top of the atmosphere is measured in terms of Dobson units (DU).Ozone hole in the area above Antarctica, (shown in purple colour in NCERT), where the ozone layer is the thinnest. The ozone hole over Antarctica develops each year between late August and early October and the ozone layer is thinnest here\n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 282','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','‘Bad’ ozone, formed in the upper atmosphere (stratosphere) that harms plants and animals and ‘good’ ozone also; this ozone is found in the lower part of the atmosphere called the troposphere, and it acts\nas a shield absorbing ultraviolet radiation from the sun','UV rays are not injurious to living organisms since DNA and proteins of living organisms preferentially absorb infra red rays, and its high energy breaks the chemical bonds within these molecules.','The thickness of the ozone in a column of air from the ground to the top of the atmosphere is measured in terms of Dalton units (DU).','The ozone hole over Antarctica develops each year between late August and early October and the ozone layer is thinnest here','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381247,'biology','Assertion:  UV rays are highly injurious to living organisms\n\nReason: DNA and proteins of living organisms preferentially absorb UV rays, and its high energy breaks the chemical bonds within these molecules','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','UV rays are highly injurious to living organisms since DNA and proteins of living organisms preferentially absorb UV rays, and its high energy breaks the chemical bonds within these molecules.\nReference:NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 282','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381248,'biology','Which of the following statements regarding Ozone depletion is incorrect','In Troposphere, UV rays act on them releasing Cl atoms, Cl degrades ozone releasing molecular chlorine, with these atoms acting merely as catalysts; Cl atoms are not consumed in the reaction.','Ozone gas is continuously formed by the action of UV rays on molecular oxygen, and also degraded into molecular O₂ in the stratosphere. There should be a balance between production and degradation of ozone in the stratosphere. Of late, the balance has been disrupted due to enhancement of ozone degradation by chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs). CFCs find wide use as refrigerants. CFCs discharged in the lower part of atmosphere move upward and reach stratosphere. In stratosphere, UV rays act on them releasing Cl atoms. Cl degrades ozone releasing molecular oxygen, with these atoms acting merely as catalysts; Cl atoms are not consumed in the reaction. \n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 282','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Ozone gas is continuously formed by the action of UV rays on molecular oxygen, and also degraded into molecular O₂ in the stratosphere.','The balance has been disrupted lately due to enhancement of ozone degradation by chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)','CFCs find wide use as refrigerants. CFCs discharged in the lower part of atmosphere move upward and reach stratosphere. In stratosphere, UV rays act on them releasing Cl atoms','In Troposphere, UV rays act on them releasing Cl atoms, Cl degrades ozone releasing molecular chlorine, with these atoms acting merely as catalysts; Cl atoms are not consumed in the reaction.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381249,'biology','Which of the following statements regarding Ozone depletion is correct','The formation of a large area of thinned ozone layer, is called as the ozone hole,','Whatever CFCs are added to the stratosphere, they have permanent and continuing effects on Ozone, levels. Although ozone depletion is occurring widely in the stratosphere, the depletion is particularly marked over the Antarctic region. This has resulted in formation of a large area of thinned ozone layer, commonly called as the ozone hole, Subsequently many more efforts have been made and protocols have laid down definite roadmaps, separately for developed and developing countries, for reducing the emission of CFCs and other ozone depleting chemicals. \nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 282, 283','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Whatever CFCs are added to the stratosphere, they only have temporary effects on Ozone','Ozone depletion is occurring widely in the stratosphere, the depletion is particularly marked over the Arctic region.','The formation of a large area of thinned ozone layer, is called as the ozone hole,','Subsequently many more efforts have been made and protocols have laid down definite roadmaps, separately for only developing and under-developed countries, for reducing the emission of CFCs and other ozone depleting chemicals','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381250,'biology','Which of the following regarding UV radiation is correct','Many more efforts have been made and protocols have laid down definite roadmaps, separately for developed and developing countries, for reducing the emission of CFCs and other ozone depleting chemicals.The degradation of natural resources can occur, not just by the action of pollutants but also by improper resource utilisation practices.','UV radiation of wavelengths shorter than UV-B, are almost completely absorbed by Earth’s atmosphere, given that the ozone layer is intact. But, UV-B damages DNA and mutation may occur. It causes aging of skin, damage to skin cells and various types of skin cancers. In human eye, cornea absorbs UV-B radiation, and a high dose of UV-B causes inflammation of cornea, called snow-blindness, cataract, etc. Such exposure may permanently damage the cornea. Recognising the deleterious affects of ozone depletion, an international treaty, known as the Montreal Protocol, was signed at Montreal (Canada) in 1987 (effective in 1989) to control the emission of ozone depleting substances. Subsequently many more efforts have been made and protocols have laid down definite roadmaps, separately for developed and developing countries, for reducing the emission of CFCs and other ozone depleting chemicals.The degradation of natural resources can occur, not just by the action of pollutants but also by improper resource utilisation practices.\n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 283.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','UV radiation of wavelengths shorter than UV-B, are almost completely radiated by Earth’s atmosphere, given that the ozone layer is intact and UV-B is damaging to skin and causes various types of cancers','A high dose of UV-A causes inflammation of cornea, called snow-blindness','The Montreal Protocol, was signed at Montreal (Canada) in 1989 (effective in 1987) to control the emission of ozone depleting substances.','Many more efforts have been made and protocols have laid down definite roadmaps, separately for developed and developing countries, for reducing the emission of CFCs and other ozone depleting chemicals.The degradation of natural resources can occur, not just by the action of pollutants but also by improper resource utilisation practices.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381251,'biology','Assertion: UV-B damages DNA and causes mutation,aging of skin, damage to skin cells and various types of skin cancers.\n\nReason: UV-B can penetrate intact ozone layer and cause damage','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','UV radiation of wavelengths shorter than UV-B, are almost completely absorbed by Earth’s atmosphere, given that the ozone layer is intact. But, UV-B damages DNA and mutation may occur. It causes aging of skin, damage to skin cells and various types of skin cancers.\n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 283.','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381252,'biology','Which of the following is incorrect regarding degradation by improper maintaineance','The development of the fertile top-soil takes only a few years ,but can be regenerated very easily due to human activities like over-cultivation, unrestricted grazing, deforestation and poor irrigation practices, resulting in arid patches of land.','The degradation of natural resources can occur, not just by the action of pollutants but also by improper resource utilisation practices.\nSoil erosion and desertification: The development of the fertile top-soil takes centuries. But, it can be removed very easily due to human activities like over-cultivation, unrestricted grazing, deforestation and poor irrigation practices, resulting in arid patches of land. When large barren patches extend and meet over time, a desert is created. Internationally, it has been recognised that desertification is a major problem nowadays, particularly due to increased urbanisation. \nWaterlogging and soil salinity: Irrigation without proper drainage of water leads to waterlogging in the soil. Besides affecting the crops, waterlogging draws salt to the surface of the soil. The salt then is deposited as a thin crust on the land surface or starts collecting at the roots of the plants. This increased salt content is inimical to the growth of crops and is extremely damaging to agriculture. Waterlogging and soil salinity are some of the problems that have come in the wake of the Green Revolution.\n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 283','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The degradation of natural resources can occur, not just by the action of pollutants but also by improper resource utilisation practices.','The development of the fertile top-soil takes only a few years ,but can be regenerated very easily due to human activities like over-cultivation, unrestricted grazing, deforestation and poor irrigation practices, resulting in arid patches of land.','When large barren patches extend and meet over time, a desert is created and has been recognised that desertification is a major problem nowadays, particularly due to increased urbanisation.','Irrigation without proper drainage of water leads to waterlogging in the soil, affects crops, draws salt to the surface of the soil and gets deposited as a thin crust on the land surface, collecting at the roots of the plants and  is extremely damaging to agriculture, in the wake of Green revolution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151408,'chemistry','A : NaCl shows Schottky defect at room temperature.\n\nR : NaCl shows ‘F centre’ at high temperature.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','F center (also called Frenkel defect) is another type of point defect that occurs in ionic solids where an anion is missing from its lattice site and occupies an interstitial position. In NaCl, F centers are produced at high temperatures due to the migration of cation vacancies.\n\nSchottky defect and F centers are two distinct types of point defects that occur in NaCl under different conditions. Schottky defect occurs at room temperature due to thermal vibrations, while F centers are produced at high temperatures due to the migration of cation vacancies','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381253,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding Deforestation ?','By the end of the century, forest cover shrunk to 21.54 %, whereas the National Forest Policy (1988) of India has recommended 33 % forest cover for the plains and 67 % for the hills','Deforestation is the conversion of forested areas to non-forested ones. According to an estimate, almost 40 % forests have been lost in the tropics, compared to only 1 % in the temperate region. The present scenario of deforestation is particularly grim in India. At the beginning of twentieth century, forests covered about 30 % of the land of India. By the end of the century, it shrunk to 21.54 %, whereas the National Forest Policy (1988) of India has recommended 33 % forest cover for the plains and 67 % for the hills.One of the major reasons is the conversion of forest to agricultural land so as to feed the growing human population.\n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 283, 284','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Deforestation is the conversion of non-forested ones to forested areas, almost 40 % forests have been recovered in the tropics, compared to only 1 % in the temperate region','The present scenario of deforestation is particularly grim in India where at the beginning of 20th century, forests covered about 50 % of the land of India','By the end of the century, forest cover shrunk to 21.54 %, whereas the National Forest Policy (1988) of India has recommended 33 % forest cover for the plains and 67 % for the hills','One of the major reasons is the conversion of forest to agricultural land so as to feed the growing grazing population.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381254,'biology','Which of the following is incorrect regarding Deforestation and Jhum Cultivation?','With increasing population, and repeated cultivation, the recovery phase in Jhum cultivation has resulted in afforestation','Trees are axed for timber, firewood, cattle ranching and for several other purposes. Slash and burn agriculture, commonly called as Jhum cultivation in the north-eastern states of India, has also contributed to deforestation. In slash and burn agriculture, the farmers cut down the trees of the forest and burn the plant remains. The ash is used as a fertiliser and the land is then used for farming or cattle grazing. After cultivation, the area is left for several years so as to allow its recovery. The farmers then move on to other areas and repeat this process. In earlier days, when Jhum cultivation was in prevalence, enough time-gap was given so that the land recovered from the effect of cultivation. With increasing population, and repeated cultivation, this recovery phase is done away with, resulting in deforestation.\nOne of the major effects is enhanced  CO₂  concentration in the atmosphere because trees that could hold a lot of carbon in their biomass are lost with deforestation, this is a major consequence of deforestation.\n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 284','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Trees are axed for timber, firewood, cattle ranching and for several other purposes.','Jhum cultivation practiced in the North-East, the farmers cut down the trees of the forest and burn the plant remains using the ash as a fertiliser and the land for cattle rearing','With increasing population, and repeated cultivation, the recovery phase in Jhum cultivation has resulted in afforestation','One of the major effects is enhanced  CO₂  concentration in the atmosphere because trees that could hold a lot of carbon in their biomass are lost with deforestation, this is a major consequence of deforestation.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381255,'biology','Assertion: Nowadays, Jhum cultivation is not suitable and leads to increased deforestation\n\nReason: Time-gap is not enough for land recovery with increasing population and repeated cultivation','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','In earlier days, when Jhum cultivation was in prevalence, enough time-gap was given so that the land recovered from the effect of cultivation. With increasing population, and repeated cultivation, this recovery phase is done away with, resulting in deforestation.\n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 284','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381256,'biology','Assertion: Deforestation contributes to Greenhouse effect and Global warming \n\nReason:  CO₂  concentration is enhanced in the atmosphere because trees that could hold a lot of carbon in their biomass are lost with deforestation.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','One of the major effects is enhanced  CO₂  concentration in the atmosphere because trees that could hold a lot of carbon in their biomass are lost with deforestation.\n CO₂  is a major contributor to Greenhouse effect that results in Global warming, contributing about 60%','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381257,'biology','Which of the following regarding deforestation is incorrect ?','Reforestation cannot occur naturally in a deforested area','Deforestation also causes loss of biodiversity due to habitat destruction, disturbs hydrologic cycle, causes soil erosion, and may lead to desertification in extreme cases. \nReforestation is the process of restoring a forest that once existed but was removed at some point of time in the past. Reforestation may occur naturally in a deforested area. However, we can speed it up by planting trees with due consideration to biodiversity that earlier existed in that area.\n\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 284','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Deforestation also causes loss of biodiversity due to habitat destruction, disturbs hydrologic cycle, causes soil erosion, and may lead to desertification in extreme cases.','Reforestation is the process of restoring a forest that once existed but was removed at some point of time in the past.','Reforestation cannot occur naturally in a deforested area','Reforestation can be sped up by planting trees in an area,where biodiversity once exisgted','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(381258,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding People\'s participation in prevention against Deforestation','A Bishnoi woman Amrita Devi showed exemplary courage by hugging a tree and daring king’s men to cut her first before cutting the tree and lived protecting the tree','The Bishnoi community is known for its peaceful co-existence with nature. The effort to cut down trees by the kings was thwarted by the Bishnois. A Bishnoi woman Amrita Devi showed exemplary courage by hugging a tree and daring king’s men to cut her first before cutting the tree. The tree mattered much more to her than her own life. Sadly, the king’s men did not heed to her pleas, and cut down the tree along with Amrita Devi. Her three daughters and hundreds of other Bishnois followed her, and thus lost their lives saving trees. Nowhere in history do we find a commitment of this magnitude when human beings sacrificed their lives for the cause of the environment\nReference: NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 284','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','In 1731, the king of Jodhpur in Nagaland wanted to deforest a forest inhabited by the Bishnois.','The Bishnois prevented afforestation by hugging a tree and preventing the king\'s men from cutting down the trees.','A Bishnoi woman Amrita Devi showed exemplary courage by hugging a tree and daring king’s men to cut her first before cutting the tree and lived protecting the tree','The son\'s of Amrita Devi, along with hundreds of Bishnois lost their lives saving trees','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31112,'chemistry','Match the elements with their respective value of electronegativity on Pauling scale:','Si-1.8,P-2.1,Br-2.8 ,I-2.5','Electronegativity Values (on Pauling scale) down a family, as shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; diagram(ne0314)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'3','match','Si,1.8','P,2.1','Br,2.8','I,2.5','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(381259,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding People\'s Participation in Prevention of deforestation','In 1974, local women showed enormous bravery in protecting trees from the axe of contractors by hugging them as a part of the Chipko Movement','The Government of India has recently instituted the Amrita Devi Bishnoi Wildlife Protection Award for individuals or communities from rural areas that have shown extraordinary courage and dedication in protecting wildlife(fauna).\nYou may have heard of the Chipko Movement of Garhwal Himalayas. In 1974, local women showed enormous bravery in protecting trees from the axe of contractors by hugging them. People all over the world have acclaimed the Chipko movement. Realising the significance of participation by local communities, the Government of India in 1980s has introduced the concept of Joint Forest Management (JFM) so as to work closely with the local communities for protecting and managing forests. In return for their services to the forest, the communities get benefit of various forest products (e.g., fruits, gum, rubber, medicine, etc.), and thus the forest can be conserved in a sustainable manner. \nReference:NCERT Class 12 Biology, Chapter 16, Page 285','Ecology >> Environmental Issues >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The Government of India has recently instituted the Amrita Devi Bishnoi Wildlife Protection Award for individuals or communities from rural areas that have shown extraordinary courage and dedication in protecting flora.','In 1974, local women showed enormous bravery in protecting trees from the axe of contractors by hugging them as a part of the Chipko Movement','Realising the significance of participation by local communities, the Government of India in 1990s has introduced the concept of Joint Forest Management (JFM) so as to work closely with the local communities for protecting and managing forests','In return for their services to the forest, the communities get benefit of various forest products (e.g., fruits, gum, rubber, medicine, etc.), and thus the forest can be conserved in a non-sustainable manner.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(260101,'chemistry','Carbonyl compounds are not the constituents of _____?','Cement and paper industry','Carbonyl compounds are of utmost importance to organic chemistry. \n\nThey are constituents of fabrics, flavourings, plastics and drugs. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-357 Paragraph - 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Fabrics','Flavouring agents','Plastics and drugs','Cement and paper industry','NEW','12-357','12-227',NULL),
(260102,'chemistry','Organic compounds containing carbon-oxygen double bond commonly called as ______?','Carbonyl','Organic compounds containing carbon-oxygen double bond (>C=O) called carbonyl group, which is one of the most important functional group in organic chemistry.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-357 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbonyl','Ethers','Carboxyl','Esters','NEW','12-357','12-227',NULL),
(260103,'chemistry','In _______ the carbonyl group is bonded to a carbon and hydrogen.','Aldehydes','In aldehydes, the carbonyl group is bonded to a carbon and hydrogen while in the ketones, it is bonded to two carbon atoms. \n\nThe carbonyl compounds in which carbon of carbonyl group is bonded to carbon or hydrogen and oxygen of hydroxyl moiety (-OH) are known as carboxylic acids, while in compounds where carbon is attached to carbon or hydrogen and nitrogen of -NH₂ moiety or to halogens are called amides and acyl halides respectively. \n\nEsters and anhydrides are derivatives of carboxylic acids.      \n                                                                                                                            \nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-357 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Ketones','Aldehydes','Carboxylic acids','Amides','NEW','12-357','12-227',NULL),
(260104,'chemistry','In _______ the carbonyl group is bonded to a carbon and carbon.','Ketones','In aldehydes, the carbonyl group is bonded to a carbon and hydrogen while in the ketones, it is bonded to two carbon atoms. \n\nThe carbonyl compounds in which carbon of carbonyl group is bonded to carbon or hydrogen and oxygen of hydroxyl moiety (-OH) are known as carboxylic acids, while in compounds where carbon is attached to carbon or hydrogen and nitrogen of -NH₂ moiety or to halogens are called amides and acyl halides respectively.\n\nEsters and anhydrides are derivatives of carboxylic acids. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-357 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Ketones','Carboxylic acids','Aldehydes','Esters','NEW','12-357','12-227',NULL),
(260105,'chemistry','The carbonyl compounds in which carbon of carbonyl group is bonded to carbon or hydrogen and oxygen of hydroxyl moiety (-OH) are known as ______?','Carboxylic acids','The carbonyl compounds in which carbon of carbonyl group is bonded to carbon or hydrogen and oxygen of hydroxyl moiety (-OH) are known as carboxylic acids.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-357 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Aldehydes','Esters','Acid halides','Carboxylic acids','NEW','12-357','12-227',NULL),
(260106,'chemistry','In compounds where carbon is attached to carbon or hydrogen and nitrogen is called______?','Amides','The carbonyl  compounds where carbon is attached to carbon or hydrogen and nitrogen of -NH₂ moiety or to halogens are called amides and acyl halides respectively. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-357 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Acyl halides','Nitriles','Amides','Esters','NEW','12-357','12-227',NULL),
(260107,'chemistry','In compounds where carbon is attached to carbon or hydrogen and halogen is called______?','Acyl halides','The carbonyl compounds in which carbon of carbonyl group is bonded to carbon or hydrogen and oxygen of hydroxyl moiety (-OH) are known as carboxylic acids, while in compounds where carbon is attached to carbon or hydrogen to halogens are called  acyl halides.\n\nEsters and anhydrides are derivatives of carboxylic acids.                                                                                                                                     \n\nReference:(NCERT - Class 12 Page-357 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Esters','Amides','Acyl halides','Nitriles','NEW','12-357','12-227',NULL),
(260108,'chemistry','Esters and anhydrides are derivatives of ______?','Carboxylic acids','Esters and anhydrides are derivatives of carboxylic acids.\n \nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-357 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Ketones','Aldehydes','Carboxylic acids','Amines','NEW','12-357','12-227',NULL),
(260109,'chemistry','The most widespread functional group in organic chemistry is_____?','Carbonyl group','Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids are widespread in plants and animal kingdom. \nThey play an important role in biochemical processes of life.  \n                                                                     \nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Halo group','Hydroxy group','Carbonyl group','Amide group','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(260110,'chemistry','Carbonyl compounds play a vital role in______.','Biochemical processes','Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids are widespread in plants and animal kingdom. \nThey play an important role in biochemical processes of life. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Combustion processes','Biochemical processes','Chemical processes','Industrial processes','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(260111,'chemistry','Carbonyl compounds add ____ ___ to nature?','Fragrance and flavour','Carbonyl compounds add fragrance and flavour to nature, for example, vanillin (from vanilla beans), salicylaldehyde (from meadow sweet) and cinnamaldehyde (from cinnamon) have very pleasant fragrances. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Only flavour','Fragrance and colour','Flavour and color','Fragrance and flavour','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(260112,'chemistry','Vanillin is extracted _____?','From vanilla beans','Carbonyl compounds add fragrance and flavour to nature, for example, vanillin (from vanilla beans), salicylaldehyde (from meadow sweet) and cinnamaldehyde (from cinnamon) have very pleasant fragrances. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','From lichens','From cinnamon','From pine trees','From vanilla beans','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(260113,'chemistry','____from meadow sweet have very pleasant fragrances?','Salicylaldehyde','Carbonyl compounds add fragrance and flavour to nature, for example, vanillin (from vanilla beans), salicylaldehyde (from meadow sweet) and cinnamaldehyde (from cinnamon) have very pleasant fragrances. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Valeraldehyde','Formaldehyde','Salicylaldehyde','Benzaldehyde','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(260114,'chemistry','____from cinnamon have very pleasant fragrances?','Cinnamaldehyde','Carbonyl compounds add fragrance and flavour to nature, for example, vanillin (from vanilla beans), salicylaldehyde (from meadow sweet) and cinnamaldehyde (from cinnamon) have very pleasant fragrances.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Salicylaldehyde','Vanillin','Cinnamaldehyde','Formaldehyde','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(260115,'chemistry','______are used in many food products.','Carbonyl compounds','Carbonyl compounds are used in many food products and pharmaceuticals to add flavours. \n\nSome of these families are manufactured for use as solvents (i.e., acetone) and for preparing materials like adhesives, paints, resins, perfumes, plastics, fabrics, etc.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbonyl compounds','Esters','Ethers','Aromatic compounds','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(260201,'chemistry','____ are often called by their common names instead of IUPAC names.','Aldehydes and ketones','Aldehydes and ketones are often called by their common names instead of IUPAC names. \n\nThe common names of most aldehydes are derived from the common names of the corresponding carboxylic acids by replacing the ending –ic of acid with aldehyde. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','Aldehydes','Ketones','Carboxylic acids','Aldehydes and ketones','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(260202,'chemistry','The common names of ketones are derived by naming two ___ or aryl groups bonded to the carbonyl group.','Alkyl','Aldehydes and ketones are often called by their common names instead of IUPAC names. \n\nThe common names of most aldehydes are derived from the common names of the corresponding carboxylic acids by replacing the ending –ic of acid with aldehyde.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','Phenyl','Alkyl','Acyl','Benzyl','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(260203,'chemistry','The simplest dimethyl ketone is called ______?','Propanone','Some ketones have historical common names, the simplest dimethyl ketone is called acetone. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','Methanone','Propanone','Butanone','Ethanone','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(260204,'chemistry','Alkyl phenyl ketones are usually named by adding the name of ___ group as prefix to the word phenone.','Acyl','Alkyl phenyl ketones are usually named by adding the name of acyl group as prefix to the word phenone.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','Alkyl','Benzyl','Acyl','Aryl','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(260205,'chemistry','The IUPAC names of open chain aliphatic aldehydes and ketones are derived from the names of the corresponding _____?','Alkanes','The IUPAC names of open chain aliphatic aldehydes and ketones are derived from the names of the corresponding alkanes by replacing the ending –e with –al and –one respectively. \n\nIn case of aldehydes the longest carbon chain is numbered starting from the carbon of the aldehyde group while in case of ketones the numbering begins from the end nearer to the carbonyl group. \n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','Alcohols','Alkanes','Carboxylic acids','Ethers','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(260206,'chemistry','Aldehyde IUPAC ends with suffix____?','al','The IUPAC names of open chain aliphatic aldehydes and ketones are derived from the names of the corresponding alkanes by replacing the ending –e with –al and –one respectively. \n\nIn case of aldehydes the longest carbon chain is numbered starting from the carbon of the aldehyde group while in case of ketones the numbering begins from the end nearer to the carbonyl group. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','al','ol','ek','ald','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(260207,'chemistry','Ketone IUPAC name ends with suffix?','one','The IUPAC names of open chain aliphatic aldehydes and ketones are derived from the names of the corresponding alkanes by replacing the ending –e with –al and –one respectively. \n\nIn case of aldehydes the longest carbon chain is numbered starting from the carbon of the aldehyde group while in case of ketones the numbering begins from the end nearer to the carbonyl group.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','al','one','keto','carboxy','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(260208,'chemistry','In case of carboxylic acid the longest  chain is numbered starting from the _______','Carboxyl group','The IUPAC names of open chain aliphatic aldehydes and ketones are derived from the names of the corresponding alkanes by replacing the ending –e with –al and –one respectively. \n\nIn case of aldehydes the longest carbon chain is numbered starting from the carbon of the aldehyde group while in case of ketones the numbering begins from the end nearer to the carbonyl group.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','Carboxyl group','Double bonded Oxygen','Single bonded oxygen','Hydrogen','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(260209,'chemistry','In case of ketones the numbering begins from the end nearer to the _____ group.','Carbonyl','The IUPAC names of open chain aliphatic aldehydes and ketones are derived from the names of the corresponding alkanes by replacing the ending –e with –al and –one respectively. \n\nIn case of aldehydes the longest carbon chain is numbered starting from the carbon of the aldehyde group while in case of ketones the numbering begins from the end nearer to the carbonyl group. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','Caboxyl','Acyl','Carbonyl','Formyl','NEW','12-359','12-229',NULL),
(260210,'chemistry','The carbonyl carbon atom is _____hybridised.','sp²','The carbonyl carbon atom is sp2 -hybridised and forms three sigma (σ) bonds. \nThe fourth valence electron of carbon remains in its p-orbital and forms a π-bond with oxygen by overlap with p-orbital of an oxygen. \n\nIn addition, the oxygen atom also has two non bonding electron pairs. \nThus, the carbonyl carbon and the three atoms attached to it lie in the same plane and the π-electron cloud is above and below this plane. \n\nThe bond angles are approximately 120° as expected of a trigonal coplanar structure.\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','s²','sp','sp³','sp²','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260211,'chemistry','The carbonyl carbon atom forms ______ bonds.','three sigma','The carbonyl carbon atom forms three sigma bonds one with oxygen one with hydrogen if aldehyde and one with alkyl/aryl carbon atom & if ketone 2 alkyl/aryl carbons.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','two sigma and one pi','three sigma','one sigma and two pi','two sigma and two pi','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260212,'chemistry','The fourth valence electron of carbon remains in its _____orbital?','p','The fourth valence electron of carbon remains in its p-orbital and forms a π-bond with oxygen by overlap with p-orbital of an oxygen. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','s','p','p2','p3','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260213,'chemistry','The oxygen atom also has _____ non bonding electron pairs?','2','In addition, the oxygen atom also has two non bonding electron pairs.\n \nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'1','mcq','2','3','4','6','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260214,'chemistry','The carbonyl carbon and the three atoms attached to it forms bond angle of ____?','120⁰','The carbonyl carbon atom is sp2 -hybridised. \n\nThe bond angles are approximately 120° as expected of a trigonal coplanar structure.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','180⁰','160⁰','120⁰','90⁰','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260215,'chemistry','The geometry of the carbonyl carbon is____.','Trigonal planar','The bond angles are approximately 120° as expected of a trigonal coplanar structure.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','Tetrahedral','Linear','Trigonal planar','planar','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260216,'chemistry','The carbon-oxygen double bond is polarised due to _____of oxygen relative to carbon.','higher electronegativity','The carbon-oxygen double bond is polarised due to higher electronegativity of oxygen relative to carbon. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph-2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','lower electronegativity','higher ionization energy','higher electron affinity','higher electronegativity','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260217,'chemistry','The carbonyl carbon is an ______?','Electrophile','The carbonyl carbon is an electrophilic (Lewis acid), and carbonyl oxygen, a nucleophilic (Lewis base) centre. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph-2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','Highly electronegative','Electropositive','Electrophile','Nucleophile','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260218,'chemistry','The carbonyl carbon is a ______?','Electron acceptor','The carbonyl carbon is an electrophilic (Lewis acid), and carbonyl oxygen, a nucleophilic (Lewis base) centre. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph-2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','Carbocation','Electron donor','Electron acceptor','Carbanion','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260219,'chemistry','The carbonyl oxygen is ______?','Nucleophile','The carbon-oxygen double bond is polarised due to higher electronegativity of oxygen relative to carbon. \n\nThe carbonyl oxygen, a nucleophilic (Lewis base) centre. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph-2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','Electropositive','Electrophile','Nucleophile','Both b and c','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260220,'chemistry','The carbonyl oxygen is ______?','Lewis base','The carbon-oxygen double bond is polarised due to higher electronegativity of oxygen relative to carbon. \n\nThe carbonyl oxygen, a nucleophilic (Lewis base) centre. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph-2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','Lewis acid','Electrophile','Lewis base','Electropositive','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260221,'chemistry','Carbonyl compounds have substantial','dipole moment','Carbonyl compounds have substantial dipole moments and are polar than ethers.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph-2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','metallic character','dipole moment','ionic character','all of the above','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260222,'chemistry','State True or False?\na. Ethers have high polarity than carbonyl compounds.\nb. The polarity of the carbonyl group is explained on the basis of resonance .','F,T','Carbonyl compounds have substantial dipole moments and are polar than ethers.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph-2)\n\nThe high polarity of the carbonyl group is explained on the basis of resonance involving a neutral and a dipolar structures.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph-2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260223,'chemistry','The high polarity of the carbonyl group is explained on the basis of ?','resonance','The high polarity of the carbonyl group is explained on the basis of resonance involving a neutral and a dipolar structures.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph-2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'2','mcq','covalent character','Fajan\'s rule','resonance','ion-ion interaction','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260301,'chemistry','Aldehydes and ketones are generally prepared by oxidation of all, except','tertiary alcohol','Aldehydes and ketones are generally prepared by oxidation of primary and secondary alcohols. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','primary alcohol','secondary alcohol','tertiary alcohol','a and b','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260302,'chemistry','Which method uses volatile alcohols for the preparation of aldehydes and ketones?','dehydrogenation of alcohols','Dehydrogenation is suitable for volatile alcohols and is of industrial application. \nIn this method alcohol vapours are passed over heavy metal catalysts (Ag or Cu). \nPrimary and secondary alcohols give aldehydes and ketones, respectively. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','oxidation of alcohols','dehydrogenation of alcohols','ozonolysis of alkenes','hydration of alkenes','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260303,'chemistry','Which method can be used industrially for the preparation of aldehydes and ketones?','dehydrogenation of alcohols','Dehydrogenation is suitable for volatile alcohols and is of industrial application.\n \nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','oxidation of alcohols','dehydrogenation of alcohols','ozonolysis of alkenes','hydration of alkenes','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260304,'chemistry','Alcohol vapours are passed over which of the following catalysts to give aldehydes and ketones?','Heavy metal','Dehydrogenation is suitable for volatile alcohols and is of industrial application. \nIn this method alcohol vapours are passed over heavy metal catalysts (Ag or Cu). \n\nPrimary and secondary alcohols give aldehydes and ketones, respectively.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','Heavy non-metal','Heavy metal','Light metal','Light non-metal','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260305,'chemistry','In dehydrogenation method alcohol vapours are passed over heavy metal catalysts like?','Ag','This method is suitable for volatile alcohols and is of industrial application. \n\nIn this method alcohol vapours are passed over heavy metal catalysts (Ag or Cu).\n \nPrimary and secondary alcohols give aldehydes and ketones, respectively.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','Ag','Mn','Fe','zinc','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260306,'chemistry','Which alcohols on dehydrogenation give aldehydes and ketones?','primary,secondary','This method is suitable for volatile alcohols and is of industrial application. \n\nIn this method alcohol vapours are passed over heavy metal catalysts (Ag or Cu). \n\nPrimary and secondary alcohols give aldehydes and ketones, respectively. \n \nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','primary,secondary','primary,secondary, tertiary','secondary, tertiary','all alcoholo','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260307,'chemistry','Which method is used to produce aldehydes & ketones from hydrocarbons?','ozonolysis','By ozonolysis of alkenes:\n\nAs we know, ozonolysis of alkenes followed by reaction with zinc dust and water gives aldehydes,ketones or a mixture of both depending on the substitution pattern of the alkene.\n\nBy hydration of alkynes:\n\nAddition of water to ethyne in the presence of H₂SO₄ and HgSO₄ gives acetaldehyde. \nAll other alkynes give ketones in this reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','ozonolysis','dehydrohalogenation','dehydration','decarboxylation','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(260308,'chemistry','Addition of water to alkynes occurs in acidic medium and in the presence of Hg 2+ ions as a catalyst.\nWhich of the following products will be formed on addition of water to but-1-yne under these conditions refer diagram?(n26073)','b','By ozonolysis of alkenes:  \n\nOzonolysis of alkenes followed by reaction with zinc dust and water gives aldehydes,ketones or a mixture of both depending on the substitution pattern of the alkene.\n\nBy hydration of alkynes: \n\nAddition of water to ethyne in the presence of H₂SO₄ and HgSO₄ gives acetaldehyde. \nAll other alkynes give ketones in this reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260308q.png'),
(260309,'chemistry','Products obtained by the ozonolysis of alkenes are ?','aldehydes, ketones','By ozonolysis of alkenes: \n \nOzonolysis of alkenes followed by reaction with zinc dust and water gives aldehydes and ketones or a mixture of both depending on the substitution pattern of the alkene.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic','aldehydes, ketones','ketones only','all of the above','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(260310,'chemistry','Ozonolysis of alkenes occurs in the presence of ?','Zn dust, water','By ozonolysis of alkenes:  \n\nOzonolysis of alkenes followed by reaction with zinc dust and water gives aldehydes,ketones or a mixture of both depending on the substitution pattern of the alkene.\n\nBy hydration of alkynes: \n\nAddition of water to ethyne in the presence of H₂SO₄ and HgSO₄ gives acetaldehyde. \n\nAll other alkynes give ketones in this reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'2','mcq','Zn dust','Zn dust, water','Mg','non polar solvents','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/260310q.png'),
(260311,'chemistry','All other alkynes give ketones during hydration of alkynes except?','ethyne','By hydration of alkynes: \n\nAddition of water to ethyne in the presence of  H₂SO₄ and HgSO₄ gives acetaldehyde. \nAll other alkynes give ketones in this reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','ethyne','methyne','1-propene','propylene','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/260311q.png'),
(260312,'chemistry','The process occurring in Rosenmund reaction is','hydrogenation','Acyl chloride (acid chloride) is hydrogenated over catalyst, palladium on barium sulphate. This reaction is called Rosenmund reduction.\n\nHydrogenation is taking place as it involves the addition of hydrogen.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'2','mcq','oxidation','reduction','hydrogenation','dehydrogenation','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(260313,'chemistry','Catalyst used in Rosenmund reduction  is','Pd','Acyl chloride (acid chloride) is hydrogenated over catalyst, palladium on barium sulphate. \n\nThis reaction is called Rosenmund reduction.\n \nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','Pd','Pt','Zn','hydrogen','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(260314,'chemistry','The primary substrate used in Stephen reaction\'s is','nitriles','Nitriles are reduced to corresponding imine with stannous chloride in the presence of hydrochloric acid, which on hydrolysis give corresponding aldehyde.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','cyanides','nitriles','nitrates','all of the above','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(260315,'chemistry','Nitriles can selectively reduced to imines by?','Diisobutylaluminium hydride','Nitriles are selectively reduced by diisobutylaluminium hydride, (DIBAL-H) to imines followed by hydrolysis to aldehydes.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','Diisobutylaluminium hydride','stannous chloride','Both (a) and (b)','None of the above','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(260316,'chemistry','DIBAL-H converts nitriles to ?','imines','Alternatively, nitriles are selectively reduced by diisobutylaluminium hydride, (DIBAL-H) to imines followed by hydrolysis to aldehydes. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','aldehyde','amines','imines','cyanides','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(260317,'chemistry','Reducing agent in stephen\'s reaction  is ?','stannous chloride','Nitriles are reduced to corresponding imine with stannous chloride in the presence of hydrochloric acid, which on hydrolysis give corresponding aldehyde.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','stannous chloride','stannic chloride','DIBAL-H','Pd on Barium sulphate','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(260318,'chemistry','All of them following can be reduced to aldehyde by DIBAL-H except','acyl chlorides','Esters are also reduced to aldehydes with DIBAL-H.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','nitriles','esters','cyanides','acyl chlorides','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(260319,'chemistry','Name the following reaction given in the diagram?(n26009)','Rosenmund','Acyl chloride (acid chloride) is hydrogenated over catalyst, palladium on barium sulphate. \n\nThis reaction is called Rosenmund reduction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','Hydrogenation','Etard','Stephen','Rosenmund','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260319q.png'),
(260320,'chemistry','Name the following reaction given in the diagram?(n26010)','Stephen','Nitriles are reduced to corresponding imine with stannous chloride in the presence of hydrochloric acid, which on hydrolysis give corresponding aldehyde.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','Hydrogenation','Etard','Stephen','Rosenmund','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260320q.png'),
(260321,'chemistry','Name the catalyst in the diagram?(n26011)','DIBAL-H','Esters are also reduced to aldehydes with DIBAL-H.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','water','DIBAL-H','hydrogen','aluminium + hydrogen gas','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260321q.png'),
(260322,'chemistry','Name the catalyst in the diagram?(n26012)','Diisobutylaluminium hydride','Nitriles are selectively reduced by diisobutylaluminium hydride, (DIBAL-H) to imines followed by hydrolysis to aldehydes.\n \nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','Water','Stephen','Diisobutylaluminium hydride','Hydrogen','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260322q.png'),
(260323,'chemistry','Name the following reaction given in the diagram?(n26013)','Etard','Use of chromyl chloride :\n\nChromyl chloride oxidises methyl group to a chromium complex, which on hydrolysis gives corresponding benzaldehyde.This reaction is called Etard reaction. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -6)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','Koch','Etard','Stephen','Rosenmund','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260323q.png'),
(260324,'chemistry','Name the reagent in the diagram?(n26014)','Chromyl chloride','Use of chromyl chloride : \n\nChromyl chloride oxidises methyl group to a chromium complex, which on hydrolysis gives corresponding benzaldehyde.\n\nThis reaction is called Etard reaction. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -6)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','Chromyl chloride','Carbon disulphide','Chromic oxide','Stannous chloride','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260324q.png'),
(260325,'chemistry','Etard reaction works on which principle ______?','oxidation','Use of chromyl chloride : \n\nChromyl chloride oxidises methyl group to a chromium complex, which on hydrolysis gives corresponding benzaldehyde.\n\nThis reaction is called Etard reaction. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -6)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','hydrogenation','reduction','hydration','oxidation','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(260326,'chemistry','Name the intermediate product in the following diagram?(n26015)','benzylidene diacetate','Use of chromic oxide (CrO₃ ): \n\nToluene or substituted toluene is converted to benzylidene diacetate on treating with chromic oxide in acetic anhydride. \n\nThe benzylidene diacetate can be hydrolysed to corresponding benzaldehyde with aqueous acid.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','toluene diacetate','benzylidene diacetate','acetic anhydride','benzylidene anhydride','NEW','12-363','12-233','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260326q.png'),
(260327,'chemistry','On treating with chromic oxide, toluene is converted to','benzilidene diacetate','Use of chromic oxide (CrO₃): \n\nToluene or substituted toluene is converted to benzylidene diacetate on treating with chromic oxide in acetic anhydride. \n\nThe benzylidene diacetate can be hydrolysed to corresponding benzaldehyde with aqueous acid.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'2','mcq','benzaldehyde','benzyl acetate','benzilidene diacetate','chromium complex','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(260401,'chemistry','The physical state of methanal  at room temperature is?','gas','Methanal is a gas at room temperature. \n\nEthanal is a volatile liquid. \n\nOther aldehydes and ketones are liquid or solid at room temperature. \n\nThe boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are higher than hydrocarbons and ethers of comparable molecular masses. \n\nIt is due to weak molecular association in aldehydes and ketones arising out of the dipole-dipole interactions. \n\nAlso, their boiling points are lower than those of alcohols of similar molecular masses due to absence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','volatile liquid','gas','liquid','solid','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(2000043745,'physics','At a certain place, the angle of dip is $30^{\\circ}$ and the horizontal component of earth\'s magnetic field 0.50 oerested. The earth\'s total magnetic field (in oerested) is','C','$B=\\dfrac{H}{\\cos \\theta}=\\dfrac{0.50}{\\cos 30^{\\circ}}=\\dfrac{0.50 \\times 2}{\\sqrt{3}}=1 / \\sqrt{3}$<br> So, correct option is (3).','The Bar Magnet','Magnetism and Matter','Medium',NULL,'$\\sqrt{3}$','1','$\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$','$\\dfrac{1}{2}$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(260402,'chemistry','The physical state of ethanal  at room temperature is?','volatile liquid','Methanal is a gas at room temperature. \n\nEthanal is a volatile liquid. \n\nOther aldehydes and ketones are liquid or solid at room temperature. \n\nThe boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are higher than hydrocarbons and ethers of comparable molecular masses. \n\nIt is due to weak molecular association in aldehydes and ketones arising out of the dipole-dipole interactions. \n\nAlso, their boiling points are lower than those of alcohols of similar molecular masses due to absence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','volatile liquid','gas','liquid','solid','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(260403,'chemistry','The boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are ______ than hydrocarbons and ethers of comparable molecular masses.','higher','Methanal is a gas at room temperature. \n\nEthanal is a volatile liquid. \n\nOther aldehydes and ketones are liquid or solid at room temperature. \n\nThe boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are higher than hydrocarbons and ethers of comparable molecular masses. \n\nIt is due to weak molecular association in aldehydes and ketones arising out of the dipole-dipole interactions. \n\nAlso, their boiling points are lower than those of alcohols of similar molecular masses due to absence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','higher','lower','equal','never higher','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(260404,'chemistry','The boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are higher than hydrocarbons and ethers of comparable molecular masses due to?','dipole-dipole bond','Methanal is a gas at room temperature.\n \nEthanal is a volatile liquid. \n\nOther aldehydes and ketones are liquid or solid at room temperature. \n\nThe boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are higher than hydrocarbons and ethers of comparable molecular masses. \n\nIt is due to weak molecular association in aldehydes and ketones arising out of the dipole-dipole interactions. \n\nAlso, their boiling points are lower than those of alcohols of similar molecular masses due to absence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','van der Waals’ forces','ion-dipole bond','dipole-dipole bond','hydrogen bonding','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(260405,'chemistry','Their boiling points are lower than those of alcohols of similar molecular masses due to absence of?','hydrogen bonding','Methanal is a gas at room temperature. \n\nEthanal is a volatile liquid. \n\nOther aldehydes and ketones are liquid or solid at room temperature. \n\nThe boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are higher than hydrocarbons and ethers of comparable molecular masses. \n\nIt is due to weak molecular association in aldehydes and ketones arising out of the dipole-dipole interactions. \n\nAlso, their boiling points are lower than those of alcohols of similar molecular masses due to absence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','van der Waals’ forces','ion-dipole bond','dipole-dipole bond','hydrogen bonding','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(260406,'chemistry','The methanal, ethanal and propanone are miscible with water because they form?','hydrogen bonding','The lower members of aldehydes and ketones such as methanal, ethanal and propanone are miscible with water in all proportions, because they form hydrogen bond with water.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','van der Waals’ forces','ion-dipole bond','dipole-dipole bond','hydrogen bonding','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(260407,'chemistry','As the size of the aldehydes and ketones increases, their odor becomes','less pungent','The solubility of aldehydes and ketones decreases rapidly on increasing the length of alkyl chain. \n\nAll aldehydes and ketones are fairly soluble in organic solvents like benzene, ether, methanol, chloroform, etc. \n\nThe lower aldehydes have sharp pungent odours. \n\nAs the size of the molecule increases, the odour becomes less pungent and more fragrant. \n\nIn fact, many naturally occurring aldehydes and ketones are used in the blending of perfumes and flavouring agents.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','more pungent','less pungent','more toxic','less fragrant','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(260408,'chemistry','As the size of the molecule increases, the odour becomes more _____.','more fragrant','The solubility of aldehydes and ketones decreases rapidly on increasing the length of alkyl chain. \n\nAll aldehydes and ketones are fairly soluble in organic solvents like benzene, ether, methanol, chloroform, etc. \n\nThe lower aldehydes have sharp pungent odours. \n\nAs the size of the molecule increases, the odour becomes less pungent and more fragrant. \n\nIn fact, many naturally occurring aldehydes and ketones are used in the blending of perfumes and flavouring agents.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','more pungent','less toxic','more fragrant','less fragrant','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(260409,'chemistry','On increasing the length of alkyl chain the solubility of aldehydes and ketones','decreases','The solubility of aldehydes and ketones decreases rapidly on increasing the length of alkyl chain. \n\nAll aldehydes and ketones are fairly soluble in organic solvents like benzene, ether, methanol, chloroform, etc.\n \nThe lower aldehydes have sharp pungent odours. \n\nAs the size of the molecule increases, the odour becomes less pungent and more fragrant. \n\nIn fact, many naturally occurring aldehydes and ketones are used in the blending of perfumes and flavouring agents.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','increases','decreases','first increase, then decrease','variable','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(260410,'chemistry','All aldehydes and ketones are  soluble in  all of them ,except','ethanol','The solubility of aldehydes and ketones decreases rapidly on increasing the length of alkyl chain. \n\nAll aldehydes and ketones are fairly soluble in organic solvents like benzene, ether, methanol, chloroform, etc.\n \nThe lower aldehydes have sharp pungent odours. \n\nAs the size of the molecule increases, the odour becomes less pungent and more fragrant. \n\nIn fact, many naturally occurring aldehydes and ketones are used in the blending of perfumes and flavouring agents.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','benzene','ether','chloroform','ethanol','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(151409,'chemistry','A : Fe₃O₄ is ferrimagnetic at room temperature but becomes paramagnetic at 850 K.\n\nR : The magnetic moment in Fe₃O₄ are aligned equally in parallel and antiparallel directions which on heating  randomise.','A is true but R is false.','A is true but R is wrong because in ferrimagnetic magnetic moment are aligned inequally.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(260411,'chemistry','Choose the correct alternative from the following regarding physical properties of carbonyl compounds.','When size of aldehyde increases, odour becomes less pungent and more fragrant.','The solubility of aldehydes and ketones decreases rapidly on increasing the length of alkyl chain. \n\nAll aldehydes and ketones are fairly soluble in organic solvents like benzene, ether, methanol, chloroform, etc. \n\nThe lower aldehydes have sharp pungent odours. \n\nAs the size of the molecule increases, the odour becomes less pungent and more fragrant. \n\nIn fact, many naturally occurring aldehydes and ketones are used in the blending of perfumes and flavouring agents.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-365 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','When size of aldehyde increases, odour becomes less pungent and more fragrant.','They are insoluble in organic solvent.','Solubility increases rapidly on increasing the length of the alkyl chain.','None of the above','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(260412,'chemistry','Arrange the following compounds in the increasing order of their boiling points.','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃< H₅C₂-O-C₂H₅< CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO < CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH','The molecular masses of these compounds are in the range of 72 to 74. \n\nSince only butan-1-ol molecules are associated due to extensive intermolecular hydrogen bonding, therefore, the boiling point of butan-1-ol would be the highest. \n\nButanal is more polar than ethoxyethane. \n\nTherefore, the intermolecular dipole-dipole attraction is stronger in the former. n-Pentane molecules have only weak van der Waals forces.\n\nReference: (NCERT-Class 12 Page-365 Solution of example','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃< H₅C₂-O-C₂H₅< CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO < CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃< H₅C₂-O-C₂H₅< CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO > CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃> H₅C₂-O-C₂H₅>CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO > CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃> H₅C₂-O-C₂H₅< CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO >CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂OH','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(260413,'chemistry','Arrange the following compounds in the increasing order of their boiling points.','CH₃CH₃<CH₃OCH₃<CH₃CHO<CH₃CH₂OH','The boiling points of aldehydes and ketones are higher than hydrocarbons and ethers of comparable molecular masses. \n\nIt is due to weak molecular association in aldehydes and ketones arising out of the dipole-dipole interactions. \n\nAlso, their boiling points are lower than those of alcohols of similar molecular masses due to absence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding.\n\nReference : (NCERT-Class 12 Page-365 paragraph#1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Physical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CH₂OH>CH₃OCH₃>CH₃CHO>CH₃CH₃','CH₃CH₂OH<CH₃OCH₃<CH₃CHO<CH₃CH₃','CH₃CH₃<CH₃OCH₃<CH₃CHO<CH₃CH₂OH','None of the above','NEW','12-365','12-235',NULL),
(260414,'biology','Which one the following is a Sexually Transmitted Disease?','Syphilis','Syphilis is one of the Sexually Transmitted Diseases.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'1','mcq','Cancer','Syphilis','Amoebiasis','Myocarditis','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(260415,'biology','The status of the fetus for genetic counselling can be determined by?','Amniocentesis','Genetic and chromosomal disorder at the foetus stage can be determined by Aminocentesis.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'1','mcq','Amino detection','Amniocentesis','Aminoacidopathy','Fetocentesis','NEW','12-058','12-042',NULL),
(260416,'biology','Which of the following is a method of birth control?','IUD','Intra Uterine Devices (IUD) are used for birth control methods.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'3','mcq','IUD','GIFT','HTF','IVF-ET','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(260417,'biology','The test which is misused for knowing the gender of the unborn baby is?','Amniocentesis','By using the method of Amniocentesis, the sex of a baby can be determined, which resulted in female foeticides. Therefore, the ban has been imposed on Amniocentesis.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'1','mcq','Clotting Test','Amniocentesis','Erythroblastosis','Angiogram','NEW','12-058','12-042',NULL),
(260418,'biology','Amniocentesis detects?','Hereditary disease','Amniocentesis alerts about the presence of any genetic deformity in a foetus.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'2','mcq','Cerebral Disorders','Cardiovascular Disorders','Hereditary disease','Skin Disorders','NEW','12-058','12-042',NULL),
(260419,'biology','The primary mechanism of action of Copper-T is?','Inhibit Fertilisation','The sole purpose of all IUDs is to inhibit Fertilisation, though its secondary role is to prevent implantation','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'1','mcq','Inhibit ovulation','Inhibit Fertilisation','Inhibit Implantation of blastocyst','Inhibit gametogenesis','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(260420,'biology','Which of the following is a copper releasing IUD?','Multiload375','Multiload375 is an Copper Releasing IUDs.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'3','mcq','Multiload375','LNG20','Progestasert','Oestrasert','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(260421,'biology','IUDs increase the phagocytosis of?','Sperms','IUDs increase the phagocytosis of sperms. Therefore, suppressing the entry of Sperms in to Female Genital Tract.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'3','mcq','Sperms','Semen','Seminal fluid','Egg','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(260422,'biology','Where does the phagocytosis of sperms by the IUDs occur?','Uterus','IUDs increase the phagocytosis of sperms within the uterus.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'2','mcq','Uterus','Vagina','Fallopian Tubes','Cervix,Vagina','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(260423,'biology','Which of the following statements is false regrading Cu Ions?','Increase conception','Cu Ions suppress fertilisation, thus, simultaneously Inhibiting conception.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'2','mcq','Suppress Sperm Motility','Suppress fertilising capacity of sperms','Increase conception','Lower Sperms capabilities.','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(260424,'biology','The hormone releasing IUDs dont act by ___?','Increasing chance of ovulation','The hormone releasing IUDs make the uterus unsuitable for implantation and the cervix hostile to the sperms.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'1','mcq','Acting on the FSH,LH pathways','Make the uterus unsuitable for implantation','Increasing cervix hostility','Increasing chance of ovulation','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(260425,'biology','Select the incorrect statement regarding IUDs. \ni)IUDs are ideal contraceptives for the females who want to delay pregnancy \nii)New generation IUDs either release Cu ions or hormones \niii)IUDs are self-insertible devices \niv)IUDs are ideal contraceptives for the females who want to space children','only iii','IUDs are ideal contraceptives for the females who want to delay pregnancy and/or space children, but they are inserted by expert doctors and nurses.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'1','mcq','i and iv','only iii','only iv','ii and iv','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(260426,'biology','Which of following is True?','IUDs make Uterus incompatible for implantation','IUDs make Uterus unsuitable for Implantation, make the cervix hostile, and prevent conception.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'2','mcq','IUDs make Uterus incompatible for implantation','IUDs decrease cervix hostility','IUDs don\'t prevent conception.','IUDs release Cd ions','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(260427,'biology','_____ is one of the most widely accepted methods of contraception in India.','IUDs','IUDs are one of most widely accepted methods of contraception in India.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'1','mcq','IUDs','Saheli','GIFT','In-vitro Fertilisation','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(260428,'biology','Identify the following diagram . (2603)','Copper T','Figure 4.2 NCERT Class 12 page no. 60  (2603).','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'1','mcq','Copper T','LNG-20','Condom for female','Tubectomy','NEW','12-060','12-044','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/260428q.png'),
(260429,'biology','Identify the following diagram .(2604)','Implants','Figure 4.3 NCERT Class 12 page no. 61  (2604).','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Birth control and IUDs',NULL,'1','mcq','Saheli','Implants','CuT','Cu-Strips','NEW','12-060','12-044','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/260429q.png'),
(260501,'chemistry','State True or False?\n\na. A nucleophile attacks the electrophile carbon atom of the nonpolar carbonyl group.\n\nb. A electrophile attacks the nucleophile carbon atom of the polar carbonyl group.','F,T','A nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon atom of the polar carbonyl group from a direction approximately perpendicular to the plane of sp2 hybridised orbitals of carbonyl carbon. \n\nThe hybridisation of carbon changes from sp2 to sp3 in this process, and a tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate is produced.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','NEW','12-366','12-236',NULL),
(260502,'chemistry','The hybridisation of carbon changes from sp² to sp³ in','nucleophillic addition reaction','A nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon atom of the polar carbonyl group from a direction approximately perpendicular to the plane of sp2 hybridised orbitals of carbonyl carbon. \n\nThe hybridisation of carbon changes from sp2 to sp3 in this process, and a tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate is produced.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','nucleophillic substitution reaction','nucleophillic addition reaction','eletrophillic addition reaction','electrophillic substitution reaction','NEW','12-366','12-236',NULL),
(260503,'chemistry','The attack of nucleophile occurs in direction approximately _____to the plane of carbonyl group.','perpendicular','A nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon atom of the polar carbonyl group from a direction approximately perpendicular to the plane of sp2 hybridised orbitals of carbonyl carbon. \n\nThe hybridisation of carbon changes from sp2 to sp3 in this process, and a tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate is produced.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','parallel','perpendicular','opposite','random','NEW','12-366','12-236',NULL),
(260504,'chemistry','Select the correct statement(s) for the given diagram:\n(n26024)','The net result is the addition of Nu and H+ across the carbon oxygen double bond','A nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon atom of the polar carbonyl group from a direction approximately perpendicular to the plane of sp2 hybridised orbitals of carbonyl carbon. \n\nThe hybridisation of carbon changes from sp2 to sp3 in this process, and a tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate is produced.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','A nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon of the non-polar carbonyl group from direction parallel to the plane of sp-hybridised orbitals of carbonyl carbon','The hybridisation of carbon changes from sp to sp² in this process','The net result is the addition of Nu and H+ across the carbon oxygen double bond','Both (a) and (c)','NEW','12-366','12-236','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260504q.png'),
(260505,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement from the following:','Aldehydes are more reactive than ketones towards NAR','Aldehydes are generally more reactive than ketones in nucleophilic addition reactions due to steric and electronic reasons. \n\nSterically, the presence of two relatively large substituents in ketones hinder the approach of nucleophile to carbonyl carbon than in aldehydes having only one such substituent. \n\nElectronically, aldehydes are more reactive than ketones because two alkyl groups reduce the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon more effectively than in former.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Ketones are more reactive than aldehydes towards NAR','Aldehydes are more reactive than ketones towards NAR','Formaldehyde is the least reactive carbonyl compounds towards NSR','Steric hindrance does not play a role to effect the reactivity of carbonyl compounds towards NAR','NEW','12-366','12-236',NULL),
(260506,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following:\nAssertion (A): a-hydrogen atoms of carbonyl compounds are acidic.\nReason (R): The strong electron releasing effect of the carbonyl group make the stabilisation of the conjugate base by the resonance.','A is correct; but R is incorrect','The acidity of α-hydrogen atoms of carbonyl compounds is due to the strong electron withdrawing effect of the carbonyl group and resonance stabilisation of the conjugate base.\n\nReference:(NCERT -Class-12 Page-371 Paragraph -1)\n\nRefer diagram(ne26098)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are correct; and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are correct; but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is correct; but R is incorrect','R is correct; but A is incorrect','NEW','12-266','12-236',NULL),
(260507,'chemistry','Why equilibrium lies largely to the right hand side for most aldehydes and the left for most ketones in the given diagram(n26025)','steric reasons','The position of the equilibrium lies largely to the right hand side for most aldehydes and to the left for most ketones due to steric reasons. \n\nThe hydrogen sulphite addition compound is water soluble and can be converted back to the original carbonyl compound by treating it with dilute mineral acid or alkali. \n\nTherefore, these are useful for separation and purification of aldehydes.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','electronic reasons','steric reasons','both (a) and (b)','bonding reasons','NEW','12-367','12-237','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260507q.png'),
(260508,'chemistry','The hydrogen sulphite addition compound is','water soluble','The position of the equilibrium lies largely to the right hand side for most aldehydes and to the left for most ketones due to steric reasons. \n\nThe hydrogen sulphite addition compound is water soluble and can be converted back to the original carbonyl compound by treating it with dilute mineral acid or alkali. \n\nTherefore, these are useful for separation and purification of aldehydes.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','water soluble','water insoluble','solvent in organic solvent','soluble in aceton','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260509,'chemistry','State True or False\n\na. The hydrogen sulphite addition product can be converted back to the original carbonyl compound.\n\nb. The hydrogen sulphate addition product can also be converted back to the original carbonyl compound.','T,F','The position of the equilibrium lies largely to the right hand side for most aldehydes and to the left for most ketones due to steric reasons. \n\nThe hydrogen sulphite addition compound is water soluble and can be converted back to the original carbonyl compound by treating it with dilute mineral acid or alkali. \n\nTherefore, these are useful for separation and purification of aldehydes.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260510,'chemistry','The hydrogen sulphite addition compound is water soluble and can be converted back to the original carbonyl compound by treating it with?','dilute acid, alkali','The position of the equilibrium lies largely to the right hand side for most aldehydes and to the left for most ketones due to steric reasons. \n\nThe hydrogen sulphite addition compound is water soluble and can be converted back to the original carbonyl compound by treating it with dilute mineral acid or alkali. \n\nTherefore, these are useful for separation and purification of aldehydes.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','dilute acid','water','dilute acid, alkali','concentrated acid, water','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260511,'chemistry','The hydrogen sulphite addition compound are useful for all, except','estimation of molecular weight of aldehydes','The position of the equilibrium lies largely to the right hand side for most aldehydes and to the left for most ketones due to steric reasons.\n \nThe hydrogen sulphite addition compound is water soluble and can be converted back to the original carbonyl compound by treating it with dilute mineral acid or alkali. \n\nTherefore, these are useful for separation and purification of aldehydes.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','purification of aldehyde','seperation of aldehyde','estimation of molecular weight of aldehydes','being converted back to the original carbonyl compound','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260512,'chemistry','Name the end product in given diagram(n26026)','cyanohydrin','Aldehydes and ketones react with hydrogen cyanide (HCN) to yield cyanohydrins. \n\nThis reaction occurs very slowly with pure HCN. \n\nTherefore, it is catalysed by a base and the generated cyanide ion (CN-) being a stronger nucleophile readily adds to carbonyl compounds to yield corresponding cyanohydrin. \n\nCyanohydrins are useful synthetic intermediates.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','nitriles','cyanohydrin','cyanide','aldehyde','NEW','12-367','12-237','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260512q.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(260513,'chemistry','Arrange the following compounds in increasing order of their property as indicated:\n\nAcetaldehyde, acetone, di-tertiary-butyl ketone, methyl tertiary-butyl ketone (reactivity towards HCN)','di-tertiary butyl ketone < methyl tertiary butyl ketone < acetone < acetaldehyde','Aldehydes are generally more reactive than ketones in nucleophilic addition reactions due to steric and electronic reasons.\n\nSterically, the presence of two relatively large substituents in ketones hinders the approach of nucleophile to carbonyl carbon than in aldehydes having only one such substituent. \n\nElectronically, aldehydes are more reactive than ketones because two alkyl groups reduce the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon more effectively than in former. \n\nAddition of HCN is the example of nucleophilic addition reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 topic:reactivity)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','di-tertiary butyl ketone < methyl tertiary butyl ketone < acetone < acetaldehyde','Acetone < acetaldehyde < methyl tertiary butyl ketone < di-tertiary butyl ketone','Acetaldehyde < methyl tertiary butyl ketone < acetone < di-tertiary butyl ketone','Methyl tertiary butyl ketone < di-tertiary butyl ketone < acetone < acetaldehyde','NEW','12-366','12-236',NULL),
(260514,'chemistry','The cyanide ion (CN⁻) is a stronger _______.','nucleophile','Aldehydes and ketones react with hydrogen cyanide (HCN) to yield cyanohydrins. \n\nThis reaction occurs very slowly with pure HCN. \n\nTherefore, it is catalysed by a base and the generated cyanide ion (CN-) being a stronger nucleophile readily adds to carbonyl compounds to yield corresponding cyanohydrin. \n\nCyanohydrins are useful synthetic intermediates.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','electrophile','nucleophile','reagent','solvent','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260515,'chemistry','Cyanohydrins are useful ________ intermediates.','synthetic','Aldehydes and ketones react with hydrogen cyanide (HCN) to yield cyanohydrins.\n\nThis reaction occurs very slowly with pure HCN. \n\nTherefore, it is catalysed by a base and the generated cyanide ion (CN-) being a stronger nucleophile readily adds to carbonyl compounds to yield corresponding cyanohydrin. \n\nCyanohydrins are useful synthetic intermediates.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-367 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','natural','pure','synthetic','all of the above','NEW','12-367','12-237',NULL),
(260601,'chemistry','Formaldehyde is well known as............. used to preserve biological specimens.','Formalin (40%) solution','In chemical industry aldehydes and ketones are used as solvents, starting materials and reagents for the synthesis of other products. \n\nFormaldehyde is well known as formalin (40%) solution used to preserve biological specimens and to prepare bakelite (a phenol-formaldehyde resin), urea-formaldehyde glues and other polymeric products. \n\nAcetaldehyde is used primarily as a starting material in the manufacture of acetic acid, ethyl acetate, vinyl acetate, polymers and drugs. \n\nBenzaldehyde is used in perfumery and in dye industries. \n\nAcetone and ethyl methyl ketone are common industrial solvents. \n\nMany aldehydes and ketones, e.g., butyraldehyde, vanillin, acetophenone, camphor, etc. are well known for their odours and flavours.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-373 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Uses of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','Acetylene (40%) solution','Acetylene (70%) solution','Formalin (40%) solution','Formalin (70%) solution','NEW','12-373','12-243',NULL),
(151410,'chemistry','A : In molecular solids the lattice points are occupied by the atoms or molecules.\n\nR : Molecular solids are generally sublime.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','In molecular solids, the constituent atoms or molecules occupy the lattice points in a crystal lattice.\nMolecular solids generally have weak intermolecular forces between the constituent molecules, which makes them easily vaporizable upon heating without melting. This process is known as sublimation.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(260602,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement from the following.','Carbolic acid is also known as benzaldehyde','In chemical industry aldehydes and ketones are used as solvents, starting materials and reagents for the synthesis of other products. \n\nFormaldehyde is well known as formalin (40%) solution used to preserve biological specimens and to prepare bakelite (a phenol-formaldehyde resin), urea-formaldehyde glues and other polymeric products.\n\nAcetaldehyde is used primarily as a starting material in the manufacture of acetic acid, ethyl acetate, vinyl acetate, polymers and drugs. Benzaldehyde is used in perfumery and in dye industries.\n\nAcetone and ethyl methyl ketone are common industrial solvents. \n\nMany aldehydes and ketones, e.g., butyraldehyde, vanillin, acetophenone, camphor, etc. are well known for their odours and flavours.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-373 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Uses of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'2','mcq','Benzaldehyde is used in perfumery','Formalin is used as a disinfectant','Acetone is one of the constituent of liquid nail polish','Carbolic acid is also known as benzaldehyde','NEW','12-373','12-243',NULL),
(260603,'chemistry','_______ is used in perfumery.','benzaldehyde','In chemical industry aldehydes and ketones are used as solvents, starting materials and reagents for the synthesis of other products. \n\nFormaldehyde is well known as formalin (40%) solution used to preserve biological specimens and to prepare bakelite (a phenol-formaldehyde resin), urea-formaldehyde glues and other polymeric products. \n\nAcetaldehyde is used primarily as a starting material in the manufacture of acetic acid, ethyl acetate, vinyl acetate, polymers and drugs. \n\nBenzaldehyde is used in perfumery and in dye industries. \n\nAcetone and ethyl methyl ketone are common industrial solvents. \n\nMany aldehydes and ketones, e.g., butyraldehyde, vanillin, acetophenone, camphor, etc. are well known for their odours and flavours.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-373 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Uses of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','mesityl oxide','benzyl alcohol','benzaldehyde','vanillic acid','NEW','12-373','12-243',NULL),
(260604,'chemistry','______ is used as a disinfectant.','formalin','In chemical industry aldehydes and ketones are used as solvents, starting materials and reagents for the synthesis of other products. \n\nFormaldehyde is well known as formalin (40%) solution used to preserve biological specimens and to prepare bakelite (a phenol-formaldehyde resin), urea-formaldehyde glues and other polymeric products.\n\nAcetaldehyde is used primarily as a starting material in the manufacture of acetic acid, ethyl acetate, vinyl acetate, polymers and drugs. \n\nBenzaldehyde is used in perfumery and in dye industries. \n\nAcetone and ethyl methyl ketone are common industrial solvents.\n \nMany aldehydes and ketones, e.g., butyraldehyde, vanillin, acetophenone, camphor, etc. are well known for their odours and flavours.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-373 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Uses of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','Acetylene','formalin','carbolic acid','none of the above','NEW','12-373','12-243',NULL),
(260605,'chemistry','______ is one of the constituent of liquid nail polish.','acetone','In chemical industry aldehydes and ketones are used as solvents, starting materials and reagents for the synthesis of other products.\n\nAcetone and ethyl methyl ketone are common industrial solvents e.g nail polish. \n\nMany aldehydes and ketones, e.g., butyraldehyde, vanillin, acetophenone, camphor, etc. are well known for their odours and flavours.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-373 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Uses of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','acetone','acetylene','formalin','carbolic acid','NEW','12-373','12-243',NULL),
