(261001,'chemistry','The carboxylic acids like alcohols evolve hydrogen with _____ metals.','Electropositive','The carboxylic acids like alcohols evolve hydrogen with electropositive metals and form salts with alkalies similar to phenols. \n\nHowever, unlike phenols they react with weaker bases such as carbonates and hydrogen carbonates to evolve carbon dioxide. \n\nThis reaction is used to detect the presence of carboxyl group in an organic compound.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-379 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Electropositive','electronegative','basic','none of the above','NEW','12-378','12-248',NULL),
(261002,'chemistry','The carboxylic acids form salts with _____ similar to phenols.','alkali','The carboxylic acids like alcohols evolve hydrogen with electropositive metals and form salts with alkalies similar to phenols. \n\nHowever, unlike phenols they react with weaker bases such as carbonates and hydrogen carbonates to evolve carbon dioxide. \n\nThis reaction is used to detect the presence of carboxyl group in an organic compound.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-379 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','acid','alkali','alcohol','All of the above','NEW','12-378','12-248',NULL),
(261005,'chemistry','Although phenoxide ion has more number of resonating structures than carboxylate ion, carboxylic acid is a stronger acid than phenol. Why?','Because in carboxylate ion the negative charge is at the more electronegative oxygen atom while in phenoxide ion negative charge is on less electronegative carbon atom.','The conjugate base of carboxylic acid, a carboxylate ion, is stabilised by two equivalent resonance structures in which the negative charge is at the more electronegative oxygen atom. \n\nThe conjugate base of phenol, a phenoxide ion, has non-equivalent resonance structures in which the negative charge is at the less electronegative carbon atom. \n\nTherefore, resonance in phenoxide ion is not as important as it is in carboxylate ion.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-379 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Because carboxylate ion has negative charge delocalised over only one O-atom','Because in carboxylate ion the negative charge is at the more electronegative oxygen atom while in phenoxide ion negative charge is on less electronegative carbon atom.','Phenoxide ion has equivalent structure','Phenoxide ion has negative charge localised on an O-atom','NEW','12-378','12-248',NULL),
(261006,'chemistry','The carboxylic acids  react with weaker bases such as carbonates and hydrogen carbonates to evolve ______.','carbon dioxide','The carboxylic acids like alcohols evolve hydrogen with electropositive metals and form salts with alkalies similar to phenols. \n\nHowever, unlike phenols they react with weaker bases such as carbonates and hydrogen carbonates to evolve carbon dioxide. \n\nThis reaction is used to detect the presence of carboxyl group in an organic compound.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-379 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','water','hydrogen','carbon dioxide','oxygen','NEW','12-378','12-248',NULL),
(261007,'chemistry','Carboxylic acids dissociate in water to give resonance stabilised?','carboxylate anions,hydronium ion','Carboxylic acids dissociate in water to give resonance stabilised carboxylate anions and hydronium ion.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-379 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','carboxylate anions,hydronium ion','carboxylate cations,hydronium ion','alcoholic','carboxyl group only','NEW','12-379','12-249',NULL),
(261008,'chemistry','Carboxylic acids, in comparison of mineral acids and alcohols respectively are?','weaker and stronger','Carboxylic acids are weaker than mineral acids, but they are stronger acids than alcohols and many simple phenols (pKa is ~16 for ethanol and 10 for phenol). \n\nIn fact, carboxylic acids are amongst the most acidic organic compounds.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','stronger and weaker','weaker and stronger','of equal strength and weaker','weaker and of equal stronger','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261009,'chemistry','The effect of the following electron withdrawing groups in increasing acidity order is?','CF₃>NO₂>CN>F','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid.\nCF₃>NO₂>CN>F\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','order','1,CF₃','2,NO₂','3,CN','4,F','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261010,'chemistry','Arrange in order of increasing acidity:','CF₃COOH>CCl₃COOH>CHCl₂COOH>NO₂CH₂COOH','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','order','1,CF₃COOH','2,CCl₃COOH','3,CHCl₂COOH','4,NO₂CH₂COOH','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261011,'chemistry','Arrange in order of increasing acidity:','BrCH₂COOH<ClCH₂COOH<FCH₂COOH<NC-CH₂COOH','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','order','1,NC-CH₂COOH','2,FCH₂COOH','3,ClCH₂COOH','4,BrCH₂COOH','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261012,'chemistry','Arrange in order of increasing acidity:','ClCH₂CH₂COOH>HCOOH>CH₃COOH>CH₃CH₂COOH','Electron withdrawing group (EWG) stabilises the carboxylate anion and strengthens the acid.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-380 Paragraph -Last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','order','1,HCOOH','2,ClCH₂CH₂COOH','3,CH₃COOH','4,CH₃CH₂COOH','NEW','12-380','12-250',NULL),
(261013,'chemistry','Direct attachment of groups such as phenyl or vinyl to the carboxylic acid ______ the acidity of corresponding carboxylic acid.','increases','Direct attachment of groups such as phenyl or vinyl to the carboxylic acid, increases the acidity of corresponding carboxylic acid, contrary to the decrease expected due to resonance effect.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','increases','decreases','has no effect','none of the above','NEW','12-381','12-251',NULL),
(261014,'chemistry','Name the effect shown in diagram(n26060)','resonance','Direct attachment of groups such as phenyl or vinyl to the carboxylic acid, increases the acidity of corresponding carboxylic acid, contrary to the decrease expected due to resonance effect.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','resonance','steric repulsion','polarity','H-bonding','NEW','12-381','12-251','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261014q.png'),
(261015,'chemistry','Carboxylic acids on heating with mineral acids or with P₂O₅ gives _____','anhydride','Carboxylic acids on heating with mineral acids such as sulfuric acid or with P₂O₅ give corresponding anhydride.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-381 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','alcohol','anhydride','aldehyde','no reaction','NEW','12-381','12-251',NULL),
(261103,'chemistry','Many aldehydes and ketones are well known for their?','odour and flavour','Many aldehydes and ketones, e.g., butyraldehyde,  vanillin, acetophenone, camphor, etc. are well known for their odours and flavours.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-373 Paragraph -last)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Chemical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','reactive nature towards ortho and /rara-position while electrophilic substitution','oval shape','odour and flavour','reactivity towards oxidation and reduction even by weak reagents','NEW','12-373','12-243',NULL),
(261106,'biology','Select the option including all the Sexually Transmitted Diseases[NEET 2020]','Gonorrhoea,Syphilis,Genital herpes','Malaria, filaria and cancer are not sexually transmitted diseases (STDs).','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Gonorrhoea,Syphilis,Genital herpes','Gonorrhoea, Malaria,Genital Herpes','AIDS,Malaria,Filaria','Cancer,AIDS,Syphilis','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(261107,'biology','Match the following [NEET 2017]','Gonorrhoea-Neisseria,Syphilis-Treponema, Genital warts-Human Papilloma Virus, AIDS-HIV','Gonorrhoea-Neisseria,Syphilis-Treponema, Genital wrats-Human Papilloma Virus, AIDS-HIV','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','match','Gonorrhoea,Neisseria','Syphilis,Treponema','Genital warts,Human Papilloma Virus','AIDS,HIV','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(261108,'biology','Which of the following isn\'t a Sexually Transmitted Disease?','Encephalitis','Encephalitis is caused due to inflammation and its not an STD.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Trichomoniasis','Encephalitis','Syphilis','Acquired Immuno Deficiency Syndrome','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(261109,'biology','Which of the following Sexually Transmitted Diseases is not completely curable?[NEET 2019]','Genital herpes','Antibiotics are effective in chlamydiasis. Gonorrhoea can also be treated by antibiotics but Genital herpes is an incurable STD. For treating genital warts, Cryosurgery is used for removing warts.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Chlamydiasis','Gonorrhoea','Genital warts','Genital herpes','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(261110,'biology','Which one of the following statements is correct regarding Sexually Transmitted Diseases (STDs)?[NEET 2013]','The chances of a 5-years boy contracting an STD is very little.','Genital herpes is an STD while sickle-cell anaemia is an autosomal hereditary disorder. The chances of a 5 year boy contracting an STD are very little since he is unlikely to have sex at this age.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','A person may contract syphilis by sharing milk with one already suffering from the disease.','Haemophilia is one of the STDs.','Genital herpes and sickle-cell anemia are both STDs.','The chances of a 5-years boy contracting an STD is very little.','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(261112,'biology','In which of the following techniques, the embryos are transferred to assist those females who cannot conceive?[NEET 2020]','ZIFT and IUT','The zygote or early embryo (with upto 8 blastomeres) could be transferred into the fallopian tube (ZIFT–Zygote Intra Fallopian Transfer) and embryos with more than 8 blastomeres, are transferred into the uterus (IUT – Intra Uterine Transfer), to allow its further development.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','ZIFT and IUT','GIFT and ZIFT','ICSI and ZIFT','GIFT and ICSI','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261113,'biology','In case of a couple where the male is having a very low sperm count, which of the following techniques will be suitable for fertilisation?[NEET 2017]','Artificial Insemination','Answer is Artificial Insemniation, because in this case mentioned in the question male alone has very low sperm count, whereas the female is normal with normal Ovaries, Tubes and Uterus, Artificial Insemination is preferred incases where the sperm quality (motility) or quantity is decreased and by bringing a closer proximity to the female gamete, the chances of fertilization are improved. ICSI is done only when the sperm is incapable of penetration of ovum even with normal motility and quantity. So in this case where the question mentions only quantity, AI (Artificial Insemniation) is the ART of choice.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Gamete Intracytoplasmic Fallopian Transfer','Artificial Insemination','Intracytoplasmic sperm injection','Both Artificial Insemination and Intracytoplasmic sperm injection.','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261114,'biology','Embryo with more than 16 blastomeres formed due to In-Vitro Fertilisation, is transferred into the?[NEET 2016]','Uterus','Embryo with more than 16 blastomeres formed during in-vitro fertilisation is transferred into the uterus.(Intra-Uterine transfer, IUT)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Uterus','Fallopian Tube','Ampulla','Cervix','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261115,'biology','In Assisted reproductive technology, IVF involves the transfer of which of the following living entities?[NEET 2014]','Zygote into the Fallopian Tube','IVF method involves In-vitro fertilisation (IVF), i.e., fertilisation of male\nand female gametes outside the body in almost similar\nconditions as that present in the body followed by the Embryo\nTransfer technique(ET).','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Ovum into the Fallopian Tube','Zygote into the Fallopian Tube','Zygote into the Uterus','Embryo with 16 blastomeres into the Fallopian Tube','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261116,'biology','A childless couple can be assisted to have a child through a technique called GIFT. The full form of this technique is?[NEET 2015]','Gamete Intra Fallopian Transfer.','Gamete Intra Fallopian Transfer (GIFT) is an assisted reproductive technology, in which both the sperms and the unfertilised oocytes (from the donor male and donor female) are transferred into the Fallopian Tube of a healthy recipient female (surrogate).','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Gamete Internal Fertilisation and Transfer','Germ cell Internal Fallopian Transfer','Gamete Insemination Fallopian Transfer','Gamete Intra Fallopian Transfer.','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261117,'biology','What does Artificial Insemination mean?[NEET 2013]','Artificial Introduction of sperms of a healthy donor into the vagina  of the recipient female.','In Artificial Insemination technique, the\nsemen of a healthy donor is introduced into the vagina or into uterus called intra-\nuterine insemination (IUI)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Artificial Introduction of sperms of a healthy donor into the vagina  of the recipient female.','Introduction of sperms of a healthy donor directly into the Fallopian Tube of the recipient female.','Transfer of sperms of a healthy donor to a test tube containing the ova of the donor female.','Transfer of sperms of husband to a test tube containing ova of a surrogate.','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261118,'biology','The stage at which the zygote is transferred into the uterus after induced fertilisation of ovum in the laboratory?\n[NEET 2013]','Morula','Cleavage divisions produce a solid ball of cells called morula. It consists of 8-16 cells.In Intra-Uterine Transfer (IUT) embryo with more than 8-blastomeres stage (morula) is used for transfer into the uterus after induced fertilisation in the laboaratory.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Blastula','Embryo at 2 blastomeres stage','Morula','Zygote','NEW','11-064','12-048',NULL),
(261119,'biology','Which of the following contraceptive methods involve the role of hormones ? [NEET 2019]','Lactational amenorrhea, Pills,Emergency contraceptives','The practice of safe sex when a woman is going through Lactational amenorrhea is based on the fact that ovulation and therefore, the menstrual cycle does not occur during the period of intense lactation following parturition. This is because breast feeding disrupts the pattern of pulsatile release of GnRH from hypothalamus and hence, the reduction in Gonadotropin Releasing Hormones. Pills are hormonal preparations (either progestogen or progestogen-estrogen combinations) in the form of tablets which are administrated orally by females. They inhibit ovulation, implantation as well as alter the quality of cervical mucus. Emergency contraceptives are pills that contain levonorgestrel, a type of progestin which helps to prevent pregnancy when taken within a few days after sex.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Pills,Emergency contraceptives,Barrier methods','Lactational amenorrhea, Pills,Emergency contraceptives','Barrier method, Lactational amenorrhea, Pills','CuT, Pills, Emergency contraceptive','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(261120,'biology','Select the hormone-releasing Intra-Uterine Device amongst the following. [NEET 2019]','Progestasert, LNG-20','Lippes Loop is a non-medicated Intra Uterine Device (IUD) while, Multiload 375 is a copper releasing IUD. Vault is a barrier method of birth control. It is a hemispheric dome-like rubber or plastic cap with thick rim meant for fitting over the cervix.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Lippes Loop, Multiload 375','Vaults, LNG-20','Multiload 375, Progestasert','Progestasert, LNG-20','NEW','11-060','12-044',NULL),
(261121,'biology','What is the action of the contraceptive, ‘Saheli’? [NEET 2018]','It blocks the estrogen receptors in the uterus, preventing eggs from getting implanted.','‘Saheli’ is a mini pill that contains a non- steroidal preparation called centchroman, which is taken once in a week after an initial intake dose of twice a week, for 3 months. It blocks the estrogen receptors in the uterus hence, alters the uterine endometrial lining and prevents the fertilised egg from being implanted.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','It blocks the estrogen receptors in the uterus, preventing eggs from getting implanted.','Increases the concentration of Estrogen and prevents ovulation in females.','Is an IUD.','Is a post-coital contraceptive.','NEW','11-061','12-045',NULL),
(261122,'biology','Select the option including all Sexually Transmitted Diseases. [NEET 2020]','Gonorrhoea, Syphilis, Genital herpes','Malaria, filariasis and cancer are not Sexually Transmitted Diseases (STDs).','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Gonorrhoea, Syphilis, Genital herpes','Gonorrhoea, Malaria, Genital herpes','AIDS, Malaria, Filariasis','Cancer, AIDS, Syphilis','NEW','11-063','12-047',NULL),
(261123,'biology','Which of the following sexually transmitted diseases is not completely curable? [NEET 2019]','Genital herpes','Antibiotics like Tetracycline, Erythromycin and Rifampicin are effective in chlamydiasis. Gonorrhoea can be treated by antibiotics such as Erythromycin, Ciprofloxacin, etc. For treating Genital warts, Cryosurgery is used for removing warts. Genital herpes is an incurable STD, while the others are curable and have good prognosis when detected early.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Chlamydiasis','Gonorrhoea','Genital warts','Genital herpes','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(261124,'biology','In which of the following techniques, the embryo is transferred, to assist those females who cannot conceive? [NEET 2020]','ZIFT and IUT','In vitro fertilisation, i.e.,fertilisation outside the body in almost similar conditions as that in the body, followed by Embryo Transfer (ET) method. In this method, ova from the wife or donor (female) and sperms from husband or donor (male) are collected and are induced to form a zygote, under simulated conditions in the laboratory. The zygote or early embryos (with upto 8 blastomeres) are then transferred into the fallopian tube (ZIFT–Zygote Intra Fallopian Transfer) and embryos with more than 8 blastomeres, are transferred into the uterus (IUT – Intra Uterine Transfer), to facilitate its further development.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','ZIFT and IUT','GIFT and ZIFT','ICSI and ZIFT','GIFT and ICSI','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261125,'biology','Venereal diseases can spread through : \n(a) Using sterile needles \n(b) Transfusion of blood from infected person to a normal healthy person \n(c) Infected mother to foetus\n(d) Kissing \n(e) Inheritance \nChoose the correct answer from the option given below [NEET 2021]','(b) and (c) only','Exchange of body fluids as in case of blood transfusion and foetal blood circulation (from mother to child) causes transmission of Sexually Transmitted Diseases or Venereal Diseases.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) and (c) only','(a), (b) and (c) only','(b), (c) and (d) only','(b) and (c) only','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(261126,'biology','Match the following. [NEET 2021]','Vaults-Entry of sperms through the Cervix is blocked; IUDs- Increase phagocytosis of sperms within the Uterus; Vasectomy-Removal of the Vas deferens; Tubectomy-Removal of the Fallopian Tube.','Vaults-Entry of sperms through the Cervix is blocked; IUDs- Increase phagocytosis of sperms within the Uterus; Vasectomy-Removal of the Vas deferens; Tubectomy-Removal of the Fallopian Tube.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','match','Vaults,Entry of sperm through Cervix is blocked','IUDs,Phagocytosis of sperms within the Uterus','Vasectomy,Removal of Vas deferens','Tubectomy,Removal of fallopian tube','NEW','12-062','12-046',NULL),
(261127,'biology','Which one of the following, is an example of Hormone releasing IUD?[NEET 2021]','LNG 20','Certain IUDs, like LNG20, release hormones to prevent implantation of the embryo and make the cervix hostile to the sperms.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Multiload 375','CuT','LNG 20','Cu 7','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(261128,'biology','Lippe’s loop is a type of contraceptive used as:[NEET 2022]','Non-Medicated IUD','Lippes Loop is a non-medicated Intra Uterine Device (IUD) while;Copper T,Multiload 375 is a copper releasing IUD. Vault is a barrier method of birth control. It is a hemispheric dome-like rubber or plastic cap with thick rim meant for fitting over the cervix.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Cervical barrier','Vault barrier','Non-Medicated IUD','Copper releasing IUD','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(261129,'biology','Match List-I with List-II with respect to methods of Contraception and their respective actions,[NEET 2022]\n(a) Diaphragms - (i) Inhibit ovulation and Implantation \n(b) Contraceptive - (ii) Increase phagocytosis of sperm within Uterus Pills \n(c) Intra uterine Devices - (iii) Absence of Menstrual cycle and ovulation following parturition \n(d) Lactational Amenorrhea - (iv) They cover the cervix blocking the entry of sperms','(a) - (iv), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iii)','Diaphragms-They cover the cervix blocking the entry of sperms and prevent entry of sperms,\nContraceptive Pills - Inhibit ovulation and Implantation as they contain combinations of estrogen and progestrone, \nIntra Uterine Devices- Increase the chance of phagocytosis of sperm within Uterus by presence of a foriegn body, \nLactational Amennorhea - Absence of menstrual cycle following parturition by negative feedback on FSH','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) - (iv), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iii)','(a) - (iv), (b) - (i), (c) - (iii), (d) - (ii)','(a) - (ii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (i), (d) - (iii)','(a) - (iii), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(261130,'biology','Which of the following statements are correct ? \n (A) Reproductive health refers to total well-being in \nall aspects of reproduction. \n (B) Amniocentesis is legally banned for sex \ndetermination in India. \n (C) "Saheli" – a new oral contraceptive for females \nwas developed in collaboration with ICMR (New Delhi). \n (D) Amniocentesis is used to determine genetic \ndisorders and survivability of foetus. \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the \noptions given below :','(3) (A), (B) and (D) only','Statement 1 is correct. Reproductive health refers to the total well-being and optimal functioning of all aspects related to reproduction, including physical, emotional, social, and behavioral aspects.\n\nStatement 2 is incorrect. Amniocentesis, a prenatal diagnostic procedure, is not legally banned for sex determination in India. However, it is regulated and allowed only for certain specific medical indications and not for sex determination purposes.\n\nStatement 3 is incorrect. "Saheli" is not a new oral contraceptive developed in collaboration with ICMR (Indian Council of Medical Research) in New Delhi. It is an oral contraceptive pill developed by Hindustan Latex Limited, a public sector undertaking in India.\n\nStatement 4 is correct. Amniocentesis is a prenatal diagnostic procedure used to determine genetic disorders and certain chromosomal abnormalities in the fetus. It involves the sampling of amniotic fluid surrounding the fetus for analysis. It can also provide information about the survivability of the fetus in certain cases.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (B) and (C) only','(2) (D) and (C) only','(3) (A), (B) and (D) only','(4) (A) and (C) only','NEW','12-058','12-042',NULL),
(261131,'biology','Given below are two statements :  Statement I :-\n Intra Cytoplasmic Sperm Injection (ICSI) is another \nspecialised procedure of in-vivo fertilisation. \nStatement II :-\n Infertility cases due to inability of the male partner to inseminate female can be corrected by artificial insemination (AI). \n In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Statement I is incorrect, but statement II is true.\n\nStatement I: Intra Cytoplasmic Sperm Injection (ICSI) is another specialized procedure of in-vivo fertilization. This statement is incorrect. Intra Cytoplasmic Sperm Injection (ICSI) is not a procedure of in-vivo fertilization. It is an assisted reproductive technology (ART) technique used in cases of severe male infertility. During ICSI, a single sperm is directly injected into an egg to achieve fertilization in vitro (outside the body).\n\nStatement II: Infertility cases due to inability of the male partner to inseminate female can be corrected by artificial insemination (AI). This statement is true. Artificial insemination (AI) is a technique used to treat infertility, specifically in cases where the male partner is unable to inseminate the female partner naturally. It involves the introduction of sperm into the reproductive tract of the female through artificial means, such as by placing prepared sperm into the cervix, uterus, or fallopian tubes. AI can be performed using the partner\'s sperm (artificial insemination by husband, AIH) or donor sperm (artificial insemination by donor, AID).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is that statement I is incorrect, but statement II is true.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(2) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','(1) Statement I is correct but statement II is \nfalse','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are true','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261132,'biology','Broad palm with single palm crease is visible in a person suffering from- [ NEET 2023]','(4) Down’s syndrome','Broad palm with single palm crease is visible in a person suffering from Down\'s syndrome.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Turner’s syndrome','(2) Klinefelter’s syndrome','(3) Thalassemia','(4) Down’s syndrome','NEW','12-058','12-042',NULL),
(261133,'biology','Which one of the following common sexually transmitted diseases is completely curable when detected early\nand treated properly? [NEET 2023]','(1) Gonorrhoea','Of these, 4 are currently curable: syphilis, gonorrhoea, chlamydia and trichomoniasis. The other 4 are incurable viral infections: hepatitis B, herpes simplex virus (HSV), HIV and human papillomavirus (HPV).','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Gonorrhoea','(2) Hepatitis-B','(3) HIV Infection','(4) Genital herpes','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261134,'biology','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A : Amniocentesis for sex determination is one of the strategies of Reproductive and Child Health\nCare Programme.\nReason R : Ban on amniocentesis checks increasing menace of female foeticide.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.','(3) A is false but R is true','Amniocentesis examines cases of female foeticide and is not used to determine sex.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Both A and R are true and R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','(2) A is true but R is false.','(3) A is false but R is true','(4) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','NEW','12-058','12-042',NULL),
(261135,'biology','IUDs are small objects made up of plastic or copper that are inserted in the uterine cavity. Which of the following statements are correct about IUDs ? \n (a) IUDs decrease phagocytosis of sperm within the uterus. \n (b) The released copper ions suppress the sperm motility. \n (c) IUDs do not make the cervix hostile to the sperm. \n (d) IUDs suppress the fertilization capacity of sperm. \n (e) The IUDs require surgical intervention for their insertion in the uterine cavity. \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below : [ RE NEET 2022]','(b) and (d) only','Option (d) is the correct answer as only statements (b) and (d) are correct because the Cu ions which are released suppress the sperm motility and the fertilising capacity of sperms.\n\nOption (a) is incorrect as statement (b) is also a correct statement.\n\nOption (b) is incorrect as IUDs increase the phagocytosis of sperms and IUD\'s insertion does not require surgical intervention.\n\nOption (c) is incorrect as hormonal IUDs suppress endometrial changes and changes in cervical mucus, thereby making the cervix hostile to the sperms.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(a), (d) and (e) only','(b) and (c) only','(b) and (d) only','(d) only','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(261136,'biology','Match List I with List II.\nList I - List II\nA. Vasectomy - I. Oral method\nB. Coitus interruptus - II. Barrier method\nC. Cervical caps - III. Surgical method\nD. Saheli - IV. Natural method\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I','A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I','A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV','A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III','A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(261137,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \n List-I - List-II \n (A) Cytokine barriers (I) Mucus coating of respiratory tract \n (B) Cellular barriers (II) Interferons \n (C) Physiological barriers (III) Neutrophils and \nmacrophages \n (D) Physical barriers (IV) Tears and Saliva \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(1) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','(2) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)','(3) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)','(4) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(261138,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \nList-I List-II\n(A) Non-medicated IUDs (I) Multiload 375\n(B) Copper releasing IUDs \n(II) Rubber barrier\n(C) Hormone releasing IUDs \n(III) Lippes loop\n(D) Vaults (IV) LNG-20\n Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','A-(III), B-(I), C-(IV), D-(II)','A-(III), B-(I), C-(IV), D-(II)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','A-(IV), B-(III), C-(I), D-(II)','A-(II), B-(IV), C-(III), D-(I)','A-(III), B-(I), C-(IV), D-(II)','A-(III), B-(IV), C-(II), D-(I)','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(261139,'biology','Given below are four methods (A-D) and their\nmodes of action (i-iv) in achieving contraception.\nSelect their correct matching from the four options\nthat follow.\nMethod Mode of Action\nA. The pill (i) Prevents sperms reaching cervix\nB. Condom (ii) Prevents implantation\nC. Vasectomy (iii) Prevents ovulation\nD. Copper T (iv) Semen contains no sperms\n (2008)','(c) A – (iii), B – (i), C – (iv), D – (ii)','NCERT XII: 60-61','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) A – (iii), B – (iv), C – (i), D – (ii)','(b) A – (ii), B – (iii), C – (i), D – (iv)','(c) A – (iii), B – (i), C – (iv), D – (ii)','(d) A – (iv), B – (i), C – (ii), D – (iii)','NEW','12-061','12-045',NULL),
(261140,'biology','Which one of the following is the most widely\naccepted method of contraception in India at\npresent?','(d) IUDs (Intra uterine devices)','NCERT XII: 61','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) Cervical caps','(b) Tubectomy','(c) Diaphragms','(d) IUDs (Intra uterine devices)','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(261141,'biology','Oral contraceptive contains','Progesterone','NCERT XII: 61','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Progesterone','LH','Oxytocin','Sterols','NEW','12-061','12-045',NULL),
(261142,'biology','What is the work of progesterone which is present in\noral contraceptive pills?\n (2000)','(a) To inhibit ovulation','NCERT XII: 61','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) To inhibit ovulation','(b) To check oogenesis','(c) To check entry of sperms into cervix and to\nmake them inactive','(d) To check sexual behaviour','NEW','12-061','12-045',NULL),
(261143,'biology','Progestogens alone or in combination with estrogens can be used as a contraceptive in the form of','Pills, injection and implants','NCERT XII: 61','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Implants only','Injection only','Pills, injection and implants','Pills only','NEW','12-061','12-045',NULL),
(261144,'biology','Medical Termination of Pregnancy (MTP) is\nconsidered safe up to how manyweeks of pregnancy? (2011)','(b) Twelve weeks','NCERT XII: 62','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) Eight weeks','(b) Twelve weeks','(c) Eighteen weeks','(d) Six weeks','NEW','12-062','12-046',NULL),
(110714,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect according to symplastic system of water movement?','Movement is against a potential gradient','Movement is down a potential gradient\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 185','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Water has to enter the cells through the cell membrane, hence the movement is relatively slower','Movement is against a potential gradient','Symplastic movement may be aided by cytoplasmic streaming','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(261145,'biology','Consider the statements given below regarding \ncontraception and answer as directed thereafter.\n(1) Medical termination of pregnancy (MTP) \nduring first trimester is generally safe.\n(2) Generally chances of conception are nil until \nmother breast-feeds the infant upto two years.\n(3) Intrauterine devices like copper-T are effective \ncontraceptives.\n(4) Contraception pills may be taken upto one week \nafter coitus to prevent conception.\nWhich two of the above statements are correct?\n (2008)','(a) 1, 3','NCERT XII: 60-62','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) 1, 3','(b) 1, 2','(c) 2, 3','(d) 3, 4','NEW','12-060','12-044',NULL),
(261146,'biology','In vitro fertilisation is a technique that involves\ntransfer of which one of the following into the\nfallopian tube?','(b) Either zygote or early embryo upto 8 cell stage','NCERT XII: 64','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) Embryo only, upto 8 cell stage','(b) Either zygote or early embryo upto 8 cell stage','(c) Embryo of 32 cell stage','(d) Zygote only','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261147,'biology','Test tube baby means a baby born when\n (2003)','(c) the ovum is fertilised externally and thereafter\nimplanted in the uterus','NCERT XII: 64','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) it is developed in a test tube','(b) it is developed through tissue culture method','(c) the ovum is fertilised externally and thereafter\nimplanted in the uterus','(d) it develops from a non-fertilised uterus.','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261148,'biology','The technique called Gamete Intra Fallopian\nTransfer (GIFT) is recommended for those females\n(Mains 2011)','(a) who cannot produce an ovum','NCERT XII: 64','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) who cannot produce an ovum','(b) who cannot retain the fetus inside uterus','(c) whose cervical canal is too narrow to allow\npassage for the sperms','(d) who cannot provide suitable environment for\nfertilisation','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261149,'biology','Which of the following sexually transmitted infections are completely curable ? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) Syphilis and trichomoniasis','Of these, 4 are currently curable: syphilis, gonorrhoea, chlamydia and trichomoniasis. The other 4 are incurable viral infections: hepatitis B, herpes simplex virus (HSV), HIV and human papillomavirus (HPV).','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) HIV infection and Trichomoniasis','(2) Syphilis and trichomoniasis','(3) Hepatitis – B and Genital herpes','(4) Genital herpes and Genital warts','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(261201,'chemistry','Identify compound X in the following sequence of reactions refer diagram(n26074)','c','Side chain chlorination of toluene gives benzal chloride, which on hydrolysis gives benzaldehyde. \n\nThis is a commercial method of manufacture of benzaldehyde.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET: 2020)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-363','12-233','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261201q.png'),
(261202,'chemistry','Reaction by which benzaldehyde cannot be prepared refer diagram(n26075)','b','PREPARATION OF BENZALDEHYDE:\n\n1. From acyl chloride (acid chloride) Acyl chloride (acid chloride) is hydrogenated over catalyst, palladium on barium sulphate. This reaction is called Rosenmund reduction (option d)\n\n2: BY THE USE OF CHROMYL CHLORIDE (CrO2Cl2):\n\nChromyl chloride oxidises methyl group to a chromium complex, which on hydrolysis gives corresponding benzaldehyde.(option c)\n\n3: BY GATTERMANN – KOCH REACTION:\n\nWhen benzene or its derivative is treated with carbon monoxide and hydrogen chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride or cuprous chloride, it gives benzaldehyde or substituted benzaldehyde. This reaction is known as Gatterman-Koch reaction.(option a)\n\nSo option b is not the method of preparation of benzaldehyde.\n \nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -4)\n(NEET : 2013)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261202q.png'),
(261203,'chemistry','Consider the following reaction given in diagram(n26076)','a','Acyl chloride (acid chloride) is hydrogenated over catalyst, palladium on barium sulphate. \n\nThis reaction is called Rosenmund reduction. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -2)\n(MAINS:2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261203q.png'),
(261204,'chemistry','Which one of the following can be oxidised to the corresponding carbonyl compound?','2-Hydroxypropane','Aldehydes and ketones are generally prepared by oxidation of primary and secondary alcohols.\n\nSecondary alcohol on oxidation gives a ketone containing the same number of carbon atoms.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:2004)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2-Hydroxypropane','ortho-Nitrophenol','Phenol','2-Methyl-2-hydroxypropane','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(261205,'chemistry','In the following reaction, product P is:\ndiagram(n26077)','c','Acyl chloride (acid chloride) is hydrogenated over catalyst, palladium on barium sulphate to give aldehyde. \n\nThis reaction is called Rosenmund reduction. \n\nBaSO₄ prevents the aldehyde from being reduced and acts as a poison to the palladium catalyst in this reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2002)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261205q.png'),
(261206,'chemistry','Which alkene on ozonolysis gives CH₃CH₂CHO and CH₃COCH₃ refer diagram(n26078)','a','By ozonolysis of alkenes:  \n\nOzonolysis of alkenes followed by reaction with zinc dust and water gives aldehydes, ketones or a mixture of both depending on the substitution pattern of the alkene.\n\nOn passing a steam of ozone through a solution of olefin in an organic solvent, an ozonide is obtained.\n\nThe ozonide on reduction with Zn and acid or H₂/Ni gives aldehydes and/or ketones.\n\nThe nature of these products helps in locating the position of the double bond in olefin.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -1)\n(NEET:2001)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261206q.png'),
(261207,'chemistry','In the reaction, CH₃CN + 2H ---ᴴᶜˡ→ X --ᵇᵒᶦˡᶦⁿᵍ ʷᵃᵗᵉʳ----->Y; the term Y is?','acetaldehyde','Nitriles are reduced to corresponding imine with stannous chloride in the presence of hydrochloric acid, which on hydrolysis give corresponding aldehyde. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:1999)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','acetaldehyde','ethanamine','acetone','dimethylamine','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(110715,'biology','The movement in Hydrilla leaf and the movement of chloroplast is due to','Cytoplasmic streaming','Cytoplasmic streaming in cells of the Hydrilla leaf; the movement of chloroplast due to streaming is easily visible\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 185','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Osmosis','Cytoplasmic streaming','Diffusion','Transpiration','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(261208,'chemistry','Ketones [RCOR₁] where R = R₁ = alkyl group. It can be obtained in one step by?','oxidation of tertiary alcohol','A tertiary alcohol is difficult to oxidise. \n\nBut when it is treated with an acidic oxidising agent under some conditions, it is oxidised to ketone and then to acids. \n\nBoth the ketone and acid contain the lesser number of carbon atoms than the starting alcohol.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-361 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:1997)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','oxidation of tertiary alcohol','reaction of acid halide with alcohols','hydrolysis of esters','oxidation of primary alcohol.','NEW','12-361','12-231',NULL),
(261209,'chemistry','The oxidation of toluene to benzaldehyde by chromyl chloride is called?','Etard reaction','Use of chromyl chloride (CrO₂Cl₂ ): \n\nChromyl chloride oxidises methyl group to a chromium complex, which on hydrolysis gives corresponding benzaldehyde. \n\nThe oxidation of toluene (C₆H₅CH₃) with chromyl chloride (CrO₂Cl₂) in CCl₄ or CS₂ to give benzaldehyde is called Etard reaction. \n\nIn this reaction, the chromyl chloride first forms a brown complex, which is separated and then decomposed with water to give benzaldehyde (C₆H₅CHO).\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -6)\n(NEET:1996)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Etard reaction','Reimer–Tiemann reaction','Wurtz reaction','Cannizzaro’s reaction.','NEW','12-362','12-232',NULL),
(261210,'chemistry','Reaction between benzaldehyde and acetophenone in presence of dilute NaOH is known as?','Cross Aldol condensation.','Cross aldol condensation: \n\nWhen aldol condensation is carried out between two different aldehydes and / or ketones, it is called cross aldol condensation. \n\nIf both of them contain α-hydrogen atoms, it gives a mixture of four products. \n\nThis is illustrated below by aldol reaction of a mixture of ethanal and propanal.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -1)\n(NEET:2020)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Aldol condensation','Cannizzaro’s reaction','Cross Cannizzaro’s reaction','Cross Aldol condensation.','NEW','12-372','12-242',NULL),
(261211,'chemistry','Consider the reactions given in diagram(n26079)','A-Ethanal, X-Ethanol, Y-But-2-enal, Z-Semicarbazone.','A:Since, A gives silver mirror test, it must be an aldehyde and aldehydes are formed by oxidation of 1° alcohols. Thus, ‘X’ is a 1° alcohol, i.e., CH₃CH₂OH.\n\nX:When the vapours of a primary or a secondary alcohol are passed over heated copper at 573 K, dehydrogenation takes place and an (A) aldehyde or a ketone is formed while tertiary alcohols undergo dehydration.\n\n(REF: Chap#25 page#340)\n\nY:Aldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy\nketones (ketol), respectively. This is known as Aldol reaction. \n\nThe aldol and ketol readily lose water to give α,β-unsaturated carbonyl compounds which are aldol condensation products.\n\n(REF: Chap#26 page 371)\n\nZ:Nucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H2N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. The reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid.\n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form>C=N-Z.\n\n(REF: Chap#26C)\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 page-368)\n(NEET:2017)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A-Methoxymethane, X-Ethanol, Y-Ethanoic acid, Z-Semicarbazide.','A-Ethanal, X-Ethanol, Y-But-2-enal, Z-Semicarbazone.','A-Ethanol, X-Acetaldehyde, Y-Butanone, Z-Hydrazone.','A-Methoxymethane, X-Ethanoic acid, Y-Acetate ion, Z-Hydrazine','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261211q.png'),
(261212,'chemistry','Of the following, which is the product formed when cyclohexanone undergoes aldol condensation followed by heating refer diagram(n26080)','a','Aldol condensation:\n\nAldehydes and ketones having at least one α-hydrogen undergo a reaction in the presence of dilute alkali as catalyst to form β-hydroxy aldehydes (aldol) or β-hydroxy ketones (ketol), respectively. \n\nThis is known as Aldol reaction.\n\nThe aldol and ketol readily lose water to give α,β-unsaturated carbonyl compounds which are aldol condensation products.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2017)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-371','12-241','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261212q.png'),
(261213,'chemistry','The correct structure of the product ‘A’ formed in the reaction given in diagram(n26081)','b','CATALYTIC HYDROGENATION :\n\nAs a rule, the carbonyl group does not add hydrogen as readily as do the carbon-carbon double and triple bonds. \n\nThus, it is fairly easy to reduce an alkene or alkyne function without affecting any carbonyl functions in the same molecule. \n\nBy using a platinum catalyst and increased temperature and pressure, it is possible to reduce aldehydes and ketones to alcohols.\n \nAs the pressure in the  reaction conditions  is very low that\'s why it only reduced the carbon carbon double bond.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 )\n(NEET-II 2016)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261213q.png'),
(261214,'chemistry','Which of the following reagents would distinguish cis-cyclopenta-1,2-diol from the trans-isomer?','Acetone','Ketones react with ethylene glycol under similar conditions to form cyclic products known as ethylene glycol ketals.\n\nReference: (NCERT Class-12 Page-368 Paragraph-1)\n\n(NEET:I 2016)\ndiagram(ne26082)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','MnO₂','Aluminium isopropoxide','Acetone','ozone','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261215,'chemistry','The correct statement regarding a carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alphacarbon, is?','a carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon rapidly equilibrates with its corresponding enol and this process is known as keto-enol tautomerism.','Acidity of α-hydrogens of aldehydes and ketones: \n\nThe aldehydes and ketones undergo a number of reactions due to the acidic nature of α-hydrogen. \n\nThe acidity of α-hydrogen atoms of carbonyl compounds is due to the strong electron withdrawing effect of the carbonyl group and resonance stabilisation of the conjugate base.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-371 Paragraph -1)\n(NEET: I 2016)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon rapidly equilibrates with its corresponding enol and this process is known as carbonylation.','a carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon rapidly equilibrates with its corresponding enol and this process is known as keto-enol tautomerism.','a carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon never equilibrates with its corresponding enol.','a carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon rapidly equilibrates with its corresponding enol and this process is known as aldehyde-ketone equilibration.','NEW','12-371','12-241',NULL),
(261216,'chemistry','The product formed by the reaction of an aldehyde with a primary amine is?','Schiff ’s base','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:I 2016)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','carboxylic acid','ketone','Schiff ’s base','aromatic acid','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261217,'chemistry','Reaction of a carbonyl compound with one of the following reagents involves nucleophilic addition followed by elimination of water. The reagent is?','hydrazine in presence of feebly acidic solution','Addition of ammonia and its derivatives: \n\nNucleophiles, such as ammonia and its derivatives H₂N-Z add to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones. \n\nThe reaction is reversible and catalysed by acid. \n\nThe equilibrium favours the product formation due to rapid dehydration of the intermediate to form >C=N-Z.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -2)\n(NEET:2015)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','hydrazine in presence of feebly acidic solution','hydrocyanic acid','sodium hydrogen sulphite','a Grignard reagent.','NEW','12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261218,'biology','Consider the statements regarding indicators of a Reproductive health-wise healthy society. Choose the correct option. \n \n1. Increased number of medically assisted deliveries and better post-natal care leading to decreased maternal and infant mortality rates.\n\n 2. Better awareness about sex-related matters.\n\n 3. Decreased medical facilities for all sex-related problems.\n\n 4. Decreased number of couples with small families.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','Better awareness about sex-related matters, increased number of medically assisted deliveries and better post-natal care lead to decreased maternal and infant mortality rates, increased number of couples with small families, better detection and cure of STDs and overall increased medical facilities for all sex-related problems, etc. all indicate improved reproductive health of the society. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 59,  paragraph  no 1)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','only 4 is incorrect','NEW','12-059','12-043',NULL),
(261219,'chemistry','The order of stability of the following tautomeric compounds refer diagram(n26084)','III > II > I','Aldehydes are generally more reactive than ketones in nucleophilic addition reactions due to steric and electronic reasons.\n \nSterically, the presence of two relatively large substituents in ketones hinders the approach of nucleophile to carbonyl carbon than in aldehydes having only one such substituent. \n\nElectronically, aldehydes are more reactive than ketones because two alkyl groups reduce the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon more effectively than in former.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-366 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:2013)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','II > I > III','I > II > III','II > III > I','III > II > I','NEW','12-366','12-236','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261219q.png'),
(261220,'chemistry','Predict the products in the given reaction in diagram(n26085)','c','Cannizzaro reaction:\n\nAldehydes which do not have an α-hydrogen atom, undergo self oxidation and reduction (disproportionation) reaction on heating with concentrated alkali. \n\nIn this reaction, one molecule of the aldehyde is reduced to alcohol while another is oxidised to carboxylic acid salt.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-372 Paragraph -3)\n(NEET:2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-372','12-242','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261220q.png'),
(261221,'chemistry','Acetone is treated with excess of ethanol in the presence of hydrochloric acid. The product obtained is,  \ndiagram(n26086)','d','Dry hydrogen chloride protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl compounds and therefore, increases the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon facilitating the nucleophilic attack of ethylene glycol.\n\nAcetals and ketals are hydrolysed with aqueous mineral acids to yield corresponding aldehydes and ketones respectively.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-368 Paragraph -1)\n(NEET:2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-368','12-238','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261221q.png'),
(261222,'chemistry','CH₃CHO and phenyl-CH₂CHO can be distinguished chemically by?','Iodoform test','Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium salts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. \n\nThe methyl group is converted to haloform. \n\nThis oxidation does not affect a carbon-carbon double bond, if present in the molecule. \n\nAcetaldehyde, acetone and methyl ketones having CH₃CO— group undergo haloform reaction.\n\nThus, CH₃CHO will give yellow precipitate with I₂ and NaOH but C₆H₅CH₂CHO will not.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-370 Paragraph -1)\n(NEET:2012)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Benedict’s test','Iodoform test','Tollens’ reagent test','Fehling’s solution test.','NEW','12-370','12-240',NULL),
(261301,'chemistry','Match List-I with List-II.\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below.\ndiagram (261301Q)','D, (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)','(i) When benzene or its derivative is treated with carbon monoxide and hydrogen chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride or cuprous chloride, it gives benzaldehyde or substituted benzaldehyde.This reaction is known as Gatterman-Koch reaction.\n                             \n(ii) Aldehydes and ketones having at least one methyl group linked to the carbonyl carbon atom (methyl ketones) are oxidised by sodium hypohalite to sodium \nsalts of corresponding carboxylic acids having one carbon atom less than that of carbonyl compound. The methyl group is converted to haloform.\n\n(iii) Carboxylic acids are esterified with alcohols or phenols in the presence of a mineral acid such as concentrated H2SO4 or HCl gas as a catalyst.\n\n(iv) Carboxylic acids having an α-hydrogen are halogenated at theα-position on treatment with chlorine or bromine in the presence of small amount of red phosphorus to give α-halo carboxylic acids. The reaction is known as Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12)\n(NEET:2021)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A,(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)','B,(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)','C,(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)','D, (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)','NEW','12-363','12-233','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261301q.png'),
(261302,'chemistry','The intermediate compound ‘X’ in the following chemical reaction is :\ndiagram(n26123)','A','Chromyl chloride oxidises methyl group to a chromium complex, which on hydrolysis \ngives corresponding benzaldehyde.\n\nThis reaction is called Etard reaction.\n\nReference: (NCERT - Class 12 Page-362 Paragraph -last)\n(NEET:2021)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','12-362','12-232','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261302q.png'),
(261303,'chemistry','Match the compounds given in List-I with List-II and select the suitable option using the codes given below.\ndiagram(261303Q)','(A)-(ii), (B)-(i), (C)-(iv), (D)-(iii)','A) The Benzoin Condensation is a coupling reaction between two aldehydes that allows the preparation of α-hydroxyketones. \n\nThe first methods were only suitable for the conversion of aromatic aldehydes.\n\nB) Phenolphthalein is obtained by heating phthalic anhydride with conc. H₂SO₄ and  Phenol.\n\nC) Fries Rearrangement is an organic rearrangement reaction in which an aryl ester is transformed into a hydroxy aryl ketone with the help of a Lewis acid catalyst and an aqueous acid. \n\nIn this reaction, an acyl group belonging to the phenolic ester migrates to the aryl ring.\n\nD) Methyl salicylate (oil of wintergreen or wintergreen oil) is an organic compound with the formula C₈H₈O₃. \n\nIt is the methyl ester of salicylic acid.\n\n(MAINS: 2011)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(A)-(iv), (B)-(i), (C)-(iii), (D)-(ii)','(A)-(iv), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iii), (D)-(i)','(A)-(ii), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(i)','(A)-(ii), (B)-(i), (C)-(iv), (D)-(iii)','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261303q.png'),
(261304,'chemistry','an acid that forms anhydride (X) and an acid imide on strongheating with ammonia is : - diagram(261304Q)','1','Hint: Benzene-1,2-Dicarboxylic acid \n\nBenzene-1,2-Dicarboxylic acid will undergo a dehydration reaction to form an anhydride. Benzene-1,2-dicarboxylic acid gives acid imide when reacts with ammonia.','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4',NULL,'12-382','12-252','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/261304q.png'),
(261305,'chemistry','ethylbenzene is obtained frome phenyl methyl ketone by using :','Zn-Hg+HCl','Hint: Clemmensen reduction reaction.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to CH2 group on treatment with zinc amalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid. The reaction is known as the Clemmensen reduction reaction','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Zn-Hg+HCl','LiAlH4','KMnO4','None of these',NULL,'12-368','12-238',NULL),
(261306,'biology','Consider the statements regarding MTP. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Intentional or voluntary termination of pregnancy before full term is called medical termination of pregnancy (MTP) or induced abortion.\n\n2. MTPs are considered relatively safe during the second trimester, i.e., up to 24 weeks of pregnancy.\n\n3. MTP is legal when the continuation of the pregnancy would involve a risk to the life of the pregnant woman or of grave injury to physical or mental health.\n\n4. MTP should not be performed when there is a substantial risk that if the child were born, would suffer from such physical or mental abnormalities as to be seriously handicapped.','Both 1 and 3 are correct.','Intentional or voluntary termination of pregnancy before full term is called medical termination of pregnancy (MTP) or induced abortion. The government of India legalised MTP in 1971 with some strict conditions to avoid its misuse. MTPs should be performed when the continuation of the pregnancy would involve a risk to the life of the pregnant woman or of grave injury to physical or mental health or when there is a substantial risk that the child born, would suffer from such physical or mental abnormalities as to be seriously handicapped. MTPs are considered relatively safe during the first trimester, i.e., up to 12 weeks of pregnancy. Second-trimester abortions are much riskier. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 62, section 4.2)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2 and 3 are correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','1,3 and 4 are correct.','NEW','12-062','12-046',NULL),
(261307,'biology','Consider the statements regarding STIs. Choose the CORRECT option. \n \n1. Gonorrhoea, syphilis, genital herpes, chlamydiasis, genital warts, trichomoniasis, hepatitis-B, and AIDS are STIs\n\n 2. Infections or diseases which are transmitted through sexual intercourse are collectively called sexually transmitted infections\n\n 3. Some of these infections like hepatitis–B and HIV can also be transmitted by sharing injection needles, surgical instruments, sharing clothes etc., with infected persons, transfusion of blood, or from an infected mother to the foetus too.\n\n 4. Except for Gonorrhoea, Syphilis, genital herpes and HIV infections, other diseases are completely incurable.','only 1 and 2 are correct','Infections or diseases which are transmitted through sexual intercourse are collectively called sexually transmitted infections (STI) or venereal diseases (VD) or reproductive tract infections (RTI). Gonorrhoea, syphilis, genital herpes, chlamydiasis, genital warts, trichomoniasis, hepatitis B and of course, the most discussed infection in recent years, HIV leading to AIDS are some of the common STIs. Some of these infections like hepatitis–B and HIV can also be transmitted by sharing injection needles, surgical instruments, etc., with infected persons, transfusion of blood, or from an infected mother to the foetus too. Except for hepatitis B, genital herpes and HIV infections, other diseases are completely curable if detected early and treated properly. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 63,  paragraph  no 2)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','only 1 and 2 are correct','2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(261308,'biology','Consider the statements regarding STIs. Choose the correct option. \n \n1. Early symptoms of most of the STIs are minor and include itching, fluid discharge, slight pain, blisters, swellings, etc.\n \n2. Complications of STI include prolapse intervertebral disc (PID), abortions, still births, ectopic pregnancies, infertility or even cancer of the reproductive tract.\n \n3. To avoid STIs avoid sex with unknown partners/multiple partners.\n\n4. In case of doubt, one should go to a qualified doctor for early detection and get complete treatment if diagnosed with infection.','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Early symptoms of most STIs are minor and include itching, fluid discharge, slight pain, swellings, etc (No blisters). Complications of STI include pelvic inflammatory diseases (PID), abortions, stillbirths, ectopic pregnancies, infertility or even cancer of the reproductive tract. To avoid STIs avoid sex with unknown partners/multiple partners. In case of doubt, one should go to a qualified doctor for early detection and get complete treatment if diagnosed with the infection. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 63, paragraph no 3)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','1,2 and 3 are correct','1,3 and 4 are correct.','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(151412,'chemistry','A : Amorphous solids are isotropic.\n\nR : Amorphous solids are not rigid.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','Amorphous solids lack long-range order in their atomic arrangement and are characterized by a disordered structure. As a result, they exhibit isotropic properties, which means they have the same physical properties in all directions.\n\nWhile some amorphous solids may not be as rigid as crystalline solids, they are still considered to be solid because they have a definite shape and volume.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(261309,'biology','Consider the statements regarding Infertility. Choose the CORRECT option. \n\n1. Infertility means the inability to produce children despite unprotected sexual cohabitation. \n\n2. The reasons for infertility could be many–physical, congenital, diseases, drugs, immunological or even psychological.\n\n3. Specialised health care units (infertility clinics, etc.) could help in the diagnosis and corrective treatment of some of these disorders and enable these couples to have children.\n\n4. The couples could be assisted to have children through certain special techniques commonly known as assisted reproductive technologies (ART).','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','Infertility means the inability to produce children inspite unprotected sexual cohabitation. The reasons for infertility could be many–physical, congenital, diseases, drugs, immunological or even psychological. Specialised health care units (infertility clinics, etc.) could help in the diagnosis and corrective treatment of some of these disorders and enable these couples to have children. The couples could be assisted to have children through certain special techniques commonly known as assisted reproductive technologies (ART). (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 64, paragraph  no 1)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2 and 3 are correct','1,3 and 4 are incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261310,'biology','Consider the statements regarding In vitro fertilisation. Choose the correct option. \n \n1. In vitro fertilisation (IVF–fertilisation outside the body in almost similar conditions as that in the body) followed by embryo transfer (ET) is one of such ART methods.\n\n2. In this method, popularly known as the test tube baby programme, ova from the wife/donor (female) and sperms from the husband/donor (male) are collected and are induced to form zygotes under simulated conditions in the laboratory.\n\n3. The zygote or early embryos (with more than 8 blastomeres) could then be transferred into the fallopian tube (ZIFT–zygote intrafallopian transfer) and embryos with less than 8 blastomeres, into the uterus (IUT – Intra uterine transfer),\n\n4. Embryos formed by in-vivo fertilisation (fusion of gametes within the female) also could be used for such transfer to assist those females who cannot conceive.','Only 3 is incorrect','In vitro fertilisation (IVF–fertilisation outside the body in almost similar conditions as that in the body) followed by embryo transfer (ET) is one such method. In this method, popularly known as the test tube baby programme, ova from the wife/donor (female) and sperms from the husband/donor (male) are collected and are induced to form zygotes under simulated conditions in the laboratory. The zygote or early embryos (with up to 8 blastomeres) could then be transferred into the fallopian tube (ZIFT–zygote intrafallopian transfer) and embryos with more than 8 blastomeres, into the uterus (IUT – Intra uterine transfer), to complete its further development. Embryos formed by in-vivo fertilisation (fusion of gametes within the female) also could be used for such transfer to assist those females who cannot conceive. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 64,  paragraph  no 2)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are correct','1, 3 and 4 are incorrect.','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261311,'biology','Consider the statements regarding artificial fertility methods. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Transfer of an ovum collected from a donor into the fallopian tube (GIFT – gamete intrafallopian transfer) of another female who cannot produce one, but can provide a suitable environment for fertilisation\n\n2. Intra cytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) is another specialised procedure to form an embryo in the laboratory in which a sperm is directly injected into the ovum.\n\n3. Infertility cases either due to the inability of the female partner to inseminate the male or due to very low sperm counts in the ejaculates, could be corrected by Intra uterine semination technique.\n\n4. In this technique, the semen collected either from the husband or a healthy donor is artificially introduced either into the vagina or into the uterus of the female through natural insemination.','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','Transfer of an ovum collected from a donor into the fallopian tube (GIFT – gamete intrafallopian transfer) of another female who cannot produce one, but can provide a suitable environment for fertilisation and further development is another method attempted. Intra cytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) is another specialised procedure to form an embryo in the laboratory in which a sperm is directly injected into the ovum. Infertility cases either due to the inability of the male partner to inseminate the female or due to very low sperm counts in the ejaculates could be corrected by the artificial insemination (AI) technique. In this technique, the semen collected either from the husband or a healthy donor is artificially introduced either into the vagina or into the uterus (IUI – intra-uterine insemination) of the female.','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','Both 3 and 4 are correct','1,2 and 3 are correct.','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261312,'biology','Assertion: Reproductive health means total well-being in all aspects of reproduction, i.e., physical, emotional, behavioral and social.\n\nReason: A society with people having physically and functionally normal reproductive organs and normal emotional and behavioral interactions among them in all sex-related aspects might be called reproductively healthy.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Reproductive health means total well-being in all aspects of reproduction, i.e., physical, emotional, behavioural and social. A society with people having physically and functionally normal reproductive organs and normal emotional and behavioural interactions among them in all sex-related aspects might be called reproductively healthy. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12: Page no 57, Paragraph no 1)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-057','12-041',NULL),
(261313,'biology','Assertion: Successful implementation of various action plans to attain reproductive health requires strong infrastructural facilities, professional expertise and material support.\n\nReason: Implementation of better techniques and new strategies from time to time are also required to provide more efficient care and assistance to people.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Successful implementation of various action plans to attain reproductive health requires strong infrastructural facilities, professional expertise and material support. These are essential to provide medical assistance and care to people with reproduction-related problems like pregnancy, delivery, STDs, abortions, contraception, menstrual problems, infertility, etc. Implementation of better techniques and new strategies from time to time are also required to provide more efficient care and assistance to people. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 58,  paragraph  no 3)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-058','12-042',NULL),
(350209,'biology','Each organism has an invariably defined range of conditions that it can tolerate, diversity in the resources it utilises and a distinct functional role in the ecological system, all these together comprise it is called','Niche','Each organism has an invariably defined range of conditions that it can tolerate, diversity in the resources it utilises and a distinct functional role in the ecological system, all these together comprise its niche.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:221, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Organism and its Environment',NULL,'2','mcq','Habitat','Niche','Population','Ecological amplitude','NEW','12-221',NULL,NULL),
(261314,'biology','Assertion: Programmes covering wider reproduction-related areas are currently in operation under the popular name ‘Reproductive and Child Health Care (RCH) programmes’.\n\nReason: RCH programme includes creating awareness among people about various reproduction-related aspects and providing facilities and support for building up a reproductively healthy society.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Improved programmes covering wider reproduction-related areas are currently in operation under the popular name ‘Reproductive and Child Health Care (RCH) programmes’. Creating awareness among people about various reproduction related aspects and providing facilities and support for building up a reproductively healthy society are the major tasks under these programmes. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 58, paragraph  no 1)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-058','12-042',NULL),
(261315,'biology','Assertion: In aminocentesis, some of the amniotic fluid of the developing foetus is taken to analyse the fetal cells and dissolved substances.\n\nReason: Amniocentesis is openly practised in India','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','In aminocentesis, some of the amniotic fluid of the developing foetus is taken to analyse the fetal cells and dissolved substances. In India, there is a statutory ban on amniocentesis for sex determination to legally check the increasing menace of female foeticides. So, Assertion is true but Reason is false. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 58, paragraph  no 3)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-058','12-042',NULL),
(261316,'biology','Assertion: According to the 2011 census report, the population growth rate was less than 3 %, i.e., 30/1000/year. \n\nReason: At this rate, our population could increase rapidly.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','According to the 2011 census report, the population growth rate was less than 2 %, i.e., 20/1000/year, a rate at which our population could increase rapidly. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 59, paragraph no 2)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-059','12-043',NULL),
(261317,'biology','Assertion: IUDs are one of the most widely accepted methods of contraception in India.\n\nReason: IUDs are ideal contraceptives for females who want to delay pregnancy and/or space children.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','IUDs are ideal contraceptives for females who want to delay pregnancy and/or space children. It is one of the most widely accepted methods of contraception in India. IUDs are the most ideal contraceptive because which it is widely accepted contraception in India. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 61, paragraph no 1)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-061','12-045',NULL),
(261318,'biology','Assertion: Progestogens alone or in combination with estrogen can also be used by females as injections or implants under the uterus\n\nReason: Their mode of action is similar to that of IUDs','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Progestogens alone or in combination with estrogen can also be used by females as injections or implants under the skin. Their mode of action is similar to that of pills and their effective periods are much longer. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 61, paragraph no 3)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-061','12-045',NULL),
(261319,'biology','Assertion: Surgical methods, also called sterilisation, are generally advised for the male/female partner as a terminal method to prevent any more pregnancies.\n\nReason: Sterilisation procedure in the male is called ‘tubectomy’','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Surgical methods, also called sterilisation, are generally advised for the male/female partner as a terminal method to prevent any more pregnancies. Sterilisation procedure in the male is called ‘vasectomy’ and that in the female, ‘tubectomy’. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 62, paragraph no 1)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-062','12-046',NULL),
(261320,'biology','Assertion: It needs to be emphasised that the selection of a suitable contraceptive method and its use should always be undertaken in consultation with qualified medical professionals.\n\nReason: The ill-effects like nausea, abdominal pain, breakthrough bleeding, irregular menstrual bleeding or even breast cancer, though not very significant, should not be totally ignored.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','It needs to be emphasised that the selection of a suitable contraceptive method and its use should always be undertaken in consultation with qualified medical professionals. One must also remember that contraceptives are not regular requirements for the maintenance of reproductive health. They are practised against a natural reproductive event, i.e., conception/pregnancy. One is forced to use these methods either to prevent pregnancy or to delay or space pregnancy due to personal reasons. No doubt, the widespread use of these methods has a significant role in checking the uncontrolled growth of the population. However, their possible ill-effects like nausea, abdominal pain, breakthrough bleeding, irregular menstrual bleeding or even breast cancer, though not very significant, should not be ignored. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 62, paragraph no 2)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-062','12-046',NULL),
(261321,'biology','Assertion: Intentional or voluntary termination of pregnancy before full term is called medical termination of pregnancy (MTP) or induced abortion.\n\nReason: Government of India legalised MTP in 1961.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Intentional or voluntary termination of pregnancy before full term is called medical termination of pregnancy (MTP) or induced abortion. The government of India legalised MTP in 1971(not 1961)  with some strict conditions to avoid its misuse. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; pate no 62, paragraph no 3)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-062','12-046',NULL),
(261322,'biology','Assertion: Prevention or early detection and cure of STIs are given prime consideration under the reproductive health-care programmes.\n\nReason: Incidences are very high in older people','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','STIs are a major threat to a healthy society. Therefore, prevention or early detection and cure of these diseases are given prime consideration under the reproductive health-care programmes. Though all persons are vulnerable to these infections, their incidences are reported to be very high among persons in the age group of 15-24 years. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 63, paragraph no 3)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(261323,'biology','Assertion: Early symptoms of most STIs are minor and include itching, slight pain, blisters, swellings, etc.\n\nReason: In case of doubt, one should wait for some time and take self treatment, then go to a qualified doctor for detection and get complete treatment if diagnosed with the infection.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Early symptoms of most STIs are minor and include itching, fluid discharge, slight pain, swellings, etc (No blisters). In case of doubt, one should go to a qualified doctor for early detection and get complete treatment if diagnosed with the infection. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 63, paragraph no 2)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-063','12-047',NULL),
(261324,'biology','Assertion: In vitro fertilisation (IVF–fertilisation outside the body in almost similar conditions as that in the body) followed by embryo transfer (ET) is one of ART methods.\n\nReason: The zygote or early embryos (with upto 8 blastomeres) could then be transferred into the fallopian tube (ZIFT–zygote intra fallopian transfer)','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','In vitro fertilisation (IVF–fertilisation outside the body in almost similar conditions as that in the body) followed by embryo transfer (ET) is one of the ART methods. The zygote or early embryos (with up to 8 blastomeres) could then be transferred into the fallopian tube (ZIFT–zygote intrafallopian transfer). (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 64, paragraph no 2)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
(261325,'biology','Assertion: Assistive reproductive techniques facilities are presently available only in very few centres in the country.\n\nReason: All the assistive reproductive techniques require extremely high precision handling by specialised professionals and expensive instrumentation.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','All the assistive reproductive techniques require extremely high precision handling by specialised professionals and expensive instrumentation. Therefore, these facilities are presently available only in very few centres in the country. (Reference   NCERT biology class 12; page no 64, paragraph no 4)','Reproduction >> Reproductive Health >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-064','12-048',NULL),
