(230701,'chemistry','____ was the first to describe the bonding features in coordination\ncompounds.','Werner','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 254; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'1','fillup','We,rn,er,fe,or',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-254','12-128',NULL),
(22907,'chemistry','The ratio of potential energy of electron in the third orbit of Li²+ ion to the KE of electron in the fourth orbit of He+ ion should be','-8 : 1','PE of Li²⁺ in 3rd orbit \n \n 2E₃ =2x -13.6 x 3²/ 3² = -13.6 ---(1)\n \n KE of He+ ion in 4th orbit = -E₄ \n = 13.6(Z²/4²) = 13.6/4 ----(2)\n \n (1)/(2) = -13.6 x 2 /( 13.6/4) = -8/1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Numerical questions',NULL,'2','mcq','8 : 1','-8 : 1','-16 : 1','1 : 1','NEW','11-042','11-042',NULL),
(230702,'chemistry','Which of the following could not be answered by Werner\'s bonding features?','All of these','Werner was the first to describe the bonding features in coordination\ncompounds. But his theory could not answer basic questions like:\n(i) Why only certain elements possess the remarkable property of\nforming coordination compounds?\n(ii) Why the bonds in coordination compounds have directional\nproperties?\n(iii) Why coordination compounds have characteristic magnetic and\noptical properties?(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 254; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Certain elements possesing the remarkable property of\nforming coordination compounds.','The bonds in coordination compounds having directional\nproperties.','Coordination compounds having characteristic magnetic and optical properties.','All of these','NEW','12-254','12-128',NULL),
(230703,'chemistry','Which of the following orbital can be used by metal atom or ion under the influence of ligands according to Valence Bond Theory?','ns, np and nd','According to this theory, the metal atom or ion under the influence of\nligands can use its (n-1)d, ns, np or ns, np, nd orbitals for hybridisation.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 254; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','ns, np and (n+1)d','ns, np and nd','ns, (n+1)p and nd','(n+2)s, np and nd','NEW','12-254','12-128',NULL),
(230704,'chemistry','Match the following.','sp³-Tetrahedral, dsp²-Square Planar, sp³d-Trigonal bipyramidal, d²sp³-Octahedral','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 254; Table 9.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'3','match','sp³,Tetrahedral','dsp²,Square Planar','sp³d,Trigonal bipyramidal','d²sp³,Octahedral','NEW','12-254','12-128',NULL),
(230705,'chemistry','What is the hybridisation for [Co(NH₃)₆]⁺³?','d²sp³','In the diamagnetic\noctahedral complex,\n[Co(NH3)6]3+, the cobalt ion\nis in +3 oxidation state\nand has the electronic\nconfiguration 3d6.Six pairs of electrons, one from each NH3 molecule, occupy the six\nhybrid orbitals. Thus, the complex has octahedral geometry and is\ndiamagnetic because of the absence of unpaired electron.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 255; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','d²sp³','sp³d²','sp³d','dsp³','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(230706,'chemistry','What is the hybridisation of [CoF₆]³⁻?','sp³d²','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 255; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','d²sp³','sp³d²','sp³d','dsp³','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(230707,'chemistry','What is true for [Co(NH₃)₆]⁺³?','All of these','In the formation of this complex, since the inner d orbital (3d) is used in hybridisation,\nthe complex, [Co(NH3)6]3+ is called an inner orbital or low spin or spin\npaired complex.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 255; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','It is inner orbital complex.','It is spin paired complex.','It is diamagnetic in nature.','All of these','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(230708,'chemistry','What is shape and hybridasation of [NiCl₄]²⁻?','sp³, Tetrahedral','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 255; Paragraph No. 3) diagram(ne2301)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','sp³, Tetrahedral','sp³, Square planar','dsp², Tetrahedral','dsp², Square planar','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(230709,'chemistry','What are the number of unpaired electrons in [NiCl₄]²⁻?','2','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 255; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','8','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(230710,'chemistry','Which of the following is paramagnetic?','[NiCl₄]²⁻','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 255; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','[Ni(CO)₄]','[Co(NH₃)₆]⁺³','[NiCl₄]²⁻','All of these','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(230711,'chemistry','What is true about [Ni(CO)₄]?','Its hydrbidisation is sp³.','[Ni(CO)4] has tetrahedral geometry but is diamagnetic since nickel is in\nzero oxidation state and contains no unpaired electron.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 255; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','It is paramagneic in nature.','It has square planar shape.','It is inner orbital complex.','Its hydrbidisation is sp³.','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(230712,'chemistry','Choose the correct statements from the following.','All of these','In[Ni(CN)₄]²⁻ each of the hybridised orbitals receives a pair of electrons from a\ncyanide ion. The compound is diamagnetic as evident from the absence\nof unpaired electron.\nIt is important to note that the hybrid orbitals do not actually exist.\nIn fact, hybridisation is a mathematical manipulation of wave equation\nfor the atomic orbitals involved.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 256; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','The hybrid orbitals do not actually exist.','Hybridisation is a mathematical manipulation of wave equation for the atomic orbitals involved.','In [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻, each of the hybridised orbitals receives a pair of electrons from a cyanide ion.','All of these','NEW','12-256','12-130',NULL),
(230713,'chemistry','Which of the following is true for [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻?','It has square planar shape.','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 256; Paragraph No. 1) diagram(ne2302)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Valence Bond Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','It is paramagneic in nature.','It has square planar shape.','It is outer orbital complex.','Its hydrbidisation is sp³.','NEW','12-256','12-130',NULL),
(230714,'biology','At the end of meiosis, how many sets of chromosomes gets incorporated into each gamete?','One set','In diploid organisms, specialised cells called meiocytes undergo meiosis. At the end of meiosis, only one set of chromosomes gets incorporated into each gamete.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:13, paragraph no:1)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Two sets','Three sets','Four sets','One set','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
