(230720,'biology','After their formation, Male and female gametes must be physically brought together to facilitate fusion.','TRUE','Male and female gametes need to be physically brought together to facilitate fertilization. In most of the organisms, male gamete is motile and female gamete is stationary.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:13, paragraph no:2)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230721,'biology','In a majority of organisms, male gamete is ________ and female gamete is __________, (respectively)','Motile,stationary','In most of the organisms, male gamete is motile and female gamete is stationary.\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:13, paragraph no:2)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Stationary,motile','Motile,stationary','Motile,motile','Stationary,stationary','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230722,'biology','Both types of gametes are motile in','Algae and Fungi','In a few fungi and algae both types of gametes are motile.\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:13, paragraph no:2)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Algae and Bacteria','Fungi and Bacteria','Algae and Fungi','Plants and animals','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230723,'biology','Transfer of gametes through water takes place in\ni) Seed plants\nii) Pteridophytes\niii) Bryophytes \niv) Algae','ii, iii, and iv','In simple plants like algae, bryophytes, and pteridophytes, water serve as the medium through which gamete transfer takes place.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:13, paragraph no:2)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','ii, iii, and iv','i, iii, and iv','i, ii and iv','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230724,'biology','Choose the correct statement','Male gametes produced are several thousand times the number of female gametes','A large number of male gametes fail to reach the female gametes. Thus, to compensate for the loss of male gametes during transport, organisms produce male gametes several thousand times the number of female gametes produced.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:13, paragraph no:2)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Male gametes produced are several thousand times the number of female gametes','Female gametes produced are several thousand times the number of male gametes','Male gametes and female gametes are produced equally low.','Male gametes and female gametes are produced equally more','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230725,'biology','Choose the correct statement','Pollen grains produced in anthers are to be transferred to stigma to facilitate fertilisation','Pollen grains produced in anthers have to be transferred to the stigma to facilitate fertilisation.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:14, paragraph no:1)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Pollen grains produced in anthers are to be transferred to stigma to facilitate fertilisation','Pollen grains produced in style are to be transferred to stamens to facilitate fertilisation','Pollen grains produced in ovary are to be transferred to anthers to facilitate fertilisation','Pollen grains produced in ovule are to be transferred to style to facilitate fertilisation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230726,'biology','Pollentubes discharge the male gametes near','Egg cell','Pollen tube discharges its contents into cytoplasm of synergids primarily which eventually disintegrates leading to transfer and fusion of the male gamete with the egg cell.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:14, paragraph no:1)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Antipodals','Integuments','Nucellus','Egg cell','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230727,'biology','Successful transfer and coming together of gametes is essential for Fertilization.','TRUE','Successful transfer and coming together of gametes is essential for the most critical event in sexual reproduction, the fertilisation.This process called syngamy results in the formation of a diploid zygote.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:14, paragraph no:1)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230728,'biology','Parthenogenesis is exhibited by:','Lizards,turkey,rotifers,honeybees','Parthenogenesis is a form of reproduction in which an egg can develop into an embryo without being fertilized by a sperm.Several lizards ,turkey, rotifers, honeybees are known to reproduce by parthenogenesis.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:14, paragraph no:3)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Honey bees,rotifers,crocodiles,fishes','Lizards,turkey,rotifers,honeybees','Lizards,fishes,birds,crocodiles','Birds,algae,fishes,crocodiles','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230729,'biology','Choose the correct statement','In most aquatic animals such as majority of algae and fishes as well amphibians syngamy occurs in external medium, outside  the body of the organism.','In most aquatic animals such as majority of algae and fishes as well amphibians syngamy occurs in external medium.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:14, paragraph no:4)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','In most aquatic animals such as majority of algae and fishes as well amphibians syngamy occurs in external medium, outside  the body of the organism.','In most aquatic animals such as majority of algae and fishes as well amphibians syngamy occurs in internal medium, outside  the body of the organism.','In most aquatic animals such as majority of algae and fishes as well amphibians syngamy occurs in external medium, inside the body of the organism.','In most aquatic animals such as majority of algae and fishes as well amphibians syngamy occurs in internal medium, inside the body of the organism.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230730,'biology','Gametic fusion in the external medium , water outside the body of organism is known as _________  fertilisation.','External','In most aquatic organisms, such as a majority of algae and fishes as well as amphibians, syngamy occurs in the external medium (water), i.e., outside the body of the organism. This type of gametic fusion is called external fertilisation.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:14, paragraph no:4)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'1','mcq','External','Internal','Exterior','Interior','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230731,'biology','Choose the correct statement with regards to external fertilisation.\n\ni) A large number of gametes are produced to ensure the survival of offspring, by increasing the chances of syngamy\n\nii) A major disadvantage is that the offsprings are vulnerable to predators threatening their survival up to adulthood.\n\niii) External fertilisation occurs in bony fishes and frogs where a large number of offspring are produced.\n\niv) Organisms exhibiting external fertilisation show great synchrony between the sexes and release a large number of gametes into the surrounding medium in order to enhance the chances of syngamy.','All are correct','Organisms exhibiting external fertilisation show great synchrony between the sexes and release a large number of gametes into the surrounding medium (water) in order to enhance the chances of syngamy. This happens in the bony fishes and frogs where a large number of offspring are produced. A major disadvantage is that the offspring are extremely vulnerable to predators threatening their survival up to adulthood.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:14, paragraph no:4)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','All are correct','i, ii and iv','ii, iii, and iv','i, ii, and iv','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230732,'biology','Intetnal Fertilisation occurs in:','Fungi, higher animals such as reptiles, birds, mammals and in a majority of plants','In the Internal fertilization, the Syngamy occurs inside the body of organisms. Example- fungi, birds, mammals. Male gametes are released in proximity to the female gametes.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:15, paragraph no:1)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Fungi, higher animals such as reptiles, birds, mammals and in a majority of plants','Fungi, higher animals such as amphibians, birds, mammals and in a majority of plants','Fungi, higher animals such as amphibians, aquatic animals,birds, mammals and in a majority of plants','Fungi, higher animals such as aquatic animals, birds, mammals and in a majority of plants','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230733,'biology','Bryophytes,Pteridophytes, Gymnosperms and Angiosperms undergo ______ fertilisation','Internal','Internal fertilization is the union of an egg and sperm cell during sexual reproduction inside the female body. Bryophytes, Pteridophytes, gymnosperms undergo internal fertilisation.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:15, paragraph no:1)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'1','mcq','External','Internal','Exterior','Interior','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230734,'biology','In fungi, higher animals such as reptiles, birds, mammals and in a majority of plants egg is formed inside the female body where they fuse with the male gamete.','TRUE','In fungi, higher animals such as reptiles, birds, mammals and in a majority of plants egg is formed inside the female body where they fuse with the male gamete.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:15, paragraph no:1)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230735,'biology','In seed plants, the non-motile male gametes are carried to female gamete by','Pollen tubes','Since the male gametes are non motile in seed plants they are carried to the female gaete by pollen tubes.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:15, paragraph no:1)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','fillup','Po,lle,n tu,bes',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230736,'biology','Assertion:Plants are defined as Monoecious and Dioecious.\n \nReason:Flowers may be Bisexual or Unisexual.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Plants may have both male and female reproductive structures in the same plant (bisexual) or on different plants (unisexual).Even a flower can have both androecium and gynoecium or only one or none\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:11, paragraph no:3)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but reason is fase','Both Assertion and Reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230737,'biology','Gametes are haploid in nature and usually a direct product of __________','Meiotic division','Gametes are haploid in nature and usually a direct product of meiotic division except in haploid organisms where gametes are formed by mitosis. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:11, paragraph no:3)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Mitotic division','Meiotic division','Cell division','Binary fission','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230738,'biology','In an attempt to compensate for the loss of male gametes during transport, what is the ratio of production of males gametes to female gamates?','Several thousand times','A large number of the male gametes, however, fail to reach the female gametes. To compensate this loss of male gametes during transport, the number of male gametes produced is several thousand times the number of female gametes produced.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:13, paragraph no:2)','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Almost the same','Ten times','Several thousand times','Hundred times','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230739,'biology','What is the medium through which gamete transfer takes place in algae, bryophytes and pteridophytes?','Water','Water is the medium through which gamete transfer takes place in algae, bryophytes and pteridophytes.','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Jelly','Air','Water','Semen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230740,'biology','Pollen grains have to be transferred to the stigma ______','Before it can lead to fertilization','Pollen grains have to be transferred to the stigma before it can lead to fertilization.','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Before it can lead to fertilization','After fertilization','At the time of fertilization','None','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230741,'biology','Ploidy of ovary, anther, egg, pollen, male gamete and zygote are respectively','2n, 2n, n, n, n, 2n','Ovary: 2n, Anther: 2n, Egg: n, pollen: n, male gamete: n, zygote: 2n','Reproduction >> Reproduction in Organisms >> Pre-fertilisation And Fertilisation Events 2',NULL,'3','mcq','2n, 2n, n, 2n, n, 2n','2n, 2n, n, n, n, 2n','2n, n, n, n, n, n','2n, 2n, n, 2n, 2n, 2n','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230801,'chemistry','The magnetic moment of coordination compounds can be measured by the magnetic ____ experiments.','susceptibility','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 256; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','fillup','su,sc,ept,ibi,li,ty',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-256','12-130','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/230801q.png'),
(110716,'biology','Most of the water flow in the roots occurs via the _______ since the cortical cells are loosely packed, and hence offer no resistance to water movement.','Apoplast','Most of the water flow in the roots occurs via the apoplast since the cortical cells are loosely packed, and hence offer no resistance to water movement.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 185','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Symplast','Apoplast','Cytoplasmic streaming','Osmosis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(230802,'chemistry','Which ions have similar magnetic behaviour and coordination entities?','Ti⁺³, V⁺³, Cr⁺³','A critical study of the magnetic data of coordination compounds of\nmetals of the first transition series reveals some complications. For\nmetal ions with upto three electrons in the d orbitals, like Ti3+ (d1); V3+\n(d2); Cr3+ (d3); two vacant d orbitals are available for octahedral hybridisation with 4s and 4p orbitals. The magnetic behaviour of these free ions and their coordination entities is similar. When more than three 3d electrons are present, the required pair of 3d orbitals for\noctahedral hybridisation is not directly available (as a consequence of Hund’s rule). Thus, for d4 (Cr2+, Mn3+), d5 (Mn2+, Fe3+), d6 (Fe2+, Co3+) cases, a vacant pair of d orbitals results only by pairing of 3d electrons which leaves two, one and zero unpaired electrons, respectively.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 256; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Ti⁺³, V⁺³, Cr⁺³','Ti⁺³, V⁺³, Co⁺³','Ti⁺³, Co⁺³, Fe⁺³','Fe⁺³, V⁺³, Cr⁺³','NEW','12-256','12-130','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/230802q.png'),
(230803,'chemistry','What is number of unpaired electrons present in [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻?','2','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 256; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','4','5','NEW','12-256','12-130','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/230803q.png'),
(230804,'chemistry','What is the number of unpaire electrons present in [MnCl₆]³⁻?','4','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 256; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','4','5','NEW','12-256','12-130','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/230804q.png'),
(230805,'chemistry','Which of the following has magnetic moment of single electron?','[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 256; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻','[Mn(CN)₆]³⁻','[FeF₆]³⁻','All of these','NEW','12-256','12-130','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/230805q.png'),
(230806,'chemistry','Which of the following is diamagnetic?','[Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 256; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻','[Mn(CN)₆]³⁻','[Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻','[CoF₆]³⁻','NEW','12-256','12-130','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/230806q.png'),
(230807,'chemistry','Choose the odd one with respect to inner orbital complex.','[CoF₆]³⁻','[Mn(CN)6]³⁻, [Fe(CN)6]³⁻\nand [Co(C2O4)3]³⁻ are inner orbital complexes involving d2sp3 hybridisation,\nthe former two complexes are paramagnetic and the latter diamagnetic.\nOn the other hand, [MnCl6]³⁻, [FeF6]³⁻ and [CoF6-]³⁻ are outer orbital\ncomplexes involving sp3d2\nhybridisation and are paramagnetic\ncorresponding to four, five and four unpaired electrons.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 256; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻','[Mn(CN)₆]³⁻','[Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻','[CoF₆]³⁻','NEW','12-256','12-130','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/230807q.png'),
(230808,'chemistry','Which of the following has d²sp³ hybridisation?','[Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 256; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','[CoF₆]³⁻','[FeF₆]³⁻','[Co(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻','All of these','NEW','12-256','12-130','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/230808q.png'),
(230809,'chemistry','What would be shape of [MnBr₄]²⁻, if magnetic moment is 5.9BM?','Tetrahedral','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 257; Example 9.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Square planar','Tetrahedral','Octahedral','Linear','NEW','12-257','12-131','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/230809q.png'),
(230810,'chemistry','Which of the following property cannot be explained by valence bond theory for coordination compounds?','Colour','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 257; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Colour','Shape','Geometry','All of these','NEW','12-257','12-131','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/230810q.png'),
(230811,'chemistry','How many of the following are shortcomings of VBT?\nA. It involves a number of assumptions.\nB. It provides quantitative interpretation of magnetic data.\nC. It does not give a quantitative interpretation of the thermodynamic\nor kinetic stabilities of coordination compounds.\nD. It does not distinguish between weak and strong ligands.','3','Shortcomings of VBT\n(i) It involves a number of assumptions.\n(ii) It does not give quantitative interpretation of magnetic data.\n(iii) It does not explain the colour exhibited by coordination compounds.\n(iv) It does not give a quantitative interpretation of the thermodynamic\nor kinetic stabilities of coordination compounds.\n(v) It does not make exact predictions regarding the tetrahedral and\nsquare planar structures of 4-coordinate complexes.\n(vi) It does not distinguish between weak and strong ligands.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 257; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','3','2','1','4','NEW','12-257','12-131','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/230811q.png'),
