(231319,'chemistry','Homoleptic complex from the following complexes is:','Potassium trioxalatoaluminate (III)','Complexes in which a metal is bound to only one kind of donor group or ligands are known as Homoleptic complexes. The formula of the given compounds is as follows:\n\n1. Triamminetriaquachromium (III)  = [Cr(NH3)3(H2O)3]3+\nIt has two different ligands, not a homoleptic complex. \n\n2. Potassium trioxalatoaluminate (III) = K3[Al(C2O4)3]\nIt has one type of ligand, thus, it is an example of a homoleptic complex. \n\n3. Diamminechloridonitrito-N-platinum (II) =  [Pt(NH3​)2​Cl(NO2​)] \nIt has three different ligands, not a homoleptic complex. \n\n4.  Pentaamminecarbonatocobalt (III) chloride  = [Co(NH3)5(CO3)]Cl\n\nIt has two different ligands, not a homoleptic complex. Thus, option second is the correct answer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Triamminetriaquachromium (III)','Potassium trioxalatoaluminate (III)','Diamminechloridonitrio-N-platinum (II)','Pentaamminecarbonatocobalt (III) Chloride','NEW','12-246','12-120',NULL),
(231320,'chemistry','A complex compound is formed by ligands nitrate and chloride. It gives 2 moles of AgCl precipate with AgNO3. What will its formula will be:','[Co(NH3)5NO3]Cl2','The complex gives two moles of AgCl precipitate with AgNO3. It indicate that two Cl atoms are present outside the complex which is possible in [Co(NH3)5NO3]Cl2 . Hence, option one is the correct answer. [Co(NH3)5Cl]NO3Cl and [Co(NH3)4ClNO3]Cl complexes contains only one Cl atom outside the coordination sphere hence, only one mole AgCl will precipitate out. [C o(NH3)4Cl2]NO3 does not contain any Cl atom outside the coordination sphere hence, it will not generate AgCl precipitate with AgNO3.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','[Co(NH3)5NO3]Cl2','[Co(NH3)5Cl]NO3Cl','[Co(NH3)4Cl2]NO3','[Co(NH3)4ClNO3]Cl','NEW','12-245','12-119',NULL),
(231321,'chemistry','The coordination number of Ni in [Ni(C2O4)3]4- is','6','The coordination number is the number of ligands attached to the central ion (more specifically, the number of donor atoms). In [Ni(C2O4)3]−4 ; Ni is in +2 oxidation state. Oxalate ligand is identate(2donor O-atoms)  . So the coordination number of [Ni(C2O4)3]−4 is 6.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','3','6','4','2','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231322,'chemistry','According to IUPAC nomenclature, sodum nitroprusside is named as','Sodium pentacyanidonitrosyl Ferrate (II)','Step 1:The structure of sodium nitroprusside is Na2[Fe(CN)5NO].\n\nStep 2: The oxidation state of Fe is +2. For CN, cyanido is used, for NO nitrosyl is used. The complex has a negative charge hence, ferrate is used for Fe. The IUPAC name of the compound is Sodium pentacyanidonitrosyl ferrate (II).','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Sodium Nitro Ferricyanide','Sodium Nitro Ferrocyanide','Sodium pentacyanidonitrosyl Ferrate (II)','Sodium pentacyanondonitrosyl Ferrate (III)','NEW','12-249','12-123',NULL),
(231323,'chemistry','The IUPAC name of the complex [Ag(H2O)2][Ag(CN)2] is:','Diaquasilver(I) dicyanidoargentate (I)','In [Ag(H₂O)₂][Ag(CN)₂], both anion and cation are complex. The cation part is [Ag(H₂O)₂]+ and the anion part is [Ag(CN)₂]-. The cation contains two H2O molecules hence, diaqua is used and Ag silver is used. The cation IUPAC name is diaquasilver(I). In anion, [Ag(CN)₂]- , two cyanido ligands are present. If the complex ion is an anion, the name of the metal ends with the suffix – ate. Hence, argentate is used. The IUPAC name of the anion part is dicyanidoargentate(I). The IUPAC name of the compound is diaquasilver(I) dicyanidoargentate (I)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Diaquasilver(I) dicyanidoargentate (I)','Diaquasilver(II) diaquaargentate (II)','Diaquasilver(II) dicyanidoargentate (II)','Dicyanidosilver(I)diaquaargentate (I)','NEW','12-249','12-123',NULL),
(231324,'chemistry','The IUPAC name of the complex [Co(NH3)3ClBrNO2] is:','Triammine bromochloronitrocobalt (III)','In the neutral complex, [Co(NH3)3)ClBrNO2], let the oxidation state of Co is x: x + 3(0) + 1(-1) + 1(-1) + 1(-1) = 0 \nx= + 3\ni.e. Co is in +3 oxidation state .According to IUPAC rules , the ligands will be written in alphabetical order. As there are three NH3 ligands so "tri" will be used. But this will not be considered in the alphabetical order. So, correct name of [Co(NH3)3)ClBrNO2] is Triamminebromochloronitrocobalt (III).','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Triammine bromochloronitrocobaltate (III)','Triammine bromochloronitrocobalt (III)','Triammine bromonitrochloronitrocobalt(III)','Triamminenitrobromochloronitrocobalt (III)','NEW','12-249','12-123',NULL),
(231325,'chemistry','The coordination compound that will give four isomers is:','[Fe(PPh)3NH3ClBr]Cl','[Fe(PPh3)3NH3ClBr]Cl will give four isomers. In coordination compounds , [Ma3bcd] type of compound gives four isomers.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','[Fe(en)3]Cl3','[Co(en)2Cl2]Cl','[Fe(PPh)3NH3ClBr]Cl','[Co(PPh)3Cl]Cl3','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231326,'chemistry','Identify the complex among the following that is not expected to exhibit isomerism','[Ni(NH3)2Cl2]','Tetrahedral complexes do not show geometrical isomerism because the relative positions of the unidentate ligands bonded to the central metal are the same with respect to each other.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','[Pt(NH3)2Cl2]','[Ni(NH3)2Cl2]','[Ni(en)3]2+','[Ni(NH3)4(H2O)2]2+','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231327,'chemistry','The octahedral complex that will not show geometrical isomerism is (A and B are mondentate ligands)','[MA5B]','[MA4B2], [MA2B4] and [MA3B3] can show geometrical isomerism. [MA5B] due to absence of symmetry of B it cannot exist in the form of cis-trans isomer.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','[MA2B4]','[MA3B3]','[MA4B2]','[MA5B]','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231328,'chemistry','The number of geometric isomers shown by [Pt(Py)(NH3)BrCl]','3','The given complex is square planar in nature and is of the type [M (abcd)]. It has three geometrical isomers.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','4','0','2','3','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231329,'chemistry','In a particular isomer of [Co(NH3)4Cl2]0, the Cl-Co-Cl angle is 90 deg and the isomer is known as','Cisisomer','[Co(NH3)4Cl2]0 has two structure, that is cis and trans','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Optical Isomer','Cisisomer','Position Isomer','Linkage Isomer','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231330,'chemistry','The number of possible isomers for the complex [Co(en)2Cl2]Cl will be (en=ethylenediammine)','3','Possible isomers of [Co(en)2 Cl2]Cl are given below. Note that Cis isomer has 2 enantiomers. Total number of stereoisomers = 2 + 1 = 3','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','2','1','3','4','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231331,'chemistry','Which of the following is most stable','[CoCl2(en)2]NO3','Ligands that have more than two or three donor sites from which they form a coordination bond with the central metal and therefore, can form a ring-like structure. These ligands are known as chelating ligands. Chelating ligand increases the stability of the complex. [CoCl2(en)2]NO3 has two chelating ligands(en) hence it is the most stable complex.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','[Co(NH3)6]2(SO4)3','[Co(NH3)4(H2O)Br](NO3)2','[Co(NH3)3(NO3)3]','[CoCl2(en)2]NO3','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231332,'chemistry','The hypothetical complex chlorodiaquatriamminecobalt(III) chloride can be represented as','[CoCl(NH3)3(H2O)2]Cl2','The given complex is chlorodiaquatriamminecobalt (III) chloride .\n1. "Aqua" is used for H2O and "di " prefix is used . "Ammine" is used for NH3 ligand, so "tri" represents three ligands. "Chloro" represents one Cl ligand is present inside the complex.\n2. Chloride ligand is also present outside . Number of ligand can be calculated as:\n-1 + 3 = x (Charge on complex) So , two Cl ligands are present outside the complex. \n3. So , the complex will be : [CoCl(NH3)3(H2O)2]Cl2','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','[CoCl(NH3)3(H2O)2]Cl2','[Co(NH3)3(H2O)Cl3]','[Co(NH3)3(H2O)2Cl]','[CoCl(NH3)3(H2O)2]Cl2','NEW','12-250','12-124',NULL),
(231333,'chemistry','Coordination compound that would exhibit optical isomerism is:','Tris-(ethylenediammine) cobalt(III) chloride','Optical isomerism occurs when a molecule is not superimposable with it\'s mirror image. Among the given compounds Tris – (ethylenediamine) cobalt (III) chloride shows optical isomers.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Diamminedichloroplatinum(II)','Trans-dicyanobis(ethylenediammine)chromium(III) chloride','Tris-(ethylenediammine) cobalt(III) chloride','Pentaamminenitrocobalt(III) iodide','NEW','12-253','12-125',NULL),
(231334,'chemistry','An anticancer agent among the following is','cis-[PtCl2(NH3)2]','Cisplatin is administered through a vein (intravenously or IV) as an infusion. Cancerous cells lose this ability. Cancer cells no longer have the normal checks and balances in place that control and limit cell division. The process of cell division, whether normal or cancerous cells, is through the cell cycle.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','mer-[Co(NH3)3Cl]','cis-[PtCl2(NH3)2]','cis-K2[PtCl2Br2]','NH2CoCl4','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231335,'chemistry','A metal ion present in vitamin B12 is','Co(III)','STEP 1: Vitamin B12, also known as cobalamin, is a water-soluble vitamin involved in metabolism. It is one of eight B vitamins. It is a cofactor in DNA synthesis , in both fatty acid and amino acid metabolism. \nSTEP 2: It is important in the normal functioning of the nervous system via its role in the synthesis of mylein , and in the maturation of red blood cells in the bone marrow.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe(II)','Co(III)','Zn(II)','Ca(II)','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231336,'chemistry','Mg is present in','Chlorophyll','Mg is present in chlorophyll. Chlorophyll is a pigment that gives plants their green color, and it helps plants create their own food through photosynthesis. The oxidation of Mg is +2.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Chlorophyll','Haemoglobin','Vitamin B12','Vitamin B','NEW','12-263','12-137',NULL),
(231337,'chemistry','CN- is a strong field ligand. This is due to the fact that','It can accept electrons from metal species','The metal-carbon bond in metal carbonyls possesses both σ and π characters. The M–C σ bond is formed by the donation of lone pair of electrons on the carbonyl carbon into a vacant orbital of the metal. The M–C π bond is formed by the donation of a pair of electrons from a filled d orbital of metal into the vacant antibonding π* orbital of carbon monoxide. The metal to ligand bonding creates a synergic effect that strengthens the bond between CO and the metal. Hence, due to σ donor and Π acceptor nature. It can accept electrons from metal ions. Thus, it has a strong coordinating ability with metals and it also acts as a strong field ligand.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','It is a pseudohalide','It can accept electrons from metal species','It forms high spin complexes with metal species','It carries a negative charge','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231338,'chemistry','The carbonyl compound with the strongest C-O bond is','[Mn(CO)6]+','STEP 1: Synergic bonding - This effect is observed when a pi-back donation of electron density occurs from an atomic orbital of one atom to the antibonding pi-orbital of another. The antibonding pi-orbital of a pi-acceptor ligand.\nSTEP 2: Due to positive oxidation state of Mn, back donation in π* ABMO of CO is minimum, therefore C–O bond is strongest.\nSTEP 3: As the electron density on metal increases, back donation in  π* ABMO of CO increases . So, the C-O bond will be weaken.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe(CO)5','[Mn(CO)6]+','Cr(CO)6','[V(CO)6]-','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231339,'chemistry','Select the correct option based on statements below \nAsseertion: The metal carbon bond in metal carbonyls posesses both σ and π character.\nReason: The ligand to metal bond is a  π bond and metal to ligand bond is a σ bond','A is true but R is false','In metal carbonyl, synergic bonding takes place. The metal-carbon bond in metal carbonyls possess both σ and π character. The M–C σ bond is formed by the donation of lone pair of electrons on the carbonyl carbon into a vacant orbital of the metal. The M–C π bond is formed by the donation of a pair of electrons from a filled d orbital of metal into the vacant antibonding π* orbital of carbon monoxide.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is true explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false','A is false but R is true','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231340,'chemistry','The number of bridging Carbonyl groups in [Co2(CO)8] and [Mn2(CO)10], respectively are','2 and 0','Metal carbonyls are coordination complexes of transition metals with carbon monoxide ligands. [Co2(CO)8] has 2 bridging ligands and [Mn2(CO)10] has zero bridging ligand.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','2 and 0','2 and 2','2 and 4','0 and 2','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231341,'chemistry','The shape of Fe(CO)5 is','Trigonal Bipyramidal','The Fe metal is connected to 5 CO ligands.\nThe electronic configuration of Fe is as follows:\nFe = [Ar] 3d6 4s2\n\nIn the presence of 5 ligands the configuration of Fe = [Ar] 3d8 4s0 4p0 4d0 . The five orbitals one 3d, three 4p, and one 4s combine and form 5 hybrid orbitals that is dsp3. Hence, the hybridization of Fe in Fe(CO)5 is dsp3, hence, geometry is trigonal bipyramidal.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','Octahedral','Square Planar','Trigonal Bipyramidal','Square pyramidal','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231342,'chemistry','A pair that contains both the ions coloured in an aqueous solution is (At no. Sc = 21, Ti = 22, Ni = 28, Cu = 29, Co = 27)','Ni2+, Ti3+','Any transition metal ion which has one or more unpaired electrons is colored due to their tendency to show d-d transition.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Ni2+, Ti3+','Sc3+, Ti3+','Sc3+, Co2+','Ni2+, Cu+','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231343,'chemistry','Out of Ti(F6)2-, Co(F6)3-, Cu2Cl2 and Ni(Cl4)2- [Ti = 22, Co = 27, Cu = 29 and Ni = 28] the colorless species','Ti(F6)2- and Cu2Cl2','The species which cannot show d-d transition due to no unpaired electrons are colorless.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','Ti(F6)2- and Co(F6)3-','Cu2Cl2 and Ni(Cl6)2-','Ti(F6)2- and Cu2Cl2','Co(F6)3- and Ni(Cl4)2-','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231344,'chemistry','A complex of ions among the following that is expexcted to absorb visible light is (At No. Zn = 30, Sc = 21, Ti = 22, Cr = 24)','[Cr(NH3)6]3+','By absorption of visible light ,it simply means that the compound shows colour and is visible to us. Also, we know that the compounds with unpaired electrons show d-d transition. Hence, those metal complexes in which the d-subshell is incomplete are expected to absorb visible light .','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','[Sc(H2O)3(NH3)3]3+','[Ti(en)2(NH3)2]4+','[Cr(NH3)6]3+','[Zn(NH3)6]2+','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231345,'chemistry','The complex ion that does not expected to absorb visible light  is','[Ni(CN)4]2-','Any orbital with incomplete d-orbitals is seen to absorb visible light.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','[Ni(CN)4]2-','[Cr(NH3)6]3+','[Fe(H2O)6]2+','[Ni(H2O)6]2+','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231346,'chemistry','The order of energy absorbed which is responsible for the color of complexes is \nA. [Ni(H2O)2(en)2]2+\nB. [Ni(H2O)4(en)]2+ \nC. [Ni(en)3]2+','C>A>B','The order of energy absorbed depends on the strength of the ligand. As the strength of the ligand increases the energy absorbed also increases. The order of ligands according to their strength is as follows: en > H2O\n[Ni(en)3]2+ has three en ligand hence it has maximum energy. As the number of en ligand decreases, the energy absorbed which is responsible for the color of complexes is decreases.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','B>A>C','A>B>C','C>B>A','C>A>B','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231347,'chemistry','The correct increasing order for the wavelengths of absorption in complexes of Co3+ is','[Co(en)3]3+ < [Co(NH3)6]3+ < [Co(H2O)6]3+','STEP 1: ∆ο depends on the ligand if the central atom ion is same in complexes. As energy is inversely proportional to the wavelength. Hence, ∆ο represents energy, Δο increases energy increases, and the wavelength decreases.\nSTEP 2: The increasing order of ligands according to their strength is as follows:\nen > NH3 > H2O\nThe order of complexes, according to do is as follows:\n[Co(H2O)6]3+ < [Co(NH3)6]3+ < [Co(en)3]3+\nSTEP 3: The increasing order for the wavelengths of absorption in the visible region for the complexes is as follows:\nCo(en)3]3+ < [Co(NH3)6]3+ < [Co(H2O)6]3+','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'3','mcq','[Co(H2O)6]3+ < [Co(en)3]3+ < [Co(NH3)6]3+','[Co(H2O)6]3+ < [Co(NH3)6]3+ < [Co(en)2]3+','[Co(NH3)6]3+ < [Co(en)3]3+ < [Co(H2O)6]3+','[Co(en)3]3+ < [Co(NH3)6]3+ < [Co(H2O)6]3+','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231348,'chemistry','The correct statement, among the following, is:','[Co(CN)6]3- has no unpaired electrons and will be in a low-spin configuration.','CN- is a strong field ligand , pairing will occur.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','[Co(CN)6]3- has no unpaired electrons and will be in a low-spin configuration.','[Co(CN)6]3- has four unpaired electrons and will be in a low-spin configuration.','[Co(CN)6]3- has four unpaired electrons and will be In a high-spin configuration.','[Co(CN)6]3- has no unpaired electrons and will be in a high-spin configuration.','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231349,'chemistry','The correct order of increasing field strength of ligands is:','SCN- < F- < (C2O4)2- < CN-','Follow spectrochemical series. π acceptor ligand are the most strongest ligand. Arrangement of different ligands in increasing order of field strength called spectrochemical series.\nThe series are as follows:\nI- < SCN- < CI- < S2- < F- < OH- < C2O42- < H2O < NH3 < CN- < CO\nThus, correct option is 4.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Previous Year Question',NULL,'2','mcq','SCN- < F- < CN- < (C2O4)2-','F- < SCN- < (C2O4)2- < CN-','CN- < (C2O4)2- < SCN- < F-','SCN- < F- < (C2O4)2- < CN-','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231401,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about "Coordination compounds" are true?\n\na. Coordination compounds are backbone of modern inorganic chemistry and biochemistry.\n\nb. Chlorophyll and hemoglobin are coordination compounds of Mg.\n\nc. Analytical reagents involve the use of coordination compounds.\n\nd. Coordination compounds are used in textile dyeing.','c,d','Coordination compounds are backbone of modern inorganic and bio - inorganic chemistry and chemical industry.\n\nChlorophyll, hemoglobin and Vit. B12 are coordination compounds of Mg, Fe and Co respectively.\n\nCoordination compounds also find many applications in electroplating, textile dyeing and medicinal chemistry.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 244; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','all of the above','NEW','12-244','12-118',NULL),
(231402,'chemistry','Primary valence of the following binary compounds is \na. CrCl₃\nb. PdCl₂\nc. CoCl₂','3,2,2','Binary compounds such as CrCl₃, CoCl₂ or PdCl₂ have primary valence of 3, 2 and 2 respectively.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 244; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2,3','3,2,1','3,2,2','2,2,1','NEW','12-244','12-118',NULL),
(231403,'chemistry','The colour of the following complex is: diagram(n2301)','yellow, purple, green, violet','The formulation of Cobalt chloride ammonia complexes is given in the diagram\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 245; Table 9.1) diagram(ne2301)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Yellow, purple, blue','Purple, green, violet','Yellow, violet, purple','yellow, purple, green, violet','NEW','12-245','12-119','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/231403q.png'),
(231404,'chemistry','Which of the following is not true according to Werner\'s theory?\na. In coordination compounds metals show two types of linkages : primary  and secondary\nb. Primary valences are normally ionisable and are satisfied by positive or negative ions.\nc. Secondary valences are normally non ionisable. They are satisfied by neutral molecules or positive ions.','b,c','Werner in 1898, propounded his theory of coordination compounds. The main postulates are:\n1. In coordination compounds metals show two types of linkages\n(valences)-primary and secondary.\n2. The primary valences are normally ionisable and are satisfied by\nnegative ions.\n3. The secondary valences are non ionisable. These are satisfied by\nneutral molecules or negative ions. The secondary valence is equal to\nthe coordination number and is fixed for a metal.\n4. The ions/groups bound by the secondary linkages to the metal have\ncharacteristic spatial arrangements corresponding to different\ncoordination numbers.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 245; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','all of the above','NEW','12-245','12-119',NULL),
(110717,'biology','The endodermis, is impervious to water because of','Casparian strip','The inner boundary of the cortex, the endodermis, is impervious to water because of a band of suberised matrix called the casparian strip.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 185','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Casparian strip','Dead cells','Organic compounds accumulation','all the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(231405,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Both double salts as well as complexes are formed by combination of two or more stable compounds in stoichiometric ratio\n\nReason: Carnallite and potassium ferrocyanide do not dissociates into simple ions when dissolved in water','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Mixed salts and Complex compounds differ in the fact that double salts such as carnallite, KCl.MgCl2.6H2O,\nMohr’s salt, FeSO4.(NH4)2SO4.6H2O, potash alum, KAl(SO4)2.12H2O, etc.\ndissociate into simple ions completely when dissolved in water.However, complex ions such as [Fe(CN)6]4– of K4 [Fe(CN)6] do not dissociate into\nFe2+ and CN– ions.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 246; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-246','12-120',NULL),
(231406,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion:The complex [Co(NH₃)₅(NO₂)]Cl₂ is obtained as red form, when nitrite ligand is bound through oxygen(-NO₂) and as yellow form, in which the nitrite ligand is bound through nitrogen (-ONO)\n\nReason: The complex shows coordination isomerism where the interchange of ligands between cationic and ionic entities of different metal ions occurs','Both statements are false.','Linkage isomerism arises in a coordination compound containing ambidentate ligand. \n\nA simple example is provided by complexes containing the thiocyanate ligand, NCS–, which may bind through the nitrogen to give M–NCS or through sulphur to give M–SCN.\n\nJorgensen discovered such behaviour in the complex[Co(NH₃)₅(NO₂)]Cl₂ is obtained as yellow form, when nitrite ligand is bound through oxygen(-NO₂) and as red form, in which the nitrite ligand is bound through nitrogen (-ONO)\n\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 253; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-253','12-127',NULL),
(231407,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about "Coordination compounds" are true?\n\na. [Co(NH₃)₆]⁺³ has a spin paired complex.\n\nb. In tetrahedral complexes one s and 3 p orbitals are hybridised to form four equivalent orbitals oriented tetrahedrally.\n\nc.  [Ni(CO)₄] is has tetrahedral geometry but is paramagnetic','a,b','In the formation of this complex, since the inner d orbital (3d) is used in hybridisation, the complex, [Co(NH3)6]3+ is called an inner orbital or low spin or spin paired complex.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 255; Paragraph No. 2)\n\n In tetrahedral complexes one s and 3 p orbitals are hybridised to form four equivalent orbitals oriented tetrahedrally\n\n"[Ni(CO)4] has tetrahedral geometry but is diamagnetic since nickel is in\nzero oxidation state and contains no unpaired electron.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 255; Paragraph No. 4)"','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','all of the above','NEW','12-255','12-129',NULL),
(231408,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: The hybrid orbitals of complex compounds do not actually exist.\n\nReason: Hybridisation is a mathematical manipulation of wave equation for the atomic orbitals involved.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Linkage isomerism arises in a coordination compound containing ambidentate ligand. \n\nA simple example is provided by complexes containing the thiocyanate ligand, NCS–, which may bind through the nitrogen to give M–NCS or through sulphur to give M–SCN.\n\nJorgensen discovered such behaviour in the complex[Co(NH₃)₅(NO₂)]Cl₂ is obtained as yellow form, when nitrite ligand is bound through oxygen(-NO₂) and as red form, in which the nitrite ligand is bound through nitrogen (-ONO)\n\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 253; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-253','12-127',NULL),
(231409,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about "Crystal field theory" are true?\n\na. CFT is an electrostatic model.\n\nb. CFT considers the metal-ligand bond to be covalent bond arising purely from electrostatic interactions between the metal ion and the ligand\n\nc. Point dipoles are treated as point charges in CFT\n\nd. The five d orbitals in an isolated gaseous atom are degenerate','a,d','CFT is an elctrostatic model which considers the metal-ligand bond to be ionic arising purely from electrostatic interactions between the metal ion and the ligand. Ligands are treated as point charges in case of anions or point dipoles in case of neutral molecules. The five d orbitals in an isolated gaseous metal atom/ion have same energy, i.e., they are degenerate. This degeneracy is maintained if a spherically symmetrical field of negative charges surrounds the metal atom/ion.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 257; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,d','all of the above','NEW','12-257','12-131',NULL),
(231410,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n\nStatement I: The degeneracy of the five d orbitals is maintained if a spherically symmetrical field of negative charges surround the metal atom/ion.\n\nStatement II: The degeneracy of the d orbitals is lifted when the negative field is due to ligands in a complex.\n\nChoose the best answer based on the statements above','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Ligands are treated as point charges in case of anions or point dipoles in case of neutral molecules.\n\nThe five d orbitals in an isolated gaseous metal atom/ion have same energy, i.e., they are degenerate. This degeneracy is maintained if a spherically symmetrical field of negative charges surrounds the metal atom/ion.\n\nHowever when this negative field is due to ligands like ammonia and water in a complex, it becomes asymmetrical and the degeneracy of the d orbital is lifted.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 257; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','NEW','12-259','12-133',NULL),
(110718,'biology','The movement of water through the root layers is ultimately _______ in the endodermis','Symplastic','The movement of water through the root layers is ultimately symplastic in the endodermis. This is the only way water and other solutes can enter the vascular cylinder\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 185','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Apoplastic','Symplastic','both a and b','Diffusion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(231411,'chemistry','What will be colour of complex if  colour is absorbed by it respectively is:\na. Yellow\nb. Blue green\nc. Blue','violet,red,yellow orange','The complementary colour is the colour\ngenerated from the wavelength left over; if green light is absorbed by\nthe complex, it appears red.\n Relationship between wavelength of light absorbed and color observed is given in diagram\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 259; Paragraph 2, Table 9.3)\ndiagram(ne2302)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','blue,violet,red','red,yellow orange, blue','violet,red,yellow orange','violet, red,pale yellow','NEW','12-259','12-133',NULL),
(231412,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n\nStatement I: In the absence of ligand the substance is colorless because the crystal field splitting does not occurs.\n\nStatement II: Ruby is colored because octahedral chromium complexes are incorporated into the alumina lattice.\n\nChoose the best answer based on the statements above','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','It is important to note that in the absence of ligand, crystal field splitting does not occur and hence the\nsubstance is colourless. \nFor example, removal of water from [Ti(H2O)6]Cl3 on heating renders it colourless.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 260; Paragraph 1)\n\n"Ruby is aluminium oxide (Al2O3) containing about 0.5-1% Cr3+ ions (d3), which are randomly distributed in positions normally occupied by Al3+. We may view these chromium(III) species as octahedral chromium(III) complexes incorporated into the alumina lattice; d–d transitions at these centres give rise to the colour.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 260; Colour of Some gemstones)"','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231413,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about "Gem stones" are true?\n\na. In emerald, Cr³⁺ occupy octahedral sites in beryl.\n\nb. Ruby is aluminium oxide containing 0.5-1% Cr³⁺.\n\nc. Emerald transmits light in green region\n\nd. The absorption bands of ruby is seen in red region.','a,b,c','"Ruby is aluminium oxide (Al2O3) containing about 0.5-1% Cr3+ ions (d3), which are randomly distributed in positions normally occupied by Al3+. We may view these chromium(III) species as octahedral chromium(III) complexes incorporated into the alumina lattice; d–d transitions at these centres give rise to the colour.\n\n Emerald transmits light in green region\n\nIn emerald, Cr³⁺ occupy octahedral sites in beryl.\n\nThe absorption band in ruby shift to longer wavelength, namely yellow-red and blue region.\n\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 260; Colour of Some gemstones)"','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,b,c','all of the above','NEW','12-261','12-135',NULL),
(231414,'chemistry','State True or False?\na. Weakness of CFT are explained by ligand field theory and valence bond theory.\nb. CFT does not take into account the covalent character of bonding between the ligand and central atom','F,T','The crystal field model is successful in explaining the formation, structures, colour and magnetic properties of coordination compounds\nto a large extent. However, from the assumptions that the ligands are point charges, it follows that anionic ligands should exert the greatest\nsplitting effect. The anionic ligands actually are found at the low end of the spectrochemical series. Further, it does not take into account\nthe covalent character of bonding between the ligand and the central\natom.\nThe weakness of CFT are explained by ligand field theory(LFT) and molecular orbital theory .\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 261; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','T,F','F,T','T,T','F,F','NEW','12-257','12-131',NULL),
(231415,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about "Homoleptic metal carbonyls" are true?\n\na. Decacarbonyldimanganese(0) is made of two square pyramidal units joined by Mn -- Mn bond. \n\nb. Octacarbonyldicobalt(0) has a Co - Co bond bridged by two CO groups.\n\nc. The metal carbon bond in metal carbonyls possess a very strong sigma character without any pi bond.','a,b','"Structure of homoleptic metal carbonyls is given in the diagram\nThe metal-carbon bond in metal carbonyls\npossess both s and p character. The M–C s bond is formed by the donation of lone pair of electrons\non the carbonyl carbon into a vacant orbital of the metal. The M–C p bond is formed by the\ndonation of a pair of electrons from a filled d orbital of metal into the vacant antibonding p* orbital of\ncarbon monoxide. The metal to ligand bonding\ncreates a synergic effect which strengthens the\nbond between CO and the metal(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 262; Paragraph 1)" \ndiagram(ne2303)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,b,c','a,c only','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231416,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about "Applications of coordination compounds" are true?\n\na. Cupron is used in qualitative and quantitative chemical analysis of various metals ions.\n\nb. The selective estimation of Ca and Mg in hard water is done due to difference in their stabilty constants of Calcium and Magnesium complex with EDTA.\n\nc. Excess of Cu and iron can be removed by chelating ligands such as D-penicillamine','a,b','"Coordination compounds find use in many qualitative and\nquantitative chemical analysis. The familiar colour reactions given by metal ions with a number of ligands (especially chelating ligands),\nas a result of formation of coordination entities, form the basis for their detection and estimation by classical and instrumental methods of analysis. Examples of such reagents include EDTA, DMG (dimethylglyoxime), a–nitroso–b–naphthol, cupron, etc.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 262; Paragraph 3)"\n\nThus, excess of copper and iron are removed by the chelating ligands D–penicillamine and desferrioxime B via the formation of coordination compounds. EDTA is used in the treatment of lead poisoning.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 263; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','all of the above','NEW','12-262','12-136',NULL),
(231417,'chemistry','What is the spin only magnetic moment value of the following :\na. [Co(NH₃)₆]⁺³\nb.  [Cr(H₂O)₆]⁺³','0,3.87 BM','"In the diamagnetic\noctahedral complex,\n[Co(NH3)6]3+, the cobalt ion\nis in +3 oxidation state\nand has the electronic\nconfiguration 3d6.Six pairs of electrons, one from each NH3 molecule, occupy the six\nhybrid orbitals. Thus, the complex has octahedral geometry and is\ndiamagnetic because of the absence of unpaired electron. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 255; Paragraph 1)" \ndiagram(ne2304)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0,2.82 BM','0,3.87 BM','2.82 BM, 3.87 BM','4.9 BM, 2.82 BM','NEW','12-256','12-130',NULL),
(231418,'chemistry','Indicate the types of isomerism exhibited by the following:\na. K[Cr((H₂O)₂(C₂O₄)₂]\nb. Pt(NH₃)(H₂O)Cl₂','Geometrical isomerism, geometrical isomerism','Geometrical isomers are given below.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12;)\ndiagram(ne2305)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Geometrical isomerism, Optical isomerism','Optical isomerism, ligand isomerism','Geometrical isomerism, ionisation isomerism','Geometrical isomerism, geometrical isomerism','NEW','12-252','12-124',NULL),
(231419,'chemistry','Calculate the overall complex dissociation equillibrium constant for [Cu(NH₃)₄]Cl₂ , given that stability constant is 2.1 x 10¹³.','4.7 x 10 ⁻¹⁴','Overall dissociation constant = 1/ stability constant\n= 1/(2.1 x 10¹³)\n= 4.7 x 10 ⁻¹⁴','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4.7 x 10 ¹⁴','4.7 x 10 ⁻¹⁴','2.1 x 10 ¹³','4.1 x 10 ¹³','NEW','12-246','12-120',NULL),
(231420,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n\nStatement I: FeSO4 solution mixed with (NH4)2SO4 solution in 1 : 1 molar ratio gives the test of Fe2+ ion\n\nStatement II: CuSO4 solution mixed with aqueous ammonia in 1 : 4 molar ratio does not give the test of Cu2+ ion\n\nChoose the best answer based on the statements above','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','On mixing FeSO₄ with ammonium sulphate , mohr\'s salt is formed, which is a double salt. \nIn aqueous solution, this salt dissociates to give Ferrous ions.\n\nwhereas, when CuSO₄ is mixed with ammonia , a complex [Cu(NH₃)₄]SO₄ is formed. As Cu ion is part of the complex, so it will not give positive test.\n\n"Mixed salts and Complex compounds differ in the fact that double salts such as carnallite, KCl.MgCl2.6H2O,\nMohr’s salt, FeSO4.(NH4)2SO4.6H2O, potash alum, KAl(SO4)2.12H2O, etc.\ndissociate into simple ions completely when dissolved in water.However, complex ions such as [Fe(CN)6]4– of K4 [Fe(CN)6] do not dissociate into\nFe2+ and CN– ions.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 246; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','NEW','12-246','12-120',NULL),
(231421,'chemistry','Amongst the following, which one has the highest magnetic moment value:\na. [Cr(H₂O)₆]⁺³\nb. [Fe(H₂O)₆]⁺²\nc. [Zn(H₂O)₆]⁺²','b','The oxidation states are: Cr (III), Fe (II) and Zn (II).\nElectronic configuration of Cr3+ = 3d³, unpaired electron = 3\nElectronic configuration of Fe2+ = 3d⁶, unpaired electron = 4\nElectronic configuration ofZn2+ = 3d¹⁰, unpaired electrons = 0\n\nSo magnetic moment of Fe2+ has maximum value of magnetic moment.','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','all of the above have same moment','NEW','12-256','12-130',NULL),
(231422,'chemistry','Among the following, which one has the minimum wavelength of absorption in the visible region :\n\na. [Ni(H₂O)₆]⁺²\nb. [Ni(NH₃)₆]⁺²\nc. [Ni(NO₂)₆]⁻⁴','[Ni(NO₂)₆]⁻⁴','CFSE depends on the strength of ligand according to spectro chemical series. \n\nSince NO₂ has maximum strength , therefore [Ni(NO₂)₆]⁻⁴ has maximum energy and thus minimum wavelenth(E=hc/λ).','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','[Ni(H₂O)₆]⁺²','[Ni(NH₃)₆]⁺²','[Ni(NO₂)₆]⁻⁴','none of the above','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(231423,'chemistry','Among the following, which one has the maximum wavelength of absorption in the visible region :\n\na. [Co(CN)₆]⁻³\nb. [Co(NH₃)₆]⁺³\nc. [Co(H₂O)₆]⁺³','[Co(H₂O)₆]⁺³','As the strength of ligand increase , CFSE increase and λ absorbed decreases. Therefore  [Co(H₂O)₆]⁺³ has maximum wavelength','Inorganic chemistry >> Coordination Compounds >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','[Co(H₂O)₆]⁺³','[Co(CN)₆]⁻³','[Co(NH₃)₆]⁺³','none of the above','NEW','12-260','12-134',NULL),
(150101,'chemistry','Which of the following is not solid?','Fuel used in generators and vehicle','Majority of solid substance like high temperature, biocompatible plastics and silver chips etc are destined to play important role in future development of science, here key points is to note that high temperature superconductors are solids and not liquid. Petrol and diesel are liquids and  are used as energy sources in Vehicles and generators\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 1; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','High-temperature superconductor','Biocompatible plastic','Silicon chip','Fuel used in generators and vehicle','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150102,'chemistry','Which of the following is a characteristic of constituent particles in solids?','They are fixed but can oscillate only about mean position.','The constituent particles in solids have a fixed position and can only oscillate about their mean position.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 1; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','They not oscillate in any direction and are fixed.','They are fixed but can oscillate only about mean position.','They are fixed and can oscillate freely in all direction.','They are not fixed but can oscillate only about mean position.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150103,'chemistry','Which of the following are properties of materials made from carbon nanotubes?','Tougher than steel','Carbon nanotubes are new materials that have the potential to provide material that is tougher than steel, lighter than aluminum and more connective than copper.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 1; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Tougher than steel','Heavier than aluminium','Less conductive than copper','Tougher than diamond','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150104,'chemistry','Which of the following can be used as surgical implants?','Biocompliant solids','Biocompliant solids are used for surgical implants. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 1; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'1','mcq','High temperature superconductors','Non-biodegradable polymers','Biocompliant solids','Non-biocompliant solids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150105,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n \n Assertion: Liquids and gases are fluids.\n \n Reasons: Their particles are not free to move.\n \n In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Assertion is correct but reason is not correct.','Liquids and gases are fluids because their particles are free to move.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 1; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion is correct but reason is not correct.','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150106,'chemistry','Biodegradable polymers are used for','packaging','Some other materials which are expected to play an important role in future are high temperature superconductors, magnetic materials, biodegradable polymers for packaging, biocompliant solids for surgical implants, etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 1 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'1','fillup','pa,ck,ing,ag',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150107,'chemistry','On the basis of nature of bonds, crystalline solids have ______ categories.','4','On the basis of nature of bond, crystalline solids are classified into four categories viz., molecular, ionic, metallic and covalent solids.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 1 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'1','mcq','3','4','5','6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150108,'chemistry','Which of the following interaction is found in polar molecular solids?','Dipole-dipole','Polar Molecular Solids: The molecules of substances like HCl, SO₂, etc. are formed by polar covalent bonds. The molecules in such solids are held together by relatively stronger dipole-dipole interactions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 1 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'3','mcq','Covalent','Ionic','Dipole-dipole','Van der waal','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150109,'chemistry','What are the factors that determine the most stable state of a substance under given temperature and pressure?','Thermal energy','Under a given set of conditions of temperature and pressure, the net effect of two opposing factors-intermolecular forces and thermal energy determine the most stable state of a substance.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 2; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Intramolecular forces','Thermal energy','Intramolecular forces','Kinetic energy','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150110,'chemistry','Among the following which are the properties of solids?\n \n a. Definite mass, volume and shape.\n \n b. Intermolecular distances are short.\n \n c. Weak intermolecular forces.\n \n d. Compressible.\n \n e. Rigid.','a,b and e','The following are the characteristic properties of the solid state:\n(i) They have definite mass, volume and shape.\n(ii) Intermolecular distances are short.\n(iii) Intermolecular forces are strong.\n(iv) Their constituent particles (atoms, molecules or ions) have fixed\npositions and can only oscillate about their mean positions.\n(v) They are incompressible and rigid.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 2; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b,d and e','a,b and c','a,b and e','b,c,d and e','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150111,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n \n Assertion: At sufficiently low temperature, substances exist in solid states.\n \n Reasons: The thermal energy is low and intermolecular force bring them so close that they occupy a fixed position. \n \n In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','At sufficiently low temperatures, substances exist in solid-state because the thermal energy is low and intermolecular forces bring them so close that they occupy fixed positions but can still oscillate about their mean position. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 2; Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150112,'chemistry','Which of the following has long-range order in the arrangement of constituent particles?','Sodium chloride','Crystalline solids have long-range order while amorphous solids have short-range order. Sodium chloride is an example of crystalline solid.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 2; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'1','mcq','Glass','Rubber','Sodium chloride','Quartz glass','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/150112q.png'),
(150113,'chemistry','How many of the statements are incorrect?\n \n a. The term amorphous comes from a Latin word.\n \n b. Amorphous solids is similar to the structure of the liquids.\n \n c. The regular pattern is not observed in amorphous solids up to small distance only.','Two','The term amorphous comes from a Greek word. Amorphous solids are similar to the structure of the liquids hence they are called super cooled or pseudo solids. The regular pattern is observed in amorphous solids up to small distance only. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 2; Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'3','mcq','One','Two','Three','Zero','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/150113q.png'),
(150114,'chemistry','Which of the following is pseudo solids?','Quartz glass','Amorphous solids are also called as pseudo solids and super cooled liquids. For example glass, plastic and rubber.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 3 Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Sodium chloride','Aluminium','Quartz','Quartz glass','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/150114q.png'),
(150115,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n \n Assertion: Some glass objects from an ancient civilisation are found to be milky in appearance.\n \n Reasons: Amorphous solids may become crystalline in some temperature. \n \n In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Amorphous solids are extremely viscous liquids they may become crystalline at some temperature. Some glass objects from an ancient civilisation are found to be milky in appearance, they become crystalline at some temperature.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 3 Paragraph No. 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150116,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n \n Assertion: Amorphous solids are isotropic.\n \n Reason: Amorphous solids have no long-range order and over distance arrangement of particles is equivalent in all directions.\n \n In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Amorphous solids have no long-range order and over distance arrangement of particles is equivalent in all directions.They are isotropic and their physical properties are same in all directions.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 3 Paragraph No. 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and the reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true. Reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150117,'chemistry','In which of the following solids, electrical resistance has different values along different directions?','Sodium chloride','Sodium chloride is an example of crystalline solid. The properties of the crystalline solid such as electrical resistance has different values along different directions because of their anisotropic nature. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 3 Paragraph No.3 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'3','mcq','Plastic','Quartz glass','Rubber','Sodium chloride','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110720,'biology','Symbiotic association of a fungus with a root system is called','Mycorrhiza','. A mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association of a fungus with a root system\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 186','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Mycorrhiza','Rhizopus','Basophyta','Dermatophytes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150118,'chemistry','Which of the following are properties of glass quartz?\n \n a. Melt at a sharp and characteristic shape.\n \n b. No definite enthalpy of fusion.\n \n c. Isotropic in nature.\n \n d. Long-range order.\n \n e. Supercooled liquid.','b,c and e','Amorphous solids have no long-range order and over distance arrangement of particles is equivalent in all directions.They are isotropic and their physical properties are same in all directions. They are also called psuedo solids or supercooled solids.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 3 Paragraph No.3 ) table 1.1','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Amorphous and crystalline solids',NULL,'2','mcq','a and d','a, c and  d','b,c and e','a,b,d and e','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150119,'biology','Which of the following property is related to meristems','Unlimited growth throughout life','Plant growth is unique because plants retain the capacity for unlimited growth throughout their life. This ability of the plants is due to the presence of meristems at certain locations in their body. The cells of such meristems have the capacity to divide and self-perpetuate','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Introduction and Growth',NULL,'1','mcq','Lacks self-perpetuate property','Unlimited growth throughout life','Producing growth hormone','Limited Division capacity','NEW','11-240','11-167',NULL),
(150120,'biology','Choose the incorrect option','Growth is expressed as increase in number of cells not size','In the former, growth is expressed as increase in cell number; the latter expresses growth as increase in size of the cell','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Introduction and Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Growth is expressed as increase in number of cells not size','Increase in protoplasm is growth at cellular level','Increase in girth of organs is secondary growth','Parameters of growth measurement are weight, length, area etc..','NEW','11-241','11-168',NULL),
(150121,'biology','Callus is','Mass of undifferentiated cell','F. Skoog and his co-workers observed that from the internodal segments of tobacco stems the callus (a mass of undifferentiated cells) proliferated only if, in addition to auxins the nutrients medium was supplemented with one of the following: extracts of vascular tissues, yeast extract, coconut milk or DNA','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Introduction and Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Differentiated cell','Undifferentiated cell','Mass of undifferentiated cell','Mass of differentiated cell','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(150122,'biology','Movement of auxin is largely','Basipetal','The movement of auxin hormone is towards the base.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Introduction and Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Centripetal','Lateral','Acropetal','Basipetal','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150301,'chemistry','Which of the following are conductors of electricity in solid as well as in molten state?','Ag','Metallic solids are conductors of electricity in solid as well as in molten state. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 6 Table 1.6)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'1','mcq','HCl','ZnS','CaF₂','Ag','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150303,'chemistry','Let "x" be the distance between two carbon atoms in the same layer of graphite and "y" be the distance between 2 carbon atoms of diamond. Which of the following is correct conclusion?','X>Y','Both graphite and diamond have carbon atoms that are covalently bonded. Each carbon atom in a diamond is joined by covalent bonds to four other carbon atoms. Each carbon atom in graphite has a covalent bond with three other carbon atoms. Carbon atoms are arranged in different layers. Carbon atoms are arranged in different layers. The distance between the adjacent carbons in graphite is less than that of diamond. The diamond carbon are in tetrahedral shape throughout the crystal while graphite have their carbons in hexagonal shape. This explains why the carbon-carbon bond in graphite is less than diamond\'s. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 6 Fig 1.3 81.4) diagram(ne1501)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'3','mcq','X>Y','X=Y','X<Y','Incomparable','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150304,'chemistry','In graphite, each carbon atom is covalently bonded to _______ atom of the same layer.','3','In graphite, each carbon atom is covalently bonded to 3 atoms of the same layer.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 6 Paragraph No.1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150305,'chemistry','The constituent particles in ionic solids are:','Both a and b','Ions are the constituent particles of ionic solids. Such solids are formed by the three dimensional arrangements of cations and anions bound by strong coulombic (electrostatic) forces.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'3','mcq','Cations','Anions','Radicals','Both a and b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150306,'chemistry','What is the nature of intermolecular bond found in ionic solids?','Electrostatic forces','Ions are the constituent particles of ionic solids. Such solids are formed by the three dimensional arrangements of cations and anions bound by strong coulombic (electrostatic) forces.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Van der waals forces','Ionic bonds','Electrostatic forces','Hydrogen bonding','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150307,'chemistry','Select the correct statement/s.\n \n \n (i) In metallic solids, metals are orderly collection of positive ions surrounded by electron pool.\n \n (ii) Each metal in metallic solid contributes only a single electron.\n \n (iii) Electrons flow in specific direction in metallic solids when electric field is applied.\n \n (iv) Thermal enegry is non uniformly distributed in metallic solids.\n \n (v) Metals are highly malleable and ductile.','i, iii and v','Metals are orderly collection of positive ions surrounded by and held together by a sea of free electrons. These electrons are mobile and are evenly spread out throughout the crystal. Each metal atom contributes one or more electrons towards this sea of mobile electrons. These free and mobile electrons are responsible for high electrical and thermal conductivity of metals. When an electric field is applied, these electrons flow through the network of positive ions. Similarly, when heat is supplied to one portion of a metal, the thermal energy is uniformly spread throughout by free electrons. Another important characteristic of metals is their lustre and colour in certain cases. This is also due to the presence of free electrons in them. Metals are highly malleable and ductile.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'3','mcq','i, ii, iii, iv and v','i, iii and v','ii, iii and iv','i, ii and v','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150308,'chemistry','Which of the following covalent solid is an electrical conductor?','Graphite','Although graphite also belongs to this class of crystals, but it is soft and is a conductor of electricity. Its exceptional properties are due to its typical structure. Carbon\natoms are arranged in different layers and each atom is covalently bonded to three of its neighbouring atoms in the same layer. The fourth valence electron of each atom is present between different layers and is free to move about. These free electrons make graphite a good conductor of electricity.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5 Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Silicon carbide','Graphite','Diamond','ZnS','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150309,'chemistry','Which of the following solids have soft physical nature?','Non polar molecular solids','They comprise either atoms, for example, argon and helium or the molecules formed by non polar covalent bonds, for example, H₂, Cl₂ and I₂. In these solids, the atoms or molecules are held by weak dispersion forces or London forces about which you have learnt in Class XI. These solids are soft and non-conductors of electricity. They have low melting points and are usually in liquid or gaseous state at room temperature and pressure.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'3','mcq','Non polar molecular solids','Polar molecular solids','Covalent solids','Ionic solids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150310,'chemistry','Solid A is a very hard electrical insulator in solid as well as in molten state and melts at extremely high temperature. What type of solid is it?','Covalent solids','A wide variety of crystalline solids of non-metals result from the formation of covalent bonds between adjacent atoms throughout the crystal. They are also called giant molecules. Covalent bonds are strong and directional in nature, therefore atoms are held very strongly at their positions. Such solids are very hard and brittle. They have extremely high melting points and may even decompose before melting. They are insulators and do not conduct electricity.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 5 Paragraph 5)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'3','mcq','Metallic solids','Molecular solids','Covalent solids','Ionic solids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150311,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n \n a. The basis or unit in the crystal lattice is an atom, molecule or ion.\n \n b. Each repeated basis or unit has the same structure and the same spatial orientation as another one in a crystal.\n \n c. The environment of each unit is the same throughout the crystal.','a and b','The basis or unit in crystal lattice is an atom, molecular ion, each repeated unit or basis has some strucuture and spatial orientation as other one in a crystal the environment of each unit is same throughout the crystal except the surface.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 6-7 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'3','mcq','b and c','a ans c','a and b','All of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150312,'chemistry','How many lattices are possible in 2-dimensions?','Five','There are five Bravais lattices in two-dimensional space. These five types, namely hexagonal, square, rectangular, rhombic, and parallelogram are shown below. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 9 fig 1.8) diagram(ne1502)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'3','mcq','Two','Five','Seven','Three','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150313,'chemistry','How many 3-dimensional lattices are possible according to bravais?','14','French mathematician, bravais showed that there are only 14 possible 3-dimensional lattices. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 10 Paragraph No:02) diagram(ne1505)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'1','mcq','2','5','7','14','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150314,'chemistry','How many crystal systems are possible in 3-dimensions?','7','There are 7 crystal system in 3-dimension, named as cubic, tetragonal,orthorhombic, hexagonal, rhombohedral, monoclinic and triclinic. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 10 Paragraph No:01) diagram(ne1505)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'1','mcq','2','5','7','14','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150315,'chemistry','Which of the following has all types of unit cells (both centred and primitive)?','Orthorhombic','In orthorhombic crystal system, all of primitive body centred, face centred and end centred unit cells are found. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 10 Table 1.3) diagram(ne1505)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Cubic','Teragonal','Orthorhombic','Triclinc','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/150315q.png'),
(150316,'chemistry','Which of the following unit cell variation is not possible in cubic crystal sysytem?','End centred','The possible varaiations in cubic crystal are primitive, body centered and face centred. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 10 Table 1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Close Packed Structures',NULL,'2','mcq','Primitive','Body centred','End centred','Face centred','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150401,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds has monoclinic crystal?','Glauber salt','Example of monoclinic crystal system-glauber salt. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 10 Table 1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','Calcite','Mohr salt','Glauber salt','Cinnabar','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150402,'chemistry','The type of bravais lattice found in an orange coloured salt of chromium that is a strong oxidising agent?','Triclinc','Example of compound having triclinic crystal system k₂Cr₂O₇ which is orange coloured and strong oxidising agent. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 10 Table 1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','Monoclinic','Triclinc','Trigonal','Tetragonal','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150403,'chemistry','The type of crystal system found in blue vitriol?','Triclinc','Example of compound having triclinic crystal system CuSO₄.5H₂O which is orange coloured and strong oxidising agent. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 10 Table 1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'2','mcq','Monoclinic','Triclinc','Trigonal','Tetragonal','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150404,'chemistry','The type of bravais lattice found in boric acid is_____?','Primitive triclinic','Primitive triclinic lattice is found in H₃BO₃. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 10 Table 1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'2','mcq','Primitive triclinic','End centred triclinic','Face-centred triclinic','Body centred clinic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150405,'chemistry','How many crystal sytem have end centred unit cells as possible variation ?','2','Only orthorhomic and monoclinic crystal systems have end centred unit cells. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 10 Table 1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150406,'chemistry','How many crystal system have body centred unit cells?','3','Cubic,tetragonal and orthorhombic crystal system have body-centred unit cells. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 10 Table 1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150407,'chemistry','In crystal structure, motif can be a _______','All of above','In crystal structure, motif is a molecule, atom or ion.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 7 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'2','mcq','Molecule','Atom','Ion','All of above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150408,'chemistry','A space lattice is _________','Pattern of points representing the locations of motifs.','A space lattice, also called a crystal lattice, is the pattern of points representing the locations of these motifs. In other words, space lattice is an abstract scaffolding for crystal structure.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 7 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'2','mcq','Pattern of points representing the locations of motifs.','Pattern of lines representing the locations of motifs.','Empty space in a crystal','Ray diagram of atoms','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150410,'chemistry','Select the correct statment/s.\n \n (i) Unit cell is the simplest part of crystal which constructs the whole crystal by repeating.\n \n (ii) Unit cells have shapes such that these fill the whole lattice without leaving space between cells.\n \n (iii) Cell with verticular sides of shortest length are chosen as unit cell.','i and ii','We need only a small part of the space lattice of a crystal to spacify crystal completely. This small part is called unit cell. One can choose unit cell in many ways. Normally that cell is chosen which has perpendicular sides of shortest length and one can construct entire crystal by translational displacement of the unit cell in three dimensions. Also, unit cells have shapes such that these fill the whole lattice without leaving space between cells.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 8 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','i and ii','Only iii','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150411,'chemistry','In the three-dimensional crystal structure, unit cell is characterised by:','Both a and b','In the three-dimensional crystal structure, unit cell is characterised by:\n(i) its dimensions along the three edges a, b and c. These edges may or may not be mutually perpendicular.\n(ii) angles between the edges, a (between b and c), b (between a and c) and r (between a and b). Thus, a unit cell is characterised by six parameters a, b, c, a , b and r.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 9 Paragraph 1) refer diagram(ne1511)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','Its dimensions along the three edges a, b and c.','Angles between the edges.','Motifs in space.','Both a and b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/150411q.png'),
(150412,'chemistry','Unit cells can be broadly divided into ______categories.','2','Unit cells can be broadly divided into two categories, primitive and centred unit cells.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 9 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/150412q.png'),
(150413,'chemistry','When constituent particles are present only on the corner positions of a unit cell, it is called ________.','Primitive unit cell','When constituent particles are present only on the corner positions of a unit cell, it is called as primitive unit cell.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 9 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','Primitive unit cell','End-centred unit cell','Body-centred unit cell','Face centred unit cell','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/150413q.png'),
(150414,'chemistry','Centred unit cell has consitutent particles present at________.','Corners and at positions other than corners','When a unit cell contains one or more constituent particles present at positions other than corners in addition to those at corners, it is called a centred unit cell.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 9 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Crystal lattices and unit',NULL,'3','mcq','Corners only','Positions other than corners','Corners and at positions other than corners','Body centre','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/150414q.png'),
(150501,'chemistry','What is no of tetrahedral voids per atom hcp?','2','If no of atoms in hcp is \'n\' then no of tetrahedral voids  \'n\' so 1 tetrahedral void per atom. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 17; Pagraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150502,'chemistry','Choose the correct statements\n \n a. Structure with pattern \'ABAB\' is called ccp or fcc.\n \n b. Magnesium and zinc crystalline as hcp.','a is incorrect but b is correct','Structure with pattern \'ABAB\' is called hcp while fcc or ccp has ABCABC Pattern\nMagnesium and zinc Crystalline as hcp (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 17; Pagraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both statements are correct','a is correct but b is incorrect','a is incorrect but b is correct','Both statements are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150503,'chemistry','Choose the correct statements\n \n a. Structure with pattern \'ABCABC\' is called hcp.\n \n b. Copper and silver crystalline as fcc or ccp.','a is incorrect but b is correct','Structure with pattern \'ABAB\' is called fcc or ccp copper and silver crystalline as fcc or ccp.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 17; Pagraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both statements are correct','a is correct but b is incorrect','a is incorrect but b is correct','Both statements are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150504,'chemistry','Select the correct statement','Both hcp and ccp have same efficiency','74% of space is fillied in both hcp and ccp and their CN is 12. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 18; Pagraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Both hcp and ccp have same efficiency','78% of space is fillied in both hcp and ccp','CN of both hcp and ccp is 6','Both 1 and 2 are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150505,'chemistry','What is the percentage of empty space in ccp?','26%','74% of space is filled in ccp therfore empty space is 100-74= 26%. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 18; Pagraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','68%','74%','32%','26%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150506,'chemistry','Choose the correct option.','In ionic solids, anions usually form close-packed structures','All octahedral or terahedral voids are not always occupied, in ionic solids, anion usually form close packed structure if cations are larger than anions would occupy the void as well.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 18; Pagraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','In ionic solids, anions usually form close-packed structures','All octahedral or tetrahedral voids are always occupied in a solid.','In ionic solids, anions always form a open structure.','Both statement 1 and 2 are correct','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150507,'chemistry','A Compound is formed by X and Y atom make ccp while Y atom occupy tetrahedral void. Formula of compound is ?','XY₂','X forms ccp so no of atoms of X=4\nY occupies TV, so no of atoms of y=8\nThe formula of compound x₄y₈=XY₂ \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 18; Example 1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','XY','XY₂','X₂Y','XY₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150508,'chemistry','A Compound is formed by X and Y atom make ccp while y atom occupies 2/3rd of octahedral void. Formula of compound is ?','X₃Y₂','No of atoms of X=4 which forms ccp =4\nY occupies 2/3rd of no of y atoms = 2/3*4= 8/3\nFormula of compound = X₄Y₈/₃= X₂Y₈ = X₃Y₂  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 18; Example 1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','XY','X₃Y₂','X₂Y₃','X₄Y₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150509,'chemistry','Contribution of each atom located at edge centre to its unit cell?','1 / 4','Each atom located at edge centre is shared between 4 unit cells. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 19; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','1','1 / 2','1 / 4','1 / 8','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22908,'chemistry','The energy of different orbitals in Li²⁺ ion (having only one electron) depend on','n only','For H-like species energy of orbitals depend on principal quantum number (n) only.','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Numerical questions',NULL,'1','mcq','n only','n and l only','n, l, m only','n, l, m, and s','NEW','11-041','11-041',NULL),
(150510,'chemistry','What is the tetrahedral void in a ccp unit cell structure?','2 at each body diagonal','Each small cube has atoms at alternate corners. In all, each small cube has 4 atoms. When joined to each other, they make a regular tetrahedron. Thus, there is one tetrahedral void in each small cube and eight tetrahedral voids in total. Each of the eight small cubes have one void in one unit cell of ccp structure. We know that ccp structure has 4 atoms per unit cell. Thus, the number of tetrahedral voids is twice the number of atoms.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 19; Paragraph 1 fig 1(a) ) diagram(ne1506)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Body Centre','Edge centre','2 at each body diagonal','4 at each body diagonal','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150511,'chemistry','What is the location of octahedral voids in fcc lattice?','Both 1 and 2','Besides the body centre, there is one octahedral void at the centre of each of the 12 edges. It is surrounded by six atoms, four belonging to the same unit cell (2 on the corners and 2 on face centre) and two belonging to two adjacent unit cells. Since each edge of the cube is shared between four adjacent unit cells, so is the octahedral void located on it. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 19; Paragraph 2 fig 2) diagram(ne1507)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Electrtical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Body Centre','Edge centre','2 at each body diagonal','Both 1 and 2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150601,'chemistry','Choose the correct statements. \n \n a. In some packing systems there is no void. \n \n b. Packing efficiency is the percentage of unoccupied space.','Both statements are incorrect','In all packing systems, there is always some void packing efficiency in the occupied space. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 20; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> General characteristics of solids states',NULL,'3','mcq','Both statements are correct','a is correct but b is incorrect','a is incorrect but b is correct','Both statements are incorrect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150602,'chemistry','Packing effciency in hcp or ccp structure is ?','√2π/6','The packing efficiency of hexagonal close packing (hcp) and cubic close packing (ccp) is 74%, which is equal to √2π/6.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 20; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> General characteristics of solids states',NULL,'2','mcq','√2π/6','√3π/8','√2π/12','π/6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150603,'chemistry','Percentage of void in hcp structure is ____?','26%','Packing efficiency of hcp structure is 74% so void is 26 %. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 20; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> General characteristics of solids states',NULL,'2','mcq','74%','32%','26%','68%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150604,'biology','Cork, secondary cortex and secondary xylem are formed through','Redifferentiation','Cork, secondary cortex and secondary xylem all are secondary structure formed from dedifferentiated cell( from interfascicular cambium and cork cambium)by redifferentiation from interfascicular cambium and cork cambium. and are responsible for secondary growth.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Differentiation, Dedifferentiation and Redifferentiation',NULL,'2','mcq','Dedifferentiation','Redifferentiation','Differentiation','Obliteration','NEW','11-245','11-172',NULL),
(150605,'biology','Which of the following is formed after dedifferentiation?','Interfascicular vascular cambium','The cells of medullary rays, adjoining these Intrafascicular cambium become meristematic (dedifferentiation) and form the interfascicular cambium, thus a continuous ring is formed.\nCork, secondary cortex and secondary xylem all are secondary structure formed from dedifferentiated cell( from interfascicular cambium and cork cambium)by redifferentiation from interfascicular cambium and cork cambium. and are responsible for secondary growth.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Differentiation, Dedifferentiation and Redifferentiation',NULL,'2','mcq','Cork','Secondary cortex','Secondary xylem','Interfascicular vascular cambium','NEW','11-245','11-172',NULL),
(150606,'biology','Find odd one with respect to differentiation','Callus formation','Mass of undifferentiated cells.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Differentiation, Dedifferentiation and Redifferentiation',NULL,'2','mcq','Callus formation','Loss of nucleus in sieve','Death of protoplasm in tracheary elements','Lignification in vessels','NEW','11-245','11-172',NULL),
(150608,'biology','The cells pushed to the periphery of root apical meristem mature as','Epidermis','Cells positioned away from root apical meristems differentiate as root-cap cells, while those pushed to the periphery mature as epidermis.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Differentiation, Dedifferentiation and Redifferentiation',NULL,'2','mcq','Epidermis','Endodermis','Hypodermis','Ectodermis','NEW','11-245','11-172',NULL),
(150701,'chemistry','If radius of sphere is \'r\' then lengh of the body diagonal in bcc is ________?','4r','The length of body diagonal is 4r, where \'r\' is the radius of sphere as all 3 spheres along the diagonal touches each other.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 21; Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'1','mcq','2r','√3r','4r','√3r/2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150702,'chemistry','What is the packing efficiency in bcc structures?','√3π/8','The packing efficiency of body-centred cubic unit cell (BCC) is 68% which is equal to √3π/8.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 21; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'2','mcq','√2π/6','√3π/8','√2π/12','π/6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150703,'chemistry','What is the percentage of void in bcc structures?','32%','Packing efficiency of bcc structure is 68% so void is 32 %. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 20; Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'2','mcq','68%','26%','32%','30%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150704,'chemistry','What is the packing Efficiency in simple cubic lattice?','π/6','Packing efficiency in simple cubic lattice = π/6.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 22; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'2','mcq','√2π/6','√3π/8','√2π/12','π/6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150705,'chemistry','What is the percentage of void in simple cubic lattice?','48%','Packing efficiency of simple cubic lattice structure is 52% so void is 48 %, \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 22; Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'2','mcq','32%','26%','48%','52%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150706,'chemistry','Which of the following is least efficient packing structure?','bcc','Packing efficiencies of hcp=ccp=74% bcc=68%. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 20 to 21)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'1','mcq','ccp','hcp','bcc','Both ccp and hcp','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151413,'chemistry','A : All crystals of same substance possess the same elements of symmetry.\n\nR : The size of crystal of same substance may vary depending upon the conditions of crystallisation.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','All crystals of the same substance possess the same elements of symmetry, the size of the crystals may vary due to different conditions of crystallization, such as temperature, pressure, and concentration of the solution.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150707,'chemistry','For real crystals with defects, the density can be slightly different due to:\n\na. Vacancies\n\nb. Dislocations\n\nc. Grain boundaries.','a,b,c','The mass of a unit cell is equal to the product of the number of atoms in a unit cell and the mass of each atom in the unit cell.  For real crystals with defects, the density can be slightly different due to vacancies, dislocations, grain boundaries etc. The density of a Unit Cell will be D = Mass/Volume. The number of atoms will depend on the kind of cell it is. To obtain the mass of a unit cell, we multiply the number of atoms ‘n’ into the mass of each atom ‘m’. Mass of Unit Cell = m × n. The density of the unit cell is the same as the density of the substance. The density of the solid can always be determined by other methods. Out of the five parameters (d, z, M, a and NA), if any four are known, we can determine the fifth.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 22 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150708,'chemistry','An element has bcc structure with edge lenght of cell as 288 pm.Iif density of element is 7.2 g/cc how many atoms are present in 208g of the element ?','24.16 x 10²³','Volume of unit cell = (288pm)³ = (288 x 10⁻¹² m)³\nVolume of 208g of elements = mass/density= 208/7.2=2.88cm³\nNo of units cells in this volume = 28.88/(288 x 10⁻¹⁰ )³ =12.08 x 10²³\nSince each bcc unit cell has 2 atoms\nso no of atoms = 2 x 12.08x 10²³ = 2416 x10²³  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 2 Example 1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'3','mcq','12.08 x 10²³','12.08 x 10²²','24.16 x 10²³','24.16 x 10²²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150709,'chemistry','Cu crystalline in fcc with unit cell edge of 3.608 x 10⁻⁸ cm. If density of Cu is 8.92g/cm, what is the atomic mass of Cu ?','63.1','In case of fcc no of atom per unit in cell is 4 atomic mass = dNₐa³/z = 8.92 X 6.022x10²³ X (3.608 x 10⁻⁸)³/4 = 63.1 \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 2 Example 1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'3','mcq','63.01','63.1','63.2','63.03','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(150710,'chemistry','Ag form up lattice and X-ray diffracation shows its edge length of unit cell to be 408.6pm. Calculate density of this sample Ag.','10.5','Density =(Z x M) / (Nₐ x a³)\nDensity = Z x (107.9 x 10⁻³)/(408.6 x 10⁻¹² )³ x 6.022 x 10²³ = 10.5 g cm⁻³ \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 23 Exaple 1.5)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Imperfections in solids',NULL,'3','mcq','10.1','10.3','10.5','10.7','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/150710q.png'),
(150801,'chemistry','Which of the following is a compound that shows metal deficiency defect?','FeO','In Crystals of FeO some Fe⁺² cations are missing and loss of positive charge is made up by presence of required number of Fe⁺³ ions. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 26 Paragraph 4 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','KCl','NaCl','ZnO','FeO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150802,'chemistry','What is the most commonly found form of FeO?','Fe₀.₉₅O','FeO is mostly found with composition of Fe₀.₉₅O it may actually range from Fe₀.₉₃O to Fe₀.₉₆O. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 26 Paragraph 4 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe₀.₉₂O','Fe₀.₉₀O','Fe₀.₉₅O','Fe₀.₉₆O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150803,'chemistry','What percentage of Fe exists as Fe⁺³ in Fe₀.₉₄O?','12.80%','Let \'x\' be no of Fe⁺³ ions, so no of Fe⁺² ions is 94-x, Since Fe₀.₉₄ is electrically neutral so 3x + 2(94-x)-2=0 on solving x=12. So % Fe⁺³ =12.8%. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 26 Paragraph 5 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'3','mcq','12%','12.80%','94%','24%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150804,'chemistry','Point defects can be classified into how many types?','3','Point defects can be classified into three types : (i) stoichiometric defects (ii) impurity defects and (iii) non-stoichiometric defects.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 24 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'3','mcq','2','3','4','5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150805,'chemistry','When some of the lattice sites are vacant, the crystal is said to have ______.','Vacancy defect','When some of the lattice sites are vacant, the crystal is said to have vacancy defect. This results in decrease in density of the substance. This defect can also develop when a substance is heated.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 24 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Thermodynamic defects','Interstitial defect','Vacancy defect','Frenkel and Schottky defects','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150806,'chemistry','How many types of non-stoichiometric defects ?','2','A large number of nonstoichiometric inorganic solids are known which contain the constituent elements in non-stoichiometric ratio due to defects in their crystal structures. These defects are of two types: (i) metal excess defect and (ii) metal deficiency defect.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 26 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'3','mcq','2','3','4','5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150807,'chemistry','What is the range of electrical conductivities in solids ion?','10⁻²⁰ to 10⁷','Range of electrical conductivities in solid is 10⁻²⁰ to 10⁷. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 26 Paragraph 6 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁻²⁷ to 10','10⁻²⁰ to 10','10⁻²⁰ to 10⁷','10⁷ to 10²²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150808,'chemistry','Select the correct range of conductivities of conductors.','10⁴ to 10⁷ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','Solids with conductivities in range of 10⁴ to 10⁷ ohm⁻¹m⁻¹ are called conductors. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 26 Paragraph 6 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁴ to 10⁷ ohm⁻¹ cm⁻¹','10⁻⁴ to 10⁻⁷ ohm⁻¹⁴⁷ cm⁻¹⁴⁷','10⁴ to 10⁷ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','10⁵ to 10⁶ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150809,'chemistry','Insulators have electrical conductivities in the range of______.','10⁻²⁰ to 10⁻¹⁰ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','Insulators have very low conductivities, ranging between 10⁻²⁰ to 10⁻¹⁰ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 27 Paragraph 1 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Magnetic properties',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁻⁴ to 10⁻⁷ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','10⁻²⁰ to 10⁻¹⁰ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','10⁻⁴ to 10⁴ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','10⁻²⁰ to 10⁻⁵⁰ ohm⁻¹ m⁻¹','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150901,'chemistry','Which of the following is used to amplify or detect audio or radio signal?','Transistors','Transistors are made by sandwiching a layer of one type of semiconductor between two layers of the other type of semiconductor. npn and pnp type of transistors are used to detect or amplify radio or audio signals. The solar cell is an efficient photo-diode used for conversion of light energy into electrical energy.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Transistors','Diode','Solar Cell','InSb','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22909,'chemistry','The number of spherical nodes and angular nodes in 3p orbital are respectively','1,1','For 3p n = 3, l = 1 \n Number of spherical nodes = n – l – 1 \n = 3 – 1 – 1 = 1 \n Number of angular nodes = l = 1','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Numerical questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1,2','1,1','2,2','2,1','NEW','11-059','11-059',NULL),
(150902,'chemistry','Which of the following componds has very fast response?','GaAs','Germanium and silicon are group 14 elements and therefore, have a characteristic valence of four and form four bonds as in diamond. A large variety of solid state materials have been prepared by combination of groups 13 and 15 or 12 and 16 to simulate average valence of four as in Ge or Si. Typical compounds of groups 13 – 15 are InSb, AlP and GaAs. Gallium arsenide (GaAs) semiconductors have very fast response and have revolutionised the design of semiconductor devices. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','GaAs','CdS','ZnS','HgTc','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150903,'chemistry','How many of the following transition metal oxides behave like metals?\n \n TiO,CrO₂,ReO₃,Vo,VO₂,TiO₃','3','Transition metal oxides show marked differences in electrical properties. TiO, CrO₂, ReO₃ are lilke metals (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2','3','4','5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150904,'chemistry','Which of the following is like metalic Cu in conductivity and apperance?','ReO₃','ReO₃ is like metalic Cu in conductivity and appearance. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','TiO','CrO₂','ReO₃','TiO₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150905,'chemistry','How many of the following transition metal oxides behave like metals?\n \n TiO,CrO₂,ReO₃,Vo,VO₂,TiO₃,VO₃,VO₄','4','Vo,VO₂,VO₃,TiO₃ show metalic or insulating properties dependinng on temperature (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 3)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2','4','6','8','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150906,'chemistry','Which of the following describes doping?','Increasing conductivity by adding an appropriate amount of suitable impurity','The conductivity of these intrinsic semiconductors is too low to be of practical use. Their conductivity is increased by adding an appropriate amount of suitable impurity. This process is called doping. Doping can be done with an impurity which is electron rich or electron deficient as compared to the intrinsic semiconductor silicon or germanium. Such impurities introduce electronic defects in them.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 28 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Increasing conductivity by adding an appropriate amount of suitable impurity','Jumping of an electron from a neighbouring atom to fill the electron hole,','Process of inducing magnetic properties in the metals.','The phenomena of changing the spin direction of electron  to change the direction of magnetic moment.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150907,'chemistry','The origin of magnetic poperties lies in the ?','Electrons','Origin of magnetic properties lies in electrons, each electrons behave like a tiny magnetic. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Protons','Neutrons','Electrons','Nucleus','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150908,'chemistry','Magnetic movement of electrons originates from','Both a and b','Every substance has some magnetic properties associated with it. The origin of these properties lies in the electrons. Each electron in an atom behaves like a tiny magnet. Its magnetic moment originates from two types of motions: \n(i) its orbital motion around the nucleus and \n(ii) its spin around its own axis.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Its orbital motion around Nucleus','Spin around its own axis','Orbital motion inside Nucleus','Both a and b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150909,'chemistry','Each electron has a','Orbital magnetic movement','Electron being a charged particle and undergoing these motions can be considered as a small loop of current which possesses a magnetic moment. Thus, each electron has a permanent spin and an orbital magnetic moment associated with it.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Temporary spin','Orbital magnetic movement','Ferromagnetism','Ferrimagnetism','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150910,'chemistry','Bohr magneton is the unit of','Magnetic moment','Magnitude of this magnetic moment is very small and is measured in the unit called Bohr magneton, μB. It is equal to 9.27x10⁻²⁴Am².\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Magnetic field','Magnetic moment','Magnetic flux','Magnetic inductance','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(150911,'chemistry','1 Bohr magneton is equal to ____','9.27x10⁻²⁴Am²','Magnitude of this magnetic moment is very small and is measured in the unit called Bohr magneton, μB. It is equal to 9.27x10⁻²⁴Am².\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 29 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','8.27x10⁻²⁸ Am²','9.27x10⁻²⁸Am²','8.27x10⁻²⁴Am²','9.27x10⁻²⁴Am²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151001,'chemistry','Which of the following is an antiferromagnetic material?','MnO','Antiferromagnetism: Substances like MnO showing antiferromagnetism have domain structure similar to ferromagnetic substance, but their domains are oppositely oriented and cancel out each other\'s magnetic moment.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Number of atoms in a unit cell',NULL,'3','mcq','MnO','MnO₂','Mn₃O₄','Fe₃O₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151002,'chemistry','How many of following materials have zero magnetic moment? H₂O, CrO₂,MnO,Fe₃O₄,O₂','2','Diamagnetic H₂O and antiferromagnetic (MnO) have zero magnetic moment.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 1)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Number of atoms in a unit cell',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151003,'chemistry','Which of the following substance are ferrimagnetic?','Fe₃O₄','Ferrimagnetism is observed when the magnetic moments of the domains in the substance are aligned in parallel and anti-parallel directions in unequal numbers. They are weakly attracted by magnetic field as compared to ferromagnetic substances. Fe₃O₄ (magnetite) and ferrites like MgFe₂O₄ and ZnFe₂O₄ are examples of such substances. These substances also lose ferrimagnetism on heating and become paramagnetic.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Number of atoms in a unit cell',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe₃O₄','Fe₂O₃','Fe','Fe₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151414,'biology','Point out an intrinsic factor involved in plant growth out of the following options','Plant growth regulators','Many of the extrinsic factors such as temperature and light, control plant growth and development via PGR. Some of such events could be: vernalisation, flowering, dormancy, seed germination, plant movements, etc.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Abscisic acid',NULL,'3','mcq','Plant growth regulators','Light','Temperature','Water','NEW','11-251','11-178',NULL),
(151415,'chemistry','A : AgBr shows both Schottky and Frenkel defect.\n\nR : AgBr is a crystalline solid.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','AgBr shows both Schottky and Frenkel defects it is a crystalline solid.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151004,'chemistry','The following arrangement of magnetic moments is observed in______. diagram(n1501)','CrO₂','A few substances like iron, cobalt, nickel, gadolinium and CrO₂ are attracted very strongly by a magnetic field. Such substances are called ferromagnetic substances. Besides strong attractions, these substances can be permanently magnetised. In solid state, the metal ions of ferromagnetic substances are grouped together into small regions called domains. Thus, each domain acts as a tiny magnet. In an unmagnetised piece of a ferromagnetic substance the domains are randomly oriented and their magnetic moments get cancelled. When the substance is placed in a magnetic field all the domains get oriented in the direction of the magnetic field and a strong magnetic effect is produced. This ordering of domains persist even when the magnetic field is removed and the ferromagnetic substance becomes a permanent magnet.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 2)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Number of atoms in a unit cell',NULL,'3','mcq','CrO₂','MnO','Fe₃O₄','Mn₂O₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/151004q.png'),
(151005,'chemistry','The following arrangement of magnetic moments is observed in______. diagram(n1502)','MnO','Antiferromagnetism: Substances like MnO showing antiferromagnetism have domain structure similar to ferromagnetic substance, but their domains are oppositely oriented and cancel out each other\'s magnetic moment.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Number of atoms in a unit cell',NULL,'3','mcq','CrO₂','MnO','Fe₃O₄','Mn₂O₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/151005q.png'),
(151006,'chemistry','The following alignment of magnetic moments is observed in? diagram(n1503)','Fe₃O₄','Ferrimagnetism is observed when the magnetic moments of the domains in the substance are aligned in parallel and anti-parallel directions in unequal numbers. They are weakly attracted by magnetic field as compared to ferromagnetic substances. Fe₃O₄ (magnetite) and ferrites like MgFe₂O₄ and ZnFe₂O₄ are examples of such substances. These substances also lose ferrimagnetism on heating and become paramagnetic.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 30 Paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Number of atoms in a unit cell',NULL,'3','mcq','CrO₂','MnO','Fe₃O₄','Mn₂O₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/151006q.png'),
(151007,'chemistry','Which defect arises when a solid is heated?','Vacancy defect','When some of the lattice sites are vacant, the crystal is said to have vacancy defect. This results in decrease in density of the substance. This defect can also develop when a substance is heated.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 31 Fig1.18 )','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Number of atoms in a unit cell',NULL,'1','mcq','Interstitial defect','Vacancy defect','Impurity defect','Metal deficiency defect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/151007q.png'),
(151008,'chemistry','The number of carbon atoms per unit cell of diamond unit cell is_______. (NEET 2013)','8','Carbon forming ccp (fcc) and also occupying half of tetrahedral voids. Total no. of carbon atoms per unit cell = 8 x 1/8 + 6 x 1/2 + 4 = 8. Diamond is like ZnS (Zinc blende)\n(NEET 2013)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Number of atoms in a unit cell',NULL,'3','mcq','6','4','2','8','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151009,'chemistry','A compound is formed by cation C and anion A. The anions form hexagonal close packed (hcp) lattice and the cations occupy 75% of octahedral\n \n voids. The formula of the compound is','C₃A₄','Number of atoms per unit cell in hcp = 6\nNumber of octahedral void in hcp = 6\nNumber of anions per unit cell = 6\nNumber of octahedral voids occupied by cations = 6 x 75/100 = 9/2\nFormula of compound = C₉/₂A₆ = C₃A₄ \n(NEET 2019)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Number of atoms in a unit cell',NULL,'3','mcq','C₄A₃','C₂A₃','C₃A₂','C₃A₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151010,'chemistry','In calcium fluoride, having the fluorite structure, the coordination numbers for calcium ion (Ca²⁺) and fluoride ion (F⁻) are','8 and 4','In fluorite structure, Ca²⁺ ions are in the face centred cubic arrangement. Each Ca²⁺ is connected to 4 F⁻ ions below it and to another set of 4 F⁻ ions above it i.e. Ca²⁺ has a coordination number of 8 and each F⁻ ion has a coordination number 4.\n(NEET-II 2016)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Number of atoms in a unit cell',NULL,'3','mcq','4 and 2','6 and 6','8 and 4','4 and 8','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151011,'biology','Auxins induce parthenocarpy in _______','Tomatoes','Auxins also induce parthenocarpy, e.g., in tomatoes. They are widely used as herbicides. 2, 4-D, widely used to kill dicotyledonous weeds, does not affect mature monocotyledonous plants. It is used to prepare weed-free lawns by gardeners. Auxin also controls xylem differentiation and helps in cell division','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Auxins',NULL,'3','mcq','Tomatoes','Carrot','Beet root','Peas','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151012,'biology','In most higher plants, the growing apical bud inhibits the growth of the lateral (axillary) buds, a phenomenon called --------- dominance.','Apical','In most higher plants, the growing apical bud inhibits the growth of the lateral (axillary) buds, a phenomenon called apical dominance.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Auxins',NULL,'3','mcq','Root','Auxin','Apical','Meristem','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151013,'biology','Which type of phytohormone is made up of indole compounds?','Auxin','The term ‘auxin’ is applied to the indole-3-acetic acid (IAA), and to other natural and synthetic compounds having certain growth regulating properties.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Auxins',NULL,'3','mcq','Zeatin','Auxin','ABA','Gibberellins','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151014,'biology','Which phytohormone promotes falling of old leaves whereas inhibits falling of young leaves?','Auxin','Auxin hormone prevent falling of young leaves but promotes falling of old leaves.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Auxins',NULL,'3','mcq','ABA','Auxin','Cytokinin','GA','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151015,'biology','Which of the following auxin is widely used as Dicot weedicide and does not affect Mature Monocot plants?','2, 4-D','Auxins also induce parthenocarpy, e.g., in tomatoes. They are widely used as herbicides. 2, 4-D, widely used to kill dicotyledonous weeds, does not affect mature monocotyledonous plants. It is used to prepare weed-free lawns by gardeners. Auxin also controls xylem differentiation and helps in cell division.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Auxins',NULL,'3','mcq','2, 4-D','IAA','IBA','NAA','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151016,'biology','Removal of shoot tips in tea plants is done to discard the effects of','Auxin','To get rid of apical dominance we do trimming of tea plant.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Auxins',NULL,'3','mcq','GA₃','Cytokinin','ABA','Auxin','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151017,'biology','Auxins helps to initiate rooting in stem cuttings, an application widely used for','Plant propagation','They help to initiate rooting in stem cuttings, an application widely used for plant propagation. NCERT Class 11th pg., 248','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Auxins',NULL,'3','mcq','Plant propagation','Growth','Movement','Vegetation','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
