(151027,'biology','Match the following-','Indole compounds-IAA, Adenine derivatives-N6 Furfurylaminopurine, Terpenes-Gibberellic acid','They could be indole compounds (indole-3-acetic acid, IAA); adenine derivatives (N6-furfurylamino purine, kinetin), derivatives of carotenoids (abscisic acid, ABA); terpenes (gibberellic acid, GA₃) or gases (ethylene, C2H4).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Auxins',NULL,'2','match','Indole compounds,IAA','Adenine derivatives,N6 Furfurylaminopurine','Terpenes,Gibberellic acid',NULL,'NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151028,'biology','Find the incorrect statement regarding Phytohormones','Ethephon comes under auxin group of PGRs','The most widely used compound as source of ethylene is ethephon. Ethephon in an aqueous solution is readily absorbed and transported within the plant and releases ethylene slowly','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Auxins',NULL,'2','mcq','ABA inhibit seed germination','Ethephon comes under auxin group of PGRs','Xylem differentiation is promoted by auxin','Ethophen comes under Inhibitor group of PGRs and is the source of Ethylene','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(151101,'chemistry','The ionic radii of A⁺ and B⁻ ions are 0.98 × 10⁻¹⁰ m and 1.81 × 10⁻¹⁰m. The coordination number of each ion in AB is','6','Radius ratio, r+/r- = 0.98 x10⁻¹⁰ / 1.81 x 10⁻¹⁰ = 0.541\nIt lies in the range of 0.414 to 0.732 hence, coordination number of each ion will be 6 as the compound will have NaCl type structure i.e., octahedral arrangement. \n(NEET-I 2016)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','8','2','6','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151102,'chemistry','Lithium has a bcc structure. Its density is 530 kg m⁻³ and its atomic mass is 6.94 g mol⁻¹. Calculate the edge length of a unit cell of lithium metal. (Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)','352 pm','For bcc, Z =2 , ρ = 530 kg m⁻³, at. mass of Li = 6.94 g mol⁻¹, Nₐ = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹\nρ = 530 kg m⁻³ = 530 x 1000 g/  1x (100)³ cm³ = 0.53 g cm⁻³\nρ = Z x at. mass/ Nₐ x a³\na³ = Z x at. mass/ Nₐ x ρ = 2 x 6.94 / 6.02 × 10²³ x 0.53 = 43.5 x 10⁻²⁴\na = 352 x 10⁻¹⁰ = 325 pm.  \n(NEET-I 2016)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','527 pm','264 pm','154 pm','352 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151103,'chemistry','A metal has a fcc lattice. The edge length of the unit cell is 404 pm. The density of the metal is 2.72 g cm⁻³. The molar mass of the metal is_______. (Nₐ Avogadro’s constant = 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)','27 g mol⁻¹','d = Z x M / a³ x Nₐ ( Z = 4 for fcc)\nM = d x Nₐ x a³ / Z =  2.72 x 6.02 × 10²³ x (404 x 10⁻¹⁰)³ / 4\nM = 27 g mol⁻¹ \n(NEET 2013)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','27 g mol⁻¹','20 g mol⁻¹','40 g mol⁻¹','30 g mol⁻¹','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151416,'chemistry','A : Number of carbon atoms per unit cell in diamond is 8.\n\nR : The structure of diamond is similar to ZnS.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Diamond shows C.N. = 8 it has ZnS type structure.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151104,'chemistry','The correct statement regarding defects in crystalline solids is','Frenkel defect is a dislocation defect','Frenkel defect is a dislocation defect as smaller ions (usually cations) are dislocated from normal sites to interstitial sites. Frenkel defect is shown by compounds having large difference in the size of cations and anions hence, alkali metal halides do not show Frenkel defect. Also, Schottky defect decreases the density of crystal while Frenkel defect has no effect on the density of crystal.\n(NEET 2015)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','Frenkel defects decrease the density of crystalline solids','Frenkel defect is a dislocation defect','Frenkel defect is found in halides of alkaline metals','Schottky defects have no effect on the density of crystalline solids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/151104q.png'),
(151105,'chemistry','Frenkel defect is a dislocation defect as smaller ions (usually cations) are dislocated from normal sites to interstitial sites. Frenkel defect is shown by compounds having large difference in the size of cations and anions hence, alkali metal halides do not show Frenkel defect. Also, Schottky defect decreases the density of crystal while Frenkel defect has no effect on the density of crystal.','liquid crystals','Liquid crystals on heating first become turbid and then on further heating turbidity completely disappears.\n(1993)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','allotropic crystals','liquid crystals','isomeric crystals','isomorphous crystals','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151106,'chemistry','Glass is a','supercooled liquid','Glass is a supercooled liquid which forms a non-crystalline solid without a regular lattice.\n(1991)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','liquid','solid','supercooled liquid','transparent organic polymer.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151107,'chemistry','Most crystals show good cleavage because their atoms, ions or molecules are','arranged in planes.','Crystals show good cleavage because their constituent particles are arranged in planes.\n(1991)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','weakly bonded together','strongly bonded together','spherically symmetrical','arranged in planes.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151108,'chemistry','The ability of a substance to assume two or more crystalline structures is called','polymorphism','The phenomenon of existence of a substance in two or more crystalline structures is called polymorphism.\n(1990)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','isomerism','polymorphism','isomorphism','amorphism','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151109,'chemistry','Cation and anion combines in a crystal to form following type of compound.','ionic','The electrostatic force of attraction which exists between oppositely charged ions is called as ionic bond.\n(2000)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','ionic','metallic','covalent','dipole-dipole','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151110,'chemistry','For two ionic solids CaO and KI, identify the wrong statement among the following.','KI is soluble in benzene.','KI is an ionic compound while benzene is not.\n(1997)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','CaO has high melting point.','Lattice energy of CaO is much larger than that of KI.','KI has high melting point.','KI is soluble in benzene.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151111,'chemistry','For orthorhombic system axial ratios are a ≠ b ≠ c and the axial angles are','α = β = γ = 90º','For orthorhombic system, α = β = γ = 90º\n(1991)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','α = β = γ ≠ 90º','α = β = γ = 90º','α = γ = 90º, β ≠ 90º','α ≠ β ≠ γ ≠ 90º','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151112,'chemistry','In a face-centred cubic lattice, a unit cell is shared equally by how many unit cells?','6','Here given unit cell is shared equally by six faces in the fcc which is shared equally by six different unit cells. \n(2005) refer diagram(ne1508)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','2','4','6','8','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151113,'chemistry','When Zn converts from melted state to its solid state, it has hcp structure, then find the number of nearest atoms.','12','hcp is a closed packed arrangement in which the unit cell is hexagonal and coordination number is 12.\n(2001)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','6','8','12','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151114,'chemistry','The fcc crystal contains how many atoms in each unit cell?','4','The contribution of eight atoms of face-centred cubic unit cell = 8 x 1/8 = 1 atom. There is one atom at each of six faces, which is shared by 2 unit cells each. The contribution of 6 face-centred atoms = 6 x 1/2 = 3. Therefore, n =1 +3 = 4\n(1996)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','6','8','4','5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151115,'chemistry','The number of atoms contained in a fcc unit cell of a monatomic substance is','4','fcc crystal contains = 8 x 1/8 + 6 x 1/2 = 4 atoms in a unit cell.  \n(1993)\nrefer diagram(ne1509)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','4','6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151116,'chemistry','The number of octahedral void(s) per atom present in a cubic close-packed structure is','1','Number of octahedral voids is same as number of atoms.\n(2012)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Packing Efficiency',NULL,'3','mcq','1','3','2','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151117,'biology','Which statement is incorrect','Spraying juvenile conifers with GA delays maturity period','Spraying juvenile conifers with GAs hastens the maturity period, thus leading to early seed production.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Gibberellins',NULL,'2','mcq','Auxin promote flowering in pineapple','Spraying juvenile conifers with GA delays maturity period','Ethylene induces flowering in mango','ABA act as an antagonist to GA','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(151201,'chemistry','Structure of a mixed oxide is cubic close packed (ccp). The cubic unit cell of mixed oxide is composed of oxide ions. One fourth of the tetrahedral voids are occupied by divalent metal A and the octahedral voids are occupied by a monovalent metal B. The formula of the oxide is:','AB₂O₂','Number of atoms in ccp = 4 = O²⁻\nNumber of tetrahedral voids = 2 × N = 2 × 4 = 8\nNumber of A²⁺ ions = 8 × 1/4 = 2\nNumber of octahedral voids = Number of B⁺ ions = N = 4\nRatio, O²⁻ : A²⁺ : B⁺ = 4 : 2 : 4 = 2 : 1 : 2\nFormula of oxide = AB₂O₂\n(Mains 2012)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ABO₂','A₂BO₂','A₂B₃O₄','AB₂O₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151202,'chemistry','A solid compound XY has NaCl structure. If the radius of the cation is 100 pm, the radius of the anion (Y⁻) will be','241.5 pm','For NaCl, r+/r- = 0.414\nGiven : radius of cation = 100 pm\n100/r- = 0.414\nr- = 100/ 0.414 = 241.5 pm\n(Mains 2011)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','275.1 pm','322.5 pm','241.5 pm','165.7 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151203,'chemistry','A compound formed by elements X and Y crystallises in a cubic structure in which the X atoms are at the corners of a cube and the Y atoms are at the facecentres. The formula of the compound is','XY₃','In a unit cell, X atoms at the corners = 1/8 x 8 = 1\nY atoms at the face centres = 1/2 x 6 = 3\nRatio of X and Y = 1 : 3. Hence formula is XY₃.\n(2004)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','XY₃','X₃Y','XY','XY₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151204,'chemistry','In cube of any crystal A-atom placed at every corners and B-atom placed at every centre of face. The formula of compound is','AB₃','‘A’ atoms are at ‘8’ corners of the cube. Thus, no. of ‘A’ atoms per unit cell =  1/8 x 8 = 1\n‘B’ atoms are at the face centre of six faces. Thus, no. of ‘B’ atoms per unit cell = 1/2 x 6 = 3\nThe formula is AB₃.\n(2000)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','AB','AB₃','A₂B₂','A₂B₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151205,'chemistry','In crystals of which one of the following ionic compounds would you expect maximum distance between centres of cations and anions?','CsI','As Cs⁺ ion has larger size than Li⁺ and I⁻ has larger size than F⁻, so maximum distance between centres of cations and anions is in CsI.\n(1998)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CsI','CsF','LiF','LiI','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151206,'chemistry','The second order Bragg diffraction of X-rays with λ = 1.00 Å from a set of parallel planes in a metal occurs at an angle 60°. The distance between the scattering planes in the crystal is','1.15 Å','According to Bragg’s equation, nλ = 2d sin θ\nAs, n = 2, λ = 1.00Å, θ = 60°, d = ?\n2dsinθ = nλ\n2dsin60° = 2 × 1 Å\n2d x √3/2 = 2 \nd = 2/√3 = 1.15 Å\n(1998)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2.00 Å','1.00 Å','0.575 Å','1.15 Å','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151207,'chemistry','The intermetallic compound LiAg crystallizes in cubic lattice in which both lithium and silver have coordination number of eight. The crystal class is','body-centred cube','A body-centred cubic unit cell consists of 8 atoms at the corners and one atom at the centre.\n(1997)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','face-centred cube','simple cube','body-centred cube','none of these.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151208,'chemistry','In the fluorite structure, the coordination number of Ca²⁺ ion is','8','In fluorite (CaF₂) structure, C.N. of Ca²⁺ = 8, C.N. of F⁻ = 4.\n(1993)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4','6','8','3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151209,'chemistry','An element has a body centered cubic (bcc) structure with a cell edge of 288 pm. The atomic radius is','√3/4 x 288 pm','For bcc structure, r = √3/4a, where a is the unit cell edge length and r is the radius of the sphere (atom). \nr = √3/4 x 288 pm\n(NEET 2020)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','√3/4 x 288 pm','√2/4 x 288 pm','4/√3 x 288 pm','4/√2 x 288 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151210,'chemistry','The vacant space in bcc lattice unit cell is','32%','Packing efficiency of bcc lattice = 68%. Hence, empty space = 32%.\n(2015, 2008)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','48%','23%','32%','26%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151211,'chemistry','If a is the length of the side of a cube, the distance between the body-centred atom and one corner atom in the cube will be','√3/2a','The distance between the body-centred atom and one corner atom is √3/2a i.e. half of the body diagonal.\n(2014)\n refer diagram(ne1510)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2/√3a','4/√3a','√3/4a','√3/2a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151212,'chemistry','AB crystallizes in a body-centred cubic lattice with edge length ‘a’ equal to 387 pm. The distance between two oppositely charged ions in the lattice is','335 pm','For a bcc lattice, 2(r+ + r-) = 3 a, where r+ = radius of cation, r- = radius of anion, a = edge length.\n(r+ + r-) = √3x 387 / 2 = 335.15 pm ≈ 335 pm\n(2010)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','335 pm','250 pm','200 pm','300 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151213,'chemistry','Lithium metal crystallises in a body-centred cubic crystal. If the length of the side of the unit cell of lithium is 351 pm, the atomic radius of lithium will be','151.8 pm','Since Li crystallises in body-centred cubic crystal, atomic radius,\nr = √3/4a (a = edge length)\nr = √3/4 x 351 =151 8. pm (Given: = 351 pm) \n(2009)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','151.8 pm','75.5 pm','300.5 pm','240.8 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151214,'chemistry','Copper crystallises in a face-centred cubic lattice with a unit cell length of 361 pm. What is the radius of copper atom in pm?','128','Since Cu crystallises in a face-centred cubic lattice, Atomic radius, r = a/2√2 (a = edge length = 361 pm)\nr = 361 / 2√2 = 127.6 ≈ 128 pm\n(2009)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','157','181','108','128','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151215,'chemistry','A metal crystallises with a face-centred cubic lattice. The edge of the unit cell is 408 pm. The diameter of the metal atom is','288 pm','For a face-centred cubic (fcc) structure, \nr = a/2√2, a = 208 pm,  r = 408/2√2 = 144 pm\nDiameter = 2r = 2 × 144 = 288 pm\n(2012)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','288 pm','408 pm','144 pm','204 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151216,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not correct?','The fraction of the total volume occupied by the atoms in a primitive cell is 0.48.','Packing fraction for a cubic unit cell is given by\nf = Z x 4/3πr³/ a³\nwhere a = edge length, r = radius of cation and anion. Efficiency of packing in simple cubic or primitive cell = π/6 = 0.52 i.e. 52% of unit cell is occupied by atoms and 48% is empty.    \n(2008)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Previous year questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The number of carbon atoms in a unit cell of diamond is 8.','The number of Bravais lattices in which a crystal can be categorized is 14.','The fraction of the total volume occupied by the atoms in a primitive cell is 0.48.','Molecular solids are generally volatile.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151301,'chemistry','Copper crystallises in fcc with a unit cell length of  361 pm. What is the radius of copper atom?\n [AIEEE-2009]','127 pm','r = a/2√2 = 361/2√2 = 127 pm','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','127 pm','157 pm','181 pm','108 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151302,'chemistry','The edge length of a face centered cubic cell of an  ionic substance is 508 pm. If the radius of the  cation is 110 pm, the radius of the anion is \n [AIEEE-2010]','144 pm','In fcc crystal, R + r= a/2\n→ 110+r = 508/2 \nr = 144 pm','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','144 pm','288 pm','398 pm','618 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151303,'chemistry','Percentages of free space in cubic close packed  structure and in body centered packed structure  are respectively [AIEEE-2010]','26% and 32%','Packing fractions of fcc and bcc lattices are 74%\nand 68%\n→ Vacancies are 26% and 32%','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','48% and 26%','30% and 26%','26% and 32%','32% and 48%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151304,'chemistry','Copper crystallises in fcc lattice with a unit cell  edge of 361 pm. The radius of copper atom is\n [AIEEE-2011]','128 pm','r = a/2√2 = 361/2√2 = 128 pm','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','157 pm','181 pm','108 pm','128 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151305,'chemistry','Lithium forms body centered cubic structure. The  length of the side of its unit cell is 351 pm. Atomic  radius of the lithium will be [AIEEE-2012]','150 pm','r = a/2√2 = 351/2√2 = 150 pm','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','300 pm','240 pm','150 pm','75 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151306,'chemistry','Which of the following exists as covalent crystals  in the solid state ? [JEE (Main)-2013]','Silicon','Silicon is a covalent crystal in which silicon atoms  are covalently bonded to give three dimensional  network','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Iodine','Silicon','Sulphur','Phosphorus','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151307,'chemistry','Experimentally it was found that a metal oxide has  formula M₀.₉₈O. Metal M, is present as M²⁺ and  M³⁺ in its oxide. Fraction of the metal which exists  as M³⁺ would be [JEE (Main)-2013]','4.08%','Let the number of moles of M³⁺ be x and that of M²⁺  is (0.98 – x)\nNumber of moles of oxide ions = 0.98 - x + 3x/2 = 1\nor, x = 0.04\nPercentage of M³⁺ = 4.08%','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','7.01%','4.08%','6.05%','5.08%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151308,'chemistry','CsCl crystallises in body centred cubic lattice. If ‘a’  is its edge length then which of the following  expressions is correct? [JEE (Main)-2014]','r(cl⁻) + r(cs⁺) = √3a/2','2r(cl⁻) + 2r(cs⁺) = √3a\n\nr(cl⁻) + r(cs⁺) = √3a/2 . Refer diagram(ne1513)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','r(cl⁻) + r(cs⁺) = 3a','r(cl⁻) + r(cs⁺) = 3a/2','r(cl⁻) + r(cs⁺) = √3a/2','r(cl⁻) + r(cs⁺) = √3a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151309,'chemistry','Sodium metal crystallizes in a body centred cubic  lattice with a unit cell edge of 4.29 Å. The radius  of sodium atom is approximately\n [JEE (Main)-2015]','1.86 Å','Edge length of BCC is 4.29 Å.\nIn BCC,\n\nedge length = 4r/√3 \n\n4.29 = 4r/√3\n\nr = 4.29 × √3/4 ≈1.86 Å','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','1.86 Å','3.22 Å','5.72 Å','0.93 Å','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151310,'chemistry','A metal crystallises in a face centred cubic  structure. If the edge length of its unit cell is \'a\',  the closest approach between two atoms in  metallic crystal will be [JEE (Main)-2017]','a/√2','In FCC, one of the face is like\nBy △ ABC,\n\n2a² = 16r²\n\na² = 1/8r² .\nRefer diagram(ne1514)','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','√2a','a/√2','2a','2√2 a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151311,'chemistry','Which type of ‘defect’ has the presence of cations  in the interstitial sites? [JEE (Main)-2018]','Frenkel defect','In Frenkel defect, cation is dislocated from its normal\n lattice site to an interstitial site','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Schottky defect','Vacancy defect','Frenkel defect','Metal deficiency defect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151312,'chemistry','The one that is extensively used as a piezoelectric  material is [JEE (Main)-2019]','Quartz','Quartz exhibits piezoelectricity. It is fact based.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Tridymite','Mica','Quartz','Amorphous silica','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151313,'chemistry','At 100°C, copper (Cu) has FCC unit cell structure  with cell edge length of x Å. What is the  approximate density of Cu (in g /cm³ ) at this  temperature? [Atomic Mass of Cu = 63.55 u]  [JEE (Main)-2019]','422\n/x³','Density = Z(M₀)/Nₐa³\n\nZ = 4(FCC)\nM₀ = 63.5 g\nNₐ = 6 × 10²³\na = x × 10⁻⁸\n\nd = 4 × 63.2 / 6 × 10³ × x³ × 10⁻²⁴ = 422/x³ g/cm³','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','422\n/x³','205/x³','105/x³','211/x³','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151314,'chemistry','Which primitive unit cell has unequal edge lengths  (a ≠ b ≠ c) and all axial angle different from 90°?  [JEE (Main)-2019]','Triclinic','For triclinic Crystal\na ≠ b ≠ c  Axial distance\nα ≠ β ≠γ ≠ 90 Axial angle','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Hexagonal','Monoclinic','Triclinic','Tetragonal','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151315,'chemistry','A compound of formula A₂B₃ has the hcp lattice.  Which atom forms the hcp lattice and what fraction  of tetrahedral voids is occupied by the other  atoms? [JEE (Main)-2019]','HCP lattice - B, \n1\n/3 Tetrahedral voids - A','A₂B₃ can be written as :  A₄B₆\nH.C.P has Six atom so \'B\' form\nH.C.P lattice and A is present in void.\nTotal tetrahedral void = 12\nFraction of tetrahedral void occupied by\nA = 4/12 = 1/3','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','HCP lattice - B, \n1\n/3 Tetrahedral voids - A','HCP lattice - A, \n2\n/3 Tetrahedral voids - B','HCP lattice - A, \n1\n/3 Tetrahedral voids - B','HCP lattice - B, \n2\n/3 Tetrahedral voids - A','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151316,'chemistry','A solid having density of 9 × 10³ kg /m³ forms  face centred cubic crystals of edge length  200√2 pm. What is the molar mass of the solid?  [Avogadro constant = 6 × 10²³/ mol , π= 3]  [JEE (Main)-2019]','0.0305 kg /mol','d = Z M / N a³ \n\n9 × 10³ = 4 M / ( 200 × √2 ×10⁻¹²)³  × 6 × 10²³ \n\nM = 0.03 kg/mole','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','0.0305 kg /mol','0.4320 kg /mol','0.0432 kg /mol','0.0216 kg /mol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151317,'chemistry','The radius of the largest sphere which fits properly  at the centre of the edge of a body centred cubic  unit cell is (Edge length is represented by ‘a’)  [JEE (Main)-2019]','0.067 a','For BCC\n\n√3 a = 4R\n\nR = √3a / 4 \n\n→ Empty space at edge = a – 2R  \n                                    = a -√3a/2    \n                                    = diameter of sphere. \n  \n→ r(sphere)      =      (a -√3a/2) / 2 = 0.067a','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','0.027 a','0.047 a','0.067 a','0.134 a','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151401,'chemistry','A : In NaCl structure, the interionic distance is a/2. (a = Unit cell edge length)\n\nR : NaCl forms face centered cubic unit cell.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','In NaCl\n\nr⁺ + r⁻ = a/2 → True\n\nF.C.C. → true\nBut (R) is not explanation of Assertion.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151402,'chemistry','A : The co-ordination number of CaF₂ is 8 : 4.\nR : Ca²⁺ ions occupy ccp lattice while F⁻ ions occupy 50% octahedral voids and 50% tetrahedral voids.','A is true but R is false.','(i) C.N. of CaF2 is 8 : 4 → true\n(ii) Ca²⁺ occupy ccp lattice while F⁻ ions occupy all tetrahedral holes not 50% O.V. and 50% T.V. i.e., reason is  wrong.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151403,'chemistry','A : The number of spheres are equal to the number of octahedral void as well as tetrahedral void.\n\nR : Octahedral void and tetrahedral void has equal size.','Both A and R are false.','(i) Number of spheres = number of O.V.\nNumber of T.V. = 2 × number of sphere\n(ii) Octahedral and tetrahedral voids does not have equal size. So, (A) & (R) are wrong','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> Assertion and Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(22911,'chemistry','How much distance an electron revolving in 3 rd orbit of He⁺ ion will travel in one second','1.458 x 10⁶ m','V = 2.188 × 10⁶(Z/m) m/s\n \n = 2.188 × 10⁶(2/3) \n \n =1.458 x 10⁶ m/s\n \n 1.458 x 10⁶ m electron revolving in 3rd orbit of He⁺ ion one second','Physical chemistry >> Structure of atom >> Numerical questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1.458 x 10⁶ m','3.28 x 10⁶ m','4.862 x 10⁵ m','2.917 x 10⁶ m','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151506,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct?\n \n (a) Ferrimagnetic substances lose ferrimagnetism on heating and become paramagnetic\n \n (b) Ferrimagnetic’substances do not lose ferrimagnetism on heating and remain ferrimagnetic\n \n (c) Antiferromagnetic substances have domain structure similar to ferromagnetic substances and their magnetic moments are not cancelled by each other\n \n (d) In ferromagnetic substances, all the domains get oriented in the direction of magnetic field and remain as such even after removing magnetic field.','a,d','Ferrimagnetic substances lose ferrimagnetism on heating and become paramagnetic. In ferromagnetic substance, domains are aligned in parallel and antiparallel direction in unequal numbers.\n\nIn ferromagnetic substances, all the domains get oriented in the direction of magnetic field and remain as such even after removing magnetic field.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','a,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151507,'chemistry','Amorphous solids can also be called\n \n (a) pseudo solids \n \n (b) true solids\n \n (c) super cooled liquids \n \n (d) super cooled solids','a,c','Amorphous solid has short range order which has a tendency to flow very slowly. Hence, it is also known as pseudo solids or super cooled liquids. Glass panes fixed to windows or doors of old buildings are invariably observed to be thicker at bottom than at the top. These are examples of amorphous solids.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','a,c','b,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151508,'chemistry','An excess of potassium ions makes KCl crystals appear violet or Lilac in colour since\n \n (a) some of the anionic sites are occupied by an unpaired electron\n \n (b) some of the anionic sites are occupied by a pair of electrons\n \n (c) there are vacancies at some’anionic sites\n \n (d) F-centres are created which impart colour to the crystals','a,d','When KC1 is heated in vapour of K, some of the Cl” leave their lattice site and create anion vacancies. This chloride ion wants to combine with K vapour to form potassium chloride. For doing so K atom loses electrons form K ions. This released electron diffuses into the crystal to get entrapped in the anion vacancy called F-centre. When visible light falls on the crystal, this entrapped electron gains energy, goes to the higher level when it comes back to the ground state, energy is released in the form of light.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,d','a,b','b,c','b,c','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151509,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true about semiconductors?\n \n (a) Silicon doped with an electron rich impurity is a p-type semiconductor\n \n (b) Silicon doped with an electron rich impurity is an n-type semiconductor\n \n (c) Delocalised electrons increase the conductivity of doped silicon\n \n (d) An electron vacancy increases the conductivity of type semiconductor','b,c','Silicon (valence electron – 4) doped with electron rich impurity is an n-type semiconductor due to extra electron and the delocalised electrons increase the conductivity of doped silicon.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','b,c','a,b','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151510,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true about metals?\n \n (a) Valence band overlap with conduction band\n \n (b) The gap between valence band and conduction band is negligible\n \n (c) The gap between valence band and conduction band cannot be determined\n \n (d) Valence band may remain partially filled.','a,b,d','In metal, valence band overlap with conduction band. The gap between valence band and conduction band is negligible and valence band may remain partially filled.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b,c','a,b,d','b,c,d','a,c,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151511,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are not true?\n \n (a) Vacancy defect results in a decrease in the density of the substance\n \n (b) Interstitial defects results in an increase in the density of the substance\n \n (c) Impurity defect has no effect on the density of the substance\n \n (d) Frenkel defect results in an increase in the density of the substance','c,d','Impurity defect changes the density of substance as impurity has different than the ion present on perfect crystal e.g., When SrCl2 is added to the NaCl crystal, it causes impurity defect, (d) Frenkel defect results neither decrease nor increase in density of substance.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151512,'chemistry','Which of the following defects is also known as dislocation defect?','Frenkel defect','In Frenkel defect, some cations occupy interstitial site and hence it is also called dislocation defect.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Frenkel defect','Schottky defect','Non-stoichiometric defect','Simple interstitial defect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151513,'chemistry','A ferromagnetic substance becomes a permanent magnet when it is placed in a magnetic field because','All the domains get oriented in the direction of magnetic field','Ferromagnetic solids can be permanently magnetised and then all the domains get oriented in the direction of applied magnetic field.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','All the domains get oriented in the direction of magnetic field','All the domains get oriented ill the direction opposite to the direction of magnetic field','Domains get oriented randomly','Domains are not affected by magnetic field','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151514,'chemistry','Which of the following is not true about the ionic solids?','The fraction of octahedral or tetrahedral voids occupied depends upon the radii of the ions occupying the voids','The fraction of octahedral or tetrahedral voids occupied depends upon the radii of the ions present at the lattice points. As we know the radii of octahedral or tetrahedral void is related to radii of atoms (r) as Radius of octahedral void (R0) = 0.414 r .\n\nRadius of tetrahedral void (R1 ) = 0.225 r  Where, r = radius of bigger atom involved.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Bigger ions form the close packed structure','Smaller ions occupy either the tetrahedral or the octahedral voids depending upon their size','Occupation of all the voids is not necessary','The fraction of octahedral or tetrahedral voids occupied depends upon the radii of the ions occupying the voids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151515,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is not true?','Ferromagnetic substances cannot be magnetized permanently','Ferromagnetic species are strongly attracted in the magnetic field and can be permanently magnetised.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Paramagnetic substances are weakly attracted by magnetic field','Ferromagnetic substances cannot be magnetized permanently','The domains in antiferromagnetic substances are oppositely oriented with respect to each other','Pairing of electrons cancel their magnetic moment in the diamagnetic substances','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151516,'chemistry','Which kind of defect is introduced by doping?','Electronic defect','When electron rich or electron deficient impurity is added to a perfect crystal, it introduces electronic defect in them.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Dislocation defect','Schottky defect','Frenkel defect','Electronic defect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151517,'biology','Match the following','Cytokinin-Adventitious shoot formation,Florigen-Hormone responsible for flowering,ABA-Plant stress hormone,Ethylene-Flowering and synchronised fruit set in pineapples','Cytokinin-Adventitious shoot formation,\nFlorigen-Hormone responsible for flowering\nABA-Plant stress hormone\nEthylene-Flowering and synchronised fruit set in pineapples','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Photoperiodism',NULL,'1','match','Cytokinin,Adventitious shoot formation','Florigen,Hormone responsible for flowering','ABA,Plant stress hormone','Ethylene,Flowering and synchronised fruit set in pineapples','OLD','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(151518,'chemistry','The percentage of empty space in a body centered cubic arrangement is','74','Packing efficiency for bcc arrangement is 68% which represents total filled space in the unit cell. Hence, empty space in a body centered arrangement is 100 – 68 = 32%.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','74','68','32','26','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151601,'chemistry','If NaCI is doped with 10⁻³ mol % SrCl₂, what is the concentration of cation vacancies?','6.022 × 10¹⁸/ mol','Let moles of NaCI = 100\n∴ Moles of SrCl₂ doped = 10⁻³\nEach Sr²⁺ will replace two Na⁺ ions. To maintain electrical neutrality it occupies one position and thus creates one cation vacancy.\n∴ Moles of cation vacancy in 100 moles NaCI = 10⁻³\nMoles of cation vacancy in one mole NaCI = 10⁻³× 10⁻² = 10⁻⁵\n∴ Number of cation vacancies = 10⁻⁵ × 6.022 × 10²³ = 6.022 × 10¹⁸/ mol','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','6.022 × 10¹⁸/ mol','6.022 × 10²³/ mol','6.022 × 10⁸/ mol','6.022 × 10⁴⁸/ mol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151602,'chemistry','Aluminium crystallises in a cubic close packed structure. Its metallic radius is 125 pm.What is the length of the side of the unit cell?','354 pm','For an fee unit cell, r = a/2√2, (given, r = 125 pm)\na = 2√2 r = 2√2 × 125 pm\n= 353.55 pm\n≅354 pm','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','155 pm','354 pm','254 pm','34 km','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151603,'chemistry','Aluminium crystallises in a cubic close packed structure. Its metallic radius is 125 pm.How many unit cells are there in 1.00 cm³ of aluminium?','2.7 × 10²²','For an fee unit cell, r = a/2√2, (given, r = 125 pm)\na = 2√2 r = 2√2 × 125 pm\n= 353.55 pm\n≅354 pm\n\nVolume of one unit cell = a³ = (354 pm)³\n= 4.4 × 10⁷pm³\n= 4.4 × 10⁷ × 10⁻³⁰cm³\n= 4.4 × 10⁻²³ cm³\nTherefore, number of unit cells in 1.00 cm³ = 1 cm³/4.4 × 10⁻²³ cm³ = 2.7 × 10²²','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','2.7 × 10²²','5.4 × 10²²','54','1','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151604,'chemistry','Gold (atomic radius = 0.144 nm) crystallises in a face centred unit cell. What is the length of the side of the unit cell ?','0.407 nm','For a face centred cubic unit cell (fcc)\nEdge length (a) = 2√2 = 2 x 1.4142 x 0.144 mm = 0.407 nm.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','0.407 nm','0.47 nm','0.307 nm','0.37 nm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151605,'chemistry','Ferric oxide crystallises in a hexagonal dose- packed array of oxide ions with two out of every three octahedral holes occupied by ferric ions. What is the formula of the ferric oxide.','Fe₂O₃','Suppose the number of oxide ions (O²⁻) in the packing = 90\n∴ Number of octahedral voids = 90\nAs 2/3rd of the octahedral voids are occupied by ferric ions, therefore, number of ferric ions 2 present = (2/3)× 90 = 60\n∴ Ratio of Fe³⁺ : O²⁻ = 60 : 90 = 2 : 3\nHence, the formula of ferric oxide is Fe₂O₃.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','Fe₂O₃','FeO₃','Fe₂O₂','FeO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151606,'chemistry','Analysis shows that nickel oxide has the formula Ni₀.₉₈ O₁.₀₀. What fractions of nickel exist as Ni²⁺ and Ni³⁺ ions?','96:4','98 Ni-atoms are associated with 100 O – atoms. Out of 98 Ni-atoms, suppose Ni present as Ni²⁺ = x\nThen Ni present as Ni³⁺ = 98 – x\nTotal charge on x Ni²⁺ and (98 – x) Ni3+ should\nbe equal to charge on 100 O²⁻ ions.\nHence, x × 2 + (98 – x) × 3 = 100 × 2 or 2x + 294 – 3x = 200 or x = 94\n∴ Fraction of Ni present as Ni²⁺ =  94/98 × 100 = 96%\n\nFraction of Ni present as Ni³⁺ = 4/98 × 100 = 4%','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','96:4','98:2','2:3','1:1','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151607,'chemistry','Copper crystallises into a fee lattice with edge length 3.61 x 10⁻⁸ cm. Calculate it\'s density ?','8.96 g/cm³','d = Z × M / a³ N = 4 × 63.5/ (3.61 x 10⁻⁸)³ × 6.023 × 10²³ = 8.96 g/cm³','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','8.96 g/cm³','896 g/cm³','0.896 g/cm³','89.6 g/cm³','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151608,'chemistry','Niobium crystallises in a body centred cubic structure. If density is 8.55 g /cm³ , calculate atomic radius of niobium, using its atomic mass 93u.','330 pm','d = Z × M / a³ N(A)\na³ = M × Z / N(A) d × 10⁻³⁰ = 93 × 2/ 8.55 × 6.023 × 10²³ ×10⁻³⁰ = 3.61 × 10⁷ \na = (3.61 × 10⁷)⅓ = 330.4 pm','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','330 pm','115 pm','660 pm','110 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151609,'chemistry','A cubic solid is made of two elements P and Q. Atoms Q are at the corners of the cube and P at the body centre. What is the formula of the compound ?','PQ','Contribution by atoms Q present at the eight corners of the cube = (1/8)×8 = 1\n\nContribution by atom P present at the body centre = 1\nThus, P and Q are present in the ratio 1:1.\n∴ Formula of the compound is PQ.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','PQ','P₂Q','PQ₂','PQ₃','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151610,'chemistry','Silver crystallises in fcc lattice. If edge length of the cell is 4.07 x 10⁻⁸ cm and density is 10.5 g/cm³, calculate the atomic mass of silver.','107.09 g/mol','d = Z × M / a³ N(A)\nM = d × a³ × N(A)/ Z\n\nM = 10.5 × 4.07 x 10⁻⁸ × 6.023 × 10²³/ 4  = 107.09 g/mol','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','107.09 g/mol','207.09 g/mol','97.09 g/mol','10709 g/mol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151611,'chemistry','A compound forms hexagonal close-packed. structure. What is the total number of voids in 0.5 mol of it?','9.033 x 10²³','No. of atoms in close packings 0.5 mol =0.5 x 6.022 x 10²³ =3.011 x 10²³\nNo. of octahedral voids = No. of atoms in packing =3.011 x 10²³\nNo. of tetrahedral voids = 2 x No. of atoms in packing = 2 x 3.011 x 10²³ = 6.022 x 10²³\nTotal no. of voids = 3.011 x 10²³ + 6.022 x 10²³\n= 9.033 x 10²³','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'2','mcq','9.033 x 10²³','9033','9.033 x 10²³','9.033 x 10²⁰','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151612,'chemistry','How many portions of an atom located at corner ?','1/8','An atom at the comer is shared by eight adjacent unit cells. Hence, portion of the atom at the comer that belongs to one unit cell=1/8.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','1/8','1/4','1','1/6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151613,'chemistry','How many portions of an atom located at body centre of a cubic unit cell is part of its neighbouring unit cell ?','1','An atom at the body centre is not shared by any other unit cell. Hence, it belongs fully to unit cell.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','1/2','1/4','1/8','1','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151614,'chemistry','A compound is formed by two elements M and N. The element N forms ccp and atoms of the element M occupy 1/3 of the tetrahedral voids. What is the formula of the compound?','N₃M₂','Let us suppose that,\nthe no. of atoms of N present in ccp = x\nSince 1/3rd of the tetrahedral voids are occupied by the atoms of M, therefore,the no. of tetrahedral voids occupied = 2x/3\nThe ratio of atoms of N and M in the compound = x : 2x/3 or 3 : 2\n∴ The formula of the compound = N₃M₂ or M₂N₃','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','N₃M₂','N₃M','NM₂','NM','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151615,'chemistry','Which of the following lattices has the highest packing efficiency (i) simple cubic (ii) body-centered cubic and (iii) hexagonal close-packed lattice?','only iii','Packing efficiency of: Simple cubic = 52.4% bcc = 68% hcp = 74%,hcp lattice has the highest packing efficiency.','Physical chemistry >> Solid state >> NCERT Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','i & ii','only iii','ii','i,ii & iii','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151616,'biology','Vernalisation stimulates flowering in','Carrot','Another example of vernalisation is seen in biennial plants. Biennials are monocarpic plants that normally flower and die in the second season. Sugarbeet, cabbages, carrots are some of the common biennials','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Vernalisation',NULL,'2','mcq','Zaminkand','Turmeric','Carrot','Ginger','OLD','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(151701,'biology','Effect of inhibitory substances can be removed by subjecting the seeds to','Chilling conditions','Effect of inhibitory substances can be removed by subjecting the seeds to chilling conditions','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Seed Dormancy',NULL,'2','mcq','Heating conditions','Chilling conditions','Boiling conditions','Watering conditions','OLD','11-253',NULL,NULL),
(151702,'biology','Which among the following is not a reason for seed dormancy','Soft seed coats and mature embryo','Impermeable and hard seed coat; presence of chemical inhibitors such as abscisic acids, phenolic acids, para-ascorbic acid; and immature embryos are some of the reasons which causes seed dormancy. This dormancy however can be overcome through natural means and various other man-made measures','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Seed Dormancy',NULL,'2','mcq','Impermeable and hard seed coat','Presence of chemical inhibitors such as abscisic acids, phenolic acids, para-ascorbic acid','Immature embryos','Soft seed coats and mature embryo','OLD','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(151703,'biology','The seed coat barrier in some seeds can be broken by ____________ using knives, sandpaper, etc. or vigorous shaking','Mechanical abrasions','Seed coat barrier in some seeds can be broken by mechanical abrasions using knives, sandpaper, etc. or vigorous shaking. In nature, these abrasions are caused by microbial action, and passage through digestive tract of animals','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Seed Dormancy',NULL,'2','mcq','Mechanical abrasions','Chemical abrasions','Physiological abrasions','Biological Abrasions','OLD','11-253',NULL,NULL),
(151704,'biology','Which certain chemicals application helps in removal of effect of inhibitory substances','Gibberellic acid and Nitrates','Effect of inhibitory substances can be removed by subjecting the seeds to chilling conditions or by application of certain chemicals like gibberellic acid and nitrates','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Seed Dormancy',NULL,'2','mcq','Gibberellic acid','Sulphates','Auxins','Gibberellic acid and Nitrates','OLD','11-253',NULL,NULL),
(151705,'biology','Changing the environmental conditions, such as light and temperature are other methods to overcome seed dormancy','TRUE','Changing the environmental conditions, such as light and temperature are other methods to overcome seed dormancy','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Seed Dormancy',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'OLD','11-253',NULL,NULL),
(151706,'biology','Which of the following is not natural mean to remove seed coats.','Vigorous shaking','This dormancy however can be overcome through natural means and various other man-made measures. For example, the seed coat barrier in some seeds can be broken by mechanical abrasions using knives, sandpaper, etc. or vigorous shaking','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Seed Dormancy',NULL,'1','mcq','Microbial action','Passage through digestive tract','Vigorous shaking','All','OLD','11-253',NULL,NULL),
(151801,'biology','The process of growth is maximum during\n[NEET 2020]','Log phase','In most systems, the initial growth is slow i.e., lag phase, and it increases rapidly thereafter – at an exponential rate i.e., log or exponential phase','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Log phase','Lag phase','Senescence','Dormancy','NEW','11-243','11-170',NULL),
(151802,'biology','Typical growth curve in plants is\n[2015 Cancelled]','Sigmoid','Geometric growth cannot be sustained for long in natural condition. Limited nutrient availability slows down the growth. It leads to a stationary phase or even a decline. Plotting the growth against time, gives a typical sigmoid or S-curve. Sigmoid curve of growth is typical of most organisms in their natural environment including plants','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Stair-steps shaped','Parabolic','Sigmoid','Linear','NEW','11-243','11-170',NULL),
(151804,'biology','Name the plant growth regulator which upon spraying on sugarcane crop, increases the length of stem, thus increasing the yield of sugarcane crop\n[NEET 2020]','Gibberellin','Spraying sugarcane crop with gibberellins increases the length of the stem and increasing the yield by as much as 20 tonnes per acre','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Cytokinin','Gibberellin','Ethylene','Abscisic acid','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(151805,'biology','Which of the following is not an inhibitory substance governing seed dormancy?\n[NEET 2020]','Gibberellic acid','Gibberellic acid involved in growth promoting activities, such as cell division, cell enlargement, pattern formation, tropic growth, flowering, fruiting and seed formation and is called plant growth promoters. It breaks seed dormancy which is antagonistic to abscisic acid','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Gibberellic acid','Abscisic acid','Phenolic acid','Para-ascorbic acid','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(151806,'biology','It takes very long time for pineapple plants to produce flowers. Which combination of hormones can be applied to artificially induce flowering in pineapple plants throughout the year to increase yield?\n[NEET 2019]','Auxin and Ethylene','Synthetic auxins like NAA and 2, 4-D are often employed for inducing flowering in pineapple. Ethylene Also stimulates flowering in pineapple and related plants.Hence, both auxin and ethylene can be applied artificially to induce flowering in pineapple plants throughout the year to increase yield','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Cytokinin and Abscisic acid','Auxin and Ethylene','Gibberellin and Cytokinin','Gibberellin and Abscisic acid','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(151807,'biology','Fruit and leaf drop at early stages can be prevented by the application of\n[NEET 2017]','Auxins','In low concentrations, auxins such as 2,4-D(2,4- Dichlorophenoxy acetic acid) is useful in preventing pre-harvest fruit drop and leaf drop','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Ethylene','Auxins','Gibberellic acid','Cytokinins','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151808,'biology','You are given a tissue with its potential for differentiation in an artificial culture. Which of the following pairs of hormones would you add to the medium to secure shoots as well as roots?\n[NEET-II 2016]','Auxin and cytokinin','Cytokinin and auxin are two plant hormones that are supplied to the tissue culture medium in definite proportions. They bring about cell division and differentiation of callus. A low auxin to cytokinin ratio promotes shoot formation whereas a high auxin to cytokinin ratio promotes rooting of callus','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','IAA and gibberellin','Auxin and cytokinin','Auxin and abscisic acid','Gibberellin and abscisic acid','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(151811,'biology','What causes a green plant exposed to the light, on only one side, to bend towards the source of light as it grows?\n[2015 Cancelled]','Auxin accumulates on the shaded side, stimulating greater cell elongation there','Auxins induce cell elongation. In a differentially illuminated plant, they accumulate in the shaded part, causing elongation of the cells in the shaded part. This unequal elongation on two sides causes the plant to curve or bend towards the light source, i.e., phototropic curvature','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Light stimulates plant cells on the lighted side to grow faster.','Auxin accumulates on the shaded side, stimulating greater cell elongation there','Green plants need light to perform photosynthesis','Green plants seek light because they are phototropic','NEW','11-247','11-174',NULL),
(151812,'biology','Dr. F. Went noted that if coleoptile tips were removed and placed on agar for one hour, the agar would produce a bending when placed on one side of freshly-cut coleoptile stumps. Of what significance is this experiment?\n[2014]','It demonstrated polar movement of auxins','Auxin was isolated by F.W. Went from tips of coleoptiles of oat seedlings,Dr. F. Went noted that if coleoptile tips were removed and placed on agar for one hour, the agar would produce a bending when placed on one side of freshly-cut coleoptile stumps. It made possible the isolation and exact identification of auxin .Darwin and his son observed that the coleoptiles of canary grass responded to unilateral illumination by growing towards the light source. This is called phototropism. They performed series of experiments which concluded that the tip of coleoptile was the site of transmittable influence that caused the bending of the entire coleoptile. F.W. Went isolated auxin from the tips of coleoptiles of oat seedlings and concluded that phototropism of oat seedlings occur. They also concluded that phototropism in the tips of coleoptiles is due to the polar movement of auxin in the stem.\nSo, the correct answer is \'it demonstrated polar movements of auxins\'','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','It made possible the isolation and exact identification of auxin','It is the basis for quantitative determination of small amounts of growth-promoting substances','It supports the hypothesis that IAA is auxin','It demonstrated polar movement of auxins','NEW','11-247','11-174',NULL),
(151813,'biology','Which one of the following growth regulators is known as ‘stress hormone’?\n[2014]','Abscisic acid','Abscisic acid prepares plants to cope with stress conditions like drought, etc. by inducing stomatal closure and other reactions. Hence, it is named stress hormone','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Abscisic acid','Ethylene','GA₃','Indole acetic acid','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(151814,'biology','During seed germination, its stored food is mobilized by\n[NEET 2013]','Gibberellin','Gibberellins are plant growth substances chemically related to terpenes and occurring naturally in plants and fungi. They promote elongation of stems, e.g., bolting in cabbage plants and the mobilization of food reserves in germinating seeds. They are influential in inducing flowering and fruit development','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','ABA','Gibberellin','Ethylene','Cytokinin','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(151815,'biology','The pineapple which under natural condition is difficult to blossom has been made to produce fruits throughout the year by application of\n[Karnataka NEET 2013]','NAA, 2, 4-D','NAA (α-Naphthalene acetic acid) and 2,4 D (2, 4 dichlorophenoxy acetic acid) are synthetic auxins. In pineapple, application of auxin promotes flowering','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','NAA, 2, 4-D','Phenylacetic acid','Cytokinin','IAA, IBA','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151816,'biology','Through their effects on plant growth regulators, what do the temperature and light control in the plants?\n[Mains 2012]','Flowering','Light and temperature may affect flowering in plants in various ways. The effect of photoperiods or daily duration of light hours (and dark periods) on flowering is called photoperiodism.Hence, flowering in certain plants not only depends on a combination of light and dark exposures but also on their relative durations. Similarly, there are plants for which flowering is either quantitatively or qualitatively dependent on exposure to low temperature. This phenomenon is termed as vernalisation. It prevents precocious reproductive development late in the growing season and enables the plant to have sufficient time to reach maturity. Thus, many plants require a warm-cold-warm sequence to complete their annual cycle','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Apical dominance','Flowering','Closure of stomata','Fruit elongation','NEW','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(151817,'biology','Which one of the following generally acts as an antagonist to gibberellins?\n[Mains 2012]','ABA','Abscisic acid or ABA is an antagonist to gibberellins','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Zeatin','Ethylene','ABA','IAA','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(151818,'biology','Phototropic curvature is the result of uneven distribution of\n[2010]','Auxin','Phototropic curvature is the result of uneven distribution of auxin. Charles Darwin and his son Francis Darwin observed that the coleoptiles of canary grass responded to unilateral illumination by growing towards the light source (phototropism). After a series of experiments, it was concluded that the tip of coleoptile contains auxin that caused the bending of the entire coleoptile in relation to the direction of light','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Gibberellin','Phytochrome','Cytokinins','Auxin','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151819,'biology','One of the commonly used plant growth hormone in tea plantations is\n[Mains 2010]','Indole-3-acetic acid','Indole-3-acetic acid (auxin) is a phytohormone which is generally produced by the growing apices of the stems and roots, from where they migrate to the regions of their action. It is observed that the growing apical bud inhibits the growth of the lateral (axillary) bud (apical dominance). Since apical meristem is the site of auxin synthesis, it is the physiological effect of the auxin which results in the phenomenon of apical dominance','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Ethylene','Abscisic acid','Zeatin','Indole-3-acetic acid','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151820,'biology','Root development is promoted by','Ethylene','Ethylene promotes root growth and root hair formation. In low concentration, ethylene is used for initiation of roots and also of lateral roots','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Abscisic acid','Auxin','Gibberellin','Ethylene','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(151821,'biology','Which one of the following pairs, is not correctly matched?\n[2007]','Gibberellic acid - Leaf fall','Gibberellic acid is a simple weakly acidic plant growth hormone which promotes cell elongation of both leaves and stems in general and internodal length of genetically dwarf plants in particular. It is in general a growth promoting hormone and does not inhibit growth. So, leaf abscission is not associated with gibberellic acid but with abscisic acid','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Gibberellic acid - Leaf fall','Cytokinin - Cell division','IAA - Cell wall elongation','Abscisic acid - Stomatal closing','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(151823,'biology','How does pruning help in making the hedge dense?','It frees axillary buds from apical dominance','Pruning is the process of cutting shoot tips to promote lateral growth of branches. Removal of shoot tips involves removal of apical buds. In the shoot tips auxins are produced. Auxins are growth promoting phytohormones. They cause apical dominance by promoting the growth of apical buds and suppressing the growth of axillary buds. So when the auxins produced in the shoot tips are removed by decapitation it results in lateral growth and plants thus show bushy appearance. This is because of a relatively high concentration of auxin in the apical bud than in the lateral buds.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','It releases wound hormones','It induces the differentiation of new shoots from the rootstock.','It frees axillary buds from apical dominance','The apical shoot grows faster after pruning','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(151824,'biology','Differentiation of shoot is controlled by\n[2003]','High cytokinin : auxin ratio','The major physiological function of cytokinin is to enhance cell division. In tissue culture the undifferentiated mass of cells formed in the culture tubes is called callus. The callus may remain in the undifferentiated condition or differentiation may take place in this. If it is differentiated, then root and shoot may be formed.Skoog and Miller had reported that cytokinins induce shoot formation and auxins induce root formation','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','High auxin : cytokinin ratio','High cytokinin : auxin ratio','High gibberellin : auxin ratio','High gibberellin : cytokinin ratio','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(151825,'biology','Dwarfness can be controlled by treating the plant with\n[2002, 1992]','Gibberellic acid','Gibberellins helps in the reversal of dwarfism in many genetically dwarf plants. External supply of gibberellic acid causes rapid elongation, e.g., rosette plants of sugarbeet when treated with GA₃ undergo marked longitudinal growth of axis','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Cytokinin','Gibberellic acid','Auxin','Anti-gibberellin','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(151901,'biology','Highest auxin concentration occurs\n[1990]','In growing tips','Auxins are well known to promote elongation of stem and coleoptile. However when exogenous auxin is given to intact plants this is not observed because the required amount of auxin is already present in plants. When the apex of shoot is removed, then the exogenous application of auxin promotes growth, this clearly indicates that growing apex, having meristematic cells, is the site where endogenous auxins are present in sufficient amount, once the apex is removed the source of auxin is also removed','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','In growing tips','In leaves','At base of plant organs','In xylem and phloem','NEW','11-247','11-174',NULL),
(151902,'biology','What is the site of perception of photoperiodism necessary for induction of flowering in plants?\n[NEET 2019]','Leaves','Shoot apices modify themselves into flower apices prior to flowering, but they themselves cannot perceive photoperiods. Leaves are the site of perception of light/dark photoperiods. It has been hypothesised that leaves contain a hormonal substance responsible for flowering, which migrates from leaves to shoot apices for inducing flowering only when when the plants are exposed to the necessary inductive photoperiod.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Leaves','Lateral buds','Pulvinus','Shoot apex','NEW','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(151903,'biology','The response of different organisms to the environmental rhythms of light and darkness is called\n[1998]','Photoperiodism','Photoperiodism is the response of plants to relative length of light and darkness.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Vernalization','Photoperiodism','Phototaxis','Phototropism','NEW','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(151904,'biology','Leaf fall can be prevented with the help of\n[1989]','Cytokinins','It helps to produce new leaves, chloroplasts in leaves, lateral shoot growth and adventitious shoot formation.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Abscisic acid','Auxins','Florigen','Cytokinins','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(151905,'biology','Which is employed for artificial ripening of banana fruits?\n[1992]','Ethylene','Ethylene is a hormone which is used for ripening of fruit. In case of unripe banana, it can be made to ripe before proper time if they are kept in ethylene atmosphere. Uncontrolled application of this gas may spoil the fruits. Ethylene is produced in mature but unripe fruits and then it initiates a chain of reactions that finally lead to ripening','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Auxin','Coumarin','Ethylene','Cytokinin','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(151906,'biology','Cut or excised leaves remain green for long if induced to root or dipped in\n[1988]','Cytokinins','Cytokinins are plant growth hormones which are basic in nature. Cytokinins induce formation of new leaves, chloroplasts in leaves, which in turn keeps the leaves green for a longer duration of time. Cytokinins applied to marketed vegetables can keep them fresh for several days. Shelf life of cut shoots and flowers is prolonged by employing the hormones','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Gibberellins','Cytokinins','Auxins','Ethylene','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(151907,'biology','Vernalisation stimulates flowering in\n[Mains 2012]','Carrot','Carrot is a biennial plant that requires stimulus of low temperature for flowering. It remains vegetative during the warm season and bears flowers and fruits only during winter. It can be made to flower in one growing season by providing low temperature treatment to young plants or seedlings which is referred to as vernalization. Hence, vernalization stimulates flowering in carrot.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Jimikand','Turmeric','Carrot','Ginger','NEW','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(151908,'biology','Treatment of seeds at low temperature under moist\n[2006]','Vernalization','Vernalization is the method of promoting flowering by exposing young plants to cold treatment, e.g., winter varieties of wheat, barley, oats and rye are given artificial cold treatment and planted in spring in areas of very harsh winters such as Soviet Union to promote flowering in them. In most cereals optimum temperature for vernalization is 4°C. Receptive organ to chilling is the apical meristem. Chelation is the process by which certain micronutrients are treated to keep them readily available to a plant once they are introduced into the soil','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Stratification','Scarification','Vernalization','Chelation','NEW','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(151909,'biology','Flowering dependent on cold treatment is\n[1992]','Vernalisation','In several plants, particularly biennials and perennials, light does not seem to be the only factor controlling the process of flowering. Temperature, particularly the low temperature treatment induce flowering. Vernalisation means ability of low temperature to convert winter cereal into spring cereal as a result of satisfaction of their low temperature requirement','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Cryotherapy','Cryogenics','Cryoscopy','Vernalisation','NEW','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(151910,'biology','An enzyme that can stimulate germination of barley seeds is\n[2006]','α-amylase','The process by which the dormant embryo of seed resumes active growth and forms a seedling is known as germination. The initial step in germination process is the uptake of water and rehydration of the seed tissues by the process of imbibition. The first visible sign of germination is the emergence of the radicle from the seed. But this event is preceded by a series of biochemical reactions. Imbibition of water causes the embryo within seed to produce α- and β-amylases. These enzymes hydrolyze the starch stored in endosperm into glucose which is necessary for use both as a respiratory substrate and as a source of carbon skeletons of the molecules needed for growth','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Invertase','α-amylase','Lipase','Protease','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(151911,'biology','Seed dormancy is due to \n[2002]','Abscisic acid','Viable seeds of some plants are unable to germinate even after getting all the necessary conditions. This inability of viable seeds to germinate even under favourable conditions, is called dormancy of seeds. This is considered to be due to some barriers or blocks inside the seeds','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Ethylene','Abscisic acid','IAA','Starch','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(151912,'biology','The process of growth is maximum during \n[NEET 2020]','log phase','In most systems, the initial growth is slow i.e., lag phase, and it increases rapidly thereafter – at an exponential rate i.e., log or exponential phase.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','log phase','lag phase','Senescence','Dormancy','NEW','11-243','11-170',NULL),
(151913,'biology','Name the plant growth regulator which upon spraying on sugarcane crop, increases the length of stem, thus increasing the yield of sugarcane crop \n[NEET 2020]','Gibberellin','Spraying sugarcane crop with gibberellins increases the length of the stem and increasing the yield by as much as 20 tonnes per acre','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Cytokinin','Gibberellin','Ethylene','Abscisic acid','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(151914,'biology','Which of the following is not an inhibitory substance governing seed dormancy? \n[NEET 2020]','Gibberellic acid','Gibberellic acid involved in growth promoting activities, such as cell division, cell enlargement, pattern formation, tropic growth, flowering, fruiting and seed formation and is called plant growth promoters. It breaks seed dormancy which is antagonistic to abscisic acid','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Gibberellic acid','Abscisic acid','Phenolic acid','Para-ascorbic acid','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(151915,'biology','It takes very long time for pineapple plants to produce flowers. Which combination of hormones can be applied to artificially induce flowering in pineapple plants throughout the year to increase yield?\n[NEET 2019]','Auxin and Ethylene','Synthetic auxins like NAA and 2, 4-D are often employed for inducing flowering in pineapple. Ethylene also stimulates flowering in pineapple and related plants. Hence, both auxin and ethylene can be applied artificially to induce flowering in pineapple plants throughout the year to increase yield.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Cytokinin and Abscisic acid','Auxin and Ethylene','Gibberellin and Cytokinin','Gibberellin and Abscisic acid','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(151916,'biology','What is the site of perception of photoperiod necessary for induction of flowering in plants? \n[NEET 2019]','Leaves','Shoot apices modify themselves into flower apices prior to flowering, but they themselves cannot perceive photoperiods. Leaves are the site of perception of light/dark photoperiods. It has been hypothesised that leaves contain a hormonal substance responsible for flowering, which migrates from leaves to shoot apices for inducing flowering only when plants are exposed to necessary inductive photoperiods','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Leaves','Lateral buds','Pulvinus','Shoot apex','NEW','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(151917,'biology','The site of perception of light in plants during photoperiodism is \n[NEET 2021]','Leaf','The site of perception of light in plants during photoperiodism is leaf. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;251,paragraph no.3)','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Leaf','Shoot apex','Stem','Plasticity','NEW','11-351','11-178',NULL),
(151918,'biology','The plant hormone used to destroy weeds in a field \n[NEET 2021]','2, 4-D','2,4-D is used to destroy weeds in a field. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;249,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','IBA','IAA','NAA','2, 4-D','NEW','11-251','11-178',NULL),
(151919,'biology','In the exponential growth equation Nt = Noert, e represents \n[NEET 2021]','The base of natural logarithms','In the exponential growth equation Nt = N0ert,\ne represents the base of natural logarithms \nNt = Population density after time t\nN0 = Population density at time zero\nr = Intrinsic rate of natural increase called biotic potential.\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;243,paragraph no.3)','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','The base of geometric logarithms','The base of natural logarithms','The base of exponential logarithms','The base of number logarithms','NEW','11-243','11-170',NULL),
(151920,'biology','Plants follow different pathways in response to environment or phases of life to form different kinds of structures. This ability is called\n[NEET 2021]','Plasticity','Plants follow different pathways in response to environment or phases of life to form different kinds of structures. This ability is called plasticity,(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;246,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Maturity','Plasticity','Flexibility','All of these','NEW','11-246','11-173',NULL),
(151921,'biology','The gaseous plant growth regulator is used in plants to :\n[NEET 2022]','promote root growth and root hair formation to increase the absorption surface','Ethylene is a gaseous plant hormone. It induces development of adventitious roots on various types of cutting. It promotes the development of lateral roots and growth of root hairs. Cytokinin helps to overcome the apical dominance.  Auxin is used to kill dicot weeds. Gibberellin speeds up the malting process','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','speed up the malting process','promote root growth and root hair formation to increase the absorption surface','help overcome apical dominance','kill dicotyledonous weeds in the fields','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(151922,'biology','Which of the following growth regulators is an adenine derivative ? [ RE NEET 2022]','(2) Cytokinin','Cytokinin is an adenine derivative responsible for cell division.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Auxin','(2) Cytokinin','(3) Ethylene','(4) Abscisic acid','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(151923,'biology','The phenomenon by which the undividing parenchyma cells start to divide mitotically during \nplant tissue culture is called as :','(2) Dedifferentiation','This phenomenon is termed as dedifferentiation. The parenchyma cells that are made to divide under controlled laboratory conditions during plant tissue culture by dedifferentiation .','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(2) Dedifferentiation','(1) Differentiation','(3) Redifferentiation','(4) Secondary growth','NEW','11-245','11-172',NULL),
(151924,'biology','Which of the following statements is not correct ? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) Plant growth is generally determinate.','Phases of plant cell growth includes:\nMeristematic phase- The cells in the apex region of root and shoot have large nuclei, rich in protoplasm and cell walls are thin.\nElongation phase- In this phase, cells enlarge, size of vacuole increases and new cell wall deposition takes place.\nMaturation phase- In this phase, cells mature and attains maximum size.\nDifferentiation- In this region, cells divide continuously.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Phase of cell elongation of plant cells is characterized by increased vacuolation.','(2) Cells in the meristematic phase of growth exhibit abundant plasmodesmatal connections.','(3) Plant growth is generally determinate.','(4) Plant growth is measurable.','NEW','11-240','11-167',NULL),
(151925,'biology','Consider the following plant tissues : \n (A) Axillary buds \n (B) Fascicular vascular cambium \n (C) Interfascicular cambium \n (D) Cork cambium \n (E) Intercalary meristem \n Identify the lateral meristems among the above. [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) (B), (C) and (D) only','lateral meristems are present on the lateral side of the stem and root of a plant and helps in increasing the girth of the plant. Fascicular vascular cambium, Interfascicular cambium and cork cambium are all examples of lateral meristems. Intercalary meristem on the other hand are located at the internodes or the base of the leaves and help in increasing the length of the internode.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (A), (C) and (D) only','(2) (B), (C) and (D) only','(3) (A), (B), (C) and (E) only','(4) (A), (B), (D) and (E) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(152001,'biology','In plant, auxin synthesis occurs in \n[1997]','Roots and shoot tips','Auxin is a plant hormone which promote and regulate its growth and development. Auxin are produced in the meristem of shoot tips and move down the plant causing various effects.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cortex','Xylem','Phloem','Roots and shoot tips','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(152002,'biology','Which of the following hormone induces cell division ? \n[1997]','Cytokinin','Cytokinins are substances which act primarily on cell division and have little or no effect on extension/growth. It delays the senescence of leaves.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Auxin','Gibberellin','Cytokinin','Trypsin','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(152003,'biology','Which of the following is a weedicide ? \n[1998]','2,4-D','2, 4-D (dichlorophenoxyacetic acid) is a common systemic pesticide/herbicide. It is a synthetic auxin (plant hormone) and is used for killing broad leaved weeds (generally dicot)','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','IAA','2,4-D','IBA','NAA','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(152004,'biology','Which of the following is gaseous hormone ?\n[1998]','Ethylene','Ethylene is the only gaseous natural plant growth regulator produced by all plant organs but its maximum production occurs in ripening fruits and during senescence','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Auxin','Ethylene','Cytokinin','GA','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(152005,'biology','A plant cell has potential to develop into a full plant. This is called \n[1998]','Totipotency','Any cell which has an ability to develop into a complete organism is totipotent','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Totipotency','gene cloning','tissue culture','Regeneration','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(152006,'biology','Which of the following induces dormancy ?\n[1999]','Abscisic acid','Abscisic acid (ABA) induces dormancy.Robinson and Warming (1964), isolated a substance responsible for dormancy in Acer pseudoplatanus, and named it as dormin. This hormone was similar to abscission. It is a naturally occurring hormone that is present in all vascular plants and some mosses but is not present in bacteria, algae, fungi and liverworts. This hormone is responsible for dormancy','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Auxin','Cytokinin','Both (a) and (b)','Abscisic acid','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(152007,'biology','The plant hormone controlling fruit ripening is\n[1999]','Ethylene','Fruit ripening is controlled by hormone, ethylene. Ethylene is a gaseous hormone. It affects the growth, development, ripening and senescence (aging) of all plants.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','IAA','GA','ABA','Ethylene','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(152008,'biology','Induction of cell division and delay in senescence is done by \n[2001,2011]','Cytokinins','Cytokinins promote cell division and inhibit the degradative reactions in detached leaves and slow down senescence in intact leaves. The effect of cytokinin in retarding ageing is called the Richmond Lang Effect.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cytokinins','Auxins','GA','CoA','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(152010,'biology','Bud dormancy can be induced by \n[2002]','ABA','Abscisic acid (ABA) is a plant hormone which promotes dormancy in seeds and buds.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','IAA','GA','ABA','Ethylene','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(152011,'biology','Avena curvature test is a bioassay for examining the activity of \n[2006]','Auxins','Went (1928) performed Avena curvature test for auxins','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Auxins','Gibberellins','Cytokinins','Ethylene','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(152012,'biology','Gibberellins can promote seed germination because of their influence on \n[2005, 2012]','Production of hydrolyzing enzymes','Gibberellins can promote seed germination in cereals due to production of hydrolyzing enzymes like D amylase and proteases','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Rate of cell division','Production of hydrolyzing enzymes','Synthesis of abscisic acid','Absorption of water through hard seed coat.','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(152101,'biology','Assertion- Due to environment, the difference in shape of leaves produced in air and those produced in water in buttercup represents the heterophyllus development. \n\nReason- The phenomenon of heterophylly is an example of plasticity','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Plants follow different pathways in response to environment or phases of life to form different kinds of structures. This ability is called plasticity, e.g., heterophylly in cotton, coriander and larkspur. In such plants, the leaves of the juvenile plant are different in shape from those in mature plants. On the other hand, difference in shapes of leaves produced in air and those produced in water in buttercup also represent the heterophyllous development due to environment','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-246','11-173',NULL),
(152102,'biology','Assertion- Gibberellins induce flowering in long day plants. \n\nReason- Genetically tall plants become dwarf by application of gibberellin.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Gibberellins also promotes bolting (internode elongation just prior to flowering) in beet, cabbages and many plants with rosette habit.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(152103,'biology','Assertion- Auxin help to prevent fruit and leaf drop at early stages. \n\nReason- Auxins promote the abscission of older mature leaves and fruits','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Auxins promote flowering e.g. in pineapples. They help to prevent fruit and leaf drop at early stages but promote the abscission of older mature leaves and fruits','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(152104,'biology','Assertion- Apical bud is a source of auxin. \n\nReason- Removal of apical bud promotes lateral bud growth as there is absence of apical dominance','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','In most higher plants, the growing apical bud inhibits the growth of the lateral (axillary) buds, a phenomenon called apical dominance. Removal of shoot tips (decapitation) usually results in the growth of lateral buds','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(152105,'biology','Assertion- Ethylene is one of the most widely used PGR in agriculture. \n\nReason- It regulates wide range of physiological process.','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Since ethylene regulates so many physiological processes, it is one of the most widely used PGR in agriculture.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(152106,'biology','Assertion- GA is called stress hormone. \n\nReason- It increases tolerance of plant to various kind of stress.','Both assertion and reason are false','ABA stimulates the closure of stomata and increases the tolerance of plants to various kinds of stresses. Therefore, it is also called the stress hormone','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(152107,'biology','Assertion- Cytokinin helps to delay leaf Senescence. \n\nReason- It promotes nutrients mobilisation','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Cytokinins help overcome the apical dominance. They promote nutrient mobilisation which helps in the delay of leaf senescence','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(152108,'biology','Assertion- Auxin promotes female flowering in cucumber. \n\nReason- Ethylene does not induce flowering in Mango','Assertion is true and reason is false','Ethylene is used to initiate flowering and for synchronising fruit-set in pineapples. It also induces flowering in mango. Since ethylene regulates so many physiological processes, it is one of the most widely used PGR in agriculture. The most widely used compound as source of ethylene is ethephon. Ethephon in an aqueous solution is readily absorbed and transported within the plant and releases ethylene slowly. Ethephon hastens fruit ripening in tomatoes and apples and accelerates abscission in flowers and fruits (thinning of cotton, cherry, walnut). It promotes female flowers in cucumbers thereby increasing the yield.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(121310,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name of the compound in the given diagram(12114)','3-keto-2-methylhex-4-enal','Aldehyde gets priority over ketone group and therefore counting starts for aldehydic carbon. (NEET 2017)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','5-formylhex-2-en-3-one','5-methyl-4-oxohex-2-en-5-a','3-keto-2-methylhex-5-enal','3-keto-2-methylhex-4-enal','NEW','11-344','11-266','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121310q.png'),
(152109,'biology','Assertion- Auxin promotes apical dominance. \n\nReason- Cytokinin helps overcome apical dominance','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','It is true that auxin promotes apical dominance and cytokinin helps overcome apical dominance but these 2 are non related to each other','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(152110,'biology','Assertion- Gibberellin promotes bolting in beet cabbage. \n\nReason- Gibberellin promote xylem differentiation.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Gibberellins also promotes bolting (internode elongation just prior to flowering) in beet, cabbages and many plants with rosette habit.\n  Auxin also controls xylem differentiation and helps in cell division.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(152111,'biology','Assertion- ABA is antagonist to auxin. \n\nReason- Auxin is a gaseous PGR.','Both assertion and reason are false','ABA acts as an antagonist to GAs. Auxin is not a gaseous PGR.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(152112,'biology','Assertion- Auxin helps in tea plantation, hedge making. \n\nReason- Kinetin does not occur naturally in plants','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Both the statements are correct but not related.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(152113,'biology','Assertion- 24-D -widely used to kill monocotyledon weeds. \n\nReason- Auxin inhibits apical dominance.','Both assertion and reason are false','2, 4-D, widely used to kill dicotyledonous weeds, does not affect mature monocotyledonous plants. Auxin promotes apical dominance.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(152114,'biology','Assertion- Ethylene helps to increase absorption surface. \n\nReason- It promotes root growth and root hair formation','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Root hair increases the surface area for absorption.','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(152115,'biology','Assertion:  It is possible for the seeds to not germinate and go into a period of suspended growth or rest. \n\nReason: The seed germinates when favourable conditions for growth exist in the environment.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','The seed germinates when favourable conditions for growth exist in the environment. In absence of such favourable conditions the seeds do not germinate and goes into a period of suspended growth or rest. Once favourable conditions return, the seeds resume metabolic activities and growth takes place. Seed Dormancy is due to presence of chemical inhibitors such as abscissic acids, phenolic acids, para-ascorbic acid; Effect of inhibitory substances can be removed by subjecting the seeds to chilling conditions or by application of certain chemicals like gibberellic acid and nitrates. Changing the environmental conditions, such as light and temperature are other methods to overcome seed dormancy.\n(Reference  : NCERT, page 239, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-239','11-166',NULL),
(152116,'biology','Assertion: All cells of a plant have different structural and functional attributes. \n\nReason: All cells of a plant are descendants of the  zygote.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','All cells of a plant are descendants of the zygote. They have different structural and functional attributes. Development is the sum of two processes: growth and differentiation. To begin with, it is essential and sufficient to know that the development of a mature plant from a zygote (fertilised egg) follow a precise and highly ordered succession of events. During this process a complex body organisation is formed that produces roots, leaves, branches, flowers, fruits, and seeds, and eventually they die (Reference  : NCERT, page 239, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-239','11-166',NULL),
(152117,'biology','Assertion: Plant growth is unique because plants retain the capacity for unlimited growth throughout their life. \n\nReason: This ability of the plants is due to their elasticity and ability to self-sabotage.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Plant growth is unique because plants retain the capacity for unlimited growth throughout their life. This ability of the plants is due to the presence of meristems at certain locations in their body. The cells of such meristems have the capacity to divide and self-perpetuate. (Reference  : NCERT, page 240, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-240','11-167',NULL),
(152118,'biology','Assertion: New cells are always being added to the plant body. \n\nReason: The presence of meristems at certain locations in their body and their the capacity to divide and self-perpetuate.','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Plant growth is unique because plants retain the capacity for unlimited growth throughout their life. This ability of the plants is due to the presence of meristems at certain locations in their body. The cells of such meristems have the capacity to divide and self-perpetuate. The form of growth wherein new cells are always being added to the plant body by the activity of the meristem is called the open form of growth. (Reference  : NCERT, page 240, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-240','11-167',NULL),
(152119,'biology','Assertion: In dicotyledonous plants and gymnosperms, the lateral meristems, vascular-cambium and cork-cambium appear later in life. \n\nReason: These are the meristems that cause the decrease in the length of the organs in which they are present.','Assertion is true and reason is false','In dicotyledonous plants and gymnosperms, the lateral meristems, vascular cambium and cork-cambium appear later in life. These are the meristems that cause the increase in the girth of the organs in which they are active. This is known as secondary growth of the plant. (Reference  : NCERT, page 241, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-241','11-168',NULL),
(152120,'biology','Assertion: One single maize root apical mersitem can give rise to more than 17,500 new cells per hour, whereas cells in watermelon may increase in size by up to 3,50,000 times. \n\nReason: In the maize root, growth is expressed as an increase in cell size; whereas in a watermelon, the expression of growth is by increase in number of cells.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Growth is, therefore, measured by a variety of parameters some of which are: increase in fresh weight, dry weight, length, area, volume and cell number. One single maize root apical mersitem can give rise to more than 17,500 new cells per hour, whereas cells in a watermelon may increase in size by upto 3,50,000 times. In the former, growth is expressed as increase in cell number; the latter expresses growth as increase in size of the cell. (Reference  : NCERT, page 241, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-241','11-168',NULL),
(152121,'biology','Assertion: The cells of the zone further away from the apex (more proximal to the phase of elongation), attain their maximal size in terms of wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications. \n\nReason: These cells are in the zone that lies the portion of axis which is undergoing the phase of elongation.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Further away from the apex, i.e., more proximal to the phase of elongation, lies the portion of axis which is undergoing the phase of maturation. The cells of this zone, attain their maximal size in terms of wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications. (Reference  : NCERT, page 242, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-242','11-169',NULL),
(152122,'biology','Assertion: Even though plants might grow in a geometric or arithmetic manner, the rate of growth cannot be expressed mathematically.\n\nReason: It is impossible to measure a biological phenomenon mathematically.','Both assertion and reason are false','The increased growth per unit time is termed as growth rate. Thus, rate of growth can be expressed mathematically. An organism, or a part of the organism can produce more cells in a variety of ways. (Reference  : NCERT, page 242, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-242','11-169',NULL),
(152123,'biology','Assertion: A stationary phase of plant growth occurs after the exponential growth phase. \n\nReason: The initial growth is slow in many plant systems.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Because of limited nutrient supply, the growth slows down leading to a stationary phase. (Reference  : NCERT, page 243, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-243','11-170',NULL),
(152124,'biology','Assertion: If we plot the parameter of growth against time, we get a typical sigmoid or S-curve. \n\nReason: In most systems, the initial growth is slow (lag phase), but because of limited nutrient supply, the growth slows down leading to a stationary phase,when nutrients become available, it enters the log or exponential growth phase. So a lag phase is followed by stationary phase and then a log phase.','Assertion is true and reason is false','In most systems, the initial growth is slow (lag phase), and it increases rapidly thereafter – at an exponential rate (log or exponential phase). Here, both the progeny cells following mitotic cell division retain the ability to divide and continue to do so. However, with limited nutrient supply, the growth slows down leading to a stationary phase. Thus, if we plot the parameter of growth against time, we get a typical sigmoid or S-curve. (Reference  : NCERT, page 243, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Assertion Reasoning question',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-243','11-170',NULL),
(121311,'chemistry','What is the structure of isobutyl group in an organic compound in the given diagram(12115)','C','Only C has an iso group in it with 4 carbons (butane). (NEET 2013)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121311q.png'),
(152201,'biology','Consider the following statements about the growth and development of plant and choose the best option.','Development is the sum of two processes: growth and differentiation.','Development is the sum of two processes: growth and differentiation. (Reference  : NCERT, page 239, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Development is the sum of three processes: Differentiation, redifferentiation and dedifferentiation.','The developmental stages of a plant are not very different than that from an animal.','Development is the sum of two processes: growth and differentiation.','Development solely refers to the growth of a plant.','NEW','11-239','11-166',NULL),
(152202,'biology','Consider the following statements about the growth and development of plant and choose the best option.','Both A and B are correct','The first step in the process of plant growth is seed germination. The seed germinates when favourable conditions for growth exist in the environment. In absence of such favourable conditions the seeds do not germinate and goes into a period of suspended growth or rest. (Reference  : NCERT, page 239, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The first step in the process of plant growth is seed germination.','The seed germinates when favourable conditions for growth exist in the environment.','Both A and B are correct','Only B is correct','NEW','11-239','11-166',NULL),
(152203,'biology','Consider the following statements about the growth of a plant below.\n\ni. Growth is accompanied by only anabolic processes.\n\nii. Growth can be defined as an irreversible permanent increase in size of an organ or its parts.\n\niii. Growth is accompanied by metabolic processes, both anabolic and catabolic.\n\nUsing the code below, choose the correct option.','ii and iii only','Growth can be defined as an irreversible permanent increase in size of an organ or its parts or even of an individual cell. Generally, growth is accompanied by metabolic processes (both anabolic and catabolic), that occur at the expense of energy. (Reference  : NCERT, page 240, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i and ii only','iii','ii and iii only','NEW','11-240','11-167',NULL),
(152204,'biology','Consider the following statements about the growth of a plant below and choose the incorrect option.','The form of growth wherein new cells are always being added to the plant body by the activity of the meristem is called the closed form of growth.','The form of growth wherein new cells are always being added to the plant body by the activity of the meristem is called the open form of growth. (Reference  : NCERT, page: 240, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Growth is regarded as one of the most fundamental and conspicuous characteristics of a living being.','Growth is accompanied by metabolic processes that occur at the expense of energy.','The form of growth wherein new cells are always being added to the plant body by the activity of the meristem is called the closed form of growth.','Expansion of a leaf is an example of growth','NEW','11-240','11-167',NULL),
(152205,'biology','Consider the following statements about the phases of growth of a plant and choose the correct option.','Growth is measured by a variety of parameters such as: increase in fresh weight, dry weight, length, area, volume and cell number.','Growth, at a cellular level, is principally a consequence of increase in the amount of protoplasm.  Growth is, therefore, measured by a variety of parameters some of which are: increase in fresh weight, dry weight, length, area, volume and cell number. (Reference  : NCERT, page 241, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Growth, at a cellular level, is principally a consequence of increase in the amount of organs.','Growth is measured by a variety of parameters such as: increase in fresh weight, dry weight, length, area, volume and cell number.','The growth of a pollen tube is measured in terms of its surface area.','An increase in length denotes the growth in a dorsiventral leaf.','NEW','11-241','11-168',NULL),
(152206,'biology','Consider the following statements about the phases of growth of a plant and choose the incorrect option.','The period of growth is generally divided into three phases, namely, meristematic, elongation and repair.','The period of growth is generally divided into three phases, namely, meristematic, elongation and maturation. (Reference  : NCERT, page 241, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The cells in the region of root apex and shoot apex are rich in protoplasm.','The cells in the region of root apex and shoot apex possess large conspicuous nuclei.','The period of growth is generally divided into three phases, namely, meristematic, elongation and repair.','The constantly dividing cells are present both at the root apex and the shoot apex.','NEW','11-241','11-168',NULL),
(152207,'biology','Consider the following statement about the phase of maturation of a plant.\n\ni. The cells of this zone, attain their maximal size in terms of wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications.\n\nii. Further away from the apex and more proximal to the phase of elongation, lies the phase of maturation.\n\niii. The cells in this zone are rich in protoplasm, possess large conspicuous nuclei.\n\nUsing the code below, choose the incorrect option.','iii only','Further away from the apex, i.e., more proximal to the phase of elongation, lies the portion of axis which is undergoing the phase of maturation. The cells of this zone, attain their maximal size in terms of wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications. (Reference  : NCERT, page 242, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i and ii only','iii only','ii and iii only','NEW','11-242','11-169',NULL),
(152208,'biology','Consider the following statement about the phase of elongation of a plant.\n\ni. Wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications are characteristics of the cells in this phase.\n\nii. Increased vacuolation and cell enlargement are characteristics of the cells in this phase.\n\niii. New cell wall deposition is the characteristic of the cells in this phase.\n\nUsing the code below, choose the correct option.','ii and iii only','The cells proximal (just next, away from the tip) to the meristematic zone represent the phase of elongation. Increased vacuolation, cell enlargement and new cell wall deposition are the characteristics of the cells in this phase. (Reference  : NCERT, page 242, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i and ii only','iii only','ii and iii only','NEW','11-242','11-169',NULL),
(70705,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason .\nAssertion:Arrhenius concept of acids and bases are not applicable for molecules like ammonia even though ammonia is basic in nature.\n\nReason:It is because they contain H⁺ group.','A is true but R is false','Arrhenius concept of acid and base, however, suffers from the limitation of being applicable only to aqueous solutions and also, does not account for the basicity of substances like, ammonia which do not possess a hydroxyl group. (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 214 ; paragraph 5)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Acids, Bases and Salts',NULL,'1','mcq','A is true but R is false','Both, A and R, are true and R is the correct explanation of A','A is false but R is true','Both, A and R, are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','NEW','11-214','11-190',NULL),
(152209,'biology','Consider the following statements about the growth rate of a plant and choose the correct option.','In most systems, the initial growth is slow (lag phase), and it increases rapidly thereafter.','In most systems, the initial growth is slow (lag phase), and it increases rapidly thereafter – at an exponential rate (log or exponential phase). Here, both the progeny cells following mitotic cell division retain the ability to divide and continue to do so. However, with limited nutrient supply, the growth slows down leading to a stationary phase. If we plot the parameter of growth against time, we get a typical sigmoid or S-curve which is a characteristic of living organism growing in a natural environment. (Reference  : NCERT, page 243, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A sigmoid curve is a characteristic of living organism growing in an artificial environment.','In most systems, the initial growth is slow (lag phase), and it increases rapidly thereafter.','In the stationary phase, both the progeny cells following mitotic cell division retain the ability to divide and continue to do so.','Exponential growth phase of plant is the last phase of the S curve','NEW','11-243','11-170',NULL),
(152210,'biology','Consider the following statements about the growth of a plant and choose the incorrect option.','Any deviation from the optimum temperature range best suited for a plant’s growth could be unfavourable but not lethal.','Every plant organism has an optimum temperature range best suited for its growth. Any deviation from this range could be detrimental to its survival. (Reference  : NCERT, page 244, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Environmental signals such as light and gravity also affect certain phases/stages of growth.','Turgidity of cells helps in extension growth.','O₂ helps in releasing metabolic energy essential for growth activities.','Any deviation from the optimum temperature range best suited for a plant’s growth could be unfavourable but not lethal.','NEW','11-244','11-171',NULL),
(152211,'biology','Consider the following statements about the growth of a plant and choose the correct option.','Both A and B','The plant cells grow in size by cell enlargement which in turn requires water. Turgidity of cells helps in extension growth. Thus, plant growth and further development is intimately linked to the water status of the plant.  O₂ helps in releasing metabolic energy essential for growth activities. Nutrients (macro and micro essential elements) are required by plants for the synthesis of protoplasm and act as source of energy. (Reference  : NCERT, page 244, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The plant cells grow in size by cell enlargement which in turn requires water.','O₂ helps in releasing metabolic energy essential for growth activities.','Both A and B','Water and O₂ help in the synthesis of protoplasm and act as source of energy.','NEW','11-244','11-171',NULL),
(152212,'biology','Consider the following statements about the differentiation, dedifferentiation and redifferentiation of a plant and choose the incorrect option.','Tracheal cells losing their protoplasm is a sign of decay of a plant.','To form a tracheary element, the cells would lose their protoplasm. They also develop a very strong, elastic, lignocellulosic secondary cell walls, to carry water to long distances even under extreme tension. This act leading to maturation is termed as differentiation (Reference  : NCERT, page 245, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Tracheal cells losing their protoplasm is a sign of decay of a plant.','The living differentiated cells, that have lost the capacity to divide can regain the capacity of division under certain conditions.','The growth in plants is open, i.e., it can be indeterminate or determinate.','Cells positioned away from root apical meristems differentiate as root-cap cells.','NEW','11-245','11-172',NULL),
(152213,'biology','Consider the following statements about the differentiation, dedifferentiation and redifferentiation of a plant.\n\ni. The cells/tissues arising out of the same meristem have the same structures at maturity.\n\nii. The differentiation in plants is said to be closed.\n\niii. The formation of meristems – interfascicular cambium and cork cambium from fully differentiated parenchyma cells is an example of dedifferentiation.\n\nSelect the incorrect answer using the code below:','i and ii only',': (i) and (ii) are not correct as differentiation in plants is open, because cells/tissues arising out of the same meristem have different structures at maturity (Reference  : NCERT, page 245, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i and ii only','ii only','iii only','NEW','11-245','11-172',NULL),
(152214,'biology','Consider the following statements about the development of a plant.\n\ni. Plants follow different pathways in response to environment or phases of life to form different kinds of structures.\n\nii. The reason why there is a difference in shapes of leaves produced in air and those produced in water in buttercup is an example of plasticity.\n\niii. In plants like cotton, coriander and larkspur which exhibit heterophylly, the leaves of the juvenile plant are similar in shape from those in mature plants.\n\nSelect the incorrect answer using the code below:','iii only','Plants follow different pathways in response to environment or phases of life to form different kinds of structures. This ability is called plasticity, e.g., heterophylly in cotton, coriander and larkspur. In such plants, the leaves of the juvenile plant are different in shape from those in mature plants. On the other hand, difference in shapes of leaves produced in air and those produced in water in buttercup also represent the heterophyllous development due to environment (Reference  s: NCERT, page 246, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >>  Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i and ii only','ii only','iii only','NEW','11-246','11-173',NULL),
(152301,'biology','Consider the following statements about the development of a plant and choose the incorrect option.','The senescence stage of a plant occurs right after plasmatic growth.','Mature cell enters the stage of senescence, according to figure 15.8 on page 246 (Reference  : NCERT, page 246, figure 15.8).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Larkspur exhibits heterophylly.','Seed germination marks the beginning of the developmental stages of a plant.','The senescence stage of a plant occurs right after plasmatic growth.','Development is a term that includes all changes that an organism goes through during its life cycle from germination of the seed to senescence.','NEW','11-246','11-173',NULL),
(152302,'biology','Consider the following statements about the characteristics of PGRs and select the correct option.','Both A and C','The plant growth regulators (PGRs) are small, simple molecules of diverse chemical composition. They could be indole compounds (indole-3-aceticacid, IAA); adenine derivatives, derivatives of carotenoids; terpenes or gases. Plant growth regulators are variously described as phytohormones (Reference  : NCERT, page 247, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The plant growth regulators (PGRs) are small, simple molecules of diverse chemical composition.','Only indole compounds such as indole-3-acetic acid can be regarded as a phytohormone.','Kinetin is a PGR.','Both A and C','NEW','11-247','11-174',NULL),
(152303,'biology','Consider the following statements about the characteristics of PGRs and select the incorrect option','PGRs are also involved in various growth promoting activities such as dormancy and abscission.','The PGRs can be broadly divided into two groups based on their functions in a living plant body. One group of PGRs are involved in growth promoting activities, and are also called plant growth promoters, e.g., auxins, gibberellins and cytokinins. They are also involved in various growth inhibiting activities such as dormancy and abscission (Reference  : NCERT, page 247, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Gibberellins are an example of plant growth promoters.','The PGRs can be broadly divided into two groups based on their functions in a living plant body.','PGRs are also involved in various growth promoting activities such as dormancy and abscission.','Both A and B','NEW','11-247','11-174',NULL),
(152304,'biology','Given below are a few statements about Physiological Effects of Plant Growth Regulators. Choose the option that is factually correct.','Auxins have been used for plant propagation.','Auxins like IAA and indole butyric acid (IBA) have been isolated from plants. They help to initiate rooting in stem cuttings, an application widely used for plant propagation. They help to prevent fruit and leaf drop at early stages but promote the abscission of older mature leaves and fruits. In most higher plants, the growing apical bud inhibits the growth of the lateral (axillary) buds, a phenomenon called apical dominance (Reference  : NCERT, page 248, paragraph 6,7,8).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Auxins have been used for plant propagation.','Auxins like IAA and indole benzoic acid (IBA) have been isolated from plants.','Auxins help to prevent fruit and leaf drop of older mature leaves and fruits','In most higher plants, the growing lateral bud inhibits the growth of the apical buds.','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(152305,'biology','Consider the following statements about the discovery of Plant Growth Regulators: \n\ni. E. Kurosawa (1926) reported the appearance of symptoms of the disease in rice seedlings when theywere treated with the active substance kinetin.\n\nii. Three independent researches reported the purification and chemical characterization of kinetin in mid 1960s.\n\niii. Miller et al. (1955), identified and crystallised the cytokinesis promoting active substance that they termed kinetin. \n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','iii only','F. Skoog and his co-workers observed that from the intermodal segments of tobacco stems the callus (a mass of undifferentiated cells) proliferated only if, in addition to auxins the nutrients medium was supplemented with one of the following: extracts of vascular tissues, yeast extract, coconut milk or DNA. Miller et al. (1955), later identified and crystallised the cytokinesis promoting active substance that they termed kinetin (Reference  : NCERT, page 248, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i and ii only','iii only','ii and iii only','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(152306,'biology','Consider the following statement about Gibberellins, and choose the correct option.','Spraying sugarcane crop with gibberellins increases the length of the stem.','Gibberellic acid (GA₃) was one of the first gibberellins to be discovered and remains the most intensively studied form. Gibberellins, cause fruits like apple to elongate and improve its shape. They also delay senescence. Spraying sugarcane crop with gibberellins increases the length of the stem (Reference  : NCERT, page 249, paragraph 2 and 3)','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Gibberellins cause fruits like apple to get ripe faster and improve its sweetness.','Spraying sugarcane crop with gibberellins increases the length of the stem.','Gibberellic acid (GA₁) was one of the first gibberellins to be discovered.','Gibberellins cannot delay senescence.','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(152307,'biology','Consider the following statements about the functions of Cytokinins. Choose the incorrect option.','Natural cytokinins are synthesised in regions where rapid cell senescence occurs.','Cytokinins have specific effects on cytokinesis, and were discovered as kinetin (a modified form of adenine, a purine) from the autoclaved herring sperm DNA. Search for natural substances with cytokinin-like activities led to the isolation of zeatin from corn-kernels and coconut milk. Natural cytokinins are synthesised in regions where rapid cell division occurs, for example, root apices, developing shoot buds, young fruits etc. (Reference  : NCERT, page 249, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Cytokinins have specific effects on cytokinesis, and were discovered as kinetin.','Search for natural substances with cytokinin-like activities led to the isolation of zeatin.','Natural cytokinins are synthesised in regions where rapid cell senescence occurs.','Both A and B','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(152308,'biology','Consider the following statements about the functions of Abscisic Acid: \n\ni. ABA acts as a synergistic molecule to GAs.\n\nii. ABA stimulates the closure of stomata.\n\niii. ABA is also called the stress hormone.\n\nChoose the correct option, using the code below.','ii and iii only','ABA stimulates the closure of stomata and increases the tolerance of plants to various kinds of stresses. Therefore, it is also called the stress hormone. In most situations, ABA acts as an antagonist to GAs (Reference  : NCERT, page 250, paragraph 5).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i and ii only','iii only','ii and iii only','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(152309,'biology','Consider the following statements about the functions of Ethylene: \n\ni. Ethylene also induces flowering in mango.\n\nii. Ethylene delays fruit ripening. \n\niii. It is one of the most widely used PGR in agriculture.\n\nChoose the incorrect option, using the code below.','ii only','Ethylene is used to initiate flowering and for synchronising fruit-set in pineapples. It also induces flowering in mango. Since ethylene regulates so many physiological processes, it is one of the most widely used PGR in agriculture. The most widely used compound as source of ethylene is ethephon. Ethephon hastens fruit ripening in tomatoes and apples and accelerates abscission in flowers and fruits (Reference  : NCERT, page 250, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','ii only','iii only','ii and iii only','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(70706,'chemistry','H₂SO₄ is a ______ acid and hence its conjugate base is _______','Strong , weak','Brönsted acid is a strong acid then its conjugate base is a weak base.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 215 ; paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Acids, Bases and Salts',NULL,'1','mcq','Weak , strong','Weak , weak','Strong , neutral','Strong , weak','NEW','11-215','11-191',NULL),
(70707,'chemistry','Conjugate bases of H₂SO₄ is','HSO₄⁻','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 215 ;  Problem 7.12) diagram(ne0710)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Acids, Bases and Salts',NULL,'1','mcq','HSO₄⁻','SO₄²⁻','H₂SO₄','H₃SO₄⁺','NEW','11-215','11-191',NULL),
(70708,'chemistry','Conjugate acids of  NH₃ is is','NH₄⁺','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 215 ;  Problem 7.13) diagram(ne0711)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Acids, Bases and Salts',NULL,'1','mcq','NH²⁻','NH₄⁺','NH₂⁻','NH₃','NEW','11-215','11-191','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/70708q.png'),
(152310,'biology','Consider the following statements about the plant growth and development process and choose the incorrect option.','Following cell division in root and shoot apical meristem cells, the growth could be asymmetric or geometrical.','In plants, meristems are the sites of growth. Following cell division in root and shoot apical meristem cells, the growth could be arithmetic or geometrical. One can define three principle phases of growth-the lag, the log and the senescent phase. Since differentiation in plants is open, thedevelopment could also be flexible (Reference  : NCERT, page 253, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Since differentiation in plants is open, the development could also be flexible.','One can define three principle phases of growth – the lag, the log and the senescent phase.','In plants, meristems are the sites of growth.','Following cell division in root and shoot apical meristem cells, the growth could be asymmetric or geometrical.','NEW','11-243','11-170',NULL),
(152311,'biology','Consider the following statements about photoperiodism.\n\ni. The site of perception of light/dark duration are the leaves.\n\nii. While shoot apices modify themselves into flowering apices prior to flowering, they by themselves cannot perceive photoperiods.\n\niii.  Only the duration of light period is important.\n\nChoose the correct option, using the code below.','i and ii only','It is now known that not only the duration of light period but that the duration of dark period is also of equal importance. Hence, it can be said that flowering in certain plants depends not only on a combination of light and dark exposures but also their relative durations.  It is also interesting to note that while shoot apices modify themselves into flowering apices prior to flowering, they (i.e., shoot apices of plants) by themselves cannot perceive photoperiods. The site of perception of light/dark duration are the leaves (Reference  : NCERT, page 252, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','ii only','i and ii only','iii only','NEW','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(152312,'biology','Assertion: Plant growth and further development is intimately linked to the water status of the plant. \n\nReason: Turgidity of cells helps in extension growth.','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','The plant cells grow in size by cell enlargement which in turn requires water. Turgidity of cells helps in extension growth. Thus, plant growth and further development is intimately linked to the water status of the plant. (Reference  : NCERT, page 244, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-244','11-171',NULL),
(152313,'biology','Assertion: Absolute growth rate and relative growth rate are two means of qualitative comparisons for growth of a plant. \n\nReason: Both methods measure the growth of plant with respect to its species.','Both assertion and reason are false','Quantitative comparisons between the growth of living system can also be made in two ways: (i) measurement and the comparison of total growth per unit time is called the absolute growth rate. (ii) The growth of the given system per unit time expressed on a common basis, e.g., per unit initial parameter is called the relative growth rate. (Reference  : NCERT, page 244, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-244','11-171',NULL),
(152314,'biology','Assertion: During differentiation, cells undergo few to major structural changes. \n\nReason: Many changes may be attributed to mutation, while others are characteristic to certain species.','Assertion is true and reason is false','The cells derived from root apical and shoot-apical meristems and cambium differentiate and mature to perform specific functions. This act leading to maturation is termed as differentiation. During differentiation, cells undergo few to major structural changes both in their cell walls and protoplasm. (Reference  s: NCERT, page 245, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-245','11-172',NULL),
(152315,'biology','Assertion: The process of differentiation for the formation of a tracheary element is best suited to carry water to long distances even under extreme tension. \n\nReason: To form a tracheary element, the cells would lose their protoplasm. They also develop a very strong, elastic, lignocellulosic secondary cell walls during this process.','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','During differentiation, cells undergo few to major structural changes both in their cell walls and protoplasm. For example, to form a tracheary element, the cells would lose their protoplasm. They also develop a very strong, elastic, lignocellulosic secondary cell walls, to carry water to long distances even under extreme tension (Reference  s: NCERT, page 245, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-245','11-172',NULL),
(152316,'biology','Assertion: Plasmatic growth occurs before the plant cell enters the development phase. \n\nReason: It occurs before the process of seed germination.','Both assertion and reason are false','Development is a term that includes all changes that an organism goes through during its life cycle from germination of the seed to senescence. Plasmatic growth occurs as a part of plant development process (Reference  : NCERT, page 245, paragraph 4 and page 246, figure 15.8).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-245','11-172',NULL),
(70709,'chemistry','Conjugate acid  and  base of water are_______  and _______ respectively.','H₃O⁺ and OH⁻','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 215 ;  Problem 7.14) diagram(ne0712)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Acids, Bases and Salts',NULL,'1','mcq','H₃O⁺ and OH⁻','H⁺ and H₃O⁺','Water does not produce any conjugate base or acid','H₂O⁺ and OH⁻','NEW','11-215','11-191',NULL),
(152317,'biology','Assertion: Heterophylly in cotton, coriander and larkspur is observed.\n\nReason: It is a genetic mutation that occurs in many varieties.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Plants follow different pathways in response to environment or phases of life to form different kinds of structures. This ability is called plasticity, e.g., heterophylly in cotton, coriander and larkspur (Reference  s: NCERT, page 246, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-246','11-173',NULL),
(152318,'biology','Assertion: Charles Darwin and his son Francis Darwin observed that the coleoptiles of canary grass responded to unilateral illumination by growing towards the light source. \n\nReason: The tip of the coleoptile was disturbed by the external influence that caused the bending of the entire coleoptile.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Charles Darwin and his son Francis Darwin observed that the coleoptiles of canary grass responded to unilateral illumination by growing towards the light source (phototropism). After a series of experiments, it was concluded that the tip of coleoptile was the site of transmittable influence that caused the bending of the entire coleoptile (Reference  : NCERT, page 247, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-247','11-174',NULL),
(152319,'biology','Assertion: The ‘bakanae’ (foolish seedling) was a disease of rice seedlings, caused by a fungal pathogen. \n\nReason: The appearance of symptoms of the disease in rice seedlings were reported when they were treated with auxins.','Assertion is true and reason is false','The ‘bakanae’ (foolish seedling) disease of rice seedlings, was caused by a fungal pathogen Gibberella fujikuroi. E. Kurosawa (1926) reported the appearance of symptoms of the disease in rice seedlings when they were treated with sterile filtrates of the fungus. The active substances were later identified as gibberellic acid (Reference  : NCERT, page 248, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(152320,'biology','Assertion: Auxins have been used extensively in agricultural and horticultural practices. \n\nReason: Some PGRs respond to wounds and stresses of biotic and abiotic origin.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','The term ‘auxin’ is applied to the indole-3-acetic acid (IAA), and to other natural and synthetic compounds having certain growth regulating properties. They are generally produced by the growing apices of the stems and roots, from where they migrate to the regions of their action. All these auxins have been used extensively in agricultural and horticultural practices (Reference  : NCERT, page 248, paragraph 6).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-248','11-175',NULL),
(152321,'biology','Assertion: Gibberellins can help to increase the length of grapes stalks.  \n\nReason: Gibberellins delay senescence.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','All GAs are acidic. They produce a wide range of physiological responses in the plants. Their ability to cause an increase in length of axis is used to increase the length of grapes stalks (Reference  : NCERT, page 249, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(152322,'biology','Assertion: Natural cytokinins are present in root apices. \n\nReason: Natural cytokinins are synthesised in regions where rapid nutrient uptake occurs.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Natural cytokinins are synthesised in regions where rapid cell division occurs, for example, root apices, developing shoot buds, young fruits etc. (Reference  : NCERT, page 249, paragraph 5).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-249','11-176',NULL),
(152323,'biology','Assertion: Ethylene is highly effective in fruit ripening. \n\nReason: It enhances the respiration rate during ripening of the fruits.','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Ethylene is highly effective in fruit ripening. It enhances the respiration rate during ripening of the fruits (Reference  : NCERT, page 250, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-250','11-177',NULL),
(152324,'biology','Assertion: Some plants require the exposure to light for a period exceeding a well-defined critical duration to induce flowering. \n\nReason: The periodic exposure to light is critical for flowering in some plants.','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','It has been observed that some plants require a periodic exposure to light to induce flowering. It is also seen that such plants are able to measure the duration of exposure to light. For example, some plants require the exposure to light for a period exceeding a well-defined critical duration (Reference  : NCERT, page 251, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-251','11-178',NULL),
(152325,'biology','Assertion: The site of perception of light/dark duration are the leaves. \n\nReason: It has been hypothesized that there is a hormonal substance(s) that is responsible for flowering. This hormonal substance migrates from leaves to root apices for inhibiting flowering only when the plants require nutrients.','Assertion is true and reason is false','The site of perception of light/dark duration are the leaves. It has been hypothesised that there is a hormonal substance(s) that is responsible for flowering. This hormonal substance migrates from leaves to shoot apices for inducing flowering only when the plants are exposed to the necessary inductive photoperiod (Reference  : NCERT, page 252, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Plant Growth and Development >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct, reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true and reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-252',NULL,NULL),
(190101,'chemistry','Surface chemistry deals with phenomena _____.','that occur at the interfaces','Surface chemistry deals with phenomena that occur at the surfaces or interfaces.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no. 123. Paragraph (Introduction))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','that occur inside the interfaces','that occur at the interfaces','that does not occur the interfaces','that occurs at junction of two interfaces','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190102,'chemistry','The interface or surface is represented by separating the bulk phases by a :','hyphen or a slash','The interface or surface is represented by separating the bulk phases by a hyphen or a slash. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no. 123. Paragraph (Introduction))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','hyphen or a comma','colon or a slash','hyphen or a slash','colon or a comma','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190103,'chemistry','There is no interface between gases due to _____ .','complete miscibility','Gas- gas interaction leads to  completely miscible system, one cannot separate the types of gases. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no. 123. Paragraph (Introduction))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','complete miscibility','partial miscibility','complete immiscibility','their complete miscibility at certain temperature and pressure','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190104,'chemistry','Which of the following phenomena is not involved in surface chemistry :','Sublimation','Many important phenomena, noticeable amongst these being corrosion, electrode processes, heterogeneous catalysis, dissolution and crystallisation occur at surfaces. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, Page no. 123. Paragraph (Introduction))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','Corrosion','Heterogeneous Catalysis','Electrode processes','Sublimation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190105,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct :\na. To accomplish surface studies meticulously, it becomes imperative to have a really clean surface.\nb. To accomplish surface studies meticulously, it becomes imperative to have a really absorbing surface.\nc.  To accomplish surface studies meticulously, it becomes imperative to have a smooth surface.\nd. To accomplish surface studies meticulously, it becomes imperative to have a sinusoidal surface.','Only a','To accomplish surface studies meticulously, it becomes imperative to have a really clean surface.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.123. Paragraph(Introduction))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','Only a','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and b','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190106,'chemistry','What pressure is required to obtain ultra clean surface on metal?','10⁻⁸ to 10⁻⁹ pascal','Under very high vacuum of the order of 10⁻⁸ to 10⁻⁹ pascal, it is now possible to obtain ultra clean surface of the metals.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.123. Paragraph (Introduction))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁻⁸ to 10⁻⁷ pascal','10⁻⁸ to 10⁻⁹ pascal','10⁻³ to 10⁻⁹ pascal','10⁻⁵ to 10⁻² pascal','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190107,'chemistry','Solid materials with clean surfaces if not stored properly can be covered by molecules of _____.','Air','Solid materials with ultra clean surfaces need to be stored in vacuum otherwise these will be covered by molecules of the major components of air namely dioxygen and dinitrogen.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.123, Paragraph (Introduction))','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Silica','Water','Air','Oxides','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190108,'chemistry','Solid materials with ultra clean surfaces need to be stored in _____.','Vacuum','Solid materials with ultra clean surfaces need to be stored in vacuum otherwise these will be covered by molecules of the major components of air namely dioxygen and dinitrogen.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.123. Paragraph (Introduction)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Vacuum','Pressure chamber','Open air','Furnace','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190109,'chemistry','Which among the following is not the feature of surface chemistry :','Absorption','Some important features of surface chemistry adsorption, catalysis and colloids including emulsions and gels.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.123. Paragraph (Introduction)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','Adsorption','Catalysis','Colloids','Absorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190110,'chemistry','The phenomena in which molecules penetrates in bulk Called _____.','Absorption','When a substance is evenly distributed throughout the bulk of a solid, the phenomenon is known as absorption. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Adsorption','Absorption','Desorption','Sorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190111,'chemistry','The surface of a solid has the tendency to ______ molecules of the phase with which it comes in contact.','attract and retain','The surface of a solid has the tendency to attract and retain the molecules of the phase with which it comes into contact. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','attract and retain','repel and retain','attract and repel','rappelling only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190112,'chemistry','In terms of adsorption, which of the following option is accurate :','Molecules remain only at the surface and do not go deeper into the bulk.','The accumulation of molecular species at the surface rather than in the bulk of a solid or liquid is termed adsorption.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','Molecules remain only at the surface and do not go deeper into the bulk.','Molecules do go deeper into the bulk','Molecules remain at the surface and also go deeper into the bulk.','Molecules do not interact Chemically with the surface','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190113,'chemistry','The accumulation of molecular species at the surface rather than in the bulk of a solid or liquid is termed as :','Adsorption','The accumulation of molecular species at the surface rather than in the bulk of a solid or liquid is termed adsorption.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Adsorption','Absorption','Sorption','Desorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190114,'chemistry','The molecular species or substance, which concentrates or accumulates at the surface is termed  as :','Adsorbate','The molecular species or substance, which concentrates or accumulates at the surface is termed adsorbate.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Adsorbate','Adsorbent','Solute','Solvent','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190115,'chemistry','The material on the surface of which the adsorption takes place is called :','Adsorbent','The material on the surface of which the adsorption takes place is called adsorbent.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Adsorbent','Adsorbate','Solute','Solvent','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190116,'chemistry','Adsorption is essentially a ______ phenomenon.','Surface','Adsorption is essentially a surface phenomenon. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph no.5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Bulk','Surface','Density','Penetration','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190117,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a good example of an adsorbent :','PVC','Solids, especially those that are finely divided, have a large surface area, act as effective adsorbents. Examples include charcoal, silica gel, alumina gel, clay, colloids, metals that are finely divided, etc.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Charcoal','Clay','Silica gel','PVC','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190118,'chemistry','Which among the following is not a good adsorbent :','Calcium chloride','Solids, especially those that are finely divided, have a large surface area, act as effective adsorbents. Examples include charcoal, silica gel, alumina gel, clay, colloids, metals that are finely divided, etc.\nCalcium chloride is a good absorbent.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'2','mcq','Alumina gel','Colloids','Finely divided metals','Calcium chloride','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190119,'chemistry','If a gas like O₂, H₂, CO, Cl₂, NH₃ or SO₂ is taken in a closed vessel containing powdered charcoal, it is observed that the pressure of the gas in the enclosed vessel :','Decreases','If a gas like O₂, H₂, CO, Cl₂, NH₃ or SO₂ is taken in a closed vessel containing powdered charcoal, it is observed that the pressure of the gas in the enclosed vessel decreases. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Increases','Decreases','remains constant','no change observed','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190120,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\n\nAssertion : If a gas like  O₂, H₂, CO, Cl₂, NH₃ or SO₂ is taken in a closed vessel containing powdered charcoal, it is observed that the pressure of the gas in the enclosed vessel decreases.\n\nReason : The gas molecules concentrate at the surface of the charcoal, i.e., gases are adsorbed at the surface.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','If a gas like O₂, H₂, CO, Cl₂, NH₃ or SO₂ is taken in a closed vessel containing powdered charcoal, it is observed that the pressure of the gas in the enclosed vessel decreases. The gas molecules concentrate at the surface of the charcoal, i.e., gases are adsorbed at the surface.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is false but the Reason is true.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190121,'chemistry','In a solution of an organic dye, say methylene blue, when animal charcoal is added and the solution is well shaken, it is observed that the filtrate turns :','Colourless','In a solution of an organic dye, say methylene blue, when animal charcoal is added and the solution is well shaken, it is observed that the filtrate turns colourless. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Blue','Violet','Intense pink','Colourless','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190122,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : In a solution of an organic dye, say methylene blue, when animal charcoal is added and the solution is well shaken, it is observed that the filtrate turns colourless. \nReason : Organic dye get adsorbed on Charcoal.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','In a solution of an organic dye, say methylene blue, when animal charcoal is added and the solution is well shaken, it is observed that the filtrate turns colourless. The molecules of the dye, thus, accumulate on the surface of charcoal, i.e., are adsorbed.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Assertion is false but the Reason is true.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190123,'chemistry','When raw sugar aqueous solution is passed through beds of animal charcoal, the result is:','Colourless','Aqueous solution of raw sugar, when passed over beds of animal charcoal, becomes colourless as the colouring substances are adsorbed by the charcoal.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry class 12, page no.124. Paragraph 5.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> General surface chemistry and Adsorption',NULL,'1','mcq','Colourless','Black','Blue','Green','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190201,'chemistry','Who suggested the term catalyst?','Berzelius','The systematic study of the effect of various foreign substances on the rates of chemical reactions was first made by Berzelius, in 1835. He suggested the term catalyst for such substances.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 130; Paragraph 5.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Thomas Graham','Berzelius','Gaber','Ibn Nafees','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(70710,'chemistry','According to lewis concept of acids and bases which is true for acids?','Acid accepts electron','G.N. Lewis in 1923 defined an acid as a species which accepts electron pair and base which donates an electron pair. (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 216 ; paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Acids, Bases and Salts',NULL,'1','mcq','Acid accepts electron','Acid accepts H⁺','Acid donates H⁺','Acid donates electron','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(190202,'chemistry','Substance which accelerate the rate of chemical reaction and themselves remain  ______ unchanged after the reaction are known as ______ .','Chemically, Catalyst','Substances, which accelerate the rate of a chemical reaction and themselves remain chemically and quantitatively unchanged after the reaction, are known as catalysts, and the phenomenon is known as catalysis.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 130; Paragraph 5.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'2','mcq','Chemically, Enzyme','Chemically, Catalyst','Qualitatively, Catalyst','Physically, Enzyme','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190203,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is true?\n(a) After the reaction, the catalyst remains chemically unchanged but not quantitatively unchanged.\n(b) Enzymes catalyse mainly biochemical reactions. \n(c) Promoters are substances that enhance the activity of a catalyst.\n(d) Poisons promote the activity of a catalyst.','Both b and c','(a) Catalysts remain chemically and quantitatively unchanged after the reaction.\n(b) Numerous reactions that occur in the bodies of animals and plants to maintain the life process are catalysed by enzymes. The enzymes are, thus, termed as biochemical catalysts and the phenomenon is known as biochemical catalysis.\n(c) Promoters are substances that enhance the activity of a catalyst.\n(d) Poisons decrease the activity of a catalyst.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 130; Paragraph 5.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190204,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is true about enzyme catalysis?','On either side of the optimum temperature, the enzyme activity decreases.','a) Enzymes are protein in nature.\nb) Each enzyme is specific for a given reaction.\nc) The rate of an enzyme reaction becomes maximum at a definite temperature, called the optimum temperature. On either side of the optimum temperature, the enzyme activity decreases. The optimum temperature range for enzymatic activity is 298-310K.\nd) The rate of an enzyme reaction becomes maximum at optimum temperature.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 134, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Enzymes are mostly poisonous in nature.','Enzymes action is non specific.','On either side of the optimum temperature, the enzyme activity decreases.','Enzymes are least reactive at optimum temperature.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190205,'chemistry','The enzyme which digest maltose called ______.','maltase','The maltase enzyme converts maltose into glucose.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 133, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','maltase','lipase','zymase','pepsin','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190206,'chemistry','Promoter are substances that :','enhance the activity of a catalyst.','Promoters are substances that enhance the activity of a catalyst.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 129, Paragraph 5.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','enhance the quantity of product.','Decrease the quantity of product.','enhance the activity of a catalyst.','inhibit the activity of a catalyst.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190207,'chemistry','Mark the correct option regarding poison, promoter & catalyst.','Promoters increase the activity of the catalyst. Poison, however, inhibits it.','Promoters are substances that enhance the activity of a catalyst while poisons decrease the activity of a catalyst. For example, in Haber’s process for manufacture of ammonia, molybdenum acts as a promoter for iron which is used as a catalyst.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 129, Paragraph 5.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Both promoter and poison enhance the activity of catalyst.','Both promoter and poison have similar nature. Whereas, catalyst is different.','The promoter and poison are neutralised by the catalyst.','Promoters increase the activity of the catalyst. Poison, however, inhibits it.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190208,'chemistry','In Haber\'s process the function of molybdenum and iron respectively :\ndiagram(n1902)','promoter and catalyst','In Haber’s process for manufacture of ammonia, molybdenum acts as a promoter for iron which is used as a catalyst.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 129, Paragraph 5.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'2','mcq','promoter and catalyst','poison and catalyst','both are catalyst','coenzyme and catalyst','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190208q.png'),
(190209,'chemistry','Which  one  is  an  example  of  heterogeneous catalysis?','Combination   between   dinitrogen   and dihydrogen   to   form   ammonia   in   the presence of finely divided iron','here  reactants  are  given  gaseous  state while the catalyst is in solid state\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 130, Paragraph 5.2.1) diagram(ne1911)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrolysis of sugar catalysed by H+ions','Decomposition  of  ozone  in  presence  of nitrogen monoxide','Combination   between   dinitrogen   and dihydrogen   to   form   ammonia   in   the presence of finely divided iron','Oxidaion  of  sulphur  dioxide  into  sulphur trioxide   in   the   presence   of   oxides   of nitrogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190210,'chemistry','The catalytic process in which the reactants and the catalyst are in different phase is known as  ______ .','heterogeneous catalysis','The catalytic process in which the reactants and the catalyst are in different phases is known as heterogeneous catalysis.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 130, Paragraph 5.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Homogenous catalysis','heterogeneous catalysis','Freundlich isotherm','adsorption isotherm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190211,'chemistry','According to the adsorption theory of catalysis, The heat of adsorption is utilized in ______ .','enhancing the rate of reaction','The old the adsorption hypothesis of catalysis, stated that reactants in gaseous or solution form are adsorbed on the surface of the solid catalyst. The rate of reaction increases as the concentration of the reactants on the surface increases. As adsorption is an exothermic process, the heat of adsorption is utilized to enhance the rate of the reaction.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 131, Paragraph 5.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','inhibiting the rate of catalysis','enhancing the rate of catalysis','inhibiting the rate of reaction','enhancing the rate of reaction','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190212,'chemistry','The modern adsorption theory is combination of :','intermediate compound theory and old adsorption theory.','The modern adsorption theory is the combination of intermediate compound formation theory and the old adsorption theory. The catalytic activity is localised on the surface of the catalyst. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 131, Paragraph 5.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','intermediate compound theory and Freundlich isotherm.','old adsorption theory and adsorption isotherm.','intermediate compound theory and old adsorption theory.','adsorption isotherm and Freundlich isotherm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(70711,'chemistry','I. OH- is Lewis acid.\nII. BCl₃ is Lewis base.','Both are false','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 216 ;  Problem 7.15) diagram(ne0713)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Acids, Bases and Salts',NULL,'1','mcq','Both are true','Both are false','I is true but II is false','II is true but I is false','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(190213,'chemistry','What is the proper sequence of the mechanisms in the modern adsorption theory?\n1.Diffusion of reactants to the surface of the catalyst.\n2.Occurrence of chemical reaction on the catalyst’s surface through \nformation of an intermediate\n3.Adsorption of reactant molecules on the surface of the catalyst.\n4.Desorption of reaction products from the catalyst surface, and thereby, \nmaking the surface available again for more reaction to occur.','1,3,2,4','1.Diffusion of reactants to the surface of the catalyst.\n2.Adsorption of reactant molecules on the surface of the catalyst.\n3.Occurrence of chemical reaction on the catalyst’s surface through formation of an intermediate.\n4.Desorption of reaction products from the catalyst surface, and thereby, making the surface available again for more reaction to occur.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 131, Paragraph 5.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2,3,4','1,3,2,4','1,2,4,3','4,2,1,3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190214,'chemistry','Which of the following explain the action of catalytic promoters and catalytic poisons?','none of these','a. The old adsorption theory explained the adsorption of reactants (gaseous state or in solutions) on solid catalyst.\nb. The intermediate compound theory explains the catalytic action.\nc. The modern adsorption theory is the combination of intermediate compound formation theory and the old adsorption theory.\nHence, none of the above explains the action catalytic promoters and catalytic poisons.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 131, Paragraph 5.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','modern adsorption theory','intermediate compound theory','old adsorption theory','none of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190215,'chemistry','The activity of solid catalyst depends upon the ______ of chemisorption to a large extent.','strength','An important feature of solid catalysts; The activity of a solid catalyst depends upon the strength of chemisorption to a large extent. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 132, Paragraph 5.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','adsorption','absorption','strength','weakness','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190216,'chemistry','The maximum catalytic activity is shown by which group in hydrogenation reaction?','Group 7-9','Catalytic activity for hydrogenation reactions increases from Group 5 to Group 11 metals, with Group 7-9 elements of the periodic table exhibiting the highest activity.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 132, Paragraph 5.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Group 5 - 11','Group 7-9','Group 5 only','Group 11 only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190217,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Different products can be obtained by utilising different catalysts but the same reactant.\nReason : The action of a catalyst is highly unselective in nature.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','A catalyst selectivity is its ability to direct a reaction to produce a specific product selectively when several products are possible under the same reaction conditions. The selectivity of several catalysts for the same reactants differs. Thus, the action of a catalyst is highly selective in nature. For example, starting with H₂ and CO, and using different catalysts, we get different products.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 132, Paragraph 5.2.2) diagram(ne1912)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190218,'chemistry','Which of the following is not the characteristic of catalyst.','Catalysts are naturally nonselective.','a) The activity of a catalyst depends upon the strength of chemisorption to a large extent.\nb) The action of a catalyst is highly selective in nature.\nc) A substance which acts as a catalyst in one reaction may fail to catalyse another reaction.\nd) Selectivity of different catalysts for same reactants is different. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 132, Paragraph 5.2.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','The strength of chemisorption affects a catalyst\'s activity.','Catalysts are naturally nonselective.','Different reactions require different catalysts.','Selectivity of several catalysts for the same reactants differs.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190219,'chemistry','Shape-selective catalysis does not depend upon :','Pore structure of product molecule.','The catalytic reaction that depends upon the pore structure of the catalyst and the size of the reactant and product molecules is called shape-selective catalysis. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 132, Paragraph 5.2.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','size of reactant.','product molecule.','Pore structure of product molecule.','Pore structure of the catalyst.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190220,'chemistry','Which among the following option(s) is true about Zeolites?','three dimensional network of silicates in which some Silicon atoms are replaced by aluminium atoms.','Zeolites are good shape-selective catalysts because of their honeycomb-like structures. They are microporous aluminosilicates with three dimensional network of silicates in which some silicon atoms are replaced by aluminium atoms giving Al–O–Si framework.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 132, Paragraph 5.2.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','three dimensional network of silicates in which some Silicon atoms are replaced by sulphur atoms.','three dimensional network of silicates in which some Silicon atoms are replaced by aluminium atoms.','two dimensional network of silicates in which some Silicon atoms are replaced by aluminium atoms.','three dimensional network of silicates in which some Sulphur atoms are replaced by aluminium atoms.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190221,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Zeolites are good shape selective catalyst.\nReason : The structure of zeolites is similar to that of honeycomb.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Zeolites are good shape-selective catalysts because of their honeycomb-like structures.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 132, Paragraph 5.2.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190222,'chemistry','Which among the following option is incorrect about Zeolites?','Zeolites are not produced naturally.','The reactions taking place in zeolites depend upon the size and shape of reactant and product molecules as well as upon the pores and cavities of the zeolites. They are found in nature as well as synthesised for catalytic selectivity. An important zeolite catalyst used in the petroleum industry is ZSM-5. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 132, Paragraph 5.2.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','ZSM 5 is an example of zeolite.','Zeolites are found naturally as well as in synthesized form.','They are porous molecules.','Zeolites are not produced naturally.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190223,'chemistry','The abbreviation ZSM 5 stands for :','Zeolite Socony Mobil 5','Zeolite Socony Mobil 5 is an abbreviation for ZSM-5. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 133, Paragraph 5.2.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Zeolite Socony Mobil 5','Zeolites Same Microphone 5','Zeolite Same Microspore 5','Zeolite Socony Microspore 5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190224,'chemistry','ZSM-5 convert alcohol directly into ______ by dehydrating them to give mixture of hydrocarbons.','gasoline','An important zeolite catalyst used in the petroleum industry is ZSM-5. It converts alcohols directly into gasoline (petrol) by dehydrating them to give a mixture of hydrocarbons.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 133, Paragraph 5.2.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','isomeric alcohol','aldehyde','acetic acid','gasoline','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190225,'chemistry','Which of the following statements describing \'Enzymes\' is correct?','Protein molecules of high molecular mass','Enzymes are complex nitrogenous organic compounds which are produced by living plants and animals. They are actually protein molecules of high molecular mass and form colloidal solutions in water.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 133, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'2','mcq','Protein molecules of low molecular mass','Simple  inorganic compound','Protein molecules of high molecular mass','Artificially synthesized only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190226,'chemistry','Enzyme form  ______  solution in water.','colloidal','Enzymes are complex nitrogenous organic compounds which are produced by living plants and animals. They are actually protein molecules of high molecular mass and form colloidal solutions in water.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 133, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','suspension','gel like','colloidal','hypertonic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190227,'chemistry','Enzymes present in living system are called ______ .','biochemical catalyst','Numerous reactions that occur in the bodies of animals and plants to maintain the life process are catalysed by enzymes. The enzymes are, thus, termed as biochemical catalysts and the phenomenon is known as biochemical catalysis.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 133, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','natural catalyst','chemical catalyst','biochemical catalyst','synthetic catalyst','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190228,'chemistry','Match the enzymes to the reactants on which they act:','Invertase-Sucrose,Zymase-Glucose,Diastase-Starch,Maltase-Maltose','Invertase converts sucrose to glucose and fructose.\nZymase converts glucose to ethyl alcohol and carbon dioxide.\nDiastase converts starch to  maltose.\nMaltase converts maltose to glucose.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 133, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'3','match','Invertase,Sucrose','Zymase,Glucose','Diastase,Starch','Maltase,Maltose','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190229,'chemistry','Match the enzymes to the reactants on which they act:','Lactase-Lactose,Lipase-Lipid,Urease-Urea,Pepsin-Protein','Lactase converts lactose to glucose and galactose.\nLipase converts lipids to fatty acids.\nUrease converts urea to ammonia and carbon dioxide.\nPepsin converts proteins to amino acids.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 133, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'3','match','Lactase,Lactose','Lipase,Lipid','Urease,Urea','Pepsin,Protein','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190230,'chemistry','Match the following enzymes with their source.','Invertase-Yeast,Zymase-Yeast,Diastase-Malt,Urease-Soybean','Yeast is the source of invertase.\nYeast is the source of zymase.\nMalt is the source of diastase.\nSoybean is a source of urease.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 133, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'3','match','Invertase,Yeast','Zymase,Yeast','Diastase,Malt','Urease,Soybean','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190231,'chemistry','Match the following enzymes with their source.','Pepsin-Stomach,Maltase-Yeast,Lactase-Curd','Stomach produces pepsin.\nYeast is the source of maltase.\nCurd is the source of lactase.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 133, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'3','match','Pepsin,Stomach','Maltase,Yeast','Lactase,Curd',NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190232,'chemistry','Diastase Convert ______ into Maltose.','Starch','Diastase converts starch to  maltose.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 133, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Starch','Galactose','Oligosaccharides','Fructose','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190233,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : One molecule of an enzyme main transform 1 million molecules of the reactant per minute.\nReason : Each enzyme is specific for a given reaction.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Enzymes are so highly efficient that one molecule of an enzyme may transform one million molecules of the reactant per minute. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 134, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190234,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Enzyme urease catalyses the hydrolysis of urea only. It does not catalyse hydrolysis of any other amide.\nReason : The rate of an enzyme reaction becomes maximum at a definite temperature, called the optimum temperature.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Highly specific nature : Each enzyme is specific for a given reaction, i.e., one catalyst cannot catalyse more than one reaction. For example, the enzyme urease catalyses the hydrolysis of urea only. It does not catalyse hydrolysis of any other amide.\nHighly active under optimum temperature : The rate of an enzyme reaction becomes maximum at a definite temperature, called the optimum temperature. On either side of the optimum temperature, the enzyme activity decreases. The optimum temperature range for enzymatic activity is 298-310K. Human body temperature being 310 K is suited for enzyme-catalysed reactions.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 134, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190235,'chemistry','The rate of an enzyme-catalysed reaction is maximum at a particular pH called optimum pH, which is between pH values  ______ .','5-7','Highly active under optimum pH : The rate of an enzyme-catalysed reaction is maximum at a particular pH called optimum pH, which is between pH values 5-7.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 134, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','4-6','5-6','5-7','6-8','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190236,'chemistry','The optimum temperature range for enzymatic activity is 298-310K. Human body temperature being ______ is suited for enzyme-catalysed reactions.','Both 310K and 37⁰C','The optimum temperature range for enzymatic activity is 298-310K. Human body temperature being 310 K is suited for enzyme-catalysed reactions.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 134, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','310K','298K','37⁰C','Both 310K and 37⁰C','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190237,'chemistry','The enzymatic activity is increased in the presence of certain substances, known as ______ .','Coenzymes','The enzymatic activity is increased in the presence of certain substances, known as coenzymes. It has been observed that when a small non-protein (vitamin) is present along with an enzyme, the catalytic activity is enhanced considerably.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 134, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Coenzymes','Poison','Vitamin','Catalyst','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/190237q.png'),
(190238,'chemistry','Co-enzyme is by its very nature :','Small,Non- Protein','The enzymatic activity is increased in the presence of certain substances, known as co-enzymes. It has been observed that when a small non-protein (vitamin) is present along with an enzyme, the catalytic activity is enhanced considerably.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 134, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'2','mcq','Small, Protein','Small,Non- Protein','Large,Non- Protein','Large, Protein','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/190238q.png'),
(190239,'chemistry','Activators are generally :','Metal ions','Activators are generally metal ions such as Na⁺, Mn²⁺, Co²⁺, Cu²⁺, etc. These metal ions, when weakly bonded to enzyme molecules, increase their catalytic activity. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 134, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Non-Metal ions','Metal ions','Protein molecules','Vitamins','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/190239q.png'),
(190240,'chemistry','Metal ions weakly bonded to enzyme molecules and  increase their catalytic activity are called :','Activators','Activators are generally metal ions such as Na⁺, Mn²⁺, Co²⁺, Cu²⁺, etc. These metal ions, when weakly bonded to enzyme molecules, increase their catalytic activity. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 134, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Activators','Co-enzymes','Vitamin','Promoters','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/190240q.png'),
(190241,'chemistry','Which ion increase the catalytic activity of Amylase.','Sodium ion','Activators are generally metal ions such as Na⁺, Mn²⁺, Co²⁺, Cu²⁺, etc. These metal ions, when weakly bonded to enzyme molecules, increase their catalytic activity. Amylase in presence of sodium chloride i.e., Na⁺ ions are catalytically very active.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 134, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Sodium ion','Cobalt ion','Manganese ion','Copper ion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190242,'chemistry','The inhibitors or poisons interact with the ______  on the enzyme surface and often reduce or completely destroy the catalytic activity of the enzymes.','Active functional groups','The inhibitors or poisons interact with the active functional groups on the enzyme surface and often reduce or completely destroy the catalytic activity of the enzymes. The use of many drugs is related to their action as enzyme inhibitors in the body.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 135, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Active functional groups','Allosteric site','Active site','Binding site','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190243,'chemistry','The correct sequence of mechanism of enzyme catalysed reaction is :\n1. Enzyme Substrate Complex.\n2. Enzyme Product Complex.\n3. Substrate attach to active site of Enzyme.\n4. Separation of Enzyme and Product.','3,1,2,4','1. Substrate attach to active site of Enzyme. \n2. Enzyme Substrate Complex.\n3. Enzyme Product Complex.\n4. Separation of Enzyme and Product.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 135, Fig 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2,4,3','1,2,3,4','3,1,2,4','2,3,4,1','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190244,'chemistry','Catalyst used in Ostwald’s process for the manufacture of nitric acid :','Platinised asbestos','Ostwald’s process for the manufacture of nitric acid. \n4NH₃ (g) + 5O₂ (g) → 4NO(g) + 6H₂O(g) \n2NO (g) + O₂ (g) → 2NO₂ (g) \n4NO₂ (g) + 2H₂O(l) + O₂ (g) → 4HNO₃ (aq)\nCatalyst : Platinised asbestos; optimum temperature 573K\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 135, Table 5.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','Finely divided iron','Platinised asbestos','Vanadium Pentoxide','manganese ion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190245,'chemistry','Why is it necessary to remove CO when ammonia is obtained by Haber’s process?','CO act as poison','CO must be removed when ammonia is manufactured using Haber\'s process because CO is a poison that inhibits the activity of the iron catalyst used in the process.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 135, Intext questions 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','CO act as Inhibitor','CO act as poison','CO act as Coenzyme','CO act as Reactant for further reaction','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190246,'chemistry','Why is the ester hydrolysis slow in the beginning and becomes faster after sometime?','Acid acts as an autocatalyst.','Ester hydrolysis can be expressed as : \nEster + Water → Acid + Alcohol\nThe acid formed during the reaction works as a catalyst, speeding up the reaction. Substances that serve as catalysts in the same reaction in which they are produced as products are known as autocatalysts.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 135, Intext questions 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','The reaction rate remains constant during the reaction.','Acid acts as an autocatalyst.','Alcohol acts as an autocatalyst.','Ester itself act as an autocatalyst.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190247,'chemistry','The role of desorption in the process of catalysis is :','making the surface of the catalyst free for new adsorption.','The role of desorption in the catalysis process is to clear the surface of the solid catalyst for new adsorption of reactants on the surface.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 135, Intext questions 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Catalysis',NULL,'1','mcq','to obtain the product.','to cause the product molecule to bind to another enzyme.','making the surface of the catalyst free for new adsorption.','making the surface of the catalyst free for new absorption.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190248,'biology','The first step in urine formation is the filtration of blood, which is carried out by the glomerulus and is called...','Glomerular filtrate','The first step in urine formation is the filtration of blood, which is carried out by the glomerulus and is called Gloemrular filtrate. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 293','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','Bowman\'s filtrate','Glomerular filtrate','Loop filtrate','JG filtrate','NEW','11-293','11-208',NULL),
(190249,'biology','Glomerular filtrate contains all of the following except? \ni. Glucose \nii. Water \niii. Urea \niv. Plasma proteins','iv only','Blood is filtered so finely through these membranes, that almost all the constituents of the plasma except the proteins pass onto the lumen of the Bowman’s capsule. Therefore, it is considered as a process of ultra filtration. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 293','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'3','mcq','Both i and ii','iii only','Both ii and iii','iv only','NEW','11-293','11-208',NULL),
(190250,'biology','Which of the following is/are correct regarding podocytes? \n\ni.They are epithelial cells \n\nii. They are arranged in intricate manner \n\niii. They form slit pores \n\niv. They are secretory cells','i ii and iii','The epithelial cells of Bowman’s capsule called podocytes are arranged in an intricate manner so as to leave some minute spaces called filtration slits or slit pores. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 293','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'3','mcq','i ii and iii','Both ii and iii','Both i and iii','Both ii and iv','NEW','11-293','11-208',NULL),
(190251,'biology','Podocytes are....','Epithelial cells','The epithelial cells of Bowman’s capsule called podocytes are arranged in an intricate manner so as to leave some minute spaces called filtration slits or slit pores.Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 293','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','Connective tissue cells','Fibrocytes','Epithelial cells','Muscle cells','NEW','11-293','11-208',NULL),
(190252,'biology','The nutrients are reabsorbed mainly through which portion of Nephron?','PCT','Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 295 Figure 19.5','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','PCT','DCT','Loop of Henle','Collecting duct','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(190253,'biology','H₂O is reabsorbed mainly through which portion of Loop of Henle?','Descending limb','Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 295 Figure 19.5','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','Thin ascending limb','Thick ascending limb','Descending limb','Through all parts of Loop of Henle','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(190254,'biology','Urea is reabsorbed through which portion of Nephron?','Thin ascending limb','Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 295 Figure 19.5','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','PCT','DCT','Descending limb','Thin ascending limb','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(190255,'biology','Which of the following is Incorrect regarding Renal Calculus?','They are mainly comprised of non-crystallised salts','Renal calculi: Stone or insoluble mass of crystallised salts (oxalates, etc.) formed within the kidney. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 299','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','They are insoluble masses','They are formed in Kidneys','They are mainly comprised of non-crystallised salts','Oxalate stones is an example of renal calculus','NEW','11-299','11-214',NULL),
(190256,'biology','Which of the following is Incorrect regarding Counter current Multiplier?','It is involved in Dilution of urine','Transport of substances facilitated by the special arrangement of Henle’s loop and vasa recta is called the counter current mechanism (Figure. 19.6). This mechanism helps to maintain a concentration gradient. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','It eastablishes Concentration gradient','It is present in Vasa recta and Loop of Henle','It is involved in Dilution of urine','It facilitates the movement of water and salts','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(190257,'biology','The portion of Nephron extending towards Medullary region?','Loop Of Henle','The Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT of the nephron are situated in the cortical region of the kidney whereas the loop of Henle dips into the medulla.Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 292','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','PCT','Loop Of Henle','DCT','Collecting ducts','NEW','11-292','11-207',NULL),
(190258,'biology','Afferent arteriole is formed by the branching of which artery?','Renal artery','Glomerulus is a tuft of capillaries formed by the afferent arteriole – a fine branch of renal artery.Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 292','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','It is direct branch of aorta','Vesicular artery','Renal artery','Testicular artery','NEW','11-292','11-207',NULL),
(190259,'biology','Blood is carried away from the Glomerulus by?','Efferent arteriole','Blood from the glomerulus is carried away by an efferent arteriole. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 292','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','Afferent arteriole','Efferent arteriole','Efferent venule','Afferent venule','NEW','11-292','11-207',NULL),
(190260,'biology','Which of the following is Mismatched?','Malpighian tubules: Annelids','Protonephridia or flame cells are the excretory structures in Platyhelminthes (Flatworms, e.g., Planaria), rotifers, some annelids and the cephalochordate – Amphioxus. Protonephridia are primarily concerned with ionic and fluid volume regulation, i.e., osmoregulation. Nephridia are the tubular excretory structures of earthworms and other annelids. Nephridia help to remove nitrogenous wastes and maintain a fluid and ionic balance. Malpighian tubules are the excretory structures of most of the insects. Reference  : NCERT Page 290','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','Protonephridia: Flatworms','Nephridia: Earthworms','Malpighian tubules: Annelids','Nephron: Human','NEW','11-290','11-205',NULL),
(190261,'biology','Green glands are also called....','Antennal glands','Antennal glands or green glands perform the excretory function in crustaceans like prawns. Reference  : NCERT Page 290','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','Antennal glands','Adrenal glands','Mammilary bodies','Pituitry gland','NEW','11-290','11-205',NULL),
(190262,'biology','Which of the following is not a charcteristic of Kidneys?','Situated between the levels of first thoracic and 5th lumbar vertebra','Kidneys are reddish brown, bean shaped structures situated between the levels of last thoracic and third lumbar vertebra close to the dorsal inner wall of the abdominal cavity. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 291','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','Kidneys are reddish brown,','Bean shaped structures','Situated between the levels of first thoracic and 5th lumbar vertebra','Lie close to the dorsal inner wall of the abdominal cavity','NEW','11-291','11-206',NULL),
(121312,'chemistry','Which among the following represents the structure of the compound whose IUPAC name is 3-ethyl-2-hydroxy-4-methylhex-3-en-5-ynoic acid in the given diagram(12116)','D','Carbon from COOH group gets first number and then we start the counting according to lowest locant rule. (NEET 2013)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-342','11-264','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121312q.png'),
(190263,'biology','Mark the Incorrect statement.','The PCTs continue as highly coiled collecting ducts','The DCTs of many nephrons open into a straight tube called collecting duct, many of which converge and open into the renal pelvis through medullary pyramids in the calyces. \nThe DCTs continue as highly coiled collecting ducts\nReference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System',NULL,'2','mcq','The DCTs of many nephrons open into a straight tube called collecting duct','Many of collecting ducts converge and open into the renal pelvis','Collecting ducts open into the renal pelvis through medullary pyramids in the calyces.','The PCTs continue as highly coiled collecting ducts','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(190301,'chemistry','The particle sizes in a colloidal system range from :','10⁻⁹ to 10⁻⁶ m','Colloidal particles are larger than simple molecules but small enough to remain suspended. Their range of diameters is between 1 and 1000 nm (10⁻⁹ to 10⁻⁶ m).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136, Paragraph 5.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁻⁹ to 10⁻¹² m','10⁻⁹ to 10⁻⁶ m','10⁻⁴ to 10⁻¹⁰ m','10⁻⁵ to 10⁻⁷ m','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190302,'chemistry','A Colloid is a :','heterogeneous system.','A colloid is a heterogeneous system in which one substance is dispersed (dispersed phase) as very fine particles in another substance called dispersion medium.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136, Paragraph 5.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','homogeneous system.','heterogeneous system.','single phase system','multi phase system','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190303,'chemistry','Depending upon the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium. How many type of Colloidal systems are there :','8','Depending upon whether the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium are solids, liquids or gases, eight types of colloidal systems are possible. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136, Paragraph 5.4.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','7','8','9','10','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190304,'chemistry','Fog is an example of :','Liquid dispersed in gas.','Fog is an example of liquid dispersed in a gaseous medium as an aerosol.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136, Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Solid dispersed in gas.','Liquid dispersed in gas.','Liquid dispersed in Solid.','Solid dispersed in liquid.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190305,'chemistry','Cod liver oil is :','Water dispersed in fat','There are two types of emulsions.\n(i) Oil dispersed in water (O/W type) and\n(ii) Water dispersed in oil (W/O type).\nIn the first system, water acts as dispersion medium. Examples of this type of emulsion are milk and vanishing cream. In milk, liquid fat is dispersed in water. In the second system, oil acts as dispersion medium. Common examples of this type are butter and cream.Cod liver oil is another example.\nCod liver oil is a nutritional supplement made from codfish liver. It contains the omega-3 fatty acids eicosapentaenoic acid and docosahexaenoic acid, similar to other fish oils.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145; Paragraph 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Fat dispersed in water','Water dispersed in fat','Liquid dispersed in solid','Fat dispersed in fat','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190306,'chemistry','Vanishing cream is an example of :','Emulsion','There are two types of emulsions.\n(i) Oil dispersed in water (O/W type) and\n(ii) Water dispersed in oil (W/O type).\nIn the first system, water acts as dispersion medium. Examples of this type of emulsion are milk and vanishing cream. In milk, liquid fat is dispersed in water. Vanishing cream is an oil in water (O in W) type emulsion.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145; Paragraph 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Emulsion','Foam','Water dispersed in oil','Suspension','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190307,'chemistry','Alcosol is colloidal system is a system :','in which dispersion medium is alcohol.','If the dispersion medium is water, the sol is called aquasol or hydrosol and if the dispersion medium is alcohol, it is called alcosol. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','in which alcohol dispersed in a gas.','in which a gas dispersed in water.','in which dispersion medium is benzene.','in which dispersion medium is alcohol.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190308,'chemistry','The dispersion medium in Gel is :','Solid','Gel is a liquid dispersed in a solid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Solid','Liquid','Gas','Water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190309,'chemistry','Liquid-Liquid colloids, are known as :','Emulsion','Liquid in liquid colloids are called as emulsion.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Aerosols','Emulsion','Foam','Gels','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190310,'chemistry','Cheese is a colloidal system of _____.','liquid dispersed in solid','Cheese is a colloidal system of liquid dispersed in solid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','liquid dispersed in solid','solid dispersed in liquid','gas dispersed in solid','solid dispersed in gas','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190311,'chemistry','Which of the following cannot be included in the category of colloids?','Brine','Blood, milk and latex are examples of naturally occurring colloids. Synthetic latex is also an example of a colloidal material.\nBrine is not a colloid as salt completely dissolves in water.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146-147; Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Milk','Blood','Latex','Brine','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190312,'chemistry','Mist is a colloidal system of :','liquid dispersed in gas.','Mist is a colloidal system of liquid dispersed in gas.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','solid dispersed in gas.','liquid dispersed in gas.','liquid dispersed in Solid.','solid dispersed in liquid.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190313,'chemistry','Insecticide spray is a colloidal system of :','liquid dispersed in gas.','Insecticide spray is a colloidal system of liquid dispersed in gas.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','solid dispersed in gas.','liquid dispersed in gas.','liquid dispersed in Solid.','solid dispersed in liquid.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190314,'chemistry','Cloud is a colloidal system of :','liquid dispersed in gas.','Cloud is a colloidal system of liquid dispersed in gas.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','solid dispersed in gas.','liquid dispersed in gas.','liquid dispersed in Solid.','solid dispersed in liquid.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190315,'chemistry','Butter is a colloidal system of :','liquid dispersed in liquid','Butter is a colloidal system of liquid dispersed in solid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','liquid dispersed in liquid','liquid dispersed in gas.','gas dispersed in solid.','solid dispersed in gas.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190316,'chemistry','Jellies is a colloidal system of :','liquid dispersed in solid.','Jellies is a colloidal system of liquid dispersed in solid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','liquid dispersed in solid.','liquid dispersed in gas.','gas dispersed in solid.','solid dispersed in gas.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190317,'chemistry','The dispersion medium in Aerosol is _____.','Gas','The dispersion medium in Aerosol is gas.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Solid','Liquid','Gas','Water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190318,'chemistry','The dispersion medium in Sol _____.','Liquid','The dispersion medium in Sol is liquid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Solid','Liquid','Gas','Water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190319,'chemistry','The dispersion medium in Solid sol _____.','Solid','The dispersion medium in solid sol is solid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Solid','Liquid','Gas','Water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190320,'chemistry','The dispersion medium in Emulsion _____.','Liquid','The dispersion medium in emulsion is liquid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Solid','Liquid','Gas','Water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190321,'chemistry','The dispersion medium in Foam _____.','Liquid','The dispersion medium in foam is liquid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Solid','Liquid','Gas','Water','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190322,'chemistry','Mist is an example of _____.','Aerosols','Mist is an example of aerosol colloidal system of liquid dispersed in gas.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Aerosols','Emulsions','Foam','Gels','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190323,'chemistry','Cloud is an example of _____.','Aerosols','Cloud is an example of aerosol colloidal system of liquid dispersed in gas.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Aerosols','Emulsions','Foam','Gels','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190401,'chemistry','Match the following :','Fog-Aerosol, Milk-Emulsion, Cheese-GelSoap, Soap leather-Foam','Fog is an example of aerosol colloidal system.\nMilk is an example of emulsion colloidal system.\nCheese is an example of gel colloidal system.\nSoap leather is an example of foam colloidal system.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136; Table 5.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'3','match','Fog,Aerosol','Milk,Emulsion','Cheese,Gel','Soap leather, Foam','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190402,'chemistry','Depending upon the nature of interaction between the dispersed phase\nand the dispersion medium, colloidal sols are divided into two categories,\nnamely ______ & ______ .','lyophilic, lyophobic','Depending upon the nature of interaction between the dispersed phase and the dispersion medium, colloidal sols are divided into two categories, namely, lyophilic (solvent attracting) and lyophobic (solvent repelling).\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','aquasol, alcosol','lyophilic, lyophobic','lipophilic, lipophobic','hydrophilic, hydrophobic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190403,'chemistry','Mark the true option regarding lyophilic colloid :\n1. Reversible sols\n2. Irreversible souls\n3. Not easily coagulated\n4. Stable\n5. Inorganic in nature','1, 3 and 4','Lyophilic colloids : The word ‘lyophilic’ means liquid-loving. Colloidal sols directly formed by mixing substances like gum, gelatine, starch, rubber, etc., with a suitable liquid (the dispersion medium) are called lyophilic sols. These sols are also called reversible sols. Furthermore, these sols are quite stable and cannot be easily coagulated.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'3','mcq','2, 3 and 5','1, 3 and 4','2, 3 and 4','Both 1 and 5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190404,'chemistry','Which of the Following lyophilic colloid :','starch sol.','Lyophilic colloids : The word ‘lyophilic’ means liquid-loving. Colloidal sols directly formed by mixing substances like gum, gelatine, starch, rubber, etc., with a suitable liquid (the dispersion medium) are called lyophilic sols.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','starch sol.','platinum sol.','Fe(OH)₃ sol.','sulphide sol.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190405,'chemistry','Which of the Following lyophobic colloid _____.','metals','Lyophobic colloids : The word ‘lyophobic’ means liquid-hating. Substances like metals, their sulphides, etc., when simply mixed with the dispersion medium do not form the colloidal sol. Their colloidal sols can be prepared only by special methods. Such sols are called lyophobic sols.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','gelatin','starch','rubber','metals','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190406,'chemistry','______  sols need stabilising agents for their preservation.','Lyophobic','Lyophobic sols are readily precipitated (or coagulated) on the addition of small amounts of electrolytes, by heating or by shaking and hence, are not stable. Further, once precipitated, they do not give back the colloidal sol by simple addition of the dispersion medium. Hence, these sols are also called irreversible sols. Lyophobic sols need stabilising agents for their preservation.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Aerosol','Lyophilic','Lyophobic','Aquasol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190407,'chemistry','Lyophobic sols are readily precipitated (or coagulated) on the addition of small amounts of  ______  by heating or by shaking.','Electrolytes','Lyophobic sols are readily precipitated (or coagulated) on the addition of small amounts of electrolytes, by heating or by shaking and hence, are not stable. Further, once precipitated, they do not give back the colloidal sol by simple addition of the dispersion medium. Hence, these sols are also called irreversible sols. Lyophobic sols need stabilising agents for their preservation.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Lyophilic Colloid','Electrolytes','Catalyst','Inhibitor','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190408,'chemistry','Depending upon the type of the particles of the dispersed phase, which of the following is not a type of colloids :','Lyophilic colloids.','Depending upon the type of the particles of the dispersed phase, colloids are classified as : multimolecular, macromolecular and associated colloids.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Multimolecular colloids.','Macromolecular colloids.','Associated colloids.','Lyophilic colloids.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190409,'chemistry','A large number of atoms or smaller molecules of a substance aggregate together to form  ______   of colloidal range (1–1000 nm).','Multimolecular colloids.','On dissolution, a large number of atoms or smaller molecules of a substance aggregate together to form species having size in the colloidal range (1–1000 nm). The species thus formed are called multimolecular colloids.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Multimolecular colloids.','Macromolecular colloids.','Associated colloids.','Lyophilic colloids.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190410,'chemistry','Macromolecules in suitable solvents form solutions in which the size of the macromolecules may be in colloidal range are called :','Macromolecular colloids.','Macromolecules in suitable solvents form solutions in which the size of the macromolecules may be in the colloidal range. Such systems are called macromolecular colloids.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137-138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Multimolecular colloids.','Macromolecular colloids.','Associated colloids.','Lyophilic colloids.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190411,'chemistry','There are some substances which at low concentrations behave as normal strong electrolytes, but at higher concentrations exhibit colloidal behaviour due to the formation of aggregates. The aggregated particles thus formed are called micelles. These are also known as :','Associated colloids.','There are some substances which at low concentrations behave as normal strong electrolytes, but at higher concentrations exhibit colloidal behaviour due to the formation of aggregates. The aggregated particles thus formed are called micelles. These are also known as associated colloids.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Multimolecular colloids.','Macromolecular colloids.','Associated colloids.','Lyophilic colloids.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190412,'chemistry','There are some substances which  at low concentrations behave as normal strong electrolytes, but at higher concentrations exhibit colloidal behaviour due to the formation of aggregates. Aggregated particles are called  ______ .','Micelles','There are some substances which at low concentrations behave as normal strong electrolytes, but at higher concentrations exhibit colloidal behaviour due to the formation of aggregates. The aggregated particles thus formed are called micelles. These are also known as associated colloids.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'2','mcq','Micelles','Macromolecular colloids','Multimolecular colloids','Lyophilic colloids','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190413,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of multimolecular colloids?','Sulphur sol','Following are the examples of multimolecular colloids. a gold sol may contain particles of various sizes having many atoms. Sulphur sol consists of particles containing a thousand or more of S₈ sulphur molecules.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Gold sol','Platinum sol','Sulphur sol','Aqueous enzyme sol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190414,'chemistry','Which of the following is not an example of macromolecular colloids?','gold sol, sulphur sol','Examples of naturally occurring macromolecules are starch, cellulose, proteins and enzymes; and those of man-made macromolecules are polythene, nylon, polystyrene, synthetic rubber, etc.\nGold and sulphur sols are an example of multicellular colloid.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','starch, cellulose','proteins, nylon','polystyrene, synthetic rubber','gold sol, sulphur sol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190415,'chemistry','Which of the following is an example of Association colloids.','soaps, synthetic detergents','A common example of an associated colloid is soaps and synthetic detergents. Multimolecular colloids include sulphur sols. Starch, cellulose, polystyrene and synthetic rubber are the examples of macromolecular sols.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','starch, cellulose','polystyrene, synthetic rubber','Sulphur sol','soaps, synthetic detergents','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190416,'chemistry','The formation of micelles takes place only above a particular temperature called _____.','Kraft temperature','The formation of micelles takes place only above a particular temperature called Kraft temperature (Tₖ ) and above a particular concentration called critical micelle concentration (CMC). \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Curie\'s temperature','Kraft temperature','Critical temperature','Optimum temperature','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190417,'chemistry','The formation of micelles takes place only above a particular concentration called _____.','Critical micelle concentration','The formation of micelles takes place only above a particular temperature called Kraft temperature (Tₖ ) and above a particular concentration called critical micelle concentration (CMC). \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 138; Paragraph 5.4.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Classification of Colloids',NULL,'1','mcq','Threshold Concentration','Critical micelle concentration','Kraft Micelles concentration','Equivalance concentration','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/190417q.png'),
(190501,'chemistry','The nature of catalyst in the given reaction is :\ndiagram(n1904)','Heterogenous','Heterogeneous catalysis refers to the catalytic process in which the reactants and catalyst are in different phases. In the given reaction, the reactants are gaseous, whereas the catalyst is solid. The catalyst is hence heterogeneous in nature.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 131; Paragraph 5.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Homogenous','Heterogenous','Enzymatic','Biochemical','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190501q.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(190502,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Emulsions of oil in water are unstable and sometimes they separate into two layers on standing. \nReson : A third component called emulsifying agent is usually added to stabilize the emulsion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Emulsions of oil in water are unstable and sometimes they separate into two layers on standing. For stabilisation of an emulsion, a third component called emulsifying agent is usually added. The emulsifying agent forms an interfacial film between suspended particles and the medium. The principal emulsifying agents for O/W emulsions are proteins, gums, natural and synthetic soaps, etc., and for W/O, heavy metal salts of fatty acids, long chain alcohols, lampblack, etc.  \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145, Paragraph 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190503,'chemistry','Emulsions of oil in water are unstable and sometimes they separate Into two layers on standing to prevent this we use :','Emulsifying agents','Emulsions of oil in water are unstable and sometimes they separate into two layers on standing. For stabilisation of an emulsion, a third component called emulsifying agent is usually added. The emulsifying agent forms an interfacial film between suspended particles and the medium. The principal emulsifying agents for O/W emulsions are proteins, gums, natural and synthetic soaps, etc., and for W/O, heavy metal salts of fatty acids, long chain alcohols, lampblack, etc.  \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145, Paragraph 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Water','Emulsifying agents','Metal ions','Electrolytes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190504,'chemistry','Which of the following is not an emulsifying agent :','Electrolytes','The principal emulsifying agents for O/W emulsions are proteins, gums, natural and synthetic soaps, etc., and for W/O, heavy metal salts of fatty acids, long chain alcohols, lampblack, etc. \nSince electrolyte is not an emulsifying agent in this situation, it is the correct option. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145, Paragraph 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Protein','Long Chain alcohol','Soap','Electrolytes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190505,'chemistry','Lampblack is :','Emulsifier','The principal emulsifying agents for O/W emulsions are proteins, gums, natural and synthetic soaps, etc., and for W/O, heavy metal salts of fatty acids, long chain alcohols, lampblack, etc.  \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145, Paragraph 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Emulsion','Emulsifier','Colloid','Protein','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190506,'chemistry','Emulsion shows :','Tyndall effect','Emulsions show Brownian movement and Tyndall effect. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145, Paragraph 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Filtration','Zeta potential','Tyndall effect','Resonance','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190507,'chemistry','Emulsions cannot be separated into their constituent liquids by :','Grinding','Emulsions can be broken into constituent liquids by heating, freezing, centrifuging, etc.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145, Paragraph 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'2','mcq','Heating','Freezing','Configuring','Grinding','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190508,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Blue colour of the sky.\nReason : Dust particles along with water suspended in air scatter blue light which reaches our eyes and the sky looks blue to us.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Dust particles along with water suspended in air scatter blue light which reaches our eyes and the sky looks blue to us.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190509,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Fog and smoke continuously float in air.\nReason : Clouds are aerosols having small droplets of water suspended in air.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','When a huge mass of air containing dust particles is cooled below its dew point, moisture from the air condenses on the surfaces of these particles, generating small droplets. Because these droplets are colloidal in nature, they continue to float in the air as mist or fog. Clouds are aerosols composed of tiny water droplets suspended in air. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190510,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : It is possible to cause artificial rain by throwing electrified sand or spraying a sol carrying charge opposite to the one on clouds from an aeroplane.\nReason : Rain is colloidal in nature.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Because rain is Colloidal in Nature, it is possible to cause artificial rain by throwing electrified sand or spraying a sol carrying charge opposite to the one on clouds from an aeroplane.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190511,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Sometimes, the rainfall occurs when two oppositely charged clouds meet.\nReason : Rain being colloidal in nature when opposite charge come in contact,  it destroy the colloid.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','When a huge mass of air containing dust particles is cooled below its dew point, moisture from the air condenses on the surfaces of these particles, generating small droplets. Because these droplets are colloidal in nature, they continue to float in the air as mist or fog. Clouds are aerosols composed of tiny water droplets suspended in air. Because of condensation in the high atmosphere, colloidal droplets of water become larger and larger in size until they fall as rain. Rainfall can occur when two oppositely charged clouds collide. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190512,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Blood in contact with ferric chloride and alum it coagulate.\nReason :  Blood is a true solution.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Blood is a colloidal solution of an albuminoid substance. The\nstyptic action of alum and ferric chloride solution is due to\ncoagulation of blood forming a clot which stops further bleeding.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190513,'chemistry','Which of the following are colloids :','Air','‣ Milk, butter, halwa, ice creams, fruit juices, etc., are all colloids in one form or the other. \n‣ Blood is a colloidal solution of an albuminoid substance.\n‣ Air is a mixture of many particles and gases. Because a colloid is a homogeneous system, air is not a colloid.  \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Blood','Halwa','Ice cream','Air','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190514,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : When river meets the sea water it forms delta.\nReason : Sea water contains electrolyte and river water is colloid.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','River water is a colloidal solution of clay. Sea water contains a number of electrolytes. When river water meets the sea water, the electrolytes present in sea water coagulate the colloidal solution of clay resulting in its deposition with the formation of delta.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190515,'chemistry','Fertile soils are colloidal in nature in which ______ acts as a protective colloid.','Humus','Fertile soils are colloidal in nature in which humus acts as a protective colloid. On account of colloidal nature, soils adsorb moisture and nourishing materials.   \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Alum','Fertilizer','Humus','Silica gel','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/190515q.png'),
(190516,'chemistry','Which among the following is not an application of colloids?','Industrial preparation of hydrochloric acid','Applications of colloids :\n(i) Electrical precipitation of smoke.\n(ii) Purification of drinking water.\n(iii) Medicines.\n(iv) Tanning of leather.\n(v) Cleansing action of soaps and detergents.\n(vi) Photographic plates and films.\n(vii) Rubber industry.\n(viii) Industrial products : Paints, inks, synthetic plastics, rubber,\ngraphite lubricants, cement, etc., are all colloidal solutions. \nHydrochloric acid is a chemically manufactured true solution. Hence, it is not a colloidal application.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrical precipitation of smoke','Purification of drinking water','Medicines','Industrial preparation of hydrochloric acid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190517,'chemistry','Which among the following is an application of colloids?','Photographic plates and films','➤ Photographic plates or films are prepared by coating an emulsion of the light sensitive silver bromide in gelatin over glass plates or celluloid films.\n➤ The homogeneous nature of organic salts, electrolytes, and propanol makes them all true solutions.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Preparation of organic salts','Photographic plates and films','Laboratory preparation of electrolytes','synthesis of propanol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190518,'chemistry','Which among the following options is correct regarding " Cottrell precipitator";','smoke particles get precipitated on it.','Smoke is a colloidal solution of solid particles such as carbon, arsenic compounds, dust, etc., in air. The smoke, before it comes out from the chimney, is led through a chamber containing plates having a charge opposite to that carried by smoke particles. The particles on coming in contact with these plates lose their charge and get precipitated. The particles thus settle down on the floor of the chamber. The precipitator is called Cottrell precipitator.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','water molecules get precipitated on it.','oxygen molecules get precipitated on it.','smoke particles get precipitated on it.','air particles get precipitated on it.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190519,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Smoke precipitated on Cottrell precipitator.\nRason : Smoke being colloid in nature contain charge and Cottrell precipitator also contain similar charge.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Smoke is a colloidal solution of solid particles such as carbon, arsenic compounds, dust, etc., in air. The smoke, before it comes out from the chimney, is led through a chamber containing plates having a charge opposite to that carried by smoke particles. The particles on coming in contact with these plates lose their charge and get precipitated. The particles thus settle down on the floor of the chamber. The precipitator is called Cottrell precipitator. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190520,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following Assertion(A) and Reason(R).\nAssertion : Alum is added to water to purify it.\nReason : Alum coagulate suspended impurities.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The water obtained from natural sources often contains suspended impurities. Alum is added to such water to coagulate the suspended impurities and make water fit for drinking purposes.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190521,'chemistry','Argyrol is a :','Silver sol','Most of the medicines are colloidal in nature. For example, argyrol is a silver sol used as an eye lotion.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Silver sol','Gold sol','Pt sol','Sulphur sol','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190522,'chemistry','Argyrol is used as :','Eye Lotion','Most of the medicines are colloidal in nature. For example, argyrol is a silver sol used as an eye lotion.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Body Lotion','Eye Lotion','Hair Cream','Use to treat Cystic fibrosis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190523,'chemistry','Colloidal antimony is used in curing -','Kala Azar','Colloidal antimony is used in curing kala azar.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Kala Azar','Pneumonia','Tuberculosis','Syphilis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190524,'chemistry','Colloidal  ______ is used for intramuscular injection.','Gold','Colloidal gold is used for intramuscular injection. \nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Gold','Silver','Platinum','Cu','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190525,'chemistry','Milk of magnesia, an emulsion, is used for-','Stomach disorder','Milk of magnesia, an emulsion, is used for stomach disorders.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 146, Paragraph 5.6)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Emulsion',NULL,'1','mcq','Stomach disorder','Brain disorder','Heart disorder','Tooth disorder','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190601,'chemistry','The correct option representing a Freundlich adsorption isotherm is :','x/m=kp⁰.³','Freundlich adsorption isotherm equation is : \nx/m=kp¹/ⁿ\nThe value of 1/n lies in between 0 to 1.\n(Odisha NEET 2019)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','x/m=kp⁰.³','x/m=kp⁻¹','x/m=kp⁻⁰.⁵','x/m=kp².⁵','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190602,'chemistry','Which among the following characteristics is associated with adsorption?','ΔG, ΔH and ΔS all are negative.','As the molecules of the adsorbate are held on the surface of the solid adsorbent, entropy decreases i.e., ΔS = –ve. \nAs ΔG = ΔH – TΔS \nFor the adsorption to occur, ΔG = –ve and it is possible only if ΔH = –ve.\n(NEET 2016)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','ΔG is negative but ΔH and ΔS are positive.','ΔG, ΔH and ΔS all are negative.','ΔG and ΔS are negative but ΔH is positive.','ΔG is negative but ΔH andΔS are positive.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190603,'chemistry','In Freundlich adsorption isotherm, the value of 1/n is :','between 0 and 1 in all cases','Freundlich adsorption isotherm equation is : \nx/m=kp¹/ⁿ\nThe value of 1/n lies in between 0 to 1.\n(NEET 2012)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','between 0 and 1 in all cases','between 2 and 4 in all cases','1 in case of physical absorption','1 in case of chemisorption.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190604,'chemistry','If x is amount of adsorbate and m is amount of adsorbent, which of the following relations is not related to adsorption process?','x/m= p×T','x/m= p×T is the incorrect relation. \nThe correct relation is amount of absorption x/m ∝ P/T. \n(NEET 2011)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','x/m = f (p) at constant T','x/m = f (T) at constant p','p = f (T) at constant (x/m)','x/m= p×T','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190605,'chemistry','The Langmuir adsorption isotherm is deduced using the assumption :','the adsorption sites are equivalent in their ability to adsorb the particles','Langmuir adsorption isotherm is based on the assumption that every adsorption site is equivalent and that the ability of a particle to bind there is independent of whether nearby sites are occupied or not occupied.\n(NEET 2007)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','the adsorption sites are equivalent in their ability to adsorb the particles','the heat of adsorption varies with coverage','the adsorbed molecules interact with each other','the adsorption takes place in multilayers.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190606,'chemistry','A plot of log(x/m) versus log P for the adsorption of a gas on a solid gives a straight line with slope equal to :','1/n','Slope equals 1/n.\nThis is according to the Freundlich adsorption isotherm.\n(NEET 2006) diagram(ne1913)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','log k','-logk','n','1/n','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190607,'chemistry','Which is not correct regarding the adsorption of a gas on surface of a solid?','On increasing temperature adsorption increases continuously.','Adsorption is the ability of a substance to concentrate or hold gases, liquids or dissolved substances upon its surface. Solids adsorb greater amounts of substances at lower temperature. In general, adsorption decreases with increase in temperature.\n(NEET 2001)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','On increasing temperature adsorption increases continuously.','Enthalpy and entropy change is negative.','Adsorption is more for some specific substance.','It is a reversible reaction.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190608,'chemistry','Which among the following statements is not correct?','The value of equilibrium constant is changed in the presence of a catalyst in the reaction at equilibrium.','Catalyst does not change the value of equilibrium constant as they affect forward as well as backward reactions equally.\n(NEET 2017)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','The value of equilibrium constant is changed in the presence of a catalyst in the reaction at equilibrium.','Enzymes catalyse mainly biochemical reactions.','Coenzymes increase the catalytic activity of enzyme.','Catalyst does not initiate any reaction.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190609,'chemistry','Which among the following statements is incorrect about enzyme catalysis?','Enzymes are least reactive at optimum \ntemperature.','The enzyme activity rises rapidly with temperature and becomes maximum at definite temperature, called optimum temperature.\n(NEET 2012 )','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Enzymes are mostly proteinous in nature.','Enzyme action is specific.','Enzymes are denatured by ultraviolet rays and \nat high temperature.','Enzymes are least reactive at optimum \ntemperature.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190610,'chemistry','The enzyme which hydrolyses triglycerides to fatty acids and glycerol is called :','Lipase','(NEET 2004) diagram(ne1914)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Maltase','Lipase','Zymase','Pepsin','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190611,'chemistry','According to the adsorption theory of catalysis, the speed of the reaction increases because :','adsorption lowers the activation energy of the reaction','Adsorption causes decrease in surface energy which appears as heat. Thus, adsorption is an exothermic process and hence lowers the activation energy of the reaction.\n(NEET 2003)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','the concentration of reactant molecules at the active centres of the catalyst becomes high due to adsorption','in the process of adsorption, the activation energy of the molecules becomes large','adsorption produces heat which increases the speed of the reaction','adsorption lowers the activation energy of the reaction','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190612,'chemistry','In colloidal state, particle size ranges from-A colloidal system has particles of which of the following size?','10⁻⁶m to 10⁻⁹m','Particle size of colloids lies in the range of 10⁻⁶ m to 10⁻⁹ m. Particles themselves are invisible even under the most powerful microscope.\n(NEET 1996)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁻⁹m to 10⁻¹²m','10⁻⁶m to 10⁻⁹m','10⁻⁴m to 10⁻¹⁰m','10⁻⁵m to 10⁻⁷m','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190613,'chemistry','Measuring zeta potential is useful in determining which property of colloidal solution?','Stability of the colloidal particles','Measuring zeta potential is useful in determining stability of the colloidal particles.\n(NEET 2020)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Viscosity','Solubility','Stability of the colloidal particles','Size of the colloidal particles','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190614,'chemistry','Which mixture of the solutions will lead to the formation of negatively charged colloidal [AgI]I⁻sol?','50 mL of 1 M AgNO₃ + 50 mL of 1.5 M KI','If colloidal sol of AgI is prepared by adding KI solution to AgNO₃ till KI is in slight excess, iodide ion (I⁻) will be adsorbed on the surface of AgI thereby, giving  a negative charge to the sol.\nAgI + I⁻ (From KI) →  AgI : I⁻ (Negative sol)\n(NEET 2019)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','50 mL of 0.1 M AgNO₃ + 50 mL of 0.1 M KI','50 mL of 1 M AgNO₃ + 50 mL of 1.5 M KI','50 mL of 1 M AgNO₃ + 50 mL of 0.2 M KI','50 mL of 2 M AgNO₃ + 50 mL of 1.5 M KI','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190615,'chemistry','On which of the following properties does the coagulating power of an ion depend?','Both magnitude and sign of the charge on the ion','According to Hardy-Schulze rule, the coagulating power of an electrolyte depends on both magnitude and sign of the charge of the effective ion or electrolyte.\n(NEET 2018)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','The magnitude of the charge on the ion alone','Size of the ion alone','Both magnitude and sign of the charge on the ion','The sign of charge on the ion alone','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190616,'chemistry','The coagulation values in millimoles per litre of the electrolytes used for the coagulation of As₂S₃ are given below :  \nI. (NaCl) = 52, \nII. (BaCl₂) = 0.69,\nIII. (MgSO₄) = 0.22','III > II > I','Coagulating power ∝ 1/Coagulation value\nLower the coagulation value, higher is the coagulating power so, the correct order is : MgSO₄ > BaCl₂ > NaCl\n(NEET-II 2016)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'3','mcq','I > II > III','II > I > III','III > II > I','III > I > II','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190617,'chemistry','Fog is a colloidal solution of :','liquid in gas','Fog is an example of aerosol in which dispersed phase is liquid and dispersion medium is gas.\n(NEET-I 2016)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','solid in gas','gas in gas','liquid in gas','gas in liquid.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190618,'chemistry','Which property of colloidal solution is independent of charge on the colloidal particles?','Tyndall effect','Tyndall effect is scattering of light by colloidal particles which is independent of charge on them.\n(NEET 2015, Cancelled 2014 )','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Electroosmosis','Tyndall effect','Coagulation','Electrophoresis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190619,'chemistry','The protecting power of lyophilic colloidal sol is expressed in terms of :','gold number','Lyophobic sols are unstable, thus they are stabilised by adding lyophilic colloids that protect them against precipitation. Lyophilic colloids are thus known as protective colloids. Their protection power is measured in gold numbers. In other words, the gold number is the smallest amount of lyophilic colloid, measured in milligrammes, that prevents flocculation of 10 mL of gold sol when 1 mL of 10% NaCl solution is added.\n(NEET 2012)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','coagulation value','gold number','critical micelle concentration','oxidation number.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190620,'chemistry','Which among the following forms micelles in aqueous solution above certain concentration?','Dodecyl trimethyl ammonium chloride','(NEET 2005) diagram(ne1915)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Dodecyl trimethyl ammonium chloride','Glucose','Urea','Pyridinium chloride','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190621,'chemistry','Position of non-polar and polar part in micelle :','polar at outer surface but non-polar at inner \nsurface','Micelles are the clusters or aggregates formed in solution by association of colloids. Usually such molecules have a lyophobic group and a lyophilic group. The long hydrocarbon is the lyophobic portion which tries to recede away from the solvent water and the ionisable lyophilic group which tends to go into water resulting into ions. As the concentration is increased the lyophobic parts receding away from the solvent approach each other and form a cluster. Thus, the lyophobic ends are in the interior and lyophilic groups projecting outward in contact with the solvent.\n(NEET 2002)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','polar at outer surface but non-polar at inner \nsurface','polar at inner surface non-polar at outer surface','distributed over all the surface','are present in the surface only.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190701,'chemistry','Consider the given reaction in the given diagram. What conclusions can you make about the catalyst?(n1906)','The catalyst is homogeneous in nature.','Homogeneous catalysis occurs when the reactants and catalyst are in the same phase (i.e., liquid or gas). Here, both the reactants and the catalyst are in the same phase. Hence,  this catalytic reaction is said to be homogeneous catalysis.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 130, Paragraph 5.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','The catalyst is heterogeneous in nature.','The catalyst is homogeneous in nature.','Catalyst and products are in the different phases.','Catalyst and reactants are in the different phases.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190701q.png'),
(190702,'chemistry','Which among the following diagram process converts ammonia to nitric oxide in the presence of platinum gauze?(n1907)','Ostwald’s process','Ostwald\'s method refers to the oxidation of ammonia into nitric oxide in the presence of platinum gauze as a catalyst.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 131, Paragraph 5.2.1)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Haber\'s Process','Solvay Process','Ostwald’s process','Bayer\'s Process','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190702q.png'),
(190703,'chemistry','Assume that a student heated a sol X to separate the dispersion medium from the dispersed phase. Then he tried to replicate the sol by just remixing it with the dispersion medium, but he failed. What would be his interpretation on the sol?','The sol is lyophobic and irreversible.','A key feature of lyophilic sols is that if the dispersion media is separated from the dispersed phase (for example, by heating till evaporation), the sol can be reconstituted by simply remixing with the dispersion medium. This is why these sols are also known as reversible sols. Hence, the sol that could not be reversed was lyophobic and irreversible.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 137, Paragraph 5.4.2)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','solid in gas','The sol is lyophilic and irreversible.','The sol is lyophobic and irreversible.','The sol is lyophobic and reversible.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(70713,'biology','The supportive skeletal structures in the human external ears and in the nose tip are examples of [MAINS 2012]','Cartilage','Cartilage is a semirigid supportive or skeletal connective tissue in which matrix is solid and made of mucoprotein or proteoglycan called chondrin. It is of four types –hyaline, fibrous, calcified and elastic. Yellow elastic fibrocartilage is found in pinna and external auditory canal of the ear, Eustachian tubes, epiglottis and tip of the nose. Its matrix contains numerous yellow fibres which form a network by uniting with one another. Due to the presence of yellow fibres, the cartilage becomes more flexible. Hence, it provides flexibility to these organs.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:104, paragraph no:1)..','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Ligament','Areolar tissue','Bone','Cartilage','NEW','11-104',NULL,NULL),
(190704,'chemistry','Which of the following statements regarding emulsions is correct?','The emulsifying agent forms an interfacial film between suspended particles and the medium.','a. Statement (a) is correct as the emulsifying agents are surface-active agents that are present between the dispersion medium and the dispersed phase during emulsion formation and prevent the dispersed phase particles from recombining.\nb. Statement (b) is incorrect as emulsions can be diluted with any amount of dispersion medium. However, when mixed, the dispersed liquid forms a separate layer.\nc. Statement (c) is also incorrect as emulsions droplets are usually negatively charged and can be precipitated by electrolytes.\nd. Statement (d) is incorrect too as Emulsions also show Brownian movement and Tyndall effect.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 145, Paragraph 5.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','The emulsifying agent forms an interfacial film between suspended particles and the medium.','Any amount of dispersed liquid can be used to dilute emulsions. However, when mixed, the dispersion medium creates a separate layer.','Electrolytes can precipitate positively charged droplets in emulsions.','Emulsions, unlike other colloids, do not exhibit Brownian movement or the Tyndall effect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190705,'chemistry','Which of the following statements about the "Freundlich isotherm" is false;\n \n a. The Freundlich isotherm is only valid if the graph between x/m and log p forms a curve; otherwise, it is not.\n \n b. Adsorption is pressure dependent when 1/n = 0 and x/m = constant.\n \n c. Adsorption varies directly with pressure when 1/n = 0, x/m = kp , i.e. x/m ∝ p.\n \n d. Freundlich isotherm fails at high pressure.','Both a and b','a. The Freundlich isotherm is only valid if the graph between x/m and log p is a straight line; otherwise, it is not.\nb. The Freundlich isotherm holds good over a limited range of pressure. When 1/n = 0,  x/m = constant, the adsorption is independent of pressure.\nc. When 1/n = 0, x/m = k p, i.e. x/m ∝ p, adsorption varies directly with pressure.\nd. The experimental isotherms always seem to approach saturation at high pressure. This cannot be explained by Freundlich isotherm. Thus, it fails at high pressure.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 127, Paragraph 5.1.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and d','Both c  and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190706,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n \n Assertion : Chemisorption is commonly referred to as "activated adsorption" since it requires high activation energy.\n \n Reason : Chemical bond formation requires a significant quantity of activation energy.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Chemisorption is also known as activated adsorption because it involves a chemical bond between the reactants and the adsorbent. The formation of chemical bonds needs a high amount of activation energy. \nHence, the reason is the correct explanation of assertion.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 125, Paragraph 5.1.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190707,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements explaining " Enzyme catalysis",\n \n a. Activators, typically non-metallic ions, increase the catalytic activity of enzyme molecules.\n \n b. Many enzymes have been isolated in pure crystalline form from living cells. They are very effective catalysts, catalysing a variety of reactions, particularly those related to natural processes.\n \n c. The main difference between enzyme and non-enzyme catalysts is that enzyme is not substrate specific.\n \n d. The active centers on the surface of enzyme particles comprise active groups such as -NH₂, -COOH, -SH, -OH, etc.','Both b and d','a. Statement (a) is incorrect as metal ions such as Na⁺, Mn²⁺ etc are commonly used as activators. When these metal ions are weakly bound to enzyme molecules, they boost catalytic activity.\nb. Statement (b) is Many enzymes have been isolated in pure crystalline form from living cells. They are very effective catalysts, catalysing a variety of reactions, particularly those related to natural processes.\nc. Statement (c) is incorrect as enzyme is substrate specific. The complementary-shaped substrate molecules fit into the enzymes\' active sites like a key fits into a lock.\nd. Statement (d) is correct as the active centres on the surface of enzyme particles comprise active groups such as -NH₂, -COOH, -SH, -OH, and etc.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 134, Paragraph 5.2.4)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both a and b','Both c and d','Both a and c','Both b and d','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190708,'chemistry','Consider a cube with 1 cm side. It has a total surface area of 6 cm². If it were divided equally into 10¹² cubes, it would be the size of large colloidal particles. What would be the surface area of the cube now?','60,000 cm²','Consider a cube with 1 cm side. It has a total surface area of 6 cm². If it were divided equally into 10¹² cubes, the cubes would be the size of large colloidal particles and have a total surface area of 60,000 cm² or 6 m².\nInitial length of each edge (a) = 1cm\nArea = 6a²\nArea = 6 (1 cm)² = 6 cm²\nthe new volume will be 1/10¹²  =10⁻¹² cm³, so the side of the new cube will be 10⁻⁴ cm \nArea of new cube = 6 x (10⁻⁴ cm)²\nArea of new cube =  6 x 10⁻⁸ cm²\nThe total surface area of 10¹² cubes = 6 x 10⁻⁸ cm² x 10¹² = 6 x 10⁴cm² =60,000 cm² or 6 m\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 136, Paragraph 5.3)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','60,000 m²','60 m²','60,000 cm²','6 cm²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190709,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R). \n \n Assertion : Colloidal solutions, when prepared, typically contain an excess of electrolytes and other soluble contaminants. \n \n Reason : Higher amounts of electrolyte are required for colloidal solution stability','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Colloidal solutions, when prepared, typically contain an excess of electrolytes and other soluble contaminants. While small amounts of electrolyte are required for colloidal solution stability, higher amounts to coagulate it. It is, therefore, the concentration of these soluble contaminants must be kept to a bare minimum. \nHence, the assertion is true, but the reason is false.\nReference : (NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 140, Paragraph 5.4.5)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121313,'chemistry','Which nomenclature is not according to IUPAC system in the given diagram(12117)','A','First priority among substituents at the same position is given to double bond, hence A is wrong. (NEET 2012)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121313q.png'),
(190801,'chemistry','Among the following, the INCORRECT statement\n about colloids is [JEE (Main)-2019]','The osmotic pressure of a colloidal solution is\n of higher order than the true solution at the\n same concentration.','The osmotic pressure of a colloidal solution is of\n lower order than that of true solution at the same\n concentration due to association of solute molecule\n till they acquire colloidal dimensions.\nS = iCRT\ni is less in colloidal solution than true solution','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','They can scatter light.','The range of diameters of colloidal particles is\n between 1 and 1000 nm.','The osmotic pressure of a colloidal solution is\n of higher order than the true solution at the\n same concentration.','They are larger than small molecules and have\n high molar mass.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190802,'chemistry','Peptization is a : [JEE (Main)-2019]','Process of converting precipitate into colloidal\n solution','Peptisation is the process of converting a precipitate\n into a colloidal sol by shaking it with dispersion\n medium in the presence of small amount of\n electrolyte.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Process of converting a colloidal solution into\n precipitate','Process of converting precipitate into colloidal\n solution','Process of converting soluble particles to form\n colloidal solution','Process of bringing colloidal molecule into\n solution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190803,'chemistry','The correct option among the following is :\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','Colloidal particles in lyophobic sols can be\n precipitated by electrophoresis.','Electrophoresis is used to coagulate lyophobic\n colloids.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Colloidal medicines are more effective because\n they have small surface area.','Colloidal particles in lyophobic sols can be\n precipitated by electrophoresis.','Brownian motion in colloidal solution is faster\n if the viscosity of the solution is very high.','Addition of alum to water makes it unfit for\n drinking.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190804,'chemistry','A gas undergoes physical adsorption on a surface\n and follows the given Freundlich adsorption\n isotherm equation\nx/m = kp½\nAdsorption of the gas increases with\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','Increase in p and decrease in T','Freundlich adsorption is applicable for physical\n adsorption. The variation of extent of adsorption with\n (i) Pressure and (ii) Temp is given by the following\n curves. Refer diagram(ne1921)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Increase in p and decrease in T','Decrease in p and decrease in T','Increase in p and increase in T','Decrease in p and increase in T','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190805,'chemistry','The principle of column chromatography is\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','Differential adsorption of the substances on the\n solid phase.','In column chromatograph a solid adsorbent is\n packed in a column and a solution containing\n number of solute particles is allowed to flow\n down the column. The solute molecules get\n adsorbed on the surface of adsorbent. So it is\n differential adsorption of the substances on the solid\n phase.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','Differential adsorption of the substances on the\n solid phase.','Gravitational force.','Differential absorption of the substances on the\n solid phase.','Capillary action','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190806,'chemistry','10 mL of 1 mM surfactant solution forms a\n monolayer covering 0.24 cm² on a polar substrate.\n If the polar head is approximated as a cube, what\n is its edge length? [JEE (Main)-2019]','2.0 pm','No. of surfactant molecule 6 × 10²³ × (10/1000) × 10⁻³ = 6 × 10¹⁸ molecules\n\nLet edge length = a cm\nTotal surface area of surfactant = 6 × 10¹⁸ a²\n=> 0.24 \na = 2 × 10⁻¹⁰ cm = 2 pm','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','2.0 pm','2.0 nm','0.1 nm','1.0 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190807,'chemistry','The aerosol is a kind of colloid in which\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','Solid is dispersed in gas','In aerosol, the dispersion medium is gas while the\n dispersed phase can be both solid or liquid.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Solid is dispersed in gas','Gas is dispersed in solid','Liquid is dispersed in water','Gas is dispersed in liquid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190808,'chemistry','Adsorption of a gas follows Freundlich adsorption\n isotherm. x is the mass of the gas adsorbed on\n mass m of the adsorbent. The plot of log \nx\n/m\n versus\n log p is shown in the given graph. \nx\n/m is\n proportional to refer diagram(n1909)','p⅔','Refer diagram(ne1922)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'3','mcq','p½','p³','p⅔','p²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190808q.png'),
(190809,'chemistry','Among the following, the false statement is\n [JEE (Main)-2019]','Latex is a colloidal solution of rubber particles\n which are charged','Latex is colloidal solution of rubber particles which\n are negatively charged.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'1','mcq','Tyndall effect can be used to distinguish\n between a colloidal solution and a true\n solution.','Latex is a colloidal solution of rubber particles\n which are charged','Lyophilic sol can be coagulated by adding an\n electrolyte.','It is possible to cause artificial rain by throwing\n electrified sand carrying charge opposite to the\n one on clouds from an aeroplane','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190810,'chemistry','Refer diagram(n1910)','H₂','More easily liquefiable a gas is (i.e. having higher\n critical temperature), the more readily it will be\n adsorbed.\n→  Least adsorption is shown by H₂ (least critical\n temperature)','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> JEE MAINS',NULL,'2','mcq','SO₂','CO₂','CH₄','H₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/190810q.png'),
(190901,'chemistry','Which of the following process does not occur at the interface of phases?','Homogeneous catalysis','There is no homogeneous catalysis.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Crystallisation','Heterogeneous catalysis','Homogeneous catalysis','Corrosion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190902,'chemistry','At the equilibrium position in the process of adsorption','∆H = T∆S','At equilibrium position during adsorption, ∆G = ∆H - T∆S = 0 . So that ∆H = T∆S','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','∆H > 0','∆H = T∆S','∆H > T∆S','∆H < T∆S','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190903,'chemistry','Which of the following interface cannot be obtained?','Gas-gas','Gas-gas interface cannot be obtained as they are completely miscible in nature.\nFor example, air is a mixture of various gases such as, 0₂, N₂, C0₂, etc.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','Liquid-liquid','Solid-liquid','Liquid-gas','Gas-gas','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190904,'chemistry','The term ‘sorption’ stands for','both absorption and adsorption','Adsorption and absorption when take place together, it is known as sorption.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','absorption','adsorption','both absorption and adsorption','desorption','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190905,'chemistry','Extent of physisorption of a gas increases with','decrease in temperature','Physical adsorption of a gas increases with decrease in temperature because, at higher temperature weak van der Waals forces between gas and the surface become difficult to exist.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','increase in temperature','decrease in temperature','decrease in surface area of adsorbent','decrease in strength of van der Waals forces','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190906,'chemistry','Extent of adsorption of adsorbate from solution phase increases with','increase in amount of adsorbate in solution','Extent of adsorption of adsorbate from solution phase increases with increase in the amount of adsorbate in the solution. As amount of adsorbate in the solution increases interaction of adsorbent increases which leads to increase in extent of adsorption.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','increase in amount of adsorbate in solution','decrease in surface area of adsorbent','increase in temperature of solution','decrease in amount of adsorbate in solution','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(190910,'chemistry','In physisorption, adsorbent does not show specificity for any particular gas because','involved van der Waals forces are universal','Physisorption is not specific to any gas since it involves van der Waals forces and no specific bonds are formed.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','involved van der Waals forces are universal','gases involved behave like ideal gases','enthalpy of adsorption is low','it is a reversible process','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
