(191010,'chemistry','A : Catalyst increases the reaction velocity.\nR : Solid catalyst provides large surface area for reaction to occur.','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','Catalyst decreases activation energy. Reactant molecules adsorb on the surface of solid catalyst and it provides\n surface area for reaction to occur.','Physical chemistry >> Surface chemistry >> Assertion & reason',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','A is true but R is false.','Both A and R are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(70714,'biology','The ciliated columnar epithelial cells in humans are known to occur in [NEET 2011]','Bronchioles and fallopian tube','The ciliated columnar epithelial cells in humans are present in the nasal passages, oviducts (Fallopian tubes) terminal bronchioles, ventricles of the brain and central canal of the spinal cord of the embryo. Columnar ciliated epithelium consists of columnar cells, which bear cilia on the free surface. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:101, paragraph no:2)..','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Eustachian tube and stomach lining','Bronchioles and fallopian tube','Bile duct and oesophagus','Fallopian tube and urethra','NEW','11-101',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/70714q.png'),
(191011,'biology','Nitrogenous waste products are eliminated mainly [1991]','Ammonia in tadpole and urea in adult frog','Ammonia is highly soluble in water, so in aquatic animals, e.g., tadpole of frog, the nitrogenous waste products are excreted in the form of ammonia. In terrestrial animals, e.g., adult frog, these wastes are excreted in the form of urea. Urea is formed of two molecules of ammonia and one molecule of CO₂ . During catabolism, proteins break up into amino acids. The amino acids are converted into keto-acids (such as α-keto-glutaric acid) with the removal of ammonia (deamination). The keto-acid enters the Krebs cycle to yield energy.  CO₂  is formed during decarboxylation. Ornithine (amino acid) combines with one molecule of NH3 and CO₂  producing citrulline (amino acid) and water. Citrulline combines with another molecule of ammonia and forms arginine (essential amino acid) and water. Arginine is broken into urea and ornithine in the presence of an enzyme arginase and water. Thus the cycle is repeated. This is called urea or ornithine or Krebs’- Henseleit cycle after the names of its discoverers. Most of the urea is produced in the liver. The liver cells continuously release urea into the blood and kidneys withdraw it from the blood to excrete it in urine. Reference  : NCERT Page 290','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Urea in tadpole and ammonia in adult frog','Ammonia in tadpole and urea in adult frog','Urea in both tadpole and adult frog','Urea in tadpole and uric acid in adult frog','NEW','11-290','11-205',NULL),
(191012,'biology','Which one of the following is not a part of a renal pyramid? [Mains 2011]','Convoluted tubules','The medulla of kidney is divided into a number of conical areas, the medulla pyramids or renal pyramids. Peritubular capillaries, loop of Henle and collecting ducts lie in the medulla (renal pyramids) while convoluted tubules lie in the cortex of kidney. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 292','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Peritubular capillaries','Convoluted tubules','Collecting ducts','Loop of Henle','NEW','11-290','11-205',NULL),
(191013,'biology','The basic functional unit of human kidney is [1997]','Nephron','A nephron is a unit of structure and function in a kidney. A kidney contains about a million nephrons, each approximately 3 cm. long. A nephron is a long tubule differentiated into four regions having different anatomical features and physiological role : Bowman’s capsule, proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), loop of Henle, and distal convoluted tubule (DCT). The latter opens into one of the collecting ducts. Nephridia are the excretory organs of annelids. Reference  : NCERT Page 290','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Nephridia','Henle’s loop','Nephron','Pyramid.','NEW','11-290','11-205',NULL),
(191014,'biology','Which one of the four parts mentioned below does not constitute a part of single uriniferous tubule? [1994]','Collecting duct','A nephron is a unit of structure and function in a kidney. A kidney contains about a million nephrons, each approximately 3 cm. long. A nephron is a long tubule differentiated into four regions having different anatomical features and physiological role : Bowman’s capsule, proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), loop of Henle, and distal convoluted tubule (DCT). The latter opens into one of the collecting ducts. Nephridia are the excretory organs of annelids. Reference  : NCERT Page 290','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Distal convoluted tubule','Collecting duct','Bowman’s capsule','Loop of Henle','NEW','11-290','11-205',NULL),
(191015,'biology','Proximal and distal convoluted tubules are parts of [1990]','Nephron','Reference  : NCERT class 11 Page No 292 Figure 19.3','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Seminiferous tubules','Nephron','Oviduct','Vas deferens','NEW','11-292','11-207',NULL),
(191017,'biology','Which of the following statements is correct? [NEET 2017]','The ascending limb of loop of Henle is impermeable to water','Descending limb of loop of Henle is permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes. Ascending limb of loop of Henle is impermeable to water but permeable to electrolytes.A nephron is a unit of structure and function in a kidney. A kidney contains about a million nephrons, each approximately 3 cm. long. A nephron is a long tubule differentiated into four regions having different anatomical features and physiological role : Bowman’s capsule, proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), loop of Henle, and distal convoluted tubule (DCT). The latter opens into one of the collecting ducts. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','The descending limb of loop of Henle is impermeable to water','The ascending limb of loop of Henle is permeable to water','The descending limb of loop of Henle is permeable to electrolytes.','The ascending limb of loop of Henle is impermeable to water','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191018,'biology','The part of nephron involved in active reabsorption of sodium is [NEET-II 2016]','Proximal convoluted tubule','From the Bowman’s capsule, a glomerular filtrate enters the proximal convoluted tubule. Absorption of selected materials takes place from the filtrate into the blood of the peritubular capillaries or vasa recta. It is termed the tubular reabsorption. Reabsorption involves both passive and active transport across the tubular epithelium. About 65 % of the glomerular filtrate is normally reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule before reaching the loop of Henle. Glucose, amino acids, vitamins, hormones, sodium, potassium, chlorides, phosphates, bicarbonates, much of water and some urea from the filtrate are absorbed. Sodium and potassium are reabsorbed by primary active transport.  Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Distal convoluted tubule','Proximal convoluted tubule','Bowman’s capsule','Descending limb of Henle’s loop','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191019,'biology','Removal of proximal convoluted tubule from the nephron will result in [2015 Cancelled]','More diluted urine','Reference  : NCERT class 11 Page No 295 Figure 19.5','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','No change in quality and quantity of urine','No urine formation','More diluted urine','More concentrated urine','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(191020,'biology','The maximum amount of electrolytes and water (70 – 80 percent) from the glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed in which part of the nephron? [2012]','Proximal convoluted tubule','Nearly all of the essential nutrients, and 70-80 % of electrolytes and water are reabsorbed by this segment. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Ascending limb of loop of Henle','Distal convoluted tubule','Proximal convoluted tubule','Descending limb of loop of Henle','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(251309,'chemistry','Methyl magnesium bromide → 2-Methylpropan-2-ol\n\nThe reaction can be carried out by ?','Water','The first step of the reaction is the nucleophilic addition of Grignard reagent to the carbonyl group to form an adduct. Hydrolysis of the adduct yields an alcohol.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 331; Paragraph No. 1). Refer diagram(ne2593)','Organic Chemistry >> Alcohols, Phenols and Ether >> Conversion Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Water','NaOH','KOH','LiOH','NEW','12-331','12-201',NULL),
(191021,'biology','Which one of the following correctly explains the function of a specific part of the human nephron? [Mains 2011]','Podocytes : create minute spaces (slit pores) for the filtration of blood into the Bowman’s capsule','The visceral layer of Bowman’s capsule surrounds the glomerulus and is composed of special type of cells, the podocytes. The podocytes are so called because they possess foot like processes (projection), the pedicels. The space between pedicels are called slit pores (= filtration slits) through which the glomerular filtrate filters. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294.','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Podocytes : create minute spaces (slit pores) for the filtration of blood into the Bowman’s capsule','Henle’s loop : most reabsorption of the major substances from the glomerular filtrate','Distal convoluted tubule : reabsorption of K+ ions into the surrounding blood capillaries','Afferent arteriole : carries the blood away from the glomerulus towards renal vein','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191101,'biology','Which one of the following statements in regard to the excretion by the human kidneys is correct? [2010]','Nearly 99 % of the glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed by the renal tubules','Urine formation involves three main processes namely, glomerular filtration, reabsorption and secretion, that takes place in different parts of the nephron. A comparison of the volume of the filtrate formed per day (180 litres per day) with that of the urine released (1.5 litres), suggest that nearly 99 percent of the filtrate is reabsorbed by the renal tubules. The descending limb of loop of Henle is permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes. The ascending limb is impermeable to water but allows transport of electrolytes actively or passively. Conditional reabsorption of Na+ and water takes place in distal convoluted tubule. It is also capable of reabsorption of HCO₃⁻. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Descending limb of loop of Henle is impermeable to water','Distal convoluted tubule is incapable of reabsorbing HCO3 – .','Nearly 99 % of the glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed by the renal tubules','Ascending limb of loop of Henle is impermeable to electrolytes','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191102,'biology','Glucose is taken back from glomerular filtrate through [1993]','Active transport','Glucose is taken back from the glomerular filtrate by the proximal convoluted tubule by active transport. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Active transport','Passive transport','Osmosis','Diffusion','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191103,'biology','Under normal conditions which one is completely reabsorbed in the renal tubule? [1991]','Glucose','The cells lining the proximal convoluted tubule are well adapted for reabsorption of materials from the filtrate. They have abundant mitochondria and bear numerous microvilli on the free side thus giving brush border appearance. The cells reabsorb entire glucose, amino acids, most of the inorganic ions, much of the water as well as some urea from the filtrate.Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Urea','Uric acid','Salts','Glucose','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191104,'biology','Brush border is characteristic of [1990]','Proximal convoluted tubule','PCT is lined by simple cuboidal brush border epithelium which increases the surface area for reabsorption. Referenc:  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Neck of nephron','Collecting tube','Proximal convoluted tubule','All of these','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191105,'biology','Reabsorption of useful substances from glomerular filtrate occurs in [1989]','Proximal convoluted tubule','PCT is lined by simple cuboidal brush border epithelium which increases the surface area for reabsorption. Referenc:  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Collecting tube','Loop of Henle','Proximal convoluted tubule','Distal convoluted tubule','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191106,'biology','Which of the following factors is responsible for the formation of concentrated urine? [NEET 2019]','Maintaining hyperosmolarity towards the medullary interstitium in the kidneys.','The counter current mechanism helps to maintain a concentration gradient in the medullary interstitium. The proximity between the Henle’s loop and vasa recta, as well as the counter current in them help in maintaining an increasing osmolarity towards the inner medullary interstitium, i.e., from 300 mOsmolL⁻¹ in the cortex to about 1200 mOsmolL⁻¹ in the inner medulla. This gradient is mainly caused by NaCl and urea. Presence of such interstitial gradient helps in an easy passage of water from the collecting tubule thereby concentrating the filtrate (urine). Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 295','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrostatic pressure during glomerular filtration','Low levels of antidiuretic hormone.','Maintaining hyperosmolarity towards the medullary interstitium in the kidneys.','Secretion of erythropoietin by Juxtaglomerular complex.','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(191107,'biology','If Henle’s loop were absent from mammalian nephron, which one of the following is to be expected? [2003]','The urine will be more dilute.','Reabsorption is a process by which useful constituents of glomerular filtrate are returned into the blood streams. It occurs in convoluted tubules (proximal convoluted tubule) as well as loop of Henle. Basically loop of Henle, in association with vasa rectae, plays an important role in the counter current mechanism (the process which makes urine hypertonic, i.e., more concentrated). Therefore, if Henle’s loop was absent from mammalian nephron the urine will be more dilute. PCT is lined by simple cuboidal brush border epithelium which increases the surface area for reabsorption. Referenc:  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','There will be no urine formation','There will be hardly any change in the quality and quantity of urine formed','The urine will be more concentrated.','The urine will be more dilute.','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191109,'biology','Which of the following would help in prevention of diuresis? [NEET 2020]','Reabsorption of Na⁺ and water from renal tubules due to aldosterone.','Aldosterone acts mainly at the renal tubules and stimulates the reabsorption of Na⁺ and water and excretion of K⁺ and phosphate ions. Thus, aldosterone helps in prevention of diuresis. PCT is lined by simple cuboidal brush border epithelium which increases the surface area for reabsorption. Referenc:  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','More water reabsorption due to undersecretion of ADH.','Reabsorption of Na⁺ and water from renal tubules due to aldosterone.','Atrial natriuretic factor causes vasoconstriction.','Decrease in secretion of renin by JG cells.','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191110,'biology','A decrease in blood pressure/volume will not cause the release of [NEET 2017]','Atrial natriuretic factor','Atrial natriuretic factor (ANF) is responsible for lowering of blood pressure and volume. The walls of the atria of the heart release ANF in response to an increase in blood volume and pressure. It opposes regulation by RAAS. It inhibits release of renin from JGA thereby inhibiting NaCl reabsorption by the collecting duct and reduces aldosterone release from adrenal gland. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 295','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Atrial natriuretic factor','Aldosterone','ADH','Renin','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(191111,'biology','Which of the following causes an increase in sodium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule? [2014]','Increase in aldosterone levels','Aldosterone is a hormone secreted by the outer layer of the adrenal gland (cortex part). Decreased blood volume and interstitial fluid level, results in decreased blood pressure, trigger aldosterone secretion. When aldosterone is present in the blood, reabsorption of Na+ in the filtrate is increased by the epithelial cells of the collecting duct. Retaining Na⁺, raises the osmotic pressure of the blood and reduces water loss from the body. When aldosterone is absent, some Na⁺ remains in the filtrate and is excreted with the urine. PCT is lined by simple cuboidal brush border epithelium which increases the surface area for reabsorption. Referenc:  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Increase in aldosterone levels','Increase in antidiuretic hormone levels','Decrease in aldosterone levels','Decrease in antidiuretic hormone levels','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191112,'biology','A fall in glomerular filtration rate (GFR) activates [Mains 2012]','Juxtaglomerular cells to release renin','The amount of the filtrate formed by the kidneys per minute is called glomerular filtration rate (GFR). GFR in a healthy individual is approximately 125 mL/minute, i.e., 180 litres per day. The kidneys have built-in mechanisms for the regulation of glomerular filtration rate. One such efficient mechanism is carried out by juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA). JGA is a special sensitive region formed by cellular modifications in the distal convoluted tubule and the afferent arteriole at the location of their contact. A fall in GFR can activate the JG cells to release renin which can stimulate the glomerular blood flow and thereby the GFR come back to normal.PCT is lined by simple cuboidal brush border epithelium which increases the surface area for reabsorption. Referenc:  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Juxtaglomerular cells to release renin','Adrenal cortex to release aldosterone','Adrenal medulla to release adrenaline','Posterior pituitary to release vasopressin.','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191113,'biology','Which one of the following statements is correct with respect to kidney function regulation? [Mains 2011]','When someone drinks lot of water, ADH release is suppressed','Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) or vasopressin increases the reabsorption of water in the distal convoluted tubule, collecting tubules and collecting ducts of the nephrons of the kidneys. As a result, the reabsorption of water from the glomerular filtrate is increased. When someone drinks lot of water, requirement of absorption of water decreases, so ADH release is suppressed. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 295.','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','When someone drinks lot of water, ADH release is suppressed','Exposure to cold temperature stimulates ADH release.','An increase in glomerular blood flow stimulates formation of angiotensin II','During summer when body loses lot of water by evaporation, the release of ADH is suppressed','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(191114,'biology','Angiotensinogen is a protein produced and secreted by [2006]','Liver cells.','Juxtaglomerular cells secrete an enzyme renin into blood. Renin changes plasma protein angiotensiongen to a peptide called angiotensin II. It increases the blood volume : \n\n(i) by inducing proximal convoluted tubules to reabsorb more NaCl and water \n\n(ii) stimulates the adrenal glands to release a hormone, called aldosterone which induces the distal convoluted tubule to absorb more Na⁺ and water. Angiotensinogen is an α-globulin protein produced by liver. Renin serves as an enzyme in the conversion of the plasma protein angiotensinogen into angiotensin. This protein stimulates the adrenal cortex to produce aldosterone which acts on the cells of the ascending limb of the loop of Henle and increases the rate of reabsorption of Na⁺. Reabsorption of Na⁺ brings about the uptake of an osmotically equivalent amount of water. Absorption of sodium and water increases blood volume and pressure. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 296','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Juxtaglomerular (JG) cells','Macula densa cells','Endothelial cells (cells lining the blood vessels)','Liver cells.','NEW','11-296','11-211',NULL),
(191115,'biology','Which of the following does not favour the formation of large quantities of dilute urine? [2015 Cancelled]','Renin','Juxtaglomerular cells secrete an enzyme renin into blood. Renin changes plasma protein angiotensiongen to a peptide called angiostensin 11. It increases the blood volume : \n\n(i) by inducing proximal convoluted tubules to reabsorb more NaCl and water \n\n(ii) stimulates the adrenal glands to release a hormone called aldosterone which induces the distal convoluted tubule to absorb more Na⁺ and water. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 296','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Renin','Atrial-natriuretic factor','Alcohol','Caffeine','NEW','11-296','11-211',NULL),
(191116,'biology','What will happen if the stretch receptors of the urinary bladder wall are totally removed? [2009]','There will be no micturition.','Micturition is the expulsion of urine from the urinary bladder. Sensory stretch receptors are responsible for the stretch reflex. If these are removed then autonomic nervous system control will not be there and bladder will always feel with minimal urine and frequently pass urine through urethra. So, micturition will continue. \nReference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 298','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Micturition will continue.','Urine will continue to collect normally in the bladder.','There will be no micturition.','Urine will not collect in the bladder','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(70712,'biology','Select the correct statement from the ones given below with respect to periplaneta americana. [AIPMT 2012]','Males bear a pair of short thread like anal styles.','The posterior segment of cockroaches bear appendages named as anal cerci. These are found in both male and female. But male cockroach can be distinguished by female ones by the presence of an extra pair of accessory appendages named as anal styles. It assists during copulation.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:111, paragraph no:2-3)..','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Nervous system located dorsally, consists of segmentally arranged ganglia joined by a pair of longitudinal connectives.','Males bear a pair of short thread like anal styles.','There are 16 very long malpighian tubules present at the junctions of midgut and hindgut.','Grinding of food is carried out only by the mouth parts','NEW','11-112',NULL,NULL),
(191117,'biology','A person who is on a long hunger strike and is surviving only on water, will have\n [AIPMT-2007]','less urea in his urine','A person who is on a long hunger strike and is surviving only on water, will have less urea in his urine. Urea, also called carbamide, is an organic chemical compound which essentially is the waste produced when the body metabolizes the breakdown of protein. Urea is manufactured in the liver by breaking down protein or amino acids and ammonia, the kidneys transfer urea from the blood to the urine. The average person excretes about 30 grams of urea a day. During total starvation with no food being eaten, the body works to conserve protein which is not consumed and the urea cycle comes to a stop with conservation of protein. So the urea concentration in urine falls, \nAmino acids are never found in Human urine under normal circumstances,There is no glucose in urine unless in pathological conditions like Diabetes;','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','less amino acids in his urine','more glucose in his blood','less urea in his urine','more sodium in his urine.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(191118,'biology','Use of an artificial kidney during hemodialysis may result in \n\n(A) Nitrogenous waste build-up in the body \n\n(B) Non-elimination of excess potassium ions \n\n(C) Reduced absorption of calcium ions from gastro-intestinal tract \n\n(D) Reduced RBC production. \n\nWhich of the following options is the most appropriate ? \n[NEET 2019]','(C) and (D) are correct.','When kidneys are completely damaged and do not function, patient often receive hemodialysis (treatment with artificial kidney). The patient is connected to the machine by a tube attached to an artery often the radial artery. Blood from the artery is pumped into a tube that runs through the dialyser. The dialyser is filled with dialysing fluid which contains the same quantities of electolytes and nutrients as normal plasma but contains no waste products. The cellophane tube (a tube bounded by thin membrane) is kept in the dialysing fluid. The membrane of the cellophane tube is impermeable to blood cells and proteins but permeable to urea,uric acid, creatinine and mineral ions. So, these wases diffuse from the blood to the dialysing fluid across the cellophane membrane.However, since both kidneys are non-functional thus absorption of Ca⁺²from small intestine will be affected as coversion of vitamin D to calcitriol will not take place which is needed for calcium absorption from small intestine(GI tract). Erythropoietin production will also be affected, thus RBC production will be reduced. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 299','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(A) and (D) are correct','(A) and (B) are correct.','(B) and (C) are correct.','(C) and (D) are correct.','NEW','11-299','11-214',NULL),
(191119,'biology','Which of the following factors is responsible for the formation of concentrated urine? [NEET 2019]','Maintaining hyperosmolarity towards the medullary interstitium in the kidneys.','The counter current mechanism helps to maintain a concentration gradient in the medullary interstitium. The proximity between the Henle’s loop and vasa recta, as well as the counter current in them help in maintaining an increasing osmolarity towards the inner medullary interstitium, i.e., from 300 mOsmolL–1 in the cortex to about 1200 mOsmolL–1  in the inner medulla. This gradient is mainly caused by NaCl and urea. Presence of such interstitial gradient helps in an easy passage of water from the collecting tubule thereby concentrating the filtrate (urine). Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 296','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrostatic pressure during glomerular filtration.','Low levels of antidiuretic hormone','Maintaining hyperosmolarity towards the medullary interstitium in the kidneys.','Secretion of erythropoietin by Juxtaglomerular complex','NEW','11-296','11-211',NULL),
(191120,'biology','Match the items given in column I with those in column II in diagram and select the correct option given below. \n(1907)','A(iv) B(i) C(ii) D(iii)','Glomerulus with bowman\'s capsule is called malphigian corpuscle. Ultrafiltration takes place in renal corpuscle of uriniferous tubule. It takes place in presence of effective filtration pressure. During the process, blood is filtered and contains only blood plasma – proteins. The filtered blood entering into Bowman’s capsule is called glomerular filtrate. Henle\'s loop is responsible for the reabsorption of water and sodium chloride from the urine. Thus, making urine concentrated. Urine travels from the collecting ducts into calyces. From there they travel into the renal pelvis through the pyramids and then through the ureter into the urinary bladder. From bladder, it travels through urethra to outside.','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','A(iv) B(v) C(ii) D(iii)','A(iv) B(i) C(ii) D(iii)','A(v) B(iv) C(i) D(ii)','A(v) B(iv) C(i) D(iii)','NEW','11-292','11-207','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/191120q.png'),
(191121,'biology','Nitrogenous waste is excreted in the form of pellet or paste by : [NEET 2022]','Pavo','Birds (Pavo) excrete nitrogenous wastes as uric acid in the form of pellet or paste with a minimum loss of water. Many bony fishes (like Hippocampus) and aquatic amphibians (like Salamandra) are ammonotelic in nature. Mammals (like Ornithorhynchus) mainly excrete urea and are called ureotelic animals.','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Ornithorhynchus','Salamandra','Hippocampus','Pavo','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(191122,'biology','Arrange the events of Renin - Angiotensin \nmechanism in correct sequence. \n (A) Activation of JG cells and release of renin. \n (B) Angiotensin II activates release of aldosterone. \n (C) Fall in glomerular blood pressure. \n (D) Reabsorption of Na+\n and water from distal \nconvoluted tubule. \n (E) Angiotensinogen is converted to Angiotensin I and then to Angiotensin II. \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(1) (C), (A), (E), (B), (D)','Explanation: The correct sequence of events in the Renin-Angiotensin mechanism is as follows:\n\n(C) Fall in glomerular blood pressure: A decrease in glomerular blood pressure is sensed by the juxtaglomerular (JG) cells.\n\n(A) Activation of JG cells and release of renin: The JG cells in the kidney respond to the decrease in blood pressure by releasing the enzyme renin into the bloodstream.\n\n(E) Angiotensinogen is converted to Angiotensin I and then to Angiotensin II: Renin acts on angiotensinogen, a protein produced by the liver, and converts it into angiotensin I. Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) further converts angiotensin I into angiotensin II.\n\n(B) Angiotensin II activates the release of aldosterone: Angiotensin II stimulates the release of aldosterone from the adrenal cortex. Aldosterone promotes the reabsorption of sodium (Na+) and water in the distal convoluted tubule of the kidney.\n\n(D) Reabsorption of Na+ and water from the distal convoluted tubule: The action of aldosterone leads to increased reabsorption of sodium and water from the distal convoluted tubule, resulting in water and sodium retention and an increase in blood volume and blood pressure.\n\nTherefore, the correct sequence is (C), (A), (E), (B), (D), which corresponds to option 1','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (C), (A), (E), (B), (D)','(2) (A), (D), (E), (C), (B)','(3) (A), (D), (C), (B), (E)','(4) (B), (A), (E), (D), (C)','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(191123,'biology','Given below are two statements : one is labelled as \n Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R) \n Assertion (A) : \nAscending limb of loop of Henle is impermeable to \nwater and allows transport of electrolytes actively or passively. \nReason (R) : \nDilution of filtrate takes place due to efflux of electrolytes in the medullary fluid. \n In the light of the above statements, choose the \ncorrect answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct \nexplanation of (A)',NULL,'Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (A) is true but (R) is false','(2) (A) is false but (R) is true','(3) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct \nexplanation of (A)','(4) Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','NEW','11-297','11-212',NULL),
(191124,'biology','Which of the following statements are correct?\nA. An excessive loss of body fluid from the body switches off osmoreceptors.\nB. ADH facilitates water reabsorption to prevent diuresis.\nC. ANF causes vasodilation.\nD. ADH causes increase in blood pressure.\nE. ADH is responsible for decrease in GFR.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(1) B, C and D only','Antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin) is released by the posterior pituitary lobe when the blood has high osmolarity (number of solute particles per litre of solution). It plays a role in reducing blood osmolarity by increasing reabsorption of water in distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and collecting duct of the nephron.\n\nWhen the blood volume and blood pressure decrease, juxtaglomerular cells in the kidneys secrete the enzyme renin. Renin catalyzes the conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin II. Angiotensin II stimulates the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone. Aldosterone promotes the absorption of NaCl and water from DCT.\n\nAn increase in blood volume and blood flow to the atria of the heart causes the release of a hormone called atrial natriuretic factor (ANF). This hormone stimulates vasodilation i.e. dilation of blood vessels and not vasoconstriction i.e. narrowing of blood vessels. This in turn decreases the blood pressure and helps in maintaining GFR.','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) B, C and D only','(2) A, B and E only','(3) C, D and E only','(4) A and B only','NEW','11-297','11-212',NULL),
(191125,'biology','Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: Nephrons are of two types: Cortical & Juxta medullary, based on their relative position in cortex\nand medulla.\nReason R: Juxta medullary nephrons have short loop of Henle whereas, cortical nephrons have longer loop\nof Henle.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(2) A is true but R is false','Henle loop of cortical nephron is small in comparison to loop of juxta medullary nephrons, which is longer.','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A.','(2) A is true but R is false','(3) A is false but R is true.','(4) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.','NEW','11-297','11-212',NULL),
(191126,'biology','Select the correct statements. \n (a) Angiotensin II activates the cortex of adrenal \ngland to release aldosterone. \n (b) Aldosterone leads to increase in blood pressure. \n (c) ANF acts as a check on renin-angiotensin \nmechanism. \n (d) ADH causes vasodilation. \n (e) Vasopressin is released from \nadenohypophysis. \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below : [ RE NEET 2022]','(4) (a), (b) and (c) only','(a), (b) and (c) only','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (a), (b) and (e) only','(2) (c), (d) and (e) only','(3) (b), (c) and (d) only','(4) (a), (b) and (c) only','NEW','11-297','11-212',NULL),
(191127,'biology','Match the pairs (a) Podocytes (b) Protonephridia (c) Nephridia (d) Renal calculi (i) crystallised oxalates (ii) Annelids (iii) Amphioxus (iv) Filtration slits (NEET 2019)','(a)- (iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d) -(i)','Protonephridia or flame cells are the excretory structures in Platyhelminthes (Flatworms, e.g., Planaria), rotifers, some annelids and the cephalochordate – Amphioxus.','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','match','(a)- (i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d) -(iv)','(a)- (i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d) -(iv)','(a)- (iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d) -(i)','(a)- (ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d) -(i)','NEW','11-296','11-211',NULL),
(191128,'biology','Presence of which of the following conditions in urine is indicative of Diabetes mellitus (NEET 2020)','Ketonuria and glycosuria','Analysis of urine helps in clinical diagnosis of many metabolic discorders as well as malfunctioning of the kidney. For example, presence of glucose (Glycosuria) and ketone bodies (Ketonuria) in urine are indicative of diabetes mellitus. (Reference: NCERT class 11, page 298 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Uremia and renal calculi','Ketonuria and glycosuria','Renal calculi and hyperglycemia','Uremai and ketonuria','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(191201,'biology','Podocytes occur in [AIIMS 1998]','Glomerulus of kidney','Podocytes are specialized visceral epithelial cell in the Bowman\'s capsule in the kidneys that wraps around the capillaries of the glomerulus. It helps filter blood in the glomerulus of the kidney. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 294','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Large intestine','Glomerulus of kidney','Wall of capillaries','Neck region','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191202,'biology','Reabsorption in tubules of nephrons occurs by [AIIMS 2000]','Both diffusion and  active transport','Tubular reabsorption is the second process in urine formation through filtrate. In this, most of the filtrate passes out of rephron tubule and returns to the blood through peritubular capillaries. Tubular reabsorption occurs by diffusion and active transport. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 296','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Osmosis','Diffusion','Active transport','Both diffusion and  active transport','NEW','11-296','11-211',NULL),
(191203,'biology','Which one of the following statements in regard to the excretion by the human kidneys is correct? [AIIMS 2013]','Nearly 99 % of the glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed by the renal tubules','Nearly 99 % of the glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed by the renal tubules. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 295','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ascending limb of Loop of Henle is impermeable to electrolytes','Descending limb of Loop of Henle is impermeable to water','Distal convoluted tubule is incapable of reabsorbing HCO3','Nearly 99 % of the glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed by the renal tubules','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(121314,'chemistry','What is the correct IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(12118)','4-ethyl-3-propylhex-1-ene','First priority is given to double bond and then carbon chain with maximum length and substituents possible is choosen. (NEET 2011)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4-ethyl-3-propylhex-1-ene','3-ethyl-4-ethenylheptane','3-ethyl-4-propylhex-5-ene','3-(1-ethylpropyl)hex-1-ene','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121314q.png'),
(191204,'biology','If Henle’s loop were absent from mammalian nephron which of the following is to be expected [AIIMS 2015]','The urine will be more dilute','Henle\'s loop is responsible for the reabsorption of water and sodium chloride from the urine. Hence, in the absence of Henle\'s loop the urine will become more dilute. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 296','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','There will be no urine formation','There will be hardly any change in the quality and quantity of urine formed','The urine will be more concentrated','The urine will be more dilute','NEW','11-296','11-211',NULL),
(191205,'biology','Assertion : Aldosterone is a steroid hormone and is important in the control of sodium and potassium ion concentration in mammals. \n\nReason : It upgrades sodium ion concentration in the ECF by promoting reabsorption ofsodium ions from renal tubules and excretion of potassium ions in urine. \n[AIIMS 2007]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Aldosterone is one of the important mineralocorticoids in humans secreted by adrenal cortex. Its main function is to regulate sodium content of the body . It increases sodium ion concentration in the blood by absorbing sodium ions from renal tubules. Excessive production of aldosterone causes a disease aldosteronism. Its symptoms include high blood pressure, high blood volume. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 297','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-297','11-212',NULL),
(191206,'biology','Assertion : Ultrafiltration takes place in presence of effective filtration pressure. \n\nReason :In ultrafiltration process, blood is filtered in Bowman’s capsule, filtered fluid contain protein and  blood corpuscles also.\n[AIIMS 2010]','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Ultrafiltration takes place in renal corpuscle of uriniferous tubule. It takes place in presence of effective filtration pressure. During the process, blood is filtered and contains only blood plasma – proteins. The filtered blood entering into Bowman’s capsule is called glomerular filtrate. Glomerular filtrate = Blood – (Blood corpuscles + plasma proteins). Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 296','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-296','11-211',NULL),
(191207,'biology','Assertion : In vertebrates, the liver is also referred as an accessory excretory organ. \n\nReason : Liver helps kidneys in the secretion of urine. \n[AIIMS 2012]','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','In vertebrates, the lungs, liver and  skin are referred as accessory excretory organs because besides the urinary system these organs also participate in the removal of waste products from the body. The liver is the principal organ for the excretion of cholesterol, bile pigments (bilirubin and biliverdin) and inactivated products of steroid hormones, some vitamins and many drugs. It secretes these substances in the bile and indirectly helps by formation of urea through amino acids in ornithine cycle. It has no role in secretion of urine. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 296','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-296','11-211',NULL),
(191208,'biology','Assertion : Hemodialysis can save and prolong the life of uremic patients. \n\nReason : Waste products like urea can be removed from the blood by the process of hemodialysis. \n[AIIMS 2014]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The blood urea level rises abnormally (uremia) in patients suffering from renal failures. In uremia patients an artificial kidney is used for removing accumulated waste products like urea from the blood by a process called hemodialysis. In this way, hemodialysis saves and prolongs the life of many uremic patients. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 298','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(191209,'biology','Assertion : In the descending limb of loop of Henle, the urine is hypertonic, whereas in ascending limb of loop of Henle, the urine is hypotonic.\n \nReason : Descending limb is impermeable to sodium, while ascending limb is impermeable to water. \n[AIIMS 2016]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Descending limb of Henle is permeable to water but not to sodium. Consequently water moves out into interstitium and concentration of sodium in tubular filtrate rises making filtrate hypertonic. Ascending loop is impermeable to water but permeable to sodium and makes the filtrate hypotonic. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 298','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Bth the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(191210,'biology','Which pair is correct (AIPMT 2000)','Sweat = temperature regulation','The sweat and sebaceous glands in the skin can eliminate certain substances through their secretions. Sweat produced by the sweat glands is a watery fluid containing NaCl, small amounts of urea, lactic acid, etc. Though the primary function of sweat is to facilitate a cooling effect on the body surface, it also helps in the removal of some of the wastes mentioned above. Sebaceous glands eliminate certain substances like sterols, hydrocarbons and waxes through sebum. This secretion provides a protective oily covering for the skin.  Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 298.','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Sweat = temperature regulation','Saliva = sense of food taste','Sebum = sexual attraction','Humerus = Hind leg','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(121315,'chemistry','What is the correct IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(12119)','2,3-dimethylpentanoyl chloride','Carbon of acyl halide gets first position. (NEET 2006)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1-chloro-1-oxo-2,3-dimethylpentane','2-ethyl-3-methylbutanoyl chloride','2,3-dimethylpentanoyl chloride','3,4-dimethylpentanoyl chloride','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121315q.png'),
(191211,'biology','Consider the following statements  (a-d) regarding kidney transplant and select two correct one out of these   (a) Even if a kidney tranplant is proper the recipient may need to take immunosupressants for a long time (b) The cell mediated immune response is responsible for graft rejection (c) The B- lymphocytes are responsible for graft rejection (d) The acception or rejection of a kidney transplant depends on specific interferons (AIPMT 2010)','(a) and (b)','Kidney transplantation is the ultimate method in the correction of acute renal failures (kidney failure). A functioning kidney is used in transplantation from a donor, preferably a close relative, to minimise its chances of rejection by the immune system of the host. Modern clinical procedures have increased the success rate of such a complicated technique. Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 298.','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(b) and (c)','(c) and (d)','(b) and (d)','(a) and (b)','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(191301,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Uric acid the most toxic form of excretory waste. \n\nR: Uric acid requires max amount of water for excretion.','Both A and R are false.','Uric acid is the least toxic form of excretory waste and it requires the least amount of water for excretion. (NCERT Class 11 pg 290)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-290','11-205',NULL),
(191302,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n \nA: Amphibians are uricosuric in nature. \n\nR: Amphibians excrete out uric acid with minimum loss of water.','Both A and R are false.','Acquatic Amphibians are ammoneotelic in nature as they excrete nitrogenous wastes in the form of ammonia with max loss of water. Terrestrial amphibians are ureotelic in nature and excrete urea.. (NCERT Class 11 pg 290)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-290','11-205',NULL),
(191303,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Birds and reptiles are ureotelic in nature. \n\nR: In birds, uric acid is converted to urea and excreted in that form.','Both A and R are false.','Birds and reptiles are uricotelic in nature and they excrete out uric acid in the form of pellets with minimum loss of water. (NCERT Class 11 pg 290)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-290','11-205',NULL),
(191304,'biology','Read the following statements about EXCRETION; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. Aquatic Amphibians are ammonotelic. \n\n2. Terrestrials amphibians are uricotelic. \n\n3. Birds are ureotelic.','Only 1 is correct','Terrestrial amphibians are ureotelic (excrete urea).  Birds/reptiles/insects are uricotelic (excrete uric acid). Aquatic Amphibians are ammonotelic.  (NCERT Class 11 pg 290)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 is correct','NEW','11-290','11-205',NULL),
(191305,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Purple gland is the main respiratory organ in Crustaceans. \n\nR: Crustaceans include spiders, prawns and planaria.','Both A and R are false.','Green gland (antental glands) are excretory organs in crustaceans. crustaceans include crab, prawn , lobsters, etc. (NCERT Class 11 pg 291)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-291','11-206',NULL),
(191306,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Excretory system in humans include kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder and urethra.\n \nR: Urethra directly connects the two kidneys.','A is true but R is false','Excretory system includes kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder and urthra. Urethra is the last part of the excretory system where the urine flows from bladder to outside through the penis. (NCERT Class 11 pg 291)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-291','11-206',NULL),
(191307,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Kidneys are the main component of the excretory system in humans. \n\nR: Kidneys are bean shaped and extend from the last cervical vertebrae upto the 3rd thoracic vertebrae.','A is true but R is false','Kidneys are reddish brown, bean shaped structures situated between the levels of last thoracic and third lumbar vertebra close to the dorsal inner wall of the abdominal cavity. (NCERT Class 11 pg 291)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-291','11-206',NULL),
(191308,'biology','Read the following statements about HUMAN KIDNEY; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. Kidney has inner cortex and outer medulla. \n\n2. The ureter extend from hilum to the urinary bladder. \n\n3. Cortical masses called pyramids extend into the calyces.','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Kidney has outer cortex and inner medula. The medulla is divided into conical masses called MEDULLARY PYRAMIDS which project into the calyces. (NCERT Class pg 291)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is correct','Only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-291','11-206',NULL),
(121316,'chemistry','Names of some compounds are given. Which one is not in IUPAC system in the given diagram(12120)','A','The correct name is 4-Ethyl-3-methylheptane as per alphabetical order. (NEET 2005)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121316q.png'),
(191309,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Cortex of kidney extends between the medullary pyramids. \n\nR; These extensions are called COLUMNS OF BELINI.','A is true but R is false','The renal cortex extends between the medullary pyramids as renal columns named as COLUMS OF BERTINI. (NCERT Class 11 pg 292)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-292','11-207',NULL),
(191310,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Glomerulus is composed of tuft of capillaries formed by the efferent arteriole. \n\nR: Efferent arteriole carries blood from renal artery to the glomerulus.','Both A and R are false.','Glomerulus is formed by tuft of capilaries of afferent arteriole. Afferent arteriole brings blood from branches of renal artery to the glomerulus. Efferent arteriole takes blood away from glomerulus towards peri-capillary plexus. (NCERT Class 11 pg 292)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-292','11-207',NULL),
(19311,'biology','Read the following statements about HUMAN KIDNEY; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. Each kidney has 1 million nephrons. \n\n2. Nephrons are either cortical or juxta-medullary in type. \n\n3. Cortical nephrons are longer than juxtra-medullary type.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Each kidney has nearly one million complex tubular structures called nephrons which are the functional units. They are of 2 types : CORTICAL or JUXTAMEDULLARY. Juxtamedullary nephrons extend into the medulla and hence longer than cortical nephrons. (NCERT Class 11 pg 292)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','NEW','11-292','11-207',NULL),
(191312,'biology','Read the following statements about NEPHRONS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. Glomerulus is also called MALPHIGIAN CORPUSCLE. \n\n2. Bowmans capsule is a cup like structure situated around the glomerulus. \n\n3. Bowmans capsule immediately continues as the Descending limb of loop of henle.','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Glomerulus with bowman\'s capsule is called malphigian corpuscle. The bowmans capsule continues as the proximal convoluted tubule. (NCERT Class 11 pg 292)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'2','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-292','11-207',NULL),
(191313,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Henle\'s loop was discovered by anatomist Friedrich Gustav Jakob. \n\nR: Henle\'s loop connects the distal tubule to collecting ducts.','A is true but R is false','Loop of Henle is a U shaped [Hair pin] structure that extends between proximal convoluted tubule and the distal convoluted tubule. (NCERT Class 11 pg 293)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-293','11-208',NULL),
(191314,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Malphigian corpuscles of juxtramedullary nephrons dip into medulla.\n \nR: Juxtamedullary nephrons are shorter than cortical nephrons.','Both A and R are false.','Malphigian corpuscles, PCT, DCT are all situated in the cortex of the kidney irrespective of the type of nephron. Juxtamedullary nephrons are longer than cortical nephrons. (NCERT Class 11 pg 293)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-293','11-208',NULL),
(191315,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Glomerular filtration is the first step of urine formation. \n\nR: Blood is filtered in the Bowman\'s capsule at the rate of 3-3.5 litres per minute.','A is true but R is false','Glomerular filtration happens at the rate of 1100-1200 (i.e. 1.1-1.2 litres) per minute. (NCERT Class 11 pg 293)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-293','11-208',NULL),
(191316,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Arrangement of Podocytes are such that they leave small spaces called Columns of Bertini. \n\nR: These slits/spaces help in reabsorbtion and secretion.','Both A and R are false.','The slits/spaces spaces left after arrangement of podocyes are known as FILTRATION SLITS or SLIT PORES. They help in ULTRA-FILTRATION of the blood in the renal corpuscle. Columns of Bertini is the columns of cortical extension between the medullary pyramids. (NCERT Class 11 pg 293)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-293','11-208',NULL),
(191317,'biology','Read the following statements about URINE FORMATION; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. In a normal individual, GFR is 1100 mL/min. \n\n2. Amount of filtrate produced by kidneys in a day is 180 litres. \n\n3. Ultra-filtration is performed by the Loop of Henle.','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','GFR in a healthy individual is approximately 125 ml/minute, i.e., 180 litres per day. Ultra-filtration is done by the Malphigian corpuscles (glomerulus + bowman\'s capsule) (NCERT Class 11 pg 294)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(121317,'chemistry','What is the correct IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(12121)','4-ethyl-3-methyloctane','Longest carbon chain with maximum substituents is choosen. (NEET 2003)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4-ethyl-3-methyloctane','3-methyl-4-ethyloctane','2,3-diethylheptane','5-ethyl-6-methyloctane','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121317q.png'),
(191318,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: GFR Is maintained by Juxtaglomerular Apparatus. \n\nR: Fall in GFR leads to release of renin from JGA which restores GFR back to normal.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','When GFR falls, JGA releases Renin which constricts the efferent arteriole and increases the glomerular filtration pressure which leads to restoring the GFR back to normal. (NCERT Class 11 pg 294)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191319,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Most of the filtrate in the nephron is reabsorbed. \n\nR: Out of 180 litres of filtrate per day, the amount of filtrate reabsorbed is 50%.','A is true but R is false','Out of 180 litres of filtrate per day, 99% of filtrate is reabsorbed from the nephron such that only 1.5 litres of urine is passed outside the body. (NCERT Class 11 pg 294)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191320,'biology','Read the following statements about NEPHRONS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. PCT is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium with brush border. \n\n2. Ascending limb of Henle\'s loop is permeable to water. \n\n3. DCT is involved in obligate reabsorption of water and sodium ions.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Ascending limb of Henle\'s loop is impermeable to water and is responsible to maintenance of medullary osmotic gradient. DCT is involved in CONDITIONAL reabsorption of water. (NCERT Class 11 pg 294)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Only 2 is correct','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191321,'biology','Read the following statements about PARTS OF NEPHRONS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. Large amounts of water is excreted in the collecting duct. \n\n2. Collecting duct allows passage of uric acid into interstitium to maintain osmotic gradent. \n\n3. Collecting duct is useful for acid-base and ionic balance of body.','Only 3 is correct','Large amount of water is absorbed into the collecting duct to produce a concentrated urine. These ducts also allow small amount of urea to pass into interstitium to maintain osmotic gradient. (NCERT Class 11 pg 295)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Assertion reason and multiple statement question.',NULL,'2','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 3 is correct','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(191401,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n \nA: PCT is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium with brush borders. \n\nR: Very small amount of solutes from the filtrate is absorbed in PCT.','A is true but R is false','PCT is lined by brush bordered simple cuboidal epithelium which increases surface area of absorption and hence 90-95% of solutes get absorbed in PCT. (NCERT Class 11 pg 295 - check fig. 19.5)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(191402,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: 2 limbs of vasa recta form a counter-current with respect to the renal tubules. \n\nR: Vasa recta is involved in counter-current multiplier which helps to pass diluted urine.','A is true but R is false','2 limbs of vasa recta is in opposite directions w.r.t the renal tubules which forms a counter-current and involved in counter-curretn exchange. This helps to pass concentrated urine. (NCERT Class 11 pg 296)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-296','11-211',NULL),
(191403,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: There is a medullary osmotic gradient in the kidneys. \n\nR: The osmolarity increases from 1200 mOsmol/L in cortex to 1500 mosmol/L in medulla.','A is true but R is false','The osmolarity increases from 300 mOsmol/L in cortex to 1200 mOsmol/L in medulla due to counter-current mechanism between closely located renal tubule and vasa recta. (NCERT Class 11 pg 296)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-296','11-211',NULL),
(191404,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Ascending limb of loop of henle exchanges NaCl with the ascending portion of vasa recta. \n\nR: Vasa recta is responsible to pass out urine containing lots of water.','Both A and R are false.','Ascending limb of henle\'s loop exchanges NaCl with the descending portion of vasa recta. Vasa recta ensures to pass out concentrated urine (i.e. urine with less amount of water). (NCERT Class 11 pg 296)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-296','11-211',NULL),
(191405,'biology','Read the following statements about MEDULLARY INTERSTITIUM GRADIENT; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. Medullary interstitial gradient is maintained by urea in interstitium. \n\n2. This gradient helps to absorb water from the ascending limb of henle\'s loop. \n\n3. Collecting duct allows small passage of uric acid into interstitium which helps to maintain this gradient.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','The medullary interstitium gradient helps to absorb water from the collecting ducts (not ascending henle\'s loop). The collecting ducts allow small passage of urea into interstitium which maintains this gradient. (NCERT Class 11 pg 295 and  297)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','11-295','11-210',NULL),
(121318,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name of CH₂=CH – CH₂– CH₂– C≣CH ?','Hex-1-ene-5-yne','The double bond gets priority over triple bond. (NEET 2002)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1,5-hexenyne','Hex-1-ene-5-yne','1-hexyne-5-ene','1,5-hexynene','NEW','11-341','11-263',NULL),
(191406,'biology','Read the following statements about ADH HORMONE; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. Anti-Diuretic Hormone (ADH) is released from anterior pituitary. \n\n2. ADH enhances water re-absorption from the collecting ducts. \n\n3. Decreased ECF volume triggers ADH release.','Both 2 and 3 are correct','ADH is released from the neuro-hypophysis (i.e. posterior pituitary). It enhances water absorption from collecting ducts through aqua-porin channels. ADH release is triggered by decreased blood volume (i.e. Extra-Cellular Fluid Volume - ECF) (NCERT Class 11 pg 297)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 2 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-297','11-212',NULL),
(191407,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: RAAS helps to maintain blood pressure through modification of ECF volume. \n\nR: RAAS releases Angiotensin II which is a powerful vaso-dialator.','A is true but R is false','RAAS releases renin which converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I. It is converted in lungs to Angiotensin II which is a powerful vaso-constrictor. It modifies GFR and maintains blood pressure. (NCERT Class 11 pg 297)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-297','11-212',NULL),
(191408,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Atrial Natri-uretic Factor deceases blood pressure. \n\nR: ANF is released when the blood flow to the atria decreases.','A is true but R is false','ANF is released when the blood flow to the heart increases such that ANF causes vaso-dilation and reduces the peripheral vascular resistance and reduces blood pressure. (NCERT Class 11 pg 297)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-297','11-212',NULL),
(191409,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Angiotensin II is formed in the lungs. \n\nR: Angiotensin Converting Enzyme is located in pulmonary capillary endothelium.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Angiotensin I is converted to Angiotensin II in the lungs with the help of enzyme Angiotensin Converting Enzyme (ACE) released from pulmonary capillary endothelium. Angiotensin II is an important vaso-constrictor and a part of RAAS mechanism which maintains the GFR and blood pressure in the body. (NCERT Class 11 pg 297)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-297','11-212',NULL),
(191410,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Micturition involves release of urine to outside body. \n\nR: During micturition,  the detrusor muscle relaxes and the urethral sphincters contract.','A is true but R is false','During micturation, the muscle in urinary bladder (called DETRUSOR MUSCLE) contracts while the urethral sphincters relax and this leads to release of urine outside body. (NCERT Class 11 pg 298)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(191411,'biology','Read the following statements about URINE; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. Urine has pH of 6.0 (slightly basic). \n\n2. Normal volume of urine in an individual is 1-1.5 litres/day. \n\n3. Urine yellow colour is produced due to presence of urobilin.','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Urine is slightly acidic (pH 6). Urine has characteristic colour due to urobilin (bile derivative) and a characteristic odour. Normal volume of urine passed is 1-1.5 litres/day. (NCERT Class 11 pg 298)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 is incorrect','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(191412,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: The presence of ketone bodies in urine is called KETONURIA. \n\nR: The common ketone bodies in urine are omega-3 fatty acids and proteins.','A is true but R is false','Presence of ketone bodies in urine is called ketonuria and indicative of diabetes mellitus. The common ketone bodies seen in urine is BETA-HYDROXY-BUTYRIC ACID AND ACETO-ACETIC ACID. (NCERT Class 11 pg 298)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(191413,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Bilirubin is excreted out through the respiratory system into urine. \n\nR: Bilirubin is produced from degradation of hemoglobin and passed out through lungs.','Both A and R are false.','Bilirubin is produced from degradation of hemoglobin, metabolised in kidneys and excreted out through the Gastro-Intestinal system. Bilirubin, Biliverdin, Cholestrol, Vitemins are all excreted out through the GI tract. (NCERT Class 11 pg 298)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(191414,'biology','Read the following statements about SWEAT AND SEBUM; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. Sweat contains urea, NaCl and lactic acid. \n\n2. Sweat is produced from the "master gland" of the body. \n\n3. Sweat and sebum also helps in eliminating substances through secretions.','Only 2 is incorrect','Master Gland of the body is known as PITUITARY GLAND and it does\'nt produce sweat. Sweat is produced from the sweat glands present in the dermis of the skin. (NCERT Class 11 pg 298)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(121319,'chemistry','Which nomenclature is not according to IUPAC system in the given diagram(12122)','A','Ketonic position should get lowest number possible. (NEET 2001)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','D','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121319q.png'),
(191415,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: During dialysis, the drained blood is mixed with heparin. \n\nR: Heparin causes coagulation by activating platelets and clotting factors.','A is true but R is false','Heparin is a both In-vivo and in-vitro anti-coagulant. It prevents clotting of bolld and maintains it in a fluid state while the blood gets filtered during hemo-dialysis. (NCERT Class 11 pg 298)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(191416,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Renal calculi refers to the stone formation (oxalates, phosphates,etc.) inside the kidneys. \n\nR: Renal calculi is produced by a process called CHOLELITHIASIS.','A is true but R is false','Cholelithiasis is a process of stone formation in the gll bladder (not kidneys). Renal calculi refers to stones in the kidney. The stones are usually calcium phosphates, oxalates, etc. They are masses of insoluble or crystalised salts in the renal pelvices and calyces. (NCERT Class 11 pg 299)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-299','11-214',NULL),
(191417,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Glomerulonephritis is a kidney disease. \n\nR: It is not characterised by inflammation of the renal tubules leading to oliguria, hematuria and RBC casts in urine.','A is true but R is false','Glomerulonephritis is a glomerular cause of kidney disease where there is inflammation of the glomeruli caused by prior secondary causes (e.g. infections). The main clinical features are oliguria (reduced urine output), hematuria (blood in urine) and RBC casts in urine. (NCERT Class 11 pg 299)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-299','11-214',NULL),
(191418,'biology','Read the following statements about SUBSTANCES IN URINE and  BLOOD; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n \n1. Accumulation of excess urea in blood is uremia. \n\n2. Excess glucose is urine is called glycosuria. \n\n3. Excess proteins (> 3 gm/day) in urine is physiological.','Only 3 is incorrect','Presence of >3 gm/day protein in urine is pathological and indicative of kidney disease. This is also known as SEVERE PROTEINURIA as seen in glomerular diseases (nephrotic syndrome).','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 is incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(191419,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Rennin helps to maintain the blood pressure by RAAS mechanism. \n\nR: Rennin is secreted from JGA in the nephrons.','Both A and R are false.','Rennin (not RENIN) is a milk curdling enzyme secreted in stomach and intestine which converts casein protein of milk into Ca-Para-Caseinate. (NCERT Class 11 pg 297)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-297','11-212',NULL),
(191420,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Anti-diuretic hormone is secreted when the blood flow to the atria increases. \n\nR: ADH reduces the reabsorption of water from kidneys and reduces blood pressure.','Both A and R are false.','ADH is released from the neuro-hypophysis (i.e. posterior pituitary). It enhances water absorption from collecting ducts through aqua-porin channels. ADH release is triggered by decreased blood volume (i.e. Extra-Cellular Fluid Volume - ECF). Instead ANF (atrial natriuretic factor) is released upon increase blood flow through atria; causes diuresis and reduces blood pressure (NCERT Class 11 pg 297)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-297','11-212',NULL),
(191421,'biology','Read the following statements about NEPHRONS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect? \n1. The distal convoluted tubule continues as the collecting duct. \n\n2. The collecting ducts drain into the renal pelvis through medullary pyramids. \n\n3.  From the renal pelvis, urine goes into ureters and then into the uterus.','Only 3 is incorrect','Urine travels from the collecting ducts into calyces. From there they travel into the renal pelvis through the pyramids and then through the ureter into the urinary bladder. From bladder, it travels through urethra to outside. (NCERT Class 11 pg 293)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-293','11-208',NULL),
(191422,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):  \n\nA: Amount of blood filtered per minute by glomerulus is one-third of the cardiac output. \n\nR: The cardiac output of a normal person is 3 litres/min.','Both A and R are false.','Amount of blood filtered per minute by kidneys is 1100-1200 mL, which is roughly one-fifth of the cardiac output. So the cardiac output is approx 5.5-6 litres/min. (NCERT Class 11 pg 293)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-293','11-208',NULL),
(191423,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):  \n\nA: Tubular secretion is a part of urine formation mechanism. \n\nR: Hydrogen ion and Potassium ions are re-absorbed from the renal tubules to maintain acid base balance.','A is true but R is false','Ultrafiltration, tubular reabsorption and tubular secretion are the steps of urine formation. K+ and H⁺ ions are actively secreted into the filtrate by tubular cells to maintain acid-base and electrolyte balance of the body. (NCERT Class 11 pg 294)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-294','11-209',NULL),
(191424,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n \nA: Micturition is initiated when the gall-bladder wall gets filled with urine. \n\nR: The gall bladder contracts and urine is released into the urethra.','Both A and R are false.','Micturition reflex is initiated when the urinary bladder gets filled with urine resulting in stretching of the its walls. The detrusor muscle of urinary bladder contracts and send urine from bladder to urethra. (NCERT Class 11 pg 298)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-298','11-213',NULL),
(191425,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):  \n\nA: Dialysing fluid during hemodialysis contains same composition as plasma including urea.\n \nR: This helps to prevent the filtering of blood and leaves the nitrogenous wastes inside blood.','Both A and R are false.','Dialysing fluid contains same composition as plasma except the NITROGENOUS WASTES (e.g. urea). The tube is made of cellophane (permeable) so that filtering of blood is possible. 9NCERT Class 11 pg 299)','Human Physiology >> Excretory Products and their Elimination >> Human Excretory System>>Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-299','11-214',NULL),
(70101,'chemistry','Both physical and chemical equilibrium processes are __________ in nature.','Dynamic','Dynamic nature of equilibrium involved in physical and chemical processes.\n(NCERT chemistry class 11;Chapter 7, page 192 ; under objectives)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Overview',NULL,'1','mcq','Dynamic','Static','Both a and b','None of the above','NEW','11-192','11-168',NULL),
(70102,'chemistry','A pure liquid is placed inside a closed chamber at a particular temperature. After sometime the pressure inside the closed chamber indicates','Constant value and > 1 atm.','It gives rise to a constant vapour pressure because of an equilibrium in which the number of molecules leaving the liquid equals the number returning to liquid from the vapour state. We say that  the system has reached equilibrium state at this stage. It shoud be remembered that air is presnt inside the chamber at 1 atm pressure. (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 192 ; paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Overview',NULL,'3','mcq','Fluctuating value and > 1 atm.','Constant value and > 1 atm.','Fluctuating value and < 1 atm .','Constant value and < 1 atm .','NEW','11-192','11-168',NULL),
(70103,'chemistry','At a state of dynamic equilibrium between liquid and vapour, what do happen between two phases?','Rate of evaporation = rate of condensation','Thus, at equilibrium, the rate of evaporation is equal to the rate of condensation. (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 192 ; paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Overview',NULL,'1','mcq','Rate of evaporation = rate of condensation','No. of moles at liquid phase = No. of moles at vapour phase','Mole fraction of two phases becomes 1/2','Rate of evaporation=rate of crystallisation','NEW','11-192','11-168',NULL),
(70104,'chemistry','A+B ⇌C , For this reaction after sometime at equilibrium, which is the most correct option','A,B & C -all are present','Based on the extent to which the reactions proceed to reach the state of chemical equilibrium, these maybe classified in three groups.\n(i) The reactions that proceed nearly to completion and only negligible concentrations of the reactants are left. In some cases, it may not be even possible to detect these experimentally.\n(ii) The reactions in which only small amounts of products are formed and most of the reactants remain unchanged at equilibrium stage.\n(iii) The reactions in which the concentrations of the reactants and products are comparable, when the system is in equilibrium.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 193 ; paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Overview',NULL,'1','mcq','Only C is present','Only A & C are present','Only B & C  are present','A,B & C -all are present','NEW','11-193','11-169',NULL),
(70105,'chemistry','The extent of reaction in a particular equilibrium depends upon -->','Conc. of reactants & temperature','The extent of a reaction in equilibrium  varies with the experimental conditions such as concentrations of reactants, temperature, etc.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 193 ; paragraph 2 )','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Overview',NULL,'1','mcq','Temperature','Conc. of reactants & temperature','Conc. of reactants','None of the above','NEW','11-193','11-169',NULL),
(70106,'biology','Regarding simple cuboidal epithelium which of the following is FALSE','Also called pavement epithelium as they appear like tiles on the floor.','The cuboidal epithelium is composed of a single layer of cube-like cells. This is commonly found in ducts of glands and tubular parts of nephrons in kidneys and its main functions are secretion and absorption. The epithelium of proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) of nephron in the kidney has microvilli,; Squamous epithelium is also called pavement epithelium as they are flattened cells that appear as tiles on the floor.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:101, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Is composed of a single layer of cube-like cells','Is commonly found in ducts of glands and tubular parts in kidney. it is associated with microvilli in PCT','Takes part in secretion and absorption','Also called pavement epithelium as they appear like tiles on the floor.','NEW','11-101',NULL,NULL),
(70107,'biology','The function of ciliated epithelium is\n\n1)Their function is to move mucus in a specific direction over the epithelium.\n\n2)Their function is to move particles in a specific direction over the epithelium, example propagation of ova in the fallopian tube\n\n3)Their function is to protect organ and replace worn out tissue.','only 1) and 2)','If the columnar or cuboidal cells bear cilia on their free surface they are called ciliated epithelium.Their function is to move particles or mucus in a specific direction over the epithelium (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:101, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'3','mcq','only 3)','All of them','only 1) and 3)','only 1) and 2)','NEW','11-101',NULL,NULL),
(70108,'biology','Identify the type of epithelium from the diagram (0701)','Squamous','NCERT class 11 Page 101 Figre 7.1','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'1','mcq','Squamous','Cuboidal','Columnar','Ciliated','NEW','11-101',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/70108q.png'),
(70109,'biology','Identify the type of epithelium from the diagram (0702)','Cuboidal','NCERT class 11 Page 101 Figre 7.1','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'1','mcq','Squamous','Cuboidal','Columnar','Ciliated','NEW','11-101',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/70109q.png'),
(70110,'biology','Identify the type of epithelium from the diagram (0703)','Columnar','NCERT class 11 Page 101 Figre 7.1','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'1','mcq','Squamous','Cuboidal','Columnar','Neural','NEW','11-101',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/70110q.png'),
(70111,'biology','Which type of tissue forms glands?','Epithelium','Either columnar or cuboidal epithelial tissue forms glands (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:102, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'1','mcq','Nervous','Epithelium','Muscular','Connective','NEW','11-101',NULL,NULL),
(70112,'biology','Epithelia are involved in all except-','Connection','Epithelial tissue forms outer layer and are not involved in connection.Connective tissue is involved in connection (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:102, paragraph no:4)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Protection','Connection','Secretion or excretion','Absorption','NEW','11-101',NULL,NULL),
(70113,'biology','Ciliated epithelium is present in','Inner surface of hollow organs, Bronchioles and fallopian tubes.','Ciliated epithelium function is to move particles or mucus in a specific direction over the epithelium. They are mainly present in the inner surface of hollow organs like bronchioles and fallopian tubes (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:101, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Inner surface of hollow organs, Bronchioles and fallopian tubes.','Bronchioles, Gut epithelium and tubules of kidney.','Fallopian tubes, tubules of kidney and gut epithelium.','excretory tubules, epididymis and bladder epithelium.','NEW','11-101',NULL,NULL),
(70114,'biology','Goblet cells (in gut) are a type of-','Unicellular gland','Goblet cells are found in the gut and helps in digestion (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:102, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Multicellular gland','Unicellular gland','Complex Multicellular gland','Salivary gland','NEW','11-102',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(70115,'biology','Identify the type of gland from the diagram (0704)','Unicellular gland','NCERT class 11 Page 102 Figure 7.2','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'1','mcq','Multicellular gland','Unicellular gland','Intercellular gland','Salivary gland','NEW','11-102',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/70115q.png'),
(70116,'biology','Identify the type of gland from the diagram (0705)','Multicellular gland','NCERT class 11 Page 102 Figure 7.2','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'1','mcq','Multicellular gland','Unicellular gland','Liver','Pancreas','NEW','11-102',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/70116q.png'),
(70117,'biology','The inability to absorb nutrients may be due to damage of which type of epithelium -','Simple columnar','Simple columnar epithelium forms the lining of the gut wall and hence helps in absorption (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:101, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Stratified squamous','Simple cuboidal','Simple columnar','Simple squamous','NEW','11-101',NULL,NULL),
(70118,'biology','Match the glandular epithelium type with the respective aspects','Bear ducts or tubes-exocrine glands,ductless glands-endocrine glands,Endocrine glands secrete-Hormones,Exocrine glands secrete-Enzymes','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:102, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'2','match','Bear ducts or tubes,exocrine glands','Ductless glands,endocrine glands','Endocrine glands secrete,Hormones','Exocrine glands secrete,Enzymes','NEW','11-102',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/70118q.png'),
(70119,'biology','Compound epithelium is made of more than one layer (multi-layered) of cells isn\'t present among','Lining of Male and Female Urinary and Genital Tract','Compound epithelium is made of more than one layer (multi-layered) of cells and thus has a limited role in secretion and absorption.Their main function is to provide protection against chemical and mechanical stresses. They cover the dry surface of the skin, the moist surface of buccal cavity, pharynx, inner lining of ducts of salivary glands and of pancreatic ducts (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:102, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'1','mcq','Dry surface of the skin  and  moist surface of buccal cavity','Pharynx','Inner lining of ducts of salivary glands and of pancreatic ducts','Lining of Male and Female Urinary and Genital Tract','NEW','11-102',NULL,NULL),
(70120,'biology','The terms squamous and cuboidal apply to the ______ of cells in ______ tissue','Shape, epithelial','Squamous and cuboidal are epithelial tissue (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:101, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'1','mcq','Location, connection','Shape, connection','Location, nerve','Shape, epithelial','NEW','11-101',NULL,NULL),
(70121,'biology','The 200 different cell types identified in humans are classified into the four tissue types -','Connective, muscle, epithelial, nerve','The structure of the cells vary according to their function. Therefore, the tissues are different and are broadly classified into four types :  (i) Epithelial, (ii) Connective, (iii) Muscular and (iv) Neural.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:100, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'1','mcq','Blood, connective, muscle, nerve','Epithelia, supportive, nerve, muscle','Connective, muscle, epithelial, nerve','Supportive, connective, nerve, blood','NEW','11-100',NULL,NULL),
(70122,'biology','Which of the following statements is false about the glands?','Endocrine glands secrete a variety of enzymes only','Endocrine glands secrete harmones (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:102, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Goblet cells secrete mucus','Exocrine glands possess duct for secretion of mucus, milk, saliva, earwax, digestive enzymes, oil and other cell products','Glandular epithelium consists of specialized columnar or cuboidal cells','Endocrine glands secrete a variety of enzymes only','NEW','11-102',NULL,NULL),
(70123,'biology','Adjacent epithelial cells are joined together by -','Desmosomes','Desmosomes are also called as adhering junctions (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:102, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'3','mcq','Desmosomes','Oxysomes','Desmonemes','Plasmodesmata','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(70124,'biology','Cell junction called tight, adhering and gap junctions are found mainly in -','Epithelial tissue','Three types of cell junctions are found in the epithelium and other tissues. These are called as tight, adhering and gap junctions. Tight junctions help to stop substances from leaking across a tissue. Adhering junctions perform cementing to keep neighbouring cells together. Gap junctions facilitate the cells to communicate with each other by connecting the cytoplasm of adjoining cells, for rapid transfer of ions, small molecules and sometimes big molecules.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:102, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Connective tissue','Epithelial tissue','Muscular tissue','Nervous tissue','NEW','11-102',NULL,NULL),
(70125,'biology','Match the following functions of cell junctions in a tissue','Stop substances from leaking-Tight junctions,Perform Cementing to keep cells together-Adhering junctions,Connecting the cytoplasm of adjoining cells to communicate-Gap junctions,Joins adjacent epithelial cells-Desmosomes','Three types of cell junctions are found in the epithelium and other tissues. These are called as tight, adhering and gap junctions. Tight junctions help to stop substances from leaking across a tissue. Adhering junctions perform cementing to keep neighbouring cells together. Gap junctions facilitate the cells to communicate with each other by connecting the cytoplasm of adjoining cells, for rapid transfer of ions, small molecules and sometimes big molecules.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:102, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'3','match','Stop substances from leaking,Tight junctions','Perform Cementing to keep cells together,Adhering junctions','Connecting the cytoplasm of adjoining cells to communicate,Gap junctions','Joins adjacent epithelial cells,Desmosomes','NEW','11-102',NULL,NULL),
(70126,'biology','Match the following types of cells with their characteristic epithelium','Flattened cells-squamous epithelium,cube-like cells-cuboidal epithelium,tall and slender cells-columnar epithelium,multilayered cells-Compound epithelium','The squamous epithelium is made of a single thin layer of flattened cells with irregular boundaries. They are found in the walls of blood vessels and air sacs of lungs and are involved in functions like forming a diffusion boundary. The cuboidal epithelium is composed of a single layer of cube-like cells. This is commonly found in ducts of glands and tubular parts of nephrons in kidneys and its main functions are secretion and absorption. The epithelium of proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) of nephron in the kidney has microvilli. The columnar epithelium is composed of a single layer of tall and slender cells. Their nuclei are located at the base. Free surface may have microvilli. They are found in the lining of stomach and intestine and help in secretion and absorption. If the columnar or cuboidal cells bear cilia on their free surface they are called ciliated epithelium.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:101, paragraph no:1-2)\\','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Epithelial Tissue',NULL,'3','match','Flattened cells,squamous epithelium','Cube-like cells,cuboidal epithelium','Tall and slender cells,columnar epithelium','Multilayered cells,Compound epithelium','NEW','11-101',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/70126q.png'),
(70201,'chemistry','Choose the correct statements out of the flollowing\n\n(i) At equilibrium the rate of both forward and backward reaction may or may not be equal\n\n(ii)At equilibrium the concentration of reactants and products may or may not be comparable\n\n(iii)The temperature at which a pure liquid is in dynamic equilibrium with its solid phase is called normal freezing point of that substance','Only (ii) and (iii) is correct','Based on the extent to which the reactions proceed to reach the state of chemical equilibrium, these may be classified in three groups.\n(i) The reactions that proceed nearly to completion and only negligible concentrations of the reactants are left. In some cases, it may not be even possible to detect these experimentally.\n(ii) The reactions in which only small amounts of products are formed and most of the reactants remain unchanged at equilibrium stage.\n(iii) The reactions in which the concentrations of the reactants and products are comparable, when the system is in equilibrium.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 193 ; paragraph 1)\n\nAt equilibrium the rate of both forward and backward reactions are equal.(NCERT Chemistry  class 11; page 193 paragraph 1)\n\nFor any pure substance at atmospheric pressure, the temperature at which the solid and liquid phases are at equilibrium is called the normal melting point or normal freezing point of the substance.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 193 ; paragraph 5)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Physical processes',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (i) and (ii) is correct','Only (i) and (iii) is correct','Only (ii) and (iii) is correct','(i), (ii) annd (iii) are correct','NEW','11-193','11-169',NULL),
(70202,'chemistry','Suppose a petridish fiiled with water is placed inside a previously dried closed vessel fitted with a manometer. What do happen after sometime ?','At first mercury level increases, then slows down to attain a constant value','We consider the example of a transparent box carrying a U-tube with mercury (manometer).Drying agent like anhydrous calcium chloride (or phosphorus penta-oxide) is placed for a few hours in the box. After removing the drying agent by tilting the box on one side, a watch glass (or petri dish) containing water is quickly placed inside the box. It will be observed that the mercury level in the right limb of the manometer slowly increases and finally attains a constant value, that is, the pressure inside the box increases and reaches a constant value. Also the volume of water in the watch glass decreases.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 193 ; paragraph 6 )','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Physical processes',NULL,'2','mcq','Mercury level is increasing gradually','Mercury level decreases','At first mercury level increases, then slows down to attain a constant value','Can\'t be predicted','NEW','11-193','11-169',NULL),
(70203,'chemistry','Two closed chambers A & B contain 5 mL of two different liquid P & Q at a certain temperature .If pressure of A system is less than B, then which option is appropriate ?','Q is more volatile than P','If the above experiment is repeated with methyl alcohol, acetone and ether, it is observed that different liquids have different equilibrium vapour pressures at the same temperature, and the liquiuid which has a higher vapour pressure is more volatile and has a lower boiling point.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 194 ; paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Physical processes',NULL,'2','mcq','Q is more volatile than P','P is more volatile than Q','Both are equally volatile','Can\'t be determined','NEW','11-194','11-170',NULL),
(70204,'chemistry','Time taken by a pure liquid for complete evaporation depends on which of the following factor?','Temperature','It is observed that in all such cases the liquid eventually disappears and the time taken for complete evaporation depends on (i) the nature of theliquid, (ii) the amount of the liquid and (iii) the temperature.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 194 ; paragraph 3)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Physical processes',NULL,'1','mcq','Temperature','Pressure','Density','Both a and b','NEW','11-194','11-170',NULL),
(70205,'chemistry','The b.p of pure water is 100 C at 1 atm. pressure. In the beautiful city of Shimla the b.p of water --->','Decreases','Water and water vapour are in equilibrium position at atmospheric pressure (1.013 bar) and at 100°C in a closed vessel. The boiling point of water is 100°C at 1.013 bar pressure.For any pure liquid at one atmospheric pressure (1.013 bar), the temperature at which the liquid and vapours are at equilibrium is called normal boiling point of the liquid. Boiling point of the liquid depends on the atmospheric pressure. It depends on the altitude of the place; at high altitude the boiling point decreases.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 194 ; paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Physical processes',NULL,'3','mcq','Constant','Decreases','Increases','Can\'t be determined','NEW','11-194','11-170',NULL),
(70206,'chemistry','If we heat a saturated sugar solution (which initially at 25 ᵒC) to 80 ᵒC, what is correct then?','Becomes unsaturated','We know from our experience that we can dissolve only a limited amount of salt or sugar in a given amount of water at room temperature. If we make a thick sugar syrup solution by dissolving sugar at a higher temperature, sugar crystals separate out if we cool the syrup to the room temperature. We call it a saturated solution when no more of solute can be dissolved in it at a given temperature. The concentration of the solute in  saturated solution depends upon the temperature.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 195 ; paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Physical processes',NULL,'2','mcq','Remains saturated','Becomes oversaturated','Becomes unsaturated','Sugar crystals precipitate out','NEW','11-195','11-171',NULL),
(70207,'chemistry','After opening a soda water bottle, some of dissolved CO₂ fizzes out. What do you conclude?','Solubility increases as pressure increases','When a soda water bottle is opened, some of the carbon dioxide gas dissolved in it fizzes out rapidly. The phenomenon arises due to  difference in solubility of carbon dioxide at different pressures. There is equilibrium between the molecules in the gaseous state and the molecules dissolved in the liquid under pressure i.e., CO₂ (gas) = CO₂ (in solution) This equilibrium is governed by Henry’s law, which states that the mass of a gas dissolved in a given mass of a solvent at any temperature is proportional to the pressure of the gas above the solvent. This amount decreases with increase of temperature. The soda water bottle is sealed under pressure of gas when its solubility in water is high. As soon as the bottle is opened, some of the dissolved carbon dioxide gas escapes to reach a new equilibrium condition required for the lower pressure, namely its partial pressure in the atmos. (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 195; paragraph 3 and 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Physical processes',NULL,'3','mcq','Solubility increases as pressure decreases','Solubility increases as pressure increases','Solubility decreases as pressure increases','No conclusion','NEW','11-195','11-171',NULL),
(70208,'chemistry','For dissolution of gases in liquids, the concentration of a gas in liquid and pressure of the gas is related as','Concentration is directly proportional to the pressure','For dissolution of gases in liquids, the concentration of a gas in liquid is proportional to the pressure (concentration) of the gas over the liquid.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 195 ; paragraph 5)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Physical processes',NULL,'1','mcq','Concentration is directly proportional to the pressure','As concentration increases pressure decreases','There is no relationship between concentration and pressure','Concentration is inversly proportional to pressure','NEW','11-195','11-171',NULL),
(70209,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement -->\n I. In an isolated system, dynamic equilibrium can be achieved.  \nII. It is not necessary that the forward and backward rate between phases of a substance remains same at dynamic equilibrium.','II is false but I is true','For the physical processes discussed above, following characteristics are common to the system at equilibrium:\n(i) Equilibrium is possible only in a closed system at a given temperature.\n(ii) Both the opposing processes occur at the same rate and there is a dynamic but stable condition.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 196; paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Physical processes',NULL,'3','mcq','I is false but II is true','Both are false','Both are true','II is false but I is true','NEW','11-196','11-172',NULL),
(70210,'chemistry','Select the correct statements regarding a reaction at equilibrium\n\n(i)The rate of forward and backward reactions are equal.\n\n(ii)The composition of the reaction mixture remains constant.\n\n(iii)The reaction stops at equilibrium.','Only (i) and (ii) is correct','Use of isotope (deuterium) in the formation of ammonia clearly indicates that chemical reactions reach a state of dynamic equilibrium in which the rates of forward and reverse reactions are equal and there is no net change in composition.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 198; paragraph) \nIf the reaction had simply stopped when they reached equilibrium, then there would have been no mixing of isotopes in this way.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 198; paragraph 1)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Physical processes',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (iii) is correct','Only (i) and (iii) is correct','Only (i) and (ii) is correct','All are correct','NEW','11-198','11-174',NULL),
(70211,'biology','Which of the following statements about the dense irregular connective tissue is correct?','Fibres are densely packed, containing fibroblast and many fibres (mostly collagen) that are oriented differently. It is present in skin','Dense irregular connective tissue has fibroblasts and many fibres (mostly collagen) that are oriented differently.This tissue is present in the skin (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:103, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Connective Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Fibres are densely packed, containing fibroblast and many fibres (mostly collagen) that are oriented differently. It is present in skin','Fibres are loosely packed, containing abundant adipocytes and many fibres with peripheral nucleus.','Fibres are loosely packed, containing fibroblast and many fibres (mostly collagen) that are oriented differently. It is present in skin','Fibres are densely packed, containing abundant adipocytes and many fibres with peripheral nucleus.','NEW','11-103',NULL,NULL),
(70212,'biology','Which of the following is specialized connective tissue?','Bone,cartilage and blood','All are forms of specialized connective tissue (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:103, paragraph no:1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Connective Tissue',NULL,'1','mcq','Bone,cartilage and blood','Bone and Bladder','Cartilage and Bladder','Brain, spinal cord','NEW','11-103',NULL,NULL),
(70213,'biology','_______ is a fluid connective tissue','Blood','Blood is a fluid connective tissue containing plasma, red blood cells (RBC), white blood cells (WBC) and platelets, (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:104, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Connective Tissue',NULL,'1','mcq','Blood','Tendon','Adipose','Muscle','NEW','11-104',NULL,NULL),
(70214,'biology','Which of the following is the predominant cell type in connective tissue proper?','Fibroblasts','Fibroblast produces the collagen fibres and also secretes the ground substance (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:103, paragraph no:3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Connective Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Fibroblasts','Lymphocytes','Macrophages','Mast cells','NEW','11-103',NULL,NULL),
