(70230,'biology','Match the following','Haversian canals -longitudinal,volkmann\'s canal- transverse/ perpendicular to haversian canal','The Haversian canals are straight and parallel to the length of femur. The Volkmann\'s canals run perpendicular to the Haversian canals.Both Haversian canals and Volkmann\'s canals contain blood vessels and nerves that provide essential nutrients and support to the bone cells.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Connective Tissue',NULL,'2','match','Haversian canals,longitudinal','volkmann\'s canal,transverse/ perpendicular to haversian canal',NULL,NULL,'NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(70231,'biology','Bone marrow in some bones is important for which of the following functions \n\n1)Production of RBC \n\n2)Breakdown of RBC in adults \n\n3)Production of WBCs and platelets\n \n4) Production of T lymphocytes','1,3 and 4','Red bone marrow of long bones is the important place for production of RBC.  It is the site of formation of WBCs and platelets also, The T lymphocytes are produced in the bone marrow and they mature in the thymus. Cell destruction after they have served their purpose is a function of spleen (Splenic macrophages) \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:104, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Connective Tissue',NULL,'3','mcq','2 and 3 only','1,3 and 4','All except 4','All are correct','NEW','11-104',NULL,NULL),
(121320,'chemistry','The IUPAC name of (CH3)2CH – CH2 – CH2Br is:','1-bromo-3-methylbutane','Lowest locant rule of substituents is followed to give numbering. (NEET 1996)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1-bromo-3-methylbutane','2-methyl-3-bromopropane','1-bromopentane','2-methyl-4-bromobutane','NEW','11-341','11-263',NULL),
(70232,'biology','Match the following','Blood cells-RBC and WBC,cartilage cells-chondrocytes,bone cells-osteocytes,phagocytic cell in connective tissue-histiocytes','RBCs and WBCs make up blood, chondrocytes make up cartilage, and osteocytes help in bone formation,histiocytes are the macrophages of skin and connective tissue (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:104, paragraph no:1-2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Connective Tissue',NULL,'3','match','Blood cells,RBC and WBC','Cartilage cells,chondrocytes','Bone cells,osteocytes','Phagocytic cell in connective tissue,histiocytes','NEW','11-104',NULL,NULL),
(70233,'biology','Bones have a ____ and ____ ground substance, rich in salts and ____ fibres-','Hard, non-pliable,collagen','Bones have a hard and non-pliable ground substance rich in calcium salts and collagen fibres which give bone its strength.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:104, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Connective Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Soft, pliable,collagen','Hard, pliable,collagen','Hard, non-pliable,collagen','Hard, pliable,salts','NEW','11-104',NULL,NULL),
(70234,'biology','Cytoplasmic process of osteocyte present in the canals of lacuna called as _______','Canaliculi','Cytoplasmic process of osteocyte present in the canals of lacuna called as canaliculi','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Connective Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Canaliculi','Dendrites','Cuniculus','Columella','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(70235,'biology','Which of the following salt predominates in the bone matrix?','Calcium phosphate','Bones have a hard and non-pliable ground substance rich in calcium salts, mainly phosphates of calcium and collagen fibres which give bone its strength.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11: page no:104, paragraph no:2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Connective Tissue',NULL,'2','mcq','Sodium chloride','Magnesium phosphate','Calcium phosphate','Calcium carbonate','NEW','11-104',NULL,NULL),
(70301,'chemistry','C+D ⇌ E , consider the general reaction. The equilibrium constant will be --->','[E]/[C][D]','To answer these questions, let us consider a general reversible reaction:  A + B ⇌ C + D where A and B are the reactants, C and D are the products in the balanced chemicalequation. On the basis of experimental studies of many reversible reactions, the Norwegian chemists Cato Maximillian Guldberg and Peter Waage proposed in 1864 that the concentrations in an equilibrium mixture are related by the following equilibrium equation,(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 199 ; paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Chemical processes',NULL,'1','mcq','[C]/[E][D]','[E]/[C][D]','[E][D]/[C]','[E][C]/[D]','NEW','11-199','11-175',NULL),
(70302,'chemistry','H₂+I₂ ⇌ 2HI , the forward & backward reaction equilibrium constants are K₁ and K₂ respectively. The correct relation between them is','K₁K₂ = 1','Equilibrium constant for the reverse reaction is the inverse of the equilibrium constant for the reaction in the forward direction.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 200 ; paragraph 6,7)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Chemical processes',NULL,'2','mcq','K₁/K₂ = -1','K₁/K₂ = 1','K₁K₂ = 1','K₁K₂ = -1','NEW','11-200','11-176',NULL),
(70303,'chemistry','The following concentrations were obtained for the formation of NH₃ from N₂ and H₂ at equilibrium at 500K. [N₂ ] = 1.5 × 10⁻²M, [H₂ ] = 3.0 ×10⁻² M and [NH₃] = 1.2 ×10⁻²M. Calculate equilibrium constant.','1.06×10^1','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 201 ; problem 7.1) diagram(ne0701)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Chemical processes',NULL,'2','mcq','1.06x10²','1.06x10⁴','1.06×10^1','1.06x10⁻³','NEW','11-201','11-177','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/70303q.png'),
(70304,'chemistry','N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), the relation between Kₚ and K꜀ is','Kp = Kc / (RT)²','In this example, Kp = Kc i.e., both equilibrium constants are equal. However, this is not always the case. For example in reaction N₂ (g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g). (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 202 ; paragraph 5)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Chemical processes',NULL,'2','mcq','Kc = Kp /(RT)²','Kp = Kc / (RT)²','Kp = (RT)² / Kc','none of the above','NEW','11-202','11-178','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/70304q.png'),
(70305,'chemistry','PCl₅, PCl₃ and Cl₂ are at equilibrium at 500 K and having concentration 1.59M PCl₃, 1.59M Cl₂ and 1.41 M PCl₅. What is equilibrium constant of the following reaction, PCl₅ ⇌ PCl₃ + Cl₂','1.79','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 202,203 ; Problem 7.3) diagram(ne0702)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Chemical processes',NULL,'2','mcq','17.9','1.79','0.179','0.0179','NEW','11-202','11-178','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/70305q.png'),
(70306,'chemistry','The value of K = 4.24 at 800K for the reaction, CO (g) + H₂O (g) ⇌ CO₂ (g) + H₂ (g) .Calculate equilibrium concentrations of CO₂, H₂, CO and H₂O at 800 K, if only CO and H₂O are present initially at concentrations of 0.10M each.','[CO₂]=[H₂]=0.067; [CO]=[H₂O]=0.033','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 203; Problem 7.4) diagram(ne0703)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Chemical processes',NULL,'3','mcq','[CO₂]=[H₂]=0.67; [CO]=[H₂O]=0.33','[CO₂]=[H₂]=0.67; [CO]=[H₂O]=0.33','[CO₂]=[H₂]=0.067; [CO]=[H₂O]=0.33','[CO₂]=[H₂]=0.067; [CO]=[H₂O]=0.033','NEW','11-203','11-179',NULL),
