(70788,'biology','Consider the following four statements (A-D) related to the common frog Rana tigrina, and select the correct option stating which ones are true (T) and which ones are false (F).\n\n(A) On dry land, it would die due to lack of O2 if its mouth is forcibly kept closed for a few days\n\n(B) It has a four-chambered heart\n\n(C) On dry land, it turns uricotelic from ureotelic\n\n(D) Its life history is carried out in pond water','TFFT','NCERT XI, Page 117, 118','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','FTTF','TFFT','TTFF','FFTT','NEW','11-118',NULL,NULL),
(70789,'biology','A unique vascular connection between the digestive tract and liver is called________.','2. Hepatic portal system','Class 11 pg 118','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1. Hepato-pancreatic system','2. Hepatic portal system','3. Renal portal system','4. Hepato-cystic system','NEW','11-118',NULL,NULL),
(70790,'biology','Ureters act as urinogenital ducts in:','1. frog\'s males','NCERT XI, page 118','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1. frog\'s males','2. human males','3. human females','4. frogs, both males and females','NEW','11-118',NULL,NULL),
(70791,'biology','Select the correct route for the passage of sperms in male frogs:','Testes → Vasa efferentia → Kidney → Bidder\'s canal → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca',NULL,'Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Testes → Vasa efferentia → Kidney → Seminal Vesicle → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca','Testes → Vasa efferentia → Bidder\'s canal → Uretar → Cloaca','Testes → Vasa efferentia → Kidney → Bidder\'s canal → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca','Testes Bidder\'s canal→Kidney → Vasa efferentia → Urinogenital duct → Cloaca','NEW','11-119',NULL,NULL),
(70792,'biology','Match List-l with List-Il regarding the organs of cockroach:\n\nList I - List II\n\n(a) Crop - (i) grinding the food particles.\n\n(b) Proventriculus - (ii) secretion of digestive juice\n\n(c) Hepatic caecae - (iii) removal of nitrogenous waste\n\n(d) Malpighian tubules - (iv) storage of food\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:','iv,i,ii,iii','Class 11 pg 113','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','iv,i,ii,iii','iii, ii,i,iv','ii,iv,i, iii','i,iv,iii,ii','NEW','11-113',NULL,NULL),
(70801,'chemistry','Suppose we dissolve a Stronger acid HA into water. What are the correct statement(s) among the following','Equilibrium almost shifts to right. H₃O⁺ is weaker acid than HA','Say, if HA is a stronger acid than H₃O⁺, then HA will donate protons and not H₃O⁺, and the solution will mainly A⁻ and H₃O⁺ contain ions. The equilibrium moves in the direction offormation of weaker acid and weaker base because the stronger acid donates a proton to the stronger base.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 216 ; paragraph 5)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'2','mcq','Equilibrium almost shifts to right. H₃O⁺ is weaker acid than HA','A⁻ is stronger base than H₂O','H₃O⁺ is weaker acid than HA','All are correct.','NEW','11-216','11-192',NULL),
(70802,'chemistry','At 298 K the ionic product Kw of pure water is','10⁻¹⁴  M²','Kw = 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ M² (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 217 ; paragraph 6)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁻¹⁴  M²','10⁻¹⁵  M²','10⁻¹⁶  M²','10⁻¹⁷  M²','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(70803,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true regardig pH\n(i)For greater accuracy of value pH meters are used.\n(ii)Variation of pH with temperature should not be ignored.\n(iii)The pH of a solution is defined as the positive logarithm to base 10 of the activity  of hydrogen ions.','Only (i) is correct','The pH of a solution is defined as the negative logarithm to base 10 of the activity  of hydrogen ions. (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 217 ; paragraph 10)\nNote that although Kw may change with temperature the variations in pH with temperature are so small that we often ignore it. (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 218 ; paragraph 6)\nFor greater accuracy pH meters are used.pH meter is a device that measures the pH-dependent electrical potential of the test solution within 0.001 precision.\n(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 218 ; paragraph 9)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'1','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Only (i) and (ii) is correct','All are correct','All are incorrect','NEW','11-217','11-193',NULL),
(70804,'chemistry','The concentration of hydrogen ion in a sample of soft drink is 3.8 × 10⁻³M. what is its pH ?','2.42','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 218 ; Problem 7.16) diagram(ne0714)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'2','mcq','2.42','4.22','3.33','5.52','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(70805,'chemistry','What is the pH of 10⁻⁸ M HCl solution?','6.98','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 218 ; Problem 7.17) diagram(ne0715)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'3','mcq','8','7.22','6.98','5.55','NEW','11-218','11-194',NULL),
(70806,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement \nI. At a given temperature larger the value of pKa , the stronger is the acid. \nII. Ka is dimensionless quantity.','I is false but II is true','At a given temperature T, K is a ameasure of the strength of the acid HX i.e., larger the value of K , the stronger is the acid. K is a dimensionless quantity with the understanding that the standard state concentration of all species is 1M.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 219 ; paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'2','mcq','II is true but I is false','Both are true','I is false but II is true','Both are false','NEW','11-219','11-195',NULL),
(70807,'chemistry','The ionization constant of HF is 3.2 × 10⁻⁴ . Calculate the degree of dissociation of HF in its 0.02 M solution. Calculate the concentration of all species present (H₃O⁺ , F⁻ and HF) in the solution and its pH.','[H₃O⁺] = [F⁻]= 2.4 × 10⁻³ M;\n[HF] =17.6 × 10⁻³ M; pH = 2.62','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 220 ; Problem 7.18) diagram(ne0716)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'3','mcq','[H₃O⁺] = [F⁻]= 2.4 × 10⁻³ M;\n[HF] =17.6 × 10⁻³ M; pH = 2.62','[H₃O⁺] = [F⁻]= 0.24 × 10⁻³ M\n;[HF] =17.6 × 10⁻³ M; pH = 2.62','[H₃O⁺] = [F⁻]= 2.4 × 10⁻³ M\n;[HF] =1.76 × 10⁻³ M; pH = 2.62','[H₃O⁺] = [F⁻]= 2.4 × 10⁻² M\n;[HF] =17.6 × 10⁻³ M; pH = 2.62','NEW','11-220','11-196',NULL),
(70808,'chemistry','The pH of 0.1M monobasic acid is 4.50. Calculate the concentration of species H⁺ , A⁻ at equilibrium. Also, determine the value of pKa of the monobasic acid.','[H⁺] = [A⁻ ] = 3.16 × 10⁻⁵; pKa = 8','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 220,221 ; Problem 7.19) diagram(ne0717)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'2','mcq','[H⁺] = [A⁻ ] = 3.16 × 10⁻⁵; pKa = 8','[H⁺] = [A⁻ ] = 3.16 × 10⁻⁵; pKa = 9','[H⁺] = [A⁻ ] = 3.16 × 10⁻⁵; pKa = 4','[H⁺] = [A⁻ ] = 3.16 × 10⁻⁵; pKa = 5','NEW','11-220','11-196',NULL),
(70809,'chemistry','Calculate the pH of 0.08M solution of hypochlorous acid, HOCl. The ionization constant of the acid is 2.5 × 10⁻⁵ . Determine the percent dissociation of HOCl.','pH =2.85;  Percent dissociation =1.76%','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 221 ; Problem 7.20) diagram(ne0718)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'3','mcq','pH =2.85;  Percent dissociation =17.6%','pH =3.5;  Percent dissociation =1.76%','pH =2.85;  Percent dissociation =0.176%','pH =2.85;  Percent dissociation =1.76%','NEW','11-221','11-197',NULL),
(70810,'chemistry','The pH of 0.004M hydrazine solution is 9.7. Calculate its ionization constant Kb.','8.96 × 10⁻⁷','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 222 ; Problem 7.21) diagram(ne0719)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'2','mcq','8.96 × 10⁻⁷','8.96 × 10⁻⁵','8.96 × 10⁻³','8.96 × 10⁻¹','NEW','11-222','11-198',NULL),
(70811,'chemistry','Calculate the pH of the solution in which 0.2M NH₄Cl and 0.1M NH₃ are present. The pKb of ammonia solution is 4.75.','8.95','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 222 ; Problem 7.22) diagram(ne0720)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'2','mcq','9.85','8.95','7.85','10.95','NEW','11-222','11-198',NULL),
(70812,'chemistry','Determine the degree of ionization and pH of a 0.05M of ammonia solution. The ionization constant of ammonia Kb =  1.77 × 10⁻⁵. Also, calculate the ionization constant of the conjugate acid of ammonia.','pH = 10.97; Ka = 5.64 × 10⁻¹⁰','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 223 ; Problem 7.23) diagram(ne0721)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'2','mcq','pH = 11.97; Ka = 5.64 × 10⁻¹⁰','pH = 8.97; Ka = 5.64 × 10⁻¹⁰','pH = 12.97; Ka = 5.64 × 10⁻¹⁰','pH = 10.97; Ka = 5.64 × 10⁻¹⁰','NEW','11-223','11-199',NULL),
(70813,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion and reason .\nAssertion : For a dibasic acid , Ka₁ is greater than Ka₂. \nReason : It is more difficult to remove a positively charged proton from a negative ion due to electrostatic forces.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','It can be seen that higher order ionization constants (Ka₂, Ka₃) are smaller than the lower order ionization constant (Ka₁ ) of a polyprotic acid. The reason for this is that it is more difficult to remove a positively charged proton from a negative ion due to electrostatic forces.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 224 ; paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'2','mcq','Both are A and R are false','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Assertion is true but reason is incorrect.','Assertion is false but reason is correct.','NEW','11-224','11-200',NULL),
(70814,'chemistry','(i)Certain acids are strong while others are weak\n(ii)Strength and polarity of H-A bond determines the extent of dissociation of acid','(i) and (ii) are true and (ii) explains (i) correctly','Some acids be stronger than others.Broadly speaking we can say that the extent of dissociation of an acid depends on the strength and polarity of the H-A bond.\n(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 224 ; paragraph 4)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Ionization of Acids and Bases',NULL,'1','mcq','(i) and (ii) are true','(i) is true (ii) is false','(i) and (ii) are true and (ii) explains (i) correctly','(i) and (ii) are false','NEW','11-224','11-200',NULL),
(70901,'chemistry','Sodium acetate is dissolved into water. The solution becomes,','Alkaline','In the first case, CH₃COONa being a salt of weak acid, CH₃COOH and strong base, NaOH gets completely ionised in aqueous solution. CH₃COONa(aq) → CH₃COO⁻ ( aq)+ Na⁺ (aq) .Acetate ion thus formed undergoes hydrolysis in water to give acetic acid and OH⁻ ions CH₃COO⁻ (aq)+H₂O(l)→ CH₃COOH(aq)+OH⁻ (aq) .Acetic acid being a weak acid (Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵) remains mainly unionised in solution. This results in increase of OH⁻  ion concentration in solution making it alkaline. The pH of such a solution is more than 7.\n(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 226 ; paragraph 3,4,5)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Buffer Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','Acidic','Alkaline','Neutral','Can\'t be predicted','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(70902,'chemistry','The pKa of acetic acid and pKb of ammonium hydroxide are 4.76 and 4.75  respectively. Calculate the pH of ammonium acetate solution.','7.005','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 226 ; Problem 7.25) diagram(ne0725)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Buffer Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','8.005','6.005','5.005','7.005','NEW','11-226','11-202',NULL),
(70903,'chemistry','If we mix 1 L of 0.1 M ammonia solution with 1 L of 0.1 M ammonium chloride solution, calculate the pH of the resulting solution. pKb of ammonia is 9.25','9.25','If molar concentration of base and its conjugate acid (cation) is same then pH of the buffer solution will be same as pKa for the base. pKa value for ammonia is 9.25; therefore a buffer of pH close to 9.25 can be obtained by taking ammonia solution and ammonium chloride solution of same molar concentration.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 227 ; paragraph 6)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Buffer Solutions',NULL,'2','mcq','9.25','8.25','10.25','11.25','NEW','11-227','11-203',NULL),
(70904,'biology','Consider following statement regarding simple epithelial tissues and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Simple cuboidal epithelium is made up of multiple number of layers\n\n(ii) Inner surface of fallopian tubes are lined by simple squamous epithelium tissue\n\n(iii) Squamous epithelium is made up of single layer of flat cells with irregular boundaries arranged like tiles on a floor \n\n(iv) Cuboidal epithelium have nuclei at the base of cells','Only (iii) is correct','The cuboidal epithelium is composed of a single layer of cube-like cells. Ciliated epithelium is mainly present in the inner surface of hollow organs like bronchioles and fallopian tubes.The squamous epithelium is made of a single thin layer of flattened cells with irregular boundaries. Columnar epithelium have nuclei which are located at the base, the nucleus is more or less central in Cuboidal cells  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:101; paragraph no. 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-101',NULL,NULL),
(100310,'chemistry','Why are superoxides are paramagnetic in nature?','Because of one unpaired electron in π*2p molecular orbital','The superoxide is paramagnetic because of one unpaired electron in π*2p molecular orbital.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1 Problem 10.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Because of one unpaired electron in π*2p molecular orbital','Because of one unpaired electron in π*3p molecular orbital.','Because of one unpaired electron in σ2p molecular orbital.','Because of one unpaired electron in σ*2p molecular orbital.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(70905,'biology','Consider following statement regarding Cuboidal epithelium and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Single layer of flat cells of cuboidal epithelium tissues with irregular boundaries present in air sacs of lungs\n\n(ii) Their major function is to move mucous in a specific direction\n\n(iii) Cells of Proximal convoluted tubule of nephron in kidney are cuboidal in nature\n\n(iv) Cuboidal epithelium has protective functions in our skin','Only (iii) is correct','The squamous epithelium is made of a single thin layer of flattened cells with irregular boundaries. They are found in the walls of blood vessels and air sacs of lungs. The epithelium of proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) of nephron in the kidney is cuboidal in nature. Ciliated \\ epithelium functions in to move particles or mucus in a specific direction over the epithelium. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:101; paragraph no. 3). \n\nCompound epithelium is made of more than one layer (multi-layered) of cells and thus has a limited role in secretion and absorption. Their main function is to provide protection against chemical and mechanical stresses. stresses. They cover the dry surface of the skin, the moist surface of buccal cavity, pharynx, inner lining of ducts of salivary glands and of pancreatic ducts. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:102; paragraph no. 2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-102',NULL,NULL),
(70906,'biology','Consider following statement regarding connective tissues and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Secretion and absorption are the main functions performed by connective tissue\n\n(ii) Elastin is  a protein secreted by cells of connective tissues\n\n(iii) Production of earwax and oil is another role of connective tissues\n\n(iv) Ducts of glands are commonly made up of cartilage','Only (ii) is correct','The columnar epithelium is composed of a single layer of tall and slender cells. Their nuclei are locatedat the base. Free surface may have microvilli. They are found in the lining of stomach and intestine and help in secretion and absorption. cuboidal epithelium is composed of a single layer of cube-like cells. This is commonly found in ducts of glands  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:101; paragraph no. 3). \n\nExocrine glands secrete mucus, saliva, earwax, oil, milk, digestive enzymes  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:102; paragraph no. 1). \n\n In all connective tissues except blood, the cells secrete fibres of structural proteins called collagen or elastin. The fibres provide strength, elasticity and flexibility to the tissue  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:103; paragraph no. 1).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Only (ii) is correct','Both (ii) and (iii) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-103',NULL,NULL),
(70907,'biology','Consider following statement regarding columnar epithelium and choose the correct option \n\n(i) They are consist of tall and slender cells\n\n(ii) They are commonly found in stomach and intestine\n\n(iii) It performs major functions like formation of diffusion boundary\n\n(iv) It contains mast cells and macrophages in its matrix','(iii) and (iv) is incorrect','The columnar epithelium is composed of a single layer of tall and slender cells. Their nuclei are located at the base. Free surfaces may have microvilli. They are found in the lining of stomach and intestine and help in secretion and absorption. The squamous epithelium is made of a single thin layer of flattened cells with irregular boundaries. They are found in the walls of blood vessels and air sacs of lungs and are involved in functions like forming a diffusion boundary  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:101; paragraph no. 3).\nStatement iii) is wrong as they have asked in context to columnar epithelium.\n Loose connective tissue contains fibroblasts (cells that produce and secrete fibres), macrophages and mast cells.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:103; paragraph no. 2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is incorrect','Only (ii) is incorrect','Both (ii) and (iii) are incorrect','(iii) and (iv) is incorrect','NEW','11-103',NULL,NULL),
(70908,'biology','Consider following statement regarding loose connective tissue and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Areolar connective tissue is  present beneath the skin\n\n(ii) Collagen fibers are present in rows between parallel bundle of cells\n\n(iii) It has cells that secrete fibres,polysaccharides with other cells like  macrophages and mast cells\n\n(iv) It serves as supportive framework for overlying epithelium','(i), (iii) and (iv) are correct','Loose connective tissue has cells and fibres loosely arranged in a semi-fluid ground substance, for example, areolar tissue present beneath the skin. Often it serves as a support framework for epithelium. It contains fibroblasts (cells that produce and secrete fibres), macrophages and mast cells.   (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:103; paragraph no. 2).  \n\nIn the dense regular connective tissues, the collagen fibres are present in rows between many parallel bundles of fibres. Tendons, which attach skeletal muscles to bones and ligaments which attach one bone to another are examples of this tissue.   (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:103; paragraph no. 3).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','(i), (iii) and (iv) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-103',NULL,NULL),
(70909,'biology','Consider following statement regarding specialized connective tissue and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Orientation of fibers show irregular pattern in irregular dense connective tissue\n\n(ii) Chondrocytes are present in small cavities within matrix of a cartilage\n\n(iii) Lacunae are the spaces which contain chondrocytes in a bone\n\n(iv) Bones are specialised connecvtive tissue that provide skeletal framework to the body','Only (iii) is incorrect','Fibres and fibroblasts are compactly packed in the dense connective tissues. Orientation of fibres show a regular or irregular pattern and are called dense regular and dense irregular tissues. In the dense regular connective tissues, the collagen fibres are present in rows between many parallel bundles of fibres.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:103; paragraph no. 3). \n\nCells of catilage (chondrocytes) are enclosed in small cavities within the matrix. Cartilage is present in the tip of nose, outer ear joints, between adjacent bones of the vertebral column, limbs and hands in adults. The bone cells (osteocytes) are  present in the spaces called lacunae. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:104; paragraph no. 2,3). \n\nbones and blood are various types of specialised connective tissues. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:104; paragraph no. 1).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is incorrect','Only (ii) is incorrect','Only (iii) is incorrect','Both (ii) and (iv) are incorrect','NEW','11-104',NULL,NULL),
(70910,'biology','Consider following statement regarding muscle tissue and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Muscle fibers are loosely arranged in semi fluid ground substance matrix\n\n(ii) Ligaments are the epithelial tissue that attach one bone to another bone\n\n(iii) A sheath of tough connective tissues enclosing muscle fibers is called Epimyscium\n\n(iv) Fusiform muscle fibers are wider at two end and conical in the central portion','Only (iii) is correct','Loose connective tissue has cells and fibres loosely arranged in a semi-fluid ground substance. Ligaments are dense connective which attach one bone to another.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:103; paragraph no. 2,3) \n\nA sheath of tough connective tissue encloses several bundles of skeletal muscle fibres  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:104; paragraph no. 5). \n\nThe smooth muscle fibres taper at both ends (fusiform) and do not show striations.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:105; paragraph no. 1).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Both (i) and (iv) are correct','NEW','11-105',NULL,NULL),
(70911,'biology','Consider following statement regarding muscle tissue and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Upon the arrival of a stimulus, muscle fibers contract and relax.\n\n(ii) Communication junctions allow the fibroblasts to secrete myofibrils\n\n(iii) Skeletal muscles are closely attached to bones\n\n(iv) Functioning of smooth muscles cannot be controlled voluntarily','Only (ii) is incorrect','Muscle fibres contract (shorten) in response to stimulation, then relax (lengthen) and return to their uncontracted state in a coordinated fashion. Their action moves the body to adjust to the changes in the environment and to maintain the positions of the various parts of the body. Skeletal muscle tissue is closely attached to skeletal bones. In a typical muscle such as the biceps, striated (striped) skeletal muscle fibres are bundled together in a parallel fashion.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:104; paragraph no. 4, 5).\n\nSmooth muscles are ‘involuntary’ as their functioning cannot be directly controlled. We usually are not able to make it contract merely by thinking about it as we can do with skeletal muscles. Communication junctions (intercalated discs) at some fusion points allow the cells to contract as a unit, i.e., when one cell receives a signal to contract, its neighbours are also stimulated to contract  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:105; paragraph no. 11,2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is incorrect','Only (ii) is incorrect','Only (iii) is incorrect','Both (ii) and (iv) are incorrect','NEW','11-105',NULL,NULL),
(70912,'biology','Consider following statement regarding neural tissue and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Neurons make up more than one half of volume of neural tissue\n\n(ii) Neuroglial cells protects and supports neurons\n\n(iii) Arrival of disturbance at output zone stimulates or inhibits the adjacent neuron \n\n(iv)An electrical impulse is swiftly conducted along the plasma membrane of that neuron','Only (i) is incorrect','Neurons, the unit of neural system are excitable cells. The neuroglial cell which constitute the rest of the neural system protect and support neurons. Neuroglia make up more than one- half the volume of neural tissue in our body. When a neuron is suitably stimulated, an electrical disturbance is generated which swiftly travels along its plasma membrane. Arrival of the disturbance at the neuron’s endings, or output zone, triggers events that may cause stimulation or inhibition of adjacent neurons and other cells  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:105; paragraph no. 3,4).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is incorrect','Only (ii) is incorrect','Only (iii) are incorrect','Both (ii) and (iv) are incorrect','NEW','11-105',NULL,NULL),
(70913,'biology','Consider following statement regarding earthworm, and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Black bodied earthworms are present in tropical region\n\n(ii) Their long antennae and flat extensions of upper body wall conceals the head of the creature\n\n(iii) The common Indian earthworms are Pheretima and Lumbricus.\n\n(iv) Their fecal deposits can be used as tracers to identify their location','Both (iii) and (iv) are correct','Earthworm is a reddish brown terrestrial invertebrate that inhabits the upper layer of the moist soil. During day time, they live in burrows made by boring and swallowing the soil. In the gardens, they can be traced by their faecal deposits known as worm castings. The common Indian\nearthworms are Pheretima and Lumbricus. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:106; paragraph no. 3).  \n\nCockroaches are brown or black bodied animals that are included in class Insecta of Phylum Arthropoda. Bright yellow, red and green coloured cockroaches have also been reported in tropical regions. Their size ranges from 1⁄4 inches to 3 inches (0.6-7.6 cm) and have long antenna, legs and flat extension of the upper body wall that conceals head. They are nocturnal omnivores that live in damp places throughout the world. They have become residents of human homes and thus are serious pests and vectors of several diseases. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:111; paragraph no. 2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Both (iii) and (iv) are correct','NEW','11-111',NULL,NULL),
(70914,'biology','Consider following statement regarding morphology of earthworm and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Male genital pore is located dorsally on 18th segment\n\n(ii) Genital pores are present on ventral side of body\n\n(iii) Clitellum has the row of S shaped structures called setae\n\n(iv) Glandular tissue is present on the 12th segment of body','Only (ii) is correct','The ventral surface is distinguished by the presence of genital openings (pores).14-16 are covered by a prominent dark band of glandular tissue called clitellum. A pair of male genital pores are present on the ventro-lateral sides of the 18th segment. Numerous minute pores called nephridiopores open on the surface of the body. In each body segment, except the first, last and clitellum, there are rows of S-shaped setae, embedded in the epidermal pits in the middle of each segment.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:107, 108).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Only (ii) is correct','Both (i) and (iii) are correct','Both (iii) and (iv) are correct','NEW','11-108',NULL,NULL),
(100311,'chemistry','What is the colour of hydroxides which are obtained by the reaction of the oxides with water?','White','The hydroxides which are obtained by the reaction of the oxides with water are all white crystalline solids.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','White','Purple','Deep blue','Yellow','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100311q.png'),
(70915,'biology','Consider following statement regarding morphology of earthworm and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Peristomium is the 1st body segment that contains mouth\n\n(ii) Each segment of the body has hardened plates called sclerites\n\n(iii) Body of earthworm is divided into three regions\n\n(iv) Setae are present on clitellum that plays major role in release of gametes','Both (ii) and (iv) are incorrect','The first body segment is called the peristomium (buccal segment) which contains the mouth. In a mature worm, segments 14-16 are covered by a prominent dark band of glandular tissue called clitellum. Thus the body is divisible into three prominent regions – preclitellar, clitellar and postclitellar segments. In each body segment, except the first, last and clitellum, there are rows of S-shaped setae, embedded in the epidermal pits in the middle of each segment.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:107, 108). \n\n In each segment of cockroach, exoskeleton has hardened plates called sclerites (tergites dorsally and sternites ventrally) that are joined to each other by a thin and flexible articular membrane (arthrodial membrane). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:111; paragraph no. 3).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is incorrect','Only (ii) is incorrect','Only (iii) are incorrect','Both (ii) and (iv) are incorrect','NEW','11-111',NULL,NULL),
(70916,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Chondrocytes are present in spaces called osteollema\n\nR: Chondrocytes interact with skeletal muscles during movement','If both Assertion and Reason are false','The intercellular material of cartilage is solid and pliable and resists compression. Cells of this tissue (chondrocytes) are enclosed in small cavities within the matrix secreted by them. The bone cells (osteocytes) are present in the spaces called lacunae. They also interact with skeletal muscles attached to them to bring about movements.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:104; paragraph no. 2,3).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-104',NULL,NULL),
(70917,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Gap junctions help to stop substances from leaking across the tissue\n\nR: Cells of epithelium tissues are loosely packed with large intracellular matrix','If both Assertion and Reason are false','Gap junctions facilitate the cells to communicate with each other by connecting the cytoplasm of adjoining cells, for rapid transfer of ions, small molecules and sometimes big molecules.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:102; paragraph no. 3). The cells of epithelium tissue are compactly packed with little intercellular matrix.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:101; paragraph no. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-101',NULL,NULL),
(70918,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Neighboring cells are joined together by adhering junctions\n\nR: Adhering junctions perform cementing to keep neighbouring cells together.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','All cells in epithelium are held together with little intercellular material. In nearly all animal tissues, specialised junctions provide both structural and functional links between its individual cells. Three types of cell junctions are found in the epithelium and other tissues. These are called as tight, adhering and gap junctions. Tight junctions help to stop substances from leaking across a tissue. Adhering junctions perform cementing to keep neighbouring cells together. Gap junctions facilitate the cells to communicate with each other by connecting the cytoplasm of adjoining cells, for rapid transfer of ions, small molecules and sometimes big molecules  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:102; paragraph no. 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-102',NULL,NULL),
(70919,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Columnar epithelium is most abundant and widely distributed in all animals\n\nR: Columnar epithelium provides support to major organs in an efficient manner','If both Assertion and Reason are false','Connective tissues are most abundant and widely distributed in the body of complex animals rather than columnar epithelium  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:102; paragraph no. 4). The columnar epithelium is composed of a single layer of tall and slender cells. Their nuclei are located at the base. Free surface may have microvilli. They are found in the lining of stomach and intestine and help in secretion and absorption. If the columnar or cuboidal cells bear cilia on their free surface they are called ciliated epithelium  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:101; paragraph no. 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-101',NULL,NULL),
(70920,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Material of bone is solid, pliable and resists compression\n\nR: Main strength of bone is due to chondrin proteins present in them','If both Assertion and Reason are false','Bones have a hard and non-pliable ground substance rich in calcium salts and collagen fibres which give bone its strength. It is the main tissue that provides structural frame to the body. Bones support and protect softer tissues and organs  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:104; paragraph no. 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-104',NULL,NULL),
(70921,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Intercalated discs at some fusion points are present in cardiac muscles.\n\nR: It allows the cells to contract as a unit, that is when one cell receives a signal to contract, its neighbours are also stimulated to contract','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Cardiac muscle tissue is a contractile tissue present only in the heart. Cell junctions fuse the plasma membranes of cardiac muscle cells and make them stick together. Communication junctions (intercalated discs) at some fusion points allow the cells to contract as a unit, i.e., when one cell receives a signal to contract, its neighbours are also stimulated to contract  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:105; paragraph no. 3)In all connective tissues except blood, the cells secrete fibres of structural proteins called collagen or elastin. The fibres provide strength, elasticity and flexibility to the tissue  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:103; paragraph no. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-103',NULL,NULL),
(70922,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Earthworms have a pair of Spermathecal apparatus, located dorsally on 10th segment \n\nR: They release sperm during ejaculation in earthworms','If both Assertion and Reason are false','Four pairs of spermathecae are located in 6th-9th segments (one pair in each segment). They receive and store spermatozoa during copulation. One pair of ovaries is attached at the inter-segmental septum of the 12th and 13th segments  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:110; paragraph no. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-110',NULL,NULL),
(70923,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Ventral surface of body of earthworm has dark median mid dorsal line \n\nR: Ventral surface of earthworm has a pointed end that acts as wedge to force open cracks in soil','If both Assertion and Reason are false','The dorsal surface of the body is marked by a dark median mid dorsal line (dorsal blood vessel) along the longitudinal axis of\nthe body. Anterior end consists of the mouth and the prostomium, a lobe which serves as a covering for the mouth and as a wedge to force open cracks in the soil into which the earthworm may crawl  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:106; paragraph no. 4)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-106',NULL,NULL),
(70924,'biology','Consider following statement regarding Cockroach and choose the correct option.\n\n(i) They belong to class Crustacea of phylum Arthropoda\n\n(ii) They are diurnal animals that live in houses\n\n(iii) In tropical regions, they may be red, green or yellow in color.\n\n(iv) They are serious pests and vector of many diseases','Both (iii) and (iv) are correct','Cockroaches are brown or black bodied animals that are included in  class Insecta of Phylum Arthropoda. Bright yellow, red and green coloured cockroaches have also been reported in tropical regions. Their size ranges from 1⁄4 inches to 3 inches (0.6-7.6 cm) and have long antenna, legs and flat extension of the upper body wall that conceals head. They are nocturnal omnivores that live in damp places throughout the world. They have become residents of human homes and thus are serious pests and vectors of several diseases  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:111; paragraph no. 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Both (iii) and (iv) are correct','NEW','11-111',NULL,NULL),
(70925,'biology','Consider following statement regarding Morphology of Cockroach and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Dorsally present sclerites are termed as sternities\n\n(ii) They have 30-32 mm long body having clitellum.\n\n(iii) Wing extends beyond the tip of abdomen in male cockroaches.\n\n(iv) Body is divided into three regions that is i.e pre clitellar region, clitellar and post clitellar region.','Only (iii) is correct','In each segment, exoskeleton has hardened plates called sclerites (tergites dorsally and sternites ventrally) that are joined to each other by a thin and flexible articular membrane (arthrodial membrane). The adults of the common species of cockroach, Periplaneta americana are about 34-53 mm long with wings that extend beyond the tip of the abdomen in males. The body of the cockroach is segmented and divisible into three distinct regions – head, thorax and abdomen  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:111; paragraph no. 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Both (iii) and (iv) are correct','NEW','11-111',NULL,NULL),
(70926,'biology','Consider following statement regarding the head of cockroach and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Head is rectangular in shape and formed by the fusion of first four body segments \n\n(ii) In flexible neck that can move only in one side.\n\n(iii) Head lies anteriorly and vertically to horizontal axis of body.\n\n(iv) Mouth parts are of biting and chewing type.','Only (iv) is correct','Head is triangular in shape and lies anteriorly at right angles to the longitudinal body axis. It is formed by the fusion of six segments and shows great mobility in all directions due to flexible neck. The head capsule bears a pair of compound eyes. A pair of thread like antennae arise from membranous sockets lying in front of eyes. Antennae have sensory receptors that help in monitoring the environment. Anterior end of the head bears appendages forming biting and chewing type of mouth parts.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:112; paragraph no. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-112',NULL,NULL),
(30431,'chemistry','Match the following:','Cs-s Block, Os-d Block, Ts-p Block, Es-f Block','Cs belongs to s-Block elements,Os belongs to d-Block elements,Ts belongs to p-Block elements and Es belongs to f-Block elements, as it is shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 83; diagram(ne0319)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'2','match','Cs,s Block','Os,d Block','Ts,p Block','Es,f Block','NEW','11-083','11-083',NULL),
(70927,'biology','Consider following statement regarding head of cockroach and choose the correct option \n\n(i) A median flexible lobe, acting as tongue known as glossopharynx\n\n(ii) Mouthparts consist of 4 pairs of mandibles and maxilla.\n\n(iii) Neck provides mobility in all directions due its flexibility.\n\n(iv) Head has two pairs of compound eyes','Only (iii) is correct','Head is triangular in shape and lies anteriorly at right angles to the longitudinal body axis. It is formed by the fusion of six segments and shows great mobility in all directions due to flexible neck. The head capsule bears a pair of compound eyes. A pair of thread like antennae arise from membranous sockets lying in front of eyes. Antennae have sensory receptors that help in monitoring the environment. Anterior end of the head bears appendages forming biting and chewing type of mouth parts. The mouthparts consisting of a labrum (upper lip), a pair of mandibles, a pair of maxillae and a labium (lower lip). A median flexible lobe, acting as tongue (hypopharynx), lies within the cavity enclosed by the mouthparts  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:112; paragraph no. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-112',NULL,NULL),
(70928,'biology','Consider following statement regarding thorax of cockroach and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Neck is an extension of prothorax\n\n(ii) Metathorax has three pairs of jumping legs.\n\n(iii) Tegmina are the fore wings that are membranous and used in flight.\n\n(iv) Mesothorax contains two pairs of wings that are used in flight.','Only (i) is correct','Thorax consists of three parts – prothorax,mesothorax and metathorax. The head is connected with thorax by a short extension of the prothorax known as the neck. Each thoracic segment bears a pair of walking legs. The first pair of wings arises from mesothorax and the second pair from metathorax. Forewings (mesothoracic) called tegmina are opmcque dark and leathery and cover the hind wings when at rest. The hind wings are transparent, membranous and are used in flight.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:112; paragraph no. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-112',NULL,NULL),
(70929,'biology','Consider following statement regarding abdomen of cockroach and choose the correct option \n\n(i) There are 10 segments of abdomen in female and male.\n\n(ii) In female gentital pouch is present in the 5th and 6th segment.\n\n(iii) 7th Sternum in female is rectangular , situated posteriorly to the body.\n\n(iv) Genital pouch in females contain anus and gonopore','Only (i) is correct','The abdomen in both males and females consists of 10 segments. In females, the 7th sternum is boat shaped and together with the 8th and 9th sterna forms a brood or genital pouch whose anterior part contains female gonopore, spermathecal pores and collateral glands. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:112; paragraph no. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-112',NULL,NULL),
(70930,'biology','Consider following statement regarding genital organs in cockroach and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Genital pouch in males lie anterior to 9th terga.\n\n(ii) Genital pouch contains spermathecal pores and collateral glands only.\n\n(iii) Anal cerci is present on the 10th segment of the body.\n\n(iv) Anal styles is present only in larval female cockroaches.','Only (iii) is correct','The abdomen in both males and females consists of 10 segments. In females, the 7th sternum is boat shaped and together with the 8th and 9th sterna forms a brood or genital pouch whose anterior part contains female gonopore, spermathecal pores and collateral glands. In males, genital pouch or chamber lies at the hind end of abdomen bounded dorsally by 9th and 10th terga and ventrally by the 9th sternum. It contains dorsal anus, ventral male genital pore and gonapophysis. Males bear a pair of short, thread- like anal styles which are absent in females. In both sexes, the 10th segment bears a pair of jointed filamentous structures called anal cerci  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:112; paragraph no. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-112',NULL,NULL),
(70931,'biology','Consider following statement regarding alimentary canal in cockroach and choose the correct option \n\n(i) Mouth directly open into wide conical passages called esophagus.\n\n(ii) Sac like structure called pharynx stores the food.\n\n(iii) Proventriculus has a layer of longitudinal muscles. \n\n(iv) Six chitinous paltes are present in gizzard called as teeth','Only (iv) is correct','The alimentary canal present in the body cavity is divided into three regions: foregut, midgut and hindgut. The mouth opens into a short tubular pharynx, leading to a narrow tubular passage called oesophagus. This in turn opens into a sac like structure called crop used for storing of food. The crop is followed by gizzard or proventriculus. It has an outer layer of thick circular muscles and thick inner cuticle forming six highly chitinous plate called teeth. Gizzard helps in grinding the food particles. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:112; paragraph no. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-112',NULL,NULL),
(70932,'biology','Consider following statement regarding hind gut in cockroach and choose the correct option \n\n(i) This part is narrow than midgut and contains gastric ceca .\n\n(ii) Brighth Red malpighian tubules help in excretion.\n\n(iii) At junction of midgut and hindgut certain anzymes are secreted.\n\n(iv) Ileum,colon and rectum are three major regions of hindgut.','Only (iv) is correct','At the junction of midgut and hindgut is present another ring of 100-150 yellow coloured thin filamentous Malpighian tubules. They help in removal of excretory products from haemolymph. The hindgut is broader than midgut and is differentiated into ileum, colon and rectum. The rectum opens out through anus. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:112; paragraph no. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-112',NULL,NULL),
(121323,'chemistry','What is the IUPAC name of the compound in the given diagram(12125)','4-hydroxy-2-methylpent-2-en-1-al','Priority functional group is used as suffix here and counting is started for it\'s carbon. (NEET 1992)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','4-hydroxy-1-methylpentanal','4-hydroxy-2-methylpent-2-en-1-al','2-hydroxy-4-methylpent-3-en-5-al','2-hydroxy-3-methylpent-2-en-5-al','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121323q.png'),
(70933,'biology','Consider following statement regarding circulatory system in cockroach and choose the correct option \n\n(i) They have closed circulatory system with ventral heart.\n\n(ii) Blood vessels directly opens into tissues. \n\n(iii) Hemolyph contains plasma and hemocytes.\n\n(iv) Heart is differentiated into one ventricle and two artria','Only (iii) is correct','Blood vascular system of cockroach is an open type. Blood vessels are poorly developed and open into space (haemocoel). Visceral organs located in the haemocoel are bathed in blood (haemolymph). The haemolymph is composed of colourless plasma and haemocytes. Heart of cockroach consists of elongated muscular tube lying along mid dorsal line of thorax and abdomen. It is differentiated into funnel shaped chambers with ostia on either side. Blood from sinuses enter heart through ostia and is pumped anteriorly to sinuses again.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:113; paragraph no. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-113',NULL,NULL),
(70934,'biology','Consider following statement regarding respiratory system in cockroach and choose the correct option \n\n(i) There is no specialized breathing devices.\n\n(ii) Exchange of gases occurs with the help of moist cutaneous surface.\n\n(iii) Opening and closing of spiracles is controlled by myogenic tissue.\n\n(iv) Tracheal tubes are sub divided into tracheoles','Only (iv) is correct','The respiratory system consists of a network of trachea, that open through 10 pairs of small holes called spiracles present on the lateral side of the body. Thin branching tubes (tracheal tubes subdivided into tracheoles) carry O₂ from the air to all the parts. The opening of the spiracles is regulated by  the sphincters. Exchange of gases take place at the tracheoles by diffusion.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:113; paragraph no. 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-113',NULL,NULL),
(70935,'biology','Consider following statement regarding excretion and osmoregulation in cockroach and choose the correct option \n\n(i) All the insects are uricotelic.\n\n(ii) Ciliated Malpighian tubule is present.\n\n(iii) Biliary apparatus helps in osmoregulation.\n\n(iv) Nephrocytes also assist in excretion.','Only (iii) is incorrect','Excretion is performed by Malpighian tubules. Each tubule is lined by glandular and ciliated cells. They absorb nitrogenous waste products and convert them into uric acid which is excreted out through the hindgut. Therefore, this insect is called uricotelic. In addition, the fat body, nephrocytes and urecose glands also help in excretion  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:114; paragraph no. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is incorrect','Both (i) and (ii) are incorrect','Only (iii) is incorrect','Both (iii) and (iv) are incorrect','NEW','11-114',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(70936,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R) regarding Cockroach:\n\nA: Pair of wings arise from prothorax and helps in jumping, getting food and escaping from predators.\n\nR: Fore wings are transparent and cover the legs while in rest.','If both Assertion and Reason are false','The first pair of wings arises from mesothorax and the second pair from metathorax. Forewings (mesothoracic) called tegmina are opmcque dark and leathery and cover the hind wings when at rest. The hind wings are transparent, membranous and are used in flight.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:112; paragraph no. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-112',NULL,NULL),
(70937,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R) regarding cockroach:\n\nA: The sperms are stored in the seminal vesicles and are glued together in the form of bundles called spermatophores.\n\nR: Spermatophores are discharged after fertilization','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','The sperms are stored in the seminal vesicles and are glued together in the form of bundles called spermatophores which are discharged during copulation.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:114; paragraph no. 5)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-114',NULL,NULL),
(70938,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R) regarding reproductive tract in Cockroach:\n\nA: Anal Cerci are jointed filamentous structures at 10th segments in only females.\n\nR: Males bear a long anal style, which is absent in females.','If both Assertion and Reason are false','Males bear a pair of short, thread- like anal styles which are absent in females. In both sexes, the 10th segment bears a pair of jointed filamentous structures called anal cerci. Anal cerci\'s purpose is to detect vibrations in the air or on the ground. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:112; paragraph no. 2)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-112',NULL,NULL),
(70939,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R) regarding Digestive tract in Cockroach:\n\nA: Digestive juice is secreted by hindgut.\n\nR: Hindgut contains hepatic ceca that has thick circular muscles.','If both Assertion and Reason are false','A ring of 6-8 blind tubules called hepatic or gastric caeca is present at the junction of foregut and midgut, which secrete\ndigestive juice. The crop is followed by gizzard or proventriculus. It has an outer layer of thick circular muscles and thick inner cuticle forming six highly chitinous plate called teeth. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:113; paragraph no. 1)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-113',NULL,NULL),
(70940,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R) regarding Cockroach:\n\nA: 8 pairs of spiracles are present dorsally and ventrally to the body\n\nR: Spiracles are openings which are under the control of heart.','If both Assertion and Reason are false','The respiratory system consists of a network of trachea, that open through 10 pairs of small holes called spiracles present on the lateral side of the body.  Spiracles are controlled by sphincters. Thin branching tubes (tracheal tubes subdivided into tracheoles) carry O₂ from the air to all the parts.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:114; paragraph no. 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-114',NULL,NULL),
(71001,'chemistry','(i)Lattice enthalpy also affects the solubility of a salt in a solvent. .\n(ii)LiF is insoluble salt while CaCl is hygroscopic salt.\n(iii)Lattice enthalpy does not affect solubilty of a salt in a solvent.\n(iv)LiF is hygroscopic salt while CaCl is insoluble salt .','(i) and (ii) are correct','The solubility depends on a number of factors important amongst which are the lattice enthalpy of the salt and the solvation enthalpy of the ions in a solution.\n(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 228 ; paragraph 2)\nSome of these (like calcium chloride) are so soluble that they are hygroscopic in nature and even absorb water vapour from atmosphere. Others (such as lithium fluoride) have so little solubility\nthat they are commonly termed as insoluble.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 228 ; paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Solubility Equilibria Of Sparingly Soluble Salts',NULL,'1','mcq','(i) and (ii) are correct','(ii) and (iii) are correct','(iii) and (iv) are correct','(i) and (iv) are correct','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71002,'chemistry','As a general rule , for a salt to be able to dissolve in a particular solvent the relation between solvation enthalpy and lattice enthalpy is ?','Solvation enthalpy should bbe greater than lattice enthalpy','As a general rule , for a salt to be able to dissolve in a particular solvent its solvation enthalpy must be greater than its lattice enthalpy.(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 228; paragraph 2)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Solubility Equilibria Of Sparingly Soluble Salts',NULL,'1','mcq','Solvation enthalpy should bbe greater than lattice enthalpy','Solvation enthalpy should be less than lattice enthalpy','They should be equal so that they nullify','There is no such relation','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(71003,'chemistry','What is Kₛₚ relation of Zr₃(PO₄)₄ if the solubility is S mol L⁻¹','S = (Kₛₚ / 6912)¹/⁷','[Zr₃⁴⁺] = 3S and [PO₄³⁻] = 4S\nand K = (3S)³(4S)⁴ = 6912 (S)⁷\nor S = {Ksp / (3³ × 4⁴ )}¹/⁷ = (Ksp / 6912)¹/⁷ (NCERT chemistry class 11; page 228; paragraph 7)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Solubility Equilibria Of Sparingly Soluble Salts',NULL,'2','mcq','S = (Kₛₚ / 6)¹/⁷','S = (Kₛₚ / 691)¹/⁷','S = (Kₛₚ / 6912)¹/⁷','S = (Kₛₚ / 69127)¹/⁷','NEW','11-228','11-204','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/71003q.png'),
(71004,'chemistry','Calculate the solubility of A₂X₃ in pure water, assuming that neither kind of ion reacts with water. The solubility product of A₂X₃ , Ksp = 1.1 × 10⁻²³.','1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L.','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 229;Problem 7.26) diagram(ne0726)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Solubility Equilibria Of Sparingly Soluble Salts',NULL,'2','mcq','1.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L.','1.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L.','1.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol/L.','1.0 × 10⁻³ mol/L.','NEW','11-229','11-205',NULL),
(71005,'chemistry','The values of Ksp of two sparingly soluble salts Ni(OH)₂ and AgCN are 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ and 6 × 10⁻¹⁷ respectively. Which salt is more soluble?','Ni(OH)₂','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 229;Problem 7.27) diagram(ne0727)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Solubility Equilibria Of Sparingly Soluble Salts',NULL,'2','mcq','Ni(OH)₂','AgCN','Both are equally soluble','Can\'t be determined','NEW','11-229','11-205',NULL),
(71006,'chemistry','Calculate the molar solubility of Ni(OH)₂ in 0.10 M NaOH. The ionic product of Ni(OH)₂ is 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁵.','2.0 × 10⁻¹³ M','(NCERT chemistry class 11; page 230;Problem 7.28) diagram(ne0728)','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> Solubility Equilibria Of Sparingly Soluble Salts',NULL,'3','mcq','2.0 × 10⁻¹¹ M','2.0 × 10⁻¹² M','2.0 × 10⁻¹³ M','2.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ M','NEW','11-230','11-206',NULL),
(71007,'biology','Consider following statement regarding nervous system in cockroach and choose the correct option\n\n(i) It consists of fused ganglia joined by ventral connectives on dorsal side \n\n(ii) Abdomen contains six pairs of ganglia .\n\n(iii) Nervous system is concentrated in the head.\n\n(iv) By cutting of head,cockroach can live for more than one month','All are false','The nervous system of cockroach consists of a series of fused, segmentally arranged ganglia joined by paired longitudinal connectives on the ventral side. Three ganglia lie in the thorax, and six in the abdomen. The nervous system of cockroach is spread throughout the body. The head holds a bit of a nervous system while the rest is situated along the ventral (belly-side) part of its body. So, now you understand that if the head of a cockroach is cut off, it will still live for as long as one week.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:114; paragraph no. 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','All are false','Both (i) and (iii) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-114',NULL,NULL),
(71008,'biology','Consider following statement regarding sensory organs in cockroach and choose the correct option\n\n(i) Labrum palps are the only chief sensory organs.\n\n(ii) Compound eyes are located ventrally on the head.\n\n(iii) 2000 ommatidia are present within each of the compound eyes.\n\n(iv) High resolution  and lower sensitivity of eyes make them less common at night','Only (iii) is correct','In cockroach, the sense organs are antennae, eyes, maxillary palps, labial palps, anal cerci, etc. The compound eyes are situated at the dorsal surface of the head. Each eye consists of about 2000 hexagonal ommatidia (sing.: ommatidium). With the help of several ommatidia, a cockroach can receive several images of an object. This kind of vision is known as mosaic  vision with more sensitivity but less resolution, being common during night (hence called nocturnal vision).  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:114; paragraph no. 3)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (iii) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Both (i) and (iii) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-114',NULL,NULL),
(121324,'chemistry','Which one of the following pairs represents stereoisomerism?','Optical isomerism and geometrical isomerism','Optical isomerism and geometrical isomerism are types of stereoisomerism. (NEET 2005)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Structural isomerism and geometrical isomerism','Optical isomerism and geometrical isomerism','Chain isomerism and rotational isomerism','Linkage isomerism and geometrical isomerism','NEW','11-349','11-271','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/121324q.png'),
(71009,'biology','Consider following statement regarding reproduction in cockroach and choose the correct option\n\n(i) Male reproductive system is present in 7-8th abdominal segments.\n\n(ii) Vas deferens directly opens into gonopore that is dorsal to anus.\n\n(iii) A pair of testes, one lying on each side,lateral to the 4-6th abdominal segments. \n\n(iv) Male stores sperms in gonopore,packed as spermatophore and discharged through the cloaca.','Only (iii) is correct','Cockroaches are dioecious and both sexes have well developed reproductive organs. Male reproductive system consists of a pair of testes one lying on each lateral side in the 4th -6th abdominal segments. From each testis arises a thin vas deferens, which opens into ejaculatory duct through seminal vesicle. The ejaculatory duct opens into male gonopore situated ventral to anus. A characteristic mushroom- shaped gland is present in the 6th-7th abdominal segments which function as an accessory reproductive gland. The external genitalia are represented by male gonapophysis or phallomere (chitinous asymmetrical structures, surrounding the male gonopore). The sperms are stored in the seminal vesicles and are glued together in the form of bundles called spermatophores which are discharged during copulation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:114; paragraph no. 4)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-114',NULL,NULL),
(71010,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Phallomere is a specific sensory structure in cockroach that maintains body balance.\n\nR: Sensory structure such as compound eyes are located on ventral side of head.','If both Assertion and Reason are false','The external genitalia are represented by male gonapophysis or phallomere (chitinous asymmetrical structures, surrounding the male gonopore). The compound eyes are situated at the dorsal surface of the head  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:114; paragraph no. 3,4)','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-114',NULL,NULL),
(71011,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Spermatophores are sperm packets discharged during copulation\n\nR: Phallomere is a structure that surrounds the spermatopores and is functional for discharge of excreta','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','The external genitalia are represented by male gonapophysis or phallomere (chitinous asymmetrical structures, surrounding the male gonopore) The sperms are stored in the seminal vesicles and are glued together in the form of bundles called spermatophores which are discharged during copulation.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:114; paragraph no. 4).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-114',NULL,NULL),
(71012,'biology','Consider following statement regarding female reproductive system in cockroach and choose the correct option\n\n(i) Two large ovaries are present dorsally in the abdomen in the 4th to 6th abdominal segments.\n\n(ii) There are four pairs of ovarian tubules/ovarioles in each ovary.\n\n(iii) Two pair of spermatheca are present on four and five segment.\n\n(iv) Oviduct joins to form a single uterus.','Only (ii) is correct','The female reproductive sysytem consists of two large ovaries, lying laterally in the 2nd – 6th abdominal segments. Each ovary is formed of a group of eight ovarian tubules or ovarioles, containing a chain of developing ova. Oviducts of each ovary unite into a single median oviduct (also called vagina) which opens into the genital chamber. A pair of spermatheca is present in the 6th segment  which opens into the genital chamber.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:114; paragraph no. 4).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Only (ii) is correct','Both (i) and (iii) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-114',NULL,NULL),
(71013,'biology','Consider following statement regarding reproduction in cockroach and choose the correct option\n\n(i) Total number of ootheca produced are 3-4 containing 10-12 eggs.\n\n(ii) Ootheca are specialized structures that surrounds the phallomere.\n\n(iii) Their nymphs are well differentiated and distinct from adults.\n\n(iv)A total of 13 molts is required for metamorphosis from larval form to adults.','Only (iv) is correct','Sperms are transferred through spermatophores. Their fertilised eggs are encased in capsules called oothecae. Ootheca is a dark reddish to blackish brown capsule, about 3/8" (8 mm) long. They are dropped or glued to a suitable surface, usually in a crack or crevice of high relative humidity near a food source. On an average, females produce 9-10 oothecae, each containing 14-16 eggs. The development of P. americana is paurometabolous, meaning there is development through nymphal stage. The nymphs look very much like adults. The nymph grows by moulting about 13 times to reach the adult form.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:114, 115).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Both (i) and (iii) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-115',NULL,NULL),
(71014,'biology','Consider following statement regarding Nervous System of frog and choose the correct option\n\n(i) Forebrain includes optic lobes, paired hemispheres and a paired diencephalon.\n\n(ii) There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves arising from brain.\n\n(iii) Peripheral nervous system consists of cranial nerves only.\n\n(iv) Medulla oblongata passes out through foramen magnum and continues as the spinal cord.','Only (iv) is correct','A peripheral nervous system contains (cranial and spinal nerves) and an autonomic nervous system (sympathetic and parasympathetic). There are ten pairs of cranial nerves arising from the brain. Brain is enclosed in a bony structure called brain box (cranium). The brain is divided into fore-brain, mid-brain and hind-brain. Forebrain includes olfactory lobes, paired cerebral hemispheres and unpaired diencephalon. The midbrain is characterised by a pair of optic lobes. Hind-brain consists of cerebellum and medulla oblongata. The medulla oblongata passes out through the foramen magnum and continues into spinal cord, which is enclosed in the vertebral column.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:119; paragraph no. 1).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Both (i) and (iii) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-119','11-83',NULL),
(71015,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Frogs are ammonotelic\n\nR: All frogs excrete ammonia as excretory product','If both Assertion and Reason are false','The frog excretes urea and thus is a ureotelic animal. Excretory wastes are carried by blood into the kidney where it is separated and excreted. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:118; paragraph no. 3).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-118','11-83',NULL),
(71016,'biology','Consider following statement regarding Excretory System of frogs and choose the correct option\n\n(i) In female, urinary bladder acts as urogenital duct.\n\n(ii) Kidney is composed of several uriniferous tubules.\n\n(iii) Urinary-bladder is dorsal to rectum.\n\n(iv) Excretory product is ammonia and released outside by cloaca.','Only (ii) is correct','The elimination of nitrogenous wastes is carried out by a well developed excretory system. The excretory system consists of a pair of kidneys, ureters, cloaca and urinary bladder. These are compact, dark red and bean like structures situated a little posteriorly in the body cavity on both sides of vertebral column. Each kidney is composed of several structural and functional units called uriniferous tubules or nephrons. Two ureters emerge from the kidneys in the male frogs. The ureters act as urinogenital duct which opens into the cloaca. In females the ureters and oviduct open seperately in the cloaca. The thin-walled urinary bladder is present ventral to the rectum which also opens in the cloaca. The frog excretes urea and thus is a ureotelic animal. Excretory wastes are carried by blood into the kidney where it is separated and excreted.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:118; paragraph no. 3).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Only (ii) is correct','Both (i) and (iii) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-118','11-83',NULL),
(71017,'biology','Consider following statement regarding blood in frog and choose the correct option\n\n(i) RBCs are enucleated and contain myoglobin.\n\n(ii) WBCs are absent in frogs.\n\n(iii) Lymph and blood have same composition.\n\n(iv) The circulation of blood is achieved by the pumping action of the muscular heart.','Only (iv) is correct','The blood is composed of plasma and cells. The blood cells are RBC (red blood cells) or erythrocytes, WBC (white blood cells) or leucocytes and platelets. RBC’s are nucleated and contain red coloured pigment namely haemoglobin. The lymph is different from blood. It lacks few proteins and RBCs. The blood carries nutrients, gases and water to the respective sites during the circulation. The circulation of blood is achieved by the pumping action of the muscular heart.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:118; paragraph no. 2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-118','11-82',NULL),
(71018,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Excretory organs in earthworms are metanephridia\n\nR: Metanephridia are present on 10th segment of the body','If both Assertion and Reason are false','The excretory organs occur as segmentally arranged coiled tubules called nephridia (sing.: nephridium). They are of three types: (i) septal nephridia, present on both the sides of intersegmental septa of segment 15 to the last that open into intestine, (ii) integumentary nephridia, attached to lining of the body wall of segment 3 to the last that open on the body surface and (iii) pharyngeal nephridia, present as three paired tufts in the 4th, 5th and 6th segments  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:109; paragraph no. 1).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-109',NULL,NULL),
(71019,'biology','Consider following statement regarding excretory system in earthworms and choose the correct option\n\n(i) Segmentally arranged excretory organs are called as metanephridia.\n\n(ii) Pharyngeal nephridia are present on three segments 4th, 5th and 6th.\n\n(iii) Wastes are released inside the body through cloaca.\n\n(iv) Septal nephridia attach to lining of body wall.','Only (ii) is correct','The excretory organs occur as segmentally arranged coiled tubules called nephridia (sing.: nephridium). They are of three types: (i) septal nephridia, present on both the sides of intersegmental septa of segment 15 to the last that open into intestine, (ii) integumentary nephridia, attached to lining of the body wall of segment 3 to the last that open on the body surface and (iii) pharyngeal nephridia, present as three paired tufts in the 4th, 5th and 6th segments. These different types of nephridia are basically similar in structure. Nephridia regulate the volume and composition of the body fluids. A nephridium starts out as a funnel that collects excess fluid from coelomic chamber. The funnel connects with a tubular part of the nephridium which delivers the wastes through a pore to the surface in the body wall into the digestive tube.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:109; paragraph no. 1).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Only (ii) is correct','Both (i) and (iii) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-109',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/71019q.png'),
(71020,'biology','Consider following statement regarding nervous system of earthworm and choose the correct option\n\n(i) Muscular response to body is provided by cranial nerves.\n\n(ii) Nerve cord in posterior region bifurcate ventrally.\n\n(iii) Cerebral ganglia provide visual input.\n\n(iv) Ganglia are arranged on a ventral paired nerve cord.','Only (iv) is correct','Nervous system is basically represented by ganglia arranged segment wise on the ventral paired nerve cord. The nerve cord in the anterior region (3rd and 4th segments) bifurcates, laterally encircling the pharynx and joins the cerebral ganglia dorsally to form a nerve ring. The cerebral ganglia along with other nerves in the ring integrate sensory input as well as command muscular responses of the body.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:109; paragraph no. 2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Both (i) and (iii) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-109',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/71020q.png'),
(121325,'chemistry','The number of isomers in C₄H₁₀O will be:','7','There are 7 isomers in C₄H₁₀O. Out of these, 4 are alcohols and 3 are ethers. (NEET 1996) Refer diagram(e1214)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> Previous years questions',NULL,'3','mcq','5','6','7','8','NEW','11-349','11-271','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/121325q.png'),
(71021,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Sense organs such as chemoreceptors are located anteriorly in earthworm.\n\nR: Chemoreceptors are those which can which react to chemical stimuli and are located anteriorly in an earthworm.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Worms have specialised chemoreceptors (taste receptors) which react to chemical stimuli. These sense organs are located on the anterior part of the worm.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:110; paragraph no. 1).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-110',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/71021q.png'),
(71022,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Earthworm is bisexual .\n\nR: A pair of testes present on 7th segment of body.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','Earthworm is hermaphrodite (bisexual), i.e. testes and ovaries are present in the same individual. There are two pairs of testes present in the 10th and 11th segments.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:110; paragraph no. 2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-110',NULL,NULL),
(71023,'biology','Consider following statement regarding male reproductive system of earthworm and choose the correct option\n\n(i) Testes are present on 7th and 8th segments.\n\n(ii) Vas deferens joins prostatic duct at segment 16.\n\n(iii) Male genital pores are located ventro-laterally on 18th segment.\n\n(iv) Two pairs of spermatheca are present at 12th segment.','Only (iii) is correct','Earthworm is hermaphrodite (bisexual), i.e., testes and ovaries are present in the same individual. There are two pairs of testes present in the 10th and 11th segments. Their vasa deferentia run up to the 18th segment where they join the prostatic duct. Two pairs of accessory glands are present one pair each in the 17th and 19th segments. The common prostate and spermatic duct (vasa deferentia) opens to the exterior by a pair of male genital pores on the ventro-lateral side of the 18th segment. Four pairs of spermathecae are located in 6th-9th segments (one pair in each segment). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:110; paragraph no. 2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (i) and (ii) are correct','Only (iii) is correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-110',NULL,NULL),
(71024,'biology','Consider following statement regarding female reproductive system and choose the correct option\n\n(i) Two pairs of ovaries are present on 10th and 11th segment.\n\n(ii) Ovarian funnels continue into oviduct.\n\n(iii) Genital pore of female is present on 14th segment.\n\n(iv) A pairs of accessory glands are also on 17th and 19th segments.','Both (ii) and (iii) are correct','One pair of ovaries is attached at the inter-segmental septum of the 12th and 13th segments. Ovarian funnels are present beneath the ovaries which continue into oviduct, join together and open on the ventral side as a single median female genital\npore on the 14th segment. In males, Two pairs of accessory glands are present one pair each in the 17th and 19th segments.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:110; paragraph no. 2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Both (ii) and (iii) are correct','Both (i) and (iii) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-110',NULL,NULL),
(71025,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R) regarding reproduction in earthworm:\n\nA: There are four pairs of spermatheca are located on 6th – 9th segment.\n\nR: During copulation, spermatozoa are received and stored in spermatheca.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Four pairs of spermathecae are located in 6th-9th segments (one pair in each segment). They receive and store spermatozoa during copulation.   (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:110; paragraph no. 2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-110',NULL,NULL),
(71026,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R) regarding reproduction in earthworm:\n\nA: Clitellum has glands cells which releases eggs and sperms.\n\nR: Clitellum is present at 18th segment of body in earthworm.','If both Assertion and Reason are false','14-16 are covered by a prominent dark band of glandular tissue called clitellum. Thus the body is divisible into three prominent regions – preclitellar, clitellar and postclitellar segments  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:107; paragraph no. 1).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-107',NULL,NULL),
(71027,'biology','Consider following statement regarding fertilization in earthworms and choose the correct option\n\n(i) Fertilization is external, takes place in water.\n\n(ii) Fertilized eggs within the cocoon are slipped off the worm and deposited in the soil.\n\n(iii) 400 worms are produced once in a week.\n\n(iv) Larva is present in earthworms.','Only (ii) is correct','Fertilisation and development occur within the cocoons which are deposited in soil. The ova (eggs) are fertilised by the sperm cells within the cocoon which then slips off the worm and is deposited in or on the soil. The cocoon holds the worm embryos. After about 3 weeks, each cocoon produces two to twenty baby worms with an average of four. Development of earthworms is direct, i.e., there is no larva formed.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:110; paragraph no. 3).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only (i) is correct','Only (ii) is correct','Both (i) and (iii) are correct','Only (iv) is correct','NEW','11-110',NULL,NULL),
(181326,'chemistry','During decomposition of an activated complex\n(a) energy is always released \n(b) energy is always absorbed\n(c) energy does not change \n(d) reactions may be formed','a,d','Activated complex is formed at the highest level of energy of the system. It is unstable and decomposes to give products with release of energy. Reactants may be formed if the reaction is reversible.','Physical chemistry >> Chemical kinetics >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','a,d','b,c','a,b,d','only c','NEW','12-113','12-079',NULL),
(71028,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Cockroaches are beneficial for human in day to day life economically.\n\nR: Cockroaches help to maintain ecological balance being a part of food chain by contamination of food.','If both Assertion and Reason are false','Many species of cockroaches are wild and are of no known economic importance yet. A few species thrive in and around human habitat. They are pests because they spoil food and contaminate it with their smelly excreta. They can transmit a variety of bacterial diseases by contaminating food material.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:115; paragraph no. 2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-115',NULL,NULL),
(71029,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Paurometabolous type of metamorphosis occurs in cockroach.\n\nR: Paurometabolous is the type of metamorphosis in which there is absence of any larval stage.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','The development of P. americana is paurometabolous, meaning there is development through nymphal stage. The nymphs look very much like adults. The nymph grows by moulting about 13 times to reach the adult form. The next to last nymphal stage has wing pads but only adult cockroaches have wings.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:115; paragraph no. 1).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-115',NULL,NULL),
(71030,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding following assertion (A) and reasoning (R):\n\nA: Frogs are ecologically beneficial as they are important components of food chain\n\nR: Frogs maintain ecological balance because these serve as important links in food chains and food webs across the ecosystem.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Frogs are beneficial for mankind because they eat insects and protect the crop. Frogs maintain ecological balance because these serve as an important link of food chain and food web in the ecosystem. In some countries the muscular legs of frog are used as food by man. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:120; paragraph no. 2).','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'1','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-120','11-84',NULL),
(71031,'biology','Assertion: Frogs shows hibernation and aestivation\nReason: This protect frog from extreme heat and cold respectively.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Structural Organisation In Animals >> Indepth questions (syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-116','11-80',NULL),
