(271314,'biology','Sickle-cell anemia is caused when','Both mother and father are carrier','Sickle-cell anemia can be transmitted from parents to the offspring when both the partners are carrier for the gene (or heterozygous). Reference   :NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Mother is carrier','Father is Carrier','Both mother and father are carrier','Both father and mother are diseased','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271315,'biology','Sickle-cell anemia is','Controlled by a single pair of allele,HbA and HbS','Sickle cell anemia  is controlled by a single pair of allele,HbA and HbS. Out of the three possible genotypes only homozygous individuals for HbS (HbSHbS) show the diseased phenotype. Heterozygous (HbAHbS) individuals appear apparently unaffected but they are carrier of the disease as there is 50 %  probability of transmission of the mutant gene to the progeny, thus exhibiting sickle-cell trait  Reference   :NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Controlled by a single pair of Chromosomes','Controlled by a single pair of genes, HbA and HbB.','Controlled by a single pair of allele,HbA and HbS','Controlled by a single pair of allele,HbS and HbS','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271316,'biology','Sickle-cell anemia is exhibited by genotype','HbS HbS','Out of three genotype HbS (HbS HbS) show the diseased phenotype. Heterozygous (HbA HbS) individuals appear apparently unaffected. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','HbA HbS','HbS HbS','HbAHbA','HbM andHbS','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271317,'biology','The defect sickle-cell anemia is due to','Substitution of Glutamic acid by Valine','In Sickle-cell anemia the defect is due to the replacement of Glutamic acid (Glu) by Valine (Val) at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of the haemoglobin molecule. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Substitution of Glutamic acid by Valine','Substitution of protein by fatty acid','Substitution of Ammonic acid by Alanine','Substitution of Glutamic acid by aspartate','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271318,'biology','In Sickle-cell anemia, substitution of Glutamic acid by Valine occurs at','Sixth position of the beta globin chain of the haemoglobin molecule','The substitution of amino acid in the globin protein results due to the single base substitution at the sixth codon of the beta globin gene of hemoglobin molecule. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','5th position of alpha globin chain of white cell','Sixth position of the beta globin chain of the haemoglobin molecule','Fourth position of the Alpha globin chain of the haemoglobin molecule','3rd position of Protein strand of hemoglobin','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271319,'biology','In Sickle-cell anemia, the substitution of amino acid in the globin protein results due to','Single base substitution','The substitution of amino acid in the globin protein results due to the single base substitution at the sixth codon of the beta globin gene. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','Single gene mutation','Single chromosome substitution','Single base substitution','Hemoglobin mutation','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271320,'biology','The change occur in sickle-cell anemia at the sixth codon of the beta globin gene from','GAG to GUG','Due to the single base substitution at the sixth codon of the beta globin gene from GAG to GUG. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','GAG to GUG','GAG to GVG','AUG to GUV','GUG to GAG','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271321,'biology','The mutant haemoglobin molecule in sickle-cell anemia undergoes','Polymerisation under low O₂ tension','The mutant haemoglobin molecule undergoes polymerisation under low O₂ tension causing the change in the shape of the RBC. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','Polymerisation under low O₂ tension','Oxidation under high O₂ tension','Polymerisation under high O₂ tension','Oxidation under low O₂ tension','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271322,'biology','The change in RBC from biconcave disc to elongated sickle like structure  occurs in','Sickle-cell anemia','Sickle Cell Anemia causes the change in the shape of the RBC from biconcave disc to elongated sickle like structure. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'1','mcq','Hemophilia','Sickle-cell anemia','Myotonic Dystrophy','Thalassemia','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271323,'biology','The Given diagram represents (2719)','Individual with sickle-cell anemia','Figure 5.15 (b) NCERT 12, Pageno. 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'1','mcq','From the normal individual','Individual with sickle-cell anemia','Thalassemia replacement','Protein synthesis and coding','NEW','12-090','12-074','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/271323q.png'),
(271324,'biology','The inborn error of metabolism is','Phenylketonuria','Phenylketonuria is a inborn error of metabolism is also inherited as the autosomal recessive trait. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'1','mcq','Sickle-cell anemia','Thalassemia','Down syndrome','Phenylketonuria','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271325,'biology','Phenylketonuria is caused due to','Lack of enzyme that converts the amino acid phenylalanine into tyrosine','The affected individual lacks an enzyme that converts the amino acid phenylalanine into tyrosine. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','Underdeveloped metabolism in individual','Defects in metabolism in inborn','Lack of enzyme that converts the amino acid phenylalanine into tyrosine','Lack of enzymes that participate in metabolism','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271326,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect in phenylketonuria','Methylalanine is accumulated','Phenylalanine is accumulated and converted into phenylpyruvic acid and other derivatives. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'1','mcq','Phenylalanine is accumulated','Phenylalanine is converted into phenylpyruvic acid and other derivatives','Phenylalanine and Its conversion are excreted through urine because of Its poor absorption by kidney','Methylalanine is accumulated','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271327,'biology','Accumulation of  phenylpyruvic acid and other derivatives in brain results in','Mental Retardation','Accumulation of phenylpyruvic acid and other derivatives in brain results in mental retardation. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Causing Coma in a patient','Mental Retardation','Paralysis of brain','Brain death','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271328,'biology','Phenylketonuria occurs due to lack of enzyme that converts the amino acid phenylalanine into','Tyrosine','The affected individual lacks an enzyme that converts the amino acid phenylalanine into tyrosine. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Valine','Glutamic acid','Tyrosine','Trypsin','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271329,'biology','Autosomal linked recessive traits are all of the following except:','Fabry disease','Sickle-cell anaemia : This is an autosome linked recessive trait that can be transmitted from parents to the offspring when both the partners are carrier for the gene (or heterozygous).Thalassemia : This is also an autosome-linked recessive blood disease\ntransmitted from parents to the offspring when both the partners are\nunaffected carrier for the gene (or heterozygous). Phenylketonuria : This inborn error of metabolism is also inherited as\nthe autosomal recessive trait.Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'1','mcq','Sickle-cell anemia','Phenylketonuria','Thalassemia','Fabry disease','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271330,'biology','Which is not the most common and prevalent Mendelian disorder:','AIDS','Most common and prevalent Mendelian disorders are Haemophilia, Cystic fibrosis, Sickle cell anaemia, Colour blindness, Phenylketonuria, Thalassemia. Reference  : NCERT PAGE 89','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Colour blindness','Phenylketonuria','Thalassemia','AIDS','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(271331,'biology','In sickle cell anemia,Amino acid is substituted at which position','6th position of Beta chain','(Glu) by Valine (Val) at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of the haemoglobin molecule.Reference  : NCERT Page 89','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','6th position of Beta chain','4th position of Beta chain','6th position of alpha chain','4th position of alpha chain','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(271332,'biology','Genetic disorder are grouped in to  \n\ni.Mendelian Disorders        \nii.Chromosomal Disorders        \niii.Hereditary disorders        \niv.Somatic disorders','i and ii','Broadly, genetic disorders may be grouped into two categories-Mendelian disorders and Chromosomal disorders. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 89','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','ii and iii','iii and iv','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(271333,'biology','Mendelian disorders are mainly due to','Alternation in the single gene','Mendelian disorders are mainly determined by alteration or mutation in the single gene. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 89','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Alternation in the single gene','Mutation in the multiple gene','Destroy of single gene','Alterationin multiple genes','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(271334,'biology','The pattern of inheritance of Mendelian disorders can be traced in a family by','Pedigree Analysis','The pattern of inheritance of such Mendelian disorders can be traced in a family by the pedigree analysis. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 89','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'1','mcq','Linkage','Inheritance of diseases','Pedigree Analysis','Genetic map','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(271335,'biology','Mendelian disorders are','Both dominant and recessive','It is important to note that Mendelian disorders may be dominant or recessive. By pedigree analysis one can easily understand whether the trait is dominant or recessive. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 89','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Mendelian Disorders',NULL,'1','mcq','Dominant only','Recessive only','Both dominant and recessive','Codominance','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(271401,'biology','Which type of thalassemia is controlled by two genes','α Thalassemia','α Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes HBA1 and HBA2 on chromosome 16 of each parent.Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','β Thalassemia','α Thalassemia','Gamma Thalassemia','Normal thalassemia','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271402,'biology','β Thalassemia is controlled by','Single gene HBB on chromosome 11','β Thalassemia is controlled by a single gene HBB on chromosome 11 of each parent and occurs due to mutation of one or both the genes.  Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','Single gene HBB on chromosome 11','Two genes HBA1 and HBA2','Three genes HBB1, HBB2, HBB3','Single gene HBB on chromosome 22','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271403,'biology','α Thalassemia is controlled by','Two genes HBA1 and HBA2 on chromosome 16','α Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes HBA1 and HBA2 on chromosome 16 of each parent and it is observed due to mutation or deletion of one or more of the four genes. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','Two genes HBA,HBB','Single gene HBA1 on chromosome 13','Two genes HBA1 and HBA2 on chromosome 16','Single gene HBA1 on chromosome 16','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271404,'biology','Which among the following is Incorrect','Sickle-cell anemia is a  quantitative problem','Thalassemia differs from sickle-cell anaemia in that the former is a quantitative problem of synthesising too few globin molecules while the latter is a qualitative problem of synthesising an incorrectly functioning globin. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Thalassemia is a quantitative problem','Sickle-cell anemia is a  qualitative problem','Thalassemia and sickle cell anemia both are defects of globin molecule','Sickle-cell anemia is a  quantitative problem','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271405,'biology','Chromosomal disorders are caused due to all except:','Normal arrangement of chromosomes','The chromosomal disorders are caused due to absence or excess or abnormal arrangement of one or more chromosomes. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Absence of one or more chromosomes','Excess arrangement of one or more chromosomes','Abnormal arrangement of one or more chromosomes','Normal arrangement of chromosomes','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271406,'biology','Failure of segregation of chromatids during cell division cycle results in gain or loss of a chromosome(s) called as','Aneuploidy','Failure of segregation of chromatids during cell division cycle results in the gain or loss of a chromosome(s), called aneuploidy. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Aneuploidy','Polyploidy','Aneurysm','Accumulation of chromosomes','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271407,'biology','Which among the following are the example of Aneuploidy','Turner\'s syndrome','Down’s syndrome results in the gain of extra copy of chromosome 21. Similarly, Turner’s syndrome results due to loss of an X chromosome in human females. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Turner\'s syndrome','Sickle cell anemia','Thalassemia','Methemoglobinemia','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271408,'biology','Which among the following is false','In human 22 pairs are allosomes and one pair of autosomes is present','The total number of chromosomes in a normal human cell is 46 (23 pairs). Out of these 22 pairs are autosomes and one pair of chromosomes are sex chromosome. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','Polyploidy is seen in plants','The total number of chromosomes in a normal human cell is 46 (23 pairs)','In human 22 pairs are allosomes and one pair of autosomes is present','Polyploidy is due to failure of cytokinesis after telophase of cell division','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271409,'biology','Additional copy of a chromosome in an individual is called as','Trisomy','Sometimes additional copy of a chromosome may be included in an individual this situation is known as Trisomy. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'1','mcq','Monosomy','Trisomy','Aneuploidy','Polyploidy','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271410,'biology','Lack one of any one pair of chromosomes in individual is called as','Monosomy','Sometimes a individual may lack one of any one pair of chromosomes such situation is called monosomy. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'1','mcq','Quality syndrome','Aneurysm','Monosomy','Trisomy','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271411,'biology','What is true about polyploidy?','In this situation there an Increase in whole set of chromosomes','Failure of cytokinesis after telophase stage of cell division results in an increase in a whole set of chromosomes in an organism and this phenomenon is known as polyploidy. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Failure of cytokinesis after prophase stage of cell division','In this situation there an Increase in whole set of chromosomes','Many chromosomes are added','In this situation there an decrease in whole set of chromosomes','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271412,'biology','The cause of Down\'s syndrome is','Additional copy of the chromosome number 21 (trisomy of 21)','The cause of this genetic disorder is the presence of an additional copy of the chromosome number 21 (trisomy of 21) Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Absence of one of the X chromosomes 45 with X0','Additional copy of the chromosome number 21 (trisomy of 21)','Additional copy of X- chromosome into a karyotype of 47,XXY','Mental Retardation and loss of one chromosome','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271413,'biology','Down\'s syndrome was first described by','Langdon Down (1866)','This disorder was first described by Langdon Down (1866). Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Langdon Down (1866)','Lucis beth (1675)','Francis Down (1966)','Catherine fim (1452)','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271414,'biology','Which among the following isn\'t an affected cause of Down\'s syndrome','Anemia','The affected individual is short statured with small round head, furrowed tongue and partially open mouth. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','Furrowed tongue','Congenital heart disease','Palm crease','Anemia','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271415,'biology','The presence of an additional copy of X- chromosome resulting into a karyotype of 47, XXY is a','Klinefelter’s Syndrome','This genetic disorder is caused due to the presence of an additional copy of X- chromosome resulting into a karyotype of 47, XXY   Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','Down syndrome','Turner\'s syndrome','Klinefelter’s Syndrome','Nephritis','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271416,'biology','Which among the following is Incorrect in Klinefelter’s Syndrome','Do not have overall masculine development','Klinefelter’s Syndrome individual has overall masculine development. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','Such an individual has overall masculine development','Such individuals are sterile','feminine development (development of breast(Gynaecomastia)','Do not have overall masculine development','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271417,'biology','Individual with Turner\'s syndromes have all charcters except','Such females are fertile','Individual with Turner\'s syndromes, Such females are sterile as ovaries are rudimentary besides other features including lack of other secondary sexual characters. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Such females are sterile','Lack of other secondary sexual characters','Ovaries are rudimentary','Such females are fertile','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271418,'biology','Turners syndrome is a','Disorder due to the absence of one of the X chromosomes (45 with X0)','It is a disorder is caused due to the absence of one of the X chromosomes, i.e., 45 with X0 Reference   :NCERT PAGE 92','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'3','mcq','Disorder due to absence of Y chromosome (45 with XO)','Disorder due to the absence of one of the X chromosomes (45 with X0)','Presence of extra X chromosome in individuals','Presence of extra Y chromosomes','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(271419,'biology','Match the following','Turner’s Syndrome-45 with X0,Down\'s syndrome-trisomy of 21,Klinefelter’s Syndrome-47 XXY,Sickle-cell anemia-Qualitative problem','Turner’s Syndrome-45 with X0,Down\'s syndrome-trisomy of 21,Klinefelter’s Syndrome-47 XXY,Sickle-cell anemia-Qualitative problem','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'2','match','Turner’s Syndrome,45 with X0','Down\'s syndrome,trisomy of 21','Klinefelter’s Syndrome,47 XXY','Sickle-cell anemia,Qualitative problem','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
