(271426,'biology','Thalassemia is classified on the basis of','Chain of the haemoglobin molecule is affected','Thalassemia can be classified according to which chain of the haemoglobin molecule is affected. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Thalassemia and Chromosomal Disorders',NULL,'2','mcq','Type of hemoglobin molecule is affected','Chain of the haemoglobin molecule is affected','Based on mutation of gene','Chain of myoglobin affected','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271501,'chemistry','The correct order of increasing basic structure for the bases NH₃, CH₃NH₂ and (CH₃)₂NH','NH₃ < CH₃NH₂ < (CH₃)₂NH','The basicity of amines in aqueous solution depends on the stability factor of the ammonium cation or conjugate acid which is formed by accepting a proton from water that in turn depends on the + I-effect of alkyl group, extent of hydrogen bonding and stearic factor. \nAll these conditions are favourable for 2° amines. Therefore, 2° amines are the strongest bases.\nIf the alkyl group is small i.e., CH₃ then there is no steric hindrance to H-bonding. \nThus, the stability due to hydrogen bonding predominates over the stability due to + I-effect of —CH₃ group therefore, primary amine is a stronger base than 3° amine, resulting in the order of decreasing basic strength for methylamines in aqueous solution:\n(CH₃)₂NH > CH₃NH₂ > (CH₃)₃N.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 398; Paragraph No. 3) (NEET2019)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃NH₂ < NH₃ < (CH₃)₂NH','(CH₃)₂NH < NH₃ < CH₃NH₂','NH₃ < CH₃NH₂ < (CH₃)₂NH','CH₃NH₂ < (CH₃)₂NH < NH₃','NEW','12-398','12-268',NULL),
(271502,'chemistry','Which of the following is the strongest base? Refer diagram(n27136)','d','In case of substituted aniline, it is observed that electron releasing groups like –OCH₃, –CH₃ increase basic strength whereas electron withdrawing groups like –NO₂, –SO₃H, –COOH, –X decrease it. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 399; Paragraph No.3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-399','12-269','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/271502q.png'),
(271503,'chemistry','Amongst the following the most basic compound is','benzylamine','In case of substituted aniline, it is observed that electron releasing groups like –OCH₃, –CH₃ increase basic strength whereas electron withdrawing groups like –NO₂, –SO₃H, –COOH, –X decrease it. \n\nIn acetanilide, CH3CO- group acts as electron withdrawing group.\nIn Aniline phenyl  acts as electron withdrawing group\n\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 399; Paragraph No.3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','p-nitroaniline','acetanilide','aniline','benzylamine','NEW','12-399','12-269',NULL),
(271504,'chemistry','Which one of the following methods is neither means for the synthesis nor for seperation of amines?','Wurtz reaction','Wurtz reaction forms alkanes from alkyl halides in the presence of Na/dry ether.\n\nBenzenesulphonyl chloride (C₆H₅SO₂Cl), which is also known as Hinsberg’s reagent, reacts with primary and secondary amines to\nform sulphonamides. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No. 8)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Curtius reaction','Wurtz reaction','Hoffman method','Hinsberg method','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL);
