(271505,'chemistry','Fluorobenzene can be synthesised in the laboratory','from aniline by diazotisation followed by heating the diazonium salt with HBF4','When arenediazonium chloride is treated with fluoroboric acid, arene diazonium fluoroborate is precipitated which on heating decomposes to yield aryl fluoride. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 8) Refer diagram(ne27136)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','by heating phenol with HF and KF','from aniline by diazotisation followed by heating the diazonium salt with HBF4','by direct flourination of benzene with F₂ gas','by reacting bromoethane  with NaF solution','NEW','12-405','12-275',NULL),
(271506,'chemistry','In the chemical reaction, \nCH₃CH₂NH₂ + CHCl₃ + KOH  → A + B + H₂O, the compound A and B are :','C₂H₅NC and KCl','Aliphatic and aromatic primary amines on heating with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide form isocyanides or carbylamines which are foul smelling substances. Secondary and tertiary amines do not show this reaction. This reaction is known as carbylamine reaction or isocyanide test and is used as a test for primary amines. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No.1). diagram(ne2790)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','C₂H₅CN and KCl','CH₃CH₂CONH₂ and KCl','C₂H₅NC and KCl','C₂H₅NC and KOH','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL),
(41423,'chemistry','The molecular geometry of AB₂E₂ type molecules:','Bent','Theoretically the shape should have been Tetrahedal but actually it is found to be bent or V-shaped, this is due to lp-bp repulsion which is more than bp-bp repulsion so the angle is reduced from 104.5 from 109.5. \n\nAs shown in diagram\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 116; Table 4.8) diagram(ne0412)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Trigonal pyramidal','Bent','See-saw','T-shaped','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(271507,'chemistry','The compounds \'A\' and \'B\' in the given reaction diagram(n27137) will be','benzene diazonium chloride and flourobenzene','When arenediazonium chloride is treated with fluoroboric acid, arene diazonium fluoroborate is precipitated which on heating decomposes to yield aryl fluoride. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 8) Refer diagram(ne27136) Refer diagram(ne27137)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','nitrobenzene and flourobenzene','phenol and benzene','benzene diazonium chloride and flourobenzene','nitrobenzene and chlorobenzene','NEW','12-401','12-271','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/271507q.png'),
(271508,'chemistry','The compounds \'A\' and \'B\' in the given reaction diagram(n27138) will be','benzene diazonium chloride and benzonitrile','The Cl⁻, Br⁻ and CN⁻ nucleophiles can easily be introduced in the benzene ring in the presence of Cu(I) ion. This reaction is called Sandmeyer reaction. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 405; Paragraph No. 4). Refer diagram(ne27138)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','fluorobenzene and phenol','benzene diazonium chloride and benzonitrile','nitrobenzene and chlorobenzene','phenol and bromobenzene','NEW','12-405','12-275','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/271508q.png'),
(271509,'chemistry','An organic compound A on reacting with NH₃ gives B. On heating B gives C. C in the presence of KOH reacts with Br₂ to give CH₃CH₂NH₂. A is','CH₃CH₂COOH','In the presence of KOH along with Br2, Hoffman bromamide reaction will occur. The complete reaction is given below in the diagram(ne27139)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃CH(CH₃)COOH','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂COOH','CH₃CH₂COOH','CH₃COOH','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(271510,'chemistry','Considering the basic strength of amines in aqueous solution, which one has the smallest pKb value?','(CH₃)₂NH','The basicity of amines in aqueous solution depends on the stability factor of the ammonium cation or conjugate acid which is formed by accepting a proton from water that in turn depends on the + I-effect of alkyl group, extent of hydrogen bonding and stearic factor. All these conditions are favourable for 2° amines. Therefore, 2° amines are the strongest bases. \nOrder of basic strength\n(CH₃)₂NH > CH₃NH₂ > (CH₃)₃N. \n\nSo the order of pKb value will be:\n(CH₃)₂NH < CH₃NH₂ < (CH₃)₃N\n\n\n\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 398; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','(CH₃)₂NH','CH₃NH₂','(CH₃)₃N','Ph-NH₂','NEW','12-398','12-268',NULL),
(271511,'chemistry','On heating an aliphatic primary amine with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide, the organic formed is','alkyl isocyanide','"Aliphatic and aromatic primary amines on heating with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide form isocyanides or carbylamines which are foul smelling substances. Secondary and tertiary amines do not show this reaction. This reaction is known as carbylamine reaction or isocyanide test and is used as a test for primary amines. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No.1). diagram(ne27126)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','alkanol','alkanediol','aklyl cyanide','alkyl isocyanide','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL),
(271512,'chemistry','In the reaction diagram(n27139), the product E is:','c','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne27140)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-401','12-271','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/271512q.png'),
(271513,'chemistry','In the Hoffman bromamide degradation reaction, the number of moles of NaOH and Br2 used per mole of amine produced are:','four moles of NaOH and one  mole of Br2','CH₃CONH₂ + Br₂ + 4NaOH → CH₃NH₂ + 2NaBr + Na₂CO₃ + 2H₂O (Hoffmann bromamide reaction). \n"Hoffmann synthesis of primary amine by the reaction of amide and bromine in the presence of NaOH. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 394; Paragraph No. 2)\nMechanism of reaction is given in diagram(ne27130)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','four moles of NaOH and two moles of Br2','two moles of NaOH and two moles of Br2','four moles of NaOH and one  mole of Br2','one  mole of NaOH and one mole of Br2','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(271514,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will give significant amount of meta-product during mononitration reaction? Refer diagram(n27140)','c','On treating aniline with H2SO4 + HNO3, anilium ion is formed .\nThis ion is strongly deactivating and meta directing in nature.\n\nThus, it gives meta product on nitration. Refer diagram(ne27141)','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-403','12-273','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/271514q.png'),
(271515,'biology','The movement of a gene from one linkage group to another is called [NEET 2015]','Translocation','Translocation is a chromosomal abnormality caused by rearrangement of parts between non-homologous chromosomes. It may cause a gene to move from one linkage group to another. Reference  : NCERT PAGE84','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Translocation','Crossing Over','Inversion','Duplication','NEW','12-084','12-066',NULL),
(271601,'chemistry','Assertion: Adrenaline and ephedrine both contain a secondary amino group and are used to increase blood pressure.\n\nReason: Benadryl contains a tertiary amino group and is used as an antihistaminic drug.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true because adrenaline and ephedrine both contain a secondary amino group and are used to increase blood pressure. The reason is also true because Benadryl contains a tertiary amino group and is used as an antihistaminic drug. However, the reason does not explain why the assertion is true.','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-405','12-275',NULL),
(271602,'chemistry','Assertion: Quaternary ammonium salts are used as surfactants.\n\nReason: Diazonium salts are intermediates in the preparation of a variety of aromatic compounds including dyes.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true because quaternary ammonium salts are used as surfactants. The reason is also true because diazonium salts are indeed intermediates in the preparation of a variety of aromatic compounds including dyes. However, the reason does not explain why the assertion is true.','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-390','12-260',NULL),
(271603,'chemistry','Assertion: Aromatic primary amines cannot be prepared by the Gabriel synthesis.\n\nReason: Aryl halides do not undergo nucleophillic substitution with the anion formed by phthalimide in the Gabriel synthesis.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The Gabriel synthesis involves the conversion of phthalimide to primary amines through the reaction of the potassium salt of phthalimide with an alkyl halide and alkaline hydrolysis. However, this method is not suitable for preparing aromatic primary amines because aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution with the anion formed by phthalimide.\nReference- Amines Page no.- 386, Paragraph-1','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen(Amines) >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-386','12-256',NULL),
(271604,'chemistry','Assertion: The Hoffmann bromamide degradation reaction involves migration of an alkyl or aryl group from the carbonyl carbon of an amide to the nitrogen atom.\n\nReason: The amine produced from the Hoffmann bromamide degradation reaction contains one carbon less than the amide from which it is produced.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The Hoffmann bromamide degradation reaction involves migration of an alkyl or aryl group from the carbonyl carbon of an amide to the nitrogen atom. The amine produced from this reaction contains one carbon less than the original amide, which can be explained by the migration of the alkyl or aryl group from the carbonyl carbon to the nitrogen atom.','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(271605,'chemistry','Assertion: The Gabriel synthesis is a useful method for the preparation of aliphatic secondary amines.\n\nReason: Phthalimide on treatment with ethanolic potassium hydroxide forms potassium salt of phthalimide which on heating with alkyl halide followed by heating produces the corresponding  amine.','Both Assertion and Reason is false','The Gabriel synthesis is a method for the preparation of aliphatic primary amines. The reaction involves the conversion of phthalimide to the primary amine through the reaction of the potassium salt of phthalimide with an alkyl halide and alkaline hydrolysis.','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason is false','NEW','12-394','12-264',NULL),
(271606,'chemistry','Assertion: Primary amines react with nitrous acid to form aliphatic diazonium salts which liberate nitrogen gas quantitatively and alcohols due to their instability.\n\nReason: Nitrous acid, which is prepared in situ from a mineral acid and sodium nitrite, reacts differently with primary, secondary, and tertiary amines, forming different products.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Primary amines react with nitrous acid to form unstable aliphatic diazonium salts which breaks down, while aromatic amines react to form aromatic diazonium salts. The nitrogen gas evolved from the decomposition of diazonium salts can be quantitatively measured, and this reaction is used to estimate amino acids and proteins. \n\nSecondary and tertiary amines do not react with nitrous acid to form diazonium salts, and the reaction with nitrous acid is different for them. Therefore, the assertion is true, and the reason is the correct explanation for it.','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason is false','NEW','12-403','12-273',NULL),
(271607,'chemistry','Assertion : N, N-diethylbenzene sulphonamide is not acidic and hence insoluble in alkali because it does not contain any hydrogen atom attached to the nitrogen atom.\n\nReason : Sulphonamide is acidic due to the presence of a strong electron-withdrawing sulphonyl group and thus, is soluble in alkali.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The reason given is true, but it does not explain why N, N-diethylbenzene sulphonamide is insoluble in alkali. The correct reason is that the compound does not have any hydrogen atom attached to the nitrogen atom, which is necessary for making the compound acidic and soluble in alkali.','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason is false','NEW','12-403','12-273',NULL),
(271608,'chemistry','Assertion: Tertiary amines do not react with benzenesulphonyl chloride, which is used for the distinction and separation of primary, secondary, and tertiary amines.\n\nReason: Benzenesulphonyl chloride is replaced by p-phenylsulphonyl chloride these days.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Tertiary aminses do not react with Hinsberg reagent. "These days benzenesulphonyl chloride is replaced by p-toluenesulphonyl chloride. \nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 401; Paragraph No. 5)"','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-401','12-271',NULL),
(271609,'chemistry','Assertion: The maximum electron density in aniline is found in the ortho- and para-positions to the –NH2 group, which becomes centers of high electron density.\n\nReason: The –NH2 group is ortho and para directing and is a powerful activating group.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason given is true and explains why the electron density is maximum in the ortho- and para-positions of aniline.','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-403','12-273',NULL),
(271610,'chemistry','Assertion: Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction (alkylation and acetylation) due to salt formation with aluminium chloride, the Lewis acid, which is used as a catalyst.\n\nReason: Nitrogen of aniline acquires positive charge and hence acts as a strong deactivating group for further reaction.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Aniline contains a basic amino group, which can react with the Lewis acid catalyst in Friedel-Crafts reactions, but it does not undergo this reaction due to the formation of a salt with the Lewis acid, aluminium chloride. The nitrogen of aniline acquires a positive charge in the presence of the Lewis acid, which acts as a strong deactivating group for further reaction. Therefore, both the assertion and the reason are true, and the reason correctly explains why the assertion is true.','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-397','12-267',NULL),
(271611,'chemistry','Assertion: Replacement of diazo group by other groups is helpful in preparing substituted aromatic compounds that cannot be obtained by direct substitution in benzene or substituted benzene.\n\nReason: Aryl fluorides and iodides can be prepared by direct halogenation reaction','Assertion is true but reason is false','Aryl fluorides and iodides cannot be prepared by direct halogenation.\nThe cyano group cannot be introduced by nucleophilic substitution of chlorine in chlorobenzene but cyanobenzene can be easily obtained from diazonium salt.\nThus, the replacement of diazo group by other groups is helpful inpreparing those substituted aromatic compounds which cannot be prepared by direct substitution in benzene or substituted benzene.\nReference: (NCERT class-12; Page No. 406; Paragraph No. 5)"','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-406','12-276',NULL),
(271612,'chemistry','Assertion: The azo products obtained from coupling reactions have an extended conjugate system.\n\nReason: Azo compounds have both the aromatic rings joined through the –N=N– bond.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Azo compounds are formed through coupling reactions and have an extended conjugate system that involves both aromatic rings joined together by the -N=N- bond.','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-406','12-276',NULL),
(271613,'chemistry','Assertion: The diazonium group is a very good leaving group.\n\nReason: Diazonium group is substituted by other groups such as Cl–, Br–, I–, CN– and OH– which displace nitrogen from the aromatic ring.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','The diazonium group is a very good leaving group, and it is substituted by other groups such as Cl–, Br–, I–, CN– and OH– which displace nitrogen from the aromatic ring. Diazonium salts undergo nucleophilic substitution reactions involving displacement of nitrogen. The reason correctly explains why the assertion is true.','Organic Chemistry >> Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Assertion is false but reason is true','NEW','12-404','12-274',NULL),
(271701,'biology','A disease caused by an autosomal primary non-disjunction is [NEET 2017]','Down\'s syndrome','Down’s syndrome is an autosomal aneuploidy,caused by the presence of an extra-chromosome number 21. Both the chromosomes of the pair 21 pass into a single egg due to non disjunction during oogenesis Reference  : NCERT PAGE  91.','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Klinefelter’s Syndrome','Turner\'s syndrome','Sickle-cell anemia','Down\'s syndrome','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271702,'biology','If a colour-blind man marries a woman who is homozygous for normal colour vision, the probability of their son being colour-blind is[NEET 2016]','0','Genotype of colour blind man – Xc Y Genotype of woman homozygous – XX for normal woman. Reference  : NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','0','0.5','0.75','1','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271703,'biology','Which of the following most appropriately describes haemophilia? [NEET 2016]','X-linked recessive gene disorder','Haemophilia is a sex-linked disease. It occurs due to the presence of a recessive sex linked gene h, which is carried by X-chromosome. Reference  : NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Chromosomal Disorder','Dominant gene disorder','Recessive gene disorder','X-linked recessive gene disorder','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271704,'biology','A colour blind man marries a woman with normal sight who has no history of Colorblindness in her family. What is the probability of their grandson being colour blind ?[NEET 2015]','0.25','When a colour blind man (XcY) marries a normal woman (XX), all of their daughters are carriers and all of their sons are normal,When the carrier daughter (XXc) is married to a normalman, the probability of their son being colour blind is 0.25,it is clear that the probability of occurrence of Colorblindness in the grandson of a colour blind man and a normal woman is 0.25. Reference  : NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Nil','0.25','0.5','1','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271705,'biology','An abnormal human baby with ‘XXX’ sex chromosomes was born due to [NEET 2015]','Formation of abnormal ova in the mother.','The abnormal baby has an extra X chromosome, thus it must have been produced by fusion of abnormal XX ovum with a normal X sperm. Abnormal XX spermis not possible because, males have XY genotype, and if produce abnormal sperms, then XY sperms and O sperms will be produced. If fusion of multiple gametes have occurred (either two ova with one sperm or two sperms with one ovum), then the human baby will have triploid genotype not the trisomy of sex chromosomes. Reference  : NCERT PAGE91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Fusion of two ova and one sperm','Fusion of two sperms and one ovum','Formation of abnormal sperms in the father','Formation of abnormal ova in the mother.','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271706,'biology','A man whose father was colour blind marries a woman who had a colour blind mother and normal father. What percentage of male children of this couple will be colour blind? [NEET 2014]','50%','The son of a woman who carries the gene has a 50 % chance of being colour blind. The mother is not herself colour blind because the gene is recessive. That means that its effect is suppressed by her matching dominant normal gene. A daughter will not normally be colour blind, unless her mother is a carrier and her father is colour blind. Reference  : NCERT PAGE 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','25%','0%','50%','75%','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271707,'biology','A human female with Turner’s syndrome [NEET 2014]','Has 45 chromosomes with XO','A human female with Turner’s syndrome has single sex chromosome i.e., 44 + X0 (45). Such females are called sterile females with rudimentary ovaries. Other associated phenotypes of this condition are short stature, webbed-neck, broad chest, lack of secondary sexual characteristics and sterility. Thus, any imbalance in the copies of the sex chromosomes may disrupt the genetic information necessary for normal sexual development. Reference  : NCERT PAGE91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Has 45 chromosomes with XO','Has one additional X chromosome','Exhibits male characters','Is able to produce children with normal husband.','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271708,'biology','Select the incorrect statement with regard to haemophilia.','It is an Autosomal recessive disease.','It is genetically due to the presence of a recessive sex linked gene h, carried by X-chromosome. A female becomes haemophiliac only when both of her X-chromosomes carry the gene (XhXh). However, such females generally die before birth because the combination of these two recessive alleles is lethal.\nReference  : NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','It is a X linked recessive disease.','A single protein clotting factor defect involved in the clotting of blood is affected.','It is a sex-linked disease, common in males','It is an Autosomal recessive disease.','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271709,'biology','If both parents are carriers for thalassaemia, which is an autosomal recessive disorder, what are the chances of pregnancy resulting in an affected child?','25%','Thalassaemia is an autosome-linked recessive blood disease transmitted from parents to the offspring when both the partners are unaffected carrier for the gene (or heterozygous).Thus, one out of 4 children will be affected which turns out to be 25percent. Reference  : NCERT Page 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','25%','100%','No chance','50%','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271710,'biology','Which one is the incorrect statement with regard to the importance of pedigree analysis?','It confirms that DNA is the carrier of genetic information.','Pedigree analysis is a system of analysis by following the movement and distribution of certai genetic traits in many generations of a family. Pedigree analysis cannot confirm that DNA is the carrier of genetic information because it is an analysis system. For DNA based experiments, molecular biology techniques are used. Reference  : NCERT PAGE 88','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','It confirms that DNA is the carrier of genetic information.','It helps to understand whether the trait in question is dominant or recessive.','It confirms that the trait is linked to one of the autosome.','It helps to trace the inheritance of a  specific trait.','NEW','12-088','12-072',NULL),
(271711,'biology','Down’s syndrome in humans is due to','Three copies of chromosome 21','Non-disjunction occurs when a pair of homologous chromosomes do not separate in meiosis but migrate to the same pole of the cell resulting in an uneven number of chromosomes in the daughter cells (45 in one and 47 in other). This numerical abnormality results in trisomy (2n + 1) and monosomy (2n – 1). Non-disjunction is more common in sex chromosomes.Reference  : NCERT PAGE91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Three ‘X’ chromosomes','Three copies of chromosome 21','Monosomy','Two ‘Y’ chromosomes.','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271712,'biology','How many true breeding pea plant varieties did Mendel select as pairs, which were similar except in one character with contrasting traits?  [NEET 2020]','14','Mendel conducted artificial pollination or cross pollination experiments using several true-breeding pea lines. He selected 14 true-breeding pea plant varieties, as pairs which were similar except for one character with contrasting traits. Some of the contrasting traits selected were smooth or wrinkled seeds, yellow or green seeds, inflated (full) or constricted green or yellow pods and tall or dwarf plants. Reference  : NCERT PAGE72','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','4','2','14','8','NEW','12-072','12-056',NULL),
(271713,'biology','Identify the wrong statement with Reference   to the gene ‘I’ that controls ABO blood groups [NEET 2020]','When IA and IB are present together, they express same type of sugar','ABO blood groups are controlled by the gene I. The gene (I) has three alleles IA, IB and i. The alleles IA and IB produce a slightly different form of the sugar while allele i does not produce any sugar. Because humans are diploid organisms, each person possesses any two of the three I gene alleles. When IA and IB are present together they both express their own types of sugars,because of Codominance. Reference  : NCERT PAGE 77','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','The gene (I) has three alleles','A person will have only two of the three alleles','When IA and IB are present together, they express same type of sugar','Allele i does not produce any sugar','NEW','12-077','12-061',NULL),
(271714,'biology','In Antirrhinum (Snapdragon), a red flower was crossed with a white flower and in F1 generation all pink flowers were obtained. When pink flowers were self crossed, the F2 generation showed white, red and pink flowers. Choose the incorrect statements from the following [NEET 2019]','Law of segregation does not apply in this experiment','Law of segregation applies in this case as when pink flowers obtained in F1 are selfed then red and white flowers are obtained in F2 which indicates that there is no mixing of gametes. Reference  : NCERT PAGE 73','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Law of segregation does not apply in this experiment','This experiment does not follow the Principle of Dominance','Pink colour in F1 is due to incomplete dominance','Ratio of F2 is 1 (red) : 2 (pink) : 1 (white).','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(271715,'biology','Experimental verification of the chromosomal theory of inheritance was done by [NEET 2020]','Morgan','Sutton and Boveri proposed the chromosomal theory of inheritance but Its experimental verification was done by Thomas Hunt Morgan. Reference  : NCERT PAGE73','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Mendel','Sutton','Boveri','Morgan','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(271716,'biology','What map unit (centimorgan) is adopted in the construction of genetic maps? [NEET 2019]','A unit of  distance  between genes on chromosomes, representing 1% cross over','Genetic map is a linear graphic representation of the sequence and relative distance of various genes present in a chromosome. 1% crossing over between two linked genes is known as 1 map unit or centiMorgan (cM). Reference  : NCERT PAGE 73','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','A  unit of distance between genes on chromosomes, representing 50% cross over','A unit of distance between two expressed genes, representing 10% cross over','A unit of distance between two expressed genes, representing 100% cross over','A unit of  distance  between genes on chromosomes, representing 1% cross over','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(271717,'biology','The frequency of recombination between gene present on the same chromosome as a measure of the distance between genes was explained by [NEET 2019]','Alfred Sturtevant','T.H. Morgan coined the term linkage to describe the physical association of genes on chromosome and term recombination to describe the generation of non- parental gene combinations. Alfred Sturtevant used the frequency of recombination between gene pairs on the same chromosome as a measure of the distance between genes.Reference  : NCERT PAGE 73','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Sutton Boveri','T.H. Morgan','Gregor J.Mendel','Alfred Sturtevant','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(271718,'biology','Which of the following characteristics represent ‘inheritance of blood groups’ in humans? [NEET 2018]\n\n (i)Dominance\n\n (ii)Codominance\n\n (iii)Multiple allele\n\n (iv)Incomplete dominance\n\n (v)Polygenic inheritance','(i), (ii) and (iii)','ABO blood group system in human beings is an example of dominance, Codominance and multiple allelism.\nIA IO, IB IO or i - Alleles shows dominant-recessive relationship.\nIA IB - Codominance is a phenomenon in which alleles of a gene do not show dominant recessive relationship and express themselves independently when present together.\nIA, IB IO or i - More than two alternate forms of a gene present on the same locus are called as multiple alleles and the mode of inheritance in the alleles is called multiple allelism. Reference  : NCERT PAGE 77','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','(ii), (iii) and (v)','(i), (ii) and (iii)','(ii), (iv) and (v)','(i), (iii) and (v)','NEW','12-077','12-061',NULL),
(271719,'biology','Select the correct match. [NEET 2020]\n\n (a)Haemophilia – Y linked\n\n (b)Phenylketonuria – Autosomal dominant trait\n\n (c)Sickle cell anaemia – Autosomrecessive traitait,chromosome -11\n\n (d)Thalassemia – X linked','c','Sickle cell anaemia – Autosomal recessive trait,chromosome -11  Reference  : NCERT PAGE 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271720,'biology','What is the genetic disorder in which an individual has an overall masculine development, gynaecomastia and is sterile? [NEET 2019]','Klinefelter’s syndrome','Klinefelter’s syndrome occurs by the union of an abnormal XX egg and a normal Y sperm or a normal X egg and abnormal XY sperm. The individual has 47 chromosomes (44 + XXY). Such persons are sterile males with undeveloped testes, mental retardation, sparse body hair, long limbs and with some female characteristics such as enlarged breast, i.e., gynaecomastia. Reference  : NCERT PAGE91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Down’s syndrome','Turner’s syndrome','Klinefelter’s syndrome','Edward syndrome','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271721,'biology','A woman has an X-linked condition on one of her X chromosomes. This chromosome can be inherited by [NEET 2018]','Both sons and daughters','Woman acts as a carrier when she has the X-linked condition on one of her X-chromosomes. Both son and daughter inherit X-chromosome from mother. Hence, one of the two daughters will be carrier and one of the two sons will be diseased. It can be explained by the given cross. Reference  : NCERT PAGE  90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Only daughters','Only sons','Only grandchildren','Both sons and daughters','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271722,'biology','The production of gametes by the parents, formation of zygotes, the F1 and F2 plants, can be understood from a diagram called :[NEET 2021]','Punnett square','The production of gametes by the parents, formation of zygotes, the F1 and F2 plants, can be understood from a diagram called Punnett square. Reference    NCERT Page 73','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Net square','Bullet square','Punch square','Punnett square','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(271723,'biology','In a cross between a male and female, both heterozygous for sickle cell anaemia gene, what percentage of the progeny will be diseased?[NEET 2021]','25%','If both parents have sickle cell trait (HbAS) there is a one in four (25%) chance that any given child could be born with sickle cell anaemia. There is also a one in four chance that any given child could be completely unaffected. There is a one in two (50%) chance that any given child will get the sickle cell trait. Reference    NCERT Page 74','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','100%','50%','75%','25%','NEW','12-074','12-058',NULL),
(271724,'biology','Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: \nMendel studied seven pairs of contrasting traits in pea plants and proposed the Laws of Inheritance.\n\nStatement II:\nSeven characters examined by Mendel in his experiment on pea plants were seed shape and color, flower color, pod shape and color, flower position and stem height.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the coorect answer from the option given below:[NEET 2022]','Both Statement I and II are correct','Gregor Mendel conducted hybridisation experiments on garden peas. \nMendel selected 14 true-breeding pea plant varieties,as pairs which were similar except for one character with contrasting traits. Reference   : NCERT Page No.70','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and II are correct','Both Statement I and II are incorrect','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.','NEW','12-070','12-054',NULL),
(271725,'biology','XO type of sex determination can be found in [NEET 2022]','Grasshoppers','Grasshopper is an example of XO type of sex determination in which the males have only one X-chromosome besides the autosomes, whereas females have a pair of X-chromosomes. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 87','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Drosophila','Birds','Grasshoppers','Monkeys','NEW','12-087','12-071',NULL),
(271726,'biology','Which of the following occurs due to the presence of autosome linked dominant trait?[NEET 2022]','Myotonic dystrophy','Myotonic Dystrophy is a autosomal Dominant trait linked Disorder. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 89','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Sickle cell anemia','Myotonic dystrophy','Haemophilia','Thalessemia','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(271727,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Mendel\'s law of Independent assortment does not hold good for the genes that are located closely on the same chromosome.\n \n Reason: Closely located genes assort independently. [NEET 2022]','The assertion is true, but the reason is false','Based upon such observations on dihybrid crosses (crosses between plants differing in two traits) Mendel proposed a second set of generalisations that we call Mendel’s Law of Independent Assortment. \n\nLaw of Independent Assortment states that ‘when two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid, segregation of one pair of characters is independent of the other pair of characters’. Reference   :NCERT PAGE 80','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false','The assertion is false, but the reason is true','NEW','12-080','12-044',NULL),
(271728,'biology','Three children in a family have blood types O, AB and B respectively. What are the genotypes of their parents? [NEET 2013]','IAi and IBi','Three children in a family have blood types O, AB and B respectively will be IAi and IBi. Reference    NCERT Page 77','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','IAi and IBi','IAIB and ii','IBIB and IAIA','IAIA and IBi','NEW','12-077','12-061',NULL),
(271729,'biology','If both parents are carriers for thalassemia, which is an autosomal recessive disorder, what are the chances of pregnancy resulting in an affectedchild? [NEET 2014)','25%','Genotype of carrier parents is – Aa (maleparent) × Aa (female parent)AA= normal child (25%) Aa = carriers child (50%) aa = affected child (25%) Reference    NCERT Page 77','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','50%','25%','100%','No chance','NEW','12-077','12-061',NULL),
(271730,'biology','Assertion : Persons suffering from haemophilia A fail to produce blood clotting factor VI. \n\n Reason :  Platelets in such persons are found in very low concentration','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Haemophilia bleeding disorder is a group of hereditary genetic disorders that impair the body’s ability to control blood clotting or coagulation. In Its most common form, Hemophilia A, clotting factor VIII is absent. In Haemophilia B, factor IX is deficient. Factor VIII participates in blood coagulation; it is a cofactor for factor IXa which, in the presence of Ca+2 and phospholipids forms a complex that converts factor X to the activated form Xa. Defects in this gene results in hemophilia A, a common recessive X-linked coagulation disorder. Prothrombin producing platelets in such persons are not found in very low concentration. Reference    NCERT Page 90','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271731,'biology','Assertion :In case of incomplete linkage, linked gene show new combination along with parental combination. \n\n Reason : In case of incomplete linkage, linked genes are separated by crossing over','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In case of incomplete linkage, the linked gene shows new combination along with parental combination due to crossing over between chromatids. Reference    NCERT Page 83','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-083','12-067',NULL),
(271732,'biology','Given below are two statements : \nStatement I : \nSickle cell anaemia and Haemophilia are autosomal dominant traits . \nStatement II : \nSickle cell anaemia and Haemophilia are disorders of the blood. \nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ RE NEET 2022]','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','Haemophilia is a sex linked recessive disease. It is caused due to the presence of a mutant allele ‘h’ on the X chromosome in both males and females. For its expression in females, 2 identical recessive alleles have to be present on both the X chromosomes. In males, since there is only one X chromosome (the other being Y), presence of a single recessive allele is enough for the expression of the trait. Hence, it is a recessive trait.\nSickle cell anaemia is caused due to mutation in the amino acid sequence of the beta globin chain of haemoglobin. The gene encoding the beta globin chain is located on chromosome number 11','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271733,'biology','A heterozygous pea plant with violet flowers was crossed with a homozygous pea plant with white flowers. \nViolet is dominant over white. Which one of the following represents the expected combinations among 40 progenies formed?','(4) 20 produced violet and 20 produced white flowers','Genotype of heterozygous violet flower is Vv\nGenotype of homozygous white flower is vv\nBy Puneet square,\nBoth white and violet flowers will be produced in equal proportion (20 : 20)\nOut of 40 progenies\nThe expected ratio of violet and white flower plant is 1 : 1.','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) All 40 produced violet flowers','(2) All 40 produced white flowers','(3) 30 produced violet and 10 produced white flowers','(4) 20 produced violet and 20 produced white flowers','NEW','12-078','12-062',NULL),
(271734,'biology','Select the correct statement/s with respect to mechanism of sex determination in Grasshopper. \n (A) It is an example of female heterogamety. \n (B) Male produces two different types of gametes \neither with or without X chromosome. \n (C) Total number of chromosomes (autosomes and \nsex chromosomes) is same in both males and \nfemales. \n (D) All eggs bear an additional X chromosome \nbesides the autosomes. \n Choose the correct answer from the options given \nbelow : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(B) and (D) only','In organisms like crickets, grasshoppers, and some other insects, the female is XX and is the homogametic sex. The male is the heterogametic sex but only has one sex chromosome. The male in XX-XO systems produces gametes with (X) or without (O) a sex chromosome.','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(B) and (D) only','(A), (C) and (D) only','(A) only','(A) and (C) only','NEW','12-083','12-067',NULL),
(271735,'biology','Select the correct statements about sickle cell \nanaemia. \n (A) There is a change in gene for beta globin. \n (B) In the beta globin, there is valine in the place of Lysine. \n (C) It is an example of point mutation. \n (D) In the normal gene U is replaced by A. \n Choose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(4) (A) and (C) only','Sickle cell anemia is a genetic disorder characterized by abnormal hemoglobin, specifically a mutation in the gene for beta globin. The statements (A) and (C) correctly describe sickle cell anemia:\n\n(A) There is a change in the gene for beta globin: This statement is true. Sickle cell anemia is caused by a mutation in the gene that codes for beta globin, one of the subunits of hemoglobin.\n\n(C) It is an example of a point mutation: This statement is also true. Sickle cell anemia is caused by a single nucleotide substitution in the DNA sequence of the beta globin gene, resulting in a change from the amino acid glutamic acid to valine at a specific position in the protein.','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(4) (A) and (C) only','(1) (B), (C) and (D) only','(2) (B) and (D) only','(3) (A), (B) and (D) only','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271736,'biology','A certain plant homozygous for yellow seeds and red flowers was crossed with a plant homozygous for green seeds and white flowers. The F1\n plants had yellow seeds and pink flowers. The F1 plants were selfed to get F2 progeny. Assuming independent assortment of the two characters, how many phenotypic categories are expected for these \ncharacters in the F2\n generation ? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(4) 6','6','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) 9','(2) 4','(3) 16','(4) 6','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(271737,'biology','If A and C make 30% and 20% of DNA, respectively, what will be the percentage composition of T and G ?','(2) T : 30%, G : 20%','T : 30%, G : 20%,    Chargaff\'s rules state that DNA from any cell of all organisms should have a 1:1 ratio (base Pair Rule) of pyrimidine and purine bases and, more specifically, that the amount of guanine is equal to cytosine and the amount of adenine is equal to thymine.','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) T : 20%, G : 30%','(2) T : 30%, G : 20%','(3) T : 30%, G : 30%','(4) T : 20%, G : 20%','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(271738,'biology','A normal girl, whose mother is haemophilic marries a male with no ancestral history of haemophilia. What will be the possible phenotypes of the \noffsprings ? \n (a) Haemophilic son and haemophilic daughter. \n (b) Haemophilic son and carrier daughter. \n (c) Normal daughter and normal son. \n (d) Normal son and haemophilic daughter. \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the \noptions given below : [ RE NEET 2022]','(2) (b) and (c) only','A carrier woman when married to a normal male, can give birth to normal son and daughter, and carrier daughter and hemophilic son as she passes one of the X chromosome having the recessive trait.','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (a) and (b) only','(2) (b) and (c) only','(3) (a) and (d) only','(4) (b) and (d) only','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(271739,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \nList-I\n(Type of cross) \nList-II\n(Phenotypic ratio)\n(A) Monohybrid Cross (I) 1 : 1\n(B) Dihybrid Cross (II) 1 : 2 : 1\n(C) Incomplete\ndominance\n(III) 3 : 1\n(D) Test Cross (IV) 9 : 3 : 3 : 1\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(1) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','(2) (A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)','(3) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','(4) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','NEW','12-079','12-063',NULL),
