(271789,'biology','Genetic variation in a population arises due to:','Mutations as well as recombination','Genetic variation in a population can arise from both mutations and recombination. Mutations are changes in the DNA sequence, while recombination is the shuffling of existing genetic material during sexual reproduction. These processes can introduce new alleles into a population, leading to genetic variation. Reproductive isolation and selection can also contribute to genetic variation, but they do not directly generate new genetic material.','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Recombination only','Mutations as well as recombination','Reproductive isolation and selection','Mutations only','NEW','12-083','12-067',NULL),
