(271801,'biology','Assertion: Aneuploidy may be of hypoploidy or hyperploidy type. \n\n Reason: Monosomy lacks one pair of chromosomes','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Aneuploidy can be either due to loss of one or more chromosomes (hypoploidy) or due to addition of one or more chromosomes to complete chromosome complement (hyperploidy). Hypopliody is mainly due to loss of a single chromosomes, monosomes (2n – 1) or due to loss of onepair of chromosomes, nullisomes (2n – 2) Reference    NCERT Page 91','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(271802,'biology','Assertion : Cross of F1 individual with recessive homozygous parent is test cross.\n\n Reason : No recessive individual are obtained in the monohybrid test cross','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','In the monohybrid test-cross both dominant and recessive traits are obtained in 1 : 1 ratio Reference    NCERT Page 75','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-075','12-059',NULL),
(271803,'biology','Assertion : In Mirabilis, selfing of F1 pink flower plants produces same phenotypic  and  genotypic ratio. \n\n Reason : Flower colour gene shows incomplete dominance','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','F2 phenotypic and genotypic ratio in monohybrid cross involving incomplete dominance is 1 : 2 : 1 Reference    NCERT Page 74','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-074','12-058',NULL),
(271804,'biology','Assertion : In humans, the gamete contributed by the male determines whether the child produced will be male or female. \n\n Reason : Sex in humans is a polygenic trait depending upon a cumulative effect of some genes on X-chromosome and some on Y-chromosome','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','In human, the gamete contributed by the male determines whether the child produced will be male or female. Sex in humans is a polygenic trait depending upon cumulative effect of some genes present on Y-chromosome. Only sex in human is amonogenic trait. Reference    NCERT Page 87','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-087','12-071',NULL),
(271805,'biology','"Consider the following statements regarding Introduction to Inheritance and Variation, choose the correct statements \n\n1) Inheritance is the basis of heredity \n\n2) Inheritance is the process of passing RNA from parents to offspring \n\n3) Variation is a branch in Biology \n\n4) Genetics deals with inheritance and variation"','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Inheritance is the process by which characters are passed on from parent to progeny; it is the basis of heredity. Inheritance is the process by which characters are passed on from parent to progeny. Variation is the degree by which progeny differ from their parents. It is under Genetics which is a branch in Biology. In a branch of biology known as Genetics, it deals with the inheritance, as well as the variation of characters from parents to offspring\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology, Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 69 ,para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-069','12-053',NULL),
(271806,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason, choose the correct answer,\n \n Assertion: We can look alike or different from our siblings\n \n Reason: Variation occurs between us and our siblings','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Sometimes we look so similar to our siblings and sometimes so different because variation of characters from parents to offspring.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology. Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 69, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-069','12-053',NULL),
(271807,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Introduction to Inheritance and Variation, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Variation occurs in both asexual reproduction and sexual reproduction\n \n 2) Selective breeding is to choose organisms with desirable traits\n \n 3) Sahiwal cows are an example of selective breeding\n \n 4) Variations do not occur in wild population','Only 2 and 3 are correct','One of the causes of variation was hidden in sexual reproduction.  Asexual reproduction will not produce variation in offspring. Humans selectively breed and select for organisms that possessed desirable characters For example, through artificial selection and domestication from ancestral wild cows, we have well-known Indian breeds, e.g., Sahiwal cows in Punjab. Humans knew from as early as 8000-1000 B.C.exploited the variations that were naturally present in the wild populations of plants and animals. \n (Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 69 ,para 4 and pg 70 ,para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-069','12-053',NULL),
(311623,'biology','Which of the following is not a step in Multiple Ovulation Embryo Transfer Technology (MOET)?(NEET 2021)','Cow is administered hormone having LH like activity for super ovulation','Superovulation (SOV) is a necessary technique to produce large numbers of embryos for embryo transfer. In the conventional methods, follicular stimulating hormone (FSH) is administered to donor cattle twice daily for 3 to 4 days','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fertilized eggs are transferred to surrogate mothers at 8-32 cell stage','Cow is administered hormone having LH like activity for super ovulation','Cow yields about 6-8 eggs at a time','Cow is fertilized by artifical insemination','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(271808,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: We can produce organisms with desired characteristics.\n \n Reason: It is due to cloning of organisms.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','Organism with desired characteristics can be produced by using selective breeding and artificial selection\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5 ,pg 69, para 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-069','12-053',NULL),
(271901,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Mendel\'s Laws of Inheritance,choose the correct statements\n \n 1) George Mendel came up with the Mendel\'s Laws of Inheritance.\n \n 2) Mendel used long beans to carry out hybridization.\n \n 3) Mendel used a large sampling size for his experiment.\n \n 4) Mendel investigated characters in his samples that were manifested as two opposing traits.','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Gregor Mendel, conducted hybridisation experiments on garden peas for and proposed the laws of inheritance in living organisms. He used garden peas not long beans when conducting hybridization His experiments had a large sampling size, which gave greater credibility to the data that he collected. Mendel investigated characters in the garden pea plant that were manifested as two opposing traits, e.g., tall or dwarf plants, yellow or green seeds\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 70, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-070','12-054',NULL),
(271902,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: The large sampling size used by Mendel plays an important role\n \n Reason: It allows Mendel to set up a basic framework of rules governing inheritance','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','His experiments had a large sampling size, which gave greater credibility to the data that he collected. Mendel investigated characters in the garden pea plant that were manifested as two opposing traits. This allowed him to set up a basic framework of rules governing inheritance.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology. Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 70, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-070','12-054',NULL),
(271903,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Mendel\'s Laws of Inheritance, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) A true-breeding line will show stable trait inheritance for more than one generation\n \n 2) Mendel conducted his experiments using a several pea plants of true-breeding line\n \n 3) An example of contrasting traits selected is colour of seeds and seed shape\n \n 4) A true-breeding line has undergone cross-pollination','Only 1 and 2 are correct','A truebreeding line is one that, having undergone continuous self-pollination, shows the stable trait inheritance and expression for several generations. Mendel conducted such artificial pollination/cross pollination experiments using several true-breeding pea lines. Some of the contrasting traits selected were smooth or wrinkled seeds, yellow or green seeds, inflated (full) or constricted green or yellow pods and tall or dwarf plants. A truebreeding line is one that, having undergone continuous self-pollination\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 70, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-070','12-054',NULL),
(271904,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Mendel conducted cross pollination experiments using several true-breeding pea lines\n \n Reason: A truebreeding line will show stable trait inheritance in the next generation only','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','Mendel conducted such artificial pollination/cross pollination experiments using several true-breeding pea lines. A truebreeding line is one that, having undergone continuous self-pollination, shows the stable trait inheritance and expression for several generations.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 70, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-070','12-054',NULL),
(271905,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Inheritance of One Gene, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) F1 progeny stands for Filial1 Progeny\n \n 2) Mendel crossed only two tall pea plants to study inheritance of one gene\n \n 3) F1 progeny will show resemblance to either one of the parents\n \n 4) If the F1 progeny plants are tall, it is impossible to find a dwarf plant among them','Only 1, 3 and 4 are correct','Mendel grew them to generate plants of the first hybrid generation. This generation is also called the Filial1 progeny or the F1. Mendel crossed tall and dwarf pea plants to study the inheritance of one gene. F1 always resembled either one of the parents, and that the trait of the other parent was not seen in them. Mendel observed that all the F1 progeny plants were tall, like one of its parents; none were dwarf\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12,Chapter 5,pg 71, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1, 3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-071','12-055',NULL),
(271906,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Mendel observed that all the F1 progeny plants show trait of both parents \n \n Reason: He carried out pollination before emasculation of the pea plant','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Mendel found that the F1 always resembled either one of the parents, and that the trait of the other parent was not seen in them. Emasculation is the removal of anther and should be carried out before pollination.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5 ,pg 71 ,para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-071','12-055',NULL),
(271907,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Inheritance of One Gene, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Mendel cross-pollinated the F1 progeny to obtain F2 progeny\n \n 2) Characters not seen in F1 generation can be seen in F2 generation\n \n 3) Mendel did not observe blending of both parental traits in the F2 progeny\n \n 4) The proportion of dwarf plants and tall plants in F2 generation observed by Mendel is 1/2 and 1/2.','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Mendel then self-pollinated the tall F1 plants The character that was not seen in the F1 generation was now expressed. The tall and dwarf traits were identical to their parental type and did not show any blending. The proportion of plants that were dwarf were 1/4th of the F2 plants while 3/4th of the F2 plants were tall\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 71, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-071','12-055',NULL),
(271908,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: The character that was not seen in the F1 generation was not expressed in F2 generation.\n \n Reason: Mendel cross-pollinated the F1 generation again','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Mendel then self-pollinated the tall F1 plants and to his surprise found that in the Filial2 generation some of the offspring were ‘dwarf’; the character that was not seen in the F1 generation was now expressed.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 71, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-071','12-055',NULL),
(271909,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Inheritance of One Gene, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Genes are passed down to offspring unchanged.\n \n 2) Allele is the unit of inheritance.\n \n 3) Genes are passed down by somatic cells.\n \n 4) Alleles are genes which code for a pair of contrasting traits .','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Mendel proposed that something was being stably passed down, unchanged, from parent to offspring through the gametes, over successive generations, now it is known as "Genes". Genes, therefore, are the units of inheritance. Genes are inherited from parent to offspring through the gametes (germ cells). Genes which code for a pair of contrasting traits are known as alleles, i.e., they are slightly different forms of the same gene.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 72 ,para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-072','12-056',NULL),
(271910,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Genes are the units of inheritance\n \n Reason: They contain the information that is required to express a particular trait in an organism.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Mendel proposed that something was being stably passed down, unchanged, from parent to offspring through the gametes, over successive generations. He called these things as ‘factors’. Now we call them as genes. Genes, therefore, are the units of inheritance. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 72, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-072','12-056',NULL),
(271911,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Inheritance of One Gene, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Usually, we use numerical symbols for genes\n \n 2) Mendel proposed that the allelic pair of genes in a true breeding is homozygous\n \n 3) Tall and dwarf are examples of phenotype\n \n 4) If T is used for the Tall trait and t for the ‘dwarf’,T and t are alleles of each other.','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','We use alphebetical symbols to represent genes. Mendel also proposed that in a true breeding, tall or dwarf pea variety the allelic pair of genes for height are identical or homozygous, TT and tt, respectively. TT and tt are called the genotype of the plant while the descriptive terms tall and dwarf are the phenotype. Capital letter is used for the trait expressed at the F1 stage and the small alphabet for the other trait. For example, in case of the character of height, T is used for the Tal trait and t for the ‘dwarf’, and T and t are alleles of each other.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 72, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-072','12-056',NULL),
(271912,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: When being asked about a genotype, we have to describe its physical characteristic\n \n Reason: Genotype means the descriptive terms of the traits','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','TT and tt are called the genotype of the plant while the descriptive terms tall and dwarf are the phenotype.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology,Class 12,.Chapter 5, pg 72, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-072','12-056',NULL),
(271913,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Inheritance of One Gene, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Tt is heterozygous\n \n 2) TT is heterozygous\n \n 3) The capital alphabet in genotype stands for recessive allele\n \n 4) Tt genotype will show similar phenotype as TT genotype','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Tt is heterozygous. TT is homozygous. The capital alphabet in genotype is for dominant allele. Mendel found the phenotype of the F1 heterozygote Tt to be exactly like the TT parent in appearance\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 72 ,para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-072','12-056',NULL),
(311624,'biology','Which of the following is not an objective of Biofortification in crops?(NEET 2021)','Improve resistance to diseases','Biofortication is used to improve the macronutrient (proteins and vitamins) and micro nutrient content','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Improve micronutrient and mineral content','Improve protein content','Improve resistance to diseases','Improve vitamin content','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(271914,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: If T is dominant for height, t is recessive for height, a plant with heterozygous genotype will be tall\n \n Reason: It will always follow the TT parent in appearance','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','As Mendel found the phenotype of the F1 heterozygote Tt to be exactly like the TT parent in appearance. In this case, heterozygous means Tt so it will be tall too \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 72, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-072','12-056',NULL),
(271915,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Inheritance of One Gene, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) We can use two different alphabets to show dominant and recessive allele of a same trait\n \n 2) Alleles can be similar in homozygotes\n \n 3) The cross between TT and tt is a monohybrid cross\n \n 4) The Tt plant is heterozygous for genes controlling two traits','Only 2 and 3 are correct','It is convenient (and logical) to use the capital and lower case of a same alphabetical symbol to remember this concept of dominance and recessiveness. Alleles can be similar as in the case of homozygotes TT and tt The cross between TT and tt is a monohybrid cross. Tt plant is heterozygous for genes controlling one character (height)\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12,Chapter 5 ,pg 72 ,para 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-072','12-056',NULL),
(271916,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Tt plant is a monohybrid cross\n \n Reason: It is homozygous for genes controlling one charateristic.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Tt plant is heterozygous for genes controlling one character (height), it is a monohybrid and the cross between TT and tt is a monohybrid cross.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 72, para 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-072','12-056',NULL),
(271917,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Inheritance of One Gene, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Segregation of allele is a random process\n \n 2) During meiosis, both alleles will be transferred to a gamete\n \n 3) In F2 generation, recessive parental trait is shown without traces of blending \n \n 4) Alleles of parental pair will separate in meiosis','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Segregation of alleles is a random process and so there is a 50 % chance of a gamete containing either allele, as has been verified by the results of the crossings. By the process of meiosis, the alleles of the parental pair separate or segregate from each other and only one allele is transmitted to a gamete. From the observation that the recessive parental trait is expressed without any blending in the F2 generation. In the process of meiosis, the alleles of the parental pair separate or segregate from each other\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 73, para 2 )','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(271918,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Inheritance of One Gene, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Hybrids that contain alleles which express contrasting traits are heterozygous\n \n 2) Production of gametes by the parents, the formation of the zygotes, the F1 and F2 can be understood using Punnett Square\n \n 3) Punnett Square is a graphical representation to calculate the probability of all possible phenotype of offspring in a phenotypic cross\n \n 4) There is a 50 % chance of a gamete containing both alleles','Only 1 and 2 are correct','These hybrids contain alleles which express contrasting traits, they are heterozygous.  The production of gametes by the parents, the formation of the zygotes, the F1 and F2 plants can be understood from a diagram called Punnett Square It is a graphical representation to calculate the probability of all possible genotypes of offspring in a genetic cross. Segregation of alleles is a random process and so there is a 50 % chance of a gamete containing either allele, as has been verified by the results of the crossings. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 73, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(271919,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Hybrids are heterozygous\n \n Reason: Segregation of alleles is a random process','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Since these hybrids contain alleles which express contrasting traits, the plants are heterozygous\nSegregation of alleles is a random process and so there is a 50 % chance of a gamete containing either allele\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 73, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(271920,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion:There can only be one allele transferred to one gamete\n \n Reason: Parental pair segregate from each other in mitosis','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','By the process of meiosis, the alleles of the parental pair separate or segregate from each other and only one allele is transmitted to a gamete.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 73 ,para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(2000107316,'physics','The angular speed of a particle moving in a circular path is doubled. Then, the centripetal acceleration of the particle is','A','Let the angular speed of particle $=\\omega$<br>As. $a^{\\prime}=\\omega^{\\prime 2} r=(2 \\omega)^{2} r\\left[\\omega^{\\prime}=2 \\omega\\right]$<br>$=4 \\omega^{2} r=4 a$<br>So, the correct option is (1).','	Kinematics of Circular Motion	','Motion in a Plane','Medium',NULL,'4 times the initial centripetal acceleration','halved','doubled','unchanged',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(271921,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: When fertilisation of F1 plant of the genotype Tt takes place, the pollen grains of genotype T have a 50 % chance to pollinate eggs of the genotype T, as well as of genotype t\n \n Reason: Gametes of the genotype T and t in equal proportion are produced','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The F1 plant of the genotype Tt when self-pollinated, produces gametes of the genotype T and t in equal proportion. When fertilisation takes place, the pollen grains of genotype T have a 50 % chance to pollinate eggs of the genotype T, as well as of genotype t. Also pollen grains of genotype t have a 50 % chance of pollinating eggs of genotype T, as well as of genotype t\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 73 ,para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(271922,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Inheritance of One Gene, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) The F1 plant of the heterozygous genotype when self-pollinated, produces gametes of the genotype T and t in equal proportion\n \n 2) As a result of random fertilisation, the resultant zygotes can be homozygous or heterozygous\n \n 3) The F1 plants of genotype Tt are self-pollinated\n \n 4) Pollens of genotype T have a 50 % chance to pollinate eggs of the genotype T, as well as of genotype t and vice versa','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','The F1 plant of the genotype Tt when self-pollinated, produces gametes of the genotype T and t in equal proportion\nAs a result of random fertilisation, the resultant zygotes can be of the genotypes TT, Tt or tt.\nThe Punnett Square shows the parental tall TT (male) and dwarf tt (female) plants, the gametes produced by them and, the F1 Tt progeny. The F1 plants of genotype Tt are self-pollinated\nWhen fertilisation takes place, the pollen grains of genotype T have a 50 % chance to pollinate eggs of the genotype T, as well as of genotype t  Also pollen grains of genotype t have a 50 % chance of pollinating eggs of genotype T as well as of genetoype t\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 73 ,para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(271923,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: The resultant zygotes can be of the genotypes TT, Tt or tt as a result from self-pollination of F1 Tt progeny\n \n Reason: This is due to random fertilisation','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','When fertilisation takes place, the pollen grains of genotype T have a 50 % chance to pollinate eggs of the genotype T, as well as of genotype t. Also pollen grains of genotype t have a 50 % chance of pollinating eggs of genotype T, as well as of genotype t. As a result of random fertilisation, the resultant zygotes can be of the genotypes TT, Tt or tt.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 73 ,para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-073','12-057',NULL),
(271924,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Inheritance of One Gene, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) With the genotype Tt, the phenotype observed is short\n \n 2) We can distinguish plants with TT genotype and Tt genotype by observing their physical characteristics\n \n 3) The genotypic ratio obtained by Mendel using Punnett Square is TT:Tt:tt = 1/4:1/2:1/4\n \n 4) Within genotypic Tt pair, it is said that T dominates over t','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Though the plants have a genotype of Tt, but the phenotypic character seen is ‘tall’\nExternally it is not possible to distinguish between the plants with the genotypes TT and Tt.\nFrom the Punnett square it is easily seen that 1/4th of the random fertilisations lead to TT, 1/2 lead to Tt and 1/4th to tt\nWithin the genopytic pair Tt only one character ‘T ’ tall is expressed. Hence the character T or ‘tall’ is said to dominate over the other allele t or ‘dwarf’ character\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5 ,pg 74, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-074','12-058',NULL),
(271925,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason, choose the correct answer,\n \n Assertion: Within the genopytic pair Tt, blending occurs as both T and t alleles are expressed\n \n Reason: T and t alleles are co-dominance','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Within the genopytic pair Tt only one character ‘T ’ tall is expressed. This is because the character T or ‘tall’ is said to dominate over the other allele t or ‘dwarf’ character\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology. Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 74 ,para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-074','12-058',NULL),
(271926,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Inheritance of One Gene, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Mendel self-pollinated the F2 plants and found that dwarf F2 plants were produced in the following generations\n \n 2) Mendel concluded that dwarf plants can be Tt or tt\n \n 3) In test cross, an organism with dominant genotype is crossed with another recessive parent\n \n 4) By just observing physical dominant traits, we cannot determine an organism\'s genotype','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Mendel self-pollinated the F2 plants and found that dwarf F2 plants continued to generate dwarf plants in F3 and F4 generations.\nHe concluded that the genotype of the dwarfs was homozygous – tt\nIn a typical test cross an organism showing a dominant phenotype (and whose genotype is to be determined) is crossed with the recessive parent instead of self-crossing.\nBy simply looking at the phenotype of a dominant trait, it is not possible to know the genotypic composition.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5 ,pg 74 ,para 4, and 5 )','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-074','12-058',NULL),
(271927,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: To determine the genotype of a dominant trait, we can use test cross\n \n Reason: Test cross involves crossing with a recessive parent','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','In a typical test cross an organism showing a dominant phenotype (and whose genotype is to be determined) is crossed with the recessive parent instead of self-crossing. The progenies of such a cross can easily be analysed to predict the genotype of the test organism.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 74 ,para 5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-074','12-058',NULL),
(271928,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Law of Dominance, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Law of Dominance is the first rule in Law of Inheritance\n \n 2) Law of Dominance is the second law in Law of Inheritance\n \n 3) Law of dominance explains the proportion of 3:1 in F1 \n \n 4) In a heterozygous pair, one allele is dominant while the other is recessive','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Today these rules are called the Principles or Laws of Inheritance: the First Law or Law of Dominance and the Second Law or Law of Segregation\nThe law of dominance also explains the proportion of 3:1 obtained at the F2\nIn a dissimilar pair of factors one member of the pair dominates (dominant) the other (recessive).\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 75 ,para 1 and 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-075','12-059',NULL),
(271929,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Law of Dominance explains proportion of Tall: Short= 3:1 obtained at the F2\n \n Reason: Law Of Dominance states that characters are controlled by discrete units called factors.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The law of dominance is used to explain the expression of only one of the parental characters in a monohybrid cross in the F1 and the expression of both in the F2.It also explains the proportion of 3:1 obtained at the F2\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5 ,pg 75 ,para 2 )','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-075','12-059',NULL),
(271930,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Law of Segregation, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) The second law in Law of Segregation is Law of Dominance\n \n 2) Law of Segregation is the second law in Law of Inheritance\n \n 3) Basis of Law of Segregation is no blending in alleles and the characteristics not shown in F1 generation are seen in F2 generation\n \n 4) The parents contain one allele only during gamete formation','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Today these rules are called the Principles or Laws of Inheritance: the First Law or Law of Dominance and the Second Law or Law of Segregation.\nLaw of Segregation is based on the fact that the alleles do not show any blending and that both the characters are recovered as such in the F2 generation though one of these is not seen at the F1 stage\nThe parents contain two alleles during gamete formation\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 75, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-075','12-059',NULL),
(271931,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: A gamete receives only one of the two alleles\n \n Reason: The parents contain one allele during gamete formation','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','Though the parents contain two alleles during gamete formation, the factors or alleles of a pair segregate from each other such that a gamete receives only one of the two factors\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 75, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-075','12-059',NULL),
(271932,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Incomplete Dominance, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Incomplete dominance shows phenotype that does not resemble both parents\n \n 2) Flower colour of plants is an example that can show incomplete dominance\n \n 3) In incomplete dominance, the genotypic ratio follows the mendelian monohybrid cross\n \n 4) Mendel observed incomplete dominance on pea plants','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Incomplete dominance has shown phenotype that did not resemble either of the two parents and was in between the two.\nThe inheritance of flower colour in the dog flower (snapdragon or Antirrhinum sp.) is a good example to understand incomplete dominance\nThe genotype ratios were exactly as we would expect in any mendelian monohybrid cross, but the phenotype ratios had changed from the 3:1 dominant : recessive ratio\nWhen experiments on peas were repeated using other traits in other plants, incomplete dominance is discovered\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 76, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-076','12-060',NULL),
(271933,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: It is possible to obtain a pink flower as offspring from red and white flowers as parents\n \n Reason: Incomplete dominance takes place.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','In a cross between true-breeding red-flowered (RR) and truebreeding white-flowered plants (rr), the F1 (Rr) was pink. What happened was that R was not completely dominant over r and this made it possible to distinguish Rr as pink from RR (red) and rr (white) . This phenomenon is known as Incomplete Dominance\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 76, para 1 )','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-076','12-060',NULL),
(271934,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Dominance, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) In a diploid organism, there is a pair of alleles\n \n 2) Gene contains information to express a particular characteristic\n \n 3) In homozygote, the two alleles can be different \n \n 4) Information on allele cannot be modified','Only 1 and 2 are correct','In a diploid organism, there are two copies of each gene, i.e., as a pair of alleles.\nEvery gene contains the information to express a particular trait\nThe two alleles need not always be identical, as in a heterozygote. In homozygote two alleles are same. \nOne of the two alleles may be different due to some changes that it has undergone which modifies the information that particular allele contains.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 76 ,para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-076','12-060',NULL),
(271935,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: In a heterozygote, the two alleles is not necessary to be identical at all times\n \n Reason: Incomplete dominance can occur in heterozygote','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The two alleles need not always be identical, as in a heterozygote becauase one of the two alleles may be different due to some changes that it has undergone which modifies the information that particular allele contains\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 76, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-076','12-060',NULL),
(271936,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Codominance, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) In co-dominance, F1 generation resembles both parents\n \n 2) ABO blood groups are controlled by gene I\n \n 3) Allele i does not produce any form of sugar\n \n 4) There are six possible genotypes of human ABO blood types','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','In the case of co-dominance the F1 generation resembles both parents\nABO blood groups are controlled by the gene I.\nThe gene (I) has three alleles IA, IB and i. The alleles IA and IB producea slightly different form of the sugar while allele i does not produce any sugar\nSince there are three different alleles, thereare six different combinations of these three alleles that are possible, andtherefore, a total of six different genotypes of the human ABO blood types is available\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 77, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-077','12-061',NULL),
(271937,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: When IA and i are present only IA expresses , and when IB and i are present IB expresses\n \n Reason: i does not produce any sugar','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','IA and IB are completely dominant over i, in other words when IA and i are present only IA expresses (because i does not produce any sugar), and when IB and i are present IB expresses.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 77, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-077','12-061',NULL),
(271938,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Codominance, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Genotype ii will produce blood type i \n \n 2) The surface of plasma membrane of the red blood cells has sugar polymers \n \n 3) The gene (I) has three alleles\n \n 4) IA and IB are completely dominant over i','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','Genotype ii codes for the blood type O (refer NCERT Biology Chapter 5 pg 77 Table 5.2)\nThe plasma membrane of the red blood cells has sugar polymers that protrude from its surface and the kind of sugar is controlled by the gene.\nThe gene (I) has three alleles IA, IB and i\nIA and IB are completely dominant over i, in other words when IA and i are present only IA expresses (because i does not produce any sugar), and when IB and i are present IB expresses.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 77, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-077','12-061',NULL),
(271939,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Each human possesses two out of three I gene alleles\n \n Reason: Humans are heterozygotes','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','Because humans are diploid organisms, each person possesses any two of the three I gene alleles\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 77, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-077','12-061',NULL),
(271940,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Codominance, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) A single gene may only cause a single effect\n \n 2) Multiple alleles can be discovered via population studies\n \n 3) ABO blood grouping has shown multiple alleles\n \n 4) Dominance is an autonomous feature of a gene','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Occasionally, a single gene product may produce more than one effect.\nSince in an individual only two alleles can be present, multiple alleles can be found only when population studies are made.\nABO blood grouping also provides a good example of multiple alleles? Here you can see that there are more than two, i.e., three alleles, governing the same character.\nDominance is not an autonomous feature of a gene or the product that it has information for. It depends as much on the gene product and the production of a particular phenotype from this product\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 78, para 1,2 and 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-078','12-062',NULL),
(2000110026,'physics','If a gas has $n$ degrees of freedom, then the ratio of $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}$ is','A','For a gas with $n$ degree of freedom,<br>$C_{P}=\\left(\\dfrac{n}{2}+1\\right) \\cdot R \\quad$ and $C_{V}=\\dfrac{n}{2} R$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{n}{2}+1\\right) R}{\\dfrac{n}{2} \\cdot R}=\\left(\\dfrac{n+2}{n}\\right)$','Specific Heat Capacity','Kinetic Theory Of Gases','Easy',NULL,'$\\dfrac{n+2}{n}$','$\\dfrac{2 n+1}{n}$','$\\dfrac{n+2}{2 n}$','$\\dfrac{n+4}{2 n}$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(271941,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: ABO blood grouping is an example of multiple alleles\n \n Reason: The alleles show more than one effect.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','ABO blood grouping also provides a good example of multiple alleles? Here you can see that there are more than two, i.e., three alleles, governing the same character.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 78, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-078','12-062',NULL),
(271942,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: When IA and IB are present together, the person will have blood type AB\n \n Reason: Co-dominance takes place','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','when IA and IB are present together they both express their own types of sugars: this is because of co-dominance. Hence red blood cells have both A and B types of sugars. The person will be categorized under blood group AB\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 78, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-078','12-062',NULL),
(271943,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Law of Independent Assortment, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) The ratio obtained by Mendel in dihybrid cross is 9:3:3:1\n \n 2) There are 4 phenotypes obtained by Mendel in monohybrid cross\n \n 3) Law of Independent Assortment is based on dihybrid cross\n \n 4) Most of the pea plants show a phenotype of round and yellow seeds in Mendel\'s dihybrid cross','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','In the dihybrid cross, the phenotypes round, yellow; wrinkled, yellow; round, green and wrinkled, green appeared in the ratio 9:3:3:1.\nThere are 4 phenotypes obtained by Mendel in dihybrid cross. In monohybrid cross, only two types of phenotype will be seen\nBased upon such observations on dihybrid crosses (crosses between plants differing in two traits) Mendel proposed a second set of generalisations that we call Mendel’s Law of Independent Assortment\nIn the dihybrid cross, the phenotypes round, yellow; wrinkled, yellow; round, green and wrinkled, green appeared in the ratio 9:3:3:1. Therefore, majority shows the dominant traits which are round and yellow seeds\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 80 ,para 1,2 and 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-080','12-064',NULL),
(271944,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: In dihybrid cross, we can see four different phenotypes\n \n Reason: Segregation of the two pairs of traits are dependent to each other','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','Based upon such observations on dihybrid crosses (crosses between plants differing in two traits) Mendel proposed a second set of generalisations that we call Mendel’s Law of Independent Assortment. The law states that ‘when two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid, segregation of one pair of characters is independent of the other pair of characters’\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 80, para 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-080','12-064',NULL),
(271945,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Law of Independent Assortment, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) From a RrYy plant, two types of gametes can be formed\n \n 2) Punnett Square cannot be used for dihybrid cross\n \n 3) Each type of gametes produced from a RrYy plant accounts for 25 percent of total gametes produced\n \n 4) The two pair of genes studied will segregate independently during meiosis','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Fifty percent of the gametes have the gene R and the other 50 percent have r. Now besides each gamete having either R or r, it should also have the allele Y or y. 50 percent of the r bearing gametes has Y and the other 50 % has y. Similarly, 50 percent of the R bearing gametes has Y and the other 50 percent has y. Thus there are four genotypes of gametes. The four types are RY, Ry, rY and ry\nThe Punnett square can be effectively used to understand the independent segregation of the two pairs of genes during meiosis and the production of eggs and pollen in the F1 RrYy plant.\nThe four types are RY, Ry, rY and ry each with a frequency of 25 percent or 1/4th of the total gametes produced. \nIndependent segregation of the two pairs of genes occurs during meiosis\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 80 ,para 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-080','12-064',NULL),
(271946,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: The four types of gametes produced by a RrYy plant has 1/4th of the total gametes produced\n \n Reason: It is due to independent segregation of the Rr and Yy genes','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Consider the segregation of one pair of genes R and r. Fifty % of the gametes have the gene R and the other 50 % have r. Now besides each gamete having either R or r, it should also have the allele Y or y. The important thing to remember here is that segregation of 50 % R and 50 % r is independent from the segregation of 50 % Y and 50 % y. Therefore, 50 % of the r bearing gametes has Y and the other 50 % has y. Similarly, 50 % of the R bearing gametes has Y and the other 50 % has y. Thus there are four genotypes of gametes. The four types are RY, Ry, rY and ry each with a frequency of 25 % or 1/4th of the total gametes produced\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Chapter 5, pg 80, para 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-080','12-064',NULL),
(2000110099,'physics','A gas mixture contains monoatomic and diatomic molecules of 2 moles each. The mixture has a total internal energy of (symbols have usual meanings)','C','$U = {n_1}{C_{{v_1}}}T + {n_2}{C_{{v_2}}}T$<br>$U = (2)\\frac{3}{2}RT + (2)\\frac{5}{2}RT$<br>$U = 8RT$','Gaseous Mixture and Two Connected Chambers','Kinetic Theory Of Gases','Medium',NULL,'3 $RT$','5 $RT$','8 $RT$','9 $RT$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(271947,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chromosomal theory of Inheritance, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Chromosomes are also known as coloured bodies\n \n 2) Mendel suggested that genes control expression of traits\n \n 3) Cell division is observed due to advancement in microscopy\n \n 4) Chromosomes are in the cytoplasm','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','"Chromosome can also be termed as coloured bodies, they are visualised by staining\nHis concept of genes (or factors, in Mendel’s words) as stable and discrete units that controlled the expression of traits\nBy this time due to advancements in microscopy that were taking place, scientists were able to carefully observe cell division\nThis led to the discovery of structures in the nucleus that appeared to double and divide just before each cell division. These were called chromosomes\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 81, para 1 and 2)"','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-081','12-065',NULL),
(271948,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Mendel\'s concept of genes are stable and discrete units that control traits expression is accepted when he initially proposed \n \n Reason: He observed them under an advanced microscope and discovered chromosome','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','His concept of genes (or factors, in Mendel’s words) as stable and discrete units that controlled the expression of traits and, of the pair of alleles which did not ‘blend’ with each other, was not accepted by his contemporaries as an explanation for the apparently continuous variation seen in nature.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 81 ,para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-081','12-065',NULL),
(271949,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chromosomes, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Behaviour of chromosomes was anti-parallel to the behaviour of genes \n \n 2) Chromosomes occur in pairs\n \n 3) The two alleles of a gene pair are located on homologous sites on homologous chromosomes.\n \n 4) Chromosomes replicate in anaphase','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Walter Sutton and Theodore Boveri noted that the behaviour of chromosomes was parallel to the behaviour of genes\nChromosomes as well as genes occur in pairs. \nThe two alleles of a gene pair are located on homologous sites on homologous chromosomes.\nChromosomes replicate in S phase (refer NCERT Biology Chapter 5 pg 81 Figure 5.5)\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 81, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-081','12-065',NULL),
(271950,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Each germ cells has only either one copy of either one of the two chromosomes\n \n Reason: Chromosomes occur in pairs','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Chromosomes as well as genes occur in pairs. It is found that chromosomes double and divide just before each cell division so in each germ cell only one copy of either one of the two chromosomes will be obtained\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 81, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-081','12-065',NULL),
(271951,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chromosomes, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) During anaphase of meiosis I, chromosomes pair align at metaphase plate\n \n 2) Segregation of chromosomes pair can lead to more than one outcomes of gametes\n \n 3) Genes do not segregate during meiosis\n \n 4) Independent assortment occurs in both mitosis and meiosis','Only 1 and 2 are correct','During Anaphase of meiosis I, the two chromosome pairs can align at the metaphase plate independently of each other\nYes, segregation may lead to different possible combination of chromosomes in gametes (refer NCERT Biology Chapter 5 pg 82 Figure 5.9)\nGenes segregate in both mitosis and meiosis so that gametes have a copy of it\nIndependent assortment only occurs during meiosis, specifically meiosis I \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 82 ,para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-082','12-066',NULL),
(271952,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Variation occurs during sexual reproduction.\n \n Reason: Independent assortment takes place during Anaphase of meiosis I and II','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','There are more than 1 possible combination of chromosomes in gametes produced because during Anaphase of meiosis I, the two chromosome pairs can align at the metaphase plate independently of each other. This process is known as Independent Assortment\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 82 ,para 1 )','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-082','12-066',NULL),
(271953,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chromosomal theory of Inheritance, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Chromosomal theory of Inheritance is a combination of Mendelian principles and knowledge of chromosomal segregation\n \n 2) Gender of Drosophila melanogaster is difficult to be distinguished\n \n 3) Drosophila melanogaster has only a few heriditary variations\n \n 4) Thomas Hunt Morgan used fruit flies to discover variation in sexual reproduction','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Sutton united the knowledge of chromosomal segregation with Mendelian principles and called it the chromosomal theory of inheritance\nThere was a clear differentiation of the sexes – the male and female flies are easily distinguishable.\nIt has many types of hereditary variations that can be seen with low power microscopes.\nExperimental verification of the chromosomal theory of inheritance by Thomas Hunt Morgan and his colleagues, led to discovering the basis for the variation that sexual reproduction produced. Morgan worked with the tiny fruit flies, Drosophila melanogaster\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 83, para 1 and 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-083','12-067',NULL),
(271954,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Houseflies are used to discover the basis of variation caused by sexual reproduction\n \n Reason: Houseflies complete their life cycle in less than two weeks','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Morgan worked with the tiny fruit flies, Drosophila melanogaster. They could be grown on simple synthetic medium in the laboratory. They complete their life cycle in about two weeks, and a single mating could produce a large number of progeny flies.There was a clear differentiation of the sexes. It has many types of hereditary variations that can be seen with low power microscopes\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 83, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-083','12-067',NULL),
(271955,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Linkage and Recombination, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Linkage is the physical association of a chromosomal pair\n \n 2) Morgan observed that the F2 ratio progeny deviated from the typical 9:3:3:1 ratio\n \n 3) Recombination describes generation of non-parental gene combinations\n \n 4) When the two genes in a dihybrid cross were situated on the same chromosome, the proportion of parental gene combinations were much higher','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','Due to the physical association or linkage of the two genes and coined the term linkage to describe this physical association of genes on chromosome and the term recombination to describe the generation of non-parental gene combinations \nMorgan observed that the two genes did not segregate independently of each other and the F2 ratio deviated very significantly from the 9:3:3:1 ratio (expected when the two genes are independent).\nWhen the two genes in a dihybrid cross were situated on the same chromosome, the proportion of parental gene combinations were much higher than the non-parental type\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 83, para 3 and 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-083','12-067',NULL),
(271956,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: When the two genes in a dihybrid cross were situated on the same chromosome,the proportion of parental gene combinations were much higher than the non-parental type\n \n Reason: It is due to linkage','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','When the two genes in a dihybrid cross were situated on the same chromosome, the proportion of parental gene combinations were much higher than the non-parental type. Morgan attributed this due to the physical association or linkage of the two genes and coined the term linkage to describe this physical association of genes on a chromosome\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5 ,pg 83, para 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-083','12-067',NULL),
(272001,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Linkage and Recombination, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Tightly linked genes show low recombination possibility\n \n 2) Distance between genes can be calculated using the frequency of recombination between gene pairs on the same chromosome\n \n 3) If percentage of linkage between two genes is high, there is a high possibility that both of them will not be inherited together\n \n 4) 5 percent linkage is more likely to show lower recombination than 17 percent','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Morgan and his group also found that even when genes were grouped on the same chromosome, some genes were very tightly linked (showed very low recombination while others were loosely linked (showed higher recombination)\nAlfred Sturtevant used the frequency of recombination between gene pairs on the same chromosome as a measure of the distance between genes and ‘mapped’ their position on the chromosome.\nHigh percentage means higher linkage and lower recombination probability, so they will be most likely be inherited together\n5 percent linkage is lower than 10 percent so it has higher recombination possibility\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 83 ,para 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-083','12-067',NULL),
(272002,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Linkage and Recombination. Choose the correct option.\n \n 1) The frequency of recombination between gene pairs on the same chromosome is used to map their position\n \n 2) When the two genes in a dihybrid cross were situated on the same chromosome, the proportion of parental gene combinations were much lower\n \n 3) Morgan carried out several monohybrid crosses in Drosophila to study genes that were sex-linked\n \n 4) The two genes studied by Morgan in Drosophila did not segregate independently of each other','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Alfred Sturtevant used the frequency of recombination between gene pairs on the same chromosome as a measure of the distance between genes and ‘mapped’ their position on the chromosome\nWhen the two genes in a dihybrid cross were situated on the same chromosome, the proportion of parental gene combinations were much higher than the non-parental type\nMorgan carried out several dihybrid crosses in Drosophila to study genes that were sex-linked\nMorgan observed that the two genes did not segregate independently of each other and the F2 ratio deviated very significantly from the 9:3:3:1 ratio\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 83 ,para 4 and 5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-083','12-067',NULL),
(272003,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Genes A and B that have 2.5 percent recombination are more likely to be inherited together than Genes A and C that have 17 percent recombination. \n \n Reason: Genes A and B are more loosely linked compared to Genes A and C.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','Genes that are tightly linked will have a higher percentage of being inherited together as they are placed close to each other and therefore will have a lower recombination percentage\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 83, para 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-083','12-067',NULL),
(272004,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Polygenic Inheritance, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Polygenic traits are controlled by three or more genes\n \n 2) Human skin colour is an example of a polygenic trait\n \n 3) A polygenic trait will reflect the phenotype depending on the constitution of each allele\n \n 4) Environment will not affect multiple genes polygenic inheritance','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Such traits are generally controlled by three or more genes and are thus called as polygenic traits\nHuman skin colour is another classic example for polygenic trait\nIn a polygenic trait the phenotype reflects the contribution of each allele, i.e., the effect of each allele is additive\nBesides the involvement of multiple genes polygenic inheritance also takes into account the influence of environment\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 85, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-085','12-069',NULL),
(272005,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Human weight is a polygenic trait\n \n Reason: It can be influenced by environment','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Human weight has no two distinct alternatives but available in a wide range. It is not so distinct in their occurrence and are spread across a gradient. Such traits are generally controlled by three or more genes and are thus called as polygenic traits. Besides the involvement of multiple genes polygenic inheritance also takes into account the influence of environment.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 85, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-085','12-069',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(272006,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Pleiotropy, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) A single gene can only exhibit one phenotypic expression\n \n 2) Mental retardation can be observed in those who was diagnosed with phenylketonuria\n \n 3) The underlying mechanism of pleiotropy in most cases is the effect of a gene on metabolic pathways\n \n 4) Phenylketouria occurs due to multiple gene mutation','Only 2 and 3 are correct','There are instances where a single gene can exhibit multiple phenotypic expression. Such a gene is called a pleiotropic gene.\nThis manifests itself through phenotypic expression characterised by mental retardation and a reduction in hair and skin pigmentation in phenylketonuria patients\nThe underlying mechanism of pleiotropy in most cases is the effect of a gene on metabolic pathways which contribute towards different phenotypes.\nThe disease is caused by mutation in the gene that codes for the enzyme phenyl alanine hydroxylase (single gene mutation).\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 85, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-085','12-069',NULL),
(272007,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Phenylketonuria is an example of Pleoitropy\n \n Reason: The single gene mutation causes multiple phenotypic expressions','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','An example of Pleoitropy is the disease phenylketonuria, which occurs in humans. The disease is caused by mutation in the gene that codes for the enzyme phenyl alanine hydroxylase (single gene mutation). This manifests itself through phenotypic expression characterised by mental retardation and a reduction in hair and skin pigmentation.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 85 ,para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-085','12-069',NULL),
(272008,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sex Determination, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Mendel discovered sex determination\n \n 2) Henking observed a specific nuclear structure all through oogenesis in a few insects\n \n 3) Sex determination initially was studied using insects\n \n 4) It was observed that 50 % of the sperm received chromosome X after spermatogenesis, whereas the other 50 % sperm did not','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Henking (1891) could trace a specific nuclear structure all through spermatogenesis in a few insects, and it was also observed by him that 50 % of the sperm received this structure after spermatogenesis, whereas the other 50 % sperm did not receive it.\nThe initial clue about the genetic/chromosomal mechanism of sex determination can be traced back to some of the experiments carried out in insects.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 85, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-085','12-069',NULL),
(272009,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sex Determination, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Grasshopper is an example of XO-type sex determination\n \n 2) All eggs bear two additional chromosomes X besides autosome\n \n 3) Most organisms use XO-sex determination\n \n 4) X-chromosome is also known as sex chromosome','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Grasshopper is an example of XO type of sex determination in which the males have only one X-chromosome besides the autosomes, whereas females have a pair of X-chromosomes.\nAll eggs bear an additional X-chromosome besides the other chromosomes (autosomes)\nIt was also observed that in a large number of insects the mechanism of sex determination is of the XO type\nDue to the involvement of the X-chromosome in the determination of sex, it was designated to be the sex chromosome, and the rest of th chromosomes were named as autosomes\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 86, para 1 )','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-086','12-070',NULL),
(121502,'chemistry','Racemic mixture is formed by mixing two','enantiomers with chiral carbon','A racemic mixture is a 50:50 mixture of two enantiomers, which are isomers that are non-superimposable mirror images of each other. Enantiomers have the same physical and chemical properties except for the direction in which they rotate plane-polarized light.\n\nFor a molecule to have enantiomers, it must have a chiral center, which is a carbon atom that is attached to four different substituents. If two compounds with chiral centers are mixed in equal amounts, a racemic mixture is formed.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','isomeric compounds','chiral compounds','meso compounds','enantiomers with chiral carbon','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(272010,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: In organisms which use XO-sex determination, eggs fertilised by sperms that do not have an X-chromosome become males\n \n Reason: Some of the sperms bear the X-chromosome whereas some do not.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','In XO type, all eggs bear an additional X-chromosome besides the other chromosomes (autosomes). On the other hand, some of the sperms bear the X-chromosome whereas some do not. Eggs fertilised by sperm having an X-chromosome become females and, those fertilised by sperms that do not have an X-chromosome become males\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 86, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-086','12-070',NULL),
(272011,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sex Determination, choose the correct statements\n \n 1)XY is known as heterogametic\n \n 2) XY type of sex determination is seen where both male and female have same number of chromosomes.\n \n 3)In human, females have Y chromosome\n \n 4) In Drosophila, males and females have different number of autosomes','Only 1 and 2 are correct','XY is heterogametic while XX is homogametic\nXY type of sex determination is seen where both male and female have same number of chromosomes.\nIn human beings and in Drosophila the males have one X and one Y chromosome, whereas females have a pair of X-chromosomes besides autosomes\nDrosphila uses XY mechanism for sex determination therefore males and females have same amount of autosomes\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 86 ,para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-086','12-070',NULL),
(272012,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sex Determination, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) All insects use the XO-sex determination system\n \n 2) Y chromosome is shorter than X chromosome\n \n 3) Females Drosophila have a pair of X-chromosomes\n \n 4) Chickens have the same sex determination system as human','Only 2 and 3 are correct','It was also observed that in a large number of insects the mechanism of sex determination is of the XO type but not all of them for example, fruit flies use XY sex determination mechanism\nAmong the males an X-chromosome is present but its counter part is distinctly smaller and called the Y-chromosome\nBoth in humans and in Drosophila, the female has a pair of XX chromosomes (homogametic) and the male XY (heterogametic) composition\nHumans use XY-sex determination mechanism which is male heterogamety while chickens use ZW-sex determination system which is designated as female heterogamety \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 86 ,para 1 and 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-086','12-070',NULL),
(272013,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: In humans, males have autosomes plus XY\n \n Reason: Humans use XY sex determination mechanism','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Among the males an X-chromosome is present but its counter part is distinctly smaller and called the Y-chromosome.Hence, the males have autosomes plus XY\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 86, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-086','12-070',NULL),
(272014,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: XO type and XY type sex determination mechanisms are male heterogamety\n \n Reason: The male organisms can produce two different type of gametes','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','In both of these types, males produce two different types of gametes, (a) either with or without X-chromosome or (b) some gametes with X chromosome and some with Y-chromosome. Such types of sex determination mechanism is designated to be the example of male heterogamety\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 86, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-086','12-070',NULL),
(272015,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sex Determination, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) In XO and XY type sex determination, both the male organisms will produce two types of gametes\n \n 2) Female chicken produces heterogametes\n \n 3) In female heterogamety, X and Y chromosomes are found in the female organism\n \n 4) Male chicken has two Z chromosomes apart from autosomes','Only 1 and 4 are correct','In XO type and XY type,males produce two different types of gametes, (a) either with or without X-chromosome or (b) some gametes with X-chromosome and some with Y-chromosome. \nTwo different types of gametes in terms of the sex chromosomes, are produced by females in chicken\nIn order to have a distinction with XY sex determination, the two different sex chromosomes of a female heterogamety has been designated to be the Z and W chromosomes\nIn these organisms the females have one Z and one W chromosome, whereas males have a pair of Z-chromosomes besides the autosomes.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology.Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 86, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-086','12-070',NULL),
(272016,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: In many birds, they are female heterogamety\n \n Reason: The female birds have X and Y chromosomes as sex chromosomes','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','In many birds, female has a pair of dissimilar chromosomes ZW and male two similar ZZ chromosomes\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 86 ,Figure 5.12)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-086','12-070',NULL),
(272017,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sex Determination in Humans, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) All humans have 23 pairs of autosomes\n \n 2) Females only produce ovum with one X-chromosome\n \n 3) The probability of fertilisation of the ovum with the sperm carrying either X or Y chromosome is 50 to 50\n \n 4) Gender of a child is determined by genetic makeup of sperm','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','Out of 23 pairs of chromosomes present, 22 pairs are exactly same in both males and females; these are the autosomes\nFemales, however, produce only one type of ovum with an X-chromosome.\nThere is an equal probability of fertilisation of the ovum with the sperm carrying either X or Y chromosome\nIt is the genetic makeup of the sperm that determines the sex of the child.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 87, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-087','12-071',NULL),
(272018,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Each pregnancy there is always 50 % probability of either a male or a female child\n \n Reason: Females produce only one type of ovum with an X-chromosome','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','During spermatogenesis among males, two types of gametes are produced. 50 % of the total sperm produced carry the X-chromosome and the rest 50 % has Y-chromosome besides\nthe autosomes.There is an equal probability of fertilisation of the ovum with the sperm carrying either X or Y chromosome. In case the ovum fertilises with a sperm carrying X-chromosome the zygote develops into a female (XX) and the fertilisation of ovum with Y-chromosome carrying sperm results into a male offspring\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 87, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-087','12-071',NULL),
(272019,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sex Determination in Honey Bee, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Male bee produces sperms by meiosis\n \n 2) Unfertilised ovum will develop as a female bee\n \n 3) The sex determination in honey bee is based on the number of sets of chromosomes an individual receives\n \n 4) Female bees are diploid','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Male bees produce sperms by mitosis\nAn offspring formed from the union of a sperm and an egg develops as a female (queen or worker), and an unfertilised egg develops as a male (drone) by means of parthenogenesis\nThe sex determination in honey bee is based on the number of sets of chromosomes an individual receives\n The females are diploid having 32 chromosomes and males are haploid\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 87, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-087','12-071',NULL),
(272020,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Bees have father and sons\n \n Reason: Bees produce sperms via meiosis','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Bees use haplo-diploid sex-determination system and has special characteristic features such as the males produce sperms by mitosis, they do not have father and thus cannot have sons, but have a grandfather and can have grandsons.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 87, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-087','12-071',NULL),
(272021,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Mutation, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Mutation is the only factor that causes variation among organisms\n \n 2) UV radiation is an example of mutagen\n \n 3) Point mutation is change in a single base pair of DNA\n \n 4) Mutation will only affect the genotype of an organism','Only 2 and 3 are correct','In addition to recombination, mutation is another phenomenon that leads to variation in DNA\nUV radiations can cause mutations in organisms – it is a mutagen.\nMutation also arise due to change in a single base pair of DNA. This is known as point mutation\nMutation is a phenomenon which results in alteration of DNA sequences and consequently results in changes in the genotype and the phenotype of an organism\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 88, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-088','12-072',NULL),
(272022,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Deletion of a segment of DNA may cause abnormalities\n \n Reason: Chromosome is altered','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','One DNA helix runs continuously from one end to the other in each chromatid, in a highly supercoiled form. Therefore loss (deletions) or gain (insertion/duplication) of a segment of DNA, result in alteration in chromosomes. Since genes are known to be located on chromosomes, alteration in chromosomes results in abnormalities or aberrations.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 88, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-088','12-072',NULL),
(220602,'chemistry','The half reaction method to balance a redox reaction is based on','splitting the redox reaction into two half reactions — one involving oxidation and the other involving reduction.','One of these methods is based on the change in the oxidation number of reducing agent and the oxidising agent and the other method is based on splitting the redox reaction into two half reactions — one involving oxidation and the other involving reduction. Both these methods are in use and the choice of their use rests with the individual using them. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274; 8.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Redox Balancing',NULL,'2','mcq','change in the oxidation number of reducing agent and the oxidising agent','splitting the redox reaction into two half reactions — one involving oxidation and the other involving reduction.','conservation of number of electrons lost by oxidising agent and gained by reducing agent','none of these','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
(272023,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Pedigree Analysis, choose the correct statements\n \n 1)In pedigree analysis the inheritance of a particular trait is represented in the family tree\n \n 2) Pedigree analysis can be used to trace the inheritance of a disease\n \n 3) Control crosses applied in pea plants are carried out in humans to study inheritance of a particular trait\n \n 4) Pedigree analysis is analysis of genotypes in several generations of a family','Only 1 and 2 are correct','In the pedigree analysis the inheritance of a particular trait is represented in the family tree over generations\nIn human genetics, pedigree study provides a strong tool, which is utilised to trace the inheritance of a specific trait, abnormality or disease.\nIt is evident that control crosses that can be performed in pea plant or some other organisms, are not possible in case of human beings\nStudy of the family history about inheritance of a particular trait provides an alternative. Such an analysis of traits in a several of generations of a family is called the pedigree analysis\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 88, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-088','12-072',NULL),
(272024,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Pedigree analysis is used to study the inheritance pattern of disease in several generations of a family\n \n Reason: Pedigree analysis involves the usage of standard symbols','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Since it is evident that control crosses that can be performed in pea plant or some other organisms, are not possible in case of human beings, study of the family history about inheritance of a particular trait provides an alternative. Such an analysis of traits in a several of generations of a family is called the pedigree analysis. Pedigree analysis has standard symbols for male, female, etc. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 88, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-088','12-072',NULL),
(272025,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Pedigree Analysis, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) DNA is the carrier of genetic information \n \n 2) Alteration of DNA happens everytime when being passed down to next generation\n \n 3) Gene is located on chromosome present in DNA \n \n 4) Mutation is the alteration in genetic material','Only 1 and 4 are correct','DNA is the carrier of genetic information\nDNA is transmitted from one generation to the other without any change or alteration. However, changes or alteration do take place occasionally\nGene is located on the DNA present in the chromosome\nAn alteration or change in the genetic material is referred to as mutation.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 89, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(272101,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Mutation of DNA may cause disorders in human\n \n Reason: DNA is carrier of genetic materials','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','DNA is the carrier of genetic information. It is hence transmitted from one generation to the other without any change or alteration. However, changes or alteration do take place occasionally. A number of disorders in human beings have been found to be associated with the inheritance of changed or altered genes or chromosomes.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 89, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(272102,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Mendelian Disorders, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Genetic disorders may be categorized into two groups mainly- Dominant genetic disorders and Recessive genetic disorders.\n \n 2) Mendelian disorders are transmitted to the offspring on the same lines\n \n 3) Haemophilia is a sex-linked disease\n \n 4) Cystic Fibrosis occurs due to the alteration in a single gene','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','Genetic disorders may be grouped into two categories – Mendelian disorders and Chromosomal disorders.\n Mendelian disorders are transmitted to the offspring on the same lines as we have studied in the principle of inheritance\nThe trait may also be linked to the sex chromosome as in case of haemophilia. It is evident that this X-linked recessive trait shows transmission from carrier female to male progeny\nMendelian disorders are mainly determined by alteration or mutation in the single gene and Cystic Fibrosis is one of them\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 89, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(272103,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Thalassemia is a dominant Genetic disorder\n \n Reason: Its recessive trait shows transmission from female to male progeny','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Thalassemia is an autosome Linked recessive blood Disorder. This is a Mendelian disorder because there is alteration in a single gene and transmitted to the offspring on the same lines.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 89, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(241804,'biology','Assertion: All four wall layers of microsporangium serve to protect it.\n\nReason: The plant needs protection with tetra-packed wall layers.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','The outer three wall layers perform the function of protection and help in dehiscence of anther to release the pollen. The innermost wall layer is the tapetum. It nourishes the developing pollen grains. (Reference  : NCERT, page 21, paragraph 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-021',NULL,NULL),
(272104,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Colour Blindness, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Colour Blindness is more likely to be diagnosed in males than females\n \n 2) Colour Blindness is due to defect either blue or green cone of eyes\n \n 3) A carrier of this disease will not be affected\n \n 4) The gene that causes Colour Blindness is found on X-chromosome','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','It occurs in about 8 % of males and only about 0.4 % of females. So, males are more likely to have colour blindness\nIt is a sex-linked recessive disorder due to defect in either red or green cone of eye resulting in failure to discriminate between red and green colour\nBecause the gene is recessive. That means that its effect is suppressed by her matching dominant normal gene. So a carrier who has both the dominant and recessive gene will be normal\nThe genes that lead to red-green colour blindness are on the X chromosome\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12,  Chapter 5, pg 89, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(272105,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Amy’s son is possible to be colorblind if she is a carrier of the disease while her husband is normal.\n \n Reason: The gene is recessive','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The genes that lead to red-green colour blindness are on the X chromosome. Males have only one X chromosome and females have two. The son of a woman who carries the gene has a 50 % chance of being colour blind. The mother is not herself colour blind because the gene is recessive\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12,  Chapter 5, pg 89, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-089','12-073',NULL),
(272106,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Haemophilia, choose the correct answer\n \n 1) A male progeny of female carrier of Haemophilia will not be affected in any way \n \n 2) Haemophilia is a sex-linked dominant disease\n \n 3) Haemophilia will result in non-stop bleeding\n \n 4) Possibility of haemophilic females is low','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Haemophilia shows its transmission from unaffected carrier female to some of the male progeny\nIt is a sex linked recessive disease\nIn this disease, a single protein that is a part of the cascade of proteins involved in the clotting of blood is affected. Due to this, in an affected individual a simple cut will result in non-stop bleeding.\nThe possibility of a female becoming a haemophilic is extremely rare because mother of such a female has to be at least carrier and the father should be haemophilic\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12,  Chapter 5, pg 90, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(272107,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: A haemophilic person may experience non-stop bleeding even with just a simple cut \n \n Reason: A set of proteins involved in the clotting of blood is mutated','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','In this disease, a single protein that is a part of the cascade of proteins involved in the clotting of blood is affected. Due to this, in an affected individual a simple cut will result in non-stop bleeding.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 90, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(272108,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sickle-Cell Anemia, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) The mutated gene in this disease is located on X-chromosome\n \n 2) This disease is controlled by a single pair of allele\n \n 3) The offspring is possible to have Sickle-Cell Anemia if both parents are carriers\n \n 4) Heterozygous individuals will exhibit phenotypes of this disease','Only 2 and 3 are correct','This is an autosome linked recessive trait not involving the sex chromosome\nThe disease is controlled by a single pair of allele, HbA and HbS\nIt can be transmitted from parents to the offspring when both the partners are carrier for the gene\nHeterozygous (HbAHbS) individuals appear apparently unaffected\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 90, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(272109,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Only homozygous individuals with two recessive genes for Sickle-cell Anaemia will show the phenotype\n \n Reason: It is is an autosome linked recessive trait','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Sickle-cell Anaemia is an autosome linked recessive trait that can be transmitted from parents to the offspring when both the partners are carrier for the gene (or heterozygous). only homozygous individuals for HbS (HbSHbS) show the diseased phenotype. Heterozygous (HbAHbS) individuals appear apparently unaffected but they are carrier of the disease\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12,  Chapter 5, pg 90, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-090','12-074',NULL),
(220603,'chemistry','Mark the correct statement.','Both Oxidation Number Method and Half Reaction Method are in use and the choice of their use rests with the individual using them.','One of these methods is based on the change in the oxidation number of reducing agent and the oxidising agent and the other method is based on splitting the redox reaction into two half reactions — one involving oxidation and the other involving reduction. Both these methods are in use and the choice of their use rests with the individual using them. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274; Paragraph 8.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Redox Balancing',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxidation Number Method is better than Half Reaction Method','Half Reaction Method is better than Oxidation Number Method','bothOxidation Number Method and Half Reaction Method are primitive and no longer used','Both Oxidation Number Method and Half Reaction Method are in use and the choice of their use rests with the individual using them.','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
(272110,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sickle-Cell Anemia, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Red blood cell in people who have this disease is an elongated sickle like shape\n \n 2) The mutant haemoglobin molecule undergoes polymerisation under low O₂ tension\n \n 3) In this disease, Glutamine is replaced by Valine\n \n 4) The single base substitution of this disease occurs at the third codon of the beta globin gene','Only 1 and 2 are correct','The change in the shape of the RBC from biconcave disc to elongated sickle like structure in this disease\nThe mutant haemoglobin molecule undergoes polymerisation under low O₂ tension causing the change in the shape of the RBC\nThe defect is caused by the substitution of Glutamic acid by Valine at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of the haemoglobin molecule. Glutamine is derived from Glutamic Acid.\nThe substitution of amino acid in the globin protein results due to the single base substitution at the sixth codon of the beta globin gene from GAG to GUG\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272111,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Sickle-cell anaemia occurs due to substitution of amino acid\n \n Reason: A single base substitution took place from GUG to GAG','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','The defect is caused by the substitution of Glutamic acid by Valine at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of the haemoglobin molecule. Glutamine is derived from Glutamic Acid.The substitution of amino acid in the globin protein results due to the single base substitution at the sixth codon of the beta globin gene from GAG to GUG\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272112,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Phenylketonuria, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Phenylpyruvic acid in urine is one of the symptoms of Phenylketonuria\n \n 2) It is an autosomal recessive disease\n \n 3) Accumulation of Phenylpyruvic acid can cause mental retardation\n \n 4) Phenylketonuria is a type of inborn error of metabolism','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','Phenylpyruvic acid is excreted through urine because of its poor absorption by kidney\nAccumulation of these in brain results in mental retardation\nThis inborn error of metabolism is also inherited as the autosomal recessive trait\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272113,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Phenylketonuria patient shows mental retardation\n \n Reason: Tyrosine accumulation takes place','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','The affected individual lacks an enzyme that converts the amino acid phenylalanine into tyrosine. As a result of this phenylalanine is accumulated and converted into phenylpyruvic acid and other derivatives. Accumulation of these in brain results in mental retardation.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272114,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Thalassemia, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Thalassemia is a sex-linked disease\n \n 2) In Thalassemia, formation of abnormal White Blood Cell occurs\n \n 3) α Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes on chromosome 16\n \n 4) Thalassemia results in production of too few globin molecules','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Thalassemia is an autosome-linked recessive blood disease\nThis causes the formation of abnormal haemoglobin molecules resulting into anaemia which is characteristic of the disease.\nα Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes HBA1 and HBA2 on chromosome 16 of each parent and it is observed due to mutation or deletion of one or more of the four genes\nThalassemia is a quantitative problem of synthesising too few globin molecules\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272115,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: In β Thalassemia, the more genes affected, the less alpha globin molecules produced\n \n Reason: It is observed due to mutation or deletion of one or more of the four genes','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','α Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes HBA1 and HBA2 on chromosome 16 of each parent and it is observed due to mutation or deletion of one or more of the four genes. The more genes affected, the less alpha globin molecules produced. While β Thalassemia is controlled by a single gene HBB on chromosome 11 of each parent and occurs due to mutation of one or both the genes\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272116,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chromosomal Disorders, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Aneuploidy is the gain or loss of a chromosome(s)\n \n 2) Polyploidy is often seen in plants\n \n 3) Chromosomal disorders are caused by abnormal arrangement of one or more chromosomes only\n \n 4) Failure of cytokinesis after telophase stage of cell division causes aneuploidy','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Failure of segregation of chromatids during cell division cycle results in the gain or loss of a chromosome(s), called aneuploidy\nThis condition of polyploidy is often seen in plants.\nThe chromosomal disorders on the other hand are caused due to absence or excess or abnormal arrangement of one or more chromosomes.\nFailure of cytokinesis after telophase stage of cell division results in an increase in a whole set of chromosomes in an organism and, this phenomenon is known as polyploidy\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para 4 and 5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272117,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Down\'s Syndrome is an example of polyploidy\n \n Reason: It results in the gain of extra copy of chromosome 20','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Failure of segregation of chromatids during cell division cycle results in the gain or loss of a chromosome(s), called aneuploidy. For example, Down’s syndrome results in the gain of extra copy of chromosome 21.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para 5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272118,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chromosomal Disorders, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Total number of autosomes in human is 46\n \n 2) Turner\'s Syndrome is an example of Chromosomal disorder\n \n 3) Trisomy of a chromosome is addition of an extra copy of chromosome in an individual \n \n 4) Monosomy of a chromosome can lead to serious consequences in an individual','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','The total number of chromosomes in a normal human cell is 46\n, out of them 44 are autosomes and the other two are sex chromosomes\nDown’s syndrome, Turner’s syndrome, Klinefelter’s syndrome are common examples of chromosomal disorders.\nEither an additional copy of a chromosome may be included in an individual or an individual may lack one of any one pair of chromosomes. These situations are known as trisomy or monosomy of a chromosome, respectively. Such a situation leads to very serious consequences\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para  6)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272119,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Turner\'s Syndrome can lead to serious consequences in female\n \n Reason: It has an extra X-chromosome in the affected female','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','Turner’s syndrome results due to loss of an X chromosome in human females. Such a situation leads to very serious consequences in the individual\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para5 and pg 92, para 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272120,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Klinefelter\'s Syndrome, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Klinefelter\'s Syndrome patients are infertile\n \n 2) Klinefelter’s syndrome patients has short-statured, undeveloped feminine characteristics.\n \n 3) Gynaecomastia occurs in Klinefelter\'s Syndrome patients\n \n 4) Karyotype of 47 is found in Klinefelter\'s Syndrome patients','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Patients of this disease are sterile.\nKlinefelter\'s Syndrome patients have tall stature with feminised character\nThe feminine development (development of breast, i.e., Gynaecomastia) is also expressed in people affected by this disease\nThis genetic disorder is also caused due to the presence of an additional copy of Xchromosome resulting into a karyotype of 47, XXY\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12,Chapter 5, pg 91, para5 and pg 92, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272121,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Klinefelter\'s Syndrome individuals are sterile\n \n Reason: The feminine development is also expressed in them','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Such individuals are sterile. It is due to the presence of an additional copy of X-chromosome resulting into a karyotype of 47, XXY. Such an individual has overall masculine development, however, the feminine development (development of breast, i.e., Gynaecomastia) is also expressed. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para5 and pg 92, para 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272122,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Turner\'s Syndrome, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Turner\'s Syndrome occurs in male\n \n 2) Turner\'s Syndrome is due to the presence of an additional copy of X-chromosome\n \n 3) Turner\'s Syndrome patients are infertile\n \n 4) Turner\'s Syndrome patients have karyotype of 45','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Turner\'s Syndrome only happens in females\nThis disorder is caused due to the absence of one of the X chromosomes\nSuch females are sterile as ovaries are rudimentary\nIt is caused due to the absence of one of the X chromosomes, i.e., 45 with X0\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12,  Chapter 5, pg 91, para5 and pg 92, para 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272123,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Turner\'s Syndrome is a type of monosomy of chromosome 23\n \n Reason: It is caused due to the absence of one of the X chromosomes','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Turner’s Syndrome r is caused due to the absence of one of the X chromosomes, i.e., 45 with X0. The situation of lacking one of any one pair of\nchromosomes is known as monosomy\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12,  Chapter 5, pg 91, para5 and pg 92, para 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(311625,'biology','Breeding crops with higher levels of vitamins and minerals or higher proteins and healthier fats is called (Neet 2022)','Bio-fortification','Breeding crops with higher levels of vitamins and minerals, or higher protein and healthier fats is known as Biofortification.  Biomagnification refers to increase in concentration of the toxicant at successive trophic levels. Bioremediation is the phenomenon of using biological organism to handle pollution.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Strategies for Enhancement of Food Production >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Bio-magnification','Bio-remediation','Bio-fortification','Bio-accumulation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(272124,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Down\'s Syndrome, choose the correct statements\n \n 1) Down\'s Syndrome can occur in both male and female\n \n 2) Down\'s Syndrome is considered as polyploidy\n \n 3) Down\'s Syndrome patients have broad palms and palm creases\n \n 4) An additional copy of chromosome is found at chromosome 21','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Down\'s Syndrome can occur in both genders\nDown’s syndrome is categorized as aneuploidy because it results in the gain of extra copy of chromosome 21\nThe affected individual is short statured with small round head, furrowed tongue and partially open mouth  Palm is broad with characteristic palm crease. Physical, psychomotor and mental development is retarded.\nThe cause of this genetic disorder is the presence of an additional copy of the chromosome number 21 (trisomy of 21)\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 5, pg 91, para5 and pg 92, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-091','12-075',NULL),
(272125,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n \n Assertion: Down\'s Syndrome only affects females\n \n Reason: It has an additional copy of the X-chromosome on chromosome number 21','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Down\'s Syndrome can happen in both males and females.The cause of this genetic disorder is the presence of an additional copy of the chromosome number 21 (trisomy of 21)\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12 ,Chapter 5, pg 92, para 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Principles Of Inheritance And Variation >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-092','12-076',NULL),
(320101,'biology','Which of the following is not a major component of biological systems on Earth?','None of the above','Besides macroscopic plants and animals,microbes are major components of biological systems on the earth. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:179, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','None of the above','Microbes','Plants','Animals','NEW','12-179','12-149',NULL),
(320102,'biology','Microbes are found in ? \n\n(i)In soil, water, air, inside our bodies and that of other animals and plants. \n\n(ii)At sites where no other life-form could possibly exist  \n\n(iii)Deep inside the geysers (thermal vents), deep in the soil, under the layers of snow several metres thick, and in highly acidic environment \n\n(iv)On all the planets of the solar system','i, ii and iii','Microbes are present everywhere – in soil, water, air, inside our bodies and that of other animals and plants. They are present even at sites where no other life-form could possibly exist–sites such as deep inside the geysers (thermal vents) where the temperature may be as high as 1000C, deep in the soil, under the layers of snow several metres thick, and in highly acidic environments. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:179, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Both i and iii','i, ii and iii','Both ii and iv','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-179','12-149',NULL),
(320103,'biology','Microbes include ?\n \n(i)Protozoa,bacteria, fungi  \n\n(ii)Animal and plant viruses  \n\n(iii)Arthropods \n\n(iv)Viroids and prions','i, ii and iv','Microbes are diverse–protozoa, bacteria, fungi and microscopic animal and plant viruses, viroids and also prions that are proteinaceous infectious agents (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:179, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Both i and ii','i, ii and iv','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-179','12-149',NULL),
(320104,'biology','Microbes like bacteria and many fungi can be grown on ________to form colonies','Nutritive media','Microbes like bacteria and many fungi can be grown on nutritive media to form colonies that can be seen with the naked, eyes. Such cultures are useful in studies on micro-organisms. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:179, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Nutritive media','Plastic','Metal surfaces','Antibiotic solutions','NEW','12-179','12-149',NULL),
(320105,'biology','Microbes grown on nutritive media to form colonies that cannot be seen with the naked eye.','FALSE','Microbes like bacteria and many fungi can be grown on nutritive media to form colonies that can be seen with the naked, eyes. Such cultures are useful in studies on micro-organisms. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:179, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-179','12-149',NULL),
(320106,'biology','Not all microbes are harmful, several microbes are useful to man in diverse ways','TRUE','Even though most microbes cause disease, not all microbes are harmful; several microbes are useful to man in diverse ways.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
