(320201,'biology','Organisms that grow in milk and convert it into curd are- \n(i)Macro-organisms  \n(ii)Micro-organisms  \n(iii)Microbes  \n(iv)Macroscopic animals \n(v)Macroscopic plants','Both ii and iii','Microbes are microorganism. Microbes are diverse–protozoa, bacteria, fungi and microscopic animal and plant viruses, viroids and also prions that are proteinaceous infectious agents. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:179, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Both v and iv','Both ii and iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-179','12-149',NULL),
(320202,'biology','What does LAB stand for?','Lactic Acid Bacteria','Micro-organisms such as Lactobacillus and others commonly called lactic acid bacteria (LAB) grow in milk and convert it to curd.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Lactic Acid Bacteria','Laboratory Associated Biogas','Lebanese Biology Generics','Little Bit Grown','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320203,'biology','Lactobacillus is commonly called as','LAB','Micro-organisms such as Lactobacillus and others commonly called lactic acid bacteria (LAB) grow in milk and convert it to curd.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','LAB','LAC','LCT','TCA','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320204,'biology','During growth, the LAB produce acids that coagulate and partially digest','Milk proteins','During growth, the LAB produce acids that coagulate and partially digest the milk proteins (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Milk fats','Milk proteins','Curd proteins','Curd fats','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320205,'biology','Small amount of curd added to the fresh milk as ______ contain millions of LAB.','Inoculum','A small amount of curd added to the fresh milk as inoculum or starter contain millions of LAB, which at suitable temperatures multiply, thus converting milk to curd (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Spiculum','Inoculum','Speculum','Baculum','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320206,'biology','Curd has improved nutritional qulity than milk due to the presence of','Vitamin B12','A small amount of curd added to the fresh milk as inoculum or starter contain millions of LAB, which at suitable temperatures multiply, thus converting milk to curd, which also improves its nutritional quality by increasing vitamin B12. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Vitamin B12','Folic Acid','Vitamin A','Vitamin C','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320207,'biology','Lactobacillus, improves the health of which organ, by keeping a check on disease causing microbes','Intestine','In our stomach and intestine too, the LAB play very beneficial role in checking disease causing microbes (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Heart','Intestine','Brain','Muscles','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320208,'biology','The dough, which is used for making foods such as dosa and idli is also fermented by _____','Bacteria','The dough, which is used for making foods such as dosa and idli is also fermented by bacteria (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Bacteria','Virus','Viroid','Protozoa','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320209,'biology','The puffed-up appearance of dough is due to the production of which gas?','CO₂','The puffed-up appearance of dough is due to the production of CO₂  gas. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₂','Oxygen','Hydrogen','Nitrogen','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320210,'biology','The dough which is used for making bread, is fermented by using','Baker’s yeast','Similarly the dough, which is used for making bread, is fermented using baker’s yeast (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Baker\'s gluten','Baker’s yeast','Baker\'s bacteria','LAB','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320211,'biology','Baker’s yeast is','Saccharomyces cerevisiae','Baker\'s yeast" is the general term given to all forms of Saccharomyces cerevisiae used to make bread products. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Penicillium notatum','Saccharomyces cerevisiae','Saccharomyces cervistyacea','Microbotryum mycetes','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320212,'biology','The process that uses Microbes for the production of a number of Traditional foods and drinks','Fermentation','A number of traditional drinks and foods are also made by fermentation by the microbes.Fermentation is a metabolic process that produces chemical changes in organic substrates through the action of enzymes (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Precipitation','Distillation','Fermentation','Grinding','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320213,'biology','‘Toddy\', a traditional drink of some parts of southern India is made by fermenting','Sap from palms','‘Toddy’, a traditional drink of some parts of southern India is made by fermenting sap from palms. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Curd from milk','Sap from palms','Molasses from sugarcane','Wine from grapes','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320214,'biology','Microbes are also used to ferment which among the following to make food? \n(i)Fish  \n(ii)Soyabean  \n(iii)Yeast \n(iv)Bamboo shoots','i, ii and iv','Microbes are also used to ferment fish, soyabean and bamboo shoots to make foods (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Only iii','i, ii and iii','Both i and ii','i, ii and iv','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320215,'biology','which is an example of one of the oldest foods that used microbes in the process of manufacturing','Cheese','Cheese, is one of the oldest food items in which microbes were used. Different varieties of cheese are known by their characteristic texture, flavour and taste, the specificity coming from the microbes used (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Cheese','Butter','Ghee','Sugar','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320216,'biology','Different varieties of cheese are known by their characteristic texture, flavour and taste, this specificity comes from the microbes used','TRUE','Cheese, is one of the oldest food items in which microbes were used. Different varieties of cheese are known by their characteristic texture, flavour and taste, the specificity coming from the microbes used (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320217,'biology','Large holes in ‘Swiss cheese’ are due to production of a large amount of','CO₂','CO₂  is produce in large amount by the microbe thereby giving more holes (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₂','Lactic acid','Nitrous oxide','Oxygen','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320218,'biology','In Swiss cheese large amount of CO₂  is produced by ?','Bacteria- Propionibacterium shermanii','The large holes in ‘Swiss cheese’ are due to production of a large amount of CO₂  by a bacterium named Propionibacterium shermanii (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Fungi- Agaricus bisporus','Yeast- Saccharomyces cerevisiae','Mushroom- Psilocybe cubensis','Bacteria- Propionibacterium shermanii','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320219,'biology','Roquefort cheese is ripened by','Fungi- Penicillium roqueforti','The ‘Roquefort cheese’ are ripened by growing a specific fungi on them, which gives them a particular flavour\n (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Fungi- Agaricus bisporus','Fungi- Penicillium roqueforti','Yeast- Saccharomyces cerevisiae','Bacteria- Propionibacterium shermanii','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320220,'biology','Assertion: Microbes cause large number of diseases only in human beings. \nReason: All microbes are harmful to other living being.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Microbes cause a large number of diseases in human beings. They also cause diseases in animals and plants. But this should not make you think that all microbes are harmful; several microbes are useful to man in diverse ways. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes In Household products',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320301,'biology','Production of microbial products on an industrial scale, requires growing microbes in very large vessels called','Fermentors','Production on an industrial scale, requires growing microbes in very large vessels called fermentors (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Industrial Products',NULL,'2','mcq','Fermentors','Biothrast','Inoculates','Autoclave','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320302,'biology','Microbes especially yeasts have been used from time immemorial for the production of beverages like wine, beer, whisky, brandy or rum.','TRUE','Microbes like yeast are widely required on industrial scale for production of products. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Industrial Products',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320303,'biology','Saccharomyces cerevisiae is\n(i)Commonly called as Brewer’s yeast  \n(ii)Baker\'s Yeast  \n(iii)Yeast that is used for fermenting malted cereals and fruit juices  \n(iv)Fungi used to make antibiotic','i, ii and iii','Saccharomyces cerevisiae used for bread-making and commonly called brewer’s yeast, is used for fermenting malted cereals and fruit juices (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Industrial Products',NULL,'2','mcq','Both ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','i, ii and iii','Both i and iv','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320304,'biology','Saccharomyces cerevisiae is used for fermenting malted cereals and fruit juices, to produce','Ethanol','Saccharomyces cerevisiae used for bread-making and commonly called brewer’s yeast, is used for fermenting malted cereals and fruit juices, to produce ethanol. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Industrial Products',NULL,'2','mcq','Methanol','Ethanol','Lactic acid','CO₂','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320305,'biology','Wine and beer are produced','Without distillation processing','Depending on the type of the raw material used for fermentation and the type of processing (with or without distillation) different types of alcoholic drinks are obtained. Wine and beer are produced without distillation (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Industrial Products',NULL,'2','mcq','Without distillation processing','With distillation processing','Without fermentation','Without microbes','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(260116,'chemistry','Carbonyl compounds are used in many _____ to add flavours.','Pharmaceuticals','Carbonyl compounds are used in many food products and pharmaceuticals to add flavours. \n\nSome of these families are manufactured for use as solvents (i.e., acetone) and for preparing materials like adhesives, paints, resins, perfumes, plastics, fabrics, etc.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Food industries','Pharmaceuticals','Textiles','Sugar cane mills','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(320306,'biology','Whisky, brandy and rum are produced','With distillation processing','Depending on the type of the raw material used for fermentation and the type of processing (with or without distillation) different types of alcoholic drinks are obtained. Wine and beer are produced without distillation whereas whisky, brandy and rum are produced by distillation of the fermented broth (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Industrial Products',NULL,'2','mcq','Without distillation processing','With distillation processing','Without fermentation','Without microbes','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320307,'biology','Raw material used for fermenting in production of whisky, brandy and rum is?','Broth','Whisky, brandy and rum are produced by distillation of the fermented broth (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Industrial Products',NULL,'2','mcq','Sap','Broth','Dough','Batter','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320308,'biology','Industrial use of microbes ranges widely from bread making to antibiotics.','TRUE','Even in industry, microbes are used to synthesise a number of products valuable to human beings. Beverages and antibiotics are some examples (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Industrial Products',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(320309,'biology','What are all the beverages made with help of yeast- \n(i) Wine \n(ii) Tea  \n(iii) Whisky  \n(iv)Carbonated drinks','i and iii','Microbes especially yeasts have been used from time immemorial for the production of beverages wine, beer, whisky, brandy or rum. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Industrial Products',NULL,'1','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320401,'biology','‘Against life’ in the context of disease causing organisms refers to','Antibiotics','Antibiotics produced by microbes are regarded as one of the most significant discoveries of the twentieth century and have greatly contributed towards the welfare of the human society. Anti is a Greek word that means ‘against’, and bio means ‘life’, together they mean ‘against life’ =in the context of disease causing organisms.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Antibiotics','Analgesic','Pro Life','Anti-inflammatory','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320402,'biology','Antibiotics with Reference   to human being refers to','Pro Life','Antibiotics with Reference   to human beings, they are ‘pro life’ and not against which means they help to prolong the life of humans.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Against life','Pro Life','Anti-inflammatory','Antiseptics','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320403,'biology','Antibiotics are chemical substances, most of which are produced by','Microbes','Antibiotics are chemical substances, which are produced by some microbes and can kill or retard the growth of other (disease-causing) microbes. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Microbes','Synthetically','Mechanically','Animals','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320404,'biology','Substances that can kill or retard the growth of other (disease-causing) microbes','Antibiotics','Antibiotics are chemical substances, which are produced by some microbes and can kill or retard the growth of other (disease-causing) microbes. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Analgesic','Antibiotics','Antispasmodic','Antiseptics','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320405,'biology','Which of the following with regards to Penicillin\n(i)First antibiotic to be discovered  \n(ii)Commonly used antibiotic  \n(iii)Discovered by extensive research\n(iv)Chemical obtained from a mould','i, ii and iv','Penicillin is commonly used and first discovered antibiotic. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Both ii and iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii and iv','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320406,'biology','Penicillin was discovered by','Alexander Fleming','Alexander Fleming while working on Staphylococci bacteria, once observed a mould growing in one of his unwashed culture plates around which Staphylococci could not grow (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Alexander Fleming','Edward Jenner','Kohler and Milstein','Crick and Watson','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320407,'biology','What was Fleming working with when he discovered Penicillin?','Staphylococci- bacteria','Alexander Fleming while working on Staphylococci bacteria, once observed a mould growing in one of his unwashed culture plates around which Staphylococci could not grow (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Staphylococci- bacteria','Penicillium- yeast','Penicillium- fungi','Staphylococci- virus','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320408,'biology','Where did Fleming observe a mould growing during the discovery of Penicillin?','Unwashed culture plates','Alexander Fleming while working on Staphylococci bacteria, once observed a mould growing in one of his unwashed culture plates around which Staphylococci could not grow (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Washed culture plates','Unwashed culture plates','Microscope','Contaminated culture medium','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320409,'biology','Staphylococci could not grow, where the mould was growing when observed by Alexander Fleming.','TRUE','Alexander Fleming while working on Staphylococci bacteria, once observed a mould growing in one of his unwashed culture plates around which Staphylococci could not grow. He found out that it was due to a chemical produced by the mould. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320410,'biology','______ could not grow in the presence of a chemical produced by ______(respectively)','Staphylococcus, Penicillium','Alexander Fleming found out that it was due to a chemical produced by the mould the Staphylococci bacteria could not grow. and he named it Penicillin after the mould Penicillium notatum (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Staphylococcus, Penicillium','Penicillium, Staphylococcus','Penicillium, Escherichia','Escherichia, Cephalosporin','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320411,'biology','Penicillin\'s full potential as an effective antibiotic was established much later by- \n(i)Ernest Chain  \n(ii)Howard Florey  \n(iii)Alexander Fleming','Both i and ii','Alexander Fleming found out that it was due to a chemical produced by the mould and he named it Penicillin after the mould Penicillium notatum. However, its full potential as an effective antibiotic was established much later by Ernest Chain and Howard Florey (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Both i and iii','Both i and ii','i, ii and iii','Both ii and iii','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320412,'biology','Penicillin was extensively used to treat American soldiers wounded in','World War II','Penicillin antibiotic was extensively used to treat American soldiers wounded in World War II. Fleming, Chain and Florey were awarded the Nobel Prize in 1945, for this discovery (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','World War I','World War II','Indo-Vietnamese War','Indo-Pak War','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320413,'biology','Who were awarded Nobel prize in 1945 for discovery of penicillium notatum. \n(i)Alexander Fleming  \n(ii)Alexander bell \n(iii) Howard Florey  \n(iv)Ernest Chain','i, iii and iv','Fleming, Chain and Florey were awarded the Nobel Prize in 1945, for this discovery. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Both i and ii','i, iii and iv','Both ii and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320414,'biology','Antibiotics have greatly improved our capacity to treat deadly diseases such as- \n(i)Rabies\n(ii)Plague  \n(iii)Diphtheria \n(iv)Whooping cough','ii, iii and iv','Antibiotics have greatly improved our capacity to treat deadly diseases such as plague, whooping cough (kali khansi), diphtheria (gal ghotu) and leprosy (kusht rog), which used to kill millions all over the globe. Rabies is a virus, immune to antibiotics (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','ii, iii and iv','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','i, ii and iv','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320415,'biology','Kali khansi is','Whooping cough','Antibiotics have greatly improved our capacity to treat deadly diseases such as plague, whooping cough (kali khansi), diphtheria (gal ghotu) and leprosy (kusht rog), which used to kill millions all over the globe (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Diphtheria','Leprosy','Whooping cough','Plague','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320416,'biology','Kusht rog is','Leprosy','Antibiotics have greatly improved our capacity to treat deadly diseases such as plague, whooping cough (kali khansi), diphtheria (gal ghotu) and leprosy (kusht rog), which used to kill millions all over the globe (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Diphtheria','Leprosy','Whooping cough','Plague','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320417,'biology','Gal ghotu is','Diphtheria','Antibiotics have greatly improved our capacity to treat deadly diseases such as plague, whooping cough (kali khansi), diphtheria (gal ghotu) and leprosy (kusht rog), which used to kill millions all over the globe (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Diphtheria','Leprosy','Whooping cough','Plague','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320418,'biology','Why was the first antibiotic called penicillin?','Because it was a chemical obtained from mould Penicillium notatum','Alexander Fleming found out that it was due to a chemical produced by the mould the Staphylococci bacteria could not grow. and he named it Penicillin after the mould Penicillium notatum(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Because it was named after the lab where it was discovered','Because it was a chemical obtained from mould Penicillium notatum','Because the bacteria killed the mould Penicillium notatum','Because the name came from the scientist who discovered it.','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(320419,'biology','Assertion: Penicillin is the antibiotic of choice to treat many bacterial infections. \nReason: Penicillin is one among many antibiotics extracted from microbes.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','After Penicillin, other antibiotics were also purified from other microbes, even then pencillin continues as a antibiotic drug of choice for many primary bacterial infections (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320420,'biology','There has been only a slight change in health care since antibiotics were discovered','FALSE','Discovery of antibiotics has shifted the whole health care system to a stage where dealing with infectious disease has become easier. Today, we cannot imagine a world without antibiotics. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Antibiotics',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320501,'biology','Microbes are also used for commercial and industrial production of certain chemicals like- \n(i)Organic Acids  \n(ii)Alcohols  \n(iii)Aldehyde  \n(iv)Enzymes','i, ii and iv','Microbes are also used for commercial and industrial production of certain chemicals like organic acids, alcohols and enzymes. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Both i and ii','i, ii and iii','i, ii and iv','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320502,'biology','Match the acid producers with acids they produce','Aspergillus niger (a fungus)-citric acid,Acetobacter aceti (a bacterium)-acetic acid,Clostridium butylicum (a bacterium)-butyric acid,Lactobacillus (a bacterium)-lactic acid','Acid producers are Aspergillus niger (a fungus) of citric acid, Acetobacter aceti (a bacterium) of acetic acid; Clostridium butylicum (a bacterium) of butyric acid and Lactobacillus (a bacterium) of lactic acid. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','match','Aspergillus niger (a fungus),citric acid','Acetobacter aceti (a bacterium),acetic acid','Clostridium butylicum (a bacterium),butyric acid','Lactobacillus (a bacterium),lactic acid','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320503,'biology','Saccharomyces cerevisiae is used for commercial production of','Ethanol','Yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) is used for commercial production of ethanol. Microbes are also used for production of enzymes. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Ethanol','Tea','Fruits','Rice','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320504,'biology','_______ are used in detergent formulations and are helpful in removing oily stains from the laundry','Lipases','Lipases are used in detergent formulations and are helpful in removing oily stains from the laundry (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Lipases','Proteases','Pectinases','Sucrases','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320505,'biology','Bottled juices are clarified by the use of','Pectinases and proteases','Bottled fruit juices bought from the market are clearer as compared to those made at home. This is because the bottled juices are clarified by the use of pectinases and proteases. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Saccharomyces cerevisiae','Ethanol','Yeast','Pectinases and proteases','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320506,'biology','_______ produced by the bacterium Streptococcus and modified by genetic engineering','Streptokinase','Streptokinase produced by the bacterium Streptococcus and modified by genetic engineering is used as a ‘clot buster\'. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Streptokinase','Pectinases','Lipases','Invertases','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320507,'biology','Clot buster is','Streptokinase','Streptokinase produced by the bacterium Streptococcus and modified by genetic engineering is used as a ‘clot buster’ for removing clots from the blood vessels of patients who have undergone myocardial infarction leading to heart attack. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Lipases','Streptokinase','Pectinases and proteases','Alcohol','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320508,'biology','Clot buster is related to- \n(i)Remove clots from the blood vessels of patients who are suffering from myocardial infarction\n(ii)Streptokinase produced by the bacterium Streptococcus and modified by genetic engineering is used as a ‘clot buster’  \n(iii)Acne treatment\niv) Cosmetic Pimple remover','Both i and ii','Streptokinase produced by the bacterium Streptococcus and modified by genetic engineering is used as a ‘clot buster’ for removing clots from the blood vessels of patients who have undergone myocardial infarction leading to heart attack ((Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Both i and ii','Both ii and iii','i, ii,iv and iii','Both i and iv','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320509,'biology','Cyclosporin A is a- \n(i)Bioactive molecule \n(ii)Unreactive atom  \n(iii)Immunosuppressive agent \n(iv)Immunobooster','Both i and iii','A bioactive molecule called cyclosporin A, that is used as an immunosuppressive agent in organ-transplant patients. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii and iii','Both i and iii','Both i and iv','Both ii and iv','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320510,'biology','Cyclosporin A is produced by','Fungus- Trichoderma polysporum','Cyclosporin A, used as an immunosuppressive agent in organ-transplant patients, is produced by the fungus Trichoderma polysporum (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Staphylococci bacteria','Yeast-Saccharomyces cerevisiae','Fungus- Trichoderma polysporum','Bioactive molecules','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320511,'biology','Statins produced by','Yeast- Monascus purpureus','Statins produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus have been commercialised as blood-cholesterol lowering agents. It acts by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for synthesis of cholesterol. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Bacteria- Propionibacterium sharmanii','Yeast- saccharomyces cerevisiae','Fungus- Trichoderma polysporum','Yeast- Monascus purpureus','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320512,'biology','Blood-cholesterol lowering agents are','Statins','Statins produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus have been commercialised as blood-cholesterol lowering agents (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Statins','Cyclosporin A','Streptokinase','Lipases','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320513,'biology','_______ acts by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for synthesis of cholesterol','Statins','Statins produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus have been commercialised as blood-cholesterol lowering agents. It acts by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for synthesis of cholesterol. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Statins','Cyclosporine','Aspirin','Penicillin','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320514,'biology','Which one of the following is a wrong matching of microbe and its industrial product, while the remaining three are correct','Clostridium butylicum-Lactic acid','Acid producers are Aspergillus niger (a fungus) of citric acid, Acetobacter aceti (a bacterium) of acetic acid; Clostridium butylicum (a bacterium) of butyric acid and Lactobacillus (a bacterium) of lactic acid. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'2','mcq','Clostridium butylicum-Lactic acid','Aspergillus Niger-Citric acid','Monascus-Statins','Acetobacter aceti-Acetic acid','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320515,'biology','Microbes are not used for production of enzymes.','FALSE','Microbes are also used for production of enzymes. Some examples are lipases, pectinases, proteases, streptokinases by streptococcus(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:3','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320601,'biology','A major component of waste water is human excreta. This municipal waste-water is also called as','Sewage','We know that large quantities of waste water are generated everyday in cities and towns. A major component of this waste water is human excreta. This municipal waste-water is also called sewage. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Sewage','Sludge','Sediments','Effluent','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(260117,'chemistry','Some of carbonyl compounds are manufactured for used as solvents that is ______?','Acetone','Some of the aldehydes and ketones are manufactured for use as solvents (i.e., acetone) and for preparing materials like adhesives, paints, resins, perfumes, plastics, fabrics, etc.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Ethanol','Ethyl acetate','Methanol','Acetone','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(320602,'biology','Before disposal, sewage is treated in sewage treatment plants (STPs) to make it less polluting.','TRUE','Sewage contains large amounts of organic matter and microbes which are pathogenic. This cannot be discharged into natural water bodies like rivers and streams directly. So, before disposal, hence, sewage is treated in sewage treatment plants (STPs) to make it less polluting.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320603,'biology','Treatment of waste water is done by the ________ naturally present in the sewage.','Heterotrophic microbes','Treatment of waste water is done by the heterotrophic microbes naturally present in the sewage. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Homotrophic microbes','Heterotrophic microbes','Parasitic microbes','Normal bacterial flora','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320604,'biology','Which part of sewage treatment steps basically involve physical removal of particles – large and small – from the sewage','Primary','Primary treatment steps basically involve physical removal of particles – large and small – from the sewage  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary','Secondary','Tertiary','Quarternary','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320605,'biology','In Primary treatment _____ and ______occurs.','Filtration and sedimentation','Primary treatment steps basically involve physical removal of particles – large and small – from the sewage through filtration and sedimentation (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Filtration and sedimentation','Distillation and filtration','Distillation and sedimentation','Distillation and aerobic reactions','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320606,'biology','Floating debris are removed by _____and grit (soil and small pebbles) are removed by_______(answer respectively)','Sequential filtration, sedimentation.','In Primary treatment steps basically involve physical removal of particles – large and small – from the sewage through filtration and sedimentation. These are removed in stages; initially, floating debris is removed by sequential filtration. Then the grit (soil and small pebbles) are removed by sedimentation (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Sequential filtration, sedimentation.','Sedimentation, sequential filtration','Distillation, aerobic reaction','Aerobic reaction, distillation','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320607,'biology','What do all the solids that settle after sedimentation form?','Primary sludge','In Primary solid settlement take place. All solids that settle form the primary sludge (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary sewage','Primary sludge','Secondary sludge','Secondary sewage','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320608,'biology','In Primary treatment of Sewage supernatant forms the _____','Effluent','All solids that settle form the primary sludge, and the supernatant forms the effluent. The effluent from the primary settling tank is taken for secondary treatment. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Effluent','Inoculum','Sludge','Affluent','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320609,'biology','Biological treatment in Sewage treatment is the other name for','Secondary treatment','Second part of sewage treatment includes the microbes playing a major role.Thats why its also called biological treatment. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Secondary treatment','Primary treatment','Mechanical treatment','Artificial treatment','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320610,'biology','The primary effluent is passed into large ______ tanks','Aeration','The primary effluent is passed into large aeration tanks where it is constantly agitated mechanically and air is pumped into it. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','fillup','Ae,ra,tio,n',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320611,'biology','In biological treatment, what happens in the large aeration tanks? \n\n1)Primary effluent is passed through large aeration tanks\n\n2)Vigorous growth of useful aerobic microbes into flocs\n\n3)Primary effluent is constantly agitated mechanically\n\n4)Air is pumped into it \n\nCorrect sequence of the events is-','1,3,4,2','The primary effluent is passed into large aeration tanks where it is constantly agitated mechanically and air is pumped into it. This allows vigorous growth of useful aerobic microbes into flocs (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','1,2,3,4','1,2,4,3','1,3,4,2','1,3,2,4','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320612,'biology','Masses of bacteria associated with fungal filaments to form mesh like structure is called as','Flocs','Flocs are masses of bacteria associated with fungal filaments to form mesh like structures (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Flocs','Lichens','Stools','Stocks','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320613,'biology','While growing, microbes consume the major part of the organic matter in the effluent. This results in','Significantly reduces the biochemical O₂ demand','In the sewage the microbes while growing consume the major part of the organic matter in the effluent. This significantly reduces the BOD (biochemical O₂ demand) of the effluent (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Significantly reduces the biochemical O₂ demand','Increases O₂ in effluent','Decrease the amount of carbon','Significantly increases the biochemical O₂ demand','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320614,'biology','BOD is','Biochemical O₂ demand','Biological agent, microbes consume the organic,chemical matter by enzyme action utilising the oxygen. BOD refers to the amount of the O₂ that would be consumed if all the organic matter in one liter of water were oxidised by bacteria  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Biochemical O₂ demand','Biological O₂ deficit','Bacterial O₂ demand','Biosynthetic O₂ demand','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(260118,'chemistry','The solvent used in preparation of adhesive is_____?','Acetone','Some of aldehydes and ketones are manufactured for use as solvents (i.e., acetone) and for preparing materials like adhesives, paints, resins, perfumes, plastics, fabrics, etc.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Toluene','Ethanol','Diethyl ether','Acetone','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(320615,'biology','The amount of the O₂ that would be consumed if all the organic matter in one liter of water were oxidised by bacteria is','Biochemical oxygen demand','BOD refers to the amount of the O₂ that would be consumed if all the organic matter in one liter of water were oxidised by bacteria, it can be referred to as Biological O₂ demand, Biochemical O₂ Demand (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','fillup','Bio,ch,emi,cal ox,yg,en, de,ma,nd,lo,gi',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320616,'biology','The sewage water is treated till the BOD is _______.','Reduced','The sewage water is treated till the BOD is reduced. The BOD test measures the rate of uptake of O₂ by microorganisms in a sample of water and thus, indirectly, BOD is a measure of the organic matter present in the water (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Increased','Reduced','Equalized','No change','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320617,'biology','BOD refers to the amount of the  CO₂  that would be consumed if all the inorganic matter in one liter of water were oxidised by bacteria.','FALSE','BOD refers to the amount of the O₂ that would be consumed if all the organic matter in one liter of water were oxidised by bacteria. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320618,'biology','BOD test measures- \n(i)Rate of uptake of O₂ by microorganisms in a sample of water \n(ii)Rate of increase in O₂ levels \n(iii)Measure of the organic matter present in the water.','Both i and iii','The sewage water is treated till the BOD is reduced. The BOD test measures the rate of uptake of O₂ by micro-organisms in a sample of water and thus, indirectly, BOD is a measure of the organic matter present in the water (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Both ii and iii','Both i and iii','Both i and ii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320619,'biology','The greater the BOD of waste water,','More is its polluting potential','The greater the BOD of waste water, more is its polluting potential. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Less is its water potential','More is its polluting potential','More is its atmospheric pressure','Less is inorganic contain','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320620,'biology','In which tanks flocs are allowed to sediment?','Settling tank','Once the BOD of sewage or waste water is reduced significantly, the effluent is then passed into a settling tank where the bacterial ‘flocs’ are allowed to sediment. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Settling tank','Aeration tank','Filtration tank','Sediment tank','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320621,'biology','Bacterial Flocs settling down with reduction in BOD of sewage is called','Activated sludge','Once the BOD of sewage or waste water is reduced significantly, the effluent is then passed into a settling tank where the bacterial ‘flocs’ are allowed to sediment. This sediment is called activated sludge. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Inactivated sludge.','Activated sludge','Anaerobic sludge','Inactive anaerobic sludge','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320622,'biology','A small part of the _______ is pumped back into the aeration tank to serve as the inoculum.','Activated Sludge','A small part of the activated sludge is pumped back into the aeration tank to serve as the inoculum (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','fillup','Ac,tiv,at,ed Slu,dge',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320623,'biology','The remaining major part of the sludge is pumped into large tanks called','Anaerobic sludge digesters','Small part of the activated sludge is pumped back into the aeration tank to serve as the inoculum. The remaining major part of the sludge is pumped into large tanks called anaerobic sludge digesters (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Aerobic sludge digesters','Activated Sludge','Anaerobic sludge digesters','Activated aerobic sludge digesters','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320624,'biology','The anaerobic bacteria digest the bacteria and the fungi in the sludge and produced which gas during this digestion? \n\n(i)Methane\n \n(ii)Nitrogen  \n\n(iii) CO₂   \n\n(iv)Hydrogen sulphide','i, iii and iv','The remaining major part of the sludge is pumped into large tanks called anaerobic sludge digesters. Here, other kinds of bacteria, which grow anaerobically, digest the bacteria and the fungi in the sludge. During this digestion, bacteria produce a mixture of gases such as methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂ . (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','i, ii and iii','i, iii and iv','i, ii and iv','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320625,'biology','The property due to which Biogas is used as an energy fuel','Inflammable','Bacteria, which grow anaerobically, digest the bacteria and the fungi in the sludge. During this digestion, bacteria produce a mixture of gases such as methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂ . These gases form biogas and can be used as source of energy as it is inflammable (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Inflammable','Non-flammable','Low combustion index','High Pollution potential','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320626,'biology','Which combination of gases form biogas?','Methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂','During the digestion, bacteria produce a mixture of gases such as methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂ . These gases form biogas and can be used as source of energy as it is inflammable.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂','Methane, sulphur and  CO₂','Methane, hydrogen sulphide and carbon monoxide','Methane, hydrogen nitrite and  CO₂','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320627,'biology','What has The Ministry of Environment and Forests initiated to save major rivers of our country from pollution? \n\n(i)Ganga Action Plan  \n\n(ii)Yamuna Action Plan  \n\n(iii)Godavari and Ganga Action plans','Both i and ii','The Ministry of Environment and Forests has initiated Ganga Action Plan and Yamuna Action Plan to save these major rivers of our country from pollution. Under these plans, it is proposed to build a large number of sewage treatment plants so that only treated sewage may be discharged in the rivers. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','Both ii and iii','i, ii and iii','Both i and ii','Both i and iii','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(320628,'biology','Under Yamuna and Ganga Action plans, it is proposed to build a large number of sewage treatment plants so that only treated sewage may be discharged in the river','TRUE','The Ministry of Environment and Forests has initiated Ganga Action Plan and Yamuna Action Plan to save these major rivers of our country from pollution. Under these plans, it is proposed to build a large number of sewage treatment plants so that only treated sewage may be discharged in the rivers (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(320629,'biology','Assertion: Sewage contains organic matter and microbes which are mostly harmful.\n\nReason: Since major component of sewage is human excreta, microbes may be both the normal flora from human gut and pathogenic bacteria','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','A major component of this waste water is human excreta.This municipal waste-water is also called sewage. It contains large amounts of organic matter and microbes. Many of which are pathogenic.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320630,'biology','Why can\'t the sewage be discharged directly into the water bodies?\n\n(i)Because of the organic matter  \n\n(ii)Because of the harmful chemicals  \n\n(iii)Because of the pathogenic microbes  \n\n(iv)Because of the processed solid waste','i, ii and iii','Sewage cannot be discharged into natural water bodies like rivers and streams directly – you can understand why. Before disposal, hence, sewage is treated in sewage treatment plants (STPs) to make it less polluting. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'2','mcq','Both i and iv','i, ii and iii','Both ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(320631,'biology','Sewage treatment is carried out in how many stages-','Two','Sewage treatment is carried out in two stages: Primary treatment and Secondary treatment or Biological treatment (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'1','mcq','One','Two','Three','Four','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320632,'biology','In sewage treatment what is released into natural water bodies?','The effluent from secondary treatment','The effluent from the secondary treatment plant is generally released into natural water bodies like rivers and streams(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'2','mcq','The effluent from primary treatment','The effluent from secondary treatment','The sediment from primary treatment','The sediment from secondary treatment','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320633,'biology','Assertion: The method of sewage treatment using microbes is the oldest yet most efficient one in the whole world.\n\nReason: The sewage treatment involves a thorough stepwise process of segregating all parts of sewage and making it less harmful and more efficient with minimal wastage of resource.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Microbes play a major role in treating millions of gallons of waste water everyday across the globe. This methodology has been practiced for more than a century now, in almost all parts of the world. Till date, no man made technology has been able to rival the microbial treatment of sewage.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320634,'biology','The reasons for the pollution of the natural water bodies are-\n\n(i) Increased urbanisation  \n\n(ii) Efficient sewage plants   \n\n(iii) Decreased industrialisation \n\n(iv) Insufficient sewage plants','Both i and iv','Increasing urbanisation has resulted in sewage being produced in much larger quantities than ever before. However the number of sewage treatment plants has not increased enough to treat such large quantities. So the untreated sewage is often discharged directly into rivers leading to their pollution and increase in water-borne diseases(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii and iii','i, iii and iv','Both i and iv','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320635,'biology','Assertion: The after-effect of the untreated sewage being dumped is a whole lot of diseases.\n\nReason: Some diseases are mainly water- borne diseases caused by the microbes in the sewage.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The untreated sewage is often discharged directly into rivers leading to their pollution and increase in water-borne diseases. Also leads to other types of diseases like vector-borne because of increased breeding grounds, etc.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in Sewage Treatment',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(320701,'biology','Which among the following is Incorrect about biogas?','It is obtained primarily from plants','Biogas is a mixture of gases (containing predominantly methane) produced by the microbial activity and which may be used as fuel (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','mcq','It is a mixture of gases and used as fuel','Containing predominantly methane','Produced by the microbial activity','It is obtained primarily from plants','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(320702,'biology','The type of the gas produced by microbes depends upon the microbes and the organic substrates they utilise.','TRUE','The microbes produce different types of gaseous end-products during growth and metabolism. The type of the gas produced depends upon the microbes and the organic substrates they utilise (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(320703,'biology','CO₂  as the main gas is produced in which of the following process? \n\n(i)Fermentation of dough  \n\n(ii)Cheese making  \n\n(iii)Production of beverages \n\n(iv)Biogas','i, ii and iii','In relation to fermentation of dough, cheese making and production of beverages, the main gas produced was CO₂ . (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','mcq','Both ii and iv','Both i and iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(320704,'biology','Bacteria which grow anaerobically on cellulosic material, produce- \n(i)Large amount of methane  \n(ii) CO₂   \n(iii)O₂  \n(iv)Nitrogen','Both i and ii','Certain bacteria, which grow anaerobically on cellulosic material, produce large amount of methane along with CO₂  and H₂ . These bacteria are collectively called methanogens (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii and iii','iii and iv','Both i and ii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(320705,'biology','_______ bacteria are commonly found in the anaerobic sludge during sewage treatment','Methanogens','Methanogens, one such common bacterium is Methanobacterium. These bacteria are commonly found in the anaerobic sludge during sewage treatment (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','fillup','Me,th,an,oge,ns',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(320706,'biology','_______ bacteria are also present in the rumen (a part of stomach) of cattle.','Methanogens','Methanogens are also present in the rumen (a part of stomach) of cattle. A lot of cellulosic material present in the food of cattle is also present in the rumen (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','mcq','Methanogens','Acidophiles','Thermophiles','Cyanobacteria','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(320707,'biology','Methanobacterium is a common example of- \n\n(i)Bacteria present in rumen of cattle  \n\n(ii)Bacteria which use methane as main energy source  \n\n(iii)Bacteria present in the anaerobic sludge during sewage treatment  \n\n(iv)Methanogens','i, iii and iv','Methanogens, one such common bacterium is Methanobacterium. These bacteria are commonly found in the anaerobic sludge during sewage treatment. They are also present in the rumen (a part of stomach) of cattle. A lot of cellulosic material present in the food of cattle is also present in the rumen (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii and iii','i, ii and iv','i, iii and iv','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(320708,'biology','In rumen, Methanobacterium help in the breakdown of','Cellulose','In rumen, these bacteria help in the breakdown of cellulose and play an important role in the nutrition of cattle. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','mcq','Fats','Proteins','Cellulose','Glucose','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(320709,'biology','Dung can be used for generation of biogas, commonly called','Gobar gas','The excreta (dung) of cattle, commonly called gobar, is rich in these bacteria. Dung can be used for generation of biogas, commonly called gobar gas. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','fillup','Go,bar gas,du,ng',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(320710,'biology','In what places are the Biogas plants most often built?','Rural areas','Cattle dung is available in large quantities in rural areas where cattle are used for a variety of purposes. So biogas plants are more often built in rural areas (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:186, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','mcq','Cities','Rural areas','River banks','Abandoned lands','NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(320711,'biology','In rural areas the biogas produced is used for cooking and lighting','TRUE','Cattle dung is available in large quantities in rural areas where cattle are used for a variety of purposes. So biogas plants are more often built in rural areas. The biogas thus produced is used for cooking and lighting. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:186, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(320712,'biology','The technology of biogas production was developed in India mainly due to the efforts of- \n(i)Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC)  \n(ii)Indian Agricultural Research Institute (IARI)  \n(iii)The Indian Renewable Energy Development Agency (IREDA)\n(iv)WFF(World Fuel Federation)','Both i and ii','The technology of biogas production was developed in India mainly due to the efforts of Indian Agricultural Research Institute (IARI) and Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC) (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:186, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','mcq','Both ii,iii and iv','i, ii,iii and iv','Both i and ii','Both i and iii','NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(320713,'biology','Identify the given diagram . (3209)','A typical biogas plant','Figure 10.8 (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:186)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'2','mcq','An aerial view of a sewage plant','A typical biogas plant','Secondary sewage treatment plant','Fermentors','NEW','12-186','12-156','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/320713q.png'),
(320714,'biology','What is the approximate length(in feet) of a biogas plant?','10 to 15','The biogas plant consists of a concrete tank (10-15 feet deep) in which bio-wastes are collected and a slurry of dung is fed.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:186, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'1','mcq','1 to 5','5 to 10','10 to 15','15 to 20','NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(320715,'biology','What is the right sequence of biogas production?  \n(a) Dung slurry inside biogas plant  \n(b) Microbial action   \n(c) Floating cover   \n(d) Remains as biofertilizer  \n(e) Through a outlet  \n(f) Pipe supply to houses  \n(g)Gas produced','a-c-b-g-e-f-d','The biogas plant consists of a concrete tank (10-15 feet deep) in which bio-wastes are collected and a slurry of dung is fed. A floating cover is placed over the slurry, which keeps on rising as the gas is produced in the tank due to the microbial activity. The biogas plant has an outlet, which is connected to a pipe to supply biogas to nearby houses. The spent slurry is removed through another outlet and may be used as fertiliser. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:186, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes in production of biogas',NULL,'3','mcq','a-c-g-b-f-e','a-b-c-e-g-f','a-c-b-g-e-f-d','a-b-c-g-e-d-f','NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(320801,'biology','_______refers to the use of biological methods for controlling plant diseases and pests.','Biocontrol','Biocontrol refers to the use of biological methods for controlling plant diseases and pests. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:186, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','Biosynthetic','Biochemical','Biocontrol','Biome','NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(320802,'biology','Which among the following statement is incorrect?','These chemical are not abused at all','In modern society, these problems have been tackled increasingly by the use of chemicals – by use of insecticides and pesticides. These chemicals are toxic and extremely harmful, to human beings and animals alike, and have been polluting our environment (soil, ground water), fruits, vegetables and crop plants. Our soil is also polluted through our use of weedicides to remove weeds.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:186, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','In modern society, the problems of plants diseases have been tackled increasingly by the use of chemicals – by use of insecticides and pesticides.','These chemicals are toxic and extremely harmful, to human beings and animals alike, and have been polluting our environment','Our soil is also polluted through our use of weedicides to remove weeds.','These chemical are not abused at all','NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(320803,'biology','Which farming practices often use chemical methods to kill both useful and harmful life forms indiscriminately?','Conventional farming','Contrary to the ‘conventional’ farming practices which often use chemical methods to kill both useful and harmful life forms indiscriminately, organic farming is a holistic approach that seeks to develop an understanding of the webs of interaction between the myriad of organisms that constitute the field fauna and flora. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:186, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','fillup','Co,nve,ntio,nal fa,rm,ing',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(320804,'biology','Assertion: An important part of the biological farming approach is to become familiar with the various life forms that inhabit the field. \nReason: Knowledge of both the predators as well as pests, and also their life cycles, the patterns of feeding and the habitats that they prefer is essential for better understanding in biological farming','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','An important part of the biological farming approach is to become familiar with the various life forms that inhabit the field, predators as well as pests, and also their life cycles, patterns of feeding and the habitats that they prefer. This will help develop appropriate means of biocontrol. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320805,'biology','Aphids and mosquitoes can be gotten rid by- \n(i)Beetle with red and black markings – the Ladybird  \n(ii)Dragonflies \n(iii)Eagles','Both i and ii','The very familiar beetle with red and black markings – the Ladybird, and Dragonflies are useful to get rid of aphids and mosquitoes, respectively (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','Both ii and iii','Both i and iii','Both i and ii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320806,'biology','An example of microbial biocontrol agents that can be introduced in order to control butterfly caterpillars is','Bacillus thuringiensis','An example of microbial biocontrol agents that can be introduced in order to control butterfly caterpillars is the bacteria Bacillus thuringiensis (often written as Bt). (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','Bacillus thuringiensis','Saccharomyces cerevisiae','Bacillus anthrax','Lactobacillus','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320807,'biology','True about bacteria Bacillus thuringiensis- \n(i)These are available in sachets as dried spores which are mixed with water  \n(ii)Sprayed onto vulnerable plants such as brassicas and fruit trees, where these are eaten by the insect larvae.  \n(iii)The bacterial disease will kill the caterpillars, but leave other insects unharmed  \n(iv)These are harmful if ingested by humans or grazing animals','i, ii and iii','The bacteria Bacillus thuringiensis (often written as Bt) are available in sachets as dried spores which are mixed with water and sprayed onto vulnerable plants such as brassicas and fruit trees, where these are eaten by the insect larvae. In the gut of the larvae, the toxin is released and the larvae get killed. The bacterial disease will kill the caterpillars, but leave other insects unharmed. They don\'t harm humans or animals as they cannot be activated in their bodies.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','Both i and ii','i, ii and iii','i, iii and iv','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320808,'biology','Because of the development of methods of genetic engineering in the last decade or so, the scientists have introduced B. thuringiensis toxin genes into plants.','TRUE','The development of methods of genetic engineering in the last decade or so, resulted in the scientists introducing B. thuringiensis toxin genes into plants. Such plants are resistant to attack by insect pests. Bt-cotton is one such example, which is being cultivated in some states of our country. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320809,'biology','A biological control being developed for use in the treatment of plant disease is','Fungus- Trichoderma','A biological control being developed for use in the treatment of plant disease is the fungus Trichoderma (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','Bacteria- Trichoderma','Fungus- Trichomonas','Fungus- Trichoderma','Bacteria- Trichomonas','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320810,'biology','Trichoderma species are free-living fungi that are very common in the _____ ecosystems.','Root','Trichoderma species are free-living fungi that are very common in the root ecosystems. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','Root','Stem','Leaves','Flower','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320811,'biology','_______ are pathogens that attack insects and other arthropods','Baculoviruses','Baculoviruses are pathogens that attack insects and other arthropods (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','fillup','Ba,cul,ovi,rus,es',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320812,'biology','The majority of baculoviruses used as biological control agents are in the genus','Nucleopolyhedrovirus','Baculoviruses are pathogens that attack insects and other arthropods. The majority of baculoviruses used as biological control agents are in the genus Nucleopolyhedrovirus. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','fillup','Nu,cle,op,olyh,edr,ovir,us',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320813,'biology','Baculoviruses viruses are excellent candidates for- \n\n(i)Species-specific  \n\n(ii)Narrow spectrum insecticidal applications  \n\n(iii)Broad spectrum applications','Both i and ii','The majority of baculoviruses used as biological control agents are in the genus Nucleopolyhedrovirus. These viruses are excellent candidates for species-specific, narrow spectrum insecticidal applications. They have been shown to have no negative impacts on plants, mammals, birds, fish or even on non-target insects. This is especially desirable when beneficial insects are being conserved to aid in an overall integrated pest management (IPM) programme, or when an ecologically sensitive area is being treated. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','Both i and ii','Both i and iii','Both ii and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320814,'biology','Pick the correct statement about baculoviruses- \n\n(i)They have been shown to have no negative impacts on plants, mammals, birds, fish or even on non-target insects \n\n(ii)No negative impact is especially desirable when beneficial insects are being conserved to aid in an overall integrated pest management (IPM) programme, or when an ecologically sensitive area is being treated  \n\n(iii)Baculoviruses are pathogens that attack insects and other arthropods  \n\n(iv)They act like bacteriophages','i, ii and iii','They have been shown to have no negative impacts on plants, mammals, birds, fish or even on non-target insects. This is especially desirable when beneficial insects are being conserved to aid in an overall integrated pest management (IPM) programme, or when an ecologically sensitive area is being treated. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','Both i and iii','Both i and ii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320815,'biology','Assertion: A key belief of the organic farmer is that biodiversity furthers health.\n\nReason: Pests are not eradicated, but instead are kept at manageable levels by a complex system of checks and balances within a living and vibrant ecosystem in this system','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Biological control of pests and diseases: In agriculture, there is a method of controlling pests that relies on natural predation rather than introduced chemicals. A key belief of the organic farmer is that biodiversity furthers health. The more variety a landscape has, the more sustainable it is. The organic farmer, therefore, works to create a system where the insects that are sometimes called pests are not eradicated, but instead are kept at manageable levels by a complex system of checks and balances within a living and vibrant ecosystem. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:186, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(320816,'biology','How does organic farming greatly reduce use of chemical pesticides used in conventional farming?','By using biodiversity of pest-predator dynamic','The organic farmer holds the view that the eradication of the creatures that are often described as pests is not only possible, but also undesirable, for without them the beneficial predatory and parasitic insects which depend upon them as food or hosts would not be able to survive. Thus, the use of biocontrol measures will greatly reduce our dependence on toxic chemicals and pesticides. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','By being very vigilant in pest control by using chemical pesticides','By using biodiversity of pest-predator dynamic','By using modern technology to eradicate pests','By growing the plants in glass houses','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320817,'biology','Trichoderma is a ______ effective as biocontrol agent against ________','Free- living fungi, several plant pathogens','Trichoderma species are free-living fungi that are very common in the root ecosystems. They are effective biocontrol agents of several plant pathogens. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as biocontrol agents',NULL,'2','mcq','Free- living bacteria, several plant pathogens','Free- living fungi, several plant pathogens','Free- living fungi, several animal pathogens','Free- living bacteria, several animal pathogen','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(320901,'biology','There are problems associated with the overuse of chemical fertilisers and there is a large pressure to switch to','Organic farming','Baculoviruses viruses are excellent candidates for- (i)Species-specific  (ii)Narrow spectrum insecticidal applications  (iii)Board spectrum applications','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Organic farming','Conventional farming','Commercial farming','Mixed farming','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320902,'biology','Organic farming involves the use of','Biofertilizers','There is a large pressure to switch to organic farming – the use of biofertilizers. Biofertilizers are organisms that enrich the nutrient quality of the soil. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Chemicals','Synthetic chemicals','Biofertilizers','Organic lava soil','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(320903,'biology','Biofertilizers are ________ that enrich the nutrient quality of the soil.','Organisms','There is a large pressure to switch to organic farming – the use of biofertilizers. Biofertilizers are organisms that enrich the nutrient quality of the soil. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Chemicals','Soil','Organisms','Compost','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320904,'biology','The main sources of biofertilizers are- \n\n(i)Bacteria\n\n(ii)Fungi  \n\n(iii)Cyanobacteria \n\n(iv)Protozoa','i, ii and iii','The main sources of biofertilizers are bacteria, fungi and cyanobacteria. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Both i and ii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','i, ii and iv','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320905,'biology','Nodules on the roots of leguminous plants formed by the symbiotic association of','Rhizobium','The nodules on the roots of leguminous plants formed by the symbiotic association of Rhizobium (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','fillup','Rh,iz,obi,um',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320906,'biology','Rhizobium fix atmospheric ______ into organic forms, which is used by the plant as nutrient.','Nitrogen','The nodules on the roots of leguminous plants formed by the symbiotic association of Rhizobium. These bacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen into organic forms, which is used by the plant as nutrient. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxygen','Nitrogen','CO₂','Phosphorus','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320907,'biology','Which of the following  bacterias can fix atmospheric nitrogen while free-living in the soil and enriching the nitrogen content of the soil.\n(i)Azospirillum  \n(ii)Azatobacter\n(iii)Rhizobium\n(iv)Rhizopus\n(v) Agrobacterium','i and ii','The nodules on the roots of leguminous plants form by symbiotic association with bacteria,Rhizobium for nitrogen fixation which is not possible in free living form of the bcteria. These bacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen into organic forms, which is used by the plant as nutrient. Other bacteria can fix atmospheric nitrogen while free-living in the soil (examples Azospirillum and Azotobacter), thus enriching the nitrogen content of the soil. Rhizopus is a fungus and Agrobacterium is used for gene transfer in Biotechnology (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','i, ii and iii','i and ii','i, ii, iii and iv','i and iii','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320908,'biology','Fungi forms symbiotic associations with plants. Which term describe it?','Mycorrhiza','Fungi are also known to form symbiotic associations with plants\n(mycorrhiza) (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Symbiotic disassociation','Mutualism','Nodule formations','Mycorrhiza','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320909,'biology','Many members of the genus ______ form mycorrhiza.','Glomus','Many members of the genus Glomus form mycorrhiza. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','fillup','Glo,mus,ga',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320910,'biology','The fungal symbiont in these associations absorbs _______ from soil and passes it to the plant','Phosphorus','The fungal symbiont in these associations absorbs phosphorus from soil and passes it to the plant. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Nitrogen','Phosphorus','Sulphur','Iron','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320911,'biology','Plants having symbiotic associations show other benefits also, such as- \n\n(i)An overall increase in plant growth and development  \n\n(ii)Tolerance to salinity and drought  \n\n(iii)Resistance to root-borne pathogens  \n\n(iv)Improve soil quality','i, ii and iii','The fungal symbiont in these associations absorbs phosphorus from soil and passes it to the plant. Plants having such associations show other benefits also, such as resistance to root-borne pathogens, tolerance to salinity and drought and an overall increase in plant growth and development.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Both i and ii','i, ii and iv','i, ii, iii and iv','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320912,'biology','Cyanobacteria are','Autotrophic microbes','Cyanobacteria are autotrophic microbes widely distributed in aquatic and terrestrial environments many of which can fix atmospheric nitrogen, e.g. Anabaena, Nostoc, Oscillatoria (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Heterotrophic microbes','Autotrophic microbes','Homotropic microbes','Parasitic Microbes','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320913,'biology','Cyanobacteria are autotrophic microbes widely distributed in- \n\n(i)Aquatic environment  \n\n(ii)Terrestrial environment  \n\n(iii)Biomes','Both i and ii','Cyanobacteria are autotrophic microbes widely distributed in aquatic and terrestrial environments many of which can fix atmospheric nitrogen, e.g. Anabaena, Nostoc, Oscillatoria (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Both ii and iii','Both i and iii','Both i and ii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320914,'biology','Atmospheric nitrogen fixing bacteria are- \n\n(i)Anabaena  \n\n(ii)Nostoc  \n\n(iii)Oscillatoria  \n\n(iv)Nitrobacter','i, ii and iii','Cyanobacteria are autotrophic microbes widely distributed in aquatic and terrestrial environments many of which can fix atmospheric nitrogen, e.g. Anabaena, Nostoc, Oscillatoria (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Both i and iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii and iv','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320915,'biology','In paddy fields, cyanobacteria serve as an important','Biofertilizers','In paddy fields, cyanobacteria serve as an important bio fertilizer. Blue green algae also add organic matter to the soil and increase its fertility. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','fillup','Bio,fer,til,ize,rs,ana,ly',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320916,'biology','________ also add organic matter to the soil and increase its fertility','Blue green algae','Blue green algae also add organic matter to the soil and increase its fertility. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','Protozoan','Blue green algae','Slime moulds','Diatoms','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(320917,'biology','Environmental pollution in today\'s lifestyles is not only because of increased urbanisation, industrialisation but also continued use of chemical fertilizers degrading the soil.','TRUE','With our present day lifestyles, environmental pollution is a major cause of concern. The use of the chemical fertilisers to meet the ever-increasing demand of agricultural produce has contributed significantly to this pollution. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(260119,'chemistry','The solvent used for preparing materials like resins is_____?','Acetone','Some of carbonyl compounds are manufactured for use as solvents (i.e., acetone) and for preparing materials like adhesives, paints, resins, perfumes, plastics, fabrics, etc.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Ethanol','Acetonitrile','Ethyl acetate','Acetone','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(320918,'biology','Assertion: Biofertilizer are replacing chemical fertilizers. \nReason: Farmers are replacing them to replenish soil nutrients.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Currently, in our country, a number of biofertilizers are available commercially in the market and farmers use these regularly in their fields to replenish soil nutrients and to reduce dependence on chemical fertilisers. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Microbes as Biofertilizers',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(321001,'biology','Conversion of milk to curd improves its nutritional value by increasing the amount of [NEET 2018]','Vitamin B12','Lactobacillus bacteria convert milk into curd. It produces acids that coagulate and partially digest the milk proteins. A small amount of curd added to the fresh milk as inoculum or starter contains millions of Lactobacillus bacteria which at suitable temperature multiply, thereby converting milk to curd which also improves its nutritional value by increasing vitamin B12 content and a number of organic acids. A  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Vitamin D','Vitamin A','Vitamin B12','Vitamin E','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(321002,'biology','Match the following columns and select the correct option. [NEET 2020]','Clostridium butylicum-Butyric acid,Trichoderma polysporum-Cyclosporin A,Monascus purpureus-Blood cholesterol lowering agent,Aspergillus niger-Citric acid','Acid producers are Aspergillus niger (a fungus) of citric acid, Acetobacter aceti (a bacterium) of acetic acid; Clostridium butylicum (a bacterium) of butyric acid and Lactobacillus (a bacterium) of lactic acid. Cyclosporin A, used as an immunosuppressive agent in organ-transplant patients, is produced by the fungus Trichoderma polysporum. Statins produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus have been commercialised as blood-cholesterol lowering agents. It acts by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for synthesis of cholesterol. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:2 and page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','match','Clostridium butylicum,Butyric acid','Trichoderma polysporum,Cyclosporine A','Monascus purpureus,Blood cholesterol lowering agent','Aspergillus niger,Citric acid','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321003,'biology','Which of the following is a commercial blood cholesterol lowering agent? [NEET 2019]','Statin','Statins  are  products  of  fermentation  activity  of  yeast Monascus purpureus which resemble mevalonate and are  competitive  inhibitors  of  b–hydroxy–b–methylglutaryl or HMG CoA reductase. This inhibits cholesterol synthesis. Statins  are,  therefore,  used  in  lowering  blood  cholesterol,  e.g., lovastatin, pravastatin, simvastatin. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Lipases','Cyclosporin A','Statin','Streptokinase','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321004,'biology','Match the following organisms with the products they produce. [NEET 2019]','Lactobacillus-Curd,Saccharomyces cerevisiae-Bread,Aspergillus niger-Citric acid,Acetobacter aceti-Acetic acid','Acid producers are Aspergillus niger (a fungus) of citric acid, Acetobacter aceti (a bacterium) of acetic acid; Clostridium butylicum (a bacterium) of butyric acid and Lactobacillus (a bacterium) of lactic acid and curd. Saccharomyces cerevisiae used for bread-making and commonly called brewer’s yeast, is used for fermenting malted cereals and fruit juices, to produce ethanol. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:2 and page no:182, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','match','Lactobacillus,Curd','Saccharomyces cerevisiae,Bread','Aspergillus niger,Citric acid','Acetobacter aceti,Acetic acid','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(321005,'biology','Which of the following is correctly matched for the product produced by them? [NEET 2017]','Saccharomyces cerevisiae : Ethanol','Methanobacterium is useful in the production of biogas. Methanogens, one such common bacterium is Methanobacterium. These bacteria are commonly found in the anaerobic sludge during sewage treatment. They are also present in the rumen (a part of stomach) of cattle. Penicillium notatum is used to produce penicillin, an  antibiotic. Alexander Fleming found out that it was due to a chemical produced by the mould and he named it Penicillin after the mould Penicillium notatum. Acetobacter  aceti  is  used  to  obtain  acetic  acid.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:185, paragraph no:4 and page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Methanobacterium : Lactic acid','Penicillium notatum : Acetic acid','Saccharomyces cerevisiae : Ethanol','Acetobacter aceti : Antibiotics','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(321006,'biology','Match column I with column II and select the correct option using the codes given below. [NEET-II 2016]','Citric acid-Aspergillus,Cyclosporin A-Trichoderma,Statins-Monascus,Butyric acid-Clostridium','Acid producers are Aspergillus niger (a fungus) of citric acid, Acetobacter aceti (a bacterium) of acetic acid; Clostridium butylicum (a bacterium) of butyric acid and Lactobacillus (a bacterium) of lactic acid. Cyclosporin A, used as an immunosuppressive agent in organ-transplant patients, is produced by the fungus Trichoderma polysporum. Statins produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus have been commercialised as blood-cholesterol lowering agents. It acts by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for synthesis of cholesterol. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:2 and page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','match','Citric acid,Aspergillus','Cyclosporin A,Trichoderma','Statins,Monascus','Butyric acid,Clostridium','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321007,'biology','Which of the following is wrongly matched in the given diagram table? (3210)','4','Clostridium  butylicum  helps  in  the  production  of   butyric   acid.   Candida lipolytica and Geotrichum candidum help in production of lipases that are added in detergents for removing oily stains from laundry.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:, paragraph no:)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-184','12-154','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/321007q.png'),
(241805,'biology','Assertion: Tapetum is located in the inner most side of the wall layers surrounding microsporangium.\n\nReason: This is because it helps dehiscence of anther to release the pollen.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','The innermost wall layer is the tapetum. It nourishes the developing pollen grains. (Reference  : NCERT, page 21, paragraph 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-021',NULL,NULL),
(321008,'biology','Match the following list of microbes and their importance. [AIPMT 2015]','Saccharomyces cerevisiae-Commercial production of ethanol,Monascus purpureus-Production of blood cholesterol lowering agents,Trichoderma polysporum-Production of immunosuppressive agent,Propionibacterium shermanii-Ripening of Swiss cheese','Baker\'s yeast" is the general term given to all forms of Saccharomyces cerevisiae used to make bread products. Cyclosporin A, used as an immunosuppressive agent in organ-transplant patients, is produced by the fungus Trichoderma polysporum. Statins produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus have been commercialised as blood-cholesterol lowering agents. It acts by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for synthesis of cholesterol. The large holes in ‘Swiss cheese’ are due to production of a large amount of CO₂  by a bacterium named Propionibacterium shermanii (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:181, paragraph no:3 and page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','match','Saccharomyces cerevisiae,Commercial production of ethanol','Monascus purpureus,Production of blood cholesterol lowering agents','Trichoderma polysporum,Production of immunosuppressive agent','Propionibacterium shermanii,Ripening of Swiss cheese','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(321009,'biology','A good producer of citric acid is [AIPMT 2013]','Aspergillus','Aspergillus niger     produces     citric     acid,     Clostridium      butylicum      produces      butyric      acid,      Saccharomyces  is  used  for  commercial  production  of  ethanol, Pseudomonas produces alkaline proteases. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:2','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Clostridium','Saccharomyces','Aspergillus','Pseudomonas.','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321010,'biology','Which of the following is put into anaerobic sludge digester for further sewage treatment? [NEET 2020]','Activated sludge','The sediment in a settling tank is called activated sludge. A small part of it is pumped back into the aeration tank to serve as the inoculum. While the remaining major part of the sludge is pumped into large tanks called anaerobic sludge digesters. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Primary sludge','Floating debris','Effluents of primary treatment','Activated sludge','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321011,'biology','Which of the following in sewage treatment removes suspended solids? [NEET 2017]','Primary treatment','In Primary treatment steps basically involve physical removal of particles – large and small – from the sewage through filtration and sedimentation. These are removed in stages; initially, floating debris is removed by sequential filtration. Then the grit (soil and small pebbles) are removed by sedimentation (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Secondary treatment','Primary treatment','Sludge treatment','Tertiary treatment','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321012,'biology','What gases are produced in anaerobic sludge digesters? [AIPMT 2014]','Methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂','The type of gases produced by microbial activity depend upon the microbes and the organic substrates they utilise. Certain bacteria, called methanogens, grow anaerobically on cellulosic material and produce large amount of methane along with  CO₂ . These bacteria are commonly found in the anaerobic sludge during sewage treatment. The remaining major part of the sludge is pumped into large tanks called anaerobic sludge digesters. Here, other kinds of bacteria, which grow anaerobically, digest the bacteria and the fungi in the sludge. During this digestion, bacteria produce a mixture of gases such as methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂ . (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Methane and  CO₂  only','Methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂','Methane, Hydrogen sulphide and Oxygen','Hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321013,'biology','The domestic sewage in large cities [AIPMT 2012]','Has very high amount of suspended solids and dissolved salts.','Sewage water can be purified by passing it through sewage treatment plants with the action of heterotrophic microorganisms. There are 2 stages of this treatment – primary and secondary. Primary treatment removes floating and suspended solids from sewage through two processes of filtration and sedimentation. First floating matter is removed through sequential filtration. The filtrate is kept in large open settling tanks where grit settles down. The sediment is called primary sludge while the supernatant is called effluent. The primary sludge traps a lot of microbes and debris. In secondary treatment, the primary effluent is taken to aeration tanks. A large number of aerobic heterotrophic microbes grow in the aeration tank. They form flocs. Flocs are masses of bacteria held together by slime and fungal filaments to form mesh like structures. The microbes digest a lot of organic matter, converting it into microbial biomass and releasing a lot of minerals. As the BOD of the waste matter is reduced to 10-15% of raw sewage, it is passed into settling tank. The sediment of settling tank is called activated sludge. The remaining is passed into a large tank called anaerobic sludge digester. It is designed for continuous operation. The aerobic microbes present in the sludge get killed. Anaerobic microbes digest the organic mass as well as aerobic microbes of the sludge. They are of two types, non-methanogenic and methanogenic. Methanogenic bacteria produce a mixture of gases containing methane, H2S and CO₂ . (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Has a high BOD as it contains both aerobic and anaerobic bacteria.','Is processed by aerobic and then anaerobic bacteria in the secondary treatment in Sewage Treatment Plants (STPs).','When treated in STPs does not really require the aeration step as the sewage contains adequate oxygen.','Has very high amount of suspended solids and dissolved salts.','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321014,'biology','Which of the following is mainly produced by the activity of anaerobic bacteria on sewage? [AIPMT 2011]','Marsh gas','Marsh gas or methane gas is mainly produced by the activities of anaerobic bacteria on sewage. Sewage contains large amounts of organic matter and microbes, many of which are pathogenic. These microbes (bacteria and fungi) are digested during secondary treatment process of sewage by anaerobic bacteria. During digestion, bacteria produce a mixture of gases such as methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂ . These gases are called biogas and can be easily used as a source of energy as it is inflammable. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Laughing gas','Propane','Mustard gas','Marsh gas','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321015,'biology','The guts of cow and buffalo possess [AIPMT 2015 Cancelled]','Methanogens','Methanogens like Methanobacterium are found in the rumen (a part of the stomach) of cattle. A lot of cellulosic material is also available in the rumen. In rumen, these bacteria help in the breakdown of cellulose and play an important role in nutrition of cattle. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Methanogens','Cyanobacteria','Fucus sp.','Chlorella sp','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(321016,'biology','Which of the following can be used as a biocontrol agent in the treatment of plant disease? [NEET 2019]','Trichoderma','Trichoderma species are free-living fungi that are very common in the root ecosystems. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Lactobacillus','Trichoderma','Chlorella','Anabaena','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321017,'biology','Select the correct group of biocontrol agents. [NEET 2019]','Trichoderma, Baculovirus, Bacillus thuringiensis','Trichoderma species are free-living fungi that are very common in the root ecosystems. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:3)Baculoviruses are pathogens that attack insects and other arthropods (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:4)An example of microbial biocontrol agents that can be introduced in order to control butterfly caterpillars is the bacteria Bacillus thuringiensis (often written as Bt). (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Nostoc, Azospirillum, Nucleopolyhedrovirus','Bacillus thuringiensis, Tobacco mosaic virus, Aphids','Trichoderma, Baculovirus, Bacillus thuringiensis','Oscillatoria, Rhizobium, Trichoderma','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321018,'biology','A biocontrol agent to be a part of an integrated pest management should be [Odisha NEET 2019]','Species-specific and inactive on non-target organisms.','The majority of baculoviruses used as biological control agents are in the genus Nucleopolyhedrovirus. These viruses are excellent candidates for species-specific, narrow spectrum insecticidal applications. They have been shown to have no negative impacts on plants, mammals, birds, fish or even on non-target insects. This is especially desirable when beneficial insects are being conserved to aid in an overall integrated pest management (IPM) programme, or when an ecologically sensitive area is being treated. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Species-specific and symbiotic.','Free living and broad spectrum.','Narrow spectrum and symbiotic.','Species-specific and inactive on non-target organisms.','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321019,'biology','Microbe used for biocontrol of pest butterfly caterpillars is [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Bacillus thuringiensis','Biopesticides are those biological agents that are used for control of weeds, insects and pathogens. The microorganisms used as biopesticides are viruses, bacteria, protozoa, fungi and mites. Some of the biopesticides are being used at a commercial scale. Most important example is the soil bacterium, Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt). Spores of this bacterium produce the insecticidal cry protein. Therefore, spores of this bacterium kill larvae of insects which eat them. An example of microbial biocontrol agents that can be introduced in order to control butterfly caterpillars is the bacteria Bacillus thuringiensis (often written as Bt). (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Saccharomyces cerevisiae','Bacillus thuringiensis','Streptococcus sp.','Trichoderma sp.','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321020,'biology','Which one of the following is an example of carrying out biological control of pests/diseases using microbes? [AIPMT 2012]','Trichoderma sp. against certain plant pathogens.','A biological control being developed for use in the treatment of plant disease is the fungus Trichoderma. Trichoderma species are free living fungi that are very common in the root ecosystems. They are effective biocontrol agents of several plant pathogens. Trichoderma species are free-living fungi that are very common in the root ecosystems. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Trichoderma sp. against certain plant pathogens.','Nucleopolyhedrovirus against white rust in Brassica','Bt-cotton to increase cotton yield','Lady bird beetle against aphids in mustard.','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321021,'biology','Select the mismatch. [NEET 2017]','Rhodospirillum– Mycorrhiza','Rhodospirillum is a free-living nitrogen fixing bacteria. Mycorrhiza is the symbiotic association between fungi and roots of higher plants. The most common fungal partners of mycorrhiza are Glomus species. Fungi are also known to form symbiotic associations with plants (mycorrhiza) (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Rhodospirillum– Mycorrhiza','Anabaena– Nitrogen fixer','Rhizobium– Alfalfa','Frankia– Alni','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(321022,'biology','Which one of the following statements is correct?','Legumes fix nitrogen only through the specialized bacteria that live in their roots','The nitrogen-fixing ability of leguminous plants is not a property of the plants as such but results from infection of their roots by bacteria in the soil, infection leading to the formation of nodules. These organisms are Gram-negative motile rods that are classified in the genus Rhizobium. The nodules on the roots of leguminous plants formed by the symbiotic association of Rhizobium. These bacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen into organic forms, which is used by the plant as nutrient. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Legumes fix nitrogen only through the specialized bacteria that live in their roots','Legumes fix nitrogen independently of the specialized bacteria that live in their roots.','Legumes fix nitrogen only through specialized bacteria that live in their leaves.','Legumes are incapable of fixing nitrogen.','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(321023,'biology','Match List-I with List-II with regards to the diagram table -NEET 2021 \n(3211)','3','Acid producers are Aspergillus niger (a fungus) of citric acid, Acetobacter aceti (a bacterium) of acetic acid; Clostridium butylicum (a bacterium) of butyric acid and Lactobacillus (a bacterium) of lactic acid. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-183','12-153','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/321023q.png'),
(321024,'biology','A river with an inflow of domestic sewage rich in organic waste may result in________ [ NEET 2016]','Death of fish due to lack of O₂','In the sewage, the microbes while growing consume the major part of the organic matter in the effluent. This significantly reduces the BOD (biochemical O₂ demand) of the effluent,thus reducing the O₂ available for the other aquatic life.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Drying of the river very soon due to algal bloom','Increased population of aquatic food web organisms','An increased production of fish due to biodegradable nutrients','Death of fish due to lack of O₂','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321025,'biology','Monascus purpureus is a yeast used commercially in the production of [AIPMT 2012]','Blood cholesterol lowering statins','Statins produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus have been commercialised as blood-cholesterol lowering agents. It acts by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for synthesis of cholesterol. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:2 and page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ethanol','Streptokinase for removing clots from the blood vessels','Citric acid','Blood cholesterol lowering statins','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321026,'biology','A nitrogen-fixing microbe associated with Azolla in rice fields is [AIPMT 2012]','Anabaena','Cyanobacteria are autotrophic microbes widely distributed in aquatic and terrestrial environments many of which can fix atmospheric nitrogen, e.g. Anabaena, Nostoc, Oscillatoria (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Spirulina','Anabaena','Frankia','Tolypothrix','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(321027,'biology','Which one of the following is an example of carrying out biological control of pests/diseases using microbes? [AIPMT 2012]','Trichoderma species against certain plant pathogens','Cyclosporin A, used as an immunosuppressive agent in organ-transplant patients, is produced by the fungus Trichoderma polysporum (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Trichoderma species against certain plant pathogens','Nucleopolyhedrovirus against white rust in Brassica','Bt-cotton to increase cotton yield','Lady bird beetle against aphids in mustard','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321028,'biology','In gobar gas, the maximum amount is that of [AIPMT 2012]','Methane','In anaerobic sludge digesters in biogas plant, different kinds of bacteria, which grow anaerobically, digest the bacteria and the fungi in the sludge. During this digestion, bacteria produce a mixture of gases such as methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂ . Major gas among these is methane, which is about 85%(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Butane','Methane','Propane','CO₂','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321029,'biology','The most common substrate used in distilleries for the production of ethanol is [AIPMT 2011]','Molasses','Saccharomyces cerevisiae used for bread-making and commonly called brewer’s yeast, is used for fermenting malted cereals and fruit juices which are nothing but molasses, to produce ethanol. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Corn meal','Soya meal','Ground gram','Molasses','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(321030,'biology','Which one of the following is not a biofertilizer? [AIPMT 2011]','Agrobacterium','Biofertilizers are those biological agents capable to fix nitrogen and restore soil nutrients. The nodules on the roots of leguminous plants formed by the symbiotic association of Rhizobium, bacteria which fix atmospheric nitrogen into organic forms. Fungi are also known to form symbiotic associations with plants(mycorrhiza). Cyanobacteria are another autotrophic microbes widely distributed in aquatic and terrestrial environments many of which can fix atmospheric nitrogen, e.g. Anabaena, Nostoc, Oscillatoria(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Agrobacterium','Rhizobium','Nostoc','Mycorrhiza','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(321031,'biology','An organism used as a biofertilizer for raising soyabean crop is [AIPMT 2011]','Rhizobium','The nodules on the roots of leguminous plants formed by the symbiotic association of Rhizobium. These bacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen into organic forms, which is used by the plant as nutrient. Other bacteria can fix atmospheric nitrogen while free-living in the soil (examples Azospirillum and Azotobacter), thus enriching the nitrogen content of the soil. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Azotobacter','Azospirillum','Rhizobium','Nostoc','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(321032,'biology','Organisms called methanogens are most abundant in [AIPMT 2011]','Cattle yard','Methanogens like Methanobacterium are found in the rumen (a part of the stomach) of cattle. A lot of cellulosic material is also available in the rumen. In rumen, these bacteria help in the breakdown of cellulose and play an important role in nutrition of cattle. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:185, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Sulphur rock','Cattle yard','Polluted stream','Hot spring','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(321033,'biology','Identify the microorganism which is responsible for the production of an immunosuppressive molecule cyclosporin A :[NEET 2022]','Trichoderma polysporum','Bioactive molecule, cyclosporin A, that is used as an immunosuppressive agent in organ transplant patients, is produced by the fungus, Trichoderma polysporum.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Trichoderma polysporum','Streptococcus cerevisiae','Aspergillus niger','Clostridium butylicum','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321034,'biology','Select the correct statements regarding dissolved \nOxygen and Biochemical Oxygen Demand. \n (A) BOD is inversely related to dissolved oxygen. \n (B) Low dissolved oxygen and high BOD lead to \nloss of aquatic life. \n (C) High BOD leads to high dissolved oxygen. \n (D) Both BOD and dissolved oxygen are indicator of \nhealth of a water body. \n (E) Both BOD and dissolved oxygen are affected by \namount of organic matter in the water body. \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the \noptions given below : [ NEET 2023]','(2) (A), (B), (D), (E) only','The correct statements regarding dissolved oxygen (DO) and biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) are as follows:\n\n(A) BOD is inversely related to dissolved oxygen: BOD is a measure of the amount of oxygen consumed by microorganisms while decomposing organic matter in water. As BOD increases, the amount of dissolved oxygen decreases.\n\n(B) Low dissolved oxygen and high BOD lead to the loss of aquatic life: Aquatic organisms require dissolved oxygen for respiration. When dissolved oxygen levels decrease due to high BOD, it can lead to oxygen depletion in the water, causing stress or death to aquatic organisms.\n\n(D) Both BOD and dissolved oxygen are indicators of the health of a water body: BOD and dissolved oxygen levels provide valuable information about the quality and health of a water body. High BOD and low dissolved oxygen levels indicate pollution and poor water quality.\n\n(E) Both BOD and dissolved oxygen are affected by the amount of organic matter in the water body: BOD measures the oxygen demand of organic matter, and the decomposition of organic matter consumes oxygen, reducing dissolved oxygen levels in the water.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(2) (A), (B), (D), (E) only','(1) (A), (B), (C), (E) only','(3) (A), (B), (C), (D) only','(4) (B), (C), (D), (E) only','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321035,'biology','Match Column-I with Column-II. \nColumn-I Column-II\n(A) Nitrococcus (I) Denitrification\n(B) Rhizobium (II) Conversion of ammonia to nitrite \n(C) Thiobacillus (III) Conversion of nitrite to nitrate \n(D) Nitrobacter (IV) Conversion of atmospheric nitrogen to ammonia [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) (A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)','(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)','(2) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','(3) (A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)','(4) (A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
