(260120,'chemistry','The solvent used for preparing materials like resins is_____?','Acetone','Some of carbonyl compounds are manufactured for use as solvents (i.e., acetone) and for preparing materials like adhesives, paints, resins, perfumes, plastics, fabrics, etc.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-358 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Ethyl acetate','Methanol','Acetone','Diethyl ether','NEW','12-358','12-228',NULL),
(321101,'biology','Chloramphenicol and erythromycin (broad spectrum antibiotics) are produced by [AIIMS 2014]','Streptomyces','Alexander Fleming found out that it was due to a chemical produced by the mould and he named it Penicillin after the mould Penicillium notatum. However, its full potential as an effective antibiotic was established much later by Ernest Chain and Howard Florey. After Penicillin, other antibiotics were also purified from other microbes. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Streptomyces','Nitrobacter','Rhizobium','Penicillium','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(321102,'biology','A patient brought to a hospital with myocardial infarction is normally immediately given: [AIIMS 2014]','Streptokinase','Streptokinase produced by the bacterium Streptococcus and modified by genetic engineering is used as a ‘clot buster’ for removing clots from the blood vessels of patients who have undergone myocardial infarction leading to heart attack. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:183, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Penicillin','Streptokinase','Cyclosporin-A','Statins','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321103,'biology','Microbes are used in -[AIIMS 2015]\n \n1. Primary treatment of sewage\n\n2. Secondary treatment of sewage \n\n3. Anaerobic sludge digester\n\n4. Production of bioactive molecules','2, 3 and 4','Microbes are not used in the primary treatment of sewage which involves settling down of sludge through sedimentation. Primary treatment steps basically involve physical removal of particles – large and small – from the sewage  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1, 3 and 4','1, 2, 3 and 4','2, 3 and 4','3 and 4','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321104,'biology','Match the following to setup right combination. [AIIMS 2016]','Escherichia coli-Production of human insulin,Rhizobium melilotae-Nif gene,Bacillus thuringiensis-Biodecomposed insectiside,Pseudomonas putida-Digestive hydrocarbon of crude oil','Recombinant human insulin is mainly produced either in E. coli or Saccharomyces cerevisiae. The E. coli expression system, is used to produce insulin precursors (IP) as inclusion bodies and fully functional insulin polypeptides are obtained finally by further processing. The nodules on the roots of leguminous plants formed by the symbiotic association of Rhizobium. These bacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen into organic forms, which is used by the plant as nutrient. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:188, paragraph no:1)The development of methods of genetic engineering in the last decade or so, resulted in the scientists introducing B. thuringiensis toxin genes into plants. Such plants are resistant to attack by insect pests. Bt-cotton is one such example, which is being cultivated in some states of our country. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:2). Pseudomonas putida, a gram negative organism is able to degrade many aromatic compounds like the hydrocarbon of crude oil.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','match','Escherichia coli,Production of human insulin','Rhizobium melilotae,Nif gene','Bacillus thuringiensis,Biodecomposed insecticide','Pseudomonas putida,Digestive hydrocarbon of crude oil','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(321105,'biology','What would happen if O₂ availability to activated sludge flocs is reduced? [AIIMS 2016]','The centre of flocs will become anoxic, which would cause death of bacteria and eventually breakage of flocs.','In the secondary treatment of sewage, if O₂ availability to activated sludge flocs (masses of bacteria associated with fungal filaments to form mesh like structure) is reduced; the centre of flocs will becomes anoxic, which would cause death of bacteria and eventually breakage of flocs. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','It will slow down the rate of degradation of organic matter.','The centre of flocs will become anoxic, which would cause death of bacteria and eventually breakage of flocs.','Flocs would increase in size as anaerobic bacteria would grow around flocs.','Protozoa would grow in large numbers.','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321106,'biology','Which one of the following statement regarding BOD is true? [AIIMS 2017]','The greater the BOD of waste water, more is its polluting potential.','BOD is the method of determining the amount of O₂ required by microorganisms to decompose the waste present in the water supply. It is a measure of organic matter present in the water. If the quantity of organic wastes in the water supply is high then the number of decomposing bacteria present in the water will also be high. As a result, BOD value will increase. The BOD test measures the rate of uptake of O₂ by microorganisms in a sample of water and thus, indirectly, BOD is a measure of the organic matter present in the water. The greater the BOD of waste water, more is its polluting potential. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:184, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The greater the BOD of waste water, more is its polluting potential.','The greater the BOD of waste water, less is its polluting potential.','The lesser the BOD of waste water, more is its polluting potential.','All of them.','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321107,'biology','The free-living fungus Trichoderma can be used for [AIIMS 2017]','Biological control of plant diseases','Trichoderma is a free-living saprophytic fungi that most commonly lives on dead organic matter in the soil and rhizosphere (root ecosystem). It inhibits pathogens through release of gliotoxin, viridin, gliovirin and trichodermin like substances. trichoderma species are free-living fungi that are very common in the root ecosystems. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:187, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Killing insects','Biological control of plant diseases','Controlling butterfly caterpillars','Producing antibiotics','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321108,'biology','Assertion : Vitamins B2 is found in cereals, green vegetables, brewer\'s yeast, egg white, milk and liver. \n\nReason : It can be commercially produced by some yeasts. [AIIMS 2014]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Alexander Fleming found out that it was due to a chemical produced by the mould and he named it Penicillin after the mould Penicillium notatum. However, its full potential as an effective antibiotic was established much later by Ernest Chain and Howard Florey. After Penicillin, other antibiotics were also purified from other microbes. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:182, paragraph no:3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(321201,'biology','Which of the following statement(s) is true about bacteriophage virus?\n\n1. They are small group of viruses who do not possess any shape\n\n2. They have very well defined shape\n\n3. Their body consists of head, collar, tale, plate, pins and prongs','2 and 3 are correct','Bacteriophage are group of viruses have well defined shape. It consists of head,, collar, tale, plate, pins and prongs. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, page no 2, paragraph no 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2 and 3 are correct','Only 1 is correct','Only 2 is correct','Only 3 is correct','NEW','12-180','12-150',NULL),
(321202,'biology','Choose the correct one?\n\n1. Cyclosporine A is bioactive molecule used as an immunosuppressive agent by the patients who undergo organ transplant \n\n2. Cephalosporin is bioactive agent used by the organ transplanted patients as a part of immunosuppressive therapy\n\n3. Cyclosporine X is bioactive molecule used as an immunosuppressive agent by the patients who undergo cardiac transplant','Only 1 is correct','Cyclosporine A is bioactive molecule used as an immunosuppressive agent by the patients who undergo organ transplant. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, page no 5, paragraph no 4) Cephalosporins are newer antibiotics belonging to the Penicillin Family with similar mechanism.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Only 2 is correct','Only 3 is correct','All are correct','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321203,'biology','Assertion: Ladybird and dragonflies are good bioactive agents. \n\nReason: Ladybird and dragonflies consume mosquitoes and insects that act as vectors.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Ladybird and dragonflies protect plants from hazardous insects and mosquitoes. (Reference  :  NCERT Biology Class 12, page no 9, paragraph no 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321204,'biology','Assertion: Chemical fertilizers pose a great threat to increasing pollution. \n\nReason: In chemical fertilizers, toxic chemicals are used and they are maginified in each trophic level.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In chemical fertilizers, hazardous chemicals are used which releases poisonous gasses. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, page no 10, paragraph no 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(321205,'biology','Assertion: Antibiotics are extracted from microbes and can kill/inhibit other microbes. \n\nReason: Antibiotics are a lethal discovery','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Antibiotics are extracted from some microbes and have ability to kill other microbes for survival. The dicovery of Penicillin was a boon for many,in treating infections and reducing death due to infections. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, page no:4, paragraph no 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(321206,'biology','Assertion: Sewage water sometimes is good for health ,as it contain some beneficial microbes. \n\nReason: All Microbes are beneficial for human health','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Sewage water is hazardous for human health. It undergone a particular treatment where it become less polluted. Later, this water is drained into river/sea.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, page no 6, paragraph no 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321207,'biology','Assertion: All microbes can grow in the presence of oxygen. \n\nReason: All microbes need O₂ to grow and are obligate aerobes','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Different microbes have different requirement. Some microbes grow in the presence of O₂ while some are killed.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, page no 4, paragraph no 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(321208,'biology','Assertion: Microbes are very harmful, they need to be eradicated completely from biological ecosystem. \n\nReason: They pose a great threat to human, animals and plants and are always pathological.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Microorganisms are very beneficial and significant if use in a proper way. They helps in various sectors of life i.e. Medicine, industry, senator and dairy. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, page no 4, paragraph no 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(321209,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Some microbes can survive in thermal vents\n\n2) Fungi is a type of microbe\n\n3) Prions are non-infectious microbes\n\n4) Microbes cannot survive in environment of very low pH value.','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Microbes are present even at sites where no other life-form could possibly exist–sites such  as deep inside the geysers (thermal vents) where the temperature may be as high as 100 degree celsius\nMicrobes are diverse–protozoa, bacteria, fungi and microscopic plant viruses, viroids and also prions that are proteinacious infectious agents\nMicrobes can survive in extreme conditions such as in highly acidic environments\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 179, para 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-179','12-149',NULL),
(321210,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\nAssertion: Microbial cultures grown on nutritive media can be seen by naked eyes\n\nReason: They form coloured colonies on the media','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Microbes like bacteria and many fungi can be grown on nutritive media to form colonies that can be seen with the naked eyes. Such cultures are useful in studies on micro-organisms.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 179, para 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-179','12-149',NULL),
(321211,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes in Household Products, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Lactic acid bacteria (LAB) cannot survive in intestine of human due to high acidity\n\n2) Lactobacillus is an example of LAB\n\n3) LAB converts milk into curd\n\n4) Curd is added into milk as inoculum instead of directly adding LAB','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','In our stomach too, the LAB play very beneficial role in checking disease causing microbes.\nMicro-organisms such as Lactobacillus and others commonly called lactic acid bacteria (LAB)\nLactic acid bacteria (LAB) grow in milk and convert it to curd\nA small amount of curd added to the fresh milk as inoculum or starter contain millions of LAB, which at suitable temperatures multiply, thus converting milk to curd\nLactobacillus can grow in the intestine if introduced, but cannot grow in the stomach because of high acidity\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 181, para 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(321212,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\nAssertion: LAB helps to improve nutritional quality of milk\n\nReason: It increases amount of calcium','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','A small amount of curd added to the fresh milk as inoculum or starter contain millions of LAB, which at suitable temperatures multiply, thus converting milk to curd, which also improves its nutritional quality by increasing vitamin B12\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 181, para 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(321213,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes in Household Products, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The puffed-up appearance of dough is due to the production of O₂ gas.\n\n2) The dough, which is used for making bread, is fermented using Propionibacterium sharmani\n\n3) The large holes in ‘Swiss cheese’ are due to production of a large amount of CO₂ \n\n4) The ‘Roquefort cheese’ are ripened by growing a specific fungi on them','Only 3 and 4 are correct','The puffed-up appearance of dough is due to the production of CO₂  gas\nThe dough, which is used for making bread, is fermented using baker’s yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae)\nThe large holes in ‘Swiss cheese’ are due to production of a large amount of CO by a bacterium named Propionibacterium sharmanii\nThe ‘Roquefort cheese’ are ripened by growing a specific fungi on them, which gives them a particular flavour\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 181, para 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(321214,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\nAssertion: Different types of cheese have distinct texture, flavour and taste\n\nReason: Different species of Lactic Acid Bacteria are added','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Different varieties of cheese are known by their characteristic texture, flavour and taste, the specificity coming from the microbes used. LAB is not added to make cheese\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 181, para 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(321215,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes in Industrial Products, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Fermentors are used to grow microbes in a very large scale\n\n2) Microbes can be used to synthesize beverages\n\n3) Antibiotics are products derived from plants\n\n4) Microbes are used in the produce of valuable things for human','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Production on an industrial scale, requires growing microbes in very large vessels called fermentors\nEven in industry, microbes are used to synthesise a number of products valuable to human beings. Beverages and antibiotics are some examples\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 181, para 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(321216,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\nAssertion: During fermentation of the dough of bread, we can observe an increase in the size of dough\n\nReason: It is due to the production of CO₂  gas','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The dough, which is used for making bread, is fermented using baker’s yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae).The puffed-up appearance of dough is due to the production of CO₂  gas. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 181, para 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-181','12-151',NULL),
(51040,'chemistry','Which of these statements are incorrect?\n\na) During random motion of gases, they collide with each other and with the walls of the container.\n\nb) Pressure is exerted by gases as a result of collision of the particles with the walls of the container.','none of these','During random motion of gases, they collide with each other and with the walls of the container. b) Pressure is exerted by gases as a result of collision of the particles with the walls of the container.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 149; Paragraph No. 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b only','both a and b','none of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(321217,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Fermented Beverages, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Fermentation of malted cereals will produce methanol\n\n2) Wine is produced without distillation\n\n3) Saccharomyces cerevisiae can be used to ferment fruit juice\n\n4) Different types of alcoholic drinks are made depending on type of microbe used for fermentation','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Brewer’s yeast is used for fermenting malted cereals and fruit juices, to produce ethanol\nWine and beer are produced without distillation whereas whisky, brandy and rum are produced by distillation of the fermented broth\nFor this purpose the same yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae used for bread-making and commonly called brewer’s yeast, is used for fermenting malted cereals and fruit juices\nDepending on the type of the raw material used for fermentation and the type of processing (with or without distillation) different types of alcoholic drinks are obtained\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 182, para 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(321218,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Propionibacterium sharmani is also known as brewer\'s yeast\n\nReason: It is used to produce fruit juice during brewing','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','The same yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae used for bread-making and commonly called brewer’s yeast, is used for fermenting malted cereals and fruit juices, to produce ethanol.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 182, para 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(321219,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Antibiotics, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Antibiotics cannot kill microbes\n\n2) Antibiotics are natural substances derived from plants\n\n3) Antibiotics can inhibit the growth of disease causing microbes\n\n4) Antibiotics are produced by microbes to work against other microbes','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Antibiotics can kill or retard the growth of other (disease-causing) microbes.\nAntibiotics are chemical substances, which are produced by some microbes\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 182, para 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(321220,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Antibiotics are chemical substances\n\nReason: They are primarily manufactured by humans','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','Antibiotics are chemical substances, which are produced by some microbes and can kill or retard the growth of other (disease-causing) microbes.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 182, para 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(321221,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Antibiotics, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Alexander Fleming discovered the first antibiotic\n\n2) Penicillin is an example of antibiotic\n\n3) The first antibiotic is named after the mould Penicillium notatum\n\n4) Fleming discovered the antibiotic Ampicillin','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Penicillin was the first antibiotic to be discovered and by Alexander Fleming\nPenicillin is a commonly used antibiotic\nHe found out that it was due to a chemical produced by the mould and he named it Penicillin after the mould Penicillium notatum\nAlexander Fleming discovered Penicillin while working on Staphylococci bacteria\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 182, para 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(321222,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\nAssertion: Penicillin is said to be the antibiotic for Staphylococci bacteria\n\nReason: Penicillin is a chemical substance produced by Penicillium notatum','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Alexander Fleming while working on Staphylococci bacteria, once observed a mould growing in one of his unwashed culture plates around which Staphylococci could not grow. He found out that it was due to a chemical produced by the mould and he named it Penicillin after the mould Penicillium notatum\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 182, para 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-182','12-152',NULL),
(321223,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Lactobacillus produces acetic acid and lactic acid\n\n2) Aspergillus niger is a bacterium that produces citric acid\n\n3) Microbes can be used to produce organic acids\n\n4) Clostridium butylicum is an acid producer','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Lactobacillus (a bacterium) is producer of lactic acid\nAspergillus niger (a fungus) is producer of citric acid\nMicrobes are also used for commercial and industrial production of certain chemicals like organic acids, alcohols and enzymes.\nClostridium butylicum (a bacterium) is producer of butyric acid\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 183, para 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321224,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Aspergillus niger and Acetobacter aceti are acid producers\n\nReason: The former produces citiric acid while the latter produces acetic acid','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Examples of acid producers are Aspergillus niger (a fungus) of citric acid, Acetobacter aceti (a bacterium) of acetic acid\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 183, para 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321225,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Modified Streptokinase is used to remove blood clots in patients suffering from myocardial infaction\n\n2) Saccharomyces cerevisiae is used to produce enzymes\n\n3) Lipases are used to remove blood stains\n\n4) Bottled juices are clarified by using enzymes','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Streptokinase produced by the bacterium Streptococcus and modified by genetic engineering is used as a ‘clot buster’ for removing clots from the blood vessels of patients who have undergone myocardial infaction leading to heart attack\nYeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) is used for commercial production of ethanol\nLipases are helpful in removing oily stains from the laundry\nBottled juices are clarified by the use of pectinases and proteases which are enzymes\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 183, para 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321226,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Bottled fruit juices bought from the market are clearer\n\nReason: Pectinases and proteases are used in the clarification of juices','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','You must have noticed that bottled fruit juices bought from the market are clearer as compared to those made at home. This is because the bottled juices are clarified by the use of pectinases and proteases.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 183, para 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321227,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Chemicals, Enzymes and other Bioactive Molecules. choose the correct statements\n\n1) Cyclosporin A. is produced by fungus\n\n2)  Statins is a blood-cholesterol level lowering agent\n\n3) Statins work by using incompetitive inhibition\n\n4) Cyclosporin A, is produced by Monascus purpureus','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Cyclosporin A, is produced by the fungus Trichoderma polysporum\nStatins have been commercialised as blood-cholesterol lowering agents.\nIt acts by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for synthesis of cholesterol.\nStatins is produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 183, para 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321228,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Statins produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus have been commercialised as blood-cholesterol lowering agents\n\nReason: Statins are bioactive molecules.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Statins produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus have been commercialised as blood-cholesterol lowering agents. It acts by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for synthesis of cholesterol.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 183, para 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321229,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes in Sewage Treatments, choose the correct statements\n\n1) A major component of waste water is domestic garbage\n\n2) Majority of microbes in sewage are pathogenic\n\n3) Sewage contains large amounts of organic matter and microbes\n\n4) Before disposal, sewage is treated using BOD machine','Only 2 and 3 are correct','A major component of this waste water is human excreta\nIt contains large amounts of organic matter and microbes. Many of which are pathogenic.\nSewage contains large amounts of organic matter and microbes\nBefore disposal, hence, sewage is treated in sewage treatment plants (STPs) to make it less polluting. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 183, para 5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321230,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Sewage cannot be discharged into natural water bodies directly\n\nReason: They  contains large amounts of organic matter and microbes which are pathogenic','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','It contains large amounts of organic matter and microbes. Many of which are pathogenic.This cannot be discharged into natural water bodies like rivers and streams directly. They are too polluted\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 183, para 5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321231,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes in Sewage Treatments, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Treatment of sewage is done by heterotrophic microbes\n\n2) Sewage treatment is done in two major steps\n\n3) Primary treatment is also known as biological treatment\n\n4) All solids that settle form the effluent, and the supernatant forms the primary sludge.','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Treatment of waste water is done by the heterotrophic microbes naturally present in the sewage\nThis treatment is carried out in two stages, Primary and Secondary treatment\nSecondary treatment is known as Biological treatment\nAll solids that settle form the primary sludge, and the supernatant forms\nthe effluent. The effluent from the primary settling tank is taken for secondary treatment.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 183, para 5 and pg 184, para 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(260231,'chemistry','Write the correct IUPAC NAME of the given compound in diagram?(n26002)','α-methoxypropionaldehyde','The common name has substituent methoxy group adjacent to aldehyde group, so referred as alpha and propion being the longest carbon chain.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'3','mcq','Valeraldehdye','Acrolein','Phthaldehyde','α-methoxypropionaldehyde','NEW','12-359','12-229','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260231q.png'),
(321232,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Sewage only contains pathogenic microbes\n\nReason: In sewage, the main component is inorganic matter and chemical pollutants','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Besides pathogenic microbes, sewage also contains heterotrophic microbes that naturally present in it to treat waste water \nA major component of this waste water is human excreta. This municipal waste-water is also called sewage. It contains large amounts of organic matter and microbes\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 183, para 5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-183','12-153',NULL),
(321233,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sewage Treatment, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Particles are removed by sedimentation only in Primary Treatment\n\n2) The solids formed after settling is known as grit\n\n3) The physical removal of particles is done according to their size from large to small\n\n4) Floating debris is removed by sequential filtration','2,3 and 4 are correct','The particles are removed from the sewage through filtration and sedimentation\nAll solids that settle form the primary sludge\nThese treatment steps basically involve physical removal of particles – large and small – from the sewage through filtration and sedimentation\nInitially, floating debris is removed by sequential filtration\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 184, para 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Assertion Reason and Mutliple statement questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321301,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Grit is removed before floating debris during primary treatment of sewage\n\nReason: Grit has a larger size than the debris','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','These treatment steps basically involve physical removal of particles – large and small. These are removed in stages; initially, floating debris is removed by sequential filtration. Then the grit (soil and small pebbles) are removed by sedimentation\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 184, para 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321302,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sewage Treatment, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Flocs are mesh-like structures formed by masses of bacteria associated with fungal filaments\n\n2) Secondary treatment of sewage is done in settling tanks\n\n3) The effluent passed to secondary treatment is the supernatant of sewage\n\n4) Aeration tanks are used during Biological Treatment','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Flocs are masses of bacteria associated with fungal filaments to form mesh like structures\nPrimary treatment uses settling tanks while aeration tanks are used in secondary treatment\nThe supernatant forms the effluent. The effluent from the primary settling tank is taken for secondary treatment\nThe primary effluent is passed into large aeration tanks during secondary treatment or also known as Biological treatment\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 184, para 2 and 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321303,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Air is pumped into aeration tanks and agitated mechanically\n\nReason: It reduces BOD of the effluent','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','It is constantly agitated mechanically and air is pumped into it. This allows vigorous growth of useful aerobic microbes into flocs. While growing, these microbes consume the major part of the organic matter in the effluent. This significantly reduces the BOD (biochemical O₂ demand) of the effluent\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 184, para 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321304,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sewage Treatment, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Sewage must be treated until BOD becomes high\n\n2) BOD refers to the amount of the O₂ that would be consumed if all the organic matter in one liter of water were oxidised by bacteria\n\n3) BOD test measures the rate of uptake of O₂ by micro-organisms in a sample of water\n\n4)  BOD is a direct measure of the O₂ present in the water','Only 2 and 3 are correct','The sewage water is treated till the BOD is reduced\nBOD refers to the amount of the O₂ that would be consumed if all the organic matter in one liter of water were oxidised by bacteria\nThe BOD test measures the rate of uptake of O₂ by micro-organisms in a sample of water\nindirectly, BOD is a measure of the organic matter present in the water\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 184, para 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321305,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: The greater the BOD of waste water, more is its polluting potential\n\nReason: BOD is a measure of the pathogenic microbes present in the water','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','BOD refers to the amount of the O₂ that would be consumed if all the organic matter in one liter of water were oxidised by bacteria. BOD test measures the rate of uptake of O₂ by micro-organisms in a sample of water and thus, indirectly, BOD is a measure of the organic matter present in the water. The greater the BOD of waste water, more is its polluting potential.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 184, para 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321306,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sewage Treatment, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The sedimented bacterial flocs are also known as activated sludge\n\n2) The bacteria and fungi in anaerobic sludge digester are digested\n\n3) Biogas is not inflammable\n\n4) The activated sludge pumped back into aeration tank is served as inoculum','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Once the BOD of sewage or waste water is reduced significantly, the effluent is then passed into a settling tank where the bacterial ‘flocs’ are allowed to sediment. This sediment is called activated sludge\nThe remaining major part of the sludge is pumped into large tanks called anaerobic sludge digesters. Here, other kinds of bacteria, which grow anaerobically, digest the bacteria and the fungi in the sludge\nBiogas can be used as source of energy as it is inflammable\nA small part of the activated sludge is pumped back into the aeration tank to serve as the inoculum\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 184, para 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321307,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: In sewage treatment, biogas is produced\n\nReason: Bacteria can produce a mixture of gases such as methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂  during digestion of sewage.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The remaining major part of the sludge is pumped into large tanks called anaerobic sludge digesters. Here, other kinds of bacteria, which grow anaerobically, digest the bacteria and the fungi in the sludge. During this digestion, bacteria produce a mixture of gases such as methane, hydrogen sulphide and  CO₂ . These gases form biogas\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 184, para 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-184','12-154',NULL),
(321308,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Sewage Treatment, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Human technology is able to replace microbial treatment of sewage to give a better result\n\n2) A small amount of STPs are enough to carry out a large scale of sewage treatment\n\n3) Yamuna Action Plan is initiated to save major rivers from pollution issue\n\n4) Urbanisation can increase the production of sewage significantly','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Till date, no manmade technology has been able to rival the microbial treatment of sewage\nThe number of sewage treatment plants has not increased enough to treat such large quantities.\nThe Ministry of Environment and Forests has initiated Ganga Action Plan and Yamuna Action Plan to save these major rivers of our country from pollution\nDue to increasing urbanisation, sewage is being produced in much larger quantities than ever before\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 185, para 1.2 and 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(321309,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Water-borne diseases have increased at an alarming rate\n\nReason: No manmade technology has been able to rival the microbial treatment of sewage.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The number of sewage treatment plants has not increased enough to treat such large quantities. So the untreated sewage is often discharged directly into rivers leading to their pollution and increase in water-borne diseases.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 185, para 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(321310,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes in Production of Biogas. choose the correct staements\n\n1) Methane is the gas that constitutes the most percentage composition of Biogas\n\n2) Main gas produced in fermentation is  CO₂ \n\n3) Methanogens are aerobic bacteria\n\n4) Type of the gas produced depends upon the microbes solely','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Biogas is a mixture of gases (containing predominantly methane) produced by the microbial activity and which may be used as fuel\nIn the examples cited in relation to fermentation of dough, cheese making and production of beverages, the main gas produced was CO₂ \nCertain bacteria, which grow anaerobically on cellulosic material, produce large amount of methane along with CO₂ \nand H₂ . These bacteria are collectively called methanogens\nThe type of the gas produced depends upon the microbes and the organic substrates they utilise.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 185, para 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(321311,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Methanogens are present in rumen of cattles\n\nReason: They play an important role in the nutrition of cattle by breaking down cellulose','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','A lot of cellulosic material present in the food of cattle is also present in the rumen. In rumen, these bacteria help in the breakdown of cellulose and play an important role in the nutrition of cattle as dietary fibre\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 185, para 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-185','12-155',NULL),
(241806,'biology','Assertion: As the anther develops, the cells of the sporogenous tissue undergo meiotic divisions.\n\nReason: This is done to increase the number of cells.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','As the anther develops, the cells of the sporogenous tissue undergo meiotic divisions to form microspore tetrads. (Reference  : NCERT, page 21, paragraph 6).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-021',NULL,NULL),
(321312,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Biogas, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The floating cover placed over covered slurry will keep on rising\n\n2) Biogas produced cannot be directly supplied for household usage\n\n3) The spent slurry can be used as fertiliser\n\n4) Biogas plants are mostly found in rural areas','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','A floating cover is placed over the slurry, which keeps on rising as the gas is produced in the tank due to the microbial activity\nThe biogas plant has an outlet, which is connected to a pipe to supply biogas to nearby houses\nThe spent slurry is removed through another outlet and may be used as fertiliser\nCattle dung is available in large quantities in rural areas where cattle are used for a variety of purposes. So\nbiogas plants are more often built in rural areas\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 186, para 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(321313,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Biogas produced is used for cooking and lighting\n\nReason: Spent slurry is removed through another outlet','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The biogas plant has an outlet, which is connected to a pipe to supply biogas to nearby houses. The biogas\nthus produced is used for cooking and lighting. The spent slurry is removed through another outlet and may be used as fertiliser.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 186, para 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(321314,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes as Biocontrol Agents, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Biocontrol can only be used to control amount of pests\n\n2) It is said that the more variety a landscape has, the more sustainable it is\n\n3) In biological control, pests are not totally killed\n\n4) Synthetic chemical pesticides will not harm the crops','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Biocontrol refers to the use of biological methods for controlling plant diseases and pests\nA key belief of the organic farmer is that biodiversity furthers health. The more variety a landscape has, the more sustainable it is\nThe organic farmer, therefore, works to create a system where the insects that are sometimes called pests are not eradicated, but instead are kept at manageable levels\nThese chemicals are toxic and extremely harmful, to human beings and animals alike, and have been polluting our environment (soil, ground water), fruits, vegetables and crop plants\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 186, para 2 and 3 )','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(321315,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Biological control is better for the biodiversity\n\nReason: It relies on natural predation rather than introduced chemicals and maintains Biodiversity','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Biological control of pests and diseases relies on natural predation. It works to create a system where the insects that are sometimes called pests are not eradicated, but instead are kept at manageable levels by a complex system of checks and balances within a living and vibrant ecosystem\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 186, para 3 )','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-186','12-156',NULL),
(321316,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes as Biocontrol Agents, choose the correct statements\n\n1)Chemicals may kill both harmless and harmful organisms\n\n2) Ladybird is a predator used in biological control method\n\n3) Dragonflies are also called aphid hawks\n\n4) Total eradication of pests using biocontrol agents and chemical method is desirable','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Chemical methods kill both useful and harmful life forms\nLadybird, and Dragonflies are useful to get rid of aphids and mosquitoes, respectively. Dragonflies are also called mosquito hawks.\nEradication of the creatures that are often described as pests is not only possible, but also undesirable, for without them the beneficial predatory and parasitic insects which depend upon them as food or hosts would not be able to survive\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 187, para 1 and 2 )','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321317,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion:  Use of biocontrol measures will greatly reduce our dependence on toxic chemicals and pesticides\n\nReason: Biological control of pests and diseases do not relies on natural predation.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Eradication of the creatures that are often described as pests is not only possible, but also undesirable, for without them the beneficial predatory and parasitic insects which depend upon them as food or hosts would not be able to survive. Thus, the use of biocontrol measures will greatly reduce our dependence on toxic chemicals and pesticides\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 187, para 1 )','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(220604,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a step in oxidation number method?','Add electrons to one side of the half reaction to balance the charges. If need be, make the number of electrons equal in the two half reactions by multiplying one or both half reactions by appropriate number.','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274; Paragraph 8.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Redox Balancing',NULL,'2','mcq','Write the correct formula for each reactant and product.','Identify atoms which undergo change in oxidation number in the reaction by assigning the oxidation number to all elements in the reaction.','Calculate the increase or decrease in the oxidation number per atom and for the entire molecule/ion in which it occurs. If these are not equal then multiply by suitable number so that these become equal.','Add electrons to one side of the half reaction to balance the charges. If need be, make the number of electrons equal in the two half reactions by multiplying one or both half reactions by appropriate number.','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
(321318,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes as Biocontrol Agents, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Bt-cotton is resistant to pests\n\n2) Trichoderma species can control pest activity of butterfly caterpillars\n\n3) Bacteria Bacillus thuringiensis is an example of microbial biocontrol agent\n\n4)  B. thuringiensis toxin genes is introduced into plants using genetic engineering','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Such plants are resistant to attack by insect pests. Bt-cotton is one such example\nA biological control being developed for use in the treatment of plant disease is the fungus Trichoderma\nAn example of microbial biocontrol agents that can be introduced in order to control butterfly caterpillars is the bacteria Bacillus thuringiensis (often written as Bt).\nBecause of the development of methods of genetic engineering in the last decade or so, the scientists have introduced B. thuringiensis toxin genes into plants\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 187, para 2 and 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321319,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Trichoderma is introduced in order to control butterfly caterpillars\n\nReason: Trichoderma releases toxin in the gut of larvae','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','An example of microbial biocontrol agents that can be introduced in order to control butterfly caterpillars is the bacteria Bacillus thuringiensis. In the gut of the larvae, the toxin is released and the larvae get killed. The bacterial disease will kill the caterpillars, but leave other insects unharmed.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 187, para 2 )','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321320,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes as Biocontrol Agents, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Nucleopolyhedrovirus will only have impact on non target insects\n\n2) Baculoviruses are pathogens that only attacks mammals\n\n3) Trichoderma are effective biocontrol agents of several plant pathogens\n\n4) Nucleopolyhedrovirus is species-specific','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Baculoviruses are pathogens that attack insects and other arthropods. The majority of baculoviruses used as biological control agents are in the genus Nucleopolyhedrovirus\n They have been shown to have no negative impacts on plants, mammals, birds, fish or even on non-target insects.\nTrichoderma species are effective biocontrol agents of several plant pathogens.\nThese viruses are excellent candidates for species-specific, narrow spectrum insecticidal applications\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 187, para 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321321,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Nucleopolyhedrovirus  are excellent candidates for species-specific, narrow spectrum insecticidal applications\n\nReason: They show no negative impacts on plants, mammals, birds, fish','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','These viruses are excellent candidates for species-specific, narrow spectrum insecticidal applications. They have been shown to have no negative impacts on plants, mammals, birds, fish or even on non-target insects.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 187, para 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-187','12-157',NULL),
(321322,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes in Biofertilisers, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Azotobacter can fix atmospheric nitrogen while free-living in the soil \n\n2) Biofertilisers are organisms that enrich the nutrient quality of the soil\n\n3) Fungi in mycorrhiza absorbs phosphorus from soil\n\n4) Rhizobium fix atmospheric nitrogen into inorganic forms','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Other bacteria can fix atmospheric nitrogen while free-living in the soil (examples Azospirillum and Azotobacter), thus enriching the nitrogen content of the soil.\nBiofertilisers are organisms that enrich the nutrient quality of the soil.\nFungi are also known to form symbiotic associations with plants(mycorrhiza). The fungal symbiont in these associations absorbs phosphorus from soil and passes it to the plant\nThese bacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen into organic forms, which is used by the plant as nutrient\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 188, para 1 and 2 )','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(321323,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Microbes as Biofertilisers, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Cyanobacteria fix phosphorus\n\n2) Blue green algae helps increasing soil fertility\n\n3) Plants forming mycorrhiza has resistance to root-borne pathogens\n\n4) Anabaena is an example Cyanobacteria','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','Cyanobacteria are autotrophic microbes widely distributed in aquatic and terrestrial environments many of which can fix atmospheric nitrogen, e.g. Anabaena, Nostoc, Oscillatoria, etc\nBlue green algae also add organic matter to the soil and increase its fertility.\nPlants having such associations show other benefits also, such as resistance to root-borne pathogens, tolerance to salinity and drought, and an overall increase in plant growth and development.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 188, para 2 and 3 )','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 1,2 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(321324,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\nAssertion: Azospirillum cannot fix atmospheric nitrogen\n\nReason: It forms symbiotic association in the form of mycorrhiza','If both Assertion and Reason are false','Other bacteria can fix atmospheric nitrogen while free-living in the soil (examples Azospirillum and Azotobacter), thus enriching the nitrogen content of the soil.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 188, para 2 )','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(321325,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\nAssertion: Oscillatoria is used as a biofertilizer\n\nReason: It is a type of Cyanobacteria and fixes nitrogen and helps increasing soil fertility','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Cyanobacteria are autotrophic microbes widely distributed in aquatic and terrestrial environments many of which can fix atmospheric nitrogen, e.g. Anabaena, Nostoc, Oscillatoria, etc. In paddy fields, cyanobacteria serve as an important biofertiliser\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 10, pg 188, para 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Microbes in Human Welfare >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW','12-188','12-158',NULL),
(140101,'chemistry','Environmental chemistry does not involve:','Movements and relative positions of celestial bodies.','Environmental chemistry deals with the study of the origin, transport, reactions, effects and fates of chemical species in the environment. Environmental studies deal with the sum of all social, economical, biological, physical and chemical interrelations with our surroundings.  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 406; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Environmental Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Fates of chemical species in the environment.','Physical and chemical interrelations with our surroundings.','Movements and relative positions of celestial bodies.','Origin of chemical species in the environment.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140102,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','Pollutants do not exist in the environment naturally.','A substance, which causes pollution, is known as pollutant. Pollutants can be solid, liquid or gaseous substances present in greater concentration than in natural  abundance and are produced due to human activities or due to natural happenings. An average human being requires nearly 12-15 times more air than the food. So, even small amounts of pollutants in the air become significant compared to similar levels present in the food.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 406; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Environmental Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Pollutants can be solid, liquid or gaseous.','Pollutants produced due to human activities or due to natural happenings.','Pollutants do not exist in the environment naturally.','Air pollution is more serious than food pollution.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140103,'chemistry','How many times more air is required as compared to food?','12 to 15','An average human being requires nearly 12-15 times more air than the food.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 406; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Environmental Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','12 to 15','20 to 25','50 to 60','5 to 10','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140104,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\nAssertion: Even small amounts of pollutants in the air become significant compared to similar levels present in the food.\nReason: Air is 15 times more required than food.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','An average human being requires nearly 12-15 times more air than the food. So, even small amounts of pollutants in the air become significant compared to similar levels present in the food.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 406; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Environmental Pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140105,'chemistry','Choose the odd one with respect to pollutants which are slowly degradable.','Discarded vegetables','Pollutants can be degradable, like discarded vegetables which rapidly break down by natural processes. On the other hand, pollutants which are slowly degradable, remain in the environment in an unchanged form for many decades. For example, substances such as dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane (DDT), plastic materials, heavy metals, many chemicals, nuclear wastes etc., once released into the environment are difficult to remove.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 406; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Environmental Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','DDT','Discarded vegetables','Heavy metals','Nuclear waste','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140106,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a pollutant transportation agent?','Light','In the process of environmental pollution, pollutants originate from a source and get transported by air or water or are dumped into the soil by human beings.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Environmental Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Light','Water','Soil','Air','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140107,'biology','For food Saprophytes depends on','Dead and decaying','Saprophytes like fungi are dependent on dead and decaying matter\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;227,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Dead and decaying','Leaves of plants','Animals','Sunlight for photosynthesis','NEW','11-227','11-154',NULL),
(140108,'biology','The mechanism of breakdown of food materials within the cell to release energy, and the trapping of this energy for synthesis of ATP is called','Cellular Respiration','Cellular respiration is the mechanism of breakdown of food materials within the cell to release energy, and the trapping of this energy for synthesis of ATP\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;227,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Cellular Respiration','Photosynthesis','HMP pathway','Calvin cycle','NEW','11-227','11-154',NULL),
(140109,'biology','The breakdown of complex molecules to yield energy takes place in; \n\n1. Cytoplasm \n\n2. Mitochondria \n\n3. Chloroplast \n\n4. Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum','Only 1,2 are correct','Photosynthesis, of course, takes place within the chloroplasts (in the eukaryotes), whereas the breakdown of complex molecules to yield energy takes place in the cytoplasm and in the mitochondria (also only in eukaryotes\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;227,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','1, 2, 4 are correct','Only 1,2 are correct','Only 1 is correct','All the above','NEW','11-227','11-154',NULL),
(140110,'biology','During Respiration considerable amount of energy is released in cell through','Breaking C-C bonds of complex molecules through oxidation','The breaking of the C-C bonds of complex compounds through oxidation within the cells, leading to release of considerable amount of energy is called respiration\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;227,paragraph no. 2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Breaking C-C bonds of complex molecules through oxidation','Breaking C-C bonds of complex molecules through reduction','Breakdown of macromolecules to produce ADP','Both a and c','NEW','11-227','11-154',NULL),
(140111,'biology','The compounds that are oxidised during Respiration process are known as ________','Respiratory Substrates','The compounds that are oxidised during this process are known as respiratory substrates.\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;227,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'2','fillup','Res,pira,to,ry Su,bstr,at,es',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-227','11-154',NULL),
(140112,'biology','Carbohydrates are oxidised to release energy, but proteins, fats and even organic acids can be used as respiratory substances in some plants, under certain conditions.','TRUE','Usually carbohydrates are oxidised to release energy, but proteins, fats and even organic acids can be used as respiratory substances in some plants, under certain conditions\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;227,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'2','fillup','TRUE,FALSE',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-227','11-154',NULL),
(140113,'biology','Energy currency of the cell is','ATP','During oxidation within a cell, all the energy contained in respiratory substrates is not released free into the cell, or in a single step. It is released in a series of slow stepwise reactions controlled by enzymes, and it is trapped as chemical energy in the form of ATP. Hence, it is important to understand that the energy released by oxidation in respiration is not (or rather cannot be) used directly but is used to synthesise ATP, which is broken down whenever (and wherever) energy needs to be utilised. Hence, ATP acts as the energy currency of the cell\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;227,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Mitochondria','ATP','ADP','NADH','NEW','11-227','11-154',NULL),
(140114,'biology','Precursors for biosynthesis of other molecules in the cell is?','Carbon skeleton produced during respiration','Energy trapped in ATP is utilised in various energy-requiring processes of the organisms, and the carbon skeleton produced during respiration is used as precursors for biosynthesis of other molecules in the cell.(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;227,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Carbon skeleton produced during respiration','ADP products','Carbon skeletons produced during expiration','tRNA','NEW','11-227','11-154',NULL),
(140115,'biology','Read the following statements and choose the correct option:','Chemical energy is broken down during respiration to release energy','Energy release during respiration due to breaking of chemical bonds.\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;227,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Chemical energy is broken down during respiration to release energy','Respiration is purely a catabolic process','Reduction of food results in the release of energy','Fungi can make their own food and derive the energy by respiration','NEW','11-227','11-154',NULL),
(140116,'biology','Match the following diagram (1401)','1 - (c) ; 2 - (a) ; 3 - (d) ; 4 - (b)','Animals-heterotrophic,breathing-release of energy,sun-main source of energy, Carnivore - obtain food indirectly from plants\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;226-227,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','1 - (a) ; 2 - (b) ; 3 - (c) ; 4 - (d)','1 - (d) ; 2 - (a) ; 3 - (b) ; 4 - (c)','1 - (b) ; 2 - (c) ; 3 - (d) ; 4 - (a)','1 - (c) ; 2 - (a) ; 3 - (d) ; 4 - (b)','NEW','11-227','11-154','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/140116q.png'),
(140117,'biology','Organisms need energy for the processes of\n\n1. Respiration \n\n2. Locomotion \n\n3. HMP shunt \n\n4.Absorption','All of them','All living organisms need energy for carrying out daily life activities, be it absorption, transport, movement, reproduction or even breathing. (Reference :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;226,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Answers 1,3,4 are correct','Answer 4 is incorrect','Answer 3 is incorrect','All of them','NEW','11-226','11-153',NULL),
(140118,'biology','What is an organism called when they obtain food from both Herbivores and Carnivores?','Heterotrophic','Animals are heterotrophic, i.e., they obtain food from plants directly (herbivores) or indirectly (carnivores). (Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;226-227,paragraph no.3)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Heterotrophic','Amphivore','Omnitrophic','Amphithrophic','NEW','11-226','11-153',NULL),
(140119,'biology','Ultimately all the food that is respired for life processes comes from _________.','Photosynthesis','Ultimately all the food that is respired for life processes comes from\nphotosynthesis. (Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;227,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'2','fillup','ph,o,to,sy,n,th,es,is',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-227','11-154',NULL),
(140120,'biology','The energy released by oxidation in respiration is used to directly synthesise ATP.','TRUE','It is important to understand that the energy released by oxidation in respiration is not (or rather cannot be) used directly but is used to synthesise ATP, which is broken down whenever (and wherever) energy needs to be utilised (Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;227,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Introduction',NULL,'2','fillup','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-227','11-154',NULL),
(140201,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true with respect to atmosphere?\nA. Atmosphere is of same thickness at all heights.\nB. There are concentric layers of air or regions.\nC. Each layer has approximately same density.','Only B','The atmosphere that surrounds the earth is not of the same thickness at all heights. There are concentric layers of air or regions and each layer has different density.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Atmospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Only A','Both B and C','Only B','A, B and C','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140202,'chemistry','The lowest region of atmosphere is called ____.','Troposphere','The lowest region of atmosphere in which the human beings along with other organisms live is called troposphere.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Atmospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Stratosphere','Troposphere','Mesosphere','Thermosphere','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140203,'chemistry','Troposphere extends up to the height of about _____ from sea level.','~10 kilometer','The lowest region of atmosphere in which the human beings along with other organisms live is called troposphere.It extends up to the height of ~10 km from sea level.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Atmospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','~1 meter','~100 meter','~10 kilometer','~100 kilometer','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140204,'chemistry','The atmospheric region including clouds is known as:','Troposphere','Troposphere is a turbulent, dusty zone containing air, much water vapour and clouds. This is the region of strong air movement and cloud formation.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Atmospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Stratosphere','Troposphere','Mesosphere','Thermosphere','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140205,'chemistry','Stratosphere lies between _____ above sea level.','10-50  km','Above the troposphere, between 10 and 50 km above sea level lies stratosphere.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Atmospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','10-50  km','100-120 km','70-80 km','400-430 km','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140206,'chemistry','The atmospheric layers containing a significant amount of water vapour and high ozone levels are referred to as ________ respectively.','Troposphere and Stratosphere','Above the troposphere, between 10 and 50 km above sea level lies stratosphere. Troposphere is a turbulent, dusty zone containing air, much water vapour and clouds. This is the region of strong air movement and cloud formation. The stratosphere, on the other hand, contains dinitrogen, dioxygen, ozone and little water vapour.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Atmospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Troposphere and Mesosphere','Mesosphere and Stratosphere','Troposphere and Stratosphere','Stratosphere and Troposphere','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140207,'chemistry','The presence of ozone in stratosphere prevents about 99.5 % of ______ rays of sun.','UV rays','The presence of ozone in the stratosphere prevents about 99.5 per cent of the sun’s harmful ultraviolet (UV) radiations from reaching the earth’s surface and thereby protecting humans and other animals from its effect.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Atmospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','IR rays','X- rays','Visible rays','UV rays','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/140207q.png'),
(140208,'biology','Plant cell catabolise the glucose molecule in such a way that','Not all the liberated energy goes out as heat and is stored as ATP','The strategy that the plant cell uses is to catabolise the glucose molecule is in such a way that not all the liberated energy goes out as heat but to formed and store in ATP (Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;228,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Do Plants Breathe?',NULL,'2','mcq','All energy should go out as heat','Not all the liberated energy goes out as heat and is stored as NADH','Utilization of complete released energy','Not all the liberated energy goes out as heat and is stored as ATP','NEW','11-228','11-155',NULL),
(140209,'biology','Assertion:The key is to oxidise glucose not in one step but in several small steps. \n\nReason:When coupled in several steps, it gives the cells time and capacity to couple the energy released into ATP molecules','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The strategy that the plant cell uses is to catabolise the glucose molecule in such a way that not all the liberated energy goes out as heat. The key is to oxidise glucose not in one step but in several small steps enabling some steps to be just large enough such that the energy released can be coupled to ATP synthesis\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;228,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Do Plants Breathe?',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-228','11-155',NULL),
(140210,'biology','The combustion reaction requires','Oxygen','Combustion take place only in the presence of oxygen\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;228,paragraph no.3)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Do Plants Breathe?',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxygen','Carbon','Cholorophyll','Nitrogen','NEW','11-228','11-155',NULL),
(140211,'biology','All living organisms retain the enzymatic machinery to partially oxidise glucose without the help of oxygen','TRUE','All living organisms retain the enzymatic machinery to partially oxidise glucose without the help of O₂ \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;228,paragraph no.3)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Do Plants Breathe?',NULL,'2','fillup','TRUE,FALSE',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-228','11-155',NULL),
(140212,'biology','Breakdown of glucose to _______ is called glycolysis.','Pyruvate','Glycolysis is a process in which glucose is broken down into pyruvic acid\n(Reference   :NCERT Class 11; page no;228,paragraph no.3)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Do Plants Breathe?',NULL,'2','fillup','Py,ru,va,te',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-228','11-155',NULL),
(140213,'biology','Plants can get along without respiratory organs, In this regard, choose which of the following is incorrect','Respiration rate is faster than animals in roots, stems and leaves','There are several reasons why plants can get along without respiratory organs. First, each plant part takes care of its own gas-exchange needs. There is very little transport of gases from one plant part to another. Second, plants do not present great demands for gas exchange. Roots, stems and leaves respire at rates far lower than animals do. Only during photosynthesis are large volumes of gases exchanged and, each leaf is well adapted to take care of its own needs during these periods. When cells photosynthesise, availability of Oxygen is not a problem in these cells since oxygen is released within the cell. Third, the distance that gases must diffuse even in large, bulky plants is not great. Each living cell in a plant is located quite close to the surface of the plant.\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;228,paragraph no.4)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Do Plants Breathe?',NULL,'2','mcq','Respiration rate is faster than animals in roots, stems and leaves','O₂ released during photosynthesis is utilized for respiration','Loose packing of parenchyma cells in leaves, stems and roots facilitates respiration','There is very little transport of gases from one plant part to another','NEW','11-228','11-155',NULL),
(141214,'biology','Which one of the following is not true regarding the release of energy during ATP synthesis through chemiosmosis? It involves: [NEET 2022]','Breakdown of electron gradient','The passage of protons through the channel is coupled to the catalytic site of the F1 component for the production of ATP. For each ATP produced, 4H⁺ passes through F0 from the intermembrane space to the matrix down the electrochemical proton gradient. \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;234,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Breakdown of proton gradient','Breakdown of electron gradient','Movement of protons across the membrane to the stroma','Reduction of NADP to NADPH on the stroma side of the membrane','NEW','11-234','11-161',NULL),
(140301,'chemistry','Choose odd one out with respect to gaseous air pollutants.','smoke','Gaseous air pollutants: These are oxides of sulphur, nitrogen and carbon, hydrogen sulphide, hydrocarbons, ozone and other oxidants. \nParticulate pollutants: These are dust, mist, fumes, smoke, smog etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','hydrocarbons','nitrogen oxides','smoke','hydrogen sulphide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140302,'chemistry','Choose odd one with respect to particulate pollutants.','carbon oxides','Gaseous air pollutants: These are oxides of sulphur, nitrogen and carbon, hydrogen sulphide, hydrocarbons, ozone and other oxidants. \nParticulate pollutants: These are dust, mist, fumes, smoke, smog etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','carbon oxides','smog','mist','fumes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140303,'chemistry','Which oxide of sulphur is most common?','Sulphur dioxide','The most common species,sulphur dioxide, is a gas that is poisonous to both animals and plants.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Sulphur dioxide','Sulphur trioxide','Sulphur monoxide','Disulphur dioxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140304,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is false with respect to sulphur oxides ?','Only high concentration of Sulphur oxides can cause emphysema','Sulphur dioxides cuases respiratory diseases like emphysema\n\nOxides of sulphur are produced when sulphur containing fossil fuel is burnt. It has been reported that even a low concentration of sulphur dioxide causes respiratory diseases e.g., asthma, bronchitis, emphysema in human beings. Sulphur dioxide causes irritation to the eyes, resulting in tears and redness. High concentration of SO₂ leads to stiffness of flower buds which eventually fall off from plants.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Sulphur oxides are produced by burning of fossil fuels.','Sulphur dioxide causes irritation to eyes.','Only high concentration of Sulphur oxides can cause emphysema','High concentration of SO₂ leads to stiffness of flower in plants.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140305,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Presence of particulate matter in polluted air catalyses the oxidation of sulphur dioxide into sulphur trioxide.\n\nReason:  The uncatalyzed sulphur dioxide oxidation is therefore rapid.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','The uncatalysed oxidation of sulphur dioxide is slow. However, the presence of particulate matter in polluted air catalyses the oxidation of sulphur dioxide to sulphur trioxide.\n2SO₂ (g) +O₂ (g) → 2SO₃(g)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140306,'chemistry','_____  form of nitrogen is washed into soil which serve as fertilizer.','NO₃⁻','Dinitrogen and dioxygen are the main constituents of air. These gases do not react with each other at a normal temperature. At high altitudes when lightning strikes, they combine to form oxides of nitrogen. NO₂ is oxidised to nitrate ion, NO₃⁻ which is washed into soil, where it serves as a fertilizer.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','NO','NO₃⁻','NO₂⁻','N₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140307,'chemistry','Choose the correct option(s) from the following regarding "Oxides of Nitrogen":\nA. Dinitrogen and dioxygen gases react at normal temperature.\nB. Dinitrogen and dioxygen combine to form nitrogen oxides at high altitudes when lightning strikes.\nC. Nitrogen oxides are also formed in automobile engine because of high temperatures.\nD.  NO₃⁻ serves as a fertilizer.','B, C and D','Dinitrogen and dioxygen are the main constituents of air. These gases do not react with each other at a normal temperature. At high altitudes when lightning strikes, they combine to form oxides of nitrogen. NO₂ is oxidised to nitrate ion, NO₃⁻ which is washed into soil, where it serves as a fertilizer. In an automobile engine, (at high temperature) when fossil fuel is burnt, dinitrogen and dioxygen combine to yield significant quantities of nitric oxide (NO) and nitrogen dioxide ( NO₂).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','A, B and C','B, C and D','A,C and D','A, B and D','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140308,'chemistry','N₂ + O₂ → X (at temperature of 1483K)\n2NO +  O₂ → Y\nIn the above given equation X and Y are:','2NO and 2NO₂','In an automobile engine, (at high temperature) when fossil fuel is burnt, dinitrogen and dioxygen combine to yield significant quantities of nitric oxide (NO) and nitrogen dioxide ( NO₂) as given below:\nN₂ + O₂ → 2NO (at temperature of 1483K)\nNO reacts instantly with oxygen to give NO₂\n2NO +  O₂ → 2NO₂\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','2NO and 2NO₂','NO₂ and NO','NO and N₂O','NO₂ and N₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140309,'chemistry','When nitric oxide combines with ozone in the stratosphere, the rate of NO₂ production is:','Faster','Rate of production of NO₂ is faster when nitric oxide reacts with ozone in the stratosphere.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 407; Paragraph No. 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Slower','Faster','Inhibited','Constant','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140310,'biology','Sucrose is converted into glucose and fructose by the enzyme','Invertase','Sucrose is converted into glucose and fructose by the enzyme, invertase, and these two monosaccharides readily enter the glycolytic pathway. Glucose and fructose are phosphorylated to give rise to glucose-6-phosphate by the activity of the enzyme hexokinase. This phosphorylated form of glucose then isomerises to produce fructose-6- phosphate.\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;229,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'3','mcq','Invertase','Sucralose','Lipase','Ligase','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(140311,'chemistry','Which of the following is carcinogenic?','hydrocarbons','Hydrocarbons are carcinogenic, i.e., they cause cancer. They harm plants by causing ageing, breakdown of tissues and shedding of leaves, flowers and twigs.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','hydrocarbons','nitrogen monoxide','sulphur dioxide','carbon monoxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/140311q.png'),
(140312,'chemistry','_____ is formed by incomplete combustion of fuel in automobiles and consists of only two elements.','hydrocarbons','Hydrocarbons are composed of hydrogen and carbon only and are formed by incomplete combustion of fuel used in automobiles.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','hydrocarbons','nitrogen monoxide','sulphur dioxide','carbon monoxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140313,'chemistry','Which is incorrect with respect to properties of carbon monoxide?','The odour is similar to that of burning matches.','Carbon monoxide (CO) is one of the most serious air pollutants. It is a colourless and odourless gas, and highly poisonous to living.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','It is colorless.','The odour is similar to that of burning matches.','It is highly toxic to living being.','It is one of the most serious air pollutant.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140314,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: CO is highly poisonous to living beings.\n\nReason: CO has ability to block delivery of oxygen to organs.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','CO is highly poisonous to living beings because of its ability to block the delivery of oxygen to the organs and tissues.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140315,'chemistry','CO is released into air mainly by _____ .','automobile exhaust','It is produced as a result of incomplete combustion of carbon. Carbon monoxide is mainly released into the air by automobile exhaust. Other sources, which produce CO, involve incomplete combustion of coal, firewood, petrol, etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','automobile exhaust','fireworks','nuclear emission','incomplete combustion of coal','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140316,'chemistry','CO binds with haemoglobin to form which complex?','Carboxyhemoglobin','It binds to haemoglobin to form carboxyhemoglobin, which is about 300 times more stable than the oxygen-haemoglobin complex.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Carboxyhemoglobin','Carbaminohemoglobin','Oxyhaemoglobin','Ferritin','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140317,'chemistry','How many of the following statements are correct?\nA. Many vehicles are poorly maintained resulting in greater release of carbon monoxide..\nB. Carboxyhemoglobin is about 300 times more stable than the oxygen-haemoglobin complex.\nC. CO is essential for human bodies to function properly.','Both A and B','Many vehicles are poorly maintained and several have inadequate pollution control equipments resulting in the release of greater amount of carbon monoxide and other polluting gases. Carbon monoxide is poisonous to human bodies because it binds to haemoglobin to form carboxyhemoglobin, which is about 300 times more stable than the oxygen-haemoglobin complex.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and B','Both A and C','Only A','A, B and C','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140318,'chemistry','In blood, when the concentration of carboxyhemoglobin reaches about _____ , the oxygen carrying capacity of blood is greatly reduced.','3-4%','In blood, when the concentration of carboxyhemoglobin reaches about 3–4 per cent, the oxygen carrying capacity of blood is greatly reduced.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','10-11%','3-4%','15-16%','25-27%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140319,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: People are advised to not smoke.\n\nReason: CO poisoning can result in oxygen deficiency which can lead to weak eyesight, headache, cardiovascular disorder.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The oxygen deficiency, results into headache, weak eyesight, nervousness and cardiovascular disorder. This is the reason why people are advised not to smoke. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140320,'chemistry','In pregnant women who has the habit of smoking the increased CO level in blood may induce:','Deformed babies','In pregnant women who have the habit of smoking the increased CO level in blood may induce premature birth, spontaneous abortions and deformed babies.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Gestational diabetes','Nausea and constant headache','Deformed babies','"Blue baby" syndrome','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140321,'chemistry','Which of the following is not the source of carbon dioxide?\nA. Volcanic eruptions.\nB. Decomposition of limestone.\nC. Burning of fossil fuels.\nD. Photosynthesis by plants.','Only D','Carbon dioxide (CO₂) is released into the atmosphere by respiration, burning of fossil fuels for energy, and by decomposition of limestone during the manufacture of cement. It is also emitted during volcanic eruptions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and C','Only B','Both C and D','Only D','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140322,'chemistry','What percentage of the atmosphere is made up of carbon dioxide?','0.03%','Carbon dioxide gas is confined to troposphere only. Normally it forms about 0.03 per cent by volume of the atmosphere.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','0.03%','0.30%','78%','21%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(140323,'chemistry','In which layer of atmosphere is carbon dioxide present?','Troposphere','Carbon dioxide gas is confined to troposphere only. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Troposphere','Stratosphere','Exosphere','Both A and B','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140324,'chemistry','Which of the following maintains the levels of CO₂ in atmosphere?','Green plants','With the increased use of fossil fuels, a large amount of carbon dioxide gets released into the atmosphere. Excess of CO₂ in the air is removed by green plants and this maintains an appropriate level of CO₂ in the atmosphere. Green plants require CO₂ for photosynthesis and they, in turn, emit oxygen, thus maintaining the delicate balance.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Humans','Insects','Green plants','Ocean','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140325,'chemistry','Which process leads to increased level of CO₂ in atmosphere, causing global warming?','Deforestation','As you know, deforestation and burning of fossil fuel increases the CO₂ level and disturb the balance in the atmosphere. The increased amount of CO₂ in the air is mainly responsible for global warming.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Tropospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Deforestation','Afforestation','Recycling of plastics','Photosynthesis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140326,'biology','In the conversion of BPGA to 3-phosphoglyceric acid (PGA)  energy is trapped by the formation of ______','ATP','The conversion of BPGA to 3-phosphoglyceric acid (PGA), is also an energy yielding process; this energy is trapped by the formation of ATP. Another ATP is synthesised\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;229,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'3','mcq','NAD+','ATP','ADP','NADH + H⁺','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(140327,'biology','Another ATP is synthesised during the conversion of PEP to','Pyruvic acid','Another ATP is synthesised during the conversion of PEP to pyruvic acid.Pyruvic acid is then the key product of glycolysis\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;229,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'3','mcq','1, 3-bisphosphoglycerate (BPGA)','3-phosphoglyceric acid (PGA)','Dihydroxyacetone phosphate','Pyruvic acid','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(140328,'biology','When 2-phosphoglycerate is converted into phosphoenolpyruvate _____ is released','H₂O','When 2-phosphoglycerate is converted into phosphoenolpyruvate H₂O is released   \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;229,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'3','mcq','H₂O','ATP','ADP','NADH + H⁺','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(140329,'biology','There are three major ways in which different cells handle pyruvic acid produced by glycolysis. They are?','Both B and C are correct','There are three major ways in which different cells handle pyruvic acid produced by glycolysis. These are lactic acid fermentation, alcoholic fermentation and aerobic respiration\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;230,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'3','mcq','Malic acid fermentation','Alcoholic fermentation','Aerobic respiration','Both B and C are correct','NEW','11-230','11-157',NULL),
(140330,'biology','Fermentation takes place under _________ conditions','Anaerobic','Fermentation takes place under anaerobic conditions i.e in the absence of oxygen\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;230,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'3','fillup','An,ae,rob,ic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-230','11-157',NULL),
(140331,'biology','Among the following in which organisms Fermentation takes place; \n\n1. Prokaryotes \n\n2. Unicellular eukaryotes \n\n3.Multicellular eukaryotes \n\n4.Fungi','Only 1,2 are correct','Fermentation takes place under anaerobic conditions in many prokaryotes and unicellular eukaryotes\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;230,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 are correct','1,2,3 are correct','1,3 are correct','Only 3,4 are correct','NEW','11-230','11-157',NULL),
(140332,'biology','For the complete oxidation of glucose to CO₂  and H₂O organisms adopt to ________','TCA cycle','Organisms adopt Krebs’ cycle which is also called as aerobic respiration. This requires O₂ supply\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;230,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'3','mcq','TCA cycle','Calvin cycle','Nitrogen cycle','EMP pathway','NEW','11-230','11-157',NULL),
(140333,'biology','Common step of both aerobic and anaerobic respiration are','EMP pathway','In Aerobic organisms  In this process, glucose undergoes partial oxidation to form two molecules of pyruvic acid. Respiration In anaerobic organisms, it is the only process in respiration. \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;229,paragraph no.4)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','EMP pathway','Tricarboxylic acid cycle','Acetyl CoA formation','Citric acid cycle','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(140334,'biology','All are true about glycolysis, except','It produces one pyruvic acid molecule from each glucose','No - One pyruvic acid from half molecule of glucose / we can say two pyruvic acid from one molecule of glucose.\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;229,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','O₂ independent pathway','It occurs in the cytoplasm of cell','It can operate by using both glucose and fructose','It produces one pyruvic acid molecule from each glucose','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(140335,'biology','Match the following diagram (1404)','1- (c) ; 2 - (a) ; 3 - (b)','Sucrose is converted into glucose and fructose by the enzyme, invertase.\nATP is utilised at two steps: first in the conversion of glucose into glucose 6-phosphate and second in the conversion of fructose 6-phosphate to fructose 1, 6-bisphosphate.\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;229, paragraph no.1 - 2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'2','mcq','1- (a) ; 2 - (b) ; 3 - (c)','1- (a) ; 2 - (c) ; 3 - (b)','1- (c) ; 2 - (a) ; 3 - (b)','1- (b) ; 2 - (a) ; 3 - (c)','NEW','11-229','11-156','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/140335q.png'),
(140336,'biology','Place the following in order','Glucose > G6P > F6P > Triose phosphate','Glucose > G6P > F6P > Triose phosphate\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;229, fig 14.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'2','order','1,Glucose','2,G6P','3,F6P','4, Triose phosphate','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(140337,'biology','Place the following in order','Triose Phosphate > Triose bisphosphate > PGA > PEP','Triose Phosphate > Triose bisphosphate > PGA > PEP\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;229, fig 14.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Glycolysis',NULL,'2','order','1,Triose Phosphate','2,Triose bisphosphate','3, PGA','4, PEP','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(140401,'chemistry','How much percent of solar energy reaching earth is absorbed by earth\'s surface?','75','About 75 % of the solar energy reaching the earth is absorbed by the earth’s surface, which increases its temperature.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'1','mcq','25','75','50','99','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140402,'chemistry','Which among following gases is not a greenhouse gas?','SO₂','Some of the heat is trapped by gases such as carbon dioxide, methane, ozone, chlorofluorocarbon compounds (CFCs) and water vapour in the atmosphere.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'2','mcq','O₃','CFCs','SO₂','CO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140403,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: CFCs, methane and ozone causes global warming.\n\nReason: These gases absorb heat and hence add to the cooling of the atmosphere.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Some of the heat is trapped by gases such as carbon dioxide, methane, ozone, chlorofluorocarbon compounds (CFCs) and water vapour in the atmosphere. Thus, they add to the heating of the atmosphere. This causes global warming.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140404,'chemistry','Which of the following describes natural greenhouse effect?','Trapping of heat by atmosphere to keep earth\'s surface warm.','In cold places flowers, vegetables and fruits are grown in glass covered areas called greenhouse. Humans also live in a greenhouse, we are not surrounded by glass but a blanket of air called the atmosphere, which has kept the temperature on earth constant for centuries. But it is now undergoing change, though slowly. Just as the glass in a greenhouse holds the sun’s warmth inside, atmosphere traps the sun’s heat near the earth’s surface and keeps it warm. This is called natural greenhouse effect because it maintains the temperature and makes the earth perfect for life.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'2','mcq','Trapping of heat by atmosphere to keep earth\'s surface warm.','Trapping of heat by surface to keep the stratosphere warm.','Trapping of heat by glass to increase yield of crops.','Cooling effect caused by ocean currents.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140405,'chemistry','Warm soil and plants emit which type of radiations?','IR rays','The warm soil and plants emit infrared radiations. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'1','mcq','IR rays','UV rays','Cosmic rays','X-rays','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140406,'chemistry','_____  is opaque to infrared radiations.','Glass','In a greenhouse, solar radiations pass through the transparent glass and heat up the soil and the plants. The warm soil and plants emit infrared radiations. Since glass is opaque to infrared radiations (thermal region), it partly reflects and partly absorbs these radiations.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'1','mcq','Earth','Glass','Soil','Plants','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140407,'chemistry','Carbon dioxide molecules are transparent to X but not to Y? Here X and Y are respectively.','Sunlight and heat radiation','Carbon dioxide molecules also trap heat as they are transparent to sunlight but not to the heat radiation.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 408; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'2','mcq','Sunlight and heat radiation','Heat radiation and sunlight','Heat radiations and UV rays','UV rays and sunlight','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140408,'chemistry','Which gas is major contributor to global warming?','CO₂','If the amount of carbon dioxide crosses the delicate proportion of 0.03 per cent, the natural greenhouse balance may get disturbed. Carbon dioxide is the major contributor to global warming.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 409; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'1','mcq','CO₂','N₂O','SO₂','CO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140409,'chemistry','Which gas is released by paddy fields, burning and rotting of vegetation in absence of oxygen?','CH₄','Methane is produced naturally when vegetation is burnt, digested or rotted in the absence of oxygen. Large amounts of methane are released in paddy fields, coal mines, from rotting garbage dumps and by fossil fuels.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 409; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₂','N₂O','CH₄','CO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140410,'chemistry','Which among the following statements are correct?\nA. Nitrous oxide is a naturally occuring gas.\nB. CFCs are also naturally occuring chemicals used for air-conditioning.\nC. CFCs can damage the ozone layer.\nD. Fossil fuels release large amounts of CFCs.','Both A and C','Large amounts of methane are released in paddy fields, coal mines, from rotting garbage dumps and by fossil fuels. Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) are man-made industrial chemicals used in air conditioning etc. CFCs are also damaging the ozone layer. Nitrous oxide occurs naturally in the environment.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 409; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and B','Both B and C','Both A and C','Both A and D','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140411,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement(s) among the following.\nA. Greenhouse gases are increased significantly in recent years due to use of chemical fertilizers and burning of fossil fuels.\nB. Global warming will lead to increase in average temperature of earth and cause flooding in low lying areas.\nC. Greenhouse gases are causing a decrease in global temperature.','Both A and B','Greenhouse gases: In recent years, their quantities have increased significantly due to the use of chemical fertilizers and the burning of fossil fuels. If these trends continue, the average global temperature will increase to a level which may lead to melting of polar ice caps and flooding of low lying areas all over the earth. Increase in the global temperature increases the incidence of infectious diseases like dengue, malaria, yellow fever, sleeping sickness etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 409; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and B','Only C','Both B and C','A, B and C','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140412,'chemistry','Which of the following steps will not help to slow the rate of global warming?','Promote burning of dry leaves and wood.','Steps to reduce the rate of global warming:\ni. If burning of fossil fuels, cutting down forests and trees add to greenhouse gases in the atmosphere, we must find ways to use these just efficiently and judiciously.\nii. One of the simple things which we can do to reduce global warming is to minimise the use of automobiles. Depending upon the situation, one can use bicycle, public transport system, or go for carpool. \niii. We should plant more trees to increase the green cover. Avoid burning of dry leaves, wood etc. It is illegal to smoke in public places and workplaces, because it is harmful not only for the one who is smoking but also for others, and therefore, we should avoid it. \niv. Many people do not understand the greenhouse effect and the global warming. We can help them by sharing the information that we have.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 409; "Think it over")','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Global warming and Greenhouse effect',NULL,'2','mcq','Promote burning of dry leaves and wood.','Sharing the information about global warming and greenhouse effect.','Minimise the use of automobiles.','Plant more trees to increase the green cover.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/140412q.png'),
(140413,'biology','Products of anaerobic respiration are','Ethyl alcohol, CO₂  or lactic acid','(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;230, fig 14.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Fermentation',NULL,'2','mcq','Ethyl alcohol and lactic acid only','Ethyl alcohol, CO₂  or lactic acid','Ethyl alcohol and CO₂  and metabolic H₂O','Ethyl alcohol, metabolic H₂O, CO₂  and lactic acid','NEW','11-230','11-157',NULL),
(140414,'biology','Cramps are formed during vigorous exercise, it is due to production of _____ in muscles.','Lactic acid','During vigorous exercise, O₂ decrease, anaerobic pathway is adopted by cell, produces lactic acid\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;230,paragraph no.2).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Fermentation',NULL,'2','mcq','Ethyl alcohol','Acetic acid','Lactic acid','Acetyl coenzyme A','NEW','11-230','11-157',NULL),
(140415,'biology','Match the following diagram (1406)','1 - (c) ; 2 - (b) ; 3 - (a)','Incomplete oxidation-anaerobic conditions complete oxidation-Aerobic respiration. End product of anaerobic respiration-Lactic acid\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;230,paragraph no 1-2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Fermentation',NULL,'2','mcq','1 - (a) ; 2 - (b) ; 3 - (c)','1 - (b) ; 2 - (c) ; 3 - (a)','1 - (c) ; 2 - (a) ; 3 - (b)','1 - (c) ; 2 - (b) ; 3 - (a)','NEW','11-230','11-157','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/140415q.png'),
(140416,'biology','Place the following in order','3-phosphoglyceric acid > 2-phosphoglycerate > Pyruvate > Acetyl CoA','3-phosphoglyceric acid > 2-phosphoglycerate > Pyruvate > Acetyl CoA\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;230, figure 14.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Fermentation',NULL,'2','order','1, 3-phosphoglyceric acid','2, 2-phosphoglycerate','3, Pyruvate','4, Acetyl CoA','NEW','11-230','11-157',NULL),
(140501,'chemistry','Normal pH of rainwater is about :','5.6','We are aware that normally rain water has a pH of 5.6 due to the presence of H⁺ ions formed by the reaction of rain water with carbon dioxide present in the atmosphere.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 409; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'1','mcq','7','6.2','5.6','8.3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140502,'chemistry','Which gas in the atmosphere is responsible for rainwater\'s normal pH of 5.6?','CO₂','Normally rain water has a pH of 5.6 due to the presence of H⁺ ions formed by the reaction of rain water with CO₂ present in the atmosphere.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 409; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'1','mcq','CO₂','SO₂','O₂','NO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140503,'chemistry','When the pH of the rain water drops below ___, it is called acid rain.','5.6','When the pH of the rain water drops below 5.6, it is called acid rain.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'1','mcq','5.6','6.5','2.2','1.6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140504,'chemistry','Which of the following does not involve wet deposition of nitrogen and sulphur oxides?','Ground','Acid rain refers to the ways in which acid from the atmosphere is deposited on the earth’s surface. Oxides of nitrogen and sulphur which are acidic in nature can be blown by wind along with solid particles in the atmosphere and finally settle down either on the ground as dry deposition or in water, fog and snow as wet deposition.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'1','mcq','Water','Fog','Snow','Ground','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140505,'chemistry','What is the nature of oxides of sulphur and nitrogen?','Acidic','Acid rain refers to the ways in which acid from the atmosphere is deposited on the earth’s surface. Oxides of nitrogen and sulphur which are acidic in nature can be blown by wind along with solid particles in the atmosphere and finally settle down either on the ground as dry deposition or in water, fog and snow as wet deposition.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'2','mcq','Acidic','Basic','Neutral','Amphoteric','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140506,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Acid rain is a byproduct of a variety of human activities that emit the oxides of sulphur and nitrogen in the atmosphere.\n\nReason: Burning of fossil fuels such as coal and oil in power stations and furnaces or petrol and diesel in motor engines produce sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Acid rain is a byproduct of a variety of human activities that emit the oxides of sulphur and nitrogen in the atmosphere. As mentioned earlier, burning of fossil fuels (which\ncontain sulphur and nitrogenous matter) such as coal and oil in power stations and furnaces or petrol and diesel in motor engines produce sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140507,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: SO₂ and NO₂ after oxidation and reaction with water are major contributors to acid rain.\n\nReason: Polluted air usually contains particulate matter that inhibit the oxidation.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','SO₂ and NO₂ after oxidation and reaction with water are major contributors to acid rain, because polluted air usually contains particulate matter that catalyse the oxidation.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140508,'chemistry','NO₂ upon oxidation in air and reaction with water forms:','Nitric acid','SO₂ and NO₂ after oxidation and reaction with water are major contributors to acid rain, because polluted air usually contains particulate matter that catalyse the oxidation.\n4NO₂ (g) + O₂ (g)+ 2H₂O (l) → 4HNO₃ (aq)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'2','mcq','Nitrous acid','Hyponitrous acid','Nitric acid','Peroxynitric acid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140509,'chemistry','SO₂ upon oxidation in air and reaction with water forms which acid?','Sulphuric acid','SO₂ and NO₂ after oxidation and reaction with water are major contributors to acid rain, because polluted air usually contains particulate matter that catalyse the oxidation.\n2SO₂ (g) + O₂ (g) + 2H₂O (l) → 2H₂SO₄ (aq)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Paragraph No. 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'2','mcq','Sulphurous acid','Sulphanilic acid','Caro\'s acid','Sulphuric acid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140510,'chemistry','_____ salts can also be seen as atmospheric haze in form of aerosol of fine particles.','Ammonium','Ammonium salts are also formed and can be seen as an atmospheric haze (aerosol of fine particles). \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'1','mcq','Magnesium','Ammonium','Calcium','Sodium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140511,'chemistry','Which of the following results in dry deposition?','Direct SO₂ absorption on both solid and liquid ground surfaces.','Aerosol particles of oxides or ammonium salts in rain drops result in wet deposition. SO₂ is also absorbed directly on both solid and liquid ground surfaces and is thus deposited as dry-deposition.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'2','mcq','Direct SO₂ absorption on both solid and liquid ground surfaces.','Aerosol particle of oxides in rain drops.','Aerosol particles of ammonium salts in rain drops.','Indirect absorption of SO₂ on solid ground.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140512,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Acid rain is harmful for agriculture, trees and plants.\n\nReason: Acid rain dissolves and washes away the nutrients required for growth.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Acid rain is harmful for agriculture, trees and plants as it dissolves and washes away nutrients needed for their growth.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140513,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is incorrect with respect to effects of acid rain?','It prevents water pipes from corrosion','It causes respiratory ailments in human beings and animals. When acid rain falls and flows as ground water to reach rivers, lakes etc. it affects plants and animal life in aquatic ecosystem. It corrodes water pipes resulting in the leaching of heavy metals such as iron, lead and copper into the drinking water. Acid rain damages buildings and other structures made of stone or metal. The Taj Mahal in India has been affected by acid rain.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Paragraph No. 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'2','mcq','It causes respiratory ailments in humans.','It results in leaching of heavy metals such iron and lead into drinking water.','It prevents water pipes from corrosion','It affects the aquatic ecosystem.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/140513q.png'),
(140514,'chemistry','Which of the following method is used to reduce the acidity of soil?','Addition of powdered limestone','Methods to reduce the formation of acid rain:\ni. This can be done by reducing the emission of sulphur dioxide and nitrogen dioxide in the atmosphere. We should use less vehicles driven by fossil fuels; use less sulphur content fossil fuels for  power plants and industries. We should use natural gas which is a better fuel than coal or use coal with less sulphur content. \nii. Catalytic converters must be used in cars to reduce the effect of exhaust fumes on the atmosphere. The main component of the converter is a ceramic honeycomb coated with precious metals — Pd, Pt and Rh. The exhaust gases containing unburnt fuel, CO and NOₓ , when pass through the converter at 573 K, are converted into CO₂ and N₂. \niii. We can also reduce the acidity of the soil by adding powdered limestone to neutralise the acidity of the soil. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Activity 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'2','mcq','Addition of powdered limestone','Use of coal with high sulphur content','Use of ammonium fertilizers','Addition of particulates pollutants','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140515,'chemistry','Exhaust gases containing CO and NOₓ when passed through converter are converted to P and Q respectively, P and Q are:','CO₂ and N₂','Catalytic converters must be used in cars to reduce the effect of exhaust fumes on the atmosphere. The main component of the converter is a ceramic honeycomb coated with precious metals — Pd, Pt and Rh. The exhaust gases containing unburnt fuel, CO and NOₓ , when pass through the converter at 573 K, are converted into CO₂ and N₂. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Activity 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'2','mcq','CO₂ and N₂O','CO₂ and N₂','C and N₂','C and NO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140516,'chemistry','Which of the following methods can be used to reduce the formation of acid rain?','By using catalytic converters in vehicles.','Methods to reduce the formation of acid rain:\ni. This can be done by reducing the emission of sulphur dioxide and nitrogen dioxide in the atmosphere. We should use less vehicles driven by fossil fuels; use less sulphur content fossil fuels for  power plants and industries. We should use natural gas which is a better fuel than coal or use coal with less sulphur content. \nii. Catalytic converters must be used in cars to reduce the effect of exhaust fumes on the atmosphere. The main component of the converter is a ceramic honeycomb coated with precious metals — Pd, Pt and Rh. The exhaust gases containing unburnt fuel, CO and NOₓ , when pass through the converter at 573 K, are converted into CO₂ and N₂. \niii. We can also reduce the acidity of the soil by adding powdered limestone to neutralise the acidity of the soil. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Activity 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'2','mcq','Use of coal over natural gas, as coal is a better fuel.','By using catalytic converters in vehicles.','By using more vehicles driven by fossil fuels.','Use of coal with high sulphur content.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140517,'chemistry','Acid rain reacts with the marble of The Taj Mahal to give X, Y and Z? \nCaCO₃ + H₂SO₄  → X + Y + Z\nX is a solid insoluble in water, Y reacts with CuSO₄ to give blue color. What is formula of Z?','CO₂','The air around the city of Agra, where the Taj Mahal is located, contains fairly high levels of sulphur and nitrogen oxides. It is mainly due to a large number of industries and power plants around the area. Use of poor quality of coal, kerosene and firewood as fuel for domestic purposes add up to this problem. The resulting acid rain reacts with marble, CaCO₃ of Taj Mahal.\nCaCO₃ + H₂SO₄  → CaSO₄ + H₂O + CO₂ \nThus, causing damage to this wonderful monument that has attracted people from around the world. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Taj Mahal and Acid Rain)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'3','mcq','CO₂','H₂O','CaSO₄','CO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140518,'chemistry','The Government of India announced an action plan in early ______ to prevent the disfiguring of this historical monument.','1995','The Government of India announced an action plan in early 1995 to prevent the disfiguring of the, historical monument, Taj Mahal. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 410; Taj Mahal and Acid Rain)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Acid Rain',NULL,'1','mcq','1995','2000','1980','1965','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140601,'chemistry','The minute solid particles or liquid droplets in air are called:','Particulates pollutants','Particulates pollutants are the minute solid particles or liquid droplets in air. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'1','mcq','Viable pollutants','Non-viable pollutants','Particulates pollutants','Biodegradable pollutants','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140602,'chemistry','Which of the following is a viable particulate present in air?','Mould','Particulates in the atmosphere may be viable or non-viable. The viable particulates e.g., bacteria, fungi, moulds, algae etc., are minute living organisms that are dispersed in the atmosphere.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'1','mcq','Smoke','Mist','Fume','Mould','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140603,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n\nStatement I: Human being are allergic to some of the fungi found in air.\n\nStatement II: Fungi are non-viable particulates and can cause plant diseases.\n\nIn light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options below.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','The viable particulates e.g., bacteria, fungi, moulds, algae etc., are minute living organisms that are dispersed in the atmosphere. Human beings are allergic to some of the fungi found in air. They can also cause plant diseases. Human beings are allergic to some of the fungi found in air. They can also cause plant diseases.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140604,'chemistry','Which type of particulates consist of solid or mixture of solid and liquid particles formed during combustion of organic matter?','Smoke','Smoke particulates consist of solid or mixture of solid and liquid particles formed during combustion of organic matter. Examples are cigarette smoke, smoke from burning of fossil fuel, garbage and dry leaves, oil smoke etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'1','mcq','Dust','Smoke','Mist','Fumes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140605,'chemistry','_____  is composed of fine solid particles produced during crushing, grinding and attribution of solid materials.','Dust','Dust is composed of fine solid particles (over 1µm in diameter), produced during crushing, grinding and attribution of solid materials. Sand from sand blasting, saw dust from wood works, pulverized coal, cement and fly ash from factories, dust storms etc., are some typical examples of this type of particulate emission.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'1','mcq','Dust','Smoke','Mist','Fumes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140606,'chemistry','_____  are generally obtained by the condensation of vapours during sublimation, distillation, boiling and several other chemical reactions.','Fumes','Mists are produced by particles of spray liquids and by condensation of vapours in air. Examples are sulphuric acid mist and herbicides and insecticides that miss their targets and travel through air  and form mists.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'1','mcq','Smoke','Mist','Fumes','Smog','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140607,'chemistry','Which type of particulates are produced by particles of spray liquids and by condensation of vapours in air?','Mist','Fumes are generally obtained by the condensation of vapours during sublimation, distillation, boiling and several other chemical reactions. Generally, organic solvents, metals and metallic oxides form fume particles.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'1','mcq','Smoke','Mist','Fumes','Smog','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140608,'chemistry','Match the following non-viable particulates with their examples.','Smoke-oil smoke, dust-pulverized coal, Mist-herbicides, Fumes-metallic oxides','‣ Examples of smoke particulates are cigarette smoke, smoke from burning of fossil fuel, garbage and dry leaves, oil smoke etc.\n‣ Sand from sand blasting, saw dust from wood works, pulverized coal, cement and fly ash from factories, dust storms etc., are some typical examples of dust particulates emission.\n‣ Examples of mist particulates are sulphuric acid mist and herbicides and insecticides that miss their targets and travel through air  and form mists.\n‣ Examples of fume particulates are organic solvents, metals and metallic oxides.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 3,4,5,6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'3','match','Smoke,oil smoke','dust,pulverized coal','Mist,herbicides','Fumes,metallic oxides','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140609,'chemistry','Match the following non-viable particulates with their examples.','Smoke-cigarette smoke, dust-fly ash, Mist-insecticides, Fumes-organic solvents','‣ Examples of smoke particulates are cigarette smoke, smoke from burning of fossil fuel, garbage and dry leaves, oil smoke etc.\n‣ Sand from sand blasting, saw dust from wood works, pulverized coal, cement and fly ash from factories, dust storms etc., are some typical examples of dust particulates emission.\n‣ Examples of mist particulates are sulphuric acid mist and herbicides and insecticides that miss their targets and travel through air  and form mists.\n‣ Examples of fume particulates are organic solvents, metals and metallic oxides.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 3,4,5,6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'3','match','Smoke,cigarette smoke','dust,fly ash','Mist,insecticides','Fumes,organic solvents','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140610,'chemistry','The effect of particulate pollutants are largely dependent on the ______.','Size of particles','The effect of particulate pollutants are largely dependent on the particle size. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'1','mcq','Nature of particles','Size of particles','Number of particles','Surrounding environment','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140611,'chemistry','Particulate pollutants bigger than P microns are likely to lodge in the nasal passage, whereas particles of about Q micron enter into lungs easily. P and Q are','5 and 10','Particulate pollutants bigger than 5 microns are likely to lodge in the nasal passage, whereas particles of about 10 micron enter into lungs easily.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'1','mcq','5 and 10','10 and 5','100 and 20','2 and 5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140612,'chemistry','Which element used to be major air pollutant emitted by vehicles?','Pb','Lead used to be a major air pollutant emitted by vehicles. Leaded petrol used to be the primary source of airborne lead emission in Indian cities.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'1','mcq','Ce','Sr','Pb','Hg','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140613,'chemistry','Which of the following affects the maturation and development of red blood cells?','Lead','Lead interferes with the development and maturation of red blood cells.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Particulate pollutants',NULL,'1','mcq','Iron','Lead','Boron','Calcium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40415,'biology','Molluscs can be described by characters like \n\ni)Body is covered by a calcareous shell. \n\nii)Unsegmented body. \n\niii)Distinct head, muscular foot and visceral hump. \n\niv)They are bilaterally symmetrical, triploblastic and coelomate animals.','i, ii, iii and iv','They are bilaterally symmetrical, triploblastic and coelomate animals. Body is covered by a calcareous shell and is unsegmented with a distinct head, muscular foot and visceral hump.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','i, ii, iii and iv','ii, iii and iv','i, ii and iv','i, iii and iv','NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(140614,'biology','Which among the following is correct according to the conversion of succinyl-CoA to succinic acid ? \n\n1. During conversion of succinyl-CoA to succinic acid a molecule of GTP is synthesised \n\n2. In a coupled reaction GTP is converted to GDP with the simultaneous synthesis of ATP from ADP. \n\n3. It is a substrate level phosphorylation \n\n4. In a coupled reaction ATP is converted to ADP with the simultaneous synthesis of GTP from GDP.','Answers 1,2,3 are correct','Succinyl-CoA is oxidised to OAA allowing the cycle to continue. During the conversion of succinyl-CoA to succinic acid a molecule of GTP is synthesised. This is a substrate level phosphorylation. In a coupled reaction GTP is converted to GDP with the simultaneous synthesis of ATP from ADP \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;232,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','Answers 1,3,4 are correct','Answers 1,2,3 are correct','Only answers 1,3 are correct','Only answers 3,4 are correct','NEW','11-232','11-159',NULL),
(140615,'biology','There are _____ reactions in Krebs cycle , where NAD+ is reduced to NADH + H⁺','3','There are three points in the Krebs cycle where NAD+ is reduced to NADH + H⁺\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;232,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-232','11-159',NULL),
(140616,'biology','FAD+ is reduced to FADH₂ in how many reactions of citric acid cycle?','1','Only one point where FAD+  is reduced to FADH₂\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;232,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-232','11-159',NULL),
(140617,'biology','First member of citric acid cycle is','Oxaloacetic acid','First member of citric acid cycle is Oxaloacetic acid \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;232,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxaloacetic acid','Succinyl-CoA','Citric acid','Iso-Citrate','NEW','11-232','11-159',NULL),
(140618,'biology','The continued oxidation of ______ via the TCA cycle requires the continued replenishment of ________','Acetyl CoA,oxaloacetic acid','The continued oxidation of acetyl CoA via the TCA cycle requires the continued replenishment of oxaloacetic acid, the first member of the cycle.\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;232,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','Acetyl CoA,oxaloacetic acid','Acetyl CoA,pyruvate','Succinyl-CoA,succinic acid','Malic acid,OAA','NEW','11-232','11-159',NULL),
(140619,'biology','Match  a, b, c and d of the given diagram (1408)','a-Acetyl coenzyme A,b-αketoglutaric acid,c-Succinic acid,d-Malic acid','a-Acetyl coenzyme A,b-αketoglutaric acid,c-Succinic acid,d-Malic acid','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle',NULL,'2','match','a,Acetyl coenzyme A','b,αketoglutaric acid','c,Succinic acid','d,Malic acid','NEW','11-232','11-159','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/140619q.png'),
(140620,'biology','Till the end of TCA cycle how many NADH⁺H⁺ are formed by the breakdown of one molecule of glucose','10','10 molecules of NADH + H⁺ are formed by the end of TCA, where 2 in glycolysis, 2 in linker reaction and 6 in Citric acid cycle\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;232,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle',NULL,'1','mcq','10','9','8','7','NEW','11-232','11-159',NULL),
(140621,'biology','How many FADH₂ have been synthesised in TCA cycle by oxidation of single Acetyl CoA','1','1 molecules FADH₂ is  formed by single molecule of acetyl CoA at the end of TCA\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;232,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-232','11-159',NULL),
(140622,'biology','In a coupled reaction _____ is converted to _____ with the simultaneous synthesis of ____ from _____.','GTP,GDP,ATP,ADP','In a coupled reaction GTP is converted to GDP with the simultaneous synthesis of ATP from ADP. (Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;232,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Tricarboxylic Acid Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','ATP,ADP,GTP,GDP','GTP,GDP,ATP,ADP','ADP,ATP,GTP,GDP','GTP,GDP,ADP,ATP','NEW','11-232','11-159',NULL),
(140701,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','Smog is a mixture of smoke, fog and sulphur dioxide.','The word smog is derived from smoke and fog. There are two types of smog:\ni. Classical smog occurs in cool humid climate. It is a mixture of smoke, fog and sulphur dioxide.\nii. Photochemical smog occurs in warm, dry and sunny climate. \nOption (b) is incorrect as classical smog is a mixture of smoke, fog and sulphur dioxide. Song may contain other compounds depending upon its type.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'1','mcq','Classical smog occurs in cool humid climate.','Smog is a mixture of smoke, fog and sulphur dioxide.','Photochemical smog occurs in dry climate.','The word smog is derived from smoke and fog.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140702,'chemistry','Which is the most common example of air pollution that occurs in many cities throughout the world?','Smog','The word smog is derived from smoke and fog. This is the most common example of air pollution that occurs in many cities throughout the world.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'1','mcq','Fog','Smog','Mist','Fumes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140703,'chemistry','Which of the following is not the seen in Classical smog?','It consists of oxidising mixture.','Classical smog occurs in cool humid climate. It is a mixture of smoke, fog and sulphur dioxide. Chemically it is a reducing mixture and so it is also called as reducing smog.     \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 10)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'2','mcq','It occurs in cool climate.','It consists of oxidising mixture.','It is also called as reducing smog.','It is a mixture of smoke, fog and sulphur dioxide.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140704,'chemistry','Which of the following is incorrect about photochemical smog?','It is also called as reducing smog.','Photochemical smog occurs in warm, dry and sunny climate. The main components of the photochemical smog result from the action of sunlight on unsaturated hydrocarbons and nitrogen oxides produced by automobiles and factories. Photochemical smog has high concentration of oxidising agents and is, therefore, called as oxidising smog.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 11)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'2','mcq','It occurs in warm and dry climate.','It\'s main component are formed by action of sunlight.','It has high concentration of oxidising agents.','It is also called as reducing smog.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140705,'chemistry','The main components of the photochemical smog result from the action of sunlight on:','Unsaturated hydrocarbons and nitrogen oxides','The main components of the photochemical smog result from the action of sunlight on unsaturated hydrocarbons and nitrogen oxides produced by automobiles and factories. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 11)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'1','mcq','Unsaturated hydrocarbons and nitrogen oxides','Saturated hydrocarbons and sulphur oxides','Saturated hydrocarbons and dinitrogen','Unsaturated hydrocarbons and nitrogen amines','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140706,'chemistry','Which of the following chemicals is present in classical smog?','sulphur dioxide','It is a mixture of smoke, fog and sulphur dioxide. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 411; Paragraph 10)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'2','mcq','sulphur dioxide','acrolein','formaldehyde','nitrogen oxides','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140707,'chemistry','NO₂ absorbs energy from sunlight breaks into X and Y; Y is very reactive and combines with dioxygen to form ozone. Here Y is:','free oxygen atom','NO₂  in turn absorbs energy from sunlight and breaks up into nitric oxide and free oxygen atom. Oxygen atoms are very reactive and combine with the O₂  in air to produce ozone.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 412; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'2','mcq','Nitric oxide','free oxygen atom','biradical of oxygen','ozone','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140708,'chemistry','Ozone combines with NO(g) to generate a brown gas; the gas formed has the formula:','NO₂','The ozone reacts rapidly with the NO(g) to regenerate NO₂. \nNO (g) + O₃ (g) → NO₂ (g) + O₂ (g)\nNO₂ is a brown gas and at sufficiently high levels can contribute to haze.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 412; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'2','mcq','NO₂','NO','N₂O','O₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140709,'chemistry','Which of the following gases contributes to haze at high levels of concentration?','NO₂','NO₂ is a brown gas and at sufficiently high levels can contribute to haze.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 412; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'1','mcq','NO₂','NO','N₂O','SO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140710,'chemistry','Choose the strong oxidising agent from the list below.','O₃','Ozone is a toxic gas and strong oxidising agent that can react with the unburnt hydrocarbons in the polluted air to produce chemicals such as formaldehyde.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 412; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'1','mcq','O₂','O₃','NH₃','H₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140711,'chemistry','Which of the following is incorrect for acrolein?','It consists of 4 carbon atom in its formula.','Acrolein (CH₂=CHCH=O) is chemical present in photochemical smog. NO₂ and O₃ are strong oxidising agents and can react with the unburnt hydrocarbons in the polluted air to produce chemicals such as formaldehyde, acrolein and peroxyacetyl nitrate (PAN).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 412; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'3','mcq','It is found in photochemical smog.','It has two double bond equivalents.','It consists of 4 carbon atom in its formula.','It has a double bonded oxygen.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140712,'chemistry','When methane reacts with ozone to generate formaldehyde, a byproduct called X is formed. X in this reaction is:','H₂O','NO₂ and O₃ are strong oxidising agents and can react with the unburnt hydrocarbons in the polluted air to produce chemicals such as formaldehyde, acrolein and peroxyacetyl nitrate (PAN). \nThe reaction between methane and ozone to produce formaldehyde is as follows:\n3CH₄ + 2O₃ → 3CH₂=O + 3H₂O\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 412; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'2','mcq','H₂O','O₂','H₂O₂','CO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140713,'chemistry','A substance called PAN is produced in the photochemical smog. PAN is abbreviated as:','Peroxyacetyl nitrate','NO₂ and O₃ are strong oxidising agents and can react with the unburnt hydrocarbons in the polluted air to produce chemicals such as formaldehyde, acrolein and peroxyacetyl nitrate (PAN). \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 412; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'1','mcq','Peroxyacetyl nitronium','Peroxyacetyl nitrate','Perhydro Acetyl nitrate','Peroxyamino nitrate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140714,'chemistry','Which of the following does not occur commonly in photochemical smog?','CFCs','The common components of photochemical smog are ozone, nitric oxide, acrolein, formaldehyde and peroxyacetyl nitrate (PAN).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 412; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'1','mcq','Ozone','PAN','Acrolein','CFCs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140715,'chemistry','Choose the correct option(s) from the following statements regarding \' Effects of photochemical smog\'?\nA. PAN act as powerful nose irritant.\nB. Ozone and nitric oxide irritate the nose and throat, their high concentration causes headache, chest pain, dryness of the throat, cough and difficulty in breathing.\nC. Photochemical smog leads to cracking of rubber.\nD. Photochemical smog also stops the corrosion of metals , rubber and painted surfaces.','Both B and C','Photochemical smog causes serious health problems. Both ozone and PAN act as powerful eye irritants. Ozone and nitric oxide irritate the nose and throat and their high concentration causes headache, chest pain, dryness of the throat, cough and difficulty in breathing. Photochemical smog leads to cracking of rubber and extensive damage to plant life. It also causes corrosion of metals, stones, building materials, rubber and painted surfaces.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 412; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and B','Both B and C','Both C and D','Both A and D','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140716,'chemistry','Primary precursors of photochemical smog include:','NO₂','Primary precursors of photochemical smog include NO₂ and hydrocarbons. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'1','mcq','NO₂','Ozone','PAN','CFCs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140717,'chemistry','Select the incorrect option for primary precursors of photochemical smog.','PAN','Primary precursors of photochemical smog include NO₂ and hydrocarbons. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'1','mcq','NO₂','hydrocarbons','PAN','NO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140718,'chemistry','Which method is typically used to prevent the release of nitrogen oxides into the atmosphere by automobiles?','Catalytic converters','Usually catalytic converters are used in the automobiles, which prevent the release of nitrogen oxide and hydrocarbons to the atmosphere. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'1','mcq','Catalytic converters','Precipitator','Plantation','Lead chamber','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140719,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Certain plants could help in reducing the photochemical smog.\n\nReason: Pinus, Juniperus, Quercus, Pyrus and Vitis can metabolise nitrogen oxide.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Certain plants e.g., Pinus, Juniperus, Quercus, Pyrus and Vitis can metabolise nitrogen oxide and therefore, their plantation could help in this matter.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Smog',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140720,'biology','The presence of O₂ is vital because it drives the whole process by removing ______ from the ETS.','Hydrogen','The presence of O₂ is vital, since it drives the whole process by removing hydrogen from the system\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;233,paragraph no.2).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrogen','CO₂','Water','Electron','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(140721,'biology','O₂ acts as the final','Hydrogen Acceptor','O₂ acts as the final hydrogen acceptor. The presence of O₂ is vital, since it drives the whole process by removing hydrogen from the system\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;233,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrogen Acceptor','Carbon acceptor','Water','Neutron','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(140722,'biology','In photophosphorylation where it is the light energy that is utilised for the production of _______ gradient required for phosphorylation','Proton','In photophosphorylation where it is the light energy that is utilised for the production of proton gradient required for phosphorylation \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;233,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','mcq','Electron','Proton','Hydrogen','Neutron','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(140723,'biology','In respiration it is the energy of ______ utilised for the phosphorylation process.','Oxidation-reduction','Unlike photophosphorylation where it is the light energy that is utilised for the production of proton gradient required for phosphorylation, in respiration it is the energy of oxidation-reduction utilised for the same process. It is for this reason that the process is called oxidative phosphorylation.\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;233,paragraph no.2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxidation-reduction','Proton Gradient','Electron Gradient','Reduction Reactions','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(140724,'biology','Energy released during the electron transport system is utilised in synthesising ATP with the help of','ATP synthase','The energy released during the electron transport system is utilised in synthesising ATP with the help of ATP synthase (complex V).\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;234,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','mcq','Ubiquinone','ATP synthase','Cytochrome c oxidase','Cytochrome bc1 complex','NEW','11-234','11-161',NULL),
(140725,'biology','Two major components, F1 and F0 is present in which complex','Complex V','Complex V consists of two major components, F1 and F0\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;234,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','mcq','Complex I','Complex II','Complex IV','Complex V','NEW','11-234','11-161',NULL),
(140726,'biology','Which among the following is true for complex V of ETS','F1 headpiece is a peripheral membrane protein complex and contains the site for synthesis of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate.','The F1 headpiece is a peripheral membrane protein complex and contains the site for synthesis of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;233-234,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','mcq','F1 headpiece is a peripheral membrane protein complex and contains the site for synthesis of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate.','F0 is an integral membrane protein complex that forms the channel through which protons cross the outer membrane.','The passage of electrons through the channel is coupled to the catalytic site of the F1 component for the production of ATP.','All the Above','NEW','11-234','11-161',NULL),
(140727,'biology','For each ATP produced,______ passes through F0','2H⁺','For each ATP produced, 2H⁺  passes through F0 from the intermembrane space to the matrix down the electrochemical proton gradient.\n\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;234,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','mcq','4H⁺','2H⁺','6H⁺','H⁺','NEW','11-234','11-161',NULL),
(140728,'biology','Passing of H⁺ through F0 take place from the intermembrane space to the ______ down the electrochemical proton gradient.','Matrix','The passage of protons through the channel is coupled to the catalytic site of the F1 component for the production of ATP. For each ATP produced, 4H⁺ passes through F0 from the intermembrane space to the matrix down the electrochemical proton gradient. \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;234,paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','fillup','Ma,tr,ix',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-234','11-161',NULL),
(140729,'biology','Match the following complex with their names diagram (1409)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (a) ; (iii) - (d) ; (iv) - (b)','Ubiquinone also receives reducing equivalents via\nFADH₂ (complex II is succinate dehydrogenase) is generated during the oxidation of succinate in the citric acid cycle. The reduced ubiquinone (ubiquinol) is then oxidised with the transfer of electrons to cytochrome c via cytochrome bc1 complex (complex III). Cytochrome c is a small protein attached to the outer surface of the inner membrane and acts as a mobile carrier for the transfer of electrons between complexes III and IV. Complex IV refers  to cytochrome c oxidase complex containing cytochromes a and a3, and two cop%res \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;233-234, paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (b) ; (iii) - (c) ; (iv) - (d)','(i) - (b) ; (ii) - (c) ; (iii) - (d) ; (iv) - (a)','(i) - (c) ; (ii) - (a) ; (iii) - (d) ; (iv) - (b)','(i) - (a) ; (ii) - (d) ; (iii) - (b) ;(iv) - (c)','NEW','11-234','11-161','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/140729q.png'),
(140730,'biology','Match the following parts a,b,c, and d of the given diagram (1410)','a-ATP,b-ADP,c-2H⁺,d-Inner mitochondrial membrane','a-ATP,b-ADP,c-2H⁺,d-Inner mitochondrial membrane','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','match','a,ATP','b,ADP','c,2H⁺','d,Inner mitochondrial membrane','NEW','11-234','11-161','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/140730q.png'),
(140731,'biology','The mechanism of membrane-linked ATP synthesis as explained by?','Chemiosmotic hypothesis','The mechanism of membrane-linked ATP synthesis as explained by chemiosmotic hypothesis. (Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;233,paragraph no.3)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation',NULL,'2','mcq','Chemiosmotic hypothesis','Electromagnetic hypothesis','Electrochemical hypothesis','Reverse osmosis theory','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(140801,'chemistry','The wavelength of UV light is:','255 nm','Ultraviolet (UV) radiations have a wavelength of λ 255 nm.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','255 nm','0.2 nm','430 nm','1200 nm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140802,'chemistry','UV radiations can cause which of the following disease?','Melanoma','Ultraviolet (UV) radiations cause skin cancer (melanoma) in humans. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Melanoma','Diabetes','AIDS','Smallpox','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140803,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: It is important to maintain the ozone shield.\n\nReason: UV rays are responsible for skin cancer and also damages the cornea.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The upper stratosphere consists of considerable amount of ozone (O₃), which protects us from the harmful ultraviolet (UV) radiations (λ 255 nm) coming from the sun. These radiations cause skin cancer (melanoma) in humans. Therefore, it is important to maintain the ozone shield.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140804,'chemistry','Ozone is thermodynamically ______.','Unstable','Ozone is thermodynamically unstable and decomposes to molecular oxygen. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Stable','Unstable','Non Reactive','Non-decomposable','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140805,'chemistry','Consider the following reactions taking place in the presence of UV light:\nA → B + B\nB + A  ⇌ O₃\nHere, A and B are:','O₂ and O','Ozone in the stratosphere is a product of UV radiations acting on dioxygen (O₂) molecules. The UV radiations split apart molecular oxygen into free oxygen (O) atoms. These oxygen atoms combine with the molecular oxygen to form ozone. \nO₂ (g)  →  O(g) + O(g) (In the presence of UV light)\nO(g) + O₂ (g)  ⇌  O₃ (g) (In the presence of UV light)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','O and O₂','O₂ and O','O and O₃','O₂ and H₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140806,'chemistry','Given reaction takes place in presence of which factor?\nO₂ + O ⇌ O₃','UV rays','Ozone in the stratosphere is a product of UV radiations acting on dioxygen (O₂) molecules. The UV radiations split apart molecular oxygen into free oxygen (O) atoms. These oxygen atoms combine with the molecular oxygen to form ozone. \nO₂ (g)  →  O(g) + O(g) (In the presence of UV light)\nO(g) + O₂ (g)  ⇌  O₃ (g) (In the presence of UV light)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Stratospheric Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','UV rays','IR rays','Temperature less than 40K','In dark (absence of sunlight)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140901,'chemistry','Around which time ozone hole was reported by scientists working at Antarctica?','1980s','In 1980s atmospheric scientists working in Antarctica reported about depletion of ozone layer commonly known as ozone hole over the South Pole.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> The Ozone Hole',NULL,'1','mcq','1980s','2010s','2020s','1950s','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140902,'chemistry','Chlorine sinks are formed in:','summer','In summer season, nitrogen dioxide and methane react with chlorine monoxide and chlorine atoms forming chlorine sinks, preventing much ozone depletion, whereas in winter, special type of clouds called polar stratospheric clouds are formed over Antarctica. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> The Ozone Hole',NULL,'1','mcq','winter','summer','autumn','spring','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140903,'chemistry','In winter, special type of clouds called polar stratospheric clouds are formed over _____.','Antarctica','In summer season, nitrogen dioxide and methane react with chlorine monoxide and chlorine atoms forming chlorine sinks, preventing much ozone depletion, whereas in winter, special type of clouds called polar stratospheric clouds are formed over Antarctica. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> The Ozone Hole',NULL,'1','mcq','Africa','Antarctica','Europe','Asia','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140904,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Much of ozone depletion is prevented during summer season in Antarctica.\n\nReason: In summer season, nitrogen dioxide and methane react with chlorine monoxide and chlorine atoms','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','In summer season, nitrogen dioxide and methane react with chlorine monoxide and chlorine atoms forming chlorine sinks, preventing much ozone depletion.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> The Ozone Hole',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140905,'chemistry','Chlorine nitrate upon hydrolysis over stratospheric clouds gives two acids A and B. A is photolysed to give chlorine radical. What is the formula for B?','HNO₃','In winter, special type of clouds called polar stratospheric clouds are formed over Antarctica.  These polar stratospheric clouds provide surface on which chlorine nitrate formed  {reaction i} gets hydrolysed to form hypochlorous acid {reaction iii}. It also reacts with hydrogen chloride produced as per reaction {reaction ii} to give molecular chlorine {reaction iv}.\nClO• (g)  +  NO₂ (g) → ClONO₂ (g)  {reaction i}\nCl• (g)  +  CH₄ (g) → CH₃• (g) + HCl(g)  {reaction ii}\nClONO₂ (g) + H₂O(g) → HOCl (g) + HNO₃ (g)  {reaction iii}\nClONO₂ (g) + HCl (g) → Cl₂ (g) + HNO₃ (g)  {reaction iv}\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> The Ozone Hole',NULL,'2','mcq','HNO₃','HNO₂','HOCl','HCl','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140906,'chemistry','Chlorine nitrate reacts with water and hydrochloric acid in separate reactions.\nClONO₂ + H₂O → HOCl + HNO₃\nClONO₂ + HCl → X + HNO₃, \nHere, X has formula of:','Cl₂','Polar stratospheric clouds provide surface on which chlorine nitrate formed gets hydrolysed to form hypochlorous acid. It also reacts with hydrogen chloride produced to give molecular chlorine.\nClONO₂ (g) + H₂O(g) → HOCl (g) + HNO₃ (g) \nClONO₂ (g) + HCl (g) → Cl₂ (g) + HNO₃ (g)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> The Ozone Hole',NULL,'2','mcq','HOCl','HClO₃','HCl','Cl₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40416,'biology','Identify the organism shown in diagram (0426)','Apple snail','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Figure 4.13(a) )','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Apple snail','Devil fish','Sepia','Aplysia','NEW','11-053','11-044','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40416q.png'),
(140907,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is incorrect with respect to \' Ozone hole formation\' ?\nA. Chlorine monoxide reacts with nitrogen dioxide to form chlorine nitrate.\nB. Methane reacts with chlorine radical to form hydrochloric acid.\nC. Summer clouds help release chlorine radicals by providing surface for reactions to occur.\nD. When sunlight returns to the Antarctica in the spring, the sun’s warmth breaks up the clouds and HOCl is photolysed by sunlight.','Only C','In winter, special type of clouds called polar stratospheric clouds are formed over Antarctica. These polar stratospheric clouds provide surface on which chlorine nitrate formed  {reaction i} gets hydrolysed to form hypochlorous acid {reaction iii}. It also reacts with hydrogen chloride produced as per reaction {reaction ii} to give molecular chlorine {reaction iv}.\nClO• (g)  +  NO₂ (g) → ClONO₂ (g)  {reaction i}\nCl• (g)  +  CH₄ (g) → CH₃• (g) + HCl(g)  {reaction ii}\nClONO₂ (g) + H₂O(g) → HOCl (g) + HNO₃ (g)  {reaction iii}\nClONO₂ (g) + HCl (g) → Cl₂ (g) + HNO₃ (g)  {reaction iv}\nWhen sunlight returns to the Antarctica in the spring, the sun’s warmth breaks up the clouds and HOCl and Cl₂ are photolysed by sunlight.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> The Ozone Hole',NULL,'2','mcq','Only A','Only B','Only C','Only D','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140908,'chemistry','Arrange in the order in which the depletion of ozone takes place.','Formation of chlorine nitrate>Hydrolysis to form chlorine>Sunlight breaks chlorine into radicals>Chlorine radicals initiate chain reaction for ozone depletion','In winter, special type of clouds called polar stratospheric clouds are formed over Antarctica.  These polar stratospheric clouds provide surface on which chlorine nitrate formed  {reaction i} gets hydrolysed to form hypochlorous acid {reaction iii}. It also reacts with hydrogen chloride produced as per reaction {reaction ii} to give molecular chlorine {reaction iv}.\nClO• (g)  +  NO₂ (g) → ClONO₂ (g)  {reaction i}\nCl• (g)  +  CH₄ (g) → CH₃• (g) + HCl(g)  {reaction ii}\nClONO₂ (g) + H₂O(g) → HOCl (g) + HNO₃ (g)  {reaction iii}\nClONO₂ (g) + HCl (g) → Cl₂ (g) + HNO₃ (g)  {reaction iv}\nWhen sunlight returns to the Antarctica in the spring, the sun’s warmth breaks up the clouds and HOCl and Cl₂ are photolysed by sunlight. The chlorine radicals thus formed, initiate the chain reaction for ozone depletion\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 413; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> The Ozone Hole',NULL,'3','order','1,Formation of chlorine nitrate','2,Hydrolysis to form chlorine','3,Sunlight breaks chlorine into radicals','4,Chlorine radicals initiate chain reaction for ozone depletion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(140909,'chemistry','Which of following is not caused by UV radiations?','Synthesis of phytoplankton','UV radiations lead to ageing of skin, cataract, sunburn, skin cancer, killing of many phytoplanktons, damage to fish productivity etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> The Ozone Hole',NULL,'1','mcq','Ageing of skin','Synthesis of phytoplankton','Cataract','Melanoma','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141001,'chemistry','Easily identified source or place of pollution is called as:','Point source','Easily identified source or place of pollution is called as point source. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Particular source','Polluting source','Non-point source','Point source','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141002,'chemistry','Choose the odd one with respect to Non-point source of pollution.','Municipal discharge pipes','Non point sources of pollution are those where a source of pollution cannot be easily identified, e.g., agricultural runoff (from farm, animals and crop-lands), acid rain, storm-water drainage (from streets, parking lots and lawns), etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Municipal discharge pipes','Agricultural runoff','Acid rain','storm-water drainage','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141003,'chemistry','Match the following pollutants with their sources.','Microbes-Domestic sewage,Plant nutrients-Chemical fertilizers,Heavy metals-Chemical factories,Radioactive substances-Mining of uranium minerals','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Table 14.1) diagram(ne1401)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'3','match','Microbes,Domestic sewage','Plant nutrients,Chemical fertilizers','Heavy metals,Chemical factories','Radioactive substances,Mining of uranium minerals','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/141003q.png'),
(141004,'chemistry','Most serious water pollutants are:','Pathogens','The most serious water pollutants are the disease causing agents called pathogens. Pathogens include bacteria and other organisms that enter water from domestic sewage and animal excreta. Human excreta contain bacteria such as Escherichia coli and Streptococcus faecalis which cause gastrointestinal diseases.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Organic waste','Pathogens','Chemical pollutants','Metallic ions','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141005,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n\nStatement I: Pathogens include bacteria and other organisms that enter water from domestic sewage and animal excreta.\n\nStatement II: Human excreta contain bacteria such as Escherichia coli and Streptococcus faecalis which cause gastrointestinal diseases.\n\nChoose the best answer based on the statements above.','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','The most serious water pollutants are the disease causing agents called pathogens. Pathogens include bacteria and other organisms that enter water from domestic sewage and animal excreta. Human excreta contain bacteria such as Escherichia coli and Streptococcus faecalis which cause gastrointestinal diseases.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 414; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Water pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141101,'chemistry','Match the Maximum Prescribed Concentration(ppm) of some metals in drinking Water.','Fe-0.2,Cu-3.0,Zn-5.0,Mn-0.05','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Table 14.2) diagram(ne1403)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'3','match','Fe,0.2','Cu,3.0','Zn,5.0','Mn,0.05','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141102,'chemistry','What is the maximum prescribed concentration(ppm) of cadmium in water?','0.005','(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Table 14.2) diagram(ne1404)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'1','mcq','0.05','5','0.5','0.005','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141103,'chemistry','Which two metals have same maximum prescribed concentration(ppm) in drinking water?','Fe and Al','Fe and Al have same maximum prescribed concentration(ppm) of 0.2 ppm.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Table 14.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe and Al','Zn and Mn','Fe and Cu','Cu and Cd','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141104,'chemistry','Fluoride deficiency can lead to which of the following diseases?','Tooth decay','For drinking purposes, water should be tested for fluoride ion concentration. Its deficiency in drinking water is harmful to man and causes diseases such as tooth decay etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'1','mcq','Tooth decay','Laxative effect','Damage to kidney','Methemoglobinemia','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141105,'chemistry','What is the optimum concentration (ppm) of fluoride ions in drinking water?','1 ppm','For drinking purposes, water should be tested for fluoride ion concentration. Its deficiency in drinking water is harmful to man and causes diseases such as tooth decay etc. Soluble fluoride is often added to drinking water to bring its concentration upto 1 ppm or 1 mg dm⁻³. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'1','mcq','1 ppm','10 ppm','50 ppm','2 ppm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141106,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Fluoride ions make enamel of teeth much harder.\n\nReason: Fluoride ions convert fluorapatite into much harder hydroxyapatite.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','The F⁻ ions make the enamel on teeth much harder by converting hydroxyapatite, the enamel on the surface of the teeth, into much harder fluorapatite.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 415; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141107,'chemistry','Fluoride ion concentration above 2 ppm causes:','Brown mottling of teeth','F⁻ ion concentration above 2 ppm causes brown mottling of teeth.  At the same time, excess fluoride (over 10 ppm) causes harmful effect to bones and teeth, as reported from some parts of Rajasthan.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'1','mcq','Brown mottling of teeth','Harmful effects to bones','Laxative effect','Methemoglobinemia','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141108,'chemistry','The maximum limit of nitrate in drinking water is:','50 ppm','The maximum limit of nitrate in drinking water is 50 ppm. Excess nitrate in drinking water can cause disease such as methemoglobinemia (‘blue baby’ syndrome).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'1','mcq','50 ppm','50 ppb','500 ppm','0.3 ppm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141109,'chemistry','Excessive sulphate in drinking causes:','Laxative effect','Excessive sulphate (>500 ppm) in drinking water causes laxative effect, otherwise at moderate levels it is harmless.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'1','mcq','Brown mottling of teeth','Harmful effects to bones','Laxative effect','Methemoglobinemia','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141110,'chemistry','The prescribed upper limit concentration of lead in drinking water is about  _____.','50 ppb','The prescribed upper limit concentration of lead in drinking water is about 50 ppb. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'1','mcq','50 ppm','50 ppb','0.005 ppb','3 ppm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141111,'chemistry','Excess nitrate in drinking water can cause disease such as:','Methemoglobinemia','Excess nitrate in drinking water can cause disease such as methemoglobinemia (‘blue baby’ syndrome).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'1','mcq','Brown mottling of teeth','Damage to kidney and liver.','Laxative effect','Methemoglobinemia','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141112,'chemistry','Match the prescribed upper limits of the ions given below in the water.','Sulphate-500 ppm,Nitrate- 50 ppm,Fluoride- 1 ppm,Lead-50 ppb','The prescribed upper limits of sulphate, nitrate, fluoride and lead are 500 ppm, 50 ppm, 1 ppm, and 50 ppb respectively.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 1,2,3,4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> International Standards for Drinking Water',NULL,'3','match','Sulphate,500 ppm','Nitrate,50 ppm','Fluoride,1 ppm','Lead,50 ppb','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141113,'biology','Oxidative phosphorylation is [NEET-II 2016]','Formation of ATP by energy released from electrons removed during substrate oxidation','Oxidative phosphorylation is the synthesis of energy rich ATP molecules with the help of energy liberated during oxidation of reduced co-enzymes (NADH, FADH₂) produced in respiration(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;233,paragraph no.3)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Formation of ATP by transfer of phosphate group from a substrate to ADP','Oxidation of phosphate group in ATP','Addition of phosphate group to ADP','Formation of ATP by energy released from electrons removed during substrate oxidation','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(141114,'biology','Cytochromes are found in [2015 Cancelled]','Cristae of mitochondria','Cytochrome is a group of proteins, each with an iron containing heme group. They are a part of electron transport chain in mitochondria (present in cristae) and chloroplasts (in thylakoids).(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;233,paragraph no.2-3)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Cristae of mitochondria','Inner membrane of Mitchondria','Matrix of mitochondria','Outer wall of mitochondria','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(141115,'biology','In mitochondria, protons accumulate in the\n[MAINS 2011]','Intermembrane space','During aerobic respiration in mitochondria, protons accumulate in the intermembrane space while electrons move through the electron transport chain.','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Outer membrane','Inner membrane','Intermembrane space','Matrix','NEW','11-234','11-161',NULL),
(141116,'biology','All enzymes of TCA cycle are located in the mitochondrial matrix except one which is located in inner mitochondrial membranes in eukaryotes and in cytosol in prokaryotes. This enzyme is\n[2007]','Succinate dehydrogenase','Mitochondrion is the organelle which bears various enzymes participating in Krebs’ cycle. Each mitochondrion is covered by double membrane. The inner membrane is selectively permeable and forms foldings called cristae. The inner membrane bears oxysomes, enzymes of fatty acids, succinate dehydrogenase (of Krebs’ cycle) and electron transport system. All other enzymes of Krebs’ cycle are present in the mitochondrial matrix','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Isocitrate dehydrogenase','Malate dehydrogenase','Succinate dehydrogenase','Lactate dehydrogenase','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(40417,'biology','Identify the organism shown in diagram (0427)','Devil fish','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53; Figure 4.13(b) )','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Apple snail','Devil fish','Sepia','Aplysia','NEW','11-053','11-044','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40417q.png'),
(141117,'biology','In which one of the following do the two names refer to one and the same thing ?\n[2003]','Tricarboxylic acid cycle and citric acid cycle','The reactions of Krebs’ cycle were worked out by Sir Hans Krebs, hence the name Krebs’ cycle. It involves many 3-C compounds such as citric acid, cis-aconitic acid and isocitric acid etc., so it is also called TCA cycle or tricarboxylic acid, cycle. It involves formation of citric acid as its first product so it is called citric acid cycle. It involves production of 24 ATP molecules','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Krebs’ cycle and Calvin cycle','Tricarboxylic acid cycle and citric acid cycle','Citric acid cycle and Calvin cycle','Tricarboxylic acid cycle and Calvin cycle','NEW','11-231','11-158',NULL),
(141118,'biology','Which of the following statements are incorrect?\n[NEET 2021]','In ETC (Electron Transport Chain), one molecule of NADH + H⁺ gives rise to 2 ATP molecules, and one FADH₂ gives rise to 3 ATP molecules','(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;233,paragraph no.1-2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxidation-reduction reactions produce proton gradient in respiration','During aerobic respiration, role is O₂ is limited to the terminal state','In ETC (Electron Transport Chain), one molecule of NADH + H⁺ gives rise to 2 ATP molecules, and one FADH₂ gives rise to 3 ATP molecules','ATP is synthesized through complex V','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(141201,'chemistry','Which of the following is a natural pesticide?','Nicotine','Prior to World War II, many naturally occurring chemicals such as nicotine (by planting tobacco plants in the crop field), were used as pest controlling substance for major crops in agricultural practices.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Nicotine','DDT','Malathion','Ammonia','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141202,'chemistry','Which among the following is not responsible for soil pollution?','Organic fertilizers','The insecticides, pesticides and herbicides cause soil pollution. Organic fertilizers, on the other hand, are sustainable and environmentally friendly.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Insecticides','Pesticides','Organic fertilizers','Herbicides','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141203,'chemistry','During World War II, _____  was found to be of great use in the control of malaria and other insect-borne diseases.','DDT','During World War II, DDT was found to be of great use in the control of malaria and other insect-borne diseases.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','DDT','Malathion','PCBs','CFCs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141204,'chemistry','What was the use of DDT after the second world war?','In agriculture to control the damages caused by insects, rodents, weeds.','During World War II, DDT was found to be of great use in the control of malaria and other insect-borne diseases. Therefore, after the war, DDT was put to use in agriculture to control the damages caused by insects, rodents, weeds and various crop diseases. However, due to adverse effects, its use has been banned in India.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','In agriculture to control the damages caused by insects, rodents, weeds.','In medicine as cure for malaria.','In industries to prepare medicine for dengue.','In medicine to cure insect-borne diseases.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141205,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect option regarding pesticides.','Eco-friendly','Pesticides are basically synthetic toxic chemicals with ecological repercussions.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Synthetic chemicals','Toxic in nature','Eco-friendly','Used in agriculture','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141206,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are incorrect regarding \'organic toxins\' ?\nA. They are water soluble and biodegradable.\nB. Aldrin and Dieldrin are organic toxins, that are insoluble and non biodegradable.\nC. They did not cause serious metabolic disorders in animals.','Both A and C','Due to the increased insect resistance of DDT, other organic toxins such as Aldrin and Dieldrin were introduced in the market by pesticide industry. Most of the organic toxins are water insoluble and nonbiodegradable. These high persistent toxins are, therefore, transferred from lower trophic level to higher trophic level through food chain. Over the time, the concentration of toxins in higher animals reach a level which causes serious metabolic and physiological disorders.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 417; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Only B','Both A and C','Both B and C','A, B and C','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141207,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: Pesticides become ineffective after some time of repeated use.\n\nReason: The repeated use of the same or similar pesticides give rise to pests that are resistant to that pesticides.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The repeated use of the same or similar pesticides give rise to pests that are resistant to that group of pesticides thus making the pesticides ineffective.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 416; Paragraph 9)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141208,'chemistry','The pollutant becomes _____ times more concentrated at each trophic level.','10','At each trophic level, the pollutant gets 10 times concentrated.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 417; Figure 14.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','10','100','2','0.5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141209,'chemistry','Which of the following is a less persistent molecule?','Organophosphates','In response to high persistence of chlorinated organic toxins, a new series of less persistent or more biodegradable products called organophosphates and carbamates have been introduced in the market. But these chemicals are severe nerve toxins and hence more harmful to humans. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 417; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Organophosphates','DDT','PCBs','Chlorinated organic toxins','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141210,'chemistry','Which of the following act as nerve toxin?','Organophosphates','In response to high persistence of chlorinated organic toxins, a new series of less persistent or more biodegradable products called organophosphates and carbamates have been introduced in the market. But these chemicals are severe nerve toxins and hence more harmful to humans. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 417; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Organophosphates','PCBs','CFCs','Aldrin','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141211,'chemistry','Sodium chlorate and sodium arsenate are example of:','Herbicides','Sodium  chlorate (NaClO₃), sodium arsenite (Na₃AsO₃) are example of herbicides.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 417; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'1','mcq','Pesticides','Herbicides','Insecticides','Fungicides','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141212,'chemistry','Choose the correct option(s) from the following statements.\nA. Most herbicides are toxic to mammals but are not as persistent as organo-chlorides.\nB. Herbicides become concentrated in food web and cause birth defects.\nC. Fields sprayed with herbicides are less prone to insect attack and plant disease.\nD. Herbicides are eco-friendly.','Both A and B','Most herbicides are toxic to mammals but are not as persistent as organo-chlorides. These chemicals decompose in a few months. Like organo-chlorides, these too become concentrated in the food web. Some herbicides cause birth defects. Studies show that cornfields sprayed with herbicides are more prone to insect attack and plant disease than fields that are weeded manually.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 417; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Soil Pollution',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and B','Both B and C','Both C and D','Both A and D','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141213,'biology','What is the net gain of ATP when each molecule of glucose in converted to two molecules of pyruvic acid? [NEET 2022]','Two','The conversion of BPGA to 3-phosphoglyceric acid (PGA), is also an energy yielding process; this energy is trapped by the formation of ATP. Another ATP is synthesized during the conversion of PEP to pyruvic acid. (Reference  : NCERT, page 229, paragraph 3, Figure 14.1).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Four','Six','Two','Eight','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(141215,'biology','What amount of energy is released from glucose during lactic acid fermentation?\n[NEET 2022]','Less than 7%','In both lactic acid and alcohol fermentation not much energy is released; less than 7 % of the energy in glucose is released and not all of it is trapped as high energy bonds of ATP.\n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;230,paragraph no.3)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Approximately 15%','More than 18%','About 10%','Less than 7%','NEW','11-230','11-157',NULL),
(141216,'biology','R.Q. is(1988)','CO₂ /oxygen','(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;236 paragraph no.1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 2',NULL,'2','mcq','C/N','N/C','CO₂ /oxygen','oxygen/CO₂','NEW','11-236','11-163',NULL),
(141217,'biology','The number of time(s) decarboxylation of isocitrate occurs during single TCA cycle is : [ RE NEET 2022]','(2) Two','In the Krebs cycle, decarboxylation occurs in two steps.\nFirst oxidative decarboxylation occurs during the conversion of isocitrate into α-ketoglutarate.\nThis reaction is catalyzed by isocitrate dehydrogenase.\nSecond oxidative decarboxylation takes place when α-ketoglutarate is converted to succinyl-coenzyme A with the help of α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) One','(2) Two','(3) Three','(4) Four','NEW','11-231','11-158',NULL),
(141218,'biology','Fatty acids are connected with the respiratory pathway through:','(3) Acetyl CoA','If fatty acids were to be respired it would first be degraded to acetyl CoA and then enter into respiratory pathway.','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Dihydroxy acetone phosphate','(2) Pyruvic acid','(3) Acetyl CoA','(4) alpha-Ketoglutaric acid','NEW','11-231','11-158',NULL),
(141219,'biology','Match List I with List II :\nList I List II\nA. Oxidative decarboxylation I. Citrate synthase\nB. Glycolysis II. Pyruvate dehydrogenase\nC. Oxidative phosphorylation III. Electron transport system\nD. Tricarboxylic acid cycle IV. EMP pathway\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023]','A – II, B – IV, C – III, D – I','A – II, B – IV, C – III, D – I','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 2',NULL,'1','mcq','A – II, B – IV, C – I, D – III','A – III, B – I, C – II, D – IV','A – II, B – IV, C – III, D – I','A – III, B – IV, C – II, D – I','NEW','11-231','11-158',NULL),
(141220,'biology','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion (A) and other is labelled as reason (R):  Assertion (A) ATP is used in two steps in glycolysis Reason(R): First ATP is used in converting glucose to glucose-6-phosphhate and second ATP is used in conversion of fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate. In the light of above statement choose correct answer from options given below (NEET 2023)','2. Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is correct explanation of (A)','ATP is utilised at two steps: first in the conversion of glucose into glucose 6-phosphate and second in the conversion of fructose  6-phosphate to fructose 1, 6-bisphosphate. \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no 229, paragraph 2)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 2',NULL,'3','mcq','1. (A) is false but (R) is true.','2. Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is correct explanation of (A)','3. Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not correct explanation of (A).','4. (A) is true but (R) is false.','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(141221,'biology','The 5-C compound formed during TCA cycle is (NEET 2022)','alpha ketoglutaric acid','It is followed by two successive steps of decarboxylation, leading to the formation of α-ketoglutaric acid and then succinyl-CoA. In the remaining steps of citric acid cycle, succinyl-CoA is oxidised to OAA allowing the cycle to continue. During the conversion of succinyl-CoA to succinic acid a molecule of GTP is synthesised. This is a substrate level phosphorylation. \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no 232, paragraph 1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 2',NULL,'3','mcq','alpha ketoglutaric acid','Oxalo succinic acid','succinic acid','Fumaric acid','NEW','11-232','11-159',NULL),
(141222,'biology','Match List  I with List II    (a) ETS complex-I (b) ETS complex-II (c) ETS complex III (d) ETS complex IV     List II (i) cytochrome bc1 (ii) Cytochrome a, a3 and two copper centers (iii) NADH dehydrogenase (iv) Ubiquinone and FADH2 dehydrogenase. Choose the correct answer from options given below (NEET 2022)','(a)-(iii),(b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)','Electrons from NADH produced in the mitochondrial matrix during citric acid cycle are oxidised by an NADH dehydrogenase (complex I), and electrons are then transferred to ubiquinone located within the inner membrane. Ubiquinone also receives reducing equivalents via FADH2 (complex II) that is generated during oxidation of succinate in the citric acid cycle. The reduced ubiquinone (ubiquinol) is then oxidised with the transfer of electrons to cytochrome c via cytochrome bc1 complex (complex III). Cytochrome c is a small protein attached to the outer surface of the inner membrane and acts as a mobile carrier for transfer of electrons between complex III and IV. Complex IV refers to cytochrome c oxidase complex containing cytochromes a and a3, and two copper centres \n(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no 233, paragraph 1)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Question 2',NULL,'3','Match','(a)-(iii),(b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)','(a)-(i),(b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)','(a)-(i),(b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)','(a)-(iii),(b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141301,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect option with respect to non-biodegradable wastes.','wastes from cotton mill','Biodegradable wastes are generated by cotton mills, food processing units, paper mills, and textile factories.\nNon-biodegradable wastes are generated by thermal power plants which produce fly ash; integrated iron and steel plants which produce blast furnace slag and steel melting slag. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 417; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Industrial Waste',NULL,'1','mcq','blast furnace slag','fly ash','tailings and mud','wastes from cotton mill','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141302,'chemistry','Match the industries with the type of waste they produce.','Cooper-mud and tailings,Iron plants-blast furnace slag,Fertilizer-gypsum,Thermal power plant-fly ash','Non-biodegradable wastes are generated by thermal power plants which produce fly ash; integrated iron and steel plants which produce blast furnace slag and steel melting slag. \nIndustries manufacturing aluminium, zinc and copper produce mud and tailings. Fertilizer industries produce gypsum. Hazardous wastes such as inflammables, composite explosives or highly reactive substances are produced by industries dealing in metals, chemicals, drugs, pharmaceuticals, dyes, pesticides, rubber goods etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 417; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Industrial Waste',NULL,'3','match','Copper,mud and tailings','Iron plants,blast furnace slag','Fertilizer,gypsum','Thermal power plants,fly ash','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141303,'chemistry','Fly ash and slag from the steel industry are utilised by the:','Cement industry','New innovations have led to different uses of waste material.  Nowadays, fly ash and slag from the steel industry are utilised by the cement industry.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 417; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Industrial Waste',NULL,'1','mcq','Fertilizer industry','Cement industry','Textile industries','Cotton mills','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141304,'chemistry','Incineration is used to destroy:','large quantities of toxic wastes','Large quantities of toxic wastes are usually destroyed by controlled incineration, whereas small quantities are burnt along with factory garbage in open bins. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 418; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Industrial Waste',NULL,'1','mcq','large quantities of toxic wastes','small quantities of wastes','large quantities of biodegradable wastes','small quantities of domestic wastes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141305,'chemistry','Green fuel obtained from plastic has a:','High octane rating','Fuel obtained from plastic waste has high octane rating. It contains no lead and is known as “green fuel”.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 418; Do you know about waste recycling?)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Industrial Waste',NULL,'2','mcq','High content of lead','Low octane rating','High octane rating','Large no. of octane ring','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141306,'chemistry','Which gas is produced when garbage is cultured with bacteria?','Methane','Technology has now been developed to produce electricity from the garbage. A pilot plant has been set up, where after removing ferrous metals, plastic, glass, paper etc. from garbage, it is mixed with water. It is then cultured with bacterial species for producing methane, commonly known as biogas. The remaining product is used as manure and biogas is used to produce electricity.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 418; Do you know about waste recycling?)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Industrial Waste',NULL,'1','mcq','Methane','Carbon dioxide','Nitrogen oxides','Oxygen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141307,'biology','Which of the metabolites is common to respiration mediated breakdown of fats, carbohydrates and proteins? [2014]','Acetyl CoA','Acetyl CoA is common to respiration mediated breakdown of fats, carbohydrates and proteins. Fats are broken down to fatty acid and glycerol and again fatty acid degraded to acetyl CoA . Protein first degraded by proteases to individual amino acids which deaminated to pyruvic acid and further decarboxylised to acetyl CoA','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fructose 1, 6 - bisphosphate','Pyruvic acid','Acetyl CoA','Glucose - 6 - phosphate','NEW','11-235','11-162',NULL),
(141308,'biology','In alcoholic fermentation, NAD+ is produced during the [2017]','Reduction of acetaldehyde to ethanol.','Alcoholic fermentation is a process in which molecules such as glucose etc. are converted into cellular energy and thereby produce ethanol and  CO₂  as metabolic waste products. During alcoholic fermentation, NAD+ is produced when acetaldehyde is reduced to ethanol(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;230,paragraph no.3)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Reduction of acetaldehyde to ethanol.','Oxidation of glucose','Oxidation of pyruvate to acetyl coA','Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP','NEW','11-230','11-157',NULL),
(141309,'biology','Assertion : Stomata are absent in submerged hydrophytes. \n  \n Reason : Respiration occurs by means of air chambers in submerged plants. [1997]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Stomata are absent since gaseous exchange takes place through diffusion in submerged plants','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141310,'biology','Assertion : Glycolysis is the first step of respiration in which glucose completely breaks into CO₂  and H₂O. \n\n Reason : In this process, there is net gain of twenty four molecules of ATP. [2009]','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Glycolysis is the process of breakdown of glucose or similar hexose sugar into two molecules of pyruvic acid through a series of enzyme mediated reactions, releasing energy (ATP) and reducing power (NADH2). It is the first step of respiration, which occurs inside the cytoplasm and is independent of O₂ . In glycolysis, two molecules of ATP are consumed during double phosphorylation of glucose to form fructose 1, 6 diphosphate. Four molecules of ATP are produced in the conversion of 1, 3-diphosphoglycerate to 3-phosphoglycerate and phosphoenolpyruvate to pyruvate whereas, two molecules of NADH₂ are formed during oxidation of glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate to 1,3-diphosphoglycerate. Since, each NADH is equivalent to 3 ATP, so net gain in glycolysis is 8 ATP','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-228','11-155',NULL),
(40418,'biology','Which one among the following is a mollusc?','Sea hare (aplysia)','Sea horse belongs to pisces and Sea cow belongs to mammalia. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 54; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','1','mcq','Sea mouse','Sea hare (aplysia)','Sea cow','Sea horse','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(141311,'biology','Assertion : The inner membrane of mitochondria contains systems involving electron transport.\n \n Reason : The mitochondrial matrix contains enzymes of Krebs cycle. [2013]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','All the oxidative enzymes of TCA are located in the matrix except succinic dehydrogenase. This enzyme catalysed the conversion of succinic acid into fumaric acid. The enzyme is an integral protein complex that is tightly bound to the inner mitochondrial membrane. In fact this enzyme is the preferred marker enzyme for inner membranes when doing mitochondrial fractionations(Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;231,paragraph no.4)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-231','11-158',NULL),
(141312,'biology','Assertion : Glycolysis occurs in cytoplasm. \n\n Reason : Enzymes for glycolysis are found in cytoplasm. It is common in aerobic/anaerobic respiration. [2002, 2014]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Glycolysis occurs in cytoplasm as all necessary enzymes are found in it. This process is common in aerobic/anaerobic respiration. In this process, one glucose molecule is converted into 2 molecules of pyruvic acid (Reference   :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no;228,paragraph no.4)','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-226','11-153',NULL),
(141401,'chemistry','How many programmes are being implemented under the broad umbrella of the Swachh Bharat Abhiyan?','2','Two programmes are being implemented under the broad umbrella of the Swachh Bharat Abhiyan. These are Swachh Bharat Mission–Urban (SBM–U) and Swachh Bharat Mission Gramin (SBM–G). \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 418; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Waste Management',NULL,'1','mcq','4','2','3','10','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141402,'chemistry','Arrange the following in the order of collection of domestic waste.','small bins>community bins>disposable site>sorting and separation','Domestic wastes are collected in small bins, which are then transferred to community bins by private or municipal workers. From these community bins, these are collected and\ncarried to the disposable site. At the site, garbage is sorted out and separated into biodegradable and non-biodegradable materials.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 418; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Waste Management',NULL,'3','order','1,small bins','2,community bins','3,disposable site','4,sorting and separation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141403,'chemistry','What kind of waste can be disposed of in landfills and transformed into compost?','Biodegradable','Non-biodegradable materials such as plastic, glass, metal scraps etc. are sent for recycling.  \nBiodegradable wastes are deposited in landfills and are converted into compost.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 418; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Waste Management',NULL,'1','mcq','Non-biodegradable','Biodegradable','Hazardous','Industrial','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141404,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: The poor management causes health problems leading to epidemics due to contamination of groundwater.\n\nReason: It is specially hazardous for those who are in direct contact with the waste such as rag pickers and workers involved in waste disposal.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The poor management causes health problems leading to epidemics due to contamination of groundwater. It is specially hazardous for those who are in direct contact with the waste such as rag pickers and workers involved in waste disposal.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 419; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Waste Management',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141405,'chemistry','Consider the following statements.\n\nStatement I: The SBM–U primarily aims at making Urban India free from open defecation and achieving 100% scientific management of solid waste in the country.\n\nStatement II: The SBM–G targets to bring about an improvement in the general quality of life in urban areas.\n\nChoose the best answer based on the statements above.','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','The SBM–U primarily aims at making Urban India free from open defecation and achieving 100% scientific management of solid waste in the country. The SBM–G targets to bring about an improvement in the general quality of life in rural areas by promoting cleanliness and hygiene, and eliminating open defecation.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 418; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Waste Management',NULL,'2','mcq','Statement I is correct, and Statement II is incorrect.','Statement I is incorrect, and Statement II is correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141406,'chemistry','The given symbol represents:\ndiagram(n1405)','Swachh Bharat Abhiyan','The given symbol represents the ‘Swachh Bharat Abhiyan’ or ‘Clean India Mission’ launched by the Government of India.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 418; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Waste Management',NULL,'3','mcq','Swachh Bharat Abhiyan','Ayushman Bharat','Skill India','Digital India','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/141406q.png'),
(141407,'biology','Assertion: During the process of Glycolysis, ATP is utilized at two steps. \n \n Reason: First in the conversion of glucose into glucose 6-phosphate and second in the conversion of fructose 6-phosphate to glucose 6-phosphate.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','ATP is utilised at two steps: first in the conversion of glucose into glucose 6-phosphate and second in the conversion of fructose 6-phosphate to fructose 1, 6-bisphosphate. (Reference  : NCERT, page 229, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(141408,'biology','Assertion: The conversion of BPGA to 3-phosphoglyceric acid (PGA), is an energy yielding process. \n \n Reason: PGAL is oxidised and with inorganic phosphate to get converted into BPGA before this step.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','PGAL is oxidised and with inorganic phosphate to get converted into BPGA. The conversion of BPGA to 3-phosphoglyceric acid (PGA), is also an energy yielding process; as this energy is trapped by the formation of ATP in this step. (Reference  : NCERT, page 229, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(141409,'biology','Assertion: Two ATP molecules are directly synthesised in the glycolysis pathway from one glucose molecule. \n \n Reason: One ATP is formed during conversion of BPGA to3-phosphoglyceric acid (PGA), and another ATP is synthesized during the conversion of PEP to pyruvic acid.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The conversion of BPGA to 3-phosphoglyceric acid (PGA), is also an energy yielding process; this energy is trapped by the formation of ATP. Another ATP is synthesized during the conversion of PEP to pyruvic acid. (Reference  : NCERT, page 229, paragraph 3, Figure 14.1).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-229','11-156',NULL),
(141410,'biology','Assertion: For the complete oxidation of glucose to CO₂ and H₂O organisms adopt Krebs’ cycle. \n \n Reason: Aerobic respiration requires O₂ supply for complete oxidation.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','For the complete oxidation of glucose to CO₂ and H₂O, however, organisms adopt Krebs’ cycle which is also called as aerobic respiration. This requires O₂ supply. (Reference  : NCERT, page 230, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-230','11-157',NULL),
(141411,'biology','Assertion: Pyruvic acid is reduced to lactic acid by lactate dehydrogenase in anaerobic respiration. \n \n Reason: The enzyme lactate dehydrogenase competes with O₂ molecules to give a different product','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','In animal cells also, like muscles during exercise, when O₂ is inadequate for cellular respiration pyruvic acid is reduced to lactic acid by lactate dehydrogenase. Lactate dehydrogenase does not compete with O₂ molecules to give a different product as there is inadequacy of oxygen.  (Reference  : NCERT, page 230, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-230','11-157',NULL),
(141412,'biology','Assertion: The final product of glycolysis, pyruvate is transported from the cytoplasm into the mitochondria. \n \n Reason: Pyruvate is the key product of glycolysis.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','For aerobic respiration to take place within the mitochondria, the final product of glycolysis, pyruvate is transported from the cytoplasm into the mitochondria. (Reference  : NCERT, page 231, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-231','11-158',NULL),
(141413,'biology','Assertion: Pyruvic dehydrogenase plays an important role in aerobic respiration. \n \n Reason: Pyruvic dehydrogenase helps carry pyruvate to the mitochondrial matrix.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Pyruvate, which is formed by the glycolytic catabolism of carbohydrates in the cytosol, after it enters mitochondrial matrix undergoes oxidative decarboxylation by a complex set of reactions catalysed by pyruvic dehydrogenase. The (Reference  s: NCERT, page 231, paragraph 6).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-231','11-158',NULL),
(141414,'biology','Assertion: The TCA cycle is also known as the citric acid cycle. \n \n Reason: The TCA cycle starts with the condensation of citric acid to yield an acetyl group with oxaloacetic acid (OAA) and water.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','The TCA cycle starts with the condensation of acetyl group with oxaloacetic acid (OAA) and water to yield citric acid (Reference  s: NCERT, page 231, paragraph 9, figure 14.3 on Page 232).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-231','11-158',NULL),
(141415,'biology','Assertion: The steps in Electron Transport System involve the respiratory process to release and utilize the energy stored in NADH⁺ and FAD+. \n \n Reason: They are reduced through the electron transport system and the electrons are passed on to CO₂ resulting in the formation of H₂O.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','The following steps (of the electron transport system) in the respiratory process are to release and utilize the energy stored in NADH⁺H⁺ and FADH₂. This is accomplished when they are oxidised through the electron transport system and the electrons are passed on to O₂ resulting in the formation of H₂O. (Reference  : NCERT, page 232, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-232','11-159',NULL),
(141416,'biology','Assertion: Cytochrome c is a small protein attached to the outer surface of the inner membrane. \n \n Reason: It acts as an antigen recognition agent.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Cytochrome c is a small protein attached to the outer surface of the inner membrane and acts as a mobile carrier for transfer of electrons between complex III and IV. (Reference  s: NCERT, page 233, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(141417,'biology','Assertion: Electrons in the Electron Transport Chain are coupled to ATP synthase (complex V) for Energy production. \n\n Reason: This is for the dephosphorylation of ATP to ADP.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','When the electrons pass from one carrier to another via complex I to IV in the electron transport chain, they are coupled to ATP synthase (complex V) for the production of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate. (Reference  : NCERT, page 233, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(141418,'biology','Assertion: The assumptions based on the Respiratory Balance sheet are not really valid in a living system. \n \n Reason: None of the intermediates in any pathway are utilised to synthesise other compounds.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','These assumptions are not really valid in a living system; all pathways work simultaneously and do not take place one after another; substrates enter the pathways and are withdrawn from it as and when necessary; ATP is utilised as and when needed; enzymatic rates are controlled by multiple means. Reference  : NCERT, page 234, paragraph 7).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-234','11-161',NULL),
(141419,'biology','Assertion: The passage of protons through the channel is coupled to the catalytic site of the F1 component. \n \n Reason: The F1 headpiece is a peripheral membrane protein complex and contains the site for synthesis of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The F1 headpiece is a peripheral membrane protein complex and contains the site for synthesis of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate. The passage of protons through thechannel is coupled to the catalytic site of the F1 component for the production of ATP. (Reference  : NCERT, page 234, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-234','11-161',NULL),
(141420,'biology','Assertion: Fermentation accounts for complete breakdown of glucose. \n \n Reason: Glucose is completely broken down into water and  CO₂  during fermentation.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Fermentation accounts for only a partial breakdown of glucose whereas in aerobic respiration it is completely degraded into water and  CO₂ . (Reference  : NCERT, page 235, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-235','11-162',NULL),
(141421,'biology','Assertion: All carbohydrates are usually first converted into glucose before they are used for respiration. \n \n Reason: It is broken down into water and  CO₂ .','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Glucose is the favoured substrate for respiration. All carbohydrates are usually first converted into glucose before they are used for respiration. Other substrates can also be respired, as has been mentioned earlier, but then they do not enter the respiratory pathway at the first step. (Reference  : NCERT, page 235, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-235','11-162',NULL),
(141422,'biology','Assertion: When carbohydrates are used as substrate and are completely oxidised, the RQ will be 1. \n \n Reason: The number of molecules determines RQ and when one molecule of CO₂ and O₂ are evolved and consumed, RQ is 1.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','The ratio of the volume of CO₂ evolved to the volume of O₂ consumed in respiration is called the respiratory quotient (RQ) or respiratory ratio. When carbohydrates are used as substrate and are completely oxidised, the RQ will be 1, because equal amounts of CO₂ and O₂ are evolved and consumed. (Reference  : NCERT, page 236, paragraph 1  and  3).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-236','11-163',NULL),
(141423,'biology','Assertion: The respiratory process has only catabolic activities. \n \n Reason: Respiratory pathway comes into the picture only during the breakdown of fatty acids.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','The respiratory pathway comes into the picture both during breakdown and synthesis of fatty acids. Because the respiratory pathway is involved in both anabolism and catabolism, it would hence be better to consider the respiratory pathway as an amphibolic pathway rather than as a catabolic one. (Reference  : NCERT, page 235, paragraph 5).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','11-235','11-162',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(141501,'chemistry','Self-sufficiency in food has been achieved in India since late ____ century.','20th','Self-sufficiency in food has been achieved in India since late 20th century by using fertilizers and pesticides and exploring improved methods of farming, good quality seeds, irrigation etc.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 419; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','20th','19th','21th','18th','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141502,'chemistry','Applying existing knowledge and principles of chemistry and other sciences to lessen the negative influence on the environment refers to:','Green chemistry','Green chemistry is a way of thinking and is about utilising the existing knowledge and principles of chemistry and other sciences to reduce the adverse impact on environment. Green chemistry is a production process that would bring about minimum pollution or deterioration to the environment. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 419; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Blue chemistry','Green chemistry','Grey chemistry','Red chemistry','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141503,'chemistry','Which work was awarded the 2005 Nobel Prize in Chemistry to Yves Chauvin, Robert H. Grubbs, and Richard R. Schrock?','Metathesis method in organic synthesis','Yves Chauvin, Institut Français du Pétrole, Rueil-Malmaison  France,  Robert H. Grubbs California Institute of Technology (Caltech), Pasadena, CA, USA and Richard R. Schrock Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), Cambridge, MA, USA won the 2005 Nobel Prize in chemistry for work that reduces hazardous waste in creating new chemicals. The trio won the award for their development of the metathesis method in organic synthesis –a way to rearrange groups of atoms within molecules that the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences likened to a dance in which couples change partners. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 419; Nobel goes to Green Chemists)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Metathesis method in organic synthesis','Metathesis method in inorganic synthesis','Quantum statistics','Ledenberg effect explanation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141504,'chemistry','What is the metastasis method in organic synthesis?','A way to rearrange groups of atoms within molecules.','Metathesis method in organic synthesis is a way to rearrange groups of atoms within molecules. The Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences likened this method to a dance in which couples change partners. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 419; Nobel goes to Green Chemists)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'2','mcq','A way to rearrange groups of atoms within molecules.','Using better catalyst for removing waste products formed in a reaction.','Using organic solvents instead of water to carry out reaction.','A way to change the molecular formula of a compound.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141505,'chemistry','Organic solvents such as benzene and toluene are highly _____.','Toxic','Organic solvents such as benzene, toluene, carbon tetrachloride etc., are highly toxic. One should be careful while using them.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Toxic','Non-toxic','Non carbon compound','Consumable','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141506,'chemistry','The extent of any reaction is independent of:','Concentration of the reactants','Extent of any reaction depends upon physical parameters like temperature, pressure and use of catalyst.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Temperature','Pressure','Catalyst','Concentration of the reactants','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141507,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n\nAssertion: It is worthwhile to carry out synthetic reactions in aqueous medium.\n\nReason: Water has a high specific heat, low volatility, is inexpensive, nonflammable, and has no carcinogenic consequences.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','During a synthesis, care must be taken to choose starting materials that can be converted into end products with yield approximately upto 100 per cent.  This can be achieved by arriving at optimum conditions of synthesis. It may be worthwhile to carry out synthetic reactions in aqueous medium since water has high specific heat and low volatility.  Water is cost effective, non inflammable and devoid of any carcinogenic effects.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141508,'chemistry','To minimise the harm to groundwater, which chemical is used to replace the halogenated solvent in dry cleaning?','Liquid carbon dioxide','Tetrachloroethene (Cl₂C=CCl₂) was earlier used as solvent for dry cleaning. The compound contaminates the groundwater and is also a suspected carcinogen. The process using this compound is now being replaced by a process, where liquefied carbon dioxide, with a suitable detergent is used. Replacement of halogenated solvent by liquid CO₂ will result in less harm to groundwater.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Liquid carbon dioxide','Liquid hydrogen peroxide','Liquid ammonia','Liquid freons','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141509,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\nAssertion: Tetrachloroethene was replaced by carbon dioxide as solvent for dry cleaning.\nReason: Carbon dioxide contaminates groundwater and is a suspected carcinogen.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Tetrachloroethene (Cl₂C=CCl₂) was earlier used as solvent for dry cleaning. The compound contaminates the groundwater and is also a suspected carcinogen. The process using this compound is now being replaced by a process, where liquefied carbon dioxide, with a suitable detergent is used. Replacement of halogenated solvent by liquid CO₂ will result in less harm to groundwater.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141510,'chemistry','Which chemical is used in bleaching to achieve better results while using less water?','Hydrogen peroxide','Hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂) is used for the purpose of bleaching clothes in the process of laundry, which gives better results and makes use of lesser amount of water.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 5)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrogen peroxide','Sodium peroxide','Carbon dioxide','Tetrachloroethene','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141511,'chemistry','Which chemical has been replaced by hydrogen peroxide in the bleaching of paper?','Chlorine gas','Chlorine gas was used earlier for bleaching paper. These days, hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂) with suitable catalyst, which promotes the bleaching action of hydrogen peroxide, is used.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 6)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Iodine gas','Chlorine gas','Carbon dioxide','Methane','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141512,'chemistry','Which catalyst is used in the single step oxidation of ethene to ethanal in aqueous medium?','Pd(II)','Ethanal (CH₃CHO) is now commercially prepared by one step oxidation of ethene in the presence of ionic catalyst in aqueous medium with a yield of 90%.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 7) diagram(ne1406)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'2','mcq','Pd(II)','Au(III)','Carbon coated Pt','Fe(II)','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141513,'chemistry','Ethanal (CH₃CHO) is now commercially prepared by one step oxidation of ethene in the presence of ionic catalyst in aqueous medium with a yield of _____.','90%','Ethanal (CH₃CHO) is now commercially prepared by one step oxidation of ethene in the presence of ionic catalyst in aqueous medium with a yield of 90%.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','45%','10%','90%','0.20%','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141514,'chemistry','Mark the correct statement while remembering the "Green Solution" for cleaning turbid water.','Powder of kernel of tamarind seeds has been found to be an effective to make waste water clean.','Powder of kernel of tamarind seeds has been found to be an effective material to make municipal and industrial waste water clean. It is non-toxic, biodegradable and cost effective material. This powder is usually discarded as agricultural waste. The present practice is to use alum to treat such water. It has been found that alum increases toxic ions in treated water and can cause diseases.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 420; Paragraph 8)','Inorganic chemistry >> Environmental Chemistry >> Green Chemistry',NULL,'2','mcq','Powder of kernel of tamarind seeds has been found to be an effective to make waste water clean.','Tamarind powder is toxic and cost-effective.','Alum decreases toxic ions in treated water and is considered beneficial.','Powder of kernel of tamarind seeds is used as a powerful fertilizer.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(141515,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation plant and choose the correct option.','Oxidation of one molecule of NADH gives rise to 3 molecules of ATP.','Oxidation of one molecule of NADH gives rise to 3 molecules of ATP, while that of one molecule of FADH₂ produces 2 molecules of ATP. (Reference  : NCERT, page 233, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Oxidation of one molecule of NADH gives rise to 3 molecules of ATP.','Oxidation of one molecule of NADH gives rise to one molecule of ATP.','One molecule of FADH₂ produces 3 molecules of ATP.','One molecule of FADH₂ produces one molecule of ATP.','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(141516,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Electron Transport System (ETS) and Oxidative Phosphorylation choose the incorrect option.','In respiration it is the energy of the light energy that is utilised for the production of proton gradient required for phosphorylation.','The presence of O₂ is vital, since it drives the whole process by removing hydrogen from the system. O₂ acts as the final hydrogen acceptor. Unlike photophosphorylation where it is the light energy that is utilised for the production of proton gradient required for phosphorylation, in respiration it is the energy of oxidation-reduction utilised for the same process. It is for this reason that the process is called oxidative phosphorylation (Reference  : NCERT, page 233, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','O₂ drives the whole process by removing hydrogen from the system.','O₂ acts as the final hydrogen acceptor.','Both A  and  B are incorrect.','In respiration it is the energy of the light energy that is utilised for the production of proton gradient required for phosphorylation.','NEW','11-233','11-160',NULL),
(141517,'biology','Consider the following assumptions about the respiratory balance sheet. \n\ni. The NADH synthesised in glycolysis is transferred into the mitochondria and undergoes oxidative phosphorylation.\n\nii. Some of the intermediates in the pathway are utilised to synthesise secondary compounds. \n\niii. Only glucose is being respired in the respiratory process. \n\nusing the code below, choose the incorrect statement/s.','ii only','The NADH synthesised in glycolysis is transferred into the mitochondria and undergoes oxidative phosphorylation. None of the intermediates in the pathway are utilised to synthesise any other compound. Only glucose is being respired – no other alternative substrates are entering in the pathway at any of the intermediary stages. (Reference  : NCERT, page 234, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i  and  ii only','ii only','iii only','NEW','11-234','11-161',NULL),
(141518,'biology','Consider the following statements about respiration and choose the correct option.','Both A  and  B are correct.','The F1 headpiece is a peripheral membrane protein complex and contains the site for synthesis of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate. F0 is an integral membrane protein complex that forms the channel through which protons cross the inner membrane. For each ATP produced, 4H⁺ passes through F0 from the intermembrane space to the matrix down the electrochemical proton gradient. (Reference  : NCERT, page 234, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','For each ATP produced, 4H⁺ passes through F0 from the intermembrane space to the matrix down the electrochemical proton gradient.','F0 is an integral membrane protein complex that forms the channel through which protons cross the inner membrane.','Both A  and  B are correct.','F1 is an integral membrane protein complex that forms the channel through which protons cross the inner membrane.','NEW','11-234','11-161',NULL),
(141519,'biology','Consider the following statements about Amphibolic Pathway.\n\ni. If fatty acids were to be respired they would first be degraded to acetyl CoA and enter the pathway.\n\nii. The proteins would be degraded by proteases and individual amino acids depending on their structure would enter the pathway at some stage.\n\niii. Glycerol would enter the pathway after being converted to CoA.\n\nusing the code below, choose the correct statement/s.','i  and  ii only','If fatty acids were to be respired they would first be degraded to acetyl CoA and enter the pathway. Glycerol would enter the pathway after being converted to PGAL. The proteins would be degraded by proteases and the individual amino acids (after deamination) depending on their structure would enter the pathway at some stage within the Krebs’ cycle or even as pyruvate or acetyl CoA. (Reference  : NCERT, page 235, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i  and  ii only','ii only','iii only','NEW','11-235','11-162',NULL),
(141520,'biology','Consider the following statements about the comparison between fermentation and aerobic respiration.\n\ni. NADH is oxidised to NAD+ rather slowly in fermentation.\n\nii.  In fermentation there is a net gain of only four molecules of ATP for each molecule of glucose degraded to pyruvic acid whereas six molecules of ATP are generated under aerobic conditions.\n\niii. Fermentation accounts for only a partial breakdown of glucose.\n\nusing the code below select the incorrect statement/s.','ii only','In fermentation there is a net gain of only two molecules of ATP for each molecule of glucose degraded to pyruvic acid whereas many more molecules of ATP are generated under aerobic conditions. NADH is oxidised to NAD+ rather slowly in fermentation. Fermentation accounts for only a partial breakdown of glucose. (Reference  s: NCERT, page 235, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i  and  ii only','ii only','iii only','NEW','11-235','11-162',NULL),
(141521,'biology','Consider the following statements about the RQ and choose the incorrect option.','The ratio of the volume of O₂ consumed to the volume of CO₂ evolved in respiration is called the respiratory quotient (RQ) or respiratory ratio.','The ratio of the volume of CO₂ evolved to the volume of O₂ consumed in respiration is called the respiratory quotient (RQ) or respiratory ratio. The respiratory quotient depends upon the type of respiratory substrate used during respiration. When carbohydrates are used as substrate and are completely oxidised, the RQ will be 1, because equal amounts of CO₂ and O₂ are evolved and consumed, (Reference  : NCERT, page 236, paragraph 1,2  and  3).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The type of respiratory substrate determines RQ.','The ratio of the volume of CO₂ evolved to the volume of O₂ consumed in respiration is called the respiratory quotient (RQ) or respiratory ratio.','The ratio of the volume of O₂ consumed to the volume of CO₂ evolved in respiration is called the respiratory quotient (RQ) or respiratory ratio.','When carbohydrates are used as substrate and are completely oxidised, the RQ will be 1.','NEW','11-236','11-163',NULL),
(141522,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Interrelationship among metabolic pathways and select the correct option.','Fatty acids would be broken down to acetyl CoA before entering the respiratory pathway.','All carbohydrates are usually first converted into glucose before they are used for respiration. During breakdown and synthesis of protein, respiratory intermediates form the link. Fatty acids would be broken down to acetyl CoA before entering the respiratory pathway when it is used as a substrate. (Reference  : NCERT, page 235, paragraph 4  and  5, figure 14.6 on page 236).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Carbohydrates are converted into simple sugars such as glycogen before they enter the process of respiration.','Fatty acids would be broken down to acetyl CoA before entering the respiratory pathway.','Amino Acids would be broken down to acetyl CoA before entering the respiratory pathway.','Proteins do not contribute in any way during the process of cellular respiration.','NEW','11-235','11-162',NULL),
(141523,'biology','Consider the following statements about the RQ of tripalmitin, if used as a substrate and select the incorrect option.','A  and  B both','When fats are used in respiration, the RQ is less than 1. When proteins are respiratory substrates the ratio would be about 0.9. (Reference  : NCERT, page 237, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The RQ is around 0.7 because when fats are used in respiration, the RQ is around 0.9.','The RQ is around 0.7 because when tripalmitin protien is respiratory substrates the ratio would be less than 0.9.','The RQ is around 0.7 because when fats are used in respiration, the RQ is less than 1.','A  and  B both','NEW','11-237','11-164',NULL),
(141524,'biology','Given below are a few statements about respiration in plants. Choose the option that is factually correct.','Both A  and  B are correct.','The breaking of C-C bonds of complex organic molecules by oxidation cells leading to the release of a lot of energy is called cellular respiration. Under anaerobic conditions either lactic acid fermentation or alcohol fermentation occurs. The initial stage of cellular respiration takes place in the cytoplasm. (Reference  : NCERT, page 237, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Cellular respiration involves the breaking of C-C bonds of complex organic molecules by oxidation cells.','Under anaerobic conditions either lactic acid fermentation or alcohol fermentation occurs.','Both A  and  B are correct.','The initial stage of cellular respiration takes place in the mitochondria.','NEW','11-237','11-164',NULL),
(141525,'biology','Consider the following statements about the respiration in plants: \n\ni. The respiratory pathway is an amphibolic pathway as it involves both anabolism and catabolism.\n\nii. The respiratory quotient depends upon the environment.\n\niii. The electrons, as they move through the system, release enough energy by using ATP.\n\nusing the code below, choose the correct statement/s.','i only','The electrons, as they move through the system, release enough energy that are trapped to synthesise ATP. The respiratory pathway is an amphibolic pathway as it involves both anabolism and catabolism. The respiratory quotient depends upon the type of respiratory substance used during respiration. (Reference  : NCERT, page 238, paragraph 1  and  2).','Plant Physiology >> Respiration in plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','i only','i  and  ii only','iii only','ii  and  iii only','NEW','11-238','11-165',NULL),
(350101,'biology','Which of the following statement(s) is\\are correct? \n\nA. Misra formulated the first postgraduate course in ecology in India.\n\nB.Over 60 scholars obtained Ph. D degree under his supervision. \n\nC. He was honoured with the Fellowships of the Indian National Science Academy and World Academy of Arts and Science, and the prestigious Sanjay Gandhi Award in Environment and Ecology. \n\nD. Due to his efforts, the Government of India established the National Committee for Environmental Planning and Coordination (1972) which, in later years, paved the way for the establishment of the Ministry of Environment and Forests (1984).','A,C,D','Misra formulated the first postgraduate course in ecology in India. Over 50 scholars obtained Ph. D degree under his supervision in ecology.He was honoured with the Fellowships of the Indian National Science Academy and World Academy of Arts and Science, and the prestigious Sanjay Gandhi Award in Environment and Ecology.Due to his efforts, the Government of India established the National Committee for Environmental Planning and Coordination (1972) which, in later years, paved the way for the establishment of the Ministry of Environment and Forests (1984).','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','A,B','B,C','C only','A,C,D','NEW','12-218','12-189',NULL),
(40419,'biology','Match the following options correctly.','Chaetopleura-Chiton,Dentalium-Tusk shell,Pinctada-Pearl oyster,Pila-Apple snail','Examples: Pila (Apple snail), Pinctada (Pearl oyster), Sepia (Cuttlefish), Loligo (Squid), Octopus (Devil fish), Aplysia (Sea hare), Dentalium (Tusk shell) and Chaetopleura (Chiton(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 54; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','match','Chaetopleura,Chiton','Dentalium,Tusk shell','Pinctada,Pearl oyster','Pila,Apple snail','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(350102,'biology','Correct order of various levels of biological organisation ?','Macromolecules, cells, tissues, organs, individual organisms, population, communities, ecosystems and biomes','Various levels of biological organisation–macromolecules, cells, tissues, organs, individual organisms, population, communities, ecosystems and biomes. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:219, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Macromolecules, tissues,cells, organs, individual organisms, population, communities, ecosystems and biomes.','Cells,macromolecules, tissues, organs, individual organisms, population, communities, ecosystems and biomes.','Macromolecules, cells, tissues, organs, individual organisms, population, communities, ecosystems and biomes','Macromolecules, cells, tissues, organs, individual organisms,communities, population,ecosystems and biomes.','NEW','12-219','12-190',NULL),
(350103,'biology','Ecology is a subject which studies _________\n \ni)Interactions with its physical (abiotic) environment.\n \nii)Interactions between the organism\n \niii)Interactions among organisms\n \niv)Death rate of organisms','i, ii, and iii','Ecology is the study of the interactions of living things with their environment. Ecologists ask questions across four levels of biological organization—organismal, population, community, and ecosystem. At the organismal level, ecologists study individual organisms and how they interact with their environments. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:220, paragraph no:1,2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and ii','ii and iii','ii and iv','NEW','12-220','12-191',NULL),
(350104,'biology','Ecology is basically concerned with four levels of biology organizations. These levels are','Organisms, populations, communities and biomes','Ecology is the study of the interactions of living things with their environment. Ecologists ask questions across four levels of biological organization—organismal, population, community, and ecosystem. At the organismal level, ecologists study individual organisms and how they interact with their environments. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:220, paragraph no:1,2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Species, populations, biomes and organisms','Organisms, populations, communities and biomes','Organisms and communities, species and population','Organisms, populations, biomes and species','NEW','12-220','12-191','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/350104q.png'),
(350201,'biology','Ecology at the organismic level is essentially __________ ecology','Physiological ecology','Ecology at the organismic level is essentially physiological ecology(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:220, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Organism and its Environment',NULL,'2','mcq','Morphological ecology','Physiological ecology','Behavioural ecology','Anatomical ecology','NEW','12-220',NULL,NULL),
(350202,'biology','Distinct seasonal variations on Earth occur due to?\ni) Rotation around sun\nii) Rotation around its own axis\niii) Tilt of its axis','i and iii','The rotation of our planet around the Sun and the tilt of its axis cause annual variations in the intensity and duration of temperature, resulting in distinct seasons.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:220, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Organism and its Environment',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iii','NEW','12-220',NULL,NULL),
(350203,'biology','Different biomes are formed due to annual variation over the earth are?\ni) Rain forest\nii) Tundra\niii) Desert\niv) Lakes','i, ii, and iii','These variations together with annual variation in precipitation (remember precipitation includes both rain and snow) account for the formation of major biomes such as desert, rainforest and tundra.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:220, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Organism and its Environment',NULL,'1','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and iv','ii and iv','i and iii','NEW','12-220',NULL,NULL),
(350204,'biology','Regional and local variations within each biome lead to the formation of a wide variety of ________.','habitat','Regional and local variations within each biome lead to the formation of a wide variety of habitats.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:220, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Organism and its Environment',NULL,'2','mcq','Niche','habitat','population','none of the above','NEW','12-220',NULL,NULL),
(350205,'biology','Assertion:On planet Earth, life exists just in a few favourable habitats not in extreme and harsh habitats like scorching Rajasthan desert, rain-soaked Meghalaya forests, deep ocean trenches, torrential streams, permafrost (snow laden) polar regions, high mountain tops, thermal springs, and stinking compost pits. \n\n\nReason:Our intestine is not a unique habitat for hundreds of species of microbes.','A and R, both are false','On planet Earth, life exists not just in a few favourable habitats but even in extreme and harsh habitats – scorching Rajasthan desert, rain-soaked Meghalaya forests, deep ocean trenches, torrential streams, permafrost (snow laden) polar regions, high mountain tops, thermal springs, and stinking compost pits, to name a few. Even our intestine is a unique habitat for hundreds of species of microbes.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Organism and its Environment',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-220',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/350205q.png'),
(350206,'biology','What are the key elements that lead to so much variation in the physical and chemical conditions of different habitats?\n \n i) Physico-electric components\n \n ii) Temperature, water, light and soil\n \n iii) Biotic factors\n \n iv) Non-biotic factors','ii and iii','The most important ones are temperature, water, light and soil. We must remember that the physico-chemical (abiotic) components alone do not characterise the habitat of an organism completely; the habitat includes biotic components also – pathogens, parasites, predators and competitors – of the organism with which they interact constantly.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:221, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Organism and its Environment',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and ii','ii and iii','i, ii, iii, and iv','NEW','12-221',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/350206q.png'),
(350207,'biology','The habitat includes biotic components such as ____________ of the organism with which they interact constantly. \n \n i) Pathogens\n \n ii) Compensator\n \n iii) Parasites\n \n iv) Predators','i, iii, and iv','The most important ones are temperature, water, light and soil. We must remember that the physico-chemical (abiotic) components alone do not characterise the habitat of an organism completely; the habitat includes biotic components also – pathogens, parasites, predators and competitors – of the organism with which they interact constantly.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:221, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Organism and its Environment',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i, iii, and iv','iii and iv','i and iii','NEW','12-221',NULL,NULL),
(350306,'biology','I. Mango trees cannot grow in temperate countries like Canada and Germany \n\nII. Snow leopards are not found in Kerala. \n\nIII. Tuna fish are rarely caught beyond tropical latitude in the ocean. \n\nIV. Average temperature exceeds 100°C in thermal springs and hydrothermal vents. \n\nV. In polar areas and high altitudes temperature goes to 70°C. \n\nVI. Temperature goes to > 50°C in tropical desert in summer. \n\nWhich of the above statements are false?','V only','Temperature ranges from sub zero levels in polar areas and high altitudes to >50 C in tropical deserts in summer. There are, however, unique habitats such as thermal springs and deep-sea hydrothermal vents where average temperatures exceed 100 C. It is general knowledge that mango trees do not and cannot grow in temperate countries like Canada and Germany, snow leopards are not found in Kerala forests and tuna fish are rarely caught beyond tropical latitudes in the ocean. You can appreciate the significance of temperature to living organisms when you realise that it affects the kinetics of enzymes and through it the metabolic activity and other physiological functions of the organism.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:221,222, paragraph no:3,1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'2','mcq','I only','II and III only','V only','V and VI only','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350307,'biology','The significance of temperature to living organisms is appreciable because it affects ____\n \n i) Physiological functions of the organism.\n \n ii) Metabolism activity.\n \n iii) Kinetics of the enzymes.\n \n iv) Physical features of the organism.','i, ii, and iii','You can appreciate the significance of temperature to living organisms when you realise that it affects the kinetics of enzymes and through it the metabolic activity and other physiological functions of the organism.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and iv','ii and iii','i and iii','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350308,'biology','A few organisms can tolerate and thrive in a wide range of temperatures they are called','Eurythermal','A few organisms can tolerate and thrive in a wide range of temperatures (they are called eurythermal).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'2','fillup','Eu,ry,th,er,ma,l',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350309,'biology','A vast majority of organisms are restricted to a narrow range of temperatures such organisms are called _______.','Stenothermal','A vast majority of organisms are restricted to a narrow range of temperatures (such organisms are called stenothermal).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'2','fillup','St,eno,thermal',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350310,'biology','The levels of thermal tolerance of different species determine to a large extent their _________ distribution.','Geographical','The levels of thermal tolerance of different species determine to a large extent their geographical distribution.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'2','mcq','Ecological','Physiological','Morphological','Geographical','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350311,'biology','Next to temperature,______ is another the most important factor influencing the life of organisms.','Water','Next to temperature ,Water is another the most important factor influencing the life of organisms.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'2','mcq','Water','Air','Soil','Organisms','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350312,'biology','Life on earth originated in water and is unsustainable without water.The given statement is true or false?','TRUE','Life on earth originated in water and is unsustainable without water.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350313,'biology','What availability is so limited in deserts that only special adaptations make it possible for organisms to live there?','Water','Water availability is so limited in deserts that only special adaptations make it possible for organisms to live there.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'2','mcq','Temperature','Water','Light','Soil','NEW','12-222',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/350313q.png'),
(350314,'biology','Pick the correct statements : \n\nA.The productivity and distribution of plants is independent of water. \n\nB.Organisms living in oceans, lakes and rivers do not face any water-related problems. \n\nC.For aquatic organisms the quality (chemical composition, pH) of water becomes important. \n\nD.Many freshwater animals cannot live for long in sea water and vice versa because of the osmotic problems, they would face. \n\nE. Some organisms are tolerant of a wide range of salinities (stenohaline) but others are restricted to a narrow range (euryhaline).','Both C, D are correct','The productivity and distribution of plants is also heavily dependent on water. You might think that organisms living in oceans, lakes and rivers should not face any water-related problems, but it is not true. For aquatic organisms the quality (chemical composition, pH) of water becomes important. Some organisms are tolerant of a wide range of salinities (euryhaline) but others are restricted to a narrow range (stenohaline). Many freshwater animals cannot live for long in sea water and vice versa because of the osmotic problems, they would face.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A , B are correct','B, C , D , E are correct','A, B, C, D,  E are correct','Both C, D are correct','NEW','12-222',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/350314q.png'),
(350315,'biology','The salt concentration (measured as salinity in parts per thousand), is less than ___ in inland waters, 30-35 in the sea and > ____ in some hypersaline lagoons.','5, 100','The salt concentration (measured as salinity in parts per thousand), is less than 5 in inland waters, 30-35 in the sea and > 100 in some hypersaline lagoons.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'1','mcq','10,100','6,50','5, 100','5,150','NEW','12-222',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/350315q.png'),
(241807,'biology','Assertion: Each cell of the sporogenous tissue is a potential ovary cell.\n\nReason: Each cell has the potential to undergo the process of mitosis.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','As each cell of the sporogenous tissue is capable of giving rise to a microspore tetrad. Each one is a potential pollen or microspore mother cell. (Reference  : NCERT, page 22, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-022',NULL,NULL),
(350316,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- The average temperature on land varies seasonally and there is change throughout the year. \n \n Reason- Temperature decreases progressively from the equator toward the poles and from plains to the mountain tops based on the availability of sunlight and humidity of air.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The average temperature on land varies seasonally, decreases progressively from the equator towards the poles and from plains to the mountain tops. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 221, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-221',NULL,NULL),
(350317,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- In recent years, there has been a growing concern about the gradually increasing average global temperatures. \n \n Reason- The levels of thermal tolerance of different species determine to a large extent their geographical distribution.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The levels of thermal tolerance of different species determine to a large extent their geographical distribution. In recent years, there has been a growing concern about the gradually increasing average global temperatures. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(350318,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Temperature and Location and choose the incorrect option. \n \n 1. Mango trees grow in temperate countries. \n \n 2. Tuna fish are rarely caught beyond tropical latitudes in the ocean. \n \n 3. Snow leopards are not found in Kerala forests. \n \n 4. Thermophiles survive in unique habitats such as thermal springs.','Only 1 is incorrect','Mango trees do not and cannot grow in temperate countries like Canada and Germany, snow leopards are not found in Kerala forests and tuna fish are rarely caught beyond tropical latitudes in the ocean. In unique habitats such as thermal springs, thermophiles are found. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','1,2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 3 is correct','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350319,'biology','Organisms tolerant of a wide range of salinities are called','Euryhaline','Some organisms are tolerant of a wide range of salinities (euryhaline) but others are restricted to a narrow range (stenohaline). (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'2','fillup','Eu,ry,ha,li,ne',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-222',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/350319q.png'),
(350320,'biology','Organisms restricted to a narrow range of salinities are called','Stenohaline','Some organisms are tolerant of a wide range of salinities (euryhaline) but others are restricted to a narrow range (stenohaline). (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'1','fillup','St,en,oh,al,ine',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350321,'biology','Many freshwater animals cannot live for long in sea water because of the_______','Osmotic problems','Many freshwater animals cannot live for long in sea water and vice versa because of the osmotic problems, they would face. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Temperature  and  Water)',NULL,'2','mcq','Temperature problems','Osmotic problems','Feeding problems','Respiratory problems','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350401,'biology','Many species of small plants like __________ growing in forests are adapted to photosynthesize optimally under very low light conditions.','Herb and shrubs','Many species of small plants like herbs and shrubs growing in forests are adapted to photosynthesise optimally under very low light conditions.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'2','mcq','Tree and herbs','Herbs only','Tree and shrubs','Herb and shrubs','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350402,'biology','Many plants are also dependent on sunlight to meet their photoperiodic requirement for flowering. The given statement is true or false?','TRUE','Many plants are also dependent on sunlight to meet their photoperiodic requirement for flowering.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350403,'biology','Many animals use the diurnal and seasonal variations in light intensity and photoperiod as cues timing of\n \n i) Migration\n \n ii) Foraging activities\n \n iii) Reproductive activities\n \n iv) Feeding','i, ii, and iii','For many animals, light is important in that they use the diurnal and seasonal variations in light intensity and duration (photoperiod) as cues for timing their foraging activities, reproductive activities and migratory activities.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iv','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350404,'biology','The availability of light on land is closely linked with that of ________','Temperature','The availability of light on land is closely linked with that of temperature.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'2','mcq','Soil','Temperature','Water','Water and Temperature','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350405,'biology','Deep (>500m) in the oceans, the environment is dark and its inhabitants are not aware of the existence of a celestial source of energy called ?','Sun','Deep (>500m) in the oceans, the environment is dark and its inhabitants are not aware of the existence of a celestial source of energy called Sun.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'2','mcq','Sun','Geothermal vents','Photosynthesis','Water Currents','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(40420,'biology','Match the following options correctly.','Body of Molluscs covered by-calcareous shell,endoskeleton of Echinodermata-calcareous ossicles','Echinodermata have an endoskeleton of calcareous ossicles and, hence, the name Echinodermata (Spiny bodied).In Molluscs Body is covered by a calcareous shell and is unsegmented with a distinct head,muscular foot and visceral hump. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 53 and 54)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','match','Body of Molluscs covered by,calcareous shell','endoskeleton of Echinodermata,calcareous ossicles',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-053','11-044',NULL),
(350406,'biology','Pick the correct statements :\n\nA.The spectral quality of solar radiation is not important for life. \n\nB.The UV component of the spectrum is not harmful to many organisms. \n\nC. Not all the colour components of the visible spectrum are available for marine plants living at different depths of the ocean.','C only','The spectral quality of solar radiation is also important for life. The UV component of the spectrum is harmful to many organisms while not all the colour components of the visible spectrum are available for marine plants living at different depths of the ocean.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222,223, paragraph no:4,1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'2','mcq','A only','B only','C only','A , B , C','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(350407,'biology','Which algae is likely to be found in the deepest waters?','Red algae','Red algae is mostly likely to be found in deep water. This has to do with the different wavelengths of light and how deep they can penetrate water. Red light has the longest wavelength of the visible spectrum which means it penetrates water the least.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222,223, paragraph no:4,1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'2','mcq','Blue algae','Green algae','Red algae','Blue-Green algae','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(350408,'biology','The nature and properties of soil in different places vary. it is dependent on\n \n i) whether soil is transported or sedimentary.\n \n ii) the weathering process.\n \n iii) the climate\n \n iv) loss of soil','i, ii, and iii','The nature and properties of soil in different places vary; it is dependent on the climate, the weathering process, whether soil is transported or sedimentary and how soil development occurred. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:223, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and ii','ii and iv','i and iv','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(350409,'biology','Percolation and water holding capacity of soil is dependant on,\n \n i) Colour of soil\n \n ii) Soil composition, grain size and aggregation.\n \n iii) Holard\n \n iv) pH of the soil','ii only','Percolation and water holding capacity of soil is dependent upon Soil composition, grain size and aggregation.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:223, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','iii and iv','ii only','iv only','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(350410,'biology','Soil composition, grain size and aggregation,these characteristics along with parameters such as _________ determine to a large extent the vegetation in any area.\n \n i) pH\n \n ii) mineral composition\n \n iii) topography\n \n iv) colour of soil','i, ii, and iii','Soil composition, grain size and aggregation,These characteristics along with parameters such as pH, mineral composition and topography determine to a large extent the vegetation in any area.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:223, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iv','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(350411,'biology','In which environment, the sediment-characteristics often determine the type of benthic animals that can thrive there?','Aquatic','In the aquatic environment, the sediment-characteristics often determine the type of benthic animals that can thrive there.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:223, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'2','mcq','Terrestrial','Aquatic','Natural','Artificial','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(350412,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- In the aquatic environment, the sediment-characteristics often determine the type of benthic animals that can thrive there. \n \n Reason- The nature and properties of soil varies from place to place.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The nature and properties of soil in different places vary; in the aquatic environment, the sediment-characteristics often determine the type of benthic animals that can thrive there. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 223, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Major Abiotic factors(Light  and  Soil)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(350501,'biology','Assertion: One would expect that during the course of millions of years of their existence, many species would have evolved a relatively constant internal (within the body) environment.\n\nReason:That permits all biochemical reactions and physiological functions to proceed with maximal efficiency and thus, enhance the overall ‘fitness’ of the species.','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','One would expect that during the course of millions of years of their existence, many species would have evolved a relatively constant internal (within the body) environment that permits all biochemical reactions and physiological functions to proceed with maximal efficiency and thus, enhance the overall ‘fitness’ of the species.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:223, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Responses to Abiotic Factors',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(350502,'biology','The constancy of overall fitness of the species could be in terms of __________ and _______________.','Optimal temperature and osmotic concentration of body fluids.','The constancy of overall fitness of the species could be in terms of optimal temperature and osmotic concentration of body fluids.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:223, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Responses to Abiotic Factors',NULL,'2','mcq','Optimal temperature and diffusion concentration of body fluids.','Critical temperature and osmotic concentration of body fluids.','Optimal temperature and osmotic concentration of body fluids.','Optimal concentration and osmotic concentration of body fluids.','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(350503,'biology','Which of the following term implies the maintenance of relatively constant internal and external conditions within organisms?','Homeostasis','The organism should try to maintain the constancy of its internal environment (a process called homeostasis) despite varying external environmental conditions that tend to upset its homeostasis.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:223, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Responses to Abiotic Factors',NULL,'1','mcq','Homeostasis','Acclimation','Adaptation','Isometry','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(41424,'chemistry','The molecular geometry of AB₄E type molecules:','See-saw','In (a) the lp is present at axial position so there are three lp—bp repulsions at 90°. In(b) the lp is in an equatorial position, and there are two lp—bp repulsions. \n\nHence, arrangement (b) is more stable. The shape shown in (b) is described as a distorted tetrahedron, a folded square or a see-saw. \n\nAs shown in diagram\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 116; Table 4.8) diagram(ne0412)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','Trigonal pyramidal','Bent','See-saw','T-shaped','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(350504,'biology','A person is able to perform his/her best when the temperature is 25 °C and wishes to maintain it so, even when it is scorchingly hot or freezingly cold outside. It could be achieved at home, in the car while travelling, and at workplace by using an air conditioner in summer and heater in winter. Pick the best answer comprehensively','Homeostasis can be achieved artificialy and physiologically','A person is able to perform his/her best when the temperature is 25 degree°C and wishes to maintain it so, even when it is scorchingly hot or freezingly cold outside. It could be achieved at home, in the car while travelling, and at workplace by using an air conditioner in summer and heater in winter. Then his/her performance would be always maximal regardless of the weather around him/her. Here the person’s homeostasis is accomplished, not through physiological, but artificial means.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:223, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Responses to Abiotic Factors',NULL,'2','mcq','Homeostasis can be achieved Physiologically only','Homeostasis can be achieved artificially only','Homeostasis can be achieved artificialy and physiologically','Homeostasis is a phenomeon of weather.','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(350505,'biology','Organisms have evolved various mechanisms to maintain homeostasis to perform its physiological and biochemical functions. This can be obtained through\n \n i) Regulation\n \n ii) Migration\n \n iii) Digestion\n \n iv) Suspension\n \n v) Conformation','i, ii, iv, and v','Organisms have evolved various mechanisms to maintain homeostasis to perform its physiological and biochemical functions. This can be obtained through regulation, conformation, and migration or suspension.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Responses to Abiotic Factors',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i, ii, iii, and iv','i, ii, iv, and v','i and ii','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(350506,'biology','Match the following parts of the given diagram (3501)','a-conformers,b-regulators,c-partial regulators','a-conformers,b-regulators,c-partial regulators\nFigure 13.3 page no-223 class 12 NCERT','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Responses to Abiotic Factors',NULL,'2','match','a,conformers','b,regulators','c,partial regulators',NULL,'NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/350506q.png'),
(350507,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Homeostasis after reading the following Paragraph and choose the correct option. Suppose a person is able to perform his/her best when the temperature is 25 degrees Centigrade and wishes to maintain it so, even when it is scorchingly hot or freezingly cold outside. It could be achieved at home, in the car while travelling, and at workplace by using an air conditioner in summer and heater in winter. Then his/her performance would be always maximal regardless of the weather around him/her. Here the person’s homeostasis is accomplished through which means? \n \n 1. Through Physiological means \n \n 2. Through artificial means \n \n 3. Through psychological means \n \n 4. Through behavioural means','Statement 2 is correct','Suppose a person is able to perform his/her best when the temperature is 25 degree Celsius and wishes to maintain it so, even when it is scorchingly hot or freezingly cold outside. It could be achieved at home, in the car while travelling, and at workplace by using an air conditioner in summer and heater in winter. Then his/her performance would be always maximal regardless of the weather around him/her. Here the person’s homeostasis is accomplished, not through physiological, but artificial means. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 223, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Responses to Abiotic Factors',NULL,'1','mcq','Statement 1 is correct','Statement 3 is correct','Statement 4 is correct','Statement 2 is correct','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(350601,'biology','All birds and mammals, and a very few lower vertebrate and invertebrate species are indeed capable of _________\n \n i) thermoregulation\n \n ii) osmoregulation\n \n iii) photosynthesis','i and ii','All birds and mammals, and a very few lower vertebrate and invertebrate species are indeed capable of such regulation (thermoregulation and osmoregulation).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iii','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350602,'biology','Evolutionary biologists believe that the ‘success’ of mammals is largely due to their ability to maintain a ______________ and thrive whether they live in Antarctica or in the Sahara desert','Constant body temperature','Evolutionary biologists believe that the ‘success’ of mammals is largely due to their ability to maintain a constant body temperature and thrive whether they live in Antarctica or in the Sahara desert.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','Constant weight','Constant body temperature','Higher body temperature','Lower body temperature','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350603,'biology','The mechanisms used by most mammals to regulate their body temperature are similar to the ones that we humans use.Given statement is true or false','TRUE','The mechanisms used by most mammals to regulate their body temperature are similar to the ones that we humans use.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350604,'biology','Humans maintain a constant body temperature of ____.','37° C','Humans maintain a constant body temperature of 37 °C.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'1','mcq','37° C','36° C','35° C','40° C','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350605,'biology','Assertion: In summer, when outside temperature is more than our body temperature, we sweat profusely. \n\nReason:Sweating helps in thermoregulation and brings up the body temperature.','A is true and R is false','In summer, when outside temperature is more than our body temperature, we sweat profusely. The resulting evaporative cooling, similar to what happens with a desert cooler in operation, brings down the body temperature.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350606,'biology','In winter when the temperature is much lower than 37° C, we start to shiver, a kind of exercise which produces heat and raises the body temperature. Given statement is true or false','TRUE','In winter when the temperature is much lower than 37 degree Celsius, we start to shiver, a kind of exercise which produces heat and raises the body temperature.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350607,'biology','Which type of organisms do not have such mechanisms to maintain internal temperatures?','Plants','Plants, on the other hand, do not have such mechanisms to maintain internal temperatures.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','Vertebrates','Invertebrates','Plants','Mammals','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350608,'biology','An overwhelming majority (99 percent) of animals and nearly all plants can maintain a constant internal environment. Given statement is true or false?','FALSE','An overwhelming majority (99 percent) of animals and nearly all plants cannot maintain a constant internal environment.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350609,'biology','In aquatic animals, the osmotic concentration of the body fluids change with that of ambient air and osmotic water concentration, they are called','Conformers','In aquatic animals, the osmotic concentration of the body fluids change with that of the ambient air, water osmotic concentration.The organism in which body temperature changes with the ambient temperature and in aquatic animals, the osmotic concentration of the body fluid change with that of the ambient water osmotic concentration, these animals are called conformers.\n\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','Conformers','Regulators','Migrators','Hibernators','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350610,'biology','Which animals and plants are simply conformers?','Aquatic','Aquatic animals and plants are simply conformers.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','Terrestrial','Aquatic','Natural','Artificial','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350611,'biology','Which regulation is energetically expensive for many organisms.','Thermoregulation','Thermoregulation is energetically expensive for many organisms.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','Thermoregulation','Osmoregulation','Gene regulation','Circadian regulation','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350612,'biology','Thermoregulation is energetically expensive particularly true for small animals like ___________ .','Shrews and hummingbirds','Thermoregulation is energetically expensive for many organisms. This is particularly true for small animals like shrews and hummingbirds.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','Parrots and dove','Shrews and parrots','Shrews and hummingbirds','Hummingbird and dove','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350613,'biology','Heat loss or heat gain is a function of __________','Surface area','Heat loss or heat gain is a function of surface area. Since small animals have a larger surface area relative to their volume, they tend to lose body heat very fast when it is cold outside; then they have to expend much energy to generate body heat through metabolism. This is the main reason why very small animals are rarely found in polar regions.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','fillup','Su,rf,ace, ar,ea',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350614,'biology','What is the main reason due to which very small animals are rarely found in polar regions.\n \n i) small animals have a larger surface area relative to their volume\n \n ii) they tend to lose body heat very fast when it is cold outside\n \n iii) they have to expend much energy to generate body heat through metabolism\n \n iv) small animals have a smaller surface area relative to their volume','i, ii, and iii','Heat loss or heat gain is a function of surface area. Since small animals have a larger surface area relative to their volume, they tend to lose body heat very fast when it is cold outside; then they have to expend much energy to generate body heat through metabolism. This is the main reason why very small animals are rarely found in polar regions.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and ii','ii and iv','i and iii','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350615,'biology','Pick the correct statements : \n\nA.During the course of evolution, the costs and benefits of maintaining a constant internal environment are taken into consideration. \n\nB.Some species have evolved the ability to regulate, but only over a limited range of environmental conditions, beyond which they simply conform. \n\nC.If the stressful external conditions are localised or remain only for a short duration, the organism has two other alternatives for survival. \n\nD.During the course of evolution, only the benefits of maintaining a variable internal environment are taken into consideration.','A , B , C','During the course of evolution, the costs and benefits of maintaining a constant internal environment are taken into consideration. Some species have evolved the ability to regulate, but only over a limited range of environmental conditions, beyond which they simply conform. If the stressful external conditions are localised or remain only for a short duration, the organism has two other alternatives for survival.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:224, paragraph no:3 and 4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'2','mcq','A and B','B and D','A ,B , C , D','A , B , C','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350616,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Evolutionary biologists believe that mammals are successful animals. \n \n Reason- Mammals have the ability to thrive whether they live in Antarctica or in the Sahara desert.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Evolutionary biologists believe that the ‘success’ of mammals is largely due to their ability to maintain a constant body temperature and thrive whether they live in Antarctica or in the Sahara desert. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Regulate and Conform',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(350701,'biology','Keoladeo National Park (Bharatpur) hosts thousands of migrating birds from Siberia and other extremely cold northern regions. This park is located in','Rajasthan','Keoladeo / Ghana bird sanctuary national park is located in Bharatpur district in Rajasthan. Every year during winters when temperature in Siberia becomes very low; migratory birds from Siberia reach this national park for breeding.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:225, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Migrate and Suspend',NULL,'2','mcq','Gujarat','West Bengal','Rajasthan','Madhya Pradesh','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(350702,'biology','Every winter the famous Keoladeo National Park (Bharatpur) in Rajasthan host thousands of migratory birds coming from _________','Siberia','Every winter the famous Keoladeo National Park (Bharatpur) in Rajasthan host thousands of migratory birds coming from Siberia.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:225, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Migrate and Suspend',NULL,'1','mcq','Australia','South america','Siberia','Desert','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(2000110746,'physics','In an atom electrons revolve around the nucleus along a path of radius $0.72$  $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ making $9.4 \\times 10^{18}$ revolutions per second. The equivalent current is [Given $e = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}C$ ]','D','Current $I=\\dfrac{e}{T}=e f$<br>$ = \\left( {1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}} \\right) \\times \\left( {9.4 \\times {{10}^{18}}} \\right) = 1.5\\,A$','Electric Current ','Current Electricity','Medium',NULL,'1.4 $A$','1.8 $A$','1.2 $A$','1.5 $A$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(350703,'biology','In bacteria, fungi and lower plants, various kinds of thick walled spores are formed which help them survive unfavourable conditions – these germinate when there is availability of suitable environment.This happens in','Suspend','Suspend: In bacteria, fungi and lower plants, various kinds of thick walled spores are formed which help them to survive unfavourable conditions – these germinate on availability of suitable environment.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:225, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Migrate and Suspend',NULL,'2','mcq','Conform','Migrate','Regulate','Suspend','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(350704,'biology','In higher plants, seeds and some other vegetative reproductive structures serve as means to tide over periods of stress besides helping in dispersal – they germinate to form new plants under favourable moisture and temperature conditions. They do so by reducing their metabolic activity and going into a state of ‘dormancy’. Given statement is true or false?','TRUE','In higher plants, seeds and some other vegetative reproductive structures serve as means to tide over periods of stress besides helping in dispersal – they germinate to form new plants under favourable moisture and temperature conditions. They do so by reducing their metabolic activity and going into a state of ‘dormancy’.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:225, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Migrate and Suspend',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
