(350903,'biology','What are some examples of a population? \n\nA.The food chain in a wetlands area \n\nB.All the cormorants in a wetland \n\nC.Rats in an abandoned dwelling \n\nD.Teakwood trees in a forest tract \n\nE. Bacteria in a culture plate \n\nF.Lotus plants in a pond.','B ,C ,D ,E ,F','All the cormorants in a wetland, rats in an abandoned dwelling, teakwood trees in a forest tract, bacteria in a culture plate and lotus plants in a pond, are some examples of a population. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','A, B , C','C , D,  F','A ,B ,C ,D , E , F','B ,C ,D ,E ,F','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(350904,'biology','Population ecology is an important area because it links ecology to\n \n i) population genetics\n \n ii) evolution\n \n iii) organisms','i and ii','Population ecology is, therefore, an important area because it links ecology to population genetics and evolution.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iii','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(350905,'biology','An individual may have births and deaths, but a population has birth rates and death rates,these rates refer to _________ births and deaths.','Per capita','An individual may have births and deaths, but a population has birth rates and death rates , these rates refer to per capita births and deaths.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','fillup','Pe,r ca,pi,ta',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(350906,'biology','If in a pond there were 20 lotus plants last year and through reproduction 8 new plants are added, taking the current population to 28, we calculate the birth rate as ___________ offspring per lotus per year','8/20 = 0.4','If in a pond there were 20 lotus plants last year and through reproduction 8 new plants are added, taking the current population to 28, we calculate the birth rate as 8/20 = 0.4 offspring per lotus per year.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','8*20 = 160','8+20 = 28','8/20 = 0.4','8-20 = 12','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(350907,'biology','If 4 individuals in a laboratory population of 40 fruitflies died during a specified time interval, say a week, the death rate in the population during that period is 4/40 = ____ individuals per fruitfly per week.','0.1','If 4 individuals in a laboratory population of 40 fruitflies died during a specified time interval, say a week, the death rate in the population during that period is 4/40 = 0.1 individuals per fruitfly per week.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','0.2','0.1','1','0.8','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(350908,'biology','Another attribute characteristic of a population is ________ .','Sex ratio','Another attribute characteristic of a population is sex ratio.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','Birth rate','Death rate','Sex ratio','Morbidity rate','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(350909,'biology','If the age distribution (% individuals of a given age or age group) is plotted for the population, the resulting structure is called an __________.','Age pyramid','If the age distribution (% individuals of a given age or age group) is plotted for the population, the resulting structure is called an age pyramid.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','Population pyramid','Age pyramid','Biomass pyramid','Ecological pyramid','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(350910,'biology','The shape of the pyramids reflects the growth status of the population they include ____________\n \n i) growing\n \n ii) stable\n \n iii) declining\n \n iv) variable','i, ii, and iii','The shape of the pyramids reflects the growth status of the population - (a) whether it is growing, (b) stable or (c) declining.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iv','i, ii, and iii','i and iii','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(350911,'biology','The size of the population tells us a lot about its status in the ________ .','Habitat','The size of the population tells us a lot about its status in the habitat.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:5)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','fillup','Ha,bi,ta,t',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-227','12-192',NULL),
(350912,'biology','Which among the following is Incorrect?','An individual organism has certain attributes whereas, a population does not.','A population has certain attributes whereas, an individual organism does not. An individual is either a male or a female but a population has a sex ratio (e.g., 60 % of the population are females and 40 % males). Although the term interbreeding implies sexual reproduction, a group of individuals resulting from even asexual reproduction is also generally considered a population for the purpose of ecological studies. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:1,2, 3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'3','mcq','A group of individuals resulting from even asexual reproduction is also generally considered a population for the purpose of ecological studies.','An individual organism has certain attributes whereas, a population does not.','An individual is either a male or a female but a population has a sex ratio.','A population has certain attributes whereas, an individual organism does not.','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(350913,'biology','What are the ecological processes we wish to investigate in a population which we always evaluate them in terms of any change in the population size?\n \n i) outcome of competition with another species\n \n ii) the impact of a predator\n \n iii) the effect of a pesticide application\n \n iv) outcome of competition within the same specie','i, ii and iii','Ecological processes we wish to investigate in a population, be it the outcome of competition with another species, the impact of a predator or the effect of a pesticide application, we always evaluate them in terms of any change in the population size. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:5 and page no:228, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iv','i and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-227','12-192',NULL),
(350914,'biology','The size of a population, in nature, ranges from?','<10 to millions','The size, in nature, could be as low as <10 (Siberian cranes at Bharatpur wetlands in any year) or go into millions (Chlamydomonas in a pond).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'1','mcq','<1000 to billions','<10 to millions','>100 to millions','<100 to millions','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(350915,'biology','Population size, technically called as ___________','Population density','Population size, technically called population density.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','fillup','Po,pu,la,ti,on de,ns,it,y',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(350916,'biology','Population size, technically called population density is designated as ?','N','Population size, technically called population density designated as N.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','PD','P','D','N','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(350917,'biology','Population size, technically called population density (designated as N), need not necessarily be measured in ________ only.','Numbers','Population size, technically called population density (designated as N), need not necessarily be measured in numbers only.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','Figures','Numbers','Decimals','Alphabets','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(350918,'biology','The measurement in number is not appropriate in which cases?\n \n i) If there are 200 carrot grass (Parthenium hysterophorus) plants but only a single huge banyan tree with a large canopy.\n \n ii) Siberian cranes at Bharatpur wetlands in any year','Only i','The size, in nature, could be as low as <10 (Siberian cranes at Bharatpur wetlands in any year)\n\nTotal number is generally the most appropriate measure of population density, it is in some cases either meaningless or difficult to determine. In an area, if there are 200 carrot grass (Parthenium hysterophorus) plants but only a single huge banyan tree with a large canopy, stating that the population density of banyan is low relative to that of carrot grass amounts to underestimating the enormous role of the Banyan in that community. \n\nIn such cases, the % cover or biomass is a more meaningful measure of the population size. Total number is again not an easily adoptable measure if the population is huge and counting is impossible or very time-consuming. If you have a dense laboratory culture of bacteria in a petri dish what is the best measure to report its density? \n\nSometimes, for certain ecological investigations, there is no need to know the absolute population densities; relative densities serve the purpose equally well.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','Only i','Only ii','None of them','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(350919,'biology','A single huge banyan tree with a large canopy in a community of 200 carrot grass. in this case ________ is a more meaningful measure of the population size','The percent cover or biomass','In an area, if there are 200 carrot grass (Parthenium hysterophorus) plants but only a single huge banyan tree with a large canopy, stating that the population density of banyan is low relative to that of carrot grass amounts to underestimating the enormous role of the Banyan in that community. In such cases, the % cover or biomass is a more meaningful measure of the population size.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','The percent cover or biomass','Numbers or bionumbers','Ratio or Viability rate','Figures or incidence','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(350920,'biology','The tiger census in our national parks and tiger reserves is often based on ?\n \n i) pug marks\n \n ii) fecal pellets\n \n iii) counting individual tiger','i and ii','The tiger census in our national parks and tiger reserves is often based on pug marks and fecal pellets.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(350921,'biology','Match the following parts of the given diagram (3502)','a-Expanding,b-Stable,c-Declining','a-Expanding,b-Stable,c-Declining\nFigure 13.4 pageno-227 class 12 NCERT','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','match','a,Expanding','b,Stable','c,Declining',NULL,'NEW','12-227','12-192','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/350921q.png'),
(350922,'biology','Although an individual organism is the one that has to cope with a changed environment, it is at the ____________ level that natural selection operates to evolve the desired traits.','Population','Although an individual organism is the one that has to cope with a changed environment, it is at the population level that natural selection operates to evolve the desired traits.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','Individual','Species','Population','Reproduction','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(350923,'biology','In fact, it is these changes in __________ that give us some idea of what is happening to the population – whether it is flourishing or declining.','Population density','In fact, it is these changes in population density that give us some idea of what is happening to the population – whether it is flourishing or declining. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','Population rate','Population density','Population number','Population size','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(350924,'biology','In nature, a given habitat has enough resources to support a maximum possible number, beyond which no further growth is possible. What is it called?','Carrying capacity','In nature, a given habitat has enough resources to support a maximum possible number, beyond which no further growth is possible. Let us call this limit as nature’s carrying capacity (K) for that species in that habitat.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','Carrying capacity','Intrinsic rate of population density','Population density','Intrinsic capacity','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(350925,'biology','Carrying capacity is denoted by,','K','In nature, a given habitat has enough resources to support a maximum possible number, beyond which no further growth is possible. Let us call this limit as nature’s carrying capacity (K) for that species in that habitat.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Attributes',NULL,'2','mcq','N','r','R','K','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(351001,'biology','What changes with time, depending on various factors including food availability, predation pressure and adverse weather.','Population size','The size of the population keeps changing with time, depending on various factors including food availability, predation pressure and adverse weather.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','fillup','Po,pu,la,ti,on si,ze',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(351002,'biology','The density of a population in a given habitat during a given period, fluctuates due to changes due to how many basic processes?','4','The density of a population in a given habitat during a given period, fluctuates due to changes in four basic processes.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','2','3','4','5','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(351003,'biology','Which processes contribute to an increase in population density?','Natality and immigration','Natality and immigration contribute to an increase in population density.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Natality and mortality','Mortality and immigration','Natality and immigration','Mortality and emigration','NEW','12-228','12-193',NULL),
(351004,'biology','Which processes contribute to an decrease in population density?','Mortality and emigration','Mortality and emigration processes contribute to an decrease in population density.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Natality and immigration','Natality and mortality','Mortality and emigration','Mortality and immigration','NEW','12-228','12-193',NULL),
(351005,'biology','_______ refers to the number of births during a given period in the population that are added to the initial density','Natality','Natality refers to the number of births during a given period in the population that are added to the initial density.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Emigration','Natality','Immigration','Mortality','NEW','12-228','12-193',NULL),
(351006,'biology','___________ is the number of individuals of the population who left the habitat and gone elsewhere during the time period under consideration','Emigration','Emigration is the number of individuals of the population who left the habitat and gone elsewhere during the time period under consideration.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Natality','Immigration','Mortality','Emigration','NEW','12-228','12-193',NULL),
(351007,'biology','___________ is the number of individuals of the same species that have come into the habitat from elsewhere during the time period under consideration.','Immigration','Immigration is the number of individuals of the same species that have come into the habitat from elsewhere during the time period under consideration.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Mortality','Natality','Immigration','Emigration','NEW','12-228','12-193',NULL),
(351008,'biology','_______ is the number of deaths in the population during a given period.','Mortality','Mortality is the number of deaths in the population during a given period.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Emigration','Mortality','Natality','Immigration','NEW','12-228','12-193',NULL),
(351009,'biology','If N is the population density at time t, then its density at time is ?','Nt+1 = Nt + [(B + I) – (D + E)]','If N is the population density at time t, then its density at time t +1 is Nt+1 = Nt + [(B + I) – (D + E)].(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:229, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Nt+1 = Nt + [(A  + B) – (D + E)]','Nt+1 = Nt + [(B + I) – (D + E)]','Nt+1 = Nt + [(I + I) – (D + E)]','Nt+1 = Nt + [(B + I) – (C + D)]','NEW','12-229','12-193',NULL),
(351010,'biology','What happens when in the population density if the number of births plus the number of immigrants (B + I) is more than the number of deaths plus the number of emigrants (D + E).?','Increase','The population density will increase if the number of births plus the number of immigrants (B + I) is more than the number of deaths plus the number of emigrants (D + E).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:229, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Decrease','Increase','Contant','Change','NEW','12-229','12-193',NULL),
(351011,'biology','Under normal conditions, ______  and  ________ are the most important factors influencing population density','Birth  and  death','Under normal conditions, births and deaths are the most important factors influencing population density.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:229, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Emigration  and  immigration','Size  and  number','Birth  and  death','Sex ratio  and  percapita','NEW','12-229','12-193',NULL),
(351012,'biology','If a new habitat is just being colonised, What may contribute more significantly to population growth than birth rates?','Immigration','If a new habitat is just being colonised, immigration may contribute more significantly to population growth than birth rates.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:229, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Mortality','Immigration','Emigration','Natality','NEW','12-229','12-193',NULL),
(351013,'biology','The size of a population for any species is a static parameter. Given statement is true or false','FALSE','If in a population of size N, the birth rates (not total number but per capita births) are represented as b and death rates (again, per capita death rates) as d, then the increase or decrease in N during a unit time period t (dN/dt) will be\ndN/dt = (b – d) × N\nLet (b–d) = r, then\ndN/dt = rN\nHence proved that the size of a population for any species is not a static parameter.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:230, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(351014,'biology','Match the following parts of the given diagram (3503)','a-natality,b-population density,c-mortality,d-emigration','Figure 13.5 page no-229 class 12 NCERT','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','match','a,natality','b,population density','c,mortality','d,emigration','NEW','12-229','12-193','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/351014q.png'),
(351015,'biology','How many types of growth models are there?','2','There are two types of growth models.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:229, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','2','3','4','5','NEW','12-229','12-194',NULL),
(351101,'biology','What availability is obviously essential for the unimpeded growth of a population?\n \n i) food\n \n ii) resources\n \n iii) space\n \n iv) maximal temperature','i, ii, and iii','Resource (food and space) availability is obviously essential for the unimpeded growth of a population.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:229, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Exponential Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','ii and iv','i only','i and iii','NEW','12-229','12-194',NULL),
(351102,'biology','What Darwin observed while developing his theory of natural selection.','When resources in the habitat are unlimited, each species has the ability to realise fully its innate potential to grow in number','Ideally, when resources in the habitat are unlimited, each species has the ability to realise fully its innate potential to grow in number, as Darwin observed while developing his theory of natural selection.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:229, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Exponential Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','When resources in the habitat are unlimited, each species has the ability to realise fully its innate potential to grow in number','When resources in the habitat are limited, each species has the ability to realise fully its innate potential to shrink in number','When resources in the habitat are unlimited, each species has the ability to realise fully its innate potential to shrink in number','When resources in the habitat are limited, each species has the ability to realise fully its innate potential to grow in number','NEW','12-229','12-194',NULL),
(351103,'biology','If in a population of size N, the birth rates (per capita births) are represented as b and death rates (per capita death rates) as d, then the increase or decrease in N during a unit time period t (dN/dt) will be?\n \n i) dN/dt = (b – d) × N\n \n ii) N/dt = rN(b-d)\n \n iii) Let (b–d) = r ;dN/dt = rN','i and iii','If in a population of size N, the birth rates (not total number but per capita births) are represented as b and death rates (again, per capita death rates) as d, then the increase or decrease in N during a unit time period t (dN/dt) will be dN/dt = (b – d) × N Let (b–d) = r, then dN/dt = rN. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:230, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Exponential Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','ii and iii','i, ii, and iii','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(351104,'biology','dN/dt = rN The r in this equation is called the _____________','Intrinsic rate of natural increase','dN/dt = rN The r in this equation is called the ‘intrinsic rate of natural increase’.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:230, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Exponential Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Intrinsic rate of natural decrease','Intrinsic rate of natural change','Intrinsic rate of natural increase','Intrinsic rate of natural constant','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(351105,'biology','The magnitude of r values, for the Norway rat is ________','0.015','The magnitude of r values, for the Norway rat the r is 0.015.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:230, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Exponential Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','0.05','0.015','0.15','0.005','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(351106,'biology','The magnitude of r values, for the flour beetle is ________','0.12','The magnitude of r values, for the the flour beetle it is 0.12.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:230, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Exponential Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','0.12','0.001','0.08','0.13','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(351107,'biology','The exponential or geometric growth pattern of a population density results in a which shaped curve when we plot N in relation to time.?','J','The exponential or geometric growth pattern of a population (Figure 13.6) and results in a J-shaped curve when we plot N in relation to time.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:230, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Exponential Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','C','J','L','S','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(351108,'biology','Any species growing ________ under unlimited resource conditions can reach enormous population densities in a short time.','Exponentially','Any species growing exponentially under unlimited resource conditions can reach enormous population densities in a short time.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:230, paragraph no:5)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Exponential Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Logistically','Exponentially','Constantly','Equally','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(351109,'biology','Who showed how even a slow growing animal like elephant could reach enormous numbers in the absence of checks.','Darwin','Darwin showed how even a slow growing animal like elephant could reach enormous numbers in the absence of checks. The following is an anecdote popularly narrated to demonstrate dramatically how fast a huge population could build up when growing exponentially.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:230, paragraph no:5)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Exponential Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Darwin','Prof.W.H.Pearsall','Ramdeo Misra','Reiter','NEW','12-230','12-195',NULL),
(351201,'biology','Asymptote in a logistic growth curve is obtained when','K = N','A population growing in a habitat with limited resources show initially a lag phase, followed by phases of acceleration and deceleration and finally an asymptote, when the population density reaches the carrying capacity.\nN = Population density at time t\nK = Carrying capacity\nHence Asymptote in a logistic growth curve is obtained when K = N.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Logistic Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','The value of \'r\' approaches zero','K = N','K > N','K < N','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(351202,'biology','Pick the incorrect option: \n\nA. No population of any species in nature has at its disposal unlimited resources to permit exponential growth.\n\nB. There will be no competition between individuals for limited resources. \n\nC. The ‘fittest’ individual will not survive and reproduce','B,  C','No population of any species in nature has at its disposal unlimited resources to permit exponential growth. This leads to competition between individuals for limited resources.This leads to competition between individuals for limited resources.Eventually, the ‘fittest’ individual will survive and reproduce.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Logistic Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','A , B','B,  C','A ,C','A ,B ,C','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(351203,'biology','A population growing in a habitat with limited resources show phases when the population density reaches the carrying capacity. With regards to the statement, choose the correct order of the phases','Lag phase, acceleration phase , deceleration phase, asymptote phase','A population growing in a habitat with limited resources show initially a lag phase, followed by phases of acceleration and deceleration and finally an asymptote,when the population density reaches the carrying capacity.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Logistic Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Lag phase, asymptote phase , deceleration phase, acceleration phase','Asymotote phase, acceleration phase , deceleration phase, lag phase','Lag phase, deceleration phase , acceleration phase, asymptote phase','Lag phase, acceleration phase , deceleration phase, asymptote phase','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(351204,'biology','A plot of N in relation to time (t) results in a which type of curve?','Sigmoid','A plot of N in relation to time (t) results in a sigmoid curve.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Logistic Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Sigmoid','J shaped','Linear','C shaped','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(351205,'biology','What is the equation given for Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth is described by','dN/dt =rN [K - N / K]','The equation given for Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth is dN/dt =rN [K - N / K].(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Logistic Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','dN/dt =N [K - N / K]','dN/dt =rN [K - N / K]','dN/dT =rN [K +N / K]','dN/dt =r [K - N / N]','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(351206,'biology','The equation given for Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth is dN/dt =rN [K - N / K] The N represents','N = Population density at time t','N = Population density at time t.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Logistic Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','N = Number of Population','N = Number of Population density at r','N = Population density at time t','N = Population rate at time t','NEW','12-231','12-196',NULL),
(351207,'biology','The equation given for Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth is dN/dt =rN [K - N / K] The K represents','Carrying capacity','K = Carrying capacity.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Logistic Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Population density at time','Carrying capacity','Intrinsic rate of natural increase','none of the above','NEW','12-231','12-196',NULL),
(351208,'biology','Since resources for growth for most animal populations are finite and become limiting sooner or later,which growth model is considered a more realistic one?','Logistic','Since resources for growth for most animal populations are finite and become limiting sooner or later, the logistic growth model is considered a more realistic one.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Logistic Growth',NULL,'2','mcq','Exponential','Logistic','Constantly','Equally','NEW','12-231','12-196',NULL),
(351301,'biology','Populations evolve to maximise their reproductive fitness, also called _______ fitness','Darwinian','Populations evolve to maximise their reproductive fitness, also called Darwinian fitness.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:6)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Life History Variation',NULL,'2','fillup','Da,rw,in,ia,n',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-231','12-196',NULL),
(351302,'biology','Which one of the given organisms will breed only once in their entire lifetime?','Pacific salmon fish and bamboo','Some organisms breed only once in their lifetime are Pacific salmon fish, bamboo.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Life History Variation',NULL,'2','mcq','Humans','Pacific salmon fish and bamboo','Common crow','Oysters','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(351303,'biology','Which organism produce a large number of small-sized offspring?\n \n i) Pelagic fishes\n \n ii) Mammals\n \n iii) Oysters','i and iii','Some produce a large number of small-sized offspring are Oysters, pelagic fishes.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Life History Variation',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','ii and iii','ii only','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(351304,'biology','Which organism produce a small number of large-sized offspring?','Mammals','Organisms produce a small number of large-sized offspring are birds, mammals.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Life History Variation',NULL,'2','mcq','Oysters','Mammals','Pelagic fishes','Pacific salmon fish','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(351305,'biology','Which group of scientists suggested that life history traits of organisms have evolved in relation to the constraints imposed by the abiotic and biotic components of the habitat in which they live','Ecologists','Ecologists suggest that life history traits of organisms have evolved in relation to the constraints imposed by the abiotic and biotic components of the habitat in which they live.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Life History Variation',NULL,'2','mcq','Paelentologists','Ecologists','Archeaologists','Marine Biologists','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(351306,'biology','Evolution of life history traits in different species has currently stopped research being conducted by ecologists. Given statement is true or false?','FALSE','Evolution of life history traits in different species is currently an important area of research being conducted by ecologists.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Life History Variation',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(351401,'biology','It is possible that a natural habitat on earth can be inhabited only by a single species. Given statement is true or false?','FALSE','There is no such habitat and such a situation is even inconceivable.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(351402,'biology','Assertion: Even a plant species, which makes its own food, cannot survive alone.\n\nReason: Plants need soil microbes to break down the organic matter in soil and return the inorganic nutrients for absorption.','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','Even a plant species, which makes its own food, cannot survive alone; it needs soil microbes to break down the organic matter in soil and return the inorganic nutrients for absorption . it is obvious that in nature, animals, plants and microbes do not and cannot live in isolation but interact in various ways to form a biological community.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(351403,'biology','Interspecific interactions arise from the interaction of populations of how many different species?','Two','Interspecific interactions arise from the interaction of populations of two different species.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Two','Three','Four','Many','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(351404,'biology','Interspecific interactions arise from the interaction of populations of two different species. They could be _______ to one of the species or both.\n \n i) Beneficial\n \n ii) Detrimental\n \n iii) Neutral\n \n iv) Non-Consequential','i, ii, and iii','Interspecific interactions arise from the interaction of populations of two different species. They could be beneficial, detrimental or neutral (neither harm nor benefit) to one of the species or both.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iv','i and iii','i, ii, and iii','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(351405,'biology','Match the following interspecific interactions with they meaning','‘+’ sign-beneficial interaction,‘-’ sign-detrimental interaction,‘0’ sign-neutral interaction','Interspecific interactions arise from the interaction of populations of two different species. They could be beneficial, detrimental or neutral (neither harm nor benefit) to one of the species or both. Assigning a ‘+’ sign for beneficial interaction, ‘-’ sign for detrimental and 0 for neutral interaction.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','match','‘+’ sign,beneficial interaction','‘-’ sign,detrimental interaction','‘0’ sign,neutral interaction',NULL,'NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(351406,'biology','Match the following name interactions with they A and B species signs','Mutualism-(+) and (+),Competition-(-) and (-),Predation-(+) and (-),Predation-(+) and (-)','Mutualism-(+) and (+),Competition-(-) and (-),Predation-(+) and (-),Predation-(+) and (-)\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, Table: 13.1L Population Interaction','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','match','Mutualism,(+) and (+)','Competition,(-) and (-)','Predation,(+) and (-)',NULL,'NEW','12-232','12-197','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/351406q.png'),
(351407,'biology','Match the following name interactions with they A and B species signs','Parasitism-(+) and (-),Commensalism-(+) and (0),Amensalism-(-) and (0)','Parasitism-(+) and (-),Commensalism-(+) and (0),Amensalism-(-) and (0)\nReference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, Table: 13.1L Population Interaction','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','match','Parasitism,(+) and (-)','Commensalism,(+) and (0)','Amensalism,(-) and (0)',NULL,'NEW','12-232','12-197',NULL),
(351408,'biology','Pick the incorrect option : \n\nA.for any species, it doesn\'t require one more species on which it can feed. \n\nB.even in minimal communities, many interactive linkages exist, although all are readily apparent. \n\nC.for any species, it does requires one more species on which it can feed. \n\nD.for any species, it doesn\'t require more than one species on which it can feed.','A,B','Can you think of any natural habitat on earth that is inhabited just by a single species? There is no such habitat and such a situation is even inconceivable. For any species, the minimal requirement is one more species on which it can feed. Even a plant species, which makes its own food, cannot survive alone; it needs soil microbes to break down the organic matter in soil and return the inorganic nutrients for absorption. And then, how will the plant manage pollination without an animal agent? It is obvious that in nature, animals, plants and microbes do not and cannot live in isolation but interact in various ways to form a biological community. Even in minimal communities, many interactive linkages exist, although all may not be readily apparent.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','A,B','B,C','C,D','A,D','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(351409,'biology','Both the species benefit in ____________ in their interactions with each other','Mutualism','Both the species benefit in mutualism.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Competition','Predation','Mutualism','Parasitism','NEW','12-232','12-197',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(351410,'biology','Both species lose in __________ in their interactions with each other','Competition','Both the species lose in competition in their interactions with each other.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Predation','Competition','Amensalism','Mutualism','NEW','12-232','12-197',NULL),
(351411,'biology','The interaction where one species is benefited and the other is neither benefited nor harmed is called ?','Commensalism','The interaction where one species is benefited and the other is neither benefited nor harmed is called commensalism.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Competition','Commensalism','Amensalism','Mutualism','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(351412,'biology','In which pair of species interaction only one species benefits and the interaction is detrimental to the other species.','Predation  and  parasitism','In both parasitism and predation only one species benefits (parasite and predator, respectively) and the interaction is detrimental to the other species (host and prey, respectively).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Competition  and  mutualism','Commensalism  and  amensalism','Parasitism  and  competition','Predation  and  parasitism','NEW','12-232','12-197',NULL),
(351413,'biology','In which species interaction ,one species is harmed whereas the other is unaffected.','amensalism','In amensalism on the other hand one species is harmed whereas the other is unaffected.\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','commensalism','competition','parasitism','amensalism','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(351414,'biology','Which three species interactions share a common characteristic?','Predation,parasitism,commensalism','Predation, parasitism and commensalism share a common characteristic– the interacting species live closely together.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Commensalism,predation,amensalism','Competition,commensalism,mutualism','Parasitism,predation,mutualism','Predation,parasitism,commensalism','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(351415,'biology','A wide variety of chemical substances that we extract from plants on a commercial scale (nicotine, caffeine, quinine, strychnine, opium, etc.,) are produced by ___________ actually as defences against grazers and browsers.','Calotropis','A wide variety of chemical substances that we extract from plants on a commercial scale (nicotine, caffeine, quinine, strychnine, opium, etc.,) are produced by Calotropis actually as defences against grazers and browsers.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','fillup','Ca,lo,tr,op,is',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(351416,'biology','_____________are the most common morphological means of defence.','Thorns','Thorns (Acacia, Cactus) are the most common morphological means of defence.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Population Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Chemicals','Thorns','Slime','Teeth','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(351501,'biology','Which interaction is the nature’s way of transferring to higher trophic levels the energy fixed by plants.','Predation','Predation is the nature’s way of transferring to higher trophic levels the energy fixed by plants.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Commensalism','Amensalism','Predation','Mutualism','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(351502,'biology','Examples of predator and prey include?\n \n i) Sparrow and seed\n \n ii) Tiger and lion\n \n iii) Tiger and deer','i and iii','Examples of predator and prey are tiger and deer , sparrow and seed.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and iii','ii and iii','ii only','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(351503,'biology','Beside energy transfer conduits across trophic levels by predators, what are the other important roles of predators?\n \n i) Help in maintaining species diversity in a community by reducing the intensity of competition among competing prey species.\n \n ii) They can\'t keep prey population under control\n \n iii) They can keep prey population under control','i and iii','Besides the energy transfer ,predators can also have other important roles like they can keep prey population under control ,also help in maintaining species diversity in a community by reducing the intensity of competition among competing prey species.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','i only','ii only','i and iii','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(351504,'biology','Which species could cause instability in the ecosystem?','Prey','Prey species could achieve very high population densities and cause ecosystem instability.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','fillup','Pr,ey',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(351505,'biology','__________ methods adopted in agricultural pest control are based on the ability of the predator to regulate prey population','Biological control','Biological control methods adopted in agricultural pest control are based on the ability of the predator to regulate prey population. Predators also help in maintaining species diversity in a community, by reducing the intensity of competition among competing prey species.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Biological control','Artificial control','Chemical method','Spray method','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(351506,'biology','When certain exotic species are introduced into a geographical area, what impact do they have on ecosystem ?','Start spreading fast','When certain exotic species are introduced into a geographical area, they become invasive and start spreading fast because the invaded land does not have its natural predators and then they can grow rapidly and causes instability in the ecosystem.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Start spreading fast','They become extinct','They cannot survive properly','They remain in same number as they were introduced','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(351507,'biology','Due to introduction of which species into Australia in the early 1920’s caused havoc by spreading rapidly into millions of hectares of rangeland?','Prickly pear cactus','The prickly pear cactus introduced into Australia in the early 1920’s caused havoc by spreading rapidly into millions of hectares of rangeland.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Purple frog','Prickly pear cactus','Nicobar pigeons','Feral cat','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(351508,'biology','Introduction of which predator brought invasive cactus under control in Australia?','Moth','The invasive cactus was brought under control due to introduction of a cactus-feeding predator a moth from its natural habitat was introduced into the country.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Ladybugs','Moth','Spiders','Mantids','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(351509,'biology','The starfish Pisaster is an important predator in which community?','American Pacific coast','In the rocky intertidal communities of the American Pacific Coast the starfish Pisaster is an important predator.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Southeast Alaska','British Columbia Coast','American Pacific coast','Mariana Trench','NEW','12-233','12-198',NULL),
(351510,'biology','In a field experiment, when all the starfish were removed from an enclosed intertidal area, more than 10 species of invertebrates became extinct within a year, because of ___________','Interspecific competition','In a field experiment, when all the starfish were removed from an enclosed intertidal area, more than 10 species of invertebrates became extinct within a year, because of interspecific competition.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Interspecific predation','Interspecific competition','Interspecific mutualism','Interspecific parasitism','NEW','12-233','12-198',NULL),
(351511,'biology','Assertion:Predators in nature are ‘prudent’. \n\nReason:If a predator is too efficient and over exploits its prey, then the prey might become extinct and following it, the predator will also become extinct for lack of food.','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','If a predator is too efficient and over exploits its prey, then the prey might become extinct and following it, the predator will also become extinct for lack of food. This is the reason why predators in nature are ‘prudent’.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-233','12-198',NULL),
(351512,'biology','Some of the Prey species have evolved various defenses to lessen the impact of predation, some of them include?\n \n i) Camouflage technique\n \n ii) Poison technique\n \n iii) Attacking technique\n \n iv) Spreading technique','i and ii','Some species of insects and frogs avoid from being detected easily by the predators by camouflage technique in which they get blended with their environment.Some are poisonous and therefore avoided by the predators.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','i only','ii only','iii only','NEW','12-233','12-198',NULL),
(351513,'biology','Some insects and frogs are cryptically coloured to avoid being detected by predators.This are called?','Camouflage','Some species of insects and frogs avoid from being detected easily by the predators by camouflage technique in which they get blended with their environment.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','fillup','Ca,mo,uf,la,ge',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-233','12-198',NULL),
(351514,'biology','Which butterfly is highly distasteful to its predator (bird) because of a special chemical present in its body?','Monarch butterfly','The Monarch butterfly is highly distasteful to its predator (bird) because of a special chemical present in its body. Interestingly, the butterfly acquires this chemical during its caterpillar stage by feeding on a poisonous weed.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Peacock butterfly','Viceroy butterfly','Monarch butterfly','Red admiral butterfly','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(351515,'biology','At which stage the butterfly acquires the poisonous chemical in its body to get protection from predator?','Caterpillar stage','The butterfly acquires this chemical during its caterpillar stage by feeding on a poisonous weed.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Pupa stage','Caterpillar stage','Adult stage','Egg stage','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(351516,'biology','Nearly 25 percent of all insects are known to be','Phytophagous','Nearly 25 % of all insects are known to be phytophagous (feeding on plant sap and other parts of plants).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','fillup','Ph,yt,op,ha,go,us',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(351517,'biology','Phytophagus means','Feeding on plant sap','For plants, herbivores are the predators. Nearly 25 % of all insects are known to be phytophagous which means feeding on plant sap and other parts of plants.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Feeding on animal sap','Feeding on plant sap','Feeding on cactus','Feeding on plant like calotropis','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(351518,'biology','Thorns in Acacia and cactus are the','Morphological means of defence','As plants cannot move from one place to other they have a astonishing variety of morphological and chemical defences against herbivores.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Physical means of defence','Physiological means of defence','Behavioral means of defence','Morphological means of defence','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(351519,'biology','Which of the following defence mechanism is present in Calotropis?','Highly poisonous cardiac glycosides','Calotropis have mechanism of defence which grow in abandoned fields.The plant produces highly poisonous cardiac glycosides and that is why you never see any cattle or goats browsing on this plant.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Thorns','Highly poisonous cardiac glycosides','Acid production','Alkaloid','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(351520,'biology','Defence mechanisms are evolved in plants or animals to protect themselves from','Predators','Defence mechanisms are evolved in plants or animals to protect themselves from predators so that they cannot be detected.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Predation',NULL,'2','mcq','Commensalism','Parasitism','Competitors','Predators','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(351601,'biology','Struggle for existence and survival of fittest are related to','Darwinism','Darwin told of the struggle for existence and survival of the fittest in nature, he was convinced that interspecific competition is a potent force in organic evolution.\n\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','Lamarckism','Darwinism','Oparins theory','Mendalism','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(351602,'biology','Why does the competition occurs generally in the ecosystem?\n \n i) competition happens because resources are limited\n \n ii) competition occurs when feeding efficiency of one species might be reduced due to the interfering and inhibitory presence of the other species even if resources (food and space) are abundant\n \n iii) Competition never happens in ecosystem','i and ii','When closely related species compete for the same resources that are limiting in the ecosystem then competition occurs generally.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','i only','iii only','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(351603,'biology','In some shallow South American lakes, visiting flamingoes and resident fishes compete for which particular food?','Zooplanktons','Zooplanktons are the common food for resident fishes and visiting flamingoes in the area of some shallow South American lakes.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','Small fishes','Tadpoles','Zooplanktons','Grass','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(351604,'biology','__________ is best defined as a process in which the fitness of one species (measured in terms of its ‘r’ the intrinsic rate of increase) is significantly lower in the presence of another species','Competition','Some of the reasons where competition can also occur due to the feeding efficiency of one species which might be reduced due to the interfering and inhibitory presence of the other species, even if resources (food and space) are abundant. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','fillup','Co,mp,et,it,io,n',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-234','12-199',NULL),
(351605,'biology','The feeding efficiency of one species might be reduced due what reasons?\n \n i) Abundance of resources\n \n ii) Interference of the other species\n \n iii) Inhibitory presence of the other species','ii and iii','Resources need not be limiting for competition to occur; in interference competition, the feeding efficiency of one species might be reduced due to the interfering and inhibitory presence of the other species, even if resources (food and space) are abundant.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and iii','ii and iii','i and ii','NEW','12-234','12-199',NULL),
(351606,'biology','How competition is best defined?','Process in which the fitness of one species is significantly lower in the presence of another species','Competition is best defined as a process in which the fitness of one species (measured in terms of its ‘r’ the intrinsic rate of increase) is significantly lower in the presence of another species. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','Process in which both closely related species are benefitted similarly','Process in which the fitness of one species is significantly lower in the presence of another species','Process in which both different species get benefited','Process in which two species are harmed','NEW','12-234','12-199',NULL),
(351607,'biology','Abingdon tortoise in Galapagos islands became extinct after goats with great browsing efficiency were introduced . It is an example of','competitive exclusion','Abingdon tortoise in Galapagos islands became extinct after goats with great browsing efficiency were introduced . It is an example of the occurrence of competition in nature comes from what is called ‘competitive exclusion’, where goats and abingdon tortoises were competing as herbivores for green plants and the goats by being efficient in browsing, eliminated the tortoises\n\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','competitive release','co-existence','interferance competition','competitive exclusion','NEW','12-234','12-199',NULL),
(351608,'biology','A species whose distribution is restricted to a small geographical area because of the presence of a competitively superior species, is found to expand its distributional range dramatically when the competing species is experimentally removed. This process is','Competitive release','Evidence for the occurrence of competition in nature comes from what is called‘competitive release’. A species whose distribution is restricted to a small geographical area because of the presence of a competitively superior species, is found to expand its distributional range dramatically when the competing species is experimentally removed. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','Competitive release','Co-existence','Interference competition','Competitive exclusion','NEW','12-234','12-199',NULL),
(351609,'biology','In Connell’s elegant field experiments done on the rocky sea coasts of Scotland,Which of the following observations was recorded ?','Barnacle Balanus dominates and excludes the smaller Barnacle Chathamalus from that zone.','In Connell’s elegant field experiments done on the rocky sea coasts of Scotland,Barnacle Balanus dominates and excludes the smaller Barnacle Chathamalus from that zone. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','Barnacle Balanus dominates and excludes the smaller Barnacle Chathamalus from that zone.','Barnacle Chathamalus dominates and excludes the smaller Barnacle  Balanus from that zone.','Barnacle Balanus dominates and excludes the smaller Warblers from that zone.','Warblers dominates and excludes the smaller balanus from that area.','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(351610,'biology','In general, _______ and ________ appear to be more adversely affected by competition than _________.','Herbivores,plants,carnivores','In general, herbivores and plants appear to be more adversely affected by competition than carnivores. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','Omnivores,plants,carnivores','Carnivores,herbivores,plants','Carnivores,plants,omnivores','Herbivores,plants,carnivores','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(351611,'biology','Which of them states that "Two closely related species competing for the same resources cannot co-exist indefinitely and the competitively inferior one will be eliminated eventually."','Gause’s ‘Competitive Exclusion Principle’','Gause’s ‘Competitive Exclusion Principle’ states that two closely related species competing for the same resources cannot co-exist indefinitely and the competitively inferior one will be eliminated eventually. This may be true if resources are limiting, but not otherwise. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','Gause’s ‘Competitive Exclusion Principle’','Gause’s ‘Competitive release Principle’','Darwinism','Cornell','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(351612,'biology','Assertion : Elimination of a competitively inferior species in a closely related or otherwise similar group is known as competitive exclusion principle. \n\nReason : If two species compete for the same resource, they could avoid competition by choosing different times for feeding or different foraging patterns.','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','Gause’s ‘Competitive Exclusion Principle’ states that two closely related species competing for the same resources cannot co-exist indefinitely and the competitively inferior one will be eliminated eventually. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(351613,'biology','Species facing competition might evolve mechanisms that promote co-existence rather than exclusion.Give one such mechanism:','Resource partitioning','Species facing competition might evolve mechanisms that promote co-existence rather than exclusion.Resource partitioning is one such mechanism that can promote co-existence rather than exclusion.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','Competitive release','Resource partitioning','Interference competition','All the above','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(351614,'biology','Five closely related species of warblers living on the same tree were able to avoid competition and co-exist due to behavioural differences in their foraging activities. This mechanism is','Resource Partitioning','Recent studies do not support such gross generalisations about competition. While they do not rule out the occurrence of interspecific competition in nature, they point out that species facing competition might evolve mechanisms that promote co-existence rather than exclusion. One such mechanism is ‘resource partitioning’. If two species compete for the same resource, they could avoid competition by choosing, for instance, different times for feeding or different foraging patterns. MacArthur showed that five closely related species of warblers living on the same tree were able to avoid competition and co-exist due to behavioural differences in their foraging activities.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','fillup','Re,so,ur,ce Pa,rt,it,io,ni,ng',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(351615,'biology','Assertion:Five closely related species of warblers living on the same tree can avoid competition and co-exist.\n\nReason:This occurs due to behavioural differences in their foraging activities.','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','MacArthur showed that five closely related species of warblers living on the same tree were able to avoid competition and co-exist due to behavioural differences in their foraging activities.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(351616,'biology','The interaction between a lion and a tiger living in the same forest and feeding on a limited number of deers is an example of :','Competition','Both Lions and Tigers are carnivores that feed on herbivores, when they are in an environment with limited resources, they compete for resources','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Competition',NULL,'2','mcq','Competition','Amensalism','Parasitism','Commensalism','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(351701,'biology','Many parasites have evolved to be host-specific (they can parasitise only a single species of host) in such a way that both host and the parasite tend to co-evolve','TRUE','Many parasites have evolved to be host-specific (they can parasitise only a single species of host) in such a way that both host and the parasite tend to co evolve; that is, if the host evolves special mechanisms for rejecting or resisting the parasite, the parasite has to evolve mechanisms to counteract and neutralise them, in order to be successful with the same host species.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-235','12-200',NULL),
(351702,'biology','What are the mechanisms that host evolves?','Rejecting','The host evolves special mechanisms for rejecting or resisting the parasite, to co-evolve, in order to be successful. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','Rejecting','Reusing','Counteract','Neutralize','NEW','12-235','12-200',NULL),
(351703,'biology','What are the mechanisms that parasite evolves when host evolves special mechanisms for rejecting or resisting the parasite?\n \n i) Counteract\n \n ii) Neutralize\n \n iii) Rejecting','i and ii','The parasite has to evolve mechanisms to counteract and neutralise them, in order to be successful with the same host species. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','i, iii, and iii','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iii','NEW','12-235','12-200',NULL),
(351704,'biology','What are the special adaptations of parasites in accordance with their life styles?\n \n i) loss of digestive system\n \n ii) high reproductive capacity.\n \n iii) presence of adhesive organs or suckers to cling on to the host\n \n iv) low reproductive capacity.','i, ii, and iii','In accordance with their life styles, parasites evolved special adaptations such as the loss of unnecessary sense organs, presence of adhesive organs or suckers to cling on to the host, loss of digestive system and high reproductive capacity. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and iv','ii and iv','i and iii','NEW','12-235','12-200',NULL),
(351705,'biology','What are the two intermediate hosts on which the human liver fluke depends to complete its life cycle?','A fish  and  a snail','The human liver fluke (a trematode parasite) depends on two intermediate hosts (a snail and a fish) to complete its life cycle.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','A fish  and  a plant','A fish  and  a snail','A snail  and  a frog','A plant  and  a snail','NEW','12-235','12-200',NULL),
(351706,'biology','Which of them is a vector of malarial parasite?','Mosquito','The malarial parasite needs a vector i.e mosquito to spread to other hosts. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','Butterfly','Mosquito','Honey bee','Human','NEW','12-235','12-200',NULL),
(351707,'biology','When parasites harm the host, what are the consequences?\n \n i) reduce the survival\n \n ii) reduce the population density\n \n iii) makes host physically weak\n \n iv) increase the population density\n \n v) increase the survival','i, ii, and iii','When Majority of the parasites harm the host; they may reduce the survival, growth and reproduction of the host and reduce its population density.They may also make host physically weak.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and v','ii and iv','i and iv','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(351708,'biology','The Parasites that feed on the external surface of the host organism are called ?','Ectoparasites','Parasites that feed on the external surface of the host organism are called ectoparasites. Ectoparasites are the organisms that live on the skin of the host,from which they derive their sustenance. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','Protozoa','Helminths','Ectoparasites','Endoparasites','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(351709,'biology','What are the most familiar examples of ectoparasites?\n \n i) lice on humans\n \n ii) ticks on dogs\n \n iii) copepods in marine fishes\n \n iv) human liver fluke','i, ii, and iii','The most familiar examples of ectoparasites include lice on humans,ticks on dogs,copepods in marine fishes etc. They describe parasitism very well. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and ii','iv','i and iv','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(351710,'biology','Which parasitic plant that is commonly found growing on hedge plants, has lost its chlorophyll and leaves in the course of evolution.','Cuscuta','Cuscuta is a parasitic plant that is commonly found growing on hedge plants, has lost its chlorophyll and leaves in the course of evolution. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','Tecoma','Aralia','Palms','Cuscuta','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(351711,'biology','Which of the following is not considered a parasite?','Female mosquito','The female mosquito is not considered a parasite, although it needs our blood for reproduction. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','Male Mosquito','Female mosquito','Leeches','Fleas','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(351712,'biology','Parasites that live inside the host body  are called?','Endoparasites','The parasites that live inside the body at different body sites like liver,kidney ,lungs, redblood cells, etc are called endoparasites (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','Ectoparasites','Endoparasites','Helminths','Protozoa','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(351713,'biology','Assertion :The life cycles of endoparasites are more complex  \n\nReason:The morphological and anatomical features are greatly simplified while emphasising their reproductive potential, making them adapt in a single host/multiple hosts where they live on and evolve','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','The life cycles of endoparasites are more complex because of their extreme specialisation. Their morphological and anatomical features are greatly simplified while emphasising their reproductive potential which makes them adapt to the host they want to live and the number of hosts they live on and stages of changes,etc\n\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(351714,'biology','_____________ in birds is a fascinating example of parasitism in which the parasitic bird lays its eggs in the nest of its host and lets the host incubate them','Brood parasitism','Brood parasitism in birds is a fascinating example of parasitism in which the parasitic bird lays its eggs in the nest of its host and lets the host incubate them.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','fillup','Br,oo,d pa,ra,si,ti,sm',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(351715,'biology','Pick the correct statement: \n\nA. Brood parasitism in birds is a example of parasitism in which the parasitic bird lays its eggs in the nest of its host and lets the host incubate them. \n\nB.During the course of evolution, the eggs of the parasitic bird have evolved to resemble the host’s egg in size and colour to reduce the chances of the host bird detecting the foreign eggs and ejecting them from the nest. \n\nC. Brood parasitism in birds is a example of parasitism in which the host bird lays its eggs in the nest of its parasite and lets the parasite incubate them. \n\nD.During the course of evolution, the eggs of the parasitic bird have evolved to resemble the host’s egg in size and colour to increase the chances of the host bird detecting the foreign eggs and ejecting them from the nest.','A ,B','Brood parasitism in birds is an example of parasitism in which the parasitic birds lays its eggs iin the nest of its host and the host incubates them. This way, the bird can save the energy that it would have spent in incubation and can use that energy iin foraging and other activities. During the course of evolution, the eggs of the parasite bird have evolved to resemble the host\'s eggs in size and colour to reduce the chances of the hose bird detecting the foreign eggs and removing them from the nest. Laying eggs by cuckoo (koel) in crow\'s nest is brood parasitism. Cowbirds also show brood parasitism. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','A,C','C ,D','A ,B','A only','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(351716,'biology','Which among the following is an example of commensalism?\n \n i) An orchid growing as an epiphyte on a mango branch, sea anemone that has stinging tentacles and the clown fish that lives among them.\n \n ii) Barnacles growing on the back of a whale.\n \n iii) The cattle egret and grazing cattle in close association.\n \n iv) Lice on human','i, ii, and iii','Commensalism is the interaction in which one species benefits and the other is neither harmed nor benefited. An orchid growing as an epiphyte on a mango branch, and barnacles growing on the back of a whale benefit while neither the mango tree nor the whale derives any apparent benefit. The cattle egret and grazing cattle in close association, a sight you are most likely to catch if you live in farmed rural areas, is a classic example of commensalism.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Parasitism',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and iv','ii and iii','i and iv','NEW','12-236','12-201',NULL),
(351801,'biology','An orchid growing as an epiphyte on a mango branch is an example of','Commensalism','An orchid growing on the branch of a mango tree is an epiphyte. Epiphytes are plants growing on other plants which, however, do not derive nutrition from them. Therefore, the relationship between a mango tree and an orchid is an example of commensalisms, where one species gets benefited while the other remains unaffected. In the above interaction, the orchid is benefited as it gets support while the mango tree remains unaffected.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Commensalism',NULL,'2','mcq','Parasitism','Mutualism','Commensalism','Amensalism','NEW','12-236','12-201',NULL),
(351802,'biology','Barnacles growing on the back of a whale is an example for?','Commensalism','Commensalism is the interaction in which one species benefits and the other is neither harmed nor benefited. An orchid growing as an epiphyte on a mango branch, and barnacles growing on the back of a whale benefit while neither the mango tree nor the whale derives any apparent benefit. The cattle egret and grazing cattle in close association, a sight you are most likely to catch if you live in farmed rural areas, is a classic example of commensalism.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Commensalism',NULL,'2','mcq','Commensalism','Mutualism','Amensalism','Parasitism','NEW','12-236','12-201',NULL),
(351803,'biology','What is a classic example of commensalism?','The cattle egret and grazing cattle','The cattle egret and grazing cattle in a close association is a classic example of commensalism. The egrets always feed close to where the cattle are grazing, As the cattle move, they stir up and flush out insects from the vegetation that might be difficult for the egrets to find and catch otherwise.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:236, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Commensalism',NULL,'2','mcq','Sea anemone and the clown fish','The cattle egret and grazing cattle','Mango branch and orchid','Barnacles growing on the back of whale','NEW','12-236','12-201',NULL),
(351804,'biology','The sea anemone does not appear to derive any benefit by hosting the clown fish. Given statement is true or false:','TRUE','Commensalism is the interaction between sea anemone that has stinging tentacles and the clown fish that lives among them. The fish gets protection from predators which stay away from the stinging tentacles. The anemone does not appear to derive any benefit by hosting the clown fish.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Commensalism',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-236','12-201',NULL),
(351805,'biology','The clown fish attracts the predators with sea anemone stinging tentacles.Given statement is true or false?','FALSE',NULL,'Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Commensalism',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE','Commensalism is the interaction between sea anemone that has stinging tentacles and the clown fish that lives among them. The fish gets protection from predators which stay away from the stinging tentacles. The anemone does not appear to derive any benefit by hosting the clown fish.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:1)',NULL,'NEW','12-236','12-201',NULL),
(351901,'biology','Lichens show the symbiotic association between?','Algae and fungi','Lichens represent an intimate mutualistic relationship between a fungus and photosynthesising algae or cyanobacteria. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','Fungi and bacteria','Bacteria and virus','Algae and fungi','Fungi and roots of higher plants','NEW','12-237','12-201',NULL),
(351902,'biology','The mycorrhizae are associations between?','Fungi and roots of higher plants','The mycorrhizae are associations between fungi and the roots of higher plants. The fungi help the plant in the absorption of essential nutrients from the soil while the plant in turn provides the fungi with energy-yielding carbohydrates.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','Bacteria and virus','Fungi and bacteria','Fungi and roots of higher plants','Algae and fungi','NEW','12-237','12-201',NULL),
(351903,'biology','The type of interactions in mycorrhizae is?','Mutualism','The fungi help the plant in the absorption of essential nutrients from the soil while the plant in turn provides the fungi with energy-yielding carbohydrates. It is an example of mutualism where both the organisms are benefited mutually. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','Commensalism','Mutualism','Amensalism','Parasitism','NEW','12-237','12-201',NULL),
(351904,'biology','The most spectacular and evolutionarily fascinating examples of mutualism are found in _____________?','Plant - animal relationships','The most spectacular and evolutionarily fascinating examples of mutualism are found in plant-animal relationships. Plants need the help of animals for pollinating their flowers and dispersing their seeds. Animals obviously have to be paid ‘fees’ for the services that plants expect from them. Plants offer rewards or fees in the form of pollen and nectar for pollinators and juicy and nutritious fruits for seed dispersers. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','Sea anemone and the clown fish relationship','Barnacle and whale relationships','Plant - animal relationships','All the above','NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(351905,'biology','Which among the following is Incorrect?\n \n i) Plants need the help of animals for pollinating their flowers and dispersing their seeds.\n \n ii) Plants offer rewards or fees by acting as pollinators.\n \n iii) Animals obviously have to be paid ‘fees’ for the services that plants expect from them.\n \n iv) Plants offer rewards or fees in the form of pollen and nectar for pollinators and juicy and nutritious fruits for seed dispersers.','ii','The most spectacular and evolutionarily fascinating examples of mutualism are found in plant-animal relationships. Plants need the help of animals for pollinating their flowers and dispersing their seeds. Animals obviously have to be paid ‘fees’ for the services that plants expect from them. Plants offer rewards or fees in the form of pollen and nectar for pollinators and juicy and nutritious fruits for seed dispersers. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','i','ii','iiii','iv','NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(351906,'biology','In many species of fig trees, there is a tight one-to-one relationship with the pollinator species of wasp. Given statement is true or false?','TRUE','In many species of fig trees, there is a tight one-to-one relationship with the pollinator species of wasp. It means that a given fig species can be pollinated only by its ‘partner’ wasp species and no other species. The female wasp uses the fruit not only as an oviposition (egg-laying) site but uses the developing seeds within the fruit for nourishing its larvae. The wasp pollinates the fig inflorescence while searching for suitable egg-laying sites. In return for the favour of pollination the fig offers the wasp some of its developing seeds, as food for the developing wasp larvae. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(351907,'biology','The plant-animal interactions often involve co-evolution of the mutualists so that','all the above','Plant-animal interactions involve co-evolution of mutualists.This means,evolution of plant i.e flower and the animal i.e pollinator species are closely linked with one another. \n\nA given plant species can only be pollinated by the partner animal species,the animal can utilise plant not only for ovipositions but also for pollination and the mutual beneficial system could be safeguarded against cheaters.\n\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','a given plant species can only be pollinated by the partner animal species','the animal can utilise plant not only for ovipositions but also for pollination','the mutual beneficial system could be safeguarded against cheaters.','all the above','NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(351908,'biology','The wasp pollinates the fig inflorescence while searching for suitable egg-laying sites. Given statement is true or false?','TRUE','In many species of fig trees, there is a tight one-to-one relationship with the pollinator species of wasp (Figure 13.7). It means that a given fig species can be pollinated only by its ‘partner’ wasp species and no other species. The female wasp uses the fruit not only as an oviposition (egg-laying) site but uses the developing seeds within the fruit for nourishing its larvae. The wasp pollinates the fig inflorescence while searching for suitable egg-laying sites. In return for the favour of pollination the fig offers the wasp some of its developing seeds, as food for the developing wasp larvae. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(351909,'biology','Orchids show a bewildering diversity of floral pattern and attract the pollinating agent which is/are _______\n \n i) Bees\n \n ii) Bumblebees\n \n iii) Bats','i and ii','Orchids show a bewildering diversity of floral patterns many of which have evolved to attract the right pollinator insect (bees and bumblebees) and ensure guaranteed pollination by it. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:238, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iii','NEW','12-238','12-202',NULL),
(41425,'chemistry','The molecular geometry of AB₃E₂ type molecules:','T-shaped','In (a) the lp are at equatorial position so there are less lp-bp repulsions as compared to others in which the lp are at axial positions. \n\nSo structure (a) is most stable. (T-shaped). \n\nAs shown in diagram\n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 117; Table 4.8) diagram(ne0413)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'2','mcq','T-shaped','Trigonal pyramidal','See-saw','Bent','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(351910,'biology','Which among the following is correct?\n \n i) The female wasp uses the fruit not only as an oviposition (egg-laying) site but uses the developing seeds within the fruit for nourishing its larvae\n \n ii) The wasp pollinates the fig inflorescence while searching for suitable egg-laying sites\n \n iii) In return for the favour of pollination the fig offers the wasp some of its developing seeds, as food for the developing wasp larvae\n \n iv) The female wasp uses the fruit only as an oviposition (egg-laying) site but does not uses the developing seeds within the fruit for nourishing its larvae','i, ii, and iii','In many species of fig trees, there is a tight one-to-one relationship with the pollinator species of wasp (Figure 13.7). It means that a given fig species can be pollinated only by its ‘partner’ wasp species and no other species. The female wasp uses the fruit not only as an oviposition (egg-laying) site but uses the developing seeds within the fruit for nourishing its larvae. The wasp pollinates the fig inflorescence while searching for suitable egg-laying sites. In return for the favour of pollination the fig offers the wasp some of its developing seeds, as food for the developing wasp larvae. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','i, ii, and iii','i and iv','ii and iv','i and iii','NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(351911,'biology','The male bee is attracted to what it perceives as a female(one petal of orchid),_________ with the flower, and during that process is dusted with pollen from the flower','Pseudocopulates','The Mediterranean orchid Ophrys employs ‘sexual deceit’ to get pollination done by a species of bee. One petal of its flower bears an uncanny resemblance to the female of the bee in size, colour and markings. The male bee is attracted to what it perceives as a female, ‘pseudocopulates’ with the flower, and during that process is dusted with pollen from the flower. When this same bee ‘pseudocopulates’ with another flower, it transfers pollen to it and thus, pollinates the flower. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:238, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','fillup','Ps,eu,do,co,pu,la,te,s',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-238','12-202',NULL),
(351912,'biology','The Mediterranean orchid Ophrys employs','Sexual deceit','The Mediterranean orchid Ophrys employs ‘sexual deceit’ to get pollination done by a species of bee. One petal of its flower bears an uncanny resemblance to the female of the bee in size, colour and markings. The male bee is attracted to what it perceives as a female, ‘pseudocopulates’ with the flower, and during that process is dusted with pollen from the flower. When this same bee ‘pseudocopulates’ with another flower, it transfers pollen to it and thus, pollinates the flower. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:238, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','Psuedocopulation','Sexual deceit','Camouflage','Diapause','NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(351913,'biology','If the female bee’s colour patterns change even slightly for any reason during evolution, pollination success will be reduced unless the orchid flower co-evolves to maintain the resemblance of its petal to the female bee. Given statement is true or false?','TRUE','If the female bee’s colour patterns change even slightly for any reason during evolution, pollination success will be reduced unless the orchid flower co-evolves to maintain the resemblance of its petal to the female bee.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:238, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Mutualism',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(352001,'biology','Niche is [NEET 2018]','The functional role played by the organism where it lives.','Each organism has an invariably defined range of conditions that it can tolerate, diversity in the resources it utilises and a distinct functional role in the ecological system, all these together comprise its niche. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 221, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','All the biological factors in the organism’s environment.','The physical space where an organism lives.','The range of temperature that the organism needs to live.','The functional role played by the organism where it lives.','NEW','12-221',NULL,NULL),
(352002,'biology','Pneumatophores occur in [NEET 2018]','Halophytes','Pneumatophores are breathing or respiratory roots which are found in plants growing in mangroves or water logged soil or saline swamps. Such plants are called halophytes.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Halophytes','Free-floating hydrophytes','Carnivorous plants','Submerged hydrophytes.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352003,'biology','Plants which produce characteristic pneumatophores and show vivipary belong to [NEET 2017]','Halophytes','Presence of pneumatophores, i.e., small negatively geotropic vertical roots and vivipary or seed germination while attached to plant are adaptations of halophytes, i.e., plants growing in saline habitat.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Halophytes','Psammophytes','Hydrophytes','Tropical savannah.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352004,'biology','Presence of plants arranged into well defined vertical layers depending on their height can be seen best in [NEET 2017]','Tropical rainforest','Presence of plants arranged into well defined vertical layers depending on their height can be seen best in Tropical rainforest','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Tropical rainforest','Grassland','Temperate forest','Tropical savannah.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352005,'biology','It is much easier for a small animal to run uphill than for a large animal, because [NEET-I 2016]','Smaller animals have a higher metabolic rate.','The small animals have higher metabolic rates than larger animals. Therefore, it is much easier for small animals to move uphill than large animals.\n \nSmall animals have a larger surface area relative to their volume(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Small animals have a lower O₂ requirement.','The efficiency of muscles in large animals is less than in the small animals.','It is easier to carry a small body weight.','Smaller animals have a higher metabolic rate.','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(352006,'biology','An association of individuals of different species living in the same habitat and having functional interactions is [NEET 2015]','Biotic community.','Biological or biotic community is an assemblage of populations of different species of plants, animals, bacteria and fungi which live in a particular area and interact with one another through competition, predation, mutualism, etc. Each biotic community has a specific composition and structure, e.g., pond community.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Ecosystem','Population','Ecological niche','Biotic community.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352007,'biology','Most animals are tree dwellers in a [NEET 2015 Cancelled]','Tropical rainforest','Tropical rainforests have a very dense plant cover. They also experience a large amount of precipitation, thus the forest floor is always damp. Thus, the conditions there have led animals to get adapted to arboreal habitats. Most animals found there are tree dwellers as almost every space on the forest floor is occupied by the vegetation.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Temperate deciduous forest','Tropical rainforest','Coniferous forest','Thorn woodland.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352008,'biology','Just as a person moving from Delhi to Shimla to escape the heat for the duration of hot summer, thousands of migratory birds from Siberia and other extremely cold northern regions move to [NEET 2014]','Keoladeo National Park.','Migrate: The organism can move away temporarily from the stressful habitat to a more hospitable area and return when stressful period is over. In human analogy, this strategy is like a person moving from Delhi to Shimla for the duration of summer. Many animals, particularly birds, during winter undertake long distance migrations to more hospitable areas. Every winter the famous Keolado National Park (Bharatpur) in Rajasthan host thousands of migratory birds coming from Siberia and other extremely cold northern regions. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:225, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Western Ghat','Meghalaya','Corbett National Park','Keoladeo National Park.','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(352009,'biology','Which of the following is not an attribute of a population? [NEET 2020]','Species interaction','A population has certain attributes whereas, an individual organism does not. An individual may have births and deaths, but a population has birth rates and death rates. In a population these rates refer to per capita births and deaths. The rates, hence, expressed are change in numbers (increase or decrease) with respect to members of the population. Here is an example. If in a pond there were 20 lotus plants last year and through reproduction 8 new plants are added, taking the current population to 28, we calculate the birth rate as 8/20 = 0.4 offspring per lotus per year. If 4 individuals in a laboratory population of 40 fruitflies died during a specified time interval, say a week, the death rate in the population during that period is 4/40 = 0.1 individuals per fruitfly per week.\nAnother attribute characteristic of a population is sex ratio. An individual is either a male or a female but a population has a sex ratio (e.g., 60 % of the population are females and 40 % males).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:2,3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Sex ratio','Natality','Mortality','Species interaction','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(352010,'biology','Carnivorous animals lions and leopards, occupy the same niche but lions predate mostly larger animals and leopards take smaller ones. This mechanism of competition is referred to as [Odissa NEET 2019]','Resource partitioning','One such mechanism is ‘resource partitioning’. If two species compete for the same resource, they could avoid competition by choosing, for instance, different times for feeding or different foraging patterns. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Character displacement','Altruism','Resource partitioning','Competitive exclusion.','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(352011,'biology','Between which among the following, the relationship is not an example of commensalism? [Odisha NEET 2019]','Female wasp and fig species.','Female wasp and fig species shows example of mutualism. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Orchid and the tree on which it grows.','Cattle Egret and grazing cattle.','Sea Anemone and Clown fish.','Female wasp and fig species.','NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(352012,'biology','Natality refers to [NEET 2018]','Birth rate','Natality refers to the number of births during a given period in the population that are added to the initial density. Death rate or mortality is the number of deaths in the population during a given time period. Immigration is the number of individuals entering a habitat. Emigration is the number of individuals of the population that left the habitat and had gone elsewhere during a time period.  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:228, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Death rate','Birth rate','Number of individuals leaving the habitat','Number of individuals entering a habitat.','NEW','12-228','12-193',NULL),
(352013,'biology','Which one of the following plants shows a very close relationship with a species of moth, where none of the two can complete its life cycle without the other? [NEET 2018]','Yucca','Yucca has an obligate mutualism with a species of moth, i.e., Pronuba. The only pollinator of this plant is the Yucca moth. The Yucca moth is likewise dependent upon the Yucca plant as Yucca moth caterpillars need to eat Yucca seed only or starve.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrilla','Yucca','Banana','Viola.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352014,'biology','In a growing population of a country, [NEET 2018]','Pre-reproductive individuals are more than the reproductive individuals.','In a population where the number of pre-reproductive individuals or the younger individual is larger than the reproductive individuals, the population will increase.\n\nIf the age distribution (% individuals of a given age or age group) is plotted for the population, the resulting structure is called an age pyramid. For human population, the age pyramids generally show age distribution of males and females in a diagram . The shape of the pyramids reflects the growth status of the population whether it is growing, it is triangular, stable population is bell shaped. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 227, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Pre-reproductive individuals are more than the reproductive individuals.','Reproductive individuals are less than the post-reproductive individuals.','Reproductive and pre-reproductive individuals are equal in number.','Pre-reproductive individuals are less than the reproductive individuals.','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(352101,'biology','Which one of the following population interactions is widely used in medical science for the production of antibiotics? [NEET 2018]','Amensalism','Amensalism is a relationship between organisms of different species in which an organism does not allow other organism to grow or live near it. Inhibition is achieved through the secretion of chemicals called allochemics. Antibiotics are a kind of allochemics produced by some microbes which in small concentration can kill or retard growth of harmful microbes without adversely affecting the host.\n\n"In amensalism on the other hand one species is harmed whereas the other is unaffected. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 233, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Commensalism','Mutualism','Parasitism','Amensalism','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(352102,'biology','Asymptote in a logistic growth curve is obtained when [NEET 2017]','K = N','Asymptote in a logistic growth curve is obtained when carrying capacity (K) reaches the population density (N), i.e., K = N.\n\n"A population growing in a habitat with limited resources show initially a lag phase, followed by phases of acceleration and deceleration and finally an asymptote, when the population density reaches the carrying capacity. A plot of N in relation to time (t) results in a sigmoid curve. This type of population growth is called Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 231, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','K = N','K > N','K < N','The value of ‘r’ approaches zero.','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(352103,'biology','Mycorrhizae are the example of [NEET 2017]','Mutualism','Mycorrhiza is a mutualistic interaction between a fungus and roots of higher plants. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Amensalism','Antibiosis','Mutualism','Fungistasis.','NEW','12-237','12-201',NULL),
(352104,'biology','Which of the following is correct for r-selected species? [NEET-II 2016]','Large number of progeny with small size.','Organisms that are r-selected (r-strategists) able to colonise a habitat rapidly, utilising the food and other resources before other organisms are established and begin to compete. The r-strategists tend to be relatively small organisms with short life spans (e.g., bacteria) and often live in temporary or unstable environments; characteristically their survival depends on their ability to produce large numbers of offspring rather than on their ability to compete.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Large number of progeny with small size.','Large number of progeny with large size','Small number of progeny with small size.','Small number of progeny with large size','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352105,'biology','If ‘+’ sign is assigned to beneficial interaction, ‘–’ sign to detrimental and ‘O’ sign to neutral interaction, then the population interaction represented by ‘+’ ‘–’ refers to [NEET-II 2016]','Parasitism.','Parasitism is an association in which one organism (the parasite) lives on (ectoparasitism) or in (endoparasitism) the body of another organism (host), from which it obtains its nutrients. This association is beneficial for the parasites as they get continuous supply of nutrients from their host and are able to rapidly multiply their numbers. But it is detrimental for the host organism as parasitic infection leads to various complications and diseases in the host body may also be fatal to the host under certain circumstances. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Mutualism','Amensalism','Commensalism','Parasitism.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352106,'biology','The principle of competitive exclusion was stated by [NEET-II 2016]','G.F. Gause','Gause’s ‘Competitive Exclusion Principle’ states that two closely related species competing for the same resources cannot co-exist indefinitely and the competitively inferior one will be eliminated eventually. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: paragraph no: 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','C. Darwin','G.F. Gause','Mac Arthur','Verhulst and Pearl.','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(352107,'biology','When does the growth rate of a population following the logistic model equal zero? The logistic model is given as dN/dt = rN(1–N/K) [NEET-I 2016]','When N/K is exactly one.','The equation given for Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth is dN/dt =rN [K - N / K].(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','When N/K equals zero.','When death rate is greater than birth rate.','When N/K is exactly one.','When N nears the carrying capacity of the habitat.','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(352108,'biology','Gause’s principle of competitive exclusion states that [NEET-I 2016]','No two species can occupy the same niche indefinitely for the same limiting resources.','Two or more species with closely similar niche requirements cannot exist indefinitely in the same area as sooner or later they come into competition for possession of it. This is called as Gause’s principle of competitive exclusion, which states that an ecological niche cannot be simultaneously and completely occupied by established populations of more than one species. Two species can live in same habitat but not in the same niche. More similar the two niches are, severe the competition is.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','No two species can occupy the same niche indefinitely for the same limiting resources.','Larger organisms exclude smaller ones through competition.','More abundant species will exclude the less abundant species through competition.','Competition for the same resources exclude species having different food pReference  s.','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(352109,'biology','In which of the following interactions both partners are adversely affected? [NEET 2015]','Competition','Competition is the rivalry between two or more organisms for obtaining the same resources such as food, light, water, space, shelter, mate, etc. Competitors adversely affect each other. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Parasitism','Mutualism','Competition','Predation.','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(352110,'biology','The following graph depicts changes in two populations ie. A and B  of herbivores in a grassy field. A possible reason for these changes is that [NEET 2015 Cancelled] diagram (3505)','Population B competed more successfully for food than population A.','Both the populations are herbivorous, thus they cannot affect each other. If the food sources for these populations A and B have decreased, then both the populations A and B would have declined. If population A have produced more offspring then the graph A should have increased. Based on the graph, population B is more successful in competing with population A, that is why number of organisms in population B increased while that in population A decreased, as they get access to limited resources.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Population A produced more offspring than population B.','Population A consumed the members of population B.','Both plant populations in this habitat decreased.','Population B competed more successfully for food than population A.','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/352110q.png'),
(352111,'biology','According to Darwin, the organic evolution is due to [NEET 2013]','Interspecific competition','According to Darwin, the organic evolution is due to interspecific competition between species.\nInterspecific competition is a form of competition in which individuals of different species compete for the same resource (food and shelter) in an ecosystem.\nFor example, in the ocean, fish is the command prey of dolphins, sharks, and other sea animals.\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:234, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Competition within closely related species.','Reduced feeding efficiency in one species due to the presence of interfering species.','Intraspecific competition','Interspecific competition','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(352112,'biology','Besides paddy fields, cyanobacteria are also found inside vegetative part of [NEET 2013]','Cycas.','Coralloid roots of Cycas have symbiotic association with blue-green algae like Nostoc and Anabaena. Coralloid roots are irregular, negatively geotropic, dichotomously branched coral like roots which do not possess root hairs and root caps.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Equisetum','Psilotum','Pinus','Cycas.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352113,'biology','A sedentary sea anemone gets attached to the shell lining of hermit crab. The association is [NEET 2013]','Commensalism','A sedentary sea anemone gets attached to the lining of the hermit crab is a type of interaction in which one species is benefitted while the other is neither harmed nor benefited.  Such a type of relations in ecology is called commensalism. Ectoparasitism is a form of parasitism where the parasite (ectoparasite) lives outside the body of the host. Amensalism is a type of species interaction where one organism is affected but another organism gets harmed. So, the correct answer is \'Option C- Commensalism\'.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:237, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Commensalism','Amensalism','Ectoparasitism','Symbiosis.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352114,'biology','A biologist studied the population of rats in a barn. He found that the average natality was 250, average mortality 240, immigration 20 and emigration 30. The net increase in population is [NEET 2013]','Zero','Natality and immigration positively contribute to the population growth while mortality and emigration are negative factors. In the given question, The net increase in population is natality + immigration = 250 + 20 = 270 The net decrease in population is mortality + emigration = 240 + 30 = 270Thus, net increase in population = 270 – 270 = 0','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','5','Zero','10','15','NEW','12-229','12-193',NULL),
(352115,'biology','The age pyramid with broad base indicates [Karnataka NEET 2013]','High percentage of young individuals.','Age pyramid is a graphic representation of abundance of individuals of different age groups with pre-reproductive individuals at the base, reproductive individuals in the middle and post-reproductive individuals at the top. Triangular age pyramid has high proportion of pre-reproductive individuals, moderate number of reproductive individuals and fewer post-reproductive individuals. It represents young or rapidly growing population. In bell-shaped age pyramid, the number of pre-reproductive and reproductive individuals is almost equal. Post-reproductive individuals are comparatively fewer. It represents stable or stationary population where growth rate is nearly zero. In urn-shaped age pyramid, the number of reproductive individuals is higher than the number of pre-reproductive individuals. It represents declining or diminishing population.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','High percentage of old individuals.','Low percentage of young individuals.','A stable population','High percentage of young individuals.','NEW','12-227','12-192',NULL),
(352116,'biology','Inspite of interspecific competition in nature, which mechanism the competing species might have evolved for their survival? [NEET 2021]','Resource partitioning','Resource partitioning is the division of limited resources by species to help avoid competition in an ecological niche. In any environment, organisms compete for limited resources, so organisms and different species have to find ways to coexist with one another.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:235, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Predation','Resource partitioning','Competitive release','Mutualism','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(352117,'biology','Amensalism can be represented as [NEET 2021]','Species A (–); Species B (0)','Mutualism-(+) and (+),Competition-(-) and (-),Predation-(+) and (-),Predation-(+) and (-),Ammensalism (-) and (0),\n\n Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, Table: 13.1L Population Interaction','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Species A (+); Species B (0)','Species A (–); Species B (0)','Species A (+); Species B (+)','Species A (–); Species B (–)','NEW','12-232','12-197',NULL),
(352118,'biology','Match List-I with List - II according to the given diagram  [NEET 2021]  diagram (3504)','4','In the absence of an external source of water, the kangaroo rat in North American deserts is capable of meeting all its water requirements through its internal fat oxidation (in which water is a by product). It also has the ability to concentrate its urine so that minimal volume of water is used to remove excretory products. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 225, Last Para)\n\nIn the polar seas aquatic mammals like seals have a thick layer of fat (blubber) below their skin that acts as an insulator and reduces loss of body heat. \nMammals from colder climates generally have shorter ears and limbs to minimise heat loss. (This is called the Allen’s Rule.) (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 226, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-225',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/352118q.png'),
(352119,'biology','Assertion (A): A person goes to high altitude and experiences \'altitude sickness\' with symptoms like breathing difficulty and heart palpitations. \n\nReason (R): Due to low atmospheric pressure at high altitude, the body does not get sufficent oxygen. [NEET 2021]','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Some organisms possess adaptations that are physiological which allow them to respond quickly to a stressful situation. If you had ever been to any high altitude place (>3,500m Rohtang Pass near Manali and Leh you must have experienced what is called altitude sickness. Its symptoms include nausea, fatigue and heart palpitations. This is because in the low atmospheric pressure of high altitudes, the body does not get enough oxygen. But, gradually you get acclimatised and stop experiencing altitude sickness. How did your body solve this problem? The body compensates low O₂ availability by increasing red blood cell production, decreasing the binding affinity of hemoglobin and by increasing breathing rate.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:226, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(A) is false but (R) is true','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','NEW','12-226',NULL,NULL),
(352120,'biology','Which one of the following cannot be connected to Predation? [NEET 2022]','Both the interacting species are negatively impacted.','In both parasitism and predation only one species benefits (parasite and predator, respectively) and the interaction is detrimental to the other species (host and prey, respectively).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, paragraph no:4) Predation is the nature’s way of transferring to higher trophic levels the energy fixed by plants.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:233, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','It helps in maintaining species diversity in a community.','It might lead to extinction of species.','Both the interacting species are negatively impacted.','It is necessitated by nature to maintain the ecological balance.','NEW','12-232','12-197',NULL),
(41426,'chemistry','Which theory provides a simple procedure to predict the shapes of covalent molecules?','VSEPR','Lewis concept is unable to explain the shapes of molecules. \n\nThis theory provides a simple procedure to predict the shapes of covalent molecules. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 112; Paragraph 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> VSEPR theory',NULL,'1','mcq','VBT','MOT','VSEPR','Lewis','NEW','11-114','11-114',NULL),
(352121,'biology','While explaining interspecific interaction of population, + sign is assigned for beneficial interaction, - sign is assigned for detrimental interaction and 0 for neutral interaction. Which of the following interactions can be assigned \'+\' for one species and \'-\' for another species involved in the interaction? [NEET 2022]','Predation','Mutualism-(+) and (+),Competition-(-) and (-),Predation-(+) and (-),Predation-(+) and (-) Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:232, Table: 13.1L Population Interaction','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Predation','Amensalism','Commensalism','Competition','NEW','12-232','12-197',NULL),
(352122,'biology','Plants offer rewards to animals in the form of pollen and nectar and the animals facilitate the pollination process. This is an example of : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) Mutualism','Plants offers reward in the form of pollen and nectar for pollinators and juicy and nutritious fruits for seeds dispersers. This is a fascinating example of mutualism found in plant-animal relationships.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Commensalism','(2) Mutualism','(3) Amensalism','(4) Competition','NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(352123,'biology','Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason \n(R). \n Assertion (A) : \nA person goes to high altitude and experiences "Altitude Sickness" with symptoms like breathing difficulty and heart palpitations. \n Reason (R) : \nDue to low atmospheric pressure at high altitude, the body does not get sufficient oxygen. \n In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below : [NEET -2023(Phase 2)]','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct \nexplanation of (A).','This statement is true, at high altitudes air pressure and oxygen level both are low. As the body does not get sufficient oxygen, the person starts to have difficulty in breathing, and the heartbeat increases to meet the body\'s oxygen needs','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct \nexplanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(1) (A) is true but (R) is false','(2) (A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-236',NULL,NULL),
(352124,'biology','If there are 250 snails in a pond, and within a year their number increases to 2500 by reproduction. What should be their birth rate per snail per year ? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','9','NEET 2023(Phase 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','10','9','15','25','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352125,'biology','Match List I with List II.\nA. Logistic growth - I. Unlimited resource availability condition\nB. Exponential growth - II. Limited resource availability condition\nC. Expanding age pyramid - III. The percent individuals of pre-reproductive age is largest followed\nby reproductive and post reproductive age groups\nD. Stable age pyramid - IV.The percent individuals of pre-reproductives and reproductive age\ngroup are same\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(4) A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV','(4) A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV','(2) A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','(3) A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I','(4) A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352126,'biology','Benthic organisms are affected the most by\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','(c) sediment characteristics of aquatic ecosystems','Benthic organisms are those that live on or near the bottom of aquatic ecosystems. Therefore, they are most affected by sediment characteristics of aquatic ecosystems, such as particle size, texture, and composition. Sediment characteristics can affect factors such as nutrient availability, oxygen levels, and water flow, which in turn can influence the distribution and abundance of benthic organisms.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) light reaching the forest floor','(b) surface turbulence of water','(c) sediment characteristics of aquatic ecosystems','(d) water-holding capacity of soil.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352127,'biology','The figure given below is a diagrammatic\nrepresentation of response of organisms to\nabiotic factors. What do (i), (ii) and (iii) represent\nrespectively?','(d) Regulator Conformer Partial regulator','NCERT XII: 222','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) Conformer Regulator Partial regulator','(b) Regulator Partial Conformer\n regulator','(c) Partial Regulator Conformer\nregulator','(d) Regulator Conformer Partial regulator','NEW','12-223',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/352127q.png'),
(352128,'biology','Consider the following statements (A – D) each\nwith one or two blanks.\n(A) Bears go into (1) during winter to (2) cold\nweather.\n(B) A conical age pyramid with a broad base\nrepresents (3) human population.\n(C) A wasp pollinating a fig flower is an example of\n(4) .\n(D) An area with high levels of species richness is\nknown as (5) .\nWhich one of the following options, givesthe correct\nfill ups for the respective blank numbers from (1) to\n(5) in the statements ?','(d) (1) – hibernation, (2) – escape, (3) – expanding\n(5) – hotspot','NCERT XII: 225-227','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a) (3) – stable (4) – commensalism, (5)-marsh','(b) (1) – aestivation, (2) – escape, (3) – stable,\n(4) – mutualism','(c) (3) – expanding, (4) – commensalism,\n(5) – biodiversity park','(d) (1) – hibernation, (2) – escape, (3) – expanding\n(5) – hotspot','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(352129,'biology','Geometric representation of age structure is a\ncharacteristic of\n(2007)','(a) population','NCERT XII: 227','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) population','(b) landscape','(c) ecosystem','(d) biotic community','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(352130,'biology','The impact of immigration on population density is','Positive','NCERT XII: 228','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Negative','Both positive and negative','Neutralized by natality','Positive','NEW','12-228','12-193',NULL),
(352131,'biology','The formula for exponential population growth is:','dN/dt = rN','NCERT XII: 230','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','dt/dN=rN','dN/rN = dt','rN/dN = dt','dN/dt = rN','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(352132,'biology','Which of the following is the correct equation of exponential growth?','Nₜ = Nₒeʳᵗ','NCERT XII: 241','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Nₜ = Nₒeʳᵖ','Nₜ = Nₒeʳᵖᵗ','Nₜ = Nₒeʳˢᵗ','Nₜ = Nₒeʳᵗ','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(352133,'biology','In the exponential growth equation Nₜ = Nₒeʳᵗ, e represent','The base of natural logarithms','NCERT XII: 241','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','The base of natural logarithms','The base of geometric logarithms','The base of number lagarithms','The base of exponential logarithms','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(352134,'biology','The logistic population growth is expressed by the\nequation','dN/dt = rN(K-N/K)','NCERT XII: 231','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','dN/dt = rN(N-K/K)','dt/dN = Nr(K-N/K)','dN/dt = rN(K-N/K)','dN/dt = rN','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(352135,'biology','Study the four statements (i–iv) given below and\nselect the two correct ones out of them.\n(i) A lion eating a deer and a sparrow feeding\non grains are ecologically similar in being\nconsumers.\n(ii) Predator star fish Pisaster helps in maintaining\nspecies diversity of some invertebrates.\n(iii)Predators ultimately lead to the extinction of\nprey species.\n(iv) Production of chemicals such as nicotine,\nstrychnine by the plants are metabolic disorders.\nThe two correct statements are\n\n(2010)','(d) (i) and (ii)','NCERT XII: 234','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) (ii) and (iii)','(b) (iii) and (iv)','(c) (i) and (iv)','(d) (i) and (ii)','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(352136,'biology','Match the items \na. Herbivores - plants  – i. Commensalism\nb. Mycorrhiza - plants – ii. Mutualism\nc. Sheep - Cattle – iii. Predation\nd. Orchid - Tree – iv. Competition','iii,ii,iv, i','NCERT XII: 234,236,237','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','iv,ii,i,iii','iii,ii,iv, i','ii,i,iii,iv','i,iii,iv,ii','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(352137,'biology','Given below are two statements: \nStatement I: Gause\'s competitive Exclusion principle states that two closely related species competing for the same resource cannot co exist indefinitely and competitively inferior one will be eliminated eventually\nStatement II: In general, carnivores are more adversely affected by competition that herbivores','Statement I is correct but statement II is false','NCERT XII: 234','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Statement I is incorrect but statement II is true','Both statement I and II are true','Both statement I and II are false','Statement I is correct but statement II is false','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(352138,'biology','Two butterfly species are competing for the same nectar of a flower in a garden. To survive and co exist together, they may avoid competition in the same garden by','choosing different foraging patterns','NCERT XII: 235','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','feeding at the same time','choosing different foraging patterns','increasing time spent on attracking each other','predating on each other','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(352139,'biology','Which one of the following is not a parasitic\nadaptation?\n\n\n(Karnataka NEET 2013)','(c) Loss of reproductive capacity','NCERT XII: 236','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) Development of adhesive organs','(b) Loss of digestive organs','(c) Loss of reproductive capacity','(d) Loss of unnecessary sense organs','NEW','12-235','12-200',NULL),
(352140,'biology','Which one of the following is most appropriately\ndefined?\n\n (Mains 2010)','(c) Predator is an organism that catches and kills\nother organism for food.','NCERT XII: 233,236','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) Host is an organism which provides food to\nanother organism.','(b) Amensalism is a relationship in which one\nspecies is benefitted whereas the other is\nunaffected.','(c) Predator is an organism that catches and kills\nother organism for food.','(d) Parasite is an organism which alwayslivesinside\nthe body of other organism and may kill it.','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(352141,'biology','Which one of the following is categorised as a\nparasite in true sense?\n\n\n\n(2011)','(c) Head louse living on the human scalp as well as\nlaying eggs on human hair.','NCERT XII: 236','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) The female Anopheles bites and sucks blood\nfrom humans.','(b) Human fetus developing inside the uterus draws\nnourishment from the mother.','(c) Head louse living on the human scalp as well as\nlaying eggs on human hair.','(d) The cuckoo (koel) lays its eggs in crow’s nest.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352142,'biology','Which of the following is a correct pair','Cuscutta - parasite','NCERT XII: 236','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Cuscutta - parasite','Dischidia & insectivorous','Opuntia - predator','Capsella - hydrophyte','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(352143,'biology','Match list I with list II\nA. A leopard and a lion in a forest / Grassland - I. Compety\nB. A cuckoo laying an egg in a crows nest - II. Brood parasitism\nC. Fungi and root of a higher plant mycorrhizae - III. Mutualism \nD. A cattle egret and a cattle in a field - IV. Commensalism','IV-I-II-III','NCERT XII: 237','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','III-I-IV-II','IV-II-I-III','IV-I-II-III','IV-I-II-III','NEW','12-236','12-201',NULL),
(352144,'biology','A male insect mistakes an orchid flower to be flower to be female due to its shape and perform the act of copulation and induces pollination.This is an example of','Pseudocopulation','NCERT XII: 238','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Mimicry','Pseudocopulation','Pseudopollination','None','NEW','12-238','12-202',NULL),
(352201,'biology','Presence of flagellated protozoans in the gut of termites are the example [2013]','Symbiosis','Presence of flagellated protozoans in the gut of termites are the example of symbiosis. In this type of association both partners are mutally benefited from each other. Termites are dependent on protozoa for breaking down their food stuff and the protozoa are dependent on the termites as host organisms.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Aiims Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Symbiosis','Parasitism','Antibiosis','Commensalism','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352202,'biology','The formula for exponential population growth is [2014]','dN / dt = rN','The formula of exponential growth is dN/dt = rN where dN/dt is the rate of change in population size, r is the biotic potential and N is the population size.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:230, paragraph no:1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Aiims Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','dN/rN = dt','rN / dN = dt','dN / dt = rN','dt / dN = rN','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(352204,'biology','If 4 individuals in a laboratory population of 40 fruitflies died during a specified time interval (i.e., a week), the death rate in the population during that period is [2014]','0.1','Mortality or Death rate refers to the death of individuals in a population. Death rate = No. of deaths/Total population = 4/40 = 0.1 individuals per fruitfly per week.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:227, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Aiims Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','0.1','0.01','0.4','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(352205,'biology','A population of 500 that experiences 55 births and 5 deaths during a one-year period. What is the reproductive rate for the population during the one-year period ? [2015]','0.1/year','The rate of growth, r, equals (55 births – 5 deaths)/ 500 per year, or 0.1/year.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Aiims Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','0.01/year','0.05/year','0.1/year','50/year','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352206,'biology','The salinity in sea water in parts per thousand (ppt) ranges between [2016]','30-35%','Sea water typically has a salinity of around 35 g/kg although lower values are typical near coasts where river enter the ocean. Rivers and lakes can have a wide range of salinities, from less than 0-0.1 g/kg to a few g/kg, although there are many places where even higher salinities are found. The dead sea has a salinity of more than 200 g/kg.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:222, paragraph no:2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Aiims Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','5-15%','30-35%','50-75%','More than 100%','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(352207,'biology','In Urn shaped age pyramid of the population the trend of growth is [2017]','Declining','Figure 13.4 Representation of age pyramids for human population','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Aiims Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Rapid','Stable','Declining','Stationary','NEW','12-227','12-197',NULL),
(352208,'biology','Assertion : Thick cuticle is mostly present in disease resistant plants.\n\nReason : Disease causing agents cannot grow on cuticle and cannot invade the cuticle.[2012]','If both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Disease resistant plants possess thick cuticle. Infectious organisms can not grow or invade cuticle.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Aiims Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','If both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352209,'biology','Assertion : In sigmoid growth curve, population finally stabilizes itself. \n\nReason : Finally, the death rate increases than the birth rate. [2013]','If Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','In sigmoid growth curve, finally, growth rate becomes stable because mortality and natality rates become equal to each other and finally the population shows zero growth rate as birth rate equals death rate.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:231, paragraph no:3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Aiims Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','If both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-231','12-19',NULL),
(352210,'biology','Assertion : Tropical rain forests are disappearing fast from developing countries such as India. \n\nReason : No value is attached to these forests because these are poor in biodiversity. [2015]','If Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Tropical rain forests have disappeared mainly due to man’s activities. Due to over population in countries like India, rain forests are cut to make place available for man to live and build houses. To build buildings and factories man has incessantly cut down trees. This has caused the depletion of rain forests.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Aiims Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','If both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352212,'biology','Assertion : Species are groups of potentially interbreeding natural populations which are isolated from other such groups.\n\nReason : Distinctive morphological characters are displayed due to reproductive isolation. [2017]','If both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','A group of individuals resembling each other in morphological, physiological, biochemical and behavioural characters constitute a species. Such individuals can breed among themselves but cannot breed with members other than their own to produce fertile offsprings. New species are formed mainly due to reproductive isolation.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12: page no:226, paragraph no:5)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Previous Aiims Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','If both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352301,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Ramdeo Misra is revered as the Father of Ecology in India. \n \n Reason- Ramdeo Misra paved the way for the establishment of the Ministry of Environment and Forests (1984).','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Ramdeo Misra is revered as the Father of Ecology in India. Due to his efforts, the Government of India established the National Committee for Environmental Planning and Coordination (1972) which, in later years, paved the way for the establishment of the Ministry of Environment and Forests (1984). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 218. Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-218','12-189',NULL),
(352302,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Term Ecology is coined by Tansley. \n \n Reason- Term ecosystem was coined by Ernst Haeckel.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Term Ecosystem is coined by Tansley and Ecology coined by Haeckel.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352303,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Ecology is concerned with four levels of biological organisation. \n \n Reason-Ecology is a subject which studies the interactions among population and between the Population and its physical (abiotic) environment.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Ecology is basically concerned with four levels of biological organisation – organisms, populations, communities and biomes. Ecology is a subject which studies the interactions among organisms and between the organism and its physical (abiotic) environment. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 220, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241808,'biology','Assertion: The microspores, as they are formed, are arranged in a cluster called the microspore tetrad.\n\nReason: The cluster contains four cells.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The microspores, as they are formed, are arranged in a cluster of four cells–the microspore tetrad (Reference  : NCERT, page 22, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-022',NULL,NULL),
(352304,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Ecology at the organismic level is essentially physiological ecology. \n \n Reason- The Biological organisations in ecology are Organisms, Population, Species and Communities.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Ecology at the organismic level is essentially physiological ecology which tries to understand how different organisms are adapted to their environments in terms of not only survival but also reproduction. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 220, Para 2) Ecology is basically concerned with four levels of biological organisation – organisms, populations, communities and biomes. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 220, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-220',NULL,NULL),
(352305,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Precipitation includes both rain and snow. \n \n Reason- Major biomes include desert, rainforest and tundra.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Annual variation in precipitation (precipitation includes both rain and snow) account for the formation of major biomes such as desert, rain forest and tundra. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 220, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-220',NULL,NULL),
(352306,'biology','Consider these statements regarding habitats and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. On planet Earth, life exists just in a few favourable habitats. \n \n 2. Our intestine is a unique habitat for hundreds of species of microbes. \n \n 3. Regional and local variations within each biome lead to the formation of a wide variety of habitats. \n \n 4. Life can be found in the scorching Rajasthan desert, rain-soaked Meghalaya forests, deep ocean trenches, torrential streams.','Only 1 is incorrect','Regional and local variations within each biome lead to the formation of a wide variety of habitats. On planet Earth, life exists not just in a few favourable habitats but even in extreme and harsh habitats – scorching Rajasthan desert, rain-soaked Meghalaya forests, deep ocean trenches, torrential streams and Even our intestine is a unique habitat for hundreds of species of microbes. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 220, last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','NEW','12-220',NULL,NULL),
(352307,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- The habitat of an organism includes biotic components also. \n \n Reason- The physico-chemical components alone characterise the habitat of an organism completely.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The physico-chemical (abiotic) components alone do not characterise the habitat of an organism completely; the habitat includes biotic components also. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 221, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-221',NULL,NULL),
(352308,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Ecological Equivalents are organisms having a similar niche. \n \n Reason- Owl and Cats share similar habitats.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Organisms that occupy similar niches are called Ecological equivalents. Example- Owl and Cat both feed on mice and exhibit similar habitat.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352309,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Temperature is the most important ecologically relevant environmental factor. \n \n Reason- Temperature affects the kinetics of enzymes and through it the metabolic activity and other physiological functions of the organism.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Temperature is the most important ecologically relevant environmental factor. We can appreciate the significance of temperature to living organisms when we realise that it affects the kinetics of enzymes and through it the metabolic activity and other physiological functions of the organism. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 221-222, Last Para and Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-221',NULL,NULL),
(352310,'biology','Consider these statements regarding key elements and factors and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. The most important abiotic factors are temperature, water, light and air. \n \n 2. Biotic components include – pathogens, parasites, predators and competitors. \n \n 3. Organisms through mutation, have evolved adaptations. \n \n 4. Physico-chemical components are also called biotic factors.','Only 2 is correct','The abiotic factors are are temperature, water, light and soil. The habitat includes biotic components also – pathogens, parasites, predators and competitors. Organism had through natural selection, evolved adaptations. Physico-chemical are abiotic components. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 221, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 is correct','1, 2, and 3 are correct','Only 1 is correct','NEW','12-221',NULL,NULL),
(260232,'chemistry','Write the correct IUPAC NAME of the given compound in diagram?(n26003)','3-hydroxybutanal','The IUPAC name is based as per the IUPAC rules -OH group placed at 3rd position of the long chain butane and aldehyde at first end. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'3','mcq','3-hydroxybutanaldehyde','3-hydroxybutanal','2-hydroxybutanal','2-hydroxybutanol','NEW','12-359','12-229','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260232q.png'),
(352311,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Niche and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Each organism has an invariably defined range of conditions.\n \n 2. Niche includes distinct functional role in the ecological system. \n \n 3. Each organism has diversity in its resources. \n \n 4. Each organism can tolerate only defined range of conditions.','All the statements are correct','Each organism has an invariably defined range of conditions that it can tolerate, diversity in the resources it utilises and a distinct functional role in the ecological system, all these together comprise its niche. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 221, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is correct','1, 2, and 3 are correct','All the statements are correct','NEW','12-221',NULL,NULL),
(352312,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Temperature and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Average temperature on land varies seasonally. \n \n 2. Polar areas can have sub-zero temperature levels. \n \n 3. In unique habitats such as thermal springs and deep-sea hydrothermal vents average temperatures exceed 1000 degree Centigrade. \n \n 4. Temperature in tropicals can be greater than 50 degree centigrade.','Only 3 is incorrect','The average temperature on land varies seasonally, It ranges from subzero levels in polar areas and high altitudes to >500C in tropical deserts in summer. There are, however, unique habitats such as thermal springs and deep-sea hydrothermal vents where average temperatures exceed 100 degree Celsius. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 221, Last  Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 2 is correct','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','NEW','12-221',NULL,NULL),
(352313,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Most mammals and birds are eurythermal. \n \n Reason- Birds and animals are restricted to narrow range of temperature.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Most mammals and birds are eurythermal as they can tolerate and thrive in a wide range of temperatures. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(352314,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- A vast majority of organisms are restricted to a narrow range of temperatures.\n \n Reason- Polar bear and lizards are stenothermals.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','A vast majority of organisms for example polar bear and lizards are restricted to a narrow range of temperatures (such organisms are called stenothermal).\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(352315,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Many freshwater animals can live for long in sea water and vice versa. \n \n Reason- These animals would not face any osmotic problems.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Many freshwater animals cannot live for long in sea water and vice versa because of the osmotic problems, they would face. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(352316,'biology','Consider these statements regarding water and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Life on earth originated in water. \n \n 2. The productivity and distribution of plants is heavily dependent on water. \n \n 3. Water\'s availability is so limited in deserts that no special adaptations make it possible for organisms to live there. \n \n 4. For aquatic organisms, the quality of water is not so important.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Life on earth originated in water and is unsustainable without water. Its availability is so limited in deserts that only special adaptations make it possible for organisms to live there. The productivity and distribution of plants is also heavily dependent on water. Quality of water and its pH is important. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(352317,'biology','Consider these statements regarding salinity and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. The salt concentration is measured as salinity in parts per hundred. \n \n 2. Salinity is less than 10 in inland waters. \n \n 3. Salinity is 20-25 in the sea.\n \n 4. Salinity is > 1000 in some hypersaline lagoons.','1,2,3 and 4 are incorrect','The salt concentration (measured as salinity in parts per thousand), is less than 5 in inland waters, 30-35 in the sea and > 100 in some hypersaline lagoons. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','1,2,3 and 4 are incorrect','1,2 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(352318,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Euryhaline are restricted to a narrow range of salinity. \n \n Reason- Stenohaline are tolerant of a wide range of salinities.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Some organisms are tolerant of a wide range of salinities (euryhaline) but others are restricted to a narrow range (stenohaline). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(260235,'chemistry','Write the correct IUPAC NAME of the given compound in diagram?(n26006)','2-4-di-ethyl-pentan-3-one','There are two ethyl side chains attached at 2nd and 4th position of pentanone structure.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'3','mcq','2-2-di-ethyl-pentan-3-one','4--butyl-butanone','2-2-butyl-butanone','2-4-di-ethyl-pentan-3-one','NEW','12-359','12-229','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260235q.png'),
(352319,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Light is very important for living organisms, particularly autotrophs. \n \n Reason- Plants produce food through photosynthesis, a process which is only possible when sunlight is available as a source of energy.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Since plants produce food through photosynthesis, a process which is only possible when sunlight is available as a source of energy, we can quickly understand the importance of light for living organisms, particularly autotrophs. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(352320,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Many species of small plants (herbs and shrubs) growing in forests are adapted to photosynthesise optimally under very low light conditions. \n \n Reason- Sciophytes are constantly overshadowed by tall,canopied trees.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Many species of small plants (herbs and shrubs) growing in forests are adapted to photosynthesise optimally under very low light conditions because they (sciophytes) are constantly overshadowed by tall, canopied trees. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Last Para )','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(352321,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- The availability of light on land is closely linked with that of temperature. \n \n Reason- The sun is the source for both temperature and light.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The availability of light on land is closely linked with that of temperature since the sun is the source for both. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(352322,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Green algae is likely to be found in the deepest waters. \n \n Reason- Green light penetrates the most inside water.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Red algae are found in the deepest of the sea because blue-green light penetrates the most inside water and red algae absorbs it. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 223, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352323,'biology','Consider these statements regarding light and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Many plants are dependent on sunlight to meet their photoperiodic requirement for flowering. \n \n 2. Plants use diurnal and seasonal variations in light intensities for their foraging and reproductive activities.\n \n 3. Foraging, reproductive and migratory activities are executed by animals using light. \n \n 4. The UV component of the spectrum is useful to many organisms.','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Many plants are dependent on sunlight to meet their photoperiodic requirement for flowering. For many animals too, light is important in that they use the diurnal and seasonal variations in light intensity and duration (photoperiod) as cues for timing their foraging, reproductive and migratory activities. The UV component of the spectrum is harmful to many organisms. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 222, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 1 is correct','NEW','12-222',NULL,NULL),
(352324,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Eluviation is washing off of lowest strata of soil. \n \n Reason- The study of soil is called Petrology.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Eluviation is the washing off of upper strata of soil and study of soil is called pedology.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352325,'biology','Consider these statements regarding soil and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Soil composition, grain size and aggregation determine the percolation and water holding capacity of the soils. \n \n 2. Parameters such as pH, mineral composition and topography have a little role in vegetation in any area. \n \n 3. Soil is independent is of the climate and the weathering process.\n \n 4. Soil cannot dictate the type of animals that can be supported in it.','Statements 2, 3 and 4 are incorrect.','Soil is dependent on the climate, the weathering process, whether soil is transported or sedimentary and how soil development occurred. Various characteristics of the soil such as soil composition, grain size and aggregation determine the percolation and water holding capacity of the soils. These characteristics along wit parameters such as pH, mineral composition and topography determine to a large extent the vegetation in any area. This in turn dictates the type of animals that can be supported. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 223, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 4 is incorrect.','Both 2 and 4 are correct.','Statements 1, 2, 3 and 4 are incorrect.','Statements 2, 3 and 4 are incorrect.','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(31113,'chemistry','Electronegativity is:','A qualitative measure.','A qualitative measure of the ability of an atom in a chemical compound to attract shared electrons to itself is called electronegativity. \n\nUnlike ionization, enthalpy and electron gain enthalpy, it is not a measureable quantity. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; Paragraph no. 1,2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'2','mcq','A measurable quantity','Quantitative measure of the ability of an atom in a chemical compound to attract shared electrons to itself','A qualitative measure.','Always directly proportional to magnitude of negative electron gain enthalpy.','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(352326,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- During the course of millions of years of their existence, many species have evolved a relatively constant internal (within the body) environment that permits all biochemical reactions and physiological functions to proceed with maximal efficiency. \n \n Reason- A highly variable external environment bothers organisms.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','A highly variable external environment should bother organisms after all. One would expect that during the course of millions of years of their existence, many species would have evolved a relatively constant internal (within the body)\nenvironment that permits all biochemical reactions and physiological functions to proceed with maximal efficiency and thus, enhance the overall ‘fitness’ of the\nspecies. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 223, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(352327,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Homeostasis is the process to maintain the constancy of an organism\'s internal environment. \n \n Reason- Homeostasis could be in terms of optimal temperature and osmotic concentration of body fluids.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Ideally then, the organism should try to maintain the constancy of its internal environment (a process called homeostasis) despite varying external environmental conditions that tend to upset its homeostasis. This constancy, for example, could be in terms of optimal temperature and osmotic concentration of body fluids. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 223, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-223',NULL,NULL),
(352328,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Homeostasis by physiologically or sometimes emotionally ensures constant body temperature, constant osmotic concentration. \n \n Reason- All birds and mammals, and many lower vertebrates and invertebrate species are capable of regulation.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Some organisms are able to maintain homeostasis by physiological (sometimes behavioural also) means which ensures constant body temperature, constant osmotic concentration, etc. All birds and mammals, and a very few lower vertebrate and invertebrate species are indeed capable of such regulation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224 Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(352329,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Humans maintain a constant body temperature of 37 degree Fahrenheit\n \n Reason- Humans are conformers.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','The mechanisms used by most mammals to regulate their body temperature are similar to the ones that we humans use. We maintain a constant body temperature of 37 degree Celsius. Humans are regulators. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(352330,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Regulation and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. In summer, when outside temperature is more than the body temperature, humans sweat profusely. \n \n 2. In winters, humans start to shiver, a kind of exercise which produces heat and decreases the body temperature. \n \n 3. Plants have different mechanisms to maintain internal temperatures. \n \n 4. Evaporative cooling brings down the body temperature.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','The mechanisms used by most mammals to regulate their body temperature are similar to the ones that we humans use. We maintain a constant body temperature of 37 degree Celsius. In summer, when outside temperature is more than our body temperature, we sweat profusely. The resulting evaporative cooling, similar to what happens with a desert cooler in operation, brings down the body temperature. In winter when the temperature is much lower than 37 degree C, we start to shiver, a kind of exercise which produces heat and raises the body temperature. Plants, on the other hand, do not have such mechanisms to maintain internal temperatures. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','All are correct','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(352331,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- An overwhelming majority (99 %) of animals an nearly all plants are conformers. \n \n Reason- They cannot maintain a constant internal environment.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','An overwhelming majority (99 %) of animals and nearly all plants cannot maintain a constant internal environment. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(241817,'biology','Assertion: The first stage of the formation of the embryo sac results in a 2-nucleate embryo sac.\n\nReason: The nucleus of the functional megaspore divides meiotically to form two nuclei which move to the same pole, divide further to give four nuclei, then those two nuclei move to the center.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','The nucleus of the functional megaspore divides mitotically to form two nuclei which move to the opposite poles, forming the 2-nucleate embryo sac.  (Reference  : NCERT, page 27, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-027',NULL,NULL),
(352332,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- In aquatic animals, the osmotic concentration of the body fluid change with that of the ambient air,water osmotic concentration. \n \n Reason- In conformers, body temperature changes with the ambient temperature.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','In aquatic animals, the osmotic concentration of the body fluids change with that of the ambient air, water osmotic concentration. Their body temperature changes with the ambient temperature. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(352333,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- In conformers, body temperature changes with the ambient temperature.\n \n Reason- Thermoregulation is energetically expensive for lower organisms only.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Conformers had not evolved to become regulators. Thermoregulation is energetically expensive for many organisms. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(352334,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Small animals have a small surface area relative to their volume. \n \n Reason- Small animals tend to lose body heat very slowly when it is cold outside.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Since small animals have a larger surface area relative to their volume, they tend to lose body heat very fast when it is cold outside. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(352335,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Small animals are rarely found in polar regions. \n \n Reason- Small animals have to expend much energy to generate body heat through metabolism.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Very small animals are rarely found in polar regions since they have to expend much energy to generate body heat through metabolism. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(352336,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Shrew is an example of a partial regulator. \n \n Reason- Some species have evolved the ability to regulate, but only over a limited range of environmental conditions, beyond which they simply conform.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Shrews are partial regulators. Partial regulators have evolved the ability to regulate, but only over a limited range of environmental conditions, beyond which they simply conform. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(352337,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- During the course of evolution, the costs and benefits of maintaining a constant internal environment are not taken into consideration. \n \n Reason- If the stressful external conditions are localised or remain only for a short duration, the organism has no other alternatives for survival.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','During the course of evolution, the costs and benefits of maintaining a constant internal environment are taken into consideration. If the stressful external conditions are localised or remain only for a short duration, the organism has two other alternatives for\nsurvival. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 224, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-224',NULL,NULL),
(352338,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Every winter the famous Keolado National Park (Bharatpur) in Rajasthan hosts thousands of migratory birds. \n \n Reason- Migratory birds come from Nigeria and other extremely cold northern regions.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Every winter the famous Keoladeo National Park (Bharatpur) in Rajasthan host thousands of migratory birds coming from Siberia and other extremely cold\nnorthern regions. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 225, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(241818,'biology','Assertion: The synergids have special cellular thickenings at the micropylar tip.\n\nReason: These are present to guide the pollen tubes into the synergid.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The synergids have special cellular thickenings at the micropylar tip called filiform apparatus, which play an important role in guiding the pollen tubes into the synergid. (Reference  : NCERT, page 27, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-027',NULL,NULL),
(352339,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- In bacteria, fungi and lower plants, various kinds of thick- walled spores are formed which help them to survive favourable conditions. \n \n Reason- Higher plants increase their metabolic activity and go into a state of ‘dormancy’.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','In bacteria, fungi and lower plants, various kinds of thick- walled spores are formed which help them to survive unfavourable conditions – these germinate on availability of suitable environment. They do so by reducing their metabolic activity and going into a state of ‘dormancy’. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 225, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(352340,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Diapause is a stage of suspended development. \n \n Reason- Under unfavourable conditions many phytoplankton species in lakes and ponds are known to enter diapause.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Under unfavourable conditions many zooplankton species in lakes and ponds are known to enter diapause, a stage of suspended development. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 225, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(352341,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Bears go into hibernation during winter. \n \n Reason- In animals, the organism, if unable to migrate, might avoid the stress by escaping in time.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','In animals, the organism, if unable to migrate, might avoid the stress by escaping in time. The familiar case of bears going into hibernation during winter is an example of escape in time. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 225, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(352342,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Snails and fish go into aestivation. \n \n Reason- Aestivation occurs to avoid summer–related problems-heat and desiccation.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Some snails and fish go into aestivation to avoid summer–related problems-heat and dessication. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 225, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(352343,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Adaptation and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Adaptation is any attribute of the organism that enables the organism to survive and reproduce in its habitat. \n \n 2. Many adaptations have evolved over a long evolutionary time. \n \n 3. Adaptation can be morphological, physiological,behavioural. \n \n 4. Adaptations are not fixed genetically.','Only 4 is incorrect','Adaptation is any attribute of the organism (morphological, physiological, behavioural) that enables the organism to survive and reproduce in its habitat. Man adaptations have evolved over a long evolutionary time and are genetically fixed. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 225, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(352344,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Kangaroo rats and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. It is capable of meeting all its water requirements through its external fat oxidation.\n \n 2. It also has the ability to concentrate its urine. \n \n 3. In fat oxidation, water is a by-product. \n \n 4. It is found in the deserts of South America.','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','In the absence of an external source of water, the kangaroo rat in North American deserts is capable of meeting all its water requirements through its internal fat oxidation (in which water is a by product). It also has the ability to concentrate its urine so that minimal volume of water is used to remove excretory products. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 225, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','All the statements are correct','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(352345,'biology','Consider these statements regarding desert plants and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. They have a thick cuticle on their leaf surfaces. \n \n 2. Desert plants like Opuntia, have no stems. \n \n 3. Leaves are reduced to spines. \n \n 4. They also have a special photosynthetic pathway (CAM) that enables their stomata to remain closed during night time.','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Many desert plants have a thick cuticle on their leaf surfaces and have their stomata arranged in deep pits (sunken) to minimise water loss through transpiration. They also have a special photosynthetic pathway (CAM) that enables their stomata to remain closed during day time. Some desert plants like Opuntia, have no leaves – they are reduced to spines– and the photosynthetic function is taken over by the flattened stems. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 225, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect','NEW','12-225',NULL,NULL),
(352346,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Mammals from hotter climates generally have shorter ears and limbs to minimise heat loss. \n \n Reason- This is called Darwin\'s Rule.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Mammals from colder climates generally have shorter ears and limbs to minimise heat loss. (This is called the Allen’s Rule.) (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 226, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-226',NULL,NULL),
(352347,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- In the polar seas aquatic mammals like seals have a thick layer of fat (blubber) below their skin. \n \n Reason- Blubber acts as an insulator and increases loss of body heat.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','In the polar seas aquatic mammals like seals have a thick layer of fat (blubber) below their skin that acts as an insulator and reduces loss of body heat. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 226, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-226',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(352348,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Symptoms of altitude sickness include nausea, fatigue and heart palpitations. \n \n Reason- In the high atmospheric pressure of high altitudes, the body does not get enough oxygen.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Altitude sickness\'s symptoms include nausea, fatigue and heart palpitations. This is because in the low atmospheric pressure of high altitudes, the body does not get enough oxygen. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 226, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-226',NULL,NULL),
(352349,'biology','Consider these statements regarding altitude sickness and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. The body compensates low O₂ availability by decreasing red blood cell production. \n \n 2. The body compensates low O₂ availability by increasing the binding affinity of hemoglobin.\n \n 3. The body compensates low O₂ availability by increasing breathing rate. \n \n 4. Gradually you can get acclimatised and stop experiencing altitude sickness.','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Gradually you get acclimatised and stop experiencing altitude sickness. How did your body solve this problem? The body compensates low O₂ availability by increasing red blood cell production, decreasing the binding affinity of hemoglobin and by increasing breathing rate. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 226, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Only 3 is correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','12-226',NULL,NULL),
(352401,'biology','Consider these statements regarding symptoms of desert lizards and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Desert lizards have the physiological ability that mammals have to deal with the high temperatures of their habitat. \n \n 2. Their body temperature is fairly kept constant by behavioural means. \n \n 3. They move into shade when the ambient temperature starts decreasing. \n \n 4. They bask in the sun and absorb heat when their body temperature drops below the comfort zone.','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Desert lizards lack the physiological ability that mammals have to deal with the high temperatures of their habitat, but manage to keep their body temperature fairly constant by behavioural means. They bask in the sun and absorb heat when their body temperature drops below the comfort zone, but move into shade when the ambient temperature starts increasing. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 226, Para 5)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Only 2 is correct','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','All the statements are correct','NEW','12-226',NULL,NULL),
(352402,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- The term interbreeding implies sexual reproduction. \n \n Reason- A group of individuals resulting from asexual reproduction is not considered a population.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Although the term interbreeding implies sexual reproduction, a group of individuals resulting from even asexual reproduction is also generally considered a population for the purpose of ecological studies. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 226, Last Para )','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-226','12-191',NULL),
(352403,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Population ecology links ecology to population genetics and evolution. \n \n Reason- An individual organism is the one that has to cope with a changed environment, it is at the population level that natural selection operates to evolve the desired traits.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','An individual organism is the one that has to cope with a changed environment, it is at the population level that natural selection operates to evolve the desired traits. Population ecology is, therefore, an important area because it links ecology to population genetics and evolution. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 227, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(352404,'biology','Consider these statements regarding population attribute and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. An individual has births and deaths. \n \n 2. A population has birth rates and death rates. \n \n 3. Birth rate is the ratio of Population added to the final population. \n \n 4. Death rate is the ratio of Population added to the initial population.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','An individual may have births and deaths, but a population has birth rates and death rates. In a population these rates refer to per capita births and deaths. The rates, hence, expressed are change in numbers (increase or decrease) with respect to members of the population. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 227, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(31114,'chemistry','Electronegativity is ________ related to the metallic properties of elements.','Inversely','Electron gain enthalpies of related elements are as follows in the given diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 90; diagram(ne0311)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'1','mcq','Inversely','Directly','Not related to','Variable','NEW','11-090','11-090',NULL),
(352405,'biology','Consider these statements regarding age pyramid and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. For human population, the age pyramids generally show age distribution of males and females in the form of a diagram . \n \n 2. An expanding population has an \'U\' shape. \n \n 3. A stable population is triangular in shape. \n \n 4. If the age distribution is plotted for the population, the resulting structure is called an age pyramid.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','If the age distribution (% individuals of a given age or age group) is plotted for the population, the resulting structure is called an age pyramid. For human population, the age pyramids generally show age distribution of males and females in a diagram . The shape of the pyramids reflects the growth status of the population whether it is growing, it is triangular, stable population is bell shaped. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 227, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Only 4 is correct','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','12-227','12-191',NULL),
(352406,'biology','Consider these statements regarding population density and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Population density is designated as N \n \n 2. In some cases, the % cover or biomass is a more meaningful measure of the population size. \n \n 3. The size, in nature, could be as low as <5 (Siberian cranes at Bharatpur wetlands in any year) or go into millions (Chlamydomonas in a pond). \n \n 4. Population density need not necessarily be measured in numbers only.','Only 3 is incorrect','The size, in nature, could be as low as <10 (Siberian cranes at Bharatpur wetlands in any year) or go into millions (Chlamydomonas in a pond). Population size, technically called population density (designated as N), need not necessarily be measured in numbers only. Although total number is generally the most appropriate measure of population density, it is in some cases either meaningless or difficult to determine. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 228, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Only 4 is correct','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(352407,'biology','Consider these statements regarding relative density and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. For certain ecological investigations, there is no need to know the absolute population densities; relative densities serve the purpose equally well. \n \n 2. Population sizes cannot be measured indirectly without actually counting them or seeing them. \n \n 3. The number of fish caught per trap is good enough measure of its total population density in the lake. \n \n 4. The tiger census in our national parks and tiger reserves is often based on pug marks and fecal pellets.','Only 2 is incorrect','Sometimes, for certain ecological investigations, there is no need to know the absolute population densities; relative densities serve the purpose equally well. For instance, the number of fish caught per trap is good enough measure of its total population density in the lake. We are mostly obliged to estimate population sizes. Indirectly, without actually counting them or seeing them. The tiger census in our national parks and tiger reserves is often based on pug marks and fecal pellets. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 228, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is correct','Only 1 is correct','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(352408,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- The size of a population for any species is not a static parameter. \n \n Reason- Population size keeps changing with time, depending on various factors.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The size of a population for any species is not a static parameter. It keeps changing with time, depending on various factors including food availability, predation pressure and adverse weather. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 228, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(352409,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Natality and emigration contribute to an increase in population density. \n \n Reason- Mortality and immigration contribute to an decrease in population density.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','The density of a population in a given habitat during a given period, fluctuates due to changes in four basic processes, two of which (natality and immigration) contribute to an increase in population density and two (mortality and emigration) to a decrease. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 228, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-228','12-192',NULL),
(352410,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Population growth and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Mortality is the number of births in the population during a given period. \n \n 2. Immigration is the number of individuals of the same species that have come into the habitat from elsewhere during the time period under consideration. \n \n 3. Emigration is the number of individuals of the population who left the habitat and gone elsewhere during the time period under consideration. \n \n 4. Natality refers to the number of deaths during a given period in the population that are added to the initial density.','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Natality refers to the number of births during a given period in the population that are added to the initial density. Mortality is the number of deaths in the population during a given period. Immigration is the number of individuals of the same species that have come into the habitat from elsewhere during the time period under consideration. Emigration is the number of individuals of the population who left the habitat and gone elsewhere during the time period under consideration. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 228, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','12-228','12-193',NULL),
(51075,'biology','The hilum is a scar on the:','1. seed, where the funicle was attached','The hilum is a scar on the seed where the funicle, which is the stalk that attaches the seed to the placenta of the fruit or ovary, was attached. The funicle serves as a conduit for nutrients and water to reach the developing seed during seed development. Once the seed is mature and ready for dispersal, the funicle dries up and detaches from the seed, leaving behind the hilum as a scar.','Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','1. seed, where the funicle was attached','2. fruit, where it was attached to the pedicle','3. fruit, where style was present','4. seed, where micropyle was present','NEW','11-077',NULL,NULL),
(352411,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Population density will increase if the number of births plus the number of immigrants (B + I) is more than the number of deaths plus the number of emigrants (D + E).\n \n Reason- If N is the population density at time t, then its density at time t +1 is Nt+1 = Nt + [(B + I) – (D + E)].','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','if N is the population density at time t, then its density at time t +1 is Nt+1 = Nt + [(B + I) – (D + E)]. You can see from the above equation that population density will increase if the number of births plus the number of immigrants (B + I) is more than the number of deaths plus the number of emigrants (D + E). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 229, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-229','12-193',NULL),
(352412,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Under normal conditions, births and immigration are the most important factors influencing population density. \n \n Reason- If a new habitat is just being colonised, birth rate may contribute more significantly to population growth than immigration.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Under normal conditions, births and deaths are the most important factors influencing population density, the other two factors assuming importance only under special conditions. For instance, if a new habitat is just being colonised, immigration may contribute more significantly to population growth than birth rates. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 229, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-229','12-193',NULL),
(352413,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Exponential Growth and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Any species growing exponentially under unlimited resource conditions can reach enormous population densities in a short time. \n \n 2. Darwin observed Exponential growth while developing his theory of natural selection. \n \n 3. Exponential graph results in a J-shaped curve. \n \n 4. Geometrical Growth is shown by dN/dt = rN.','All the statements are correct','Any species growing exponentially under unlimited resource conditions can reach enormous population densities in a short time. When resources in the habitat are unlimited, each species has the ability to realise fully its innate potential to grow in number, as Darwin observed while developing his theory of natural selection. If in a population of size N, the birth rates (not total number but per capita births) are represented as b and death rates (again, per capita death rates) as d, then the increase or decrease in N during a unit time period t (dN/dt) will be dN/dt = (b – d) × N Let (b–d) = r, then dN/dt = rN. The above equation describes the exponential or geometric growth pattern of a population and results in a J-shaped curve. When we plot N in relation to time. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 230, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is Correct','Only 1 and 2 are incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are correct','All the statements are correct','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(352414,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Intrinsic rate and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. r is known as intrinsic rate of natural increase \n \n 2. It is also known as Biotic potential. \n \n 3. It is a very important parameter chosen for assessing impacts of only biotic factors on population growth. \n \n 4. For the Norway rat the r is 0.15, and for the flour beetle it is 0.012. \n \n 5. In 1981, the r value for the human population in India was 0.0205.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','r is intrinsic rate of natural increase, also called Biotic potential. It is a very important parameter chosen for assessing impacts of any biotic or abiotic factor on population growth. For the Norway rat the r is 0.015, and for the flour beetle it is 0.12. In 1981, the r value for human population in India was 0.0205. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 230, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1, 3 and 5 are correct','Both 3 and 5 are correct','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Only 1, 3 4, and 5 are correct.','NEW','12-230','12-194',NULL),
(352415,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Logistic Growth and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. No Population of any species in nature has at its disposal unlimited resources to permit exponential growth. \n \n 2. Only the ‘fittest’ individual will survive and reproduce. \n \n 3. In nature, a given habitat has enough resources to support a maximum possible number, beyond which no further growth is possible. It is called carrying capacity (K) for that species in that habitat. \n \n 4. The geometrical growth model is considered a more realistic one.','Only 4 is incorrect','No population of any species in nature has at its disposal unlimited resources to permit exponential growth. This leads to competition between individuals for limited resources. Eventually, the ‘fittest’ individual will survive and reproduce. In nature, a given habitat has enough resources to support a maximum possible number, beyond which no further growth is possible. Let us call this limit as nature’s carrying capacity (K) for that species in that habitat. Since resources for growth for most animal populations are finite and become limiting sooner or later, the logistic growth model is considered a more realistic one. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 231, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 4 is incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','All the options are incorrect','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(352416,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Logistic Growth and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. A population growing in a habitat with limited resources show initially a lag phase. \n \n 2. Lag phase is reached when the population density reaches the carrying capacity. \n \n 3. A plot of N in relation to time (t) results in a sigmoid curve. \n \n 4. This population growth is called Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth.','Only 2 is incorrect','A population growing in a habitat with limited resources show initially a lag phase, followed by phases of acceleration and deceleration and finally an asymptote, when the population density reaches the carrying capacity. A plot of N in relation to time (t) results in a sigmoid curve. This type of population growth is called Verhulst-Pearl Logistic Growth. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 231, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','All the options are correct','NEW','12-231','12-195',NULL),
(352417,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Populations evolve to maximise their reproductive fitness. \n \n Reason- Reproductive fitness is also called Darwinian fitness.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Populations evolve to maximise their reproductive fitness, also called Darwinian fitness (high r value), in the habitat in which they live. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 231, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-231','12-196',NULL),
(352418,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Organisms having more reproductive fitness tend to have higher r. \n \n Reason- Ecologists suggest that life history traits of organisms have evolved in relation to the constraints imposed by the abiotic and biotic components of the habitat in which they live.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Populations evolve to maximise their reproductive fitness, also called Darwinian fitness (high r value), in the habitat in which they live. Ecologists suggest that life history traits of organisms have evolved in relation to the constraints imposed by the abiotic and biotic components of the habitat in which they live. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 232, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(352419,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Bamboo plants breed once in their life time. \n \n Reason- Pacific salmon fish breed many times during their life.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Some organisms breed only once in their lifetime (Pacific salmon fish, bamboo). (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 232, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(352420,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- There is no such habitat where only one species is present and such a situation is even inconceivable. \n \n Reason- For any species, the minimal requirement is one more species on which it can feed.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','There is no such habitat and such a situation is even inconceivable. For any species, the minimal requirement is one more species on which it can feed. Even a plant species, which makes its own food, cannot survive alone; it needs soil microbes to break down the organic matter in soil and return the inorganic nutrients for absorption. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 232, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(352421,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- In nature, animals, plants and microbes can live in isolation and also interact in various ways to form a biological community. \n \n Reason- In minimal communities, many interactive linkages need not to be necessarily exist.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','In nature, animals, plants and microbes do not and cannot live in isolation but interact in various ways to form a biological community. Even in minimal communities, many interactive linkages exist, although all may not be readily apparent. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 232, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(352422,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Interspecific interactions arise from the interaction of populations within a species. \n \n Reason- In competition, only one species benefits.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Interspecific interactions arise from the interaction of populations of two different species. Both species do not get benefitted in competition. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 232, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-232','12-196',NULL),
(352423,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- In proto-coperation, both species can live equally without association too. \n \n Reason- Predation, parasitism and commensalism share a common characteristic– the interacting species live closely together.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Predation, parasitism and commensalism share a common characteristic– the interacting species live closely together. Protocooperation is a form of mutualism, but the cooperating species do not depend on each other for survival. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 232, Last Para )','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(51076,'biology','Family Fabaceae differs from Solanaceae and Liliaceae. With respect to the stamens, pick out the characteristics specific to family Fabaceae but not found in Solanaceae or Liliaceae.\n\n1.Epiphyllous and Dithecous anthers\n\n2.Diadelphous and Dithecous anthers\n3.Polyadelphous and epipetalous stamens\n\n4.Monoadelphous and Monothecous anthers','2',NULL,'Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-078',NULL,NULL),
(352424,'biology','Consider these statements regarding population interaction and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. In commensalism the one species is harmed whereas the other is unaffected. \n \n 2. The interaction where one species is benefitted and the other is neither benefitted nor harmed is called amensalism. \n \n 3. Both the species benefit in mutualism. \n \n 4. In both parasitism and predation only one species benefits.','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Both the species benefit in mutualism. In both parasitism and predation only one species benefits (parasite and predator, respectively) and the interaction is detrimental to the other species (host and prey, respectively). The interaction where one species is benefitted and the other is neither benefitted nor harmed is called commensalism. In amensalism on the other hand one species is harmed whereas the other is unaffected. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 233, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','All the statements are correct','NEW','12-232','12-197',NULL),
(352425,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Predation is nature’s way of transferring to higher trophic levels the energy fixed by plants. \n \n Reason- They act as ‘conduits’ for energy transfer across trophic levels.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','You can think of predation as nature’s way of transferring to higher trophic levels the energy fixed by plants. Besides acting as ‘conduits’ for energy transfer across trophic levels, predators play other important roles. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 233, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(352426,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- When certain exotic species are introduced into a geographical area, they become invasive. \n \n Reason- Prey species could achieve very high population densities and cause ecosystem instability.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','For predators, prey species could achieve very high population densities and cause ecosystem instability. When certain exotic species are introduced into a geographical area, they become invasive and start spreading fast because the invaded land does not have its natural predators. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 233, Para 3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-233','12-197',NULL),
(352427,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Predators increase the intensity of competition among competing prey species. \n \n Reason- Predators have very little role in maintaining species diversity in a community.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Predators help in maintaining species diversity in a community, by reducing the intensity of competition among competing prey species. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 233, Para 3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-233','12-198',NULL),
(352428,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Predators in nature are ‘prudent’. \n \n Reason- If a predator is too efficient and overexploits its prey, then the prey might become extinct and following it, the predator will also become extinct for lack of food.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If a predator is too efficient and overexploits its prey, then the prey might become extinct and following it, the predator will also become extinct for lack of food. This is the reason why predators in nature are ‘prudent’. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 233, Last Para )','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-233','12-198',NULL),
(352429,'biology','Consider these statements regarding predation and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. The prickly pear cactus introduced into Australia in the early 1930’s caused havoc. \n \n 2. The invasive cactus was brought under control only after a cactus-feeding predator was introduced. \n \n 3. Biological control methods adopted in agricultural pest control are based on the ability of the predator to regulate prey population.\n \n 4. Nearly 15 % of all insects are known to be phytophagous (feeding on plant sap and other parts of plants).','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Nearly 25 % of all insects are known to be phytophagous (feeding on plant sap and other parts of plants). The prickly pear cactus introduced into Australia in the early 1920’s caused havoc by spreading rapidly into millions of hectares of rangeland. Finally, the invasive cactus was brought under control only after a cactus-feeding predator (a moth) from its natural habitat was introduced into the country. Biological control methods adopted in agricultural pest control are based on the ability of the predator to regulate prey population. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 233, Last Para )','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','All the statements are correct','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(352430,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- The Monarch butterfly is highly tasteful to its predator because of a special chemical present in its wings. \n \n Reason- The butterfly acquires this chemical during its adult stage.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','The Monarch butterfly is highly distasteful to its predator (bird) because of a special chemical present in its body. Interestingly, the butterfly acquires this chemical during its caterpillar stage by feeding on a poisonous weed. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 234, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(352431,'biology','Consider these statements regarding predation and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. In the rocky intertidal communities of the African Pacific Coast the starfish Pisaster is an important predator. \n \n 2. In a field experiment, when all the starfish were removed from an enclosed intertidal area, more than 100 species of invertebrates became extinct. \n \n 3. Invertebrates became extinct within a year, because of intra-specific competition. \n \n 4. Some species of snakes and frogs are cryptically-coloured (camouflaged).','All the statements are incorrect','Some species of insects and frogs are cryptically-coloured (camouflaged). In the rocky intertidal communities of the American Pacific Coast the starfish Pisaster is an important predator. In a field experiment, when all the starfish were removed from an enclosed intertidal area, more than 10 species of invertebrates became extinct within a year, because of inter- specific competition. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 233, Para 3 and Para 4)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','All the statements are incorrect','NEW','12-233','12-198',NULL),
(352432,'biology','Consider these statements regarding predation and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Thorns  are the least common  means of defence, while chemical substances being the most common morphological means.\n \n 2. A wide variety of chemical substances that we extract from plants on a commercial scale (nicotine, caffeine, quinine, strychnine, opium, etc.,) are produced by them actually as defences against grazers and browsers. \n \n 3. Plant produces highly poisonous cardiac proteins and that is why you never see any cattle or goats browsing on this plant. \n \n 4. Many plants produce and store chemicals that make the herbivore sick when they are eaten, inhibit feeding or digestion, disrupt its reproduction or even kill it.','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Thorns (Acacia, Cactus) are the most common morphological means of defence. Many plants produce and store chemicals that make the herbivore sick when they are eaten, inhibit feeding or digestion, disrupt its reproduction or even kill it. Calotropis produces highly poisonous cardiac glycosides and that is why you never see any cattle or goats browsing on this plant. A wide variety of chemical substances that we extract from plants on a commercial scale (nicotine, caffeine, quinine, strychnine, opium, etc.,) are produced by them actually as defences against grazers and browsers. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 234, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','All the statements are incorrect','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(352433,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Competition occurs only when closely related species compete for the same resources that are limiting. \n \n Reason- Totally unrelated species cannot compete for the same resource.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','It is generally believed that competition occurs when closely related species compete for the same resources that are limiting, but this is not entirely true. Total unrelated species could also compete for the same resource. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 234, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(352501,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Resources need not be limiting for competition to occur.\n \n Reason- In interference competition, the feeding efficiency of one species might be reduced due to the interfering and inhibitory presence of the other species, even if resources (food and space) are abundant.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Resources need not be limiting for competition to occur; in interference competition, the feeding efficiency of one species might be reduced due to the interfering and inhibitory presence of the other species, even if resources (food and space) are abundant. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 234, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-234','12-199',NULL),
(352502,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Competition is the process in which the fitness of one species is significantly lower in the presence of another species. \n \n Reason- Fitness measured in terms of its ‘r’ is the intrinsic rate of increase.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Competition is best defined as a process in which the fitness of one species (measured in terms of its ‘r’ the intrinsic rate of increase) is significantly lower in the presence of another species. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 234, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-234','12-199',NULL),
(352503,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- A species whose distribution is restricted to a small geographical area because of the presence of a competitively superior species, is found to expand its distributional range dramatically when the competing species is experimentally removed. \n \n Reason- This is Gause’s ‘Competitive Exclusion Principle’.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','A species whose distribution is restricted to a small geographical area because of the presence of a competitively superior species, is found to expand its distributional range dramatically when the competing species is experimentally removed. This is called ‘competitive release’. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 235, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(51077,'biology','The viability of seeds is tested by:','2. 2, 3, 5 triphenyl tetrazolium chloride',NULL,'Structural Organisation In Plants And Animals >> Morphology Of Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','1. 2, 6 dichlorophenol indophenols','2. 2, 3, 5 triphenyl tetrazolium chloride','3. DMSO','4. Safranine','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352504,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Two closely related species competing for the same resources can co-exist but the competitively inferior one will be eliminated eventually. \n \n Reason- In general, carnivores  appear to be more adversely affected by competition than Herbivores.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','In general, herbivores and plants appear to be more adversely affected by competition than carnivores. Gause’s ‘Competitive Exclusion Principle’ states that two closely related species competing for the same resources cannot co-exist indefinitely and the competitively inferior one will be eliminated eventually. More recent studies do not support such gross generalisations about competition. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 235, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(352505,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- If two species compete for the same resource, they could avoid competition by choosing, for instance, different times for feeding or different foraging patterns. \n \n Reason- Species facing competition might evolve mechanisms that promote co-existence rather than exclusion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Species facing competition might evolve mechanisms that promote co-existence rather than exclusion. One such mechanism is ‘resource partitioning’. If two species compete for the same resource, they could avoid competition by choosing, for instance, different times for feeding or different foraging patterns. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 235, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-235','12-199',NULL),
(352506,'biology','Consider these statements regarding examples of competition and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. In some deep North American lakes, visiting flamingoes and resident fishes compete for their common food, the zooplankton in the lake. \n \n 2. The Abingdon tortoise in Galapagos Islands became extinct within a year after goats were introduced on the island, apparently due to the greater browsing efficiency of the goats. \n \n 3. Connell’s elegant field experiments showed that on the rocky sea coasts of Norway, the larger and competitively superior barnacle dominates, and excludes the smaller barnacle Chathamalus from that zone.\n \n 4. MacArthur showed that five closely related species of warblers living on the different trees were able to avoid competition and co-exist due to behavioural differences in their foraging activities.','All the statements are incorrect','In some shallow South American lakes, visiting flamingoes and resident fishes compete for their common food, the zooplankton in the lake. The Abingdon tortoise in Galapagos Islands became extinct within a decade after goats were introduced on the island, apparently due to the greater browsing efficiency of the goats. Connell’s elegant field experiments showed that on the rocky sea coasts of Scotland, the larger and competitively superior barnacle Balanus dominates the intertidal area, and excludes the smaller barnacle Chathamalus from that zone. MacArthur showed that five closely related species of warblers living on the same tree were able to avoid competition and co-exist due to behavioural differences in their foraging activities. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 234 Last Para, Page No. 235 Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1, 2, 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are incorrect','All the statements are incorrect','All the statements are correct','NEW','12-234','12-198',NULL),
(352507,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Many parasites have evolved to be host-specific. \n \n Reason- If the host evolves special mechanisms for rejecting or resisting the parasite, the parasite cannot counteract and neutralise them.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Many parasites have evolved to be host-specific (they can parasitise only a single species of host) in such a way that both host and the parasite tend to co-evolve; that is, if the host evolves special mechanisms for rejecting or resisting the parasite, the parasite has to evolve mechanisms to counteract and neutralise them, in order to be successful with the same host species. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 236, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-235','12-200',NULL),
(352508,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Parasites that feed on the external surface of the host organism are called ectoparasites. \n \n Reason- Lice on humans are endoparasites.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Parasites that feed on the external surface of the host organism are called ectoparasites. The most familiar examples of this group are the lice on humans and ticks on dogs. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 236, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(352509,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Cuscuta, a parasitic plant that is commonly found growing on hedge plants. \n \n Reason- It has chlorophyll and derives its nutrition from the host plant.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Cuscuta, a parasitic plant that is commonly found growing on hedge plants, has lost its chlorophyll and leaves in the course of evolution. It derives its nutrition from the host plant which it parasitises. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 236, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(352510,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \n Assertion- Mosquito needs our blood for reproduction. \n \n Reason-The female mosquito is considered a parasite.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The female mosquito is not considered a parasite, although it needs our blood for reproduction. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 236, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(352511,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Adaptions of parasites and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Parasites show loss of unnecessary sense organs. \n \n 2. They have presence of adhesive organs or suckers. \n \n 3. Highly developed digestive system. \n \n 4. Show low reproductive capacity.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','In accordance with their life styles, parasites evolved special adaptations such as the loss of unnecessary sense organs, presence of adhesive organs or suckers to cling on to the host, loss of digestive system and high reproductive capacity. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 235, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are correct','All the statements are incorrect','NEW','12-235','12-200',NULL),
(352512,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Parasites and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. The human liver fluke is an trematode parasite \n \n 2. Human liver fluke depends on two intermediate hosts- A snail and a pig. \n \n 3. Majority of the parasites reduces growth and reproduction of the host. \n \n 4. The malarial parasite needs a vector.','Only 2 is incorrect','The human liver fluke (a trematode parasite) depends on two intermediate hosts (a snail and a fish) to complete its life cycle. The malarial parasite needs a vector (mosquito) to spread to other hosts. Majority of the parasites harm the host; they may reduce the survival, growth and reproduction of the host and reduce its population density. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 236, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','All the statements are incorrect','NEW','12-235','12-200',NULL),
(352513,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Brood Parasitism and choose the correct option. \n \n 1. Parasitic bird lays its eggs in the nest of its host. \n \n 2. Host ejects the eggs from the nest. \n \n 3. Crow and Cuckoo show brood parasitism. \n \n 4. During the course of evolution, the eggs of the parasitic bird have evolved to resemble the host’s egg in size and colour.','Only 2 is incorrect','Brood parasitism in birds is a fascinating example of parasitism in which the parasitic bird lays its eggs in the nest of its host and lets the host incubate them. During the course of evolution, the eggs of the parasitic bird have evolved to resemble the host’s egg in size and colour to reduce the chances of the host bird detecting the foreign eggs and ejecting them from the nest.  Example- Crow and Cuckoo. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 236, Para 3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','All the statements are incorrect','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(352514,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Endoparasites are those that live inside the host body at the same sites. \n \n Reason- The life cycles of endoparasites are less complex because of their specialisation.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Endoparasites are those that live inside the host body at different sites (liver, kidney, lungs, red blood cells, etc.). The life cycles of endoparasites are more complex because of their extreme specialisation. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 236, Para 3)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-236','12-200',NULL),
(352515,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- The egrets always forage close to where the cattle are grazing. \n \n Reason- The cattle, as they move, stir up and flush out insects from the vegetation that otherwise might be difficult for the egrets to find and catch.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The egrets always forage close to where the cattle are grazing because the cattle, as they move, stir up and flush out insects from the vegetation that otherwise might be difficult for the egrets to find and catch. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 236, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-236','12-201',NULL),
(352516,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n \n Assertion- Sea anemone has stinging tentacles and the clown fish lives among them. \n \n Reason- The clown fish does not appear to derive any benefit by hosting the anemone.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Example of commensalism is the interaction between sea anemone that has stinging tentacles and the clown fish that lives among them. The fish gets protection from predators which stay away from the stinging tentacles. The anemone does not appear to derive any benefit by hosting the clown fish. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 237, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-237','12-201',NULL),
(352517,'biology','Consider these statements regarding commensalism and choose the correct option\n \n1.The cattle egret and grazing cattle are in close association. \n\n2.An orchid growing as an epiphyte on a mango branch shows commensalism. \n\n3.Whales get benefited when barnacles grow on them. \n\n4.The clown fish gets protection from predators due to sea anemone.','Only 3 is incorrect','An orchid growing as an epiphyte on a mango branch, and barnacles growing on the back of a whale benefit while neither the mango tree nor the whale derives any apparent benefit. The cattle egret and grazing cattle in close association. The fish gets protection from predators which stay away from the stinging tentacles.  (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 236, Last Para )','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 is correct','Only 3 is incorrect','Only 2 is correct','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','12-236','12-201',NULL),
(352518,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option. \n\nAssertion- Lichens represent an intimate mutualistic relationship between a fungus and higher plants. \n\nReason- Mycorrhizae are associations between fungi and photosynthetic algae.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Lichens represent an intimate mutualistic relationship between a fungus and photosynthesising algae or cyanobacteria. Similarly, the mycorrhizae are associations between fungi and the roots of higher plants. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 237, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-237','12-201',NULL),
(352519,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n \nAssertion- In mycorrhizae, fungi help the plant in the absorption of essential nutrients from the soil. \n\nReason- The plant in turn provides the fungi with energy-yielding proteins.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The fungi help the plant in the absorption of essential nutrients from the soil while the plant in turn provides the fungi with energy-yielding carbohydrates. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 237, Para 2)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-237','12-201',NULL),
(352520,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Process in which animals pollinate flowers is called Zoophilly.\n\nReason- Process in which animals disperse seeds is called Zoochory.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Plants need the help of animals for pollinating their flowers- Zoophilly and dispersing their seeds - Zoochory. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 237, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(352521,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Plants offer rewards or fees in the form of pollen and nectar for pollinators.\n\nReason- Animals obviously have to be paid ‘fees’ for the services that plants expect from them.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Animals obviously have to be paid ‘fees’ for the services that plants expect from them. Plants offer rewards or fees in the form of pollen and nectar for pollinators and juicy and nutritious fruits for seed dispersers. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 237, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(352522,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- The evolutions of the flower and its pollinator species are tightly linked with one another. \n\nReason- The mutually beneficial system should be safeguarded against ‘cheaters’, i.e., animals that try to steal pollen without aiding in pollination.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The mutually beneficial system should be safeguarded against ‘cheaters’, for example, animals that try to steal nectar without aiding in pollination. Plant-animal interactions often involve co-evolution of the mutualists, that is, the evolutions of the flower and its pollinator species are tightly linked with one another. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 237, Last Para) e.g the fig species is pollinated only by its partner wasp species. The wasp uses fig fruits as egg laying site and uses developing seeds for nourishing larvae.The wasp pollinate the fig searching for suitable egg laying site...','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(352523,'biology','Consider the following Assertion and Reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion- Commensalism is the inhibition of growth of one species from another. \n\nReason- Antibiosis is an example of commensalism.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Amensalism is the inhibition of growth of one species to another. Example- Antibiosis is the antagonism resulting from the toxicity of secondary metabolites produced.','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(352524,'biology','Consider these statements regarding mutualism by fig and wasp and choose the correct option. \n\n1. In many species of fig trees, there is a tight one-to-one relationship with the pollinator species of wasp. \n\n2. A given fig species can be pollinated only by its ‘partner’ wasp species and no other species. \n\n3. The wasp pollinates the fig inflorescence while searching for suitable egg-laying sites. \n\n4. Fig offers the wasp some of its developing seeds, as food for the developing wasp larvae.','All the statements are correct','In many species of fig trees, there is a tight one-to-one relationship with the pollinator species of wasp. It means that a given fig species can be pollinated only by it ‘partner’ wasp species and no other species. The female wasp uses the fruit not only as an oviposition (egg-laying) site but uses the developing seeds within the fruit for nourishing its larvae. The wasp pollinates the fig inflorescence while searching for suitable egg-laying sites. In return for the favour of pollination the fig offers the wasp some of its developing seeds, as food for the developing wasp larvae. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 237, Last Para)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1, 2, 3 are correct','All the statements are correct','NEW','12-237','12-202',NULL),
(352525,'biology','Consider these statements regarding Ophrys and choose the correct option.\n\n1. The Mediterranean orchid Ophrys employs ‘sexual deceit’.\n\n2. One petal and calyx of its flower bears an uncanny resemblance to the female of the wasp in size,colour and markings.\n\n3. The male bee is attracted to what it perceives as a female,‘pseudocopulates’ with the flower.\n\n4. When this same bee ‘pseudocopulates’ with another flower, fertilisation occurs.','2 and 4 are incorrect','The Mediterranean orchid Ophrys employs ‘sexual deceit’ to get pollination done by a species of bee. One petal of its flower bears an uncanny resemblance to the female of the bee in size, colour and markings. The male bee is attracted to what it perceives as a female, ‘pseudocopulates’ with the flower, and during that process is dusted with pollen from the flower. When this same bee ‘pseudocopulates’ with another flower, it transfers pollen to it and thus, pollinates the flower. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Page No. 338, Para 1)','Ecology >> Organisms And Populations >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 4 is incorrect','Only 1 is correct','2 and 4 are incorrect','All the statements are correct','NEW','12-238','12-202',NULL),
(10101,'chemistry','The branch of science that studies the preparation, properties, structure and reactions of material substances is called ?','Chemistry','The branch of science that studies the preparation, properties, structure and reactions of material substances is called Chemistry.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 1 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Geology','Biology','Chemistry','Physics','NEW','11-001','11-001',NULL),
(10102,'chemistry','Chemistry came up as a result of which interesting thing?','Both Philosopher’s stone and Elixir of life','Chemistry was not studied for its own sake, rather it came up as a result of search for two interesting things:\n \n (i) Philosopher’s stone (Paras) which would convert all baser metals e.g., iron and copper into gold.\n \n (ii)‘Elexir of life’ which would grant immortality.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 1 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Philosopher’s stone','Elixir of life','Sorcerer\'s stone','Both Philosopher’s stone and Elixir of life','NEW','11-001','11-001',NULL),
(10103,'chemistry','Philosopher’s stone (Paras) would convert all baser metals into __________.','Gold','Philosopher’s stone (Paras) which would convert all baser metals e.g., iron and copper into gold.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 1 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Diamond','Gold','Iron','Silver','NEW','11-001','11-001',NULL),
(10104,'chemistry','Elixir of life’ is supposed to grant________.','Immortality','‘Elixir of life’ would grant immortality.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 1 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Mortality','Immortality','Immense muscular strength','None of these','NEW','11-001','11-001',NULL),
(10105,'chemistry','Chemistry was developed mainly in the form of Alchemy and Iatrochemistry during which period?','1300-1600 CE.','Chemistry was developed mainly in the form of Alchemy and Iatrochemistry during 1300-1600 CE.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 1 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','1200-1500 CE.','1300-1600 CE.','1000-1100 CE.','1600-1900 CE.','NEW','11-001','11-001',NULL),
(10106,'chemistry','What are the two main forms that chemistry developed to during 1300-1600 CE?','Latrochemistry and Alchemy','Chemistry developed mainly in the form of Alchemy and Iatrochemistry during 1300-1600 CE.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 1 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Latrochemistry and Alchemy','Alchemy and Supra Chemistry','Latrochemistry and Supra Chemistry','Latrochemistry and Modern Chemistry','NEW','11-001','11-001',NULL),
(10107,'chemistry','Modern chemistry took shape in the which century in Europe?','18th','Modern chemistry took shape in 18th century Europe, after a few centuries of alchemical traditions which were introduced in Europe by the Arabs.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 1 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','16th','17th','18th','19th','NEW','11-001','11-001',NULL),
(10108,'chemistry','Who introduced Alchemical traditions in Europe?','Arabs','Modern chemistry took shape in the 18th century Europe, after a few centuries of alchemical traditions which were introduced in Europe by the Arabs.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 1 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Europeans','Arabs','Chinese','Indians','NEW','11-001','11-001',NULL),
(10109,'chemistry','Which cultures included much knowledge of chemical processes and techniques?','The Chinese and the Indian','The Chinese and the Indian had their own alchemical traditions. \n \n These included much knowledge of chemical processes and techniques.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','The Chinese and the Arabian','The Chinese and the Indian','The Indian and the Arabian','The European and the Arabian','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10110,'chemistry','What was Chemistry called in ancient India?\n \n 1) Rasayan Shastra\n \n 2) Rastantra\n \n 3) Ras Kriya\n \n 4) Rasvidya','1,2,3,4','In ancient India, the chemistry was called Rasayan Shastra, Rastantra, Ras Kriya, or Rasvidya.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','2,4','1,3','2,3,4','1,2,3,4','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10111,'chemistry','Choose the correct option\n \n I) Systematic excavations at Mohenjodaro in Punjab prove that the story of the development of chemistry in India is very old.\n \n II) Systematic excavations at Harappa in Punjab prove that the story of the development of chemistry in India is very old.','Only Statement II is correct','Systematic excavations at Mohenjodaro in Sindh and Harappa in Punjab prove that the story of the development of chemistry in India is very old.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the statements are false','Both the statements are true','Only Statement I is correct','Only Statement II is correct','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10112,'chemistry','What was not included in the Chemistry of ancient India?','Paper making','Chemistry in ancient India included metallurgy, medicine, the manufacture of cosmetics, glass, dyes, etc.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Metallurgy','Manufacture of cosmetics','Glass','Paper making','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10113,'chemistry','Gypsum cement that has been used in the construction work does not contain which of the following','Traces of Aluminium Carbonate','Gypsum cement has been used in construction work.\n \nIt contains lime, sand, and traces of CaCO₃.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Lime','Sand','Traces of CaCO₃.','Traces of Aluminium Carbonate','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10114,'chemistry','Consider the following statements and choose the correct answer\n \n a) The mass production of pottery can be regarded as the earliest chemical process.\n \n b) Archaeological findings show that baked bricks were not used in construction work during the Harappan period.','Only Statement a) is true','Archaeological findings show that baked bricks were used in construction work. \n \n It shows the mass production of pottery, which can be regarded as the earliest chemical process.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'2','mcq','Both statements are false','Only Statement a) is true','Statement a) is false','Both statements are true','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10115,'chemistry','What was faience, a sort of glass, made by Harappans used as?','Ornament','Harappans made faience, a sort of glass that was used as ornaments.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Weapon','Cement','Medicine','Ornament','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10116,'chemistry','Consider the following statements and choose the correct answer\n \n I. Harappan melted and forged a variety of objects from metals, such as lead,silver, gold and copper.\n \n II. They improved the hardness of copper for making artefacts by using tin and arsenic.','Both the statements are true','Harappans melted and forged a variety of objects from metals, such as lead, silver, gold and copper.\n \n They improved the hardness of copper for making artefacts by using tin and arsenic. \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the statements are true','Statement I is true and II is false','Only statement II is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-002','11-002','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/10116q.png'),
(10117,'chemistry','What did Harappans use to improve the hardness of copper for making artifacts?','Sn and As','Harappans melted and forged a variety of objects from metals, such as lead (Pb), silver (Ag), gold (Au), and copper (Cu). \n \n They improved the hardness of copper for making artifacts by using tin (Sn) and arsenic (As).\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Sn and As','As and Pb','Pb and Au','As only','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10118,'chemistry','A number of glass objects were found in \n \n a.Maski in South India\n \n b.Hastinapur in South India\n \n c.Taxila in North India\n \n Choose the correct option.','Both a and c','A number of glass objects were found in Maski in South India (1000–900 BCE), and Hastinapur and Taxila in North India (1000–200 BCE).\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and c','Only a','only c','All are correct','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10119,'chemistry','What coloring agents are used to color glass and glazes?','Metal oxides','Glass and glazes were colored by the addition of coloring agents like metal oxides.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 2 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Development of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Dyes','Transition elements','Metal oxides','Purified metal salts','NEW','11-002','11-002',NULL),
(10201,'chemistry','Drugs such as Cisplatin and Taxol are effective in which therapy?','Cancer','Some of these drugs are Cisplatin and Taxol, which are effective in cancer therapy. \n \n The drug AZT (Azidothymidine) is used for helping AIDS patients.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 4 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Importance of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','AIDS','Tuberculosis','Cancer','Brain tumours','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(10202,'chemistry','Which patients have been helped by the use of drug AZT (Azidothymidine)?','AIDS patients','The drug AZT (Azidothymidine) is used for helping AIDS patients.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 4 Paragraph-3).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Importance of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Pneumonia patients','Measles patients','AIDS patients','Cancer patients','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(10203,'chemistry','Which is a hazardous refrigerant responsible for ozone depletion in the stratosphere?','CFCs (Chlorofluorocarbons)','Safer alternatives to environmentally hazardous refrigerants, like CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons), responsible for ozone depletion in the stratosphere, have been successfully synthesised.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 4 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Importance of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','CFCs (Chlorofluorocarbons)','CFM (Chlorofluoromethane)','CIC (Chloroiodocarbons)','CIM (Chloroiodomethane)','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(10204,'chemistry','Which one of the following statement is correct?\n \n (i) Solids have definite volume and definite shape. \n \n (ii) Liquids have definite volume but do not have definite shape. They take the shape of the container in which they are placed. \n \n (iii) Gases have neither definite volume no definite shape. They do not completely occupy the space in the container in which they are placed.','T,T,F','(i) Solids have definite volume and definite shape.\n \n (ii) Liquids have definite volume but do not have definite shape. They take the shape of the container in which they are placed.\n \n (iii) Gases have neither definite volume no definite shape. They completely occupy the space in the container in which they are placed.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 4 Paragraph-last)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Importance of Chemistry',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T,T','T,T,F','T,F,F','F,F,F','NEW','11-004','11-004',NULL),
(10205,'chemistry','The three states of matter are interconvertible by changing the conditions of ________ and ________.','Temperature and Pressure','These three states of matter are interconvertible by changing the conditions of temperature and pressure.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 5 Paragraph-2).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Importance of Chemistry',NULL,'1','mcq','Volume and Pressure','Temperature and Pressure','Volume and Temperature','Catalyst','NEW','11-005','11-005',NULL),
(10206,'biology','______ are convenient categories we use to study organisms.','Taxa','Convenient categories we use to study organisms, the scientific term for these categories is taxa. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 7; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Biodiversity And Classification',NULL,'2','fillup','T,a,xa',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-007','11-005',NULL),
(10207,'biology','Which of the following features form the basis of modern taxonomic studies?\n\ni.External and internal features\n\nii. Structure of cell\n\niii. Development process\n\niv. Ecological information','i,ii,iii,iv','External and internal structure, along with the structure of cells, development process and ecological information of organisms are essential and form the basis of modern taxonomic studies. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 7; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Biodiversity And Classification',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii','i,ii,iv','i,ii,iii,iv','ii,iv','NEW','11-007','11-005',NULL),
(10208,'biology','Biological names are generally derived from_____','Latin','Biological names are Latinised or derived from Latin irrespective of their origin. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 7; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Biodiversity And Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','German','French','English','Latin','NEW','11-007','11-005',NULL),
(10209,'biology','The presence of external ears and body hair are characteristics of','Mammals','Suppose we were to say ‘mammals’, you would, of course, think of animals with external ears and body hair. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 7; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Biodiversity And Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Reptiles','Birds','Mammals','Fishes','NEW','11-007','11-005',NULL),
(10210,'biology','Carolus Linnaeus named his publication____','Systema Naturae','Linnaeus used Systema Naturae as the title of his publication. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 8; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Biodiversity And Classification',NULL,'2','fillup','Sys,te,ma, Na,tu,rae,bio,lo,gy',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-008','11-006',NULL),
(10211,'biology','Which of the following is/are TRUE..\n\ni. Plants as such can form a taxa . \n\nii. ‘Animals’, ‘mammals’ and ‘dogs’ represent taxa at different levels.\n\niii. The process of classifying organisms into different taxa based on characteristics is called oncology\n\niv. Fungi as such can form a taxon.','i,ii,iv','Here you must recognise that taxa can indicate categories at very different levels. ‘Plants’ – also form a taxa. ‘Wheat’ is also a taxa. Similarly, ‘animals’, ‘mammals’, ‘dogs’ are all taxa – but you know that a dog is a mammal and mammals are animals. Therefore, ‘animals’, ‘mammals’ and ‘dogs’ represent taxa at different levels. Hence, based on characteristics, all living organisms can be classified into different taxa. This process of classification is taxonomy. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 7; Paragraph No. 4 , 5)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Biodiversity And Classification',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii,iv','i,ii,iii,iv','i,ii,iii','ii,iii,iv','NEW','11-007','11-005',NULL),
(10212,'biology','The branch of science that deals with relationships among organisms are called______','Systematics','Systematics takes into account evolutionary relationships among organisms. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 8; Paragraph No. 4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Biodiversity And Classification',NULL,'1','fillup','Sys,te,ma,tics,mo,de,rn',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-008','11-006',NULL),
(10213,'biology','The order in which the basic processes in taxonomy are followed','Characterisation > Identification > Classification > Nomenclature','Characterisation, Identification, Classification, and Nomenclature are the basic processes of taxonomy. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 8; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Biodiversity And Classification',NULL,'3','mcq','Characterisation > Identification > Nomenclature > Classification','Characterisation > Identification > Classification > Nomenclature','Identification >Characterisation >Classification > Nomenclature','Characterisation > Nomenclature > identification > Classification','NEW','11-008','11-006',NULL),
(10214,'biology','______ takes into account evolutionary relationships between organisms.','Systematics','Linnaeus used Systema Naturae as the title of his publication. The scope of systematics was later enlarged to include identification, nomenclature and classification. Systematics takes into account evolutionary relationships between organisms.','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Biodiversity And Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Systematics','Ecology','Taxonomy','Zoology','NEW','11-008','11-005',NULL),
(10215,'biology','Human beings were, since long, not only interested in knowing more about different kinds of organisms and their diversities, but also the relationships among them. This branch of study was referred to as _______.','Systematics','Human beings were, since long, not only interested in knowing more about different kinds of organisms and their diversities, but also the relationships among them. This branch of study was referred to as systematics. The word systematics is derived from the Latin word ‘systema’ which means systematic arrangement of organisms. Linnaeus used Systema Naturae as the title of his publication.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12; Page 220; Paragraph No : 1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Biodiversity And Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Ecology','Systematics','Taxonomy','Zoology','NEW','11-008','11-005',NULL),
(10216,'biology','Which is a correct form of Biological Nomenclature?','Mangifera indica Linn.',NULL,'Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Biodiversity And Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Mangifera indica Linn.','Mangifera indica','Mangifera indica Binom','Mangifera Indica','NEW','11-009','11-006',NULL),
(10301,'chemistry','A ________ contains particles of two or more pure substances which may be present in it in any ratio.','Mixture','A mixture contains particles of two or more pure substances which may be present in it in any ratio.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 5 Paragraph-6).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Elements','Compounds','Mixture','Both a and b','NEW','11-005','11-005',NULL),
(10302,'chemistry','In a ________ mixture, the components completely mix with each other.','Homogeneous','In a homogeneous mixture, the components completely mix with each other.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 5 Paragraph-6).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Heterogenous','Homogeneous','Immiscible','Homologous','NEW','11-005','11-005',NULL),
(10303,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a mixture?','Distilled alcohol','Since components of distilled alcohol cannot be seperated by simple methods and the constituent particles are present in fixed composition, it falls under the category of pure substances\n \n However, tea diesel and rock salt are mixtures as the constituent particles are present in any ratio in them, that is, their composition is not uniform throughout.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 5 Paragraph-5,6).','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Tea','Diesel','Distilled alcohol','Rock salt','NEW','11-005','11-005',NULL),
(10304,'chemistry','Pure substances are further classified into ________ and compounds and mixtures are further classified to ___________ and homogenous mixtures.','i) Elements\n ii) Heterogeneous mixtures','Matter is classified into pure substance and mixture. \n \n Pure substances are forther classified into elements and compounds and mixtures are further classified to heterogenous mixtures and homogenous mixtures.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 5 Paragraph-Fig 1.2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'3','mcq','i) Microscopic level\n ii) Heterogeneous mixtures','i) Heterogeneous mixtures\n ii) Elements','i) Homogeneous mixtures, \n ii) Elements','i) Elements\n ii) Heterogeneous mixtures','NEW','11-005','11-005',NULL),
(10305,'chemistry','What is/are the method/s to separate components of mixtures?','All of these','The components of a mixture can be separated by using physical methods, such as simple hand-picking, filtration, crystallisation, distillation, etc.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 5 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Filtration','Distillation','Simple hand-picking','All of these','NEW','11-005','11-005',NULL),
(31115,'chemistry','Which of the following properties generally increases down the group?','Atomic radius','Atomic radius generally increases down the group. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; Figure 3.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'1','mcq','Atomic radius','Electron gain enthalpy','Ionisation enthalpy','Electronegativity','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(10306,'chemistry','State True(T) or False(F).\n \n A) Pure substance cannot be separated by simple physical methods. \n \n B) Heterogenous mixtures cannot be separated by simple hand-picking.','T,F','Pure substances, Glucose has a fixed composition.\n \n Also, its constituents- Carbon,Hydrogen and Oxygen-cannot be separated by simple physical methods. \n \n Mixtures of salt and sugar, grains and pulses along with some dirt (often stone pieces), are heterogeneous mixtures.\n Hence can be separated out by hand-picking.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 5 Paragraph-7, 8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,T','F,F','NEW','11-005','11-005',NULL),
(10307,'chemistry','What are pure substances further classified to?','Elements and compounds','Pure substances can further be classified into elements and compounds.\n \n Metals and non-metals together make elements.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 5 Paragraph-8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'2','mcq','Elements and compounds','Atoms and molecules','Homogeneous substances and heterogeneous substances','Metals and non-metals','NEW','11-005','11-005',NULL),
(10308,'chemistry','The atoms of different elements are _______ in nature.','Different','The atoms of different elements are different in nature. \n \n They have different no. of electrons as well as neutrons and protons.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 6 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Same','Completely same but with different no. of electrons','Different','None of these','NEW','11-006','11-006',NULL),
(10309,'chemistry','The constituent particles of a compound are _______ which are formed by two or more atoms.','Molecules','The constituent particles of a compound are molecules which are formed by two or more atoms.\n \n However, constituent particles of elements are atoms.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 6 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Molecules','Atoms','Compounds','Solid','NEW','11-006','11-006',NULL),
(10310,'chemistry','Presence of atoms in a fixed ratio is characteristic of a particular _______.','Compound','Presence of atoms in a fixed ratio is characteristic of a particular compound.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 6 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Compound','Element','Mixture','Ore','NEW','11-006','11-006',NULL),
(10311,'chemistry','The properties of a compound are ___________ to those of its constituent elements.','Different','Also, the properties of a compound are different from those of its constituent elements.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 6 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Same','Slightly different','Different','Slightly same','NEW','11-006','11-006',NULL),
(10312,'chemistry','Which gas burns with pop sound?','Hydrogen','It is interesting to note that hydrogen burns with a pop sound and oxygen is a supporter of combustion, but water is used as a fire extinguisher.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 6 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrogen','Oxygen','Water','Carbon dioxide','NEW','11-006','11-006',NULL),
(10313,'chemistry','Which among the following are physical properties?','Both a and b','Physical properties, such as colour, odour, melting point, boiling point, density are used to describe matter.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 6 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Melting point','Boiling point','Composition','Both a and b','NEW','11-006','11-006',NULL),
(10314,'chemistry','Which among the following are chemical properties?','All of these','Properties, like composition, combustibility, reactivity with acids and bases, etc. are chemical properties.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 6 Paragraph last)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Reactivity with acids and bases','Composition','Combustibility','All of these','NEW','11-006','11-006',NULL),
(10315,'chemistry','_________ measurement of properties is required for scientific investigation.','Quantitative','Quantitative measurement of properties is required for scientific investigation.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 7 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Qualitative','Quantitative','Mathematical','Chemical and physical','NEW','11-007','11-007',NULL),
(10316,'chemistry','Any quantitative observation or measurement is represented by A followed by B, in which it is measured. \n A and B respectively are ?','A → number\n B → units','Any quantitative observation or measurement is represented by a number followed by units in which it is measured.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 7 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'2','mcq','A → units\n B → number','A → alphabet\n B → units','A → number\n B → units','A → roman numeral\n B → units','NEW','11-007','11-007',NULL),
(10317,'chemistry','State True(T) or False(F).\n \n A) Each modern industrialised country, including India, has a National Metrology Institute (NMI), which maintains standards of measurements.\n \n B) This responsibility has been given to the National Physical Laboratory (NPL), New Delhi.','T,T','Each modern industrialised country, including India, has a National Metrology Institute (NMI), which maintains standards of measurements. \n \n This responsibility has been given to the National Physical Laboratory (NPL), New Delhi.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 7 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,F','F,T','NEW','11-007','11-007',NULL),
(10318,'chemistry','State True(T) or False(F).\n \n A) National Physical Laboratory (NPL),New Delhi establishes experiments to realise the base units and derived units of measurement and maintains National Standards of Measurement. \n \n B) These standards are periodically inter -compared with standards maintained at other National Metrology Institutes in the world, as well as those, established at the International Bureau of Standards in Vienna.','T,F','National Physical Laboratory (NPL), New Delhi establishes experiments to realise the base units and derived units of measurement and maintains National Standards of Measurement. \n \n These standards are periodically inter -compared with standards maintained at other National Metrology Institutes in the world, as well as those, established at the International Bureau of Standards in Paris.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 7 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,F','F,T','NEW','11-007','11-007',NULL),
(10319,'chemistry','The SI system has _____ base units.','seven','The SI system has seven base units.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 7 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','six','five','fourteen','seven','NEW','11-007','11-007',NULL),
(10320,'chemistry','What is the SI unit of mass?','kg','The SI unit of mass is kilogram(kg).\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 8 Paragraph-Table)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','gm','kg','km','mg','NEW','11-008','11-008',NULL),
(10321,'chemistry','The unit of luminous intensity is?','Candela','The unit of luminous intensity is Candela.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 7 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Newton','Corpuscle','Candela','Brightness','NEW','11-007','11-007',NULL),
(10322,'chemistry','State True(T) or False(F).\n \n A) The metre is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the speed of light in vacuum c to be 299792458 when expressed in the unit m/s where the second is defined in terms of the Caesium frequency.\n \n B) Time is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the caesium frequency, the undisturbed ground-state hyperfine transition frequency of the caesium-133 atom, to be 9192631770 when expressed in the unit Hz.','T,T','Both the statements are correct as they correctly define the two SI units that are Metre and time.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 9 Paragraph-Table) diagram(ne0102)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,F','F,T','NEW','11-009','11-009',NULL),
(10323,'chemistry','What is one fermi equal to?','10⁻¹³ cm','One fermi is equal to 10⁻¹⁵ m. \n \n However 1 cm = 10⁻² m. \n \n Therefore, 1 fermi in cm is equal to 10⁻¹³ cm.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 9 Paragraph-Table) diagram(ne0103)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁻¹⁵ cm','10⁻¹⁰cm','10⁻¹³ cm','10⁻¹²cm','NEW','11-009','11-009',NULL),
(10324,'chemistry','What is the prefix for 10¹⁸ ?','Exa','The prefix used for 10¹⁸ is Exa\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 9 Paragraph-Table) diagram(ne0103)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Giga','Kilo','Nano','Exa','NEW','11-009','11-009',NULL),
(10325,'chemistry','What is the prefix for 10⁻²¹ (in m)?','Zepto','The prefix used for 10⁻²¹ is Zepto.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 9 Paragraph-Table) diagram(ne0103)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Yotta','Zepto','Yocto','Atto','NEW','11-009','11-009',NULL),
(10326,'chemistry','State True or False.\n \n A) Mass of a substance is the amount of matter present in it. \n \n B) Weight is the force exerted by gravity on an object.','T,T','Mass of a substance is the amount of matter present in it, while weight is the force exerted by gravity on an object. \n \n The mass of a substance is constant, whereas, its weight may vary from one place to another due to change in gravity. \n \n You should be careful in using these terms.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 9 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','T,T','T,F','F,F','F,T','NEW','11-009','11-009',NULL),
(10328,'chemistry','What is the use of the instruement in which \'Pan release\' is found?','Analysing balance','Pan release\' is the part of the instrument called analytical balance.\n \n The use of analytical balance is to analyse balance.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 9 Paragraph-Fig 1.5) diagram(ne0104)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'3','mcq','Beam balance','Analysing balance','Glass balance','Chemical balance','NEW','11-009','11-009',NULL),
(10329,'chemistry','Guess the correct name of the instrument marked in the diagram(n0103)','Burette','The correct name of the instrument is Burettte.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 10 Paragraph-Fig 1.7)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'3','mcq','Burette','Pipette','Graduated cylinder','Volumetric flask','NEW','11-010','11-010','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/10329q.png'),
(10330,'chemistry','If the density is more, how are particles packed?','Closely','Density of a substance tells us about how closely its particles are packed. \n \n If density is more, it means particles are more closely packed.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 10 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Loosely','Closely','Randomly','Systematically.','NEW','11-010','11-010',NULL),
(10331,'chemistry','What is the SI unit of temperature?','Kelvin','Kelvin is the SI unit of temperature.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 10 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Kelvin','Celcius','Fahrenheit','Candela','NEW','11-010','11-010','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/10331q.png'),
(10332,'chemistry','A measured temperature on Fahrenheit scale is 200 °F. What will this reading be on Celsius scale?','93.3 °C','°F = °C(9/5) + 32\n \n 200 °F = °C(9/5) + 32\n \n °C(9/5) = 168\n \n °C = 93.3 °C\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 10 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','92.3° C','94° C','30° C','93.3 °C','NEW','11-010','11-010',NULL),
(10333,'chemistry','A measured temperature on Fahrenheit scale is 312 °F. What will this reading be on Kelvin scale?','428.7 K','°F = °C(9/5) + 32\n \n 312 °F = °C(9/5) + 32\n \n °C(9/5) = 280 \n \n °C = 155.5°C\n \n K = °C + 273.15 \n     = 155.5 + 273.15\n     = 428.7 K\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 10 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'2','mcq','155.5 K','428.7 K','429 K','428.6 K','NEW','11-010','11-010',NULL),
(10401,'chemistry','In scientific notation, 0.00016 can be written as -','1.6xl0⁻⁴','0.00016 can be written as 1.6 × 10⁻⁴. \n \n Here, the decimal has to be moved four places to the right and (–4) is the exponent in the scientific notation. \n \n From the concept of \'order of magnitude\' N × 10ⁿ exponent having positive or negative values and N is a number (called digit term) which varies between 1.000... and 9.999....\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 10 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','1.6xl0⁻⁴','1.6xl0⁻³','1.6xl0⁻²','1.6xl0⁻¹','NEW','11-010','11-010',NULL),
(10402,'chemistry','The scientific notation of multiplication product of (5.6 x 10⁵) x (6.9 x 10⁸) is ?','3.864 x 10¹⁴','(5.6 x 10⁵) x (6.9 x 10⁸) \n \n = (5.6 x 6.9)(10⁸⁺⁵) \n \n = 38.64 x 10¹³ \n \n = 3.864 x 10¹⁴.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 12 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','3864 x 10¹¹','386.4x10¹²','38.64 x 10¹³','3.864 x 10¹⁴','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(10403,'chemistry','The scientific notation of division product of 2.7 x 10⁻³/5.5 x 10⁴ is -','4.909 x 10⁻⁸','(2.7/5.5) (10⁻³⁻⁴)\n \n = 0.4909 x 10⁻⁷ \n \n = 4.909 x 10⁻⁸.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 12 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','0.4909 x 10⁻⁷','4.909 x 10⁻⁸','0.50 x 10⁻⁷','5.0 x 10⁻⁸','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(10404,'chemistry','What is the correct scientific notation of subtraction of (2.5 x 10⁻²) - (4.8 x 10⁻³) ?','2.02 x 10⁻²','For these two operations, first the numbers are written in such a way that they have the same exponent.\n \n After that, the coefficients (digit terms) are added or subtracted.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 12 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','2.02 x 10⁻²','0.202 x 10⁻³','0.2 x 10⁻³','2 x 10⁻²','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(10405,'chemistry','Express the following in the scientific notation: 8008','8.008 x 10³','While writing the scientific notation of 8008, decimal has to be moved 3 places to the right and exponent of 10 becomes 3.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 10 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','8008','800.8 x 10¹','80.08 x 10²','8.008 x 10³','NEW','11-010','11-010',NULL),
(10406,'chemistry','What is/are the number of significant figures in Avogadro’s number?','Four','Avogadro’s number is 6.022 x 10²³. \n \n Therefore, four significant figures are present in Avogadro’s number.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 13 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','Four','Two','One','Twenty-three','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(10407,'chemistry','State True(T) or False(F).\n \n A) In numbers written in scientific notation,all digits are significant. \n \n B) Zeros between two non-zero digits are significant. \n \n C) Zeros at the end or right of a number are significant provided that the decimal is present.','T,T,T','All the statements are true.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 12 Paragraph-7,8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T,T','T,T,F','T,F,F','F,T,F','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(10408,'chemistry','Choose the correct for the following.\n \n Assertion : Significant figures for 0.500 is 3 whereas for 500 it is 1.\n \n Reason : Zero at the end or right of a number are significant provided they are not on the right side of the decimal point.','Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.','Zeros at the end or right of a number are significant, provided they are on the right side of the decimal point. \n \n For example, 0.200 g has three significant figures.\n \n But, if otherwise, the terminal zeros are not significant if there is no decimal point. \n \n For example, 100 has only one significant figure, but 100. has three significant figures and 100.0 has four significant figures.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 12 Paragraph-7,8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct and reason is the correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion and reason both are correct but reason is not the correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(10409,'chemistry','Choose the correct for the following.\n \n Assertion : 1.265 has three significant figures.\n \n Reason : All numbers right to the decimal point are significant.','Assertion and reason both are incorrect.','All non-zero digits are significant.\n \n Since 1.265 has four significant digits, assertion is incorrect.\n \n Reason is incorrect because in the assertion digit to the left of decimal point too is significant.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 12 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and reason both are correct and reason is the correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion and reason both are correct but reason is not the correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect','Assertion and reason both are incorrect.','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(10410,'chemistry','What refers to the closeness of various measurements for the same quantity?','Precision','Precision refers to the closeness of various measurements for the same quantity.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 13 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'1','mcq','Accuracy','Precision','Units','Measurements','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(10411,'chemistry','What is the agreement of a particular value to the true value of the result?','Accuracy','Accuracy is the agreement of a particular value to the true value of the result.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 13 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'1','mcq','Measurements','Units','Precision','Accuracy','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(10412,'chemistry','Which of the following is the most accurate measurement?','9.000 m','Accuracy is the agreement of a particular value to the true value of the result.\n \n 9.000 is the correct answer because it gives accuracy till the third decimal place.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 13 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','9 m','9.0 m','9.00 m','9.000 m','NEW','11-013','11-013','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/10412q.png'),
(10413,'chemistry','Given that, the true value for a result is 2.00 g. \n \n Three students A, B and C take two measurements and report the results. Data to illustrate precision and accuracy as given below. \n \n Which of the following students got the values which are both precise and accurate? \n \n Derive from the table given in diagram(n0104)','C','Given that, the true value for a result is 2.00 g and student ‘ A\' takes two measurements and report the result as 1.95 g and 1.93 g. \n \n The values are precise as they are close to each other but are not accurate. \n \n Another student (B) repeats the experiment and obtains 1.94 g and 2.05 g as the results for two measurements. These observations are neither precise nor accurate. \n \n The third student (C) repeats these measurements and reports 2.01 g and 1.99 g as the result, these values are both precise and accurate.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 13 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','None of them','NEW','11-013','11-013','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/10413q.png'),
(10414,'chemistry','What should be the result reported in this addition in terms of significant figures should be 12.11 + 18.0 + 1.012 = 31.122 ?','31.1','In addition or subtraction, the result cannot have more digits to the right of the decimal point than either of the original numbers.\n \n Here, 18.0 has only one digit after the decimal point and, therefore the result should be reported only upto one digit after the decimal point which is 31.1.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 13 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','31.12','31.1','31.122','31.01','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(10415,'chemistry','What should be the result reported in the following multiplication of significant figures, 2.5 x 1.25 = 3.125?','3.1','In these operations, the result must be reported with not more significant figures as in the measurement with the fewest significant figures.\n \n In this question, 2.5 has the fewest number of significant figures which is 2.\n \n Hence the result is written such that the no. of significant figures in the answer is 3.1.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 13 Paragraph-7)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','3.125','3.01','3.12','3.1','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(51102,'chemistry','Which of the following property of water can be used to explain the spherical shape of rain droplets?\n \n a)viscosity\n \n b)surface tension\n \n c)density\n \n d)pressure','only b','The spherical shape of rain droplets is due to surface tension. The lowest energy state \n \n of a liquid will be when the surface area is minimum. Surface tension tries to decrease \n \n the surface area of the liquid to the minimum. The rain droplets are spherical because \n \n for a given volume, a sphere has minimum surface area\n \n (Reference NCERT class 11 page 155 paragraph 4)','Physical chemistry >> States of matter >> Examplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','only b','only c','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10416,'chemistry','State True(T) or False(F).\n \n A) If the rightmost digit to be removed is more than 5, the preceding number is decreased by one. \n \n B) If the rightmost digit to be removed is less than 5, the preceding number is not changed. \n \n C) If the rightmost digit to be removed is 5, then the preceding number is not changed if it is an even number but it is increased by one if it is an odd number.','F,T,T','All the statements are true except first statement. \n \n The correct statement is \'If the rightmost digit to be removed is more than 5, the preceding number is increased by one. \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 13 Paragraph-9)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','T,T,T','T,T,F','T,F,F','F,T,T','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(10417,'chemistry','Choose the correct for the following.\n \n Assertion: Rounding up 2808 upto three significant figures will be 2810. \n \n Reason : The terminal zeros are not significant if there is no decimal point.','Assertion is correct, reason is correct; reason is a correct explanation for assertion.','Zeros at the end or right of a number are significant, provided they are on the right side of the decimal point.\n \n But, if otherwise, the terminal zeros are not significant if there is no decimal point. \n \n Here, the digit to the right of zero is more than 5. \n \n Therefore the preceding number is increased by 1 and thus, as per the above rules of significance of zero, 2810 has 3 SF.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 12 Paragraph-7,8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion is correct, reason is correct; reason is a correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion and reason both are correct but reason is not the correct explanation for assertion.','Assertion is correct,but reason is incorrect.','Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.','NEW','11-012','11-012',NULL),
(10418,'chemistry','If 6.25 is to be rounded off to A and if 6.35 is to be rounded off to B. What do A and B refer to?','A → 6.2, \n B → 6.4','If the rightmost digit to be removed is 5, then the preceding number is not changed if it is an even number but it is increased by one if it is an odd number. \n \n For example, if 6.35 is to be rounded by removing 5, we have to increase 3 to 4 giving 6.4 as the result. \n \n However, if 6.25 is to be rounded off it is rounded off to 6.2.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 13 Paragraph-10)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','A → 6.3, \n B → 6.3','A → 6.1, \n B → 6.3','A → 6.2, \n B → 6.4','A → 6.3, \n B →6.4','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(10419,'chemistry','Often, while calculating, there is a need to convert units from one system to other. What is the method used to accomplish this called as?','All of these','The method used to accomplish conversion of units from one system to the other can be termed as factor label method or unit factor method or dimensional analysis.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 13 Paragraph-11)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','Factor label method','Unit factor method','Dimensional analysis','All of these','NEW','11-013','11-013',NULL),
(10420,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect conversion factor.','1 m = 3.937 inches','1 m = 39.37 inches. \n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 14 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','1 inch = 2.54 cm','1 Litre = 10⁻³ m³','1 m = 3.937 inches','1 atm = 1.01325 X 10⁵ Pa','NEW','11-014','11-014',NULL),
(10421,'chemistry','A jug contains 2L of milk. Calculate the volume of milk in m³.','2 x 10⁻³','Since 1 L = 1000 cm³ and 1 cm = 10⁻² m, which gives \n \n => 1 L = (1000 x 10⁻²)³ = 10⁻³ m³. \n \n Now 2L = 2 x 10⁻³ m³.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 14 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','2 x 10⁻³','2 x 10³','2 x 10000','2 x 10⁵','NEW','11-014','11-014',NULL),
(10422,'chemistry','How many seconds are there in 2 days?','172800 s','Here, we know 1 day = 24 hours (h) \n \n => 24 x 60 mins = 24 x 60 x 60 secs, \n \n So 2 days will have \n \n => 2 x 24 x 60 x 60 =172800s.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 14 Paragraph-last example)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Uncertainty in Measurement',NULL,'2','mcq','17280 s','1.7 x 10⁶ s','172800 s','107 x 10⁴ s','NEW','11-014','11-014',NULL),
(10501,'chemistry','When heated 4.88 g of KClO₃ produces 1.92 g of O₂ and 2.96 g of KCl. Which law is illustrated by the above experiment?','The result illustrates the law of conservation of mass.','The conclusion is that matter can neither be created nor destroyed. \n \n This is called ‘Law of Conservation of Mass’.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 14 Paragraph-8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Laws Of Chemical Combinations',NULL,'1','mcq','The result illustrates the law of conservation of mass.','The result illustrates the law of multiple proportion.','The result illustrates law of Definite Composition.','None of the above.','NEW','11-014','11-014',NULL),
(10502,'chemistry','Proust worked with the two samples of cupric carbonate, one of which was of natural origin and the other was synthetic one. He found that, the composition of elements present in it was same for both the samples as shown below. Which law is in favour of the data given in the diagram?(n0105)','The result illustrates law of Definite Composition.','Proust concluded that irrespective of the source, a given compound always contains same elements combined together in the same proportion by mass.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 15 Paragraph-2)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Laws Of Chemical Combinations',NULL,'3','mcq','The result illustrates the law of conservation of mass.','The result illustrates the law of multiple proportion.','The result illustrates law of Definite Composition.','The result illustrates Avogadro’s Law.','NEW','11-015','11-015','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/10502q.png'),
(10503,'chemistry','Which pair which illustrates the law of multiple proportions?','PbO, PbO₂','According to this law, if two elements can combine to form more than one compound, the masses of one element that combine with a fixed mass of the other element, are in the ratio of small whole numbers.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 15 Paragraph-3)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Laws Of Chemical Combinations',NULL,'2','mcq','PH₃ ,HCl','CuCl₂ ,CuSO₄','PbO, PbO₂','H₂S, SO₂','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(10504,'chemistry','Which of the following statements indicates that the law of multiple proportion is being followed?','Carbon forms two oxides namely CO₂ and CO, where masses of oxygen which combine with fixed mass of carbon are in the simple ratio 2 : 1.','According to this law, if two elements can combine to form more than one compound, the masses of one element that combine with a fixed mass of the other element, are in the ratio of small whole numbers.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 15 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Laws Of Chemical Combinations',NULL,'2','mcq','Sample of carbon dioxide taken from any source will always have carbon and oxygen in the ratio 1 : 2.','Carbon forms two oxides namely CO₂ and CO, where masses of oxygen which combine with fixed mass of carbon are in the simple ratio 2 : 1.','When magnesium burns in oxygen, the amount of magnesium taken for the reaction is equal to the amount of magnesium in magnesium oxide formed.','At constant temperature and pressure, 200 mL of hydrogen will combine with 100 mL oxygen to produce 200 mL of water vapour.','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(10505,'chemistry','Two volumes of Hydrogen react with one volume of Oxygen to give two volumes of water vapour.\n \n Hydrogen(100 ml) + Oxygen(A) -----> Water(100 ml). \n \n The volumes of Hydrogen and Oxygen which combine together bear a simple ratio of B.\n \n What are the missing terms A and B respectively?','A → 50 mL, \n B → 2:1','The volumes of Hydrogen and Oxygen which combine (i.e., 100 mL and 50 mL) bear a simple ratio of 2:1.\n \n 100 mL of Hydrogen reacts with 50 mL of Oxygen to form 100 mL of water. \n \n Thus, the ratio of Hydrogen to Oxyen is 2:1\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 15 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Laws Of Chemical Combinations',NULL,'2','mcq','A → 25 mL, \n B → 1:2','A → 50 mL, \n B → 1:2','A → 50 mL, \n B → 2:1','A → 25 mL, \n B → 2:1','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(10506,'chemistry','45.4 L of Dinitrogen reacted with 22.7 L of Dioxygen and 45.4 L of Nitrous oxide was formed. The reaction is given below \n \n 2N₂ + O₂ ---> 2N₂O. \n \n Which law is being obeyed in this experiment?','Gay Lussac’s law of gaseous volumes','The ratio between the volumes of the reactants and the product in the given question is simple, i.e. 2 : 1 : 2, it proves the Gay Lussac’s law of gaseous volumes, which states that when gases combine or are produced in a chemical reaction, they do so in a simple ratio by volume provided all gases are at the same temperature and pressure.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 15 Paragraph-7)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Laws Of Chemical Combinations',NULL,'2','mcq','Law of conservation of mass.','Law of definite proportion.','Law of multiple proportion.','Gay Lussac’s law of gaseous volumes','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(10507,'chemistry','Gay Lussac’s discovery of integer ratio in volume relationship is actually the law of definite proportions by _______.','Volume','Gay Lussac’s discovery of integer ratio in volume relationship is actually the law of definite proportions by volume.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 15 Paragraph-9)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Laws Of Chemical Combinations',NULL,'1','mcq','Volume','Mass','Weight','Fluid','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(10508,'chemistry','Whose work explained properly \'The Gay Lussca\'s law\' in 1811?','Avogadro','The Gay Lussac’s law was explained properly by the work of Avogadro in 1811.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 15 Paragraph-9)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Laws Of Chemical Combinations',NULL,'1','mcq','Avogadro','Proust','Dalton','Lavoisier','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(10509,'chemistry','According to Avogadro’s law, two volumes of hydrogen combine with one volume of oxygen to give two volumes of water without leaving any __________.','Unreacted oxygen.','According to Avogadro’s law, two volumes of Hydrogen combine with one volume of Oxygen to give two volumes of water without leaving any unreacted Oxygen.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 15 Paragraph-9)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Laws Of Chemical Combinations',NULL,'2','mcq','Mass of oxygen.','Residual mass of oxygen.','Reacted oxygen.','Unreacted oxygen.','NEW','11-015','11-015',NULL),
(10510,'chemistry','Avogadro’s proposal was published in the French Journal de ____________.','Physique.','Avogadro’s proposal was published in the French Journal de Physique.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 16 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Laws Of Chemical Combinations',NULL,'1','mcq','Chimique.','La science.','Physique.','Particules.','NEW','11-016','11-016',NULL),
(10511,'biology','Which of the following is less general in characters as compared to genus? [2001]','Species','Genus comprises a group of related species which has more characters in common in comparison to species of other genera. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 9; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Species','Division','Class','Family','NEW','11-009','11-007',NULL),
(10512,'biology','‘Taxon’ is the unit of a group of [1996]','Taxonomy','Each category, referred to as a unit of classification, in fact, represents a rank and is commonly termed as taxon (pl.: taxa). \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 8; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Order','Taxonomy','Species','Genes.','NEW','11-008','11-006',NULL),
(10513,'biology','Linnaeus is credited with [1993]','Binomial nomenclature','Binomial nomenclature of scientific naming was first given by C. Linnaeus (1735) in his book Systema Naturae and later in “Species Plantarum” (1753). He used two latin words for any organism, the first being generic name and the second is specific name. The generic name begins with capital letter and the species name with small letter.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 6; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Binomial nomenclature','Theory of biogenesis','Discovery of microscope','Discovery of blood circulation.','NEW','11-006','11-004',NULL),
(10514,'biology','Sequence of taxonomic categoriess is [1992]','Division–class–order–family–tribe–genus–\nspecies.','To construct the hierarchy of classification, one or more species are grouped into a genus, one or more of genera into a family, families are clubbed into order, orders into class, classes into phylum and various phyla into kingdom.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 10; Figure 1.1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Class–phylum–tribe–order–family–genus–\nspecies','Division–class–family–tribe–order–genus–\nspecies','Division–class–order–family–tribe–genus–\nspecies.','Phylum–order–class–tribe–family–genus–\nspecies.','NEW','11-010','11-008',NULL),
(10515,'biology','A group of plants or animals with similar traits of any rank is [1992, 1991]','Taxon.','A taxon (plural taxa) or taxonomic unit, is a name designating an organism or group of organisms. A taxon is assigned a rank and can be placed at a particular level in a systematic hierarchy reflecting evolutionary relationships.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 8; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Species','Genus','Order','Taxon.','NEW','11-008','11-006',NULL),
(10516,'biology','A taxon is [1992, 1990]','A taxonomic group of any ranking','The word taxon signifies a taxonomic group of any rank which represents the real biological organisms included in a category. The term taxon was coined by Adolf Meyer (1926) for animals and H.J Lam (1948) used this term in plant science. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 8; Paragraph No. 3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A group of related families','A group of related species','A type of living organisms','A taxonomic group of any ranking','NEW','11-008','11-006',NULL),
(10517,'biology','Basic unit or smallest taxon of taxonomy/ classification is  [1990]','Species','Basic unit or smallest taxon of taxonomy/ classification is species. Species is a group of individuals that remain relatively constant in their characteristics; can be distinguished from other species and do not normally interbreed.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 9; Paragraph No. 1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Species','Kingdom','Family','Variety.','NEW','11-009','11-006',NULL),
(10518,'biology','In the taxonomic categories which hierarchial arrangement in ascending order is correct in case of animals [NEET 2022]','Kingdom , Phylum , Class , Order , Family , Genus , Species','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 10; Figure 1.1) diagram (e0110)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Kingdom , Phylum , Class , Order , Family , Genus , Species','Kingdom , Class, Phylum ,Famliy, Order ,Genus ,Species','Kingdom ,Order ,Class , Phylum , Family , Genus , Species','Kingdom , Order ,Phylum , Class , Family , Genus , Species','NEW','11-010','11-008',NULL),
(10519,'biology','Biological organisations starts with [2007]','Submicroscopic molecular level','Properties of cellular organelles are not present in the molecular constituents of the organelle but arise as a result of interactions among the molecular components comprising the organelle.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 5; Paragraph No.3)\nFrom this we can understand that biological organisation starts with  molecular level . The interaction at molecular level leads to orgenelle and further  to cells , whose interaction leads to tissues further to organ and organsim .','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cellular level','Organismic level','Atomic level','Submicroscopic molecular level','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10520,'biology','Select the correctly written scientific name of Mango which was first described by Carolus Linnaeus [2019]','Mangifera indica Linn','The scientific name of mango is written as Mangifera indica. Let us see how it is a binomial name. In this name Mangifera represents the genus while indica, is a particular species, or a specific epithet. Other universal rules of nomenclature are as follows:\n\n1. Biological names are generally in Latin and written in italics. They are Latinised or derived from Latin irrespective of their origin.\n\n2. The first word in a biological name represents the genus while the second component denotes the specific epithet.\n\n3. Both the words in a biological name, when handwritten, are separately underlined, or printed in italics to indicate their Latin origin.\n\n4. The first word denoting the genus starts with a capital letter while the specific epithet starts with a small letter. It can be illustrated with the example of Mangifera indica.\n\nName of the author appears after the specific epithet, i.e., at the end of the biological name and is written in an abbreviated form, e.g., Mangifera indica Linn. It indicates that this species was first described by Linnaeus.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 7 ; Paragraph No. 1 and 2)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Mangifera Indica','Mangifera indica Car.Linn','Mangifera indica Linn','Mangifera indica','NEW','11-007','11-005',NULL),
(10521,'biology','Which of the following is against the rules of ICBN? [Odisha NEET 2019]','Generic and Specific names should be written starting with small letters.','The scientific name of mango is written as Mangifera indica. Let us see how it is a binomial name. In this name Mangifera represents the genus while indica, is a particular species, or a specific epithet. Other universal rules of nomenclature are as follows:\n\n1. Biological names are generally in Latin and written in italics. They are Latinised or derived from Latin irrespective of their origin.\n\n2. The first word in a biological name represents the genus while the second component denotes the specific epithet.\n\n3. Both the words in a biological name, when handwritten, are separately underlined, or printed in italics to indicate their Latin origin.\n\n4. The first word denoting the genus starts with a capital letter while the specific epithet starts with a small letter. It can be illustrated with the example of Mangifera indica.\n\nName of the author appears after the specific epithet, i.e., at the end of the biological name and is written in an abbreviated form, e.g., Mangifera indica Linn. It indicates that this species was first described by Linnaeus.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 7  ; Paragraph No. 1 and 2)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Hand written Scientific names should be underlined','Every species should have a generic name and a specific epithet.','Scientific names are in Latin and should be italicized.','Generic and Specific names should be written starting with small letters.','NEW','11-007','11-004',NULL),
(10522,'biology','The term "New Systematics" was introduced by? [1998]','Julian Huxley','The term "New Systematics" was introduced by Julian Huxley\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Bentham and Hooker','Linnaeus','Julian Huxley','A.P de Candolle','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10523,'biology','The book "Genera Plantarum" was written by ? [1999]','Bentham and Hooker','The book Genera Plantarum was written by Bentham and Hooker.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Engler and Prantl','Bentham and Hooker','Bessey','Hutchinson','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10524,'biology','Biosystematics aims at [2003]','The classification of organisms based in their evolutionary history and establishing their phylogeny on the totality of various parameters from all fields of studies.','Biosystematics is essentially finding relationship among organisms . It involves steps like classification , identification , nomenclature and finally draws evolutionary relation among various organisms with the help of other various other fields.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','the classification of organisms based on broad morphological characters','delimiting various taxa of organisms and establishing their relationships','The classification of organisms based in their evolutionary history and establishing their phylogeny on the totality of various parameters from all fields of studies.','identification and arrangement of organisms on the basis of cytological characteristics','NEW','11-008','11-005',NULL),
(10525,'biology','The term Phylum was given by? [1992]','Haeckel','The term Phylum was given by Haeckel\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cuvier','Haeckel','Theophrastus','Linnaeus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10526,'biology','Species are considered as [ AIPMT 2003]','Real basic units of classification','Species are the real basic unit of classification and lowest in the hierarchy . \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 10; Figure 1.1) diagram (e0102)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Real basic units of classification','Lowest units of classification','Artificial concept of human mind, which cannot be defined in absolute terms','Real units of classification devised by taxonomists','NEW','11-010','11-008',NULL),
(10527,'biology','One of the most important functions of Botanical Garden is ? [2005]','They allow ex situ conservation of germplasm','These specialised gardens have collections of living plants for Reference  . Plant species in these gardens are grown for identification purposes. ex-situ conservation of germplasm means conservation of  a species outside its natural habitat.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 12  ; Paragraph No.2)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','They provide beautiful area for recreation','One can observe tropical plants here','They allow ex situ conservation of germplasm','They provide the natural habitat for wildlife','NEW','11-012',NULL,NULL),
(10528,'biology','Static concept of species was put forward by ? [1988]','Linnaeus','Static concept of species was put forward by Linnaeus\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','de Candolle','Linnaeus','Theophrastus','Darwin','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10529,'biology','Match the items given in column 1 with those in column 2 and select the correct option given below  [NEET 2018] \nRefer the diagram table given below (0108)','a - iii ; b - iv ; c - i ; d - ii','Herbarium is a store house of collected plant specimens that are dried, pressed and preserved on sheets.\n\nMuseums have collections of preserved plant and animal specimens for study and Reference  .\n\nKey is another taxonomical aid used for identification of plants and animals based on the similarities and dissimilarities. The keys are based on the contrasting characters generally in a pair called couplet. It represents the choice made between two opposite options\n\nCatalogue is a list that enumerates methodically all the species found in an area with brief description aiding identification. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 11 - 14)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a - i ; b - iv ; c - iii ; d - ii','a - iii ; b - ii ; c - i ; d - iv','a - ii ; b - iv ; c - iii ; d - ii','a - iii ; b - iv ; c - i ; d - ii','NEW','11-011',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/10529q.png'),
(10530,'biology','Which one of the following is sceintifically correctly named, correctly prinnted according to the International Rules of Nomenclature and correctly described? [[Mains 2012]','Plasmodium falciparum- A protozoan pathogen causing the most serious type of malaria','The scientific name of mango is written as Mangifera indica. Let us see how it is a binomial name. In this name Mangifera represents the genus while indica, is a particular species, or a specific epithet. Other universal rules of nomenclature are as follows:\n\n1. Biological names are generally in Latin and written in italics. They are Latinised or derived from Latin irrespective of their origin.\n\n2. The first word in a biological name represents the genus while the second component denotes the specific epithet.\n\n3. Both the words in a biological name, when handwritten, are separately underlined, or printed in italics to indicate their Latin origin.\n\n4. The first word denoting the genus starts with a capital letter while the specific epithet starts with a small letter. It can be illustrated with the example of Mangifera indica.\n\nName of the author appears after the specific epithet, i.e., at the end of the biological name and is written in an abbreviated form, e.g., Mangifera indica Linn. It indicates that this species was first described by Linnaeus.\n\nMusca domestica is a common houselfy , Plasmodium falciparum is protozoan,felis tigris is The Indian tiger,E.coli is a bacteria . From these and from the above mentioned rules of standardised naming B is the correct option . \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 7  ; Paragraph No. 1 and 2)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Musca domestica- the common house lizard, a reptile','Plasmodium falciparum- A protozoan pathogen causing the most serious type of malaria','felis tigris- The Indian tiger, well protected in Gir forests','E.coli- full name Entamoeba coli, a commonly occurring bacterium in the human intestine.','NEW','11-007','11-004',NULL),
(10531,'biology','Linnaeus evolved a system of nomenclature called  [1990]','Binomial nomenclature','Binomial nomenclature of scientific naming was first given by C. Linnaeus [1735] in his book Systema Naturae and later in “Species Plantarum” [1753]. He used two latin words for any organism, the first being generic name and the second is specific name. The generic name begins with capital letter and the species name with small letter.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 6; Paragraph No. 5)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Monomial nomenclature','Vernacular nomenclature','Binomial nomenclature','Polynomial nomenclature','NEW','11-007','11-004',NULL),
(10532,'biology','House fly belongs to _____ family. [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(4) Muscidae','The family Muscidae is a large family of flies, commonly known as the house fly family. It includes various species of flies, including the common house fly (Musca domestica). House flies are widespread and can be found in close association with human habitats, where they are often considered pests due to their potential role in transmitting diseases.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is option 4: Muscidae.','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Cyprinidae','(2) Hominidae','(3) Calliphoridae','(4) Muscidae','NEW','11-011','11-008',NULL),
(10533,'biology','X\' and \'Y\' are the components of Binomial nomenclature. This naming system was proposed by \'Z\' :','(1) X-Generic name, Y-Specific epithet, Z-Carolus Linnaeus','X - Generic name, Y - Specific epithet, Z - Carolus Linnaeus\n\nIn binomial nomenclature, which is the scientific naming system used for organisms, the generic name (X) represents the genus to which the species belongs, and the specific epithet (Y) represents the particular species within that genus. The naming system was proposed by Carolus Linnaeus, a Swedish botanist, physician, and zoologist, who is often referred to as the father of modern taxonomy. He developed this system in the 18th century to provide a standardized and universal way of naming and classifying organisms.','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) X-Generic name, Y-Specific epithet, Z-Carolus Linnaeus','(2) X-Specific epithet, Y-Generic name, Z-R.H. Whittaker','(3) X-Specific epithet, Y-Generic name, Z-Carolus Linnaeus','(4) X-Generic name, Y-Specific epithet, Z-R.H. Whittaker','NEW','11-006','11-004',NULL),
(10534,'biology','Which of the following are true about the taxonomical aid \'key\' ? \n (a) Keys are based on the similarities and dissimilarities. \n (b) Key is analytical in nature. \n (c) Keys are based on the contrasting characters in pair called couplet. \n (d) Same key can be used for all taxonomic categories. \n (e) Each statement in the key is called Lead. \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023]','(3) (a), (b), (c) and (e) only','(a), (b), (c) and (e) only','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) (a), (b) and (c) only','(2) (b), (c) and (d) only','(3) (a), (b), (c) and (e) only','(4) (a), (c), (d) and (e) only','NEW','11-012',NULL,NULL),
(10601,'chemistry','Consider the following statements. \n \n (i) Atoms of H, O, N and C have identical properties but different mass. \n \n (ii) Matter is divisible into atoms which are further indivisible. \n \n (iii) The ratio of N: H in NH₃ is 1 : 3 and N : O in nitric oxide is 2 : 1. \n \n (iv) Dalton’s atomic theory supports law of conservation of mass. \n \n Which of the following pairs of statements is true according to Dalton’s atomic theory ?','(ii) and (iv)','1. Atoms of different elements have different chemical properties.\n \n 2. Atoms are indivisible. \n \n 3. The ratio of N: H in NH₃ is 1 : 3 and N : O in nitric oxide is 1 : 1. \n \n 4. Since Dalton\'s theory states that atoms can neither be created nor be destroyed, it accounts for law of conservation of mass.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 16 Paragraph-5)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Dalton\'s Atomic Theory',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) and (ii)','(ii) and (iii)','(i) and (iv)','(ii) and (iv)','NEW','11-016','11-016',NULL),
(10602,'chemistry','Dalton’s theory could explain the laws of ______ combination.','Chemical','Dalton’s theory could explain the laws of chemical combination.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 16 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Dalton\'s Atomic Theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Multiple','Definite','Chemical','Physical','NEW','11-016','11-016',NULL),
(10603,'chemistry','Dalton’s theory could not explain the laws of gaseous \'A\'. What does A refer to?','Volumes','Dalton’s theory could not explain the laws of gaseous volumes.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 16 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Dalton\'s Atomic Theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Mixtures','Volumes','Compositions','Proportions','NEW','11-016','11-016',NULL),
(10604,'chemistry','Combining of what was not provided in Dalton\'s theory?','Atoms','Dalton’s theory could not provide the reason for combining of atoms.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 16 Paragraph-6)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Dalton\'s Atomic Theory',NULL,'1','mcq','Matter','Volumes','Atoms','Compounds','NEW','11-016','11-016',NULL),
(10605,'biology','Assertion: Potato and Brinjal will share common characteristics in the taxa Phylum.\n \nReason: Both of them belong to the same Phylum Solanum','Assertion and Reason are false','Potato and brinjal are two different species but both belong to the genus Solanum.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no:9 , Paragraph : 3 )\nFirstly solanum is a genus and automatically the reason and assertion  is wrong.','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-009','11-006',NULL),
(10606,'biology','Assertion: Museums have collections of preserved plant and animal specimens for study and Reference  .\n\nReason: This is to allow education of the people about these organisms.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Biological museums are generally set up in educational institutes such as schools and colleges. Museums have collections of preserved plant and animal specimens for study and Reference  .\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no:12 , Paragraph : 3 )','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-012',NULL,NULL),
(10607,'biology','Assertion: Museums allow people to have the chance to view specimens of organisms and educate themselves.\n \nReason: This is the reason why they are set up in government buildings to allow easy access.','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Biological museums are generally set up in educational institutes such as schools and colleges. Museums have collections of preserved plant and animal specimens for study and Reference  .Specimens are preserved in the containers or jars in preservative solutions. Plant and animal specimens may also be preserved as dry specimens. Insects are preserved in insect boxes after collecting, killing and pinning. Larger animals like birds and mammals are usually stuffed and preserved. Museums often have collections of skeletons of animals too.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no:12 , Paragraph : 3 )','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-012',NULL,NULL),
(10608,'biology','Assertionn : Taxonomic studies are carried out from the actual specimens which are collected from the field and preserved\n\nReason : Taxonomic keys are tools that help in identification based on characteristics.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','These studies are carried out from the actual specimens which are collected from the field and preserved as referrals in the form of herbaria, museums and in botanical gardens and zoological parks.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no:1 , Paragraph : 3 )\nTaxonomic keys are tools that help in identification based on characteristics.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no:1 , Paragraph : 4 )','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-011',NULL,NULL),
(10609,'biology','Assertion: In both plants and animals growth is indeterminate\n\n Reason: All kind of cell division finally leads to growth.','Both assertion and reason are false .','A multicellular organism grows by cell division. In plants, this growth by cell division occurs continuously throughout their life span. In animals, this growth is seen only up to a certain age. However, cell division occurs in certain tissues to replace lost cells.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no:4 , Paragraph : 1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Both assertion and reason are false .','NEW','11-004',NULL,NULL),
(10610,'biology','Assertion: Some organisms can reproduce either sexually or asexually. \n\nReason: This is determined by the availability of resources and the atmosphere around them.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','In multicellular organisms, reproduction refers to the production of progeny possessing features more or less similar to those of parents. Invariably and implicitly we refer to sexual reproduction. Organisms reproduce by asexual means also.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no:4 , Paragraph : 2)\nReproduction method is affected by the resources available .','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-004',NULL,NULL),
(10611,'biology','Assertion: Metabolism though fundamental for life has exceptions. \n\nReasons: Dormant organisms don\'t require energy to survive.','Assertion and Reason are false','Another characteristic of life is metabolism. All living organisms are made of chemicals. Metabolism is a defining feature of all living organisms without exception.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no:4 and 5 , Paragraph : 3)\nOrganism undergo dormancy or hibernation also require energy for their basic metabolism that keeps them alive.They use up energy ehich is already stored in some froms.','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-004',NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(10612,'biology','Assertion: Not all organisms can reproduce sexually. \n\nReason; This is due to the lack of ability of some organisms to produce gametes.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','All organisms does not produce gametes . Some reproduce by simple asexual methods like budding , fragmentation which does not involves the formation of gametes\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 )','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10613,'biology','Assertion: Museums serve as a quick referral system in taxonomic studies. \n\nReason: Insects are collected ,killed and pinned in insect boxes but they are not preserved as a part in museums','Assertion and Reason are false','Biological museums are generally set up in educational institutes such as schools and colleges. Museums have collections of preserved plant and animal specimens for study and Reference  . Specimens are preserved in the containers or jars in preservative solutions. Plant and animal specimens may also be preserved as dry specimens. Insects are preserved in insect boxes after collecting, killing and pinning. Larger animals like birds and mammals are usually stuffed and preserved. Museums often have collections of skeletons of animals too. Herbaria serve as quick referral systems in taxonomical studies.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no:12 , Paragraph : 3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-012',NULL,NULL),
(10614,'biology','Assertion: All organisms are linked to one another by the presence of a common genetic code. \n\nReason: The genetic code ultimately gives rise to a chain of amino-acids that are the fundamental to forming proteins in all organisms.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','All living organisms – present, past and future, are linked to one another by the sharing of the common genetic material, but to varying degrees.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ;page : 5 , paragraph : 4)\nAll living organism are linked  to one another to varying degrees because they some common genetic materail . Genetic material are build up from genetic codes . Genetic codes give rise to amino acids which form proteins and hence the character is expressed.','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10615,'biology','Assertion: Organisms differ from one another. \n\nReason: This is because each organism has a genetic code that codes for organism-specific amino acids.','Assertion is true, Reason is false','All living organisms – present, past and future, are linked to one another by the sharing of the common genetic material, but to varying degrees.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ;page : 5 , paragraph : 4)\nAll living organism are linked  to one another to varying degrees because they some common genetic material . Genetic material are build up from genetic codes . Genetic codes give rise to amino acids which form proteins and hence a particular character is expressed. A particular code codes for the same amino acid whatever the organism may be . Therefore we cant tell that the codes are organism specific.','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10616,'biology','Assertion: Properties of tissues are not present in the constituent cells\n \nReason: This is due to numerous interactions among the constituent cells.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Properties of tissues are not present in the constituent cells but arise as a result of interactions among the constituent cells. Similarly, properties of cellular organelles are not present in the molecular constituents of the organelle but arise as a result of interactions among the molecular components comprising the organelle.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no:5 , Paragraph : 3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10617,'biology','Assertion: It is necessary to devise some means to make classification and study of organisms possible and easy, under Taxonomy\n\nReason: It is nearly impossible to study all living organisms without a systematic approach.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Since it is nearly impossible to study all the living organisms, it is necessary to devise some means to make this possible. This process is classification.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no: 7 , Paragraph : 3)\nHence, based on characteristics, all living organisms can be classified into different taxa. This process of classification is taxonomy.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no: 7 , Paragraph : 4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-007','11-005',NULL),
(10618,'biology','Assertion: Zoologic parks are essential for learning more about organisms. \n\nReason: They enable us to learn about their food habits and behaviour.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','These are the places where wild animals are kept in protected environments under human care and which enable us to learn about their food habits and behaviour. All animals in a zoo are provided, as far as possible, the conditions similar to their natural habitats.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no: 12 , Paragraph : 1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-012',NULL,NULL),
(10619,'biology','Assertion: Each taxon represents a unit of classification. \n\nReason: Classification is not a single step process but involves hierarchy of steps in which each step represents a rank or category.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Classification is not a single step process but involves hierarchy of steps in which each step represents a rank or category.\nEach taxon represents a unit of classification. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11 ; Page no: 8 , Paragraph : 4,6)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is not the correct explanation of assertion','Assertion is true, Reason is false','Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-008','11-005',NULL),
(10620,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding characteristics of living organism. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. All living organisms grow.\n\n2. Reproduction is a characteristic of living organisms.\n\n3. The least complicated feature of all living organisms is the ability to sense their surroundings or environment and respond to these inner and outer environmental stimuli\n\n4. One characteristic of life is metabolism.','1,2,and 4 are correct','All living organisms grow. Reproduction is a characteristic of living organisms. The most obvious and technically complicated feature of all living organisms is this ability to sense their surroundings or environment and respond to these environmental stimuli. One characteristic of life is metabolism. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 3-5; section 1.1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are correct','1,2,and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10621,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding living organisms. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. The environmental stimuli could be physical, chemical or biological.\n\n2. Increase in mass and increase in number of individuals are twin characteristics of growth.\n\n3. All living organisms are made of chemicals and energy. \n\n4. In multicellular organisms, reproduction refers to the production of progeny possessing features very different to those of parents.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','The environmental stimuli which could be physical, chemical or biological. Increase in mass and increase in number of individuals are twin characteristics of growth. All living organisms are made of chemicals. In multicellular organisms, reproduction refers to the production of progeny possessing features more or less similar to those of parents. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 3-5; section 1.1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10622,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding living beings. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Unicellular organisms grow by cell division.\n\n2. Fungi multiply and spread easily due to the millions of asexual spores they produce.\n\n3. A multicellular organism grows by cell division.\n\n4. The chemicals in living organisms, small and big, belonging to various classes, sizes, functions, etc., are constantly being made and changed into some other biomolecules.','All 1,2,3 and 4 are correct.','Unicellular organisms grow by cell division. These chemicals, small and big, belonging to various classes, sizes, functions, etc., are constantly being made and changed into some other biomolecules. A multicellular organism also grows by cell division. Fungi multiply and spread easily due to the millions of asexual spores they produce.  \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 3-5; section 1.1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','All 1,2,3 and 4 are correct.','2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10623,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding characteristics of living organisms. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. There are thousands of metabolic reactions occurring simultaneously inside and outside all living organisms,\n\n2. We sense our environment through our sense organs.\n\n3. In lower organisms like saccharomyces and hydra, we observe budding.\n\n4. In plants, the growth by cell division occurs continuously throughout their life span.','Only 1 is incorrect','There are thousands of metabolic reactions occurring simultaneously inside all living organisms. We sense our environment through our sense organs. In lower organisms like yeast(saccharomyces) and hydra, we observe budding. In plants, the growth by cell division occurs continuously throughout their life span. (Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 3-5; section 1.1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10624,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding organisms. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Plants respond to external factors like light, water, temperature, other organisms, pollutants, etc.\n\n2. In animals, this growth is seen only up to a certain age.\n\n3. In Planaria (round worms), we observe true regeneration.\n\n4. All plants, animals, fungi and microbes exhibit metabolism.','only 3 is incorrect','Plants respond to external factors like light, water, temperature, other organisms, pollutants, etc. In animals, this growth is seen only up to a certain age. All plants, animals, fungi and microbes exhibit metabolism. In Planaria (flat worms), we observe true regeneration. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 3-5; section 1.1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1and 2 are correct.','1,2 3, and 4 are correct.','only 3 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect.','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10625,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding growth. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Non-living objects also grow if we take increase in body mass as a criterion for growth.\n\n2. In living organisms, growth is from inside.\n\n3. In majority of higher animals and plants, growth and reproduction are mutually exclusive events.\n\n4. Growth can be taken as a defining property of living organisms.','1,2 and 3 are correct','In majority of higher animals and plants, growth and reproduction are mutually exclusive events. One must remember that increase in body mass is considered as growth. Non-living objects also grow if we take increase in body mass as a criterion for growth. Mountains, boulders and sand mounds do grow. However, this kind of growth exhibited by non-living objects is by accumulation of material on the surface. In living organisms, growth is from inside. Growth, therefore, cannot be taken as a defining property of living organisms. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 4; Paragraph no; 1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','1,2 and 3 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-004',NULL,NULL),
(10626,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding reproduction in living. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Reproduction cannot be an all-inclusive defining characteristic of living organisms.\n\n2. There are many organisms which do not reproduce (mules, sterile worker bees, infertile human couples, etc).\n\n3. When it comes to unicellular organisms like bacteria, unicellular algae (volvox) or Amoeba, reproduction is not synonymous with growth, i.e., increase in number of cells.\n\n4. The fungi, the filamentous algae, the protozoa of mosses, all cannot multiply by fragmentation.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','There are many organisms which do not reproduce (mules, sterile worker bees, infertile human couples, etc). When it comes to unicellular organisms like bacteria, unicellular algae or Amoeba, reproduction is synonymous with growth, i.e., increase in number of cells. volvox is a multicellular algae. The fungi, the filamentous algae, the protonema of mosses, all easily multiply by fragmentation. reproduction also cannot be an all-inclusive defining characteristic of living organisms. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 4; Paragraph no 2)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','only 4 is incorrect','NEW','11-004',NULL,NULL),
(10627,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding metabolism. Choose the correct option.\n \n1. The sum total of all the chemical reactions occurring in our body is metabolism.\n\n2. Metabolic reactions can be demonstrated outside the body in cell-free systems.\n\n3. Metabolism is a defining feature of all living organisms with some exceptions.\n\n4. Cellular organisation of the body is not the defining feature of life forms.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','The sum total of all the chemical reactions occurring in our body is metabolism. No non-living object exhibits metabolism. Metabolic reactions can be demonstrated outside the body in cell-free systems. An isolated metabolic reaction(s) outside the body of an organism, performed in a test tube is neither living nor non-living. Hence, while metabolism is a defining feature of all living organisms without exception, isolated metabolic reactions in vitro are not living things but surely living reactions. Hence, cellular organisation of the body is the defining feature of life forms.\n (Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 5; Paragraph no 1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect.','Only 3 is incorrect.','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10628,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding consiousness. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. All organisms, from the prokaryotes to the most complex eukaryotes can sense and respond to environmental cues.\n\n2. Photoperiod affects reproduction in seasonal breeders in plants only. \n\n3. All organisms are not ‘aware’ of their surroundings.\n\n4. Human being is the only organism who is aware of himself, i.e., has self-consciousness.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','All organisms, from the prokaryotes to the most complex eukaryotes can sense and respond to environmental cues. Photoperiod affects reproduction in seasonal breeders, both plants and animals. All organisms handle chemicals entering their bodies. All organisms therefore, are ‘aware’ of their surroundings. Human being is the only organism who is aware of himself, i.e., has self-consciousness. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 5; Paragraph no 2)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','only 1 is correct','only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10629,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding living cells. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Properties of cellular organelles are not present in the molecular constituents of the organelle but arise as a result of interactions among the molecular components comprising the organelle.\n\n2. Properties of tissues are not present in the constituent cells but arise as a result of interactions among the constituent cells.\n\n3. All living phenomena are due to underlying interactions.\n\n4. These interactions result in emergent properties at a lower level of organisation.','Only 4 is incorrect','All living phenomena are due to underlying interactions. Properties of tissues are not present in the constituent cells but arise as a result of interactions among the constituent cells. Similarly, properties of cellular organelles are not present in the molecular constituents of the organelle but arise as a result of interactions among the molecular components comprising the organelle. These interactions result in emergent properties at a higher level of organisation. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 5; Paragraph no 4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 4 is incorrect','1, 2 and 3 are incorrect','only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct.','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10630,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding organisms. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Living organisms are self-replicating, evolving and self-regulating interactive systems capable of responding to external stimuli.\n\n2. Biology is the story of life on earth.\n\n3. All living organisms – present, past and future, are neither linked to one another nor they share any common genetic material.\n \n4. Biology is the story of evolution of living organisms on earth.','1,2 and 4 are correct','Living organisms are self-replicating, evolving and self-regulating interactive systems capable of responding to external stimuli. Biology is the story of life on earth. Biology is the story of evolution of living organisms on earth. All living organisms – present, past and future, are linked to one another by the sharing of the common genetic material, but to varying degrees. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 5; Paragraph no 4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','Only 1 is incorrect.','1,2 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','NEW','11-005',NULL,NULL),
(10631,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding diversity of living organisms. Choose the correct option.\n \n1. The number of species that are known and described range between 17-18 million. \n\n2. The local names do not vary from place to place, within a country.\n\n3. Nomenclature is naming of living organisms such that a particular organism is known by the same name all over the world.\n\n4. Nomenclature or naming is also possible when the organism is not described correctly.','only 3 is correct','The number of species that are known and described range between 1.7-1.8 million. The local names would vary from place to place, even within a country. Nomenclature is naming of living organisms such that a particular organism is known by the same name all over the world. Nomenclature or naming is only possible when the organism is described correctly. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 6; section 1.2 )','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are correct','only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-006',NULL,NULL),
(10632,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomical aids. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Museums often have collections of skeletons of animals too.\n\n2. Zoological parks are the places where wild animals are kept in protected environments under human care. \n\n3. Only one taxonomic key is required for all taxonomic category such as family, genus and species for identification purposes.\n\n4. Taxonomic studies of various species of plants, animals and other organisms are useful in agriculture, forestry, industry and in general in knowing our bio-resources and their diversity.','1,2 and 4 are correct','Separate taxonomic keys are required for each taxonomic category such as family, genus and species for identification purposes. Museums often have collections of skeletons of animals too. Zoological parks are the places where wild animals are kept in protected environments under human care. Taxonomic studies of various species of plants, animals and other organisms are useful in agriculture, forestry, industry and in general in knowing our bio-resources and their diversity. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 11-13; Section 1.4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','Only 1 is incorrect.','1,2 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','OLD','11-013',NULL,NULL),
(10633,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomical aids. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Keys are generally analytical in nature.\n\n2. In key there is acceptance of only one and rejection of the other.\n\n3. Zoological parks enable us to learn about their food habits and behaviour.\n\n4. Identification of organisms requires intensive laboratory and field studies.','All 1,2,3 and 4 are correct','Keys are generally analytical in nature. In key there is acceptance of only one and rejection of the other. Zoological parks enable us to learn about their food habits and behaviour. Identification of organisms requires intensive laboratory and field studies. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 11-13; Section 1.4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct','All 1,2,3 and 4 are correct','2,3 and 4 are correct','DELETED','11-013',NULL,NULL),
(10634,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomical aids. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. The collection of actual specimens of plant and animal species is essential and is the secondary source of taxonomic studies after collecting photographic information.\n\n2. All animals in a zoo are provided, as far as possible, the conditions similar to their natural habitats.\n\n3. Key represents the choice made between two opposite options.\n\n4. In museum, larger animals like birds and mammals are usually stuffed and preserved.','Only 1 is incorrect','The collection of actual specimens of plant and animal species is essential and is the prime source of taxonomic studies.  All animals in a zoo are provided, as far as possible, the conditions similar to their natural habitats. Key represents the choice  made between two opposite options. Larger animals like birds and mammals are usually stuffed and preserved. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 11-13; Section 1.4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','only 2 is incorrect','DELETED','11-013',NULL,NULL),
(10635,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomical aids. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Taxonomical aids are also fundamental to studies and essential for training in systematics.\n\n2. Museums have collections of preserved plant and animal specimens for study and Reference  .\n\n3. Each statement in the key is called a Lock.\n\n4. Children love visiting these parks, commonly called Zoos.','only 3 is incorrect','Taxonomical aids are also fundamental to studies and essential for training in systematics. Museums have collections of preserved plant and animal specimens for study and Reference  . Children love visiting these parks, commonly called Zoos Each statement in the key is called a lead. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 11-13; Section 1.4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','1,2 3, and 4 are correct.','only 3 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect.','DELETED','11-011',NULL,NULL),
(10636,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomical aids. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Biological museums are generally set up in educational institutes such as schools and colleges.\n\n2. Flora, manuals, monographs and catalogues are some other means of recording descriptions.\n\n3. Taxonomical aids is used for classification of an organism, and the information gathered is also stored along with the specimens.\n\n4. The keys are based on the contrasting characters generally four in a pair called couplet.','1,2 and 3 are correct','The keys are based on the contrasting characters generally in a pair called couplet. Flora, manuals, monographs and catalogues are some other means of recording descriptions. Taxonomical aids is used for classification of an organism, and the information gathered is also stored along with the specimens. Biological museums are generally set up in educational institutes such as schools and colleges. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 11-13; Section 1.4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','1,2 and 3 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','DELETED','11-011',NULL,NULL),
(10637,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomical aids. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Herbarium is a store house of collected plant specimens that are dried, pressed and preserved on sheets.\n\n2. In museum, plant and animal specimens may also be preserved as dry specimens.\n\n3. The famous botanical gardens are at Kiev (U.S), Indian Botanical Garden, Howrah (India) and at National Botanical Research Institute, Uttarkhand (India).\n\n4. The herbarium sheets also carry a label providing information about date, day, and place of collection, English, and botanical names, family, collector’s name','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','Herbarium is a store house of collected plant specimens that are dried, pressed and preserved on sheets. The herbarium sheets also carry a label providing information about date and place of collection, English, local and botanical names, family, collector’s name. The famous botanical gardens are at Kew (England), Indian Botanical Garden, Howrah (India) and at National Botanical Research Institute, Lucknow (India). In museum, plant and animal specimens may also be preserved as dry specimens. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 11-13; Section 1.4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Only 1 is incorrect.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','only 4 is incorrect','DELETED','11-011',NULL,NULL),
(260236,'chemistry','Write the correct IUPAC NAME of the given compound in diagram?(n26007)','4-fluoroacetophenone','The IUPAC name shows that fluorine group is present at 4th position of phenyl ring & acetone is attached to the first position of phenyl ring. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'3','mcq','4-fluoroacetophenyl ketone','4-fluoroacetylphenone','4-fluoroacylphenone','4-fluoroacetophenone','NEW','12-359','12-229','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260236q.png'),
(10638,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomical aids. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Flora, manuals, monographs and catalogues help in correct identification.\n\n2. Flora contains the actual account of habitat and distribution of plants of a given area.\n \n3. Manuals are useful in providing information for identification of names of species found in an area.\n\n4. Monographs contain information on all the species of any one class.','1,2 and 3 are correct','Flora, manuals, monographs and catalogues are some other means of recording descriptions. They also help in correct identification. Flora contains the actual account of habitat and distribution of plants of a given area. These provide the index to the plant species found in a particular area. Manuals are useful in providing information for identification of names of species found in an area. Monographs contain information on any one taxon. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 11; Paragraph no 1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','1,2 and 3 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','DELETED','11-011',NULL,NULL),
(10639,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomical aids. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Plant species in botanical gardens are grown for identification purposes and each plant is labelled indicating its botanical/scientific name and its family.\n\n2. Key is another taxonomical aid used for identification of plants and animals based on the similarities and dissimilarities.\n\n3. Herbarium sheets are arranged according to a Nationally accepted system of classification.\n\n4. In museum, Specimens are preserved in the containers or jars in water solutions.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Plant species in these gardens are grown for identification purposes and each plant is labelled indicating its botanical/scientific name and its family. Key is another taxonomical aid used for identification of plants and animals based on the similarities and dissimilarities. In museum, specimens are preserved in the containers or jars in preservative solutions. Herbarium sheets are arranged according to a universally accepted system of classification. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 11-13; Section 1.4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are correcT','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect.','Only 3 is incorrect.','DELETED','11-011',NULL,NULL),
(10640,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomical aids. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. The specimens, along with their descriptions on herbarium sheets, become a store house or repository for future use.\n\n2. Insects are preserved live in insect boxes after collecting, and pinning.\n\n3. Botanical gardens are specialised gardens that have collections of living plants for food production.\n\n4. Herbaria serve as quick referral systems in taxonomical studies.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','The specimens, along with their descriptions on herbarium sheets, become a store house or repository for future use. Botanical gardens are specialised gardens have collections of living plants for Reference  . Herbaria also serve as quick referral systems in taxonomical studies. Insects are preserved in insect boxes after collecting, killing and pinning. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 11-13; Section 1.4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Assertion Reason  and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','only 1 is correct','only 2 is incorrect','DELETED','11-011',NULL,NULL),
(10701,'chemistry','One atomic mass unit is defined as a mass exactly equal to \'X\' of the mass of one carbon atom. \'X\' is-','one-twelfth','One atomic mass unit is defined as a mass exactly equal to one-twelfth of the mass of one carbon atom.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 16 Paragraph-last)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Atomic And Molecular Masses',NULL,'1','mcq','one-sixth','one-twelfth','one-third','one-fourth','NEW','11-016','11-016',NULL),
(10702,'chemistry','Carbon has the following three isotopes with relative abundances and masses (amu), what will be the average atomic mass of carbon, calculate from the diagram?(n0106)','12.011 u','From the above data, the average atomic mass of carbon will come out to be:\n \n (0.98892) (12 u) + (0.01108) (13.00335 u) + (2 × 10⁻¹²) (14.00317 u) \n \n = 12.011 u.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 17 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Atomic And Molecular Masses',NULL,'2','mcq','12.000 u','12.011 u','12.015 u','13.000 u','NEW','11-017','11-017','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/10702q.png'),
(10703,'chemistry','Calculate the atomic mass (average) of Chlorine using the following data given in diagram(n0107)','35.4527 u','Average atomic mass is given by (percentage abundance x atomic weight) / 100\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 17 Paragraph-4) diagram(ne0108)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Atomic And Molecular Masses',NULL,'3','mcq','35.1968 u','35.4527 u','36.244 u','35.901 u','NEW','11-017','11-017','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/10703q.png'),
(10704,'chemistry','Calculate the atomic mass (average) of argon using the following data given in diagram(n0108)?','39.948 u','Molar mass of Ar = 35.96755 x 0.00337 + 37.96272 x 0.00063 + 39.96924 x 0.99600\n \n = 39.948 g /mol.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 17 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Atomic And Molecular Masses',NULL,'3','mcq','39.892 u','39.91 u','39.948 u','39.978 u','NEW','11-017','11-017','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/10704q.png'),
(10705,'chemistry','In Sodium chloride, what is one Na⁺ ion surrounded by ?','6 Cl⁻ ions','It may be noted that in sodium chloride, one Na⁺ ion is surrounded by six Cl⁻ ion and vice-versa.\n \n NaCl is a formula mass and doesn\'t represents no.of atoms actually present in unit cell.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 17 Paragraph-8)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Atomic And Molecular Masses',NULL,'2','mcq','2 Cl⁻ ions','4 Cl⁻ ions','12 Cl⁻ ions','6 Cl⁻ ions','NEW','11-017','11-017',NULL),
(10706,'chemistry','Calculate the molecular mass of Glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) molecule.','180.162 u','Molecular mass of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) = 6(12.011 u) + 12(1.008 u) + 6(16.00 u) \n \n = (72.066 u) + (12.096 u) + (96.00 u) \n \n = 180.162 u. \n \n Molecular mass is written in terms of u or amu. 1 amu = 1.66 x 10⁻²⁴g.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Problem-1.1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Atomic And Molecular Masses',NULL,'3','mcq','180 u','183.162 u','190 u','180.162 u','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(10707,'biology','Assertion: Aristotle used Systema Naturae as the title of his publication.\n\nReason: Systematics takes into account evolutionary and behavioral relationships between organisms.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Linnaeus used Systema Naturae as the title of his publication. Systematics takes into account evolutionary relationships between organisms. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 8; Paragraph no 4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-008','11-005',NULL),
(10708,'biology','Assertion: The ‘animals’, ‘mammals’, ‘dogs’ are all taxa\n\nReason: The ‘animals’, ‘mammals’ and ‘dogs’ represent taxa at different levels.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The ‘animals’, ‘mammals’, ‘dogs’ are all taxa – but you know that a dog is a mammal and mammals are animals. Therefore, ‘animals’, ‘mammals’ and ‘dogs’ represent taxa at different levels. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 7; Paragraph no 4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-007','11-005',NULL),
(10709,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding scientific names. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Binomial nomenclature system is given by Carl Linnaeus.\n\n2. Each name has two components – the Generic name and the specific epithet.\n\n3. They also ensure that such a name has not been used more than twice for any other known organism.\n\n4. Description of any organism should enable the people (in any part of the world) to arrive at the same name.','1,2 and 4 are correct','The scientific names ensure that each organism has only one name. Description of any organism should enable the people (in any part of the world) to arrive at the same name. They also ensure that such a name has not been used for any other known organism. Biologists follow universally accepted principles to provide scientific names to known organisms. Each name has two components – the Generic name and the specific epithet. This system of providing a name with two components is called Binomial nomenclature. This naming system given by Carolus Linnaeus is being practised by biologists all over the world. (Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 6; Paragraph no 4 and 5)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','1,2 and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 4 are correct','2,3 and 4 are correct.','NEW','11-006','11-005',NULL),
(10710,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding scientific names. Choose the CORRECT OPTION.\n\n1. Both the words in a biological name, when handwritten, are separately underlined.\n\n2. The first word in a biological name represents the specific epithet while the second component denotes the genus.\n\n3. The first word denoting the genus starts with a capital letter while the specific epithet starts with a small letter.\n\n4. Biological names are generally in Latin and written in italized script.','Only 2 is INCORRECT','Biological names are generally in Latin and written in italics. They are Latinised or derived from Latin irrespective of their origin. The first word in a biological name represents the genus while the second component denotes the specific epithet. Both the words in a biological name, when handwritten, are separately underlined, or printed in italics to indicate their Latin origin. The first word denoting the genus starts with a capital letter while the specific epithet starts with a small letter. It can be illustrated with the example of Mangifera indica. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 7; Paragraph no 1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is INCORRECT','Both 1 and 3 are INCORRECT','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-007','11-004',NULL),
(10711,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding classification. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Classification is the process by which anything is grouped into convenient categories based on some easy experimented characters.\n\n2. The scientific term for these categories is taxa.\n\n3. The ‘animals’, ‘mammals’ and ‘dogs’ represent taxa at different levels.\n\n4. All living organisms can be classified into different taxa. This process of classification is taxonomy.','2,3 and 4 are correct','Classification is the process by which anything is grouped into convenient categories based on some easily observable characters. The scientific term for these categories is taxa. The ‘animals’, ‘mammals’ and ‘dogs’ represent taxa at different levels. All living organisms can be classified into different taxa. This process of classification is taxonomy. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 7; Paragraph no 3 and 4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','2,3 and 4 are correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are correct.','NEW','11-007','11-004',NULL),
(10712,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomy. Choose the CORRECT option. \n\n1. Study of different kinds of organisms and their diversities, also the relationships among them is referred to as systematics. \n\n2. Characterisation, identification, classification and nomenclature these processes are not required for taxonomy.\n\n3. External and internal structure, along with the structure of cell, development process and ecological evolution of organisms aren\'t essential to form the basis of modern taxonomic studies.\n\n4. The word systematics is derived from the Latin word ‘systema’ which means systematic arrangement of organisms.','Both 1 and 4 are correct','External and internal structure, along with the structure of cell, development process and ecological information of organisms are essential and form the basis of modern taxonomic studies. Hence, characterisation, identification, classification and nomenclature are the processes that are basic to taxonomy. Human beings were, since long, not only interested in knowing more about different kinds of organisms and their diversities, but also the relationships among them. This branch of study was referred to as systematics. The word systematics is derived from the Latin word ‘systema’ which means systematic arrangement of organisms. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 8; Paragraph no 1,2 and 4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-008','11-005',NULL),
(10713,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomy. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Classification is not a single step process but involves hierarchy of steps in which each step represents a rank or category.\n\n2. Each category, referred to as a unit of classification, in fact, represents a rank and is commonly termed as taxanim.\n\n3. All categories together constitute the taxonomic hierarchy.\n\n4. Category is a part of overall taxonomic arrangement, it is called the taxonomic assemblage.','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Classification is not a single step process but involves hierarchy of steps in which each step represents a rank or category. Since the category is a part of overall taxonomic arrangement, it is called the taxonomic category and all categories together constitute the taxonomic hierarchy. Each category, referred to as a unit of classification, in fact, represents a rank and is commonly termed as taxon. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 8; Paragraph no 4)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2 and 3 are correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','1,3 and 4 are correct.','NEW','11-008','11-005',NULL),
(10714,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomic categories  Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Taxonomic studies consider a group of individual organisms with fundamental similarities as a species.\n\n2. Order is the assemblage of families which exhibit a few similar characters.\n\n3. Genus Panthera, comprising lion, tiger, leopard is put along with genus, Felis (cats) in the family canidae.\n\n4. Classes comprising animals like fishes, amphibians, reptiles, birds along with mammals constitute the next higher category called Phylum.','Only 3 is incorrect.','Genus Panthera, comprising lion, tiger, leopard is put along with genus, Felis (cats) in the family Felidae. Taxonomic studies consider a group of individual organisms with fundamental similarities as a species. Order being a higher category, is the assemblage of families which exhibit a few similar characters. Classes comprising animals like fishes, amphibians, reptiles, birds along with mammals constitute the next higher category called Phylum. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 9-10; Section 1.3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect.','Both 1 and 3 are correct','1, 3 and 4 are incorrect.','NEW','11-009','11-007',NULL),
(10715,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding  taxonomic categories. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. The similar characters are less in number in order as compared to different genera included in a family.\n\n2. Three different genera Solanum, Petunia and Datura are placed in the family Solanaceae. \n\n3. All the classes of Division Chordata, based on the common features like presence of notochord, nerves and dorsal hollow neural system, are included in division Chordata.\n\n4. One should be able to distinguish one species from the other closely related species based on the distinct physiological differences.','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','The similar characters are less in number in order as compared to different genera included in a family. Three different genera Solanum, Petunia and Datura are placed in the family Solanaceae. All these, based on the common features like presence of notochord and dorsal hollow neural system, are included in phylum Chordata. One should be able to distinguish one species from the other closely related species based on the distinct morphological differences. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 9-10; Section 1.3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','Both 3 and 4 are correct','1,2 and 3 are correct.','NEW','11-009','11-007',NULL),
(10716,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding  taxonomic categories. Choose the CORRECT option. \n\n1. All the three names, indica, tuberosum and leo, represent the specific epithets\n\n2. In case of plants, classes with a few similar characters are assigned to a higher category called Division.\n\n3. Families are characterised on the basis of only reproductive features of plant species.\n\n4. Plant families like Convolvulaceae, Solanaceae are included in the order Polymoniales mainly based on the floral characters.','1,2,and 4 are correct','Families are characterised on the basis of both vegetative and reproductive features of plant species. In case of plants, classes with a few similar characters are assigned to a higher category called Division. All the three names, indica, tuberosum and leo, represent the specific epithets. Plant families like Convolvulaceae, Solanaceae are included in the order Polymoniales mainly based on the floral characters. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 9-10; Section 1.3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are correct','1,2,and 4 are correct','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-009','11-007',NULL),
(10717,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding  taxonomic categories. Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Family, has a group of related genera with still less number of similarities as compared to genus and species.\n\n2. All animals belonging to various phyla are assigned to the highest category called Kingdom Animalia in the classification system of animals.\n\n3. The animal order, Carnivora, includes families like Primidae, Felidae and Canidae.\n\n4. Mangifera, Solanum and Panthera are genera and represents another lower level of taxon or category than species','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','Family, has a group of related genera with still less number of similarities as compared to genus and species. All animals belonging to various phyla are assigned to the highest category called Kingdom Animalia in the classification system of animals. The animal order, Carnivora, includes families like Felidae and Canidae. Mangifera, Solanum and Panthera are genera and represents another higher level of taxon or category than species. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 9-10; Section 1.3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','1 and 3 is incorrect.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-009','11-007',NULL),
(10718,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding  taxonomic categories.  Choose the correct option. \n\n1. We can say that genera are aggregates of distantly related species.\n\n2. The Kingdom Plantae, on the other hand, is distinct, and comprises all plants from various divisions.\n\n3. Class includes related orders.\n\n4. Each genus may have one or more than one specific epithets representing different organisms, but having morphological similarities.','Only 1 is incorrect','We can say that genera are aggregates of closely related species. The Kingdom Plantae, on the other hand, is distinct, and comprises all plants from various divisions. Class includes related orders. Each genus may have one or more than one specific epithets representing different organisms, but having morphological similarities. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 9-10; Section 1.3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect.','only 2 is incorrect','NEW','11-009',NULL,NULL),
(10719,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding taxonomic categories Choose the correct option. \n\n1. Panthera has another specific epithet called Tigris and Solanum includes species like Nigrum and Melongena.\n\n2. Genus comprises a group of related species which has more characters in common in comparison to species of other genera.\n\n3. Class Primata  includes order carnivora and mammalia\n\n4. Taxonomists have also developed sub-categories in this hierarchy to facilitate more sound and scientific placement of various taxa.','only 3 is incorrect','Panthera has another specific epithet called tigris and Solanum includes species like nigrum and melongena. Genus comprises a group of related species which has more characters in common in comparison to species of other genera. Order Primata comprising monkey, gorilla and gibbon is placed in class. Mammalia along with order Carnivora that includes animals like tiger, cat and dog. taxonomists have also developed sub-categories in this hierarchy to facilitate more sound and scientific placement of various taxa. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 9-10; Section 1.3)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect.','1,2 and 4 are incorrect.','only 3 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect.','NEW','11-010','11-008',NULL),
(10720,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding common characteristics in taxa. Choose the CORRECT option.\n \n1. As we go higher from species to kingdom, the number of common characteristics goes on decreasing\n\n2. Lower the taxa, more are the characteristics that the members within the taxon share.\n\n3. Higher the category, greater is the difficulty of determining the relationship to other taxa at the same level.\n\n4.Higher the category ,more easy it becomes to determine the relation between the other taxa at the same level.','1,2 and 3 are correct','As we go higher from species to kingdom, the number of common characteristics goes on decreasing. Lower the taxa, more are the characteristics that the members within the taxon share. Higher the category, greater is the difficulty of determining the relationship to other taxa at the same level. Hence, the problem of classification becomes more complex. \n(Reference   NCERT biology class 11; Chapter 1; Page no 11; Paragraph no 1)','Diversity In Living World >> The Living World >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','Both 1 and 4 are correct.','1,2 and 3 are correct','1,2 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-011',NULL,NULL),
(100101,'chemistry','What are s-block elements?','The s-block elements of the periodic table are those in which the last electron enters the outermost s-orbital.','The s-block elements of the periodic table are those in which the last electron enters the outermost s-orbital.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','The s-block elements of the periodic table are those in which the first electron enters the innermost s-orbital.','The s-block elements of the periodic table are those in which the last electron enters the \'p\' or \'d\' orbital','The s-block elements of the periodic table are those in which the last electron enters the outermost shell.','The s-block elements of the periodic table are those in which the last electron enters the outermost s-orbital.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100102,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','P-subshell can accommodate only two electrons.','S-orbital can accommodate only two electrons. Orbitals can always carry only two electrons at max. P-subshell has 3 orbitals and can accomodate 6 electrons.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','S-orbital can accommodate only two electrons.','S-subshell can accommodate only one orbital','K shell can accomodate only two electrons.','P-subshell can accommodate only two electrons.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100103,'chemistry','How many groups are present in S-block?','Two','As the s-orbital can accommodate only two electrons, two groups (1 & 2) belong to the s-block of the periodic table. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Two','One','Six','Seven','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100104,'chemistry','Which of the following does not belong to 1st group of periodic table?','Radium','Group 1 of the periodic table consists of the elements: Lithium, Sodium, Potassium, Rubidium, Caesium and Francium.(Mentioned from lowest to highest atomic number). Radium is a Group-2 element.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Sodium','Radium','Caesium','Francium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100105,'chemistry','Which of the following belongs to group 1 of periodic table?','Fr','Group 1 of the periodic table consists of the elements: Lithium, Sodium, Potassium, Rubidium, Caesium and Francium.(Mentioned from lowest to highest atomic number) \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Ra','Fr','Rf','Nh','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100106,'chemistry','What are Group-1 elements also called?','Alkali metals','Group 1 of the periodic table are collectively known as the alkali metals. These are so called because they form hydroxides on reaction with water which are strongly alkaline in nature.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Alkaline earth metals','Alkali metals','Transistion elements','Inner transistion elements','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100107,'chemistry','Which of the following elements are not of group 2?','Re','The elements of Group 2 include Beryllium, Magnesium, Calcium, Strontium, Barium and Radium. Re stands for Rhenium and it is d block element.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Be','Ba','Re','Ra','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100108,'chemistry','Which among the following is not a S block element?','Co','The elements of Group 2 include Beryllium, Magnesium, Calcium, Strontium, Barium and Radium. Co stands for cobalt which is a d block element.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Sr','K','Be','Co','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100109,'chemistry','Arrange the following group 1 elements in increasing order of their atomic number.','Li < Rb < Cs < Fr','Group 1 of the periodic table consists of the elements: Lithium, Sodium, Potassium, Rubidium, Caesium and Francium.(Mentioned from lowest to highest atomic number). So the correct answer is Li < Rb < Cs < Fr\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','Li < Fr < Cs < Rb','Li < Rb < Cs < Fr','Li < Cs < Rb < Fr','Li < Rb < Fr < Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100110,'chemistry','Arrange the following group 2 elements in increasing order of their atomic number.','Ca < Sr < Ba < Ra','The elements of Group 2 include Beryllium, Magnesium, Calcium, Strontium, Barium and Radium. (Mentioned from lowest to highest atomic number). SO the correct answer is Ca < Sr < Ba < Ra\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','Ca < Sr < Ra < Ba','Ca < Ra < Ba < Sr','Ca < Ba < Sr < Ra','Ca < Sr < Ba < Ra','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100111,'chemistry','What do alkali metals form on reaction with water?','Hydroxides','Group 1 of the periodic table are collectively known as the alkali metals. These are so called because they form hydroxides on reaction with water which are strongly alkaline in nature.\n\nReference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydroxides','Metal oxides','Superoxides','No reaction takes place','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100112,'chemistry','What is the nature of alkali metal hydroxides?','Strongly alkaline','Alkali metals form hydroxides on reaction with water which are strongly alkaline in nature.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Strongly acidic','Weakly acidic','Weakly alkaline','Strongly alkaline','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100113,'chemistry','Match appropriately','Group 1 element-Rb,Alkaline earth metal-Sr,Group 13 element-B,Radioactive-Fr','The correct match is-\n\nGroup 1 element - Rb\n\nGroup 13 element - B\n\nAlkaline earth metal - Sr\n\nRadioactive - Fr\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1,2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'3','match','Group 1 element,Rb','Alkaline earth metal,Sr','Group 13 element,B','Radioactive,Fr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100114,'chemistry','Which Group 2 element is not called as alkaline earth metal?','Beryllium','The elements of Group 2 with the exception of Beryllium are commonly known as the alkaline earth metals.\n\nAlkaline earth metals are so called because their oxides and hydroxides are alkaline in nature and these metal oxides are found in the earth’s crust. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Strontium','Barium','Beryllium','Radium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100115,'chemistry','Which among the following is abundant when compared to others?','Na','Among the alkali metals Sodium and Potassium are abundant and Lithium, Rubidium and Caesium have much lower abundances. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Li','Na','Rb','Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100116,'chemistry','What is the mass number of longest-lived isotope of Fr?','223','Francium is highly radioactive; its longest-lived isotope ²²³Fr has a mass of 223 u.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','213','230','221','223','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100117,'chemistry','What is the half life of the longest-lived isotope of Fr?','21 minutes','Francium is highly radioactive; its longest-lived isotope ²²³Fr has a half-life of only 21 minutes.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','21 days','21 minutes','21 seconds','21 years','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100118,'chemistry','Which among the following ranks fifth in the earth\'s crust?','Ca','Of the alkaline earth metals Calcium ranks fifth in abundance in the earth’s crust.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Mg','Ca','Ba','Be','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100119,'chemistry','Which among the following is the most abundant group 2 element?','Ca','Of the alkaline earth metals Calcium and Magnesium rank fifth and sixth in abundance respectively in the earth’s crust.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Mg','Ca','Na','Ba','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100120,'chemistry','Which among the following is the rarest Group 2 element?','Ra','Radium is the –10 rarest of all comprising only 10 per cent of igneous rocks\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Ba','Be','Ra','Sr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100121,'chemistry','Arrange the following group 2 elements in increasing order of their abundance.','Ra < Be < Mg < Ca','Of the alkaline earth metals Calcium and Magnesium rank fifth and sixth in abundance respectively in the earth’s crust. Strontium and Barium have much lower abundances. Beryllium is rare and Radium is the rarest of all comprising only 10¹⁰ per cent of igneous rocks\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'3','mcq','Ca < Be < Mg < Ra','Ra < Be < Ca < Mg','Ra < Be < Mg < Ca','Ra < Mg < Be < Ca','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100121q.png'),
(100122,'chemistry','Which element comprises 10⁻¹⁰ percent of igneous rocks?','Radium','Radium is the  rarest of all comprising only 10⁻¹⁰ per cent of igneous rocks\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Barium','Francium','Radium','Strontium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100122q.png'),
(100123,'chemistry','What are igneous rocks?','A type of rock formed from magma (molten rock) that has cooled and hardened.','A type of rock formed from magma (molten rock) that has cooled and hardened. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; footnote)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','A type of rock formed from magma (molten rock) that has cooled and hardened.','Rocks that often start as sediments carried in rivers and deposited in lakes and oceans.','Rocks that have started out as some other type of rock, but have been substantially changed from their original form.','Rocks like sandstone, limestone, shale etc','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100123q.png'),
(100124,'chemistry','What is the thin, rocky outer layer of the Earth called?','Crust','The thin, rocky outer layer of the Earth is crust. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; footnote)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Mantle','Lithosphere','Crust','Soil layer','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100124q.png'),
(100125,'chemistry','___ and ___, the first elements of Group 1 and Group 2 respectively exhibit some properties which are different from those of the other members of the respective group.','Lithium, Beryllium','Lithium and Beryllium, the first elements of Group 1 and Group 2 respectively exhibit some properties which are different from those of the other members of the respective group. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Lithium, Beryllium','Hydrogen, Beryllium','Lithium, Hydrogen','Berylium, Hydrogen','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100126,'chemistry','To which element does Lithium show diagonal similarity?','Magnesium','Lithium shows similarities to Magnesium in many of their properties. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Beryllium','Aluminium','Magnesium','Calcium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100127,'chemistry','To which element Beryllium does show diagonal similarity?','Aluminium','In the anomalous properties elements of second period resemble the second element of the following group. Thus, lithium shows similarities to magnesium and beryllium to aluminium in many of their properties. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Magnesium','Aluminium','Calcium','Lithium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100128,'chemistry','In diagonal relationship, what does the first element of group 2 resemble to?','It resembles second element of group 13','In the anomalous properties the elements resemble the second element of the following group. Thus, Beryllium-the first element of group 2 resembles to Aluminium-2nd element group 13  in many of their properties. \n\nReference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','It resembles second element of group 13','It resembles first element of group 13','It resembles second element of group 3','It resembles second element of group 1','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100129,'chemistry','Which of the following is false?','Radium is abundant in intracellular fluid.','Monovalent Sodium and Potassium ions and divalent Magnesium and Calcium ions are found in large proportions in biological fluids.Thus, statement "Radium is abundant in intracellular fluid" is false. All the other statements are true.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Ions of group 1 elements are monovalent.','Ions of group 2 elements are divalent.','Some S block ions perform important biological functions such as maintenance of ion balance and nerve impulse conduction.','Radium is abundant in intracellular fluid.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100130,'chemistry','Which of the following is not found in large proportions in biological fluids?','Be','Monovalent Sodium and Potassium ions and divalent Magnesium and Calcium ions are found in large proportions in biological fluids. Be is not found thus this option is eliminated.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Na','Mg','Ca','Be','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100131,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the duration of the cell cycle. Which of the following is/are correct?\n\n1. Human cells divide every 24 hours.\n\n2. Saccharomyces takes 90 minutes for a single cell division\n\n3. Skin cells of humans take a day to perform the cell division process.','All are correct','A typical eukaryotic cell cycle for eg. human cells take 24 hours to divide. Epithelial cells and skin cells are both human eukaryotic cells. Saccharomyces i.e. Yeast take 90 minutes to complete the process of cell division.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 163, Paragraph no 1)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Cell Cycle and Phases of Cell Cycle',NULL,'3','mcq','All are correct','only 1 is correct','1 and 3 are correct','only 2 is correct','NEW','11-163','11-121',NULL),
(100132,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding phases of the cell cycle. Which of the following is/are correct?\n\n1. The cell cycle has 2 phases Interphase and M (Meiotic) phase in all cells\n\n2. Sequence of cell cycle is M phase- Interphase- M phase.\n\n3. M phase starts with the division of cytoplasm (Karyokinesis) and ends with the separation of daughter chromosomes (Cytokinesis)','only 2 is correct','The cell cycle has 2 phases. The Interphase and M phase is the mitosis phase. The interphase represents the phase between two successive M phases. The M Phase starts with the nuclear division, corresponding to the separation of daughter chromosomes (karyokinesis) and usually ends with the division of cytoplasm (cytokinesis) \n (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 162, Paragraph no 2)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Cell Cycle and Phases of Cell Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','1 and 2 are correct','only 1 is correct','2 and 3 are correct','only 2 is correct','NEW','11-162','11-120',NULL),
(100133,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the interphase of the cell cycle. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. In the S phase there is a replication of DNA because of which the chromosome number will be doubled.\n2. During the S phase there is DNA and Centriole replication in the nucleus.\n3. During the G2 phase proteins are synthesized','only 3 is correct','In S Phase there is a doubling of DNA but not chromosomes. So, the chromosome number remains the same while the DNA content is doubled.  DNA replication occurs in the nucleus whereas centriole duplication occurs in the cytoplasm. During the G2 phase, there are proteins synthesized in preparation for mitosis. (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 163, Paragraph no 4 and 5)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Cell Cycle and Phases of Cell Cycle',NULL,'3','mcq','all are correct','2 and 3 are correct','only 3 is correct','2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-163','11-121',NULL),
(100134,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding mitosis in onion root tip cells. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. There are 16 chromosomes in onion root tip cells\n2. There are 16, 32, and 16 chromosomes present at G1, after S, after the M phase\n3. DNA content of cell at G1, after S and at G2, after M phase are 2C, 4C, 4C, 2C respectively','1 and 3 are correct','There are 16 chromosomes in onion root tip cells. In S Phase there is a doubling of DNA but not chromosomes. So, the chromosome number remains the same while the DNA content is doubled. So, There are 16, 16, and 16 chromosomes present at G1, after S, after the M phase and the DNA content of cells at G1, after S and at G2, after the M phase is 2C, 4C, 4C, and 2C respectively. In G2 the DNA content will be twice because the cell divides at the M phase, not in the G2 phase.   (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 164, orange box)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Cell Cycle and Phases of Cell Cycle',NULL,'3','mcq','only 1 is correct','1 and 3 are correct','1 and 2 are correct','all are correct','NEW','11-164','11-122',NULL),
(100135,'biology','All the plants cells show mitotic division only in diploid cells.','FALSE','Against this, the plants can show mitotic divisions in both haploid and diploid cells. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:164, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Cell Cycle and Phases of Cell Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-164','11-122',NULL),
(100136,'biology','Assertion: Cell division in human cells lasts only about an hour. \nReason: The interphase lasts about 95% of the duration of the cell cycle.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The human cell takes about 24 hours to complete the process of cell division. The interphase lasts for more than 95% which equates to approximately 23 hours and the m phase is completed in the last hour.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 163, Paragraph no 2)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Cell Cycle and Phases of Cell Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-163','11-121',NULL),
(100137,'biology','Assertion: There are 16 chromosomes after the S phase in the tip of onion root cells.\nReason: In the S phase there is a replication of DNA.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','There are 16 chromosomes in the tip of onion root cells. In  S Phase there is a doubling of DNA but not chromosomes. So, the chromosome number remains the same.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 163-4, Paragraph no 1 and orange box.)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Cell Cycle and Phases of Cell Cycle',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-164','11-122',NULL),
(100138,'biology','Assertion: During the division of a cell, DNA replication and cell growth take place.\nReason: The sequence of events by which a cell duplicates its genome, synthesizes the other constituents of the cell and eventually divides into four daughter cells is termed the cell cycle.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','During the division of a cell, there is duplication of DNA material and cell growth seen. The cell cycle is the sequential stages of duplication of the genome, synthesis of other cell constituents and division into two daughter cells. So, Assertion is true but Reason is false.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 162, Paragraph no 2)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Cell Cycle and Phases of Cell Cycle',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-162','11-120',NULL),
(100201,'chemistry','What is the pattern of trends in the physical and chemical properties of alkali metals with the increasing atomic number?','Regular','The alkali metals show regular trends in their physical and chemical properties with the increasing atomic number. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Irregular','Distorted','Regular','Plateau-like','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100202,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','The alkaline earth metal atoms have the largest sizes in a particular period of the periodic table.','The correct statement is \'The alkali metals have the largest sizes in a particular period of the periodic table."\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','All the alkali metals have one valence electron outside the noble gas core.','The loosely held s-electron in the outermost valence shell of group 1 elements makes them the most electropositive metals.','Group 1 elements are never found in free state in nature.','The alkaline earth metal atoms have the largest sizes in a particular period of the periodic table.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100203,'chemistry','Which of the following is electronic configuration of Cs?','[Xe] 6s¹','The correct electronic configuration of Cs is [Xe] 6s¹ .\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Electronic Configuration Table)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','[Rn] 6s¹','[Xe] 5s¹','[Rn] 7s¹','[Xe] 6s¹','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100204,'chemistry','Match the elements with their electronic configuration.','K-[Ar] 4s¹,Fr-[Rn] 7s¹,Rb-[Kr] 5s¹,Cs-[Xe] 6s¹','The correct matching is -\n\nK -  [Ar] 4s¹  \n  \nFr - [Rn] 7s¹\n\nRb - [Kr] 5s¹\n\nCs - [Xe] 6s¹\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Electronic Configuration Table)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','match','K,[Ar]4s¹','Fr,[Rn]7s¹','Rb,[Kr] 5s¹','Cs,[Xe] 6s¹','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100205,'chemistry','What is the size of monovalent ions as compared to their parent atom?','Smaller','The monovalent ions are smaller than the parent atom.This is beacuse of increased effective nuclear charge on electrons.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph- 10.1.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Smaller','Larger','Same','None','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100206,'chemistry','Arrange the following group 1 elements in increasing order of their atomic radius.','Na < K < Rb < Cs','The atomic and ionic radii of alkali metals increase on moving down the group i.e., they increase in size while going from Li to Cs. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Na < K < Cs < Rb','K < Na < Rb < Cs','Na < K < Rb < Cs','Cs < K < Rb < Na','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100207,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','Because of the large size, group 1 elements have high density.','Li⁺ has maximum degree of hydration beacause of its small size.\n\nThe ionization enthalpies of the alkali metals are considerably low and decrease down the group from Li to Cs. \n\nThis is because the effect of increasing size outweighs the increasing nuclear charge, and the outermost electron is very well screened from the nuclear charge. \n\nBecause of the large size, group 1 elements have low density.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.3; 10.1.4; 10.1.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','The hydration enthalpies of alkali metal ions decrease with increase in ionic sizes.','Li⁺ has maximum degree of hydration','Ionization enthalpies of alkali metals decrease down the group.','Because of the large size, group 1 elements have high density.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100208,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in increasing order of their ionisation energy.','Cs < Rb < K < Li','As we go down the group, ionisation energy decreases because of poor shielding of d and f orbitals it is easier to remove electron.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Li < Rb < K < Cs','Cs < Rb < K < Li','Li < K < Rb < Cs','Cs < Rb < Li < K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100209,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','The ionization enthalpies of the alkali metals are considerably high.','Alkaline earth metals are so called because their oxides and hydroxides are alkaline in nature and these metal oxides are found in the earth’s crust.\n \nIn diagonal relationship the elements of group 1 and group 2 resemble the second element of the following group. \n\nThe diagonal relationship is due to the similarity in ionic sizes and /or charge/radius ratio of the elements. \n\nThe ionization enthalpies of the alkali metals are considerably low. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 299; Paragraph 2| Page 300 Paragraph 1, 10.1.2, 10.1.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','Alkaline earth metals are so called because their oxides and hydroxides are alkaline in nature and these metal oxides are found in the earth’s crust.','The diagonal relationship is due to the similarity in ionic sizes and /or charge/radius ratio of the elements.','In diagonal relationship the elements of group 1 and group 2 resemble the second element of the following group.','The ionization enthalpies of the alkali metals are considerably high.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100210,'chemistry','Salts of which element are maximum hydrated?','Lithium','Li⁺ has maximum degree of hydration beacause of its small size and for this reason Lithium salts are mostly hydrated, e.g., LiCl. 2H₂O\n\n(Reference-  NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 2 of 10.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Lithium','Beryllium','Sodium','Caesium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100211,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in increasing order of their hydration energy.','Cs < Rb < K < Na','The hydration enthalpies of alkali metal ions decrease with increase in ionic sizes. Hydration enthalpy depends on size of ions and attraction between water molecules and ions. Smaller the ion, more the attraction, higher the hydration enthalpy.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Na < K < Rb < Cs','Cs < K < Rb < Na','Na < Rb < K < Cs','Cs < Rb < K < Na','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100212,'chemistry','How many water of crystallisation are present in hydrated LiCl?','2','Li⁺ has maximum degree of hydration and for this reason lithium salts are mostly hydrated, e.g., LiCl· 2H₂O\n\n(Reference-  NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100213,'chemistry','What is the appearance of alkali metals?','Silvery white','All the alkali metals are silvery white metals. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Silvery white','Dark brown','Black','Greenish yellow','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100213q.png'),
(100214,'chemistry','When touched, how do alkali metals seem?','Soft','All the alkali metals are silvery soft metals. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Soft','Hard','Rough','Gelatinous','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100214q.png'),
(100215,'chemistry','How do alkali metals weigh?','Light','All the alkali metals are silvery white, soft and light metals. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Light','Heavy','Dense','Thick','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100215q.png'),
(100216,'chemistry','In which flame do alkali metals and their salts impart color?','Oxidising flame','The alkali metals and their salts impart characteristic colour to an oxidizing flame.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidising flame','Reducing flame','Disproportioning flame','Self-decomposition flame','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100217,'chemistry','Mark the correct statement.','Alkali metals have weak metallic bonding due to the presence of only a single valence electron in them.','Option a, b and c have incorrect statements. The correct statements are -\n\n1. The alkali metals and their salts impart characteristic colour to an oxidizing flame. \n\n2. This is because the heat from the flame excites the outermost orbital electron to a higher energy level. \n\n3. When the excited electron comes back to the ground state, there is emission of radiation in the visible region of the spectrum.\n\n4. Alkali metals have weak metallic bonding due to the presence of only a single valence electron in them. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'2','mcq','The alkali metals and their salts impart characteristic colour to a reducing flame.','Colour is because the heat from the flame excites the inner orbital electrons to a higher energy level.','When the excited electron comes back to the ground state, there is emission of radiation in the ultraviolet region of the spectrum','Alkali metals have weak metallic bonding due to the presence of only a single valence electron in them.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100218,'chemistry','How are melting and boiling points of alkali metals?','Low','The melting and boiling points of the alkali metals are low. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','High','Very high','Intermediate','Low','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100219,'chemistry','Arrange the following group 1 elements in increasing order of their wavelength emitted during flame test.','Na < Li < K < Rb','The correct order is Na (589.2 nm) < Li (670.8 nm) < K (766.5 nm) < Rb (780.0 nm).\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.5 TABLE)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','mcq','Rb < K < Li < Na','Na < Li < K < Rb','Rb < Li < K < Na','Na < K < Li < Rb','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100220,'chemistry','Match the group 1 elements with their respective wavelength emitted (in nm) during flame test.','Li-670.8 nm,Na-589.2 nm,K-766.5 nm,Cs-455.5 nm','The correct match is Li - 670.8 nm    Na - 589.2 nm   K - 766.5 nm   Cs - 455.5 nm\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.5 TABLE)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','match','Li,670.8 nm','Na,589.2 nm','K,766.5 nm','Cs,455.5 nm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100221,'chemistry','Match the group 1 elements with their respective colors during flame test.','Li-Crimson red,Na-Yellow,Rb-Red violet,Cs-Blue','The correct match is Li - Crimson red      Na - Yellow,    Rb - Red violet,    Cs - Blue\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 300; Paragraph 10.1.5 TABLE)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'3','match','Li,Crimson red','Na,Yellow','Rb,Red violet','Cs,Blue','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100301,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','The stability of the peroxide or superoxide increases, as the size of the metal ion decreases in group 1.','The correct statement is "The stability of the peroxide or superoxide increases, as the size of the metal ion increases in group 1." \n\nThis is due to the stabilisation of large anions by larger cations through lattice energy effects.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','All the common compounds of the alkali metals are generally ionic in nature.','The stability of the peroxide or superoxide increases, as the size of the metal ion decreases in group 1.','Under appropriate conditions pure compounds M2O, M2O2 and MO2 may be prepared.(M = alkali metal)','Sodium peroxide is widely used as an oxidising agent in inorganic chemistry.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100302,'chemistry','On combustion in excess of air, which of the following is not true:','Na forms mainly peroxide and some oxide','On combustion in excess of air, Lithium forms mainly the oxide, Li₂O (plus some peroxide Li₂O₂), Sodium forms the peroxide, Na₂O₂ (and some superoxide NaO₂) whilst Potassium, Rubidium and Caesium form the superoxides, MO₂. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Li forms mainly oxide and some peroxide','Na forms mainly peroxide and some oxide','Potassium forms superoxide.','Caesium and Rubidium form superoxides.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100303,'chemistry','The increasing stability of the peroxide or superoxide, as the size of the metal ion increases, is due to the stabilisation of large anions by larger cations through ____ energy effects.','lattice','The increasing stability of the peroxide or superoxide, as the size of the metal ion increases, is due to the stabilisation of large anions by larger cations through lattice energy effects. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','lattice','hydration','sublimation','ionisation','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100304,'chemistry','What does hydrolysis of alkali metal oxide give?','Metal ion and hydroxide ion only','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1 Reactions)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Metal ion, hydroxide ion and oxygen gas','Metal ion and hydroxide ion only','Metal ion, hydroxide ion and hydrogen peroxide','Metal ion, hydroxide ion, hydrogen peroxide and oxygen gas','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100305,'chemistry','What does hydrolysis of alkali metal peroxide give?','Metal ion, hydroxide ion and hydrogen peroxide','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1 Reactions)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Metal ion, hydroxide ion and oxygen gas','Metal ion and hydroxide ion only','Metal ion, hydroxide ion and hydrogen peroxide','Metal ion, hydroxide ion, hydrogen peroxide and oxygen gas','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100312,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','The alkali metal halides, MX, (X=F,Cl,Br,I) are all high melting, colourful crystalline solids.','The hydroxides which are obtained by the reaction of the oxides with water are all white crystalline solids. \n\nThe alkali metal hydroxides are the strongest of all bases and dissolve freely in water with evolution of much heat on account of intense hydration. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.1) \n\nThe alkali metal halides, MX, (X=F,Cl,Br,I) are all high melting, colourless crystalline solids. \n\nThey can be prepared by the reaction of the appropriate oxide, hydroxide or carbonate with aqueous hydrohalic acid (HX).\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','The alkali metal hydroxides are the strongest of all bases.','Alkali metal hydroxides dissolve freely in water with evolution of much heat on account of intense hydration.','The alkali metal halides, MX, (X=F,Cl,Br,I) are all high melting, colourful crystalline solids.','Alkali metal halides can be prepared by the reaction of the appropriate oxide, hydroxide or carbonate with aqueous hydrohalic acid (HX).','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100312q.png'),
(100313,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in increasing order of energy realeased on formation of metal fluoride.','Cs < K < Na < Li','Negative enthalpy of formation values for fluorides become less negative as we go down the group whilst the reverse is true for chlorides, bromides and iodides.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Cs < K < Li < Na','Li < Na < K < Cs','Cs < K < Na < Li','Cs < Na < K < Li','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100313q.png'),
(100314,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in increasing order of energy realeased on formation of metal chlorides.','Li < Na < Rb < Cs','Negative enthalpy of formation values for fluorides become less negative as we go down the group whilst the reverse is true for chlorides, bromides and iodides.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Li < Na < Cs < Rb','Cs < Rb < Na < Li','Cs < Na < Li < Rb','Li < Na < Rb < Cs','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100314q.png'),
(100315,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in increasing order of energy realeased on formation of metal bromides.','Li < Na < K < Cs','Negative enthalpy of formation values for fluorides become less negative as we go down the group whilst the reverse is true for chlorides, bromides and iodides.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Cs < K < Na < Li','Cs < Na < Li < K','Li < Na < K < Cs','Li < Na < Cs < K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100316,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in increasing order of energy realeased on formation of metal iodides.','Li < Na < K < Rb','Negative enthalpy of formation values for fluorides become less negative as we go down the group whilst the reverse is true for chlorides, bromides and iodides.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Li < Na < Rb < K','Li < Na < K < Rb','Rb < Na < Li < K','Rb < K < Na < Li','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100317,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in increasing order of negative enthalpy of formation for a particular metal in formation of a metal halide.','I < Br < Cl < F','For a given metal ∆ H always becomes less negative from fluoride to iodide. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','I < Br < Cl < F','F < Br < Cl < I','F < Br < Cl < I','F < Cl < Br < I','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100318,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in increasing order of melting point of metal halide for a particular alkali metal.','I < Br < Cl < F','The melting and boiling points always follow the trend: \n\nfluoride > chloride > bromide > iodide.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','F < Cl < Br < I','I < Br < Cl < F','F < Br < Cl < I','F < Br < Cl < I','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100319,'chemistry','Arrange the elements in increasing order of boiling point of metal halide for a particular alkali metal.','I < Br < Cl < F','The melting and boiling points always follow the trend: \n\nfluoride > chloride > bromide > iodide.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','F < Br < Cl < I','F < Cl < Br < I','F < Br < Cl < I','I < Br < Cl < F','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100320,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','CsI has relatively high solubility in water is due to its low lattice enthalpy','The low solubility of LiF in water is due to its high lattice enthalpy whereas the low solubility of CsI is due to smaller hydration enthalpy of its two ions. \n\nLiF is soluble in pyridine because of its covalent nature.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','All alkali metal halides are soluble in water.','LiCl is soluble in pyridine','LiF has relatively low solubility in water is due to its high lattice enthalpy','CsI has relatively high solubility in water is due to its low lattice enthalpy','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100321,'chemistry','Which of the following is least soluble in water?','LiF','The low solubility of LiF in water is due to its high lattice enthalpy whereas the low solubility of CsI is due to smaller hydration enthalpy of its two ions.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','LiF','NaF','RbF','CsF','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100322,'chemistry','Which of the following is least soluble in water?','CsI','The low solubility of LiF in water is due to its high lattice enthalpy whereas the low solubility of CsI is due to smaller hydration enthalpy of its two ions.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','RbI','CsI','KI','LiI','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110721,'biology','The coordination between roots and fungus in mycorrhiza is','The fungal filaments form a network around the young root or they penetrate the root cells','A mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association of a fungus with a root system. The fungal filaments form a network around the young root or they penetrate the root cells. The hyphae have a very large surface area that absorb mineral ions and water from the soil from a much larger volume of soil that perhaps a root cannot do.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 186','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','The fungal filaments form a network around the young root or they penetrate the root cells','The hyphae have a low surface area that absorb mineral ions and water from the soil.','A synergistic association','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100323,'chemistry','Mark the correct statement.','Halides of lithium(except Fluoride) are soluble in ethanol, acetone and ethylacetate.','Other halides of lithium are soluble in ethanol, acetone and ethylacetate; LiCl is soluble in pyridine also. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2) \n\nOxo-acids are those in which the acidic proton is on a hydroxyl group with an oxo group attached to the same atom.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)\n\nLithium being very small in size polarises a large carbonate ion leading to the formation of more stable Li₂O and CO₂.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','Halides of lithium(except Fluoride) are soluble in ethanol, acetone and ethylacetate.','Oxo-acids are those in which the acidic proton is on a hydroxyl group with an oxo group attached to the adjacent atom.','The alkali metals do not form salts with all the oxo-acids.','Lithium being very large in size polarises large carbonate ion in lithium carbonate leading to the formation of more stable Li₂O and CO₂.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100324,'chemistry','Which of the following is an oxoacid?','Sulphuric acid','Oxo-acids are those in which the acidic proton is on a hydroxyl group with an oxo group attached to the same atom e.g., carbonic acid ; sulphuric acid.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','HCl','HI','Uric acid','Sulphuric acid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100325,'chemistry','Which of the following is true regarding thermal property of alkali metal salts of oxo-acids?','Alkali metal salts of oxo-acids are thermally stable.','Alkali metal salts of oxo-acids are generally soluble in water and thermally stable.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Alkali metal salts of oxo-acids  are thermally unstable.','Alkali metal salts of oxo-acids  are thermally energetic.','Alkali metal salts of oxo-acids are thermally stable.','Alkali metal salts of oxo-acids  are thermal equilibrium is inexistent.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100326,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement wrt alkali metal salts of oxo-acids.','All of their carbonates and hydrogencarbonates also are highly unstable to heat.','Alkali metals\' carbonates (M₂CO₃) and in most cases the hydrogencarbonates (MHCO₃) also are highly stable to heat.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'2','mcq','They are generally soluble in water.','All of their carbonates and hydrogencarbonates also are highly unstable to heat.','The alkali metals form salts with all the oxo-acids.','Lithium carbonate is not so stable to heat.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100327,'chemistry','Arrange the compounds in increasing order of thermal stability.','Lithium carbonate < Sodium carbonate < Potassium carbonate < Caesium carbonate','As the electropositive character increases down the group, the stability of the carbonates and hydorgencarbonates increases. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Caesium carbonate < Sodium carbonate < Potassium carbonate < Lithium carbonate','Caesium carbonate < Potassium carbonate < Sodium carbonate < Lithium carbonate','Lithium carbonate < Potassium carbonate < Sodium carbonate < Caesium carbonate','Lithium carbonate < Sodium carbonate < Potassium carbonate < Caesium carbonate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100327q.png'),
(100328,'chemistry','Arrange the compounds in increasing order of thermal stability.','Lithium bicarbonate < Sodium bicarbonate < Potassium bicarbonate < Rubidium bicarbonate','As the electropositive character increases down the group, the stability of the carbonates and hydorgencarbonates increases. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Rubidium bicarbonate <  Potassium bicarbonate < Sodium bicarbonate < Lithium bicarbonate','Rubidium bicarbonate < Sodium bicarbonate < Potassium bicarbonate < Lithium bicarbonate','Lithium bicarbonate < Potassium bicarbonate < Sodium bicarbonate < Rubidium bicarbonate','Lithium bicarbonate < Sodium bicarbonate < Potassium bicarbonate < Rubidium bicarbonate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100328q.png'),
(100329,'chemistry','Hydrogen carbonate of which alkali metal does not exist in solid state?','Li','Lithium hydrogencarbonate does not exist as a solid. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 303; Paragraph 10.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements- General Characteristics of Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Li','Cs','Rb','K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100333,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding metaphase 1 and 2. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. The bivalent chromosomes align on the equatorial plate\n2. The microtubules from the opposite poles of the spindle attach to the kinetochore of non-homologous chromosomes.\n3. The chromosomes align at the equator and the microtubules from opposite poles of the spindle get attached to the\nkinetochores of sister chromatids','1 and 3 are correct','In metaphase 1 The bivalent chromosomes align on the equatorial plate. The microtubules from the opposite poles of the spindle attach to the kinetochore of homologous chromosomes. In metaphase 2 the chromosomes align at the equator and the microtubules from opposite poles of the spindle get attached to the kinetochores of sister chromatids.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 162, Paragraph no 5)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'3','mcq','2 and 3 are correct','1 and 3 are correct','2 and 3 are correct','all are correct','NEW','11-162','11-120',NULL),
(100334,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding Metaphase. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. Disintegration of nuclear envelope causing chromosomes to be spread in the cytoplasm.\n2. Chromosomal structure is not so clearly seen\n3. Centrosomes are attached to astral fibres','only 1 is correct','The complete disintegration of the nuclear envelope marks the start of the second phase of mitosis, hence the chromosomes are spread through the cytoplasm of the cell. \nBy metaphase, the condensation of chromosomes is completed\nand they can be observed clearly. Small disc-shaped structures at the surface of the centromeres are called\nkinetochores. These structures serve as the sites of attachment of spindle fibres (Astral fibres).  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 165, Paragraph no 2)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'2','mcq','1 and 2 are correct','only 1 is correct','1 and 3 are correct','only 3 is correct','NEW','11-165','11-123',NULL),
(110722,'biology','In mycorrhiza ,fungus provides provide sugars and N-containing compounds to roots ,roots in turn provides minerals and water to the fungus.','FALSE','The fungus provides minerals and water to the roots, in turn the roots provide sugars and N-containing compounds to the mycorrhizae\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 186','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100335,'biology','Why are chromosomes easily studied in metaphase?','Chromosomes are condensed and nuclear membrane is disintegrated','The complete disintegration of the nuclear envelope marks the start of the second phase of mitosis, hence the chromosomes are spread through the cytoplasm of the cell. By this stage, condensation of chromosomes is completed and they can be observed clearly under the microscope. This then, is the stage at which morphology of chromosomes is most easily studied. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:165, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'2','mcq','Chromosomes are divided into daughter chromosomes','Chromosomes are condensed and inside nucleus','Chromosomes are condensed and nuclear membrane is disintegrated','Chromosomes are magnified and visible','NEW','11-165','11-123',NULL),
(100336,'biology','Identify the marked structures in the given diagram  (1024)','Centromere region with kinetochore attached to spindle fibres of opposite poles','The metaphase is characterised by all the chromosomes coming to lie at the equator with one chromatid of each chromosome connected by its kinetochore to spindle fibres from one pole and its sister chromatid connected by its kinetochore to spindle fibres from the opposite pole. Centromere is the region where the two sister chromatids are held together whereas kinetochore is the protein complex on the chromosome where spindle fibers are attached during cell division attaching them to the poles. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:165, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'2','mcq','Kinetochore region with centromere attached to spindle fibres of opposite poles','Centromere region with kinetochore attached to spindle fibres of opposite poles','Kinetochore region with centromere attached to spindle fibres of same poles','Centromere region with kinetochore attached to spindle fibres of same pole','NEW','11-165','11-123','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100336q.png'),
(100337,'biology','Assertion: Chromosomal material condenses to form compact mitotic chromosomes.\nReason: The chromosomal material becomes untangled during thee process of chromatin condensation.','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Prophase is marked by the initiation of condensation of chromosomal material. Chromosomal material condenses to form compact mitotic chromosomes. The chromosomel material becomes untangled during the process of chromatin condensation. Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 164, Paragraph no 3)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-164','11-122',NULL),
(100338,'biology','Assertion: Alignment of chromosomes at equator forms metaphase plate\nReason: Spindle fibres from the centrosome are attached to kinetochores.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Spindle fibres attach to kinetochores of chromosomes. Chromosomes are moved to the spindle equator and get aligned along the metaphase plate through spindle fibres to both poles.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 165, Paragraph no 2)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-165','11-123',NULL),
(100339,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding Prophase. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. Prophase comes after the G2 phase of interphase\n2. Here the condensation of chromosomes is completed\n3. Centrosome radiates out asters to the poles. \n4. Parts of a chromosome are centromere and 2 chromatids.','1 and 4 are correct','Prophase which is the first stage of karyokinesis of mitosis follows the S and G2 phases of interphase. Prophase is marked by the initiation of condensation of chromosomal material and not the complete condensation. Each centrosome radiates out microtubules called asters from the opposite poles. Chromosomes are seen to be composed of two chromatids attached to the centromere.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 164, Paragraph no 3 and 4)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'2','mcq','1 and 2 are correct','1 and 4 are correct','2 and 4 are correct','1 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-164','11-122',NULL),
(100340,'biology','In the M-phase, the only component of the cell involved is the nucleus.','FALSE','M-phase is the most dramatic period of the cell cycle, involving a major reorganisation of virtually all components of the cell. There are 4 divisions for karyogenesis- prophase, metaphase, anaphase and telphase followed by cytokinesis where cytoplasm and other cell organelles also divide. So cell division by mitosis involves all the components of the cell. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:164, paragraph no:3)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-164','11-122',NULL),
(100341,'biology','Mitosis is called equational divison because','Same number of chromosomes between parent and progeny cells.','Plant cells however, are enclosed by a relatively inextensible cell wall, thererfore they undergo cytokinesis by a different mechanism. In plant cells, wall formation starts in the centre of the cell and grows outward to meet the existing lateral walls. The formation of the new cell wall begins with the formation of a simple precursor, called the cell-plate that represents the middle lamella between the walls of two adjacent cells. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:166, paragraph no:3)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'2','mcq','Same number of chromosomes between parent and progeny cells.','Different number of chromosomes between parent and progeny cells.','Different number of chromatids between parent and progeny cells.','Different size of chromatids between parent and progeny cells.','NEW','11-166','11-124',NULL),
(100342,'biology','Assertion: For convenience mitosis has been divided into four stages of nuclear division (karyokinesis),\n\n Reason: It is very essential to understand that cell division is a progressive process and very clear-cut lines can be drawn between various stages.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Though for convenience mitosis has been divided into four stages of nuclear division (karyokinesis), it is very essential to understand that cell division is a progressive process and very clear-cut lines cannot be drawn between various stages. And every cell in the body is at different stages of cell division at a given point in time. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:164, paragraph no:3)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-164','11-122',NULL),
(100343,'biology','Cell division is- i) Regressive ii)Unidirectional iii) Progressive iv) Multidirectional','ii and iii','Though for convenience mitosis has been divided into four stages of nuclear division (karyokinesis), it is very essential to understand that cell division is a progressive process and very clear-cut lines cannot be drawn between various stages.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:164, paragraph no:3)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'2','mcq','only ii','i and iv','iii and iv','ii and iii','NEW','11-164','11-122',NULL),
(100344,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the M phase. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. In prophase, Chromosomal material condenses to form compact mitotic chromosomes.\n2. In anaphase, Centromeres split and chromatids separate.\n3. In metaphase, Chromosomes are moved to the spindle equator and get aligned along the metaphase plate through spindle fibres to both poles\n4. In telophase, Chromosomes cluster at opposite spindle poles and their identity is lost as discrete elements.','all are correct','In prophase, Chromosomal material condenses to form compact mitotic chromosomes. In anaphase, Centromeres split and chromatids separate. In metaphase, Chromosomes are moved to the spindle equator and get aligned along the metaphase plate through spindle fibres to both poles. Chromosomes cluster at opposite spindle poles and their identity is lost as discrete elements.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 164-166, Bullet points).','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'3','mcq','1,2 and 3 are correct','all are correct','2,3 and 4 are correct','2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-166','11-124',NULL),
(100345,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding cell cycle. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. In prophase, Nucleolus, Golgi complex and ER reform.\n2. In Telophase, Chromatids move to opposite poles.\n3. In metaphase, Spindle fibres attach to kinetochores of chromosomes.\n4. In anaphase, The two asters together with spindle fibres form mitotic apparatus.','only 3 is correct','In Telophase, Nucleolus, Golgi complex and ER reform. In Anaphase, Chromatids move to opposite poles. In metaphase, Spindle fibres attach to kinetochores of chromosomes. In Prophase, the two asters together with spindle fibres form mitotic apparatus.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 164-166, Bullet points)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'3','mcq','2 and 3 are correct','only 3 is correct','1 and 2 are correct','only 2 is correct','NEW','11-166','11-124',NULL),
(100346,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding the M phase. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. M phase is the phase where the division occurs\n2. Two major processes Karyokinesis and Cytokinesis occurs in the M phase of equational division\n3. Sequence of phases Prophase- Metaphase- Telophase- Anaphase','1 and 2 are correct','M phase is the phase where the division occurs. Two major processes Karyokinesis and Cytokinesis occur in the M phase of equational division. The sequence of phases is Prophase- Metaphase- Anaphase- Telophase.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 164, Paragraph no 2)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Prophase and Metaphase',NULL,'2','mcq','1 and 3 are correct','2 and 3 are correct','1 and 2 are correct','all are correct','NEW','11-164','11-122',NULL),
(100401,'chemistry','Lithium compounds are soluble in which solvents?','Organic solvents','There is increased covalent character of lithium compounds which is responsible for their solubility in organic solvents.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'1','mcq','Inorganic solvents','Ionic solvents','Organic solvents','Polar solvents','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100402,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','Lithium is relatively softer than other alkali metals.','The anomalous behaviour of lithium is due to the : \n\n(i) exceptionally small size of its atom and ion, and \n\n(ii) high polarising power (i.e., charge/ radius ratio). \n\nLithium shows diagonal relationship to Magnesium which has been discussed subsequently. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3) \n\nLithium is much harder. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'2','mcq','The anomalous behaviour of lithium is due to the exceptionally small size of its atom and ion.','The anomalous behaviour of lithium is due to it’s high polarising power (i.e., charge/ radius ratio)','Lithium shows diagonal relationship to Magnesium.','Lithium is relatively softer than other alkali metals.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100403,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement wrt lithium when compared with other alkali metals.','Lithium hydrogencarbonate is obtained in the solid form while all other elements form liquid hydrogencarbonates.','Lithium\'s m.p. and b.p. are higher than the other alkali ₂metals. \n\nLithium is least reactive but the strongest reducing agent among all the alkali metals. \n\nLithium hydrogencarbonate is not obtained in the solid form while all other elements form solid hydrogencarbonates. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'2','mcq','Lithium\'s melting point and boiling point are higher than the other alkali metals.','Lithium is least reactive.','Lithium hydrogencarbonate is obtained in the solid form while all other elements form liquid hydrogencarbonates.','Liyhium is strongest reducing agent among all the alkali metals.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100403q.png'),
(100404,'chemistry','On combustion in air, what does lithium, unlike other alkali metals, form?','Mainly monoxide and nitride','On combustion in air it forms mainly monoxide and the nitride unlike other alkali metals.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxide only','Nitride only','Mainly monoxide and nitride','Mainly monoxide and some peroxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100405,'chemistry','What is the nature of LiCl?','Deliquescent','LiCl is deliquescent.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'1','mcq','Deliquescent','Hygroscopic','Dessicant','Efflorescent','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100406,'chemistry','Which of the following forms no ethynide on reaction with ethyne?','Li','Lithium unlike other alkali metals forms no ethynide on reaction with ethyne. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'1','mcq','Li','Na','Cs','Rb','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100407,'chemistry','Mark the false reaction.','4NaNO₃ → 2Na₂O + 4NO₂ + O₂','Lithium nitrate when heated gives lithium oxide, Li₂O, whereas other alkali metal nitrates decompose to give the corresponding nitrite. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'1','mcq','4LiNO₃ → 2Li₂O + 4NO₂ + O₂','4NaNO₃ → 2Na₂O + 4NO₂ + O₂','2NaNO₃→ 2NaNO₂ + O₂','2KNO₃→ 2KNO₂ + O₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100408,'chemistry','Which of the following is least soluble?','Li₂O','LiF and Li₂O are comparatively much less soluble in water than the corresponding compounds of other alkali metals. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'1','mcq','Li₂O','Na₂O','K₂O','Rb₂O','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100409,'chemistry','Mark the correct statement.','Lithium nitrate when heated gives lithium oxide, Li₂O , whereas other alkali metal nitrates decompose to give the corresponding nitrite.','Lithium nitrate when heated gives lithium oxide, Li₂O, whereas other alkali metal nitrates decompose to give the corresponding nitrite. \n\nLiCl is deliquescent and crystallises as a hydrate, LiCl.2H₂O whereas other alkali metal chlorides do not form hydrates. \n\nLithium is least reactive but the strongest reducing agent among all the alkali metals.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'2','mcq','Alkali metal chlorides except lithium crystallise as a hydrate.','Lithium nitrate when heated gives lithium oxide, Li₂O , whereas other alkali metal nitrates decompose to give the corresponding nitrite.','Li is most reactive alkali metal as it has lowest standard reduction potential.','Lithium forms ethynide on reaction with ethyne.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100410,'chemistry','Why does similarity between lithium and magnesium arise?','Because of similar size','The similarity between lithium and magnesium is particularly striking and arises because of their similar sizes. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'1','mcq','Because of similar charge','Because of similar group in periodic table','Because of similar electron configuration','Because of similar size','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100411,'chemistry','Match the species with their respective radius(pm).','Li-152 pm,Mg-160 pm,Li⁺-76 pm, Mg⁺²-72 pm','The similarity between lithium and magnesium is particularly striking and arises because of their similar sizes: \n\natomic radii, Li = 152 pm, Mg = 160 pm; \n\nionic radii : Li⁺ = 76 pm, Mg⁺²= 72 pm.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'3','match','Li,152 pm','Mg,160 pm','Li⁺,76 pm','Mg⁺²,72 pm','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100412,'chemistry','Mark the false statement.','Hydroxides of both lithium and magnesium are highly soluble.','Lithium and magnesium react slowly with water. \n\nTheir oxides and hydroxides are much less soluble and their hydroxides decompose on heating. \n\nBoth form a nitride, Li₃N and Mg₃N₂, by direct combination with nitrogen.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.2 point(ii))','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'2','mcq','Lithium and magnesium react slowly with water.','Lithium forms nitride by direct combination with nitrogen.','Magnesium forms nitride by direct combination with nitrogen.','Hydroxides of both lithium and magnesium are highly soluble.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100413,'chemistry','Both Li and Mg are ___ than other elements in their respective group.','harder','Both Li and Mg are harder than other elements in their respective group.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'1','mcq','harder','softer','heavier','colorful','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100414,'chemistry','Both Li and Mg are ___ than other elements in their respective group.','lighter','Both lithium and magnesium are harder and lighter than other elements in the respective groups. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'1','mcq','softer','heavier','lighter','colorful','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100415,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is true regarding oxides of Li and Mg?','They do not combine with excess oxygen to give superoxide.','Their oxides and hydroxides are much less soluble.\n\nThe oxides, Li₂O and MgO do not combine with excess oxygen to give any superoxide.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'2','mcq','They are highly soluble','They combine with excess oxygen to give superoxide.','They do not combine with excess oxygen to give superoxide.','They do not combine with excess oxygen to give peroxide.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100416,'chemistry','Mark the false statement.','Both LiCl and MgCl₂ crystallise as dihydrates.','Both LiCl and MgCl2 are deliquescent and crystallise from aqueous solution as hydrates, LiCl·2H₂O and MgCl₂·8H₂O. \n\nTheir oxides and hydroxides are much less soluble and their hydroxides decompose on heating.\n\nThe carbonates of lithium and magnesium decompose easily on heating.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'2','mcq','The carbonates of lithium and magnesium decompose easily on heating.','Lithium and magnesium oxides and hydroxides are much less soluble in water.','Lithium and magnesium hydroxides decompose on heating','Both LiCl and MgCl₂ crystallise as dihydrates.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100417,'chemistry','Which of the following does not form solid hydrogen carbonate?','Mg','Solid hydrogencarbonates are not formed by lithium and magnesium. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'1','mcq','Na','Cs','K','Mg','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100418,'chemistry','Both LiCl and MgCl₂ are ___ in ethanol.','soluble','Both LiCl and MgCl₂ are soluble in ethanol.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'1','fillup','In,sol,ub,le',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100419,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','Both lithium and magnesium are heavier than other elements in their respective groups.','Both lithium and magnesium are harder and lighter than other elements in the respective groups. \n\nBoth LiCl and MgCl₂ are deliquescent and crystallise from aqueous solution as hydrates.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'2','mcq','LiCl is deliquescent','MgCl₂ is deliquescent','Both lithium and magnesium are heavier than other elements in their respective groups.','The carbonates of lithium and magnesium decompose easily on heating to form the oxides and CO₂','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100419q.png'),
(100420,'chemistry','In MgCl₂.xH₂O, what is x?','8','MgCl₂ crystallises as octahydrate.i.e MgCl₂.8H₂O\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Anomalous properties of Lithium',NULL,'1','mcq','2','4','6','8','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100421,'biology','Assertion: In anaphase, Centromeres split and chromatids separate.\nReason: The centromere of each chromosome not directed towards the pole and hence at the leading edge.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','In anaphase, the daughter chromosomes of the future daughter nuclei, begin their migration towards the two opposite poles. the centromere of each chromosome remains directed towards the pole and hence at the leading edge, with the arms of the chromosome trailing behind. The chromatids separate so there is no sister chromatids present in anaphase.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 165, Paragraph no 3)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Anaphase and Telophase',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-165','11-123',NULL),
(100422,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding telophase 1 and 2. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. The nuclear membrane and nucleolus reappear, cytokinesis follows and this is called a dyad of cells\n2. Meiosis ends with telophase II\n3. Two groups of chromosomes get enclosed by a nuclear envelope','all are correct','In telophase 1, the nuclear membrane and nucleolus reappear, cytokinesis follows and this is called a dyad of cells. Meiosis ends with telophase II, in which the two groups of chromosomes once again get enclosed by a nuclear envelope.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 169, Paragraph no 2)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Anaphase and Telophase',NULL,'2','mcq','all are correct','2 and 3 are correct','1 and 3 are correct','only 2 is correct','NEW','11-169','11-127',NULL),
(100423,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding Interkinesis. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. The stage between the two meiotic divisions is called interkinesis \n2. It is generally short-lived.\n3. There is a replication of DNA during interkinesis.','1 and 2 are correct','The stage between the two meiotic divisions is called interkinesis and is generally short-lived. There is no replication of DNA during interkinesis. Interkinesis is followed by prophase II.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 169, Paragraph no 2).','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Anaphase and Telophase',NULL,'2','mcq','all are correct','1 and 2 are correct','1 and 3 are correct','2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-169','11-127',NULL),
(100424,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding Telophase. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. Chromosomes lose their individuality\n2. The nuclear envelope develops around the chromosome clusters at each pole forming two daughter nuclei. \n3. Last stage of karyokinesis','all are correct','In the final stage of karyokinesis, i.e., telophase, the chromosomes that have reached their respective poles decondense and lose their individuality. The nuclear envelope develops around the chromosome clusters at each pole forming two daughter nuclei.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 166, Paragraph no 3)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Anaphase and Telophase',NULL,'3','mcq','all are correct','1 and 3 are correct','2 and 3 are correct','only 3 is correct','NEW','11-166','11-124',NULL),
(100501,'chemistry','Match the molecular formula with their correct common names.','NaCl-Common salt,NaOH-Caustic soda,NaHCO₃-Baking soda,Na₂CO₃.10H₂O-Washing soda','NaCl - Common salt,\n\nNaOH - Caustic soda,\n\nNaHCO₃ - Baking soda,\n\nNa₂CO₃.10H₂O - Washing soda\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'3','match','NaCl,Common salt','NaOH,Caustic soda','NaHCO₃,Baking soda','Na₂CO₃.10H₂O,Washing soda','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100502,'chemistry','By which process is sodium carbonate prepared?','Solvay process','Sodium carbonate is generally prepared by Solvay Process. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','Speck process','Deacon\'s process','Solvay process','Haber\'s process','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100503,'chemistry','In solvay process, advantage of which sodium compound is taken?','Sodium hydrogen carbonate','In this process, advantage is taken of the low solubility of sodium hydrogencarbonate.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.4 2nd paragraph)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','Sodium hydrogen carbonate','Sodium sulphate','Sodium carbonate','Sodium chloride','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100504,'chemistry','Mark the false statement with respect to Solvay’s process.','Ammonium hydrogen carbonate is prepared by passing CO₂ to a concentrated solution of sodium hydroxide saturated with ammonia.','The correct statement is ""Ammonium hydrogen carbonate is prepared by passing CO₂ to a concentrated solution of sodium chloride" saturated with ammonia."\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'3','mcq','NaHCO₃ gets precipitated in the reaction of sodium chloride with ammonium hydrogen carbonate.','Ammonium hydrogen carbonate is prepared by passing CO₂ to a concentrated solution of sodium hydroxide saturated with ammonia.','Sodium hydrogen carbonate crystal separates.','Calcium chloride is obtained as a by-product','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100504q.png'),
(100505,'chemistry','Crystals of which sodium compound are heated to give sodium carbonate in Solvay’s process?','Sodium hydrogen carbonate.','Sodium hydrogencarbonate crystal separates. These are heated to give sodium carbonate\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 304; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'3','mcq','Sodium chloride','Sodium sulphate','Sodium hydrogen carbonate.','Sodium hydroxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100506,'chemistry','In this process NH₃ is recovered when the solution containing NH₄Cl is treated with -','Ca(OH)₂','In this process NH₃ is recovered when the solution containing NH₄Cl is treated with Ca(OH)₂.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'2','mcq','Ca(OH)₂','Mg(OH)₂','NaOH','KOH','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100507,'chemistry','Why Solvay process cannot be extended to the manufacture of potassium carbonate?','Because Potassium hydrogen carbonate is too soluble to be precipitated by the addition of ammonium hydrogencarbonate to a saturated solution of potassium chloride.','It may be mentioned here that Solvay process cannot be extended to the manufacture of potassium carbonate because potassium hydrogencarbonate is too soluble to be precipitated by the addition of ammonium hydrogencarbonate to a saturated solution of potassium chloride. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'2','mcq','Because Potassium carbonate is highly unstable.','Because Potassium hydrogen carbonate is too soluble to be precipitated by the addition of ammonium hydrogencarbonate to a saturated solution of potassium chloride.','Because Potassium forms complex with ammonium ion','Because Potassium hydrogencarbonate has a vey high thermal stability.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100508,'chemistry','In Na₂CO₃.xH₂O, what is x for washing soda?','10','Sodium carbonate is a white crystalline solid which exists as a decahydrate, Na₂CO₃.10H₂O .\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','10','8','6','2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100509,'chemistry','Mark the correct statement with respect to washing soda.','It is readily soluble in water.','Sodium carbonate is a white crystalline solid which exists as a decahydrate. \n\nIt is readily soluble in water. \n\nOn heating, the decahydrate loses its water of crystallisation to form monohydrate. \n\nCarbonate part of sodium carbonate gets hydrolysed by water to form an alkaline solution. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'2','mcq','It is readily soluble in water.','Sodium carbonate is a dark, crystalline solid.','It forms an acidic solution in water.','On heating, the decahydrate loses its water of crystallisation to form dihydrate.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100510,'chemistry','Above what temperature, the monohydrate becomes completely anhydrous?','373K','Above 373K, the monohydrate becomes completely anhydrous and changes to a white powder called soda ash.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','373K','473K','473⁰C','373⁰C','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100511,'chemistry','Above 373K, the monohydrate becomes completely anhydrous and changes to a white powder.What is white powder called?','Soda ash','Above 373K, the monohydrate becomes completely anhydrous and changes to a white powder called soda ash.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','Dead burnt mass','Amalgam','Soda ash','Monohydrate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100512,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','The solubility of NaCl increases appreciably with increase in temperature.','The most abundant source of sodium chloride is sea water which contains 2.7 to 2.9% by mass of the salt. \n\nIn tropical countries like India, common salt is generally obtained by evaporation of sea water. \n\nApproximately 50 lakh tons of salt are produced annually in India by solar evaporation.\n\nThe solubility does not increase appreciably with increase in temperature. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'2','mcq','The most abundant source of sodium chloride is sea water which contains 2.7 to 2.9% by mass of the salt.','In tropical countries like India, common salt is generally obtained by evaporation of sea water.','Approximately 50 lakh tons of salt are produced annually in India by solar evaporation.','The solubility of NaCl increases appreciably with increase in temperature.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100513,'chemistry','Crude sodium chloride, generally obtained by ____ of brine solution.','crystallisation','Crude sodium chloride, generally obtained by crystallisation of brine solution.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','crystallisation','evapouration','chromatography','electrophoresis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100514,'chemistry','Which of the following are impurities present in brine?\n\nA. Sodium sulphate\n\nB. Calcium sulphate\n\nC. Magnesium sulphate \n\nD. Calcium chloride \n\nE. Magnesium chloride','A+B+D+E','Brine solution, contains Sodium sulphate, Calcium sulphate, Calcium chloride and Magnesium chloride as impurities.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'3','mcq','A+B+C+E','A+C+D+E','A+B+C+D','A+B+D+E','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100515,'chemistry','What makes Calcium chloride and Magnesium chloride impurities?','Their deliquescent nature','Calcium chloride and magnesium chloride are impurities because they are deliquescent (absorb moisture easily from the atmosphere).\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','Their dessicant nature','Their hygroscopic nature.','Their deliquescent nature','Their efflorescent nature.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100516,'chemistry','Mark the false statement.','CaCl₂ is hygroscopic','Calcium chloride and magnesium chloride are impurities because they absorb moisture easily from the atmosphere(deliquescent).\n\nTo obtain pure sodium chloride, the crude salt is dissolved in minimum amount of water and filtered to remove insoluble impurities. \n\nIt has a solubility of 36.0 g in 100 g of water at 273 K. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'2','mcq','Calcium chloride and magnesium chloride are impurities because they absorb moisture easily from the atmosphere.','To obtain pure sodium chloride, the crude salt is dissolved in minimum amount of water and filtered to remove insoluble impurities.','Sodium chloride has a solubility of 36.0 g in 100 g of water at 273 K.','CaCl₂ is hygroscopic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100517,'chemistry','At which temperature, Sodium chloride melts?','1081 K','Sodium chloride melts at 1081K.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','1081 K','473 K','473⁰C','1081⁰C','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100518,'chemistry','To obtain pure sodium chloride, the crude salt is dissolved in minimum amount of water and filtered to remove insoluble impurities. TWhat is the solution is then saturated with?','HCl','To obtain pure sodium chloride, the crude salt is dissolved in minimum amount of water and filtered to remove insoluble impurities. \n\nThe solution is then saturated with hydrogen chloride gas. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','HCl','MgCl₂','CaCl₂','Na₂SO₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100519,'chemistry','Calcium and Magnesium chloride are  ___  soluble than/to Sodium chloride.','more','Calcium and magnesium chloride are more soluble than sodium chloride.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','more','less','equally','none','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110723,'biology','Pinus seeds cannot germinate and establish without the presence of mycorrhizae is an example of','Obligate association with the mycorrhizae','Some plants have an obligate association with the mycorrhizae. For example, Pinus seeds cannot germinate and establish without the presence of mycorrhizae\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 186','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','mcq','Obligate association with the mycorrhizae','Symbiotic association with the mycorrhiza','Undetermined parasitic association','None of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100520,'chemistry','Mark the correct statement with respect to obtaining pure sodium chloride from brine.','Calcium and magnesium chloride, being more soluble than sodium chloride, remain in solution.','Brine solution, contains sodium sulphate, calcium sulphate, calcium chloride and magnesium chloride as impurities. \n\nTo obtain pure sodium chloride, the crude salt is dissolved in minimum amount of water and filtered to remove insoluble impurities.\n\nThe solution is then saturated with hydrogen chloride gas. \n\nCrystals of pure sodium chloride separate out. \n\nCalcium and magnesium chloride, being more soluble than sodium chloride, remain in solution. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'2','mcq','To obtain pure sodium chloride, the crude salt is dissolved in minimum amount of water and filtered to remove soluble impurities.','The solution is then saturated with MgCl₂ . Crystals of pure sodium chloride separate out.','Calcium and magnesium chloride, being more soluble than sodium chloride, remain in solution.','Sodium sulphate is not an impurity in brine.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100521,'chemistry','How is Sodium hydroxide prepared commercially?','By the electrolysis of sodium chloride','Sodium hydroxide is generally prepared commercially by the electrolysis of sodium chloride.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','By the electrolysis of sodium carbonate','By the electrolysis of sodium bicarbonate','By the electrolysis of sodium chloride','By the electrolysis of sodium amalgam','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100522,'chemistry','What is the name of the cell used to prepare Sodium hydroxide commercially?','Castner-Kellner cell','Sodium hydroxide is generally prepared commercially by the electrolysis of sodium chloride in Castner-Kellner cell. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','Castner-Kellner cell','Hall-Heroult cell','Mond’s cell','Van Arkel’s cell','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100523,'chemistry','What is used at cathode during electrolysis of brine solution in Castner-Kellner cell?','Mercury','A brine solution is electrolysed using a mercury cathode and a carbon anode.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','Mercury','Carbon','Graphite','Platinum','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100524,'chemistry','What is used at anode during electrolysis of brine solution in Castner-Kellner cell?','Carbon','A brine solution is electrolysed using a mercury cathode and a carbon anode.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','Mercury','Carbon','Aluminium','Platinum','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100525,'chemistry','During electrolysis of brine solution, which gas is evolved?','Chlorine gas is evolved at the anode.','Chlorine gas is evolved at the anode. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'2','mcq','Chlorine gas is evolved at the anode.','Chlorine gas is evolved at the cathode.','Hydrogen gas is evolved at the anode.','Hydrogen gas is evolved at the cathode.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100526,'chemistry','Mark the false statement/reaction with respect to preparation of NaOH in Castner-Kellner cell .','2Na-amalgam + 2H₂O → 2NaOH+ 2Hg','2Na-amalgam + 2H₂O → 2NaOH+ 2Hg +H₂ \n\nSodium metal discharged at the cathode combines with mercury to form sodium amalgam. \n\nChlorine gas is evolved at the anode. \n\nThe amalgam is treated with water to give sodium hydroxide and hydrogen gas. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'2','mcq','Sodium metal discharged at the cathode combines with mercury to form sodium amalgam.','The amalgam is treated with water to give sodium hydroxide and hydrogen gas.','2Na-amalgam + 2H₂O → 2NaOH+ 2Hg','Chlorine gas is evolved at the anode.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100527,'chemistry','What is the colour of Sodium hydroxide?','White','Sodium hydroxide is a white, translucent solid. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','White','Purple','Deep blue','Yellow','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(100528,'chemistry','Sodium hydroxide is :','translucent solid','Sodium hydroxide is a white, translucent solid. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 305; Paragraph 10.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Important Compounds of Sodium',NULL,'1','mcq','translucent solid','transparent liquid','opaque solid','translucent liquid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100601,'chemistry','Match the element with its amount present (in g) in a typical 70 Kg man','Na-90 g,K-170 g,Fe-5 g,Cu-0.06 g','A typical 70 kg man contains about 90 g of Na and 170 g of K compared with only 5 g of iron and 0.06 g of copper. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Biological importance of Sodium and Potassium',NULL,'3','match','Na,90 g','K,170 g','Fe,5 g','Cu,0.06 g','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100602,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a function of Na and K in our body?','Cell membrane integrity','Na and K ions participate in the transmission of nerve signals, in regulating the flow of water across cell membranes and in the transport of sugars and amino acids into cells. \n\nCa performs the function of cell membrane integrity.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Biological importance of Sodium and Potassium',NULL,'2','mcq','Transmission of nerve signals.','Regulating the flow of water across cell membranes','Transport of sugars and amino acids into cells','Cell membrane integrity','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100603,'chemistry','Sodium ions are found primarily on the ___ of cells.','outside','Sodium ions are found primarily on the outside of cells. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Biological importance of Sodium and Potassium',NULL,'1','mcq','inside','outside','within','under','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100604,'chemistry','Where are Sodium ions primarily found?\n\n1. Interstitial fluid\n\n2. Blood plasma\n\n3. Intercellular fluid\n\n4. Inside of cells','In both 1. and 2.','Sodium ions are found primarily on the outside of cells, being located in blood plasma and in the interstitial fluid which surrounds the cells.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Biological importance of Sodium and Potassium',NULL,'3','mcq','In both 2. and 3.','In only 2.','In both 1. and 2.','In all 4 of the given','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110724,'biology','Match the following a and b of the given diagram (1103)','a-symplast,b-apoplast,c-endodermis,d-xylem','Figure 11.6 NCERT class 11 page.no-184 (1106)\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 186','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','match','a,symplast','b,apoplast','c,endodermis','d,xylem','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/110724q.png'),
(100605,'chemistry','Mark the false statement.','Potassium ions are found primarily on the outside of cells','Sodium ions are found primarily on the outside of cells.\n\nThus, potassium ions are the most abundant cations within cell fluids, where they activate many enzymes, participate in the oxidation of glucose to produce ATP and, with sodium, are responsible for the transmission of nerve signals. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Biological importance of Sodium and Potassium',NULL,'2','mcq','Potassium ions activate many enzymes.','Potassium ion participate in the oxidation of glucose to produce ATP','Na and K are responsible for transmission of nerve signals.','Potassium ions are found primarily on the outside of cells','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100606,'chemistry','Sodium and potassium are not different in which of their following functions?','Their qualitative ability to penetrate cell membranes','Sodium and potassium, although so similar chemically, differ quantitatively in their ability to penetrate cell membranes, in their transport mechanisms and in their efficiency to activate enzymes.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Biological importance of Sodium and Potassium',NULL,'2','mcq','Their quantitative ability to penetrate cell membranes','Their transport mechanisms','Their efficiency to activate enzymes','Their qualitative ability to penetrate cell membranes','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100607,'chemistry','Which ions are most abundant in cell fluid?','Potassium','Potassium ions are the most abundant cations within cell fluids. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Biological importance of Sodium and Potassium',NULL,'1','mcq','Sodium','Potassium','Lithium','Rubidium','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100608,'chemistry','Match the ions with their concentrations (mmol/L).','Blood plasma Na⁺-143 mmol/L,Blood plasma K⁺-5 mmol/L,Red blood cell Na⁺-10 mmol/L,Red blood cell K⁺-105 mmol/L','As a typical example, in blood plasma, sodium is present to the extent of 143 mmol/L , whereas the potassium level is only 15 mmol/L within the red blood cells. \n\nThese concentrations change to 10 mmol/L (Na⁺) and 105 mmol/L (K⁺ ). \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Biological importance of Sodium and Potassium',NULL,'3','match','Blood plasma Na⁺,143 mmol/L','Blood plasma K⁺,5 mmol/L','Red blood cell Na⁺,10 mmol/L','Red blood cell K⁺,105 mmol/L','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100609,'chemistry','Sodium-Potassium pump consumes about how much kg per 24 h in a resting human?','15 kg','The ionic gradients demonstrate that a discriminatory mechanism, called the Sodium-Potassium pump, operates across the cell membranes which consumes more than one-third of the ATP used by a resting animal and about 15 kg per 24 h in a resting human. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Biological importance of Sodium and Potassium',NULL,'1','mcq','12 kg','15 kg','18 kg','5 kg','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100610,'chemistry','Mark the false statement.','Sodium ions participate in the oxidation of glucose to produce ATP.','All the statements are correct except \'Sodium ions participate in the oxidation of glucose to produce ATP.\' . \n\nThe correct statement is \'Potassium ions participate in the oxidation of glucose to produce ATP.\'\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.5)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 1 elements - Biological importance of Sodium and Potassium',NULL,'2','mcq','There is a very considerable variation in the concentration of sodium and potassium ions found on the opposite sides of cell membranes.','The ionic gradients of Na and K demonstrate that a discriminatory mechanism, called the sodium-potassium pump, operates across the cell membranes.','Potassium ions are the most abundant cations within cell fluids, where they activate many enzymes.','Sodium ions participate in the oxidation of glucose to produce ATP.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100611,'biology','During which stage chromosomes start pairing together?','Zygotene','During Zygotene chromosomes start pairing together and this process of association is called synapsis.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'2','mcq','Pachytene','Leptotene','Zygotene','Diplotene','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100612,'biology','In Zygotene, paired chromosomes are called_________.','Homologous','During Zygotene chromosomes start pairing together and this process of association is called synapsis. Such paired chromosomes are called homologous chromosomes(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)...','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','fillup','Ho,mo,lo,go,us',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100613,'biology','The complex formed by a pair of synapsed homologous chromosomes is called a ________.','Both Bivalent and Tetrad','The complex formed by a pair of synapsed homologous chromosomes is called a bivalent or a tetrad. However, these are more clearly visible at the next stage.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'2','mcq','Bivalent','Tetrad','Both Bivalent and Tetrad','Synaptonemal complex','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100614,'biology','The first two stages of prophase I are relatively short-lived compared to the next stage.','TRUE','The first two stages of prophase I are relatively short-lived compared to the next stage that is pachytene. Electronmicrographs of this stage indicate that chromosome synapsis is accompanied by the formation of complex structure calledsynaptonemal complex which is mainly proteinaceous complex for stabilisation of bivalent/tetravalent. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:1-2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100615,'biology','During_______the four chromatids of each bivalent chromosomes becomes distinct and clearly appears as tetrads','Pachytene','The first two stages of prophase I are relatively short-lived compared to the next stage that is pachytene. During this stage, the four chromatids of each bivalent chromosomes becomes distinct and clearly appears as tetrads.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'2','mcq','Pachytene','Zygotene','Diplotene','Leptotene','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100616,'biology','______are the sites at which crossing over occurs between non-sister chromatids of the homologous chromosomes.','Recombination nodules','Pachytene stage is characterised by the appearance of recombination nodules, the sites at which crossing over occurs between non-sister chromatids of the homologous chromosomes. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','fillup','Re,co,mb,ina,t,ion, no,du,les',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100617,'biology','Assertion: Crossing over is also an enzyme-mediated process \nReason : Crossing over leads to recombination of genetic material on the two chromosomes','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Crossing over is also an enzyme-mediated process and the enzyme involved is called recombinase. Crossing over leads to recombination of genetic material on the two chromosomes. Recombination between homologous chromosomes is completed by the end of pachytene, leaving the chromosomes linked at the sites of crossing over.Recombinases are enzymes that catalyse site-specific recombination events within DNA. As recombination requires enzyme, crossing over must be enzyme mediated. Hence, reason is not the correct explanation for the assertion as there is no mention of the enzyme in the reason, it only specifies the significance of Crossing over','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100618,'biology','Recombination between homologous chromosomes is completed by the end of:','Pachytene','Recombination between homologous chromosomes is completed by the end of pachytene, leaving the chromosomes linked at the sites of crossing over.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'2','mcq','Pachytene','Leptotene','Zygotene','Diplotene','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100619,'biology','Match the following','Appearance of recombination nodules-Pachytene, Dissolution of the synaptonemal complex-Diplotene, Chromosomes are fully condensed-Diakinesis','Pachytene stage is characterised by the appearance of recombination nodules, the sites at which crossing over occurs. The beginning of diplotene is recognised by the dissolution of the synaptonemal complex and chiasma formation. The final stage of meiotic prophase I is diakinesis. This is marked by terminalisation of chiasmata, the chromosomes are fully condensed and the meiotic spindle is assembled to prepare the homologous chromosomes for separation.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2,3,4)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','match','Appearance of recombination nodules,Pachytene','Dissolution of the synaptonemal complex,Diplotene','Chromosomes are fully condensed,Diakinesis',NULL,'NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100620,'biology','Identify the diagram (1011)','Prophase I','Figure 10.3 NCERT class 11 page no: 169 represents prophase I of meiosis I; diagram (e1011)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','mcq','Prophase I','Metaphase I','Anaphase I','Telophase I','NEW','11-169','11-127','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100620q.png'),
(100621,'biology','The final stage of meiotic prophase I is _____  which is marked by terminalisation of chiasmata.','Diakinesis','The final stage of meiotic prophase I is diakinesis. This is marked by terminalisation of chiasmata.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:4)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'1','mcq','Diakinesis','Leptotene','Zygotene','Diplotene','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100622,'biology','Diakinesis represents transition to','Metaphase','By the end of diakinesis, the nucleolus disappears and the nuclear envelope also breaks down. Diakinesis represents transition to metaphase.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:4)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'2','mcq','Metaphase','Interphase','Anaphase','Telophase','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100623,'biology','During which stages (or prophase I substages) of meiosis do you expect to find the bivalents and DNA replication respectively?','Pachytene and S phase (of interphase just prior to prophase I)','In bivalent formation of chromosomes during meiosis, the homologous chromosomes are arranged in pairs. The phenomenon is called synapsis and it occurs during the end of zygotene stage, and may also appear at the start of pachytene but disappear towards the mid phase of pachytene,  DNA replication occurs during S phase or synthetic phase which is the second phase of interphase. During zygotene, homologous chromosomes begin to align along their entire length by a process called synapsis that is necessarily precise. Each pair of chromosomes is held together by a ribbon-like protein and forms the synaptonemal complex. Then, during pachytene, the pairs of chromosomes become condensed and coiled.','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','mcq','Pachytene and interphase (between two meiotic divisions)','Pachytene and interphase (just prior to prophase I)','Pachytene and S phase (of interphase just prior to prophase I)','Zygotene and S phase (of interphase prior to prophase I)','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100624,'biology','In oocytes of some vertebrates which substage of meiosis prophase can last for months or years','Diplotene','In oocytes of some vertebrates, diplotene can last for months or years. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:3)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','mcq','Leptotene','Diplotene','Pachytene','Zygotene','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100625,'biology','Assertion: During zygotene, chromosomes show bivalent stage.\nReason: Bivalent is composed an unit of homologous chromosomes','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','During zygotene, because of the pairing of the homologues, the nucleus contains half the number of chromosomes. Each unit is a bivalent composed of two homologous chromosomes.','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100627,'biology','Assertion : Meiotic division is reductional \nReason : In Meiosis, diploid cells after crossing over divide to form haploid daughter cells','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion','Diploid cell produce haploid cell by meiosis. It occurs due to synapsis where a diploid cells splits into two cells with haploid chromosomes. A chromosome has two chromatids, hence a bivalent has 4 chromatids.','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(110725,'biology','Match the following a and b of the given diagram (1104)','a-Symplastic path,b-Apoplastic path,c-Casparian strip','Figure 11.7 NCERT class 11 page.no-185 (1107)','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> How do Plants Absorb Water?',NULL,'2','match','a,Symplastic path','b,Apoplastic path','c,Casparian strip',NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/110725q.png'),
(100628,'biology','Assertion : Crossing over leads to recombination of genetic material on the two chromosomes.\n\n Reason : Crossing over is the exchange of genetic material between two homologous chromosomes.','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion','Crossing over occurs during pachytene. Recombination involves mutual exchange of the corresponding segments of nonsister chromatids of homologous chromosomes. It takes place by breakage and reunion of chromatid segments. Breakage, called nicking, is assisted by an enzyme endonuclease and reunion termed annealing, is aided by an enzyme ligase. Crossing over leads to recombination of genetic material on the two chromosomes.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100629,'biology','Assertion: The process of pairing of the chromosomes is called synapsis.\n\n Reason : Synapsis occurs during leptotene stage.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Synapsis occurs during zygotene stage of prophase. During this stage, chromosomes start pairing together and this process is called synapsis. Such paired chromosomes are called homologous chromosomes. Synapsis is accompanied by the formation of complex structure called synaptonemal complex. The compex formed by a pair of synapsed homologous chromosomes is called a bivalent or a tetrad.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100630,'biology','Assertion : Chiasmata is formed during diplotene. \n\nReason : Chiasmata are formed due to deposition of nucleoproteins.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','The points of attachment between the homologous chromosomes after the partial dissolution of nucleoprotein/synaptonemal complex, the X shaped structures are called chiasmata. It occurs during diplotene substage of prophase I. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:3)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100631,'biology','Meiosis is seen in- i) Spermatogenesis ii) Oogensis iii) Microgametogensis iv) Megagametogenesis.','i, ii, iii and iv','Meiosis ensures the production of haploid phase in the life cycle of sexually reproducing organisms whereas fertilisation restores the diploid phase. We come across meiosis during gametogenesis in plants and animals. This leads to the formation of haploid gametes. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:167, paragraph no:3)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','iii and iv','NEW','11-167','11-125',NULL),
(100632,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding Meiosis. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. Seen in Gametogenesis\n2. Production of daughter cells with half number of chromosomes\n3. Diploid gamete formation during the sexually active phase','1 and 2 are correct','The cell division that reduces the chromosome number by half results in the production of haploid daughter cells. This kind of division is called meiosis. Meiosis ensures the production of the haploid phase in the life cycle of sexually reproducing organisms whereas fertilisation restores the diploid phase. We come across meiosis during gametogenesis in plants and animals. This leads to the formation of haploid gametes.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 167, Paragraph no 3)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'2','mcq','all are correct','1 and 2 are correct','1 and 3 are correct','2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-167','11-125',NULL),
(100633,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding key features of meiosis. Which of the following is/are correct?\n1. Have two cycles Meiosis 1 and Meiosis 2\n2. Two times DNA replication\n3. Pairing of homologous chromosomes and recombination between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.','1 and 3 are correct','Meiosis involves two sequential cycles of nuclear and cell division called meiosis I and meiosis II but only a single cycle of replication. Meiosis involves the pairing of homologous chromosomes and recombination between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 167, bullet points)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'3','mcq','1 and 3 are correct','2 and 3 are correct','1 and 2 are correct','all are correct','NEW','11-167','11-125',NULL),
(100634,'biology','Assertion: Meiosis involves two sequential cycles of nuclear and cell division called\nmeiosis I and meiosis II and two cycles of DNA replication. \nReason: Four diploid cells are formed at the end of meiosis II.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','Meiosis involves two sequential cycles of nuclear and cell division called meiosis I and meiosis II but only a single cycle of replication. Four haploid cells are formed at the end of meiosis II.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 167, bullet points)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-167','11-125',NULL),
(100635,'biology','Assertion: Crossing over is the exchange of genetic material between two homologous chromosomes\nReason: Crossing over leads to recombination of the genetic material of the four chromosomes','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Crossing over is the exchange of genetic material between two homologous chromosomes. Crossing over leads to the recombination of the genetic material of the two chromosomes, not four.  (Reference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 168, Paragraph no 2)','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis and Prophase I',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(110801,'chemistry','The element having less than 4 electrons in its Valance shell ,but it is not a metal','B','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','B','Al','Ga','C','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(110802,'chemistry','Which element forms an principal ion with charge 3+','Al','Even though many mentioned elements are capable of 3+,The Principal ion for Aluminium is 3+.Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph 3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'1','mcq','B','Al','C','Si','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(100636,'biology','The beginning of diplotene is recognised by','Dissolution of the synaptonemal complex and bivalents separate from each other except at the sites of crossovers.','Beginning of diplotene is recognised by the dissolution of the synaptonemal complex and the tendency of the recombined homologous chromosomes of the bivalents to separate from each other\nexcept at the sites of crossovers.','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Meiosis I',NULL,'2','mcq','Dissolution of the synaptonemal complex only','Terminalisation of chiasmata','Bivalents separate from each other except at the sites of crossovers','Dissolution of the synaptonemal complex and bivalents separate from each other except at the sites of crossovers.','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100701,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a group 2 element ?','Na','The group 2 elements comprise Beryllium, Magnesium, Calcium, Strontium, Barium and Radium. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Be','Mg','Na','Sr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100701q.png'),
(100702,'chemistry','Which of the following is not an alkaline earth metal ?','Be','Group 2 elements except beryllium are known as alkaline earth metals.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraphgraph 10.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Be','Mg','Ca','Sr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100703,'chemistry','Be shows diagonal relationship with which element?','Al','The first element Beryllium differs from the rest of the members and shows diagonal relationship to Aluminium.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.6)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Be','Al','Ga','In','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100703q.png'),
(100704,'chemistry','Alkaline Earth elements have how many electrons in their valence shell?','2','Group 2 elements have two electrons in the s-orbital of the valence shell.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.6.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100704q.png'),
(100705,'chemistry','How is the general electronic configuration of alkaline earth metals represented?','ns²','The general electronic configuration may be represented as [noble gas] ns². \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.6.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','ns¹','ns²','ns²np¹','ns²np²','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100706,'chemistry','What kinds of compounds are predominantly formed by group 2 elements?','Ionic','Like alkali metals, the compounds of these elements are also predominantly ionic \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 306; Paragraph 10.6.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements- Properties and Uses',NULL,'1','mcq','Ionic','Covalent','Acidic','Coordinate','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
