(100803,'chemistry','The alkaline earth metals form compounds which are predominantly ionic but less ionic than the corresponding compounds of alkali metals. This is due to ________ nuclear charge and smaller size.','increased','The alkaline earth metals form compounds which are predominantly ionic but less ionic than the corresponding compounds of alkali metals. \n\nThis is due to increased nuclear charge \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','decreased','increased','equal','less','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100803q.png'),
(100804,'chemistry','The alkaline earth metals form compounds which are predominantly ionic but less ionic than the corresponding compounds of alkali metals due to increased nuclear charge and ______ size','smaller','The alkaline earth metals form compounds which are predominantly ionic but less ionic than the corresponding compounds of alkali metals. \n\nThis is due to increased nuclear charge and smaller size \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','larger','smaller','equal','massive','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100805,'chemistry','The oxides and other compounds of Beryllium and Magnesium are ____ covalent than those formed by the heavier and large sized members.','more','The oxides and other compounds of Beryllium and Magnesium are more covalent than those formed by the heavier and large sized members (Ca, Sr, Ba). \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','lesser','more','equally','cannot be compared','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100805q.png'),
(100806,'chemistry','What is the general formula of oxides of group 2?','MO','The alkaline earth metals burn in oxygen to form the monoxide MO. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','MO₃','MO','MO₂','MO₄','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100806q.png'),
(100807,'chemistry','Which among the following monoxides doesn\'t have a rock-salt structure?','BeO','The alkaline earth metals burn in oxygen to form the monoxide, MO which, except for BeO, have rock-salt structure. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','MgO','CaO','BeO','SrO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100807q.png'),
(100808,'chemistry','What is the nature of BeO?','Amphoteric','BeO is amphoteric while oxides of other elements are ionic in nature.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','Acidic','Basic','Amphoteric','Neutral','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100809,'chemistry','What is the nature of alkaline earth metal oxides [except BeO]?','Basic','All these oxides except BeO are basic in nature and react with water to form sparingly soluble hydroxides.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','Acidic','Basic','Amphoteric','Neutral','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100810,'chemistry','What is the trend of solubility of Group 2 hydroxides with increasing atomic number from Mg(OH)₂ to Ba(OH)₂.?','Solubility increases','The solubility of these hydroxides increase with increasing atomic number from Mg(OH)₂ to Ba(OH)₂.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','Solubility increases','Solubility decreases','Solubility stays same','Solubility follows no regular pattern','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100811,'chemistry','What is the trend of thermal stability of Group 2 hydroxides with increasing atomic number from Mg(OH)₂ to Ba(OH)₂.?','Thermal stability increases','The thermal stability and the basic character of these hydroxides increase with increasing atomic number from Mg(OH)₂ to Ba(OH)₂. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','Thermal stability increases','Thermal stability decreases','Thermal stability stays same','Thermal stability follows no regular pattern','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100812,'chemistry','What is the trend of basic character of Group 2 hydroxides with increasing atomic number from Mg(OH)₂ to Ba(OH)₂.?','Basic character increases','The solubility, thermal stability and the basic character of these hydroxides increase with increasing atomic number from Mg(OH)₂ to Ba(OH)₂.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','Basic character increases','Basic character decreases','Basic character stays same','Basic character follows no regular pattern','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100813,'chemistry','The alkaline earth metal hydroxides are ______ basic than alkali metal hydroxides.','less','The alkaline earth metal hydroxides are, less basic than alkali metal hydroxides.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 309; Paragraph 10.7)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - General Characteristics',NULL,'1','mcq','more','equally','less','cannot be compared','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100901,'chemistry','Which member of group 2 exhibits anomalous behavior?','Be','Beryllium, the first member of the Group 2 metals, shows anomalous behaviour as compared to magnesium and rest of the members. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'1','mcq','Be','Mg','Ca','Ba','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100902,'chemistry','How many statements are correct? \n\n1) Be has exceptionally small atomic size.\n\n2) Be has low ionisation enthalpy. \n\n3) Be forms predominantly ionic compounds. \n\n4) Be compounds get easily hydrolysed.','2','Beryllium has exceptionally small atomic and ionic sizes and thus does not compare well with other members of the group. \n\nBecause of high ionisation enthalpy and small size it forms compounds which are largely covalent and get easily hydrolysed. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100903,'chemistry','Assertion : Be forms largely covalent compounds \n\nReason : It has high ionisation enthalpy and small size\n\nChoose the correct option.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Beryllium has exceptionally small atomic and ionic sizes and thus does not compare well with other members of the group. \n\nBecause of high ionisation enthalpy and small size it forms compounds which are largely covalent and get easily hydrolysed. \n\nThus, both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Reason is false but the Assertion is true','Both the statements are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100904,'chemistry','Beryllium does not exhibit coordination number more than ______.','4','Beryllium does not exhibit coordination number more than four. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'1','mcq','3','4','5','6','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100905,'chemistry','The remaining members of the group (other than Be) can have a coordination number of _____ by making use of d-orbitals.','6','Beryllium does not exhibit coordination number more than four as in its valence shell there are only four orbitals. \n\nThe remaining members of the group can have a coordination number of six by making use of d-orbitals. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'1','mcq','4','5','6','8','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100906,'chemistry','The oxide of Beryllium is _____ in nature.','Amphoteric','The oxide and hydroxide of beryllium, unlike the hydroxides of other elements in the group, are amphoteric in nature. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'1','mcq','Acidic','Basic','Neutral','Amphoteric','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100907,'chemistry','What is the nature of hydroxide of Beryllium?','Amphoteric','The oxide and hydroxide of beryllium, unlike the hydroxides of other elements in the group, are amphoteric in nature. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'1','mcq','Acidic','Basic','Neutral','Amphoteric','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100908,'chemistry','Assertion : Beryllium resembles Aluminium in some ways;\n \nReason : The charge/radius ratio of dipositive Be ion is nearly the same as that of the tripositive Al ion.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The ionic radius of Be²⁺ is estimated to be 31 pm; the charge/radius ratio is nearly the same as that of the Al³⁺ ion. \n\nHence beryllium resembles aluminium in some ways. \n\nThus, both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Reason is false but the Assertion is true','Both the statements are false','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100909,'chemistry','Like Aluminium, Beryllium is not readily attacked by acids because of the presence of an ______ film on the surface of the metal','oxide','Like aluminium, beryllium is not readily attacked by acids because of the presence of an oxide film on the surface of the metal.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'1','mcq','sulphide','carbonate','oxide','chloride','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100910,'chemistry','What does Beryllium hydroxide give on dissolving it in excess of alkali?','Beryllate ion','Beryllium hydroxide dissolves in excess of alkali to give a beryllate ion. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'1','mcq','Beryllate ion','Hydroxide ion','Oxide ion','Peroxide ion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100911,'chemistry','What does Aluminium hydroxide give on dissolving it in excess of alkali?','Aluminate ion','Beryllium hydroxide dissolves in excess of alkali to give a beryllate ion, just as aluminium hydroxide gives aluminate ion. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydroxide ion','Aluminate ion','Peroxide ion','Oxide ion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100912,'chemistry','How many statements are correct for chlorides of Be and Al?\n \n1) Have chloro bridged structure in vapour state \n\n2) Insoluble in organic solvents \n\n3) Strong Lewis bases \n\n4) Used as Friedel Crafts\' catalysts`','2','The chlorides of both Beryllium and Aluminium have Chloro-bridged chloride structure in vapour phase. \n\nBoth the chlorides are soluble in organic solvents and are strong Lewis acids. \n\nThey are used as Friedel Craft catalysts.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Anomalous Behaviour of Be and Diagonal relationship',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(100914,'biology','The enzyme recombinase is required at which stage of meiosis?[NEET 2014]','Pachytene','Pachytene is characterized by the appearance of recombination nodules, the sites at which crossing over occurs between non­sister chromatids of the homologous chromosomes.Nodules contain multienzyme complex called recombinase. Recombinase is made of endonuclease, exonuclease, unwindase, R­protein, etc.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Pachytene','Zygotene','Diplotene','Diakinesis','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100915,'biology','The complex formed by a pair of synapsed homologous chromosomes is called [NEET 2013]','Bivalent','During zygotene stage chromosomes start pairing together and this process of association is called synapsis.Such paired chromosomes are called homologous chromosomes. Electron micrographs of this stage indicate that chromosome synapsis is accompanied by the formation of complex structure called synaptonemal complex. The complex formed by a pair of synapsed homologous chromosomes is called a bivalent or a tetrad. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Bivalent','Axoneme','Equatorial plate','Kinetochore','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100916,'biology','A stage in cell division is shown in the diagram . Select the answer which gives correct identi­ fication of the stage with its characteristics. (1019)','Telophase-Nuclear envelope reforms,Golgi complex reforms','The figure given in the question shows a stage of mitotic cell division called as telophase stage.The individual chromosomes are no longer seen and chromatin material tends to collect in a mass at the two poles. This is the stage which shows the following key events: - Chromosomes cluster at opposite spindle poles and their identity is lost as discrete elements. -Nuclear envelope assembles around the chromosome clusters. -Nucleolus, Golgi complex and ER reform.  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:166, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Cytokinesis-Cell plate formed, mitochondria distributed between two daughter cells.','Telophase-Endoplasmic reticulum and nucleolus not reformed yet.','Telophase-Nuclear envelope reforms,Golgi complex reforms','Late anaphase-Chromosomes move away from equatorial plate, Golgi complex not present.','NEW','11-166','11-124','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100916q.png'),
(100917,'biology','During meiosis I, the chromosomes start pairing at[NEET 2013]','Zygotene','During zygotene or zygonema of meiotic prophase I the chromosomes become shorter and thicker. The homologous chromosomes come to lie side­by­side in pairs. This pairing of homologous chromosomes is known as synapsis, or syndesis. A pair of homologous chromosomes lying together is called a bivalent(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Zygotene','Pachytene','Diplotene','Leptotene','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100918,'biology','During the metaphase stage of mitosis, spindle fibres attach to chromosomes at[NEET 2013]','Kinetochore','In metaphase, chromosomes consisting of two sister chromatids get arranged at equator. Discontinuous fibres radiate out from two spindle poles and get connected to the disc shaped structure at the surface of the centromere called kinetochores. These are known as chromosome fibres or tractile fibrils. A kinetochore is a complex protein structure that is analogous to a ring for the microtubule hook; it is the point where microtubules attach themselves to the chromosome(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:165, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Kinetochore','Both centromere and kinetochore','Centromere, kinetochore and areas adjoining centromere','Centromere','NEW','11-165','11-123',NULL),
(100919,'biology','A stage of mitosis is shown in the diagram . Which stage is it and what are its characteristics?[ NEET 2013] (1020)','Metaphase ­ Chromosomes moved to spindle equator, chromosomes made up of two sister chromatids.','In metaphase, chromosomes consisting of two sister chromatids get arranged at equator. Discontinuous fibres radiate out from two spindle poles and get connected to the disc shaped structure at the surface of the centromere called kinetochores. These are known as chromosome fibres or tractile fibrils. A kinetochore is a complex protein structure that is analogous to a ring for the microtubule hook; it is the point where microtubules attach themselves to the chromosome(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:165, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Metaphase ­ Spindle fibers attached to kinetochores, centromeres split and chromatids separate.','Metaphase ­ Chromosomes moved to spindle equator, chromosomes made up of two sister chromatids.','Anaphase ­ Centromeres split and chromatids separate and start moving away.','Late prophase ­ Chromosomes move to spindle equator','NEW','11-165','11-123','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/100919q.png'),
(100920,'biology','During gamete formation, the enzyme recombinase participates during[AIPMT 2012]','Prophase I','During gamete formation, the enzyme recombinase participates during pachytene stage of prophase I. This stage is characterized by the appearance of recombination nodules, the sites at which crossing over occurs between non­sister chromatids of the homologous chromosomes. Crossing over is the exchange of genetic material between two homologous chromosomes. Crossing over is also an enzyme­mediated process and the enzyme involved is called recombinase.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:1-2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Metaphase I','Anaphase II','Prophase I','Prophase II','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100921,'biology','Identify the meiotic stage in which the homologous chromosomes separate while the sister chromatids remain associated at their centromeres.[AIPMT 2012]','Anaphase I','During anaphase I, from each tetrad two chromatids of a chromosome move as a unit (dyad) to one pole of a spindle, and the remaining two chromatids of its homologue migrate to the opposite pole. Thus, the homologous chromosomes of each pair, rather than the chromatids of a chromosome, are separated. As a result, half of the chromosomes, which appear in early prophase, go to each pole. Thus the paternal and maternal chromosomes of each homologous pair segregate during anaphase I independently of the other chromosomes. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:169, paragraph no:1)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Metaphase I','Metaphase II','Anaphase I','Anaphase II','NEW','11-169','11-127',NULL),
(100922,'biology','Select the correct option with respect to mitosis[AIPMT 2011]','Chromosomes move to the spindle equator and get aligned along equatorial plate in metaphase','The plane of alignment of the chromosomes at metaphase is referred to as the metaphase plate. The key features of metaphase are, (1) Spindle fibres attach to kinetochores of chromosomes (2) Chromosomes are moved to spindle equator and get aligned along metaphase plate through spindle fibres to both poles. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:165, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Chromatids separate but remain in the cen­tre of the cell in anaphase','Chromatids start moving towards opposite poles in telophase','Golgi complex and endoplasmic reticulum are still visible at the end of prophase','Chromosomes move to the spindle equator and get aligned along equatorial plate in metaphase','NEW','11-165','11-123',NULL),
(100923,'biology','At metaphase, chromosomes are attached to the spindle fibres by their[AIPMT 2011]','Kinetochores','The key feature of metaphase is the attachment of spindle fibres to kinetochores of chromosomes. Kinetochores are disc­shaped structures at the surface of the centromeres. These structures serve as the sites of attachment of spindle fibres to the chromosomes that are moved into position. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:165, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Satellites','Secondary constrictions','Kinetochores','Centromeres','NEW','11-165','11-123',NULL),
(100924,'biology','During mitosis, ER and nucleolus begin to disappear at[AIPMT 2010]','Early Prophase','During early prophase of mitosis, nucleus and cell become spheroid. Viscocity and refractivity of cytoplasm increases. DNA molecules condense to form shortened chromosome. Endoplasmic reticulum and nucleolus starts disappearing.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:165, paragraph no:1)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Late Prophase','Early Metaphase','Late Metaphase','Early Prophase','NEW','11-165','11-123',NULL),
(100925,'biology','Which of the following stages of meiosis involves division of centromere?[NEET 2021]','Anaphase II','Anaphase II: It begins with the simultaneous splitting of the centromere of each chromosome (which was holding the sister chromatids together), allowing them to move toward opposite poles of the cell by shortening of microtubules attached to kinetochores.  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Telophase II','Metaphase I','Metaphase II','Anaphase II','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(100926,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. [NEET 2021]','S phase-DNA replication, G2 phase-Proteins are synthesized, Quiescent stage-Inactive phase, G1 phase-Interval between mitosis and initiation of DNA replication','The interphase is divided into three further phases: 1) G1 phase (Gap 1)- G1 phase corresponds to the interval between mitosis and initiation of DNA replication 2) S phase (Synthesis)-S or synthesis phase marks the period during which DNA synthesis or replication takes place wherebthe amount of DNA per cell doubles. 3) G2 phase (Gap 2)- During the G2 phase, proteins are synthesised in preparation for mitosis while cell growth continues. G0 phase is the inactive stage, cells in this stage remain metabolically active but no longer proliferate..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:163; paragraph no:4-5 and page no:164; paragraph no:1)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','match','S phase,DNA replication','G2 phase,Proteins are synthesized','Quiescent stage,Inactive phase','G1 phase,Interval between mitosis and initiation of DNA replication','NEW','11-164','11-122',NULL),
(100927,'biology','The centriole undergoes duplication during [NEET 2021]','S-phase','In animal cells, during the S phase of interphase, DNA replication begins in the nucleus and the centriole duplicates in the cytoplasm. These duplicated centrioles move towards opposite poles of the cell in prophase of mitosis. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:163, paragraph no:4)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','G2 phase','S-phase','Prophase','Metaphase','NEW','11-163','11-121',NULL),
(100928,'biology','The fruit fly has 8 chromosomes (2n) in each cell. During interphase of Mitosis if the number of chromosomes at G1 phase is 8, what would be the number of chromosomes after S phase?[NEET 2021]','8','S or synthesis phase marks the period during which DNA synthesis or replication takes place. During this time the amount of DNA per cell doubles. If the initial amount of DNA is denoted as 2C then it increases to 4C. However, there is no increase in the chromosome number; if the cell had diploid or 2n number of chromosomes at G1, even after S phase the number of chromosomes remains the same, i.e., 2n.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:163, paragraph no:5)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','32','8','16','4','NEW','11-163','11-121',NULL),
(101001,'chemistry','What is the approximate temperature range for manufacturing CaO? (in K)','1070 K - 1270 K','CaO is prepared on a commercial scale by heating limestone in a rotary kiln at 1070-1270 K.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Some Important Compounds of Calcium',NULL,'1','mcq','970 K - 1070 K','1070 K - 1270 K','1270 K - 1470 K','1370 K - 1470 K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/101001q.png'),
(101002,'chemistry','CaO is prepared on a commercial scale by heating limestone in a ______ kiln at 1070-1270 K.','rotary','CaO is prepared on a commercial scale by heating limestone in a rotary kiln at 1070-1270 K.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Some Important Compounds of Calcium',NULL,'1','mcq','rotary','sprung','gas','electric','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101003,'chemistry','Which gas is released on decomposition of CaCO₃?','Carbon dioxide','The reaction is given as : CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Some Important Compounds of Calcium',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon monoxide','Carbon dioxide','Carbon trioxide','Carbon sesquioxide','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/101003q.png'),
(101004,'chemistry','What is the colour of CaO?','White','Calcium oxide is a white amorphous solid. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Some Important Compounds of Calcium',NULL,'1','mcq','Reddish','Grey','Black','White','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101005,'chemistry','Melting point of CaO lies in which range of temperature? (in K)','2500 K - 3000 K','It has a melting point of 2870 K.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Some Important Compounds of Calcium',NULL,'1','mcq','1500 K - 2000 K','2000 K - 2500 K','2500 K - 3000 K','3000 K - 3500 K','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101006,'chemistry','On exposure to atmosphere, what does CaO absorb?','Moisture and Carbon dioxide','On exposure to atmosphere, it absorbs moisture and carbon dioxide. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 310; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Some Important Compounds of Calcium',NULL,'1','mcq','Moisture and Carbon monoxide','Moisture and Carbon dioxide','Moisture and Nitrous oxide.','Moisture and Oxygen gas','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101007,'chemistry','The addition of limited amount of water breaks the lump of lime. What is this process?','Slaking of lime','The addition of limited amount of water breaks the lump of lime. \n\nThis process is called slaking of lime. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 311; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Some Important Compounds of Calcium',NULL,'1','mcq','Purification of lime','Precipitation of lime','Slaking of lime','Suspension of lime','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101008,'chemistry','What does quick lime slaked with soda give?','Soda lime','Quick lime slaked with soda gives solid sodalime. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 311; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Some Important Compounds of Calcium',NULL,'1','mcq','Soda lime','Milk of magnesia','Slaked lime','Milk of lime','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101009,'chemistry','CaO may not combine with how many among the following at high temperatures?\n \n1) Silicon dioxide \n\n2) Tetraphosphorous decaoxide \n\n3) Sodium oxide \n\n4) Rubidium superoxide','2','Being a basic oxide, CaO combines with acidic oxides at high temperature.\n\nOut of the given four compounds in the question, CaO combines with Silicon dioxide and Tetraphosphorous decaoxide. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 311; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Some Important Compounds of Calcium',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101010,'chemistry','CaO may combine with how many among the following at high temperatures? \n\n1) Silicon dioxide \n\n2) Tetraphosphorous decaoxide \n\n3) Sodium oxide \n\n4) Rubidium superoxide','2','Being a basic oxide, CaO combines with acidic oxides at high temperature. \n\nOut of the given four compounds in the question, CaO combines with Silicon dioxide and Tetraphosphorous decaoxide. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 311; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Some Important Compounds of Calcium',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101011,'chemistry','Which is the cheapest form of alkali?','CaO','CaO is an important primary material for manufacturing cement and is the cheapest form of alkali. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 311; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Some Important Compounds of Calcium',NULL,'1','mcq','CaO','MgO','SrO','BaO','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
