(101102,'chemistry','How much gram of Ca is present in adult body?','1200 g','An adult body contains about 25 g of Mg and 1200 g of Ca compared with only 5 g of iron and 0.06 g of copper. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','1200 g','1400 g','1600 g','1800 g','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101103,'chemistry','How much gram of Fe is present in adult body?','5 g','An adult body contains about 25 g of Mg and 1200 g of Ca compared with only 5 g of iron and 0.06 g of copper. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','5 g','15 g','25 g','30 g','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101104,'chemistry','How much gram of Cu is present in adult body?','0.06 g','An adult body contains about 25 g of Mg and 1200 g of Ca compared with only 5 g of iron and 0.06 g of copper. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','0.06 g','0.6 g','0.006 g','6 g','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101105,'chemistry','What is used as co-factor by all enzymes that utilise ATP in phosphate transfer?','Mg','All enzymes that utilise ATP in phosphate transfer require magnesium as the cofactor.\n \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','Be','Mg','Ca','Sr','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101106,'chemistry','What does the main pigment for the absorption of light in plants is chlorophyll contain?','Mg','The main pigment for the absorption of light in plants is chlorophyll which contains magnesium.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','Mg','Ca','Sr','Na','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101107,'chemistry','About 99 % of body _______ is present in bones and teeth.','Ca','About 99 % of body calcium is present in bones and teeth. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','Ca','Mg','P','Na','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101108,'chemistry','The calcium concentration in plasma is regulated at about ____ mg/L.','100','The calcium concentration in plasma is regulated at about 100 mg/L.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','100','200','300','400','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101109,'chemistry','Bone is not an inert and unchanging substance but is continuously being solubilised and redeposited to the extent of _____ mg per day in man.','400','Bone is not an inert and unchanging substance but is continuously being solubilised and redeposited to the extent of 400 mg per day in man.\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','400','300','500','600','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101110,'chemistry','How many from the following are the functions of Ca?\n \n1) Role in neuromucular function \n\n2) Part of chlorophyll \n\n3) Cell membrane integrity \n\n4) Transport of oxygen in blood\n \n5) Blood coagulation \n\n6) Interneuronal transmission','4','Ca also plays important roles in neuromuscular function, interneuronal transmission, cell membrane integrity and blood coagulation. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','4','5','6','3','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101111,'chemistry','How many from the following are not the functions of Ca?\n \n1) Role in neuromucular function \n\n2) Part of chlorophyll \n\n3) Cell membrane integrity \n\n4) Transport of oxygen in blood \n\n5) Blood coagulation \n\n6) Interneuronal transmission','2','Ca also plays important roles in neuromuscular function, interneuronal transmission, cell membrane integrity and blood coagulation. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','2','3','4','5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101112,'chemistry','Calcium concentration in plasma is regulated by ________ hormones.','calcitonin','Calcium concentration in plasma is maintained by two hormones: \n\ncalcitonin and parathyroid hormone. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 312; Paragraph 10.9)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','calcitonin','thyroid','pituatary','hypothalmic','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101113,'chemistry','What is the daily requirement of Ca and Mg in the human body?','0.2 to 0.3 g','The daily requirement in the human body has been estimated to be 200-300 mg.','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements - Biological Importance of Mg and Ca',NULL,'1','mcq','0.2 to 0.3 g','1.2 to 1.4 g','0.8 to 1.0 g','0.5 to 0.6 g','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101114,'biology','Assertion : Interphase is resting stage.\n\n Reason : The interphase cell is metabolically inactive.[AIIMS 2012 ]','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Previously interphase is called resting stage because there is no apparent activity related to cell division. The interphase cell is metabolically quite active. Interphase consist of three subphases (G1 , G2 and S). Synthesis of DNA occurs in S phase. G1 is the period between the end of mitosis and the start of S phase. G2 is the phase between S phase and the next mitosis. As the synthesis of DNA and proteins occurs in interphase so, it is considered as metabolically active phase. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:163, paragraph no:1-5)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-163','11-121',NULL),
(101115,'biology','Assertion : During zygotene, chromosomes show bivalent stage. \n\nReason : Bivalent is half the number of chromosomes[AIIMS 2013]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','During zygotene, because of the pairing of the homologues, the nucleus contains half the number of chromosomes. Each unit is a bivalent composed of two homologous chromosomes.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:2)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(101116,'biology','Assertion : The stage between two meiotic divisions is called interkinesis. \n\nReason : Interkinesis is generally short lived.[AIIMS 2016]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Interkinesis or interphase II is a period of rest that cells of some species enter during meiosis, between meiosis I and meiosis II. No DNA replication occurs during interkinesis however it does occur during the interphase I stage of meiosis. Interkinesis is generally short lived. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:169, paragraph no:1)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-169','11-127',NULL),
(101117,'biology','Assertion : Diplotene is characterized by the presence of chiasmata. \n\nReason : Diplotene can last for months and years in oocytes of some vertebrates.[AIIMS 2016]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The beginning of diplotene is recognised by the dissolution of the synaptonemal complex and the tendency of the recombined homologous chromosomes of the bivalents to separate from each other except at the sites of crossovers. These X-shaped structures, are called chiasmata. In oocytes of some vertebrates, diplotene can last for months or years.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11; page no:168, paragraph no:3)..','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-168','11-126',NULL),
(101201,'chemistry','Alkaline earth elements\' oxides and hydroxides are ____ basic than the alkali metal oxides and hydroxides.','less','Their oxides and hydroxides are less basic than the alkali metal oxides and hydroxides. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313; Summary)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements >>Summary',NULL,'1','mcq','more','less','equally','cannot be compared','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101202,'chemistry','Cement is manufactured by heating a pulverised mixture of limestone and clay in a ______','rotary kiln','Cement is manufactured by heating a pulverised mixture of limestone and clay in a rotary kiln. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313; Summary)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements >>Summary',NULL,'1','mcq','rotary kiln','electric kiln','gas kiln',NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/101202q.png'),
(101203,'chemistry','The first element in each of these groups, lithium in Group 1 and beryllium in Group 2 shows similarities in properties to the _____ member of the next group.','second','The first element in each of these groups, lithium in Group 1 and beryllium in Group 2 shows similarities in properties to the second member of the next group. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313; Summary)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Group 2 Elements >>Summary',NULL,'1','mcq','first','second','third','fourth','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101204,'biology','The stage of cell cycle at which Chromosomes are moved to spindle equator','Metaphase.','Solution to Exercise question 6(i)\nReference  : NCERT biology class 11: Page no: 165, Metaphase, Paragraph no 2','Cell Structure and Functions >> Cell Cycle and Division >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Anaphase','Metaphase.','Prophase','Cytokinesis','NEW','11-165','11-123',NULL),
(101301,'chemistry','What is the oxidation state of Na in Na₂O₂ ?','1','Let oxidation state of Na be x. \n\nThen 2x - 2 = 0 which upon solving comes to 1. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','-1','-2','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101302,'chemistry','How many statements are not correct? \n\n1) Alkali metals cannot be obtained by chemical reduction methods \n\n2) Alkaline earth metals cannot be obtained by chemical reduction methods\n \n3) Li is used in photocells \n\n4) Ce is used in photocells','2','Alkali and alkaline earth metals not be obtained by chemical reduction methods. \n\nPotassium and Caesium, rather than lithium used in photoelectric cells. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101303,'chemistry','How many statements are correct? \n\n1) Alkali metals cannot be obtained by chemical reduction methods \n\n2) Alkaline earth metals cannot be obtained by chemical reduction methods\n \n3) Li is used in photocells \n\n4) Ce is used in photocells','2','Alkali and alkaline earth metals not be obtained by chemical reduction methods. \n\nPotassium and Caesium, rather than lithium used in photoelectric cells. \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise)','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101304,'chemistry','How many statements are not correct? \n\n1) Beryllium and magnesium do not give colour to flame whereas other alkaline earth metals do so.\n\n2) Potassium carbonate cannot be prepared by Solvay process.\n\n3) Li₂CO₃ is decomposed at a lower temperature whereas Na₂CO₃ at higher temperature.\n\n4) Alkali metals are easily found freely in nature.','1','1) Beryllium and magnesium do not give colour to flame whereas other alkaline earth metals do so \n\n2) Potassium carbonate cannot be prepared by Solvay process \n\n3) Li₂CO₃ is decomposed at a lower temperature whereas Na₂CO₃ at higher temperature \n\n4) Alkali metals aren\'t found freely in nature \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','2','3','4','1','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101305,'chemistry','How many statements are correct? \n\n1) Beryllium and magnesium do not give colour to flame whereas other alkaline earth metals do so.\n\n2) Potassium carbonate cannot be prepared by Solvay process.\n\n3) Li₂CO₃ is decomposed at a lower temperature whereas Na₂CO₃ at higher temperature.\n\n4) Alkali metals are easily found freely in nature.','3','1) Beryllium and magnesium do not give colour to flame whereas other alkaline earth metals do so \n\n2) Potassium carbonate cannot be prepared by Solvay process\n \n3) Li₂CO₃ is decomposed at a lower temperature whereas Na₂CO₃ at higher temperature \n\n4) Alkali metals aren\'t found freely in nature \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','2','3','4','1','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101306,'chemistry','How many statements are not correct? \n\n1) The hydroxides and carbonates of Sodium and Potassium are easily soluble in water while the corresponding salts of Magnesium and Calcium are sparingly soluble in water.\n\n2) LiF is almost insoluble in water.\n\n3) LiCl is almost insoluble in water.\n\n4) LiCl is soluble in acetone.\n\n5) Lithium salts are commonly hydrated.','1','1) The hydroxides and carbonates of Sodium and Potassium are easily soluble in water while the corresponding salts of magnesium and calcium are sparingly soluble in water.\n\n2) LiF is almost insoluble in water.\n\n3) LiCl is soluble in water.\n\n4) LiCl is soluble in acetone \n\n5) Lithium salts are commonly hydrated \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(101307,'chemistry','How many statements are correct? \n\n1) The hydroxides and carbonates of Sodium and Potassium are easily soluble in water while the corresponding salts of Magnesium and Calcium are sparingly soluble in water.\n\n2) LiF is almost insoluble in water.\n\n3) LiCl is almost insoluble in water.\n\n4) LiCl is soluble in acetone.\n\n5) Lithium salts are commonly hydrated.','4','1) The hydroxides and carbonates of Sodium and Potassium are easily soluble in water while the corresponding salts of magnesium and calcium are sparingly soluble in water.\n\n2) LiF is almost insoluble in water.\n\n3) LiCl is soluble in water.\n\n4) LiCl is soluble in acetone \n\n5) Lithium salts are commonly hydrated \n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 313-314; Back Exercise )','Inorganic chemistry >> The s Block Elements >> Back Exercise',NULL,'3','mcq','2','3','4','5','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(130101,'chemistry','Petrol, diesel and kerosene oil are obtained from petroleum through which process?','Fractional distillation','Petrol, diesel and kerosene oil are obtained by the fractional distillation of petroleum found under the earth’s crust. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373 Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Destructive distillation','Fractional distillation','Simple distillation','Filtration','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130102,'chemistry','LNG, a type of fuel, is obtained through natural gas by the process of?','Liquefaction','‘LNG’ (liquified natural gas)  is obtained by liquefaction of natural gas. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Fractional\ndistillation','Filtration','Liquefaction','Purification','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130103,'chemistry','CNG stands for','Compressed Natural Gas','CNG stands for compressed natural gas. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Compressed Natural Gas','Combined Natural Gas','Converted Natural Gas','Conducted Natural Gas','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130104,'chemistry','LPG is the abbreviated form of?','Liquified Petroleum Gas','LPG is the abbreviated form of liquified petroleum gas. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Liquid Petroleum Gas','Liquified Petroleum Gas','Liquified Petrol Gas','Liquid Petrol Gas','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130105,'chemistry','Coal gas is obtained through coal by process of?','Destructive distillation','Coal gas is obtained by the destructive distillation of coal. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Filtration','Liquefaction','Fractional distillation','Destructive distillation','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130106,'chemistry','Natural gas is found in ______ strata during drilling of oil wells.','upper','Natural gas is found in upper strata during drilling of oil wells.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Introduction',NULL,'1','fillup','up,pe,r,lo,we,r',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130107,'chemistry','Which is the least polluting fuel?','LPG','LPG is used as a domestic fuel with the least pollution.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','CNG','LPG','kerosene','LNG','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130108,'chemistry','Which among the following is/are used as domestic fuel?\n(a) LPG\n(b) Kerosene\n(c) CNG','(a) and (b)','LPG is used as a domestic fuel with the least pollution. \n\nKerosene oil is also used as a domestic fuel but it causes some pollution.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','(a) and (b)','(a) and (c)','(b) and (c)','All of the above','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130109,'chemistry','Automobiles need fuels like','All of the above','Automobiles need fuels like petrol, diesel and CNG. \n\nPetrol and CNG operated automobiles cause less pollution.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Petrol','Diesel','CNG','All of the above','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130110,'chemistry','The source of energy in fuels is _______','hydrocarbons','All the fuels contain mixture of hydrocarbons, which are sources of energy. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Introduction',NULL,'1','fillup','hy,dr,oc,ar,bo,ns,gen',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130111,'chemistry','Which of the following are the uses of hydrocarbons?','All of the above','Hydrocarbons are also used for the manufacture of polymers like polythene, polypropene, polystyrene etc. \n\nHigher hydrocarbons are used as solvents for paints. \n\nThey are also used as the starting materials for manufacture of many dyes and drugs. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-373, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Starting material for dyes and drugs','Manufacture of polymers','Solvents for paints','All of the above','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(130201,'chemistry','Depending upon the types of carbon-carbon bonds present, hydrocarbons can be classified into three main categories as?','Saturated, Unsaturated and Aromatic','Depending upon the types of carbon-carbon bonds present, they can be classified into three main categories – (i) saturated (ii) unsaturated and (iii) aromatic hydrocarbons. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph -1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Classification',NULL,'2','mcq','Saturated, Unsaturated and Aromatic','Straight, Branched, and Ring','Alkane, Alkene and Alkyne','Natural, Synthetic, Semisynthetic','NEW','11-374','11-295',NULL),
(130202,'chemistry','If different carbon atoms are joined together to form open chain of carbon atoms with single bonds, they are termed as _________','alkanes','If different carbon atoms are joined together to form open chain of carbon atoms with single bonds, they are termed as alkanes. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Classification',NULL,'1','fillup','al,ka,nes, yn,ene',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-374','11-295','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/130202q.png'),
(130203,'chemistry','Closed chains or ring form of carbon atoms is termed as ________','cycloalkanes','If carbon atoms form a closed chain or a ring, they are termed as cycloalkanes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Classification',NULL,'1','fillup','cy,clo,al,ka,nes,yne,ene',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-374','11-295',NULL),
(130204,'chemistry','Hydrocarbons that contain carbon-carbon multiple bonds – double bonds, triple bonds or both are?','Unsaturated hydrocarbon','Unsaturated hydrocarbons contain carbon-carbon multiple bonds – double bonds, triple bonds or both. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Saturated hydrocarbons','Unsaturated hydrocarbon','Non-saturated hydrocarbon','Averes','NEW','11-374','11-295',NULL),
(130205,'chemistry','_______ hydrocarbons are a special type of cyclic compounds.','aromatic','Aromatic hydrocarbons are a special type of cyclic compounds. \n\nAny one of a vast group of unsaturated chemical compounds known as aromatic compounds are distinguished by having one or more planar rings of atoms connected by covalent bonds of two different types. \n\nThe term "aromaticity" describes the distinct stability of these molecules. \n\nThe thing that makes them special is the conjugation stability.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Classification',NULL,'1','fillup','ar,om,at,ic,Al,ka,nes',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-374','11-295','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/130205q.png'),
(130206,'biology','The small bubbles formed around green parts of the plant when placed in sunlight were later identified as','Oxygen','When green plants were placed in sunlight small bubbles were formed around the green parts of the plant. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 207)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in higher plants >> Early Experiments',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrogen','Oxygen','CO₂','Nitrogen','NEW','11-207','11-134',NULL),
