(130301,'chemistry','Saturated open chain hydrocarbons containing carbon - carbon single bonds are','alkanes','Alkanes are saturated open chain hydrocarbons containing carbon - carbon single bonds. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','al,ka,nes, yn,ene',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130302,'chemistry','The first member of alkane family, which is a gas, found in coal mines and marshy places is _________','methane','Methane is the first member of alkane family. \n\nMethane is a gas found in coal mines and marshy places. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','me,th,ane,ene,yne',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130303,'chemistry','The molecular formula of ethane is?','C₂H₆','Hydrocarbon with molecular formula C₂H₆ is known as ethane. \n\nYou can consider C₂H₆ as derived from methane by replacing one hydrogen atom by -CH₃ group. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 2).','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','C₄H₁₀','C₂H₆','C₆H₁₂','C₃H₈','NEW','11-374','11-296','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/130303q.png'),
(130304,'chemistry','Homologous Series of alkanes is obtained by','Replacement of one H-atom and addition of -CH₃','A homologous series is a group of substances that have some chemical characteristics but have some functional groups that are different from one another by CH₂.\n\nThis series is obtained by replacing the hydrogen of terminal carbon atom with -CH₃. \n\nThe members of a homologous series have a same general formula. \n\nC₂H₆ as derived from methane by replacing one hydrogen atom by -CH₃ group. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 2).','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','Addition of -CH₃ group','Replacement of one H-atom and addition of -CH₃','Addition of another -CH₄ group','Replacement of H-atom and addition of -CH₄','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130305,'chemistry','The number of covalent bonds present in ethane molecule are','Seven','Each carbon has tetravalency and hydrogen is bonded by a single covalent bond. \n\nThe ethane contains 2 carbon atoms and six hydrogen atoms all bonded by a single bond. \n\nThus the six covalent bonds with hydrogen and a single covalent bond between the two carbons make a total of seven bonds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Four','Eight','Seven','Nine','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130306,'chemistry','Alkanes are inert under normal conditions as they do not react with acids, bases and other reagents. Hence, they were earlier known as','Paraffins','Alkanes are inert under normal conditions as they do not react with acids, bases and other reagents. \n\nHence, they were earlier known as paraffins. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Inert gases','Paraffins','Inert hydrocarbon','Kerosene','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130307,'chemistry','Guess the structure of the alkane given in the diagram. diagram(n1301)','Tetrahedral','According to VSEPR theory, methane has a tetrahedral structure. \n\nTetrahedral molecular geometry has a central atom in the centre and four substituents at the four corners of regular tetrahedron. \n\nThe angle between any  H-C-H bond is 109.5°. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Tetrahedral','Trigonal planar','Square planer','Pyramidal','NEW','11-374','11-296','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130307q.png'),
(130308,'chemistry','The general formula for alkanes is?','CₙH₂ₙ₊₂','General formula for alkanes is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, where the n represents the number of carbon atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','CₙH₂ₙ','CₙH₂ₙ-₂','CₙH₂ₙ₊₂','CₙH₂ₙ₊₁','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130309,'chemistry','In structure of methane, all H-C-H bond angles are of?','109.5°','According to VSEPR theory, methane has a tetrahedral structure, in which carbon atom lies at the centre and the four hydrogen atoms lie at the four corners of a regular tetrahedron. \n\nAll H-C-H bond angles are of 109.5°. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','105.5°','109.5°','107.5°','103.5°','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130310,'chemistry','In alkanes, tetrahedra are joined together in which C-C and C-H bond lengths are __ and __ respectively','154 pm and 112 pm','In alkanes, tetrahedra are joined together in which C-C and C-H bond lengths are 154 pm and 112 pm respectively. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','163 pm and 112 pm','154 pm and 112 pm','154 pm and 110 pm','160 pm and 122 pm','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130311,'chemistry','C–C and C–H σ bonds are formed by ________ overlapping','head-on','C–C and C–H σ bonds are formed by head-on overlapping of sp³ hybrid orbitals of carbon and 1s orbitals of hydrogen atoms. \n\nThe end to end overlap of orbitals along the internuclear axis, is called the head-on overlap or the axial overlap.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','fillup','he,ad,-on,-ta,il',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-374','11-296','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/130311q.png'),
(130312,'chemistry','Hybridisation of carbon atom in alkanes?','sp³','C–C and C–H σ bonds are formed by head-on overlapping of sp³ hybrid orbitals of carbon and 1s orbitals of hydrogen atoms. \n\nThe hybridization is referred to as being of the sp³ type when the carbon atom is linked to four additional atoms.  \n\n1 s and 3 p orbitals of the carbon atom in the same shell combine to form four new equivalent orbitals. \n\nIt gives tetrahedral configuration with 109.5⁰ bond angle.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','sp','sp²','sp³','dsp','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130313,'chemistry','Which orbital of Hydrogen take part in alkane formation?','1s','The hydrogen atom contains a single valence electron that is present in its 1s orbital. \n\nC–C and C–H σ bonds are formed by head-on overlapping of sp³ hybrid orbitals of carbon and 1s orbitals of hydrogen atoms. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','1s','2s','sp³','2p','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130314,'chemistry','The number of structural isomers of butane are?','Two','All alkanes with four or more carbon atoms exhibit structural isomerism, which means that each molecular formula can have two or more distinct structural formulas. \n\nFour carbon atoms of C₄H₁₀ can be joined either in a continuous chain or with a branched chain.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','Three','Four','Five','Two','NEW','11-375','11-297',NULL),
(130315,'chemistry','The number of structural isomers of pentane are?','Three','All alkanes with four or more carbon atoms exhibit structural isomerism, which means that each molecular formula can have two or more distinct structural formulas. \n\nPentane has 3 isomeric structure. \n\nThese are n-pentane, isopentane and neopentane. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','Three','Four','Five','Two','NEW','11-375','11-297',NULL),
(130316,'chemistry','Which of the following isomer of pentane has highest boiling point? diagram(n1302)','n-pentane','With increasing molecular size, alkanes\' boiling points rise. \n\nThe more branched the chain in isomer, the lower the boiling point is expected to be. \n\nVan der Waals dispersion forces are only active across very small distances between molecules and are weaker for shorter molecules. \n\nThe boiling point of n-pentane, 2-methylbutane and 2,2-dimethylpropane is 309K, 301K and 282.5K respectively.\n\nStructures III, IV and V possess the same molecular formula but have different properties like boiling point.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','n-pentane','2,2-dimethylpropane','Both (a) and (b)','2-methylbutane','NEW','11-375','11-297','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130316q.png'),
(130317,'chemistry','Which of the following isomer of pentane has lowest boiling point? diagram(n1302)','2,2-dimethylpropane','With increasing molecular size, alkanes\' boiling points rise. \n\nThe more branched the chain in isomer, the lower the boiling point is expected to be. \n\nVan der Waals dispersion forces are only active across very small distances between molecules and are weaker for shorter molecules. \n\nThe boiling point of n-pentane, 2-methylbutane and 2,2-dimethylpropane is 309K, 301K and 282.5K respectively.\n\nStructures III, IV and V possess the same molecular formula but have different properties like boiling point.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','n-pentane','2,2-dimethylpropane','2,3-dimethylbutane','2-methylbutane','NEW','11-375','11-297','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130317q.png'),
(130318,'chemistry','The IUPAC nomenclature of the given compound is: diagram(n1303)','n-hexane','The structure contains 6 carbon atoms all having single bond with the adjacent atoms. \n\nThey are in a straight chain and no branched chain is present. Hence, it is n-hexane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1301)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','n-hexane','1-methylneopentane','2-ethylpropane','1-methylpentane','NEW','11-375','11-297','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130318q.png'),
(130319,'chemistry','The IUPAC nomenclature of the given compound is: diagram(n1304)','2-methylpentane','The structure contains a total of 6 carbon atoms. There is a branch on the 2nd carbon atom. The branched chain contains a methyl group. The molecule is a saturated one and thus has single bonds through out. \n\nThere are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). \n\nThus the name of the given compound is 2-methylpentane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1301)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','1-methylneopentane','n-hexane','2-ethylpropane','2-methylpentane','NEW','11-375','11-297','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130319q.png'),
(110803,'chemistry','Boron can\'t exist in 3+ oxidation state due to','all of these','Boron after losing one electron comes to 1+ oxidation state, Boron 1+ \n have 2s subshell completely filled,completely filled subshell being very \n stable avoids to remove electron,so second and third electron is very \n difficult to remove from boron 1+(very high ionization energy) moreover\n size of boron is very small which makes boron to like addition of electron \n rather removal of electron, 3+ oxidation state increase the oxidizing \n character of boron which makes it unstable.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','small size as compared to its group members','High ionization Enthalpy','High electron affinity','all of these','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(130320,'chemistry','The IUPAC nomenclature of the given compound is: diagram(n1305)','3-methylpentane','The structure contains a total of 6 carbon atoms. There is a branch on the 3rd carbon atom. The branched chain contains a methyl group. The molecule is a saturated one and thus has single bonds through out. \n\nThere are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). \n\nThus the name of the given compound is 3-methylpentane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1301)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','3-methylpentane','1-methylneopentane','2-ethylpropane','1-methylpentane','NEW','11-375','11-297','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130320q.png'),
(130321,'chemistry','The IUPAC nomenclature of the given compound is: diagram(n1306)','2,3-dimethylbutane','The structure contains a total of 6 carbon atoms. There are two branchs on the 2nd and 3rd carbon atom. Both branched chains contains a methyl group. The molecule is a saturated one and thus has single bonds through out. \n\nThere are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). \n\nThus the name of the given compound is 2,3-dimethylbutane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1301)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','2-ethylpropane','2,3-dimethylbutane','2-methylpentane','2,2-dimethylbutane','NEW','11-375','11-297','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130321q.png'),
(130322,'chemistry','The IUPAC nomenclature of the given compound is: diagram(n1307)','2,2-dimethylbutane','The structure contains a total of 6 carbon atoms. There are two branchs on the 2nd carbon atom. Both branched chains contains a methyl group. The molecule is a saturated one and thus has single bonds through out. \n\nThere are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). \n\nThus the name of the given compound is 2,2-dimethylbutane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1301)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','1-methylneopentane','2-ethylpropane','1-methylpentane','2,2-dimethylbutane','NEW','11-375','11-297','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130322q.png'),
(130323,'chemistry','Which of the following is not an isomer of hexane?','2-ethylpentane','The compound contains a total of 7 carbon atoms and thus is not an isomer of hexane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - example) diagram(ne1301)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','2-ethylpentane','2,3-dimethylbutane','2-methylpentane','3-methylpentane','NEW','11-375','11-297','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/130323q.png'),
(130401,'chemistry','________ reaction method is used for industrial preparation of dihydrogen gas.','steam','Methane reacts with steam at 1273 K in the presence of nickel catalyst to form carbon monoxide and dihydrogen. \n\nThis method is used for industrial preparation of dihydrogen gas.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','st,ea,m,wa,ter,hot',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(130402,'chemistry','Higher alkanes on heating breakdown into smaller fragments by the process called ________','pyrolysis','Higher alkanes on heating to higher temperature decompose into lower alkanes, alkenes etc. \n\nSuch a decomposition reaction into smaller fragments by the application of heat is called pyrolysis or cracking.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 7)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','py,ro,ly,sis,re,fo,rm,ing',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(130403,'chemistry','Pyrolysis of alkanes is believed to be a _______ reaction.','Free radical','Pyrolysis of alkanes is believed to be a free radical reaction. \n\nAlkanes break by a free radical process. \n\nIn the petroleum sector, it is significant. \n\nBy cracking, the higher alkane molecules produce lower ones (petrol C6 to C11).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 7)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Nucleophile substitution mechanism','Electrophilic substitution mechanism','Nucleophile addition mechanism','Free radical','NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(130404,'chemistry','Identify the reactant in reaction given in the diagram?(n1333)','Dodecane','Dodecane, a constituent of kerosene oil on heating to 973K in the presence of platinum, palladium or nickel gives a mixture of heptane and pentene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 7)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Dodecane','Nonane','Undecane','Decane','NEW','11-382','11-304','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130404q.png'),
(130405,'chemistry','Preparation of oil gas or petrol gas from kerosene oil or petrol involves the principle of ______','pyrolysis','Preparation of oil gas or petrol gas from kerosene oil or petrol involves the principle of pyrolysis.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 7)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','py,ro,ly,sis,re,fo,rm,ing',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(130406,'chemistry','Electron distribution of the sigma molecular orbital is _________ around the internuclear axis of the C–C bond','symmetrical','Alkanes contain carbon-carbon sigma (σ) bonds. \n\nElectron distribution of the sigma molecular orbital is symmetrical around the internuclear axis of the C–C bond which is not disturbed due to rotation about its axis. This permits free rotation about C–C single bond. \n\nThis rotation results into different spatial arrangements of atoms in space which can change into one another. \n\nSuch spatial arrangements of atoms which can be converted into one another by rotation around a C-C single bond are called conformations or conformers or rotamers.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','a,sy,mm,et,ri,cal',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-383','11-305',NULL),
(260237,'chemistry','Write the correct IUPAC NAME of the given compound in diagram?(n26008)','2-methylcyclohexane-1-carbaldehyde','In cyclohexane at 2nd position methyl group is attached and at 1st position aldehyde group is attached.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-359 Paragraph -1)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Nomenclature and Structure of Carbonyl Group',NULL,'3','mcq','2-cyclomethyl-hexane-1-carbaldehyde','1-methylcyclohexane-2-carbaldehyde','2-methylcyclohexane-1-carbaldehyde','2-methylcyclohexan-1-al','NEW','12-359','12-229','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260237q.png'),
(130407,'chemistry','Spatial arrangements of atoms in which conversion of one another takes place by rotation around C—C bond is known as','All of these','Alkanes contain carbon-carbon sigma (σ) bonds. Electron distribution of the sigma molecular orbital is symmetrical around the internuclear axis of the C–C bond which is not disturbed due to rotation about its axis. This permits free rotation about C–C single bond. \n\nThis rotation results into different spatial arrangements of atoms in space which can change into one another. \n\nSuch spatial arrangements of atoms which can be converted into one another by rotation around a C-C single bond are called conformations or conformers or rotamers.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','Rotamers','Conformers','Conformations','All of these','NEW','11-383','11-305',NULL),
(130408,'chemistry','Rotation around a C-C single bond is not completely free. It is hindered by a small energy barrier of _____kJ mol–1.','1','Alkanes can thus have infinite number of conformations by rotation around C-C single bonds. \n\nHowever, it may be remembered that rotation around a C-C single bond is not completely free. \n\nIt is hindered by a small energy barrier of 1-20 kJ mol–1 due to weak repulsive interaction between the adjacent bonds. \n\nSuch a type of repulsive interaction is called torsional strain.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','1','21','31','41','NEW','11-383','11-305',NULL),
(130409,'chemistry','Repulsive interaction between the adjacent bonds is called______.','torsional strain','Alkanes can thus have infinite number of conformations by rotation around C-C single bonds. \n\nHowever, it may be remembered that rotation around a C-C single bond is not completely free. \n\nIt is hindered by a small energy barrier of 1-20 kJ mol–1 due to weak repulsive interaction between the adjacent bonds. \n\nSuch a type of repulsive interaction is called torsional strain.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','fillup','to,rs,io,nal ,st,ra,in',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-383','11-305',NULL),
(130410,'chemistry','The possible rotamers of ethane is/are','Infinite','Ethane molecule (C2H6) contains a carbon – carbon single bond with each carbon atom attached to three hydrogen atoms. \n\nConsidering the ball and stick model of ethane, keep one carbon atom stationary and rotate the other carbon atom around the C-C axis. \n\nThis rotation results into infinite number of spatial arrangements of hydrogen atoms attached to one carbon atom with respect to the hydrogen atoms attached to the other carbon atom. Thus there are infinite number of conformations of ethane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','2','3','4','Infinite','NEW','11-383','11-305',NULL),
(130411,'chemistry','Eclipsed and the staggered conformations of ethane can be represented by','Both (a) and (b)','Eclipsed and the staggered conformations can be represented by Sawhorse and Newman projections.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','Sawhorse projection','Newman projection','Both (a) and (b)','None of the above','NEW','11-383','11-305',NULL),
(130412,'chemistry','Conformation of ethane in which hydrogen atoms attached to two carbon atoms are closest to each other is known as','Eclipsed','Such a conformation in which hydrogen atoms attached to two carbons are as closed together as possible is called eclipsed conformation. \n\nTwo atoms or groups that have an eclipsed conformation have a dihedral angle of 0°. \n\nThey are aligned with respect to their ties to the axis of rotation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Eclipsed','Skew boat','Staggered','Partially eclipsed','NEW','11-383','11-305',NULL),
(130413,'chemistry','Conformation of ethane in which hydrogen atoms attached to two carbon atoms are far apart from each other is known as','Staggered','The configuration in which hydrogens are as far apart as possible is known as the staggered conformation. \n\nA chemical conformation of an ethane-like molecule, known as a "staggered conformation", has the substituents spaced as far apart from one another as possible. \n\nTo achieve this, the torsional angle must be 60°.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Eclipsed','Skew boat','Staggered','Partially eclipsed','NEW','11-383','11-305','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/130413q.png'),
(130414,'chemistry','Identify the type of projection shown in diagram?(n1334)','Sawhorse projection','In Sawhorse projection, the molecule is viewed along the molecular axis. \n\nIt is then projected on paper by drawing the central C–C bond as a somewhat longer straight line.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Sawhorse projection','Newman projection','Fischer projection','CIP projection','NEW','11-383','11-305','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130414q.png'),
(130415,'chemistry','Identify the type of projection shown in diagram?(n1335)','Newman projection','In Newman projection, the molecule is viewed at the C–C bond head on. \n\nThe carbon atom nearer to the eye is represented by a point.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Sawhorse projection','Newman projection','Fischer projection','CIP projection','NEW','11-383','11-305','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130415q.png'),
(130416,'chemistry','Identify the type of conformation shown in diagram?(n1336)','Staggered','The configuration in which hydrogens are as far apart as possible is known as the staggered conformation. \n\nA chemical conformation of an ethane-like molecule, known as a "staggered conformation", has the substituents spaced as far apart from one another as possible. \n\nTo achieve this, the torsional angle must be 60°\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Eclipsed','Skew boat','Staggered','Partially eclipsed','NEW','11-383','11-305','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130416q.png'),
(130417,'chemistry','Identify the type of conformation shown in diagram?(n1337)','Eclipsed','Such a conformation in which hydrogen atoms attached to two carbons are as closed together as possible is called eclipsed conformation. \n\nTwo atoms or groups that have an eclipsed conformation have a dihedral angle of 0°. \n\nThey are aligned with respect to their ties to the axis of rotation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','Eclipsed','Skew boat','Staggered','Partially eclipsed','NEW','11-383','11-305','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130417q.png'),
(130418,'chemistry','Which form of ethane has the least torsional strain?','Staggered','In staggered form of ethane, the electron clouds of carbon-hydrogen bonds are as far apart as possible. \n\nThus, there are minimum repulsive forces, minimum energy and maximum stability of the molecule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Eclipsed','Skew boat','Staggered','Partially eclipsed','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130419,'chemistry','Energy difference between eclipsed and staggered form of ethane is','12.5 kJ mol-¹','The energy difference between the two extreme forms is of the order of 12.5 kJ mol–¹, which is very small. \n\nThe staggered conformation is comparatively more stable with less energy, whereas the eclipsed conformation is the least stable with the most energy.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','12.5 kJ mol-¹','15.5 kJ mol-¹','14.5 kJ mol-¹','12.5 J mol-¹','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130420,'chemistry','The energy required to overcome energy barrier between eclipsed and staggered conformation of ethane molecule is gained through','Intermolecular collisions','The energy difference between the two extreme forms is of the order of 12.5 kJ mol–¹, which is very small. \n\nEven at ordinary temperatures, the ethane molecule gains thermal or kinetic energy which is sufficient enough to overcome this energy barrier of 12.5 kJ mol–1 through intermolecular collisions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','Intramolecular collisions','Intermolecular collisions','Molecular collisions','Atomic collisions','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130421,'chemistry','Which is most stable conformer of ethane-1, 2-diol, refer diagram?(n1338)','b','Among the given structures, structure (a) and (b) are staggered forms of ethane-1,2-diol. \n\nOn the other hand, structure (c) and (d) are eclipsed forms of ethane-1,2-diol. \n\nEclipsed forms are less stable than staggered forms due to electronic repulsion. \n\nBetween (a)and (b) structure (b) shows H-bonding due to which it is more stable than structure (a).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-384','11-306','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130421q.png'),
(130422,'biology','The electrons of photosystem-I are replaced by','Photosystem-ll','The electrons needed to replace those removed from photosystem-I are provided by photosystem-II. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 212)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> The Electron Transport',NULL,'1','mcq','Electron transport chain','Water splitting','Photosystem-ll','Access electrons','NEW','11-212','11-139',NULL),
(130423,'biology','The water splitting complex is associated with','PS-II','The water splitting complex is associated with the PS II. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 212)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> The Electron Transport',NULL,'1','mcq','PS-II','PS-I','Electrons transport chain','Reaction centre','NEW','11-212','11-139',NULL),
(130424,'biology','PS-II is located on the inner surface of _______ membrane','Thylakoid','PS II is physically located on the inner side of the membrane of the thylakoid. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 212)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> The Electron Transport',NULL,'1','mcq','Thylakoid','Stroma','Grana lamellae','Grana','NEW','11-212','11-139',NULL),
(130425,'biology','Match the following','Chlorophyll a-blue green in the chromatogram, Chlorophyll b-yellow green, Xanthophylls-yellow, Carotenoids-yellow orange.','A chromatographic separation of the leaf pigments shows that the colour that we see in leaves is not due to a single pigment but due to four\npigments: Chlorophyll a (bright or blue green\nin the chromatogram), chlorophyll b (yellow\ngreen), xanthophylls (yellow) and carotenoids\n(yellow to yellow-orange)NCERT class 11th Pg., 210','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> The Electron Transport',NULL,'3','match','Chlorophyll a,blue green in the chromatogram','Chlorophyll b,yellow green','Xanthophylls,yellow','Carotenoids,yellow orange','NEW','11-210','11-137',NULL),
(130501,'chemistry','Alkenes are _______ hydrocarbons containing at least one double bond.','unsaturated','Alkenes are unsaturated hydrocarbons containing at least one double bond.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','un,sa,tu,ra,ted',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130502,'chemistry','General formula for alkenes is______?','CₙH₂ₙ','General formula for alkenes is CₙH₂ₙ.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','CₙH₂ₙ-₂','CₙH₂ₙ','CₙH₂ₙ₊₂','CₙH₂ₙ₊₁','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130503,'chemistry','Alkenes are also known as ______','olefins','Alkenes are also known as olefins (oil forming). \n\nSince the first member ethylene or ethene (C2H4) was found to form an oily liquid on reaction with chlorine.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','paraffins','olefins','acetylenes','liquifins','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130504,'chemistry','Ethylene or ethene was found to form an oily liquid on reaction with ______.','chlorine','The first member of alkene, ethylene or ethene (C₂H₄), was found to form an oily liquid on reaction with chlorine.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','ch,lo,ri,ne,hy,dr,og,en',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130505,'chemistry','Carbon-carbon sigma bond enthalpy is about','397 kJ mol-¹','Carbon-carbon double bond in alkenes consists of one strong sigma (σ) bond (bond enthalpy about 397 kJ mol–¹) due to head-on overlapping of sp 2 hybridised orbitals and one weak pi (π) bond (bond enthalpy about 284 kJ mol–¹) obtained by lateral or sideways overlapping of the two 2p orbitals of the two carbon atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','117 kJ mol-¹','407 kJ mol-¹','397 kJ mol-¹','284 kJ mol-¹','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130506,'chemistry','_____ strong bond in double bond in ethene.','Sigma','Carbon-carbon double bond in alkenes consists of one strong sigma (σ) bond (bond enthalpy about 397 kJ mol–¹) due to head-on overlapping of sp 2 hybridised orbitals and one weak pi (π) bond (bond enthalpy about 284 kJ mol–¹) obtained by lateral or sideways overlapping of the two 2p orbitals of the two carbon atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','si,gm,a,pi',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130507,'chemistry','Hybridisation of orbitals of carbon forming double bond?','sp²','Carbon-carbon double bond in alkenes consists of one strong sigma (σ) bond (bond enthalpy about 397 kJ mol–¹) due to head-on overlapping of sp 2 hybridised orbitals and one weak pi (π) bond (bond enthalpy about 284 kJ mol–¹) obtained by lateral or sideways overlapping of the two 2p orbitals of the two carbon atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','sp','sp²','dsp','sp³','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130508,'chemistry','Carbon-carbon pi bond enthalpy is about','284 kJ mol-¹','Carbon-carbon double bond in alkenes consists of one strong sigma (σ) bond (bond enthalpy about 397 kJ mol–¹) due to head-on overlapping of sp 2 hybridised orbitals and one weak pi (π) bond (bond enthalpy about 284 kJ mol–¹) obtained by lateral or sideways overlapping of the two 2p orbitals of the two carbon atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','117 kJ mol-¹','407 kJ mol-¹','397 kJ mol-¹','284 kJ mol-¹','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130509,'chemistry','Pi bond is obtained by______ overlapping of the two 2p orbitals of the two carbon atoms','sideways','Carbon-carbon double bond in alkenes consists of one strong sigma (σ) bond (bond enthalpy about 397 kJ mol–¹) due to head-on overlapping of sp 2 hybridised orbitals and one weak pi (π) bond (bond enthalpy about 284 kJ mol–¹) obtained by lateral or sideways overlapping of the two 2p orbitals of the two carbon atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','he,ad,on,si,de,wa,ys',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130510,'chemistry','Which type of overlapping results in the formation of sigma and pi bonds respectively?','Head on and sideways respectively','Carbon-carbon double bond in alkenes consists of one strong sigma (σ) bond (bond enthalpy about 397 kJ mol–¹) due to head-on overlapping of sp 2 hybridised orbitals and one weak pi (π) bond (bond enthalpy about 284 kJ mol–¹) obtained by lateral or sideways overlapping of the two 2p orbitals of the two carbon atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','Lateral and sideways respectively','Sideways and head on respectively','Head on and sideways respectively','Head on and head on respectively','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130511,'chemistry','The length of double bond in ethene is about','134pm','The double bond is shorter in bond length (134 pm) than the C–C single bond (154 pm).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','154pm','164pm','134pm','124pm','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130512,'chemistry','The presence of the pi (π) bond makes alkenes behave as sources of_______','electrons','Pi (π) bond is a weaker bond due to poor sideways overlapping between the two 2p orbitals. \n\nThus, the presence of the pi (π) bond makes alkenes behave as sources of loosely held mobile electrons. \n\nTherefore, alkenes are easily attacked by reagents or compounds which are in search of electrons. \n\nSuch reagents are called electrophilic reagents.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','on,ot,pr,el,ec,tr,ons',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130513,'chemistry','Alkenes are ________ attacked by reagents or compounds which are in search of electrons.','easily','Pi (π) bond is a weaker bond due to poor sideways overlapping between the two 2p orbitals. \n\nThus, the presence of the pi (π) bond makes alkenes behave as sources of loosely held mobile electrons. \n\nTherefore, alkenes are easily attacked by reagents or compounds which are in search of electrons. \n\nSuch reagents are called electrophilic reagents.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','ra,re,ea,si,ly',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130514,'chemistry','The reagents which attack electrons are called______ reagent.','electrophilic','Pi (π) bond is a weaker bond due to poor sideways overlapping between the two 2p orbitals. \n\nThus, the presence of the pi (π) bond makes alkenes behave as sources of loosely held mobile electrons. \n\nTherefore, alkenes are easily attacked by reagents or compounds which are in search of electrons. \n\nSuch reagents are called electrophilic reagents.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','nu,cl,eo,el,ec,tro,ph,il,ic',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130515,'chemistry','The presence of weaker π-bond makes alkenes unstable molecules in comparison to ______','alkanes','The presence of weaker π-bond makes alkenes unstable molecules in comparison to alkanes and thus, alkenes can be changed into single bond compounds by combining with the electrophilic reagents.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','fillup','al,ka,ne,s,al,ky,ne',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130516,'chemistry','Strength of the double bond_____','681 KJmol-¹','Strength of the double bond (bond enthalpy, 681 kJ mol-¹).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'1','mcq','284KJmol-¹','348KJmol-¹','397KJmol-¹','681 KJmol-¹','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130517,'chemistry','Guess the type of sigma bond omitted in the orbital diagram?(n1339)','sp²-sp²','The orbitals that are overlapping in the diagram are sp² orbitals of both the given compound. \n\nThus the sigma bond formed by this head-on overlap is sp²-sp².\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - figure) diagram(ne1311)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','sp²-s','sp²-p','p-p','sp²-sp²','NEW','11-384','11-306','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130517q.png'),
(130518,'chemistry','Guess the type of bond omitted in the orbital diagram?(n1340)','p-p','The orbitals that are overlapping in the diagram are p orbitals of the adjacent carbon atoms in ethene. \n\nThus the pi bond formed by this sideways overlap is p-p.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-385, Paragraph - figure) diagram(ne1312)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','sp²-s','sp²-p','p-p','sp²-sp²','NEW','11-385','11-307','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130518q.png'),
(130519,'chemistry','Select the correct structure according to the IUPAC name 2,8-Dimethyl-3, 6-decadiene, refer diagram?(n1341)','a','For nomenclature of alkenes in IUPAC system, the longest chain of carbon atoms containing the double bond is selected. \n\nNumbering of the chain is done from the end which is nearer to the double bond. \n\nThe suffix ‘ene’ replaces ‘ane’ of alkanes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-385, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-385','11-307','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130519q.png'),
(130520,'chemistry','Select the correct structure according to the IUPAC name 4-Ethyl-2,6-dimethyl-dec-4-ene, refer diagram?(n1341)','d','For nomenclature of alkenes in IUPAC system, the longest chain of carbon atoms containing the double bond is selected. \n\nNumbering of the chain is done from the end which is nearer to the double bond. \n\nThe suffix ‘ene’ replaces ‘ane’ of alkanes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-385, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkenes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-385','11-307','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130520q.png'),
(130601,'chemistry','General formula of alkynes?','CₙH₂ₙ-₂','The number of hydrogen atoms is still less in alkynes as compared to alkenes or alkanes. \n\nTheir general formula is CₙH₂ₙ-₂.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-392, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'1','mcq','CₙH₂ₙ-₂','CₙH₂ₙ','CₙH₂ₙ','CₙH₂ₙ₊₁','NEW','11-392','11-314',NULL),
(130602,'chemistry','Which among the following is correct for ethyne?','All of the above','The first stable member of alkyne series is ethyne which is popularly known as acetylene. \n\nAcetylene is used for arc welding purposes in the form of oxyacetylene flame obtained by mixing acetylene with oxygen gas.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-392, Paragraph -3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'2','mcq','The first stable member of alkyne series is ethyne.','Ethyne is popularly known as acetylene','Acetylene is used for arc welding purposes.','All of the above','NEW','11-392','11-314',NULL),
(130603,'chemistry','The correct IUPAC name of the given structure in diagram?(n1360)','3-methylbut-l-yne','In IUPAC system they, are named as derivatives of the corresponding alkanes replacing ‘ane’ by the suffix ‘yne’. \n\nThe position of the triple bond is indicated by the first triply bonded carbon.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-392, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'3','mcq','2-methylbut-l-yne','l-methylbut-3-yne','3-methylbut-l-yne','2-methylbut-3-yne','NEW','11-392','11-314','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130603q.png'),
(130604,'chemistry','The correct IUPAC name of the given compound {CH₂=CH-C≡C-CH₃}','pent-1-ene-3-yne','In IUPAC system they, are named as derivatives of the corresponding alkanes replacing ‘ane’ by the suffix ‘yne’. \n\nThe position of the triple bond is indicated by the first triply bonded carbon and \'-ene\' groups comes after \'-yne\' in priority table.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-392, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'2','fillup','pe,nt,-1-,en,e-,3-,yne',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-392','11-314',NULL),
(130605,'chemistry','How many alkyne structural isomers are possible for C₆H₁₀?','7','C₆H₁₀ shows 7 structural isomers.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -example) diagram(ne1322)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'3','mcq','7','6','5','4','NEW','11-393','11-315',NULL),
(130606,'chemistry','The strength of C≡C bond','823 kJ mol-¹','Ethyne molecule consists of one C–C σ bond, two C–H σ bonds and two C–C π bonds. \n\nThe strength of C≡C bond (bond enthalpy 823 kJ mol-¹).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'1','mcq','681 kJ mol-¹','823 kJ mol-¹','348 kJ mol-¹','417 kJ mol-¹','NEW','11-393','11-315',NULL),
(130607,'chemistry','Total number of sigma and pi-bonds present in ethyne are','3 and 2 respectively','Ethyne molecule consists of one C–C σ bond, two C–H σ bonds and two C–C π bonds. \n\nThe strength of C≡C bond (bond enthalpy 823 kJ mol-¹).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'1','mcq','4 and 3 respectively','3 and 2 respectively','2 and 3 respectively','1 and 2 respectively','NEW','11-393','11-315',NULL),
(130608,'chemistry','The geometry of ethyne molecule is','linear','Electron cloud between two carbon atoms is cylindrically symmetrical about the internuclear axis. \n\nThus, ethyne is a linear molecule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'1','mcq','linear','trigonal planar','tetrahedral','bent','NEW','11-393','11-315',NULL),
(130609,'chemistry','Predict the omitted reagent in the diagram?(n1361)','CaC₂','On industrial scale, ethyne is prepared by treating calcium carbide with water.\n \nCalcium carbide is prepared by heating quick lime with coke.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'3','mcq','CaCO₃','CaO','CaC₂','C₂','NEW','11-393','11-315','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130609q.png'),
(130610,'chemistry','Which method is used for industrial synthesis of alkynes?','From calcium carbide','On industrial scale, ethyne is prepared by treating calcium carbide with water.\n \nCalcium carbide is prepared by heating quick lime with coke.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'1','mcq','From calcium carbide','From vicinal dihalides','From alkenes','From carboxylic acid','NEW','11-393','11-315',NULL),
(130611,'chemistry','What are A and B in following reaction given in diagram?(n1362)','b','Vicinal dihalides on treatment with alcoholic potassium hydroxide undergo dehydrohalogenation. \n\nOne molecule of hydrogen halide is eliminated to form alkenyl halide which on treatment with sodamide gives alkyne.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-394','11-316','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130611q.png'),
(130612,'chemistry','Ethylene has characteristic _____','odour','Ethylene has characteristic odour.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'1','fillup','od,ou,r,le,ss,no',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-394','11-316',NULL),
(130613,'chemistry','Hydrogen atoms of ethyne attached to triply bonded carbon atom are acidic in nature due to','Both (a) and (b)','Hydrogen atoms in ethyne are attached to the sp hybridised carbon atoms whereas they are attached to sp² hybridised carbon atoms in ethene and sp³ hybridised carbons in ethane. \n\nDue to the maximum percentage of s character (50%), the sp hybridised orbitals of carbon atoms in ethyne molecules have highest electronegativity; hence, these attract the shared electron pair of the C-H bond of ethyne to a greater extent than that of the sp² hybridised orbitals of carbon in ethene and the sp³ hybridised orbital of carbon in ethane. \n\nThus in ethyne, hydrogen atoms can be liberated as protons more easily as compared to ethene and ethane. \n\nHence, hydrogen atoms of ethyne attached to triply bonded carbon atom are acidic in nature.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'2','mcq','Maximum percentage of s-character of sp','Highest electronegativity of sp-orbital than sp and sp 3 -orbitals','Both (a) and (b)','More bond energy than single and double bonds','NEW','11-394','11-316',NULL),
(130614,'chemistry','Which one is the correct order of acidity?','Both (a) and (b)','Alkanes, alkenes and alkynes follow the following trend in their acidic behaviour.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -last) diagram(ne1323)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'3','mcq','HC≡CH > H₂C=CH₂ > CH₃–CH₃','HC≡CH>CH₃-C≡CH>>CH₃-C≡C-CH₃','Both (a) and (b)','HC≡CH > H₂C=CH₂ = CH₃–CH₃','NEW','11-394','11-316',NULL),
(130615,'chemistry','Arrange benzene, n-hexane and ethyne in decreasing order of acidic behaviour','Ethyne > benzene > hexane','Acidic character increases with increasing s-character of the orbital. \n\nHence, decreasing order of acidic behaviour of benzene, n-hexane and ethyne is ethyne > benzene > hexane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -last)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'3','mcq','Benzene > hexane > ethyne','Benzene > ethyne > hexane','Ethyne > benzene > hexane','Hexane > benzene > ethyne','NEW','11-394','11-316',NULL),
(130616,'chemistry','Addition in unsymmetrical alkynes takes place according to','Markovnikov’s rule','Addition in unsymmetrical alkynes takes place according to Markovnikov rule. \n\nMajority of the reactions of alkynes are the examples of electrophilic addition reactions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'1','mcq','Anti-Markovnikov’s addition','Markovnikov’s rule','Saytzeff\'s rule','Hofmann rule','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(130617,'chemistry','Majority of the addition reactions of alkynes are the examples of','electrophilic addition reaction','Addition in unsymmetrical alkynes takes place according to Markovnikov rule. \n\nMajority of the reactions of alkynes are the examples of electrophilic addition reactions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'1','mcq','electrophilic addition reaction','electrophilic substitution reaction','nucleophilic addition reaction','nucleophilic substitution reaction','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(130618,'chemistry','Predict the catalyst of the reaction given in diagram?(n1363)','Pt/Pd/Ni','The reaction is hydration reaction and the catalyst used for thsi reaction is Pt/Pd/Ni.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -13.62 reaction) diagram(ne1324)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'3','mcq','AlCl₃','Zn, H⁺','Pt/Pd/Ni','Fe/HCl','NEW','11-395','11-317','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130618q.png'),
(130619,'chemistry','Consider and answer according to the reaction given in the diagram?(n1364)','d','The reaction here is addition of halogen. \n\nThe product formed formed in the first step is 1,2-dibromopropene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -13.64 reaction) diagram(ne1325)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-395','11-317','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130619q.png'),
(130620,'chemistry','When CH₃—C≡CH reacts with excess of HBr then product obtained is','2,2-dibromopropane','Two molecules of hydrogen halides (HCl, HBr, HI) add to alkynes to form gem dihalides (in which two halogens are attached to the same carbon atom).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'3','mcq','bromoethene','dibromoethene','2-bromopropene','2,2-dibromopropane','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(130621,'chemistry','Predict the correct intermediate and product in the following reaction given in diagram?(n1365)','d','The reaction in the first step is the addition of water molecule according to the markovnikov\'s rule. \n\nIn the next step tautomerism takes place to give a more stable final product.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -13.68 reaction) diagram(ne1326)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-395','11-317','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130621q.png'),
(130622,'chemistry','The polymer polyethyne is represented as','—(CH=CH–CH=CH)ₙ—','Under suitable conditions, linear polymerisation of ethyne takes place to produce polyacetylene or polyethyne which is a high molecular weight polyene containing repeating units of (CH = CH – CH = CH ) and can be represented as —( CH = CH – CH = CH)ₙ—.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -last)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'2','mcq','—(CH=CH–CH=CH)ₙ—','—(CH=CH–CH₂--)ₙ—','—(CH=CH=CH)ₙ—','—(CH=CH–CH--CH₂)ₙ—','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(130623,'chemistry','Which among the following is true for Polyethyne?','All of the above','Thin film of polyacetylene can be used as electrodes in batteries. \n\nThese films are good conductors, lighter and cheaper than the metal conductors.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph -1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'2','mcq','Thin film of polyacetylene can be used as electrodes in batteries.','Polyethyne films are cheaper than metals.','Polyethyne films are lighter','All of the above','NEW','11-396','11-318',NULL),
(130624,'chemistry','Which of the following molecules on passing through red hot iron tube at 873 K undergoes cyclic polymerisation?','Both (b) and (c)','Ethyne on passing through red hot iron tube at 873K undergoes cyclic polymerization. \n\nThree molecules polymerise to form benzene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'2','mcq','Ethene','Ethyne','Acetylene','Both (b) and (c)','NEW','11-396','11-318',NULL),
(130625,'chemistry','Calculate the total no. of sigma and pi bonds in the product formed by the reaction shown in the diagram?(n1366)','σ bonds = 12, pi bonds = 3','Product formed is benzene which has σ bonds = 12, pi bonds = 3.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Alkynes',NULL,'3','mcq','σ bonds = 6, pi bonds = 6','σ bonds = 12, pi bonds = 3','σ bonds = 12, pi bonds = 6','σ bonds = 15, pi bonds = 3','NEW','11-396','11-318','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130625q.png'),
(130626,'biology','What happens due to conformational changes in CF₁','ATP synthesis','Conformational change in the CF1 particle of the ATP synthase makes the enzyme synthesis several molecules of energy-packed ATP. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 215)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Chemiosmotic Hypothesis',NULL,'1','mcq','ADP synthesis','ATP synthesis','NADP synthesis','ATP break down','NEW','11-215','11-142',NULL),
(130627,'biology','Which of the following are the driving materials for chemiosmosis','Proton gradient, ATP Synthases and proton pump','Chemiosmosis requires a membrane, a proton pump, a proton gradient and ATP synthase. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 215)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Chemiosmotic Hypothesis',NULL,'1','mcq','Proton pump','Proton gradient, ATP Synthases and proton pump','ATP synthase and electrons','ATP Synthases and electron pump','NEW','11-215','11-142',NULL),
(130628,'biology','For which of the following purposes energy is used in chemiosmosis','Both To pump protons and Gradient formation','Energy is used to pump protons across a membrane, to create a gradient or a high concentration of protons within the thylakoid lumen and ATP synthesis due to conformational changes in CF₁ (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 215)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Chemiosmotic Hypothesis',NULL,'1','mcq','To pump protons','Gradient formation','ATP synthase','Both To pump protons and Gradient formation','NEW','11-215','11-142',NULL),
(130629,'biology','The ATP formed as a result of chemiosmosis is immediately utilized in','Biosynthetic reactions of stroma, CO₂  fixation and sugar synthesis','Along with the NADPH produced by the movement of electrons, the ATP will be used immediately in the biosynthetic reaction taking place in the stroma, responsible for fixing CO₂  and synthesis of sugars. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 215)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Chemiosmotic Hypothesis',NULL,'2','mcq','Biosynthetic reactions of stroma, CO₂  fixation and sugar synthesis','CO₂ fixation','Sugar synthesis and CO₂  fixation','Sugar synthesis','NEW','11-215','11-142',NULL),
(130630,'biology','Identify the missing parts of the diagram (1304)','Plastocyanin','NCERT class 11 Page No 214 Figure 13.7','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Chemiosmotic Hypothesis',NULL,'1','mcq','Plastoquinone','Plastocyanin','Lumen','Stroma','NEW','11-214','11-141','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/130630q.png'),
(130631,'biology','Where is NADP reductase enzyme located in the chloroplast?','outer side ( stroma) of thylakoid membrane of the chloroplast','The NADP reductase enzyme is located on the stromal side of the membrane. Its outside the thylakoid membrane and lies  in the stroma NCERT Class 11th, Pg. No. 214','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Chemiosmotic Hypothesis',NULL,'3','mcq','lumen of thylakoid','inner membranes of chloroplast','Outer membrane of chloroplast','outer side ( stroma) of thylakoid membrane of the chloroplast','NEW','11-214','11-141',NULL),
(110804,'chemistry','The Ralative Stabilty of 1+ oxidation state in heavier elements of Boron Family is in order','Al<Ga<In<Tl','Heavier members of B family involve d or f subshell which are diffused,\n offers less screening effect ,due to this ns2 electrons are difficult to re-\n move as compared np1 electron, presenece of d or f- electrons make the\n ns electron pair inert so called inert pair effect,heavier members only like\n to remove np electron to form 1+ ion, inert pair effect increase as no. of \n electrons in d or f subshell increase ,\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 318; Paragraph No.3)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','Al<Ga<In<Tl','Al>Ga>In>Tl','Ga>In>Tl>Al','Tl>In>Ga<Al','NEW','11-318',NULL,NULL),
(130632,'biology','Choose the correct option concerning photosynthesis','Acording to chemiosmosis, protons in the stroma of chloroplast decrease in number, while in the lumen there is accumulation of protons.','Hence, within the chloroplast, protons in the stroma decrease in number, while in the lumen there is accumulation of protons.  This creates a proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane as well as a measurable decrease in pH in the lumen\nIn photosystem II the reaction centre chlorophyll a absorbs 680 nm\nwavelength of red light causing electrons to become excited  (Pg. 214, NCERT Class 11th)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Chemiosmotic Hypothesis',NULL,'2','mcq','Wavelength of blue light causes electron to get excited  in PS II','In respiration, protons accumulate in the stromal of the mitochondria when electrons move through the ETS','Acording to chemiosmosis, protons in the stroma of chloroplast decrease in number, while in the lumen there is accumulation of protons.','All of them','NEW','11-214','11-141',NULL),
(130701,'chemistry','Aromatic hydrocarbons are also known as _____','arenes','These hydrocarbons are also known as ‘arenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph -3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'1','fillup','be,nzo,ar,en,es',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-396','11-318',NULL),
(130702,'chemistry','Since most of them possess _____ odour they are called aromatic compounds','pleasant','Since most of them possess pleasant odour (Greek; aroma meaning pleasant smelling), the class of compounds was named as ‘aromatic compounds’.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph -3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'1','fillup','bad,pl,ea,sa,nt,le,ss',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-396','11-318',NULL),
(130703,'chemistry','In a majority of reactions of aromatic compounds, the unsaturation of benzene ring is _______','retained','Benzene ring is highly unsaturated but in a majority of reactions of aromatic compounds, the unsaturation of benzene ring is retained.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph -3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'1','fillup','re,ta,in,ed,lo,st',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-396','11-318',NULL),
(130704,'chemistry','Name the given structure in diagram?(n1367)','biphenyl','The IUPAC name of the given compound is biphenyl. \n\nIt has 2 benzene rings linked by a single covalent bond.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph -3) diagram(ne1327)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','anthracene','naphthalene','biphenyl','phenanthrene','NEW','11-396','11-318','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130704q.png'),
(130705,'chemistry','The common name and IUPAC name of the structure shown in diagram?(n1368)','(o-Xylene), 1,2-Dimethylbenzene','The 1, 2 or 1, 6 is known as the ortho (o–), the 1, 3 or 1, 5 as meta (m–) and the 1, 4 as para (p–) disubstituted compounds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','(o-xylene), toluene','(o-Xylene), 1,2-Dimethylbenzene','(o-Xylene), 1,3-Dimethylbenzene','(p-Xylene), 1,2-Dimethylbenzene','NEW','11-396','11-318','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130705q.png'),
(130706,'chemistry','Benzene was discovered by','Faraday','Benzene was isolated by Michael Faraday in 1825.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph -1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'1','mcq','Ramsay','Faraday','Kekule','Priestley','NEW','11-396','11-318',NULL),
(130707,'chemistry','The ring structure of benzene was proposed by','Kekule','August Kekulé in 1865 proposed the structure for benzene having cyclic arrangement of six carbon atoms with alternate single and double bonds and one hydrogen atom attached to each carbon atom.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-397, Paragraph -1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'1','mcq','Wohler','Faraday','Kekule','Davy','NEW','11-397','11-319',NULL),
(130708,'chemistry','How many isomers of 1,2-dibromobenzene are possible according to Kekule?','2','The Kekulé structure indicates the possibility of two isomeric 1, 2-dibromobenzenes. \n\nIn one of the isomers, the bromine atoms are attached to the doubly bonded carbon atoms whereas in the other, they are attached to the singly bonded carbons.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-397, Paragraph -3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-397','11-319',NULL),
(130709,'chemistry','The number of sp² -hybrid orbitals in a molecule of benzene is','18','All the six carbon atoms in benzene are sp² hybridized. \n\nTwo sp² hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom overlap with sp² hybrid orbitals of adjacent carbon atoms to form six C—C sigma bonds which are in the hexagonal plane. \n\nThe remaining sp² hybrid orbital of each carbon atom overlaps with s orbital of a hydrogen atom to form six C—H sigma bonds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-398, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','24','18','12','6','NEW','11-398','11-320',NULL),
(130710,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct regarding structure of benzene? \n\nI. All the six carbons in benzene are sp² -hybridised. \n\nII.sp²-hybridised orbital of carbon combines with another  sp²-hybridised orbital of carbon to give C—C sigma bonds.\n \nIII. C—H o-bond is obtained by lateral overlapping of sp² -hybridised orbital of C and 15-orbital of H-atom. \n\nChoose the correct statements.','I and II are correct','All the six carbon atoms in benzene are sp² hybridized. \n\nTwo sp² hybrid orbitals of each carbon atom overlap with sp² hybrid orbitals of adjacent carbon atoms to form six C—C sigma bonds which are in the hexagonal plane. \n\nThe remaining sp² hybrid orbital of each carbon atom overlaps with s orbital of a hydrogen atom to form six C—H sigma bonds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-398, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','I, II and III are correct','I and II are correct','I and III are correct','II and III are correct','NEW','11-398','11-320',NULL),
(130711,'chemistry','X-Ray diffraction data reveals that benzene is a _______ molecule.','planar','X-Ray diffraction data reveals that benzene is a planar molecule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'1','mcq','linear','planar','trigonal planar','bent','NEW','11-399','11-321',NULL),
(130712,'chemistry','X-ray diffraction study of benzene reveals that, \n\nI. it is a planar molecule. \n\nII. C— C bond lengths are of the same order which is intermediate between C— C single bond and C—C double bond.\n \nIII. benzene is reluctant towards the addition reaction under normal conditions. \n\nIV. it explains the unusual behaviour of benzene. \n\nWhich of the above statements are true?','All of these','X-Ray diffraction data reveals that benzene is a planar molecule. \n\nHad any one of the above structures of benzene (A or B) been correct, two types of C—C bond lengths were expected. \n\nHowever, X-ray data indicates that all the six C—C bond lengths are of the same order (139 pm) which is intermediate between C— C single bond (154 pm) and C—C double bond (133 pm). \n\nThus the absence of pure double bond in benzene accounts for the reluctance of benzene to show addition reactions under normal conditions, thus explaining the unusual behaviour of benzene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -3)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','I, II and III','II, III and IV','I, III and IV','All of these','NEW','11-399','11-321',NULL),
(130713,'chemistry','The conditions for aromaticity is','All of these','All the criteria are needed to be satisfied by a compound to be aromatic.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','Planarity','Complete delocalisation of the π electrons in the ring','Presence of (4n + 2) π electrons in the ring where n is an integer (n = 0, 1, 2, . . .).','All of these','NEW','11-399','11-321',NULL),
(130714,'chemistry','Examine the options and select the one that is not aromatic. Four structures are given in diagram?(n1369)','d','There is no delocalisation in pi electrons in the fourth option and it does not follow huckel\'s rule so it is non aromatic.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130714q.png'),
(130715,'chemistry','Examine the options and select the ones that are not aromatic, structures are given in diagram?(n1382)','All are non-aromatic','None of the compounds is  aromatic as there is no delocalisation of pi (n) electrons in the ring and they do not follow Huckel\'s rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','All are non-aromatic','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130715q.png'),
(130716,'chemistry','Predict the omitted product in the diagram?(n1370)','C₆H₆','Sodium salt of benzoic acid on heating with soda lime gives benzene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','C₆H₆','C₆H₅—Na','C₆H₅—ONa','C₅H₅—Na','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130716q.png'),
(130717,'chemistry','Predict the omitted reagent in the diagram?(n1371)','C₆H₅—OH','Phenol is reduced to benzene by passing its vapours over heated zinc dust.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'3','mcq','C₆H₅—Na','C₆H₅—ONa','C₆H₅—OH','C₆H₅—OONa','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130717q.png'),
(130718,'chemistry','Aromatic compounds burn with sooty flame because','They have a relatively high percentage of carbon.','Aromatic hydrocarbons burn with sooty flame because they have high carbon concentration in compound.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'2','mcq','They have a ring structure of carbon atoms.','They have a relatively high percentage of hydrogen.','They have a relatively high percentage of carbon.','They resist reaction with oxygen of air.','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130719,'chemistry','Arenes are characterised by','Electrophilic substitution reaction','Arenes are characterised by electrophilic substitution reactions. \n\nHowever, under special conditions they can also undergo addition and oxidation reactions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Aromatic Hydrocarbons',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrophilic addition reaction','Electrophilic substitution reaction','Nucleophilic addition reaction','Nucleophilic substitution reaction','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130801,'chemistry','Which of the following alkane cannot be made in good yield by Wurtz reaction?','n-heptane','Wurtz reaction is used for the preparation of higher alkanes containing even number of C-atoms. \n\nThus this reaction cannot be used for the preparation of n-heptane, as it contains odd number of carbon atoms.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 2)\n(NEET 2020)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','n-hexane','2, 3-dimethylbutane','n-heptane','n-butane','NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130802,'chemistry','The alkane that gives only one monochloro product on chlorination with Cl₂ in presence of diffused sunlight is','neopentane','In chlorination of alkanes, hydrogen is replaced by chlorine. \n\nIn neopentane, only one type of hydrogen is present, hence replacement of any hydrogen atom will give the same product.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 8)\n(NEET 2019)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2,2-dimethylbutane','neopentane','n-pentane','isopentane','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(130803,'chemistry','Hydrocarbon (A) reacts with bromine by substitution to form an alkyl bromide which by Wurtz reaction is converted to gaseous hydrocarbon containing less than four carbon atoms. (A) is','CH₄','Wurtz reaction is used for the preparation of higher alkanes containing even number of C-atoms by reacting alkyl halide with sodium metal in dry etheral. \n\nThe methane is first converted to bromomethane which is then treated through Wurtz reaction to form ethane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 2)\n(NEET 2018) diagram(ne1334)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','C₂H₂','CH₂=CH₂','CH₃--CH₃','CH₄','NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130804,'chemistry','With respect to the conformers of ethane, which of the following statements is true?','Both bond angle and bond length remain same','Conformers of ethane have different dihedral angles but same bond angle and bond lengths.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 1)\n(NEET 2018)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Bond angle changes but bond length remains same.','Both bond angle and bond length change','Both bond angle and bond length remain same','Bond angle remains same but bond length changes','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130805,'chemistry','The correct statement regarding the comparison of staggered and eclipsed conformations of ethane, is','The staggered conformation of ethane is more stable than eclipsed conformation, because staggered conformation has no torsional strain.','Magnitude of torsional strain depends upon the angle of rotation about C—C bond. \n\nStaggered form has the least torsional strain and the eclipsed form has the maximum torsional strain. \n\nSo, the staggered conformation of ethane is more stable than the eclipsed conformation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 1)\n(NEET 2016)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The eclipsed conformation of ethane is more stable than staggered conformation even though the eclipsed conformation has torsional strain.','The staggered conformation of ethane is more stable than eclipsed conformation, because staggered conformation has no torsional strain.','The staggered conformation of ethane is less stable than eclipsed conformation, because staggered conformation has torsional strain.','The eclipsed conformation of ethane is more stable than staggered conformation, because eclipsed conformation has no torsional strain.','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130806,'chemistry','The most stable conformation of n-butane in the following structures shown in diagram?(n1383)','b','The anti-conformation is the most stable conformation of n-butane as in this, the bulky methyl groups are as far apart as possible thereby keeping steric repulsion at a minimum.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 1)\n(NEET 2010)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-384','11-306','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130806q.png'),
(130807,'chemistry','Liquid hydrocarbons can be converted to a mixture of gaseous hydrocarbons by','cracking','The process of cracking converts higher alkanes into smaller alkanes and alkenes. \n\nThis process can be used for production of natural gas.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 7)\n(NEET 2010)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','oxidation','cracking','distillation under reduced pressure','hydrolysis','NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(130808,'chemistry','The most stable conformation of ethylene glycol in the following structures shown in diagram?(n1384)','d','The conformation (d) is most stable because of intermolecular H-bonding.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 1)\n(NEET 2010)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-384','11-306','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130808q.png'),
(130809,'chemistry','Dihedral angle in staggered form of ethane is','0°','The staggered form of ethane has the following structure and the dihedral angle is 60°, which means ‘H’ atoms are at an angle of 60° to each other.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 4)\n(NEET 2000)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','0°','120°','60°','180°','NEW','11-383','11-305',NULL),
(130810,'chemistry','Which of the following reaction is expected to readily give a hydrocarbon product in good yields, refer diagram?(n1385)','c','When an aqueous solution of sodium or potassium salt of carboxylic acid is electrolysed, hydrocarbon is evolved at anode.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 3)\n(NEET 1997)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-379','11-301','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130810q.png'),
(130811,'chemistry','In the commercial gasolines, the type of hydrocarbons which are more desirable is','branched hydrocarbon','The branching of chain increases the octane number of a fuel. \n\nHigh octane number means better fuel.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 5)\n(NEET 1997)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','linear unsaturated hydrocarbon','toluene','branched hydrocarbon','straight-chain hydrocarbon.','NEW','11-379','11-301',NULL),
(130812,'chemistry','The most stable conformation of n-butane is','staggered','The anti-conformation is the most stable conformation of n-butane as in this, the bulky methyl groups are as far apart as possible thereby keeping steric repulsion at a minimum.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 1)\n(NEET 1997)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','gauche','staggered','skew boat','eclipsed','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130813,'chemistry','Which of the following is used as an anti knocking material?','T.E.L.','Tetraethyl lead, is used as an anti knocking agent in gasoline used for running automobiles.\n(NEET 1996)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Glyoxal','Freon','T.E.L.','Ethyl alcohol','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130814,'chemistry','Reactivity of hydrogen atoms attached to different carbon atoms in alkanes has the order','tertiary > secondary > primary','The reactivity of H-atom depends upon the stability of free radicals, therefore reactivity of H-atom follows the order : 3º > 2º > 1º.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 1)\n(NEET 1993)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','tertiary > primary > secondary','primary > secondary > tertiary','both (a) and (b)','tertiary > secondary > primary','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(130815,'chemistry','An alkene on ozonolysis gives methanal as one of the product. Its structure from the options given in diagram?(n1386)','c','Ozonolysis involves ozone addition to give aldehyde or ketone depending upon the position of double bond in alkene. \n\nIn order to have methanal, the reactant should have a terminal double bond like in the structure (c). \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -4 )\n(NEET 2020) diagram(ne1335)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130815q.png'),
(130816,'chemistry','An alkene A on reaction with O₃ and Zn H₂O gives propanone and ethanal in equimolar ratio. Addition of HCl to alkene A gives B as the major product. The structure of product B is, refer diagram?(n1387)','d','Based on the question, the reactant predicted is 2-methyl-2-butene, This compounds gives propanone and ethanal on ozonolysis and undergoes Markovnikov\'s addition with HCl to give (d) as final product.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 5)\n(NEET 2019)  diagram(ne1336)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-389','11-311','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130816q.png'),
(130817,'chemistry','The most suitable reagent for the following conversion shown in diagram?(n1388)','H₂, Pd/C, quinoline','Alkenes reduced by Pd/C are having cis geometry. \n\nHowever, alkynes on reduction with sodium in liquid ammonia form trans alkenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 4)\n(NEET 2019)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','AlCl₃','Na/liquid NH₃','H₂, Pd/C, quinoline','Zn/HCl','NEW','11-387','11-309','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130817q.png'),
(130818,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds shall not produce propene by reaction with HBr followed by elimination or direct only elimination reaction,refer diagram?(n1389)','c','All the given compounds undergo β-elimination except (c). \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 1) \n(NEET-II 2016) diagram(ne1337)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-388','11-310','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130818q.png'),
(130819,'chemistry','The compound that will react most readily with gaseous bromine has the formula','C₃H₆','Rate of free radical substitution with bromine gas depends upon the stability of free radical. \n\nPropenyl free radical is allylic free radical which is more stable.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 1)\n(NEET-II 2016)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','C₃H₆','C₂H₂','C₄H₁₀','C₂H₄','NEW','11-389','11-311',NULL),
(130820,'chemistry','In the reaction with HCl, an alkene reacts in accordance with the Markovnikov’s rule to give a product 1-chloro-1-methyl-cyclohexane. The possible alkene is,refer diagram?(n1390)','c','Both (A) and (B) will add the hydrogen of HCl to the carbon atom containing greater number of hydrogen atoms according to the Markovnikov\'s rule. \n\nBoth will give 1-chloro-1-methylcyclohexane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 5)  \n(NEET 2015) diagram(ne1338)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-389','11-311','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130820q.png'),
(130821,'chemistry','The major product in the following reaction given in diagram?(n1391)','a','The compound will add the water molecule according to the Markovnikov\'s rule. \n\nHowever, there will be an unexpected major product (a). \n\nThis unexpected outcome may be explained by a carbocation rearrangement from the initial 2º-carbocation to a 3º-carbocation by a 1,2-shift of a methyl group. \n\nThe 3º- carbocation is more stable and will react with hydroxyl group to form (a).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-331, Paragraph - 2) \n(NEET 2012) diagram(ne1339)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-389','11-311','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130821q.png'),
(130901,'chemistry','Select the set with the steps included in SE reaction.\n\n(I) Generation of electrophile\n\n(II) Formation of arenium ion\n\n(III) Carbanion intermediate formation\n\n(IV) Replace of proton\n\n(V) Removal of hydrogen atom\n\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(I), (III) and (V)','In Electrophilic Substitution Reaction an electrophile substitutes or replaces another electrophile (mainly removal of Hydrogen Atom).','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(I), (III) and (V)','(I), (III), (IV) and (V)','(I), (II) and (V)','(I), (II), (III) and (V)','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(130902,'chemistry','Select the benzene ring activating group.','–NH₂, –NHR, –NHCOCH₃, –OCH₃, –SR',NULL,'Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','NH₂, –SO₃H, –NHR,–CN, –CHO','–NH₂, –NHR, –NHCOCH₃, –OCH₃, –SR','–NO₂, –CN, –CHO,\n–COR, –COOH,','–COR, –COOH, –SO₃H,  –NHCOCH₃','NEW','11-403','11-325',NULL),
(130903,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a carcinogenic hydrocarbon? Refer diagram(n13124)','3',NULL,'Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-404','11-326','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130903q.png'),
(130904,'chemistry','Which of the following statement supports the aromatic substitution reaction?','The NO₂ substitutes hydrogen in benzene','Electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions are organic reactions wherein an electrophile replaces an atom which is attached to an aromatic ring. \n\nCommonly, these reactions involve the replacement of a hydrogen atom belonging to a benzene ring with an electrophile.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The chlorine of CH₃Cl substitutes the hydrogen in benzene','The SO₃ substitutes the methyl group in toluene','The NO₂ substitutes hydrogen in benzene','The benzene ring open to give n-hexane','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(130905,'chemistry','Which of the following is produced in the given reaction? Refer diagram (n13125)','2','In the presence of AlCl₃, benzene and 1-chloropropane react to generate a primary carbocation. \n\nCompared to secondary carbocation, initial carbocation is less stable. \n\nAs a result, a secondary carbocation is created from this original carbocation by a reaction of rearrangement.\n\nThis rearrangement results in the production of isopropyl benzene rather than n-propyl benzene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -9)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130905q.png'),
(130906,'chemistry','Identify the cyclic compound that is not aromatic. Refer diagram(n13126)','4','Aromatic hydrocarbons are hydrocarbons that have delocalized pi electrons and sigma bonds between their carbon atoms in a ring. \n\nBenzene was considered as parent ‘aromatic’ compound. \n\nNow, the name is applied to all the ring systems whether or not having benzene ring, possessing following characteristics. (i) Planarity (ii) Complete delocalisation of the pi electrons in the ring (iii) Presence of (4n + 2) pi electrons in the ring.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-399, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130906q.png'),
(130907,'chemistry','Which of the following is used as reagent for the addition of water to alkyne?','Hg²⁺/ H⁺','One molecule of water adds to alkynes on warming with mercuric sulphate and dilute sulphuric acid at 333 K to form carbonyl compounds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Anhyd. AlCl₃','H₂SO₄','Hg²⁺/ H⁺','Pt/Pd/Ni','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(130908,'chemistry','The colour of solution of bromine in carbon tetrachloride is','Reddish orange','The solution of bromine in carbon tetrachloride has reddish orange colour.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Blue-green','Reddish orange','Reddish brown','Pink','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(130909,'chemistry','H-C-C bond angle in alkyne is','180°','The remaining sp hybridised orbital of each carbon atom undergoes overlapping along the internuclear axis with the 1s orbital of each of the two hydrogen atoms forming two C-H sigma bonds. \n\nH-C-C bond angle is of 180°.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-393, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','60°','90°','180°','120°','NEW','11-393','11-315',NULL),
(130910,'chemistry','Which is the following statement is false about benzene?','Each carbon atom in benzene is sp hybridized','Each carbon atom in the benzene ring is sp² hybridized.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-398, Paragraph -4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','There are six delocalized electron in resonance about the ring','Each carbon atom in benzene is sp hybridized','The single and double bond alternate with each other.','Each carbon atom has one unhybridized p orbital.','NEW','11-398','11-320',NULL),
(130911,'chemistry','Identify the intermediate formed in the following reaction. Refer diagram(n13127)','CH₃CH₂=CHBr','Vicinal dihalides on treatment with alcoholic potassium hydroxide undergo dehydrohalogenation. \n\nOne molecule of hydrogen halide is eliminated to form alkenyl halide which on treatment with sodamide gives alkyne.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CH₂=CHBr','CH₃CH₂=CH₂','CH₃CHBr=CHOH','CH₃CH₂=CHOH','NEW','11-394','11-316','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130911q.png'),
(130912,'chemistry','First _______ members of alkyne are gases and next _______ are liquids.','three, eight respectively','First three members are gases, the next eight are liquids and the higher ones are solids.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-394, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','three, eight respectively','four, eighteen respectively','four, twelve respectively','three, twelve respectively','NEW','11-394','11-316',NULL),
(130913,'chemistry','The IUPAC names of the compound is: Refer diagram(n13128)','3,4-dipropyl-1,3-hexadien-5-yne','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1 The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). \n\nThus the name of the given compound is 3,3-diethyl-5-isopropyl-4-methyloctane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','3,4-dipropyl-3,5-hexadien-1-yne','3,4-dipropyl-1,3-hexadien-5-yne','3,4-dipropyl-1,5-hexadien-3-yne','3,4-diisopropyl-1,3-hexadien-5-yne','NEW','11-374','11-296','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130913q.png'),
(130914,'chemistry','The repulsive interaction between the electron clouds, which affects stability of a conformation, is called _____','torsional strain','The repulsive interaction between the electron clouds, which affects stability of a conformation, is called torsional strain.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-384, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','dihedral angle','torsional angle','torsional strain','eclipse','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(130915,'chemistry','Identify the option with correct set of statements.\n\n(i) The eclipsed conformation has two groups on opposite carbons close\n\n(ii)The dihedral angle in staggered is 0°\n\n(iii) The torsional angle in eclipse is 0°\n\n(iv) The staggered conformation has opposite groups maximum distance apart','(i), (iii) and (iv)','One such conformation in which hydrogen atoms attached to two carbons are as closed together as possible is called eclipsed conformation and the other in which hydrogens are as far apart as possible is known as the staggered conformation.\n\nThe torsional angle in eclipse and staggered is 0° and 60°.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-383, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(i), (ii) and (iv)','(i), (iii) and (iv)','(iii) and (iv)','(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)','NEW','11-383','11-305',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(130916,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n\nAssertion (A): Carbon is tetravalent, however it has 2 unpaired electron in valence shell.\n\nReason(R): Carbon undergoes sp³ hybridization to form 4 covalent bonds. This bonding ability is used to make long chained hydrocarbons.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Given below are two statements.\n\nAssertion (A): Carbon is tetravalent, however it has 2 unpaired electron in valence shell.\n\nReason(R): Carbon undergoes sp³ hybridization to form 4 covalent bonds. This bonding ability is used to make long chained hydrocarbons.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct.','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct.','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130917,'chemistry','The word paraffin is derived from which latin words?','Parum, affinis','The word paraffin is derived from 2 Latin words atin : parum, little; affinis, affinity.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Para, affin','Paras, affinus','Pram, affines','Parum, affinis','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130918,'chemistry','Match the reaction with their products. Refer diagram(n13129)','(i) - (b), (ii) - (c), (iii) - (a)','During chlorination, alkylation and acylation of benzene, anhydrous AlCl₃, being a Lewis acid helps in generation of the elctrophile Cl, R , RC O (acylium ion) respectively by combining with the attacking reagent.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph - 3) diagram(ne1350)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) - (b), (ii) - (c), (iii) - (a)','(i) - (a), (ii) - (c), (iii) - (b)','(i) - (b), (ii) - (a), (iii) - (c)','(i) - (c), (ii) - (b), (iii) - (a)','NEW','11-401','11-323','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/130918q.png'),
(130919,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is correct regarding the arenium ion?','It is formed as a result of attack of electrophile on the benzene.','Attack of electrophile results in the formation of σ-complex or arenium ion in which one of the carbon is sp³ hybridized.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph - 6)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','It has enhanced aromatic character.','It is formed as a result of attack of electrophile on the benzene.','It gets stabilized by losing its proton.','It is formed by the attack of electrophile on the benzaldehyde.','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(130920,'chemistry','Which of the following set has statements that are correct regarding the arenium ion?\n\n(i) It is formed as a result of attack of nucleophile on the carboxylic acid.\n\n(ii) It gets stabilized by hybridization.\n\n(iii) One of the carbon atom in arenium ion is sp hybridized.\n\n(iv) It has lowered aromatic character.\n\n(v) It is formed as a result of attack of electrophile on the benzene.','(ii), (iv) and (v)','Attack of electrophile results in the formation of σ-complex or arenium ion in which one of the carbon is sp³ hybridized. \n\nThe arenium ion gets stabilised by resonance.\n\nSigma complex or arenium ion loses its aromatic character because delocalisation of electrons stops at sp³ hybridised carbon.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401,402, Paragraph - 6 and 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (iv) and (v)','(iii), (iv) and (v)','(iii), (ii) and (iv)','(ii), (iv) and (v)','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(130921,'chemistry','Which of the following produce BHC on adding benzene to chlorine molecule?','UV radiations','Under ultra-violet light, three chlorine molecules add to benzene to produce benzene hexachloride, C6H6Cl6 which is also called gammaxane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','anhyd. AlCl₃','FeCl₃','UV radiations','HCl','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(130922,'chemistry','Three chlorine molecules add to benzene to produce benzene hexachloride, C₆H₆Cl₆ which is also called','Gammaxane','Three chlorine molecules add to benzene to produce benzene hexachloride, C6H6Cl6 which is also called gammaxane. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-402, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Benzene chloride','Gammaxane','Sumitomo Bakelite','Both (a) and (b)','NEW','11-402','11-324','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/130922q.png'),
(130923,'chemistry','The sulphuric acid is used in nitration','both (a) and (b)','A nitro group is introduced into benzene ring when benzene is heated with a mixture of concentrated nitric acid and concentrated sulphuric acid (nitrating mixture). \n\nThe sulphuric acid promotes the synthesis of reactive nitronium ions by acting as a dehydrating agent. \n\nThe nitronium ion is added to the aromatic molecule during nitration, which takes place in the aqueous acid phase.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-374, Paragraph - 1) diagram(ne1351)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','acts as dehydrating agent','promotes the formation of nitronium ion','produces sulphonic acid','both (a) and (b)','NEW','11-374','11-296',NULL),
(110805,'chemistry','which oxidation state is highly oxidizing','3+','Oxidizing power is linked to electronegativity, and electronegativity linked\n with size, smaller the size more will be electronegativity,more will be the \n oxidzing power, more the oxidation no.smaller will be the size of cation \n oxidizing power is actually the ability to oxidize others and reduce itself.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'2','mcq','0','1+','2+','3+','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(131001,'chemistry','Arrange the following in decreasing order of their boiling points. \n\nA. n-butane\nB. 2-methylbutane\nC. n-pentane\nD. 2,2-dimethylpropane','C > B > A > D','The boiling point depends on various factors such as intermolecular forces, molecular weight, and branching in the molecule.\n\nIn the given list of compounds (n-butane, 2-methylbutane, n-pentane, and 2,2-dimethylpropane), n-pentane has the highest boiling point because it has the highest molecular weight and no branching, which creates the strongest intermolecular forces.\n\n2,2-dimethylpropane has the lowest boiling point among the given compounds because it has the lowest molecular weight and the most branching. The branching reduces its intermolecular forces and therefore its boiling point.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A > B > C > D','B > C > D > A','D > C > B > A','C > B > A > D','NEW','11-375','11-297',NULL),
(131002,'chemistry','Arrange the halogens F₂ , Cl₂ , Br₂ , I₂  in order of their increasing\nreactivity with alkanes.','I₂ < Br₂ < Cl₂ < F₂','As we go down the group in the periodic table, the size of the halogen atom increases, and its ability to form a bond with the carbon atom in the alkane molecule decreases. Hence, the reactivity of halogens with alkanes increases as we move from fluorine to iodine.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cl₂ < F₂ < I₂ < Br₂','F₂ < Cl₂ < Br₂ < I₂','I₂ < Br₂ < Cl₂ < F₂','Br₂ < I₂ < F₂ < Cl₂','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(131003,'chemistry','The increasing order of reduction of alkyl halides with zinc and dilute\nHCl is','R -Cl < R − Br < R − I','The reduction of alkyl halides with zinc and dilute HCl is a nucleophilic substitution reaction.\n\nAs we move from iodine to chlorine in the halogen group, the bond strength between the halogen and the carbon atom in the alkyl halide increases. This makes it harder to replace the halogen atom, and thus reduces the reactivity of the alkyl halide towards this reduction reaction.Therefore, the decreasing order of reactivity of alkyl halides towards reduction with zinc and dilute HCl is R-Cl > R-Br > R-I','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','R − Cl < R − I < R − Br','R -Cl < R − Br < R − I','R − I < R − Br < R − Cl','R − Br < R − I < R − Cl','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(131004,'chemistry','The correct IUPAC name of the following alkane is: Refer diagram(n13135)','3,6-diethyl-2-methyloctane','The parent chain contains eight carbon atoms (octane).\nThe location of the two ethyl groups are specified by the numbers 3 and 6, which means they are attached to the third and sixth carbon atoms in the chain.\nThere is one methyl group attached to the second carbon atom in the chain.\n\nTherefore, the correct IUPAC name of the alkane is 3,6-diethyl-2-methyloctane.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3,6-diethyl-2-methyloctane','5-isopropyl -3-ethyloctane','3-ethyl-5-isopropyloctane','3-isopropyl-6-ethyloctane','NEW','11-388','11-310','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131004q.png'),
(131005,'chemistry','5 The addition of HBr to 1-butene gives a mixture of products A, B and C. The mixture consists of : Refer diagram(n13136)','A and B as major and C as minor products','The alkene will follow Markownikoff\'s rule to give major product. C does not follows the rule so it will be minor product.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','A and B as major and C as minor products','B as major, A and C as minor products','B as minor, A and C as major products','A and B as minor and C as major products','NEW','11-389','11-311','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131005q.png'),
(131006,'chemistry','Which of the following will not show geometrical isomerism? Refer diagram(n13137)','d','Geometrical isomers are those isomers which possess the same molecular and structural formula but differ in the arrangement of atoms or groups in space due to hindered rotation around the double bonded atoms.\n\nSince option d has same two CH3 group attached around double bond, therefore will not show geometrical isomerism\n\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-386, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-386','11-308','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131006q.png'),
(131007,'chemistry','Arrange the following hydrogen halides in order of their decreasing reactivity with propene.','HI > HBr > HCl','HI > HBr > HCl\n\nThis is because hydrogen iodide (HI) is the most reactive of the three hydrogen halides due to the large size of the iodine atom, which makes the H-I bond weaker and more polarizable. This allows the H-I bond to easily break and form a reactive iodide ion. As a result, HI reacts the most readily with propene.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','HCl > HBr > HI','HBr > HI > HCl','HI > HBr > HCl','HCl > HI > HBr','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(131008,'chemistry','Arrange the following alkyl halides in decreasing order of the rate of β  − elimination reaction with alcoholic KOH. Refer diagram(n13138)','A > C > B','The rate of b-elimination reaction with alcoholic KOH depends on the stability of the carbocation intermediate that is formed during the reaction. The more stable the carbocation intermediate, the faster the rate of the reaction.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','A>B>C','C > B > A','B > C > A','A > C > B','NEW','11-388','11-310','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131008q.png'),
(131009,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions of methane is incomplete combustion? Refer diagram(n13139)','c','During incomplete combustion of alkanes with insufficient amount of air or dioxygen, carbon black is formed which is used in the manufacture of ink, printer ink, black pigments and as filters.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 9)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-381','11-303','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131009q.png'),
(131010,'chemistry','Some oxidation reactions of methane are given below. Which of them is/are controlled oxidation reactions? Refer diagram(n13140)','c,d','Alkanes on heating with a regulated supply of dioxygen or air at high pressure and in the presence of suitable catalysts give a variety of oxidation products which includes methanol, methanal, ethanoic acid and 2-Methylpropan-2-ol. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-382','11-304','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131010q.png'),
(131011,'chemistry','Which of the following alkenes on ozonolysis give a mixture of ketones only? Refer diagram(n13141)','c,d','Ozonolysis of alkenes involves the addition of ozone molecule to alkene to form ozonide, and then cleavage of the ozonide by Zn-H₂O to smaller molecules. \n\nThis reaction is highly useful in detecting the position of the double bond in alkenes or other unsaturated compounds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -4). Refer diagram(ne1353)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131011q.png'),
(131012,'chemistry','For an electrophilic substitution reaction, the presence of a halogen atom in the benzene ring .............\n\n(a) deactivates the ring by inductive effect\n\n(b) deactivates the ring by reasonance\n\n(c) increases the charge density at ortho and para position relative to meta position by resonance.\n\n(d) directs the incoming electrophile to meta position by increasing the charge density relative to ortho and para position.','a,c','For an electrophilic substitution reaction, the presence of a halogen atom in the benzene ring affects the reactivity and the orientation of the incoming electrophile. Therefore it directs the incoming electrophile to meta position by increasing the charge density relative to ortho and para position.\n\nThe halogen atom in the benzene ring is an electron-withdrawing group due to its electronegativity. It withdraws electron density from the ring by the inductive effect, which reduces the electron density of the benzene ring','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(131013,'chemistry','In an electrophilic substitution reaction of nitrobenzene, the presence of nitro group..........\n\n(a) deactivates the ring by inductive effect\n\n(b) activates the ring by inductive effect\n\n(c) decreases the charge density at ortho and para position of the ring relative to meta position by resonance\n\n(d) increases the charge density at meta position relative to the ortho and para positions of the ring by resonance','a,c','In an electrophilic substitution reaction of nitrobenzene, the presence of nitro group affects the reactivity and the orientation of the incoming electrophile. Therefore it deactivates the ring by inductive effect.\n\nThe decreased electron density at the ortho and para positions, and the increased electron density at the meta position, make the nitro group direct the incoming electrophile to the meta position of the ring.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-401','11-323',NULL),
(131014,'chemistry','The molecules having dipole moment are:\n\na. 2,2-Dimethylpropane\n\nb. trans-Pent-2-ene\n\nc. cis-Hex-3-ene\n\nd. 2,2,3,3,- Tetramethylbutane','b,c','Both trans-Pent-2-ene and cis-Hex-3-ene are unsymmetrical molecules, and each of them has polar covalent bonds. Due to the difference in electronegativity between the carbon and hydrogen atoms in the molecules, there is an uneven distribution of electron density, which creates a permanent dipole moment.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-399','11-321',NULL),
(131015,'chemistry','Four structures are given in options (a) to (d). Examine them and select the aromatic structures. Refer diagram(n13142)','a,c','A compound is considered aromatic if it meets the following three conditions, known as Hückel\'s rule:\n\nThe compound must be cyclic, meaning it has a closed ring of atoms.\n\nThe compound must be planar, meaning all the atoms in the ring lie in the same plane.\n\nThe compound must follow the 4n+2 rule','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,c','b,c','a,d','c,d','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131015q.png'),
(131101,'chemistry','Which of these will not react with acetylene?','dil. NaOH','Acetylene is an unsaturated hydrocarbon and is highly reactive towards many reagents. It reacts with Na in liquid NH3 to form sodium acetylide. \n\nHowever, Dilute NaOH is a weak base and does not have the ability to deprotonate acetylene.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','dil. NaOH','ammonical AgNO3','Na in liq. NH3','HCl','NEW','11-387','11-309',NULL),
(131102,'chemistry','Whiat is the product when acetylene reacts with hypochlorous acid?','Cl₂CHCHO','When acetylene (ethyne) reacts with hypochlorous acid (HOCl), the product formed is dichloroacetaldehyde (Cl₂CHCHO).\n\nThe reaction occurs as follows:\n\nThe HOCl molecule dissociates to form H⁺ and OCl⁻ ions:\n\nHOCl → H⁺ + OCl⁻\n\nThe H⁺ ion protonates the triple bond of acetylene to form the acetylene cation:\n\nHC≡CH + H⁺ → HC≡CH₂⁺\n\nThe OCl⁻ ion attacks the acetylene cation to form chloroacetylene:\n\nHC≡CH₂⁺ + OCl⁻ → HC≡CCl + H₂O\n\nChloroacetylene is further chlorinated by HOCl to give dichloroacetaldehyde:\n\nHC≡CCl + HOCl → Cl₂CHCHO','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','CH3COCl','ClCH₂CHO','Cl₂CHCHO','ClCH₂COOH','NEW','11-387','11-309',NULL),
(131103,'chemistry','On mixing a certain alkane with chlorine and irradiating it with ultraviolet light, it forms only one monochloroalkane. This alkane could be:','neopentane','In neopentane all the H atoms are primary, thereform equivalent. Refer diagram(ne1354)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','pentane','isopentane','neopentane','propane','NEW','11-375','11-297',NULL),
(131104,'chemistry','Butene may be converted to butane by reaction with','Pd/H2','Dihydrogen gas adds to alkenes and alkynes in the presence of finely divided catalysts like platinum, palladium or nickel to form alkanes. \n\nThis process is called hydrogenation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - 5)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Sn-HCl','Zn-Hg','Pd/H2','Zn-HCl','NEW','11-378','11-300',NULL),
(131105,'chemistry','Which one of the following has minimum boiling point?','Isobutane','With increasing molecular size, alkanes\' boiling points rise. \n\nThe more branched the chain in isomer, the lower the boiling point is expected to be. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-375, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Butene','Butyne','Butane','Isobutane','NEW','11-375','11-297',NULL),
(131106,'chemistry','2-Methylbutane on reacting with bromine in the presence of sunlight gives mainly','2-bromo-2-methylbutane','When 2-methylbutane (also known as isopentane) reacts with bromine in the presence of sunlight, it undergoes a substitution reaction in which one of the hydrogen atoms on the carbon chain is replaced by a bromine atom. Refer diagram(ne1355)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','1-bromo-3-methylbutane','2-bromo-3-methylbutane','2-bromo-2-methylbutane','1-bromo-2-methylbutane','NEW','11-389','11-311',NULL),
(131107,'chemistry','Reaction of one molecule of HBr with one molecule of 1,3 butadiene at 40\'C gives predominantly','1-bromo-2-butene under thermodynamically controlled conditions','The reaction of one molecule of hydrogen bromide (HBr) with one molecule of 1,3-butadiene (CH2=CH-CH=CH2) at 40°C results in an addition reaction. There are two possible sites of addition on the butadiene molecule: the 1,2-position or the 1,4-position.\n\nThe predominant product formed under these conditions is 3-bromobutene, which is the product of 1,2-addition. Refer diagram(ne1356)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','1-bromo-2-butene under kinetically controlled conditions','3-bromobutene under thermodynamically controlled conditions','1-bromo-2-butene under thermodynamically controlled conditions','3-bromobutene under kinetically controlled conditions','NEW','11-389','11-311',NULL),
(131108,'chemistry','The compound formed as a result of oxidation of ehtylbenzene by KMnO4 is','benzoic acid','KMnO4 oxidises alkyl group of alkyl benzene to -COOH group forming benzoic acid. Refer diagram(ne1357)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','benzyl alcohol','benzophenone','acetophenone','benzoic acid','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(131109,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions will yield 2,2-dibromopropane? Refer diagram(n13143)','b','According to Markonikoff rule, option b will form 2,2-Dibromopropane. Refer diagram(ne1358)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-389','11-311','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131109q.png'),
(131110,'chemistry','The reaction of toluene with Cl2 in presence of FeCl3 gives predominantly','o- and p- chlorotoluene','The reaction of Cl₂, in presence of FeCl₃, with toluene yields a ring substitution product \n\nIn presence of sunlight, free radical reaction takes place. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -5) Refer diagram(ne1359)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','m-chlorobenzene','benzoyl chloride','benzyl chloride','o- and p- chlorotoluene','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
