(131112,'chemistry','The hydrocarbon which can react with sodium in liquid ammonia is: Refer diagram(n13145)','b','Terminal alkynes (1-butyne) react with NaNH₂ to form sodium acetylide and evolve hydrogen','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-394','11-316','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131112q.png'),
(131113,'chemistry','The treatment of CH3MgX with propyne produces:','d','Terminal alkyne has acidic hydrogen which protonates Grignard reagent producing CH4. Refer diagram(ne1361)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-378','11-300','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131113q.png'),
(131114,'chemistry','The non aromatic compound among the following is: Refer diagram(n13146)','d','"A compound is considered aromatic if it meets the following three conditions, known as Hückel\'s rule:\n\nThe compound must be cyclic, meaning it has a closed ring of atoms.\n\nThe compound must be planar, meaning all the atoms in the ring lie in the same plane.\n\nThe compound must follow the 4n+2 rule"','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131114q.png'),
(131115,'chemistry','Which branched chain isomer of the hydrocarbon with molecular mass 72u gives only one isomer of mono substituted alkyl halide?','Neopentane','In chlorination of alkanes, hydrogen is replaced by chlorine. \n\nIn neopentane, only one type of hydrogen is present, hence replacement of any hydrogen atom will give the same product.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-381, Paragraph - 8)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Tertiary butyl chloride','Neopentane','Isohexane','Neohexane','NEW','11-381','11-303',NULL),
(131116,'chemistry','2-Hexyne gives trans-2-hexene on treatment with:','Li/NH3','Alkenes reduced by Pd/C are having cis geometry. \n\nHowever, alkynes on reduction with sodium in liquid ammonia form trans alkenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Pt/H2','Li/NH3','Pd/BaSO4','LiAlH4','NEW','11-387','11-309',NULL),
(131117,'chemistry','In the following sequence of reactions, the product C is: Refer diagram(n13147)','Ph-CHO','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne1362)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','Ph-CH2OH','Ph-CHO','Ph-COOH','Ph-CH3','NEW','11-387','11-309','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131117q.png'),
(131118,'chemistry','The reaction with propene with HOCl(Cl2 + H2O) proceeds through the intermediate:','CH₃-CH⁺-CH₂-Cl','The intermediate will be formed as shown in the diagram(ne1363)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃-CH(OH)-CH₂⁺','CH₃-CHCl-CH₂⁺','CH₃-CH⁺-CH₂-OH','CH₃-CH⁺-CH₂-Cl','NEW','11-387','11-309',NULL),
(131119,'chemistry','The trans-alkene are formed by the reduction of alkynes with:','Na in liq. NH3','Alkenes reduced by Pd/C are having cis geometry. \n\nHowever, alkynes on reduction with sodium in liquid ammonia form trans alkenes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','H2-Pd/C, BaSO4','NaBH4','Na in liq. NH3','Sn-HCl','NEW','11-387','11-309',NULL),
(131120,'biology','C₄ plants show saturation at','360 µlL⁻¹','C₄ plants show saturation at about 360 µlL⁻¹. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 222)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Factors Affecting Photosynthesis',NULL,'1','mcq','360 µlL','360 µlL⁻¹','360 µlL⁻²','360 µlL⁺¹','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131121,'biology','What is the saturation level of C₃ plants','450 µlL⁻¹','C₃ plants respond to increased CO₂ concentration and saturation is seen only beyond 450 µlL⁻¹','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Factors Affecting Photosynthesis',NULL,'1','mcq','450 µlL⁻','450 µlL⁻¹','450 µlL','450 µlL¹','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131122,'biology','The C₃ plants response to high CO₂ by increasing the rate of __________','Photosynthesis','C₃ plants respond to higher CO₂  concentration by showing increased rates of photosynthesis leading to higher productivity. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 222)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Factors Affecting Photosynthesis',NULL,'1','mcq','Photorespiration','Transportation','Transpiration','Photosynthesis','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131123,'biology','Which of the following plants grow in  CO₂  enriched atmosphere to increase yield','Tomato and bell pepper','Greenhouse crops such as tomatoes and bell pepper. They are allowed to grow in  CO₂  enriched atmosphere that leads to higher yields. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 222)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Factors Affecting Photosynthesis',NULL,'1','mcq','Tomatoes','Corn','Bell pepper','Tomato and bell pepper','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131124,'biology','Which of the following reactions are temperature controlled','Dark reactions','The dark reactions being enzymatic are temperature controlled as enzymes are temperature sensitive. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 222)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Factors Affecting Photosynthesis',NULL,'1','mcq','Light reactions','Dark reactions','Calvin Cycle','Cyclic phosphorylation','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131125,'biology','Which of the following plants respond to higher temperature to increase growth','C₄ plants','The C₄ plants respond to higher temperatures and show higher rate of photosynthesis. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 222)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Factors Affecting Photosynthesis',NULL,'1','mcq','C₃ plants','Tropical plants','C₄ plants','Mature plants','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131126,'biology','The Optimum  temperature for C₃ plants is at','Lower temperature','C₃ plants have a much lower temperature optimum. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 222)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Factors Affecting Photosynthesis',NULL,'1','mcq','High temperature','Lower temperature','Sub optimal temperature','Both a and c','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131127,'biology','_________ plants have higher temperature optimum','Tropical','Tropical plants have a higher temperature optimum than the plants adapted to temperate climates. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 222)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Factors Affecting Photosynthesis',NULL,'1','mcq','Temporal','Diurnal','Tropical','Perennial','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131128,'biology','Which of the following compound reduces due to water stress','CO₂','Water stress causes the stomata to close hence reducing the CO₂ availability. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 222)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Factors Affecting Photosynthesis',NULL,'1','mcq','O₂','CO₂','H₂O','H₂O₂','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131129,'biology','What are the impacts of water stress on plant','Lower Metabolism and reduction in leaf surface area','Water stress makes leaves wilt, thus, reducing the surface area of the leaves and their metabolic. (Reference  : NCERT Class 11th, Pg. 222)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Factors Affecting Photosynthesis',NULL,'1','mcq','Lower metabolism','Wilting of leaves and higher metabolism','Reduction in leaf surface area and reduction in water','Lower Metabolism and reduction in leaf surface area','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131201,'chemistry','Which of them will form propane as product? Refer diagram(n13148)','b,c','Dihydrogen gas adds to alkenes and alkynes in the presence of finely divided catalysts like platinum, palladium or nickel to form alkanes. \n\nThis process is called hydrogenation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - 5) Refer diagram(ne1364)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','NEW','11-378','11-300','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131201q.png'),
(131202,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction is : Refer diagram(n13149)','Propane','Alkyl halides (except fluorides) on reduction with zinc and dilute hydrochloric acid give alkanes. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-378, Paragraph - 6) diagram(ne1365)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ethane','Propane','Methane','Propene','NEW','11-378','11-300','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131202q.png'),
(131203,'chemistry','The given reaction undergoes : Refer diagram(n13150)','Decarboxylation','Sodium salts of carboxylic acids on heating with soda lime (mixture of sodium hydroxide and calcium oxide) give alkanes containing one carbon atom less than the carboxylic acid. \n\nIn order to prepare propane we will use butanoic acid. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-379, Paragraph - 2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Kolbe\'s electrolysis','Decarboxylation','Wurtz reaction','Hydrogenation','NEW','11-379','11-301','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131203q.png'),
(131204,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction is : Refer diagram(n13151)','Trichloromethane','Halogenation takes place either at higher temperature (573-773 K) or in the presence of diffused sunlight or ultraviolet light.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-380, Paragraph - 5) Refer diagram(ne1366)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Chloromethane','Dichloromethane','Trichloromethane','Tetrachloromethane','NEW','11-380','11-302','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131204q.png'),
(131205,'chemistry','In the given reaction, ethanoic acid can be formed by: Refer diagram(n13152)','only c','Alkanes on heating with a regulated supply of dioxygen or air at high pressure and in the presence of suitable catalysts give a variety of oxidation products which includes methanol, methanal, ethanoic acid and 2-Methylpropan-2-ol.  \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 1) Refer diagram(ne1367)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','only c','only b','NEW','11-382','11-304','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131205q.png'),
(131206,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction is : Refer diagram(n13153)','2-Methylpropan-2-ol','Ordinarily alkanes resist oxidation but alkanes having tertiary H atom can be oxidized to corresponding alcohols by potassium permanganate. \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 2)diagram(ne1368)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','2-Methylpentane','3-Methylpethane','2-Methylpropan-2-ol','Methanol','NEW','11-382','11-304','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131206q.png'),
(131207,'chemistry','The given reaction occurs in the presence of : Refer diagram(n13154)','Anhy. AlCl3/HCl','n-Alkanes on heating in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride and hydrogen chloride gas isomerise to branched chain alkanes.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 3) Refer diagram(ne1369)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ni','Mo2O3','Cu/523K/100atm','Anhy. AlCl3/HCl','NEW','11-382','11-304','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131207q.png'),
(131208,'chemistry','Which of them will not be formed on cracking of C6H14?','C5H10','Higher alkanes on heating to higher temperature decompose into lower alkanes, alkenes etc. \n\nSuch a decomposition reaction into smaller fragments by the application of heat is called pyrolysis or cracking.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-382, Paragraph - 7) Refer diagram(ne1370)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH4','C6H12','C5H10','C3H6','NEW','11-382','11-304',NULL),
(131209,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction respectively  is : Refer diagram(n13155)','cis-Alkene, trans-Alkene','Alkynes on partial reduction with calculated amount of dihydrogen in the presence of palladised charcoal partially deactivated with poisons like sulphur compounds or quinoline give alkenes.Alkenes thus obtained are having cis geometry.\n\nHowever, alkynes on reduction with sodium in liquid ammonia form trans alkenes\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-387, Paragraph - 4)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','cis-Alkene, cis-Alkene','cis-Alkene, trans-Alkene','trans-Alkene, cis-Alkene','trans-Alkene, trans-Alkene','NEW','11-387','11-309','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131209q.png'),
(131210,'chemistry','The given reaction undergoes : Refer diagram(n13156)','Dehydrohalogenation','Alkyl halides (R-X) on heating with alcoholic potash (potassium hydroxide dissolved in alcohol) eliminate one molecule of halogen acid to form alkenes. \n\nThis reaction is known as dehydrohalogenation ( removal of halogen acid). \n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 1)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Dehydrgenation','Dehydrohalogenation','Acidic dehydration','Dehalogenation','NEW','11-388','11-310','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131210q.png'),
(131211,'chemistry','The given reaction occurs in the presence of : Refer diagram(n13157)','conc. H2SO4','Alcohols on heating with concentrated sulphuric acid form alkenes with the elimination of one water molecule. \n\nThis reaction is called acidic dehydration.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-388, Paragraph - 4) Refer diagram(ne1371)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Zn','conc. H2SO4','Na/liq. NH3','CCl4','NEW','11-388','11-310','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131211q.png'),
(131212,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction is :  Refer diagram(n13158)','2-Methylpropan-2-ol','In the presence of a few drops of concentrated sulphuric acid alkenes react with water to form alcohols, in accordance with the Markovnikov rule.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -1) Refer diagram(ne1372)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','2-Methylpropanol','Propan-2-ol','Ethane-1,2 diol','2-Methylpropan-2-ol','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131212q.png'),
(131213,'chemistry','Propane-1,2-diol can be formed from propene on treatment with :','dil. KMnO4/273 K','Alkenes on reaction with cold, dilute, aqueous solution of potassium permanganate (Baeyer’s reagent) produce vicinal glycols. \n\nDecolorization of KMnO₄ solution is used as a test for unsaturation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -2) Refer diagram(ne1373)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','H+','dil. KMnO4/273 K','CCl4','conc. H2SO4','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(131214,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction will be. Refer diagram(n13159) :','Propan-2-one','Acidic potassium permanganate or acidic potassium dichromate oxidises alkenes to ketones and/or acids depending upon the nature of the alkene and the experimental conditions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -3) Refer diagram(ne1374)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ethanoic acid','Propan-2-one','Propan-1,2-diol','Propanal','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131214q.png'),
(131215,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction will be: Refer diagram(n13160)','Ethanoic acid','Acidic potassium permanganate or acidic potassium dichromate oxidises alkenes to ketones and/or acids depending upon the nature of the alkene and the experimental conditions.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-391, Paragraph -3) Refer diagram(ne1375)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ethanoic acid','Propan-2-one','Propan-1,2-diol','Propanal','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131215q.png'),
(131216,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction will be. Refer diagram(n13161)','1,1,2,2-Tetrabromopropane','The reddish orange colour of bromine solution in carbon tetrachloride is discharged when bromine adds up to an unsaturation site. \n\nThis reaction is used as a test for unsaturation.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-389, Paragraph - 1) Refer diagram(ne1376)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','2,2-Dibromopropane','1,1,2,2-Tetrabromopropane','Propanone','Dimethylacetylene','NEW','11-389','11-311','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131216q.png'),
(131217,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction will be. Refer diagram(n13162)','Propanone','One molecule of water adds to alkynes on warming with mercuric sulphate and dilute sulphuric acid at 333 K to form carbonyl compounds.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-395, Paragraph -4) Refer diagram(ne1377)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','2,2-Dibromopropane','1,1,2,2-Tetrabromopropane','Propanone','Dimethylacetylene','NEW','11-395','11-317','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131217q.png'),
(131218,'chemistry','Ethyne in the presence of iron tube at 873K forms','Benzene','Ethyne on passing through red hot iron tube at 873K undergoes cyclic polymerization. \n\nThree molecules polymerise to form benzene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-396, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','2,2-Dibromopropane','1,1,2,2-Tetrabromopropane','Benzene','Napthalene','NEW','11-396','11-318',NULL),
(131219,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction will be. Refer  diagram(n13163)','Benzene','Phenol is reduced to benzene by passing its vapours over heated zinc dust.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -1) Refer diagram(ne1378)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Benzene','Napthalene','Toluene','Phenanthrene','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131219q.png'),
(131220,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction will be. Refer diagram(n13164)','Benzene sulphonic acid','The replacement of a hydrogen atom by a sulphonic acid group in a ring is called sulphonation. \n\nIt is carried out by heating benzene with fuming sulphuric acid (oleum).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-400, Paragraph -6) Refer diagram(ne1379)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Benzene','Napthalene','Toluene','Benzene sulphonic acid','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131220q.png'),
(131221,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction will be. Refer diagram(n13165) \n\na. Acetophenone\nb. Acetic acid\nc. Acetone','a,b','The reaction of benzene with an acyl halide or acid anhydride in the presence of Lewis acids (AlCl3) yields acyl benzene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph -1) Refer diagram(ne1380)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','NEW','11-401','11-323','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131221q.png'),
(131222,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction will be. Refer diagram(n13166)','Hexachlorobenzene','Benzene on treatment with excess of chlorine in the presence of anhydrous AlCl₃ can be chlorinated to hexachlorobenzene (C₆Cl₆).\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph -2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Hexachlorobenzene','Cyclohexane','Benzene hexachloride','Chlorobenzene','NEW','11-401','11-323','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131222q.png'),
(131223,'chemistry','The product of the given reaction will be. Refer diagram(n13167)','Benzene hexachloride','Under ultra-violet light, three chlorine molecules add to benzene to produce benzene hexachloride, C6H6Cl6 which is also called gammaxane.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-402, Paragraph -4) Refer diagram(ne1381)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Hexachlorobenzene','Cyclohexane','Benzene hexachloride','Chlorobenzene','NEW','11-402','11-324','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131223q.png'),
(131224,'biology','What will be the number of Calvin cycles to generate one molecule of hexose? [AIPMT 1996]','6','Photosynthesis is actually oxidation reduction process in which water is oxidised and CO₂ reduced to carbohydrates. The reduction of CO₂ to carbohydrates needs assimilatory powers, i.e., ATP and NADPH₂. The process of photosynthesis involves two steps–(i) Light dependent phase or photochemical reaction.(ii) Light independent phase or dark reaction.In Calvin cycle, CO₂ acceptor molecule is RuBP or RuDP. The enzyme catalyzing this reaction is RuBP-carboxylase/oxygenase (RuBisCO). As Calvin cycle takes in only one carbon (as CO₂) at a time, so it takes six turns of the cycle to produce a net gain of six carbons (i.e., hexose or glucose). In this cycle, for formation of one mole of hexose sugar (Glucose), 18 ATP and 12 NADPH₂ are used. For 6 molecules of CO₂ needs 18 ATP and 12 NADPH₂ molecules so for assimilation of one molecule of CO₂ it needs 3 ATP and 2 NADPH₂ molecules.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','8','9','4','6','NEW','11-218','11-145',NULL),
(131225,'biology','The primary acceptor, during CO₂ fixation in C₃ plants, is [AIPMT 1995]','Ribulose 1, 5-diphosphate (RuDP)','The Calvin cycle is also known as C₃ cycle because CO₂ reduction is cyclic process and first stable product in this cycle is a 3-C compound (i.e., 3-phosphoglyceric acid or 3-PGA). In this cycle, CO₂ acceptor molecule is RuBP or RuDP (i.e., Ribulose 1, 5-bisphosphate or Ribulose 1, 5-diphosphate). There occurs covalent bonding of CO₂ to RuBP and the enzyme catalyzing this reaction is RuBP-carboxylase/oxygenase (RuBisCO).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','Phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP)','Ribulose 1, 5-diphosphate (RuDP)','Phosphoglyceric acid (PGA)','Ribulose monophosphate (RMP)','NEW','11-216','11-143',NULL),
(131226,'biology','The  CO₂  acceptor in Calvin cycle/ C₃-plants is [AIPMT 1993]','Ribulose 1, 5-diphosphate (RuDP)','The Calvin cycle is also known as C₃ cycle because CO₂ reduction is cyclic process and first stable product in this cycle is a 3-C compound (i.e., 3-phosphoglyceric acid or 3-PGA). In this cycle, CO₂ acceptor molecule is RuBP or RuDP (i.e., Ribulose 1, 5-bisphosphate or Ribulose 1, 5-diphosphate). There occurs covalent bonding of CO₂ to RuBP and the enzyme catalyzing this reaction is RuBP-carboxylase/oxygenase (RuBisCO).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP)','Ribulose 1, 5-diphosphate (RuDP)','Phosphoglyceric acid (PGA)','Ribulose monophosphate (RMP)','NEW','11-216','11-143',NULL),
(131227,'biology','Which technique has helped in investigation of Calvin cycle? [AIPMT 1991]','Radioactive isotope technique','By employing 14C labelled  CO₂  14CO₂ in photosynthesis and observing the appearance of characteristic radiations in different reaction intermediates and product in different experiments, Calvin and his co-workers were able to formulate the complete metabolic path of carbon assimilation in the form of cycle which is called Calvin cycle','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','X-ray crystallography','X-ray technique','Radioactive isotope technique','Intermittent light','NEW','11-215','11-142',NULL),
(131228,'biology','Dark reactions of photosynthesis occur in [AIPMT 1991]','Stroma outside photosynthetic lamellae','The dark reactions of photosynthesis are purely enzymatic and slower than the primary photochemical reaction. They take place in stroma portion of the chloroplast and independent of light, i.e., they can occur either in presence or in absence of light provided that assimilatory power is available','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Granal thylakoid membranes','Stromal lamella membranes','Stroma outside photosynthetic lamellae','Peri Plastidial space.','NEW','11-209','11-136',NULL),
(131229,'biology','CO₂  joins the photosynthetic pathway in [AIPMT 1988]','Dark reaction','The dark reactions or Blackman’s reactions or biosynthetic phase involves three steps: (i) Fixation of CO₂, (ii) Reduction of CO₂ and (iii) Synthesis of other compound from glucose. It is independent of light. Pigment System I is relatively very weakly fluorescent while pigment system II is strongly fluorescent. Light energy absorbed by pigment in the two systems is ultimately trapped by the P700 and P680. Light reaction is associated with the grana of the chloroplasts. It takes place in the presence of light only.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','PS I','PS II','Light reaction','Dark reaction','NEW','11-209','11-136',NULL),
(131230,'biology','Phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) is the primary CO₂ acceptor in [NEET 2017]','C₄ plants','The primary CO₂  acceptor is a 3-carbon molecule phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) and is present in the mesophyll cells.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','C₄ plants','C₂ plants','C₃ and C₄ plants','C₃ plants','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131231,'biology','A plant in your garden avoids photorespiratory losses, has improved water use efficiency, shows high rates of photosynthesis at high temperatures and has improved efficiency of nitrogen utilisation. In which of the following physiological groups would you assign this plant? [NEET-I 2016]','C₄','The C4 plants respond to higher temperatures and show higher rate of photosynthesis while C3 plants have a much lower temperature optimum.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','CAM','Nitrogen fixer','C₃','C₄','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131232,'biology','Bundle sheath cells [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Are rich in RuBisCO.','C₄ plants show kranz type of anatomy. In kranz anatomy, the mesophyll is undifferentiated and its cells occur in concentric layers around vascular bundles. The vascular bundles are surrounded by large sized bundle sheath cells which are arranged in wreath like manner in one to several layers. In C₄ plants there are two carboxylation reactions, first in mesophyll chloroplast and second in bundle sheath chloroplast. RuBP is present in bundle sheath chloroplasts where C₃ cycle takes place','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Are rich in PEP carboxylase','Lack RuBisCO','Lack both RuBisCO and PEP carboxylase','Are rich in RuBisCO.','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131233,'biology','In kranz anatomy, the bundle sheath cells have [Mains 2011]','Thick walls, no intercellular spaces and large number of chloroplasts','The C₄ plants are adapted to dry tropical regions and have greater productivity of biomass. They have special type of leaf anatomy known as kranz anatomy. In this type of anatomy the bundle sheath cells form several layers around the vascular bundles; they are characterised by having a large number of chloroplasts, thick walls impervious to gaseous exchange and no intercellular spaces','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Thin walls, many intercellular spaces and no chloroplasts','Thick walls, no intercellular spaces and large number of chloroplasts','Thin walls, no intercellular spaces and several chloroplasts','Thick walls, many intercellular spaces and few chloroplasts','NEW','11-218','11-145',NULL),
(131234,'biology','Study the diagram given below. In which of the following options correct words for all the three blanks A, B and C are indicated? diagram (1305)','A-Fixation, B-Decarboxylation, C-Regeneration','Figure 13.8 Pg 217, NCERT Class 11th.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'1','mcq','A-Carboxylation, B-Decarboxylation, C-Reduction','A-Decarboxylation, B-Reduction, C-Regeneration','A-Fixation, B-Transamination, C-Regeneration','A-Fixation, B-Decarboxylation, C-Regeneration','NEW','11-217','11-144','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/131234q.png'),
