(131304,'chemistry','Following reaction can be carried out on treatment with:\n\nBenzene → Acetophenone','Anhy. AlCl3/ CH3COCl','The reaction of benzene with an acyl halide or acid anhydride in the presence of Lewis acids (AlCl3) yields acyl benzene.\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class-11, Page-401, Paragraph -1) diagram(ne1328)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Conversion Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Anhy. AlCl3/ CH3COCl','Acidic KMnO4','Alk. KMnO4','O3/Zn Dust','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(131305,'chemistry','Following reaction can be carried out on treatment with:\n\nEthene → Benzene','Br2/CCl4 → Alc. KOH → NaNH2/Liq. NH3 → Red hot Fe tube','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne1385)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Conversion Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Br2/CCl4 → Alc. KOH → NaNH2/Liq. NH3 → Red hot Fe tube','Alc. KOH → NaNH2/Liq. NH3 → Red hot Fe tube','Conc. HNO3/conc. H2SO4 → Anhy. Cl2','Anhy. FeCl3 → conc. HNO3/conc H2SO4 → Fractional distillation','NEW','11-394','11-316',NULL),
(131306,'biology','In sugarcane plant ¹⁴CO₂ is fixed to malic acid, in which the enzyme that fixes CO₂ is [AIPMT 2003]','Phosphoenol pyruvic acid carboxylase','C4 pathway was first reported in members of Family Gramineae (grasses) like sugarcane, maize, sorghum, etc. In C4 plants, PEP case (PEP carboxylase) is the key enzyme used to fix CO₂ in C4 plants. Oxaloacetic acid is a 4-C compound and is the first stable product so this pathway is known as C4 cycle','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase','Phosphoenol pyruvic acid carboxylase','Ribulose phosphate kinase','Fructose phosphatase.','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131307,'biology','Stomata of CAM plants [AIPMT 2003]','Open during the night and close during the day','Stomata of most plants open at sunrise and close in darkness to allow the entry of CO₂ needed for photosynthesis during the daytime. Certain succulents that are native to hot, dry conditions (e.g., cacti, Kalanchoe, and Bryophyllum) act in an opposite manner. They open their stomata at night, fix  CO₂  into organic acids in the dark, and close their stomata during the day. This is an appropriate way to absorb CO₂ through open stomata at night, when transpiration stress is low, and conserve water during the heat of the day. These plants show Crassulacean Acid Metabolism (CAM)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Are always open','Open during the day and close at night','Open during the night and close during the day','Never open','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(131308,'biology','Which pair is wrong? [AIPMT 2001]','C₃-maize','C₄ pathway was first reported in members of Family Gramineae (grasses) like sugarcane, maize, sorghum etc','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','C₃-maize','C₄-kranz anatomy','Calvin cycle-PGA','Hatch and Slack cycle - OAA','NEW','11-218','11-145',NULL),
(131309,'biology','Which is the first CO₂ acceptor enzyme in C₄ plants? [AIPMT 2000]','PEP- carboxylase','In C4 plants, PEP case (PEP carboxylase) is the key enzyme used to fix CO₂ in C4 plants.Oxaloacetic acid is a 4-C compound and is the first stable product so this pathway is known as C4 cycle.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','RuDP carboxylase','Phosphoric acid','RuBisCO','PEP- carboxylase','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131310,'biology','In C₄ plants, CO₂ combines with [AIPMT 1996]','Phosphoenol pyruvate','C4 pathway was first reported in members of Family Gramineae (grasses) like sugarcane, maize, sorghum, etc. In C4 plants, PEP case (PEP carboxylase) is the key enzyme used to fix CO₂  in C4 plants. Oxaloacetic acid is a 4-C compound and is the first stable product so this pathway is known as C4 cycle.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Phosphoenol pyruvate','Phosphoglyceraldehyde','Phosphoglyceric acid','Ribulose diphosphate','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131311,'biology','In C₄ plants, CO₂ fixation is done by [AIPMT 1996]','Mesophyll cells','The C₄ plants have a characteristic leaf anatomy called kranz anatomy. Here two types of chloroplasts are present - bundle sheath chloroplasts and mesophyll chloroplasts. In C₄ plants,there are two carboxylation reactions which occur first in mesophyll chloroplasts and then in bundle sheath chloroplasts. CO₂ acceptor molecule in mesophyll chloroplasts is PEP (Phosphoenol pyruvate) and not Ribulose 1, 5-bisphosphate. Further it has enzyme PEP-carboxylase for initial CO₂ fixation. RuBP-carboxylase is absent in mesophyll chloroplasts but is present in bundle sheath chloroplasts. The first product formed is oxaloacetic acid and hence it is known as C₄ cycle. Bundle sheath cells fix CO₂ through C₃ cycle','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Sclerenchyma','Chlorenchyma and hypodermis','Mesophyll cells','Guard cells.','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131312,'biology','The CO₂ fixation during C₄ pathway occurs in the chloroplast of [AIPMT 1995]','Mesophyll cells','The C₄ plants have a characteristic leaf anatomy called kranz anatomy. Here two types of chloroplasts are present - bundle sheath chloroplasts and mesophyll chloroplasts. In C₄ plants,there are two carboxylation reactions which occur first in mesophyll chloroplasts and then in bundle sheath chloroplasts. CO₂ acceptor molecule in mesophyll chloroplasts is PEP (Phosphoenol pyruvate) and not Ribulose 1, 5-bisphosphate. Further it has enzyme PEP-carboxylase for initial CO₂ fixation. RuBP-carboxylase is absent in mesophyll chloroplasts but is present in bundle sheath chloroplasts. The first product formed is oxaloacetic acid and hence it is known as C₄ cycle. Bundle sheath cells fix CO₂ through C₃ cycle','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Guard cells','Bundle sheath cells','Mesophyll cells','Spongy parenchyma.','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131313,'biology','Which one is a C₄-plant? [AIPMT 1993]','Maize/corn','The C4 plants include maize and corn','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Papaya','Pea','Potato','Maize/corn','NEW','11-218','11-145',NULL),
(131314,'biology','The enzyme that catalyses  CO₂  fixation in C₄ plants is [AIPMT 1992]','PEP carboxylase','C4 pathway was first reported in members of Family Gramineae (grasses) like sugarcane, maize, sorghum, etc. In C4 plants, PEP case (PEP carboxylase) is the key enzyme used to fix CO₂ in C4 plants. Oxaloacetic acid is a 4-C compound and is the first stable product so this pathway is known as C4 cycle.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','RuBP carboxylase','PEP carboxylase','Carbonic anhydrase','Carboxy Dismutase','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131315,'biology','The first  CO₂  acceptor in C₄-plants is [1992, 1990]','Phosphoenol-pyruvate','In C₄ cycle, CO₂ combines with phosphoenol pyruvic acid to form oxaloacetic acid with the help of enzyme phosphoenolpyruvate carboxylase (PEPcase). The oxaloacetic acid breaks up into pyruvic acid and CO₂ combines with RuBP to form PGA as in Calvin cycle.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Phosphoenol-pyruvate','Ribulose 1, 5-diphosphate','Oxaloacetic acid','Phosphoglyceric acid.','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131316,'biology','Kranz anatomy is typical of [AIPMT 1990]','C₄ plants','C4 plants have the specialized anatomy called Kranz anatomy','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','C₄ plants','C₃ plants','C₂ plants','CAM plants','NEW','11-218','11-145',NULL),
(131317,'biology','In C₄ plants, Calvin cycle operates in [AIPMT 1989]','Stroma of bundle sheath chloroplasts','The C₄ plants have a characteristic leaf anatomy called kranz anatomy. Here two types of chloroplasts are present - bundle sheath chloroplasts and mesophyll chloroplasts. In C₄ plants,there are two carboxylation reactions which occur first in mesophyll chloroplasts and then in bundle sheath chloroplasts. CO₂ acceptor molecule in mesophyll chloroplasts is PEP (Phosphoenol pyruvate) and not Ribulose 1, 5-bisphosphate. Further it has enzyme PEP-carboxylase for initial CO₂ fixation. RuBP-carboxylase is absent in mesophyll chloroplasts but is present in bundle sheath chloroplasts. The first product formed is oxaloacetic acid and hence it is known as C₄ cycle. Bundle sheath cells fix CO₂ through C₃ cycle','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Stroma of bundle sheath chloroplasts','Grana of bundle sheath chloroplasts','Grana of mesophyll chloroplasts','Stroma of mesophyll chloroplasts','NEW','11-221','11-148',NULL),
(131318,'biology','The oxygenation activity of RuBisCO enzyme in photorespiration leads to the formation of [NEET 2020]','1 molecule of 3-C compound','During photorespiration in C₃ plants, some O₂ does bind to RuBisCO and RuBP instead of being converted to 2 molecules of PGA binds with O₂ to form one molecule of phosphoglycerate (3 Carbon) and phosphoglycolate (2 Carbon).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','2 molecules of 3-C compound','1 molecule of 3-C compound','1 molecule of 6-C compound','1 molecule of 4-C compound and 1 molecule of 2-C compound','NEW','11-215','11-142',NULL),
(131319,'biology','The process which makes major difference between C₃ and C₄ plants is [NEET-II 2016]','Photorespiration','Photorespiration is the light dependent process of oxygenation of ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) and release of  CO₂  by the photosynthetic organs of a plant. It leads to oxidation of considerable amount of photosynthetic products to CO₂ and H₂O without the production of useful energy. Photorespiration occurs only in C₃ plants because at high temperature and high O₂ concentration RuBP carboxylase changes to RuBP oxygenase. Photorespiration is absent in C₄ plants. Peroxisome and mitochondria are required for completing the process.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Glycolysis','Calvin cycle','Photorespiration','Respiration','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131320,'biology','A process that makes important difference between C₃ and C₄ plants is [AIPMT 2012]','Photorespiration','Photorespiration is the light dependent process of oxygenation of ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) and release of  CO₂  by the photosynthetic organs of a plant. It leads to oxidation of considerable amount of photosynthetic products to CO₂ and H₂O without the production of useful energy. Photorespiration occurs only in C₃ plants because at high temperature and high O₂ concentration RuBP carboxylase changes to RuBP oxygenase. Photorespiration is absent in C₄ plants. Peroxisome and mitochondria are required for completing the process.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Transpiration','Glycolysis','Photosynthesis','Photorespiration','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131321,'biology','The correct sequence of cell organelles during photorespiration is [AIPMT 2012]','Chloroplast, mitochondria, peroxisome','NCERT Class 11th Pg., 220','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Chloroplast, Golgi-bodies, mitochondria','Chloroplast, rough endoplasmic reticulum, dictyosomes','Chloroplast, mitochondria, peroxisome','Chloroplast, vacuole, peroxisome.','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131322,'biology','C₄ plants are more efficient in photosynthesis than C₃ plants due to [AIPMT 2010]','Lower rate of photorespiration','Rate of net photosynthesis in C₃ plants is 15- 35 mg CO₂/dm² /hr while in C₄ plants is 40-80 mg CO₂ / dm² /hr. This variation in rate is due to photorespiration. Photorespiration is an inhibitory process which decreases the rate of photosynthesis. In excess of O₂ RuBP carboxylase converts to RuBP oxygenase. As a result glycolate synthesis is enhanced and leads to begin photorespiration. Photorespiration is negligible or absent in C₄ plants and present only in C₃ plants. So C₄ plants are photosynthetically more efficient','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Higher leaf area','Presence of larger number of chloroplasts in the leaf cells','Presence of thin cuticle','Lower rate of photorespiration','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131323,'biology','Which one of the following is wrong in relation to photorespiration? [AIPMT 2003]','It is a characteristic of C₄ plants.','Photorespiration is the light dependent process of oxygenation of ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) and release of  CO₂  by the photosynthetic organs of a plant. It leads to oxidation of considerable amount of photosynthetic products to CO₂ and H₂O without the production of useful energy. Photorespiration occurs only in C₃ plants because at high temperature and high O₂ concentration RuBP carboxylase changes to RuBP oxygenase. Photorespiration is absent in C₄ plants. Peroxisome and mitochondria are required for completing the process','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','It occurs in chloroplast','It occurs in day time only','It is a characteristic of C₄ plants.','It is a characteristic of C₃ plants.','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131324,'biology','Photorespiration is favoured by [AIPMT 1996]','High O₂ and low CO₂','Photorespiration is the light dependent process of oxygenation of ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) and release of  CO₂  by the photosynthetic organs of a plant. It leads to oxidation of considerable amount of photosynthetic products to CO₂ and H₂O without the production of useful energy. Photorespiration occurs only in C₃ plants because at high temperature and high O₂ concentration RuBP carboxylase changes to RuBP oxygenase. Photorespiration is absent in C₄ plants. Peroxisome and mitochondria are required for completing the process','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','High temperature and low O₂','High humidity and temperature','High O₂ and low CO₂','High CO₂ and low O₂','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131325,'biology','The substrate for photorespiration is [AIPMT 1989]','Glycolate','In photorespiration glycolate is formed','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Phosphoglyceric acid','Glycolate','Serine','Glycine','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131326,'biology','With Reference   to factors affecting the rate of photosynthesis, which of the following statements is not correct? [NEET 2017]','C₃ plants respond to higher temperature with enhanced photosynthesis while C₄ plants have much lower temperature optimum','C₄ plants respond to higher temperature with enhanced photosynthesis while C₃ plants have lower temperature optimum.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Increasing atmospheric CO₂ concentration up to 0.05% can enhance CO₂ fixation rate','C₃ plants respond to higher temperature with enhanced photosynthesis while C₄ plants have much lower temperature optimum','Tomato is a greenhouse crop which can be grown in CO₂-enriched atmosphere for higher yield','Light saturation for CO₂ fixation occurs at 10% of full sunlight','NEW','11-223','11-150',NULL),
(131327,'biology','Plants adapted to low light intensity have [AIPMT 2004]','Larger photosynthetic unit size than the sun plants','To absorb more sunlight (quantitatively), the plants growing in low light conditions have larger photosynthetic unit size. It means that they have more number of chlorophyll molecules per reaction center. to trap more light energy available to them.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Larger photosynthetic unit size than the sun plants','Higher rate of CO₂fixation than the sun plants','More extended root system','Leaves modified to spines.','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131328,'biology','The rate of photosynthesis is higher in [AIPMT 1999]','Red light','Plants can use a small portion of light which falls upon them. Chlorophyll-a and chlorophyll-b absorb too much light in the blue and red region.Carotenoids absorb light mostly in the blue region of spectrum of light. In monochromatic lights, maximum photosynthesis occurs in red light, followed by blue light and poor photosynthesis in green light. Under very high light intensity solarisation phenomenon occurs. It involves photooxidation of different cellular components including chlorophyll','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Very high light','Continuous light','Red light','Green light.','NEW','11-210','11-137',NULL),
(131329,'biology','‘The law of limiting factors’ was proposed by [AIPMT 1996]','Blackman','Blackman (1905) gave the law of limiting factors which states that when a process is conditioned as to its rapidity by a number of separate factors, the rate of the process is limited by the pace of the slowest process. It is the factor which is present in minimum amount.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Liebig','Hatch and Slack','Blackman','Arnon','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131330,'biology','Which of the following is not a product of light reaction of photosynthesis? [NEET 2018]','NADH','In light reaction of photosynthesis, ATP, NADPH and O₂ are produced. NADPH participates in anabolic reactions (e.g., photosynthesis) that consume energy in order to build up larger molecules. NADH participates in catabolic reactions (e.g., respiration) that break down molecules to release energy','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','ATP','NADH','NADPH','Oxygen','NEW','11-215','11-142',NULL),
(131331,'biology','In light reaction, plastoquinone facilitates the transfer of electrons from [NEET 2020]','PSII to Cyt B6f complex','After excitement, e– is passed from PS-II(P680) to primary electron acceptor (Pheophytin). From primary e– acceptor, e– is passed to plastoquinone. Plastoquinone (PQ) in turn transfer its e– to Cyt b6f complex. Therefore plastoquinone facilitates the transfer of electrons from PSII to Cyt b6f complex','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','PSII to Cyt B6f complex','Cyt B6f complex to PSI','PS-I to NADP+','PS-I to ATP synthase','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(131332,'biology','The oxygenation activity of RuBisCO enzyme in photorespiration leads to the formation of [NEET 2020]','1 molecule of 3-C compound','During photorespiration in C3 plants, some O₂ does bind to RuBisCO and RuBP instead of being converted to 2 molecules of PGA binds with O₂ to form one molecule of phosphoglycerate (3 Carbon) and phosphoglycolate (2 Carbon).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','2 molecules of 3-C compound','1 molecule of 3-C compound','1 molecule of 6-C compound','1 molecule of 4-C compound and 1 molecule of 2-C compound','NEW','11-215','11-142',NULL),
(131333,'biology','The first stable product of CO₂  fixation in sorghum is[NEET 2021]','Oxaloacetic acid','In C4 plants, initial fixation of  CO₂  occurs in mesophyll cells. The primary acceptor of CO₂  i.e., phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) combines with CO₂  in the presence of PEP carboxylase or PEPcase to form oxaloacetic acid, which is the first stable  CO₂  fixation product in C4 pathway','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Phosphoglyceric acid','Pyruvic acid','Oxaloacetic acid','Succinic acid','NEW','11-216','11-143',NULL),
(131334,'biology','Which of the following statements is incorrect? [NEET 2021]','Cyclic photophosphorylation involves both PS I and PS II','NCERT Class 11th, Pg., 213 in first and second paragraph.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Cyclic photophosphorylation involves both PS I and PS II','Both ATP and NADPH + H⁺ are synthesized during non-cyclic photophosphorylation','Stroma lamellae have PS I only and lack NADP reductase','Grana lamellae have both PS I and PS II','NEW','11-213','11-140',NULL),
(131335,'biology','Given below are two statements : \n\nStatement I : The primary CO₂  acceptor in C4 plants is phosphoenolpyruvate and is found in the mesophyll cells. \n\nStatement II : Mesophyll cells of C4 plants lack RuBisCo enzyme. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:\n\n[NEET 2022]','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','The primary CO₂  acceptor is a 3-carbon molecule phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) and is present in the mesophyll cells. The enzyme\nresponsible for this fixation is PEP carboxylase or PEPcase. It is important to register that the mesophyll cells lack RuBisCO enzyme. The C4 acid OAA is formed in the mesophyll cells.\nMesophyll cells of C4 plants lack RuBisCO enzyme.\n\n(NCERT 11th, chapter 13, page 219, paragraph no. 2)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131336,'biology','Which one of the following is not true regarding the release of energy during ATP synthesis through chemiosmosis? It involves:\n\n[NEET 2022]','Breakdown of electron gradient','Chemiosmosis requires a membrane, a proton pump, a proton gradient and ATP synthase. Energy is used to pump protons across a membrane to create a gradient or a high concentration of protons within the thylakoid lumen. The NADP reductase enzyme is located on the stroma side of the membrane. Along with the electrons that come from the acceptor of electrons of PS I, protons are necessary for reduction of NADP+ to NADPH + H⁺ . The process does not involve breaking of electron gradient.\n\n(NCERT 11th, chapter 13, page 214)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Breakdown of proton gradient','Breakdown of electron gradient','Movement of protons across the membrane to the stroma','Reduction of NADP to NADPH₂ on the stroma side of the membrane','NEW','11-214','11-141',NULL),
(131337,'biology','What is the role of large bundle sheath cells found around the vascular bundles in C4 plants?\n\n[NEET 2022]','To increase the number of chloroplast for the operation of Calvin cycle','The large cells around the vascular bundles of C4 plants form bundle sheath. These cells have large number of chloroplasts to perform calvin cycle.\n\n(NCERT 11th, chapter 13, page 218, paragraph no. 2)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','To provide the site for photorespiratory pathway','To increase the number of chloroplast for the operation of Calvin cycle','To enable the plant to tolerate high temperature','To protect the vascular tissue from high light intensity','NEW','11-218','11-145',NULL),
(131338,'biology','When one CO2 molecule is fixed as one molecule of\ntriose phosphate, which of the following\nphotochemically made, high energy chemical\nintermediates are used in the reduction phase ? [ RE NEET 2022]','(4) 2 ATP + 2 NADPH','2 ATPs and 2NADPH are used to fix one molecule of CO2 into one molecule of triose phosphate.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) 1 ATP + 1 NADPH','(2) 1 ATP + 2 NADPH','(3) 2 ATP + 1 NADPH','(4) 2 ATP + 2 NADPH','NEW','11-217','11-144',NULL),
(131339,'biology','Which out of the following statements is incorrect ? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) Cyclic photophosphorylation involves both PS I \nand PS II','In cyclic photophosphorylation, only PS-I is involved. The electrons released from PS-I are cycled back to the same photosystem. Hence, the source of electrons is not water in cyclic photophosphorylation. ATP is synthesised during cyclic photophosphorylation but NADPH + H+ is not synthesised.','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Grana lamellae have both PS I and PS II','(2) Cyclic photophosphorylation involves both PS I \nand PS II','(3) Both ATP and NADPH + H+\n are synthesised during non-cyclic photophosphorylation.','(4) Stroma lamellae lack PS II and NADP reductase','NEW','11-213','11-140',NULL),
(131340,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \nList-I List-II\n(A) Chlorophyll a (I) Yellow to yellow orange\n(B) Chlorophyll b (II) Yellow green\n(C) Xanthophyll (III) Blue green\n(D) Carotenoid (IV) Yellow\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(1) (A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(2) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)','(3) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)','(1) (A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)','(4) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','NEW','11-210','11-137',NULL),
(131401,'chemistry','Assertion: Hydrocarbons play a key role in providing energy through fuels such as LPG, CNG, petrol, diesel, and kerosene oil\n\nReason: Hydrocarbons are used in manufacture of polymers, dyes, and drugs.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Hydrocarbons are important compounds in our daily life and play a key role in providing energy through fuels such as LPG, CNG, petrol, diesel, and kerosene oil. Hydrocarbons are also used for the manufacture of polymers, dyes, and drugs.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(131402,'chemistry','Assertion: LPG is a cleaner domestic fuel compared to kerosene oil.\n\nReason: LPG causes less pollution compared to kerosene oil when used as a domestic fuel.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','LPG is used as a domestic fuel with the least pollution, while kerosene oil is also used as a domestic fuel but causes some pollution.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(131403,'chemistry','Assertion: Petrol and CNG operated automobiles cause less pollution.\n\nReason: Petrol and CNG are fuels that contain hydrocarbons, which are sources of energy','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Automobiles need fuels like petrol,\ndiesel and CNG. Petrol and CNG operated automobiles cause less pollution. All these fuels contain mixture of hydrocarbons, which are sources of energy','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(131404,'chemistry','Assertion: Natural gas is found in the upper strata during drilling of oil wells.\n\nReason: Compressed natural gas is obtained by compressing Coal gas','Assertion is true but reason is false','Coal gas is obtained by the destructive distillation of coal.\nNatural gas is found in upper strata during drilling of oil wells. The gas after compression is known as compressed natural gas. \n\nLPG is used as a domestic fuel with the least pollution. Kerosene oil is also used as a domestic fuel but it causes some pollution.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(131405,'chemistry','Assertion: Hydrocarbons are the starting materials for the manufacture of many dyes and drugs.\n\nReason: Hydrocarbons are used as solvents for paints and are also sources of energy.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Hydrocarbons are used as starting materials for the manufacture of many dyes and drugs, and are also used as solvents for paints and sources of energy','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(131406,'chemistry','Assertion: Coal gas is obtained by the destructive distillation of coal.\n\nReason: Coal gas is found in upper strata during drilling of oil wells.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Coal gas is obtained by the destructive distillation of coal.\nNatural gas is found in upper strata during drilling of oil wells. The gas after compression is known as compressed natural gas. \n\nLPG is used as a domestic fuel with the least pollution. Kerosene oil is also used as a domestic fuel but it causes some pollution.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-373','11-295',NULL),
(110806,'chemistry','In which oxidation state compounds are more ionic','1+','More oxiadtion No. more will be the electronegativity so electronegativity\n difference will be smaller, 3+ state is highly electronegative than 2+ which\n is more tham 1+, more electronegative difference between bonded atoms\n more will be the ionic character.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.1)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','3+','0','1+','2+','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(131407,'chemistry','Assertion: Alkanes can be prepared from saturated hydrocarbons by hydrogenation using catalysts such as platinum, palladium, or nickel.\n\nReason: Platinum and palladium catalyze the halogenation reaction at room temperature','Both Assertion and reason are false','Correct assertion and reason statement is : Assertion: Alkanes can be prepared from unsaturated hydrocarbons by hydrogenation using catalysts such as platinum, palladium, or nickel.\n\nReason: Platinum and palladium catalyze the hydrogenation reaction at room temperature, whereas relatively higher temperature and pressure are required with nickel catalysts','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(131408,'chemistry','Assertion: Alkanes are almost non-polar molecules due to the covalent nature of C-C and C-H bonds and very little difference of electronegativity between carbon and hydrogen atoms.\n\nReason: Alkanes possess weak van der Waals forces, which results in low boiling points for smaller molecules','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The covalent nature of C-C and C-H bonds and the little difference in electronegativity between carbon and hydrogen atoms make alkanes almost non-polar molecules. They possess weak van der Waals forces, which result in low boiling points for smaller molecules and high boiling points for larger molecules due to the increase in intermolecular van der Waals forces with an increase in molecular mass.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(131409,'chemistry','Assertion: Halogenation of alkanes is an example of a chain reaction. \n\nReason: The free radicals produced in the first two steps combine in pairs to form a molecule.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Halogenation of alkanes is an example of a chain reaction is true because the reaction occurs in three steps - initiation, propagation, and termination. The reason that the free radicals produced in the first two steps combine in pairs to form a molecule is also true and is the correct explanation of the assertion. \n\nThe propagation steps keep the reaction going until all the reactants are consumed, and the termination steps are those in which the free radicals combine to form a molecule which cannot generate any other free radical. \n\nThus, the halogenation of alkanes is a chain reaction, where the reaction intermediates in the first two steps regenerate the reactant','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-395','11-317',NULL),
(131410,'chemistry','Assertion: One or more hydrogen atoms of alkanes can be replaced by halogens, nitro group and sulphonic acid group.\n\nReason: These reactions in which hydrogen atoms of alkanes are substituted are known as addition reactions.','Assertion is true but reason is false','One or more hydrogen atoms of alkanes can be replaced by halogens, nitro group and sulphonic acid group. This is because alkanes undergo substitution reactions in which hydrogen atoms are replaced by these groups.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> Assertion Reason Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but reason is false','Both Assertion and reason are false','NEW','11-400','11-322',NULL),
(131501,'chemistry','the isobutyl group among the following is - Diagram(131501Q)','4','"Hint: IUPAC name of the group is 2-methylpropyl.\n\nExplanation: \n\nOption 1 is sec-butyl\n\n\nOption 2 is n-butyl\n\n\nOption 3 is tert-butyl\n\n\nOption 4 is the iso-butyl or isomeric butyl group.\n"','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-376','11-298','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131501q.png'),
(131502,'chemistry','The compound C7H8 undergoes the following reaction. the product C is - diagram(131502Q)','m-Bromotoluene','Hint: First reaction is a free radical substitution reaction\n\nThe first reaction is an example of a free radical substitution reaction. The Cl2 is taken in excess thus all the hydrogen from CH3 group is replaced by Cl atom. In the second step, an electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction takes place.\n\nThe CCl3 is an electron-withdrawing group and meta directing. Thus, bromination occurs at the meta position. In the first step, reduction takes place. \n\nThe overall reaction is as follows:','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','m-Bromotoluene','o-Bromotoluene','3-Bromo- 2,4,6-trichlorotoluene','p-Bromotoluene','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131502q.png'),
(131503,'chemistry','given the following compounds a) heptane b) Butane c) 2- methylbutane d)2-methylpropane e) hexane .the correct option regarding decreasing boiling points of these compounds is :','a>e>c>b>d','Hint: As branching increases the boiling point decreases. \n\nStep 1: \n\nThe boiling point of alkane depends on the intermolecular force of attraction between the molecules. As the number of carbon atoms increases, the surface area of the molecule increases, and because of it Van der Waal\'s force of attraction increases.  \n\nThe force of attraction also depends on the branching. As branching increases, the surface area also decreases and the force of attraction decreases. \n\nStep 2: \n\nThus, decreasing the order of boiling points of the following alkanes is as follows:\n\nHeptane > Hexane > 2-methylbutane > butane > 2-methylpropane\n\nThe boiling point of a given compound is as follows: \n\nHeptane = 371.4 K\nHexane =  341.9 K\n2-methylbutane = 300.9 K\n2-methylpropane = 261 K \nButane = 272.4 K\n\nHence, option 4th is the correct answer.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a>c>e>d>b','c>d>a>e>b','a>e>b>c>d','a>e>c>b>d','NEW','11-380','11-302',NULL),
(131504,'chemistry','the molecular formula of diphenylmethane is C3H12 . the no. of structural isomers possible when one of the hydrogen atoms is replaced by chlorine atom :','4','HINT: 4 structural isomers are possible.\n\nExplanation: \n\nA structural isomer of a compound is another compound whose molecule has the same number of atoms of each element, but with logically distinct bonds between them. \n\nThe molecular formula of diphenyl methane shows four isomers in form of mono chloro derivatives.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','4','8','7','6','NEW','11-386','11-308',NULL),
(131505,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is correct about eliminantion reaction of 2-bromopentane to form pent-2-ene? a)B-elemination reaction b)follows zaitsev rule c)dehydrohalogenation reaction  d)dehydration reaction','a,b,c','Hint: The reaction is an example of an elimination reaction\n\nThe reaction is as follows:  - diagram (131505E) It is ß-elimination, follows Zaitsev rule because more substituted alkene is obtained as a product. In this reaction, hydrogen and Br are removed simultaneously. Hence it is also known as dehydrohalogenation.\n\ndiagram(131505E-2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,c,d','b,c,d','a,b,d','a,b,c','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(131506,'chemistry','compound A on reaction with compound B reacts with alc.KOH to give gas C which decolorizes baeyer reagent.Ozonolysis of compound C gives only HCHO compound.the compound A is:','C2H6','Hint: Compound C is an alkene.\n\nThe first reaction is an example of a free radical substitution reaction. The second reaction is an example of an elimination reaction. The compound C must be an alkene because an alkene decolourizes baeyer reagent and give an ozonolysis reaction.\n\nThe reactions are as follows: (diagram131506E)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','C2H6','C2H4','C4H10','C2H5Cl','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(131507,'chemistry','compound diagram(131507Q) on reaction with NaIO4 in the presence of KMnO4 gives:','CH3COCH3+CH3COOH','HINT: Oxidative cleavage of olefins.\n\nExplanation: \n\nOxidative cleavage of olefins with NaIO4 and catalytic KMnO4 yielding a mixture of ketone and carboxylic acid.\n\nGeneral mechanism of this reaction is  : - diagram(131507E) For the given reactant the mechanism is : diagram(131507E-2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH3COCH3','CH3COCH3+CH3COOH','CH3COCH3 + CH3CHO','CH3CHO+CO2','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131507q.png'),
(131508,'chemistry','diagram(131508Q) this compound is obtained by ozonlysis . the starting cyclic compound is which of the following : diagram(131508Q-2)','1','Hint: The starting cyclic compound name is 1,4-dimethylcyclopent-1-ene.\n\nIt is an oxidation reaction, in which O3 reacts with alkene and convert it into aldehyde or ketone or carboxylic acid.\n\nTo obtain the product from ozonolysis, just cleave the double bond and add =O.\n\nThe ozonolysis product of the given reactant is as follows: diagram(131508E)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4',NULL,'11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131508q.png'),
(131509,'chemistry','the molecule among the following that has hybridization sp2,sp2,sp and sp from left to right is : - diagram(131509Q)','2','Hint: Triple bond carbon is sp and double bond carbon is sp2 \n\nFrom left to right, the first and second carbon atoms are double bond carbon atoms hence hybridization is sp2 ,\n\nand the third and fourth carbon atom is triple bond carbon atoms, hence, the hybridization is sp.\n\nThe correct structure is as follows:\n\nCH\n2\nsp\n2\n \n=\nCH\nsp\n2\n \n−\nC\nsp\n \n≡\nC\nsp\n \n−\nH','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-384','11-306','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131509q.png'),
(131510,'chemistry','the compound obtained by addition of water to an alkyne having more than two carbons , in the presence of HgSO4 and dilute H2SO4 at 333K,is :','a ketone','Hint: Only acetylene gives ethanal and other alkynes give ketone.\n\nExplanation:\n\nLike alkanes and alkenes, alkynes are also immiscible and do not react with water. However, one molecule of water adds to alkynes on warming with mercuric sulphate and dilute sulphuric acid at 333 K to form carbonyl compounds.\n\nThe reaction is as follows:  diagram(131510E) diagram(131510E-2)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a vicinal diol','an aldehyde','an alcohol','a ketone','NEW','11-392','11-314',NULL),
(131511,'chemistry','which compound amongst the following is not an aromatic compound? Refer diagram(n13121)',NULL,NULL,'Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'11-396','11-318','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131511q.png'),
(131512,'chemistry','consider the following compound /species - digram(131512Q) the no.of compounds/species which obey HUckel\'s rule are:','4','Hint: Huckel\'s rule 4n + 2 = 𝝅 electron.\n\nIn organic chemistry, Hückel\'s rule predicts that a planar ring molecule will have aromatic properties if it has 4n + 2 π electrons where n is a n=0 or any positive integer. \n\n1. \nIt has 10 π electrons. \n\n4n + 2 = 10\n4n = 8\nn = 2\nIt follows Huckel\'s rule.\n2. \nIt has 6 π electrons. \n4n + 2 = 6\n4n = 4\nn = 1\nIt follows Huckel\'s rule.\n3. \nIt has 4 π electrons. \n4n + 2 = 4\n4n = 2\nn = 1/2\nDoesn\'t follow Huckel\'s rule.\n\n4. \nIt has 4 π electrons. \n4n + 2 = 4\n4n = 2\nn = 1/2\nDoesn\'t follow Huckel\'s rule.\n\n5. \nIt has 4 π electrons. \n4n + 2 = 4\n\nDoesn\'t follow Huckel\'s rule.\n\n6. \nIt has 8 π electrons. \n4n + 2 = 8\n4n = 6\nn = 3/2\nDoesn\'t follow Huckel\'s rule.\n\n7. \n\nIt has 14 π electrons. \n4n + 2 = 14\n4n = 12\nn = 3\nIt follows Huckel\'s rule.\n\nHence, 4 compounds follow Huckel\'s rule.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','5','4','6','2','NEW','11-399','11-321','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131512q.png'),
(131513,'chemistry','When 2-Bromopentane reacts with Ethanolic-KOH , the main product that is formed:','Trans-2-Pentene','When 2-bromopentane reacts with KOH then an elimination reaction occurs and an alkene is obtained as a product. In this reaction, Saytzeff\'s rule is followed and trans alkene is obtained as a product.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cis-2-Pentene','Trans-2-Pentene','1-Pentene','None of the above','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(131514,'chemistry','Refer to Diagram(131514Q)','1','Step 1:The first reaction is an elimination reaction and then an electrophilic addition reaction takes place.The reaction occurs with the rearrangement of the carbocation.In the first reaction, alcohol is converted into an alkene.Step 2:In the second step, the major product reacts with HBr and give an electrophilic addition reaction.In this reaction, an alkene is converted into alkyl bromide.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-388','11-310',NULL),
(131515,'chemistry','Refer to Diagram(131515Q)','1','Rearrangement of carbocation takes place. The reaction is an example of an electrophilic addition reaction. In this reaction, rearrangement of carbocation takes place.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-402','11-324',NULL),
(131516,'chemistry','Refer to Diagram(131516Q)','1','The starting cyclic compound name is 1,4-dimethylcyclopent-1-ene. It is an oxidation reaction, in which O3 reacts with alkene and convert it into aldehyde or ketone or carboxylic acid. To obtain the product from ozonolysis, just cleave the double bond and add =O.Refer to Diagram(131516E)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(110807,'chemistry','Which one of these is related to BF3','all of these','B have 3 electrons to make bond with others, by making three bonds it \n have 6 electrons in its last shell (three from itself and three from other )\n but it needs total 8 electrons for its last shell ,its last shell have two sub-\n shell(s,p) it needs 2 e for s and 6 e for p, by making 3 bonds it only gets 6\n that\'s why it want to two more to complete its octet,so it is e deficient.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','electron deficient','incomplete octet','lewis acid','all of these','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(131517,'chemistry','An Alkene "A" on reaction with O3 and Zn-H2O  gives propanone and ethanal in equimolar ratio.The addition of HCl to Alkene "A" gives "B" as major product.The structure of product "B" is:Refer to Diagram(131517Q)','4','Hint: Zn prevents the further oxidation of carbonyl compounds to carboxylic acids. Explanation: (1) Ozonolysis is a type of weak oxidative cleavage where we cleave alkenes (double bonds) into either ketones, aldehydes or carboxylic acid using ozone. Ozonolysis is always followed by zinc dust to prevent further oxidation of the compound formed. Zinc prevents the compound from further making more bonds and hence stops the reaction process. (2) The addition of HCl to 2-methyl-2-butene forms 2-chloro-2-methylbutane as the major product. The reaction follows electrophilic addition and Markovnikov\'s rule.Refer to Diagram(131517E)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(131518,'chemistry','Refer to diagram(131518Q)','1','HINT: Alkene on ozonolysis gives aldehydes/ ketones depending on the structure of the alkene. Explanation: Ozonolysis is the best method for locating the double bond position in unknown alkene. An alkene is treated with ozone in an inert solvent to form an ozonide. The ozonide is treated with Zn/H2O to obtain the carbonyl compounds.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131518q.png'),
(131519,'chemistry','Compound X on reaction with O₃ followed by Zn/H₂O gives formaldehyde and 2-methylpropanal as products.\nThe compound X is?','3-MethylBut-1-ene','Hint: Compound must contains a terminal double bond When alkene reacts with O 3 followed by Z n / H 2 O then ozonolysis reaction occurs and aldehyde or ketone is obtained as a product.Refer to DIagram(131519E)','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Pent-2-ene','3-MethylBut-1-ene','2-MethylBut-1-ene','2-MethylBut-2-ene','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(131520,'chemistry','In the Given Hydrocarbon in the diagram(131520Q).The state of Carbons 1,3 and 5 are:','sp,sp3,sp2','Hint: Double bond carbon atom is sp2, triple bond carbon is sp and single bond carbon is sp3. The first carbon forms a triple bond hence it is sp hybridized. The third carbon forms a single bond hence it hybridization is sp3, and the fourth carbon forms a double bond hence its hybridization is sp2. Therefore, the state of hybridisation of carbons 1, 3 and 5 are sp, sp3 and sp2 respectively.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','sp2.sp.sp3','sp,sp3,sp2','sp,sp2,sp3','sp3,sp2,sp','NEW','11-384','11-306','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131520q.png'),
(131521,'chemistry','The Correct Order Of Acidity is- Refer to Diagram(131521Q)','1','Hint: Electronegativity order : sp carbon > sp 2 carbon > sp 3 carbon Since s orbital is closest to the nucleus, thus electron which is present in orbitals with more s character will be more attracted by the nucleus, and as a result electronegativity of sp is greater than the other two. Therefore sp is more acidic followed by sp2 and sp3. Acidic character order: sp > sp 2 > sp 3 The conjugate base of an alkyne is more stable than the conjugate base of alkene and alkane. In alkyne, a negative charge is formed in sp carbon, an alkene, it is formed in sp 2 carbon , and in alkane, it is formed in sp 3 carbon . The sp carbon is highly electronegative hence alkyne is highly acidic followed by alkene and than alkane.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-384','11-306',NULL),
(131522,'chemistry','Refer to Diagram(131522Q)','1','Hint: Benzylic carbocation is highly stable It is an example of electrophilic addition reaction where proton addition takes place across the double bond resulting in more stable carbocation formation and subsequent addition of nucleophile to form addition product','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-402','11-324',NULL),
(131523,'chemistry','When Phenyl Acteylene react with dilute H2SO4 in the presence of HgSO4,It gives Refer to diagram(131523Q)','1','HINT: Oxymercuration - demercuration reaction followed by keto- enol tautomerism. Explanation: Phenyl acetylene reacts with dil. H 2 S O 4 in presence of H g S O 4 gives acetophenone. In the first step , Oxymercuration - demercuration reaction occur with  dil.  H2SO4 in presence of HgSO4 which results in the formation of enol . Keto form is more stable here than enol form . So, keto - enol tautomerism takes place resulting in formation of acetophenone.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-391','11-313','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131523q.png'),
(131524,'chemistry','Refer to Diagram(131524Q):','4','Hint: The compound P is cumene','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-391','11-313',NULL),
(131525,'chemistry','The increasing order of electrophilic substitution for the following compounds is:Refer to Diagram(131525Q)','1','Hint: The group increases the electron density in benzene ring, increases it reactivity towards electrophilic substitution. The electron donating group increases the reactivity of benzene ring towards electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction and electron withdrawing group decreases the reactivity of benzene ring electrophilic substitution. The OCH3 is the electron donating group, CH3 also an electron donating group but CF3 is an electron withdrawing group.  OCH3 donate electron via resonance , methyl group donate electron via hyperconjugaton and CF3 withdraw electron via -I effect. Resonance is more powerful effect than hyperconjugation. Hence, anisole is more reactive than toluene. The correct order is : IV<I<II<III','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-398','11-320','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131525q.png'),
(131526,'chemistry','The Reaction among the following in the diagram(131526Q) that proceeds through an electrophilic substituion reaction is:','3','Hint: Chlorination in a benzene ring is an example of an electrophilic substitution reaction. When the benzene ring reacts with Cl2 in the presence of AlCl3 then hydrogen from the benzene ring is substituted by the Cl atom. The reaction follows an electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction.','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-400','11-322','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/131526q.png'),
(131527,'chemistry','Refer to Diagram(131527Q):','1','Hint: Free radical addition reaction followed by substitution reaction','Organic chemistry >> Hydrocarbon >> New PYQ Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-402','11-324',NULL),
(241809,'biology','Assertion: The hard outer layer of a pollen grain is called the exine is made up of sporopollenin.\n\nReason: Pollen grains are generally in spherical shape.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Pollen grains are generally spherical measuring about 25-50 micrometers in diameter. The hard outer layer called the exine is made up of sporopollenin because it is one of the most resistant organic material known. It can withstand high temperatures and strong acids and alkali. No enzyme that degrades sporopollenin is so far known. (Reference  : NCERT, page 23, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-023',NULL,NULL),
(131528,'biology','Assertion: The Hatch and Slack Pathway is a cyclic process.\n\nReason: The 3-carbon molecule that is a product of subsequent steps, is transported back to the mesophyll where it is converted to PEP again, thus, completing the cycle.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','The primary  CO₂  acceptor is a 3-carbon molecule phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) and is present in the mesophyll cells. In the bundle sheath cells, C4 acids are broken down to release  CO₂  and a 3-carbon molecule. The 3-carbon molecule is transported back to the mesophyll where it is converted to PEP again, thus, completing the cycle. (Reference  : NCERT, page 219, paragraph 2 and 3).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131529,'biology','Assertion: The basic pathway that results in the formation of the sugars, the Calvin pathway, is common to the C3 and C4 plants.\n\nReason: The  CO₂  released in the bundle sheath cells enters the C3 or the Calvin pathway, a pathway common to all plants.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Calvin pathway occurs in all photosynthetic plants; it does not matter whether they have C3 or C4 (or any other) pathways. (correct reason of assertion)\nThe  CO₂  released in the bundle sheath cells enters the C3 or the Calvin pathway, a pathway common to all plants. The bundle sheath cells are rich in an enzyme Ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase (RuBisCO), but lack PEPcase. Thus, the basic pathway that results in the formation of the sugars, the Calvin pathway, is common to the C3 and C4 plants. (Reference  : NCERT, page 220, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131530,'biology','Assertion: In C3 plants some O₂ binds to RuBisCO, and hence  CO₂  fixation is decreased.\n\nReason: Some plants require the absorption of O₂ instead of  CO₂  for photosynthesis.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','It is the relative concentration of O₂ and  CO₂  that determines which of the two will bind to the enzyme. In C3 plants some O₂ does bind to RuBisCO, and hence  CO₂  fixation is decreased. Here the RuBP instead of being converted to 2 molecules of PGA binds with O₂ to form one molecule of phosphoglycerate and phosphoglycolate (2 Carbon) in a pathway called photorespiration. (Reference  : NCERT, page 220, paragraph 5).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131531,'biology','Assertion: In C4 plants photorespiration does not occur.\n\nReason: They have a mechanism that increases the concentration of Carbon dioxie at the enzyme site.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','In C4 plants photorespiration does not occur. This is because they have a mechanism that increases the concentration of  CO₂  at the enzyme site. This takes place when the C4 acid from the mesophyll is broken down in the bundle sheath cells to release  CO₂ – this results in increasing the intracellular concentration of  CO₂ . In turn, this ensures that the RuBisCO functions as a carboxylase minimising the oxygenase activity. (Reference  : NCERT, page 220, paragraph 6).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131532,'biology','Assertion: The Photorespiratory pathway results in the synthesis of sugars and ATP.\n\nReason:  CO₂  binding is increased in photorespiration.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','In C3 plants some O₂ does bind to RuBisCO, and hence CO₂  fixation is decreased. In the photorespiratory pathway, there is neither synthesis of sugars, nor of ATP. Rather it results in the release of  CO₂  with the utilisation of ATP (Reference  : NCERT, page 220, paragraph 5).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131533,'biology','Assertion: Despite the presence of a green leaf and optimal light and adequate CO₂  conditions, the plant may not photosynthesise if the temperature is very low.\n\nReason: The plant is heat sensitive and its growth is determined by the temperature mainly','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','If a chemical process is affected by more than one factor, then its rate will be determined by the factor which is nearest to its minimal value: it is the factor which directly affects the process if its quantity is changed (Law of Limiting Factors) (Reference  : NCERT, page 222, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-221','11-148',NULL),
(131534,'biology','Assertion: Light is a strong limiting factor in nature.\n\nReason: There is a linear relationship between incident light and growth rates.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','There is a linear relationship between incident light and  CO₂  fixation rates at low light intensities. At higher light intensities, gradually the rate does not show further increase as other factors become limiting. What is interesting to note is that light saturation occurs at 10 % of the full sunlight. Hence, except for plants in shade or in dense forests, light is rarely a limiting factor in nature (Reference  : NCERT, page 222, paragraph 5).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131535,'biology','Assertion: Current availability of  CO₂  levels is limiting to the C3 plants.\n\nReason:  CO₂  is the major limiting factor for photosynthesis.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','CO₂  is the major limiting factor for photosynthesis. The concentration of CO₂  is very low in the atmosphere (between 0.03 and 0.04 %). Increase in concentration upto 0.05 % can cause a\nincrease in CO₂  fixation rates; beyond this the levels can become damaging over longer periods.The C4 plants show saturation at about 360 μlL-1 while C3 responds to increased  CO₂  concentration and saturation is seen only beyond 450 μlL-1.Thus, current\navailability of CO₂  levels is limiting to the C3 plants (Reference  : NCERT, Page 223, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-223','11-150',NULL),
(131536,'biology','Assertion: Tropical plants have a higher temperature optimum than the plants adapted to temperate climates.\n\nReason: The temperature optimum for photosynthesis of different plants also depends on the habitat that they are adapted to.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','The temperature optimum for photosynthesis of different plants also depends on the habitat that they are adapted to. Tropical plants have a higher temperature optimum than the plants adapted to temperate climates. (Reference  : NCERT, page 223, paragraph 5).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-223','11-150',NULL),
(131537,'biology','Assertion: Water stress can enhance the  CO₂  availability.\n\nReason: Stomata open wider to capture a greater amount of  CO₂  when the plant is under water stress.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Water stress causes the stomata to close hence reducing the  CO₂  availability and thus lesser photosynthesis. (Reference  : NCERT, page 223, paragraph 6).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-223','11-150',NULL),
(131538,'biology','Assertion: Within the leaves, the mesophyll cells have a large number of chloroplasts.\n\nReason: Chloroplasts are responsible for  CO₂  fixation.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Within the leaves, the mesophyll cells have a large number of chloroplasts that are responsible for  CO₂  fixation , Leaves are the site of photosynthesis , that is why leaves contain mesophyl cells that contain large number of chloroplasts.(Reference  : NCERT, page 224, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-224','11-151',NULL),
(131539,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Photosynthesis in Higher Plants and choose the incorrect option.','Heterotrophs do not depend on sunlight energy even indirectly','The green plants make or rather synthesize the food they need through photosynthesis and are therefore called autotrophs. The autotrophic nutrition is found only in plants and all other organisms that depend on the green plants for food are heterotrophs. Green plants carry out ‘photosynthesis’, a physico-chemical process by which they use light energy to drive the synthesis of organic compounds. Ultimately, all living forms on earth depend on sunlight for energy. The use of energy from sunlight by plants doing photosynthesis is the basis of life on earth. (Reference  : NCERT, page 206, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Heterotrophs do not depend on sunlight energy even indirectly','Light energy is necessary for performing photosynthesis.','Both A and B are incorrect','Light energy is used to drive the production of organic compounds in photosynthesis.','NEW','11-206','11-133',NULL),
(131540,'biology','Consider the following statements about the photosynthesis in higher plants.\ni. Photosynthesis is the primary source of all food on earth.\n\nii. Photosynthesis is responsible for the release of O₂ into the atmosphere by green plants.\n\niii. Photosynthesis is responsible for the capturing of O₂ from the atmosphere by green plants.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Only i and ii are correct','Photosynthesis is important due to two reasons: it is the primary source of all food on earth. It is also responsible for the release of O₂ into the atmosphere by green plants.Photosynthesis is responsible for the capturing of CO₂  from the atmosphere by green plants. (Reference  : NCERT, page 206, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','11-206','11-133',NULL),
(131541,'biology','Consider the experiment where a part of a leaf is enclosed in a test tube containing some KOH soaked cotton, while the other half is exposed to air. The setup is then placed in light for some time. The result is that the exposed part of the leaf tested positive for starch while the portion that was in the tube, tested negative. Choose the correct option.','KOH absorbs all the  CO₂  and none of it is available for photosynthesis.','In an experiment where a part of a leaf is enclosed in a test tube containing some KOH soaked cotton (which absorbs  CO₂ ), while the other half is exposed to air. The setup is then placed in light for some time. On testing for the presence of starch later in the two parts of the leaf, you must have found that the exposed part of the leaf tested positive for starch while the portion that was in the tube, tested negative. This showed that  CO₂  was required for photosynthesis. (Reference  : NCERT, page 207, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','KOH does not let light be captured in the tube portion of the leaf.','KOH absorbs all the  CO₂  and none of it is available for photosynthesis.','Both A and B are incorrect','Light does not enter the tube portion properly.','NEW','11-207','11-134',NULL),
(131542,'biology','Consider the following statements about the early experiments regarding photosynthesis.\ni.Priestley hypothesised that plants restore to the air whatever breathing animals and burning candles remove.\n\nii.Priestly discovered oxygen.\n\niii.Jan Ingenhousz discovered oxygen.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Only i and ii are correct','Priestley, you may recall, discovered O₂ in 1774. He concluded that a burning candle or an animal that breathe the air, both somehow, damage the air. But when he placed a mint plant in the same bell jar, he found that the mouse stayed alive and the candle continued to burn. Priestley hypothesised as follows: Plants restore to the air whatever breathing animals and burning candles remove. (Reference  : NCERT, page 207, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','11-207','11-134',NULL),
(131543,'biology','Consider the following statements about the experiments conducted by T.W Engelmann.\ni.Using a prism he split light into its spectral components and then illuminated a green alga, Cladophora, placed in a suspension of aerobic bacteria.\n\nii.The bacteria were used to detect the sites of O₂ evolution.\n\niii.He observed that the bacteria accumulated mainly in the region of green light of the split spectrum.\n\nSelect the incorrect answer using the code below:','Only iii is incorrect','Using a prism, T.W Engelmann split light into its spectral components and then illuminated a green alga, Cladophora, placed in a suspension of aerobic bacteria. The bacteria were used to detect the sites of O₂ evolution. He observed that the bacteria accumulated mainly in the region of blue and red light of the split spectrum. (Reference  s: NCERT, page 208, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect','Only i and ii are incorrect','Only ii and iii are incorrect','Only iii is incorrect','NEW','11-208','11-135',NULL),
(131544,'biology','Consider the following statements about the early experiments on photosynthesis and choose the correct option.','In green plants, oxidation of water leads to the formation of oxygen.','T.W Engelmann split light into its spectral components and then illuminated a green alga, Cladophora, placed in a suspension of aerobic bacteria. The bacteria were used to detect the sites of O₂ evolution. He observed that the bacteria accumulated mainly in the region of blue and red light of the split spectrum. Cornelius van Niel (1897-1985), who,based on his studies of purple and green bacteria, demonstrated that photosynthesis is essentially a light-dependent reaction. In green plants water is the hydrogen donor and is oxidised to oxygen. (Reference  : NCERT, page 208, paragraph 2,4 and 5).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','In green plants, oxidation of water leads to the formation of oxygen.','O₂ evolved by the green plant comes from  CO₂ .','Cornelius van Niel based his studies on spectral components and then illuminated a green alga, Cladophora.','Priestley observed that the bacteria accumulated mainly in the region of only blue light of the split spectrum.','NEW','11-208','11-135',NULL),
(131545,'biology','Consider the following statements about photosynthesis and select the incorrect option.','Both A and C are incorrect.','The stroma of the chloroplast contains enzymes required for the synthesis of carbohydrates and proteins. It also contains small, double-stranded circular DNA molecules and ribosomes.(Chapter Cell Unit of Life page 136)\nWithin the chloroplast there is membranous system consisting of grana, the stroma lamellae, and the matrix stroma . There is a clear division of labour within the chloroplast. In stroma, enzymatic reactions synthesise sugar(glucose), which in turn forms starch. (Reference  : NCERT, page 209, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Within the chloroplast there is membranous system consisting of ribosomes.','There is a no clear division of labour within the chloroplast and each organelle is assigned any task according to the requirements of the plant cell.','Both A and C are incorrect.','In stroma, enzymatic reactions synthesise sugar, which in turn forms starch.','NEW','11-209','11-136',NULL),
(131546,'biology','Consider the following statements about photosynthesis select the incorrect option.','Photosynthesis takes place only in the green leaves of plants.','Photosynthesis does take place in the green leaves of plants but it does so also in other green parts of the plants. Mesophyll cells in the leaves, have a large number of chloroplasts. The membrane system is responsible for trapping the light energy and also for the synthesis of ATP and NADPH. (Reference  : NCERT, page 209, paragraph 2,3 and 4).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Mesophyll cells in the leaves, have a large number of chloroplasts.','The membrane system inside chloroplasts is responsible used for the synthesis of ATP and NADPH.','Photosynthesis takes place only in the green leaves of plants.','Both A and B are incorrect.','NEW','11-209','11-136',NULL),
(131547,'biology','Given below are a few statements about photosynthesis in plants. Choose the option that is factually incorrect.','Maximum absorption is done in the blue, red and green regions.','The wavelengths at which maximum photosynthesis occurs in a plant. The wavelengths at which there is maximum absorption by chlorophyll a, i.e., in the blue and the red regions, also shows higher rate of photosynthesis. Hence, we can conclude that chlorophyll a is the chief pigment associated with photosynthesis. (Reference  : NCERT, page 210, paragraph 4, Figure 13.3b).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Maximum absorption is done by chlorophyll a.','Maximum absorption is done in the blue and red regions.','Chlorophyll a is the chief pigment in photosynthesis','Maximum absorption is done in the blue, red and green regions.','NEW','11-210','11-137',NULL),
(131548,'biology','Consider the following statements about photosynthesis in plants: \ni. Chlorophyll b is associated with yellow green in the chromatogram.\n\nii. Chlorophyll a is associated with blue to red in the chromatogram.\n\niii. Carotenoids are associated with red and green in the chromatogram.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Only i is correct','A chromatographic separation of the leaf pigments shows that the colour that we see in leaves is not due to a single pigment but due to four pigments: Chlorophyll a (bright or blue green in the chromatogram), chlorophyll b (yellow green), xanthophylls (yellow) and carotenoids (yellow to yellow-orange) (Reference  : NCERT, page 210, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct','NEW','11-210','11-137',NULL),
(110808,'chemistry','The no. of electrons around the central atom in trivalent state of B and Al are','6','3 from B and Al and three from bonded atoms.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.2)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Chemical properties',NULL,'3','mcq','6','8','3','10','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(131549,'biology','Consider the following statements about photosynthesis select the incorrect option.','The LHC are made up of one pigment molecule of each kind bound to chlorophylls.','The LHC are made up of hundreds of pigment molecules bound to proteins. The single chlorophyll a molecule forms the reaction centre. In PS I the reaction centre chlorophyll a has an absorption peak at 700 nm, hence is called P700, while in PS II it has absorptionmaxima at 680 nm, and is called P680. (Reference  : NCERT, page 211, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','In PS I the reaction center is called P700.','The single chlorophyll a molecule forms the reaction centre.','The LHC are made up of one pigment molecule of each kind bound to chlorophylls.','A and B both are correct.','NEW','11-211','11-138',NULL),
(131550,'biology','Consider the following statements about the light reactions and select the incorrect option.','Light reactions or the ‘Photochemical’ phase include light absorption, water splitting, O₂ release. The formation of ATP and NADPH takes place in dark reactions.','Light reactions or the ‘Photochemical’ phase include light absorption, water splitting, O₂ release, and the formation of high-energy chemical intermediates, ATP and NADPH. Several protein complexes are involved in the process.The pigments are organised into two discrete photochemical light harvesting complexes (LHC) within the Photosystem I (PSI) and Photosystem II (PS II). These are named in the sequence of their discovery, and not in the sequence in which they function during thelight reaction. (Reference  : NCERT, page 211, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions Part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Photosystems are named after the sequence of their discovery.','Protein complexes are crucial to the process of light reactions.','Light reactions or the ‘Photochemical’ phase include light absorption, water splitting, O₂ release. The formation of ATP and NADPH takes place in dark reactions.','Both A and B are incorrect.','NEW','11-211','11-138',NULL),
(131601,'biology','Consider the following statements about electron transport in plants: \ni. The electrons are not used up as they pass through the electron transport chain.\n\nii. The addition of these electrons reduces NADP+ to NADPH + H⁺\n\niii. Electrons in the reaction centre of PS I are also excited when they receive red light of wavelength 680nm.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Only i and ii are correct.','The electrons are not used up as they pass through the electron transport chain, but are passed on to the pigments of photosystem PS I. Simultaneously, electrons in the reaction centre of PS I are also excited when they receive red light of wavelength 700nm and are transferred to another accepter molecule that has a greater redox potential. These electrons then are moved downhill again, this time to a molecule of energy-rich NADP+. The addition of these electrons reduces NADP+ to NADPH + H⁺(Reference  : NCERT, page 212, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct.','Only ii and  iii are correct.','Only iii is correct','NEW','11-212','11-139',NULL),
(131602,'biology','Consider the following statements about splitting of water and select the incorrect option.','The water splitting complex is associated with the PS I.','The electrons that were moved from photosystem II must be replaced. This is achieved by electrons available due to splitting of water. The splitting of water is associated with the PS II; water is split into 2H⁺, [O] and electrons.This creates oxygen, one of the net products of photosynthesis. The electrons needed to replace those removed from photosystem I are provided by photosystem II. (Reference  : NCERT, page 212, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The electrons that were moved from photosystem II must be replaced by electrons available due to splitting of water.','The water splitting complex is associated with the PS II.','The water splitting complex is associated with the PS I.','O₂ is generated from Water molecule not from CO₂','NEW','11-212','11-139',NULL),
(131603,'biology','Consider the following statements about the chemiosmotic hypothesis and select the correct option.','The chemiosmotic hypothesis has been put forward to explain the mechanism of electron transport and proton gradient across membranes.','The chemiosmotic hypothesis has been put forward to explain the mechanism of ATP synthesis in chloroplast. Like in respiration, in photosynthesis too, ATP synthesis is linked to development of a proton gradient across a membrane. This time these are the membranes of thylakoid, plant thylakoid membrane based theory, not Animal membranes(Reference  : NCERT, page 213, paragraph 3).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','In case of respiration, the proton gradient across the membranes of thylakoid are considered.','The chemiosmotic hypothesis has been put forward to explain the mechanism of electron transport and proton gradient across membranes.','ADP synthesis is linked to development of a proton gradient across a membrane.','Chemiosmotic Hypothesis is applicable for animal membranes as well','NEW','11-213','11-140',NULL),
(131604,'biology','Consider the following statements about Cyclic and Non-cyclic Photo-phosphorylation and choose the correct option.','The two photosystems are connected through an electron transport chain.',': Living organisms have the capability of extracting energy from oxidisable substances and store this in the form of bond energy. Photo-phosphorylation is the synthesis of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate in the presence of light. The two photosystems are connected through an electron transport chain (Reference  : NCERT, page 212 and 213, paragraph 4 and 1, respectively).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Living organisms have the capability of extracting energy from reducible substances and store this in the form of mechanical energy.','Photo-phosphorylation is the synthesis of ATP from ADP and inorganic phosphate in the absence of light.','The two photosystems are connected through an electron transport chain.','Both A and B are correct.','NEW','11-213','11-140',NULL),
(131605,'biology','Consider the following statements about chemiosmotic hypothesis: \ni. The NADP reductase enzyme is located on the stromal side of the membrane.\n\nii. Protons are necessary for the reduction of NADP+ to NADPH⁺ H⁺\n\niii. Protons in the stroma increase in number.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Only i and ii are correct.','The NADP reductase enzyme is located on the stroma side of the membrane. Along with electrons that come from the acceptor of electrons of PS I, protons are necessary for the reduction of NADP+ to NADPH⁺ H⁺. These protons are also removed from the stroma. Hence, within the chloroplast, protons in the stroma decrease in number, while in the lumen there is accumulation of protons. (Reference  : NCERT, page 214, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct','NEW','11-214','11-141',NULL),
(131606,'biology','Consider the following statements about chemiosmotic hypothesis: \ni. The primary accepter of electron transfers its electron to an electron carrier.\n\nii. As electrons move through the photosystems, protons are transported across the membrane.\n\niii. The primary acceptor of electron which is located towards the outer side of the membrane.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Only ii and iii are correct.','As electrons move through the photosystems, protons are transported across the membrane. This happens because the primary accepter of electron which is located towards the outer side of the membrane transfers its electron not to an electron carrier but to an H carrier. (Reference  : NCERT, page 214, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct','NEW','11-214','11-141',NULL),
(131607,'biology','Consider the following statements about the biosynthetic phase.\ni. ATP and NADPH are used to drive the processes leading to the synthesis of food.\n\nii. The products of light reaction are ATP, NADPH and Oxygen.\n\niii. Immediately after light becomes unavailable, the biosynthetic process also immediately stops.\n\nSelect the incorrect answer :','Only iii is incorrect.','The products of light reaction are ATP, NADPH and Oxygen. Of these, O₂ diffuses out of the chloroplast while ATP and NADPH are used to drive the processes leading to the synthesis of food, more accurately, sugars. This is the biosynthetic phase of photosynthesis. This process does not directly depend on the presence of light but is dependent on the products of the light reaction, i.e., ATP and NADPH, besides  CO₂  and water. Immediately after light becomes unavailable, the biosynthetic process continues for some time, and then stops. If then, light is made available, the synthesis starts again. (Reference  : NCERT, page 215, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect','Only i and ii are incorrect','Only ii and iii are incorrect.','Only iii is incorrect.','NEW','11-215','11-142',NULL),
(131608,'biology','Consider the following statements about the chemiosmotic hypothesis. Choose the correct option.','ATP will be used immediately in the biosynthetic reaction taking place in the stroma.','The breakdown of the gradient provides enough energy to cause a conformational change in the CF₁ particle of the ATP synthase, which makes the enzyme synthesise several molecules of energy-packed ATP. ATP synthase has a channel that allows diffusion of protons back across the membrane; this releases enough energy to activate ATP synthase enzyme that catalyses the formation of ATP. (Reference  : NCERT, page 215, paragraph 1 and 2).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','ATP synthase has a channel that allows diffusion of electrons back across the membrane.','ATP will be used immediately in the biosynthetic reaction taking place in the stroma.','The breakdown of the gradient provides enough time to cause a breakdown of the CF₁.','ATP synthase enzyme catalyses the breakdown of ATP.','NEW','11-215','11-142',NULL),
(131609,'biology','Consider the following statements about the discovery of Melvin Calvin regarding photosynthesis.\ni. The primary acceptor molecule is a 5-carbon ketose sugar.\n\nii. Calvin pathway occurs in all photosynthetic plants.\n\niii. The primary acceptor molecule is a 6-carbon ketose sugar.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Only i and ii are correct','The studies very unexpectedly showed that the acceptor molecule was a 5-carbon ketose sugar – ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP). The Calvin pathway occurs in all photosynthetic plants; it does not matter whether they have C3 or C4 (or any other) pathways (Reference  : NCERT, page 216, paragraph 3 and 4).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct','NEW','11-216','11-143',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(131610,'biology','Consider the following statements about the discovery of Melvin Calvin.\ni. Calvin and his co-workers worked out the whole pathway and showed that the pathway operated in a non-cyclical manner.\n\nii.  They concluded that the RuBP was regenerated.\n\niii. The Calvin cycle can be described under three stages.','Only i is incorrect','Calvin and his co-workers then worked out the whole pathway and showed that the pathway operated in a cyclic manner; the RuBP was regenerated. The Calvin cycle can be described under three stages: carboxylation, reduction and regeneration (Reference  s: NCERT, page 216, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect','Only i and ii are incorrect','Only ii and iii are incorrect.','Only iii is incorrect.','NEW','11-216','11-143',NULL),
(131611,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Calvin cycle and choose the correct option.','ATP and NADPH are used to form a carbohydrate.','Reduction includes a series of reactions that lead to the formation of glucose. The regeneration steps require one ATP for phosphorylation to form RuBP. Carbohydrate is formed at the expense of the photochemically made ATP and NADPH. (Reference  : NCERT, page 217, paragraph 1and2, figure 13.8).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Reduction of 3-PGA leads to formation of RuBP.','Regeneration includes a series of reactions that lead to the formation of glucose.','Triose phosphate is reduced to regenerate ADP and NADPH.','ATP and NADPH are used to form a carbohydrate.','NEW','11-217','11-144',NULL),
(131612,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Calvin Cycle and select the incorrect option.','RuBisCo is the primary acceptor molecule.','Regeneration of the  CO₂  acceptor molecule RuBP is crucial if the cycle is to continue uninterrupted. The acceptor molecule is a 5-carbon ketose sugar – ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP).  CO₂  is utilised for the carboxylation of RuBP. (Reference  : NCERT, page 217, paragraph 1 and 2, Figure 13.8).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Atmospheric  CO₂  helps in carboxylation step.','RuBP is crucial if the cycle is to continue uninterrupted.','Both A and B are incorrect.','RuBisCo is the primary acceptor molecule.','NEW','11-217','11-144',NULL),
(131613,'biology','Consider the following statements about what goes in and what comes out of the Calvin cycle: \ni. Six  CO₂  molecules go in; one molecule of glucose comes out.\n\nii.18 ADP molecules that come out are accounted for by the 18 ATP molecules that go in.\n\niii. One  CO₂  molecules go in; one molecule of glucose comes out.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Only i and ii are correct.','To make one molecule of glucose 6 turns of the cycle are required. For every  CO₂  molecule entering the Calvin cycle, 3 molecules of ATP and 2 of NADPH are required. (Reference  : NCERT, page 218, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct','NEW','11-218','11-145',NULL),
(110901,'chemistry','Boron is unreactive in','crystalline form','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Reactivity towards air',NULL,'1','mcq','amorphous form','liquid form','crystalline form','gas form','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(131614,'biology','Given below are a few statements about C4 pathway in plants. \nChoose the option that is factually correct.','The particularly large cells around the vascular bundles of the C4 plants are called bundle sheath cells.','C4 plants lack a process called photorespiration and have greater productivity of biomass. The particularly large cells around the vascular bundles of the C4 plants are called bundle sheath cells, and the leaves which have such anatomy are said to have ‘Kranz’ anatomy. (Reference  : NCERT, page 218, paragraph 4 and 6).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','C4 plants have lesser productivity of biomass.','C4 plants perform a process called photorespiration.','The particularly large cells around the vascular bundles of the C4 plants are called bundle sheath cells.','The leaves which have particularly large cells around the vascular bundles are said to have ‘Bundle’ anatomy.','NEW','11-218','11-145',NULL),
(131615,'biology','Consider the following statements about C4 plants: \ni. The primary  CO₂  acceptor in mesophyll cells is RuBP.\n\nii. Hatch and Slack Pathway is a cyclic process.\n\niii. The C4 acid OAA is formed in the mesophyll cells.\n\nSelect the incorrect answer using the code below:','Only i is incorrect','The Hatch and Slack Pathway, is again a cyclic process. The primary  CO₂  acceptor is a 3-carbon molecule phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) and is present in the mesophyll cells. The C4 Acid OAA is formed in the mesophyll cells. (Reference  : NCERT, page 219, paragraph 1 and 2).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect','Only i and ii are incorrect','Only ii and iii are incorrect.','Only iii is incorrect.','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131616,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Hatch and Slack Pathway, and select the incorrect option.','In the bundle sheath cells these C4 acids are broken down to release Oxygen.','The primary  CO₂  acceptor is a 3-carbon molecule phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) and is present in the mesophyll cells. The enzyme responsible for this fixation is PEP carboxylase or PEPcase. In the bundle sheath cells these C4 acids are broken down to release  CO₂  and a 3-carbon molecule. (Reference  : NCERT, page 219, paragraph 2 and 3).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The enzyme responsible for this fixation of  CO₂  is PEP carboxylase.','The 4-carbon compounds like malic acid or aspartic acid in the mesophyll cells are transported to the bundle sheath cells.','In the bundle sheath cells these C4 acids are broken down to release Oxygen.','The primary acceptor of CO₂  is  3-carbon molecule phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP)','NEW','11-219','11-146',NULL),
(131617,'biology','Consider the following statements about Photorespiration in plants: \ni.  CO₂  fixation is where RuBP combines with  CO₂  to form 2 molecules of 3PGA, that is catalysed by RuBisCO.\n\nii. RuBisCO that is the most abundant enzyme in the world\n\niii. RuBisCO has a much greater affinity for O₂ when the  CO₂ :O₂ is nearly equal.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Only i and ii are correct.','CO₂  fixation is where RuBP combines with  CO₂  to form 2 molecules of 3PGA, that is catalysed by RuBisCO. RuBisCO that is the most abundant enzyme in the world. RuBisCO has a much greater affinity for  CO₂  when the  CO₂ :O₂ is nearly equal. (Reference  : NCERT, page 220, paragraph 4).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131618,'biology','Consider the following statements about photorespiration and select the correct option.','The biological function of photorespiration is not known yet.','In C3 plants some O₂ does bind to RuBisCO, and hence  CO₂  fixation is decreased. In the photorespiratory pathway, there is neither synthesis of sugars, nor of ATP. The biological function of photorespiration is not known yet. (Reference  : NCERT, page 220, paragraph 5).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','In C4 plants some O₂ does bind to RuBisCO, and hence  CO₂  fixation is decreased.','In the photorespiratory pathway, there is no synthesis of sugars, but two molecules of ATP are synthesized.','The biological function of photorespiration is not known yet.','C3 plants contain an isoform of RuBisCo that never binds to CO₂','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131619,'biology','Consider the differences between C3 and C4 plants and select the correct option.','B and C both are correct.','Primary acceptor in C3 Plant is a 5-carbon ketose sugar – ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP).The primary CO₂  acceptor is a 3-carbon molecule phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) and is present in the bundle sheath cells of C4 plants. In C4 plants photorespiration does not occur. \n\nThe cell types involved in initial carboxylation: In C3 plants, the initial carboxylation occurs in mesophyll cells, while in C4 plants, the initial carboxylation occurs in mesophyll and bundle sheath cells.\n\n (Reference  : NCERT, page 219, 220, paragraph 2,6 respectively).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','a.C4 plants have photorespiration but C3 plants do not.','b.The primary  CO₂  acceptor in both plants is different.','c.The cell types in which the initial carboxylation occurs are different.','B and C both are correct.','NEW','11-220','11-147',NULL),
(131620,'biology','Consider the following statements about the factors affecting photosynthesis choose the correct option.','Though several factors interact and simultaneously affect photosynthesis or  CO₂  fixation, usually one factor is the major cause or is the one that limits the rate.','The plant or internal factors are dependent on the genetic predisposition and the growth of the plant. The external factors would include the availability of sunlight, temperature,  CO₂  concentration and water. As a plant photosynthesises, all these factors will simultaneously affect its rate. Hence, though several factors interact and simultaneously affect photosynthesis or  CO₂  fixation, usually one factor is the major cause or is the one that limits the rate. (Reference  : NCERT, page 222, paragraph 1 and 2).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The plant or internal factors are dependent on the genetic predisposition and the temperature.','The external factors would include the availability of sunlight, temperature,  CO₂  concentration and genetic predisposition.','Though several factors interact and simultaneously affect photosynthesis or  CO₂  fixation, usually one factor is the major cause or is the one that limits the rate.','Both A and B are correct.','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(110902,'chemistry','which reaction protects the Al from corrosion','Oxidation','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Reactivity towards air',NULL,'1','mcq','Nitration','Oxidation','Reduction','Hydration','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(110903,'chemistry','Amorphous B and Al metal on heating in air from','B₂O₃ . Al₂O₃','Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Reactivity towards air',NULL,'1','mcq','B₂O. Al₃O','B₄O₃. Al₄O₃','B₃O₂ . Al₃O₂','B₂O₃ . Al₂O₃','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(131621,'biology','Consider the following statements about light affecting photosynthesis: \ni. Light saturation occurs at 10 % of the full sunlight.\n\nii. There is a linear relationship between incident light and  CO₂  fixation rates at low light intensities.\n\niii. Light is a strong limiting factor in nature.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Both i and ii are correct','There is a linear relationship between incident light and  CO₂  fixation rates at low light intensities. At higher light intensities gradually the rate does not show further increase as other factors become limiting. What is interesting to note is that light saturation occurs at 10 % of the full sunlight. Hence, except for plants in shade or in dense forests, light is rarely a limiting factor in nature. (Reference  : NCERT, page 222, paragraph 5).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Both i and ii are correct','Both ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','11-222','11-149',NULL),
(131622,'biology','Consider the following statements about  CO₂  concentration as a factor affecting photosynthesis: \ni. Some greenhouse crops such as tomatoes and bell pepper are allowed to grow in  CO₂  enriched atmosphere that leads to higher yields.\n\nii. Increase in concentration upto 0.05 % can cause an increase in  CO₂  fixation rates.\n\niii. Current availability of  CO₂  levels is limiting to the C4 plants.\n\nSelect the incorrect answer using the code below:','Only iii is incorrect.','Increase in concentration upto 0.05 % can cause an increase in  CO₂  fixation rates; beyond this the levels can become damaging over longer periods.  the C4 plants show saturation at about 360 μlL-1 while C3 responds to increased  CO₂  concentration and saturation is seen only beyond 450 μlL-1. Thus, current availability of  CO₂  levels is limiting to the C3 plants. The fact that C3 plants respond to higher  CO₂  concentration has been used for some greenhouse crops such as tomatoes and bell pepper. They are allowed to grow in  CO₂  enriched atmosphere that leads to higher yields. (Reference  : NCERT, page 223, paragraph 1, 2 and 3).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect','Both i and ii are incorrect','Both ii and iii are incorrect','Only iii is incorrect.','NEW','11-223','11-150',NULL),
(131623,'biology','Consider the following statements about temperature as a factor affecting photosynthesis:\ni. The dark reactions being enzymatic are temperature controlled.\n\nii. The C4 plants respond to higher temperatures and show higher rate of photosynthesis.\n\niii. Tropical plants have a lower temperature optimum than the plants adapted to temperate climates.\n\nSelect the incorrect answer using the code below:','Only iii is incorrect.','The dark reactions being enzymatic are temperature controlled. The C4 plants respond to higher temperatures and show higher rate of photosynthesis while C3 plants have a much lower temperature optimum. The temperature optimum for photosynthesis of different plants also depends on the habitat that they are adapted to. Tropical plants have a higher temperature optimum than the plants adapted to temperate climates. (Reference  : NCERT, page 223, paragraph 4 and 5).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect','Both i and ii are incorrect','Both ii and iii are incorrect','Only iii is incorrect.','NEW','11-223','11-150',NULL),
(131624,'biology','Consider the following statements about water as a factor affecting photosynthesis and choose the correct option.','Water stress can reduce  CO₂  availability.','Even though water is one of the reactants in the light reaction, the effect of water as a factor is more through its effect on the plant, rather than directly on photosynthesis. Water stress causes the stomata to close hence reducing the  CO₂  availability. Besides, water stress also makes leaves wilt, thus, reducing the surface area of the leaves and their metabolic activity as well. (Reference  : NCERT, page 223, paragraph 6).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Water is one of the reactants in the dark reaction.','The effect of water as a factor is directly on photosynthesis.','Water stress can reduce  CO₂  availability.','Water stress also increases the surface area of the leaves.','NEW','11-223','11-150',NULL),
(131625,'biology','Consider the following statements about photosynthesis:\ni. In the carbon fixation cycle, CO₂  is added to the cycle in the presence of enzyme, RuBisCO.\n\nii. RuBisCO also catalyses a wasteful oxygenation reaction in C4 plants: photorespiration.\n\niii. During Calvin Cycle ATP and NADPH synthesised in the dark reaction are utilised.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Only i is correct.','In the carbon fixation cycle,  CO₂  is added by the enzyme, RuBisCO, to a 5- carbon compound RuBP that is converted to 2 molecules of 3-carbon PGA. This is then converted to sugar by the Calvin cycle, and the RuBP is regenerated. During this process ATP and NADPH synthesised in the light reaction are utilised. RuBisCO also catalyses a wasteful oxygenation reaction in C3 plants: photorespiration. (Reference  : NCERT, page 224, paragraph 2).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct.','Only i and ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct.','Only iii is correct','NEW','11-224','11-151',NULL),
(131626,'biology','Consider the following statements about photosynthesis:\ni. There are two photosystems, PS I and PS II.\n\nii.  PS I has a 680 nm absorbing chlorophyll a.\n\niii. PS II has a P700 reaction centre.\n\nSelect the incorrect answer using the code below:','Both ii and iii are incorrect','There are two photosystems, PS I and PS II. PS I has a 700 nm absorbing chlorophyll a P700 molecule at its reaction centre, while PS II has a P680 reaction centre that absorbs red light at 680 nm. (Reference  s: NCERT, page 224, paragraph 1).','Plant Physiology >> Photosynthesis in Higher Plants >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect','Both i and ii are incorrect','Both ii and iii are incorrect','Only iii is incorrect.','NEW','11-224','11-151',NULL),
(220101,'chemistry','Originally, the term _____- was used to describe the addition of oxygen to an element or a compound.','oxidation',NULL,'Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','ox,id,at,io,n',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(220102,'chemistry','What is nearly the percentage of dioxygen in the earth\'s atmosphere?','20',NULL,'Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'2','mcq','10','20','30','40','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(220103,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : Many elements commonly occur on the earth in the form of their oxides. \nReason : Oxygen is present in the atmosphere in a good amount','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion',NULL,'Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(220104,'chemistry','Assertion : Very few elements commonly occur on the earth in the form of their oxides. Reason : Oxygen is present in the atmosphere in a good amount (nearly 20%)','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Because of the presence of dioxygen in the atmosphere (~20%), many elements combine with it and this is the principal reason why they commonly occur on the earth in the form of their oxides. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 263; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(220105,'chemistry','2 Mg (s) + O₂ (g) → 2 _____ (s)','MgO','2 Mg (s) + O₂ (g) → 2 MgO (s) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 263; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','Mg,O,O₂,Mg₂',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(220107,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : 2 Mg (s) + O₂ (g) → 2 MgO (s) Reason : S (s) + O₂ (g) → SO₂ (g)','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','S (s) + O₂ (g) → SO₂ (g) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 263; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(220108,'chemistry','What is the physical state of water which is denoted in combustion reactions?','liquid','CH₄ (g) + 2O₂ (g) → CO₂ (g) + 2H₂O (l) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','mcq','solid','liquid','gas','plasma','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220109,'chemistry','CH₄ (g) + 2O₂ (g) → _____ (g) + 2H₂O (l)','CO₂','CH₄ (g) + 2O₂ (g) → CO₂ (g) + 2H₂O (l) (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','C,O₂,C₂,O',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220110,'chemistry','The scope of term oxidation was broadened to include the removal of ________ from a substance','hydrogen','The scope of term oxidation was broadened to include the removal of hydrogen from a substance (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264; Paragraph 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Classical Idea',NULL,'1','fillup','hy,dr,og,en',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220301,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : When hydrogen and oxygen combine to form water, it is assumed that there is an electron transfer from H to O and consequently H₂ is oxidised and O₂ is reduced. Reason : The charge transfer is only partial and is perhaps better described as an electron shift rather than a complete loss of electron by H and gain by O','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','Though not simple in its approach, yet we can visualise the H atom as going from a neutral (zero) state in H₂ to a positive state in H₂O, the O atom goes from a zero state in O₂ to a dinegative state in H₂O. It is assumed that there is an electron transfer from H to O and consequently H₂ is oxidised and O₂ is reduced. However, as we shall see later, the charge transfer is only partial and is perhaps better described as an electron shift rather than a complete loss of electron by H and gain by O. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(220302,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : In order to keep track of electron shifts in chemical reactions involving formation of covalent compounds, a more practical method of using oxidation number has been developed. Reason : In this method, it is always assumed that there is a complete transfer of electron from a less electronegative atom to a more electonegative atom.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','In order to keep track of electron shifts in chemical reactions involving formation of covalent compounds, a more practical method of using oxidation number has been developed. In this method, it is always assumed that there is a complete transfer of electron from a less electronegative atom to a more electonegative atom.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(220303,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion: Oxidation number denotes the oxidation state of an element in a compound ascertained according to a set of rules formulated on the basis that electron pair in a covalent bond belongs entirely to more electronegative element. Reason: The assumption of electron transfer is made for book-keeping\n purpose only and it will become obvious at a later stage that it leads to the simple description of redox reactions.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','Oxidation number denotes the oxidation state of an element in a compound ascertained according to a set of rules formulated on the basis that electron pair in a covalent bond belongs entirely to more electronegative element. The assumption of electron transfer is made for book-keeping purpose only and it will become obvious at a later stage in this unit that it leads to the simple description of redox reactions (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Oxidation Number',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','The statement of the Assertion is false but the Reason is true','Both the statements are false','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
