(220522,'chemistry','Pb₃O₄ + 8HCl gives','3PbCl₂ + Cl₂ + 4H₂O','Pb₃O₄ is actually a stoichiometric mixture of 2 mol of PbO and 1 mol of PbO₂ . In PbO₂ , lead is present in +4 oxidation state, whereas the stable oxidation state of lead in PbO is +2. PbO₂ thus can act as an oxidant (oxidising agent) and, therefore, can oxidise Chloride ion of HCl into chlorine. We may also keep in mind that PbO is a basic oxide. Since HNO₃ itself is an oxidising agent therefore, it is unlikely that the reaction may occur between PbO₂ and HNO₃. However, the acid-base reaction occurs. It is the passive nature of PbO₂ against HNO₃ that makes the reaction different from the one that follows with HCl. \nProblem 8.7(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Paradox of Fractional Oxidation number',NULL,'3','mcq','3PbCl₂ + Cl₂ + 4H₂O','PbCl₄ + Cl₂ + 4H₂O','3PbCl₂ + 4H₂O','3PbCl₄ + 4H₂O','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
(220523,'chemistry','Pb₃O₄ + 4HNO₃ gives','2Pb(NO₃)₂ + PbO₂ + 2H₂O','Pb₃O₄ is actually a stoichiometric mixture of 2 mol of PbO and 1 mol of PbO₂ . In PbO₂ , lead is present in +4 oxidation state, whereas the stable oxidation state of lead in PbO is +2. PbO₂ thus can act as an oxidant (oxidising agent) and, therefore, can oxidise Chloride ion of HCl into chlorine. We may also keep in mind that PbO is a basic oxide. Since HNO₃ itself is an oxidising agent therefore, it is unlikely that the reaction may occur between PbO₂ and HNO₃. However, the acid-base reaction occurs. It is the passive nature of PbO₂ against HNO₃ that makes the reaction different from the one that follows with HCl. \nProblem 8.7(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Paradox of Fractional Oxidation number',NULL,'3','mcq','Pb(NO₃)₂ + PbO₂ + 2H₂O','2Pb(NO₃)₂ + PbO₂ + 2H₂O','3PbO₂ + 4NO₂','No Reaction','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
(220524,'chemistry','What doesn’t happen when Pb₃O₄ reacts with HCl ?','PbO₂ is more stable than PbO and is thus a very good oxidising agent and thus oxidises Hcl','Pb₃O₄ is actually a stoichiometric mixture of 2 mol of PbO and 1 mol of PbO₂ . In PbO₂ , lead is present in +4 oxidation state, whereas the stable oxidation state of lead in PbO is +2. PbO₂ thus can act as an oxidant (oxidising agent) and, therefore, can oxidise Chloride ion of HCl into chlorine. We may also keep in mind that PbO is a basic oxide. Since HNO₃ itself is an oxidising agent therefore, it is unlikely that the reaction may occur between PbO₂ and HNO₃. However, the acid-base reaction occurs. It is the passive nature of PbO₂ against HNO₃ that makes the reaction different from the one that follows with HCl. \nProblem 8.7(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Paradox of Fractional Oxidation number',NULL,'2','mcq','PbO₂ acts as an oxidant and oxidises Hcl','PbO being basic undergoes acid base reaction with Hcl','Chlorine gas and water are formed','PbO₂ is more stable than PbO and is thus a very good oxidising agent and thus oxidises Hcl','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
(220525,'chemistry','What happens when Pb₃O₄ reacts with HNO₃ ?','PbO₂ and HNO₃ don’t react both being oxidising agents.','Pb₃O₄ is actually a stoichiometric mixture of 2 mol of PbO and 1 mol of PbO₂ . In PbO₂ , lead is present in +4 oxidation state, whereas the stable oxidation state of lead in PbO is +2. PbO₂ thus can act as an oxidant (oxidising agent) and, therefore, can oxidise Chloride ion of HCl into chlorine. We may also keep in mind that PbO is a basic oxide. Since HNO₃ itself is an oxidising agent therefore, it is unlikely that the reaction may occur between PbO₂ and HNO₃. However, the acid-base reaction occurs. It is the passive nature of PbO₂ against HNO₃ that makes the reaction different from the one that follows with HCl. \nProblem 8.7(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Paradox of Fractional Oxidation number',NULL,'2','mcq','PbO₂ and HNO₃ don’t react both being oxidising agents.','PbO being acidic doesn’t react with HNO₃.','PbO₂ and HNO₃ undergo an acid base reaction.','Pb₃(NO₃)₈ is formed','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
