(220605,'chemistry','Mark the false statement with respect to redox reaction balancing.','Calculating the increase or decrease in the oxidation number per atom and for the entire molecule/ion in which it occurs is not necessary in oxidation number method.','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274; Paragraph 8.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Redox Balancing',NULL,'2','mcq','In writing equations for oxidation-reduction reactions, just as for other reactions, the compositions and formulas must be known for the substances that react and for the products that are formed.','If you realise that in a redox reaction, two substances are reduced and nothing is oxidised or vice-versa, something is wrong. Either the formulas of reactants or products are wrong or the oxidation numbers have not been assigned properly.','In oxidation number method, make the numbers of hydrogen atoms in the expression on the two sides equal by adding water (H2O) molecules to the reactants or products.','Calculating the increase or decrease in the oxidation number per atom and for the entire molecule/ion in which it occurs is not necessary in oxidation number method.','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
(220701,'chemistry','In acid-base systems we come across with a titration method for finding out the strength of one solution against the other using a ___ sensitive indicator.','pH','In acid-base systems we come across with a titration method for finding out the strength of one solution against the other using a pH sensitive indicator.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'1','fillup','pH,re,dox',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220702,'chemistry','In redox systems, the titration method can be adopted to determine the strength of a reductant/oxidant using a _____ sensitive indicator.','redox','In redox systems, the titration method can be adopted to determine the strength of a reductant/oxidant using a redox sensitive indicator.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'1','fillup','pH,re,dox',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220703,'chemistry','____ acts as self indicator.','KmnO₄⁻','In one situation, the reagent itself is intensely coloured, e.g., permanganate ion. Here it acts as the self indicator. The visible end point in this case is achieved after the last of the reductant is oxidised and the first lasting – tinge of pink colour appears at MnO4 – concentration as low as 10^-6 mol dm . This ensures a minimal ‘overshoot’ in colour beyond the equivalence point, the point where the reductant and the oxidant are equal in terms of their mole stoichiometry. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'1','mcq','KmnO₄⁻','Cu(II)','dichromate','diphenylamine','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220704,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','The first lasting tinge of pink colour appears at permanganate ion concentration 10⁻⁶ mole/m³.','In one situation, the reagent itself is intensely coloured, e.g., permanganate ion. Here it acts as the self indicator. The visible end point in this case is achieved after the last of the reductant is oxidised and the first lasting – tinge of pink colour appears at MnO4 – concentration as low as 10^-6 mol dm . This ensures a minimal ‘overshoot’ in colour beyond the equivalence point, the point where the reductant and the oxidant are equal in terms of their mole stoichiometry. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','In one situation of redox indicators, the reagent itself is intensely coloured, e.g., permanganate ion','The visible end point in permanganate ion is achieved after the last of the reductant is oxidised.','The first lasting tinge of pink colour appears at permanganate ion concentration 10⁻⁶ mole/m³.','equivalence point is the point where the reductant and the oxidant are equal in terms of their mole stoichiometry.','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220705,'chemistry','The first lasting tinge of pink colour appears at permanganate ion concentration 10⁻⁶ mole/m³. This ensures a minimal ‘____’ in colour beyond the equivalence point','overshoot','In one situation, the reagent itself is intensely coloured, e.g., permanganate ion. Here it acts as the self indicator. The visible end point in this case is achieved after the last of the reductant is oxidised and the first lasting – tinge of pink colour appears at MnO4 – concentration as low as 10^-6 mol dm . This ensures a minimal ‘overshoot’ in colour beyond the equivalence point, the point where the reductant and the oxidant are equal in terms of their mole stoichiometry. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'1','fillup','ov,er,sho,ot',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220706,'chemistry','The first lasting tinge of pink colour can appear and present at  permanganate concentration during a titration as low as-','10⁻⁶ mole/dm³','In one situation, the reagent itself is intensely coloured, e.g., permanganate ion. Here it acts as the self indicator. The visible end point in this case is achieved after the last of the reductant is oxidised and the first lasting – tinge of pink colour appears at MnO4 – concentration as low as 10^-6 mol dm . This ensures a minimal ‘overshoot’ in colour beyond the equivalence point, the point where the reductant and the oxidant are equal in terms of their mole stoichiometry. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'1','mcq','10⁻⁶ mole/m³','10⁻⁶ mole/dm³','10⁻⁷ mole/m³.','10⁻⁷ mole/dm³.','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220707,'chemistry','Which of the following is not the correct use of redox indicator?','A reagent which changes its colour at a certain pH can be used.','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','Indicators which are oxidised immediately after the last bit of the reactant is consumed, producing a dramatic colour change.','In some situations, the reagent itself is intensely coloured acting as redox indicator.','Reagents which are able to oxidise I⁻ ions ex Cu(II) and then titrating iodine using thiosulfate.','A reagent which changes its colour at a certain pH can be used.','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(241811,'biology','Assertion: It has become a fashion in recent years to use pollen tablets as food supplements.\n\nReason: Pollen grains are rich in nutrients.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Pollen grains are rich in nutrients. It has become a fashion in recent years to use pollen tablets as food supplements. (Reference  : NCERT, page 24, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-024',NULL,NULL),
(220708,'chemistry','Mark the correct statement.','Dichromate ion oxidises the indicator substance diphenylamine just after the equivalence point to produce an intense blue colour','If there is no dramatic auto-colour change , there are indicators which are oxidised immediately after the last bit of the reactant is consumed, producing a dramatic colour change. The best example is afforded by dichromate ion , which is not a self-indicator, but oxidises the indicator substance diphenylamine just after the equivalence point to produce an intense blue colour, thus signalling the end point.  (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','Dichromate ion is a self indicator','Dichromate ion oxidises the indicator substance diphenylamine just after the equivalence point to produce an intense purple colour','Dichromate ion reduces the indicator substance diphenylamine just after the equivalence point to produce an intense blue colour','Dichromate ion oxidises the indicator substance diphenylamine just after the equivalence point to produce an intense blue colour','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220709,'chemistry','Iodine gives an ____ colour with starch.','intense blue','If there is no dramatic auto-colour change , there are indicators which are oxidised immediately after the last bit of the reactant is consumed, producing a dramatic colour change. The best example is afforded by dichromate ion , which is not a self-indicator, but oxidises the indicator substance diphenylamine just after the equivalence point to produce an intense blue colour, thus signalling the end point.  (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'1','mcq','intense red','intense blue','light blue','light violet','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220710,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','Iodine has a very specific non redox reaction with thiosulfate ions.','There is yet another method which is interesting and quite common. Its use is restricted to those reagents which are able to oxidise I⁻ ions, say, for example, Cu(II)\nThis method relies on the facts that iodine itself gives an intense blue colour with starch and has a very specific reaction with thiosulphate ions, which too is a redox reaction.\nI₂, though insoluble in water, remains in solution containing KI as KI₃.  On addition of starch after the liberation of iodine from the reaction of Cu²⁺ ions on iodide ions, an intense blue colour appears. This colour disappears as soon as the iodine is consumed by the thiosulphate ions. Thus, the end-point can easily be tracked and the rest is the stoichiometric calculation only. \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu(II) ions oxidises iodide ions.','Iodine has a very specific non redox reaction with thiosulfate ions.','I₂ is insoluble in water.','In one of the methods of redox titration, on addition of starch after the liberation of iodine from the reaction of Cu(II) ions on iodide ions, an intense blue colour appears. This colour disappears as soon as the iodine is consumed by the thiosulphate ions. Thus, the end-point can easily be tracked','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220711,'chemistry','I₂ remains in solution containing KI as','KI₃','There is yet another method which is interesting and quite common. Its use is restricted to those reagents which are able to oxidise I⁻ ions, say, for example, Cu(II)\nThis method relies on the facts that iodine itself gives an intense blue colour with starch and has a very specific reaction with thiosulphate ions, which too is a redox reaction.\nI₂, though insoluble in water, remains in solution containing KI as KI₃.  On addition of starch after the liberation of iodine from the reaction of Cu²⁺ ions on iodide ions, an intense blue colour appears. This colour disappears as soon as the iodine is consumed by the thiosulphate ions. Thus, the end-point can easily be tracked and the rest is the stoichiometric calculation only. \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'1','mcq','KI₃','KI₂','K₂I₂','K₂I₄','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220712,'chemistry','Cu(II) ions oxidise iodide ions to form','Cu₂I₂(s) + I₂(aq)','There is yet another method which is interesting and quite common. Its use is restricted to those reagents which are able to oxidise I⁻ ions, say, for example, Cu(II)\nThis method relies on the facts that iodine itself gives an intense blue colour with starch and has a very specific reaction with thiosulphate ions, which too is a redox reaction.\nI₂, though insoluble in water, remains in solution containing KI as KI₃.  On addition of starch after the liberation of iodine from the reaction of Cu²⁺ ions on iodide ions, an intense blue colour appears. This colour disappears as soon as the iodine is consumed by the thiosulphate ions. Thus, the end-point can easily be tracked and the rest is the stoichiometric calculation only. \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'3','mcq','Cu₂I₂(s) + I₂(aq)','CuI(s) + I₂(aq)','Cu₂I₂(aq) + I₂(s)','I₂(aq)','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220713,'chemistry','Iodine reacts with thiosulfate ion to form','I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻','There is yet another method which is interesting and quite common. Its use is restricted to those reagents which are able to oxidise I⁻ ions, say, for example, Cu(II)\nThis method relies on the facts that iodine itself gives an intense blue colour with starch and has a very specific reaction with thiosulphate ions, which too is a redox reaction.\nI₂, though insoluble in water, remains in solution containing KI as KI₃.  On addition of starch after the liberation of iodine from the reaction of Cu²⁺ ions on iodide ions, an intense blue colour appears. This colour disappears as soon as the iodine is consumed by the thiosulphate ions. Thus, the end-point can easily be tracked and the rest is the stoichiometric calculation only. \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'3','mcq','I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻','I⁻ + SO₄²⁻','I⁻ + SO₄²⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻','No Reaction','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(241812,'biology','Assertion: Athletes cannot benefit from pollen consumption.\n\nReason: It hinders with their athletic ability.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Pollen consumption has been claimed to increase the performance of athletes and race horses (Reference  : NCERT, page 24, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-024',NULL,NULL),
(110905,'chemistry','The oxides of B are','acidic','As boron is typical non- metal, oxides of non metals are acidic in nature\n when dissolve in water from acids .\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.4)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Reactivity towards air',NULL,'1','mcq','acidic','basic','amphoteric','neutral','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(220714,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement.','Iodometric titration method relies on the facts that iodine itself gives an intense brown colour with starch and has a very specific reaction with thiosulphate ions','There is yet another method which is interesting and quite common. Its use is restricted to those reagents which are able to oxidise I⁻ ions, say, for example, Cu(II)\nThis method relies on the facts that iodine itself gives an intense blue colour with starch and has a very specific reaction with thiosulphate ions, which too is a redox reaction.\nI₂, though insoluble in water, remains in solution containing KI as KI₃.  On addition of starch after the liberation of iodine from the reaction of Cu²⁺ ions on iodide ions, an intense blue colour appears. This colour disappears as soon as the iodine is consumed by the thiosulphate ions. Thus, the end-point can easily be tracked and the rest is the stoichiometric calculation only. \n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277; Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','Iodometric titration is quite common. Its use is restricted to those reagents which are able to oxidise I⁻ ions.','Concept of redox processes has been evolving with time and is still evolving.','In recent past the oxidation process is visualised as a decrease in electron density and reduction process as an increase in electron density around the atom(s) involved in the reaction.','Iodometric titration method relies on the facts that iodine itself gives an intense brown colour with starch and has a very specific reaction with thiosulphate ions','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(220715,'chemistry','What doesn’t happens when zinc rod is dipped in Copper(II) sulfate solution?','Zinc ions are oxidised to metallic zinc','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','Heat is released','Electrons are directly transferred from Zinc to copper ions.','Copper ions are reduced to metallic copper','Zinc ions are oxidised to metallic zinc','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(220716,'chemistry','We take copper sulphate solution in a beaker and put a copper strip or rod in it. We also take zinc sulphate solution in another beaker and put a zinc rod or strip in it. Zinc and copper rods/strips are connected through wires and a salt bridge is inserted in the solution beakers.\nMark the incorrect option','Oxidation occurs at cathode','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','Reaction takes place in either of the beakers and at the interface of the metal and its salt solution','Transfer of electrons takes place indirectly.','Zinc rod acts as anode','Oxidation occurs at cathode','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(220717,'chemistry','A redox couple is defined as having together the ___ and reduced forms of a substance taking part in an oxidation or reduction half reaction.','oxidised','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'1','fillup','ox,id,ised,yg,en',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(220718,'chemistry','Mark the false statement with respect to Daniell cell','In the external circuit current travels from anode to cathode.','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the reduced and oxidized forms of the same species are present.','Zn rod acts as anode and Cu rod as cathode.','In the external circuit current travels from anode to cathode.','The circuit is completed inside the cell by the migration of ions through the salt bridge.','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(220719,'chemistry','In Daniell cell,','the current flows from cathode to anode in the external circuit','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','direction of electrons and the current is same.','the current flows from cathode to anode in the internal circuit','the current flows from cathode to anode in the external circuit','Zn rod acts as cathode.','NEW','11-278','11-250','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/220719q.png'),
(220720,'chemistry','Redox couple is represented by','separating the oxidised form from the reduced form by a vertical line or a slash representing an interface (e.g. solid/solution).','(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278; Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Electrode processes',NULL,'2','mcq','separating the oxidised form from the reduced form by a vertical line or a slash representing an interface (e.g. solid/solution).','separating the solid form from liquid form by a vertical line or a slash representing an interface.','separating the either solid or liquid form from liquid form by a vertical line or a slash representing an interface.','separating the oxidised form from the reduced form by a comma representing an interface (e.g. solid,solution).','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
