(220807,'chemistry','Which of the following processes does not involve oxidation of iron?','Formation of Fe(CO)₅ from Fe','[AIPMT-2015]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Liberation of hydrogen gas from steam by iron at high temperature.','Rusting of iron sheets','Decolourisation of blue CuSO₄ solution by iron','Formation of Fe(CO)₅ from Fe','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(220808,'chemistry','(a) H₂O₂ + O₃→ H₂O + 2O₂\n(b) H₂O₂ + Ag₂O → 2Ag + H₂O + O₂\nRole of hydrogen peroxide in the above reactions is respectively','reducing in both a and b','[AIPMT-2014]','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a- oxidising, b- reducing','b- oxidising, a- reducing','reducing in both a and b','oxidising in both a and b','NEW','11-264','11-236','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/220808q.png'),
(220901,'chemistry','Select the inaccurate option(s) from the following statements which explain about "Redox Reactions",  \n(a) The term "redox" was developed to describe a type of chemical processes in which oxidation and reduction always occur chronologically.\n(b) An oxidising agent is an electron donor, whereas a reducing agent is an electron acceptor (s).\n(c) Environmental challenges such as the Hydrogen Economy (the use of liquid hydrogen as fuel) and the formation of the \'Ozone Hole\' have begun to be classified as redox phenomena.','Both a and b','Option (a) is incorrect, the term "redox" was developed to describe a type of chemical processes in which oxidation and reduction always occur simultaneously not chronologically.\nOption (b) is inaccurate too as an oxidising agent is an electron(s) acceptor, whereas a reducing agent is an electron(s) donor.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 263; Introduction)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Only a','Both a and b','Both a and c','None of them','NEW','11-263','11-235',NULL),
(220902,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R)\nAssertion : If we submerge a strip of metallic zinc in a copper nitrate solution for an hour, the strip may start to develop a reddish metallic copper coating. The solution\'s original blue color may begin to fade.\nReason :  The solution\'s original blue color may begin to fade, because Zn reacts with copper nitrate to form Zn(NO₃)₂ and Cu.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','If you soak a strip of metallic zinc in an aqueous solution of copper nitrate for approximately an hour, you will see that the strip becomes covered with reddish metallic copper and the original blue colour of the solution vanishes. The aqueous solution of copper nitrate and metallic zinc react as follows:\nZn(s) + Cu²⁺ (aq) → Zn²⁺ (aq) + Cu(s)\nZinc is oxidised in this process because it loses electrons to generate Zn²⁺. Evidently, if zinc is oxidised by releasing electrons, something must be reduced, taking the electrons released by zinc. The copper ion is reduced by absorbing electrons from zinc. Cu²⁺ ions give the solution a characteristic blue colour. Therefore, the fading of the blue colour of the solution is caused by the reduction of Cu²⁺ ions to Cu.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both the statements are false.','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(220903,'chemistry','Consider the following reaction:\nCo + Ni²⁺ → Co²⁺ + Ni\nAt equilibrium, chemical tests reveal that both Ni²⁺(aq) and Co²⁺(aq)  are present at moderate concentrations.\nThis explains that:','neither the reactants nor the products are greatly favoured.','Co + Ni²⁺ → Co²⁺ + Ni\nAt equilibrium, chemical tests reveal that both Ni²⁺(aq) and Co²⁺(aq)  are present at moderate concentrations. In this case, neither the reactants [Co(s) and Ni²⁺(aq)] nor the products [Co²⁺(aq) and Ni (s)] are greatly favoured.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266-267; Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','neither the reactants nor the products are greatly favoured.','the reactants are greatly favoured but the products are not.','the products are greatly favoured but the reactants are not.','equilibrium equally favors both reactants and products.','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(220904,'chemistry','Which of the following statement(s) are inaccurate with respect to " Oxidation Number".\n(a) If two or more than two atoms of an element are present in the molecule/ion such as Na₂S₂O₃/Cr₂O₇²⁻, the oxidation number of the atom of that element will then be the average of the oxidation number of all the atoms of that element. \n(b) Halogens except fluorine, In all their compounds, have an oxidation number of 1.\n(c) In polyatomic ion, the algebraic sum of all the oxidation numbers of atoms of the ion must equal zero.','Both b and c','Statement (b) is incorrect because when halogens (Cl, Br, and I) appear as halide ions in their compounds, they have an oxidation number of -1. Oxoacids and oxoanions, which are formed when chlorine, bromine, and iodine react with oxygen, have positive oxidation numbers. Fluorine, on the other hand, has an oxidation number of -1 in all of its compounds.\n Statement (c) is inaccurate too as in polyatomic ion, the algebraic sum of all the oxidation numbers of atoms of the ion must equal the charge on the ion.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268; Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and c','none of them','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(220905,'chemistry','Select the option(s) from the following statements that are false with respect to "Types of Redox Reactions" ,\n(a)  All combustion processes, including elemental dioxygen, as well as other reactions involving elements other than dioxygen, fall under the category of combination reactions.\n(b) Both reactants must be in elemental form for a reaction to be classified as a combination reaction.\n(c) Decomposition reactions are widely used in metallurgical procedures that separate pure metals from their ores.','Both b and c','Statement (b) is inaccurate as in a combination reaction A + B  →   C either A and B, or both A and B must be in the elemental form for such a reaction to be a redox reaction. \nStatement (c) is inaccurate too as Metal displacement reactions are used in metallurgical procedures that separate pure metals from their ores.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 270; Paragraph 8.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','both a and b','both b and c','both a and c','All of them','NEW','11-270','11-242',NULL),
(241814,'biology','Assertion: Integuments encircle the nucellus.\n\nReason: Integuments provide protection to the nucellus.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Each ovule has one or two protective envelopes called integuments. Integuments encircle the nucellus except at the tip where a small opening called the micropyle is organised. (Reference  s: NCERT, page 25, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-025',NULL,NULL),
(220906,'chemistry','Suppose we are to balance the equation showing the oxidation of Fe²⁺ ions to Fe³⁺ ions by dichromate ions (Cr₂O₇)²⁻ in acidic medium, wherein, Cr₂O₇²⁻ ions are reduced to Cr³⁺ ions. What would be the Half  reactions for the this situation?','Oxidation half : Fe²⁺ (aq) →  Fe³⁺(aq) \nReduction half :Cr₂O₇²⁻ (aq) + 14H⁺ (aq) → 2 Cr³⁺(aq) + 7H₂O','Suppose we are to balance the equation showing the oxidation of Fe²⁺ ions to Fe³⁺ ions by dichromate ions (Cr₂O₇)²⁻  in acidic medium, wherein, Cr₂O₇²⁻  ions are reduced to Cr³⁺ ions. The following steps are involved in this task. \nStep 1:Produce unbalanced equation for the reaction in ionic form: Fe²⁺(aq) + Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) →  Fe³⁺(aq) +  Cr³⁺(aq) \nStep 2:Separate the equation into half reactions:\nOxidation half : Fe²⁺ (aq) →  Fe³⁺(aq) \nReduction half :Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) + 14H⁺ (aq) → 2Cr³⁺(aq) + 7H₂O\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 275,76; Half Reaction Method)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Oxidation half : Fe²⁺ (aq) →  Fe³⁺(aq) \nReduction half :Cr₂O₇²⁻ (aq) + 14H⁺ (aq) → 2 Cr³⁺(aq) + 7H₂O','Oxidation Half: Cr₂O₇²⁻ (aq) + 14H⁺ (aq) → 2 Cr³⁺(aq) + 7H₂O\nReduction half : Fe²⁺ (aq) →  Fe³⁺(aq)','Oxidation Half:   Fe³⁺ (aq) → Fe²⁺ (aq)\nReduction half : Cr₂O₇²⁻ (aq) → 2Cr³⁺(aq) + 7O(aq)','Half Reaction Method does not apply.','NEW','11-275','11-247',NULL),
(220907,'chemistry','Match the correct oxidation states of P, O and S in the following species NaH₂PO₄, CaO₂ and KAl(SO₄)₂.','NaH₂PO₄-(+5), CaO₂-(-1), KAl(SO₄)₂.12H₂O-(+6)','(i) NaH₂PO₄ \nLet the oxidation state of P be x. ⇒  (+1)+2(+1)+x+4(-2) = 0 , x = +5.\n(ii)CaO₂\nLet the oxidation state of O be x. ⇒ (+2) + 2x = 0 , x = -2\n(iii)KAl(SO₄)₂.12H₂O\nIgnore the water molecule because its neutral.\nLet the oxidation state of S be x. ⇒ (+1)+(+3)+2[(x)+4(-2)] = 0 , 4+2x-16=0 , 2x=12 , x=+6.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','match','NaH₂PO₄,(+5)','CaO₂,(-1)','KAl(SO₄)₂.12H₂O,(+6)',NULL,'NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220908,'chemistry','Match the correct oxidation states of B, S and P in the following species NaBH₄, H₂S₂O and H₄P₂O₇.','NaBH₄-(+3), H₂S₂O₇-(+6), H₄P₂O₇-(+5)','(i)NaBH₄\nLet oxidation state of boron be "x"\nAs this is metal hydride, therefore the oxidation state of hydrogen is −1. ⇒ +1+x−4=0 , x=+3\n(ii)H₂S₂O₇\nLet the oxidation number of sulphur be x.\nThe oxidation number of H and O are +1 and −2 respectively. ⇒ 2(+1)+2x+7(−2)=0 , 2+2x−14=0 , x=+6\n(iii)H₄P₂O₇\nLet X be the oxidation state of P. ⇒ 4(+1)+2X+7(−2)=0 , X=+5\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','match','NaBH₄,(+3)','H₂S₂O₇,(+6)','H₄P₂O₇,(+5)',NULL,'NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220909,'chemistry','What would be the oxidation state of S and Mn in NaHSO₄ and K₂MnO₄ respectively.','+6,+6','(i)NaHSO₄\nLet oxidation state of S be "x". ⇒1+1+x+(4×−2)=0 ; 2+x−8=0 ; x=+6.\n(ii)K₂MnO₄\nLet x be the oxidation number of Mn. ⇒ 2+x+4(−2)=0 ; x=+6\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','+6,+6','+6,-6','+4,-4','+4,+4','NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(220910,'chemistry','The oxidation number of I, S and Fe in KI₃ , H₂S₄O₆ and Fe₃O₄ is ______ respectively.','Iodine - (0 and -1), Sulphur - (0 and +5), Iron - (+2 and +3)','(i) KI₃ \nIn KI₃ molecule , the oxidation number of the two I atoms forming the I₂ molecule is 0 , whereas the  oxidation number of the the I atom forming the coordinate bond is  −1.\n(ii) H₂S₄O₆\nThe oxidation number of two of the four S atoms is +5 and the oxidation number of the other two S atoms is O.\n(iii) Fe₃O₄\nHere , one of the three Fe atoms exhibits the oxidation number of +2 and the other two Fe atoms exhibit the oxidation number of +3.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 280; Exercise 8.1) diagram(e220912)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','match','Iodine , (0 and -1)','Sulphur , (0 and +5)','Iron , (+2 and +3)',NULL,'NEW','11-280','11-252',NULL),
(221001,'chemistry','Which of the following is not an example of redox reaction?','BaCl₂ + H₂SO₄-> BaSO₄ + 2HCI','Redox reactions represent those reactions which involve change in oxidation number of the interacting species. (i.e ., oxidation and reduction)Following are the examples of redox reaction.           a.CuO + H₂-> Cu+ H₂O\nb.Fe₂ O₃ + 3CO-> 2Fe + 3CO₂\nc.2K + F₂-> 2KF.                      Option (d) is not an example of redox reaction.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 276; Paragraph 8.2.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CuO+ H₂ -> Cu+H₂O','Fe₂O₃ + 3CO -> 2Fe+ 3CO₂','2K+ F2-> 2KF','BaCl₂ + H₂SO₄-> BaSO₄ + 2HCI','NEW','11-276','11-248',NULL),
(221002,'chemistry','E⁻ values of some redox couples are given below. On the basis of these values choose the correct option. E⁻ values:                     Br₂/Br⁻=+1.90\nAg⁺ /Ag(s) =+0.80\nCu²⁺/ Cu(s)=+0.34;                 I₂(s)/I⁻  =+0.54','Cu will reduce Br2','Ans.(d) Given that E⁻ values of\nBr₂/Br⁻ =+190 V\nAg/Ag⁺ =- 080V\nCu²⁺/ Cu(s) = +034V\nI⁻  /I₂ (s)=- 054V\nBr ⁻ / Br2 = - 1.90V\nThe E⁻ values  show that copper will reduce Br2.if theE⁻ of the following redox reaction is positive\n2Cu+ Br₂ -> CuBr₂\nNow Cu ->Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻   ; E⁻ =-034V\nCu+ Br₂-> CuBr₂;        E⁻ = +075V\nSince, E⁻  of this reaction is positive, therefore, Cu can reduce Br₂ While other reaction will give negative value(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278 ;Paragraph 8.4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cu will reduce Br⁻','Cu will reduce Ag','Cu will reduce I⁻','Cu will reduce Br2','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(221003,'chemistry','Using the standard electrode potential, find out the pair between which redox reaction is not feasible. E⁰ values:\nFe³⁺ / Fe²⁺ = +0.77;\nI₂/I⁻ = +0.54;\nCu²⁺ / Cu =+0.34;\nAg⁺  /Ag = +0.80 V','Ag and Fe³⁺','We should Calculate the E⁰ of the four redax reactions. If E⁰ of a reaction isnegative, that reaction will not occur.\nAns.(d)\n(a) 2 Fe³⁺ + 2e⁻  -> 2Fe²⁺   ; E⁰ =+077V\n2I⁻ -> I₂ + 2e⁻; E⁰ = - 054V (sign of E⁻ is reversed)\n2 Fe³⁺ +2I⁻->2 Fe²⁺ + I₂: E⁰ (cell)=+ 0.23 V\nThis reaction is feasible since E⁰ (cell)is positive\n(b)Cu->Cu²⁺ +2e⁻ ; E⁰ =- 0.34V (sign ofEhasbeenreversed)\n2 Ag⁺  + 2e⁻ -> 2Ag ; E⁰ =+ 0.80V\nCu+ 2Ag⁺  -> 2Cu²⁺ + + 2Ag ; E⁰ = + 0.46V This reaction is feasible since E⁰ is positive.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278 ;Paragraph 8.4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Fe³⁺ and I-','Ag* and Cu','Fe + and Cu','Ag and Fe³⁺','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(110906,'chemistry','The oxides of Al and Ga are','Amphoteric','Their oxides are amphoteric beahave as acids with base and as base \n with acids.\n Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11;Page 319; Paragraph No.5)','Inorganic Chemistry >> The p-Block elements >> Group 13-Reactivity towards air',NULL,'2','mcq','Basic','Neutral','Amphoteric','Acidic','NEW','11-319',NULL,NULL),
(221004,'chemistry','Thiosulphate reacts differently with iodine and bromine in the reactions given below       2S₂O₃²⁻+I₂ ->S₄O₆²⁻ +2I⁻                \nS₂O₃²⁻ +2Br₂ +H₂O->2SO₄²⁻ + 2Br⁻ + 10H⁺                \nWhich of the following statements justifies the above dual behaviour of thiosulphate?','Bromine is a stronger oxidant than iodine','Ans. (a)\nBromine being stronger oxidising agent than I₂, oxidises S of S₂O₃²⁻ to SO₄²⁻  whereas I₂, oxidises it only into S₄O₆²⁻ ion(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277;Paragraph 8.3.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Bromine is a stronger oxidant than iodine','Bromine is a weaker oxidant than iodine','Thiosulphate undergoes oxidation by bromine and reduction by iodine in these reactions','Bromine undergoes oxidation and iodine undergoes reduction in these reactions','NEW','11-275','11-247',NULL),
(221005,'chemistry','The oxidation number of an element ina compound is evaluated on the basis of certain rules. Which of the following is incorrect in this respect?','The oxidation number of hydrogen is always +1','Ans. (a) Oxidation number of hydrogen is always +1 is a wrong rule since, it is +1 in hydrogen halides, - 1 in hydrides and zero in H₂ molecule.                                \nAll the other three statements (b), (c) and (d) are correct.                                (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278 ;Paragraph 8.4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The oxidation number of hydrogen is always +1','The algebraic sum of all the oxidation numbers in a compound is zero','An element in the free or the uncombined state bears oxidation number zero','In all its compounds, the oxidation number of fluorine is -1','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(221006,'chemistry','In which of the following compounds, an element exhibits two different oxidation states?','NH₄NO3','Ans. (b) NH₄NO3 is actually NH₄⁺   and NO₃⁻ . It is an ionic compound.                        \nThe oxidation number of nitrogen in the two species is different as shown below Let oxidation number of N in NH₄⁺ is x.                        (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268;Paragraph 8.3.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','NH₂ OH','NH₄NO3','N₂H₄','N₂H','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(221007,'chemistry','Which of the following arrangements represent increasing oxidation number of the central atom ?','CrO₂⁻, CIO₃⁻ , CrO₄²⁻ , MnO₄⁻','In the oxides the oxidation number of oxygen is -2    Let the oxidation number of central atom be X         CrO₂⁻ :                                               X + (2 x (-2)) = - 1 \n X = +3\nCrO₂ :\nx + (4 x (-2)) = - 2 \nX = +6\nClO₃⁻ \nx + (3 x (-2)) = - 1\n X = +5\nMnO₂:\nX + (4 x (-2)) = - 1\nX = +7\nHence, correct answer is CrO₂⁻, CIO₃⁻ , CrO₄²⁻ , MnO₄⁻ (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CrO₂⁻, CIO₃⁻ , CrO₄²⁻ , MnO₄⁻','CIO₃⁻,CrO₄²⁻,MnO₄⁻,CrO₂⁻','CrO₂⁻,ClO₃⁻,MnO₄⁻,CrO₄²⁻','CrO₄²⁻ , MnO₄⁻,CrO₂⁻ , CIO₃⁻','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221008,'chemistry','The largest oxidation number exhibited by an element depends on its outer electronic configuration. With which of the following outer electronic configurations the element will exhibit largest oxidation number?','3d⁵ 4s²','Ans. (d) Highest oxidation number of any transition element = (n -1)d electrons + ns electrons Therefore, large the number of electrons in the 3d orbitals, higher is the maximum oxidation number(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 268;Paragraph 8.3.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','3d¹ 4s²','3d³ 4s²','3d⁵ 4s¹','3d⁵ 4s²','NEW','11-268','11-240',NULL),
(221009,'chemistry','Identify disproportionation reaction                                      (a) CH₄   + 2O₂  ->CO₂ +2H₂O\n(b) CH₄ +4Cl₂     ->CCI₄ + 4HCI\n(C) 2F₂  + 2OH⁻   ->2F⁻ +OF₂ +H₂O.                                        (d) 2NO₂  +  2OH⁻ - >NO₂⁻ + NO₃⁻+H₂O','2NO₂  +  2OH⁻ -->NO₂⁻ + NO₃⁻+H₂O','Ans. (d) Reactions in which the same substance is oxicised as well as reduced are called disproportionation reactions. Writing the O.N of each element above its symbol in the given reactions\n(a) CH₄   + 2O₂  ->CO₂ +2H₂O\n(b) CH₄ +4Cl₂     ->CCI₄ + 4HCI\n(C) 2F₂  + 2OH⁻   ->2F⁻ +OF₂ +H₂O\n(d) 2NO₂  +  2OH⁻ - >NO₂⁻ + NO₃⁻+H₂O                                     Thus, in reaction (d), N is both oxidised as well as reduced since the O.N. of N increases from +4 in NO₂, to +5 in NO₃⁻; and decreases from +4 in NO2 to +3 in NO₂⁻.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272;Paragraph 8.3.1 )','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₄   + 2O₂  ->CO₂ +2H₂O','CH₄ +4Cl₂     ->CCI₄ + 4HCI','2F₂  + 2OH⁻   ->2F⁻ +OF₂ +H₂O','2NO₂  +  2OH⁻ -->NO₂⁻ + NO₃⁻+H₂O','NEW','11-272','11-244','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/221009q.png'),
(221010,'chemistry','Which of the following elements does not show disproportionation tendency ?','F','Ans. (c) Being the most electronegative element, F can only be reduced and hence it always shows an oxidation number of -1. Further, due to the absence of d\'-orbitals it cannot be oxidised and henceit does not show positive oxidation numbers\nIn other words. F cannot be oxidised as well as reduced simultaneously and hence does not show disproportionation reactions(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272;Paragraph 8.3.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cl','Br','F','I','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(221011,'chemistry','Which ofthe following statement(s) is/are not true about the following decomposition reaction? \n2 KCIO₃ ->2KCl +3O₂ \n\na. Potassium is undergoing oxidation        \n\nb. Chlorine is undergoing oxidation        \n\nc. Oxygen is reduced','a,b,c','We should write the oxidation number of each element above its symbol. then                                     a.        The O.N. of K does not change, K undergoes neither reduction nor oxidation. Thus. option (a) is not correct.\nb.        The ON of chlorine decreases from +5 in KCIO₃, to -1 in KCI. hence CI undergoes reduction.\nc.        Since, O.N of oxygen increases from-2 in KCIO₃, to 0 in O2, oxygen is oxidised.                         \nd.        This statement is not correct because Cl is undergoing reduction and O is undergoing oxidation (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 270 ;Paragraph 8.3.1.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,c','NEW','11-270','11-242',NULL),
(241815,'biology','Assertion: The hilum represents the junction between ovary and funicle.\n\nReason: The body of the ovary fuses with funicle in the region called hilum.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','The body of the ovule fuses with funicle in the region called hilum. Thus, hilum represents the junction between ovule and funicle. (Reference  : NCERT, page 25, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-025',NULL,NULL),
(221012,'chemistry','Identify the correct statement (s) in relation to the following        \nZn +2HCI-> ZnCl₂ +H₂ reaction\n\na. Zinc is acting as an oxidant	\n\nb. Chlorine is acting as a reductant	\n\nc. Hydrogen ion is acting as an oxidant	\n\nd. Zinc is acting as a reductant','c,d','Ans. (c, d)\nWriting the oxidation number of each element above its symbol, so that\nZn + 2HCI -> ZnCl₂+H₂ \n(a) The oxidation number of Zn increases from 0 in Zn to +2 in ZnCl₂. therefore, Zn acts as a reductant Thus, option (a) is incorrect.                                                           b.        The oxidation number of chlorine does not change, therefore, it neither acts as a reductant nor an oxidant. Therefore, option(b) is incorrect                                        \nc.        The oxidation number of hydrogen decreases from +1 in H⁺ to 0 inH₂ , therefore, H⁺  acts as an oxidant Thus, option (c) is correct.                                \nd.        As explained in option (a). Zn acts as reductant, therefore, it cannot act as an oxidant Thus, option (d) is correct.                                (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265 ;Paragraph 8.2 )','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,b,d','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221013,'chemistry','The exhibition of various oxidation states by an element is also related to the outer orbital electronic configuration of its atom. Atom (s) having whichof the following outermost electronic configurations will exhibit more than one oxidation state in its compounds.\n\na. 3s¹ 	\n\nb. 3d¹ 4s² 	\n\nc. 3d² 4s² 	\n\nd. 3s² 3p³','b,c,d','Elements which have only s-electrons in the valence shell do not show more than one oxidation state. Thus, element with 3s¹ as outer electronic configuration shows onlyone oxidation state of +1.                                                      p -Blockelements also show variable oxidation states due to a number of reason such as involvement of d-orbitals and inert pair effect e.g ., element (d) with 3s² 3p³  as (i.e ., P) asthe outer electronic configuration shows variable oxidation states of +3 and +5 due to involvement of d-orbitals(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265;Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','b,c,d','a,b,d','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221014,'chemistry','Identify the correct statements with reference to the given reaction                                       \nP₄ +3OH⁻ +3H₂ O ->PH₃ +3H₂ PO₂⁻ \n\na. Phosphorus is undergoing reduction only        \n\nb. Phosphorus is undergoing oxidation only        \n\nc. Phosphorus is undergoing oxidation as well as reduction        \n\nd. Hydrogen is undergoing neither oxidation nor reduction','c,d','P₄ +3OH⁻ +3H₂ O ->PH₃ +3H₂ PO₂⁻                             \n\nIn this reaction, O N. of P increases from 0 in P₄ to +1 in H₂ PO₂⁻   ; and decreases to - 3 in PH₃. therefore, P undergoes both oxidation as well as reduction Thus, options (a) and (b) are wrong and option(c)iscorrect.\nFurther, O.N. of H remains +1 in all the compounds, i.e ., H neither undergoes oxidation nor reduction Thus, option (d) iscorrect.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265;Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','b,d','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221015,'chemistry','Which of the following electrodes will act as anodes, which connected to Standard Hydrogen Electrode ?\n\na. Al/ Al³  E⁻  =- 1.66	\n\nb. Fe/Fe²  E⁻ =- 0.44	\n\nc. Cu/Cu²  E⁻  =+0.34	\n\nd. F₂(g) / 2F⁻(aq) E⁻ =0.287','a,b','All electrodes which have negative electrode potential are stronger reducing agents than H₂ gas hence acts as anode when connected to standard hydrogen gas (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278 ;Paragraph 8.4.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,d','b,d','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(221016,'chemistry','Assertion (A) Among halogens fluorine is the best oxidant. Reason (R) Fluorine is the most electronegative atom.','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','Ans. (b) Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion. Among halogen F. is the best oxidant because it has the highest E value(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265;Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221017,'chemistry','Assertion (A) In the reaction between potassium permanganate and potassium iodide, permanganate ions act as oxidising agent.\nReason (R) Oxidation state of manganese changes from +2 to +7 during the reaction.','Both A and R are false','10KI +2KMnO₄ + 8H₂SO₄->2MnSO₄ +6K₂SO₄ +8H₂O +5I₂               Oxidation state of Mn decreases from +7 to +2.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 276 PROBLEM 8.10)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-276','11-248',NULL),
(221018,'chemistry','Assertion (A) The decomposition of hydrogen peroxide to form water and oxygen is an example of disproportionation reaction.\nReason (R) The oxygen of peroxide is in -1 oxidation state and it is converted to zero oxidation state in O₂  and -2 oxidation state in H₂O','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','2H₂O₂ -> 2H₂O +O₂ The above reaction is a example of disproportionation reaction .(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272;Paragraph 8.3.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
