(221301,'chemistry','What is the oxidation number to the underlined elements in each of the following species        NaH₂PO₄','5','NaH₂PO₄.                                         Let the O.N. of P = X.\nWe know that,\nThe O.N. of Na =+1\nThe O.N. of H = +1 \nThe O.N. of 0 = - 2\nThen, we have\n1(+1) + 2(+1) + 1(x) + 4(-2) = 0\n1 + 2+x - 8 = 0\nx = +5\nHence, the O.N of P is +5 (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','5','4','3','-5','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221302,'chemistry','What is the oxidation number to the underlined elements in each of the following species NaHSO₄','6','NaHSO₄                                           Let the O.N of S be X\nThe O.N. of Na = +1\nThe O.N. of H = +1\nThe O.N. of 0 = - 2\n1(+1) + 1(+1) + 1(x) + 4(-2) = 0\n1+1+x - 8 = 0\nx =+6\nHence, the O.N. of S is +6. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','-6','5','6','-5','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221303,'chemistry','What is the oxidation number to the underlined elements in each of the following species H₄P₂O₇','5','H₄P₂O₇.                                                 Let the O.N. of P = x.\nWe know that,\nThe O.N. of H = +1\nThe O.N. of 0 = - 2 \nThen, we have\n4(+1) + 2 (X) + 7(-2) = 0\n4+2X -14 = 0 \n2X = + 10\nX = +5\nHence the O.N of P is+5\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','-3','5','3','4','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221304,'chemistry','What is the oxidation number to the underlined elements in each of the following species K₂MnO₄','6','K₂MnO₄                                               Let the O.N. of Mn = x.\nWe know that,\nThe O.N. of K = +1\nThe O.N. of O = - 2\nThen, we have\n2(+1) + x + 4(-2) = 0\nx=+6\nHence, the O.N. of Mn is +6\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','-1','3','4','6','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221305,'chemistry','What is the oxidation number to the underlined elements in each of the following species CaO₂','-1','CaO₂ \nLet the O.N. of O = x.                       We know that,                                                The O.N. of Ca = +2                   Then,we have\n(+2) + 2(x) + 0\n2 + 2x = 0\nx =- 1\nHence, the O.N. of O is -1\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','-1','3','4','6','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221306,'chemistry','What is the oxidation number to the underlined elements in each of the following species NaBH₄','3','NaBH₄\nLet the O.N. of B = x.\nWe know that,\nThe O.N. of Na = +1\nThe O.N. of H =+1\nThen, we have\n1(+1) + 1(x) + 4(-1) = 0\n1+x -4 = 0. \nx = - 3\nHence, the O.N. of B is +3\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','-1','3','4','6','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221307,'chemistry','What is the oxidation number to the underlined elements in each of the following species H₂S₂O₇','6','H₂S₂O₇                                             Let the O.N. of S = x.                        We know that,\nThe O.N. of H = +1\nThe O.N. of 0 = - 2 Then, we have\n2(+1) + 2(x) + 7(-2) = 0\n=> 2 + 2x - 14 = 0\n=> 2x = 12.                               Hence the O.N of S is +6\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','-1','3','4','6','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221308,'chemistry','What is the oxidation number to the underlined elements in each of the following species KAl(SO₄)₂.12H₂O','6','KAl(SO₄)₂.12H₂O                                  Let the O.N. of S = x.\nWe know that,\nThe O.N. of K= +1\nThe O.N. of Al = +3\nThe O.N. of 0 = - 2 \nThe O.N. of H = +1\nThen, we have\n1(+1) + 1(+3) + 2x + 8(-2) + 24(+1) + 12(-2) = 0\n 1 + 3 + 2x - 16 + 24 - 24 = 0\nx= +6\nHence, the O.N. of S is +6\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','-1','3','4','6','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221309,'chemistry','In the following reaction, which species is undergoing oxidation?                                     2 Fe₂O₃+ 3 C → 4 Fe + 3 CO₂','C','Explanation: In the given reaction, carbon (C) is undergoing oxidation because it loses electrons and its oxidation state increases from 0 to +4, while the oxidation state of iron (Fe) decreases from +3 to 0, indicating reduction. Fe₂O₃ is a reactant and its oxidation state remains unchanged, CO₂ is a product and its oxidation state also remains unchanged. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265;Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Fe₂O₃','C','Fe','CO2','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221310,'chemistry','Which of the following is a redox reaction?','2 H₂O   → 2 H₂ + O₂','Explanation: In the given reaction, water (H2O) is undergoing a redox reaction because it is being split into hydrogen gas (H2) and oxygen gas (O2) through the process of electrolysis. Oxygen is being reduced to O₂ by gaining electrons, while hydrogen is being oxidized to H2 by losing electrons. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265;Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2 H₂O   → 2 H₂ + O₂','NaCl + AgNO₃ → NaNO₃ + AgCl','CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂','2 HCl + Mg(OH)2 → 2 H2O + MgCl2','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221311,'chemistry','Which of the following substances acts as an oxidizing agent?','Cl₂','Explanation: Chlorine (Cl₂) is a strong oxidizing agent as it readily accepts electrons and gets reduced to Cl-. In the process, it oxidizes other substances by taking electrons from them, thereby causing their oxidation. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 265;Paragraph 8.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','H₂O','Cl₂','Fe₂O₃','SO₂','NEW','11-265','11-237',NULL),
(221312,'chemistry','What is the change in the oxidation state of nitrogen in the following reaction?                    NH₄⁺ + 3 O₂ → N₂ + 6 H2O','5','Explanation: In the given reaction, nitrogen (N) changes its oxidation state from -3 in NH₄⁺to 0 in N₂, indicating an increase of 5 units. Hence, the change in the oxidation state of nitrogen is +5. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> Exercise Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','-3','-1','3','5','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(241816,'biology','Assertion: A single megaspore mother cell undergoes meiotic division.\n\nReason: Meiosis results in the production of four megaovules.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','The MMC undergoes meiotic division. Meiosis results in the production of four megaspores (Reference  s: NCERT, page 26, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-026',NULL,NULL),
(221401,'chemistry','Assertion: In a redox reaction, the species that undergoes reduction gains electrons.           \n\nReason: Oxidation is defined as the gain of electrons or a decrease in oxidation state.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Explanation: The assertion statement is correct. In a redox (reduction-oxidation) reaction, reduction refers to the process of gaining electrons or a decrease in oxidation state of a species. Reduction occurs when a species accepts one or more electrons from another species, resulting in a decrease in its oxidation state.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264;Paragraph 8.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(221402,'chemistry','Assertion: Reduction potential is a measure of the ability of a species to undergo reduction.               \n\nReason: Reduction potential is a measure of the tendency of a species to gain electrons and get reduced in a redox reaction.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Explanation: Reduction potential is a measure of the tendency of a species to gain electrons and get reduced in a redox reaction. It indicates the ability of a species to undergo reduction, with higher reduction potential values indicating a stronger tendency to gain electrons and undergo reduction.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 278;Paragraph 8.4.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-278','11-250',NULL),
(221403,'chemistry','Assertion: In a redox reaction, the species that undergoes oxidation loses protons. \n\nReason: Oxidation is defined as the loss of electrons or an increase in oxidation state','The statement of the Assertion is false, but the Reason is true.','Explanation: The reason statement is correct. Oxidation is the process of losing electrons or an increase in oxidation state of a species. It does not involve the loss of protons, which are positively charged particles found in the nucleus of an atom. In a redox reaction, the species that undergoes oxidation loses electrons and may gain protons from another species, but it does not lose protons.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264;Paragraph 8.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','The statement of the Assertion is false, but the Reason is true.','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(221404,'chemistry','Assertion: In a redox reaction, oxidation always occurs at the anode.                              Reason: Anode is the electrode where electrons are lost during a redox reaction.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Explanation: In a redox reaction, oxidation is the loss of electrons, while reduction is the gain of electrons. Anode is the electrode where oxidation occurs, which means electrons are lost. So, the reason given is correct. However, oxidation can also occur at the cathode, depending on the specific redox reaction. For example, in the electrolysis of molten sodium chloride, oxidation occurs at the cathode, and reduction occurs at the anode. Hence, the assertion is true, but the reason is false.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 277;Paragraph 8.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-277','11-249',NULL),
(221405,'chemistry','Assertion: Oxidizing agent is reduced during a redox reaction.                           Reason: Oxidizing agent gains electrons and undergoes reduction.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Explanation: The assertion is true because oxidizing agent is a species that causes oxidation in another species by accepting electrons. During this process, the oxidizing agent gains electrons and undergoes reduction. Reduction is the process of gaining electrons. So, the reason given is also true, as oxidizing agent indeed gains electrons and undergoes reduction. Hence, both the assertion and reason are true.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 269;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-269','11-241',NULL),
(221406,'chemistry','Assertion: In a redox reaction, the species that gains electrons is reduced.                       Reason: Reduction is the process of losing electrons.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Explanation: The assertion is true because reduction is the process of gaining electrons. The reason given is incorrect, as reduction involves gain of electrons, not loss. Oxidation is the process of losing electrons. In a redox reaction, one species is oxidized (loses electrons) and another species is reduced (gains electrons), and these two processes occur simultaneously. Hence, the assertion is true, but the reason is false.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264;Paragraph 8.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(121401,'chemistry','Identify the correct IUPAC name of the compound shown in diagram(121401Q):','3-ethyl-4,4-dimethylheptane','In the compound "3-ethyl-4,4-dimethylheptane", the prefix "3-ethyl" indicates that the ethyl group is attached to the third carbon atom of the parent chain, and the prefix "4,4-dimethyl" indicates that there are two methyl groups attached to the fourth carbon atom of the parent chain.\n\nAlkyl groups are written in alphabetically, thus lower locant 3 is assigned to ethyl.\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 345)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','3-ethyl-4,4-dimethylheptane','4,4-dimethyl-3-ethylheptane','5-ethyl-4, 4-dimethylheptane','4,4-bis(methyl)-3-ethylheptane','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121401q.png'),
(221407,'chemistry','Assertion: Redox reactions are always spontaneous.         Reason: Redox reactions involve transfer of electrons from one species to another.','The statement of the Assertion is false, but the Reason is true.','Explanation: The assertion is false because redox reactions can be spontaneous or non-spontaneous, depending on the specific reaction conditions. Spontaneity of a redox reaction is determined by the change in Gibbs free energy (ΔG) for the reaction. \n\nThe reason given is true, as redox reactions involve transfer of electrons from one species to another. However, spontaneity of a redox reaction depends on other factors as well, such as temperature, pressure, and concentration of reactants and products. Hence, the assertion is false, but the reason is true.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264;Paragraph 8.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','The statement of the Assertion is false, but the Reason is true.','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(221408,'chemistry','Assertion: Reduction potential is a measure of the tendency of a species to gain electrons. \n\nReason: A species with a higher reduction potential has a higher tendency to undergo oxidation.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Reduction potential is a measure of the tendency of a species to gain electrons. It is a measure of the ability of a chemical species to accept electrons and undergo reduction. The reduction potential is defined as the tendency of a species to be reduced, relative to a standard hydrogen electrode (SHE), which is assigned a reduction potential of 0 V.\n\nA species with a higher reduction potential has a higher tendency to undergo reduction. This is because a species with a higher reduction potential has a greater affinity for electrons and is more likely to accept electrons and undergo reduction, compared to a species with a lower reduction potential\n\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266;Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(221409,'chemistry','Assertion: Oxidation is the process of losing electrons.               Reason: Loss of electrons results in an increase in the oxidation number of an element.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The assertion is true and the reason is true. Loss of electrons results in an increase in the oxidation number of an element, and oxidation is defined as the process of losing electrons. (Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264;Paragraph 8.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(221410,'chemistry','Assertion: The oxidation number of oxygen is  -2 in most of the  compounds.                       \n\nReason: Oxygen is a highly electronegative element.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The assertion is true and the reason is true. Oxygen is a highly electronegative element and commonly exhibits an oxidation number of -2 in most compounds.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221411,'chemistry','Assertion: In an electrolytic cell , reduction always occurs at the cathode.                             \n\nReason: Cathode is the site where electrons are lost during a redox reaction.','Both statements are false.','In an electrolytic cell where an external source of electrical energy is used  reduction occurs at the cathode and oxidation occurs at the anode.\n\nReference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266;Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(221412,'chemistry','Assertion: Balancing of redox reactions can be done by adjusting the coefficients of the reactants and products.                             Reason: Balancing coefficients represent the number of electrons transferred during a redox reaction.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The assertion is true and the reason is true. Balancing of redox reactions involves adjusting the coefficients of the reactants and products to ensure that the number of electrons transferred is equal on both sides of the equation.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 274;Paragraph 8.3.2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-274','11-246',NULL),
(221413,'chemistry','Assertion: In a disproportionation reaction, an element is simultaneously oxidized and reduced.                           Reason: Disproportionation reactions  involves the transfer of electrons from the element','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','Disproportionation reactions always involve the transfer of electrons between atoms of the same element, which leads to the oxidation of some atoms and the reduction of others. \n\nThis is because in a disproportionation reaction, the same element undergoes both oxidation and reduction simultaneously, resulting in the formation of two different oxidation states of the same element..(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 272;Paragraph 8.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-272','11-244',NULL),
(221414,'chemistry','Assertion: Reduction potential is a measure of an element\'s tendency to gain electrons.                           Reason: Elements with higher reduction potentials have a greater tendency to undergo reduction.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The assertion is true and the reason is true. Reduction potential is a measure of an element\'s tendency to gain electrons, and elements with higher reduction potentials have a greater tendency to undergo reduction.\n\nElements with higher reduction potentials have a greater tendency to undergo reduction because they have a greater affinity for electrons. In other words, they are more likely to accept electrons and be reduced.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 266;Paragraph 8.2.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-266','11-238',NULL),
(221415,'chemistry','Assertion: The oxidation number of hydrogen is always +1 in all compounds.                    Reason: Hydrogen has a lower electronegativity compared to most elements.','The statement of the Assertion is false, but the Reason is true.','The assertion is false and the reason is false. The oxidation number of hydrogen can vary depending on the compound it is in, and hydrogen can have an oxidation number of -1 in some cases, such as in metal hydrides.\nHydrogen does have a lower electronegativity than most of the  elements, but there are also elements with lower electronegativities than hydrogen.\n(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 267;Paragraph 8.3)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','The statement of the Assertion is false, but the Reason is true.','NEW','11-267','11-239',NULL),
(221416,'chemistry','Assertion: Redox reactions always involve a change in the oxidation number of the reacting species.                              Reason: Change in oxidation number indicates a transfer of electrons.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The assertion is true and the reason is true. Redox reactions involve the transfer of electrons, resulting in a change in the oxidation number of the reacting species.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264;Paragraph 8.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(221417,'chemistry','Assertion: Reduction is the process of adding oxygen to a substance.                           Reason: Addition of oxygen increases the oxidation number of an element.','Both statements are false.','The assertion is false and the reason is false. Reduction is the process of gaining electrons, not adding oxygen, and addition of oxygen does not necessarily increase the oxidation number of an element.(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 264;Paragraph 8.1.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-264','11-236',NULL),
(221418,'chemistry','Assertion: Displacement reactions are redox reactions.                   Reason: Displacement reactions involve the transfer of ions between reactants.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The assertion is true and the reason is true. Displacement reactions involve the transfer of ions between reactants, which is a characteristic of redox reactions where a more reactive element displaces a less reactive element from its compound(Reference- NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 2); Page 270;Paragraph 8.3.1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Redox Reactions >> AR and Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion and the Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.','The statement of the Assertion is true, but the Reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','11-270','11-242',NULL),
(221501,'biology','Hormones produce their effects on target tissues by binding to specific proteins called-','Hormone receptors','Hormones produce their effects on target tissues by binding to specific proteins called hormone receptors located in the target tissues only..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 339;paragraph No.4).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Mechanism of Hormone Action',NULL,'3','mcq','Growth factors','Hormone receptors','Tissue receptors','Growth receptors','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221502,'biology','Hormone receptors present on the cell membrane of the target cells are:','Membrane-bound receptors, Hydrophilic','Hormone receptors present on the cell membrane of the target cells are called membrane-bound receptors and the receptors present inside the target cell are called intracellular receptors','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Mechanism of Hormone Action',NULL,'3','mcq','Membrane-bound receptors, Hydrophilic','Intra-cellular receptors, Hydrophobic','Nuclear receptors, Hydrophobic','Membrane-bound receptors, Hydrophobic','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221503,'biology','Hormone receptors present on the cell membrane of the target cells are called-','Membrane-bound receptors','Hormone receptors present on the cell membrane of the target cells are called membrane-bound receptors and the receptors present inside the target cell are called intracellular receptors (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 339;paragraph No.4).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Mechanism of Hormone Action',NULL,'3','mcq','Membrane-bound receptors','Intra-cellular receptors','Tissue receptors','Receptor TK','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221504,'biology','One receptor can bind with multiple hormones.','FALSE','Each receptor is specific to one hormone only and hence receptors are specific.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 339;paragraph No.4)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Mechanism of Hormone Action',NULL,'3','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221505,'biology','Hormone-Receptor complex formation leads to certain ___________ changes in the target tissue.','Biochemical','Hormone-Receptor complex formation leads to certain biochemical changes in the target tissue..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 339;paragraph No.4).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Mechanism of Hormone Action',NULL,'2','mcq','Structural','Biochemical','Physical','Mechanical','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
