(221601,'biology','Which one of the following statements is correct? (2006)','Endocrine glands regulate neural activity and nervous system regulates endocrine glands','The endocrine system links the brain to the organs that control body metabolism, growth and development, and reproduction. The endocrine system is regulated by feedback. For example, the hormones that are regulated by the pituitary gland, a signal is sent from the hypothalamus to the pituitary gland in the form of a “releasing hormone,” which stimulates the pituitary to secrete a “stimulating hormone” into the circulation. The stimulating hormone then signals the target gland to secrete its hormone. As the level of this hormone rises in the circulation, the hypothalamus and the pituitary gland shut down secretion of the releasing hormone and the stimulating hormone, which in turn slows the secretion by the target gland. This system results in stable blood concentrations of the hormones that are regulated by the pituitary gland.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Endocrine glands regulate neural activity, but not vice versa','Neurons regulate endocrine activity, but not vice versa','Endocrine glands regulate neural activity and nervous system regulates endocrine glands','Neither hormones control neural activity nor the neurons control endocrine activity','NEW','11-331','11-239',NULL),
(221602,'biology','Presence of which of the following conditions in urine are indicative of diabetes mellitus? [NEET 2020]','Ketonuria and Glycosuria','Diabetes mellitus is associated with formation of harmful compounds known as ketone bodies, i.e., ketonuria and loss of glucose through urine, i.e.,glycosuria..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 338;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Uremia and Ketonuria','Uremia and Renal Calculi','Ketonuria and Glycosuria','Renal calculi and Hyperglycaemia','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(221603,'biology','Select the correct statement. [NEET 2020]','Glucocorticoids stimulate gluconeogenesis.','Glucocorticoids also stimulates gluconeogenesis, lipolysis and proteolysis; and inhibit cellular uptake and utilisation of amino acids. Glucagon is associated with hyperglycemia while insulin is associated with hypoglycemia..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 337;paragraph No.4).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Glucocorticoids stimulate gluconeogenesis.','Glucagon is associated with hypoglycemia.','Insulin acts on pancreatic cells and adipocytes.','Insulin is associated with hyperglycemia.','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(260328,'chemistry','Commercial method of manufacture of benzaldehyde is by','Side chain chlorination of toluene','By side chain chlorination followed by hydrolysis Side chain chlorination of toluene gives benzal chloride, which on hydrolysis gives benzaldehyde. \n\nThis is a commercial method of manufacture of benzaldehyde. \n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','Gatterman-Koch reaction','Oxidation of methyl benzene','Reduction of nitriles and ester','Side chain chlorination of toluene','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(221604,'biology','Which of the following hormones can play a significant role in osteoporosis? [NEET 2018]','Estrogen and parathyroid hormone','Estrogen plays a central role in control of bone strength. In a healthy individual, bone mass is maintained by balanced activity of bone forming osteoblasts and bone resorbing osteoclasts. Both these cell types are reported to respond to estrogen. Estrogen promotes the activity of osteoblast and induces osteoclast apoptosis. After menopause the level of this hormone declines. Hence, in an ageing female osteoporosis occurs. Parathormone promotes mobilisation of calcium from bone into blood. Excessive activity of parathormone causes demineralisation of bone leading to osteoporosis. Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 338','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Aldosterone and prolactin','Progesterone and aldosterone','Estrogen and parathyroid hormone','Parathyroid hormone and prolactin','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(221605,'biology','GnRH, a hypothalamic hormone, needed in reproduction, acts on [NEET 2017]','Anterior pituitary gland and stimulates secretion of LH and FSH','Gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH) is secreted by the hypothalamus which stimulates the anterior lobe of pituitary gland to secrete luteinising hormone (LH) and Follicle Stimulating Hormone (FSH)..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 332;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Anterior pituitary gland and stimulates secretion of LH and FSH','Posterior pituitary gland and stimulates secretion of oxytocin and FSH','Posterior pituitary gland and stimulates secretion of LH and relaxin','Anterior pituitary gland and stimulates secretion of LH and oxytocin','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221606,'biology','Hypersecretion of growth hormone in adults does not cause further increase in height, because [NEET 2017]','Epiphyseal plates close after adolescence','Epiphyseal plate is a hyaline cartilage plate in the metaphysis at each end of long bone. It is part of long bone where new bone growth takes place. In adults, elevated levels of GH results in acromegaly where no increase in height occurs because of ossified epiphyseal plate.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Epiphyseal plates close after adolescence','Bones lose their sensitivity to growth hormone in adults','Muscle fibres do not grow in size after birth','Growth hormone becomes inactive in adults','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221607,'biology','A temporary endocrine gland in the human body is [NEET 2017]','Corpus luteum','Corpus luteum is a temporary endocrine gland in human females. It secretes progesterone which stimulates the uterine glands to produce increased amount of watery mucus and is also essential for maintenance of endothelium. In absence of fertilisation, corpus luteum disintegrate leading to menstruation.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Corpus cardiacum','Corpus luteum','Corpus allatum','Pineal gland','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221608,'biology','Graves’ disease is caused due to [NEET-II 2016]','Hypersecretion of thyroid gland','Exophthalmic goitre or Graves’ disease is a thyroid enlargement (goitre) in which the thyroid secretes excessive amount of thyroid hormone. It is characterised by exophthalmia (protrusion of eye balls because of fluid accumulation behind them), loss of weight, slightly rise in the body temperature, excitability, rapid heart beat, nervousness and restlessness..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 335;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Hyposecretion of thyroid gland','Hypersecretion of thyroid gland','Hyposecretion of adrenal gland','Hypersecretion of adrenal gland','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(221609,'biology','Name a peptide hormone which acts mainly on hepatocytes, adipocytes and enhances cellular glucose uptake and utilisation. [NEET-II 2016]','Insulin','Insulin is a peptide hormone, secreted by the β cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas, that promotes the uptake of glucose by body cells, particularly in the liver (hepatocytes) and muscles (adipocytes) and thereby controls its concentration in the blood. .(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 337;paragraph No.5).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Insulin','Glucagon','Secretin','Gastrin','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(221610,'biology','The posterior pituitary gland is not a ‘true’ endocrine gland because [NEET-II 2016]','It only stores and releases hormones','Posterior lobe of pituitary gland does not secrete any hormone. Its hormones are synthesised by the hypothalamus. It only stores and releases these hormones. Hence, it cannot be considered as true endocrine gland..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 333;paragraph No.1).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','It is provided with a duct','It only stores and releases hormones','It is under the regulation of hypothalamus','It secretes enzymes','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221611,'biology','Which of the following pairs of hormones are not antagonistic (having opposite effects) to each other? [NEET-I 2016]','Relaxin-Inhibin','Relaxin hormone is secreted by ovary and placenta during pregnancy, which relaxes ligaments in pelvis and softens and widens cervix during childbirth. Inhibin secreted by granulosa cells in the ovaries inhibits secretion of FSH by anterior pituitary. Thus, relaxin and inhibin have different functions and are not antagonistic.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Aldosterone-Atrial Natriuretic Factor','Relaxin-Inhibin','Parathormone-Calcitonin','Insulin-Glucagon','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(221612,'biology','Which one of the following hormones is not involved in sugar metabolism? [2015]','Aldosterone','Aldosterone (salt-retaining hormone) is the principal mineralocorticoid in humans, secreted by adrenal cortex. Its main function is to regulate sodium content of the body.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Insulin','Glucagon','Cortisone','Aldosterone','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(221613,'biology','Which one of the following hormones though synthesised elsewhere, is stored and released by the master gland? [2015]','Antidiuretic hormone','Two hormones viz oxytocin (OT) and antidiuretic hormone (ADH) are synthesised in the hypothalamus, but stored and released by the posterior lobe of pituitary gland..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 333;paragraph No.1).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Prolactin','Melanocyte stimulating hormone','Antidiuretic hormone','Luteinising hormone','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(121402,'chemistry','The IUPAC name for the compound is: Refer diagram(12142)','4-oxopentanoic acid','Since COOH has higher priority than ketone, therefore nomenclature is done keeping -COOH as the main chain.\n\n Since the carboxylic acid functional group (-COOH) is always considered as the highest priority functional group, the carboxylic acid group is located at the end of the parent chain and is indicated by the suffix "-oic acid". Therefore, the complete IUPAC name for the compound is "5-oxopentanoic acid".\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 345)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1-hydroxypentane-1,4-dione','1,4-dioxopentanol','1-carboxybutan-3-one','4-oxopentanoic acid','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121402q.png'),
(221614,'biology','Fight-or-flight reactions cause activation of [2014]','The adrenal medulla, leading to increased secretion of epinephrine and norepinephrine','Hormones epinephrine and norepinephrine are secreted from adrenal medulla. They are emergency hormones released in condition of stress, emergency, etc. Epinephrine and norepinephrine are also released by adrenergic nerve fibres of sympathetic nervous system where they act as neurotransmitters..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 336;paragraph No.3).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The parathyroid glands, leading to increased metabolic rate','The kidney, leading to suppression of reninangiotensin-aldosterone pathway','The adrenal medulla, leading to increased secretion of epinephrine and norepinephrine','The pancreas leading to a reduction in the blood sugar levels','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(221615,'biology','A pregnant female delivers a baby who suffers from stunted growth, mental retardation, low intelligence quotient and abnormal skin. This is the result of [NEET 2013]','Deficiency of iodine in diet','Iodine is needed for the synthesis of T3 and T4. Iodine binds to the tyrosine residues in thyroglobulin, which is then hydrolysed into iodotyrosines that combine to form triiodothyronine (T3) or thyroxine (tetraiodothyronine or T4). Therefore, deficiency of iodine in the diet of a pregnant female will lead to improper synthesis of thyroid hormones in newly borne infant. The deficiency of thyroid hormones in infants causes ‘cretinism’ whose symptoms are slow heart beat, lower blood pressure, decrease in temperature, stunted growth, low intelligence quotient and abnormal skin..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 334-335;paragraph No.3).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Cancer of the thyroid gland','Oversecretion of pars distalis','Deficiency of iodine in diet','Low secretion of growth hormone','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(221616,'biology','Nor-epinephrine\n(i) is released by sympathetic fibers \n(ii) is released by parasympathetic fibers \n(iii) increases the heart rate \n(iv) decreases blood pressure. \nWhich of the above statements are correct? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','(i) and (iii)','Nor-epinephrine is secreted by some neurons of the sympathetic nervous system and also by adrenal medulla. It accelerates heart rate..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 336;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (iii)','(ii) and (iii)','(ii) and (iv)','(i) and (iv)','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(221617,'biology','Which of the following represents the action of insulin? [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Decreases blood glucose levels by forming glycogen.','Insulin is a peptide hormone, which plays a major role in the regulation of glucose homeostasis. Insulin acts mainly on hepatocytes and adipocytes (cells of adipose tissue), and enhances cellular glucose uptake and utilisation. As a result, there is a rapid movement of glucose from blood to hepatocytes and adipocytes resulting in decreased blood glucose levels (hypoglycemia). Insulin also stimulates conversion of glucose to glycogen (glycogenesis) in the target cells. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 337-338;paragraph No.5).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Increases blood glucose level by stimulating glucagon production.','Decreases blood glucose levels by forming glycogen.','Increases blood glucose levels by promoting cellular uptake of glucose.','Increases blood glucose levels by hydrolysis of glycogen.','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(221618,'biology','A person entering an empty room suddenly finds a snake right in front on opening the door. Which one of the following is likely to happen in his neurohormonal control system? [2012]','Sympathetic nervous system is activated releasing epinephrine and norepinephrine from adrenal medulla.','Adrenaline and noradrenaline are rapidly secreted in response to stress of any kind and during emergency situations and are called emergency hormones or hormones of Fight or Flight.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 336;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Sympathetic nervous system is activated releasing epinephrine and norepinephrine from adrenal medulla.','Neurotransmitters diffuse rapidly across the cleft and transmit a nerve impulse.','Hypothalamus activates the parasympathetic division of brain.','Sympathetic nervous system is activated releasing epinephrine and norepinephrine from adrenal cortex.','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(221619,'biology','The 24 hour (diurnal) rhythm of our body such as the sleep-wake cycle is regulated by the hormone [Mains 2011]','Melatonin','Melatonin is a hormone secreted by the pineal gland and retinas of vertebrates. Melatonin secretion by the pineal gland is linked to the dark-light cycle of the organism’s environment, being greatest at night and lowest by day. The hormone is involved in regulating certain diurnal and seasonal changes in the body, such as the reproductive cycle in seasonally breeding animals. It is used as a drug to treat sleep disorders and symptoms of jet lag.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 342;paragraph No.1).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Calcitonin','Prolactin','Adrenaline','Melatonin','NEW','11-342','11-250',NULL),
(221620,'biology','Injury to adrenal cortex is not likely to affect the secretion of which one of the following? [2010]','Adrenaline','Adrenal glands or (suprarenal glands) are two triangular endocrine glands, each of which covers the superior surface of a kidney. Each gland has two parts, the medulla and cortex. The medulla forms the grey core of the gland; it consists mainly of chromaffin tissue and is stimulated by the sympathetic nervous system to produce adrenaline and noradrenaline. The cortex is a yellowish tissue surrounding the medulla. It is stimulated by pituitary hormones (principally ACTH) to produce three kinds of corticosteroid hormones, which affect carbohydrate metabolism (e.g., cortisol), electrolyte metabolism (e.g., aldosterone), and the sex glands (estrogens and androgens). Thus injury to adrenal cortex is not likely to affect the secretion of adrenaline..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 336;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Aldosterone','Both androstenedione and dehydroepiandrosterone','Adrenaline','Cortisol','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(221621,'biology','Which one of the following pairs is incorrectly matched? [2010]','Glucagon – Beta cells (source)','Glucagon is a hormone, secreted by the a-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas, that increases the concentration of glucose in the blood by stimulating the metabolic breakdown of glycogen. It thus antagonizes the effects of insulin.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Glucagon – Beta cells (source)','Somatostatin – Delta cells (source)','Corpus luteum – Relaxin (secretion)','Insulin – Diabetes mellitus (disease)','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(2000034244,'physics','The value of acceleration due to gravity at a depth of 1600 $km$ is equal to [Radius of earth 6100 $km$','C','The acceleration due to gravity at a depth below the earth\'s surface is,<br>${g_d} = g\\left( {1 - \\frac{d}{{{R_e}}}} \\right) = 9.8\\left( {1 - \\frac{{1600}}{{6400}}} \\right)$<br>$ = 9.8 \\times 0.75 = 7.35\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$','Acceleration Due to Gravity of the Earth','Gravitation','Medium',NULL,'$9.8\\,m\\,{s^{ - 2}}$','$4.9\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$','$7.35\\,m\\,{s^{ - 2}}$','$19.6\\,m\\,{s^{ - 2}}$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221622,'biology','Erythropoietin hormone which stimulates RBC formation is produced by [NEET 2021]','Juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney','Erythropoietin (EPO) is produced by the kidney and used to make red blood cells. Erythropoetin-stimulating agents are used often for people with long-term kidney disease and anemia.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney','Alpha cells of pancreas','The cells of rostral adenohypophysis','The cells of bone marrow','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221701,'biology','Toxic agents present in food which interfere with thyroxine synthesis lead to the development of [2010]','Simple goitre','Simpe goitre is due to decreased synthesis of Thyroid which may be due to either toxic agents in food or other multifactrial onset.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Toxic goitre','Cretinism','Simple goitre','Thyrotoxicosis','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221702,'biology','A health disorder that results from the deficiency of thyroxine in adults and characterised by \n(i) a low metabolic rate\n(ii) increase in body weight and \n(iii) tendency to retain water in tissues is [2009]','Myxoedema','Myxoedema is caused by deficiency of thyroid hormone or thyroxine in adults. It is characterised by low metabolic rate, body gain, puffy appearance, low body temperature, etc. This disease can be treated by administration of thyroid hormones.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Simple goitre','Myxoedema','Cretinism','Hypothyroidism','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221703,'biology','Which one of the following pair of organs includes only the endocrine glands? [2008]','Parathyroid and adrenal','Parathyroid and adrenal glands are the endocrine glands because they manufacture hormones and secretes them directly into the blood stream to act at distant sites in the body. Thyroid and pituitary are its other examples.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Thymus and testes','Adrenal and ovary','Parathyroid and adrenal','Pancreas and parathyroid','NEW','11-332','11-240',NULL),
(221704,'biology','The blood calcium level is lowered by the deficiency of [2008]','Parathormone','Parathormone is secreted by chief cells of the parathyroid that regulates the metabolism of calcium and phosphate. It increases calcium absorption from the intestine and also increases calcium resorption from the nephrons of the kidneys. So, its deficiency leads to low blood calcium level.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 335;paragraph No.4).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both calcitonin and parathormone','Calcitonin','Parathormone','thyroxine','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(221705,'biology','Feeling the tremors of an earthquake a scared resident of seventh floor of a multistoryed building starts climbing down the stairs rapidly. Which hormone initiated this action? [2007]','Adrenaline','Adrenaline (epinephrine), also called emergency hormone, is a hormone, produced by the medulla of the adrenal glands, that increases heart activity, improves the power and prolongs the action of muscles and increases the rate and depth of breathing to prepare the body for ‘fright, flight or fight’. At the same time it inhibits digestion and excretion. Similar effects are produced by stimulation of the sympathetic nervous system..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 336;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Adrenaline','Glucagon','Gastrin','Thyroxine','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(221706,'biology','A person is having problems with calcium and phosphorus metabolism in his body. Which one of the following glands may not be functioning properly? [2007]','Parathyroid','Parathormone is secreted by chief cells of the parathyroid that regulates the metabolism of calcium and phosphate. It increases calcium absorption from the intestine and also increases calcium resorption from the nephrons of the kidneys. So, its deficiency leads to low blood calcium level.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 335;paragraph No.4).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Parotid','Pancreas','Thyroid','Parathyroid','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(221707,'biology','Which hormone causes dilation of blood vessels, increased O₂ consumption and glucogenesis? [2006]','Adrenaline','Adrenaline is the hormone secreted by adrenal medulla. It prepares the animal to face special conditions created by physical stress. All these conditions require more energy which is provided by increasing heart beat, blood pressure, respiratory rate, sugar level of blood, blood supply of heart and skeletal muscles and brain through dilation of their small arteries and oxidative metabolism. It also stimulates the breakdown of liver and  muscle.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 336-337;paragraph No.4)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Glucagon','ACTH','Insulin','Adrenaline','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(221708,'biology','Which of the following is an accumulation site and release centre of neurohormones? [2006]','Posterior pituitary lobe','Hypothalamus is the site of Synthesis, but the neurohormones are passed to the posterior lobe through the hypophyseal tract and get accumulated and released from there;','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Anterior pituitary lobe','Posterior pituitary lobe','Intermediate lobe of the pituitary','Hypothalamus','NEW','11-332','11-240',NULL),
(221709,'biology','A steroid hormone which regulates glucose metabolism is [2006]','Cortisol','In our body, cortisol is the main glucocorticoid. Corticoids, which regulate the balance of water and electrolytes in our body are called mineralocorticoids.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 337;paragraph No.1).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Cortisone','Cortisol','Corticosterone','11-deoxycorticosterone','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(221710,'biology','Which one of the following pairs correctly matches a hormone with a disease resulting from its deficiency? [2004]','Luteinising Hormone- Failure of ovulation','Ovulation occurs under the influence of LH (luteinising hormone) and FSH (follicle stimulating hormone) of anterior pituitary gland. Thus, deficiency of luteinizing hormone results in failure of ovulation.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Luteinising Hormone- Failure of ovulation','Insulin - Diabetes insipidus','Thyroxine - Tetany','Parathyroid - Diabetes mellitus hormone','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221711,'biology','Which one of the following pairs correctly matches a hormone with a disease resulting from its deficiency ? [2003]','Parathyroid hormone – Tetany','Tetany is usually caused by reduction in the blood calcium level, which may be due to underactive parathyroid glands.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Relaxin – Gigantism','Prolactin – Cretinism','Parathyroid hormone – Tetany','Insulin – Diabetes insipidus','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(2000034272,'physics','The energy that will be ideally radiated by a 100 $k W$ transmitter in 1 hour is','D','$\\mathrm{E}=\\mathrm{P} \\times \\mathrm{t}=100 \\times 10^{3} \\times 3600$<br>$=36 \\times 10^{7} J$','Power','Work, Energy and Power','Easy',NULL,'$36 \\times {10^4}\\,J$','$36 \\times 10^{5} J$','$1 \\times {10^5}\\,J$','$36 \\times {10^7}\\,J$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221712,'biology','Acromegaly is caused by [2002]','Excess of STH','Acromegaly is caused by excess of STH (somatotrophic hormone), released by anterior lobe of pituitary after adolescence. The bones of the lower jaw and limbs become abnormally enlarge but the body does not attain a giant stature. Excess of thyroxine causes cretinism and myxoedema. Excess of adrenaline causes increased BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate), heart beat, excitement, etc.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 333;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Excess of STH','Excess of thyroxine','Deficiency of thyroxine','Excess of adrenaline','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221713,'biology','Adrenaline directly affects on [2002]','SA node','Adrenaline directly affects the SA node to increase rate of heartbeat. Adrenaline prepares the body for emergency reactions like fight and flight. Thus there is increase in heart rate, breathing rate, blood pressure, glucose level in blood, peripheral circulation, etc..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 336;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','SA node','β-cells of Langerhans','Dorsal root of spinal nerve','Epithelial cells of stomach','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(221714,'biology','When both ovaries are removed from rat then which hormone is decreased in blood? [2002]','Estrogen','Ovary secretes two hormones. Estrogen before ovulation and progesterone after ovulation. Oxytocin, prolaction are pituitary hormones and gonadotropin releasing factor is secreted by hypothalamus of brain to stimulate pituitary for the secretion of gonadotropic hormones..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 338;paragraph No.5).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Oxytocin','Prolactin','Estrogen','Gonadotropin releasing factor','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(221715,'biology','Mainly which type of hormones control the menstrual cycle in human beings? [2002]','FSH, LH, estrogen','Menstrual cycle is controlled by several endocrinal parameters. In beginning of the cycle FSH (follicle stimulating hormone) of pituitary initiates development of an ovarian follicle. A growing ovarian follicle gradually secretes increasing amount of estrogen. This in turn leads to sudden surge of LH secretion by the pituitary. As the LH (luteinising hormone) level in blood suddenly increases there is ovulation. Thus only FSH or LH cannot control all the events of menstrual cycle. Progesterone is released by a corpus luteum after ovulation which actually prepares the uterus for a possible pregnancy','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','FSH','LH','FSH, LH, estrogen','progesterone','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(221716,'biology','Melatonin is secreted by [2000]','Pineal body','Pineal gland secretes melatonin, which plays a very important role in the regulation of 24-hour (diurnal) rhythms.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 334;paragraph No.3).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Pineal body','Skin','Pituitary gland','Thyroid','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221717,'biology','MSH is secreted by [2000]','Middle lobe of pituitary','Middle lobe of pituitary secretes a hormone named melanocyte-stimulating hormone.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 333;paragraph No.1).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Anterior lobe of pituitary','Middle lobe of pituitary','Posterior lobe of pituitary','Endostyle','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221718,'biology','Secretion of progesterone by corpus luteum is initiated by [1999]','LH','Luteinising Hormone (LH) in the male, induces the interstitial cells of the testes to produce male sex hormones named androgens such as testosterone. In the female, the luteinising hormone causes ovulation, secretion of female sex hormone, estrogen from the maturing ovarian follicle and progesterone by the corpus luteum.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Testosterone','Thyroxine','MSH','LH','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(221719,'biology','The gonadotrophic hormones are secreted by [1999]','Anterior lobe of pituitary','Gonadotrophic hormones are secreted by anterior lobe of pituitary gland. They are as follows: (i) Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH): It stimulates growth of ovarian follicles and their secretion of estrogens in the female and spermatogenesis (formation of sperms) in the male. (ii) Interstitial cell stimulating hormone (ICSH): It activates the Leydig’s (interstitial) cells of the testis to secrete androgens. In female, it stimulates the corpus luteum of the ovary to secrete progesterone. In female it is termed luteinising hormone (LH)..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 333;paragraph No.1).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Anterior lobe of pituitary','Interstitial cells of testes','Adrenal cortex','Posterior part of thyroid','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221720,'biology','Diabetes is due to [1999]','Hormonal deficiency','Diabetes mellitus is caused by the deficiency of hormone insulin which is secreted by pancreas. Insulin lowers the blood-glucose level. Deficiency of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) leads to diabetes insipidus..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 338;paragraph No.1).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Enzyme deficiency','Iodine deficiency','Na+ deficiency','Hormonal deficiency','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(221721,'biology','Calcitonin is a thyroid hormone which [1998]','Lowers calcium level in blood','Thyrocalcitonin regulates calcium levels in our blood by decreasing it.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 342;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Elevates calcium level in blood','Has no effect on calcium','Elevates potassium level in blood','Lowers calcium level in blood','NEW','11-342','11-250',NULL),
(221722,'biology','The hormone which regulates the basal metabolism in our body is secreted from [1998]','Thyroid','The basal metabolism is the minimum amount of energy the body uses in order to maintain vital processes of the body. Generally, this expenditure of energy is expressed in terms of heat production per unit of body surface per day on the basal metabolic rate (BMR). Thyroid is the largest endocrine gland secreting three hormones : thyroxine, triiodothyronine and calcitonin. Thyroxine and triiodothyronine control BMR of the body by regulating the rate of oxidation and production of energy.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 335;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Adrenal cortex','Pancreas','Pituitary','Thyroid','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(221723,'biology','Which hormone stimulates the secretion of milk from female? [1996]','Prolactin','Prolactin hormone stimulates the growth of milk glands during pregnancy and the secretion of milk after delivery. Oxytocin causes release of milk during sucking by the infant. LH causes ovulation and secretion of estrogen and progesterone from ovarian follicle and corpus luteum respectively. Oxytocin, LH and prolactin are released by anterior lobe of pituitary gland. Progesterone is secreted by corpus luteum.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Oxytocin','Progesterone','LH','Prolactin','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221724,'biology','The immediate cause of induction of ovulation in human female is the large plasma surge of [1994]','LH','Prolactin hormone stimulates the growth of milk glands during pregnancy and the secretion of milk after delivery. Oxytocin causes release of milk during sucking by the infant. LH causes ovulation and secretion of estrogen and progesterone from ovarian follicle and corpus luteum respectively. Oxytocin, LH and prolactin are released by anterior lobe of pituitary gland. Progesterone is secreted by corpus luteum.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','LH','FSH','Progesterone','Estradiol','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221725,'biology','Testosterone is produced by [1993]','Leydig’s cells','The Leydig cells or interstitial cells, which are present in the intertubular spaces produce a group of hormones called androgens mainly testosterone.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 338;paragraph No.3).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Sertoli cells','Leydig’s cells','Oxyntic cells','Pituitary gland','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(221726,'biology','ADH or vasopressin is [1991]','Hormone secreted by pituitary that promotes reabsorption of water from glomerular filtrate','Vasopressin acts mainly at the kidney and stimulates resorption of water and electrolytes by the distal tubules and thereby reduces loss of water through urine (diuresis). Hence, it is also called as anti-diuretic hormone (ADH)(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 334;paragraph No.1).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Enzyme that hydrolyses peptides','Hormone secreted by pituitary that promotes reabsorption of water from glomerular filtrate','Hormone that promotes glycogenolysis','Energy rich compound connected with muscle contraction','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221727,'biology','Leydig cells are found in _____ and their secretion is _____ [1991]','Testis and testosterone','Testis is composed of seminiferous tubules and stromal or interstitial tissue. The Leydig cells or interstitial cells, which are present in the intertubular spaces produce a group of hormones called androgens mainly testosterone(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 338;paragraph No.3).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Ovary and estrogen','Liver and cholesterol','Pancreas and glucagon','Testis and testosterone','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(221728,'biology','Insulin is a [1990]','Hormone','Insulin is a peptide hormone, secreted by the β cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas. It plays a major role in the regulation of glucose homeostasis..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 337;paragraph No.5).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Vitamin','Lipid','Hormone','Enzyme','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(221729,'biology','Hormones thyroxine, adrenaline and the pigment melanin are formed from [1997]','Tyrosine','Amino-acid derivatives (e.g., epinephrine). .(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 340;paragraph No.1).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Tyrosine','Proline','Tryptophan','Glycine','NEW','11-340','11-248',NULL),
(221730,'biology','Which one of the following endocrine glands stores its secretion in the extracellular space before discharging it into the blood? [1995]','Thyroid','The thyroid gland secretes three hormones: thyroxine or tetraiodothyronine (T4), triiodothyronine (T3) and calcitonin. Thyroxine and Triiodothyronine are iodinated forms of the amino acid tyrosine. They are stored in the colloid that fills the follicles and are released to the blood when needed. The storage occurs in an unusual place, the extracellular colloid..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 3;paragraph No.1).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Testis','Thyroid','Pancreas','Adrenal','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221731,'biology','According to the accepted concept of hormone action, if receptor molecules are removed from target organs, then the target organ will [1995]','Not respond to the hormone','The molecules of hormones that are amino acid derivatives, peptides or proteins are large and insoluble in lipids and cannot enter the target cell. Therefore, they act at the cell surface. They bind to specific receptor molecules located on the surface of the cell membrane. Therefore, if receptor molecules are removed from target organs, then the target organ will not respond to the hormone.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Continue to respond to the hormone without any difference','Not respond to the hormone','Continue to respond to the hormone but will require higher concentration','Continue to respond to the hormone but in the opposite way','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221732,'biology','Which of the following are not the effects of Parathyroid hormone?\n(a) Stimulates the process of bone resorption\n(b) Decreases Ca2+ level in blood\n(c) Reabsorption of Ca2+ by renal tubules \n(d) Decreases the absorption of Ca2+ from digested food\n(e) Increases metabolism of carbohydrates \nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','(b), (d) and (e) only','Parathyroid increases Calcium level in the blood by Bone resorption, increased absorption from the gut and reabsorption from kidneys and has no effects on carbohydrate metabolism','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) and (c) only','(b), (d) and (e) only','(a) and (e) only','(b) and (c) only','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(221733,'biology','Given below are two statements : \n Statement I :\n Parathyroid hormone acts on bones and stimulates the process of bone resorption. \nStatement II :\n Parathyroid hormone along with Thyrocalcitonin plays a significant role in carbohydrate metabolism. \n In the light of the above statements, choose the \ncorrect answer from the options given below :[ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(1) Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I: Parathyroid hormone acts on bones and stimulates the process of bone resorption. This statement is correct. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) is responsible for regulating calcium and phosphate levels in the body. One of its main functions is to stimulate bone resorption, which involves the breakdown of bone tissue to release calcium into the bloodstream.\n\nStatement II: Parathyroid hormone along with Thyrocalcitonin plays a significant role in carbohydrate metabolism. This statement is false. Parathyroid hormone and thyrocalcitonin (also known as calcitonin) are involved in calcium metabolism and do not have a significant role in carbohydrate metabolism.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is 1. Statement I is correct, but statement II is false.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','(2) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are false','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(2000031830,'physics','The dimensions of $\\dfrac{1}{2} \\varepsilon_{0} E^{2}$ ( $\\varepsilon_{0}$ is permittivity of free space, $E$ is electric field) are','C','Quantity $\\dfrac{1}{2} \\varepsilon_{0} E^{2}$ is the electrostatic energy per unit volume.<br>$\\therefore$ Its dimensions $=\\dfrac{\\left[M L^{2} T^{-2}\\right]}{\\left[L^{3}\\right]}$<br>$=\\left[M L^{-1} T^{-2}\\right]$','Dimensions','Units & Measurements','Medium',NULL,'$\\left[M L T^{-1}\\right]$','$\\left[M L^{2} T^{-3}\\right]$','$\\left[M L^{-1} T^{-2}\\right]$','$\\left[M L^{2} T^{-1}\\right]$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221734,'biology','Which of the following statements are correct with respect to the hormone and its function ? \n (A) Thyrocalcitonin (TCT) regulates the blood calcium level.  \n(B) In males, FSH and androgens regulate spermatogenesis. \n (C) Hyperthyroidism can lead to goitre. \n (D) Glucocorticoids are secreted in Adrenal Medulla. \n (E) Parathyroid hormone is regulated by circulating levels of sodium ions. \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :[ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) (A) and (B) only','(A) Thyrocalcitonin (TCT) does regulate the blood calcium level by decreasing its concentration. It inhibits the release of calcium from bones and promotes its excretion by the kidneys.\n\n(B) In males, follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and androgens (such as testosterone) do regulate spermatogenesis. FSH stimulates the production of sperm cells in the testes, while androgens play a role in the maturation and development of sperm.\n\n(C) Hyperthyroidism is a condition characterized by excessive production of thyroid hormones. It does not lead to goiter. Goiter is commonly associated with hypothyroidism, which is an underactive thyroid gland.\n\n(D) Glucocorticoids are secreted in the adrenal cortex, not the adrenal medulla. The adrenal medulla secretes catecholamines such as adrenaline (epinephrine) and noradrenaline (norepinephrine).\n\n(E) Parathyroid hormone (PTH) is regulated by the levels of calcium ions in the blood, not sodium ions. When blood calcium levels are low, PTH is released to increase calcium reabsorption from the kidneys and stimulate the release of calcium from bones.\n\nTherefore, option 2, which includes statements (A) and (B) only, is the correct choice.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(2) (A) and (B) only','(4) (A) and (D) only','(3) (B) and (C) only','(1) (C) and (E) only','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(221735,'biology','Which of the following are NOT under the control of thyroid hormone?\nA. Maintenance of water and electrolyte balance\nB. Regulation of basal metabolic rate\nC. Normal rhythm of sleep-wake cycle\nD. Development of immune system\nE. Support the process of RBCs formation\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(2) C and D only','Regulation of basal metabolic rate is function of thyroid hormone. C. Sleep-wake cycle is maintain by melotonine hormone of pineal gland.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) B and C only','(2) C and D only','(3) D and E only','(4) A and D only','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221736,'biology','Given below are two statements : one is labelled as \nAssertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason \n(R). \nAssertion (A) : \n FSH which interacts with membrane bound receptors does not enter the target cell. \n Reason (R) : \n Binding of FSH to its receptors generates second \nmessenger (cyclic AMP) for its biochemical and physiological responses. \n In the light of the above statements, choose the\nmost appropriate answer from the options given \nbelow ;','(2) Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Assertion (A) is correct because FSH, or follicle-stimulating hormone, is a peptide hormone that does not enter the target cell. Instead, it interacts with membrane-bound receptors on the surface of the target cell. This is a common feature of peptide hormones, as they are too large and hydrophilic to pass through the cell membrane.\n\nReason (R) is also correct. When FSH binds to its receptor, it triggers a cascade of intracellular events, including the production of second messengers like cyclic AMP (cAMP). The second messengers then go on to activate various biochemical and physiological responses within the target cell, such as cell growth and hormone production. This is a typical process for signal transduction initiated by the binding of a hormone to its cell surface receptor.\n\nHowever, (R) is not the correct explanation of (A) because it doesn\'t directly address why FSH doesn\'t enter the target cell. The reason FSH does not enter the target cell is that it is a peptide hormone, which cannot cross the cell membrane. Instead, it interacts with cell surface receptors to initiate a signaling cascade. The generation of second messengers, like cAMP, is a part of the signaling process that takes place after FSH binds to its receptor, but it is not the reason why FSH doesn\'t enter the cell.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the \ncorrect explanation of (A)','(2) Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(3) (A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(4) (A) is not correct but (R) is correc','NEW','11-340','11-248',NULL),
(221801,'biology','To yield more milk, cow is injected with [1997]','Prolactin','Prolactin (also called leuteotrophic) hormone is associated with lactation (secretion of milk from the mammary gland). Therefore, to yield more milk cow is injected with prolactin hormone. Sorbitol is a sweetner found in some fruits (like apple, peers etc). Gonadotrophs a type of basophil in the adenohypohysis (anterior pituitary gland) whose granules secrete FSH (follicele-stimulating hormone) and LH (luteinizing hormone). Stilbesterol is synthetic estrogen used in hormone therapy, as a post-coital contraceptive, and as a growth-promoting agent for livestock.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sorbitol','Prolactin','Gonadotrophs','Stilbesterol','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221802,'biology','Treatment with alloxan destroys [1998]','E-cells of Langerhans','Alloxan treatment damages E-cells of Islets of langerhans which leads to hyperglycaemia and glycosuria.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Stilt cells','E-cells of Langerhans','Sertoli cells','Cells of Leydig','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221803,'biology','Addison\'s disease result from [1998]','Hyposecretion of adrenal gland','Addisons disease occurs due to hyposecretion of both mineralocorticoids (aldosterone) and glucocorticoids (cortisol) from the layers of adrenal cortex called zona glomerulosa and zona fasciculata respectively. The disease is characterized by excessive loss of Na+ , Cl– and HCO3 increased K+ level in blood, weight loss, muscle weakness, fatigue, low blood pressure, and sometimes darkening of the skin in both exposed and nonexposed parts of the body.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 336;paragraph No.2)..','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Hyposecretion of gonads','Hyposecretion of adrenal gland','Hypertrophy of kidney','Hyposecretion of pituitary gland','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(221804,'biology','The development of adult characteristics in a moulting insect is promoted by [1998]','Ecdysone','Ecdysone is a moulting hormone of insects. Ecdysone is produced from prothoracic gland that triggers moulting and metamorphosis. Thyroxine is thyroid hormone that stimulates body metabolism and helps regulate body growth and development. Pheromone is a chemical screted by an animal that influences the behavior or development of other members of the same species.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Thyroxine','Ecdysone','Pheromone','None of the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121403,'chemistry','The IUPAC name for the compound is: Refer diagram(12143)','1-chloro-4-methyl-2-nitrobenzene','Benzene ring as the parent structure, with a nitro group (-NO2) and a chlorine atom as substituents. The nitro group is located at the 2-position, and the chlorine atom is located at the 1-position. The methyl group is located at the 4-position of the benzene ring. Therefore, the name of the compound follows the IUPAC naming conventions, and the complete name of the compound is "1-chloro-4-methyl-2-nitrobenzene".\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 345)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1-chloro-2-nitro-4-methylbenzene','1-chloro-4-methyl-2-nitrobenzene','2-chloro-1-nitro-5-methylbenzene','m-nitro-p-chlorotoluene','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121403q.png'),
(221805,'biology','Heart beat increases at the time of interview because of [1999]','Adrenaline','Adrenaline (amine hormone) is secreted by adrenal medulla on stimulation of sympathetic nervous system for meeting an emergency or stress condition like fear injury accident etc. Hence, it also called as emergency hormone. It increases blood pressure, respiration rate, sugar level in blood etc. It prepares the body to face stress at the time of interview by increasing heart beat. So it is also called stress hormone. \nRenin is proteolytic enzyme synthesized,stored, and secreted by the juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney. It plays a role in regulation of blood pressure by catalyzing the conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I and II which in turn stimulates the release aldosterone from adrenal gland. \nRennin, a coagulating enzyme produced from the stomach of human body, catalyzes the coagulation of milk by converting milk protein, caesin into paracaesinate. \nADH (antidiuretic hormone) is secreted by the posterior portion of the pituitary gland that constricts blood vessels raises blood pressure, and reduces excretion of urine.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Renin','Rennin','Adrenaline','Diuretic ADH','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(221806,'biology','Conn’s disease is caused by the over secretion of [1999]','Aldosterone','Conn\'s syndrome/aldosteronism is due to hypersecretion of aldosterone. Aldosetrone is secreted from zona glomerulosa layer of adrenal cortex. The principle action of the aldosterone is retention of sodium. Conn’s syndrome is characterized by rise in blood volume and blood pressure; muscular weakness; high sodium and low potassium level in the blood plasma resulting in kidney damage with polyuria and tetany and metabolic disorder','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','ADH','ACTH','Oxytocin','Aldosterone','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221807,'biology','Acromegaly is due to hypersecretion of [2001]','Growth hormone','Excess secretion of growth hormone in adults especially in middle age can result in severe disfigurement (especially of the face) called Acromegaly.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 333;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Insulin','Thyroxine','Growth hormone','None of the above','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221808,'biology','Steroid hormones are similar in structure to','Cholesterol','Steroid hormones are fat soluble and have sterol group. They are derived from cholesterol, e.g. hormones of adrenal cortex, testis and ovaries. Amine hormones are derived form tyrosine amino acids and have amino group, e.g. thyroxine, epinephrine and nor epinephrine. Coenzyme A is essential to metabolism of carbohydrates and fats and some amino acids. Glycerol is a clear, colorless, viscous, sweet-tasting liquid organic compound of the alcohol family.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Tryosine','Cholesterol','Coenzyme A','Glycerol','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221809,'biology','A person passes much urine and drinks much water but his blood glucose level normal. This condition may be the result of [2003]','A reduction in vasopressin secretion from posterior pituitary','When a person passes more urine and drinks more water, he is suffering from diabetes insipidus. It is caused by reduction in vasopressin (ADH) secretion from post pituitary gland..(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 334;paragraph No.2).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A reduction in insulin secretion from pancreas','A reduction in vasopressin secretion from posterior pituitary','A fall in the glucose concentration in urine','An increase in secretion of glucagon','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221810,'biology','The source of somatostatin is same as that of [2003]','Insulin and glucagon','The source of somatostatin is same as that of insulin and glucagons. All these hormones are secreted from endocrine part (called Islets of Langerhans) of pancreas. These endocrine parts contain different types of endorcrine cells which secretes different hormones.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Thyroxine and calcitonin','Insulin and glucagon','Somatotropin and prolactin','Vasopressin and oxytocin','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221811,'biology','Which endocrine gland is called ‘The Throne of immunity’? [2016]','Thymus','Thymus stimulates T-cells which regulate the production of antibodies.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Spleen','Thymus','Pineal','Adrenal medulla','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221812,'biology','Which of the following hormone acts upon the renal tubule and blood capillaries ? [2017]','Vasopressin','ADH (or vasopressin) is secreted by posterior pituitary gland. It acts on kidney tubule and blood capillaries and concentrates the urine by promoting the reabsorption of water and salts into the cortical collecting ducts.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 11;page 334;paragraph No.1).','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Glucagon','Aldosterone','Vasopressin','Glucocorticoids','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221813,'biology','Assertion : Mammary glands are apocrine glands.\nReason : The distal part containing secretory granules break down and leaves as a secretion. [2009]','Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Based on the mode of secretion, the glands are of three types : mesocrine, apocrine and holocrine. Mammary glands that are present in mammals to feed the young ones with milk are the example of apocrine glands. In apocrine glands, the secretion accumulates as secretory granules in the distal part of the cell. This part later breaks down and leaves as a secretion.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(221814,'biology','Assertion : Hormone calcitonin has antagonistic effect to that of parathormone.\nReason : Calcitonin decreases blood calcium level while parathormone increases blood calcium level. [2009]','Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Calcitonin or thyrocalcitonin is secreted by parafollicular cells of thyroid stroma. It retards bone dissolution and stimulates excretion of calcium in urine. Thus, it lowers calcium level in extra cellular fluid (ECF). Parathormone is secreted by chief cells of parathyroid gland and is also known as Collip\'s hormone. It maintains blood calcium level by increasing its absorption from food in intestine and its reabsorption from nephrons in the kidney. Maintenance of proper calcium level is in fact, a combined function of parathormone and calcitonin. When calcium level falls below normal parathormone maintains it by promoting its absorption, reabsorption and also by demineralisation of bones. When blood calcium level exceeds above normal then calcitonin hormone increases excretion of calcium in urine','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(221815,'biology','Assertion :A person with diabetes insipidus feels thirsty. \nReason : A person with diabetes insipidus suffers from excess secretion of vasopressin. [2010]','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Vasopressin or antidiuretic hormone is secreted by posterior pituitary gland. The deficiency of vassopressin results in a disorder known as diabetes insipidus. The main symptoms of diabetes insipidus are increase in thirst and increase in urination.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221816,'biology','Assertion: Failure of secretion of hormone vasopressin causes diabetes mellitus in the patient. \nReason: Vasopressin increases the volume of urine by increasing the reabsorption of water from the urine [2011]','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','Vasopressin or anti-diuretic hormone (ADH) reduces the volume of urine by increasing the reabsorption of water from the urine in the distal convoluted tubules, collecting tubules and collecting ducts in the kidney. It does so by rendering the walls of these tubules leads to diabetes insipidus (increased urination). Although the volume of urine is increased. No glucose appears in the urine of such patients. Diabetes mellitus is a disease which is caused due to the failure of insulin hormone secretion by the pancreatic islets. The osmotic effect of glucose in the urine considerably increases the volume of urine, due to which thirst is also enhanced. In extreme cases, the patient suffers from coma and may die.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','The Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221817,'biology','How many statements are correct regarding Hormones? \ni. They are non nutrient substances \nii. They are released in copious amount \niii. They act as Intercllular mesengers \niv. They re released into blood','3','Hormones are non-nutrient chemicals which act as intercellular messengers and are produced in trace amounts.  Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 339','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221818,'biology','Hormones act as Intracellular messengers.','FALSE','Hormones are non-nutrient chemicals which act as intercellular messengers and are produced in trace amounts.  Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 331','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-331','11-239',NULL),
(221819,'biology','Grave\'s disease is characterized by:\ni.Hyperthyroidism \nii. Eye ball retraction \niii.Increased BMR \niv. Weight loss','i,iii and iv','Exopthalmic goitre is a form of hyperthyroidism, characterised by enlargement of the thyroid gland, protrusion of the eyeballs, increased basal metabolic rate, and weight loss, also called Graves’ disease.  Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 335','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i,ii and iii','i,ii and iv','i and iii only','i,iii and iv','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(221820,'biology','The hormones released by the Thyroid Gland are:','T3, T4 and Calcitonin','These follicular cells synthesise two hormones, tetraiodothyronine or thyroxine (T4) andtriiodothyronine (T3). Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 339','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','T3 and T4 only','T3, T4 and Calcitonin','Calcitonin and Parathormone','Thyroid and Parathormone','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221821,'biology','Which one of the following is involved in Lowering Plasma Ca levels','Calcitonin only','Thyrocalcitonin regulates calcium levels in our blood by decreasing it Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 342','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Calcitonin only','Calcitonin and PTH','T4 and Calcitonin','T4 and PTH','NEW','11-342','11-250',NULL),
(221822,'biology','Adrenals are in close approximity to which Abdominal Viscera?','Kidney','Our body has one pair of adrenal glands, one at the anterior part of each kidney Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 336','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Spleen','Kidney','Thymus','Thyroid Gland','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(221823,'biology','Which of the following hormone is not released by the Adrenals? \ni. Catecholamines \nii.Erythropoietin \niii. Cortisol \niv. Renin','ii and iv only','The adrenal medulla secretes two hormones called adrenaline or epinephrine and noradrenaline or norepinephrine. These are commonly called as catecholamines.Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 336','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i,ii and iii','ii and iii only','ii and iv only','iii and iv only','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(221824,'biology','Which of the following hormone is not a STERIOID?','T4','Steroids (e.g., cortisol, testosterone, estradiol and progesterone)Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 340','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Cortisol','Testosterone','T4','Progesterone','NEW','11-340','11-248',NULL),
(221825,'biology','Which of the following is a Second Messenger? \ni. Ca++ Ion \nii. cAMP \niii. IP3 \niv. Tyrosine kinase','i,ii and iii','Hormones which interact with membrane-bound receptors normally do not enter the target cell, but generate second messengers (e.g., cyclicAMP, IP3 Ca++ etc) Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 340','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii only','ii and iii only','iii and iv only','i,ii and iii','NEW','11-340','11-248',NULL),
(221826,'biology','Which of the following is an Androgen?','Testosterone','The Leydig cells or interstitial cells, which are present in the intertubular spaces produce a group of hormones called androgens mainly testosterone. Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 338','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Estrogen','Progesterone','Testosterone','Corticosteroids','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(221827,'biology','Which is not a male accessory sex organ?  \ni. Epididymis \nii. Vas deferens \niii. Seminal vesicles \niv.Prostate gland \nv. Testis','v only','Testis is a primary male sex organ not an accessory part, Androgens regulate the development, maturation and functions of\nthe male accessory sex organs like epididymis, vas deferens, seminal\nvesicles, prostate gland, urethra etc. Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 340','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii only','v only','ii and iv only','iii and iv only','NEW','11-340','11-248',NULL),
(221828,'biology','Which of the following hormones produce anabolic (synthetic) effects on protein and carbohydrate metabolism?','Androgens','These hormones produce anabolic (synthetic) effects on protein and carbohydrate metabolism. Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 338','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','T4','Insulin','Androgens','Cortisol','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(221829,'biology','On the basis of Chemical nature, Hormones are divided into? \ni. Steroid \nii. Peptide \niii. Amino acids derivatives \niv. Sugars','i, ii and iii','On the basis of their chemical nature, hormones can be divided into groups : (i) peptide, polypeptide, protein hormones (e.g., insulin, glucagon,pituitary hormones, hypothalamic hormones, etc.) (ii) steroids (e.g., cortisol, testosterone, estradiol and progesterone)(iii) iodothyronines (thyroid hormones)\n(iv) amino-acid derivatives (e.g., epinephrine).Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 340','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i, ii and iii','iii and iv only','ii and iv only','ii and iii only','NEW','11-340','11-248',NULL),
(221830,'biology','How many Hormones are produced by Pars Distalis of  the pituitary?','6','Pars distalis produces six tropic hormones.  Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 341','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2','4','6','5','NEW','11-341','11-249',NULL),
(221831,'biology','Which one is incorrect about erythropoietin?','Stimulates WBC production','The juxtaglomerular cells of kidney produce a peptide hormone called\nerythropoietin which stimulates erythropoiesis (formation of RBC) Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 339','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Stimulates erythropoiesis','Is a peptide hormone','Is secreted by JG cells','Stimulates WBC production','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221832,'biology','Whic of the following hormones are secreted by GIT?  \ni. Gastrin \nii. Secretin \niii. Calcitonin  \niv. Gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP).','i,ii and iv','Endocrine cells present in different parts of the gastro-intestinal tract secrete four major peptide hormones, namely gastrin, secretin,ncholecystokinin (CCK) and gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP). Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 339','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','i and ii only','ii and iv only','i,ii and iv','iii and iv only','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221833,'biology','Which of the following is the main hormone that supports Pregnancy?','Progesterone','Progesterone supports pregnancy  Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 339','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Progesterone','Estrogen','FSH','LH','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221834,'biology','Which statement is Incorrect regarding ANF?','Causes constriction of the blood vessels','The atrial wall of our heart secretes a very important peptide hormone called atrial natriuretic factor (ANF), which decreases blood pressure. When blood pressure is increased, ANF is secreted which causes dilation of the blood vessels. This reduces the blood pressure.  Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 339','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Peptide hormone','Decreases blood pressure.','When blood pressure is increased, ANF is secreted','Causes constriction of the blood vessels','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221835,'biology','Testis performs dual functions as a primary sex organ as well as an endocrine gland.','TRUE','Testis performs dual functions as a primary sex organ as well as an endocrine glandReference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 338','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(221836,'biology','Female sexual behavior is regulated by which hormone?','Estrogen','Estrogens also regulate female sexual behaviour. Reference  : NCERT Class 11 Page 339 3rd line','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration >> Previous Year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Lactogen','FSH','Estrogen','LH','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(221901,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R): \n\nA: Hormones are produced by exocrine glands and act on distant organs. \n\nR: Hormones act as nutrient chemicals produced in large amount.','Both A and R are false.','Hormones are produced from endocrine (ductless) glands which act on distant organs. They are non-nutrient chemicals produced in trace amounts. (NCERT Class 11 pg 331 section 22.1)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-331','11-239',NULL),
(221902,'biology','Read the following statements about HORMONES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Hormones are non-nutrient chemicals. \n\n2. Hormones are secreted from duct containing glands. \n\n3. Hormones act on adnexal cells. \n\n4. Hormones are produced in trace amounts.','Both 1 and 4 are correct','Hormones are non-nutrient chemicals which act as intercellular messengers and are produced in trace amounts. They are produced in endocrine glands (ductless) and act on distant organs. (NCERT Class 11 pg 331 section 22.1)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 1 and 4 are correct','Only 3 is correct','NEW','11-331','11-239',NULL),
(221903,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Hormones are produced from ductless exocrine glands. \n\nR: Pituitary, Pineal, thyroid, adrenal, thymus, uterus, gonads are all endocrine glands.','Both A and R are false.','Hormones are Hormones are non-nutrient chemicals which act as intercellular messengers and are produced in trace amounts. Pituitary, pineal, thyroid, adrenal, pancreas, parathyroid, thymus and gonads are the endocrine glands in our body (not uterus) (NCERT Class 11 pg 332 section 22.2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-332','11-240',NULL),
(221904,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Hypothalamus is located in forebrain and produces hormones from neuro-secretory cells. \n\nR: Hormones from hypothalamus are only "releasing" in nature.','A is true but R is false','Hypothalamus is a part of diencephalon, which is a prt of forebrain. It produces hormones from the neuro-secretory cells called nuclei. The hypothalamic hormones are both "releasing" and "inhibiting" in nature. (NCERT Class 11 pg 332 section 22.2.1)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-332','11-240',NULL),
(260329,'chemistry','Principle of the following reaction given in diagram(n26016)','chlorination','By side chain chlorination followed by hydrolysis Side chain chlorination of toluene gives benzal chloride, which on hydrolysis gives benzaldehyde. \n\nThis is a commercial method of manufacture of benzaldehyde.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','oxidisation','chlorination','hydration','sulphonation','NEW','12-363','12-233','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260329q.png'),
(221905,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Hypothalamic hormones are both releasing and inhibiting in nature. \n\nR: GnRH released from hypothalamus stimulates pituitary to release GH.','A is true but R is false','The hypothalamic hormones are both "releasing" and "inhibiting" in nature. Gonadotrophin Releasing Hormone (GnRH) from hypothalamus stimulates pituitary to secrete Gonadotrophins (FSH and LH) (NCERT Class 11 pg 332 section 22.2.1)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-332','11-240',NULL),
(221906,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Posterior pituitary is regulated by hypothalamus through portal circulation. \n\nR: Posterior pituitary secretes GH and GnRH.','Both A and R are false.','Posterior pituitary (NEURO-hypophysis) is controlled by hypothalamus under direct neural regulation. Posterior pituitary secretes Vasopressin (ADH) and Oxytocin. (NCERT Class 11 pg 332 section 22.2.1)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-332','11-240',NULL),
(221907,'biology','Read the following statements about ENDOCRINE GLANDS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Pancreas is both an endocrine and exocrine gland. \n\n2. GnRH is a inhibiting hormone released from anterior pituitary. \n\n3. Somatostatin inhibits GH release from pituitary. \n\n4. Anterior pituitary is regulated by hypothalamus via nerves.','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Pancreas is both an endocrine and exocrine gland. GnRH is a releasing hormone secreted from hypothalamus. Somatostatin released from hypothalamus inhibits GH release from pituitary. Anterior pituitary is regulated by hypothalamus via portal circulation. (NCERT Class 11 pg 332 section 22.2, 22.2.1)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 4 is correct','NEW','11-332','11-240',NULL),
(221908,'biology','Read the following statements about PITUITARY GLAND; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Pituitary is located in a bony cavity called sella tursica in the occipital bone. \n\n2. It is connected to the thyroid gland by a stalk. \n\n3. Neurohypophysis secretes ADH and oxytocin. \n\n4. Adenohypophysis is under hypothalamic regulation through portal system.','Both 3 and 4 are correct','The pituitary gland is located in a bony cavity called sella tursica and is attached to hypothalamus by a stalk. Neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary) secretes ADH and oxytocin. Hypothalamus regulates anterior pituitary via portal circulation system. (NCERT Class 11 pg 332-333 section 22.2.1 and 22.2.2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Only 4 is incorrect','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221909,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Anterior pituitary is also known as Neurohypophysis. \n\nR: Anterior pituitary is under direct neural regulation by hypothalamus.','Both A and R are false.','Anterior pituitary is also known as ADENO-HYPOPHYSIS. It is regulated by the hypothalamus via the portal circulatory system. (NCERT Class 11 pg 332-333 section 22.2.1 and 22.2.2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221910,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: TSH (Thyrotropin Stimulating Hormone) is released from hypothalamus. \n\nR: TSH stimulates pituitary to release thyroid hormone.','Both A and R are false.','TSH is Thyroid Stimulating Hormone, It is secreted from the anterior pituitary. It stimulates the thyroid gland and secretes T3 and T4. (NCERT Class 11 pg 333 section 22.2.2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221911,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Gigantism occurs due to oversecretion of GH from pituitary gland. \n\nR: GH release is inhibited by somatostatin (from hypothalamus).','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Over-secretion of GH stimulates abnormal growth of the body leading to gigantism. GH release from pituitary is inhibited by SOMATOSTATIN from Hypothalamus. (NCERT Class 11 pg 333 section 22.2.2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221912,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Acromegaly occurs due to low levels of GH in middle age. \n\nR: Oversecretion of GH leads to pituitary dwarfism and stunting.','Both A and R are false.','Over-secretion of GH stimulates abnormal growth of the body leads to gigantism and low secretion of GH results in stunted growth resulting in pituitary dwarfism. Excess secretion of growth hormone in adults (especially in middle age) can result in severe disfigurement (especially of the face) called Acromegaly. (NCERT Class 11 pg 333 section 22.2.2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221913,'biology','Read the following statements about PITUITARY HORMONES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Prolactin helps in milk formation. \n\n2. ACTH stimulates adrenal cortex to secrete adrenaline/epinephrine. \n\n3. LH and FSH inhibit spermatogenesis in males. \n\n4. TSH is secreted from anterior pituitary.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Prolactin helps in milk formation and growth of mammary glands. ACTH stimulates adrenal cortex to secrete glucocorticoids. LH and FSH enhance spermatogenesis in males. TSH is secreted from anterior pituitary. (NCERT Class 11 pg 333 section 22.2.2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-333','11-241',NULL),
(221914,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: MSH stimulates melanocytes and regulates pigmentation of skin. \n\nR: MSH is secreted from posterior pituitary gland.','A is true but R is false','MSH is secreted from PARS INTERMEDIA of pituitary gland. It acts on melanocytes and regulates pigmentation of skin. (NCERT Class 11 pg 334 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221915,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Oxytocin is also called the MILK EJECTION HORMONE. \n\nR: It causes vigorous contraction of uterus and helps in child birth.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Oxytocin is released from posterior pituitary. It helps to eject milk from the mammary gland to the mouth of suckling infant. It also help in uterine contraction and helps in child birth. (NCERT Class 11 pg 334 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221916,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Pineal gland is located on the ventral side of forebrain. \n\nR: It secretes melanin that plays a role in CIRCADIAN RHYTM (sleep-wake cycle) of the body.','Both A and R are false.','The pineal gland is located on dorsal side of the forebrain. It secretes MELATONIN (not melanin), which maintains the CIRCADIAN RHYTHM (sleep-wake cycle), body temperature, etc. (NCERT Class 11 pg 334 section 22.2.3)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221917,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Thyroid gland secretes T3 and T4 hormones under the influence of TSH. \n\nR: Thyroid gland is located on either side of esophagus at the level of T4 vertebrae.','A is true but R is false','Thyroid gland secretes T3 and T4 hormones under the stimulating effect of TSH. Thyroid gland is located on either side of the trachea and the 2 lobes are connected by isthmus. (NCERT Class 11 pg 334 section 22.2.4)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221918,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Fluorine is needed for the production of thyroid hormones. \n\nR: Deficiency of dietary fluorine leads to goitre.','Both A and R are false.','Iodine is essential for the normal rate of hormone synthesis in the thyroid. Deficiency of iodine in our diet results in hypothyroidism and enlargement of the thyroid gland, commonly called goitre. (NCERT Class 11 pg 334 section 22.2.4)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-334','11-242',NULL),
(221919,'biology','Read the following statements about THYROID GLAND; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Hypothyroidism in pregnancy leads to cretinism. \n\n2. Thyroid carcinoma can lead to hyperthyroidism. \n\n3. Graves disease is characterised by enophthalmos. \n\n4. Thyroid hormones regulate the BMR of the body.','Only 3 is incorrect','Hypothyroidism during pregnancy causes defective development and maturation of the growing baby leading to stunted growth (cretinism), mental retardation. Cancer of the thyroid gland leads to high levels of thyroid hormones and hyperthyroidism. Graves disease is characterised by protusion of eyeball (Exopthalmos). Thyroid hormones play an important role in the regulation of the basal metabolic rate. (NCERT Class 11 pg 335 paragraph 1,2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(222001,'biology','Read the following statements about THYROID  and  PARATHYROID GLANDS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Thyroid gland is located on either side of trachea. \n\n2. Parathyroid glands are present on the back side of thyroid gland. \n\n3. Thyroid hormones support erythropoeisis. \n\n4. Thyrocalcitonin regulates the Na+ homeostasis of the body.','Only 4 is incorrect','Thyroid gland is located on either side of trachea and connected by an isthmus. T3 and T4 help in maintaining BMR, RBC formation (erythrooeisis), water and electrolyte balance. Parathyroid glands are located on back side of thyroid gland. Thyroid gland also secretes a protein hormone called thyrocalcitonin (TCT) which regulates the blood calcium levels. (NCERT Class 11 pg 335 paragraph 1,2,3 and section 22.2.5)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are correct','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(222002,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: PTH is secreted from the thyroid gland and important in Ca+2 homeostasis. \n\nR: PTH reduces the Ca+2 levels in blood (hypocalcemia).','Both A and R are false.','The parathyroid glands secrete a peptide hormone called parathyroid hormone (PTH). Parathyroid hormone (PTH) increases the Ca2+ levels in the blood. (NCERT Class 11 pg 335 section 22.2.5)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(222003,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: PTH is a hypercalcemic hormone. \n\nR: PTH increases Ca+2 levels in urine.','A is true but R is false','PTH is a hypercalcemic hormone, such that it increases Ca+2 levels in blood. It increases renal reabsorption of Ca+2 and reduces calcium levels in urine. It also increases Ca+2 absorption from the digested food. (NCERT Class 11 pg 335 section 22.2.5)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(222004,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Thymosins are secreted by the thyroid gland. \n\nR: Thymosins are responsible for the differentiation of RBCs for providing immunity to the body.','Both A and R are false.','Thymosins are hormones secreted from THYMUS. These hormones are helpful in differentiation of T-lymphocytes, which provide cell-mediated immunity. It also promotes production of antibodies for humoral immunity. (NCERT Class 11 pg 335 section 22.2.6)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-335','11-243',NULL),
(222005,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Adrenal gland has central medulla and peripheral cortex. \n\nR: Reduced production from adrenal medulla causes Addison\'s disease.','A is true but R is false','Adrenal gland has centrally located tissue known as the adrenal medulla, and outside this lies the adrenal cortex. Reduced production of hormones by the adrenal cortex alters carbohydrate metabolism causing acute weakness and fatigue leading to a disease called Addison’s disease. (NCERT Class 11 pg 336 section 22.2.7)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(222006,'biology','Read the following statements about ADRENAL GLAND HORMONES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Adrenal medulla produces glucorticoids and mineralocorticoids. \n\n2. Catecholamines are the emergency hormones of the body. \n\n3. Adrenaline causes miosis. \n\n4. Norepinephrine  causes increased heart beat and respiratory rate.','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','The adrenal medulla secretes two hormones called adrenaline or epinephrine and noradrenaline or norepinephrine. Catecholamines are also known as EMERGENCY hormones. All catecholamines cause increased heart rate, respiratory rate and pupillary dilation (MYDRIASIS). (NCERT Class 11 pg 336 section 22.2.7)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(222007,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Emergency hormones cause piloerection and mydriasis. \n\nR: Emergency hormones are secreted from the peripheral parts of adrenal glands.','A is true but R is false','Emergency hormones (catecholamines) cause increase in alertness, pupilary dilation, piloerection (raising of hairs), sweating etc. Both the hormones increase the heart beat, the strength of heart contraction and the rate of respiration. They are secreted from adrenal medulla (central part of adrenal gland). (NCERT Class 11 pg 336 section 22.2.7)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(222008,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Catecholamines perform glycogenolysis, lipolysis and proteolysis. \n\nR: Catecholamines secretion reduces during stress or emegency situations.','A is true but R is false','Catecholamines also stimulate the breakdown of glycogen (glycogenolysis) resulting in an increased concentration of glucose in blood. In addition, they also stimulate the breakdown of lipids (lipolysis) and proteins (proteolysis). They are secreted rapidly during stress or emegencies. (NCERT Class 11 pg 336 section 22.2.7)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(222009,'biology','Read the following statements about ADRENAL CORTEX; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Aldosterone is secreted from adrenal medulla. \n\n2. Cortisol is a glucocorticoid. \n\n3. Adrenal cortex has three layers namely zona reticularis, zona cribrosa and zona glomerulosa. \n\n4. Glucorticoids enhance inflammatory reactions and increase immune response.','Only 2 is correct','Aldosterone is secreted from adrenal cortex (zona glomerulosa). Cortisol is a glucocorticoid. Adrenal gland has three layers namely zona reticularis (inner layer), zona fasciculata (middle layer) and zona glomerulosa (outer layer). Glucorticoids suppress immune reaction and reduce inflammation. (NCERT Class 11 pg 337 paragraph 1,2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(222010,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Glucorticoids perform gluconeogenesis, lipolysis and proteolysis. \n\nR: Cortisol stimulates erythropoiesis.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Glucocorticoids stimulate gluconeogenesis, lipolysis and proteolysis and inhibit cellular uptake and utilisation of amino acids.Cortisol stimulates the RBC production (erythropoesis) (NCERT Class 11 pg 337 paragraph 2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(222011,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Aldosterone maintains fluid and electrolyte balance. \n\nR: Adrenal androgens help in development of secondary sexual characters.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Aldosterone acts mainly at the renal tubules and stimulates the reabsorption of Na+ and water and excretion of K+ and phosphate ions. Thus, aldosterone helps in the maintenance of electrolytes, body fluid volume. Androgenic steroids are also secreted by the adrenal cortex which play a role in the growth of axial hair, pubic hair and facial hair during puberty (Secondary sexual characters) (NCERT Class 11 pg 337 paragraph 2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(260330,'chemistry','Recognize the given name reaction in the diagram(n26017)','Gatterman – Koch reaction','Gatterman – Koch reaction: \n\nWhen benzene or its derivative is treated with carbon monoxide and hydrogen chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride or cuprous chloride, it gives benzaldehyde or substituted benzaldehyde.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','Koch','Etard','Stephen','Gatterman – Koch reaction','NEW','12-363','12-233','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260330q.png'),
(222012,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Pancreas is a mixocrine gland. \n\nR: Alpha cells of pancreas secrete both insulin and glucagon.','A is true but R is false','Pancreas is a mixocrine gland because it has both endocrine  and  exocrine components. Alpha cells secrete glucagon and beta cells secrete insulin. (NCERT Class 11 pg 227 section 22.2.8)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(222013,'biology','Read the following statements about PANCREATIC HORMONES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Glucagon is released from alpha cells of Islets of Langerhans of Pancreas. \n\n2. Insulin is a hyperglycemic hormone. \n\n3. There are 1-2 thousand Islets of Langerhans in normal pancreas. \n\n4. Glucagon reduces cellular utilisation of glucose.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Glucagon is a peptide hormone released by alpha cells in pancreas. Insulin is a hypoglycemic hormone (reduces blood levels of glucose). There are 1-2 million Islets of Langerhans in a normal pancreas. Glucagon reduces cellular utilisation of glucose and hence increases blood levels of glucose. (NCERT Class 11 pg 337 section 22.2.8)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(222014,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Insulin is a hypoglycemic hormone. \n\nR: Insulin acts on hepatocytes, adipose tissues and reduces blood levels of glycogen.','A is true but R is false','Insulin is a peptide hormone, secreted by the β cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas. Insulin acts mainly on hepatocytes and adipocytes (cells of adipose tissue), and enhances cellular glucose uptake and utilisation. As a result, there is a rapid movement of glucose from blood to hepatocytes and adipocytes resulting in decreased blood glucose levels (hypoglycemia). (NCERT Class 11 pg 337-338 section 22.2.8)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(222015,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Diabetes Insipidus is characterised by ketonuria. \n\nR: Prolonged hypoglycemia and increased insulin levels cause diabetes mellitus.','Both A and R are false.','Diabetes mellitus (not diabetes insipidus) is characterised by formation of ketone bodies in urine (ketonuria). DM shows reduced insulin levels and prolonged hyperglycemia. (NCERT Class 11 pg 338 paragraph 2)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(222016,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Interstitial cells of Leydig produces lutenizing hormone (LH). \n\nR: Androgens are produced only from adrenal cortex.','Both A and R are false.','LH is produced from anterior pituitary and acts on Leydig cells , which produce androgens in males namely TESTOSTERONE. So, androgens are produced from both testis and adrenal cortex. (NCERT Class 11 pg 338 section 22.2.9)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(222017,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Testosterone has both anabolic and androgenic effect. \n\nR: Testosterone helps in sexual maturation as well as synthetic effect on proteins and carbohydrates.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Testosterone has anabolic effect (i.e. synthetic effect on carbogydrate , proteins, etc). It also has androgenic effect (i.e. helps in spermatogensis, sexual character development, maintaining sexual urge or libido). (NCERT Class 11 pg 338 section 22.2.9)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(222018,'biology','Read the following statements about HORMONES IN FEMALES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Uterus is the primary sex organ in female. \n\n2. Estrogen and progesterone are peptide hormones. \n\n3. Corpus luteum mainly secretes progesterone. \n\n4. The mature follicle that undergoes ovulation is called Graffian follicle.','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Ovaries are the primary sex organs in females. Estrogen and progesterone are steroid hormones produced from ovaries. Corpus luteum mainly secretes progesterone in the secretory phase of menstrual cycle or during pregnancy. The mature follicle that undergoes ovulation is called Graffian follicle. (NCERT Class 11 pg 338 section 22.2.10)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-338','11-246',NULL),
(222019,'biology','Read the following statements about MISCELLANEOUS HORMONES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Atrial Natriuretic Factor reduces blood pressure. \n\n2. ANF is a part of RAAS\n\n3. JG cells of kidney produces erythropeitin leading to anemia. \n\n4. Gastrin acts on gastric glands and increase HCl and pepsinogen secretion.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','ANF is not a part of RAAS. When excessive blood enters atria, ANF is released which causes vasodilation and reduces blood pressure. JG cells of kidney release erythropoetin and cause RBC production (not anemia). Gastrin secreted from G cells of intestine act on gastric glands and increase HCl and pepsinogen secretion. (NCERT Class 11 pg 339 section 22.3)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(222020,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: CCK acts on pancreas and gall-bladder and enhance pancreatic juice and bile secretion. \n\nR: Secretin acts on endocrine pancreas and cause water and bicarbonate secretion.','A is true but R is false','CCK acts on both pancreas and gall bladder and stimulates the secretion of pancreatic enzymes and bile juice respectively. Secretin acts on the EXOCRINE pancreas and stimulates secretion of water and bicarbonate ions. (NCERT Class 11 pg 339 section 22.3)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-339','11-247',NULL),
(222021,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Estrogen is a polypeptide hormone. \n\nR: During hormone action, the hormone-receptor complex is formed in the cytoplasm.','Both A and R are false.','Estrogen is a steroid hormone (i.e. lipophilic and hydrophobic). The hormone-receptor complex of estrogen is formed in the nucleus. (NCERT Class 11 pg 340 and fig. 22.5 b.)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-340','11-248',NULL),
(222022,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: FSH interact with membrane bound receptors. \n\nR: During FSH action, cAMP or Ca+2 acts as primary messengers.','A is true but R is false','FSH (poly-peptide hormone) acts with membrane bound receptors. This leads to generation of secondary messengers namely cyclic AMP (cAMP) or Ca+2. (NCERT Class 11 pg 340 section 22.4 and figure 22.5 a. )','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-340','11-248',NULL),
(222023,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Glucagon released from alpha cells of pancreas is a polypeptide hormone. \n\nR: T3 and T4 are epinephrine derivatives containing fluorine molecules.','A is true but R is false','Glucoagon is released from alpha cells of Islets of Langerhans in pancreas. It is a polypeptide hormone. T3 and T4 are thyroid hormones containing iodine atoms. (NCERT Class 11 pg 340 section 22.4)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-340','11-248',NULL),
(222024,'biology','Read the following statements about HORMONES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Oxytocin helps in milk ejection from mammary glands. \n\n2. Corpus luteum mainly secretes estrogen \n\n3. Epinephrine from adrenal cortex is an amino-acid derivative. \n\n4. Thyroid hormones are secreted under the influence of TSH.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Oxytocin (from posterior pituitary) helps to eject milk from mammry glands into mouth of suckling infants. Corpus Luteum mainly secretes progesterone. Epinephrine from adrenal MEDULLA is an amino acid derivative. TSH stimulates thyroid hormone secretion. (NCERT Class 11 pg 333 section 22.2.2, pg 336 section 22.2.7, pg 338 section 22.2.10, pg340 section 22.4)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','NEW','11-340','11-248',NULL),
(222025,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Corticoids (from adrenal medulla) involved in glucose metabolism are called mineralocorticoids. \n\nR: The common mineralocorticoids are cortisol, cortisone, etc.','Both A and R are false.','Corticoids (from adrenal cortex) involved in carbohydrate metabolism is called GLUCORTICOIDS. The common mineralocorticoid is ALDOSTERONE. The common glucocorticoids are cortisol, cortisone, etc. (NCERT Class 337 paragraph 1)','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW','11-337','11-245',NULL),
(222026,'biology','Injury to adrenal cortex is not likely to affect the secretion of which of the following?','Adrenaline','Adrenal glands or (suprarenal glands) are two triangular endocrine glands, each of which covers the superior surface of a kidney. Each gland has two parts, the medulla and cortex. The medulla forms the grey core of the gland; it consists mainly of chromaffin tissue and is stimulated by the sympathetic nervous system to produce adrenaline and noradrenaline. The cortex is a yellowish tissue surrounding the medulla. It is stimulated by pituitary hormones (principally ACTH) to produce three kinds of corticosteroid hormones, which affect carbohydrate metabolism (e.g., cortisol), electrolyte metabolism (e.g., aldosterone), and the sex glands (estrogens and androgens). Thus injury to adrenal cortex is not likely to affect the secretion of adrenaline.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Aldosterone','Both androstenedione and dehydroepiandrosterone','Adrenaline','Cortisol','NEW','11-336','11-244',NULL),
(222027,'biology','The amino acid tryptophan is the precursor for the synthesis of:','Melatonin and serotonin','Tryptophan is the precursor for the synthesis of Melatonin and serotonin.','Human Physiology >> Chemical Coordination and Integration>> Indepth questions (Syllabus Reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Estrogen and progesterone','Cortisol and cortisone','Melatonin and serotonin','Thyroxine and triiodothyronine','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(240101,'chemistry','Which of the following statement regarding haloalkane and haloarenes is true?','Carbon atom attached to halogen is sp³ in haloalkanes and sp² in haloarenes.','Haloalkanes contain halogen atom(s) attached to the sp³ hybridised carbon atom of an alkyl group whereas haloarenes contain halogen atom(s) attached to sp²  hybridised carbon atom(s) of an aryl group.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 289 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbon atom attached to halogen is sp³ in haloalkanes and sp³ in haloarenes.','Carbon atom attached to halogen is sp³ in haloalkanes and sp² in haloarenes.','Carbon atom attached to halogen is sp² in haloalkanes and haloarenes.','Carbon atom attached to halogen is sp³ in haloalkanes and sp in haloarenes.','NEW','12-289','12-159',NULL),
(240102,'chemistry','Which of the following is the solvent of choice for the non-polar substances?','Ethyl Bromide','Many halogen containing organic compounds are used as solvents for relatively non-polar compounds and as starting materials for the synthesis of wide range of organic compounds.  \n\n(Reference:  NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 289 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Ethyl Bromide','Water','0','Liquid Oxygen','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(240103,'chemistry','Which is the correct order of dipole moment of the following compounds?','CH₃Cl>CH₃F>CH₃Br>CH₃I','The more the electronegativity is between the bonded atoms the more is the dipole moment between them. \n\nThe same is expected in case of alkyl halides but CH₃F has low dipole moment than CH₃Cl. \n\nFluorine is more electronegative than chlorine, but the carbon-fluorine bond is also significantly shorter: 139 pm vs. 178 pm. \n\nBecause of this, fluoromethane dipole moment is lower than that of chloromethane.\n\nNCERT Table 10.2( NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 294 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃Cl>CH₃F>CH₃Br>CH₃I','CH₃I>CH₃F>CH₃Br>CH₃Cl','CH₃Cl>CH₃F>CH₃I>CHBr','CH₃F>CH₃Cl>CH₃Br>CH₃I','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240104,'chemistry','Which of the following is chlorine containing antibiotic and is used for which disease?','Chloramphenicol & Typhoid','Chlorine containing antibiotic, chloramphenicol, produced by microorganisms is very effective for the treatment of typhoid fever. \n\nChlorine containing antibiotic, chloramphenicol, produced by microorganisms is very effective for the treatment of typhoid fever.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 289 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Chloramphenicol & Typhoid','Actinomycin & Dengue','Penicillin & Syphilis','Doxycycline & Typhus','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(240105,'chemistry','Which of the following hormone in our body requires halogen iodine for synthesis and lack of which causes which disorder?','Thyroxine & Goitre','Our body produces iodine containing hormone, thyroxine, the deficiency of which causes a disease called goiter. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 289 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Thyroxine & Goitre','Adrenaline & Edema','ADH & Diabetes','Oxytocin & Acromegaly','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(240106,'chemistry','Which of the following is synthetic halogen compounds, and what is its use?','Chloroquine & Malaria Treatment','Synthetic halogen compounds, viz. chloroquine is used for the treatment of malaria.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 289 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Chloroquine & Malaria Treatment','Halothane & Malaria','Ampicillin & Fever','Chloramphenicol & Typhoid','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(240107,'chemistry','Which of the following statement represents correct uses of haloalkanes and haloarenes and their derivatives?','Both A & B','Halothane is used as an anaesthetic during surgery. \n\nCertain fully fluorinated compounds are being considered as potential blood substitutes in surgery.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 289; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Introduction',NULL,'1','mcq','Halothane is used as anaesthesia in surgery','Certain fluorinated substances are possible for use as blood substitute','Both A & B','Haloalkanes are used in flaming materials','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(240108,'biology','The number and length of stamens are ________ inflowers of different species.','Variable','The number and length of stamens are variable in flowers of different species.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'1','mcq','Same','Variable','Fixed','Both 1 and 3','NEW','12-021','12-005',NULL),
(240109,'biology','Assertion:If you were to collect a stamen each from ten flowers (each from different species) and arrange them on a slide, you would be able to appreciate the large variation in size seen in nature.\n\nReason:The number and length of stamens are same in flowers of different species.','Assertion is true but Reason is false','The number and length of stamens are variable in flowers of different species.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Asserttion and Reason are true and Reason is correct explaniation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explination of Assertion','Assertion is true but Reason is false','Both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW','12-021','12-005',NULL),
(240110,'biology','The long and slender stalk in stamen is known as','Filament','The long and slender stalk in stamen is known as Filament','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'1','mcq','Filament','Anther','Pollen sacs','Pistil','NEW','12-021','12-005',NULL),
(240111,'biology','The terminal generally bilobed structure in the stamen is known as','Anther','The terminal generally bilobed structure in the stamen is known as Anther','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'1','mcq','Filament','Anther','Pollen sacs','Pistil','NEW','12-021','12-005',NULL),
(240112,'biology','Match the following','Long and slender stalk-Filament,Terminal generally bilobed structure-Anther','There are two parts of a typical stamen- the long and slender stalk called the filament, and the terminal generally bilobed structure is anther.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'2','match','Long and slender stalk,Filament','Terminal generally bilobed structure,Anther',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-021','12-005',NULL),
(240113,'biology','A typical angiosperm anther is_______with each lobe having two theca','Bilobed','A typical angiosperm anther is Bilobed with each lobe having two theca','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'1','fillup','Bi,lo,be,d',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-021','12-005',NULL),
(240114,'biology','Angiosperms are dithecous','TRUE','A typical angiosperm anther is bilobed with each lobe having two theca, i.e., they are dithecous','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-021','12-005',NULL),
(240115,'biology','Match the following according to the diagram (2401)','Style-a,Stigma-b','Figure 2.1 NCERT class 12 page no: 20','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'2','match','Style,a','Stigma,b',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-020','12-004','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240115q.png'),
(240116,'biology','Match the following according to the diagram (2402)','Anther-c,Petal-d','Figure 2.1 NCERT class 12 page no: 20','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'2','match','Anther,c','Petal,d',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-020','12-004','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240116q.png'),
(240117,'biology','Match the following according to the diagram (2403)','Filament-e,Sepal-f','Figure 2.1 NCERT class 12 page no: 20','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'2','match','Filament,e','Sepal,f',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-020','12-004','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240117q.png'),
(240118,'biology','Identify the diagram (2404)','A typical stamen','Figure 2.2 NCERT class 12 page no: 21','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'1','mcq','Three–dimensional cut section of an anther','A typical stamen','Anther','Pollen sacs','NEW','12-021','12-005','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240118q.png'),
(240119,'biology','Match the following according to the diagram (2405)','Anther-a,Filament-b','Figure 2.2 NCERT class 12 page no: 21','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Stamen',NULL,'2','match','Anther,a','Filament,b',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-021','12-005','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240119q.png'),
(240201,'chemistry','Haloalkanes containing  one and two halogens in their structure are called?','Monohalogen and Dihalogen compounds Respectively','Haloalkanes and haloarenes may be classified as mono, di, or polyhalogen (tri-,tetra-, etc.) compounds depending on whether they contain one, two or more halogen atoms in their structures.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 290; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Monohalogen and Dihalogen compounds Respectively','Dihalogen and Monohalogen  compounds respectively','Dihalogen and Trihalogen  compounds respectively','Dihaloarenes and Monohaloarenes  compounds respectively','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(260331,'chemistry','Reducing agent in Gatterman – Koch reaction?','CuCl','Gatterman – Koch reaction: \n\nWhen benzene or its derivative is treated with carbon monoxide and hydrogen chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride or cuprous chloride, it gives benzaldehyde or substituted benzaldehyde.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -3)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'1','mcq','CuCl','H₂','H₂O','O₂','NEW','12-363','12-233',NULL),
(240202,'chemistry','What is the basis of classification of monohalogen compounds?','On basis of hybridization of carbon atom to which halogen is attached','Mono Halo Compounds may further be classified according to the hybridisation of the carbon atom to which the halogen is bonded.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 290; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','On basis of number of halogen atom','On basis of polarity of compound','On basis of physical property','On basis of hybridization of carbon atom to which halogen is attached','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(240203,'chemistry','Which of the given compound represent mono haloarene? diagram(n2401)','a','Mono Haloarene are the compounds which have a single halogen attached to the benzene ring.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 290; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Classification',NULL,'3','mcq','a','b','c','both a & b','NEW','12-290','12-160','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240203q.png'),
(240204,'chemistry','Alkyl halides or haloalkanes can be further classified into?','Primary, Secondary, tertiary','They are further classified as primary, secondary or tertiary according to the nature of carbon to which halogen is attached. \n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 290; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary, Secondary, tertiary','On basis of hybridization','Allylic and benzylic halide','On the basis of number of carbon atoms','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(240205,'chemistry','Which of the following is correct about allylic halide?','Halogen atom is attached to sp³ carbon adjacent to C=C','These are the compounds in which the halogen atom is bonded to an sp³ -hybridised carbon atom adjacent to carbon-carbon double bond (C=C) i.e. to an allylic carbon.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 290; Paragraph No. 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Halogen atom is attached to sp³ carbon adjacent to C=C','Halogen atom is attached to sp²  carbon','Halogen atom is attached to sp³ carbon with no C=C bond','Halogen atom is attached to carbon adjacent to tert. carbon atom','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(240206,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is true regarding the benzylic halide?','Halogen is attached to sp³ carbon adjacent to benzene ring.','These are the compounds in which the halogen atom is bonded to an sp³-hybridised carbon atom attached to an aromatic ring. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 290; Paragraph No. 5)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Halogen is attached to sp³ carbon adjacent to benzene ring.','Halogen is attached to sp²  carbon adjacent to benzene ring.','Halogen is attached to sp carbon adjacent to benzene ring.','Halogen is attached to benzene ring.','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(240207,'chemistry','Which of the following contains C=C sp² bond?','Both A & B','Vinylic halides are the compounds in which the halogen atom is bonded to sp²-hybridised carbon atom of a carbon-carbon double bond (C = C). \n\nAryl halides  are the compounds in which the halogen atom is directly bonded to the sp²-hybridised carbon atom of an aromatic ring.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 291; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Classification',NULL,'1','mcq','Vinylic halide','Aryl halide','Both A & B','Dihaloalkane','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(240208,'biology','Which one of the following is not a wall layer of microsporangium ?','Filament','Filament is not a wall layer of microsporangium.A typical microsporangium is surrounded by four layers: Epidermis, endothecium, middle layers and the tapetum.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Microsporangium',NULL,'3','mcq','Epidermis','Endothecium','Tapetum','Filament','NEW','12-021','12-005',NULL),
(240209,'biology','The outer three wall layers of microsporangium-','Both a and b','The outer three wall layers perform the function of protection and help in dehiscence of anther to release the pollen.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Microsporangium',NULL,'1','mcq','Perform the function of protection','Help in dehiscence of anther to release the pollen.','Both a and b','Provide shape to anther','NEW','12-021','12-005',NULL),
(240210,'biology','The innermost wall layer is-','Tapetum','The innermost wall layer is the tapetum.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Microsporangium',NULL,'2','mcq','Epidermis','Endothecium','Tapetum','Endodermis','NEW','12-021','12-005',NULL),
(240211,'biology','_______nourishes the developing pollen grains','Tapetum','The innermost wall layer is the tapetum. It nourishes the developing pollen grains.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Microsporangium',NULL,'2','mcq','Epidermis','Endothecium','Tapetum','Endodermis','NEW','12-021','12-005',NULL),
