(240310,'chemistry','IUPAC name of this compound is__________. diagram(n2453)','dichloromethane','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1. The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl).\n\nThe IUPAC of the given compound  is dichloromethane.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Table 10.1  Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 292 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Nomenclature',NULL,'3','mcq','benzyl chloride','chlorophenylmethane','methylene chloride','dichloromethane','NEW','12-293','12-163','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240310q.png'),
(240311,'chemistry','Assertion: H₂SO₄ cannot be used along with KI in the conversion of an alcohol to an alkyl iodide.\n\nReason: It converts KI to corresponding acid, HI which is then oxidised by it to I₂.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Sulfuric acid is an oxidising agent. \n\nWhen used, it causes alcohols to react with KI, converting KI to the equivalent HI and oxidising the HI. \n\nConsequently, when alcohols and KI combine, sulfuric acid is not necessary.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Intext Question Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 297 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Nomenclature',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-293','12-163',NULL),
(240312,'chemistry','What are the possible IUPAC name for orthochlorotoluene?','Both A & B','There are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1. The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl).\n\nThere are two possible IUPAC names of orthochlorotoluene.\n\n1. 1-chloro-2-methyl benzene\n\n2. 2-chlorotoluene\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12, page 292; table 10.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Nomenclature',NULL,'1','mcq','1-chloro-2-methylbenzene','2-chlorotoluene','Both A & B','2-chloro-2-methylbenzene','NEW','12-293','12-163',NULL),
