(240501,'chemistry','In preparation of chloroalkanes which of the following reagent is best?','SOCl₂','Thionyl chloride is best reagent as the byproduct formed in this reaction.\n  \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','SOCl₂','PCl₅','PCl₃','HCl','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240502,'chemistry','Which of the following can be used for the preparation of chloroalkanes from alcohol?','All of the Above','The hydroxyl group of an alcohol is replaced by halogen on reaction with concentrated halogen acids, phosphorus halides or thionyl chloride  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','SOCl₂','PCl₅','HCl','All of the Above','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240503,'chemistry','Major product in the following reaction in the present of dry acetone is: diagram(n2470)','CH₃CH₂I','Alkyl iodides are often prepared by the reaction of alkyl chlorides/ bromides with NaI in dry acetone. \n\nThis reaction is known as Finkelstein reaction.\n\n(Reference: Intext Question 10.5 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 297)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','CH₃CH₂I','CH₃CH₂Br','CH₃CH₂F','CH₃CH₂Cl','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240503q.png'),
(240504,'chemistry','Which alcohol doesn\'t requires ZnCl₂ as catalyst during the process of halogenation?','Tertiary Alcohol','The reactions of primary and secondary alcohols with HCl require the presence of a catalyst, ZnCl₂.\n\nWith tertiary alcohols, the reaction is conducted by simply shaking the alcohol with concentrated HCl at room temperature.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary Alcohol','Secondary Alcohol','Tertiary Alcohol','All of the Above','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240505,'chemistry','Which physical process is used for the synthesis of the alkyl bromides?','Constant boiling with HBr (48%)','Constant boiling with HBr (48%) is used for preparing alkyl bromide. \n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Constant boiling with HBr (48%)\\','Constant boiling with HBr (98%)','Shaking with HBr(48%)','Constant boiling with HCl (48%)','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(260334,'chemistry','Name the reagent in the diagram(n26018)','dialkyl cadmium','Treatment of acyl chlorides with dialkyl cadmium, prepared by the reaction of cadmium chloride with Grignard reagent, gives ketones.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-363 Paragraph -4)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','cadmium chloride','dialkyl cadmium','magnesium chloride','grignard reagent','NEW','12-363','12-233','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260334q.png'),
(240506,'chemistry','Which process is accompanied for the best production of the alkyl iodides ?','React with KI or NaI with 95% orthophosphoric acid','Good yields of R—I may be obtained by heating alcohols with sodium or potassium iodide in 95% orthophosphoric acid.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','React with KI or NaI with 95% orthophosphoric acid','React with KI or NaI with 48% orthophosphoric acid','React with KI or NaI with 95% pyrophosphoric acid','React with KI or NaI with 75% orthophosphoric acid','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240507,'chemistry','Order of Reactivity of alcohols with haloacids is ?','3°>2°>1°>CH₃OH','It is due to the presence of the nature of carbocation present in its structure. \n\nGreater the stability of carbocation bonded with -OH, greater will be its ease of formation from alkyl halide and faster will be the rate of reaction. \n\nThe order of reactivity of alcohols with a given haloacid is acid is 3 >2>1   \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','3°>2°>1°>CH₃OH','3°>1°>2°>CH₃OH','1°>2°>3°>CH₃OH','3°>1°>CH₃OH> 2°','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240508,'chemistry','Why methods used for the preparation of alkyl halides like reaction with haloacid cannot be used for aryl halide?','Due to partial double bond character','Methods are not applicable for the preparation of aryl halides because the carbon-oxygen bond in phenols has a partial double bond character and is difficult to break being stronger than a single bond.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Due to partial double bond character','Due to deactivating groups','Due to low reactivity','Due to paramagnetic nature of compound','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240509,'chemistry','Which mechanism is used for the synthesis of  mixture of isomeric mono- and poly haloalkanes.   from the alkanes?','Free Radical substitution','Free radical chlorination or bromination of alkanes gives a complex mixture of isomeric mono- and poly haloalkanes.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','Free Radical substitution','Free Radical elimination','Sɴ1','Electrophilic substitution','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240510,'chemistry','Why the yield of product in production of haloalkanes from alkanes is low ?','Due to multiple products formed','Free radical chlorination or bromination of alkanes gives a complex mixture of isomeric mono- and poly haloalkanes  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Due to multiple products formed','Due to explosive nature of reaction','As gaseous products are formed','Due to low reactivity of aromatic ring','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240511,'chemistry','What are the products formed in the chlorination of n-butane ?','Both A and B','Free radical chlorination or bromination of alkanes gives a complex mixture of isomeric mono- and poly haloalkanes, which is difficult to separate as pure compounds. \n\nConsequently, the yield of any single compound is low.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','2-chlorobutane','1-chlorobutane','Both A and B','1-chloro-2-methylbutane','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240512,'chemistry','By which methods alkene can be converted to haloalkanes','Both A and B','An alkene is converted to corresponding alkyl halide by reaction with hydrogen chloride, hydrogen bromide or hydrogen iodide.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Addition of haloacid','Addition with halogen','Both A and B','Substitution of halogen','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240513,'chemistry','Major product obtained by addition of HCl in alkene is decided by?','Markovnikov\'s Rule','Only one predominates as per Markovnikov’s rule. \n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph No.3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Markovnikov\'s Rule','Saytzeff Rule','AntiMarkovnikov\'s Rule','The polarity of bond in HX','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(240514,'chemistry','Major product obtained by addition of HCl to  n-propene is ?','2-chloropropane','Only one predominates as per Markovnikov’s rule.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph No.3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'2','mcq','2-chloropropane','1-chloropropane','Both will be major','n-chloropropane','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240514q.png'),
(240515,'chemistry','Which solvent is used for reaction of Br₂ to alkene ?','CCl₄','In the laboratory, addition of bromine in CCl4 to an alkene resulting in discharge of reddish brown colour of bromine.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph No.4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','CCl₄','CO₂','Water','Alcohol','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240515q.png'),
(240516,'chemistry','Which of the following is the most important test for unsaturation ?','Addition of Br₂ in CCl₄','In the laboratory, addition of bromine in CCl₄ to an alkene resulting in discharge of reddish brown colour of bromine constitutes an important method for the detection of double bond in a molecule. \n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph No.4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Addition of Br₂ in CCl₄','Addition of Cl₂ in alkene','Addition of HBr','Addition of F₂ in alkene','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240516q.png'),
(240517,'chemistry','What is product formed when ethene reacts with Br₂ in CCl₄?','Vicinal dibromide','The addition results in the synthesis of vic-dibromides, which are colourless. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph No.4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Vicinal dibromide','Geminal dibromide','Both','1,1,2,2-tetrabromoethane','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240517q.png'),
(240518,'chemistry','How many monochloro structural isomers expected to be formed on free radical monochlorination of (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₃?','4','In the given molecule, there are four different types of hydrogen atoms. \n\nReplacement of these hydrogen atoms will give the following\n\n1. (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₂Cl\n\n2. (CH₃)₂CHCH(Cl)CH₃\n\n3. (CH₃)₂C(Cl)CH₂CH₃\n\n4. CH₃CH(CH₂Cl)CH₂CH₃\n\n(Reference: NCERT Example 10.3 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 295)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240518q.png'),
(2000034275,'physics','A satellite is orbiting close to the earth and has a kinetic energy $K$. The minimum extra kinetic energy required by it to just overcome the gravitation pull of the earth is','B','Given that<br>$K.E = K,P.E =  - 2\\,K$<br>$T.E =  - 2\\,K + K =  - K$<br>To overcome gravitational pull, total energy will have to be zero. So required extra K.E. $=K$','Earth Satellites','Gravitation','Easy',NULL,'$\\sqrt 3 \\,K$','$K$','$2\\sqrt 2 \\,K$','$2K$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(240519,'chemistry','Product of the given reaction is: diagram(n2469)','2-chlorobutane','The addition takes place according to Markovnikov\'s rule. \n\nThe hydrogen adds to terminal carbon with more hydrogen and chlorine adds to carbon 2 to give 2-chlorobutane.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Example 10.4 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 297  )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'3','mcq','2-chlorobutane','3-chlorobutane','4-chlorobutane','1-chlorobutane','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240519q.png'),
(240520,'chemistry','Which statement correctly tells about the Finkelstein reaction?','Used to synthesis RI','Alkyl iodides are often prepared by the reaction of alkyl chlorides/ bromides with NaI in dry acetone. This reaction is known as Finkelstein reaction.  \n(Reference  : NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph No.5)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Used to synthesis RI','Used For synthesis of RF','Alkyl fluoride is exchanged with NaI','Tells us about the addition of HX to alkene','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240520q.png'),
(240521,'chemistry','How many possible monochloro structural isomers expected to be formed on free radical monochlorination of (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₃  ?','4','In the given molecule, there are four different types of hydrogen atoms. \n\nReplacement of these hydrogen atoms will give the following.\n\n1. (CH₃)₂CHCH₂CH₂Cl\n\n2. (CH₃)₂CHCH(Cl)CH₃\n\n3.(CH₃)₂C(Cl)CH₂CH₃\n\n4. CH₃CH(CH₂Cl)CH₂CH₃\n\n(Reference: NCERT Example 10.4: SbF₃  Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Example 10.3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','4','8','10','2','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240521q.png'),
(240522,'chemistry','Which solvent is used in the Finkelstein reaction?','Dry acetone','Dry acetone is used here because it can precipitate the Nacl and NaBr formed in reaction so that reaction can go ahead i.e, le chatelier\'s principle.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph 7)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Dry acetone','CCl₄','Water','Alcohol','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240522q.png'),
(240523,'chemistry','The synthesis of alkyl fluoride by the halogen exchange reaction can be done by exchanging bromide and chloride with which of following metallic fluoride?','All of the Above','The synthesis of alkyl fluorides is best accomplished by heating an alkyl chloride/bromide in the presence of a metallic fluoride such as AgF, Hg₂F₂ , CoF₂  or SbF₃\n   \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','AgF','Hg₂F₂','CoF₂','All of the Above','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240523q.png'),
(240524,'chemistry','What will be the product of following reaction CH₃Br + AgF ?','CH₃F','The synthesis of alkyl fluorides is best accomplished by heating an alkyl chloride/bromide in the presence of a metallic fluoride such as AgF, Hg₂F₂ ,CoF₂ or SbF₃  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','CH₃F','CH₃Cl','CH₃Br','AgCl','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240524q.png'),
(240525,'chemistry','The reaction of arenes with halogen in the presence of Lewis acid to form aryl halide follows which mechanism?','Electrophilic substitution','Aryl chlorides and bromides can be easily prepared by electrophilic substitution of arenes with chlorine and bromine respectively in the presence of Lewis acid catalysts like iron or iron(III) chloride \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Electrophilic substitution','Free radical substitution','Free radical elimination','Nucleophilic substitution','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240525q.png'),
(240526,'chemistry','What are products formed by reaction of toluene with chlorine In presence of Lewis acid ?','Both A & B','As  methyl group increases the electron density on ortho and para positions. \n\nSo that electrophile formed can substitute the ortho and para site forming both products but para is major due to steric hindrance. \n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Para chlorotoluene','Ortho chlorotoluene','Meta chlorotoluene','Both A & B','NEW','12-295','12-165','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240526q.png'),
(240527,'chemistry','Ortho and Paragraph isomers can be easily be separated by difference in which Physical property ?','melting point','The ortho and Paragraph isomers can be easily separated due to large difference in their melting points  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','melting point','boiling point','density','molecular mass','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(240528,'chemistry','Why fluro and iodo compounds can not be prepared by reacting arenes with halogen ?','Both a and b','Reactions with iodine are reversible in nature and require the presence of an oxidising agent (HNO₃,HIO₄ to oxidise the HI formed during iodination. \n\nFluoro compounds are not prepared by this method due to high reactivity of fluorine. \n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Methods of preparation of Haloalkanes',NULL,'1','mcq','Due to high reactivity of fluorine','Due to formation of HI which is reducing agent','Both a and b','They require presence of reducing agent','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(240601,'chemistry','Which of the following halogen compounds are gaseous in nature?','all of the above','Molecular state of a compound depends upon molecular weight, So low molecular weight compounds are generally gaseous in nature.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 298; Paragraph 1).','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','methyl chloride','methyl bromide','ethyl chloride','all of the above','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240602,'chemistry','Arrange each set of compounds in order of increasing boiling points. 1-chloropropane, Isopropyl chloride, 1-chlorobutane.','isopropylchloride, 1-chloropropane, 1-chlorobutane','The following is correct order of increasing boiling point:\n\nIsopropylchloride, 1-chloropropane, 1-chlorobutane\n\n(Reference: NCERT Intext Question Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 297 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','1-chloropropane, Isopropylchloride, 1-chlorobutane','isopropylchloride, 1-chlorobutane, 1-chloropropane','isopropylchloride, 1-chloropropane, 1-chlorobutane','all have same boiling point','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240603,'chemistry','Arrange each set of compounds in order of increasing boiling points. bromomethane, bromoform, chloromethane, cibromomethane.','chloromethane, bromomethane, dibromomethane, bromoform.','The correct order is chloromethane, bromomethane, dibromomethane, bromoform.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Intext Question Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 297 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','chloromethane, bromomethane, dibromomethane, bromoform.','chloromethane, bromoform, dibromomethane, bromomethane.','bromomethane, chloromethane, dibromomethane, bromoform.','bromoform, chloromethane, dibromomethane, bromomethane.','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240604,'chemistry','Which of the following force of interaction exists between the halogen derivatives?','Both A & B','As we have already learnt, molecules of organic halogen compounds are generally polar. \n\nDue to greater polarity as well as higher molecular mass as compared to the parent hydrocarbon, the intermolecular forces of attraction (dipole-dipole and van der Waals) are stronger in the halogen derivatives. \n\nThat is why the boiling points of chlorides, bromides and iodides are considerably higher than those of the hydrocarbons of comparable molecular mass.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 298; Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Dipole dipole','Van der wal forces','Dipole induced Dipole','Both A & B','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240605,'chemistry','Assertion: the boiling points of chlorides, bromides and iodides are considerably higher than those of the hydrocarbons of comparable molecular mass. \n\nReason: Due to greater polarity as well as higher molecular mass as compared to the parent hydrocarbon, the intermolecular forces of attraction (dipole-dipole and van der Waals) are stronger in the halogen derivatives.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Due to greater polarity as well as higher molecular mass as compared to the parent hydrocarbon, the intermolecular forces of attraction (dipole-dipole and van der Waals) are stronger in the halogen derivatives. \n\nThat is why the boiling points of chlorides, bromides and iodides are considerably higher than those of the hydrocarbons of comParagraphble molecular mass. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240606,'chemistry','For the same alkyl group, the boiling points of alkyl halides decrease in the order:','RI> RBr> RCl> RF','With the increase in size and mass of halogen atom, the magnitude of van der Waal forces increases.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','RI>RBr>RF>RCl','RI> RBr> RCl> RF','RF>RI>RBr>RCl','RF>RCl>RBr>RI','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240607,'chemistry','Assertion :For the same alkyl group, the boiling points of alkyl halides decrease in the order: RI> RBr> RCl> RF.\n \nReason: with the increase in size and mass of halogen atom, the magnitude of van der Waal forces increases','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','For the same alkyl group, the boiling points of alkyl halides decrease in the order: RI> RBr> RCl> RF. \n\nThis is because with the increase in size and mass of halogen atom, the magnitude of van der Waal forces increases.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240608,'chemistry','Which of the following will have the lowest boiling point? diagram(n2404)','C','As the number of carbons in an alkane increases, so do its boiling points. \n\nThis is due to the fact that the intermolecular attractive forces, while individually small, become cumulatively stronger as the molecule\'s atom and electron counts rise. \n\nBranched alkanes often have lower boiling points than unbranched alkanes with the same carbon concentration. \n\nThe stronger van der Waals interactions between the molecules of the unbranched alkanes lead this to happen.\n\n(Reference:NCERT Figure 10.1 NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','A','B','C','None of the above','NEW','12-298','12-168','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240608q.png'),
(240609,'chemistry','Assertion :The para-isomers are high melting as compared to their ortho- and meta-isomers \n\nReason :It is due to symmetry of para-isomers that fits in crystal lattice better as compared to ortho- and meta-isomers.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','The para-isomers are high melting as compared to their ortho- and meta-isomers. \n\nIt is due to symmetry of para-isomers that fits in crystal lattice better as compared to ortho- and meta-isomers. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 direct line  Pg 298 Last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240610,'chemistry','The correct order of decreasing melting point is: diagram(n2405)','C>A>B','The para-isomers are high melting as compared to their ortho and meta-isomers. \n\nIt is due to symmetry of para-isomers that fits in crystal lattice better as compared to ortho- and meta-isomers.\n\nNCERT Figure(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 Last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','A>B>C','A>C>B','B>C>A','C>A>B','NEW','12-298','12-168','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240610q.png'),
(240611,'chemistry','Correct order of density is: diagram(n2406)','C>B>A','Bromo, iodo and polychloro derivatives of hydrocarbons are heavier than water. \n\nThe density increases with increase in number of carbon atoms, halogen atoms and atomic mass of the halogen atoms.\n\nNCERT Figure (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 Last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','A>B>C','A>C>B','B>C>A','C>B>A','NEW','12-298','12-168','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240611q.png'),
(240612,'chemistry','Solubility of haloalkane in water is','Slightly soluble','The haloalkanes are very slightly soluble in water. \n\nIn order to dissolve haloalkane in water, energy is required to overcome the attractions between the haloalkane molecules and break the hydrogen bonds between water molecules. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 299 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'1','mcq','Soluble','Slightly soluble','Insoluble','Both A and B','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240613,'chemistry','Assertion: The solubility of haloalkanes in water is low. \n\nReason: Less energy is released when new attractions are set up between the haloalkane and the water molecules as these are not as strong as the original hydrogen bonds in water.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','The haloalkanes are very slightly soluble in water. \n\nIn order to dissolve haloalkane in water, energy is required to overcome the attractions between the haloalkane molecules and break the hydrogen bonds between water molecules. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 299 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240614,'chemistry','Assertion : Haloalkanes tend to dissolve in organic solvents.\n\nReason: The new intermolecular attractions between haloalkanes and solvent molecules have much the same strength as the ones being broken in the separate haloalkane and solvent molecules.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','The haloalkanes are very slightly soluble in water. \n\nIn order to dissolve haloalkane in water, energy is required to overcome the attractions between the haloalkane molecules and break the hydrogen bonds between water molecules. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 299 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240615,'chemistry','Arrange each set of compounds in order of increasing boiling points.','chloromethane, bromomethane, dibromomethane, bromoform','The correct order is chloromethane, bromomethane, dibromomethane, bromoform.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Intext Question 10.6 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 297  )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','chloromethane, bromomethane, dibromomethane, bromoform','bromomethane, bromoform, chloromethane, dibromomethane.','chloromethane, bromoform, dibromomethane, bromomethane.','bromoform, chloromethane, dibromomethane, bromomethane.','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240616,'chemistry','Arrange each set of compounds in order of increasing boiling points.','isopropylchloride, 1-chloropropane, 1-chlorobutane','The following is correct order of increasing boiling point:\n\nIsopropylchloride, 1-chloropropane, 1-chlorobutane\n\n(Reference: NCERT Intext Question 10.6 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 297  )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Physical Properties',NULL,'2','mcq','isopropylchloride, 1-chloropropane, 1-chlorobutane','1-chloropropane, isopropyl chloride, 1-chlorobutane','isopropylchloride, 1-chlorobutane, 1-chloropropane','all have same boiling point','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(240617,'biology','Function of filiform apparatus is _','To guide pollen tubes into the synergids','The synergies have special cellular thickenings at the micropylar tip called filiform apparatus, which plays an important role in guiding the pollen tubes into the ovules','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> pollen pistil interactions',NULL,'3','mcq','To perform mitosis','To perform meiosis','As food reservoir','To guide pollen tubes into the synergids','NEW','12-027','12-011',NULL),
(240618,'biology','A typical angiosperm Embryo sac at maturity is','8-nucleate, 7-celled','A typical angiosperm Embryo sac at maturity is 8-nucleate, 7-celled.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Embryo sac',NULL,'2','mcq','7-nucleate, 8-celled','8-nucleate, 7-celled','4-nucleate, 3-celled','3-nucleate, 4-celled','NEW','12-027','12-011',NULL),
(240701,'chemistry','Which aromatic amine, when dissolved or suspended in cold aqueous mineral acid, is treated with sodium nitrite, a diazonium salt is formed?','Primary','When a primary aromatic amine, dissolved or suspended in cold aqueous mineral acid, is treated with sodium nitrite, a diazonium salt is formed. \n \n(Reference :Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 296, Paragraph 3 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary','Secondary','Tertiary','Quaternary','NEW','12-299','12-170',NULL),
(240702,'chemistry','Nucleophiles are rich species in','electron','Nucleophiles are electron rich species. \n\nTherefore, they attack at that part of the substrate molecule which is electron deficient. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 299 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','electron','proton','neutron','neutrino','NEW','12-299','12-170',NULL),
(240703,'chemistry','The reactions of haloalkanes may be divided into the following categories:','All the above','The reactions of haloalkanes may be divided into the following categories: 1. Nucleophilic substitution 2. Elimination reactions 3. Reaction with metals. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 299 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Nucleophilic substitution','Elimination reactions','Reaction with metals','All the above','NEW','12-299','12-170',NULL),
(240704,'chemistry','Assertion: nucleophiles are electron rich species. \n\nReason: they attack at that part of the substrate molecule which is electron rich.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Nucleophiles are electron rich species. \n\nTherefore, they attack at that part of the substrate molecule which is electron deficient. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 299 Last Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-299','12-170',NULL),
(240705,'chemistry','The reaction in which a nucleophile replaces already existing nucleophile in a molecule is called','Nucleophilic substitution reaction','A substitution reaction takes place and halogen atom, called leaving group departs as halide ion. \n\nSince the substitution reaction is initiated by a nucleophile, it is called nucleophilic substitution reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Displacement reaction','Electrophilic reaction','Elimination reaction','Nucleophilic substitution reaction','NEW','12-299','12-170',NULL),
(240706,'chemistry','In this type of reaction, a nucleophile reacts with haloalkane (the substrate) having a partial positive charge on the carbon atom bonded to halogen. Which reaction is this?','Nucleophilic substitution reaction','In this type of reaction, a nucleophile reacts with haloalkane (the substrate) having a partial positive charge on the carbon atom bonded to halogen. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Displacement reaction','Electrophilic reaction','Elimination reaction','Nucleophilic substitution reaction','NEW','12-299','12-170',NULL),
(240707,'chemistry','What does the given equation represents: diagram(n2407)','Nucleophilic substitution reaction','In this type of reaction, a nucleophile reacts with haloalkane (the substrate) having a partial positive charge on the carbon atom bonded to halogen.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Displacement reaction','Electrophilic reaction','Elimination reaction','Nucleophilic substitution reaction','NEW','12-299','12-170','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240707q.png'),
(240708,'chemistry','Example of ambident nucleophiles is / are','both a and b','Groups like cyanides and nitrites possess two nucleophilic centres and are called ambident nucleophiles. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 170 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','cyanides','nitrites','alcohols','both a and b','NEW','12-299','12-170',NULL),
(240709,'chemistry','Assertion :Cyanides and nitrites are called ambidentate nucleophiles.\n \nReason : they possess two nucleophilic centres. Find out the correct statement about given expanation.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Groups like cyanides and nitrites possess two nucleophilic centres and are called ambident nucleophiles. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 170 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','Mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-299','12-170',NULL),
(240710,'chemistry','Haloalkanes react with KCN to form alkyl cyanides as main product while AgCN forms isocyanides as the chief product. Why? \n\na) KCN is predominantly ionic and provides cyanide ions in solution.\n\nb) Although both carbon and nitrogen atoms are in a position to donate electron pairs, the attack takes place mainly through carbon atom and not through nitrogen atom since C—C bond is more stable than C—N bond. \n\nc) AgCN is mainly covalent in nature and nitrogen is free to donate electron pair forming isocyanide as the main product.','All the above','KCN is predominantly ionic and provides cyanide ions in solution. \n\nAlthough both carbon and nitrogen atoms are in a position to donate electron pairs, the attack takes place mainly through carbon atom and not through nitrogen atom since C—C bond is more stable than C—N bond. \n\nHowever, AgCN is mainly covalent in nature and nitrogen is free to donate electron pair forming isocyanide as the main product. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 301 Example 10.5)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','All the above','NEW','12-299','12-170',NULL),
(240711,'chemistry','Assertion: nitrite ion represents an ambident nucleophile with two different points of linkage. \n\nReason: The linkage through oxygen results in alkyl nitrites while through nitrogen atom, it leads to nitroalkanes.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','Nitrite ion also represents an ambident nucleophile with two different points of linkage [–O— N=O]. \n\nThe linkage through oxygen results in alkyl nitrites while through nitrogen atom, it leads to nitroalkanes. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 301 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-299','12-170',NULL),
(240712,'chemistry','The reaction between CH₃Cl and hydroxide ion to yield methanol and chloride ion follows which order kinetics?','Sɴ2','The reaction between CH₃Cl and hydroxide ion to yield methanol and chloride ion follows a second order kinetics. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 301 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Sɴ1','Sɴ2','Sɴ3','Zero Order','NEW','12-300','12-171',NULL),
(240713,'chemistry','What type of reaction does the following equation represents: diagram(n2408)','Sɴ2','The reaction between CH₃Cl and hydroxide ion to yield methanol and chloride ion follows a second order kinetics, i.e., the rate depends upon the concentration of both the reactants.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 301 Paragraph 1 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sɴ2','Sɴ3','Sɴ4','Zero Order','NEW','12-300','12-171','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240713q.png'),
(240714,'chemistry','Sɴ2 reaction consists of : \n\na) the incoming nucleophile interacts with alkyl halide causing the carbon-halide bond to break and a new bond is formed between carbon and attacking nucleophile. \n\nb) These two processes take place simultaneously in a single step and no intermediate is formed.','Both a and b','The incoming nucleophile interacts with alkyl halide causing the carbon-halide bond to break and a new bond is formed between carbon and attacking nucleophile. \n\nHere it is C-O bond formed between C and -OH. \n\nThese two processes take place simultaneously in a single step and no intermediate is formed .\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 301 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','Both a and b','None of the above','NEW','12-300','12-171',NULL),
(240715,'chemistry','Sɴ2 reaction consists of :\na) As the reaction progresses and the bond between the incoming nucleophile and the carbon atom starts forming, the bond between carbon atom and leaving group weakens. \n\nb) In transition state all the three C-H bonds are not in the same plane and the attacking and leaving nucleophiles are partially attached to the carbon.','a','In Sɴ2 as the reaction progresses and the bond between the incoming nucleophile and the carbon atom starts forming, the bond between carbon atom and leaving group weakens. \n\nAs this happens, the three carbon-hydrogen bonds of the substrate start moving away from the attacking nucleophile. \n\nIn transition state all the three C-H bonds are in the same plane and the attacking and leaving nucleophiles are partially attached to the carbon.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','Both a and b','None of the above','NEW','12-300','12-171',NULL),
(240716,'chemistry','In Sɴ2 reactions, configuration of carbon is','Inverted','As the attacking nucleophile approaches closer to the carbon, C-H bonds still keep on moving in the same direction till the attacking nucleophile attaches to carbon and leaving group leaves the carbon. \n\nAs a result configuration is inverted, the configuration of carbon atom under attack inverts in much the same way as an umbrella is turned inside out when caught in a strong wind. \n\nThis process is called as inversion of configuration.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Inverted','Retented','Changes to 90°','Changes to 60°','NEW','12-300','12-171',NULL),
(240718,'chemistry','Special arrangement of functional groups around carbon is called its','Configuration','Special arrangement of functional groups around carbon is called its configuration.\n \nNCERT Box (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Inversion','Retention','Configuration','Chirality','NEW','12-300','12-171','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240718q.png'),
(240719,'chemistry','Correct Statements based on the diagram is/are: diagram(n2409)','All of the above','These are the two structures of the same compound. \n\nThey differ in special arrangement of functional groups attached to carbon. Structure (A) is mirror image of Structure (B). \n\nWe say configuration of carbon in structure (A) is mirror image of the configuration of carbon in structure (B).\n \nNCERT Box( NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','These are the two structures of the same compound','They differ in special arrangement of functional groups attached to carbon','Structure (A) is mirror image of Structure (B)','All of the above','NEW','12-300','12-171','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240719q.png'),
(240720,'chemistry','Assertion: Of the simple alkyl halides, methyl halides react most rapidly in Sɴ2 reactions. \n\nReason: There are 4 hydrogen atoms.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Of the simple alkyl halides, methyl halides react most rapidly in Sɴ2 reactions because there are only three small hydrogen atoms. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302 last Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-300','12-171','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240720q.png'),
(240721,'chemistry','Assertion: Tertiary halides are the least reactive in Sɴ2 reactions.\n \nReason: Bulky groups hinder the approaching nucleophile.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Tertiary halides are the least reactive because bulky groups hinder the approaching nucleophile. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302 last Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-300','12-171',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(240722,'chemistry','The order of reactivity followed in Sɴ2 is:','Primary halide > Secondary halide > Tertiary halide','Thus the order of reactivity followed is: Primary halide > Secondary halide > Tertiary halide. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 303 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary halide > Tertiary halide > Secondary halide','Tertiary halide > Secondary halide > Primary halide','Primary halide > Secondary halide > Tertiary halide','All have same','NEW','12-300','12-171',NULL),
(240801,'chemistry','Another name for asymmetric carbon atom is','Stereocenter','Dutch scientist, J. Van’t Hoff and French scientist, C. Le Bel in the same year (1874), independently argued that the spatial arrangement of four groups (valencies) around a central carbon is tetrahedral and if all the substituents attached to that carbon are different, the mirror image of the molecule is not superimposed (overlapped) on the molecule; such a carbon is called asymmetric carbon or stereocenter.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 306 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Stereocenter','Central Atom','Steroatom','Steromolecule','NEW','12-304','12-176',NULL),
(240802,'chemistry','Optical activity in some organic compounds.\n \nA) Asymmetry \n\nB) Chiral carbon \n\nC) Non superimposability of mirror images.','All the above','The asymmetry of the molecule along with non superimposability of mirror images is responsible for the optical activity in such organic compounds.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 306 first Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','A','B','C','All the above','NEW','12-304','12-176',NULL),
(240803,'chemistry','An object that cannot be superimposed on its mirror image is called','Chiral','An object that cannot be superimposed on its mirror image is called Chiral. \nNCERT Box ( NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305  )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Chiral','Achiral','Isomer','Stereomer','NEW','12-304','12-176',NULL),
(240804,'chemistry','Chiral Molecules are:\n \na) optically inactive \n\nb) mirror images \n\nc) superimposable. \n\nCorrect Options are:','Only b','The objects which are non- superimposable on their mirror image (like a pair of hands) are said to be chiral and this property is known as chirality. \n\nChiral molecules are optically active. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 306 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','a and c','b and c','Both a and b','Only b','NEW','12-304','12-176',NULL),
(240805,'chemistry','Select the Correct statements among the following: \n\nA) B is mirror image of A. \n\nB) B is rotated by 180° and C is obtained. \n\nC) C is superimposable on A. diagram(n2414)','All A, B, C','All the given statements are correct.\n\nNCERT Figure 10.5 (Reference:NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 306.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and B','Both B and C','Both A and C','All A, B, C','NEW','12-304','12-176','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240805q.png'),
(240806,'chemistry','Select the correct statements in relation to the given diagram among the following: \n\nA) B is mirror image of A\n\nB) B is rotated by 180° to get C \n\nC) C is non superimposable on its mirror image\n\n(n2415)','All A, B, C','All the given statements are correct.\n\nNCERT Figure 10.5 (Reference:NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 306.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and B','Both B and C','Both A and C','All A, B, C','NEW','12-304','12-176','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240806q.png'),
(240807,'chemistry','Which of the following are examples of Chiral molecules?\n  \na) 2-chlorobutane \n\nb)2, 3-dihyroxypropanal (OHC–CHOH–CH₂OH) \n\nc) bromochloro-iodomethane (BrClCHI) \n\nd)2-bromopropanoic acid (H₃C–CHBr–COOH).','All A, B, C, D','Some common examples of chiral molecules such as 2-chlorobutane, 2, 3-dihyroxypropanal, (OHC–CHOH–CH₂OH), bromochloro-iodomethane (BrClCHI), 2-bromopropanoic acid (H₃C–CHBr–COOH)  \n\n(Reference:( NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and B','A, B, C','Both A and C','All A, B, C, D','NEW','12-304','12-176',NULL),
(240808,'chemistry','What does the given diagram of molecule represents about its nature: diagram(n2416)','Chiral molecule','A kind of molecule known as a chiral molecule possesses an unsuperposable mirror counterpart. \n\nThe existence of an asymmetric carbon atom is the characteristic that causes chirality in compounds most frequently. \n\nThe description of an object that cannot be superposed on its mirror counterpart is referred to as "chiral" in general.\n\nNCERT Figure 10.7(Reference:NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Achiral molecule','Chiral molecule','Stereoisomer','Optically inactive molecule','NEW','12-304','12-176','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240808q.png'),
(240809,'chemistry','The stereoisomers related to each other as non- superimposable mirror images are called','Enantiomers','The stereoisomers related to each other as non- superimposable mirror images are called enantiomers.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Enantiomers','Isomer','Stereoisomer','Metamer','NEW','12-304','12-176',NULL),
(121405,'chemistry','In which of the following, functional group isomerism is not possible?','Alkyl halides','Two or more compounds having the same molecular formula but different functional groups are called functional isomers and this phenomenon is termed as functional group isomerism. \n\nAlkyl halides do not show functional isomerism\n\nReference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 348; Paragraph No. 3 )','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Alcohols','Aldehydes','Alkyl halides','Cyanides','NEW','11-348','11-270',NULL),
(240810,'chemistry','Statements true regarding enantiomers are: \n\na) Enantiomers possess identical physical properties namely, melting point, boiling point, refractive index, etc. \n\nb) They only differ with respect to the rotation of plane polarised light. \n\nc) If one of the enantiomer is dextrorotatory, the other will be laevo rotatory.','All a, b, c','Enantiomers possess identical physical properties namely, melting point, boiling point, refractive index. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307 third Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both a and c','All a, b, c','NEW','12-304','12-176',NULL),
(240811,'chemistry','Assertion: A mixture containing two enantiomers in equal proportions will have zero optical rotation.\n \nReason: The rotation due to one isomer will be cancelled by the rotation due to the other isomer.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','A mixture containing two enantiomers in equal proportions will have zero optical rotation, as the rotation due to one isomer will be cancelled by the rotation due to the other isomer. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307 Last Paragraph.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-305','12-177',NULL),
(240812,'chemistry','A mixture containing two enantiomers in equal proportions is called','Both a and b','A mixture containing two enantiomers in equal proportions will have zero optical rotation, as the rotation due to one isomer will be cancelled by the rotation due to the other isomer. \n\nSuch a mixture is known as racemic mixture or racemic modification. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307 Last Paragraph.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Racemic mixture','Racemic modification','Both a and b','Neutral mixture','NEW','12-304','12-175',NULL),
(240813,'chemistry','A racemic mixture is represented by:','Both a and b','A racemic mixture is represented by prefixing dl or (±) before the name, for example (±) butan-2-ol. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307 Last Paragraph.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Prefixing dl','Prefixing (±)','Both a and b','Using abbreviation R','NEW','12-305','12-177',NULL),
(240814,'chemistry','The process of conversion of enantiomer into a racemic mixture is known as.','racemisation','The process of conversion of enantiomer into a racemic mixture is known as racemisation. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307 Last Paragraph.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','elimination','racemisation','enanitomation','substitution','NEW','12-305','12-177',NULL),
(240815,'chemistry','Identify chiral and achiral molecules in each of the following pair of compounds. diagram(n2417)','A is Chiral','A chiral carbon is the one that has all the four substituents different.\n\nNCERT Example 10.8 (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','A is Chiral','B is chiral','Both are chiral','Both are achiral','NEW','12-305','12-177','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240815q.png'),
(240816,'chemistry','Identify chiral and achiral molecules in each of the following pair of compounds. diagram(n2418)','A is Chiral','A chiral carbon is the one that has all the four substituents different.\n\nNCERT Example 10.8( Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','A is Chiral','B is chiral','Both are chiral','Both are achiral','NEW','12-305','12-177','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240816q.png'),
(240817,'chemistry','Identify chiral and achiral molecules in each of the following pair of compounds. diagram(n2419)','A is Chiral','A chiral carbon is the one that has all the four substituents different.\n\nNCERT Example 10.8( Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','A is Chiral','B is chiral','Both are chiral','Both are achiral','NEW','12-305','12-177','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240817q.png'),
(240818,'chemistry','The preservation of the spatial arrangement of bonds to an asymmetric centre during a chemical reaction or transformation is called','Retention','Retention of configuration is the preservation of the spatial arrangement of bonds to an asymmetric centre during a chemical reaction or transformation.  \n\n(Reference: Chemistry Class 12 Pg 308 first Paragraph.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Racemisation','Retention','Inversion','Both A and B','NEW','12-305','12-177','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240818q.png'),
(240819,'chemistry','If during a reaction, no bond to the stereocenter is broken, the product will have the same general configuration of groups around the stereocenter as that of reactant. Such a reaction is said to proceed with','Retention','In general, if during a reaction, no bond to the stereocenter is broken, the product will have the same general configuration of groups around the stereocenter as that of reactant. \n\nSuch a reaction is said to proceed with retention of the configuration. \n \n(Reference: Chemistry Class 12 Pg 308,second Paragraph.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Racemisation','Retention','Inversion','Both A and B','NEW','12-305','12-177',NULL),
(240820,'chemistry','Configuration of reaction is diagram(n2420)','Retention','In general, if during a reaction, no bond to the stereocenter is broken, the product will have the same general configuration of groups around the stereocenter as that of reactant. \n\nSuch a reaction is said to proceed with retention of the configuration.  \n\n(Reference: Chemistry Class 12 Pg 308,second Paragraph.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Racemisation','Retention','Inversion','Both A and B','NEW','12-305','12-177','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240820q.png'),
(240821,'chemistry','Process occurred leading to formation A is: diagram(n2421)','Retention','If (A) is the only compound obtained, the process is called retention of configuration. Note that configuration has been rotated in A. \n\nIf (B) is the only compound obtained, the process is called inversion of configuration. Configuration has been inverted in B. \n\nIf a 50:50 mixture of A and B is obtained then the process is called racemisation and the product is optically inactive, as one isomer will rotate the plane polarised light in the direction opposite to another. \n\nNCERT Figure ( NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 308.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Racemisation','Retention','Inversion','Both A and B','NEW','12-305','12-177','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240821q.png'),
(260335,'chemistry','Name the reaction shown in the diagram(n26019)','Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction','When benzene or substituted benzene is treated with acid chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, it affords the corresponding ketone. \n\nThis reaction is known as Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction.\n\nReference : (NCERT - Class 12 Page-364 Paragraph -2)','Organic Chemistry >> Aldehydes,ketones & carboxylic acids >> Preparation of Aldehydes and Ketones',NULL,'3','mcq','Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction','Friedel-Craft','Acylation reaction','Friedels reaction','NEW','12-364','12-234','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/260335q.png'),
(240822,'chemistry','Process occurred leading to formation B is: diagram(n2421)','Inversion','If (A) is the only compound obtained, the process is called retention of configuration. Note that configuration has been rotated in A. \n\nIf (B) is the only compound obtained, the process is called inversion of configuration. Configuration has been inverted in B. \n\nIf a 50:50 mixture of A and B is obtained then the process is called racemisation and the product is optically inactive, as one isomer will rotate the plane polarised light in the direction opposite to another. \n\nNCERT Figure ( NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 308.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Racemisation','Retention','Inversion','Both A and B','NEW','12-306','12-178','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240822q.png'),
(240823,'chemistry','Process occurred leading to formation A+B is: diagram(n2421)','Racemisation','If (A) is the only compound obtained, the process is called retention of configuration. Note that configuration has been rotated in A. \n\nIf (B) is the only compound obtained, the process is called inversion of configuration. Configuration has been inverted in B. \n\nIf a 50:50 mixture of A and B is obtained then the process is called racemisation and the product is optically inactive, as one isomer will rotate the plane polarised light in the direction opposite to another. \n\nNCERT Figure (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 308.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Racemisation','Retention','Inversion','Both A and B','NEW','12-306','12-178','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240823q.png'),
(240824,'chemistry','Configuration of A in the reaction is: diagram(n2421)','Rotated','If (A) is the only compound obtained, the process is called retention of configuration. Note that configuration has been rotated in A. \n\nIf (B) is the only compound obtained, the process is called inversion of configuration. Configuration has been inverted in B. \n\nIf a 50:50 mixture of A and B is obtained then the process is called racemisation and the product is optically inactive, as one isomer will rotate the plane polarised light in the direction opposite to another.\n \nNCERT Figure ( NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 308.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Rotated','Inverted','Racemised','Both A and B','NEW','12-306','12-178','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240824q.png'),
(240901,'chemistry','Mixing the solution of freshly prepared diazonium salt with what results in the replacement of the diazonium group by –Cl or –Br?','Both A and B','Mixing the solution of freshly prepared diazonium salt with cuprous chloride or cuprous bromide results in the replacement of the diazonium group by –Cl or –Br. \n \n(Reference: Class 12 NCERT Chemistry Page 296, Paragraph 3 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Cuprous Chloride','Cuprous Bromide','Cuprous Iodide','Both A and B','NEW','12-296','12-166',NULL),
(240902,'chemistry','A secondary alkyl halide will prefer:','Either Sɴ1 or Sɴ2 or elimination depending upon the strength of base/nucleophile.','A primary alkyl halide will prefer a Sɴ2 reaction, a secondary halide- Sɴ2 or elimination depending upon the strength of base/nucleophile and a tertiary halide- Sɴ1 or elimination depending upon the stability of carbocation or the more substituted alkene. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Box  NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Sɴ2','Sₙ3','Either Sɴ1 or Sɴ2 or elimination depending upon the strength of base/nucleophile.','Sɴ1 or elimination depending upon the stability of carbocation.','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(240903,'chemistry','A tertiary alkyl halide will prefer:','Sɴ1 or elimination depending upon the stability of carbocation.','A primary alkyl halide will prefer a Sɴ2 reaction, a secondary halide- Sɴ2 or elimination depending upon the strength of base/nucleophile and a tertiary halide- Sɴ1 or elimination depending upon the stability of carbocation or the more substituted alkene. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Box  NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Sₙ3','Sₙ4','Either Sɴ1 or Sɴ2 or elimination depending upon the strength of base/nucleophile.','Sɴ1 or elimination depending upon the stability of carbocation.','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(240904,'chemistry','What does the given diagram depicts: diagram(n2426)','Elimination','Atoms are eliminated as molecules or compounds during an elimination reaction. \n\nA metal, an acid, or a base typically catalyse elimination. \n\nSubstitution reactions and elimination reactions frequently clash. \n\nElimination usually results in formation of an alkene.\n\nNCERT Figure (Reference:NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Substitution','Elimination','Racemisation','Self condensation','NEW','12-308','12-180','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240904q.png'),
(240905,'chemistry','What does the given diagram depicts: diagram(n2427)','Substitution','Which route will be taken up depends upon the nature of alkyl halide, strength and size of base/nucleophile and reaction conditions. \n\nThus, a bulkier nucleophile will prefer to act as a base and abstracts a proton rather than approach a tetravalent carbon atom (steric reasons) and vice versa. \n\nSimilarly, a primary alkyl halide will prefer a SN₂ reaction, a secondary halide- SN₂ or elimination depending upon the strength of base/nucleophile and a tertiary halide- SN₁ or elimination depending upon the stability of carbocation or the more substituted alkene.\n\nNCERT Figure (Reference:NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Substitution','Elimination','Racemisation','Self condensation','NEW','12-308','12-180','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240905q.png'),
(240906,'chemistry','Most organic chlorides, bromides and iodides react with certain metals to give compounds containing carbon-metal bonds. Such compounds are known as:','Organo-metallic compounds','Most organic chlorides, bromides and iodides react with certain metals to give compounds containing carbon-metal bonds. \n\nSuch compounds are known as organo-metallic compounds. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Open-chain compound','Organo-metallic compounds','Alicyclic compound','Aromatic compounds','NEW','12-308','12-180','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240906q.png'),
(240907,'chemistry','An important class of organo-metallic compounds was discovered by','Victor Grignard','An important class of organo-metallic compounds discovered by Victor Grignard in 1900 is alkyl magnesium halide, RMgX, referred as Grignard Reagents. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Alexander Zaitsev','William Nicol','Victor Grignard','Marie Curie','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240907q.png'),
(30432,'chemistry','At the end of each period is a noble gas element with a closed valence shell _______ configuration.','ns^2np^6','The outermost electronic configuration varies from ns2np1 to ns2np6in each period. \n\nAt the end of each period is a noble gas element with a closed valence shell ns2np6 configuration.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 84; Paragraph No. 2)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','ns^2np^6','ns^2np^4','ns^1np^6','ns^1np^4','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(240908,'chemistry','While attempting to find an efficient catalyst for the process of methylation, who noted that Zn in diethyl ether had been used for this purpose and wondered whether the Mg/ether combination might be successful?','Victor Grignard','While attempting to find an efficient catalyst for the process of methylation, he noted that Zn in diethyl ether had been used for this purpose and wondered whether the Mg/ether combination might be successful.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Box Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 311 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Victor Grignard','Jacobus Hendricus','William Nicole','Marie Curie','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240908q.png'),
(240909,'chemistry','In 1912, who was awarded Nobel Prize?','Victor Grignard','In 1910, Grignard obtained a professorship at the University of Nancy and in 1912, he was awarded the Nobel prize for Chemistry which he shared with Paul Sabatier who had made advances in nickel catalysed hydrogenation.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Box Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 311 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Victor Grignard','Jacobus Hendricus','William Nicole','Marie Curie','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240909q.png'),
(240910,'chemistry','An important class of organo-metallic compounds discovered by Victor Grignard in 1900 is','alkyl magnesium halide','An important class of organo-metallic compounds discovered by Victor Grignard in 1900 is alkyl magnesium halide, RMgX, referred as Grignard Reagents. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','alkyl magnesium carboxylic acid','alkyl magnesium halide','alkyl sodium halide','alkyl halide','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240910q.png'),
(240911,'chemistry','Grignard reagents are obtained by the reaction of haloalkanes with which metal in dry ether?','Magnesium','These reagents are obtained by the reaction of haloalkanes with magnesium metal in dry ether. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Magnesium','Calcium','sodium','caesium','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240911q.png'),
(240912,'chemistry','Identify the diagram: diagram(n2428)','All of the above','An important class of organo-metallic compounds discovered by Victor Grignard in 1900 is alkyl magnesium halide, RMgX, referred as Grignard Reagents. \n\nThese reagents are obtained by the reaction of haloalkanes with magnesium metal in dry ether.  \n\n(Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','alkyl magnesium halide','organo-metallic compounds','Grignard Reagent','All of the above','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240912q.png'),
(240913,'chemistry','Correct statements regarding Grignard Reagents are: \n\nA) the carbon-magnesium bond is covalent but highly polar. \n\nB) carbon pulls electrons from electropositive magnesium. \n\nC) the magnesium halogen bond is essentially ionic.','All of the above','In the Grignard reagent, the carbon-magnesium bond is covalent but highly polar, with carbon pulling electrons from electropositive magnesium; the magnesium halogen bond is essentially ionic. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 311 first Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','A and B','A and C','Only B','All of the above','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240913q.png'),
(240914,'chemistry','Select the options with which Grignard Reagent reacts to give hydrocarbons:  \n\nA) any source of proton \n\nB) alcohols \n\nC) Amines \n\nD) water','All of the above','Grignard reagents are highly reactive and react with any source of proton to give hydrocarbons. \n\nEven water, alcohols, amines are sufficiently acidic to convert them to corresponding hydrocarbons. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 311 first Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','A and B','A and C','Only B','All of the above','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240914q.png'),
(240915,'chemistry','Grignard reagents were first reported in','1900','An important class of organo-metallic compounds discovered by Victor Grignard in 1900 is alkyl magnesium halide, RMgX, referred as Grignard Reagents.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Box Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 310 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','1919','1900','1901','1917','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240915q.png'),
(240916,'chemistry','Major Product of this reaction is diagram(n2429)','Hydrocarbon','Grignard reagents are highly reactive and react with any source of proton to give hydrocarbons. \n\nEven water, alcohols, amines are sufficiently acidic to convert them to corresponding hydrocarbons. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 311 second Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Alcohol','Hydrocarbon','Aldehyde','Ketone','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240916q.png'),
(240917,'chemistry','Assertion: Reactions dealing with Grignard reagents is carried out in dry ether. \n\nReason: It is necessary to avoid even traces of moisture from a Grignard reagent.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','It is necessary to avoid even traces of moisture from a Grignard reagent. \n\nThat is why reaction is carried out in dry ether.\n \n(Reference:NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 311 third Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/240917q.png'),
(240918,'chemistry','If the reagent is NaOH, and nucleophile is OH then substitution product is','ROH','If the reagent is NaOH, and nucleophile is OH then substitution product is ROH.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Table 10.4 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 300 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','ROH','RNC','RCN','R-I','NEW','12-299','12-170','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/240918q.png'),
(240919,'chemistry','Alkyl halides react with sodium in dry ether to give hydrocarbons containing double the number of carbon atoms present in the halide. This reaction is known as:','Wurtz Reaction','Alkyl halides react with sodium in dry ether to give hydrocarbons containing double the number of carbon atoms present in the halide. \n\nThis reaction is known as Wurtz reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 311 fourth Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'1','mcq','Swartz Reaction','Wurtz Reaction','Fitting reaction','Sterks reaction','NEW','12-309','12-181',NULL),
(30433,'chemistry','Identify the placement of the elements of Group 3 to 12 in the Periodic Table:','Centre','The d-Block Elements; are the elements of Group 3 to 12 in the centre of the Periodic Table. \n\nAs shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 83-84; Paragraph No. 3; diagram(ne0319)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronic Configuration of Elements',NULL,'1','mcq','Left corner','Right corner','Centre','Bottom','NEW','11-084','11-084',NULL),
(240920,'chemistry','Order of reactivity of the following compounds in Sɴ1 reaction is:','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br < (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br < CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ < (CH₃)₃CBr','Order of reactivity of the following compounds in Sɴ1 reaction is: \n\nCH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br < (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br < CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ < (CH₃)₃CBr\n\n(Reference: NCERT Example 10.7 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 304)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br < (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br < CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ < (CH₃)₃CBr','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br < (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br < (CH₃)₃CBr < CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ < (CH₃)₃CBr','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(240921,'chemistry','Order of reactivity of the following compounds in Sɴ2 reaction is:','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr','Order of reactivity of the following compounds in Sɴ2 reaction is: \n\nCH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr\n\n(Reference: NCERT Example 10.7 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 304)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br < (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br < CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ < (CH₃)₃CBr','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br < (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br < (CH₃)₃CBr < CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ < (CH₃)₃CBr','NEW','12-303','12-175',NULL),
(240922,'chemistry','What does the given diagram depicts? diagram(n2430)','Wurtz Reaction','Alkyl halides react with sodium in dry ether to give hydrocarbons containing double the number of carbon atoms present in the halide. \n\nThis reaction is known as Wurtz reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 311 fourth Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Swartz Reaction','Wurtz Reaction','Fitting reaction','Sterks reaction','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240922q.png'),
(240923,'chemistry','Assertion: Aryl halides are extremely less reactive towards nucleophilic substitution reactions. \n\nReason: C—Cl bond acquires a partial double bond character due to resonance and bond cleavage in haloarene is difficult.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','C—Cl bond acquires a partial double bond character due to resonance. \n\nAs a result, the bond cleavage in haloarene is difficult than haloalkane and therefore, they are less reactive towards nucleophilic substitution reaction. \n\n( Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 311 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-309','12-181',NULL),
(240924,'chemistry','Assertion: C—Cl bond length in haloalkane is 177pm while in haloarene is 169 pm. \n\nReason: The sp²  hybridised carbon with a greater s-character is more electronegative and can hold the electron pair of C—X bond less tightly than sp³-hybridised carbon in haloalkane with more s-character','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','The sp²  hybridised carbon with a greater s-character is more electronegative and can hold the electron pair of C—X bond more tightly than sp³-hybridised carbon in haloalkane with less s-character. \n\nThus, C—Cl bond length in haloalkane is 177pm while in haloarene is 169 pm. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 312 second Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-310','12-182',NULL),
(240925,'chemistry','Assertion: Haloarenes are less reactive than haloalkanes towards nucleophilic substitution reaction. \n\nReason: It is difficult to break a longer bond than a shorter bond.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','C—Cl bond length in haloalkane is 177pm while in haloarene is 169 pm. \n\nSince it is difficult to break a shorter bond than a longer bond, therefore, haloarenes are less reactive than haloalkanes towards nucleophilic substitution reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 312 second Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-310','12-182',NULL),
(240926,'chemistry','What is the product of reaction in the one given below? diagram(n2431)','Phenol','Chlorobenzene can be converted into phenol by heating in aqueous sodium hydroxide solution at a temperature of 623K and a pressure of 300 atmospheres.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 312 fifth Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Phenol','Benzene','Nitrobenzene','Benzyl alcohol','NEW','12-310','12-182','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240926q.png'),
(240927,'chemistry','What does the given diagram depicts? diagram(n2432)','Nitrophenol','The presence of an electron withdrawing group (-NO₂ ) at ortho- and Para-positions increases the reactivity of haloarenes. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 312 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Phenol','Di-nitrophenol','Nitrophenol','Tri-Nitrophenol','NEW','12-310','12-182','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240927q.png'),
(240928,'chemistry','What does the given diagram depicts? diagram(n2433)','Di-nitrophenol','The presence of an electron withdrawing group (-NO₂ ) at ortho- and Paragraph-positions increases the reactivity of haloarenes. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 312 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Phenol','Di-nitrophenol','Nitrophenol','Tri-Nitrophenol','NEW','12-311','12-183','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240928q.png'),
(240929,'chemistry','What does the given diagram depicts? diagram(n2434)','Tri-Nitrophenol','The presence of an electron withdrawing group (-NO₂ ) at ortho- and Paragraph-positions increases the reactivity of haloarenes. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 312 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Phenol','Di-nitrophenol','Nitrophenol','Tri-Nitrophenol','NEW','12-311','12-183','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240929q.png'),
(71511,'chemistry','Which of the following will produce a buffer solution when mixed in equal volumes?','1 mol /dm³ NH₄OH and 0.05 mol /dm³ HCl','In 1 mol /dm³ NH₄OH and 0.05 mol /dm³ HCl , all HCl will be neutralized and NH₄Cl will be formed. Also some NH₄OH will be left unneutralized. Thus, the final solution will contain NH₄OH and NH₄Cl and hence will form a buffer.','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'2','mcq','1 mol /dm³ NH₄OH and 0.1 mol /dm³ HCl','0.05 mol /dm³ NH₄OH and 0.1 mol /dm³ HC1','1 mol /dm³ NH₄OH and 0.05 mol /dm³ HCl','1 mol /dm³ CH₃COONa and 0.1 mol /dm³ NaOH','NEW','11-228','11-204',NULL),
(240930,'chemistry','Assertion: NO₂  group show its effect only at ortho- and Paragraph- positions and not at meta- position. \n\nReason: in case of meta-nitrobenzene, none of the resonating structures bear the negative charge on carbon atom bearing the –NO₂  group.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','no effect on reactivity of haloarenes is observed by the presence of electron withdrawing group at meta-position. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 313 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-312','12-184',NULL),
(240931,'chemistry','Assertion: substitution reactions in haloalkanes are not specifically directed to ortho or para positions\n \nReason: substitution can occur at any position where a hydrogen atom is available for replacement by the halogen.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Haloarenes undergo the usual electrophilic reactions of the benzene ring such as halogenation, nitration, sulphonation and Friedel-Crafts reactions. \n\nHalogen atom besides being slightly deactivating is o, p- directing; therefore, further substitution occurs at ortho- and Paragraph- positions with respect to the halogen atom. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-312','12-184',NULL),
(240932,'chemistry','Major Product in the given reaction is: diagram(n2435)','1,4-dichlorobenzene','Major Product in the given reaction is 1,4 - dichlorobenzene.\n\nThe chlorine is ortho-para director so the most stable compound formed is para.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Reaction NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314  )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','1-chloro-4-benzene','1,4-dichlorobenzene','4-chlorobenzenesulphonic acid','1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene','NEW','12-312','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240932q.png'),
(240933,'chemistry','Major Product in the given reaction is: diagram(n2436)','1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene','Major Product in the given reaction is 1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene.\n\nThe reaction is nitration. \n\nThe chlorine is ortho-para director so the most stable compound formed is para.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Reaction NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','1-chloro-4-benzene','1,4-dichlorobenzene','4-chlorobenzenesulphonic acid','1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene','NEW','12-312','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240933q.png'),
(240934,'chemistry','Major Product in the given reaction is diagram(n2437)','4-chlorobenzenesulphonic acid','The chlorine is ortho-para director so the most stable compound formed is para.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Reaction NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314  )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','1-chloro-4-benzene','1,4-dichlorobenzene','4-chlorobenzenesulphonic acid','1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene','NEW','12-312','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240934q.png'),
(240935,'chemistry','Major Product in the given reaction is diagram(n2438)','1-chloro-4-methylbenzene','The chlorine is ortho-para director so the most stable compound formed is para.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Reaction NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','1-chloro-2-methylbenzene','1-chloro-4-methylbenzene','2-chloroacetophenone','4-chloroacetophenone','NEW','12-313','12-185','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240935q.png'),
(240936,'chemistry','Major Product in the given reaction is diagram(n2439)','4-chloroacetophenone','The chlorine is ortho-para director so the most stable compound formed is para.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Reaction NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','1-chloro-2-methylbenzene','1-chloro-4-methylbenzene','2-chloroacetophenone','4-chloroacetophenone','NEW','12-313','12-185','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/240936q.png'),
(240937,'chemistry','Assertion: Reactivity is controlled by the stronger inductive effect and orientation is controlled by resonance effect. \n\nReason: The resonance effect tends to oppose the inductive effect for the attack at ortho- and Paragraph- positions and hence makes the deactivation less for ortho- and Paragraph- attack.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','The resonance effect tends to oppose the inductive effect for the attack at ortho- and para positions and hence makes the deactivation less for ortho- and para attack. \n\nReactivity is thus controlled by the stronger inductive effect and orientation is controlled by resonance effect.\n\nNCERT Example 10.9 NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 315)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Chemical Reactions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-313','12-185',NULL),
(241001,'chemistry','Due to its objectionable smell, what has been replaced by other formulations?','Iodoform','Due to its objectionable smell, it has been replaced by other formulations containing iodine. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317, Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Chloroform','Tetrachloromethane','Iodoform','Bromoform','NEW','12-315','12-187',NULL),
(241002,'chemistry','Carbon compounds containing more than one halogen atom are usually referred to as','Polyhalogen Compounds','Carbon compounds containing more than one halogen atom are usually referred to as polyhalogen compounds. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Polyhalogen Compounds','Polycarbon Compounds','Monohalogen Compounds','Dihalogen compounds','NEW','12-315','12-187',NULL),
(241003,'chemistry','Which compound is widely used as a solvent as a paint remover, as a propellant in aerosols, and as a process solvent in the manufacture of drugs?','Dichloromethane','Dichloromethane is widely used as a solvent as a paint remover, as a propellant in aerosols, and as a process solvent in the manufacture of drugs. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187',NULL),
(241004,'chemistry','In iodoform, antiseptic properties are due to the liberation of ?','Free iodine','Antiseptic properties are due to the liberation of free iodine and not due to iodoform itself. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317, Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Free chlorine','Free iodine','Free bromine','Free fluorine','NEW','12-315','12-187',NULL),
(241005,'chemistry','Read the statements and choose the option for which the statements are valid.\n \nA) It is used as a metal cleaning and finishing solvent.\n \nB) It harms the human central nervous system. \n\nC) Direct contact with the eyes can burn the cornea.','Dichloromethane','It is also used as a metal cleaning and finishing solvent. Methylene chloride harms the human central nervous system. \n\nExposure to lower levels of methylene chloride in air can lead to slightly impaired hearing and vision. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187',NULL),
(241006,'chemistry','Higher levels of which compound in air cause dizziness, nausea, tingling and numbness in the fingers and toes?','Dichloromethane','Higher levels of methylene chloride in air cause dizziness, nausea, tingling and numbness in the fingers and toes. \n\nIn humans, direct skin contact with methylene chloride causes intense burning and mild redness of the skin. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 second Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187',NULL),
(241007,'chemistry','In humans, direct skin contact with which compound causes intense burning and mild redness of the skin?','Dichloromethane','Higher levels of methylene chloride in air cause dizziness, nausea, tingling and numbness in the fingers and toes. \n\nIn humans, direct skin contact with methylene chloride causes intense burning and mild redness of the skin.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 second Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187',NULL),
(241008,'chemistry','The major use of which compound today is in the production of the freon refrigerant R-22?','Chloroform','The major use of chloroform today is in the production of the freon refrigerant R-22.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 third Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/241008q.png'),
(241009,'chemistry','Breathing about 900 parts of which compound per million parts of air (900 parts per million) for a short time can cause dizziness, fatigue, and headache?','Chloroform','Inhaling chloroform vapours depresses the central nervous system. \n\nBreathing about 900 parts of chloroform per million parts of air (900 parts per million) for a short time can cause dizziness, fatigue, and headache. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 third Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/241009q.png'),
(241010,'chemistry','Which compound is slowly oxidised by air in the presence of light to an extremely poisonous gas, carbonyl chloride?','Chloroform','Chloroform is slowly oxidised by air in the presence of light to an extremely poisonous gas, carbonyl chloride, also known as phosgene. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 third Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241010q.png'),
(241011,'chemistry','Chloroform was once used as a general anaesthetic in surgery but has been replaced by less toxic, safer anaesthetics such as','Ether','It was once used as a general anaesthetic in surgery but has been replaced by less toxic, safer anaesthetics, such as ether. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 third Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Ether','Aldehyde','Alcohol','Ketone','NEW','12-315','12-187','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241011q.png'),
(241012,'chemistry','Carbonyl chloride is also called','Phosgene','Carbonyl chloride is also called Phosgene\n\n(Reference: NCERT Reaction  NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Phosphene','Phosgene','Phenyl','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241012q.png'),
(241013,'chemistry','The product of the reaction is. diagram(n2444)','Phosgene','The product of the reaction is phosgene. \n\nChloroform is slowly oxidised by air in the presence of light to an extremely poisonous gas, carbonyl chloride, also known as phosgene.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Reaction  NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Phosphene','Phosgene','Phenyl','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241013q.png'),
(241014,'chemistry','What was earlier used as an antiseptic?','Iodoform','Iodoform was used earlier as an antiseptic. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317, Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Chloroform','Tetrachloromethane','Iodoform','Bromoform','NEW','12-315','12-187','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241014q.png'),
(241015,'chemistry','Assertion: Chloroform is stored in closed dark coloured bottles completely filled so that air is kept out. \n\nReason: Chloroform is slowly oxidised by air in the presence of light to an extremely poisonous gas, carbonyl chloride, also known as phosgene.','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Chloroform is slowly oxidised by air in the presence of light to an extremely poisonous gas, carbonyl chloride, also known as phosgene. \n\nIt is therefore stored in closed dark coloured bottles completely filled so that air is kept out. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 third Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-315','12-187','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/241015q.png'),
(241016,'chemistry','Until the mid 1960s, which compound was widely used as a cleaning fluid, both in industry, as a degreasing agent, and in the home, as a spot remover and as fire extinguisher?','Carbon tetrachloride','Until the mid 1960s, it was also widely used as a cleaning fluid, both in industry, as a degreasing agent, and in the home, as a spot remover and as fire extinguisher. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 last Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/241016q.png'),
(241017,'chemistry','Exposure to which compound can make the heart beat irregularly or stop?','Carbon tetrachloride','Exposure to CCl₄ can make the heart beat irregularly or stop.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 last Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/241017q.png'),
(241018,'chemistry','Which compound causes liver cancer in humans?','Carbon tetrachloride','There is some evidence that exposure to carbon tetrachloride causes liver cancer in humans. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 last Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-315','12-187','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241018q.png'),
(241019,'chemistry','The chlorofluorocarbon compounds of methane and ethane are collectively known as','Freons','The chlorofluorocarbon compounds of methane and ethane are collectively known as freons. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-316','12-188',NULL),
(241020,'chemistry','Which compounds are extremely stable, unreactive, non-toxic, non- corrosive and easily liquefiable gases?','Freons','They are extremely stable, unreactive, non-toxic, non- corrosive and easily liquefiable gases. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Polyhalogen Compounds',NULL,'1','mcq','Freons','Dichloromethane','Chloroform','Carbon tetrachloride','NEW','12-316','12-188',NULL),
(241101,'chemistry','Phosgene is a common name for','Carbonyl Chloride','Chloroform is slowly oxidised by air in the presence of light to an extremely poisonous gas, carbonyl chloride, also known as phosgene. \n\nIt is therefore stored in closed dark coloured bottles completely filled so that air is kept out. \n\nThe common name of phosgene is carbon chloride.\n\n(NEET 1998)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Phosphoryl chloride','Carbonyl Chloride','Carbon dioxide and phosphine','Thinonyl chloride','NEW','12-315','12-187',NULL),
(241102,'chemistry','Industrial preparation of chloroform employs  acetone and','Calcium hypochlorite','The reaction is carried out in following steps. \n\n(NEET 1993)\n\nRefer diagram(ne2401)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Phosgene','Calcium hypochlorite','Chlorine gas','Sodium chloride','NEW','12-315','12-187',NULL),
(241103,'chemistry','Which of the following is an optically active compound?','2-chlorobutane','An optically active compound is one that can rotate optically. \n\nThe optically active compound should have a chiral carbon which in this case 2-chlorobutane has. \n\nThus it is optically active.\n\n(NEET 1989)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1-butanol','1-propanol','2-chlorobutane','4-hydroxyheptane','NEW','12-304','12-175',NULL),
(241104,'chemistry','Benzene reacts with n-propyl chloride in the  presence of anhydrous AlCl₃ to give','isopropylbenzene','The reaction taking place here is Friedel-craft alkylation. \n\nAn electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction called a Friedel-Crafts alkylation occurs when an aromatic ring\'s pi bond attacks a carbocation, leading to the replacement of one of the aromatic protons with an alkyl group. \n\nThe electrophile produced here has a secondary carbon adjacent to the carbocation. \n\nDue to which it rearranges to a more stable secondary carbocation. \nThus giving the final product as isopropylbenzene. \n\n(NEET 1993)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','3-propyl-1-chlorobenzene','n-propylbenzene','no reaction','isopropylbenzene','NEW','12-312','12-184',NULL),
(241105,'chemistry','Grignard reagent is prepared by the reaction between','Magnesium and alkyl halide','Most organic chlorides, bromides and iodides react with certain metals to give compounds containing carbon-metal bonds. \n\nSuch compounds are known as organo-metallic compounds.\n\nAn important class of organo-metallic compounds discovered by Victor Grignard in 1900 is alkyl magnesium halide, RMgX, referred as Grignard Reagents. \n\nThese reagents are obtained by the reaction of haloalkanes with magnesium metal in dry ether.\n\n(NEET 1994)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Magnesium and alkane','Magnesium and aromatic carbon','Zinc and alkyl halide','Magnesium and alkyl halide','NEW','12-308','12-180',NULL),
(214106,'chemistry','XeF₆ has a __________ molecular structure.','distorted octahedral','XeF₆ has a distorted octahedral molecular structure.\n(Reference\' (NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Page No. 210 Paragraph No. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> The p-Block Elements >> Group 18 Elements - Chemical Properties',NULL,'3','mcq','distorted octahedral','Square pyramidal','Square planar','Pyramidal','NEW','12-210',NULL,NULL),
(241201,'chemistry','Which of the following statement/s are/is correct about haloalkanes?\n\nI) They persist in environment due to resistant to breakdown.\n\nII) They occur in nature.\n\nIII) They have no application in industries.\n\nIV) They are clinically useful.','I), II), IV)','Halogenated compounds persist in the environment due to their resistance to breakdown by soil bacteria. \n\nMany halogen containing organic compounds occur in nature and some of these are clinically useful. \n\nThese classes of compounds find wide applications in industry as well as in dayto- day life.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 289 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I), III), IV)','I), II), IV)','II)','I), II), III)','NEW','12-289','12-159',NULL),
(241202,'chemistry','The homologous series of haloalkanes can be represented by general formula:','CₙH₂ₙ+₁X','In alkyl halides, the halogen atom is bonded to an alkyl group (R).\n\nThey form a homologous series represented by CₙH₂ₙ+₁X.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 290 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CₙH₂ₙ-₁X','CₙH₂ₙ+₁Xₙ','CₙH₂ₙ+₁X','X₂CₙH₂ₙ+₁','NEW','12-290','12-160',NULL),
(241203,'chemistry','For dihalogen derivatives of haloarenes, which prefixes are commonly used?','o-, p-, m-','For dihalogen derivatives, the prefixes o-, m-, p- are used in common system.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 291 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','mono, di, tri','n-, iso-, tert-','gem, vic','o-, p-, m-','NEW','12-291','12-163',NULL),
(241204,'chemistry','The dihaloalkanes having the same type of halogen atoms are named as:','both alkylidene and alkylene dihalides','The dihaloalkanes having the same type of halogen atoms are named as alkylidene or alkylene dihalides.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 291 Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','alkylidene','alkylene dihalides','geminal halides','both alkylidene and alkylene dihalides','NEW','12-291','12-163',NULL),
(241205,'chemistry','In a dihaloalkane when both the halogen atoms are present on the same carbon atom, it is known as_______.','all of above','The dihalo-compounds having both the halogen atoms are further classified as geminal halides or gem-dihalides when both the halogen atoms are present on the same carbon atom of the chain and vicinal halides or vic-dihalides when halogen atoms are present on adjacent carbon atoms.\n\nIn common name system, gem-dihalides are named as alkylidene halides and vic-dihalides are named as alkylene dihalides. \n\nIn IUPAC system, they are named as dihaloalkanes.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 291 Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','geminal halides','gem-dihalides','dihaloalkanes','all of above','NEW','12-291','12-163',NULL),
(241206,'chemistry','In a dihaloalkane when the halogen atoms are present on the adjacent carbon atom, it is known as_______.','all of above','The dihalo-compounds having both the halogen atoms are further classified as geminal halides or gem-dihalides when both the halogen atoms are present on the same carbon atom of the chain and vicinal halides or vic-dihalides when halogen atoms are present on adjacent carbon atoms. \n\nIn common name system, gem-dihalides are named as alkylidene halides and vic-dihalides are named as alkylene dihalides. \n\nIn IUPAC system, they are named as dihaloalkanes.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 291 Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','vicinal halides','vic-dihalides','dihaloalkanes','all of above','NEW','12-291','12-163','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/241206q.png'),
(241207,'chemistry','What does free radical chlorination or bromination of alkanes gives?','a complex mixture of isomeric mono- and polyhaloalkanes','Free radical chlorination or bromination of alkanes gives a complex mixture of isomeric mono- and polyhaloalkanes, which is difficult to separate as pure compounds. \n\nConsequently, the yield of any single compound is low.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 294 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','monohaloalkanes','polyhaloalkanes','cyclic haloalkanes','a complex mixture of isomeric mono- and polyhaloalkanes','NEW','12-294','12-166',NULL),
(241208,'chemistry','What is the product of reaction in the one given below? diagram(n2497)','R-I','Free radical chlorination or bromination of alkanes gives a complex mixture of isomeric mono- and polyhaloalkanes, which is difficult to separate as pure compounds. \n\nConsequently, the yield of any single compound is low.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 294 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','NaI','R-X','NaOH','R-I','NEW','12-296','12-168','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241208q.png'),
(241209,'chemistry','Given below are two statements.\n\nAssertion: During Sandmeyer\'s reaction,replacement of the diazonium group by iodine does not require the presence of cuprous halide.\n\nReason: Because iodide may be quickly and irreversibly oxidised, cuprous halide does not need to be used as a catalyst in this reaction. \n\nTherefore, unlike the Sandmeyer reaction, which uses cuprous halide as a catalyst to convert aryl diazonium salt into aryl halide, this reaction does not require cuprous halide.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Replacement of the diazonium group by iodine does not require the presence of cuprous halide and is done simply by shaking the diazonium salt with potassium iodide.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 296 Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are true and reason is a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Both assertion and reason are true but reason is not a correct explanation of the  assertion.','Assertion is true but the reason is false.','Both assertion and reason are false.','NEW','12-297','12-167',NULL),
(241210,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct about haloalkanes?\n\nI) Alkyl halides are colourless when pure.\n\nII) Bromides and iodides develop colour when exposed to light.\n\nIII) Volatile halogen compounds have sweet smell.\n\nIV) They are all solides.','I), II), III)','Alkyl halides are colourless when pure. \n\nHowever, bromides and iodides develop colour when exposed to light. \n\nMany volatile halogen compounds have sweet smell.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 297 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I), III), IV)','I), II), IV)','II), IV)','I), II), III)','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(241211,'chemistry','Higher members of alkyl halides are _____.','liquids or solids','Higher members of alkyl halides are liquids or solids.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','liquids','solids','gases','liquids or solids','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(241212,'chemistry','Molecules of organic halogen compounds are generally _______.','polar','Molecules of organic halogen compounds are generally polar.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','fillup','po,lar,non',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(241213,'chemistry','The boiling points of isomeric haloalkanes _______ with________ in branching.','decreases, increases','The boiling points of isomeric haloalkanes decrease with increase in branching.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','increases, decreases','decreases, increases','decreases, decreases','increases\nincreases','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(241214,'chemistry','Which of the following has lowest boiling point?','2-bromo-2-methylbutane','The boiling points of isomeric haloalkanes decrease with increase in branching.2-bromo-2-methylpropane has the lowest boiling point among the three isomers.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1-bromobutane','2-bromobutane','2-bromo-2-methylbutane','all have same boiling point','NEW','12-298','12-168','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241214q.png'),
(241215,'chemistry','Which one is not the reaction catagory of haloalkanes?','Substitution reaction','The reactions of haloalkanes may be divided into the following categories:\n\n1. Nucleophilic substitution\n\n2. Elimination reactions\n\n3. Reaction with metals.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 299 Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Nucleophilic substitution','Elimination reactions','Reaction with metals.','Substitution reaction','NEW','12-299','12-169','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241215q.png'),
(241216,'chemistry','In what kind of reaction are used as substrate?','All of above','Haloalkanes are substrate in all these reactions.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Nucleophilic substitution','Elimination reactions','Reaction with metals.','All of above','NEW','12-300','12-170','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/241216q.png'),
(241217,'chemistry','During the nucleophilic substitution reaction, the halogen is attached to_______ carbon atom.','sp³ hybridised','It is one of the most useful classes of organic reactions of alkyl halides in which halogen is bonded to sp³ hybridised carbon.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','sp³ hybridised','sp² hybridised','sp hybridised','dsp³ hybridised','NEW','12-300','12-170','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/241217q.png'),
(241218,'chemistry','How many mechanism/s is/are there for nucleophilic substitution reaction?','2','This reaction has been found to proceed by two different mechanisms; Sɴ1 and Sɴ2 mechanism.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 301 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW','12-301','12-171','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/241218q.png'),
(241219,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are correct about substitution reaction?\n\nI) Alkyl halides act as substrate during this reaction.\n\nII) The hydroxyl group acts as the leaving group.\n\nIII) The reaction is initiated by nucleophile.\n\nIV) The reaction only proceeds if the incoming nucleophile is less stronger than the leaving group.','I), III)','Alkyl halides act as substrate during this reaction. \n\nThe halogen group acts as the leaving group. The reaction is initiated by nucleophile. \n\nThe reaction only proceeds if the incoming nucleophile is less stronger than the leaving group.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','only IV)','I), II), IV)','II), IV)','I), III)','NEW','12-300','12-170','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241219q.png'),
(241220,'chemistry','Which of the following statement/s is/are correct about 3D structure of compound?\n\nI) The solid wedge represents the bond coming out of the paper.\n\nII) Dashed line going down the paper.\n\nIII) A straight line representing bond in the plane of the paper.','I), II), III)','The solid wedge represents the bond coming out of the paper, dashed line going down the paper and a straight line representing bond in the plane of the paper.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 301 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I), II),','I), II), III)','II), III)','Only III)','NEW','12-301','12-171','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241220q.png'),
(241221,'chemistry','In transition state, the carbon atom is bonded to______.','all of above','In the transition state, the carbon atom is simultaneously bonded to incoming nucleophile and the outgoing leaving group. \n\nSuch structures are unstable and cannot be isolated.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','incoming nucleophile','outgoing leaving group','five atoms','all of above','NEW','12-302','12-172','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241221q.png'),
(241222,'chemistry','In transition state, the carbon atom is ______.','both isolated and unstable','In the transition state, the carbon atom is simultaneously bonded to incoming nucleophile and the outgoing leaving group. \n\nSuch structures are unstable and cannot be isolated.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','isolated','unstable','sp hybridized','both isolated and unstable','NEW','12-302','12-172',NULL),
(241223,'chemistry','What is order of reactivity of four isomeric bromobutanes in Sɴ2 reaction?','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr','Of the two primary bromides, the carbocation intermediate derived from (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br is more stable than derived from CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br because of greater electron donating inductive effect of (CH₃)₂CH- group. \n\nTherefore, (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br is more reactive than CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br in Sɴ1 reactions. \n\nCH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ is a secondary bromide and (CH₃)₃CBr is a tertiary bromide. \nHence the above order is followed in Sɴ1. \n\nThe reactivity in Sɴ2 reactions follows the reverse order as the steric hindrance around the electrophilic carbon increases in that order.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 305 Example 10.7 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Advanced Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr > CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br < (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br > CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr','CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHCH₂Br < CH₃CH₂CH(Br)CH₃ > (CH₃)₃CBr','NEW','12-305','12-175',NULL),
(241301,'chemistry','The order of reactivity of alcohols given in diagram(n24138) with halogen acids is ____','(C) > (B) > (A)','This reaction follows Sₙ1 mechanisms, so carbocations are formed as intermediate product.\nSince tertiary carbocation is more stable than secondary and primary carbocation , therefore tertiary alcohol will have the highest reactivity to halogen acids.\n\n" Tertiary alkyl halides undergo Sɴ1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of tert -carbocations. \nFurther, greater the stability of carbocation, greater will be its ease of formation from alkyl halide and faster will be the rate of reaction. In case of alkyl halides,  alkyl halides undergo SN1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of carbocations.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 Paragraph 1 )"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(A) > (B) > (C)','(C) > (B) > (A)','(B) > (A) > (C)','(A) > (C) > (B)','NEW','12-304','12-174','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241301q.png'),
(241302,'chemistry','Which of the following alcohols given in diagram(n24140) will yield the corresponding alkyl chloride on reaction with concentrated HCl at room temperature?','d only','The reactions of primary and secondary alcohols with HCl require the presence of a catalyst, ZnCl₂.\n\nWith tertiary alcohols, the reaction is conducted by simply shaking the alcohol with concentrated HCl at room temperature.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a only','b,c','a,c','d only','NEW','12-294','12-162','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241302q.png'),
(241303,'chemistry','Identify the product in the following  reaction diagram(n24109).','1,2-dibromoethane','When ethene (CH2=CH2) reacts with bromine (Br2) in the presence of a solvent such as carbon tetrachloride (CCl4), it undergoes an addition reaction, where two bromine atoms are added to the two carbon atoms of the double bond. The product is 1,2-dibromoethane.\n\n(Reference :NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','1,2-dibromoethane','1-bromo-2-chloroethane','1,2-dichloroethane','1,1,2,2-tetrabromoethane','NEW','12-296','12-164','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241303q.png'),
(241304,'chemistry','Toluene reacts with a halogen in the presence of iron (III) chloride giving ortho and para halo compounds. The reaction is','electrophilic substitution reaction','In this reaction, Cl is generated as electrophile which attacks benzene ring. So it is electrophillic substitution reaction.\nThe mechanism is given in the diagram(ne2418)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','electrophilic elimination reaction','free radical addition reaction','electrophilic substitution reaction','nucleophillic addition reaction','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241305,'chemistry','Ethylidene chloride is a','gem-dihalide','The dihalo-compounds having both the halogen atoms are further classified as geminal halides or gem-dihalides when both the halogen atoms are present on the same carbon atom of the chain and vicinal halides or vic-dihalides when halogen atoms are present on adjacent carbon atoms. \n\nIn common name system, gem-dihalides are named as alkylidene halides and vic-dihalides are named as alkylene dihalides. \n\nIn IUPAC system, they are named as dihaloalkanes.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 291 Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','vic-halide','gem-dihalide','allylic halide','vinylic halide','NEW','12-291','12-161',NULL),
(241306,'chemistry','What is \'A\' in the following reaction diagram(n24110)','c only','The addition takes place according to Markovnikov\'s rule. \n\nThe hydrogen adds to terminal carbon with more hydrogen and chlorine adds to the other carbon .\n\n(Reference: NCERT Example 10.4 Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 297  )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a only','b,c,d','c only','c,d','NEW','12-297','12-165','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241306q.png'),
(241307,'chemistry','Which of the following alkyl halides will undergo  Sɴ1 reaction most readily?','(CH₃)₃C--I','This reaction follows Sₙ1 mechanisms, so carbocations are formed as intermediate product.\n\nDue to large size difference of C and I, C---I bond will be very weak and will undergo Sₙ1 mechanism most readily.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 Paragraph 1 )"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(CH₃)₃C--F','(CH₃)₃C--Cl','(CH₃)₃C--Br','(CH₃)₃C--I','NEW','12-304','12-174',NULL),
(241308,'chemistry','Which is the correct IUPAC name for the compound in the given diagram(n24111)?','1-bromo-2-methylbutane','The common names of alkyl halides are derived by naming the alkyl group followed by the name of halide. In the IUPAC system of nomenclature, alkyl halides are named as halo substituted hydrocarbons.\n\nThere are several sets of rules for IUPAC nomenclature. \n\n1. The longest continuous carbon chain should be found and named.\n2. List the names of the organisations that make up this chain.\n3. Starting at the end of the chain closest to a substituent group, sequentially number the chain.\n4. Indicate each substituent group\'s location with the relevant number and name.\n\n5. Put the name together by listing the groups alphabetically using the whole name. (e.g., cyclopropyl before isobutyl). The chlorine is attached to the secondary carbon atom in propane which is the middle or the second one.\n\n(Reference  :NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 291; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1-bromo-2-ethylpropane','1-bromo-2-ethyl-2-methylethane','1-bromo-2-methylbutane','2-methyl-1-bromobutane','NEW','12-291','12-161','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241308q.png'),
(241309,'chemistry','What should be the correct IUPAC name for diethylbromomethane?','3-bromopentane','Structure of the compound is given in the diagram(ne2418). So the correct IUPAC name will be 3-bromopentane\n\n(Reference  :NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 291; Paragraph No. 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','1-bromo-1, 1-diethylmethane','3-bromopentane','1-bromo-1-ethylpropane','none of the above','NEW','12-291','12-161',NULL),
(241310,'chemistry','The reaction of toluene with chlorine in the presence of iron and in the absence of light yields ______. diagram(n24112)','d','As  methyl group increases the electron density on ortho and para positions. \n\nSo that electrophile formed can substitute the ortho and para site forming both products but para is major due to steric hindrance. \n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','a,b','b','d','NEW','12-296','12-165','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241310q.png'),
(241311,'chemistry','Chloromethane on treatment with excess of ammonia yields mainly','Methanamine','The reaction will proceed as given:\n\nCH₃Cl+ NH₃ ⟶ CH₃NH₂ + HCl\n\nSo methanamine will be the product formed.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','N, N-dimethylmethanamine','N-methylmethanamine','Methanamine','mixture containing all these in equal proportion','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241312,'chemistry','Molecules whose mirror image is non-superimposable over them are known as chiral. Which of the following molecules is chiral in nature?','2-bromobutane','2-bromobutane is an optically active molecule.\n\nThe objects which are non- superimposable on their mirror image (like a pair of hands) are said to be chiral and this property is known as chirality. \n\nChiral molecules are optically active. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 306 first Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2-bromobutane','1-bromobutane','2-bromopropane','2-bromopropan-2-ol','NEW','12-306','12-176',NULL),
(241313,'chemistry','Reaction of Ph-CH₂Br with aqueous sodium hydroxide follows','Sɴ1','This reaction follows Sₙ1 mechanisms, so carbocations are formed as intermediate product.\n\n\n" Tertiary alkyl halides undergo Sɴ1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of tert -carbocations. \nFurther, greater the stability of carbocation, greater will be its ease of formation from alkyl halide and faster will be the rate of reaction. In case of alkyl halides,  alkyl halides undergo SN1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of carbocations.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 Paragraph 1 )"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Sɴ1','Sɴ2','Any of the above two depending upon the temperature of reaction','Saytzeff rule','NEW','12-304','12-176','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241313q.png'),
(241314,'chemistry','Which of the carbon atoms present in the molecule is asymmetric? Refer diagram(n24113)','2,3','A kind of molecule known as a chiral molecule possesses an unsuperposable mirror counterpart. \n\nThe existence of an asymmetric carbon atom is the characteristic that causes chirality in compounds most frequently. \n\nThe description of an object that cannot be superposed on its mirror counterpart is referred to as "chiral" in general.\n\nNCERT Figure 10.7(Reference:NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','1,2,3,4','2,3','1,3','1,2,3','NEW','12-307','12-179','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241314q.png'),
(241315,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds will give racemic mixture on nudeophilic substitution by OH ion?Refer diagram(n24114)','i and iii','Since option i and iii contains a chiral carbon, so they will give racemic product\n\nA mixture containing two enantiomers in equal proportions will have zero optical rotation, as the rotation due to one isomer will be cancelled by the rotation due to the other isomer. \n\nSuch a mixture is known as racemic mixture or racemic modification. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 307 Last Paragraph.)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','i','i,ii','ii,iii,i','i and iii','NEW','12-307','12-179','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241315q.png'),
(241316,'chemistry','Arrange the compounds in increasing order of rate of reaction towards nucleophilic substitution according to  the given diagram(n24115)','(i) < (iii) < (ii)','The rate of reaction towards nucleophilic substitution. is directly proportional to electron withdrawing group present at ortho and para positions.\n\nDue to partial double bond character and is difficult to break being stronger than a single bond. So option (i) has least reactivity \n\n"Nucleophiles are electron rich species. \n\nTherefore, they attack at that part of the substrate molecule which is electron deficient. "A substitution reaction takes place and halogen atom, called leaving group departs as halide ion. \n\nSince the substitution reaction is initiated by a nucleophile, it is called nucleophilic substitution reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 1)"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) < (ii) < (iii)','(iii) < (ii) < (i)','(i) < (iii) < (ii)','(iii) < (i) < (ii)','NEW','12-300','12-170','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241316q.png'),
(241317,'chemistry','Arrange the compounds in increasing order of rate of reaction towards nucleophilic substitution according to  the given diagram(n24116)','(ii)< (iii)< (i)','The rate of reaction towards nucleophilic substitution. is directly proportional to electron withdrawing group present at ortho and para positions and inversely proportional to electron donating group.\n\nSince in option (iii) methyl group is present at meta position, so its donating effect is lesser .\n\nOption (i) has highest reactivity as no electron releasing group is present.\n\n"Nucleophiles are electron rich species. \n\nTherefore, they attack at that part of the substrate molecule which is electron deficient. "A substitution reaction takes place and halogen atom, called leaving group departs as halide ion. \n\nSince the substitution reaction is initiated by a nucleophile, it is called nucleophilic substitution reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 1)"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) < (ii) < (iii)','(iii) < (ii) < (i)','(i) < (iii) < (ii)','(ii)< (iii)< (i)','NEW','12-300','12-170','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241317q.png'),
(241318,'chemistry','Arrange the compounds in increasing order of rate of reaction towards nucleophilic substitution according to the given diagram(n24117)','(i) < (ii) < (iii)','The rate of reaction towards nucleophilic substitution. is directly proportional to electron withdrawing group present at ortho and para positions.\n\nDue to maximum no. of -NO₂ (electron withdrawing group) therefore option (iii) has highest reactivity \n\n"Nucleophiles are electron rich species. \n\nTherefore, they attack at that part of the substrate molecule which is electron deficient. "A substitution reaction takes place and halogen atom, called leaving group departs as halide ion. \n\nSince the substitution reaction is initiated by a nucleophile, it is called nucleophilic substitution reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 1)"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','(i) < (ii) < (iii)','(iii) < (ii) < (i)','(i) < (iii) < (ii)','(ii)< (iii)< (i)','NEW','12-300','12-170','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241318q.png'),
(241319,'chemistry','Arrange the compounds in increasing order of rate of reaction towards nucleophilic substitution according to  the given diagram(n24118)','(iii) < (ii) < (i)','The rate of reaction towards nucleophilic substitution. inversely proportional to electron donating group.\n\nSince in option (iii) methyl group is present at both ortho and para  position, so it has the least reactivity.\n\nOption (i) has highest reactivity as no electron releasing group is present.\n\n"Nucleophiles are electron rich species. \n\nTherefore, they attack at that part of the substrate molecule which is electron deficient. "A substitution reaction takes place and halogen atom, called leaving group departs as halide ion. \n\nSince the substitution reaction is initiated by a nucleophile, it is called nucleophilic substitution reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 300 Paragraph 1)"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) < (ii) < (iii)','(iii) < (ii) < (i)','(i) < (iii) < (ii)','(ii)< (iii)< (i)','NEW','12-300','12-170','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241319q.png'),
(241320,'chemistry','Which is the correct increasing order of boiling points of the following compounds?','Butane < 1-chlorobutane < 1-bromobutane < 1-iodobutane','Branched compound have low boiling point.\n\n"As the number of carbons in an alkane increases, so do its boiling points. This is due to the fact that the intermolecular attractive forces, while individually small, become cumulatively stronger as the molecule\'s atom and electron counts rise. Branched alkanes often have lower boiling points than unbranched alkanes with the same carbon concentration. The stronger van der Waals interactions between the molecules of the unbranched alkanes lead this to happen."\n(Reference:NCERT Figure 10.1 NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Butane < 1-chlorobutane < 1-bromobutane < 1-iodobutane','1-iodobutane < 1-bromobutane < 1-chlorobutane < Butane','Butane < 1-iodobutane < 1-bromobutane < 1-chlorobutane','Butane < 1-chlorobutane < 1-iodobutane < 1-bromobutane','NEW','12-298','12-168','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/241320q.png'),
(241321,'chemistry','Which is the correct increasing order of boiling points of the following compounds?','1-bromoethane < 1-bromopropane < 1-bromobutane < Bromobenzene','Branched compound have low boiling point.\n\n"As the number of carbons in an alkane increases, so do its boiling points. This is due to the fact that the intermolecular attractive forces, while individually small, become cumulatively stronger as the molecule\'s atom and electron counts rise. Branched alkanes often have lower boiling points than unbranched alkanes with the same carbon concentration. The stronger van der Waals interactions between the molecules of the unbranched alkanes lead this to happen."\n(Reference:NCERT Figure 10.1 NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Bromobenzene < 1-bromobutane < 1-bromopropane < 1-bromoethane','Bromobenzene < 1-bromoethane < 1-bromopropane < 1-bromobutane','1-bromopropane < 1-bromobutane < 1-bromoethane < Bromobenzene','1-bromoethane < 1-bromopropane < 1-bromobutane < Bromobenzene','NEW','12-298','12-168','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/241321q.png'),
(241322,'chemistry','Which of the statements are correct about the reaction in the given diagram(n2787)?','In (iii) carbon atom is sp2 hybridised','In transition state all the three C-H bonds are in the same plane and the attacking and leaving nucleophiles are partially attached to the carbon. So during transition state Carbon atom is sp2 hybridised.\n\nIn Sɴ2 as the reaction progresses and the bond between the incoming nucleophile and the carbon atom starts forming, the bond between carbon atom and leaving group weakens. \n\nAs this happens, the three carbon-hydrogen bonds of the substrate start moving away from the attacking nucleophile. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (v) both are nucleophiles','In (iii) carbon atom is sp3 hybridised','In (iii) carbon atom is sp2 hybridised','(i) and (v) both are electrophiles','NEW','12-302','12-172','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241322q.png'),
(241323,'chemistry','Which of the statements are correct about the reaction in the given diagram(n2787)?','both a and b','The reaction follows Sɴ2 mechanism. So nucleophile attacks the carbon at 180° to the leaving group, so the reactant and product have opposite configuration\n\nIn Sɴ2 as the reaction progresses and the bond between the incoming nucleophile and the carbon atom starts forming, the bond between carbon atom and leaving group weakens.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The given reaction follows SN2 mechanism','(i i) and (iv) have opposite configuration','(ii) and (iv) have same configuration','both a and b','NEW','12-302','12-172','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241323q.png'),
(71512,'chemistry','In which of the following solvents is silver chloride most soluble?','Aqueous ammonia','Silver chloride forms a soluble complex with aqueous ammonia.\nAgCl + 2NH₃→ [Ag(NH₃)₂]Cl','Physical Chemistry >> Equilibrium >> NCERT Examplar',NULL,'1','mcq','0.1 mol /dm³ AgN0₃ solution','0.1 mol /dm HCl solution','H₂0','Aqueous ammonia','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241324,'chemistry','Which of the statements are correct about the reaction intermediate in the given diagram(n2787)?\n\na. Intermediate (iii) is unstable because in this carbon is attached to 5 atoms\n\nb. Intermediate (iii) is unstable because carbon atom is sp 2 hybridised\n\nc. Intermediate (iii) is stable because carbon atom is sp 2 hybridised\n\nd. Intermediate (iii) is less stable than the reactant (ii)','a,d','In transition state all the three C-H bonds are in the same plane and the attacking and leaving nucleophiles are partially attached to the carbon. So during transition state Carbon atom is sp2 hybridised.\n\nIn Sɴ2 as the reaction progresses and the bond between the incoming nucleophile and the carbon atom starts forming, the bond between carbon atom and leaving group weakens. \n\nAs this happens, the three carbon-hydrogen bonds of the substrate start moving away from the attacking nucleophile. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 302 Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,d','a,b,c','NEW','12-302','12-172','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241324q.png'),
(241325,'chemistry','Which of the statements are correct about the mechanism of reaction in the given diagram(n2788)?\n\na. "A carbocation will be formed as an intermediate in the reaction\n\nb. OH- will attach the substrate (ii) from one side and Cl- will leave it simultaneously from other side\n\nc. An unstable intermediate will be formed in which OH- and Cl- will be attached by weak bonds\n\nd. Reaction proceeds through SN1 mechanism"','a,d','"Reaction proceeds through SN1 mechanism"" and a carbocation will be formed as an intermediate in the reaction.\n\nTertiary alkyl halides undergo Sɴ1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of tert -carbocations. Further, greater the stability of carbocation, greater will be its ease of formation from alkyl halide and faster will be the rate of reaction. \n\nIn case of alkyl halides, alkyl halides undergo SN1 reaction very fast because of the high stability of carbocations. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 304 Paragraph 1 )"','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,d','NEW','12-304','12-174','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241325q.png'),
(241326,'chemistry','Which of the statements is correct about the mechanism of reaction in the given diagram(n2788)?','The rate of reaction depends on the concentration of only (ii)','Reaction proceeds through SN1 mechanism"" and a carbocation will be formed as an intermediate in the reaction.\n\nSo, the molecularity of the reaction will be 1 and will depend only on the conc. of (ii)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','The rate of reaction depends on the concentration of only (ii)','The rate of reaction depends on concentration of both (i) and (ii)','Molecularity of reaction is two','All of the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241326q.png'),
(241327,'chemistry','Ethylene chloride and ethylidene chloride are isomers. Identify the correct statements.\n\na.  Both the compounds form same product on treatment with alcoholic KOH\n\nb.  Both the compounds form same product on treatment with aq. NaOH\n\nc. Both the compounds form same product on reduction\n\nd.  Both the compounds are optically active','a,c','The different sets of reaction is given in the diagram(ne2419). Since both the compounds are optically inactive so the correct answer is option a and c.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241327q.png'),
(241328,'chemistry','Which of them are gem dihalides?\n\na. Ethylidene chloride\n\nb. Ethylene chloride\n\nc. Methylene chloride\n\nd. Benzyl chloride','a,c','The dihalo-compounds having both the halogen atoms are further classified as geminal halides or gem-dihalides when both the halogen atoms are present on the same carbon atom of the chain and vicinal halides or vic-dihalides when halogen atoms are present on adjacent carbon atoms.\n\nEthylidene chloride: Cl₂CHCH₃\nEthylene chloride: Cl-CH₂CH₂-Cl\nMethylene chloride: CH₂Cl₂\n\nIn common name system, gem-dihalides are named as alkylidene halides and vic-dihalides are named as alkylene dihalides. \n\nIn IUPAC system, they are named as dihaloalkanes.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 291 Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-291','12-161','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241328q.png'),
(241329,'chemistry','Which of the following are secondary bromides?\n\na. (CH₃)₂CHBr\n\nb. (CH₃)₃CCHBr\n\nc. (CH₃)CH(Br)CH₂CH₃\n\nd. (CH₃)₂C(Br)CH₂CH₃','a,c','Secondary bromides are those compounds in which the  carbon bonded to bromine is further bonded to two alkyl groups.\n\nSo the correct answer is option a and c','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/match/241329q.png'),
(241330,'chemistry','Alkyl halides are prepared from alcohols by treating with\n\na. HCl and ZnCl₂\n\nb. Red P and Br₂\n\nc. H₂SO₄ and KI','a,b','The reactions of primary and secondary alcohols with HCl require the presence of a catalyst, ZnCl₂.\n\nAlcohols when treated with red P and Br₂ yields alkyl bromides.\n\n"Good yields of R—I may be obtained by heating alcohols with sodium or potassium iodide in 95% orthophosphoric acid.  \n\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','a,b,c','NEW','12-294','12-164',NULL),
(241331,'chemistry','Alkyl fluorides are synthesised by alkyl chloride/bromide in presence of:\n\na. CaF₂\n\nb. CoF₂\n\nc. Hg₂F₂\n\nd. NaF','b,c','The synthesis of alkyl fluorides is best accomplished by heating an alkyl chloride/bromide in the presence of a metallic fluoride such as AgF, Hg₂F₂ , CoF₂  or SbF₃\n   \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-296','12-166',NULL),
(241332,'chemistry','Aryl chlorides and bromides can be easily prepared by electrophilic substitution of arenes with chlorine and bromine respectively in the presence of Lewis acid catalysts. \nBut why does preparation of aryl iodides requires presence of an oxidising agent?','To oxidise the HI formed during iodination','Reactions with iodine are reversible in nature and require the presence of an oxidising agent (HNO₃,HIO₄ to oxidise the HI formed during iodination. \n\nFluoro compounds are not prepared by this method due to high reactivity of fluorine. \n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 3)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Because iodination reactions require the presence of oxygen','To oxidise the HI formed during iodination','To oxidise the I₂ formed during iodination','Because iodination reactions are irreversible in nature','NEW','12-296','12-166',NULL),
(241333,'chemistry','Why p-dibromobenzene has higher melting point than its o-dibromobenzene isomer?','Due to symmetry','The para-isomers are high melting as compared to their ortho and meta-isomers. \n\nIt is due to symmetry of para-isomers that fits in crystal lattice better as compared to ortho- and meta-isomers.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 Last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Due to resonance','Due to H- bonding','Due to symmetry','Both have same melting point','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(241334,'chemistry','Antiseptic property of iodoform is due to ?','Iodine','Antiseptic property of iodine is due to I₂ and not due to iodoform.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317, Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Iodoform','Iodine','Nascent Iodine','Both Iodine and Nascent Iodine','NEW','12-317','12-187',NULL),
(241335,'chemistry','Compound ‘A’ with molecular formula C4H9 Br is treated with aq. KOH solution. \nThe rate of this reaction depends upon the concentration of the compound ‘A’ only. \n\nWhen another optically active isomer ‘B’ of this compound was treated with aq. KOH solution, the rate of reaction was found to be dependent on concentration of compound and KOH both.\n\nCompound A and B is :','2-bromo-2-methylpropane and 2-bromobutane','Compound A undergoes Sn1 mechanism, so it will be a tertiary halide ie. 2-bromo-2-methylpropane \n\nCompound B undergoes Sn2 mechanism since it depends on the concentration of both the reactants. Its strucutural name will be 2-bromobutane','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','2-bromo-2-methylpropane and 2-bromobutane','2-bromobutane and 2-bromo-2-methylpropane','Bromobutane only','2-bromo-2-methylpropane and 2-bromo-2-methylpropane','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241401,'chemistry','The product formed in the given reaction diagram(n24128) is:','NaX','Alkyl iodides are often prepared by the reaction of alkyl chlorides/ bromides with NaI in dry acetone. \n\nThis reaction is known as Finkelstein reaction.\n\nNaCl or NaBr thus formed is precipitated in dry acetone. It facilitates the forward reaction according to Le Chatelier’s Principle.\n\n(Reference:  Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 295)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','NaX','Sodium acetate','Propanone','Alkyl chlorides','NEW','12-295','12-165','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241401q.png'),
(241402,'chemistry','The alkyl halides not used in Finkelstein reaction are?\n\na. Alkyl flourides\n\nb. Alkyl chlorides\n\nc. Alkyl bromides\n\nd. Silver flourides','a,d','Finkelstein reaction:\nAlkyl iodides are often prepared by the reaction of alkyl chlorides/ bromides with NaI in dry acetone. \n\nNaCl or NaBr thus formed is precipitated in dry acetone. It facilitates the forward reaction according to Le Chatelier’s Principle.\n\n(Reference:  Class 12 Chemistry NCERT Page 295)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','a,d','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(241403,'chemistry','Which of them is  correct for Swarts reaction?','Alkyl flourides is prepared by heating alkyl chloride in the presence of metallic flourides','Swarts reaction:\nThe synthesis of alkyl fluorides is best accomplished by heating an alkyl chloride/bromide in the presence of a metallic fluoride such as AgF, Hg₂F₂ , CoF₂  or SbF₃\ndiagram(ne2439)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Alkyl flourides is prepared by heating alkyl chloride in the presence of metallic flourides','Alkyl flourides is prepared by cooling alkyl chloride in the presence of metallic flourides','Alkyl flourides is prepared by heating alkyl chloride in the presence of non-metallic flourides','Alkyl flourides is prepared by cooling alkyl chloride in the presence of metallic flourides and iodides','NEW','12-295','12-165',NULL),
(241404,'chemistry','Identify the reaction in the given diagram(n24129) which occurs in the presence of AgF?','Swarts reaction','Swarts reaction:\nThe synthesis of alkyl fluorides is best accomplished by heating an alkyl chloride/bromide in the presence of a metallic fluoride such as AgF, Hg₂F₂ , CoF₂  or SbF₃\ndiagram(ne2439)\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Finkelstein reaction','Sandmeyer reaction','Mendius reaction','Swarts reaction','NEW','12-295','12-165','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241404q.png'),
(241405,'chemistry','Identify the reaction in the given diagram(n24130)?','Sandmeyer reaction','When a primary aromatic amine, dissolved or suspended in cold aqueous mineral acid, is treated with sodium nitrite, a diazonium salt is formed. \n\nThis is called Sandmeyer Reaction.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Finkelstein reaction','Sandmeyer reaction','Mendius reaction','Swarts reaction','NEW','12-296','12-166','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241405q.png'),
(241406,'chemistry','A diazonium salt is produced as intermediate in','Sandmeyer reaction','When a primary aromatic amine, dissolved or suspended in cold aqueous mineral acid, is treated with sodium nitrite, a diazonium salt is formed. \n\nThis is called Sandmeyer Reaction.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Finkelstein reaction','Sandmeyer reaction','Mendius reaction','Swarts reaction','NEW','12-296','12-166',NULL),
(241407,'chemistry','Identify the reaction in the given diagram(n24131)?','Sn2 reaction','Sɴ2 reaction consists of : \n\na) the incoming nucleophile interacts with alkyl halide causing the carbon-halide bond to break and a new bond is formed between carbon and attacking nucleophile. \n\nb) These two processes take place simultaneously in a single step and no intermediate is formed. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 301; Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Finkelstein reaction','Sandmeyer reaction','Sn2 reaction','Swarts reaction','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241407q.png'),
(241408,'chemistry','Identify the reaction in the given diagram(n24132) which occurs in the presence of Na and Ether?','Wurtz reaction','Alkyl halides react with sodium in dry ether to give hydrocarbons containing double the number of carbon atoms present in the halide. \n\nThis reaction is known as Wurtz reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 311 fourth Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fittig reaction','Sandmeyer reaction','Wurtz reaction','Swarts reaction','NEW','12-309','12-181','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241408q.png'),
(241409,'chemistry','The reaction in the given diagram(n24133) will undergo?','Friedel Craft reaction','Haloarenes undergo the usual electrophilic reactions of the benzene ring such as halogenation, nitration, sulphonation and Friedel-Crafts reactions. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 first Paragraph )\ndiagram(ne2440)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Friedel Crafts reaction','Wurtz Fittig reaction','Wurtz reaction','Friedel Craft reaction','NEW','12-312','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241409q.png'),
(241410,'chemistry','The major product formed in reaction in the given diagram(n24133) will be?','1-Chloro-4-methylbenzene','Haloarenes undergo the usual electrophilic reactions of the benzene ring such as halogenation, nitration, sulphonation and Friedel-Crafts reactions. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 first Paragraph )\ndiagram(ne2440)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','1-Chloro-2-methylbenzene','1-Chloro-4-methylbenzene','2-Chloroacetophenone','Biphenyl','NEW','12-312','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241410q.png'),
(241411,'chemistry','The major product formed in reaction in the given diagram(n24134) will be?','2-Chloroacetophenone','Haloarenes undergo the usual electrophilic reactions of the benzene ring such as halogenation, nitration, sulphonation and Friedel-Crafts reactions. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 314 first Paragraph )\ndiagram(ne2441)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','1-Chloro-2-methylbenzene','1-Chloro-4-methylbenzene','2-Chloroacetophenone','Biphenyl','NEW','12-312','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241411q.png'),
(241412,'chemistry','The reaction in the given diagram(n24135) will undergo?','Wurtz Fittig reaction','"A mixture of an alkyl halide and aryl halide gives an alkyl arene when treated with sodium in dry ether and is called Wurtz-Fittig reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 316 first Paragraph )"\ndiagram(ne2442)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Friedel Crafts reaction','Wurtz Fittig reaction','Wurtz reaction','Fittig reaction','NEW','12-313','12-185','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241412q.png'),
(241413,'chemistry','The reaction in the given diagram(n24105) is?','Fittig reaction','Aryl halides also give analogous compounds when treated with sodium in dry ether, in which two aryl groups are joined together. \n\nIt is called Fittig reaction. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 316 second Paragraph )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> Named Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Friedel Crafts reaction','Wurtz Fittig reaction','Wurtz reaction','Fittig reaction','NEW','12-313','12-185','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241413q.png'),
(241414,'biology','A drupe develops in','Mango','Some fleshy fruits such as mango, plum etc. usually have a single hard stone that encloses a seed, called drupe','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Mango','Wheat','Pea','Tomato','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241415,'biology','When pollen grains are not transferred from anthers to stigma in a flower, due to the barrier, it is called','Herkogamy','Herkogamy – when there is some physical barrier present between the stamens and carpels avoiding any chance of self pollination. Cleistogamy – when the flowers remain closed, selfpollination is the rule. Dichogamy – when the two sexes mature at different times','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Herkogamy','Heterogamy','Cleistogamy','Dichogamydichogamy','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241416,'biology','The parenchyma tissue which forms the bulk of ovule where the sporogenous tissue is produced is','Nucellus','The parenchyma tissue which forms the bulk of ovule where the sporogenous tissue is produced is Nucellus','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Megaspore mother cell','Nucellus','Ovule','Embryo sac','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241417,'biology','Unisexuality of flowers prevents','Autogamy, but not geitonogamy','Unisexuality of flowers prevents autogamy, but not geitonogamy. In self fertilisation , the male and female gametes are derived from the same individual. Among plants, self fertilization also called autogamy is common in many cultivated species, eg., wheat and oats. However, self fertilization is a form of inbreeding and does not allow for the mixing of genetic material; if it occurs over a number of generations it will result in offspring being less vigorous and productive than those resulting from cross fertilization.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Autogamy, but not geitonogamy','Both geitonogamy and xenogamy','Geitonogamy, but not xenogamy','Autogamy and geitonogamy','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241418,'biology','Which one of the following represents an ovule, where the embryo sac becomes horse-shoe shaped and the funiculus and micropyle are close to each other?','Amphitropous','Amphitropous : Both body of ovule and embryo sac are curved. The embryo sac assumes horse-shoe shape. e.g. Papaveraceae.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Amphitropous','Circinotropous','Atropous','Anotropous','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241419,'biology','If an angiospermic male plant is diploid and female plant tetraploid, the ploidy level of endosperm will be','Pentaploid','The male gamete will be haploid (n). 2 polar nuclei will be diploid (2n). Endosperm formed by fusion of male gamete with two polar nuclei will be pentaploid.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Haploid','Triploid','Tetraploid','Pentaploid','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241420,'biology','These plants flower and fruit only once in their life time and die after fruiting. These are','Monocarpic plants','Monocarpic plants are the plants in which flowers and fruits are formed only once after vegetative growth of several years e.g., some bamboos, Agave, etc.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Monocarpic plants','Polycarpic plants','Vegetative plants','Reproductive plants','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241421,'biology','Select the mismatched pair.','Pollen grain — Male gamete','In angiosperms, microsporangium is equivalent to pollen sac, megasporangium is equivalent to nucellus and embryo sac is equivalent to female gametophyte. Pollen grain or microspore represents immature male gametophyte.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Microsporangium — Pollen sac','Megasporangium — Nucellus','Pollen grain — Male gamete','Embryo sac — Female gametophyte','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241422,'biology','A typical angiospermous ovule is attached to the placenta by means of a stalk called X. Body of the ovule fuses with X in the region called Y. Identify X and Y.','Funicle Hilum','Ovule is an integumented megasporagium found in spermatophytes which develops into seed after fertilization. An angiospermic ovule is typically an ovoid and whitish structure. It occurs inside ovary where it is attached to a parenchymatous cushion called placenta either singly or in a cluster. The ovule is stalked. The stalk is called funiculus or funicle. The point of attachment of the body of the ovule with the funiculus is known as hilum.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Funicle Hilum','Hilum Funicle','Funicle Micropyle','Hilum Chalaza','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241423,'biology','Which of the following options is correct?','All of these','Autogamy (Gk. autos-self, gamos-marriage) is a type of self pollination in which an intersexual or perfect flower is pollinated by its own pollen. Geitonogamy (Gk. geiton-neighbour, gamos-marriage) is a type of pollination in which pollen grains of one flower are transferred to the stigma of another flower belonging to either the same plant or genetically similar plant. In geitonogamy, the flowers often show modifications similar to ones found in xenogamy or cross pollunation. Xenogamy (Gk. xenos-strange, gamos-marriage) or cross pollination is the transfer of pollen grains from anther of one flower to the stigma of a genetically different flower.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of the same flower – Autogamy','Transfer of pollen grains from the anther of one flower to the stigma of another flower of same plant – Geitonogamy.','Transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of a genetically different plant – Xenogamy','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241424,'biology','Which of the following is not a water pollinated plant?','Cannabis','Cannabis is wind pollinated plant.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Zostera','Vallisneria','Hydrilla','Cannabis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241425,'biology','Growth of pollen tube towards embryo sac is','Chemotropic','The pollen tube eats its way through the solid part of the stigma and style by secreting pectinases and hydrolytic enzymes. Pollen tube travels intercellularly and chemotropically along the concentration gradient of calcium – boron -inositol sugar complex.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Chemotropic','Thigmotaxis','Geotropic','Ionotropic','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241426,'biology','The part of gynoecium that determines the complatible nature of pollen is','Stigma','Stigma is the terminal receptive part of the pistil which functions as landing platform for the pollen grains. It also determines the compatibility of the pollen grains','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Stigma','Style','Ovary','Synergids','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241427,'biology','The innermost layer of anther is tapetum whose function is','Nutrition','The tapetal layer is of great physiological significance as all the food material entering into the sporogenous tissue diffuses through this layer. Ultimately, the cells of tapetal layer disorganise. Thus, tapetum makes a nutritive layer for the developing microspores.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Dehiscence','Mechanical','Nutrition','Protection','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241428,'biology','The female gametophyte of a typical dicot at the time of fertilization is','7-celled','The typical and the most common type of embryo sac, found in 80% flowering plants is called Polygonum type of embryosac. It contains 8 nuclei but 7 cells – 3 micropylar, 3 chalazal and one central. It is formed by one meiosis and three mitosis.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','8-celled','7-celled','6-celled','5-celled','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241429,'biology','One of the most resistant biological material present in the exine of pollen grain is','Sporopollenin','Outer layer (exine) of pollen grain is made of a highly resistant substance called sporopollenin (Zelisch, 1932). Sporopollenin is not degraded by any enzyme. It is not affected by high temperature, strong acid or strong alkali. Because of sporopollenin, pollen grains are well preserved as fossils.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Pectocellulose','Sporopollenin','Suberin','Cellulose','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241430,'biology','What is the function of germ pore?','Initiation of pollen tube','One or more thin areas present in the exine of pollen grain are known as germ pores. The germ pores are apertures in the exine layer of the pollen grain where the sporopollenin is absent. The germ pore helps in the formation of the pollen tube and the release of the male gametes during fertilization. There are usually three germ pores in dicots (tricolpate) and one in monocots (monocolpate).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Emergence of radicle','Absorption of water for seed germination','Initiation of pollen tube','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241431,'biology','How many meiotic divisions are required for the formation of 100 functional megaspores?','100','Megaspore mother cell (MMC) is the sporogenous cell that divides to give 4 haploid megaspores. Out of these 4 megaspores, only one is functional and other three degenerate. Hence 100 meiotic divisions are required for the formation of 100 functional megaspores.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','100','50','25','26','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241432,'biology','Study of pollen grains is called','Palynology','Study of pollen grains is called Palynology','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Micrology','Anthology','Palynology','Pomology','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241433,'biology','Which of these is a condition that makes flowers invariably autogamous?','Cleistogamy','Autogamy is possible only when anther and stigma are close together and there is synchrony in pollen release and stigma receptivity. As in case of cleistogamous flowers, the flowers remain closed so that anthers and stigmas are never exposed. The flowers undergo only self pollination. No external pollinating agency is required.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Dioecy','Self incompatibilty','Cleistogamy','Xenogamy','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241434,'biology','An embryo may sometimes develop from any cell of embryo sac other than egg. It is termed as','Apogamy','Apogamy is the reproduction without the fusion of gametes, and usually without meiosis. The term may include any form of vegetative reproduction. The production of a diploid gametophyte from the sporophyte due to the absence of meiosis is known as apospory. Parthenogenesis is the development of an unfertilized egg into a complete individual without fertilization. Production and development of seedless fruits is called parthenocarpy.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Apospory','Apogamy','Parthenogenesis','Parthenocarpy','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241435,'biology','Endosperm is completely consumed by the developing embryo in','Pea and groundnut','Endosperm represents the food storing tissue of a seed. It is produced as a result of double fertilization in angiosperms. In most monocots and some dicot seeds, the food reserve remains in the endosperm. They are called endospermic or albuminous seeds, e.g., cereals, castor, coconut, rubber. However, in the majority of dicot seeds (e.g., pea, gram, bean, mustard, groundnut) and some monocot seeds (e.g., orchids, Sagittaria), the endosperm is consumed during seed development and the food is stored in cotyledons and other regions. They are called nonendospermic or exalbuminous seeds','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Pea and groundnut','Maize and castor','Castor and groundnut','Maize and pea','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241436,'biology','The portion of embryonal axis between plumule (future shoot) and cotyledons is called','Epicotyl','Part of embryo axis between the plumule and cotyledonary node is epicotyl (above the level of cotyledons) while the part between radicle and cotyledonary node is called hypocotyl (below the level of cotyledons).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Hypocotyl','Epicotyl','Coleohize','Coleoptile','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241437,'biology','Stigma of a flower is removed before pollination. The flower will','Not form fruit','Pollination will not occur','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Form fruit normally','Not form fruit','Form deformed fruit','Form fruit smaller than normal size','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241438,'biology','In a pea flower, all petals are removed before pollination. The flower will','Not form fruit','There will not be insect pollination','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Form fruit normally','Not form fruit','Form smaller pod','Form deformed pod','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241439,'biology','A drop of glue is placed on the stigma of a flower before pollination. The flower will','Not form fruit','Germination of pollens will not occur in ovule','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Not form fruit','Form normal fruit','Form sticky fruit','Form fruit filled with glue','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241440,'biology','An apomictic seed contains an embryo that is','Genetically identical to its parent.','Apomixis is a form of asexual reproduction in plants where seeds are produced without fertilization.There is no genetic contribution from a pollen donor or a male parent. As a result, the offspring produced through apomixis are clones of the maternal plant, making them genetically identical to their parent.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Produced when two sperm fertilize one egg.','Developed from one egg alone.','The result of parental self-fertilization','Genetically identical to its parent.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241441,'biology','The embryo is carefully taken out of pea seed and sown in the soil and watered normally. New plant will','Not be formed','Embryo can not grow without food stored in cotyledons','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Be healthier','Be weaker','Not be formed','Be formed normally','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241442,'biology','Angiospermic plant has chromosome number of 24. The number of chromosomes in pollens will be','12','Pollens are formed by meiosis.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','6','12','24','48','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241443,'biology','What is the fate of the seven cells of the embryo sac','Two become fertilized; the others disintegrate.','One male gamete fuses with egg, another male gamete fuses with polar nuclei. Other than this synergids, antipodals all degenerate.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','All but one disintegrate upon fertilization.','Two become fertilized; the others disintegrate.','Two become fertilized; the others fuse to form endosperm.','All are involved in nuclear fusion events.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241444,'biology','A close relation between flower and pollinating agent is best exhibited by','Yucca','Obligate symbiotic relationship is present between Yucca flowers and moth, Tageticula','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Other Important Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cocos','Salvia','Yucca','Avena','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241501,'chemistry','Identify the compound A from the given reaction diagram(n24136)','H₂0','The hydroxyl group of an alcohol is replaced by halogen on reaction with concentrated halogen acids, phosphorus halides or thionyl chloride  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)\ndiagram(ne2443)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','HCl','H₂0','Zn(OH)₂','R-OCl','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241501q.png'),
(241502,'chemistry','Identify the compound A from the given reaction diagram(n24137)','NaHSO₄','The hydroxyl group of an alcohol is replaced by halogen on reaction with concentrated halogen acids, phosphorus halides or thionyl chloride  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)\ndiagram(ne2443)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','NaHSO₄','HCl','H₃PO₃','SO₂','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241502q.png'),
(241503,'chemistry','Identify the compound A from the given reaction diagram(n24139)','H₃PO₃','The hydroxyl group of an alcohol is replaced by halogen on reaction with concentrated halogen acids, phosphorus halides or thionyl chloride  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)\ndiagram(ne2443)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','NaHSO₄','HCl','H₃PO₃','SO₂','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241503q.png'),
(241504,'chemistry','Identify the compound A  from the given reaction diagram(n24139)','H3PO3','The hydroxyl group of an alcohol is replaced by halogen on reaction with concentrated halogen acids, phosphorus halides or thionyl chloride  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 294; Paragraph No.1)\ndiagram(ne2443)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','POCl3','HCl','H2O','H3PO3','NEW','12-294','12-164','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241504q.png');
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(241506,'chemistry','Identify the compound A from the given reaction diagram(n24147)','vic-Dibromide','In the laboratory, addition of bromine in CCl4  to an alkene resulting in discharge of reddish brown colour of bromine constitutes an important method for the detection of double bond in a molecule\n\nThe addition results in the synthesis of vic-dibromides, which are colourless. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 295; Paragraph No.4)\ndiagram(ne2444)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Bromoethane','vic-Bromide','vic-Dibromide','gem-Dibromide','NEW','12-295','12-165','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241506q.png'),
(241507,'chemistry','Identify the other compound formed from the given reaction diagram(n24146)','N₂','When a primary aromatic amine, dissolved or suspended in cold aqueous mineral acid, is treated with sodium nitrite, a diazonium salt is formed. \n\nThis is called Sandmeyer Reaction.  \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 296; Paragraph 4)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','I₂','N₂','Benzene','Benzene diazonium chloride','NEW','12-296','12-166','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241507q.png'),
(241508,'chemistry','The compound formed from the given reaction diagram(n24145) is','Phenol','Chlorobenzene can be converted into phenol by heating in aqueous sodium hydroxide solution at a temperature of 623K and a pressure of 300 atmospheres.\n\nThe presence of electron withdrawing groups at ortho and para position increases the reactivity of haloarenes.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 312 fifth Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Phenol','Chlorobenzene','p-hydroxychlorobenzene','No reaction will occur','NEW','12-312','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241508q.png'),
(241509,'chemistry','The compound formed from the given reaction diagram(n24144) is','b','Chlorobenzene can be converted into phenol by heating in aqueous sodium hydroxide solution at a temperature of 623K and a pressure of 300 atmospheres.\n\nThe presence of electron withdrawing groups at ortho and para position increases the reactivity of haloarenes.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 312 fifth Paragraph)\ndiagram(ne2445)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-312','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241509q.png'),
(241510,'chemistry','The compound formed from the given reaction diagram(n24142) is','d','Chlorobenzene can be converted into phenol by heating in aqueous sodium hydroxide solution at a temperature of 623K and a pressure of 300 atmospheres.\n\nThe presence of electron withdrawing groups at ortho and para position increases the reactivity of haloarenes.\n \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 312 fifth Paragraph)\ndiagram(ne2446)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-312','12-184','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241510q.png'),
(241511,'chemistry','The major product formed from the given reaction diagram(n24142) is','picric acid','refer to diagram(241511E)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'2','mcq','phenol','2,4 dinitrophenol','p-nitrophenol','picric acid','NEW','-',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241511q.png'),
(241512,'chemistry','The major product formed from the given reaction diagram(n24143) is','Phosgene','The product of the reaction is phosgene. \n\nChloroform is slowly oxidised by air in the presence of light to an extremely poisonous gas, carbonyl chloride, also known as phosgene.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Reaction  NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 317 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> NCERT Reactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Carboxyl chloride','Phosgene','Iodoform','DDT','NEW','12-317','12-189','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/241512q.png'),
(241513,'biology','In majority of angiosperms [NEET-II 2016]','Reduction division occurs in the megaspore mother cells','In majority of angiosperms Reduction division occurs in the megaspore mother cells','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Egg has a filiform apparatus','There are numerous antipodal cells','Reduction division occurs in the megaspore mother cells','A small central cell is present in that embryo sac','NEW','12-026',NULL,NULL),
(241514,'biology','Pollination in water hyacinth and water lily is brought about by the agency of [NEET-II 2016]','Insects or wind','In many aquatic plants with emergent flowers, pollination occurs by wind or insects, e.g., lotus, water lily, water hyacinth','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Water','Insects or wind','Birds','Bats','NEW','12-029',NULL,NULL),
(241515,'biology','The ovule of an angiosperm is technically equivalent to [NEET-II 2016]','Megasporangium','The ovule of an angiosperm is equivalent to integumented megasporangium','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Megasporangium','Megasporophyll','Megaspore mother cell','Megaspore','NEW','12-025',NULL,NULL),
(241516,'biology','Which one of the following statements is not true? [NEET-I 2016]','Tapetum helps in the dehiscence of anther','Tapetum is the innermost wall layer pf microsporangium that nourishes developing pollen grains.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Pollen grains of many species cause severe allergies','Stored pollen in liquid nitrogen can be used in the crop breeding programmes','Tapetum helps in the dehiscence of anther','Exine of pollen grains is made up of\n sporopollenin','NEW','12-023',NULL,NULL),
(241517,'biology','Which of the following statements is not correct? [NEET-I 2016]','Pollen grains of many species can germinate on the stigma of a flower, but only one pollen tube of the same species grows into the style','Pollen-pistil interaction is the group of events that occur from the time of pollen deposition over the stigma to the time of pollen tube entry into ovule. It is a safety measure to ensure that illegitimate crossing does not occur. Pollen grains of number of plants may settle over a stigma. The pollens belonging to same species would germinate while other fail to do so, but the pollen tube of the compatible pollen will grow through the style to reach the ovule whereas growth of incompatible pollens will be arrested at stigmatic disc or sometimes in the beginning part of style.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Pollen germination and pollen tube growth are regulated by chemical components of pollen interacting with those of the pistil','Some reptiles have also been reported as pollinators in some plant species','Pollen grains of many species can germinate on the stigma of a flower, but only one pollen tube of the same species grows into the style','Insects that consume pollen or nectar without bringing about pollination are called pollen nectar robbers','NEW','12-031',NULL,NULL),
(241518,'biology','Proximal end of the filament of stamen is attached to the[NEET-I 2016]','Thalamus or petal',NULL,'Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Placenta','Thalamus or petal','Anther','Connective','NEW','12-021',NULL,NULL),
(241519,'biology','Filiform apparatus is characteristic feature of [AIPMT 2015]','Synergids','Filliform apparatus is a mass of finger-like projections of the wall into the cytoplasm. It is present in synergids (help cells) of the embryo sac, in the micropylar region. It guides the pollen tube inside the ovule towards the embryo sac.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Aleurone cell','Synergids','Generative cell','Nucellar embryo','NEW','12-027',NULL,NULL),
(241520,'biology','In angiosperms, microsporogenesis and megasporo-genesis [AIPMT 2015]','Involve meiosis','In angiosperms, microsporogenesis, i.e., formation of microspores (or pollen grains) occurs by the meiotic divisions of diploid microspore mother cells (or pollen mother cells). Microsporogenesis takes place in the anther. Megasporogenesis, i.e., formation of megaspores occurs by the meiotic divisions of diploid megaspore mother cells. Megasporogenesis takes place in the ovule.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Involve meiosis','Occur in ovule','Occur in anther','Form gametes without further divisions','NEW','12-026',NULL,NULL),
(241521,'biology','Male gametophyte in angiosperms produces [AIPMT 2015]','Two sperms and a vegetative cell','The protoplast of the male gametophyte divides mitotically to produce two unequal cells — a small generative cell and a large vegetative cell. The generative cell divides later into two non-motile male gametes (or sperms). Thus, the male gametophyte in angiosperms produces two sperms and a vegetative cell. The vegetative cell, later on, grows to produce pollen tube.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Single sperm and two vegetative cells','Three sperms','Two sperms and a vegetative cell','Single sperm and a vegetative cell','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241522,'biology','Which of the following are the important floral rewards to the animal pollinators ?[AIPMT 2015 Cancelled]','Nectar and pollen grains',NULL,'Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Floral fragrance and calcium crystals','Protein pellicle and stigmatic exudates','Colour and large size of flower','Nectar and pollen grains','NEW','12-029',NULL,NULL),
(241523,'biology','Which one of the following may require pollinators, but is genetically similar to autogamy?[AIPMT 2015 Cancelled]','Geitonogamy','Geitonogamy involves transfer of the pollen from one flower of a plant to the stigma of another flower of the same plant, e.g., in maize. As the pollen has to move from one flower to another flower, it requires a pollinating agent. Yet it is genetically similar to autogamy, as both the flowers of the plant, share the same genotype of the plant.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Apogamy','Cleistogamy','Geitonogamy','Xenogamy','NEW','12-028',NULL,NULL),
(121406,'chemistry','The fragrance of flowers is due to the presence of some steam volatile organic compounds called essential oils. These are generally insoluble in water at room temperature but are miscible with water vapour in vapour phase. A suitable method for the extraction of these oils from the flowers is','Steam distillation','Steam distillation technique is applied to separate substances which are steam volatile and are immiscible with water. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 359; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Distillation','Crystallisation','Distillation under reduced pressure','Steam distillation','NEW','11-359','11-281',NULL),
(241524,'biology','Which one of the following statements is not true?[AIPMT 2015 Cancelled]','Honey is made by bees by digesting pollen collected from flowers','Honey is made from nectar through a process of regurgitation and evaporation. Honeybees transform saccharides (carbohydrates) into honey by regurgitating it a number of times, until it is partially digested. The bees do the regurgitation and digestion as a group. After the last regurgitation, the aqueous solution is still high in water, the process continues by evaporation of much of the water and enzymatic transformation. Honey is produced by bees as a food source.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The flowers pollinated by flies and bats secretefoul odour to attract them','Honey is made by bees by digesting pollen collected from flowers','Pollen grains are rich in nutrients and they are used in the form of tablets and syrups','Pollen grains of some plants cause severe allergies and bronchial afflictions in some people','NEW','12-030',NULL,NULL),
(241525,'biology','The hilum is a scar on the [AIPMT 2015 Cancelled]','Seed, where funicle was attached','Ovule is an integumented megasporangium found in spermatophytes which develops into seed after fertilisation. An angiospermic ovule is typically an ovoid and whitish structure. It occurs inside ovary where it is attached to a parenchymatous cushion called placenta either singly or in a cluster. The ovule is stalked. The stalk is called funiculus or funicle. The point of attachment of the body of the ovule with the funiculus is known as hilum. It is present as a scar on a mature seed.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Fruit, where style was present','Seed, where micropyle was present','Seed, where funicle was attached','Fruit, where it was attached to pedicel','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
