(241617,'biology','Persistent nucellus in the seed is known as [NEET 2019]','Perisperm','In some seeds, remains of nucellus persist. This residual nucellus which persists in the seed is called perisperm, e.g., black pepper, coffee, castor, cardamum, Nymphaea.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Tegmen','Chalaza','Perisperm','Hilum','NEW','12-036',NULL,NULL),
(241618,'biology','Which one of the following statements regarding post-fertilisation development in flowering plants is incorrect? [NEET 2019]','Ovules develop into embryo sac','Ovules develop into seeds.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Ovules develop into embryo sac','Ovary develops into fruit','Zygote develops into embryo','Central cell develops into endosperm','NEW','12-035',NULL,NULL),
(241619,'biology','The coconut water from tender coconut represents [NEET-1 2016]','Free nuclear endosperm','Coconut has multicellular endosperm (called coconut meal) in the outer part and free nuclear as well as vacuolate endosperm (called coconut milk or coconut water) in the centre.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Free nuclear proembryo','Free nuclear endosperm','Endocarp','Fleshy mesocarp','NEW','12-035',NULL,NULL),
(241620,'biology','Coconut water from a tender coconut is [2015]','Free nuclear endosperm','Coconut has multicellular endosperm (called coconut meal) in the outer part and free nuclear as well as vacuolate endosperm (called coconut milk or coconut water) in the centre.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Innermost layers of the seed coat','Degenerated nucellus','Immature embryo','Free nuclear endosperm','NEW','12-035',NULL,NULL),
(241621,'biology','Which one of the following fruits is parthenocarpic [AIPMT 2015]','Banana','Parthenocarpic fruits are the fruits which are formed without fertilisation. These fruits are naturally seedless, e.g., banana.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Jackfruit','Banana','Brinjal','Apple','NEW','12-037',NULL,NULL),
(241622,'biology','Non-albuminous seed is produced in [AIPMT 2014]','Pea','In majority of dicot seeds, including pea, the endosperm is consumed during seed development and the food is stored in cotyledons and other regions. They are called non-endospermic or exalbuminous seeds.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Maize','Castor','Wheat','Pea','NEW','12-036',NULL,NULL),
(241623,'biology','Seed coat is not thin, membranous in [NEET 2013]','Coconut',NULL,'Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Groundnut','Gram','Maize','Coconut','NEW','12-035',NULL,NULL),
(241624,'biology','Perisperm differs from endosperm in [NEET 2013]','Being a diploid tissue','Both perisperm and endosperm are nutritive layers. Perisperm is residual persistent nucellus of seed prior to fertilisation while endosperm develops when one of the sperm cells fuses with two haploid polar nuclei. Thus, perisperm is diploid while endosperm is a triploid tissue.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Being a diploid tissue','Its formation by fusion of secondary nucleus with several sperms','Being a haploid tissue','Having no reserve food.','NEW','12-036',NULL,NULL),
(241625,'biology','Albuminous seeds store their reserve food mainly in [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Endosperm','In some seeds, the endosperm persists in the seed as food storage tissue. Such seeds are called endospermic or albuminous, e.g., castor, maize, wheat, barley, rubber, coconut.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Endosperm','Cotyledons','Hypocotyl','Perisperm','NEW','12-036',NULL,NULL),
(241626,'biology','The viability of seeds is tested by [Karnataka NEET 2013]','2, 3, 5 triphenyl tetrazolium chloride','Viability of seeds can be known by two methods: (i) ability to germinate, (ii) testing their ability to respire. All viable seeds respire. This can be tested by immersing a section of seed containing the embryo in 0.1% solution of 2,3,5-triphenyl tetrazolium chloride. The viable embryo will turn pink due to conversion of colourless triphenyl tetrazolium chloride into insoluble coloured dye called triphenyl formazone due to reduction.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','2, 6 dichlorophenol indophenol','2, 3, 5 triphenyl tetrazolium chloride','DMSO','Safranine','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241627,'biology','Seed formation without fertilisation in flowering plants involves the process of [NEET-1 2016]','Apomixis','Apomixis is a reproductive process which does not involve gametic fusion. In apomictic flowering plants there is no fertilisation and embryos develop simply by division of a cell of ovule.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Somatic hybridisation','Apomixis','Sporulation','Budding','NEW','12-038',NULL,NULL),
(241628,'biology','Nucellar polyembryony is reported in species of','Citrus','In nucellar polyembryony, some of the nucellar cells surrounding the embryo sac start dividing. Then it protrudes into the embryo sac and develop into the embryos. In such species, each ovule contains many embryos. Occurrence of more than one embryo in a seed is referred as polyembryony. Nucellar polyembryony is found in many of the Citrus and mango varieties.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Citrus','Gossypium','Triticum','Brassica','NEW','12-039',NULL,NULL),
(241629,'biology','The plant parts which consist of two generations- one within the other [NEET2020]\n(1) pollen grains inside the anther\n(2) germinated pollen grain with two male gametes\n(3) seed inside the fruit\n(4) embryo sac inside the ovule','(1) and (4).',NULL,'Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) only','(1), (2), and (3)','(3) and (4)','(1) and (4).','NEW','12-023',NULL,NULL),
(241630,'biology','In water hyacinth and water lily, pollination takes place by [NEET 2020]','insects or wind','In many aquatic plants with emergent flowers, pollination occurs by wind or insects, e.g., lotus, water lily, water hyacinth.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','insects or wind','water currents only','wind and water','insects and water','NEW','12-029',NULL,NULL),
(241631,'biology','Pollen grains can be stored for several years in liquid nitrogen having a temperature of [NEET 2018]','–196°C','Pollen grains can be stored for several years in liquid nitrogen at –196°C. This is also known as cryopreservation','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','–120°C','–80°C','–196°C','–160°C','NEW','12-024',NULL,NULL),
(241632,'biology','Which of the following has proved helpful in preserving pollen as fossils? [NEET 2018]','Sporopollenin','Exine of pollen grain is made up of highly resistant fatty substance called sporopollenin, which is not degraded by any enzyme. It is not affected by high temperature, strong acid or strong alkali. Because of the sporopollenin, pollen grains are well preserved as microfossils','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Pollenkitt','Cellulosic intine','Oil content','Sporopollenin','NEW','12-023',NULL,NULL),
(241633,'biology','Winged pollen grains are present in [NEET 2018]','Pinus','Each pollen grain of Pinus has two wing like structures which enable it to float in air, as an adaptation for dispersal by wind. Pollen grains of mustard, Cycas and mango are not winged','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','mustard','Cycas','mango','Pinus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241634,'biology','What is the fate of the male gametes discharged in the synergid? [NEET 2019]','One fuses with the egg and other fuses with central cell nuclei','During double fertilisation in angiosperms, one male gamete fuses with the egg to form the diploid zygote (syngamy or generative fertilisation). The diploid zygote finally develops into embryo. The other male gamete fuses with the two polar nuclei (or secondary nucleus) to form the triploid primary endosperm nucleus, PEN (triple fusion or vegetative fertilisation).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','One fuses with the egg and other fuses with central cell nuclei','One fuses with the egg, other(s) degenerates in the synergid','All fuse with the egg','One fuses with the egg, other(s) fuse(s) with synergid nucleus','NEW','12-034',NULL,NULL),
(241635,'biology','Double fertilisation is [NEET 2018]','syngamy and triple fusion','Double fertilisation   is   unique   and universal feature of angiosperms. Total number of nuclei involved in double fertilisation is five, i.e., 2 in syngamy and 3 in triple fusion. Syngamy is fusion of one male gamete with egg to form zygote. Triple fusion occurs when the second male gamete fuses with 2 polar nuclei or secondary nucleus to form triploid primary endosperm nucleus','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','fusion of two male gametes of a pollen tube with two different eggs','fusion of one male gamete with two polar nuclei','fusion of two male gametes with one egg','syngamy and triple fusion','NEW','12-034',NULL,NULL),
(241636,'biology','Persistent nucellus in the seed is known as [NEET 2019]','perisperm','In some seeds, remains of nucellus persist. This residual nucellus which persists in the seed is called perisperm, e.g., black pepper, coffee, castor, cardamum, Nymphaea.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','tegmen','chalaza','perisperm','hilum','NEW','12-036',NULL,NULL),
(241637,'biology','Which one of the following statements regarding post-fertilisation development in flowering plants is incorrect? [NEET 2019]','Ovules develop into embryo sac','Ovules develop into seeds','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','Ovules develop into embryo sac','Ovary develops into fruit','Zygote develops into embryo','Central cell develops into endosperm','NEW','12-035',NULL,NULL),
(241638,'biology','The term used for transfer of pollen grains from anthers of one plant to stigma of a different plant which, during pollination, brings genetically different types of pollen grains to stigma, is [NEET 2021]','Xenogamy','The term used for transfer of pollen grains from anthers of one plant to stigma of a different plant which, during pollination, brings genetically different types of pollen grains to stigma, is Xenogamy','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Cleistogamy','Xenogamy','Geitonogamy','Chasmogamy','NEW','12-028',NULL,NULL),
(241639,'biology','A typical angiosperm embryo sac at maturity is [NEET 2021]','8-nucleate and 7-celled','The embryo sac of angiosperms consists of seven cells. A typical angiosperm embryo at maturity is called 7 celled 8 nucleates. The cells are arranged in three sets. In the centre, there are two polar nuclei which are a part of the large central cell.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','8-nucleate and 8-celled','8-nucleate and 7-celled','7-nucleate and 8-celled','7-nucleate and 7-celled','NEW','12-027',NULL,NULL),
(241640,'biology','In some members of which of the following pairs of families, pollen grains retain their viability for months after release?[NEET 2021]','Rosaceae ; Leguminosae','In some members of Rosaceae ; Leguminosae families, pollen grains retain their viability for months after release','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Rosaceae ; Leguminosae','Poaceae ; Rosaceae','Poaceae ; Leguminosae','Poaceae ; Solanaceae','NEW','12-024',NULL,NULL),
(241641,'biology','Which part of the fruit, labelled in the given figure makes it a false fruit? (24103)','C → Thalamus','The given figure is of a false fruit. False fruit develops from other floral parts and thalamus alongwith the development of ovary wall','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','A → Mesocarp','B → Endocarp','C → Thalamus','D → Seed Answer','NEW','12-037',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/241641q.png'),
(241642,'biology','Identify the incorrect statement related to Pollination [NEET 2022]','Moths and butterflies are the most dominant pollinating agents among insects','Among the animals, insects, particularly bees are the dominant biotic pollinating agents.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Pollination by water is quite rare in flowering plants','Pollination by wind is more common amongst abiotic pollination','Flowers produce foul odours to attract flies and beetles to get pollinated','Moths and butterflies are the most dominant pollinating agents among insects','NEW','12-030',NULL,NULL),
(40421,'biology','Match the following scientific names with the common name.','Chaetopleura-Chiton,Echinus-Sea urchin,Dentalium-Tusk shell,Antedon-Sea lily','Examples: Pila (Apple snail), Pinctada (Pearl oyster), Sepia (Cuttlefish), Loligo (Squid), Octopus (Devil fish), Aplysia (Sea hare), Dentalium (Tusk shell) and Chaetopleura (Chiton).Examples of Echinodermata: Asterias (Star fish), Echinus (Sea urchin), Antedon\n(Sea lily), Cucumaria (Sea cucumber) and Ophiura (Brittle star).(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 54; Paragraph No. 1,2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','3','match','Chaetopleura,Chiton','Echinus,Sea urchin','Dentalium,Tusk shell','Antedon,Sea lily','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(241643,'biology','Given below are two statements : Statement I : Cleistogamous flowers are invariably autogamous Statement II : Cleistogamy is disadvantageous as there is no chance for cross pollination In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :[NEET 2022]','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Cleistogamous flowers does not open at all. In such flowers autogamy occurs. Lack of cross pollination is a disadvantage of cleistogamy.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct Answer','NEW','12-028',NULL,NULL),
(241644,'biology','In general the egg apparatus of embryo sac in angiosperm consists of : [ RE NEET 2022]','one egg cell,two synergids, three antipodal cells, two polar nuclei',NULL,'Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','one egg cell,two synergids, three antipodal cells, two polar nuclei','One egg cell, two synergids, two antipodal cells, \ntwo Polar nuclei','One egg cell, three synergids, two antipodal \ncells, two Polar nuclei','One egg cell, two synergids, two antipodal cells, \nthree Polar nuclei','NEW','12-027',NULL,NULL),
(241645,'biology','In which of the following sets of families, the pollen grains are viable for months? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) Leguminosae, Solanaceae and Rosaceae','In some members of Rosaceae, Solanaceae, Leguminosae pollen maintain viability for months.\nIn some cereals like rice and wheat belonging to Poaceae family to pollen loose viability within 30 \nminutes of their release','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Rosaceae, Liliaceae and Poaceae','(2) Leguminosae, Solanaceae and Rosaceae','(3) Solanaceae, Poaceae and Liliaceae','(4) Brassicaceae, Liliaceae and Poaceae','NEW','12-024',NULL,NULL),
(241646,'biology','Which one of the following is NOT an advantage of inbreeding? [ NEET 2023 ]','(3) It decreases the productivity of inbred population, after continuous inbreeding.','Decrease the productivity of inbreeding is not an advantage character.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) It exposes harmful recessive genes but are eliminated by selection.','(2) Elimination of less desirable genes and accumulation of superior genes takes place due to it.','(3) It decreases the productivity of inbred population, after continuous inbreeding.','(4) It decreases homozygosity.','NEW','12-031',NULL,NULL),
(241701,'chemistry','RMgI is an organometallic compound due to','C--- Mg bond','Most organic chlorides, bromides and iodides react with certain metals to give compounds containing carbon-metal bonds. \n\nSuch compounds are known as organo-metallic compounds. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 310 last Paragraph)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Mg--I bond','C---I bond','C--- Mg bond','C---H bond','NEW','12-310','12-182',NULL),
(241702,'chemistry','The compound formed on heating chlorobenzene with chloral in the presence of concentrated sulphuric acid is','DDT','DDT is formed by heating chloral with Chlorobenzene.\n\nDDT is a chlorophenylethane that has additional chloro substituents at positions 4 of the phenyl substituents, making it 1,1,1-trichloro-2,2-diphenylethane. \n\nThis organochlorine pesticide is frequently used.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 318 Paragraph 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','gammexane','DDT','freon','hexachloroethane','NEW','12-318','12-190',NULL),
(241703,'chemistry','Acetyl bromide reacts with excess of CH₃MgI followed by treatment with a saturated solution of NH₄Cl gives','2-methyl 2 propanol','The reaction will proceed as given in the diagram(ne2432)','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','acetone','acetamine','2-methyl 2 propanol','acetyl iodide','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241704,'chemistry','On mixing ethyl acetate with aqueous sodium chloride, the composition of the resultant solution is','CH₃COOC₂H₅+NaCl','No reaction will occur between ethyl acetate and aq. NaCl','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','CH₃COOC₂H₅+NaCl','CH₃COONa+C₂H₅OH','CH₃COCl+C₂H₅OH+ NaOH','CH₃Cl+C₂H₅COONa','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(241705,'chemistry','Which one of the following has the minimum boiling point?','Isobutene','As the number of carbons in an alkane increases, so do its boiling points. \n\nThis is due to the fact that the intermolecular attractive forces, while individually small, become cumulatively stronger as the molecule\'s atom and electron counts rise. \n\nBranched alkanes often have lower boiling points than unbranched alkanes with the same carbon concentration. \n\nThe stronger van der Waals interactions between the molecules of the unbranched alkanes lead this to happen.\n\n(Reference:NCERT Figure 10.1 NCERT Chemistry Class 12 Pg 298 )','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','n-butane','1-butyne','1-butene','Isobutene','NEW','12-298','12-168',NULL),
(241706,'chemistry','Which one of the following is reduced with zinc and hydrochloric aic?','Butan-2-one','Carbonyl compounds undergoes Clemmenson reduction to form alkanes.','Organic Chemistry >> Haloalkanes and Haloarenes >> JEE Mains Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Ethyl acetate','Acetic acid','Acetamide','Butan-2-one','NEW',NULL,'-',NULL),
(241801,'biology','Assertion:  The diversity of structures of the inflorescences, flowers and floral parts, shows an amazing range of adaptations.\n\nReason: This is to ensure formation of the end products of asexual reproduction, the seeds and flowers.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','All flowering plants show sexual reproduction. A look at the diversity of structures of the inflorescences, flowers and floral parts, shows an amazing range of adaptations to ensure formation of the end products of sexual reproduction (Reference  : NCERT, page 19, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-019',NULL,NULL),
(241802,'biology','Assertion: Pre-fertilization events lead to the differentiation of floral primordium.\n\nReason: The floral primordium needs to be divided into smaller parts to make space for pollen.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Several hormonal and structural changes are initiated which lead to the differentiation and further development of the floral primordium. (Reference  : NCERT, page 20, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-020',NULL,NULL),
(241819,'biology','Assertion: A typical angiosperm embryo sac, at maturity, is 7-celled.\n\nReason: It has a total of 8 nuclei.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Six of the eight nuclei are surrounded by cell walls and organised into cells; the remaining two nuclei, called polar nuclei are situated below the egg apparatus in the large central cell. The large central cell, as mentioned earlier, has two polar nuclei. Thus, a typical angiosperm embryo sac, at maturity, though 8-nucleate is 7-celled. (Reference  : NCERT, page 27, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-027',NULL,NULL),
(241820,'biology','Assertion: Both types of gametes have to be brought together for fertilisation to occur.\n\nReason: Male and female gametes in flowering plants are produced in the pollen grain and embryo sac.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Male and female gametes in flowering plants are produced in the pollen grain and embryo sac, respectively. As both types of gametes are non-motile, they have to be brought together for fertilization to occur. (Reference  : NCERT, page 27, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-027',NULL,NULL),
(241821,'biology','Assertion: In Autogamy, the anthers and the stigma should lie close to each other.\n\nReason: Commelina produces two types of flowers.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','In Autogamy, the anthers and the stigma should lie close to each other so that self-pollination can occur. (Reference  : NCERT, page 28, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-028',NULL,NULL),
(241822,'biology','Assertion: Genetically, geitonogamy is similar to autogamy.\n\nReason: Autogamy and geitonogamy are both kinds of pollination.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Genetically, geitonogamy is similar to autogamy since the pollen grains come from the same plant. (Reference  : NCERT, page 28, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-028',NULL,NULL),
(241823,'biology','Assertion: The flowers produce enormous amount of pollen.\n\nReason: A large amount of pollen grains can help compensate for the pollen loss.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','To compensate for this uNCERTainties and associated loss of pollen grains, the flowers produce enormous amount of pollen when compared to the number of ovules available for pollination. (Reference  : NCERT, page 28, paragraph 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-028',NULL,NULL),
(241824,'biology','Assertion: Geitonogamy is the only type of pollination which during pollination brings genetically different types of pollen grains to the stigma.\n\nReason: It is functionally self-pollination.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Geitonogamy is functionally cross-pollination. Xenogamy is the only type of pollination which during pollination brings genetically different types of pollen grains to the stigma. (Reference  : NCERT, page 28, paragraph 3 and 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-028',NULL,NULL),
(241825,'biology','Assertion: Wind pollination also requires that the pollen grains are heavier and stick well.\n\nReason: This helps them stick to the plant better.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Wind pollination also requires that the pollen grains are light and non-sticky so that they can be transported in wind currents. (Reference  : NCERT, page 29, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-029',NULL,NULL),
(241826,'biology','Assertion: It is believed, particularly for some bryophytes and pteridophytes, that their pollen distribution is limited.\n\nReason: They need for wind for the transport of male gametes and fertilisation.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','It is believed, particularly for some bryophytes and pteridophytes, that their distribution is limited because of the need for water for the transport of male gametes and fertilisation. (Reference  : NCERT, page 29, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-029',NULL,NULL),
(241827,'biology','Assertion: Continued self-pollination is not good for plants.\n\nReason: It can result in multiple flowerings simultaneously.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Continued self-pollination result in inbreeding depression.  (Reference  : NCERT, page 31, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-031',NULL,NULL),
(241828,'biology','Assertion: If both male and female flowers are present on the same plant such as castor and maize (monoecious), it prevents geitonogamy.\n\nReason: One way to prevent self-pollination is the production of multisexual flowers.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Another device to prevent self-pollination is the production of unisexual flowers. If both male and female flowers are present on the same plant such as castor and maize (monoecious), it prevents autogamy but not geitonogamy. (Reference  : NCERT, page 31, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-031',NULL,NULL),
(241829,'biology','Assertion: In several species such as papaya, both autogamy and geitonogamy can be prevented.\n\nReason: Male and female flowers are present on different plants, that is each plant is either male or female (dioecy).','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In several species such as papaya, male and female flowers are present on different plants, that is each plant is either male or female (dioecy). This condition prevents both autogamy and geitonogamy. (Reference  : NCERT, page 31, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-031',NULL,NULL),
(241830,'biology','Assertion: Self-incompatibility can prevent inbreeding.\n\nReason: It prevents self-pollen (from the same flower or other flowers of the same plant) from fertilising the ovules by inhibiting pollen germination or pollen tube growth in the pistil.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','One device to prevent inbreeding is self-incompatibility. This is a genetic mechanism and prevents self-pollen (from the same flower or other flowers of the same plant) from fertilizing the ovules by inhibiting pollen germination or pollen tube growth in the pistil. (Reference  : NCERT, page 31, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-031',NULL,NULL),
(241831,'biology','Assertion: There is nothing plant breeders can get help with by knowing about pollen-pistil interaction.\n\nReason: Plant-pistil interaction is purely a plant’s internal reproductive process.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','The knowledge gained would help the plant breeder in manipulating pollen-pistil interaction, even in incompatible pollinations, to get desired hybrids. (Reference  : NCERT, page 33, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-033',NULL,NULL),
(241832,'biology','Assertion: Emasculated flowers have to be covered with a bag of suitable size.\n\nReason: Emasculation involves bisexual flowers which have a removal of anthers from their flower bud before the anther dehisces using a pair of forceps.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Emasculated flowers have to be covered with a bag of suitable size, generally made up of butter paper, to prevent contamination of its stigma with unwanted pollen. (Reference  : NCERT, page: 33, paragraph 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-033',NULL,NULL),
(241833,'biology','Assertion: Double Fertilization involves double fusion.\n\nReason: The male gamete moves towards the two polar nuclei located in the central cell and fuses with them to produce a diploid primary endosperm nucleus.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','The other male gamete moves towards the two polar nuclei located in the central cell and fuses with them to produce a triploid primary endosperm nucleus. Double fertilization involves the fusion of three haploid nuclei it is termed triple fusion. Since two types of fusions, syngamy and triple fusion take place in an embryo sac the phenomenon is termed double fertilisation. (Reference  : NCERT, page 34, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-034',NULL,NULL),
(241834,'biology','Assertion: Zygote is a diploid cell.\n\nReason: One of the female gametes moves towards the pollen cell and fuses with all its nuclei.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','One of the male gametes moves towards the egg cell and fuses with its nucleus thus completing the syngamy. This results in the formation of a diploid cell, the zygote. (Reference  : NCERT, page 34, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-034',NULL,NULL),
(241835,'biology','Assertion: Endosperm development precedes embryo development.\n\nReason: The endosperm tissue provides nutrition for the developing embryo.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Endosperm development precedes embryo development. The primary endosperm cell divides repeatedly and forms a triploid endosperm tissue. The cells of this tissue are filled with reserve food materials and are used for the nutrition of the developing embryo. (Reference  : NCERT, page 35, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-035',NULL,NULL),
(241836,'biology','Assertion: In endosperm development, the PEN undergoes successive nuclear divisions and this stage of endosperm development is called free-nuclear endosperm.\n\nReason: Nuclear divisions lead to free nuclei.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','In the most common type of endosperm development, the PEN undergoes successive nuclear divisions to give rise to free nuclei. This stage of endosperm development is called free-nuclear endosperm. (Reference  : NCERT, page 35, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-035',NULL,NULL),
(241837,'biology','Assertion: The cotyledons of the embryo are simple structures which are generally thick.\n\nReason: A typical seed consists of cotyledons.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','The cotyledons of the embryo are simple structures, generally thick and swollen due to storage of food reserves (Reference  : NCERT, page 36, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-036',NULL,NULL),
(241838,'biology','Assertion: Non-albuminous seeds have no residual endosperm.\n\nReason: Endosperm as it is completely consumed during embryo development.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Non-albuminous seeds have no residual endosperm as it is completely consumed during embryo development (Reference  : NCERT, page 36, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-036',NULL,NULL),
(241839,'biology','Assertion: Young seedlings are under-nourished until they are capable of photosynthesis on their own.\n\nReason: Seedlings are fragile and must be protected.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','As they have sufficient food reserves, young seedlings are nourished until they are capable of photosynthesis on their own. (Reference  : NCERT, page 38, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-038',NULL,NULL),
(241840,'biology','Assertion: Dehydration and dormancy of mature seeds are crucial for seeds. \n\nReason: This can help get rid of bad seeds.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Dehydration and dormancy of mature seeds are crucial for storage of seeds which can be used as food throughout the year. (Reference  : NCERT, page 38, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-038',NULL,NULL),
(241841,'biology','Assertion: Hybrid varieties of several of our food and vegetable crops are being extensively cultivated.\n\nReason: Cultivation of hybrids has tremendously increased productivity.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Hybrid varieties of several of our food and vegetable crops are being extensively cultivated. Cultivation of hybrids has tremendously increased productivity. (Reference  : NCERT, page 39, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-039',NULL,NULL),
(241842,'biology','Assertion: Research is going on in many laboratories around the world to understand the genetics of apomixes.\n\nReason: It is a potentially dangerous genetic process that must be dealt with.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Because of the importance of apomixis in hybrid seed industry, active research is going on in many laboratories around the world to understand the genetics of apomixis and to transfer apomictic genes into hybrid varieties. (Reference  : NCERT, page 39, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-039',NULL,NULL),
(241843,'biology','Assertion: Compatible pollination results in pollen tube growth through the style.\n\nReason: The process of pollen inhibition is activated.','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Following compatible pollination, pollen grain germinates on the stigma and the resulting pollen tube grow through the style, enter the ovules and finally discharges two male gametes in one of the synergids. (Reference  s: NCERT, page 40, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-040',NULL,NULL),
(241844,'biology','Assertion: Angiosperms exhibit triple fertilisation.\n\nReason: Three fusion events occur in each embryo sac.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','Angiosperms exhibit double fertilisation because two fusion events occur in each embryo sac, namely syngamy and triple fusion. (Reference  : NCERT, page 40, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect','NEW','12-040',NULL,NULL),
(241845,'biology','Consider the following statements about the sexual reproduction of flowers, and choose the correct option.','Flowers have always been used as symbols for conveying important human feelings such as love, affection, happiness, grief, mourning.','All flowering plants show sexual reproduction. A look at the diversity of structures of the inflorescences, flowers and floral parts, shows an amazing range of adaptations to ensure formation of the end products of sexual reproduction, the fruits and seeds. (Reference  : NCERT, page 19, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Most flowering plants show sexual reproduction','Flowers have always been used as symbols for conveying important human feelings such as love, affection, happiness, grief, mourning.','Diverse structured of flowers are to ensure asexual reproduction.','A and C both','NEW','12-019',NULL,NULL),
(241846,'biology','Consider the following statements about pre-fertilization and select the incorrect option.','The flowering plant does not decide beforehand about flowering.','Much before the actual flower is seen on a plant, the decision that the plant is going to flower has taken place. In the flower the male and female reproductive structures, the androecium and the gynoecium differentiate and develop. (Reference  : NCERT, page 20, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The flower has certain Pre-fertilization steps.','In the flower, the androecium and the gynoecium differentiate.','The flowering plant does not decide beforehand about flowering.','Both 1 and 2','NEW','12-020',NULL,NULL),
(241847,'biology','Given below are a few statements about Pre-fertilization events. Choose the option that is factually correct.','Hormonal and structural changes lead to development of the floral primordium.','Much before the actual flower is seen on a plant, the decision that the plant is going to flower has taken place. Several hormonal and structural changes are initiated which lead to the differentiation and further development of the floral primordium. Inflorescences are formed which bear the floral buds and then the flowers. (Reference  : NCERT, page 20, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Several hormonal and structural changes are initiated which stop differentiation.','The plant’s decision to flower or not to flower does not lead to any changes.','Inflorescences are formed which bear the leaf buds and then leaves.','Hormonal and structural changes lead to development of the floral primordium.','NEW','12-020',NULL,NULL),
(241848,'biology','Consider the following statements about the male and female reproductive parts of a flower: \ni. The androecium is the female reproductive part and the gynoecium is the male reproductive part.\nii. Gynoecium consists of a whorl of stamens.\niii. Androecium consists of a whorl of stamens.\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Only iii is correct','In the flower the male and female reproductive structures, the androecium and the gynoecium differentiate and develop. You would recollect that the androecium consists of a whorl of stamens representing the male reproductive organ and the gynoecium represents the female reproductive organ. (Reference  : NCERT, page 20, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','12-020',NULL,NULL),
(241849,'biology','Consider the following statements Stamen, Microsporangium and Pollen Grain, and choose the correct option.','The number and length of stamens are variable in flowers of different species','The two parts of a typical stamen are the long and slender stalk called the filament, and the terminal generally bilobed structure called the anther. The proximal end of the filament is attached to the thalamus or the petal of the flower. The number and length of stamens are variable in flowers of different species. (Reference  : NCERT, page 21, paragraph 1)','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The two parts of a typical stamen consist of the short and thick stalk called the filament, and the axial generally tetralobed structure called the anther.','The distal end of the filament is attached to the thalamus.','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','The number and length of stamens are variable in flowers of different species','NEW','12-021',NULL,NULL),
(241850,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Stamen, Microsporangium and Pollen Grain. Choose the incorrect option.','The microsporangia develop further and become ovaries.','The bilobed nature of an anther is very distinct in the transverse section of the anther. The microsporangia develop further and become pollen sacs. The anther is a four-sided (tetragonal) structure consisting of four microsporangia (Reference  : NCERT, page 21, paragraph 2,3 and 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','The bilobed nature of an anther is very distinct in the transverse section of the anther','The microsporangia develop further and become ovaries.','The anther is a four-sided (tetragonal) structure consisting of four microsporangia','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect.','NEW','12-021',NULL,NULL),
(241901,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Stamen, Microsporangium and Pollen Grain: \n\ni. A typical microsporangium’s wall layers are three in number: the epidermis, endothecium, and the tapetum.\n\nii. The outer three wall layers perform the function of nourishment.\n\niii. Cells of the tapetum possess dense cytoplasm and generally have more than one nucleus.\n\nChoose the correct option, using the code below.','Only iii is correct','In a transverse section, a typical microsporangium appears near circular in outline. It is generally surrounded by four wall layers, the epidermis, endothecium, middle layers and the tapetum. The outer three wall layers perform the function of protection and help in dehiscence of anther to release the pollen. The innermost wall layer is the tapetum. It nourishes the developing pollen grains. The epidermis, endothecium, middle layers and the tapetum. Cells of the tapetum possess dense cytoplasm and generally have more than one nucleus. (Reference  : NCERT, page 21, paragraph 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','12-021',NULL,NULL),
(121407,'chemistry','During hearing of a court case, the judge suspected that some changes in the documents had been carried out. He asked the forensic department to check the ink used at two different places. According to you which technique can give the best results?','Thin layer chromatography','Thin layer chromatography (TLC) is another type of adsorption chromatography, which involves separation of substances of a mixture over a thin layer of an adsorbent coated on glass plate. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 361; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Column chromatography','Solvent extraction','Distillation','Thin layer chromatography','NEW','11-361','11-283',NULL),
(241902,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Stamen, Microsporangium and Pollen Grain: \n\ni. A typical angiosperm anther is trilobed with each lobe having four theca.\n\nii. When the anther is young, a group of heterogenous cells called the microsepalous tissue occupies the terminals of microsporangium.\n\niii. As the anther develops, the cells of the sporogenous tissue undergo meiotic divisions.\n\nChoose the correct option, using the code below.','Only i and ii are incorrect','When the anther is young, a group of compactly arranged homogenous cells called the sporogenous tissue occupies the centre of each microsporangium. A typical angiosperm anther is bilobed with each lobe having two theca, i.e., they are dithecous. As the anther develops, the cells of the sporogenous tissue undergo meiotic divisions to form microspore tetrads. (Reference  : NCERT, page 21, paragraph 2,5 and 6).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect','Only i and ii are incorrect','Only ii and iii are incorrect','Only iii is incorrect','NEW','12-021',NULL,NULL),
(241903,'biology','Consider the following statements about pollen grains choose the correct option','The inner wall of the pollen grain is called the intine.','Pollen grains are generally spherical measuring about 25-50 micrometers in diameter. The hard outer layer called the exine is made up of sporopollenin. The inner wall of the pollen grain is called the intine. (Reference  : NCERT, page 23, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Pollen grains are generally spherical measuring about 25-75 micrometers in diameter.','The hard outer layer called the exine is made up of sporogenous.','The inner wall of the pollen grain is called the intine.','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','NEW','12-023',NULL,NULL),
(241904,'biology','Consider the following statements about the pollen grain and choose the incorrect option','There are certain enzymes that degrade sporopollenin.','The cytoplasm of pollen grain is surrounded by a plasma membrane. No enzyme that degrades sporopollenin is so far known. Pollen grain exine has prominent apertures called germ pores (Reference  : NCERT, page 23, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','There are certain enzymes that degrade sporopollenin.','Pollen grain exine has prominent apertures called germ pores','The cytoplasm of pollen grain is surrounded by a plasma membrane','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect.','NEW','12-023',NULL,NULL),
(241905,'biology','Consider the following statements about the viability of a pollen grain.\n\ni. The time period of viability is highly variable.\n\nii. The viability of a pollen grain does depend on the temperature.\n\niii.  The viability of a pollen grain does not depend on the humidity.\n\nChoose the correct option, using the code below.','Only i and ii are correct','The period for which pollen grains remain viable is highly variable and to some extent depends on the prevailing temperature and humidity. (Reference  : NCERT, page 24, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','12-024',NULL,NULL),
(241906,'biology','Consider the following statements about pollen grains and choose the incorrect option.','It is not possible to store pollen grains.','The period for which pollen grains remain viable is highly variable. In some cereals such as rice and wheat, pollen grains lose viability within 30 minutes of their release, and in some members of Rosaceae, Leguminoseae and Solanaceae, they maintain viability for months. It is possible to store pollen grains of a large number of species for years in liquid nitrogen (-196°C).  (Reference  : NCERT, page 24, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','The period for which pollen grains remain viable can vary.','It is not possible to store pollen grains.','Solanaceae can maintain viability for months.','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect.','NEW','12-024',NULL,NULL),
(241907,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Pistil, Megasporangium and Embryo sac and choose the INCORRECT option.      1.  Arising from the placenta are the megasporangia, commonly called ovaries. 2. "The body of the ovule fuses with funicle in the region called hilum. 3. "Hilum represents the junction between ovule and funicle. 4. Only one ovule is present in all the species of plants','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Arising from the placenta are the megasporangia, commonly called ovules. The body of the ovule fuses with funicle in the region called hilum. Thus, hilum represents the junction between ovule and funicle. The number of ovules in an ovary may be one to many (Reference  : NCERT, page 25, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect.','NEW','12-025',NULL,NULL),
(241908,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Pistil, Megasporangium and Embryo sac and choose the correct option.','The process of formation of megaspores from the megaspore mother cell is called megasporogenesis.','An ovule generally has a single embryo sac formed from a megaspore. The process of formation of megaspores from the megaspore mother cell is called megasporogenesis. Located in the nucellus is the embryo sac or female gametophyte. (Reference  : NCERT, page 25, paragraph 3 and 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','An ovule generally has four embryo sacs formed from a megaspore','The process of formation of megaspores from the megaspore mother cell is called megasporogenesis.','Located in the nucellus is the embryo sac or male gametophyte','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect.','NEW','12-025',NULL,NULL),
(241909,'biology','Consider the following statements about the kinds of pollination and choose the correct option.','Depending on the source of pollen, pollination can be divided into three types','Depending on the source of pollen, pollination can be divided into three types. In Autogamy, pollination is achieved within the same flower. In a normal flower which opens and exposes the anthers and the stigma, complete autogamy is rather rare. (Reference  : NCERT, page 27, paragraph 5).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Depending on the source of pollen, pollination can be divided into three types','In a normal flower which opens and exposes the anthers and the stigma, complete autogamy is rather common.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Pollination is achieved within the same flower in Xenogamy.','NEW','12-027',NULL,NULL),
(121408,'chemistry','The principle involved in paper chromatography is','partition','Partition chromatography is based on continuous differential partitioning of components of a mixture between stationary and mobile phases. Paper chromatography is a type of partition chromatography. In paper chromatography, a special quality paper known as chromatography paper is used. Reference: (NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page No. 362; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','adsorption','partition','solubility','volatilty','NEW','11-362','11-284',NULL),
(241910,'biology','Consider the following statements about Autogamy below.\n\ni. Cleistogamous flowers do not open at all.\n\nii. Cleistogamous flowers are invariably xenogamous.\n\niii. Cleistogamous flowers produce assured seed-set only in the presence of pollinators.\n\nUsing the code below, choose the correct option.','Only i is correct','Oxalis, and Commelina produce two types of flowers – chasmogamous flowers which are similar to flowers of other species with exposed anthers and stigma, and cleistogamous flowers which do not open at all. In such flowers, the anthers and stigma lie close to each other. When anthers dehisce in the flower buds, pollen grains come in contact with the stigma to effect pollination. Thus, cleistogamous flowers are invariably autogamous. Cleistogamous flowers produce assured seed-set even in the absence of pollinators. (Reference  : NCERT, page 28, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','12-028',NULL,NULL),
(241911,'biology','Consider the following statements about Autogamy and choose the incorrect option','Oxalis and Commelina produce four types of flowers','In Autogamy, transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of the same flower. Autogamy in such flowers requires synchrony in pollen release and stigma receptivity and also, the anthers and the stigma should lie close to each other so that self-pollination can occur. Some plants such as Viola (common pansy), Oxalis, and Commelina produce two types of flowers. Cleistogamous flowers produce assured seed-set even in the absence of pollinators. (Reference  : NCERT, page: 27 and 28, paragraph 5 and1, respectively).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Oxalis and Commelina produce four types of flowers','Cleistogamous flowers produce assured seed-set even in the absence of pollinators','In autogamy, there is transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of the same flower.','Autogamy in flowers requires synchrony in pollen release and stigma receptivity.','NEW','12-027',NULL,NULL),
(241912,'biology','Consider the following statements about the agents of pollination and choose the correct option','Majority of plants use biotic agents for pollination','Plants use two abiotic (wind and water) and one biotic (animals) agents to achieve pollination. Pollen grains coming in contact with the stigma is a chance factor in both wind and water pollination. Majority of plants use biotic agents for pollination. Only a small proportion of plants use abiotic agents. (Reference  : NCERT, page 28, paragraph 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Assertion Reason and Multiple statement questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Majority of plants use abiotic agents for pollination','Plants use one abiotic (water) and one biotic (animals) agents to achieve pollination','Majority of plants use biotic agents for pollination','Pollen grains coming in contact with the stigma is a chance factor in water pollination only.','NEW','12-028',NULL,NULL),
(242001,'biology','Consider the following statements pollination and choose the incorrect option','Pollination by wind is more common amongst biotic pollinations','Pollination by wind is more common amongst abiotic pollinations. Wind pollination also requires that the pollen grains are light and non-sticky so that they can be transported in wind currents. They often possess well-exposed stamens. Wind- pollinated flowers often have a single ovule in each ovary. (Reference  : NCERT, page 29, paragraph 1)','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Pollination by wind is more common amongst biotic pollinations','Wind pollination also requires that the pollen grains are light and non-sticky.','Flowers that are pollinated by wind often possess well-exposed stamens.','Wind- pollinated flowers often have a single ovule in each ovary.','NEW','12-029',NULL,NULL),
(242002,'biology','Consider the following statement about pollination of a plant.\n\ni. All aquatic plants use water for pollination.\n\nii. In Vallisneria, the female flower reach the surface of water by the long stalk.\n\niii. In seagrasses, female flowers remain submerged in water.\n\nUsing the code below, choose the correct option.','Only i is incorrect.','Not all aquatic plants use water for pollination. In Vallisneria, the female flower reach the surface of water by the long stalk and the male flowers or pollen grains are released on to the surface of water. In another group of water pollinated plants such as seagrasses, female flowers remain submerged in water and the pollen grains are released inside the water. Pollen (Reference  : NCERT, page 29, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect.','Only i and ii are incorrect','Only ii and iii are incorrect','Only iii is incorrect','NEW','12-029',NULL,NULL),
(242003,'biology','Consider the following statement about the pollination of a plant.\n\ni. Water-pollination is quite common in grasses.\n\nii. Pollination by water is quite common in flowering plants.\n\niii. Wind-pollinated flowers often have a single ovule in each ovary.\n\niv. A familiar example is the corn cob - the tassels you see are nothing but the stigma and style which wave in the wind to trap pollen grains.\n\nUsing the code below, choose the correct option.','Only iii & iv is correct','Wind-pollination is quite common in grasses. Wind- pollinated flowers often have a single ovule in each ovary and numerous flowers packed into an inflorescence; a familiar example is the corn cob - the tassels you see are nothing but the stigma and style which wave in the wind to trap pollen grains. Pollination by water is quite rare in flowering plants (Reference  : NCERT, page 29, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only iv is correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii & iv is correct','NEW','12-029',NULL,NULL),
(242004,'biology','Consider the following statements about the pollination and choose the correct option','Pollen grains in many species are long, ribbon like and they are carried passively inside the water.','In Vallisneria, the female flower reach the surface of water by the long stalk. Pollen grains in many species are long, ribbon like and they are carried passively inside the water.  Some examples of water pollinated plants are Vallisneria and Hydrilla which grow in fresh water and several marine sea-grasses such as Zostera. (Reference  : NCERT, page 29, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Pollen grains in many species are long, ribbon like and they are carried passively inside the water.','In Vallisneria, the Male flower reach the surface of water by the short stalk.','Some examples of water pollinated plants are grasses.','Both 2 and 3 are correct.','NEW','12-029',NULL,NULL),
(40422,'biology','Water vascular system and tube feet is the characteristic of ________.','Echinodermata','The most distinctive feature of echinoderms is the presence of water vascular system.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 54; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Arthropoda','Echinodermata','Molluscs','Bony fishes','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(242005,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Pollen-pistil Interaction choose the incorrect option.','Pollination guarantees the transfer of the right type of pollen','Pollination does not guarantee the transfer of the right type of pollen. The pistil has the ability to recognise the pollen, whether it is of the right type (compatible) or of the wrong type (incompatible). If it is of the right type, the pistil accepts the pollen and promotes post-pollination events (Reference  : NCERT, page 31, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The pistil has the ability to recognise the pollen.','If the pollen is of the right type, the pistil accepts the pollen and promotes post-pollination events.','Pollination guarantees the transfer of the right type of pollen','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect.','NEW','12-031',NULL,NULL),
(242006,'biology','Consider the following statements about Pollen-pistil Interaction and choose the correct option.','The ability of the pistil to recognise the is the result of a continuous dialogue between pollen grain and the pistil.','The ability of the pistil to recognise the pollen followed by its acceptance or rejection is the result of a continuous dialogue between pollen grain and the pistil. This dialogue is mediated by chemical components of the pollen interacting with those of the pistil. It is only in recent years that botanists have been able to identify some of the pollen and pistil components and the interactions leading to the recognition, followed by acceptance or rejection. Following compatible pollination, the pollen grain germinates on the stigma to produce a pollen tube through one of the germ pores (Reference  : NCERT, page 32, paragraph 1 and 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The ability of the pistil to recognise the is the result of a continuous dialogue between pollen grain and the pistil.','Pollen-pistil dialogue is mediated by physical components of the pollen interacting with those of the pistil.','Botanists have not yet been able to identify the pollen and pistil components and the interactions.','Following compatible pollination, the pollen grain germinates on the anther to produce a pollen tube through one of the ovules','NEW','12-032',NULL,NULL),
(242007,'biology','Consider the following statements about Pollen-pistil Interaction ,Choose the incorrect option','If the pollen is of the wrong type, the pistil accepts the pollen by preventing pollen germination','If pollen is of the right type, the pistil accepts the pollen and promotes post-pollination events that leads to fertilization. If the pollen is of the wrong type, the pistil rejects the pollen by preventing pollen germination. The ability of the pistil to recognise the pollen followed by its acceptance or rejection is the result of a continuous dialogue between pollen grain and the pistil. (Reference  : NCERT, page 32, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If the pollen is of the wrong type, the pistil accepts the pollen by preventing pollen germination','If the pollen is of the wrong type, the pistil rejects the pollen by preventing pollen germination','The ability of the pistil to recognise the pollen followed by its acceptance or rejection.','Post-pollen events lead to fertilization.','NEW','12-032',NULL,NULL),
(242008,'biology','Consider the following statements about Parthenocarpy.\n\ni. Seeds develops without fertilisation in parthenocarpy.\n\nii.  When fruits develop without seeds, they are called parthenocarpic fruits.\n\niii. Banana is a parthenocarpic fruit.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','All of them are correct','Although in most of the species, fruits are the results of fertilisation, there are a few species in which fruits develop without fertilisation. Such fruits are called parthenocarpic fruits. Banana is one such example. (Reference  : NCERT, page 37, paragraph 1)','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','All of them are correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','12-037',NULL,NULL),
(242009,'biology','Consider the following statements about plant seeds.\n\ni. Parthenocarpy can be induced through the application of growth hormones.\n\nii. Seeds have better adaptive strategies for dispersal to new habitats.\n\niii. Seed formation is more independent.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below','Only iii is incorrect','Parthenocarpy can be induced through the application of growth hormones. Since reproductive processes such as pollination and fertilisation are independent of water, seed formation is more dependable. Seeds have better adaptive strategies for dispersal to new habitats and (Reference  s: NCERT, page 37, paragraph 1 and 2)','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect','Only i and ii are incorrect','Only ii and iii are incorrect','Only iii is incorrect','NEW','12-037',NULL,NULL),
(242010,'biology','Consider the following statements about double fertilisation and choose the correct option','one of the male gamete moves towards the egg cell and fuses with its nucleus thus completing the\nsyngamy','After entering one of the synergids, the pollen tube releases the two male gametes into the cytoplasm of the synergid. One of the male gametes moves towards the egg cell and fuses with its nucleus thus completing the syngamy. This results in the formation of a diploid cell, the zygote. (Reference  : class XII NCERT, page 34, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','After entering one of the synergids, the pollen tube releases a single male gamete into the cytoplasm.','one of the male gamete moves towards the egg cell and fuses with its nucleus thus completing the\nsyngamy','Syngamy results in the formation of a diploid cell, the embryo.','Both 1 and 3 both are correct','NEW','12-034',NULL,NULL),
(242011,'biology','Consider the following statements about and select the incorrect option','Triple fusion involves the fusion of three diploid nuclei','One of the male gametes moves towards the egg cell and fuses with its nucleus thus completing the syngamy. This results in the formation of a diploid cell, the zygote. The other male gamete moves towards the two polar nuclei located in the central cell and fuses with them to produce a triploid primary endosperm nucleus (PEN). As this involves the fusion of three haploid nuclei it is termed triple fusion. (Reference  : NCERT, page 34, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Triple fusion involves the fusion of three diploid nuclei','One of the male gametes moves towards the egg cell and fuses with its nucleus thus completing the syngamy.','The other male gamete moves towards the two polar nuclei located in the central cell and fuses with them to produce a triploid primary endosperm nucleus (PEN).','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','NEW','12-034',NULL,NULL),
(40423,'biology','Identify the organism shown in diagram (0428)','Asterias','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 54; Figure 4.14(a) )','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Asterias','Ophiura','Echinus','Antedon','NEW','11-054','11-045','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40423q.png'),
(40424,'biology','Identify the organism shown in diagram (0429)','Ophiura','(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 54; Figure 4.14(b) )','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','3','mcq','Asterias','Ophiura','Echinus','Antedon','NEW','11-054','11-045','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/40424q.png'),
(242012,'biology','Given below are a few statements about Endosperm. Choose the option that is factually INCORRECT','The number of free nuclei formed before cellularisation is constant','The coconut water from tender coconut that you are familiar with, is nothing but free-nuclear endosperm (made up of thousands of nuclei) and the surrounding white kernel is the cellular endosperm. Endosperm may either be completely consumed by the developing embryo (e.g., pea, groundnut, beans) before seed maturation or it may persist in the mature seed (e.g. castor and coconut) and be used up during seed germination. The number of free nuclei formed before cellularisation varies greatly. (Reference  : NCERT, page 35, paragraph 1 and 2)','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The number of free nuclei formed before cellularisation is constant','In pea, the endosperm is completely consumed by the developing embryo','In castor, endosperm persists in the mature seed.','Coconut water is nothing but free-nuclear endosperm.','NEW','12-035',NULL,NULL),
(242013,'biology','Consider the following statements about the embryo: \n\ni. The zygote gives rise to the proembryo.\n\nii. The portion of embryonal axis above the level of cotyledons is the epicotyl.\n\niii. A typical dicotyledonous embryo consists of an embryonal axis and one cotyledon.\n\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:','Ony i and ii are correct.','The zygote gives rise to the proembryo and subsequently to the globular, heart-shaped and mature embryo. A typical dicotyledonous embryo (Figure 2.14a), consists of an embryonal axis and two cotyledons. The portion of embryonal axis above the level of cotyledons is the epicotyl, which terminates with the plumule or stem tip. (Reference  : NCERT, page 35, paragraph 3 and 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Ony i and ii are correct.','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','12-035',NULL,NULL),
(242014,'biology','Consider the following statements choose the correct option','The cylindrical portion below the level of cotyledons is hypocotyl','Embryo develops at the micropylar end of the embryo sac where the zygote is situated. Most zygotes divide only after certain amount of endosperm is formed. The cylindrical portion below the level of cotyledons is hypocotyl (Reference  : NCERT, page 35, paragraph 4)','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Embryo develops at the anterior end of the embryo sac where the ovule is situated.','Most zygotes divide only before all of endosperm is formed.','Both 1 and 2 are correct.','The cylindrical portion below the level of cotyledons is hypocotyl','NEW','12-035',NULL,NULL),
(242015,'biology','Consider the following statements about the seed and Choose the incorrect option','Non-albuminous seeds have a few residual endospermic tissue matter.','Seeds are formed inside fruits. Mature seeds may be non-albuminous or ex-albuminous. Non-albuminous seeds have no residual endosperm as it is completely consumed during embryo development (Reference  : NCERT, page 36, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Seeds are formed inside fruits.','Mature seeds may be non-albuminous or ex-albuminous','Non-albuminous seeds have a few residual endospermic tissue matter.','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect.','NEW','12-036',NULL,NULL),
(242016,'biology','Consider the following statements about the seed: \n\ni. As the seed matures, its water content is increased and it swells up.\n\nii. The embryo may enter a state of inactivity.\n\niii. As the seed matures, its water content is reduced.\n\nChoose the correct option, using the code below','Only ii and iii are correct','As the seed matures, its water content is reduced and seeds become relatively dry. The general metabolic activity of the embryo slows down. The embryo may enter a state of inactivity called dormancy, or if favourable conditions are available (adequate moisture, O₂ and suitable temperature), they germinate. (Reference  : NCERT, page 36, paragraph 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','12-036',NULL,NULL),
(242017,'biology','Consider the following statements about the seed: \n\ni. As ovules mature into seeds, the ovary develops into a fruit.\n\nii. Many fruits have evolved mechanisms for dispersal of seeds.\n\niii. In most plants, by the time the fruit develops from the ovary, other floral parts regenerate and grow in size.\n\nChoose the correct option, using the code below','Only iii is incorrect','As ovules mature into seeds, the ovary develops into a fruit. In most plants, by the time the fruit develops from the ovary, other floral parts degenerate and fall off. Many fruits have evolved mechanisms for dispersal of seeds. (Reference  : NCERT, page 36, paragraph 5 and 6).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect','Only i and ii are incorrect','Only ii and iii are incorrect','Only iii is incorrect','NEW','12-036',NULL,NULL),
(242018,'biology','Consider the following statements about seeds and choose the correct option','Seed is the basis of our agriculture','Seed is the basis of our agriculture. Dehydration and dormancy of mature seeds are crucial for storage of seeds which can be used as food throughout the year and also to raise crop in the next season. (Reference  : NCERT, page 38, paragraph 2).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Seed is the basis of our agriculture','Dehydration and dormancy of mature seeds is fatal for the agriculture.','Dehydration and dormancy of mature seeds hinders with the process of raising crop in the next season.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','NEW','12-038',NULL,NULL),
(242019,'biology','Consider the following statements about the seed and choose the incorrect option','In all species the seeds lose viability within a few months.','The period for which seeds remain alive after they are dispersed varies greatly. In a few species the seeds lose viability within a few months. A recent record of 2000 years old viable seed is of the date palm, Phoenix dactylifera discovered during the archeological excavation at King Herod’s palace near the Dead Sea. (Reference  : NCERT, page 38, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','A recent record of 2000 years old viable seed is of the date palm','In all species the seeds lose viability within a few months.','The period for seeds remaining alive after dispersed varies greatly.','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW','12-038',NULL,NULL),
(242020,'biology','Consider the following statements about the seed.\n\ni. The hard seed coat provides protection to the young embryo.\n\nii. Seeds are able to generate new genetic combinations leading to variations.\n\niii. Seeds of a large number of species live for several hours only.\n\nChoose the correct option, using the code below.','Only i and ii are correct','The hard seed coat provides protection to the young embryo. Being products of sexual reproduction, they generate new genetic combinations leading to variations. Seeds of a large number of species live for several years. (Reference  : NCERT, page 38, paragraph 3).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','12-038',NULL,NULL),
(242021,'biology','Consider the following statements about Apomixis and Polyembryony and choose the incorrect option.','Asteraceae is unable to produce seeds without fertilisation','A few flowering plants such as some species of Asteraceae and grasses, have evolved a special mechanism, to produce seeds without fertilisation, called apomixis. Apomixis is a form of asexual reproduction that mimics sexual reproduction. There are several ways of development of apomictic seeds. (Reference  : NCERT, page 38, paragraph 6).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','There are several ways of development of apomictic seeds.','Apomixis is a form of asexual reproduction that mimics sexual reproduction','Asteraceae is unable to produce seeds without fertilisation','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect.','NEW','12-038',NULL,NULL),
(242022,'biology','Consider the following statements about Apomixis and Polyembryony and choose the incorrect option.','Occurrence of more than one seed is referred to as polyembryony','More often, as in many Citrus and Mango, varieties some of the nucellar cells surrounding the embryo sac start dividing, protrude into the embryo sac and develop into the embryos. Occurrence of more than one embryo in a seed is referred to as polyembryony. (Reference  : NCERT, page 39, paragraph 1).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Occurrence of more than one seed is referred to as polyembryony','In Mango, each ovule contains many embryos.','In Citrus, each ovule contains only one embryo.','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect.','NEW','12-039',NULL,NULL),
(242023,'biology','Consider the following statements about Sexual Production in Flowering Plants:\n \ni. A typical anther is bilobed, trithecous and tetrasporangiate.\n\nii. A typical anther is trilobed, trithecous and tetrasporangiate.\n\niii. A typical anther is bilobed, dithecous and tetrasporangiate.\n\nChoose the correct option, using the code below.','Only iii is correct','A typical anther is bilobed, dithecous and tetrasporangiate. (Reference  : NCERT, page 39, paragraph 4).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is correct','Only i and ii are correct','Only ii and iii are correct','Only iii is correct','NEW','12-039',NULL,NULL),
(242024,'biology','Consider the following statements about the Sexual Production in Flowering Plants:\n \ni. Ovules are present in the ovary.\n\nii. The ovules have an opening called funicle.\n\niii. The ovules have a stalk called hilum.\n\nChoose the correct option, using the code below.','Only ii and iii are incorrect','Ovules are present in the ovary. The ovules have a stalk called funicle, protective integument(s), and an opening called micropyle. (Reference  : NCERT, page 39, Paragraph 5).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only i is incorrect','Only i and ii are incorrect','Only ii and iii are incorrect','Only iii is incorrect','NEW','12-039',NULL,NULL),
(242025,'biology','Consider the following statements about Sexual Production in Flowering Plants choose the correct option.','The developing embryo passes through different stages before maturation.','A phenomenon called apomixis is found in some angiosperms, particularly in grasses. The developing embryo passes through different stages such as the proembryo, globular and heart-shaped stages before maturation. Some angiosperms produce more than one embryo in their seed. This phenomenon is called polyembryony. (Reference  : NCERT, page 40, paragraph 3,4 and 5).','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >>Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Some angiosperms undergo polyembryony and produce more than one ovary in their seed.','A phenomenon called apomixis is found in some gymnosperms.','The developing embryo passes through different stages before maturation.','Both 1 and 2 are correct','NEW','12-040',NULL,NULL),
(242026,'biology','Large, colorful, fragrant flowers with nectar are seen in (NEET 2023)','insect pollinated flowers','Majority of insect-pollinated flowers are large, colourful, fragrant and rich in nectar. When the flowers are small, a number of flowers are clustered into an inflorescence to make them conspicuous.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','wind pollinated flowers','insect pollinated flowers','bird pollinated flowers','bat pollinated flowers','NEW','12-030',NULL,NULL),
(242027,'biology','Which one of the following plants shows a very close relationship with a species of moth, where none of the two can complete its life cycle without the other? [NEET 2018]','Yucca','Yucca has an obligate mutualism with a species of moth, i.e., Pronuba. The only pollinator of this plant is the Yucca moth. The Yucca moth is likewise dependent upon the Yucca plant as Yucca moth caterpillars need to eat Yucca seed only or starve.','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Hydrilla','Yucca','Banana','Viola.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(242028,'biology','To ensure that only the desired pollen falls on the stigma in artificial hybridization process a. The female flower buds of plants producing unisexual flowers need not to be bagged. b. There is no need to emasculate unisexual flower of selected female parent. c. Emasculated flowers are to be bagged immediately after cross pollination. d. emasculated flowers are to be bagged after removal of anthers. e. Bisexual flowers, showing protogyny, are never selected for cross.','(b), (c) and (d) only','If the female parent bears bisexual flowers, removal of anthers from the flower bud before the anther dehisces using a pair of forceps is necessary. This step is referred to as emasculation. Emasculated flowers have to be covered with a bag of suitable size, generally made up of butter paper, to prevent contamination of its stigma with unwanted pollen. This process is called bagging. When the stigma of bagged flower attains receptivity, mature pollen grains collected from anthers of the male parent are dusted on the stigma, and the flowers are rebagged, and the fruits allowed to develop.\nIf the','Reproduction >> Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants >> Previous Year Questions 2',NULL,'3','mcq','(a), (b) and (c) only','(b), (c) and (d) only','(b), (c) and (e) only','(a), (d) and (e) only','NEW','12-033',NULL,NULL),
(280101,'chemistry','The study of what occurs chemically within a living system is called ______.','Biochemistry','The pursuit of knowledge of what goes on chemically within a living system falls in the domain of biochemistry. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 411; Paragraph (Introduction))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','Organic Chemistry','Biometry','Biochemistry','Analytical Chemistry','NEW','12-411','12-281',NULL),
(280102,'chemistry','Which of the following is essential constituent of food?','Carbohydrates','Proteins and carbohydrates are essential constituents of our food.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 411; Paragraph no. (Introduction))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbohydrates','Minerals','Starch','Glucose','NEW','12-411','12-281',NULL),
(280103,'chemistry','Carbohydrates are primarily produced by ______.','Plants','Carbohydrates are primarily produced by plants and form a very large group of naturally occurring organic compounds. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 411; Paragraph no. 14.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','Plants','Synthetic organic compounds','Inorganic compounds','Animal fat','NEW','12-411','12-281',NULL),
(280104,'chemistry','Which of the following is a carbohydrate but does not follow the carbohydrate general formula?','Rhamnose','Rhamnose, C₆H₁₂O₅ is a carbohydrate but does not fit the general formula of carbohydrates Cₓ(H₂O)ᵧ.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 411; Paragraph no. 14.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'2','mcq','Glucose','Acetic Acid','Rhamnose','Starch','NEW','12-411','12-281',NULL),
(280105,'chemistry','Hydrolysis of carbohydrates produces:','aldehydes and ketones','The carbohydrates may be defined as optically active polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones or the compounds which produce such units on hydrolysis.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 411; Paragraph no. 14.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','ketones and amines','aldehydes and ketones','alcohols and sugars','phosphates and water','NEW','12-411','12-281',NULL),
(280106,'chemistry','Sugar present in milk is called?','Lactose','The most common sugar, used in our homes is named as sucrose whereas the sugar present in milk is known as lactose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 411; Paragraph no. 14.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','Sucrose','Lactose','Galactose','Fructose','NEW','12-411','12-281',NULL),
(280107,'chemistry','Carbohydrates are classified on the basis of :','Behaviour on hydrolysis','Carbohydrates are classified on the basis of their behaviour on hydrolysis.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Paragraph no. 14.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'2','mcq','Behaviour on hydrolysis','Behaviour on oxidation','Behaviour on reduction','Behaviour on combustion','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280108,'chemistry','How many monosaccharides occur in nature?','20','About 20 monosaccharides are known to occur in nature. Some common examples are glucose, fructose, ribose, etc.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Paragraph no. 14.1.1 (i))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','10','20','35','40','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280109,'chemistry','Carbohydrates that yield two to ten monosaccharide units, on hydrolysis, are called _______.','oligosaccharides','Carbohydrates that yield two to ten monosaccharide units, on hydrolysis, are called oligosaccharides.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Paragraph no. 14.1.1(ii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','oligosaccharides','polysaccharides','monosaccharides','disaccharides','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280201,'chemistry','Which of the following is not true about glucose?','Glucose cannot exist in a free state; it is always present in a combined state.','a. Glucose occurs freely in nature as well as in the combined form. \n b. It is present in sweet fruits and honey. \n c. Ripe grapes also contain glucose in large amounts.\n d. It can be prepared from starch and sucrose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','Glucose cannot exist in a free state; it is always present in a combined state.','It is also present in honey and sweet fruits.','Ripe grapes contain glucose in large amounts.','It can be prepared from starch and sucrose.','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280202,'chemistry','Which of the following is formed when sucrose is boiled in alcoholic solution with HCl?','Glucose and fructose in equal amounts.','If sucrose is boiled with dilute HCl in alcoholic solution, glucose and fructose are obtained in equal amounts.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry class 12; Page 412; Paragraph no.14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','Glucose and fructose in equal amounts.','Glucose with a little amount of fructose.','Glucose and galactose in equal amounts.','Fructose and galactose in equal amounts.','NEW','12-412','12-282',NULL),
(280203,'chemistry','Which of the following is commercial method for preparation of glucose?','Hydrolysis of starch','Commercially glucose is obtained by hydrolysis of starch by boiling it with dilute H₂SO₄ at 393 K under pressure.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 413; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'1','mcq','Hydrolysis of sucrose','Hydrolysis of starch','Hydrolysis of glycogen','Hydrolysis of galactose','NEW','12-413','12-283',NULL),
(280204,'chemistry','Which of the following is also known as dextrose?','Glucose','Glucose is an aldohexose and is also known as dextrose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 413; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'1','mcq','Fructose','Mannose','Glucose','Lactose','NEW','12-413','12-283',NULL),
(280205,'chemistry','Which compound is the most abundant organic compound on the earth?','Glucose','Glucose is an aldohexose and is also known as dextrose. It is the monomer of many of the larger carbohydrates, namely starch, cellulose. It is probably the most abundant organic compound on earth.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 413; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'1','mcq','Water','Glucose','Collagen','Phospholipid','NEW','12-413','12-283',NULL),
(280206,'chemistry','Which compound is formed when glucose is heated with HI?','n-hexane','On prolonged heating with HI, glucose forms n-hexane, suggesting that all the six carbon atoms are linked in a straight chain.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 413; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1) \n diagram(ne2813)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','Cyclohexane','n-hexane','Cyclopentane','n-heptane','NEW','12-413','12-283',NULL),
(280207,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions indicate the presence of aldehydic group present in the glucose?','Reaction with bromine water','Glucose gets oxidised to six carbon carboxylic acid (gluconic acid) on reaction with a mild oxidising agent like bromine water. This indicates that the carbonyl group is present as an aldehydic group.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 413; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1) \n diagram(ne2814)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','Reaction with hydrogen cyanide','Reaction with hydroxylamine','Reaction with bromine water','Reaction with gluconic acid','NEW','12-413','12-283',NULL),
(280208,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions indicate the presence of carbonyl group present in the glucose?','Reaction with hydroxylamine','Glucose reacts with hydroxylamine to give an oxime and adds a hydrogen cyanide molecule to give cyanohydrins. These reactions confirm the presence of a carbonyl group in glucose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 413; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1) \n diagram(ne2815)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','Reaction with hydrogen cyanide','Reaction with hydroxylamine','Reaction with bromine water','Reaction with cyanohydrin','NEW','12-413','12-283',NULL),
(280209,'chemistry','What is the product of acetylation of glucose with acetic anhydride?','Glucose pentaacetate','Acetylation of glucose with acetic anhydride gives glucose pentaacetate which confirms the presence of five –OH groups.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 413; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1) \n diagram(ne2816)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','Glucose pentaacetate','Glucose tetraacetate','Glucose triacetate','Glucose monoacetate','NEW','12-413','12-283',NULL),
(280210,'chemistry','Which of the following acid is formed when gluconic acid reacts with nitric acid?','Saccharic acid','On oxidation with nitric acid, glucose as well as gluconic acid both yield a dicarboxylic acid, saccharic acid.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 414; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1) \n diagram(ne2817)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','Saccharic acid','Gluconic acid','Fumaric acid','Maleic acid','NEW','12-414','12-284',NULL),
(280211,'chemistry','The oxidation reaction of glucose with nitric acid indicates presence of which group?','Primary alcohol','On oxidation with nitric acid, glucose as well as gluconic acid both yield a dicarboxylic acid, saccharic acid.\n This indicates the presence of a primary alcoholic (–OH) group in glucose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 414; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','Secondary alcohol','Primary alcohol','Aldehyde','Ketone','NEW','12-414','12-284',NULL),
(280212,'chemistry','What is the name of the compound shown below? \n diagram(n2801)','Gluconic acid','The name for the compound shown is Gluconic acid.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 414; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'3','mcq','Glucaric acid','Gluconic acid','Fumaric acid','Malic acid','NEW','12-414','12-284','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/280212q.png'),
(280213,'chemistry','Which is the correct configuration of D-glucose based on Fischer\'s spatial arrangement of –OH groups? \n diagram(n2802)','(B)','The exact spatial arrangement of different –OH groups was given by Fischer after studying many other properties. Its configuration is correctly represented as (B).\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 414; Paragraph no.14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'1','mcq','(A)','(B)','(C)','(D)','NEW','12-414','12-284','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/280213q.png'),
(280214,'chemistry','Which statement is incorrect for D(+)-glucose?','(+)\' here represents laevorotatory nature of molecule.','Glucose is correctly named as D(+)-glucose. ‘D’ before the name of glucose represents the configuration whereas ‘(+)’ represents dextrorotatory nature of the molecule.\n It should be remembered that ‘D’ and ‘L’ have no relation with the optical activity of the compound.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 414; Paragraph no.14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','"D" here represents configuration of glucose.','(+)\' here represents laevorotatory nature of molecule.','"D" is not related with optical activity.','D(+)-glucose is the correct word for glucose.','NEW','12-414','12-284',NULL),
(280215,'chemistry','The relative configuration of stereoisomers of carbohydrates is indicated with respect to :','Glyceraldehyde','The letters ‘D’ or ‘L’ before the name of any compound indicate the relative configuration of a particular stereoisomer of a compound with respect to configuration of some other compound, configuration of which is known. In the case of carbohydrates, this refers to their relation with a particular isomer of glyceraldehyde.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 414; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'1','mcq','Dihydroxy ketone','Glyceraldehyde','Acetone','Glucose','NEW','12-414','12-284',NULL),
(280216,'chemistry','How many asymmetric carbons are present in glyceraldehyde?','1','Glyceraldehyde contains one asymmetric carbon atom and exists in two enantiomeric forms.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 414; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'1','mcq','2','1','3','0','NEW','12-414','12-284',NULL),
(280217,'chemistry','The given structure is called : diagram(n2803)','D-Glyceraldehyde','Glyceraldehyde contains one asymmetric carbon atom and exists in two enantiomeric forms.\n (+) Isomer of glyceraldehyde has ‘D’ configuration. It means that when its structural formula is written on paper, the –OH group lies on right hand side in the structure.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 414; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'3','mcq','D-Glyceraldehyde','D-Mannose','L-Arabinose','L-Glyceraldehyde','NEW','12-414','12-284','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/280217q.png'),
(280218,'chemistry','For assigning the configuration of monosaccharides, it is the ______ which is compared.','lowest asymmetric carbon atom','For assigning the configuration of monosaccharides, it is the lowest asymmetric carbon atom which is compared.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 415; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','highest asymmetric carbon atom','lowest asymmetric carbon atom','highest symmetric carbon atom','lowest symmetric carbon atom','NEW','12-415','12-285',NULL),
(280219,'chemistry','(+) Glucose is assigned D-configuration because :','lowest asymmetric carbon of glucose is on the right side which is comparable to (+) glyceraldehyde.','In (+) glucose, –OH on the lowest asymmetric carbon is on the right side which is comparable to (+) glyceraldehyde, so (+) glucose is assigned D-configuration.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 415; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'3','mcq','highest asymmetric carbon of glucose is on the right side which is comparable to (+) glyceraldehyde.','lowest asymmetric carbon of glucose is on the right side which is comparable to (+) glyceraldehyde.','lowest symmetric carbon of glucose is on the right side which is comparable to (+) glycogen.','highest symmetric carbon of glucose is on the right side which is comparable to (+) glycogen.','NEW','12-415','12-285',NULL),
(280220,'chemistry','The structure of glucose and glyceraldehyde is written in a way that most oxidised carbon (in this case –CHO) is at the _____.','top','The structure of glucose and glyceraldehyde is written in a way that most oxidised carbon (in this case –CHO) is at the top.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 415; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'1','mcq','center','top','bottom','along the middle','NEW','12-415','12-285',NULL),
(280221,'chemistry','Which of the following cannot be explained by open chain structure of glucose?','Negative Schiff\'s test','1. Despite having the aldehyde group, glucose does not give Schiff’s test and it does not form the hydrogensulphite addition product with NaHSO₃. \n 2. The pentaacetate of glucose does not react with hydroxylamine indicating the absence of free —CHO group. \n 3. Glucose is found to exist in two different crystalline forms which are named as α and β. The α-form of glucose (m.p. 419 K) is obtained by crystallisation from concentrated solution of glucose at 303 K while the β-form (m.p. 423 K) is obtained by crystallisation from hot and saturated aqueous solution at 371 K.\n This behaviour could not be explained by the open chain structure for glucose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 415; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','Presence of primary alcoholic group','Presence of aldehydic group','Negative Schiff\'s test','Rapid reaction with hydroxylamine','NEW','12-415','12-285',NULL),
(283124,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: VNTR which is a mini-satellite will show bands of different sizes after hybridization\n\nReason: It shows high degree of polymorphism','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The VNTR belongs to a class of satellite DNA referred to as mini-satellite and its size varies in size from 0.1 to 20 kb. This is because VNTR is arranged tandemly in many copy numbers. The copy number varies from chromosome to chromosome in an individual. The numbers of repeat show very high degree of polymorphism \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 122, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(280222,'chemistry','Which of the following does not support the cyclic form of glucose?','It has a free —CHO group.','1. Despite having the aldehyde group, glucose does not give Schiff’s test and it does not form the hydrogensulphite addition product with NaHSO₃. \n 2. The pentaacetate of glucose does not react with hydroxylamine indicating the absence of free —CHO group. \n 3. Glucose is found to exist in two different crystalline forms which are named as α and β. The α-form of glucose (m.p. 419 K) is obtained by crystallisation from concentrated solution of glucose at 303 K while the β-form (m.p. 423 K) is obtained by crystallisation from hot and saturated aqueous solution at 371 K.\n This behaviour could not be explained by the open chain structure for glucose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 415; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','Negative Schiff\'s test.','The pentaacetate of glucose does not react with hydroxylamine, suggesting the absence of a free —CHO group.','It exists in two crystalline forms.','It has a free —CHO group.','NEW','12-415','12-285',NULL),
(280223,'chemistry','Which form of glucose is obtained by crystallisation from hot and saturated aqueous solution of glucose at 371K?','β glucose','The a-form of glucose (m.p. 419 K) is obtained by crystallisation from concentrated solution of glucose at 303 K while the b-form (m.p. 423 K) is obtained by crystallisation from hot and saturated aqueous solution at 371 K.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 415; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','β glucose','α glucose','δ glucose','γ glucose','NEW','12-415','12-285',NULL),
(280224,'chemistry','What is true about the cyclic form of glucose?','It forms six-membered ring.','It was proposed that one of the –OH groups may add to the –CHO group and form a cyclic hemiacetal structure. It was found that glucose forms a six-membered ring in which –OH at C-5 is involved in ring formation.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 415; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'2','mcq','It is a cyclic acetal structure.','It is an open hemiacetal structure.','It forms six-membered ring.','It forms 5 membered ring.','NEW','12-415','12-285',NULL),
(280225,'chemistry','The two cyclic hemiacetal forms of glucose differ only in the configuration of the hydroxyl group at :','C1, which is called anomeric carbon.','The two cyclic hemiacetal forms of glucose differ only in the configuration of the hydroxyl group at C1, is called anomeric carbon.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 415; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'1','mcq','C5, which is called anomeric carbon.','C3, which is called anomeric carbon.','C2, which is called anomeric carbon.','C1, which is called anomeric carbon.','NEW','12-415','12-285',NULL),
(280226,'chemistry','The six membered cyclic structure of glucose is called ____ structure.','pyranose structure','The six membered cyclic structure of glucose is called pyranose structure (α– or β–), in analogy with pyran.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'1','mcq','pyranose structure','pyran structure','dextrose structure','ketonose structure','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(280227,'chemistry','Which of the following is true for pyran?','Ring consists of an oxygen and 5 carbon atoms.','Pyran is a cyclic organic compound with one oxygen atom and five carbon atoms in the ring.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Glucose',NULL,'3','mcq','Open chain organic compound.','It is aromatic structure.','Ring consists of an oxygen and 5 carbon atoms.','Pyran shows resonance.','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(280228,'biology','The proposition of Double helix model was also based on the observation of _______ for double stranded DNA.','Erwin Chargaff','The proposition of Double helix model was also based on the observation of Erwin Chargaff for double stranded DNA.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:97, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Friedrich Meischer','James Watson and Francis Crick','Maurice Wilkins and Rosalind Franklin','Erwin Chargaff','NEW','12-097','12-082',NULL),
(280229,'biology','Erwin Chargaff observed that for double stranded DNA, the ratios between Adenine and Thymine and Guanine and Cytosine are','Constant and equals one.','Erwin Chargaff observed that for double stranded DNA, the ratios between Adenine and Thymine and Guanine and Cytosine are constant and equals one.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:97, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Variable','Only constant','Only Equals','Constant and equals one.','NEW','12-097','12-082',NULL),
(280230,'biology','The complementation of one DNA strands leads to the','Prediction of other','The complementation of the DNA strands helps us to predict the sequence of the other strand by taking one strand as the template(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Transcription of other','Dissociation of other','Prediction of other','Formation of other','NEW','12-097','12-082',NULL),
(280231,'biology','Which of the following strands acts as a template for the synthesis of new strand?','Parental DNA','Parental DNA acts as template. The strand is used to synthesize new strands(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Parental DNA','Daughter DNA','Alpha DNA','Beta DNA','NEW','12-097','12-082',NULL),
(280232,'biology','The newly synthesized strands are called as','Daughter DNA','When parental DNA acts as a template, the newly synthesized strand is called as daughter DNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Double helix DNA','Linear DNA','Daughter DNA','Circular DNA','NEW','12-097','12-082',NULL),
(280233,'biology','What kind of similarity is present in daughter DNA and parental DNA?','Genetically','The daughter DNA is genetically identical to the parental DNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Phenotypically','Genetically','Chemically','Biologically','NEW','12-097','12-082',NULL),
(280234,'biology','The two DNA chains have _________ polarity','Anti parallel','The two chains have anti-parallel polarity. It means, if one chain has the polarity 5\'-->3\', the other has 3\'-->5\'(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','fillup','An,ti p,ara,ll,el',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-097','12-082',NULL),
(280235,'biology','What kind of bonding is present between bases of two strands?','Hydrogen','The bases in two strands are paired through hydrogen bond (H-bonds) forming base pairs (bp)(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Ionic','Hydrogen','Covalent','London dispersion','NEW','12-097','12-082',NULL),
(280236,'biology','What is the pattern of coiling in two strands?','Right hand','The two chains are coiled in a right-handed fashion(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Left hand','Right hand','Anti clockwise','Parallel','NEW','12-097','12-082',NULL),
(280237,'biology','Who proposed central dogma in molecular biology?','Francis Crick','Francis Crick proposed the Central dogma in molecular biology(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:98, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Erwin Chargaff','James Watson','Francis Crick','Rosalind Franklin','NEW','12-098','12-083',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(280238,'biology','According to central dogma, the genetic information flows in which direction','DNA-->RNA-->Protein','Central dogma states that the genetic information flows from DNA-->RNA-->Protein(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:98, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','RNA --> DNA --> Protein','DNA-->Protein-->RNA','DNA-->RNA-->Protein','RNA-->DNA-->Protein','NEW','12-098','12-083',NULL),
(280239,'biology','What is the flow of information in viruses?','RNA to DNA','In some viruses the flow of information is in reverse direction, that is, from RNA to DNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','DNA to RNA','Proteins to RNA','RNA to Proteins','RNA to DNA','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280240,'biology','_________ always comes opposite to pyrimidine','Purine','A purine comes opposite to a pyrimidine(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','fillup','Pur,ine,ebo,lir',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280241,'biology','Match the following','Cytosine and Guanine-3 H bonds,Adenine and Thymine-2 H bonds,Helix pitch-3.4nm,Base pairs-10 each turn','Adenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Thymine from opposite strand and vice-versa. Similarly, Guanine is bonded with Cytosine with three H-bonds.The two chains are coiled in a right-handed fashion. The pitch of the helix is 3.4 nm (a nanometre is one billionth of a metre, that is 10⁻⁹ m) and there are roughly 10 bp in each turn(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','match','Cytosine and Guanine,3 H bonds','Adenine and Thymine,2 H bonds','Helix pitch,3.4nm','Base pairs,10 each turn','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280242,'biology','Match the following','Stacking of base pairs-Additional stability,Distance between base pairs-0.34nm,Central Dogma-Francis Crick','The distance between a bp in a helix is approximately 0.34 nm. The plane of one base pair stacks over the other in double helix. This, in addition to H-bonds, confers stability of the helical structure(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:98, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','match','Stacking of base pairs,Additional stability','Distance between base pairs,0.34nm','Central Dogma,Francis Crick',NULL,'NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280243,'biology','Identify the missing part of diagram and select the correct option (2802)','a)Replication  b)Transcription  c)Translation  d)Proteins','Francis Crick proposed the Central dogma in molecular biology, which states that the genetic information flows from DNAàRNAàProtein.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:98, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','a)Replication  b)Translation  c)Transcription  d)DNA formation','a)Replication  b)Transcription  c)Translation  d)Proteins','a)Translation  b)Transcription  c)RNA synthesis d)Replication','a)Replication  b)Transcription  c)Splicing  d)Translation','NEW','12-099','12-084','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/280243q.png'),
(280244,'biology','What kind of structural representation does a nucleosome has?','Beads on strings','Nucleosome are present as beads on the string(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Threads on string','Stacks on helix','Beads on strings','Beads on helix','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280245,'biology','At what stage of cell division does the nucleosome condense?','Metaphase','Nucleosome which forms beads-on-string structure in chromatin is packaged to form chromatin fibers that are further coiled and condensed at metaphase stage(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Interphase','Metaphase','Prophase','Telophase','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280246,'biology','Assertion: The genetic code is a must for translation. Reason: The genetic code determines the sequences of amino acids in proteins','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The genetic code that determines the sequences of amino acids in proteins, the process of protein synthesis (translation) and elementary basis of their regulation.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:95, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Introduction','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280247,'biology','Assertion: There are only 2 types of nitrogenous base in the genome- purines and pyramidines Reason: There are 5 types of nitrogenous bases- adenine, guanine, cytosine, thymine and uracil.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','There are two types of nitrogenous bases – Purines(Adenine and Guanine), and Pyrimidines (Cytosine, Uracil and Thymine )(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:96, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280248,'biology','Which among the following are nucleosides? \n\n(i) Uridine\n(ii) Uridosine \n(iii) Thymidine \n(iv) Deoxythyimidine','i and iv','A nitrogenous base is linked to the OH of 1\' C pentose sugar through a N-glycosidic linkage to form a nucleoside, such as adenosine or deoxyadenosine, guanosine or deoxyguanosine, cytidine or deoxycytidine and uridine or deoxythymidine. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:96, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i, ii,  iii and iv','i and iv','ii and iii','i, iii and iv','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280249,'biology','What are the 2 ends of polynucleotide chain?','OH at 3\' end and free at 5\' end','5\' -end of sugar, which is referred to as 5’-end of polynucleotide chain. Similarly, at the other end of the polymer the sugar has a free OH of 3\'C group which is referred to as 3\' -end of the polynucleotide chain. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:97, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','OH at 5\' end and OH at 3\' end','OH at 5\' end and free at 3\' end','OH at 3\' end and free at 5\' end','Free at 5\' end and Free at 3\' end','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280250,'biology','What made study of DNA so difficult? \n(i) It\'s acidic nature \n(ii) It\'s a very long polymer \n(iii) It is unstable and reactive','i and ii','DNA as an acidic substance present in nucleus was first identified by Friedrich Meischer in 1869. He named it as ‘Nuclein’. However, due to technical limitation in isolating such a long polymer intact, the elucidation of structure of DNA remained elusive for a very long period of time. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:97, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','ii and iii','i and ii','i and iii','all of the above','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280251,'biology','Watson and Cricks double helix proposition was based on which unique hallmark property?','Base pairing of DNA','One of the hallmarks of Watson and Crick\'s proposition was base pairing between the two strands of polynucleotide chains which confers a very unique property to the polynucleotide chains. This proposition was also based on the observation of Erwin Chargaff that for a double stranded DNA, the ratios between Adenine and Thymine and Guanine and Cytosine are constant and equals one. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:97, paragraph no:3 and 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Length of DNA','Stability of DNA','Base pairing of DNA','Reactivity of DNA','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280252,'biology','Assertion: DNA is made of two polynucleotide chains, where the backbone is constituted by sugar-phosphate, and the bases project inside. \nReason: The primary most salient feature of DNA is it\'s structure for stability','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','It is made of two polynucleotide chains, where the backbone is constituted by sugar-phosphate, and the bases project inside.Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280253,'biology','Why the distance between two polynucleotide chains in DNA remains almost constant?','Purines and pyrimidine pairing makes sure of it.','Adenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Thymine from opposite strand and vice-versa. Similarly, Guanine is bonded with Cytosine with three H-bonds. As a result, always a purine comes opposite to a pyrimidine. Their structures complementing ecah other generates approximately uniform distance between the two strands of the helix                                                                                               (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Purines and pyrimidine pairing makes sure of it.','Because of the charges inside the polynucleotide chains','Because of the force by histone proteins','Because of 3\'-5\' ends being opposite to each other.','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280254,'biology','What is the name of process of transfer of information from RNA to DNA called as?','Reverse transcription','In some viruses the flow of information is in reverse direction, that is, from RNA to DNA with help of reverse transcriptase by a process called reverse transcription. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Structure of Polynucleotide Chain','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Inverse transcription','Reverse transcription','Converse transcription','Opposite transcription','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280301,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect statement regarding " Cyclic Fructose".','Furanose is a five membered cyclic compound with five carbon atoms.','Fructose can also exist in two cyclic forms which are obtained by the addition of —OH at C5 to the carbonyl group. The ring, thus formed is a five membered ring and is named as furanose with analogy to the compound furan. Furan is a five membered cyclic compound with one oxygen and four carbon atoms.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Fructose',NULL,'3','mcq','Fructose forms a five membered ring called furanose.','Fructose is obtained by the addition of -OH at C5 to the carbonyl group.','Furanose shows analogy to the compound furan.','Furanose is a five membered cyclic compound with five carbon atoms.','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(280302,'chemistry','Which of the following is true for "Fructose" ?','Fructose is used as a sweetener in its pure form.','Fructose is an important ketohexose. It is obtained along with glucose by the hydrolysis of disaccharide, sucrose. It is a natural monosaccharide found in fruits, honey and vegetables. In its pure form it is used as a sweetener. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Fructose',NULL,'2','mcq','It is an important aldohexose.','Fructose is used as a sweetener in its pure form.','It is a natural disaccharide found in fruits and honey.','Fructose is obtained by the hydrolysis of galactose.','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(280303,'chemistry','Which carbon has ketone functional group in fructose?','C2','Fructose also has the molecular formula C₆H₁₂O₆ and on the basis of its reactions it was found to contain a ketone functional group at carbon number 2 and six carbons in straight chain as in the case of glucose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Fructose',NULL,'2','mcq','C3','C4','C5','C2','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(280304,'chemistry','Which of the following is true for furan?','Furan is a cyclic molecule with four carbon atoms and one oxygen atom.','Furan is a five membered cyclic compound with one oxygen and four carbon atoms.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Fructose',NULL,'2','mcq','Furan is a cyclic molecule with five carbon atoms and one oxygen atom.','Furan is a cyclic molecule with four carbon atoms and one oxygen atom.','Furan is an open chain molecule with five carbon atoms and one oxygen atom.','Furan is an open chain molecule with four oxygen atoms and one carbon atom.','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(280305,'chemistry','What is true about the structure shown in the figure? \n diagram(n2804)','It is a laevorotatory compound.','Fructose also has the molecular formula C₆H₁₂O₆ and on the basis of its reactions it was found to contain a ketone functional group at carbon number 2 and six carbons in straight chain as in the case of glucose. It belongs to D-series and is a laevorotatory compound.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Fructose',NULL,'3','mcq','It is a laevorotatory compound.','It belongs to L-series.','It exists in six membered cyclic form.','It has 3 cyclic structures.','NEW','12-416','12-286','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/280305q.png'),
(280306,'chemistry','What is the relation between the structures shown below? \n diagram(n2805)','Anomers','The two cyclic hemiacetal forms of glucose differ only in the configuration of the hydroxyl group at C1, called anomeric carbon (the aldehyde carbon before cyclisation).\n Such isomers, i.e., α-form and β-form, are called anomers.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Fructose',NULL,'3','mcq','Epimers','Anomers','Geometrical isomers','No isomerism','NEW','12-416','12-286','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/280306q.png'),
(280307,'biology','Histones organize to form eight molecule structure called as','histone octamer','Histones are organised to form a unit of eight molecules called histone octamer(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','fillup','hi,st,on,e o,ct,am,er,eb',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280308,'biology','The wrapping of DNA around histone octamer forms','Nucleosome','The negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form a structure called nucleosome(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Nucleotide','Nucleoside','Nucleosome','Nucleolus','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280309,'biology','How many DNA base pairs are present in nucleosome?','200bp','A typical nucleosome contains 200 bp of DNA helix(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','250bp','200bp','270bp','210bp','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280310,'biology','What are thread like stained(coloured) bodies in nucleus?','Chromatin','Nucleosomes constitute the repeating unit of a structure in nucleus called chromatin. These are thread like stained (coloured) bodies seen in nucleus(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Chromatin','Chromosomes','Centrioles','Chromatid','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280311,'biology','Chromatin further condense to form','Chromosomes','Chromatin is packaged to form chromatin fibers that are further coiled and condensed at metaphase stage of cell division to form chromosomes(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Chromatin fibers','Sister chromatids','Chromosomes','Centrioles','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280312,'biology','Which proteins are required at higher levels of chromatin packaging?','NHC','The packaging of chromatin at higher level requires additional set of proteins that collectively are referred to as Non-histone Chromosomal (NHC) proteins(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','RHC','NHC','THC','BRC','NEW','12-100','12-085',NULL),
(280313,'biology','What are the characteristics of euchromatin? \ni)Loosely packed  \nii)Transcriptionally active  \niii)Stains light','i, ii and iii','In a typical nucleus, some region of chromatin are loosely packed (and stains light) and are referred to as euchromatin. Euchromatin is said to be transcriptionally active chromatin(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:100, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and iii','i and ii','i, ii and iii','ii and iii','NEW','12-100','12-085',NULL),
(280314,'biology','Which of the following structures are inactive, dark and closely packed?','Heterochromatin','The chromatin that is more densely packed and stains dark are called as Heterochromatin. These are transcriptionally inactive regions(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:100, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Euchromatin','Chromatin','Heterochromatin','Chromosomes','NEW','12-100','12-085',NULL),
(280315,'biology','What happens to chromatin fibers at metaphase stage of cell division?','Condensation','At metaphase chromatin fibers condense to form chromosomes(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Condensation','Deletion','Insertion','Packaging','NEW','12-100','12-085',NULL),
(280316,'biology','Which structure is formed as a result of chromatin condensation?','Chromosomes','Chromatin fibers condense to form chromosomes at metaphase(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Chromatid','Chromosomes','Heterochromatin','Nucleosomes','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280317,'biology','Assertion:The length of DNA comes out to be approximately 2.2 metres. A length that is far greater than the dimension of a typical nucleus (approximately 10⁻⁶m). \nReason:The positively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form a structure called nucleosome. A typical nucleosome contains 200 bp of DNA helix\n.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','The length of DNA comes out to be approximately 2.2 metres. A length that is far greater than the dimension of a typical nucleus (approximately 10–6 m).The negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form a structure called nucleosome. A typical nucleosome contains 200 bp of DNA helix.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:2 and 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280318,'biology','What activity gives euchromatin a upper hand than heterochromatin?','Transcription','Euchromatin is said to be transcriptionally active chromatin, whereas heterochromatin is inactive. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:100, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Transcription','Translation','Replication','Transformation','NEW','12-100','12-085',NULL),
(280319,'biology','How many loops does the DNA wrap around 1 nucleosome?','1.65','The DNA in nucleoid is organised in large loops held by proteins. The negatively charged DNA is wrapped 1.65 times around the positively charged histone octamer to form a structure called nucleosome. A typical nucleosome contains 200 bp of DNA helix (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','1.25','1.45','1.65','2.25','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280320,'biology','Assertion: The DNA which is negatively charged combines with histones which are positively charged. Reason: Histones are proteins carrying both positive and negative charged amino acids.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Histones are rich in the basic amino acid residues lysine and arginine.Both the amino acid residues carry positive charges in their side chains. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Packaging of DNA Helix','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(280401,'chemistry','A linkage between two monosaccharide units through oxygen atom is called a _____ linkage.','Glycosidic linkage','A linkage between two monosaccharide units through oxygen atom is called a glycosidic linkage.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','Peptide linkage','Glycosidic linkage','Phosphodiester bond','Hydrogen bond','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(280402,'chemistry','In disaccharides, if the reducing groups of monosaccharides i.e., aldehydic or ketonic groups are bonded, the disaccharides are:','non-reducing sugars','In disaccharides, if the reducing groups of monosaccharides i.e., aldehydic or ketonic groups are bonded, these are non-reducing sugars.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','reducing sugars','non-reducing sugars','oxidizing sugars','non-oxidizing sugars','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(280403,'chemistry','Which of the following is a non-reducing sugar?','Sucrose','Since the reducing groups of glucose and fructose are involved in glycosidic bond formation, sucrose is a non-reducing sugar.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'2','mcq','Fructose','Glucose','Sucrose','Maltose','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(280404,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a reducing sugar?','Sucrose','Sugars in which these functional groups are free, are called reducing sugars, for example, maltose and lactose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','Fructose','Maltose','Lactose','Sucrose','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(280405,'chemistry','Which of the following linkage is present in sucrose?','C1 of glucose links with C2 of fructose.','These two monosaccharides are held together by a glycosidic linkage between C1 of a-D-glucose and C2 of b-D-fructose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'2','mcq','C1 of glucose links with C2 of fructose.','C1 of fructose links with C2 of glucose.','C1 of glucose links with C1 of fructose.','C1 of glucose links with C4 of fructose.','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(280406,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n Assertion: Sucrose is non-reducing sugar.\n Reason: The reducing groups of glucose and fructose are involved in glycosidic bond formation.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Since the reducing groups of glucose and fructose are involved in glycosidic bond formation, sucrose is a non reducing sugar. Hence, Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(280407,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n Assertion: Sucrose is dextrorotatory but after hydrolysis the mixture becomes laevorotatory.\n Reason: Laevo Rotation of fructose is more than dextrorotation of glucose.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Sucrose is dextrorotatory but after hydrolysis gives dextrorotatory glucose and laevorotatory fructose. Since the laevo rotation of fructose (–92.4°) is more than dextrorotation of glucose (+ 52.5°), the mixture is laevorotatory.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(280408,'chemistry','Which of the following is also known as invert sugar?','Sucrose','Sucrose is dextrorotatory but after hydrolysis gives dextrorotatory glucose and laevorotatory fructose. Since the laevorotation of fructose (–92.4°) is more than dextrorotation of glucose (+ 52.5°), the mixture is laevorotatory. Thus, hydrolysis of sucrose brings about a change in the sign of rotation, from dextro (+) to laevo (–) and the product is named as invert sugar.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','Sucrose','Fructose','Maltose','Mannose','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(280409,'chemistry','Which of the following is true about maltose?','Maltose is composed of two glucose units in which C1 of one glucose is linked to C4 of another glucose unit.','Maltose, another disaccharide, is composed of two a-D-glucose units in which C1 of one glucose is linked to C4 of another glucose unit. The free aldehyde group can be produced at C1 of second glucose in solution and it shows reducing properties so it is a reducing sugar. Hydrolysis of maltose gives two molecules of d-glucose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'2','mcq','Maltose is non-reducing sugar.','Maltose is composed of two glucose units in which C1 of one glucose is linked to C4 of another glucose unit.','Hydrolysis of maltose produces three molecules of fructose.','Maltose is a polysaccharide linked by glycosidic bond.','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(280410,'chemistry','Which of the following is commonly known as milk sugar?','Lactose','Lactose is more commonly known as milk sugar since this disaccharide is found in milk.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 418; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','Galactose','Lactose','Maltose','Mannose','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(280411,'chemistry','Lactose is composed of :','β glucose and β galactose','Lactose is composed of β-D-galactose and β-D-glucose. The linkage is between C1 of galactose and C4 of glucose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 418; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Disaccharides',NULL,'2','mcq','β glucose and α galactose','β glucose and β galactose','α glucose and β galactose','β glucose and β fructose','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(280501,'chemistry','Which of the following are most encountered carbohydrates in nature?','Polysaccharides','Polysaccharides contain a large number of monosaccharide units joined together by glycosidic linkages. These are the most commonly encountered carbohydrates in nature. They mainly act as the food storage or structural materials.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 418; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','Polysaccharides','Monosaccharides','Disaccharides','Trisaccharides','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(280502,'chemistry','Which of the following is main storage polysaccharide of plants?','Starch','Starch is the main storage polysaccharide of plants.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 418; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','Sucrose','Cellulose','Starch','Glycogen','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(280503,'chemistry','Which of the following is water soluble component of starch?','Amylose','Starch is a polymer of α-glucose and consists of two components— Amylose and Amylopectin. Amylose is water soluble component which constitutes about 15-20% of starch.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 418; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'2','mcq','Amylose','Amylopectin','Glycogen','Cellulose','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(280504,'chemistry','Which of the following does not contain starch?','Milk','High content of starch is found in cereals, roots, tubers and some vegetables.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 418; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'1','mcq','Cereals','Vegetables','Tuber','Milk','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(280505,'chemistry','Which component makes up the majority of the starch?','Amylopectin','Amylopectin is insoluble in water and constitutes about 80- 85% of starch. Amylose is water soluble component which constitutes about 15-20% of starch.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 418; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'2','mcq','Amylose','Amylopectin','Glycogen','Cellulose','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(280506,'chemistry','Which among the following options is true about Amylose?','It is formed by glucose units held together by C1-C4 glycosidic linkage.','Amylose is water soluble component which constitutes about 15-20% of starch. Chemically amylose is a long unbranched chain with 200-1000 α-D-(+)-glucose units held together by C1– C4 glycosidic linkage.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 418; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'2','mcq','It is a branched polymer chain with 200-1000 units of glucose.','It is an unbranched polymer chain with 200-1000 units of fructose.','It is formed by glucose units held together by C1-C4 glycosidic linkage.','Amylose is insoluble in water.','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(280507,'chemistry','Amylopectin\'s branch structure includes a glycosidic linkage between:','C1-C6 of α-D-glucose units','Amylopectin is a branched chain polymer of a-D-glucose units in which chain is formed by C1–C4 glycosidic linkage whereas branching occurs by C1–C6 glycosidic linkage.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 418; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Polysaccharides',NULL,'2','mcq','C1-C6 of α-D-glucose units','C4-C6 of α-D-glucose units','C1-C4 of α-D-glucose units','C1-C2 of α-D-glucose units','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(280601,'chemistry','In the Ayurvedic system of medicine, \'Vaids\' employed honey as a:','source of energy','Honey has been used for a long time as an instant source of energy by ‘Vaids’ in ayurvedic system of medicine.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 419; Paragraph no. 14.1.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Importance of Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','source of sugar','source of energy','source of antiseptic','source of ointment','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(280602,'chemistry','Which carbohydrate is acts as storage in animals?','Glycogen','Carbohydrates are used as storage molecules as starch in plants and glycogen in animals.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 419; Paragraph no. 14.1.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Importance of Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','Cellulose','Glycogen','Starch','Insulin','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(280603,'chemistry','Which among the following is not an application carbohydrates?','Nylon','Carbohydrates are a vital source of immediate energy in our bodies.\n In Ayurvedic medicine, honey has been employed as an instant source of energy.\n Carbohydrates are employed in plants as store starch, while glycogen carbohydrates are used in the textile industry.\n As a synthetic polymer, the right answer is Nylon.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 419; Paragraph no. 14.1.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Importance of Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','Textile industry','breweries','Paper','Nylon','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(280604,'chemistry','Which among the following is not the use of cellulose?','Polyester','We build furniture, etc. from cellulose in the form of wood and clothe ourselves with cellulose in the form of cotton fibre.\n They provide raw materials for many important industries like textiles, paper, lacquers and breweries.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Paragraph no. 14.1.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Importance of Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','Cotton fibres','Furniture','Breweries','Polyester','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280605,'chemistry','Which of the following sugars are present in nucleic acids?','Aldopentoses','Two aldopentoses viz. D-ribose and 2-deoxy-D-ribose are present in nucleic acids.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Paragraph no. 14.1.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Importance of Carbohydrates',NULL,'2','mcq','Aldohexoses','Ketohexoses','Aldopentoses','Ketopentoses','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280606,'chemistry','Carbohydrates are found in biosystem in combination with :','Proteins and lipids.','Carbohydrates are found in biosystem in combination with many proteins and lipids.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Paragraph no. 14.1.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Importance of Carbohydrates',NULL,'1','mcq','Proteins and lipids.','Fats and salts.','Salts and amino acids.','Proteins and acids.','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(40425,'biology','Match the following with the correct side of mouth and anus in echinodermata.','mouth-lower (ventral) side,anus-upper (dorsal) side','In Echinoderms Digestive system is complete with mouth on the lower (ventral) side and anus on the upper (dorsal) side. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 54; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','match','mouth,lower (ventral) side','anus,upper (dorsal) side',NULL,NULL,'NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(280607,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n Assertion: There is absence of aldehyde group in pentaacetate of glucose.\n Reason: Pentaacetate form exist in cyclical structure.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Pentaacetate of glucose does not react to form oxime since it doesn\'t contain a free –OH group at C-1 position.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Intext Question 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Importance of Carbohydrates',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280608,'chemistry','What are products obtained from hydrolysis of lactose?','Galactose and Glucose','Galactose and Glucose are obtained from hydrolysis of lactose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Intext Question 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Importance of Carbohydrates',NULL,'2','mcq','Glucose and Maltose','Galactose only','Maltose only','Galactose and Glucose','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280609,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n Assertion: Glucose is soluble in water but cyclohexane is not.\n Reason: Glucose can form hydrogen bonds with water.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Glucose is soluble in water since it forms hydrogen bonds with water but cyclohexane does not.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Intext Question 14.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Importance of Carbohydrates',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280610,'biology','Identify the missing part of the diagram (2803)','a)Infection  b)Blending  c)Centrifugation','Figure 6.5 (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:102)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Genetic Material is DNA','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','a)Infection  b)Blending  c)Centrifugation','a)Blending  b)Centrifugation  c)Infection','a)Insertion  b)Blending  c)Replication','a)Insertion  b)Blending  c)Centrifugation','NEW','12-102','12-087','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/280610q.png'),
(280611,'biology','Assertion: Polymorphism in DNA sequence is the basis of evolution and speciation. Reason: Polymorphism arises due to mutations which keep on accumulating generation after generation.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','These mutations keep on accumulating generation after generation, and form one of the basis of variability/polymorphism. There are a variety of different types of polymorphisms ranging from single nucleotide change to very large scale changes. For evolution and speciation, such polymorphisms play a very important role.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Genetic Material is DNA','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-122',NULL,NULL),
(280612,'biology','Correct sequence in Hershey and Chase\'s experiment is','Bacteriophage attaches- genetic material enters bacteria- it incorporates into bacterial genetic material- bacteria treats it as its own and replicates','The bacteriophage attaches to the bacteria and its genetic material then enters the bacterial cell. The bacterial cell treats the viral genetic material as if it was its own and subsequently manufactures more virus particles. Hershey and Chase worked to discover whether it was protein or DNA from the viruses that entered the bacteria. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:101, paragraph no:6)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Genetic Material is DNA','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Bacteriophage attaches-bacteriophage enters bacteria- bacteriophage enters nucleus- its genetic material incorporates into bacterial genetic material- bacteriophage leaks out- bacteria replicates','Bacteriophage attaches-bacteriophage enters bacteria resides in cytoplasm- its genetic material incorporates into bacterial genetic material- bacteriophage leaks out- bacteria replicates','Bacteriophage attaches- genetic material enters nucleus- without incorporating into bacterial genetic material- it replicates as separate entity','Bacteriophage attaches- genetic material enters bacteria- it incorporates into bacterial genetic material- bacteria treats it as its own and replicates','NEW','12-101','12-086',NULL),
(280701,'chemistry','Which of the following are the most abundant biomolecules of the living system?','Proteins','Proteins are the most abundant biomolecules of the living system.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Paragraph no. 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Carbohydrates','Lipids','Proteins','Vitamins','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280702,'chemistry','Which of them is not a chief sources of protein?','Celery','Chief sources of proteins are milk, cheese, pulses, peanuts, fish, meat, etc. They occur in every part of the body and form the fundamental basis of structure and functions of life.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Paragraph no. 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Milk','Cheese','Celery','Meat','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280703,'chemistry','Mark the false statement regarding proteins?','Proteins are polymers of nucleotides.','a. Proteins are also required for growth and maintenance of body.\n b. The word protein is derived from the Greek word, “proteios” which means primary or of prime importance.\n c. All proteins are polymers of a-amino acids.\n d. Chief sources of proteins are milk, cheese, pulses, peanuts, fish, meat, etc.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Paragraph no. 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Proteins are also required for growth and maintenance of body.','The word protein is derived from Greek word, "proteios" which means energy.','Proteins are polymers of nucleotides.','Chief sources of proteins are milk, cheese, peanuts, etc.','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280704,'chemistry','Which of the following amino acids are obtained on hydrolysis of proteins?','α amino acids','Only α-amino acids are obtained on hydrolysis of proteins.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Paragraph no. 14.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','α amino acids','β amino acids','γ amino acids','Both α and β','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280705,'chemistry','____ is so named since it has sweet taste.','Glycine','Glycine is so named since it has sweet taste (in Greek glykos means sweet.)\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Paragraph no. 14.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Glutamine','Glycine','Glycerin','Tyrosine','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280706,'chemistry','____ amino acid was first obtained from cheese.','Tyrosine','Tyrosine was first obtained from cheese (in Greek, tyros means cheese.)\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Paragraph no. 14.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Tyrosine','Alanine','Leucine','Glycine','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280707,'chemistry','What is commonly used to represent Amino acids?','Three letter symbol','Amino acids are generally represented by a three letter symbol, sometimes one letter symbol is also used. For example, Glycine is commonly represented by three letter symbol \'Gly\' or by one letter code \'G\' .\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Paragraph no. 14.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Two letter code','Three letter symbol','Chemical formula','Chemical structure','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280708,'chemistry','What is the one letter code for Arginine?','R','Arginine is commonly represented by three letter symbol \'Arg\' or by one letter code \'R\' .\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','R','A','G','F','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280709,'chemistry','Which of the following contains two chiral carbons?','Isoleucine','Isoleucine is one of the two proteinogenic amino acids which contain a second chiral carbon atom beside the amino group-bearing α-C.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'3','mcq','Isoleucine','Glycine','Alanine','Leucine','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280710,'chemistry','Which of the following contains amino group in the side chain?','Lysine','Lysine contains a positively charged amino on its side-chain that is sometimes involved in forming hydrogen bonds with negatively charged non-protein atoms (e.g. anions or carboxylate groups).\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Isoleucine','Lysine','alanine','Leucine','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280711,'chemistry','What is the position of hydroxyl group in tyrosine phenyl ring?','Para','The positional description para, abbreviated p, means that the hydroxyl group and side chain on the phenyl ring are across from each other\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Ortho','Meta','Para','Phenol','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280712,'chemistry','What is the three letter code for Asparagine?','Asn','The three letter code for Asparagine is Asn.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Asn','Asp','Asa','Asg','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280713,'chemistry','Which of the following has five membered aromatic side chain?','His','Histidine has five membered aromatic side chain.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'3','mcq','His','Pro','Glu','Arg','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280714,'chemistry','Which of the following amino acids contain thiol group in the side chain?','Cys','Cysteine (Cys) has a thiol group that form a disulfide bond with other Cysteine through oxidation.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Ser','Cys','Met','Lys','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280715,'chemistry','Which of the following amino acid has secondary amino group present?','Proline','Proline contains a secondary amine group, called an imine, instead of a primary amine group.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Serine','Methionine','Lysine','Proline','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280716,'chemistry','Which single letter code represents the amide containing amino acid?','Q','Glutamine (Q) is an α-amino acid that has a side chain is similar to that of glutamic acid, except the carboxylic acid group is replaced by an amide.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Q','R','A','K','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280717,'chemistry','Choose the essential amino acid among the following.','Trp','Tryptophan(Trp) is an essential amino acid while Proline (Pro), Asparagine (Asn) and Tyrosine (Tyr) are non- essential amino acids.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 420; Table 14.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Trp','Pro','Tyr','Asn','NEW','12-420','12-290',NULL),
(280801,'chemistry','Proteins are the polymers of α-amino acids and they are connected to each other by :','Peptide bond','Proteins are the polymers of α-amino acids and they are connected to each other by peptide bond or peptide linkage.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Glycosidic bond','Peptide bond','Phosphodiester bond','Hydrogen bond','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(280802,'chemistry','Which of the following molecule is eliminated when peptide bond is formed?','Water','The reaction between two molecules of similar or different amino acids, proceeds through the combination of the amino group of one molecule with the carboxyl group of the other.\n This results in the elimination of a water molecule and formation of a peptide bond.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','Water','Ammonia','Hydrochloric Acid','Hydrogen','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(280803,'chemistry','What is the name of the peptide formed when glycine combines with amino group of alanine?','Glycylalanine','When carboxyl group of glycine combines with the amino group of alanine, we get a dipeptide, glycylalanine.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'2','mcq','Glycylalanine','Allylglycine','Glyco-alanine','Ala-gly','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(280804,'chemistry','When the number of amino acids is more than _____ then the products are called polypeptide.','10','When the number of such amino acids is more than ten, then the products are called polypeptides.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','10','8','4','3','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(280805,'chemistry','A protein is a :','A polypeptide with more than 100 amino acid residues and a molecular mass more than 10,000u.','A polypeptide with more than hundred amino acid residues, having molecular mass higher than 10,000u is called a protein.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 422; Paragraph 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Structure of proteins',NULL,'1','mcq','A polypeptide with more than 10,000 amino acid residues and a molecular mass more than 10,000u.','A polypeptide with more than 10 amino acid residues and a molecular mass more than 100u.','A polypeptide with more than 100 amino acid residues and a molecular mass more than 10,000u.','A polypeptide with more than 100 amino acid residues and a molecular mass more than 100u.','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(40426,'biology','Spiny body with radial symmetry in adult and bilateral in larva belong to -','Echinodermata','The adult echinoderms are radially symmetrical but larvae are bilaterally symmetrical. They are triploblastic and coelomate animals.(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 11; Page 54; Paragraph No. 2)','Diversity In Living World >> Animal Kingdom >> Arthropoda,Mollusca,Echinodermata','Animal Kingdom','2','mcq','Echinodermata','Arthropoda','Mollusca','Annelida','NEW','11-054','11-045',NULL),
(280901,'chemistry','Mark the incorrect starement regarding " Enzymes".','Almost all the enzymes are fibrous proteins.','The coordination of different chemical reactions in living organisms makes life possible. This occurs with the help of certain biocatalysts called enzymes. Almost all the enzymes are globular proteins. Enzymes are very specific for a particular reaction and for a particular substrate. They are generally named after the compound or class of compounds upon which they work. For example, the enzyme that catalyses hydrolysis of maltose into glucose is named as maltase. Sometimes enzymes are also named after the reaction, where they are used. The ending of the name of an enzyme is -ase.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 425; Paragraph 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'1','mcq','Almost all the enzymes are fibrous proteins.','Enzymes are very specific for a given reaction and substrate.','Enzymes are usually named after the compound or class of compounds or the reaction on which they work.','The reactions that take place in the body are supported by biocatalysts known as enzymes.','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
(280902,'chemistry','Oxidoreductase is an example of an enzyme named after :','named after the reaction in which they are used.','Sometimes enzymes are also named after the reaction, where they are used. For example, the enzymes which catalyse the oxidation of one substrate with simultaneous reduction of another substrate are named as oxidoreductase enzymes. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 425; Paragraph 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'1','mcq','the compound upon which they work.','the class of compounds upon which they work.','named after the reaction in which they are used.','named after the substrate upon which they work.','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
(280903,'chemistry','On which of the following compound does maltase act?','Maltose','The enzyme that catalyses hydrolysis of maltose into glucose is named as maltase.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 425; Paragraph 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'1','mcq','Maltose','Glucose','Mannose','Mannitol','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
(280904,'chemistry','Which of the following is incorrect for enzymes?','Enzymes increase the activation energy of the reaction.','a. Similar to the action of chemical catalysts, enzymes are said to reduce the magnitude of activation energy. \n b. Enzymes are needed only in small quantities for the progress of a reaction. \n c. For example, activation energy for acid hydrolysis of sucrose is 6.22 kJ mol⁻¹, while the activation energy is only 2.15 kJ mol⁻¹ when hydrolysed by the enzyme, sucrase.\n d. Enzymes are very specific for a particular reaction and for a particular substrate. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 425; Paragraph 14.3.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','Enzymes increase the activation energy of the reaction.','Enzymes are needed in only small quantities for progress of reaction.','Sucrose is hydrolysed by enzyme sucrase.','Enzymes are very specific for a given reaction and substrate.','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
(280905,'chemistry','Which of the following is not true for vitamins?','Most of the vitamins are formed in our body.','a. Most of the vitamins cannot be synthesised in our body but plants can synthesise almost all of them, so they are considered as essential food factors. \n b. The bacteria of the gut can produce some of the vitamins required by humans.\n c. All the vitamins are generally available in our diet.\n d. Although vitamins are only required in trace amounts in our diet, their deficiency causes specific diseases.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 425; Paragraph 14.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','Most of the vitamins are formed in our body.','Bacteria of gut can produce some of the vitamins.','All the vitamins are generally available in our diet.','Deficiency of vitamins causes specific diseases.','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
(280906,'chemistry','Which of the following are generally regarded as organic compounds required in the diet in small amounts to perform specific biological functions for normal maintenance of optimum growth?','Vitamins','It has been observed that certain organic compounds are required in small amounts in our diet but their deficiency causes specific diseases.\n These compounds are called vitamins.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 425; Paragraph 14.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'1','mcq','Vitamins','Nucleic Acids','Fat','Carbohydrates','NEW','12-425','12-295',NULL),
(280907,'chemistry','Choose the correct option(s) from the following statements that explain " Vitamins":\n a. Vitamins are inorganic compounds.\n b. Vitamins are classified basis of structure. \n c. Vitamins are designated by alphabets A, B, C, D, etc.\n d. Some vitamins have sub-groups, such as B₁, B₂, B₆, B₁₂, and etc.','Both c and d','a. Vitamins are generally regarded as organic compounds required in the diet in small amounts to perform specific biological functions.\n b. Different vitamins belong to various chemical classes and it is difficult to define them on the basis of structure. \n c. Vitamins are designated by alphabets A, B, C, D, etc.\n d. Some vitamins have sub-groups, such as B₁, B₂, B₆, B₁₂, and etc.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Paragraph 14.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and c','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280908,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n Assertion: The term “Vitamine” was coined from the word vital + amine since the earlier identified compounds had amino groups.\n Reason: Most the Vitamins did not contain amino groups.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The term “Vitamine” was coined from the word vital + amine since the earlier identified compounds had amino groups.\n Later work showed that most of them did not contain amino groups, so the letter ‘e’ was dropped and the term vitamin is used these days.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Paragraph 14.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280909,'chemistry','Choose the vitamin that is not soluble in fat.','Vitamin C','Vitamins which are soluble in fat and oils but insoluble in water are vitamins A, D, E and K.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Paragraph 14.4.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','Vitamin A','Vitamin D','Vitamin C','Vitamin E','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(280910,'chemistry','Which of the following is stored in liver?','Vitamin A','Vitamins which are soluble in fat and oils but insoluble in water are the fat soluble vitamins. These are vitamins A, D, E and K. They are stored in liver and adipose (fat storing) tissues.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Paragraph 14.4.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Enzymes and Vitamins',NULL,'2','mcq','Vitamin A','Vitamin C','Folic Acid','Vitamin B₃','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(281001,'chemistry','Which part in nucleus is responsible for heredity?','Chromosomes','The particles in nucleus of the cell, responsible for heredity, are called chromosomes which are made up of proteins and another type of biomolecules called nucleic acids.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 427; Paragraph 14.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'1','mcq','Chromosomes','Nucleoplasm','Nuclear membrane','Ribosome','NEW','12-427','12-297',NULL),
(281002,'chemistry','Nucleic acids are polymers of ______.','Nucleotides','Since nucleic acids are long chain polymers of nucleotides, so they are also called polynucleotides.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 427; Paragraph 14.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'1','mcq','Nucleosides','Nucleotides','Proteins','DNA','NEW','12-427','12-297',NULL),
(281003,'chemistry','Who proposed the that DNA molecule takes shape of double helix?','Watson and Crick','James Dewey Watson is best known for his discovery of the structure of DNA for which he shared with Francis Crick and Maurice Wilkins. They proposed that DNA molecule takes the shape of a double helix, an elegantly simple structure that resembles a gently twisted ladder.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 427; Paragraph 14.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'1','mcq','Watson and Crick','Watson and Pauling','Pauling and Crick','Feynman and Richard','NEW','12-427','12-297',NULL),
(281004,'chemistry','Which of the following is not found upon complete hydrolysis of DNA?','Ribose sugar','Complete hydrolysis of DNA (or RNA) yields a pentose sugar, phosphoric acid and nitrogen containing heterocyclic compounds (called bases).\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 427; Paragraph 14.5.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Ribose sugar','Phosphoric acid','Adenine','Cytosine','NEW','12-427','12-297',NULL),
(281005,'chemistry','What is the name for the structure shown? \n diagram(n2807)','β-D-deoxyribose','Complete hydrolysis of DNA yields a pentose sugar, phosphoric acid and nitrogen containing heterocyclic compounds. The sugar moiety is β-D-2-deoxyribose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 427; Paragraph 14.5.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'3','mcq','β-L-deoxyribose','β-D-deoxyribose','β-L-ribose','α-D-deoxyribose','NEW','12-427','12-297','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/281005q.png'),
(281006,'chemistry','Which of the following true for structures of nitrogenous bases?','Both guanine and adenine have two rings.','Adenine and guanine are purines with two connected ring structures. Uracil has 4 nitrogen atoms in its ring while 3 Cytosine has nitrogen atom in its ring.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 428; Paragraph 14.5.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Both guanine and adenine have two rings.','Methylated cytosine is called as uracil.','Uracil has 3 nitrogen atom in its ring.','Cytosine has no nitrogen atom in its ring.','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281007,'chemistry','Identify the nitrogenous base shown below. \n diagram(n2808)','Cytosine','The structure represents cytosine because of the characteristic amine group on carbon 4, instead of the carbonyl group that is present on carbon 4 of both thymine and uracil.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 428; Paragraph 14.5.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'3','mcq','Cytosine','Adenine','Uracil','Thymine','NEW','12-428','12-298','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/281007q.png'),
(281008,'chemistry','A nucleoside is formed:','when a base attaches to the 1′ position of the sugar.','A unit formed by the attachment of a base to 1′ position of sugar is known as nucleoside.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 428; Paragraph 14.5.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'1','mcq','when phosphoric acid attaches to the 5′ position of the sugar.','when a base attaches to the 1′ position of the sugar.','when a phosphodiester bond forms between the sugar\'s 5′ and 3′ carbon atoms.','when a carbonyl group attaches to the 1′ position of the sugar.','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281009,'chemistry','Which of the following nitrogenous base is not present in RNA?','Thymine','DNA contains four bases viz. adenine (A), guanine (G), cytosine (C) and thymine (T). RNA also contains four bases, the first three bases are same as in DNA but the fourth one is uracil (U).\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 428; Paragraph 14.5.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Thymine','Uracil','Adenine','Guanine','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281010,'chemistry','In what position is phosphoric acid joined to a nucleoside to form a nucleotide?','5\'','In nucleosides, the sugar carbons are numbered as 1\', 2\', 3\', etc. in order to distinguish these from the bases. When nucleoside is linked to phosphoric acid at 5\'-position of sugar moiety, we get a nucleotide.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 428; Paragraph 14.5.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','1\'','2\'','5\'','3\'','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281011,'chemistry','What is true about phosphodiester bond?','It exists between carbons of pentose sugar.','Nucleotides are joined together by phosphodiester linkage between 5\' and 3\' carbon atoms of the pentose sugar.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 429; Paragraph 14.5.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','It exists between carbons of pentose sugar.','It exists between carbons of nitrogenous bases.','It exists between carbons of pentose sugar and nitrogenous bases.','It exists between carbon of pentose sugar and nitrogen of nitrogenous bases.','NEW','12-429','12-299',NULL),
(281012,'chemistry','Sequence of nucleotides is the:','Primary structure','Information regarding the sequence of nucleotides in the chain of a nucleic acid is called its primary structure.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 429; Paragraph 14.5.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'1','mcq','Primary structure','Secondary structure','Tertiary structure','Quaternary structure','NEW','12-429','12-299',NULL),
(281013,'chemistry','Which of the following statement is incorrect for DNA?','Adenine forms hydrogen bonds with uracil.','James Watson and Francis Crick gave a double strand helix structure for DNA. \n a. Two nucleic acid chains are wound about each other and held together by hydrogen bonds between pairs of bases. \n b. The two strands are complementary to each other because the hydrogen bonds are formed between specific pairs of bases. \n c. Adenine forms hydrogen bonds with thymine.\n d. Cytosine forms hydrogen bonds with guanine.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 429; Paragraph 14.5.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Two nucleic acid chains are wound about each other and held together by hydrogen bonds between pairs of bases.','The two strands are complementary to each other.','Adenine forms hydrogen bonds with uracil.','Cytosine forms hydrogen bonds with guanine.','NEW','12-429','12-299',NULL),
(40501,'chemistry','Lewis- Langmuir theory explain the formation of:','Covalent bond','Langmuir (1919) refined the Lewis postulations by abandoning the idea of the stationary cubical arrangement of the octet, and by introducing the term covalent bond. \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'1','mcq','Covalent bond','Metallic bond','Hydrogen bond','Vander Waal\'s bonds','NEW','11-102','11-102',NULL),
(281014,'chemistry','Which of the following true for RNA?','In secondary structure of RNA single stranded helix is present which sometimes folds back on itself.','a. In secondary structure of RNA single stranded helix is present which sometimes folds back on itself. RNA molecules are of three types and they perform different functions. They are named as messenger RNA (m-RNA), ribosomal RNA (r-RNA) and transfer RNA (t-RNA).\n b. RNA molecules are of three types.\n c. Different types of RNA perform different functions. \n d. They are named as messenger RNA (m-RNA), ribosomal RNA (r-RNA) and transfer RNA (t-RNA).\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 429; Paragraph 14.5.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','In secondary structure of RNA single stranded helix is present which sometimes folds back on itself.','RNA molecules of 2 types only.','Different types of RNA perform the same function.','tRNA is transport RNA found in plants only.','NEW','12-429','12-299',NULL),
(281015,'chemistry','What was the role of Har Gobind Khorana in science?','Cracked the genetic code.','It was at Cambridge, U.K. that he got interested in both proteins and nucleic acids. Dr Khorana shared the Nobel Prize for Medicine and Physiology in 1968 with Marshall Nirenberg and Robert Holley for cracking the genetic code.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 430)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'1','mcq','Cracked the genetic code.','Made the Ramachandran plot.','Isolated DNA for first time.','Discovered and named RNA.','NEW','12-430','12-300',NULL),
(281016,'chemistry','Choose the correct statement.','DNA is same for every cell and cannot be altered.','It is known that every individual has unique fingerprints. These occur at the tips of the fingers and have been used for identification for a long time but these can be altered by surgery. A sequence of bases on DNA is also unique for a person and information regarding this is called DNA fingerprinting. It is same for every cell and cannot be altered by any known treatment. \n DNA fingerprinting is now used:\n (i) In forensic laboratories for identification of criminals.\n (ii) To determine paternity of an individual.\n (iii) To identify the dead bodies in any accident by comparing the DNA of parents or children.\n (iv) To identify racial groups to rewrite biological evolution.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 430)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Fingerprints cannot be altered by surgery.','DNA is same for every cell and cannot be altered.','DNA fingerprinting cannot be used to determine paternity of individual.','A sequence of bases on DNA is the same for all the organisms of a species.','NEW','12-430','12-300',NULL),
(281017,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement.','Proteins are synthesised by various DNA molecules in the cell but the message for the synthesis of a particular protein is present in RNA.','a. DNA is the chemical basis of heredity and may be regarded as the reserve of genetic information. DNA is exclusively responsible for maintaining the identity of different species of organisms over millions of years. \n b. A DNA molecule is capable of self duplication during cell division and identical DNA strands are transferred to daughter cells. \n c. Actually, the proteins are synthesised by various RNA molecules in the cell but the message for the synthesis of a particular protein is present in DNA.\n d. Another important function of nucleic acids is the protein synthesis in the cell. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 430; Paragraph 3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','DNA is exclusively responsible for maintaining the identity of different species of organisms over millions of years.','DNA molecule is capable of self duplication during cell division.','Proteins are synthesised by various DNA molecules in the cell but the message for the synthesis of a particular protein is present in RNA.','An important function of nucleic acids is the protein synthesis in the cell.','NEW','12-430','12-300',NULL),
(281018,'biology','Energy for replication of a DNA comes from-','From the de-phosphorylation of charged nucleotides before replication','Energetically replication is a very expensive process. Deoxyribonucleoside triphosphates serve dual purposes. In addition to acting as substrates, they provide energy for polymerisation reaction as the two terminal phosphates in a deoxynucleoside triphosphates are high-energy phosphates, same as in case of ATP. Monophosphate are attached to the deoxyribose sugar by  ester bonds, subsequently the bi- and tri- phosphates are attached to the pre-exciting monophosphate by acid anhydride bonds which have a lot of trap energy in them which can be used for the energy requirement of the process of replication and transcription. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:106, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Machinery and the Enzymes','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','From the cellular metabolism','From the breakdown of energy reserves','From the anaerobic metabolism','From the de-phosphorylation of charged nucleotides before replication','NEW','12-106','12-091',NULL),
(281019,'biology','DNA polymerases are self sufficient and independent molecules, capable of polymerasing nucleotides for any region of the coding DNA strand without any regulatory molecules involved.','FALSE','The DNA polymerases on their own cannot initiate the process of replication. Also the replication does not initiate randomly at any place in DNA. There is a definite region where the replication originates. Such regions are termed as origin of replication.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:106, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Machinery and the Enzymes','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-106','12-091',NULL),
(281020,'biology','Replication is a process which initiates wherever and whenever it can','FALSE','The DNA polymerases on their own cannot initiate the process of replication. Also the replication does not initiate randomly at any place in DNA.  Also the replication does not initiate randomly at any place in DNA. There is a definite region in E. coli DNA where the replication originates. Such regions are termed as origin of replication.                                                                                    (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:106, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Machinery and the Enzymes','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-106','12-091',NULL),
(281101,'chemistry','Which among the following is an intercellular messenger?','Hormones','Hormones are molecules that act as intercellular messengers.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 430; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'1','mcq','Proteins','Deoxyribonucleic acid','Ribonucleic acid','Hormones','NEW','12-430','12-300',NULL),
(281102,'chemistry','What is the chemical nature of estrogen?','Steroid','In terms of chemical nature, some of these hormones are steroids, e.g., estrogens and androgens.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 430; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Carbohydrate','Protein','Steroid','Peptide','NEW','12-430','12-300',NULL),
(281103,'chemistry','Which hormone among the following is amino acid derivative?','Epinephrine','Some hormones are amino acid derivatives such as epinephrine and norepinephrine.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 430; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Androgen','Epinephrine','Endorphins','Insulin','NEW','12-430','12-300',NULL),
(281104,'chemistry','Choose the incorrect statement about insulin.','Glucagon mimics the action of insulin and works simultaneously with it.','Hormones have several functions in the body. They help to maintain the balance of biological activities in the body. The role of insulin in keeping the blood glucose level within the narrow limit is an example of this function. Insulin is released in response to the rapid rise in blood glucose level. On the other hand hormone glucagon tends to increase the glucose level in the blood.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 430; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Insulin is release in response to rapid rise in blood glucose level.','Glucagon mimics the action of insulin and works simultaneously with it.','Insulin tends to decrease the blood sugar level.','The role of insulin is keeping the blood glucose level within the narrow limit','NEW','12-430','12-300',NULL),
(281105,'chemistry','Thyroxine is derived from which amino acid?','Tyrosine','Thyroxine produced in the thyroid gland is an iodinated derivative of amino acid tyrosine.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 430; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Tryptophan','Tyrosine','Phenylalanine','Histidine','NEW','12-430','12-300',NULL),
(281106,'chemistry','Which of the following statements are true?\n \n a. Abnormally low level of thyroxine leads to hypothyroidism.\n \n b. Low level of iodine in the diet may lead to hypothyroidism and enlargement of the thyroid gland.\n \n c. Condition of hypothyroidism is controlled by adding sodium iodide to commercial salt.\n \n d. Hyperthyroidism is characterised by lethargyness and obesity.','a, b and c','Abnormally low level of thyroxine leads to hypothyroidism which is characterised by lethargyness and obesity.\n Increased level of thyroxine causes hyperthyroidism. \n Low level of iodine in the diet may lead to hypothyroidism and enlargement of the thyroid gland. \n This condition is largely being controlled by adding sodium iodide to commercial table salt (“Iodised” salt).\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','a, b and c','b, c and d','a, c and d','a, b and d','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281107,'chemistry','Steroidal hormones are produced by which gland in the body?','Adrenal cortex','Steroid hormones are produced by adrenal cortex and gonads (testes in males and ovaries in females). \n Hormones released by the adrenal cortex play very important role in the functions of the body.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Adrenal medulla','Adrenal cortex','Hypothalamus','Thyroid gland','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281108,'chemistry','Which part is affected in the person having addison\'s disease?','Adrenal cortex','The mineralocorticoids control the level of excretion of water and salt by the kidney. If adrenal cortex does not function properly then one of the results may be Addison’s disease characterised by hypoglycemia, weakness and increased susceptibility to stress.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Adrenal medulla','Adrenal cortex','Testes','Thyroid gland','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281109,'chemistry','Addison\'s disease is treated using which hormones?','Glucocorticoids and Mineralocorticoids','Addison\'s disease is fatal unless it is treated by glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Nucleic Acids',NULL,'2','mcq','Glucocorticoids and Mineralocorticoids','Glucocorticoids and Adrenaline','Thyroxine and Mineralocorticoids','Pitocin and Mineralocorticoids','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281201,'chemistry','Sucrose on hydrolysis gives _____.','α-D-glucose + β-D-fructose','One molecule of sucrose on hydrolysis gives one molecule of glucose and one molecule of fructose. In sucrose, two monosaccharides are held together by a glycosidic linkage between C-1 of α-D-glucose and C-2 of β-D-fructose.\n (NEET 2020)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','β-D-glucose + α-D-fructose','α-D-glucose + β-D-glucose','α-D-fructose + β-D-fructose.','α-D-glucose + β-D-fructose','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(281202,'chemistry','The difference between amylose and amylopectin is?','Amylopectin have 1 → 4 α-linkage and 1 → 6 α-linkage','Amylopectin is branched chain polymer while amylose is straight chain polymer both which makes up starch. Amylopectin have 1 → 4 α-linkage and 1 → 6 α-linkage while amylose has only 1 → 4 α-linkage.\n (NEET 2018)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Amylopectin have 1 → 4 α-linkage and 1 → 6 α-linkage','Amylose have 1 → 4 α-linkage and 1 → 6 β-linkage','Amylopectin have 1 → 4 α-linkage and 1 → 6 β-linkage','Amylose is made up of glucose and galactose.','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(281203,'chemistry','Which one given below is a non-reducing sugar?','Sucrose','The reducing groups of glucose and fructose are involved in glycosidic bond.\n (NEET-I 2016)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Sucrose','Glucose','Maltose','Lactose','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(281204,'chemistry','Which of the following is a basic amino acid?','Lysine','Lysine, Arginine are the examples of basic amino acids as they have extra amino group in their structure.\n (NEET 2020)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Serine','Tyrosine','Lysine','Alanine','NEW','12-421','12-291',NULL),
(281205,'chemistry','The non-essential amino acid among the following is?','Alanine','Non-essential amino acids, notably glycine, alanine, glutamic acid and glutamine, account for 80% of the total free amino acid nitrogen.\n (NEET 2019)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Lysine','Leucine','Valine','Alanine','NEW','12-421','12-291',NULL),
(281206,'chemistry','Which structure(s) of proteins remain(s) intact during denaturation process?','Primary structure only.','Denaturation involves alteration of the secondary, tertiary, or quaternary structures of proteins resulting in a loss of bioactivity.\n (Odisha NEET 2019)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Both secondary and tertiary structures.','Primary structure only.','Secondary structure only.','Tertiary structure only.','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(281207,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds can form a zwitter ion?','Glycine','Compounds having both acidic and basic group can form zwitter ion. Amino acids form zwitter ions at a particular pH.\n (NEET 2018)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Benzoic acid','Acetanilide','Glycine','Aniline','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(281208,'chemistry','In a protein molecule, various amino acids are linked together by?','Peptide bond','Amino acids are linked to each other by peptide bond formed between amino group and carboxylic acid with release of water.\n (NEET-I 2016)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'1','mcq','Dative bond','Alpha-glycosidic bond','Peptide bond','Beta-Glycosidic bond','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(281209,'chemistry','The central dogma of molecular genetics states that the genetic information flows from?','DNA → RNA → Proteins','Genetic information flows from: DNA Transcription → RNA Translation → Proteins\n (NEET 2016)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Amino acids → Proteins → DNA','DNA → RNA → Proteins','DNA → Carbohydrates → Proteins','DNA → RNA → Carbohydrates','NEW','12-430','12-300',NULL),
(281210,'chemistry','The correct statement regarding RNA and DNA, respectively is?','The sugar component in RNA is ribose and the sugar component in DNA is 2′-deoxyribose.','The sugar component in RNA is ribose and the sugar component in DNA is 2′-deoxyribose.\n (NEET 2016)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','The sugar component in RNA is a arabinose and the sugar component in DNA is ribose.','The sugar component in RNA is 2′-deoxyribose and the sugar component in DNA is arabinose.','The sugar component in RNA is arabinose and the sugar component in DNA is 2′-deoxyribose.','The sugar component in RNA is ribose and the sugar component in DNA is 2′-deoxyribose.','NEW','12-427','12-297',NULL),
(281211,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not correct?','Denaturation makes the proteins more active.','In denaturation protein\'s activity is lost and structural changes takes place leading to loss of tertiary and secondary structures.\n (NEET 2017)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Previous year question',NULL,'2','mcq','Ovalbumin is a simple food reserve in egg white.','Denaturation makes the proteins more active.','Blood proteins thrombin and fibrinogen are involved in blood clotting.','Insulin maintains sugar level in the blood of a human body.','NEW','12-424','12-294','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/281211q.png'),
(281301,'chemistry','Match the vitamins to their alternate names.','Vitamin B₆-Pyridoxine, Vitamin B₂-Riboflavin, Vitamin B₁-Thiamine, Vitamin C-Ascorbic acid','The alternate names for Vitamin B₆, Vitamin B₂, Vitamin B₁, Vitamin C are Pyridoxine, Riboflavin, Thiamine and Ascorbic acid respectively.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','match','Vitamin B₆,Pyridoxine','Vitamin B₂,Riboflavin','Vitamin B₁,Thiamine','Vitamin C,Ascorbic acid','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(281302,'chemistry','Which of the following is not a glucocorticoid function?','Control the level of water and salt excretion.','Glucocorticoids regulate glucose metabolism, modulate inflammatory responses, and have a role in stress responses. The mineralocorticoids regulate the kidney\'s excretion of water and salt.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Assist with stress reactions.','Control the carbohydrate metabolism.','Modulate inflammatory reactions.','Control the level of water and salt excretion.','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281303,'chemistry','The hormone responsible for preparing the uterus for implantation of a fertilised egg is:','Progesterone','Progesterone is responsible for preparing the uterus for implantation of fertilised egg.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Estradiol','Progesterone','Epinephrine','Thyroxine','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281304,'chemistry','Choose the correct options from the following statements about "Hormones":\n a. Hormones are transported from the endocrine glands to the site of action through receptors.\n b. Responses to external stimuli are mediated by glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids.\n c. Hormones are produced by endocrine glands in the body.\n d. Adrenaline hormone dysfunction can be fatal if not treated.','Both c and d','a. Statement (a) is incorrect because hormones are poured directly into the bloodstream, where they are transported to the site of action.\n b. Statement (b) is also incorrect because responses to external stimuli are mediated by epinephrine and norepinephrine.\n c. Statement (c) is correct as hormones are produced by endocrine glands in the body.\n d. Statement (d) is correct too as adrenaline hormone dysfunction can be fatal if not treated. i.e., If adrenal cortex does not function properly then one of the results may be Addison’s disease characterised by hypoglycemia, weakness and increased susceptibility to stress.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Paragraph 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and d','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281305,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n \n Assertion: Vitamin C be stored in our body.\n \n Reason: Vitamin C is a fat-soluble vitamin.','Both statements are false.','Vitamin C cannot be stored in our bodies because it is a water-soluble vitamin that is eliminated through sweat or urine. Hence, the assertion is true, but the reason is false.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 431; Intext Questions 14.6)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-431','12-301',NULL),
(281306,'chemistry','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion (A) and reason (R).\n \n Assertion: The water in an egg disappears when it is heated.\n \n Reason: The egg denatures and coagulates, and the water contained within it is absorbed by the coagulated protein via H-bonding.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','When we boil an egg in water, the globular proteins in it turn into a rubber like insoluble mass that absorbs all of the water in the egg by forming a hydrogen bond with it. The coagulation of albumin in egg white is an example of protein denaturation.\n Hence, both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 424; Intext Questions 14.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.','Both the assertion and the reason are correct, but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.','The assertion is true, but the reason is false.','Both statements are false.','NEW','12-424','12-294',NULL),
(281307,'chemistry','Select the correct option(s) from the following statements about "Disaccharides":\n a. Disaccharides with free functional groups are referred to as reducing sugars.\n b. When disaccharides are hydrolyzed with dilute acids or enzymes, they produce two molecules of the same or different monosaccharides.\n c. If the reducing groups of monosaccharides, such as aldehydic or ketonic groups, are not bonded in disaccharides, these are non-reducing sugars.\n d. Lactose is a non-reducing sugar.','Both a and b','a. Statement (a) is correct as the disaccharides (sugars) with free functional groups are referred to as reducing sugars.\n b. Statement (b) is also correct because when disaccharides are hydrolyzed with dilute acids or enzymes, they produce two molecules of the same or different monosaccharides.\n c. Statement (c) is incorrect because if the reducing groups of monosaccharides, such as aldehydic or ketonic groups, are bonded in disaccharides, these are non-reducing sugars.\n d. Statement (c) is incorrect too because lactose is a reducing sugar as it has a free aldehyde group produced at its monomer glucose.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both a and b','Both b and c','Both c and d','Both a and d','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(281308,'chemistry','For assigning the configuration of monosaccharides,','The lowest asymmetric carbon atom is compared.','When allocating the configuration of monosaccharides, the lowest asymmetric carbon atom is compared.\n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 415; Paragraph 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','The lowest asymmetric carbon atom is compared.','The highest asymmetric carbon atom is compared.','The centeral symmetric carbon atom is compared.','The aldehyde or ketone group is compared.','NEW','12-415','12-285',NULL),
(281309,'chemistry','The cyclic structure of glucose is more correctly represented by :','Haworth structure','The cyclic structure of glucose is more correctly represented by Haworth structure. \n (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Most Challenging Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Fischer projection','Haworth structure','D\' and \'L\' from','mutarotation','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(281401,'chemistry','Glycogen is a branched chain polymer of a- glucose units in which chain is formed by C —C4 glycosidic linkage where as branching occurs by the formation of C1-C6 glycosidic linkage. Structure of glycogen is similar to','amylopectin','Starch is a polymer of α-glucose and consists of two components— Amylose and Amylopectin. Amylose is water soluble component which constitutes about 15-20% of starch.\nGlycogen is also known as animal starch because its structure is similar to amylopectin and is rather more highly branched.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 418; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','amylose','amylopectin','cellulose','glucose','NEW','12-418','12-288',NULL),
(281402,'chemistry','Which of the following polymer is stored in the liver of animals?','Glycogen','Glycogen is present in liver, muscles and brain. When the body needs glucose, enzymes break the glycogen down to glucose. It is also found in yeast and fungi.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 419; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Amylose','Cellulose','Amylopectin','Glycogen','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(281403,'chemistry','Sucrose(cane sugar) is a disaccharide. One molecule of sucrose on hydrolysis gives','1 molecule of glucose +1 molecule of fructose','Sucrose is a disaccharide. These two monosaccharides are held together by a glycosidic linkage between C1 of a-D-glucose and C2 of b-D-fructose.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','2 molecules of glucose','2 molecules of glucose + 1 molecule of fructose','1 molecule of glucose +1 molecule of fructose','2 molecules of fructose','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(281404,'chemistry','Which of the following pairs represents anomers? Refer diagram(n2812)','c','The two cyclic hemiacetal forms of glucose differ only in the configuration of the hydroxyl group at C1, called anomeric carbon (the aldehyde carbon before cyclisation).\nSuch isomers, i.e., α-form and β-form, are called anomers.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-416','12-286','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/281404q.png'),
(281405,'chemistry','Proteins are found to have two different types of secondary structures viz a-helix and b-pleated sheet structure. a-helix structure of protein is stabilised by','hydrogen bonds','The secondary structure of protein refers to the shape in which a long polypeptide chain can exist.\nα-Helix is one of the most common ways in which a polypeptide chain forms all possible hydrogen bonds by twisting into a right handed screw.\n\nIn β-pleated sheet structure all peptide chains are stretched out to nearly maximum extension and then laid side by side which are held together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph 14.2.3 (ii))','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','peptide bond','van der waals forces','hydrogen bonds','dipole-diplole interactions','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(281406,'chemistry','In disaccharides, if the reducing groups of monosaccharides, i.e., aldehydic or ketonic groups are bonded, these are non-reducing sugars. Which of the following disaccharide is a non-reducing sugar? Refer diagram(n2813)','b','"In disaccharides, if the reducing groups of monosaccharides i.e., aldehydic or ketonic groups are bonded, these are non-reducing sugars.\n"Since the reducing groups of glucose and fructose are involved in glycosidic bond formation, sucrose is a non-reducing sugar.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)"','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-417','12-287','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/281406q.png'),
(281407,'chemistry','Which of the following acids is a vitamin?','Ascorbic acid','The alternate names for Vitamin B₆, Vitamin B₂, Vitamin B₁, Vitamin C are Pyridoxine, Riboflavin, Thiamine and Ascorbic acid respectively.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Table 14.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Aspartic acid','Ascorbic acid','Adipic acid','Saccharic acid','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(281408,'chemistry','Dinucleotide is obtained by joining two nucleotides together by phosphodiester linkage. Between which carbon atoms of pentose sugars of nucleotides are these linkages present?','5\' and 3\'','"In nucleosides, the sugar carbons are numbered as 1\', 2\', 3\', etc. in order to distinguish these from the bases. When nucleoside is linked to phosphoric acid at 5\'-position of sugar moiety, we get a nucleotide.\n\n"Nucleotides are joined together by phosphodiester linkage between 5\' and 3\' carbon atoms of the pentose sugar.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 429; Paragraph 14.5.2)"','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','5\' and 3\'','1\' and 5\'','5\' and 5\'','3\' and 3\'','NEW','12-429','12-299',NULL),
(281409,'chemistry','Nucleic acids are the polymers of','nucleotides','Since nucleic acids are long chain polymers of nucleotides, so they are also called polynucleotides.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 427; Paragraph 14.5)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','nucleosides','nucleotides','bases','sugars','NEW','12-427','12-297',NULL),
(281410,'chemistry','Which of the following statements is not true about glucose?','It is present in furanose form','The six membered cyclic structure of glucose is called pyranose structure (α– or β–), in analogy with pyran.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','It is an aldohexose','On heating with HI it forms n-hexane','It is present in furanose form','It does not give 2, 4- DNP test','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(281411,'chemistry','Each polypeptide in a protein has amino acids linked with each other in a specific sequence. This sequence of amino acids is said to be ......... .','primary structure','Each polypeptide in a protein has amino acids linked with each other in a specific sequence and it is this sequence of amino acids that is said to be the primary structure of that protein.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','primary structure','secondary structure','tertiary structure','quaternary structure','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(281412,'chemistry','DNA and RNA contain four bases each. Which of the following bases in not present in RNA?','Thymine','DNA contains four bases viz. adenine (A), guanine (G), cytosine (C) and thymine (T). RNA also contains four bases, the first three bases are same as in DNA but the fourth one is uracil (U).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 428; Paragraph 14.5.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Adenine','Uracil','Thymine','Cytosine','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281413,'chemistry','Which of the following B group vitamins can be stored in our body?','Vitamin B12','B group vitamins and vitamin C are soluble in water so they are grouped together. Water soluble vitamins must be supplied regularly in diet because they are readily excreted in urine and cannot be stored (except vitamin B12) in our body.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Paragraph 14.4.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Vitamin B1','Vitamin B2','Vitamin B6','Vitamin B12','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(281414,'chemistry','Which of the following bases is not present in DNA?','Uracil','DNA contains four bases viz. adenine (A), guanine (G), cytosine (C) and thymine (T). RNA also contains four bases, the first three bases are same as in DNA but the fourth one is uracil (U).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 428; Paragraph 14.5.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Adenine','Thymine','Cytosine','Uracil','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281415,'chemistry','There cyclic structures of monosaccharides are given below which of these are anomers. Refer diagram(n2814)','I and II','The two cyclic hemiacetal forms of glucose differ only in the configuration of the hydroxyl group at C1, called anomeric carbon (the aldehyde carbon before cyclisation).\nSuch isomers, i.e., α-form and β-form, are called anomers.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','I and II','II and III','I and III','III is anomer and I and II','NEW','12-416','12-286','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/281415q.png'),
(281416,'chemistry','Which of the following reactions of glucose can be explained only by its cyclic structure?','Pentaacetate of glucose does not react with hydroxyl amine','The reaction that can be explained only by the cyclic structure of glucose is "Glucose reacts with hydroxylamine to form an oxime". This is because the cyclic structure of glucose forms a hemiacetal or a cyclic acetal, which has a reactive carbonyl group that can react with hydroxylamine to form an oxime.\n\nThe other reactions listed, such as glucose forming a pentaacetate or glucose being oxidized by nitric acid to gluconic acid, do not rely solely on the cyclic structure of glucose and can be explained by the chemical properties of glucose as a monosaccharide. Additionally, the fact that the pentaacetate of glucose does not react with hydroxylamine is simply a property of the pentaacetate molecule and not necessarily related to the cyclic structure of glucose.','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Glucose forms pentaacetate','Glucose reacts with hydroxylamine to form an oxime','Pentaacetate of glucose does not react with hydroxyl amine','Glucose is oxidised by nitric acid to gluconic acid','NEW','12-413','12-283',NULL),
(281417,'chemistry','Optical rotations of some compounds alongwith their structures are given below: Which of them have D configuration. Refer diagram(n2815)','I, II, III','Glyceraldehyde contains one asymmetric carbon atom and exists in two enantiomeric forms.\n(+) Isomer of glyceraldehyde has ‘D’ configuration. It means that when its structural formula is written on paper, the –OH group lies on right hand side in the structure.\nThe letters ‘D’ or ‘L’ before the name of any compound indicate the relative configuration of a particular stereoisomer of a compound with respect to configuration of some other compound, configuration of which is known. In the case of carbohydrates, this refers to their relation with a particular isomer of glyceraldehyde.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 414; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','I, II, III','II, III','I, II','III','NEW','12-414','12-284','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/281417q.png'),
(281418,'chemistry','Carbohydrates are classified on the basis of their behaviour on hydrolysis and also as reducing or non-reducing sugar. Sucrose is a ......... .\n\na. monosaccharide\nb. disaccharide\nc. reducing sugar\nd. non reducing sugar','b,d','Since the reducing groups of glucose and fructose are involved in glycosidic bond formation, sucrose is a non-reducing sugar.\nSucrose is dextrorotatory but after hydrolysis gives dextrorotatory glucose and laevorotatory fructose. Since the laevorotation of fructose (–92.4°) is more than dextrorotation of glucose (+ 52.5°), the mixture is laevorotatory. Thus, hydrolysis of sucrose brings about a change in the sign of rotation, from dextro (+) to laevo (–) and the product is named as invert sugar.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 417; Paragraph no. 14.1.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','c,d','b,d','NEW','12-417','12-287',NULL),
(281419,'chemistry','Proteins can be classified into two types on the basis of their molecular shape, i.e., fibrous proteins and globular proteins. Examples of globular proteins are\n\na. insulin\nb. keratin\nc. albumin\nd. myosin','a,c','Globular proteins are usually soluble in water. Insulin and albumins are the common examples of globular proteins. Keratin is a fibrous protein, that are generally insoluble in water.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 423; Paragraph no. 14.2.3)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(281420,'chemistry','Which of the following carbohydrates are branched polymer of glucose?\n\na. Amylose\nb. Amylopectin\nc. Cellulose\nd. Glycogen','b,d','"Amylopectin is a branched chain polymer of a-D-glucose units in which chain is formed by C1–C4 glycosidic linkage whereas branching occurs by C1–C6 glycosidic linkage.\n\nGlycogen is also known as animal starch because its structure is similar to amylopectin and is rather more highly branched.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 419; Paragraph no. 14.1.4)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> Exemplar Based Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a,b','b,c','a,c','b,d','NEW','12-419','12-289',NULL),
(281501,'chemistry','Glucose on prolonged heating with HI gives','n-hexane','On prolonged heating with HI, glucose forms n-hexane, suggesting that all the six carbon atoms are linked in a straight chain.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 413; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.1) \ndiagram(ne2813)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','n-hexane','hexene','hexanoic acid','6-iodohexanal','NEW','12-413','12-283',NULL),
(281502,'chemistry','Which of the following vitamins given below is water soluble?','Vitamin C','B group vitamins and vitamin C are soluble in water so they are grouped together. Water soluble vitamins must be supplied regularly in diet because they are readily excreted in urine and cannot be stored (except vitamin B12) in our body.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 426; Paragraph 14.4.1)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Vitamin C','Vitamin D','Vitamin E','Vitamin K','NEW','12-426','12-296',NULL),
(281503,'chemistry','Which one of the following bases in not present in DNA?','Quinoline','DNA contains four bases viz. adenine (A), guanine (G), cytosine (C) and thymine (T). RNA also contains four bases, the first three bases are same as in DNA but the fourth one is uracil (U).\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 428; Paragraph 14.5.1) JEE Main 2014','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','Quinoline','Adenine','Cytosine','Thymine','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281504,'chemistry','Synthesis of each molecule of glucose in photosynthesis involves','18 molecules of ATP','The synthesis of each molecule of glucose in photosynthesis involves 18 molecules of ATP. This is because the synthesis of glucose in photosynthesis involves the Calvin cycle, which requires energy in the form of ATP to power the various chemical reactions that take place. \n\nOverall equation:\n6 CO2 + 18 ATP + 12 NADPH + 12 H+ → C6H12O6 + 18 ADP + 18 Pi + 12 NADP+ + 6 H2O [JEE Main 2013]','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','18 molecules of ATP','10 molecules of ATP','8 molecules of ATP','6 molecules of ATP','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281505,'chemistry','Which of the following compounds can be detected by Molisch\'s test?','Sugars','Molisch\'s test is a chemical test that is used to detect the presence of sugars or other compounds that can be broken down into simple sugars. \n\nThe test involves adding alpha-naphthol to the test solution and then adding concentrated sulfuric acid. If sugars are present, a purple ring will form at the interface between the two layers, indicating a positive result for the test. \n[AIEEE 2012]','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','Nitro compounds','Sugars','Amines','Primary alcohols','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281506,'chemistry','Which one of the following statements is correct?','All amino acids except glycine are optically active.','An amino acid is said to be optically active if it can rotate the plane of polarized light. All amino acids, except glycine, have a central carbon atom that is bonded to four different groups: an amino group (-NH2), a carboxyl group (-COOH), a hydrogen atom (-H), and a side chain or R-group. \n\nGlycine, however, is not optically active because its R-group is a hydrogen atom, meaning that there is no chiral center in the molecule. As a result, glycine does not exist as enantiomers and cannot rotate plane-polarized light. [AIEEE 2012]','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','All amino acids except lysine are optically active.','All amino acids are optically a','All amino acids except glycine are optically active.','All amino acids except glutamic acids are optically active.','NEW','12-422','12-292',NULL),
(281507,'chemistry','The presence or abscence of hydroxy group on which carbon atom of sugar differentiates RNA and DNA?','2nd','The presence or absence of a hydroxy (-OH) group on the 2\' carbon atom of the sugar differentiates RNA (ribonucleic acid) and DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid). \nThe sugar in RNA is ribose, which has a hydroxy group (-OH) attached to the 2\' carbon atom. In contrast, the sugar in DNA is deoxyribose, which has a hydrogen atom (-H) instead of a hydroxy group on the 2\' carbon atom. [AIEEE 2011]','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'3','mcq','1st','2nd','3rd','4th','NEW','12-428','12-298',NULL),
(281508,'chemistry','The change in the optical rotation of freshly prepared solution of glucose is known as','mutarotation','Sucrose does not show mutarotation.\nMutarotation is the phenomenon of change in optical rotation shown by freshly prepared solutions of sugars. \nHowever, this property is not exhibited by all sugars.\nOnly those sugars which have a free aldehyde (—CHO) or ketone (>C O) group are capable of showing mutarotation. \nSucrose lacks free aldehyde or ketone group and is therefore, incapable of showing mutarotation.\n[AIEEE 2011`]','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','tautomerism','racemization','specific rotation','mutarotation','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281509,'chemistry','Biuret test is not given by:','carbohydrates','The Biuret test is a laboratory test used to detect proteins or peptides in a sample. It produces a violet or blue color in the presence of peptide bonds. Carbohydrates do not contain peptide bonds and do not react with the test, so they cannot be detected by the Biuret test. [AIEEE 2010]','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','carbohydrates','polypeptides','urea','proteins','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(281510,'chemistry','The two functional groups present in a typical carbohydrate are','C=O and -OH','The two functional groups present in a typical carbohydrate are C=O (carbonyl group) and -OH (hydroxyl group).The carbonyl group (C=O) is typically found in the form of a ketone or an aldehyde group and is located at one end of the carbohydrate molecule. The hydroxyl group (-OH) is found on each of the remaining carbon atoms and is responsible for the molecule\'s solubility in water. [AIEEE 2009]','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','-OH and -COOH','-CHO and -COOH','C=O and -OH','-OH and -CHO','NEW','12-411','12-281',NULL),
(281511,'chemistry','β-D-(+) glucose, α-D-(+) glucose are','anomers','The two cyclic hemiacetal forms of glucose differ only in the configuration of the hydroxyl group at C1, called anomeric carbon (the aldehyde carbon before cyclisation).\nSuch isomers, i.e., α-form and β-form, are called anomers.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'1','mcq','conformers','epimers','anomers','enatiomers','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(281512,'chemistry','The secondary structure of a protein refers to','α- helical backbone','"The secondary structure of protein refers to the shape in which a long polypeptide chain can exist. They are found to exist in two different types of structures viz. α-helix and β-pleated sheet structure. \n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 432; Exercise 14.13)" [AIEEE 2007]','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','α- helical backbone','hydrophobic interactions','sequence of α- amino acids','fixed configuration of the polypeptide backbone','NEW','12-423','12-293',NULL),
(281513,'chemistry','The term anomers of glucose refers to','isomers of glucose that differ in configuration at carbon 1','The two cyclic hemiacetal forms of glucose differ only in the configuration of the hydroxyl group at C1, called anomeric carbon (the aldehyde carbon before cyclisation).\nSuch isomers, i.e., α-form and β-form, are called anomers.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 416; Paragraph no. 14.1.2.2)','Organic chemistry >> Biomolecules >> JEE Mains',NULL,'2','mcq','isomers of glucose that differ in configurations at carbons one and four','a mixture of D glucose and L glucose','enantiomers of glucose','isomers of glucose that differ in configuration at carbon 1','NEW','12-416','12-286',NULL),
(281601,'biology','What are the effects of deletions and rearrangements in DNA segments? \n i)Loss or gain of gene  \nii)Loss or gain of function\niii)Unchanged gene','i and ii','Effects of large deletions and rearrangements in a segment of DNA are easy to comprehend. It may result in loss or gain of a gene and so a function(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:113, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Mutations and Genetic Code','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-113','12-098',NULL),
(281602,'biology','Point mutation in beta globin changes glutamate to ______','Valine','A classical example of point mutation is a change of single base pair in the gene for beta globin chain that results in the change of amino acid residue glutamate to valine(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:113, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Mutations and Genetic Code','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Arginine','Histidine','Lysine','Valine','NEW','12-113','12-098',NULL),
(281603,'biology','Which is the condition that takes place due to point mutation in beta globin?','Sickle cell anemia','Change of glutamate residue to valine results into a diseased condition called as sickle cell anemia.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:113, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Mutations and Genetic Code','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Hypoglycemia','Diabetes','Sickle cell anemia','Cancer','NEW','12-113','12-098',NULL),
(281604,'biology','When reading frame changes from the point of insertion ,the mutation is called as','Frameshift insertion mutation','When the reading frame from the point of insertion or deletion. However, such mutations are referred to as frameshift insertion or deletion mutations respectively according to the mutation that has occured','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Mutations and Genetic Code','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Frameshift insertion mutation','Frameshift Deletion mutation','Manipulative mutation','Single gene translocation','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281605,'biology','In which condition the reading frame remains unaltered?  \ni)Insertion of multiple bases\nii)Deletion of multiple bases\niii)Insertion or deletion of three bases','only (iii)','Insertion or deletion of three or its multiple bases, insert or delete in one  in one or multiple codon, hence one or multiple amino acids, the reading frame remains unaltered from that point onwards (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:114, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Mutations and Genetic Code','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','only (iii)','ii and iii','i and iii','i and ii','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281701,'biology','Why should there be a mechanism to read mRNA code for amino acid synthesis?','Amino acids can\'t read code','From the very beginning of the proposition of code, it was clear to Francis Crick that there has to be a mechanism to read the code and also to link it to the amino acids, because amino acids have no structural specialities to read the code uniquely(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:114, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> tRNA the Adapter Molecule','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Amino acids can\'t read code','Amino acids can read code','Codes are encrypted','Codes are lengthy','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281702,'biology','Which molecule can simultaneously read code and bind to amino acids?','Adapter','Francis Crick postulated the presence of an adapter molecule that would on one hand read the code and on other hand would bind to specific amino acids(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:114, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> tRNA the Adapter Molecule','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Regulator','Transducer','Adapter','Terminator','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281703,'biology','Which of the following can act as an adapter molecule?','tRNA','The tRNA previously known sRNA, soluble RNA can act as an adapter molecule which read codes and binds to amino acids(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:114, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> tRNA the Adapter Molecule','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','mRNA','tRNA','rRNA','siRNA','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281704,'biology','Which part of tRNA contains complementary codons and amino acid acceptor end?','Anticodon loop','The tRNA has an anticodon loop that has bases complementary to the code, and it also has an amino acid acceptor end to which it binds to amino acids(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:114, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> tRNA the Adapter Molecule','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Codon loop','Elongation loop','Anticodon loop','Termination loop','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281705,'biology','Translation is initiated by _______ tRNA','Initiator','For initiation, there is another specific tRNA that is referred to as initiator tRNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:114, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> tRNA the Adapter Molecule','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','fillup','Ini,tia,tor,ebo,mep',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281706,'biology','For which codons there is no tRNA?','Stop codons','There are no tRNAs for stop codons(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:114, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> tRNA the Adapter Molecule','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Universal codons','Start codons','Terminal codons','Stop codons','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281707,'biology','What does secondary structure of RNA look like?','Clover leaf','The secondary structure of tRNA has been depicted that looks like a clover-leaf(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:114, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> tRNA the Adapter Molecule','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Loop','Helix','Clover leaf','Stem','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
