(281710,'biology','All RNA\'s are same in every organism, for every amino acid and for every protein.','FALSE','Each tRNA is specific for each amino acid (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:114, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> tRNA the Adapter Molecule','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281801,'biology','Polymerisation of amino acids to form polypeptide is called as','Translation','Translation refers to the process of polymerisation of amino acids to form a polypeptide(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:114, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Transpiration','Transcription','Translation','Replication','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(281802,'biology','How is the sequence of amino acids defined?','mRNA sequence','The order and sequence of amino acids are defined by the sequence of bases in the mRNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:114, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Codon sequence','Template strand','Anticodon sequence','mRNA sequence','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281803,'biology','Which bonds are present between amino acids?','Peptide bonds','The amino acids are joined by a bond which is known as a peptide bond(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:114, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Glycosidic bonds','Peptide bonds','Hydrogen bonds','Covalent bonds','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281804,'biology','Which events occurs in the first phase of translation? \ni)Charging of tRNA\nii)Utilization of GTP  \niii)Aminoacylation of tRNA','i and iii','Formation of a peptide bond requires energy.The first phase itself amino acids are activated in the presence of ATP and linked to their cognate tRNA – a process commonly called as charging of tRNA or aminoacylation of tRNA would be favoured energetically(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:114, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','i and ii','i and iii','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281805,'biology','In which tRNA, formation of peptide bonds is energetically favourable?','Aminoacylated tRNA','If two such charged tRNAs are brought close enough, the formation of peptide bond between them(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:114, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Activated tRNA','Aminoacylated tRNA','Methylated tRNA','Modified tRNA','NEW','12-114','12-099',NULL),
(281806,'biology','The presence of which compound enhances peptide bond formation?','Catalyst','The presence of a catalyst would enhance the rate of peptide bond formation(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:115, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Activator','Terminator','Adapter','Catalyst','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281807,'biology','Which cellular organelle is responsible to synthesize proteins?','Ribosomes','The cellular factory responsible for synthesising proteins is the ribosome(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Mitochondria','Ribosomes','Nucleus','Lysosomes','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281808,'biology','What are the characteristics of ribosomes? \ni)Has structural RNAs  \nii)Contains 80 different proteins  \niii)Has two subunits','i, ii and iii','The ribosome consists of structural RNAs and about 80 different proteins. In its inactive state, it exists as two subunits(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','ii and iii','i and ii','i, ii and iii','i and iii','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281809,'biology','Which component of ribosome initiates the translation process?','Small subunit','When the small subunit encounters an mRNA, the process of translation of the mRNA to protein begins(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Large subunit','Structural RNAs','Small subunit','Both Subunits','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281810,'biology','What are the functions of two sites on large subunit? \ni)Bind amino acids  \nii)Keep amino acid close  \niii)Add peptide bonds','i and ii','There are two sites in the large subunit, for subsequent amino acids to bind to and thus, be close enough to each other for the formation of a peptide bond(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and ii','i, ii and iii','i and iii','ii and iii','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281811,'biology','Which particular enzyme acts as a catalyst in peptide bond formation?','Ribozyme','The ribosome also acts as a catalyst, the 23S rRNA in bacteria is the enzyme- ribozyme catalyses the formation of peptide bond(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Peptidase','Protease','Ribozyme','Riboside','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281812,'biology','Which codons flank the translational unit?\ni)AUG  \nii)Stop codon  \niii)Polypeptide codes','i, ii and iii','A translational unit in mRNA is the sequence of RNA that is flanked by the start codon (AUG) and the stop codon and codes for a polypeptide(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281813,'biology','An mRNA also has some additional sequences that are not translated and are referred as','UTR','An mRNA also has some additional sequences that are not translated and are referred as untranslated regions (UTR).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;  page no:115, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','ATR','UTR','URB','TRE','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281814,'biology','What are the features of untranslated regions UTRs on mRNA? \ni)5\' -end (before start codon)  \nii)3\' -end (after stop codon)  \niii)Neccesary for efficient translation','i, ii and iii','The UTRs are present at both 5\' -end (before start codon) and at 3\' -end (after stop codon). They are required for efficient translation process(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;  page no:115, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and iii','i and ii','i, ii and iii','ii and iii','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281815,'biology','At which codon ribosome binds mRNA to initiate translation?','AUG','For initiation, the ribosome binds to the mRNA at the start codon (AUG) that is recognised only by the initiator tRNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','AGG','AUG','UGG','AGU','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281816,'biology','What events occur during elongation? \ni)Movement of ribosome along codons  \nii)Complementary base pairing \niii)Amino acid linkage to tRNA','i, ii and iii','In the elongation stage, complexes composed of an amino acid linked to tRNA, sequentially bind to the appropriate codon in mRNA by forming complementary base pairs with the tRNA anticodon. The ribosome moves from codon to codon along the mRNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','i and iii','i and ii','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281817,'biology','After the progressive addition of amino acids, they are translated into','Polypeptide','Amino acids are added one by one, translated into Polypeptide sequences dictated by DNA and represented by mRNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Protein','Polypeptide','mRNA','DNA','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281818,'biology','At the end, a ______ binds to the stop codon, terminating translation and releasing the complete polypeptide from the ribosome.','Release factors','At the end, a release factor binds to the stop codon, terminating translation and releasing the complete polypeptide from the ribosome(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Translation','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Enhancing factors','Adapters','Release factors','Terminating factors','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281901,'biology','Other than transcription at what other levels gene expression is regulated? \ni)Splicing  \nii)Transportation \niii)Translation','i, ii and iii','Gene expression can be regulated at several levels. In eukaryotes, the regulation could be exerted at transcriptional level (formation of primary transcript, processing level (regulation of splicing), transport of mRNA from nucleus to the cytoplasm, translational level(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i, ii and iii','i and iii','ii and iii','i and ii','NEW','12-115','12-100',NULL),
(281902,'biology','What is the function of enzyme beta galactosidase?','Lactose-->Galactose-->Glucose','An enzyme called beta-galactosidase is synthesised by E. coli, it is used to catalyse the hydrolysis of a disaccharide, lactose into galactose and glucose(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Lactose-->Galactose-->Glucose','Galactose->Lactose-->Glucose','Glucose-->Galactose-->Lactose','Lactose-->Glucose-->Galactose','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(281903,'biology','Why do bacteria synthesis beta galactosidase?','Catalyse the hydrolysis of lactose for energy','An enzyme called beta-galactosidase is synthesised by E. coli, it is used to catalyse the hydrolysis of a disaccharide, lactose into galactose and glucose; the bacteria use them as a source of energy(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','For respiration','Catalyse the hydrolysis of lactose for energy','For carbohydrate synthesis','For glucose breakdown','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(281904,'biology','What are some general factors that regulate gene expression? \ni)Metabolic  \nii)Physiological  \niii)Environmental','i, ii and iii','In simple terms, it is the metabolic, physiological or environmental conditions that regulate the expression of genes(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(281905,'biology','In prokaryotes, what is the predominant site for control of gene expression?','Transcriptional initiation','In prokaryotes, control of the rate of transcriptional initiation is the predominant site for control of gene expression(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Transcriptional initiation','Transcriptional elongation','Transcriptional termination','Splicing','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(281906,'biology','The activity of RNA polymerase in transcriptional unit is regulated by interaction with','Accessory proteins','In a transcription unit, the activity of RNA polymerase at a given promoter is in turn regulated by interaction with accessory proteins, which affect its ability to recognise start sites(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Enzymes','Accessory proteins','Amino acids','Proteins','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(281907,'biology','Regulatory proteins can act as- \ni)Activators \nii)Repressors  \niii)Enzymes','i and ii','These regulatory proteins can act both positively (activators) and negatively (repressors)(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(281908,'biology','The accessibility of promoter regions of prokaryotic DNA is in many cases regulated by the interaction of proteins with sequences termed as','Operators','The accessibility of promoter regions of prokaryotic DNA is in many cases regulated by the interaction of proteins with sequences termed operators(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Activators','Transducers','Operators','Enhancers','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(281909,'biology','Where is the operator region located? \ni)Adjacent to promoter  \nii)The sequences of the operator bind a repressor protein\niii) Adjacent to terminator','i and ii','The operator region is adjacent to the promoter elements in most operons and in most cases the sequences of the operator bind a repressor protein(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(281910,'biology','With which accessory proteins does the lac operator interact specifically?','Lac repressors','Lac operator is present only in the lac operon and it interacts specifically with lac repressor only(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Lac activator','Lac repressors','Lac transducers','Lac enzymes','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(281911,'biology','Assertion: Beta-galactosidase is synthesized all the time. \nReason: Even if lactose is not present in the environment as energy source, beta-galactosidase is synthesized.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','An enzyme called beta-galactosidase is synthesised by E. coli, is used to catalyse the hydrolysis of a disaccharide, lactose into galactose and glucose; the bacteria use them as a source of energy. Hence, if the bacteria do not have lactose around them to be utilised for energy source, they would no longer require the synthesis of the enzyme beta-galactosidase. The development and differentiation of embryo into adult organisms are also a result of the coordinated regulation of expression of several sets of genes. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(281912,'biology','Which statement is true','Each operon has specific operator and specific repressor','Each operon has its specific operator and specific repressor.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Regulation of Gene Expression','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Each operon has specific operator and specific repressor','Each operon has  specific operator but non-specific repressor','Each operon has specific repressor but non- specific operator','Each operon has non-specific repressor but non- specific operator','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(282001,'biology','Which scientist contributed to the understanding of lac operon? \ni)Edward Tatum  \nii)Francois Jacob  \niii)Jacque Monod','ii and iii','The elucidation of the lac operon was also a result of a close association between a geneticist, Francois Jacob and a biochemist, Jacque Monod(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(282002,'biology','In lac operon, which structural gene is regulated by common promoters and regulatory genes?','Polycistronic gene','In lac operon (here lac refers to lactose), a polycistronic structural gene is regulated by a common promoter and regulatory genes(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Monocistronic gene','Tricistronic gene','Polycistronic gene','Bicistronic gene','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(282003,'biology','Other than the inhibitor gene, what are the other structural genes present in lac operon? \ni)z  \nii)y  \niii)a  \niv)x','i, ii and iii','The lac operon consists of one regulatory gene (the i gene – here the term i does not refer to inducer, rather it is derived from the word inhibitor) and three structural genes (z, y, and a)(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(282005,'biology','Why is lactose known as an inducer in lac operon?','Acts as substrate','Lactose is the substrate for the enzyme beta-galactosidase and it regulates switching on and off of the operon. Hence, it is termed as inducer(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:117, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','It is the final product','Acts as substrate','Is carbohydrate in nature','Used for energy','NEW','12-117','12-102',NULL),
(282006,'biology','Which enzyme is used to transport lactose into the cells?','Permease','In the absence of a preferred carbon source such as glucose, if lactose is provided in the growth medium of the bacteria, the lactose is transported into the cells through the action of permease(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:117, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Inducer','Transacetylase','Permease','Beta galactosidase','NEW','12-117','12-102',NULL),
(282007,'biology','What is the function of the repressor?Why are primary transcripts subjected to splicing? \ni)Binds to operators  \nii)Formation of lactose  \niii)Prevention of operon transcription','i and iii','The repressor protein binds to the operator region of the operon and prevents RNA polymerase from transcribing the operon(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:117, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','iii and ii','ii and iii','NEW','12-117','12-102',NULL),
(282008,'biology','In lac operon, by interacting with _________, the repressor is inactivated','Lactose','In the presence of an inducer, such as lactose or allolactose, the repressor is inactivated by interaction with the inducer(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:117, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Lactose','Galactose','Mannose','Glucose','NEW','12-117','12-102',NULL),
(282009,'biology','What kind of regulation occurs in lac operon?','Negative regulation','Regulation of lac operon by repressor is referred to as negative regulation In lac operon, lactose acts as an inducer. If lactose is provided in the medium for the bacteria, the regulatory gene is activated. The inducer will bind to the repressor protein and render it inactive which allows transcription of the operon. Thus, the lac operon is negatively regulated.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:117, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Positive regulation','Median regulation','Negative regulation','Automated regulation','NEW','12-117','12-102',NULL),
(282010,'biology','What is the nature of regulatory gene of lac operon?','Inhibitor','The lac operon consists of one regulatory gene the i gene – here the term i does not refer to inducer, rather it is derived from the word inhibitor(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:116, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Inducer','Inhibitor','Initiator','Activator','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(282011,'biology','Which of the following is not a structural gene of lac operon?','b','The structural genes of lac operon include z, y, a(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','z','y','a','b','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(282012,'biology','Match the following enzymes with the genes encoding them','Lac operon repressor-i gene,Beta galactosidase-z gene,Transacetylase-a gene,Permease-y gene','The i gene codes for the repressor of the lac operon. The z gene codes for beta galactosidase (b-gal), which is primarily responsible for the hydrolysis of the disaccharide, lactose into its monomeric units, galactose and glucose. The y gene codes for permease, which increases permeability of the cell to b galactosides. The a gene encodes a transacetylase.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:117, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','match','Lac operon repressor,i gene','Beta galactosidase,z gene','Transacetylase,a gene','Permease,y gene','NEW','12-117','12-102',NULL),
(282013,'biology','Beta galactosidase hydrolysis lactose to form','Glucose, Galactose','Beta galactosidase is primarily responsible for the hydrolysis of the disaccharide, lactose into its monomeric units, galactose and glucose(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:117, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Glucose, Fructose','Glucose, Sucrose','Glucose, Galactose','Glucose, Ribose','NEW','12-117','12-102',NULL),
(282014,'biology','Which enzyme is known to increase the permeability of cell to beta galactosidase?','Permease','Permease is the enzyme which increases permeability of the cell to β-galactosides(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:117, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Transacetylase','Beta galactosidase','Permease','Lactate','NEW','12-117','12-102',NULL),
(282015,'biology','Which gene encodes for transacetylase and beta galactosidase?','a,z','Transacetylase is encoded by gene a and beta galactosidase is encoded by gene z(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','a,z','a,y','i,y','i,z','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(282016,'biology','How does gene works in an operon?','Together','In most other operons as well, the genes present in the operon are needed together to function in the same or related metabolic pathway(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Together','Antagonistically','Individually','Successively','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(282017,'biology','______ were the first to elucidate a transcriptionally regulated system, such arrangement is very common in bacteria and is referred to as operon.','Francois Jacob and Jacque Monod','Jacob and Manod were the first to elucidate a transcriptionally regulated system, such arrangement is very common in bacteria and is referred to as operon.  (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Oswald Avery, Colin MacLeod and Maclyn McCarty','Gregor Mendel, Walter Sutton, Thomas Hunt Morgan','Francois Jacob and Jacque Monod','Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(282018,'biology','Assertion: Operons are transcriptionally-regulated systems in bacteria. \n\nReason: Some of the examples are try operon, bro operon, her operon, wall operon, lac operon.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','A transcriptionally regulated system arrangement is very common in bacteria and is referred to as operon. To name few such examples, lac operon, trp operon, ara operon, his operon, val operon, etc (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-116','12-101',NULL),
(282019,'biology','What is the correct sequence?','Inducer-Repressor inactivated-RNA Polymerase-Promoter-Transcription','In the presence of an inducer, such as lactose or allolactose, the repressor is inactivated by interaction with the inducer. This allows RNA polymerase access to the promoter and transcription proceed. Essentially, regulation of lac operon can also be visualised as regulation of enzyme synthesis by its substrate. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:117, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> The Lac Operon','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Inducer-Promoter-Repressor inactivated-RNA Polymerase-Transcription','Inducer-RNA Polymerase-Promoter-Repressor inactivated-Transcription','Inducer-RNA Polymerase-Repressor inactivated-Promoter-Transcription','Inducer-Repressor inactivated-RNA Polymerase-Promoter-Transcription','NEW','12-117','12-102',NULL),
(282101,'biology','Which biomolecule determines the genetic makeup of an organism?','DNA','Genetic make-up of an organism or an individual lies in the DNA sequences(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:118, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','RNA','DNA','Proteins','Amino acids','NEW','12-118','12-103',NULL),
(282102,'biology','Which technique made DNA isolation and cloning feasible?','Genetic engineering','With the establishment of genetic engineering techniques where it was possible to isolate and clone any piece of DNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:118, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Artificial recombination','Genetic encryption','Gene silencing','Genetic engineering','NEW','12-118','12-103',NULL),
(282103,'biology','In which year was the human genome project launched?','1990','The project of sequencing human genome was launched in the year 1990(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:118, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','1980','2000','1990','2002','NEW','12-118','12-103',NULL),
(282104,'biology','Other than forming the database, what were the goals of human genome project? \ni)Sequence 3 billion base pair  \nii)Identify human genes  \niii)Improve data analysis tools','i, ii and iii','Following were the goals of human genome project to-(i) identify all the approximately 20,000-25,000 genes in human DNA; (ii) Determine the sequences of the 3 billion chemical base pairs that make up human DNA; (iii) Improve tools for data analysis(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:118, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and iii','i and ii','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-118','12-103',NULL),
(282105,'biology','How long was the human genome project and when was it completed?','13 years, 2003','The Human Genome Project was a 13-year long project which was completed in 2003(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:118, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','14 years, 2004','10 years, 2000','13 years, 2003','12 years, 2002','NEW','12-118','12-103',NULL),
(282106,'biology','What non-human models other than bacteria and yeast have been sequenced? \ni)Drosophila  \nii)Caenorhabditis elegans  \niii)Arabidopsis','i, ii and iii','Many non-human model organisms, such as bacteria, yeast, Caenorhabditis elegans (a free living non-pathogenic nematode), Drosophila (the fruit fly), plants (rice and Arabidopsis), etc., have also been sequenced(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','ii and iii','NEW','12-119','12-104',NULL),
(282107,'biology','What was the technique used to identify all genes expressed in RNA?','Expressed sequence tags','One approach focused on identifying all the genes that are expressed as RNA (referred to as Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs)(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Expressed sequence tags','Sequence annotation','Sanger sequencing','Alignment techniques','NEW','12-118','12-103',NULL),
(282108,'biology','What was the blind technique used to sequence the whole genome?','Sequence annotation','The blind approach of simply sequencing the whole set of genome that contained all the coding and non-coding sequence, and later assigning different regions in the sequence with functions is known as Sequence Annotation(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Expressed sequence tags','Nanopore sequencing','Sequence annotation','Sequencing by ligation','NEW','12-119','12-104',NULL),
(282109,'biology','What are the events that occur in DNA sequencing? \ni)Isolation  \nii)Conversion into fragments  \niii)Cloning  \niv)Slicing','i, ii and iii','For sequencing, the total DNA from a cell is isolated and converted into random fragments of relatively smaller sizes and cloned in suitable host using specialised vectors(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and ii','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-119','12-104',NULL),
(282110,'biology','What is the effect of cloning on DNA?','Amplification','The cloning resulted into amplification of each piece of DNA fragment so that it subsequently could be sequenced with ease(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Reduction','Isolation','Amplification','Translation','NEW','12-119','12-104',NULL),
(282111,'biology','Which vectors are used for gene insertion when bacteria and yeast are host organisms?','BAC, YAC','The commonly used hosts are bacteria and yeast, and the vectors are called as BAC bacterial artificial chromosomes and YAC yeast artificial chromosomes(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','BAk, YAK','BRC, YRC','BAC, YAC','BRA, YRA','NEW','12-119','12-104',NULL),
(282112,'biology','What principle method is used to sequence the DNA fragments?','Sanger sequencing','The fragments were sequenced using automated DNA sequencers that worked on the principle of a method developed by Frederick Sanger known as sanger sequencing(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:119, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Genap sequencing','Sanger sequencing','Nano sequencing','Micro sequencing','NEW','12-119','12-104',NULL),
(282113,'biology','In Human Genome Project,On what basis the sequence fragments were arranged?','Overlapping region','The sequences were then arranged based on some overlapping regions(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Coding sequences','Non coding sequences','Junk region','Overlapping region','NEW','12-119','12-104',NULL),
(282114,'biology','What was the last chromosome to be sequenced and when was it sequenced?','Chr 1, 2006','The sequence of chromosome 1 was completed only in May 2006, this was the last of the 24 human chromosomes – 22 autosomes and X and Y – to be sequenced(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Chr 1, 2006','Chr 23, 2005','Chr 18, 2007','Chr 2, 2000','NEW','12-119','12-104',NULL),
(282115,'biology','Which information was used to generate physical and genetic maps on genome? \ni)Restriction endonuclease polymorphism  \nii)Microsatellite sequences  \niii)Base pair compatibility','i and ii','The map was generated using information on polymorphism of restriction endonuclease recognition sites, and some repetitive DNA sequences known as microsatellites(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:120, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and iii','i and ii','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-120','12-105',NULL),
(282116,'biology','Human genome project closely associated with the rapid development of a new area in biology called','Bioinformatics','Human Genome Project (HGP) was called a mega project. You can imagine the magnitude and the requirements for the project if we simply define the aims of the project as follows: Human genome is said to have approximately 3 x 109 bp, and if the cost of sequencing required is US $ 3 per bp (the estimated cost in the beginning), the total estimated cost of the project would be approximately 9 billion US dollars. Further, if the obtained sequences were to be stored in typed form in books, and if each page of the book contained 1000 letters and each book contained 1000 pages, then 3300 such books would be required to store the information of DNA sequence from a single human cell. The enormous amount of data expected to be generated also necessitated the use of high speed computational devices for data storage and retrieval, and analysis. HGP was closely associated with the rapid development of a new area in biology called Bioinformatics. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:118, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Bioinformatics','Biotechnics','Biomechanics','Biostatistics','NEW','12-118','12-103',NULL),
(282117,'biology','Methods used in HGP involved-\n(i) Identifying all the genes that are expressed as RNA. \n(ii) Sequencing the whole set of genome that contained all the coding and non-coding sequence. \n(iii) Following the sequencing, assigning different regions in the sequence with functions','i, ii and iii','The methods in HGP involved two major approaches. One approach focused on identifying all the genes that are expressed as RNA (referred to as Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs). The other took the blind approach of simply sequencing the whole set of genome that contained all the coding and non-coding sequence, and later assigning different regions in the sequence with functions (a term referred to as Sequence Annotation). (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','ii and iii','i and ii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-119','12-102',NULL),
(282118,'biology','Correct order for Sequence Annotation is','Isolating DNA- Breaking it into random fragments of smaller size- Specialized vector- Cloning in host- Amplification','For sequencing, the total DNA from a cell is isolated and converted into random fragments of relatively smaller sizes (recall DNA is a very long polymer, and there are technical limitations in sequencing very long pieces of DNA) and cloned in a suitable host using specialized vectors. The cloning resulted into amplification of each piece of DNA fragment so that it subsequently could be sequenced with ease. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Human Genome Project','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Isolating DNA- Specialized vector- Cloning in host- Amplification- Breaking it into random fragments of smaller size','Isolating DNA- Specialized vector- Cloning in host- Breaking it into random fragments of smaller size- Amplification','Isolating DNA- Breaking it into random fragments of smaller size- Specialized vector- Cloning in host- Amplification','Isolating DNA- Specialized vector- Breaking it into random fragments of smaller size- Cloning in host- Amplification','NEW','12-119','12-102',NULL),
(282201,'biology','What is the base count of dystrophin?','2.4 million bases','The largest known human gene being dystrophin at 2.4 million bases(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:120, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Salient Features of Human Genome','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','2.2 million bases','2.3 million bases','2.4 million bases','2.8 million bases','NEW','12-120','12-103',NULL),
(282202,'biology','What is the percentage of nucleotide bases similar in all organisms?','99.90%','Almost all (99.9 %)  of genetic pattern are exactly the same in all people, it is that 0.1% that makes every human unique(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:120, paragraph no:2).','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Salient Features of Human Genome','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','50%','90%','88.80%','99.90%','NEW','12-120','12-103',NULL),
(282203,'biology','What is the function of repetitive sequences, In human genome project ?','Chromosome structure determination','Repetitive sequences are stretches of DNA sequences that are repeated many times, sometimes hundred to thousand times. They are thought to have no direct coding functions, but they shed light on chromosome structure, dynamics and evolution(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:120, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Salient Features of Human Genome','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Splicing','Assistance in transcription','Protein encoding','Chromosome structure determination','NEW','12-120','12-103',NULL),
(282204,'biology','Match the following','Human genome-3164.7 million bp,Average gene-3000 bases,Largest known human gene-Dystrophin,Total number of genes is estimated-30000','Some of the salient observations drawn from human genome project are as follows:The human genome contains 3164.7 million bp; The average gene consists of 3000 bases, but sizes vary greatly, with the largest known human gene being dystrophin at 2.4 million bases;The total number of genes is estimated at 30,000(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:120, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Salient Features of Human Genome','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','match','Human genome,3164.7 million bp','Average gene,3000 bases','Largest known human gene,Dystrophin','Total number of genes is estimated,30000','NEW','12-120','12-103',NULL),
(282205,'biology','Match the following','Genome codes for proteins-Less than 2%,Chromosome 1-2968 genes,Chromosome Y-231 genes,1.4 million locations-Single nucleotide polymorphism in humans','Other salient observations-The functions are unknown for over 50 % of the discovered genes; Less than 2 % of the genome codes for proteins; Repeated sequences make up very large portion of the human genome; Chromosome 1 has most genes (2968), and the Y has the fewest (231); Scientists have identified about 1.4 million locations where singlebase DNA differences (SNPs – single nucleotide polymorphism, pronounced as ‘snips’) occur in humans.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:120, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Salient Features of Human Genome','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','match','Genome codes for proteins,Less than 2%','Chromosome 1,2968 genes','Chromosome Y,231 genes','1.4 million locations,Single nucleotide polymorphism in humans','NEW','12-120','12-103',NULL),
(282301,'biology','What is a quick way to compare DNA sequences of many individuals?','DNA fingerprinting','DNA fingerprinting is a very quick way to compare the DNA sequences of any two individuals(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:121, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','DNA sequencing','DNA fingerprinting','Genetic engineering','DNA microarray','NEW','12-121','12-104',NULL),
(282302,'biology','In which regions DNA fingerprinting is used to find differences?','Repetitive DNA','DNA fingerprinting involves identifying differences in some specific regions in DNA sequence called as repetitive DNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:121, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Satellite DNA','Telomeric DNA','Repetitive DNA','Junk DNA','NEW','12-121','12-104',NULL),
(282303,'biology','Which technique is used to differentiate repetitive DNA from bulk genomic DNA?','Density gradient centrifugation','These repetitive DNA are separated from bulk genomic DNA as different peaks during density gradient centrifugation(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:121, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Gel electrophoresis','Density gradient centrifugation','Vortex mixing','Autoclave sanitizing','NEW','12-121','12-104',NULL),
(282304,'biology','In density gradient centrifugation small peaks indicate __________ DNA','Satellite','The bulk DNA forms a major peak and the other small peaks are referred to as satellite DNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:121, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','fillup','Sat,ell,ite,ebi,bfo',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-121','12-104',NULL),
(282305,'biology','Based on what parameters, satellite DNAs are classified into micro and mini satellites? \ni)Base composition \nii)Segment length \niii)Repetitive units','i, ii and iii','Depending on base composition (A : T rich or G:C rich), length of segment, and number of repetitive units, the satellite DNA is classified into many categories, such as micro-satellites, mini-satellites(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:121, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i and iii','ii and iii','Only i','i, ii and iii','NEW','12-121','12-104',NULL),
(282306,'biology','What is the characteristics of satellite DNA? \ni)Large portion of human genome \nii)Show polymorphism and normally do not code for any proteins \niii)Base of fingerprinting','i, ii and iii','These sequences normally do not code for any proteins, but they form a large portion of human genome. These sequence show high degree of polymorphism and form the basis of DNA fingerprinting(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:121, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','i, ii and iii','Only i','ii and iii','i and iii','NEW','12-121','12-104',NULL),
(282307,'biology','Why do DNA from various tissues become tool in forensic investigations?','Show the same degree of polymorphism','Since DNA from every tissue (such as blood, hair-follicle, skin, bone, saliva, sperm etc.), from an individual show the same degree of polymorphism, they become very useful identification tool in forensic applications(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:121, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','They are duplex','Have H-bonding','Show the same degree of polymorphism','Contain ribose sugar','NEW','12-121','12-104',NULL),
(282308,'biology','Which technique is the basis of paternity testing?','DNA fingerprinting','Polymorphisms are inheritable from parents to children so DNA fingerprinting which relies on polymorphism is the basis of paternity testing(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:121, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Gene sequencing','DNA fingerprinting','Microarray techniques','Nano sequencing','NEW','12-121','12-104',NULL),
(282309,'biology','In which cells,  mutations can arise? \ni)Somatic cells  \nii)Germ cells  \niii)Cancerous cells','i and ii','New mutations may arise in an individual either in somatic cells or in the germ cells, cells that generate gametes in sexually reproducing organisms(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:121, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Only i','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','i and ii','NEW','12-121','12-104',NULL),
(282310,'biology','What happens to a germ cell mutation if it does not appear in the individual?','Shows up in other population members','If a germ cell mutation does not seriously impair individual’s ability to have offspring who can transmit the mutation, it can spread to the other members of population(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:121, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Never shows up','Remains benign','Effects other organs','Shows up in other population members','NEW','12-121','12-104',NULL),
(282311,'biology','Under what conditions, an inheritable mutation becomes DNA polymorphism?','Observed at high frequency','When an inheritable mutation is observed in a population at high frequency, it is referred to as DNA polymorphism(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','When it passes to other generations','When it impacts organs','Observed at high frequency','Remains unexpressed','NEW','12-122','12-105',NULL),
(282312,'biology','In which DNA sequences, the probability of DNA polymorphism is higher?','Non coding','The probability of such variation to be observed in non coding DNA sequence would be higher as mutations in these sequences may not have any immediate effect/impact in an individual’s reproductive ability(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:122, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Coding','Spliced','Non-Template','Non coding','NEW','12-122','12-105',NULL),
(282313,'biology','Who developed DNA fingerprinting technique?','Alec Jeffreys','The technique of DNA Fingerprinting was initially developed by Alec Jeffreys(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Alex Michael','Alec Jeffreys','Alan Michelledes','Hans Christian','NEW','12-122','12-105',NULL),
(282314,'biology','Alec Jeffreys used satellite DNA as ______','Probe','He used a satellite DNA as probe that shows very high degree of polymorphism(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Probe','Vaccine','Antigen','CRISPR- Cas 9 method','NEW','12-122','12-105',NULL),
(282315,'biology','The probe used as satellite DNA is known as','VNTR','It was called as Variable Number of Tandem Repeats (VNTR)(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','VNTR','TNBR','CNOR','TVNR','NEW','12-122','12-105',NULL),
(282316,'biology','What were the steps involved in Southern blot hybridisation using radiolabelled VNTR as a probe? \ni)DNA isolation  \nii)DNA digestion by restriction endonucleases  \niii)DNA fragment separation by electrophoresis','i, ii and iii','It included (i) isolation of DNA, (ii) digestion of DNA by restriction endonucleases, (iii) separation of DNA fragments by electrophoresis, (iv) transferring (blotting) of separated DNA fragments to synthetic membranes, such as nitrocellulose or nylon, (v) hybridisation using labelled VNTR probe, and (vi) detection of hybridised DNA fragments by autoradiography(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Only i','ii and iii','i, ii and iii','i and iii','NEW','12-122','12-105',NULL),
(282317,'biology','Which of the following process is not involved in southern blot hybridisation?','Joining of DNA fragments by endonucleases','The processes involved in southern blot hbridisation include transferring (blotting) of separated DNA fragments to synthetic membranes, such as nitrocellulose or nylon, hybridisation using labelled VNTR probe, and detection of hybridised DNA fragments by autoradiography(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Blotting of DNA fragments to membranes','Hybridisation using probe','Joining of DNA fragments by endonucleases','DNA fragment detection by autoradiography','NEW','12-122','12-105',NULL),
(282318,'biology','To which DNA class VNTR belongs to?','Mini satellite','The VNTR belongs to a class of satellite DNA referred to as mini-satellite(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','Junk DNA','Mini satellite','Micro satellite','Repetitive DNA','NEW','12-122','12-105',NULL),
(282319,'biology','What is the size of VNTR?','0.1 to 20 kb','The size of VNTR varies in size from 0.1 to 20 kb(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','1','mcq','0.1 to 20 kb','1.2 to 20 kb','0.1 to 22 kb','0.2 to 22kb','NEW','12-122','12-105',NULL),
(282320,'biology','Identify the missing part of the diagram (2806)','a)Paternal chromosome  b)Maternal chromosome  c)Number of short tandem repeats','Figure 6.16 (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:123)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','3','mcq','a)Maternal chromosome  b)Paternal chromosome  c)Number of short fragments','a)Paternal chromosome  b)Maternal chromosome  c)Number of short tandem repeats','a)Paternal chromosome  b)Maternal chromosome  c)Amplified segments','a)Maternal chromosome  b)Paternal chromosome  c)Short amplified fragments','NEW','12-123','12-106','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/282320q.png'),
(283125,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA Fingerprinting, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Sensitivity of DNA Fingerprinting is increased using special catalysts\n\n2) DNA from a single cell is ample to carry out DNA Fingerprinting analysis with the use of Polymerase Chain Reaction\n\n3)Monozygotic twins will show similar characteristic patterns in the bands\n\n4) VNTR is the only probe used in DNA Fingerprinting','Only 2 and 3 are correct','The sensitivity of the technique has been increased by use of polymerase chain reaction (PCR)\nThe sensitivity of the technique has been increased by use of polymerase chain reaction leading to DNA from a single cell is enough to perform DNA fingerprinting analysis.\nThese bands give a characteristic pattern for an individual DNA which differs from individual to individual in a population except in the case of monozygotic (identical) twins\nCurrently, many different probes are used to generate DNA fingerprints.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 122, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282321,'biology','Assertion: Polymorphism in DNA sequence is the basis of genetic mapping of human genome as well as of DNA fingerprinting. \n\nReason: Polymorphism (variation at genetic level) arises due to mutations.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','As polymorphism in DNA sequence is the basis of genetic mapping of human genome as well as of DNA fingerprinting, it is essential that we understand what DNA polymorphism means in simple terms. Polymorphism (variation at genetic level) arises due to mutations. If an inheritable mutation is observed in a population at high frequency, it is referred to as DNA polymorphism. There is a variety of different types of polymorphisms ranging from single nucleotide change to very large scale changes. One such example is when Alec Jeffrey used a satellite DNA as probe that shows very high degree of polymorphism. It was called as Variable Number of Tandem Repeats (VNTR) used for DNA fingerprinting.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:121, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> DNA Fingerprinting','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-121','12-104',NULL),
(282401,'biology','Which of the following statements is correct? (NEET 2020)','Adenine pairs with thymine through two h-bonds.','Adenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Thymine from opposite strand and vice-versa. Similarly, Guanine is bonded with Cytosine with three H-bonds.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 1','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Adenine pairs with thymine through two h-bonds.','Adenine pairs with thymine through one h-bond.','Adenine pairs with thymine through three h-bonds.','Adenine does not pair with thymine.','NEW','12-97','12-82',NULL),
(282402,'biology','Purines found both in DNA and RNA are (NEET 2019)','Adenine and guanine','Purines consist of double ringed nitrogenous bases such as Adenine and Guanine(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:96, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 1','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Cytosine and thymine','Adenine and thymine','Adenine and guanine','Guanine and cytosine.','NEW','12-96','12-81',NULL),
(282403,'biology','The association of histone H1 with a nucleosome indicates that (NEET 2017)','The DNA is condensed into a chromatin fibre','Histones help in packaging of DNA. In eukaryotes, DNA packaging is carried out with the help of positively charged basic proteins called histones. Histones are of five types – H1, H2A, H2B, H3 and H4. The association of H1 histone with nucleosome indicates that DNA remains in its condensed form. Nucleosome is the unit of compaction. H1 is attached over the linker DNA. The linker DNA, consisting of H1 histone connects two adjacent nucleosomes. They together consititute chromatosome. It gives rise to a chromatin fibre after further condensation.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 1','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','DNA replication is occurring','The DNA is condensed into a chromatin fibre','The DNA double helix is exposed','Transcription is occurring','NEW','12-99','12-84',NULL),
(282404,'biology','In sea urchin DNA, which is double stranded, 17% of the bases were shown to be cytosine. The percentages of the other three bases expected to be present in this DNA are (2015 Cancelled)','G=17%, A=33%, T=33%','According to Chargaff ’s rule, the amount of adenine is always equal to that of thymine and the amount of guanine is always equal to that of cytosine, i.e., A = T and G = C. Also, the purines and pyrimidines are always in equal amounts, i.e., A + G = T + C. Now, given dsDNA has 17% cytosine and hence guanine will be also 17%. So, A + T must be 66%, therefore, percentage of adenine or thymine would be 66/2 = 33%.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 1','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','G=17%, A=33%, T=33%','G=8.5%, A=50%, T=24.5%','G=34%, A=24.5%, T=24.5%','G=17%, A=16.5%, T=32.5%.','NEW','12-97','12-82',NULL),
(282405,'biology','The diagram shows an important concept in the genetic implication of DNA. Fill in the blanks A to C. [NEET 2013] (2809)','A - Transcription, b - Translation, c - Francis Crick','The expression of the genetic material occurs normally through the production of proteins. This involves two consecutive steps. These are transcription and translation. The DNA codes for the production of messenger RNA (mRNA) during transcription. Messenger RNA carries coded information to ribosomes. The ribosomes read this information and use it for protein synthesis. This process is called translation. F.H.C. Crick described this unidirectional flow of information in 1958 as the ‘central dogma of molecular biology’.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:98, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 1','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','A - Transcription, b - Translation, c - Francis Crick','A - Translation, b - Extension, c - Rosalind Franklin','A - Transcription, b - Replication, c - James Watson','A - Translation, b - Transcription, c - Ervin Chargaff','NEW','12-98','12-83','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/282405q.png'),
(282406,'biology','The experimental proof for semi-conservative replication of DNA was first shown in a (NEET 2018)','Bacterium','Semi-conservative replication of DNA was proved by the work of Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl (1958) using bacterium Escherichia coli.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;  page no:105, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 1','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Fungus','Bacterium','Plant','Virus','NEW','12-105','12-90',NULL),
(282407,'biology','The final proof for DNA as the genetic material came from the experiments of (NEET 2017)','Hershey and Chase','The unequivocal proof that DNA is the genetic material came from the experiments of Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase (1952).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:101, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 1','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Hershey and Chase','Avery, Macleod and Mccarty','Hargobind Khorana','Griffith','NEW','12-101','12-86',NULL),
(283126,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: DNA at least from two cells is required in DNA Fingerprinting\n\nReason: DNA Fingerprinting has a low sensitivity','If both Assertion and Reason are false','The sensitivity of the technique has been increased by use of polymerase chain reaction. Consequently, DNA from a single cell is enough to perform DNA fingerprinting analysis\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 122, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282408,'biology','Taylor conducted the experiments to prove semi-conservative mode of chromosome replication on (NEET II 2016)','Vicia faba','Taylor et al. (1957) conducted experiment on Vicia faba (broad bean) to prove semi-conservative replication of DNA. He fed dividing cells of root tips of Vicia faba with radioactive 3H containing thymine instead of normal thymine and found that all the chromosomes became radioactive. Labelled thymine was then replaced with normal one. Next generation came to have radioactivity in one of the two chromatids of  each  chromosome  while  in  subsequent  generation  radioactivity  was  present  in  50%  of  the  chromosomes.  This  is  possible  only  if  out  of  the  two  strands  of  a  chromosome,  one  is  formed  afresh  while  the  other  is  conserved at each replication.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:106, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 1','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Vinca rosea','Vicia faba','Drosophila melanogaster','E. coli','NEW','12-106','12-91',NULL),
(282409,'biology','A molecule that can act as a genetic material must fulfill the traits given below, except (NEET 2016)','It should be unstable structurally and chemically','Genetic material should be structurally and chemically stable otherwise its expression will change and lead to loss of several metabolic functions, etc.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:104, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 1','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','It should be able to express itself in the form of ‘mendelian characters','It should be able to generate its replica','It should be unstable structurally and chemically','It should provide the scope for slow changes that are required for evolution','NEW','12-104','12-89',NULL),
(282410,'biology','Transformation was discovered by (2014)','Griffith','Transformation was first studied by S.F. Griffith in 1928 while studying Streptococcus pneumoniae.He found that R-type non-virulent bacteria pick up virulence from heat killed virulent S-type bacteria and transform into virulent forms. It was this experiment which indicated presence of a ‘transforming principle’ which was later found out to be DNA, by Avery et al.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:101, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 1','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Meselson and Stahl','Hershey and Chase','Griffith','Watson and Crick','NEW','12-101','12-86',NULL),
(282501,'biology','Which one of the following is not applicable to RNA? (2015)','Chargaff ’s rule','Chargaff ’s rules are applicable only for double stranded DNA molecule. These are not applicable for single stranded DNA or RNA molecules. Chargaff ’s rules state that DNA helices contain equal molar ratios of A and T, G and C. This is because in a dsDNA molecule, complementary base pairing occurs between A and T, and C and G base pairs. This complementary base pairing is not possible in case of single stranded RNA molecule. Thus, Chargaff ’s rules are not applicable to RNA.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:97, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','3','mcq','Heterocyclic nitrogenous bases','Chargaff ’s rule','Complementary base pairing','5’ phosphoryl and 3’ hydroxyl ends','NEW','12-97','12-82',NULL),
(282502,'biology','During DNA replication, Okazaki fragments are used to elongate (NEET 2017)','The lagging strand away from the replication fork','Lagging strand is a replicated strand of DNA which is formed in short segments called Okazaki fragments. Its growth is discontinuous. The direction of growth of the lagging strand is 3’ → 5’ though in each Okazaki fragment it is 5’ → 3 ’(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:106, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','3','mcq','The lagging strand towards replication fork','The leading strand away from replication fork','The lagging strand away from the replication fork','The leading strand towards replication fork','NEW','12-106','12-91',NULL),
(282503,'biology','Name the enzyme that facilitates opening of DNA helix during transcription. (NEET 2020)','RNA polymerase','In a transcription unit, the activity of RNA polymerase at a given promoter is in turn regulated by interaction with accessory proteins, which affect its ability to recognise start sites(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','DNA ligase','DNA helicase','DNA polymerase','RNA polymerase','NEW','12-116','12-100',NULL),
(282504,'biology','What will be the sequence of mRNA produced by the following stretch of DNA?\n 3′ ATGCATGCATGCATG 5′- template strand, \n 5′  TACGTACGTACGTAC 3′- coding strand? \n(Odisha NEET 2019)','5′UACGUACGUACGUAC 3′','Coding strand and mRNA have same nucleotide sequence except, ‘T’ - Thymine is replaced by ‘U’ - Uracil in mRNA.The other strand which has the polarity (5\'→3\') and the sequence same as RNA (except thymine at the place of uracil), is displaced during transcription. Strangely, this strand (which does not code for anything) is referred to as coding strand(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:108, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','3′AUGCAUGCAUGCAUG5′','5′UACGUACGUACGUAC 3′','3′ UACGUACGUACGUAC 5′','5′ AUGCAUGCAUGCAUG 3′','NEW','12-108','12-93',NULL),
(282505,'biology','Match the following RNA polymerase with their transcribed products : (Odisha NEET 2019)','RNA polymerase I-rRNA,RNA polymerase II-hnRNA,RNA polymerase III-tRNA','There is a clearcut division of labour. The RNA polymerase I transcribes rRNAs (28S, 18S, and 5.8S), whereas the RNA polymerase III is responsible for transcription of tRNA, 5srRNA, and snRNAs (small nuclear RNAs). The RNA polymerase II transcribes precursor of mRNA, the heterogeneous nuclear RNA (hnRNA).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:111, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','match','RNA polymerase I,rRNA','RNA polymerase II,hnRNA','RNA polymerase III,tRNA',NULL,'NEW','12-111','12-95',NULL),
(282506,'biology','Select the correct statement. (NEET 2018)','Punnett square was developed by a british scientist.','Franklin Stahl along with Matthew Meselson proved semi-conservative mode of replication in DNA. Punnett square was developed by a British geneticist, Reginald C. Punnett. Spliceosome formation is part of post-transcriptional change in eukaryotes. Transduction was discovered by Joshua Lederberg and Norton Zinder in the bacterium Salmonella.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;  page no:105, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Franklin stahl coined the term ‘‘linkage’’','Punnett square was developed by a british scientist.','Spliceosomes take part in translation.','Transduction was discovered by S. Altman','NEW','12-106','12-91',NULL),
(282507,'biology','AGGTATCGCAT is a sequence from the coding strand of a gene. What will be the corresponding sequence of the transcribed mRNA? [NEET 2018]','AGGUAUCGCAU','Coding strand and mRNA have same nucleotide sequence except, ‘T’ - Thymine is replaced by ‘U’ - Uracil in mRNA.The other strand which has the polarity (5\'→3\') and the sequence same as RNA (except thymine at the place of uracil), is displaced during transcription. Strangely, this strand (which does not code for anything) is referred to as coding strand(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:108, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','AGGUAUCGCAU','UGGTUTCGCAT','ACCUAUGCGAU','UCCAUAGCGUA','NEW','12-108','12-93',NULL),
(282508,'biology','Which of the following RNAs should be most abundant in animal cell? (NEET 2017)','rRNA','rRNA (ribosomal RNA) is the most abundant of all types of RNA (70-88%). Hence, it will be present in highest amount. Percentage of tRNA and mRNA is 15% and  2-5%  respectively.  miRNA  (micro  RNA)  are  21-22  bp long RNA that bring degeneration of mRNA. In bacteria, there are three major types of RNAs: mRNA (messenger RNA), tRNA (transfer RNA), and rRNA (ribosomal RNA). All three RNAs are needed to synthesise a protein in a cell (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:109, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','tRNA','mRNA','mRNA','rRNA','NEW','12-109','12-94',NULL),
(282509,'biology','Spliceosomes are not found in cells of (NEET 2017)','Bacteria','Spliceosomes help in removal of introns. They will  not  occur  in  prokaryotes  because  prokaryotes  do  not  have  introns  and  thus,  processing  does  not  require  splicing of mRNA.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:109, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Fungi','Animals','Bacteria','Plants.','NEW','12-109','12-94',NULL),
(282510,'biology','The equivalent of a structural gene is (NEET II 2016)','Cistron','Cistron  (or  gene)  is  a  length  of  DNA  that  contains the information for coding a specific polypeptide chain or a functional RNA molecule (i.e., transfer RNA or ribosomal RNA). Hence, cistron is a unit of function. Currently such a gene is called structural gene. A cistron is a segment of DNA coding for a polypeptide (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:109, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Muton','Cistron','Operon','Recon.','NEW','12-109','12-94',NULL),
(282511,'biology','Which of the following rRNAs acts as structural RNA as well as ribozyme in bacteria? (NEET II 2016)','23s rRNA','23S rRNA acts as structural RNA as well as ribozyme in bacteria.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:109, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','5s rRNA','18s rRNA','23s rRNA','5.8s rRNA','NEW','12-109','12-094',NULL),
(282512,'biology','DNA-dependent RNA polymerase catalyses transcription on one strand of the DNA which is called the (NEET II 2016)','Template strand','The strand of DNA on which RNA polymerase binds to catalyse transcription is called template strand. It is also known as master or antisense strand. It has the polarity of 3′→ 5′.Parental DNA acts as template. The strand is used to synthesize new strands(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Template strand','Coding strand','Alpha strand','Antistrand.','NEW','12-97','12-82',NULL),
(282513,'biology','Select the correct option (2014)','5′ – 3′ 3′ – 5′','RNA polymerase initiates and extends the RNA (chain elongation) and functions always in 5′ to 3′ direction. The structural component of DNA has 3′to 5′ polarity. It is also called template DNA strand or antisense (–) strand.Parental DNA acts as template. The strand is used to synthesize new strands(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','3','mcq','5′ – 3′ 3′ – 5′','3′ – 5′ 5′ – 3′','5′ – 3′ 5′ – 3′','3′ – 5′ 3′ – 5′','NEW','12-97','12-82','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/282513q.png'),
(282514,'biology','If the distance between two consecutive base pairs is 0.34 nm and the total number of base pairs of a DNA double helix in a typical mammalian cell is 6.6 × 10^9 bp, then the length of the DNA is approximately (NEET 2020)','2.2 meters','Taken the distance between two consecutive base pairs as 0.34 nm (0.34×10⁻⁹ m), if the length of DNA double helix in a typical mammalian cell is calculated (simply by multiplying the total number of bp with distance between two consecutive bp, that is, 6.6 × 10⁹ bp × 0.34 × 10⁻⁹m/bp), it comes out to be approximately 2.2 metres.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','2.0 meters','2.5 meters','2.2 meters','2.7 meters.','NEW','12-099','12-084',NULL),
(282515,'biology','Under which of the following conditions there will be no change in the reading frame of following mRNA? 5’ AACAGCGGUGCUAUU 3’ (NEET 2019)','Deletion of GGU from 7th, 8th and 9th positions','Insertion or deletion of three or its multiple bases insert or delete in one or multiple codon hence one or multiple amino acids, and reading frame remains unaltered from that point onwards.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:113, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Deletion of GGU from 7th, 8th and 9th positions','Insertion of GAT 5th position','Deletion of G from 5th position','insertion of A and G at 4th and 5th position respectively.','NEW','12-113','12-097',NULL),
(282601,'biology','Which of the following features of genetic code does allow bacteria to produce human insulin by recombinant DNA technology? (NEET 2019)','Genetic code is nearly universal.','As genetic code is nearly universal means almost all organisms (from a virus, bacteria to a tree or human being) will have amino acids coded by same kind of codons as given in checkerboard. So, this property is utilised to produce human insulin using bacteria.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:111, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Genetic code is specific.','Genetic code is not ambiguous.','Genetic code is redundant.','Genetic code is nearly universal.','NEW','12-111','12-095',NULL),
(282602,'biology','If there are 999 bases in an RNA that code for a protein with 333 amino acids, and the base at position 901 is deleted such that the length of the RNA becomes 998 bases, how many codons will be altered? (NEET 2017)','33','1   codon   consists   of   3   bases.   Therefore,   a   deletion on 901 position will affect 33 codons.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:112, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','11','33','333','1','NEW','12-112','12-096',NULL),
(282603,'biology','Which one of the following is the starter codon? (NEET I 2016)','AUG','Polypeptide synthesis is signalled by two initiator codons or start codons i.e., AUG (methionine codon) and rarely by GUG (valine codon).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:112, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','3','mcq','UAA','UAG','AUG','UGA','NEW','12-112','12-096',NULL),
(282604,'biology','Which of the following is not a property of the genetic code? (Karnataka NEET 2013)','Ambiguous','Genetic code is non-ambiguous. Non-ambiguous code means that there is no ambiguity about a particular code. One codon specifies only one amino acid and not any  other.  There  are  64  codons.  Out  of  64,  3  are  stop  codons  or non-sense codons,  i.e., these do not code for any amino acid and rest 61 code for one of the 20 amino acids.  Neither  of  them  code  for  more  than  one  amino  acids except GUG which normally code for valine but in certain conditions it also codes for N-formyl methionine as initiation codon.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:111, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','3','mcq','Non-overlapping','Ambiguous','Degeneracy','Universal','NEW','12-111','12-095',NULL),
(282605,'biology','The first phase of translation is (NEET 2020)','Recognition of an anti-codon','Translation is the process of polymerisation of amino acids to form a polypeptide. The order and sequence of amino acids are defined by the sequence of bases in the mRNA. The amino acids are joined by a bond that is known as a peptide bond. Formation of a peptide bond requires energy. So, in the first phase itself amino acids are activated in the presence of ATP and linked to their cognate tRNA–a process commonly called as charging of tRNA or aminoacylation of tRNA.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:114, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Binding of mRNA to ribosome','Recognition of DNA molecule','Aminoacylation of tRNA','Recognition of an anti-codon','NEW','12-114','12-098',NULL),
(282606,'biology','Many ribosomes may associate with a single mRNA to form multiple copies of a polypeptide simultaneously. Such strings of ribosomes are termed as (NEET 2018)','Polysome','The association of many ribosomes with single mRNA leads to formation of polyribosomes or polysomes. It occurs during translation process of protein synthesis.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Polysome','Polyhedral bodies','Plastidome','Nucleosome','NEW','12-115','12-099',NULL),
(282607,'biology','A complex of ribosomes attached to a single strand of RNA is known as (NEET I 2016)','Polysome','The association of many ribosomes with single mRNA leads to formation of polyribosomes or polysomes. It occurs during translation process of protein synthesis.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:115, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Polypeptide','Okazaki fragment','Polysome','Polymer','NEW','12-115','12-099',NULL),
(282608,'biology','All of the following are part of an operon except (NEET 2018)','An enhancer','Operon concept is for prokaryotes that consist of  operator  gene,  promoter  gene,  regulator  gene  and  structural gene. Structural, operator and regulator genes are  also  present  in  eukaryotic  gene  expression  along  with  enhancer  gene  but  enhancer  gene  is  present  only  in  eukaryotic  gene  expression.  It  changes  the  rate  of  transcription of structural genes.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','An operator','Structural genes','An enhancer','A promoter','NEW','12-116','12-100',NULL),
(282609,'biology','Which of the following is required as inducer(s) for the expression of Lac operon? (NEET I 2016)','Lactose','In Lac operon, lactose is an inducer. It binds with suppressor and inactivates it. It allows RNA polymers access to the promoter and transcription proceeds(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:117, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Lactose','Lactose and galactose','Glucose','Galactose','NEW','12-117','12-101',NULL),
(282610,'biology','Gene regulation governing lactose operon of E.coli that involves the lac I gene product is (2015 Cancelled)','Negative and inducible because repressor protein prevents transcription','The control of expression of lac operon is negative (as it is turned off normally) and inducible. Inducible operon is an operon which remains switched off normally but becomes operational in the presence of  an  inducer  (lactose,  actually  allolactose  a  metabolite  of  lactose,  in  case  of  lac  operon).  The  inducible  operon  generally   functions   in   catabolic   pathways.   In   the   presence of an inducer, the repressor has a higher affinity for the inducer than for the operator gene. When lactose is added, a few lactose molecules are carried into the cell by the enzyme lactose permease as  small amount of this enzyme  is  present  in  the  cell  even  when  the  operon  is  not working. These few lactose molecules are converted into  allolactose  molecules  which  act  as  an  inducer  and  bind to the repressor (a product of regulator gene). The repressor-inducer complex fails to join with the operator gene, thus it is turned on.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:117, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Negative and repressible because repressor protein prevents transcription','Feedback inhibition because excess of b-galactosidase can switch off transcription','Positive and inducible because it can be induced by lactose','Negative and inducible because repressor protein prevents transcription','NEW','12-117','12-101',NULL),
(282611,'biology','Which one of the following is wrongly matched? (2014)','Both A and C','Transcription  is  the  process  in  living  cells  in which the genetic information of DNA is transferred to mRNA  as  first  step  of  gene  expression.  An  operon  consists  of  structural  genes,  promoter,  operator  and  regulator gene.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Transcription - writing information from DNA to trna.','Translation - using information in mrna to make protein.','Operon - structural genes, operator and promoter.','Both A and C','NEW','12-116','12-100',NULL),
(300101,'chemistry','Drugs are chemical of -------------.','Low Molecular mass','Drugs  are chemicals of low molecular masses (~100 – 500u). These interact with macromolecular targets and produce a biological response.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 447; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','High Molecular mass','High Molecular mass then Protein.','Low Molecular mass','Mass equivalent to Fatty acid','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282612,'biology','Which enzyme will be produced in a cell if there is a nonsense mutation in the lac Y gene? (NEET 2013)','β-galactosidase','A non-sense mutation is the one which stops polypeptide synthesis due to the formation of termination or non-sense codon. In lac operon, sequence of structural genes is Z, Y and A, which respectively code for b-galactosidase, lactose permease and transacetylase. If the gene Y has non-sense mutation, gene expression will stop at it, resulting in non-expression of both gene Y and successive gene A. Thus, only b-galactosidase enzyme will be produced.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Transacetylase','Lactose permease and transacetylase','β-galactosidase','Lactose permease','NEW','12-116','12-100',NULL),
(282613,'biology','In an inducible operon, the genes are (Karnataka NEET 2013)','Usually not expressed unless a signal turns them ”on”','The control of expression of lac operon is negative (as it is turned off normally) and inducible. Inducible operon is an operon which remains switched off normally but becomes operational in the presence of  an  inducer  (lactose,  actually  allolactose  a  metabolite  of  lactose,  in  case  of  lac  operon).  The  inducible  operon  generally   functions   in   catabolic   pathways.   In   the   presence of an inducer, the repressor has a higher affinity for the inducer than for the operator gene. When lactose is added, a few lactose molecules are carried into the cell by the enzyme lactose permease as  small amount of this enzyme  is  present  in  the  cell  even  when  the  operon  is  not working. These few lactose molecules are converted into  allolactose  molecules  which  act  as  an  inducer  and  bind to the repressor (a product of regulator gene). The repressor-inducer complex fails to join with the operator gene, thus it is turned on.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:117, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Usually not expressed unless a signal turns them ”on”','Usually expressed unless a signal turns them “off ”','Never expressed','Always expresser','NEW','12-117','12-101',NULL),
(282614,'biology','Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs) refers to (NEET 2019)','Genes expressed as RNA','Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs) are genes that are expressed as RNA. It is used in sequencing of human genome(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Novel DNA sequences','Genes expressed as RNA','Polypeptide expression','DNA polymorphism','NEW','12-119','12-103',NULL),
(282615,'biology','Identify the correct order of organisation of genetic material from largest to smallest. (2015)','Genome, chromosome, gene, nucleotide','In genome all the genes are contained in a single set of chromosomes. The instructions in our genome are present in the form of DNA. DNA has a complicated structure in the form of a double helix. Single strands of DNA are coiled up into structures called chromosomes. Within the chromosomes, segments of DNA are “read” together to form genes. Thus, a gene is a segment of DNA or chromosome situated at a specific locus (gene locus) which carries coded information associated with a specific function and can undergo crossing over as well as mutation. A nucleotide is the basic unit of DNA made up of a pentose sugar, phosphoric acid and a nitrogenous base.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:96, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Genome, chromosome, gene, nucleotide','Chromosome, genome, nucleotide, gene','Chromosome, gene, genome, nucleotide','Genome, chromosome, nucleotide, gene','NEW','12-096','12-081',NULL),
(282616,'biology','Satellite DNA is important because it (2015)','Shows high degree of polymorphism in population and also the same degree of polymorphism in an individual, which is heritable from parents to children','Satellite DNA is that part of repetitive DNA which has long repetitive nucleotide sequences in tandem that forms a separate fraction on density ultracentrifugation. DNA fingerprinting involves identifying differences in some specific regions in DNA sequence called as repetitive DNA, because in these sequences, a small stretch of DNA is repeated many times. These repetitive DNA sequences are separated from bulk genomic DNA as different peaks during density gradient centrifugation. The bulk DNA forms a major peak and the other small peaks are referred to as satellite DNA. Depending on base composition (A:T rich or G:C rich), length of segment, and number of repetitive units, the satellite DNA is classified into many categories, such as micro-satellites, mini-satellites, etc. These sequences normally do not code for any proteins, but they form a large portion of human genome. These sequences show high degree of polymorphism and form the basis of DNA fingerprinting. Since DNA from every tissue (such as blood, hair-follicle, skin, bone, saliva, sperm, etc.) from an individual show the same degree of polymorphism, they become very useful identification tool in forensic applications.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Does not code for proteins and is same in all members of the population','Codes for enzymes needed for DNA replication','Codes for proteins needed in cell cycle','Shows high degree of polymorphism in population and also the same degree of polymorphism in an individual, which is heritable from parents to children','NEW','12-122','12-106',NULL),
(282617,'biology','Which of the following is not required for any of the techniques of DNA fingerprinting available at present? (NEET I 2016)','Zinc finger analysis','Any small, functional, freely folded domain in which coordination of one or more zinc ions is required to stabilise its structure is known as zinc finger. The zinc finger domains are widely dispersed in eukaryotic genomes and are actively involved in sequence specific binding to DNA/RNA and contribute in protein-protein recognitions.Currently, many different probes are used to generate DNA fingerprints. 2021(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:123, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Restriction enzymes','DNA-DNA hybridisation','Polymerase chain reaction','Zinc finger analysis','NEW','12-123','12-107',NULL),
(282618,'biology','One of the most frequently used techniques in DNA fingerprinting is (Karnataka NEET 2013)','VNTR','The VNTR belongs to a class of satellite DNA referred to as mini-satellite(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','VNTR','SSCP','SCAR','AFLP','NEW','12-122','12-106',NULL),
(300102,'chemistry','Drug produce  biological response when-','It interact with macromolecular targets.','Drugs  are chemicals of low molecular masses (~100 – 500u). These interact with macromolecular targets and produce a biological response.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 447; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','It interact with micromolecular targets.','It interact with macromolecular targets.','It interact with both micro and macromolecular targets.','None of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282619,'biology','DNA fingerprinting refer to (2014)','Molecular analysis of profiles of DNA samples','DNA fingerprinting is a technique of determining nucleotide sequences of certain areas of DNA which are unique to each individual. The difference of about 0.1% or 3 × 106 base pairs (out of 3 × 109 bp) provides individuality to each human being. Human genome possesses numerous small noncoding but inheritable sequences of bases which are repeated many times. These sequences occur near telomere, centromeres, Y chromosome and heterochromatic area. The area with same sequence of bases repeated several times is called repetitive DNA. It is separated as satellite from the bulk DNA during density gradient centrifugation and hence called satellite DNA where, repetition of bases is in tandem. Satellite DNAs show polymorphism (the occurrence of mutations in a population at high frequency), which is the basis of genetic mapping of human genome as well as DNA fingerprinting. While mutations in genes produce alleles with different expressions, mutations in non-coding repetitive DNA have no immediate impact. These mutations which have piled up with time form the basis of polymorphism.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Molecular analysis of profiles of DNA samples','Analysis of DNA samples using imprinting devices','Techniques used for molecular analysis of different specimens of DNA','Techniques used for identification of fingerprints of individuals','NEW','12-122','12-106',NULL),
(282620,'biology','Select the correct match. (NEET 2018)(a) Ribozyme - Nucleic acid  (b) F2 × Recessive parent- Dihybrid cross (c) T.H. Morgan- Transduction (d) G. Mendel-Transformation','a','Ribozymes (ribonucleic acid enzymes) are RNA molecules that are capable of catalysing specific biochemical reactions similar to the action of protein enzymes.Previous discoveries by Gregor Mendel, Walter Sutton, Thomas Hunt Morgan and numerous other scientists had narrowed the search to the chromosomes located in the nucleus of most cells. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:100, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-100','12-85',NULL),
(282621,'biology','Match the following RNA polymerase with their transcribed products : (Odisha NEET 2019)','RNA polymerase I-rRNA,RNA polymerase II-hnRNA,RNA polymerase III-tRNA','There is a clearcut division of labour. The RNA polymerase I transcribes rRNAs (28S, 18S, and 5.8S), whereas the RNA polymerase III is responsible for transcription of tRNA, 5srRNA, and snRNAs (small nuclear RNAs). The RNA polymerase II transcribes precursor of mRNA, the heterogeneous nuclear RNA (hnRNA).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:111, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','match','RNA polymerase I,rRNA','RNA polymerase II,hnRNA','RNA polymerase III,tRNA',NULL,'NEW','12-108','12-093',NULL),
(282622,'biology','AGGTATCGCAT is a sequence from the coding strand of a gene. What will be the corresponding sequence of the transcribed mRNA?','AGGUAUCGCAU','Coding strand and mRNA have same nucleotide sequence except, ‘T’ - Thymine is replaced by ‘U’ - Uracil in mRNA.The other strand which has the polarity (5\'→3\') and the sequence same as RNA (except thymine at the place of uracil), is displaced during transcription. Strangely, this strand (which does not code for anything) is referred to as coding strand(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:108, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3','Molecular basis of inheritance','3','mcq','AGGUAUCGCAU','UGGTUTCGCAT','ACCUAUGCGAU','UCCAUAGCGUA','NEW','12-099','12-0',NULL),
(282623,'biology','If the distance between two consecutive base pairs is0.34 nm and the total number of base pairs of a DNA double helix in a typical mammalian cell is 6.6 × 109 bp, then the length of the DNA is approximately','2.2 meters','Taken the distance between two consecutive base pairs as 0.34 nm (0.34×10⁻⁹ m), if the length of DNA double helix in a typical mammalian cell is calculated (simply by multiplying the total number of bp with distance between two consecutive bp, that is, 6.6 × 10⁹ bp × 0.34 × 10⁻⁹m/bp), it comes out to be approximately 2.2 metres.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'3','mcq','2.0 meters','2.5 meters','2.2 meters','2.7 meters','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282624,'biology','Under which of the following conditions there will be no change in the reading frame of following mRNA? 5’ AACAGCGGUGCUAUU 3’','Deletion of GGU from 7th, 8th and 9th positions','Insertion or deletion of three or its multiple bases insert or delete in one or multiple codon hence one or multiple amino acids, and reading frame remains unaltered from that point onwards.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:113, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Deletion of GGU from 7th, 8th and 9th positions','Insertion of G at 5th position','Deletion of G from 5th position','Insertion of A and G at 4th and 5th position respectively','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282625,'biology','Which of the following features of genetic code does allow bacteria to produce human insulin by recombinant DNA technology?','Genetic code is nearly universal','As genetic code is nearly universal means almost all organisms (from a virus, bacteria to a tree or human being) will have amino acids coded by same kind of codons as given in checkerboard. So, this property is utilised to produce human insulin using bacteria.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:111, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Genetic code is specific','Genetic code is not ambiguous','Genetic code is redundant','Genetic code is nearly universal','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282626,'biology','The first phase of translation is (NEET 2020)','Aminoacylation of tRNA','Translation is the process of polymerisation of amino acids to form a polypeptide. The order and sequence of amino acids are defined by the sequence of bases in the mRNA. The amino acids are joined by a bond that is known as a peptide bond. Formation of a peptide bond requires energy. So, in the first phase itself amino acids are activated in the presence of ATP and linked to their cognate tRNA–a process commonly called as charging of tRNA or aminoacylation of tRNA(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:, paragraph no:)Translation is the process of polymerisation of amino acids to form a polypeptide. The order and sequence of amino acids are defined by the sequence of bases in the mRNA. The amino acids are joined by a bond that is known as a peptide bond. Formation of a peptide bond requires energy. So, in the first phase itself amino acids are activated in the presence of ATP and linked to their cognate tRNA–a process commonly called as charging of tRNA or aminoacylation of tRNA.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:114, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Binding of mRNA to ribosome','Recognition of DNA molecule','Aminoacylation of tRNA','Recognition of an anti-codon','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282627,'biology','Many ribosomes may associate with a single mRNA to form multiple copies of a polypeptide simultaneously.Such strings of ribosomes are termed as (NEET 2018)','Polysome','The association of many ribosomes with single mRNA leads to formation of polyribosomes or polysomes. It occurs during translation process of protein synthesis.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:, paragraph no:)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 3',NULL,'2','mcq','Polysome','Polyhedral bodies','Plastidome','Nucleosome.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282701,'biology','Match the following genes of the Lac operon with their respective products. (NEET 2019)','Lac operon repressor-i gene,Beta galactosidase-z gene,Transacetylase-a gene,Permease-y gene','The i gene codes for the repressor of the lac operon. The z gene codes for beta galactosidase (b-gal), which is primarily responsible for the hydrolysis of the disaccharide, lactose into its monomeric units, galactose and glucose. The y gene codes for permease, which increases permeability of the cell to b galactosides. The a gene encodes a transacetylase.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12;page no:117, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','match','Lac operon repressor,i gene','Beta galactosidase,z gene','Transacetylase,a gene','Permease-y gene','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282702,'biology','Select the correct match. \n(a) Alec Jeffreys - Streptococcus pneumoniae \n(b) Alfred Hershey and - TMV Martha Chase \n(c) Matthew Meselson - Pisum sativum and F. Stahl \n(d) Francois Jacob and - Lac operon Jacques Monod','d','Alec Jeffreys (1984) invented the DNA fingerprinting technique. Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase proved that DNA is genetic material using T2bacteriophages in an experiment. Matthew Meselson and F. Stahl proved semiconservative mode of replication in bacterium E. coli.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:105, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282703,'biology','All of the following are part of an operon except (NEET 2018)','an enhancer','Operon concept is for prokaryotes that consist of  operator  gene,  promoter  gene,  regulator  gene  and  structural gene. Structural, operator and regulator genes are  also  present  in  eukaryotic  gene  expression  along  with  enhancer  gene  but  enhancer  gene  is  present  only  in  eukaryotic  gene  expression.  It  changes  the  rate  of  transcription of structural genes.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:116, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','an operator','structural genes','an enhancer','a promoter.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282704,'biology','Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs) refers to','genes expressed as RNA','Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs) are genes that are expressed as RNA. It is used in sequencing of human genome(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:119, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','novel DNA sequences','genes expressed as RNA','polypeptide expression','DNA polymorphism','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282705,'biology','Which is the "Only enzyme" that has "Capability" to catalyse Initiation, Elongation and Termination in the process of transcription in prokaryotes? (NEET 2021)','DNA dependent RNA polymerase','The DNA-dependent RNA polymerase (DdRP or RNAP) is an essential enzyme of transcription of replicating systems of prokaryotic and eukaryotic organisms as well as cytoplasmic DNA viruses. Since the two strands have opposite polarity and the DNA-dependent RNA polymerase also catalyse the polymerisation in only one direction, that is, 5\'→3\', the strand that has the polarity 3\'→5\' acts as a template(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:108, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','DNase','DNA dependent DNA polymerase','DNA dependent RNA polymerase','DNA Ligase','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282706,'biology','Complete the flow chart on central dogma(NEET 2021).  \nDNA---(a)--DNA----(b)-----> mRNA------(c)-----> (d)','(a)-Replication; (b)-Transcription; (c)-Translation; (d)-Protein','The expression of the genetic material occurs normally through the production of proteins. This involves two consecutive steps. These are transcription and translation. The DNA codes for the production of messenger RNA (mRNA) during transcription. Messenger RNA carries coded information to ribosomes. The ribosomes read this information and use it for protein synthesis. This process is called translation. F.H.C. Crick described this unidirectional flow of information in 1958 as the ‘central dogma of molecular biology’.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:98, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(a)-Transduction; (b)-Translation; (c)-Replication; (d)-Protein','(a)-Replication; (b)-Transcription; (c)-Transduction; (d)-Protein','(a)-Translation; (b)-Replication; (c)-Transcription;(d)-Transduction','(a)-Replication; (b)-Transcription; (c)-Translation; (d)-Protein','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282707,'biology','What is the role of RNA polymerase III in the process of transcription in eukaryotes?(NEET 2021)','Transcribes tRNA, 5s rRNA and snRNA','In eukaryote cells, RNA polymerase III (also called Pol III) transcribes DNA to synthesize ribosomal 5S rRNA, tRNA and other small RNAs. The genes transcribed by RNA Pol III fall in the category of "housekeeping" genes whose expression is required in all cell types and most environmental conditions(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:111, paragraph no:1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','Transcribes only snRNAs','Transcribes rRNAs (28S, 18S and 5.8S)','Transcribes tRNA, 5s rRNA and snRNA','Transcribes precursor of mRNA','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282708,'biology','DNA fingerprinting involves identifying differences in some specific regions in DNA sequence, called as (NEET 2021)','Repetitive DNA','DNA fingerprinting is a technique of determining nucleotide sequences of certain areas of DNA which are unique to each individual. The difference of about 0.1% or 3 × 106 base pairs (out of 3 × 109 bp) provides individuality to each human being. Human genome possesses numerous small noncoding but inheritable sequences of bases which are repeated many times. These sequences occur near telomere, centromeres, Y chromosome and heterochromatic area. The area with same sequence of bases repeated several times is called repetitive DNA. It is separated as satellite from the bulk DNA during density gradient centrifugation and hence called satellite DNA where, repetition of bases is in tandem. Satellite DNAs show polymorphism (the occurrence of mutations in a population at high frequency), which is the basis of genetic mapping of human genome as well as DNA fingerprinting. While mutations in genes produce alleles with different expressions, mutations in non-coding repetitive DNA have no immediate impact. These mutations which have piled up with time form the basis of polymorphism.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:122, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','Polymorphic DNA','Satellite DNA','Repetitive DNA','Single nucleotides','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282709,'biology','Identify the correct statement. (NEET 2021)','RNA polymerase binds with Rho factor to terminate the process of transcription in bacteria','In the cell, Rho binds to untranslated naked RNAs and terminates the synthesis of mRNA at the end of a significant number of operons.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:, paragraph no:)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','Split gene arrangement is characteristic of prokaryotes','In capping, methyl guanosine triphosphate is added to the 3\' end of hnRNA','RNA polymerase binds with Rho factor to terminate the process of transcription in bacteria','The coding strand in a transcription unit is copied to an mRNA','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282710,'biology','If Adenine makes 30% of the DNA molecule, what will be the percentage of Thymine, Guanine and Cytosine in it?  (NEET 2021)','T : 30 ; G : 20 ; C : 20','According to Chargaff ’s rule, the amount of adenine is always equal to that of thymine and the amount of guanine is always equal to that of cytosine, i.e., A = T and G = C. Also, the purines and pyrimidines are always in equal amounts, i.e., A + G = T + C. Now, given dsDNA has 17% cytosine and hence guanine will be also 17%. So, A + T must be 66%, therefore, percentage of adenine or thymine would be 66/2 = 33%.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','T : 20 ; G : 25 ; C : 25','T : 20 ; G : 30 ; C : 20','T : 20 ; G : 20 ; C : 30','T : 30 ; G : 20 ; C : 20','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282711,'biology','Which of the following RNAs is not required for the synthesis of protein? (NEET 2021)','siRNA','Ribosomal RNA (rRNA) associates with a set of proteins to form ribosomes. These complex structures, which physically move along an mRNA molecule, catalyze the assembly of amino acids into protein chains. They also bind tRNAs and various accessory molecules necessary for protein synthesis(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','siRNA','mRNA','tRNA','rRNA','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282712,'biology','Statement I: The codon \'AUG\' codes for methionine and phenylalanine. \nStatement II: \'AAA\' and \'AAG\' both codons code for the amino acid lysine. \nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.(NEET 2021)','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','AUG has dual functions. It codes for Methionine (met) , and it also act as initiator codon. AAA\' and \'AAG\' both codons code for the amino acid lysine (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:112, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282713,'biology','Which one of the following statements about Histones is wrong ?(NEET 2021)','The pH of histones is slightly acidic','Histones are rich in the basic amino acid residues lysine and arginine. Both the amino acid residues carry positive charges in their side chains(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','Histones carry positive charge in the side chain','Histones are organized to form a unit of 8 molecules','The pH of histones is slightly acidic','Histones are rich in amino acids - Lysine and Arginine','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282714,'biology','The process of translation of mRNA to proteins begins as soon as :','The small subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA','The process of translation of mRNA to proteins begins as soon as the small subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','The small subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA','The larger subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA','Both the subunits join together to bind with mRNA','The tRNA is activated and the larger subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282715,'biology','DNA polymorphism forms the basis of :','Both genetic mapping and DNA finger printing','DNA polymorphism forms the basis of both DNA fingerprinting and genetic mapping, on a wide level of applications.','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','Genetic mapping','DNA finger printing','Both genetic mapping and DNA finger printing','Translation','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282716,'biology','Read the following statements and choose the set of correct statements :\n(a) Euchromatin is loosely packed chromatin\n(b) Heterochromatin is transcriptionally active\n(c) Histone octamer is wrapped by negatively charged DNA in nucleosome\n(d) Histones are rich in lysine and arginine\n(e) A typical nucleosome contains 400 bp of DNA helix\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :','(a), (c), (d) Only','Euchromatin is loosely packaged to enable easy transcription and expression; Histone octamer is wrapped by negatively charged DNA(200 base pairs) in nucleosome to enable effective packaging, and the histone proteins are rich in basic amino acids like lysine and arginine;\nHeterochromatin is transcriptionally inactive,refer with the diagram for better understanding. diagram (e2820)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(b), (d), (e) Only','(a), (c), (d) Only','(b), (e) Only','(a), (c), (e) Only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282717,'biology','If a geneticist uses the blind approach for sequencing the whole genome of an organism, followed by \nassignment of function to different segments, the methodology adopted by him is called as :','Sequence annotation','This blind approach involved sequencing the whole genome (coding and non-coding) and later assigning functions to the different regions, so it saves time and sequences a lot of DNA content in the same procedure','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','Sequence annotation','Gene mapping','Expressed sequence tags','Bioinformatics','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282718,'biology','In an E. Coli strain i gene gets mutated and its product can not bind the inducer molecule. If growth medium \nis provided with lactose, what will be the outcome?','z, y, a genes will not be translated','In an E. Coli strain i gene gets mutated and its product can not bind the inducer molecule. If growth medium \nis provided with lactose, lactose is an inhibitor in gene regulation z, y, a genes will not be translated','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','Only z gene will get transcribed','z, y, a genes will be transcribed','z, y, a genes will not be translated','RNA polymerase will bind the promoter region','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282719,'biology','The recombination frequency between the genes a  and  c is 5%, b  and  c is 15%, b  and  d is 9%, a  and  b is 20%, c  and  \nd is 24% and a  and  d is 29%. What will be the sequence of these genes on a linear chromosome?','a, c, b, d','1% recombination = 1 Centi Morgan\nTo place the genes on a linear chromosome start adding them from the lowest recombination, \na-c =5%,\nb-c =15%,\nb-d=9%,\na-b=20%,\nc-d= 24%,\na-d=29%,\nStart with the lowest, so a and c;','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','a, d, b, c','d, b, a, c','a, c, b, d','a, c, b, d','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282720,'biology','In lac operon, z gene codes for : [ RE NEET 2022]','(1) β-galactosidase','The Z gene codes for β-galactosidase, which is primarily responsible for the hydrolysis of the disaccharide lactose into its monomeric units, galactose and glucose. The Y gene codes for permease, which increases permeability of the cell to β-galactosides. The A gene encodes a transacetylase.','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) β-galactosidase','(2) Permease','(3) Repressor','(4) Transacetylase','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282721,'biology','Choose the CORRECT answer from the options given below : [ RE NEET 2022 ]  \na. lac I, b. lac Z, c. lac Y d. lac A           (i) transacetylase  (ii) Permease (iii) Beta galactosidase ( iv) repressor','(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)','The lac operon consists of one regulatory gene (the I gene) and three structural genes (Z, Y, and A). The I gene codes for the repressor of the lac operon. The Z gene codes for β-galactosidase, which is primarily responsible for the hydrolysis of the disaccharide lactose into its monomeric units, galactose and glucose. The Y gene codes for permease, which increases permeability of the cell to β-galactosides. The A gene encodes a transacetylase.','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(a) - (iv), (b) - (i), (c) - (iii), (d) - (ii)','(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)','(a) - (i), (b) - (ii), (c) - (iii), (d) - (iv)','(a) - (ii), (b) - (iii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282722,'biology','Given below are two statements : \n Statement I :\n DNA polymerases catalyse polymerisation only in one direction, that is 5\' —→ 3\' \nStatement II : \n During replication of DNA, on one strand the replication is continuous while on other strand it is discontinuous. \n In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :','(1) Both Statement I and Statement II are \ncorrect','The DNA dependent DNA polymerases catalyse polymerization in 5\' → 3\' direction. The replication is continuous on template strand with polarity 3′→5′ . It is known as leading daughter strand. On the other template strand with polarity 5′→3′, replication is discontinuous and this daughter strand is called lagging daughter strand.','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) Both Statement I and Statement II are \ncorrect','(2) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','(3) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','(4) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282723,'biology','With reference to Hershey and Chase experiments. \nSelect the correct statements. \n (A) Viruses grown in the presence of radioactive phosphorus contained radioactive DNA. \n (B) Viruses grown on radioactive sulphur contained \nradioactive proteins. \n (C) Viruses grown on radioactive phosphorus \ncontained radioactive protein. \n (D) Viruses grown on radioactive sulphur contained \nradioactive DNA. \n (E) Viruses grown on radioactive protein contained \nradioactive DNA. \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the \noptions given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) (A) and (B) only','The unequivocal proof that DNA is the genetic material came from the experiments of Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase (1952). They worked with viruses that infect bacteria called bacteriophages. They worked to discover whether it was protein or DNA from the virus that entered the bacteria. They grew some viruses on a medium that contained radioactive phosphorus and some others on a medium that contained sulfur. Viruses grown in the presence of radioactive phosphorus contained radioactive DNA but not radioactive protein because DNA contains phosphorus but protein does not. Similarly, viruses grown on radioactive sulfur contained radioactive protein but not radioactive DNA because DNA does not contain sulfur.','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(2) (A) and (B) only','(1) (D) and (E) only','(4) (B) and (D) only','(3) (A) and (C) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282724,'biology','Which one of the following acts as an inducer for lac operon ? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) Lactose','Lactose is required as inducer for the expression of Lac operon.','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) Sucrose','(2) Lactose','(3) Glucose','(4) Galactose','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282725,'biology','Given below are two statements : \n Statement I : \n RNA being unstable, mutate at a faster rate. \n Statement II : \n RNA can directly code for synthesis of proteins hence can easily express the characters. \n In the light of the above statements, choose the \ncorrect answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both DNA and RNA can mutate.\nRNA being unstable mutates at a faster rate\nRNA is also known to be catalytic, hence reactive.\nThe presence of thymine at the place of uracil confers additional stability to DNA.','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are true','(1) Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','(2) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282726,'biology','Which scientist conducted an experiment with ³²P\nand ³⁵S labelled phages for demonstrating that DNA is the genetic material ?','(2) A. D Hershey and M.J. Chase','Alfred D. Hershey and Martha Chase (1952) used radioactive phosphorus (32P) and radioactive sulfur (35S) and showed that when bacteriophage T2 infects its host cell, Escherichia coli, the phosphorus-containing DNA of the viral particle, not the sulfur-containing protein of the viral coat, enters the host cell and serves the genetic information for viral replication','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) James D. Watson and F.H. C. Crick','(2) A. D Hershey and M.J. Chase','(3) F. Griffith','(4) O.T. Avery, C.M. MacLeod and M. McCarty','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282727,'biology','Given below are two statements : \n Statement I : \n The process of copying genetic information from one strand of the DNA into RNA is termed as transcription. \n Statement II : \n A transcription unit in DNA is defined primarily by the three regions in the DNA i.e., a promotor, the structural gene and a terminator. \n In the light of the above statements, choose the \ncorrect answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Statement I: The process of copying genetic information from one strand of DNA into RNA is indeed termed as transcription. During transcription, an RNA molecule is synthesized based on the sequence of the DNA template strand.\n\nStatement II: A transcription unit in DNA is defined primarily by the three regions mentioned: a promoter, the structural gene, and a terminator. The promoter region is responsible for initiating transcription by binding to RNA polymerase. The structural gene contains the coding sequence for the RNA molecule being transcribed. The terminator region signals the end of transcription.\n\nBoth statements are true and accurately describe the process of transcription and the components of a transcription unit. Therefore, the correct option is 3: Both statement I and statement II are true.','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) Statement I is true but Statement II is false','(2) Statement I is false but Statement II is true','(3) Both Statement I and Statement II are true','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are false','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282728,'biology','Which one of the following is the sequence on corresponding coding strand, if the sequence on mRNA formed is as follows 5’AUCGAUCGAUCGAUCGAUCGAUCG AUCG 3’? [ NEET 2023]','(2) 5’ ATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCG 3’','(2) 5’ ATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCG 3’','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) 3’ UAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGC 5’','(2) 5’ ATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCG 3’','(3) 3’ ATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCGATCG 5’','(4) 5’ UAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGC 3’','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282729,'biology','Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: In prokaryotes, the positively charged DNA is held with some negatively charged proteins in a\nregion called nucleoid.\nStatement II: In eukaryotes, the negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone\noctamer to form nucleosome.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023 ]','(3) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','Statement I is incorrect. In prokaryotes, there is no well-defined nucleus or nucleosomes as found in eukaryotes. The DNA in prokaryotes is located in a region called the nucleoid, but it is not associated with histones or proteins in the same way as eukaryotic DNA.\nStatement II is true. In eukaryotes, the negatively charged DNA is indeed wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form nucleosomes, which are the basic units of chromatin organization.','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','(2) Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.','(3) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true.','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282730,'biology','Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: RNA mutates at a faster rate.\nStatement II: Viruses having RNA genome and shorter life span mutate and evolve faster.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','RNA mutates as faster rate as it is an unstable genetic material found in certain virus.','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) Both Statement I and Statement II are false.','(2) Statement I is true but Statement II is false.','(3) Statement I is false but Statement II is true.','(4) Both Statement I and Statement II are true.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282731,'biology','Against the codon 5\' UAC 3\', what would be the sequence of anticodon on tRNA ? [ RE NEET 2022]','(4) 5\' GUA 3\'','5\' GUA 3\'','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) 5\' AUG 3\'','(2) 5\' ATG 3\'','(3) 5\' GTA 3\'','(4) 5\' GUA 3\'','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282732,'biology','Match List I with List II.\nList I - List II\nA. Gene ‘a’- I. β-galactosidase\nB. Gene ‘y’ - II. Transacetylase\nC. Gene ‘i’ - III. Permease\nD. Gene ‘z’ - IV. Repressor protein\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below: [ NEET 2023]','(1) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I','A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Questions 4',NULL,'2','mcq','(1) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I','(2) A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II','(3) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','(4) A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282801,'biology','Which one of the following represents a palindromic sequence in DNA? [2014]','5\' - GAATTC - 3\'\n3\' - CTTAAG - 5\'','A palindromic sequence is a nucleic acid sequence (DNA or RNA) that is the same\nwhether read 5\' (five-prime) to 3\' (three prime) on one strand or 5\' to 3\' on the\ncomplementary strand with which it forms a double helix.\n5 - GAATTC - 3\n3 - CTTAAG - 5\nIt is a palindromic sequence of DNA cut by\nrestriction enzyme EcoRI.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','5\' - GAATTC - 3\'\n3\' - CTTAAG - 5\'','5\' - CCAATG - 3\'\n3\' - GAATCC - 5\'','5\' - CATTAG - 3\'\n3\' - GATAAC - 5\'','5\' - GATACC - 3\'\n3\' - CCTAAG - 5\'','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282802,'biology','There are three genes a, b, c. Percentage of crossing over between a and b is 20%, b and c is 28% and a and c is 8%. What is the sequence of genes on chromosome? [2015]','b, a, c','The salient features of genetic code are as follows:(i) The codon is triplet. 61 codons code for amino acids and 3 codons do not code for any amino acids, hence they function as stop codons;(ii) Some amino acids are coded by more than one codon, hence the code is degenerate; (iii) The codon is read in mRNA in a contiguous fashion. There are no punctuations; (iv) The code is nearly universal: for example, from bacteria to human UUU would code for Phenylalanine (phe). Some exceptions to this rule have been found in mitochondrial codons, and in some protozoans; (v) AUG has dual functions. It codes for Methionine (met) , and italso act as initiator codon; (vi) UAA, UAG, UGA are stop terminator codons. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:112, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','b, a, c','a, b, c','a, c, b','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282803,'biology','Which one of the following group of codons is called as degenerate codons? [2016]','UAA, UAG and UGA','Degenerate codons (also called as non - sense codons or terminator codons) do not code for any amino acids. Three types of degenerate codons are UAG (amber), UAA (ochre) and UGA (opal).(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:112, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','UAA, UAG and UGA','GUA, GUG, GCA, GCG and GAA','UUC, UUG, CCU, CAA and CUG','UUA, UUG, CUU, CUC, CUA and CUG','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282804,'biology','The given figure shows the structure of nucleosome with their parts labelled as A, B and C. Identify A, B and C. [2017] Refer to the diagram (2807)','A – DNA; B – H1 histone; C – Histone octamer','Nucleosome is a structural unit of a\neukaryotic chromosome which consists of\na length of DNA coiled around a core of\nhistones and are thought to be present only\nduring interphase of the cell cycle. In the\ngiven figure of nucleosome structure, the\nparts marked as A, B and C are respectively\nDNA, H1 histones and histone octamer.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A – DNA; B – H1 histone; C – Histone octamer','A – H1 histone; B – DNA; C – Histone octamer','A – Histone octamer; B – RNA; C – H1 histone','A – RNA; B – H1 histone; C – Histone octamer','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/282804q.png'),
(300103,'chemistry','Consider the following statements.\nI. Drugs are chemicals of low molecular mass (~100-500 u) \nII. Chemicals use for therapeutic effect is called chemotherapy.\nIII. Analgesics kill or arrest the growth of micro-organisms.\nThe correct statements is:','I and II','Drugs  are chemicals of low molecular masses (~100 – 500u). These interact with macromolecular targets and produce a biological response.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 447; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','I and III','I and II','I only','III only','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300104,'chemistry','Use of Chemical for Therapeutic effect is called __________.','Chemotherapy','Use of chemicals for therapeutic effect is called  chemotherapy.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 447; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','medicine','Chemotherapy','Physiotherapy','Chemogenesis','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282805,'biology','Match the codons given incolumn I with their respective amino acids given in column II and choose the correct answer. [2017] Refer to the diagram (2808)','A – III; B – IV; C – I; D – V; E – II','The salient features of genetic code are as follows:(i) The codon is triplet. 61 codons code for amino acids and 3 codons do not code for any amino acids, hence they function as stop codons;(ii) Some amino acids are coded by more  than one codon, hence the code is degenerate; (iii) The codon is read in mRNA in a contiguous fashion. There are no punctuations; (iv) The code is nearly universal: for example, from bacteria to human UUU would code for Phenylalanine (phe). Some exceptions to this rule have been found in mitochondrial codons, and in some protozoans; (v) AUG has dual functions. It codes for Methionine (met) , and italso act as initiator codon; (vi) UAA, UAG, UGA are stop terminator codons. (Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:112, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A – III; B – IV; C – I; D – V; E – II','A – III; B – I; C – IV; D – V; E – II','A – III; B – IV; C – V; D – I; E – II','A – II; B – IV; C – I; D – V; E – III','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/282805q.png'),
(282806,'biology','Assertion : DNA is associated with proteins. \n\nReason : DNA binds around histone proteins that form a pool and the entire structure is called a nucleosome. [2013]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','A chain of DNA has 140 base pairs, make 1.75 turns and twist around a histone octamer forming nucleosome. The core of nucleosome consists of 4 histones H2A, H2B, H3 and H4.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282807,'biology','Assertion : The uptake of DNA during transformation is an active, energy requiring process. \n\nReason : Transformation occurs in only those bacteria, which possess the enzymatic machinery involved in the active uptake and recombination [2014]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Transformation does not involve passive\nentry of DNA molecules through permeable\ncell walls and membranes. It does not occur\n\'naturally\' in all species of bacteria, only in\nthose species possessing the enzymatic\nmachinery involved in the active uptake\nand recombination processes. Even in\nthese species, all cells in a given population\nare not capable of active uptake of DNA.\nOnly competent cells, which possess a so\ncalled competence factor are capable of\nserving as recipients in transformation.Transformation was first studied by S.F. Griffith in 1928 while studying Streptococcus pneumoniae.He found that R-type non-virulent bacteria pick up virulence from heat killed virulent S-type bacteria and transform into virulent forms. It was this experiment which indicated presence of a ‘transforming principle’ which was later found out to be DNA, by Avery et al.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:101, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282808,'biology','Assertion : UAA, UAG and UGA terminate protein synthesis. \n\nReason : They are not recognised by tRNA. [2011, 2014]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Synthesis of polypeptide terminates when\na nonsense codon of mRNA reaches the\nA– site. There are three nonsense codons–\nUAA, UAG and UGA. These codons are\nnot recognised by any of the tRNAs.\nTherefore, no more aminoacyl tRNA\nreaches the A site. The P– site tRNA is\nhydrolysed and the completed polypeptide\nis released in the presence of a release\nfactor.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:112, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282809,'biology','Assertion : In a DNA molecule, A–T rich parts melt before G–C rich parts. \n\nReason : In between A and T there are three H–bond, whereas in between G and C there are two H-bonds. [2010, 2015]','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','In a DNA molecule, A-T rich parts melt\nbefore G-C rich parts because there are two\nH-bond between A and T whereas in\nbetween G and C, there are three H-bond.(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:97, paragraph no:5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282810,'biology','Assertion : Replication and transcription occur in the nucleus but translation takes place in the cytoplasm. \n\nReason : mRNA is transferred from the nucleus into cytoplasm where ribosomes and amino acids are available for protein synthesis. [2008, 2015, 2017]','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','In eukaryotes, the replication and transcription takes place in the nucleus. mRNA comes out from the nucleus through\nnuclear pore. In cytoplasm, translation occurs. In prokaryote, there is no nuclear membrane, so replication, transcription and translation all occur in the cytoplasm. In prokaryotes, such as, E. coli, though they do not have a defined nucleus, the DNA is not scattered throughout the cell(Reference  :NCERT Biology Class 12; page no:99, paragraph no:2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Previous Year Aiims Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.','Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.','The Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','Both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300105,'chemistry','Most of the drug used as medicine are ______ if taken in higher doses.','poison','Most of the drugs used as medicines are potential poisons, if taken in doses higher than those recommended. Use of chemicals for therapeutic effect is called  chemotherapy.  (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 447; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','sedative','poison','cyanides','hypnotics','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282901,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA and choose the correct options.\n\n1) Length of DNA is indicated in terms of number of base pairs existing in the DNA. \n\n2) DNA is made up of multiple chains of ribonucleotides held by hydrogen bonds.\n\n3) Bacteriophage lambda has 48502 base pairs which is 24251 nucleotides. \n\n4) Cytosine is common for both DNA and RNA','1 and 4 only are correct','Length of DNA is measured in terms of base pairs and referred to the number of nucleotides in the DNA. It can also be described in the number of pairs of nucleotides or number of base pairs. DNA’s polymer is deoxyribonucleotides while ribonucleotides are polymers of RNA. 1 base pair equals to 2 nucleotides therefore 48502 base pairs should have 97004 nucleotides. Cytosine is found in both DNA and RNA. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, page 96, paragraph 2 and 3 )','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','1 and 2 only are correct','2 and 3 only are correct','1,2 and 3 only are correct','1 and 4 only are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282902,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA and choose the correct statements: \n\n1) Nucleotide is made up of two components which are pentose sugar and phosphorus group.\n\n2) In DNA, Purines include Adenosine and Guanosine only while Pyrimidines include Cytosine and Thymine only. \n\n3) A nitrogenous base is linked to - OH of 1\' C pentose sugar through an N-glycosidic linkage to form a nucleoside. \n\n4) Two nucleotides are linked through 5’-3’phosphoester linkage to form a dinucleotide.','Only 2 is correct','Nucleotide is made up of three components which are nitrogenous base, pentose sugar and phosphate group. It should be phosphate group instead of phosphorus group.\n•  Pyrimidines contain uracil which can be found in RNA only \n•  A phosphate group is linked to OH of 5\' C of a nucleoside through phosphoester linkage.\n•  Two nucleotides are linked through 3\'-5\' phosphodiester linkage to form a dinucleotide. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, page 96, paragraph 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 2 is correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 1 and 3 are correct','Only 1 and 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282903,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion: Uridine is a nucleoside formed in RNA while deoxyuridine is a nucleoside formed in DNA.\n\nReason: Uracil is linked with ribose sugar in RNA to form Uridine while when linked with deoxyribose sugar in DNA it forms deoxyuridine.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','There is no deoxyuridine. Uridine only presents in RNA while the nitrogenous base in DNA that replaces it in DNA is Thymine so the nucleoside formed should be deoxythymidine.\n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, page 96, paragraph 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282904,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason and choose the correct option.  \n\nAssertion: A DNA polymer formed has a free phosphate moiety at 5’ end of the pentose sugar. \n\nReason: A phosphate group is linked to OH of 5\' C of a nucleoside through phosphoester linkage','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Phosphate group at 5’end can form ester bond with free hydroxyl group on 3’ carbon of next nucleotide\n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, Page 96, Paragraph 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282905,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA and choose the correct options. \n\n1) The Double Helix model is proposed by Maurice Wilkins and Rosalind Franklin.\n\n2) The ratio of Uracil and Adenosine in RNA is not constant.\n\n3) The backbone of a polynucleotide chain is formed by sugar and phosphate\n\n4) The nitrogenous bases linked to sugar moiety project from the backbone','Only 3 and 4 are correct','The people who proposed the Double Helix model are James Watson and Francis Crick. \nUracil is base-paired with Adenosine in RNA therefore they must have the same amount. \nThe backbone of a polynucleotide chain is formed due to sugar and phosphates.\nThe nitrogenous bases linked to sugar moiety project from the backbone\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, Page 97, Paragraph 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 3 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282906,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA structure and choose the correct statements.\n \n1) In DNA, every nucleotide residue has an additional –OH group present at 2\' -position in the deoxyribose.\n\n2) Base sequences can be predicted easily by knowing the sequence of one of the strands. \n\n3) The number of H-bonds formed between base pairs is two. \n\n4) The backbone is constituted by sugar-phosphate, and the bases project inside.','Only 2 and 4 are correct','The presence of additional -OH group at 2’ position occurs in ribose of RNA not deoxyribose and DNA. \nThe base pairs are complementary to each other therefore yes they can be predicted easily as A always to T/U and G to C.\nAdenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Thymine from opposite strand and vice-versa. Similarly, Guanine is bonded with Cytosine with three H-bonds.the backbone is constituted by sugar-phosphate, and the bases project inside.\nThe backbone is constituted by sugar-phosphate, and the bases project inside.\n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, Page 97, Paragraph 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300106,'chemistry','What are medicine?','Drug which produces therapeutic and usefull effects','When therapeutic response of drugs are useful then they called Medicine.  (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 447; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Drug which produce many side effcts','Drug which use for addiction','Drug which produces therapeutic and usefull effects','None of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300107,'chemistry','Drug commonly interact with','All the above','Drug interact with macromolecular targets and produce a biological response.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 447; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','DNA','Carbohydrates','Protein','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(282907,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Polynucleotide Chain, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The distance between a bp in a helix is approximately 0.34 nm.\n\n2) The DNA is made up of two polynucleotide chains\n\n3) Purine will pair with purine while pyrimidine pairs with another pyrimidine\n\n4) Adenine forms 3 H-bonds with its respective base pair from opposite strand','Only 1 and 2 are correct','The pitch of the helix is 3.4 nm and there are roughly 10 bp in each turn. Consequently, the distance between a bp in a helix is approximately 0.34 nm\nOne of the salient features of the Double-helix structure of DNA is it is made of two polynucleotide chains, where the backbone is constituted by sugar-phosphate, and the bases project inside\nAlways a purine comes opposite to a pyrimidine. This generates approximately uniform distance between the two strands of the helix\nAdenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Thymine from opposite strand and vice-versa. Similarly, Guanine is bonded with Cytosine with three H-bonds.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, Page 97, Paragraph 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282908,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason and choose the correct option.  \n\nAssertion: The new DNA molecule produced will be identical to the parental DNA  \n\nReason: Parental DNA will break and replicate both it strands to form a completely new daughter DNA before joining back.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The new DNA molecule is genetically similar to parental DNA because each strand of parental DNA acts as a template for synthesis of daughter strand. But, the parental strands will form a new DNA with the complementary daughter strand instead of the previous complementary parental strand.\n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12 , Chapter 6, Page 97, Paragraph 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282909,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason and choose the correct option.  \n\nAssertion: The two chains of DNA has anti-parallel polarity.\n\nReason: One chain has the polarity 5\' to 3’ while the other has 3’ to 5’.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The two chains have anti-parallel polarity. It means, if one\nchain has the polarity 5\'to3\', the other has 3\'to5\' \n(Reference  : NCERT  Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, Page 97, Paragraph 5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282910,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding to DNA and choose the correct statements.\n\n1) The two DNA strands are coiled in a left-handed fashion.\n\n2) All living cells can reverse the process of Central Dogma. \n\n3) Central dogma is the process where instructions from DNA flows to RNA then proceeds to produce a functional protein. \n\n4)The plane of one nucleotide stacks over the other in triple helix.','Only 3 is correct','The two DNA strands are coiled in a right-handed fashion.\nOnly certain viruses can reverse central dogma which is from RNA to DNA. For instance, human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) . \nCentral dogma means the flow of instruction from DNA to RNA then to functional protein.\nThe plane of one base pair stacks over the other in double helix. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, page 98, paragraph 1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Only 2 is correct','Only 3 is correct','Only 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282911,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason and choose the correct option.  \n\nAssertion: DNA is a stable helical structure that is susceptible to hydrolysis, free radicals and can even survive at temperature as low as 4 degree Celsius.\n\nReason: The plane of one base pair stacks over the other in double helix is the only contributor to its stability.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','DNA is a stable helical structure that is susceptible to hydrolysis, free radicals and can even survive at temperature as low as 4 degree Celsius. The H bonds formed between the nucleotides also increase the stability of DNA. (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 page 98 paragraph 1 )','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282912,'biology','Consider the statements below regarding Structure of Polynucleotide Chain and choose the correct statements\n\n1)  DNA which is positively charged is held with proteins that are negatively charged in nucleoid in prokaryotes. \n\n2) The length of DNA is calculated by multiplying total number of nucleotides with distance between any two base pairs.\n\n3)  Histones are rich in the basic amino acid residues lysine and arginine. \n\n4)The packaging of chromatin at higher level requires additional set of proteins called  Non-histone Chromosomal (NHC) proteins.','Only 3 and 4 are correct','DNA which is negatively charged is held with proteins that are positively charged in nucleoid in eukaryotes.\nLength of DNA double\nhelix is calculated by multiplying the total number of bp with distance between two consecutive bp.\nLysine and arginine residues carry positive charges in their side chains. \nThe packaging of chromatin at higher level requires additional set of proteins known as Non-histone Chromosomal (NHC) proteins\n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, pg 99, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 3 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300108,'chemistry','Following structure represents diagram(3001)','Sulphonamides','Sulphonamides (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Asphaneramine','Protonsil','Sulphonamides','Equanil','DELETED',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/300108q.png'),
(300109,'chemistry','Drugs can be classified on the basis of:','All of these','Classification of Drugs: (a) On the basis of pharmacological effect. (b) On the basis of drug action.  (c) On the basis of chemical structure. (d) On the basis of molecular targets. (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Chemical Structures','Drug Action','Molecular targets','All of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282913,'biology','Consider the statements below regarding Structure of Polynucleotide Chain and choose the correct statements\n\n1)  Histones are organised to form Histone octamer\n\n2) Chromatin are seen as ‘beads-on-string’ structure when viewed under electron microscope\n\n3) Histones are rich in amino acids lysine and asparagine.\n\n4)The DNA in nucleoid is organised in large loops held by proteins in prokaryotes','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Histones are organised to form a unit of eight molecules called histone octamer. Since octa carries the meaning of eight \nThe nucleosomes in chromatin are seen as ‘beads-on-string’ structure when viewed under electron microscope (EM).\nHistones are rich in the basic amino acid residues lysine and arginine.\nThe DNA in nucleoid is organised in large loops by proteins in prokaryotes. \n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, page 99, paragraph 3 and 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282914,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding the packaging of DNA helix, choose the correct statements\n\n1) In E.Coli, the DNA is not scattered all over the place \n\n2) A protein acquires charge depending the abundance of amino acids residues with charged side chains\n\n3) Non-histone Chromosomal (NHC) proteins is involved in formation euchromatin\n\n4) Heterochromatin is a transcriptionally active chromatin','Only 1 and 2 are correct','In,E. coli, though they do not have a defined nucleus, the DNA is not scattered throughout the cell. DNA is held in nucleoid\nA protein acquires charge depending upon the abundance of amino acids residues with charged side chains.\nThe packaging of chromatin at higher level requires additional set of proteins that collectively are referred to as Non-histone Chromosomal (NHC) proteins\nEuchromatin is said to be transcriptionally active chromatin, whereas heterochromatin is inactive.\n (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12 page 99,  paragraph 3 and 4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282915,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason and choose the correct option.  \n\nAssertion: The beads-on-string structure in chromatin is packaged to form chromatin fibers that are further coiled and condensed at metaphase stage of cell division. \n\nReason: This allows to form chromosomes during metaphase','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The beads-on-string structure in chromatin is packaged to form chromatin fibers that are further coiled and condensed at metaphase stage\nof cell division to form chromosomes. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, pg 99, paragraph6)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282916,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason and choose the correct option.  \n\nAssertion: Nucleosome is made up of DNA and histones \n\nReason: The positively charged DNA will wrap around negatively charged histone octamer','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','DNA is negatively charged histone octamer is positively charged (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12 , pg 99, paragraph6)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282917,'biology','Considering the following statements regarding the Transforming Principle and choose the correct statements\n\n1) Frederick Griffith uses Staphylococcus pneumoniae to conduct his study for Transforming Principle.\n\n2) Mice infected with S strain die due to pneumoniae infection but mice infected with R strain survived.  \n\n3) When heat-killed s strain and live r strain are injected into mice, they die. \n\n4)Griffith was able to kill bacteria by heating them.','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','Frederick Griffith, in a series of experiments with Streptococcus pneumoniae (bacterium responsible for pneumonia), witnessed a miraculous transformation in the bacteria.  \nS strain is virulent strain because it does not have mucous polysaccharide coat while R strain has it. • R strain is transformed by S strain and managed to synthesise the polysaccharide coat to develop virulence. \nGriffith was able to kill bacteria by heating them. (Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12 , Chapter 6, pg 100, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282918,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason and choose the correct option.  \n\nAssertion: The Streptococcus pneumoniae produces two types of colonies which are smooth, shiny colonies and rough colonies \n\nReason: The ones producing smooth colonies have oligosaccharide coats covering them','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The Streptococcus pneumoniae produces two types of colonies which are smooth, shiny colonies and rough colonies. The S strain bacteria have a mucous (polysaccharide) coat, while R strain does not. (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 100 paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282919,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Transforming Principle and choose the correct statements.\n\n1) It is discovered that DNA alone in R strain can cause virulence. \n\n2) Initially, genetic material was thought to be a protein. \n\n3) Proteases can cause transformation. \n\n4) Protein and RNA are transforming substances.','Only 2 is correct','They discovered that DNA alone from S bacteria caused R bacteria to become transformed. \nPrior to the work of Oswald Avery, Colin MacLeod and Maclyn McCarty. \nThey also discovered that protein-digesting enzymes (proteases) and RNA-digesting enzymes (RNases) did not affect transformation (1933-44), the genetic material was thought to be a protein. \nprotein-digesting enzymes (proteases) and RNA-digesting enzymes (RNases) did not affect transformation, so the transforming substance was not a protein or RNA. (Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 101 paragraph5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Only 2 is correct','Only 3 is correct','Only 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282920,'biology','Considering the following statements regarding DNA as genetic material\n\n1) Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase worked on bacteriophages. \n\n2) All DNA and protein contains phosphorus.\n\n3) Genetic material from bacterial cell will enter bacteriophage. \n\n4) DNA does not contain sulfur.','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase (1952). They worked\nwith viruses that infect bacteria called bacteriophages\nDNA contains phosphorus but protein does not.\nThe bacteriophage attaches to the bacteria and its genetic material\nthen enters the bacterial cell.\nDNA does not contain sulfur.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, pg 101, paragraph8)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282921,'biology','Considering the following statements regarding DNA as genetic material \n\n1)The bacterial cell treats the viral genetic material as foreign substances\n\n2)Hershey and Chase grew viruses on mediums that contained radioactive phosphorus and radioactive sulfur.\n\n3) Bacteriophage is in the bacterial cell\n\n4) Viruses grow on radioactive sulphur contained radioactive protein','Only 2 and 4 are correct','The The bacterial cell treats the viral genetic material as if it was its own\nThey grew some viruses on a medium that contained radioactive phosphorus and some others on medium that contained radioactive sulfur.*The bacteriophage attaches to the bacteria and its genetic material then enters the bacterial cell.\nViruses grown on radioactive sulfur contained radioactive protein but not radioactive DNA\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 101 paragraph7)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282922,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason and choose the correct option.\n\nAssertion: Viruses grown in the presence of radioactive phosphorus contained radioactive DNA but not radioactive protein\n\nReason: DNA contains phosphorus but protein does not.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Viruses grown in the presence of radioactive phosphorus contained radioactive DNA but not radioactive protein because DNA contains phosphorus but protein does not. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 101 paragraph7)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282923,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason and choose the correct option.  \n\nAssertion: DNA can cause the transformation of genetic material.\n\nReason: Digestion with RNA-ase did not inhibit transformation.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Digestion with DNase did inhibit transformation, suggesting that the DNA caused the transformation.They also discovered that protein-digesting enzymes (proteases) and RNA-digesting enzymes (RNases) did not affect transformation, so the transforming substance was not a protein or RNA\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 101 paragraph7)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282924,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA as genetic material and choose the correct statements\n\n1) The virus particles were separated from the bacteria by using a blender.\n\n2) The Hershey-Chase experiment involves only infection and blending. \n\n3) Radioactive phages are attached to E.Coli bacteria in Hershey- Chase experiment\n\n4) Bacteria that were infected with viruses that had radioactive proteins were not radioactive','Only 3 and 4 are correct','The virus particles were separated from the bacteria by spinning them in a centrifuge.\nThe Hershey-Chase experiment involves Infection, Blending and Centrifugation\nRadioactive phages were allowed to attach to E. coli bacteria.\nBacteria that were infected with viruses that had radioactive proteins were not radioactive. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 102 paragraph1and 2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282925,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: In Hershey-Chase experiment, it is proven DNA is the genetic material that being passed on to bacteria from virus\n\nReason: Bacteria which was infected with viruses that had radioactive DNA were radioactive,','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Bacteria which was infected with viruses that had radioactive DNA were radioactive, indicating that DNA was the material that passed from the virus to the bacteria.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 102 paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282926,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Properties of Genetic Material, choose the correct statements\n\n1) RNA is the genetic material for QB Bacteriophage\n\n2) A genetic material must be able to replicate itself\n\n3) Genetic material is only structurally stable but not chemicaly\n\n4) RNA and DNA contain ribose sugars only','Only 1 and 2 are correct','RNA is the genetic material (for example, Tobacco Mosaic viruses, QB bacteriophage, etc.)\nA molecule that can act as genetic material should be able to generate its replica (Replication).\nGenetic material should be stable chemically and structurally.\nRNA contains ribose sugar while DNA contains deoxyribose sugar \nReference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 103 para1 and 3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282927,'biology','Consider the following statement regarding Properties of Genetic Material, choose the correct statements.\n\n1) Genetic material is able to undergo mutation \n\n2) Heat can totally destroy all the properties of genetic materials \n\n3) Protein can express itself in the form of Mendelian characteristics.\n\n4) DNA is a better genetic material than RNA','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Genetic materials should provide the scope for slow changes (mutation) that are required for evolution.\nIn Griffith’s ‘transforming principle’ itself that heat, which killed the bacteria, at least did not destroy some of the properties of genetic material\nGenetic material should be able to express itself in the form of \'Mendelian Characters’. Protein is not a genetic material\nDNA chemically is less reactive and structurally more stable when compared to RNA\nReference  :NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 103 paragraph1 and 3','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282928,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer.\n\nAssertion: DNA chemically is less reactive and structurally more stable when compared to RNA.\n\nReason: RNA is a reactive group and makes RNA labile and easily degradable.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Further, 2\'-OH group present at every nucleotide in RNA is a reactive group and makes RNA labile and easily degradable. RNA is also now known to be catalytic, hence reactive. Therefore, DNA chemically is less reactive and structurally more stable when compared to RNA.\nReferenve: NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 103 paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282929,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer.\n\nAssertion: DNA mutate at a faster rate than RNA.\n\nReason: RNA has a more stable than DNA and evolves at a faster rate. .','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','In fact, RNA being unstable, mutate at a faster rate. Consequently, viruses having RNA genome and having shorter life span mutate and evolve faster.\nReference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 103 paragraph9','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282930,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: RNA is catalytic therefore making it reactive\n\nReason: 4\'-OH group can be found at every nucleotide in RNA','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','2\'-OH group present at every nucleotide in RNA is a reactive group and makes RNA labile and easily degradable. RNA is also now known to be catalytic, hence reactive.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 103 paragraph5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282931,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding RNA, choose the correct statements\n\n1) RNA is involved in translation only.\n\n2) RNA can act as a catalyst in certain biochemical reaction.\n\n3) RNA is a stable and reactive catalyst.\n\n4) DNA is a product of evolution from RNA.','Only 2 and 4 are correct','There is now enough evidence to prove that essential life processes (such as metabolism, translation, splicing, etc.), evolved around RNA not just translation.\nRNA used to act as a genetic material as well as a catalyst in important biochemical reactions that are catalysed by RNA catalysts.\nRNA being a catalyst was reactive and hence unstable. \nDNA has evolved from RNA with chemical modifications that make it more stable.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 104 paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282932,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason , choose the correct answer \n\nAssertion: RNA cannot be used as a catalyst in biochemical reactions\n\nReason: RNA is not reactive enough.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','RNA is used to act as a genetic material as well as a catalyst as not all biochemical reactions are catalysed by protein enzymes. However, due to its high reactivity, RNA is not stable.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg104 paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282933,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Replication, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Watson and Crick proposed the presence of copying mechanism in protein.\n\n2) After completion of replication, the parental strands will rejoin. \n\n3) Parental strands act as template to synthesize new complementary daughter strands.\n\n4) DNA molecule that has one parental and one newly synthesized strand is known as semi-conservative.','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Watson and Crick had proposed a scheme for replication of DNA, suggesting a possible copying mechanism for genetic material. \n After the completion of replication, each DNA molecule would have one parental and one newly synthesised strand.\nThe scheme suggested that the two strands would separate and act as a template for the synthesis of new complementary strands. \nAfter the completion of replication, each DNA molecule would have one parental and one newly synthesised strand. This scheme was termed as semiconservative DNA replication\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 104 paragraph6)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282934,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer. \n\nAssertion: After the completion of replication, each DNA molecule would have one parental and one newly synthesised strand.\n\nReason: The parental DNA will separate into 2 strands and synthesize daughter strands that are complementary to them.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The two parental strands would separate and act as a template for the synthesis of new complementary strands. After the completion of replication, each DNA molecule would have one parental and one newly synthesised strand. This scheme was termed as semiconservative DNA replication\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 104 paragraph6)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282935,'biology','Consider the statements regarding Replication, choose the correct statements\n\n1) DNA polymerase uses DNA template to inhibit  polymerization of deoxynucleotides.\n\n2) Mistake during replication does not affect the genetic material as error checking mechanism is present\n\n3)Deoxyribonucleoside triphosphates serve dual purposes.\n\n4)The two terminal phosphates in a deoxynucleoside triphosphates are high-energy phosphates','Only 3 and 4 are correct','DNA polymerase, it uses a DNA template to catalyse the polymerisation of deoxynucleotides.\nThe DNA Polymerases also have to catalyse the reaction with high degree of accuracy. Any mistake during replication would result into mutations. \nDeoxyribonucleoside triphosphates serve dual purposes. In addition to acting as substrates, they provide energy for polymerisation reaction\nThe two terminal phosphates in a deoxynucleoside triphosphates are high-energy phosphates, same as in case of ATP\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 106 paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282936,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Replication, choose the correct statements\n\n1) DNA polymerase must be highly efficient catalysts.\n\n2) E.Coli completes replication at a faster rate compared to human.\n\n3) E.Coli uses RNA polymerases instead of DNA polymerases\n\n4) ATP has high energy phosphorus.','Only 1 and 2 are correct','DNA polymerases are highly efficient enzymes as they have to catalyse polymerisation of a large number of nucleotides in a very short time\nE. coli that has only 4.6 ×10⁶ bp (compare it with human whose diploid content is 6.6 × 10⁹ bp), completes the process of replication within 18 minutes.\nIn living cells, such as E. coli, the process of replication requires a set of catalysts (enzymes). The main enzyme is referred to as DNA-dependent DNA polymerase as E.Coli is a prokaryote and contains DNA.\nThe two terminal phosphates in a deoxynucleoside triphosphates are high-energy phosphates, same does ATP. They are not phosphorus, should be phosphates. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 106 paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282937,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, and choose the correct answer.\n\nAssertion: DNA-dependent enzyme is known as DNA Polymerase.\n\nReason: It uses a DNA template to catalyse the polymerisation of deoxynucleotides.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','The main enzyme is referred to as DNA-dependent DNA polymerase, since it uses a DNA template to catalyse the polymerisation of deoxynucleotides. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 106 paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282938,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, and choose the correct answer.\n\nAssertion: DNA polymerases requires high degree of accuracy and be able to work fast.\n\nReason: This is because DNA degrades very quickly once the parental strand is separated','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','DNA polymerases has to be accurate to prevent mutation in organism and work fast as there is a lot nucleotides to be replicated in a short amount of time \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6 pg 106 paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282939,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Replication, choose the correct statements.\n\n1) Many additional catalysts are needed for Replication.\n\n2) No matter how long the DNA strand is, it can be separated in entire length.\n\n3) DNA polymerase catalysts in 5\' to 3\' direction only\n\n4) All the strands and fragments are joined by DNA hydrolase.','Only 1 and 3 are correct','In addition to DNA-dependent DNA polymerases, many additional enzymes are required to complete the process of replication with high degree of accuracy.\nFor long DNA molecules, since the two strands of DNA cannot be separated in its entire length due to very high energy requirement\nThe DNA-dependent DNA polymerases catalyse polymerisation only in one direction, that is 5\'to3\'.\nOnly the discontinuously synthesised fragments are later joined by the enzyme DNA ligase\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6  pg 106 paragraph5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 3 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282940,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: There are some additional complications that will occur at replication fork during replication.\n\nReason: DNA polymerase only replicates in 3\' to 5\' direction','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The DNA-dependent DNA polymerases catalyse polymerisation only in one direction, that is 5\'to3\'. This creates some additional complications at the replicating fork.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 106 paragraph5)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282941,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Replication, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The region where humans\' DNA starts replicating is known as the origin of replication\n\n2) Replication can occur at any point or segment of DNA.\n\n3) DNA polymerases alone cannot initiate replication.\n\n4) A vector plays an important role in replication.','Only 3 and 4 are correct','There is a definite region in E. coli DNA where the replication originates. Such regions are termed as origin of replication. It does not apply on human\nThe replication does not initiate randomly at any place in DNA.\nThe DNA polymerases on their own cannot initiate the process of replication. \nBecause of the requirement of the origin of replication that a piece of DNA if needed to be propagated during recombinant DNA procedures,requires a vector to provide the origin\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology Class 12, Chapter 6, pg 107 paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282942,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: E.Coli DNA can replicate at any point of the DNA \n\nReason: It does not require vector to provide origin of replication','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','There is a definite region in E. coli DNA where the replication originates known as origin of replication.This is because of the requirement of the origin of replication that a piece of DNA if needed to be propagated during recombinant DNA procedures,requires a vector. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 107 paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282943,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Transcription, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Transcription is the process of copying RNA materials to synthesize a functional protein. \n\n2) In transcription, only a segment of DNA and one of the strands is copied.\n\n3) Principle of complementarity is implied in transcription\n\n4) Adenosine will form new base pair with thymine during transcription.','Only 2 and 3 are correct','The process of copying genetic information from one strand of the DNA into RNA is termed as transcription. From RNA to functional protein is known as translation. \nIn transcription only a segment of DNA and only one of the strands is copied into RNA.\nThe principle of complementarity governs the process of transcription\nThe adenosine complements now forms base pair with uracil instead of thymine. because uracil replaces thymine in RNA. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 107, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 3 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282944,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Complementarity is applied in transcription too just like replication.\n\nReason: Adenosine is paired with Thymine while Cytosine is paired with Guanosine.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The principle of complementarity governs the process of transcription, except the adenosine complements now forms base pair with uracil instead of thymine  because uracil replace thymine in RNA. Guanosine remains its base pairing with Cytosine for in both transcription and replication. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 107, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282945,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Transcription, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Both DNA strands cannot act as templates at the same time during transcription\n\n2) An RNA is double stranded as it is transcripted from double stranded DNA\n\n3) Transcription of both parental strands will code for RNA with same sequences.\n\n4) One segment of DNA coding for two types of protein is impossible.','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Both the strands are not copied during transcription.Unlike in the process of replication, which once set in, the total DNA of an organism gets duplicated, in transcription only a segment of DNA and only one of the strands is copied into RNA.\nTwo RNA molecules if produced simultaneously would be complementary to each other, hence would form a double stranded RNA. This would prevent RNA from being translated into protein and the exercise of transcription would become a futile one therefore RNA must not be double-stranded\nIf both strands act as a template, they would code for RNA molecule with different sequences because complementary does not mean similar\nIf one segment of the DNA would be coding for two different proteins, and this would complicate the genetic information transfer machinery so it should not take place\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 107, paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282946,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Both strands of DNA are not copied in transcription, only a segment and one strands of the DNA.\n\nReason: It might results in RNA molecules of different  sequences and lead to different amino acid hence producing a different protein.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','In transcription only a segment of DNA and only one of the strands is copied into RNA. becauseif both strands act as a template, they would code for RNA molecule with different sequences ,   and in turn, if they code for proteins, the sequenceof amino acids in the proteins would be different.  \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 107, paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(40502,'chemistry','In Cl-Cl molecule the valence shell of both Cl atoms have:','8 electrons','The electronic configuration of ₁₇Cl = 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁵. \n\nThe valence shell have 7 electrons and need one electron to attain noble gas configuration.\n\nTwo Cl atoms share one electron with each other to form a covalent bond between them in this way they both fullfill their octet demand in valence shell (Cl⨯⚈Cl). \n\nReference: (NCERT chemistry Class 11; page 102; paragraph no.4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Chemical bonding and molecular structure >> Covalent bond',NULL,'2','mcq','8 electrons','17 electrons','18 electrons','34 electrons','NEW','11-102','11-102','/ncert/pyq/biology/match/40502q.png'),
(282947,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Transcription, choose the correct statements\n\n1) A transcription unit is made up of two regions in the DNA\n\n2) DNA- dependent RNA polymerase can catalyze pomerisation in both 5\' to 3\' and 3\' to 5\' directions\n\n3)Coding strand is a strand that does not code for any amino acid.\n\n4) The 3\' to 5\' template is a template strand.','Only 3 and 4 are correct','A transcription unit is primarily made up of 3 regions namely Promoter, Structural Gene and Terminator.\nDNA-dependent RNA polymerase catalyses the polymerisation in only one direction, that is, 5\'→3\'\nThe strand that does not code for anything is referred as coding strand\nThe strand that has the polarity 3\'→5\' acts as a template, and is also referred to as template strand.\n(Reference  : NCERT, Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 108, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 3 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282948,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: DNA-dependent RNA polymerase can catalyze the polymerization in one way only\n\nReason: Both the strands are in 5\' to 3\' direction','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The DNA-dependent RNA polymerase also catalyse the polymerisation in only one direction, that is, 5\'→3\',The two strands have opposite polarity.The strand that has the polarity 3\'→5\' acts as a template, and is also referred to as template strand.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 108, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282949,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Transcription, choose the correct statements\n\n1) DNA sequence provides binding site for DNA polymerase\n\n2) Terminator is located at downstream of the coding strand\n\n3) Promoter defines the template and coding strands of a transcription unit\n\n4) There will not be any regulatory sequences present further upstream and downstream of promoter.','Only 2 and 3 are correct','It is a DNA sequence that provides binding site for RNA polymerase\nThe terminator is located towards 3\' -end (downstream) of the coding strand and it usually defines the end of the process of transcription\n It is the presence of a promoter in a transcription unit that also defines the template and coding strands. \nThere are additional regulatory sequences that may be present further upstream or downstream to the promoter. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 108, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 3 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(282950,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: The definition of coding and template strand can be reversed.\n\nReason: Promoter and terminator can switch their positions','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','By switching promoter\'s position with terminator, the definition of coding and template strands could be reversed.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 108, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 1',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283001,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Transcription, choose the correct statements\n\n1) DNA is the functional unit of inheritance\n\n2) The genes in monocistronic structural genes are split\n\n3) Exons are non coding sequences or sequences that are not expressed\n\n4) Regulatory genes codes for certain amino acid.','Only 1 and 2 are correct','A DNA is defined as the functional unit of inheritance.\nIn eukaryotes, the monocistronic structural genes have interrupted coding sequences – the genes in eukaryotes are split.\nThe coding sequences or expressed sequences are defined as exons. Introns or intervening sequences do not appear in mature or processed RNA\n the regulatory sequences are loosely defined as regulatory genes, even though these sequences do not code for any RNA or protein.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 109, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 3 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283002,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: In prokaryotes, the monocistronic structural genes have interrupted coding sequences\n\nReason: The genes in prokaryotes are split','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','The structural gene in a transcription unit could be said as monocistronic (mostly in eukaryotes) or polycistronic (mostly in bacteria or prokaryotes). In eukaryotes, the monocistronic structural genes have interrupted coding sequences – the genes in eukaryotes are split. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 109, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283003,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Exons are said to be interrupted by introns\n\nReason: Introns appear on appear in mature RNA where exons exist','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The coding sequences or expressed sequences are defined as exons. Exons are said to be those sequence that appear in mature or processed RNA. The exons are interrupted by introns. Introns or intervening sequences do not appear in mature or processed RNA.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 109, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283004,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Transcription, choose the correct statements\n\n1) rRNAs play a structural and catalytic role in transcription\n\n2) tRNA stands for transportation RNAs\n\n3) Process of Transcription in bacteria involves three main steps only.\n\n4) The three types of RNAs are required to synthesize protein in a cell.','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Ribosmal RNAs (rRNAs) play structural and catalytic role during translation not transcription.\ntRNA stands for transfer RNAs\nTranscription of bacteria involve initiation, elongation and termination.\n All three RNAs are needed to synthesise a protein in a cell.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 109, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 3 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283005,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: All three types of RNAs are needed for protein synthesis.\n\nReason: The tRNA provides the template, mRNA brings aminoacids and reads the genetic code, and rRNAs play structural and catalytic role during transcription.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','All the three types of RNAs are needed to synthesize protein in a cell.\nThe mRNA provides the template, tRNA brings aminoacids and reads the genetic code, and rRNAs play structural and catalytic role during translation. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter, 6 pg 109, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283006,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Transcription in Bacteria, choose the correct statement\n\n1) In Initiation, nucleotide diphosphate is used as substrate\n\n2) RNA polymerase will fall of when reaching terminator\n\n3) There is single DNA-dependent RNA polymerase that catalyses transcription of all types of RNA in bacteria. \n\n4) The polymerization does not follow the rule of complementarity','Only 2 and 3 are correct','It uses nucleoside triphosphates as substrate not diphosphate\nOnce the polymerases reaches the terminator region, the nascent RNA falls off, so also the RNA polymerase.\n There is single DNA-dependent RNA polymerase that catalyses transcription of all types of RNA in bacteria.\nRNA polymerase polymerises in a template depended fashion following the rule of complementarity\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 109, paragrapH₂  and pg 110, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 3 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283007,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer \n\nAssertion: RNA polymerase falls off from binding site after initiation of transcription \n\nReason: RNA does not facilitate in elongation nor termination','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','RNA polymerase binds to promoter and initiates transcription (Initiation). It somehow also facilitates opening of the helix and continues elongation. Once the polymerases reaches the terminator region, the nascent RNA falls off, so also the RNA polymerase. This results in termination of transcription.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 110, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283008,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Transcription, choos the correct statements\n\n1) In bacteria, transcription and translation can be carried out at the same time\n\n2) RNA polymerase only associates with initiation factor (σ)\n\n3) RNA polymerase cannot initiate and terminate transcription on its own\n\n4) Transcription and translation in bacteria takes place in different compartments','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Translation can begin much before the mRNA is fully transcribed in bacteria. Consequently, the transcription and translation can be coupled in bacteria\n It associates transiently with initiation-factor (σ) and termination-factor (ρ)\n It associates transiently with initiation-factor (σ) and termination-factor (ρ) to initiate and terminate the transcription,\nIn bacteria, since the mRNA does not require any processing to become active, and also since transcription and translation take place in the same compartment (there is no separation of cytosol and nucleus in bacteria),\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 110, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283009,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer \n\nAssertion: RNA polymerase\'s specificity can be altered to initiate or terminate transcription\n\nReason: It associates transiently with initiation-factor (σ) and termination-factor (ρ)','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','It associates transiently with initiation-factor (σ) and termination-factor (ρ) to initiate and terminate the transcription, respectively. Association with these factors alter the specificity of the RNA polymerase to either initiate or terminate.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 110, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283010,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding RNA polymerases, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Only 1 RNA polymerase is required for transcription in eukaryotes\n\n2) RNA polymerase III is responsible for transcription of small nuclear RNAs\n\n3) RNA polymerase II transcribes heterogeneous nuclear RNA \n\n4) 18S, and 5.8S rRNAs are transcribed by  RNA polymerase I','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','There are at least three RNA polymerases in the nucleus in eukaryotes\nRNA polymerase III is responsible for transcription of tRNA, 5srRNA, and snRNAs (small nuclear RNAs)\nRNA polymerase II transcribes precursor of mRNA, the heterogeneous nuclear RNA (hnRNA).\nThe RNA polymerase I transcribes rRNAs (28S, 18S, and 5.8S)\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 111, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283011,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: There are at least three types of RNA polymerases in the nucleus of eukaryotes\n\nReason: The different types of RNA polymerases transcribe different types of RNAs.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','In Eukaryotics, There are at least three RNA polymerases in the nucleus.There is a clear cut division of labour.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 111, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283012,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Transcription, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Small nuclear RNA undergoes capping and tailing during transcription of eukaryotes\n\n2) The fully processed hnRNA will be transported out from nucleus for translation\n\n3) Primary transcripts contain exons only\n\n4) In capping, methyl guanosine triphosphate is added','Only 2 and 4 are correct','hnRNA undergoes additional processing called as capping and tailing. not snRNAs\nIt is the fully processed hnRNA, now called mRNA, that is transported out of the nucleus for translation \nThe primary transcripts contain both the exons and the introns and are non-functional.\nIn capping an unusual nucleotide (methyl guanosine triphosphate) is added to the 5\'-end of hnRNA\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 111, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 3 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283013,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Introns have to be removed \n\nReason: Introns are non-functional','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The primary transcripts contain both the exons and the introns and are non-functional. Hence, it is subjected to a process called splicing where the introns are removed and exons are joined in a defined order\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 111, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283014,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding translation, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Process of translation does not depend on complementarity\n\n2) Change in nucleic acids can alter amino acids in proteins\n\n3) During translation, a nucleic acid is copied to form an amino acid\n\n4) In translation, genetic material can be transferred directly from DNA to synthesize amino acid','Only 1 and 2 are correct','The process of translation requires transfer of genetic information from a polymer of nucleotides to synthesise a polymer of amino acids. Neither does any complementarity exist between nucleotides and amino acids,\nChange in nucleic acids (genetic material) were responsible for change in amino acids in proteins.\nDuring replication and transcription a nucleic acid was copied to form another nucleic acid.The process of translation requires transfer of genetic information from a polymer of nucleotides to synthesise a polymer of amino acids. \nIn translation, the transcribed RNA is translated into amino acid to form a functional protein\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 111, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Only 1 and 3 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283015,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Translation does not depend on complementarity\n\nReason: It is the formation of protein directly from transcribed RNA','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','The process of translation requires transfer of genetic information from a polymer of nucleotides to synthesise a polymer of amino acids. Neither does any complementarity exist between nucleotides and amino acids,\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 111, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283016,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Codons, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Three codons will make up one amino acid\n\n2) There are 64 amino acids in total that can be formed by the 4 known bases\n\n3) The 4 bases can combine with other bases to form a codon\n\n4) Only 20 amino acids are available to form various functional proteins.','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','In order to code for all the 20 amino acids, the code should be made up of three nucleotides\nA permutation combination of  (4 × 4 × 4) would generate 64 codons; generating many more codons than required.\nSince there are only 4 bases and if they have to code for 20 amino acids, the code should constitute a combination of bases.\nTo code for all the 20 amino acids, the code should be made up of three nucleotides\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 111, paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283017,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: There are 64 types of amino acids in total\n\nReason: There are only 4 bases and by using permutation recombination of 4x4x4 , we will get 64','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','There are 4 bases and only 20 amino acids are present. 64 amino acids is more than what we need, therefore impossible\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 111, paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283018,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Codons, choose the correct statements\n\n1) There are three stop terminator codons\n\n2) All the 64 codons code for amino acids\n\n3) Codon in mRNA is read with pauses inbetween\n\n4) Certain amino acids can be coded by different codons.','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only UAA, UAG, UGA are stop terminator codons\n61 codons code for amino acids and 3 codons do not code for any amino acids, hence they function as stop codons.\nThe codon is read in mRNA in a contiguous fashion. There are no punctuations so no pauses in between\nSome amino acids are coded by more than one codon, hence the code is degenerate for example Alanine and Serine\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 112, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283019,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: UAA, UAG, UGA are stop codons\n\nReason: They code for amino acids and terminate translation','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','UAA, UAG, UGA are stop terminator codons as they do not code for any amino acids, hence they function as stop codons\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 112, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283020,'biology','Consider the statements regarding Codons, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The codes are all universal in all organisms with a few exceptions\n\n2) AUG codes for Methionine and acts as initiator codon\n\n3) UAG is an initiator codon\n\n4) UUU codes for phenylalanine in humans only.','Only 1 and 2 are correct','The code is nearly universal.Some exceptions to this rule have been found in mitochondrial codons, and in some protozoans\nAUG has dual functions. It codes for Methionine (met) , and it also act as initiator codon\nUAA, UAG, UGA are stop terminator codons.\nThe code is nearly universal: for example, from bacteria to human UUU would code for Phenylalanine (phe).\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 112, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283021,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: UGA has dual functions\n\nReason: UGA acts as a stop codon and can code for amino acid.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','UGA is a stop terminator codon that does not code for any amino acid.On the other hand, AUG has dual functions. It codes for Methionine (met) , and it also act as initiator codon.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 112, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283022,'biology','Consider the statemenrs regarding Mutations and Genetic Code. choose the correct statements\n\n1) Sickle Cell Anemia is an example of disease caused by deletion mutation\n\n2) Point mutation involves insertion or deletion or change of a base \n\n3)  Large deletions and rearrangements in a segment of DNA may result in loss of function\n\n4) Insertion or deletion of one or two bases changes the entire reading frame','Only 2 and 3 are correct','A classical example of point mutation is a change of single base pair in the gene for beta globin chain that results in the change of amino acid residue glutamate to valine. It results into diseased condition called as sickle cell anemia.\nA point mutation means a single base pair is added, deleted or changed.\nEffects of large deletions and rearrangements in a segment of DNA may result in loss or gain of a gene and so a function.\nInsertion or deletion of one or two bases changes the reading frame from the point of insertion or deletion. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 113, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283023,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Sickle Cell Anemia is considered as a type disease caused by mutation\n\nReason: It is due to the change of amino acid residue glutamate to valine.','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','A classical example of point mutation is a change of single base pair in the gene for beta globin chain that results in the change of amino acid residue glutamate to valine. It results into a diseased condition called as sickle cell anemia.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 113, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283024,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding tRNA, choose the correct statements\n\n1) There is no specific tRNA that acts as initiator for the initiation\n\n2) tRNA has an anticodon loop that has bases complementary to the code\n\n3) tRNAs are specific for each amino acid\n\n4) The actual structure of tRNA looks like a four-leaf clover.','Only 2 and 3 are correct','For initiation, there is another specific tRNA that is referred to as initiator tRNA\ntRNA has an anticodon loop that has bases complementary to the code, and it also has an amino acid acceptor end to which it binds to amino acids\ntRNAs are specific for each amino acid\nThe secondary structure of tRNA has been depicted that looks like a clover-leaf. In actual structure, the tRNA is a compact molecule which looks like inverted L\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 114, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10327,'chemistry','What is the change in mass and weight with change in place?','Mass is constant while weight is variable.','Mass of a substance is the amount of matter present in it, while weight is the force exerted by gravity on an object. \n \n The mass of a substance is constant, whereas, its weight may vary from one place to another due to change in gravity.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 9 Paragraph-1)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Classification of matter',NULL,'1','mcq','Mass is constant while weight is variable.','Mass is variable while weight is constant.','Both mass and weight are constant','Both mass and weight are variable','NEW','11-009','11-009',NULL),
(283025,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding tRNA, choose the correct statements\n\n1) tRNA has an amino acid acceptor end\n\n2) tRNA is also known as the adaptor molecule\n\n3) tRNAs are not amino-acid specific hence can bind to any of them\n\n4) Stop codons require a special terminator tRNA and initiator tRNA for initiation','Only 1 and 2 are correct','tRNA has an amino acid acceptor end to which it binds to amino acids.\ntRNA is assigned the role as an adapter molecule\ntRNAs are specific for each amino acid\nFor initiation, there is another specific tRNA that is referred to as initiator tRNA. However, there are no tRNAs for stop codons. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 114, paragraph3.)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283026,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: tRNAs are specific for each amino acid\n\nReason: tRNAs can one hand read the code and on other hand would bind to specific amino acids.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','tRNA has an anticodon loop that has bases complementary to the code, and it also has an amino acid acceptor end to which it binds to amino acids\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 113, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283027,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Translation, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The order and sequence of amino acids depends on the sequence of bases in the mRNA.\n\n2) The amino acids are joined by peptide bond.\n\n3) Initially amino acid is activated by presence of ADP\n\n4) Formation of amino acid does not require energy','Only 1 and 2 are correct','The order and sequence of amino acids are defined by the sequence of bases in the mRNA.\nThe amino acids are joined by a bond which is known as a peptide bond.\nIn the first phase itself amino acids are activated in the presence of ATP and linked to their cognate tRNA\nFormation of a peptide bond requires energy in the form of ATP\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 114, paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283028,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Charging of tRNA or aminoacylation of tRNA takes place\n\nReason:  Formation of a peptide bond requires energy','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Formation of a peptide bond requires energy. Therefore, in the first phase itself amino acids are activated in the presence of ATP and linked to their cognate tRNA – a process commonly called as charging of tRNA or aminoacylation of tRNA to be more specific. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 114, paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283029,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Translation, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Rate of peptide bond formation can be enhanced by amount of tRNA present\n\n2) In the active state of ribosome, it exists as two subunits\n\n3) Ribosome is responsible to carry out translation\n\n4) Translation starts when small subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA to be synthesized into proteins','Only 3 and 4 are correct','The presence of a catalyst would enhance the rate of peptide bond formation. It does not involve amount of tRNA*\nIn its inactive state, it exists as two subunits; a large subunit and a small subunit. \nThe cellular factory responsible for synthesising proteins is the ribosome.\nWhen the small subunit encounters an mRNA, the process of translation of the mRNA to protein begins.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 115, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283030,'biology','Consider the following assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: There are two sites in the small subunit of ribosome\n\nReason: When the small subunit encounters an mRNA, amino acids can bind and form peptide bond','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','There are two sites in the large subunit, for subsequent amino acids to bind to and thus, be close enough to each other for the formation of a peptide bond. When the small subunit encounters an mRNA, it initiates translation\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 115, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283031,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding translation, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Untranslated regions are present before start codon and after stop codon\n\n2) A translational unit in rRNA codes for a polypeptide\n\n3) mRNA will be entirely translated without leaving any regions behind\n\n4) The ribosome also acts as a catalyst','Only 1 and 4 are correct','An mRNA also has some additional sequences that are not translated and are referred as untranslated regions (UTR). The UTRs are present at both 5\' -end (before start codon) and at 3\' -end (after stop codon).\nA translational unit in mRNA is the sequence of RNA that is flanked by the start codon (AUG) and the stop codon and codes for a polypeptide. It should not be rRNA\nAn mRNA also has some additional sequences that are not translated and are referred as untranslated regions (UTR). \nThe ribosome also acts as a catalyst to form peptide bonds\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 115, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(10801,'chemistry','In SI system, mole (symbol, mol) was introduced as _______ base quantity for the amount of a substance.','Seventh','In SI system, mole (symbol, mol) was introduced as seventh base quantity for the amount of a substance.\n \n Reference: (NCERT Class-11 Page- 18 Paragraph-4)','Physical chemistry >> Some basic concepts of chemistry >> Mole Concept And Molar Masses',NULL,'1','mcq','Seventh','Sixth','Fifth','Fourth','NEW','11-018','11-018',NULL),
(283032,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: 23S rRNA in bacteria is important in translation\n\nReason: It aids in formation of peptide bonds','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','The ribosome also acts as a catalyst (23S rRNA in bacteria is the enzyme- ribozyme) for the formation of peptide bond.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 115, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283033,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: The two sites in large subunit of ribosome allow subsequent amino acid to bind to\n\nReason: The amino acids are close enough to form polypeptide bond between them','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','There are two sites in the large subunit, for subsequent amino acids to bind to and thus, be close enough to each other for the formation of a peptide bond. Polypeptide bond will not be formed \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 115, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283034,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding translation, choose the correct statements\n\n1)The ribosome moves from one amino acid to another along the mRNA\n\n2) For initiation, the ribosome binds to the mRNA at UAG\n\n3) The start codon is only recognized by a specific tRNA\n\n4) At the end, a release factor binds to the stop codon, terminating translation and releasing the complete polypeptide from the ribosome.','Only 3 and 4 are correct','The ribosome moves from codon to codon along the mRNA, while amino acids are added one by one, translated into Polypeptide sequences\nFor initiation, the ribosome binds to the mRNA at the start codon (AUG). UAG is a stop codon\nRibosome binds to the mRNA at the start codon (AUG) that is recognised only by the initiator tRNA\nAt the end, a release factor binds to the stop codon, terminating translation and releasing the complete polypeptide from the ribosome.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 115, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283035,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer \n\nAssertion: Ribosome binds to mRNA which is linked to initiator factor\n\nReason: Initiator tRNA will recognize the initiator factor to initiate translation','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','There is no initiation factor. For initiation, the ribosome binds to the mRNA at the start codon (AUG) that is recognised only by the initiator tRNA. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 115, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283036,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Regulation of Gene Expression, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Gene regulation can be carried out at various levels. \n\n2) In prokaryotes, genes can be regulated during transport of mRNA from nucleus to the cytoplasm\n\n3) Gene regulation can be conducted at four levels\n\n4) Regulation of splicing is a type of gene regulation.','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Considering that gene expression results in the formation of a polypeptide, it can be regulated at several levels.\nProkaryotes do not transport mRNA from nucleus to the cytoplasm\nIn eukaryotes, gene regulation can be done in transcriptional level, processing level, transport of mRNA from nucleus to the cytoplasm and translational level\nRegulation of splicing occurs during processing level therefore it is a type of regulation.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 115, paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283037,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Regulation of gene expression only occur at the highest level which is during translation\n\nReason: During translation, DNA is expressed to form functional proteins, therefore, requires a regulatory mechanism','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Regulation of gene expression refers to a very broad term that may occur at various levels. Considering that gene expression results in the formation of a polypeptide, it can be regulated at several levels. During translation, mRNA transcribed from DNA will be used as a template to synthesize amino acids resulting in formation of protein\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 115, paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283038,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Regulation of Gene Expression, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Metabolism can affect regulation of gene expression\n\n2) Beta-galactosidase is used to catalyse hydrolysis of polysaccharide in E.coli\n\n3) E.Coli will not synthesize enzyme beta-galactosidase if lactose is not available\n\n4) Regulation of certain genes expression plays a role in the development of an organism','Only 1, 3 and 4 are correct','It is the metabolic, physiological or environmental conditions that regulate the expression of genes. Therefore, metabolism is correct\nBeta-galactosidase synthesized by E.Coli is used to catalyse the hydrolysis of a disaccharide, lactose into galactose and glucose\nIf the bacteria do not have lactose around them to be utilised for energy source, they would no longer require the synthesis of the enzyme beta-galactosidase\nThe development and differentiation of embryo into adult organisms are also a result of the coordinated regulation of expression of several sets of genes.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 116, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1, 3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283039,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: E.Coli will not synthesize beta-galactosidase enzyme if galactose is not present\n\nReason: It does not have galactose to be utilized as energy source','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Beta-galactosidase is synthesised by E. coli, it is used to catalyse the hydrolysis of a disaccharide, lactose into galactose and glucose; the bacteria use them as a source of energy. Hence, if the bacteria do not have lactose around them to be utilised for energy source, they would no longer require the synthesis of the enzyme beta-galactosidase.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 116, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283040,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Regulation of Gene expression, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Regulatory proteins can act as activator and repressor\n\n2) Accessibilty of promoter regions of prokaryotic DNA is regulated by interaction with specific activator\n\n3) Interaction with accessory proteins in a transcription unit will affect the rate of transcription\n\n4) Each operon has a specific operator and specific repressor','Only 1 and 4 are correct','These regulatory proteins can act both positively (activators) and negatively (repressors). \nThe accessibility of promoter regions of prokaryotic DNA is in many cases regulated by the interaction of proteins with sequences termed operators\n In a transcription unit, the activity of RNA polymerase at a given promoter is in turn regulated by interaction with accessory proteins, which affect its ability to recognise start sites\nThe operator region is adjacent to the,promoter elements in most operons and in most cases the sequences of the operator bind a repressor protein. Each operon has its specific operator and specific repressor.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 116, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283041,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Ability of RNA polymerase to recognise start sites can be affected\n\nReason: Operon will bind to the specific repressor to regulate interaction.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','In a transcription unit, the activity of RNA polymerase at a given promoter is in turn regulated by interaction with accessory proteins, which affect its ability to recognise start sites. Each operon has its specific operator and specific repressor. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 116, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283042,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Lac Operon, choose the correct statements\n\n1)Lac operon interacts specifically with lac operator only\n\n2) Lac operator consists one structural gene and three regulatory genes\n\n3) Z gene in lac operon hydrolyzes lactose into galactose and glucose\n\n4) Transacetylase is one of the gene products pf lac operon','Only 3 and 4 are correct','Lac operator is present only in the lac operon and it interacts specifically with lac repressor only. Lac operon will not interact with lac operator.\nThe lac operon consists of one regulatory gene (i gene) and three structural genes (z, y, and a).\nThe z gene codes for beta-galactosidase (β-gal), which is primarily responsible for the hydrolysis of the disaccharide, lactose into its monomeric units, galactose and glucose\nThe a gene encodes a transacetylase\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 116, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283043,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Lac Operon, choose the correct statements\n\n1) his operon is one of the examples of operon\n\n2) The monocistronic structural gene in lac operon is regulated by the regulatory genes and a common promoter.\n\n3) i gene is one of the regulatory genes in Lac operon\n\n4) Beta-galactosidase, permease and transacetylase are gene products in Lac operon','Only 1 and 4 are correct','To name few operon examples, lac operon, trp operon, ara operon, his operon, val operon, etc.\n In lac operon, a polycistronic structural gene is regulated by a common promoter and regulatory genes.\nThe i gene codes for the repressor of the lac operon. It is the one and only regulatory gene in Lac operon\nThe z gene codes for beta-galactosidase, the y gene codes for permease and a gene encodes a transacetylase. Hence, all the three gene products in lac operon are required for metabolism of lactose\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 116, paragraph3 and paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283044,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: The genes present in the lac operon are needed together to function in the same or related metabolic pathway\n\nReason: All the four gene products in lac operon are required for metabolism of lactose.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','In most operons as well, the genes present in the operon are needed together to function in the same or related metabolic pathway. However, there are only three gene products that will be produced by the structural genes of lac operon. The genes are z,y and a genes.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 116, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121409,'chemistry','What is the correct order of decreasing stability of the following cations? Refer diagram(12145)','II>I>III','I. The first carbocation has no electron-withdrawing group to stabilize the positive charge, and hence is the least stable of the three.\n\nII. The second carbocation has an electron-donating methoxy (-OCH3) group that can stabilize the positive charge through resonance and inductive effects. Therefore, this carbocation is more stable than the first one.\n\nIII. The third carbocation has least stability because of -I effect of -OCH3 group.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','II>I>III','II>III>I','III>I>II','I>II>III','NEW','11-350','11-272','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121409q.png'),
(283045,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Lac operon, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Lactose is termed as activator of Lac operon\n\n2) A low level of lac operon expression has to be present in a cell at all time\n\n3) Lactose is the substrate for beta-galactosidase\n\n4) Lactose provided cannot be transported into cell without presence of glucose','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Lactose regulates switching on and off of the operon. Hence, it is termed as inducer not activator\nA very low level of expression of lac operon has to be present in the cell all the time, otherwise lactose cannot enter the cells\nLactose is the substrate for the enzyme beta-galactosidase and it regulates switching on and off of the operon.\nIn the absence of glucose, if lactose is provided in the growth medium of the bacteria, the lactose can be transported into the cells through the action of permease\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 117, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283046,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Lactose can still be transported into cell even though there is no preferred carbon source\n\nReason: Lactose is substrate for enzyme beta-galactosidase','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','Lactose is the substrate for the enzyme beta-galactosidase and it regulates switching on and off of the operon. It can still enter a cell without preferred carbon source via permease activity as low level of expression of lac operon is maintained in cell at all time\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 117, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283047,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Lac Operon, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The repressor is only synthesized when required\n\n2) In the presence of an inducer, such as lactose or allolactose, the repressor is inactivated by interaction with the inducer\n\n3) Lac operon is a type of regulation of enzyme synthesis by its substrate\n\n4) Repressor protein prevents RNA polymerase from transcribing of mRNA','Only 2 and 3 are correct','The repressor of the operon is synthesised all-the-time from the i gene.\n In the presence of an inducer, such as lactose or allolactose, the repressor is inactivated by interaction with the inducer\nRegulation of lac operon can also be visualised as regulation of enzyme synthesis by its substrate because beta-galactosidase is the enzyme while lactose being its substrate\nThe repressor protein binds to the operator region of the operon and prevents RNA polymerase from transcribing the operon.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 117, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283048,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: RNA polymerase is inhibited from transcribing operon by allolactose\n\nReason: Allolactose is a repressor','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','First of all, allolactose is an inducer that inactivates repressor. Next, repressor protein binds to the operator region of the\noperon to prevent RNA polymerase from transcribing the operon\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 117, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283049,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Human Genome Project, choose the correct statements\n\n1) If two individuals differ, their DNA sequences will be totally different\n\n2) Amino acids that code for protein determines the genetic makeup of an organism\n\n3) DNA can be cloned \n\n4) Human genome is a large information of DNA sequence from a single human cell','Only 3 and 4 are correct','If two individuals differ, then their DNA sequences should also be different, at least at some places. However, they are not entirely different as they will still be some common DNA sequences coding for certain similar protein\nThe sequence of bases in DNA that determines the genetic information of a given organism. In other words, genetic make-up of an organism or an individual lies in the DNA sequences.\nWith the establishment of genetic engineering techniques, yes, any desired piece of DNA can be cloned\nHuman genome is said to have approximately 3 x 10⁹ bp so it the information is extremely large\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 118, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions part 2',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283101,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: If two individuals differ, then their DNA sequences should also be different at certain places\n\nReason: It is the different amount of nucleotides in DNA determines the genetic information of a given organism','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','It is true that the DNA sequences in two different individuals should at least contain some differences. This is becauseit is the sequence of bases in DNA that determines the genetic information of a given organism \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 118, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283102,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Human Genome Project, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Data of Human Genome Project is stored in specialised laboratory\n\n2) Human DNA is made up 3 billion chemical base pairs\n\n3) One of the goals of Human Genome Project is to share the technology with other sectors\n\n4) Human Genome Project has been completed','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','Data of HGP is stored as databases\n Determine the sequences of the 3 billion chemical base pairs that make up human DNA is part of the goals of HGP\nTransfer related technologies to other sectors, such as industries is a goal to be achieved from HGP\nThe Human Genome Project was a 13-year project and completed in 2003\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 118, paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283103,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: DNA sequencing technique in HGP can only be applied on human DNA\n\nReason: DNA makeup of other organisms are different from us therefore requires different technique','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','DNA sequences can be applied in health care, agriculture, energy production, environmental remediation. Many non-human model organisms, such as bacteria, yeast have been sequenced\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 119, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283104,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Human Genome Project, choose the correct statements\n\n1) There are two major approaches in the methodolgy of Human Genome Project\n\n2) In sequencing, desired DNA segment from a cell is obtained \n\n3) Sequence Annotation involves the coding and non-coding sequences\n\n4) Yeast can be used as a host in sequencing','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','The two approaches are Expressed Sequence Tags and Sequence Annotation\nFor sequencing, the total DNA from a cell is isolated and converted into random fragments of relatively smaller sizes.\nSequence Annotation is a blind approach of simply sequencing the whole set of genome that contained all the coding and non-coding sequence, and later assigning different regions in the sequence with functions\nThe commonly used hosts were bacteria and yeast, and the vectors were called as BAC (bacterial artificial chromosomes), and YAC (yeast artificial chromosomes).\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 119, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283105,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer \n\nAssertion: The cloning resulted into amplification of each piece of DNA fragment \n\nReason: It focused on identifying all the genes that are expressed as RNA','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','The approach of identifying all the genes that are expressed as RNA is referred to as Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs).\nThe cloning resulted into amplification of each piece of DNA fragment due to the total DNA from a cell is isolated and converted into random fragments of relatively smaller sizes and cloned in suitable host using specialised vectors. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 119, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283106,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion:  Total DNA from a cell will be turned into smaller pieces in sequencing\n\nReason: It cannot fit into the bacteria and yeast host','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','For sequencing, the total DNA from a cell is isolated and converted into random fragments of relatively smaller sizes. This is because DNA is a very long polymer, and there are technical limitations in sequencing very long pieces of DNA\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 119, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283107,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Human Genome Project, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Generation of overlapping fragments is important in sequencing\n\n2) Frederick Singer developed method to determine sequences of amino acids in proteins\n\n3) Normal computer database can be used to store the information obtained from sequencing\n\n4) The fragments were sequenced using automated DNA sequencers','Only 1 and 4 are correct','These sequences were then arranged based on some overlapping regions present in them. This required generation of overlapping fragments for sequencing.\nFrederick Sanger is the person who developed the method of determining amino acid sequences in proteins, not Singer\nAlignment of these sequences was humanly not possible. Therefore, specialised computer based programs were developed. \nThe fragments were sequenced using automated DNA sequencers that worked on the principle of a method developed by Frederick Sanger.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 119, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283108,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Generation of overlapping fragments has to be done during sequencing\n\nReason: These sequences were subsequently annotated','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','These sequences were then arranged based on some overlapping regions present in them. This required generation of overlapping fragments for sequencing.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 119, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283109,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Salient Features of Human Genome, choose the correct statements\n\n1) All genes have the same size\n\n2) Chromosome Y has the fewest genes\n\n3) Repeated sequences can be repeated up to thousand times\n\n4) Most of the nucleotide bases are different in all humanity','Only 2 and 3 are correct','The average gene consists of 3000 bases, but sizes vary greatly,\nChromosome 1 has most genes (2968), and the Y has the fewest (231).\nRepetitive sequences are stretches of DNA sequences that are repeated many times, sometimes hundred to thousand times.\nAlmost all (99.9 %) nucleotide bases are exactly the same in all people.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 120, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283110,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Repeated sequences make up very large portion of the human genome\n\nReason: They code for different essential amino acids','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','Repetitive sequences are stretches of DNA sequences that are repeated many times, sometimes hundred to thousand times so they make up a large portion of the human genome\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 120, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283111,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA Fingerprinting, choose the correct statements\n\n1) The difference in DNA causes difference in physical appearance among individuals\n\n2) DNA fingerprinting is used compare the differences in the fingerprint of any two individuals\n\n3) Sequencing DNA is often used because it is more efficient\n\n4) DNA fingerprinting is used among individuals of a same population','Only 1 and 4 are correct','It is these differences in sequence of DNA which make every individual unique in their phenotypic appearance. Phenotypic refers to physical\nDNA fingerprinting is to compare the DNA sequences of any two individuals\nSequencing the DNA every time is a daunting and expensive task. DNA fingerprinting is quicker \nDNA fingerprinting is a very quick way to compare the DNA sequences of any two individuals or among individuals in a population.\n\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 121, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283112,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: DNA fingerprinting is a more desirable way than sequencing to compare DNA sequences\n\nReason: DNA fingerprinting is a quicker','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','DNA fingerprinting is a very quick way to compare the DNA sequences of any two individuals, while sequencing involves more complicated steps and more daunting not to mention the higher cost\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 121, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283113,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA Fingerprinting, choose the correct statements\n\n1) DNA from blood and hair has different degree of polymorphism\n\n2) DNA can be separated by density gradient centrifugation\n\n3) DNA Fingerprinting recognizes differences in repetitive DNA \n\n4) There is only one type of satellite DNA','Only 2 and 3 are correct','DNA from every tissue (such as blood, hair-follicle, skin, bone, saliva, sperm etc.), from an individual show the same degree of polymorphism\nDNA are separated from bulk genomic DNA as different peaks during density gradient centrifugation\nDNA fingerprinting involves identifying differences in some specific regions in DNA sequence called as repetitive DNA\n Depending on base composition  length of segment, and number of repetitive units, the satellite DNA is classified into many categories, such as micro-satellites, mini-satellites etc\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 121, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283114,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA Fingerprinting, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Satellite DNA is the major peak when repetitive DNA is being separated\n\n2) DNA Fingerprinting can be used in determining blood relationship between two individuals\n\n3) Basis of DNA Fingerprinting is built on polymorphism in DNA sequence\n\n4) Hair follicle cannot be used in forensic identification','Only 2 and 3 are correct','The bulk DNA forms a major peak and the other small peaks are referred to as satellite DNA.\nDNA fingerprinting is the basis of paternity testing\n Polymorphism in DNA sequence is the basis of genetic mapping of human genome as well as of DNA fingerprinting\nSince DNA from every tissue (such as blood, hair-follicle, skin, bone, saliva, sperm etc.), from an individual show the same degree of polymorphism, they become very useful identification tool in forensic applications\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 121, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283115,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: DNA Fingerprinting can be applied in paternity testing\n\nReason: DNA from every tissue of individuals shows same degree of polymorphism','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','DNA from every tissue from an individual show the same degree of polymorphism, they become very useful identification tool in forensic applications. Further, as the polymorphisms are inheritable from parents to children, DNA fingerprinting is the basis of paternity testing\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 121, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283116,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Satellite DNAs can be classified into two categories\n\nReason: It is solely categorised depending on the base composition which is A:T rich or G:C rich','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Depending on base composition (A : T rich or G:C rich), length of segment, and number of repetitive units, the satellite DNA is classified into many categories, such as micro-satellites, mini-satellites etc\n(Reference  :NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 121, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283117,'biology','Consider the statements regarding Polymorphism, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Polymorphism is a result of mutations\n\n2) New mutations may arise in both somatic and germ cells\n\n3) Germ cells can be found in all types of organisms\n\n4) Polymorphism means variation in a phenotypic degree','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Polymorphism arises due to mutations\nNew mutations may arise in an individual either in somatic cells or in the germ cells\nGerm cells are cells that generate gametes in sexually reproducing organisms therefore not found in asexual organisms.\nPolymorphism is variation at genetic level\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 121, paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283118,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Polymorphism will only occur in sexual organisms\n\nReason: Polymorphism can occur in germ cells only','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','Polymorphisms can occur in both somatic or germ cells therefore polymorphism can still take place in asexual organisms. In fact, it arises in every species\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 121, paragraph4)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false.','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283119,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding Polymorphism, choose the correct statements\n\n1) DNA polymorphism is inheritable\n\n2) Germ cell mutation can spread via sexual reproduction\n\n3) Variations in non-coding sequence will not have immediate effect in an individual\'s reproductive ability\n\n4) There is a variety of different types of polymorphisms','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','If an inheritable mutation is observed in a population at high frequency, it is referred to as DNA polymorphism\nIf a germ cell mutation does not seriously impair individual’s ability to have offspring who can transmit the mutation, it can spread through sexual reproduction\nMutations in non-coding sequences may not have any immediate effect/impact in an individual’s reproductive ability\nThere is a variety of different types of polymorphisms ranging from single nucleotide change to very large scale changes.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 122, paragraph1','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only 1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283120,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: DNA Polymorphism is more likely to be seen in non-coding DNA sequences\n\nReason: Mutations in these sequences have any immediate effect/impact on an individual','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','The probability of such variation to be observed in noncoding DNA sequence would be higher as mutations in these sequences may not have any immediate effect/impact in an individual’s reproductive ability. These mutations keep on accumulating generation after generation, and form one of the basis of polymorphism.\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 122, paragraph1)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283121,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA Fingerprinting, choose the correct statements\n\n1) Alec Jeffreys used a VNTR as probe to show high degree of polymorphism\n\n2) DNA Fingerprinting technique showed by Alec Jeffreys involves Western Blotting\n\n3) DNA fragments are separated using electrophoresis in DNA Fingerprinting\n\n4) Isolation of DNA is the first step to be carried out during DNA Fingerprinting','Only1,3 and 4 are correct','He used a satellite DNA as probe that shows very high degree of polymorphism.  It was called as Variable Number of Tandem Repeats (VNTR). \nThe technique, as used earlier, involved Southern blot hybridisation using radiolabelled VNTR as a probe\nSeparation of DNA fragments by electrophoresis is one of the steps in DNA Fingerprinting\n The first step in DNA Fingerprinting is isolation of DNA before being digested and so on \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 122, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1,2 and 3 are correct','Only1,3 and 4 are correct','Only 2,3 and 4 are correct','1,2,3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283122,'biology','Consider the assertion and reason, choose the correct answer\n\nAssertion: Variable Number of Tandem Repeats is used as a radiolabelled probe by Alec Jeffreys in DNA Fingerprinting\n\nReason: It shows high degree of ionisation','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','The technique of DNA Fingerprinting was initially developed by Alec Jeffreys. He used a satellite DNA as probe that shows very high degree of polymorphism. It was called as Variable Number of Tandem Repeats (VNTR)\n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 122, paragraph2)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is true but Reason is false','If both Assertion and Reason are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(283123,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA Fingerprinting, choose the correct statements\n\n1) VNTR is arranged in many copy numbers\n\n2) The copy numbers of VNTR are the same in all chromosomes\n\n3) The bands after hybridization with VNTR probe show same sizes\n\n4)The size of VNTR varies from 0.1 to 20kb','Only 1 and 4 are correct','The VNTR is a small DNA sequence is arranged tandemly in many copy numbers \nThe copy number of VNTR varies from chromosome to chromosome in an individual.\nThe numbers of repeat show very high degree of polymorphism so after hybridisation with VNTR probe, it gives many bands of differing sizes\nThe numbers of repeat show very high degree of polymorphism. As a result the size of VNTR varies in size from 0.1 to 20 kb. \n(Reference  : NCERT Biology class 12, chapter 6, pg 122, paragraph3)','Genetics and Evolution >> Molecular Basis of Inheritance >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 and 2 are correct','Only 1 and 4 are correct','Only 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121410,'chemistry','Correct IUPAC name of the compound is : Refer diagram(12146)','3,4-dimethyl hexane','There are five carbon atoms in the parent chain (pentane) and that the two methyl groups are attached to the second and third carbon atoms. The positions of the methyl groups are indicated by the numbers 2 and 3, respectively. Therefore, the complete IUPAC name for the compound is "2,3-dimethylpentane".','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','2-ethyl-3-methyl pentane','3,4-dimethyl hexane','2-sec-butylbutane','2,3-dimethylbutane','NEW','11-341','11-263','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121410q.png'),
(300110,'chemistry','Assertion: drug classification based on pharmacological effect is useful for doctors.\nReason: it provides the whole range of drugs available for the treatment of particular type of problem.','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','On the basis of pharmacological effect This classification is based on pharmacological effect of the drugs. It is useful for doctors because it provides them the whole range of drugs available for the treatment of a particular type of problem. For example, analgesics have pain killing effect, antiseptics kill or arrest the growth of microorganisms.  (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is True  but the Reason is false','Both the Assertion and Reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300111,'chemistry','Which of the following drug classification is very useful for doctors','Pharmacological effect','On the basis of pharmacological effect This classification is based on pharmacological effect of the drugs. It is useful for doctors because it provides them the whole range of drugs available for the treatment of a particular type of problem. For example, analgesics have pain killing effect, antiseptics kill or arrest the growth of microorganisms.  (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Chemical Structures','Drug Action','Molecular targets','Pharmacological effect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300112,'chemistry','Which of the Following is an Example of Drug based on its Pharmacological effect :','All the above','On the basis of pharmacological effect This classification is based on pharmacological effect of the drugs. It is useful for doctors because it provides them the whole range of drugs available for the  treatment of a particular type of problem. For example, analgesics have pain killing effect, antiseptics kill or arrest the growth of microorganisms.  (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Antiseptics','Analgesics','Antipyretics','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300113,'chemistry','Anti-inflammatory type of drugs works on a Particular  ____________ Process.','Biochemical','On the basis of drug action It is based on the action of a drug on a particular biochemical process. For example, all antihistamines inhibit the action of the compound, histamine which causes inflammation in the body. There are various ways in which action of histamines can be blocked.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','fillup','Bio,chem,ical,Phys,logi,cal',NULL,NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300114,'chemistry','Anhistamines fall in which classification of Drug','Classification On the basis of drug action','On the basis of drug action It is based on the action of a drug on a particular biochemical process. For example, all antihistamines inhibit the action of the compound, histamine which causes inflammation in the body. There are various ways in which action of histamines can be blocked.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Classification the basis of pharmacological effect','Classification On the basis of drug action','Classification On the basis of chemical structure','Classification On the basis of molecular targets','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300115,'chemistry','Drug Classified on the basis of chemical structure share _________ structural features and often have  similar ___________ activities .','Common,Pharmacological','On the basis of chemical structure It is based on the chemical structure of the drug. Drugs classified in this way share common structural features and often have similar pharmacological activity.  For example, sulphonamides have common structural feature, given below.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Common,Physiological','Uncommon,Physiological','Common,Biochemical','Common,Pharmacological','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300116,'chemistry','Which of the following class of drug have similar chemical structure and often have similar physiological activity','Classification On the basis of chemical structure','On the basis of chemical structure It is based on the chemical structure of the drug. Drugs classified in this way share common structural features and often have similar pharmacological activity. For example, sulphonamides have common structural feature, given below.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Classification the basis of pharmacological effect','Classification On the basis of drug action','Classification On the basis of chemical structure','Classification On the basis of molecular targets','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300117,'chemistry','Sulphonamides are example of which class of drugs','Chemical Structures','On the basis of chemical structure It is based on the chemical structure of the drug. Drugs classified in this way share common structural features and often have similar pharmacological activity. For example, sulphonamides have common structural feature, given below.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Chemical Structures','Drug Action','Molecular targets','Pharmacological effect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300118,'chemistry','The classification based on________________ is the most useful classification for medicinal chemists.','Molecular targets','The classification based on molecular targets is the most useful classification for medicinal chemists.(Reference: NCERT- Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Chemical Structures','Drug Action','Molecular targets','Pharmacological effect','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300119,'chemistry','Drugs possessing some common structural features may have the same ____________ on targets.','Mechanism of action','Drugs possessing some common structural features may have the same mechanism of action on targets. The classification based on molecular targets is the most useful classification for medicinal chemists.(Reference: NCERT- Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Physiological activity','Mechanism of action','Same effect','None of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300120,'chemistry','Drug usually interact with','All the above','Drugs usually interact with biomolecules such as carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids. These are called target molecules or drug targets.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Lipid','Carbohydrates','Protein','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300121,'chemistry','Assertion: The classification based on molecular targets is the most useful classification for medicinal chemists.\n\nReason: Drugs possessing some common structural features may have the same mechanism of action on targets','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','Drugs possessing some common structural features may have the same mechanism of action on targets. The classification based on molecular targets is the most useful classification for medicinal chemists.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 1)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both the Assertion and the Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion','The Assertion and the Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion','Assertion is false but the Reason is true.','Both the Assertion and Reason are false.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300122,'chemistry','__________which perform the role of biological catalysts in the body are called __________.','Protein, Enzymes','Proteins which perform the role of biological catalysts in the body are called  enzymes.  (Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'2','mcq','Protein, Coenzyme','Lipid, Enzymes','Protein, Enzymes','Nucleic acid,Promoter','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300123,'chemistry','Which are crucial to communication system in the body are','Receptor','Which are crucial to communication system in the body are called  receptors.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Enzyme','Receptor','Protein  bodies','None of these','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(300124,'chemistry','Carrier proteins carry ________molecules across the cell membrane','Polar','Carrier proteins carry polar molecules across the cell membrane.(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 448; Paragraph No. 2)','Organic Chemistry >> Chemistry in everydaylife >> Drugs and Drug-Target Interactions',NULL,'1','mcq','Non polar','Polar','Neutral','Reactive','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
