(301111,'biology','HIV patients need not be separated from family.','TRUE','It is important to note that HIV/AIDS is not spread by mere touch or physical contact; it spreads only through body fluids. It is, hence, imperative, for the physical and psychological well-being, that the HIV/AIDS infected persons are not isolated from family and society','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301112,'biology','The time between infection with HIV and appearance of symptoms of AIDS is-','5-10 years','There is always a time-lag between the infection and appearance of AIDS symptoms. This period may vary from a few months to many years (usually 5-10 years).','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','15-20 years','5-10 years','25-30 years','35-40 years','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301113,'biology','After getting into the body of the person, the virus enters into_________ where RNA genome of the virus replicates.','Macrophages','After getting into the body of the person, the virus enters into macrophages where RNA genome of the virus replicates to form viral DNA with the help of the enzyme reverse transcriptase.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','Eosinophils','Neutrophils','Macrophages','Lymphocytes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301114,'biology','HIV RNA genome replicates to form viral DNA with the help of the enzyme-','Reverse transcriptase','After getting into the body of the person, the virus enters into macrophages where RNA genome of the virus replicates to form viral DNA with the help of the enzyme reverse transcriptase.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','Reverse transcriptase','DNA polymerase','RNA polymerase','Restriction endonucleases','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301115,'biology','_______ directs the infected cells to produce virus particles.','Viral DNA','After getting into the body of the person, the virus enters into macrophages where RNA genome of the virus replicates to form viral DNA with the help of the enzyme reverse transcriptase. This viral DNA gets incorporated into host cell’s DNA and directs the infected cells to produce virus particles','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','Host RNA','Viral DNA','Viral RNA','Host DNA','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301116,'biology','The _________ continue to produce virus and in this way acts like a HIV factory.','Macrophages','The macrophages continue to produce virus and in this way acts like a HIV factory.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','Eosinophils','Neutrophils','Macrophages','Lymphocytes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301117,'biology','HIV enters into _______, replicates and produce progeny viruses.','Helper T lymphocytes','HIV enters into helper t-lymphocytes (TH ), replicates and produce progeny viruses. The progeny viruses released in the blood attack other helper T-lymphocytes. This is repeated leading to a progressive decrease in the number of helper T lymphocytes in the body of the infected person','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','B lymphocytes','Macrophages','Eosinophils','Helper T lymphocytes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301118,'biology','Long term infection with HIV causes a decrease in-','Helper T lymphocytes','HIV enters into helper t-lymphocytes (TH ), replicates and produce progeny viruses. The progeny viruses released in the blood attack other helper T-lymphocytes. This is repeated leading to a progressive decrease in the number of helper T lymphocytes in the body of the infected person','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','B lymphocytes','Macrophages','Eosinophils','Helper T lymphocytes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301119,'biology','Which of the following is an infection that is probable in HIV patients and not in healthy individuals?','Mycobacterium','Due to decrease in the number of helper T lymphocytes, the person starts suffering from infections that could have been otherwise overcome such as those due to bacteria especially mycobacterium, viruses, fungi and even parasites like toxoplasma','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','Typhoid','Mycobacterium','Polio','Diphtheria','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301120,'biology','Diagnostic test for HIV is-','ELISA','A widely used diagnostic test for AIDS is enzyme linked immuno-sorbent assay (ELISA)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','Widal','Coombs','ELISA','RDT','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301121,'biology','The treatment for AIDS is :','Anti-retroviral drugs','Treatment of AIDS with anti-retroviral drugs is only partially effective. They can only prolong the life of the patient but cannot prevent death, which is inevitable','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','Anti-bacterial drugs','Anti-fungal drugs','Anti-retroviral drugs','Anti-spasmodic drugs','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301122,'biology','Which of the following is spread through conscious behaviour patterns?','AIDS','HIV infection, more often, spreads due to conscious behaviour patterns and is not something that happens inadvertently, like pneumonia or typhoid.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','Typhoid','AIDS','Pneumonia','Ascariasis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301123,'biology','Which of the following can help prevent HIV AIDS? \n1.Use of disposable needles \n2.Practising safe sex \n3.Regular HIV testing \n4.Regular exercise','1,2,3','Making blood (from blood banks) safe from HIV, ensuring the use of only disposable needles and syringes in public and private hospitals and clinics, free distribution of condoms, controlling drug abuse, advocating safe sex and promoting regular check-ups for HIV in susceptible populations, are some such steps taken up.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> AIDS',NULL,'2','mcq','1,2','1,3','2,3,4','1,2,3','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301201,'biology','Which of the following is the basis for cancer?','Unregulated cell growth and differentiation','In our body, cell growth and differentiation is highly controlled and regulated. In cancer cells, there is breakdown of these regulatory mechanisms.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Cancer',NULL,'2','mcq','Contracted HIV','Unregulated cell growth and differentiation','Decrease of platelets','Decrease cell regulators','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301202,'biology','Which of the following property is not of cancer cells but of normal cells?','Contact inhibition','Normal cells show a property called contact inhibition by virtue of which contact with other cells inhibits their uncontrolled growth. Cancer cells appears to have lost this property','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Cancer',NULL,'2','mcq','Dysplasia','Contact inhibition','Meiotic division','Metastasis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301203,'biology','How many types of tumours are there?','2','Tumors are of two types: benign and malignant. Benign tumors normally remain confined to their original location and do not spread to other parts of the body and cause little damage. The malignant tumors, on the other hand are a mass of proliferating cells called neoplastic or tumor cells.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Cancer',NULL,'1','mcq','1','2','3','4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301204,'biology','_____________ tumors normally remain confined to their original location.','Benign','Benign tumors normally remain confined to their original location and do not spread to other parts of the body and cause little damage.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Cancer',NULL,'2','mcq','Benign','Malignant','Pre-malignant','No fixed rule','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301205,'biology','_______tumors are a mass of proliferating cells called neoplastic or tumor cells.','Malignant','The malignant tumors, on the other hand are a mass of proliferating cells called neoplastic or tumor cells.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Cancer',NULL,'2','mcq','Benign','Malignant','Pre-malignant','No fixed rule','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301206,'biology','Tumour cells are sloughed off, reach distant sites and start a new tumour mass there. This is called-','Metastasis','As these cells actively divide and grow they also starve the normal cells by competing for vital nutrients. Cells sloughed from such tumors reach distant sites through blood, and wherever they get lodged in the body, they start a new tumor there. This property called metastasis is the most feared property of malignant tumors.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Cancer',NULL,'2','mcq','Self sufficiency','Angiogenesis','Metastasis','Limitless multiplication','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301207,'biology','Agents transforming normal cells into cancer cells are called-','Carcinogens','Transformation of normal cells into cancerous neoplastic cells may be induced by physical, chemical or biological agents. These agents are called carcinogens.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Cancer',NULL,'2','fillup','Ca,rci,no,ge,ns',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301208,'biology','Carcinogens are-\n1.Physiological\n2.Biological\n3.Physical \n4.Chemical','2,3,4','Transformation of normal cells into cancerous neoplastic cells may be induced by physical, chemical or biological agents. These agents are called carcinogens.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Cancer',NULL,'2','mcq','1,2','2,3','1,2,3','2,3,4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301209,'biology','Which of the following radiations are not carcinogenic?','Visible light','Ionising radiations like x-rays and gamma rays and non-ionizing radiations like uv cause DNA damage leading to neoplastic transformation','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Cancer',NULL,'2','mcq','X-rays','Gamma rays','UV rays','Visible light','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
