(301334,'biology','Tobacco contains _______, an alkaloid.','Nicotine','Tobacco contains a large number of chemical substances including nicotine, an alkaloid.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Drugs and Alcohol Abuse',NULL,'1','mcq','Caffeine','Nicotine','Ephedrine','Strychnine','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301335,'biology','Nicotine stimulates ______ gland.','Adrenal','Nicotine stimulates adrenal gland to release adrenaline and nor-adrenaline into blood circulation, both of which raise blood pressure and increase heart rate.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Drugs and Alcohol Abuse',NULL,'1','mcq','Thyroid','Pituitary','Parathyroid','Adrenal','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301336,'biology','Smoking increases _________ content in blood.','Carbon monoxide','Smoking increases carbon monoxide (CO) content in blood and reduces the concentration of haembound oxygen.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Drugs and Alcohol Abuse',NULL,'2','mcq','Caffeine','Tobacco','CO₂','Carbon monoxide','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301337,'biology','________ is a very effective sedative and painkiller, and is very useful in patients who have undergone surgery','Morphine','Drugs like barbiturates, amphetamines, benzodiazepines, and other similar drugs, that are normally used as medicines to help patients cope with mental illnesses like depression and insomnia, are often abused. Morphine is a very effective sedative and painkiller, and is very useful in patients who have undergone surgery.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Drugs and Alcohol Abuse',NULL,'2','fillup','Mo,rp,hi,ne',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301338,'biology','Match the following','Opioids-binds to specific opioid receptors present in CNS and GI tract, Cannabinoids-interact with cannabinoid receptors present in the brain,Cocaine-has a potent stimulating action on CNS producing a sense of euphoria,Morphine-very effective sedative and painkiller which useful in patients who have undergone surgery','Opioids-drugs which bind to specific opioid receptors present in our central nervous system and gastrointestinal tract.,\nCannabinoids-a group of chemicals which interact with cannabinoid receptors present principally in the brain.,\nCocaine-has a potent stimulating action on central nervous system producing a sense of euphoria and increased energy.\nMorphine-very effective sedative and painkiller which useful in patients who have undergone surgery','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Drugs and Alcohol Abuse',NULL,'3','match','Opioids,binds to specific opioid receptors present in CNS and GIT','Cannabinoids,interact with cannabinoid receptors present in the brain','Cocaine,has a potent stimulating action on CNS producing a sense of euphoria','Morphine,very effective sedative and painkiller which useful in patients who have undergone surgery','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301339,'biology','Match the following','Heroin-obtained by acetylation of morphine,morphine-latex of poppy plant papaver somniferum,cocaine-erythroxylum coca,cannabinoids-inflorescences of the plant cannabis sativa','Heroin-obtained by acetylation of morphine,\nMorphine-latex of poppy plant papaver somniferum,\nCocaine-erythroxylum coca,\ncannabinoids-inflorescences of the plant cannabis sativa','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Drugs and Alcohol Abuse',NULL,'2','match','Heroin,obtained by acetylation of morphine','Morphine,latex of poppy plant papaver somniferum','Cocaine,erythroxylum coca','Cannabinoids,inflorescences of the plant cannabis sativa','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301340,'biology','_______ is associated with increased incidence of cancer of urinary bladder, gastric ulcer, emphysema and coronary heart disease :','Smoking','Smoking is associated with increased incidence of cancers of lung, urinary bladder and throat,\nbronchitis, emphysema, coronary heart disease, gastric ulcer, etc. 1st paragraph .. pg.160','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Drugs and Alcohol Abuse',NULL,'2','mcq','Alcohol','STDs','Drug abuse','Smoking','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301401,'biology','The period between _____ years of age may be thought of as adolescence period.','12 - 18','The period between 12-18 years of age may be thought of as adolescence period.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Adolescence and Drug/Alcohol abuse',NULL,'2','mcq','9 - 24','10 - 20','10 - 19','12 - 18','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301402,'biology','Which of the following is/are a contributing factor(s) to alcohol abuse? \n1.Peer pressure\n2.Isolation\n3.Stress\n4.Need for adventure','1,2,3,4','Curiosity, need for adventure and excitement, and experimentation, constitute common causes, which motivate youngsters towards drug.the first use of drugs or alcohol, . Other factors that have been seen to be associated with drug and alcohol abuse among adolescents are unstable or unsupportive family structures and peer pressure. may be out of curiosity or experimentation, but later the child starts using these to escape facing problems. Of late, stress, and alcohol use.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Adolescence and Drug/Alcohol abuse',NULL,'2','mcq','1,2,3','1,2','1,4','1,2,3,4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301501,'biology','______ is a psychological attachment to certain effects  associated with drugs and alcohol.','Addiction','Addiction is a psychological attachment to certain effects – such as euphoria and a temporary feeling of well-being – associated with drugs and alcohol.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'1','mcq','Addiction','Dependence','Vulnerability','Enjoyment','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301502,'biology','With repeated use of drugs, the tolerance level of the receptors present in our body __________.','Increases','With repeated use of drugs, the tolerance level of the receptors present in our body increases.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'2','mcq','Decreases','Stays the same','Fluctuates frequently','Increases','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301503,'biology','Dependence is the tendency of the body to manifest a characteristic and _______ syndrome if regular dose of drugs/alcohol is abruptly discontinued.','Withdrawal','Dependence is the tendency of the body to manifest a characteristic and unpleasant withdrawal syndrome if regular dose of drugs/alcohol is abruptly discontinued.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'1','mcq','Schnitzler','Down','Withdrawal','Goodpasture','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301504,'biology','Excessive drug usage may lead to-','Heart failure','Excessive doses of drugs may lead to coma and death due to respiratory failure, heart failure or cerebral hemorrhage.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'1','mcq','Heart failure','Active system','Respiratory enhancement','No effect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301505,'biology','Which of the following is a symptom of withdrawal syndrome?\n1.Anxiety\n2.Nausea\n3.Shakiness\n4.Euphoria','1,2,3','Withdrawal syndrome is characterised by anxiety, shakiness, nausea and sweating, which may be relieved when use is resumed again.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'2','mcq','1 only','1,2','4 only','1,2,3','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301506,'biology','What does taking drugs \'intravenously\' mean?','Injecting','Those who take drugs intravenously (direct injection into the vein using a needle and syringe), are much more likely to acquire serious infections like AIDS and hepatitis B.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'1','mcq','Snorting','Injecting','Inhaling','Orally ingesting','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301507,'biology','Administering drugs intravenously often leads to diseases like ____.','AIDS','Those who take drugs intravenously (direct injection into the vein using a needle and syringe), are much more likely to acquire serious infections like AIDS and hepatitis B.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'2','mcq','AIDS','Down syndrome','Schnitzler syndrome','Hepatitis A','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301508,'biology','AIDS can be transmitted through _________.','Sexual contact/ infected blood','Both can be transmitted through sexual contact or infected blood.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'1','mcq','Sexual contact/ physical contact','Physical contact/ infected blood','Physical contact/ sneezing','Sexual contact/ infected blood','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301509,'biology','Administering drugs intravenously often leads to diseases like ____.','Hepatitis B','Those who take drugs intravenously (direct injection into the vein using a needle and syringe), are much more likely to acquire serious infections like AIDS and hepatitis B.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'2','mcq','Hepatitis B','Down syndrome','Schnitzler syndrome','Hepatitis A','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301510,'biology','Chronic use of alcohol causes-','Cirrhosis','The use of alcohol during adolescence may also have long-term effects. It could lead to heavy drinking in adulthood. The chronic use of drugs and alcohol damages nervous system and liver (cirrhosis).','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'3','mcq','Cirrhosis','Downs syndrome','Intestinal obstruction','Asthma','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301511,'biology','Which of the following is not used by athletes to enhance performance?','Beta blockers','Another misuse of drugs is what certain sportspersons do to enhance their performance. They (mis)use narcotic analgesics, anabolic steroids, diuretics and certain hormones in sports to increase muscle strength and bulk and to promote aggressiveness and as a result increase athletic performance','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'2','mcq','Hormones','Analgesics','Steroids','Beta blockers','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301512,'biology','Which of the following is used by athletes to enhance performance?\n1.Dopamine\n2.Analgesics\n3.Steroids\n4.Hormones','2,3,4','Another misuse of drugs is what certain sportspersons do to enhance their performance. They (mis)use narcotic analgesics, anabolic steroids, diuretics and certain hormones in sports to increase muscle strength and bulk and to promote aggressiveness and as a result increase athletic performance','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'2','mcq','1,2,3','1,3','1,4','2,3,4','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301513,'biology','In females, increased aggressiveness, mood swings, depression, abnormal menstrual cycles, excessive hair growth on the face and body, enlargement of clitoris, deepening of voice are all side effects of-','Steroids','The side-effects of the use of anabolic steroids in females include masculinisation (features like males), increased aggressiveness, mood swings, depression, abnormal menstrual cycles, excessive hair growth on the face and body, enlargement of clitoris, deepening of voice.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'2','mcq','Hormones','Analgesics','Steroids','Beta blockers','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301514,'biology','In males, increased aggressiveness, mood swings, depression, reduction of size of the testicles, decreased sperm production, potential for kidney and liver dysfunction, breast enlargement, premature baldness, enlargement of the prostate gland are all side effects of-','Steroids','In males steroid abuse causes includes acne, increased aggressiveness, mood swings, depression, reduction of size of the testicles, decreased sperm production, potential for kidney and liver dysfunction, breast enlargement, premature baldness, enlargement of the prostate gland.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Addiction and Dependence',NULL,'2','mcq','Hormones','Analgesics','Steroids','Beta blockers','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301601,'biology','Which of the following is an effective measure to control drug abuse?\n1.Education\n2.Steroids\n3.Avoidance of stressors\n4.Medical help','1,3,4','Educating and counselling him/ her to face problems and stresses, and to accept disappointments and failures as a part of life, - Help from parents and peers should be sought immediately so that they can guide appropriately, A lot of help is available in the form of highly qualified psychologists, psychiatrists, and deaddiction and rehabilitation programmes to help individuals who have unfortunately got in the quagmire of drug/alcohol abuse.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Prevention and control',NULL,'2','mcq','1,3,4','3,4','1,2,3','2,3','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301602,'biology','Who amongst the following can play the most important role against drug abuse?','Teachers','Alert parents and teachers need to look for and identify the danger signs discussed above. Even friends, if they find someone using drugs or alcohol, should not hesitate to bring this to the notice of parents or teacher in the best interests of the person concerned.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Prevention and control',NULL,'2','mcq','Houseworkers','Drug sellers','Friends','Teachers','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301701,'biology','Transplantation of tissues/organs fails often due to non­acceptance by the patient’s body. Which type of immune response is responsible for such rejections?[2017]','Cell mediated immune response','Transplantation of tissue/organ often fails due to non acceptance by the patient’s body therefore, tissue matching and blood group matching are essential before undertaking any graft/transplant. When the immune system recognises the protein in the transplanted tissue or organ as foreign, it initiates cellular immunity. As a result of this, there is a rejection of transplanted organs. To suppress the immune response during transplantation, histocompatibility antigen and immunosuppressants play an important role','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cell mediated immune response','Hormonal immune response','Physiological immune response','Autoimmune response','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301702,'biology','Malt constitutes about _________ percent of the lymphoid tissue in human body.[2017]','50%','Malt are significant aggregations of lymphoid tissues which are seen in relation to the mucosa of the major tracts like respiratory, alimentary canal and urogenital tracts. It constitutes about 50 percent of the lymphoid tissue in human body','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','20%','70%','10%','50%','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301703,'biology','Which of the following sets of diseases is caused by bacteria?[2016]','Cholera and tetanus','Cholera is caused by bacterium vibrio cholerae, tetanus is caused by bacterium clostridium tetani, typhoid is caused by bacterium salmonella typhi, small pox is caused by variola virus, mumps is caused by paramyxovirus, herpes is caused herpes simplex virus and influenza is caused by orthomyxovirus.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cholera and tetanus','Typhoid and smallpox','Tetanus and mumps','Herpes and influenza','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301704,'biology','Which of the following is correct regarding AIDS causative agent HIV?[2016]','HIV is enveloped virus that contains two identical molecules of single stranded RNA','HIV is spherical virus with a diameter of about 90­120 nm. Its genome consists of a single­ stranded RNA filament segmented into two identical filaments and associated with reverse transcriptase enzymes. The envelope consists of a lipid bilayer derived from host cell membrane and projecting knob like glycoprotein spikes. It contains two protein coats. HIV is a retrovirus that attacks helper T cells. Without an adequate supply of helper cells, the immune system cannot signal B cells to produce antibodies to kill infected cells, thus body becomes susceptible to infections. This immune deficiency is described by the name acquired immune deficiency syndrome or AIDS.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','HIV is enveloped virus containing one molecule of single stranded RNA and one molecule of reverse transcriptase.','HIV is enveloped virus that contains two identical molecules of single stranded RNA','HIV is enveloped retrovirus.','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301705,'biology','Antivenom injection contains preformed antibodies while polio drops that are administered into the body contain[2016]','Attenuated pathogens','The sabin vaccine or trivalent ‘oral polio vaccine’ consists of attenuated viral strains','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Gamma globulin','Attenuated pathogens','Activated pathogens','Harvested antibodies.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301706,'biology','Which of the following statements is not true for cancer cells in relation to mutations?[2016]','Mutations inhibit production of telomerase.','Mutations inactivate the checkpoints of the cell cycle and cause loss of control over the cell cycle is the correct statement. If mutations inhibit the production of telomerase it will lead to the shortening of chromosomes during each replication cycle and results in cell death.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Mutations inactivate the cell control.','Mutations inhibit production of telomerase.','Mutations in proto oncogenes accelerate the cell cycle.','Mutations destroy telomerase inhibitor.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301707,'biology','In higher vertebrates,the immune system can distinguish self­cells and non­self. If this property is lost due to genetic abnormality and it attacks self­cells, then it leads to[2016]','Autoimmune disease','Autoimmunity is a disorder of the body’s defence mechanisms in which an immune response is elicited against its own tissues, which are thereby damaged or destroyed. Autoimmunity may be caused due to genetic or environmental factors.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Autoimmune disease','Active immunity','Allergic response','Graft rejection','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301708,'biology','If you suspect major deficiency of antibodies in a person,to which of the following would you look for confirmatory evidence?[2015]','Serum globulins','Serum globulins are proteins that include gamma globulins (antibodies) and a variety of enzymes and carrier/ transport proteins. The specific profile of the globulins is determined by protein electrophoresis (spep), which separates the proteins according to size and charge. There are four major groups that can be identified : alpha­1 globulins, alpha­2 globulins, beta globulins and gamma globulins. Once the abnormal group has been identified, further studies can determine the specific protein excess or deficit. Since the gamma fraction usually makes up the largest portion of the globulins, therefore antibody deficiency is mainly related with the low level of serum globulins.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Haemocytes','Serum globulins','Fibrinogen in plasma','Serum albumins','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301709,'biology','Which of the following immunoglobulins does constitute the largest percentage in human milk?[2015]','IgA','IgA immunoglobulins are the second most abundant class of immunoglobulins, which are mainly found in sweat, tears, saliva, mucus, colostrum and gastrointestinal secretions.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','IgA','IgG','IgD','IgM','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301710,'biology','Which of the following diseases is caused by a protozoan? [2015]','Babesiosis','Babesiosis is a malaria­like parasitic disease caused by infection with babesia, a parasitic protozoan. Babesiosis has long been recognised as a disease of cattle and other domestic animals, until human forms of babesiosis had been discovered. Babesia parasites reproduce in red blood cells of mammals and cause haemolytic anaemia, quite similar to malaria. The parasite is transmitted by ticks.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Babesiosis','Influenza','Syphilis','None of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301711,'biology','Grafted kidney may be rejected in a patient due to[2015]','Cell mediated immune response.','Cell mediated immune response (CMIS) consists of T lymphocytes. It reacts against transplants. Transplantation may result in the rejection of the transplanted organs. The immune system recognises the protein in the transplanted tissue or organ as foreign and initiates cellular immunity against it.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Passive immune response','Innate immune response','Humoral immune response','Cell mediated immune response.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301712,'biology','Match each disease with its correct type of vaccine[2015]','Tuberculosis-harmless bacteria, whooping cough-killed bacteria, diphtheria- inactivated toxin, polio-harmless virus','Tuberculosis vaccine contains live bacteria that have been weakened (attenuated), so that they stimulate the immune system but do not cause disease in healthy people,Whooping cough vaccines cannot give you whooping cough since they do not contain any live bacteria-inactivated parts of the bacterium that causes whooping cough,for diphtheria Inactivated means that the toxin will no longer cause the disease but it does trigger the body to make antibodies that give it immunity against the toxin','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','match','Tuberculosis,harmless bacteria','Whooping cough,killed bacteria','Diphtheria,inactivated toxin','Polio,harmless virus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301713,'biology','The active form of entamoeba histolytica feeds upon [2015]','Erythrocytes, mucosa and submucosa of colon','Entamoeba histolytica(gr.,Entos: within + amoeba: change +histos: tissue +lysis: dissolve) is the causative organism of amoebic dysentery or amoebiasis in man. It is a microscopic endoparasite of man. It is commonly found in the upper part of the large intestine (colon) and is very often lodged in the liver, lungs, brain and testes. In its life cycle, it occurs in three distinct forms (i) trophozoite or magna form, (ii) precystic or minuta form, and (iii) cystic form. Trophozoite is the most active, motile and feeding form which is pathogenic to man. It lives in the mucosa and submucous layers of the colon and feeds on these layers and erythrocytes.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Food in intestine','Blood only','Erythrocytes, mucosa and submucosa of colon','Mucosa and submucosa of colon only.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301714,'biology','HIV that causes AIDS, first starts destroying[2015]','Helper T lymphocytes','The AIDS retrovirus, called human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), mounts a direct attack on cd4 + t helper cells because it recognizes the cd4 coreceptors associated with these cells. HIV’s attack on cd4 + t cells cripples the immune system in at least three ways. First, HIV infected cells die only after releasing replicated viruses that infect other cd4 + t cells, until the entire population of cd4 + t cell is destroyed. Second, HIV causes infected cd4 + t cells, to secrete a soluble suppressing factor that blocks other t cells from responding to the HIV antigen. Finally, HIV may block transcription of mhc genes, hindering the recognition and destruction of infected cd4 + t cells, and thus protecting those cells from any remaining vestiges of the immune system. The combined effect of these responses to HIV infection is to wipe out the human immune defense.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Helper T lymphocytes','Thrombocytes','B lymphocytes','Leucocytes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301715,'biology','At which stage of HIV infection does one usually show symptoms of AIDS?[2015]','When HIV damages large number of helper  T-­ lymphocytes','AIDS is a disorder of cell­mediated immune system of the body. Virus responsible for AID is HIV (human immunodeficiency virus). There is a reduction in the number of helper T cells which stimulate antibody production by B cells. This results in the loss of natural defence against viral infection.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Within 15 days of sexual contact with an infected person','When the infected retro virus enters host cells','When HIV damages large number of helper  T-­ lymphocytes','When the viral DNA is produced by reverse transcriptase','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301716,'biology','Infection of ascaris usually occurs by[2013]','Drinking water containing eggs of ascaris','Man acquires infection of ascaris by directly injecting ascaris eggs, containing the infective second stage larva, with contaminated food or water. Life cycle of ascaris is monogenetic. There is no vector or intermediate host','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Tse­tse fly','Mosquito bite','Drinking water containing eggs of ascaris','Eating imperfectly cooked pork','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(301717,'biology','Which one of the following statements is correct regarding sexually transmitted diseases (STDS)?[2013]','The chances of a 5 ­year old boy contracting a std are very little.','Syphilis is caused by bacterium treponema pallidum. It is a sexually transmitted disease (std) which is transferred through sexual intercourse with infected person. Haemophilia is a x linked genetic disorder of blood. It is not transmitted via any sexual practice. Genital herpes is an std while sickle­cell anaemia is an autosomal hereditary disorder.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','A person may contact syphilis by sharing milk with one already suffering from the disease.','Haemophilia is one of the stds.','Genital herpes and sickle­cell anaemia are both stds','The chances of a 5 ­year old boy contracting a std are very little.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301718,'biology','Identify the site where wuchereria bancrofti is normally found in human body.[2013]','Lymphatic vessels of the lower limbs','Wuchereria bancrofti is a dreaded human parasite. It is a digenetic parasite completing its life cycle in two hosts, the final host is man harbouring the adult worm. The disease passes through four stages in human beings: in the first stage, the patient has increased eosinophils, enlarged lymph nodes. Second or carrier stage is symptomless.Third stage is characterised by filarial fever, inflammation of lymph nodes (lymphadenitis) and lymph vessels (lymphangiectasis) and reversible lymphoedema (excess fluid in tissues due to obstruction of lymph vessels) in various body parts. The fourth or final stage is manifested by lymphoedema accompanied by thickening of subcutaneous tissues and skin so that there is permanent swelling mostly of feet, legs, thighs, scrotal sacs, breast etc. It is called elephantiasis.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Muscles of the legs','Blood vessels of the thigh region','Skin between the fingers','Lymphatic vessels of the lower limbs','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301719,'biology','Which one of the following is a hallucinogenic drug?[2013]','Lysergic acid diethylamide','LSD is a psychedelic drug since it causes optical and auditory hallucinations and induces behavioural abnormalities. Opium and morphine are opiate narcotics that suppress brain activity and relieve pain. Caffeine is a stimulant that temporarily stimulates the nervous system.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Caffeine','Morphine','Lysergic acid diethylamide','Opium','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301720,'biology','The infectious stage of plasmodium that enters the human body is [NEET 2020]','Sporozoites','Plasmodium enters the human body as sporozoites (infectious form) through the bite of infected female anopheles mosquito','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Trophozoites','Sporozoites','Female gametocytes','Male gametocytes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301721,'biology','Match the following diseases with the causative organism and select the correct option. [NEET 2019]','Typhoid-Salmonella,Pneumonia-Hemophilus, Filariasis-Wuchereria,Malaria-Plasmodium','Salmonella typhi is a pathogenic bacterium which causes typhoid fever in human beings.Bacteria like Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae are responsible for the disease pneumonia in humans which infects the alveoli (air filled sacs) of the lungs. Wuchereria (W. bancrofti and W. malayi), the filarial worms cause a slowly developing chronic inflammation of the organs in which they live for many years, usually the lymphatic vessels of the lower limbs and the disease is called elephantiasis or filariasis.Plasmodium (P. vivax, P. malaria and P. falciparum) are responsible for different types of malaria. Of these, malignant malaria caused by Plasmodium falciparum is the most serious one and can even be fatal.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','match','Typhoid,Salmonella','Pneumonia,Hemophilus','Filariasis,Wuchereria','Malaria,Plasmodium','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301722,'biology','Identify the correct pair representing the causative agent of typhoid fever and the confirmatory test for typhoid. (NEET 2019)','Salmonella typhi / widal test','Typhoid is caused by bacteria salmonella typhi, transmitted through fecal oral route. It results in high fever, abdominal pain and frequent stools and is confirmed by widal test','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Salmonella typhi / widal test','Plasmodium vivax / uti test','Streptococcus pneumoniae / widal test','Salmonella typhi / anthrone test','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301723,'biology','In which disease does mosquito transmitted pathogen cause chronic inflammation of lymphatic vessels? [NEET 2018]','Elephantiasis','Elephantiasis is caused by the parasitic filarial worm, wuchereria bancrofti which is transmitted from an infected person to a healthy person via the bite of mosquito (especially culex). It is characterised by gross enlargement of the skin and underlying connective tissues caused by lymphatic obstruction of lymph vessels by these worms which prevent drainage of lymph from the surrounding tissues. The parts commonly affected are legs, feet, thighs but scrotum, breasts and vulva may also be involved.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Elephantiasis','Ascariasis','Ringworm disease','Amoebiasis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301724,'biology','Identify the wrong statement with Reference   to immunity [NEET 2020]','Active immunity is quick and gives full response','Active immunity is slow and takes time to give its full effective response','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','When exposed to antigen (living or dead) antibodies are produced in the host’s body. It is called “active immunity','When ready-made antibodies are directly given, it is called “passive immunity”.','Active immunity is quick and gives full response','Fetus receives some antibodies from mother, it is an example for passive immunity','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301725,'biology','Which of the following immune responses is responsible for rejection of kidney graft? [NEET 2019]','Cell-mediated immune response','Transplantation of tissue/organ often fails due to non-acceptance by the patient’s body therefore, tissue matching and blood group matching are essential before undertaking any graft/transplant. When the immune system recognises the protein in the transplanted tissue or organ as foreign, it initiates cellular immunity. As a result of this, there is a rejection of transplanted organs. To suppress the immune response during transplantation, histocompatibility antigen and immunosuppressants play an important role.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Cell-mediated immune response','Auto-immune response','Humoral immune response','Inflammatory immune response','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301726,'biology','Colostrum, the yellowish fluid, secreted by mother during the initial days of lactation is very essential to impart immunity to the new born infants because it contains','Immunoglobulin a','Colostrum (mother’s first milk) rich in immunoglobulin a(iga) antibodies are passed to newborn through the breast feed and provides natural passive immunity','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Immunoglobulin a','Natural killer cells','Monocytes','Macrophages','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301727,'biology','Which of the following is not an autoimmune disease?  [NEET 2018]','Alzheimer’s disease','Autoimmune diseases are certain unrelated disorders caused by inflammation and destruction of tissues by the body’s own immune response. These disorders include acquired hemolytic anaemia, pernicious anaemia, rheumatoid arthritis, myasthenia gravis, psoriasis, vitiligo, etc. Alzheimer’s disease is a chronic neurodegenerative irreversible, progressive brain disorder that slowly destroys memory and thinking skills','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','Psoriasis','Rheumatoid arthritis','Alzheimer’s disease','Vitiligo','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301728,'biology','Drug called ‘heroin’ is synthesised by [NEET 2019]','Acetylation of morphine','Heroin (smack ) is chemically diacetylmorphine which is white, odourless, bitter crystalline compound. It is obtained by acetylation of morphine, extracted from latex of poppy plant','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Nitration of morphine','Methylation of morphine','Acetylation of morphine','Glycosylation of morphine','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301729,'biology','Which part of poppy plant is used to obtain the drug “smack”? [NEET 2018]','Latex','This is obtained by acetylation of morphine, which is extracted from the latex of poppy plant Papaver somniferum','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Flowers','Latex','Roots','Leaves','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301730,'biology','Match the following (NEET 2021)','Filariasis-wuchereria bancrofti,amoebiasis-entamoeba histolytica,pneumonia-haemophilus influenzae,ringworm-trichophyton','Filariasis-wuchereria bancrofti,\nAmoebiasis-entamoeba histolytica,\nPneumonia-haemophilus influenzae,\nRingworm-trichophyton','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'3','match','Filariasis,wuchereria bancrofti','Amoebiasis,entamoeba histolytica','Pneumonia,haemophilus influenzae','Ringworm,trichophyton','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301731,'biology','Diacetyl morphine is also called as : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(4) Smack','Diacetyl morphine is a common street name for the drug heroin. It is also known by various other street names, including "smack." Heroin is an opioid drug synthesized from morphine and is highly addictive. It is classified as a Schedule I controlled substance due to its high potential for abuse and the severe health risks associated with its use.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) Amphetamine','(2) Barbiturate','(3) Crack','(4) Smack','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(301732,'biology','In which blood corpuscles, the HIV undergoes replication and produces progeny viruses? [ NEET 2023]','(4) TH cells','HIV undergoes replication & produces progeny virus in helper T-cells','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous Year Questions',NULL,'1','mcq','(1) B-lymphocytes','(2) Basophils','(3) Eosinophils','(4) TH cells','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
