(302130,'biology','Which of the following is an autoimmune disorder?[2013]','Myasthenia gravis','In myasthenia gravis, your immune system produces antibodies that block or destroy many of your muscles\' receptor sites for a neurotransmitter called acetylcholine','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Myasthenia gravis','Osteoporosis','Muscular dystrophy','Gout','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302131,'biology','Which of the following is based upon the principle of antigen-antibody interaction?[2014]','ELISA','The ELISA is a fundamental tool of clinical immunology, and is used as an initial screen for HIV detection. Based on the principle of antigen- antibody interaction, this test allows for easy visualization of results.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','PCR','ELISA','r-DNA technology','RNA','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302132,'biology','Pasteurella/yersinia pestis (causal agent of bubonic plague) is transmitted by[2016]','Xenopsylla','The Oriental rat flea, also known as the tropical rat flea, is a parasite of rodents, primarily of the genus Rattus, and is a primary vector for bubonic plague and murine typhus. This occurs when a flea that has fed on an infected rodent bites a human.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cimex','Xenopsylla','Pediculus','Aedes','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302133,'biology','Which of the following pairs is not correctly matched ?[2016]','Tetanus – pasteurella pestis','Tetanus is caused by clostridium tetani while plague is caused by pasteurella pestis.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Cholera – vibrio cholerae','German measles – rubella virus','Whooping cough – bordetella pertussis','Tetanus – pasteurella pestis','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302134,'biology','Human immuno deficiency virus (HIV) has a protein coat and a genetic material which is [2017]','Single stranded rna','The human immunodeficiency virus is a lentivirus that causes the acquired immunodeficiency syndrome, a condition in humans in which progressive failure of the immune system allows life-threatening opportunistic infections and cancers to thrive. HIV has a protein coat and a genetic material which is single stranded rna.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Single stranded dna.','Single stranded rna','Double stranded rna.','Double stranded dna.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302135,'biology','Which of the following disease is viral as well as transmitted by mosquitoes?[2017]','Yellow fever and dengue','Yellow fever and dengue are viral diseases, and they are transmitted by mosquitoes.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Elephantiasis and dengue','Yellow fever and sleeping sickness','Encephalitis and sleeping sickness','Yellow fever and dengue','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(121411,'chemistry','Ionic species are stabilised by the dispersal of charge. Which of the following carboxylate ion is the most stable? Refer diagram(12147)','d','The stability of an ionic species is dependent on the dispersal of charge on the ion. In general, the stability of a carboxylate ion is increased by the electron-withdrawing substituents that stabilize the negative charge on the carboxylate ion .\n\nOption d ion has two fluorine atoms, which are highly electronegative and stabilizes the negative charge on the carboxylate ion more efficiently than the other ions. Therefore, this ion is the most stable of all the given carboxylate ions.','Organic chemistry >> Organic chemistry - some basic principles and techniques >> NCERT Exemplar Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','11-350','11-272','/ncert/pyq/chemistry/mcq/121411q.png'),
(302152,'biology','Assertion : The antibodies separated from serum are homogenous.\n\nReason : Monoclonal antibodies are homogenous immunological reagents.[2013]','Both the assertion and reason are incorrect.','From hyperimmunized animals, the blood serum may be taken and antibodies may be isolated from this serum. However, the antibodies, whenever separated from serum after induction due to an antigen, are usually heterogeneous, because the cells keep on producing a variety of antibodies. Monoclonal antibodies (MABS), on the other hand are homogeneous immunological reagents of defined specificity so that these can be utilized for diagnosis and screening of disease.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct, but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.','Both the assertion and reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302153,'biology','Assertion : Interferons are effective against viruses. \n\nReason : Proteins which can be synthesized only by genetic engineering are effective against viruses.[2013,2014]','Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.','Interferons are proteins that are effective against most viruses. They are naturally produced by virus infected cells. The proteins interact with adjacent cells and make them resistant to virus attack. Now interferons are also being manufactured through genetic engineering. Interferons control the multiplication of virus particles by inhibiting their protein synthesis.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct, but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.','Both the assertion and reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302154,'biology','Assertion : Cancer cells are virtually immortal until the body in which they reside dies.\n\nReason : Cancer is caused by damage to genes regulating the cell division cycle.[2006,2015]','Both assertion and reason are correct, but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','MALT are significant aggregations of lymphoid tissues which are seen in relation to the mucosa of the major tracts like respiratory, alimentary canal and urogenital tracts. It constitutes about 50 percent of the lymphoid tissue in human body','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct, but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.','Both the assertion and reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302155,'biology','Assertion : Cocaine has a potent stimulating action on central nervous system, producing a sense of euphoria and increased energy. \n\nReason : Injecting the microbes intentionally during immunisation or infectious organisms gaining access into body during natural infection induces active immunity.\n[AIIMS 2016]','Both assertion and reason are correct, but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Cocaine is obtained from the cocaplant, erythroxylum coca. Cocaine is commonly called as coke or crack which is usually snored. It has a potent stimulating action of euphoria and increased energy. It interferes with the transport of the neurotransmitter dopamine. Its increased dosages cause hallucinations.','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct, but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.','Both the assertion and reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302156,'biology','Assertion: Artificially acquired passive immunity results when antibodies or lymphocytes produced outside the host are introduced into a host.\n \nReason: A bone marrow transplant given to a patient with genetic immunodeficiency is an example of artificially acquired passive immunity. \n[AIIMS 2017]','Both assertion and reason are correct, but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Artificially acquired passive immunity results when antibodies or lymphocytes that have been produced outside the host are introduced into a host. This type of immunity is immediate short lived, lasting only a few weeks to a few months. An example is bone marrow transplant given to a patient with genetic immunodeficiency','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Previous year AIIMS Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.','Both assertion and reason are correct, but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.','Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.','Both the assertion and reason are incorrect.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302201,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Health of humans is affected by infections only. \n\nR: Health is defined as simply the absence of disease and physical fitness.','Both A and R are false.','Health is affected by lifestyle, infections, genetic disorders, etc. Health is defined as a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being. (NCERT Class 12 pg 145-146 paragraph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302202,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Genetic disorders are acquired by the child after being born. \n\nR: Balanced diet, sedentary lifestyle and hygiene maintains good health.','Both A and R are false.','Genetic disorders – deficiencies with which a child is born and deficiencies/defects which the child inherits from parents from birth. Balanced diet, active lifestyle (exercises) and hygiene maintains good health. (NCERT Class 12 pg 145-146 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302203,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Immunisation against infectious agents is achieved through vaccination. \n\nR: Diseases can be both infectious and non-infectious.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Vaccination helps to develop immunity against vaccine preventable infectious diseases. Diseases can be infectious (communicable) or non-infectious(non-communicable). (NCERT Class 12 pg 146 paragraph 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302204,'biology','Read the following statements about TYPHOID; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Typhoid is caused by Mycobacterium Typhae. \n\n2. The pathogen is transmitted by sexual route. \n\n3. There is sustained fever with weakness and headache in typhoid. \n\n4. Typhoid is confirmed by VDRL test.','Only 3 is correct','typhoid fever is caused by Salmonella Typhae. It is transmitted through food and water (faeco-oral route) and is characterised by high fever, headache and weakness. Typhoid is confirmed by WIDAL TEST. (NCERT Class 12 pg 146 section 8.1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302205,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Pneumonia is caused by Salmonella typhae. \n\nR: Pneumonia is a lung disease where the bronchi are infected and filled with fluid.','Both A and R are false.','Pneumonia is caused by STREPTOCOCCUS PNEUMONIAE and HEMOPHILUS INFLUENZAE. In pneumonia, the alveoli of the lungs are infected and filled with fluid leading to respiratory problems. (NCERT Class 12 pg 147 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302206,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Plague and Diptheria are viral diseases in humans. \n\nR: Widal test confirms syphilis infection in humans.','Both A and R are false.','Plague and Diptheria are bacterial diseases in man. Widal test is used to confirm typhoid infection. (NCERT Class 12 pg 147 paragraph 1,2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302207,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Common cold is characterised by nasal congestion, nasal discharge, sore throat, cough, etc. \n\nR: The disease is transmitted via the droplets released by infected person while sneezing.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','The common cold is characterised by nasal congestion and discharge, sore throat, hoarseness, cough, headache, tiredness, etc., which usually last for 3-7 days. It is transmitted by droplets of infecter individuals while sneezong or using contaminated objects. (NCERT Clas 12 pg 147 paragraph 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302208,'biology','Read the following statements about INFECTIOUS DISEASES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Typhoid is confirmed by Widal test. \n\n2. Common cold is caused by Rhino Virus. \n\n3. Plague is a bacterial disease in man. \n\n4. Malaria is transmitted by droplets from infected individuals while sneezing.','Only 4 is incorrect','Typhoid is confirmed by WIDAL test. Common cold is viral in origin and caused by Rhino Virus.PLague and diptheria are bacterial in origin. Malaria is transmitted by the bite of female anopheles mosquito. (NCERT Class 12 pg 147 paragraph 1,2,3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302209,'biology','Read the following statements about MALARIA; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Plasmodium is a tiny protozoan which can cause malaria. \n\n2. Plasmodium vivax causes the most serious type of malaria. \n\n3. Malaria is transmitted via bite of female Aedes mosquito. \n\n4. Sporozoites are stored in salivary glands of mosquitoes.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Plasmodium is a tiny protozoan which can cause malaria. Plasmodium falciparum causes the most serious type of malaria. Malaria is transmitted via bite of female Anopheles mosquito. Sporozoites are stored in salivary glands of mosquitoes. (NCERT Class 12 pg 147 paragraph 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302210,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Fertilisation of gametes of Plasmodium ocurs in human blood. \n\nR: Plasmodium is transmitted by the bite of female aedes aegypti mosquito.','Both A and R are false.','Fertilisation of the gametes (sexual form) of Plasmodium occurs in the gut of mosquito. Malaria is transmitted from one human to another by the bite of infected female anopheles mosquito. (NCERT Class 12 pg 148 fig. 8.1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302211,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Lysis of WBCs lead to recurrent cycles of fever in malaria. \n\nR: Plasmodium reproduces sexually in the liver cells.','Both A and R are false.','Parasites reproduce asexually in red blood cells bursting RBCs leading to repeated cycles of fever and other symptoms. Plasmodium reproduces asexually in the liver cells of humans. (NCERT Class 11 pg 148 fig. 8.1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302212,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Gametocytes of Plasmodium develops in the RBCS of human blood. \n\nR: Sexual fertilisation of the gametes occur in the gut of male anopheles mosquito.','A is true but R is false','Sexual stages (gametocytes) develop in the red blood cells of human blood. Fertilisation and development of the parasite occurs in the gut of the female anopheles mosquito. (NCERT Class 12 pg 148 fig. 8.1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302213,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Amoebic dysentery is caused by a protozoan called Entamoeba histolytica. \n\nR: Amoebic dysentery is transmitted via the birth canal to the newborn child.','A is true but R is false','Entamoeba histolytica is a protozoan parasite in the large intestine of human which causes amoebiasis (amoebic dysentery). Symptoms of this disease include constipation, abdominal pain and cramps, stools with excess mucous and blood clots. It is transmitted by faeco-oral route (from faeces to the food by housefly) (NCERT Class 12 pg 148 paragraph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302214,'biology','Read the following statements about INFECTIOUS DISEASES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Ascaris is a platyhelminthes (flat-worm). \n\n2. Wuchereria is the causative agent of giardiasis. \n\n3. Ascariasis includes internal bleeding, muscular pain, fever, etc. \n\n4. Filarial worms can cause elephantiasis.','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Ascaris is a round worm causing ASCARIASIS. Symptoms of these disease include internal bleeding, muscular pain, fever, anemia and blockage of the intestinal passage. Wuchereria is a filarial worm causing elephantiasis. (NCERT Class 149 paragraph 2,3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302215,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: W. bancrofti cause chronic inflammation of the lymphatic channels in humans. \n\nR: The genital organs are spared in elephantiasis.','A is true but R is false','W. bancrofti causes elephantiasis by causing slow, chronic inflammation of the lymphatic channels. It causes gross deformities and also INVOLVES the GENITALS. (NCERT Class 12 pg 149 paragraph 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302216,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Ringworm is caused by fungi genus Microsporum, Epidermophyton and Trichophyton. \n\nR: Ringworm involves wet, soft and non-itchy lesions on skins and nails.','A is true but R is false','Fungi of genus Microsporum, Trichophyton and Epidermophyton are responsible for ringworms.. Appearance of dry, scaly lesions on various parts of the body such as skin, nails and scalp are common symptoms. (NCERT Class 12 pg 149 paragraph 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302217,'biology','Read the following statements about RINGWORM; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Ringworm causes dry, scaly lesions on skin, nails and scalp. \n\n2. Microphyton genus of fungi causes ringworm. \n\n3. Hot and humid climate help ringworm fungi to grow. \n\n4. Ringworm is transmitted by sexual route.','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Microsporum,Trichophyton and Epidermophyton are responsible for ringworms. Appearance of dry, scaly lesions on skin, nails and scalp are the main symptoms of the disease. Heat and moisture helpthese fungi to grow. Ringworms are generally acquired from soil or by using towels, clothesor even the comb of infected individuals. (NCERT Class 11 pg 149 paragraph 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302218,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Immunity refers to the ability of the host body to fight against the disease causing organism. \n\nR: Physical barrier in innate immunity comprise of acid in stomach and saliva in mouth.','A is true but R is false','This overall ability of the host to fight the disease-causing organisms is called immunity. Physical barriers (innate immunity) : Skin on our body is the main barrier which prevents entry of the micro-organisms. Mucus coating of the epithelium lining the respiratory, gastrointestinal and urogenital tracts also help in trapping microbes entering our body. (NCERT Class 12 pg 150 section 8.2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302219,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: PMNLs, Natural killer cells and Monocytes form the cellular barrier of innate immunity. \n\nR: Innate immunity is a specific type of defence acquired after birth.','A is true but R is false','Innate immunity is a non-specific type of defence present at the time of birth. Cellular barriers : Certain types of leukocytes (WBC) of our body like polymorpho-nuclear leukocytes (PMNL-neutrophils) and monocytes and natural killer (type of lymphocytes) in the blood as well as macrophages in tissues can phagocytose and destroy microbes. (NCERT Class 12 pg 150 section 8.2.1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302220,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Physiological barriers of innate immunity involves the acid in stomach, saliva in mouth and tears in eyes. \n\nR: Vaccination has helped in eradication of small-pox from the world.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Innate immunity is a non-specific type of defence present at the time of birth. Physiological barriers : Acid in the stomach, saliva in the mouth, tears from eyes–all prevent microbial growth. Regular vaccinisation has helped to eradicate smallpox from the world. (NCERT Class 12 pg 150 section 8.2.1 and paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302221,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Acquired immunity is pathogen specific and aquired after birth. \n\nR: Acquired immunity shows a higher intensity  primary response than a secondary response.','A is true but R is false','Aquired immunity is a pathogen specific memory based immunity acquired after birth. It produces a low intensity promary response. On re-exposure to pathogens, it shows a high intensity secondary response. (NCERT Class 12 pg 151 section 8.2.2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302222,'biology','Read the following statements about IMMUNITY; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. T and B lymphocytes mediate acquired immunity. \n\n2. Secondary response of acquired immunity is of higher response. \n\n3. Each anti-body has 4 light chains and 2 heavy chains. \n\n4. Antibody is represented as H2L4','Both 1 and 2 are correct','T and B lymphocytes mediate acquired immunity. Acquired immunity secondary response is of higher intensity than primary response. Anti-body has 2 light chains and 2 heavy chains. It is represented as H2L2. (NCERT Class 12 pg 151 section 8.2.2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 3 is correct','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302223,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: CMI is responsible for organ graft transplantation rejection. \n\nR: CMI is mediated by the B lymphocytes and the monocytes.','A is true but R is false','During transplant, the body is able to differentiate ‘self ’ and ‘nonself’ and the cell-mediated immune response is responsible for the graft rejection. T lymphocytes mediate CMI. (NCERT Class 12 pg 152 paragraph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302224,'biology','Read the following statements about IMMUNITY; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. IgA, IgM, IgG, etc are the proteins produced by T lymphocytes. \n\n2. CMI is mediated by T Lymphocytes. \n\n3. Antibodies are represented as H1L2. \n\n4. Anti-body chains are linked by di-sulphide bonds.','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','IgA, IgM, IgG, etc are the proteins produced by B lymphocytes. CMI is mediated by T Lymphocytes. Antibodies are represented as H2L2. Anti-body chains are linked by di-sulphide bonds. (NCERT Class 12 pg 151 section 8.2.2 and fig. 8.4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302225,'biology','Read the following statements about IMMUNITY; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Administration of pre-formed antibodies to protect host is called active immunity. \n\n2. Colostrum from mother to child is an example of active immunity. \n\n3. CMI is responsible for transplant rejection. \n\n4. Vaccination is an example of passive immunity.','Only 3 is correct','Administration of pre-formed antibodies to protect host is called PASSIVE immunity. Transfer of Colostrum from mother to child is an example of PASSIVE immunity. CMI is responsible for transplant rejection. Vaccination is an example of ACTIVE immunity. (NCERT Class 12 pg 152 section 8.2.3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Only 3 is correct','Both 1 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302226,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Administration of snake anti-venom is a type of passive immunity. \n\nR: Administration of pre-formed antibodies is called passive immunisation.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Administration of pre-formed antibodies is called passive immunisation. Snake anti-venom contains pre-formed antibodies, hence its administration is a type of PASSIVE IMMUNITY. (NCERT Class 12 pg 152 section 8.2.4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302227,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Vaccination helps to generate memory among host defence mechanism. \n\nR: Vaccination is a type of natural passive immunity.','A is true but R is false','Vaccination refers to injection of attenuated/killed mibrobes such that it can stimulate the immune system of host and also genertae memory. So, it is a type of ACTIVE IMMUNITY. (NCERT Class 12 section 8.2.4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302228,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Hepatitis B vaccine is produced by recombinant DNA technology. \n\nR: Hepatitis B vaccine contains pre-formed antibodies and is an example of passive immunisation.','A is true but R is false','Hepatitis B vaccine is produced by Recombinant DNA technology. This allows large scale production and hence greater availability for immunisation, e.g., hepatitis B vaccine produced from yeast. Hepatits B vaccine does not contain pre-formed toxins. It is an example of ACTIVE IMMUNITY. (NCERT Class 12 pg 152 section 8.2.4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302229,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Allergies are mediated by \'secretory IgA\' antibodes. \n\nR: Allergy is an attenuated response of the immune system against the self antigens.','Both A and R are false.','The exaggerated response of the immune system to certain antigens present in the environment is called allergy. Allergy is mediated by IgE antibodies. (NCERT Class 12 section 8.2.5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(2000037772,'physics','Two cylinders $A$ and $B$ of equal capacity are connected to each other via a stop cock. A contains an ideal gas at standard temperature and pressure. $B$ is completely evacuated. The entire system is thermally insulated. The stop cock is suddenly opened. The process is:','D','$\\Delta U=0$. Hence $U_{i}=U_{f} \\Rightarrow \\Delta T=0$','Thermodynamic Processes','Thermodynamics (Physics)','Medium',NULL,'adiabatic','isochoric','isobaric','isothermal',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302230,'biology','Read the following statements about ALLERGY; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Allergy is mediated by IgE antibodies. \n\n2. Anti-histamines, adrenaline and steroids aggravate allergic reactions. \n\n3. Asthma is a type of allergy. \n\n4. The common allergens are mites, dust, dander, etc.','Only 2 is incorrect','Allergy is mediated by IgE antibodies. The common allergens are mites in dust, animal danders, etc. Drugs like anti-histamine, adrenalin and steroids quickly reduce the symptoms of allergy. Asthma is also a type of allergy. (NCERT Class 12 pg 153 section 8.2.5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302231,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Autoimmune diseases are those where the bodies defence mechanism attacks the foreign pathogenic organisms. \n\nR: Autoimmunity occurs when body can differentiate between self and foreign substances.','Both A and R are false.','Sometimes due to genetic causes or unknown reasons, the body\'s defence mechanism attacks body own self due to its inability to differentiate between self and non-self. These are called autoimmune diseases. (NCERT Class 12 pg 153 section 8.2.6)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302232,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Lymphoid organs are those where the origin and/or maturation of the platelets take place. \n\nR: Spleen, tomsils are lymph nodes are primary lymphoid organs.','Both A and R are false.','Lymphoid organs: These are the organs where origin and/or maturation and proliferation of lymphocytes occur. The primary lymphoid organs are bone marrow and thymus. (NCERT Class 12 section 8.2.7)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302233,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: In primary lymphoid organs, immature lymphocytes differentiate into antigen sensitive lymphocytes. \n\nR: Peyer\'s patch of small intestine and appendix are secondary lymphoid organs.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','The primary lymphoid organs are bone marrow and thymus where immature lymphocytes differentiate into antigen-sensitive lymphocytes.Spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils, Peyer’s patches of small intestine and appendix are secondary lymphoid organs. (NCERT Class 12 pg 154 paragraph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302234,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Lymphocytes become effector cells at secondary lymphoid organs. \n\nR: Thymus keeps on increasing in size with age and is largest at old age.','A is true but R is false','The secondary lymphoid organs provide the sites for interaction of lymphocytes with the antigen, which then proliferate to become effector cells. The thymus is quite large at the time of birth but keeps reducing in size with age. (NCERT Class 12 pg 154 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302235,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Apart from kidney, spleen also acts like a blood filter. \n\nR: It traps blood borne micro-organism and contains lymphocytes and macrophages.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','The spleen is a large beanshaped organ. It mainly contains lymphocytes and phagocytes. It acts as a filter of the blood by trapping blood-borne microorganisms. (NCERT Class 12 pg 154 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302236,'biology','Read the following statements about LYMPHOID ORGANS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. MALT comprises 50% of total lymphoid tissue in the human body. \n\n2. Spleen and appendix are primary lymphoid organs. \n\n3. Thymus is a lobed organ near heart which increases in size with age. \n\n4. Peyer\'s patch is located in small intestine.','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','MALT comprises of 50% of total lymphoid tissues in the body. Spleen and appendix are secondary lymphoid organs. Thymus is a lobed organ near heart which reduces in size with age. Peyer\'s patch (secondary lymphoid tissue) is located in SMALL INTESTINE. (NCERT Class 12 pg 154 paragraph 1,2,3,4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 2 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302237,'biology','Read the following statements about AIDS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. AIDS was first reported in 1989. \n\n2. AIDS is caused by HIV, a group of DNA viruses. \n\n3. AIDS is an acquired disease. \n\n4. Transmission of AIDS is high in intra-oral drug abusers.','Only 3 is correct','AIDS was first reported in 1981. It is an acquired disease caused by HIV, a group of retroviruses (RNA viruses). Transmission of AIDS is high in INTRA-VENOUS drug abusers. (NCERT Class 12 pg 154 section 8.3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Only 3 is correct','Only 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302238,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Infected mother might transmit HIV to her child through the placenta. \n\nR: AIDS (discovered in 1981) is caused by HIV, a group of retroviruses.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Transmission of HIV-infection generally occurs by (a) sexual contact with infected person, (b) by transfusion of contaminated blood and blood products, (c) by sharing\ninfected needles as in the case of intravenous drug abusers and (d) from infected mother to her child through placenta. AIDS was first reported in 1981 and is caused by HIV (retrovirus). (NCERT Class 12 pg 154 section 8.3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302239,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: HIV produces viral DNA with the help of reverse dehydrogenase enzyme. \n\nR: This enzyme produces RNA from cDNA.','Both A and R are false.','HIV produces viral DNA inside host using REVERSE TRANSCRIPTASE. This produces cDNA from viral RNA. (NCERT Class 12 pg 155 fig. 8.6)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302240,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Reverse transcriptase in HIV produces cDNA from viral RNA. \n\nR: HIV is a group of DNA containing retroviruses that causes AIDS.','A is true but R is false','Reverse transcriptase produces cDNA from viral RNA in the host cells. HIV is a group of retroviruses containing ssRNA causing AIDS. (NCERT Class 12 pg 155 fig. 8.6)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302241,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Period between HIV infection and appearance of symptoms range from 5-10 years. \n\nR: HIV is spread via mere physical touch.','A is true but R is false','There is always a time-lag between the infection and appearance of AIDS symptoms. This period may vary from a few months to many years (usually 5-10 years). HIV is not spread by mere physical touch. It is spread via intravenous route or through sexual contact. (NCERT Class 12 pg 155 paragraph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302242,'biology','Read the following statements about AIDS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. HIV mainly attacks the helper T lymphocytes. \n\n2. HIV contains reverse transcriptase enzyme. \n\n3. AIDS can be diagnosed using ELISA. \n\n4. Anti-retroviral therapy is fully effective in treatment.','Only 4 is incorrect','HIV mainly attacks the helper T lymphocytes to produce progeny viruses. HIV contains reverse transcriptase enzyme. AIDS can be diagnosed using ELISA. Anti-retroviral therapy is PARTIALLY effective in treatment. (NCERT Class 12 pg 156 paragraph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302243,'biology','Read the following statements about AIDS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. For AIDS, cure is better than prevention. \n\n2. NACO educates common people about AIDS. \n\n3. Intra-oral drug abusers have high chance of getting AIDS. \n\n4. Anti-retroviral therapy is partially effective against AIDS.','Only 3 is incorrect','For AIDS, cure is better than prevention because anti-retroviral therapy is partially effective. NACO (NATIONAL AIDS CONTROL ORGANISATION) educates common people about AIDS. INTRA-VENOUS drug abusers have high chance of getting AIDS. Anti-retroviral therapy is partially effective against AIDS. (NCERT Class 12 pg 155-156 pararaph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302244,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Free distribution of condoms, usage of disposable needles  and  syringes are helpful in preventing AIDS. \n\nR: NACO educates general public regarding AIDS.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Making blood (from blood banks) safe from HIV, ensuring the use of only disposable needles and syringes in public\nand private hospitals and clinics, free distribution of condoms, controlling drug abuse, advocating safe sex and promoting regular check-ups prevent AIDS. NACO educates people regarding AIDS. (NCERT Class 12 pg 156 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302245,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Anti-retroviral therapy can prevent death in AIDS. \n\nR: In AIDS, the patient becomes immuno-competent and suffers from fungal/parasitic infections.','Both A and R are false.','Treatment of AIDS with anti-retroviral drugs is only partially effective. They can only prolong\nthe life of the patient but cannot prevent death. A progressive decrease in the number of helper T-lymphocytes in the body lead to patient becoming immuno-deficient. (NCERT Class 12 pg 156 paragraph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302246,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Cancer cells show the property of contact inhibition. \n\nR: Normal cells show uncontrolled cell division upon coming in contact with adjacent cells.','Both A and R are false.','In cancer cells, there is breakdown of all regulatory mechanisms. Normal cells show a property called contact inhibition by virtue of which contact with other cells inhibits their uncontrolled growth. Cancer cells appears to have lost this property. (NCERT Class 12 pg 157 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302247,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Benign tumors proliferate rapidly and cause damage to adjacent cells. \n\nR: Malignant cells remain confined to their original location and do not spread.','Both A and R are false.','Benign tumors normally remain confined to their original location and do not spread to other parts of the body and cause little damage. The malignant tumors divide rapidly and damage surrounding tissues. They also undergo metastasis. (NCERT Class 12 pg 157 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302248,'biology','Read the following statements about CANCERS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. X-Ray is an important non-ionising carcinogen. \n\n2. Benign tumors always undergo metastasis. \n\n3. Activation of proto-oncogenes may lead to cancers. \n\n4. CT uses magnetic field to create a 2D image of internal organs.','Only 3 is correct','X-Ray is an important IONISING carcinogen. Benign tumors normally don\'t undergo metastasis. Activation of proto-oncogenes may lead to cancers. CT uses X RAYS to create a 3D image of internal organs. (NCERT Class 12 pg 157 paragraph 3,4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Only 3 is correct','Only 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302249,'biology','Read the following statements about CANCERS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Normal cells show the property of contact inhibition. \n\n2. X-Ray is an ionising carcinogen. \n\n3. MRI uses strong magnetic field to detetct pathological changes. \n\n4. Histopathological studies involve CT scan of tissues.','Only 4 is incorrect','Normal cells show the property of contact inhibition. They stop diving upon contact with adjacent cells. X-Ray is an ionising carcinogen that causes DNA damage and mutations. MRI uses strong magnetic field to detetct pathological changes. Histopathological studies involve BIOPSY and Staining of tissues. (NCERT CLASS 12 pg 157 paragraph 2,3,4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302250,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Surgery is the only treatment modality for cancers. \n\nR: Radiotherapy for cancers has side effects like alopecia.','Both A and R are false.','Cancers can be treated by a combination of surgery, immunotherapy, radiotherapy and chemotherapy. CHEMOTHERAPY for cancer treatment has side effects like alopecia (i.e. hair loss) (NCERT Class 12 pg 158 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302251,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Alpha-Interferon is a biological response modifier. \n\nR: MRI uses strong magnetic fields  and  non-ionsing radiations to detect pathological changes in the tissues.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Alpha-Interferon is an biological immune reponse modifier that activates immune system and destroys tumors. MRI (MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING) uses strong magnetic fields/ non-ionsing radiations to detect pathological changes in tisses. (NCERT Class 12 pg 157-158 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302252,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Di-acetyl morphine is also called SMACK. \n\nR: Heroin is a white, sour and amorphous substance.','A is true but R is false','Heroin commonly called smack is chemically diacetylmorphine which is a white, odourless, bitter crystalline compound. This is obtained by acetylation of morphine. (NCERT Class 12 pg 158 section 8.5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302253,'biology','Read the following statements about DRUGS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Heroin is obtained from latex of poppy plant. \n\n2. SMACK is a white odourless and bitter crystalline compund. \n\n3. Opiods bind to H1 receptors in GIT and CNS. \n\n4. Morphine is chemically called Acetyl-salicylate.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Heroin is obtained from latex of poppy plant. SMACK is a white odourless and bitter crystalline compund. Opiods bind to specific opiod (e.g. mu) receptors in GIT and CNS. Morphine is chemically called DI-ACETYL MORPHINE. (NCERT Class 12 pg 158 section 8.5).','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302254,'biology','Read the following statements about DRUGS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Cannibinoids are obtained from latex of poppy plant. \n\n2. Cannabinoids have effects on the CVS of the body. \n\n3. Charas, ganga, marijuana are forms of di-acetyl morphine. \n\n4. Cannabinoids is taken by ingestion or inhalation.','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Cannibinoids are obtained from inflorescences of CANNABIS SATIVA. Cannabinoids have effects on the CVS of the body. Charas, ganga, marijuana are forms of Cannabinoids. Cannabinoids is taken by ingestion or inhalation. (NCERT Class 12 pg 159 paragraph 1,2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both 1 and 3 are correct','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302255,'biology','Read the following statements about DRUGS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Cocaine is obtained from Erythroxylum coca. \n\n2. Cocaine is a CNS depressant and makes a person drowsy. \n\n3.  Excessive dose of cocaine has hallucinogenic properties. \n\n4. Datura produces substances that can treat hallucinations.','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Cocaine is obtained from Erythroxylum coca. Cocaine is a CNS STIMULANT and makes a person active. Excessive dose of cocaine has hallucinogenic properties. Datura produces substances that can CAUSE hallucination. (NCERT Class 12 pg 159 paragraph 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Only 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302256,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Erythroxylum coca is native to India. \n\nR: It is used to obtain SMACK, which is a white, odourless, amorphous compund.','Both A and R are false.','Coca alkaloid or cocaine is obtained from coca plant Erythroxylum coca, native to South America. Cocaine, commonly called coke or crack. (NCERT Class 12 pg 159 paragraph 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302257,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Nicotine increases heart rate and blood pressure. \n\nR: Nicotine stimulates adrenal gland to secrete catecholamines.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Nicotine (during smoking) stimulates adrenal gland to release adrenaline and nor-adrenaline into blood circulation, both of which raise blood pressure and increase heart rate. (NCERT Class 12 pg 160 paragraph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302258,'biology','Read the following statements about DRUGS  and  ADDICTION; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Smoking is a risk factor for lung and urinary bladder cancer. \n\n2. Nicotine increases heart rate and BP. \n\n3. Products of Atropa belladona has hallucinogenic effect. \n\n4. SMACK is colourless, odourless, bitter and amorphous compound.','Only 4 is incorrect','Smoking is a risk factor for lung and urinary bladder cancer,bronchitis, emphysema, coronary heart disease, gastric ulcer. Nicotine increases heart rate and BP. Products of Atropa belladona (atropine) has hallucinogenic effect. SMACK is colourless, odourless, bitter and CRYSTALLINE compound. (NCERT Class 12 pg 159 paragraph2,3 -160 paragraph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Only 4 is incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302259,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Adolescence is the bridging period between adulthood and senility. \n\nR: Smoking during adolescence helps in maintaining good health.','Both A and R are false.','Adolescence is the bridging period between Childhood and Adulthood. Smoking during adolescence does not help in maintaining good health; instead becomes an addiction. (NCERT Class 12 pg 160 section 8.5.1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'3','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302260,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Peer pressure  and  Unstable family is associated with drug abuse in adolescence. \n\nR: Smoking increases the risk of urinary bladder cancer.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Factors associated with drug and alcohol abuse among adolescents are unstable or unsupportive family structures and peer pressure. Smoking is associated with increased incidence of cancers of lung, urinary bladder and throat,\nbronchitis, emphysema, coronary heart disease, gastric ulcer. (NCERT Class 12 pg 160 paragraph 1 and section 8.5.1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Assertion Reason and Multiple Statement Questions',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302301,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Smoking causes O₂ deficiency in the body. \n\nR: Smoking increases CO content and reduces the haem-bound oxygen.','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Smoking increases carbon monoxide (CO) content in blood and reduces the concentration of haembound oxygen. This causes O₂ deficiency in the body. (NCERT Class 12 pg 160 paragraph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302302,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Repeated drug use leads to increased tolerance of receptors of the body. \n\nR: Consequently, the receptors can respond to lower doses of drugs.','A is true but R is false','With repeated use of drugs, the tolerance level of the receptors present in our body increases. Consequently the receptors respond only to higher doses of drugs or alcohol leading to greater intake and addiction. (NCERT Class 12 pg 161 section 8.5.2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302303,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Drug addict experiences Withdrawal Symptoms when the drug dose is suddenly increased. \n\nR: Withdrawal symptoms are characterised by calmness, euphoria and a feeling of well-being.','Both A and R are false.','Unpleasant withdrawal syndrome are experienced if regular dose of drugs/alcohol is abruptly discontinued. This is characterised by anxiety, shakiness, nausea and sweating, which may be relieved when drug use is resumed. (NCERT Class 12 pg 161 section 8.5.2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302304,'biology','Read the following statements about DRUG ADDICTION; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Anxiety, shakiness, nausea, etc are seen in withdrawal symptoms. \n\n2. Tolerance leads to receptors responding to higher dose of drugs only. \n\n3. Unexplained absence from school and good academic performance are warning signs of drug abuse. \n\n4. Nicotine decreases heart rate and blood pressure.','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Anxiety, shakiness, nausea, etc are seen in withdrawal symptoms. Tolerance to drugs leads to receptors responding to higher dose of drugs only. Unexplained absence from school and poor academic performance are warning signs of drug abuse. Nicotine increases heart rate and blood pressure by relelase of adrenaline. (NCERT Class 12 pg 160 paragraph 1 - pg 161 section 8.5.3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 3 and 4 are incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302305,'biology','Read the following statements about DRUG ADDICTION; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Excessive alcohol abuse for a long time causes cirrhosis of spleen. \n\n2. Intravenous drug abusers are at risk of Hepatitis B infection. \n\n3. Smack is a bitter, white crystalline substance. \n\n4. Withdrawal symptoms occur due to sudden stoppage of drugs in an addict.','2, 3 and 4 are correct','Excessive alcohol abuse for a long time causes cirrhosis of LIVER. Intravenous drug abusers are at risk of Hepatitis B infection (through blood route). Smack (POPPY) is a bitter, white crystalline substance. Withdrawal symptoms occur due to sudden stoppage of drugs in an addict. (NCERT Class 12 pg 158 section 8.5- 162 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','2, 3 and 4 are correct','Only 4 is incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302306,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Anabolic steroids in females can cause masculinisation, mood swings. \n\nR: Sportsperson abuse anabolic steroids to increase athletic performance.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','The side-effects of the use of anabolic steroids in females include masculinisation (features like males), increased aggressiveness, mood swings, depression, abnormal menstrual cycles, excessive hair growth on the face and body, enlargement of clitoris, deepening of voice. Sportpersons abuse anabolic steroids to increase muscle strength and bulk and to promote aggressiveness and as a result increase athletic performance. (NCERT Class 12 pg 162 paragraph 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302307,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Misuse of anabolic steroids by males can cause acne, prostate enlargement, mood swings, etc. \n\nR: Intra-oral drug abusers have higher risk of acquiring Hepatitis B infection.','A is true but R is false','Misuse of anabolic steroids by males can cause acne, increased aggressiveness, mood swings, depression, reduction of size of the testicles, decreased sperm production, potential for kidney and liver dysfunction, breast enlargement, premature baldness, enlargement of the prostate gland. INTRA-VENOUS drug abusers have higher risk of acquiring Hepatitis B infection. (NCERT Class 12 paragraph 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302308,'biology','Read the following statements about DISEASES, DRUG ABUSE AND ADDICTION; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Avoiding peer pressure can prevent drug abuse during adolescence. \n\n2. Irregular menstrual cycles occur in females abusing anabolic steroids. \n\n3. AIDS can be acquired through infected needle usage. \n\n4. Withdrawal is characterised by euphoria.','Only 4 is incorrect','Avoiding peer pressure (proper nurture) can prevent drug abuse during adolescences. Irregular menstrual cycles in females abusing anabolic steroids. AIDS can be acquired through infected needle (hematogenous route) usage. Withdrawal symptom is characterised by shakiness, nausea, sweating and anxiety. (NCERT Class 12 pg 161 section 8.5.2 - pg 162 section 8.5.4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Both 1 and 4 are correct','Only 4 is incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302309,'biology','Read the following statements about ADDICTION PREVENTION; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Psychologists/Psychiatrists can help in rehabilitation of drug addicts. \n\n2. Unexplained good academic performance is a warning marker of drug abuse. \n\n3. Cocaine makes a person lethargic. \n\n4. AIDS is caused by a DNA virus.','Only 1 is correct','Psychologists/Psychiatrists can help in rehabilitation of drug addicts. Unexplained poor academic performance and absence from school is a warning marker of drug abuse. Cocaine makes a person alert, active and euphoric. AIDS is caused by a RNA (retro) virus. (NCERT class 12 pg 159 paragraph 3, pg 161 section 8.5.3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is correct','Both 2 and 4 are correct','Both 3 and 4 are correct','Only 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302310,'biology','Read the following statements about DRUGS AND DISEASES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Alcohol intake during pregnancy can affect the foetus. \n\n2. Cannabinoids are obtained from inflorescences of Erythroxylum coca. \n\n3. Atropa belladona products have hallucinogenic effects. \n\n4. Cancer cells show contact inhibition property.','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Alcohol intake during pregnancy can affect the foetus. Cannabinoids are obtained from inflorescences of  Cannabis sativa.  Atropa belladona products have hallucinogenic effects. Cancer cells DO NOT SHOW contact inhibition property. (NCERT Class 12 pg 157 paragraph 3, pg 159 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Both 1 and 3 are incorrect','Only 3 is incorrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302311,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Benzodiazepines are used to treat depression and insomnia in patients. \n\nR: Morphine is an effective antacid and used to treat hyper-acidity.','A is true but R is false','Benzodiazepines, barbiturates and amphetamines are used to treat depression and insomnia in patients. Morphine is an effective analgesic, sedative and painkiller. (NCERT Class 12 pg 159 paragraph 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302312,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Nicotine stimulates adrenal gland to secrete adrenaline into circulation. \n\nR: Nicotine is an effective painkiller and sedative.','A is true but R is false','Nicotine stimulates adrenal gland to secrete  adrenaline into circulation, which raise blood pressure and increase heart rate. Nicotine is an alkaloid. with no therapeutic role. (NCERT Class 12 pg 160 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302313,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Allergy is mediated by IgE type of antibodies. \n\nR: Anti-histamines and steroids can reduce the symptoms of allergy.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','The antibodies produced in allergy are of IgE type. The use of drugs like anti-histamine, adrenalin and steroids quickly reduce the symptoms of allergy. (NCERT Class 12 pg 153 section 8.2.5)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(31116,'chemistry','Match the property with the direction in which it increases in tha periodic table:','Electron gain enthalpy-left to right,Non metallic character-bottom left to top right diagnol,Atomic radius-right to left,Metallic character-top right to bottom left diagnol','Ionisation enthalpy is inversely proportional to electron gain enthalpy. \n\nAs shown in diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; diagram(ne0312)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'3','match','Electron gain enthalpy,left to right','Non metallic character,bottom left to top right diagnol','Atomic radius,right to left','Metallic character,top right to bottom left diagnol','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(302314,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Origin and maturation of lymphocytes occur in a primary lymphoid organ. \n\nR: Spleen and Peyer\'s patch are secondary lymphoid organs.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Lymphocytes originate and mature in primary lymphoid organs such as bone marrow and thymus. Example of secondary lymphoid organs are spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils, Peyer’s patches of small intestine and appendix. (NCERT Class 12 pg 153 section 8.2.7)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302315,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Colostrum contains abundant IgG that provides passive immunity to child. \n\nR: Snake anti-venom is an example of natural active immunisation.','Both A and R are false.','Colostrum contains abundant IgA that provides passive immunity to child. Snake anti-venom is an example of natural PASSIVE immunisation because it contains pre-formed antibodies. (NCERT Class 12 pg 152 section 8.2.3 and 8.2.4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302316,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Hepatitis B vaccine administration is an example of active immunisation. \n\nR: Hepatitis B is produced by Recombinant DNA technology.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Hepatitis B vaccine administration is an example of active immunisation because host body is exposed to natural antigens leading to immune system activation. Hepatitis B is produced by Recombinant DNA technology from YEAST. (NCERT Class 12 pg 152 section 8.2.4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302317,'biology','Read the following statements about INFECTIOUS DISEASES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Internal bleeding, muscular pain, fever and anemia is seen in ascariasis. \n\n2. Wuchereria can cause Elephantiasis. \n\n3. Ringworm causes dry, scaly and itchy lesions on skin and nails \n\n4. Plasmodium malariae causes most serious type of malaria.','Only 4 is incorrect','Internal bleeding, muscular pain, fever and anemia is seen in ascariasis. Wuchereria bancrofti/malayi can cause Elephantiasis. Ringworm (fungal infections) causes dry, scaly and itchy lesions on skin and nails. Plasmodium falciparum causes most serious type of malaria. (NCERT Class 12 147 paragraph 4, pg 149 paragraph 2, 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are incorrect','Only 4 is incorrect','Both 2 and 4 are correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302318,'biology','Read the following statements about INFECTIOUS DISEASES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Genitals and limbs undergo gross deformity in elephantiasis. \n\n2. Trichosporum is a fungal genus causing ringworm. \n\n3. Diptheria is caused by a bacteria. \n\n4. Smallpox is completely eradicated from the world.','Only 2 is incorrect','Genitals and limbs undergo gross deformity in elephantiasis caused by Wuchereria sp. TRICHOPHYTON, MICROSPORUM and EPIDERMOPHYTON are the fungal genus causing ringworm. Diptheria is caused by a bacteria named Corynebacterium diptheriae. Smallpox is completely eradicated from the world. (NCERT Class 12 pg 149 paragraph 3,4- pg 150 paragraph 2)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 3 and 4 are incorrrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302319,'biology','Read the following statements about INFECTIOUS DISEASES; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Polio can be controlled by vaccination (named Oral Polio Vaccine). \n\n2. Chickengunya is transmitted by Anopheles mosquito. \n\n3. Hepatitis B vaccine is a recombinant vaccine. \n\n4. AIDS is caused by a retro virus.','Only 2 is incorrect','Polio can be controlled by vaccination (named Oral Polio Vaccine) as per NATIONAL IMMUNISATION SCHEDULE. Chicken-gunya is transmitted by AEDES mosquito. Hepatitis B vaccine is a recombinant vaccine made from yeast. AIDS is caused by a retro virus (named HIV). (NCERT Class 12 pg 150 paragraph 1, pg 152 section 8.2.4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Only 2 is incorrect','Both 1 and 2 are correct','Both 3 and 4 are incorrrect','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302320,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Monocytes and natural killer cells are cytokine barriers of innate immunity. \n\nR: Humoral immunity is responsible for graft rejection.','Both A and R are false.','Monocytes and natural killer cells are CELLULAR barriers of innate immunity. CELL MEDIATED IMMUNITY (CMI) is responsible for graft rejection. (NCERT Class 12 pg 151 paragraph 2 and pg 152 paragraph 1)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302321,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Spleen is a blood filter and a secondary lymphoid organ. \n\nR: MALT constitutes 50% of total lymphoid tissue in the body.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','Spleen acts as a filter of the blood by trapping blood-borne microorganisms. Spleen is a secondary lymphoid organ. MALT (Mucosa Associated Lymphoid Tissue) constitutes about 50 % of the lymphoid tissue in human body. (NCERT Class 12 pg 154 paragraph 2,3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302322,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: AIDS can be acquired by contaminated blood transfusion. \n\nR: HIV mainly affects the helper T lymphocytes.','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','AIDS can be acquired by sexual contact, contaminated blood transfusion, mother to child, intravenous drug abusers. HIV replicated in the helper T lymphocytes and causes progressive decrease in their number of T lymphocytes. (NCERT Class 12 pg 154 section 8.3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302323,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: X ray and UV rays are chemical ionising carcinogens. \n\nR: Cancer cells stop dividing upon contact with adjacent cells.','Both A and R are false.','X ray and UV rays are Physical ionising and non-ionising carcinogens respectively. Cancer cells do not stop dividing upon contact with adjacent cells (i.e. they dont show CONTACT INHIBITION) (NCERT Class 12 pg 157 paragraph 2,3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302324,'biology','Choose the correct option regarding the following assertion(A) and reason(R):\n\nA: Smack is an anti-depressant and makes a person alert and active. \n\nR: Cocaine is obtained from the latex of Papaver somniferum.','Both A and R are false.','Heroin (SMACK) is a depressant and slows down body functions. Cocaine is obtained from Erythroxylum coca, native to South America. (NCERT Class 12 pg 159 paragraph 1, 3)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A','Both A and R are true and R is not the correct explanation of A','A is true but R is false','Both A and R are false.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(302325,'biology','Read the following statements about DRUGS; Which of the following is/are correct/incorrect?\n\n1. Morphine is an effective sedative and painkiller. \n\n2. Datura products can cause analgesia. \n\n3. Cocaine has stimulating effects on CNS. \n\n4. Charas, ganga and marijuana are types of opiods.','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Morphine is an effective sedative and painkiller (ANALGESIC). Datura products can cause HALLUCINATION. Cocaine has stimulating effects on CNS. Charas, ganga and marijuana are types of CANNABINOIDS. (NCERT Class 12 pg 159 paragraph 2, 3, 4)','Biology In Human Welfare >> Human Health and Disease >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Only 1 is incorrect','Both 2 and 3 are correct','Both 2 and 4 are incorrect','Only 4 is correct','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(330101,'biology','_______ deals with techniques of using live organisms or enzymes from organisms to produce products and processes useful to humans.','Biotechnology','Biotechnology deals with techniques of using live organisms or enzymes from organisms to produce products and processes useful to humans. In short Biotechnology, the use of biology to solve problems and make useful products.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Introduction','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Biotechnology','Biotherapy','Biology','Bio-Informatics','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(330102,'biology','Microbe-mediated processess like making curd, bread and wine are not forms of biotechnology. Choose whether the given statement is true/false.','FALSE','Making curd, bread or wine, which are all microbe-mediated processes, could also be thought as a form of biotechnology.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Introduction','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(330103,'biology','Which of the following is not an example of Biotechnology','Planting a wild variety of crop that gives high yield','However, it is used in a restricted sense today, to refer to such of those processes which use genetically modified organisms to achieve the same on a larger scale. Further, many other processes/techniques are also included under biotechnology. For example, in vitro fertilisation leading to a ‘test-tube’ baby, synthesising a gene and using it, developing a DNA vaccine or correcting a defective gene, are all part of biotechnology.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Introduction','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Planting a wild variety of crop that gives high yield','Development of a DNA vaccine','In-Vitro Fertilisation','Correction of a defective gene','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(330104,'biology','Which of the following techniques come under Biotechnology \n\ni)Modifying a gene\n\nii)Synthesizing a new gene  \n\niii)Developing a DNA vaccine    \n\niv)Correcting a defective gene','i, ii, iii and iv','Synthesising a gene and using it, developing a DNA vaccine or correcting a defective gene, are all part of biotechnology.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Introduction','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','i','i and ii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(330105,'biology','EFB stands for','European Federation of Biotechnology','The European Federation of Biotechnology (EFB) was established by European scientists in 1978.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Introduction','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Ex-vivo Fertilized Baby','Eurasian Federation for Biotechnology','European Federation of Biotechnology','European Fund of Biotechnology','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(330106,'biology','_______ has given a definition of biotechnology that encompasses both traditional view and modern molecular biotechnology.','European Federation of Biotechnology','The European Federation of Biotechnology (EFB) has given a definition of biotechnology that encompasses both traditional view and modern molecular biotechnology. the definition given by EFB is as follows:‘the integration of natural science and organisms, cells, parts thereof, and molecular analogues for products and services.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Introduction','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','The Biomedical Engineering Society','Indian Council of Medical Research','European Federation of Biotechnology','American Federation of Biotechnology','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(330107,'biology','The definition of Biotechnology given by European Federation of Biotechnology (EFB) is','‘The integration of natural science and organisms, cells, parts thereof, and molecular analogues for products and services’.','The definition of Biotechnology given by EFB is ‘the integration of natural science and organisms, cells, parts thereof, and molecular analogues for products and services’.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Introduction','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','‘The integration of natural science and organisms, cells, parts thereof, and molecular analogues for products and services’.','The area of biology that uses living processes, organisms or systems to manufacture products or technology intended to improve the quality of human life\'.','The exploitation of biological processes for  industrial and other purposes, especially the genetic manipulation of  microorganisms for the production of antibiotics, hormones, etc.','The area of biology that uses living processes or organisms to  produce products intended to improve the quality of human life','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(31117,'chemistry','On Pauling scale, electronegativity of C is similar to:','S','On Pauling scale, electronegativity of C is similar to S. Electronegativity Values (on Pauling scale) across the periods, as shown in the diagram \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 91; diagram(ne0313)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'2','mcq','P','N','S','Si','NEW','11-091','11-091',NULL),
(330108,'biology','Biotechnology is used in a restricted sense\ntoday, to refer to such of those processes which','using genetically modified organisms on \nlarger scale','However, it is used in a restricted sense\ntoday, to refer to such of those processes which use\ngenetically modified organisms to achieve the same on a\nlarger scale.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Introduction','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','making curd','using genetically modified organisms on \nlarger scale','making bread','making wine','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(330201,'biology','Which of the following techniques can alter the genetic material  \n\ni)Genetic engineering   \n\nii)Genetic screening   \n\niii) Electroporation   \n\niv)Electrophoresis','only i','Genetic engineering is the process of using recombinant DNA (rDNA) technology to alter the genetic makeup of an organism. Traditionally, humans have manipulated genomes indirectly by controlling breeding and selecting offspring with desired traits. Even though electrophoresis is used in the separation of DNA fragments, it is not gene modifying.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','only i','only ii','i and iv','i, ii and iv','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(330202,'biology','Maintenance of sterile environment in chemical engineering processes is desired to produce which of the following products?\n\n i)Antibiotics \n\n ii)Vaccines  \n\niii)Enzymes   \n\niv)Fossil Fuels','i, ii and iii','In order to produce desired biotechnology products such as antibiotics, vaccines, enzymes, etc, we should provide a sterile environment in the lab. Fossil Fuels cannot be synthesised in the lab, they take millions of years to form.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','i','i and ii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(330203,'biology','Which of the following statement is true','Asexual reproduction preserves the genetic information, while sexual reproduction permits variation.','The asexual reproduction provides opportunities for preserving the unique combinations of genetic setup, some of which may be beneficial to the organism as well as the population.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Sexual reproduction preserves the genetic information, while asexual reproduction permits variation.','Asexual reproduction preserves the genetic information, while sexual reproduction permits variation.','Both asexual and sexual reproduction preserve genetic information','Both asexual and sexual reproduction permit variation.','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(330204,'biology','_______ reproduction permits variation.','Sexual','The sexual reproduction provides opportunities for variations and formulation of unique combinations of genetic setup, some of which may be beneficial to the organism as well as the population.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Sexual','Asexual (Binary Fission)','Asexual(Fragmentation)','Asexual(Budding)','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(330205,'biology','Why recombinant DNA technology is more useful than traditional hybridisation procedures?  \n\ni)Isolation and introduction of only specific or a set of desirable genes without introducing undesirable genes into the target genome is possible\n\nii)Use of gene cloning and gene transfer can be effectively employed for better use\n\niii)Decreased chance of inclusion and multiplication of undesirable genes along with the desired genes.  \n\niv)Recombinants always produce undesirable gene products','i, ii and iii','Traditional hybridisation procedures used in plant and animal breeding, very often lead to inclusion and multiplication of undesirable genes along with the desired genes. Techniques of genetic engineering which include creation of recombinant DNA, use of gene cloning and gene transfer, overcome this limitation and allows us to isolate and introduce only one or a set of desirable genes without introducing undesirable genes into the target organism.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(330206,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect according to fate of piece of DNA?','The piece of DNA would not be metabolised and if metabolised would consume large amounts of the cell energy.','Most likely the fate of any piece of DNA would be not be able to multiply itself in the progeny cells of the organism. But, when it gets integrated into the genome of the recipient, it may multiply and be inherited along with the host DNA. This is because the alien piece of DNA has become part of a chromosome, which has the ability to replicate.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','The piece of DNA might not be able to multiply itself in the progeny cells of the organism','When it gets integrated into the genome of the recipient, it may multiply and be inherited along with the host DNA','The alien piece of DNA would become part of a chromosome, when integrated into a recipient which has the ability to replicate','The piece of DNA would not be metabolised and if metabolised would consume large amounts of the cell energy.','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(330207,'biology','The DNA sequence within the chromosome\'s genetic sequence which is responsible for the initation of replication is called as','Origin Of Replication','In a chromosome there is a specific DNA sequence called the Origin of Replication, which is responsible for initiating replication.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Okazaki fragment','Orifice of replication','Origin Of Replication','Origin of Translation','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(330208,'biology','An alien DNA is linked with the origin of replication, so that, this alien piece of DNA can replicate and multiply itself in the host organism. This can also be called as ____________','Cloning- Making multiple identical copies of any template DNA','An alien DNA is linked with the origin of replication, so that, this alien piece of DNA can replicate and multiply itself in the host organism. This can also be called as cloning or making multiple identical copies of any template DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Cloning- Making multiple identical copies of any template DNA','Bioprocessing- Removal and retrieval of the product aside from impurities.','Gene transfer- Transfer of gene into host','Polymerase Chain reaction- Generating multiple copies of genetic material','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(31118,'chemistry','The increase in electronegativities across a period is accompanied by ____ in non-metallic properties of elements.','Increase','The increase in electronegativities across a period is accompanied by an increase in non-metallic properties (or decrease in metallic properties) of elements.\n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11; Page 92; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Electronegativity',NULL,'1','mcq','Increase','Decrease','Unchanged','Reversible','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(330209,'biology','The first recombinant DNA was constructed by linking an antibiotic resistant gene with the native plasmid of which bacteria','Salmonella typhimurium','The construction of the first recombinant DNA emerged from the possibility of linking a gene encoding antibiotic resistance with a native plasmid (autonomously replicating circular extra-chromosomal DNA) of Salmonella typhimurium.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Escherichia coli','Salmonella typhimurium','Clostridium butylicum','Acetobacter aceti','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(330210,'biology','Which year did Cohen and Boyer isolate an antibiotic resistance gene by cutting out a piece of DNA from a plasmid having antibiotic resistance','1972','Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer accomplished this in 1972 by isolating the antibiotic resistance gene by cutting out a piece of DNA from a plasmid which was responsible for conferring antibiotic resistance.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','1962','1972','1982','1965','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(330211,'biology','Molecular Scissors is a popular name used for','Restriction enzymes','Restriction enzymes are also called \'molecular scissors\' as they cleave DNA at or near specific recognition sequences known as restriction sites. These enzymes make one incision on each of the two strands of DNA and are also called restriction endonucleases.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','DNA Polymerase','RNA polymerase','Restriction enzymes','Ligase','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330212,'biology','Restriction enzymes is also known as','Molecular scissors','Restriction enzymes are also called \'molecular scissors\' as they cleave DNA at or near specific recognition sequences known as restriction sites. These enzymes make one incision on each of the two strands of DNA and are also called restriction endonucleases.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Molecular Enzymes','Molecular scissors','Replication Scissors','Molecular Glue','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330213,'biology','Autonomously replicating circular extrachromosomal DNA in prokaryotesis called','Plasmid','A plasmid is a small, often circular DNA molecule found in bacteria and other cells. Plasmids are separate from the bacterial chromosome and replicate independently of it. They generally carry only a small number of genes, notably some associated with antibiotic resistance. It is an autonomously replicating circular extrachromosomal DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Chromatin','Plasmid','Phagemid','Nucleoid','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(330214,'biology','Vector used to deliver an alien piece of DNA into the host organism, by integration and replication along with host genome','Plasmid','A Plasmid can be used as vector to deliver an alien piece of DNA into the host organism.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','fillup','Pl,as,mid',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330215,'biology','The linking of antibiotic resistance gene with the plasmid vector became possible with','DNA ligase','A Plasmid can be used as vector to deliver an alien piece of DNA into the host organism.The linking of antibiotic resistance gene with the plasmid vector became possible with the enzyme DNA ligase,which acts on the cut ends of the DNA molecules and joins their ends.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','DNA ligase','Endonucleases','DNA polymerase','Exonucleases','NEW','12-195','12-165','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/330215q.png'),
(330216,'biology','The new combination of circular autonomously replicating DNA created in vitro and is known as _____________','Recombinant DNA','The new combination of circular autonomously replicating DNA created in vitro and is known as recombinant DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Vector DNA','Recombinant DNA','Plasmid DNA','Registered DNA','NEW','12-195','12-165','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/330216q.png'),
(330217,'biology','When this recombinant DNA is transferred into _________, a bacterium closely related to Salmonella, it could replicate using the new host’s _________ enzyme and make multiple copies','Escherichia coli,DNA polymerase','When this DNA is transferred into Escherichia coli, a bacterium closely related to Salmonella, it could replicate using the new host’s DNA polymerase enzyme and make multiple copies','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Escherichia coli,DNA polymerase','Escherichia coli,RNA polymerase','Salmonella typhi,DNA polymerase','Salmonella typhi,RNA polymerase','NEW','12-195','12-165','/ncert/pyq/physics/mcq/330217q.png'),
(330218,'biology','The following are basic steps in genetically modifying an organism, Choose in the correct order - \n\nA. Identification of DNA with desirable genes;\n\nB. Introduction of the identified DNA into the host; \n\nC. Maintenance of introduced DNA in the host and transfer of the DNA to its progeny.','A, B and C','There are three basic steps in genetically modifying an organism —\n\n(i) Identification of DNA with desirable genes;\n\n(ii) Introduction of the identified DNA into the host; \n\n(iii )Maintenance of introduced DNA in the host and transfer of the DNA to its progeny.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','C, B and A','A, C and B','A, B and C','B, A and C','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330219,'biology','Which of the following are genetic engineering techniques \n\ni)Recombinant DNA technolgy \n\nii)Transduction\n\niii)Gene transfer     \n\niv)Gene cloning','i, iii and iv','The techniques of genetic engineering which include creation of recombinant DNA, use of gene cloning and gene transfer','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','i, ii','iii and iv','i, iii and iv','ii and iii','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330220,'biology','Isolation of the antibiotic resistance gene by cutting out a piece of DNA from a plasmid in 1972 was first done by','Cohen and Boyer','Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer accomplished this in 1972 by isolating the antibiotic resistance gene by cutting out a piece of DNA from a plasmid which was responsible for conferring antibiotic resistance.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Meselson and Stahl','Cohen and Boyer','Watson and Crick','Jean and Maurice','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330221,'biology','Which of the following are examples of vectors used  in rDNA technology?   \n\ni)Plasmid transferring alien piece of DNA \n\nii)Mosquito transferring malarial parasite   \n\niii)Viruses carrying genetically modified DNA or RNA for transfer.\n\niv)Plasmids carrying amino acid residues into bacterium','i and iii','Vectors are primarily responsible for transferring genetic material like DNA or RNA or a gene from a bacteria or a virus to another bacteria or to a host cell. Hence the first and third options are examples of vectors in rDNA technology, while the second option is an example of vector transmitting a disease. The fourth option involves transfer of nutrients which is not done by plasmids, which is altogether a false statement','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330222,'biology','Which of the following is the function of Ligase enzyme?','Joins the cut ends of DNA','DNA ligase, which acts on cut DNA molecules and joins their ends.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Acts as an transporter enzyme','Replaces mismatched nucleotides in DNA molecule','Cuts RNA molecules and joins their ends','Joins the cut ends of DNA','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330223,'biology','Genetic engineering includes: \n1. Techniques to alter the\nchemistry of DNA and RNA\n2.to introduce rDNA into host organisms\n3.change the\nGenotype of the host organism.\n4.manufacture of biotechnological products like wine, Antibiotics, vaccines, enzymes, etc.','1, 2','Among many, the two core techniques that enabled birth\nof modern biotechnology are :\n(i) Genetic engineering : Techniques to alter the\nchemistry of genetic material (DNA and RNA), and (ii) Bioprocess engineering: Maintenance of sterile (microbial contamination-free) ambience\nin chemical engineering processes to enable growth of only the\ndesired microbe/eukaryotic cell in large quantities for the\nmanufacture of biotechnological products like antibiotics,\nvaccines, enzymes, etc.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','1, 2','2,3','1,2,3,4','3,4','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330224,'biology','The ______provides opportunities for variations\nand formulation of unique combinations of genetic setup, some of which\nmay be beneficial to the organism as well as the population.','Sexual Reproduction','You probably appreciate the advantages of sexual reproduction over\nasexual reproduction. The former provides opportunities for variations\nand formulation of unique combinations of genetic setup, some of which\nmay be beneficial to the organism as well as the population.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Asexual reproduction','Sexual Reproduction','Biotechnology','Gene cloning','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330225,'biology','State True or False. The multiplication of any alien piece of DNA in an organism\nit needs to be a part of a chromosome(s) which has a specific sequence\nknown as ‘origin of recombination\'','FALSE','the multiplication of any alien piece of DNA in an organism\nit needs to be a part of a chromosome(s) which has a specific sequence\nknown as ‘origin of replication’.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(330226,'biology','Consider the following statement, Select the suitable INCORRECT option.  The cutting of DNA at specific locations became possible with the discovery of the so-called','plasmid','The cutting of DNA at\nspecific locations became possible with the discovery of the so-called"‘molecular scissors’– restriction enzymes.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Principles Of Biotechnology','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','restriction enzymes','molecular scissors','restriction endonuclease','plasmid','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330301,'biology','What tools are necessary for the Recombinant DNA technology to be accomplished?    \n\nI)Restriction and Polymerase enzymes  \n\nii)Host organism  \n\niii)Ligases\n    \niv)Vectors','i, ii, iii and iv','Recombinant DNA technology can be accomplished only if we have the key tools, i.e., restriction enzymes, polymerase enzymes, ligases, vectors and the host organism.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','i','i and ii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330302,'biology','Which year were, the two enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bacteriophage in bacteria were first isolated?\nWhich Bacteria were they isolated first from?','1963,Escherichia coli','In the year 1963, the two enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bacteriophage in Escherichia coli were isolated.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','1962,Salmonella typhimurium','1963,Escherichia coli','1963,Psuedomonas syringae','1962,Clostridium butylicum','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330303,'biology','From the two restriction enzyme isolated from the Escherichia coli ,one of these added methyl groups to DNA, while the other cut DNA. The one that cut DNA was','Restriction endonuclease','One of the restriction enzyme isolated from the Escherichia coli ,one of these added methyl groups to DNA, while the other cut DNA. The later was called Restriction Endonucleases.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Restriction endonuclease','Recombinant DNA','DNA polymerase','Restriction Exonuclease','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330304,'biology','The first restriction endonuclease was','Hind II','The first restriction endonuclease–Hind II, whose functioning depended on a specific DNA nucleotide sequence was isolated and characterised five years later.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','EcoR II','Hind II','Hind III','AvaI','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330305,'biology','Restriction endonuclease Hind II always cuts DNA molecule at a particular point by recognising a specific sequence of','Six base pairs','Hind II was the first discovered restriction endonuclease enzyme. It has been isolated from Haemophilus influenzae. It cuts DNA molecules at a particular point by recognising a specific sequence of six base pairs. This specific sequence is known as the recognition sequence for Hind II.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Five base pairs','Seven base pairs','Six base pairs','Three base pairs','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(31201,'chemistry','Nowadays, the word _____ is used for valence.','Oxidation state','Nowadays the term oxidation state is frequently used for valence. \n\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 11 (Part 1); Page 92; Paragraph no. 4)','Inorganic chemistry >> Classification of elements and periodicity in properties >> Oxidation states',NULL,'1','mcq','Oxidation state','Power of Hydrogen','Atomic No.','Configuration','NEW','11-092','11-092',NULL),
(330306,'biology','Assertion: Hind II is a restriction endonuclease \nReason: Hind II always cuts DNA molecules at a particular point by recognising a specific sequence of six base pairs.','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','The first restriction endonuclease was Hind-II,Hind-II always cut DNA molecules at a particular point by recognising a specific sequence of six base pairs','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330307,'biology','The specific base sequence called _________ for Hind II where it cuts DNA molecules at a particular point by recognising a specific sequence of six base pairs','Recognition sequence','It was found that Hind II always cut DNA molecules at a particular point by recognising a specific sequence of six base pairs. This specific base sequence is known as the recognition sequence for Hind II.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Recognition sequence','Recombinant sequence','Polymerase sequence','Endonuclease sequence','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330308,'biology','Which one is an example of restriction endonuclease?','EcoR1','The convention for naming these enzymes is the first letter of the name comes from the genus and the second two letters come from the species of the prokaryotic cell from which they were isolated, e.g., EcoRI comes from Escherichia coli RY 13.In EcoRI, the letter R is derived from the name of strain.Roman numbers following the names indicate the order in which the enzymes were isolated from that strain of bacteria.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','pBR322','EcoR1','pUCl8','pSC101','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330309,'biology','In EcoRI, the letter ‘R’ is derived from the name of _____ and Roman numbers following the names indicate the _______ in which the enzymes were isolated.','strain,order','In EcoRI, the letter ‘R’ is derived from the name of strain. Roman numbers following the names indicate the order in which\nthe enzymes were isolated from that strain of bacteria.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','order,strain','strain,order','class,order','species,class','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330310,'biology','Restriction enzymes belong to a larger class of enzymes called?','Nucleases','Restriction enzymes belong to a larger class of enzymes called nucleases.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Proteases','Nucleases','Ligases','Lipases','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(330311,'biology','How many kinds of nucleases are there?','2','There are two kinds of nucleases. Endonucleases and Exonucleases.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','2','3','4','5','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(330312,'biology','What are the functions of Exonucleases and Endonucleases respectively?','Exonuclease-Remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA , Endonuclease-making addition at specific positions within the DNA.','Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA whereas, endonucleases make cuts at specific positions within the DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','match','Exonuclease,Remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA','Endonuclease,making addition at specific positions within the DNA',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(330313,'biology','________ are enzymes that remove nucleotides from the ends of DNA.','Exonucleases','Exonucleases are enzymes that work by cleaving nucleotides one at a time from the end (exo) of a polynucleotide chain. A hydrolyzing reaction that breaks phosphodiester bonds at either the 3′ or the 5′ end occurs. Or  Exonucleases are enzymes that remove nucleotides from the ends of DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Exonucleases','Endonucleases','Ligases','Polymerases','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(330314,'biology','________ are enzymes that make cuts at specific positions in the DNA.','Endonucleases','Endonucleases are enzymes that cleave the phosphodiester bond within a polynucleotide chain. Some of them have no regard to sequence when cutting DNA, but many others do so only at specific nucleotide sequences. The latter group is often called restriction endonucleases or restriction enzymes. Or Endonucleases are enzymes that make cuts at specific positions in the DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Exonucleases','Endonucleases','Ligases','Polymerases','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(330315,'biology','Each restriction endonuclease functions in the following order','Inspection, recognition and cleaving the specific site','Each restriction endonuclease functions by ‘inspecting’ the length of a DNA sequence. Once it finds its specific recognition sequence, it\nwill bind to the DNA and cut each of the two strands of the double\nhelix at specific points in their sugar -phosphate backbones','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Inspection, recognition and cleaving the specific site','Recognition, inspection and cleaving the specific site','Cleaving the specific site, Inspection and recognition','Inspection, cleaving site and recognition','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(330316,'biology','Assertion: Each restriction endonuclease functions by first ‘inspecting’ the length of a DNA sequence.\n\nReason: The specific recognition sequence has to be recognised before cleaving, after recognition it will bind to the DNA and cut each of the two strands of the double helix at specific points in their sugar -phosphate backbones.','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','Each restriction endonuclease functions by ‘inspecting’ the length of a DNA sequence. Once it finds its specific recognition sequence, it will bind to the DNA and cut each of the two strands of the double helix at specific points in their sugar -phosphate backbones. Each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific palindromic nucleotide sequences in the DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(330317,'biology','Each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific sequence called ______in DNA','Palindromic nucleotide sequences','Each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific palindromic nucleotide sequences in the DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Palindromic nucleoside sequences','Palindromic nucleotide sequences','Perfect nucleotide sequences','Recognition site','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(330318,'biology','What is an example of palindromes?','MALAYALAM','These are groups of letters that form the same words when read both forward and backward, e.g., “MALAYALAM”. As against a word-palindrome where the same word is read in both directions, the palindrome in DNA is a sequence of base pairs that reads same on the two strands when orientation of reading is kept the same.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','INDIA','MALAYALAM','BEAUTIFUL','HINDI','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(330319,'biology','Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the _____sites.','Palindrome','Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on the opposite strands','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Palindrome','Opposite','Central','Repetitive','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330320,'biology','Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on the opposite strands. This leaves pieces of DNA with','Single stranded portions at the ends','Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on the opposite strands. This leaves single stranded portions at the ends. There are overhanging stretches called sticky ends on each strand','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Double stranded portions at the middle','Single stranded portions at the ends','Single and double strands at the end','Single and double strands at the middle','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330321,'biology','The overhanging stretches of DNA after the action of restriction enzymes are called','sticky ends','Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on the opposite strands. This leaves single stranded portions at the ends. There are overhanging stretches called sticky ends on each strand','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','selectable marker','sticky ends','non-sticky ends','repetitive strands','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330322,'biology','Stickiness is attributed to formation of which type of bonds following cleaving by a restriction endonuclease?','Hydrogen','Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on the opposite strands. This leaves single stranded portions at the ends. There are overhanging stretches called sticky ends on each strand. These are named so because they form hydrogen bonds with their complementary cut counterparts. This stickiness of the ends facilitates the action of the enzyme DNA ligase.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Ionic','Hydrogen','Phosphoesterase','Phosphodiesterase','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330323,'biology','Stickiness of the ends after the action of restriction enzymes facilitates gluing process by the the action of which enzyme?','DNA ligase','This stickiness of the ends facilitates the action of the enzyme DNA ligase.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','DNA polymerase','DNA ligase','RNA ligase','RNA polymerase','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330324,'biology','Why are restriction endonucleases used in genetic engineering ?','in the process of making recombinant molecules of DNA.','Restriction endonucleases are used in genetic engineering to form ‘recombinant’ molecules of DNA, which are composed of DNA from different sources/genomes.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','in the process of making recombinant molecules of DNA.','to induce mutations','to silence defective gene','to silence carrier gene','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330325,'biology','When cut by the same restriction enzyme, the resultant DNA fragments have the','Same kind of ‘sticky-ends’','When cut by the same restriction enzyme, the resultant DNA fragments have the same kind of ‘sticky-ends’ and, these can be joined together (end-to-end) using DNA ligases','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Different \'sticky ends\'','Same kind of ‘sticky-ends’','One sticky end and one nonsticky end','Two non sticky ends','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330326,'biology','Unless the vector and the source DNA are cut with the same restriction enzyme, the desired recombinant vector molecule cannot be created.','TRUE','You may have realised that normally, unless one cuts the vector and the source DNA with the same restriction enzyme, the recombinant vector molecule cannot be created','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330327,'biology','The cutting of DNA by restriction endonucleases results in','Fragments of DNA','The cutting of DNA by restriction endonucleases results in the fragments of DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Fragments of DNA','Fragments of Nucleotides','Fragments of Nuceosides','Fragments of nitrogen bases and phosphate sugar backbones','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330328,'biology','Choose the correct options: \n\nA. Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on the opposite strands. \n\nB. There are overhanging stretches called sticky ends on each strand. \n\nC. Sticky ends are named so because they form hydrogen bonds with their complementary cut counterparts.\n\nD. This stickiness of the ends facilitates the action of the enzyme DNA ligase.','A,B,C and D','Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on the opposite strands. This leaves single stranded portions at the ends. There are overhanging stretches called sticky ends on each strand. These are named so because they form hydrogen bonds with their complementary cut counterparts. This stickiness of the ends facilitates the action of the enzyme DNA ligase.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','A, B and D','A only','A, C and D','A,B,C and D','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330329,'biology','What group of enzymes are used in genetic engineering to form ‘recombinant’ molecules of DNA by selectively isolating specific desired DNA from an source?','Restriction endonucleases','Restriction endonucleases are used in genetic engineering to form ‘recombinant’ molecules of DNA','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Restriction endonucleases','Restriction exonucleases','Recognition sequence','Plasmid','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330330,'biology','When cut by the same restriction enzyme, the resultant DNA fragments have the same kind of ‘sticky-ends’ and, these can be joined together (end-to-end) using DNA ligases. The given statement is true or false?','TRUE','When cut by the same restriction enzyme, the resultant DNA fragments have the same kind of ‘sticky-ends’ and, these can be joined together (end-to-end) using DNA ligases','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330331,'biology','Assertion:Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA to produce sticky ends. \n\nReason:Stickiness of the ends facilities the action of the enzyme DNA polymerase.','A is true and R is false','Restriction enzyme, a type of endonuclease, functions by "inspecting" the length of a DNA sequence. Once it finds a recognition sequence, it binds and cut each of the two strands of the double helix at specific point leaving single stranded portions at the ends. This results in overhanging stretches called sticky ends. These are named so because they form hydrogen bonds with their complementary counter parts i.e., they can join similar complementary ends of DNA fragment from some other source with the help of DNA ligase. This stickiness of the ends facilitates the action of the enzyme DNA ligase, not DNA polymerase.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330332,'biology','The following sequences reads the same on the two strands in 5\' --> 3\' direction, and if read in the 3\' --> 5\' direction for its complementary strand. Is it true? \n5\'----GAATTC-----3\'','Yes','The following sequences reads the same on the two strands in 5\' à 3\' direction. This is also true if read in the 3\' à 5\' direction. \nBecause it is a palindromic sequence and will read similar in both strands when read from 5\'-3\' or 3\'-5\'\n5\'---GAATTC---3\'\n3\'---CTTAAG---5\'','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Yes','No','Sometimes Yes, Sometimes No','Given information is not correct','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330333,'biology','Name the technique used for separation of DNA fragments specifically.','Gel electrophoresis','The cutting of DNA by restriction endonucleases results in the fragments of DNA. These fragments can be separated by a technique known as gel electrophoresis.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Gel electrophoresis','Paper Chromatography','Polymerase chain reaction','Western Blotting','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330334,'biology','DNA fragments are ______ charged molecules','Negatively','DNA fragments are negatively charged molecules they can be separated by forcing them to move towards the anode under an electric field through a medium/matrix.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','They contain no charge','Positively','Negatively','Neutral','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330335,'biology','Which natural polymer is the most commonly used matrix for the separation of DNA fragments?','Agarose','The natural polymer Agarose is used as a matrix for separation of DNA fragments which is extracted from Sea weeds.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Cellulose','Agarose','Strach','Resin','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330336,'biology','The DNA fragments separate (resolve) according to their which property?','Size','The DNA fragments separate (resolve) according to their size through sieving effect provided by the agarose gel.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Surface area','Size','Shape','Colour','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330337,'biology','The smaller the fragment size, the farther it moves during Gel Electrophoresis','TRUE','The DNA fragments separate (resolve) according to their size through sieving effect provided by the agarose gel. Hence, the smaller the fragment size, the farther it moves.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330338,'biology','Agarose is a natural polymer extracted from','Sea weeds','The most commonly used matrix is agarose which is a natural polymer extracted from sea weeds.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Sea urchin','Sea weeds','Sea cucumbers','Sea Corals','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330339,'biology','The DNA fragments separate according to their _____ through ______ provided by the agarose gel.','size, sieving effect','The DNA fragments separate (resolve) according to their size through sieving effect provided by the agarose gel. Hence, the smaller the fragment size, the farther it moves.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','length, sieving effect','size, sieving effect','weight, waving effect','size, waving effect','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330340,'biology','Which among the following is true according to gel electrophoresis of DNA?   \n\ni)DNA fragments are negatively charged molecules they can be separated by forcing them to move towards the anode under an electric field through a medium/matrix.  \n\nii)DNA fragments are negatively charged molecules they can be separated by forcing them to move towards the cathode under an electric field through a medium/matrix.   \n\niii)Smaller the fragment size, the farther it moves.   \n\niv)Larger the fragment size, the farther it moves.','i and iii','Since DNA fragments are negatively charged molecules they can be separated by forcing them to move towards the anode under an electric field through a medium/matrix  Nowadays the most commonly used matrix is agarose which is a natural polymer extracted from sea weeds. The DNA fragments separate (resolve) according to their size through sieving effect provided by the agarose gel. Hence, the smaller the fragment size, the farther it moves','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','i and iii','i and iv','ii and iii','ii and iv','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330341,'biology','The separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the DNA with a compound under UV light exposure, what is that compound?','Ethidium Bromide','The separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the DNA with a compound known as ethidium bromide followed by exposure to UV radiation','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Chilled ethanol','Ethidium Bromide','DNA polymerase','H\\ugol\'s Iodine','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330342,'biology','The pure DNA fragments cannot be seen in the visible light and without staining','TRUE','The separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the DNA with a compound known as ethidium bromide followed by exposure to UV radiation (you cannot see pure DNA fragments in the visible light and without staining','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330343,'biology','Since DNA has a _______ charge, it moves towards the ______ electrode of the electrophoretic chamber','Negative, Positive','Since DNA has a negative charge, it moves towards the positive electrode of the electrophoretic chamber.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Positive, Negative','Positive, Positive','Negative, Positive','Neutral, Neutral','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330344,'biology','What color do the bands of DNA in a ethidium bromide stained gel exposed to UV light will have?','Bright orange','The separated DNA fragments during gel electrophoresis can be\nvisualised only after staining the DNA\nwith a compound known as ethidium\nbromide followed by exposure to UV\nradiation . You can see bright\norange coloured bands of DNA in a\nethidium bromide stained gel\nexposed to UV light','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Light pink','Bright red','Bright orange','Dark Grey','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330345,'biology','The separated bands of DNA are cut out from the agarose gel and extracted from the gel piece. This step is known as','Elution','The separated bands of DNA are cut out from the agarose gel and extracted from the gel piece. This step is known as elution. The DNA fragments purified in this way are used in constructing recombinant DNA by joining them with cloning vectors','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Elution','Excavation','Extermination','Dilution','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330346,'biology','Choose the correct pair of statement(s).\n\nA. Chilled Water is used in the spooling process for DNA extraction\n\nB. The separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the RNA with a compound known as ethidium chloride followed by exposure to UV radiation\n \nC. You cannot see bright orange coloured bands of DNA in an ethidium chloride stained gel when exposed to UV light.\n\nD. The separated bands of DNA are cut out from the agarose gel and extracted from the gel piece. This step is known as elution. \n\nE. The DNA fragments purified in this way are used in constructing recombinant DNA by joining them with cloning vectors.','D and E','Chilled Ethanol is used in the spooling process and extraction of DNA, before electrophoresis.The separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the DNA with a compound known as ethidium bromide followed by exposure to UV radiation (you cannot see pure DNA fragments in the visible light and without staining). You can see bright orange coloured bands of DNA in a ethidium bromide stained gel exposed to UV light. The separated bands of DNA are cut out from the agarose gel and extracted from the gel piece. This step is known as elution. The DNA fragments purified in this way are used in constructing recombinant DNA by joining them with cloning vectors.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','A,B,C ,D and E','A,B and  C','A,C and E','D and E','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330347,'biology','The given diagram represents steps in (3303)','Recombinant DNA technology','Figure 11.2 pageno-197 NCERT 12th class','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Gene therapy','Recombinant DNA technology','Polymerase chain reaction','Gel Electrophoresis','NEW','12-197','12-167','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/330347q.png'),
(330348,'biology','The given diagram represents which process in DNA isolation (3304)','Gel electrophoresis','Figure 11.3 pageno-198 NCERT 12th class','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Gel electrophoresis','Paper Chromatography','Liquid base chromatography','Polymerase chain reaction','NEW','12-198','12-168','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/330348q.png'),
(330349,'biology','In the given diagram , \'a\' represents the size of (3305)','DNA bands','Figure 11.3 pageno-198 NCERT 12th class','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','RNA bands','DNA bands','Ligase enzyme','Sticky ends','NEW','12-198','12-168','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/330349q.png'),
(330350,'biology','Which of the following statements are true','Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA whereas,\nendonucleases make cuts at specific positions within the DNA.','Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA whereas,\nendonucleases make cuts at specific positions within the DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Endonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA whereas, \nexonucleases make cuts at specific positions within the DNA.','Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA whereas,\nendonucleases make cuts at specific positions within the DNA.','Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the histones whereas,\nendonucleases make cuts at specific positions within the octamer.','Endonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the histone whereas,\nexonucleases make cuts at specific positions within the octamer.','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330351,'biology','Restriction endonuclease cuts each of the two strands of the double\nhelix DNA at specific points in their','Sugar -phosphate backbone','Each restriction endonuclease functions by ‘inspecting’ the length of\na DNA sequence. Once it finds its specific recognition sequence, it\nwill bind to the DNA and cut each of the two strands of the double\nhelix at specific points in their sugar -phosphate backbones','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Phosphate-base backbone','Sugar -phosphate backbone','Sugar-base backbone','Base-Phosphate backbone','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330352,'biology','In the DNA sequence, GAATTC, EcoRI cuts at the point between','G and A','Restriction enzyme EcoRI cuts the DNA between bases G and A only when the sequence in DNA is GAATTC. (NCERT Biology, Class 12, Chapter 11, Page 197, Image 11.1)','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','G and A','A and A','G and C','A and T','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330353,'biology','Which of the following represents the directipons used while describing structure of  DNA helices? \n\ni)3\' --> 5\'   \n\nii)5\' --> 3\'  \n\niii)3\' --> 7\'  \n\niv)7\' --> 3\'','i and ii','The two strands of DNA helices are represented as 3\' --> 5\'  and 5\' --> 3\', where one strand runs antiparallely to the others','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','i and ii','iii and iv','i and iv','ii and iii','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330354,'biology','Gel electrophoresis is an technique used for','Separation of DNA fragments','The cutting of DNA by\nrestriction endonucleases results in the fragments of DNA. These fragments\ncan be separated by a technique known as gel electrophoresis.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Separation of DNA fragments','Insertion of DNA fragments into a plasmid','Incubating the DNA fragments','Purifying RNA components','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330355,'biology','Assertion: In gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments are separated by forcing them to move towards cathode under the influence of an electric field.  \n\nReason: Since DNA fragments are negatively charged molecules they can be separated by forcing them to move towards the cathode under an electric field through a medium/matrix.','Both A and R are false','DNA is a negatively charged molecule. Therefore on application of an electric field it moves towards positively charged anode. Hence, both assertion and reason are not true.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','Both A and R are false','A is correct but R is false','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330356,'biology','In gel electrophoresis, Agarose is used as a matrix owing to its______','Sieving effect','The DNA fragments\nseparate (resolve) according to their size through sieving effect provided\nby the agarose gel.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Sieving effect','Positively charge nature','Sticky effect','floculation and high gold number','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330357,'biology','In gel electrophoresis, the electric field is used when?','Electric charge is applied to create a field through the matrix to facilitate movement of DNA fragments','DNA fragments are negatively charged molecules they can be separated\nby forcing them to move towards the anode under an electric field through\na medium/matrix.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Electric charge is applied to create a field through the matrix to facilitate movement of DNA fragments','To Charge the wells before adding DNA','To prepare Agar matrix by Agar electrolysis','To make the positively charged DNA negative','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330358,'biology','(A)Assertion: In gel electrophoresis, the smaller the DNA fragment size, the farther it moves.    \n         \n(R)Reason:Smaller DNA fragments pass faster through the seive pores when electric field is applied, as they can move easily and a strongly attracted towards the anode.','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for A','The DNA fragments\nseparate (resolve) according to their size through sieving effect provided\nby the agarose gel. Hence, the smaller the fragment size, the farther it\nmoves.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for A','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the A','Both A and R are false','A is correct but R is false','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330359,'biology','Why is elution step done after gel electrophoresis?','To Purify and isolate the desired DNA fragment and process it for rDNA technology','Elution is done so that the DNA fragments\npurified in this way are used in\nconstructing recombinant DNA by\njoining them with cloning vectors.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','To construct a vector for the rDNA','To repurpose the Agar and Ethidium bromide gel','To avoid mutations while handling Ethidium Bromide','To Purify and isolate the desired DNA fragment and process it for rDNA technology','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330360,'biology','Which of the following has a tendency to replicate on its own in the host, independent of the host\'s chromosomal DNA?\n\n i) Plasmids \n\n ii)Bacteriophages  \n\niii)Mitochondrial DNA  \n \niv)Ti plasmid','i, ii, iii and iv','Plasmids and bacteriophages have the ability to replicate within bacterial cells independent of the control of chromosomal DNA. Mitochondria of eukaryotes have their own genome and own machinery for directing their self replication','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','i, ii and iv','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330361,'biology','More\nthan_____ restriction enzymes that have been isolated from over _____ strains\nof bacteria','900,  230','Besides Hind II, today we know more\nthan 900 restriction enzymes that have been isolated from over 230 strains\nof bacteria each of which recognise different recognition sequences.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','900,  230','230, 900','930, 200','200, 930','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(330362,'biology','Which of the following is correct for Hind-II? \n1. functioning\ndepended on a specific DNA nucleotide sequence \n2. Discoverd in 1968 \n3. The first restriction endonuclease discovered\n4. Discovered in 1963.\n5. Functioning Discovered in 1968.','5,4,3,1','The first restriction endonuclease–Hind II, whose functioning\ndepended on a specific DNA nucleotide sequence was isolated and\ncharacterised five years later','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','5,4,3,1','5,4,3,2,1','2,4,5','1,2,4','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330363,'biology','EcoRI comes from-','Escherichia coli RY 13','EcoRI comes from\nEscherichia coli RY 13','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Escherichia coli RY 13','Escherichia coli RY 16','Escherichia coli RY 14','Escherichia coli RY 12','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330364,'biology','While naming a Restriction Enzymes,  the first letter of the name\ncomes from the _____ and the second two letters come from the ______','Genus , Species','The convention for naming these enzymes is the first letter of the name\ncomes from the genus and the second two letters come from the species of\nthe prokaryotic cell from which they were isolated, e.g., EcoRI comes from\nEscherichia coli RY 13.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Plasmid, Vector','plasmid, species','Genus , Species','Species,  Genus','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330365,'biology','rDNA molecules are composed of DNA from\ndifferent sources/genomes.','TRUE','Restriction endonucleases are used in genetic engineering to form\n‘recombinant’ molecules of DNA, which are composed of DNA from\ndifferent sources/genomes.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Restriction enzymes','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330401,'biology','Choose whether the given statement is true or false? \nThe plasmids and bacteriophages have the ability to replicate within bacterial cells independent of the control of chromosomal DNA.','TRUE','The plasmids and bacteriophages have the ability to replicate within bacterial cells independent of the control of chromosomal DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330402,'biology','Which of the folowing has a high copy number per cells, because of high copy numbers of their genome in a bacterial cell','Bacteriophages','Bacteriophages because of their high number per cell, have very high copy numbers of their genome within the bacterial cells.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Bacteriophages','Mitochondrial DNA','Plasmid DNA','Artificial chromosomes','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330403,'biology','How many copies of plasmid may be present per cell?','15-100','Plasmids may have only one or two copies per cell whereas others may have 15-100 copies per cell.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','15-100','Two','one','all of the above','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330404,'biology','Vectors used in genetic engineering, are engineered in such a way that they have following function(s)? \n\ni) Easy linking of foreign DNA to the host DNA\n\nii)Easy selection of recombinants from non-recombinants.   \n\niii)Transfer of cytoplasm between two cells\n\niv) Easy linking of foriegn RNA','i and ii','Vectors used at present, are engineered in such a way that they help easy linking of foreign DNA and selection of recombinants from non-recombinants.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','i','i and ii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(330405,'biology','The sequence \'ori\' in a given stretch of DNA initates the process of ?','Replication','This is a sequence from where replication starts and any piece of DNA when linked to this sequence can be made to replicate within the host cells. This sequence is also responsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA. So, if one wants to recover many copies of the target DNA it should be cloned in a vector whose origin support high copy number','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Replication','Transcription','Mutation','Translation','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330406,'biology','Which of the following is used to differentiate transformants from non-transformants in a medium, during the cloning process','Selectable marker','Selectable marker, which helps in identifying and eliminating non transformants and selectively permitting the growth of the transformants.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Antidote resistance','Selectable marker','Competent host','Both A and B','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330407,'biology','A procedure through which foreign DNA is inserted into the host genome?','Transformation','Transformation is a procedure through which a piece of DNA is introduced in a host bacterium','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Transformation','Elution','Spooling','Genetic engineering','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330408,'biology','Which of the following antibiotics is a compatible one for being used as a selective marker for E.Coli?','Chloramphenicol','The genes encoding resistance to antibiotics such as ampicillin, chloramphenicol, tetracycline or kanamycin, etc., are considered useful selectable markers for E. coli.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Vancomycin','Chloramphenicol','Cephalexin','Amphotericin B','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330409,'biology','The genes encoding resistance to antibiotics such as ampicillin, chloramphenicol, tetracycline or kanamycin, etc., are considered useful as __________ for E. coli.\nThe normal E. coli cells do not carry resistance against any of these antibiotics','Selectable markers','Normally, the genes encoding resistance to antibiotics such as ampicillin, chloramphenicol, tetracycline or kanamycin, etc., are considered useful selectable markers for E. coli. The normal E. coli cells do not carry resistance against any of these antibiotics','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Antibiotics','Selectable markers','Recognition site','Transformation zones','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330410,'biology','What is  the requirement for a suitable vector inorder to link the alien DNA for the commonly used restriction enzymes?','Recognition sites','In order to link the alien DNA, the vector needs to have very few, preferably single, recognition sites for the commonly used restriction enzymes.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Recognition sites','Origin of Replication','Transformation zone','Promoter region','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330411,'biology','Presence of more than one recognition sites within the vector will generate several fragments, which will complicate the gene cloning. Given statement is true or false?','TRUE','Presence of more than one recognition sites within the vector will generate several fragments, which will complicate the gene cloning','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330412,'biology','The ligation of alien DNA is carried out at a restriction site present in one of the _____ antibiotic resistance genes.','2','The ligation of alien DNA is carried out at a restriction site present in one of the two antibiotic resistance genes.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','2','3','1','4','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330413,'biology','A foreign DNA is ligated at the BamH I site of tetracycline resistance gene in the vector pBR322. Which of the following statement is true? \n\ni)Recombinant plasmids will lose tetracycline resistance due to insertion of foreign DNA \n\nii)The recombinants will grow in a ampicillin containing medium but not on that containing tetracycline.\n\niii)Non-recombinants will not grow in the medium containing both the antibiotics.\n\niv) Non- recombinants will grow on the medium containing both the antibiotics','i, ii and iv','For example, you can ligate a foreign DNA at the BamH I site of tetracycline resistance gene in the vector pBR322. The recombinant plasmids will lose tetracycline resistance due to insertion of foreign DNA but can still be selected out from non-recombinant ones by plating the transformants on tetracycline containing medium. The transformants growing on ampicillin containing medium are then transferred on a medium containing tetracycline. The recombinants will grow in ampicillin containing medium but not on that containing tetracycline. But, non- recombinants will grow on the medium containing both the antibiotics. In this case, one antibiotic resistance gene helps in selecting the transformants, whereas the other antibiotic resistance gene gets ‘inactivated due to insertion’ of alien DNA, and helps in selection of recombinants','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','i, ii','i, iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii and iv','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330414,'biology','Assertion: Selection of recombinants due to inactivation of antibiotic resistance is a cumbersome procedure. \n\nReason: It requires simultaneous plating on two plates having different antibiotics.','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','Selection of recombinants due to inactivation of antibiotics is a cumbersome procedure because it requires simultaneous plating on two plates having different antibiotics. Therefore, alternative selectable markers have been developed which differentiate recombinants from non-recombinants on the basis of their ability to produce colour in the presence of a chromogenic substrate. In this, a recombinant DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of an enzyme, β-galactosidase. This results into inactivation of the gene for synthesis of this enzyme, which is referred to as insertional inactivation.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330415,'biology','In the process of insertional inactivation is','A recombinant DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of enzyme β-galactosidase, resulting in inactivation of the enzyme','Alternative selectable markers have been developed which differentiate recombinants from the non-recombinants on the basis of their ability to produce colour in the presence of a chromogenic substrate. In this, a recombinant DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of an enzyme, galactosidase. This results into inactivation of the enzyme, which is referred to as insertional inactivation. The presence of a chromogenic substrate gives blue coloured colonies if the plasmid in the bacteria does not have an insert. Presence of insert results into insertional inactivation of the -galactosidase gene and the colonies do not produce any colour, these are identified as recombinant colonies.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','A recombinant DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of enzyme β-galactosidase, resulting in inactivation of the enzyme','A recombinant DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid','A recombinant DNA is inserted within the recognition site for EcoRI','A recombinant DNA is inserted within the coding sequence of enzyme β-galactosidase, resulting in activation of the enzyme','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(330416,'biology','Insertion of rDNA results in the insertional inactivation of the β-galactosidase gene in the colonies. Which of the following is true for these colonies during identification ?','The recombinant colonies do not produce any colour','Presence of insert results into insertional inactivation of the\nβ-galactosidase gene and the colonies do not produce any colour,\nthese are identified as recombinant colonies.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','The recombinant colonies do not produce any colour','The recombinant colonies show darker colour','The non-recombinant colonies do not produce any colour','The non-recombinant colonies show darker colour','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(330417,'biology','Assertion: In insertional inactivation, blue colour produced by bacterial colonies indicates that the plasmid does not have an insert into the bacterial genome. \n\nReason :Presence of insert is at the site of enzyme beta-galactosidase resulting in insertional inactivation of beta-galactosidase enzyme and the colonies do not produce any colour.','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','The presence of a chromogenic substrate gives blue coloured colonies if the plasmid in the bacteria does not have an insert. Presence of insert results in the insertional inactivation of the β-galactosidase gene and the colonies do not produce any colour, these are identified as recombinant colonies.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is false and R is true','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(330418,'biology','Which of the following microbes transform normal plant and animal cells to cancerous cells(respectively)?','Agrobacterium tumefaciens and Retroviruses','Agrobacterium tumefaciens, a pathogen of several dicot plants is able to deliver a piece of DNA known as \'T-DNA\' to transform normal plant cells into tumour and direct these cells to produce the chemicals required by pathogen. Similarly , retroviruses in animal cell have the ability to produce cancerous cells from normal cells.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Retroviruses and Rhizobium','Escherichia coli and Agrobacterium tumefaciens','Agrobacterium tumefaciens and Retroviruses','Agrobacterium tumefaciens and Arhizogenes','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(330419,'biology','The organism, which is used for gene transfer in higher plants is','Agrobacterium tumefaciens','Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a pathogen of several dicot plants. It is used for the gene transfer in higher organisms. It is able to deliver a piece of DNA known as \'T-DNA\' to transform normal plant cells into a tumor and direct these tumor cells to produce the chemicals required by the pathogen. This bacterium invades plants at the site of wound, transforming them and nearby cells to form a tumor called crown gall.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Agrobacterium tumefaciens','E.coli','Acetobacter aceti','Bacillus thuringiensis','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(330420,'biology','The tumour -inducing ( Ti ) plasmid has now been modified into a cloning vector  and rendered harmless by','Deleting tumor forming genes','The tumor inducing (Ti) plasmid of Agrobacterium tumifaciens has now been modified into a cloning vector which is no more pathogenic to the plants but is still able to use the mechanisms to deliver genes of our interest into a variety of plants.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Adding tumour forming genes','Deleting tumor forming genes','Adding genes resistant to endonucleases','Insertional inactivation','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(330421,'biology','The given diagram represents (3309)','E. coli cloning vector pBR322 showing restriction sites','Figure 11. 4 pageno-199 NCERT class 12th','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','S.typhi cloning vector pBR322 showing restriction sites','E. coli cloning vector pBR322 showing restriction sites','A.tumefcaiens cloning vector pUC412 showing restriction sites','A.tumefcaiens cloning vector pBR312 showing restriction sites','NEW','12-199','12-169','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/330421q.png'),
(330422,'biology','The linking of a bacteriophage or a plasmid DNA with an alien DNA, causes ____________','Increase in the number of alien DNA equal to the copy number of the plasmid or the bacteriophage','If we are able to link an alien piece of DNA with bacteriophage or plasmid DNA, we can multiply its numbers equal to the copy number of the plasmid or bacteriophage.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Increase in the number of alien DNA equal to half of the copy number of the plasmid or the bacteriophage','Increase in the number of alien DNA equal to the copy number of the plasmid or the bacteriophage','Increase in the number of alien DNA equal to double of the copy number of the plasmid or the bacteriophage','Increase in the number of alien DNA equal to four times the copy number of the plasmid or the bacteriophage','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(330423,'biology','The possible features required to facilitate cloning into a vector are    \n\ni)ORI   \n\nii)Selectable marker  \n\niii)Cloning sites  \n\niv)Chilled Ethanol','i, ii and iii','Origin of replication, selectable markers and cloning sites are required to faciltilate cloning into a vector','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','i and ii','i and iii','i, ii and iii','i, ii, iii and iv','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330424,'biology','Which sequence is responsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA during cloning.','Origin of Replication','ORI is a sequence from where\nreplication starts and any piece of DNA when linked to this sequence\ncan be made to replicate within the host cells. This sequence is also\nresponsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','GAATTC','TATA sequence','Palindromic sequence','Origin of Replication','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330425,'biology','Ligation of alien DNA during cloning is done at which site?','Restriction site of antibiotic resistance gene','The ligation of alien DNA is carried out at a restriction site present in one of the two antibiotic resistance genes. For example, you can ligate a foreign DNA at the BamH I site of tetracycline resistance gene in the vector pBR322.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Origin of replication','Restriction site of selective marker gene','Restriction site of antibiotic resistance gene','Ligation at any site would produce the same result','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330426,'biology','Selectable markers which differentiate the recombinant from non-recombinant strain during cloning uses which of the following property ?','Production of colour in presence of chromogeinc substrate','Alternative selectable markers have been developed which differentiate\nrecombinants from non-recombinants on the basis of their ability\nto produce colour in the presence of a chromogenic substrate.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Production of colour in presence of chromogeinc substrate','Production of colour in presence of non- chromogeinc substrate','Production of odor in presence of chromogeinc substrate','Production of odor in presence of non-chromogeinc substrate','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330427,'biology','Apart from prokaryotes, which of the following contain plasmids and can be used in rDNA technology','Yeast','Yeast is the eukaryotic organisms that  has plasmids and can be used as host in gene cloning experiments','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cloning Vectors','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Yeast','Bacteria','Humans','Dolphins','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(330501,'biology','In rDNA technology the term \'competent\' refers to','Increase the efficiency with which DNA enters the bacterium through pores in its cell wall','The term \'competent\' refers to increase the efficiency with which DNA enters the bacterium through pores in its cell wall','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Increase in the competition between the cells','Making cells impermeable to DNA','Increase the efficiency with which DNA enters the bacterium through pores in its cell wall','Making cells permeable for divalent cations','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(330502,'biology','DNA is a ______ molecule','hydrophilic','The base of the DNA molecule is made up of sugar phosphate backbone which is a major structural component of DNA. This backbone is constructed from alternating ribose sugar and phosphate molecule which are highly polar. Because of the backbone being polar the DNA molecule is hydrophilic.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','hydrophilic','hydrophobic','lipophilic','lipophobic','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(110908,'biology','Which among the following is incorrect according to the microfibrils of guard cells?','Orientation of the microfibrils in the cell walls of the guard cells has no role in the transpiration process','When turgidity increases within the two guard cells flanking each stomatal aperture or pore, the thin outer walls bulge out and force the inner walls into a crescent shape. The opening of the stoma is also aided due to the orientation of the microfibrils in the cell walls of the guard cells. Cellulose microfibrils are oriented radially rather than longitudinally making it easier for the stoma to open\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 187','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','The opening of the stoma is also aided due to the orientation of the microfibrils in the cell walls of the guard cells','Cellulose microfibrils are oriented radially rather than longitudinally making it easier for the stoma to open','Orientation of the microfibrils in the cell walls of the guard cells has no role in the transpiration process','Cellulose microfibrils help stomata to open easier ,thus playing a role in transpiration process.','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(330503,'biology','The correct sequence of making a cell competent is','Treatment with divalent cations, incubation of cells with recombinant DNA on ice, heat shock (42°C) placing on ice','In order to force bacteria to take up the plasmid, the bacterial cells must first be made ‘competent’ to take up DNA. This is done by treating them with a specific concentration of a divalent cation, such as calcium, which increases the efficiency with which DNA enters the bacterium through pores in its cell wall. Recombinant DNA can then be forced into such cells by incubating the cells with recombinant DNA on ice, followed by placing them briefly at 420C (heat shock), and then putting them back on ice. This enables the bacteria to take up the recombinant DNA','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Treatment with divalent cations, incubation of cells with recombinant DNA on ice, heat shock (42°C) placing on ice','Heat shock (42°C), incubation of cells with recombinant DNA on ice treatment with divalent cations placing on ice','Treatment with divalent cations, placing on ice incubation of cells with recombinant DNA on ice heat shock (42°C)','Incubation of cells with recombinant DNA on ice, heat shock (42°C) treatment with divalent cations placing on ice','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330504,'biology','---------------method involves the direct injection of recombinant DNA into the nucleus of an animal cell','Micro Injection','In a micro-injection, recombinant DNA is directly injected into the nucleus of an animal cell. In another method, suitable for plants, cells are bombarded with high-velocity micro-particles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA. This method is known as biolistics or gene gun.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Micro Injection','Transformation','Spooling','Biolistics','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330505,'biology','Recombinant DNA can then be forced into cells by incubating the cells with recombinant DNA on______, followed by placing them briefly at ____ (heat shock), and then putting them back on ice','ice,42°C','Recombinant DNA can then be forced into such cells by incubating the cells with recombinant DNA on ice, followed by placing them briefly at 420C (heat shock), and then putting them back on ice. This enables the bacteria to take up the recombinant DNA','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','ice,42°C','water,45°C','ethanol,47°C','gel,42°C','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330506,'biology','Cells are bombarded with high velocity micro-particles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA in a method known as','Biolistics','Cells are bombarded with high velocity micro-particles of gold or tungsten\ncoated with DNA in a method known as biolistics or gene gun.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Biolistics','Recombination','Transformation','Spooling','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330507,'biology','Match the following','Biolistics-Plant Cells,Micro injection-Animal cells','In a method known as micro-injection, recombinant DNA is directly injected into the nucleus of an animal cell. In another method, suitable for plants, cells are bombarded with high velocity micro-particles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA in a method known as biolistics or gene gun','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','match','Biolistics,Plant Cells','Micro injection,Animal cells',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330508,'biology','Assertion: In biolistics method, cells are bombarded with high velocity micro-particles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA. \n\nReason :Biolistics are specifically suitable for plant cells,Microinjections are not suitable as the cell wall is rigid.','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','In another method, suitable for plants, cells are bombarded with high velocity micro-particles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA in a method known as biolistics or gene gun. And the last method uses ‘disarmed pathogen’ vectors, which when allowed to infect the cell, transfer the recombinant DNA into the host.\nIf the Assertion and rreason were to be switched, Option 1 would be suitable as the reason supports and reasons out the assertion.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','A and R, both are false','A is true and R is false','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330509,'biology','Which of the following when allowed to infect the cell, transfers the recombinant DNA into the host.','Disarmed pathogen vectors','This is not the only way to introduce alien DNA into host cells. In a method known as micro-injection, recombinant DNA is directly injected into the nucleus of an animal cell. In another method, suitable for plants, cells are bombarded with high velocity micro-particles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA in a method known as biolistics or gene gun. And the last method uses ‘disarmed pathogen’ vectors, which when allowed to infect the cell, transfer the recombinant DNA into the host','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Regular vector','Disarmed pathogen vectors','Armed pathogen','Plasmid vector','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330510,'biology','Assertion: Cells are treated with a divalent cation such as calcium to increase the DNA\'s efficiency to enter into the bacterial cell   \n\nReason: Because DNA is hydrophobic it cannot pass through cell membrane','A is correct but R is false','Since DNA is a hydrophilic molecule, it cannot pass through cell membranes. Why? In order to force bacteria to take up the plasmid, the bacterial cells must first be made ‘competent’ to take up DNA. This is done by treating them with a specific concentration of a divalent cation, such as calcium, which increases the efficiency with which DNA enters the bacterium through pores in its cell wall.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Both A and R are correct and R is correct reason for Assertion','A and R both are correct but R is not the correct reason for the Assertion','Both A and R are false','A is correct but R is false','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330511,'biology','In order to force bacteria to take up the plasmid, the\nbacterial cells must first be made _______ to take up DNA.','Competent','In order to force bacteria to take up the plasmid, the\nbacterial cells must first be made ‘competent’ to take up DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Competent','Incompetent','Cancerous','infect with retrovirus','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330512,'biology','Treatment with Divalent cation increases the efficiency with which cell takes up the DNA.','TRUE','This is\ndone by treating them with a specific concentration of a divalent cation,\nsuch as calcium, which increases the efficiency with which DNA enters the bacterium through pores in its cell wall.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Competent Host','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330602,'biology','_______ is the genetic material of all organisms without exception','Nucleic acid','Nucleic acid is the genetic material of all organisms\nwithout exception.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Isolation of the Genetic Material (DNA)','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Nucleic acid','Amino Acids','RNA','mRNA','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330601,'biology','Which of the following are related to isolation of DNA?  \n\n i)Cut the DNA with restriction enzymes, it needs to be in pure form, free from other macro-molecules  \n\nii)Break the cell open to release DNA along with other macromolecules such as RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and also lipids  \n\niii)Culturing the host cells in a medium at large scale and extraction of the desired product','i and ii','In order to cut the DNA with restriction enzymes, it needs to be in pure form, free from other macro-molecules. Since the DNA is enclosed within the membranes, we have to break the cell open to release DNA along with other macromolecules such as RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and also lipids. The third statement is not in regard to isolation of DNA, but to the following steps after DNA isolation.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Isolation of the Genetic Material (DNA)','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','i','i and ii','i and iii','ii and iii','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330604,'biology','Match the cells with the enzymes they\'re treated with','lysozyme-bacteria,cellulase-plant cells, chitinase-fungus','Cell wall of any cell is initially broken before the isolation of DNA. This can be achieved by treating\nthe bacterial cells/plant or animal tissue with enzymes such as\nlysozyme (bacteria), cellulase (plant cells), chitinase (fungus).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Isolation of the Genetic Material (DNA)','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','match','lysozyme,bacteria','cellulase,plant cells','chitinase,fungus',NULL,'NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330605,'biology','During isolation of DNA from a cell, RNA can be removed by treatment with','ribonuclease','The RNA can be removed by treatment with ribonuclease whereas proteins can be removed by treatment with protease','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Isolation of the Genetic Material (DNA)','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','ribonuclease','protease','chilled ethanol','spooling','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330606,'biology','During isolation of DNA from a cell, proteins can be removed by treatment with','Protease','The RNA can be removed by treatment with ribonuclease whereas proteins can be removed by treatment with protease','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Isolation of the Genetic Material (DNA)','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Ribonuclease','Protease','Chilled ethanol','Spooling','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330607,'biology','In isolation of DNA, purified DNA ultimately precipitates out after the addition of','chilled ethanol','Other molecules can be removed by appropriate treatments and purified DNA ultimately precipitates out after the addition of chilled ethanol. This can be seen as collection of fine threads in the suspension','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Isolation of the Genetic Material (DNA)','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','ribonuclease','protease','chilled ethanol','spooling','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330608,'biology','Purified DNA ultimately precipitates out after the addition of chilled ethanol.How is this visualised in suspension?','fine threads','Other molecules can be removed by appropriate treatments and purified DNA ultimately precipitates out after the addition of chilled ethanol. This can be seen as collection of fine threads in the suspension','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Isolation of the Genetic Material (DNA)','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','chilled ethanol','fine threads','long beads','thick threads','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330609,'biology','The following processes show steps of DNA isolation, which of the following options indicates the correct sequence in a plant cell','Cellulase, Protease, RNAse, Spooling','First cell membrane dissolution using cellulase, protease to dissolve histones, and RNAse to dissolve RNA and in the end isolation by spooling','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Isolation of the Genetic Material (DNA)','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Cellulase, Protease, RNAse, Spooling','Chitinase,RNAse,Protease,Spooling','Lysozyme, Protease, RNAse, Spooling','Spooling, Protease, RNAse and Chitinase','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330610,'biology','Identify the process given diagram (3306)','Spooling','Figure 11.5 pageno-201 NCERT class 12th','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Isolation of the Genetic Material (DNA)','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','PCR','Gel Electrophoresis','Culture Medium','Spooling','NEW','12-201','12-171','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/330610q.png'),
(330611,'biology','Genes are located on long molecules of DNA\ninterwined with proteins called','Histones','Genes are located on long molecules of DNA\ninterwined with proteins such as histones.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Isolation of the Genetic Material (DNA)','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Endonucleases','Ligase','Protease','Histones','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330612,'biology','Recombinant DNA technology involves several steps in a specific sequence. Which among the following isn\'t a step involved?','Using RNA interference to check rate of process','Recombinant DNA technology involves several steps in specific sequence such as isolation of DNA, fragmentation of DNA by restriction endonucleases, isolation of a desired DNA fragment, ligation of the DNA fragment into a vector, transferring the recombinant DNA into the host, culturing the host cells in a medium at large scale and extraction of the desired product.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Isolation of the Genetic Material (DNA)','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Isolation of DNA and fragmentation of DNA by restriction endonucleases','Isolation of a desired DNA fragment and ligation of the DNA fragment into a vector','Transferring the recombinant DNA into the host and culturing the host cells in a medium at large scale and extraction of the desired product.','Using RNA interference to check rate of process','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(330701,'biology','Restriction enzyme digestions are performed by ______ purified DNA molecules with the enzyme','Incubating','Restriction enzyme digestions are performed by incubating purified DNA molecules with the restriction enzyme','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cutting of DNA at Specific Locations','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Incubating','Mixing','Grinding','Spooling','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(330702,'biology','Restriction enzyme digestions are performed by incubating purified DNA molecules with the restriction enzyme, at the optimal conditions for that specific enzyme.','TRUE','Restriction enzyme digestions are performed by incubating purified DNA\nmolecules with the restriction enzyme, at the optimal conditions for that\nspecific enzyme.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cutting of DNA at Specific Locations','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(330703,'biology','Which of the following is employed to check the progression of a restriction enzyme digestion?','Agarose gel electrophoresis','Agarose gel electrophoresis is employed to check the progression of a restriction enzyme digestion','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cutting of DNA at Specific Locations','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Spooling','Agarose gel electrophoresis','Grinding','PCR','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(330704,'biology','Agarose gel electrophoresis is employed to check the progression of','Restriction enzyme digestion','Agarose gel electrophoresis is employed to check the progression of a restriction enzyme digestion','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cutting of DNA at Specific Locations','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','RNA polymerase incubation','Restriction enzyme digestion','Protease digestion','Isomerase digestion','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(330705,'biology','In Agarose gel electrophoresis,DNA is a negatively charged molecule, hence it moves towards','Positive Electrode Anode','Agarose gel electrophoresis is employed to check the progression of a restriction enzyme digestion. DNA is a negatively charged molecule, hence it moves towards the positive electrode (anode)','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cutting of DNA at Specific Locations','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Positive Electrode Anode','Positive Electrode Cathode','Negative Electrode Anode','Negative Electrode Cathode','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(330706,'biology','Which enzyme is added after the gene of interest from source DNA and vector are mixed ?','Ligase','The joining of DNA involves several processes. After having cut the source DNA as well as the vector DNA with a specific restriction enzyme, the cut out ‘gene of interest’ from the source DNA and the cut vector with space are mixed and ligase is added. This results in the preparation of recombinant DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cutting of DNA at Specific Locations','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Ligase','Restriction Exonuclease','Restriction Endonuclease','Helicase','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(330707,'biology','The cut out ‘gene of interest’ from the source DNA and the cut vector with space are mixed and ligase is added. This results in the preparation of?','Recombinant DNA','The joining of DNA involves several processes. After having cut the source DNA as well as the vector DNA with a specific restriction enzyme, the cut out ‘gene of interest’ from the source DNA and the cut vector with space are mixed and ligase is added. This results in the preparation of recombinant DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cutting of DNA at Specific Locations','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Vector DNA','Recombinant DNA','Host DNA','Recombinant RNA','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(330708,'biology','In making rDNA molecule, both the vector and the DNA has to be cut by the same restriction endonuclease. State true or false.','TRUE','Restriction enzyme digestions are performed by incubating purified DNA\nmolecules with the restriction enzyme, at the optimal conditions for that\nspecific enzyme. Agarose gel electrophoresis is employed to check the\nprogression of a restriction enzyme digestion. DNA is a negatively charged\nmolecule, hence it moves towards the positive electrode (anode)\n(Figure 11.3). The process is repeated with the vector DNA also.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Cutting of DNA at Specific Locations','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(330801,'biology','Process used to generate multiple copies of the gene (or DNA) of interest,in vitro.','Polymerase Chain Reaction','PCR stands for Polymerase Chain Reaction. In this reaction, multiple copies of the gene (or DNA) of interest is synthesised in vitro','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Biotechnology : Amplification of Gene of Interest using PCR','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Polymerase Chain Reaction','Gel Electrophoresis','Centrifugation','Western Blotting','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(330802,'biology','Polymerase Chain Reaction comprise','Two sets of primers','PCR stands for Polymerase Chain Reaction. In this reaction, multiple copies of the gene (or DNA) of interest is synthesised in vitro using two sets of primers (small chemically synthesised oligonucleotides that arecomplementary to the regions of DNA) and the enzyme DNA polymerase','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Biotechnology : Amplification of Gene of Interest using PCR','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','One set of primer','Two sets of primers','DNA Ligase enzyme','Protease','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(330803,'biology','Small chemically synthesised oligonucleotides that are complementary to the regions of DNA, used in PCR','Primers','Primers (small chemically synthesised oligonucleotides that are complementary to the regions of DNA)','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Biotechnology : Amplification of Gene of Interest using PCR','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Primers','snRNA','mRNA','Recombinant DNA','NEW','12-203','12-173',NULL),
