(331106,'biology','The formulations has to undergo\nthorough clinical trials as in case of drugs in the Downstream processing. State True of false.','TRUE','Such formulation has to undergo\nthorough clinical trials as in case of drugs.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Downstream Processing','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'NEW','12-205','12-175',NULL),
(331107,'biology','Strict quality control testing\nfor each product is also required in','Downstream processing','Strict quality control testing\nfor each product is also required','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Downstream Processing','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Downstream processing','Upstream processing','Separation','Purification','NEW','12-205','12-175',NULL),
(331201,'biology','The DNA molecule to which the gene of interest is integrated for cloning is called','Vector.','Vector is a DNA molecule that carries a foreign DNA segment and replicates inside a host cell. The vector DNA and foreign DNA carrying gene of interest are cut by the same restriction endonuclease enzyme to produce complementary sticky ends. With the help of DNA ligase enzyme, the complementary sticky ends of the two dnas are joined to produce a recombinant DNA (rDNA), which is then introduced into the host cell.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Template','Carrier','Transformer','Vector.','NEW','12-198','12-178',NULL),
(331202,'biology','The cutting of DNA at specific locations became possible with the discovery of [NEET 2015]','Restriction enzymes','Restriction enzymes recognise specific base sequences in a DNA molecule and cut its strands, e.g., EcoR I cuts DNA strands in the base sequence GAATTC.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Selectable markers','Ligases','Restriction enzymes','Probes','NEW','12-195','12-175',NULL),
(331203,'biology','Identify the wrong statement with regard to restriction enzymes.\n[NEET 2020]','Sticky ends can be joined by using DNA ligases.','The cuts made by restriction endonucleases can be only sealed DNA ligases bit however, they have asked a question with regard to restriction endonucleases, so it is not in regard to endonucleases.\nRestriction enzymes are part of a larger category of enzymes known as nucleases. Exonucleases and endonucleases are the two types.\nEndonucleases make cuts at specified places within the DNA, whereas exonucleases remove nucleotides off the ends.\nThe length of a DNA sequence is \'inspected\' by each restriction endonuclease. It will connect to the DNA and cut each of the two strands of the double helix at precise spots in their sugar phosphate backbones after it has found its specific recognition sequence.\nEach restriction endonuclease recognises nucleotide sequences in DNA that are palindromic.\nIn genetic engineering, restriction endonucleases are employed to create\'recombinant\' DNA molecules, which are made up of DNA from different sources/genomes.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Each restriction enzyme functions by inspecting the length of a DNA sequence.','They cut the strand of DNA at palindromic sites.','They are useful in genetic engineering.','Sticky ends can be joined by using DNA ligases.','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331204,'biology','Choose the correct pair from the following.\n [NEET 2020]','Ligases - Join the two DNA molecules','Polymerases are enzymes which extend the primers using the nucleotides provided in the\nreaction and the genomic DNA as template. Nucleases catalyze the cleavage of phosphodiester bonds. Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA and ligases joins two DNA strands. So Ligases - Join the two DNA molecules\n is the correct option','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Ligases - Join the two DNA molecules','Polymerases - Break the DNA into fragments','Nucleases - Separate the two strands of DNA','Exonucleases - Make cuts at specific positions within DNA','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331205,'biology','The specific palindromic sequence which is recognised by EcoRI is\n [NEET 2020]','5′ - GAATTC - 3′, 3′ - CTTAAG - 5′','The palindromes in DNA are base pair sequences that are same when read forward (left to right) or backward (right to left) from a central axis of symmetry. Thus, GAATTC/CTTAAG is a palindromic sequence which is recognised by ecori.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','5′ - GAATTC - 3′, 3′ - CTTAAG - 5′','5′ - GGAACC - 3′, 3′ - CCTTGG - 5′','5′ - CTTAAG - 3′, 3′ - GAATTC - 5′','5′ - GGATCC - 3′, 3′ - CCTAGG - 5′','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(110910,'biology','Match the following plants with they leaves type','Dicot plants-Dorsiventral,Monocot plants-Isobilateral','Dicot plants-Dorsiventral,Monocot plants-Isobilateral\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 187','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','match','Dicot plants,Dorsiventral','Monocot plants,Isobilateral',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331206,'biology','The sequence that controls the copy number of the linked DNA in the vector, is termed\n[NEET 2020]','Ori site','Ori site is a sequence from where replication starts and any piece of DNA when linked to this sequence can be made to replicate within the host cells. This sequence is also responsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA in the vector. So, if one wants to recover many copies of the target DNA it should be cloned in a vector whose origin support high copy number.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Selectable marker','Ori site','Palindromic sequence','Recognition site','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331207,'biology','In gel electrophoresis, separated DNA fragments can be visualized with the help of \n[NEET 2020]','Ethidium bromide in UV radiation','In gel electrophoresis , separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the DNA with a compound i.e., ethidium bromide and followed by exposure to UV radiation as bright orange coloured bands.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Acetocarmine in bright blue light','Ethidium bromide in UV radiation','Acetocarmine in UV radiation','Ethidium bromide in infrared radiation.','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331208,'biology','Following statements describe the characteristics of the enzyme restriction endonuclease. Identify the incorrect statement.\n[NEET 2019]','The enzyme binds to DNA at specific sites and cuts only one of the two strands.','The restriction endonuclease enzyme inspects the length of a DNA sequence. Once it recognises specific sequence, it binds to the DNA and cuts each of the two strands of the double helix at specific points in their sugar phosphate backbone. Special sequence in the DNA recognised by restriction endonuclease is called palindromic nucleotide sequence.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','The enzyme recognises a specific palindromic nucleotide sequence in the DNA.','The enzyme cuts DNA molecule at identified position within the DNA.','The enzyme binds to DNA at specific sites and cuts only one of the two strands.','The enzyme cuts the sugar-phosphate backbone at specific sites on each strand.','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331209,'biology','A selectable marker is used to \n[Odisha NEET 2019]','Help in eliminating the non-transformants, so that the transformants can be regenerated.','A selectable marker, which helps in identifying and eliminating non-\ntransformants and selectively permitting the growth of the transformants.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Help in eliminating the non-transformants, so that the transformants can be regenerated.','Identify the gene for a desired trait in an alien organism.','Select a suitable vector for transformation in a specific crop.','Mark a gene on a chromosome for isolation using restriction enzyme.','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331210,'biology','Given below are four statements pertaining to separation of DNA fragments using gel electrophoresis. Identify the incorrect statements.\n [Odisha NEET 2019]\n\n(i) DNA is negatively charged molecule and so it is loaded on gel towards the anode terminal. \n\n(ii) DNA Fragments travel along the surface of the gel whose concentration does not affect relative movement of DNA. \n\n(iii)Smaller the size of DNA fragment larger is the distance it travels through. \n\n(iv) Pure DNA can be visualized directly by exposing UV radiation.','(i), (ii) and (iv)','DNA is negatively charged molecule and so it is loaded on gel towards the cathode terminal. Concentration of agar is inversely proportional to the distance travelled by DNA fragments. The separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the DNA with a compound known as ethidium bromide followed by exposure to UV radiation (you cannot see pure DNA fragments in the visible light and without staining).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','(i), (iii) and (iv)','iv only','(ii), (iii) and (iv)','(i), (ii) and (iv)','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331211,'biology','Which of the following is commonly used as a vector for introducing a DNA fragment in human lymphocytes?\n [NEET 2018]','Retrovirus','Retroviruses cause cancer in animals including humans. So modified retroviruses are used to transfer desirable genes into animal cells. It is used in gene therapy, in which lymphocytes from blood of patient are taken and grown in culture medium outside the body, a functional gene is introduced by using a retroviral vector into these lymphocytes which are again reintroduced into the patient body.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Retrovirus','Ti plasmid','Λ phage','PBR322','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(331212,'biology','The DNA fragments separated on an agarose gel can be visualised after staining with \n[NEET 2017]','Ethidium bromide','The separated DNA fragments can be seen only after staining them with a compound known as ethidium bromide (etbr) followed by exposure to UV radiation as bright orange coloured bands.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Acetocarmine','Aniline blue','Ethidium bromide','Bromophenol blue','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331213,'biology','DNA fragments are\n [NEET 2017]','Negatively charged.','DNA molecules are negatively charged. It has phosphate in its backbone making it negatively charged.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Negatively charged.','Neutral.','Either positively or negatively charged depending on their size.','Positively charged.','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331214,'biology','A gene whose expression helps to identify transformed cell is known as \n[NEET 2017]','Selectable marker.','Some genes called “selectable markers” help in selecting those host cells which contain the vectors (transformants) and eliminating the non-transformants.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Vector','Plasmid','Structural gene','Selectable marker.','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331215,'biology','What is the criterion for DNA fragments movement on agarose gel during gel electrophoresis ? \n[NEET 2017]','The smaller the fragment size, the farther it moves.','Electrophoresis is a technique used for the separation of substances of different ionic properties. Since the DNA fragments are negatively charged molecules, they can be separated by allowing them to move towards the anode. DNA fragments move towards the anode according to their molecule size through the pores of agarose gel. Thus, the smaller fragments move farther away as compared to larger fragments.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','The smaller the fragment size, the farther it moves.','Positively charged fragments move to farther end.','Negatively charged fragments do not move.','The larger the fragment size, the farther it moves.','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331216,'biology','A foreign DNA and plasmid cut by the same restriction endonuclease can be joined to form a recombinant plasmid using\n [NEET-2 2016]','Ligase.','Ligase is a class of enzymes that catalyse the formation of covalent bonds using the energy released by the cleavage of ATP. Ligases are important in the synthesis and repair of many biological molecules, including DNA ligase and used in genetic engineering to insert foreign DNA into cloning vectors.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','EcoRI','Taq polymerase','Polymerase III','Ligase.','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331217,'biology','Which of the following restriction enzymes produces blunt ends? [NEET2 2016]','EcoRV','EcoRV is a type II restriction endonuclease isolated from certain strains of E.coli. It creates blunt ends. It recognises the palindromic sequence of 6 bases. SalI, xhoi and hindiii restriction enzymes produce sticky ends.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Sal I','EcoRV','XhoI','HindIII','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(331218,'biology','Which of the following is not a feature of the plasmids? \n[NEET1 2016]','Single-stranded','Plasmids are extra-chromosomal, self-replicating, usually circular, double-stranded DNA molecules that serve as vectors which carry foreign DNA segment and replicate inside host cell.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Transferable','Single-stranded','Independent replication','Circular structure','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331219,'biology','Which of the following is a restriction endonuclease?\n [NEET-1 2016]','Hind II','Hind II is the first restriction endonuclease. It was isolated from Haemophilusinfluenzae Rd. It always cut DNA at specific position producing blunt ends. DNase I is an endonuclease that cleaves DNA preferentially at phosphodiester linkages adjacent to a pyrimidine nucleotide. RNase is a type of nuclease that catalyses the degradation of RNA into smaller components. It can be endoribonuclease or exoribonuclease. A protease is an enzyme that perform proteolysis, i.e., protein catabolism by hydrolysis of the peptide bonds.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','DNase I','RNase','Hind II','Protease','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331220,'biology','The introduction of T-DNA into plants involves \n[NEET 2015]','Infection of the plant by Agrobacterium tumefaciens.','Ti plasmid (tumor inducing) from the soil bacterium Agrobacterium tumefaciens is effectively used as vector for gene transfer to plant cells. The part of Ti plasmid transferred into plant cell DNA, is called the T-DNA. This T-DNA with desired DNA spliced into it, is inserted into the chromosomes of the host plant where it produces copies of itself. Such plant cells are then cultured, induced to multiply and differentiate to form plantlets. By transferring into soil, the plantlets grow into mature plants, carrying the foreign gene, expressed throughout the new plant.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Exposing the plants to cold for a brief period.','Allowing the plant roots to stand in water.','Infection of the plant by Agrobacterium tumefaciens.','Altering the ph of the soil, then heat-shocking the plants.','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL);
INSERT INTO `ncert_book_pyq` (`unique_id`, `subject`, `question`, `answer`, `explanation`, `topic_name`, `chapter_name`, `difficulty`, `quiz_type`, `option_a`, `option_b`, `option_c`, `option_d`, `syllabus_update`, `ncert22_page`, `ncert23_page`, `image_url`) VALUES
(331221,'biology','Which vector can clone only a small fragment of DNA? \n[NEET 2014]','Plasmid','Plasmids have been modified to be used as vectors. They can clone DNA fragments of about 10 kbp size while cosmid can carry upto 45 kbp, YAC can carry upto 1000-2500 kbp and BAC can carry around 300 – 330 Kbp long DNA fragments.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Bacterial artificial chromosome','Yeast artificial chromosome','Plasmid','Cosmid','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331222,'biology','Commonly used vectors for human genome sequencing are \n[NEET 2014]','BAC and YAC','Bacterial artificial chromosome (BAC) vectors are based on natural, extra-chromosomal plasmid of E. Coli. BAC vector contains genes for replication and maintenance of the F-factor, a selectable marker and cloning site. These vectors can accommodate upto 300-350 kb of foreign DNA and are also being used in genome sequencing project. Yeast artificial chromosome (YAC) vectors are used to clone DNA fragments of more than 1mb in size. Therefore, they have been exploited extensively in mapping the large genomes, e.g., in the Human Genome Project. These vectors contain the telomeric sequence, the centromere and the autonomously replicating sequence from yeast chromosomes.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','T - DNA','BAC and YAC','Expression vectors','T/A cloning vectors.','NEW','12-119',NULL,NULL),
(331223,'biology','The colonies of recombinant bacteria appear white in contrast to blue colonies of non-recombinant bacteria because of\n [NEET 2013]','Non-recombinant bacteria containing beta galactosidase.','The presence of restriction sites within the markers tetr and ampr of plasmid permits an easy selection for cells transformed with recombinant plasmid. Insertion of the DNA fragment into the plasmid makes antibiotic resistance genes nonfunctional, for example, insertion of the DNA fragment into the plasmid (pbr322) using pst i or Pvu I makes ampr nonfunctional. Bacterial cells containing such a recombinant pbr322 will be unable to grow in the presence of ampicillin, but will grow on tetracycline. This process, however, becomes burdensome because it requires simultaneous plating on two plates having different antibiotics. Thus, alternative selectable marker is developed to differentiate recombinants and non-recombinants on the basis of their ability to produce colour in the presence of a chromogenic substance. Here, a recombinant DNA is inserted in the coding sequence of an enzyme b-galactosidase. PUC 18 plasmid contains this gene which allows it to produce b-galactosidase which degrades certain sugars and produces a blue pigment when exposed to specific substrate analog. If the plasmid in the bacterium does not have an insert, i.e., is non-recombinant, the presence of chromogenic substrate gives blue coloured colonies. Presence of insert in the plasmid in recombinant bacterium does not produce any colour, such bacterial colonies are marked as recombinant colonies.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Insertional inactivation of alpha galactosidase in recombinant bacteria.','Inactivation of glycosidase enzyme in recombinant bacteria.','Non-recombinant bacteria containing beta galactosidase.','Insertional inactivation of alpha galactosidase in non-recombinant bacteria.','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331224,'biology','DNA fragments generated by the restriction endo-nucleases in a chemical reaction can be separated by \n[NEET 2013]','Electrophoresis','The cutting of DNA by\nrestriction endonucleases results in the fragments of DNA. These fragments\ncan be separated by a technique known as gel electrophoresis.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Electrophoresis','Restriction mapping','Centrifugation','Polymerase chain reaction.','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(331225,'biology','Which of the following is not a part of PCR cycle','Renaturation','PCR is a cycle of three steps: 1. DENATURATION - the strands of the DNA are melted apart by heating to 95°C; 2. ANNEALING - the temperature is reduced to ~ 55°C to allow the primers to anneal tothe target DNA; 3. POLYMERISATION / EXTENSION','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Annealing','Denaturation','Polymerization','Renaturation','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(331226,'biology','Given below is a sample of a portion of DNA strand giving the base sequence on the opposite strands. What is so special shown in it?\n5′ ----GAATTC ----3′ ;\n3′ ----CTTAAG-----5′.','Palindromic sequence of base pairs','Palindromes are groups of letters\nthat form the same words when read both forward and backward so the given nucleotide sequence represent a palindromic sequence.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 1','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Replication completed','Deletion mutation','Start codon at the 5′ end','Palindromic sequence of base pairs','NEW','12-196','12-172',NULL),
(331301,'biology','Match the organism with its use in biotechnology. Use the diagram for Reference   (3312)','a - iv, b - iii, c - i, d - ii','Thermus aquaticus is an organism whic is related to a special type of DNA polymerase which is thermally very stable called as Taq polymerase. So the correct option is Thermus aquaticus,DNA polymerase','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','a - iii, b - ii, c - iv, d - i','a - iii, b - iv, c - i, d - ii','a - ii, b - iv, c - iii, d - i','a - iv, b - iii, c - i, d - ii','NEW','12-203','12-173','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/331301q.png'),
(331302,'biology','DNA precipitation out of a mixture of biomolecules can be achieved by treatment with \n[NEET 2019]','Chilled ethanol','In order to cut the DNA with restriction enzymes, it needs to be in pure form, free from other macromolecules. Since the DNA is enclosed by the membranes, we have to break the cell open to release DNA and other macromolecules like RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and lipids. It is obtained by treating the bacterial cells/plant or animal tissue with enzymes. Other molecules are removed by appropriate treatments and purified DNA ultimately precipitates out after the addition of chilled ethanol.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Chilled chloroform','Isopropanol','Chilled ethanol','Methanol at room temperature.','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(331303,'biology','Which one of the following equipments is essentially required for growing microbes on a large scale, for industrial production of enzymes? \n[NEET 2019]','Bioreactor','Small volume cultures cannot yield appreciable quantities of products.\nTo produce in large quantities, the development of bioreactors, where\nlarge volumes (100-1000 litres) of culture can be processed, was required.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Bioreactor','BOD incubator','Sludge digester','Industrial oven','NEW','12-204','12-174',NULL),
(331304,'biology','The correct order of steps in Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is\n[NEET 2018]','Denaturation, annealing, extension.','A single PCR amplification cycle involves three basic steps, denaturation heating of target DNA to high temperature resulting in separation of two strands, annealing two oligonucleotide primers anneal or hybridise to each single template DNA and extension. Ta q DNA polymerase synthesises DNA between primers and primers extend towards each other such that DNA stranded segment lying between the two is copied.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Extension, denaturation, annealing','Annealing, extension, denaturation','Denaturation, extension, annealing','Denaturation, annealing, extension.','NEW','12-202','12-171',NULL),
(331305,'biology','The process of separation and purification of expressed protein before marketing is called\n[NEET 2017]','Downstream processing','After the formation of the product in the bioreactor it undergoes some processes before a finished product is ready for marketing. The process includes separation and purification of products which are collectively called downstream processing.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Downstream processing','Bioprocessing','Postproduction processing','Upstream processing.','NEW','12-205','12-175',NULL),
(331306,'biology','Stirred-tank bioreactors have been designed for \n[NEET-2 2016]','Availability of O₂ throughout the process','A stirred-tank reactor is usually cylindrical or with a curved base to facilitate the mixing of the reactor contents. The stirrer facilitates, even mixing and O₂ availability throughout the bioreactor.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Purification of product','Addition of preservatives to the product','Availability of O₂ throughout the process','Ensuring anaerobic conditions in the culture vessel.','NEW','12-204','12-174',NULL),
(331307,'biology','Which of the following is not a component of downstream processing? \n[NEET-2 2016]','Expression','After the formation of the product in bioreactor, it undergoes some processes before a finished product to be ready for marketing. Downstream processing includes separation and purification process. The product obtained is subjected to quality control, testing and kept in suitable preservatives.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Separation','Purification','Preservation','Expression','NEW','12-205','12-175',NULL),
(331308,'biology','The Taq polymerase enzyme is obtained from \n[NEET1 2016]','Thermus aquaticus','Taq polymerase, generally used in PCR is isolated from thermophilic bacterium Thermusaquaticus.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Bacillus subtilis','Pseudomonas putida','Thermus aquaticus','Thiobacillus ferroxidans.','NEW','12-203','12-173',NULL),
(331309,'biology','An analysis of chromosomal DNA using the Southern hybridization technique does not use \n[2014]','PCR.','PCR is used only for amplification of DNA. It is not directly involved in Southern hybridisation technique.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Electrophoresis','Blotting','Autoradiography','PCR.','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(110911,'biology','The lower surface of a dorsiventral leaf has a greater number of stomata while in an isobilateral leaf they are about equal on both surfaces','TRUE','Usually the lower surface of a dorsiventral (often dicotyledonous) leaf has a greater number of stomata while in an isobilateral (often monocotyledonous\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 187','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','TRUE','FALSE',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331310,'biology','In vitro clonal propagation in plants is characterized by \n[2014]','PCR and RAPD','Clonal propagation can be characterized by PCR and RAPD. The polymerase chain reaction (PCR) technique, generates microgram (μg) quantities of DNA copies (upto billion copies) of the desired DNA (or RNA) segment, present even as a single copy in the initial preparation, in a matter of few hours. RAPD stands for Random Amplification of Polymorphic DNA. It is a type of PCR, but the segments of DNA that are amplified are random. No knowledge of the DNA sequence for the targeted gene is required, as the primers will bind somewhere in the sequence, but it is not certain exactly where. Its resolving power is much lower than targeted, species specific DNA comparison methods, such as short tandem repeats.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','PCR and RAPD','Northern blotting','Electrophoresis and HPLC','Microscopy.','NEW','12-202','12-171',NULL),
(331311,'biology','Which of the following is not correctly matched for the organism and its cell wall degrading enzyme? \n[NEET 2013]','Algae – Methylase','Cell wall of algae is made up of cellulase, pectin and mucilage. These substances cannot be degraded by methylase. Methylase is a type of transferase enzyme that transfers a methyl group from a donor to an acceptor.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Algae – Methylase','Fungi – Chitinase','Bacteria – Lysozyme','Plant cells – Cellulase','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(331312,'biology','During the process of isolation of DNA, chilled ethanol is added to\n [Karnataka NEET 2013]','Precipitate DNA','Ethanol is much less polar than water. Adding it to the solution disrupts the screening charges exerted by water. The electrical attraction between phosphate and any positive ions (Na+) present in solution becomes strong enough to form a stable ionic bond and DNA precipitates. Ethanol precipitation is a widely used technique to purify, or concentrate nucleic acid.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Precipitate DNA','Break open the cell to release DNA','Facilitate action of restriction enzymes','Remove proteins such as histones.','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(331313,'biology','Identify the wrong statement with regard to restriction enzymes. [NEET 2020]','Sticky ends can be joined by using DNA ligases.','Restriction enzymes are part of a larger category of enzymes known as nucleases. Exonucleases and endonucleases are the two types.\nEndonucleases make cuts at specified places within the DNA, whereas exonucleases remove nucleotides off the ends.\nThe length of a DNA sequence is \'inspected\' by each restriction endonuclease. It will connect to the DNA and cut each of the two strands of the double helix at precise spots in their sugar phosphate backbones after it has found its specific recognition sequence.\nEach restriction endonuclease recognises nucleotide sequences in DNA that are palindromic.\nIn genetic engineering, restriction endonucleases are employed to create\'recombinant\' DNA molecules, which are made up of DNA from different sources/genomes.Since they have asked with respect to restriction enzymes and all statements pertaining are true, Sticky ends have free, dangling, or unpaired nitrogen bases that can link with complementary bases on other DNA segments to form recombinant DNA. In the absence of sticky ends, a ligase is necessary to link two DNA segments together.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','Each restriction enzyme functions by inspecting the length of a DNA sequence','They cut the strand of DNA at palindromic sites','They are useful in genetic engineering','Sticky ends can be joined by using DNA ligases.','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(331314,'biology','Choose the correct pair from the following.[NEET 2020]\n\n(a)Ligases - Join the two DNA molecules\n\n(b)Polymerases - Break the DNA into fragments\n\n(c)Nucleases - Separate the two strands of DNA\n\n(d) Exonucleases - Make cuts at specific positions within DNA','a','Polymerases are enzymes which extend the primers using the nucleotides provided in the\nreaction and the genomic DNA as template. Nucleases catalyze the cleavage of phosphodiester bonds. Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA and ligases joins two DNA strands. So Ligases - Join the two DNA molecules\n is the correct option','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','a','b','c','d','NEW','12-195','12-171',NULL),
(331315,'biology','The specific palindromic sequence which is recognised by EcoRI in the give diagram (3301)','5\' - GAATTC - 3\' \n3\'- CTTAAG - 5\'','Figure 11.1 pageno-196 NCERT 12th class','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','5\' - GAATTC - 3\' \n3\'- CTTAAG - 5\'','5\'- GGAACC - 3\'  \n3\' - CCTTGG - 5\'','5\' - CTTAAG - 3\'   \n3\'- GAATTC - 5\'','5\'- GGATCC - 3\' \n3\' - CCTAGG - 5\'','NEW','12-196','12-166','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/331315q.png'),
(331316,'biology','The sequence that controls the copy number of the linked DNA in the vector, is termed \n[NEET 2020]','Ori site','Ori site is a sequence from where replication starts and any piece of DNA when linked to this sequence can be made to replicate within the host cells. This sequence is also responsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA in the vector. So, if one wants to recover many copies of the target DNA it should be cloned in a vector whose origin support high copy number','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','selectable marker','Ori site','palindromic sequence','recognition site.','NEW','12-199','12-179',NULL),
(331317,'biology','In gel electrophoresis, separated DNA fragments can be visualized with the help of \n[NEET 2020]','ethidium bromide in UV radiation','In gel electrophoresis , separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the DNA with a compound i.e., ethidium bromide and followed by exposure to UV radiation as bright orange coloured bands','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','acetocarmine in bright blue light','ethidium bromide in UV radiation','acetocarmine in UV radiation','ethidium bromide in infrared radiation','NEW','12-198','12-178',NULL),
(110912,'biology','Transpiration is affected by several external factors like?','Light  and  humidity','Transpiration is affected by several external factors: temperature, light,humidity, wind speed\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 187','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','Water status of plant','Light  and  humidity','Soil','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110913,'biology','Plant factors that affect transpiration include?','% of open stomata','Plant factors that affect transpiration include number and distribution of stomata, percent of open stomata, water status of the plant, canopy structure etc\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 187','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','% of open stomata','Number of chloroplast','Glucose status of the plant  and  canopy structure','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331318,'biology','Following statements describe the characteristics of the enzyme restriction endonuclease. Identify the incorrect statement. \n[NEET 2019]','The enzyme binds DNA at specific sites and cuts only one of the two strands.','The restriction endonuclease enzyme inspects the length of a DNA sequence. Once it recognises specific sequence, it binds to the DNA and cuts each of the two strands of the double helix at specific points in their sugar phosphate backbone. Special sequence in the DNA recognised by restriction endonuclease is called palindromic nucleotide sequence','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','The enzyme recognises a specific palindromic nucleotide sequence in the DNA','The enzyme cuts DNA molecule at identified position within the DNA.','The enzyme binds DNA at specific sites and cuts only one of the two strands.','The enzyme cuts the sugar-phosphate backbone at specific sites on each strand','NEW','12-196','12-176',NULL),
(331319,'biology','Which of the following is commonly used as a vector for introducing a DNA fragment in human lymphocytes? \n[NEET 2018]','Retrovirus','Retrovirus is a commonly used vector to introduce specific genes into bone marrow stem cells in the  gene therapy and  treatment of many blood related and enzyme related disorders.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Retrovirus','Ti plasmid','λ phage','pBR322','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(331320,'biology','DNA precipitation out of a mixture of biomolecules can be achieved by treatment with\n [NEET 2019]','chilled ethanol','In order to cut the DNA with restriction enzymes, it needs to be in pure form, free from other macromolecules. Since the DNA is enclosed by the membranes, we have to break the cell open to release DNA and other macromolecules like RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and lipids. It is obtained by treating the bacterial cells/plant or animal tissue with enzymes. Other molecules are removed by appropriate treatments and purified DNA ultimately precipitates out after the addition of chilled ethanol.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','chilled chloroform','isopropanol','chilled ethanol','methanol at room temperature','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(331321,'biology','Which one of the following equipments is essentially required for growing microbes on a large scale, for industrial production of enzymes?\n[NEET 2019]','Bioreactor','Small volume cultures cannot yield appreciable quantities of products.\nTo produce in large quantities, the development of bioreactors, where\nlarge volumes (100-1000 litres) of culture can be processed, was required.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Bioreactor','BOD incubator','Sludge digester','Industrial oven','NEW','12-204','12-174',NULL),
(331322,'biology','The correct order of steps in Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is','denaturation, annealing, extension','Polymerase chain reaction or PCR consists of the following three steps: Denaturation- The two DNA strands of template DNA separate from each other when heated to 92℃. \nAnnealing- The primers anneal to the 3’ end of single strands of DNA. Extension- The primers are extended by DNA polymerase by the addition of nucleotides to form complete strands of DNA. Hence the sequence of steps is denaturation, annealing, extension.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','extension, denaturation, annealing','annealing, extension, denaturation','denaturation, extension, annealing','denaturation, annealing, extension','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(331323,'biology','DNA strands on a gel stained with ethidium bromide when viewed under UV radiation, appear as\n[NEET 2021]','Bright orange bands','Ethidium Bromide or EtBr is an intercalating agent. EtBr is commonly used to detect nucleic acids inluding double stranded DNA or single single-stranded RNA. When exposed to UV light, it will fluoresce with an orange colour, intensifying almost 20-fold after binding to DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Bright blue bands','Yellow bands','Bright orange bands','Dark red bands','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331324,'biology','Which of the following is a correct sequence of steps in a PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction)?\n[NEET 2021]','Denaturation, Annealing, Extension','Denaturation to separate strands, Annealing(adhere) with the addition of primers , Extension of the primer. are the steps in PCR process.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Annealing, Denaturation, Extension','Denaturation, Annealing, Extension','Denaturation, Extension, Annealing','Extension, Denaturation, Annealing','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(331325,'biology','During the purification process for recombinant DNA technology, addition of chilled ethanol precipitates out :\n[NEET 2021]','DNA','During isolating DNA, the RNA can be removed by\ntreatment with ribonuclease whereas proteins can be removed by\ntreatment with protease. Other molecules can be removed by appropriate\ntreatments and purified DNA ultimately precipitates out after the addition\nof chilled ethanol.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Polysaccharides','RNA','DNA','Histones','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(331326,'biology','A specific recognition sequence identified by endonucleases to make cuts at specific positions within the DNA is \n[NEET 2021]','Palindromic Nucleotide sequences','Endonucleases which are a part of restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre\nof the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on the opposite\nstrands.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Poly(A) tail sequences','Degenerate primer sequence','Okazaki sequences','Palindromic Nucleotide sequences','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(331327,'biology','Which of the following is not an application of PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction)?\n[NEET 2021]','Purification of isolated protein','PCR is primarily done for amplifying a particular gene of interest. The process can also reveal the presence of mutations in the specific gene which is indirectly useful for molecular diagnosis of various diseases','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Detection of gene mutation','Molecular diagnosis','Gene amplification','Purification of isolated protein','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(331328,'biology','Plasmid pBR322 has Pstl restriction enzyme site within gene ampR that confers ampicillin resistance. If this enzyme is used for inserting a gene for β-galactoside production and the recombinant plasmid is inserted in an E.coli strain \n[NEET 2021]','It will not be able to confer ampicillin resistance to the host cell','- Due to the insertion of the gene at Pst I of ampR region of pBR322, the ampR gene is inactivated.\n- This is called insertional activation.\n- As a result, ampicillin resistance would not be conferred.\n\nPlasmid pBR322 has a Pst l restriction enzyme site within gene ampR that confers ampicillin resistance. If this enzyme is used for inserting a gene for β-galactoside production and the recombinant plasmid is inserted in an E.coli strain, it will not be able to confer ampicillin resistance to the host cell.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','It will be able to produce a novel protein with dual ability','It will not be able to confer ampicillin resistance to the host cell','The transformed cells will have the ability to resist ampicillin as well as produce β-galactoside','It will lead to lysis of host cell','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331329,'biology','During the process of gene amplification using PCR, if very high temperature is not maintained in the beginning, then which of the following steps of PCR will be affected first? \n[NEET 2021]','Denaturation','The initial denaturation step is commonly performed at 94–98°C for 1–3 minutes. The time and temperature of this step can vary depending on the nature of the template DNA and salt concentrations of buffer., and this very high temperature, is very essential for the denaturation process where the DNA splits.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Ligation','Annealing','Extension','Denaturation','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(331330,'biology','A specific recognition sequence identified by endonucleases to make cuts at specific positions within the DNA is: \n[NEET 2021]','Palindromic Nucleotide sequences','A palindromic sequence is the same backwards and forwards on both sides . This means that the enzyme recognizes the sequence no matter from which side the enzyme approaches the DNA. A palindromic sequence also increases the chance that both strands of DNA are cut','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Poly(A) tail sequences','Degenerate primer sequence','Okazaki sequences','Palindromic Nucleotide sequences','NEW','12-190',NULL,NULL),
(331331,'biology','The process of translation of mRNA to proteins begins as soon as :\n[NEET 2022]','The small subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA','When the small subunit of ribosome encounters an mRNA, the process of translation of the mRNA to protein begins. This process is followed by the binding of bigger/larger subunit. t-RNA is activated by the addition of amino acid prior to the attachment of ribosome, in the first phase.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','The small subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA','The larger subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA','Both the subunits join together to bind with mRNA','The tRNA is activated and the larger subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA','NEW','12-203','12-173',NULL),
(331332,'biology','DNA polymorphism forms the basis of :\n[NEET 2022]','Both genetic mapping and DNA finger printing','Polymorphism in DNA sequence is the basis of genetic mapping of human genome as well as of DNA fingerprinting.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Genetic mapping','DNA finger printing','Both genetic mapping and DNA finger printing','Translation','NEW','12-119',NULL,NULL),
(331333,'biology','Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). \n\nAssertion (A) : Polymerase chain reaction is used in DNA amplification. \n\nReason (R) : The ampicillin resistant gene is used as a selectable marker to check transformation In the light of the above statements, \n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :\n[NEET 2022]','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Polymerase chain reaction is used in DNA amplification. Ampicillin resistance gene is a selectable marker that helps to check transformation by selection of transformants.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(331334,'biology','Read the following statements and choose the set of correct statements : \n\n(a) Euchromatin is loosely packed chromatin\n\n(b) Heterochromatin is transcriptionally active \n\n(c) Histone octomer is wrapped by negatively charged DNA in nucleosome \n\n(d) Histones are rich in lysine and arginine \n\n(e) A typical nucleosome contains 400 bp of DNA helix \n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :\n[NEET 2022]','(a), (c), (d) Only','Heterochromatin is transcriptionally inactive. A typical nucleosome contains 200 bp of DNA helix. Euchromatin is the loosely packed chromatin region. The negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form a structure called nucleosome. Histones are rich in basic amino acid residues lysine and arginine.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','(b), (d), (e) Only','(a), (c), (d) Only','(b), (e) Only','(a), (c), (e) Only','NEW','12-099',NULL,NULL),
(331335,'biology','Which one of the following statement is not true regarding gel electrophoresis technique?\n[NEET 2022]','The presence of chromogenic substrate gives blue coloured DNA bands on the gel.','bright colored bands of DNA can be observed in the gel when EtBr (Ethidium bromide) treated DNA is exposed to UV light.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','The process of extraction of separated DNA strands from gel is called elution.','The separated DNA fragments are stained by using ethidium bromide.','The presence of chromogenic substrate gives blue coloured DNA bands on the gel.','Bright orange coloured bands of DNA can be observed in the gel when exposed to UV light.','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331336,'biology','The appearance of recombination nodules on homologous chromosomes during meiosis characterizes :\n[NEET 2022]','Sites at which crossing over occurs','Pachytene stage of meiosis is characterised by the appearance of recombination nodules, the sites at which crossing over occurs between non sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Cell cycle and cell division','2','mcq','Synaptonemal complex','Bivalent','Sites at which crossing over occurs','Terminalization','NEW','11-168',NULL,NULL),
(331337,'biology','Transposons can be used during which one of the following ? \n[NEET 2022]','Gene Silencing','gene silencing is the correct answer, as the source of the complementary RNA for RNAi could be mobile \ngenetic elements (transposons) that replicate via an RNA intermediate.\n\nothers are incorrect because: \n- autoradiography usally follows hybridisation. \n- polymerase chain reaction is used to make copies of the DNA sample and does not need transposons.\n- transposons are not required during gene sequencing.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology and its applications','3','mcq','Polymerase Chain Reaction','Gene Silencing','Autoradiography','Gene sequencing','NEW','12-209',NULL,NULL),
(331338,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. diagram \nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :\n(3311)','(a)-(iii),\n(b)-(i),\n(c)-(iv),\n(d)-(ii)','In metacentric chromosome, centromere is in the middle of the chromosomes. Acrocentric chromosome has centromere close to the end of the chromosome. In submetacentric chromosome, centromere is slightly away from the middle of the chromosome. Telocentric chromosome has terminal centromere.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Cell cycle and cell division','3','mcq','(a)-(iii),\n(b)-(i),\n(c)-(iv),\n(d)-(ii)','(a)-(i),\n(b)-(iii),\n(c)-(ii),\n(d)-(iv)','(a)-(ii),\n(b)-(iii),\n(c)-(iv),\n(d)-(i)','(a)-(i),\n(b)-(ii),\n(c)-(iii),\n(d)-(iv)','NEW','11-099',NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/331338q.png'),
(331339,'biology','In the following palindromic base sequences of DNA, which one can be cut easily by particular restriction \nenzyme?\n[NEET 2022]','5\'GAATTC3\';\n3\'CTTAAG5\'','A palindromic DNA sequence is a DNA sequence of base pairs that reads same on the two strands when orientation of reading is kept the same.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','5\'GATACT3\';\n3\'CTATGA5\'','5\'GAATTC3\';\n3\'CTTAAG5\'','5\'CTCAGT3\';\n3\'GAGTCA5\'','5\'GTATTC3\';\n3\'CATAAG5\'','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(331340,'biology','If a geneticist uses the blind approach for sequencing the whole genome of an organism, followed by assignment of function to different segments, the methodology adopted by him is called as :\n[NEET 2022]','Sequence annotation','Sequencing the whole set of genome that contained all the coding and non-coding sequences and later assigning different regions in the sequence with fuctions is called sequence annotation.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Sequence annotation','Gene mapping','Expressed sequence tags','Bioinformatics','NEW','12-119',NULL,NULL),
(331341,'biology','Given below are two statements:\n\nStatement I:\nRestriction endonucleases recognise specific sequence to cut DNA known as palindromic nucleotide \nsequence.\n\nStatement II:\nRestriction endonucleases cut the DNA strand a little away from the centre of the palindromic site.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:\n[NEET 2022]','Both are correct Statement II and Statement I','Each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific palindromic nucleotide sequences in the DNA. It will bind to the DNA and cut each of the two strands of double helix at specific points. Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindrome site; but \nbetween the same two bases on the opposite strands. So both the statements I and II are correct.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both are correct Statement II and Statement I','Both are incorrect Statement II and Statement I','Statement II is correct but Statement I is incorrect','Statement II is incorrect but Statement I is correct','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(331342,'biology','In an E. Coli strain i gene gets mutated and its product can not bind the inducer molecule. \nIf growth medium is provided with lactose, what will be the outcome?\n[NEET 2022]','z ,y ,a genes will not be translated','As the product of ‘i’ gene binds with the operator region and blocks the transcription and translation of z, y and a genes. It’s product is prevented from binding to the operator by attaching it with the inducer. As the inducer can now no more capable of binding with the repressor, thus, in all the cases, operator always gets attached with the repressor thereby preventing the transcription and transmission of z, y and a. Even in the presence of lactose, transcription and translation of z, y and a would not occur','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','Only z gene will get transcribed','z, y, a genes will be transcribed','z ,y ,a genes will not be translated','RNA polymerase will bind the promoter region','NEW','12-116',NULL,NULL),
(331343,'biology','In gene therapy of Adenosine Deaminase (ADA) deficiency, the patient requires periodic infusion of genetically engineered lymphocytes because :\n[NEET 2022]','Genetically engineered lymphocytes are not immortal cells.','genetically engineered lymphocyctes are not immortal cells and die after some time.\n\nother options are incorrect because:\n- the lymphocytes from patient’s blood are grown in culture, outside the body but it is not the correct reason.\n-  if the gene isolated from bone marrow cells producing ADA is introduced into cells at early embryonic stages, it could be a permanent cure.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology and its applications','2','mcq','Retroviral vector is introduced into these lymphocytes','Gene isolated from marrow cells producing ADA is introduced into cells at embryonic stages','Lymphocytes from patient\'s blood are grown in culture, outside the body.','Genetically engineered lymphocytes are not immortal cells.','NEW','12-213',NULL,NULL),
(331344,'biology','Ten E.coli cells with 15N - dsDNA are incubated in medium containing 14N nucleotide. After 60 minutes, how many E.coli cells will have DNA totally free from 15N?\n[NEET 2022]','60 cells','- In 1st generation, 15N containing 10 E.coli cells will produce 20 hybrid cells in 20 minutes.\n- In the 2nd generation, in 20 minutes, 20 hybrid cells will produce 40 cells, i.e., 20 hybrid cells with 15N- 14N and 20 cells with 14N.\n- In the 3rd generation, in 20 minutes, 40 cells will produce, 20 14N -containing cells and 20 hybrid.\nand also 40 14N-containing cells.\nso total will be 60  14N containing cells.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Molecular basis of inheritance','2','mcq','20 cells','40 cells','60 cells','80 cells','NEW','12-105',NULL,NULL),
(331345,'biology','The recombination frequency between the genes a and c is 5%, b and c is 15%, b and d is 9%, a and b is 20%, c and d is 24% and a and d is 29%. What will be the sequence of these genes on a linear chromosome?\n[NEET 2022]','a, c, b, d','1% recombination frequency = 1 centi Morgan To place the genes on a linear chromosome, decreasing order of recombination frequency will be considered.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','a, d, b, c','d, b, a, c','a, b, c, d','a, c, b, d','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331346,'biology','Which of the following is not a desirable feature of a cloning vector?\n[NEET 2022]','Presence of two or more recognition sites','Cloning vectors are the carriers of the desired gene in the host cell. \nThe features desirable in a cloning vector are:-\n• Presence of origin of replication\n• Presence of marker genes\n• Presence of very few, preferably single recognition site for the commonly used restriction enzymes','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Presence of origin of replication','Presence of a marker gene','Presence of single restriction enzyme site','Presence of two or more recognition sites','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331347,'biology','Given below are two statements : one is labelled as \nAssertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). \n Assertion (A) :\n When a particular restriction enzyme cuts strand of \nDNA, overhanging stretches or sticky ends are formed. \nReason (R) : \n Some restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a \nlittle away from the centre of palindromic site. \n In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :','(1) Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the\ncorrect explanation of (A)','Restriction enzyme, a type of endonuclease, functions by "inspecting" the length of a DNA sequence. Once it finds a recognition sequence, it binds and cut each of the two strands of the double helix at specific point leaving single stranded portions at the ends. This results in overhanging stretches called sticky ends. These are named so because they form hydrogen bonds with their complementary counter parts i.e., they can join similar complementary ends of DNA fragment from some other source with the help of DNA ligase. This stickiness of the ends facilitates the action of the enzyme DNA ligase, not DNA polymerase','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','(1) Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the\ncorrect explanation of (A)','(2) Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(3) (A) is correct but (R) is not correct','(4) (A) is not correct but (R) is correct','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(331348,'biology','Separation of DNA, fragments is done by a \ntechnique known as :','(4) Gel electrophoresis','The cutting of DNA by restriction endonucleases results in the fragments of DNA. These fragments can be separated by a technique known as gel electrophoresis.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','(1) Polymerase Chain Reaction','(2) Recombinant technology','(3) Southern blotting','(4) Gel electrophoresis','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331349,'biology','Ligation of foreign DNA at which of the following site will result in loss of tetracycline resistance of pBR322? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(2) BamH I','Option (2) is the answer because,\nIn pBR322, the restriction site for BamH I is present in tet\nR gene. Therefore, ligation of a foreign DNA \nwill results in loss of tetracycline resistance and recombinants will be tetracycline sensitive.\nOption (1) is not the answer because, In pBR322, the restriction site of EcoR I is present outside the \nregion of ampR gene and tetR gene.\nOption (3) and (4) are not the answers because, in pBR322, the restriction sites for Pst I and Pvu I are \npresent in ampR gene. So, ligation of foreign DNA will result in loss of ampicillin resistance and \nrecombinants will be ampicillin sensitive.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','(1) EcoR I','(2) BamH I','(3) Pst I','(4) Pvu I','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331350,'biology','Thermostable DNA polymerase used in PCR was isolated from : [NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) Thermus aquaticus','Option (3) is the answer, because Thermostable DNA polymerase (Taq polymerase) used in PCR was \nisolated from a bacterium Thermus aquaticus.\nOption (1) is not the answer as Agrobacterium tumifaciens is a pathogen of several dicot plants.\nOption (2) is not the answer as, specific Bt toxin genes were isolated from Bacillus thuringiensis and\nincorporated into the several crop plants for the development of pest resistant crops.\nE. Coli was used as a host by Eli lily to produce human insulin.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','(1) Agrobacterium tumifaciens','(2) Bacillus thuringiensis','(3) Thermus aquaticus','(4) Escherichia coli','NEW','12-203','12-173',NULL),
(331351,'biology','Which of the following can act as molecular scissors? [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(1) Restriction enzymes','Restriction enzymes are also called \'molecular scissors\' as they cleave DNA at or near specific recognition sequences known as restriction sites. These enzymes make one incision on each of the two strands of DNA and are also called restriction endonucleases.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','(1) Restriction enzymes','(2) DNA ligase','(3) RNA polymerase','(4) DNA polymerase','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331352,'biology','Which of the following statement is incorrect about \nAgrobacterium tumifaciens ?','(1) It is used to deliver gene of interest in both prokaryotic as well as eukaryotic host cells.','The correct statement is:\n\nIt is used to deliver the gene of interest in both prokaryotic as well as eukaryotic host cells.\nAgrobacterium tumefaciens is a soil bacterium that is widely used in biotechnology to deliver genes of interest into plant cells. However, it is not used to deliver genes into prokaryotic host cells. Agrobacterium-mediated gene transfer is mainly utilized for introducing foreign genes into the genomes of plants and generating genetically modified organisms (GMOs).\n\nThe incorrect statement is:\n\nIt is used to deliver the gene of interest in both prokaryotic as well as eukaryotic host cells.\nThe other options are correct: 2. \'Ti\' plasmid from Agrobacterium tumefaciens used for gene transfer is not pathogenic to plant cells.\n\nIt transforms normal plant cells into tumor cells.\nIt delivers \'T-DNA\' into plant cells.\nTherefore, the correct option is 1: It is used to deliver the gene of interest in both prokaryotic as well as eukaryotic host cells','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','(1) It is used to deliver gene of interest in both prokaryotic as well as eukaryotic host cells.','(2) \'Ti\' plasmid from Agrobacterium tumifaciens used for gene transfer is not pathogenic to plant cell.','(3) It transforms normal plant cells into tumor cells.','(4) It delivers \'T-DNA\' into plant cell.','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(331353,'biology','Which one of the following techniques does not serve the purpose of early diagnosis of a disease for its\nearly treatment? [ NEET 2023]','(1) Serum and Urine analysis','Conventional methods are ineffective for early disease diagnosis and detection.\nEarly detection techniques: \n\nRDT \nELISA \nPCR','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology and its applications','1','mcq','(1) Serum and Urine analysis','(2) Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) technique','(3) Enzyme Linked Immuno-Sorbent Assay (ELISA) technique','(4) Recombinant DNA Technology','NEW','12-212',NULL,NULL),
(331354,'biology','Which of the following is not a cloning vector? [ NEET 2023]','(3) Probe','Probe is not a cloning vector.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','(1) YAC','(2) pBR322','(3) Probe','(4) BAC','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331355,'biology','Refer to the following statements for agarose-gel \nelectrophoresis : \n (a) Agarose is a natural polymer obtained from sea-weed. \n (b) The separation of DNA molecules in agarose-gel \nelectrophoresis depends on the size of DNA. \n (c) The DNA migrates from negatively-charged electrode to the positively-charged electrode \n (d) The DNA migrates from positively-charged electrode to the negatively-charged electrode. \n Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below : [ RE NEET 2022]','(2) (a), (b) and (c) only','Option (c) is the correct answer as the most commonly used matrix in gel electrophoresis is agarose which is a natural polymer extracted from sea weeds.\n\nIn agarose gel electrophoresis, the DNA fragments separate (resolve) according to their size through sieving effect provided by the agarose gel.\n\nSince, DNA fragments are negatively charged molecules, they migrate towards the anode (positively-charged electrode) under an electric field through a medium/martix.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','(1) (a) and (b) only','(2) (a), (b) and (c) only','(3) (a), (b) and (d) only','(4) (b), (c) and (d) only','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331356,'biology','Match List-I with List-II. \nList-I List-II\n(A) Kanamycin (I) Delivers genes into animal cells \n(B) ClaI (II) Selectable marker \n(C) Disarmed retroviruses (III) Restriction site\n(D) Kanamycin R\ngene (IV) Antibiotic resistance \nChoose the correct answer from the options given below : [ NEET 2023(Phase 2)]','(3) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','1','mcq','(1) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)','(2) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)','(3) (A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','(4) (A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331357,'biology','Genetic engineering involves:\n\n(AIPMT 1998)','Use of restriction endonuclease on bacterial DNA and formation of new traits','Genetic engineering : Techniques to alter the chemistry of genetic material (DNA and RNA), to introduce these into host organisms and thus change the phenotype of the host organism.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Use of restriction endonuclease on bacterial DNA and formation of new traits','Use of Ligase for cutting DNA','Developing instruments','Use of statistic in genetics','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(331358,'biology','Which one of the following techniques made it possible to genetically engineer living organisms?\n(AIPMT 2011)','Recombinant DNA techniques','Traditional hybridisation procedures used in plant and animal breeding, very often lead to inclusion and multiplication of undesirable genes along with the desired genes. The techniques of genetic engineering which include creation of recombinant DNA, use of gene cloning and gene transfer, overcome this limitation and allows us to isolate and introduce only one or a set of desirable genes without introducing undesirable genes into the target organism.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Hybridization','20Oxalo succinic acid','X-ray diffraction','Heavier isotope labelling','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(331359,'biology','Which of the following is most used in genetic engineering:\n (AIPMT 1998)','E. coil and Agrobacterium','Genetic engineering is a branch of science that manipulates an organism\'s genome using diverse biotechnological techniques to generate species with desired qualities. However, the process requires the isolation, transfer, and insertion of additional genetic information into host genomes, as well as the long-term preservation of genomes in organisms and selecting markers. Experiments have revealed that certain bacteria may naturally take in and express foreign DNA. It has been discovered that E.coli may transmit DNA laterally to and from other species, whereas Agrobacterium can transfer genes to plants and fungus via crown gall disease.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','E. coil and Agrobacterium','Mycobacteria and Salmonella','Aspergillus','Penicillium','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331360,'biology','Restriction endonucleases:\n\n(AIPMT 2004)','Are synthesized by bacteria as part of their defense mechanism.','In the year 1963, the two enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bacteriophage in Escherichia coli were isolated. One of these added methyl groups to DNA, while the other cut DNA. The later was called restriction endonuclease.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Are used in genetic engineering for ligating two DNA molecules.','Are used for in vitro DNA synthesis.','Are synthesized by bacteria as part of their defense mechanism.','Are present in mammalian cells for the degradation of DNA when the cell dies.','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331361,'biology','First discovered restriction endonuclease that always cuts DNA molecules at a particular point by recognizing a specific sequence of six base pairs is:\n\n(NEET 2020)','Hind II','In the year 1963, the two enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bacteriophage in Escherichia coli were isolated. One of these added methyl groups to DNA, while the other cut DNA. The later was called restriction endonuclease.\nThe first restriction endonuclease–Hind II, whose functioning depended on a specific DNA nucleotide sequence was isolated and characterised five years later. It was found that Hind II always cut DNA molecules at a particular point by recognising a specific sequence of  six base pairs.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','EcoRI','Adenosine deaminase','Thermostable DNA polymerase','Hind II','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331362,'biology','There is a restriction endonuclease called Eco RI. What does the \'co\' part in it stand for?\n\n(AIMPT 2011)','Coli','The convention for naming these enzymes is the first letter of the name comes from the genus and the second two letters come from the species of the prokaryotic cell from which they were isolated, e.g., EcoRI comes from Escherichia coli RY 13.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Coelom','Coenzyme','Coli','Colon','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331363,'biology','Which one of the following represents a palindromic sequence in DNA?\n(AIMPT 2012)','5\'-GAATTC-3\'\n3\'-CTTAAG-5','Do you know what palindromes are? These are groups of letters that form the same words when read both forward and backward, e.g., “MALAYALAM”. As against a word-palindrome where the same word is read in both directions, the palindrome in DNA is a sequence of base pairs that reads same on the two strands when orientation of reading is kept the same.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','5\'-CCAATG-3\'\n3\'-GAATCC-5\'','5\'-CATTAG-3\'\n3\'-GATAAC-5\'','5\'-GATACC-3\' \n3\'-CCTAAG-5\'','5\'-GAATTC-3\'\n3\'-CTTAAG-5','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331364,'biology','Given below are two statements: One is labeled as Assertion (A) and the other is labeled as Reason (R)\n\nAssertion (A):\n\nRestriction enzyme is a type of endonuclease\n\nReason (R):\n\nRestriction enzyme cuts the two stands of DNA at specific positions within the DNA\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:\n\n(NEET 2022)','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Restriction enzymes belong to a larger class of enzymes called nucleases. These are of two kinds; exonucleases and endonucleases. Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA whereas, endonucleases make cuts at specific positions within the DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct.','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct.','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331365,'biology','Refer to the following statements for agarose-gel electrophoresis:\n\na. Agarose is a natural polymer obtained from sea-weed.\n\nb. The separation of DNA molecules in agarose-gel electrophoresis depends on the size of DNA.\n\nc. The DNA migrates from negatively-charged electrode to the positively- charged electrode.\n\nd. The DNA migrates from positively-charged electrode to the negatively- charged electrode.\n\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :\n\n(NEET 2022)','(a), (b) and (c) only','Option (c) is the correct answer since the most widely used matrix in gel electrophoresis is agarose, a natural polymer derived from seaweeds.\n\nAgarose gel electrophoresis separates (resolves) DNA fragments based on their size using the sieve effect given by the agarose gel.\n\n\nBecause DNA fragments are negatively charged molecules, they migrate to the anode (positively charged electrode) under an electric field via a medium/martix.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','a) and (b) only','(a), (b) and (c) only','(a), (b) and (d) only','b), (c) and (d) only','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331366,'biology','In genetic engineering, the antibiotic- resistance gene is used:\n(AIMPT 2012)','As selectable markers','Selectable markers are frequently antibiotic resistance genes; bacteria that have undergone a process to insert foreign DNA are cultured on an antibiotic-containing medium, and bacterial colonies that can grow have successfully taken up and expressed the injected genetic material.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','To select healthy vectors','As sequences from where replication starts','To keep the cultures free of infection','As selectable markers','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331367,'biology','The figure below is the diagrammatic representation of the E. coli vector pBR 322. Which one of the given options correctly identifies its certain components (s)?   (AIMPT 2012) diagram (331367Q)','AmpR, tetiR-antibiotic resistance genes','E. coli cloning vector pBR322 contains restriction sites such as Hind III, EcoRI, and BamHI.\nori - the origin of replication.\nAntibiotic resistance genes: ampR, tetR.\n\nrop stands for the proteins involved in plasmid replication.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Ori-original restriction enzyme','Rop-reduced osmotic pressure','Hind III, Eco RI-selectable markers','AmpR, tetiR-antibiotic resistance genes','NEW','12-199','12-169','/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/331367q.png'),
(331368,'biology','The two antibiotic resistance genes on vector pBR 322 are for: (NEET 2019)','Ampicillin and Tetracycline','The right selection is \'Ampicillin and Tetracycline\'.\n\nThis is because the E.Coli cloning vector pBR322 has antibiotic resistance genes for ampicillin and tetracycline.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Tetracycline and Kanamycin','Ampicillin and Tetracycline','Ampicillin and Chloramphenicol','Chloramphenicol and Tetracycline','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331369,'biology','Identify the properties of a good vector used in rDNA technology:\n\n(a) It should have origin of replication supporting a high copy number\n\n(b) It should have preferably more than \'2\' recognition sites\n\n(c) The restriction sites in vector should be in the antibiotic- resistant genes\n\n(d) It should have suitable marker genes\n\n(e) It should be easy to isolate and purify\n\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:\n(NEET 2022)','(a), (c), (d) and (e) only','Characteristics of a cloning vectors:\n\n-It must be small in size and self-replicating within the host cell.\n-It must include a restriction site for Restriction Endonuclease enzymes.\n-The introduction of donor DNA fragments must not interfere with the vector\'s replication properties.\n-It must contain a marker gene that can be used to later identify the recombinant cell.\n-It must have many cloning sites.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(a), (c) and (e) only','(c), (d) and (e) only','(a), (b) and (c) only','(a), (c), (d) and (e) only','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331370,'biology','Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\n\nAssertion (A):\n\nIn rDNA technology non-recombinants transformed bacteria grow on the medium containing ampicillin as well as medium containing tetracycline\n\nReason (R):\n\nRecombinant plasmids contain the foreign gene of interest.\n\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below: (NEET 2022)','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct.',NULL,'Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(A) is correct but (R) is not correct.','(A) is not correct but (R) is correct.','Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).','Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(2000039353,'physics','A small liquid drop of radius $R$ is divided into 27 identical liquid drops. If the surface tension is $T$, then the work done in the process will be','B','Radius of big drop $=R$<br>Number of drops $n=27$<br>Surface tension $=T$<br>$W=4 \\pi R^{2} T\\left(n^{\\frac{1}{3}}-1\\right)$<br>$W=4 \\pi R^{2} T\\left(27^{\\frac{1}{3}}-1\\right)$<br>$=8 \\pi R^{2} T$','Surface Tension','Mechanical Properties of Fluids','Hard',NULL,'$3 \\pi R^{2} T$','$8 \\pi R^{2} T$','$4 \\pi R^{2} T$','$\\dfrac{1}{8} \\pi R^{2} T$',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331371,'biology','Spooling is:\n (NEET 2020)','Collection of isolated DNA','DNA spooling is a method of collecting DNA in the form of a spool wrapped around a glass rod. This is the final step of DNA extraction. During DNA isolation, ethanol is used in the final stage to precipitate the DNA, which may then be spooled onto a glass rod.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Amplification of DNA','Cutting of separated DNA bands from the agarose gel','Transfer of separated DNA fragments to synthetic membranes','Collection of isolated DNA','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(331372,'biology','In gene gun method used to introduce alien DNA into host cells, microparticles of _____ metal are used.\n(NEET 2023)','Tungsten or gold','Microparticles made of tungsten or gold are often utilized in the gene gun method, a technique for introducing foreign DNA into host cells.\n\nThe gene cannon approach, also known as the biolistic particle delivery system, entails coating microscopic microparticles with foreign DNA before propelling them into the target cells. The microparticles serve as carriers for the foreign DNA and are driven into the host cells via high-pressure gas or mechanical force.\n\nTungsten and gold are preferred materials for microparticles due to their density and ability to penetrate target cell walls and membranes. Microparticles are normally coated with the required DNA and then delivered to host cells, where they can integrate into the cell\'s genome and express foreign genes.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Silver','Zinc','Copper','Tungsten or gold','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(331373,'biology','For transformation, microparticles coated with DNA to be bombarded with gene gun are made up of:\n (AIMPT 2012)','Gold or tungsten','A gene gun, also known as a biolistic particle delivery system, is a device that injects genetic material into cells. It was originally created for plant transformation. The payload is a heavy metal particle, such as gold or tungsten, covered in plasmid DNA. This technique is sometimes referred to as bioballistics or biolistics.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Silver or platinum','Platinum or zinc','Silicon or platinum','Gold or tungsten','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(331374,'biology','Biolistics (gene-gun) is suitable for:\n(AIMPT 2012)','Transformation of plant cells','The biolistic gun, often known as the gene gun method, was initially created to directly transfer genes into plant cells. It is a method for transferring genes to plants without the use of any vectors (plasmids, etc.).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Transformation of plant cells','Constructing recombinant DNA by joining with vectors','DNA fingerprinting','Disarming pathogen vectors','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(331375,'biology','Main steps in the formation of Recombinant DNA are given below. Arrange these steps in a correct sequence.\n\nA. Insertion of recombinant DNA into the host cell.\n\nB. Cutting of DNA at specific location by restriction enzyme.\n\nC. Isolation of desired DNA fragment.\n\nD. Amplification of gene of interest using PCR.\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:\n\n(NEET 2023)','B, C, D, A','The correct sequence of formation of recombinant DNA are given as following:\n1. Cutting of DNA at specific locations by restriction enzyme.\n2. Isolation of the desired DNA fragment.\n3. Amplification of the gene of interest using PCR.\n4. Insertion of recombinant DNA into the host cell.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','B, D, A, C','B, C, D, A','C, A, B, D','C, B, D, A','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(331376,'biology','Select the correct statement from the following:\n(NEET 2020)','Restriction enzyme digestions are performed by incubating purified DNA molecules with the restriction enzymes of optimum conditions.','PCR is a technique for amplification of DNA. Gel electrophoresis is used to separate DNA fragments. DNA ligase is used to connect the piece of DNA to the vector.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Gel electrophoresis is used for the amplification of a DNA segment.','The polymerase enzyme joins the gene of interest and the vector DNA.','Restriction enzyme digestions are performed by incubating purified DNA molecules with the restriction enzymes of optimum conditions.','PCR is used for the isolation and separation of genes of interest.','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(331377,'biology','In recombinant DNA technology antibiotics are used:\n(NEET 2020)','as selectable markers','In recombinant DNA technology, antibiotics serve as selectable markers.\nRecombinant DNA technology is the process of modifying genetic material outside of an organism to produce enhanced or desired characteristics.\nThe selectable markers are part of cloning vectors, which include antibiotic-producing protein genes. In recombinant DNA technologies Bacteria are treated to the introduction of a foreign gene using recombinant DNA technology and cultured on an antibiotic-containing media.\nThe bacteria that can thrive on that media have successfully incorporated the foreign gene.\nThe selectable markers aid in identifying and removing non-transformants. As a result, transformants can be grown selectively.\nGenes that convey antibiotic resistance are commonly utilized as selectable markers.\nSelectable markers confer resistance to microorganisms to that specific antibiotic.\nAmpicillin, kanamycin, chloramphenicol, and tetracycline are some of the most common selectable markers.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','to keep medium bacteria-free','to detect alien DNA','to impart disease-resistance to the host plant','as selectable markers','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331378,'biology','Function of Nucleases:\n(AIPMT 2019)','Breaks phosphodiester bond','The purpose of nucleases is to disrupt phosphodiester bonds. A nuclease is an enzyme that can cleave phosphodiester links between nucleotide subunits of nucleic acids. Nucleases are often separated into endonucleases and exonucleases, though certain enzymes may fall into both categories. Well-known nucleases include deoxyribonuclease and ribonuclease.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Break the polynucleotide chain by breaking each terminal nucleotide','Breaks phosphodiester bond','Breaks peptide bonds','Breaks ester bonds','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(331379,'biology','An enzyme catalysing the removal of nucleotides from ends of DNA is','Exonuclease','Page 196, XII NCERT','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','DNA ligase','Endonuclease','Exonuclease','Protease','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(331380,'biology','Which of the following cut the DNA from specific places:','Restriction endonuclease (EcoRI)','Page 196, XII NCERT','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Restriction endonuclease (EcoRI)','Ligase','Exonuclease','Alkaline phosphate','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(331381,'biology','Which one of the following hydrolyses internal phosphodiester bonds in a polynucleotide chain:','Endonuclease',NULL,'Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Lipase','protease','Exonuclease','Endonuclease','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331382,'biology','In a mixture, DNA fragments are separated by-','Electrophoresis','Page 198, XII NCERT','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Bioprocess engineering','Restriction digestion','Electrophoresis','Polymerase chain reaction','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331383,'biology','Separation of DNA, fragments is done by a technique known as:','Gel electrophoresis','Page 198, XII NCERT','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Polymerase Chain Reaction','Recombinant technology','Southern blotting','Gel electrophoresis','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331384,'biology','Upon exposure to UV radiation, DNA stained with ethidium bromide will show:','Bright orange colour',NULL,'Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Bright orange colour','Bright red colour','Bright Blue colour','Bright yellow colour','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331385,'biology','The bacteria generally used for genetic engineering is:','Agrobacterium','Page 200, XII NCERT','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Agrobacterium','Bacillus','Pseudomonas','Clostridium','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(331386,'biology','Which one of the following bacteria has found extensive use in genetic engineering work in plants?','Agrobacterium tumefaciens','Page 200, XII NCERT','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Clostridium septicum','Xanthomonas citri','Bacillus coagulens','Agrobacterium tumefaciens','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(331387,'biology','The enzyme (a) is needed for isolating genetic material from plant cells and enzyme (b) for isolating genetic material from fungus. Choose the correct pair of options from the following:','(a) Cellulase (b) Chitinase','Class 12 pg 201','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','(a) Cellulase (b) Protease','(a) Cellulase (b) Chitinase','(a) Chitinase (b) Lipase','(a) Cellulase (b) Lipase','NEW','12-201','12-171',NULL),
(331388,'biology','Which one of the following is a case of wrong matching?','Vector DNA- Site for tRNA synthesis','NCERT XII, Page 202','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Somatic hybridization- 1. Fusion of two diverse cells','Vector DNA- Site for tRNA synthesis','Micropropagation- In vitro production of plants in large numbers','Callus - Unorganized mass of cells produced in tissue culture','NEW','12-202','12-172',NULL),
(331389,'biology','In Lederberg\'s replica plating experiment, what shall be used to obtain streptomycin resistant strain?','Complete medium and streptomycin',NULL,'Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Minimal medium and streptomycin','Complete medium and streptomycin','Only minimal medium','Only complete medium','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(331390,'biology','Stirred-tank bioreactors have been designed for:','Availability of oxygen throughout the process','Page 204, XII NCERT','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Questions 2','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Addition of preservatives to the product','Purification of the product','Ensuring anaerobic conditions in the culture vessel','Availability of oxygen throughout the process','NEW','12-204','12-174',NULL),
(331402,'biology','Genes of interest can be selected from a genomic library by using \n[AIIMS 2014]','DNA probes','A hybridization probe is a fragment of DNA of variable length which is used in DNA samples to detect the presence of nucleotide sequence (the DNA target) that are complementary to the sequence in the probe. The probe hybridizes to single-stranded DNA whose base sequence allow probe target base-pairing due to complementary between the probe and target.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Aiims Questions','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Restriction enzymes','Cloning vectors','DNA probes','RNA probes','NEW','12-204','12-174',NULL),
(331403,'biology','Choose the correct option showing a meaningful gene expression .\n [AIIMS 2015]','5\'.............GAATTC.............3\',\n3\'.............CTTAAG.............5\'','Palindromic sequences in DNA molecule are group of bases that forms the same sequence when read in both forward and backward direction. In the given question, only option this option represent a palindromic sequence.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Aiims Questions','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','5\'.............CGTTCG.............3\',\n3\'.............ATGGTA.............5\'','5\'.............GATATG.............3\',\n3\'.............CTACTA.............5\'','5\'.............GAATTC.............3\',\n3\'.............CTTAAG.............5\'','5\'.............CACGTA.............3\', \n3\'.............CTCAGT.............5\'','NEW','12-196','12-166',NULL),
(331404,'biology','Which of the following statement is not correct About cloning vector ? \n[AIIMS 2017]','Selectable marker selectively permits the growth of the non-transformants.','Selectable marker selectively permitting the growth of the transformants.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Aiims Questions','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','‘Ori’ is a sequence responsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA.','Selectable marker selectively permits the growth of the non-transformants.','In order to link the alien DNA, the vector needs to have single recognition site for the commonly used restriction enzymes.','It is generally small in size.','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(110914,'biology','The transpiration driven ascent of xylem sap depends mainly on the physical properties of water.Which among the following isn\'t a physical property?','Water potential','The transpiration driven ascent of xylem sap depends mainly on the following physical properties of water: • Cohesion – mutual attraction between water molecules. • Adhesion – attraction of water molecules to polar surfaces (such as the surface of tracheary elements).\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 188','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','Cohesion','Adhesion','Surface Tension','Water potential','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331405,'biology','Assertion : In recombinant DNA technology, human genes are often transferred into bacteria (prokaryotes) or yeast (eukaryote). \n\nReason : Both bacteria and yeast multiply very fast to form huge population, which express the desired gene\n[AIIMS 2008, 2015]','If both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Recombinant DNA is a form of synthetic DNA that is engineered through the combination or insertion of one or more strands, thereby combining DNA sequences that would not normally occur together. In terms of genetic modification, recombinant DNA is produced through the addition of relevant DNA into an existingorganismal genome, such as the plasmid of bacteria, to code for or alter different traits for a specific purpose, such as immunity. It differs from genetic recombination, in that it does not occur through processes within the cell or ribosome, but is exclusively engineered. Recombinant protein is protein that is derived from recombinant DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Aiims Questions','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','If both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331406,'biology','Assertion : Insertion of recombinant DNA within the coding sequence of β-galactosidase results in colourless colonies.\nReason : Presence of insert results in inactivation of enzyme β-galactosidase known as insertional inactivation.\n[AIIMS 2017]','If both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','Alternative markers have been developed that can differentiate recombinants from non-recombinants based upon their ability to produce colour in presence of a chromogenic substrate. The plasmid in the bacteria, lacking an insert produces blue-colored colonies, while those plasmids with an insert do not produce any colour due to insertional inactivation of the enzyme, β-galactosidase.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology : Principles And Processes >> Previous Year Aiims Questions','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','If both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.','If both Assertion and Reason are correct, but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.','If Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.','If both the Assertion and Reason are incorrect.','NEW','12-200','12-170',NULL),
(331501,'biology','Consider the following statements: \n\nStatement I: Herbert Boyer was the first scientist to discover restriction endonucleases.\n\nStatement II: Herbert Boyer discovered EcoRI, a restriction enzyme that leaves sticky ends in DNA.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Herbert Boyer discovered EcoRI in E. coli, but Hamilton Smith was the first scientist to isolate HindII, a restriction endonuclease from H. influenzae.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(331502,'biology','Consider the following statements; Which of the statements is/are INCORRECT? \n\n(i) Herbert Boyer and Stanley Cohen were the first to demonstrate the process of genetic transformation in bacteria\n\n(ii) Herbert Boyer and Stanley Cohen only demonstrated recombination of DNA only,NOT genetic cloning \n\n(iii)Herbert Boyer discovered the restriction enzyme that leaves \'sticky ends\' after cutting the DNA\n\n(iv) Stanley Cohen developed the method for reinsertion and self replication of plasmid into bacteria','(ii) only','Herbert Boyer discovered EcoRI in E. coli that leaves sticky ends in the DNA after restriction digestion. Meanwhile, Stanley Cohen discovered a procedure by which bacteria can take up a plasmid, which then self replicates to produce clones of itself. Boyer and Cohen collaborated to develop the techniques for creating recombinant DNA, its uptake in bacterial cell and eventually replication and expression of the recombinant gene. They essentially established the field of recombinant DNA technology, which is now synonymous with biotechnology.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(ii) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(i), (iii) and (iv) only','(ii) only','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(331503,'biology','Assertion: The discovery of enzymes that can cleave DNA molecules to leave sticky ends in them preceded the development of genetic engineering techniques\n\nReason: Sticky ends in DNA are single stranded overhangs that can spontaneously ligate to molecules with the same overhangs.','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Herbert Boyer discovered EcoRI in E. coli and found that upon digestion with this enzyme, the cut DNA had staggered ends that spontaneously attached to another cut DNA with the same staggered ends without the need of any extra molecular manipulation. These staggered ends came to be known as \'sticky ends.\' He, in collaboration with S. Cohen, developed the tecniques for recombinant DNA creation and subsequent genetic transformation in bacteria. Only after discovery of restriction endonucleases, genetic sequencing gained wider access to gene sequencing and rDNA technology was discovered.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(331504,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\nStatement I: Stanley Cohen was the first scientist to discover a bacterial plasmid\n\nStatement II: Stanley Cohen was the first to develop a plasmid vector for genetic transformation','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Plasmids have been known since the 1950\'s. Joshua Lederberg was the first to coin the term \'plasmid\' to define any extrachromosomal hereditary material in 1952. Stanley Cohen developed a method to isolate plasmid from bacterial cells and then reinsert it into other cells while allowing it to retain its self replication and cloning properties. He, along with Herbert Boyer, was the first to develop a plasmid vector that was later shown to introduce recombinant DNA into bacterial cells leading to the inception of genetic transformation and use of bacteria for protein expression.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(331505,'biology','Assertion: Preparation of curd from milk is a part of biotechnological processes but production of monoclonal antibodies is not.\n\nReason: Preparation of curd entails the use of bacteria.','(A) is false but (R) is true','Both the preparation of curd from milk on a large scale and production of monoclonal antibodies are a part of biotechnological processes. Biotechnology as a field entails all those techniques wherein living organisms including bacteria are used to produce products of human use. Preparation of curd involves the use of Lactobacillus that convert the lactose sugar into lactic acid. Monoclonal antibody production utilizes a technique known as hybridoma technology wherein immortal B cells primed to a specific antigen are cultured in vitro to produce a specfic monoclonal antibody.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(331506,'biology','Consider the following statements: \n\n(i) Genetic manipulation of living organisms for industrial and commercial applications falls under the field of biotechnology\n\n(ii) Development of RNA vaccines for COVID 19 can be considered an application of biotechnology\n\n(iii) Wine making and curdling of milk  cannot be considered a biotechnological process\n\n(iv) Antibiotic and amino acid production from living organisms can be considered a biotechnological process\n\nChoose the CORRECT statement(s).','(i), (ii) and (iv)','Biotechnology encompasses all the processes and techniques that utilize living organisms and their derivatives (e.g. enzymes) for industrial and commercial applications. This includes the simpler processes like wine making and curd making, and genetic manipulation of bacterial cells to produce different compounds required for human use, such as antibiotics, amino acids, peptides, etc. The development of RNA/DNA vaccines is also a part of biotechnological processes.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(i), (ii) and (iv)','(i), (iii) and (iv)','(ii), (iii) and (iv)','(i) and (iv) only','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(331507,'biology','Assertion: DNA vaccines is one of the recent applications of biotechnology\n\nReason: A plasmid encoding an antigen against which immune response is required, is inserted in tissues','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','The techniques of recombinant DNA technology are applied to produce DNA vaccines. In the process, a plasmid is modified by a gene that codes for an antigen against which immune response is to be mounted.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(331508,'biology','Assertion: In vitro fertilization (IVF) involves the fertilization of a female egg by a sperm outside the female body, hence the name \'test tube baby\'\n\nReason: Genes can be isolated and recombined in different manners to get recombinant DNA that can lead to expression of the desired traits','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','IVF is a technique wherein the egg is fertilized with a sperm in a laboratory dish and the zygote is then implanted in the female uterus. It is a widely utilized techniques for people suffering from infertility or other reproductive disorders. However, genetic manipulation is not applied during IVF. Genetic manipulation, till date, has been performed to express proteins and other products of human use in bacterial or other living cells in a laboratory environment.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-193','12-163',NULL),
(331509,'biology','Consider the following statements: Which of the statement(s) is/are CORRECT?\n\n(i) Modern biotechnology encompasses ONLY the techniques of recombinant DNA technology (RDT) and related applications\n\n(ii) Bioprocess engineering utilizes RDT, and culture living cells in large scale to produce industrially/commercially relevant products, e.g. amino acids\n\n(iii) Bioprocess engineering cultures living cells in large scale to produce industrially/commercially relevant products but does not utilize/require RDT\n\n(iv) Bioprocess engineering is a subfield of modern biotechnology','(ii) and (iv)','Modern biotechnology consists of both RDT or genetic engineering and bioprocess engineering. RDT is often employed to introduce a gene of interest to bacterial cells to produce a certain compound, e.g. insulin, at large scales. RDT is also employed to increase the expression of a compound that is already produced by bacteria, e.g. amino acids. Thus, RDT is integral to the modern field of bioprocess engineering.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(i) and (ii)','(ii) only','(i) and (iii)','(ii) and (iv)','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(331510,'biology','Assertion: Recombination of DNA is a natural process that occurs during cell division\n\nReason: Homologous non-sister chromatids pair up to form DNA crossovers during reductional cell division.','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','During meiosis (reductional division), homologous chromosomes pair up and exchange different segments of genetic material to produce recombinant DNA. The site at which the exchange occurs is referred to as a DNA crossover. It occurs to maintain genetic variability in the gametes and offsprings.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(110915,'biology','Mutual attraction between water molecules is ________ physical property of water','Cohesion','• Surface Tension – water molecules are attracted to each other in the liquid phase more than to water in the gas phase.\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 188','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','Cohesion','Attraction','Adhesive and cohesive property','Adhesion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110916,'biology','Attraction of water molecules to polar surfaces (such as the surface of tracheary elements) is _______ physical property of water.','Adhesion','Adhesion – attraction of water molecules to polar surfaces (such as the surface of tracheary elements).\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 188','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','Cohesion','Attraction','Adhesive and cohesive property','Adhesion','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331511,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\nStatement I: Any foreign DNA can replicate independently after entering a host cell, even without an "ori" region.\n\nStatement II: A plasmid can replicate independently of the chromosomal DNA.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Foreign DNA cannot replicate independently of the host genome unless it has an origin of replication (ori). Thus, foreign DNA needs to be integrated to the host genome containing an ori for it to be replicated and be inherited. Plasmids are self replicating, extrachromosomal DNA that are found in some bacteria and fungi. They have their own origin of replication, hence can replicate independently of the chromosomal DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(331512,'biology','Consider the following statements about plasmids:\n\n(i) Plasmids are naturally occuring DNA with the ability to self replicate.\n\n(ii) All plasmids are artificially created by genetic engineering that can self replicate only after the introduction of origin of replication\n\n(iii) Some plasmids naturally provide genetic advantage to the host cell, e.g. antibiotic resistance\n\n(iv) Plasmids can be found exclusively in prokaryotic cells\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(ii) and (iv)','Plasmids are naturally occuring, circular extra chromosomal DNA present in bacteria and some eukaryotes (yeast). They are capable of self replicating and cloning themselves independently of the chromosomal DNA as they have their own origin of replication. Plasmids can provide genetic advantages, e.g. by conferring antibiotic resistance, and other survival skills to the host cell. Artificial plasmids are used in molecular cloning and expression experiments, wherein a gene of interest is inserted into a plasmid along with other genes for selection (selectable markers) for the specific purpose of its expression in a host cell. In that case, plasmids are also known as vectors for cloning.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(ii) and (iv)','(iv) only','(i) and (iii)','(i), (iii) and (iv)','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(331513,'biology','Assertion: Two different DNA molecules cleaved by the same restriction enzyme can be joined together by ligase\n\nReason: Restriction endonucleases hydrolyze the phosphodiester bond within the polynucleotide chain','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Restriction endonucleases are a class of enzymes that can cleave within the DNA at specific sites. Every restriction endonuclease has a specific recognition site, which is mostly palindromic in nature. Within the recognition site, the restriction endonuclease hydrolyzes* the phosphodiester bond in both the strands. Thus, even if the two DNA molecules are different, a specific restriction enzyme would cut at the same recognition site in both the DNA molecules and create DNA fragments with the same sticky ends. These sticky ends can form a double helix by complementary base pairing and are joined together by DNA ligase. (*Hydrolyzation is the process by which a water molecule is added to break a bond)','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331514,'biology','Statement I: Origin of replication is a prerequisite for the cloning of foreign DNA\n\nStatement II: Restriction endonucleases recognize palindromic sequences in prokaryotic DNA only.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Origin of replication (ori) is a specific DNA sequence within the genome which is required for initiation of replication. For the replication/cloning of foreign DNA in a host organism, presence of ori is essential., as the origin of replication has high affinity to the enzymes that are involved in replication.Restriction endonucleases recognize specific palindromic sequences in all types of cells, regardless of the source of DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW','12-194','12-164',NULL),
(331515,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding restriction enzymes and cloning:\n\n(i) Restriction endonucleases cut at either the 5\' or 3\' terminal of a double stranded DNA molecule\n\n(ii) Restriction enzymes are used as a defense mechanism by bacteria.\n\n(iii) Restriction enzymes are required in cloning for ligation of DNA molecules\n\n(iv) Restriction endonucleases are used by bacteria to promote genetic variability in their genome\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(ii) only','Restriction endonucleases are synthesized by bacterial cells to defend against bacteriophages (viruses that infect bacteria, not bacteria). They cut at specific recognition sites within the DNA molecule. Enzymes that specifically cut DNA at 5\' or 3\' terminal are referred to as exonucleases. The enzyme used to ligate two DNA fragments is called DNA ligase. Genetic variability in bacterial cells is maintained by conjugation, a method of sharing genetic material with other cells of the same or different bacterial strain.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(i) and (iv) only','(i) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(ii) only','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331516,'biology','Statement I: Cloning is referred to as the introduction of foreign DNA into the genome of host cell\n\nStatement II: For successful cloning, the foreign DNA must be integrated into the genome having an origin of replication','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Cloning refers to the copying of a particular gene or DNA segment integrated in a host genome. For successful cloning, the DNA segment should be integrated downstream to the origin of replication, which is the region in the host genome responsible for initiation of replication. Formation of multiple copies of the foreign DNA by replication and passing on to the progeny is referred to as cloning.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331517,'biology','Assertion: 5\'GTCGAC3\' DNA nucleotide sequence can be a potential restriction endonuclease recognition site\n\nReason: Restriction endonucleases bind to sites that are palindromic in nature','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Every restriction endonuclease recognizes a specific palindromic site in the DNA, i.e., the regions that read the same in 5\' to 3\' direction. For example, the binding site for EcoRI is 5\'GAATTC\'3\'. The complementary strand is identical when read in 5\' to 3\' direction (i.e. GAATTC). The sequence GTCGAC is also palindromic in nature, hence it can be a potential recognition site for a restriction endonuclease','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331518,'biology','Assertion: In recombinant DNA technology, only plasmids are used as vectors\n\nReason: A foreign gene can only be integrated into a plasmid that is then inserted into a host cell for cloning','(A) is false but (R) is true','In recombinant DNA technology, a foreign gene of interest is inserted into another DNA molecule, which is often a plasmid, by the action of restriction enzymes and ligases. The DNA molecule that carries the foreign gene is referred to as a \'vector.\' Plasmids are the most commonly used vectors. However, yeast chromosomes and \'disarmed\' viruses are also used as moelcular vectors for cloning and expression of foreign genes','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331519,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding recombinant DNA technology (RDT): \n\n(i) Restriction endonucleases and ligases are the only enzymes used in RDT\n\n(ii) The location of the foreign gene in the host genome is crucial for cloning\n\n(iii) The location of the foreign gene in a plasmid vector with respect to the origin of replication is not important for cloning\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(ii) and (iii) only','In addition to endonucleases and ligases, polymerases are also utilized in RDT for amplification of a desired gene before integration into a host genome. The location of the foreign gene in the host genome is crucial for cloning-it must be inserted downstream of  the origin of replication (ori) for it to replicated. However, plasmids are circular DNA molecules-their both ends are bound to form a circle. Thus, the location of the foreign gene in a plasmid vector with respect to the ori is not important.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(ii) and (iii) only','(i) only','(i) and (iii) only','(iii) only','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331520,'biology','Statement I: EcoRI was identified before Hind II \n\nStatement II: The numerals in the names of restriction enzymes given according to the order in which they were isolated','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','HindII was the first restriction endonuclease to be isolated. It was isolated from Hemophilus influenzae Rd. Their is a set of defined rules for the nomenclature of restriction enzymes. The letters are derived from the name and strain of the bacteria from which the enzyme was isolated and the Roman numeral refers to the order in which the enzyme was isolated from the particular bacterial strain. For example, EcoRI was the first restriction endonuclease to be isolated from the bacteria Escherichia coli RY 13, hence the name.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331521,'biology','Assertion: The sticky ends of DNA fragments cut by EcoRI can join together spontaneously without the help of enzymatic ligase\n\nReason: Ligases are required to join the DNA fragments cut by restriction endonucleases','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','After restriction digestion, the \'sticky ends\' at the opposite ends of DNA fragments can spontaneously reanneal by complementary base pairing without the need of enzymes. However, the phosphdiester bond that is broken during restriction digestion cannot be reannealed on its own. Thus ligases are required for rejoining of the strands by creating the phosphodiester bond in the sugar-phosphate backbone.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(331522,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding enzymes used in RDT:\n\n(i) Restriction endonucleases work by breaking the phosphodiester bond in the DNA backbone\n\n(ii) Restriction endonucleases work by breaking the phosphate bond that is either internal or at the 5\' terminal of the DNA molecule\n\n(iii) Ligases are enzymes that rejoin the hydrogen bonds in the cleaved DNA molecules\n\n(iv) Ligases are enzymes that rejoin both the hydrogen and phosphodiester bonds in cleaved DNA molecules\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i) only','Restriction endonucleases first break the phosphodiester bonds in the DNA backbone to create two separate fragments. Endonucleases specifically cleave within the DNA molecules. The enzymes that cleave the phosphate bonds at the DNA terminals are referred to as exonucleases. Ligases rejoin the broken DNA fragments by rejoining only the phosphodiester bond between the fragments. The hydrogen bond formation among the bases is spontaneous and is subject to complementary base pairing (A pairs with T and G pairs with C).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(i) and (iv) only','(i) only','(ii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(110917,'biology','Surface tension is ?','Water molecules are attracted to each other in the liquid phase more than to water in the gas phase','The transpiration driven ascent of xylem sap depends mainly on the following physical properties of water: • Cohesion – mutual attraction between water molecules. • Adhesion – attraction of water molecules to polar surfaces (such as the surface of tracheary elements).\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 188','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','Attraction of water molecules to polar surfaces','Water molecules are attracted to each other in the liquid phase more than to water in the gas phase','Mutual attraction between water molecules','none of the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331523,'biology','Statement I: Transformation refers to the introduction of recombinant DNA into a host cell, e.g bacteria\n\nStatement II: For successful transformation, the plasmid vector must be integrated to the intrinsic nucleoidal bacterial DNA for replication','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Plasmids are naturally occuring, circular extra chromosomal DNA present in bacteria and some eukaryotes (yeast). They are capable of self replicating and cloning themselves independently of the chromosomal DNA as they have their own origin of replication. Hence, they need not be integrated into the host genome for successful cloning and expression.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331524,'biology','Assertion: DNA fragments obtained after restriction digestion can be separated using gel electrophoresis.\n\nReason: The size of the DNA fragments determines their location and movements in the agarose gel upon electrophoresis.','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Upon restriction digestion, DNA is cut into fragments of many sizes. Upon the application of electric field, the negative charge on the DNA fragments allows them to move towards the positive electrode (anode) in a matrix commonly constituted by agarose gel. The agarose gel consists of pores of uniform size. And since the charge on every DNA fragment is roughly the same, the speed at which they migrate in the gel is determined by their size–the larger the fragment, the slower it will move through the pores. Hence, DNA fragments of different sizes are located in different positions in the gel matrix.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331525,'biology','Consider the following statements in the context of genetic engineering:\n\n(i) Restriction endonucleases always create sticky ends after cleaving the DNA\n\n(ii) Hydrogen bonds are formed when DNA fragments join after restriction digestion\n\n(iii) Ligases, like endonucleases, identify palindromic sequences and join the DNA fragments together\n\n(iv) Recombinant DNA can be formed by mixing DNA molecules with restriction endonucleases and DNA ligase\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(ii) and (iv) only','Restriction endonucleases recognize palindromic sequences in a DNA molecule. The process in which a restriction enzyme binds to a DNA recognition site and cleaves it into fragments is referred to as restriction digestion. Many restriction enzymes cut away from the center of their recognition sites; this leads to the creation of staggering ends knowns as \'sticky ends\'. However, there are many restriction endonucleases that cleave at the center of the recognition site and create blunt ends. For example, EcoRI creates sticky ends but SmaI produces blunt ends. The nitrogenous bases in the polynucleotide chain of DNA are bound together by hydrogen bonds (A and T are bound by 2 hydrogen bonds and G and C are bound by 3 hydrogen bonds). Thus, during rejoining of DNA fragments, the bases at the sticky ends rejoin via hydrogen bond formation. Unlike endonucleases, ligases rejoin the phosphdiester bonds in broken DNA fragments regardless of the sequence of bases. It is possible to create recombinant DNA by mixing different kinds of DNA with restriction enzymes and DNA ligase.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(i), (ii) and (iv)','(i), (iii) and (iv)','(iv) only','(ii) and (iv) only','NEW','12-197','12-167',NULL),
(331526,'biology','Two DNA samples- A and B- from two different bacterial cells. Sample A was cleaved with only EcoRI and sample B was cleaved only with HindIII. The DNA fragments obtained from the digestion were mixed together with DNA ligase. The following statements are the possible consequences of this experiment:\n\n(i) Recombinant DNA with fragments from sample A and B would be obtained after ligase action\n\n(ii) No recombinant DNA would be obtained because the endonucleases used for A and B are different\n\n(iii) Recombinant DNA with fragments from sample A and B would be obtained along with the original A and B samples after ligase action\n\n(iv) No recombinant DNA would be obtained because ligase cannot join fragments with different sticky ends.\n\nWhich of the above statements present the CORRECT consequence(s) of the experiment?','(ii) only','In order to yield recombinant DNA, the source DNA and the vector/host DNA must be cut by the same restriction endonuclease. This allows the creation of the same kind of staggered or sticky ends at the end, which enables joining of the fragments by complementary base pairing. Ligase rejoins the phosphodiester bond in the fragmented DNA to yield \'unbroken\' recombinant DNA. Ligase enables the formation of the phosphodiester bonds regardless of the sequence of bases.Even though restriction endonucleases are specific, ligases are not specific.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(ii) and (iv) only','(ii) only','(iii) only','(i) only','NEW','12-197','12-164',NULL),
(331527,'biology','Consider the following statements: \n\n(i) Gel electrophoresis can separate DNA fragments on the basis of their charge only, each fragment of DNA even if they have equal length have different charge.\n\n(ii) Gel electrophoresis can separate DNA fragments on the basis of their mass only\n\n(iii) Gel electrophoresis allows the visualization of DNA with naked eye\n\n(iv) In genetic engineering, gel electrophoresis is performed before the formation of recombinant DNA\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(i) and (iii) only','Gel electrophoresis is a technique that is utilized to separate DNA molecules of different sizes. It utilizes a gel made up of agarose onto which the DNA mixture is loaded and an electric field is applied to it. Since DNA molecules are negatively charged, they move towards the positive electrode (anode) under external electric field. The agarose gel has a particular pore size, that allows DNA molecules of certain size to move more freely than others. Since all DNA molecules have essentially the same charge, they get resolved in the gel on the basis of their size (and hence, mass)- the smaller DNA molecules move faster than the larger ones. DNA fragments resolved in such a manner are not visible by naked eye. For visualization, a dye known as ethidium bromide is added in the gel which gives orange colour to DNA when viewed under UV light. In genetic engineering, gel electrophoresis is performed after restriction digestion to separate the different DNA fragments obtained. The desired fragments are isolated from the gel for the formation of recombinant DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(i) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(i), (iii) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iv) only','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331528,'biology','Assertion: In genetic engineering, the DNA fragments obtained after restriction digestion undergo gel electrophoresis\n\nReason: DNA fragments in gel cannot be visualized without staining','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','After restriction digestion, many DNA fragments are obtained that are separated from each other by gel electrophoresis. This allows the separation of the desired fragments from the non desired ones. Thus, gel electrophoresis is performed to separate the different sized DNA fragments from each other and isolation of the desired fragments. In order to visualize the DNA, staining is performed and DNA is seen as orange bands in UV light.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331529,'biology','Statement I: During gel electrophoresis, ethidium bromide stains DNA and allows its movement in the gel matrix\n\nStatement II: Agarose is a natural polymer that binds to DNA and allows its migration during gel electrophoresis','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Ethidium bromide is used to stain DNA and visualize it under UV irradiation. Agarose is natural polymer that is used in its gel form in gel electrophoresis. It does not bind or interact with DNA. Instead, the pores in the gel act as sieve that causes DNA molecules of different sizes to migrate with different speeds in the gel matrix','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331530,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Copy number is defined as the number of plasmids present in a bacterial cell\n\n(ii) Copy number is defined as the number of copies a plasmid can create in a bacterial cell\n\n(iii) Bacteriophages are viruses that can replicate independently of the host genome\n\n(iv) Bacteriophages are bacterial cells that have high copy number of plasmids\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i) and (iii) only','Copy number is defined as the number of copies of a plasmid in a particular bacterial cell. High copy number plasmids often lead to vectors with high expression of foreign gene. Bacteriophages are viruses that infect bacteria and can replicate independent of the host bacterial genome. Bacteriophages are also used as vectors in gene cloning.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','2','mcq','(i) and (iv) only','(i) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','NEW','12-195','12-165',NULL),
(331531,'biology','A linear DNA molecule with 3 restriction sites for EcoRI was incubated with EcoRI and underwent complete digestion.\nThe obtained fragments underwent gel electrophoresis and were visualized by ethidium bromide staining. \nThe following statements refer to the possible outcomes of the experiment:\n\n(i) Maximum of 4 bands would be observed in the gel\n\n(ii) No bands would be visible as the DNA would be completely digested and broken into very small fragments\n\n(iii) Only two bands should be visible as one DNA molecule consists a maximum of one restriction site for a particular endonuclease\n\n(iv)  Maximum of 3 bands would be visible in the gel\n\nWhich of the above statements best represents the CORRECT outcome(s) of the experiment?','(i) only','There is no upper limit to the number of restriction sites a DNA molecule can possess. Complete digestion refers to the situation where the restriction endonuclease acts at all the available restriction sites in a DNA molecule. Thus, a DNA molecule with 3 restriction sites would yield maximum of 4 bands in gel electrophoresis upon restriction digestion.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(i) only','(ii) only','(iii) only','(iv) only','NEW','12-198','12-168',NULL),
(331532,'biology','Assertion: Cloning vectors are modified to contain at least one antibiotic resistance gene.\n\nReason: Selectable markers help in differentiating between the transformants and non transformant cells','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Selectable markers are genes that help in identifying the transformant cells from the non transformant cells. For example, antibiotic resistance genes allow or disallow the growth of the transformant cells in the media and helps in their selection.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331533,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) pBR322 was the first natural plasmid isolated from E. coli that was used as a cloning vector\n\n(ii) pBR322 was the first artificially created plasmid and used as a cloning vector in E. coli\n\n(iii) pBR322 consists of only one restriction site for EcoRI\n\n(iv) pBR322 uses antibiotic resistance against ampicillin and kanamycin as selectable markers\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(i) and (iv) only','pBR322 was the first artificial plasmid developed to be used as a cloning vector in E. coli. It consists of only one restriction site for EcoRI and uses antibiotic resistance against ampicillin and tetracycline as selectable markers.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(i), (iii) and (iv)','(i) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331534,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Presence of an origin of replication (ori) is the only requirement for a plasmid to be a cloning vector\n\n(ii) Plasmids and bacteriophages can be used as cloning vectors\n\n(iii) pBR322 was the first natural plasmid to be modified for use as a cloning vector in E. coli\n\n(iv) pBR322 consists of multiple restriction sites for HindIII\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(ii) only','In addition to the ori, selectable markers and restriction sites for attachment of foreign DNA are also required for a plasmid to be a cloning vector. pBR322 is the first artificially created plasmid for the purpose of cloning in E. coli. It consists of one restriction site per restriction enzyme','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','(i), (ii) and (iii) only','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','(ii) only','(iii) only','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331535,'biology','Assertion: There should be multiple restriction sites for a particular restriction enzyme in a cloning vector\n\nReason: Selectable markers are used for identifying the presence of foreign DNA in bacterial cells','(A) is false but (R) is true','There should only be a single restriction site for every common restriction enzyme in a cloning vector. This simplifies the gene cloning process by creating only a single DNA fragment after restriction digestion. Selectable markers such as antibiotic resistance allows the identification of transformant cells that contain the foreign DNA versus the non transformant cells.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331536,'biology','Assertion: Foreign DNA is inserted within an antibiotic resistance gene in pBR322 cloning vector\n\nReason: Insertional inactivation is a commonly used method to select transformants from non transformant cells','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','The insertion of foreign DNA into an antibiotic resistance gene results in the inactivation of that gene. Consequently, the non transformant cells can grow in the antibiotic containing medium and transformants cannot. In pBR322, if the foreign DNA is inserted in the ampicillin resistance gene then the transformant cells would grow only in tetracycline containing medium and not in ampicillin containing one. While the non transformant cells would grow in all media regardless of the antibiotic.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason','Biotechnology: Principles and Processes','3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW','12-199','12-169',NULL),
(331537,'biology','A foreign DNA and pBR322 are digested by BamHI and allowed to ligate together. The recombinant cloning vector was introduced in E. coli and the bacteria was allowed to grow in different media. Consider the following statements in this regard:\n\n(i) The transformants would grow in medium containing only tetracycline but not in medium containing ampicillin\n\n(ii) The non transformants would grow in medium containing both tetracycline and ampicillin\n\n(iii) The transformants would grow in medium containing both tetracycline and ampicillin\n\n(iv) The transformants would grow in medium containing only ampicillin and not tetracycline\n\nWhich of the above statements best represent the CORRECT outcome of the above experiment?','(ii) and (iv) only','In pBR322 , the BamHI restriction site is present within the tetracycline resistance gene (tetR). The insertion of the foreign DNA into the tetR region leads to the inactivation of the tetracycline resistance gene. Thus, the transformants will not be able to grow in any medium containing tetracycline. On the other hand, since the non transformants will have both the ampR and tetR genes functional, they will be able to grow in media containing either or both of the antibiotics.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii) and (iv) only','(i) and (ii) only','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','(i), (iii) and (iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331538,'biology','Statement I: During cloning, insertional activation is the most commonly employed method to isolate transformants from non-transformants.\n\nStatement II: Non-transformants show blue colour formation upon culture plating while transformants do not.','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','In order to isolate transformants after cloning, ‘insertional inactivation’ is the most commonly employed method, wherein the gene of interest is inserted within a selectable marker (e. g. within the gene coding for enzyme β-galactosidase). Thus, the transformants do not express β-galactosidase and hence lose their ability to produce colour, while non-transformants show blue colonies.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331539,'biology','Assertion: Antibiotic resistance and blue-white screening are both examples of insertional inactivation\n\nReason: Foreign DNA is ligated within the coding region of the selectable marker.','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Insertional inactivation is referred to as the process wherein a gene is inactivated due to the ligation of a foreign DNA in its coding region. This method is used by selectable markers in cloning vectors to identify between transformants and non transformants. Antibiotic resistance and blue white screening are both examples of insertional inactivation wherein the foreign DNA is inserted into the antibiotic resistance and color producing protein genes, respectively.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331540,'biology','Consider the following statements in respect to blue white screening in cloning:\n\n(i) The transformants form blue color colonies leading to easy identification\n\n(ii) The non transformants form blue colour colonies\n\n(iii) The transformants possess an enzyme beta-galactosidase that converts substrate into colorless product in the medium\n\n(iv) The non transformants possess enzyme beta-galactosidase that converts substrate into blue product in the medium  \n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(ii) and (iv) only','In blue white screening, the foreign DNA is inserted into the coding region of a gene that codes for the enzyme beta-galactosidase. Because of the insertion, the gene gets inactivated. Hence, the transformants are not able to produce beta-galactosidase. Non transformants use up a chromogenic substrate with the help of the enzyme and convert it into a blue colored product. But transformants are unable to do so. Hence, non transformants form blue colored colonies while transformants form colorless colonies.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iii) only','(ii) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110918,'biology','Match the following','Tensile strength-an ability to resist a pulling force,capillarity-the ability to rise in thin tubes','Tensile strength-an ability to resist a pulling force,capillarity-the ability to rise in thin tubes\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 188','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','match','Tensile strength,an ability to resist a pulling force','Capillarity,the ability to rise in thin tubes',NULL,NULL,'DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331541,'biology','Assertion: In blue white screening, the gene coding for the beta-galactosidase enzyme is used as a selectable marker\n\nReason: Transformants and non transformants can be differentiated on the basis of the colony color','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','In blue white screening, the foreign DNA is inserted into the coding region of a gene that codes for the enzyme beta-galactosidase. Because of the insertion, the gene gets inactivated. Hence, the transformants are not able to produce beta-galactosidase. Non transformants use up a chromogenic substrate with the help of the enzyme and convert it into a blue colored product. But transformants are unable to do so. Hence, non transformants form blue colored colonies while transformants form colorless colonies.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331542,'biology','Assertion: pBR322 is a cloning vector for bacteria and plants\n\nReason: Bacteria can be used to clone foreign genes in eukaryotic cells','(A) is false but (R) is true','pBR322 is an artificially created cloning vector which is only used to clone genes in E. coli. Since plants are eukaryotic in nature, they require more complex machinery to clone a foreign gene. Bacteria can be used to clone foreign genes in eukaryotic cells, for example Agrobacterium tumefaciens can be used to clone genes in dicot plants.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331543,'biology','Assertion: Viruses are natural cloning vectors \n\nReason: Some viruses can cause cancer','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Some viruses can integrate their genetic material into the host cell\'s chromosomes and direct it to express proteins required for its survival. Hence, they can be referred to as natural cloning vectors. Viruses also bring about othe changes in the host cells such that they may lose the ability to control cell division and cause cancer.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331544,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) Ti plasmid from Agrobacterium tumefaciens is used as a cloning vector in plants\n\n(ii) Ti plasmid from Agrobacterium tumefaciens is responsible for causing tumors in plants\n\n(iii) Retroviruses can be modified to transfer gene of interest in animal cells only\n\n(iv) Retroviruses can be modified to transfer gene of interest in both animal and plant cells\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(i), (ii) and (iii)','Ti plasmid in Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a virulent plasmid that causes tumors in dicot plants. It inserts a piece of DNA into the host cell genome, known as the "T DNA" which transforms normal cells into tumor cells and directs them to produce chemical required for the survival and reproduction of the pathogenic bacteria. This Ti plasmid has been modified to act as a cloning vector which inserts gene of interest in the host genome but does not cause tumor. Retroviruses can be modified to act as cloning vectors only in animal cells.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii) and (iv)','(i), (ii) and (iii)','(i), (iii) and (iv)','(i) and (iii)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331545,'biology','Assertion: Treating bacterial cells with calcium ions increases the efficiency of DNA uptake\n\nReason: The cell membrane is hydrophobic and does not allow the DNA to pass through','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','The bacterial cell membrane consists of double layer of phospolipids whose internal layer is hydrophobic in nature. And since DNA molecule is hydrophilic, it remains outside of the cells. In order to force the cells to take up DNA, they are treated with divalent cations such as calcium. These ions interact with the outer layer of the phospholipids leading to formation of pores. These pores allow the DNA to move inside the bacterial cells.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331546,'biology','Statement I: Recombinant DNA is directly injected into the nucleus of the eukaryotic cells in microinjection techniques.\n\nStatement II:Prokaryotic cells have pores in their cell wall from where recombinant DNA can enter','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Recombinant DNA can be introduced in animal cells (eukaryotes) by directly injecting it into the nucleus of the cell. This method is called microinjection. Prokaryotic cells like bacteria cannot naturally uptake recombinant DNA. They are treated with divalent cations that disrupts the cell wall and leads to the formation of pores. Recombinant DNA enters the bacterial cell through these pores.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331547,'biology','Consider the following statements in the context of genetic engineering, :\n\n(i) Competent cells are those don\'t possess the machinery to clone a foreign gene\n\n(ii) Competent cells are those that can take up recombinant DNA \n\n(iii) Competent cells are those that have plasmids in their cytoplasm\n\n(iv) Competent cells are those that have been transformed with a Algae.\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(ii) and (iii) only','Cells are said to be competent when they are able to take up recombinant DNA for cloning and expression.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','(ii) and (iii) only','(ii) and (iv) only','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','(ii) and (iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110919,'biology','Physical properties of water give','An ability to resist a pulling force','These physical properties(cohesion,adhesion,surface tension) give water high tensile strength, i.e., an ability to resist a pulling force, and high capillarity, i.e., the ability to rise in thin tubes\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 188','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','An ability to resist a pulling force','The ability to rise in thick tubes','Low tensile strength and capillarity','All the above','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331548,'biology','Assertion: Ti plasmid is an example of a disarmed pathogenic vector\n\nReason: Plant cells are bombarded with noble metals at high speed to introduce foreign gene into plant cells','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Ti plasmid is present in Agrobacterium tumefaciens, a pathogenic bacteria which causes tumors in plants. This plasmid inserts a DNA segment into the plant cell genome which further directs it to produce chemicals required for the bacterial survival. Ti plasmid has now been modified such that it does not cause tumors while maintaining the ability to insert foreign DNA into the plant cell genome. Thus, it is also referred to as a disarmed pathogenic vector. In another method to introduce foreign genes in plant cells, noble metals like gold are coated with recombinant DNA and thrown at plant cells with high speed so that they disrupt the cell wall and enter the plant cell. This method is known as gene gun or biolistics.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331549,'biology','Assertion: DNA can be separated from histone proteins using ribonuclease\n\nReason: DNA is isolated from the rest of the cellular components before restriction digestion','(A) is false but (R) is true','DNA is isolated from the cells before restriction digestion as the presence of other macromolecules like proteins and RNA may interfere with the process. DNA is isolated from proteins by the action of proteases. Ribonucleases are enzymes that degrade RNA and separate it from DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331550,'biology','Consider the following statements regarding DNA isolation:\n\n(i) DNA is separated from histones and proteins by protease.\n\n(ii) RNA is degraded by ribonuclease\n\n(iii) Chilled ethanol is added leading to DNA precipitation\n\n(iv) Lysozymes and detergents degrade the cell membranes and organelles\n\nWhich of the following options correctly represents the sequence of the steps of DNA isolation mentioned above?','(iv), (ii), (i), (iii)','DNA isolation first involves the disruption of the cell wall and membrane which is achieved by detergents and lysozyme. They disrupt the phospholipid bilayer of the cell membrane. Thereafter, ribonuclease is used for the degradation of RNA and proteases are used to degrade proteins. Finally, chilled ethanol is used to precipitate DNA from the solution and it appears as fine threads in the solution.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)','(iii), (i), (ii), (iv)','(iv), (ii), (i), (iii)','(iv), (iii), (ii), (i)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331551,'biology','Assertion: DNA can be separated from histones using specific type of protease called Proteinase K\n\nReason: Proteases can degrade proteins','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Histones are a kind of proteins that are bound with DNA the nucleus to form nucleosomes. Proteinase K is a type of protease that degrades proteins.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331552,'biology','Statement I: Polymerase chain reaction is the method of cloning multiple copies of a gene in a living cell\n\nStatement II: Primers are oligonucleotides that bind to the gene of interest','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a technique to clone multiple copies of a gene outside of a living cell. Primers are short oligonucleotides that bind to the gene of interest and are extended to form copies of the gene.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331553,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) The steps in polymerase chain reaction (PCR) in order of sequence are annealing, denaturation and extension\n\n(ii) DNA polymerase adds nucleotides to the primers and creates a new copy of the gene of interest\n\n(iii) The denaturation step involves treatement of DNA at high temperature to separate the DNA strands\n\n(iv) The gene of interest cannot be replicated if primers are not added to the PCR mixture\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are INCORRECT?','(i) only','The steps in PCR in order of sequence are denaturation, annealing and extension.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','(i) and (iii) only','(i) only','(i) and (iv)','(i), (iii) and (iv) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331554,'biology','Assertion: The DNA polymerase used in PCR is isolated from Thermus aquaticus\n\nReason: In PCR, DNA is kept at a high temperature to separate the strands of DNA and Thermus aquaticus DNA polymerase is high adapted to high temperatures','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','The denaturation step in PCR leads to the separation of DNA strands, which eventually enables the gene of interest to be replicated. DNA polymerase required in PCR is isolated from Thermus aquaticus as it is able to withstand very high temperatures.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331555,'biology','Statement I: PCR is conducted to facilitate restriction digestion of the gene of interest\n\nStatement II: PCR is a natural cloning technique adapted from the bacteria Thermus aquaticus','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','PCR is conducted to create multiple copies of the gene of interest obtained after restriction digestion. It is not a natural cloning technique. It is a technique that was developed by Kary Mullis in the 1980s for gene amplification. It uses a DNA polymerase enzyme isolated from Thermus aquaticus.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331556,'biology','Consider the following steps involved in the process of recombinant DNA technology:\n\n(i) Digestion by restriction endonucleases\n\n(ii) Amplification by polymerase chain reaction\n\n\n(iii) Separation using gel electrophoresis\n\n(iv) Rejoining of phosphodiester bonds by ligases\n\n(v) Transformation of bacterial cells\n\nWhich of the following options best represent the correct sequence of the steps mentioned above?','(i), (iii), (ii), (iv), (v)','For genetic engineering, a DNA containing the gene of interest and a cloning vector is digested with a restriction endonuclease. Thereafter, the fragments undergo gel electrophoresis followed by PCR for the separation and amplification of the gene of interest. The gene of interest is then ligated with the vector and introduced in the bacterial cells leading to transformation.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii), (iii), (iv), (v)','(i), (iii), (ii), (iv), (v)','(i), (iv), (iii), (ii), (v)','(i), (iv), (ii), (v), (iii)','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331557,'biology','Statement I: Recombinant protein is a type of protein that is formed by the expression of a recombinant foreign gene in a host cell\n\nStatement II: Recombinant protein is a type of protein that is formed by the fusion of two polypeptides from different sources','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Proteins expressed after cloning of a recombinant gene are referred to as recombinant proteins.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331558,'biology','Assertion: Eukaryotic proteins that are expressed in bacterial cells are termed as recombinant proteins\n\nReason: The ultimate goal of recombinant DNA technology is to obtain the recombinant gene product','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','Eukaryotic genes can be ligated in a suitable vector and cloned in a bacterial cell. After cloning, the gene can be induced to express into proteins. such proteins are called recombinant proteins. For example, recombinant human insulin is produced in E. coli cells. The final aim of genetic engineering is to utilize living cells to produce products of human use.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'2','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331559,'biology','Assertion: Bioreactors only increase the efficiency of cloning and not the amount of the final product\n\nReason: Large volumes (100-1000 litres) of cells can be cultured in bioreactors','(A) is false but (R) is true','Bioreactors are systems designed to culture living cells in very large volumes. This allows the production of higher biomass ultimately leading to the production of a high amount of the end product. Bioreactors are primarily used to yield large amounts of recombinant proteins.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)','(A) is true but (R) is false','(A) is false but (R) is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331560,'biology','Statement I: In a continuous culture system, the cells are continuously cultured in small flasks for long periods of time\n\nStatement II: Stirred tank reactors are a type of continuous culture system','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','Continuous culture systems are often employed at the large scale to yield large amount of desired product. In these systems, used media is continuously flushed out and fresh media is added to ensure continuous growth of living cells. This is usually achieved in large vessels called bioreactors. The stirred type bioreactors can also operate in a continuous mode.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331561,'biology','Consider the following statements:\n\n(i) The stirrer in stirred tank reactors ensure that the bacterial cells don\'t settle at the bottom of the bioreactor\n\n(ii) Sparged stirred reactors have more O₂ availability than the simple stirred reactors\n\n(iii) Bioreactors can be used only for culturing bacterial cells\n\n(iv) Air is passed through the bioreactor after sterilization to control contamination in a bioreactor\n\nWhich of the above statements is/are CORRECT?','(ii) and (iv) only','Sparged stirred reactors have spargers that circulate sterile gas filled bubbles in the culture to increase the surface area of availability of oxygen. In order to avoid any contamination, air is first sterilized by heat and then passed through the culture in the bioreactor. Bioreactors for culturing differen types of plant and animal cells have been designed. The stirrer in a bioreactor is to ensure even mixing of all components and availability of O₂ throughout the reactor.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','(i), (ii) and (iv) only','(i), (iii) and (iv)','(ii) and (iv) only','(i) and (iii) only','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331562,'biology','Statement I: Downstream processing is the process of separation and purification of recombinant proteins from the cultured cells\n\nStatement II: Downstream processing is not required when using bioreactors','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Downstream processing is the collection of different procedures involved in the separation and purification of the desired products from the cultured living cells. Since bioreactors produce large quantities of biomass, downstream processing is an essential step after culturing in bioreactors has reached a desired level.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Multiple statement question and Assertion Reason',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331601,'biology','Restriction enzymes are derived from','Bacteria','Roman numbers following the names indicate the order in which\nthe enzymes were isolated from that strain of bacteria.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Bacteria','Virus','Prions','Protozoa','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331602,'biology','Which type of restriction enzymes are used in rDNA technology?','Type II','Type II restriction enzymes are heavily used in recombinant DNA techniques. Type II enzymes consist of single, separate proteins for restriction and modification. One enzyme recognizes and cuts DNA, the other enzyme recognizes and methylates the DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Type II','Type I','Type III','Type IV','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331603,'biology','When was functions of HindII discovered?','1968','In the year 1963, the two enzymes responsible for restricting the growth\nof bacteriophage in Escherichia coli were isolated. The first restriction endonuclease–Hind II, whose functioning\ndepended on a specific DNA nucleotide sequence was isolated and\ncharacterised five years later.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','1968','1969','1966','1963','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331604,'biology','Maximum number of bases in plasmids discovered so far:','500 kilo base','The maximum number of plasmids discovered so far is 500 kilo base\nSo, the correct answer is \'500 kilo base\'','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','5 kilo base','50 kilo base','5000  kilo base','500 kilo base','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331605,'biology','In transgenics, expression of transgene in target tissues is determined by','reporter','In a transgenic organism, reporter genes are used to determine whether a particular DNA construct has been successfully introduced into a cell, organ or tissue. Whereas promoter is the region of DNA that initiates the DNA transcription, an enhancer is the sequence of DNA that enhances the transcription when binds to the specific proteic and transgene is the gene that taken from the genome of one organism and introduces into the genome of another organism.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Reporter','promoter','transgene','enhancer','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331606,'biology','There is a restriction endonuclease called EcoRI. What does the "co" part in it stand for?','coli','The convention for naming these enzymes is the first letter of the name\ncomes from the genus and the second two letters come from the species of\nthe prokaryotic cell from which they were isolated, e.g., EcoRI comes from\nEscherichia coli RY 13.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','mcq','coelom','coenzyme','colon','coli','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331607,'biology','Restriction sites for Pvu I and Pvu II respectively are in which genes of pBR 322','ampR, rop','Restriction sites for Pvu I and Pvu II for pBR322 are present on the amp\nR\n and rop gene sites of the plasmid. The amp\nR\n is the ampicillin resistance site which is used as a selectable marker. The rop gene codes for rop protein in the vector.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','rop, ampR','ampR, rop','rop, ori','ori, rop','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331608,'biology','In pBR322 cloning vector, function of rop-gene is:','It codes for protein involved in the replication of the plasmid','It codes for protein involved in the replication of the plasmid\nThe gene ‘rop’ present in pBR322 cloning vector codes for the proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','It codes for protein involved in the replication of the plasmid','Helpful in transcription','Helpful in translation','All of the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331609,'biology','What will happen when Puv II restriction enzymes is used for making recombinant DNA?','All of the above','It codes for protein involved in the replication of the plasmid\nThe gene ‘rop’ present in pBR322 cloning vector codes for the proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid. Using Puv II Restriction enzymes will result in removal of ROP gene. Thus the plasmid won\'t be able to replicate.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Plasmid will not be able to replicate','Protein used in replication of plasmid will not be formed','rDNA plasmid will not be formed','All of the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331610,'biology','In the given diagram, which of the following lane shows migration of undigested DNA? (3304)','1','Figure 11.3 pageno-198 NCERT 12th class','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2 and 3','2 and 4','all of the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/331610q.png'),
(331611,'biology','In the given diagram, which of the following lane shows migration of digested DNA? (3304)','2-4','Figure 11.3 pageno-198 NCERT 12th class','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','1','2, 3','2-4','2 ,4','NEW',NULL,NULL,'/ncert/pyq/biology/mcq/331611q.png'),
(331612,'biology','Which of the following is not a characteristic feature of the vector pBR322?','The ampicillin-resistance gene has restriction sites for BamHI and Sall.','pBR322 was the first artificial cloning vector constructed by Bolivar and Rodriguez in 1977.\n\nIn pBR322 p refers to plasmid; BR refers to Bolivar and Rodriguez, the scientists that constructed this plasmid; 322 refers to the number given to distinguish this plasmid from the other plasmids developed in the same laboratory.\n\nPlasmid pBR322 contains two resistance genes namely ampicillin resistance (ampR) gene and tetracycline resistance (tetR). These resistance genes are used as selectable markers, besides this plasmid pBR322 has a variety of unique recognition sites for restriction endonucleases.\n\nTwo unique sites i.e., Pstl and Pvul are located within the ampR gene whereas BamHI and Sall are located within the tetR gene.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','The restriction site for Pvu II is present in the replication of plasmid (rop) gene.','The ampicillin-resistance gene has restriction sites for BamHI and Sall.','It was the first artificial cloning vector constructed in 1997 by Bolivar and Rodriguez.','It has two antibiotic-resistance genes: ampR and tetR.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331613,'biology','As DNA is a hydrophilic negatively charged molecule, it means','It is difficult for DNA to cross the lipid bilayer in membrane as there is no water between the two layers','DNA isolation first involves the disruption of the cell wall and membrane which is achieved by detergents and lysozyme. They disrupt the phospholipid bilayer of the cell membrane. Thereafter, ribonuclease is used for the degradation of RNA and proteases are used to degrade proteins. Finally, chilled ethanol is used to precipitate DNA from the solution and it appears as fine threads in the solution.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','It can\'t cross cell membrane because it is negatively charged','It is difficult for DNA to cross the lipid bilayer in membrane as there is no water between the two layers','DNA can cross cell membrane','It usually crosses cell membrane by the help of porins','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331614,'biology','Why is DNA present in the Nucleus?','All of these',NULL,'Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','DNA is a hydrophilic molecule','All the machinery for replication, enzymes, and cofactors are found in the nucleus','It is protected in the nucleus from digestive enzymes and unstable conditions in the cytoplasm','All of these','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331615,'biology','pBR322 is a','dsDNA','pBR322 DNA is a commonly used plasmid cloning vector in E. coli . The molecule is a double-stranded circle 4,361* base pairs in length . pBR322 contains the genes for resistance to ampicillin and tetracycline, and can be amplified with chloramphenicol.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','dsDNA','ssDNA','ssRNA','dsRNA','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331616,'biology','size of pBR322 is','4.36 kb','pBR322 DNA is a commonly used plasmid cloning vector in E. coli . The molecule is a double-stranded circle 4,361* base pairs in length . pBR322 contains the genes for resistance to ampicillin and tetracycline, and can be amplified with chloramphenicol.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','4.36 kb','4 bp','4 kb','4.36 bp','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331617,'biology','The source organisms that possess Taq polymerase are','Both of these','The source of Taq Polymerase - It isolated from the thermophilic bacterium, Thermus aquaticus.\n\nThe function of this Enzyme - Taq polymerase is a thermostable enzyme which remains active during the high temperature- induced denaturation of DNA.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','None of these','Thermophilic bacterium','Both of these','Thermus aquaticus','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331618,'biology','Palaeontologists unearthed a human skull during excavation. A small fragment of the scalp tissue was still attached to it. Only a little DNA could be extracted from it. If the genes of ancient man need to be analyzed, the best way of getting a sufficient amount of DNA from this extract is','By subjecting the DNA to polymerase chain reaction.','The Polymerase chain reaction(PCR) is a technique by which small samples of DNA can be quickly amplified. Starting with only one gene sized piece of DNA, this technique is used to make billions of copies in few hours.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','By hybridizing the DNA with a DNA probe.','By subjecting the DNA to polymerase chain reaction.','By subjecting the DNA to gel electrophoresis.','By treating the DNA with restriction endonucleases.','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331619,'biology','Which is true regarding insertional inactivation of Beta- galactosidase enzyme?','All of the above','As an enzyme, β-galactosidase cleaves the disaccharide lactose to produce galactose and glucose which then ultimately enter glycolysis. This enzyme also causes transgalactosylation reaction of lactose to allolactose which then finally cleaved to monosaccharides','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Allolactose will not be formed','Bacteria will not grow in lactose medium','it provide evidence of positive clones by means of blue-white detection.','All of the above','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331620,'biology','During gene cloning, gene taxi is the term used for','plasmid','During gene cloning, plasmid is described as a vector and used as a genetic taxi to deliver genes of interest to other unrelated organisms. The gene is inserted into a host cell, usually by way of a plasmid.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Bacterium','Protozoa','plasmid','Vaccine','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331621,'biology','Match the following.','Puv II-Proteus vulgaris,Pst I-Providencia stuartii,Sal I-Streptomyces Albus,Bam HI-Bacillus amyloliquefaciens','The PvuII restriction-modification system is a type II system isolated from the gram-negative bacterium Proteus vulgaris. Sal I is an enzyme isolated from the bacterium Streptomyces albus. BamHI (pronounced "Bam H one") (from Bacillus amyloliquefaciens) is a type II restriction endonucleasePstI is a type II restriction endonuclease isolated from the Gram negative species, Providencia stuartii.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'2','match','Puv II,Proteus vulgaris','Pst I,Providencia stuartii','Sal I,Streptomyces Albus','Bam HI,Bacillus amyloliquefaciens','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331622,'biology','If a researcher starts his work of DNA extraction from a plant cell, the very first enzyme, he would use will be','Cellulase','Since the DNA is enclosed within the\nmembranes, we have to break the cell open to release DNA along\nwith other macromolecules such as RNA, proteins,\npolysaccharides and also lipids. This can be achieved by treating\nthe bacterial cells/plant or animal tissue with enzymes such as\nlysozyme (bacteria), cellulase (plant cells), chitinase (fungus).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Cellulase','Protease','Lysozyme','Chitinases','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331623,'biology','In Gel electrophoresis, agarose gel can be used to separate','Both RNA and DNA','Gel electrophoresis is the technique to separate and analyse the macromolecules (DNA, RNA and proteins) and their fragments, on the basis of their size and charge. In Gel electrophoresis technique gel is used as sieving medium on which charged macromolecules are moved. Agarose gel is used to analyse and separate large macromolecule such as DNA and RNA whereas polyacrylamide gel is used for small biomolecule such as protein.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','DNA','RNA','Proteins','Both RNA and DNA','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331624,'biology','Gel electrophoresis can be used to separate','ALL OF THE ABOVE','Gel electrophoresis is the technique to separate and analyse the macromolecules (DNA, RNA and proteins) and their fragments, on the basis of their size and charge. In Gel electrophoresis technique gel is used as sieving medium on which charged macromolecules are moved. Agarose gel is used to analyse and separate large macromolecule such as DNA and RNA whereas polyacrylamide gel is used for small biomolecule such as protein.','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','DNA','RNA','Proteins','ALL OF THE ABOVE','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(331625,'biology','Statement 1: Agrobacterium tumefaciens is popular in genetic engineering because this bacterium is associated with the roots of all cereal and pulse crops.\n\nStatement 2: A gene incorporated in the bacterial chromosomal genome gets automatically transferred to the crop with which the bacterium is associated.','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Agrobacterium tumefaciens infects certain plants, in which Ti plasmid (not chromosomal genome) causes the formation of tumour-like growth called a crown gall. Agrobacterium does not infect grasses (i.e., cereals).','Biotechnology and Its Applications >> Biotechnology: Principles And Processes >> Indepth questions (Syllabus reduction)',NULL,'3','mcq','Both Statement I and Statement II are true','Both Statement I and Statement II are false','Statement I is correct but Statement II is false','Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true','NEW',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(110920,'biology','In plants capillarity is aided by the small diameter of the tracheary elements which are','Tracheids and vessel elements','In plants capillarity is aided by the small diameter of the tracheary elements – the tracheids and vessel elements\nReference  :NCERT Class 11th Pg., 188','Plant Physiology >> Transport in plants >> Transpiration',NULL,'3','mcq','Tracheids and vessel elements','Fibers and tubules','Xylem parenchyma','Sclereids and fibers','DELETED',NULL,NULL,NULL),
(80101,'chemistry','Choose the odd one out with respect to excellent source of nuclear energy in modern times.','Au','Iron, copper, silver and gold are among the transition elements that have played important roles in the development of human civilisation. The inner transition elements such as Th, Pa and U are proving excellent sources of nuclear energy in modern times.\n(Reference: NCERT Chemistry Class 12; Page 215; Paragraph No. 1)','Inorganic chemistry >> The d- and f-Block Elements >> Introduction',NULL,'2','mcq','Th','Pa','U','Au','NEW','12-215','12-089',NULL);
